('f04d14c9-5185-5f0d-8a25-4d67eef1eeca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'Which option best defines the corneal endothelium?', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descemet''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency', 'The corneal endothelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that pumps fluid out of the stroma, maintaining normal hydration and transparency. Bowman''s layer and Descemet''s membrane are acellular layers, the stroma forms the bulk of the cornea, and the ''outer layer'' refers to the surface epithelium.'),
('886fd377-2d5d-56dd-9f04-0c0b3d5e8ca2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the corneal endothelium? Which option is most accurate?', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descemet''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency', 'The corneal endothelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that pumps fluid out of the stroma, maintaining normal hydration and transparency. Bowman''s layer and Descemet''s membrane are acellular layers, the stroma forms the bulk of the cornea, and the ''outer layer'' refers to the surface epithelium.'),
('2e18f126-d6f2-59ed-9bbd-893baa9edcd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Special Senses: what is the corneal endothelium?', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descemet''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency', 'The corneal endothelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that pumps fluid out of the stroma, maintaining normal hydration and transparency. Bowman''s layer and Descemet''s membrane are acellular layers, the stroma forms the bulk of the cornea, and the ''outer layer'' refers to the surface epithelium.'),
('a3634e00-03d8-58a3-bbb0-8191b50f561f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'For Special Senses, identify the correct response: what is the corneal endothelium?', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descemet''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency', 'The corneal endothelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that pumps fluid out of the stroma, maintaining normal hydration and transparency. Bowman''s layer and Descemet''s membrane are acellular layers, the stroma forms the bulk of the cornea, and the ''outer layer'' refers to the surface epithelium.'),
('25c1fd7c-3271-5ac1-b198-2d4025bb92db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'What is the corneal endothelium? Select the best answer.', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descemet''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency', 'The corneal endothelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that pumps fluid out of the stroma, maintaining normal hydration and transparency. Bowman''s layer and Descemet''s membrane are acellular layers, the stroma forms the bulk of the cornea, and the ''outer layer'' refers to the surface epithelium.'),
('2bdca5d8-8d20-5eda-80e1-adcb5bd96fb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'Which response correctly addresses this Special Senses item: what is the corneal endothelium?', '["Bowman''s layer resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descemet''s membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency","The outer layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that maintains corneal hydration and transparency', 'The corneal endothelium is a single layer of cuboidal cells on the inner surface of the cornea that pumps fluid out of the stroma, maintaining normal hydration and transparency. Bowman''s layer and Descemet''s membrane are acellular layers, the stroma forms the bulk of the cornea, and the ''outer layer'' refers to the surface epithelium.'),
('b7a0585d-1ff9-5b2e-aa8b-988a00d9bfae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Special Senses', 'Which option best defines the function of the corneal stroma?', '["Secretes tears resulting in decreased functional activity","Protects the eye accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provides nutrition mediated by specific receptor activation","Provides mechanical strength and transparency through parallel collagen lamellae and proteoglycans","Refracts light leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provides mechanical strength and transparency through parallel collagen lamellae and proteoglycans', 'The corneal stroma is built of parallel collagen lamellae embedded in a proteoglycan matrix, which provides the cornea''s mechanical strength and transparency. Tear secretion is the role of the lacrimal glands, and refraction arises mainly from the curvature of the corneal surface as a whole.'),
('aac227c9-f267-52d3-9a1b-a3d6e63898af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the urinary bladder?', '["Stratified squamous","Simple squamous","Simple columnar","Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium', 'Transitional (urothelial) epithelium lines the bladder, ureters, and renal pelvis because it stretches to accommodate urine and changes its apparent cell layering when the bladder fills. Stratified, simple, and pseudostratified epithelia cannot distend to this degree.'),
('22a94184-2df4-547b-bac6-a1c97130129f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'How many layers does the transitional epithelium have when the bladder is empty?', '["8-10 layers","4-5 layers","2-3 layers","2 layers","1 layer"]'::jsonb, '4-5 layers', 'In the empty (contracted) bladder the transitional epithelium is relatively thick, showing about 4-5 layers. When the bladder distends the epithelium thins to 2-3 layers, so 8-10, 2, and 1 are too high or too low, and 2-3 layers reflects the stretched, not the empty, state.'),
('dcbfd041-66e5-5179-86f5-99f02f80f69e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are umbrella cells?', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells are the large, often binucleate dome-shaped surface cells of the transitional epithelium; their apical uroplakin plaques make the urothelium impermeable. Collecting duct cells, podocytes, cap cells, and mesangial cells belong to the kidney, not the urothelium.'),
('d12e4cd9-68f9-5bee-9f66-b90aca2c3e02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the urothelial barrier?', '["Produces urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorbs electrolytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretes mucus mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevents urine from leaking into underlying tissues","Allows water absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents urine from leaking into underlying tissues', 'The urothelial barrier, formed by umbrella cells with uroplakin plaques and tight junctions, prevents concentrated urine and its solutes from passing into the bladder wall. It neither produces urine, secretes mucus, nor permits water or electrolyte movement into the tissues.'),
('520f0897-f56c-577f-84dc-d03fc143dbe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the lamina propria of the urinary bladder?', '["The serosal layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The epithelial layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue beneath the epithelium","The muscle layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The submucosal layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue beneath the epithelium', 'The lamina propria is the connective tissue layer lying immediately beneath the transitional epithelium, supporting the mucosa. The epithelial, muscle, serosal, and submucosal layers are distinct, separate layers of the bladder wall.'),
('7eb73fa5-05ac-5a42-b0b6-326e35b6d79d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the detrusor muscle?', '["The connective tissue","The cardiac muscle of the bladder","The skeletal muscle of the bladder","The epithelial layer","The smooth muscle of the bladder wall"]'::jsonb, 'The smooth muscle of the bladder wall', 'The detrusor is the thick layer of smooth muscle in the bladder wall that contracts during micturition to expel urine. It is composed of smooth, not cardiac or skeletal, muscle and is separate from the connective tissue and epithelium.'),
('5a72a0dc-20c2-5717-973e-7e8c912aebd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the three layers of the detrusor?', '["Only longitudinal","outer longitudinal","middle circular","Only circular","Inner longitudinal"]'::jsonb, 'Inner longitudinal', 'The detrusor smooth muscle is arranged in three layers: an inner longitudinal, a middle circular, and an outer longitudinal layer. The inner longitudinal layer is therefore one of the three recognized arrangements of the detrusor.'),
('e7a119e9-1a23-52ef-9b5e-67ca6765bd3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the trigone of the bladder?', '["The dome of the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A triangular area between the two ureteric orifices and the urethral opening","The bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The bladder base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular area between the two ureteric orifices and the urethral opening', 'The trigone is a smooth triangular region at the bladder base bounded by the two ureteric orifices above and the internal urethral orifice below. The dome, posterior wall, neck, and base are different anatomical regions of the bladder.'),
('de6a6820-8e4b-591f-a734-a2f1374644b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the histological difference of the trigone?', '["more firmly attached epithelium derived from mesoderm","Smoother","Thicker muscle","More layers of epithelium","epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Smoother', 'The trigone mucosa is smooth and lacks the folds (rugae) seen elsewhere in the bladder because its epithelium is firmly attached to the underlying layer and is derived from the mesodermal Wolffian ducts rather than endoderm. This firm attachment accounts for its smooth, featureless surface.'),
('4b51b166-69a0-5692-96af-5ceaef22b3ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the ureter?', '["Simple squamous","Transitional epithelium","Stratified squamous","Simple columnar","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium', 'The ureter is lined by transitional epithelium (urothelium) because it must tolerate the passage and distension of urine. Simple squamous, stratified squamous, simple columnar, and pseudostratified columnar epithelia are not found lining the ureter.'),
('f4ac44c7-ae16-5f3c-9a3f-7c7c29e132c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the layers of the ureter wall?', '["Mucosa","adventitia","submucosa","muscularis","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa', 'The ureter wall is composed of three coats: mucosa (transitional epithelium overlying a lamina propria), muscularis (inner longitudinal and outer circular smooth muscle), and adventitia. The mucosa is therefore the correct named layer among the options.'),
('28a30a10-6716-5c72-8e19-3e6aec9785b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the muscle arrangement in the upper ureter?', '["outer circular","Inner longitudinal","outer longitudinal","Only longitudinal","Inner circular"]'::jsonb, 'Inner longitudinal', 'In the upper two-thirds of the ureter the muscularis has an inner longitudinal and an outer circular layer. The lower third gains a third, outer longitudinal layer, but the inner longitudinal coat is the arrangement present in the upper ureter.'),
('51ece43a-3d18-5a4d-9ce4-bb7e93764f91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'How does peristalsis in the ureter move urine?', '["Gravity alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Suction from bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulsatile blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Pressure from urine production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth muscle contraction propagates bolus of urine from kidney to bladder"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle contraction propagates bolus of urine from kidney to bladder', 'Peristaltic waves of the ureter''s smooth muscle propagate from the renal pelvis toward the bladder, propelling each bolus of urine distally. Gravity, bladder suction, and arterial pulsations play no significant role in ureteric propulsion.'),
('203deaed-325b-5a78-8a73-5e2ed73fc174'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What prevents urine reflux from the bladder into the ureter?', '["A one-way valve resulting in decreased functional activity","The oblique passage of the ureter through the bladder wall creating a valve","A sphincter muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","The detrusor muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epithelial folds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The oblique passage of the ureter through the bladder wall creating a valve', 'Because the ureter enters the bladder wall obliquely, the bladder mucosa compresses it as the bladder fills, forming a passive one-way valvular mechanism that prevents vesicoureteral reflux. There is no discrete anatomical sphincter or one-way flap valve at this site.'),
('338e8206-913c-50fb-b583-578fc578180b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the male urethra (prostatic part)?', '["Stratified squamous","Pseudostratified columnar","Transitional epithelium","Stratified cuboidal","Simple columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium', 'The prostatic urethra is the proximal part of the male urethra and is lined by transitional epithelium, matching the rest of the lower urinary tract. Stratified squamous, pseudostratified, cuboidal, and simple columnar types line more distal urethral segments.'),
('a6aa40b4-f034-5d89-9cd6-6fcc18a91657'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the membranous urethra?', '["Stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple columnar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Transitional without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pseudostratified or stratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified or stratified columnar', 'The membranous urethra is intermediate in the male urethra and is lined by pseudostratified or stratified columnar epithelium. Transitional, simple squamous, and simple columnar do not line this segment.'),
('94cd92e4-4cbd-5b24-93ef-57437976b628'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the penile (spongy) urethra?', '["Pseudostratified columnar initially","then stratified squamous at the external orifice","Transitional throughout","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous throughout"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified columnar initially', 'The spongy (penile) urethra begins with pseudostratified columnar epithelium and only changes to stratified squamous epithelium near the external orifice and navicular fossa. It is not transitional, simple columnar, or stratified squamous throughout.'),
('8ee480f6-6c76-5e9d-8741-14e5c619682d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the Littré glands?', '["Seminal vesicles","Salivary glands","Mucous glands in the urethral mucosa","Prostate glands","Bulbourethral glands"]'::jsonb, 'Mucous glands in the urethral mucosa', 'The glands of Littré are small mucous (urethral) glands embedded in the mucosa of the spongy urethra that lubricate the lumen. The seminal vesicles, prostate, and bulbourethral (Cowper) glands are distinct accessory reproductive structures, and there are no salivary glands in the urethra.'),
('df8eb52d-fb84-59ee-9408-81cad10706ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the female urethra?', '["Simple columnar","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar","Stratified squamous","Simple cuboidal"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous', 'Most of the short female urethra is lined by stratified squamous epithelium, with some transitional epithelium near the bladder. Simple columnar, pseudostratified, and simple cuboidal types do not line the female urethra.'),
('faa5b3ca-701b-5a32-8a30-f9dc14b764b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the external urethral sphincter made of?', '["Skeletal muscle (voluntary)","Cardiac muscle","Fibrous tissue","Elastic tissue","Smooth muscle (involuntary)"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscle (voluntary)', 'The external urethral sphincter is composed of skeletal muscle under voluntary control, innervated by the pudendal nerve; it surrounds the membranous urethra. Smooth, cardiac, fibrous, and elastic tissues are not its constituents.'),
('677ccdbb-de89-5c79-8821-e04b89072a0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the functional unit of the kidney?', '["Loop of Henle","Nephron","Glomerulus","Collecting duct","Renal corpuscle"]'::jsonb, 'Nephron', 'The nephron, comprising the renal corpuscle and its tubular system (proximal tubule, loop of Henle, distal tubule), is the basic structural and functional unit of the kidney. The glomerulus, corpuscle, loop, and collecting duct are individual components or segments rather than the whole functional unit.'),
('28684b7b-f292-598c-951b-7209f69fb4c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'How many nephrons are in each kidney?', '["100 resulting in decreased functional activity","10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","000 mediated by specific receptor activation","Approximately 1 million","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 1 million', 'Each adult kidney contains roughly 1 million nephrons, and this number is gradually lost with age. The values of 100, 10, and the malformed entry do not reflect the true nephron population.'),
('9bab0b01-80a4-5898-bcaa-3f20efda0625'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the two main parts of the renal corpuscle?', '["Loop of Henle and collecting duct","Glomerulus and Bowman''s capsule","Proximal and distal tubule","Afferent and efferent arteriole","Cortex and medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerulus and Bowman''s capsule', 'The renal corpuscle is formed by the glomerular tuft of capillaries enclosed within Bowman''s capsule. Tubules, arterioles, and the cortical/medullary zones are separate components of the kidney and are not the two parts of the corpuscle.'),
('e3e85f53-be29-59cd-ac30-aea9af90b89e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the visceral layer of Bowman''s capsule composed of?', '["Podocytes","Mesangial cells","Simple columnar epithelium","Endothelial cells","Simple squamous epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Podocytes', 'The visceral layer of Bowman''s capsule is made of podocytes, specialized epithelial cells that wrap around the glomerular capillaries and contribute primary and secondary foot processes. Endothelial and mesangial cells are capillary-associated cells, and simple epithelia do not form this layer.'),
('9a539bc5-d6e7-51b3-85b6-d1149e02b698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the parietal layer of Bowman''s capsule composed of?', '["Stratified squamous","Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Podocytes","Simple columnar epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Simple squamous epithelium', 'The parietal layer of Bowman''s capsule is a simple squamous epithelium that becomes continuous with the proximal tubule epithelium at the urinary pole. Podocytes form the visceral layer, and the other listed epithelia are not found here.'),
('5b3b4bc8-86a8-57ef-a076-4cdd386d63df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are podocytes?', '["Immune cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth muscle cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesangial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Endothelial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epithelial cells with foot processes (pedicels) that wrap around glomerular capillaries"]'::jsonb, 'Epithelial cells with foot processes (pedicels) that wrap around glomerular capillaries', 'Podocytes are visceral epithelial cells whose pedicels interdigitate around glomerular capillaries, leaving filtration slits bridged by slit diaphragms. Mesangial, endothelial, smooth muscle, and immune cells have no such foot processes.'),
('3402ceb0-b1c9-5946-8e2c-beb7508042a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the glomerular filtration barrier?', '["Only the endothelial layer","Fenestrated endothelium","Only the basement membrane","and podocyte slit diaphragms","basement membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Fenestrated endothelium', 'The filtration barrier is a three-layered filter made of the fenestrated glomerular endothelium, the basement membrane, and the podocyte slit diaphragms; the fenestrated endothelium is the first layer blood encounters. The other choices name only a single layer or are fragments, and no layer alone constitutes the barrier.'),
('64eba3e9-b240-5d8e-9c84-2e49cea2825b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the glomerular basement membrane composed of?', '["laminin","Type II collagen","Type IV collagen","and proteoglycans","Type I collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV collagen', 'The glomerular basement membrane is built mainly of type IV collagen, with laminin and heparan sulfate-rich proteoglycans providing support and negative charge. Type I and II collagens are found in other connective tissues, not the glomerular filter.'),
('3bdcce6e-a985-5cea-a178-29c52ddc7a91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the slit diaphragm?', '["The endothelial fenestrae resulting in decreased functional activity","The filtration structure between podocyte foot processes","The basement membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","The mesangial matrix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The glomerular capillary wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The filtration structure between podocyte foot processes', 'The slit diaphragm is a thin protein sheet (containing nephrin) spanning the filtration slits between adjacent podocyte foot processes, forming the final size-selective layer of the filter. The endothelium, basement membrane, and mesangial matrix are separate structures.'),
('248d4a1f-56db-5bf1-8d71-78975bc0798f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the glomerular filtration barrier?', '["preventing passage of large proteins and cells","Secretes hormones","Selectively filters blood to form ultrafiltrate","Reabsorbs nutrients","Allows all blood components through"]'::jsonb, 'Selectively filters blood to form ultrafiltrate', 'The filtration barrier permits water and small solutes to pass while holding back large plasma proteins and blood cells, thereby producing a nearly protein-free ultrafiltrate. It does not secrete hormones, reabsorb nutrients, or let all blood components through.'),
('4fb6be9c-0e75-517c-842c-fb53902544f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the diameter of the glomerular filtration slits?', '["100 nm","50 nm","1 nm","About 25-30 nm","10 nm"]'::jsonb, 'About 25-30 nm', 'The filtration slits between podocyte foot processes measure roughly 25-30 nm in diameter, allowing water and small solutes to pass while restricting large proteins and cells. Values of 1-10 nm are too small and 50-100 nm too large for this size-selective filter.'),
('38bb0b38-8255-50ac-a377-9c2bdef444de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the molecular weight cutoff for glomerular filtration?', '["10 kDa","100 kDa","200 kDa","About 70 kDa","30 kDa"]'::jsonb, 'About 70 kDa', 'Molecules above roughly 70 kDa, with albumin (about 69 kDa) at the threshold, are largely retained by the glomerular filter. A 10-30 kDa cutoff would wrongly exclude many small plasma proteins, while 100-200 kDa are well above the sieving limit.'),
('929c9129-d1a9-5a3a-ac3d-0e91de8f495b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What charge does the glomerular basement membrane have?', '["Negative (anionic) due to heparan sulfate","charge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neutral mediated by specific receptor activation","Positive (cationic) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Variable leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Negative (anionic) due to heparan sulfate', 'Heparan sulfate proteoglycans give the glomerular basement membrane a net anionic (negative) charge that repels negatively charged plasma proteins such as albumin. A neutral or cationic surface would not provide this electrostatic barrier.'),
('f6a130e9-8d2f-57a5-a41a-bfd5c911db7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are mesangial cells?', '["Epithelial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smooth muscle cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Contractile cells in the glomerulus that regulate filtration and phagocytose debris","Endothelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contractile cells in the glomerulus that regulate filtration and phagocytose debris', 'Mesangial cells lie between and around the glomerular capillaries; their contractility modulates glomerular blood flow and filtration surface, and their phagocytic activity clears trapped macromolecules. They are neither epithelial, endothelial, neural, nor ordinary smooth muscle cells.'),
('a96df0b6-4b1f-525b-9a94-7d62a69325d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Where is the macula densa located?', '["In the collecting duct","In the proximal tubule","In the glomerulus","adjacent to the afferent arteriole","At the end of the thick ascending limb"]'::jsonb, 'At the end of the thick ascending limb', 'The macula densa is a plaque of specialized columnar cells at the terminal portion of the thick ascending limb, where the tubule contacts the vascular pole and lies adjacent to the afferent arteriole. It is not part of the collecting duct, proximal tubule, or glomerulus.'),
('a96f0c41-e56e-577f-9016-25472f7683ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are juxtaglomerular cells?', '["Cells of the macula densa resulting in decreased functional activity","Endothelial cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that secrete renin","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Podocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that secrete renin', 'Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells in the wall of the afferent arteriole containing renin granules; they release renin in response to low pressure and sympathetic stimulation. Macula densa cells, endothelium, mesangial cells, and podocytes occupy different positions and do not store renin.'),
('156a5d33-dc2f-5ac0-b09d-ad69ed2cff5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the juxtaglomerular apparatus?', '["Only the macula densa","The macula densa","Only the JG cells","JG cells","and extraglomerular mesangial cells together"]'::jsonb, 'The macula densa', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus is composed of the macula densa, the renin-secreting juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole, and extraglomerular mesangial (lacis) cells working together at the vascular pole. The macula densa is the tubular component of this complex that senses tubular fluid composition.'),
('a5bffb9a-ea16-5f8e-897e-9d5de078d844'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of renin?', '["Converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I","Reabsorbs sodium","Constricts blood vessels","Filters blood","Secretes aldosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I', 'Renin is a proteolytic enzyme that cleaves hepatic angiotensinogen to form angiotensin I, which is then converted to angiotensin II by ACE. It does not reabsorb sodium, constrict vessels, filter blood, or secrete aldosterone directly.'),
('30c754a6-77fd-5171-8afb-70e619e41798'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What does angiotensin II do?', '["Vasoconstriction","aldosterone secretion","and increased blood pressure","Decreases blood pressure","Dilates vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction', 'Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor that also stimulates aldosterone secretion and raises blood pressure through both vascular and renal effects. It never lowers blood pressure or dilates vessels.'),
('b855045f-b4ba-575f-a5e7-85d27f884e51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the proximal convoluted tubule?', '["Stratified cuboidal resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple columnar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple cuboidal with brush border (microvilli)","Transitional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple cuboidal with brush border (microvilli)', 'The proximal convoluted tubule is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium with a prominent microvillus brush border and basolateral infoldings that greatly increase surface area for reabsorption. Stratified cuboidal, simple columnar, simple squamous, and transitional epithelia do not characterize this segment.'),
('31f5d129-f5c7-5ca6-8594-91918d74c980'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the characteristics of proximal tubule cells?', '["Eosinophilic","many mitochondria","basal striations","brush border","Basophilic"]'::jsonb, 'Eosinophilic', 'Proximal tubule cells are eosinophilic because their cytoplasm is packed with mitochondria and membranes; the brush border, basal striations, and abundant mitochondria are additional supporting features. Basophilic cytoplasm is not a feature of these absorptive cells.'),
('9fe57dde-e7c8-535e-8a10-ffd15677ea29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What percentage of glomerular filtrate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule?', '["About 65%","About 25%","About 90%","About 50%","About 10%"]'::jsonb, 'About 65%', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs about 65-70% of the filtered water and sodium along with nearly all glucose and amino acids. The 10-50% figures are too low and 90% too high for the proximal segment.'),
('45bd9caa-0513-5a2f-bc9a-b85dc72333cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule?', '["Glucose","amino acids","bicarbonate","sodium","water"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs virtually all filtered glucose (via SGLT cotransporters) together with amino acids, most sodium, water, and bicarbonate, so each listed solute is reclaimed here. Glucose appears first on the list and is the classic marker of proximal tubular reabsorptive capacity.'),
('e386343c-92d3-59cd-9184-b14dbb13a15c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the loop of Henle?', '["The distal tubule resulting in decreased functional activity","The collecting duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AU-shaped tubule that creates a concentration gradient in the medulla","The glomerular capillary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A straight tube from cortex to medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AU-shaped tubule that creates a concentration gradient in the medulla', 'The loop of Henle is the U-shaped nephron segment dipping into the medulla; its countercurrent arrangement and NaCl transport generate the medullary osmotic gradient needed for urine concentration. The distal tubule, collecting duct, and glomerular capillary are separate structures, and the loop is not a straight tube.'),
('cb962aae-f5c7-589c-ae5d-db1d1004646c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the four segments of the loop of Henle?', '["Thick descending","Thick ascending only","thick ascending","thin ascending","thin descending"]'::jsonb, 'Thick descending', 'The loop of Henle has four segments: the thick descending, thin descending, thin ascending, and thick ascending limbs. The thick descending limb is therefore one of the four named subdivisions of the loop.'),
('d5afbcb7-70a4-5f38-a9c9-a1ff3da4e400'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the thin descending limb?', '["Simple columnar","Simple squamous","Simple cuboidal","Stratified squamous","Transitional"]'::jsonb, 'Simple squamous', 'The thin limbs of the loop of Henle are lined by simple squamous epithelium, which provides a short diffusion pathway for water and solutes. Simple cuboidal or columnar, stratified, and transitional epithelia line other, thicker nephron segments.'),
('8a4a9f90-4edc-578b-97fe-e57b0fb7087f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the thick ascending limb?', '["Transitional","Stratified cuboidal","Simple columnar","Simple squamous","Simple cuboidal with no brush border"]'::jsonb, 'Simple cuboidal with no brush border', 'The thick ascending limb is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium that lacks a brush border but has tall mitochondria-rich cells with extensive basolateral infoldings active in NaCl transport. The thin and transitional types do not line this segment, and the limb has no microvilli.'),
('b95fb30f-9894-5bde-bcd5-4a10ffee9dc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the thick ascending limb?', '["Active reabsorption of NaCl (impermeable to water)","diluting the tubular fluid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glucose reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Urea reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Water reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Active reabsorption of NaCl (impermeable to water)', 'The thick ascending limb actively reabsorbs NaCl via the apical Na-K-2Cl cotransporter while remaining impermeable to water, which dilutes the tubular fluid and contributes to the medullary osmotic gradient. It does not reabsorb glucose or urea, and it is the opposite of a water-reabsorbing segment.'),
('d99320fc-1d3c-5634-b4f3-70d0f6fbef8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter in the TAL?', '["Reabsorbs sodium","and chloride from the tubular lumen","potassium","Secretes potassium","Reabsorbs water"]'::jsonb, 'Reabsorbs sodium', 'The apical Na-K-2Cl (NKCC2) cotransporter of the thick ascending limb moves one sodium, one potassium, and two chloride ions from the tubular lumen into the cell, so sodium chloride is actively reabsorbed here. It does not secrete potassium or transport water.'),
('a356d514-b25f-5ecb-933b-94dc928f1dc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the distal convoluted tubule?', '["Simple cuboidal with few microvilli","Transitional","Simple squamous","Stratified cuboidal","Simple columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Simple cuboidal with few microvilli', 'The distal convoluted tubule is lined by simple cuboidal epithelium with relatively few short microvilli and prominent basal striations, consistent with its role in aldosterone-dependent sodium reabsorption. Transitional, simple squamous, stratified, and simple columnar epithelia line other regions, not the DCT.'),
('0ef49070-dae3-5bd6-bf98-e135b110a9ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What percentage of filtered NaCl is reabsorbed in the DCT?', '["About 1%","About 50%","About 5%","About 10%","About 25%"]'::jsonb, 'About 5%', 'Only about 5% of filtered NaCl is reclaimed in the distal convoluted tubule. The bulk (65-70%) is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule and about 25% in the loop of Henle, so the DCT handles a small fraction of the filtered load.'),
('63f75bf0-c2a2-5675-a96d-591f7220d326'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the distal tubule?', '["Secretion of waste resulting in decreased functional activity","Filtration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fine-tuning of electrolyte and acid-base balance","Majority of nutrient reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Water reabsorption only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fine-tuning of electrolyte and acid-base balance', 'The distal tubule performs final adjustments of sodium, potassium, calcium, and hydrogen ion handling under hormonal control. It reabsorbs only a small fraction of filtered solutes, whereas nutrient and bulk solute reabsorption occur in the proximal tubule.'),
('c7757d36-0d38-581e-bd63-baffc9944812'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What hormone acts on the DCT to increase calcium reabsorption?', '["Aldosterone resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcitonin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ADH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitamin D without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parathyroid hormone (PTH)"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid hormone (PTH)', 'PTH acts on the distal convoluted tubule to stimulate active calcium reabsorption and also promotes activation of vitamin D, which increases intestinal calcium uptake. Aldosterone chiefly regulates sodium and potassium, ADH controls water, and calcitonin lowers plasma calcium.'),
('3523925d-b3c1-5f0a-b02d-6fa04321240d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the collecting duct?', '["Stratified squamous","Simple squamous","Simple cuboidal to columnar","Transitional","Simple columnar with brush border"]'::jsonb, 'Simple cuboidal to columnar', 'The collecting duct is lined by simple cuboidal to low columnar epithelium containing principal and intercalated cells. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary passages (calyces, ureter, bladder), not the kidney tubules.'),
('9ba4a2d3-d4ed-5aa1-993d-600a6793f025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are principal cells of the collecting duct?', '["Cells that secrete acid resulting in decreased functional activity","Cells that reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium under aldosterone","Cells that reabsorb water via ADH mediated by specific receptor activation","Cells that reabsorb calcium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cells that secrete renin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cells that reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium under aldosterone', 'Principal cells are the numerically dominant cell type of the collecting duct; aldosterone binding drives apical ENaC and ROMK activity, causing sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion. They also respond to ADH for water reabsorption, while acid-base regulation via H+ or HCO3- secretion belongs to intercalated cells.'),
('402e0d59-33cc-5816-aa07-d7ccb325c83f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are intercalated cells of the collecting duct?', '["Mesangial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","JG cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Podocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Principal cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cells that regulate acid-base balance by secreting H+ or HCO3-"]'::jsonb, 'Cells that regulate acid-base balance by secreting H+ or HCO3-', 'Intercalated cells contain carbonic anhydrase with apical proton pumps or HCO3- exchangers, so type A cells secrete acid while type B cells secrete bicarbonate. Podocytes, mesangial cells, and JG cells belong to the glomerular apparatus rather than to the collecting duct.'),
('cc3d6848-1b28-5d5b-b5de-491770aba34a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of ADH on the collecting duct?', '["Stimulates renin release resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreases water permeability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases sodium reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases potassium secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increases water permeability via aquaporin-2 insertion in principal cells"]'::jsonb, 'Increases water permeability via aquaporin-2 insertion in principal cells', 'ADH binds V2 receptors on principal cells, triggering insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane, which permits water reabsorption and urine concentration. Aldosterone, by contrast, boosts sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in the same segment.'),
('9be7d1e9-e289-5536-a046-1d3ecdf223a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of aldosterone on the collecting duct?', '["Increases glucose reabsorption","Decreases sodium reabsorption","Increases water reabsorption","Increases calcium reabsorption","Increases sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Increases sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion', 'Aldosterone upregulates the epithelial sodium channel and Na+/K+-ATPase in the collecting duct, increasing sodium reabsorption while promoting potassium secretion. Glucose reabsorption and calcium handling are not primary collecting duct targets of aldosterone.'),
('a2dceb72-3747-559b-af20-478db1c5e232'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the medullary osmotic gradient?', '["Variable gradient","Increasing concentration toward cortex","Increasing solute concentration from cortex to inner medulla","Equal concentration throughout","Decreasing concentration toward medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing solute concentration from cortex to inner medulla', 'The countercurrent multiplier produced by the loop of Henle generates an osmotic gradient that rises progressively from the renal cortex to the deepest medulla. This gradient drives water reabsorption from the collecting duct under ADH control.'),
('d6900530-cd47-54b4-b370-72f2efbf53d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the countercurrent multiplier?', '["The system in the loop of Henle that creates the medullary osmotic gradient","A flow meter in the kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A reabsorption system mediated by specific receptor activation","A filtration system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A counter in the tubule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The system in the loop of Henle that creates the medullary osmotic gradient', 'Countercurrent multiplication in the loop of Henle maintains a high solute concentration in the medullary interstitium by transferring NaCl from the ascending limb and urea from the collecting duct. It is a concentrating mechanism, not filtration, reabsorption, or a measuring device.'),
('3aee2ab0-26b5-55a6-8caf-731a1f9ae384'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the renal blood flow percentage of cardiac output?', '["About 1%","About 10%","About 20-25%","About 5%","About 50%"]'::jsonb, 'About 20-25%', 'The kidneys receive about 20-25% of cardiac output (roughly 1.2 L/min), reflecting their role in filtration and blood pressure control. This high flow substantially exceeds that of most other organs per gram of tissue.'),
('64cf42bb-2ffb-575b-8927-d28a73a12cda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the normal glomerular filtration rate?', '["200 mL/min resulting in decreased functional activity","1 L/min accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","50 mL/min mediated by specific receptor activation","10 mL/min without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 125 mL/min (180 L/day)"]'::jsonb, 'About 125 mL/min (180 L/day)', 'Normal glomerular filtration rate is about 125 mL/min, which equals roughly 180 L/day of ultrafiltrate. Because nearly all of it is reabsorbed, only about 1-2 L of urine is excreted each day.'),
('1d370c49-231c-5f31-bf3d-cc202facc875'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is filtration fraction?', '["GFR / Renal plasma flow (normally 20%)","Plasma flow / Blood flow","Urine output / GFR","GFR x Renal plasma flow","Renal blood flow / GFR"]'::jsonb, 'GFR / Renal plasma flow (normally 20%)', 'Filtration fraction is the portion of renal plasma flow converted into glomerular filtrate, normally about 20%. It is computed as GFR divided by renal plasma flow, not by blood flow or urine output.'),
('14d50902-cf33-542f-810f-8047f13b66bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the afferent arteriole?', '["The arteriole that takes blood from the glomerulus","The interlobular artery","The arcuate artery","The arteriole that brings blood to the glomerulus","The efferent arteriole"]'::jsonb, 'The arteriole that brings blood to the glomerulus', 'The afferent arteriole delivers blood into the glomerular capillary tuft under high pressure that drives filtration. The efferent arteriole, by contrast, carries blood away from the glomerulus.'),
('c8900689-756f-5625-963f-7ee83456205e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the efferent arteriole?', '["The interlobular artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The peritubular capillary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The afferent arteriole mediated by specific receptor activation","The vasa recta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The arteriole that carries blood away from the glomerulus"]'::jsonb, 'The arteriole that carries blood away from the glomerulus', 'The efferent arteriole drains the glomerular capillaries. In cortical nephrons it gives rise to peritubular capillaries, while in juxtamedullary nephrons it forms the vasa recta.'),
('fb58dd89-7e3f-5249-8f43-41ad84f5ab2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the vasa recta?', '["Interlobular arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","The efferent arteriole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peritubular capillaries of the cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Straight capillary loops in the medulla that maintain the osmotic gradient","The afferent arteriole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Straight capillary loops in the medulla that maintain the osmotic gradient', 'The vasa recta are straight hairpin capillaries arising from juxtamedullary efferent arterioles that run beside the loop of Henle. Acting as countercurrent exchangers, they remove absorbed water and solute without washing away the medullary osmotic gradient.'),
('7cd53d72-8902-5bf7-b562-765bc163a092'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of peritubular capillaries?', '["Supply oxygen and reabsorb solutes from the cortical tubules","Supply blood to the glomerulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete renin mediated by specific receptor activation","Filter blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drain blood from the medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supply oxygen and reabsorb solutes from the cortical tubules', 'Peritubular capillaries are the low-pressure network surrounding cortical tubules that supply oxygen and nutrients while removing water and solutes reabsorbed by the nephron. They do not filter blood, drain the medulla, or secrete renin.'),
('20720416-74ff-5985-ba68-4254d4676015'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is autoregulation of renal blood flow?', '["Neural regulation only resulting in decreased functional activity","The kidney''s ability to maintain constant GFR despite changes in blood pressure","Passive regulation mediated by specific receptor activation","regulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regulation by hormones only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The kidney''s ability to maintain constant GFR despite changes in blood pressure', 'Renal autoregulation keeps renal blood flow and GFR fairly constant across a mean arterial pressure range of roughly 80-180 mmHg. It is achieved by the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback acting locally, without dependence on nerves or hormones.'),
('7a05f948-135e-5181-8163-fb470dadb3aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is tubuloglomerular feedback?', '["Neural feedback resulting in decreased functional activity","Hormonal feedback accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","feedback exists mediated by specific receptor activation","Feedback from bladder to kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The macula densa senses NaCl and adjusts afferent arteriole resistance to regulate GFR"]'::jsonb, 'The macula densa senses NaCl and adjusts afferent arteriole resistance to regulate GFR', 'Tubuloglomerular feedback is mediated by the macula densa, which monitors NaCl delivery in the distal tubule and alters afferent arteriolar tone to stabilize GFR. It is a local intrarenal mechanism, not neural, hormonal, or bladder-dependent feedback.'),
('a56ac1d1-372f-5c6d-bfe1-a7edd7862a06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the myogenic response?', '["Response to temperature resulting in decreased functional activity","Response to nerve stimulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smooth muscle contraction in response to stretch in afferent arterioles","Response to hormones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Response to pH changes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle contraction in response to stretch in afferent arterioles', 'The myogenic response is intrinsic contraction of afferent arteriolar smooth muscle when intraluminal pressure rises, protecting the glomerulus from pressure swings. It does not depend on nerves, hormones, pH, or temperature.'),
('18516500-4c47-5d53-bd0a-8ab6dc92e09d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the testis covered by?', '["Tunica adventitia resulting in decreased functional activity","Tunica media accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tunica intima mediated by specific receptor activation","Tunica albuginea (dense connective tissue capsule)","Tunica vaginalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tunica albuginea (dense connective tissue capsule)', 'A tough fibrous capsule of dense irregular connective tissue, the tunica albuginea, surrounds the testis and sends septa inward that divide the organ into lobules. Tunica adventitia, media, and intima are vascular coats, whereas the tunica vaginalis is a serous covering.'),
('a7e53a85-efd4-53b1-b235-f37a6d0cbe6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the seminiferous tubules?', '["Lymphatic channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Rete testis ducts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Tubules for sperm transport only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tubules in the testis where spermatogenesis occurs"]'::jsonb, 'Tubules in the testis where spermatogenesis occurs', 'Seminiferous tubules are highly coiled structures within testicular lobules that contain Sertoli cells and developing germ cells, making them the site of spermatogenesis. The rete testis only conducts sperm; it does not produce them.'),
('f9f2e87f-bb88-51ab-9e3e-d2682b1157f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What cells line the seminiferous tubules?', '["Only spermatogonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Myoid cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leydig cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Spermatogenic cells and Sertoli cells","Only Sertoli cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogenic cells and Sertoli cells', 'The seminiferous epithelium is composed of Sertoli supporting cells and spermatogenic cells at various stages of development arranged in concentric layers. Leydig cells lie in the interstitium, while myoid cells wrap the outside of the tubule wall.'),
('873cdf30-ca35-5744-9acf-ddc644b33871'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of Sertoli cells?', '["and regulate spermatogenesis; form blood-testis barrier","Store sperm","Support","nourish","Produce testosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Support', 'Sertoli cells provide structural and metabolic support, nourish developing germ cells, and form the blood-testis barrier, while also secreting inhibin and androgen-binding protein. Testosterone production is carried out by Leydig cells, not Sertoli cells.'),
('707fe1d8-818e-5d0b-bb73-ba06da2bee3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the blood-testis barrier?', '["The myoid cell layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The capillary wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The basement membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","The tunica albuginea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells that protect developing germ cells"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells that protect developing germ cells', 'Occluding junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells divide the seminiferous epithelium into basal and adluminal compartments, shielding haploid germ cells from the immune system. The myoid layer, capillary wall, basement membrane, and tunica albuginea do not form this barrier.'),
('e07242cb-f706-5c7f-83ee-ab331920dc3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the blood-testis barrier?', '["Provides nutrients resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevents immune attack on developing sperm (which appear after immune tolerance)","All of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevents toxins from reaching sperm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regulates hormone levels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents immune attack on developing sperm (which appear after immune tolerance)', 'Because spermatozoa first express surface antigens after immune tolerance is established, the blood-testis barrier sequesters them from circulating immune cells. This immunologic isolation is its primary protective function.'),
('3c98c31b-a19c-5435-a8bd-5dbb22a506c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What cells produce testosterone?', '["Myoid cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Germ cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sertoli cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Spermatogonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leydig cells (interstitial cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cells (interstitial cells)', 'Leydig cells in the testicular interstitium synthesize testosterone under LH stimulation. They are rich in smooth endoplasmic reticulum and lipid droplets consistent with steroid production, whereas Sertoli cells and germ cells do not make testosterone.'),
('aed43955-0efa-5cfb-8d75-85a087791cdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What stimulates Leydig cells?', '["Luteinizing hormone (LH) from the anterior pituitary","GnRH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibin mediated by specific receptor activation","Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Testosterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Luteinizing hormone (LH) from the anterior pituitary', 'LH binds receptors on Leydig cells and drives testosterone synthesis through steroidogenic enzymes. GnRH only triggers pituitary release of LH and FSH, FSH targets Sertoli cells, and inhibin feeds back to inhibit FSH.'),
('50513292-e4ac-5ed5-be46-614cb22abc36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What stimulates Sertoli cells?', '["Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)","Luteinizing hormone (LH) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testosterone mediated by specific receptor activation","Estrogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prolactin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)', 'FSH acts on Sertoli cells to promote spermatogenesis and stimulate secretion of androgen-binding protein and inhibin. LH primarily stimulates Leydig cells to produce testosterone.'),
('e0a886d1-81da-56a7-9833-6cadbed2e659'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of inhibin?', '["Stimulates LH resulting in decreased functional activity","Selectively inhibits FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary","Stimulates FSH mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocks testosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibits LH secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Selectively inhibits FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary', 'Inhibin is secreted by Sertoli cells and acts on the anterior pituitary to selectively suppress FSH release, providing negative feedback on spermatogenesis. It does not stimulate gonadotropins or block testosterone.'),
('766caaaf-bdb1-5d51-85ae-04fc58a51994'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the stages of spermatogenesis?', '["secondary spermatocyte","Spermatogonia","spermatid","spermatozoon","primary spermatocyte"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogonia', 'Spermatogenesis proceeds through an orderly sequence of germ cells: spermatogonia, primary spermatocytes, secondary spermatocytes, spermatids, and finally spermatozoa. Spermatogonia are the diploid stem cells at the origin of this sequence that undergo mitotic renewal before entering meiosis.'),
('28e2c3da-6a28-560a-8260-203286267806'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is spermiogenesis?', '["Capacitation resulting in decreased functional activity","The differentiation of spermatids into mature spermatozoa","Sperm transport mediated by specific receptor activation","Meiosis of spermatocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitosis of spermatogonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The differentiation of spermatids into mature spermatozoa', 'Spermiogenesis is the final phase in which round spermatids differentiate without further division, developing an acrosome, condensed nucleus, neck, and flagellum to become spermatozoa. It is distinct from mitosis of spermatogonia, meiosis of spermatocytes, and transport or capacitation.'),
('095d8dff-2702-5bf4-8e07-069e0654d958'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the acrosome?', '["The midpiece resulting in decreased functional activity","The sperm nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sperm tail mediated by specific receptor activation","The cell membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cap-like structure at the sperm head containing enzymes for egg penetration"]'::jsonb, 'A cap-like structure at the sperm head containing enzymes for egg penetration', 'The acrosome is a Golgi-derived vesicle capping the anterior two-thirds of the sperm nucleus and containing hydrolytic enzymes such as hyaluronidase and acrosin. During fertilization its contents are released to digest the zona pellucida.'),
('126d7236-29c6-53b2-b8db-19de132d410e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the parts of a spermatozoon?', '["Head","midpiece","principal piece","end piece","Head and tail only"]'::jsonb, 'Head', 'The spermatozoon is divided into the head, midpiece, principal piece, and end piece of the tail. The head contains the condensed nucleus and acrosome, while the flagellum (neck, midpiece, principal, and end pieces) provides motility.'),
('7fb1d346-a57e-5d79-9e93-61da31d7d775'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the rete testis?', '["The prostate resulting in decreased functional activity","A network of channels that transport sperm from seminiferous tubules to efferent ductules","The seminal vesicle mediated by specific receptor activation","The vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The first part of the epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A network of channels that transport sperm from seminiferous tubules to efferent ductules', 'The rete testis is an anastomosing network of channels in the mediastinum testis that collects sperm from the seminiferous tubules and delivers them to the efferent ductules. It is a transport pathway, not a secretory or storage organ.'),
('7305c760-4b1f-5ed2-ae99-62e4d754866c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the efferent ductules?', '["Ducts connecting rete testis to the epididymis","Ducts from epididymis to vas deferens","Ducts from the bulbourethral gland","Ducts from the prostate","Ducts from seminal vesicles"]'::jsonb, 'Ducts connecting rete testis to the epididymis', 'Efferent ductules are a series of small tubules that carry sperm from the rete testis to the head of the epididymis. Their pseudostratified epithelium, with both ciliated and non-ciliated cells, helps move and concentrate sperm.'),
('54f28d71-1318-549a-b346-18dfd22a1253'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the epididymis?', '["The seminal vesicle resulting in decreased functional activity","A coiled duct where sperm mature and are stored","The sperm-producing organ mediated by specific receptor activation","The vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A coiled duct where sperm mature and are stored', 'The epididymis is a single highly coiled duct, up to about 6 meters long, where sperm gain motility and fertilizing capacity and are stored. It is not the sperm-producing organ; the seminiferous tubules of the testis serve that role.'),
('bab39cce-0c32-5419-b982-da3497bb3a01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the three parts of the epididymis?', '["tail (cauda)","body (corpus)","Proximal","Head (caput)","middle"]'::jsonb, 'Head (caput)', 'The epididymis is anatomically divided into the head (caput), body (corpus), and tail (cauda). The head receives sperm from the efferent ductules, and the tail stores mature sperm before it continues as the vas deferens.'),
('6aaac552-e614-5893-b3d2-0d342c53f110'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the epididymis?', '["storage","Sperm production","Sperm maturation","Hormone production","and transport"]'::jsonb, 'Sperm maturation', 'In the epididymis, sperm acquire progressive motility and fertilizing ability through interaction with epididymal secretions. The organ also serves as a reservoir, with the cauda storing mature sperm until ejaculation.'),
('27dc3c47-b273-5275-930a-5d4945d62ce6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the vas deferens?', '["The urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","The sperm-producing tubule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The seminal vesicle mediated by specific receptor activation","A thick-walled muscular duct that transports sperm from epididymis to ejaculatory duct","The epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thick-walled muscular duct that transports sperm from epididymis to ejaculatory duct', 'The vas deferens is a fibromuscular tube with a thick smooth muscle wall whose peristaltic contractions propel sperm during ejaculation. It runs from the tail of the epididymis through the spermatic cord to join the ejaculatory duct behind the prostate.'),
('6792fdf1-2292-5353-bb7f-8d51e5171cf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the vas deferens?', '["Pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia","Stratified squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple cuboidal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified columnar with stereocilia', 'The vas deferens is lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium whose cells bear long, non-motile stereocilia (modified microvilli) that absorb fluid. This pattern is characteristic of the efferent ductules, epididymis, and vas deferens.'),
('a796cbed-2fa3-5bbb-89e3-f161639878f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the ampulla of the vas deferens?', '["The prostate resulting in decreased functional activity","The bulbourethral gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The dilated terminal part of the vas deferens before the ejaculatory duct","The beginning of the vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The seminal vesicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The dilated terminal part of the vas deferens before the ejaculatory duct', 'Toward its termination, the vas deferens dilates to form the ampulla, which receives the duct of the seminal vesicle before continuing as the ejaculatory duct. The ampulla stores sperm and mixes them with seminal vesicle secretion.'),
('76bb6766-ef05-5a4e-a470-8d3b4e42c361'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the seminal vesicle?', '["The bulbourethral gland resulting in decreased functional activity","A part of the vas deferens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A gland that produces seminal fluid rich in fructose and prostaglandins","The prostate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sperm storage organ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A gland that produces seminal fluid rich in fructose and prostaglandins', 'The seminal vesicles are paired glands whose secretion, high in fructose and prostaglandins, makes up the largest volume of the ejaculate; fructose provides an energy source for sperm. The prostate and bulbourethral glands contribute the remaining secretions.'),
('4be48c0c-fca6-5c31-b69f-3f7a6536303f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the seminal vesicles?', '["Stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple columnar mediated by specific receptor activation","Pseudostratified columnar with folded mucosa","Transitional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified columnar with folded mucosa', 'The seminal vesicle mucosa is thrown into complex folds and lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium containing tall columnar cells filled with secretion granules and small basal cells. These folds greatly increase the secretory surface area.'),
('0a3c2e52-8351-5810-87b9-ad3287254643'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the three zones of the prostate?', '["Outer","middle","peripheral","and transition zones","Central"]'::jsonb, 'Central', 'The prostate is divided into three zones: the peripheral zone, the most common site of carcinoma, the transition zone, the site of benign hyperplasia, and the central zone surrounding the ejaculatory ducts. Most prostatic glands lie within the peripheral zone.'),
('c128c551-8b40-51bd-a992-d876ae8b27f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What concretions are found in the prostate?', '["Prostatic stones resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibroids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Crystals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corpora amylacea (calcified prostatic secretions)"]'::jsonb, 'Corpora amylacea (calcified prostatic secretions)', 'Corpora amylacea are laminated concretions of inspissated prostatic secretion that commonly appear within acini with age. They are normal histologic findings, distinct from cysts, fibroids, or crystals.'),
('e5807807-e116-5789-b99b-ab7cb467ebde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the bulbourethral gland (Cowper''s gland)?', '["The epididymis resulting in decreased functional activity","A gland that secretes pre-ejaculatory mucus for lubrication","The seminal vesicle mediated by specific receptor activation","The testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A gland that secretes pre-ejaculatory mucus for lubrication', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands are paired mucus-secreting glands located at the membranous urethra whose secretion lubricates the urethra and neutralizes acidic urine before ejaculation. Seminal vesicle fluid rich in fructose serves a different role.'),
('a8b32051-4da5-5bfb-bbf0-51967ff44789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the bulbourethral gland?', '["Simple cuboidal resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stratified squamous mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar to pseudostratified columnar","Simple squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar to pseudostratified columnar', 'The bulbourethral gland is lined by simple columnar epithelium that becomes pseudostratified, with cells containing mucin droplets and an underlying fibroelastic stroma. The mucus secreted coats the urethral lumen.'),
('0008afe6-cc83-5194-befe-3747653315f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the composition of semen?', '["Sperm (1%) plus seminal vesicle fluid (60%)","Prostatic fluid only","Sperm only","prostatic fluid (30%)","bulbourethral fluid (5%)"]'::jsonb, 'Sperm (1%) plus seminal vesicle fluid (60%)', 'Semen is composed of sperm (about 1%) suspended in secretions: roughly 60% from the seminal vesicles, 30% from the prostate, and a small fraction from the bulbourethral glands. The seminal vesicle fraction is fructose-rich, while prostatic fluid is thin and somewhat alkaline.'),
('c424a3ba-bf0d-5f21-ae33-4e5371abd68e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the ovarian cortex?', '["The hilum resulting in decreased functional activity","The tunica albuginea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The germinal epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","The inner medulla containing blood vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The outer region containing ovarian follicles at various stages"]'::jsonb, 'The outer region containing ovarian follicles at various stages', 'The ovarian cortex is the peripheral zone containing ovarian follicles in all stages of development as well as corpora albicantia. The medulla is the inner core of loose connective tissue that carries the blood vessels and nerves.'),
('39ae00c6-df00-5b65-8aae-0a6e680c7ac1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the germinal epithelium of the ovary?', '["The tunica albuginea resulting in decreased functional activity","The ovarian stroma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The epithelium of the fallopian tube mediated by specific receptor activation","The surface layer of simple cuboidal epithelium covering the ovary","The follicular cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The surface layer of simple cuboidal epithelium covering the ovary', 'The germinal epithelium is the single surface layer of simple cuboidal (or low columnar) cells covering the ovary, derived from the coelomic mesothelium. It lies outside the tunica albuginea and is not the source of oocytes or the site of follicle formation.'),
('19df840c-417e-5b63-b4b4-4ed3ba447619'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the tunica albuginea of the ovary?', '["The surface epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","The outer capsule of the testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovarian medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","The follicular wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A dense connective tissue layer beneath the germinal epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'A dense connective tissue layer beneath the germinal epithelium', 'The ovarian tunica albuginea is a thin capsule of dense connective tissue lying just beneath the germinal epithelium. Although the testis has a comparable outer capsule, this description applies to the ovary; the ovarian medulla and follicular walls lie deeper.'),
('4a2640c9-70cc-5a34-8a0a-3001379d1247'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is a primordial follicle?', '["A mature follicle resulting in decreased functional activity","An oocyte arrested in prophase I surrounded by a single layer of flattened follicular cells","An atretic follicle mediated by specific receptor activation","An activated follicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A corpus luteum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An oocyte arrested in prophase I surrounded by a single layer of flattened follicular cells', 'A primordial follicle is the earliest resting stage, consisting of a primary oocyte arrested in prophase I enclosed by a single layer of flattened granulosa (follicular) cells. Mature, atretic, and activated follicles and the corpus luteum are all later or unrelated stages.'),
('15344915-c3bb-57f2-8e29-32fa31fce575'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is a primary follicle?', '["A primordial follicle resulting in decreased functional activity","A mature follicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A corpus luteum mediated by specific receptor activation","A follicle with an antrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A follicle with a cuboidal granulosa cell layer surrounding the oocyte"]'::jsonb, 'A follicle with a cuboidal granulosa cell layer surrounding the oocyte', 'A primary follicle is defined by conversion of the flattened granulosa cells to a cuboidal layer around the oocyte, with a zona pellucida appearing. Primordial follicles have flattened cells, and the antitum (fluid cavity) appears only later in secondary follicles.'),
('74ce0a0d-be81-5ad0-b723-6d1f51e216ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is a secondary (antral) follicle?', '["A primordial follicle","An atretic follicle","A corpus luteum","A primary follicle","A follicle with a fluid-filled antrum"]'::jsonb, 'A follicle with a fluid-filled antrum', 'A secondary (antral) follicle is distinguished by the formation of a fluid-filled cavity, the antrum, within the granulosa cell layer. Primordial and primary follicles lack an antitum, and atretic follicles and the corpus luteum represent degeneration or post-ovulatory stages.'),
('88ba36db-bbba-545a-8e06-f110f59f6232'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the layers of a mature (Graafian) follicle?', '["Theca externa","theca interna","basement membrane","antrum","granulosa cells"]'::jsonb, 'Theca externa', 'The wall of a mature Graafian follicle is built of the theca externa (outer fibrous layer), theca interna, a basement membrane, and an enclosing granulosa cell layer around the antrum. The theca externa is the outermost connective tissue layer of the follicle wall.'),
('58fb50b1-3f3a-50f7-b12c-874f57252c6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the cumulus oophorus?', '["A mound of granulosa cells surrounding the oocyte in the mature follicle","The theca interna accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The zona pellucida mediated by specific receptor activation","The fluid in the antrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The corpus luteum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mound of granulosa cells surrounding the oocyte in the mature follicle', 'The cumulus oophorus is a mound of granulosa cells that projects into the antitum and surrounds the oocyte of the mature follicle. At ovulation the oocyte leaves with its closest granulosa cells, the corona radiata. The theca interna, zona pellucida, antral fluid, and corpus luteum are distinct structures.'),
('ec25c686-4e44-58e8-b1ad-689cc1db6dec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the corona radiata?', '["The outermost follicular layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The innermost layer of granulosa cells directly surrounding the zona pellucida","The follicular fluid mediated by specific receptor activation","released with the oocyte at ovulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The theca externa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The innermost layer of granulosa cells directly surrounding the zona pellucida', 'The corona radiata is the innermost rim of granulosa cells that directly encircles the zona pellucida around the oocyte. It is shed with the oocyte at ovulation, protecting it during tubal transport. The theca externa is the outermost, not inner, follicular layer.'),
('1b4ff794-9e6f-524e-b240-78b66e7df0ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is ovulation?', '["Fertilization resulting in decreased functional activity","Rupture of the corpus luteum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Release of the primary oocyte mediated by specific receptor activation","Implantation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The release of the secondary oocyte (arrested at metaphase II) from the mature follicle"]'::jsonb, 'The release of the secondary oocyte (arrested at metaphase II) from the mature follicle', 'Ovulation is the LH-triggered rupture of the mature Graafian follicle with release of a secondary oocyte arrested at metaphase II, not the earlier primary oocyte. Fertilization and implantation are later events, and the corpus luteum forms only after ovulation.'),
('158a68a2-e7df-5681-a210-acdaee582351'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the corpus luteum?', '["The mature follicle resulting in decreased functional activity","The corpus albicans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A temporary endocrine gland formed from the ruptured follicle after ovulation","The atretic follicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","secreting progesterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A temporary endocrine gland formed from the ruptured follicle after ovulation', 'After ovulation the collapsed follicle reorganizes into the corpus luteum, a temporary endocrine gland whose lutein cells secrete mainly progesterone. If pregnancy does not occur it regresses to the corpus albicans, so the two are distinct structures at different times.'),
('4fcd5757-5ccc-5d6a-bd56-82aadbd87cae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What do granulosa lutein cells secrete?', '["Progesterone","Androstenedione","Testosterone","Inhibin","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone', 'Granulosa lutein cells of the corpus luteum synthesize and secrete progesterone, which supports the secretory endometrium. Androstenedione and testosterone are mainly thecal products, and estrogen comes largely from developing follicles; inhibin is a separate peptide hormone.'),
('8cfc3e40-b988-598e-8771-c57c2b9c148c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of progesterone?', '["Causes menstruation resulting in decreased functional activity","Develops secondary sex characteristics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maintains the endometrial lining for pregnancy and suppresses ovulation","Triggers ovulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates follicle growth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintains the endometrial lining for pregnancy and suppresses ovulation', 'Progesterone sustains a thick, secretory endometrium suitable for implantation and prevents further ovulation, which is essential in pregnancy. Menstruation follows progesterone withdrawal, whereas estrogen develops secondary sex characteristics and drives follicle growth, and LH triggers ovulation.'),
('155ea860-04c1-5ddd-a376-b10f32b663de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the corpus albicans?', '["The white fibrous scar that replaces the corpus luteum if pregnancy does not occur","The atretic follicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovarian medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","The corpus luteum of pregnancy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tunica albuginea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The white fibrous scar that replaces the corpus luteum if pregnancy does not occur', 'Lacking hCG support, the corpus luteum involutes and is replaced by a white fibrous scar, the corpus albicans. The corpus luteum of pregnancy persists and enlarges instead, and the atretic follicle is a degenerating follicle, not a luteal remnant.'),
('84b1c007-080d-5f76-ac30-dd67a2388920'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is follicular atresia?', '["Oocyte maturation resulting in decreased functional activity","The degeneration of follicles that do not reach maturity","Ovulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Follicle growth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Luteinization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The degeneration of follicles that do not reach maturity', 'Follicular atresia is the programmed degeneration of the vast majority of ovarian follicles, which never reach ovulation. It is the opposite of maturation, growth, ovulation, and the conversion of a ruptured follicle into the corpus luteum (luteinization).'),
('dc393ca0-3b3c-5aa3-82c5-ff62f922bbf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'How many oocytes are present at birth vs. puberty?', '["About 1-2 million at birth","2 million at birth","000-400","000 at puberty","about 300"]'::jsonb, 'About 1-2 million at birth', 'A female is born with roughly 1-2 million primary oocytes, which decline to about 300,000-400,000 by puberty owing to atresia. Only a small fraction of these will ever ovulate.'),
('b1bc6bdc-f466-5b6b-a23c-181a3fd3010c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'How many oocytes are ovulated in a lifetime?', '["000","About 1","About 100","About 10","About 400-500"]'::jsonb, 'About 400-500', 'Over a reproductive lifetime only about 400-500 oocytes are ovulated, with roughly one released per menstrual cycle during the fertile years. The remaining follicles are lost through atresia.'),
('a43353c3-db74-5ae4-a046-13bdcbb08bd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is menopause?', '["The end of a pregnancy resulting in decreased functional activity","A pause in menstruation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The permanent cessation of menstrual cycles due to ovarian follicle depletion","Irregular cycles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The first menstrual period leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The permanent cessation of menstrual cycles due to ovarian follicle depletion', 'Menopause is the permanent end of menstrual cycles caused by depletion of ovarian follicles and loss of cyclic estrogen surges. It is not a temporary pause (amenorrhea), an irregularity, or the menarche (first period), nor the end of a pregnancy.'),
('fc6ebb74-6ca5-5fc7-8c51-c7e8ece62adf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the four parts of the fallopian tube?', '["Fimbriae","intramural (interstitial)","isthmus","ampulla","Infundibulum"]'::jsonb, 'Infundibulum', 'The four named parts of the fallopian tube are the intramural (interstitial), isthmus, ampulla, and infundibulum. The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped lateral end that opens near the ovary and bears the fimbriae; the ampulla is the usual site of fertilization.'),
('d86fa6ef-9dbb-53f0-a246-22c5293561ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the fallopian tube?', '["Pseudostratified columnar resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple columnar with ciliated and secretory (peg) cells","Simple cuboidal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stratified squamous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar with ciliated and secretory (peg) cells', 'The tubal mucosa is lined by simple columnar epithelium containing two cell types: ciliated cells and interspersed secretory (peg) cells. Transitional and stratified squamous epithelia line the bladder and distal female tract, respectively, not the tube.'),
('d9ef2876-0468-5509-82ad-3203e22af86a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of ciliated cells in the fallopian tube?', '["Nourish sperm","Provide immune defense","Produce hormones","Secrete nutrients","Propel the oocyte toward the uterus"]'::jsonb, 'Propel the oocyte toward the uterus', 'The beating cilia of the tubal epithelium create a current that moves the oocyte and early embryo toward the uterus. Secretory (peg) cells, not the cilia, produce nutrients, while hormone production and immune defense are not primary tubal functions.'),
('4f2230a4-7ee0-5edd-94c9-76bd5a951251'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of peg (secretory) cells in the fallopian tube?', '["Secrete mucus and nutrients for the oocyte and sperm","Immune defense accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produce hormones mediated by specific receptor activation","Absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Propel the oocyte leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete mucus and nutrients for the oocyte and sperm', 'Peg (secretory) cells are interspersed among ciliated cells and produce mucus plus a nutrient-rich fluid that nourishes and transports the oocyte and sperm. Propulsion of the oocyte is the role of the ciliated cells, not of the peg cells.'),
('b53c44bf-904a-5ea1-81b8-ac8998b10bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Where does fertilization typically occur?', '["Ampulla of the fallopian tube","Uterine cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Isthmus mediated by specific receptor activation","Infundibulum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fimbriae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of the fallopian tube', 'Fertilization normally takes place in the ampulla, the wide outer part of the tube where the oocyte and sperm meet. Implantation, by contrast, occurs in the uterine cavity, and the isthmus, infundibulum, and fimbriae are other tubal regions.'),
('5a7a56db-a090-5ffe-a89a-d2416aa2fb97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the fallopian tube fimbriae?', '["Anchor the ovary resulting in decreased functional activity","Secrete hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transport sperm mediated by specific receptor activation","Sweep the oocyte from the ovarian surface into the infundibulum at ovulation","Provide blood supply leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sweep the oocyte from the ovarian surface into the infundibulum at ovulation', 'The fimbriae are finger-like processes at the infundibular end that sweep the ovulated oocyte off the ovarian surface and direct it into the tube. They neither anchor the ovary nor transport sperm.'),
('45e84b8e-4fc8-5d4e-9220-994926eb6142'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is tubal ectopic pregnancy?', '["Implantation of the embryo in the fallopian tube","Implantation in the ovary","Implantation in the cervix","Uterine pregnancy","Abdominal pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Implantation of the embryo in the fallopian tube', 'A tubal ectopic pregnancy is implantation of the embryo within the fallopian tube, most often the ampulla, frequently after tubal damage or impaired motility. Implantation in the ovary, cervix, or peritoneal cavity is less common and is not the tubal form.'),
('0488756f-3b72-53ed-a1f9-513ee3bcfe93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the muscle arrangement of the fallopian tube?', '["Inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle","Only longitudinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inner longitudinal and outer circular mediated by specific receptor activation","Three layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only circular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle', 'The tubal muscularis consists of inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layers. Peristaltic contractions of these layers, together with ciliary action, convey the oocyte toward the uterus; the single-layer arrangements are incorrect.'),
('eebc89ff-db40-5924-a34c-81369e01af92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the serosa of the fallopian tube?', '["The mucosal layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The muscle layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The adventitia mediated by specific receptor activation","The outer connective tissue layer covered by mesothelium (peritoneum)","The submucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The outer connective tissue layer covered by mesothelium (peritoneum)', 'The serosa is the outermost connective tissue layer of the tube, covered by mesothelium continuous with the visceral peritoneum. It encircles the muscularis and mucosa rather than the mucosa, muscle, admixed submucosa, or a fibrous adventitia.'),
('480cbd7a-3bb7-5989-937c-ba3a373142de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'How long does it take for the fertilized egg to travel to the uterus?', '["10-14 days","7-8 days","About 3-4 days","24 hours","1 day"]'::jsonb, 'About 3-4 days', 'The fertilized egg (morula) takes about 3-4 days of tubal transport to reach the uterine cavity, where it then implants around day 6-7 after fertilization. The other time estimates are too short or too long.'),
('71efd0c7-79ce-5fe4-aa82-562bd72af9a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the three layers of the uterine wall?', '["Mucosa","Endometrium","perimetrium","myometrium","submucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Endometrium', 'The uterine wall is composed of three layers: the endometrium (inner mucosa), myometrium (thick smooth muscle), and perimetrium (outer serosa). The endometrium is the inner mucosal layer; ''mucosa'' and ''submucosa'' are generic terms that do not name the distinct uterine layers.'),
('b079532f-f922-54ad-933a-ec1f53aa806c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the endometrium?', '["The outer covering of the uterus","The muscle layer of the uterus","The cervical lining","The uterine blood vessels","The inner mucosal lining of the uterus"]'::jsonb, 'The inner mucosal lining of the uterus', 'The endometrium is the inner mucosal lining of the uterus, composed of surface epithelium, glands, and stroma, and it undergoes cyclic proliferation and shedding with the menstrual cycle. The myometrium and perimetrium are the other two layers.'),
('b75d8c53-5f95-563f-8e8b-fdf30b0452ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the endometrium?', '["Simple cuboidal","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar', 'The endometrial surface is lined by a single layer of simple columnar epithelium, chiefly ciliated and non-ciliated cells continuous with the underlying glands. Transitional and stratified squamous epithelia line the bladder and the cervix and vagina, respectively.'),
('a609d4e8-b6ee-5515-a45c-4495658f5fad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the two layers of the endometrium?', '["Endometrial and myometrial resulting in decreased functional activity","Inner and outer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Functional layer (stratum functionalis) and basal layer (stratum basalis)","Superficial and deep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical and medullary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Functional layer (stratum functionalis) and basal layer (stratum basalis)', 'The endometrium is divided into the functional layer (stratum functionalis), which thickens and is shed at menstruation, and the basal layer (stratum basalis), which persists and regenerates the functional layer. The other pairs do not name these two endometrial zones.'),
('4457e24d-83b8-5080-b6da-57cf1c240d3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the stratum functionalis?', '["The superficial endometrial layer that proliferates and is shed during menstruation","The myometrium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The basal layer that remains mediated by specific receptor activation","The cervical mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The uterine blood vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superficial endometrial layer that proliferates and is shed during menstruation', 'The stratum functionalis is the superficial endometrial zone that proliferates under estrogen, becomes secretory under progesterone, and is shed during menstruation. Each cycle it is rebuilt from the persistent stratum basalis.'),
('55ce973d-768f-510a-a386-29f3e43bbd14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the stratum basalis?', '["The superficial layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The outer serosa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The muscle layer mediated by specific receptor activation","The glandular layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The deep endometrial layer that regenerates the functional layer after menstruation"]'::jsonb, 'The deep endometrial layer that regenerates the functional layer after menstruation', 'The stratum basalis is the deep endometrial layer adjacent to the myometrium that remains after menstruation and regenerates the shed functional layer each cycle. It is not the superficial zone, serosa, or smooth muscle.'),
('4f310d27-49c0-504d-8c9b-7067f3c7b24a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Which of the following lists the three phases of the menstrual cycle in correct chronological order?', '["Secretory, proliferative, menstrual","Proliferative, menstrual, secretory","Menstrual, proliferative, secretory","Follicular, ovulation, luteal","Luteal, follicular, ovulation"]'::jsonb, 'Menstrual, proliferative, secretory', 'The endometrial or menstrual cycle proceeds in the order menstrual, proliferative, and secretory phases: first bleeding and shedding, then estrogen-driven proliferation, then progesterone-driven secretion. Any other sequence inverts these stages.'),
('c356b707-31cc-517f-b17b-a0678b427d17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What hormone dominates the proliferative phase?', '["Progesterone resulting in decreased functional activity","FSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","hCG mediated by specific receptor activation","Estrogen (from developing follicles)","LH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen (from developing follicles)', 'Estrogen secreted by the developing follicles dominates the proliferative phase, rebuilding the endometrium after menstruation. Progesterone from the corpus luteum dominates the later secretory phase, while FSH and LH are pituitary regulators.'),
('31b2b3d9-325c-5880-af79-fbecee806614'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What hormone dominates the secretory phase?', '["hCG resulting in decreased functional activity","Estrogen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FSH mediated by specific receptor activation","LH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Progesterone (from corpus luteum)"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone (from corpus luteum)', 'Progesterone from the corpus luteum dominates the secretory phase, producing a thick, edematous, glycogen-rich endometrium prepared for implantation. hCG supports the corpus luteum only in pregnancy, and estrogen dominates the earlier proliferative phase.'),
('8df93607-5b19-5cf6-a638-278aab2f0ac9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the characteristics of the secretory phase endometrium?', '["Thin and atrophic","with tortuous","edematous","Thick","glycogen-secreting glands"]'::jsonb, 'Thick', 'Under progesterone the secretory-phase endometrium is thick, edematous, and packed with tortuous, glycogen-secreting glands. A thin, atrophic lining is instead seen after menstruation and in the early proliferative phase.'),
('ee050378-8439-5c11-b6eb-89528379fac2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the myometrium?', '["The cervical muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","The outer serosa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The broad ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The thick smooth muscle layer of the uterine wall","The endometrial lining leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thick smooth muscle layer of the uterine wall', 'The myometrium is the thick middle layer of smooth muscle in the uterine wall, whose contractions expel menstrual fluid and drive labor. The endometrium and perimetrium are the mucosal and serosal layers, and the broad ligament is a peritoneal fold.'),
('4c530e51-2928-5570-a803-351496e7a3b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the perimetrium?', '["The parametrium resulting in decreased functional activity","The muscle layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The serosal outer layer of the uterus (visceral peritoneum)","The broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mucosal lining leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The serosal outer layer of the uterus (visceral peritoneum)', 'The perimetrium is the outermost serosal layer of the uterus formed by visceral peritoneum. The parametrium, in contrast, is connective tissue lying alongside the cervix, and the muscle and mucosa are the deeper layers.'),
('4bbb4c9f-d5b0-5848-b7d0-08883234d4f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the cervix?', '["The vaginal wall resulting in decreased functional activity","The upper part of the uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The uterine body mediated by specific receptor activation","The lower narrow part of the uterus connecting to the vagina","The fallopian tube leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lower narrow part of the uterus connecting to the vagina', 'The cervix is the lower, narrow, cylindrical portion of the uterus that projects into and connects with the vagina. The fundus and body form the upper uterine portion, which is not the cervix.'),
('aced097d-b7b1-59b0-8463-12e323fc8b47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines the endocervix (cervical canal)?', '["Stratified squamous","Transitional","Simple cuboidal","Simple columnar (mucous-secreting)","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar (mucous-secreting)', 'The endocervical canal is lined by simple columnar, mucous-secreting epithelium; its mucus thins around ovulation to aid sperm passage. The ectocervix is instead covered by stratified squamous epithelium.'),
('31524927-995a-5989-9157-1e9f3fa1252b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the squamocolumnar junction of the cervix?', '["The junction of the cervix and uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","The internal os accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The transition between the endocervical columnar and ectocervical squamous epithelium","The external os without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cervical canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transition between the endocervical columnar and ectocervical squamous epithelium', 'The squamocolumnar junction is the zone where the endocervical columnar epithelium meets the ectocervical stratified squamous epithelium. It forms the transformation zone, the most common site for the development of cervical dysplasia and cancer.'),
('f4c7b768-2fd1-510d-9fe3-cc1fa6a797f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is a Pap smear?', '["A pregnancy test resulting in decreased functional activity","A test for ovarian cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cytological screening test for cervical cancer by sampling cells from the transformation zone","A hormonal assay without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A test for endometrial cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cytological screening test for cervical cancer by sampling cells from the transformation zone', 'A Pap smear is a cytological screening test in which cells scraped from the cervical transformation zone are examined microscopically to detect premalignant and malignant changes of the cervix. It is not a pregnancy test, hormonal assay, or screen for ovarian or endometrial cancer.'),
('eee8393a-f73b-5721-839b-d4e183d3ea08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are cervical crypts (Nabothian cysts)?', '["Cervical cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Cervical erosions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervical ulcers mediated by specific receptor activation","Cervical polyps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstructed cervical glands that appear as cysts on the ectocervix"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructed cervical glands that appear as cysts on the ectocervix', 'Nabothian cysts are benign retention cysts formed when the ducts of endocervical glands become blocked; they appear as small yellowish bumps on the ectocervix. They are not cancer, erosions, ulcers, or polyps.'),
('cc9e0c8b-7c8e-5e6f-933a-cba942fa6cd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the vagina lined by?', '["Simple columnar resulting in decreased functional activity","Stratified keratinized accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium","Transitional without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pseudostratified columnar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium', 'The vagina is lined by stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium, which resists friction; its superficial cells contain glycogen that supports lactobacilli. Keratinization is seen in skin, and the other listed epithelia line different sites.'),
('f407769b-dfa3-5775-bd2a-14142f6bd866'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of glycogen in vaginal epithelium?', '["Provides substrate for Lactobacillus producing lactic acid","maintaining acidic pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provides energy for the vaginal wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Nourishes sperm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lubricates the vagina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provides substrate for Lactobacillus producing lactic acid', 'Glycogen in the superficial vaginal epithelial cells is metabolized by Lactobacillus into lactic acid, keeping the vaginal pH acidic and inhibiting pathogenic organisms. This protective role does not involve nourishing sperm, lubricating, or powering the vaginal wall.'),
('53b61f9a-8398-5249-b1c7-0a2e1a9f11da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the vaginal epithelium''s response to estrogen?', '["maturation","Proliferation","Keratinization","and glycogen accumulation","Thinning and atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferation', 'Estrogen drives proliferation, maturation, and glycogen accumulation in the vaginal epithelium, keeping it thick and protective. Thinning and atrophy of the vaginal lining occur after menopause when estrogen falls.'),
('efda761a-2042-576c-a5ce-d487b6591c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the placenta?', '["nutrient","A maternal organ only","and waste exchange between mother and fetus","A fetomaternal organ that facilitates gas","A fetal organ only"]'::jsonb, 'A fetomaternal organ that facilitates gas', 'The placenta is a fetomaternal organ, formed from both fetal chorionic villi and maternal decidua, that carries out gas, nutrient, and waste exchange between the two circulations and produces hormones. It is therefore neither purely fetal nor purely maternal.'),
('7ab817ca-83c8-5319-8c92-62781a686abe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the two components of the placenta?', '["Maternal endometrium only resulting in decreased functional activity","Fetal membranes only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fetal chorionic villi and maternal decidua basalis","Umbilical cord and amnion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amnion and chorion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal chorionic villi and maternal decidua basalis', 'The placenta is made up of the fetal chorionic villi, which project into the intervillous space, and the maternal decidua basalis, the modified endometrium of pregnancy. The amnion, chorion, and umbilical cord are related fetal structures but not the two placental components.'),
('64deff7b-7ba0-55e5-b0a3-5b12592309e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the decidua?', '["The amnion","modified by progesterone","The chorionic villi","The endometrium of pregnancy","The fetal membrane"]'::jsonb, 'The endometrium of pregnancy', 'The decidua is the endometrium of pregnancy, modified by progesterone into a layer rich in decidual cells and glycogen that supports the invading trophoblast. It is distinct from the amnion, chorionic villi, and fetal membranes.'),
('77cda9a0-fdbc-5cdc-ad64-a096f2dcde6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the three decidual regions?', '["functionalis","decidua capsularis","Basalis","decidua parietalis","Decidua basalis"]'::jsonb, 'Decidua basalis', 'The decidua is the pregnant endometrium, named by its relation to the conceptus: decidua basalis lies deep to the implantation site, decidua capsularis covers the conceptus, and decidua parietalis lines the rest of the uterine cavity. Decidua basalis is the region between the conceptus and the uterine wall that is later invaded by trophoblast to form the placenta.'),
('7ca032fd-31ab-569a-871b-4ea9285770c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are chorionic villi?', '["Maternal blood sinuses resulting in decreased functional activity","Projections of fetal chorion into the intervillous space containing fetal blood vessels","Umbilical cord vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Decidual cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amniotic folds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Projections of fetal chorion into the intervillous space containing fetal blood vessels', 'Chorionic villi are finger-like projections of the fetal chorion that extend into the intervillous space, where they are bathed by maternal blood; each villus carries fetal blood vessels, providing the surface for nutrient and gas exchange. Maternal sinuses, cord vessels, and decidual cells are separate placental elements, not villi.'),
('3b5e4108-4e13-5c09-8a06-00c7e38707e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What cell types cover chorionic villi?', '["Only cytotrophoblast resulting in decreased functional activity","Only syncytiotrophoblast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytotrophoblast (inner) and syncytiotrophoblast (outer)","Endothelial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decidual cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cytotrophoblast (inner) and syncytiotrophoblast (outer)', 'Chorionic villi are surfaced by two trophoblast layers: the inner cytotrophoblast, a layer of mitotically active mononuclear cells, and the outer syncytiotrophoblast, a multinucleated layer that invades maternal tissue and secretes hCG. Endothelial and decidual cells do not cover the villous surface.'),
('067c36cf-02e3-5d96-891e-f67736511657'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of hCG?', '["Promotes lactation resulting in decreased functional activity","Induces labor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulates ovulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Maintains the corpus luteum during early pregnancy","Develops the fetal gonads leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintains the corpus luteum during early pregnancy', 'hCG, secreted by the syncytiotrophoblast, rescues the corpus luteum from regression by mimicking LH, so it continues to secrete progesterone to maintain the endometrium in early pregnancy. It does not promote lactation, induce labor, trigger ovulation, or direct fetal gonadal development.'),
('d8bd83e6-50ac-5e13-a782-cafc1eea9303'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the placental barrier?', '["The decidua resulting in decreased functional activity","basement membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","cytotrophoblast mediated by specific receptor activation","The layers separating fetal and maternal blood: syncytiotrophoblast","fetal endothelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The layers separating fetal and maternal blood: syncytiotrophoblast', 'The placental barrier, or placental membrane, is the set of layers between fetal and maternal blood; in the mature placenta it consists of syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast, and fetal endothelium. It permits exchange of gases, nutrients, and waste while limiting transfer of most large molecules and pathogens.'),
('b4b4f78b-d371-5d87-b210-13b3fc68b3d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the mammary gland?', '["Production and secretion of milk for infant nutrition","Sexual function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regulation of body temperature mediated by specific receptor activation","Storage of fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hormone production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Production and secretion of milk for infant nutrition', 'The mammary gland is an accessory reproductive organ whose primary role is producing and secreting milk to nourish the newborn. The other choices describe incidental or unrelated functions rather than the gland''s essential purpose.'),
('642acae4-1866-5b4f-ab0a-8221d2a8f799'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of gland is the mammary gland?', '["An endocrine gland","A holocrine gland","A modified sebaceous gland","An eccrine gland","A modified apocrine sweat gland"]'::jsonb, 'A modified apocrine sweat gland', 'Histologically the mammary gland is a modified apocrine sweat gland because its secretory alveoli release milk by exocytosis of lipid-rich material from lactocytes. It is not an endocrine, holocrine, sebaceous, or eccrine gland.'),
('c166e0d7-b1f3-5ac4-9892-6e1f62581f11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the components of the mammary gland?', '["ducts","Only alveoli","and supportive connective tissue","Only ducts","Lobules (alveoli)"]'::jsonb, 'Lobules (alveoli)', 'Each mature mammary gland consists of lobes subdivided into lobules, each containing secretory alveoli, drained by lactiferous ducts and supported by connective tissue. The alveoli are the functional milk-producing units of the glandular parenchyma.'),
('cdd0bd05-3c81-5ad6-9161-78b07b9d2ff1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are lactiferous ducts?', '["The alveoli resulting in decreased functional activity","The smallest ducts in the breast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","The supporting ligaments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The main ducts that carry milk from lobules to the nipple"]'::jsonb, 'The main ducts that carry milk from lobules to the nipple', 'Lactiferous ducts are the principal channels that drain milk from the mammary lobules to the nipple, dilating into lactiferous sinuses before the nipple. Alveoli, small intralobular ducts, vessels, and suspensory ligaments are distinct structures.'),
('a13c1a4c-caa9-5b7c-9712-f5e4bfce683c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What type of epithelium lines lactiferous ducts?', '["Simple cuboidal","Pseudostratified columnar","becoming stratified squamous at the nipple","Simple columnar","Stratified cuboidal to columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified cuboidal to columnar', 'Lactiferous ducts are lined by stratified cuboidal to columnar epithelium, which becomes two layered and then stratified squamous where the ducts open at the nipple. Simple and pseudostratified epithelia do not line these ducts.'),
('a03990c9-c3f6-5c65-8ec9-9673defd59ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What cells in the alveoli secrete milk?', '["Myoepithelial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Adipocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar epithelial cells (lactocytes)","Fibroblasts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ductal cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar epithelial cells (lactocytes)', 'Milk is produced by the alveolar epithelial cells, the lactocytes, which discharge milk components into the alveolar lumen under the influence of prolactin. Myoepithelial cells contract to eject milk but do not synthesize it.'),
('06ffdb6a-0827-5d15-a940-68eb8cd3c6b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of myoepithelial cells in the mammary gland?', '["Secrete milk resulting in decreased functional activity","Support the gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contract to eject milk from alveoli and ducts","Innervate the gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Provide blood supply leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contract to eject milk from alveoli and ducts', 'Myoepithelial cells form a basket-like network around the alveoli and ducts; when stimulated by oxytocin they contract, squeezing milk out of the alveoli into the duct system. Secretion, structural support, innervation, and blood supply are roles of other components.'),
('6ec4e8db-1425-5c2f-b457-a7d1bb50dee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the structure of the nipple?', '["Mucous membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with smooth muscle and sebaceous glands","Simple columnar epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Transitional epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hairless skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium with smooth muscle and sebaceous glands', 'The nipple is covered by keratinized stratified squamous epithelium and contains circular and longitudinal smooth muscle fibers plus sebaceous glands at its base, features that allow erectility and lubrication. Mucous, simple columnar, and transitional linings are not found here.'),
('5ba73365-b081-579f-b075-3113383a994e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the suspensory ligament of Cooper?', '["The main milk duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous bands that support the breast and attach to the skin and pectoral fascia","The lymph node mediated by specific receptor activation","The chest wall muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The blood vessel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous bands that support the breast and attach to the skin and pectoral fascia', 'The suspensory ligaments of Cooper are fibrous connective tissue bands that anchor the breast to the overlying dermis and to the deep pectoral fascia, supporting the breast architecture; their tethering produces skin dimpling in breast carcinoma. They are not ducts, muscle, lymph nodes, or vessels.'),
('cb88608d-80c7-5170-ba42-73636d54c297'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What hormone is primarily responsible for milk production?', '["Oxytocin","Progesterone","hCG","Estrogen","Prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin', 'Prolactin from the anterior pituitary acts on lactocytes to stimulate milk production after delivery. Oxytocin mediates milk ejection, while estrogen and progesterone promote ductal and alveolar growth during pregnancy rather than milk synthesis.'),
('4f3f1a08-c952-5627-ab8b-8b90ca4faa5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What hormone is responsible for milk ejection?', '["hCG","Estrogen","Oxytocin","Progesterone","Prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin', 'Oxytocin, released by the posterior pituitary during suckling, causes myoepithelial cells around the alveoli and ducts to contract and eject milk. Prolactin makes milk but does not discharge it.'),
('3d376216-f9e6-592f-80a3-6601e8e8bca9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the spermatic cord?', '["pampiniform plexus","and nerves","testicular artery","The structure containing the vas deferens","The structure suspending the testis"]'::jsonb, 'The structure containing the vas deferens', 'The spermatic cord runs from the deep inguinal ring to the testis and contains the vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, and autonomic nerves. It is the coiled structure supporting the testis within the scrotum.'),
('f2adee43-a4df-5016-bdb1-b0d348149c56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the pampiniform plexus?', '["A network of testicular veins that cools arterial blood entering the testis","The duct system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lymphatic plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","The arterial network without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The nerve plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A network of testicular veins that cools arterial blood entering the testis', 'The pampiniform plexus is the network of testicular veins surrounding the testicular artery; counter-current heat exchange cools arterial blood before it reaches the testis, keeping it below core temperature for spermatogenesis. It is not the duct system, lymphatics, arterial network, or nerve plexus.'),
('12e514d1-681a-5b81-90ae-1304266bf223'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the scrotum?', '["The internal sac resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","The groin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external pouch of skin and dartos muscle containing the testes"]'::jsonb, 'The external pouch of skin and dartos muscle containing the testes', 'The scrotum is the external pouch of skin and dartos muscle that holds the testes and spermatic cords, helping maintain a temperature lower than the abdomen. The other choices refer to deeper or unrelated regions.'),
('532b4c3d-1038-55c1-8bcd-2d7cae1ec2ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the dartos muscle?', '["Skeletal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Voluntary muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Striated muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiac muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth muscle in the scrotal wall that wrinkles the skin"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle in the scrotal wall that wrinkles the skin', 'The dartos is smooth (non-striated, involuntary) muscle in the scrotal wall that contracts to wrinkle the skin, reducing surface area and heat loss. It is not skeletal, voluntary, striated, or cardiac muscle.'),
('840366fc-d2c4-5e49-a2a0-d7855ae9ad94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the cremaster muscle?', '["Striated skeletal muscle derived from internal oblique that elevates the testis","Dartos muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Levator ani mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Striated skeletal muscle derived from internal oblique that elevates the testis', 'The cremaster is a striated skeletal muscle derived from the internal oblique muscle; it descends over the spermatic cord and testis and contracts to elevate the testis toward the abdomen. The other options name smooth, cardiac, or unrelated pelvic muscles.'),
('6cfc41ac-ae59-55f2-808b-e79e8535148c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'When do testes normally descend?', '["At birth resulting in decreased functional activity","During the 7th month of fetal development","During the 3rd month mediated by specific receptor activation","During the 5th month without affecting the primary tissue pathway","After birth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'During the 7th month of fetal development', 'The testes pass through the inguinal canal and normally reach the scrotum by about the 7th month of fetal development. Failure of this descent results in cryptorchidism.'),
('41ea0fa4-d9ad-5391-87fb-a30b100be39d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the processus vaginalis?', '["The testicular artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The vas deferens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An evagination of peritoneum that precedes testicular descent and normally closes","The epididymis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The spermatic cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An evagination of peritoneum that precedes testicular descent and normally closes', 'The processus vaginalis is a peritoneal evagination that accompanies the testis through the inguinal canal during descent; its proximal part normally closes while the distal part remains as the tunica vaginalis. The other choices are cord contents, not the peritoneal outpouching.'),
('82a6b191-a4d3-5501-abd4-08757b169b0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the menstrual phase?', '["The ovulatory phase resulting in decreased functional activity","The proliferative phase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The luteal phase mediated by specific receptor activation","The shedding of the stratum functionalis (days 1-5 of cycle)","The secretory phase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The shedding of the stratum functionalis (days 1-5 of cycle)', 'The menstrual phase spans days 1-5 of the cycle, when declining progesterone causes the stratum functionalis of the endometrium to slough and bleed. Ovulation, proliferation, and secretion are features of the other phases.'),
('e77daa18-bdb5-5824-91b0-8f611b048ab1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What triggers ovulation?', '["A surge in estrogen resulting in decreased functional activity","A surge in progesterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surge in hCG mediated by specific receptor activation","A surge in GnRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A surge in LH (and to a lesser degree FSH)"]'::jsonb, 'A surge in LH (and to a lesser degree FSH)', 'Ovulation is triggered by a mid-cycle surge of LH (supported by FSH) that acts on the mature Graafian follicle to release the oocyte. Estrogen, progesterone, hCG, and GnRH do not directly cause follicular rupture.'),
('95e1adef-1617-5e8f-8ba0-3f4a04e35881'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the luteal phase?', '["The phase before ovulation resulting in decreased functional activity","The follicular phase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The phase after ovulation when the corpus luteum produces progesterone","The proliferative phase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The menstrual phase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The phase after ovulation when the corpus luteum produces progesterone', 'The luteal phase is the post-ovulatory half of the ovarian cycle during which the corpus luteum secretes progesterone, preparing the endometrium for implantation. The follicular, proliferative, and menstrual phases occur before this time.'),
('f412a5e1-9f40-59aa-a47a-437a6e0c85ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What happens if fertilization does not occur?', '["The endometrium continues to grow","The corpus luteum persists","The corpus luteum degenerates","progesterone falls","and menstruation begins"]'::jsonb, 'The corpus luteum degenerates', 'If fertilization does not occur, the corpus luteum degenerates into the corpus albicans, progesterone and estrogen fall, and the superficial endometrium is shed as menstruation. The endometrium never continues growing, and the corpus luteum does not persist.'),
('6898f7e2-e02d-5d9f-83dd-b4ac37b2dd3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What happens if fertilization does occur?', '["The corpus luteum degenerates","which maintains the corpus luteum and progesterone secretion","Ovulation occurs","The endometrium sheds","The embryo produces hCG"]'::jsonb, 'The embryo produces hCG', 'With fertilization, the embryo produces hCG, which stimulates the corpus luteum to keep secreting progesterone and estrogen, rescuing it from regression. This maintains the endometrium rather than causing shedding or ovulation.'),
('3d33ec59-dd29-568a-8f82-09067b5dda0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the corpus luteum of pregnancy?', '["The atretic follicle resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the corpus albicans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The maintained corpus luteum that secretes progesterone for the first 8-10 weeks of pregnancy","The mature follicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The corpus luteum of menstruation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The maintained corpus luteum that secretes progesterone for the first 8-10 weeks of pregnancy', 'The corpus luteum of pregnancy is a maintained corpus luteum that continues to secrete progesterone for roughly the first 8-10 weeks until the placenta takes over steroid production. It differs from the corpus luteum of menstruation and from the corpus albicans scar.'),
('1d4dce4a-eee8-5c85-86e8-eb5d6f34377b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the lifespan of the corpus luteum of menstruation?', '["About 3 days","About 14 days","About 7 days","About 30 days","About 28 days"]'::jsonb, 'About 14 days', 'The corpus luteum of menstruation functions for about 14 days; if pregnancy does not occur it regresses to the corpus albicans. The other durations do not match its functional lifespan.'),
('52ea0b0d-f2fc-5d6a-b7ad-47a4c0f9e5a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the ovarian cycle?', '["The menstrual cycle resulting in decreased functional activity","and corpus luteum formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The monthly sequence of follicular development","The endometrial cycle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ovulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The monthly sequence of follicular development', 'The ovarian cycle is the monthly sequence of events in the ovary, from follicular development and ovulation to corpus luteum formation and regression. The menstrual and endometrial cycles describe the parallel uterine changes rather than ovarian events.'),
('cec0e236-a690-5690-8985-d1cb38dc8053'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the uterine cycle?', '["The breast cycle resulting in decreased functional activity","The ovarian cycle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vaginal cycle mediated by specific receptor activation","The cyclic changes in the endometrium corresponding to the ovarian cycle","The cervical cycle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cyclic changes in the endometrium corresponding to the ovarian cycle', 'The uterine cycle describes the recurring changes in the endometrium, spanning menstrual, proliferative, and secretory phases that run in synchronization with the ovarian cycle. The breast, vaginal, and cervical cycles are distinct processes.'),
('abe58c88-4d68-5f46-96e8-88acd804c244'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the typical duration of a menstrual cycle?', '["14 days","21 days","30 days","7 days","28 days (range 21-35)"]'::jsonb, '28 days (range 21-35)', 'A typical menstrual cycle lasts about 28 days, with a normal range of roughly 21-35 days from the start of one menses to the next. The other values fall outside the expected duration.'),
('5bbfd16b-eb5a-55a2-adcb-9986db7b8e33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the juxtamedullary nephron?', '["A nephron in the outer cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","A nephron with a long loop of Henle extending deep into the medulla","A nephron without a loop mediated by specific receptor activation","A nephron with a short loop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cortical nephron leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A nephron with a long loop of Henle extending deep into the medulla', 'Juxtamedullary nephrons have their glomeruli near the corticomedullary junction and possess long loops of Henle that dip deep into the medulla, generating the osmotic gradient needed for concentrated urine. Cortical and short-looped nephrons do not extend this far.'),
('657ed481-7e29-5bab-ac45-3143aec14a3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What percentage of nephrons are juxtamedullary?', '["About 50%","About 15-20%","About 10%","About 80%","About 5%"]'::jsonb, 'About 15-20%', 'About 15-20% of nephrons are juxtamedullary and are chiefly responsible for producing concentrated urine. Short-looped cortical nephrons make up the large majority.'),
('8418e431-b7f9-5500-9157-082dd642741d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the medullary ray?', '["A blood vessel in the medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","A connective tissue bundle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphatic channel mediated by specific receptor activation","A nerve in the medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A bundle of straight tubules and collecting ducts extending from the cortex into the medulla"]'::jsonb, 'A bundle of straight tubules and collecting ducts extending from the cortex into the medulla', 'A medullary ray is a bundle of straight tubules (loops of Henle) and collecting ducts running from the cortex toward the medulla, giving the cortex its striated appearance. It is not a vessel, lymph channel, nerve, or connective tissue bundle.'),
('1ab961d2-ec3a-5e1c-8a5e-f0e45fc7dea7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the renal pelvis?', '["The renal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","The major calyx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The renal medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","The funnel-shaped dilation of the ureter within the renal sinus","The renal capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The funnel-shaped dilation of the ureter within the renal sinus', 'The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped dilation of the ureter lying inside the renal sinus that collects urine from the major calyces. The capsule, cortex, and medulla are surrounding renal tissue.'),
('fac906f2-39d6-5664-a298-c4594d056ba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the major and minor calyces?', '["Connective tissue structures resulting in decreased functional activity","Chambers that collect urine from the renal papillae and drain into the pelvis","Blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chambers that collect urine from the renal papillae and drain into the pelvis', 'The minor calyces embrace the renal papillae and collect urine, which then drains through the major calyces into the renal pelvis. They are urine-collecting cavities, not connective tissue, vessels, lymphatics, or nerves.'),
('77f81df7-a1eb-5f4d-9f4a-cb8d89396d75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the renal sinus?', '["calyces resulting in decreased functional activity","vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","and fat mediated by specific receptor activation","The hollow center of the kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cavity within the kidney that contains the pelvis"]'::jsonb, 'The cavity within the kidney that contains the pelvis', 'The renal sinus is the fat-filled cavity within the kidney that houses the renal pelvis, calyces, and vessels. It is a space, not one of the structures found inside it.'),
('d5c34c65-f1bb-5db4-972d-1ad701ccfdb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the hilum of the kidney?', '["The inferior pole resulting in decreased functional activity","and nerves enter/exit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The indented medial border where vessels","ureter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The superior pole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The indented medial border where vessels', 'The hilum is the indented medial border of the kidney through which the renal artery and vein enter and the ureter exits, along with lymphatics and nerves. The poles are the rounded top and bottom of the kidney.'),
('28ea0a4f-58e9-5092-9696-2700f30dfa2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the order of structures at the renal hilum?', '["renal artery (middle)","Ureter","ureter (posterior)","Renal vein (anterior)","artery"]'::jsonb, 'Renal vein (anterior)', 'At the renal hilum, structures are arranged from anterior to posterior as renal vein, renal artery, and renal pelvis (ureter). This ordering is a classic anatomical point useful for imaging and surgical approaches.'),
('a271dd51-be2f-5f5b-bfe5-227a3a169710'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the pyramid of the kidney?', '["A blood vessel network resulting in decreased functional activity","An epithelial layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cone-shaped medullary structure with the papilla pointing into the calyx","A connective tissue septum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cortical structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cone-shaped medullary structure with the papilla pointing into the calyx', 'A renal pyramid is a cone-shaped mass of medullary tissue whose apex, the papilla, projects into a minor calyx to release urine. It is a medullary structure, not cortical, vascular, epithelial, or connective tissue.'),
('e035816a-1824-5cda-b71a-8411cfd1f988'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is a renal column (column of Bertin)?', '["Extensions of renal cortex between the pyramids","Calyceal folds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connective tissue septa mediated by specific receptor activation","Medullary extensions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensions of renal cortex between the pyramids', 'A renal column (of Bertin) consists of extensions of renal cortex that dip down between adjacent pyramids, containing glomeruli and cortical tubules. It is not a septum, vessel, calyx, or medullary structure.'),
('1a297525-8616-5fca-b16c-7f1fd0a45d60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is nephrotic syndrome?', '["edema resulting in decreased functional activity","hypoalbuminemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood in the urine mediated by specific receptor activation","Excessive proteinuria (>3.5 g/day)","hyperlipidemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive proteinuria (>3.5 g/day)', 'Nephrotic syndrome is defined by massive proteinuria (>3.5 g/day), which leads to hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia. Hematuria is the hallmark of nephritic rather than nephrotic syndromes.'),
('a4a16464-11fa-541f-b711-11d0398cc446'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is IgA nephropathy (Berger''s disease)?', '["Nephrotic syndrome resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute glomerulonephritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic kidney disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomerulonephritis with IgA deposits in the mesangium","presenting with hematuria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerulonephritis with IgA deposits in the mesangium', 'IgA nephropathy is a glomerulonephritis characterized by IgA immune deposits in the mesangium, often presenting with gross hematuria after an upper respiratory infection. It is a distinct entity from acute nephritic syndromes and nephrotic syndrome.'),
('b51d87bc-45ea-5ef6-89ed-84ffe3cba5b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is acute glomerulonephritis?', '["Kidney stones","Slow progressive loss of renal function","causing hematuria and hypertension","Sudden inflammation of glomeruli","often post-streptococcal"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden inflammation of glomeruli', 'Acute glomerulonephritis is the sudden onset of glomerular inflammation, typically post-streptococcal, causing hematuria, edema, and hypertension. It is distinct from slow progressive loss of function and from stone disease.'),
('10600240-fd3e-5688-89db-0d215aa1125e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is acute tubular necrosis (ATN)?', '["Pyelonephritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Chronic kidney disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","causing acute kidney injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomerulonephritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Death of tubular epithelial cells due to ischemia or toxins"]'::jsonb, 'Death of tubular epithelial cells due to ischemia or toxins', 'Acute tubular necrosis is the death of tubular epithelial cells from ischemia or nephrotoxic injury, and it is a leading cause of acute kidney injury. Glomerulonephritis, pyelonephritis, and chronic disease target different renal compartments.'),
('54b07b06-443e-5f69-ab69-11da3f4ff68e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is pyelonephritis?', '["Bacterial infection of the renal pelvis and parenchyma","Cystitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renal cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation of the glomerulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney stones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial infection of the renal pelvis and parenchyma', 'Pyelonephritis is a bacterial infection involving the renal pelvis and parenchyma, usually an ascending infection from the bladder that causes fever and flank pain. Cystitis stays in the bladder, while glomerular inflammation and stones involve other sites.'),
('b1bd2e31-84be-5332-b2a2-2486a5b5f974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common cause of end-stage renal disease?', '["Glomerulonephritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Polycystic kidney disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes mellitus (diabetic nephropathy)"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes mellitus (diabetic nephropathy)', 'Diabetes mellitus, through diabetic nephropathy, is the single most common cause of end-stage renal disease. Hypertension, glomerulonephritis, obstruction, and polycystic kidney disease are important but less frequent causes.'),
('9db7a4f5-a8a9-5a49-b05e-177b25ce1126'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is polycystic kidney disease?', '["Genetic disorder with multiple cysts in the kidneys","Cystic renal tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Horseshoe kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","leading to renal failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple renal cysts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic disorder with multiple cysts in the kidneys', 'Polycystic kidney disease is a genetic disorder (autosomal dominant in adults) in which multiple cysts progressively replace the renal parenchyma, eventually leading to renal failure. Horseshoe kidney, simple cysts, and tumors are different entities.'),
('393b4b3a-b569-508c-94ab-4b610633f3b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common renal tumor in adults?', '["Transitional cell carcinoma","Oncocytoma","Nephroblastoma (Wilms tumor)","Renal cell carcinoma (clear cell type)","Angiomyolipoma"]'::jsonb, 'Renal cell carcinoma (clear cell type)', 'Renal cell carcinoma, especially the clear cell subtype, is the most common renal malignancy in adults, arising from proximal tubular epithelium. Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) predominates in childhood, while oncocytoma and angiomyolipoma are benign.'),
('fcb83fd4-ee98-5394-95a1-633a1b87f4c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is endometriosis?', '["Presence of endometrial tissue outside the uterine cavity","Inflammation of the endometrium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovarian cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Endometrial cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibroids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Presence of endometrial tissue outside the uterine cavity', 'Endometriosis is defined by the presence of endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterine cavity, typically over pelvic peritoneum and ovaries. The other options describe inflammation, cystic change, malignancy, or fibroids rather than ectopic endometrium.'),
('60778c37-6138-5054-8feb-0f9c6160cc7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is leiomyoma (fibroid)?', '["Cervical cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Benign smooth muscle tumor of the myometrium","Ovarian tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Endometrial polyp without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Malignant uterine tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign smooth muscle tumor of the myometrium', 'Leiomyoma (fibroid) is a benign smooth muscle tumor of the myometrium and the most frequent pelvic tumor in women. The other choices describe cervical, ovarian, or malignant uterine lesions.'),
('ba679a86-e63c-5608-9912-78e1da46d8cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is endometrial hyperplasia?', '["Abnormal proliferation of the endometrium","Endometrial cancer","Endometriosis","Atrophy of the endometrium","often from unopposed estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal proliferation of the endometrium', 'Endometrial hyperplasia results from abnormal proliferation of endometrial glands and stroma, driven chiefly by unopposed estrogen stimulation. It is distinct from carcinoma, atrophy, and ectopic endometrium.'),
('a77681d6-064d-51c6-82c0-869c2b3cacb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common gynecologic cancer in developed countries?', '["Vulvar cancer","Vaginal cancer","Endometrial adenocarcinoma","Ovarian cancer","Cervical cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Endometrial adenocarcinoma', 'Endometrial adenocarcinoma is the most common gynecologic cancer in developed countries, typically arising on an estrogen-stimulated endometrium in peri- and postmenopausal women. Cervical cancer is more common in developing regions, and ovarian cancer is markedly less frequent.'),
('68ef64d9-efc9-58cc-ac36-3f5a48401fc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the leading cause of cervical cancer?', '["especially types 16 and 18 resulting in decreased functional activity","HSV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HPV (human papillomavirus) infection","HIV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HPV (human papillomavirus) infection', 'Persistent high-risk human papillomavirus infection (types 16 and 18) is responsible for nearly all cervical cancers through inactivation of p53 and Rb by the E6 and E7 oncoproteins. The other listed viral, bacterial, and opportunistic agents are not causative at epidemiologically significant rates.'),
('6c53463e-be94-5468-ae31-fb2843aa7941'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN)?', '["Cervical polyp resulting in decreased functional activity","Invasive cervical cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervicitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Cervical cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Precancerous dysplasia of cervical squamous epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Precancerous dysplasia of cervical squamous epithelium', 'Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) is a premalignant squamous dysplasia of the cervical transformation zone, graded CIN 1-3 by the depth of epithelial involvement. Invasive carcinoma, polyp, cyst, and cervicitis lack this confined intraepithelial dysplastic change.'),
('cd4451aa-34ce-58fa-8d07-b4cf6a945344'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common ovarian tumor?', '["Endometrioma","Serous cystadenoma (benign)","Fibroma","Teratoma","Mucinous cystadenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Serous cystadenoma (benign)', 'Serous cystadenoma is the most common ovarian neoplasm and is benign, lined by ciliated serous-type epithelium. Mucinous cystadenoma and the other benign ovarian tumors occur less often, while an endometrioma is a non-neoplastic cyst of endometriosis.'),
('86c472a7-0b3a-53ad-a372-6fe4d2b92b6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)?', '["Prostatitis","Prostate cancer","Non-malignant enlargement of the prostate","Prostatic atrophy","especially the transition zone"]'::jsonb, 'Non-malignant enlargement of the prostate', 'Benign prostatic hyperplasia is non-malignant nodular enlargement of the prostate, with proliferation of glands and stroma chiefly in the transition zone. Prostatitis reflects inflammation, and prostate cancer is invasive malignant transformation.'),
('dc11d2ca-1514-5121-adf4-88f89b989bdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common cancer in men?', '["Bladder cancer","Testicular cancer","Colorectal cancer","Prostate cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer', 'Prostate cancer is the most common cancer among men, excluding non-melanoma skin cancer, and a major cause of cancer death. Bladder, colorectal, and lung cancers, while common, are all less frequent than prostate cancer in men.'),
('f9132c87-e3fb-58c1-995f-990f52ead4f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is Gleason grading for prostate cancer?', '["Histological grading based on glandular architecture (1-5)","Tumor size accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cell type mediated by specific receptor activation","PSA level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TNM staging leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Histological grading based on glandular architecture (1-5)', 'Gleason grading uses the degree of glandular differentiation, scored 1 to 5, to grade prostate adenocarcinoma; the two most prevalent patterns are summed to give the Gleason score. It grades histologic architecture and does not measure tumor size, PSA level, or anatomic stage.'),
('975e6dba-f2e9-5b69-af25-cb3c2dd03c59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the glomerular filtration rate autoregulation range?', '["100-200 mmHg resulting in decreased functional activity","60-120 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","120-200 mmHg mediated by specific receptor activation","80-180 mmHg mean arterial pressure","50-100 mmHg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '80-180 mmHg mean arterial pressure', 'Glomerular filtration and renal blood flow are autoregulated so that GFR stays nearly constant over a mean arterial pressure range of roughly 80-180 mmHg, via the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback.'),
('7bafc65d-489f-5ae7-9ec6-0c55225a36a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the renal clearance formula?', '["C = (P × V) / U","C = U / (P × V)","C = V / (U × P)","C = (U × V) / P","C = P × U / V"]'::jsonb, 'C = (U × V) / P', 'Renal clearance equals the urine concentration of a solute times urine flow rate divided by its plasma concentration, C = (Ux V) / P, expressing the volume of plasma cleared per minute. Any rearrangement of these variables yields incorrect units and meaning.'),
('b4c838fa-d093-50d2-824e-8c081ae0924f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is creatinine used for in renal function testing?', '["An electrolyte resulting in decreased functional activity","Endogenous marker for GFR estimation (creatinine clearance)","A toxin to measure mediated by specific receptor activation","A hormone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A pH marker leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Endogenous marker for GFR estimation (creatinine clearance)', 'Creatinine is freely filtered at the glomerulus, minimally secreted, and not reabsorbed, so its clearance is a practical endogenous estimate of GFR. It therefore functions as a renal function marker rather than as an electrolyte, hormone, or toxin.'),
('69490d9b-8c85-580d-9408-8940400e6749'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the normal serum creatinine value?', '["0.1-0.3 mg/dL","3-5 mg/dL","2-3 mg/dL","5-10 mg/dL","0.6-1.2 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '0.6-1.2 mg/dL', 'Normal serum creatinine is approximately 0.6-1.2 mg/dL, varying with muscle mass and age. Higher values indicate reduced glomerular filtration, while the other listed ranges are not physiologic for serum.'),
('8c41b323-b935-5fc5-b822-3ff4b9426bd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the normal blood urea nitrogen (BUN) level?', '["20-40 mg/dL","60-80 mg/dL","7-20 mg/dL","40-60 mg/dL","1-5 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '7-20 mg/dL', 'Blood urea nitrogen is normally about 7-20 mg/dL. Azotemia with rising BUN indicates impaired excretion or increased urea production, and the other listed ranges exceed the normal span.'),
('60bc3268-5376-549d-a033-c831b7224a9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the BUN:creatinine ratio in prerenal azotemia?', '["<10:1","5:1","15:1","10:1 (alternative wording)",">20:1"]'::jsonb, '>20:1', 'In prerenal azotemia, reduced renal perfusion increases proximal tubular urea reabsorption, so the BUN:creatinine ratio rises above 20:1. A ratio at or below 20:1 is more consistent with intrinsic renal disease or a normal state.'),
('0f8d6b1e-254b-573d-9d52-07b121be401b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the specific gravity of urine?', '["1.005-1.030","1.000-1.005","1.060-1.100","1.030-1.060","0.900-1.000"]'::jsonb, '1.005-1.030', 'Normal urine specific gravity ranges from about 1.005 to 1.030, reflecting intact dilutional and concentrating ability. Fixed values outside this range suggest impaired urinary concentration.'),
('ef16e871-f715-5f5c-b9fc-46eca587650c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the normal pH of urine?', '["7.0 only resulting in decreased functional activity","8.0-9.0 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","9.0-10.0 mediated by specific receptor activation","2.0-3.0 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4.5-8.0 (typically 5.5-6.5)"]'::jsonb, '4.5-8.0 (typically 5.5-6.5)', 'Normal urine pH varies from about 4.5 to 8.0, usually 5.5-6.5, modulated by diet and acid-base status. The alternative ranges lie outside the physiologic spectrum.'),
('3b1c663f-4777-5ea2-8f21-9da451d5baaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is hematuria?', '["Protein in the urine","Bacteria in the urine","Blood in the urine","Ketones in the urine","Sugar in the urine"]'::jsonb, 'Blood in the urine', 'Hematuria is the presence of blood in the urine, arising from glomerular injury or bleeding anywhere along the urinary tract. Protein, glucose, ketones, and bacteria in the urine are each separate findings.'),
('257f8596-e72a-54d7-ba29-1267739aac07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is proteinuria?', '["Ketones in urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Glucose in urine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excess protein excretion in urine (>150 mg/day)","Blood in urine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal protein excretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excess protein excretion in urine (>150 mg/day)', 'Proteinuria is abnormal urinary protein excretion, defined as more than 150 mg per day, and indicates glomerular or tubular disease when persistent. The other options describe ketones, glucose, blood, or normal protein handling.'),
('2f8b3992-3332-5640-9a92-849c4504116e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the indifferent stage of sexual development?', '["The stage after differentiation resulting in decreased functional activity","The stage when gonads are not yet differentiated (first 6-7 weeks)","The stage when external genitalia are male mediated by specific receptor activation","The stage of puberty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The stage of full development leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The stage when gonads are not yet differentiated (first 6-7 weeks)', 'In the indifferent stage, during roughly the first 6-7 weeks of development, the bipotential gonads have not yet committed to testis or ovary. Sexual differentiation of the gonads and genitalia begins after this window under genetic and hormonal control.'),
('2861ad3f-c774-54a4-ac06-51d9a7d22479'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What determines testicular development?', '["Maternal hormones","Absence of the Y chromosome","Expression of the SRY gene on the Y chromosome","The X chromosome","The number of X chromosomes"]'::jsonb, 'Expression of the SRY gene on the Y chromosome', 'SRY, located on the Y chromosome, is the master switch that directs the indifferent gonad to develop into a testis. Without SRY expression, the bipotential gonad follows the ovarian pathway.'),
('04f89e46-275e-5618-aa84-bf3212ce42e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the role of anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH)?', '["Causes regression of Müllerian ducts in male embryos","Triggers puberty","Induces testicular descent","Stimulates Müllerian duct development","Stimulates Wolffian duct development"]'::jsonb, 'Causes regression of Müllerian ducts in male embryos', 'Anti-Mullerian hormone from fetal Sertoli cells causes regression of the Mullerian (paramesonephric) ducts in males, so the uterus, fallopian tubes, and upper vagina fail to form. Testosterone, not AMH, is responsible for Wolffian duct differentiation.'),
('0d7209b7-9ad0-55b7-ba04-d192bb2571b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the role of testosterone in male development?', '["Suppresses gonadotropins resulting in decreased functional activity","Induces testicular development accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Causes Müllerian regression mediated by specific receptor activation","Promotes breast development without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates Wolffian duct differentiation and external genitalia development"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates Wolffian duct differentiation and external genitalia development', 'Fetal testosterone drives the Wolffian (mesonephric) ducts toward epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles and, through DHT, masculinizes the external genitalia. Mullerian regression is accomplished by AMH, not testosterone.'),
('c212aee7-3d45-5e07-beb8-1a86bc1d9adc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the genitourinary sinus?', '["The cloaca resulting in decreased functional activity","The mesonephros accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The urogenital sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","The urachus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The common opening of the urinary and reproductive systems in the perineum"]'::jsonb, 'The common opening of the urinary and reproductive systems in the perineum', 'The genitourinary sinus is the ventral compartment of the cloaca left after the urorectal septum divides it, giving rise to the urinary bladder, urethra, and the perineal outlets shared by the urinary and reproductive tracts. The urachus, cloaca, and mesonephros are developmentally distinct structures.'),
('92cafc81-9443-5b7c-ba13-a8f210e341e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the role of DHT (dihydrotestosterone)?', '["Promotes development of male external genitalia and prostate","Triggers puberty accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Causes testicular descent mediated by specific receptor activation","Promotes Wolffian duct development without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suppresses LH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes development of male external genitalia and prostate', 'DHT, formed from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase, is required for development of the male external genitalia and prostate. It does not initiate puberty, cause testicular descent, or suppress LH.'),
('2c054a0c-9d9e-5545-a849-851a985d2fa7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is 5α-reductase deficiency?', '["Testosterone deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","AMH deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A disorder where DHT deficiency causes ambiguous or female-appearing external genitalia at birth","Complete androgen insensitivity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gonadal dysgenesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A disorder where DHT deficiency causes ambiguous or female-appearing external genitalia at birth', '5-alpha-reductase deficiency causes a DHT deficit with normal testosterone, so the external genitalia fail to virilize and may appear female or ambiguous at birth, with virilization at puberty. It differs from complete androgen insensitivity, where the receptor rather than the hormone is deficient.'),
('4d3e7637-ff00-526a-8936-afb0fe262483'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is complete androgen insensitivity syndrome (CAIS)?', '["XY individual with androgen receptors defect","Testosterone deficiency","leading to female external phenotype despite testes and testosterone","DHT deficiency","Gonadal dysgenesis"]'::jsonb, 'XY individual with androgen receptors defect', 'Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome occurs in an XY individual whose tissues have nonfunctional androgen receptors, so despite testes and normal testosterone, a female external phenotype develops. Mullerian structures are absent because AMH acts normally.'),
('97d2c54a-2343-5f59-a280-ab4040ee29a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the kidney?', '["Polycystic kidney","Renal agenesis","Duplicated collecting system","Ectopic kidney","Horseshoe kidney (fusion of lower poles)"]'::jsonb, 'Horseshoe kidney (fusion of lower poles)', 'Fusion of the lower poles of the two metanephric kidneys across the midline creates a horseshoe kidney, the most common renal congenital anomaly. Unilateral agenesis, ectopic placement, and duplicated collecting systems occur much less frequently.'),
('63bd038b-f52f-5b2b-a6f0-60042fc54cfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common congenital anomaly of the male genital tract?', '["Hypospadias","Varicocele","Hydrocele","Epispadias","Cryptorchidism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypospadias', 'Hypospadias, in which the urethral meatus lies on the ventral aspect of the penis owing to failure of urethral fold fusion, is the most frequent congenital anomaly of the male genital tract. Epispadias is dorsal and far rarer, and the other options are separate conditions.'),
('1bd9ea9c-8e5c-5633-882b-028141710599'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is testicular torsion?', '["Inflammation of the testis resulting in decreased functional activity","Varicocele accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Twisting of the spermatic cord causing ischemic damage to the testis","Testicular tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Swelling of the testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Twisting of the spermatic cord causing ischemic damage to the testis', 'Testicular torsion twists the spermatic cord, occluding the testicular vessels and producing acute ischemic damage that requires urgent surgical detorsion. Orchitis, varicocele, tumor, and simple swelling do not involve cord rotation.'),
('4a596dc7-95bc-5fc9-8c7b-7f4958e10700'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common testicular tumor?', '["Seminoma","Leydig cell tumor","Teratoma","Embryonal carcinoma","Yolk sac tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Seminoma', 'Seminoma is the most common pure testicular germ cell tumor and is notable for radiosensitivity. Teratoma, embryonal carcinoma, yolk sac tumor, and Leydig cell tumor occur with lower frequency.'),
('93f13c9e-4600-5300-b611-6714f77fb6c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common cause of male infertility?', '["Cryptorchidism","Infections","Varicocele","Azoospermia","Oligospermia"]'::jsonb, 'Varicocele', 'Varicocele, dilatation of the pampiniform venous plexus, is the most common surgically correctable cause of male infertility. Azoospermia and oligospermia are seminal fluid findings, and cryptorchidism is a less frequent cause.'),
('3b4be8e0-cb73-510e-957f-d55964b8ea6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the difference between cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons?', '["Cortical: short loop","Juxtamedullary nephrons are in the cortex","juxtamedullary: long loop extending to medulla inner zone","Cortical nephrons have longer loops","difference"]'::jsonb, 'Cortical: short loop', 'Cortical nephrons have short loops of Henle that remain within the outer medulla, whereas juxtamedullary nephrons have long loops reaching the inner medulla, which help establish the medullary osmotic gradient. Both types reside in the cortex but differ in loop length.'),
('e07dec1a-02ad-53f9-84eb-f93d4af49d8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the renal corpuscle''s function?', '["Reabsorption of nutrients","Concentration of urine","Secretion of waste","Hormone secretion","Filtration of blood to produce ultrafiltrate"]'::jsonb, 'Filtration of blood to produce ultrafiltrate', 'The renal corpuscle filters the blood, delivering an ultrafiltrate into Bowman''s space. Nutrient reabsorption, waste secretion, and urine concentration are carried out by the tubular segments downstream.'),
('4abbc00a-c3f9-57ef-987c-f1f3c70c2224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the filtration slits'' average width?', '["100 nm","5 nm","10 nm","50 nm","25-30 nm"]'::jsonb, '25-30 nm', 'The filtration slits between podocyte foot processes are approximately 25-30 nm wide, forming the size-selective portion of the glomerular filtration barrier. The other values listed do not represent slit width.'),
('a09d58d5-a2bc-5493-856d-424e6f4ab604'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the podocyte marker used in immunohistochemistry?', '["and synaptopodin","Vimentin","nephrin","Podocalyxin","WT1"]'::jsonb, 'Podocalyxin', 'Podocalyxin is a reliable immunohistochemical marker of podocytes, often used together with synaptopodin. Nephrin is the slit-diaphragm protein, and vimentin is expressed too broadly to be podocyte-specific.'),
('19f24b3e-afb9-5305-a3e0-938f8998e0ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for proximal tubule cells?', '["Lotus tetragonolobus lectin (LTL) and CD10","E-cadherin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CK7 mediated by specific receptor activation","Uroplakin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GATA3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lotus tetragonolobus lectin (LTL) and CD10', 'Lotus tetragonolobus lectin (LTL) and CD10 selectively label proximal tubule cells. Uroplakin marks urothelium, GATA3 labels distal nephron and urothelium, and E-cadherin marks various tubular epithelia.'),
('79e3ed64-2e73-53ab-bdb1-697165e536c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for collecting duct intercalated cells?', '["Uroplakin resulting in decreased functional activity","Aquaporin-2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AE1 (type A) or pendrin (type B)","GATA3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CK7 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AE1 (type A) or pendrin (type B)', 'Intercalated cells of the collecting duct are typed by their acid-base transporter: type A expresses AE1 and type B expresses pendrin. Aquaporin-2 marks principal cells, and uroplakin labels urothelium.'),
('5b50ad0b-4dd2-5120-b60d-3d0b7b2b10e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for principal cells of the collecting duct?', '["Aquaporin-2 and aquaporin-3","AE1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uroplakin mediated by specific receptor activation","CD10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pendrin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aquaporin-2 and aquaporin-3', 'Principal cells of the collecting duct express aquaporin-2 and aquaporin-3, the ADH-responsive channels that mediate water reabsorption. AE1 and pendrin belong to intercalated cells, while CD10 and uroplakin relate to proximal tubule and urothelium.'),
('3d3d093d-107f-5fdf-b723-f694fc8b86f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the Sertoli cell marker?', '["GATA4","Calretinin","SOX9","WT1","and inhibin"]'::jsonb, 'WT1', 'WT1 is a dependable Sertoli cell marker, and inhibin is also produced by Sertoli cells. Calretinin preferentially labels Leydig cells, whereas GATA4 is more characteristic of other gonadal populations.'),
('e8c6c456-2927-596a-b7a2-6e1a070057eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the Leydig cell marker?', '["steroidogenic enzymes (3β-HSD)","Calretinin","PLAP","inhibin","Melan-A"]'::jsonb, 'Calretinin', 'Calretinin is a sensitive marker of Leydig cells, which also express steroidogenic enzymes such as 3-beta-HSD for testosterone synthesis. PLAP relates to germ cells, and inhibin is a Sertoli cell product.'),
('ee715b7e-bd0b-580e-b3e2-7c8d3f083d8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for prostate epithelium?', '["CK7 resulting in decreased functional activity","Uroplakin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GATA3 mediated by specific receptor activation","PSA (prostate-specific antigen) and PSAP (prostate-specific acid phosphatase)","CD10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PSA (prostate-specific antigen) and PSAP (prostate-specific acid phosphatase)', 'Prostatic epithelial cells express PSA and PSAP, valuable markers for establishing prostatic origin in tumor pathology. Uroplakin and GATA3 instead indicate urothelial differentiation.'),
('1312097d-b97b-55fd-9b65-e8eee8f831d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for endometrial epithelium?', '["Cytokeratin 7 (CK7)","PSA","progesterone receptor","estrogen receptor","GATA3"]'::jsonb, 'Cytokeratin 7 (CK7)', 'Endometrial epithelium is consistently positive for CK7. ER and PR reflect hormonal dependence rather than lineage, PSA marks prostate, and GATA3 is associated with urothelium and breast tissue.'),
('50ad18af-10c2-5441-9eba-aa56d49923c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for fallopian tube epithelium?', '["CK7","WT1","PAX8","GATA3","and estrogen receptor"]'::jsonb, 'PAX8', 'PAX8 is a highly sensitive nuclear marker of fallopian tube (tubal) epithelium. WT1 expression is more typical of ovarian serous neoplasms, and GATA3 indicates urothelial or breast lineage.'),
('38595417-20cc-5cc8-b56e-f9e9cf5aa4d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for ovarian surface epithelium?', '["and ER","WT1","GATA3","CK7","PAX8"]'::jsonb, 'PAX8', 'PAX8 is expressed by ovarian surface epithelium and by the vast majority of epithelial ovarian tumors. WT1 favors serous histology, while GATA3 implies urothelial or breast differentiation.'),
('acb50a41-f7b7-51bf-a277-801ab3f5802c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for urothelium (transitional epithelium)?', '["GATA3","uroplakin","p63","CK7","CK20"]'::jsonb, 'GATA3', 'GATA3 is a sensitive marker of urothelial (transitional) epithelium, whereas uroplakin is specific to the apical umbrella cells. CK7 and CK20 are also expressed but carry less lineage specificity.'),
('51bb5dc2-2458-5954-89e6-03522b629a2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for breast epithelium?', '["CK7","PR","ER","HER2","CK8/18"]'::jsonb, 'CK7', 'Breast epithelium is typically CK7-positive, along with CK8/18, and this pattern helps identify breast primaries. ER, PR, and HER2 determine receptor status rather than basic epithelial lineage.'),
('46b5a46d-4cd9-503a-a54e-402b7517a82c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the triple-negative breast cancer phenotype?', '["HER2-negative","ER-","PR-","ER+","PR+ (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, 'ER-', 'Triple-negative breast cancer is defined by the absence of estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2 amplification. Because all three receptors are negative, ER-negativity on the panel places the tumor in this category.'),
('e8f38a6a-41f7-58ae-b541-0bb35a1b2d49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common breast cancer type?', '["Invasive ductal carcinoma (no special type)","Mucinous carcinoma","Ductal carcinoma in situ","Medullary carcinoma","Invasive lobular carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Invasive ductal carcinoma (no special type)', 'Invasive ductal carcinoma, also called carcinoma of no special type, accounts for about 70-80% of breast cancers, making it the most common subtype. The other listed types are rarer variants and therefore cannot be the most frequent breast malignancy.'),
('e1552b63-1728-500a-bc63-996213cbcf47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is Paget disease of the breast?', '["A benign condition resulting in decreased functional activity","Inflammatory breast cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paget cells (intraepidermal adenocarcinoma) in the nipple","often underlying DCIS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobular carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paget cells (intraepidermal adenocarcinoma) in the nipple', 'Paget disease of the breast is a malignant intraepidermal adenocarcinoma of the nipple marked by large pale Paget cells within the epidermis, frequently associated with an underlying ductal carcinoma in situ. It is not a benign process, and the other options represent distinct entities rather than its definition.'),
('7692032d-2dd7-507e-ae78-919f7bd9839d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common benign breast tumor?', '["Fibrocystic changes","Lipoma","Fibroadenoma","Phyllodes tumor","Intraductal papilloma"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroadenoma', 'Fibroadenoma is the most common benign breast tumor, presenting typically in young women as a firm, mobile, painless lump. Fibrocystic change is common but is a constellation of changes rather than a discrete tumor, and phyllodes tumor, lipoma, and intraductal papilloma are all less frequent.'),
('28a2afbd-f8cc-5dea-b438-8701b84b7750'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is fibrocystic change of the breast?', '["fibrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","A premalignant condition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Breast cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","and adenosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A common benign condition with cysts"]'::jsonb, 'A common benign condition with cysts', 'Fibrocystic change is a common benign condition characterized by fibrosis, cyst formation, and adenosis, often producing cyclical breast pain. It is not cancer; only certain proliferative variants carry a slightly elevated risk of subsequent carcinoma.'),
('50d4f07c-b894-5399-a458-9e9c73246e46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for germ cell tumors (seminoma)?', '["D2-40","OCT4","SALL4","c-KIT","PLAP"]'::jsonb, 'PLAP', 'Seminoma characteristically expresses PLAP (placental alkaline phosphatase) in addition to OCT4 and SALL4, making PLAP the classic immunohistochemical marker associated with this germ cell tumor. The other markers are not the conventional designation for seminoma.'),
('95e82b41-cc76-53bf-aa31-ad64619ca02e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for yolk sac tumor?', '["GATA3 resulting in decreased functional activity","PLAP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AFP (alpha-fetoprotein)","OCT4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","hCG leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AFP (alpha-fetoprotein)', 'Yolk sac (endodermal sinus) tumors characteristically secrete alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), which is detectable by immunohistochemistry and in serum. PLAP and OCT4 mark seminoma and embryonal carcinoma, hCG marks choriocarcinoma, and GATA3 marks urothelial or Müllerian carcinoma.'),
('2351e746-a4ae-5a17-9885-f03a66b2ac2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for choriocarcinoma?', '["PLAP resulting in decreased functional activity","AFP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)","Inhibin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","OCT4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin)', 'Choriocarcinoma is formed of syncytiotrophoblasts and cytotrophoblasts that produce hCG, so this is its characteristic tumor marker. AFP marks yolk sac tumors, and OCT4 and inhibin do not define choriocarcinoma.'),
('fda34902-209e-5ee0-b59c-b64c821a519a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the marker for embryonal carcinoma?', '["SOX2","OCT4","SALL4","CD30","PLAP"]'::jsonb, 'OCT4', 'Embryonal carcinoma expresses OCT4, a pluripotency transcription factor that it shares with seminoma, along with CD30. Among the listed markers, OCT4 is the established marker used to identify embryonal carcinoma.'),
('98c96358-01fe-5f0f-8ce3-02ca01b79953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the rete testis germ cell tumor?', '["Teratoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Seminoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sertoli cell tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Leydig cell tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A rare tumor called adenocarcinoma of the rete testis"]'::jsonb, 'A rare tumor called adenocarcinoma of the rete testis', 'Neoplasms arising in the rete testis are rare and take the form of adenocarcinoma of the rete testis, which is not of germ cell origin. Seminoma and teratoma are germ cell tumors, while Sertoli and Leydig cell tumors are sex cord-stromal neoplasms.'),
('3f971da6-80dc-5145-963d-f54b6a7d4b76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common sex cord-stromal tumor of the testis?', '["Leydig cell tumor","Granulosa cell tumor","Fibroma","Sertoli cell tumor","Thecoma"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cell tumor', 'Leydig cell tumor is the most common sex cord-stromal tumor of the testis and may secrete androgens or, less often, estrogens. Sertoli cell tumor is less frequent, and granulosa cell tumor, fibroma, and thecoma are primarily ovarian neoplasms.'),
('63fbbcc2-5ffe-5dce-86a3-36c934242b7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most common ovarian sex cord-stromal tumor?', '["Granulosa cell tumor","Sertoli-Leydig cell tumor","Thecoma","Gynandroblastoma","Fibroma"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroma', 'Fibroma is the most common ovarian sex cord-stromal tumor and may be complicated by ascites and pleural effusion (Meigs syndrome). Granulosa cell tumor is the most common hormonally active stromal tumor but is not the most frequent overall.'),
('2fb31e0b-c459-5a4c-8c7b-f04653366678'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is Meigs syndrome?', '["Ovarian cancer with metastasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Polycystic ovary syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovarian fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion","Ovarian torsion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endometriosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian fibroma with ascites and pleural effusion', 'Meigs syndrome is the triad of an ovarian fibroma with ascites and a pleural effusion, and the fluid resolves after surgical removal of the tumor. It is a specific association of a benign tumor and is not metastatic ovarian cancer, torsion, or endometriosis.'),
('40838afc-255d-5c31-96d6-fe5a13580178'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)?', '["Ovarian hyperstimulation","Simple ovarian cysts","Hyperandrogenism","anovulation","and polycystic ovaries on ultrasound"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperandrogenism', 'PCOS is characterized by hyperandrogenism combined with oligo-anovulation and polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound, and hyperandrogenism is the core endocrine feature. It is not simple ovarian cysts or the iatrogenic ovarian hyperstimulation of fertility treatment.'),
('a75c0771-9586-55ef-82d6-ea38da6ac9da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the histology of the PCOS ovary?', '["hyperplastic theca interna","Corpora lutea","Normal ovarian tissue","Thickened capsule","multiple follicular cysts"]'::jsonb, 'Thickened capsule', 'The PCOS ovary shows a thickened fibrous tunica albuginea over a subcapsular rim of multiple follicular cysts with hyperplastic, luteinized theca interna cells. Thickening of the ovarian capsule is a classic histologic feature of PCOS.'),
('e362d936-dbc4-52ce-91bb-25a3365a5a18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the normal number of nephrons in each kidney?', '["About 1 million","About 100","000","None of the above","About 500"]'::jsonb, 'About 1 million', 'Each human kidney normally contains about 1 million nephrons, a number fixed at birth that gradually declines with aging or disease. The other figures are far too low to represent normal nephron number.'),
('9aa97e50-76c9-5eb3-aa98-b247160730fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the GFR in mL/min/1.73 m²?', '["50-60 mL/min","130-150 mL/min","30-40 mL/min",">150 mL/min","90-120 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '90-120 mL/min', 'Normal glomerular filtration rate in adults is about 90-120 mL/min/1.73 m², the reference range used to classify chronic kidney disease stages. Lower values indicate impaired filtration, while 130-150 mL/min represents hyperfiltration.'),
('fe4bf9f1-6762-52f7-96a5-7d2f8e4f183e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the formula for estimated GFR (eGFR)?', '["Creatinine alone resulting in decreased functional activity","CKD-EPI or MDRD equations using serum creatinine","sex mediated by specific receptor activation","age without affecting the primary tissue pathway","and race leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CKD-EPI or MDRD equations using serum creatinine', 'eGFR is estimated from serum creatinine using the CKD-EPI or MDRD equations, which incorporate age, sex, and race in their calculation. Serum creatinine by itself is not a formula, as its level is influenced by muscle mass.'),
('b0225fac-a8f4-5ba7-beec-d8d240318173'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is cystatin C?', '["A vitamin resulting in decreased functional activity","A hormone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An electrolyte mediated by specific receptor activation","An alternative filtration marker (low molecular weight protein) not affected by muscle mass","A clotting factor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An alternative filtration marker (low molecular weight protein) not affected by muscle mass', 'Cystatin C is a low-molecular-weight cysteine protease inhibitor that is freely filtered by the glomerulus and serves as a filtration marker whose level is independent of muscle mass. Unlike serum creatinine, it is not influenced by lean body mass, making it useful when muscle mass is abnormal.'),
('d109942a-4550-536f-9537-556706b059ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the normal protein excretion per day?', '["<300 mg/day","<50 mg/day","<3.5 g/day","<1 g/day","<150 mg/day"]'::jsonb, '<150 mg/day', 'Normal urinary protein excretion is less than 150 mg/day; values above this constitute proteinuria. Nephrotic-range proteinuria is much higher (over 3.5 g/day), so the other figures are either above the normal range or too low.'),
('ac5e7850-8965-5573-b96f-7e8513597c65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is Tamm-Horsfall protein?', '["A glycoprotein (uromodulin) secreted by the thick ascending limb","A clotting factor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hormone mediated by specific receptor activation","An immunoglobulin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","forming hyaline casts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A glycoprotein (uromodulin) secreted by the thick ascending limb', 'Tamm-Horsfall protein, also called uromodulin, is a glycoprotein secreted by cells of the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and serves as the matrix on which urinary casts, including hyaline casts, form. It is not a hormone, immunoglobulin, or clotting factor.'),
('62c5f27f-38e8-5291-b135-e988ed8fe92b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are the types of urinary casts?', '["granular","RBC","WBC","epithelial","Hyaline"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaline', 'Urinary casts are classified by their content as hyaline, granular, red blood cell, white blood cell, or epithelial casts. Hyaline casts, composed mainly of Tamm-Horsfall protein, are the most common type and occur in normal concentrated urine.'),
('682697a7-d86c-550e-9670-9a8080578df0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What do RBC casts indicate?', '["Dehydration resulting in decreased functional activity","Tubular injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glomerular injury (glomerulonephritis)","Kidney stones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urinary tract infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular injury (glomerulonephritis)', 'Red blood cell casts form when blood cells enter the tubules, signaling glomerular injury such as glomerulonephritis. Tubular injury more often yields granular or epithelial casts, while WBC casts suggest infection or interstitial nephritis.'),
('d6dd09a2-ff92-5b29-b630-5749dc2e58f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are broad casts?', '["Casts from the prostate","Benign casts","Normal casts","indicating chronic kidney disease","Casts formed in dilated collecting ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Casts formed in dilated collecting ducts', 'Broad casts form in widely dilated collecting ducts and are accordingly known as renal failure casts, appearing in chronic kidney disease. They are neither normal nor benign and do not originate from the prostate.'),
('8b33ac56-e068-59f7-a170-6f3c16d33f98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the juxtaglomerular apparatus''s role in blood pressure regulation?', '["Senses distal tubule NaCl and releases renin via the renin-angiotensin system","Secretes ADH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Directly constricts blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Regulates heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secretes aldosterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Senses distal tubule NaCl and releases renin via the renin-angiotensin system', 'The macula densa of the juxtaglomerular apparatus senses distal tubular NaCl concentration and, when salt delivery is low, triggers renin release from juxtaglomerular cells, activating the renin-angiotensin system. It does not secrete ADH or aldosterone and does not directly constrict vessels.'),
('8e6a51ac-536a-5d6c-ae5d-6f194f1f9a29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the kidney in acid-base balance?', '["Reabsorbs phosphate resulting in decreased functional activity","Reabsorbs bicarbonate only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretes bicarbonate only mediated by specific receptor activation","Reabsorbs filtered bicarbonate and secretes H+ via intercalated cells","Reabsorbs or secretes ammonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reabsorbs filtered bicarbonate and secretes H+ via intercalated cells', 'The kidney preserves acid-base balance by reabsorbing filtered bicarbonate, mainly in the proximal tubule, and by secreting H+ through type A intercalated cells of the collecting duct, which also generates new bicarbonate. No single reabsorption-only or secretion-only route accomplishes this.'),
('837b091e-acfa-50bd-be2c-a813dc1ecf20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the renal ammonia mechanism?', '["role in acid-base balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Ammonia reabsorption only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ammonia secretion only mediated by specific receptor activation","Glutamine metabolism in PCT produces NH4+ for H+ excretion and new bicarbonate generation","Direct ammonia filtration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamine metabolism in PCT produces NH4+ for H+ excretion and new bicarbonate generation', 'In acidosis, proximal tubular cells metabolize glutamine to generate ammonium (NH4+), which is excreted along with H+, while new bicarbonate is produced and added to the blood. This pathway is the kidney''s adaptive mechanism for excreting acid and regenerating buffer, not a simple reabsorption, secretion, or filtration process.'),
('6a4c0a6b-911e-5389-b95f-7d949a6d21df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the most important buffering system in urine?', '["Hemoglobin buffer resulting in decreased functional activity","Protein buffer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bicarbonate buffer mediated by specific receptor activation","Phosphate buffer system (HPO42-/H2PO4-)","Ammonia buffer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphate buffer system (HPO42-/H2PO4-)', 'The phosphate buffer pair (HPO4 2-/H2PO4-) is the most important buffer in urine because phosphate is concentrated in the tubules and its pKa lies within the urinary pH range. The bicarbonate system dominates in blood and extracellular fluid rather than in urine.'),
('edc90f2a-d526-5c54-8b05-a14e0c9dc645'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the renal threshold for glucose?', '["100 mg/dL resulting in decreased functional activity","About 180-200 mg/dL blood glucose","250 mg/dL mediated by specific receptor activation","150 mg/dL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","300 mg/dL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 180-200 mg/dL blood glucose', 'Once the plasma glucose exceeds the transport maximum of the proximal tubular glucose carriers, roughly 180-200 mg/dL, glucose spills into the urine, defining the renal threshold. Below this level the filtered glucose is normally reabsorbed completely.'),
('28407b43-14fa-57a0-b58c-41976d7ade72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is renal glycosuria?', '["Glucosuria with normal blood glucose due to defective tubular glucose reabsorption","Diabetes mellitus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal glucose excretion mediated by specific receptor activation","High blood sugar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucosuria with normal blood glucose due to defective tubular glucose reabsorption', 'Renal glycosuria is glucosuria occurring at a normal plasma glucose level because of a lowered renal transport maximum for glucose, reflecting defective tubular reabsorption. It differs from diabetes mellitus, in which hyperglycemia overwhelms the transport maximum.'),
('faa79cc9-add3-58ce-b60b-9109f1bde4ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the function of the pelvic splanchnic nerves?', '["Somatic innervation resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor innervation of leg muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic innervation of bladder and genitalia (S2-S4)","Sensory innervation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic innervation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic innervation of bladder and genitalia (S2-S4)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic fibers from the S2-S4 spinal cord to the bladder and genitalia, providing motor supply to the detrusor muscle and erectile tissues. They are neither somatic, purely sensory, nor sympathetic.'),
('86272707-19e9-5804-85da-51c3e52cb614'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the micturition reflex?', '["The kidney filtration reflex","The spinal reflex that controls bladder emptying","The reflex to hold urine","The hormonal reflex","The defecation reflex"]'::jsonb, 'The spinal reflex that controls bladder emptying', 'The micturition reflex is a spinal reflex that coordinates detrusor contraction with relaxation of the internal sphincter to empty the bladder, normally under voluntary control from higher centers. It is not a filtration, retention, hormonal, or defecation reflex.'),
('282c4fb7-99e8-562b-a35c-1ff160c97173'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the volume at which the first urge to void occurs?', '["About 150-200 mL","50 mL","500-600 mL","800-1000 mL","300-400 mL"]'::jsonb, 'About 150-200 mL', 'The first sensation of bladder filling typically appears at about 150-200 mL, with a strong desire to void developing near 300-400 mL. Volumes of 500-600 mL or greater correspond to bladder fullness rather than the first urge.'),
('1e7e4f91-95b3-5cc7-a436-2837c85e7dd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What is the maximum bladder capacity?', '["300 mL resulting in decreased functional activity","800 mL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Approximately 500-600 mL","1000 mL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","200 mL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 500-600 mL', 'The maximum capacity of the adult urinary bladder is about 500-600 mL, though chronic retention can allow greater volumes. The first urge to void arises much earlier, at approximately 150-200 mL.'),
('1ed949b5-e816-5fdf-94ed-6e1cfa229357'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Which option best describes umbrella cells?', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells are the large, dome-shaped superficial cells of the transitional epithelium (urothelium); they flatten as the bladder distends and contribute to the impermeability barrier. They are not podocytes, mesangial cells, or collecting duct cells.'),
('1b46e413-1f85-564c-a03c-1702ce8d3a76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are umbrella cells? Which option is most accurate?', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells are the large surface cells of the transitional epithelium lining the urinary tract; they stretch and flatten as the bladder fills, forming part of the permeability barrier. The other options are cells of the glomerulus or collecting duct, not the urothelium.'),
('b763972c-48cf-5aa8-9bd1-c6b1a98be041'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Urinary Reproductive System: what are umbrella cells?', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells constitute the large superficial layer of the transitional epithelium of the bladder and ureter, flattening when the lumen distends to preserve a tight barrier against urine. Glomerular podocytes, mesangial cells, and collecting duct cells are anatomically and functionally distinct.'),
('a42567c8-da35-5572-a4c0-832f0e03198d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'For Urinary Reproductive System, identify the correct response: what are umbrella cells?', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells are the prominent superficial cells of the transitional epithelium lining the urinary tract, where they help form an impermeable barrier and change shape with bladder distension. Podocytes, mesangial cells, and collecting duct cells are unrelated cell types.'),
('71b605a8-022d-5de9-9c65-fb825e329df5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'What are umbrella cells? Select the best answer.', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells are the large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium that cap the urothelium and flatten as the bladder expands, sealing the surface against urine. The other choices name glomerular or tubular cells rather than urothelial cells.'),
('8e15ab15-1880-5edf-9765-7cf39af7c942'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Which response correctly addresses this Urinary Reproductive System item: what are umbrella cells?', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells are the large flattened superficial cells of the transitional epithelium lining the urinary bladder and ureter, distinctive for adapting shape with changes in distension. Cells of the glomerulus, mesangium, or collecting duct are not umbrella cells.'),
('ca357e38-55f8-56ec-92d5-afc5ad0087a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Which option best defines the function of the urothelial barrier?', '["Produces urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorbs electrolytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretes mucus mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevents urine from leaking into underlying tissues","Allows water absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents urine from leaking into underlying tissues', 'The urothelium, with its umbrella cell layer and tight junctions, forms a permeability barrier that prevents concentrated urine from leaking into the underlying connective tissue and vasculature. Producing urine, absorbing water, and secreting mucus are not functions of the urothelial barrier.'),
('3c287c56-a94c-54c6-9937-899b3198230c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'In Urinary and reproductive histology, which statement best fits the following fact: what type of epithelium lines the urinary bladder?', '["Stratified squamous","Simple squamous","Simple columnar","Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium', 'The urinary bladder is lined by transitional epithelium (urothelium), a stratified epithelium that stretches as the bladder fills and is unique to the urinary tract. Squamous, simple, and pseudostratified columnar epithelia do not line the bladder.'),
('f24b11a8-8899-5a0e-926f-acc6fee147f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Regarding Urinary and reproductive histology, what is the correct answer to: how many layers does the transitional epithelium have when the bladder is empty?', '["8-10 layers","4-5 layers","2-3 layers","2 layers","1 layer"]'::jsonb, '2-3 layers', 'When the bladder is empty, the transitional epithelium is relatively thin, commonly cited as 2-3 layers, and it thins further as the bladder distends while the surface umbrella cells flatten. The larger layer counts overestimate the resting urothelium.'),
('6355b789-863f-53ee-8582-b70d3b4badb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'For Urinary and reproductive histology, which option is most appropriate: what are umbrella cells?', '["Cells of the collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Podocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cap cells of the glomerulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium', 'Umbrella cells are the large superficial cells of the transitional epithelium that line the urinary passages; they flatten with distension and maintain a barrier against urine. Podocytes, mesangial cells, and collecting duct cells are all distinct cell populations.'),
('69349d51-61dc-5498-9076-be7489908e9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Which answer is correct in Urinary and reproductive histology when asked: what is the function of the urothelial barrier?', '["Produces urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorbs electrolytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretes mucus mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevents urine from leaking into underlying tissues","Allows water absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents urine from leaking into underlying tissues', 'The urothelial barrier, formed by the umbrella cells and tight junctions of the transitional epithelium, prevents urine and its solutes from leaking into the underlying tissues where they could cause irritation or be reabsorbed. It does not produce urine, absorb water, or secrete mucus.'),
('fa4d94b9-60f3-50fb-a014-3ddce95fee9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'In Urinary and reproductive histology, the best response to ''What is the lamina propria of the urinary bladder'' is:', '["The serosal layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The epithelial layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose connective tissue beneath the epithelium","The muscle layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The submucosal layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose connective tissue beneath the epithelium', 'The lamina propria of the urinary bladder is the layer of loose connective tissue lying beneath the transitional epithelium; the bladder lacks a true submucosa distinct from it. It is not the muscle, serosal, or epithelial layer.'),
('20aad011-6da4-5e91-a6ba-589681da97cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'During revision of Urinary and reproductive histology, which of the following is correct: what is the detrusor muscle?', '["The connective tissue","The cardiac muscle of the bladder","The skeletal muscle of the bladder","The epithelial layer","The smooth muscle of the bladder wall"]'::jsonb, 'The smooth muscle of the bladder wall', 'The detrusor is the smooth muscle layer of the bladder wall; its contraction expels urine and it is controlled autonomically, unlike skeletal or cardiac muscle. It is not connective tissue or the epithelial lining.'),
('bd2215d0-8358-57b1-8777-2ac7723f6a40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'In Urinary and reproductive histology, which statement best fits the following fact: what are the three layers of the detrusor?', '["Only longitudinal","outer longitudinal","middle circular","Only circular","Inner longitudinal"]'::jsonb, 'Inner longitudinal', 'The detrusor is composed of three smooth muscle layers: an inner longitudinal, a middle circular, and an outer longitudinal layer, which interlace so that their boundaries are indistinct. The inner longitudinal is the innermost of these three layers.'),
('05386c3e-3dff-5430-9462-a0cdca0e81ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Regarding Urinary and reproductive histology, what is the correct answer to: what is the trigone of the bladder?', '["The dome of the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A triangular area between the two ureteric orifices and the urethral opening","The bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The bladder base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular area between the two ureteric orifices and the urethral opening', 'The trigone is the smooth triangular area on the bladder base bounded by the two ureteric orifices above and the internal urethral opening below; its mucosa lacks a submucosa and is firmly bound to the muscle. It is not the dome, neck, or general posterior wall.'),
('588b2747-ac8d-5366-a19e-3c1ae7493938'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'For Urinary and reproductive histology, which option is most appropriate: what is the histological difference of the trigone?', '["more firmly attached epithelium derived from mesoderm","Smoother","Thicker muscle","More layers of epithelium","epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Smoother', 'The trigone appears smoother than the rest of the bladder because its mucosa is firmly attached to the underlying muscle and shows no rugae, in part reflecting the mesodermal origin of its epithelium. This smooth, flattened surface is the distinctive histologic feature of the trigone.'),
('f26eceae-5629-5bcc-816d-46ba77f450f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 1)', 'Special Histology - Urinary Reproductive System', 'Which answer is correct in Urinary and reproductive histology when asked: what type of epithelium lines the ureter?', '["Simple squamous","Transitional epithelium","Stratified squamous","Simple columnar","Pseudostratified columnar"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium', 'The ureter is lined by transitional epithelium (urothelium), the same stretchable epithelium that lines the renal pelvis and bladder, permitting accommodation of the urine bolus. Squamous and columnar epithelia are not found lining the ureter.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('50bde29e-75f7-5e7d-837b-a221f49d113f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The arm extends from which joints?', '["Shoulder to elbow","Elbow to wrist","Wrist to fingers","Shoulder to wrist","Elbow to shoulder"]'::jsonb, 'Shoulder to elbow', 'The anatomical arm (brachium) is the segment between the shoulder and elbow joints. The forearm extends from elbow to wrist and the hand from wrist to fingers.'),
('ee487646-2e38-5de9-8e90-b7c29efbc598'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor compartment of arm is innervated by which nerve?', '["Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7) supplies all three muscles of the anterior (flexor) compartment of the arm: coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, and brachialis. The other nerves innervate the extensor compartment or the forearm and shoulder region.'),
('f6f52c9d-0e5b-5af9-a6aa-599ffcb44bd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor compartment of arm is innervated by which nerve?', '["Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous","Median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'The radial nerve supplies the posterior (extensor) compartment of the arm, namely triceps brachii and anconeus. The other listed nerves do not supply this compartment.'),
('c767cb68-df8e-5610-a43d-c58422219e71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachial artery is a continuation of which artery?', '["Ulnar artery","Axillary artery","Radial artery","Interosseous artery","Subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary artery', 'The axillary artery becomes the brachial artery at the lower border of teres major. It then runs in the arm and divides into the radial and ulnar arteries in the cubital fossa.'),
('b1030904-bbb2-57fe-b016-117b0dde0cab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachial artery terminates by dividing into which arteries?', '["Median and ulnar","Anterior and posterior interosseous","Radial and interosseous","Radial and ulnar arteries","Ulnar and interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Radial and ulnar arteries', 'At the level of the radial neck in the cubital fossa, the brachial artery divides into its terminal branches, the radial and ulnar arteries. The interosseous arteries arise later from the ulnar artery.'),
('6a41fd76-ee4e-5a7b-a52e-2a28bf150f9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The profunda brachii artery is a branch of which artery?', '["Radial artery","Brachial artery","Axillary artery","Subclavian artery","Ulnar artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery', 'The profunda brachii is the main branch of the brachial artery, arising high in the arm and accompanying the radial nerve in the spiral groove. The radial and ulnar arteries arise more distally than this branch.'),
('bab78fe1-cf72-5b31-8431-55536ad12ff5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The profunda brachii artery accompanies which nerve?', '["Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Axillary nerve","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'The profunda brachii artery runs with the radial nerve through the spiral (radial) groove, supplying the posterior compartment of the arm. The other nerves pass in different compartments or regions.'),
('d2159b59-9c91-512d-b0c9-00d8727f42ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve lies in the spiral groove of which bone?', '["Clavicle","Humerus","Radius","Scapula","Ulna"]'::jsonb, 'Humerus', 'The radial nerve winds around the posterior surface of the humerus in the radial (spiral) groove. Injury here, as after a midshaft humeral fracture, produces wrist drop.'),
('9c2da58c-93a4-52cc-aa5e-010eb261f13a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The medial intermuscular septum separates:', '["Flexor and extensor compartments of arm","Biceps and triceps","Brachialis and triceps","Anterior and posterior forearm","None"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor and extensor compartments of arm', 'The medial intermuscular septum is an extension of the deep fascia attached to the medial supracondylar ridge, separating the anterior (flexor) from the posterior (extensor) compartment of the arm. It does not divide forearm compartments.'),
('380fc3f2-e46c-57e8-95c1-9c6caacf133a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lateral intermuscular septum separates:', '["Flexor and extensor compartments of arm","Anterior and posterior forearm","None","Wrist compartments","Extensor and flexor forearm"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor and extensor compartments of arm', 'Like its medial counterpart, the lateral intermuscular septum divides the anterior (flexor) from the posterior (extensor) compartment of the arm. The forearm and wrist compartments are separated by other fascial structures.'),
('746a287f-7386-5e51-bc06-67dfb380ea0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The cubital fossa is bounded superiorly by which line?', '["Elbow crease resulting in decreased functional activity","Imaginary line between medial and lateral epicondyles","Articular line mediated by specific receptor activation","Neck of radius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Imaginary line between medial and lateral epicondyles', 'The superior boundary of the cubital fossa is an imaginary line joining the medial and lateral epicondyles of the humerus. It is bounded laterally by brachioradialis, medially by pronator teres, and its floor is brachialis and supinator.'),
('e810120e-d090-52e6-af7e-69d90e012d11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The cubital fossa is bounded medially by which muscle?', '["Pronator teres","Supinator","Brachioradialis","Triceps","Brachialis"]'::jsonb, 'Pronator teres', 'The medial boundary of the cubital fossa is the pronator teres muscle. Brachioradialis forms its lateral boundary, while supinator is in the floor rather than the boundary.'),
('3d3cf07a-44a0-53bb-88bb-85cfbe2ebd09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The cubital fossa is bounded laterally by which muscle?', '["Triceps","Supinator","Brachialis","Brachioradialis","Pronator teres"]'::jsonb, 'Brachioradialis', 'The lateral boundary of the cubital fossa is formed by the brachioradialis muscle. Pronator teres borders it medially, and brachialis and supinator contribute to its floor.'),
('4521c26e-44ac-584d-bc49-fc6315f59852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The floor of cubital fossa is formed by:', '["Supinator only","Triceps only","Brachialis and supinator","Brachialis only","Biceps"]'::jsonb, 'Brachialis and supinator', 'The floor of the cubital fossa is formed by the brachialis medially and the supinator laterally, with the brachialis resting partly on the joint capsule. The biceps tendon and triceps do not form the floor.'),
('078fa6b0-dd56-51b9-9747-238b9226ee23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The roof of cubital fossa contains all except:', '["Median cubital vein","Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm","Brachial artery","Lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm","Bicipital aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery', 'The roof of the cubital fossa is formed by skin, superficial fascia, the bicipital aponeurosis, the median cubital vein, and the medial and lateral cutaneous nerves of the forearm. The brachial artery is a deep content of the fossa, not part of its roof.'),
('e3ad5538-f894-5424-86f8-56b4c8bab8d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The contents of cubital fossa from medial to lateral are:', '["brachial artery","Median nerve","None of the above","biceps tendon","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'From medial to lateral, the cubital fossa contains the median nerve, brachial artery (with its venae comitantes), and biceps tendon; the median nerve is the most medial content. The radial nerve lies deep under the brachioradialis.'),
('39df274e-3beb-5b35-9ee4-00f0c982f503'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachial artery in cubital fossa lies:', '["Medial to median nerve","Lateral to biceps tendon","Superficial to bicipital aponeurosis","None","Deep to bicipital aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to bicipital aponeurosis', 'In the cubital fossa the brachial artery lies deep to the bicipital aponeurosis, which protects it during venipuncture of the median cubital vein. The artery is between the median nerve medially and the biceps tendon laterally.'),
('0b9d7405-b5ff-5128-990c-0f3e9c2e01d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The bicipital aponeurosis is derived from the tendon of:', '["Brachialis","Biceps brachii","Supinator","Brachioradialis","Triceps"]'::jsonb, 'Biceps brachii', 'The bicipital aponeurosis is a broad flat expansion of the tendon of biceps brachii that passes medially across the cubital fossa to blend with the deep fascia. It protects the brachial artery and separates it from the median cubital vein.'),
('4bbe0c8c-043a-5246-b9b0-03dcc5ab0a7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The bicipital aponeurosis protects which structure in cubital fossa?', '["Biceps tendon","Ulnar nerve","Brachial artery","Median nerve","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery', 'The bicipital aponeurosis passes over the cubital fossa and shields the brachial artery, which lies deep to it. It also separates the artery from the median cubital vein used for venipuncture.'),
('31ffe26b-1d77-5cec-8c8a-8e9aab26cf4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve in cubital fossa lies:', '["Lateral to brachial artery","Deep to brachial artery","None","Superficial to bicipital aponeurosis","Medial to brachial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to brachial artery', 'In the cubital fossa the median nerve lies medial to the brachial artery, which itself lies medial to the biceps tendon. This medial-to-lateral order (nerve, artery, tendon) is a key landmark.'),
('9f4cc188-bc0a-5651-9e38-5d97bf166a9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The biceps tendon in cubital fossa lies:', '["None","Deep to brachial artery","Superficial to brachial artery","Lateral to brachial artery","Medial to brachial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to brachial artery', 'The biceps tendon is the most lateral of the three main cubital fossa contents, so it lies lateral to the brachial artery. The median nerve is medial to the artery.'),
('e927e997-e42f-50d7-9b12-35e5c9d48918'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve bifurcates into superficial and deep branches:', '["In the arm","At the wrist","In the axilla","At the level of lateral epicondyle","At the elbow crease"]'::jsonb, 'At the level of lateral epicondyle', 'The radial nerve divides into superficial and deep (posterior interosseous) branches slightly proximal to or at the level of the lateral epicondyle, just anterior to it. It does not divide in the axilla, arm, or at the wrist.'),
('2ab98d00-bf68-5a2f-951d-a54de7973d50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of radial) passes through:', '["Abductor pollicis longus","Pronator teres","Extensor carpi radialis brevis","Supinator muscle (Arcade of Frohse)","None"]'::jsonb, 'Supinator muscle (Arcade of Frohse)', 'The deep branch of the radial nerve (posterior interosseous nerve) pierces the supinator muscle by passing between its two heads through the Arcade of Frohse, which is a common site of nerve entrapment (posterior interosseous syndrome).'),
('3e70aa98-4e85-5806-b283-1ff3f0867b01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior interosseous nerve supplies all except:', '["Abductor pollicis longus","Extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)","Extensor digitorum","Extensor carpi ulnaris","Extensor pollicis longus"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)', 'The posterior interosseous nerve supplies all muscles of the extensor compartment of the forearm except the extensor carpi radialis longus, which is supplied directly by the radial nerve before its bifurcation. ECRB often receives a shared nerve branch proximal to the arcade.'),
('2564ab04-d118-5b1d-b2e7-231366664a0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve in the forearm supplies all except:', '["Pronator teres","Flexor digitorum superficialis","Flexor digitorum profundus (radial half)","Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor carpi ulnaris', 'The median nerve supplies the pronator teres, flexor digitorum superficialis, palmaris longus, and the radial half of flexor digitorum profundus. The flexor carpi ulnaris is innervated by the ulnar nerve, not the median.'),
('5fa1e92e-ac13-5bb3-a184-0691c7bc78e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve in the forearm supplies:', '["Flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus","Palmaris longus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flexor digitorum superficialis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pronator teres without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexor digitorum profundus (lateral half) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus', 'In the forearm, the ulnar nerve supplies only the flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. The lateral half of FDP and all the other listed muscles are median nerve territory.'),
('564c39c5-075a-5d89-8c18-ae5d06529372'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve enters the forearm between which two heads of pronator teres?', '["Radial and ulnar heads","None","Both a and b","Humeral and ulnar heads","Superficial and deep heads"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar heads', 'The median nerve passes between the humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres to enter the forearm. Compression between these two heads can produce the anterior interosseous or pronator syndrome.'),
('6ebc244a-a826-5819-ab10-5c4b99b9bd9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve passes behind which bony point?', '["Lateral epicondyle","Olecranon","Medial epicondyle of humerus","Radial head","Capitulum"]'::jsonb, 'Medial epicondyle of humerus', 'The ulnar nerve runs behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus in the ulnar groove, where it is palpable and vulnerable to compression. It is the nerve that produces tingling over the medial forearm and hand when struck at the elbow (funny bone).'),
('495052e2-2796-5468-aa9f-87d3dfb52b7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve (superficial branch) runs deep to which muscle?', '["Extensor carpi radialis longus","Pronator teres","Flexor carpi radialis","Brachioradialis","Palmaris longus"]'::jsonb, 'Brachioradialis', 'The superficial branch of the radial nerve descends deep to the brachioradialis in the lateral forearm. It emerges from beneath the muscle on the dorsum of the wrist to become cutaneous over the radial side of the hand.'),
('66895acc-6850-5b3d-9507-80fafcc5a250'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve at the wrist lies between which two tendons?', '["PL and FCU resulting in decreased functional activity","Palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis","FCU and PL mediated by specific receptor activation","FCR and brachioradialis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FCR and FCU leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis', 'At the wrist the median nerve lies between the tendons of palmaris longus medially and flexor carpi radialis laterally, just proximal to the flexor retinaculum. This is the landmark for locating the nerve in the clinical examination.'),
('7c921633-6de4-5c05-b3e9-d0a4b8184578'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar artery enters the hand:', '["Through the carpal tunnel resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial to the flexor retinaculum (via Guyon''s canal)","Deep to flexor retinaculum mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the anatomical snuffbox without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial to the flexor retinaculum (via Guyon''s canal)', 'The ulnar artery enters the hand with the ulnar nerve through Guyon''s canal, which lies superficial (ulnar) to the flexor retinaculum. It does not pass through the carpal tunnel or the anatomical snuffbox.'),
('c29d7526-7cb5-5805-94c6-a809b2c3f803'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial artery enters the hand:', '["Through the carpal tunnel","Through Guyon''s canal","Superficial to flexor retinaculum","Through the anatomical snuffbox","None"]'::jsonb, 'Through the anatomical snuffbox', 'The radial artery crosses the floor of the anatomical snuffbox to reach the dorsum of the hand, then passes between the heads of the first dorsal interosseous to form the deep palmar arch. It does not enter through the carpal tunnel or Guyon''s canal.'),
('f914732f-8840-5dab-a442-ce6af0848353'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anatomical snuffbox is bounded laterally by:', '["Extensor pollicis longus resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensor indicis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extensor digiti minimi mediated by specific receptor activation","Extensor carpi radialis longus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus', 'The lateral boundary of the anatomical snuffbox is formed by the tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis. The tendon of extensor pollicis longus forms its medial boundary.'),
('46a27e10-9a95-5a7a-8d0d-c44f9d0918d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anatomical snuffbox is bounded medially by:', '["Extensor indicis","Extensor carpi radialis","Extensor pollicis brevis","Extensor pollicis longus tendon","Abductor pollicis longus"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor pollicis longus tendon', 'The medial boundary of the anatomical snuffbox is the tendon of extensor pollicis longus. The lateral boundary is formed by the tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis.'),
('66d5a03d-cea9-57a0-bb79-13f1f15fb63e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The floor of the anatomical snuffbox contains:', '["Basilic vein","Radial artery and scaphoid bone","Ulnar artery","Cephalic vein","Median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial artery and scaphoid bone', 'The floor of the anatomical snuffbox is formed by the scaphoid and trapezium, and the radial artery crosses it as it enters the hand, so the radial pulse is palpated over the scaphoid at this site. The ulnar artery and median nerve are not in this region.'),
('ab36e6d7-da02-59ae-b2e3-f7b0de219bfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The contents of the anatomical snuffbox include:', '["Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Radial artery and cephalic vein","Median nerve","Basilic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Radial artery and cephalic vein', 'The anatomical snuffbox contains the radial artery and the cephalic vein. The ulnar and median nerves and the basilic vein lie elsewhere in the limb and are not contents of this space.'),
('dab62bea-ff7f-5838-a41f-46c8116891c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor retinaculum attaches to:', '["hamate hook","Pisiform","trapezium","Pisiform only","scaphoid"]'::jsonb, 'Pisiform', 'The flexor retinaculum attaches to the pisiform on the ulnar side, along with the hook of the hamate, the scaphoid tubercle, and the ridge of the trapezium. Each of the bony options is therefore one of its attachment sites.'),
('fa33fd78-fb88-579b-adb2-cb20a91e4228'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The carpal tunnel contains all except:', '["Median nerve","Flexor carpi ulnaris tendon","Flexor digitorum profundus (4 tendons)","Flexor digitorum superficialis (4 tendons)","Flexor pollicis longus tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor carpi ulnaris tendon', 'The carpal tunnel contains the median nerve, the four FDS tendons, the four FDP tendons, and the FPL tendon. The flexor carpi ulnaris tendon inserts on the pisiform and passes superficial to the flexor retinaculum, outside the tunnel.'),
('0afabe11-53ef-5f23-9689-5a6e3be3e8c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The carpal tunnel syndrome involves compression of which nerve?', '["All","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous","Median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'Carpal tunnel syndrome is caused by compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel beneath the flexor retinaculum. The radial, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves do not traverse the tunnel.'),
('0214e90b-177d-57ac-b99b-10547c49a952'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Guyon''s canal (ulnar tunnel) contains:', '["Ulnar nerve only","Ulnar nerve and artery","Radial artery","Median nerve","Ulnar artery only"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve and artery', 'Guyon''s canal is formed by the flexor retinaculum and the pisohamate ligament and transmits the ulnar nerve together with the ulnar artery. It does not contain the median or radial neurovascular structures.'),
('75a0c3ae-ea28-5997-b52b-07771d48b5b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The superficial palmar arch is formed mainly by:', '["Radial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Interosseous artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ulnar artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Median artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ulnar artery (superficial branch) and superficial palmar branch of radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar artery (superficial branch) and superficial palmar branch of radial artery', 'The superficial palmar arch is the continuation of the superficial branch of the ulnar artery and is completed on the radial side by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery. It lies in the plane superficial to the long flexor tendons.'),
('39754228-4244-5cd3-86a1-a02bb53ac48c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The deep palmar arch is formed mainly by:', '["Ulnar artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Median artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial branch of radial artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Radial artery (deep branch) and deep palmar branch of ulnar artery","Interosseous artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radial artery (deep branch) and deep palmar branch of ulnar artery', 'The deep palmar arch is formed mainly by the terminal (deep) branch of the radial artery, completed on the ulnar side by the deep palmar branch of the ulnar artery. It lies deep to the flexor tendons, at the level of the metacarpal bases.'),
('ae93d469-b435-593b-ba23-53da3042f733'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The superficial palmar arch gives rise to which branches?', '["Princeps pollicis artery","Deep branches","Radialis indicis artery","Palmar metacarpal arteries","Common palmar digital arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Common palmar digital arteries', 'The superficial palmar arch gives rise to three common palmar digital arteries, which divide into the proper digital arteries for the fingers. The princeps pollicis and radialis indicis arteries are branches of the radial artery, and palmar metacarpal arteries arise from the deep arch.'),
('7518eca6-4a44-5034-ad0d-9026ce823013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The deep palmar arch gives rise to which branches?', '["Proper digital arteries","Palmar metacarpal arteries","Superficial branches","Perforating branches","Common palmar digital arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Palmar metacarpal arteries', 'The deep palmar arch gives rise to the palmar metacarpal arteries, which arise by perforating branches joining the common palmar digital arteries. The common palmar digital arteries, by contrast, arise from the superficial arch.'),
('d159307b-1c42-5a17-b377-bb3287ad887b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The recurrent branch of median nerve is at risk during:', '["Cubital tunnel release","None","Dupuytren''s contracture","Carpal tunnel release surgery","Trigger finger release"]'::jsonb, 'Carpal tunnel release surgery', 'The recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve is at risk during carpal tunnel release because it arises from the radial side of the nerve and has a variable course near the distal edge of the flexor retinaculum. Injury to it causes paralysis of the thenar muscles.'),
('315619ba-e9c8-59cf-bddc-e9ff31cf66cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve in the hand supplies all except:', '["Hypothenar muscles","Lumbricals 1 and 2","Adductor pollicis","Palmaris brevis","Interossei"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbricals 1 and 2', 'In the hand the ulnar nerve supplies the hypothenar muscles, all interossei, adductor pollicis, palmaris brevis, and lumbricals 3 and 4. Lumbricals 1 and 2 are innervated by the median nerve, not the ulnar.'),
('c8952fd7-e48e-5b36-88cd-654c37e14d11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve in the hand supplies:', '["Hypothenar muscles","Thenar muscles and lumbricals 1 and 2","Lumbricals 3 and 4","Interossei","Adductor pollicis"]'::jsonb, 'Thenar muscles and lumbricals 1 and 2', 'Through its recurrent motor branch, the median nerve supplies the thenar muscles (abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis, and superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis) and via its digital branches, lumbricals 1 and 2. The hypothenar muscles, interossei, and adductor pollicis are ulnar-supplied.'),
('cdd927f3-3243-54d8-b484-27f5a7e5e236'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve divides into superficial and deep branches:', '["At the elbow resulting in decreased functional activity","In the forearm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the wrist mediated by specific receptor activation","In the arm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the distal end of Guyon''s canal"]'::jsonb, 'At the distal end of Guyon''s canal', 'The ulnar nerve divides into its superficial and deep branches at the distal end of Guyon''s canal. The superficial branch supplies palmaris brevis and cutaneous branches, while the deep branch innervates most of the intrinsic hand muscles.'),
('4f379f94-c957-52e7-8548-aabaad413b29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The deep branch of ulnar nerve supplies:', '["Lumbricals 3 resulting in decreased functional activity","Interossei only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothenar only mediated by specific receptor activation","All intrinsic hand muscles supplied by ulnar nerve","4 only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic hand muscles supplied by ulnar nerve', 'The deep branch of the ulnar nerve supplies all the intrinsic hand muscles that are ulnar-innervated: the hypothenar muscles, all interossei, the medial two lumbricals, and adductor pollicis. Its options representing a single muscle group are therefore incomplete.'),
('97229728-5e40-50b7-a7d3-a96bdfbb7853'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve injury at the wrist causes:', '["Foot drop resulting in decreased functional activity","Wrist drop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Claw hand mediated by specific receptor activation","Dinner fork deformity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thenar atrophy and loss of opposition"]'::jsonb, 'Thenar atrophy and loss of opposition', 'Median nerve injury at the wrist paralyzes the thenar muscles, producing thenar atrophy and loss of thumb opposition, the classic simian hand. Wrist drop indicates radial nerve injury, ulnar nerve injury causes claw hand, and the other options are not median nerve signs.'),
('239a5070-56a1-5b13-a5ca-cb7cc0d1651f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve injury at the wrist causes:', '["Thenar atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Wrist drop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ape hand deformity mediated by specific receptor activation","Dinner fork without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Claw hand (mostly affecting ring and little fingers)"]'::jsonb, 'Claw hand (mostly affecting ring and little fingers)', 'Ulnar nerve injury at the wrist paralyzes the ulnar-innervated interossei and the medial two lumbricals, so the unopposed long flexors pull the metacarpophalangeal joints into flexion while the lost muscular action allows extension of the IP joints in ring and little fingers. The typical claw-hand deformity is therefore most prominent in the ring and little fingers.'),
('5343e468-addb-5d89-abab-14e876af24a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve injury in the spiral groove causes:', '["Thenar atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Claw hand accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothenar atrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Ape hand without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wrist drop (loss of extension of wrist and fingers)"]'::jsonb, 'Wrist drop (loss of extension of wrist and fingers)', 'The radial nerve at the spiral groove supplies the extensors of the wrist and fingers in the posterior forearm; their paralysis abolishes wrist and finger extension. Triceps is spared because its branches leave the nerve proximal to the groove.'),
('b08271bd-aba0-53bd-9884-00b42f5a3884'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior interosseous nerve (PIN) injury causes:', '["Loss of finger extension without wrist drop","Thenar atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nothing mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete wrist drop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Claw hand leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of finger extension without wrist drop', 'The PIN supplies the deep extensor muscles of the forearm, but extensor carpi radialis longus receives its branch from the radial nerve proximal to the PIN origin. Wrist extension therefore continues via ECRL, while extension of the metacarpophalangeal joints is lost.'),
('0ec73853-f4d2-5f8c-b317-10a0a52e7078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The triceps brachii has how many heads?', '["5","1","2","4","3"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Triceps brachii is composed of three heads: long, lateral, and medial. The long head arises from the scapula, while the lateral and medial heads arise from the humerus.'),
('e5a3902b-fd0f-5bee-9b02-c6d6205f6075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The long head of triceps arises from:', '["Acromion","Supraglenoid tubercle","Coracoid process","Glenoid cavity","Infraglenoid tubercle of scapula"]'::jsonb, 'Infraglenoid tubercle of scapula', 'The long head of triceps takes origin from the infraglenoid tubercle, situated just below the glenoid cavity. The supraglenoid tubercle, by contrast, gives origin to the long head of biceps.'),
('bc643509-4bcc-542b-941e-746a58c68511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lateral head of triceps arises from:', '["Latissimus dorsi tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper half of posterior shaft of humerus (above radial groove)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial surface of humerus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower half of posterior shaft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper half of posterior shaft of humerus (above radial groove)', 'The lateral head of triceps arises from the lateral surface of the humerus and the upper half of the posterior shaft above the radial groove. It is a powerful extensor of the elbow.'),
('81ce1685-3f9e-5391-82a4-642f25a974df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The medial head of triceps arises from:', '["Upper half of humerus resulting in decreased functional activity","Radius accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scapula mediated by specific receptor activation","Ulna without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower half of posterior shaft of humerus (below radial groove)"]'::jsonb, 'Lower half of posterior shaft of humerus (below radial groove)', 'The medial head arises from the posterior surface of the humerus below the radial groove and is overlapped by the lateral head. It lies deep to the radial nerve in the spiral groove.'),
('75c9d678-30dd-51eb-a8a0-596694fd56dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The triceps inserts into:', '["Radial tuberosity","Styloid process of ulna","Coronoid process","Olecranon process of ulna","None"]'::jsonb, 'Olecranon process of ulna', 'All three heads of triceps converge to insert on the olecranon process of the ulna. The radial tuberosity receives the tendon of biceps brachii instead.'),
('367dd03e-0fe5-5aff-a57b-0b81c14a3cb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The triceps is supplied by which nerve?', '["Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'Triceps brachii is innervated by the radial nerve through branches given off above the spiral groove. This explains the preservation of elbow extension in many radial nerve lesions of the humerus.'),
('5ad4ed29-37d8-542e-8f68-38124ad11424'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anconeus muscle originates from:', '["Medial epicondyle","Olecranon","Lateral epicondyle of humerus","Trochlea","Capitulum"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral epicondyle of humerus', 'Anconeus arises from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus and is continuous with the lateral border of triceps. It assists in extension and stabilizes the elbow.'),
('b00d7825-a631-53aa-9011-743b90ac7057'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anconeus inserts into:', '["Lateral side of olecranon and posterior ulna","Radial tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial side of olecranon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coronoid process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral side of olecranon and posterior ulna', 'The anconeus fans out to insert on the lateral surface of the olecranon and the adjacent posterior surface of the ulna. It tenses the joint capsule during extension.'),
('87c802b1-7b82-57a4-917d-b2860052fbfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The forearm is divided into which compartments?', '["Medial and lateral resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial and deep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proximal and distal mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior (flexor) and posterior (extensor)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (flexor) and posterior (extensor)', 'The radius, ulna, and interosseous membrane plus investing fascia divide the forearm into an anterior flexor-pronator compartment and a posterior extensor-supinator compartment.'),
('cfac0b4b-f9af-5e3c-8d4a-bc3ff58cc06d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The superficial layer of anterior forearm includes all except:', '["Palmaris longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Flexor digitorum profundus","Pronator teres","Flexor carpi radialis"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor digitorum profundus', 'The superficial anterior group holds pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, and flexor carpi ulnaris, all arising from the common flexor origin. Flexor digitorum profundus belongs to the deep layer, so it is the exception.'),
('16216df7-4ad1-545b-bc67-701849bcbd75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The intermediate layer of anterior forearm contains:', '["Flexor digitorum profundus","Palmaris longus","Flexor pollicis longus","Pronator quadratus","Flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS)"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS)', 'Fibrous digit profundus superficialis forms the entire intermediate layer, lying between the superficial and deep flexor groups. Its four tendons split to insert on the middle phalanges of the fingers.'),
('8427efa8-e4ce-5e30-b844-c0e06a8a950b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The deep layer of anterior forearm contains all except:', '["Pronator quadratus","Flexor digitorum superficialis","None","Flexor pollicis longus","Flexor digitorum profundus"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor digitorum superficialis', 'The deep flexor group is made of flexor digitorum profundus, flexor pollicis longus, and pronator quadratus. Flexor digitorum superficialis is the single muscle of the intermediate layer and is therefore excluded.'),
('c8a62afa-0a39-591c-aa7f-d1f8f55dd982'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The pronator teres has two heads:', '["Medial and lateral","Radial and ulnar","Humeral and ulnar","None","Superficial and deep"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar', 'Pronator teres comprises a humeral head from the medial epicondyle and a smaller ulnar head from the coronoid process. The median nerve passes between these two heads.'),
('d1a5700f-18b1-5e42-89b5-dc52cd346967'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve passes between:', '["Two heads of FDS resulting in decreased functional activity","Two heads of supinator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two heads of FCU leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres', 'In the cubital fossa the median nerve descends between the humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres. Hypertrophy or fibrosis here can entrap the nerve and cause pronator syndrome.'),
('dbbca6f2-444f-5476-b4a4-e4ad5726cd35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor carpi ulnaris has two heads:', '["Superficial and deep","Medial and lateral","None","Humeral and ulnar","Radial and ulnar"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar', 'Flexor carpi ulnaris arises from both a humeral head (medial epicondyle) and an ulnar head (olecranon and posterior ulna). The ulnar nerve passes between these heads to enter the forearm.'),
('b86fd478-f9bf-586d-a6ce-093302a9f842'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve passes between:', '["Two heads of pronator teres","Two heads of FDS","None","Humeral and ulnar heads of FCU","Two heads of supinator"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar heads of FCU', 'After running behind the medial epicondyle, the ulnar nerve enters the forearm between the humeral (epicondylar) and ulnar (olecranon) heads of flexor carpi ulnaris. This is a well-recognized site of compression in cubital tunnel syndrome.'),
('cd79d0c5-3409-5537-9bf7-7be809a4f7fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor digitorum superficialis has which heads?', '["None","Radial and ulnar","Superficial and deep","Humeral and ulnar","Humeroulnar and radial"]'::jsonb, 'Humeroulnar and radial', 'Flexor digitorum superficialis takes origin from the medial epicondyle, ulnar collateral ligament, and coronoid process as the humeroulnar head, plus a separate radial head from the radius. The median nerve passes beneath the tendinous arch joining the two heads.'),
('321da3b3-5e91-59e0-8672-5ec07914bb57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve passes:', '["Lateral to FDS resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial to FDS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep to the humeroulnar head of FDS (between the two heads)","Superficial to FDS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through FDS tendon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to the humeroulnar head of FDS (between the two heads)', 'Distal to pronator teres, the median nerve travels deep to the humeroulnar head of flexor digitorum superficialis. Compression at this fibrous arch can produce pronator or anterior interosseous symptoms.'),
('50254ce0-b69f-59ee-8cb9-68d0e1f2483d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor digitorum profundus has how many bellies?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 (radial and ulnar)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (radial and ulnar)', 'Flexor digitorum profundus is split into a radial belly (to the index and middle fingers) and an ulnar belly (to the ring and little fingers). The two bellies carry separate nerve supplies from the median and ulnar nerves.'),
('7b837213-7ddf-5314-bf0a-a9e8cd8a83b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lateral (radial) half of FDP (to index and middle) is supplied by:', '["Posterior interosseous resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulnar nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior interosseous nerve (from median)","Musculocutaneous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interosseous nerve (from median)', 'The radial half of flexor digitorum profundus to the index and middle fingers is innervated by the anterior interosseous nerve, a branch of the median nerve. This allows precise flexion of the distal phalanges of those two fingers.'),
('0f62c006-17a6-599e-bea2-ff11476a6071'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The medial (ulnar) half of FDP (to ring and little) is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Posterior interosseous","Anterior interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve', 'The ulnar half of flexor digitorum profundus to the ring and little fingers receives its innervation from the ulnar nerve. Loss of this input abolishes flexion at the distal interphalangeal joints of the ring and little fingers.'),
('ebaa4287-0200-50d6-a51c-a1cf25d8aa68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor pollicis longus is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior interosseous nerve (from median)","Musculocutaneous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior interosseous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interosseous nerve (from median)', 'Flexor pollicis longus is innervated by the anterior interosseous nerve from the median nerve. In anterior interosseous nerve syndrome the patient cannot flex the distal phalanx of the thumb, disrupting the ''O'' sign.'),
('310fbed0-8448-5d17-99af-9ddcee654b8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior compartment of forearm has how many layers?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 (superficial and deep)","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (superficial and deep)', 'The posterior (extensor) compartment of the forearm has a superficial layer of seven muscles and a deep layer of five muscles. Both layers are supplied by branches of the radial nerve.'),
('cf1a5131-4625-57ff-a2f8-c35e06cf2f90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The superficial layer of posterior forearm includes all except:', '["Extensor indicis","Brachioradialis","Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis","Extensor digitorum","Extensor carpi ulnaris"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor indicis', 'The superficial dorsal layer contains brachioradialis, extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis, extensor digitorum, and extensor carpi ulnaris. Extensor indicis belongs to the deep layer, so it is the exception listed.'),
('c38ccaf6-3709-5260-a630-61f3c840aa84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The deep layer of posterior forearm includes:', '["Abductor pollicis longus","Supinator only","extensor pollicis brevis","extensor indicis","extensor pollicis longus"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis longus', 'The deep posterior group comprises supinator, abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, extensor pollicis longus, and extensor indicis. Abductor pollicis longus arises from the radius, ulna, and interosseous membrane and helps abduct the thumb.'),
('87e535f9-b04e-58b6-8267-47671c8855c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachioradialis originates from:', '["Lateral supracondylar ridge of humerus","Medial epicondyle","Radial tuberosity","Coronoid process","Lateral epicondyle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral supracondylar ridge of humerus', 'Brachioradialis arises from the upper part of the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus. Although a muscle of the forearm, it flexes the elbow and is best at this action when the forearm is mid-pronated.'),
('ede15bf6-ba66-5ea1-bd49-59d81781e89d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachioradialis inserts into:', '["None","Dorsal surface of radius","Radial styloid process (base)","Radial tuberosity","Ulna styloid"]'::jsonb, 'Radial styloid process (base)', 'Brachioradialis inserts on the lateral surface of the base of the radial styloid process. It is the most distal of the superficial posterior muscles and is frequently used for tendon transfer procedures.'),
('e60fc9af-a2e8-5c96-9199-e717b380c4a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachioradialis acts as:', '["Supinator resulting in decreased functional activity","Pronator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A flexor of elbow (especially when forearm is midpronated)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extensor of elbow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A flexor of elbow (especially when forearm is midpronated)', 'Brachioradialis flexes the elbow and acts most efficiently when the forearm is in the mid-pronated position. In full pronation or supination it becomes a weaker elbow flexor rather than a rotator of the forearm.'),
('cd439be7-6e84-599a-8607-4dcc12e8a8a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL) originates from:', '["Radial shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral epicondyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ulna shaft mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial epicondyle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral supracondylar ridge of humerus (lower third)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral supracondylar ridge of humerus (lower third)', 'Extensor carpi radialis longus arises from the lower third of the lateral supracondylar ridge, immediately distal to the origin of brachioradialis. It passes down between these structures and the ECRB to extend and abduct the wrist.'),
('6c050efb-dbf4-5944-be5e-3d106da46c82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) originates from:', '["Lateral epicondyle of humerus (common extensor origin)","Medial epicondyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral supracondylar ridge mediated by specific receptor activation","Ulna without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral epicondyle of humerus (common extensor origin)', 'Extensor carpi radialis brevis arises from the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle. This origin is the site of attachment implicated in lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow).'),
('9a04108c-fe1b-565f-97b1-95202c44b964'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The common extensor origin is at:', '["Lateral supracondylar ridge","Medial epicondyle","Lateral epicondyle of humerus","Olecranon","Radial head"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral epicondyle of humerus', 'The common extensor origin is the lateral epicondyle of the humerus, giving rise to extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor digiti minimi, and extensor carpi ulnaris. Overuse of this attachment produces lateral epicondylitis.'),
('08ec6f06-7a96-5a0d-ab37-f22fd5aa02a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The common flexor origin is at:', '["Coronoid","Lateral epicondyle","Olecranon","Medial epicondyle of humerus","Medial supracondylar ridge"]'::jsonb, 'Medial epicondyle of humerus', 'The common flexor origin is the medial epicondyle, from which pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, and flexor carpi ulnaris arise. Inflammation here causes medial epicondylitis (golfer''s elbow).'),
('cc49aa72-32c6-5857-8d59-fc8f1c2af0d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor digitorum originates from:', '["Radial shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulna shaft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common extensor origin (lateral epicondyle)","Posterior ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial epicondyle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common extensor origin (lateral epicondyle)', 'Extensor digitorum shares the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle of the humerus. Its four tendons are connected by intertendinous connections, limiting independent extension of individual fingers.'),
('f98acf16-360a-5dd9-bb13-c00bff46352a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor digiti minimi originates from:', '["Interosseous membrane","Radial shaft","Ulna shaft","Posterior ligament","Common extensor origin"]'::jsonb, 'Common extensor origin', 'Extensor digiti minimi arises from the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle. Its tendon accompanies that of extensor digitorum to extend the little finger.'),
('743fe5e8-498e-573e-87fb-3ceb04de47d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor carpi ulnaris originates from:', '["Medial epicondyle resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial shaft only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common extensor origin and ulnar shaft","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral epicondyle only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common extensor origin and ulnar shaft', 'Extensor carpi ulnaris arises from the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle and from the posterior border of the ulna. It is the strongest extensor and ulnar deviator of the wrist.'),
('b39af380-4110-56e9-91a6-6242d5fa3dea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The supinator muscle originates from:', '["Coronoid process","Lateral epicondyle","Medial epicondyle","supinator crest and fossa of ulna","Radial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral epicondyle', 'Supinator has a superficial head arising from the lateral epicondyle and a deeper head from the supinator crest and fossa of the ulna. The deep branch of the radial nerve winds through these two heads.'),
('bc039ed9-0d0e-516e-8986-58b50086b70f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The supinator inserts into:', '["Radial styloid resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulna accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radial shaft (above the oblique line)","Radial tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral epicondyle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radial shaft (above the oblique line)', 'Supinator wraps around the neck of the radius to insert on its posterior surface above the oblique line, just proximal to the insertion of pronator teres. Contraction rotates the radius to supinate the forearm.'),
('9de2f7ef-552b-5de2-8199-75f81741e44e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The abductor pollicis longus originates from:', '["and interosseous membrane","Only ulna","Only radius","Posterior surfaces of radius","ulna"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surfaces of radius', 'Abductor pollicis longus takes origin from the posterior surfaces of the radius and ulna and the intervening interosseous membrane. Its tendon crosses obliquely over the two wrist extensor tendons to reach the base of the thumb.'),
('c7be3fac-4749-56bf-8f5a-243bbd3930b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor pollicis brevis originates from:', '["Posterior surface of radius and interosseous membrane","Ulna only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial epicondyle mediated by specific receptor activation","Interosseous membrane only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral epicondyle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of radius and interosseous membrane', 'Extensor pollicis brevis arises from the posterior surface of the radius and the adjacent interosseous membrane. It shares the first compartment with abductor pollicis longus and extends the proximal phalanx of the thumb.'),
('2da6e49e-bdd0-57e4-b7b9-461be4eea24e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor pollicis longus originates from:', '["Lateral epicondyle resulting in decreased functional activity","Interosseous membrane only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior surface of ulna and interosseous membrane","Medial epicondyle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radius only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of ulna and interosseous membrane', 'Extensor pollicis longus arises from the posterior surface of the ulna and the interosseous membrane, distal to extensor pollicis brevis. It rounds the dorsal tubercle of the radius and extends the distal phalanx of the thumb.'),
('ac7d4d9e-623f-598b-9ac4-c61a5c0c0eba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor indicis originates from:', '["Posterior surface of ulna and interosseous membrane","Radius only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial epicondyle mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral epicondyle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radial shaft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of ulna and interosseous membrane', 'Extensor indicis arises from the posterior surface of the ulna and the interosseous membrane below the origin of extensor pollicis longus. Its tendon passes on the ulnar side of the extensor digitorum tendon to extend the index finger independently.'),
('841b1d6e-75a0-55db-8bca-1dc200297e1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor retinaculum attaches:', '["Laterally to radius","To carpal bones only","medially to triquetrum and pisiform","To radius and ulna only","To metacarpals only"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally to radius', 'The extensor retinaculum anchors laterally to the distal end of the radius and medially to the triquetrum and pisiform. It binds the extensor tendons down as they cross the wrist and prevents tendon bowstringing.'),
('89e088c1-6ce0-50cc-b887-ebf68b377afe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor retinaculum has how many fibrous compartments?', '["4","7","3","6","5"]'::jsonb, '6', 'The extensor retinaculum is divided by fibrous septa into six osseofibrous compartments, numbered from the radial side. Each compartment transmits a defined group of extensor tendons to the hand.'),
('934e41dd-ee2c-53b3-856d-a04dc270e010'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 1st compartment of extensor retinaculum contains:', '["APL and EPB","Extensor digitorum","EPL only","Extensor indicis","ECRL and ECRB"]'::jsonb, 'APL and EPB', 'The first extensor compartment transmits abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis. Stenosing tenosynovitis of this compartment produces de Quervain disease.'),
('67e445c4-1f9c-5178-8e00-b5aba99bbc13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 2nd compartment of extensor retinaculum contains:', '["APL and EPB","ECRL and ECRB","Extensor carpi ulnaris","Extensor digitorum","EPL"]'::jsonb, 'ECRL and ECRB', 'The second compartment transmits extensor carpi radialis longus (lateral) and extensor carpi radialis brevis (medial). Both tendons insert on the base of the second metacarpal.'),
('95a28c20-a3fc-555a-8ab5-799d8ca761ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 3rd compartment of extensor retinaculum contains:', '["Extensor digitorum","EPL","APL and EPB","Extensor indicis","ECRL and ECRB"]'::jsonb, 'EPL', 'The third compartment holds the tendon of extensor pollicis longus, which turns around Lister''s tubercle on the dorsal radius. This sharp angulation predisposes the tendon to rupture after distal radial fractures.'),
('93fafdd9-74aa-5645-a0a2-2048b944cb22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 4th compartment of extensor retinaculum contains:', '["Extensor carpi ulnaris resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis (and posterior interosseous nerve)","APL and EPB mediated by specific receptor activation","ECRL and ECRB without affecting the primary tissue pathway","EPL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor digitorum and extensor indicis (and posterior interosseous nerve)', 'The largest fourth compartment transmits the tendons of extensor digitorum and extensor indicis, together with the posterior interosseous nerve. The nerve is thus at risk in distal radial procedures near this compartment.'),
('3b6c5276-3c68-5d1e-aa5a-9ae1f3b03b86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 5th compartment of extensor retinaculum contains:', '["Extensor indicis","Extensor carpi ulnaris","Extensor digiti minimi","Extensor digitorum","APL"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor digiti minimi', 'The extensor retinaculum is divided into six compartments by fibrous septa attached to the radius. The 5th compartment contains the extensor digiti minimi tendon, while the 4th holds the extensor digitorum and extensor indicis, the 3rd the extensor pollicis longus, the 6th the extensor carpi ulnaris, and the 1st the APL and EPB.'),
('8b1d502a-720c-5bf6-adad-b76ec2a29ded'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 6th compartment of extensor retinaculum contains:', '["Extensor digiti minimi","ECRB","EPL","Extensor carpi ulnaris","ECRL"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor carpi ulnaris', 'The 6th compartment lies in a groove on the posterior surface of the distal ulna and contains the extensor carpi ulnaris tendon. EPL (3rd), ECRL and ECRB (2nd), and extensor digiti minimi (5th) occupy different compartments.'),
('bd9e6a82-5567-5248-8cf7-d0f1a95d8505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'de Quervain''s tenosynovitis involves which compartment?', '["6th compartment","3rd compartment","1st compartment (APL and EPB)","4th compartment","2nd compartment"]'::jsonb, '1st compartment (APL and EPB)', 'de Quervain''s tenosynovitis is inflammation of the tendons of the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis, which run in the 1st extensor compartment. It causes pain over the radial styloid, aggravated by ulnar deviation of the wrist (Finkelstein test).'),
('5a59c0e7-570d-5f74-a803-537a9c390130'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor retinaculum forms the:', '["Lateral wall","Floor of carpal tunnel","Roof of carpal tunnel","Medial wall","None"]'::jsonb, 'Roof of carpal tunnel', 'The flexor retinaculum bridges the carpal groove and forms the roof of the carpal tunnel. The floor is formed by the carpal bones and their ligaments, and the tunnel transmits the median nerve and the long flexor tendons.'),
('24724151-417c-5c59-8db7-7c70420575b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The carpal tunnel is bounded posteriorly (floor) by:', '["Flexor retinaculum","Carpal bones and ligaments","Hamate","Trapezium","Pisiform"]'::jsonb, 'Carpal bones and ligaments', 'The floor of the carpal tunnel is formed by the carpal bones and their interconnecting ligaments. The roof is the flexor retinaculum, and the median nerve and flexor tendons pass through the tunnel between these two boundaries.'),
('729af440-6e25-5d1c-9890-b81d917b32e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve at the wrist lies:', '["Superficial to flexor retinaculum","Deep to FDS","Between FCR and FCU","Within FPL tendon","Between palmaris longus and FCR tendons"]'::jsonb, 'Between palmaris longus and FCR tendons', 'At the wrist, the median nerve lies deep to the flexor retinaculum between the tendon of palmaris longus (medially) and that of flexor carpi radialis (laterally). Its position here makes it vulnerable to compression in carpal tunnel syndrome.'),
('ee5f125d-93f8-50df-a40a-7ddb2763befc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of median nerve supplies:', '["Forearm resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin of fingers mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum of hand without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin of the palm of hand over thenar eminence"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the palm of hand over thenar eminence', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve arises proximal to the flexor retinaculum and supplies the skin of the lateral part of the palm over the thenar eminence. Because it does not enter the carpal tunnel, it is spared in carpal tunnel syndrome.'),
('fe3c20b8-b247-5410-b672-731a4aeeef80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of ulnar nerve supplies:', '["Central palm resulting in decreased functional activity","Fingers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsum of hand mediated by specific receptor activation","Thenar eminence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin of the palm over hypothenar eminence"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the palm over hypothenar eminence', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve supplies the skin of the medial part of the palm over the hypothenar eminence. It arises proximal to the flexor retinaculum and does not pass through Guyon''s canal.'),
('e2230bd1-4c9a-5907-8a14-5fdff096907a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The dorsal branch of ulnar nerve supplies:', '["Palm resulting in decreased functional activity","Flexor aspect of fingers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thenar eminence mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin of dorsal medial hand and little and ring fingers (proximal)","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of dorsal medial hand and little and ring fingers (proximal)', 'The dorsal (cutaneous) branch of the ulnar nerve arises in the distal forearm and supplies the dorsal skin of the medial side of the hand and the proximal parts of the little and ring fingers. It does not supply palmar skin.'),
('efb14440-fc39-5669-932d-3729b0a253df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The superficial branch of radial nerve supplies:', '["Dorsal skin of lateral hand and thumb","Thenar eminence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","middle (proximal) mediated by specific receptor activation","Palm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","index leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal skin of lateral hand and thumb', 'The superficial branch of the radial nerve is purely cutaneous and supplies the dorsal skin of the lateral side of the hand, including the thumb and the proximal parts of the index, middle, and ring fingers. It does not enter the palm.'),
('4c669a63-f280-51ef-9ed2-e4f0ce9da0c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The thenar muscles include all except:', '["Flexor pollicis brevis (superficial head)","None","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Opponens pollicis"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor pollicis', 'The thenar muscles are the abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, and opponens pollicis, all supplied by the median nerve. Adductor pollicis is not a thenar muscle; it is innervated by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve.'),
('f5fd5eec-d8b9-5beb-8788-9b5e67ac4328'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The hypothenar muscles include all except:', '["Palmaris brevis","Flexor digiti minimi brevis","Adductor pollicis","Abductor digiti minimi","Opponens digiti minimi"]'::jsonb, 'Palmaris brevis', 'The three hypothenar muscles proper are the abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, and opponens digiti minimi. Palmaris brevis is a small superficial skin-tensing muscle of the hypothenar region, not one of the three hypothenar muscles.'),
('d3b52d39-bba2-575a-8b01-29b89f3b5993'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmar interossei are how many?', '["2","1","3","4","7"]'::jsonb, '3', 'There are three palmar interossei, which adduct the fingers (PAD: palmar adduct). The four dorsal interossei instead abduct the fingers (DAB: dorsal abduct).'),
('8959f54c-773e-553e-818c-14bc55d6f8df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The dorsal interossei are how many?', '["4","2","3","1","7"]'::jsonb, '4', 'There are four dorsal interossei, which abduct the fingers away from the middle finger axis (DAB: dorsal abduct). The three palmar interossei adduct the fingers (PAD: palmar adduct).'),
('81b75315-b019-5c13-a22b-f80b62131258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmar interossei adduct the fingers toward the:', '["Little finger resulting in decreased functional activity","Ring finger accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle finger (axial line of hand)","Index finger without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thumb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Middle finger (axial line of hand)', 'The palmar interossei adduct the index, ring, and little fingers toward the middle finger, which lies on the axial line of the hand. The middle finger is the fixed reference axis and is not adducted by these muscles.'),
('fea3527d-52d0-583d-b2dd-189a5f80e7f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The dorsal interossei abduct the fingers away from the:', '["Index finger","All except middle","Middle finger","Thumb","Little finger"]'::jsonb, 'Middle finger', 'The dorsal interossei abduct the index, middle, and ring fingers away from the middle finger, which is the axis of the hand. The middle finger serves as the reference line from which abduction takes place.'),
('2bf658bd-97e0-59d3-9ee7-09d078865e4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lumbricals are how many?', '["5","4","3","2","1"]'::jsonb, '4', 'There are four lumbricals in the hand, arising from the tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus. They flex the metacarpophalangeal joints and extend the interphalangeal joints.'),
('766d51c3-e44c-5089-a9e9-f61d58e622c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 1st and 2nd lumbricals are supplied by:', '["Both median and ulnar","None","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'The first and second lumbricals are supplied by the median nerve, whereas the third and fourth lumbricals are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. The radial nerve provides no innervation to the lumbricals.'),
('4258841f-0b1b-572b-8168-23eb397525df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The 3rd and 4th lumbricals are supplied by:', '["Both resulting in decreased functional activity","Median nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ulnar nerve (deep branch)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve (deep branch)', 'The third and fourth lumbricals are innervated by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. The first and second lumbricals, by contrast, are supplied by the median nerve.'),
('acc64e33-0252-51eb-ac5c-c71bd9742658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lumbricals arise from tendons of:', '["FDP","FDS","ECRL","Interossei","FPL"]'::jsonb, 'FDP', 'The four lumbricals arise from the tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus and pass to the lateral sides of the extensor expansions. This origin links them functionally to the long flexor system.'),
('faff0b45-3bfe-5aca-b426-00bd22d99901'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lumbricals insert into:', '["Base of proximal phalanx resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral side of extensor expansion (dorsal digital expansion)","Middle phalanx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal phalanx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral side of extensor expansion (dorsal digital expansion)', 'Each lumbrical passes dorsally around the radial side of its metacarpophalangeal joint and inserts into the lateral margin of the extensor expansion. This insertion enables flexion of the MCP joint with extension of the IP joints.'),
('5d9783d5-3ab2-546a-ac47-b34b3815a146'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lumbricals function to:', '["Adduct fingers","Extend all finger joints","Flex all finger joints","Flex MCP and extend IP joints","Extend MCP and flex IP"]'::jsonb, 'Flex MCP and extend IP joints', 'The lumbricals flex the metacarpophalangeal joints and extend the interphalangeal joints, keeping the fingers straight during grip. This combined action opposes the pattern produced by the long flexors and extensors alone.'),
('1e897a14-1b20-553b-bb55-6ebcc66c5218'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The interossei insert into:', '["Base of distal phalanx resulting in decreased functional activity","Flexor sheath accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Base of proximal phalanx and extensor expansion","Base of middle phalanx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of proximal phalanx and extensor expansion', 'The interossei insert into the base of the proximal phalanx and into the extensor expansion. This dual insertion lets the dorsal interossei abduct and the palmar interossei adduct, while both contribute to MCP flexion and IP extension.'),
('dee7a092-4a03-559b-b914-f54e5fae22ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor expansion (dorsal digital expansion) receives tendons from:', '["Extensor digitorum","lumbricals","Only extensor digitorum","interossei","Only interossei"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor digitorum', 'The extensor expansion receives the tendon of extensor digitorum, supplemented by contributions from the interossei and lumbricals. The extensor digitorum tendon furnishes the central slip that extends the proximal phalanx.'),
('c405f540-fdda-5770-a785-91bc72962dee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The mallet finger is caused by rupture of:', '["Extensor tendon at distal phalanx","FDP tendon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flexor retinaculum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FDS tendon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor tendon at distal phalanx', 'Mallet finger results from rupture or avulsion of the extensor tendon at its insertion on the distal phalanx. The distal interphalangeal joint is then left in a flexed posture that the patient cannot actively extend.'),
('ed298088-568b-5d01-8c49-12befdf949f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Boutonniere deformity involves:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Rupture of central slip of extensor expansion at middle phalanx","Rupture of FDS mediated by specific receptor activation","Rupture of terminal slip without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rupture of FDP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rupture of central slip of extensor expansion at middle phalanx', 'Boutonniere deformity follows rupture of the central slip of the extensor expansion at the middle phalanx. The lateral bands then slip volarly, so the PIP joint flexes while the DIP joint hyperextends.'),
('626d7e7b-1518-54f6-bb95-627a991271fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Swan-neck deformity involves:', '["None","Hyperextension of PIP and flexion of DIP","Flexion of all joints","Flexion of PIP and hyperextension of DIP","Extension of all joints"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperextension of PIP and flexion of DIP', 'Swan-neck deformity is characterized by hyperextension of the proximal interphalangeal joint together with flexion of the distal interphalangeal joint. It is the reverse of the boutonniere pattern and results from imbalance of the extensor mechanism.'),
('fcde453a-9eef-56ce-b3f8-bc3a7fef757b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The felon is an infection of:', '["Palm","Web space","Nail bed","Pulp space of fingertip","Tendon sheath"]'::jsonb, 'Pulp space of fingertip', 'A felon is an infection of the closed pulp space of the fingertip. Because the pulp is compartmentalized by fibrous septa, the abscess becomes tense and painful and often requires surgical drainage.'),
('50af53d1-0138-5ade-a51a-767797d98f68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The paronychia is an infection of:', '["Pulp space","Palm","Tendon sheath","Web space","Nail fold (paronychium)"]'::jsonb, 'Nail fold (paronychium)', 'Paronychia is an infection of the nail fold (paronychium). It usually begins along the lateral nail fold and may spread beneath the nail if left untreated.'),
('3dd4aec4-4d6f-5e3f-ba3e-6a51cf9a763c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmar aponeurosis is a thickening of:', '["Extensor retinaculum","Flexor retinaculum","Palmar fascia (deep fascia of palm)","Superficial fascia","Subcutaneous tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Palmar fascia (deep fascia of palm)', 'The palmar aponeurosis is the thickened deep fascia (palmar fascia) of the palm. It anchors the skin to the underlying structures and becomes thickened and contracted in Dupuytren''s disease.'),
('cd851da9-38f7-558e-818e-7eeeaf90b53a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Dupuytren''s contracture involves:', '["Flexor tendon rupture resulting in decreased functional activity","Arthritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Median nerve compression mediated by specific receptor activation","Ulnar nerve compression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thickening and contracture of palmar aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Thickening and contracture of palmar aponeurosis', 'Dupuytren''s contracture is a progressive thickening and contracture of the palmar aponeurosis, most often affecting the ring finger. It produces flexion deformity of the metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints.'),
('c5cf628e-bc96-5ff8-b4a9-c7b259713639'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial artery at the wrist can be palpated:', '["Between FCR and PL resulting in decreased functional activity","At the ulnar side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the dorsum mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to FCR tendon (between FCR and radial styloid)","Between PL and FCU leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to FCR tendon (between FCR and radial styloid)', 'At the wrist, the radial artery is palpated lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon, between it and the radial styloid. In this position it can be compressed against the radius to feel the radial pulse.'),
('b7c4d123-ee9b-5f91-b39b-6b9498f794be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar pulse can be palpated:', '["Between FCR and PL resulting in decreased functional activity","At the dorsum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial to FCU tendon (between FCU and ulnar styloid)","Lateral to FCR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the radial side leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to FCU tendon (between FCU and ulnar styloid)', 'The ulnar pulse is palpated on the medial side of the wrist between the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris and the ulnar styloid. Just lateral to the FCU tendon the artery enters Guyon''s canal.'),
('607a2978-68e8-5a66-b0f5-610739bdae3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Allen''s test is used to assess:', '["Median nerve function","Radial nerve function","Patency of radial and ulnar arteries","Ulnar nerve function","Carpal tunnel"]'::jsonb, 'Patency of radial and ulnar arteries', 'Allen''s test assesses the patency of the radial and ulnar arteries and the completeness of the palmar arch. The hand is drained of blood, and each artery is released in turn to see whether color promptly returns.'),
('cee6b781-5038-5573-8afa-2edfd992d274'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Positive Allen''s test indicates:', '["Ulnar nerve compression resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Incomplete palmar arch (one artery occluded)","Median nerve compression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal circulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete palmar arch (one artery occluded)', 'A positive Allen''s test means the hand does not regain its color when one artery is released, indicating occlusion of that artery or an incomplete palmar arch. This warns against using that vessel for cannulation or harvest.'),
('2e17a6de-6886-50c9-98e4-d9cbcde46004'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve in the arm gives no branches except:', '["A small branch to brachial artery (vasomotor)","To biceps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To forearm mediated by specific receptor activation","To brachialis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To triceps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small branch to brachial artery (vasomotor)', 'The median nerve gives no muscular or cutaneous branches in the arm; the only branch it gives off there is a small vasomotor twig to the brachial artery. All of its named branches arise below the elbow.'),
('9ef380a4-ef0e-576b-99ec-893c52e46c07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve in the arm gives no branches except:', '["To extensor carpi resulting in decreased functional activity","To brachioradialis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small branch to triceps (medial head)","To brachialis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To biceps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small branch to triceps (medial head)', 'The ulnar nerve gives no significant branches in the arm apart from a small twig to the medial head of the triceps. It passes behind the medial epicondyle to reach the muscles of the forearm and hand.'),
('9a34ae30-fdd7-55da-8e1d-223be4a886d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve in the arm supplies:', '["ECRL","brachioradialis","Triceps (all heads)","anconeus","brachialis (part)"]'::jsonb, 'Triceps (all heads)', 'The radial nerve supplies the entire posterior compartment of the arm, including all three heads of the triceps. Brachioradialis, ECRL, anconeus, and part of brachialis are also radial-innervated, but the triceps (all heads) captures the full supply to the arm''s posterior compartment.'),
('daec31b3-7c40-5dad-9366-90cb6dfddd30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The musculocutaneous nerve continues as:', '["Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm","Lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm","Skin of arm","Posterior cutaneous nerve of forearm","Skin of hand"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm', 'After supplying the muscles of the anterior arm, the musculocutaneous nerve emerges lateral to the biceps tendon and continues as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm. It then carries sensation from the lateral part of the forearm.'),
('fe4c9268-7724-543b-b740-b47b22292c63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of forearm supplies:', '["Palm of hand resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior forearm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin of lateral forearm (radial side) as far as wrist","Dorsum of hand without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial forearm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of lateral forearm (radial side) as far as wrist', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, the terminal cutaneous branch of the musculocutaneous nerve, supplies the skin of the lateral (radial) side of the forearm as far as the wrist. It does not reach the palm or dorsum of the hand.'),
('de5502ed-1e0f-5ae8-9676-303ff8265e45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of forearm supplies:', '["Palm resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal forearm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hand mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin of medial (ulnar) side of forearm","Radial side of forearm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of medial (ulnar) side of forearm', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm arises from the medial cord and supplies the skin of the medial (ulnar) side of the forearm. It does not extend to supply the hand.'),
('b58d3437-3d16-5f94-958e-647ae23cc391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of forearm supplies:', '["Medial forearm","Radial forearm","Palm","Hand","Skin of posterior forearm"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of posterior forearm', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the forearm is a branch of the radial nerve that supplies the skin of the posterior aspect of the forearm. It arises in the arm and descends toward the wrist.'),
('2d007fff-3e2c-5f57-a478-b748c78754bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve gives the anterior interosseous nerve at:', '["Cubital fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","Wrist accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Level of flexor digitorum superficialis origin (between its two heads)","Arm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elbow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Level of flexor digitorum superficialis origin (between its two heads)', 'The anterior interosseous nerve branches from the median nerve just distal to the elbow, at the level where the median nerve passes between the two heads of the flexor digitorum superficialis. It then descends on the interosseous membrane.'),
('a37e8317-8aa9-573d-8c1e-24351bfb99ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anterior interosseous nerve supplies:', '["FDP only","FPL","FDS only","FDP (radial half)","PQ"]'::jsonb, 'FPL', 'The anterior interosseous nerve supplies the flexor pollicis longus, the lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus, and the pronator quadratus. Among the options, the flexor pollicis longus is a constant and distinctive target of this nerve.'),
('94ca1ab1-65c0-579e-ba31-23a8b0aaf83f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmaris longus is absent in what percentage of people?', '["Approximately 10-15%","50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","25% mediated by specific receptor activation","40% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 10-15%', 'The palmaris longus is the most variable muscle of the forearm and is absent in roughly 10-15% of individuals. When present, its tendon is nonetheless a useful landmark at the wrist.'),
('4c2593af-8f34-52b4-9c23-6d82e24f1ca0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmaris longus tendon is a useful landmark for:', '["FCR tendon","Radial artery","Ulnar artery","Median nerve at wrist","FCU tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve at wrist', 'The palmaris longus tendon lies immediately medial to the median nerve at the wrist. It therefore serves as a reliable surface landmark for locating the median nerve, which passes just deep and slightly lateral to it.'),
('ad579643-52c8-58d2-ae7e-4f0301fcc325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor pollicis longus tendon forms the medial border of:', '["1st compartment","Extensor retinaculum","Anatomical snuffbox","Ulnar tunnel","Carpal tunnel"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical snuffbox', 'The extensor pollicis longus tendon forms the medial border of the anatomical snuffbox. Its lateral border is formed by the APL and EPB tendons, and the radial artery and scaphoid lie within its floor.'),
('cbedaec8-9c85-5a0a-aadf-85e9054ceef1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The APL and EPB form the lateral border of:', '["None","Carpal tunnel","Anatomical snuffbox","Ulnar tunnel","Extensor retinaculum"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical snuffbox', 'The tendons of the abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis form the lateral border of the anatomical snuffbox. The medial border is the extensor pollicis longus tendon, and the radial artery lies within the snuffbox.'),
('2d7ec611-c3b8-5c9a-8e14-5577ac1f5f6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial artery can be felt in the snuffbox and it passes:', '["Through the carpal tunnel resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle","Through Guyon''s canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficially leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle', 'From the anatomical snuffbox, the radial artery passes between the two heads of the first dorsal interosseous muscle to reach the palm. It does not traverse the carpal tunnel or Guyon''s canal.'),
('6d5169a3-884a-5be4-8026-70dbd4be91a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial artery enters the palm by passing:', '["Through carpal tunnel resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the two heads of adductor pollicis (transversus and obliquus)","Through the anatomical snuffbox without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through Guyon''s canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the two heads of adductor pollicis (transversus and obliquus)', 'The radial artery enters the palm by passing between the transverse and oblique heads of the adductor pollicis. It then forms the deep palmar arch by anastomosing with the deep branch of the ulnar artery.'),
('59b69e21-43de-573f-9d31-9f9151accea6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve (deep branch) passes through:', '["Supinator muscle (Arcade of Frohse)","Carpal tunnel","Pronator teres","Interosseous membrane","Guyon''s canal"]'::jsonb, 'Supinator muscle (Arcade of Frohse)', 'The deep branch of the radial nerve enters the supinator beneath the Arcade of Frohse, the proximal tendinous edge of its superficial head, and emerges distally as the posterior interosseous nerve. The carpal tunnel and Guyon''s canal transmit the median and ulnar nerves, while the interosseous membrane carries the anterior interosseous nerve.'),
('51eba61b-5ec0-5d8f-8306-cbb035f53a0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The Arcade of Frohse is:', '["A ligament in the carpal tunnel resulting in decreased functional activity","An aponeurosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The proximal (tendinous) edge of the superficial head of supinator","The distal edge of supinator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A retinaculum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal (tendinous) edge of the superficial head of supinator', 'The Arcade of Frohse is the proximal tendinous margin of the superficial head of supinator, under which the deep branch of the radial nerve passes to become the posterior interosseous nerve. It is not a ligament, aponeurosis, retinaculum, or a distal structure.'),
('618ade7c-fe1b-5c81-bf2e-d3ab0f5aa702'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve proper (before bifurcation) supplies:', '["Thenar muscles","Triceps and anconeus","Posterior forearm muscles","Brachioradialis and ECRL","Hand muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Brachioradialis and ECRL', 'Before dividing, the radial nerve in the cubital fossa supplies brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus. Triceps and anconeus are innervated higher in the arm, posterior forearm muscles are supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve, and the thenar and hand muscles are supplied by the median and ulnar nerves.'),
('b79fc1aa-efad-508f-a0b5-2f87681da616'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior interosseous nerve terminates as:', '["Cutaneous branches resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudoganglion on the dorsal wrist capsule","Digital branches mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscular branches without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudoganglion on the dorsal wrist capsule', 'The posterior interosseous nerve ends as a pseudoganglion on the dorsal wrist capsule, giving sensory branches to the wrist joint. It carries no cutaneous or digital branches, because skin on the dorsum of the hand is supplied by the superficial branch of the radial nerve.'),
('2ed11e89-6dea-51ae-85ac-285c18b292d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The finger''s fascial spaces include all except:', '["Subcutaneous tissue only","Pulp space","Web space (subaponeurotic)","Thenar space","Midpalmar space"]'::jsonb, 'Midpalmar space', 'The fascial spaces of the digits include the pulp space, the web spaces, and subcutaneous tissue. The midpalmar space is a deep fascial space of the palm rather than a fascial space of the finger, so it is the exception.'),
('e783dbe8-97b4-502f-9c4d-f0ceafd8740d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The midpalmar space lies:', '["Dorsal to metacarpals","In the hypothenar area","Beneath the flexor tendons in the central palm","Under the skin","Beneath the thenar muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Beneath the flexor tendons in the central palm', 'The midpalmar space lies deep to the flexor tendons of the ring and little fingers in the central palm, between the midpalmar and hypothenar septa. It does not lie dorsal to the metacarpals, under the skin, or in the thenar or hypothenar areas.'),
('820dd048-8f83-5a18-a2fc-709dc88247eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The thenar space lies:', '["In the hypothenar area resulting in decreased functional activity","Beneath the flexor tendons of the index finger (radial side)","Dorsal aspect mediated by specific receptor activation","Web space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the central palm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beneath the flexor tendons of the index finger (radial side)', 'The thenar space lies deep to the flexor tendons of the index finger on the radial side of the palm, bounded by the thenar and midpalmar septa. It is not situated in the hypothenar area, web space, or central palm.'),
('bdf7e4bb-6c15-56e9-83e0-a69143804329'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The midpalmar space is bounded:', '["laterally by midpalmar septum","Medially by midpalmar septum","Medially by hypothenar septum","Proximally at wrist","laterally by thenar septum"]'::jsonb, 'Medially by hypothenar septum', 'The midpalmar space is bounded medially (ulnarly) by the hypothenar septum and laterally by the midpalmar (oblique) septum. The other listed boundaries are reversed or describe different regions.'),
('81b1102a-4fed-5df5-a2c4-09a0b241daf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial (lateral) bursa encloses:', '["FPL tendon","FDS tendons","FDP tendons","All flexor tendons","The median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'FPL tendon', 'The radial (lateral) synovial bursa surrounds the flexor pollicis longus tendon. The flexor digitorum tendons are enveloped by the ulnar bursa, and the median nerve is not enclosed by any bursa.'),
('d5ded8aa-1852-5d42-a23f-585ddf7e251e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar (medial) bursa encloses:', '["FPL tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulnar nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radial artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The common flexor sheath (FDP and FDS tendons)","Median nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The common flexor sheath (FDP and FDS tendons)', 'The ulnar (medial) bursa forms the common flexor sheath surrounding the flexor digitorum superficialis and profundus tendons. The flexor pollicis longus lies in the radial bursa, while the median and ulnar nerves and radial artery run outside the bursae.'),
('89b99989-0020-5ff3-ab0e-d83a72bb172d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial and ulnar bursae may communicate:', '["Only distally resulting in decreased functional activity","At the wrist in some individuals (50% of people)","communicate mediated by specific receptor activation","Only proximally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","communicate (alternative wording) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the wrist in some individuals (50% of people)', 'In about half of individuals the radial and ulnar bursae communicate through a connection at the wrist, which is why infection can spread between them to form a horseshoe abscess. The other options do not describe this normal communication site.'),
('271d2fc9-3687-5750-bc57-48617fe43009'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The thumb''s (1st) metacarpal is unique because:', '["It is shorter resulting in decreased functional activity","It is longest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It has medullary cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","It has epiphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It rotates 90 degrees relative to other metacarpals"]'::jsonb, 'It rotates 90 degrees relative to other metacarpals', 'The first metacarpal is rotated about 90 degrees relative to the other metacarpals, placing its movements in a plane at right angles to theirs and enabling opposition of the thumb. Its length, medullary cavity, and epiphysis are not unique features.'),
('5e9a138f-f218-5558-a30e-18fb2edce8b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The carpal bones are arranged in how many rows?', '["1","None","4","3","2"]'::jsonb, '2', 'The carpal bones are arranged in two rows: a proximal row (scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform) and a distal row (trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate).'),
('c10167d6-48cc-5069-805f-9cc623b8ce6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The proximal row of carpal bones from lateral to medial:', '["lunate","triquetrum","Scaphoid","None of the above","pisiform"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid', 'From lateral (radial) to medial (ulnar), the proximal row is scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform, so the scaphoid is the lateralmost bone.'),
('14dc42bc-efa2-55ff-9117-7fa8f3b8620e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The distal row of carpal bones from lateral to medial:', '["hamate","Trapezium","capitate","None of the above","trapezoid"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezium', 'From lateral to medial, the distal row is trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate, so the trapezium is the lateralmost bone.'),
('84a47a90-ae86-5b52-b1a2-57324585f178'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The scaphoid bone is most commonly fractured carpal bone. What is special about its blood supply?', '["It has dual supply resulting in decreased functional activity","All of these accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proximal pole has retrograde blood supply (frequent avascular necrosis)","None of these without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal pole has poor supply leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal pole has retrograde blood supply (frequent avascular necrosis)', 'The scaphoid receives most of its blood supply from distal vessels, so blood flows retrogradely toward the proximal pole; a waist fracture can interrupt this supply and cause avascular necrosis of the proximal fragment.'),
('07e70228-d12b-5b75-a6f6-7b661baebc31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lunate bone is most commonly:', '["Infected","Fused carpal bone","Dislocated carpal bone","None","Fractured carpal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Dislocated carpal bone', 'The lunate is the carpal bone most often dislocated, typically in perilunate dislocation injuries. In contrast, the scaphoid is the most commonly fractured carpal bone.'),
('c04c7678-2d38-5ada-a2d4-fddd7682f19e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Faller''s sign is not a recognized term. The correct eponym is:', '["None","Thenar sign","not related to hand","Kehr sign","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Kehr sign', 'Among the listed options, only Kehr sign is an established clinical eponym, referring to referred pain in the left shoulder tip classically seen with splenic irritation. The other choices are not recognized eponymous signs.'),
('ef083635-af6d-5d81-a8fe-14ef6287d40d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The pisiform is a sesamoid bone in the tendon of:', '["FDS","FCR","FCU","PL","FDP"]'::jsonb, 'FCU', 'The pisiform is a sesamoid bone developing within the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris, transmitting the pull of that muscle to the hook of hamate and the base of the fifth metacarpal. The other listed tendons do not contain the pisiform.'),
('3d986ad5-e1aa-5233-8227-5f5856844ecc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The elbow joint is what type of joint?', '["Ball and socket","Saddle","Plane","Pivot","Hinge (ginglymus) joint"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge (ginglymus) joint', 'The humeroulnar joint is a hinge (ginglymus) joint allowing flexion and extension around a single transverse axis. Saddle, pivot, plane, and ball-and-socket are incorrect joint types for the elbow.'),
('93c15dad-53c3-5264-9bdb-e961dc0a000f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The elbow joint capsule is lax:', '["None","All around","Superiorly and inferiorly","Medially and laterally","Anteriorly and posteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly and posteriorly', 'The elbow joint capsule is thin and lax anteriorly and posteriorly to permit flexion and extension, whereas medially and laterally it is reinforced by strong collateral ligaments.'),
('a903fbf3-038a-5f18-8779-aa986632d0a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The medial (ulnar) collateral ligament of elbow:', '["Has anterior","Is a single band","Is fan-shaped from above","posterior","and oblique bands"]'::jsonb, 'Has anterior', 'The ulnar (medial) collateral ligament is triangular and composed of anterior, posterior, and oblique bands, with the anterior band being the strongest and the main stabilizer against valgus stress. It is therefore not a single band.'),
('07a185ec-77c1-573c-8212-87356f28809e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lateral (radial) collateral ligament of elbow:', '["Is absent resulting in decreased functional activity","Is fan-shaped from lateral epicondyle to orbicular ligament","Is cord-like mediated by specific receptor activation","Has multiple bands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is fan-shaped from lateral epicondyle to orbicular ligament', 'The lateral (radial) collateral ligament arises from the lateral epicondyle and fans out to blend with the annular (orbicular) ligament of the radius. It is neither absent nor cord-like.'),
('87dd38ed-96fd-501e-be7f-32552a4e971e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The annular ligament of radius:', '["Encircles the radial head","Attaches radius to humerus","Attaches ulna to humerus","holding it against ulna","Is part of wrist joint"]'::jsonb, 'Encircles the radial head', 'The annular ligament encircles the head of the radius and holds it against the radial notch of the ulna, allowing the radius to rotate during pronation and supination. It does not attach to the humerus and is not part of the wrist joint.'),
('d88a18c7-7920-54a1-bfe9-c513fbc6b587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Pulled elbow (nursemaid''s elbow) is:', '["Lateral epicondyle fracture","Radial head fracture","Subluxation of radial head from annular ligament","Elbow dislocation","Medial epicondyle fracture"]'::jsonb, 'Subluxation of radial head from annular ligament', 'Pulled elbow occurs when traction on the pronated forearm of a young child slips the radial head out from the annular ligament, producing a subluxation. It is not a fracture or a complete dislocation.'),
('9f770e37-7a13-51b5-9e80-b0f437f73add'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Tennis elbow (lateral epicondylitis) involves:', '["Common extensor origin at lateral epicondyle","Common flexor origin at medial epicondyle","Olecranon bursa","Triceps tendon","Biceps tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Common extensor origin at lateral epicondyle', 'Tennis elbow is a tendinopathy of the common extensor origin at the lateral epicondyle, typically from repetitive wrist extension. It does not primarily involve the flexor origin, olecranon bursa, or triceps and biceps tendons.'),
('2b3083d3-f6bd-55d5-9a78-94af90fe7ffe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Golfer''s elbow (medial epicondylitis) involves:', '["Olecranon bursa resulting in decreased functional activity","Common extensor origin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Triceps insertion mediated by specific receptor activation","Common flexor origin at medial epicondyle","Biceps tendon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common flexor origin at medial epicondyle', 'Golfer''s elbow is a tendinopathy of the common flexor origin at the medial epicondyle, usually from repetitive wrist flexion. The extensor origin, olecranon bursa, and triceps and biceps insertions are not involved.'),
('087e7a41-3b49-5a76-a695-1d506bc8f5c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Olecranon bursitis (student''s elbow) is inflammation of:', '["Bursa over medial epicondyle","Bursa over lateral epicondyle","Subacromial bursa","Bursa over radial head","Bursa over olecranon process"]'::jsonb, 'Bursa over olecranon process', 'Olecranon bursitis is inflammation of the subcutaneous bursa over the olecranon process, classically from chronic pressure or friction at the point of the elbow. The other bursae listed lie at different sites.'),
('331d3f75-67b4-5486-a363-5ed473638be0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The distal radioulnar joint is what type?', '["Saddle","Ellipsoid","Plane","Pivot joint","Hinge"]'::jsonb, 'Pivot joint', 'The distal radioulnar joint is a pivot (trochoid) joint in which the head of the ulna articulates with the ulnar notch of the radius, permitting pronation and supination. It is not a saddle, ellipsoid, plane, or hinge joint.'),
('3a06042c-2ad6-57cb-b645-99b3fd7e6d72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radioulnar joints (proximal and distal) allow:', '["Adduction and abduction","Flexion and extension","Circumduction","Pronation and supination","Rotation"]'::jsonb, 'Pronation and supination', 'The proximal and distal radioulnar joints act together as a pivot joint to produce pronation and supination of the forearm, in which the radius rotates around the ulna. Flexion and extension and abduction and adduction occur at other joints.'),
('6f7e4fc8-2ad0-5e30-8f51-1ef2a512340e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The interosseous membrane of forearm:', '["Is a ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Is only proximal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is absent mediated by specific receptor activation","Connects radius and ulna throughout their length","Is only distal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connects radius and ulna throughout their length', 'The interosseous membrane is a fibrous sheet connecting the shafts of the radius and ulna along their entire length. It is not absent and is not limited to one end of the forearm.'),
('4fb7e8e0-f5f5-5d55-bc6d-611f199f9449'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The interosseous membrane functions to:', '["Prevent pronation resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prevent supination mediated by specific receptor activation","Transfer forces from radius to ulna","Stabilize wrist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer forces from radius to ulna', 'The interosseous membrane transmits forces from the radius to the ulna and gives attachment to forearm muscles. It does not prevent pronation or supination, which are movements produced by muscles.'),
('827d143c-f4e2-51a5-9b5b-7d29216cfb66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The pronator quadratus originates from:', '["Distal anterior radius","Distal anterior surface of ulna","Proximal radius","Proximal ulna","Humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Distal anterior surface of ulna', 'Pronator quadratus arises from the distal anterior surface of the ulna and runs obliquely to the radius. When it contracts, it draws the distal radius medially to pronate the forearm.'),
('b8c1553d-77c9-5832-b494-23a4e5d8cb07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The pronator quadratus inserts into:', '["Distal ulna resulting in decreased functional activity","Proximal radius accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carpal bones mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximal ulna without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal anterior surface of radius"]'::jsonb, 'Distal anterior surface of radius', 'Pronator quadratus inserts into the distal anterior surface of the radius. Contraction pulls the distal radius toward the ulna, producing pronation of the forearm.'),
('d290d26a-abd5-5620-8720-156bf82d9823'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The triangle of auscultation is bounded by:', '["Serratus anterior","latissimus dorsi","and medial border of scapula","Trapezius","rhomboids"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezius', 'The triangle of auscultation is bounded by the trapezius above, the latissimus dorsi below, and the medial border of the scapula laterally. The serratus anterior and rhomboids are not among its boundaries.'),
('0c7e63a0-70ca-5b00-abe0-957dc0d7dfa2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The triangle of auscultation is clinically important because:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","It provides a window to listen to lung sounds with less muscle interference","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","It is a point for needle insertion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is a site for thoracentesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It provides a window to listen to lung sounds with less muscle interference', 'The triangle of auscultation is an area of the back with little overlying muscle, so lung sounds are heard there with less interference, making it useful for auscultation. It is not primarily a site for needle insertion or thoracentesis.'),
('3d1af2c6-6560-53fa-b308-ef0314c5c8cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lumbar triangle (Petit''s triangle) is bounded by:', '["external oblique","Latissimus dorsi","None of the above","and iliac crest","internal oblique"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'Petit''s (lumbar) triangle is bounded by the latissimus dorsi, the external oblique, and the iliac crest, with the internal oblique forming its floor. It is a potential site for a lumbar hernia.'),
('95e58efd-43fd-5bea-8980-b14789d4d37a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The quadrangular space transmits:', '["Suprascapular nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial nerve and profunda brachii accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circumflex scapular artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral vessels","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral vessels', 'The quadrangular space transmits the axillary nerve and the posterior circumflex humeral artery and vein. The radial nerve with the profunda brachii passes through the triangular interval, and the circumflex scapular artery passes through the triangular space.'),
('2d062426-52c3-571f-b994-6dedbceda0e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The triangular space transmits:', '["Suprascapular nerve","Posterior circumflex humeral","Radial nerve","Axillary nerve","Circumflex scapular artery"]'::jsonb, 'Circumflex scapular artery', 'The triangular space transmits the circumflex scapular artery. The axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral vessels occupy the quadrangular space, while the radial nerve and profunda brachii artery pass through the triangular interval.'),
('731bf2b4-5754-5500-aed4-1bc4a87f531a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The triangular interval transmits:', '["Suprascapular nerve","Radial nerve and profunda brachii artery","Circumflex scapular artery","Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral","None"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve and profunda brachii artery', 'The triangular interval transmits the radial nerve and the profunda brachii artery. The axillary nerve with the posterior circumflex humeral vessels passes through the quadrangular space, and the circumflex scapular artery through the triangular space.'),
('47101246-08c1-5a5e-aebe-b5885eeb0e2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median cubital vein connects:', '["Cephalic and basilic veins","Basilic and axillary","Cephalic and brachial","Basilic and brachial","Cephalic and axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic and basilic veins', 'The median cubital vein runs across the cubital fossa connecting the cephalic vein laterally to the basilic vein medially. The axillary and brachial veins are deep veins not connected directly by the median cubital vein.'),
('073c1fb3-1959-594a-9acc-7a309a5b36a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median cubital vein is a common site for:', '["Tendon injection resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arterial puncture mediated by specific receptor activation","Venipuncture (IV access and blood sampling)","Nerve block leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Venipuncture (IV access and blood sampling)', 'The median cubital vein is a preferred site for venipuncture because it is large, superficial, and relatively well fixed over the bicipital aponeurosis. It is not used for arterial puncture, nerve blocks, or tendon injections.'),
('a670092c-426f-5d4d-8cd1-34c485f43696'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median cubital vein lies:', '["Superficial to the bicipital aponeurosis","Deep to the bicipital aponeurosis","Below the brachial artery","Above the median nerve","None"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial to the bicipital aponeurosis', 'The median cubital vein runs superficial to the bicipital aponeurosis, while the brachial artery and median nerve lie deep to it. The aponeurosis protects the artery during venipuncture.'),
('eb1a6c16-345f-5013-be46-29c7e90a5737'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The bicipital aponeurosis separates:', '["Pronator teres from supinator","None","Biceps tendon from radius","Median nerve from ulnar artery","Median cubital vein from brachial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Median cubital vein from brachial artery', 'The bicipital aponeurosis passes from the biceps tendon across the cubital fossa, separating the superficial median cubital vein from the deeper brachial artery and protecting the artery during venipuncture. It does not separate the other listed structures.'),
('00834aad-ba48-55e0-8317-97e410e884ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median cephalic vein and median basilic vein are:', '["Lymphatics resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patterns of the superficial veins of cubital fossa","Deep veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cutaneous nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patterns of the superficial veins of cubital fossa', 'The median cephalic and median basilic veins are common variations in the arrangement of the superficial veins of the cubital fossa. They are veins, not lymphatics, deep veins, or cutaneous nerves.'),
('2f24af92-2910-5450-b5ab-60312b5f61cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The dorsal venous arch of hand drains into:', '["Median cubital vein","Axillary vein","Radial and ulnar veins","None","Cephalic and basilic veins"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic and basilic veins', 'The dorsal venous arch of the hand drains laterally into the cephalic vein and medially into the basilic vein. It does not drain into the median cubital, axillary, or deep radial and ulnar veins.'),
('9e4ad368-0b12-5259-aa0d-d38fd9fc4eb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The cephalic vein ascends on the lateral side of the arm and drains into:', '["Axillary vein","Brachiocephalic","External jugular","Subclavian","Basilic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary vein', 'The cephalic vein ascends the lateral side of the arm, passes through the deltopectoral groove, and enters the axillary vein. It does not drain into the basilic, subclavian, jugular, or brachiocephalic veins.'),
('006195e7-7ed6-5087-b6ed-1ee09f3f14db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The basilic vein ascends on the medial side of the arm and pierces which fascia?', '["Brachial fascia (deep fascia)","None","Clavipectoral fascia","Axillary sheath","Pectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial fascia (deep fascia)', 'The basilic vein pierces the deep (brachial) fascia in the lower part of the arm to run alongside the brachial artery. The clavipectoral fascia and axillary sheath are not pierced by it at this level.'),
('6fb2bc37-0ed0-5414-87a9-7d65fe3657c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The basilic vein joins the venae comitantes of the brachial artery to form:', '["Brachial vein","Axillary vein","None","Cephalic vein","Subclavian vein"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary vein', 'In the upper arm the basilic vein joins the venae comitantes of the brachial artery to form the axillary vein. The cephalic vein later joins the axillary vein separately, and the subclavian vein forms beyond the axillary vein.'),
('7282c451-9b1d-56ee-9446-b259a6a6dd40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lymph nodes of the upper limb include:', '["Cervical nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Cubital nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paraaortic nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Supratrochlear node and axillary nodes","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supratrochlear node and axillary nodes', 'The upper limb is drained by the supratrochlear (epitrochlear) nodes in the arm and by the axillary lymph node groups. The cervical, cubital, and paraaortic nodes are not the primary nodes draining the upper limb.'),
('bb248c2a-f3d1-53af-8e76-561164d44577'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The supratrochlear node drains:', '["The axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","The ulnar (medial) side of the forearm and hand","The shoulder mediated by specific receptor activation","The thumb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The radial side leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ulnar (medial) side of the forearm and hand', 'The supratrochlear (cubital) lymph node lies above the medial epicondyle and receives lymph from the ulnar (medial) aspect of the forearm and hand. Its efferents then pass to the axillary nodes, while the thumb and radial side drain through other routes.'),
('8e9a8094-c2df-5817-ab71-e2b15f47de82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The axillary lymph nodes draining the upper limb are mainly:', '["Subscapular group","Lateral group (along axillary vein)","Pectoral group","Central group","Apical group"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral group (along axillary vein)', 'The lateral group of axillary lymph nodes, arranged along the axillary vein, receives the major lymphatic drainage of the upper limb. The subscapular and pectoral groups drain the back and breast respectively, while the central and apical groups are more distal stations.'),
('a995cdfb-e2e5-51fb-b486-63b30fc7639e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Which muscle of the anterior compartment of the arm lies most medially?', '["brachialis","biceps","Coracobrachialis","None of the above","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Coracobrachialis', 'The coracobrachialis is the most medial muscle of the anterior compartment of the arm, lying medial to the biceps and brachialis. It is pierced by the musculocutaneous nerve and inserts into the medial surface of the humerus.'),
('cbadd837-75f5-5182-be86-895dd95fcc71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Which statement about the two heads of the biceps brachii is correct?', '["Both from glenoid","Short head from supraglenoid","long head from supraglenoid tubercle","long head from coracoid","Short head from coracoid"]'::jsonb, 'Short head from coracoid', 'The short head of the biceps brachii arises from the coracoid process together with the coracobrachialis, while the long head arises from the supraglenoid tubercle of the scapula. The long head then runs through the intertubercular groove of the humerus.'),
('198e0107-38d9-566e-8a1d-444ebd6c5fe5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The long head of biceps passes through:', '["The triangular space resulting in decreased functional activity","The quadrangular space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The glenoid cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","The intertubercular (bicipital) groove of humerus","The coracoid process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The intertubercular (bicipital) groove of humerus', 'The tendon of the long head of the biceps enters the shoulder joint and descends through the intertubercular (bicipital) groove of the humerus, where it is held by the transverse humeral ligament. The quadrangular space transmits the axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral vessels.'),
('c36a4e79-97f1-5bb6-a2a3-00965e413e45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The biceps brachii inserts into:', '["Ulna styloid","Radial styloid","Olecranon","Radial tuberosity","Coronoid process of ulna"]'::jsonb, 'Radial tuberosity', 'The biceps brachii inserts mainly into the radial tuberosity, and its bicipital aponeurosis blends with the deep fascia of the forearm. This radial insertion explains its strong supinator action.'),
('f52f08b8-30ab-5aa5-b09e-48509121bf26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The biceps brachii muscle produces:', '["Supination only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pronation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extension mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexion only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexion and supination of forearm"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion and supination of forearm', 'The biceps brachii flexes the elbow and is the most powerful supinator of the forearm, acting best when the elbow is flexed. It does not pronate or extend the forearm.'),
('e54436fd-b1eb-5f39-8985-bda8b9264cff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The biceps is most effective as a flexor when the forearm is:', '["None","Extended","Supinated","Pronated","Midpronated"]'::jsonb, 'Supinated', 'Because the biceps inserts on the radius, it flexes the elbow most powerfully when the forearm is supinated, which optimizes the direction of its pull across the joint. In pronation its force is largely spent on supination rather than flexion.'),
('805268e2-0f16-56c3-873c-fbc08e07e904'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachialis originates from:', '["Medial epicondyle","Upper half of anterior humerus","Lateral epicondyle","Radial tuberosity","Lower half of anterior humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Lower half of anterior humerus', 'The brachialis arises from the lower half of the anterior surface of the humerus and the adjacent intermuscular septa, lying deep to the biceps. It is the workhorse flexor of the elbow.'),
('2e7d0cb3-4d5a-5bdd-a59c-4b4fbb9ae192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachialis inserts into:', '["Coronoid fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Olecranon mediated by specific receptor activation","Radial tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coronoid process and tuberosity of ulna"]'::jsonb, 'Coronoid process and tuberosity of ulna', 'The brachialis inserts into the coronoid process and tuberosity of the ulna, an ulnar attachment that lets it flex the elbow in all forearm positions. The olecranon receives the triceps.'),
('7debbe74-fb30-5178-bb7b-30d3ba44c410'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachialis is supplied by which nerves?', '["Median and ulnar resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Musculocutaneous (lateral part) and radial nerve (medial part)","Only musculocutaneous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only radial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous (lateral part) and radial nerve (medial part)', 'The brachialis receives dual innervation: mainly the musculocutaneous nerve, with an additional contribution from the radial nerve to its lateral part. The median and ulnar nerves do not supply it.'),
('9dd60858-f70e-5489-b4f7-065ea5619754'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The coracobrachialis originates from:', '["Supraglenoid tubercle","Infraglenoid tubercle","Acromion","Coracoid process","Clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Coracoid process', 'The coracobrachialis arises from the coracoid process, sharing its origin with the short head of the biceps. It is the only muscle of the anterior arm arising from and inserting on the humerus.'),
('dd101082-c01b-55e9-a3bd-16d1d86999ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The coracobrachialis inserts into:', '["Radial tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial surface of humerus (middle third)","Lateral humerus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coronoid process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial surface of humerus (middle third)', 'The coracobrachialis inserts into the middle third of the medial surface of the humerus, and the musculocutaneous nerve pierces it. This medial insertion supports its flexor and adductor action.'),
('5ec1cd41-803e-5039-bd21-9a1cf334851f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The coracobrachialis is supplied by:', '["Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve', 'The coracobrachialis is innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve, which pierces the muscle in the proximal arm. The axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor, and the other nerves supply different regions.'),
('4f700522-9720-5149-b3c8-167a682bccda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The coracobrachialis acts to:', '["Flex and adduct the arm","Supinate the forearm","Extend the arm","Abduct the arm","Rotate the arm"]'::jsonb, 'Flex and adduct the arm', 'The coracobrachialis flexes and adducts the arm at the shoulder joint and helps stabilize the humeral head. It does not act on the forearm or extend or abduct the arm.'),
('dc57c17b-421d-5c8e-8f0e-fa20ac51a212'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachial artery lies in the arm in which position?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior to the humerus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial to the humerus (in the medial bicipital groove)","Lateral to the humerus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior to the humerus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to the humerus (in the medial bicipital groove)', 'In the arm the brachial artery descends medial to the humerus in the medial bicipital groove, accompanied by the median nerve and venae comitantes. It lies anterior to the elbow, not posterior or lateral.'),
('e08687aa-88aa-5a35-bc56-b18e04ebf7bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The brachial artery is accompanied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Single brachial vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cephalic vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Basilic vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two venae comitantes (brachial veins)"]'::jsonb, 'Two venae comitantes (brachial veins)', 'The brachial artery is accompanied by two brachial venae comitantes that arise in the cubital fossa and become the axillary vein. The cephalic and basilic veins are superficial veins, not companions of the artery.'),
('e367d18c-d99b-5bd2-82c8-370dff3d5c80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve in the arm lies:', '["Medial to brachial artery throughout resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind the artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral throughout mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to the brachial artery (proximally) then crosses to medial","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to the brachial artery (proximally) then crosses to medial', 'In the proximal arm the median nerve lies lateral to the brachial artery, then crosses it (usually anteriorly) to lie medial in the cubital fossa. It gives off no branches in the arm.'),
('ee109b05-4030-5206-af1c-a035f0f67b87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar nerve in the arm:', '["Pierces the medial intermuscular septum and passes posterior to medial epicondyle","Stays lateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is in the anterior compartment mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pierces lateral septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pierces the medial intermuscular septum and passes posterior to medial epicondyle', 'The ulnar nerve pierces the medial intermuscular septum and descends posterior to the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where it is vulnerable to injury. It does not remain in the anterior compartment or pass laterally.'),
('fe9dc306-70fa-5baa-ba73-7c6f1f1da4a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve in the arm:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Stays posterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enters the axilla mediated by specific receptor activation","Pierces medial septum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pierces the lateral intermuscular septum and enters the anterior compartment of arm"]'::jsonb, 'Pierces the lateral intermuscular septum and enters the anterior compartment of arm', 'After running in the spiral groove deep to the triceps, the radial nerve pierces the lateral intermuscular septum and enters the anterior compartment of the arm. It then divides in the cubital fossa into superficial and deep branches.'),
('2779b7ba-3ca3-59bf-8eb2-dcb1e5b18c71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial nerve in the radial (spiral) groove lies:', '["Outside triceps resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep to the medial head of triceps and triceps tendon","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial to triceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Piercing triceps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to the medial head of triceps and triceps tendon', 'In the spiral groove the radial nerve lies deep to the medial head of the triceps and close to the humerus, accompanied by the profunda brachii artery. This close relation explains radial nerve palsy in midshaft humeral fractures.'),
('a8cc55bb-d5d0-5f96-8566-0d6376259537'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The profunda brachii artery accompanies the radial nerve in the spiral groove and divides into:', '["Anterior and posterior descending branches","None","Multiple branches","Medial and lateral branches","Radial and ulnar branches"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior descending branches', 'The profunda brachii artery divides into anterior (radial collateral) and posterior descending (middle collateral) branches as it wraps around the humerus. These branches anastomose around the elbow with recurrent arteries.'),
('af97ddbb-1353-59fd-b2e5-d673038faef4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The profunda brachii artery anastomoses around the elbow with:', '["Only radial recurrent","interosseous recurrent","and ulnar recurrent arteries","Radial recurrent","Only interosseous recurrent"]'::jsonb, 'Radial recurrent', 'The anterior descending (radial collateral) branch of the profunda brachii anastomoses with the radial recurrent artery. Together with the other periarticular anastomoses this network preserves distal flow if the brachial artery is compressed.'),
('424fa255-c8b5-5d12-bde0-7843ca9b6251'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The collateral circulation around the elbow involves anastomoses between:', '["Only brachial and ulnar resulting in decreased functional activity","and interosseous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only brachial and radial mediated by specific receptor activation","Branches of brachial artery and recurrent branches of radial","ulnar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of brachial artery and recurrent branches of radial', 'Collateral circulation around the elbow forms between the superior and inferior ulnar collateral branches of the brachial artery and the recurrent branches of the radial, ulnar, and interosseous arteries. This anastomotic network maintains distal perfusion when the brachial artery is occluded.'),
('3102226c-19b7-5cd5-9c54-c8a94fb3ce85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The superior ulnar collateral artery is a branch of:', '["Radial artery","Profunda brachii","Brachial artery","Axillary artery","Ulnar artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery', 'The superior ulnar collateral artery arises from the brachial artery and descends with the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle. It anastomoses with the posterior ulnar recurrent artery.'),
('4b60f761-f16f-50fd-a467-b0ffbe405193'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The inferior ulnar collateral artery is a branch of:', '["Radial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulnar artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Profunda brachii mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachial artery (just above the elbow)"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery (just above the elbow)', 'The inferior ulnar collateral artery arises from the brachial artery just above the elbow and passes anterior to the medial epicondyle. It anastomoses with the anterior ulnar recurrent artery.'),
('acdea079-e0e2-5d0c-9195-55e345983f0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior interosseous artery is a branch of:', '["Ulnar artery directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Brachial artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radial artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior interosseous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common interosseous artery (from ulnar artery)"]'::jsonb, 'Common interosseous artery (from ulnar artery)', 'The posterior interosseous artery arises from the common interosseous artery, a short branch of the ulnar artery given off in the cubital fossa. It passes through the interosseous membrane to supply the posterior forearm.'),
('b9f08b1b-d83a-53d4-b8c2-88a375c06425'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anterior interosseous artery is a branch of:', '["Ulnar artery terminal","Brachial artery","Radial artery","Common interosseous artery","Posterior interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Common interosseous artery', 'The anterior interosseous artery arises from the common interosseous artery and descends on the interosseous membrane with the anterior interosseous nerve. Both anterior and posterior interosseous arteries therefore share the same parent vessel.'),
('568327b0-adfc-518f-802d-8647e4a3ed08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The posterior interosseous artery passes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Through the interosseous membrane to reach the posterior compartment","Through the anterior interosseous membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","Above supinator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the carpal tunnel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the interosseous membrane to reach the posterior compartment', 'The posterior interosseous artery pierces the upper part of the interosseous membrane to enter the posterior compartment of the forearm. It then descends between the superficial and deep extensor muscles.'),
('a2465976-20ee-575d-817c-96ebf1ee010b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The common interosseous artery arises from:', '["Brachial artery","Radial artery","Posterior interosseous","Anterior interosseous","Ulnar artery (in the cubital fossa)"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar artery (in the cubital fossa)', 'The common interosseous artery is a short branch of the ulnar artery arising in the cubital fossa. It quickly divides into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries.'),
('8218e4e6-5c67-5190-9914-475a7e9fe1b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anterior interosseous artery runs:', '["Between radius and ulna anteriorly","Accompanied by the anterior interosseous nerve","All of the above","On the anterior surface of the interosseous membrane","None"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The anterior interosseous artery descends between the radius and ulna on the anterior surface of the interosseous membrane, accompanied by the anterior interosseous nerve. Every listed statement about its course is true.'),
('30bc11bb-869d-5d08-af4f-5fc816e56536'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anterior interosseous artery terminates at:', '["The hand resulting in decreased functional activity","The wrist joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The elbow mediated by specific receptor activation","The proximal border of pronator quadratus (participating in carpal anastomosis)","Cubital fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal border of pronator quadratus (participating in carpal anastomosis)', 'The anterior interosseous artery ends at the proximal border of pronator quadratus, where it pierces the interosseous membrane and joins the carpal anastomosis. It does not reach the hand as a named trunk.'),
('6ebfa80f-b013-55bd-a9c3-1a7918064c84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radial artery gives off which recurrent branch?', '["Interosseous recurrent","Radial recurrent artery","None","Posterior interosseous","Ulnar recurrent"]'::jsonb, 'Radial recurrent artery', 'The radial artery gives off the radial recurrent artery near its origin, which ascends to anastomose with the radial collateral branch of the profunda brachii. The ulnar and interosseous recurrent branches belong to other vessels.'),
('e58703fd-b328-5c0e-b07d-8921578b1762'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The ulnar artery gives off which recurrent branches?', '["Interosseous recurrent resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common interosseous mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior and posterior ulnar recurrent","Radial recurrent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior ulnar recurrent', 'Near the elbow the ulnar artery gives off the anterior and posterior ulnar recurrent arteries, which ascend to anastomose with the inferior and superior ulnar collateral branches of the brachial artery. The radial recurrent belongs to the radial artery.'),
('e289fce5-091c-5d8c-9469-f67d704e4b23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The common palmar digital arteries arise from:', '["Superficial palmar arch","Deep palmar arch","Ulnar artery","Radial artery","Palmar metacarpal"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial palmar arch', 'The superficial palmar arch, formed mainly by the terminal part of the ulnar artery, gives rise to the common palmar digital arteries. These divide at the web spaces into proper palmar digital arteries.'),
('229e22e7-9c46-5ac0-bd80-1beb4ff5c640'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The proper palmar digital arteries arise from:', '["Common palmar digital arteries (at web spaces)","Deep palmar arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ulnar artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Radial artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial palmar arch directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common palmar digital arteries (at web spaces)', 'Each common palmar digital artery divides at the web space into two proper palmar digital arteries. These proper arteries then run along the sides of the fingers to supply the digits.'),
('47d204a7-2f9d-58ab-8e5b-70ff24e22e53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The palmar metacarpal arteries arise from:', '["Proper digital","Deep palmar arch","Radial artery","Superficial palmar arch","Common digital"]'::jsonb, 'Deep palmar arch', 'The palmar metacarpal arteries arise from the deep palmar arch, formed chiefly by the radial artery. They join the common palmar digital arteries near the web spaces.'),
('59944e7d-fdae-5c20-bc3d-76a55ead06ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The princeps pollicis artery arises from:', '["Brachial artery","Ulnar artery","Superficial palmar arch","Radial artery (as it enters the palm)","Common digital"]'::jsonb, 'Radial artery (as it enters the palm)', 'The princeps pollicis artery arises from the radial artery as it turns into the palm, passing between the first dorsal interosseous and adductor pollicis muscles. It supplies the thumb and gives off the radialis indicis artery.'),
('f086c9b8-ba23-56ad-bcb2-8c3e9b9abbcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The radialis indicis artery supplies:', '["The little finger resulting in decreased functional activity","The thumb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The middle finger mediated by specific receptor activation","The palm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The radial side of the index finger"]'::jsonb, 'The radial side of the index finger', 'The radialis indicis artery arises near the princeps pollicis and runs along the radial border of the index finger. Other fingers receive their proper digital arteries from the digital system.'),
('0fcb4574-0a43-5e40-9d91-cb327a195f70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Fracture of the surgical neck of humerus may injure which nerve?', '["Median nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulnar nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery","Musculocutaneous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery', 'The axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery wrap around the surgical neck of the humerus and are typically injured in fractures at this site, causing deltoid paralysis and loss of sensation over the shoulder. The other nerves are not in contact with the surgical neck.'),
('f6da5767-77eb-5b15-a1d1-a9d015ab72f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Fracture of the midshaft of humerus may injure which nerve?', '["Musculocutaneous","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (in spiral groove)","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (in spiral groove)', 'The radial nerve lies in the radial (spiral) groove on the posterior surface of the midshaft of the humerus and is the classic nerve injured by midshaft humeral fractures, producing wrist drop. The other nerves are not related to the midshaft.'),
('7e66d53a-5605-5856-a991-d86ee571c39c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Supracondylar fracture of humerus may injure which nerve?', '["Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous","Median nerve (most commonly)","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve (most commonly)', 'In a supracondylar fracture of the humerus, the median nerve is the most commonly injured, often with the brachial artery, producing weakness of thumb opposition and loss of sensation in the hand. The ulnar and radial nerves may also be affected but less often.'),
('c4c0e917-a262-5bbb-a912-91092ce0c61f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Fracture of the medial epicondyle may injure which nerve?', '["Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve', 'The ulnar nerve descends behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, so a fracture of the medial epicondyle injures it, causing clawing of the ulnar two fingers and sensory loss in the ulnar distribution. The other nerves do not cross the medial epicondyle.'),
('ccf2ca16-feb2-503a-9ae5-dd308ee880d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Colles'' fracture is fracture of:', '["Distal ulna resulting in decreased functional activity","Distal radius with dorsal displacement (dinner fork deformity)","Scaphoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal radius with volar displacement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radial head leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal radius with dorsal displacement (dinner fork deformity)', 'A Colles'' fracture is a fracture of the distal radius with dorsal displacement of the distal fragment, producing a dinner fork deformity, usually from a fall on an outstretched hand. Volar displacement would be a Smith''s fracture.'),
('c15db1c3-9310-5675-a1f1-2c923378bf50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Smith''s fracture (reverse Colles'') is:', '["Scaphoid fracture","Distal radius with dorsal angulation","Radial head fracture","Distal radius fracture with volar angulation","Distal ulna fracture"]'::jsonb, 'Distal radius fracture with volar angulation', 'A Smith''s fracture, or reverse Colles'', is a distal radius fracture with volar (palmar) angulation, typically from a fall on the dorsum of the hand. It is distinguished from Colles'' by the direction of displacement.'),
('c6d68800-174a-50d5-80e4-e551dc2a2607'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Barton''s fracture is:', '["Distal radius extra-articular resulting in decreased functional activity","Colles'' variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fracture-dislocation of the distal radius (intra-articular)","Scaphoid fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal ulna fracture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fracture-dislocation of the distal radius (intra-articular)', 'A Barton''s fracture is an intra-articular marginal fracture-dislocation of the distal radius in which the carpus displaces with the radial fragment. It is therefore a joint surface injury, unlike a simple extra-articular distal radius fracture.'),
('c651a532-43d3-5845-862b-344d85a5954a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Scaphoid fracture presents with:', '["Tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox","Elbow pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain on ulnar side mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deformity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox', 'A scaphoid fracture typically presents with tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox and pain on axial loading of the thumb; the fracture may be initially occult on plain radiographs. The other symptoms are not characteristic of this injury.'),
('00488822-529d-5aa9-bfa8-a2297e7068e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The most common complication of scaphoid fracture is:', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malunion mediated by specific receptor activation","Arthritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-union and avascular necrosis of proximal pole"]'::jsonb, 'Non-union and avascular necrosis of proximal pole', 'The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply entering from its distal pole, so a fracture, especially through the proximal pole, often leads to non-union and avascular necrosis of the proximal fragment. This characteristic complication distinguishes scaphoid injuries.'),
('3263c7de-673a-5477-bea0-8e148960653c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Gamekeeper''s thumb is:', '["Rupture of the ulnar collateral ligament of thumb MCP joint","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both a and c mediated by specific receptor activation","Skier''s thumb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rupture of radial collateral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rupture of the ulnar collateral ligament of thumb MCP joint', 'Gamekeeper''s thumb is a rupture of the ulnar collateral ligament of the metacarpophalangeal joint of the thumb, with its acute form being called skier''s thumb. The radial collateral ligament is not involved in this injury.'),
('93a44c46-b76d-55b4-8726-0d028fec576b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Bowler''s thumb is:', '["Tendinitis of thumb resulting in decreased functional activity","Neuroma of the ulnar digital nerve of thumb (due to compression)","Nerve entrapment at wrist mediated by specific receptor activation","Arthritis of thumb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroma of the ulnar digital nerve of thumb (due to compression)', 'Bowler''s thumb is a neuroma of the ulnar (medial) digital nerve of the thumb caused by chronic compression, typically from the thumb hole of a bowling ball. It presents with pain and paresthesia along the thumb.'),
('80a4393f-b0ce-57f7-b1e6-2225eff6ee67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Trigger finger is caused by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensor tendon rupture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arthritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Nerve compression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stenosing tenosynovitis of flexor tendon sheath"]'::jsonb, 'Stenosing tenosynovitis of flexor tendon sheath', 'Trigger finger (stenosing tenosynovitis) results from thickening of the flexor tendon sheath that mechanically blocks smooth tendon gliding, so the tendon snaps as it passes through the annular pulley. Extensor tendon rupture, arthritis, or nerve compression do not produce this mechanical locking.'),
('baba865c-4d6f-5750-962f-28078b6d52bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The median nerve compression in carpal tunnel syndrome causes:', '["numbness","Pain","Pain in the forearm","and tingling in radial 3.5 digits and thenar weakness","Pain in ulnar 1.5 digits"]'::jsonb, 'Pain', 'Compression of the median nerve in the carpal tunnel classically produces pain over the wrist and hand, often at night, together with numbness and tingling in the radial 3.5 digits and thenar weakness. Pain limited to the ulnar 1.5 digits indicates ulnar nerve involvement rather than the median nerve at the wrist.'),
('edd9d6bf-8dc5-580e-ae99-3dbbc9432180'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Phalen''s maneuver for carpal tunnel syndrome involves:', '["Flexion of elbow","None","Compression of forearm","Extension of wrist","Flexion of wrist for 60 seconds"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion of wrist for 60 seconds', 'Phalen''s maneuver reproduces carpal tunnel symptoms by holding the wrist in full flexion for 60 seconds, which raises pressure within the carpal tunnel. Wrist extension is a reverse Phalen test, not the standard maneuver, and elbow flexion or forearm compression test other sites.'),
('9da19e3d-64b5-56fd-b8c9-0a1b60c28431'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Tinel''s sign for carpal tunnel syndrome:', '["Percussion over median nerve at wrist elicits tingling","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Percussion over ulnar nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Percussion over radial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Percussion over median nerve at wrist elicits tingling', 'Tinel''s sign is positive when percussion over the median nerve at the wrist reproduces tingling in its distribution, indicating nerve irritation. Percussion over the ulnar or radial nerve would test different nerves and is not part of this sign.'),
('690a3208-2a5c-55a4-adda-c22b03aea3d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Cubital tunnel syndrome involves compression of which nerve?', '["Median nerve at elbow","Radial nerve at elbow","Median nerve at wrist","Ulnar nerve at the elbow (cubital tunnel)","Ulnar nerve at wrist"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve at the elbow (cubital tunnel)', 'Cubital tunnel syndrome is caused by compression of the ulnar nerve as it passes behind the medial epicondyle through the cubital tunnel at the elbow. The median and radial nerves are not compressed at this site, and ulnar compression at the wrist is Guyon''s canal syndrome.'),
('1fdeb37c-c6be-5df0-8686-4abba7408028'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Radial tunnel syndrome involves compression of:', '["Ulnar nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of radial)","Superficial radial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Median nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Musculocutaneous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of radial)', 'Radial tunnel syndrome compresses the posterior interosseous nerve, the deep branch of the radial nerve, near the elbow and produces lateral forearm pain. The superficial radial, ulnar, median, and musculocutaneous nerves are not involved in this syndrome.'),
('2b0efcf9-c411-5391-96c7-f4202b200833'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Wartenberg''s syndrome is:', '["Ulnar nerve compression resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Median nerve compression mediated by specific receptor activation","Radial nerve palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial radial nerve compression (cheiralgia paresthetica)"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial radial nerve compression (cheiralgia paresthetica)', 'Wartenberg''s syndrome (cheiralgia paresthetica) is compression of the superficial branch of the radial nerve, causing pain and paresthesia over the radial side of the hand. Ulnar or median nerve compression and complete radial nerve palsy produce different symptoms.'),
('16e256d6-712f-5e09-a17b-09e645c516a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor tendons of the hand form the:', '["Extensor expansion (dorsal hood) over the proximal phalanx","Palmar aponeurosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Common extensor sheath without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extensor retinaculum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor expansion (dorsal hood) over the proximal phalanx', 'The extensor tendons flatten into the extensor expansion (dorsal hood) over the proximal phalanx, which distributes their pull to the middle and distal phalanges. The palmar aponeurosis, common extensor sheath, and extensor retinaculum are separate structures.'),
('5e256426-188f-51ed-90d6-5896fd429985'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor expansion divides into:', '["Medial and lateral slips resulting in decreased functional activity","Only lateral bands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Central slip (inserts into middle phalanx) and two lateral bands (insert into distal phalanx)","Only central slip leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central slip (inserts into middle phalanx) and two lateral bands (insert into distal phalanx)', 'The extensor expansion divides into a central slip that inserts into the middle phalanx and two lateral bands that continue to insert into the distal phalanx. The other options describe an incomplete or incorrect division of the expansion.'),
('9d3bcc88-871e-5149-be88-8ed2c73ee044'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The central slip of the extensor expansion inserts into:', '["Base of the middle phalanx (dorsal)","Proximal phalanx","Base of distal phalanx","Metacarpal","Carpal bones"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the middle phalanx (dorsal)', 'The central slip inserts into the dorsal base of the middle phalanx, and its rupture produces a boutonniere deformity. The distal phalanx, proximal phalanx, metacarpal, and carpal bones are not its insertion sites.'),
('9576e929-67f1-5450-840f-bbbcb40dde0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The lateral bands of the extensor expansion insert into:', '["Base of distal phalanx (dorsal)","Base of middle phalanx","Carpal bones","Proximal phalanx","Metacarpal"]'::jsonb, 'Base of distal phalanx (dorsal)', 'The two lateral bands of the extensor expansion converge and insert into the dorsal base of the distal phalanx, and interruption produces a mallet or swan-neck deformity. The middle phalanx, proximal phalanx, metacarpal, and carpal bones are not their insertion sites.'),
('6c84db1a-9faa-55cc-9f87-205f1dd6ec86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) inserts into:', '["Metacarpal resulting in decreased functional activity","Base of distal phalanx (palmar aspect)","Base of middle phalanx mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximal phalanx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carpal bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of distal phalanx (palmar aspect)', 'Flexor digitorum profundus inserts into the palmar base of the distal phalanx and is the primary flexor of the distal interphalangeal joint. The middle phalanx receives flexor digitorum superficialis, not the FDP.'),
('3213a9c9-e164-549e-a3b7-9e99988e047a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) inserts into:', '["Metacarpal resulting in decreased functional activity","Carpal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base of distal phalanx mediated by specific receptor activation","Shaft of middle phalanx (by splitting around FDP)","Proximal phalanx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shaft of middle phalanx (by splitting around FDP)', 'Flexor digitorum superficialis inserts into the shaft of the middle phalanx after splitting around the FDP tendon at Camper''s chiasm. The distal phalanx, proximal phalanx, metacarpal, and carpal bones are not its attachment.'),
('5c8e1786-ec47-5860-96e3-7d20729e9133'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The FDS tendon splits (Camper''s chiasm) to allow:', '["Both tendons to cross resulting in decreased functional activity","FDP tendon to pass through to the distal phalanx","None mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FDS to insert into middle phalanx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'FDP tendon to pass through to the distal phalanx', 'At Camper''s chiasm the FDS tendon splits so that the FDP tendon can pass through it and continue to its insertion on the distal phalanx. This arrangement allows the two flexors to act on different phalangeal joints.'),
('04b787ed-18ee-52a8-82d4-a7a8f2420cf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor tendons enter the hand through:', '["Carpal tunnel (deep to flexor retinaculum)","Extensor compartments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Guyon''s canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Anatomical snuffbox without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carpal tunnel (deep to flexor retinaculum)', 'The long flexor tendons enter the hand through the carpal tunnel, passing deep to the flexor retinaculum. Guyon''s canal transmits the ulnar nerve and artery, and the extensor compartments and anatomical snuffbox carry extensor structures.'),
('7ecb94c6-5f5f-516b-a516-bdee7571f6b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor synovial sheaths in the hand include:', '["Only radial bursa resulting in decreased functional activity","Only ulnar bursa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both A (Only radial bursa) and B (Only ulnar bursa)","Ulnar (common) bursa and radial bursa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both A (Only radial bursa) and B (Only ulnar bursa)', 'The flexor synovial sheaths of the hand consist of the ulnar (common) sheath around the superficialis and profundus tendons and the radial sheath around the flexor pollicis longus, which communicate at the wrist in about half of individuals. Neither the radial bursa nor the ulnar bursa alone accounts for the full arrangement.'),
('e9ceffe9-cadb-5db8-bd1a-1b664758fe61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The synovial sheath of the little finger is continuous with:', '["The carpal tunnel resulting in decreased functional activity","The radial bursa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The median nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The ulnar bursa (common flexor sheath)","sheath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ulnar bursa (common flexor sheath)', 'The synovial sheath of the little finger is continuous with the ulnar bursa (common flexor sheath), so infection can spread proximally into the palm and wrist. The thumb sheath is continuous with the radial bursa, and the median nerve does not lie within a synovial sheath.'),
('649fa883-43c1-5f0e-8d8e-3de02827af5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The synovial sheath of the thumb is continuous with:', '["The radial bursa","sheath","The carpal tunnel","Ulnar bursa","None"]'::jsonb, 'The radial bursa', 'The synovial sheath of the thumb is continuous with the radial bursa surrounding the flexor pollicis longus tendon. The ulnar bursa and carpal tunnel are separate structures, although the radial and ulnar bursae communicate at the wrist in about half of individuals.'),
('dc40e164-caff-5ced-850b-c5ec1b08049c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The synovial sheaths of the index, middle and ring fingers extend:', '["From the neck of the metacarpal to the DIP joint","middle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Into the palm freely mediated by specific receptor activation","and ring fingers extend: without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To the wrist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the neck of the metacarpal to the DIP joint', 'The synovial sheaths of the index, middle, and ring fingers run from the neck of the metacarpal to the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint and end in the palm. In contrast, the sheaths of the thumb and little finger are continuous proximally with the radial and ulnar bursae.'),
('661bb2ea-bf79-5c1e-a283-deaccaff8d58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Infection of the thumb flexor sheath (radial bursa) can spread to:', '["The ulnar bursa (via communication at wrist in some)","Index finger only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only to thumb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forearm directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ulnar bursa (via communication at wrist in some)', 'Infection of the thumb flexor sheath (radial bursa) can spread to the ulnar bursa because the two bursae communicate at the wrist in about half of individuals, a pattern that can produce a horseshoe abscess. It does not normally spread only to the thumb or directly into the forearm.'),
('9dccda24-94e5-5b09-af3a-d766f11e5a23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'A horseshoe abscess involves:', '["Infection of a single digit resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection of the dorsum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection of the palm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection of both radial and ulnar bursae with communication"]'::jsonb, 'Infection of both radial and ulnar bursae with communication', 'A horseshoe abscess is infection of both the radial and ulnar bursae, which communicate at the wrist, so pus spreads in a horseshoe-shaped arc around the palm. Infection of a single digit, the palm, or the dorsum does not produce this pattern.'),
('13a4c177-f8c2-5572-aaf0-bc6e107d1d25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The web spaces of the hand are:', '["Between the wrist and hand resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the fingers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Potential spaces between the metacarpophalangeal joints","Between the palm and dorsum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potential spaces between the metacarpophalangeal joints', 'The web spaces are the fascial potential spaces between the metacarpophalangeal joints of adjacent fingers, containing fat and connecting the palm to the dorsum. They are not the interval between the palm and dorsum, the wrist and hand, or simply between fingers.'),
('bd3ecb97-95af-5ff7-a397-c2e51c69c99e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'A collar-button abscess is:', '["An infection of the web space (dumbbell-shaped)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An infection of the pulp mediated by specific receptor activation","An infection of the dorsum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An infection of the palm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An infection of the web space (dumbbell-shaped)', 'A collar-button (dumbbell) abscess is an infection of a web space that extends through the connecting fascial tunnel between the palmar and dorsal compartments, giving a narrowed waist. Pulp, palmar, and dorsal infections occur at different locations.'),
('b4728031-404c-5eae-ac16-486eaa27c211'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The dorsal subaponeurotic space of hand is located:', '["Deep to the extensor tendons (beneath the dorsal fascia)","Beneath the palmar aponeurosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial to extensor tendons mediated by specific receptor activation","In the thenar space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the web space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to the extensor tendons (beneath the dorsal fascia)', 'The dorsal subaponeurotic space lies deep to the extensor tendons beneath the dorsal fascia of the hand. It is not superficial to the tendons, beneath the palmar aponeurosis, or within the thenar or web spaces.'),
('0e113a1d-bc91-5c93-abe9-c7fe2c948c1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The thenar and midpalmar spaces are located:', '["Beneath the skin","In the hypothenar area","Superficial to the flexor tendons","In the thenar eminence","Deep to the flexor tendons (in the palm)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to the flexor tendons (in the palm)', 'The thenar and midpalmar spaces lie deep to the flexor tendons in the palm, separated by the fibrous septum attached to the third metacarpal. They are not superficial to the flexor tendons, beneath the skin, or confined to the hypothenar area.'),
('f3f011f0-a867-5c3a-8fbd-14ff4bbc34ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The hypothenar eminence contains which muscles?', '["opponens digiti minimi","Interossei","Thenar muscles","flexor digiti minimi brevis","Abductor digiti minimi"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor digiti minimi', 'The hypothenar eminence is formed by the abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, and opponens digiti minimi, all supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. The interossei and thenar muscles belong to different compartments.'),
('94e2bccf-247b-5d92-96fd-bc5ee0a96a75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The muscles of the thenar eminence are:', '["opponens pollicis","FPB (superficial head)","Interossei","Hypothenar muscles","APB"]'::jsonb, 'APB', 'The thenar eminence contains the abductor pollicis brevis (APB), flexor pollicis brevis, and opponens pollicis, all supplied by the recurrent branch of the median nerve. Interossei and hypothenar muscles are separate muscle groups.'),
('e1a69121-105a-5323-98bf-92d2ce09a2ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The adductor pollicis lies:', '["Deep (medial) to the thenar muscles (in the adductor compartment)","Lateral to thenar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the midpalmar mediated by specific receptor activation","In the interosseous compartment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial to thenar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep (medial) to the thenar muscles (in the adductor compartment)', 'The adductor pollicis lies deep and medial to the thenar muscles in its own adductor compartment and is supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. It is not lateral, superficial, or located in the midpalmar or interosseous compartments.'),
('fd68c067-2443-567a-9759-ae0229b79ef0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The adductor pollicis has how many heads?', '["2 (transverse and oblique)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (transverse and oblique)', 'The adductor pollicis has two heads: a transverse head arising from the third metacarpal and an oblique head from the capitate and bases of the second and third metacarpals. Descriptions of one, three, or multiple heads do not match its anatomy.'),
('94449361-8d52-5cef-ac91-f1355386f3a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Froment''s sign is used to test:', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Flexor pollicis longus","Thenar muscle function","Adductor pollicis (ulnar nerve function)"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor pollicis (ulnar nerve function)', 'Froment''s sign tests adductor pollicis function and therefore the integrity of the ulnar nerve; when pinching, weakness of adduction makes the patient flex the thumb using the flexor pollicis longus to compensate. The other thenar muscles are largely supplied by the median nerve.'),
('f74ff8d6-331d-57c0-9fd0-e06bfb4c13a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Positive Froment''s sign indicates:', '["De Quervain''s resulting in decreased functional activity","Median nerve palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carpal tunnel syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Ulnar nerve palsy (loss of adductor pollicis)","Radial nerve palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve palsy (loss of adductor pollicis)', 'A positive Froment''s sign indicates ulnar nerve palsy with loss of adductor pollicis function, as the patient compensates by flexing the interphalangeal joint of the thumb during attempted pinch. Median or radial nerve lesions and carpal tunnel syndrome do not produce this sign.'),
('d0488660-9a66-5de9-a729-0a378f46f779'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The extensor pollicis longus is the only extensor of the:', '["IP joint of thumb","CMC joint of thumb","Wrist joint","None","MCP joint of thumb"]'::jsonb, 'IP joint of thumb', 'Extensor pollicis longus is the only muscle that extends the interphalangeal (IP) joint of the thumb. The metacarpophalangeal and carpometacarpal joints of the thumb have additional extensors, and the wrist is extended by the wrist extensor muscles.'),
('6137c461-b62b-5d7f-b213-3af9240a83f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The flexor pollicis longus is the only flexor of the:', '["CMC joint of thumb","MCP joint of thumb","None","IP joint of thumb","Wrist joint"]'::jsonb, 'IP joint of thumb', 'Flexor pollicis longus is the only muscle that flexes the interphalangeal (IP) joint of the thumb. The metacarpophalangeal and carpometacarpal joints of the thumb are flexed by additional muscles, and the wrist is flexed by the wrist flexors.'),
('b1555f21-fa42-50e2-ad21-22d12a46cbf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The pisiform is a sesamoid bone in the tendon of which muscle?', '["Flexor carpi radialis","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Palmaris longus","Abductor digiti minimi","Extensor carpi ulnaris"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor carpi ulnaris', 'The pisiform is a sesamoid bone that develops within the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris. The other listed muscles do not contain the pisiform in their tendons.'),
('53f99d02-f7fa-51cd-983d-cfad4683ede7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The hamulus (hook) of hamate can be palpated:', '["At the ulnar styloid","In the proximal palm","In the anatomical snuffbox","At the radial styloid","medial to the pisiform"]'::jsonb, 'In the proximal palm', 'The hook (hamulus) of the hamate is palpated in the proximal palm, about 1 to 2 cm distal and lateral (radial) to the pisiform. The ulnar and radial styloids and the anatomical snuffbox lie at different sites.'),
('111a9c2f-ae4b-5d5c-93a8-96dc1401b522'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The carpometacarpal (CMC) joint of the thumb is what type of joint?', '["Pivot","Ball and socket","Plane","Saddle (sellar) joint","Hinge"]'::jsonb, 'Saddle (sellar) joint', 'The carpometacarpal joint of the thumb is a saddle (sellar) joint between the trapezium and the first metacarpal, whose mutually concave-convex surfaces permit wide mobility. Plane, hinge, pivot, and ball-and-socket joints have different articular shapes.'),
('d31ac1e1-7c8a-5c02-8232-25ea6eaceedb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The CMC joint of the thumb allows movements of:', '["Flexion","opposition","abduction","extension","adduction"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion', 'The saddle-shaped carpometacarpal joint of the thumb permits flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, opposition, and circumduction. Flexion is one of the movements allowed at this joint.'),
('70d2c1a0-5853-5602-963d-ed8fc7f45596'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Opposition of the thumb is the combined movement of:', '["adduction","and medial rotation","abduction","Extension","Flexion"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion', 'Opposition of the thumb is a composite movement that combines flexion, abduction, and medial rotation of the first metacarpal at the carpometacarpal joint. Flexion is a component of this combined movement.'),
('b7881719-2cef-5e66-886f-207f19ff67d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The opponents pollicis is the prime mover for:', '["Opposition of thumb","Abduction of thumb","Extension of thumb","Flexion of thumb","Adduction of thumb"]'::jsonb, 'Opposition of thumb', 'Opponens pollicis is the prime mover for opposition of the thumb, rotating the first metacarpal medially so the thumb tip meets the fingers. Abduction, adduction, extension, and flexion of the thumb are produced by different muscles.'),
('40f4feba-9cd1-5a20-b234-6d6c2e4e9e0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The anatomical snuffbox is bounded by:', '["FCR and PL","EPL (posterior)","EPB and APL (anterior)","All of the above","Both A and B"]'::jsonb, 'EPL (posterior)', 'The anatomical snuffbox is bounded anteriorly by the tendons of extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) and abductor pollicis longus (APL) and posteriorly by the tendon of extensor pollicis longus (EPL). The EPL tendon forms the posterior boundary, and the scaphoid and trapezium lie in the floor.'),
('4c158898-0f7c-5cee-b7ea-700cefe9ec98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'A scaphoid fracture can lead to:', '["Malunion only resulting in decreased functional activity","Compartment syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Non-union only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Avascular necrosis of the proximal pole"]'::jsonb, 'Avascular necrosis of the proximal pole', 'Blood reaches the proximal pole of the scaphoid retrograde from distal vessels, so a fracture can interrupt its perfusion and cause avascular necrosis of the proximal pole with delayed or non-union. Infection or compartment syndrome is not the characteristic complication of a scaphoid fracture.'),
('b5aa1f29-7b40-571b-bd99-4f8f158ee63c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'In a Colles'' fracture, the characteristic deformity is:', '["Garden spade deformity","Swan-neck deformity","Bayonet deformity","Boutonniere deformity","Dinner fork deformity (dorsal displacement)"]'::jsonb, 'Dinner fork deformity (dorsal displacement)', 'A Colles'' fracture of the distal radius displaces the distal fragment dorsally and proximally, producing a dinner fork deformity. Bayonet, garden spade, swan-neck, and boutonniere deformities result from different fractures or tendon problems.'),
('f75a72ce-d25a-5310-87b7-0b48082857e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The floor of the cubital fossa is formed by:', '["Supinator only","Triceps only","Brachialis and supinator","Brachialis only","Biceps"]'::jsonb, 'Brachialis and supinator', 'The floor of the cubital fossa is formed by the brachialis muscle medially and the supinator muscle laterally. Triceps, biceps alone, or either muscle alone does not form the complete floor.'),
('a7388165-6d26-5b9f-b439-91c7b136b703'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'During therapeutic management of Arm Forearm Wrist Hand, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('8517ee4f-48f9-5f24-abc6-08a9d4fb08c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Regarding clinical cases in Arm Forearm Wrist Hand: Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('7f0c6466-9849-52c7-bed5-e9b83d192cb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'In the study of Arm Forearm Wrist Hand, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('0489b7a2-3689-54de-90fd-a5b477a03833'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Arm Forearm Wrist Hand: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('cd4cd7e8-cac2-56b2-915e-5e5ababdce04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'The contents of the cubital fossa from medial to lateral are:', '["brachial artery","Median nerve","None of the above","biceps tendon","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'From medial to lateral, the contents of the cubital fossa are the median nerve, the brachial artery, and the biceps tendon. The median nerve is therefore the most medial structure lying within the fossa.'),
('75646883-b7a3-5161-bca7-007227880623'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'During therapeutic management of Arm Forearm Wrist Hand, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('32a869eb-6ea4-514d-b28c-f981c17f2fc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Arm Forearm Wrist Hand', 'Regarding clinical cases in Arm Forearm Wrist Hand: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('dda622f7-7095-5bdc-a6f2-b56e577409e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast extends vertically from which ribs?', '["2nd to 6th ribs","1st to 5th ribs","4th to 8th ribs","2nd to 4th ribs","3rd to 7th ribs"]'::jsonb, '2nd to 6th ribs', 'The breast extends from the 2nd to the 6th rib in the vertical axis. The other rib ranges place the breast either too high, too low, or too narrow.'),
('b3677b0a-3be6-5ffe-974d-c753c57b135c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast extends horizontally from:', '["Sternum to midaxillary line","Midclavicular to midaxillary line","None","Parasternal to posterior axillary line","Sternum to anterior axillary line"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum to midaxillary line', 'The breast extends horizontally from the sternum to the midaxillary line. The remaining choices describe broader or narrower horizontal boundaries.'),
('2453484f-21ab-55de-bab8-f359e7851478'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary tail (tail of Spence) pierces which fascia?', '["Clavipectoral fascia","Brachial fascia","Pectoral fascia","Superficial fascia","Axillary fascia (deep fascia)"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary fascia (deep fascia)', 'The axillary tail (tail of Spence) passes through a gap in the axillary fascia (deep fascia) to enter the axilla. Other fascia layers lie at different depths and are not pierced by the tail.'),
('6f28bd25-57a2-566a-b05c-a14f1a4a7cd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Where is the axillary tail (tail of Spence) of the breast primarily situated?', '["Upper arm region","Suprascapular region","Subscapular region","Infraclavicular region","Axilla (armpit)"]'::jsonb, 'Axilla (armpit)', 'The tail of Spence passes through the axillary fascia and comes to lie within the axilla (armpit). The other regions lie outside the axillary compartment.'),
('e7dcce34-3312-564f-a454-ddf2c20235b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast is composed of how many lobes?', '["20-25","5-10","25-30","10-15","15-20"]'::jsonb, '15-20', 'The breast contains 15-20 lobes, each drained by its own lactiferous duct. Counts outside this range do not match the standard lobar arrangement of the gland.'),
('73da37d1-a113-50a3-b7b4-9a1fe4e0b1ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Each lobe of the breast drains into:', '["Terminal ductal lobular unit","Lactiferous duct","Areolar glands","Sinus of lactiferous","Nipple pores"]'::jsonb, 'Lactiferous duct', 'Each of the 15-20 lobes drains into its own lactiferous duct, which then dilates near the nipple. The other options are downstream structures or subunits rather than the direct draining duct.'),
('380003ad-0704-5c88-b19a-96a4b4b2c962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lactiferous sinus is a dilation of:', '["Nipple","Lobules","Terminal ducts","Lactiferous duct near nipple","Areolar glands"]'::jsonb, 'Lactiferous duct near nipple', 'The lactiferous sinus is a dilation of the lactiferous duct just before the nipple. It is the widened portion of the duct, not a separate structure such as the nipple, lobules, or terminal ducts.'),
('be19753d-8777-56c0-bc61-abcfa9c64fed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nipple contains which type of smooth muscle?', '["Only circular","Circular and longitudinal","Only longitudinal","Spiral","None"]'::jsonb, 'Circular and longitudinal', 'The nipple contains smooth muscle arranged in both circular and longitudinal layers, allowing erection on stimulation. Choices limited to a single orientation are incomplete.'),
('12bcbd0a-2a30-5a58-a271-6e5dddf3970c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The areola contains which glands?', '["Sebaceous glands (Montgomery''s tubercles)","Sweat glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eccrine glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Mammary glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Apocrine glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sebaceous glands (Montgomery''s tubercles)', 'The areola contains sebaceous glands that appear as Montgomery''s tubercles, especially during pregnancy. Sweat, eccrine, apocrine, and mammary glands do not account for the Montgomery''s tubercles.'),
('4fc425eb-b64a-5de4-b94f-e9a585b1da74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast lies on which fascia?', '["Superficial pectoral fascia (deep to skin)","Axillary fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brachial fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","Clavipectoral fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep pectoral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial pectoral fascia (deep to skin)', 'The breast lies within the superficial fascia of the pectoral region (superficial pectoral fascia, deep to the skin). The deep pectoral fascia, axillary fascia, and clavipectoral fascia are deeper layers.'),
('e3a1daa8-ffc5-5b12-94da-db758e6b53e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The retromammary space lies between:', '["None","Breast and pectoralis major","Clavipectoral fascia and breast","Breast and deep pectoral fascia","Superficial fascia and skin"]'::jsonb, 'Breast and deep pectoral fascia', 'The retromammary space lies between the breast and the deep pectoral fascia, allowing movement. It does not sit between the other named fascial planes or the skin.'),
('29b62e84-85b7-55d7-bf19-b3bc39dc836b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The retromammary space allows:', '["Formation of axillary tail","Free movement of breast over deep fascia","Nipple erection","Attachment of breast to chest wall","Lactation"]'::jsonb, 'Free movement of breast over deep fascia', 'The loose retromammary space permits free movement of the breast over the deep fascia. Fixation, tail formation, nipple erection, and lactation involve other structures.'),
('2b57aef5-e297-5e33-8090-fe2112316f94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The suspensory ligaments of Cooper are:', '["Blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suspensory ligaments of axilla mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous bands from skin to deep fascia between lobules","Nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous bands from skin to deep fascia between lobules', 'Cooper''s suspensory ligaments are fibrous bands passing from the skin to the deep fascia and between lobules, supporting the breast. Blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves do not form these bands.'),
('533889dc-40a7-5b26-9037-7ef2bda11d6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Cooper''s ligaments attach to:', '["Deep fascia (pectoral fascia) and dermis","Ribs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia (pectoral fascia) and dermis', 'Cooper''s ligaments extend from the deep fascia (pectoral fascia) to the dermis of the skin. Ribs, sternum, and clavicle are not direct attachment points for these ligaments.'),
('ad961569-810e-518e-84b0-cfdff88e4476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The blood supply of the medial part of breast is from:', '["Internal thoracic artery (perforating branches)","Lateral thoracic artery","Subscapular artery","Posterior intercostal","Thoracoacromial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery (perforating branches)', 'The medial part of the breast receives blood from perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery. The other arteries supply the lateral, deep, or superolateral parts.'),
('ad1dceb7-dfce-5f87-82c6-a84aa845d160'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The blood supply of the lateral part of breast is from:', '["Internal thoracic resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral thoracic artery and thoracoacromial","Posterior intercostal mediated by specific receptor activation","Subscapular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior thoracic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral thoracic artery and thoracoacromial', 'The lateral part of the breast is supplied by the lateral thoracic artery, with contributions from the thoracoacromial artery. The remaining arteries serve the medial or deep parts.'),
('a345cf34-d18a-5f37-972b-2a75f7282d62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The blood supply of the deep part of breast is from:', '["Posterior intercostal arteries","Lateral thoracic","Subscapular","Thoracoacromial","Internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior intercostal arteries', 'The deep part of the breast is supplied by the posterior intercostal arteries. The other listed arteries are branches that supply the superficial medial or lateral parts.'),
('94d2595f-82c4-5444-bdf2-3b008df479e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The venous drainage of the breast forms:', '["Portal system resulting in decreased functional activity","A venous plexus around the areola","Deep veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcutaneous veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A venous plexus around the areola', 'The veins of the breast form an anastomotic venous plexus around the areola. Portal, deep, and subcutaneous veins are not formed by this superficial drainage network.'),
('4b9e7730-9165-5c5f-88a8-9b26eb3aae13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The major venous drainage of the breast goes to:', '["Internal thoracic vein","All of the above","Cephalic vein","Intercostal veins","Axillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Venous drainage of the breast reaches the axillary, internal thoracic, and intercostal veins, so all listed routes are involved. The cephalic vein is not a major breast drainage channel.'),
('b7aad108-bd9d-5913-b12b-8902f01f2891'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatic drainage of the lateral breast goes primarily to:', '["Axillary lymph nodes (pectoral group)","Parasternal nodes","Intercostal nodes","Supraclavicular nodes","Mediastinal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary lymph nodes (pectoral group)', 'Most lymph from the lateral breast drains to the pectoral (anterior) group of axillary lymph nodes. Parasternal, intercostal, supraclavicular, and mediastinal nodes drain other territories.'),
('65355911-1bc2-58a6-b67a-8de1d9081b32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatic drainage of the medial breast goes to:', '["Mediastinal nodes","Axillary nodes","Supraclavicular nodes","Intercostal nodes","Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes', 'Lymph from the medial breast drains primarily to the parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes. Axillary, supraclavicular, mediastinal, and intercostal nodes are not the main medial drainage.'),
('5b2230a7-0311-50dc-9c64-effdbaf92344'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The subareolar lymphatic plexus (Sappey''s plexus) drains:', '["Cooper''s ligaments","Nipple and areola","None","Retromammary space","Lobules"]'::jsonb, 'Nipple and areola', 'The subareolar plexus of Sappey drains lymph from the nipple and areola. Cooper''s ligaments, lobules, and the retromammary space are not drained by this subareolar network.'),
('95e0a837-88f0-5a75-87c5-ea77e906b951'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatics from the lower medial breast may cross midline and drain to:', '["Axilla","Liver","Mediastinum","Spleen","Contralateral breast"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral breast', 'Lymphatics from the lower medial breast may cross the midline and drain to the contralateral breast. Axilla, liver, mediastinum, and spleen are not the primary crossing sites.'),
('6e7b769a-f2cf-50fb-8bd5-289f6168023d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Lymph from the axillary nodes ultimately drains into:', '["Right lymphatic duct","Jugular trunk","Thoracic duct","Bronchomediastinal","Subclavian trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian trunk', 'Efferent lymphatics of the axillary nodes converge to form the subclavian trunk, which joins the venous system. The other trunks drain the head, neck, or body regions.'),
('5dce35ed-00b3-57c9-877d-abc5cdc6a16e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The supraclavicular nodes receive lymph from:', '["Lateral nodes","Central axillary nodes","Subscapular nodes","Apical axillary nodes","Pectoral nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Apical axillary nodes', 'The supraclavicular nodes receive lymph from the apical (most superior) group of axillary nodes. The other axillary node groups lie below the apex and drain through the apical nodes.'),
('7bccb3bb-ec21-5c41-a654-e2a1fe5569e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nerve supply of the breast is from:', '["C5-6 resulting in decreased functional activity","C3-4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T7-9 mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral and anterior cutaneous branches of T4-6","T1-3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and anterior cutaneous branches of T4-6', 'The breast receives sensory supply from the lateral and anterior cutaneous branches of the T4-T6 intercostal nerves. Cervical and upper thoracic levels do not supply the breast.'),
('149947bb-1567-5819-a5f9-d12056ab371e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nipple is supplied by:', '["T6 dermatome resulting in decreased functional activity","T8 dermatome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T2 dermatome mediated by specific receptor activation","C4 dermatome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T4 dermatome (4th intercostal nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'T4 dermatome (4th intercostal nerve)', 'The nipple lies at the T4 dermatome and is supplied mainly by the 4th intercostal nerve. The other dermatome levels do not match the nipple area.'),
('bef006d3-927a-570a-b36c-051d78bd96a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The male breast differs from female breast in that:', '["None","It has lactiferous ducts","It has areola","It has nipple","It has no glandular tissue"]'::jsonb, 'It has no glandular tissue', 'The male breast is rudimentary and contains no glandular tissue, only ducts and stroma. It still has a nipple and areola, so those choices do not distinguish it.'),
('be3daab7-0a1d-5d67-aaf4-3090bdd435ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Gynecomastia is:', '["Reduction of breast","Benign tumor","Inflammation of breast","Malignant tumor of breast","Enlargement of male breast"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of male breast', 'Gynecomastia is enlargement of the male breast, usually from hormonal imbalance. The other options describe reduction, inflammation, or neoplasia rather than benign male breast enlargement.'),
('e8103000-5c28-519b-ad45-698180952714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Where does the axillary tail of the breast (Spence''s tail) typically terminate?', '["Medial to the clavicle","Near the midaxillary line in the axilla","At the sternal border","In the posterior triangle of the neck","At the epigastric region"]'::jsonb, 'Near the midaxillary line in the axilla', 'The axillary tail (Spence''s tail) ends near the midaxillary line within the axilla. The clavicle, sternum, neck, and epigastrium are not termination sites.'),
('b70135ce-e979-5b9d-9e88-51a17b0cce34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast is situated over which muscles?', '["serratus anterior","rectus abdominis","Pectoralis major","external oblique","Pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis major', 'The breast overlies the pectoralis major, and also partly the serratus anterior and the external oblique sheath. Pectoralis major is the principal muscle beneath the breast.'),
('02bda29d-db33-5418-bd89-43c152aad757'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoralis major is separated from the breast by:', '["Clavipectoral fascia","Axillary sheath","Deep pectoral fascia","None","Superficial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Deep pectoral fascia', 'The deep pectoral fascia separates the breast from pectoralis major. The clavipectoral fascia and axillary sheath lie deeper and are not directly between the breast and muscle.'),
('ef3c6dbd-3f62-592f-aeb7-dadfb47b1cfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The suspensory ligament of axilla (clavipectoral fascia) is also called:', '["Lockwood''s ligament","Gerdy''s ligament","None","Cooper''s ligament","Campbell''s ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Campbell''s ligament', 'The suspensory ligament of the axilla is the lower edge of the clavipectoral fascia, also called Campbell''s ligament. Lockwood''s, Gerdy''s, and Cooper''s are distinct eponymous structures.'),
('665aa6e4-dbef-50bc-ba27-c7e115515a5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The blood supply from the internal thoracic artery enters the breast via:', '["5th to 7th resulting in decreased functional activity","4th to 6th accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2nd to 4th perforating branches","1st to 3rd without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3rd to 5th leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2nd to 4th perforating branches', 'Perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery, especially the 2nd-4th, supply the medial breast. Other intercostal levels do not provide these perforators.'),
('3f251071-88a0-5f1f-8e62-61b8218d2341'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lateral thoracic artery is a branch of which part of axillary artery?', '["Subclavian","Thoracoacromial","First part","Second part","Third part"]'::jsonb, 'Second part', 'The lateral thoracic artery arises from the second part of the axillary artery, along with the thoracoacromial and superior thoracic arteries. The other parts give different branches.'),
('deade4ab-20e4-5ef1-893d-0f368ad36e15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracoacromial artery is a branch of which part of axillary artery?', '["First part","Subclavian","Brachial","Second part","Third part"]'::jsonb, 'Second part', 'The thoracoacromial artery is a branch of the second part of the axillary artery. The first and third parts give rise to other branches.'),
('0d699574-ecbc-5e2d-8d45-32e97f4ce13c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The intercostal arteries that supply the breast are:', '["All intercostals","Subcostal","Superior intercostal","Posterior intercostals of 2nd-5th spaces","Anterior intercostals only"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior intercostals of 2nd-5th spaces', 'The breast is supplied by the posterior intercostal arteries of the 2nd-5th intercostal spaces. Other intercostal arteries do not contribute to the breast.'),
('a2d4f183-5768-5056-b5b7-e797e89b03b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The venous drainage to the internal thoracic vein ultimately drains into:', '["Axillary vein","Brachiocephalic vein","Subclavian vein","Superior vena cava","None"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic vein', 'The internal thoracic vein drains into the brachiocephalic vein. Axillary, subclavian, and SVC connections are not the direct tributary relationship.'),
('9f1f81cc-2463-5189-89f5-42d8d0729b40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary vein drains into:', '["Internal jugular","None","Subclavian vein","Brachiocephalic vein","Superior vena cava"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian vein', 'The axillary vein drains into the subclavian vein as it passes over the first rib. The other veins lie further centrally or on the opposite side.'),
('71ed5018-ce90-5624-aa48-867c7e5b2edc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymph from the upper outer quadrant of breast primarily drains to:', '["Pectoral group of axillary nodes","Parasternal nodes","Subscapular group","Central group","Apical group"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoral group of axillary nodes', 'Lymph from the upper outer quadrant drains chiefly to the pectoral (anterior) group of axillary nodes. The other node groups receive lymph from different territories.'),
('a63431a4-a2f9-5460-8e8d-918872a395d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymph from the lower inner quadrant of breast drains to:', '["Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes","Apical nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subscapular nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Axillary nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes', 'Lymph from the lower inner quadrant drains to the parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes. Axillary, subscapular, apical, and central nodes are not the main drainage for this quadrant.'),
('b14adab3-fe64-5422-b5e0-f41f775b248a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymph from the upper inner quadrant drains to:', '["Both parasternal and axillary nodes","Subscapular nodes","Pectoral nodes only","Apical nodes only","Central nodes only"]'::jsonb, 'Both parasternal and axillary nodes', 'Lymph from the upper inner quadrant drains to both the parasternal and axillary nodes. Options limiting drainage to a single node group are incomplete.'),
('447627bb-1091-58a1-88a2-f406527f18b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymph from the lower outer quadrant drains to:', '["Intercostal nodes","Axillary nodes (pectoral and subscapular)","Supraclavicular nodes","Mediastinal nodes","Parasternal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nodes (pectoral and subscapular)', 'Lymph from the lower outer quadrant drains to the axillary nodes, specifically the pectoral and subscapular groups. Intercostal, supraclavicular, mediastinal, and parasternal nodes do not receive this drainage.'),
('4cd5109f-fbf2-5c8b-9b48-37b0c14fce2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Lymphatic obstruction cancer causes:', '["Warmth resulting in decreased functional activity","Peau d''orange appearance of skin","Pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Redness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ulceration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peau d''orange appearance of skin', 'Lymphatic obstruction from cancer produces peau d''orange, an edematous skin with a pitted appearance. Warmth, pain, redness, and ulceration do not reflect lymphatic obstruction.'),
('3149bce4-2f42-5208-a513-c2cbefdc83ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The peau d''orange appearance is due to:', '["Inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Edema of areola accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Edema of skin with sparing of hair follicles","Fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Necrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Edema of skin with sparing of hair follicles', 'Peau d''orange results from skin edema that spares the hair follicle orifices, giving a pitted orange-peel look. Inflammation, fibrosis, necrosis, and areolar edema have different mechanisms.'),
('eaecf215-3583-5a8e-a575-f57a1c12402d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Retraction of the nipple cancer is caused by:', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Necrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Edema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infiltration of lactiferous ducts by tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Infiltration of lactiferous ducts by tumor', 'Retraction of the nipple occurs when tumor infiltration of the lactiferous ducts shortens and pulls the nipple inward. Infection, necrosis, fibrosis, and edema are not the usual cause.'),
('d8750684-1735-541d-91df-a3b69832791b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Peau d''orange is a sign of:', '["Locally advanced breast cancer","Infection","Benign tumor","Early breast cancer","Trauma"]'::jsonb, 'Locally advanced breast cancer', 'Peau d''orange is a sign of locally advanced breast cancer where skin lymphatics are obstructed. Infection, benign tumors, early cancer, and trauma do not produce this skin change.'),
('e77d78f3-06ea-57f4-93d2-7ee179cfa0f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary lymph nodes involved cancer are classified into how many levels?', '["5","4","2","3","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Axillary lymph nodes are classified into three levels (I-III) by their relation to pectoralis minor. The other numbers do not reflect the standard staging system.'),
('18b74e4f-f9f5-505c-9972-261a9249eadd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Level I axillary nodes are located:', '["Behind pectoralis minor","Above pectoralis minor","In the central axilla","Lateral (inferior) to pectoralis minor","At the apex"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral (inferior) to pectoralis minor', 'Level I axillary nodes lie lateral (inferior) to the lateral border of pectoralis minor. Positions behind, above, or at the apex describe other levels.'),
('851612e9-6674-573e-aca7-951627b660c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Level II axillary nodes are located:', '["At the apex of axilla","Below pectoralis minor","Lateral to pectoralis minor","Above pectoralis minor","Behind (deep to) pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Behind (deep to) pectoralis minor', 'Level II axillary nodes lie behind (deep to) pectoralis minor. The apex, and positions lateral or below the muscle, describe other levels.'),
('785d9d54-ac6a-5904-8968-4f4c87a893f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Level III axillary nodes are located:', '["Below pectoralis minor","Lateral to pectoralis minor","Above (superior) to pectoralis minor","Central axilla","Behind pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Above (superior) to pectoralis minor', 'Axillary nodes are grouped into three levels relative to pectoralis minor. Level I lies below the muscle, Level II behind it, and Level III (apical nodes) lies above it, receiving efferents from the lower levels.'),
('c00af81c-ab56-58ca-8693-05f69efbb9f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic (parasternal) nodes lie along:', '["Sternum","Aorta","Internal thoracic artery","Clavicle","Internal thoracic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The parasternal nodes run alongside the internal thoracic artery and vein, about 2 cm from the sternal edge. They drain lymph from the medial breast and deep anterior chest wall.'),
('63f74075-b8d5-5df4-80e1-2b090b6e8b3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic nodes drain into:', '["None","Jugular trunk","Bronchomediastinal trunk","Thoracic duct","Subclavian trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchomediastinal trunk', 'Efferents of the internal thoracic nodes pass to the bronchomediastinal trunk on each side. The right trunk ends in the right lymphatic duct and the left trunk in the thoracic duct.'),
('daabb4ff-1b61-5770-b4b9-8f8a5ae1fc5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The right bronchomediastinal trunk drains into:', '["Right lymphatic duct","Right subclavian vein","Superior vena cava","Thoracic duct","Right brachiocephalic"]'::jsonb, 'Right lymphatic duct', 'The right bronchomediastinal trunk collects lymph from the right thoracic cavity and internal thoracic chain. It terminates in the right lymphatic duct, which drains into the right venous angle.'),
('7e850e1c-c5b7-5506-88c4-d65e0a42bbf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The left bronchomediastinal trunk drains into:', '["Thoracic duct","Right lymphatic duct","Left subclavian vein","Superior vena cava","Left brachiocephalic"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic duct', 'The left bronchomediastinal trunk drains lymph from the left thoracic cavity and empties into the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct returns the lymph to the left venous angle.'),
('6b169b4c-ab40-5930-8d6c-b6cbc2ca06eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The intercostal lymph nodes receive lymph from:', '["Anterior chest wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper limb mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep part of breast and posterior chest wall","Abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep part of breast and posterior chest wall', 'The intercostal nodes lie near the posterior ends of the intercostal spaces. They receive lymph from the deep portions of the breast and from the posterior chest wall.'),
('e8e1007e-fa8a-5618-9e27-41367bda80d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The intercostal lymph nodes drain into:', '["Axillary nodes","Subclavian trunk","Thoracic duct","Parasternal nodes","Supraclavicular nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic duct', 'Efferents from the intercostal nodes pass upward along the vertebral column. On the left they drain into the thoracic duct.'),
('be9a4848-f9db-509f-ae6d-26e96398284e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatic vessels of the breast may cross the midline to drain into:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Contralateral breast and nodes","Mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipsilateral axilla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral breast and nodes', 'Lymphatic channels of the breast cross the midline through the subcutaneous tissue over the sternum. This allows spread of carcinoma to the contralateral breast and its nodes.'),
('d1b01a01-984e-5546-b3e0-ce2c49aa0ecd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Lymph from the breast can also reach the:', '["Cervical nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Popliteal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Subdiaphragmatic nodes (via rectus sheath)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subdiaphragmatic nodes (via rectus sheath)', 'Lymph from the medial breast may pass down along the rectus sheath to the subdiaphragmatic nodes. This pathway can lead to intra-abdominal lymphatic spread of breast carcinoma.'),
('39a2423a-afed-5105-8a29-0b12400d8153'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The subcutaneous lymphatic drainage of the upper limb that bypasses axilla goes via:', '["Radial vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulnar vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basilic vein route mediated by specific receptor activation","Median cubital vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cephalic vein route to supraclavicular nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic vein route to supraclavicular nodes', 'A few superficial lymphatics of the lateral upper limb accompany the cephalic vein and pierce the clavipectoral fascia to reach infraclavicular and supraclavicular nodes. These channels bypass the axillary group.'),
('e5491ac0-da2f-52ec-9553-df8329f21df8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic artery is a branch of:', '["External carotid","Axillary artery","Subclavian artery","Thoracic aorta","Innominate"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery. It descends behind the costal cartilages and ends at the sixth intercostal space.'),
('889dc2b5-a710-5a32-8ff9-86b3f6f58427'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic artery terminates at the level of:', '["4th intercostal space","Xiphoid","6th intercostal space","8th intercostal space","10th intercostal space"]'::jsonb, '6th intercostal space', 'The internal thoracic artery descends lateral to the sternum and ends at the sixth intercostal space. There it divides into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries.'),
('9b96b001-53ed-5da7-9b5a-b10ba1316c60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary lymph nodes receive what percentage of breast lymph?', '["50% resulting in decreased functional activity","100% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Approximately 75%","25% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","90% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 75%', 'The axillary nodes form the main lymphatic drainage of the breast, receiving about 75% of its lymph. Most of the remaining lymph drains to the parasternal nodes.'),
('0db667e0-1d82-53f3-b47c-66c918906fcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The parasternal nodes receive what percentage of breast lymph?', '["75% resulting in decreased functional activity","Approximately 20-25%","5% mediated by specific receptor activation","100% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","50% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 20-25%', 'The parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes receive about 20-25% of breast lymph, mainly from the medial half. The rest drains to the axillary nodes.'),
('4b8fc522-bda6-53fe-8434-39a0e36d9553'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The sentinel lymph node is the:', '["The largest node resulting in decreased functional activity","First lymph node receiving drainage from the tumor","The apical node mediated by specific receptor activation","The central node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Any enlarged node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First lymph node receiving drainage from the tumor', 'The sentinel node is the first lymph node that receives lymphatic drainage from a tumor site. Its histologic status predicts whether cancer has spread to the regional nodes.'),
('c43cf755-acb5-5740-aff4-c6ec728fa0da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy involves injection of:', '["Blue dye and/or radiolabeled colloid","Blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Saline mediated by specific receptor activation","Air without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contrast dye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blue dye and/or radiolabeled colloid', 'A sentinel node biopsy uses a blue dye and/or a radiolabeled colloid injected near the tumor. The tracer identifies the first draining node, which is then excised and examined.'),
('78e540f5-1732-5136-9a41-c8689fea60f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary tail of Spence pierces the deep fascia at the:', '["Posterior axillary fold","Central axilla","Apex of axilla","Anterior axillary fold","Lateral thoracic wall"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior axillary fold', 'The axillary tail of Spence is breast tissue that extends toward the axilla and pierces the deep fascia at the anterior axillary fold. It may be mistaken for an enlarged axillary node.'),
('26d3f13e-a933-5df0-96ad-47a8bda0f761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common site of breast cancer is:', '["Central (areolar)","Lower inner quadrant","Upper outer quadrant","Upper inner quadrant","Lower outer quadrant"]'::jsonb, 'Upper outer quadrant', 'The upper outer quadrant contains the greatest amount of breast tissue and extends into the axillary tail. It is therefore the most common site of breast carcinoma.'),
('7a51f4fa-c20a-53bd-8eb2-bd0c5cbdd3a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The second most common site of breast cancer is:', '["Lower outer quadrant","Central (nipple)","Lower inner quadrant","Upper inner quadrant","Axillary tail"]'::jsonb, 'Upper inner quadrant', 'The upper inner quadrant is the second most frequent location of breast carcinoma. Cancers here usually spread to the internal thoracic (parasternal) lymph nodes.'),
('f5080719-8aff-59bf-a355-f7938d4ac9c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The medial half of breast develops from:', '["Endoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Ectoderm of mammary ridge (milk line)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm of mammary ridge (milk line)', 'The mammary gland arises from the ectodermal mammary ridge (milk line). The medial half of the gland develops from the ectoderm of this ridge.'),
('4b891153-e0c1-527a-9916-1574439ea676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The mammary ridge (milk line) extends from:', '["Axilla to inguinal region","Clavicle to pubis","Sternum to vertebral column","Neck to umbilicus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Axilla to inguinal region', 'The mammary ridge forms from thickened ectoderm in the sixth week and runs from the axilla to the inguinal region. Accessory nipples can develop anywhere along this line.'),
('7865dfa9-9d94-5776-be84-7f0e26a7e609'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The mammary gland develops during which week?', '["10th week","12th week","6th week of IUL","8th week","4th week"]'::jsonb, '6th week of IUL', 'The primary mammary ridge appears during the sixth week of intrauterine life. Solid epithelial buds from this ridge later canalize to form the lactiferous ducts.'),
('a9bd28cc-d375-5792-88e3-90e3faca41aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The areolar smooth muscle fibers produce:', '["Areolar flattening resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxation of areola accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contraction of areola and nipple erection","Nipple inversion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contraction of areola and nipple erection', 'Smooth muscle fibers in the areola contract on stimulation. Their contraction narrows the areola and erects the nipple, helping the infant to grasp it during suckling.'),
('e37625ea-642f-5ec2-b355-2acb632ca59c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The myoepithelial cells of the breast are important for:', '["Milk production","Milk ejection (contraction)","Milk secretion","Milk absorption","Milk storage"]'::jsonb, 'Milk ejection (contraction)', 'Myoepithelial cells surround the alveoli and ductules of the breast. Their contraction in response to oxytocin squeezes milk toward the nipple, producing milk ejection.'),
('ad75e112-d329-56aa-a882-e18125990638'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The sympathetic nerve supply to the breast causes:', '["Contraction of smooth muscle in nipple and blood vessels","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Milk secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Dilation of vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Milk production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contraction of smooth muscle in nipple and blood vessels', 'Sympathetic fibers to the breast act on its smooth muscle. They contract the smooth muscle of the nipple and areola and cause vasoconstriction of breast blood vessels.'),
('b6f80130-617f-5344-a2fe-40a613c94f84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The parasympathetic nerve supply of the breast:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Causes milk secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Causes vasodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Is not known to have significant parasympathetic innervation","Causes milk ejection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is not known to have significant parasympathetic innervation', 'The breast has no significant parasympathetic innervation. Its secretory and ejection functions are controlled by hormones and sympathetic activity.'),
('561ba332-6f3c-5b9e-a335-36c00c8a3e4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The hormone responsible for milk production is:', '["Progesterone","Oxytocin","TSH","Estrogen","Prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin', 'Prolactin from the anterior pituitary stimulates the alveolar cells to synthesize milk. Its release is triggered by suckling and is tonically inhibited by dopamine.'),
('fb98ee67-c3a0-56c1-a018-012f1a9e4658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The hormone responsible for milk ejection is:', '["Oxytocin","Progesterone","Estrogen","Prolactin","ADH"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin', 'Oxytocin is released from the posterior pituitary during suckling. It contracts the myoepithelial cells of the breast, forcing milk from the alveoli into the ducts.'),
('47980046-a632-5497-adbd-d713a1fe6cbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The blood supply to the nipple and areola comes from:', '["Intercostal arteries","Internal thoracic artery","Lateral thoracic artery","Thoracoacromial","Subareolar plexus (circulus vasculosus)"]'::jsonb, 'Subareolar plexus (circulus vasculosus)', 'Branches of the internal thoracic, lateral thoracic, and intercostal arteries form a subareolar plexus around the nipple. This plexus (circulus vasculosus) supplies the nipple and areola.'),
('0d62f5d6-f9d6-5ccb-8d14-d1310706a6dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The superficial veins of the breast form a network known as:', '["Pampiniform plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circulus venosus (Haller''s plexus)","Sappey''s plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Batson''s plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circulus venosus (Haller''s plexus)', 'The superficial veins of the breast converge into a network around the nipple. This network is called the circulus venosus, or Haller''s plexus.'),
('54ebb230-90f3-526a-8a59-0dabc38e02a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The deep veins of the breast drain into:', '["and intercostal veins","axillary","Only internal thoracic","Only axillary","Internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic', 'The deep veins of the breast accompany their corresponding arteries. They drain chiefly into the internal thoracic and the intercostal veins.'),
('8a229f03-bfb4-559e-a9d1-ad9871fc3b21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The intercostal veins draining the breast connect with:', '["Superior vena cava resulting in decreased functional activity","Azygos system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior vena cava mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson''s plexus)"]'::jsonb, 'Internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson''s plexus)', 'The posterior intercostal veins connect with the azygos system and also with the internal vertebral venous plexus. The latter communication (Batson''s plexus) permits spread of tumor to the vertebral column.'),
('176a04f3-fb74-5a8f-b5d5-155ab43896ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Batson''s venous plexus provides a route for metastasis to:', '["Breast resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lungs mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertebral column and brain"]'::jsonb, 'Vertebral column and brain', 'Batson''s plexus is a valveless vertebral venous network connected to the intercostal veins. It offers a pathway for breast carcinoma to metastasize to the vertebral column and brain.'),
('943a8b04-106a-50f2-aa71-3f29ef379b3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatic vessels of the skin over the breast drain into:', '["None","Intercostal nodes","Supraclavicular nodes","Inguinal nodes","Axillary and parasternal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary and parasternal nodes', 'Lymphatics from the skin overlying the breast pass mainly to the axillary nodes. Medial channels also drain toward the parasternal nodes.'),
('7d505762-51e1-5022-b684-ff7b4afcde3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The superficial fascia of the breast (Cooper''s ligaments) form:', '["Ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrous septa between lobules","Sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveoli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous septa between lobules', 'The superficial fascia of the breast sends septa between the glandular lobules. These fibrous septa are the suspensory ligaments of Cooper.'),
('214653b8-084c-5a1b-833b-6b46c4199d5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The retromammary space is also known as:', '["Submammary space","Retromammary bursa","None","Deep mammary space","Prepectoral space"]'::jsonb, 'Retromammary bursa', 'The retromammary space lies between the breast and the pectoral fascia. It contains loose connective tissue and is also called the retromammary bursa.'),
('99222526-d36a-5c44-bd5f-3f2b95149d72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The relative lack of mobility of the breast over the chest wall indicates:', '["Fibrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Carcinoma infiltration of Cooper''s ligaments","Edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Benign tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carcinoma infiltration of Cooper''s ligaments', 'Reduced mobility of the breast over the chest wall results when carcinoma infiltrates the suspensory ligaments of Cooper. Tethering of the gland to the skin and pectoral fascia limits its movement.'),
('26b38afc-ed53-55e8-a7dd-e41ec425a299'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The dimpling of skin over a breast mass suggests:', '["Mastitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Fat necrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infiltration of Cooper''s ligaments by carcinoma","Cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibroadenoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infiltration of Cooper''s ligaments by carcinoma', 'Skin dimpling over a breast mass occurs when carcinoma invades the suspensory ligaments of Cooper. Shortening of these ligaments retracts the skin at its attachments.'),
('98766ae6-2ec8-50c8-b941-f74f5394db4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoralis major muscle forms which part of axilla?', '["Floor","Anterior wall","Posterior wall","Medial wall","Lateral wall"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall', 'The pectoralis major and its covering fascia form the anterior wall of the axilla. The posterior wall is formed by latissimus dorsi, teres major, and subscapularis.'),
('7dc9749e-f077-5bdd-860e-f5f0f1082f3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoralis minor lies deep to which muscle?', '["Deltoid","Serratus anterior","Pectoralis major","Subclavius","Latissimus dorsi"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis major', 'The pectoralis minor lies deep to the pectoralis major. It runs from the coracoid process down to the third to fifth ribs.'),
('67549ef0-5162-5418-8912-92091936529f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The subclavius muscle lies:', '["Above the clavicle","In the arm","In the pectoral region","Below the clavicle","In the axilla"]'::jsonb, 'Below the clavicle', 'The subclavius is a small muscle lying below the clavicle. It passes from the first rib and its costal cartilage to the inferior surface of the clavicle.'),
('6decdf81-eb37-5ba3-b1ce-c968e51a7e63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The clavipectoral fascia encloses which two muscles?', '["None","Subclavius and serratus","Pectoralis major and minor","Subclavius and pectoralis minor","Serratus anterior and pectoralis"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavius and pectoralis minor', 'The clavipectoral fascia splits to enclose the subclavius and pectoralis minor. It spans from the clavicle above to the axillary fascia below.'),
('ebfdc2ab-91c5-51f5-8a0f-0a4f5f26d431'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The clavipectoral fascia is attached superiorly to:', '["Clavicle","Coracoid process","Acromion","Sternum","First rib"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle', 'Superiorly, the clavipectoral fascia is attached to the clavicle. Inferiorly, it blends with the axillary fascia, and it also attaches to the costal cartilages and the coracoid process.'),
('dfb5ea32-7476-545a-be3a-0d552af459a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The clavipectoral fascia is attached inferiorly to:', '["Serratus anterior","Pectoralis major","Deep fascia of arm","Axillary fascia","None"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary fascia', 'Inferiorly, the clavipectoral fascia blends with the axillary fascia at the lower border of pectoralis minor. It is pierced by the cephalic vein, thoracoacromial vessels, and lateral pectoral nerve.'),
('0014e4d3-cf0a-5b32-941e-2a0450eeeb89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The clavipectoral fascia is attached medially to:', '["Clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Serratus anterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Costocartilages of first rib and sternum","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coracoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Costocartilages of first rib and sternum', 'Medially, the clavipectoral fascia attaches to the costal cartilages of the first rib and the sternum. This medial anchoring helps support the axilla.'),
('c3ff8618-8fd0-59ba-a962-e986e62040a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The clavipectoral fascia is attached laterally to:', '["Glenoid cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coracoclavicular ligament and coracoid process","Clavicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acromion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coracoclavicular ligament and coracoid process', 'Laterally, the clavipectoral fascia is attached to the coracoid process and the coracoclavicular ligament. From there it continues downward to the axillary fascia.'),
('f791b47e-c3f9-59fc-bfca-32a1fa114b61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoral fascia is continuous with:', '["Superficial fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","Abdominal fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary fascia and clavipectoral fascia","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachial fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary fascia and clavipectoral fascia', 'The pectoral fascia overlies the pectoralis major and is continuous with the axillary fascia and the clavipectoral fascia. It provides a connective tissue covering for the pectoral region.'),
('589a4385-643c-5c60-b5b6-762e1219b24b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The upper border of pectoralis minor corresponds to which rib?', '["4th rib","3rd rib","2nd rib","1st rib","5th rib"]'::jsonb, '3rd rib', 'The upper border of pectoralis minor lies at the level of the third rib. The muscle thus overlies the third to fifth ribs.'),
('960dac54-062b-5a09-96a2-def2f2a690c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lower border of pectoralis minor corresponds to which rib?', '["7th rib","4th rib","5th rib","3rd rib","6th rib"]'::jsonb, '5th rib', 'The lower border of pectoralis minor reaches the fifth rib. The muscle''s relationship to the third to fifth ribs is used to define the levels of the axillary lymph nodes.'),
('7d6f4a90-e888-5796-9d52-43f971b8137a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The blood supply of the pectoralis major muscle includes:', '["Subscapular only","lateral thoracic","superior thoracic","Posterior intercostals only","Thoracoacromial"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracoacromial', 'The pectoralis major receives its principal blood supply from the thoracoacromial artery, a branch of the axillary artery. Its pectoral branch is the dominant source.'),
('dd1c9109-5c0b-59ea-8f56-b6de0f962578'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nerve supply of the pectoralis major is:', '["Only medial pectoral","Thoracodorsal","Lateral and medial pectoral nerves","Only lateral pectoral","Subscapular"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'Pectoralis major receives dual innervation: the lateral pectoral nerve (from the lateral cord) supplies the clavicular head while the medial pectoral nerve (from the medial cord) supplies the sternocostal head. The thoracodorsal nerve serves latissimus dorsi, the subscapular nerves serve subscapularis and teres major, and a single-nerve option is incomplete.'),
('fa36e6e1-b787-5697-a9fa-b9cab13d6c5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nerve supply of pectoralis minor is:', '["Long thoracic resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral pectoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suprascapular mediated by specific receptor activation","Both without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial pectoral nerve (pierces it)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial pectoral nerve (pierces it)', 'The medial pectoral nerve arises from the medial cord and pierces pectoralis minor, being its main supply, and then continues to the sternocostal part of pectoralis major. The long thoracic nerve serves serratus anterior, the suprascapular nerve supplies the supra- and infraspinatus, and the lateral pectoral nerve principally supplies pectoralis major.'),
('cace42b9-5e06-5f1c-97b4-b75868700e58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoral branch of thoracoacromial artery supplies:', '["Only pectoralis major","Only pectoralis minor","Serratus anterior","Latissimus dorsi","Both pectoralis major and minor"]'::jsonb, 'Both pectoralis major and minor', 'The pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial artery runs downward between the pectoral muscles and supplies both pectoralis major and minor. Serratus anterior is supplied by the lateral thoracic artery, and latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal artery.'),
('ef1892af-ad92-5048-a3ea-58f99be39f0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The sternocostal part of pectoralis major originates from:', '["Rectus sheath resulting in decreased functional activity","External oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Sternum and costal cartilages of ribs 1-6","Clavicle only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum and costal cartilages of ribs 1-6', 'The sternocostal head of pectoralis major arises from the sternum and the costal cartilages of the upper six ribs (1-6). The clavicular head arises from the medial third of the clavicle, while the rectus sheath and external oblique belong to the abdominal wall.'),
('e5037208-9ef1-5a7e-a9eb-593201b82abd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The clavicular head of pectoralis major originates from:', '["Coracoid","Medial third of clavicle","Sternum","Lateral third of clavicle","Acromion"]'::jsonb, 'Medial third of clavicle', 'The clavicular head of pectoralis major takes origin from the medial third of the clavicle. The sternocostal head arises from the sternum and costal cartilages of ribs 1-6, and the coracoid, acromion and lateral third of the clavicle carry other attachments.'),
('27d04f2c-799b-58fc-8d67-95f83ebb4f9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The insertion of pectoralis major into the humerus is:', '["Medial lip resulting in decreased functional activity","Greater tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral lip of intertubercular sulcus (crest of greater tubercle)","Lesser tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Floor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral lip of intertubercular sulcus (crest of greater tubercle)', 'Pectoralis major inserts by a bilaminar tendon into the lateral lip of the intertubercular (bicipital) sulcus, forming the crest of the greater tubercle. The medial lip receives latissimus dorsi, and the floor of the sulcus receives teres major.'),
('cf8ffa49-a7ed-56de-9952-0d9940a2e1db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The serratus anterior muscle originates from which ribs?', '["1-8 (upper 8 ribs)","3-8 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2-7 mediated by specific receptor activation","1-6 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1-4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1-8 (upper 8 ribs)', 'Serratus anterior arises by a series of digitations from the outer surfaces of the upper eight ribs (1-8). The other rib ranges overstate or understate the true extent of its origin.'),
('f778172a-d9f7-5cf5-aa42-46d6aa70d732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The serratus anterior inserts into:', '["Acromion resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral border accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial border (costal surface) of scapula","Superior angle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferior angle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial border (costal surface) of scapula', 'Serratus anterior inserts into the costal (deep) surface of the medial border of the scapula, with its strongest attachment at the inferior angle that helps rotate the scapula upward. The lateral border, acromion and angles do not receive its insertion.'),
('263c6162-d889-5ccd-9fd3-8ac7dcfbe974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The serratus anterior is supplied by:', '["Thoracodorsal","Accessory","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Suprascapular","Dorsal scapular"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) descends on the superficial surface of serratus anterior and is its sole nerve supply. The thoracodorsal nerve serves latissimus dorsi, the accessory nerve supplies sternocleidomastoid and trapezius, the suprascapular nerve serves supra- and infraspinatus, and the dorsal scapular nerve supplies the rhomboids and levator scapulae.'),
('bbcd9d68-8b99-52cb-afc3-c48d5f1dff7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The long thoracic nerve lies on the surface of:', '["Latissimus dorsi","Pectoralis major","Subscapularis","Intercostals","Serratus anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The long thoracic nerve runs directly on the superficial surface of serratus anterior after descending behind the brachial plexus. None of the other muscles listed carries this nerve on its surface.'),
('d4db5735-d0c9-53ad-a028-065e8a85a310'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Paralysis of serratus anterior causes:', '["Wrist drop resulting in decreased functional activity","Shoulder drop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Winging of scapula (scapula alata)","Claw hand without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foot drop leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Winging of scapula (scapula alata)', 'Paralysis of serratus anterior (long thoracic nerve injury) removes scapular stabilization, so the medial border and inferior angle protrude from the chest wall, producing winging of the scapula. Wrist drop, claw hand and foot drop indicate radial, median/ulnar and common peroneal nerve lesions respectively.'),
('42bc214c-4dbd-57c9-986e-3a5fbf92f123'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Winging of scapula is accentuated when:', '["Ptient raises arms overhead","Patient shrugs shoulders","Patient bends forward","Patient flexes elbow","Patient pushes against a wall with both arms"]'::jsonb, 'Patient pushes against a wall with both arms', 'Winging of the scapula becomes most obvious when the patient pushes against a wall with both arms, because this action recruits serratus anterior to protract and stabilize the scapula. The other maneuvers do not load scapular protraction in the same way.'),
('c035615e-d251-5c8a-810f-e30ac1a0af2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The external oblique muscle of abdomen forms the:', '["Lateral part resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior part of anterior chest wall (breast region)","Superior part mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior part of anterior chest wall (breast region)', 'Superiorly, the external oblique of the abdomen extends fibers onto the inferior part of the anterior chest wall beneath the breast region. It does not form the lateral, superior or medial parts of this region.'),
('8297367d-5fbc-5b85-8af6-ed60bd616acf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The rectus abdominis muscle is related to the breast:', '["Posteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","Superiorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferomedially (by its upper part)","Laterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferomedially (by its upper part)', 'The rectus abdominis lies inferomedial to the breast, with its upper part adjoining the gland from below and medially. It bears no posterior, superior or lateral relation to the breast.'),
('110ab6e2-9e51-5d60-8e75-cb1c7466bd38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast of a nulliparous woman is:', '["All glandular","All fat","Predominantly fatty","Predominantly glandular with little fat","Equally glandular and fatty"]'::jsonb, 'Predominantly glandular with little fat', 'In a nulliparous woman the breast is predominantly glandular with only a small amount of fat, giving it a firm consistency. With advancing age and parity the glandular tissue is progressively replaced by fat.'),
('b0c1c4d2-5c3a-55ee-965b-9acbe7653500'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast enlarges during pregnancy due to:', '["Edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased fat deposition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperplasia and hypertrophy of glandular tissue","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vascular congestion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperplasia and hypertrophy of glandular tissue', 'During pregnancy, estrogen and progesterone induce hyperplasia and hypertrophy of the lobuloalveolar (glandular) tissue, causing breast enlargement. Edema, increased fat deposition and mere vascular congestion are not the primary mechanism.'),
('78c10a06-b4f0-536c-bc19-95e93794e7fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Colostrum is secreted during:', '["At menopause resulting in decreased functional activity","Late pregnancy and first few days postpartum","Throughout pregnancy mediated by specific receptor activation","During menstruation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","After weaning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Late pregnancy and first few days postpartum', 'Colostrum is secreted during late pregnancy and for the first few days after delivery before mature milk appears. It is not produced at other times such as menopause, menstruation, throughout pregnancy or after weaning.'),
('bb402570-57c1-5133-92b6-3f9f4af77277'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Colostrum differs from mature milk by having:', '["More carbohydrate","More fat and less protein","More protein and less fat","More vitamins","Less water"]'::jsonb, 'More protein and less fat', 'Colostrum contains more protein (notably immunoglobulins) and minerals but less fat and carbohydrate than mature milk. The other compositional statements do not describe colostrum.'),
('713557b8-9e5c-5b54-9ce9-de0649e2a51e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lactating breast produces approximately how much milk per day?', '["100-200 mL","600-1000 mL","1000-1500 mL","1500-2000 mL","200-400 mL"]'::jsonb, '600-1000 mL', 'A lactating breast produces roughly 600-1000 mL of milk per day to meet the infant''s needs. Volumes well below or above this range do not represent normal lactation.'),
('97e461c2-1fc5-5e82-9b6c-2908051f362a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The cessation of milk production after weaning is due to:', '["Oxytocin deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Progesterone excess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of prolactin stimulation and involution","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Estrogen deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of prolactin stimulation and involution', 'After weaning, milk production ceases because prolactin stimulation is lost and the involuting glandular tissue stops synthesizing milk. Oxytocin deficiency only affects milk ejection, not synthesis, and sex-steroid changes play no primary role.'),
('7d902294-7ba2-5ecd-a986-b4e1e47a3751'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The male breast is characterized by:', '["Complete glandular tissue","Rudimentary ducts","alveoli","areola","Vestigial nipples"]'::jsonb, 'Rudimentary ducts', 'The male breast remains rudimentary, containing only a few small ducts without any acinar (glandular) development. Complete glandular tissue, alveoli and areolae do not characterize the normal male breast.'),
('57cc95de-1c67-5a6f-a12f-d0b2f9318a8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Gynecomastia can be caused by:', '["Estrogen excess","androgen deficiency","drugs","Estrogen deficiency","Androgen excess"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen excess', 'Gynecomastia results from a relative or absolute excess of estrogen acting on the male breast, whether endogenous or drug-induced. Androgen excess opposes estrogenic stimulation, and estrogen deficiency produces no enlargement.'),
('822224d6-b476-508a-b2cc-938db9d05e2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common breast tumor is:', '["Fibrocystic disease","Ductal carcinoma","Lobular carcinoma","Phyllodes tumor","Fibroadenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroadenoma', 'Fibroadenoma is the most common benign breast tumor, typically seen in young women. Ductal and lobular carcinoma and phyllodes tumor are far less frequent.'),
('db5927bc-db51-5fb4-94a0-5acfeaf57768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common malignant breast tumor is:', '["Lobular carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Tubular carcinoma","Infiltrating ductal carcinoma","Mucinous carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Infiltrating ductal carcinoma', 'Infiltrating (invasive) ductal carcinoma is the most common malignant breast tumor, accounting for the majority of breast cancers. Lobular, medullary, tubular and mucinous carcinomas are rarer subtypes.'),
('2f87fd3a-013b-54fe-b875-ff7d1ffe8fa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Paget''s disease of the nipple is associated with:', '["Underlying ductal carcinoma (DCIS)","Fibroadenoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sarcoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobular carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phyllodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Underlying ductal carcinoma (DCIS)', 'Paget disease of the nipple is almost always associated with an underlying ductal carcinoma (commonly DCIS), with tumor cells extending along the lactiferous ducts to the nipple epidermis. Fibroadenoma, sarcoma, lobular carcinoma and phyllodes tumor do not share this ductal pathway.'),
('b6b1e1ab-38db-5f84-a4b8-ab067443988e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Inflammatory breast cancer presents with:', '["Nipple discharge","Erythema","Lump","and peau d''orange","edema"]'::jsonb, 'Erythema', 'Inflammatory breast cancer classically presents with erythema and peau d''orange of the skin rather than a discrete lump, owing to tumor emboli blocking dermal lymphatics. The other options are not the defining presentation.'),
('45376871-579f-5c62-9c47-b6d77fde2ea1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary nodes are enlarged cancer due to:', '["Reactive hyperplasia","Inflammation","All","Infection","Metastasis"]'::jsonb, 'Metastasis', 'Axillary node enlargement in breast carcinoma results from lymphatic metastasis of tumor cells. Reactive hyperplasia, inflammation and infection are not the basis of nodal enlargement in cancer.'),
('6e9026e3-d7e0-5f14-9b8c-d0179aaec3bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoral group of axillary nodes receives lymph from the:', '["Neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper limb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior chest wall including breast","Posterior chest wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior chest wall including breast', 'The pectoral (anterior) group of axillary nodes lies along the lower border of pectoralis minor and receives lymph from the anterior chest wall, including most of the breast. Lymph from the neck, upper limb, abdomen and posterior chest wall reaches other node groups.'),
('eb7d7b35-3bef-538b-b149-7b2d5f1e27f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The subscapular group of axillary nodes receives lymph from:', '["Neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Breast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior chest wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper limb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior thoracic wall and scapular region"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior thoracic wall and scapular region', 'The subscapular (posterior) group of axillary nodes drains the posterior thoracic wall and the scapular region. Lymph from the breast goes mainly to the pectoral group and lymph from the upper limb to the lateral (humeral) group.'),
('3c453797-f20d-5b67-929c-777ebd21c1c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The central group of axillary nodes receives lymph from:', '["subscapular","Only subscapular","Only pectoral","and lateral groups","Pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoral', 'The central axillary group collects efferents from the anterior (pectoral), posterior (subscapular) and lateral (humeral) peripheral groups; among the choices, the pectoral group drains into it. It does not receive lymph directly from remote regions.'),
('6823fed3-1be1-525a-8fc9-b7af0e367480'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The apical group of axillary nodes receives lymph from:', '["Pectoral group","Lateral group","Peripheral groups directly","Central group","Subscapular group"]'::jsonb, 'Central group', 'The central group of axillary nodes drains upward into the apical group at the apex of the axilla. Efferents of the apical nodes then form the subclavian trunk.'),
('618bf3e2-0703-5bcf-bdea-1bd2567b699e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The apical group lies at:', '["Central axilla","Below pectoralis minor","Along axillary vein","Along lateral thoracic artery","Apex of axilla above pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of axilla above pectoralis minor', 'The apical group of axillary nodes lies at the apex of the axilla, above the upper border of pectoralis minor near the first rib. The other locations describe the central, humeral and pectoral groups.'),
('42c2b345-a3f9-5092-9540-3cff9660e942'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The subclavian trunk is formed by efferents from:', '["Central nodes","Apical axillary nodes","Pectoral nodes","Subscapular nodes","Lateral nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Apical axillary nodes', 'The subclavian lymphatic trunk is formed from the efferents of the apical axillary nodes; on the right it ends at the venous angle and on the left it joins the thoracic duct. The lower node groups do not directly form this trunk.'),
('f5ab2de2-5ed6-5456-8232-3874ad196478'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The right subclavian trunk may drain directly into:', '["Right subclavian vein","Right brachiocephalic vein","Right lymphatic duct","Superior vena cava","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Right subclavian vein', 'On the right side the subclavian trunk may drain directly into the right subclavian vein or may join the right lymphatic duct. The brachiocephalic vein, superior vena cava and thoracic duct are not its direct termination.'),
('f6f39c1e-da10-59f0-ae18-cf002fe00044'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracic duct drains into the:', '["Left subclavian vein at its junction with left internal jugular","Right subclavian vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right atrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Left brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior vena cava leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left subclavian vein at its junction with left internal jugular', 'The thoracic duct terminates in the venous system at the junction of the left subclavian vein and the left internal jugular vein (left venous angle). The right subclavian vein receives the right lymphatic duct, and the heart''s chambers receive venous blood, not lymph.'),
('aaa1f0bb-f5c9-5ff4-8d25-39679c37f329'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The right lymphatic duct drains into:', '["Right atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","Right brachiocephalic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior vena cava mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right subclavian vein at its junction with right internal jugular"]'::jsonb, 'Right subclavian vein at its junction with right internal jugular', 'The right lymphatic duct drains into the venous system at the junction of the right subclavian vein and the right internal jugular vein (right venous angle). The atrium, brachiocephalic vein and superior vena cava are not its termination.'),
('da6eb3af-e659-5c16-b39d-0e822e3a468c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic (parasternal) nodes lie at which intercostal spaces?', '["1st-6th","1st-4th","1st-7th","2nd-5th","3rd-7th"]'::jsonb, '1st-6th', 'The internal thoracic (parasternal) nodes lie along the internal thoracic vessels at the anterior ends of the first six intercostal spaces. The other intercostal ranges do not match their distribution.'),
('9b70f756-374a-5fb6-8695-eaaa994bd93d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic nodes receive lymph from:', '["Medial breast","Upper limb","upper abdominal wall","Lateral breast","anterior chest wall"]'::jsonb, 'Medial breast', 'The internal thoracic (parasternal) nodes drain lymph mainly from the medial part of the breast. Lymph from the lateral breast, the upper limb and the general anterior wall is carried by the pectoral, humeral and other axillary groups respectively.'),
('26263339-88eb-5c7f-a635-67d4c6143fea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The intercostal lymph nodes lie:', '["Along the clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","In the axilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the intercostal spaces near the heads of ribs","In the neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Along the sternum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the intercostal spaces near the heads of ribs', 'Intercostal lymph nodes are located in the intercostal spaces near the heads of the ribs, adjacent to the posterior intercostal vessels. The axilla, sternum, clavicle and neck contain different node groups.'),
('c2bb7320-3ce1-5b3b-9c7c-33600a8ce79c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The intercostal nodes receive lymph from:', '["Anterior chest wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neck mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper limb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep posterior chest wall and deep breast"]'::jsonb, 'Deep posterior chest wall and deep breast', 'The intercostal nodes drain lymph from the deep posterior chest wall and the deep part of the breast. Lymph from the anterior chest wall, upper limb and abdomen drains into other groups.'),
('258783c8-d847-57dd-91ea-6a9263a31be3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatics of the skin of the breast communicate with:', '["Only axillary nodes","Lymphatics of opposite breast via midline","Only deep nodes","Only parasternal nodes","Only ipsilateral nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatics of opposite breast via midline', 'Superficial lymphatics of the breast skin cross the midline and communicate with those of the opposite breast, permitting contralateral spread of cancer. Drainage is therefore not confined to ipsilateral axillary or parasternal nodes.'),
('ec8c8199-991c-59a5-8644-97d7fbafb612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The mammary gland is a modified:', '["Sebaceous gland","Sweat gland (apocrine)","Eccrine gland","Holocrine gland","Hormonal gland"]'::jsonb, 'Sweat gland (apocrine)', 'The mammary gland is a modified apocrine sweat gland, developing from ectoderm as downgrowths of the skin. Sebaceous and eccrine glands and holocrine secretion do not describe its structure and mode of secretion.'),
('eeac9189-c7d2-50ec-9101-fe9164bcd170'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The embryological origin of the mammary gland is from:', '["All three germ layers","Mesoderm","Neural crest","Endoderm","Ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm', 'The mammary glands arise as downgrowths of the surface ectoderm along the embryonic milk line. The mesoderm, endoderm and neural crest do not contribute the glandular epithelium.'),
('88668c25-356f-52a4-a506-db18d3dadec9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The galactocele is:', '["Calcification resulting in decreased functional activity","A tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A milk cyst (retention cyst of lactiferous duct)","Fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An abscess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A milk cyst (retention cyst of lactiferous duct)', 'A galactocele is a milk-containing retention cyst of a lactiferous duct, developing during or after lactation. It is neither a tumor, calcification, fibrosis nor an abscess.'),
('92e0e51c-9617-5f43-9739-7d3afe0ce45a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Acute mastitis during lactation is most commonly caused by:', '["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pyogenes","Pseudomonas","E. coli","Clostridium"]'::jsonb, 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'Acute lactational (puerperal) mastitis is most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus entering through the nipple, often from the infant''s nose and throat. The other organisms are uncommon causes of lactational mastitis.'),
('a5b9d0b6-442a-567c-bdbf-97051f9c3e66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Mammary duct ectasia is associated with:', '["Fibroadenoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Dilation of lactiferous ducts with periductal inflammation","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilation of lactiferous ducts with periductal inflammation', 'Mammary duct ectasia is characterized by dilation of the subareolar lactiferous ducts accompanied by periductal inflammation and fibrosis. It is not a fibroadenoma, simple cyst, carcinoma or pure infection.'),
('163fa9c6-f85c-5ddc-a300-f187609af02b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The blood supply of the breast from the axillary artery includes:', '["Only thoracoacromial","thoracoacromial","Lateral thoracic","Only lateral thoracic","and subscapular branches"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral thoracic', 'Among the branches of the axillary artery, the lateral thoracic artery is the principal supply to the breast. The pectoral branches of the thoracoacromial artery and intercostal perforators contribute, but the named axillary contribution is the lateral thoracic artery.'),
('f22d5993-fa81-56c4-905a-18f58b713a87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The accessory breast tissue can occur along:', '["The thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","The milk line (from axilla to groin)","The neck mediated by specific receptor activation","The back without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The arm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The milk line (from axilla to groin)', 'Accessory breast tissue develops along the embryonic milk line, which extends from the axilla to the groin, and so may appear anywhere along this line. The thigh, neck, back and arm lie off this line.'),
('a3c3d987-1258-50e8-89f2-687dfaff936e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Accessory nipples (polythelia) are most common in:', '["Upper chest","Inframammary region","Abdomen","Back","Axilla"]'::jsonb, 'Axilla', 'Accessory nipples (polythelia) are most often found in the axilla, at the upper end of the milk line. They are less common in the inframammary, chest, abdominal and back regions.'),
('621f7644-a361-5e9b-b902-3b95ba5dcfe9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The supernumerary breast tissue most commonly occurs in:', '["Upper chest","Axilla (axillary tail)","Inframammary fold","Groin","Epigastrium"]'::jsonb, 'Axilla (axillary tail)', 'Supernumerary breast tissue most commonly develops in the axilla, where it is referred to as the axillary tail (of Spence). Other sites along the milk line are far less frequent.'),
('37712045-afb5-54e3-90c3-fe50dd9f010b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The subareolar breast abscess often requires:', '["Radial incision to avoid damage to lactiferous ducts","Circular incision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertical incision mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horizontal incision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radial incision to avoid damage to lactiferous ducts', 'The lactiferous ducts run radially toward the nipple, so a radial incision parallels them and minimizes ductal injury and postoperative fistula. Circular, horizontal, or vertical incisions would transect multiple ducts across the subareolar region.'),
('918372f0-e87f-5b3b-8f29-edda2647f51f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lactiferous ducts are arranged:', '["Randomly resulting in decreased functional activity","Longitudinally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circularly mediated by specific receptor activation","Concentrically without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radially (like spokes of a wheel)"]'::jsonb, 'Radially (like spokes of a wheel)', 'The 15-20 lactiferous ducts converge from the breast lobules and radiate toward the nipple like spokes of a wheel. This radial arrangement is why surgical incisions in the breast are planned radially to protect the ducts.'),
('827143c7-58b6-5d1b-b178-3012379720c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nipple is located at the level of which rib/space?', '["4th intercostal space (T4 dermatome)","2nd intercostal space","5th intercostal space","6th intercostal space","3rd intercostal space"]'::jsonb, '4th intercostal space (T4 dermatome)', 'In both sexes the nipple lies in the 4th intercostal space, corresponding clinically to the T4 dermatome. The 2nd intercostal space is a landmark for central venous access, not the nipple.'),
('f04ac9b3-2d16-5277-9e3f-2366cb32e062'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast is suspended from the deep fascia by:', '["Bones","Cooper''s ligaments","Muscles","Skin","Ligaments of axilla"]'::jsonb, 'Cooper''s ligaments', 'The suspensory ligaments of Cooper pass from the deep (pectoral) fascia through the breast to the skin, maintaining the shape and support of the breast. Malignant involvement of these ligaments produces skin tethering or dimpling (''peau d''orange'').'),
('d9b6b2a6-6189-558c-aa5c-4ab570bfe91f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The relaxation of Cooper''s ligaments in old age causes:', '["Nipple discharge","Nipple retraction","Breast ptosis (sagging)","None","Areolar enlargement"]'::jsonb, 'Breast ptosis (sagging)', 'With age the suspensory (Cooper) ligaments become lax, so the supportive framework weakens and the breast sags (ptosis). Nipple retraction suggests malignancy, not simply ligamentous laxity.'),
('90ddde8e-6c9e-5be0-b166-5a315558636d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The areola contains smooth muscle fibers arranged:', '["Randomly","Longitudinally","Only circularly","Only radially","Circularly and radially"]'::jsonb, 'Circularly and radially', 'The areola contains smooth muscle fibers arranged both circularly and radially, which contract to make the nipple erect. The presence of both orientations is why neither ''only circularly'' nor ''only radially'' is accurate alone.'),
('114b0da4-b0bf-57d9-9f3f-60519a0ea33c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The Montgomery''s tubercles are actually:', '["Nerve endings","Areolar sebaceous glands","Hair follicles","Blood vessels","Apocrine sweat glands"]'::jsonb, 'Areolar sebaceous glands', 'Montgomery''s tubercles are enlarged sebaceous (areolar) glands in the areola whose openings appear as small bumps. They differ from apocrine glands and contain no hair follicles or nerve corpuscles.'),
('d5b604b6-bed7-56e7-9af4-82f2cf6ad6ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The secretion of Montgomery''s glands helps:', '["Produce milk resulting in decreased functional activity","Lubricate and protect the areola and nipple during lactation","Excrete sweat mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce pheromones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lubricate and protect the areola and nipple during lactation', 'Montgomery''s glands secrete an oily, protective lubricant that shields the areola and nipple during breastfeeding and prevents cracking. Milk is produced by the alveolar glandular tissue, not by these glands.'),
('deb266f5-5801-5207-bb28-8545104f2599'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nipple may contain traces of:', '["elastin","Smooth muscle","nerve endings","But smooth muscle","collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle', 'The nipple contains smooth muscle fibers that contract during suckling or cold to make the nipple erect and empty the lactiferous ducts. Collagen, elastin, and nerve endings are present in skin generally but are not the distinctive trace component of the nipple.'),
('79a5b6db-3ba3-544b-8d5e-0502d9df14cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nipple is rich in:', '["Nerve endings (Meissner''s corpuscles and free endings)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hair follicles mediated by specific receptor activation","Sweat glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve endings (Meissner''s corpuscles and free endings)', 'The nipple is densely innervated by Meissner''s corpuscles and free nerve endings, which provide the tactile sensitivity important for the suckling reflex. Hair follicles and eccrine sweat glands are sparse or absent on the nipple.'),
('4bc9e6e6-0434-5439-abdb-ead1fdd06997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The parasternal lymph nodes are also called:', '["Mediastinal nodes","Intercostal nodes","Axillary nodes","Cervical nodes","Internal thoracic nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic nodes', 'Parasternal nodes lie along the internal thoracic vessels and are therefore synonymous with the internal thoracic (or internal mammary) nodes. They receive medial breast lymph and channel it into the bronchomediastinal trunk.'),
('5a9b6ffb-3104-537e-9da1-4b998f97dd84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The efferent lymphatics from the lower part of breast may pass through:', '["Femoral canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectus sheath to subdiaphragmatic nodes","Obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectus sheath to subdiaphragmatic nodes', 'Lymphatics from the medial and lower part of the breast descend within the rectus sheath to the subdiaphragmatic (hepatic) nodes and can thereby reach the liver. This route explains hepatic spread in some breast cancers.'),
('9e624bfe-655a-5074-9f4d-ae418e5e7989'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The sentinel node is identified using which dye?', '["Isosulfan blue (Lymphazurin)","Methylene blue","Evans blue","Indigo carmine","Congo red"]'::jsonb, 'Isosulfan blue (Lymphazurin)', 'Sentinel lymph node mapping typically uses isosulfan blue (Lymphazurin) dye plus a radioactive colloid tracer to locate the first draining node. Methylene blue, Evans blue, and Congo red are not the standard mapping agents.'),
('d69a1b6d-2618-52e1-b5ad-b7988185a7fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast tissue extends into the axilla as:', '["All of the above","Axillary tail (Spence''s tail)","Superior tail","Axillary process","Mammary tail"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary tail (Spence''s tail)', 'Superolateral breast tissue extends into the axilla along the axillary fascia as the axillary tail of Spence, which is excised during mastectomy. ''All of the above'' cannot be correct because the other named tail types are not recognized structures.'),
('18ba0674-9b45-55b4-872f-00186f9c989d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The skin of the breast receives sensory innervation from:', '["Supraclavicular nerves only resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior and lateral cutaneous branches of 2nd-6th intercostal nerves","Brachial plexus only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervical plexus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and lateral cutaneous branches of 2nd-6th intercostal nerves', 'The breast skin is supplied by the anterior and lateral cutaneous branches of the 2nd-6th intercostal nerves (T2-T6). A small superomedial area receives supraclavicular fibers, but the primary supply is the intercostal nerve pattern.'),
('12c9bcd3-c514-5b6a-bd42-a191953e9df3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common congenital anomaly of the breast is:', '["Amastia","Nipple inversion","Accessory breast (polymastia)","Athelia","Accessory nipple (polythelia)"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory nipple (polythelia)', 'Polythelia, presence of an accessory (supernumerary) nipple, is the most common congenital breast anomaly, usually along the embryonic milk line. Athelia, amastia, and polymastia are rarer.'),
('1b25a577-09ec-5e26-848e-1b6a5f1debf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Amastia is:', '["Absence of breast tissue","Small breast","Nipple absence","Extra nipple","Extra breast"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of breast tissue', 'Amastia is the congenital absence of breast tissue. Nipple absence is athelia, extra nipples are polythelia, and extra breast tissue is polymastia.'),
('acc521d7-b80b-5d68-a72b-08a5cdaf9d81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Athelia is:', '["Absence of nipple","Nipple discharge","Fused nipple","Extra nipple","Erectile nipple"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of nipple', 'Athelia is the congenital absence of the nipple. Absence of breast tissue is amastia, while extra breast tissue and extra nipples are polymastia and polythelia respectively.'),
('7961f012-7095-5a16-bcb4-5f2f8b404d60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Polymastia is:', '["Large breast","Absence of breast","Extra nipple","Small breast","Presence of extra breast(s)"]'::jsonb, 'Presence of extra breast(s)', 'Polymastia is the presence of supernumerary (extra) breast tissue, typically along the embryonic milk line. An extra nipple is polythelia, whereas amastia denotes absence of breast tissue.'),
('62138e2f-1121-559b-99ee-4c9e7157d495'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The suspensory ligament of the axilla (clavipectoral fascia) is important in surgery because:', '["It contains the axillary vein","It must be divided to access the axilla","It prevents spread of infection","None","It supports the breast"]'::jsonb, 'It must be divided to access the axilla', 'The clavipectoral fascia forms the suspensory ligament of the axilla, and it must be divided during axillary dissection to gain access to the axillary vessels and lymph nodes. The axillary vein lies deeper and is not contained within this ligament.'),
('e152a46f-a64b-578a-9eae-cd737d018209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoralis minor muscle is used as a landmark in axillary dissection because:', '["It protects the axillary vein","It divides axillary nodes into 3 surgical levels","None","It protects the axillary artery","It protects the brachial plexus"]'::jsonb, 'It divides axillary nodes into 3 surgical levels', 'Pectoralis minor is the surgical landmark that divides the axillary lymph nodes into three levels: level I below, level II deep to, and level III above the muscle. This level system guides the extent of axillary dissection.'),
('a497cdbf-2bcf-5fce-b23d-9a8ad37b022e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lateral pectoral nerve is also called:', '["Thoracodorsal","Medial pectoral","Subscapular","Lateral anterior thoracic nerve","Long thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral anterior thoracic nerve', 'The lateral pectoral nerve is also known as the lateral anterior thoracic nerve because it arises from the lateral cord of the brachial plexus. Thoracodorsal, long thoracic, and subscapular nerves are distinct structures.'),
('a5dd414a-40ad-5e3a-8cef-33e109fcefe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The medial pectoral nerve is also called:', '["Subscapular","Thoracodorsal","Long thoracic","Lateral pectoral","Medial anterior thoracic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial anterior thoracic nerve', 'The medial pectoral nerve is also called the medial anterior thoracic nerve because it arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus. It pierces pectoralis minor and supplies both pectoral muscles.'),
('8de30d9b-a3d2-5f3c-92f1-105809a7da2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lateral pectoral nerve (C5-7) supplies:', '["Pectoralis major mainly (pierces clavipectoral fascia)","Subclavius accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both mediated by specific receptor activation","Serratus anterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pectoralis minor mainly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis major mainly (pierces clavipectoral fascia)', 'The lateral pectoral nerve (C5-C7) runs through the clavipectoral fascia to supply mainly the clavicular head of pectoralis major, with a branch to the muscle''s sternocostal part. Subclavius is supplied by the nerve to subclavius, and serratus anterior by the long thoracic nerve.'),
('ddef35b3-d7fa-59d8-80be-48d4c1e980d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoral branch of thoracoacromial artery runs between:', '["Subclavius and pectoralis","Serratus anterior and pectoralis","Pectoralis major and skin","Clavicle and pectoralis","Pectoralis major and minor"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis major and minor', 'The pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial trunk descends between pectoralis major and minor, within the clavipectoral fascia, supplying both muscles. It does not course between the serratus anterior or subclavius and the pectorals.'),
('af116d70-bc91-5f83-87c0-d372f2838f74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast can be divided into how many quadrants?', '["3","4","5","6","2"]'::jsonb, '4', 'The breast is conventionally divided into four quadrants by a horizontal and vertical line through the nipple (upper/lower, outer/inner). The upper outer quadrant contains the axillary tail and is the most common site of breast carcinoma.'),
('40bfe35d-b8da-5f89-baa1-f46ee3968aec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast has a central (areolar) region that contains:', '["Only skin","glandular tissue","Only muscle","The nipple and areola","Only fat"]'::jsonb, 'The nipple and areola', 'The central part of the breast is the areolar region, consisting of the nipple surrounded by the pigmented areola. Glandular and fatty tissue occupy the peripheral glandular regions rather than defining the central zone.'),
('24b6b8ed-728e-59b2-9af5-86f3fdb993aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatic drainage of the breast may reach the liver via:', '["Portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Bile ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphatics crossing the diaphragm (subdiaphragmatic nodes)"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatics crossing the diaphragm (subdiaphragmatic nodes)', 'Lymphatics from the medial and lower breast pass along the rectus sheath to subdiaphragmatic nodes, then across the diaphragm to the liver, which is how breast cancer can metastasize to the liver. The portal vein and hepatic artery are blood vessels, not lymphatic pathways.'),
('5f5f40fa-b884-5e19-86d6-46f6c41ec400'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatics of the breast may communicate with those of the opposite breast through:', '["Subcutaneous lymphatics across midline","Intercostal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep lymphatics mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supraclavicular nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous lymphatics across midline', 'Superficial subcutaneous lymphatics cross the midline and connect the two breasts, allowing contralateral (cross-metastasis) spread of cancer. Intercostal and supraclavicular nodes drain the deep and superior routes but do not link the breasts.'),
('3a858198-eee7-5d97-8876-0e6b9b0e358a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic artery anastomoses with the:', '["Inferior epigastric resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep circumflex iliac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcostal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior epigastric artery (at 6th intercostal space)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior epigastric artery (at 6th intercostal space)', 'The internal thoracic artery terminates by dividing into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries; the superior epigastric anastomoses with the inferior epigastric within the rectus sheath. The subcostal artery does not anastomose with the internal thoracic.'),
('4ca0c480-cda5-5d56-851b-c4bccb5ffc33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The superior epigastric artery is the terminal branch of:', '["Superior thoracic","Internal thoracic artery","Subscapular","Thoracoacromial","Lateral thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The internal thoracic (internal mammary) artery ends by dividing into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. The lateral thoracic, thoracoacromial, and subscapular arteries are branches of the axillary artery.'),
('e63ae97e-0382-50d5-a5a2-5f0d86c88031'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The musculophrenic artery is a terminal branch of:', '["Subscapular","Thoracoacromial","Lateral thoracic","Internal thoracic artery","Superior thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The internal thoracic artery divides at the 6th costal cartilage level into the musculophrenic artery and the superior epigastric artery, so the musculophrenic is one of its two terminal branches. It courses along the costal margin supplying the diaphragm and lower intercostal spaces.'),
('69627fe1-c256-53cc-b237-0d095a10c797'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The intercostal spaces are supplied by which arteries?', '["Subclavian only resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal thoracic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior and anterior intercostal arteries","Only posterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only anterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior and anterior intercostal arteries', 'Each intercostal space receives a posterior intercostal artery (from the thoracic aorta or costocervical trunk) and an anterior intercostal artery (from the internal thoracic or musculophrenic artery), which anastomose within the space. No single source supplies the spaces alone.'),
('a382adfc-fd23-5667-afd5-e8688b9f774e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The 3rd posterior intercostal artery is a branch of:', '["Thoracic aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Subclavian accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal thoracic mediated by specific receptor activation","Axillary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior intercostal artery (from costocervical trunk)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior intercostal artery (from costocervical trunk)', 'The superior intercostal artery, a branch of the costocervical trunk of the subclavian artery, gives the posterior intercostal arteries of the 1st, 2nd, and often the 3rd spaces. The thoracic aorta supplies the 4th-11th posterior intercostal arteries.'),
('394541ba-7af7-5df4-8052-70c81b42cdb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The 4th-11th posterior intercostal arteries are branches of:', '["Costocervical","Internal thoracic","Axillary","Subclavian","Thoracic aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic aorta', 'The descending thoracic aorta gives off the posterior intercostal arteries for the 3rd or 4th through 11th spaces. The first few posterior intercostals arise instead from the superior intercostal artery of the costocervical trunk.'),
('562bd124-3232-5bbe-949e-1d3129a744d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The anterior intercostal arteries for the 1st-6th spaces are branches of:', '["Musculophrenic artery","Lateral thoracic","Thoracoacromial","Superior epigastric","Internal thoracic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The internal thoracic artery gives the anterior intercostal arteries for the 1st-6th spaces, while the musculophrenic artery supplies the 7th-9th spaces. The lateral thoracic and thoracoacromial are axillary branches unrelated to intercostal supply.'),
('4e1d3529-317e-5636-9629-f9524a2170c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The anterior intercostal arteries for the 7th-9th spaces are branches of:', '["Thoracoacromial","Superior epigastric","Musculophrenic artery","Lateral thoracic","Internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Musculophrenic artery', 'The musculophrenic artery, a terminal branch of the internal thoracic, supplies the anterior intercostal arteries of the 7th-9th spaces as it runs along the costal margin. The superior epigastric artery descends in the rectus sheath and does not supply intercostal spaces.'),
('07e4f1ee-e265-584f-9b0a-7ae033ecf1c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The 10th and 11th intercostal spaces receive blood from:', '["None","Superior epigastric","Internal thoracic","Inferior epigastric","Subcostal"]'::jsonb, 'Superior epigastric', 'The 10th and 11th intercostal spaces lack anterior intercostal arteries from the internal thoracic or musculophrenic system and receive their anterior supply from the superior epigastric artery. The internal thoracic supplies only the upper six intercostal spaces.'),
('ec4cde12-c27b-5482-981f-4f5836699c0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The posterior intercostal veins drain into:', '["Inferior vena cava resulting in decreased functional activity","Azygos/hemiazygos system on the right/left","Internal thoracic mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior vena cava without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Azygos/hemiazygos system on the right/left', 'Right posterior intercostal veins drain into the azygos vein and left ones into the hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins. The internal thoracic veins collect the anterior intercostal veins, not the posterior group.'),
('0b56070c-4a4d-5dbc-b40a-0ccb1904b7fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The anterior intercostal veins drain into:', '["Azygos","Intercostal veins","Brachiocephalic","Internal thoracic vein","Hemiazygos"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic vein', 'The anterior intercostal veins drain into the internal thoracic vein, which runs upward and empties into the brachiocephalic vein. The azygos and hemiazygos systems receive the posterior intercostal veins instead.'),
('ae5a64d4-d18e-5138-abaf-c522410e2fbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The internal thoracic vein drains into:', '["Subclavian vein","Azygos","Superior vena cava","Internal jugular","Brachiocephalic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic vein', 'Each internal thoracic vein ascends and empties into the brachiocephalic vein of the same side. It does not drain directly into the subclavian, azygos, or internal jugular veins, though it runs close to them.'),
('7d40e23a-34ea-5346-a8e8-c821262a91c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The azygos vein drains into:', '["Inferior vena cava","Azygos","Superior vena cava","Right atrium","Left brachiocephalic"]'::jsonb, 'Superior vena cava', 'The azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung and opens into the superior vena cava just above the pericardium. It is not a tributary of the inferior vena cava or the left brachiocephalic vein.'),
('453b8b82-5c49-59a6-8d01-aad2f800f22c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Into which structure does the hemiazygos vein on the left side ultimately drain?', '["Superior vena cava directly","Right brachiocephalic vein","The azygos vein on the right","Inferior vena cava","Left subclavian vein"]'::jsonb, 'The azygos vein on the right', 'The hemiazygos vein crosses the midline behind the aorta (around T8-T9) to join the azygos vein, which then drains into the superior vena cava. It reaches the superior vena cava only indirectly through the azygos vein.'),
('80bccb32-9c6a-5980-8f86-f1020e020dab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The accessory hemiazygos vein drains posterior intercostal veins from which levels?', '["T1-T4","T5-T8","T9-T12","T1-T12","T4-T7"]'::jsonb, 'T5-T8', 'The accessory hemiazygos vein, descending on the left, drains the posterior intercostal veins of the middle thoracic levels, typically T5-T8, and often ends in the azygos vein. The upper (T1-T4) and lower (T9-T12) groups drain differently.'),
('cc04f92a-4d8d-5c8a-a01c-117dc13eeb1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Lymph from the right side of the head and neck, right upper limb, and right chest drains into:', '["Right lymphatic duct","and right chest drains into:","right upper limb","Right subclavian trunk","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Right lymphatic duct', 'The right lymphatic duct drains the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right hemithorax, emptying into the right venous angle. The thoracic duct drains the remaining, far larger part of the body.'),
('5a95f0a9-8785-58f7-aa3f-64ce96693edd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracic duct drains the:', '["Entire body except the areas drained by the right lymphatic duct","All areas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left side only mediated by specific receptor activation","Right side of body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower body only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Entire body except the areas drained by the right lymphatic duct', 'The thoracic duct is the main lymphatic collecting channel and drains the whole body except the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right hemithorax, which go to the right lymphatic duct. It is not confined to the left side or lower body.'),
('27eaca52-cd5b-584d-9eb2-f2005577ca71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The right lymphatic duct is formed by the union of:', '["Jugular and subclavian only","and bronchomediastinal trunks","Left jugular and subclavian","subclavian","Right jugular"]'::jsonb, 'Right jugular', 'The right lymphatic duct results from the junction of the right jugular, right subclavian, and right bronchomediastinal lymphatic trunks. Among the choices, the right jugular trunk is a genuine constituent of that union, whereas the left trunks join the thoracic duct.'),
('9350a342-b719-590a-ba78-b2d1eb5e598d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracic duct begins at:', '["Fourth ventricle","Cisterna chyli (at L1-L2 level)","Diaphragm","Mediastinum","Cisterna magna"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna chyli (at L1-L2 level)', 'The thoracic duct arises from the cisterna chyli, a saccular lymphatic dilatation in front of the L1-L2 vertebrae. The cisterna magna and fourth ventricle are intracranial CSF spaces, unrelated to the origin of the thoracic duct.'),
('860beeb0-715e-51a4-8246-1a4502cf51d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The cisterna chyli receives lymph from:', '["Intestinal and lumbar trunks","Thoracic","Subclavian and jugular","Bronchomediastinal","None"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal and lumbar trunks', 'The cisterna chyli collects lymph from the intestinal and lumbar (right and left) lymphatic trunks. The jugular, subclavian, and bronchomediastinal trunks open into the great veins rather than into the cisterna chyli.'),
('cf48ebe1-ce03-555a-bb7a-cc5a30da07d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracic duct enters the thorax through:', '["Foramen of Morgagni","Esophageal hiatus","Aortic hiatus of diaphragm (T12)","None","Caval opening"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic hiatus of diaphragm (T12)', 'The thoracic duct passes from abdomen to thorax through the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm at the T12 level, alongside the aorta and azygos vein. The esophageal hiatus transmits the esophagus and the caval opening the inferior vena cava.'),
('63eb7012-8b56-5c9e-bc2a-9fa879dc8ca2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracic duct terminates at:', '["Left brachiocephalic resulting in decreased functional activity","Right venous angle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior vena cava mediated by specific receptor activation","Subclavian without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left venous angle (junction of left subclavian and internal jugular)"]'::jsonb, 'Left venous angle (junction of left subclavian and internal jugular)', 'The thoracic duct drains lymph from most of the body and empties at the confluence of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins, the left venous angle. The right lymphatic duct, by contrast, terminates at the right venous angle. The brachiocephalic vein and superior vena cava are venous vessels, not sites of lymphatic termination.'),
('213b546b-ec21-57e9-a763-81418526fd04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The right lymphatic duct terminates at:', '["Subclavian","Left venous angle","Right venous angle","Right brachiocephalic","Superior vena cava"]'::jsonb, 'Right venous angle', 'The right lymphatic duct drains the right upper limb, the right side of the thorax, and the right head and neck, and it opens at the right venous angle where the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins meet. The left venous angle receives the thoracic duct rather than the right duct. The listed venous trunks are not termination points for this duct.'),
('350c995a-1f31-5412-a197-12f2fb85aa86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The supratrochlear lymph node lies:', '["Above the lateral epicondyle","Above the medial epicondyle of humerus","In the arm","In the axilla","In the cubital fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Above the medial epicondyle of humerus', 'The supratrochlear lymph node is situated in the superficial fascia a little above the medial epicondyle of the humerus, along the course of the basilic vein. It drains the medial side of the hand and forearm. The lateral epicondyle, the arm, and the axilla are not its location.'),
('d853aeb3-2c52-55c7-a77b-9b75b6824621'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The supratrochlear node sends efferents to:', '["None","Supraclavicular nodes","Cervical nodes","Parasternal nodes","Axillary nodes (lateral group)"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nodes (lateral group)', 'Efferents of the supratrochlear node accompany the basilic vein and reach the axilla, emptying into the lateral group of axillary lymph nodes. The supraclavicular, cervical, and parasternal groups are not the direct destination of these efferents.'),
('0cdedcbd-533e-56a5-a9fc-9674d6822b27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary tail of the breast may be confused with:', '["None","Subclavian aneurysm","Enlarged pectoralis muscle","Axillary lymphadenopathy","Pancoast tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary lymphadenopathy', 'The axillary tail of Spence is a projection of breast tissue into the axilla that can be palpated as a soft mass and may be mistaken for enlarged axillary lymph nodes. It is a normal tongue of glandular tissue, unlike an aneurysm, muscle enlargement, or an apical lung tumor.'),
('e096ddcf-bb92-536c-a1d7-f469b3cae21c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common site for fibroadenoma is:', '["Lower inner quadrant","Upper inner quadrant","Central","Upper outer quadrant","Lower outer quadrant"]'::jsonb, 'Upper outer quadrant', 'Fibroadenoma, like breast carcinoma, arises most often in the upper outer quadrant because this quadrant contains the greatest volume of breast tissue. The other quadrants are affected less frequently.'),
('a9da3d5e-d013-5281-b912-3d5922217604'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Female breast cancer is most common in which age group?', '["60-70 years","50-60 years","20-30 years","30-40 years","Perimenopausal (40-50 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Perimenopausal (40-50 years)', 'In the relevant clinical population, breast carcinoma most commonly presents during the perimenopausal decade of 40-50 years, a period of climacteric hormonal change. The younger decades carry a much lower incidence, while the 50-70 year ranges are beyond the usual peak in this setting.'),
('28c0d3a0-7751-5e22-af3e-70b50cfed92c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common genetic mutations associated with breast cancer are:', '["HER2/neu","BRCA1 and BRCA2","ATM","PTEN","P53"]'::jsonb, 'BRCA1 and BRCA2', 'BRCA1 and BRCA2 are the tumor suppressor genes most frequently mutated in hereditary breast cancer and account for the majority of inherited breast-ovarian cancer syndromes. HER2 amplification, ATM, PTEN, and P53 are relevant to breast cancer but are not the most common germline mutations.'),
('180cc9b3-937d-572f-90ec-109b90aac91e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'BRCA1 mutation carriers have increased risk of:', '["Prostate cancer","Lung cancer","Colon cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Breast and ovarian cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast and ovarian cancer', 'BRCA1 carriers have a markedly elevated lifetime risk of both breast and ovarian cancer, together defining the hereditary breast-ovarian cancer syndrome. Prostate and pancreatic cancer risks are modestly increased and are not the characteristic presentation.'),
('c2f9c244-92d2-555d-a7b2-e27c651ee5cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'BRCA2 mutation is associated with:', '["Colon cancer only","None","Breast cancer in males and females","Prostate cancer only","Ovarian cancer only"]'::jsonb, 'Breast cancer in males and females', 'BRCA2 mutations predispose to breast cancer in both sexes and carry a relatively higher risk of male breast cancer than BRCA1 mutations. The other options limit the risk to a single organ or deny any association.'),
('6bc3e00d-ef59-5cac-997d-3df95507a64d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Her-2/neu overexpression cancer indicates:', '["Good prognosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hormone sensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Poor prognosis (aggressive tumor)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","difference leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Poor prognosis (aggressive tumor)', 'HER-2/neu (ERBB2) amplification drives more aggressive tumor behavior with rapid proliferation and a worse prognosis unless targeted anti-HER2 therapy is given. It does not denote good prognosis, hormone sensitivity, or a favorable marker.'),
('f3a1e08e-b048-588f-8aab-11673846a80a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Tamoxifen therapy is effective in:', '["None","Triple-negative breast cancer","HER2-positive breast cancer","Estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer","All breast cancers"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer', 'Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator that blocks estrogen signaling, so it benefits only tumors that express estrogen receptors. Triple-negative and ER-negative HER2-positive tumors lack the receptor and do not respond to it.'),
('2ac750be-3845-5df0-a619-e05884dcf1c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The retromammary space is important because:', '["It contains Cooper''s ligaments","It contains blood vessels","It contains the lactiferous ducts","It contains nerves","It allows the breast to move freely over the chest wall"]'::jsonb, 'It allows the breast to move freely over the chest wall', 'The retromammary space is a loose areolar layer between the deep layer of the superficial fascia and the pectoral fascia, and this looseness permits the breast to glide freely over the chest wall. Cooper''s ligaments, ducts, vessels, and nerves lie within the gland itself rather than in this space.'),
('8c49fdc2-e36e-5f9d-a43b-8b9f5335578b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Invasion of the retromammary space by carcinoma causes:', '["Skin dimpling","Nipple retraction","Nipple discharge","Fixation of breast to chest wall","Peau d''orange"]'::jsonb, 'Fixation of breast to chest wall', 'Carcinoma that spreads from the retromammary space into the underlying pectoral fascia anchors the breast to the chest wall so that the mass becomes fixed and immobile. Skin dimpling, nipple retraction, and peau d''orange result from invasion of Cooper''s ligaments and dermal lymphatics rather than the retromammary space.'),
('84410713-7e82-5662-bcb0-7a089a7615d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The nipple-areola complex is supplied by:', '["Only lateral thoracic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only intercostals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Branches of internal thoracic and lateral thoracic arteries","Only thoracoacromial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only internal thoracic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of internal thoracic and lateral thoracic arteries', 'The nipple-areola complex receives blood from branches of the internal thoracic artery medially and the lateral thoracic artery laterally, supplemented by intercostal perforators. No single artery alone provides its entire supply.'),
('82d68be9-32f2-5064-b97d-4e1e78e6f57c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast lymphatics begin as:', '["Lymphatic capillaries in the interlobular connective tissue","Within the areola accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Within the nipple mediated by specific receptor activation","Within the alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Within the ducts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic capillaries in the interlobular connective tissue', 'Lymph drainage of the breast begins as blind-ending lymphatic capillaries in the interlobular connective tissue that surrounds the lobules. From these channels lymph passes to the subareolar plexus and then chiefly to the axillary nodes.'),
('0975ae6b-a3b6-54c4-8403-f13c2badaf18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The subareolar lymphatic plexus (Sappey''s plexus) drains into:', '["Cervical nodes","Pectoral axillary nodes","Parasternal nodes","Mediastinal nodes","Subclavian nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoral axillary nodes', 'Sappey''s subareolar plexus collects lymph from the nipple, areola, and central breast and directs it mainly to the pectoral (anterior) group of axillary lymph nodes. The parasternal, cervical, mediastinal, and subclavian nodes are secondary drainage routes rather than the principal target.'),
('fd416c11-f01f-548e-9bdc-b55423164d8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The infraclavicular lymph nodes (Dell''orto''s nodes) receive lymph from:', '["Central group","Subscapular group","Apical axillary group","Pectoral group","Lateral group"]'::jsonb, 'Apical axillary group', 'The infraclavicular (Dell''orto''s) nodes represent the upward continuation of the apical group of axillary nodes, so they receive the efferent lymph of the apical group. The central, subscapular, pectoral, and lateral groups drain within the axilla and are not the direct source.'),
('a3837222-3460-549d-b877-1f67ee0731b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The deltopectoral lymph nodes lie:', '["In the infraclavicular region resulting in decreased functional activity","Along the cephalic vein in deltopectoral groove","In the axilla mediated by specific receptor activation","In the supraclavicular region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the arm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Along the cephalic vein in deltopectoral groove', 'The deltopectoral lymph nodes are located in the deltopectoral groove, accompanying the cephalic vein as it ascends between the deltoid and pectoralis major muscles. They receive lymph chiefly from the shoulder region and are not in the infraclavicular space proper, the axilla, or the arm.'),
('ce72bc30-2409-50a5-9b9f-427b244c3af9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The deltopectoral nodes drain into:', '["Parasternal nodes","Axillary nodes","None","Supraclavicular nodes","Infraclavicular nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Supraclavicular nodes', 'The deltopectoral nodes, traveling with the cephalic vein, ultimately empty into the supraclavicular lymph nodes at the root of the neck. The parasternal, axillary, and infraclavicular groups are not their destination.'),
('0287840d-74e7-5882-905f-7ccb38708f0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The cephalic vein may carry lymph from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The axilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The radial side of hand and forearm","The ulnar side without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The middle finger leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The radial side of hand and forearm', 'Lymphatics of the radial (lateral) side of the hand and forearm travel with the cephalic vein and drain toward the axillary and deltopectoral nodes. The ulnar side drains with the basilic vein rather than the cephalic vein.'),
('36c7a89a-7815-5c96-831a-a8fb2f2af5f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The basilic vein may carry lymph from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The radial side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ulnar (medial) side of hand and forearm","The axilla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The thumb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ulnar (medial) side of hand and forearm', 'Lymphatics of the ulnar (medial) side of the hand and forearm accompany the basilic vein, passing through the supratrochlear node to reach the axillary nodes. The radial side and thumb drain via the cephalic vein, not the basilic vein.'),
('202035d0-15f9-5b98-b1b5-c70b41711f86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The lymphatic vessels of the breast have valves that:', '["Direct lymph flow toward the axilla","Prevent infection","Direct lymph flow away from the axilla","None","Prevent backflow"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent backflow', 'The valves of the breast lymphatics ensure one-way flow toward the axilla by preventing retrograde or backward flow of lymph. They do not direct flow in any specific route, nor do they prevent infection.'),
('4970ebda-a9bd-56e1-82b5-62e1c8b909ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The skin lymphatics of the breast communicate with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasternal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphatics of the opposite breast across midline","Only axillary nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only deep lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatics of the opposite breast across midline', 'The rich cutaneous lymphatic network of the breast crosses the anterior midline, so the superficial lymphatics of one breast communicate with those of the opposite breast, enabling contralateral spread of carcinoma. The other options describe only single pathways.'),
('6e448aa4-8605-53c6-9658-e36a75976a5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The incidence of bilateral breast cancer is:', '["different resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Higher in patients with BRCA mutations","Rare without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Low leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher in patients with BRCA mutations', 'Because BRCA1/BRCA2 carriers carry a strong genetic predisposition, their risk of developing bilateral (including contralateral) breast cancer is substantially higher than in the general population. In sporadic disease bilateral breast cancer is uncommon.'),
('bc4d690e-bccf-59a7-8619-d6e6dac1dfc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common histologic type of breast cancer is:', '["Lobular carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Infiltrating ductal carcinoma (NOS)","Mucinous carcinoma","Tubular carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Infiltrating ductal carcinoma (NOS)', 'Invasive ductal carcinoma, not otherwise specified, is by far the most common histologic type, accounting for roughly 75-80% of invasive breast cancers. Lobular, medullary, mucinous, and tubular carcinomas are less frequent special subtypes.'),
('30d8f79b-794d-5064-baf0-6ed91855d187'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Invasive lobular carcinoma is characterized by:', '["Gland formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyalinization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcification mediated by specific receptor activation","Necrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Indian file pattern of infiltration"]'::jsonb, 'Indian file pattern of infiltration', 'Invasive lobular carcinoma characteristically infiltrates as single-file cords of small cells arranged in a linear or Indian file pattern and tends to be diffusely spreading rather than forming a discrete mass. Gland formation, hyalinization, calcification, and necrosis are not its defining features.'),
('87f42417-cefc-5010-984e-380ba95fd647'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Medullary carcinoma of breast has:', '["lymphocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Poor prognosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrotic stroma mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphocytic infiltrate and better prognosis","Calcifications leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytic infiltrate and better prognosis', 'Medullary carcinoma is a well-demarcated tumor with a dense lymphocytic infiltrate at its periphery and carries a more favorable prognosis than infiltrating ductal carcinoma. Fibrotic stroma, calcifications, and poor prognosis belong to other tumor types.'),
('8b55a239-c65f-5220-8722-a3e4e8692e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Mucinous (colloid) carcinoma of breast contains:', '["Necrosis","Extracellular mucin","Fibrosis","Calcifications","Hyaline"]'::jsonb, 'Extracellular mucin', 'Mucinous (colloid) carcinoma is defined by abundant extracellular mucin in which clusters of tumor cells float, often in older women with a relatively good prognosis. Necrosis, fibrosis, hyaline, and calcification are not its characteristic component.'),
('5c2d159b-05db-50b6-85ad-2d0f322d9a2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Paget''s disease of the nipple presents as:', '["Ulcer resulting in decreased functional activity","Nipple discharge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lump mediated by specific receptor activation","Eczematous rash of nipple and areola","Nipple retraction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eczematous rash of nipple and areola', 'Paget''s disease of the nipple presents with a slowly spreading eczematous, erythematous, crusting rash of the nipple and areola caused by intraepidermal tumor cells. Nipple discharge, ulceration, retraction, and a palpable lump are presentations of other breast lesions.'),
('40a29bec-ca52-5635-a5f2-f3f35c35a6a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Paget''s disease is associated with:', '["Underlying DCIS (ductal carcinoma in situ)","Sarcoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lobular carcinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Medullary carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucinous carcinoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Underlying DCIS (ductal carcinoma in situ)', 'Paget''s disease of the nipple is almost always associated with an underlying ductal carcinoma in situ or invasive ductal cancer that extends along the lactiferous ducts to the nipple epidermis. Sarcoma and lobular, medullary, or mucinous tumors are not the associated lesion.'),
('992c49cf-efc5-59be-a1f4-13e78957d037'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The tumor cells in Paget''s disease (Paget cells) are:', '["Large cells with clear cytoplasm and pleomorphic nuclei","Small round cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Signet ring cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Spindle cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large cells with clear cytoplasm and pleomorphic nuclei', 'Paget cells are large, rounded intraepidermal tumor cells with abundant pale or clear cytoplasm and markedly pleomorphic, hyperchromatic nuclei with prominent nucleoli. Small round cells, signet ring cells, and spindle cells belong to other tumor types.'),
('e6c18707-065a-55eb-a796-f91efd1f82d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Phyllodes tumor is characterized by:', '["Necrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductal proliferation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leaf-like pattern with stromal hypercellularity","Lobular proliferation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leaf-like pattern with stromal hypercellularity', 'Phyllodes tumor is a biphasic fibroepithelial neoplasm whose characteristic leaf-like or frond-like architecture results from overgrowth of a cellular stroma that projects into epithelial-lined clefts. Ductal or lobular proliferation, necrosis, and calcification are not its defining features.'),
('817d67b7-0e28-55ce-a75b-9fd961e078e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Phyllodes tumor can be:', '["borderline","Only benign","Benign","Only malignant","or malignant"]'::jsonb, 'Benign', 'Phyllodes tumors form a spectrum and can be benign, borderline, or malignant, although most are benign. It is therefore accurate to state that a phyllodes tumor can be benign, whereas labeling it as only benign or only malignant is incorrect.'),
('57b64c6a-e83d-5351-ae54-aeb83525b3fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Fat necrosis of breast can mimic:', '["Carcinoma clinically and mammographically","Mastitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibroadenoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carcinoma clinically and mammographically', 'Fat necrosis of the breast produces a firm, irregular lump that may be fixed to the skin, and on mammography can show spiculated margins or calcifications, so it can closely mimic carcinoma by both clinical examination and imaging. It is a benign, usually post-traumatic or postsurgical process.'),
('55a8493d-b9e8-5d4e-a972-d703f2034bdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Fat necrosis of breast is often associated with:', '["Infection","Pregnancy","Trauma","Lactation","Cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma', 'Fat necrosis of the breast most often follows trauma, including blunt injury, surgery, and biopsy, and results from aseptic saponification of adipose tissue. Infection, pregnancy, lactation, and cancer are not the usual precipitating events.'),
('aef6cf70-5dcc-5de4-927c-4c5cd5c5f732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Mammography is used for:', '["Diagnosis of cysts","Screening and diagnosis of breast cancer","Diagnosis of mastitis","All","Diagnosis of fibroadenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Screening and diagnosis of breast cancer', 'Mammography is the principal imaging modality for screening asymptomatic women and for evaluating symptomatic patients for breast cancer. Cysts and fibroadenomas are typically characterized by ultrasound rather than by mammography, so the specific roles listed are not mammography''s primary use.'),
('7e410e24-8878-587a-8d03-389f8a8b21fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Breast density on mammography is due to:', '["Glandular tissue (fibroglandular density)","Fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcification mediated by specific receptor activation","Necrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glandular tissue (fibroglandular density)', 'Mammographic breast density reflects the amount of fibroglandular tissue, which appears white on the image, relative to fat, which appears dark. Dense glandular tissue can mask small cancers, and neither calcification, necrosis, nor isolated fibrosis accounts for density.'),
('7b10d33b-a9ac-562e-844a-2b62a0ecc40f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'BIRADS classification is used for:', '["Biopsy technique resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgical staging accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mammography reporting and risk assessment","Chemotherapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radiotherapy planning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mammography reporting and risk assessment', 'The BI-RADS (Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System) provides a standardized lexicon for mammography reporting and assigns a category that reflects the likelihood of malignancy and guides further management. It is not a biopsy, staging, chemotherapy, or radiotherapy tool.'),
('31391d00-4d36-5d81-a142-59c9b0fd174b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'BIRADS 4 indicates:', '["Probably benign resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Highly suggestive of malignancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Suspicious abnormality (biopsy recommended)","Benign leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suspicious abnormality (biopsy recommended)', 'A BI-RADS 4 category denotes a suspicious finding for which biopsy is recommended, and it is subdivided into 4A, 4B, and 4C with increasing levels of suspicion. It lies between BI-RADS 3 (probably benign) and BI-RADS 5 (highly suggestive of malignancy).'),
('0238b01c-3b8c-5acc-a0db-bc04f0fc2198'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'BIRADS 5 indicates:', '["Benign resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Incomplete mediated by specific receptor activation","Probably benign without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Highly suggestive of malignancy"]'::jsonb, 'Highly suggestive of malignancy', 'A BI-RADS 5 category represents findings highly suggestive of malignancy, with a probability of cancer of at least 95%, and mandates biopsy and definitive management. Normal, benign, probably benign, and incomplete correspond to categories 1, 2, 3, and 0 respectively.'),
('167d07ea-8cc6-5c18-8c36-7275fcda5ce1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Ultrasound of breast is useful for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Distinguishing cysts from solid masses","Staging mediated by specific receptor activation","Biopsy guidance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mammography screening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distinguishing cysts from solid masses', 'Ultrasound is the preferred method for separating simple cysts, which appear as anechoic well-circumscribed lesions, from solid masses because this distinction cannot be made reliably by clinical examination or by mammography alone. Screening is the role of mammography, not ultrasound.'),
('ba64c5ec-d1b0-54ee-9ea4-2d4119cf0577'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'MRI of breast is indicated for:', '["Routine screening","problem-solving","First-line imaging","High-risk screening","implant evaluation"]'::jsonb, 'High-risk screening', 'Breast MRI is used for supplemental screening of high-risk women, particularly BRCA mutation carriers and those with a strong family history, where it is added to mammography and ultrasound. It is not used for routine or first-line imaging of the general population.'),
('f2fbb23f-1b48-5856-85bb-1363bc7c89da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Core needle biopsy is preferred over FNA for:', '["Cytology only resulting in decreased functional activity","Safety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rapid diagnosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Histologic diagnosis with architecture preserved","Cost savings leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Histologic diagnosis with architecture preserved', 'Core needle biopsy removes a tissue core that preserves the architecture of the lesion, permitting distinction of in situ from invasive carcinoma as well as histologic grading and receptor testing. FNA provides cytology only and loses tissue architecture.'),
('bc8b55fa-82ac-5a4a-ae39-e2d49cf0f3b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Stereotactic biopsy uses which imaging guidance?', '["Mammography","CT","PET","Ultrasound","MRI"]'::jsonb, 'Mammography', 'Stereotactic biopsy localizes lesions, such as microcalcifications, that are visible only on mammography, using X-ray-based stereotactic guidance. Ultrasound guidance is used for sonographically visible masses, not for stereotactic biopsy.'),
('8e061e39-4761-58fd-911a-b64651fac166'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common cause of bloody nipple discharge is:', '["Fat necrosis","Intraductal papilloma","Lobular carcinoma","Mastitis","Ductal carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Intraductal papilloma', 'Intraductal papilloma is the most common cause of bloody or serosanguineous nipple discharge, typically unilateral and spontaneous. Carcinoma, mastitis, and fat necrosis can occasionally cause discharge but are far less common causes of blood-stained secretion.'),
('47a56c48-724c-5507-9447-4d6ee9ea1845'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Nipple discharge that is bilateral and milky (galactorrhea) is associated with:', '["Hyperprolactinemia","Mastitis","Pregnancy","Intraductal papilloma","Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia', 'Bilateral milky nipple discharge, or galactorrhea, is typically caused by hyperprolactinemia, whether due to a pituitary prolactinoma, drugs, or hypothyroidism. Pregnancy and mastitis produce discharge by other mechanisms, while papilloma and carcinoma more often yield blood-stained secretion.'),
('d0c8199a-b284-5990-83f9-1b91d30de89b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The sentinel lymph node biopsy avoids:', '["Complete axillary dissection in node-negative patients","Treatment of cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Biopsy of the tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnosis of cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete axillary dissection in node-negative patients', 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy identifies the first draining node and, when that node is negative, spares node-negative patients the morbidity of a complete axillary dissection. It does not treat, diagnose, or replace tumor biopsy.'),
('7368c5dc-9cb5-5d1f-9576-af45b246ff9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The osseous metastases from breast cancer most commonly affect:', '["Skull","pelvis","Spine","femur","ribs"]'::jsonb, 'Spine', 'Breast cancer metastasizes to bone chiefly in the axial skeleton, and the spine is the single most common osseous site, followed by the pelvis, ribs, and skull. The appendicular bones such as the femur are affected less often.'),
('4a989ff0-43b8-5c2d-8679-6f77d596c978'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The most common visceral sites of breast cancer metastasis are:', '["Lungs","Liver only","liver","bones","brain"]'::jsonb, 'Lungs', 'The lungs are the most common visceral site of breast cancer metastasis, followed by the liver and brain. The liver-only and bone options are too restricted, and bone represents osseous rather than visceral spread.'),
('295861d6-092d-554c-bd1d-9e501bd0721d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Breast cancer staging uses the TNM system. T stands for:', '["Tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Time accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Therapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor size and extent","Type leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor size and extent', 'In the TNM system, T describes the primary tumor''s size and extent, N its regional nodal involvement, and M distant metastasis. Tissue, Time, Therapy, and Type are unrelated staging descriptors.'),
('770a9203-e719-5654-b382-b9744fa2226b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'T1 breast cancer is defined as tumor size:', '["Less than 1 cm","2-5 cm","2 cm or less","Up to 5 mm","More than 5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2 cm or less', 'T1 breast cancers measure 2 cm or less at greatest dimension. Tumors of 2-5 cm are T2 and those over 5 cm are T3, so the other ranges are too large.'),
('54044198-00f9-5976-a296-9260445a7ace'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'T2 breast cancer is defined as tumor size:', '[">2 cm but ≤5 cm","≤2 cm",">5 cm","None","Any size with chest wall involvement"]'::jsonb, '>2 cm but ≤5 cm', 'T2 lesions exceed 2 cm but are no larger than 5 cm in greatest dimension. Tumors of 2 cm or less are T1, those over 5 cm are T3, and chest wall or skin extension indicates T4.'),
('78292ba1-b375-52e2-a69a-7187db1c61be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'T3 breast cancer is defined as tumor size:', '["Any size",">5 cm","≤2 cm","Any size with skin involvement",">2 cm but ≤5 cm"]'::jsonb, '>5 cm', 'T3 breast cancer exceeds 5 cm in greatest dimension. Tumors of 2 cm or less are T1, those of 2-5 cm are T2, and skin or chest wall extension moves the stage to T4.'),
('7c11b878-3ba5-5f51-8119-1c225e89b560'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'T4 breast cancer includes:', '["Tumor with lymph node involvement resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor >5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tumor of any size with extension to chest wall/skin","Tumor with metastases without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor >10 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor of any size with extension to chest wall/skin', 'T4 denotes a tumor of any size that directly extends to the chest wall or skin (ulceration, peau d''orange). Nodal and distant spread are classified by the N and M categories, not by tumor size.'),
('78a9e7d5-c1b7-558a-b0db-7e6f6efba8ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'N1 breast cancer indicates:', '["Fixed axillary nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Supraclavicular nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Movable ipsilateral axillary lymph node metastasis","Internal mammary nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Movable ipsilateral axillary lymph node metastasis', 'N1 refers to movable ipsilateral axillary lymph node metastasis. Fixed or matted axillary nodes (N2) and supraclavicular nodes (N3) represent more advanced nodal disease.'),
('ad0a0730-7660-5c83-880b-5b8a9ed13b7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'N2 breast cancer indicates:', '["Supraclavicular nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Distant nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fixed/matted ipsilateral axillary nodes or clinically apparent internal mammary nodes","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed/matted ipsilateral axillary nodes or clinically apparent internal mammary nodes', 'N2 indicates fixed or matted ipsilateral axillary nodes or clinically apparent internal mammary nodes. Supraclavicular and contralateral nodal disease are classified as N3.'),
('a5bc8c1f-93a0-5eed-9254-c9f3a5e22ecf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'N3 breast cancer indicates:', '["Contralateral axillary nodes","supraclavicular","or internal mammary nodes with axillary nodes","Ipsilateral axillary nodes only","Metastasis to infraclavicular"]'::jsonb, 'Metastasis to infraclavicular', 'N3 breast cancer includes ipsilateral infraclavicular (subclavian) nodal metastasis, internal mammary nodes combined with axillary nodes, and ipsilateral supraclavicular nodes. The infraclavicular descriptor is a recognized N3 finding, while the other options are incomplete or misstate the category.'),
('a337d3be-04ef-5382-a0a3-ac8b58158d66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'M1 breast cancer indicates:', '["Distant metastasis present","Local recurrence","metastasis","None","Lymph node involvement only"]'::jsonb, 'Distant metastasis present', 'M1 indicates the presence of distant metastasis, which defines stage IV disease. Local recurrence and lymph node involvement belong to the T and N categories, not to M.'),
('27bb6800-04bf-5625-bc0f-c0086725fb9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'HER2/neu status is important for:', '["Guiding targeted therapy (trastuzumab/Herceptin)","Chemotherapy only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgery only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endocrine therapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Guiding targeted therapy (trastuzumab/Herceptin)', 'HER2 amplification predicts response to the targeted agent trastuzumab (Herceptin). Endocrine therapy is directed by ER/PR status, and chemotherapy is a broader, non-targeted modality.'),
('639eb521-9f22-5191-94d3-989cdf9c1cfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Triple-negative breast cancer lacks:', '["Only HER2","PR","All receptors","and HER2 expression","ER"]'::jsonb, 'All receptors', 'Triple-negative breast cancer lacks estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and HER2 expression simultaneously. Loss of a single receptor, such as ER alone, does not define this subtype.'),
('6cba7ccc-dceb-5c1e-b22f-6e63cd2d67e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Triple-negative breast cancer is associated with:', '["HER2 amplification","PR positivity","BRCA1 mutations","BRCA2 mutations","ER positivity"]'::jsonb, 'BRCA1 mutations', 'Triple-negative and basal-like breast cancers are strongly associated with BRCA1 germline mutations. BRCA2 relates more to lobular and ER-positive disease, while HER2 amplification and ER or PR positivity are not characteristic of this subtype.'),
('854c51f4-b555-5883-8b6a-2bac22a62771'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The risk factors for breast cancer include:', '["nulliparity","Late menarche","family history","Early menarche","late menopause"]'::jsonb, 'Early menarche', 'Early menarche, along with late menopause, prolongs lifetime estrogen exposure and is an established risk factor for breast cancer. Late menarche shortens estrogen exposure and is protective.'),
('91f809db-5644-57ad-8c22-c6c26d0565b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Protective factors against breast cancer include:', '["breastfeeding","exercise","Early pregnancy","Late pregnancy","multiparity"]'::jsonb, 'Early pregnancy', 'A full-term pregnancy at an early age confers lasting protection against breast cancer through hormone-driven differentiation of the gland. Late pregnancy and nulliparity are associated with increased risk.'),
('dc8915b1-36dd-5ee5-81fd-1f847749b73e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Breast cancer incidence is lower in:', '["Western women resulting in decreased functional activity","All populations equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rural women only mediated by specific receptor activation","Urban women only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asian and African women compared to Western women"]'::jsonb, 'Asian and African women compared to Western women', 'Breast cancer incidence is substantially lower in Asian and African women than in Western populations. The difference reflects genetic, lifestyle, and reproductive factors rather than a uniform incidence across all populations.'),
('9939a846-183e-5581-a89a-100f3bd377bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Mammographic screening reduces breast cancer mortality by approximately:', '["70-80%","20-30%","50-60%","100%","10%"]'::jsonb, '20-30%', 'Screening mammography reduces breast cancer mortality by roughly 20-30% in average-risk women. Reductions of 50% or more exceed the demonstrated benefit.'),
('be6e34ef-e808-59b1-9c0b-19fbfbe82359'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The recommended age to start mammographic screening in average-risk women is:', '["20-30 years","30-40 years","50-60 years","60-70 years","40-50 years"]'::jsonb, '40-50 years', 'Guidelines generally recommend that average-risk women begin screening mammography around age 40-50 years. Screening before age 40 has not shown a mortality benefit in average-risk women.'),
('cc338c28-681a-5c0f-8a2f-b8f30338cece'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast is a target organ for which hormones?', '["prolactin","progesterone","Only estrogen","oxytocin","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen', 'Estrogen is a primary hormone acting on the breast, promoting ductal growth and elongation. Prolactin and progesterone also act on the gland, so single-hormone choices are incomplete, ''only estrogen'' is false, and oxytocin acts mainly on myoepithelial cells during milk ejection.'),
('21d29d94-c8a4-50df-ac08-57261c66c7a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Estrogen stimulates:', '["Ductal growth and elongation (ductogenesis)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Milk secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar development without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Milk ejection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ductal growth and elongation (ductogenesis)', 'Estrogen promotes ductal growth and elongation (ductogenesis). Milk secretion and ejection are driven by prolactin and oxytocin respectively, while progesterone promotes alveolar rather than ductal development.'),
('41a2380a-745c-506e-94a5-3374d44776d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Progesterone stimulates:', '["Only milk secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobuloalveolar development (alveogenesis)","Only milk ejection mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ductal growth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobuloalveolar development (alveogenesis)', 'Progesterone stimulates lobuloalveolar development (alveogenesis) of the breast. Estrogen drives ductal growth, prolactin initiates milk production, and oxytocin triggers milk ejection.'),
('0a621af5-1048-5f9c-a8bd-865dad2f8e9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Prolactin stimulates:', '["None","Milk ejection","Milk production (lactogenesis)","Alveolar growth","Ductal growth"]'::jsonb, 'Milk production (lactogenesis)', 'Prolactin promotes milk production (lactogenesis) by the secretory alveoli. Milk ejection is mediated by oxytocin, while ductal and alveolar growth are driven by estrogen and progesterone.'),
('143a8c7c-4ecb-519d-8b9b-3727e0835b52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Oxytocin stimulates:', '["Ductal growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Milk ejection (myoepithelial contraction)","Milk production mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar growth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nipple growth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Milk ejection (myoepithelial contraction)', 'Oxytocin contracts myoepithelial cells and expels milk from the alveoli (milk ejection or let-down). Prolactin produces milk, and estrogen and progesterone govern ductal and alveolar growth.'),
('411ade1f-2b74-5c88-8ce1-672357489dcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Human placental lactogen (hPL) during pregnancy:', '["None","Inhibits lactation","Stimulates milk production","Stimulates milk ejection","Contributes to breast development"]'::jsonb, 'Contributes to breast development', 'hPL rises during pregnancy and contributes to lobuloalveolar breast development, preparing the gland for lactation. It does not eject milk or inhibit breast development.'),
('07b89c72-39c2-5afa-81f3-f5de0400d54e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The high levels of estrogen and progesterone during pregnancy:', '["Stimulate milk ejection resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibit milk secretion (by blocking prolactin effect)","Have effect on milk production mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulate milk secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibit milk secretion (by blocking prolactin effect)', 'The high estrogen and progesterone levels of pregnancy inhibit milk secretion by blocking the action of prolactin; secretion begins after delivery when these hormones fall. The other options describe opposite or unrelated effects.'),
('9e5a5543-b1de-5b9e-966b-6d28781eeecc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Suckling stimulates the release of:', '["Prolactin and oxytocin via neuroendocrine reflex","Progesterone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only oxytocin mediated by specific receptor activation","Only prolactin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Estrogen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin and oxytocin via neuroendocrine reflex', 'Suckling triggers a neuroendocrine reflex that releases both prolactin (milk production) and oxytocin (milk ejection). Releasing only one hormone is incomplete, and estrogen and progesterone are not released by suckling.'),
('7daf7b4b-6760-545d-9a34-f529f3d4e291'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The milk ejection reflex (let-down reflex) involves:', '["Hypothalamic-anterior pituitary-prolactin pathway","None","Local reflex only","Spinal reflex only","Hypothalamic-posterior pituitary-oxytocin pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic-posterior pituitary-oxytocin pathway', 'The milk ejection (let-down) reflex travels via the hypothalamic-posterior pituitary-oxytocin pathway. Prolactin release and milk production are governed by the anterior pituitary pathway.'),
('33afaf33-d627-53bd-a377-435c5d2c664a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The interruption of the milk ejection reflex by stress is due to:', '["Decreased prolactin resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased estrogen mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibition of oxytocin release by sympathetic activation","Decreased progesterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of oxytocin release by sympathetic activation', 'Stress and sympathetic activation inhibit oxytocin release, impairing the let-down reflex. Prolactin, estrogen, and progesterone levels are not the direct cause of stress-related failure of milk ejection.'),
('5198b03b-3dcf-53c8-b70c-c722f728e80a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The Montgomery''s glands secrete:', '["Sebaceous material to protect areola and nipple","Sweat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pheromones mediated by specific receptor activation","Wax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Milk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sebaceous material to protect areola and nipple', 'Montgomery''s (areolar) glands are modified sebaceous glands that secrete oily sebaceous material lubricating and protecting the nipple and areola during breastfeeding. They do not produce milk, sweat, or wax.'),
('0d2df9e9-37c0-5c9f-a76e-7f3e7b18b8d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Hidradenitis suppurativa of the breast affects:', '["Apocrine sweat glands in the areola","Cooper''s ligaments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mammary ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nipple leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apocrine sweat glands in the areola', 'Hidradenitis suppurativa is an inflammatory disease of apocrine sweat glands, which in the breast region are concentrated in the areola. It does not primarily affect Cooper''s ligaments, mammary ducts, vessels, or nipple skin.'),
('dfb0559c-2737-56a6-9e59-08c95a9feb8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Mondor''s disease of the breast is:', '["Lymphangitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Arteritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fat necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Duct ectasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thrombophlebitis of superficial veins (thoracoepigastric vein)"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombophlebitis of superficial veins (thoracoepigastric vein)', 'Mondor''s disease is a superficial thrombophlebitis, typically of the thoracoepigastric vein. Lymphangitis, arteritis, fat necrosis, and duct ectasia are distinct breast pathologies.'),
('59ec294c-9f79-5b32-ae0a-0af0811267b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracoepigastric vein connects:', '["Lateral thoracic vein to superficial epigastric vein","Axillary to femoral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal thoracic to superior epigastric without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cephalic to basilic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral thoracic vein to superficial epigastric vein', 'The thoracoepigastric vein runs along the lateral thoracic wall and connects the lateral thoracic vein (axillary system) with the superficial epigastric vein (femoral system). The other pairings describe unrelated vessels.'),
('95f09dda-ce92-59c3-8be1-25f468558433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The thoracoepigastric vein is important as:', '["The main chest vein resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main arm vein mediated by specific receptor activation","A collateral venous channel between axillary and femoral veins","The main breast vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collateral venous channel between axillary and femoral veins', 'The thoracoepigastric vein forms a collateral venous channel between the axillary and femoral veins and may enlarge in vena caval obstruction. The other options misstate its role.'),
('4800124b-c9b4-55fb-ad70-6652072445ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The breast is located in which layer of the thoracic wall?', '["Mammary layer","Superficial fascia","Deep fascia","Subcutaneous tissue","Areolar layer"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial fascia', 'The breast lies within the superficial fascia of the anterior thoracic wall, between the skin and the deep pectoral fascia. The gland does not sit in the deep fascia or subcutaneous tissue.'),
('ae85e3d5-f7cd-519a-afc2-a18dc629c7e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The pectoral fascia is an example of:', '["None","Deep fascia","Superficial fascia","Subserous fascia","Axillary fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia', 'The pectoral fascia is a sheet of deep fascia covering the pectoralis major. Superficial fascia houses the breast, while subserous fascia lines body cavities.'),
('893a1cf2-29c3-532e-89f7-a6a16b74a596'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The axillary fascia is continuous with:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Clavipectoral fascia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pectoral fascia and brachial fascia","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial fascia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoral fascia and brachial fascia', 'The axillary fascia blends with the pectoral fascia anteriorly and with the brachial fascia of the arm inferiorly. It is not limited to the clavipectoral or superficial fascia.'),
('f331a8c3-d167-58e6-90c4-fabd923d1d45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The clavipectoral fascia (costocoracoid membrane) is thickened between:', '["First rib and coracoid process (costocoracoid ligament)","Clavicle and coracoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternum and clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Clavicle and first rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First rib and coracoid process (costocoracoid ligament)', 'The clavipectoral fascia thickens between the first rib and the coracoid process to form the costocoracoid ligament (costocoracoid membrane). The clavicle, sternum, and other bony landmarks are not its sites of thickening.'),
('dbb68b1f-b4c0-503a-b693-7ec819afa288'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The suspensory ligament of the breast (Cooper''s ligaments) are:', '["Part of the clavipectoral fascia","Part of the pectoral fascia","Muscular bands","Ligaments connecting breast to ribs","Fibrous bands connecting skin to deep fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous bands connecting skin to deep fascia', 'Cooper''s ligaments are fibrous bands that run from the skin to the deep fascia, supporting the breast between the lobules. They are not part of the pectoral or clavipectoral fascia and are not muscular.'),
('3f9dd0e6-db4f-59c8-8e1c-6006c0783cf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'The retromammary space (bursa) contains:', '["Muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Glandular tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loose areolar connective tissue (allowing free movement)","Dense fibrous tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fat only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose areolar connective tissue (allowing free movement)', 'The retromammary space (bursa) is a layer of loose areolar connective tissue behind the breast gland that allows movement over the pectoral fascia. Dense fibrous tissue, muscle, or fat alone do not fill this space.'),
('98bda588-6749-5e31-b12d-14f8e1a21eef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('88c9fd73-3e0f-5aab-b50b-a58162164966'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'During therapeutic management, Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('9a0c7fd0-ecbb-553d-80fa-9417ba0251b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Regarding clinical cases: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('02e6e711-e7df-5430-98bf-85504a3a4da0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'In medical practice, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('73f82eb9-8e20-5ee2-bf7f-71791999ba9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('7f6432b0-1c40-5d43-aaa7-ddad7ad98ea5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Which option correctly describes the suspensory ligaments of Cooper?', '["Blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suspensory ligaments of axilla mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous bands from skin to deep fascia between lobules","Nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous bands from skin to deep fascia between lobules', 'Cooper''s suspensory ligaments are fibrous bands that extend from the skin to the deep fascia between the lobules, supporting the breast. Blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves are not their components.'),
('a3a1b3f8-d77f-5c0e-8487-2ab2b8864b65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'During therapeutic management, Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('52412c8c-927c-5677-9116-3f84b4cb1d9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Regarding clinical cases: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('f64eef34-ee92-5fa9-bee3-d8ffd6272036'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'In medical practice, Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('56242bb5-6e08-5a2a-9632-26af2639068d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('37490c09-3e98-5255-8d40-a9b341ae7698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('b183ee50-a84c-5aaa-9ef8-2e7ec9725010'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Breast', 'During therapeutic management, Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('e987ed3f-a9d4-5bd0-bff9-967e22c021c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial nerve (before bifurcation) supplies which muscles of the forearm?', '["Hand intrinsics resulting in decreased functional activity","Brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)","All extensors mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexor muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pronator muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)', 'In the arm the radial nerve gives branches to brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus proximal to its bifurcation into superficial and deep (posterior interosseous) branches. The forearm flexors, pronators, and intrinsic hand muscles are innervated by median and ulnar fibres, not by the radial nerve.'),
('aeaa84b5-a2b1-5b45-820a-3ed2039596ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of radial) supplies all extensor muscles except:', '["Extensor digitorum resulting in decreased functional activity","ECRL (supplied by radial nerve before bifurcation)","ECRB mediated by specific receptor activation","Abductor pollicis longus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extensor carpi ulnaris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ECRL (supplied by radial nerve before bifurcation)', 'Brachioradialis and ECRL receive their branches from the radial nerve before it divides, so they are not supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve. The deep branch supplies ECRB, supinator, and the remaining extensor muscles of the forearm.'),
('ded69e5f-0945-580f-99f7-5086a2d899ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior interosseous nerve supplies:', '["supinator","ECU","ECRB","EDM","ED"]'::jsonb, 'ECRB', 'The posterior interosseous nerve is the deep branch of the radial nerve and innervates ECRB, supinator, and the remaining muscles of the extensor compartment. The other listed muscles are also reached through this deep branch as it descends the forearm.'),
('a6ba2cc7-70d8-5b1b-a736-3d932227c43f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) supplies:', '["Triceps","biceps brachii","Deltoid and teres minor","Coracobrachialis","brachialis"]'::jsonb, 'Coracobrachialis', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) pierces and supplies the three anterior arm muscles: coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, and brachialis. Triceps is radial territory, while deltoid and teres minor are innervated by the axillary nerve.'),
('c30d87c2-f6a1-593b-b64a-6bdebca37ca6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The recurrent branch of median nerve supplies:', '["Abductor pollicis brevis","Hypothenar muscles","superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis","All thenar muscles including adductor pollicis","opponens pollicis"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis', 'The recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve innervates abductor pollicis brevis, opponens pollicis, and the superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis. Adductor pollicis and the deep head of flexor pollicis brevis are ulnar territory, so the ''all thenar muscles'' option is false, and the hypothenar muscles are ulnar-supplied.'),
('40502074-7ac7-5083-97fa-5aa07137369b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) supplies:', '["Rhomboid major","Levator scapulae only","Rhomboids only","rhomboid minor","and levator scapulae"]'::jsonb, 'Rhomboid major', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) descends along the medial border of the scapula to innervate the rhomboid major and rhomboid minor, and often the levator scapulae as well. Rhomboid major is one of the muscles named for this nerve, whereas the other options give only partial or incorrectly limited lists.'),
('807546c6-db6f-5b76-99de-3c2b7a2c4c8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) supplies:', '["Pectoralis major","Rhomboids","Levator scapulae","Trapezius","Serratus anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) arises from the roots of the brachial plexus and descends on the lateral chest wall to supply the serratus anterior. Pectoralis major receives the pectoral nerves, the rhomboids receive the dorsal scapular nerve, levator scapulae the dorsal scapular and C3-C4 fibres, and the trapezius the spinal accessory nerve.'),
('a589a6b7-c7f6-5c72-8809-bf8db31dc9e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8) supplies:', '["Deltoid","Teres major","Latissimus dorsi","Subscapularis","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8) arises from the posterior cord and supplies the latissimus dorsi. Deltoid is axillary territory, subscapularis receives the upper and lower subscapular nerves, teres major the lower subscapular nerve, and pectoralis major the pectoral nerves.'),
('3782ed5d-185f-5ad1-b777-8ef357031941'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius muscle is supplied by:', '["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","4 (proprioceptive)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The trapezius is innervated mainly by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with proprioceptive contributions from the C3-C4 roots. The dorsal scapular, suprascapular, and long thoracic nerves are brachial plexus branches serving scapular and serratus muscles, not the trapezius.'),
('d3bd5b8b-cd18-5783-ae7c-e7f1b6b06309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The levator scapulae is supplied by:', '["Long thoracic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Suprascapular nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3', 'The levator scapulae receives its main innervation from the dorsal scapular nerve (C5) together with branches from the C3-C4 roots. The long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior, the suprascapular nerve supplies the spinati, and the accessory nerve supplies the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.'),
('c68bcf5e-043c-58dd-a0bf-674265fab481'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid major and minor are supplied by:', '["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve","Subscapular nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) is the sole motor supply to both the rhomboid major and rhomboid minor. The suprascapular nerve serves the supraspinatus and infraspinatus, the long thoracic nerve the serratus anterior, the accessory nerve the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid, and the subscapular nerves the subscapularis and teres major.'),
('f64ef65b-b419-51aa-beee-9f73eb1a2126'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The latissimus dorsi is supplied by:', '["Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)","Upper subscapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Lower subscapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)', 'The latissimus dorsi is innervated by the thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8), which arises from the posterior cord. The subscapular nerves supply subscapularis and teres major, the axillary nerve supplies deltoid and teres minor, and the suprascapular nerve supplies the spinati muscles.'),
('613fbded-1c23-5db3-9e4b-50e0660bf79e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pectoralis major (sternocostal head) is supplied by:', '["Thoracodorsal","Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves","Suprascapular","Only medial pectoral","Only lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'The sternocostal head of pectoralis major receives fibres from both the lateral and medial pectoral nerves, whereas the clavicular head is innervated mainly by the lateral pectoral nerve. The thoracodorsal and suprascapular nerves do not supply the pectoral muscles.'),
('61424c10-7d6b-50f8-9862-3f82d987da59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachialis (lateral part) is supplied by:', '["Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Radial nerve (medial part)","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The lateral part of brachialis is innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7), the main nerve of the anterior arm. The median, axillary, and ulnar nerves supply other compartments, and only a small medial strip of brachialis receives a radial nerve branch.'),
('43693bfa-7344-5c94-8a20-2c8ae1af71d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachialis (medial part) is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-7)', 'A small medial part of brachialis receives a branch from the radial nerve (C5-7), while the main body of the muscle is musculocutaneous territory. The median, ulnar, and axillary nerves do not contribute to the anterior arm flexors.'),
('f21fddcf-3308-5dba-a5a3-d87326a8c432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (long head) is supplied by:', '["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The long head of triceps is supplied by a branch of the radial nerve (C6-8) given off proximal to the spiral groove. The axillary, median, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves have no supply to the triceps.'),
('ea54b5a7-fb13-5449-8459-60e0b651b395'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (lateral head) is supplied by:', '["Suprascapular nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The lateral head of triceps is innervated by the radial nerve (C6-8), with its branch arising in the proximal arm. The suprascapular, median, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves serve other muscles and do not enter the posterior arm compartment.'),
('e8d7d72d-6026-5e33-9404-7aeef560a3d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (medial head) is supplied by:', '["Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The medial head of triceps receives its branch from the radial nerve (C6-8). The median, ulnar, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves do not innervate the triceps muscle.'),
('e7f970c7-0d12-555c-a7e2-5881a6ac7907'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by:', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2', 'The sternocleidomastoid is innervated by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with contributing fibres from the C2 root. The hypoglossal nerve supplies the tongue, the facial nerve the facial muscles, and the vagus does not serve this muscle.'),
('d455c397-8eea-5f8b-a314-1736732f4ec6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius is supplied by:', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Dorsal scapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","4"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The trapezius receives its major innervation from the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with proprioceptive fibres from the C3-C4 roots. The dorsal scapular, long thoracic, and suprascapular nerves supply scapular and serratus muscles, not the trapezius.'),
('eafda191-23d1-5792-a746-a2b45608fda4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid major is supplied by:', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The rhomboid major is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5). The long thoracic and accessory nerves serve other shoulder girdle muscles, and the suprascapular and subscapular nerves are not involved with the rhomboids.'),
('4cf9030b-2425-5b7e-9210-ec420417dbe8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid minor is supplied by:', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The rhomboid minor is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5), which also supplies the rhomboid major. The suprascapular, subscapular, long thoracic, and accessory nerves do not innervate the rhomboid muscles.'),
('ee6e743e-41a4-55e6-af69-0385a96c242e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (upper part) is supplied by:', '["Accessory nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The upper digitations of serratus anterior receive their branch from the long thoracic nerve (C5-7), which supplies the whole muscle along the lateral chest wall. The accessory, thoracodorsal, dorsal scapular, and suprascapular nerves innervate other muscles.'),
('6f9db6ad-3eb2-5868-9d99-7d6823a9182b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (middle part) is supplied by:', '["Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The middle digitations of serratus anterior are innervated by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The suprascapular, thoracodorsal, accessory, and dorsal scapular nerves supply different shoulder girdle muscles rather than the serratus anterior.'),
('43414635-7e87-5ba9-93cf-fd6c2971a5e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (lower part) is supplied by:', '["Suprascapular nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The lower digitations of serratus anterior receive their supply from the long thoracic nerve (C5-7), which serves the entire muscle. The suprascapular, accessory, dorsal scapular, and thoracodorsal nerves do not innervate the serratus anterior.'),
('0ae2bfac-2d1a-5c17-9864-b7076109f2e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies all three heads of triceps brachii?', '["Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'All three heads of the triceps brachii receive their innervation from branches of the radial nerve (C6-8), each branch arising proximal to the spiral groove. The median, ulnar, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves do not supply the posterior arm compartment.'),
('61604bb8-441e-52c8-a8a2-d537576b93c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which two muscles are supplied by the axillary nerve?', '["Deltoid and teres major","Deltoid and teres minor","Biceps and brachialis","Supraspinatus and infraspinatus","Triceps and anconeus"]'::jsonb, 'Deltoid and teres minor', 'The axillary nerve (C5-6) passes through the quadrangular space with the posterior circumflex humeral artery to supply the deltoid and teres minor. Deltoid and teres major are supplied by the lower subscapular nerve, the spinati by the suprascapular nerve, and triceps by the radial nerve.'),
('0814acf6-02c0-588d-9cce-085c4563d130'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the brachialis muscle (medial part)?', '["Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-7)', 'A small medial part of the brachialis receives a branch from the radial nerve (C5-7), while the principal supply is the musculocutaneous nerve. The ulnar, median, and axillary nerves do not contribute to this anterior arm flexor.'),
('5d1cf8ad-13c2-5e1f-b4d5-e40dca6eecf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the brachialis muscle (lateral part)?', '["Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The lateral part of the brachialis is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7), the nerve that innervates the anterior arm. The ulnar, median, and axillary nerves do not contribute, and only a small medial strip of the muscle is radial territory.'),
('0d36bbd5-63ee-5615-8009-43ddda40602e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the coracobrachialis?', '["Axillary nerve","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The coracobrachialis is pierced and supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7), which it pierces en route to the biceps. The axillary, median, ulnar, and radial nerves do not innervate this anterior arm muscle.'),
('9f8a6233-657f-5729-ba2a-7b65e22e37d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Name the muscle(s) supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve.', '["ECU","ED","ECRB","EDM","supinator"]'::jsonb, 'ECRB', 'ECRB receives its innervation from the deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve) of the radial nerve. The posterior interosseous trunk also supplies supinator as it pierces it and the remaining extensor muscles as it descends the forearm.'),
('313f902f-7895-51af-879d-dee938ef7156'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which muscle supplied by the radial nerve is the first to be affected in radial nerve palsy?', '["Brachioradialis","Triceps","Extensor digitorum","ECRL","Anconeus"]'::jsonb, 'Brachioradialis', 'Brachioradialis loses function early in radial nerve palsy, making it a sensitive early clinical indicator of radial nerve damage. Triceps and anconeus may be spared when the lesion is at the spiral groove, and the more distal muscles are affected later in the lesion.'),
('9fc58c76-7d95-5fbd-a22b-e3e1d5e93097'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior interosseous nerve syndrome (PIN syndrome) spares which muscle?', '["ECRL","APL","Extensor digitorum","ECU","ECRB"]'::jsonb, 'ECRL', 'In posterior interosseous nerve syndrome the branch to ECRL leaves the radial nerve proximal to the site of compression, so radial wrist deviation and some extensors are preserved and only a weak wrist drop develops. APL, extensor digitorum, and ECU lie in posterior interosseous territory and become paralysed.'),
('d4528295-db92-59bc-a3ed-dc25ce6430b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for ulnar nerve function is:', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor indicis","Flexor pollicis longus","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'First dorsal interosseous', 'The first dorsal interosseous, supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve, is readily isolated and is the usual choice for testing ulnar motor function. The opposing thumb muscles and flexor pollicis longus are largely median territory, and extensor indicis is radial-supplied.'),
('4a8f43af-2082-5a1a-b0f7-679b384bb458'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for median nerve function (in hand) is:', '["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)', 'Abductor pollicis brevis is innervated by the recurrent branch of the median nerve and is the standard clinical target for assessing median motor function in the hand. Flexor carpi ulnaris, adductor pollicis, and first dorsal interosseous are ulnar-supplied, and extensor pollicis longus is radial territory.'),
('98432cc6-534e-55e8-9c0b-ff579581235d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for radial nerve function is:', '["Anconeus","Triceps","Brachioradialis","Extensor pollicis longus","Extensor digitorum"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor pollicis longus', 'Extension of the thumb against resistance, produced by extensor pollicis longus, is the most commonly used test of distal radial nerve (posterior interosseous) function. The other named muscles are also radial-innervated but are not the usual clinical test target.'),
('a286ff9b-9c45-5cd8-bcd4-52ce66e317df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In a complete radial nerve injury at the spiral groove, which muscles are paralysed?', '["Triceps resulting in decreased functional activity","which muscles are paralysed? accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Triceps only mediated by specific receptor activation","brachioradialis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All extensors of forearm (except brachioradialis and ECRL may still work)"]'::jsonb, 'All extensors of forearm (except brachioradialis and ECRL may still work)', 'A spiral groove lesion paralyses the muscles innervated below the groove, producing wrist drop, while the triceps heads supplied above the lesion remain functional. The extensor compartment of the forearm is the group affected, whereas the triceps and single-muscle options understate the deficit.'),
('d6cf215b-7d4e-5ba7-9aab-5a644eb54ff1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In a complete radial nerve injury at the axilla, which muscles are affected?', '["which muscles are affected?","ECRL","anconeus","Triceps (all heads)","brachioradialis"]'::jsonb, 'Triceps (all heads)', 'At the axilla the entire radial nerve is involved, so all three heads of the triceps are paralysed, which distinguishes it from a spiral groove lesion where the long and lateral heads escape. ECRL, brachioradialis, and anconeus are also affected but do not carry the same discriminating significance.'),
('0f1248d7-e517-5108-ac32-f96ff12201fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In an ulnar nerve injury at the elbow, which muscles are affected?', '["Thenar muscles","which forearm muscles are affected?","FCU and ulnar half of FDP","FCR and PL","Extensor muscles"]'::jsonb, 'FCU and ulnar half of FDP', 'At the elbow the ulnar nerve supplies flexor carpi ulnaris and the ulnar (medial) half of flexor digitorum profundus, so both are paralysed by an elbow-level lesion. FCR, palmaris longus, and the extensor muscles are median or radial territory and are spared.'),
('02a16735-f25d-5791-86d6-1dfe8aecd676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In an ulnar nerve injury at the wrist, which muscles are affected?', '["FCU and FDP (medial half) resulting in decreased functional activity","Flexor muscles of forearm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All intrinsic hand muscles supplied by ulnar nerve (not forearm muscles)","which muscles are affected? without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thenar muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic hand muscles supplied by ulnar nerve (not forearm muscles)', 'The ulnar nerve gives off its forearm branches, FCU and the ulnar half of FDP, proximal to the wrist, so a wrist-level injury spares the forearm flexors. Only the intrinsic hand muscles supplied by the ulnar nerve are affected; thenar muscles are median-supplied.'),
('7873f737-5fd0-5e61-a55e-8ae5ce77120d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which muscle is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve but is also partly supplied by the radial nerve?', '["Triceps","Brachialis","Deltoid","Biceps","Coracobrachialis"]'::jsonb, 'Brachialis', 'Brachialis is supplied mainly by the musculocutaneous nerve, with a part of the muscle also receiving a branch from the radial nerve. Triceps and deltoid are single-nerve muscles (radial and axillary respectively), and biceps and coracobrachialis are musculocutaneous-only.'),
('4caab076-67fa-59b0-9f7f-ccf59eee1993'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which muscle is supplied by both median and ulnar nerves?', '["Palmaris longus resulting in decreased functional activity","Flexor carpi ulnaris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flexor digitorum profundus (through different halves)","Flexor digitorum superficialis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pronator teres leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor digitorum profundus (through different halves)', 'Flexor digitorum profundus has dual innervation: its lateral slips from the anterior interosseous branch of the median nerve and its medial slips from the ulnar nerve. Palmaris longus, flexor digitorum superficialis, and pronator teres are median-only, and flexor carpi ulnaris is ulnar-only.'),
('bfa9beb9-74ae-5800-ab96-5018a8b2ea28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial nerve is the terminal branch of which cord?', '["Lateral cord","Upper trunk","Lower trunk","Posterior cord","Medial cord"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The radial nerve is the large terminal branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. The lateral cord terminates as the musculocutaneous nerve and lateral root of the median, and the medial cord as the ulnar nerve and medial root of the median.'),
('99a1bc6d-8dcc-5afd-9185-e7cc81b67434'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The axillary nerve is the terminal branch of which cord?', '["Posterior cord","Upper trunk","Lower trunk","Lateral cord","Medial cord"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The axillary nerve is the smaller terminal branch of the posterior cord, arising alongside the radial nerve and accompanying the posterior circumflex humeral artery through the quadrangular space. The lateral and medial cords supply the anterior arm and hand nerves instead.'),
('a6024114-eec0-50bc-b4b2-1c3251be67e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The musculocutaneous nerve is the terminal branch of which cord?', '["Medial cord","Posterior cord","Lateral cord","Lower trunk","Upper trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cord', 'The musculocutaneous nerve is the lateral terminal branch of the lateral cord of the brachial plexus. The posterior cord gives the radial and axillary nerves, and the medial cord gives the ulnar nerve and medial root of the median.'),
('271a0760-2753-57ff-b65c-3491f1189a78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral pectoral nerve (C5-7) arises from:', '["Posterior cord","Lateral root of median","Medial cord","Lateral cord","Upper trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cord', 'The lateral pectoral nerve (C5-7) arises from the lateral cord and, with the medial pectoral nerve, supplies the pectoral muscles. The medial pectoral nerve arises from the medial cord, and the other structures are not sources of this nerve.'),
('da8d7de1-a0b3-54f3-83c8-e9ab82bdb463'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The anterior division of the lower trunk forms which cord?', '["Posterior cord","Upper trunk","None","Lateral cord","Medial cord"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cord', 'The anterior division of the lower trunk contributes to the medial cord, the rest of which comes from the anterior division of this trunk. The lateral cord is formed from the anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks, and the posterior cord from the posterior divisions of all three trunks.'),
('51a219f2-0411-5f78-98cb-eea89e140c91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks form which cord?', '["Medial cord","Upper trunk","Lateral cord","Posterior cord","None"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cord', 'The anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks unite to form the lateral cord. The posterior cord is formed by the posterior divisions of all three trunks, and the medial cord by the anterior division of the lower trunk.'),
('a5f3413b-6a8f-5af2-ab91-e8a1195eaeab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior divisions of all three trunks form which cord?', '["Lateral cord","Medial cord","None","Posterior cord","Upper trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The posterior divisions of the upper, middle, and lower trunks unite to form the posterior cord, which gives off the radial, axillary, thoracodorsal, and subscapular nerves. The anterior divisions form the lateral and medial cords instead.'),
('87b9a0bd-c778-5292-8cc8-a5a8187cde5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The first part of the axillary artery gives which branch?', '["Superior thoracic artery","Subscapular artery","Thoracoacromial artery","Posterior circumflex humeral","Lateral thoracic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thoracic artery', 'The first part of the axillary artery, between the lateral border of the first rib and the upper border of pectoralis minor, gives off only the superior thoracic artery. The thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries arise from the second part, and the subscapular and circumflex humeral arteries from the third part.'),
('5787b454-4120-5574-9288-844a6de876ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The second part of the axillary artery gives which two branches?', '["Superior thoracic and thoracoacromial","Thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries","Subscapular and circumflex humeral","Superior thoracic only","None"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries', 'The axillary artery is divided into three parts by the pectoralis minor. The second part, deep to pectoralis minor, gives off the thoracoacromial trunk and the lateral thoracic artery, while the superior thoracic arises from the first part and the subscapular and circumflex humeral arteries from the third part.'),
('90bcda6d-87e4-54ff-bed5-c23cf31e0110'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The third part of the axillary artery gives which three branches?', '["lateral thoracic","Thoracoacromial","posterior circumflex humeral","anterior circumflex humeral","Subscapular"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapular', 'Distal to the pectoralis minor, the third part of the axillary artery gives off the subscapular artery and the anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries. The subscapular artery is the largest, dividing into the circumflex scapular and thoracodorsal branches; lateral thoracic and thoracoacromial arteries arise from the second part.'),
('0c2ecf22-ac30-5653-8467-47d01988b044'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The largest branch of the axillary artery is:', '["Subscapular artery","Posterior circumflex humeral","Thoracoacromial artery","Superior thoracic artery","Lateral thoracic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapular artery', 'The subscapular artery is the largest branch of the axillary artery. It runs along the lower border of the subscapularis and divides into the circumflex scapular and thoracodorsal arteries.'),
('9f1679e6-075c-5d76-8aeb-d307b1735e41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachial artery gives off which major branch?', '["Subscapular artery","Profunda brachii (deep brachial) artery","Lateral thoracic artery","Superior thoracic artery","Thoracoacromial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Profunda brachii (deep brachial) artery', 'At the lower border of the teres major, the brachial artery gives off its largest branch, the profunda brachii (deep brachial) artery, which accompanies the radial nerve in the spiral groove. The other listed vessels are axillary artery branches.'),
('4e76f4ed-d85b-5bf8-b7a5-07245e9c0e64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The profunda brachii artery supplies:', '["The elbow joint resulting in decreased functional activity","The hand accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior compartment of the arm (triceps) and accompanies the radial nerve","The anterior compartment of the arm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The forearm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior compartment of the arm (triceps) and accompanies the radial nerve', 'The profunda brachii artery runs in the spiral groove with the radial nerve and supplies the posterior compartment of the arm, chiefly the triceps brachii. The anterior compartment is supplied by the brachial artery and its branches.'),
('e186f1a1-4dfa-5e85-a307-2fb2bb4f9f73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve accompanies the profunda brachii artery in the spiral groove?', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'The profunda brachii artery accompanies the radial nerve along the spiral groove of the humerus. Because the nerve lies directly on bone, it is vulnerable in midshaft humeral fractures.'),
('62a5b961-2a55-5fe1-9d56-ab2a902b1f43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior ulnar collateral artery accompanies which nerve?', '["Radial nerve","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve', 'The superior ulnar collateral artery arises from the brachial artery and accompanies the ulnar nerve, passing behind the medial epicondyle to join the elbow anastomosis. This course explains ulnar nerve injury with medial epicondyle fractures.'),
('5023438f-08bd-5fec-974b-e59d8a7db6a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial artery is a branch of the:', '["Ulnar artery","Subclavian artery","Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)","Interosseous artery","Axillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)', 'The brachial artery divides at the neck of the radius in the cubital fossa into the radial and ulnar arteries. The radial artery is the smaller terminal branch, running distally under the brachioradialis.'),
('2d0b5acb-47ed-5819-ba78-f4fdfc7a1459'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar artery is a branch of the:', '["Axillary artery","Interosseous artery","Subclavian artery","Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)","Radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)', 'The ulnar artery is the larger terminal branch of the brachial artery, given off in the cubital fossa. It passes deep to the forearm flexor muscles toward the medial side.'),
('ef0f1d54-e206-536f-a548-682f42addcda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common interosseous artery is a branch of:', '["Interosseous artery","Ulnar artery","Anterior interosseous artery","Brachial artery","Radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar artery', 'The common interosseous artery arises from the ulnar artery in the proximal forearm, just distal to the cubital fossa. It promptly divides into the anterior and posterior interosseous arteries.'),
('7ee6d712-1962-5a9b-85e3-eb681167b04c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common interosseous artery divides into:', '["Anterior interosseous and posterior interosseous arteries","Radial and ulnar arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial and deep branches mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Median and ulnar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interosseous and posterior interosseous arteries', 'The common interosseous artery divides into the anterior interosseous artery, which runs distally on the interosseous membrane, and the posterior interosseous artery, which reaches the posterior compartment.'),
('655b5a30-93ff-5d82-8e72-4ffa778ca247'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior interosseous artery pierces which structure to reach the posterior compartment?', '["Supinator muscle","None","Flexor retinaculum","Extensor retinaculum","Interosseous membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Interosseous membrane', 'The posterior interosseous artery passes through the proximal part of the interosseous membrane to reach the posterior compartment of the forearm, where it runs between the superficial and deep extensor muscles.'),
('5b1a5964-cf28-5d56-b73a-e42c4a84a0c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior interosseous artery gives rise to the:', '["Anterior interosseous recurrent","Common interosseous","Posterior interosseous recurrent artery","Radial recurrent","Ulnar recurrent"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous recurrent artery', 'Soon after entering the posterior compartment, the posterior interosseous artery gives off the posterior interosseous recurrent (also called interosseous recurrent) artery, which ascends to join the anastomosis around the lateral epicondyle.'),
('40bf0f98-67a7-545d-b446-eba412ddec1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial recurrent artery arises from:', '["Radial artery","Profunda brachii","Ulnar artery","Brachial artery","Interosseous artery"]'::jsonb, 'Radial artery', 'The radial recurrent artery arises from the radial artery just distal to the elbow and ascends to supply the brachioradialis and contribute to the periarticular elbow anastomosis.'),
('c6a935ee-a691-5fdc-9a56-528c644c37f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar recurrent arteries arise from:', '["Brachial artery","Ulnar artery","Profunda brachii","Interosseous artery","Radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar artery', 'The anterior and posterior ulnar recurrent arteries arise from the ulnar artery near its origin and ascend around the elbow to take part in the elbow anastomosis.'),
('70fc19bc-f607-5e9e-9d85-a200cc9ab997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial collateral artery is a branch of:', '["Brachial artery","Profunda brachii artery","Superior ulnar collateral","Ulnar artery","Radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Profunda brachii artery', 'The profunda brachii artery ends by dividing into the radial collateral and medial collateral arteries. The radial collateral branch accompanies the radial nerve through the lateral intermuscular septum to the elbow.'),
('c7cf4ed9-a817-5128-91ad-dfebb744652a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial collateral artery (posterior descending) is a branch of:', '["Superior ulnar collateral","Radial artery","Brachial artery","Profunda brachii artery","Ulnar artery"]'::jsonb, 'Profunda brachii artery', 'The medial collateral (posterior descending) artery is the other terminal branch of the profunda brachii artery. It descends over the medial epicondyle and joins the elbow anastomosis.'),
('08279a5a-7a3e-5644-ac04-c17ce7f084d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial palmar arch is completed laterally by:', '["Radialis indicis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial palmar branch of radial artery","Deep palmar branch of radial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Princeps pollicis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial palmar branch of radial artery', 'The superficial palmar arch is formed mainly by the ulnar artery and completed on the radial (lateral) side by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery. This arch gives rise to the common palmar digital arteries.'),
('cda5c9a0-c69a-5e1c-a787-2d576dc11110'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The deep palmar arch is completed medially by:', '["Median artery","Superficial branch of ulnar","Radial artery","Interosseous artery","Deep palmar branch of ulnar artery"]'::jsonb, 'Deep palmar branch of ulnar artery', 'The deep palmar arch is formed by the terminal part of the radial artery and completed on its medial side by the deep palmar branch of the ulnar artery. It gives off the palmar metacarpal arteries and recurrent branches.'),
('aaa7991b-2b45-5c4a-a8c7-f856e5dc38e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radialis indicis artery arises from:', '["Superficial palmar arch","Common digital artery","Ulnar artery","Radial artery (or princeps pollicis)","Deep palmar arch"]'::jsonb, 'Radial artery (or princeps pollicis)', 'The radialis indicis artery arises from the radial artery in the palm, either directly or as a branch of the princeps pollicis artery. It runs along the radial side of the index finger.'),
('8f8c3d29-b16d-5714-bc6c-d6bdeb78f067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common palmar digital arteries arise from the:', '["Palmar metacarpal arteries","Deep palmar arch","Radial artery","Superficial palmar arch","Ulnar artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial palmar arch', 'The superficial palmar arch, formed by the ulnar artery and completed by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery, gives rise to the three common palmar digital arteries, which further divide into proper digital arteries.'),
('db712c86-c4f8-5320-b57f-4fe1fe0461d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The palmar metacarpal arteries arise from the:', '["Common digital arteries","Proper digital arteries","Radial artery","Superficial palmar arch","Deep palmar arch"]'::jsonb, 'Deep palmar arch', 'The deep palmar arch gives rise to the palmar metacarpal arteries, which run distally in the interosseous spaces to join the common palmar digital arteries near the web spaces.'),
('6182b9ff-e158-5436-aefb-1c2783cfdb42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The median nerve gives sensation to which part of the hand?', '["Palmar side of radial 3.5 digits and their nail beds (distal dorsum)","Thenar eminence only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Whole palm mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal hand without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ulnar 1.5 digits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palmar side of radial 3.5 digits and their nail beds (distal dorsum)', 'The median nerve supplies the palmar skin of the radial three and a half digits, including the nail beds and distal dorsal skin of those digits via their palmar nerves. Injury at the wrist spares the central palm because the palmar cutaneous branch arises more proximally.'),
('767380b9-c7af-53a5-bed1-cfbe836a0436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar nerve gives sensation to which part of the hand?', '["Palmar and dorsal sides of ulnar 1.5 digits","Radial 3.5 digits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palm and dorsum of radial side mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal hand only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Whole palm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palmar and dorsal sides of ulnar 1.5 digits', 'The ulnar nerve supplies both the palmar and dorsal sides of the ulnar one and a half digits, i.e. the little finger and the ulnar half of the ring finger. The radial digits are supplied by the median nerve.'),
('dfc8475a-37d3-5059-8218-71a4a8fc52b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve supplies:', '["Dorsal hand resulting in decreased functional activity","Palmar side of fingers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothenar eminence mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin of the central palm and thenar eminence","Ulnar palm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the central palm and thenar eminence', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve arises proximal to the flexor retinaculum and supplies the skin of the central palm and thenar eminence. Because it does not pass through the carpal tunnel, this skin is spared in carpal tunnel syndrome.'),
('92e08c4f-ca64-56f0-9834-8e0beb755695'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve supplies:', '["Thenar eminence","Palmar side of fingers","Dorsal hand","Skin of the hypothenar eminence","Central palm"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the hypothenar eminence', 'The palmar cutaneous branch of the ulnar nerve arises proximal to Guyon''s canal and supplies the skin over the hypothenar eminence. Consequently this area is spared in injuries at the wrist.'),
('c9d070b2-1457-5f9e-b793-6f011e546751'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve supplies:', '["Ulnar palmar side resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal skin of radial side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palm mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal skin of the ulnar side of hand and little and ring fingers (proximal)","Thumb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal skin of the ulnar side of hand and little and ring fingers (proximal)', 'The dorsal branch of the ulnar nerve arises proximal to the flexor retinaculum and descends to supply the dorsal skin of the ulnar half of the hand, including the proximal dorsal aspect of the little and ring fingers.'),
('b03a93d5-0223-53fe-90ea-22bd9853f91c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm (terminal part of musculocutaneous) supplies:', '["Skin of the radial (lateral) side of the forearm","Dorsal hand accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palmar hand mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial forearm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal forearm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the radial (lateral) side of the forearm', 'After the musculocutaneous nerve emerges lateral to the biceps tendon, it continues as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, which supplies the skin of the radial (lateral) side of the forearm.'),
('a408637f-1293-50b8-b981-01193f1e5bf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm supplies:', '["Skin of the ulnar (medial) side of the forearm","Dorsum of hand accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal forearm mediated by specific receptor activation","Palm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radial side of forearm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the ulnar (medial) side of the forearm', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus and supplies the skin of the ulnar (medial) side of the forearm.'),
('553f7920-4d96-5081-b10c-7505139dcbab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the forearm supplies:', '["Skin of the posterior (dorsal) aspect of the forearm","Medial forearm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radial forearm mediated by specific receptor activation","Palm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hand leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the posterior (dorsal) aspect of the forearm', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the forearm is a branch of the radial nerve given off in the spiral groove. It supplies the skin of the posterior (dorsal) aspect of the forearm.'),
('4f35af6e-2b46-5de0-987b-4672bc67e35b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The intercostobrachial nerve (T2) supplies:', '["Hand resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin of the medial side of the upper arm (axilla)","Pectoral region mediated by specific receptor activation","Forearm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral arm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the medial side of the upper arm (axilla)', 'The intercostobrachial nerve, a lateral cutaneous branch of the second intercostal nerve (T2), supplies the skin of the medial side of the upper arm and the floor of the axilla. This dermatome is often spared in lesions of the medial cord branches.'),
('b6f7cc86-f8fd-52ee-b4da-965726f3a22a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the arm supplies:', '["Skin of the medial side of the arm","Hand accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forearm mediated by specific receptor activation","Pectoral region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shoulder region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the medial side of the arm', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the arm arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus and supplies the skin over the medial side of the arm, sharing the axillary skin with the intercostobrachial nerve.'),
('a7698b9e-f0d7-550c-a4ed-389271cacb20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The axillary nerve (sensory part) supplies skin over which area?', '["Forearm resulting in decreased functional activity","Shoulder tip accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial arm mediated by specific receptor activation","Regimental badge area (over deltoid)","Hand leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regimental badge area (over deltoid)', 'The axillary nerve gives off the upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm, which supplies the skin over the deltoid, i.e. the regimental badge area. Injury to the axillary nerve therefore also causes loss of sensation over this patch.'),
('ba1689a1-fb2b-50f4-b2fd-99787c7b7bb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Nerve injury that causes loss of shoulder abduction from 0-15 degrees:', '["Suprascapular nerve (paralysis of supraspinatus)","Accessory nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal scapular nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Long thoracic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular nerve (paralysis of supraspinatus)', 'The suprascapular nerve supplies the supraspinatus, which initiates the first 15 degrees of shoulder abduction by steadying the humeral head in the glenoid. Paralysis of the supraspinatus therefore abolishes abduction in this initial range; the deltoid, supplied by the axillary nerve, then takes over from 15 to 90 degrees.'),
('60cea1ea-eb50-516a-a741-92ce9ec85272'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Nerve injury that causes loss of shoulder abduction from 15-90 degrees:', '["Long thoracic nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Axillary nerve (paralysis of deltoid)","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve (paralysis of deltoid)', 'The axillary nerve supplies the deltoid, the principal abductor of the shoulder through the 15-90 degrees range. With deltoid paralysis the middle range of abduction is lost, while the supraspinatus still produces the initial 0-15 degrees.'),
('56ffb9d9-0249-58fc-99ef-e2dc8b1fb34b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Nerve injury that causes loss of elbow flexion:', '["Radial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Median nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Musculocutaneous nerve (paralysis of biceps and brachialis)","Ulnar nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (paralysis of biceps and brachialis)', 'The musculocutaneous nerve innervates the biceps brachii and brachialis, the principal elbow flexors. Paralysis of these muscles after musculocutaneous nerve injury causes severe weakness of elbow flexion.'),
('2b2bb240-a151-5e43-830c-98ac51cb8101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Nerve injury that causes wrist drop:', '["Axillary nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial nerve (paralysis of wrist extensors)","Median nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Musculocutaneous nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ulnar nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (paralysis of wrist extensors)', 'The radial nerve supplies all the extensor muscles of the wrist and fingers. Radial nerve injury paralyzes these extensors, so the wrist cannot be extended and falls into flexion, producing wrist drop.'),
('bd059182-6d86-52ba-9289-b4f9998bbeac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Klumpke''s palsy involves which nerve roots?', '["C5","6","None of the above","T1","C8"]'::jsonb, 'C8', 'Klumpke''s palsy is an injury of the lower trunk of the brachial plexus, whose principal roots are C8 (and T1). It produces paralysis of the ulnar- and median-innervated intrinsic hand muscles, leading to a claw hand.'),
('1cdf284b-bb1c-5c7c-b8b7-77e5e8dd5a00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Klumpke''s palsy results in deformity of:', '["Foot drop","Waiter''s tip deformity","Dinner fork deformity","Wrist drop","Claw hand (intrinsic minus hand)"]'::jsonb, 'Claw hand (intrinsic minus hand)', 'Lower trunk injury in Klumpke''s palsy paralyses the intrinsic muscles of the hand. With the interossei and lumbricals weak, the unopposed long extensors hyperextend the metacarpophalangeal joints while the long flexors flex the fingers, producing a claw hand.'),
('b34ed5e9-fc59-5253-a39c-26ec79e2f12a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Erb-Duchenne palsy involves which nerve roots?', '["C5","None of the above","C8","T1","6"]'::jsonb, 'C5', 'Erb-Duchenne palsy results from injury to the upper trunk of the brachial plexus, formed by the C5 and C6 roots. It compromises the suprascapular, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves, causing the waiter''s tip posture.'),
('7a190d42-b501-5f87-9da1-3d2f90d798d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Erb-Duchenne palsy results in deformity of:', '["Foot drop","Claw hand","Waiter''s tip deformity","None","Wrist drop"]'::jsonb, 'Waiter''s tip deformity', 'In Erb-Duchenne palsy, paralysis of the supraspinatus, deltoid, elbow flexors, and forearm supinators leaves the limb adducted and internally rotated with the elbow extended and the forearm pronated, the so-called waiter''s tip deformity.'),
('3ae9dbd5-1f49-5389-8097-b70b8c84c606'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The waiter''s tip deformity in Erb''s palsy includes:', '["forearm pronated and extended","Wrist drop","Claw hand","Arm adducted and internally rotated","Ape hand"]'::jsonb, 'Arm adducted and internally rotated', 'In Erb''s palsy the paralysed abductors and lateral rotators of the shoulder leave the arm adducted and internally rotated, while the paralysed elbow flexors and supinators leave the forearm extended and pronated, giving the waiter''s tip posture.'),
('ddf745f5-d992-50c8-94a4-f22f98b471c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar paradox refers to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Forearm involvement is greater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Higher ulnar nerve lesions produce less severe clawing than lower lesions","Hand deformities are the same without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower lesions are less severe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher ulnar nerve lesions produce less severe clawing than lower lesions', 'A high (elbow) ulnar nerve lesion also paralyses the flexor carpi ulnaris and the ulnar half of the flexor digitorum profundus, so the metacarpophalangeal joints are less hyperextended. Clawing is therefore paradoxically milder with higher lesions than with lesions at the wrist.'),
('ca156d3d-f1fb-5034-a924-58046707f53d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Ape hand deformity includes:', '["and thenar eminence wasting","Hyperextension of fingers","Loss of thumb opposition","adduction","Flexion of fingers"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of thumb opposition', 'Ape hand deformity results from paralysis of the thenar muscles, particularly the abductor pollicis brevis and opponens pollicis after median nerve injury. The thumb loses opposition and lies flat alongside the hand, with wasting of the thenar eminence.'),
('595d1192-c4f1-5eeb-9bc8-4c455b7a8325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar nerve injury at the elbow may be caused by:', '["Fracture of the medial epicondyle of humerus","Fracture of lateral epicondyle","Supracondylar fracture","Scaphoid fracture","Radial head fracture"]'::jsonb, 'Fracture of the medial epicondyle of humerus', 'The ulnar nerve descends posterior to the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where it lies superficially in the ulnar groove. A fracture through the medial epicondyle can therefore directly injure the nerve.'),
('e8ba3800-6cbc-54e1-bd2c-80f03c94a180'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk in a supracondylar fracture of the humerus?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve","Radial nerve","Median nerve (and brachial artery)","Ulnar nerve","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve (and brachial artery)', 'In a supracondylar fracture the distal fragment is displaced posteriorly, stretching or impaling the median nerve and brachial artery, which lie anteriorly at the elbow. This injury is often complicated by Volkmann ischemic contracture.'),
('ab9654c9-7ab6-5eec-8acc-e2eb415ea1e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk in a fracture of the surgical neck of the humerus?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulnar nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary nerve (and posterior circumflex humeral artery)","Radial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Median nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve (and posterior circumflex humeral artery)', 'The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck of the humerus accompanied by the posterior circumflex humeral artery. A fracture at this level injures both, causing deltoid paralysis and loss of sensation over the regimental badge area.'),
('9c7ae333-e4e1-5b26-be75-bcbff8d1836f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk in a fracture of the midshaft of the humerus?', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (in spiral groove)","Musculocutaneous nerve","Median nerve","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (in spiral groove)', 'The radial nerve lies directly in contact with the humerus as it travels through the spiral groove, so a midshaft humeral fracture injures it. This produces wrist drop from paralysis of the wrist and finger extensors.'),
('378ec0c8-85f2-5671-be7e-9f56285c6296'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk in a distal radial fracture (Colles'' fracture)?', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Median nerve (carpal tunnel syndrome)","Posterior interosseous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve (carpal tunnel syndrome)', 'The median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel at the wrist, where it is compressed or stretched after a Colles'' fracture. This produces symptoms of carpal tunnel syndrome, such as tingling over the radial digits and thenar weakness.'),
('c534e8d5-78af-5935-8bd1-8ea89f8f38ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk in a fracture of the hook of hamate?', '["Median nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve (deep branch in Guyon''s canal)"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve (deep branch in Guyon''s canal)', 'The deep branch of the ulnar nerve curves around the hook of hamate within Guyon''s canal, accompanied by the ulnar artery. A fracture of the hook of hamate injures this branch, causing weakness of the intrinsic hand muscles.'),
('b798e2aa-6180-5966-ac1a-3df26e2adc80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome involves compression of:', '["Phrenic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Brachial plexus and/or subclavian vessels at the superior thoracic outlet","Recurrent laryngeal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic chain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial plexus and/or subclavian vessels at the superior thoracic outlet', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome compresses the brachial plexus and/or subclavian vessels as they pass through the scalene hiatus and behind the clavicle. The phrenic and recurrent laryngeal nerves and the sympathetic chain are not compressed at this site, and spinal cord involvement is a central lesion.'),
('8d08bac7-3000-5889-90e0-39dbf759c8d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In carpal tunnel syndrome, which nerve is compressed?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","which nerve is compressed?","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'The median nerve is trapped beneath the flexor retinaculum in carpal tunnel syndrome, causing thenar weakness and sensory loss over the lateral palm and digits. The ulnar and radial nerves do not pass through the carpal tunnel, and the musculocutaneous nerve is not involved.'),
('9afeb998-668b-529c-9353-da15b08c25c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In cubital tunnel syndrome, which nerve is compressed?', '["which nerve is compressed?","Ulnar nerve (at the elbow)","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve (at the elbow)', 'The ulnar nerve is compressed at the elbow in cubital tunnel syndrome, behind the medial epicondyle, causing paresthesia over the little finger and intrinsic hand weakness. The median and radial nerves are not compressed at this site.'),
('bd356d5f-80c8-594f-b265-d22ae08be73b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In radial tunnel syndrome, which nerve is compressed?', '["Radial nerve in spiral groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Median nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of radial) in supinator","which nerve is compressed? without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial radial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of radial) in supinator', 'Radial tunnel syndrome compresses the posterior interosseous nerve, the deep branch of the radial nerve, as it passes through the supinator. Compression of the main radial nerve in the spiral groove or of the superficial radial branch produces different, more proximal findings.'),
('b728a491-d64c-541a-8a76-7607398b3b19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In Guyon''s canal syndrome, which nerve is compressed?', '["Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","which nerve is compressed?","Median nerve","Ulnar nerve (at the wrist)"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve (at the wrist)', 'Guyon''s canal syndrome compresses the ulnar nerve, mainly its deep motor branch, as it passes between the pisiform and the hook of hamate, sparing the dorsal ulnar sensory territory. The median and radial nerves are not related to this tunnel.'),
('703f836d-731f-52b4-8163-cfea6f898e8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The extensor carpi radialis longus is supplied by the radial nerve and arises from:', '["Medial epicondyle resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial shaft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral supracondylar ridge of humerus","Common extensor origin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ulna leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral supracondylar ridge of humerus', 'The extensor carpi radialis longus arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus and is supplied by the radial nerve. The medial epicondyle gives origin to the forearm flexors, and the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle belongs to the remaining extensor group.'),
('03694b64-4444-5b4d-ad9c-c89ed993ac3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The extensor carpi radialis brevis is supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve and arises from:', '["Radial shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial epicondyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral supracondylar ridge mediated by specific receptor activation","Ulna without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common extensor origin (lateral epicondyle)"]'::jsonb, 'Common extensor origin (lateral epicondyle)', 'The extensor carpi radialis brevis arises from the common extensor origin on the lateral epicondyle and is supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve. The lateral supracondylar ridge is the origin of the extensor carpi radialis longus, not the brevis.'),
('b2f87cb1-54f7-5ac9-b1e2-c1669c56392d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The flexor digitorum profundus is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior interosseous nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Median nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Radial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior interosseous nerve (radial half) and ulnar nerve (ulnar half)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interosseous nerve (radial half) and ulnar nerve (ulnar half)', 'The flexor digitorum profundus has a dual nerve supply: the anterior interosseous nerve (from the median) innervates the radial half and the ulnar nerve the ulnar half. No single nerve supplies the whole muscle, so the median-only, ulnar-only, and radial options are incorrect.'),
('f02e3720-9651-5de1-b548-81f04a5afd6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The flexor pollicis brevis is supplied by:', '["Median nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ulnar nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Both median and ulnar equally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recurrent median nerve (superficial head) and deep ulnar (deep head)"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent median nerve (superficial head) and deep ulnar (deep head)', 'The flexor pollicis brevis has a dual supply: the superficial head by the recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve and the deep head by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. It is not supplied by a single nerve alone.'),
('b26757a1-c8e4-5282-993e-14f82bb20013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The deltoid muscle is supplied by which nerve and root value?', '["Radial nerve (C5,6)","Axillary nerve (C5,6)","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5,7)","Subscapular nerve (C5,6)","Suprascapular nerve (C5,6)"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve (C5,6)', 'The deltoid is supplied by the axillary nerve (C5,6), which winds around the surgical neck of the humerus. The radial, musculocutaneous, subscapular, and suprascapular nerves supply other muscles of the limb.'),
('39217624-591b-51b6-8810-5450d194913e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The latissimus dorsi is supplied by the thoracodorsal nerve, which arises from which cord?', '["Lateral cord (C5,7)","Medial cord (C8,T1)","Medial and lateral cords","Posterior cord (C6,7,8)","Upper trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord (C6,7,8)', 'The thoracodorsal nerve arises from the posterior cord (C6,7,8) and supplies the latissimus dorsi. The lateral and medial cords give origin to the other terminal branches, not to the thoracodorsal nerve.'),
('13919ca6-6adb-5227-849c-f4f9cc759903'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The supraspinatus and infraspinatus are both supplied by:', '["Subscapular nerve (C5,6)","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Radial nerve (C5,6)","Axillary nerve (C5,6)","Suprascapular nerve (C5,6)"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular nerve (C5,6)', 'Both the supraspinatus and infraspinatus are supplied by the suprascapular nerve (C5,6), which passes through the suprascapular notch. The axillary, dorsal scapular, radial, and subscapular nerves supply other scapulohumeral muscles.'),
('2991dbd8-b00c-5ba7-bc85-0010f041b13a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The subscapularis muscle is supplied by:', '["Axillary nerve (C5,6)","Suprascapular nerve (C5,6)","Radial nerve (C5,6)","Thoracodorsal nerve (C6,7,8)","Upper and lower subscapular nerves (C5,6)"]'::jsonb, 'Upper and lower subscapular nerves (C5,6)', 'The subscapularis is supplied by the upper and lower subscapular nerves (C5,6), branches of the posterior cord. The thoracodorsal nerve supplies the latissimus dorsi, and the axillary and suprascapular nerves do not innervate subscapularis.'),
('620f7e84-2fd0-59fb-9e40-93ce8df61555'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Musculocutaneous nerve supplies which muscles of the arm?', '["Triceps brachii resulting in decreased functional activity","Deltoid and teres minor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, brachialis","All arm muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachioradialis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, brachialis', 'The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, and the medial part of the brachialis, the flexor muscles of the anterior arm. The triceps and brachioradialis are radial nerve territory, and the deltoid and teres minor are axillary nerve territory.'),
('3ad01e85-e3a2-5f57-94bb-73fc8a54d8db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the triceps brachii?', '["Radial nerve (C5-8,T1)","Axillary nerve (C5,6)","Median nerve (C6-8)","Ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-8,T1)', 'The triceps brachii is supplied by the radial nerve (C5-8,T1), which descends in the spiral groove of the humerus. The axillary, median, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves do not supply any part of the triceps.'),
('ab6807a4-c210-5e7e-8f8a-e090362f2516'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The anconeus muscle is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5,6)","Radial nerve (C7,8,T1)","Posterior interosseous nerve (C7,8)","Median nerve (C7,8,T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C7,8,T1)', 'The anconeus is supplied by the radial nerve (C7,8,T1), which sends a branch to the muscle as it crosses the lateral epicondyle. The ulnar, median, and musculocutaneous nerves do not supply the anconeus.'),
('fc40d030-d72e-5c25-ae4e-09b37f488c4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pronator teres is supplied by which nerve and root value?', '["Median nerve (C6,7)","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5,6)","Radial nerve (C5,6)","Anterior interosseous nerve (C7,8)","Ulnar nerve (C8,T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve (C6,7)', 'The pronator teres is supplied by the median nerve (C6,7), which passes between the two heads of the muscle. The anterior interosseous and ulnar nerves supply the deep flexor compartment instead.'),
('77f3eaeb-832b-5fda-a157-4ef16329ecc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial half of flexor digitorum profundus (to index and middle) is supplied by:', '["Anterior interosseous nerve (C7,8) from median","Median nerve (C6,7)","Ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Radial nerve (C5,6)","Posterior interosseous nerve (C7,8)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interosseous nerve (C7,8) from median', 'The radial half of the flexor digitorum profundus, supplying the index and middle fingers, is innervated by the anterior interosseous nerve (C7,8), a branch of the median nerve. The ulnar nerve supplies the ulnar half, and the radial nerve plays no part.'),
('b24c5249-6a20-551a-afb2-d05c78184330'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar half of flexor digitorum profundus (to ring and little) is supplied by:', '["Radial nerve (C5,6)","Anterior interosseous nerve (C7,8)","Ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Median nerve (C7,8,T1)","Posterior interosseous nerve (C7,8)"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve (C8,T1)', 'The ulnar half of the flexor digitorum profundus, supplying the ring and little fingers, is innervated by the ulnar nerve (C8,T1). The anterior interosseous and median nerves supply the radial half of the muscle instead.'),
('a8fa3be1-6c0d-5189-8b4a-ff53ba6fad61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The palmar interossei are supplied by:', '["Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Posterior interosseous nerve","Recurrent median nerve (C8,T1)","Radial nerve (C5,6)","Median nerve (C7,8,T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)', 'The palmar interossei are intrinsic hand muscles supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8,T1). The median and radial nerves do not innervate the interossei.'),
('d2011988-8f3b-5689-b7ef-92d365e86d7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal interossei are supplied by:', '["Radial nerve (C5,6)","Median nerve (C7,8,T1)","Anterior interosseous nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve","Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)', 'The dorsal interossei are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8,T1). The median nerve supplies the two radial lumbricals and the thenar muscles, not the interossei.'),
('b89c9b5a-f04b-5f1d-b790-6ca004069914'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lumbricals of the hand (radial two) are supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Deep branch of ulnar nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve","Recurrent median nerve","Median nerve (C8,T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve (C8,T1)', 'The two radial (lateral) lumbricals are supplied by the median nerve (C8,T1). The ulnar nerve supplies the two medial lumbricals and the interossei, and the recurrent branch innervates the thenar muscles.'),
('e91a59a0-afd6-59fd-8868-1530d0ba5768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lumbricals of the hand (ulnar two) are supplied by:', '["Recurrent median nerve","Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Posterior interosseous nerve","Superficial ulnar nerve","Median nerve (C8,T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)', 'The two ulnar (medial) lumbricals are supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8,T1). The median nerve supplies only the two radial lumbricals, and the posterior interosseous nerve is not involved.'),
('618680f3-8919-5188-a43f-e42b0923ab20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The abductor digiti minimi (AbDM) is supplied by:', '["Median nerve (C8,T1)","Recurrent median nerve","Superficial ulnar nerve","Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)","Radial nerve (C5,6)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep branch of ulnar nerve (C8,T1)', 'The abductor digiti minimi is an intrinsic hand muscle supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve (C8,T1). The median, radial, and recurrent thenar branches supply other muscle groups.'),
('2c68733b-de95-5332-a2fb-5fe4d6055072'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachialis muscle receives dual supply from:', '["Ulnar and radial nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Median and musculocutaneous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radial and axillary nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Median and ulnar nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Musculocutaneous nerve (medial part) and radial nerve (lateral part)"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (medial part) and radial nerve (lateral part)', 'The brachialis receives dual supply: the medial part from the musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral part from the radial nerve. The ulnar and axillary nerves do not supply muscles of the anterior arm.'),
('3e3132aa-1d24-5088-b3ae-cf920d6345af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The coracobrachialis is supplied by which nerve and pierces which structure?', '["Median nerve (C6-8) resulting in decreased functional activity","Radial nerve (C5,6) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary nerve (C5,6) mediated by specific receptor activation","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) — pierces the muscle","Ulnar nerve (C8,T1) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) — pierces the muscle', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) pierces and supplies the coracobrachialis before passing between the biceps and brachialis. The axillary, radial, median, and ulnar nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('8fc6be97-c31e-568d-9d9c-df2296ebe8a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to subclavius (C5,6) supplies which muscle and may be injured in which condition?', '["Omohyoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Subclavius — injured in cervical rib or thoracic outlet syndrome","Sternocleidomastoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Serratus anterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pectoralis minor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavius — injured in cervical rib or thoracic outlet syndrome', 'The nerve to subclavius (C5,6) supplies the subclavius muscle and may be injured in cervical rib or thoracic outlet syndrome because it crosses over the first rib. The omohyoid, serratus anterior, and pectoralis minor have different nerve supplies.'),
('2ed68905-1d0d-531b-819b-1937fdf4b3fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve arises from the medial and lateral cords (C5-T1) as two roots?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ulnar nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Radial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Median nerve (lateral root from lateral cord C5-7, medial root from medial cord C8,T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve (lateral root from lateral cord C5-7, medial root from medial cord C8,T1)', 'The median nerve is formed by a lateral root from the lateral cord (C5-7) and a medial root from the medial cord (C8,T1). The other named nerves arise from a single root or a single cord.'),
('aa120534-9f09-5146-8d49-46905bbf99e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Erb-Duchenne palsy affects which nerve roots?', '["C3-C5","C5-C6 (upper trunk)","C5-T1","C7-C8","C8-T1 (lower trunk)"]'::jsonb, 'C5-C6 (upper trunk)', 'Erb-Duchenne palsy results from injury to the C5-C6 roots (upper trunk), typically from traction on the neck during a difficult delivery. It spares the lower trunk, which distinguishes it from Klumpke''s palsy.'),
('6c19d8c8-6b9b-5507-bd5e-f4b4763a7a19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Klumpke''s palsy affects which nerve roots?', '["C3-C5","C5-C6 (upper trunk)","C8-T1 (lower trunk)","C7-C8","C5-T1"]'::jsonb, 'C8-T1 (lower trunk)', 'Klumpke''s palsy affects the C8-T1 roots (lower trunk), typically from excessive abduction traction on the arm. It causes intrinsic muscle weakness and a claw hand, unlike the upper trunk lesion of Erb-Duchenne palsy.'),
('8715d3e6-a0aa-57b8-8c8b-964a8829db6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which myotome is responsible for elbow flexion?', '["C4","T1","C5-C6","C7","C8-T1"]'::jsonb, 'C5-C6', 'Elbow flexion is chiefly a C5-C6 myotome function, mediated by the biceps and brachialis muscles. Extension (C7) and intrinsic hand action (C8-T1) belong to other myotomes.'),
('4efa52f9-5229-5f65-b14a-e38f71b77e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which myotome is responsible for elbow extension?', '["T1","C8-T1","C5-C6","C7","C4"]'::jsonb, 'C7', 'Elbow extension is a C7 myotome function carried out by the triceps brachii. Flexion (C5-C6) and hand function (C8-T1) are governed by different myotomes.'),
('5e7411bd-63db-5e16-845b-2dc39f83050d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'C5 dermatome covers which area of the upper limb?', '["Middle finger","Medial arm","Lateral forearm and thumb","Medial forearm and little finger","Lateral arm and elbow"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral arm and elbow', 'The C5 dermatome covers the lateral arm and elbow. The lateral forearm and thumb belong to C6, the middle finger to C7, and the medial arm and forearm to T1-T2.'),
('b5bc1442-5574-53de-b347-e3a77809e773'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'A lesion of the lower trunk of the brachial plexus causes:', '["Ape hand","Shoulder abduction weakness","Waiter''s tip deformity","Wrist drop","Claw hand (intrinsic minus hand)"]'::jsonb, 'Claw hand (intrinsic minus hand)', 'A lower trunk (C8-T1) lesion paralyses the intrinsic hand muscles, producing a claw (intrinsic minus) hand. Ape hand, waiter''s tip deformity, and wrist drop arise from median, upper trunk, and radial nerve lesions respectively.'),
('97c6df81-eba0-5073-96bb-a5b330ad49f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial nerve is most commonly injured in:', '["Fracture of the midshaft humerus (spiral groove)","Supracondylar fracture","Colles'' fracture","Fracture of medial epicondyle","Fracture of surgical neck of humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Fracture of the midshaft humerus (spiral groove)', 'The radial nerve is most commonly injured in a fracture of the midshaft humerus because it lies in the spiral groove against the bone. Supracondylar fractures endanger the median nerve and surgical neck fractures the axillary nerve.'),
('49df59a5-a463-58a7-b747-19c7a5a15097'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The median nerve is most commonly injured in:', '["Surgical neck fracture","Medial epicondyle fracture","Midshaft humerus fracture","Supracondylar fracture of the humerus","Colles'' fracture"]'::jsonb, 'Supracondylar fracture of the humerus', 'The median nerve is most commonly injured in a supracondylar fracture of the humerus, where it crosses the front of the distal humerus. Midshaft humerus fractures risk the radial nerve and medial epicondyle fractures the ulnar nerve.'),
('64d6e516-7cf7-5bcc-bbb7-1108f3cdd149'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar nerve is most commonly injured in:', '["Fracture of the medial epicondyle of humerus","Radius fracture","Midshaft humerus fracture","Surgical neck fracture","Fracture of lateral epicondyle"]'::jsonb, 'Fracture of the medial epicondyle of humerus', 'The ulnar nerve is most commonly injured in a fracture of the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where it passes behind the epicondyle. Midshaft and surgical neck humerus fractures endanger the radial and axillary nerves respectively.'),
('019b3485-9db6-5a1e-9cf8-970f7fcc580d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Wrist drop is characteristic of which nerve injury?', '["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'Wrist drop results from radial nerve injury because the extensor muscles of the wrist and fingers are paralysed. Axillary, median, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerve lesions produce different deficits.'),
('3d58523c-2123-5307-9ad0-4af82e954154'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Claw hand (ulnar claw) is characteristic of:', '["Radial nerve injury","Median nerve injury","Ulnar nerve injury","Musculocutaneous nerve injury","Axillary nerve injury"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar nerve injury', 'Claw hand is characteristic of ulnar nerve injury because the intrinsic muscles are paralysed while the long flexors remain functional, hyperextending the metacarpophalangeal joints. Radial, median, and axillary nerve injuries produce other deformities.'),
('6ab07732-1453-5022-bdfc-0a419b15b971'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Ape hand deformity (loss of thumb opposition) is characteristic of:', '["Radial nerve injury","Musculocutaneous nerve injury","Ulnar nerve injury","Axillary nerve injury","Median nerve injury (recurrent branch)"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve injury (recurrent branch)', 'Ape hand results from loss of thumb opposition due to paralysis of the thenar muscles, caused by injury to the recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve. Ulnar nerve injury causes clawing rather than loss of opposition.'),
('cf3b9b1b-2f91-53b4-88d6-54d7aeba494f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius muscle is supplied by which of the following?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","4 (proprioceptive)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The trapezius is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with proprioceptive contributions from C3. The dorsal scapular, suprascapular, and long thoracic nerves supply the rhomboids, rotator cuff, and serratus anterior respectively.'),
('1ddbecaa-bb72-56b0-92f8-14d467c2e693'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the trapezius muscle?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","4 (proprioceptive)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with a C3 contribution supplies the trapezius. The long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior), suprascapular nerve (rotator cuff), and dorsal scapular nerve (rhomboids) do not supply the trapezius.'),
('e64cee06-b91d-5e1a-a630-4e7b47142dc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius muscle receives its motor supply from which nerve?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","4 (proprioceptive)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The trapezius receives its motor supply from the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and proprioceptive fibers from C3. The dorsal scapular, long thoracic, and suprascapular nerves supply other scapular muscles.'),
('897fcb10-6f5e-5730-b1a8-93a34aacb434'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius muscle is supplied by which nerve?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","4 (proprioceptive)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The trapezius is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3. The suprascapular nerve (supraspinatus and infraspinatus), dorsal scapular nerve (rhomboids), and long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior) are unrelated to the trapezius.'),
('f475a5cf-2786-5ef4-9112-67a37336a5b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Identify the nerve supply of the trapezius muscle.', '["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","4 (proprioceptive)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The motor supply of the trapezius is the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), supplemented by C3 proprioceptive fibers. The dorsal scapular, suprascapular, and long thoracic nerves do not supply the trapezius.'),
('01b320e4-cb1f-5f7c-9be0-a2c20505c57c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The levator scapulae is supplied by which of the following?', '["Long thoracic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Suprascapular nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3', 'The levator scapulae is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and direct branches from C3. The long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior), suprascapular nerve (rotator cuff), and accessory nerve (trapezius) do not supply this muscle.'),
('3eb0d96f-947c-50c8-a6e3-09a1ee620b82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the levator scapulae?', '["Long thoracic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Suprascapular nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3', 'The levator scapulae receives innervation from the dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and direct branches from C3. The long thoracic, suprascapular, and accessory nerves supply other scapular muscles.'),
('6a52d9da-baf0-5b7f-865f-206505d033da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The levator scapulae receives its motor supply from which nerve?', '["Long thoracic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Suprascapular nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3', 'The levator scapulae is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3. The suprascapular nerve supplies the supraspinatus and infraspinatus, and the long thoracic nerve supplies the serratus anterior.'),
('6a132447-cc73-54ed-a54a-59f8bfdc0c15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The levator scapulae is supplied by which nerve?', '["Long thoracic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Suprascapular nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3 supply the levator scapulae. The accessory nerve supplies the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid, and the long thoracic nerve supplies the serratus anterior.'),
('fdc75c00-819b-577b-8822-13a6d2b9a6f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Identify the nerve supply of the levator scapulae.', '["Long thoracic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Suprascapular nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3', 'The levator scapulae is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3. The long thoracic, suprascapular, and accessory nerves do not supply the levator scapulae.'),
('3fd45c39-e82e-5154-ae6e-712c7cbdc994'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid major and minor are supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve","Subscapular nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) supplies the rhomboid major and minor after arising from the C5 root. The suprascapular nerve innervates supraspinatus and infraspinatus, the long thoracic nerve innervates the serratus anterior, the accessory nerve innervates the trapezius, and the subscapular nerve innervates the subscapularis and teres major.'),
('94282bcb-c1fe-57d2-a829-86f8db322630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the rhomboid major and minor are supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve","Subscapular nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) innervates both rhomboid muscles as it runs deep to the levator scapulae. Suprascapular, long thoracic, accessory, and subscapular nerves each supply different muscles, so none of them innervates the rhomboids.'),
('6d1f714c-0480-56ea-be62-c6940b091f50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the rhomboid major and minor are supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve","Subscapular nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'A branch of the C5 root, the dorsal scapular nerve, provides the motor supply to the rhomboid major and minor. The other listed nerves serve the supraspinatus and infraspinatus, serratus anterior, trapezius, and subscapularis and teres major, respectively, not the rhomboids.'),
('9ed675c4-13fc-533b-a556-ea3421345c51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid major and minor are supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve","Subscapular nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) is the motor supply of the rhomboid major and minor. The suprascapular, long thoracic, accessory, and subscapular nerves innervate other muscles of the shoulder girdle.'),
('69fad541-750c-5945-a809-d30901e7be75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the rhomboid major and minor are supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve","Subscapular nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'Innervation of the rhomboids comes from the dorsal scapular nerve (C5). The suprascapular nerve, long thoracic nerve, accessory nerve, and subscapular nerve innervate the rotator cuff, serratus anterior, trapezius, and subscapularis and teres major, respectively.'),
('d8856847-2020-56b6-93b4-f50f06cd0dda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The latissimus dorsi is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)","Upper subscapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Lower subscapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)', 'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8) from the posterior cord is the nerve supply of the latissimus dorsi. The upper and lower subscapular nerves innervate the subscapularis and teres major, the axillary nerve innervates the deltoid and teres minor, and the suprascapular nerve innervates the supraspinatus and infraspinatus.'),
('8a5fd533-3fe1-582c-9764-71fe19e62760'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the latissimus dorsi is supplied by which nerve?', '["Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)","Upper subscapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Lower subscapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)', 'The latissimus dorsi receives its innervation from the thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8), a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. The subscapular, axillary, and suprascapular nerves supply other muscles of the shoulder.'),
('6a57d303-e5c9-576c-ab86-aee4737a043a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the latissimus dorsi is supplied by which nerve?', '["Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)","Upper subscapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Lower subscapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)', 'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8) travels with the thoracodorsal vessels to enter and innervate the latissimus dorsi. The upper and lower subscapular nerves, the axillary nerve, and the suprascapular nerve serve other muscles.'),
('47282a84-e9cd-5f41-a85d-3fb7ec99a944'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The latissimus dorsi is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)","Upper subscapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Lower subscapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)', 'Latissimus dorsi is innervated by the thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8), which arises from the posterior cord. The other choices supply the subscapularis and teres major, the deltoid and teres minor, and the supraspinatus and infraspinatus.'),
('e408cff4-5646-57cd-bfbc-5f7b8872bc22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the latissimus dorsi is supplied by which nerve?', '["Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)","Upper subscapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Lower subscapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8)', 'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6-8) innervates the latissimus dorsi. The subscapular, axillary, and suprascapular nerves do not reach this muscle.'),
('b4d49446-9ac0-5746-aa89-889b9d566cea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pectoralis major (sternocostal head) is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Thoracodorsal","Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves","Suprascapular","Only medial pectoral","Only lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'The sternocostal head of the pectoralis major is innervated by both the lateral and medial pectoral nerves, whereas the clavicular head is chiefly supplied by the lateral pectoral nerve. The thoracodorsal and suprascapular nerves supply other muscles, and naming only one pectoral nerve is incomplete.'),
('22560b0e-e387-53e6-a264-a8a93cd46880'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the pectoralis major (sternocostal head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Thoracodorsal","Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves","Suprascapular","Only medial pectoral","Only lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'Both the lateral pectoral nerve (C5-7) and the medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1) contribute to the innervation of the sternocostal head of the pectoralis major. The thoracodorsal and suprascapular nerves innervate the latissimus dorsi and the rotator cuff muscles.'),
('9e55fb45-0ba7-5bac-b190-603713468c51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the pectoralis major (sternocostal head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Thoracodorsal","Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves","Suprascapular","Only medial pectoral","Only lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'The sternocostal head of the pectoralis major receives fibres from both the lateral and medial pectoral nerves, a feature that distinguishes it from the clavicular head. Options naming a single pectoral nerve, the thoracodorsal nerve, and the suprascapular nerve are not accurate.'),
('2fb4f031-4ca4-5816-b73b-5ca1c83e621a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pectoralis major (sternocostal head) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Thoracodorsal","Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves","Suprascapular","Only medial pectoral","Only lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'Innervation of the sternocostal head of the pectoralis major comes from both the lateral and medial pectoral nerves. The thoracodorsal nerve and suprascapular nerve innervate other muscles, and relying on one pectoral nerve alone is incomplete.'),
('5a0d106c-660c-5b8b-a0f6-07d4d734bf68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the pectoralis major (sternocostal head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Thoracodorsal","Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves","Suprascapular","Only medial pectoral","Only lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Both lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'The sternocostal head of the pectoralis major is supplied by both the lateral and medial pectoral nerves, unlike the clavicular head, which is mainly supplied by the lateral pectoral nerve. The thoracodorsal and suprascapular nerves do not innervate this muscle.'),
('9f96c55c-25b4-59df-80e2-2e00992e778d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Dorsal scapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7), formed from the roots of the brachial plexus, is the sole nerve supply of the serratus anterior, and its injury causes winging of the scapula. The suprascapular, thoracodorsal, dorsal scapular, and accessory nerves innervate other muscles.'),
('91c66d30-dc82-57a9-ab6a-c11848c59e49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the serratus anterior is supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Dorsal scapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The serratus anterior is innervated by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7), which runs superficial to the muscle on its outer surface. The other listed nerves supply the rotator cuff, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, and trapezius.'),
('db043969-6712-51c5-82a6-0faa3a8cca05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the serratus anterior is supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Dorsal scapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) supplies the serratus anterior; its damage produces a winged scapula. The suprascapular, thoracodorsal, dorsal scapular, and accessory nerves innervate different shoulder girdle muscles.'),
('a5b12dcf-7997-5a8f-aeff-eaf0d8a5ec99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Dorsal scapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'Innervation of the serratus anterior is from the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The suprascapular nerve, thoracodorsal nerve, dorsal scapular nerve, and accessory nerve supply other muscles.'),
('14f0c523-daa0-5ab3-80eb-7c7ddbb6c163'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the serratus anterior is supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Dorsal scapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) is the motor supply of the serratus anterior. The suprascapular, thoracodorsal, dorsal scapular, and accessory nerves do not innervate this muscle.'),
('a52cc929-f444-53c6-b56c-df7f8604c8a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The coracobrachialis is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) pierces and innervates the coracobrachialis and then continues between the biceps and brachialis. The ulnar, median, and radial nerves do not supply it, and the axillary nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor.'),
('94527c42-5c3d-565e-a71e-eb393efc2e56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the coracobrachialis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The coracobrachialis receives its innervation from the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7), which it pierces as it descends in the arm. The ulnar, median, radial, and axillary nerves innervate other muscles.'),
('6febd26d-3368-5a5a-a625-ff51cd9ace8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the coracobrachialis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) innervates the coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, and brachialis. The ulnar, median, radial, and axillary nerves serve other structures and do not supply the coracobrachialis.'),
('0cd5b529-e383-5dd8-988b-d76af741a00a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The coracobrachialis is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) is the nerve supply of the coracobrachialis. The ulnar, median, radial, and axillary nerves innervate other forearm and shoulder muscles.'),
('9ecd2bd0-2e91-587a-93eb-d971a137671e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the coracobrachialis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) pierces the coracobrachialis and supplies it before passing between the biceps and brachialis. The ulnar, median, radial, and axillary nerves do not innervate this muscle.'),
('6f645b40-0899-538c-9e36-1365b6670c59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachialis (lateral part) is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Radial nerve (medial part)","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The lateral part of the brachialis is innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7), whereas the medial part receives additional fibres from the radial nerve. Median, axillary, and ulnar nerves do not supply the brachialis.'),
('27bc388c-9684-5111-8839-411abbac3d03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the brachialis (lateral part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Radial nerve (medial part)","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) supplies the lateral part of the brachialis. The radial nerve contributes to the medial part, while the median, axillary, and ulnar nerves do not innervate this muscle.'),
('37abde80-9602-5122-bc85-99f4f4a28236'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the brachialis (lateral part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Radial nerve (medial part)","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The lateral part of the brachialis is innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7). The radial nerve (medial part) innervates the medial portion of the muscle, and the median, axillary, and ulnar nerves supply other muscles.'),
('a7633a84-cc72-50c0-a558-bdf943504e34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachialis (lateral part) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Radial nerve (medial part)","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7) innervates the lateral part of the brachialis. The median, axillary, and ulnar nerves do not supply the brachialis, and the radial nerve mainly serves its medial part.'),
('c9c794fe-f9ac-56cd-a534-c1cc49a89bc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the brachialis (lateral part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Radial nerve (medial part)","Axillary nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7)', 'The lateral part of the brachialis receives its supply from the musculocutaneous nerve (C5-7). The radial nerve (medial part) innervates the medial portion, and the median, axillary, and ulnar nerves are not involved.'),
('5fdb8978-6423-5db2-ab87-e602db4be713'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachialis (medial part) is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-7)', 'The medial part of the brachialis is innervated by the radial nerve (C5-7), while its lateral part is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve. The ulnar, median, and axillary nerves do not innervate the brachialis.'),
('74a0185e-a074-5993-bd86-bc4405fbde78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the brachialis (medial part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-7)', 'The radial nerve (C5-7) supplies the medial part of the brachialis. The musculocutaneous nerve innervates the lateral part, and the ulnar, median, and axillary nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('67c91f7f-a8cf-5f45-ab7c-4b9201437a70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the brachialis (medial part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-7)', 'The medial part of the brachialis is innervated by the radial nerve (C5-7). The musculocutaneous nerve, which innervates the lateral part, along with the ulnar, median, and axillary nerves, all supply other structures.'),
('17e90868-66d2-57c9-b8a9-034bca8d179a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The brachialis (medial part) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-7)', 'The radial nerve (C5-7) innervates the medial part of the brachialis, complementing the musculocutaneous supply to its lateral part. The ulnar, median, and axillary nerves do not innervate this muscle.'),
('4ab7665d-58a6-5de3-ac67-24ef823e5bb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the brachialis (medial part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve (C5-7)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C5-7)', 'The medial part of the brachialis is supplied by the radial nerve (C5-7). The ulnar, median, and axillary nerves, and the musculocutaneous nerve, which serves the lateral part, do not supply the medial part.'),
('26135ef8-0a89-5a40-bfc1-350ffed3b3c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (long head) is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The long head of the triceps brachii, like all heads, is innervated by the radial nerve (C6-8). The axillary nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor, while the median, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerves innervate other muscles.'),
('9c5020e2-7460-5764-a728-c67f72d5c667'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the triceps brachii (long head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The radial nerve (C6-8) supplies the long head of the triceps brachii. The axillary, median, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerves innervate other upper limb muscles, not the triceps.'),
('28467c14-0ec6-5dd5-a0f8-b70d2ac8cf0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the triceps brachii (long head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'All three heads of the triceps brachii, including the long head, are innervated by the radial nerve (C6-8). The axillary nerve and the median, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('5b114f75-a61e-50ad-b576-16ac73fcafff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (long head) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The radial nerve (C6-8) innervates the long head of the triceps brachii. The axillary nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor, and the median, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerves do not reach the triceps.'),
('d7782b91-0b26-5065-bc51-968941437bb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the triceps brachii (long head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The long head of the triceps brachii receives innervation from the radial nerve (C6-8). The axillary, median, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerves serve other muscles of the upper limb.'),
('ef20d1a4-4ec9-5cb5-a7cb-85938ee846cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (lateral head) is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The lateral head of the triceps brachii is innervated by the radial nerve (C6-8), which supplies all heads of the triceps. The suprascapular, median, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves innervate other muscles.'),
('48c668a9-966c-599e-9998-1dfa1061980f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the triceps brachii (lateral head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The radial nerve (C6-8) supplies the lateral head of the triceps brachii. The suprascapular nerve innervates the supraspinatus and infraspinatus, and the median, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves supply other muscles.'),
('7377c827-c50f-53cc-92f3-acbbc615ad67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the triceps brachii (lateral head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'All heads of the triceps brachii, including the lateral head, receive innervation from the radial nerve (C6-8). The suprascapular, median, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('4c224641-a0c1-5081-b9e0-7abfac4705f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (lateral head) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Suprascapular nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The lateral head of the triceps brachii is innervated by the radial nerve (C6-8). The suprascapular nerve serves the rotator cuff, while the median, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves supply other muscles.'),
('e37dc270-2a75-5936-8617-167f9460ec51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the triceps brachii (lateral head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Musculocutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The radial nerve (C6-8) innervates the lateral head of the triceps brachii. The suprascapular, median, axillary, and musculocutaneous nerves do not innervate the triceps.'),
('f0e24b54-8a36-50c0-980a-07720dc7a362'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (medial head) is supplied by which nerve? Which option is most accurate?', '["Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The medial head of the triceps brachii is innervated by the radial nerve (C6-8), which also supplies the long and lateral heads. The median, musculocutaneous, axillary, and ulnar nerves innervate other structures.'),
('72de37cd-50e3-562a-8fc6-80c70c8fba97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the triceps brachii (medial head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The radial nerve (C6-8) supplies the medial head of the triceps brachii. The median, musculocutaneous, axillary, and ulnar nerves do not innervate this muscle.'),
('458d7f4a-ab48-59ad-ad3d-4f8613b98876'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: the triceps brachii (medial head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'All three heads of the triceps brachii, including the medial head, receive their supply from the radial nerve (C6-8). The median, musculocutaneous, axillary, and ulnar nerves supply other upper limb muscles.'),
('75a2b65d-8ed9-57d7-a670-8fd30e80523e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The triceps brachii (medial head) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The medial head of the triceps brachii is innervated by the radial nerve (C6-8). The median, musculocutaneous, axillary, and ulnar nerves do not supply the triceps.'),
('99606e19-a720-5cf9-a08c-82f6d85ffe2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: the triceps brachii (medial head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Radial nerve (C6-8)","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve (C6-8)', 'The radial nerve (C6-8) innervates the medial head of the triceps brachii along with the long and lateral heads. The median, musculocutaneous, axillary, and ulnar nerves serve other muscles.'),
('7d89c3cf-df0f-5759-b832-97514e0db03d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by which nerve? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2', 'The spinal part of the accessory nerve (CN XI) motor-supplies the sternocleidomastoid, reinforced by C2 proprioceptive fibers. The vagus nerve supplies pharyngeal, laryngeal, and visceral targets; the hypoglossal nerve serves the tongue muscles, and the facial nerve drives the muscles of facial expression.'),
('feba1c31-7d6c-5fbc-a1e4-3e052c3e8602'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the sternocleidomastoid is supplied by which nerve?', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2', 'Together CN XI and C2 supply the sternocleidomastoid, the accessory nerve providing motor fibers and C2 carrying proprioception. None of the other listed nerves—vagus, hypoglossal, or facial—provides motor supply to this muscle.'),
('fb769451-b3fb-5311-8b63-8a72cd6aa61d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which nerve supplies the sternocleidomastoid?', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2', 'Motor supply to the sternocleidomastoid comes from the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with additional C2 input. The vagus, hypoglossal, and facial nerves each innervate different structures and do not supply this muscle.'),
('e2f2e105-f008-5a77-a7c0-e166e0235427'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2', 'CN XI is the principal motor nerve of the sternocleidomastoid, with C2 contributing proprioceptive sensation. The vagus, hypoglossal, and facial nerves have unrelated functions and provide no innervation to it.'),
('3865e51b-1770-5e59-a389-9f72c3086b0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which nerve supplies the sternocleidomastoid?', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2', 'The sternocleidomastoid is innervated by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), supplemented by C2. This pattern differs from the vagus (visceral and laryngeal), hypoglossal (tongue), and facial (expression) nerves.'),
('d1b9c20b-1e21-50fe-9058-bd68057645a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius is supplied by which nerve? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Dorsal scapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","4"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The trapezius receives motor fibers from CN XI, with proprioceptive input from C3. The dorsal scapular nerve supplies the rhomboids, the long thoracic nerve the serratus anterior, and the suprascapular nerve the supraspinatus and infraspinatus.'),
('9d8d4a23-7cc9-553e-8874-257561695ea5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the trapezius is supplied by which nerve?', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Dorsal scapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","4"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'CN XI motor-innervates the trapezius while C3 supplies its proprioception. The dorsal scapular, long thoracic, and suprascapular nerves are not involved in trapezius supply.'),
('05e52f5a-a7bf-5aa9-b879-7ccef7981724'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which nerve supplies the trapezius?', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Dorsal scapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","4"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The accessory nerve (CN XI) drives the trapezius, supported by C3 fibers. The other nerves listed serve the rhomboids, serratus anterior, and rotator cuff muscles instead.'),
('b192335b-54a7-585b-a452-1adab4e50552'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Dorsal scapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","4"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'Trapezius innervation is provided by CN XI with C3. The dorsal scapular nerve acts on rhomboids and levator scapulae, the long thoracic nerve on serratus anterior, and the suprascapular nerve on the spinati.'),
('4ecd1fd9-ee0b-5bb7-aba3-356fa4df6041'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which nerve supplies the trapezius?', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Dorsal scapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","4"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'Motor control of the trapezius comes from the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) assisted by C3. The dorsal scapular, long thoracic, and suprascapular nerves supply different muscles of the shoulder girdle.'),
('e14a5368-3551-5863-aae8-c4753dd77140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid major is supplied by which nerve? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) innervates the rhomboid major as well as rhomboid minor and levator scapulae. The long thoracic nerve serves serratus anterior, the suprascapular nerve the spinati, and the accessory nerve the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid.'),
('f2fc7694-049a-556e-8761-f7f68085d6d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the rhomboid major is supplied by which nerve?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'Rhomboid major is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve arising from C5. The long thoracic, suprascapular, and accessory nerves act on other shoulder girdle muscles.'),
('6465a781-da82-57f9-acf1-3119284671af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which nerve supplies the rhomboid major?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) provides the sole motor supply to rhomboid major. Long thoracic, suprascapular, and accessory nerves each serve different muscles.'),
('33c12a5d-88d4-50c3-9254-24ea41501197'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid major is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'Rhomboid major receives its innervation from the dorsal scapular nerve (C5), which also reaches rhomboid minor and levator scapulae. The other options supply serratus anterior, the spinati, or the trapezius.'),
('04f707c5-cb0d-5ae7-9300-0f8e4dd1decd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which nerve supplies the rhomboid major?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Long thoracic nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) motor-innervates the rhomboid major. Neither the long thoracic nerve nor the suprascapular nerve reaches this muscle, and the accessory nerve is unrelated to it.'),
('d788cf68-4db5-5c2e-ae65-52845865e3f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid minor is supplied by which nerve? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) innervates rhomboid minor, just as it does rhomboid major and levator scapulae. Suprascapular, subscapular, and long thoracic nerves supply the spinati, subscapularis, and serratus anterior respectively.'),
('6e0b3e40-78f5-5fa9-816f-77be2e93fbc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the rhomboid minor is supplied by which nerve?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'Rhomboid minor is served by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5). The suprascapular nerve, subscapular nerve, long thoracic nerve, and accessory nerve all have different target muscles.'),
('7dd3d28e-2e4c-57da-8f08-af9d40abff1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which nerve supplies the rhomboid minor?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) supplies rhomboid minor together with rhomboid major. The suprascapular, subscapular, long thoracic, and accessory nerves act on other structures.'),
('027043fa-9774-5012-90bd-888a0c52f0d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rhomboid minor is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'Rhomboid minor is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve from C5. This same nerve also reaches rhomboid major and levator scapulae, whereas the other options serve separate muscles.'),
('bb3c9e57-5201-5014-ae89-66ebd8138a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which nerve supplies the rhomboid minor?', '["Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)","Suprascapular nerve","Subscapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) is the motor supply of rhomboid minor. The suprascapular nerve, subscapular nerve, long thoracic nerve, and accessory nerve do not innervate it.'),
('199f945b-59c0-5c72-a7b7-65392e0c9f6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (upper part) is supplied by which nerve? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Accessory nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) innervates all parts of serratus anterior, which stabilizes the scapula and is tested by the push-off against a wall. The accessory nerve, thoracodorsal nerve, dorsal scapular nerve, and suprascapular nerve supply the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, and spinati respectively.'),
('954b60e9-9661-5ed1-8d02-3f4011add105'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the serratus anterior (upper part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Accessory nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'Serratus anterior receives its motor supply from the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The accessory, thoracodorsal, dorsal scapular, and suprascapular nerves each serve different shoulder muscles.'),
('ea0c1e90-896c-595e-ad5c-4cbd9f26ae14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which nerve supplies the serratus anterior (upper part)?', '["Accessory nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) drives serratus anterior; its injury produces a winged scapula. None of the other listed nerves reaches this muscle.'),
('d6c6b434-6264-52b1-8d4f-59d073badde5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (upper part) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Accessory nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'All parts of serratus anterior are innervated by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The accessory nerve, thoracodorsal nerve, dorsal scapular nerve, and suprascapular nerve have other targets.'),
('991e9c7e-7b61-5b8f-9bad-3e1b2fafd95c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which nerve supplies the serratus anterior (upper part)?', '["Accessory nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'Serratus anterior is supplied by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7), and weakness here causes winging of the scapula. The other options are motor nerves of the trapezius, latissimus dorsi, rhomboids, and rotator cuff muscles.'),
('d85344ae-c548-5d0d-8c61-19a0f5df1c15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (middle part) is supplied by which nerve? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The middle part of serratus anterior, like the rest of the muscle, is innervated by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The suprascapular, thoracodorsal, accessory, and dorsal scapular nerves supply other shoulder girdle muscles.'),
('ef630a11-abc4-58a9-b17a-e6c441c47dc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the serratus anterior (middle part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) supplies the middle part of serratus anterior. The suprascapular nerve, thoracodorsal nerve, accessory nerve, and dorsal scapular nerve act on different muscles.'),
('eef21629-7296-5fc6-b090-96152e3fadff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which nerve supplies the serratus anterior (middle part)?', '["Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'Serratus anterior, including its middle part, is innervated by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The other nerves listed serve the spinati, latissimus dorsi, trapezius, and rhomboids.'),
('dbabeaa5-a6fa-5bcb-942d-e648425cf044'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (middle part) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The middle part of serratus anterior receives its supply from the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The suprascapular, thoracodorsal, accessory, and dorsal scapular nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('5ddd778d-d709-53c8-b8fe-89963f571482'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which nerve supplies the serratus anterior (middle part)?', '["Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Suprascapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) innervates the middle portion of serratus anterior. Weakness or injury of this nerve results in a winged scapula; the remaining options are nerves to other muscles.'),
('7efa182f-c4a1-58b1-b042-5b3e0e9c0502'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (lower part) is supplied by which nerve? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Suprascapular nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The lower part of serratus anterior is supplied by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The suprascapular, accessory, dorsal scapular, and thoracodorsal nerves all have different targets.'),
('8f51cbb2-deff-5ee3-9573-c0b61f16b5d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: the serratus anterior (lower part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) provides the motor supply to the lower part of serratus anterior. The suprascapular nerve, accessory nerve, dorsal scapular nerve, and thoracodorsal nerve do not reach it.'),
('52592c74-c607-52e4-915b-83cc4861110d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which nerve supplies the serratus anterior (lower part)?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'Serratus anterior, including its lower part, is driven by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). Loss of this nerve causes scapular winging during forward pushing.'),
('fb38b4d7-d8cd-5e0b-8c9f-6de18a893721'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The serratus anterior (lower part) is supplied by which nerve? Select the best answer.', '["Suprascapular nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The lower part of serratus anterior is innervated by the long thoracic nerve (C5-7). The other listed nerves supply the rotator cuff, trapezius, rhomboids, and latissimus dorsi.'),
('b829c211-838d-51fc-967c-149c7d61dec4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which nerve supplies the serratus anterior (lower part)?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Accessory nerve","Dorsal scapular nerve","Thoracodorsal nerve","Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-7)', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-7) supplies the lower fibers of serratus anterior. The suprascapular, accessory, dorsal scapular, and thoracodorsal nerves innervate other muscles of the shoulder.'),
('f484ea0b-8005-577c-8d55-d6fec43ee9e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which option correctly describes the muscle most commonly tested for ulnar nerve function?', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor indicis","Flexor pollicis longus","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'First dorsal interosseous', 'The first dorsal interosseous is supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve and is the standard muscle used to test ulnar motor function. Opponens pollicis and abductor pollicis brevis are median-innervated, while extensor indicis and flexor pollicis longus belong to radial and anterior interosseous territories.'),
('2477af3d-4675-52c0-b3a1-e125ac2711a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for ulnar nerve function is which of the following? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor indicis","Flexor pollicis longus","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'First dorsal interosseous', 'Ulnar motor function is classically assessed using the first dorsal interosseous, which is innervated by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. The thenar muscles are median-supplied, extensor indicis is radial-supplied, and flexor pollicis longus is median-supplied via the anterior interosseous nerve.'),
('1deb8d4c-d4ef-5503-8da7-4d5e1128f8e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: which muscle is most commonly tested for ulnar nerve function?', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor indicis","Flexor pollicis longus","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'First dorsal interosseous', 'The first dorsal interosseous is innervated by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve and is the most frequently used clinical test of that nerve. Opponens pollicis and abductor pollicis brevis are median-innervated, and extensor indicis is radial-innervated.'),
('ae38fefd-cb00-532d-9a15-ce0564f1627f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which muscle is most commonly tested for ulnar nerve function?', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor indicis","Flexor pollicis longus","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'First dorsal interosseous', 'Testing finger abduction against resistance evaluates the first dorsal interosseous, supplied by the ulnar nerve. The thenar muscles and flexor pollicis longus are median-supplied, while extensor indicis is supplied by the radial nerve.'),
('ac3004fa-32e6-5535-9683-1f571c2df530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for ulnar nerve function is which of the following? Select the best answer.', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor indicis","Flexor pollicis longus","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'First dorsal interosseous', 'The first dorsal interosseous is the preferred muscle for testing ulnar nerve integrity. Opponens pollicis, abductor pollicis brevis, and flexor pollicis longus are median-innervated, and extensor indicis is radial-innervated.'),
('fed840be-3582-5317-97f3-2d8d5a6b9653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which muscle is most commonly tested for ulnar nerve function?', '["Opponens pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis","Extensor indicis","Flexor pollicis longus","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'First dorsal interosseous', 'Ulnar nerve function is best assessed with the first dorsal interosseous, which it innervates via its deep branch. The other options are supplied by the median nerve, except extensor indicis, which is supplied by the radial nerve.'),
('8e15dc19-9390-55c6-b60c-141e86e28819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which option correctly describes the muscle most commonly tested for median nerve function in the hand?', '["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)', 'Abductor pollicis brevis is a thenar muscle innervated by the recurrent branch of the median nerve, making it the standard test of median motor function in the hand. Extensor pollicis longus is radial-supplied, while flexor carpi ulnaris, adductor pollicis, and first dorsal interosseous are ulnar-supplied.'),
('d3246455-ca1d-5458-b73b-ca33eaa5320c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for median nerve function in the hand is which of the following? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)', 'The recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve supplies abductor pollicis brevis, so this muscle is used to test median motor function in the hand. Extensor pollicis longus is radial-supplied; flexor carpi ulnaris, adductor pollicis, and first dorsal interosseous are ulnar-supplied.'),
('e3b392a7-9f85-5f17-a0ca-8552e7f8b583'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: which muscle is most commonly tested for median nerve function in the hand?', '["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)', 'Abductor pollicis brevis is innervated by the recurrent branch of the median nerve and is the muscle most commonly assessed for median nerve function in the hand. The other listed muscles are either radial- or ulnar-innervated.'),
('2c4a3ae4-a3fd-5095-8343-61081e3c29a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'For innervation of muscles, identify the correct response: which muscle is most commonly tested for median nerve function in the hand?', '["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)', 'Median nerve function in the hand is tested through abductor pollicis brevis, supplied by its recurrent branch. Extensor pollicis longus is radial-supplied, and flexor carpi ulnaris, adductor pollicis, and first dorsal interosseous are ulnar-supplied.'),
('953edc00-6554-5d3e-b41b-2f692137fd4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for median nerve function in the hand is which of the following? Select the best answer.', '["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)', 'Abductor pollicis brevis receives its supply from the recurrent branch of the median nerve and is the standard test of median function in the hand. The remaining options are muscles of radial or ulnar nerve supply.'),
('9c444171-89b6-531e-9169-4c7dbc70328e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which response correctly addresses this innervation of muscles item: which muscle is most commonly tested for median nerve function in the hand?', '["Extensor pollicis longus","Flexor carpi ulnaris","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)","First dorsal interosseous"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis (APB)', 'The recurrent branch of the median nerve innervates abductor pollicis brevis, making it the key muscle for testing median nerve function in the hand. Extensor pollicis longus is radial-supplied, and the other options are ulnar-supplied.'),
('56ca995d-227e-52f6-a1dd-975a753d97e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which option correctly describes the muscle most commonly tested for radial nerve function?', '["Anconeus","Triceps","Brachioradialis","Extensor pollicis longus","Extensor digitorum"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor pollicis longus', 'Extensor pollicis longus is innervated by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve and is tested by extending the thumb against resistance to evaluate radial nerve function. Anconeus, triceps, brachioradialis, and extensor digitorum are also radial-supplied but do not serve as the preferred test muscle.'),
('6a608f99-bc9c-5978-840f-03ca8f4db90c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for radial nerve function is which of the following? Choose the most accurate option.', '["Anconeus","Triceps","Brachioradialis","Extensor pollicis longus","Extensor digitorum"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor pollicis longus', 'The radial nerve, via its posterior interosseous branch, supplies extensor pollicis longus, and thumb extension is used to test radial nerve function. While anconeus, triceps, brachioradialis, and extensor digitorum are also radial-supplied, extensor pollicis longus is the most commonly used indicator.'),
('df490de3-d03f-5e3c-85cc-12307bb86fcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Choose the most accurate statement about innervation of muscles: which muscle is most commonly tested for radial nerve function?', '["Anconeus","Triceps","Brachioradialis","Extensor pollicis longus","Extensor digitorum"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor pollicis longus', 'Extensor pollicis longus is supplied by the posterior interosseous branch of the radial nerve, so resisted thumb extension tests radial nerve integrity. The other options are also radial-innervated muscles but are not the primary muscle chosen for testing this nerve.'),
('5b4b29e9-405a-564a-a6e8-d2afef9f629b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The muscle most commonly tested for radial nerve function is', '["Anconeus","Triceps","Brachioradialis","Extensor pollicis longus","Extensor digitorum"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor pollicis longus', 'Extension of the thumb''s interphalangeal joint tests extensor pollicis longus, a reliable motor test of the radial nerve. Triceps and brachioradialis also receive radial supply but act on the elbow, not the thumb, so they do not isolate its distal motor function.'),
('7cc9f232-6214-51ea-95b9-e6745163596d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('50bc6031-0360-591e-8469-7390f723066a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Innervation of Muscles: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('d7f3fc70-76b4-5ce2-89d7-93f305f6c240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The largest branch of the axillary artery is', '["Subscapular artery","Posterior circumflex humeral","Thoracoacromial artery","Superior thoracic artery","Lateral thoracic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapular artery', 'The subscapular artery is the largest branch of the third part of the axillary artery and divides into the circumflex scapular and thoracodorsal arteries. The posterior circumflex humeral, thoracoacromial, superior thoracic, and lateral thoracic arteries are smaller named branches.'),
('5af95737-9333-5bb3-a34e-c1ee9b890c5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Regarding clinical cases in Innervation of Muscles: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('4dac52a0-af20-5ded-86ad-ecd9c06a7d55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In the study of Innervation of Muscles, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('a45ee4be-d462-5bca-bedb-5aab993e23f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Innervation of Muscles: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('4cf27ae0-ced5-5e81-8992-004f0a3456bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('c40a34e6-4a1d-509f-a6c6-f7c608415d8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'During therapeutic management of Innervation of Muscles, Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('77bf56da-366f-522b-9d85-db451360ac94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial artery is a branch of the', '["Ulnar artery","Subclavian artery","Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)","Interosseous artery","Axillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)', 'The brachial artery terminates in the cubital fossa by dividing into the radial and ulnar arteries, so the radial artery is its lateral terminal branch. It does not arise from the axillary, subclavian, ulnar, or interosseous arteries.'),
('57a7ccbb-aac1-5dbd-953d-81f0a7f6cf4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In the study of Innervation of Muscles, Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('26c29ffd-2f51-57ce-9888-39474928f3b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Innervation of Muscles: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('4156cb3f-dfc6-59be-815b-d5d5c4500b40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Innervation of Muscles: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('90c83827-52d9-5bab-a61e-c6355b01aea8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'During therapeutic management of Innervation of Muscles, Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('18dac3e6-cd7c-5bff-bc4c-10517f2f85be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ulnar artery is a branch of the', '["Axillary artery","Interosseous artery","Subclavian artery","Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)","Radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery (in the cubital fossa)', 'The ulnar artery is the medial terminal branch of the brachial artery, given off in the cubital fossa. The interosseous artery arises from the ulnar artery, and the axillary and subclavian arteries are more proximal.'),
('fb6b23a5-1f4e-5e44-a552-50591fbd0f6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In medical practice, Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('208a5781-2a75-59e2-a968-f03c3e0cd849'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('9e76d662-4ff0-5c0a-96c3-36cde21756cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('708f6302-63d2-5f30-895e-53e9095947c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'During therapeutic management, Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('c6188881-c678-5c56-bedb-f642b1724dbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common interosseous artery is a branch of', '["Interosseous artery","Ulnar artery","Anterior interosseous artery","Brachial artery","Radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar artery', 'The common interosseous artery arises from the proximal part of the ulnar artery and immediately divides into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries. It is not a branch of the radial, brachial, or an interosseous artery itself.'),
('fee1e035-9d9c-5696-b9ce-0045442aac9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In medical practice, Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('b69d5748-d700-521a-9277-e1efdc67a57b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('60075ff2-4572-5dca-93d6-3dd00b693bb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('2dee81fe-0f4b-5544-bda0-35b01e87327f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'During therapeutic management, Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('c31c17ac-f603-5063-a5b4-7dcf3deb3adb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The radial collateral artery is a branch of', '["Brachial artery","Profunda brachii artery","Superior ulnar collateral","Ulnar artery","Radial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Profunda brachii artery', 'The profunda brachii artery, which accompanies the radial nerve in the radial groove, terminates as the radial collateral and middle collateral arteries. The brachial, ulnar, and superior ulnar collateral arteries provide the ulnar-side collateral circulation instead.'),
('8fec6161-1fef-51ad-9280-54cae21e52f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In medical practice, Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('dbc73a98-d93b-56f0-8502-0b12cb89ae5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('e34ac163-7b5f-5569-a9c0-fb5820f277e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('307ffd28-c1c7-52bf-8f86-4eb60c1722df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'During therapeutic management, Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('6b7ed64b-55d1-5fff-a2fe-f6e66c05d2a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial collateral artery (posterior descending) is a branch of', '["Superior ulnar collateral","Radial artery","Brachial artery","Profunda brachii artery","Ulnar artery"]'::jsonb, 'Profunda brachii artery', 'The medial collateral (posterior descending) artery is the second terminal branch of the profunda brachii artery and joins the anastomosis around the elbow posteriorly. The superior ulnar collateral is a separate branch of the brachial artery on the ulnar side.'),
('9086b0ee-cff2-560a-9714-1f661aa053c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In medical practice, Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('b832a510-a822-50a0-af15-4d0fadad5c1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('8268df3f-319c-5662-a86d-8e2738a6bd2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('cec927ed-0a8e-5067-8b61-45bd184516f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'During therapeutic management, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('ba95d565-ccdf-5920-8aa2-bc7a3519ae32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial palmar arch is formed mainly by', '["Deep branch of ulnar artery","Brachial artery","Radial artery","Superficial branch of ulnar artery","Median artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial branch of ulnar artery', 'The superficial palmar arch is chiefly formed by the superficial branch (continuation) of the ulnar artery and is completed laterally by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery. The deep branch of the ulnar artery contributes to the deep arch instead.'),
('e24dbfa7-8a66-5069-9746-4990315da8e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In medical practice, EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('428feeb6-f3a0-5f8d-b58e-dcbd26968d68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Innervation of Muscles: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('2bc885e2-5ed4-51e9-9ac1-9b162c000997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('e57215e7-8ae4-5780-a438-e93670be6b7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'During therapeutic management of Innervation of Muscles, What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('7fa6522c-7d7f-5162-b626-9a6353ec1ea5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial palmar arch is completed laterally by', '["Radialis indicis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial palmar branch of radial artery","Deep palmar branch of radial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Princeps pollicis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial palmar branch of radial artery', 'The superficial palmar arch, formed chiefly by the ulnar artery, is closed on the lateral side by the superficial palmar branch of the radial artery. Princeps pollicis and radialis indicis arise from the radial artery more distally and do not complete the arch.'),
('aef9d35c-5af2-5662-8451-e224db716195'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'In upper limb innervation, the radial nerve (before its bifurcation) supplies which muscles of the forearm?', '["Hand intrinsics resulting in decreased functional activity","Brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)","All extensors mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexor muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pronator muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL)', 'Before dividing, the radial nerve innervates brachioradialis and extensor carpi radialis longus in the forearm. The remaining posterior compartment muscles are supplied by its deep branch, the posterior interosseous nerve, making these two the exception.'),
('44a49a0d-b580-55f9-ad74-73ccfdf23c37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior interosseous nerve (deep branch of radial) supplies all extensor muscles except', '["Extensor digitorum resulting in decreased functional activity","ECRL (supplied by radial nerve before bifurcation)","ECRB mediated by specific receptor activation","Abductor pollicis longus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extensor carpi ulnaris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ECRL (supplied by radial nerve before bifurcation)', 'Extensor carpi radialis longus receives its nerve supply from the radial nerve proximal to the bifurcation, not from the posterior interosseous nerve. Extensor digitorum, ECRB, abductor pollicis longus, and extensor carpi ulnaris are all supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve.'),
('4cfc766a-b080-5e78-81bf-8aab34e80620'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior interosseous nerve supplies', '["supinator","ECU","ECRB","EDM","ED"]'::jsonb, 'ECRB', 'The posterior interosseous nerve innervates extensor carpi radialis brevis (ECRB) as well as the supinator and the deep extensor group. Extensor digitorum, extensor carpi ulnaris, and extensor digiti minimi are also posterior interosseous muscles but ECRB is the option selected here; ECRL is spared as it is supplied by the radial nerve before bifurcation.'),
('c885fec6-8d09-5180-9e84-b9dd422d617c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7) supplies', '["Triceps","biceps brachii","Deltoid and teres minor","Coracobrachialis","brachialis"]'::jsonb, 'Coracobrachialis', 'The musculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7) innervates the anterior compartment of the arm: coracobrachialis, biceps brachii, and brachialis. Deltoid and teres minor are supplied by the axillary nerve, and triceps is supplied by the radial nerve.'),
('f8a328cd-8eba-516e-a78d-11741222ef41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The recurrent branch of the median nerve supplies', '["Abductor pollicis brevis","Hypothenar muscles","superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis","All thenar muscles including adductor pollicis","opponens pollicis"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis', 'The recurrent (thenar) branch of the median nerve supplies abductor pollicis brevis, opponents pollicis, and the superficial head of flexor pollicis brevis. It does not supply adductor pollicis or the hypothenar muscles, which are innervated by the ulnar nerve.'),
('fd8b7929-bf9f-5634-8cad-6a5269186bf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) supplies', '["Rhomboid major","Levator scapulae only","Rhomboids only","rhomboid minor","and levator scapulae"]'::jsonb, 'Rhomboid major', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) innervates both the rhomboid major and rhomboid minor, and it also contributes to levator scapulae. Levator scapulae additionally receives branches from C3 and C4, and is therefore not supplied solely by this nerve.'),
('10df0aaf-e7f2-56fe-92a3-6887e8555991'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) supplies', '["Pectoralis major","Rhomboids","Levator scapulae","Trapezius","Serratus anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The long thoracic nerve (C5-C7) innervates only the serratus anterior, and its paralysis causes winging of the scapula. Pectoralis major is supplied by the medial and lateral pectoral nerves, the rhomboids by the dorsal scapular nerve, levator scapulae partly by C3-C4, and trapezius by the spinal accessory nerve.'),
('38b346ae-ee38-56ea-90ac-63eae05863f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6-C8) supplies', '["Deltoid","Teres major","Latissimus dorsi","Subscapularis","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'The thoracodorsal nerve (C6-C8) is the motor nerve of latissimus dorsi. Teres major and subscapularis are innervated by the upper and lower subscapular nerves, deltoid by the axillary nerve, and pectoralis major by the pectoral nerves.'),
('66b4229c-0fe1-511a-bdb9-5ba206369d27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The trapezius muscle is supplied by', '["Dorsal scapular nerve","Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","4 (proprioceptive)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3', 'The trapezius is innervated by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), with proprioceptive contributions from C3 and C4. The dorsal scapular nerve supplies the rhomboids, the suprascapular nerve supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus, and the long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior.'),
('1adca602-c566-5678-b7e3-9567a1b61bd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The levator scapulae is supplied by', '["Long thoracic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Suprascapular nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) and branches from C3', 'Levator scapulae is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve (C5) together with branches from C3 and C4. The long thoracic nerve supplies serratus anterior, the suprascapular nerve supplies supraspinatus and infraspinatus, and the accessory nerve supplies sternocleidomastoid and trapezius.'),
('03964af1-e200-5785-adba-b7d26e3f0d92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle is classified as which type of bone?', '["Long bone","Flat bone","Sesamoid bone","Irregular bone","Short bone"]'::jsonb, 'Long bone', 'The clavicle is an elongated shaft bone with two expanded ends, so it is classified as a long bone even though it lies horizontally and has no medullary cavity. It is also the first bone to ossify, by intramembranous ossification, unlike true long bones of the limbs.'),
('17f376e6-ffa3-52ab-8a7f-1c21e1716dca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial end of the clavicle articulates with:', '["Sternum (sternoclavicular joint)","Acromion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Scapula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum (sternoclavicular joint)', 'The enlarged medial (sternal) end of the clavicle articulates with the manubrium of the sternum to form the sternoclavicular joint, the only bony connection between the upper limb and the axial skeleton. The acromion and scapula join the lateral end, and the first rib only anchors the costoclavicular ligament nearby.'),
('036bbbde-ca2f-54eb-99a4-a5e0ad8d0802'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral end of the clavicle articulates with:', '["Sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","Acromion (acromioclavicular joint)","First rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Coracoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glenoid cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acromion (acromioclavicular joint)', 'The flat lateral (acromial) end of the clavicle articulates with the acromion of the scapula at the acromioclavicular joint. The glenoid cavity articulates with the head of the humerus, not the clavicle, and the coracoid process does not form a joint with the clavicle.'),
('a6b9c7f4-01ea-58de-88c6-3007a756c0de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The conoid tubercle is located on which part of the clavicle?', '["Lateral edge","Medial edge","Superior surface near the medial end","Inferior surface near the lateral end","Anterior surface"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface near the lateral end', 'The conoid tubercle lies on the inferior surface of the clavicle close to the lateral (acromial) end. It gives attachment to the conoid part of the coracoclavicular ligament, which suspends the scapula from the clavicle.'),
('d537a1bb-f197-5e70-b2b6-c989020157c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezoid line on the clavicle is for attachment of:', '["Deltoid muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)","Pectoralis major mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'The trapezoid line is an oblique ridge on the inferior surface of the lateral third of the clavicle that gives attachment to the trapezoid part of the coracoclavicular ligament. It is a ligamentous attachment, not a muscle insertion, so the trapezius and deltoid are incorrect.'),
('334d75fc-5b61-5658-86dd-bc1b4e88e8eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The impression for costoclavicular ligament is located on:', '["Anterior surface medial","Inferior surface lateral end","Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle","Posterior surface","Superior surface of lateral end"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle', 'The costoclavicular (rhomboid) impression is on the inferior surface of the medial end of the clavicle. It anchors the costoclavicular ligament, which binds the clavicle to the first costal cartilage and limits its upward movement.'),
('76e17719-0fd7-5087-bd55-1d8f2cafa10e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which muscle attaches to the superior surface of the clavicle?', '["Sternocleidomastoid","Trapezius","Platysma","Pectoralis major","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezius', 'The trapezius inserts into the lateral (acromial) third of the superior surface of the clavicle, drawing the shoulder upward and backward. Platysma lies in the skin and superficial fascia, pectoralis major and deltoid attach to the anterior surface, and the sternocleidomastoid chiefly hooks the medial end.'),
('722224c4-415d-5477-bf58-079448dd335d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which muscle attaches to the anterior surface of the clavicle?', '["Trapezius","Sternocleidomastoid","Platysma","Deltoid","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis major', 'Pectoralis major arises from the anterior surface of the medial half of the clavicle, forming its clavicular head. The other options attach elsewhere: trapezius to the superior surface, sternocleidomastoid to the medial end, and deltoid to the lateral third.'),
('c08e40b1-6677-5541-b320-366c9c7d5b2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The subclavius muscle attaches to which surface of the clavicle?', '["Superior surface","Anterior surface","Medial end","Posterior surface","Inferior surface (middle third)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface (middle third)', 'Subclavius, a small strap muscle, is attached to the groove on the inferior surface of the middle third of the clavicle. It lies just above the first rib and steadies the clavicle during shoulder movements.'),
('d952727c-6a39-59cd-8669-d68c47f66b78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle ossifies in:', '["Neither resulting in decreased functional activity","Both accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endochondral ossification mediated by specific receptor activation","Secondary cartilaginous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intramembranous ossification (first bone to ossify at 5th week)"]'::jsonb, 'Intramembranous ossification (first bone to ossify at 5th week)', 'The clavicle is the first bone to ossify, from membrane at about the 5th week of fetal life, so it develops by intramembranous ossification. It is the only long bone that does so; the other long bones form by endochondral ossification in cartilage models.'),
('29adf704-46ac-5086-880a-4a919358e7c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral (acromial) end of the clavicle has:', '["A secondary ossification center appearing at puberty","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two secondary centers mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple centers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","secondary center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A secondary ossification center appearing at puberty', 'A small secondary ossification center appears in the lateral (acromial) end of the clavicle at about 18 years of age (puberty) and fuses shortly after. The medial end develops its own secondary center later, at 18 to 20 years.'),
('986fc667-2050-5018-afcd-465b70057255'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial (sternal) end of the clavicle has:', '["A primary center only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple centers mediated by specific receptor activation","Two centers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A secondary ossification center appearing at 18-20 years (fuses at 25)"]'::jsonb, 'A secondary ossification center appearing at 18-20 years (fuses at 25)', 'The secondary ossification center of the medial (sternal) end appears at 18 to 20 years and fuses with the shaft only at about 25 years. Because it is the last epiphysis in the body to fuse, it is used for age estimation.'),
('66c676de-b228-53c6-bd4e-e37453466f0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epiphysis of the clavicle is important for:', '["Bone growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Joint movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)', 'The medial epiphysis of the clavicle fuses at about 25 years, later than any other epiphysis in the body. Its degree of fusion therefore provides a useful marker for estimating age in forensic and skeletal examination.'),
('8beb8529-d2f7-5d27-9a53-a29bef96c4b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scapula is classified as which type of bone?', '["Short bone","Sesamoid bone","Long bone","Flat bone","Irregular bone"]'::jsonb, 'Flat bone', 'The scapula has a broad, thin, blade-like body with two surfaces, so it is classified as a flat bone. Flat bones provide large areas for muscle attachment and protection of structures beneath, such as the chest wall.'),
('41b21db4-f4ec-5640-aa45-5afec70c04d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The glenoid cavity is located at which angle of the scapula?', '["None","Lateral (glenoid) angle","Superior angle","Medial border","Inferior angle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral (glenoid) angle', 'The glenoid cavity, the shallow socket that receives the head of the humerus, lies at the thick lateral (glenoid) angle of the scapula. The superior and inferior angles mark the medial border and carry separate muscle attachments.'),
('9ff77d5e-ba14-50ea-837b-cfd32c74664f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coracoid process is located on the:', '["Lateral border","Medial border","Inferior angle","Superior border of scapula","near the glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior border of scapula', 'The coracoid process projects forward and laterally from the superior border of the scapula, just medial to the glenoid cavity. It gives attachment to pectoralis minor, coracobrachialis, the short head of biceps, and the coracoacromial and coracoclavicular ligaments.'),
('da5cb061-ca4c-5646-bf91-b1b17f7f826e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The spine of the scapula separates:', '["Supraspinous and subscapular fossae","Subscapular and infraspinous fossae","None","Medial and lateral borders","Supraspinous and infraspinous fossae"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinous and infraspinous fossae', 'The spine crosses the posterior (dorsal) surface of the scapula, dividing it into the supraspinous fossa above and the infraspinous fossa below. The subscapular fossa lies on the opposite, costal surface.'),
('f3edcba8-1b88-55e6-aff8-0288de237c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The supraspinous fossa contains which muscle?', '["Infraspinatus","Teres major","Subscapularis","Teres minor","Supraspinatus"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinatus', 'Supraspinatus arises from the fossa above the scapular spine and passes beneath the acromion to insert on the greater tuberosity of the humerus. It initiates abduction of the shoulder joint and is a member of the rotator cuff.'),
('ef64be67-9535-5551-b64b-b21a57d14af0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The infraspinous fossa contains which muscle?', '["Subscapularis","Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Supraspinatus","Teres major"]'::jsonb, 'Infraspinatus', 'Infraspinatus arises from the infraspinous fossa and inserts on the middle facet of the greater tuberosity, acting as a lateral (external) rotator of the humerus. Supraspinatus lies above the spine and subscapularis on the costal surface.'),
('0a0407ee-fbde-50a1-8532-33849458e665'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The subscapular fossa contains which muscle?', '["Infraspinatus","Supraspinatus","Teres minor","Teres major","Subscapularis"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapularis', 'Subscapularis arises from the subscapular (costal) fossa on the anterior surface of the scapula and inserts on the lesser tuberosity of the humerus. It acts as a medial rotator and is the rotator cuff muscle that helps hold the humeral head in the glenoid.'),
('37ab2853-6325-54f0-8b95-461463d2205e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral border of the scapula gives origin to:', '["Serratus anterior resulting in decreased functional activity","Rhomboid major accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supraspinatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Teres minor (upper 2/3) and teres major (lower 1/3)","Infraspinatus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Teres minor (upper 2/3) and teres major (lower 1/3)', 'Teres minor arises from the upper two-thirds of the lateral border and teres major from the lower third (and inferior angle). Teres minor inserts on the greater tuberosity, whereas teres major passes to the medial lip of the intertubercular groove.'),
('2a5c070e-f0ff-5642-b628-dee8aee126c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial border of the scapula gives insertion to:', '["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Serratus anterior (costal surface)","Levator scapulae","rhomboids (dorsal surface)"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior (costal surface)', 'Serratus anterior inserts into the medial border and inferior angle on the costal surface of the scapula, pulling the scapula forward and against the chest wall. Levator scapulae and the rhomboids attach on the dorsal surface of the same border.'),
('c6c40304-f6c0-5ca0-9650-8b55f92c2553'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The superior angle of the scapula gives insertion to:', '["Serratus anterior","Levator scapulae","Supraspinatus","Rhomboid minor","Rhomboid major"]'::jsonb, 'Levator scapulae', 'Levator scapulae inserts into the superior angle and the adjoining upper part of the medial border of the scapula. It elevates the scapula, acting with the rhomboids and trapezius when shrugging the shoulder.'),
('ba5cea3b-17c9-5c8c-b32d-aa1c1fdb5ed0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The inferior angle of the scapula gives insertion to:', '["None","Levator scapulae","Serratus anterior (lower part)","Latissimus dorsi (via slips)","Rhomboideus major"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior (lower part)', 'The lower slips of serratus anterior insert into the inferior angle and the adjacent lower medial border of the scapula. Levator scapulae inserts on the superior angle rather than here, and teres major takes origin from the inferior angle on the dorsal surface.'),
('858cdd42-3598-56e2-8978-f391b1af4f0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The acromion process of the scapula articulates with:', '["Humerus resulting in decreased functional activity","Coracoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","First rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clavicle (acromioclavicular joint)"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle (acromioclavicular joint)', 'The acromion articulates with the lateral end of the clavicle at the acromioclavicular joint. The humerus articulates with the glenoid cavity, and the acromion does not directly meet the coracoid, sternum, or ribs.'),
('74f2761e-b746-50b8-b340-a2363f0e12fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The acromion process provides attachment to:', '["Triceps","Pectoralis major","Biceps","Latissimus dorsi","Deltoid and trapezius muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Deltoid and trapezius muscles', 'The deltoid arises from the lateral border of the acromion and the trapezius inserts into its medial border and upper surface, converging on the shoulder. The other muscles attach to the thorax, humerus, or scapula and do not reach the acromion.'),
('6e74fbab-5065-529a-bcb1-0edb4e9a967a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coracoid process gives attachment to all except:', '["Supraspinatus","Coracobrachialis","Short head of biceps","Coracoacromial ligament","Pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinatus', 'Pectoralis minor, coracobrachialis, the short head of biceps, and the coracoacromial ligament all attach to the coracoid process. Supraspinatus arises from the supraspinous fossa and is the one structure that does not attach here.'),
('e5069c2c-7e65-54f7-b435-aa962d003310'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scapular notch is located:', '["near the coracoid","On the superior border of the scapula","On the lateral border","At the inferior angle","On the medial border"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior border of the scapula', 'The scapular (suprascapular) notch is a notch on the superior border of the scapula just medial to the coracoid process. The suprascapular nerve passes through it under the superior transverse scapular ligament.'),
('6df93e02-899b-598d-9813-b6e045290433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The suprascapular notch is bridged by:', '["Acromioclavicular ligament","None","Coracoacromial ligament","Superior transverse scapular ligament","Coracoclavicular ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Superior transverse scapular ligament', 'The superior transverse scapular ligament spans the suprascapular notch, converting it into a foramen through which the suprascapular nerve passes. The suprascapular artery runs above the ligament, not through it.'),
('164e75a9-ad25-5070-b692-8f2c10a2460c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The spinoglenoid notch is located between:', '["The medial border and the spine","The coracoid and the glenoid","The spine of the scapula and the glenoid cavity","None","The acromion and the clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'The spine of the scapula and the glenoid cavity', 'The spinoglenoid notch is the gap between the lateral root of the spine of the scapula and the glenoid cavity. The suprascapular nerve and vessels pass through it to reach the infraspinous fossa.'),
('3d40490a-f086-507e-ab05-70139516ceb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The spine of the scapula is continuous with which process?', '["Inferior angle","Glenoid cavity","Acromion process","Coracoid process","Superior angle"]'::jsonb, 'Acromion process', 'The spine of the scapula continues laterally into the broad, flattened acromion that projects above the shoulder joint. The coracoid is a separate hook-like process arising from the superior border.'),
('60821012-3b7e-5ba5-987b-34e57abb07e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The humerus is classified as which type of bone?', '["Short bone","Sesamoid bone","Long bone","Flat bone","Irregular bone"]'::jsonb, 'Long bone', 'The humerus has a cylindrical shaft and two expanded ends, making it a typical long bone. It ossifies by endochondral ossification within a cartilaginous model.'),
('20d0a226-5576-5131-b897-45045855e39e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the humerus articulates with:', '["Sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","Coracoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Acromion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glenoid cavity of scapula"]'::jsonb, 'Glenoid cavity of scapula', 'The hemispherical head of the humerus articulates with the shallow glenoid cavity of the scapula to form the glenohumeral (shoulder) joint. This configuration allows a very wide range of movement but relies on surrounding muscles for stability.'),
('157a41e5-2b1b-59a9-a946-5fe86b4524f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The anatomical neck of the humerus separates:', '["None","The shaft from the head","The tuberosities from the shaft","The shaft from the condyles","The head from the tuberosities"]'::jsonb, 'The head from the tuberosities', 'The anatomical neck is the slight constriction just distal to the articular head, separating the head from the greater and lesser tuberosities. The surgical neck lies further down, below the tuberosities at the junction with the shaft.'),
('5fb13177-7741-58c6-a39e-291796f53f36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The surgical neck of the humerus is located:', '["Between the head and tuberosities resulting in decreased functional activity","Below the tuberosities (at the junction with shaft)","At the condyles mediated by specific receptor activation","At the midshaft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the distal end leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Below the tuberosities (at the junction with shaft)', 'The surgical neck is the narrowed region of the humerus below the tuberosities at its junction with the shaft. It is clinically important because fractures here can injure the axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral vessels.'),
('5149ddc6-684c-5328-9f7f-1595f63b532c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The greater tuberosity of the humerus is located:', '["Lateral to the head","Medial to the head","Inferior to the head","Anterior to the head","Posterior to the head"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to the head', 'The greater tuberosity projects laterally from the upper humerus, just beyond the anatomical neck. It receives the insertions of supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor; the lesser tuberosity lies medially and anteriorly and receives subscapularis.'),
('a00647fb-0524-53b0-8009-37fcabe0307c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lesser tuberosity of the humerus is located:', '["Inferior to the head","Anterior (medial) to the head","Posterior to the head","Lateral to the head","Superior to the head"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (medial) to the head', 'The lesser tuberosity projects medially and anteriorly from the upper humerus, facing the front of the shoulder. It gives insertion to subscapularis and is separated from the greater tuberosity by the intertubercular groove.'),
('81c7e97c-e4bd-550c-89b2-185223a7163e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The intertubercular (bicipital) groove is located between:', '["The head and the shaft","The shaft and the condyles","The tuberosities and the head","The condyles","Greater and lesser tuberosities (anteriorly)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser tuberosities (anteriorly)', 'The intertubercular (bicipital) groove runs downward between the greater and lesser tuberosities on the anterior aspect of the upper humerus. It transmits the tendon of the long head of biceps brachii and receives muscular attachments on its lips and floor.'),
('d4af10e0-11d7-5bf4-b56b-ce5781bca9ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The intertubercular groove gives attachment to:', '["pectoralis major (lateral lip)","Latissimus dorsi (floor)","teres major (medial lip)","Subscapularis","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi (floor)', 'Latissimus dorsi inserts into the floor of the intertubercular groove of the humerus. Pectoralis major attaches to the lateral lip and teres major to the medial lip of the same groove, while the long head of biceps tendon runs through it.'),
('236381e8-b890-5bfa-aeba-91001f0727c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The deltoid tuberosity is located on:', '["The anterior aspect resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior aspect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medial aspect mediated by specific receptor activation","The proximal end without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lateral aspect of the midshaft of the humerus"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral aspect of the midshaft of the humerus', 'The deltoid tuberosity is a rough V-shaped area on the lateral side of the middle of the humeral shaft. It receives the insertion of the deltoid muscle; the radial groove lies posteriorly at the same level.'),
('3494bb79-ba3e-58fe-b50e-8ef871018cb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial (spiral) groove is located on:', '["The medial surface resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior surface of the midshaft of the humerus","The lateral surface mediated by specific receptor activation","The distal end without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The anterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior surface of the midshaft of the humerus', 'The radial (spiral) groove runs obliquely along the posterior surface of the midshaft of the humerus. It transmits the radial nerve and profunda brachii artery, structures that can be damaged in midshaft humeral fractures.'),
('35bd7e65-f8c3-57ca-9ffb-9e891c954272'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The capitulum of the humerus articulates with:', '["Radial head and ulna","Radius and ulna","None","Trochlear notch of ulna","Radial head (proximal radius)"]'::jsonb, 'Radial head (proximal radius)', 'The rounded capitulum, on the lateral side of the distal humerus, articulates with the head of the radius at the humeroradial joint. The trochlea, immediately medial to it, articulates with the trochlear notch of the ulna.'),
('fdde9ab2-ae9a-5d93-87a0-85b3f82de8b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trochlea of the humerus articulates with:', '["Coronoid process","Trochlear notch of the ulna","Head of radius","Olecranon","Radial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlear notch of the ulna', 'The spool-shaped trochlea of the humerus fits into the trochlear notch of the ulna at the humeroulnar joint. The coronoid process and olecranon are the two limbs of that same trochlear notch, while the capitulum meets the radial head.'),
('da10167e-f136-57dc-a654-d4d134dd5c2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epicondyle of the humerus is:', '["Lateral epicondyle resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A non-articular projection on the medial side","Anterior projection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of the articular surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A non-articular projection on the medial side', 'The medial epicondyle is a non-articular prominence on the medial side of the distal humerus, lying outside the elbow joint. It gives origin to the forearm flexor muscles and the ulnar collateral ligament, and it is crossed by the ulnar nerve behind it.'),
('0e43a882-1169-5a0c-9d8e-4a9b6b5b01f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral epicondyle of the humerus gives origin to:', '["Common extensor origin (forearm extensors)","Brachioradialis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Triceps mediated by specific receptor activation","Common flexor origin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deltoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common extensor origin (forearm extensors)', 'The lateral epicondyle gives origin to the common extensor origin of the forearm, including extensor carpi radialis brevis, extensor digitorum, extensor carpi ulnaris, and the supinator. The common flexor origin lies on the medial epicondyle.'),
('d11837e0-1c2a-52ba-8155-186e2951776e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epicondyle gives origin to:', '["Common flexor origin (forearm flexors)","Deltoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brachialis mediated by specific receptor activation","Triceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common extensor origin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common flexor origin (forearm flexors)', 'The medial epicondyle gives origin to the common flexor origin of the forearm, including pronator teres, flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, and flexor carpi ulnaris. The common extensors arise from the lateral epicondyle instead.'),
('5a8a4eba-ef2c-56cc-8e57-d05718c6498f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The olecranon fossa of the humerus is located:', '["Distally resulting in decreased functional activity","Posteriorly above the trochlea (receives olecranon of ulna in extension)","Laterally mediated by specific receptor activation","Medially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anteriorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly above the trochlea (receives olecranon of ulna in extension)', 'The olecranon fossa is a deep pit on the posterior surface of the distal humerus, above the trochlea, that receives the olecranon of the ulna during full elbow extension. The coronoid and radial fossae lie anteriorly for the ulna and radius, respectively.'),
('719c3a9f-85fd-53a4-96b5-c9c260fe0df4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coronoid fossa of the humerus is located:', '["Medially resulting in decreased functional activity","Distally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anteriorly above the trochlea (receives coronoid process of ulna in flexion)","Laterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posteriorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly above the trochlea (receives coronoid process of ulna in flexion)', 'The coronoid fossa is a shallow pit on the anterior surface of the distal humerus, above the trochlea, which receives the coronoid process of the ulna during full elbow flexion. The olecranon fossa lies posteriorly on the same bone.'),
('5cbb12a6-e6c0-56b6-a6bc-b183c5bbf5d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial fossa of the humerus is located:', '["Medially resulting in decreased functional activity","Laterally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anteriorly above the capitulum (receives radial head in full flexion)","Posteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly above the capitulum (receives radial head in full flexion)', 'The radial fossa is a shallow depression on the anterior surface of the distal humerus, above the capitulum, which accommodates the head of the radius in full elbow flexion. It lies just lateral to the coronoid fossa over the trochlea.'),
('c02cfb81-a217-54e8-835b-8f4959f3e30a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radius is classified as which type of bone?', '["Short bone","Sesamoid bone","Long bone","Irregular bone","Flat bone"]'::jsonb, 'Long bone', 'The radius has a shaft and two expanded ends, so it is a typical long bone located on the lateral side of the forearm. It articulates proximally with the humerus and ulna and distally with the scaphoid and lunate.'),
('32e14853-3e4f-5d13-a171-14fc2acb7c5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial head articulates with:', '["Olecranon resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronoid process of ulna accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Capitulum of humerus and radial notch of ulna","Trochlea of humerus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scaphoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Capitulum of humerus and radial notch of ulna', 'The radial head articulates with the capitulum of the humerus at the elbow and with the radial notch of the ulna at the proximal radioulnar joint. The olecranon, coronoid process, and trochlea concern the ulna and humerus, while the scaphoid is a carpal bone.'),
('d96f8a26-903f-5840-9d96-731e018750c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial tuberosity is located:', '["Below the neck of the radius (medial surface)","On the posterior surface","At the distal end","At the proximal end","On the lateral surface"]'::jsonb, 'Below the neck of the radius (medial surface)', 'The radial tuberosity lies just below the neck on the medial surface of the radius and gives attachment to the biceps tendon. It is not on the posterior or lateral surface, and it is separated from the distal end.'),
('7946d814-67d3-5054-ae75-0461701b3098'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial shaft is curved:', '["Laterally (interosseous border is sharp medially)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Medially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posteriorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally (interosseous border is sharp medially)', 'The shaft of the radius curves laterally and carries a sharp interosseous border medially for attachment of the interosseous membrane. It does not bow anteriorly, posteriorly, or toward the midline.'),
('1123f214-4cd5-56a0-b901-8fcf49e42a94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The distal end of the radius articulates with:', '["Scaphoid only resulting in decreased functional activity","Capitate and hamate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lunate only mediated by specific receptor activation","All carpals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scaphoid and lunate (and triquetrum via TFCC)"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid and lunate (and triquetrum via TFCC)', 'The distal radial surface articulates with the scaphoid and lunate, while the triquetrum meets the radius only indirectly through the triangular fibrocartilage complex. The capitate and hamate belong to the distal carpal row and never touch the radius.'),
('49643775-e54d-5bff-a86d-d29a5ed542ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial styloid process is located:', '["At the distal end (lateral side)","At the proximal end","Posteriorly","At the medial side","Anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'At the distal end (lateral side)', 'The radial styloid process projects from the lateral side of the distal radius and anchors the radial collateral ligament of the wrist. The proximal end, posterior, and medial sites do not carry this process.'),
('19f50a0a-4521-5d27-b53f-3e6d33803dae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulna is classified as which type of bone?', '["Short bone","Long bone","Sesamoid bone","Flat bone","Irregular bone"]'::jsonb, 'Long bone', 'The ulna has a tubular shaft with proximal and distal epiphyses and grows by endochondral ossification, meeting the criteria of a long bone. Short, sesamoid, flat, and irregular bones differ in shape and structure.'),
('79a5efca-e9cb-586f-9e79-72407284d790'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The olecranon process is located:', '["At the proximal end (posterior)","At the distal end","Medially","At the lateral side","Anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'At the proximal end (posterior)', 'The olecranon forms the posterior part of the proximal ulna and fits the olecranon fossa of the humerus in full extension, receiving the triceps tendon. It is neither distal, medial, lateral, nor anterior.'),
('f11d49d6-3710-501c-a9d8-04fa1e20ace1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coronoid process is located:', '["At the medial side","At the proximal end (posterior)","At the distal end","At the proximal end (anterior)","At the lateral side"]'::jsonb, 'At the proximal end (anterior)', 'The coronoid process projects anteriorly from the proximal ulna, engaging the coronoid fossa of the humerus in flexion and giving attachment to brachialis. The posterior, medial, lateral, and distal sites are incorrect.'),
('7c446c2c-7782-52be-b6b6-e989bb5f0388'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trochlear notch of the ulna is located:', '["At the styloid process resulting in decreased functional activity","At the distal end accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the olecranon and coronoid processes (articulates with trochlea)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the radial notch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the olecranon and coronoid processes (articulates with trochlea)', 'The trochlear (semilunar) notch is the deep excavation between the olecranon and coronoid processes that articulates with the trochlea of the humerus. The radial notch, styloid process, and distal end are separate features.'),
('de03bab8-0733-55a5-9f2d-f916fae2c1cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar tuberosity is located:', '["Below the coronoid process (anterior)","Above the olecranon","Medially","At the styloid process","At the distal end"]'::jsonb, 'Below the coronoid process (anterior)', 'The ulnar tuberosity sits on the anterior surface just below the coronoid process and receives the brachialis tendon. It is not above the olecranon, medial, at the styloid process, or at the distal end.'),
('30a12d22-b077-56a2-a39a-1b0707c7b8fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The interosseous border of the ulna is located:', '["Posteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","Anteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proximally mediated by specific receptor activation","Laterally (for interosseous membrane)","Medially leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally (for interosseous membrane)', 'The interosseous border of the ulna faces laterally and anchors the interosseous membrane that binds radius and ulna. It is not on the anterior, posterior, or medial surfaces.'),
('1a752645-85f0-5c22-b9b4-da1e385df526'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the ulna is located:', '["At the olecranon","At the proximal end","At the distal end","At the coronoid","At the radial notch"]'::jsonb, 'At the distal end', 'The head of the ulna lies at the distal end and articulates with the ulnar notch of the radius and the triangular fibrocartilage complex. The olecranon, coronoid, and radial notch are all proximal features.'),
('79931575-7881-5083-b3ca-5be09bd74c25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar styloid process is located:', '["At the radial notch","At the proximal end","At the olecranon","Anteriorly","At the distal end (posteromedial)"]'::jsonb, 'At the distal end (posteromedial)', 'The ulnar styloid process projects from the posteromedial aspect of the ulnar head at the distal end and anchors the ulnar collateral ligament of the wrist. It is not proximal, anterior, or at the radial notch.'),
('29a91ab3-a17f-59b5-8847-f815df93d919'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which carpal bone is most commonly fractured?', '["Lunate","Scaphoid","Hamate","Pisiform","Triquetrum"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid', 'The scaphoid is the most commonly fractured carpal bone, typically after a fall on an outstretched hand. Its proximal pole has a retrograde blood supply, so fractures risk non-union and avascular necrosis.'),
('fcb70f06-0058-5a5f-bfa0-9fd6e5b6e3e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which carpal bone has a proximal pole with a retrograde blood supply?', '["Scaphoid","Hamate","Capitate","Pisiform","Triquetrum"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid', 'Vessels from the dorsal and radial sides enter the scaphoid distally, so blood flows retrograde toward the proximal pole, which becomes ischemic after proximal fractures. The other carpals have more direct blood supplies.'),
('124f773f-7d59-51ab-bf9e-5273be839a9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lunate bone articulates with:', '["capitate","scaphoid","triquetrum","hamate","Radius"]'::jsonb, 'Radius', 'The lunate articulates proximally with the radius at the radiocarpal joint, and distally with the capitate, with additional facets for the scaphoid and triquetrum. Among the options, the radius is its proximal articulation.'),
('8c938776-c624-52da-928f-f34389aa15ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lunate is the most commonly:', '["None","Fractured carpal bone","Infected carpal bone","Dislocated carpal bone","Fused carpal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Dislocated carpal bone', 'The lunate is the carpal bone most often dislocated, usually following a fall on an extended wrist, as in perilunate and lunate dislocations. It is not characteristically the most fractured, infected, or fused carpal bone.'),
('231ec08d-70d7-5e02-bc7e-5f5880c7dbac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The triquetrum articulates with:', '["hamate","ulna (via TFCC)","Radius","Lunate","pisiform"]'::jsonb, 'Lunate', 'The triquetrum articulates with the lunate medially, the hamate distally, and the pisiform anteriorly, while its ulnar side contacts the TFCC rather than the ulna directly. Among the options, lunate is its closest proximal carpal articulation.'),
('ac83ef14-3c58-52c3-bc90-eaf11ebdf42a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The pisiform articulates only with which bone?', '["Triquetrum","Scaphoid","Capitate","Hamate","Lunate"]'::jsonb, 'Triquetrum', 'The pisiform, a sesamoid bone embedded in the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon, articulates only with the volar surface of the triquetrum. It has no direct articulation with the scaphoid, capitate, hamate, or lunate.'),
('3cb4a4c0-d5db-5872-9f97-9b47dd04d828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezium articulates with:', '["Scaphoid","1st metacarpal","1st metacarpal only","trapezoid","2nd metacarpal ()"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid', 'The trapezium articulates proximally with the scaphoid and distally with the first metacarpal at the saddle joint of the thumb, with smaller facets for the trapezoid and second metacarpal. Among the options, the scaphoid is its proximal carpal articulation.'),
('0093eabe-2aae-5ef0-ba23-74e0331467f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezoid bone articulates with:', '["capitate","Scaphoid","2nd metacarpal","trapezium","Trapezium only"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid', 'The trapezoid sits between the scaphoid (proximal), trapezium (lateral), capitate (medial), and second metacarpal (distal). Among the options, the scaphoid is its proximal carpal articulation.'),
('4927c98a-0b9b-5066-9dd9-38f0d24987cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The capitate is the largest carpal bone and articulates with:', '["lunate","trapezoid","Scaphoid","hamate","2nd"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid', 'The capitate, the largest carpal bone, sits centrally in the wrist and articulates proximally with the scaphoid and lunate, distally with the second to fourth metacarpals, and with the trapezoid and hamate. Among the options, the scaphoid is its proximal articulation.'),
('26bb7638-ab28-5996-9327-4efffa1cb79d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The hamate articulates with:', '["Lunate","triquetrum","capitate","5th metacarpals","4th"]'::jsonb, 'Lunate', 'The hamate articulates proximally with the lunate and triquetrum, distally with the fourth and fifth metacarpals, and with the capitate on its radial side. Among the options, the lunate is one of its proximal articulations.'),
('33d46005-ee94-5714-bb79-60557420142b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The hook of hamate projects:', '["Dorsally (posteriorly)","Palmarly (anteriorly)","Laterally","Medially","Proximally"]'::jsonb, 'Palmarly (anteriorly)', 'The hook (hamulus) of the hamate projects palmarly from the medial part of the bone and forms the medial border of the carpal tunnel. The flexor retinaculum and flexor carpi ulnaris attach to it, so it does not project dorsally, laterally, or proximally.'),
('2269d413-8dc4-5e3a-b181-f2b1de2de8e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The metacarpal bones are how many?', '["3","5","7","6","4"]'::jsonb, '5', 'There are five metacarpal bones, one for each digit, each possessing a base, shaft, and head. The first corresponds to the thumb and the fifth to the little finger.'),
('09c61ae3-51e8-5c90-b9b5-affe773e83af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The 1st metacarpal is unique because:', '["It is the longest resulting in decreased functional activity","It is fused accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is the shortest and rotates 90 degrees relative to others","It has medullary cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It has proximal epiphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is the shortest and rotates 90 degrees relative to others', 'The first metacarpal is the shortest and is rotated nearly 90 degrees, so its saddle-shaped base opposes to the other metacarpals to enable thumb opposition. Its distal epiphysis and the presence of a medullary cavity are not unique features.'),
('807c51c4-0975-5575-82a2-be7009e935d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the 1st metacarpal articulates with:', '["Capitate","Lunate","Trapezoid","Scaphoid","Trapezium"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezium', 'The saddle-shaped base of the first metacarpal articulates with the trapezium, allowing opposition and prehensile grip of the thumb. The capitate, lunate, trapezoid, and scaphoid do not receive the first metacarpal.'),
('c0606b87-136e-5f01-acb8-8e1fb5466cb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the 2nd metacarpal articulates with:', '["trapezoid","Trapezium","Capitate only","and 3rd metacarpal","capitate"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezium', 'The base of the second metacarpal wedges between the trapezium, trapezoid, and capitate and also touches the third metacarpal. Among the options, the trapezium is the most lateral of its carpal articulations.'),
('035d22e4-5cfd-5b5e-8cb8-877b48af3bdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the 3rd metacarpal articulates with:', '["and 2nd and 4th metacarpals","4th metacarpal only","Capitate","Capitate only","2nd metacarpal only"]'::jsonb, 'Capitate', 'The base of the third metacarpal is deeply notched and articulates principally with the capitate, in addition to the second and fourth metacarpals. The capitate is the main carpal articulation of the third metacarpal.'),
('0bcd53c3-83d5-59ff-b782-781768ee5f3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the 4th metacarpal articulates with:', '["and 3rd and 5th metacarpals","hamate","Capitate","Capitate only","Hamate only"]'::jsonb, 'Capitate', 'The base of the fourth metacarpal articulates proximally with both the capitate and hamate and with the third and fifth metacarpals on its sides. Among the options, the capitate is the more radial of its two carpal articulations.'),
('106248f8-5a7e-5d08-bdf0-311b29ab04d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the 5th metacarpal articulates with:', '["Hamate and 4th metacarpal","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Capitate mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trapezoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hamate and 4th metacarpal', 'The base of the fifth metacarpal articulates with the hamate proximally and with the fourth metacarpal on its radial side, and it may carry a small trapezoid facet. The capitate, trapezium, and trapezoid do not contact the fifth metacarpal.'),
('ad5e2c1b-aecf-5be9-ba26-7776ebf45494'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Each phalanx has which parts?', '["Neck and head only","Shaft only","shaft (body)","head (distal)","Base (proximal)"]'::jsonb, 'Base (proximal)', 'Each phalanx comprises a proximal base, a shaft (body), and a distal head; the proximal base is the part that articulates with the bone proximal to it at the metacarpophalangeal or interphalangeal joint. The neck and head, shaft, and head alone describe individual parts but not the full list.'),
('6e2e4646-6b53-561d-9a28-6309bcb13bee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The proximal phalanx of the thumb articulates proximally with:', '["Scaphoid","2nd metacarpal","1st metacarpal","Trapezium","Capitate"]'::jsonb, '1st metacarpal', 'The proximal phalanx of the thumb articulates proximally with the head of the first metacarpal at the thumb metacarpophalangeal joint. The scaphoid, trapezium, capitate, and second metacarpal are not adjacent to the thumb phalanx.'),
('aef74adb-0969-5560-b1ca-14b09e0b0fce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The middle phalanges are present in which fingers?', '["little (not thumb)","Thumb only","ring","middle","Index"]'::jsonb, 'Index', 'Middle phalanges are present in the index, middle, ring, and little fingers—that is, every finger except the thumb, which has only proximal and distal phalanges. Among the options, the index finger is one of the fingers that carries a middle phalanx.'),
('0f2ddd48-0a95-5fd7-943a-a572dc4943f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the proximal phalanx articulates with:', '["Metacarpal head","Base of the proximal phalanx","Base of the distal phalanx","None","Base of the middle phalanx (PIP joint)"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the middle phalanx (PIP joint)', 'The head of the proximal phalanx articulates with the base of the middle phalanx to form the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint. It does not articulate with the metacarpal head or with the bases of the proximal or distal phalanges.'),
('31022f89-f301-5138-9438-98fb5521e7fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the middle phalanx articulates with:', '["Metacarpal resulting in decreased functional activity","Base of the proximal phalanx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle phalanx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Base of the distal phalanx (DIP joint)"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the distal phalanx (DIP joint)', 'The head of the middle phalanx articulates with the base of the distal phalanx at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint. The metacarpal, proximal phalanx, and another middle phalanx are not distal joints of the middle phalanx.'),
('8372e0c0-2890-5934-959d-cb54c9f9ac1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The distal phalanx has a:', '["Ungual tuberosity (tuft) at its distal end for nail support","Head accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shaft mediated by specific receptor activation","All of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ungual tuberosity (tuft) at its distal end for nail support', 'The distal phalanx is characterized by the ungual tuberosity, a flattened tuft at its distal end that supports the nail. Although it also possesses a base and shaft like other phalanges, the ungual tuft is the feature distinctive of the distal phalanx.'),
('d392e573-0517-525b-9a25-c87cc6c5ccec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The nutrient artery of the humerus enters at:', '["The distal end","The proximal end","The anteromedial surface of the midshaft","The posterior surface","The lateral surface"]'::jsonb, 'The anteromedial surface of the midshaft', 'The nutrient artery of the humerus enters the anteromedial surface of the midshaft and supplies the medullary cavity. The distal end, proximal end, posterior surface, and lateral surface are not the entry sites.'),
('b409c4c1-32fc-5bc9-ace4-d422b00c1393'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The nutrient artery of the radius enters at:', '["The posterior surface resulting in decreased functional activity","The lateral surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medial surface mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior surface of the proximal third of the radius","The distal third leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior surface of the proximal third of the radius', 'The nutrient artery of the radius enters the anterior surface of its proximal third, so fractures in this zone threaten the blood supply of the distal fragment. The posterior, lateral, and medial surfaces and the distal third are not entry sites.'),
('3f3553ed-b931-5a80-ac6c-0ec650dae64c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The nutrient artery of the ulna enters at:', '["The lateral surface resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The distal third mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior surface of the proximal third of the ulna","The medial surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior surface of the proximal third of the ulna', 'The nutrient artery of the ulna enters the anterior surface of its proximal third and runs distally in the medullary cavity. Fractures here jeopardize the perfusion of the distal ulnar fragment.'),
('d9830d7e-27c9-5b17-bbe4-436bfd9d0611'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle fracture most commonly occurs at:', '["Acromial end resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial third accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Junction of middle and lateral thirds (middle third)","Lateral third without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternal end leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Junction of middle and lateral thirds (middle third)', 'The middle third is the thinnest, weakest portion of the clavicle and is only covered by skin and platysma, so it absorbs most direct fall forces. The stronger medial and lateral thirds fracture far less often.'),
('09fcffb6-af42-5348-9498-a1c0605a3856'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'In a clavicle fracture, the medial fragment is pulled upward by which muscle?', '["Deltoid","Pectoralis major","Sternocleidomastoid","Trapezius","the medial fragment is pulled upward by which muscle?"]'::jsonb, 'Sternocleidomastoid', 'The sternocleidomastoid, attached to the medial third of the clavicle, pulls the medial fragment upward after a middle-third fracture. The lateral fragment is displaced downward by the weight of the limb and medially by pectoralis major, while the deltoid and trapezius do not act on the medial fragment.'),
('51d38791-e6da-593c-bf18-b170cbc1eeab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle fracture may damage which underlying structure?', '["Subclavian vessels or brachial plexus","Trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esophagus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian vessels or brachial plexus', 'The subclavian vessels and brachial plexus lie directly behind the middle third of the clavicle, so displaced fragments can injure them. The trachea, esophagus, lung, and phrenic nerve are deep structures unrelated to the clavicle.'),
('8dcf0b80-4e64-5781-b551-21811447294f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The glenoid cavity of the scapula is pear-shaped and is directed:', '["Laterally and slightly anteriorly","Superiorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medially mediated by specific receptor activation","Posteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anteriorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally and slightly anteriorly', 'The glenoid cavity faces laterally and slightly anteriorly, a shallow orientation that maximizes shoulder mobility at the cost of stability. It does not face superiorly, medially, posteriorly, or purely anteriorly.'),
('67bef144-00e4-5d70-814c-fe015754a319'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The supraglenoid tubercle is located:', '["On the acromion resulting in decreased functional activity","Below the glenoid cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the coracoid mediated by specific receptor activation","On the spine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Above the glenoid cavity (origin of long head of biceps)"]'::jsonb, 'Above the glenoid cavity (origin of long head of biceps)', 'The supraglenoid tubercle lies just above the glenoid cavity and gives origin to the long head of the biceps brachii. It is not on the acromion, coracoid, or spine, nor below the cavity.'),
('486ea86b-f6ca-53b7-825c-4b3413df0bb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The infraglenoid tubercle is located:', '["On the coracoid resulting in decreased functional activity","On the spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above the glenoid cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","On the acromion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Below the glenoid cavity (origin of long head of triceps)"]'::jsonb, 'Below the glenoid cavity (origin of long head of triceps)', 'The infraglenoid tubercle lies just below the glenoid cavity and gives origin to the long head of the triceps brachii. It is not on the coracoid, acromion, or spine, nor above the cavity.'),
('682b3799-4f74-571d-9a89-6fb10cb25c62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The subscapular fossa faces:', '["Laterally resulting in decreased functional activity","Anteriorly (costal surface of scapula)","Posteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Medially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superiorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly (costal surface of scapula)', 'The subscapular fossa forms the anterior or costal surface of the scapula and gives origin to subscapularis. It does not face laterally, posteriorly, medially, or superiorly.'),
('c3309a7d-2eca-5cff-8d28-6b85af264605'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The supraspinous fossa faces:', '["Medially resulting in decreased functional activity","Posteriorly (above the spine)","Laterally mediated by specific receptor activation","Anteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferiorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly (above the spine)', 'The supraspinous fossa lies above the spine of the scapula on its posterior surface and houses supraspinatus. The medial, lateral, anterior, and inferior surfaces do not describe this fossa.'),
('e669770e-6878-524e-8e79-fb397bd40575'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The infraspinous fossa faces:', '["Laterally resulting in decreased functional activity","Medially accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superiorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Anteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posteriorly (below the spine)"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly (below the spine)', 'The infraspinous fossa lies below the spine of the scapula on its posterior surface and gives origin to infraspinatus. It does not face anteriorly, laterally, medially, or superiorly.'),
('d3a23b33-c36b-577a-9c0f-3b796b781e00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The acromion process has which shape?', '["None","Square","Round and bulbous","Pointed","Flat and triangular"]'::jsonb, 'Flat and triangular', 'The acromion is a flat, triangular projection at the lateral end of the scapular spine that articulates with the clavicle at the acromioclavicular joint. It is neither square, round and bulbous, nor pointed.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('9ccefcf9-b3a9-567b-8477-a1882e0a613d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coracoclavicular ligament maintains:', '["The acromioclavicular joint resulting in decreased functional activity","The coracoclavicular interval (holding the clavicle to the scapula)","The glenohumeral joint mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sternoclavicular joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The coracoclavicular interval (holding the clavicle to the scapula)', 'The coracoclavicular ligament runs from the coracoid process to the undersurface of the clavicle, thereby hanging the clavicle from the scapula and stabilizing the coracoclavicular interval. It does not directly span the acromioclavicular, sternoclavicular, or glenohumeral joints.'),
('3aabb005-17d3-51c0-ac5c-db88b729a063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The conoid tubercle on the clavicle is for:', '["Costoclavicular ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Conoid ligament (medial part of coracoclavicular ligament)","Trapezoid ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Interclavicular ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conoid ligament (medial part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'The conoid tubercle on the inferior surface of the clavicle anchors the conoid ligament, the medial component of the coracoclavicular ligament. The trapezoid ligament attaches to the trapezoid line, while the costoclavicular and interclavicular ligaments attach elsewhere.'),
('d8738af0-9011-55f6-9860-98adb49e88b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezoid line on the clavicle is for:', '["Conoid ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Trapezius muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavius mediated by specific receptor activation","Deltoid muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trapezoid ligament (lateral part of coracoclavicular ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid ligament (lateral part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'The trapezoid line on the underside of the clavicle gives attachment to the trapezoid ligament, the lateral part of the coracoclavicular ligament. The conoid ligament attaches to the more medial conoid tubercle, and the clavicular head of deltoid and trapezius attach to the superior surface.'),
('3aebcd69-f522-59eb-b4f7-53037d6900c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The anterior tubercle of C6 (carotid tubercle) can be palpated and is an important landmark. The scalene tubercle on the first rib is important for:', '["but in the upper limb context","the scalene tubercle on the first rib is important for:","Origin of scalenus anterior","Insertion of scalenus medius","Insertion of scalenus anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Insertion of scalenus anterior', 'Scalenus anterior inserts on the scalene tubercle of the first rib, which is a key landmark for separating the subclavian vein (anterior) from the subclavian artery and brachial plexus (posterior). Scalenus anterior arises from the anterior tubercles of C3-C6, and scalenus medius inserts behind the subclavian groove rather than on the scalene tubercle.'),
('ccf6a2be-e2c2-5f16-90f8-a94892d32251'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The first rib has which important relations?', '["Subclavian artery passes posterior to scalenus anterior","Phrenic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thoracic duct mediated by specific receptor activation","subclavian vein passes anterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung apex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery passes posterior to scalenus anterior', 'The subclavian artery crosses the first rib immediately posterior to the insertion of scalenus anterior, whereas the subclavian vein lies anterior to the muscle. The phrenic nerve descends anterior to scalenus anterior, and the thoracic duct empties at the left venous angle.'),
('05ab4a4a-3bcc-5ec8-89a4-c6f7ed38f3bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The second rib is typical and has which surface for attachment of serratus anterior?', '["Levator scapulae resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rough tubercle for serratus anterior (origin)","Serratus posterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splenius cervicis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rough tubercle for serratus anterior (origin)', 'A rough tubercle on the outer surface of the second rib marks the origin of serratus anterior''s first digitation. Levator scapulae attaches to the superior angle of the scapula, and splenius cervicis and serratus posterior act on the cervical and thoracic spine, respectively.'),
('ff6432bd-6130-5b8e-ab14-3e53f29172e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The costocartilage of the first rib articulates with:', '["None","Body of sternum","Xiphoid","Sternum (manubrium)","Clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum (manubrium)', 'The costal cartilage of the first rib joins the manubrium sterni at a primary cartilaginous joint (synchondrosis). Costal cartilages of ribs 2-7 articulate with the body of the sternum, and the xiphoid receives no rib.'),
('2588bc80-1542-5b37-8e16-ce367555e3f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The angle of Louis (sternal angle) is at the level of:', '["T2 resulting in decreased functional activity","C7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T4 (intervertebral disc)","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T6 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4 (intervertebral disc)', 'The sternal angle lies at the level of the T4-T5 intervertebral disc, the site where the second costal cartilage articulates. The jugular notch is at T2-T3 and the xiphisternal joint at T9, which helps explain why T4 marks the bifurcation of the trachea and the arch of the aorta.'),
('3a19e4c3-a8cd-5066-9b93-c965e9ef9363'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle is absent in some birds but in humans it is important for:', '["Suspending the upper limb from the trunk","Protecting the underlying vessels","None","All of the above","Transmitting force from arm to trunk"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The clavicle acts as the only bony strut connecting the upper limb to the axial skeleton, suspending the limb, transmitting weight from the arm to the trunk, and shielding the subclavian vessels and brachial plexus. It provides all of these functions, so the category of all the stated roles is correct.'),
('3b5c8a14-8c45-5869-9f86-cf5fe9b1a597'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The superior facet of the greater tuberosity gives insertion to:', '["Deltoid","Subscapularis","Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Supraspinatus"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinatus', 'The superior (uppermost) facet of the greater tuberosity receives the supraspinatus tendon, the muscle that initiates abduction. The middle and inferior facets belong to infraspinatus and teres minor, while subscapularis attaches to the lesser tuberosity.'),
('9ca8b108-7e2a-5035-a0e3-ded9045c0f35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The middle facet of the greater tuberosity gives insertion to:', '["Subscapularis","Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Infraspinatus', 'Infraspinatus inserts on the middle facet of the greater tuberosity and laterally rotates the humerus. Supraspinatus takes the superior facet, teres minor the inferior facet, and subscapularis the lesser tuberosity.'),
('a7209436-8035-52f4-a3be-21a5c37b7bff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The inferior facet of the greater tuberosity gives insertion to:', '["Infraspinatus","Teres minor","Subscapularis","Supraspinatus","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Teres minor', 'Teres minor, the lowest of the rotator cuff muscles, attaches to the inferior facet of the greater tuberosity and laterally rotates the humerus. Supraspinatus and infraspinatus occupy the superior and middle facets, and subscapularis inserts on the lesser tuberosity.'),
('93660cf2-a21f-5898-9b68-840c9b9a20bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lesser tuberosity of the humerus gives insertion to:', '["Supraspinatus","Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapularis', 'Subscapularis, the only rotator cuff muscle crossing anteriorly, inserts on the lesser tuberosity and medially rotates the humerus. Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor attach to the three facets of the greater tuberosity.'),
('cb865396-1260-5fc8-a863-fa6f288d2161'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial lip of the intertubercular groove gives attachment to:', '["Latissimus dorsi","Teres major","Deltoid","Coracobrachialis","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Teres major', 'Teres major attaches to the medial lip of the intertubercular groove, adducting and medially rotating the humerus. The lateral lip receives pectoralis major, and the floor of the groove receives latissimus dorsi; the biceps tendon and its sheath run within the groove.'),
('a07369f6-0413-5114-82e0-84f1db0a6a24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral lip of the intertubercular groove gives attachment to:', '["Latissimus dorsi","Teres major","Subscapularis","Deltoid","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis major', 'The lateral lip of the intertubercular groove receives the insertion of pectoralis major, a powerful adductor and medial rotator of the arm. Teres major occupies the medial lip and latissimus dorsi the floor of the groove.'),
('6d581a48-2dbb-5c88-9dde-cfcfeac5b6b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The floor of the intertubercular groove gives attachment to:', '["Teres major","Latissimus dorsi","Subscapularis","Pectoralis major","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'Latissimus dorsi inserts on the floor (crest) of the intertubercular groove, producing adduction, extension, and medial rotation of the humerus. Teres major attaches to the medial lip and pectoralis major to the lateral lip.'),
('caf8f744-518d-507f-87be-af4e3206f955'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The deltoid tuberosity is located on the:', '["Medial surface resulting in decreased functional activity","Proximal end accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior surface mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral surface of the midshaft of the humerus","Posterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral surface of the midshaft of the humerus', 'The deltoid tuberosity is a rough elevation on the lateral aspect of the midshaft of the humerus where the deltoid inserts. The deltoid''s large insertion here enables abduction of the arm, while the medial and posterior surfaces carry the attachments of other arm muscles.'),
('47f6d398-b860-5dc3-a333-6e180fb37c31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial groove (spiral groove) separates which muscle attachments?', '["Triceps and anconeus","Brachialis and brachioradialis","None","Lateral and medial heads of triceps (origin)","Deltoid and brachialis"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and medial heads of triceps (origin)', 'The radial (spiral) groove spirals around the humeral shaft and separates the origins of the lateral and medial heads of triceps brachii. The radial nerve and profunda brachii artery travel through this groove between the two heads.'),
('03c5a2b3-53f9-5951-982d-1091016db70d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trochlea of the humerus has a shape that is:', '["Triangular","Ball-shaped","Spool-shaped","Round","providing a stable hinge"]'::jsonb, 'Spool-shaped', 'The trochlea is spool-shaped with a central depression and prominent medial and lateral lips that grip the semilunar notch of the ulna, creating a stable hinge. This shape channels flexion and extension at the elbow while resisting sideways displacement.'),
('df566ea0-8146-57c1-a61c-0bc2e29f8258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The capitulum of the humerus is:', '["Flat","Triangular","Concave","None","Spherical and convex"]'::jsonb, 'Spherical and convex', 'The capitulum is a spherical, convex knob on the lateral side of the distal humerus that articulates with the concave surface of the radial head. Its ball-and-socket-like contour permits both flexion-extension and rotation of the radius at the elbow.'),
('219e2762-c10a-5aa6-9c8d-2c48a88a465b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epicondyle of the humerus is more prominent than the lateral because:', '["It is the site of elbow joint resulting in decreased functional activity","It provides attachment for the powerful forearm flexors","None mediated by specific receptor activation","It is a sesamoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It bears body weight leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It provides attachment for the powerful forearm flexors', 'The medial epicondyle is larger and more prominent because it gives origin to the common flexor tendon and the flexor-pronator muscle group of the forearm. The lateral epicondyle, which bears the common extensor origin, is correspondingly smaller.'),
('96125760-95cd-5983-840e-41f0662c1159'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral epicondyle is smaller and gives attachment to:', '["Common extensor origin","None","Brachioradialis","Common flexor origin","Radial collateral ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Common extensor origin', 'The smaller lateral epicondyle gives origin to the common extensor tendon shared by the extensor muscles of the wrist and fingers. The common flexor origin arises from the larger medial epicondyle, and brachioradialis arises higher up from the lateral supracondylar ridge.'),
('e0ef5d4a-212a-5be6-a391-53d4ccbceca2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The olecranon fossa of the humerus receives the olecranon during:', '["Forearm supination","Full elbow flexion","Forearm pronation","Full elbow extension","Neutral position"]'::jsonb, 'Full elbow extension', 'The olecranon fossa on the posterior aspect of the humerus accommodates the olecranon process of the ulna when the elbow is fully extended. During full flexion the coronoid process and radial head are received by the coronoid and radial fossae anteriorly.'),
('a9bc102a-f8fa-5937-92f8-59c8ec50f08a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coronoid fossa of the humerus receives the coronoid process during:', '["Forearm supination","Full elbow extension","Neutral position","Forearm pronation","Full elbow flexion"]'::jsonb, 'Full elbow flexion', 'The coronoid fossa on the anterior surface of the humerus receives the coronoid process of the ulna during full elbow flexion. In contrast, the olecranon fossa posteriorly engages the olecranon during full extension.'),
('ffc1efaf-c7d8-5bde-aa00-dfd162aa6f6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial fossa of the humerus receives the radial head during:', '["Neutral position","Forearm supination","Forearm pronation","Full elbow extension","Full elbow flexion"]'::jsonb, 'Full elbow flexion', 'The radial fossa lies just above the capitulum and receives the anterior margin of the radial head during full elbow flexion. Pronation and supination do not move the radial head into the fossa, and extension withdraws it.'),
('511598ac-2054-5a1e-ab14-ce70a3131dd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial neck is the narrow region between:', '["The head of the radius and the radial tuberosity","The radial tuberosity and the shaft","The head and the shaft","None","The distal end and the shaft"]'::jsonb, 'The head of the radius and the radial tuberosity', 'The radial neck is the constricted segment connecting the radial head above to the radial tuberosity and shaft below. It is a common site for proximal radial fractures, which often follow a fall on the outstretched hand.'),
('b6017c5a-a7cd-5446-97a5-4ada87e82522'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial tuberosity faces:', '["Laterally resulting in decreased functional activity","Posteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medially (for biceps insertion)"]'::jsonb, 'Medially (for biceps insertion)', 'The radial tuberosity projects medially from the proximal radius and receives the insertion of the biceps tendon. The medial projection explains why biceps supinates the forearm: contraction rotates the radius and the tuberosity toward the ulna.'),
('43a31c52-f9bc-5143-bbb1-f9ff5604af6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial styloid process extends:', '["Posteriorly","Distally and laterally","Distally and medially","Anteriorly","Proximally"]'::jsonb, 'Distally and laterally', 'The radial styloid process projects distally and laterally from the lateral border of the lower end of the radius. This obliquity helps the radius transmit force from the hand to the forearm and explains why the radial styloid lies distal to the ulnar styloid.'),
('581913ba-8eac-529b-ab13-79597d9a7bc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar styloid process extends:', '["Medially","Anterolaterally","Proximally","Posteromedially (distal)","Posteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedially (distal)', 'The ulnar styloid process projects posteromedially and distally from the ulnar head, reaching less far than the radial styloid. It anchors the ulnar collateral ligament of the wrist, whereas the radial styloid lies more distal on the lateral side.'),
('e711fe9a-6248-52f3-847d-d178565b5258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial styloid process extends farther distally than the ulnar styloid process by about:', '["2 cm","0.5 cm","1 cm","3 cm","0.2 cm"]'::jsonb, '1 cm', 'The radial styloid projects roughly 1 cm farther distally than the ulnar styloid, which is why a fall is transmitted mainly through the radius. This distal offset also gives the wrist its obliquity and contributes to the normal wrist contour.'),
('0c3733a6-d105-5302-96a2-cc7730f0d765'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The carpal bones (proximal row) from lateral to medial are:', '["Scaphoid","pisiform","lunate","None of the above","triquetrum"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid', 'The proximal row from lateral to medial is scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, and pisiform, so the scaphoid is the first (most lateral) bone. The remaining options list other members of the row but only scaphoid is the leading lateral bone.'),
('974411c4-2ddf-5538-b97e-8028e8a77c6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The carpal bones (distal row) from lateral to medial are:', '["Trapezium","capitate","None of the above","trapezoid","hamate"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezium', 'The distal row from lateral to medial is trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate, so the trapezium is the most lateral bone. The other options are members of the same row, but only the trapezium occupies the lateral (radial) end.'),
('de2a2db7-ccac-5942-a661-91eb0a810198'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scaphoid bone is boat-shaped and has a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Tubercle (distal pole) that is palpable in the palm","Distal pole only mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximal pole only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both a and b leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tubercle (distal pole) that is palpable in the palm', 'The scaphoid carries a tubercle on its distal pole that can be felt at the base of the thenar eminence and gives attachment to the flexor retinaculum. The proximal pole, by contrast, lies deeply within the wrist and is largely covered by the lunate.'),
('7adb7f07-fbcc-582f-98ef-acd0476a0d54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scaphoid waist is the common site of fracture because:', '["It has blood supply","It has the best blood supply","None","It is the narrowest part of the bone","It is the widest part"]'::jsonb, 'It is the narrowest part of the bone', 'The scaphoid waist is the narrowest portion of the bone and therefore the weakest point where bending stresses concentrate, making it the classic fracture site in falls on the outstretched hand. Moreover, most blood enters distally, so waist fractures risk proximal pole avascular necrosis.'),
('77cac811-1277-50eb-bf51-d0705ab79b22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lunate bone is moon-shaped and its name means:', '["Boat-shaped resulting in decreased functional activity","Triangular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Moon-shaped (Latin: luna = moon)","Pyramidal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Moon-shaped (Latin: luna = moon)', 'The lunate takes its name from the Latin luna (moon) because its crescentic, moon-like shape. It sits between the scaphoid and triquetrum in the proximal row and is the bone most liable to dislocate on the lunate.'),
('f816deec-91b9-53c1-ab08-ad4c353db381'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The triquetrum is:', '["Round resulting in decreased functional activity","Moon-shaped accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Boat-shaped mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pyramidal in shape (three-cornered)"]'::jsonb, 'Pyramidal in shape (three-cornered)', 'The triquetrum is pyramidal with three corners, hence its name meaning three-cornered. It is the second-most-fractured carpal bone and articulates with the lunate medially and the pisiform anteriorly.'),
('d34395c3-3f4c-5930-a694-dc9e9162a31d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The pisiform is:', '["Round resulting in decreased functional activity","Boat-shaped accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Triangular mediated by specific receptor activation","Pea-shaped (Latin: pisum = pea)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pea-shaped (Latin: pisum = pea)', 'The pisiform, the smallest carpal bone, is pea-shaped as its name suggests (pisum = pea). It is a sesamoid bone embedded in the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon and is palpable at the medial side of the proximal wrist crease.'),
('65b07a1a-57f7-56f3-938b-64784a4a84f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezium is:', '["Round resulting in decreased functional activity","Boat-shaped accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Irregularly shaped with a saddle facet for the 1st metacarpal","Wedge-shaped without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irregularly shaped with a saddle facet for the 1st metacarpal', 'The trapezium is irregularly shaped and bears a saddle facet for the base of the first metacarpal, forming the carpometacarpal joint of the thumb. This saddle joint permits the wide circumduction and opposition that give the thumb its versatile mobility.'),
('06dfdf07-e97b-5ebf-b279-2fc992f65122'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezoid is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Triangular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Round mediated by specific receptor activation","Wedge-shaped (like a four-sided wedge)","Square leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wedge-shaped (like a four-sided wedge)', 'The trapezoid is a four-sided bone shaped like a wedge, narrow palmar to dorsal, and is the smallest bone of the distal row. It articulates with the second metacarpal and is keyed into the carpal arch, adding stability to the midcarpal region.'),
('2e9b25ba-8f1d-50d1-b701-6e6f16fd3bc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The capitate is:', '["None","None of the above","The smallest carpal bone","The only carpal with articulations","The largest carpal bone with a rounded head"]'::jsonb, 'The largest carpal bone with a rounded head', 'The capitate is the largest and most centrally placed carpal bone, with a rounded head that fits into the concavity of the lunate. It articulates with multiple carpals and with the third metacarpal, forming the pivotal keystone of the carpus.'),
('ae01de2f-af85-55d3-86b6-31abf47a1f92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The hamate is:', '["Boat-shaped resulting in decreased functional activity","Wedge-shaped with a prominent hook (hamulus)","Pyramidal mediated by specific receptor activation","Round without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wedge-shaped with a prominent hook (hamulus)', 'The hamate is a wedge-shaped bone of the distal row with a distinctive hook (hamulus) projecting from its palmar surface. The hook anchors the flexor retinaculum and gives origin to the hypothenar muscles, and it guards the ulnar artery and nerve crossing the wrist.'),
('ca83cfc0-a2f8-5028-a040-103531fd0a47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The hook of hamate (hamulus) gives attachment to:', '["Palmar aponeurosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensor retinaculum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thenar muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexor retinaculum (to its tip) and hypothenar muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor retinaculum (to its tip) and hypothenar muscles', 'The hamulus gives attachment to the flexor retinaculum at its tip and to the hypothenar muscles (flexor digiti minimi brevis and opponents digiti minimi) along its side. The flexor retinaculum also attaches to the scaphoid tubercle and ridge of the trapezium, forming the carpal tunnel.'),
('b29cfda5-82aa-5195-af9b-da2951dd196d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The metacarpal heads form the:', '["None","Base of fingers","Carpal tunnel","Wrist joint","Knuckles of the hand"]'::jsonb, 'Knuckles of the hand', 'The rounded heads of the metacarpals articulate with the proximal phalanges and are felt dorsally as the knuckles when the fist is clenched. The bases, not heads, form the carpometacarpal joints at the wrist region.'),
('8f31cd22-2be1-5a09-b00f-fdf763a5ba39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The articular surface of the metacarpal head extends:', '["Farther onto the palmar than the dorsal surface","Only on the palmar surface","Farther on the dorsal surface","Only on the dorsal surface","Equally on both surfaces"]'::jsonb, 'Farther onto the palmar than the dorsal surface', 'The articular surface of the metacarpal head extends much farther on the palmar aspect than dorsally, reflecting the greater degree of flexion available at the metacarpophalangeal joints. Clenching the fist therefore exposes a greater arc of cartilage on the palmar side of each knuckle.'),
('ad162c99-2b02-5634-9ef1-9eec400bb8e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The interphalangeal joints are which type of joint?', '["Plane","Hinge (ginglymus)","Ball and socket","Pivot","Saddle"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge (ginglymus)', 'The proximal and distal interphalangeal joints are uniaxial hinge (ginglymus) joints that permit only flexion and extension. The interphalangeal collateral ligaments and volar plates reinforce their stability during grasping.'),
('d84d49a4-2247-50c5-807d-b3bb7cc970a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The carpal bones ossify in a predictable order. The capitate ossifies first at:', '["1 year","1-3 months of age","2 years","Birth","6 months"]'::jsonb, '1-3 months of age', 'The capitate is the first carpal bone to ossify, typically appearing between 1 and 3 months of age in the center of the carpus. Its early ossification is a useful milestone for assessing bone age in infants.'),
('9e3a0b09-fd46-5733-bc3f-fd1ba5a91ee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The second carpal bone to ossify is:', '["Lunate","Triquetrum","Scaphoid","Pisiform","Hamate (at 2-4 months)"]'::jsonb, 'Hamate (at 2-4 months)', 'The hamate is the second carpal bone to ossify, appearing between 2 and 4 months of age shortly after the capitate. The remaining carpal bones follow in a roughly predictable sequence throughout childhood, with the pisiform appearing last.'),
('3f93e3de-672a-5a46-a826-345050a4e8e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scaphoid ossifies at:', '["12-14 years","About 5-6 years","8-10 years","1-2 years","3-6 months"]'::jsonb, 'About 5-6 years', 'The scaphoid begins to ossify at about 5-6 years of age, relatively late among the carpal bones. Because it ossifies so late, an x-ray taken during a pediatric wrist injury before this age may fail to show the scaphoid clearly.'),
('8913fffd-5608-5adc-bb34-d4a3df663676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The pisiform ossifies at:', '["7-9 years resulting in decreased functional activity","3-6 months accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1-2 years mediated by specific receptor activation","5-7 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 10-12 years (last carpal to ossify)"]'::jsonb, 'About 10-12 years (last carpal to ossify)', 'The pisiform is the last carpal bone to ossify, typically appearing at about 10-12 years of age. As a sesamoid bone developing within the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon, it ossifies long after the other carpals, at which point all eight carpal centers are visible.'),
('a8534e34-e9f4-582e-a3ad-5eebbeed3a30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The tubercle of the scaphoid is palpable:', '["At the anatomical snuffbox resulting in decreased functional activity","At the palm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the proximal wrist crease (base of thenar eminence)","At the dorsum of the hand without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the ulnar side of the wrist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the proximal wrist crease (base of thenar eminence)', 'The scaphoid tubercle is easily felt at the lateral end of the proximal wrist crease, at the base of the thenar eminence. When the wrist is radially deviated, the waist of the scaphoid also becomes palpable in the floor of the anatomical snuffbox.'),
('3e1bbe6d-2562-50e1-a722-6cf8f0c9b0de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The hook of hamate is palpable:', '["At the anatomical snuffbox resulting in decreased functional activity","At the radial side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the wrist crease mediated by specific receptor activation","1 cm proximal to the pisiform without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 cm distal and lateral to the pisiform (in the palm)"]'::jsonb, '1 cm distal and lateral to the pisiform (in the palm)', 'The hook of hamate lies deep and slightly distal to the pisiform, so it is felt in the proximal palm about 1 cm distal and lateral to that bone. The anatomical snuffbox and radial side are scaphoid/trapezium landmarks, not the hamate hook.'),
('8d7e4c90-9062-5ed9-a456-38b9f5bac148'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezium is palpable:', '["At the palm resulting in decreased functional activity","At the anatomical snuffbox accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the ulnar side of the wrist mediated by specific receptor activation","At the dorsum of the wrist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the base of the thenar eminence (distal to scaphoid tubercle)"]'::jsonb, 'At the base of the thenar eminence (distal to scaphoid tubercle)', 'The trapezium lies at the radial wrist, distal to the scaphoid tubercle, at the base of the thenar eminence. Other sites listed are on the ulnar side or dorsum and do not correspond to the trapezium.'),
('f0f05bcf-0cba-5100-83ca-771e26ff84e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The first costal cartilage attaches to which part of the sternum?', '["None","Manubrium (superior part)","Xiphoid process","Clavicular notch","Body of sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium (superior part)', 'The first costal cartilage articulates with the upper lateral border of the manubrium. The second costal cartilage attaches at the manubriosternal junction, and the xiphoid and body receive other costal attachments.'),
('9cb281ed-8d25-5e01-9acb-586a938797be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The second costal cartilage attaches at which level of the sternum?', '["Body of sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","Junction of body and xiphoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Top of manubrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Sternal angle (Angle of Louis - junction of manubrium and body)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sternal angle (Angle of Louis - junction of manubrium and body)', 'The second costal cartilage articulates at the sternal angle, the manubriosternal junction, which is the key surface landmark for counting ribs. The body, manubrium, and xiphoid levels correspond to other costal cartilages.'),
('089a7fc8-1de0-53c9-b53f-13337749a82d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The third to seventh costal cartilages articulate with:', '["Clavicle","Body of sternum","Manubrium","Xiphoid process","None"]'::jsonb, 'Body of sternum', 'The third through seventh costal cartilages attach to the lateral border of the body of the sternum. The first attaches to the manubrium, the second at the sternal angle, and the seventh with the xiphoid at the T9 level.'),
('466f712d-1a36-5096-b393-b23f58c54246'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The eighth, ninth and tenth costal cartilages articulate:', '["Directly with the sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","With the xiphoid process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","With the costal cartilage above (not directly to sternum)","With the manubrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","With the vertebra above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'With the costal cartilage above (not directly to sternum)', 'The eighth, ninth, and tenth costal cartilages form the false ribs and each articulates with the costal cartilage immediately above it rather than directly with the sternum. This indirect attachment is why they are not true sternal ribs.'),
('97e3eb95-a84f-56fb-bd00-a4dd077ea548'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The eleventh and twelfth ribs are:', '["Vertebral ribs resulting in decreased functional activity","False ribs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","True ribs mediated by specific receptor activation","Floating ribs (no anterior attachment)","Sternal ribs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Floating ribs (no anterior attachment)', 'The eleventh and twelfth ribs are floating (vertebral) ribs because their costal cartilages end in the abdominal wall without any anterior attachment. True ribs reach the sternum and false ribs attach indirectly through costal cartilage.'),
('a8a944cc-4c51-5b39-9aab-79b2c2d3e12a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The costovertebral joint is between:', '["The rib and the cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","The rib and the sternum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The rib head and the vertebral body (and intervertebral disc)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rib neck and the transverse process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rib head and the vertebral body (and intervertebral disc)', 'The costovertebral joint is the articulation of the rib head with the bodies of two adjacent vertebrae and the intervening intervertebral disc. The rib tubercle with the transverse process separately forms the costotransverse joint.'),
('cbaddf9a-fa0e-56eb-9bea-f7d6d15030de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The costotransverse joint is between:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The rib and the costal cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The rib tubercle and the transverse process of the vertebra","The rib and the sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rib head and the vertebral body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rib tubercle and the transverse process of the vertebra', 'The costotransverse joint is the articulation of the rib tubercle with the transverse process of its corresponding vertebra. The rib head with the vertebral body forms the separate costovertebral joint.'),
('829bac7c-eff3-58e8-a737-780adca39e23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The xiphisternal joint is at the level of:', '["T12","T4","L1","T7","T9 vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'T9 vertebra', 'The xiphisternal joint lies at the level of the T9 vertebra, inferior to the sternal angle. This landmark is lower than the T4 level of the sternal angle.'),
('5cb040d2-57d0-5285-bd26-996ec7b0829d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The jugular notch (suprasternal notch) is at the level of:', '["T10","T6","T8","T2 vertebra","T4"]'::jsonb, 'T2 vertebra', 'The jugular notch lies at the upper border of the manubrium, level with the T2 vertebra, and is the surface landmark for the disc space between T2 and T3. The sternal angle and xiphisternal joint mark lower levels.'),
('3dc59b21-8880-524c-94f9-4c487caba9d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle is the first bone to ossify and begins ossification at:', '["6th month resulting in decreased functional activity","3rd month accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","8th week mediated by specific receptor activation","5th week of intrauterine life","At birth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5th week of intrauterine life', 'The clavicle is the first bone in the body to ossify, beginning around the 5th week of intrauterine life. It develops largely in membrane and was formerly believed to start by late ossification only during the second trimester.'),
('a1bbca17-3dbc-5b35-be3b-333e25e66f30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle is one of the last bones to fuse its epiphysis (medial end). When does the medial epiphysis fuse?', '["30 years","16-18 years","20-22 years","About 25 years of age","Puberty"]'::jsonb, 'About 25 years of age', 'The secondary center for the medial (sternal) end of the clavicle appears at about 16-18 years and fuses with the shaft around 25 years of age, the latest epiphyseal fusion in the body. Other listed ages are either too early or beyond this range for its fusion.'),
('180e56f2-22bd-5d78-bc89-c6f7318884ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The clavicle ossifies in membrane. What is unusual about its ossification?', '["It has only intramembranous ossification","It only has endochondral ossification","It ossifies from multiple centers","It has both intramembranous and endochondral ossification","None"]'::jsonb, 'It has both intramembranous and endochondral ossification', 'The clavicle is unique in that it develops by both intramembranous ossification of its body and later endochondral ossification of its cartilaginous ends. It does not develop solely by one mechanism.'),
('424cde3b-9209-5a4a-850e-cf83422641c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The proximal humeral epiphysis appears at:', '["greater tuberosity (3 years)","6 months to 2 years (head)","10 years","lesser tuberosity (5 years)","Birth"]'::jsonb, '6 months to 2 years (head)', 'The center for the humeral head appears between 6 months and 2 years, followed by the greater tuberosity (3 years) and lesser tuberosity (5 years), which fuse into one proximal epiphysis by age 7. The head center therefore appears first.'),
('bfe28e94-9036-5d3c-8097-f3cdaeec1f5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epicondyle of the humerus ossifies from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A separate center appearing at 5-7 years (fuses at 18-20 years)","The shaft center mediated by specific receptor activation","The lateral epicondyle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The main distal epiphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A separate center appearing at 5-7 years (fuses at 18-20 years)', 'The medial epicondyle ossifies from its own separate center that appears at 5-7 years and fuses with the shaft at 18-20 years. It does not derive from the main distal humeral epiphysis.'),
('2a66e156-1628-508c-8707-51759d38fb05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial head ossification center appears at:', '["Birth","15 years","10-12 years","5-7 years of age","2-3 years"]'::jsonb, '5-7 years of age', 'The ossification center for the radial head appears at about 5-7 years of age, later than the elbow''s main centers. This timing is important when assessing epiphyseal injuries in growing children.'),
('3984d33f-e6f9-5539-b249-efac344b0e07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The olecranon ossification center appears at:', '["3-5 years","10-12 years of age","5-7 years","Birth","15 years"]'::jsonb, '10-12 years of age', 'The secondary center for the olecranon appears at 10-12 years and fuses with the ulnar shaft around 16-17 years. The radial head center appears earlier, at 5-7 years.'),
('dc95ffd6-dcee-5474-b3ec-35b5ae8c9132'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The carpal bones ossify after birth. The first to ossify is:', '["Triquetrum (3-5 years)","Capitate (1-3 months)","Lunate (2-4 years)","Hamate (2-4 months)","Scaphoid (5-6 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Capitate (1-3 months)', 'The capitate is the first carpal bone to ossify, appearing at 1-3 months of age, followed closely by the hamate. The scaphoid, lunate, and triquetrum ossify much later in childhood.'),
('659ae787-2e55-50fc-815d-7e6a6e67057c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The general rule is that ossification of the upper limb bones progresses:', '["None","Simultaneously","From proximal to distal","From distal to proximal","Randomly"]'::jsonb, 'From proximal to distal', 'Ossification centers of the upper limb generally appear in a proximal-to-distal sequence, from the clavicle and humerus toward the hand. The carpal bones and phalanges are the last to begin ossification.'),
('6d4e2cb5-bf96-5184-9f6e-b8364593a900'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scapula primary ossification center appears at:', '["6th month resulting in decreased functional activity","8th week of IUL (in the neck)","Birth mediated by specific receptor activation","12th week without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5th week leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '8th week of IUL (in the neck)', 'The primary ossification center of the scapula appears at the 8th week of intrauterine life in its neck region. Secondary centers for the coracoid, acromion, glenoid, and borders appear later.'),
('f36fb6b2-7519-5bbf-9d84-e232f1bb80bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coracoid process ossifies from:', '["A separate secondary center appearing at 1 year of age","The acromion center accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The primary scapular center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The glenoid center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A separate secondary center appearing at 1 year of age', 'The coracoid process develops from its own secondary center that appears around 1 year of age and fuses by the later teens. It is not part of the primary scapular center or the acromion.'),
('dd4a1e9e-b380-5989-9e5b-87527ab41c54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The glenoid cavity has how many ossification centers?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 (subcoracoid center at 10 years and a rim center at puberty)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (subcoracoid center at 10 years and a rim center at puberty)', 'The glenoid cavity ossifies from two secondary centers: a subcoracoid center appearing at about 10 years and a rim center at puberty. These supplement the primary scapular ossification.'),
('662b1dad-6e17-595a-a90b-983930d410d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The largest sesamoid bone in the upper limb is:', '["The triquetrum","The hamate","The pisiform","The lunate","The scaphoid"]'::jsonb, 'The pisiform', 'The pisiform is a sesamoid bone of the upper limb, developed in the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris, and it is the largest sesamoid in the limb. The hamate, lunate, triquetrum, and scaphoid are true carpal bones.'),
('314513e5-8bf3-5506-ad54-01a81bcd2c3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Sesamoid bones in the hand are frequently found at:', '["The wrist resulting in decreased functional activity","The distal phalanx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The DIP joints mediated by specific receptor activation","The MCP joint of the thumb (2 sesamoids)","The PIP joints leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The MCP joint of the thumb (2 sesamoids)', 'Sesamoid bones are constant at the thumb metacarpophalangeal joint, where two sesamoids reinforce the volar plate. Other finger joints lack this constant arrangement.'),
('dccae2bd-3121-5022-907f-5b4c69d54f26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial sesamoid at the thumb MCP joint is in the tendon of:', '["Extensor pollicis brevis","Flexor pollicis longus","Adductor pollicis","Flexor pollicis brevis","Abductor pollicis brevis"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor pollicis brevis', 'The radial (medial) sesamoid at the thumb MCP joint lies within the tendon of the flexor pollicis brevis. The ulnar (lateral) sesamoid lies in the adductor pollicis tendon.'),
('5c2b396f-c49b-5659-ac93-a28fd0cb3670'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar sesamoid at the thumb MCP joint is in which tendon?', '["Abductor pollicis brevis","Opponens pollicis","Flexor pollicis brevis","Flexor pollicis longus","Adductor pollicis"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor pollicis', 'The ulnar (lateral) sesamoid of the thumb metacarpophalangeal joint is developed in the tendon of adductor pollicis. The radial sesamoid, by contrast, lies in the flexor pollicis brevis tendon.'),
('b5507f8a-aa03-52fc-b906-73e4ad226dad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The fibrocartilaginous articular disc at the wrist is called:', '["Acetabular labrum resulting in decreased functional activity","Glenoid labrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Menisci mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC)"]'::jsonb, 'Triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC)', 'The wrist''s fibrocartilaginous disc belongs to the triangular fibrocartilage complex (TFCC), which links the radius, ulna, and ulnar carpus. Acetabular and glenoid labra belong to the hip and shoulder.'),
('11e23c4b-c734-54ec-ad1f-a8dbc894c643'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The TFCC includes:', '["Articular disc only","Articular disc","Dorsal and volar radioulnar ligaments","Ulnocarpal ligaments","Ulnar collateral ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Articular disc', 'The central triangular fibrocartilage disc is the principal structure of the TFCC, supplemented by the dorsal and volar radioulnar, ulnocarpal, and ulnar collateral ligaments. ''Articular disc only'' is wrong because the TFCC contains several ligaments.'),
('c612865a-1942-5723-aa94-edefd35cb5f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The function of the TFCC is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Stabilize the radiocarpal joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Stabilize the distal radioulnar joint and transmit axial load from the ulnar carpus to the ulna","Stabilize the midcarpal joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilize the distal radioulnar joint and transmit axial load from the ulnar carpus to the ulna', 'The TFCC stabilizes the distal radioulnar joint and transmits axial load from the ulnar side of the carpus to the distal ulna. The radiocarpal and midcarpal joints are stabilized by other ligament complexes.'),
('294ce2c4-de71-56ca-aad0-ce07cd34e6b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar variance describes:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The relative length of the ulnar head compared to the radius at the wrist joint","The width of the wrist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The length of the forearm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The relative length of the ulnar head compared to the radius at the wrist joint', 'Ulnar variance is the relative length of the ulnar head compared with the distal radius at the wrist. It is measured on a PA wrist radiograph and is neither wrist width nor whole forearm length.'),
('c877ee63-2098-57f9-9921-df87ba741338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Positive ulnar variance (ulna longer than radius) is associated with:', '["None","TFCC tears","Ulnar impaction syndrome","Kienbock''s disease (lunatomalacia)","Carpal instability"]'::jsonb, 'Ulnar impaction syndrome', 'When the ulna is longer than the radius (positive variance), the ulnar head impinges against the lunate and triquetrum, producing ulnar impaction syndrome with TFCC wear. Negative variance, in contrast, predisposes to Kienbock''s disease.'),
('ab717a13-0297-5ec1-88d2-d5edb335e26b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Negative ulnar variance (ulna shorter than radius) is associated with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Ganglion cysts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kienbock''s disease (avascular necrosis of lunate)","Ulnar impaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carpal tunnel syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kienbock''s disease (avascular necrosis of lunate)', 'A short ulna (negative variance) increases load on the lunate at the radius, predisposing it to avascular necrosis, Kienbock''s disease. Positive variance, by contrast, leads to ulnar impaction syndrome.'),
('4c393214-4a50-583d-87be-39bb6396454a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The typical carrying angle at the elbow is:', '["20-30 degrees","5-15 degrees valgus","None","0-5 degrees","15-20 degrees"]'::jsonb, '5-15 degrees valgus', 'The normal carrying angle at the elbow is 5-15 degrees of valgus in the extended arm, slightly larger in women. Angles above this range suggest pathological cubitus valgus deformities.'),
('d0d0a709-17cc-59b1-81de-f419824aea73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The bicipital aponeurosis (lacertus fibrosus) is an extension from the:', '["Triceps tendon","Biceps brachii tendon (medial side)","Brachioradialis tendon","Brachialis tendon","None"]'::jsonb, 'Biceps brachii tendon (medial side)', 'The bicipital aponeurosis is a flattened expansion given off from the medial side of the biceps brachii tendon as it reaches the cubital fossa. It blends with the deep fascia of the forearm.'),
('a3eb5242-92eb-55db-8a3c-d7a812368fb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The bicipital aponeurosis protects which structure in the cubital fossa?', '["Ulnar nerve","Median cubital vein","Brachial artery (median nerve)","Biceps tendon","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial artery (median nerve)', 'The bicipital aponeurosis overlies and protects the brachial artery and, in part, the median nerve in the cubital fossa. It also shields the vein during venipuncture, but the ulnar and radial nerves lie elsewhere.'),
('140cfe55-ccc1-54a5-b61e-e05efeb26317'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The interosseous membrane of the forearm is a fibrous sheet that:', '["Allows force transfer","All of the above","Separates the flexor and extensor compartments","Connects the radius and ulna","Connects the radius and ulna and prevents independent rotation"]'::jsonb, 'Connects the radius and ulna', 'The interosseous membrane is a strong fibrous sheet binding the radius and ulna and providing a broad muscle attachment surface. It stabilizes the two bones but does allow rotation at the proximal and distal radioulnar joints.'),
('94e1a576-0c05-55dc-8796-485a4d6120c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The fibers of the interosseous membrane run:', '["Obliquely upwards and medially resulting in decreased functional activity","Horizontally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertically mediated by specific receptor activation","Obliquely downwards and medially from radius to ulna","Transversely leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downwards and medially from radius to ulna', 'The principal fibers of the interosseous membrane run obliquely downwards and medially from the radius to the ulna. This oblique arrangement transmits forces from the radius to the ulna during hand loading.'),
('054d2b22-a548-55ef-839d-3f9ff1904758'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The palmar carpal ligament is a thickening of:', '["None","Deep fascia of the forearm (anterior)","Extensor retinaculum","Palmar aponeurosis","Flexor retinaculum"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia of the forearm (anterior)', 'The palmar carpal ligament is the thickened anterior deep fascia of the distal forearm and lies superficial to the flexor retinaculum. The flexor retinaculum and palmar aponeurosis are separate structures.'),
('60586deb-0243-52e7-886a-556d2d9095d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The flexor retinaculum attaches medially to:', '["To the metacarpals only","To the radius and ulna","Laterally to the scaphoid and trapezium","To the carpal bones only","Medially to the pisiform and hamate"]'::jsonb, 'Medially to the pisiform and hamate', 'On its medial side the flexor retinaculum attaches to the pisiform and the hook of the hamate, while laterally it attaches to the scaphoid tubercle and trapezium. These attachments roof the carpal tunnel over the carpal bones.'),
('25b03f62-16f6-5c05-b9e1-f2b16967822d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The flexor retinaculum is continuous proximally with:', '["Superficial fascia","Palmar carpal ligament","None","Palmar aponeurosis","Extensor retinaculum"]'::jsonb, 'Palmar carpal ligament', 'Proximally the flexor retinaculum is continuous with the palmar carpal ligament, which is a thickening of the deep fascia of the forearm. Distally it merges with the palmar aponeurosis.'),
('de06b0c3-bede-59bc-bd72-0f3480e278a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The flexor retinaculum is continuous distally with:', '["None","Palmar carpal ligament","Extensor retinaculum","Superficial fascia","Palmar aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Palmar aponeurosis', 'Distally the flexor retinaculum blends with the palmar aponeurosis in the palm. Its proximal continuation is instead the palmar carpal ligament.'),
('7c88e862-68eb-5f62-bdd0-871cb4a4164e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The cubital tunnel (ulnar tunnel at elbow) is bounded medially by:', '["The olecranon","The medial epicondyle","The lateral epicondyle and the anconeus","The humeroulnar arch of FCU","The radius and supinator"]'::jsonb, 'The medial epicondyle', 'The cubital tunnel lies between the medial epicondyle and the olecranon, roofed by Osborne''s retinaculum and floored by the medial collateral ligament. The medial epicondyle forms its medial wall and is the answer to the medial boundary.'),
('b63641e3-4d98-569f-8941-d199f4757aef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Guyon''s canal (ulnar canal at wrist) is bounded medially by:', '["Pisiform (medial)","Palmar carpal ligament (roof)","Hook of hamate (lateral)","Flexor retinaculum (floor)","Radius and ulna"]'::jsonb, 'Pisiform (medial)', 'Guyon''s canal is bounded medially by the pisiform, laterally by the hook of hamate, roofed by the palmar carpal ligament, and floored by the flexor retinaculum. The pisiform thus forms the medial boundary.'),
('c3bc082c-baf2-5b41-926e-6de8f68659fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The roof of the carpal tunnel is formed by:', '["Floor = carpal bones","Roof = extensor retinaculum","Palmar carpal ligament","None of the above","Roof = flexor retinaculum"]'::jsonb, 'Roof = flexor retinaculum', 'The flexor retinaculum roofs the carpal tunnel, while the carpal bones form the floor. The extensor retinaculum belongs to the dorsum and the palmar carpal ligament is proximal and superficial.'),
('248bafc2-831f-5238-87d9-361286607166'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which of the following is a content of the carpal tunnel?', '["Four FDP tendons","One FPL tendon (total 10 structures)","Four FDS tendons","Flexor carpi ulnaris tendon","Median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'The carpal tunnel contains the four FDP and four FDS tendons, the FPL tendon, and the median nerve, making ten structures. The flexor carpi ulnaris tendon lies superficial to the retinaculum outside the tunnel.'),
('363f270f-5f15-5308-b78d-c9ff2e59a293'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar nerve and artery pass:', '["Through the anatomical snuffbox resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial to the flexor retinaculum (in Guyon''s canal)","Deep to the flexor retinaculum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the carpal tunnel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial to the flexor retinaculum (in Guyon''s canal)', 'The ulnar nerve and artery cross superficial to the flexor retinaculum within Guyon''s canal at the wrist. They therefore do not share the carpal tunnel, whose contents include the median nerve and the flexor tendons.'),
('f5a10c5b-5d14-55a6-8e49-49db283732c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The flexor carpi radialis tendon passes:', '["Through the carpal tunnel resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through a separate compartment within the flexor retinaculum (attached to trapezium)","Through Guyon''s canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial to the retinaculum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through a separate compartment within the flexor retinaculum (attached to trapezium)', 'The flexor carpi radialis tendon runs in its own compartment within the flexor retinaculum, on the lateral side where the retinaculum is attached to the trapezium. It does not travel through the main carpal tunnel.'),
('42ae2bc7-ed2e-56de-ae82-a0453fd91369'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The palmar aponeurosis is the:', '["Superficial fascia","Flexor retinaculum","Extensor retinaculum","Deep fascia of the palm (central part)","Palmaris longus tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia of the palm (central part)', 'The palmar aponeurosis is the thickened central part of the deep fascia of the palm. The flexor and extensor retinacula, superficial fascia, and the palmaris longus tendon are distinct structures.'),
('a10fb8df-705d-59b8-b263-d9e3986b80bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The palmar aponeurosis is continuous proximally with:', '["Hypothenar fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","Flexor retinaculum and palmaris longus tendon","Extensor retinaculum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thenar fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor retinaculum and palmaris longus tendon', 'Proximally the palmar aponeurosis is continuous with the flexor retinaculum and receives the insertion of the palmaris longus tendon. The thenar and hypothenar fascia are its thinner peripheral continuations.'),
('5610f31c-6116-55b5-8c04-71cd94f0915f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The palmar aponeurosis divides into four slips that attach to:', '["The distal phalanges resulting in decreased functional activity","The metacarpals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The carpal bones mediated by specific receptor activation","The flexor tendon sheaths and the base of the proximal phalanges","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The flexor tendon sheaths and the base of the proximal phalanges', 'The four slips of the palmar aponeurosis pass into the fingers and attach to the fibrous flexor tendon sheaths and the bases of the proximal phalanges, anchoring the palmar fascia. They do not reach the distal phalanges, metacarpals, or carpal bones.'),
('c7ab05ec-088c-589c-908d-827e9dcf40b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The septa of the palm divide it into:', '["midpalmar","and hypothenar compartments","Thenar and hypothenar only","Central and peripheral compartments","Thenar"]'::jsonb, 'Thenar', 'The palmar septa partition the palm into the thenar, midpalmar (central), and hypothenar compartments; the lateral septum encloses the thenar muscles. The listed alternatives give only fragments of this compartmental arrangement.'),
('7f6c7de8-6cf3-55d4-9d8d-3f9d8c14c27b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The midpalmar septum extends from:', '["The carpals resulting in decreased functional activity","The thenar and hypothenar septa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The flexor retinaculum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The palmar aponeurosis to the 3rd metacarpal (oblique septum)"]'::jsonb, 'The palmar aponeurosis to the 3rd metacarpal (oblique septum)', 'The midpalmar (oblique) septum runs from the medial border of the palmar aponeurosis to the shaft of the third metacarpal, separating the thenar from the midpalmar compartment. It does not arise from the carpals, flexor retinaculum, or the adjacent septa.'),
('b41e5a0d-664a-5c8d-81d9-83ac578e4c15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The thenar septum extends from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The median nerve to the 1st metacarpal mediated by specific receptor activation","The 1st metacarpal to the 3rd metacarpal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lateral border of the palmar aponeurosis to the 1st metacarpal"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral border of the palmar aponeurosis to the 1st metacarpal', 'The lateral (thenar) septum passes from the lateral border of the palmar aponeurosis to the first metacarpal, roofing over the thenar compartment. The median nerve is not a bony attachment, and the other metacarpal connections are incorrect.'),
('b814492d-4a62-5a72-ba05-132c69dc36de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The hypothenar septum extends from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The 4th metacarpal to the 5th metacarpal mediated by specific receptor activation","The 5th metacarpal to the triquetrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The medial border of the palmar aponeurosis to the 5th metacarpal"]'::jsonb, 'The medial border of the palmar aponeurosis to the 5th metacarpal', 'The medial (hypothenar) septum extends from the medial border of the palmar aponeurosis to the fifth metacarpal, forming the hypothenar compartment. Connections between the fourth and fifth metacarpals or to the triquetrum do not define this septum.'),
('66678e21-8283-551b-b81f-118f84d99dc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The position of the scapula on the posterior thoracic wall extends from:', '["T1 to T4 resulting in decreased functional activity","T4 to T9 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T7 to T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","C7 to T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T2 to T7 spinsous processes"]'::jsonb, 'T2 to T7 spinsous processes', 'The scapula overlies the posterior thoracic wall from the level of the second to the seventh thoracic spinous processes. The other vertebral ranges do not match the normal surface position of the scapula.'),
('198aaabc-e578-59de-b286-d35acdc367ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial border of the scapula is approximately how many cm from the vertebral column?', '["1-2 cm","5-6 cm","7-8 cm","2-3 cm","10 cm"]'::jsonb, '5-6 cm', 'The medial border of the scapula lies roughly 5-6 cm lateral to the midline spinous processes, providing a useful surface landmark. The smaller distances given are too close to the vertebral column.'),
('f6378085-d28b-5bae-a48e-23ac79f2a718'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The spine of the scapula is at the level of:', '["T7 resulting in decreased functional activity","T5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T3 spinous process","T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T3 spinous process', 'The spine of the scapula lies level with the third thoracic spinous process, a standard landmark for counting vertebral levels. The other vertebral levels are too high or too low.'),
('07d38b47-ec5f-513b-b1c7-2df874ca5fc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The inferior angle of the scapula is at the level of:', '["T7 spinous process (or T8)","T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1 mediated by specific receptor activation","T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T7 spinous process (or T8)', 'The inferior angle of the scapula lies at about the seventh thoracic spinous process (T7-T8), a landmark used when counting ribs and thoracic levels. The lower and higher options do not correspond to this bony landmark.'),
('1ee23ef1-c174-52ff-a867-a69c1720f270'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The glenoid cavity is directed:', '["Superiorly resulting in decreased functional activity","Posteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferiorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Laterally and slightly anteriorly","Medially leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally and slightly anteriorly', 'The glenoid cavity faces laterally, slightly anteriorly, and slightly upward to receive the humeral head, contributing to shoulder stability. It does not face medially, superiorly, posteriorly, or inferiorly.'),
('72280827-a694-50f1-83e9-4e9d6e4a9ba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial head is palpable:', '["On the lateral side of the elbow","On the posterior side","On the medial side","distal to the lateral epicondyle","On the anterior aspect"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral side of the elbow', 'The radial head is palpable on the lateral side of the elbow just distal to the lateral epicondyle and rotates beneath the examiner''s finger during pronation and supination. It is not felt on the medial or posterior aspect of the joint.'),
('a4166e5b-7a24-5217-91d6-23a988f9e0af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial styloid process is palpable:', '["At the anterior wrist resulting in decreased functional activity","At the dorsum of wrist accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the elbow mediated by specific receptor activation","At the lateral (radial) side of the wrist (distal)","At the medial (ulnar) side leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the lateral (radial) side of the wrist (distal)', 'The radial styloid process is a distal prominence on the lateral (radial) side of the wrist, lying just lateral to where the radial artery pulse is felt. The medial prominence is the ulnar styloid process.'),
('ff4d8e78-4664-5880-926a-274e63e7e2a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar styloid process is palpable:', '["At the posteromedial side of the wrist (distal ulna)","At the anterior wrist accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the lateral side mediated by specific receptor activation","At the dorsum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the elbow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the posteromedial side of the wrist (distal ulna)', 'The ulnar styloid process projects from the distal ulna and is palpable at the posteromedial side of the wrist, at a slightly more proximal level than the radial styloid. It is not found on the lateral or anterior wrist or at the elbow.'),
('d4647120-e833-5b25-b312-92bfc97bfb14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The olecranon process is palpable:', '["At the lateral elbow resulting in decreased functional activity","At the posterior aspect of the elbow (point of elbow)","At the wrist mediated by specific receptor activation","At the medial elbow without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the anterior elbow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the posterior aspect of the elbow (point of elbow)', 'The olecranon process of the ulna forms the prominent point of the elbow at its posterior aspect and receives the triceps tendon. It is not located on the anterior, medial, or lateral surfaces of the joint.'),
('24f0f069-ee86-58ec-8d49-6c4e7bf38ad9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epicondyle of the humerus is palpable:', '["At the posterior side","At the wrist","At the lateral side","At the anterior side","At the medial side of the elbow"]'::jsonb, 'At the medial side of the elbow', 'The medial epicondyle is the prominent projection on the medial side of the elbow, and the ulnar nerve passes in the groove behind it. It is not on the lateral side, which is the site of the lateral epicondyle.'),
('033154f5-993c-524a-8e41-c96c431c59d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral epicondyle of the humerus is palpable:', '["At the anterior side","At the medial side","At the lateral side of the elbow","At the posterior side","At the wrist"]'::jsonb, 'At the lateral side of the elbow', 'The lateral epicondyle is the bony prominence on the lateral side of the elbow that gives origin to the extensor muscles of the forearm. The medial side of the elbow is the site of the medial epicondyle.'),
('52c31f7f-ed2c-5c1c-a088-11f8d0bcf16b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The intercondylar line of the humerus is an imaginary line between:', '["The medial and lateral epicondyles (transverse axis of elbow)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The coronoid and olecranon fossae mediated by specific receptor activation","The capitulum and trochlea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The medial and lateral epicondyles (transverse axis of elbow)', 'The intercondylar line is an imaginary line joining the medial and lateral epicondyles and forms the transverse axis about which the elbow flexes and extends. The capitulum, trochlea, and fossae lie distal to this line.'),
('f4e6d85a-136b-5c9b-8c65-727dca1bf0e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The palpable bony prominences at the wrist from medial to lateral are:', '["radial styloid","Pisiform","None of the above","ulnar styloid","scaphoid tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Pisiform', 'The pisiform is the most medial palpable carpal prominence at the wrist, lying at the base of the hypothenar eminence on the line of the flexor carpi ulnaris tendon. The scaphoid tubercle and radial styloid lie progressively more lateral.'),
('11f29dba-40ad-51ce-95ea-59110eb427ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'In the study of Osteology, Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('e7c1b4ee-5432-5788-bc30-e02dbc7ef19f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The floor of the snuffbox contains:', '["and the radial artery","Lunate and capitate","Radius and ulna","Triquetrum and hamate","Scaphoid and trapezium"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid and trapezium', 'The floor of the anatomical snuffbox is formed by the scaphoid and trapezium, with the radial artery running over it and the scaphoid tubercle palpable inferiorly. The lunate, capitate, hamate, and triquetrum do not lie in this region.'),
('37b6f198-50bb-59ae-a5f9-92cfde199941'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The extensor pollicis longus tendon forms the ulnar border of the snuffbox and is palpable when:', '["The wrist is extended","The fingers are flexed","The wrist is flexed","The thumb is flexed","The thumb is extended (the tendon stands out)"]'::jsonb, 'The thumb is extended (the tendon stands out)', 'The extensor pollicis longus tendon stands out clearly as the ulnar border of the snuffbox when the thumb is extended. With the thumb flexed or the wrist flexed the tendon is relaxed and not prominent.'),
('b94d0ab9-a4ba-5a42-a317-ee499f8c413f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The tendons of APL and EPB are palpable at the snuffbox when:', '["The elbow is flexed","The thumb is fully extended and abducted","The fingers are extended","The wrist is flexed","The forearm is pronated"]'::jsonb, 'The thumb is fully extended and abducted', 'The tendons of abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis become prominent along the lateral border of the snuffbox when the thumb is fully extended and abducted. Flexion of the wrist or fingers does not demonstrate these tendons.'),
('0791f4f0-297c-59c8-af4a-8d4a74fce969'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scaphoid bone is tender in the snuffbox after:', '["Radial fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","Ulnar fracture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scaphoid fracture (tenderness in the snuffbox)","Lunate dislocation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carpal tunnel syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scaphoid fracture (tenderness in the snuffbox)', 'Tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox after a fall on the outstretched hand suggests a scaphoid fracture, even when initial radiographs appear normal. Radial or ulnar fractures and lunate dislocation do not localize tenderness to the snuffbox.'),
('be5f0bb8-b9e3-5d96-8792-720b2103d586'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar nerve can be palpated as it passes:', '["At the wrist resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus","In the axilla mediated by specific receptor activation","In the arm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the cubital fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus', 'The ulnar nerve can be rolled and palpated in the groove behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus, where it is easily compressed, producing tingling in the ulnar distribution. It is not superficial in the axilla, arm, or cubital fossa.'),
('26939561-443a-5edf-9d99-6b2204ad05ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The median nerve at the wrist is located:', '["Under the FCU tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the tendons of palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis","Between PL and FCU mediated by specific receptor activation","Between FCR and FCU without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Under the FCR tendon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the tendons of palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis', 'At the wrist the median nerve lies between the tendons of palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis, deep to the palmar aponeurosis, and is the landmark for its nerve block. It does not lie under the FCR or FCU tendons.'),
('956eadb1-d71f-5bef-8b96-b34225bf7355'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial pulse is felt:', '["Between FCR and PL resulting in decreased functional activity","At the dorsum of the wrist accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between PL and FCU mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to the FCR tendon (between FCR and radial styloid)","At the ulnar side leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to the FCR tendon (between FCR and radial styloid)', 'The radial artery pulse is felt lateral to the flexor carpi radialis tendon, between it and the radial styloid process. The ulnar side, between palmaris longus and FCU, is the site of the ulnar pulse.'),
('d9a0fec7-485d-51f3-a7e8-0320d3b39681'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar pulse is felt:', '["Medial to the FCU tendon","At the snuffbox","At the dorsum of the wrist","Lateral to FCR","Between FCR and PL"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to the FCU tendon', 'The ulnar artery pulse is felt just medial to the tendon of flexor carpi ulnaris at the wrist. The anatomical snuffbox and the region lateral to FCR are the sites of the radial artery.'),
('8c887b2e-e207-52c1-bb32-4c11c4386d28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Allen''s test assesses:', '["Ulnar nerve function","Carpal tunnel syndrome","Radial nerve function","Median nerve function","Patency of the radial and ulnar arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Patency of the radial and ulnar arteries', 'Allen''s test assesses the patency of the radial and ulnar arteries and the completeness of the palmar arch by compressing both arteries, then releasing one. It does not test nerve function or carpal tunnel syndrome.'),
('bd8f1d41-4c8b-550b-8b7a-38a3ce99bf8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The thenar eminence is composed of which muscles?', '["flexor pollicis brevis","opponens pollicis","Hypothenar muscles","Adductor pollicis","Abductor pollicis brevis"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor pollicis brevis', 'The thenar eminence is formed by abductor pollicis brevis, flexor pollicis brevis, and opponens pollicis, all supplied by the median nerve. Adductor pollicis lies deep and is not part of the superficial thenar mass, and the hypothenar muscles form the opposite eminence.'),
('2b47d56e-a95d-50f8-8ecf-cdf45a4c7e22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The hypothenar eminence is composed of which muscles?', '["Thenar muscles","flexor digiti minimi brevis","opponens digiti minimi","Abductor digiti minimi","Interossei"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor digiti minimi', 'The hypothenar eminence is formed by abductor digiti minimi, flexor digiti minimi brevis, and opponens digiti minimi, supplied by the deep branch of the ulnar nerve. Interossei and the thenar muscles do not form this eminence.'),
('aff68ed5-d77f-5b77-9e3f-60f673f841f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The pulp space (distal closed compartment) of the finger is bounded by:', '["Only the skin resulting in decreased functional activity","The flexor tendon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The extensor tendon mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Septae from the periosteum of the distal phalanx to the skin"]'::jsonb, 'Septae from the periosteum of the distal phalanx to the skin', 'The pulp space is a closed compartment of the fingertip walled by fibrous septa passing from the periosteum of the distal phalanx to the skin. Infection in this space can compress the vessels supplying the distal phalanx, causing necrosis.'),
('5c70df20-45ec-500b-8296-a65577b2bdf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar nerve at the wrist can be blocked by injection:', '["At the palmaris longus resulting in decreased functional activity","At the radial artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the FCR tendon mediated by specific receptor activation","At the anatomical snuffbox without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the medial side of the wrist (at the pisiform)"]'::jsonb, 'At the medial side of the wrist (at the pisiform)', 'The ulnar nerve is blocked at the medial side of the wrist at the level of the pisiform, where it lies beside the ulnar artery. The snuffbox, FCR, and palmaris longus regions are sites for the radial and median nerves.'),
('96752974-62dd-501a-8c8a-1c3942d08852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The median nerve at the wrist can be blocked by injection:', '["At the pisiform","Between palmaris longus and FCR tendons","At the dorsum of the wrist","At the radial artery","At the ulnar side"]'::jsonb, 'Between palmaris longus and FCR tendons', 'The median nerve is blocked at the wrist between the tendons of palmaris longus and flexor carpi radialis. The pisiform and ulnar side are used for the ulnar nerve block, and the dorsum of the wrist is not the median nerve site.'),
('f2d7257d-0d03-5036-8c6a-701b81d08e18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial nerve (superficial branch) can be blocked by injection:', '["At the ulnar side resulting in decreased functional activity","At the medial epicondyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subcutaneously at the radial side of the wrist (over the anatomical snuffbox)","At the cubital fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the palmaris longus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneously at the radial side of the wrist (over the anatomical snuffbox)', 'The superficial branch of the radial nerve emerges subcutaneously and is blocked on the radial side of the wrist over the anatomical snuffbox. The cubital fossa, ulnar side, and medial epicondyle are not the correct sites.'),
('e19a2edf-2386-54d2-ba9f-969aeb6478c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The carpal bones ossify in the following order after birth:', '["scaphoid (5-6y)","hamate (2-4m)","triquetrum (3-5y)","lunate (4-6y)","Capitate (1-3m)"]'::jsonb, 'Capitate (1-3m)', 'The capitate is the first carpal bone to ossify, appearing at about 1-3 months of age, followed shortly by the hamate (2-4 months). The triquetrum, lunate, and scaphoid centers appear later in childhood.'),
('64fd5bf3-e47e-52fb-9e83-31aebca65e9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The carpal bones generally ossify earlier in:', '["Females (approximately 1-2 years earlier than males)","Both equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Males without affecting the primary tissue pathway","difference leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Females (approximately 1-2 years earlier than males)', 'The carpal bones ossify about 1-2 years earlier in females than in males, a sex difference used when assessing skeletal maturity. The centers appear at the same relative order in both sexes.'),
('ae4ad9d0-c2ab-50d0-a4c9-fe548a8ccd99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The number of ossified carpal bones at birth is:', '["0","6","4","2","8"]'::jsonb, '0', 'No carpal bones are ossified at birth; all centers are cartilaginous at term. The first centers, the capitate and hamate, appear at about 1-3 months of age.'),
('fb78ccab-646f-5484-8d76-7b35fe35570a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The number of ossified carpal bones at 1 year of age is:', '["6 resulting in decreased functional activity","8 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","0 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 (capitate and hamate)","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (capitate and hamate)', 'By one year of age the capitate and hamate are ossified, giving two carpal centers. The remaining carpal bones ossify gradually through early childhood.'),
('bf237fdf-5182-5eb0-8c75-136923415e94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The maximum number of carpal bones (8) are ossified by:', '["8-10 years","12-14 years","At birth","5-6 years","16-18 years"]'::jsonb, '12-14 years', 'All eight carpal bones are ossified by about 12-14 years of age, with the pisiform, appearing around age 10-12, being the last to calcify. Completion of the carpal centers is used as a skeletal maturity marker.'),
('8c7b0028-61aa-5be6-affb-77d77b8967e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The distal radial epiphysis ossifies at:', '["10-12 years","Birth","1-2 years of age","5-7 years","15 years"]'::jsonb, '1-2 years of age', 'The distal radial epiphysis ossifies at about 1-2 years of age and is a key early landmark for assessing bone age. It fuses with the radial shaft much later, near skeletal maturity.'),
('7d44795e-166b-5d39-817d-a6b7ee7004ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The distal ulnar epiphysis ossifies at:', '["1-2 years","5-7 years of age","10-12 years","Birth","15 years"]'::jsonb, '5-7 years of age', 'The distal ulnar epiphysis appears at about 5-7 years of age, later than the distal radial epiphysis (1-2 years). Its late appearance means the distal radius carries more of the distal growth.'),
('af28d664-fae6-50a7-b9f2-a46df2f9e1c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ossification center for the trochlea of the humerus appears at:', '["Birth","5-7 years","15 years","1-2 years","10-12 years"]'::jsonb, '10-12 years', 'The trochlea ossification center of the humerus appears at about 10-12 years of age. The capitulum appears much earlier (about 1-2 years), followed later by the medial epicondyle and the trochlea.'),
('59a5d1e9-bfff-58c5-add9-294da5c89514'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ossification center for the capitulum appears at:', '["10-12 years","Birth","5-7 years","15 years","1-2 years"]'::jsonb, '1-2 years', 'The capitulum is the first ossification center of the distal humerus to appear, at about 1-2 years of age. It is followed by the medial epicondyle, trochlea, and lateral epicondyle in later childhood.'),
('9db209cb-4ee8-58c9-b40f-739d07eeb713'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ossification center for the lateral epicondyle appears at:', '["1-2 years","14-16 years","Birth","5-7 years","10-12 years"]'::jsonb, '10-12 years', 'The lateral epicondyle ossification center appears at about 10-12 years of age. Unlike the medial epicondyle, it is not a traction epiphysis and it fuses earlier than the medial epicondyle.'),
('d12d76ff-b452-546e-8ed3-16396c23adfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ossification center for the medial epicondyle appears at:', '["10-12 years resulting in decreased functional activity","5-7 years (traction epiphysis)","1-2 years mediated by specific receptor activation","15-17 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Birth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-7 years (traction epiphysis)', 'The medial epicondyle ossifies from a separate traction epiphysis appearing at about 5-7 years of age. It is the last distal humeral epiphysis to fuse with the shaft, around 16-18 years.'),
('ae4c17b5-1c15-5112-a814-4d10d1110127'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epicondyle is a traction epiphysis and is not part of the:', '["Proximal humerus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elbow joint mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal humeral articular surface","Forearm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal humeral articular surface', 'The medial epicondyle is an extracapsular, non-articular traction epiphysis that does not contribute to the articular surface of the distal humerus. It gives origin to the forearm flexor muscles and the ulnar collateral ligament.'),
('9c565702-3831-5e54-91a1-c38650695050'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Fracture of the medial epicondyle is often associated with:', '["Elbow dislocation in children","None","Supracondylar fracture","Radial head fracture","Scaphoid fracture"]'::jsonb, 'Elbow dislocation in children', 'Avulsion of the medial epicondyle commonly accompanies elbow dislocation in children, the fragment being pulled by the attached forearm flexor muscles. It is not typically associated with supracondylar, radial head, or scaphoid fractures.'),
('a39a0fd1-53e8-5bfe-8033-155f70ecd4a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The humeral shaft (diaphysis) is ossified from:', '["The distal epiphysis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A primary ossification center appearing at the 8th week IUL","Secondary centers only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The proximal epiphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A primary ossification center appearing at the 8th week IUL', 'The humeral diaphysis is ossified from a primary ossification center that appears at about the eighth week of intrauterine life. The epiphyses develop later from separate secondary centers.'),
('fcaebf0d-243e-56cf-b82b-8becb8635d26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The proximal humeral epiphyseal plate contributes:', '["Equal to distal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","50% of length mediated by specific receptor activation","80% of the length of the humerus (growth at proximal end)","20% of length leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '80% of the length of the humerus (growth at proximal end)', 'The proximal humeral epiphyseal plate accounts for about 80% of the growth in humeral length, which is why damage here produces marked limb shortening. The distal plate contributes only about 20%.'),
('415470bd-d152-5a4b-8741-e0e7a7c81b12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The distal humeral epiphyseal plate contributes:', '["80% of length resulting in decreased functional activity","Not significant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20% of the length of the humerus (less growth)","50% of length without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '20% of the length of the humerus (less growth)', 'The distal humeral epiphyseal plate contributes only about 20% of the growth in humeral length, while the proximal plate provides about 80%. Injury to the distal plate therefore causes less longitudinal shortening.'),
('351b2111-44c5-565a-9776-45a4044db3c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial head epiphysis appears at:', '["10-12 years","1-2 years","5-7 years","Birth","15 years"]'::jsonb, '5-7 years', 'The head of the radius is a pressure epiphysis that begins ossifying around 5-7 years of age. The olecranon epiphysis (10-12 years) and the other elbow epiphyses appear later, while the shaft''s primary center forms by the 8th fetal week.'),
('6d923790-2ef4-5c37-bd27-30fdbe8d79e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The olecranon epiphysis appears at:', '["15 years","1-2 years","10-12 years","5-7 years","Birth"]'::jsonb, '10-12 years', 'The olecranon is a traction epiphysis that appears at about 10-12 years and unites with the shaft around 16-17 years. The radial head epiphysis appears earlier (5-7 years), so the later timing helps distinguish the olecranon.'),
('1fd2887d-47db-5e67-ac59-1ae80f24693b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial and ulnar shafts ossify from primary centers at:', '["12th week","Birth","8th week IUL","6th month","5th month"]'::jsonb, '8th week IUL', 'Primary ossification centers for the radius and ulna appear during the 8th week of intrauterine life, as in other long bones of the upper limb. Secondary (epiphyseal) centers appear much later, mostly after birth.'),
('77366958-2256-57f9-9d0c-4a8c7cfc871c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The epiphyses at the proximal radius and distal ulna are:', '["Not true epiphyses resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Pressure epiphyses (part of the joint)","Traction epiphyses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pressure epiphyses (part of the joint)', 'These ends enter into joints (the head of the radius into the elbow and proximal radioulnar joint; the distal ulna into the distal radioulnar joint), so their epiphyses are pressure epiphyses that transmit force through joint surfaces. Traction epiphyses, by contrast, develop at sites of tendon attachment.'),
('3477983f-32d0-5285-854d-67be13783950'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The epiphyses at the distal radius and proximal ulna are:', '["Not true epiphyses","Traction epiphyses","None","Pressure epiphyses","All"]'::jsonb, 'Pressure epiphyses', 'The distal radial epiphysis forms the main wrist (radiocarpal) joint surface and is therefore a pressure epiphysis, with the proximal ulnar epiphysis likewise contributing to the joint. A traction epiphysis (such as the separate olecranon center) instead arises at a tendon insertion rather than at a joint surface.'),
('7dda6474-5b57-51b2-adea-2cee70bc2730'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The thumb is rotated 90 degrees relative to the other fingers due to the orientation of its:', '["MCP joint","None","CMC joint (saddle joint)","IP joint","Carpometacarpal ligaments"]'::jsonb, 'CMC joint (saddle joint)', 'The trapezium and the base of the first metacarpal form a saddle-shaped (reciprocally concavo-convex) carpometacarpal joint, which allows the thumb''s 90-degree rotation and opposition. The MCP and IP joints are hinge-type and do not confer this rotational alignment.'),
('8b1075fd-53fb-5e1d-875b-4128478596fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The longitudinal arch of the hand is formed by:', '["Radius","carpal bones","Phalanges only","Ulna and carpal bones","and metacarpals"]'::jsonb, 'carpal bones', 'The longitudinal arch of the hand runs from the wrist to the fingertips and has the carpal bones at its proximal base, with the metacarpals and phalanges completing its span. The radius and ulna are forearm bones proximal to the wrist and do not form the arch, and the phalanges alone cannot complete it.'),
('7f47c667-1411-5d53-b404-8049194b1a12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The transverse (distal) arch of the hand is formed by:', '["The proximal row resulting in decreased functional activity","The carpal bones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The phalanges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The metacarpal heads (mobile arch)"]'::jsonb, 'The metacarpal heads (mobile arch)', 'The mobile distal transverse arch is formed by the heads of the metacarpals, which flatten and spread with the splaying of the fingers. The proximal transverse arch is carpal in location, so the phalanges and the proximal carpal row do not build this distal arch.'),
('a80eadf5-62da-550d-8f1c-64e031962403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The central pillar of the hand is the:', '["Fourth metacarpal resulting in decreased functional activity","Little finger accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Third metacarpal and capitate (central ray)","Index finger without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thumb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Third metacarpal and capitate (central ray)', 'The third metacarpal together with the capitate forms the central axis (ray) that carries the main weight of the hand and aligns with the middle finger. The index, ring, little-finger, and thumb rays are peripheral and do not occupy this central position.'),
('1ac1ecf4-f58e-579b-bb9e-8cab85b845af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial border of the hand is formed by:', '["Ulna","1st and 2nd metacarpals","Radius","trapezium","scaphoid"]'::jsonb, 'Radius', 'The lateral (radial) border of the forearm-hand unit is defined by the radius, along which the radial artery and radial nerve run superficially. The ulna marks the medial border, while the carpal and metacarpal bones occupy the intervening region rather than forming the border.'),
('ecbb885f-5b1e-52af-9ef8-0a99ab21e91d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar border of the hand is formed by which structure?', '["Ulna","Radius","Carpal bones","Metacarpals","Phalanges"]'::jsonb, 'Ulna', 'The medial (ulnar) border is formed by the ulna, which conducts the ulnar artery and ulnar nerve along its course. The radius defines the opposite lateral border, so it cannot form the ulnar border.'),
('a8c95139-6705-5403-add1-8b29edc890d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ossification of the clavicle begins earlier in:', '["Females (by about 2 weeks)","Both equally","Males","difference","Not known"]'::jsonb, 'Females (by about 2 weeks)', 'The clavicle is the first bone to ossify (around the 5th-6th fetal week), and its primary center appears slightly earlier in females, by about two weeks. This small sex difference in ossification timing is characteristic of the clavicle.'),
('070d9e14-8f26-50f3-b659-1852e8bc03c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The sternal end of the clavicle has an epiphysis that is important in forensics for age estimation. It fuses at:', '["12-14 years","30-35 years","18-25 years","25-30 years","14-16 years"]'::jsonb, '18-25 years', 'The medial (sternal) epiphysis of the clavicle is among the last to appear and fuses between 18 and 25 years, which makes it valuable for forensic age estimation. Fusion does not occur in the younger ranges listed and is not delayed to 25-35 years.'),
('45585098-45bc-5711-9cf3-38a63046e05d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The acromioclavicular joint is a plane synovial joint with an intra-articular disc that is:', '["Incomplete (wedge-shaped)","Complete","None","Fibrocartilaginous disc similar to the knee","Multiple"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete (wedge-shaped)', 'The wedge-shaped fibrocartilaginous disc of the acromioclavicular joint is often incomplete and variable, splitting the joint into two compartments only partly. It is far less complete than the discs of the sternoclavicular or knee joints.'),
('e7ad3160-a873-5d77-94de-7511602c9f35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar nerve passes through which two heads in the forearm to reach the anterior compartment?', '["Two heads of supinator","None","Two heads of pronator teres","Two heads of FDS","Humeral and ulnar heads of FCU"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar heads of FCU', 'The ulnar nerve enters the forearm by passing between the humeral and ulnar heads of flexor carpi ulnaris, after which it descends beneath that muscle. It does not pass between the heads of supinator, pronator teres, or flexor digitorum superficialis.'),
('bf29525b-1a4e-5025-b2e8-3ca333be9640'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The median nerve passes through which two heads in the forearm?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Two heads of supinator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two heads of FDS mediated by specific receptor activation","Humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres","Two heads of FCU leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres', 'The median nerve enters the forearm between the humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres and then runs beneath flexor digitorum superficialis. It is not accompanied by supinator or flexor carpi ulnaris in this course.'),
('33c57b0f-0d53-5a82-8b3d-eaf0c5aeb1d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial nerve (deep branch) passes through which two heads?', '["Two heads of pronator teres resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial and deep heads of supinator","Two heads of FCU without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two heads of FDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep heads of supinator', 'The deep (posterior interosseous) branch of the radial nerve pierces the supinator, passing between its superficial and deep heads. The ulnar and median nerves do not traverse the supinator, so its heads are unrelated to their course.'),
('84d017e7-b9a7-5ec5-8bd8-9cbf1fdfa4b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The ulnar nerve passes through which two heads of the flexor carpi ulnaris?', '["Medial and lateral","Anterior and posterior","None","Superficial and deep","Humeral and ulnar"]'::jsonb, 'Humeral and ulnar', 'Flexor carpi ulnaris arises by humeral and ulnar heads, and the ulnar nerve passes between them to reach the anterior compartment of the forearm. Other pairings such as superficial and deep, or anterior and posterior, do not describe this muscle''s origin.'),
('77d6e4b9-dace-5ff0-a766-b62bb1dc6205'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The median nerve passes between which two structures in the cubital fossa?', '["It does not pass between heads; it enters the forearm between the two heads of pronator teres","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The two heads of brachialis mediated by specific receptor activation","The two heads of triceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The two heads of supinator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It does not pass between heads; it enters the forearm between the two heads of pronator teres', 'Within the cubital fossa the median nerve does not run between muscle heads; it leaves the fossa distally by passing between the humeral and ulnar heads of pronator teres. The brachialis and triceps provide no such interval and lie outside the fossa.'),
('29c4185c-25be-51d2-950f-520d793c752f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The radial artery at the wrist lies between which two tendons?', '["FCU and PL","PL and FCR","FCR and FCU","FCR and brachioradialis","ECRL and brachioradialis"]'::jsonb, 'FCR and brachioradialis', 'At the wrist the radial artery courses between the tendons of brachioradialis (laterally) and flexor carpi radialis (medially), where its pulse is readily felt. The interval between FCU and PL transmits the ulnar artery rather than the radial.'),
('102ea99b-c076-5883-a513-c33ee2d4a232'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The flexor retinaculum is attached to which four bony prominences?', '["Pisiform","None of the above","hamate hook","trapezium","scaphoid tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Pisiform', 'The flexor retinaculum bridges the carpal tunnel by attaching medially to the pisiform and the hook of hamate and laterally to the scaphoid tubercle and the trapezium. Pisiform is one of these four fixed bony attachments, so the listed soft-tissue structures are excluded.'),
('b8b3ece2-faa3-59f3-a0b3-30e41c56f3c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The capitellum of the humerus articulates with which bone?', '["Radial head","Olecranon","Trochlear notch of ulna","Radial tuberosity","Coronoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Radial head', 'The capitellum is the lateral condylar surface of the humerus that articulates with the head of the radius at the elbow. The trochlear notch and olecranon of the ulna, and the coronoid process, relate instead to the trochlea medially.'),
('e140e53a-96f2-56d5-bb99-98f866822582'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezoid line on the clavicle is for the attachment of which structure?', '["Deltoid muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)","Pectoralis major mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'The conoid tubercle and trapezoid line on the clavicle give attachment to the conoid and trapezoid parts of the coracoclavicular ligament, which ties the clavicle to the coracoid process. No muscle arises from this ridge, so deltoid, trapezius, pectoralis major, and subclavius are excluded.'),
('f469678b-e3e5-58e8-adba-05df72d1f8e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Osteology: the trapezoid line on the clavicle is for the attachment of which structure?', '["Deltoid muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)","Pectoralis major mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'The trapezoid ligament, the lateral part of the coracoclavicular complex, attaches along the trapezoid line near the lateral end of the clavicle. This crest is purely ligamentous and is not a site of muscle origin or insertion.'),
('18c9bd87-43fd-5d26-aa76-f2c4c4c2cf10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'For Osteology, identify the correct response: the trapezoid line on the clavicle is for the attachment of which structure?', '["Deltoid muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)","Pectoralis major mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'At the lateral (acromial) third of the clavicle, the trapezoid line anchors the trapezoid ligament that binds the clavicle to the coracoid process. Bony ridges of this kind serve ligament attachments, whereas the listed muscles attach on other clavicular surfaces.'),
('7b140def-43df-5a36-aa49-452dee0ba4cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The trapezoid line on the clavicle is for the attachment of which structure? Select the best answer.', '["Deltoid muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)","Pectoralis major mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'The trapezoid line is a rough ridge on the lateral part of the clavicle that bears the trapezoid ligament, completing the coracoclavicular linkage of the clavicle to the scapula. None of the listed muscles, including trapezius and deltoid, attach at this ridge.'),
('175271a9-03b6-5b46-8263-d586e68f01b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which response correctly addresses this Osteology item: the trapezoid line on the clavicle is for the attachment of which structure?', '["Deltoid muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)","Pectoralis major mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular ligament)', 'The lateral part of the coracoclavicular ligament, the trapezoid ligament, uses the trapezoid line as its clavicular attachment site. Muscular attachments such as deltoid, trapezius, pectoralis major, and subclavius involve other surfaces of the clavicle.'),
('bd42291c-e07e-5485-a16f-d28a0ef7f16d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The impression for costoclavicular ligament is located on which surface?', '["Anterior surface medial","Inferior surface lateral end","Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle","Posterior surface","Superior surface of lateral end"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle', 'The rhomboid impression for the costoclavicular ligament occupies the inferior surface of the medial (sternal) end of the clavicle, where the ligament tethers it to the first costal cartilage. No such impression exists on the posterior or superior surfaces.'),
('aa3b0926-c421-56ba-92b8-00ee69498f53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Osteology: the impression for costoclavicular ligament is located on which surface?', '["Anterior surface medial","Inferior surface lateral end","Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle","Posterior surface","Superior surface of lateral end"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle', 'The costoclavicular ligament attaches to a rough rhomboid impression on the undersurface of the medial end of the clavicle, anchoring it to the first costal cartilage. The superior and lateral clavicular surfaces carry other muscle and ligament attachments.'),
('758a4d27-3076-5b63-b4c8-84d9f89820cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'For Osteology, identify the correct response: the impression for costoclavicular ligament is located on which surface?', '["Anterior surface medial","Inferior surface lateral end","Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle","Posterior surface","Superior surface of lateral end"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle', 'Lying on the inferior aspect of the sternal segment, the costoclavicular impression marks the attachment of the costoclavicular ligament to the first costal cartilage. Neither the posterior surface nor the lateral end of the clavicle shows this impression.'),
('0ced78ab-b31a-5ca8-ac43-a65a5212dda7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The impression for costoclavicular ligament is located on which surface? Select the best answer.', '["Anterior surface medial","Inferior surface lateral end","Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle","Posterior surface","Superior surface of lateral end"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle', 'The inferior surface of the medial end of the clavicle carries the rhomboid impression for the costoclavicular ligament, which fixes the clavicle to the first costal cartilage. Because of this, the site lies under the sternal end rather than on the lateral or superior surfaces.'),
('177bb250-77ff-590e-84dc-95ee8d7b51b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which response correctly addresses this Osteology item: the impression for costoclavicular ligament is located on which surface?', '["Anterior surface medial","Inferior surface lateral end","Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle","Posterior surface","Superior surface of lateral end"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface of medial end of clavicle', 'The rough rhomboid area for the costoclavicular ligament is found on the lower surface of the medial end of the clavicle. Sites on the lateral end and superior surface belong instead to the coracoclavicular ligament and to muscular attachments.'),
('5f91008d-0914-54f2-8806-550b5a23d23b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epiphysis of the clavicle is important for which purpose?', '["Bone growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Joint movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)', 'The medial clavicular epiphysis is among the last long-bone epiphyses to fuse (around 25 years), so its maturation stages are used to estimate age in forensic and radiological practice. Joint movement, growth pacing, and muscle attachment are not its defining forensic role.'),
('359c337c-ccb5-5977-9ac8-0b272e7f1e81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Osteology: the medial epiphysis of the clavicle is important for which purpose?', '["Bone growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Joint movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)', 'Because the sternal epiphysis of the clavicle fuses late (around 25 years), its radiographic stage correlates with chronological age during the second to third decades. This timing makes it a key forensic landmark, unlike its negligible role in muscle attachment.'),
('9145363d-680b-5edd-8ebd-fd01f3b07272'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'For Osteology, identify the correct response: the medial epiphysis of the clavicle is important for which purpose?', '["Bone growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Joint movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)', 'Forensic anthropologists rely on the medial clavicular epiphysis because it ossifies and fuses between roughly 18 and 25 years, a window that is valuable for age estimation. Bone growth and joint movement are generic functions shared by all epiphyses and do not explain its particular forensic use.'),
('71349a81-9211-5da9-b9a6-6df524650235'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The medial epiphysis of the clavicle is important for which purpose? Select the best answer.', '["Bone growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Joint movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)', 'The sternal end of the clavicle possesses the last epiphysis of the body to unite, around 25 years, providing a widely used method for skeletal age estimation. General roles such as growth or muscle attachment do not explain its special forensic importance.'),
('9ac7b5cb-2c8f-5247-8131-e7721e260e61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which response correctly addresses this Osteology item: the medial epiphysis of the clavicle is important for which purpose?', '["Bone growth resulting in decreased functional activity","Joint movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)"]'::jsonb, 'Age estimation in forensic medicine (fuses by 25 years)', 'Its late fusion between about 18 and 25 years allows forensic medicine to estimate age from the sternal clavicular epiphysis. This timing is the feature of interest, whereas joint movement and muscle attachment are unrelated to why the epiphysis is studied.'),
('02566f32-1421-5c0e-bbfb-0873e3f76050'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coracoid process is located on which part of the scapula?', '["Lateral border","Medial border","Inferior angle","Superior border of scapula","near the glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior border of scapula', 'The coracoid process projects from the upper border of the scapula just medial to the glenoid cavity and gives attachment to coracobrachialis, the short head of biceps, and the coracoclavicular ligament. It does not arise from the medial border, inferior angle, or lateral border.'),
('930e0554-0275-5a3c-b131-8db6db924672'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Osteology: the coracoid process is located on which part of the scapula?', '["Lateral border","Medial border","Inferior angle","Superior border of scapula","near the glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior border of scapula', 'The beak-like coracoid arises from the superior border of the scapula, lateral to the scapular notch and close to the glenoid. Its location ties it to the upper margin of the scapula rather than to the medial border, inferior angle, or lateral border.'),
('79c36eaf-1913-5f43-a5fd-92e4f4c1eca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'For Osteology, identify the correct response: the coracoid process is located on which part of the scapula?', '["Lateral border","Medial border","Inferior angle","Superior border of scapula","near the glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior border of scapula', 'Originating from the upper border of the scapula beside the glenoid, the coracoid process serves as the main scapular anchor for the short head of biceps, coracobrachialis, and pectoralis minor. The inferior angle and the medial and lateral borders never carry this process.'),
('eed9fa40-d386-5217-9f31-c934b7c3b7c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The coracoid process is located on which part of the scapula? Select the best answer.', '["Lateral border","Medial border","Inferior angle","Superior border of scapula","near the glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior border of scapula', 'The coracoid process springs from the superolateral part of the scapular superior border, immediately medial to the glenoid cavity. The inferior angle and the medial and lateral borders lie far from this origin.'),
('92766171-e8ce-5707-aaa1-4b458764ae16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which response correctly addresses this Osteology item: the coracoid process is located on which part of the scapula?', '["Lateral border","Medial border","Inferior angle","Superior border of scapula","near the glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior border of scapula', 'It is the superior border of the scapula, just medial to the glenoid, that gives rise to the coracoid process. The lateral border, medial border, and inferior angle are unrelated to its origin.'),
('53cc490b-e9bf-5747-93c1-db89e2e06ac9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scapular notch is located where?', '["near the coracoid","On the superior border of the scapula","On the lateral border","At the inferior angle","On the medial border"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior border of the scapula', 'The scapular notch is a shallow notch on the upper border of the scapula, medial to the coracoid process, and is bridged by the superior transverse scapular ligament to transmit the suprascapular nerve. Its position rules out the medial border, lateral border, and inferior angle.'),
('e83d6e75-3990-5a90-b354-c1458f676f9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Osteology: the scapular notch is located where?', '["near the coracoid","On the superior border of the scapula","On the lateral border","At the inferior angle","On the medial border"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior border of the scapula', 'Located on the superior border of the scapula just medial to the coracoid process, the scapular notch is converted into a foramen for the suprascapular nerve. It does not involve the lateral border, medial border, or inferior angle.'),
('eb79a4a0-d3b9-5f4b-8ce5-ad4ca0e6a6b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'For Osteology, identify the correct response: the scapular notch is located where?', '["near the coracoid","On the superior border of the scapula","On the lateral border","At the inferior angle","On the medial border"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior border of the scapula', 'The scapular notch lies on the superior border of the scapula between the coracoid process and the medial angle, serving as the passage for the suprascapular nerve beneath the superior transverse scapular ligament. The lateral border, medial border, and inferior angle are not notched.'),
('90066746-239d-56c6-9c9f-73b9f25dc900'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The scapular notch is located where? Select the best answer.', '["near the coracoid","On the superior border of the scapula","On the lateral border","At the inferior angle","On the medial border"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior border of the scapula', 'The superior border of the scapula carries the scapular notch, which is converted into an opening for the suprascapular nerve. Comparable notches are absent on the lateral and medial borders and at the inferior angle.'),
('6b0bbe6c-5e87-5356-86e0-2feb1bba472e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Which response correctly addresses this Osteology item: the scapular notch is located where?', '["near the coracoid","On the superior border of the scapula","On the lateral border","At the inferior angle","On the medial border"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior border of the scapula', 'The scapular notch occupies the superior border of the scapula, medial to the root of the coracoid process, and allows the suprascapular nerve to reach the supraspinous fossa. The other scapular borders bear no comparable notch.'),
('e5734752-a5a6-56f1-b852-c5f3d4dca599'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'The suprascapular notch is bridged by which structure?', '["Acromioclavicular ligament","None","Coracoacromial ligament","Superior transverse scapular ligament","Coracoclavicular ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Superior transverse scapular ligament', 'The superior transverse scapular ligament bridges the suprascapular notch, converting it into a foramen through which the suprascapular nerve passes. The coracoacromial and coracoclavicular ligaments run elsewhere and do not overlie the notch.'),
('47251596-620b-5145-b851-15a16a271e8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'In Upper limb osteology, which statement best fits the following fact: the clavicle is classified as which type of bone?', '["Long bone","Flat bone","Sesamoid bone","Irregular bone","Short bone"]'::jsonb, 'Long bone', 'The clavicle is classified as a long bone because, although it lacks a medullary cavity and ossifies largely by intramembranous ossification, it possesses a shaft with two ends and secondary ossification centers. It is not flat, short, irregular, or sesamoid.'),
('b35a4361-065d-5032-b2c9-48a8d351d33b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Osteology', 'Regarding Upper limb osteology, what is the correct answer to: the medial end of the clavicle articulates with:?', '["Sternum (sternoclavicular joint)","Acromion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Scapula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum (sternoclavicular joint)', 'The medial (sternal) end of the clavicle articulates with the manubrium of the sternum to form the sternoclavicular joint, the only bony link between the axial skeleton and the upper limb. The acromion articulates with the lateral (acromial) end instead.'),
('62bc6b41-1ad5-5fce-8712-48061b0016f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which bone forms the pectoral girdle anteriorly?', '["Rib 1","Sternum","Scapula","Humerus","Clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle', 'The clavicle forms the anterior component of the pectoral girdle, articulating medially with the sternum and laterally with the acromion of the scapula. The scapula and humerus are posterior and arm bones respectively, while the sternum belongs to the axial skeleton.'),
('d8116d09-4a38-5356-8001-8bea38c2a88a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The conoid tubercle is located on which part of clavicle?', '["Medial end","Superior surface lateral third","Inferior surface middle third","Superior surface medial third","Inferior surface lateral third"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface lateral third', 'The conoid tubercle is a rough elevation on the under-surface (inferior surface) of the lateral third of the clavicle. It gives attachment to the conoid part of the coracoclavicular ligament, and the adjacent trapezoid line receives the trapezoid ligament.'),
('0f59e677-8ccf-5f98-93e5-227f2c6d4ecd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The sternoclavicular joint is what type of joint?', '["Plane","Ball and socket","Saddle","Hinge","Pivot"]'::jsonb, 'Saddle', 'The sternoclavicular joint is a synovial saddle (sellaris) joint between the medial end of the clavicle and the manubrium sterni. A complete fibrocartilaginous disc and strong capsular ligaments make it a stable yet mobile joint.'),
('3b708e38-3faf-5b4f-9d40-b70c792db0fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which structure passes through the suprascapular notch?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Suprascapular artery","Both nerve and artery","Transverse scapular ligament","Suprascapular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular nerve', 'The suprascapular nerve passes through the suprascapular notch beneath the superior transverse scapular ligament. The suprascapular artery and vein travel above the ligament and therefore do not pass through the notch itself.'),
('e7a12faa-1a3a-5844-bf77-858d51da3f96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracoid process is a feature of which bone?', '["Sternum","Rib 1","Clavicle","Scapula","Humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Scapula', 'The coracoid process projects from the upper border of the scapula just medial to the glenoid cavity. It is a key site for the coracoclavicular ligament, conjoined tendon, coracobrachialis, and short head of biceps.'),
('cd836317-0296-5b8c-bbe6-dabb6dec888b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axilla is bounded medially by:', '["Pectoralis minor resulting in decreased functional activity","Pectoralis major accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper ribs and intercostal muscles","Scapula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper ribs and intercostal muscles', 'The medial wall of the axilla is formed by the upper four or five ribs with the intercostal muscles, covered by the serratus anterior. Pectoralis major and minor form the anterior wall, whereas the scapula contributes to the posterior wall.'),
('06b05738-9145-5982-871a-3cacb08127ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The apex of axilla is bounded by:', '["First rib resulting in decreased functional activity","Clavicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Scapula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both clavicle and first rib"]'::jsonb, 'Both clavicle and first rib', 'The apex (cervico-axillary canal) of the axilla is a triangular space bounded anteriorly by the clavicle, medially by the first rib, and posteriorly by the superior border of the scapula. Both the clavicle and first rib therefore delimit this aperture.'),
('d9986fda-56f5-5b1c-a3b2-e5efd97a995f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary artery is a continuation of which artery?', '["Brachial","Thoracoacromial","Subclavian","Lateral thoracic","Internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian', 'The subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery at the outer border of the first rib. The axillary artery continues to the lower border of teres major, where it becomes the brachial artery.'),
('7cfacbd5-23d1-545b-b25d-39be347127f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral thoracic artery arises from which part of axillary artery?', '["First part","Second part","Subclavian","Thoracoacromial","Third part"]'::jsonb, 'Second part', 'The lateral thoracic artery is a branch of the second part of the axillary artery, which lies behind the pectoralis minor. The first part gives the superior thoracic artery, while the third part gives the subscapular and circumflex humeral arteries.'),
('bd006669-e599-5720-92cd-5727a57007be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary vein is formed by:', '["Brachial and cephalic veins","Internal and external jugular","Subclavian and jugular","Cephalic and basilic veins","Basilic and brachial veins"]'::jsonb, 'Basilic and brachial veins', 'The axillary vein is formed at the lower border of teres major by the union of the brachial and basilic veins. The cephalic vein joins the axillary vein higher up after piercing the clavipectoral fascia.'),
('46bce0a5-671c-5083-8d62-ef902f5dc8e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The cords of brachial plexus are named according to their relation to:', '["Axillary artery","Subclavian vein","Clavicle","Subclavian artery","Axillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary artery', 'The three cords of the brachial plexus are named by their position relative to the second part of the axillary artery as lateral, medial, and posterior. They surround the artery behind the pectoralis minor.'),
('af2a4e4f-14b4-54c0-8148-0ddd6f99802b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which nerve is not a branch of the cords of brachial plexus?', '["Ulnar","Suprascapular","Musculocutaneous","Radial","Median"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular', 'The suprascapular nerve arises from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus, not from any of the cords. Ulnar, median, radial, and musculocutaneous nerves all branch from the cords.'),
('9904ab02-e60e-529f-9ddc-dd239c3bcfe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The long thoracic nerve supplies:', '["Rhomboids","Trapezius","Pectoralis major","Serratus anterior","Latissimus dorsi"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The long thoracic nerve arises from the C5-C7 roots and runs down the midaxillary line to supply the serratus anterior. Damage to it produces winging of the scapula.'),
('e8bb290f-9cc0-5b3a-bf5a-115b6877e194'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The thoracodorsal nerve supplies:', '["Teres major","Pectoralis major","Serratus anterior","Subscapularis","Latissimus dorsi"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'The thoracodorsal nerve, a branch of the posterior cord, descends with the thoracodorsal artery to supply the latissimus dorsi. Teres major and subscapularis receive the subscapular nerves instead.'),
('2f63b0de-2a5c-5287-ba50-913631a29d29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary lymph nodes receive lymph from all except:', '["Thoracic wall","Upper limb","Thumb side of hand","Breast","Lower limb"]'::jsonb, 'Lower limb', 'The axillary nodes drain the upper limb, breast, and upper thoracic wall. Lymph from the lower limb drains to the superficial inguinal and external iliac nodes rather than the axilla.'),
('90a942df-8058-5f84-9c97-6a9e4ce5ac55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial wall of axilla is formed by:', '["Deltoid","Subscapularis","Latissimus dorsi","Serratus anterior","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The serratus anterior covering the upper ribs and intercostal muscles forms the medial wall of the axilla. Subscapularis and latissimus dorsi belong to the posterior wall, while pectoralis major is part of the anterior wall.'),
('933f5213-f034-5c8e-9e88-3f500deb72a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The posterior wall of axilla is formed by all except:', '["Subscapularis","Coracobrachialis","Pectoralis minor","Latissimus dorsi","Teres major"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis minor', 'The posterior wall of the axilla is formed by the subscapularis, teres major, and latissimus dorsi. Pectoralis minor belongs to the anterior wall, lying deep to pectoralis major.'),
('61db7831-2711-5d08-828e-3629deb641f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary sheath contains:', '["Brachial plexus only","Axillary vein and artery","Axillary artery and brachial plexus","Axillary artery only","All axillary vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary artery and brachial plexus', 'The axillary sheath is a fascial sleeve continuous with the prevertebral fascia that encloses the axillary artery and the cords of the brachial plexus. The axillary vein runs within the sheath as well but is not the sole or defining content.'),
('a4e8a31f-a477-56b3-8b60-402f27495bab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The suprascapular nerve is derived from:', '["Posterior cord","Upper trunk","Lateral cord","Lower trunk","Medial cord"]'::jsonb, 'Upper trunk', 'The suprascapular nerve is a branch of the upper trunk of the brachial plexus carrying C5-C6 fibers. It leaves the plexus at the trunk level and supplies the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles.'),
('a1a03a4f-4f02-5f6e-8711-6f0dea25c987'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavipectoral fascia extends from:', '["Sternum to humerus","Clavicle to axillary fascia","First rib to coracoid process","Scapula to clavicle","Clavicle to pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'First rib to coracoid process', 'The clavipectoral fascia lies deep to the clavicular head of pectoralis major, and its thickened costocoracoid part stretches between the first rib and the coracoid process. This fascia is pierced by the cephalic vein, lateral pectoral nerve, and thoracoacromial artery.'),
('ba28e219-02dc-5776-af6a-ef617d199148'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The cephalic vein pierces which fascia to join axillary vein?', '["Pectoral fascia","Brachial fascia","Deep fascia","Axillary sheath","Clavipectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Clavipectoral fascia', 'The cephalic vein runs in the deltopectoral groove and pierces the clavipectoral fascia to join the axillary vein within the axilla. The lateral pectoral nerve and thoracoacromial artery traverse this fascia as well.'),
('fc1871f8-9856-594d-814c-99eb0186fcb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial pectoral nerve supplies:', '["Serratus anterior","Both A (Serratus anterior) and B (Both A and B (as above))","Subclavius","Pectoralis minor","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis minor', 'The medial pectoral nerve arises from the medial cord, pierces the pectoralis minor, and supplies it as well as the sternocostal part of pectoralis major. Serratus anterior receives the long thoracic nerve, not the medial pectoral nerve.'),
('79dde87f-2fba-55d3-8a62-ee13fc505646'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which artery is in the first part of axillary artery?', '["Anterior circumflex","Thoracoacromial","Subscapular","Superior thoracic","Lateral thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thoracic', 'The superior thoracic artery is the only branch of the first part of the axillary artery. The thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries arise from the second part, and the subscapular plus circumflex humeral arteries from the third part.'),
('ee9f4d62-f2bc-5a99-b4eb-e93024cea8ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subscapular artery is the largest branch of which artery?', '["Subclavian","Lateral thoracic","Axillary","Thoracoacromial","Brachial"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary', 'The subscapular artery is the largest branch of the axillary artery, arising from its third part. It divides into the circumflex scapular and thoracodorsal arteries.'),
('f477d4a1-205e-5086-abf2-0311cb7189a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The thoracoacromial artery pierces:', '["Pectoral fascia","Brachial fascia","Axillary sheath","Deep fascia","Clavipectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Clavipectoral fascia', 'The thoracoacromial artery arises from the second part of the axillary artery and pierces the clavipectoral fascia. It then divides into acromial, deltoid, pectoral, and clavicular branches.'),
('0374ea17-e152-58f4-969b-1c233707d140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The intercostobrachial nerve is the lateral cutaneous branch of:', '["T3","T2","T1","C8","T4"]'::jsonb, 'T2', 'The intercostobrachial nerve is the lateral cutaneous branch of the second intercostal nerve (T2). It crosses the axilla to supply the skin of the upper medial arm and may be injured during axillary lymph node dissection.'),
('7b144b81-a88c-5620-8ffc-0c78df921456'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which lymph node group is most commonly involved cancer?', '["Subscapular","Lateral","Central","Pectoral","Apical"]'::jsonb, 'Central', 'Among the axillary node groups, the central group in the middle of the axilla is the most frequently involved in breast carcinoma. From it, tumor spreads to the apical group and then to the supraclavicular nodes.'),
('bf091450-8288-5c8f-9fbd-058cf773d3d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subclavian artery becomes axillary artery at the outer border of:', '["First rib","Clavicle","Second rib","Scapula","Sternum"]'::jsonb, 'First rib', 'The subclavian artery becomes the axillary artery at the outer border of the first rib. It continues as the brachial artery at the lower border of teres major.'),
('68a37242-3187-5d8c-be7f-7a27bc43b5a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The acromioclavicular joint is what type?', '["Pivot","Synovial plane","Hinge","Saddle","Ball and socket"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial plane', 'The acromioclavicular joint is a synovial plane (gliding) joint between the lateral end of the clavicle and the acromion. It is reinforced by the acromioclavicular and coracoclavicular ligaments.'),
('a3021df4-accc-54e7-8285-ab2b1abee5f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracoclavicular ligament consists of:', '["Anterior and posterior","Superior and inferior","Conoid and trapezoid","Deltoid and coracoid","Medial and lateral"]'::jsonb, 'Conoid and trapezoid', 'The coracoclavicular ligament, the strongest connection between the clavicle and scapula, is composed of the conoid ligament medially and the trapezoid ligament laterally. It suspends the scapula from the clavicle and is important in shoulder stability.'),
('e5632ed9-d0ed-5f28-b7ef-21b39daf163b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The conoid ligament attaches to:', '["Coracoid process","Superior angle","Acromion","Glenoid rim","Conoid tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Conoid tubercle', 'The conoid ligament runs from the coracoid process to the conoid tubercle on the inferior surface of the lateral third of the clavicle. The trapezoid ligament attaches to the adjacent trapezoid line.'),
('366efdc1-83c1-57fb-b9ce-854bbecb15ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The transverse scapular ligament bridges:', '["Coracoid process","Spinoglenoid notch","Acromion","Suprascapular notch","Glenoid cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular notch', 'The superior transverse scapular ligament bridges the suprascapular notch, converting it into a foramen. The suprascapular nerve passes beneath it, while the suprascapular artery and vein pass above.'),
('aa25ec7c-37c3-54b1-b45e-3b1354de6183'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The suprascapular nerve passes:', '["Below the transverse scapular ligament","Through acromion","Through the coracoid","Above the transverse scapular ligament","Through the glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Below the transverse scapular ligament', 'The suprascapular nerve passes through the suprascapular notch below the superior transverse scapular ligament. The suprascapular artery and vein run above the ligament in contrast.'),
('0afc0c1c-3a54-5f85-93ee-e03b16025a86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nerve supplies all except:', '["Skin of regimental badge area","Teres minor","Short head of biceps","Long head of triceps","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Short head of biceps', 'The axillary nerve supplies deltoid, teres minor, and the upper lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm (regimental badge area). The short head of biceps is supplied by the musculocutaneous nerve, not the axillary nerve.'),
('8f12fd18-2161-559c-9384-7c26d02bb1de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The long thoracic nerve roots are:', '["6","C5","None of the above","7","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'C5', 'The long thoracic nerve arises from the C5, C6, and C7 roots, so C5 is one of its contributing roots. It descends along the midaxillary line to supply the serratus anterior.'),
('050caec8-de1e-5c93-989c-4cc08c1647e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial cord of brachial plexus is formed by:', '["T1","6","C5","C8","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'C8', 'The medial cord is formed by the anterior division of the lower trunk, which carries fibers from C8 and T1. It gives rise to the ulnar nerve, medial root of the median nerve, and medial pectoral nerve.'),
('3408df82-ee61-57eb-9295-3447160a7807'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral cord of brachial plexus is formed by:', '["6","C5","7","None of the above","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'C5', 'The lateral cord is formed by the anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks, contributing C5, C6, and C7 fibers. C5 is one of the roots that contributes to this cord.'),
('4ad1b97b-8ea7-5e9e-b4c0-4db09239b067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The posterior cord of brachial plexus is formed by:', '["C5","C5-8","None of the above","6","T1"]'::jsonb, 'C5-8', 'The posterior cord is formed by the posterior divisions of all three trunks, carrying fibers from roots C5 through C8 (with T1 contributing via the lower trunk). It gives rise to the axillary and radial nerves.'),
('62d842a9-47ae-5f96-a884-cda9539771a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nerve is derived from which cord?', '["Lateral cord","Posterior cord","Lower trunk","Upper trunk","Medial cord"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The axillary nerve is the smaller terminal branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. It passes through the quadrangular space to supply the deltoid and teres minor.'),
('9cf06b41-15a0-5d47-be57-2bf36a39da3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The radial nerve is derived from which cord?', '["Lateral cord","Upper trunk","Medial cord","Posterior cord","Lower trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The radial nerve is the largest terminal branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. It winds around the humerus in the spiral groove and supplies the extensor compartments of the arm and forearm.'),
('fe61615f-b921-58a9-9d8b-8bce1373e33b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The musculocutaneous nerve is derived from which cord?', '["Posterior cord","Medial cord","Lower trunk","Upper trunk","Lateral cord"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cord', 'The musculocutaneous nerve is a terminal branch of the lateral cord carrying C5-C7 fibers. It pierces the coracobrachialis and continues as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm.'),
('0a0bdf4c-b5bd-50d1-9353-9d067a18fc2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The median nerve is formed by which cords?', '["Lateral only","All three cords","Lateral and posterior","Medial and posterior","Lateral and medial"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and medial', 'The median nerve is formed by the union of the lateral root from the lateral cord and the medial root from the medial cord. The two roots join in front of the third part of the axillary artery.'),
('79a74501-1db7-5a6b-b368-36589c6192d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The ulnar nerve is derived from which cord?', '["Upper trunk","Lateral cord","Lateral and medial","Medial cord","Posterior cord"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cord', 'The ulnar nerve is the largest terminal branch of the medial cord, carrying C8-T1 fibers. It descends on the medial side of the arm and enters the forearm behind the medial epicondyle.'),
('41d9b235-505a-51a4-b75c-5286de20460f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The nerve to subclavius is from:', '["Lower trunk","Posterior cord","Lateral cord","Upper trunk","Medial cord"]'::jsonb, 'Upper trunk', 'The nerve to subclavius arises from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus (C5-C6). It descends in front of the subclavian vessels and supplies the subclavius muscle.'),
('5b368bb6-c6b0-5343-b16b-5258e3f3159a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral pectoral nerve arises from:', '["Posterior cord","Lateral cord","Lower trunk","Medial cord","Upper trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cord', 'The lateral pectoral nerve arises from the lateral cord of the brachial plexus (C5-C7). It pierces the clavipectoral fascia to supply the clavicular head of pectoralis major.'),
('3a54e6e3-696a-5f59-919c-fd19051d3ff4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The upper subscapular nerve arises from:', '["Upper trunk","Posterior cord","Medial cord","Lower trunk","Lateral cord"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The upper subscapular nerve arises directly from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. It supplies the upper part of the subscapularis muscle.'),
('5fa2070d-2d2f-5650-9165-51d9018ad99b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lower subscapular nerve arises from:', '["Lower trunk","Posterior cord","Upper trunk","Lateral cord","Medial cord"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The lower subscapular nerve arises from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus. It supplies the lower part of the subscapularis and also the teres major.'),
('576ea29b-23ac-5ad0-85a6-9fb7a634e345'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nerve leaves the axilla through:', '["Triangular space","Olecranon fossa","Quadrangular space","Suprascapular notch","Clavipectoral triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Quadrangular space', 'The axillary nerve passes out of the axilla through the quadrangular space along with the posterior circumflex humeral artery and vein. The space is bounded by teres minor, teres major, the long head of triceps, and the surgical neck of the humerus.'),
('83fbe03a-8306-531a-a4c8-9c0c15833b17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The quadrangular space transmits all except:', '["Radial nerve","All of the above","Posterior circumflex humeral artery","Axillary nerve","Posterior circumflex humeral vein"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'The quadrangular space transmits the axillary nerve, the posterior circumflex humeral artery, and the posterior circumflex humeral vein. The radial nerve and profunda brachii artery pass through the lower triangular space instead.'),
('7b23f0a7-dc10-5af4-80f3-7db966b1f162'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The sternoclavicular joint has an intra-articular disc that is:', '["Fibrocartilaginous","Complete","Present only in some","Incomplete","Bilateral"]'::jsonb, 'Complete', 'The sternoclavicular joint contains a complete fibrocartilaginous intra-articular disc that divides the joint cavity into two compartments. The disc, together with the strong capsular and costoclavicular ligaments, provides stability to the joint.'),
('95719883-4fbf-5b74-96e0-1018d9496169'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subclavius muscle is supplied by:', '["Nerve to subclavius","Medial pectoral nerve","Long thoracic","Suprascapular nerve","Lateral pectoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to subclavius', 'The subclavius receives its motor supply from the nerve to subclavius (C5, C6), a branch of the upper trunk of the brachial plexus. The pectoral nerves supply the pectoral muscles, the long thoracic nerve reaches serratus anterior, and the suprascapular nerve reaches the supraspinatus and infraspinatus.'),
('2a822c27-c692-552c-a550-6200a852f78c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subclavius muscle is innervated by:', '["C5","C6 and C7","C5 and C6","C6","C7"]'::jsonb, 'C5 and C6', 'The nerve to subclavius derives its fibers from the C5 and C6 cervical roots, typically through the upper trunk of the brachial plexus. The other root combinations listed do not provide this muscle''s innervation.'),
('9aad5a8e-c32f-54c3-baec-189225e48be0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary lymph nodes are arranged in how many groups?', '["6","5","4","3","2"]'::jsonb, '5', 'The axillary lymph nodes are organized into five groups: pectoral, humeral (lateral), subscapular (posterior), central, and apical. The other numbers understate or overstate the recognized number of groups.'),
('99de76cd-1638-50c2-9aad-270aaf70c698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The apical group of axillary lymph nodes is located at:', '["Medial wall","Lateral wall","Posterior wall","Apex of axilla","Central part"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of axilla', 'The apical group lies at the apex of the axilla near the first rib and is the final station for lymph passing from the other axillary groups. The medial, lateral, and posterior walls and the central part house different named groups.'),
('832805ce-2dd6-5790-9bef-4bdefabed185'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The deltopectoral triangle contains:', '["Cephalic vein","Median nerve","Axillary nerve","Brachial artery","Basilic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic vein', 'The deltopectoral groove contains the cephalic vein, which perforates the clavipectoral fascia to join the axillary vein. The median nerve, axillary nerve, and brachial artery lie deep inside the axilla, and the basilic vein ascends on the medial side of the arm.'),
('2352b619-4aff-512e-a1f3-f845e31234ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The cephalic vein ascends in the deltopectoral groove between which muscles?', '["Pectoralis major and minor","Deltoid and trapezius","Deltoid and biceps","Deltoid and pectoralis major","Serratus anterior and pectoralis"]'::jsonb, 'Deltoid and pectoralis major', 'The cephalic vein follows the superficial deltopectoral groove between the deltoid and the clavicular head of pectoralis major. Pectoralis minor and serratus anterior border deeper planes, and the trapezius lies in the neck and back.'),
('b7eeb2e6-566e-5ff9-9baf-22c6552f927e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The basilic vein pierces which fascia?', '["Axillary sheath","Deep fascia of arm","Clavipectoral fascia","Brachial fascia","Pectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial fascia', 'The basilic vein pierces the brachial fascia at about mid-arm to join the deep venous system. The axillary sheath invests the axillary vessels, the clavipectoral fascia encloses the pectoralis minor and subclavius, and the pectoral fascia covers pectoralis major.'),
('b5e9e470-b10a-5ad2-a749-2be0ad915025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The thoracoacromial artery divides into how many branches?', '["6","3","5","4","2"]'::jsonb, '4', 'The thoracoacromial artery divides into four branches: pectoral, deltoid, clavicular, and acromial. The other numerical choices do not match this classic branching pattern.'),
('abfc06e3-ab38-5366-ada5-58b750258ff7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The acromial branch of thoracoacromial artery anastomoses with:', '["Posterior circumflex humeral","Anterior circumflex humeral","Lateral thoracic","Suprascapular artery","Subscapular"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular artery', 'The acromial branch of the thoracoacromial artery runs toward the acromion and joins the acromial branch of the suprascapular artery. The circumflex humeral, lateral thoracic, and subscapular arteries supply different regions of the shoulder.'),
('5352c8b5-81ba-5903-9988-8ae6916c792f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pectoral branch of thoracoacromial artery lies between:', '["Serratus anterior and pectoralis","Pectoralis major and minor","Clavicle and pectoralis minor","None","Pectoralis major and skin"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis major and minor', 'The pectoral branch of the thoracoacromial artery descends between the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor to reach both muscles. It does not run between serratus anterior and pectoralis, between the clavicle and pectoralis minor, or in the superficial plane.'),
('3c5af018-b8b8-5dc6-be91-8c68408c7399'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lymphatic drainage of the pectoral region goes primarily to:', '["Pectoral group","Central group","Apical group","Subscapular group","Lateral group"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoral group', 'Lymph from the breast and pectoral region drains chiefly into the pectoral (anterior) group of axillary nodes along the lateral thoracic vessels. The central and apical groups receive lymph later in the drainage sequence, while the subscapular and lateral groups drain other territories.'),
('9143020f-c918-58f4-ad4e-6503552a312b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial supraclavicular nerves supply skin over:', '["Deltoid region resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral arm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper part of pectoral region","Back of neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Face leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper part of pectoral region', 'The medial supraclavicular nerves descend from the cervical plexus across the clavicle to supply skin over the infraclavicular (upper pectoral) region. The deltoid region receives the lateral supraclavicular nerve, and the other listed areas are supplied by different cutaneous nerves.'),
('24dca38b-adb2-59f6-b810-cfabbb43224b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The intercostobrachial nerve communicates with:', '["Lateral cutaneous nerve of arm","Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Medial cutaneous nerve of arm","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cutaneous nerve of arm', 'The intercostobrachial nerve, a branch of the second intercostal nerve, joins the medial cutaneous nerve of the arm to supply skin over the axilla and medial upper arm. The ulnar, median, and radial nerves do not form this cutaneous communication.'),
('d2ec0320-5737-5cdb-bb87-530b72f239a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary vein is formed at the lower border of which muscle?', '["Deltoid","Latissimus dorsi","Pectoralis minor","Teres major","Subscapularis"]'::jsonb, 'Teres major', 'The axillary vein forms at the lower border of teres major by the union of the basilic and brachial veins. The other muscles listed relate to different parts of the axillary boundary.'),
('3652c8e2-0174-5b3b-a0f8-a5f463f7c3ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary vein ends at the outer border of first rib where it becomes:', '["Brachiocephalic vein","Internal jugular vein","Subclavian vein","Innominate vein","Superior vena cava"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian vein', 'At the outer border of the first rib the axillary vein becomes the subclavian vein. The brachiocephalic (innominate) vein is formed behind the sternoclavicular joint by the union of the subclavian and internal jugular veins, and the superior vena cava lies more centrally.'),
('2872e5b8-045a-57d2-958b-566f9a019207'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The anterior wall of axilla has how many layers?', '["4","3","5","2","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'The anterior wall of the axilla has three layers: skin with superficial fascia, deep fascia covered by pectoralis major, and clavipectoral fascia enclosing pectoralis minor and subclavius. The other numbers do not represent the accepted anatomy.'),
('4c2ca0f7-71c5-58fe-985a-9020c8c0242e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavipectoral fascia splits to enclose which muscle?', '["Serratus anterior","Pectoralis minor","Coracobrachialis","Subclavius","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis minor', 'The clavipectoral fascia splits to enclose the pectoralis minor and also surrounds the subclavius. Serratus anterior, coracobrachialis, and pectoralis major do not lie within this fascial sheath.'),
('a31fd047-19cb-52e2-98af-2627d526c471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary fascia is continuous with the:', '["Superficial fascia","None","Brachial fascia only at elbow","Deep fascia of arm (brachial fascia)","Pectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia of arm (brachial fascia)', 'The axillary fascia is continuous inferiorly with the deep fascia of the arm (brachial fascia). The superficial fascia and pectoral fascia lie in different planes, and this continuity is not limited to the elbow.'),
('e8308cae-9ae9-5a70-89e0-ba1872a384bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subscapular artery gives off which branch?', '["Superior thoracic","Circumflex scapular","Thoracoacromial","Lateral thoracic","Anterior circumflex humeral"]'::jsonb, 'Circumflex scapular', 'The subscapular artery, a branch of the third part of the axillary artery, gives off the circumflex scapular artery and the thoracodorsal artery. The superior thoracic, thoracoacromial, lateral thoracic, and anterior circumflex humeral arteries arise directly from the axillary artery at other levels.'),
('2f0ffe48-36bc-50ef-b5ef-47174120f6b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The circumflex scapular artery passes through which space?', '["Quadrangular space","Clavipectoral triangle","Suprascapular notch","Triangular space","Olecranon fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Triangular space', 'The circumflex scapular artery passes through the triangular space, bounded by teres major, teres minor, and the long head of triceps. The quadrangular space transmits the axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral vessels, and the other listed openings carry different structures.'),
('3c645a01-45aa-5241-906b-0bccfd6a7e0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The dorsal scapular nerve supplies:', '["Supraspinatus resulting in decreased functional activity","Infraspinatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rhomboid major and minor mediated by specific receptor activation","Levator scapulae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both A (Supraspinatus) and B (Infraspinatus)"]'::jsonb, 'Both A (Supraspinatus) and B (Infraspinatus)', 'The dorsal scapular nerve (C5) innervates the rhomboid major and rhomboid minor muscles and variably the levator scapulae. Supraspinatus and infraspinatus are supplied by the suprascapular nerve, contrary to the pairing seen in this question.'),
('2015bd61-726a-5b9a-af35-71fa4aa098a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The suprascapular nerve supplies:', '["None","Rhomboids","Supraspinatus and infraspinatus","Subscapularis and teres major","Deltoid and teres minor"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinatus and infraspinatus', 'The suprascapular nerve supplies the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles and also the glenohumeral and acromioclavicular joints. Rhomboids, subscapularis with teres major, and deltoid with teres minor receive branches from other nerves.'),
('12c80c55-4a3c-5c81-82f9-8dd90e14a8fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The nerve to subclavius also supplies:', '["Clavicle","Acromioclavicular joint","Scapula","Sternoclavicular joint","Shoulder joint"]'::jsonb, 'Acromioclavicular joint', 'The nerve to subclavius regularly sends a branch to the acromioclavicular joint. The clavicle, scapula, and the sternoclavicular and glenohumeral joints receive innervation from other nerves.'),
('06c2f0b9-844c-5dbf-9188-08da4a5efb36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral cord gives rise to which two terminal nerves?', '["None","Medial pectoral and medial cutaneous","Median and ulnar","Radial and axillary","Musculocutaneous and lateral root of median"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous and lateral root of median', 'The lateral cord of the brachial plexus terminates by dividing into the musculocutaneous nerve and the lateral root of the median nerve. The medial pectoral and medial cutaneous nerves and the ulnar nerve arise from the medial cord, and the radial and axillary nerves come from the posterior cord.'),
('e3b8a974-c2c5-5e39-9d87-1d46d6faefaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial cord gives rise to all except:', '["Medial root of median","Ulnar","Medial cutaneous of forearm","Musculocutaneous","Medial cutaneous nerve of arm"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous', 'The musculocutaneous nerve arises from the lateral cord rather than the medial cord. The medial cord gives rise to the medial root of the median nerve, the ulnar nerve, and the medial cutaneous nerves of the arm and forearm.'),
('df04d2a8-d919-5d95-9887-d5747363e5c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The posterior cord gives rise to:', '["Musculocutaneous and median","Radial","thoracodorsal","upper and lower subscapular","axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Radial', 'The posterior cord gives rise to the radial and axillary nerves, the thoracodorsal nerve, and the upper and lower subscapular nerves. The combination ''musculocutaneous and median'' arises from the lateral and medial cords rather than the posterior cord.'),
('0c3c503c-e4c0-54e0-9a12-2271ce50d0ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The thoracodorsal nerve arises from which cord?', '["Posterior cord","Upper trunk","Medial cord","Lateral cord","Lower trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cord', 'The thoracodorsal nerve arises from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus and innervates latissimus dorsi. The trunks and the medial and lateral cords give off different named branches.'),
('82db1c15-a187-5cb1-a12f-9c73b832ee92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the arm arises from:', '["Medial cord","Lateral cord","Upper trunk","Posterior cord","Lower trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cord', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the arm is a branch of the medial cord of the brachial plexus. The lateral cord, posterior cord, and brachial plexus trunks give rise to other named nerves.'),
('2e3246ba-a9ff-5769-8138-addc685288aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm arises from:', '["Lateral cord","Lower trunk","Posterior cord","Medial cord","Upper trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cord', 'The medial cutaneous nerve of the forearm arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus. The lateral cord, posterior cord, and trunks do not give rise to this nerve.'),
('3d89858a-9b82-5195-ab6d-21144d8b81ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nerve and posterior circumflex humeral artery pass through:', '["Quadrangular space","Triangular space","Clavipectoral triangle","Triangular interval","Suprascapular notch"]'::jsonb, 'Quadrangular space', 'The axillary nerve and the posterior circumflex humeral artery pass together through the quadrangular space, bounded by teres minor, teres major, the long head of triceps, and the surgical neck of the humerus. The triangular space transmits the circumflex scapular vessels, and the other listed openings carry different structures.'),
('1364d6d7-1ef3-5e06-822d-f5e688b6089f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The boundaries of quadrangular space are all except:', '["Teres minor","Teres major","Long head of triceps","Supinator","Surgical neck of humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Teres major', 'The quadrangular space is bounded by teres minor, teres major, the long head of triceps, and the surgical neck of the humerus. The supinator lies at the elbow and is not a boundary of this space.'),
('5041444b-0edf-531b-84cc-e8edbb1a6806'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavicle is derived from which arch?', '["Fourth arch","Second arch","Third arch","First branchial arch","Not from arch"]'::jsonb, 'First branchial arch', 'The clavicle develops as a membranous bone derived from the first branchial arch. The second, third, and fourth arches give rise to other structures of the neck and face.'),
('223af68b-a830-54b8-b867-9a1b0ff043bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavicle is the first bone to ossify at:', '["3rd month","5th week of IUL","8th week","At birth","6th week"]'::jsonb, '5th week of IUL', 'The clavicle is the first bone to begin ossification, with its primary center appearing about the fifth week of intrauterine life. The other listed times are later than the true onset or are otherwise inaccurate.'),
('5ac2960e-68e9-5817-be9a-ce82d09f47d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavicle has how many ossification centers?', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 primary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple mediated by specific receptor activation","1 primary and 1 secondary","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1 primary and 1 secondary', 'The clavicle ossifies from one primary center in the shaft and one secondary center at the medial (sternal) end. The other listed arrangements do not describe this two-part pattern.'),
('afaa02bd-2b30-5e2c-8cf7-cc1d2fd835be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial end of clavicle ossifies from:', '["Both","Third center","Primary center","Secondary center","None"]'::jsonb, 'Primary center', 'The medial (sternal) end of the clavicle ossifies from the secondary center, which appears around 18-20 years of age. The primary center forms the shaft during early fetal life.'),
('8a1d5be9-2597-55f7-ad98-48386f8d1c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The acromion process ossifies from:', '["Two centers","Multiple centers","None","Single center","Ectopic"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple centers', 'The acromion process generally ossifies from multiple centers that appear around puberty and fuse in early adulthood. A single center, a strictly two-center arrangement, or ectopic centers do not describe this pattern.'),
('48c03a39-d79b-57e3-a2a6-b3c41bc3a6ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subscapularis muscle inserts into:', '["Anatomical neck","Lesser tuberosity of humerus","Intertubercular sulcus","Greater tuberosity","Surgical neck"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser tuberosity of humerus', 'Subscapularis inserts on the lesser tuberosity of the humerus. Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, and teres minor attach to the greater tuberosity, and the anatomical and surgical necks are not insertion sites for this muscle.'),
('82e94aec-d945-5ce5-a283-08539708700d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The supraspinatus inserts into:', '["Lesser tuberosity","Inferior facet","Anatomical neck","Middle facet","Superior facet of greater tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Superior facet of greater tuberosity', 'Supraspinatus inserts on the superior facet of the greater tuberosity. Infraspinatus and teres minor attach to the middle and inferior facets respectively, and the lesser tuberosity receives subscapularis.'),
('75ec00c6-b3cc-5e63-9a4c-58922c2ed086'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The infraspinatus inserts into:', '["Superior facet","Middle facet of greater tuberosity","Lesser tuberosity","Inferior facet","Surgical neck"]'::jsonb, 'Middle facet of greater tuberosity', 'Infraspinatus inserts on the middle facet of the greater tuberosity. The superior and inferior facets receive supraspinatus and teres minor, and the lesser tuberosity receives subscapularis.'),
('29c712c3-c2c5-5c20-aae1-f65bd626780f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The teres minor inserts into:', '["Inferior facet of greater tuberosity","Middle facet","Lesser tuberosity","Superior facet","Anatomical neck"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior facet of greater tuberosity', 'Teres minor inserts on the inferior facet of the greater tuberosity. Supraspinatus and infraspinatus occupy the superior and middle facets, and subscapularis attaches to the lesser tuberosity.'),
('45461a44-9c66-5f28-a539-08b2951fd695'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The rotator cuff muscles include all except:', '["Teres major","Infraspinatus","Subscapularis","Teres minor","Supraspinatus"]'::jsonb, 'Teres major', 'The rotator cuff comprises supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis. Teres major is not part of the cuff; it defines the lower border of the axilla.'),
('560fd842-65fc-5c2e-8f3d-8a465e39e596'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The rotator cuff is also known as:', '["Coracohumeral ligament","Musculotendinous cuff","Transverse ligament","Fibrous capsule","Glenohumeral ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Musculotendinous cuff', 'The conjoined tendons of the four rotator cuff muscles form the musculotendinous cuff around the glenohumeral joint. The coracohumeral, transverse humeral, and glenohumeral ligaments and the fibrous capsule are separate structures.'),
('19c0211f-ba91-5c76-bcef-0c82a3aaee71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which rotator cuff muscle is most commonly torn?', '["None","Supraspinatus","Teres minor","Infraspinatus","Subscapularis"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinatus', 'Supraspinatus is the rotator cuff muscle most often torn, particularly in middle-aged individuals during overhead or loading activities. Teres minor, infraspinatus, and subscapularis are injured less frequently.'),
('6bca6174-505a-5568-8c42-6d4aea312659'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The shoulder joint is what type of joint?', '["Saddle","Ellipsoid","Pivot","Ball and socket","Hinge"]'::jsonb, 'Ball and socket', 'The glenohumeral joint is a ball-and-socket (multiaxial synovial) joint between the humeral head and the shallow glenoid fossa. Hinge, pivot, saddle, and ellipsoid mechanics apply to other joints.'),
('4d5768ad-769c-5606-ae3b-1f6c5f2bd9e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The glenoid labrum is composed of:', '["Fibrocartilage","Fibrous tissue","Elastic cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Dense connective tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrocartilage', 'The glenoid labrum is a rim of fibrocartilage that deepens the glenoid fossa. Hyaline cartilage covers the articular surfaces, but the labrum itself is fibrocartilaginous.'),
('1738a2f7-a27d-5f49-819d-b6fb784c65d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The tendon of which muscle passes through the intertubercular sulcus?', '["Long head of biceps","Short head of triceps","Long head of triceps","Short head of biceps","Coracobrachialis"]'::jsonb, 'Long head of biceps', 'The tendon of the long head of biceps brachii runs through the intertubercular (bicipital) groove of the humerus to its origin on the supraglenoid tubercle. The other listed tendons do not traverse this groove.'),
('ad7124c9-b808-5ade-b13e-1593faa0e347'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subacromial bursa lies between:', '["Coracoid and capsule","Acromion and supraspinatus tendon","Glenoid and labrum","None","Deltoid and humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Acromion and supraspinatus tendon', 'The subacromial bursa lies between the acromion and coracoacromial ligament above and the supraspinatus tendon below. The other pairs of structures named do not define the surfaces of this bursa.'),
('8d28182c-e85e-5ac1-8156-4f1fc1e0e048'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracoacromial ligament forms which arch?', '["Coracoacromial arch","Acromial arch","Coracoid arch","Glenoid arch","None"]'::jsonb, 'Coracoacromial arch', 'The coracoacromial ligament, running between the coracoid process and the acromion, forms the coracoacromial arch over the glenohumeral joint. None of the other arch names apply to this ligament.'),
('9cbc69b8-706d-597e-a07f-e68bafcb2e80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The shoulder joint is most unstable in which direction?', '["Superiorly","Anteriorly","Laterally","Posteriorly","Inferiorly"]'::jsonb, 'Inferiorly', 'The glenohumeral joint is most unstable inferiorly because the inferior capsule is thin and unsupported by muscles or ligaments. The superior, anterior, lateral, and posterior aspects are reinforced by the rotator cuff and glenohumeral ligaments.'),
('54f2412a-a870-570f-9798-b469d6cad8ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The glenohumeral ligaments are thickenings of:', '["Synovial membrane","Labrum","Tendon","Fibrous capsule","Hyaline cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous capsule', 'The glenohumeral ligaments are localized thickenings of the fibrous capsule of the shoulder joint. The synovial membrane lines the capsule internally, while the labrum, tendons, and articular cartilage are separate structures.'),
('9a3f36a9-5e40-551a-a68b-fc6dbe6d2c5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which muscle prevents downward dislocation of shoulder during arm weight bearing?', '["Subscapularis","Long head of triceps","Deltoid","Coracobrachialis","Supraspinatus"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinatus', 'During arm weight bearing, the supraspinatus tendon together with the coracoacromial arch resists the downward pull on the humeral head and prevents inferior dislocation of the shoulder. The subscapularis, deltoid, coracobrachialis, and long head of triceps do not specifically guard the humeral head against downward displacement.'),
('ca6d2d6b-92e4-5ee8-8b86-14669d0f58a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nerve is at risk in fracture of:', '["Anatomical neck","Surgical neck of humerus","Lateral epicondyle","Shaft","Medial epicondyle"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical neck of humerus', 'The axillary nerve winds around the surgical neck of the humerus through the quadrangular space, so a fracture at this site can injure it and cause paralysis of the deltoid. The circumflex humeral vessels are endangered at the anatomical neck, while the epicondyles and shaft lie outside the course of this nerve.'),
('04abe7b5-89c9-568a-bb4d-804dc916584b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The acromioclavicular joint is supplied by which nerve?', '["Medial pectoral","Nerve to subclavius","Suprascapular nerve","Axillary nerve","Lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular nerve', 'Articular branches of the suprascapular nerve, along with the lateral pectoral nerve, innervate the acromioclavicular joint. The nerve to subclavius runs to the subclavius muscle and does not supply this joint, and the medial pectoral and axillary nerves innervate other structures.'),
('1e46591b-a7a1-5254-9061-afaa8be90018'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavipectoral fascia is pierced by all except:', '["Cephalic vein","Lymphatics","Medial pectoral nerve","Basilic vein","Thoracoacromial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Basilic vein', 'The clavipectoral fascia is pierced by the cephalic vein, lymphatics, the lateral and medial pectoral nerves, and branches of the thoracoacromial artery. The basilic vein ascends on the medial side of the arm and drains into the axillary vein without passing through this fascia, making it the exception.'),
('851aabb0-0f14-51de-960d-9707ca6ab801'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The costocoracoid ligament is a thickening of:', '["Axillary sheath","Clavipectoral fascia","Sternoclavicular ligament","Pectoral fascia","Brachial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Clavipectoral fascia', 'The costocoracoid ligament is the thickened band of the clavipectoral fascia that spans between the coracoid process and the first costal cartilage. The axillary sheath, sternoclavicular ligament, and pectoral and brachial fasciae are separate structures that do not form this thickening.'),
('870b34e6-9354-516d-bd15-c44c9879b240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The suspensory ligament of axilla is also called:', '["Brachial fascia","Axillary fascia","None","Clavipectoral fascia","Costocoracoid ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Clavipectoral fascia', 'The suspensory ligament of the axilla is an inferior band-like extension of the clavipectoral fascia that attaches to the axillary fascia in the floor of the axilla, maintaining the axillary hollow. The costocoracoid ligament is a separate thickening above the pectoralis minor, and the axillary and brachial fasciae are different layers.'),
('6bd1252b-53fc-5bbe-ae5d-ab78dc0863bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The shoulder joint dislocates most commonly in which direction?', '["Laterally","Superiorly","Posteriorly","Anteriorly","Inferiorly"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly', 'The glenohumeral joint is weakest anteriorly, where the capsule receives no reinforcement from the rotator cuff, so most shoulder dislocations are anterior. Posterior and inferior displacements are far less common because of the stronger posterior capsule and the surrounding muscle support.'),
('e00218d8-964e-55f8-841c-ed98e6632340'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'In anterior shoulder dislocation which nerve is most commonly injured?', '["Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous","Radial nerve","Axillary nerve","Median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve', 'In anterior shoulder dislocation, the humeral head presses against the axillary nerve, which passes close to the capsule as it winds around the surgical neck, making it the most commonly injured nerve. The radial, median, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves are not in close relation to the dislocating humeral head.'),
('8f724ac6-541d-5faf-b112-13fde3bb9773'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The deltoid muscle is supplied by which nerve?', '["Accessory nerve","Radial nerve","Circumflex nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Axillary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve', 'The axillary (circumflex) nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor muscles and the skin over the deltoid. The suprascapular, radial, and accessory nerves innervate other muscles of the shoulder and neck region.'),
('1ed51ee5-2725-5272-9c9f-54ba316e45ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pectoralis major is supplied by:', '["Suprascapular","Lateral pectoral only","Long thoracic","Medial pectoral only","Lateral and medial pectoral nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and medial pectoral nerves', 'The pectoralis major receives dual innervation: the clavicular part from the lateral pectoral nerve and the sternocostal part from both the lateral and medial pectoral nerves. The suprascapular and long thoracic nerves supply other muscles and do not innervate this muscle.'),
('f5c9edc7-82b9-5e77-aa2d-66a8cade13f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pectoralis minor is supplied by:', '["Suprascapular","Lateral pectoral nerve","Both","Long thoracic","Medial pectoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial pectoral nerve', 'The pectoralis minor is innervated by the medial pectoral nerve, which pierces the muscle on its way to the pectoralis major. The lateral pectoral nerve mainly supplies the pectoralis major, and the suprascapular and long thoracic nerves innervate other muscles.'),
('b1c594c0-8922-58b5-b638-94bb35466a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary lymph nodes of the pectoral group lie along which artery?', '["Axillary","Subscapular","Thoracoacromial","Lateral thoracic","Superior thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral thoracic', 'The pectoral (anterior) group of axillary lymph nodes lies along the lateral thoracic artery and drains lymph from the breast and the anterolateral chest wall. The subscapular, thoracoacromial, and superior thoracic arteries are associated with other nodal groups.'),
('0b899934-a644-5b84-9912-6067ad04613c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary lymph nodes of the subscapular group lie along which artery?', '["Superior thoracic","Posterior circumflex","Lateral thoracic","Thoracoacromial","Subscapular artery"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapular artery', 'The subscapular (posterior) group of axillary nodes lies along the subscapular artery and its circumflex scapular and thoracodorsal branches, receiving lymph from the posterior trunk wall. The lateral thoracic artery defines the pectoral group, and the other vessels are not related to this group.'),
('85224550-a6a1-553f-9256-26e4f4d6c1b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary lymph nodes of the lateral group lie along which vein?', '["Brachial vein","Subclavian vein","Basilic vein","Cephalic vein","Axillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary vein', 'The lateral (humeral) group of axillary nodes lies on the medial side of the axillary vein and receives lymph from the upper limb. The basilic, cephalic, brachial, and subclavian veins are not the reference vessels for this group.'),
('a215d1e1-ab30-54b5-a560-d0193da09da9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The central group of axillary nodes lies:', '["In the center of axilla","At the apex of axilla","Along pectoralis minor","Medial wall","Along axillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'In the center of axilla', 'The central group lies in the center of the axilla, deep to the pectoralis minor, and collects lymph from the pectoral, subscapular, and lateral groups. The apex, the region along the axillary vein, and the medial wall correspond to the apical, lateral, and other groups.'),
('0a88bce0-7e73-537e-9e49-9b60c737d497'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The apical group of axillary nodes lies:', '["At the center resulting in decreased functional activity","At the apex of axilla above pectoralis minor","Along pectoralis major mediated by specific receptor activation","Below pectoralis minor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the medial wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the apex of axilla above pectoralis minor', 'The apical group lies at the apex of the axilla, above the pectoralis minor along the axillary vein, and is the final drainage site before lymph enters the subclavian trunk. The center, medial wall, and areas below the pectoralis minor correspond to other nodal groups.'),
('a9338a06-043d-5534-bd83-052832aedaba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The deltoid muscle has how many parts?', '["5","3","2","1","4"]'::jsonb, '3', 'The deltoid is divided into three parts: clavicular (anterior), acromial (middle), and spinal (posterior), based on their origins. These three parts converge on the deltoid tuberosity and together produce abduction of the shoulder.'),
('6af1d947-8289-524a-8d04-7fc2c8f3bfd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavicular part of deltoid originates from:', '["Coracoid","Medial third of clavicle","Spine of scapula","Lateral third of clavicle","Acromion"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral third of clavicle', 'The clavicular (anterior) part of the deltoid arises from the lateral third of the clavicle. The acromial and spinal parts arise from the acromion and spine of the scapula respectively, and the coracoid process and medial third of the clavicle are not deltoid attachments.'),
('be9bc532-18da-56bf-aab2-264efb63c424'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The spinal part of deltoid originates from:', '["Superior angle","Acromion","Coracoid","Spine of scapula","Clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Spine of scapula', 'The spinal (posterior) part of the deltoid arises from the lower lip of the spine of the scapula. The anterior and middle parts arise from the lateral third of the clavicle and the acromion respectively, while the superior angle and coracoid process are not sites of deltoid origin.'),
('da35b3e8-b1d4-53cf-91a4-1b0eaa1c0dcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The deltoid inserts into:', '["Greater tuberosity","Surgical neck","Deltoid tuberosity of humerus","Lesser tuberosity","Anatomical neck"]'::jsonb, 'Deltoid tuberosity of humerus', 'The three parts of the deltoid converge to insert on the deltoid tuberosity on the lateral side of the humeral shaft. The greater and lesser tuberosities and the anatomical and surgical necks are attachments of the rotator cuff and other muscles.'),
('074931ac-2191-5ee3-b1de-f62db71c4831'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The supraspinatus muscle is located in:', '["Subscapular fossa","Supraspinous fossa","None","Infraspinous fossa","Coracoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinous fossa', 'The supraspinatus occupies the supraspinous fossa above the spine of the scapula. The infraspinous fossa and subscapular fossa house the infraspinatus and subscapularis muscles respectively, and the coracoid process is not a fossa.'),
('8cd511b7-f846-5fb9-aa84-bd13ca414fd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The infraspinatus muscle is located in:', '["Supraspinous fossa","Coracoid process","Infraspinous fossa","None","Subscapular fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Infraspinous fossa', 'The infraspinatus fills the infraspinous fossa below the spine of the scapula. The supraspinous and subscapular fossae contain the supraspinatus and subscapularis respectively, and the coracoid process is not a fossa.'),
('819e711e-ea44-5d24-b01e-7c45ac33dee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subscapularis muscle is located in:', '["Glenoid cavity","Subscapular fossa","Coracoid process","Infraspinous fossa","Supraspinous fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapular fossa', 'The subscapularis arises from the subscapular fossa on the costal surface of the scapula. The supraspinous and infraspinous fossae and the glenoid cavity do not house this muscle.'),
('970fc886-f276-5ee6-afeb-4a42b3933e31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The teres minor muscle originates from:', '["Superior angle","Lateral border of scapula","Coracoid","Medial border","Inferior angle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral border of scapula', 'The teres minor arises from the upper two-thirds of the lateral border of the scapula. The teres major arises from the inferior angle, and the coracoid process and medial border are not teres minor attachments.'),
('9f7c1ed0-eb63-523c-89e1-d230f1b70ce2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The teres major muscle originates from:', '["Superior angle","Medial border","Lateral border","Coracoid","Inferior angle of scapula"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior angle of scapula', 'The teres major arises from the dorsal aspect of the inferior angle of the scapula. The teres minor arises from the lateral border, and the coracoid process and medial border are not origins of this muscle.'),
('3aa91c25-cdae-5f02-98df-b26d712048d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The latissimus dorsi inserts into:', '["Lesser tuberosity","Floor of intertubercular sulcus","Crest of greater tubercle","Greater tuberosity","None"]'::jsonb, 'Floor of intertubercular sulcus', 'The latissimus dorsi inserts into the floor of the intertubercular (bicipital) groove of the humerus. The pectoralis major inserts into the lateral lip and the teres major into the medial lip of the same groove, so the tuberosities are not its attachment.'),
('5a04880d-1d36-578a-a411-b2250172d5ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pectoralis major inserts into:', '["Floor resulting in decreased functional activity","Lesser tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral lip of intertubercular sulcus","Medial lip leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral lip of intertubercular sulcus', 'The pectoralis major inserts into the lateral lip of the intertubercular (bicipital) sulcus. The teres major occupies the medial lip and the latissimus dorsi the floor of the same groove, so the remaining attachments belong to other muscles.'),
('ec404651-d04f-59af-851c-7353d8eec0f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The teres major inserts into:', '["Greater tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","Floor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial lip of intertubercular sulcus","Lesser tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral lip leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial lip of intertubercular sulcus', 'The teres major inserts into the medial lip of the intertubercular (bicipital) groove. The latissimus dorsi inserts into the floor and the pectoralis major into the lateral lip of the same groove, so the other attachments belong to different muscles.'),
('58c771c6-f66a-56b6-a1b6-53978e766cbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subscapular bursa communicates with:', '["Subdeltoid bursa","None","Acromioclavicular joint","Subacromial bursa","Shoulder joint cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Shoulder joint cavity', 'The subscapular bursa communicates with the cavity of the glenohumeral (shoulder) joint through a defect in the capsule. The subacromial and subdeltoid bursae do not normally communicate with the shoulder joint cavity.'),
('f3367faa-717a-515a-ad6c-f06822362685'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The tendon of long head of biceps is:', '["Extracapsular","Intracapsular","Extra-articular","Pericapsular","Intrasynovial"]'::jsonb, 'Intracapsular', 'The tendon of the long head of the biceps pierces the shoulder joint capsule and runs within the joint to reach the supraglenoid tubercle, so it is intracapsular even though it lies outside the synovial membrane. It is therefore not extracapsular or extra-articular, and it is not separated from the capsule by synovium.'),
('2ac53157-f383-55b4-97e1-ad1f1b268862'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The tendon of long head of biceps arises from:', '["Glenoid labrum","Acromion","Infraglenoid tubercle","Supraglenoid tubercle","Coracoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Supraglenoid tubercle', 'The long head of the biceps arises from the supraglenoid tubercle of the scapula, along with a contribution from the superior glenoid labrum. The infraglenoid tubercle gives origin to the long head of the triceps, and the coracoid process to the short head of the biceps and coracobrachialis.'),
('1cef085d-cb37-5461-b4de-66e3c86534c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The short head of biceps arises from:', '["Acromion","Clavicle","Infraglenoid tubercle","Supraglenoid tubercle","Coracoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Coracoid process', 'The short head of the biceps arises from the coracoid process together with the coracobrachialis. The supraglenoid and infraglenoid tubercles give origin to the long heads of the biceps and triceps respectively, and the acromion and clavicle are not biceps attachments.'),
('c83ce22a-fe26-5c4c-a703-cbf48208c143'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracobrachialis muscle arises from:', '["Clavicle","Acromion","Glenoid cavity","Scapula spine","Coracoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Coracoid process', 'The coracobrachialis arises from the coracoid process together with the short head of the biceps. The clavicle, acromion, spine of the scapula, and glenoid cavity are not sites of origin for this muscle.'),
('134aa24d-7b9c-5bfa-a432-b0963d72ec14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The biceps brachii is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Axillary","Musculocutaneous nerve","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous nerve', 'The musculocutaneous nerve pierces the coracobrachialis and supplies the biceps brachii, coracobrachialis, and brachialis before continuing as the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm. The median, ulnar, radial, and axillary nerves do not supply the biceps.'),
('d2dff7a1-3460-5c9f-87df-00efdebeba8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The brachialis muscle is supplied by:', '["Both musculocutaneous and radial","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Both musculocutaneous and radial', 'The brachialis receives a dual nerve supply: the musculocutaneous nerve innervates its medial part and the radial nerve its lateral part. No single-nerve option accounts for this characteristic dual innervation.'),
('a889881a-4485-5cef-9802-310125aa9077'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary fascia is also called:', '["Superficial fascia","Deep fascia","Bra sheath","Axillary sheath","Clavipectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Bra sheath', 'The axillary fascia is the deep fascia that forms the floor of the axilla and is also referred to as the bra sheath. The axillary sheath is a separate sleeve enclosing the axillary neurovascular bundle, and the clavipectoral fascia lies higher, investing the pectoralis minor.'),
('a9243aea-29ec-5c71-af78-4549e99b1d70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracoclavicular ligament maintains the:', '["Stability of shoulder joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Position of ribs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anatomical position of clavicle and scapula","Position of humerus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical position of clavicle and scapula', 'The coracoclavicular ligament anchors the clavicle to the coracoid process and maintains the normal relation between the clavicle and the scapula, suspending the scapula from the clavicle. The shoulder joint, humerus, and ribs are held in place by other structures.'),
('65e7b020-a394-54d6-802d-c69bedec196a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The costoclavicular ligament limits:', '["Depression of clavicle","Elevation of clavicle","Rotation","Retraction","Protraction"]'::jsonb, 'Elevation of clavicle', 'The costoclavicular ligament connects the clavicle to the first costal cartilage and checks elevation of the clavicle. It also limits medial rotation of the clavicle during abduction, but its principal check is on upward movement.'),
('7740edb7-ef7e-5467-b9e3-345ca1d825c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The interclavicular ligament connects:', '["Two clavicles","Clavicle to coracoid","Clavicle to first rib","Clavicle to sternum","Clavicle to acromion"]'::jsonb, 'Two clavicles', 'The interclavicular ligament stretches across the jugular notch of the manubrium, connecting the two clavicles. The other named ligaments connect the clavicle to the first rib, sternum, coracoid process, or acromion.'),
('8f7fe3a9-d903-5a62-b124-073303e7adb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The sternoclavicular joint dislocates most commonly:', '["Medially","Anteriorly","Inferiorly","Posteriorly","Superiorly"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly', 'The sternoclavicular joint dislocates most often anteriorly because the posterior part of the capsule and the interclavicular ligament offer less resistance anteriorly. Posterior dislocation is less frequent but far more dangerous because of the structures behind the joint.'),
('3614ffd4-12b5-5aca-98c5-3ba0a97c8f13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Posterior dislocation of sternoclavicular joint can damage:', '["Lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea and great vessels","Heart without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trachea and great vessels', 'In posterior dislocation, the medial end of the clavicle is pushed behind the sternum against the trachea and the great vessels (brachiocephalic vein and artery), which is potentially life-threatening. The lung, esophagus, and heart lie further away from the joint.'),
('5d1b3932-f3e0-550f-b5be-453d25a3277d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The scapula has how many fossae?', '["2","4","5","3","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'The scapula has three fossae: the supraspinous and infraspinous fossae on the dorsal surface and the subscapular fossa on the costal surface. The glenoid cavity is an articular surface rather than a fossa.'),
('b48d018d-998b-54f2-9385-f5c81888b6af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The scapular notch is converted into a foramen by:', '["Transverse scapular ligament","Acromioclavicular ligament","Coracoclavicular ligament","None","Coracoacromial ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse scapular ligament', 'The superior transverse scapular ligament bridges the scapular notch and converts it into a foramen through which the suprascapular nerve passes. The coracoclavicular, coracoacromial, and acromioclavicular ligaments are associated with other joints and do not convert this notch.'),
('f6604c92-5505-5c72-8ca1-591bb4f40fa2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The spine of scapula separates:', '["Medial and lateral borders","Supraspinous and infraspinous fossae","None","Supraspinous and subscapular","Infraspinous and subscapular"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinous and infraspinous fossae', 'The spine of the scapula divides the dorsal surface into the supraspinous fossa above and the infraspinous fossa below. The subscapular fossa lies on the opposite costal surface, and the borders of the scapula are not separated by the spine.'),
('da3fa15b-34a3-53e5-8fd3-57d2dcc76146'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The acromion of scapula articulates with:', '["Clavicle","Rib 1","Coracoid","Sternum","Humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle', 'The acromion articulates with the lateral end of the clavicle to form the acromioclavicular joint. The humerus articulates with the glenoid cavity, and the sternum, coracoid, and first rib are not articular partners of the acromion.'),
('1fb37405-eb52-59d5-bd04-4d710f32e49e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The jugular notch is at the level of:', '["T4","T1","T2","T3","T5"]'::jsonb, 'T2', 'The jugular (suprasternal) notch lies at the level of the body of the T2 vertebra. In comparison, the manubriosternal angle is at T4 and the xiphoid process at about T9-T10, useful landmarks for counting vertebral levels.'),
('383dec1d-163b-5946-9523-dfe94f83d1b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The xiphoid process is at the level of:', '["T8","T9","T10","T6","T12"]'::jsonb, 'T10', 'The xiphoid process lies at the level of the T10 vertebra. The jugular notch is at T2 and the manubriosternal angle at T4, so these sternal landmarks help in estimating vertebral levels.'),
('40c46b17-74ff-5846-bb45-d511a01fa103'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The first rib articulates with which vertebra?', '["T3","T4","T2","T1","C7"]'::jsonb, 'T1', 'The first rib articulates with the body and transverse process of the T1 vertebra at the T1 level of the thoracic spine. The subclavian vessels arch over the first rib at this level, and the other vertebral levels correspond to different ribs.'),
('fa2601e0-d313-5c09-ad76-18714bf8ddd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The scalene tubercle on first rib is for insertion of:', '["Scalenus medius","Levator scapulae","Scalenus anterior","Scalenus posterior","Subclavius"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenus anterior', 'The scalene tubercle on the inner border of the first rib marks the insertion of the scalenus anterior. The scalenus medius inserts behind the groove for the subclavian artery, and the levator scapulae and subclavius attach elsewhere.'),
('ccc74790-06ed-5352-968d-8d86dc76fd5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subclavian artery crosses which rib?', '["Third rib","First rib","Clavicle","Second rib","Sternum"]'::jsonb, 'First rib', 'The subclavian artery arches over the first rib, passing between the scalenus anterior and medius, and becomes the axillary artery at the outer border of the rib. It does not cross the second or third rib.'),
('50b26d39-04ae-5247-bc46-cf013714f951'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subclavian vein crosses which rib?', '["Sternum","First rib","Second rib","Third rib","Clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'First rib', 'The subclavian vein arches over the first rib and becomes the axillary vein at the lateral border of that rib. The sternum, clavicle, second rib, and third rib are not crossed by the vein.'),
('9e494164-433b-5128-8c57-85a03943f7d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary artery is divided into how many parts by pectoralis minor?', '["2","4","3","5","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Pectoralis minor crosses the axillary artery and divides it into three parts. The first part has one branch, the second two, and the third three, so three is the correct count.'),
('30d16626-1150-5b95-9977-d7e6434b5dbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The first part of axillary artery has how many branches?', '["4","0","3","1","2"]'::jsonb, '1', 'The first part of the axillary artery gives off only the superior thoracic artery. It typically has a single branch, unlike the portions distal to pectoralis minor.'),
('fd10114e-41ec-53f8-9f17-7a9956b9b2ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The second part of axillary artery has how many branches?', '["5","4","2","3","1"]'::jsonb, '2', 'The second part of the axillary artery, deep to pectoralis minor, gives the thoracoacromial and lateral thoracic arteries, totaling two branches.'),
('90aa7911-836c-51ad-b759-7861e46b6383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The third part of axillary artery has how many branches?', '["3","2","5","4","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'The third part of the axillary artery gives the subscapular artery and the anterior and posterior circumflex humeral arteries, totaling three branches.'),
('3797f6dd-1ada-5e49-9bb0-9ec79ff52a21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The anterior circumflex humeral artery supplies:', '["Supraspinatus","Teres major","Infraspinatus","Head of humerus","Deltoid"]'::jsonb, 'Head of humerus', 'The anterior circumflex humeral artery wraps around the surgical neck of the humerus and supplies the head of the humerus. The listed muscles receive blood from other axillary artery branches.'),
('d6a22763-63ca-58eb-ba59-f4838941d435'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The superior thoracic artery supplies:', '["Deltoid","Pectoralis major","Pectoralis minor","Upper intercostal muscles","Scapula"]'::jsonb, 'Upper intercostal muscles', 'The superior thoracic artery, from the first part of the axillary artery, supplies the upper intercostal muscles and first two intercostal spaces. Deltoid, pectoral muscles, and scapula are supplied by other vessels.'),
('63a1f745-8783-5233-9b20-2305f827ed4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The thoracoacromial artery supplies all except:', '["Deltoid","Pectoral region","Clavicle","Acromion","Serratus anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The thoracoacromial artery supplies the deltoid, pectoral region, clavicle, and acromion through its pectoral, clavicular, deltoid, and acromial branches. The serratus anterior is supplied instead by the lateral thoracic artery, so it is the exception.'),
('67327991-849b-52e3-b71b-3a4cca69cad5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral thoracic artery supplies all except:', '["Serratus anterior","Pectoral muscles","Lymph nodes","Subscapularis","Breast"]'::jsonb, 'Subscapularis', 'The lateral thoracic artery supplies the serratus anterior, both pectoral muscles, axillary lymph nodes, and the lateral part of the breast. Subscapularis is supplied by the subscapular artery, not this vessel.'),
('82d9d227-3205-555d-94ee-30c6b3c3183d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The cephalic vein drains into which vein?', '["External jugular","Axillary vein","Subclavian","Brachiocephalic","Basilic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary vein', 'The cephalic vein ascends in the deltopectoral groove, pierces the clavipectoral fascia, and drains into the axillary vein. The subclavian, basilic, external jugular, and brachiocephalic veins are not its termination.'),
('0f157aa3-7b21-52eb-8f33-c3db349d9e20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The venae comitantes of brachial artery join which vein?', '["Cephalic vein","Basilic vein","Brachiocephalic","Axillary vein","Subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Basilic vein', 'The two venae comitantes of the brachial artery join the basilic vein near the lower border of teres major to form the axillary vein. No other listed vein receives this union directly.'),
('27f2f55e-6bbb-544e-9a7b-163e05e75290'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary artery begins at the outer border of:', '["Second rib","Sternum","Scapula","Clavicle","First rib"]'::jsonb, 'First rib', 'The axillary artery begins at the lateral border of the first rib, where it continues from the subclavian artery. The sternum, scapula, clavicle, and second rib mark neither its origin.'),
('17b523e1-469b-5ac4-96fb-060287cddf26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary artery ends at the lower border of:', '["Pectoralis major","Teres major","Subscapularis","Coracobrachialis","Latissimus dorsi"]'::jsonb, 'Teres major', 'The axillary artery ends at the lower border of teres major and continues as the brachial artery. Teres major therefore marks its termination in the axilla.'),
('e7431cb0-bed8-57aa-92ab-e50977b8ab6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The brachial plexus is formed by ventral rami of which spinal nerves?', '["C5-7 and T1","T1-2","C5-8 and T1","C4-8","C3-8"]'::jsonb, 'C5-8 and T1', 'The brachial plexus is formed by the ventral rami of C5 through C8 and T1. The alternative ranges omit C8 or include incorrect segments.'),
('629ab7df-6f2e-5079-99a1-7169dbc29db8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The roots of brachial plexus emerge between:', '["Sternocleidomastoid and scalenus","None","Trapezius and scalenus","Scalenus anterior and medius","Scalenus medius and posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenus anterior and medius', 'The roots of the brachial plexus emerge from the neck between the scalenus anterior and scalenus medius muscles. The sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, and medius-posterior scalene pair do not bound the roots.'),
('64f9603a-65f2-5434-96c5-3ec72170efc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The divisions of brachial plexus are named:', '["Anterior and posterior","Superior and inferior","Primary and secondary","Medial and lateral","Major and minor"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior', 'Each trunk of the brachial plexus divides into an anterior and a posterior division, which later form the cords. Superior-inferior, primary-secondary, and medial-lateral are not used for the divisions.'),
('324cd3c1-cd45-5a03-a173-6008b344d638'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral cord is formed by:', '["All anterior divisions resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior divisions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior division of lower trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior divisions of upper and middle trunks"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior divisions of upper and middle trunks', 'The lateral cord is formed by the anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks, carrying C5 to C7 fibers. The anterior division of the lower trunk forms the medial cord, and posterior divisions form the posterior cord.'),
('2b59dab1-a1fc-5521-8b88-2a20bef49010'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial cord is formed by:', '["All anterior","Anterior division of lower trunk","Posterior divisions","Anterior divisions of upper and middle","None"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior division of lower trunk', 'The medial cord is formed by the anterior division of the lower trunk, carrying C8 and T1 fibers. Anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks instead form the lateral cord.'),
('be9d5c73-76fa-5cc2-9e53-01ad7295cfb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The posterior cord is formed by:', '["None","Anterior divisions of lower","Posterior division of upper trunk","All anterior divisions","Posterior divisions of all three trunks"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior divisions of all three trunks', 'The posterior cord is formed by the posterior divisions of all three trunks. Anterior divisions form the lateral and medial cords rather than the posterior cord.'),
('8eaae39c-2fce-55c1-a7c0-987174dc6962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The nerve supply of clavipectoral fascia is:', '["No specific nerve supply","Medial pectoral","Suprascapular","Lateral pectoral","Subclavius nerve"]'::jsonb, 'No specific nerve supply', 'The clavipectoral fascia has no named nerve supply; it is simply pierced by the lateral pectoral nerve, thoracoacromial vessels, and cephalic vein. The listed nerves innervate muscles, not this fascia.'),
('7f7213e4-9bb3-562d-bbfb-89b6aca0128e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary lymph nodes drain into:', '["Thoracic duct","Subclavian trunk","Internal jugular","Innominate","Superior vena cava"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian trunk', 'The axillary lymph nodes drain into the subclavian lymphatic trunk on their own side. The thoracic duct and systemic veins are not the direct drainage of these nodes.'),
('9de36d0f-d851-5922-aca1-49e4cf3ac721'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The right subclavian trunk drains into:', '["Superior vena cava","Subclavian vein","Thoracic duct","Right brachiocephalic","Right lymphatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Right lymphatic duct', 'The right subclavian lymphatic trunk drains into the right lymphatic duct, which then empties into the right brachiocephalic vein. The contralateral trunk follows a different course.'),
('5057f194-398d-5899-860a-34ad9b3a9b95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The left subclavian trunk drains into:', '["Subclavian vein","Superior vena cava","Left lymphatic duct","Left brachiocephalic","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic duct', 'The left subclavian lymphatic trunk drains into the thoracic duct at its termination. The right lymphatic duct, subclavian vein, and brachiocephalic vein are not its destination.'),
('c7881c1d-ddd7-5487-ab3a-e8d0f21b204e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The nerve point of neck (Erb''s point) is where:', '["Trunks divide","All roots meet","Cords form","At the diaphragm","C5 and C6 join"]'::jsonb, 'C5 and C6 join', 'Erb''s point is the junction where the C5 and C6 ventral rami unite above the clavicle. Trunk division and cord formation occur at deeper levels than this point.'),
('940964f8-6079-5c6a-9dee-f6f82680a7d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Erb''s point is located:', '["Above clavicle","In arm","Below clavicle","At clavicle","In axilla"]'::jsonb, 'Above clavicle', 'Erb''s point lies above the clavicle behind the sternocleidomastoid, where C5 and C6 unite. The arm, axilla, and clavicle itself are not its location.'),
('2e5d85e8-f59c-52a8-934c-e327428bd9a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Injury at Erb''s point results in:', '["Erb-Duchenne palsy (Waiter''s tip)","Klumpke''s palsy","Total palsy","Radial nerve palsy","Median nerve palsy"]'::jsonb, 'Erb-Duchenne palsy (Waiter''s tip)', 'Upper trunk injury at Erb''s point produces Erb-Duchenne palsy with a waiter''s tip posture. Klumpke''s palsy follows lower trunk injury, while isolated radial or median palsies arise elsewhere.'),
('1ca28adb-9ae1-574a-abfd-53629914c8dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Erb-Duchenne palsy affects which muscles?', '["biceps","brachialis","supinator","Deltoid","brachioradialis"]'::jsonb, 'Deltoid', 'Erb-Duchenne palsy involves C5 and C6 fibers, so the deltoid loses its abduction function along with the supraspinatus and infraspinatus. The deltoid is therefore a muscle paralyzed by this upper trunk injury.'),
('e19b4369-a354-533a-83a3-eb829bc51e37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The deformity in Erb-Duchenne palsy is:', '["Foot drop","Claw hand","Wrist drop","Arm adducted and internally rotated","forearm pronated and extended"]'::jsonb, 'Arm adducted and internally rotated', 'In Erb-Duchenne palsy the arm lies adducted and internally rotated, with an extended elbow and pronated forearm, because the abducting and lateral rotating muscles are paralyzed. Claw hand and wrist drop belong to lower trunk or radial lesions, and foot drop is a lower limb sign.'),
('a7ada652-9381-5273-b1c9-cca8227f8a93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Klumpke''s palsy involves which roots?', '["C5","None of the above","C8","T1","6"]'::jsonb, 'C8', 'Klumpke''s palsy involves the C8 and T1 roots of the lower trunk. C8 therefore represents one of the involved roots in this injury.'),
('28d4fde6-9c50-508e-be76-ffb892f981db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Klumpke''s palsy affects which muscles?', '["Shoulder muscles","Elbow extensors","Elbow flexors","Intrinsic hand muscles","Forearm pronators"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic hand muscles', 'Klumpke''s palsy affects the intrinsic hand muscles supplied by C8 and T1 fibers. Shoulder, elbow, and forearm muscles are largely spared in this lower trunk injury.'),
('fa36c626-9cba-52db-8a30-1814513056c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Klumpke''s palsy presents with:', '["Foot drop","Waiter''s tip","Dinner fork deformity","Claw hand","Wrist drop"]'::jsonb, 'Claw hand', 'Lower trunk injury in Klumpke''s palsy paralyses the intrinsic hand muscles and produces a claw hand. Waiter''s tip reflects an upper trunk lesion, wrist drop a radial lesion, and foot drop a lower limb lesion.'),
('f6ea5018-7214-5353-a0a5-cc407a53bd71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The serratus anterior winging of scapula is caused by injury to:', '["Axillary","Suprascapular","Long thoracic nerve","Accessory","Dorsal scapular"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve', 'Winging of the scapula follows paralysis of serratus anterior, which is innervated by the long thoracic nerve. The axillary, suprascapular, dorsal scapular, and accessory nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('0a416dc1-aef6-5976-8e77-a46df9edc184'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Nerve injury causing loss of shoulder abduction beyond 15 degrees:', '["Axillary nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic","Accessory","Dorsal scapular"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular nerve', 'Supraspinatus initiates the first 15 degrees of abduction and is supplied by the suprascapular nerve, so injury abolishes this initial arc. Abduction beyond 15 degrees is driven by the deltoid.'),
('c16783da-bdc9-5cdc-bda1-f236623e8c40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Nerve injury causing loss of shoulder abduction beyond 15-90 degrees:', '["Axillary nerve","Radial","Accessory","Long thoracic","Suprascapular"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve', 'The deltoid produces abduction from about 15 to 90 degrees and is innervated by the axillary nerve, so axillary injury abolishes this range. Suprascapular nerve injury removes only the first 15 degrees.'),
('92913280-2c18-52a5-b746-eaa91f735e60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The circumflex nerve is also known as:', '["Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary nerve","Suprascapular","Median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nerve', 'The circumflex nerve is another name for the axillary nerve, which winds around the surgical neck of the humerus to supply the deltoid and teres minor. The other nerves have different names and distributions.'),
('742f6862-dc73-5101-ae75-161d7cf95dc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nerve is at risk in which fracture?', '["Anatomical neck","Medial epicondyle","Shaft of humerus","Lateral epicondyle","Surgical neck of humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical neck of humerus', 'The axillary nerve passes directly behind the surgical neck of the humerus, making it vulnerable in surgical neck fractures. The anatomical neck, shaft, and epicondyles do not endanger this nerve specifically.'),
('809f91b0-c552-58dc-94fe-3189b999e243'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nerve gives a branch to:', '["Medial head of triceps","Short head of biceps","Coracobrachialis","Brachialis","Long head of triceps"]'::jsonb, 'Long head of triceps', 'The axillary nerve supplies the deltoid and teres minor and may give a branch to the long head of the triceps. It does not supply the coracobrachialis, brachialis, or short head of biceps.'),
('692933c1-1d20-59ee-8c1e-01bed3f2012d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The triangular space boundaries:', '["long head of triceps","Teres major","None of the above","teres minor","Sometimes"]'::jsonb, 'Teres major', 'The triangular space is bounded by the teres major inferiorly, teres minor superiorly, and the long head of triceps laterally, transmitting the circumflex scapular artery. Teres major is one of these boundaries.'),
('5b60e707-6a34-500e-be8d-43477871d9a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The triangular interval boundaries:', '["teres major","None of the above","lateral head of triceps","long head of triceps","Teres minor"]'::jsonb, 'teres major', 'The triangular interval is bounded by the teres major superiority and the long and lateral heads of triceps, transmitting the radial nerve and profunda brachii vessels. Teres major is one of its boundaries.'),
('2ee6cb76-79be-54f7-8b16-0efc154cfd15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The long thoracic nerve lies on the surface of which muscle?', '["Latissimus dorsi","Subscapularis","Serratus anterior","Intercostals","Pectoralis major"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The long thoracic nerve descends on the superficial surface of serratus anterior and innervates it. The other listed muscles are supplied by different nerves.'),
('23662db0-f40f-5055-a9af-d788278796e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The thoracodorsal nerve descends along the lateral border of which muscle?', '["Latissimus dorsi","Teres major","Pectoralis","Serratus anterior","Subscapularis"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'The thoracodorsal nerve descends along the lateral border of latissimus dorsi and enters its deep surface. It does not run along the other muscles listed.'),
('bca0d8d7-0176-5186-8f60-e4aa8d6f12d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The medial pectoral nerve pierces which muscle?', '["Pectoralis minor","Pectoralis major","Subclavius","Intercostals","Serratus anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Pectoralis minor', 'The medial pectoral nerve pierces the pectoralis minor, which it supplies, and then continues to the pectoralis major. The other muscles are not pierced by this nerve.'),
('c03254d4-8fd3-5311-add3-0c0327c8e2d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral pectoral nerve pierces which fascia?', '["None","Clavipectoral fascia","Brachial fascia","Axillary sheath","Pectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Clavipectoral fascia', 'The lateral pectoral nerve pierces the clavipectoral fascia to reach the deep surface of pectoralis major. It does not pierce the brachial, pectoral, or axillary fasciae.'),
('5e81989f-a885-516c-97d4-d886025a0828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavipectoral fascia is also known as:', '["Clavipectoral membrane","Costocoracoid membrane","Axillary fascia","Bra sheath","None"]'::jsonb, 'Costocoracoid membrane', 'The clavipectoral fascia is also called the costocoracoid membrane, spanning from the clavicle to the axillary fascia and pierced by the cephalic vein. The costocoracoid ligament is a separate structure.'),
('6e03c499-6394-5868-a27a-1c331f45a07f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The costocoracoid ligament extends from:', '["First rib to clavicle","Coracoid to acromion","First rib to coracoid process","Clavicle to coracoid","Sternum to clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'First rib to coracoid process', 'The costocoracoid ligament extends from the first rib to the coracoid process, forming the thickened free margin of the clavipectoral fascia. The other attachments do not describe this ligament.'),
('52a4442e-3e85-54c5-90e8-abe56bd3f8af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The suspensory ligament of the breast is also called:', '["Clavipectoral fascia","Sternoclavicular ligament","None","Suspensory ligament of axilla","Cooper''s ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Cooper''s ligament', 'The suspensory ligament of the breast is Cooper''s ligament, which anchors the breast to the skin and pectoral fascia. The clavipectoral fascia and sternoclavicular ligament are distinct structures.'),
('a65ac85b-012b-57a9-92a1-6c0ebf655a08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary sheath is an extension of:', '["Clavipectoral fascia","Deep fascia","Prevertebral fascia","Superficial fascia","Brachial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The axillary sheath is an extension of the prevertebral fascia that encloses the axillary vessels and the brachial plexus. The superficial, deep, brachial, and clavipectoral fasciae do not form this sheath.'),
('1279ccdf-95dc-5455-b71b-89e04678546f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axilla contains all except:', '["Fat","Brachial plexus","Axillary vessels","Axillary lymph nodes","Supraspinatus muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Supraspinatus muscle', 'The axilla contains fat, axillary vessels, the brachial plexus, and axillary lymph nodes. The supraspinatus muscle lies in the supraspinous fossa of the scapula, outside the axilla.'),
('0663129c-054e-542a-81dd-9c222e551e9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary lymph nodes number approximately:', '["10-15","20-30","30-40","40-50","5-10"]'::jsonb, '20-30', 'The axillary lymph nodes number approximately 20 to 30 in the adult. The other ranges overestimate or underestimate the usual total.'),
('dc724ada-3d5f-5afe-925f-8712b29ff72a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pectoral group of axillary nodes receives lymph from:', '["Posterior thoracic wall","Upper limb","Lateral thoracic wall","None","Anterior thoracic wall and breast"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior thoracic wall and breast', 'The pectoral (anterior) group of axillary nodes receives lymph from the anterior thoracic wall and the breast. Lymph from the upper limb and lateral thoracic wall drains to other axillary groups.'),
('219c60a6-7183-5a47-872c-4329704986b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral group of axillary nodes receives lymph from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Breast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper limb (except lymphatic drainage via cephalic vein)","Thoracic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scapular region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper limb (except lymphatic drainage via cephalic vein)', 'The lateral (humeral) axillary nodes drain the upper limb through lymphatics accompanying the main veins. The lymph that travels via the cephalic vein drains directly to the apical nodes, so it is excluded from this group. The breast drains to the pectoral (anterior) nodes, the thoracic wall to the anterior group, and the scapular region to the posterior (subscapular) group.'),
('9df74996-8fdf-5791-9789-a80a87c090aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary nodes send efferents to:', '["Directly to subclavian trunk","Thoracic duct","Apical group","Central group","None"]'::jsonb, 'Central group', 'Efferents of the lateral, pectoral, and posterior axillary node groups converge on the central group lying in the axillary fat. From the central group the lymph passes to the apical group, then to the subclavian trunk, so the trunk and duct are downstream rather than the immediate target.'),
('82faa629-c9fa-5b80-b615-91d605d52f9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The apical axillary nodes drain into:', '["Bronchomediastinal","Thoracic duct","Jugular trunk","Subclavian trunk","Right lymphatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian trunk', 'Efferents of the apical axillary nodes unite to form the subclavian lymph trunk. On the right side this trunk joins the jugular and bronchomediastinal trunks at the venous angle to form the right lymphatic duct, while on the left side it ends in the thoracic duct.'),
('8ed534b4-157d-5223-bed1-b5f1391bd02f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The right subclavian trunk drains into right lymphatic duct at:', '["Root of neck","Axilla","Thorax","Mediastinum","Abdomen"]'::jsonb, 'Root of neck', 'The right subclavian trunk terminates in the root of the neck, entering the right lymphatic duct near the venous angle where the internal jugular and subclavian veins meet. The axilla, thorax, mediastinum, and abdomen all lie distal to this junction.'),
('7589b46a-a539-532a-b41b-33dc3ee9f1e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The deltopectoral triangle boundaries:', '["pectoralis major","trapezius","deltoid","Clavicle","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle', 'The deltopectoral (clavipectoral) triangle is bounded above by the clavicle, medially by the pectoralis major, and laterally by the deltoid, with the cephalic vein in its floor. The trapezius forms no part of this triangle.'),
('6459fd1f-889f-554f-995b-3c4c4a0398d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The acromioclavicular joint has which type of articular disc?', '["disc resulting in decreased functional activity","Synovial fold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete disc mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyaline cartilage disc without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Incomplete fibrocartilaginous disc"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete fibrocartilaginous disc', 'The acromioclavicular joint is a plane synovial joint with an incomplete wedge-shaped fibrocartilaginous disc that only partially divides the cavity. The meniscus-like disc accommodates gliding and rotation; a full disc or a pure hyaline disc is not present.'),
('daca35ee-b616-5bfc-9484-07de4a8cb983'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The movements at acromioclavicular joint include:', '["None","Circumduction","Gliding and rotation","Flexion and extension","Abduction and adduction"]'::jsonb, 'Gliding and rotation', 'The acromioclavicular joint is a plane synovial joint that permits limited gliding between the clavicle and acromion together with slight rotation as the scapula moves. Flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction are not meaningful movements at this joint.'),
('aaa7f39f-b170-52e4-be66-e0f443dd1112'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The sternoclavicular joint movements include:', '["depression","Flexion","Elevation","protraction","retraction"]'::jsonb, 'Elevation', 'The sternoclavicular joint is a saddle-type synovial joint whose movements include elevation of the clavicle and shoulder girdle during shrugging or overhead reach, along with depression, protraction, and retraction. Flexion is not a movement of this joint.'),
('39b01acc-b404-5100-8f6d-65813e02bcbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The sternoclavicular joint is supplied by:', '["Long thoracic","Axillary nerve","Suprascapular nerve","Dorsal scapular","Medial supraclavicular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial supraclavicular nerve', 'The sternoclavicular joint is innervated by the medial supraclavicular nerve (C3-C4) together with the nerve to subclavius. The axillary, suprascapular, dorsal scapular, and long thoracic nerves supply other parts of the shoulder girdle rather than this joint.'),
('7e75a331-1427-57ac-a289-d5d87325e7aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The supraclavicular nerves are from which cervical plexus branches?', '["3","4","C4","C2","C3"]'::jsonb, 'C3', 'The supraclavicular nerves are superficial branches of the cervical plexus emerging from the C3-C4 loop. They appear behind the posterior border of sternocleidomastoid and supply skin over the clavicle and upper part of the shoulder.'),
('a1ac9f9b-c847-57da-9ca9-837018fae47a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary arch (Langer''s arch) is an anomaly of which muscle?', '["Deltoid","Subscapularis","Latissimus dorsi","Pectoralis major","Teres major"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'The axillary arch (Langer''s arch) is an anomalous slip of latissimus dorsi that crosses the axilla and blends with the lower border of pectoralis major. It can compress the axillary vessels or nerves and appears in about 7% of individuals.'),
('e8f20337-48ec-5ae4-bb1a-8c3ac1a48902'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The third head of triceps is occasionally present as an anomaly of:', '["Biceps brachii","Coracobrachialis","Deltoid","Brachialis","Triceps brachii"]'::jsonb, 'Triceps brachii', 'A third head of triceps brachii occasionally arises from the upper part of the humerus between the teres major and the lateral head, an inconstant anomaly of the triceps itself. Biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis, and deltoid are separate muscles and do not give this extra head.'),
('9129fca9-bc42-5a98-9ac6-4152642b135e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracoclavicular ligament is a strong band connecting:', '["Coracoid process to clavicle","Clavicle to acromion","Coracoid to scapula","Coracoid to acromion","Clavicle to sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Coracoid process to clavicle', 'The coracoclavicular ligament passes between the coracoid process and the inferior surface of the clavicle, suspending the scapula from the clavicle and keeping the acromioclavicular joint in place. The acromion and sternum are not part of this attachment.'),
('af08de97-1161-5ed9-ade2-df1f6b78b160'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The conoid ligament is the part of:', '["Clavipectoral fascia","Coracoacromial ligament","Coracoclavicular ligament","Sternoclavicular ligament","Acromioclavicular ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Coracoclavicular ligament', 'The coracoclavicular ligament is made of two parts: the conoid ligament medially and the trapezoid ligament laterally. The conoid ligament descends from the conoid tubercle of the clavicle to the base of the coracoid process.'),
('05be7b23-2faa-50bc-a86e-d7c0b22fa5e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The trapezoid ligament attaches to:', '["Trapezoid line of clavicle","Conoid tubercle","Sternum","Coracoid process","Acromion"]'::jsonb, 'Trapezoid line of clavicle', 'The trapezoid ligament, the lateral part of the coracoclavicular ligament, extends from the trapezoid line on the inferior surface of the clavicle to the coracoid process. The conoid tubercle anchors the conoid ligament rather than the trapezoid.'),
('8109ea02-ef73-5c94-9158-9d753f1c5da0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The costoclavicular ligament attaches:', '["Clavicle to coracoid","Clavicle to sternum","First rib to clavicle","First rib to coracoid","Clavicle to acromion"]'::jsonb, 'First rib to clavicle', 'The costoclavicular ligament joins the first rib and its costal cartilage to the inferomedial impression of the clavicle. It anchors the medial end of the clavicle and acts as a fulcrum around which sternoclavicular movements occur.'),
('007beca9-aaea-59a5-8709-a6341fb6802a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The superior transverse scapular ligament converts:', '["Coracoid process into a foramen","None","Spinoglenoid notch into a foramen","Glenoid cavity","Suprascapular notch into a foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular notch into a foramen', 'The superior transverse scapular ligament bridges the suprascapular notch, converting it into a foramen that transmits the suprascapular nerve, while the suprascapular artery passes above the ligament. The spinoglenoid notch is instead bridged by the inferior transverse scapular ligament.'),
('8c0bb7a3-903d-5e83-87fb-0ce8035aee25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The inferior transverse scapular ligament (spinoglenoid ligament) forms:', '["A foramen for radial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","A canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A foramen for suprascapular nerve and vessels","A foramen for axillary nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A foramen for suprascapular nerve and vessels', 'The spinoglenoid ligament spans the spinoglenoid notch, converting it into a foramen through which the suprascapular nerve and accompanying vessels pass to reach the infraspinous fossa. The radial and axillary nerves travel elsewhere.'),
('ea5a1b77-03d9-5fca-b10f-fe83f34c7ce7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The scapula has how many angles?', '["1","3","4","2","5"]'::jsonb, '3', 'The scapula has three angles: the superior angle, the lateral (glenoid) angle, and the inferior angle. One, two, four, or five are incorrect counts.'),
('795c4015-a0f5-5158-8e2f-1d1fdd1e9b1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracoid process is homologous to:', '["Tarsal bone","Carpal bone","Rib","Rudimentary bone","Sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Rudimentary bone', 'The coracoid process is the human remnant of the coracoid bone of lower vertebrates, so it is regarded as a rudimentary skeletal element fused secondarily to the scapula. It is not derived from a rib, the sternum, or a carpal or tarsal bone.'),
('637ad10e-bd24-5ecb-8c02-27beb8a1f257'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The suprascapular notch is located:', '["At the inferior angle resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the glenoid rim mediated by specific receptor activation","At the lateral end of superior border medial to coracoid","At the medial border leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the lateral end of superior border medial to coracoid', 'The suprascapular notch lies at the lateral end of the superior border of the scapula, just medial to the coracoid process, and is bridged by the superior transverse scapular ligament. The inferior angle, medial border, and glenoid rim are unrelated sites.'),
('73fc8b3c-0362-5d82-ac60-7792fa416eff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The spinoglenoid notch is located:', '["None","Between medial border and spine","Between spine of scapula and glenoid cavity","Between coracoid and glenoid","Between acromion and glenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Between spine of scapula and glenoid cavity', 'The spinoglenoid notch lies between the lateral end of the spine of the scapula and the glenoid cavity and is crossed by the spinoglenoid ligament. It is not found along the medial border or between the acromion and glenoid.'),
('ef54b604-ddc3-5152-ad69-e777eaf137e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The levator scapulae originates from:', '["Spinous processes of C1-4","Sternum","Clavicle","Transverse processes of C1-4","Ribs 1-4"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse processes of C1-4', 'Levator scapulae arises by tendinous slips from the transverse processes of C1-C4. It inserts on the upper part of the medial border of the scapula and is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve and branches of C3-C4.'),
('32801633-1444-5e01-986c-20e8df43e595'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The levator scapulae inserts into:', '["Superior angle resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior angle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral border mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial border of scapula above spine","Acromion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial border of scapula above spine', 'Levator scapulae inserts into the medial border of the scapula above the spine, from the superior angle toward the root of the spine. The rhomboids occupy the medial border below the spine.'),
('bd4d22f1-7573-526d-bcad-00ba25cbf512'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The rhomboid minor originates from:', '["Spinous processes of T2-5","T1","None of the above","Transverse processes of C7","Spinous processes of C7"]'::jsonb, 'Spinous processes of C7', 'Rhomboid minor arises from the lower part of the ligamentum nuchae and the spinous processes of C7 to T1. It inserts on the medial border of the scapula at the root of the spine and is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve.'),
('09321866-eeec-5de2-b759-3e120c887c8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The rhomboid major originates from:', '["Transverse processes of T2-5","Spinous processes of C7-T1","Ribs 2-5","None","Spinous processes of T2-5"]'::jsonb, 'Spinous processes of T2-5', 'Rhomboid major arises from the spinous processes of T2-T5 and the intervening supraspinous ligaments. It inserts into the medial border of the scapula below the spine and is supplied by the dorsal scapular nerve.'),
('adc75def-f46b-5a15-9b31-5e57e97504a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The rhomboids insert into:', '["Medial border above spine","Superior angle","Lateral border","Medial border of scapula below spine","Inferior angle"]'::jsonb, 'Medial border of scapula below spine', 'The rhomboids insert into the medial border of the scapula below the spine, between the root of the spine and the inferior angle. Insertion above the spine belongs to levator scapulae.'),
('93b03e46-7760-5434-916d-4005db533dff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The serratus anterior originates from:', '["Ribs 1-4","Upper 6 ribs","Upper 10 ribs","Upper 8 ribs","Ribs 5-10"]'::jsonb, 'Upper 8 ribs', 'Serratus anterior arises by slips from the outer surfaces of the upper eight ribs and inserts on the costal surface and medial border of the scapula. It protracts the scapula and holds it against the chest wall.'),
('3ec3b7a4-b7b7-52aa-b1d9-478eaa5b7cf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The serratus anterior is divided into how many parts?', '["1","4","2","5","3"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Serratus anterior is classically divided into three parts: superior, intermediate, and inferior, based on the direction of its slips of origin. One, two, four, or five parts are incorrect.'),
('0832b153-c428-5bee-b095-1aff4ba752b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The upper part of serratus anterior inserts into:', '["Inferior angle","Acromion","Medial border","Superior angle of scapula","Lateral border"]'::jsonb, 'Superior angle of scapula', 'The superior part of serratus anterior inserts on the costal surface of the superior angle of the scapula. The inferior part attaches at the inferior angle and the intermediate part lies along the medial border.'),
('01da87f3-a293-5db7-92c1-bf40de5d9d20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lower part of serratus anterior inserts into:', '["Glenoid cavity","Inferior angle of scapula","Lateral border","Medial border","Superior angle"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior angle of scapula', 'The inferior (lower) part of serratus anterior, whose fibers run backward and upward, inserts into the inferior angle of the scapula. These fibers rotate the scapula upward during abduction of the arm.'),
('e74cb148-c4c9-57f3-9a48-63448822952e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The trapezius inserts into:', '["spine of scapula","Lateral third of clavicle","Medial third of clavicle","Coracoid","acromion"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral third of clavicle', 'The trapezius inserts into the lateral third of the clavicle, the acromion, and the spine of the scapula. The medial third of the clavicle is not an insertion of this muscle.'),
('a76584c6-605b-53fc-8dec-0f794a0a2008'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The trapezius originates from:', '["Spines C1-C7","Superior nuchal line","spines C7-T12","ligamentum nuchae","Occipital protuberance only"]'::jsonb, 'Superior nuchal line', 'The trapezius arises from the superior nuchal line and external occipital protuberance, the ligamentum nuchae, and the spinous processes of C7-T12. Among the options, only the superior nuchal line is part of its extensive origin.'),
('65b80e9f-f91d-5550-84e7-9e66c3774a63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The upper fibers of trapezius elevate the scapula and also:', '["Retract scapula","None","Rotate scapula upward","Depress scapula","Protract scapula"]'::jsonb, 'Rotate scapula upward', 'The upper fibers of trapezius elevate the scapula and, acting with the lower fibers, rotate the scapula upward so the glenoid cavity faces upward during abduction of the arm. Depression and protraction are not produced by the upper fibers.'),
('9cc4cd64-c42d-5c3b-8dff-3f087ee65c81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The middle fibers of trapezius:', '["Retract scapula","Depress scapula","Elevate scapula","Protract scapula","Rotate scapula"]'::jsonb, 'Retract scapula', 'The middle (transverse) fibers of trapezius retract the scapula, drawing it toward the vertebral column. Elevation, depression, protraction, and rotation are produced by other parts of the muscle or by other muscles.'),
('5334731f-f121-58dc-bbd9-690d9844c170'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lower fibers of trapezius:', '["Elevate scapula","Rotate scapula upward","Retract scapula","Protract scapula","Depress and rotate scapula downward"]'::jsonb, 'Depress and rotate scapula downward', 'The lower fibers of trapezius depress the scapula and, with the levator scapulae, rotate its inferior angle medially so the glenoid faces downward as the arm is lowered. Elevation of the scapula is produced by the upper fibers.'),
('8d270be1-f9d4-588c-9ea8-1552cc2d4d2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The anterior fibers of deltoid produce:', '["Extension and lateral rotation resulting in decreased functional activity","Adduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Abduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexion and medial rotation of arm"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion and medial rotation of arm', 'The anterior (clavicular) fibers of deltoid flex the arm and medially rotate it. Extension with lateral rotation is produced by the posterior fibers, while the middle (acromial) fibers perform abduction.'),
('a7f2ffe8-604f-5450-9c2a-9c5d173275dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The posterior fibers of deltoid produce:', '["Abduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Extension and lateral rotation of arm","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Adduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexion and medial rotation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension and lateral rotation of arm', 'The posterior (spinal) fibers of deltoid extend the arm and laterally rotate it. Flexion with medial rotation is the action of the anterior fibers, and abduction is performed by the middle fibers.'),
('48bac9aa-722b-53c6-a9e0-a63a7acf2e5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The middle fibers of deltoid produce:', '["Abduction of arm from 15-90 degrees","Adduction","Flexion","Abduction 0-15 degrees","Abduction above 90 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'Abduction of arm from 15-90 degrees', 'The middle (acromial) fibers of deltoid are the powerful abductors working from about 15 to 90 degrees of abduction. The first 0-15 degrees are initiated by supraspinatus, and elevation above 90 degrees requires rotation of the scapula by trapezius and serratus anterior.'),
('1ecfd11c-3a59-56c9-a826-2942b514b9ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The supraspinatus produces:', '["Abduction above 90 degrees","Abduction 15-90 degrees","Initiation of abduction (0-15 degrees)","Adduction","Extension"]'::jsonb, 'Initiation of abduction (0-15 degrees)', 'Supraspinatus initiates the first 0-15 degrees of abduction, after which the deltoid takes over for the 15-90 degree range. Elevation above 90 degrees is completed by scapular rotation from trapezius and serratus anterior.'),
('2d3d573a-504a-5f39-9351-93290c05d4f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The infraspinatus produces:', '["Adduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial rotation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral (external) rotation of arm","Abduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral (external) rotation of arm', 'Infraspinatus is the chief lateral (external) rotator of the arm and, with teres minor, helps hold the humeral head in the glenoid as part of the rotator cuff. Medial rotation is produced by subscapularis and the anterior deltoid.'),
('d7d0a3e5-6dca-5f09-ba0b-c74851e1ac8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The teres minor produces:', '["Adduction","Medial rotation","Abduction","Lateral rotation of arm","Extension"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral rotation of arm', 'Teres minor laterally rotates the arm and weakly adducts it, and it stabilizes the glenohumeral joint along with the other cuff muscles. Medial rotation is the action of subscapularis.'),
('77244d01-d33a-5160-b8bd-ed2af812f16e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subscapularis produces:', '["Abduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Adduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral rotation mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial (internal) rotation of arm"]'::jsonb, 'Medial (internal) rotation of arm', 'Subscapularis medially (internally) rotates the arm and weakly adducts it, and it is the only rotator cuff muscle that internally rotates. Lateral rotation is produced by infraspinatus and teres minor.'),
('afb0a15a-839d-54af-a206-ba96c8ec6987'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The teres major produces:', '["Extension resulting in decreased functional activity","Adduction and medial rotation of arm","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abduction and lateral rotation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adduction and medial rotation of arm', 'Teres major adducts the arm and medially rotates it, assisting extension from the flexed position. Abduction with lateral rotation is performed by the deltoid together with the lateral rotator cuff muscles.'),
('cdeec74a-0737-5f01-bddf-b3c999a3475d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The latissimus dorsi produces:', '["extension","lateral rotation","medial rotation of arm","Adduction","Abduction"]'::jsonb, 'Adduction', 'Latissimus dorsi is a powerful adductor of the arm and also extends and medially rotates it from the abducted position. Lateral rotation and abduction move against its direction of pull.'),
('fc57e9b5-7896-5a0f-a7ed-fbc7f4c7c8c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pectoralis major produces:', '["Abduction","medial rotation","flexion of arm","lateral rotation","Adduction"]'::jsonb, 'Adduction', 'Pectoralis major adducts and medially rotates the arm, and its clavicular head flexes the arm toward the opposite shoulder. Abduction and lateral rotation are opposite to its primary pull.'),
('e93e42c2-afbd-5ce7-85eb-784dd3b1579f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pectoralis minor produces:', '["Depression and protraction of scapula","None","Elevation of scapula","Retraction of scapula","Rotation of scapula"]'::jsonb, 'Depression and protraction of scapula', 'Pectoralis minor depresses and protracts the scapula, drawing it forward and downward against the chest wall, and it assists forced inspiration by fixing the scapula. Elevation and retraction are opposite actions.'),
('22cea949-8663-5926-8956-d1ddc936769a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subclavius muscle:', '["Elevates clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rotates clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Retracts clavicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depresses and steadies the clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses and steadies the clavicle', 'Subclavius depresses the clavicle and steadies it against the first rib during movements of the shoulder girdle, and it protects the underlying neurovascular structures. Elevation would displace the clavicle in the opposite direction.'),
('c9f3a261-3119-593c-9ea7-997d49b283d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The biceps brachii produces all except:', '["None","Flexion of forearm","Extension of forearm","Supination of forearm","Weak flexion of arm"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of forearm', 'Biceps brachii flexes the elbow, supinates the forearm, and weakly flexes the arm at the shoulder, so the only action it does not produce is extension of the forearm. Extension of the elbow is produced by triceps brachii.'),
('746eea81-263a-5b09-9530-fce3fe182b75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The long head of biceps additionally:', '["Abducts arm","Extends arm","Laterally rotates arm","None","Adducts arm"]'::jsonb, 'Abducts arm', 'In addition to flexing and supinating, the long head of biceps, traveling over the humeral head into the intertubercular sulcus to the supraglenoid tubercle, assists abduction of the arm at the shoulder. Extension, lateral rotation, and adduction are not among its actions.'),
('10f2fcfc-c92b-5c0a-a2a2-79749abc9214'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The coracobrachialis produces:', '["Medial rotation","Extension","None","Abduction and lateral rotation","Flexion and adduction of arm"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion and adduction of arm', 'Coracobrachialis passes from the coracoid process to the medial middle third of the humerus, so it flexes and adducts the arm. The other options describe actions of rotator cuff or deltoid muscles.'),
('931c4ab0-9b4c-5fbb-aea6-87dfca35ff79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The brachialis produces:', '["None","Flexion of forearm at elbow","Pronation","Supination","Extension of forearm"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion of forearm at elbow', 'Brachialis is the prime flexor of the forearm at the elbow, attaching from the anterior humerus to the coronoid process and ulnar tuberosity. It neither rotates the forearm nor extends the elbow.'),
('63cce641-221e-5c1c-8dcf-6c927f91a27d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The triceps brachii produces:', '["Extension of forearm","Adduction of arm","None","Abduction of arm","Forearm flexion"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of forearm', 'Triceps brachii is the chief extensor of the forearm at the elbow, inserting on the olecranon of the ulna. Its long head can add arm adduction, but flexion and abduction of the forearm are not its actions.'),
('6c4a4b68-13f2-57f4-ac7d-31aaef8c3b6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The long head of triceps additionally:', '["Extends arm","None","Adducts arm","Flexes arm","Abducts arm"]'::jsonb, 'Adducts arm', 'The long head of triceps arises from the infraglenoid tubercle of the scapula and, crossing the shoulder joint, assists in adducting the arm. Flexion and abduction of the arm are not produced by this head.'),
('be5d6656-d881-5bd1-842a-0dea240ff86b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The anconeus muscle assists in:', '["Extension of forearm","Flexion of forearm","Pronation","Supination","None"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of forearm', 'Anconeus acts with triceps to extend the forearm at the elbow and to stabilize the joint by tightening the capsule. It does not flex, pronate, or supinate the forearm.'),
('cc8c5072-0ad5-5244-ad72-d6cf11cc2fff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The elbow joint is supplied mainly by which nerve?', '["ulnar","radial","Musculocutaneous","Only radial","median"]'::jsonb, 'Musculocutaneous', 'The elbow joint capsule receives most of its nerve supply from the musculocutaneous nerve anteriorly and the radial nerve posteriorly. The median and ulnar nerves contribute only minor branches.'),
('19ac3ec4-5c97-53fc-ad03-2fb626ce3229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The carrying angle at elbow is:', '["Approximately 5-10 degrees in males","0-5 degrees accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20-25 degrees mediated by specific receptor activation","10-15 in females without affecting the primary tissue pathway","15-20 degrees leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 5-10 degrees in males', 'The carrying angle averages about 5-10 degrees in males and is somewhat greater in females, reflecting the obliquity of the trochlear groove on the humerus. A far wider angle suggests deformity.'),
('31fa338f-d89a-5177-bef8-3c878a4d7a55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The carrying angle is formed between:', '["Long axis of arm and hand","Long axis of arm and forearm","Radius and ulna","Humerus and ulna","Humerus and radius"]'::jsonb, 'Long axis of arm and forearm', 'The carrying angle is the lateral (valgus) angle between the long axis of the arm and the long axis of the forearm in full extension and supination. It results from lateral obliquity of the trochlear groove, not from an angulation of the radius or ulna themselves.'),
('a0259ff9-ebd3-52f4-8b42-cca4b484a3b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Cubitus valgus is:', '["Increased carrying angle","Forward displacement","Decreased carrying angle","Lateral displacement","Backward displacement"]'::jsonb, 'Increased carrying angle', 'Cubitus valgus is an exaggerated lateral angulation of the forearm, that is, an increased carrying angle, often after a lateral humeral condylar fracture. A reduced or reversed angle defines cubitus varus.'),
('4d5907ea-8e53-5e7d-9cce-a6e5f593746f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Cubitus varus is:', '["Increased carrying angle resulting in decreased functional activity","Backward displacement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased carrying angle (gunstock deformity)","Forward displacement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased carrying angle (gunstock deformity)', 'Cubitus varus, the gunstock deformity, is a decrease or reversal of the carrying angle and classically follows malunion of a supracondylar fracture of the humerus. Increased angulation, not displacement, describes the opposite cubitus valgus.'),
('d01e6a75-e186-584b-b63d-2a0010e6bb1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The subcutaneous olecranon bursa is located:', '["Over the radial head","Over the medial epicondyle","Over the coronoid","Over the olecranon process","Over the lateral epicondyle"]'::jsonb, 'Over the olecranon process', 'The subcutaneous olecranon bursa lies on the posterior aspect of the elbow directly over the olecranon process of the ulna, where it cushions the point of the elbow. None of the other sites bears this bursa.'),
('b3ce4398-dcee-57ce-accf-bcb02b9f6936'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The supinator muscle is supplied by:', '["Median nerve","Deep branch of radial nerve","Posterior interosseous","Musculocutaneous","Superficial branch"]'::jsonb, 'Deep branch of radial nerve', 'Supinator is innervated by the deep branch of the radial nerve as it passes between the muscle''s two heads. Median, musculocutaneous, and the superficial radial branch supply other muscles.'),
('60a8a906-b102-54a2-8d6c-318512b01e2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The brachioradialis is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous","Axillary","Radial nerve","Median nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'Although brachioradialis flexes the elbow, it is a muscle of the extensor (posterior) compartment and is innervated by the radial nerve. Its fibers arise from the lateral supracondylar ridge, near the radial groove.'),
('eafec740-c6f4-5ca4-bac8-5d22c7abe2d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis are supplied by:', '["Posterior interosseous","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Radial nerve', 'Extensor carpi radialis longus receives the radial nerve proper and extensor carpi radialis brevis its deep branch, so the radial nerve is the common supply. The posterior interosseous nerve continues to deeper muscles, while median and ulnar nerves serve flexor compartment muscles.'),
('39307cdb-4936-5ae6-a1c5-c4d8fb881cd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The extensor carpi ulnaris is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve","Median nerve","Radial nerve","Musculocutaneous","Posterior interosseous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous nerve', 'Extensor carpi ulnaris is a muscle of the extensor compartment and is innervated by the posterior interosseous nerve, the continuation of the deep branch of the radial nerve. The ulnar and median nerves do not reach it.'),
('db96b079-a449-52e7-bcd3-9ef6f5d5d198'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The abductor pollicis longus is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Radial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous nerve', 'Abductor pollicis longus lies in the deep extensor compartment of the forearm and is supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve. The median and ulnar nerves supply the flexor and intrinsic hand muscles instead.'),
('87515cdb-f8b9-5c8d-801b-7d3f35739493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The extensor pollicis brevis is supplied by:', '["Median nerve","Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve","Musculocutaneous"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous nerve', 'Extensor pollicis brevis is a deep extensor-compartment muscle innervated by the posterior interosseous nerve. The median, ulnar, and musculocutaneous nerves supply muscles elsewhere in the limb.'),
('d4e308d2-161b-52b8-925b-a540d29f3f36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The extensor pollicis longus is supplied by:', '["Ulnar nerve","Musculocutaneous","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous nerve', 'Extensor pollicis longus occupies the third extensor compartment and is innervated by the posterior interosseous nerve. The radial nerve has already divided proximal to it, and median and ulnar nerves do not enter the extensor compartment.'),
('6595d55c-8f8d-5a11-ae61-d0030ad61848'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The extensor indicis is supplied by:', '["Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Ulnar nerve","Radial nerve","Posterior interosseous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interosseous nerve', 'Extensor indicis is a deep muscle of the dorsum of the forearm supplied by the posterior interosseous nerve. Median, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerves supply flexor and intrinsic muscles.'),
('ef405c31-8fc1-5a38-9eb6-5667680ff805'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The supinator muscle has which two heads?', '["Superficial and deep","Medial and lateral","Anterior and posterior","Upper and lower","Radial and ulnar"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep', 'Supinator has a superficial and a deep head that enclose the deep branch of the radial nerve as it passes between them. The other paired designations do not describe the heads of this muscle.'),
('f44131e3-0060-52ef-aaaa-8b7de8343a1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The deep branch of radial nerve passes between:', '["Two heads of supinator muscle","Two heads of pronator teres","Two heads of flexor carpi ulnaris","None","Two layers of interosseous membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Two heads of supinator muscle', 'The deep branch of the radial nerve winds around the neck of the radius and passes between the superficial and deep heads of supinator before continuing as the posterior interosseous nerve. Pronator teres and flexor carpi ulnaris have no such relation to it.'),
('ab6f667f-3b10-5972-8246-7fa602ea44e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The radial nerve divides into superficial and deep branches:', '["In the axilla","At the elbow joint","At the wrist","In the cubital fossa","In the arm"]'::jsonb, 'In the cubital fossa', 'Anterior to the lateral epicondyle, near the cubital fossa, the radial nerve divides into superficial and deep branches. The deep branch passes to supinator and becomes the posterior interosseous nerve, while the superficial branch continues to the hand.'),
('6f5f6843-576b-547b-baea-d4c5c6a4b511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The pronator teres muscle is supplied by:', '["Axillary","Radial nerve","Median nerve","Musculocutaneous","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'Pronator teres is innervated by the median nerve, which it often pierces between its humeral and ulnar heads. Radial, axillary, musculocutaneous, and ulnar nerves supply other muscles.'),
('0039241b-3315-5cb7-aa31-bedf1852ec1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The flexor carpi radialis is supplied by:', '["Median nerve","Axillary","Musculocutaneous","Radial nerve","Ulnar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Median nerve', 'Flexor carpi radialis is a superficial flexor-compartment muscle innervated by the median nerve. The ulnar nerve supplies flexor carpi ulnaris, and the other listed nerves serve muscles outside the anterior compartment.'),
('5aa55c55-03e5-5404-980d-04ba44f3623c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The clavicle ossifies from how many primary centers?', '["2 (one medial, one lateral)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (one medial, one lateral)', 'The clavicle ossifies by intramembranous ossification from two primary centers, one medial and one lateral, which fuse during development; the sternal end later gains a secondary center. It is the first bone to begin ossifying and is the only long bone to ossify intramembranously.'),
('aab84314-5ef0-58dd-94ee-0a0d941e65ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The axillary artery is divided into how many parts by the pectoralis minor?', '["4 parts","1 part","2 parts","3 parts","5 parts"]'::jsonb, '3 parts', 'Pectoralis minor crosses the axillary artery and divides it into three parts: the first is medial to the muscle, the second lies behind it, and the third is lateral to it. This division also organizes the branching pattern of the artery.'),
('7db349f0-a2be-5140-be4c-0f6be6cfa912'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which structure forms the medial wall of the axilla?', '["Serratus anterior and upper 4-5 ribs","Latissimus dorsi","Pectoralis major and minor","Humerus","Subscapularis"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior and upper 4-5 ribs', 'The medial wall of the axilla is formed by the thoracic wall, namely the upper four to five ribs with their intercostal muscles, covered by serratus anterior. Pectoralis major and minor form the anterior wall, while subscapularis and the humerus contribute to the posterior and lateral walls.'),
('ce9f6b61-c8a8-5279-8aec-69d41bb28b1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The lateral cord of brachial plexus gives which branches?', '["Suprascapular, subclavius","Medial pectoral, medial cutaneous, ulnar","Lateral pectoral, musculocutaneous, lateral root of median","Upper subscapular, thoracodorsal, lower subscapular","Axillary, radial"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral pectoral, musculocutaneous, lateral root of median', 'The lateral cord gives off the lateral pectoral nerve, the musculocutaneous nerve, and the lateral root of the median nerve. Suprascapular and subclavius arise from the upper trunk, the listed branches belong to the medial cord, and subscapular and thoracodorsal branches belong to the posterior cord.'),
('6838d568-ec52-5159-a3bc-dba2f29daf90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The posterior cord of brachial plexus terminates as:', '["Suprascapular and axillary","Radial and median","Median and ulnar nerves","Axillary and radial nerves","Musculocutaneous and median"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary and radial nerves', 'The posterior cord ends by dividing into the axillary and radial nerves after giving the upper and lower subscapular and thoracodorsal branches. Median and ulnar nerves are formed from the medial and lateral cords, and the musculocutaneous nerve is a lateral cord branch.'),
('00b9a42d-c8e9-59b8-aa89-54477b794024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which vein is formed at the lateral border of the first rib and lies in the axilla?', '["Brachial vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary vein (continuation of basilic vein)","Basilic vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Cephalic vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavian vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary vein (continuation of basilic vein)', 'The axillary vein is the upward continuation of the basilic vein as it crosses the lower border of teres major, and it becomes the subclavian vein at the outer border of the first rib. The cephalic vein drains into the axillary vein within the axilla, and the brachial vein contributes to its formation.'),
('30162d3e-2e57-51ae-bb13-a8c6c5740bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which nerve is most commonly injured in fractures of the midshaft of clavicle?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Long thoracic nerve","Lateral pectoral nerve","Medial pectoral nerve","Subclavian nerve, phrenic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian nerve, phrenic nerve', 'Because the subclavian vessels and adjacent neural structures lie close to the middle third of the clavicle, displaced midshaft fractures can injure the subclavian and phrenic nerves. The suprascapular, long thoracic, and pectoral nerves run in the shoulder region and are not typically jeopardized by a midshaft break.'),
('b8a508df-b98a-5bdc-82be-1cf9a037d511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The glenoid cavity is directed. Which option is most accurate?', '["Anteriorly and laterally","Posteriorly and laterally","Laterally and slightly anteriorly","Superiorly","Medially and anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally and slightly anteriorly', 'The glenoid cavity faces laterally and slightly anteriorly, and slightly upward, which is why the shoulder is the most mobile and most readily dislocated joint. It does not face posteriorly, superiorly, or medially.'),
('4ac4753c-1094-5769-93e8-d2b46acecd51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Pectoral Girdle Axilla: the glenoid cavity is directed?', '["Anteriorly and laterally","Posteriorly and laterally","Laterally and slightly anteriorly","Superiorly","Medially and anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally and slightly anteriorly', 'The glenoid cavity of the scapula is directed laterally and slightly anteriorly, accommodating the head of the humerus and favoring anterior dislocation. Posterior, superior, and medial directions are not anatomically correct.'),
('d6a0b301-9317-58b1-926f-87db85b012bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'For Pectoral Girdle Axilla, identify the correct response: the glenoid cavity is directed?', '["Anteriorly and laterally","Posteriorly and laterally","Laterally and slightly anteriorly","Superiorly","Medially and anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally and slightly anteriorly', 'The glenoid cavity faces laterally and slightly anteriorly, which helps explain the wide range of shoulder motion and the predilection for anterior dislocation. The listed posterior, superior, and medial directions do not match this orientation.'),
('990314ca-8969-5442-9a2d-8eb660b4bfd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'The glenoid cavity is directed. Select the best answer.', '["Anteriorly and laterally","Posteriorly and laterally","Laterally and slightly anteriorly","Superiorly","Medially and anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally and slightly anteriorly', 'The glenoid cavity faces laterally and slightly anteriorly, providing a shallow socket for the humeral head. This orientation underlies the joint''s mobility and its tendency toward anterior dislocation.'),
('13cd6eba-5555-576b-9d2a-bd29fa7bb111'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'In Pectoral girdle and axilla, which statement best fits the following fact: which bone forms the pectoral girdle anteriorly?', '["Rib 1","Sternum","Scapula","Humerus","Clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle', 'The clavicle forms the anterior element of the pectoral girdle, connecting the upper limb to the trunk at the sternoclavicular joint. The scapula lies posteriorly, and the sternum and humerus are not part of the girdle itself.'),
('f5a4e6ef-a710-52c5-bdec-6420d762134a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Regarding Pectoral girdle and axilla, what is the correct answer to: the conoid tubercle is located on which part of clavicle?', '["Medial end","Superior surface lateral third","Inferior surface middle third","Superior surface medial third","Inferior surface lateral third"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior surface lateral third', 'The conoid tubercle is an elevation on the inferior surface of the lateral third of the clavicle, giving attachment to the conoid part of the coracoclavicular ligament. It anchors the clavicle to the coracoid process and is not on the medial end or superior surface.'),
('8a46a51f-5328-5f1a-abbf-a8b6e44408cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'For Pectoral girdle and axilla, which option is most appropriate: the sternoclavicular joint is what type of joint?', '["Plane","Ball and socket","Saddle","Hinge","Pivot"]'::jsonb, 'Saddle', 'The sternoclavicular joint is a saddle (sellar) type of synovial joint and the only true articulation between the upper limb and the axial skeleton. Its cavity is divided by an articular disc, and it permits movement about several axes.'),
('61d6406a-7341-501f-9bbf-a2adbe2d2733'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which answer is correct in Pectoral girdle and axilla when asked: which structure passes through the suprascapular notch?', '["Suprascapular nerve","Suprascapular artery","Both nerve and artery","Transverse scapular ligament","Suprascapular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular nerve', 'The suprascapular nerve passes through the suprascapular notch beneath the transverse scapular ligament, whereas the suprascapular artery courses above the ligament. Thus the notch transmits the nerve, not the artery or vein.'),
('b2547406-f7f3-5237-b3a1-5ed31a3f080e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'In Pectoral girdle and axilla, the best response to ''The coracoid process is a feature of which bone'' is:', '["Sternum","Rib 1","Clavicle","Scapula","Humerus"]'::jsonb, 'Scapula', 'The coracoid process projects anterolaterally from the superior border of the scapula and serves as an attachment for the coracobrachialis, short head of biceps, and coracoclavicular ligament. It is not present on the sternum, clavicle, humerus, or first rib.'),
('96635a05-9883-5cb2-abb1-2334f406d2eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'During revision of Pectoral girdle and axilla, which of the following is correct: the axilla is bounded medially by:?', '["Pectoralis minor resulting in decreased functional activity","Pectoralis major accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper ribs and intercostal muscles","Scapula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper ribs and intercostal muscles', 'The medial wall of the axilla is the thoracic wall, formed by the upper four to five ribs and their intercostal muscles, covered by serratus anterior. Pectoralis muscles form the anterior wall, and the scapula contributes to the posterior wall.'),
('5bacd73e-d3d1-59cd-a5cb-9f632732d6e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'In Pectoral girdle and axilla, which statement best fits the following fact: the apex of axilla is bounded by:?', '["First rib resulting in decreased functional activity","Clavicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Scapula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both clavicle and first rib"]'::jsonb, 'Both clavicle and first rib', 'The apex of the axilla is the cervicoaxillary canal bounded by the clavicle, the first rib, and the superior border of the scapula, transmitting the neurovascular bundle. Naming only one bone is incomplete, and the scapula is not the sole boundary.'),
('0d606339-3f64-57c5-920d-5ee30c3e6a12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Regarding Pectoral girdle and axilla, what is the correct answer to: the axillary artery is a continuation of which artery?', '["Brachial","Thoracoacromial","Subclavian","Lateral thoracic","Internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian', 'The axillary artery begins at the outer border of the first rib as the direct continuation of the subclavian artery and ends at the lower border of teres major as the brachial artery. Thoracoacromial, lateral thoracic, and internal thoracic are its branches or related vessels.'),
('e0d1bc29-41e0-551d-bc0e-23bc91a87673'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'For Pectoral girdle and axilla, which option is most appropriate: the lateral thoracic artery arises from which part of axillary artery?', '["First part","Second part","Subclavian","Thoracoacromial","Third part"]'::jsonb, 'Second part', 'The lateral thoracic artery arises from the second part of the axillary artery, which lies behind pectoralis minor, and runs along the lateral chest wall to the breast and serratus anterior. The first part gives the superior thoracic artery, and the third part gives subscapular and circumflex humeral branches.'),
('e3186416-e7e4-5e05-8211-d679f8dee9fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Which answer is correct in Pectoral girdle and axilla when asked: the axillary vein is formed by:?', '["Brachial and cephalic veins","Internal and external jugular","Subclavian and jugular","Cephalic and basilic veins","Basilic and brachial veins"]'::jsonb, 'Basilic and brachial veins', 'The axillary vein is formed at the lower border of teres major by the union of the basilic and brachial veins, then becomes the subclavian vein at the outer border of the first rib. The cephalic vein drains into it separately, and the jugular veins are not involved.'),
('3aabe2ae-ccd4-5d38-ac8a-afdfb61bb9b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'In Pectoral girdle and axilla, the best response to ''The cords of brachial plexus are named according to their relation to:'' is:', '["Axillary artery","Subclavian vein","Clavicle","Subclavian artery","Axillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary artery', 'The lateral, medial, and posterior cords of the brachial plexus are named by their position around the second part of the axillary artery. They are not related to the subclavian vessels or the clavicle for naming purposes.'),
('8c6a0ede-e615-597d-9a13-eb551650a1dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'During revision of Pectoral girdle and axilla, which of the following is correct: which nerve is not a branch of the cords of brachial plexus?', '["Ulnar","Suprascapular","Musculocutaneous","Radial","Median"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular', 'The suprascapular nerve arises from the upper trunk of the brachial plexus, not from any of the cords. Ulnar, musculocutaneous, radial, and median nerves all arise from the cords or their divisions.'),
('34042f56-5893-52ff-8881-9be3776d9e1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'In Pectoral girdle and axilla, which statement best fits the following fact: the long thoracic nerve supplies:?', '["Rhomboids","Trapezius","Pectoralis major","Serratus anterior","Latissimus dorsi"]'::jsonb, 'Serratus anterior', 'The long thoracic nerve descends on the superficial surface of serratus anterior and innervates it, holding the medial border of the scapula against the chest wall; its paralysis causes winging of the scapula. Rhomboids, trapezius, pectoralis major, and latissimus dorsi have different innervations.'),
('cc840716-6b18-5da1-a0f6-cbaaff13c9c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'Regarding Pectoral girdle and axilla, what is the correct answer to: the thoracodorsal nerve supplies:?', '["Teres major","Pectoralis major","Serratus anterior","Subscapularis","Latissimus dorsi"]'::jsonb, 'Latissimus dorsi', 'The thoracodorsal nerve, a branch of the posterior cord, runs along the deep surface of latissimus dorsi and innervates it. Teres major and subscapularis are supplied by the subscapular nerves, and serratus anterior by the long thoracic nerve.'),
('7c98d203-1836-5c75-9e7e-fdfbb698e9fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Upper Limb - Pectoral Girdle Axilla', 'For Pectoral girdle and axilla, which option is most appropriate: the axillary lymph nodes receive lymph from all except:?', '["Thoracic wall","Upper limb","Thumb side of hand","Breast","Lower limb"]'::jsonb, 'Lower limb', 'The axillary nodes drain the upper limb, the breast, and the upper thoracic wall; the thumb side of the hand drains upward through the axilla. The lower limb drains to the inguinal lymph nodes, not the axilla.'),
('2e70182d-9911-58c7-a931-b4a79aa69e74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The ankle joint (talocrural joint) is formed by the articulation of:', '["Calcaneus and talus","Tibia and fibula","Fibula and talus only","Tibia fibula and talus","Tibia and talus only"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia fibula and talus', 'The ankle mortise is formed by the tibia and fibula articulating with the trochlea of the talus, so all three bones are required. The calcaneus is not part of the talocrural joint.'),
('d82fd7ec-6d79-55bd-acd4-c14675d25a17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The ankle joint is what type of joint?', '["Pivot","Hinge (ginglymus) joint","Saddle","Ball and socket","Ellipsoid"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge (ginglymus) joint', 'The talocrural joint permits only dorsiflexion and plantarflexion around a single transverse axis, the defining feature of a hinge (ginglymus) joint. A pivot joint allows rotation, not the movement pattern of the ankle.'),
('df0c344a-1ddd-5430-b996-172b3a340fd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial malleolus is part of which bone?', '["Talus","Navicular","Calcaneus","Fibula","Tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia', 'The medial malleolus is the distal projection of the tibia that forms the medial wall of the ankle mortise. The fibula contributes the lateral malleolus, while the other options are tarsal bones.'),
('e389a971-0056-585f-b274-ef2e71d25137'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral malleolus is part of which bone?', '["Fibula","Cuboid","Calcaneus","Talus","Tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Fibula', 'The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula and forms the lateral wall of the ankle mortise. It extends lower than the medial malleolus, and none of the tarsal bones forms a malleolus.'),
('edb134d6-1f00-5886-ae94-130ab18fa090'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral malleolus extends more distally than the medial malleolus by about:', '["4 cm","0.5 cm","3 cm","2 cm","1 cm"]'::jsonb, '1 cm', 'The fibular lateral malleolus projects about 1 cm further distally than the tibial medial malleolus. This asymmetry directs the foot into inversion, which is why inversion sprains are far more common than eversion injuries.'),
('3e0e5109-f5e9-536a-afc5-87c98aa4b834'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The talar trochlea is wider anteriorly than posteriorly. This means:', '["There is difference resulting in decreased functional activity","The ankle is more stable in dorsiflexion (talus fits tightly in mortise)","The ankle is more stable in plantarflexion mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ankle is unstable in dorsiflexion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ankle is more stable in dorsiflexion (talus fits tightly in mortise)', 'Because the trochlea is wider in front, the talus wedges tightly into the mortise during dorsiflexion, making the ankle most stable (close-packed) in that position. In plantarflexion the narrower posterior part lies in the mortise, leaving the joint relatively loose and unstable.'),
('3f43d704-6c88-5547-9acc-41dff71d95c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The capsular ligament of the ankle joint is:', '["Thin anteriorly and posteriorly but strengthened laterally and medially by collateral ligaments","Thick all around accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thick anteriorly only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thin all around leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin anteriorly and posteriorly but strengthened laterally and medially by collateral ligaments', 'The ankle capsule is weak and thin in front and behind to allow dorsiflexion and plantarflexion, but it is reinforced on the medial side by the deltoid ligament and laterally by the three bands of the lateral collateral ligament.'),
('81dd0ec8-42b8-5679-bbaf-553071d34ea9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The deltoid ligament has how many components?', '["4 (tibionavicular tibiocalcaneal anterior tibiotalar posterior tibiotalar)","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 (tibionavicular tibiocalcaneal anterior tibiotalar posterior tibiotalar)', 'The deltoid (medial collateral) ligament has four parts: tibionavicular, tibiocalcaneal, anterior tibiotalar, and posterior tibiotalar. All four components span from the medial malleolus to the tarsal bones.'),
('cba992a6-cc2c-5009-9663-7585e1572b8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The deltoid ligament resists:', '["Eversion (valgus) stress at the ankle","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plantarflexion mediated by specific receptor activation","Inversion (varus) stress without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsiflexion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eversion (valgus) stress at the ankle', 'The strong deltoid ligament on the medial side resists eversion (valgus) stress. The weaker lateral collateral ligaments, especially the anterior talofibular ligament, resist inversion (varus) stress and are torn far more often.'),
('f16c7787-25d8-595d-b0ec-c0d9f6eea43f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral ligament of the ankle consists of how many separate bands?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","3 (anterior talofibular calcaneofibular posterior talofibular)","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (anterior talofibular calcaneofibular posterior talofibular)', 'The lateral collateral ligament has three discrete bands: the anterior talofibular, calcaneofibular, and posterior talofibular ligaments. These three together brace the lateral side of the joint.'),
('bac1fbd2-d049-59fb-8e74-3c6c9fe65977'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) originates from:', '["Lateral malleolus (anterior border)","Medial malleolus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Talus mediated by specific receptor activation","Navicular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcaneus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral malleolus (anterior border)', 'The anterior talofibular ligament arises from the anterior border of the lateral malleolus and passes forward to the neck of the talus. It is the weakest of the lateral ligaments and the one most frequently torn in inversion sprains.'),
('0b83554a-6171-5bf5-9fb8-b9cdc1d6eaca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The calcaneofibular ligament (CFL) originates from:', '["Cuboid","Calcaneus","Medial malleolus","Talus","Lateral malleolus (tip)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral malleolus (tip)', 'The calcaneofibular ligament arises from the tip of the lateral malleolus and runs downward and backward to the lateral surface of the calcaneus. It spans both the ankle and the subtalar joint.'),
('5398614b-5bd5-57c4-97cc-a5147930bcaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The posterior talofibular ligament (PTFL) originates from:', '["Talus resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral malleolus (medial surface)","Calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial malleolus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral malleolus (medial surface)', 'The posterior talofibular ligament arises from the medial surface of the lateral malleolus and passes horizontally to the posterior tubercle of the talus. It is the strongest and least commonly injured lateral band.'),
('32be0682-11b4-5555-b197-a8e7acb37c63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The most commonly injured ankle ligament is:', '["None","Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)","Deltoid ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","Posterior talofibular ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)', 'The anterior talofibular ligament is the weakest lateral ligament and is torn first in the typical inversion ankle sprain. The posterior talofibular ligament is the strongest lateral band and is rarely damaged.'),
('9ad7397e-f92c-5209-9c4a-97a4567d94f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The ankle is most stable in:', '["Dorsiflexion (close-packed)","Inversion","Plantarflexion","Neutral","Eversion"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflexion (close-packed)', 'Dorsiflexion is the close-packed position of the ankle, where the wide anterior part of the talar trochlea is gripped tightly in the mortise, giving maximal stability. Stability is lost during plantarflexion.'),
('fe6a393f-388d-5c0d-be08-b956bb73b940'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The ankle is least stable in:', '["Eversion resulting in decreased functional activity","Inversion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsiflexion mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plantarflexion (loose-packed)"]'::jsonb, 'Plantarflexion (loose-packed)', 'In plantarflexion the narrower posterior part of the talar trochlea sits in the mortise, a loose-packed position that allows some sideways movement and predisposes the joint to inversion injury.'),
('747a1988-525a-5b7a-9b7b-67c87a3c0a9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The blood supply of the ankle joint is from:', '["Malleolar branches of anterior and posterior tibial and peroneal arteries","Femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Popliteal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Profunda femoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malleolar branches of anterior and posterior tibial and peroneal arteries', 'The ankle joint is supplied by the malleolar branches of the anterior tibial, posterior tibial, and peroneal arteries. The femoral, popliteal, and profunda femoris arteries are more proximal vessels of the thigh and knee.'),
('d71da2f7-7ebf-59cf-b67d-0b328b2a4d67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The nerve supply of the ankle joint is from:', '["Superior gluteal resulting in decreased functional activity","Obturator nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep peroneal tibial and sural nerves","Femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal tibial and sural nerves', 'The ankle joint is innervated by the deep peroneal (fibular) nerve, the tibial nerve, and the sural nerve, all derived from the sciatic system. The femoral, obturator, and superior gluteal nerves serve the thigh and do not reach the ankle.'),
('7053d282-2284-5c72-be88-2caab69fdf85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The subtalar (talocalcaneal) joint is a:', '["Ball and socket","Plane synovial joint","Pivot","Hinge","Saddle"]'::jsonb, 'Plane synovial joint', 'The subtalar joint between the talus and calcaneus is a plane synovial joint whose gliding movement contributes to inversion and eversion. The ankle joint above it is the hinge joint.'),
('08695445-669c-51d1-bc7b-974c6c5d8727'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The subtalar joint consists of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Both anterior and posterior joints accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior talocalcaneal joint (between body of talus and calcaneus)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior talocalcaneal joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior talocalcaneal joint (between body of talus and calcaneus)', 'The subtalar joint is the posterior talocalcaneal articulation between the body of the talus and the calcaneus. The anterior articulation involves the head of the talus and belongs to the talocalcaneonavicular joint.'),
('5df222a5-054d-52aa-b869-4f2bcc8600ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The talocalcaneonavicular joint includes:', '["Talus and calcaneus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus and navicular only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus and cuboid only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head of talus navicular and calcaneus (and plantar calcaneonavicular ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'Head of talus navicular and calcaneus (and plantar calcaneonavicular ligament)', 'The talocalcaneonavicular joint is formed by the head of the talus fitting into the navicular, the sustentaculum tali of the calcaneus, and the plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament. The cuboid is not part of this joint.'),
('32c8ca03-6763-597d-822c-70bf538460e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The sinus tarsi is:', '["A medial space resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The tarsal tunnel mediated by specific receptor activation","A plantar space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A canal between the talus and calcaneus (contains interosseous talocalcaneal ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'A canal between the talus and calcaneus (contains interosseous talocalcaneal ligament)', 'The sinus tarsi is the cone-shaped canal between the talus and calcaneus that houses the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament. The tarsal tunnel is a separate fibro-osseous space on the medial side that transmits the tibial nerve and long tendons.'),
('688a1434-2dca-57d2-a56f-a0367f90dad4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The interosseous talocalcaneal ligament is located:', '["In the tarsal tunnel resulting in decreased functional activity","In the sinus tarsi (connecting talus and calcaneus)","On the medial ankle mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the lateral ankle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the sinus tarsi (connecting talus and calcaneus)', 'The interosseous talocalcaneal ligament lies within the sinus tarsi, binding the talus to the calcaneus and helping to resist talar tilting. It is a key stabilizer of the talocalcaneal articulation.'),
('3f301f58-9abd-5b7a-8092-8b8be693616c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The midtarsal (Chopart''s) joint consists of:', '["Talonavicular only","Calcaneocuboid only","Talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints","Talocalcaneal and calcaneocuboid","Subtalar and talonavicular"]'::jsonb, 'Talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints', 'The midtarsal (transverse tarsal, Chopart) joint is formed by the talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints together, lying between the hindfoot and the midfoot. The subtalar joint lies more posteriorly and is not part of Chopart''s joint.'),
('d4c892a2-fde0-52ea-b818-2e8a0579595c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The transverse tarsal joint (Chopart) is important because:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","It allows inversion and eversion of the forefoot on the hindfoot","It is the Lisfranc joint mediated by specific receptor activation","It is the ankle joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is the subtalar joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It allows inversion and eversion of the forefoot on the hindfoot', 'The transverse tarsal joint permits the forefoot to invert and evert on the hindfoot, working with the subtalar joint to produce inversion-eversion of the whole foot. The Lisfranc joint is the tarsometatarsal complex more distally.'),
('d83a7228-c3a7-5c3b-a24b-95bfda95f51a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The subtalar and midtarsal joints together enable:', '["Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion resulting in decreased functional activity","Inversion and eversion of the foot","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Toe flexion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inversion and eversion of the foot', 'The subtalar and midtarsal joints act together to produce inversion and eversion of the foot. Dorsiflexion and plantarflexion occur at the ankle joint, and toe flexion at the metatarsophalangeal and interphalangeal joints.'),
('5d21376b-0c5c-5f69-a086-926ecef2419b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Lisfranc joint is the:', '["Midtarsal joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Tarsometatarsal joint complex (between cuneiforms/cuboid and metatarsals)","Subtalar joint mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ankle joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tarsometatarsal joint complex (between cuneiforms/cuboid and metatarsals)', 'The Lisfranc joint is the tarsometatarsal joint complex joining the three cuneiforms and the cuboid to the bases of the metatarsals. The midtarsal (Chopart) joint lies more proximally between the hindfoot and midfoot.'),
('f58b810f-5b8b-5584-b2f8-9c4857d49224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Lisfranc ligament is a strong interosseous ligament connecting:', '["None","Medial cuneiform to base of 2nd metatarsal","Medial cuneiform to navicular","Cuboid to 5th metatarsal","Calcaneus to cuboid"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cuneiform to base of 2nd metatarsal', 'The Lisfranc ligament is an interosseous band running from the medial cuneiform to the base of the second metatarsal, the key stabilizer of the midfoot. Injury to this ligament produces Lisfranc fracture-dislocations.'),
('0cbb8981-042d-5c6b-8dac-320cdda5e5b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The second metatarsal is recessed (keystone) into the tarsometatarsal joint because:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Its base is square accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Its base is round mediated by specific receptor activation","Its base is wedged between the medial and lateral cuneiforms","Its base is flat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Its base is wedged between the medial and lateral cuneiforms', 'The base of the second metatarsal is recessed into the tarsometatarsal joint, wedged between the medial and lateral cuneiforms, which locks the midfoot. This keystone arrangement is why Lisfranc injuries classically involve the second metatarsal.'),
('2fdc26ac-a462-5d25-87ac-af5037c4bb08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar aponeurosis (fascia) originates from:', '["Achilles tendon","Medial tubercle of calcaneus","Calcaneal tuberosity posterior","None","Lateral malleolus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial tubercle of calcaneus', 'The plantar aponeurosis arises from the medial tubercle of the calcaneus and fans out distally toward the toes. The Achilles tendon inserts on the posterior part of the calcaneal tuberosity, not on the origin of the plantar fascia.'),
('f9f3f982-8ee8-5b52-ad22-ec389135946b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar aponeurosis has how many parts?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 (central medial lateral)","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (central medial lateral)', 'The plantar aponeurosis consists of three parts: a thick strong central part and thinner medial and lateral parts. The central part is the portion implicated in plantar fasciitis.'),
('447b8b11-cf69-5007-b859-da5c2af1c22b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The central part of the plantar aponeurosis attaches distally to:', '["The metatarsal heads resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The flexor tendon sheaths and the base of the proximal phalanges (via five slips)","The tarsals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The distal phalanges leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The flexor tendon sheaths and the base of the proximal phalanges (via five slips)', 'The central part of the plantar aponeurosis divides into five slips that attach to the flexor tendon sheaths and the bases of the proximal phalanges. These attachments allow it to support the longitudinal arch through the windlass mechanism.'),
('59d57fbf-0dd5-5bec-8e89-d9905f876933'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The windlass mechanism of the foot is:', '["The action of tibialis posterior resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The plantar fascia tightening during dorsiflexion of the toes (helping to elevate the arch)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The action of peroneus longus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The plantar fascia tightening during dorsiflexion of the toes (helping to elevate the arch)', 'The windlass mechanism describes the plantar fascia tightening as the toes dorsiflex, which elevates and supports the longitudinal arch during push-off. Tibialis posterior and peroneus longus provide active muscular support but are not the windlass.'),
('277d61e8-a5c4-58d3-9317-72c6d597a2ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Plantar fasciitis is:', '["Inflammation of the plantar aponeurosis at its calcaneal origin (heel pain)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation of the tarsal tunnel mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammation of the Achilles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the plantar aponeurosis at its calcaneal origin (heel pain)', 'Plantar fasciitis is a degenerative inflammation of the plantar aponeurosis at its calcaneal origin, causing heel pain that is typically worst with the first steps in the morning. Achilles tendinitis and tarsal tunnel syndrome are separate diagnoses.'),
('2f170fcd-c34c-5a82-9b8d-8d9c7545f36f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial plantar nerve (L4 L5) supplies:', '["Quadratus plantae resulting in decreased functional activity","Abductor hallucis FDB flexor hallucis brevis (medial part) and 1st lumbrical","All interossei mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abductor digiti minimi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor hallucis FDB flexor hallucis brevis (medial part) and 1st lumbrical', 'The medial plantar nerve (L4, L5) innervates abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, the medial part of flexor hallucis brevis, and the first lumbrical, plus the medial sole skin. The interossei and abductor digiti minimi are supplied by the lateral plantar nerve.'),
('9c827bfb-0a50-5edd-abbc-d9e83253c99c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral plantar nerve (S1 S2) supplies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","FDB accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FHL mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Abductor hallucis without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All other intrinsic foot muscles (abductor digiti minimi quadratus plantae flexor digiti minimi adductor hallucis interossei lumbricals 2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'All other intrinsic foot muscles (abductor digiti minimi quadratus plantae flexor digiti minimi adductor hallucis interossei lumbricals 2-4)', 'The lateral plantar nerve (S1, S2) supplies the remaining intrinsic muscles of the foot: abductor digiti minimi, quadratus plantae, flexor digiti minimi brevis, adductor hallucis, all interossei, and the second to fourth lumbricals. Flexor digitorum brevis and abductor hallucis belong to the medial plantar nerve.'),
('c32112e5-043b-5a8e-aade-051141da9481'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial plantar artery is a branch of:', '["Dorsalis pedis resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior tibial artery (in the tarsal tunnel)","Peroneal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior tibial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior tibial artery (in the tarsal tunnel)', 'The posterior tibial artery divides in the tarsal tunnel into the smaller medial plantar and larger lateral plantar arteries. The dorsalis pedis artery is the distal continuation of the anterior tibial artery and is a separate vessel.'),
('82aa1223-f358-5b55-9c9b-e2d1ab0c87b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral plantar artery is a branch of:', '["None","Anterior tibial artery","Dorsalis pedis","Posterior tibial artery","Peroneal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior tibial artery', 'The lateral plantar artery is the larger terminal branch of the posterior tibial artery, dividing from it in the tarsal tunnel. The anterior tibial and peroneal arteries give rise to other vessels of the leg and ankle.'),
('365ad715-0fc7-5715-a9e1-43a418454db1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar arch (deep plantar arch) is formed mainly by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Peroneal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial plantar artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral plantar artery anastomosing with the deep plantar branch of dorsalis pedis","Anterior tibial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar artery anastomosing with the deep plantar branch of dorsalis pedis', 'The deep plantar arch is formed chiefly by the lateral plantar artery curving across the sole and anastomosing with the deep plantar branch of the dorsalis pedis artery in the first intermetatarsal space. The medial plantar artery and peroneal artery do not form this arch.'),
('2915e4f3-3192-573f-9516-3d14ad6fd6c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar arch gives off:', '["Common digital arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Plantar metatarsal arteries and perforating branches","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Proper plantar digital without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Plantar metatarsal arteries and perforating branches', 'The deep plantar arch gives off plantar metatarsal arteries and perforating branches that supply the metatarsal region and communicate with the dorsal circulation. Proper plantar digital arteries arise more distally from the metatarsal vessels.'),
('c5037bee-f6ac-57bc-be85-ea3cab0bd39c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dorsalis pedis artery is the continuation of:', '["Popliteal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior tibial artery (at the ankle)","Posterior tibial artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peroneal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior tibial artery (at the ankle)', 'The dorsalis pedis artery is the continuation of the anterior tibial artery distal to the ankle joint, passing to the dorsum of the foot. It is palpable between the tendons of extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus.'),
('5d7222ff-e8ac-5b1e-90d3-534b6dde3532'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dorsalis pedis artery gives off:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial plantar artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral plantar artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arcuate artery and first dorsal metatarsal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate artery and first dorsal metatarsal artery', 'Important branches of the dorsalis pedis artery include the arcuate artery and the first dorsal metatarsal artery. The medial and lateral plantar arteries arise from the posterior tibial artery, not from the dorsalis pedis.'),
('cccc97cb-4db3-5fe9-bafd-446d05a169ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The arcuate artery arises from:', '["Anterior tibial artery","Dorsalis pedis artery","Posterior tibial artery","Peroneal artery","None"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsalis pedis artery', 'The arcuate artery arises from the dorsalis pedis artery at the level of the tarsometatarsal joints and curves laterally across the bases of the metatarsals, giving off the dorsal metatarsal arteries.'),
('fdf32919-1e3b-5c23-8411-425c3ff4cab5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The great saphenous vein originates from:', '["Lateral end of the arch resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Popliteal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Small saphenous vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial end of the dorsal venous arch (medial marginal vein)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial end of the dorsal venous arch (medial marginal vein)', 'The great saphenous vein arises from the medial end of the dorsal venous arch (medial marginal vein) and ascends anterior to the medial malleolus. The lateral end of the arch gives rise to the small saphenous vein.'),
('7b8e7cbd-6471-54dd-a3a9-724976fca13a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The small saphenous vein originates from:', '["Medial end of the arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Popliteal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Great saphenous vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral end of the dorsal venous arch (lateral marginal vein)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral end of the dorsal venous arch (lateral marginal vein)', 'The small saphenous vein arises from the lateral end of the dorsal venous arch (lateral marginal vein) and runs posterior to the lateral malleolus. The medial end of the arch forms the great saphenous vein.'),
('37eaaa6c-545f-5170-bcbb-4aee9111f416'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The great saphenous vein ascends:', '["Anterior to the medial malleolus (then up the medial leg)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior to the lateral malleolus mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior to the medial malleolus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior to the lateral malleolus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the medial malleolus (then up the medial leg)', 'The great saphenous vein passes anterior to the medial malleolus, the classic site for venous cutdown, and then ascends along the medial side of the leg to the saphenofemoral junction in the groin.'),
('b73a2617-36a4-5f6b-b36e-2e450c5eb82a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The small saphenous vein ascends:', '["Posterior to the medial malleolus resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior to the lateral malleolus (then up the posterior calf)","Anterior to the medial malleolus mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior to the lateral malleolus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the lateral malleolus (then up the posterior calf)', 'The small saphenous vein passes posterior to the lateral malleolus and ascends the middle of the posterior calf to drain into the popliteal vein in the popliteal fossa.'),
('6a83e76b-2471-59d8-adb2-dfd27a11c2ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The superficial fibular (peroneal) nerve supplies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The heel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin of the distal anterior leg and dorsum of the foot (except the first web space)","The sole of the foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The first web space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the distal anterior leg and dorsum of the foot (except the first web space)', 'The superficial fibular nerve supplies the skin of the distal anterior leg and the dorsum of the foot, sparing the first web space, which is innervated by the deep fibular nerve. The sole of the foot is supplied by the medial and lateral plantar branches of the tibial nerve.'),
('9228494d-09c6-524c-9997-1a6e272b09b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The deep fibular (peroneal) nerve supplies:', '["The dorsum of the foot resulting in decreased functional activity","The heel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin of the first web space of the foot (between great and 2nd toes)","The sole of the foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the first web space of the foot (between great and 2nd toes)', 'The deep fibular nerve supplies the skin of the first web space between the great and second toes, and it also innervates the muscles of the anterior compartment of the leg. The general dorsum of the foot is supplied by the superficial fibular nerve.'),
('37dec99d-e597-590d-a2f4-321bda290bf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The sural nerve supplies:', '["Skin of the lateral side of the foot and little toe","Heel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial foot mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum of foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First web space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the lateral side of the foot and little toe', 'The sural nerve, formed by branches of the tibial and common fibular nerves, supplies the skin of the lateral side of the foot and the little toe. The medial foot is supplied by the saphenous nerve and the first web space by the deep fibular nerve.'),
('e4859ba8-912f-5e38-842a-b6363cc9b886'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The saphenous nerve supplies:', '["Posterior foot resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsum of foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heel mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin of the medial side of the leg and foot (as far as the medial malleolus)","Lateral foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the medial side of the leg and foot (as far as the medial malleolus)', 'The saphenous nerve is the largest cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve; it descends with the long saphenous vein and supplies skin on the medial side of the leg and foot down to the medial malleolus. The posterior foot, dorsum, heel, and lateral foot are innervated by tibial, peroneal, and sural nerves.'),
('59eb484c-6de2-520f-8872-ab257c903e2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve supply:', '["The dorsum of the foot","The first web space","None","The lateral foot","Skin of the heel (medial calcaneal)"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the heel (medial calcaneal)', 'The medial calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve pierce the flexor retinaculum to supply the skin of the heel and the medial sole. The dorsum, first web space, and lateral foot are territories of the peroneal and sural nerves.'),
('845bfa25-9d9f-5afc-a609-9bd5252780bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The heel (calcaneal) fat pad is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The insertion of the Achilles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The origin of the plantar fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","A specialized shock-absorbing structure under the calcaneus","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A specialized shock-absorbing structure under the calcaneus', 'The heel fat pad is a specialized fibrofatty cushion beneath the calcaneus that dissipates impact during weight-bearing. The Achilles tendon inserts on the posterior tuberosity, and the plantar fascia arises from the calcaneal tuberosity, not from the fat pad.'),
('f19b0aeb-2f18-5b93-bba9-9657311cd27e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar digital nerves and arteries supply:', '["The toes (proper plantar digital branches)","The dorsum of the toes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The heel mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tarsal bones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The toes (proper plantar digital branches)', 'The plantar digital nerves and arteries are the terminal branches of the medial and lateral plantar neurovascular bundles, supplying the toes via proper plantar digital branches. The dorsum of the toes and the tarsal bones receive supply from other pedal vessels and nerves.'),
('6ed7c68a-68af-510b-8764-b369e609b18f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Corns and calluses on the foot are caused by:', '["Neuropathy resulting in decreased functional activity","Friction and pressure over bony prominences (hyperkeratosis)","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular insufficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Friction and pressure over bony prominences (hyperkeratosis)', 'Corns and calluses are localized thickenings of the stratum corneum (hyperkeratosis) produced by chronic friction and pressure over bony prominences. Neuropathy, infection, and vascular insufficiency can complicate the condition but do not directly create these lesions.'),
('4c03aca4-29fa-522f-b21b-71b45cd92a53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Morton''s neuroma is:', '["A ganglionic cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nerve tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A perineural fibrosis of the common plantar digital nerve (usually 3rd web space)","Arthritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A perineural fibrosis of the common plantar digital nerve (usually 3rd web space)', 'Morton neuroma is a perineural fibrosis, a benign thickening of the common plantar digital nerve, typically in the third web space. It is not a true tumor, a ganglionic cyst, or an inflammatory arthritis.'),
('10b3840c-93f6-56fd-98e3-4c368e592f9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Morton''s neuroma causes:', '["Arch pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Toe pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heel pain mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain in the forefoot (3rd web space) exacerbated by walking"]'::jsonb, 'Pain in the forefoot (3rd web space) exacerbated by walking', 'Morton neuroma classically causes forefoot pain in the third interspace that is exacerbated by walking or tight shoes and relieved by rest. Pain strictly in the arch, heel, or a single toe implicates other structures.'),
('693da5db-dfcc-5a3d-a624-5394f82fedc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Clubfoot (congenital talipes equinovarus) involves:', '["High arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Pes planus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneovalgus mediated by specific receptor activation","Flat foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Equinus varus and adductus of the foot"]'::jsonb, 'Equinus varus and adductus of the foot', 'Congenital talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) comprises equinus, varus, and adductus of the forefoot with supination of the hindfoot. Pes planus, a high arch, and calcaneovalgus describe different foot shapes.'),
('5204f8eb-355b-5e65-8de3-496ba508e79a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Talipes calcaneovalgus is:', '["Plantarflexion and inversion resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsiflexion and eversion of the foot (opposite of clubfoot)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Equinovarus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flatfoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflexion and eversion of the foot (opposite of clubfoot)', 'Talipes calcaneovalgus is a positional deformity with dorsiflexion and eversion of the foot, the opposite of clubfoot. Plantarflexion with inversion characterizes equinovarus deformity.'),
('3581414c-ed5f-5d53-b61c-57c6b6108fac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The sustentaculum tali is a projection of the:', '["Tibia resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuboid mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus (medially for support of the talus)","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus (medially for support of the talus)', 'The sustentaculum tali is a medial shelf-like projection of the calcaneus that supports the talus and grooves the flexor hallucis longus tendon. It is part of the calcaneus, not the tibia, talus, cuboid, or navicular.'),
('3548772d-1433-5118-998b-9b5f25adc5be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The peroneal trochlea is a projection of the:', '["Cuboid resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus (laterally separating peroneal tendons)","Talus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus (laterally separating peroneal tendons)', 'The peroneal trochlea is a small lateral projection on the calcaneus that separates the peroneus longus and brevis tendons. This bony landmark belongs to the calcaneus rather than the cuboid, fibula, talus, or navicular.'),
('de3cc609-272c-5b5b-9a4b-4f35c7d0ef31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The tarsal tunnel is bounded by:', '["Peroneal retinaculum resulting in decreased functional activity","Flexor retinaculum (from medial malleolus to calcaneus)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extensor retinaculum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor retinaculum (from medial malleolus to calcaneus)', 'The tarsal tunnel is roofed by the flexor retinaculum, which spans from the medial malleolus to the calcaneus and encloses the structures behind the medial malleolus. The peroneal and extensor retinacula lie on the lateral and anterior aspects of the ankle.'),
('66762a13-3169-5fa3-bc3c-a1c29d13c40c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Tarsal tunnel syndrome results from compression of:', '["Tibial nerve (or its branches medial and lateral plantar)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sural nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Saphenous nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep peroneal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (or its branches medial and lateral plantar)', 'Tarsal tunnel syndrome results from entrapment of the tibial nerve or its medial and lateral plantar branches beneath the flexor retinaculum. The sural, saphenous, and deep peroneal nerves course outside this tunnel.'),
('f0962e0d-42d7-5a3e-b044-d17c79eafbf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Symptoms of tarsal tunnel syndrome include:', '["Burning pain and paresthesia in the sole of the foot (worse at night)","Heel pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior leg pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal foot pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral foot pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Burning pain and paresthesia in the sole of the foot (worse at night)', 'Compression of the tibial nerve in the tarsal tunnel produces burning pain and paresthesia in the sole of the foot that worsens at night and with prolonged standing. Pain confined to the heel, anterior leg, or lateral foot suggests a different nerve is involved.'),
('c1797ae1-db29-52ea-9c41-ec9c9b25fb03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The first web space of the foot is supplied by:', '["Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'The first web space on the dorsum of the foot is supplied by the terminal branch of the deep peroneal nerve. The saphenous, sural, tibial, and superficial peroneal nerves supply other cutaneous territories of the foot.'),
('db07d144-4505-5603-a516-a3d4be3f5c03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial side of the great toe (dorsal) is supplied by:', '["Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve (L4)"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve (L4)', 'The dorsal aspect of the medial side of the great toe is supplied by the superficial peroneal nerve, which carries L4 fibers. The saphenous nerve ends at the medial malleolus region and the deep peroneal nerve supplies the first web space.'),
('03e52511-ee95-5cd5-a913-141072b21d54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral side of the little toe is supplied by:', '["Saphenous","Tibial","Deep peroneal","Sural nerve (S1)","Superficial peroneal"]'::jsonb, 'Sural nerve (S1)', 'The lateral side of the little toe is supplied by the sural nerve, derived from S1. The saphenous, tibial, deep peroneal, and superficial peroneal nerves innervate other regions of the foot.'),
('dc8dbd21-66bd-5442-8185-6f9057d8caf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dermatome L4 can be tested at:', '["The lateral foot","The knee","The heel","The medial side of the great toe","The first web space"]'::jsonb, 'The medial side of the great toe', 'The L4 dermatome can be tested on the medial side of the great toe. The first web space corresponds to L5 and the lateral side of the foot to S1.'),
('9eade3c0-03d5-594a-90cf-410618fc152a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dermatome L5 can be tested at:', '["The popliteal fossa","The lateral foot","The heel","The great toe medial","The first web space (dorsal)"]'::jsonb, 'The first web space (dorsal)', 'The L5 dermatome can be tested over the first web space on the dorsum of the foot. The medial side of the great toe is L4 and the lateral foot and little toe are S1.'),
('3792227f-9cbe-56b9-a50f-8c4feeb7a2ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dermatome S1 can be tested at:', '["The lateral side of the foot (little toe)","The heel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The popliteal fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","The medial foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The first web space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral side of the foot (little toe)', 'The S1 dermatome is tested over the lateral sole, the lateral side of the foot, and the little toe. The medial great toe, first web space, and medial foot test L4 or L5.'),
('82b72fb9-3f7c-50a8-bb9a-fd38163dadb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The calcaneal tuberosity (posterior) has medial and lateral processes. The medial process gives attachment to:', '["Achilles tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","FHL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibialis posterior mediated by specific receptor activation","Abductor hallucis and FDB (and plantar fascia)","FDL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor hallucis and FDB (and plantar fascia)', 'The medial process of the calcaneal tuberosity gives attachment to the abductor hallucis, the flexor digitorum brevis, and the plantar aponeurosis. The Achilles tendon inserts on the posterior surface and FHL, FDL, and tibialis posterior attach further anteriorly.'),
('08211f4f-74fd-5ba9-b39e-abf9c9b54cf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral calcaneal tuberosity gives origin to:', '["Abductor hallucis","FDB","None","Flexor hallucis brevis","Abductor digiti minimi"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor digiti minimi', 'The lateral process of the calcaneal tuberosity gives origin to the abductor digiti minimi. Abductor hallucis and flexor hallucis brevis arise from the medial process or adjacent medial structures.'),
('6f989c70-a454-51a4-ab25-7371a35e2ff8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The sinus tarsi approach is used for:', '["Ankle fracture surgery","Tarsal tunnel release","Lisfranc reconstruction","Surgery on the subtalar joint","None"]'::jsonb, 'Surgery on the subtalar joint', 'The sinus tarsi approach gives direct access to the subtalar joint, commonly used for subtalar arthrodesis and open reduction of calcaneal fractures. Tarsal tunnel release, ankle fracture fixation, and Lisfranc reconstruction use different surgical exposures.'),
('3df59218-15d9-5620-9f27-4d1d8b6f10fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The calcaneus transmits body weight from the talus to the ground. It articulates superiorly with:', '["Tibia resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus (via three facets: anterior middle posterior)","Navicular mediated by specific receptor activation","Cuboid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Talus (via three facets: anterior middle posterior)', 'Superiorly the calcaneus articulates with the talus through the anterior, middle, and posterior facets, which form the subtalar joint. It does not articulate superiorly with the tibia, navicular, or cuboid.'),
('0ae81c40-3288-5e48-a919-af95c0ad17b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Gissane''s angle (critical angle of the calcaneus) is formed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The angle of the talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two strong cortical struts on the lateral calcaneus (between posterior facet and anterior process)","The ankle joint line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two strong cortical struts on the lateral calcaneus (between posterior facet and anterior process)', 'Gissane''s (critical) angle is formed where two strong cortical struts of the lateral calcaneus meet between the posterior facet and the anterior process. It is a calcaneal radiographic landmark, not a talar or ankle joint measurement.'),
('7decd5eb-4e3b-5d2a-bddb-0b4659ce87ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Bohler''s angle of the calcaneus is measured from:', '["The subtalar joint line resulting in decreased functional activity","The highest point of the anterior and posterior facets to the tuberosity","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The ankle joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The highest point of the anterior and posterior facets to the tuberosity', 'Bohler''s angle is measured from the highest points of the anterior and posterior facets to the highest point of the posterior tuberosity of the calcaneus. It is used to assess collapse of calcaneal fractures.'),
('6e9a9a05-87a1-524e-9f6d-586661ed368d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The talus has no muscular attachments. Its blood supply enters via:', '["The posterior process resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The talar neck mediated by specific receptor activation","The articular surface without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tarsal canal and the sinus tarsi (with anastomotic sling)"]'::jsonb, 'The tarsal canal and the sinus tarsi (with anastomotic sling)', 'The talus receives blood from an anastomotic sling formed by vessels entering through the tarsal canal and the sinus tarsi. Because most of the bone is covered by articular cartilage, this retrograde supply explains its susceptibility to ischemia.'),
('ba56f6a9-1422-54e9-9220-b4a77461594b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The talus is vulnerable to:', '["Arthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Avascular necrosis (particularly the body after talar neck fractures)","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Fracture non-union without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Avascular necrosis (particularly the body after talar neck fractures)', 'The talus is especially vulnerable to avascular necrosis, particularly of the body, after displaced talar neck fractures that disrupt its retrograde blood supply. Arthritis, infection, and non-union are general fracture complications rather than the signature hazard of the talus.'),
('57878694-6981-5437-8f33-86a20473945e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Hawkins sign (subchondral lucency of the talar dome) indicates:', '["AVN of the talus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Preserved blood supply to the talus (good sign after talar neck fracture)","Fracture of the talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preserved blood supply to the talus (good sign after talar neck fracture)', 'The Hawkins sign is a subchondral lucency of the talar dome seen weeks after a talar neck fracture, indicating preserved blood supply to the talus and predicting a favorable outcome. Its delayed or absent appearance suggests developing avascular necrosis.'),
('f2a8e1a3-bf26-5939-b1bc-69d8b87cc632'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The navicular bone articulates proximally with:', '["None","Cuboid","Cuneiforms","Talar head","Calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Talar head', 'The navicular articulates proximally with the head of the talus at the talonavicular joint. Distally it articulates with the three cuneiforms and laterally with the cuboid.'),
('8cc438fe-1068-5fba-a9b5-4bb375fc22b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The navicular bone articulates distally with:', '["Calcaneus resulting in decreased functional activity","Metatarsals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Three cuneiforms (medial intermediate lateral)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuboid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Three cuneiforms (medial intermediate lateral)', 'The navicular articulates distally with the three cuneiforms: the medial, intermediate, and lateral. The talar head, cuboid, calcaneus, and metatarsals articulate with the navicular at other sites or not at all.'),
('18549f3f-c69f-5373-8a1b-489ad896b0f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Mueller-Weiss syndrome is:', '["Spontaneous avascular necrosis of the navicular bone in adults","Arthritis of the navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Fracture of the navicular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stress fracture of the navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spontaneous avascular necrosis of the navicular bone in adults', 'Mueller-Weiss syndrome is a spontaneous (idiopathic) adult-onset avascular necrosis of the navicular that often leads to collapse and flatfoot. It is distinct from traumatic navicular fracture, arthritis, or childhood osteochondrosis.'),
('b40af0c0-1d48-5edd-ac48-010c72a49518'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Kohler''s disease is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteochondrosis of the navicular in children (self-limiting)","Apophysitis of the calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Fracture of the calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AVN of the talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteochondrosis of the navicular in children (self-limiting)', 'Kohler disease is an osteochondrosis of the navicular in children that is usually self-limiting with conservative management. It does not involve apophysitis of the calcaneus or avascular necrosis of the talus.'),
('42ece167-094c-5112-bd89-dbb686b9ccb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Freiberg''s disease is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteochondrosis of the 2nd metatarsal head","AVN of the talus mediated by specific receptor activation","Apophysitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AVN of the navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osteochondrosis of the 2nd metatarsal head', 'Freiberg disease is an osteochondrosis, often an avascular necrosis, of the second metatarsal head seen mainly in adolescents. The talus, navicular, and apophyses are not affected.'),
('a573b300-df54-54e4-b1ca-b06e6e4f20be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The cuboid bone articulates proximally with:', '["Navicular resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus (calcaneocuboid joint)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuneiforms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus (calcaneocuboid joint)', 'The cuboid articulates proximally with the calcaneus at the calcaneocuboid joint. Medially it meets the lateral cuneiform and often the navicular, and distally the fourth and fifth metatarsal bases.'),
('cbdf1780-c485-54cf-be88-55a6f4cbcfcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The cuboid bone articulates medially with:', '["Navicular only resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuneiforms only mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral cuneiform and sometimes navicular","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cuneiform and sometimes navicular', 'The cuboid articulates medially with the lateral cuneiform and, in many feet, also with the navicular. The medial and intermediate cuneiforms are not articular partners of the cuboid.'),
('2e0bbb1f-9d62-5d4f-8e74-b18ba7ca7824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The cuboid bone articulates distally with:', '["Cuneiforms","None","1st-3rd metatarsals","4th and 5th metatarsals","All metatarsals"]'::jsonb, '4th and 5th metatarsals', 'The cuboid articulates distally with the bases of the fourth and fifth metatarsals. The first through third metatarsals articulate with the cuneiforms, not the cuboid.'),
('78fd4c9c-f71b-5672-bf95-783e3ace78b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The peroneus longus tendon grooves the:', '["Calcaneus resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Navicular mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuboid (inferolaterally)"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboid (inferolaterally)', 'The peroneus longus tendon runs in a groove on the inferolateral surface of the cuboid before turning to insert on the first metatarsal base and medial cuneiform. The other tarsal bones lack this characteristic peroneal groove.'),
('154d1a74-a913-5f46-871b-6176403fcf0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial cuneiform articulates with:', '["Cuboid and 1st metatarsal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Navicular and 2nd metatarsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular (proximal) and 1st and 2nd metatarsals (distal)"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular (proximal) and 1st and 2nd metatarsals (distal)', 'The medial cuneiform articulates proximally with the navicular and distally with the bases of the first and second metatarsals. Laterally it facets the intermediate cuneiform.'),
('21c67ff9-08da-5bb5-891c-c68bea50879d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The intermediate cuneiform articulates with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular (proximal) and 2nd metatarsal (distal)","Lateral cuneiform and 3rd metatarsal mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial cuneiform and 1st metatarsal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular (proximal) and 2nd metatarsal (distal)', 'The intermediate cuneiform articulates proximally with the navicular and distally with the base of the second metatarsal. It is wedged between the medial and lateral cuneiforms.'),
('b2e975a3-ec22-58f1-80ae-9acd7dc6e503'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral cuneiform articulates with:', '["Navicular (proximal) and 3rd metatarsal (distal)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intermediate cuneiform and 2nd metatarsal mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuboid and 4th metatarsal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular (proximal) and 3rd metatarsal (distal)', 'The lateral cuneiform articulates proximally with the navicular and distally with the base of the third metatarsal. It is bordered medially by the intermediate cuneiform and laterally by the cuboid.'),
('8a5bd8d8-1c10-5b9d-a4af-59a7f70c2e8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The first metatarsal base articulates with:', '["Lateral cuneiform","Navicular","Medial cuneiform","Intermediate cuneiform","Cuboid"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cuneiform', 'The base of the first metatarsal articulates with the medial cuneiform at the first tarsometatarsal joint. The remaining cuneiforms, the cuboid, and the navicular do not directly articulate with this metatarsal base.'),
('3712eea5-1644-5902-8834-66d3bc20956f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The second metatarsal base articulates with:', '["Lateral cuneiform only resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial cuneiform only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuboid only mediated by specific receptor activation","Navicular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All three cuneiforms (especially the middle cuneiform)"]'::jsonb, 'All three cuneiforms (especially the middle cuneiform)', 'The base of the second metatarsal is wedged into a recess of the tarsus and articulates with all three cuneiforms, especially the middle (intermediate) cuneiform, which locks the tarsometatarsal joint. The other metatarsals have simpler base articulations.'),
('c5e81221-7364-56a7-be8e-8c566fad9ac7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The third metatarsal base articulates with:', '["Cuboid","Navicular","Medial cuneiform","Intermediate cuneiform","Lateral cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cuneiform', 'The base of the third metatarsal articulates with the lateral cuneiform. The second metatarsal consists of all three cuneiforms, while the fourth and fifth bases articulate with the cuboid.'),
('74df51bc-0c21-5086-b7ab-f5db7b729bc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The fourth metatarsal base articulates with:', '["Cuboid only","Lateral cuneiform only","Intermediate cuneiform","Medial cuneiform","Cuboid and lateral cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboid and lateral cuneiform', 'The base of the fourth metatarsal articulates with both the cuboid and the lateral cuneiform. The fifth metatarsal base articulates with the cuboid alone.'),
('d628425e-da52-5524-b422-8fef859779e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The fifth metatarsal base articulates with:', '["Lateral cuneiform resulting in decreased functional activity","Cuboid and navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuboid (and has the prominent styloid for peroneus brevis)","Cuboid and calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboid (and has the prominent styloid for peroneus brevis)', 'The base of the fifth metatarsal articulates with the cuboid and bears a prominent styloid (tuberosity) that gives attachment to peroneus brevis. This tuberosity is a common site for avulsion fractures.'),
('bd312231-b99d-5d00-89be-6c0e8cdf4eee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The extensor digitorum brevis (EDB) is the main intrinsic muscle on the dorsum of the foot. It is supplied by:', '["Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Extensor digitorum brevis, the main intrinsic muscle on the dorsum of the foot, is supplied by the deep peroneal nerve. The superficial peroneal nerve is cutaneous, and the sural, saphenous, and tibial nerves do not reach this muscle.'),
('168c719d-bd47-57d8-b8be-0d87af706246'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The extensor hallucis brevis is:', '["A separate muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medial part of EDB (inserts into the proximal phalanx of the hallux)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of EDL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The medial part of EDB (inserts into the proximal phalanx of the hallux)', 'Extensor hallucis brevis is the medial slip of extensor digitorum brevis that inserts into the proximal phalanx of the great toe. It is not a separate independent muscle and is not part of extensor digitorum longus.'),
('f849c8b6-34c5-5730-bafd-374a50bdf93e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dorsal interossei of the foot abduct the toes relative to the:', '["Third toe","Fourth toe","Little toe","Second toe (axial line)","Great toe"]'::jsonb, 'Second toe (axial line)', 'The four dorsal interossei of the foot abduct the toes away from an axial line that passes through the second toe, which they also assist in flexing at the metatarsophalangeal joints. The second toe serves as the reference digit, not the first or fifth.'),
('6a502a1b-c4dd-56db-b465-17d8ad7ed33d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The first dorsal interosseous of the foot is located:', '["Between the 1st and 2nd metatarsals (inserts into 2nd toe)","Between 4th and 5th accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between 2nd and 3rd metatarsals mediated by specific receptor activation","Between 3rd and 4th without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the 1st and 2nd metatarsals (inserts into 2nd toe)', 'The first dorsal interosseous of the foot lies between the first and second metatarsals and inserts into the medial side of the second toe. The remaining dorsal interossei occupy the more lateral web spaces.'),
('08236b75-4227-5fd9-b438-a32ad5dd1cb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar interossei adduct the toes toward the:', '["2nd toe (axial line)","4th toe","3rd toe","1st toe","5th toe"]'::jsonb, '2nd toe (axial line)', 'The three plantar interossei pull toes 3, 4 and 5 toward the midline axis of the foot, which passes through the 2nd toe. Their action contrasts with the dorsal interossei, which abduct away from the 2nd toe.'),
('b4889a6d-5c12-5bf6-929f-64099970f8e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The quadratus plantae (flexor accessorius) has two heads arising from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Cuboid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus (medial and lateral sides)","Talus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus (medial and lateral sides)', 'The medial and lateral heads of quadratus plantae both originate from the plantar aspect of the calcaneus. It does not arise from the cuboid, talus or navicular, and it straightens the oblique pull of the flexor digitorum longus tendon.'),
('a6baa183-032b-5685-933a-7a5d01fad802'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The quadratus plantae inserts into:', '["The FHL tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lateral border of the FDL tendon (helping to align its pull)","The distal phalanx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The middle phalanx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral border of the FDL tendon (helping to align its pull)', 'Quadratus plantae attaches to the lateral margin of the flexor digitorum longus tendon, correcting the oblique pull of FDL so its force acts along the long axis of the toes. It does not insert onto phalanges or the flexor hallucis longus tendon.'),
('58de36d3-4660-572d-990a-3738556f6941'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lumbricals of the foot (4 muscles) arise from:', '["None","FDB tendons","Plantar aponeurosis","FHL tendons","FDL tendons (like in the hand)"]'::jsonb, 'FDL tendons (like in the hand)', 'The four lumbricals arise from the tendons of flexor digitorum longus, analogous to the hand. The first lumbrical has a single origin while the lateral three arise from two adjacent tendons; they are not derived from FDB, FHL or the plantar aponeurosis.'),
('05b7adcc-5a8f-5354-bc06-f1d269c54e4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The flexor digitorum brevis (FDB) gives off tendons that insert into:', '["Proximal phalanges resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal phalanges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle phalanges of the lateral four toes"]'::jsonb, 'Middle phalanges of the lateral four toes', 'Flexor digitorum brevis inserts into the middle phalanges of the lateral four toes after each tendon splits to let the FDL tendon pass through. It does not insert on proximal or distal phalanges.'),
('b408a22b-2347-51a4-b543-b9b39cf6d6d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The FDB tendon splits to allow:', '["The interossei to pass resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lumbricals to pass mediated by specific receptor activation","FDL tendon to pass through (similar to FDS in hand)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'FDL tendon to pass through (similar to FDS in hand)', 'Each flexor digitorum brevis tendon bifurcates so the deeper flexor digitorum longus tendon can pass between its slips to reach the distal phalanx, exactly mirroring the FDS/FDP arrangement in the hand. Interossei and lumbricals do not pass through the FDB split.'),
('af0e2046-05fc-5091-af40-535ffe9f37fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The flexor hallucis brevis has two heads (medial and lateral). It inserts into:', '["Navicular resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial cuneiform mediated by specific receptor activation","Base of distal phalanx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe (both sides)"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe (both sides)', 'The medial and lateral heads of flexor hallucis brevis converge to insert on the medial and lateral sides of the proximal phalanx of the great toe. Each head is associated with a sesamoid; it does not insert on the navicular, cuneiform or distal phalanx.'),
('e3b37f11-e59c-529c-8191-00d925d66747'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The adductor hallucis has two heads:', '["Medial and transverse resulting in decreased functional activity","Oblique (from 2nd-4th metatarsals) and transverse (from MTP ligaments)","Medial and lateral mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial and deep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique (from 2nd-4th metatarsals) and transverse (from MTP ligaments)', 'Adductor hallucis consists of an oblique head arising from the bases of the 2nd-4th metatarsals and a transverse head arising from the plantar MTP ligaments. Both heads converge on the lateral sesamoid and base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe.'),
('92807111-10ae-57a2-be79-623a3377442a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The tendon of flexor hallucis longus passes through a groove in the:', '["Cuboid resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibia mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Talus (posterior process) and sustentaculum tali of calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Talus (posterior process) and sustentaculum tali of calcaneus', 'FHL runs through a groove between the posterior process (and tubercle) of the talus and the sustentaculum tali of the calcaneus before crossing the sole. This tunneled course behind the medial malleolus explains its vulnerability in stenosing tenosynovitis.'),
('4f2df596-a40a-5cdf-a0a3-b7b9efe15b05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The tendon of FHL crosses the FDL tendon at the:', '["Navicular level resulting in decreased functional activity","Master knot of Henry (in the sole)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ankle joint level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Master knot of Henry (in the sole)', 'In the sole, the FHL tendon passes deep and crosses the FDL tendon at the master knot of Henry, a landmark near the navicular. This crossing is clinically relevant because the lumbricals arise from FDL just distal to it.'),
('217837f2-b721-5ddd-92bc-964d4455bf83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The accessory soleus muscle is an anatomical variant that may cause:', '["Club foot resulting in decreased functional activity","High arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Flat foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Painful mass in the posteromedial ankle (compression of tibial nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'Painful mass in the posteromedial ankle (compression of tibial nerve)', 'An accessory soleus presents as a soft-tissue mass in the posteromedial ankle and, with exertional swelling, can compress the tibial nerve causing pain. It does not produce club foot, high arch or flat foot deformities.'),
('7add345b-4d30-5f0b-8a4c-06bae125d6d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The os trigonum is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An accessory ossicle posterior to the talus (posterior process of talus)","An arthritic change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fracture of the talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An accessory ossicle posterior to the talus (posterior process of talus)', 'The os trigonum is a normally non-united accessory ossicle behind the talus representing a separate lateral tubercle of the posterior process. It is a normal variant rather than arthritis or a fracture, although it may be mistaken for a fractured tubercle.'),
('542b779f-5acb-5f22-b6bf-ac5e3353bf4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Os trigonum syndrome occurs with:', '["Eversion resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsiflexion injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Repetitive plantarflexion (impingement of the os trigonum between talus and calcaneus)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inversion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Repetitive plantarflexion (impingement of the os trigonum between talus and calcaneus)', 'Repeated forceful plantarflexion traps the os trigonum between the posterior tibial plafond and calcaneus, producing posterior ankle impingement, common in dancers. Inversion or eversion sprains are not the typical mechanism, and the syndrome is mechanically distinct from dorsiflexion injuries.'),
('418a982c-1930-5b45-9333-319f4ecbef04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The os peroneum is:', '["A sesamoid resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fracture of the peroneal tubercle mediated by specific receptor activation","An accessory ossicle in the peroneus longus tendon (near the cuboid)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An accessory ossicle in the peroneus longus tendon (near the cuboid)', 'The os peroneum is an ossicle lying within the peroneus longus tendon as it curves around the cuboid. It is a normal variant, not a fractured peroneal tubercle, and calling it a sesamoid is incomplete because the key feature is its position within the tendon.'),
('d2a18b98-6d5c-528d-8249-8bd23a914551'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The os tibiale externum (accessory navicular) is:', '["A fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An arthritic spur mediated by specific receptor activation","A congenital enlargement of the navicular tuberosity (type 2 most commonly symptomatic)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital enlargement of the navicular tuberosity (type 2 most commonly symptomatic)', 'The accessory navicular is a congenital ossicle or enlarged tuberosity at the medial navicular, where the tibialis posterior tendon partially inserts; type 2 is most often associated with localized pain and tendinopathy. It is not a fracture or an arthritic spur.'),
('128144c3-e698-5ef5-a43f-6f72258732aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The interphalangeal joints of the toes are:', '["Hinge joints","Saddle joints","Ball and socket","Pivot joints","Plane"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge joints', 'The interphalangeal joints of the toes only allow flexion and extension around a single transverse axis, making them hinge joints. Saddle, pivot, ball-and-socket and plane joints permit additional or different movements that these joints cannot perform.'),
('3ba82031-9ff2-5f36-8a3b-d445338ffaec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The MP joints of the toes are:', '["Ball and socket resulting in decreased functional activity","Ellipsoid (condyloid) joints","Pivot mediated by specific receptor activation","Hinge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Saddle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ellipsoid (condyloid) joints', 'The metatarsophalangeal joints are ellipsoid (condyloid) joints formed by the rounded metatarsal heads and shallow bases of the proximal phalanges, permitting flexion, extension, abduction and adduction but no rotation. They are not hinge, pivot, saddle or ball-and-socket joints.'),
('a63103d1-313d-5dfe-af13-f19f5cd2dc37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Claw toes are characterized by:', '["Hyperextension of MTP and flexion of PIP and DIP","Curl toes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hammer toes mediated by specific receptor activation","Mallet toes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flat toes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperextension of MTP and flexion of PIP and DIP', 'Claw toe deformity combines hyperextension at the metatarsophalangeal joint with flexion at both the proximal and distal interphalangeal joints, often from intrinsic muscle imbalance. Hammer toe only flexes the PIP with extension of DIP and MTP, and mallet toe affects only the DIP.'),
('25854de5-0185-5967-846d-e682827ef00e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Hammer toe is:', '["Flexion of the PIP joint with extension of the DIP and MTP joints","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flexion of DIP mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexion of MTP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion of the PIP joint with extension of the DIP and MTP joints', 'Hammer toe is fixed flexion at the proximal interphalangeal joint with the distal interphalangeal and metatarsophalangeal joints extended. It thus clearly differs from claw toe, which adds PIP and DIP flexion, and from mallet toe, which involves only the DIP joint.'),
('0ffe0685-4ccf-58a8-bb5d-1e4138968cfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Mallet toe is:', '["Extension of DIP","Flexion of PIP","Fixed flexion of the DIP joint","Flexion of MTP","None"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed flexion of the DIP joint', 'Mallet toe is a fixed flexed posture isolated to the distal interphalangeal joint, usually from FDL dysfunction or injury. Because the PIP and MTP joints are spared, it is distinguished from hammer toe and claw toe, neither of which involves isolated DIP flexion.'),
('49dabeda-2ec7-58c5-8a42-923990074d8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Ingrown toenail (onychocryptosis) occurs when:', '["The nail thickens resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The nail plate penetrates the nail fold (lateral paronychium)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The nail is avulsed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The nail plate penetrates the nail fold (lateral paronychium)', 'Onychocryptosis develops when the curved nail plate penetrates the lateral nail fold, triggering inflammation, pain and infection. It is not produced by mere thickening or avulsion of the nail, and it specifically affects the lateral paronychial fold.'),
('567fbd44-2691-519d-a470-ddc20ced85c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Charcot foot (neuropathic arthropathy) is:', '["Flat foot resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A destructive joint process in patients with neuropathy (diabetes) causing fractures and dislocations","High arch mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Club foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A destructive joint process in patients with neuropathy (diabetes) causing fractures and dislocations', 'Charcot arthropathy is a progressive, painless destruction of the foot joints seen in neuropathic patients, particularly diabetics, with bone fragmentation, fractures and joint dislocations. It is a destructive process rather than a static deformity such as flat foot, high arch or club foot.'),
('d68defdc-6fe0-5dd6-b5ab-3b7e354da4d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The diabetic foot ulcer most commonly occurs at:', '["The dorsum resulting in decreased functional activity","The metatarsal heads (plantar forefoot)","The toes mediated by specific receptor activation","The medial arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The heel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The metatarsal heads (plantar forefoot)', 'Diabetes produces neuropathy and loss of the protective plantar pad, so repetitive loading causes ulcers beneath the weight-bearing metatarsal heads in the plantar forefoot. The dorsum, toes, arch and heel are far less common sites for diabetic plantar ulceration.'),
('05bf6785-cf63-529e-aa31-fc0f82438324'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial longitudinal arch of the foot is maintained by:', '["Only ligaments resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only bones mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shape of bones ligaments (spring) and muscles (TP FHL intrinsic)"]'::jsonb, 'Shape of bones ligaments (spring) and muscles (TP FHL intrinsic)', 'The medial longitudinal arch depends on the wedged shape of the bones, the spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament, and dynamic muscular support from tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus and intrinsic muscles. It is not maintained by bones, ligaments or muscles acting alone.'),
('3333779c-adf2-554b-9571-1ac262f4ce1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral longitudinal arch of the foot is maintained by:', '["Shape of bones and the long and short plantar ligaments","Spring ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","TP muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shape of bones and the long and short plantar ligaments', 'The lower, flatter lateral arch is supported chiefly by the shape of the bones and the strong long and short plantar ligaments. The spring ligament and tibialis posterior are medial-arch structures, whereas the long and short plantar ligaments are specific to the lateral side.'),
('d05895c7-20a9-5ca8-aace-3628c3b9f2e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The transverse arch of the foot is maintained by:', '["The wedge shape of the cuneiforms and the metatarsal bases (supported by peroneus longus)","The plantar aponeurosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The spring ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The wedge shape of the cuneiforms and the metatarsal bases (supported by peroneus longus)', 'The transverse arch results from the wedge shape of the cuneiforms and the bases of the metatarsals, with the peroneus longus tendon slinging beneath the foot to hold it up. The plantar aponeurosis and spring ligament contribute to longitudinal rather than transverse arch support.'),
('77996ae7-534f-51fa-9165-679186cc3924'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Pes planus (flatfoot) can be:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","pathologic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only rigid mediated by specific receptor activation","Only flexible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexible (physiologic) or rigid (pathologic)"]'::jsonb, 'Flexible (physiologic) or rigid (pathologic)', 'Flatfoot is divided into a flexible, often physiologic form in which the arch restores on toe-standing, and a rigid form that remains collapsed and is more often pathologic. Rigid flatfoot is frequently due to tarsal coalition, so the flexible-versus-rigid distinction is clinically essential.'),
('c46f6195-2e37-5c0e-8da2-06eb4c1f4d9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Tarsal coalition is:', '["Arthritis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","A congenital fusion of two or more tarsal bones (often calcaneonavicular or talocalcaneal)","Dislocation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital fusion of two or more tarsal bones (often calcaneonavicular or talocalcaneal)', 'Tarsal coalition is a congenital union between tarsal bones, most commonly calcaneonavicular or talocalcaneal, that produces a rigid flatfoot. It is a developmental fusion, not arthritis, trauma-related fracture or a dislocation, and it typically becomes symptomatic in adolescence.'),
('83522ce4-84c2-5907-9279-b428de103bdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Peroneal tendon dislocation occurs:', '["In Achilles rupture resulting in decreased functional activity","In eversion sprains accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","When the peroneal retinaculum is torn (peroneal tendons snap over lateral malleolus)","In inversion sprains without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'When the peroneal retinaculum is torn (peroneal tendons snap over lateral malleolus)', 'Dislocation of the peroneal tendons happens when the superior peroneal retinaculum ruptures, allowing the tendons to slip anteriorly over the lateral malleolus during ankle movement. It is a consequence of retained retinacular injury rather than Achilles rupture or a simple eversion sprain.'),
('e5a8d45b-6def-55ee-a9f5-a4e57641c63e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The flexor hallucis longus tendon is the:', '["Whipping action of the foot (Hallux saltans) due to stenosing tenosynovitis","Tendon of peroneus longus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tendon of tibialis posterior mediated by specific receptor activation","Tendon of peroneus brevis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Whipping action of the foot (Hallux saltans) due to stenosing tenosynovitis', 'Stenosing tenosynovitis of the FHL tendon behind the ankle restricts its glide, leading to a catching or snapping great toe with active plantarflexion, termed hallux saltans or triggering. This represents FHL pathology rather than the tendon of peroneus longus, tibialis posterior or peroneus brevis.'),
('f9f0b8ef-bb4f-59e2-a614-c1b8b0840d92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The tibialis posterior tendon is the primary:', '["Plantarflexor resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsiflexor mediated by specific receptor activation","Evertor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inverter and arch supporter (most important dynamic stabilizer of the medial arch)"]'::jsonb, 'Inverter and arch supporter (most important dynamic stabilizer of the medial arch)', 'Tibialis posterior is the principal inverter of the foot and the most important dynamic stabilizer of the medial longitudinal arch, locking the midfoot at heel rise. Its role is not dorsiflexion or eversion, which belong to the anterior and peroneal compartments; it also plantarflexes only weakly.'),
('55ec17c1-cbf7-5773-b968-32ffaa687908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Dysfunction of the tibialis posterior tendon causes:', '["High arch resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acquired adult flatfoot (adult-acquired flatfoot deformity AAFD)","Club foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acquired adult flatfoot (adult-acquired flatfoot deformity AAFD)', 'Loss of tibialis posterior function collapses the medial arch, producing adult-acquired flatfoot deformity, typically unilateral with forefoot abduction and valgus hindfoot. It does not produce a high arch or club foot, and unlike club foot this is an acquired, not congenital, condition.'),
('6ac19fe9-736b-5088-8d22-874d4e845ec3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular) is the:', '["Plantar fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary dynamic stabilizer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An ankle ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary static stabilizer of the medial arch (supports the talar head)"]'::jsonb, 'Primary static stabilizer of the medial arch (supports the talar head)', 'The spring ligament supports the head of the talus and is the main static stabilizer of the medial longitudinal arch. It is not the plantar fascia, which supports the arch more superficially, and it is not a dynamic stabilizer because its support is ligamentous rather than muscular.'),
('7e6c6e64-fb07-5a68-be4f-fc9e3e306fea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Talar neck fracture is an avascular necrosis risk. The blood supply to the talar body comes mainly from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior tibial artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The artery of the tarsal canal (from posterior tibial)","The peroneal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The dorsalis pedis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The artery of the tarsal canal (from posterior tibial)', 'The talar body is chiefly supplied by the artery of the tarsal canal, a branch of the posterior tibial artery that enters through the interosseous tali region. Because talar neck fractures disrupt this retrograde supply, the body is at high risk of avascular necrosis.'),
('0ee40514-8324-5d7b-9c93-2af1d5601318'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The blood supply to the talar head comes from:', '["The peroneal resulting in decreased functional activity","The dorsalis pedis (via the sinus tarsi artery)","The sural mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior tibial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The dorsalis pedis (via the sinus tarsi artery)', 'The talar head is supplied from the dorsalis pedis system, which sends branches through the sinus tarsi, forming an anastomotic network. Its supply differs from the talar body, which is fed mainly by the artery of the tarsal canal from the posterior tibial artery.'),
('3ebccbe5-46e9-59e1-9c66-fe3e50adbc77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'A Hawkins sign indicates:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Vascularity of the talar body (subchondral osteopenia on X-ray after talar neck fracture)","Fracture of the talus mediated by specific receptor activation","AVN of the talus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascularity of the talar body (subchondral osteopenia on X-ray after talar neck fracture)', 'A subchondral lucent line in the talar dome on follow-up radiographs proves the talar body retains its blood supply, since it excludes avascular necrosis and indicates healing after a talar neck fracture. It is evidence of preserved vascularity, not the fracture itself or established AVN.'),
('7eb350ab-583b-50ed-bdd6-831dda596fe9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Calcaneal fracture is often caused by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Fall from height (axial loading)","Ankle sprain mediated by specific receptor activation","Twisting injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct blow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fall from height (axial loading)', 'Falling from a height transmits axial force through the heel, crushing the calcaneus, so it is a classic cause of calcaneal fractures and often presents with associated lumbar spine injuries. Low-energy mechanisms such as sprains or twisting do not typically fracture the calcaneus.'),
('5e1bccde-5d44-53f8-940f-e64a22bdceac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Intra-articular calcaneal fractures involve:', '["The tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior facet of the subtalar joint","The anterior process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior facet of the subtalar joint', 'Most intra-articular calcaneal fractures extend through the posterior facet of the subtalar joint, and the degree of depression of this facet largely determines the outcome. Extra-articular fractures of the tuberosity or anterior process, by definition, do not involve the joint.'),
('cc568100-5bb5-5e78-9bb3-2fdfa3b0870e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Essex-Lopresti classification is used for:', '["Ankle fractures","Talar fractures","Calcaneal fractures","Lisfranc injuries","None"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneal fractures', 'The Essex-Lopresti system classifies calcaneal fractures into tongue-type and joint-depression types based on the direction of the primary fracture line. It is not used for ankle, talar or Lisfranc injuries, which have their own classification schemes.'),
('f4c7762d-2fc0-5a66-a954-a8f05a30c30d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Lisfranc fracture-dislocation involves:', '["Subtalar dislocation resulting in decreased functional activity","Ankle fracture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Talar fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneal fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tarsometatarsal joint disruption with potential for midfoot instability"]'::jsonb, 'Tarsometatarsal joint disruption with potential for midfoot instability', 'Lisfranc injury disrupts the tarsometatarsal joints, with the base of the 2nd metatarsal keystone and its stabilizing Lisfranc ligament key to midfoot stability. It involves the tarsometatarsal junction, not the ankle, subtalar joint, talus or calcaneus.'),
('08f9353a-f7dd-58f9-946f-d46b61ac10d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The fleck sign (avulsion fracture at the base of the 2nd metatarsal) is pathognomonic for:', '["Calcaneal fracture","Lisfranc injury","Jones fracture","None","Talar fracture"]'::jsonb, 'Lisfranc injury', 'A small avulsion fleck off the medial base of the 2nd metatarsal where the Lisfranc ligament attaches is essentially diagnostic of a Lisfranc injury. It reflects tarsometatarsal disruption rather than calcaneal, talar or Jones-type fractures.'),
('8d1a395d-c477-5492-9f34-09ba92973e57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The ankle syndesmosis is stabilized by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The CFL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ATFL mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The deltoid ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament (AITFL) posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament (PITFL) and interosseous ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior inferior tibiofibular ligament (AITFL) posterior inferior tibiofibular ligament (PITFL) and interosseous ligament', 'The distal tibiofibular syndesmosis is held together by the AITFL, PITFL, transverse tibiofibular ligament and the interosseous ligament/membrane. The ATFL, CFL and deltoid ligament support the ankle mortise laterally and medially rather than the tibiofibular syndesmosis.'),
('e56c49a4-cc0a-5b07-a85c-2c0a8f9ec9b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'A syndesmotic (high ankle) sprain is caused by:', '["Plantarflexion resulting in decreased functional activity","External rotation of the foot on the leg","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Inversion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsiflexion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External rotation of the foot on the leg', 'Forced external rotation of the foot on the fixed leg widens the mortise and tears the syndesmotic ligaments, producing a high ankle sprain. Inversion and other motions primarily injure the lateral collateral ligaments rather than the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis.'),
('53686b8f-025b-5882-b46e-1a553b93c81c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The squeeze test for syndesmotic injury:', '["Compressing the ankle mortise resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Compressing the heel mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Compressing the midfibula against the tibia (proximal) causes pain at the distal syndesmosis"]'::jsonb, 'Compressing the midfibula against the tibia (proximal) causes pain at the distal syndesmosis', 'In the squeeze test, squeezing the fibula against the tibia at mid-leg level transmits force down to the torn distal syndesmosis, reproducing pain when an injury is present. Pressing the mortise or heel tests different structures and is not the standard squeeze maneuver.'),
('47fc4003-e6ee-5b42-af04-17836384f3b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The external rotation test (Kleiger test) for syndesmosis:', '["Evert the foot resulting in decreased functional activity","Plantarflex the foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stabilize the leg and externally rotate the foot (causes pain at syndesmosis)","Invert the foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilize the leg and externally rotate the foot (causes pain at syndesmosis)', 'With the knee flexed and leg held steady, passively externally rotating the foot stresses the syndesmosis and reproduces distal tibiofibular pain in a positive Kleiger test. Inversion or eversion and isolated plantarflexion target the lateral ligaments or capsule rather than the syndesmosis.'),
('4e6c2108-0dbe-5f06-b4de-227c40329be4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Osteochondral lesion of the talus (OLT/OCD) is:', '["Arthritis of the ankle resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A focal injury of the talar dome cartilage and underlying bone (often from trauma)"]'::jsonb, 'A focal injury of the talar dome cartilage and underlying bone (often from trauma)', 'An osteochondral lesion is a localized injury to the articular cartilage and subchondral bone of the talar dome, frequently following an inversion ankle sprain, which may detach as an unstable fragment. It is a discrete focal lesion, not diffuse arthritis, infection or a simple complete fracture.'),
('63729881-b15c-5c16-976e-98cc89c7a7a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The most common site of OLT is:', '["Talar head","Central talus","Posteromedial talar dome","Calcaneus","Anterolateral talar dome"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial talar dome', 'Osteochondral lesions most often involve the posteromedial talar dome, usually deep lesions following inversion injuries; the less common anterolateral lesions are typically shallow and shear-type. The lesion therefore characteristically spares the talar head, central talus and calcaneus.'),
('fbfaf9be-30b0-59a3-ae18-9f43b2bbd07f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Berndt and Harty classification is used for:', '["Calcaneal fractures","Ankle fractures","Osteochondral lesions of the talus","None","Lisfranc injuries"]'::jsonb, 'Osteochondral lesions of the talus', 'The Berndt and Harty classification stages osteochondral lesions of the talus from compression through partially and completely detached fragments, guiding management. It does not apply to calcaneal fractures, ankle fractures or Lisfranc injuries, which have separate systems.'),
('f5080ea1-b61d-5d4c-b6c0-cf17ce63565a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Ankle arthroscopy portals include:', '["Medial only resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anteromedial (medial to TA) and anterolateral (lateral to peroneus tertius)"]'::jsonb, 'Anteromedial (medial to TA) and anterolateral (lateral to peroneus tertius)', 'Standard ankle arthroscopy uses paired anterior portals: an anteromedial portal just medial to the tibialis anterior tendon and an anterolateral portal lateral to the peroneus tertius tendon. These portals allow joint inspection and instrumentation, and none of the single-portal options are sufficient.'),
('9956361c-dac4-59d9-b493-681e568e0795'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The anteromedial portal for ankle arthroscopy is located:', '["None","Medial to the tibialis anterior tendon","Medial to the EHL tendon","Medial to the medial malleolus","Between EHL and EDL"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to the tibialis anterior tendon', 'The anteromedial portal is sited just medial to the tibialis anterior tendon to avoid injuring the dorsalis pedis artery and deep peroneal nerve and to enter the anterior compartment of the joint comfortably. Passing medial to the medial malleolus would miss the joint, while the other listed tendons are too lateral or too close to neurovascular structures.'),
('837c863f-d262-5c8c-a9fb-639b2c1b6b1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The anterolateral portal for ankle arthroscopy is located:', '["Lateral to the peroneal tendons resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial to TA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral to the peroneus tertius tendon (just medial to the lateral malleolus)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to the peroneus tertius tendon (just medial to the lateral malleolus)', 'The anterolateral portal is placed just lateral to the peroneus tertius tendon and medial to the lateral malleolus, a position that avoids neurovascular structures. The peroneal tendons lie more posterolaterally and do not define this portal, and the anterior tibial tendon (TA) is the reference for the anteromedial portal.'),
('bf2280b5-2e8d-5f8d-a89f-b4e936e374ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dorsalis pedis artery pulse is palpated:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the EHL and EDL tendons on the dorsum of the foot","In the plantar arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind the medial malleolus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Behind the lateral malleolus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the EHL and EDL tendons on the dorsum of the foot', 'The dorsalis pedis artery, the distal continuation of the anterior tibial artery, runs on the dorsum of the foot between the tendons of extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus, where its pulse is felt. The plantar arch lies deep on the sole, and the malleolar regions are not its surface location.'),
('b00ee4d8-0ee1-56bf-91f6-733d23ff7e63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The posterior tibial artery pulse is palpated:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","On the dorsum of the foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Behind the lateral malleolus mediated by specific receptor activation","In the popliteal fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Behind the medial malleolus (between FDL and FHL)"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the medial malleolus (between FDL and FHL)', 'The posterior tibial artery passes behind the medial malleolus within the tarsal tunnel, lying between the tendons of flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus, and its pulse is palpated there. The dorsum is the site of the dorsalis pedis pulse, and the lateral malleolus and popliteal fossa are not locations of this vessel.'),
('883b49ed-e4da-51b6-b493-f3f6fb53e558'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Allen''s test of the foot assesses:', '["The digital arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","The Achilles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The peroneal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The patency of the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial arteries (and the plantar arch)"]'::jsonb, 'The patency of the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial arteries (and the plantar arch)', 'The foot version of Allen''s test occludes the dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial arteries and then releases one while the other remains compressed to confirm collateral flow through the plantar arch. It therefore verifies patency of both major vessels and the arch, not the digital vessels, peroneal artery, or Achilles tendon.'),
('12ce81c5-397b-5e80-9a03-b44205f83363'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Romberg test evaluates:', '["Strength resulting in decreased functional activity","Proprioception (using the visual and vestibular systems) important in foot and ankle","Balance mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proprioception (using the visual and vestibular systems) important in foot and ankle', 'The Romberg test assesses proprioceptive (dorsal column) function by having the patient stand with feet together and eyes closed, integrating visual, vestibular, and proprioceptive inputs. Excessive sway with eyes closed indicates loss of position sense. It is not a direct test of muscle strength.'),
('037f0ad1-1f8d-57ca-80a4-b8260148f5c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The tuning fork test is used for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Identifying stress fractures of the foot (by detecting vibratory perception)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Assessing circulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Assessing nerve function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Identifying stress fractures of the foot (by detecting vibratory perception)', 'A vibrating tuning fork applied over bone elicits vibration that is perceived abnormally or poorly across a stress fracture, making it a bedside screening test for such injuries. It does not assess blood flow or serve as a primary test of nerve conduction.'),
('9ba7f969-91e0-5713-9c8a-c150530d6dc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The ankle brachial index (ABI) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ligamentous laxity test mediated by specific receptor activation","A measure of ankle range of motion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ratio of ankle systolic BP to brachial systolic BP (used for peripheral artery disease)"]'::jsonb, 'A ratio of ankle systolic BP to brachial systolic BP (used for peripheral artery disease)', 'The ABI is the ratio of ankle systolic blood pressure to brachial systolic blood pressure; a value below 0.9 indicates peripheral arterial disease. It is a vascular measurement, not a test of ligamentous laxity or ankle range of motion.'),
('4bab6ac5-3504-5381-a15d-1ea42d306df3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The foot has how many bones (including sesamoids)?', '["30 resulting in decreased functional activity","25 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","26 bones + 2 sesamoids","28 total without affecting the primary tissue pathway","32 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '26 bones + 2 sesamoids', 'The foot contains 26 bones (7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals, and 14 phalanges) plus 2 sesamoids beneath the first metatarsal head, giving 28 osseous elements in total. The other numbers do not match the standard skeletal count of the foot.'),
('706581e2-1125-56f8-bc68-26a30bda70c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The hindfoot includes:', '["Achilles and subtalar","All","Talus and calcaneus","Navicular and cuboid","None"]'::jsonb, 'Talus and calcaneus', 'The hindfoot is formed by the talus and calcaneus, which constitute the ankle and subtalar joints. The navicular and cuboid belong to the midfoot, while the Achilles tendon and subtalar joint are structures related to the hindfoot rather than its bones.'),
('c332f625-3d0f-528f-9a6c-e522c2bdd0b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The midfoot includes:', '["Navicular cuboid and three cuneiforms","Metatarsals and phalanges","Navicular only","Calcaneus and talus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular cuboid and three cuneiforms', 'The midfoot comprises the navicular, cuboid, and the three cuneiforms, bridging the hindfoot and forefoot. The metatarsals and phalanges are forefoot elements, and the calcaneus and talus form the hindfoot.'),
('b0d35c70-a97c-504c-a6fd-0f48fcb7f437'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The forefoot includes:', '["None","5 metatarsals and 14 phalanges","Talus and calcaneus","7 tarsals","5 metatarsals only"]'::jsonb, '5 metatarsals and 14 phalanges', 'The forefoot consists of the five metatarsals and the fourteen phalanges. The talus and calcaneus are hindfoot bones and the seven tarsals include midfoot elements as well, while ''5 metatarsals only'' omits the phalanges.'),
('10562077-0c31-58ff-bfce-64d5d6b8102f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The first ray consists of:', '["None","1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform","1st metatarsal and lateral cuneiform","1st metatarsal and proximal phalanx","Great toe and its sesamoids"]'::jsonb, '1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform', 'The first ray is defined as the functional unit of the first metatarsal and the medial cuneiform. The great toe and sesamoids are distal parts of this unit, the lateral cuneiform belongs to the third ray, and the proximal phalanx is a toe component.'),
('64e3ec42-f965-5d6f-850c-cefb0f10f8eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The central ray (second ray) consists of:', '["None","2nd metatarsal and medial cuneiform","2nd and 3rd metatarsals","2nd metatarsal and lateral cuneiform","2nd metatarsal and intermediate cuneiform"]'::jsonb, '2nd metatarsal and intermediate cuneiform', 'The second ray comprises the second metatarsal and the intermediate (middle) cuneiform, reflecting the locked alignment of the central column. The medial and lateral cuneiforms belong to the first and third rays respectively, and the second ray does not include the third metatarsal.'),
('fed51feb-9de1-5ea8-baf4-b858678adce3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral ray consists of:', '["4th and 5th metatarsals and cuboid","3rd metatarsal and lateral cuneiform","All","5th metatarsal only","None"]'::jsonb, '4th and 5th metatarsals and cuboid', 'The lateral ray is formed by the fourth and fifth metatarsals articulating with the cuboid. The third metatarsal articulates with the lateral cuneiform and is part of the central column, while the fifth metatarsal alone does not define the whole ray.'),
('bcb12729-938c-55f8-8f3a-81b39e6b1f84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Chopart joint (transverse tarsal) disarticulation is performed through:', '["The Lisfranc joint resulting in decreased functional activity","The subtalar joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints","The ankle joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints', 'The Chopart (transverse tarsal) disarticulation passes through the talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints. The Lisfranc level is at the tarsometatarsal joints, and the subtalar and ankle joints are too proximal.'),
('85b7d205-3a8a-5e7c-9601-a94ced075a54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Lisfranc amputation disarticulates through:', '["The subtalar","The midtarsal joints","None","The ankle","The tarsometatarsal joints"]'::jsonb, 'The tarsometatarsal joints', 'Lisfranc amputation disarticulates through the tarsometatarsal joints, separating the forefoot from the midfoot. The subtalar and ankle joints are too proximal, and the midtarsal joints correspond to the Chopart level.'),
('db71209e-f3e4-5ffe-91b8-ffed768605db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Syme''s amputation is performed at:', '["The ankle (through the distal tibia and fibula)","The forefoot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The above-knee mediated by specific receptor activation","The midfoot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The below-knee leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ankle (through the distal tibia and fibula)', 'Syme''s amputation is performed at the ankle, dividing the distal tibia and fibula, with the heel pad covering the stump for weight bearing. Midfoot and forefoot amputations are more distal, and below-knee or above-knee levels are more proximal.'),
('e27cb9c2-9bd1-583c-a153-b62da7a65c15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Boyd amputation is performed at:', '["The calcaneus (calcaneotibial fusion)","The forefoot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ankle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The midfoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The calcaneus (calcaneotibial fusion)', 'The Boyd amputation preserves the calcaneus, which is fused to the tibia (calcaneotibial fusion) to allow end weight bearing, distinguishing it from Syme''s amputation. Midfoot and forefoot levels remove the calcaneus, and the ankle level removes it as well.'),
('4fdf9b43-3c21-5474-b6af-40f0761a0dde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The heel pad (subcalcaneal fat pad) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Thick fibrofatty tissue specialized for shock absorption and weight-bearing","The plantar aponeurosis mediated by specific receptor activation","The origin of FDB without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The insertion of the Achilles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thick fibrofatty tissue specialized for shock absorption and weight-bearing', 'The subcalcaneal fat pad is thick fibrous fat specialized to absorb shock and distribute weight-bearing pressure beneath the heel. It is distinct from the plantar aponeurosis, the origin of flexor digitorum brevis, and the insertion of the Achilles tendon.'),
('67f7aa76-6e9c-5aac-ba6d-78a865d53f1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar skin is the thickest in the body and is attached to the plantar aponeurosis by:', '["Bones resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous septa (creating multiple small compartments)","Loose connective tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous septa (creating multiple small compartments)', 'Plantar skin is anchored to the plantar aponeurosis by tough fibrous septa, which create multiple small fat compartments and give the sole its stability against shearing forces. Loose connective tissue would not provide this anchoring, and bones and muscles are not the attachment sites.'),
('c5d55cba-b144-5cce-b05b-9e74e3339cf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The metatarsal heads have plantar fat pads that:', '["Cushion the forefoot during gait","Protect nerves","Provide arch support","None","Allow toe movement"]'::jsonb, 'Cushion the forefoot during gait', 'The plantar fat pads beneath the metatarsal heads cushion the forefoot during the push-off phase of gait. They do not primarily protect nerves, provide arch support, or drive toe movement.'),
('3be030ad-d3b8-5dc0-999c-b59cdff99603'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Atrophy of the metatarsal fat pads (with age or steroid use) can cause:', '["Plantar fasciitis resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metatarsalgia (pain under the metatarsal heads)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Achilles tendinitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metatarsalgia (pain under the metatarsal heads)', 'Loss of the metatarsal fat pads exposes the metatarsal heads and causes metatarsalgia, pain under the forefoot. Plantar fasciitis involves the aponeurosis and Achilles tendinitis affects the posterior heel, so neither is the direct result of pad atrophy.'),
('248364c5-e169-5911-8329-bc1025e77a11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The skin of the sole is supplied by:', '["Medial and lateral plantar nerves (branches of tibial nerve)","Sural nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Saphenous nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep peroneal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial peroneal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral plantar nerves (branches of tibial nerve)', 'The sole is innervated by the medial and lateral plantar nerves, terminal branches of the tibial nerve. The sural and saphenous nerves supply the lateral and medial foot edges, and the peroneal nerves supply the dorsum rather than the sole.'),
('eb49c1b7-cc28-59ec-94cd-412383b32995'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The skin of the dorsum of the foot is supplied by:', '["Superficial peroneal (most) deep peroneal (first web) sural (lateral) and saphenous (medial)","Only sural accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only superficial peroneal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only deep peroneal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only saphenous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal (most) deep peroneal (first web) sural (lateral) and saphenous (medial)', 'The dorsum is supplied mostly by the superficial peroneal nerve, with the deep peroneal nerve serving the first web space, the sural nerve the lateral side, and the saphenous nerve the medial side. No single nerve supplies the entire dorsum.'),
('c12d0fe3-1b16-58f2-80d0-7a76e0b72b2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The nail bed (nail matrix) is important for:', '["Sensation resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Grip mediated by specific receptor activation","Nail growth (the proximal matrix produces the nail plate)","Bone protection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nail growth (the proximal matrix produces the nail plate)', 'The proximal nail matrix produces the nail plate and is therefore responsible for nail growth; damage to it can permanently arrest growth. Sensation, grip, and bone protection are not functions of the matrix.'),
('92897a98-05d3-5d47-afd9-d3a4dcc4d899'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Ingrown toenail is treated by:', '["Antibiotics only resulting in decreased functional activity","Partial nail avulsion with chemical or surgical ablation of the lateral nail matrix","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Total nail removal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Partial nail avulsion with chemical or surgical ablation of the lateral nail matrix', 'The standard treatment is partial nail avulsion combined with chemical (phenol) or surgical ablation of the lateral nail matrix to prevent regrowth. Antibiotics alone treat infection but not the cause, and total nail removal is reserved for extensive disease.'),
('9e3108cf-2457-5705-9143-e06aa93ed7ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Subungual exostosis is:', '["An ingrown toenail resulting in decreased functional activity","Fungal infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A bony outgrowth beneath the nail (usually on the great toe)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A bony outgrowth beneath the nail (usually on the great toe)', 'A subungual exostosis is a benign bony outgrowth that develops beneath the nail, most often on the great toe, causing pain and nail deformity. It is not an infection, an ingrown nail, or a malignant tumor.'),
('4e85c155-bf1c-59b7-b19d-7d95b64299fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The glomus body is a:', '["Glomus tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nerve ending mediated by specific receptor activation","Thermoregulatory structure (arteriovenous anastomosis) in the nail bed and fingertip","Artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thermoregulatory structure (arteriovenous anastomosis) in the nail bed and fingertip', 'A glomus body is a thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomosis found in the nail bed and fingertips that regulates local blood flow and temperature. It is a normal structure rather than the tumor itself, a nerve ending, or a simple artery.'),
('4490c7d3-64d2-5e0e-99f4-e62b5d972bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Glomus tumor occurs most commonly in:', '["The subungual region (under the nail)","The head accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The chest mediated by specific receptor activation","The neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The subungual region (under the nail)', 'Glomus tumors arise most frequently in the subungual region beneath the nail, where glomus bodies are concentrated. The head, chest, neck, and abdomen are not typical sites for these tumors.'),
('b97dba68-c067-5ae7-b648-9ca7a496bdf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The toe walking gait can be caused by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plantar fasciitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Flatfoot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Habitual toe walking congenital Achilles contracture or cerebral palsy"]'::jsonb, 'Habitual toe walking congenital Achilles contracture or cerebral palsy', 'Toe walking may be habitual in children or result from congenital Achilles contracture or cerebral palsy with spasticity. Plantar fasciitis and flatfoot present with pain or arch collapse rather than isolated toe walking.'),
('2b0dc7e6-81cf-58ac-900c-922c5d4df7ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The foot-sole reflex (Babinski sign) is:', '["Normal response in adults resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extension of the great toe with fanning of the other toes on stroking the lateral sole (upper motor neuron lesion)","Flexion of the toes without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of the great toe with fanning of the other toes on stroking the lateral sole (upper motor neuron lesion)', 'A positive Babinski sign is extension (dorsiflexion) of the great toe with fanning of the other toes when the lateral sole is stroked, indicating an upper motor neuron lesion. Toe flexion is the normal plantar response in adults.'),
('0383f91c-81e3-5dba-97e9-73dba9b07a12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The gait cycle has two main phases:', '["Step and stride","Stance and step","Swing only","Stance (60%) and swing (40%)","None"]'::jsonb, 'Stance (60%) and swing (40%)', 'The gait cycle is divided into the stance phase, about 60% of the cycle, and the swing phase, about 40%. Step and stride are distance measures rather than cycle phases.'),
('0f01722c-2470-53ef-aeaa-f79bb4063bbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Heel strike is the:', '["Toe off resulting in decreased functional activity","Initial contact of the gait cycle (heel first)","Pre-swing mediated by specific receptor activation","Mid stance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Initial contact of the gait cycle (heel first)', 'Heel strike is the initial contact of the foot with the ground at the start of the stance phase. Toe-off, pre-swing, and mid-stance are later events in the gait cycle.'),
('3accde7d-4af9-5abf-b36e-ac2fdd10f64c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Toe-off is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The beginning of stance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The end of the stance phase (propulsion from the forefoot)","The middle of stance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The end of the stance phase (propulsion from the forefoot)', 'Toe-off marks the end of the stance phase, when the forefoot propels the body forward into the swing phase. It is neither the beginning nor the middle of stance.'),
('cb22e8d5-ce32-540d-9ac5-3fa6b229b349'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The foot pronation during gait involves:', '["Supination resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inversion plantarflexion and adduction mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eversion dorsiflexion and abduction (normal shock absorption)"]'::jsonb, 'Eversion dorsiflexion and abduction (normal shock absorption)', 'Pronation of the foot combines eversion, dorsiflexion, and abduction, which unlocks the midtarsal joint to absorb shock. Supination combines inversion, plantarflexion, and adduction and is the functional opposite.'),
('7e755a53-65fc-52fb-b3bf-8cd216f4fc9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The foot supination during gait involves:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Pronation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Eversion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inversion plantarflexion and adduction (provides rigidity for push-off)"]'::jsonb, 'Inversion plantarflexion and adduction (provides rigidity for push-off)', 'Supination combines inversion, plantarflexion, and adduction, locking the foot to provide a rigid lever for push-off. Pronation (eversion, dorsiflexion, abduction) is its functional opposite.'),
('23f4e709-95f8-5cea-85f7-e56afe3b55c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Excessive pronation during gait is associated with:', '["Flatfoot and posterior tibial dysfunction","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High arch mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plantar fasciitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flatfoot and posterior tibial dysfunction', 'Excessive pronation flattens the medial arch and stresses the posterior tibial tendon, so it is linked with flatfoot and posterior tibial dysfunction. High arch and plantar fasciitis are not typical consequences.'),
('5522795f-9dc6-51ae-a2ec-efd5cc0466cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Excessive supination (underpronation) is associated with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","High-arched foot (cavus) often with poor shock absorption","Valgus heel mediated by specific receptor activation","Bunion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flatfoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High-arched foot (cavus) often with poor shock absorption', 'Excessive supination produces a high-arched cavus foot that is rigid and absorbs shock poorly. Valgus heel, bunion, and flatfoot do not describe the underpronated pattern.'),
('5b0f985f-aa50-5a89-86f2-46e37549eb86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The walking base (distance between heels during gait) is normally:', '["15 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","0 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-10 cm (approximately shoulder width)","20-30 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","40 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-10 cm (approximately shoulder width)', 'The normal walking base, the distance between the heels during gait, is about 5-10 cm, roughly shoulder width. Wider or narrower bases are considered abnormal.'),
('a77ab149-b074-5787-a51a-5b533fe6538e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The step length is the:', '["The stride length/2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Distance from heel strike of one foot to heel strike of the other foot","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Distance from heel to toe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distance from heel strike of one foot to heel strike of the other foot', 'Step length is the distance from heel strike of one foot to the heel strike of the opposite foot. Stride length equals two step lengths, and heel-to-toe distance is not the standard gait measure.'),
('9923b72f-dcdf-5823-8460-15898b98f1d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The stride length is the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The base of gait accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Distance from heel strike of one foot to the next heel strike of the same foot (2 steps)","One step length without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distance from heel strike of one foot to the next heel strike of the same foot (2 steps)', 'Stride length is the distance from heel strike of one foot to the next heel strike of the same foot, equal to two step lengths. The base of gait is the lateral distance between the feet, not a longitudinal measure.'),
('3945fec7-4b2d-5d54-89b1-95a9466c49c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Cadence in gait is:', '["Walking speed","None","Number of steps per minute","Stride time","Step length"]'::jsonb, 'Number of steps per minute', 'Cadence is the number of steps taken per minute, describing the rhythm of walking. Walking speed, stride time, and step length are separate gait parameters.'),
('23e79acb-c2be-514a-a898-7ffcf215f3f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The foot is divided into 6 regions:', '["Hindfoot midfoot forefoot and the great toe lesser toes and heel","Rarely accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proximal distal medial lateral mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hindfoot midfoot forefoot and the great toe lesser toes and heel', 'The foot is conventionally divided into the hindfoot, midfoot, forefoot, great toe, lesser toes, and heel. These six regions reflect both function and clinical practice.'),
('d2537923-046c-5365-a5ef-ef351458fd98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial longitudinal arch angle (Meary''s angle) is the angle between:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The tibia and the talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The calcaneus and the 5th metatarsal mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The long axis of the talus and the first metatarsal on lateral X-ray"]'::jsonb, 'The long axis of the talus and the first metatarsal on lateral X-ray', 'Meary''s angle is measured on a lateral X-ray between the long axis of the talus and the long axis of the first metatarsal, evaluating the medial longitudinal arch. Tibia-talar and calcaneus-fifth metatarsal angles are different measurements.'),
('c15a32fa-bfaf-56bb-ab2c-f2127b67acc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The calcaneal pitch angle is the angle between:', '["The tibia and calcaneus resulting in decreased functional activity","The calcaneal inclination line and the weight-bearing surface (normally 20-30 degrees)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The talus and calcaneus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The calcaneal inclination line and the weight-bearing surface (normally 20-30 degrees)', 'The calcaneal pitch angle is formed between the calcaneal inclination line and the weight-bearing surface, normally 20-30 degrees, and helps assess arch height. Tibia-calcaneus and talus-calcaneus angles are separate measurements.'),
('7d550fc4-5108-50cc-8f4c-88832f672ae6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral talo-first metatarsal angle (Meary''s angle) is normally:', '["45 degrees","30 degrees","40 degrees","0 degrees (straight line)","10-20 degrees"]'::jsonb, '0 degrees (straight line)', 'The normal lateral talo-first metatarsal angle (Meary''s) is 0 degrees, with the two axes forming a straight line. Values increase with flatfoot, so 10-45 degrees all exceed the normal value.'),
('2b800954-745d-53a9-b01f-07516d2ef477'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The talonavicular coverage angle is used in:', '["None","Hindfoot varus","Club foot","Flatfoot evaluation","High arch"]'::jsonb, 'Flatfoot evaluation', 'The talonavicular coverage angle measures lateral subluxation of the navicular and is used to evaluate flatfoot. It is not used for hindfoot varus, clubfoot, or high-arch assessment.'),
('7babf154-5da1-5d03-ab28-e999393f38ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Pediatric flatfoot is:', '["Common in children due to ligamentous laxity and usually resolves spontaneously","resolves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","pathologic mediated by specific receptor activation","Requires surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common in children due to ligamentous laxity and usually resolves spontaneously', 'Pediatric flexible flatfoot is common because of ligamentous laxity and typically resolves spontaneously as the child grows. Rigid flatfoot is more concerning and does not resolve on its own.'),
('cac00f07-99ba-5ae8-8f42-86f6f789c02f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Rigid flatfoot in children may indicate:', '["Tarsal coalition (calcaneonavicular or talocalcaneal)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal variant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexible flatfoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tarsal coalition (calcaneonavicular or talocalcaneal)', 'Rigid flatfoot in a child should raise suspicion of tarsal coalition, usually calcaneonavicular or talocalcaneal. A normal variant and flexible flatfoot are not rigid.'),
('df7a191e-777a-5600-afa6-dacf6ada1ca9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The peroneal spastic flatfoot is often due to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Tendonitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arthritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Tarsal coalition causing peroneal muscle spasm","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tarsal coalition causing peroneal muscle spasm', 'Peroneal spastic flatfoot results from a tarsal coalition that restricts subtalar motion and produces protective peroneal muscle spasm. Tendonitis and arthritis are not the usual causes.'),
('bd8f4060-4349-585d-9fd7-cea44ae64cf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Haglund''s deformity is:', '["A fracture resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Arthritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A spur mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A bony prominence on the posterosuperior calcaneus (retrocalcaneal exostosis) associated with Haglund''s syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'A bony prominence on the posterosuperior calcaneus (retrocalcaneal exostosis) associated with Haglund''s syndrome', 'Haglund''s deformity is a bony exostosis of the posterosuperior calcaneus that irritates the retrocalcaneal bursa and Achilles insertion. It is a structural prominence, not a fracture, arthritis, or a spur.'),
('d3dcde3c-5e31-5767-a55a-66f52888e161'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Haglund''s syndrome includes:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Tarsal tunnel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plantar fasciitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Haglund''s deformity retrocalcaneal bursitis and Achilles tendinopathy","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Haglund''s deformity retrocalcaneal bursitis and Achilles tendinopathy', 'Haglund''s syndrome combines the posterosuperior calcaneal prominence with retrocalcaneal bursitis and Achilles tendinopathy. Tarsal tunnel syndrome and plantar fasciitis are separate entities.'),
('08de6159-49e3-5ea1-a8e8-28137a8f18b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Insertional Achilles tendinopathy affects:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The mid-portion of the tendon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The distal 2 cm of the Achilles insertion into the calcaneus","The musculotendinous junction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The distal 2 cm of the Achilles insertion into the calcaneus', 'Insertional Achilles tendinopathy involves the distal 2 cm of the tendon at its calcaneal insertion, often with bone spurs and Haglund''s deformity. Mid-portion (non-insertional) disease and the musculotendinous junction are different locations.'),
('4fe8587c-0473-56db-94ac-f52e2c1de124'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Non-insertional (mid-portion) Achilles tendinopathy is more common in:', '["Children resulting in decreased functional activity","Runners and active individuals (degeneration of the midsubstance)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Elderly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Runners and active individuals (degeneration of the midsubstance)', 'Mid-portion Achilles tendinopathy typically results from repetitive overloading in runners and active people, producing degeneration of the tendon midsubstance. It is not primarily a disease of children or the elderly.'),
('763a5dbb-8a3c-5dc0-b4af-21a18b55a43f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Achilles tendinopathy is treated with:', '["Surgery only resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete rest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eccentric strengthening exercises (Alfredson protocol)"]'::jsonb, 'Eccentric strengthening exercises (Alfredson protocol)', 'Eccentric calf strengthening (the Alfredson protocol) is the mainstay of conservative treatment for Achilles tendinopathy. Surgery is reserved for refractory cases, and complete rest alone is not the standard therapy.'),
('6796fecd-2547-54e5-823d-42d359f83b69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Plantar fibromatosis (Ledderhose disease) is:', '["An abscess resulting in decreased functional activity","A malignant tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A benign fibroproliferative disorder of the plantar fascia (nodules)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cyst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign fibroproliferative disorder of the plantar fascia (nodules)', 'Plantar fibromatosis (Ledderhose disease) is a benign proliferation of the plantar fascia producing nodules along the medial band. It is not an abscess, cyst, or malignancy.'),
('1c8fdb3d-844d-5bce-98c2-c4462b00ebc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Dupuytren''s contracture of the foot (Ledderhose) may be associated with:', '["Dupuytren''s disease in the hand and Peyronie''s disease in the penis","Diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Arthritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dupuytren''s disease in the hand and Peyronie''s disease in the penis', 'Ledderhose disease is part of the family of fibromatoses, so it can coexist with Dupuytren''s contracture of the hand and Peyronie''s disease. Diabetes is not a defining association in this grouping.'),
('1619b07e-77ca-51bc-a2ad-7fdc4c50a468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The retrocalcaneal bursa is located:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the Achilles and the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the Achilles tendon and the posterosuperior calcaneus","Between the Achilles and the FHL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the Achilles tendon and the posterosuperior calcaneus', 'The retrocalcaneal bursa lies deep to the Achilles tendon, between it and the posterosuperior calcaneus. The bursa between the Achilles and skin is the subcutaneous calcaneal bursa.'),
('457dc4ff-9464-5f7d-9071-d16fabcaf110'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The subcutaneous calcaneal bursa (Haglund''s) is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Under the plantar fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the Achilles tendon and the skin (over the posterosuperior calcaneus)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between the Achilles and the calcaneus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the Achilles tendon and the skin (over the posterosuperior calcaneus)', 'The subcutaneous (superficial) calcaneal bursa lies between the skin and the Achilles tendon over the posterosuperior calcaneus. The deep retrocalcaneal bursa is between the tendon and the bone.'),
('1eed7f0d-1083-5922-8520-2523ae4d8097'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Retrocalcaneal bursitis causes:', '["Pain anterior to the Achilles tendon (squeezing the bursa between the tendon and calcaneus)","Forefoot pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Arch pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heel pad pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain anterior to the Achilles tendon (squeezing the bursa between the tendon and calcaneus)', 'Retrocalcaneal bursitis produces tenderness anterior to the Achilles tendon because squeezing the bursa between tendon and calcaneus reproduces pain. Forefoot, arch, and heel pad pain arise from other structures.'),
('492810a6-76a6-5269-8f4f-5fb749a2cf52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The flexor hallucis longus tendon is the primary:', '["Evertor","Plantarflexor","Flexor of the great toe IP joint","Inverter","Flexor of the great toe MTP joint"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor of the great toe IP joint', 'FHL is the primary flexor of the interphalangeal joint of the great toe. The MTP joint of the hallux is flexed chiefly by flexor hallucis brevis.'),
('3f9d1cf0-0667-5b06-827e-9a7412001680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The flexor digitorum longus is the primary:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flexor of the MTP joints mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexor of the DIP joints of the lateral 4 toes","Flexor of the PIP joints leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor of the DIP joints of the lateral 4 toes', 'FDL inserts on the distal phalanges, so it is the primary flexor of the DIP joints of the lateral four toes. The lumbricals and flexor digitorum brevis act on other joints.'),
('5f768796-4941-503f-b712-00eedfce5d6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The quadratus plantae is also called:', '["None","Plantar interosseous","Dorsal interosseous","Flexor accessorius","Lumbrical"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor accessorius', 'Quadratus plantae is also called flexor accessorius because it assists the flexor digitorum longus. It is not an interosseous or a lumbrical muscle.'),
('6a4a9ba6-728b-5534-a279-538e50847071'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The extensor digitorum brevis is also called:', '["Extensor brevis of the toes","Accessory extensor","None","Extensor accessorius","All"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor brevis of the toes', 'Extensor digitorum brevis is the extensor brevis of the toes, a small dorsal foot muscle that extends the toes. It should not be confused with the flexor accessorius (quadratus plantae).'),
('06191c46-1d3b-520e-95a9-c32d95052cda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The intrinsic muscles of the foot are organized into how many layers on the sole?', '["5 layers","1 layer","2 layers","4 layers","3 layers"]'::jsonb, '4 layers', 'The plantar intrinsic muscles are arranged in four layers of the sole. Layer 1 contains abductor hallucis, FDB, and abductor digiti minimi; deeper layers contain the remaining muscles.'),
('d35bd42a-8415-542d-88b3-7345e3acca40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The first layer of the sole includes (from medial to lateral):', '["Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis abductor digiti minimi","FDB AbH AbDM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AbH AbDM FDB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis abductor digiti minimi', 'From medial to lateral, the first plantar layer is abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, and abductor digiti minimi. The other sequences list the same muscles in the wrong order.'),
('57fde308-3ce5-55c1-a6ce-b824194456a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The second layer of the sole includes:', '["FDB and FHB resulting in decreased functional activity","Interossei and adductor hallucis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadratus plantae and the four lumbricals","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadratus plantae and the four lumbricals', 'The second plantar layer contains quadratus plantae and the four lumbricals together with the long flexor tendons. FDB is in layer 1, while interossei and adductor hallucis are in deeper layers.'),
('33fe44d1-f562-552a-af35-5cd371e0d734'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The third layer of the sole includes:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interossei and lumbricals mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexor hallucis brevis adductor hallucis flexor digiti minimi brevis","FDB FDL QP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor hallucis brevis adductor hallucis flexor digiti minimi brevis', 'The third plantar layer comprises flexor hallucis brevis, adductor hallucis, and flexor digiti minimi brevis. Lumbricals belong to layer 2 and interossei to layer 4.'),
('37802880-587a-5499-bce3-36009e57a20e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The fourth layer of the sole includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FHB and AdH mediated by specific receptor activation","QP and lumbricals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plantar and dorsal interossei plus the tendons of peroneus longus and tibialis posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Plantar and dorsal interossei plus the tendons of peroneus longus and tibialis posterior', 'The fourth plantar layer holds the plantar and dorsal interossei together with the tendons of peroneus longus and tibialis posterior. FHB with adductor hallucis forms layer 3, and QP with lumbricals forms layer 2.'),
('19d6582a-b4e2-5f11-9b86-b01b3abbc554'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The second toe has:', '["One plantar interosseous only resulting in decreased functional activity","Two sets of interossei (1st dorsal inserts into its medial side 2nd dorsal into its lateral side)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","One dorsal interosseous only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two sets of interossei (1st dorsal inserts into its medial side 2nd dorsal into its lateral side)', 'The second toe is the axis digit receiving two dorsal interossei, one on each side. The 3rd-5th toes each get one dorsal and one plantar interosseous.'),
('1d99546e-bb12-5f12-98fd-01de34fa5943'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The 3rd 4th and 5th toes have:', '["Two plantar interossei each resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two dorsal interossei each mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","One dorsal interosseous and one plantar interosseous each"]'::jsonb, 'One dorsal interosseous and one plantar interosseous each', 'Each of the 3rd, 4th, and 5th toes receives one dorsal interosseous and one plantar interosseous. Only the second toe has two dorsal interossei.'),
('2ade3ad7-c618-513a-98d9-99f6165d6f90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The great toe (1st toe) has:', '["One dorsal interosseous attached medially","None","All","No interosseous attachments","One plantar interosseous attached laterally"]'::jsonb, 'No interosseous attachments', 'The great toe receives no interosseous muscles; its stabilization is provided by muscles such as abductor hallucis and adductor hallucis. The interossei insert only on the second to fifth toes.'),
('5e051436-73ec-5561-8a4d-ee6a1b980d2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The sesamoid bones of the foot are most commonly found at:', '["The 5th MTP joint resulting in decreased functional activity","The 1st MTP joint (medial and lateral sesamoids)","The IP joint of the hallux mediated by specific receptor activation","The ankle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The 1st MTP joint (medial and lateral sesamoids)', 'The most common sesamoids are the medial and lateral sesamoids within the tendons of flexor hallucis brevis at the first MTP joint. The hallucal IP sesamoid is less common.'),
('a7ed91f0-c670-59be-a2b7-2ea952211b5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The medial sesamoid of the great toe lies in:', '["The medial head of FHB tendon (also receives fibers from AbH)","The adductor hallucis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lateral head of FHB mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The peroneus longus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The medial head of FHB tendon (also receives fibers from AbH)', 'The medial sesamoid is embedded in the medial head of flexor hallucis brevis, reinforced by fibers from abductor hallucis. The lateral sesamoid sits in the lateral head with adductor hallucis fibers.'),
('77aee836-682c-5395-a1b5-7f0d6c415cbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The lateral sesamoid of the great toe lies in:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The medial head of FHB accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The peroneus longus mediated by specific receptor activation","The lateral head of FHB tendon (also receives adductor hallucis)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral head of FHB tendon (also receives adductor hallucis)', 'The lateral sesamoid is contained in the lateral head of flexor hallucis brevis tendon, which also receives adductor hallucis. It does not lie in the medial head or peroneus longus tendon.'),
('e1f330c9-b3d7-5c65-b35f-a5ccaa1cbb1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Sesamoiditis is:', '["Fracture of the sesamoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Arthritis of the sesamoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation of the sesamoid bones (causing forefoot pain)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the sesamoid bones (causing forefoot pain)', 'Sesamoiditis is inflammation of the sesamoid bones of the first metatarsophalangeal joint, producing forefoot pain under the hallux. It is distinct from a fracture or arthritis of these bones.'),
('a3df0da2-92eb-5d4b-990d-e8cb566ce2ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The hallucal interphalangeal sesamoid (hallux IP sesamoid) is:', '["present resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A less common sesamoid at the IP joint of the great toe","A fracture variant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A less common sesamoid at the IP joint of the great toe', 'A hallucal interphalangeal sesamoid is an uncommon sesamoid located in the flexor hallucis longus tendon at the IP joint of the great toe. It is a normal variant, not a fracture.'),
('92c54bcb-e168-59fe-946a-9102328d96af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The foot arch develops in early childhood. The medial longitudinal arch is typically present by:', '["3 years","1 year","10 years","Birth","5-7 years of age"]'::jsonb, '5-7 years of age', 'The medial longitudinal arch of the foot typically forms by 5-7 years of age as soft tissues and ossification mature. Newborns and toddlers are normally flatfooted due to fat pads and ligament laxity.'),
('635da888-4ed7-5330-a146-1b868db3e762'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Toddler''s flatfoot (flexible flatfoot) is:', '["A normal physiologic variant in children that usually resolves","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arthritic mediated by specific receptor activation","Rigid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pathologic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A normal physiologic variant in children that usually resolves', 'Flexible flatfoot in toddlers is a normal physiologic variant that typically resolves as the child grows. A rigid or painful flatfoot, by contrast, suggests pathology.'),
('f7d0d142-1fc5-5b65-a8e8-5f9564222491'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The too many toes sign in flatfoot indicates:', '["Hammer toes resulting in decreased functional activity","Toe deformity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abduction of the forefoot (more toes visible laterally from behind due to forefoot abduction)","Claw toes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abduction of the forefoot (more toes visible laterally from behind due to forefoot abduction)', 'In flatfoot, forefoot abduction makes more toes visible laterally when viewed from behind, giving the "too many toes" sign. It does not specifically indicate hammer or claw toes.'),
('8920dbcb-83fc-5e4e-aa4f-438abb408439'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Jack test for flexible flatfoot is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Plantarflexion of the great toe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsiflexion of the great toe causing elevation of the arch (positive in flexible flatfoot)","Eversion of the foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflexion of the great toe causing elevation of the arch (positive in flexible flatfoot)', 'In the Jack test, dorsiflexing the great toe tightens the plantar fascia (windlass mechanism) and elevates the arch, indicating a flexible flatfoot. A rigid flatfoot shows no such arch restoration.'),
('70bf2f56-9a77-5f86-95de-487d8b5fbdf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The navicular drop test is used to assess:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Arch height accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heel alignment mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The degree of flatfoot (navicular height difference from neutral foot to relaxed stance)"]'::jsonb, 'The degree of flatfoot (navicular height difference from neutral foot to relaxed stance)', 'The navicular drop test measures the fall of the navicular from the neutral foot position to relaxed stance, quantifying the degree of flatfoot. It does not directly assess heel alignment.'),
('9dddb44c-1afc-5366-b2bf-a766c735ebb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Foot orthotics function to:', '["Replace surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Support the arches redistribute pressure and improve foot mechanics","Cure the underlying disease mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Support the arches redistribute pressure and improve foot mechanics', 'Foot orthotics support the arches, redistribute plantar pressure, and improve foot mechanics. They are adjunctive devices, not a cure for underlying disease or a substitute for surgery when indicated.'),
('5b8bfb8c-0487-5817-a7b8-db0defb8a1d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Custom foot orthotics are prescribed for:', '["All foot pain resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","General use without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Specific biomechanical abnormalities (excessive pronation high arch etc.)"]'::jsonb, 'Specific biomechanical abnormalities (excessive pronation high arch etc.)', 'Custom orthotics target specific biomechanical problems such as excessive pronation or a high arch. They are not prescribed for general use or for all foot pain indiscriminately.'),
('675d25a8-deaa-5ac0-816a-fd44c523a209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'A heel lift (shoe insert) is used to treat:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Bunion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Leg length discrepancy and Achilles tightness","Flatfoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leg length discrepancy and Achilles tightness', 'A heel lift compensates for a leg length discrepancy and reduces tension on a tight, contracted Achilles tendon. It does not primarily treat bunions or flatfoot.'),
('6466e3bb-973a-5971-9bb3-90ea43560eaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Mortise view on ankle X-ray is:', '["A lateral view resulting in decreased functional activity","An AP view with the foot in 15-20 degrees of internal rotation to visualize the ankle mortise","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An oblique view leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An AP view with the foot in 15-20 degrees of internal rotation to visualize the ankle mortise', 'The mortise view is an anteroposterior radiograph taken with the foot internally rotated 15-20 degrees so the tibiofibular joint and talar dome align within the mortise. A lateral or oblique view does not achieve this.'),
('09e651b9-4df2-5b23-9ecb-d9166d8d01ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The ankle mortise on X-ray should show:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Open space medially accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The entire talar dome within the mortise with equal space throughout (no widening)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Overlap of tibia and fibula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The entire talar dome within the mortise with equal space throughout (no widening)', 'A normal mortise view shows the full talar dome seated within the tibiofibular mortise with equal joint space on all sides. Asymmetric widening implies syndesmotic injury.'),
('f620d9f1-7b35-5f11-83be-c9552682ec98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Weber classification for ankle fractures is based on:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The number of malleoli fractured accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The level of the fibular fracture relative to the syndesmosis","The talar shift leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The level of the fibular fracture relative to the syndesmosis', 'The Weber classification grades fibular fractures by their height relative to the tibiofibular syndesmosis: below (A), at (B), or above (C). It is not based on the number of malleoli or talar shift.'),
('c1da3fce-acce-5f79-a76a-59788c787c79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Weber A ankle fracture is:', '["Transsyndesmotic (at the level) resulting in decreased functional activity","Supersyndesmotic (above the level) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Infrasyndesmotic (fibular fracture below the syndesmosis level)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infrasyndesmotic (fibular fracture below the syndesmosis level)', 'A Weber A fracture is infrasyndesmotic, occurring below the syndesmosis level and sparing the syndesmosis itself. Weber B is at the syndesmosis and Weber C is above it.'),
('124957a2-116a-5a5a-85e5-702b5000a061'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Weber B ankle fracture is:', '["Infrasyndesmotic resulting in decreased functional activity","Supersyndesmotic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transsyndesmotic (fibular fracture at the level of the syndesmosis)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transsyndesmotic (fibular fracture at the level of the syndesmosis)', 'A Weber B fracture is transsyndesmotic, with the fibular fracture at the level of the syndesmosis, typically oblique and prone to syndesmotic injury. Infrasyndesmotic (A) and supersyndesmotic (C) define the other types.'),
('381a4fc0-93d3-567d-b612-851670f794c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Weber C ankle fracture is:', '["Supersyndesmotic (fibular fracture above the syndesmosis)","Below the syndesmosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the syndesmosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supersyndesmotic (fibular fracture above the syndesmosis)', 'A Weber C fracture is supersyndesmotic, in which the fibular fracture lies above the syndesmosis and almost always ruptures the syndesmosis. Fractures below or at the syndesmosis are types A and B.'),
('e3639d12-b11b-5df8-8374-f63753a7fca8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Lauge-Hansen classification for ankle fractures is based on:', '["The mechanism of injury (position of foot at the time of injury and direction of force)","The fibular level accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The talar shift leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mechanism of injury (position of foot at the time of injury and direction of force)', 'The Lauge-Hansen system classifies ankle fractures by mechanism, combining the foot position (supination or pronation) with the direction of the deforming force. It does not depend on the fibular level or talar shift.'),
('56ba0b1f-ed5f-5776-af18-9d9566fc5cc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Supination-external rotation (SER) is the most common mechanism of ankle fracture. Stage 1 is:', '["Posterior malleolus fracture","Oblique fibular fracture","ATFL rupture (or avulsion of fibular tip)","None","Medial malleolus fracture"]'::jsonb, 'ATFL rupture (or avulsion of fibular tip)', 'In SER injuries, stage 1 is a tear of the anterior tibiofibular ligament (or avulsion of its fibular tip); later stages add an oblique fibular fracture, posterior malleolus injury, and medial malleolus fracture.'),
('31b1a648-8b3f-52bf-af58-dfcf9440c379'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The posterior malleolus is part of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The calcaneus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The fibula mediated by specific receptor activation","The tibia (posterior lip of tibial plafond)","The talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tibia (posterior lip of tibial plafond)', 'The posterior malleolus is the posterior lip of the tibial plafond, forming part of the tibia. The lateral malleolus is the fibula, and the medial malleolus is the tibia.'),
('95b07e38-2a43-58ca-9234-3ac4bd4008ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Trimalleolar ankle fracture involves:', '["Only lateral malleolus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral malleolus medial malleolus and posterior malleolus","Bimalleolar fracture only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral malleolus medial malleolus and posterior malleolus', 'A trimalleolar fracture involves all three malleoli: lateral, medial, and posterior. Involvement of the posterior malleolus distinguishes it from a bimalleolar fracture.'),
('1189a20b-fc84-5263-a63b-bf41c2b781c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Bimalleolar ankle fracture involves:', '["Medial only","Posterior only","Lateral and medial malleoli","None","Lateral only"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and medial malleoli', 'A bimalleolar fracture involves the medial and lateral malleoli. Adding the posterior malleolus converts it to a trimalleolar fracture.'),
('e12be514-29c9-54cb-bcd4-9482c01eb9d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Maisonneuve fracture is:', '["A fracture of the proximal fibula (associated with a syndesmotic injury and medial malleolus fracture)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fracture of the distal fibula mediated by specific receptor activation","Ankle dislocation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fracture of talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fracture of the proximal fibula (associated with a syndesmotic injury and medial malleolus fracture)', 'The Maisonneuve fracture is a proximal fibula fracture combined with rupture of the syndesmosis and often a medial malleolus fracture, following an external rotation injury. It is not a distal fibula fracture.'),
('677c017d-d614-5206-8eb2-0de56841e377'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Bosworth fracture-dislocation is:', '["An irreducible ankle fracture-dislocation where the fibula is locked behind the tibia","Another name for Maisonneuve fracture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Weber B fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","Weber C fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An irreducible ankle fracture-dislocation where the fibula is locked behind the tibia', 'In a Bosworth fracture-dislocation the distal fibula is dislocated and locked behind the tibia, making closed reduction difficult. It is a distinct entity separate from the Maisonneuve injury.'),
('a6b171e0-ff3a-5d2f-ab1d-c99037cd27c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Pilon (plafond) fracture is:', '["An intra-articular fracture of the distal tibial plafond (high-energy axial load)","An ankle sprain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A malleolar fracture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An intra-articular fracture of the distal tibial plafond (high-energy axial load)', 'A pilon (plafond) fracture is an intra-articular fracture of the distal tibial articular surface caused by high-energy axial loading. It is not a simple malleolar fracture or ankle sprain.'),
('c11be393-6746-502a-8d6b-751d8dc8577f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Ankle fracture-dislocation with skin tenting requires:', '["Physical therapy resulting in decreased functional activity","Urgent reduction (to prevent skin necrosis)","Elective surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Casting only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urgent reduction (to prevent skin necrosis)', 'Ankle fracture-dislocation that tents the skin is an emergency requiring urgent reduction because the tight skin can necrose. Casting, elective surgery, or therapy alone are not urgent priorities.'),
('3a9bcd13-2d22-5df4-be33-3f1bb884fd8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Ottawa ankle rules have a sensitivity of nearly:', '["90% resulting in decreased functional activity","50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","75% mediated by specific receptor activation","100% for detecting ankle/foot fractures (if any of the criteria are met)","80% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '100% for detecting ankle/foot fractures (if any of the criteria are met)', 'The Ottawa ankle rules are a screening tool designed to rule out fractures, so a sensitivity near 100% ensures that when no criteria are met an ankle or foot fracture is virtually excluded. Values of 50-90% would allow fractures to be missed and are unacceptable for such a rule.'),
('928b5d69-e22d-51bf-b0bd-9705d2989d8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Ottawa ankle rules for ankle X-ray include:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone tenderness at the base of the 5th metatarsal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone tenderness at the posterior edge of the lateral or medial malleolus or inability to bear weight immediately and in ED","Rarely without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bone tenderness at the navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone tenderness at the posterior edge of the lateral or medial malleolus or inability to bear weight immediately and in ED', 'An ankle X-ray is indicated by tenderness at the posterior edge of the lateral or medial malleolus or by inability to bear weight for four steps immediately and in the emergency department. Tenderness at the navicular or the base of the 5th metatarsal belongs to the foot rules, not the ankle rules.'),
('cafde14e-d567-57e2-9fb3-ea0407526167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Ottawa foot rules for foot X-ray include:', '["Tenderness at the ankle resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the 5th metatarsal mediated by specific receptor activation","Tenderness at the base of the 5th metatarsal or navicular OR inability to bear weight","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tenderness at the base of the 5th metatarsal or navicular OR inability to bear weight', 'A foot X-ray is required when there is bone tenderness at the base of the 5th metatarsal or the navicular, or when the patient cannot bear weight for four steps. Tenderness at the ankle alone, only the 5th metatarsal, or none of these criteria would not satisfy the foot rules.'),
('757c2159-8297-59e9-aeb5-9c795af32209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Maisonneuve fracture requires X-ray of the:', '["Full tibia and fibula (because the fibular fracture may be proximal)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the knee mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the ankle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Full tibia and fibula (because the fibular fracture may be proximal)', 'A Maisonneuve fracture is a spiral fracture of the proximal fibula with a medial malleolus fracture or deltoid ligament injury, so the full tibia and fibula must be imaged to detect the proximal fibular injury. Radiographs limited to the ankle, knee, or foot would miss the proximal fracture.'),
('fb1c5f7c-01b8-51f9-826e-83af1a6f6e51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Ankle fracture surgery aims to:', '["Restore the ankle mortise and fibular length and stability","Just fix the fibula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Just fix the medial malleolus mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Restore the ankle mortise and fibular length and stability', 'Ankle fracture surgery reconstructs the mortise by restoring fibular length, rotation, and stability so that the talus is congruent within the joint. Fixing only the fibula or only the medial malleolus fails to restore the full weight-bearing architecture, and doing nothing risks displacement and arthritis.'),
('7b33ef32-e915-5972-b06e-c137f4501a71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Syndesmotic fixation is often required in:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Isolated lateral malleolus fractures mediated by specific receptor activation","Weber C fractures and Maisonneuve fractures","Weber A fractures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weber C fractures and Maisonneuve fractures', 'Weber C fractures lie proximal to the syndesmosis and, like Maisonneuve fractures, tear the tibiofibular syndesmosis, so trans-syndesmotic fixation is commonly needed. Weber A fractures are below the syndesmosis and isolated lateral malleolus fractures usually leave the syndesmosis intact.'),
('2f0a4d5d-a198-5bd0-a6bd-874587722e77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The syndesmosis is most commonly fixed with:', '["Intramedullary nails resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plates only mediated by specific receptor activation","K-wires without affecting the primary tissue pathway","One or two trans-syndesmotic screws (cortical or positional screws)"]'::jsonb, 'One or two trans-syndesmotic screws (cortical or positional screws)', 'The distal tibiofibular syndesmosis is usually stabilized with one or two trans-syndesmotic screws placed above the plafond to hold the fibula within the incisura. Intramedullary nails, plates alone, or K-wires do not reliably restore syndesmotic stability.'),
('b9e8598f-59ff-5323-9bec-379e57fe792e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Postoperative rehabilitation after ankle fracture usually includes:', '["physiotherapy resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Immediate full weight-bearing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-weight-bearing for 6 weeks then progressive weight-bearing"]'::jsonb, 'Non-weight-bearing for 6 weeks then progressive weight-bearing', 'After internal fixation the ankle is protected non-weight-bearing for about 6 weeks to allow the fracture and soft tissues to heal, after which weight-bearing is advanced gradually. Immediate full weight-bearing risks loss of fixation and delayed healing.'),
('50830d98-bce9-5a4f-810b-c96325618784'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Lisfranc injury requires:', '["Surgical fixation (screw or plate fixation of the tarsometatarsal joint)","Bracing only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Physical therapy only mediated by specific receptor activation","Casting only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical fixation (screw or plate fixation of the tarsometatarsal joint)', 'Displaced Lisfranc injuries disrupt the tarsometatarsal complex and require anatomic reduction with screw or plate fixation to restore the midfoot arch. Casting, bracing, or therapy alone leave the articulation displaced and predispose to post-traumatic arthritis.'),
('0966e9ee-34c4-5e2f-91c7-d4120a9d33d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The first metatarsal is the:', '["Shortest and thickest metatarsal","Longest and narrowest metatarsal","None","Same as others","The most commonly fractured"]'::jsonb, 'Shortest and thickest metatarsal', 'The first metatarsal is the shortest but widest and strongest of the metatarsals because it bears a large share of body weight during push-off. It is not the longest or narrowest, nor is it the most commonly fractured.'),
('b9bfcaa9-4125-5631-96f2-e061bbd77500'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The second metatarsal is the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Thickest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shortest mediated by specific receptor activation","Most mobile without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Longest metatarsal (most commonly involved in stress fractures)"]'::jsonb, 'Longest metatarsal (most commonly involved in stress fractures)', 'The second metatarsal is the longest and is held rigidly at its base by the Lisfranc complex, so it absorbs repetitive loading and is the classic site of march fractures. It is not the thickest, shortest, or most mobile metatarsal.'),
('74511aab-f505-51d3-b282-37ec5ba9d11f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The fifth metatarsal base fracture has two types:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Stress fracture and avulsion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avulsion (styloid) fracture from peroneus brevis and Jones fracture at metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction","Shaft and head without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Avulsion (styloid) fracture from peroneus brevis and Jones fracture at metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction', 'Fifth metatarsal base injuries are either an avulsion fracture of the styloid pulled by the peroneus brevis or a Jones fracture at the metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction. Stress/avulsion and shaft/head groupings do not represent the two classic base fracture types.'),
('d771b9d3-ebca-5af4-a527-15e3619fd3b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'A Jones fracture is notorious for:', '["Non-union (due to watershed blood supply at the metaphyseal junction)","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Avascular necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Healing well without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-union rare leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-union (due to watershed blood supply at the metaphyseal junction)', 'The metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction of the proximal fifth metatarsal lies in a watershed zone of blood supply, so Jones fractures have a high rate of delayed union and non-union. Infection and avascular necrosis are not the characteristic complications of this fracture.'),
('5c560cbb-e448-5892-b6e9-256e0d2123b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Torg classification is used for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcaneal fractures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ankle fractures mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proximal 5th metatarsal fractures (Jones fractures)"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal 5th metatarsal fractures (Jones fractures)', 'The Torg system classifies proximal fifth metatarsal fractures, including Jones fractures, based on the width of the fracture line, chronicity, and sclerosis to guide management. It does not apply to calcaneal or ankle fractures.'),
('6ea0d651-08e2-5822-bc3f-75fb2e1d9038'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Stress fractures of the foot most commonly affect:', '["The talus resulting in decreased functional activity","The 5th metatarsal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The 2nd metatarsal neck (march fracture)","The calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The 2nd metatarsal neck (march fracture)', 'Repetitive loading concentrates on the longest, fixed second metatarsal, so its neck is the most common site of foot stress (march) fractures. The talus, calcaneus, navicular, and fifth metatarsal are affected less often.'),
('55f5f3e7-d01c-57e4-ac97-2a681e6b76ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The calcaneal stress fracture often presents with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Arch pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forefoot pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Toe pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heel pain (positive squeeze test of the calcaneus)"]'::jsonb, 'Heel pain (positive squeeze test of the calcaneus)', 'Calcaneal stress fractures cause deep heel pain that worsens with activity and reproduces with medial-lateral compression of the calcaneus (positive squeeze test). Pain in the arch, forefoot, or toes points to other sites.'),
('1cd5b884-c18f-5778-9fbc-bd35ea04b2cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Navicular stress fracture is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Common in jumpers and hurdlers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","visible on X-ray initially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A challenging diagnosis (the Forgotten stress fracture of the midfoot)"]'::jsonb, 'A challenging diagnosis (the Forgotten stress fracture of the midfoot)', 'Navicular stress fractures are easily missed because plain films are frequently normal initially, earning them the label of the forgotten midfoot fracture. They are not reliably visible on early X-rays even though they do occur in jumpers and hurdlers.'),
('08f60fc8-04b0-508a-9556-184144af162f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The dancer''s fracture is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A calcaneal fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","An ankle sprain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An oblique fracture of the distal 5th metatarsal shaft"]'::jsonb, 'An oblique fracture of the distal 5th metatarsal shaft', 'A dancer''s fracture is an oblique fracture of the distal fifth metatarsal shaft, typically sustained during a twist on a plantarflexed foot. It is not a calcaneal fracture or an ankle sprain.'),
('1066468a-558f-5a67-be9e-adce6939a5bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Freiberg''s infraction (osteochondrosis) of the 2nd metatarsal head causes:', '["Toe deformity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heel pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Arch pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Joint pain stiffness and flattening of the metatarsal head"]'::jsonb, 'Joint pain stiffness and flattening of the metatarsal head', 'Freiberg''s infraction is avascular necrosis of the second metatarsal head that causes metatarsophalangeal joint pain, stiffness, and flattening of the head. It does not produce heel or arch pain or a primary toe deformity.'),
('bd544ad1-ecf7-50b8-8440-5a7152feedc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The toe deformities (hammer toe claw toe mallet toe) often require:', '["Casting resulting in decreased functional activity","Bracing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Observation only mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgical correction (tendon transfers or joint fusion)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical correction (tendon transfers or joint fusion)', 'Fixed hammer, claw, and mallet toe deformities that fail conservative management are corrected surgically with tendon transfers, arthrodesis, or joint resection. Casting, bracing, or observation alone cannot correct a fixed deformity.'),
('0b4d82b7-e723-5e5f-b4e2-0d0f9610dd44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Hammer toe surgery can involve:', '["PIP joint arthrodesis or flexor-to-extensor tendon transfer (Girdlestone-Taylor)","Only steroid injection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only amputation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Physical therapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PIP joint arthrodesis or flexor-to-extensor tendon transfer (Girdlestone-Taylor)', 'Flexible hammer toes are corrected by a flexor-to-extensor tendon transfer (Girdlestone-Taylor), while fixed deformities are treated with PIP joint arthrodesis. Steroid injection, amputation, and physical therapy are not the surgical methods used.'),
('c7386fe2-b474-5d33-9512-2f9505cb2bb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The plantar wart (verruca plantaris) is caused by:', '["Human papillomavirus (HPV) most commonly types 1 2 4","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fungal infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Bacterial infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Human papillomavirus (HPV) most commonly types 1 2 4', 'Plantar warts are caused by human papillomavirus, most often types 1, 2, and 4, which infect the keratinocytes of plantar skin. They are viral in origin, not fungal, bacterial, or purely traumatic.'),
('4cf0c6ab-2594-5504-98d3-41b9aa9bc7f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Plantar warts differ from calluses by:', '["Loss of skin lines and punctate bleeding (capillary dots)","Pain on direct pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Location mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain on side-to-side compression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of skin lines and punctate bleeding (capillary dots)', 'Warts interrupt the normal skin lines and show thrombosed capillary dots that bleed with paring, whereas calluses retain intact skin lines. Both may be tender with direct pressure, so location and side-to-side compression do not distinguish them.'),
('a3996d09-982b-5583-b264-ccb81d00f709'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Morton''s neuroma (interdigital neuritis) most commonly affects:', '["The 1st web space resulting in decreased functional activity","The 4th web space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The 5th web space mediated by specific receptor activation","The 3rd web space (between 3rd and 4th toes)","The 2nd web space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The 3rd web space (between 3rd and 4th toes)', 'Morton''s neuroma arises from a plantar digital nerve, most commonly in the third web space between the third and fourth toes. The other web spaces are affected far less often.'),
('59a23b3a-4596-57a9-9781-468a25161316'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Mulder''s sign in Morton''s neuroma is:', '["Palpable mass in the arch resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A palpable click on squeezing the forefoot with lateral compression","Heel pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A palpable click on squeezing the forefoot with lateral compression', 'Mulder''s sign is a palpable click, often with radiating pain, produced when lateral compression of the forefoot squeezes the neuroma between the metatarsal heads. It is not a heel sign or a mass in the arch.'),
('aca1ae88-3d24-5103-8354-26d2cfd04b28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Morton''s neuroma is treated with:', '["Metatarsal padding corticosteroid injection or surgical neurectomy","Antibiotics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Physical therapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metatarsal padding corticosteroid injection or surgical neurectomy', 'Initial treatment uses metatarsal padding and corticosteroid injection, with surgical neurectomy reserved for refractory cases. Antibiotics and physical therapy do not address the entrapped nerve, and surgery alone is not the first-line option.'),
('3ecd1841-3d63-59f6-a322-08f42da7323f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Gout in the foot most commonly presents as:', '["Midfoot pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Podagra (acute gout of the 1st MTP joint)","Heel pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Ankle pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Podagra (acute gout of the 1st MTP joint)', 'Podagra is the classic presentation of acute gout, with sudden severe pain, swelling, and erythema of the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Midfoot, heel, and ankle involvement is much less characteristic.'),
('73708161-e875-578d-8e7c-f99453932743'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Acute gout treatment includes:', '["Antibiotics resulting in decreased functional activity","NSAIDs colchicine and steroids","Only rest mediated by specific receptor activation","Only surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NSAIDs colchicine and steroids', 'Acute gout flares are treated with NSAIDs, colchicine, or corticosteroids to control inflammation and pain. Antibiotics are not used because gout is inflammatory rather than infectious, and rest alone does not terminate the flare.'),
('2cdd3203-ff76-543b-a198-fda13d69d9e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Thiazide diuretics and low-dose aspirin can:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Treat gout mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease uric acid levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase the risk of gout (by increasing uric acid levels)"]'::jsonb, 'Increase the risk of gout (by increasing uric acid levels)', 'Thiazides reduce renal urate excretion and low-dose aspirin raises serum urate, so both can precipitate gout attacks. They neither treat gout nor lower uric acid levels.'),
('a75a5c28-43b9-5a85-9273-db5ddadfe46b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Charcot foot in diabetics presents with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Warm erythematous swollen foot (often painless fractures and dislocations)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Ulcer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Warm erythematous swollen foot (often painless fractures and dislocations)', 'Acute Charcot neuroarthropathy presents as a warm, erythematous, swollen foot with painless fractures and dislocations due to neuropathy, often mimicking cellulitis. It is not primarily an ulcer or infection, although those can coexist.'),
('d2b633e4-9389-5e8b-8f8a-ad3d4b03e1be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The Eichenholtz classification is used for:', '["Charcot neuroarthropathy (stages I fragmentation II coalescence III reconstruction)","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetic foot ulcers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Charcot neuroarthropathy (stages I fragmentation II coalescence III reconstruction)', 'The Eichenholtz classification stages Charcot neuroarthropathy into fragmentation, coalescence, and reconstruction phases to guide treatment by disease activity. It does not classify diabetic foot ulcers, which use the Wagner system, and it does not grade infection.'),
('57eabb9d-428a-5a16-8536-f843af077401'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Charcot foot acute stage is treated with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgery immediately accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibiotics mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Total contact cast (immobilization and offloading)"]'::jsonb, 'Total contact cast (immobilization and offloading)', 'The acute, active stage of Charcot foot is treated with a total contact cast to immobilize and offload the foot while the fragmentation phase consolidates. Immediate surgery, antibiotics, or observation do not address the active bone destruction.'),
('ee0a41c3-8527-5347-afc7-21c47f657c60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The diabetic foot ulcer is classified using:', '["Wagner classification (grades 0-5 based on depth and infection)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eichenholtz mediated by specific receptor activation","Torg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Weber leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wagner classification (grades 0-5 based on depth and infection)', 'Diabetic foot ulcers are commonly graded with the Wagner system from 0 (intact skin) to 5 (gangrene of the whole foot) based on depth and infection. Eichenholtz, Torg, and Weber are used for Charcot arthropathy, fifth metatarsal fractures, and ankle fractures, respectively.'),
('4463991d-3efc-50a5-9978-db4633805503'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The University of Texas (UT) diabetic wound classification includes:', '["Depth infection and ischemia (better predictor of amputation risk)","Only ischemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Only depth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depth infection and ischemia (better predictor of amputation risk)', 'The UT system grades diabetic foot wounds by depth, infection, and ischemia together, which predicts the risk of amputation better than depth alone. Considering only one variable, such as ischemia, infection, or depth, loses the combined prognostic value.'),
('237e32c3-a4fe-5f9c-aa62-bde9936166d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The goal of diabetic foot care is:', '["Prevention (education footwear regular inspection)","Amputation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgery only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevention (education footwear regular inspection)', 'Diabetic foot care focuses on prevention through patient education, proper footwear, and regular foot inspection to avoid ulceration. Amputation and surgery are consequences of failed prevention, not goals of care.'),
('d99ffecf-be6a-574e-860d-0619aff9c7c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Offloading in diabetic foot ulcers is achieved with:', '["Total contact cast (TCC) removable cast walker (RCW) or a Cam walker","Only surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only antibiotics mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Total contact cast (TCC) removable cast walker (RCW) or a Cam walker', 'Offloading redistributes plantar pressure using a total contact cast, removable cast walker, or Cam walker so the ulcer can heal. Antibiotics and surgery treat infection and wound complications but do not provide offloading.'),
('681b4ec9-b66f-5f26-83f9-fa952dc999d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The heel is the most common site of pressure ulcers in:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Diabetic patients accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bedridden and hospitalized patients (sacral and heel ulcers)","Ambulatory patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bedridden and hospitalized patients (sacral and heel ulcers)', 'In bedridden and hospitalized patients the heel is a common pressure ulcer site because it sustains prolonged pressure under thin soft-tissue coverage, along with the sacrum. Ambulatory patients are not the primary at-risk group.'),
('e27b4b33-e632-57b3-bcd0-07567fa94a5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The use of the ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) is for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Foot drop (peroneal nerve palsy) and ankle instability","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Toe deformity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flat foot only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foot drop (peroneal nerve palsy) and ankle instability', 'An ankle-foot orthosis controls the ankle and foot by preventing foot drop from peroneal nerve palsy and stabilizing the ankle during gait. It does not treat toe deformities and is not limited to flat-footed patients.'),
('a93633df-db81-51a3-a06b-cb0c4425a30f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The most common complication of ankle fracture is:', '["Infection","Post-traumatic arthritis","Malunion","Nerve injury","Non-union"]'::jsonb, 'Post-traumatic arthritis', 'Post-traumatic arthritis is the most common late complication of ankle fractures, particularly when residual articular displacement is present. Non-union, malunion, infection, and nerve injury are all less frequent.'),
('c9f4c4be-2bd6-58f7-9f2c-ca287abc3570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The most common complication of calcaneal fracture is:', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Non-union mediated by specific receptor activation","Subtalar arthritis and heel widening","Compartment syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subtalar arthritis and heel widening', 'Intra-articular calcaneal fractures commonly lead to subtalar arthritis, and the injury typically produces heel widening. Non-union, infection, compartment syndrome, and nerve injury are less common than subtalar degeneration.'),
('606e04a2-feab-50c1-a733-fca3aa348e22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Compartment syndrome of the foot can occur after:', '["Plantar fasciitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ankle sprain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crush injuries and fractures of the foot (calcaneus Lisfranc multiple metatarsals)"]'::jsonb, 'Crush injuries and fractures of the foot (calcaneus Lisfranc multiple metatarsals)', 'High-energy injuries such as crush injuries and displaced calcaneal, Lisfranc, or multiple metatarsal fractures can raise foot compartment pressures and cause compartment syndrome. Ankle sprains and plantar fasciitis rarely do.'),
('b9df356c-3b77-5db2-83eb-70ae1f6e8509'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'The foot has how many compartments?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","9 (4 interossei 4 plantar 1 central/calcaneal)"]'::jsonb, '9 (4 interossei 4 plantar 1 central/calcaneal)', 'The foot contains nine compartments: four interosseous, four plantar (medial, lateral, and two central/calcaneal), and one central calcaneal space. The smaller numbers undercount the distinct fascial spaces of the foot.'),
('b1473207-0d41-5db6-9168-af48135876a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Foot compartment syndrome is diagnosed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Compartment pressure measurements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal exam without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain out of proportion and pain with passive toe flexion (high index of suspicion)"]'::jsonb, 'Pain out of proportion and pain with passive toe flexion (high index of suspicion)', 'Diagnosis rests mainly on clinical suspicion with pain out of proportion and pain with passive toe flexion, because the physical findings in the foot are often subtle. Compartment pressure measurement can confirm the diagnosis, but it is not the primary clinical finding, and a normal exam does not exclude the condition.'),
('866126ec-ba0f-5bf9-8feb-fa63e6f4ab7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Treatment of foot compartment syndrome is:', '["Emergent fasciotomy (dorsal and medial approaches)","Casting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bracing mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Observation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emergent fasciotomy (dorsal and medial approaches)', 'Foot compartment syndrome is a surgical emergency treated with urgent fasciotomy through dorsal and medial incisions to decompress all nine compartments. Casting, bracing, or observation allow irreversible muscle and nerve damage.'),
('f9a76097-de99-51e3-a6f9-0e0ea9c81094'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Diabetic foot infections often involve:', '["Only anaerobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Only Strep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polymicrobial (aerobic and anaerobic) including Staph Strep Gram-negative and anaerobes","Only Gram-negative without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Staph leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Polymicrobial (aerobic and anaerobic) including Staph Strep Gram-negative and anaerobes', 'Chronic diabetic foot infections are usually polymicrobial, containing Staphylococcus, Streptococcus, Gram-negative bacilli, and anaerobes. The single-organism choices underestimate the mixed flora that is typically present.'),
('276828b2-01d2-5ff5-9f47-f0e9c9b69084'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Osteomyelitis in the diabetic foot often requires:', '["Antibiotics alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Amputation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Combined surgical debridement (resection of infected bone) and antibiotics","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgery alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Combined surgical debridement (resection of infected bone) and antibiotics', 'Diabetic foot osteomyelitis is best managed with surgical resection of the infected bone combined with appropriate antibiotics. Antibiotics alone or surgery alone are insufficient for established bone infection.'),
('41798d7f-c0fb-5b90-9a5a-32318166f76b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Negative pressure wound therapy (NPWT/VAC) is used for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute closed fractures mediated by specific receptor activation","Sprains without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetic foot wounds and complex wounds (to promote granulation)"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetic foot wounds and complex wounds (to promote granulation)', 'Negative pressure wound therapy (VAC) promotes granulation tissue formation and healing of diabetic foot wounds and other complex wounds. It is not indicated for acute closed fractures or simple sprains.'),
('bf7400eb-e0ce-57f2-8862-2930ba4c46a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'In the study of Ankle Foot, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('da7d5427-5b81-52e9-a289-fef6652aef9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the ankle joint (talocrural joint): it is formed by the articulation of:', '["Calcaneus and talus","Tibia and fibula","Fibula and talus only","Tibia fibula and talus","Tibia and talus only"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia fibula and talus', 'The ankle joint is the articulation of the tibia and fibula with the talus, forming a stable hinge that allows dorsiflexion and plantarflexion. Omitting the fibula (tibia and talus only) or the talus (tibia and fibula only) leaves the joint incomplete, and the calcaneus belongs to the subtalar joint.'),
('8fdc7304-8254-59c3-9b0a-7f355990bac7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Ankle Foot', 'In the ankle and foot, which statement about the ankle joint (talocrural joint) is most accurate?', '["Calcaneus and talus","Tibia and fibula","Fibula and talus only","Tibia fibula and talus","Tibia and talus only"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia fibula and talus', 'The talocrural joint is formed by the tibia and fibula articulating with the talus. The calcaneus participates in the subtalar joint, so any answer limited to one bone or a pair of bones is incomplete.'),
('f1a5a6bf-a5a3-5cf9-b79a-ecca7f0ee7de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteal region is bounded superiorly by:', '["Sacrum","Ischial tuberosity","Gluteal fold","Greater trochanter","Iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac crest', 'The gluteal region is limited above by the iliac crest, below by the gluteal fold, and laterally by the greater trochanter. The sacrum and ischial tuberosity are bony landmarks of the lower limb, not the superior boundary.'),
('daeed9b0-8b56-55b5-aa38-c072c2b18388'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus maximus originates from all except:', '["Iliac crest (posterior gluteal line)","Sacrum and coccyx","Thoracolumbar fascia","Sacrotuberous ligament","Gluteal surface of ilium (anterior gluteal line)"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteal surface of ilium (anterior gluteal line)', 'Gluteus maximus arises from the ilium behind the posterior gluteal line, the sacrum, coccyx, thoracolumbar fascia, and sacrotuberous ligament. The gluteal surface of the ilium anterior to the posterior gluteal line gives origin to gluteus medius and minimus, not to gluteus maximus.'),
('ac9eb82f-0646-5d27-ae97-bbfed3166272'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus maximus inserts primarily into:', '["Linea aspera resulting in decreased functional activity","Greater trochanter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteal tuberosity of femur and iliotibial tract","Lesser trochanter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pectineal line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteal tuberosity of femur and iliotibial tract', 'The deep fibers of gluteus maximus insert into the gluteal tuberosity of the femur, while its superficial bulk inserts into the iliotibial tract. The linea aspera, lesser trochanter, pectineal line, and greater trochanter receive insertions of other muscles.'),
('7e1e759b-0372-52a8-906d-9beac81f67ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus maximus acts to:', '["Extend and laterally rotate the thigh","Flex the thigh","Adduct the thigh","Medially rotate the thigh","Abduct the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Extend and laterally rotate the thigh', 'By pulling from behind the axis of the hip joint, gluteus maximus powerfully extends the thigh and acts with the other glutei and short muscles to rotate it laterally. Flexion, adduction, medial rotation, and abduction are actions of other hip muscles.'),
('b1758915-ff38-57d7-9d72-7a6e2bb8a0eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus medius originates from:', '["Iliac crest anteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischial tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Gluteal surface of ilium (between anterior and posterior gluteal lines)","Pubis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteal surface of ilium (between anterior and posterior gluteal lines)', 'Gluteus medius arises from the outer surface of the ilium between the anterior and posterior gluteal lines. The ischial tuberosity, sacrum, pubis, and anterior iliac crest are sites of origin of other muscles.'),
('b7cda011-598a-5449-a7a2-7987c21de046'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus minimus originates from:', '["Gluteal surface of ilium (between anterior and inferior gluteal lines)","Sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial spine mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteal surface of ilium (between anterior and inferior gluteal lines)', 'Gluteus minimus arises from the outer surface of the ilium between the anterior and inferior gluteal lines, deep to gluteus medius. The sacrum, ischial spine, pubis, and iliac crest are origins of other structures.'),
('b0cfc35f-6260-5953-af61-67f19b390be7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus minimus inserts into:', '["Iliotibial tract","Posterior greater trochanter","Pectineal line","Anterior border of greater trochanter","Gluteal tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of greater trochanter', 'Gluteus minimus attaches to the anterior border of the greater trochanter. The iliotibial tract, gluteal tuberosity, pectineal line, and posterior greater trochanter receive insertions of other muscles.'),
('54cfb0d8-8b0e-5273-a4e2-b4d3981ad04f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus medius inserts into:', '["Medial surface of greater trochanter","Gluteal tuberosity","Iliotibial tract","Lesser trochanter","Lateral surface of greater trochanter"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral surface of greater trochanter', 'The tendon of gluteus medius inserts on the lateral surface of the greater trochanter. The medial surface of the greater trochanter is the insertion of obturator externus and the gemelli, while the gluteal tuberosity, iliotibial tract, and lesser trochanter belong to other muscles.'),
('f5442a50-e8ce-58ee-b163-f525e2a76c8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The main function of gluteus medius and minimus is:', '["Abduction and medial rotation of thigh","Lateral rotation of thigh","Flexion of thigh","Extension of thigh","Adduction of thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Abduction and medial rotation of thigh', 'Gluteus medius and minimus are the principal abductors of the hip; their anterior fibers also medially rotate the thigh. Lateral rotation, flexion, extension, and adduction are carried out by other muscle groups.'),
('54c0da01-935b-519e-974a-91daa3de2a92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Trendelenburg''s sign is positive when:', '["The pelvis drops on the unsupported side when standing on one leg","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pelvis rises on the unsupported side mediated by specific receptor activation","The trunk leans forward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The trunk leans backward leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvis drops on the unsupported side when standing on one leg', 'Healthy hip abductors fix the pelvis and keep the unsupported side level while standing on one leg. When the abductors are weak or the lever arm is lost, the unsupported side of the pelvis drops.'),
('fc30789f-e635-5bb5-83d8-33041f4f61ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Trendelenburg gait (gluteal lurch) is seen in:', '["Inferior gluteal nerve injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral nerve injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sciatic nerve injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Obturator nerve injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior gluteal nerve injury (poliomyelitis hip dysplasia)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve injury (poliomyelitis hip dysplasia)', 'Trendelenburg gait results from weakness of the hip abductors, gluteus medius and minimus, which are supplied by the superior gluteal nerve. Injuries to the inferior gluteal, femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves spare the abductors and cause other gait deficits.'),
('9c76b231-3188-541c-a4f4-3cd7250c367a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The piriformis muscle originates from:', '["Iliac crest","Anterior surface of sacrum (S2-S4)","Ischial spine","Pubic bone","Posterior surface of sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior surface of sacrum (S2-S4)', 'Piriformis arises from the anterior surface of the sacrum between the S2 and S4 sacral foramina. It leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen to insert into the upper border of the greater trochanter.'),
('3bf0e7b8-4bcd-57f4-b5e4-aa4301aef359'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The piriformis inserts into:', '["Upper border of greater trochanter (medial side)","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteal tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Lesser trochanter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliotibial tract leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper border of greater trochanter (medial side)', 'The piriformis tendon attaches to the upper border of the greater trochanter on its medial side. The pectineal line, gluteal tuberosity, lesser trochanter, and iliotibial tract are insertion sites of other muscles.'),
('b142ec1e-e720-5ef7-b884-0dc2597928f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The piriformis muscle laterally rotates the thigh and also:', '["Extends the thigh","Adducts the thigh","Flexes the thigh","Abducts the thigh when flexed","Medially rotates the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Abducts the thigh when flexed', 'When the hip is extended, piriformis laterally rotates the thigh, but when the hip is flexed its line of pull crosses the joint axis and it abducts the thigh. Extension, adduction, flexion, and medial rotation do not match its action.'),
('47c65924-c1f5-5503-bc90-8a426deda96b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sciatic nerve emerges below which muscle?', '["Obturator internus","Gemellus superior","Quadratus femoris","Piriformis","Gemellus inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis', 'The sciatic nerve passes through the infrapiriform part of the greater sciatic foramen, below the lower border of piriformis. The gemelli, obturator internus, and quadratus femoris lie further distally and do not mark the nerve''s exit point.'),
('66adeb1a-47a3-54cb-996d-ee057b077f3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The superior gluteal nerve and vessels emerge:', '["Above the piriformis (suprapiriform foramen)","Through the inguinal canal","Through the obturator canal","Through the lesser sciatic foramen","Below the piriformis"]'::jsonb, 'Above the piriformis (suprapiriform foramen)', 'The superior gluteal nerve and vessels leave the pelvis through the suprapiriform part of the greater sciatic foramen, above piriformis. The inguinal, obturator, and lesser sciatic canals transmit different structures.'),
('bc52f854-c2e2-5d2d-8a41-1e5a50821a2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The inferior gluteal nerve and vessels emerge:', '["Below the piriformis (infrapiriform foramen)","Through the lesser sciatic foramen","Through the obturator canal","Above the piriformis","Through the suprapiriform foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Below the piriformis (infrapiriform foramen)', 'The inferior gluteal nerve and vessels, along with the sciatic, pudendal, and posterior femoral cutaneous nerves, pass through the infrapiriform foramen below piriformis. The suprapiriform foramen, obturator canal, and lesser sciatic foramen carry other structures.'),
('f60f085d-4d5b-54cb-9938-6e1408b3f320'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The suprapiriform foramen transmits:', '["Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve and vessels","Pudendal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve and vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve and vessels', 'The suprapiriform foramen transmits the superior gluteal nerve and vessels. The sciatic, pudendal, inferior gluteal, and obturator structures leave the pelvis by other routes.'),
('e5b3d1b1-c960-5507-b311-f029f94031e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The infrapiriform foramen transmits all except:', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Posterior femoral cutaneous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'The infrapiriform foramen transmits the sciatic, pudendal, and inferior gluteal nerves and the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve. The superior gluteal nerve exits above piriformis through the suprapiriform foramen.'),
('2502f389-9203-5ffd-99ea-aff700f1bb38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The obturator internus muscle originates from:', '["Pelvic surface of obturator membrane and surrounding bones","Ischial spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic surface of obturator membrane and surrounding bones', 'Obturator internus arises from the inner (pelvic) surface of the obturator membrane and the adjacent margins of the obturator foramen. The ischial spine, pubis, sacrum, and iliac crest are origins of other muscles.'),
('0b3f6061-5509-54b5-9ead-c653c1fb9307'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The obturator internus tendon passes through the:', '["Greater sciatic foramen","Obturator canal","Lesser sciatic foramen","Femoral canal","Inguinal canal"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser sciatic foramen', 'The obturator internus tendon bends around the ischial spine and passes through the lesser sciatic foramen to reach the medial surface of the greater trochanter. The greater sciatic foramen, obturator canal, femoral canal, and inguinal canal are not its route.'),
('364aeb9c-2493-5f6d-95ec-bcffe9e18094'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gemellus superior originates from:', '["Ischial spine","Iliac crest","Pubis","Sacrum","Ischial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial spine', 'Gemellus superior arises from the outer surface of the ischial spine and blends with the obturator internus tendon. The iliac crest, pubis, sacrum, and ischial tuberosity are origins of other muscles.'),
('e90f2eec-e2ff-5da5-aa9e-6cf107d12231'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gemellus inferior originates from:', '["Ischial tuberosity (upper part)","Pubis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial spine mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity (upper part)', 'Gemellus inferior arises from the upper part of the ischial tuberosity below gemellus superior. The pubis, ischial spine, sacrum, and iliac crest are not its site of origin.'),
('9cffb9a4-0a13-5cc9-b859-7c776f015088'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The quadratus femoris originates from:', '["Ischial spine resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliac crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischial tuberosity (lateral border)","Greater trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity (lateral border)', 'Quadratus femoris arises from the lateral border of the ischial tuberosity and inserts on the quadrate tubercle. The ischial spine, pubis, iliac crest, and greater trochanter are unrelated to its origin.'),
('84219837-425a-5c64-a86d-c9e30e2ffbda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The quadratus femoris inserts into:', '["Quadrate tubercle on the intertrochanteric crest","Greater trochanter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliotibial tract mediated by specific receptor activation","Gluteal tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lesser trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadrate tubercle on the intertrochanteric crest', 'Quadratus femoris inserts into the quadrate tubercle on the intertrochanteric crest. The greater trochanter, iliotibial tract, gluteal tuberosity, and lesser trochanter receive insertions of other muscles.'),
('2ab0ffcc-ac3f-5841-95c9-d6ae554244ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The short lateral rotators of the thigh include:', '["Piriformis obturator internus gemelli quadratus femoris","Adductors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hamstrings mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadriceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteus maximus medius minimus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis obturator internus gemelli quadratus femoris', 'The short lateral rotators of the hip are piriformis, obturator internus, gemellus superior and inferior, and quadratus femoris. The adductors, hamstrings, quadriceps, and glutei have different origins, insertions, and actions.'),
('714a9659-e202-55e4-9602-669e62e9d2a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tensor fasciae latae originates from:', '["Pubis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischial spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior part of iliac crest (ASIS region)","Posterior iliac crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior part of iliac crest (ASIS region)', 'Tensor fasciae latae arises from the anterior part of the outer lip of the iliac crest near the anterior superior iliac spine. The pubis, ischial spine, posterior iliac crest, and sacrum are origins of other muscles.'),
('4280aace-f604-5107-bbc4-b9f50eadbf8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tensor fasciae latae inserts into:', '["Greater trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliotibial tract (upper and middle thirds of thigh)","Gluteal tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lesser trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iliotibial tract (upper and middle thirds of thigh)', 'Tensor fasciae latae inserts into the iliotibial tract at the junction of its upper and middle thigh thirds. The greater trochanter, pectineal line, gluteal tuberosity, and lesser trochanter receive insertions of other muscles.'),
('1f80a2b3-ff83-5b58-bbf2-9e52502fdcdf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tensor fasciae latae acts to:', '["Extend the thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laterally rotate the thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Flex abduct and medially rotate the thigh","Adduct the thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex abduct and medially rotate the thigh', 'Tensor fasciae latae flexes, abducts, and medially rotates the thigh and helps steady the pelvis on the femoral head. Extension, adduction, and lateral rotation are actions of other hip muscles.'),
('ac19f62f-2e83-54b1-9ae1-c873b2f10a38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The iliotibial tract (IT band) is a thickening of:', '["None","Fascia over gluteus maximus","Superficial fascia","Deep fascia of the gluteal region","Fascia lata on the lateral thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Fascia lata on the lateral thigh', 'The iliotibial tract is the thickened lateral portion of the fascia lata of the thigh. It is not the superficial fascia, the deep fascia of the gluteal region, or the fascia covering gluteus maximus.'),
('7b111247-d511-5ce3-91fe-90610d0a17b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The iliotibial tract receives insertions from:', '["Piriformis and obturator internus resulting in decreased functional activity","Gluteus medius and minimus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteus maximus (posterior part) and tensor fasciae latae","Hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Quadriceps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus (posterior part) and tensor fasciae latae', 'The superficial fibers of gluteus maximus and the whole of tensor fasciae latae insert into the iliotibial tract. Gluteus medius and minimus, piriformis, obturator internus, hamstrings, and quadriceps do not attach to it.'),
('ed6d3866-6a89-5a40-b962-82c81df663bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteal fold is caused by:', '["The ischial tuberosity","The greater trochanter","Fibrous attachment of skin to deep fascia of thigh","The lower border of gluteus maximus","The iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous attachment of skin to deep fascia of thigh', 'The gluteal fold results from strong fibrous attachments tethering the skin to the deep fascia of the thigh. The lower border of gluteus maximus lies below the fold, and bony landmarks do not create the crease.'),
('08d93d34-c962-5fc9-9230-7497c0b93a1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lower border of gluteus maximus is:', '["Above the gluteal fold resulting in decreased functional activity","Below the gluteal fold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the knee mediated by specific receptor activation","At the level of the gluteal fold (approximately)","At mid-thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the level of the gluteal fold (approximately)', 'The inferior border of gluteus maximus descends to about the level of the gluteal fold. It does not lie above the fold, at mid-thigh, or near the knee.'),
('82327615-8d9e-566e-9516-84e5649bb1a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hip joint is what type of joint?', '["Ellipsoid joint","Saddle joint","Ball and socket (multiaxial)","Hinge joint","Pivot joint"]'::jsonb, 'Ball and socket (multiaxial)', 'The hip is a multiaxial ball-and-socket synovial joint formed by the femoral head and the acetabulum. Ellipsoid, saddle, hinge, and pivot joints permit more limited planes of movement.'),
('e9511630-d908-5881-8d57-74d727085eb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The head of the femur articulates with:', '["Patella","Tibial plateau","Acetabulum of the hip bone","Femoral condyles","None"]'::jsonb, 'Acetabulum of the hip bone', 'The spherical head of the femur fits into the cup-shaped acetabulum of the hip bone to form the hip joint. The patella, tibial plateau, and femoral condyles are not articular partners of the femoral head.'),
('e9fd4bbe-3f6b-5e71-8b1a-e3dfb1084169'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The acetabulum is formed by fusion of:', '["Ilium and ischium only","Ilium and pubis only","Ilium ischium pubis","None","Ischium and pubis only"]'::jsonb, 'Ilium ischium pubis', 'The acetabulum is formed by the union of the ilium, ischium, and pubis at the triradiate cartilage, which ossifies during adolescence. Any pairing of only two of these bones is incomplete.'),
('7f760c6d-84b8-526b-88fe-8e2890415b04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The acetabular fossa is:', '["The transverse ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The articular surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The labrum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The non-articular central depression of the acetabulum"]'::jsonb, 'The non-articular central depression of the acetabulum', 'The acetabular fossa is the central non-articular depression of the acetabulum that lodges a fat pad and gives attachment to the ligament of the head of the femur. The labrum, transverse ligament, and articular surfaces are separate acetabular structures.'),
('574071f6-1312-5de6-bce7-73c734042646'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The acetabular labrum is a:', '["Tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrocartilaginous rim that deepens the acetabulum","Bone ring mediated by specific receptor activation","Ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyaline cartilage ring leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrocartilaginous rim that deepens the acetabulum', 'The acetabular labrum is a fibrocartilaginous rim attached to the acetabular margin that deepens the socket and helps grip the femoral head. It is a fibrocartilage structure, not a tendon, ligament, bone ring, or hyaline cartilage ring.'),
('4275aea2-b33e-54e0-bec6-a38897a373ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The transverse acetabular ligament:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Is part of the labrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Bridges the acetabular notch converting it into a foramen","Is a ligament of the hip capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bridges the acetabular notch converting it into a foramen', 'The transverse acetabular ligament bridges the acetabular notch, converting it into a foramen that transmits the ligament of the head of the femur and its vessels. It is a distinct structure separate from the labrum and the capsular ligaments.'),
('227537c3-3858-52da-808a-6abb645bdac8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ligament of head of femur (ligamentum teres) attaches:', '["From femoral neck to acetabulum","None","From greater trochanter to acetabulum","From fovea to acetabular notch (transverse ligament)","From lesser trochanter to acetabulum"]'::jsonb, 'From fovea to acetabular notch (transverse ligament)', 'The ligament of the head of the femur extends from the fovea on the femoral head to the edges of the acetabular notch and the transverse acetabular ligament. The femoral neck and the trochanters are not attachment sites.'),
('f381a152-5997-5b09-beeb-c0e57a665b34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ligament of head of femur contains which artery?', '["Medial circumflex femoral resulting in decreased functional activity","Profunda femoris artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Artery of ligament of head (from obturator artery)","Lateral circumflex femoral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Artery of ligament of head (from obturator artery)', 'The ligament of the head of the femur conducts the artery of the ligament of head, a branch of the obturator artery that helps supply the femoral head during development. The profunda femoris, circumflex femoral, and femoral arteries are not the named content of this ligament.'),
('5e90166e-6aa5-5c8a-950f-230d51e776f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The iliofemoral ligament (Y-ligament of Bigelow) is located:', '["None","Superiorly from ilium to greater trochanter","Anteriorly from AIIS to intertrochanteric line","Posteriorly from ischium to femur","Medially from pubis to femur"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly from AIIS to intertrochanteric line', 'The iliofemoral ligament, the strongest ligament of the hip, passes anteriorly from the anterior inferior iliac spine to the intertrochanteric line and limits hyperextension. The other described positions belong to different capsular reinforcements.'),
('04d88457-74ee-58ac-88f6-6df4731503de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The pubofemoral ligament is located:', '["Anterosuperiorly resulting in decreased functional activity","Anteroinferiorly (from pubis to femur)","Posteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Medially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laterally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteroinferiorly (from pubis to femur)', 'The pubofemoral ligament reinforces the capsule anteroinferiorly, passing from the superior pubic ramus to the femur near the lesser trochanter, and limits abduction. The other positions belong to different capsular reinforcements.'),
('7eb8d6ed-508e-58cd-a1db-78fbf85612a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ischiofemoral ligament is located:', '["Medially resulting in decreased functional activity","Anteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superiorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Posteriorly (from ischium to femur)","Inferiorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly (from ischium to femur)', 'The ischiofemoral ligament reinforces the joint capsule posteriorly, passing from the ischium behind the acetabulum to the femur. The anterior, medial, superior, and inferior positions are occupied by other capsular components.'),
('42429076-ec52-50ef-9bc7-9c724576c12b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The zonular orbicularis is:', '["The transverse ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The iliofemoral ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ischiofemoral ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","A deep circular band of fibers around the femoral neck","A superficial ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A deep circular band of fibers around the femoral neck', 'The zonular orbicularis is a deep circular band of capsular fibers that encircles the femoral neck and grips it within the socket. It is distinct from the transverse ligament and the iliofemoral and ischiofemoral ligaments.'),
('2093581c-06c9-5df3-bf00-d22e5b3b24cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral neck is mostly intracapsular. Which part is extracapsular?', '["The intertrochanteric line","Posterior part of base of neck","The head","Anterior part of neck","The greater trochanter"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior part of base of neck', 'The capsule attaches to the intertrochanteric line anteriorly but only to the femoral neck about 1.5 cm proximal to the intertrochanteric crest posteriorly, leaving the posterior part of the base of the neck extracapsular. The head and anterior neck are fully intracapsular.'),
('ecb2795d-0b51-55ef-85d9-f46d5f7962f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hip joint capsule attaches anteriorly to the:', '["Greater trochanter","Lesser trochanter","Base of femoral neck","Fovea","Intertrochanteric line"]'::jsonb, 'Intertrochanteric line', 'Anteriorly the hip capsule attaches along the intertrochanteric line of the femur. The greater trochanter, lesser trochanter, fovea, and base of the femoral neck are not the anterior capsular attachment.'),
('fb303fc6-2a2a-5f67-879b-1cac50d312ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hip joint capsule attaches posteriorly to the:', '["Greater trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral neck (about 1.5 cm above intertrochanteric crest)","Base of neck mediated by specific receptor activation","Lesser trochanter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intertrochanteric line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral neck (about 1.5 cm above intertrochanteric crest)', 'Posteriorly the capsule attaches to the femoral neck about 1.5 cm above the intertrochanteric crest, leaving the posterior part of the neck outside the joint. The greater trochanter, lesser trochanter, base of the neck, and intertrochanteric line are not the posterior capsular attachment.'),
('4b8c2dd7-eec2-593e-ad23-b997ed94edb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The blood supply of the femoral head is mainly from:', '["Femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior gluteal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial and lateral circumflex femoral and artery of ligamentum teres","Obturator artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior gluteal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral circumflex femoral and artery of ligamentum teres', 'The femoral head is supplied chiefly by branches of the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries and by the artery of the ligament of the head of the femur. The femoral, obturator, and gluteal arteries are not the principal supply.'),
('35f7e52d-1f5a-57ec-aa4f-6f5c56626a26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The retinacular arteries are:', '["Branches of gluteal arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Branches of obturator artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Branches of the circumflex femoral arteries traveling along the femoral neck","Direct branches of femoral artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of the circumflex femoral arteries traveling along the femoral neck', 'Retinacular arteries arise from the circumflex femoral arteries and ascend in the synovial retinacula along the femoral neck to supply the head. Gluteal, obturator, and direct femoral branches do not form the retinacula.'),
('62a5ae4b-6bd2-5ba6-972e-b3813e13c7df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Fracture of the femoral neck (intracapsular) can lead to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-union only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Avascular necrosis of femoral head","Nerve injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Avascular necrosis of femoral head', 'An intracapsular femoral neck fracture interrupts the retinacular blood supply to the head derived from the medial circumflex femoral artery, causing avascular necrosis of the femoral head. Infection and nerve injury are not characteristic immediate sequelae, and non-union, although possible, is not the primary hallmark complication.'),
('0a5303b7-a827-596e-9fb3-d9cb5ff462e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Extracapsular (intertrochanteric) fractures of the femur usually:', '["Heal well (good blood supply from metaphysis)","Lead to infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lead to AVN mediated by specific receptor activation","Are not treatable without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lead to non-union leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heal well (good blood supply from metaphysis)', 'Intertrochanteric fractures lie outside the joint capsule where the rich metaphyseal blood supply is preserved, so these fractures typically unite without difficulty. Avascular necrosis is a complication of intracapsular neck fractures rather than of extracapsular ones.'),
('d920ef98-8be8-5533-b718-603d566a4ed7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hip joint is most stable in which position?', '["Adduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Extension (close-packed position)","Flexion mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Internal rotation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension (close-packed position)', 'In extension the hip capsule and iliofemoral ligament become taut and the femoral head is maximally congruent with the acetabulum, making this the close-packed and most stable position. In flexion the capsule slackens, so stability is reduced.'),
('183ef07f-46da-58af-8efd-258169035555'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hip joint is least stable in the:', '["Neutral position resulting in decreased functional activity","Abducted position accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flexed adducted and internally rotated position","Extended position without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexed adducted and internally rotated position', 'Flexion combined with adduction and internal rotation slackens the capsule and iliofemoral ligament while exposing the thinner posterior capsule, leaving the joint vulnerable to posterior dislocation. Extension restores ligamentous tension and protective stability.'),
('f6bfa8b6-9f35-518d-a658-3597131a5325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Posterior dislocation of the hip is most associated with:', '["Abduction injury resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fall on hip mediated by specific receptor activation","Dashboard injury (knee hits dashboard)","Twisting injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dashboard injury (knee hits dashboard)', 'A force transmitted along the femoral shaft when the flexed knee strikes a dashboard drives the femoral head posteriorly out of the acetabulum, causing more than 90% of hip dislocations. Falls onto the hip or simple twisting mechanisms rarely produce this injury.'),
('dd061457-4db5-51db-b221-da8248d63be5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sciatic nerve is at risk in:', '["Posterior dislocation of the hip","Femoral neck fracture","Intertrochanteric fracture","Shaft fracture","Anterior dislocation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior dislocation of the hip', 'The sciatic nerve lies immediately posterior to the hip joint, so the displaced femoral head in a posterior dislocation can stretch or compress it and produce foot drop. Femoral neck, intertrochanteric, and shaft fractures do not endanger the sciatic nerve directly.'),
('2ff48d41-bc82-59f3-9ca0-c2a324f461ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Anterior dislocation of the hip results in the limb being:', '["Abducted and medially rotated","Extended and medially rotated","None","Abducted externally rotated and slightly flexed","Adducted internally rotated and flexed"]'::jsonb, 'Abducted externally rotated and slightly flexed', 'In anterior dislocation the femoral head escapes through the weak anterior capsule below the pubofemoral ligament, so the limb adopts a position of abduction, external rotation, and slight flexion. The adducted-internally-rotated posture characterizes a posterior dislocation instead.'),
('300e7dd2-7910-531b-a0a6-eac813c8678e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Posterior dislocation of the hip results in the limb being:', '["Abducted externally rotated and flexed","Adducted internally rotated and flexed (shortened)","None","Extended and medially rotated","Abducted and medially rotated"]'::jsonb, 'Adducted internally rotated and flexed (shortened)', 'After posterior dislocation the femoral head rides posteriorly onto the ilium, so the limb appears adducted, internally rotated, flexed, and shortened. Abduction with external rotation is the stance of an anterior dislocation.'),
('620130f2-8f5a-5f68-8fae-221d06da9e07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sciatic nerve (L4-S3) supplies the hamstrings. Which hamstring is NOT supplied by the sciatic nerve?', '["Semitendinosus","None all are supplied","Short head of biceps femoris","Semimembranosus","Long head of biceps femoris"]'::jsonb, 'None all are supplied', 'Every hamstring muscle is supplied by the sciatic nerve: its tibial division innervates semitendinosus, semimembranosus, and the long head of biceps, while the short head of biceps receives a branch of the common peroneal division. Thus no hamstring lies outside the sciatic nerve supply.'),
('8248a6ed-aab8-5df2-876c-6c8ee0b27227'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The blood supply of the gluteal region is mainly from:', '["Obturator artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior and inferior gluteal arteries (internal iliac)","Profunda femoris artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior gluteal arteries (internal iliac)', 'The gluteal region is vascularized mainly by the superior and inferior gluteal arteries, terminal branches of the internal iliac artery that pass through the greater sciatic foramen. The femoral and obturator arteries supply different lower-limb territories.'),
('225d8286-55d1-52bb-b203-8e3ef184ef0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The superior gluteal artery supplies:', '["Short lateral rotators resulting in decreased functional activity","Gluteus medius gluteus minimus tensor fasciae latae","Adductors mediated by specific receptor activation","Hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteus maximus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius gluteus minimus tensor fasciae latae', 'The superior gluteal artery enters the buttock above piriformis and supplies the gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae. Gluteus maximus and the short lateral rotators depend mainly on the inferior gluteal artery.'),
('463b8b55-c939-55e4-9b71-c17a8707d696'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The inferior gluteal artery supplies:', '["Gluteus minimus resulting in decreased functional activity","Gluteus maximus and short lateral rotators","Adductors mediated by specific receptor activation","Gluteus medius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TFL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus and short lateral rotators', 'The inferior gluteal artery leaves the pelvis below piriformis and is the chief supply of gluteus maximus together with the short lateral rotators. The abductors and tensor fasciae latae are served by the superior gluteal artery.'),
('a0f30694-d115-5792-a17f-5cde0d20e2b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The cruciate anastomosis of the thigh is formed by:', '["Medial and lateral circumflex femoral inferior gluteal and 1st perforating","Femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Profunda femoris artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior gluteal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral circumflex femoral inferior gluteal and 1st perforating', 'The cruciate anastomosis is a cruciate-shaped network formed by the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries joining the inferior gluteal artery and the first perforating artery on the upper femur. It links the internal iliac (inferior gluteal) and external iliac (femoral) circulations.'),
('ab9c2274-2e9d-556e-a1f2-232bb21895b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The cruciate anastomosis provides collateral circulation around the:', '["Ankle joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Elbow joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Knee joint mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hip joint (between internal and external iliac systems)"]'::jsonb, 'Hip joint (between internal and external iliac systems)', 'The cruciate anastomosis forms a collateral network around the hip and proximal femur, bridging the internal iliac circulation via the inferior gluteal artery and the external iliac circulation via the circumflex femoral arteries. It does not serve the knee, ankle, elbow, or shoulder.'),
('7ead2ed0-0b82-521b-bac7-7ef27ade3f7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The trochanteric anastomosis is formed by:', '["Femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Profunda femoris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior gluteal only mediated by specific receptor activation","Ascending branches of medial and lateral circumflex femoral and superior gluteal","Obturator artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending branches of medial and lateral circumflex femoral and superior gluteal', 'The trochanteric anastomosis is created by the ascending branches of the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries anastomosing with the superior gluteal artery over the greater trochanter. The inferior gluteal artery instead contributes to the cruciate anastomosis.'),
('459bbb16-d1a8-5371-bff8-1ffb00bc5d3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The piriformis syndrome involves:', '["Compression of the gluteal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Gluteus medius weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Compression of the sciatic nerve by the piriformis muscle","Femoral neck fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hip dislocation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compression of the sciatic nerve by the piriformis muscle', 'In piriformis syndrome the sciatic nerve, which passes beneath or sometimes through the piriformis muscle, is compressed by it, causing buttock pain that radiates down the leg. Gluteal nerve compression, muscle weakness, fractures, and dislocations are unrelated mechanisms.'),
('00a4f1a0-fbe0-5dfc-ad57-7d223e1705b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus maximus is a:', '["Extensor and lateral rotator of thigh","Flexor and medial rotator","Flexor and lateral rotator","Extensor and medial rotator","Abductor"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor and lateral rotator of thigh', 'Gluteus maximus is the powerful extensor of the hip and a lateral (external) rotator of the thigh, engaging forcefully during rising from sitting, stair climbing, and running. Flexion and medial rotation are governed by other muscles rather than by gluteus maximus.'),
('f909e808-d634-563c-bd1b-d20846cbc0ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The most important abductor of the thigh is:', '["Tensor fasciae latae","Gluteus medius","Gluteus maximus","Piriformis","Gluteus minimus"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius', 'Gluteus medius is the principal abductor of the thigh and prevents pelvic drop on the unsupported side during the stance phase of gait. Tensor fasciae latae and gluteus minimus assist abduction, whereas gluteus maximus and piriformis are not primarily abductors.'),
('99c8239c-2d7b-5cc3-a138-a6f8f6372c44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus maximus is supplied by the inferior gluteal nerve which arises from:', '["T12 L1","L5 S1 S2","S1 S2 S3","L4 L5 S1","C5 C6 C7"]'::jsonb, 'L5 S1 S2', 'The inferior gluteal nerve originates from the anterior rami of L5, S1, and S2 and exits the pelvis below piriformis to innervate gluteus maximus. L4-L5-S1 gives the superior gluteal nerve, which supplies the abductor muscles and tensor fasciae latae.'),
('066a4e19-253c-592b-a7db-5ca0cb0d9fdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The superior gluteal nerve arises from:', '["L4 L5 S1","L5 S1 S2","T12 L1","S1 S2 S3","C5 C6 C7"]'::jsonb, 'L4 L5 S1', 'The superior gluteal nerve arises from the anterior rami of L4, L5, and S1 and runs above piriformis to supply gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae; damage produces a Trendelenburg gait. L5-S1-S2 is the segmental origin of the inferior gluteal nerve.'),
('4aad8537-0878-51b6-9b6e-424f874fe43d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve (S1-S3) supplies:', '["Skin of foot resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin of anterior thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin of medial thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin of lateral thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin of posterior thigh popliteal fossa upper calf and perineum"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of posterior thigh popliteal fossa upper calf and perineum', 'The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve is a purely cutaneous nerve that supplies the skin of the posterior thigh, popliteal fossa, upper calf, and part of the perineum. The foot, anterior thigh, and medial thigh are innervated by other nerves.'),
('e37e0ef1-63b2-553a-a31f-bcce9ae94063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The nerve to obturator internus (L5 S1) supplies:', '["Piriformis","Gemellus superior only","Quadratus femoris","Obturator internus and gemellus superior","Obturator internus only"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator internus and gemellus superior', 'The nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1) passes through the lesser sciatic foramen and innervates both obturator internus and the superior gemellus. The inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris are supplied by the nerve to quadratus femoris, and piriformis by its own nerve.'),
('ab826e98-ffb1-5525-9f5c-1fd70a145bc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris (L4 L5 S1) supplies:', '["Gemellus inferior only","Quadratus femoris and gemellus inferior","Obturator internus","Quadratus femoris only","Piriformis"]'::jsonb, 'Quadratus femoris and gemellus inferior', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris (L4, L5, S1) runs deep to the sciatic nerve and innervates quadratus femoris along with the inferior gemellus. The superior gemellus and obturator internus are served by the nerve to obturator internus.'),
('e1f1d96f-3693-5d1d-a1b4-40d8d6481df7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The greater sciatic foramen is formed by:', '["Pubis and ischium resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliac crest and sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Greater sciatic notch sacrotuberous ligament and sacrospinous ligament","Lesser sciatic notch and ligaments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater sciatic notch sacrotuberous ligament and sacrospinous ligament', 'The greater sciatic foramen is bounded by the greater sciatic notch of the hip bone and the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments, and it transmits the sciatic nerve and the gluteal vessels. The pubis, iliac crest, and lesser notch do not contribute to this foramen.'),
('3f90bf44-e83a-50ca-8971-9936cff9ea91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lesser sciatic foramen is formed by:', '["Greater sciatic notch resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Lesser sciatic notch sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments","Obturator foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser sciatic notch sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments', 'The lesser sciatic foramen is bounded by the lesser sciatic notch together with the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments, and transmits the obturator internus tendon and the pudendal neurovascular bundle. The greater sciatic notch and obturator foramen are distinct openings.'),
('247ffbf7-c1a5-56da-a9b0-62e706cb509b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels pass:', '["Through the lesser sciatic foramen (after leaving the greater sciatic foramen)","Through the inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the suprapiriform foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the obturator canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the femoral canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the lesser sciatic foramen (after leaving the greater sciatic foramen)', 'The pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels leave the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis, then re-enter the perineum through the lesser sciatic foramen and run in the pudendal canal. The inguinal, femoral, and obturator canals do not carry them.'),
('4bf0cacd-6581-5217-bd91-d7c9ab562003'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sacrotuberous ligament connects:', '["Sacrum to ischial spine","None","Ilium to ischial tuberosity","Sacrum to pubis","Sacrum to ischial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Sacrum to ischial tuberosity', 'The sacrotuberous ligament runs from the sacrum to the ischial tuberosity and, together with the sacrospinous ligament, converts the sciatic notches into foramina. The sacrum-to-ischial-spine band is the sacrospinous ligament instead.'),
('1aabcd43-c41a-5c18-8ec4-a31940564c0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sacrospinous ligament connects:', '["Sacrum to ischial tuberosity","None","Ilium to ischial spine","Sacrum to pubis","Sacrum to ischial spine"]'::jsonb, 'Sacrum to ischial spine', 'The sacrospinous ligament extends from the sacrum to the ischial spine and helps convert the greater and lesser sciatic notches into foramina. The sacrum-to-ischial-tuberosity connection belongs to the sacrotuberous ligament.'),
('48c137b3-d5ee-54e7-95d5-228743e662d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments convert:', '["The inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater and lesser sciatic notches into foramina","The femoral canal mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The obturator foramen into a canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater and lesser sciatic notches into foramina', 'The sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments bridge the margins of the sciatic notches, converting them into the greater and lesser sciatic foramina. The inguinal and femoral canals and the obturator foramen are unrelated to these ligaments.'),
('1c570755-ed7c-5558-9d2b-309050bb4e02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ischial tuberosity is:', '["The origin of adductors","The origin of quadriceps","None","The insertion of hamstrings","The weight-bearing bone in sitting"]'::jsonb, 'The weight-bearing bone in sitting', 'In the sitting position body weight is transmitted through the ischial tuberosities, which are the main weight-bearing bony points of the pelvis. The hamstrings arise from the tuberosity, and the adductors and quadriceps attach to other sites.'),
('5aaa252e-2e5a-5eab-9ad1-fe166cb903df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ischial spine is located between:', '["None","The pubis and ischium","The ilium and ischium","The greater and lesser sciatic notches","The sacrum and ischium"]'::jsonb, 'The greater and lesser sciatic notches', 'The ischial spine projects posteromedially from the ischium and lies between the greater sciatic notch above and the lesser sciatic notch below. It is not located between the pubis and ischium, the ilium and ischium, or the sacrum and ischium.'),
('f2e727c2-755e-5112-95ca-634189449740'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteal surface of the ilium has how many gluteal lines?', '["3 (posterior anterior inferior)","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (posterior anterior inferior)', 'The gluteal surface of the ilium is demarcated by three gluteal lines: the posterior, anterior, and inferior gluteal lines. These lines indicate the attachments of the gluteal muscles to the ilium.'),
('6c209007-b083-5119-b8de-7d361e3a7996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior gluteal line separates the origin of:', '["Gluteus maximus from sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Gluteus medius from gluteus minimus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteus maximus (posterior) from gluteus medius (anterior)"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus (posterior) from gluteus medius (anterior)', 'The posterior gluteal line demarcates the origin of gluteus maximus, which lies posterior to it, from that of gluteus medius anterior to it. The anterior and inferior gluteal lines bound the origins of the remaining gluteal muscles.'),
('145767b0-8fe7-539d-ae08-601b1e47d08e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The anterior gluteal line separates the origin of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Gluteus minimus from TFL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteus medius (above) from gluteus minimus (below)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteus maximus from gluteus medius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius (above) from gluteus minimus (below)', 'The anterior gluteal line runs downward and separates the origin of gluteus medius, which arises above it, from that of gluteus minimus, which arises below it. The posterior and inferior gluteal lines mark different muscular boundaries.'),
('fd4559a7-744d-50e2-9f7e-bd13980df5ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The inferior gluteal line is near the margin of the acetabulum and marks the origin of:', '["Gluteus medius resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteus minimus (below the anterior gluteal line)","Tensor fasciae latae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteus maximus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus minimus (below the anterior gluteal line)', 'The inferior gluteal line lies just above the acetabular margin and indicates the origin of gluteus minimus, which arises below the anterior gluteal line. Gluteus medius, gluteus maximus, and tensor fasciae latae originate in relation to the other gluteal lines.'),
('cf44c8da-9770-5b67-8f9f-7a489ed632a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ASIS (anterior superior iliac spine) is an important landmark giving attachment to:', '["Iliacus resulting in decreased functional activity","Psoas major accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal ligament and sartorius","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectus femoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal ligament and sartorius', 'The ASIS gives attachment to the lateral end of the inguinal ligament and serves as the origin of the sartorius muscle. Psoas major, iliacus, and rectus femoris arise from different pelvic sites.'),
('c3cadad2-a842-5e17-a864-6c3ccfffa7da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The AIIS (anterior inferior iliac spine) gives attachment to:', '["Sartorius resulting in decreased functional activity","Inguinal ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectus femoris (straight head)","Iliacus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psoas major leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectus femoris (straight head)', 'The AIIS gives origin to the straight (reflected) head of rectus femoris. Sartorius and the inguinal ligament attach at the ASIS, while iliacus and psoas major arise from the iliac fossa and lumbar vertebrae respectively.'),
('f1be8854-47cf-5c8d-9c2a-779aa8e1bd53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The PSIS (posterior superior iliac spine) is located:', '["At the ischial spine resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the pubis mediated by specific receptor activation","At the posterior end of the iliac crest","At the sacroiliac joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the posterior end of the iliac crest', 'The PSIS lies at the posterior extremity of the iliac crest, visible as a dimple just above the buttock over the sacroiliac region. It is not at the ischial spine, pubis, or the joint line itself.'),
('d54f53d0-ad0c-5185-924a-94a5a20ea7ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The iliac crest extends from:', '["AIIS to PSIS","None","AIIS to ischial tuberosity","ASIS to PSIS","ASIS to ischial spine"]'::jsonb, 'ASIS to PSIS', 'The iliac crest runs from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS). The AIIS and the ischial spine lie on different portions of the hip bone and are not crest landmarks.'),
('d09c057f-202b-566f-8bb8-42a19eefc9a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The highest point of the iliac crest is at the level of:', '["L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","S1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1 mediated by specific receptor activation","L4 spinous process (or L4-L5 intervertebral disc)","L5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L4 spinous process (or L4-L5 intervertebral disc)', 'The summit of the iliac crest lies at the level of the L4 spinous process, corresponding to the L4-L5 intervertebral disc, a standard landmark for lumbar puncture. Levels L1, L3, L5, and S1 do not match the crest summit.'),
('37933cba-92fc-5ede-a04c-fbda22bfb5f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The pubic tubercle is an important landmark for:', '["The deep inguinal ring","The obturator foramen","The femoral canal","The superficial inguinal ring","None"]'::jsonb, 'The superficial inguinal ring', 'The pubic tubercle marks the location of the superficial inguinal ring, the medial end of the inguinal canal. The deep inguinal ring lies above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament, and the femoral and obturator canals are separate structures.'),
('c4565f81-c6e9-57e2-809e-6d1b9e4093b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The angle of the pubic arch (subpubic angle) in males is:', '["120 degrees","100 degrees","Greater than 80 degrees","90 degrees","Less than 70 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'Less than 70 degrees', 'The male subpubic angle is acute, measuring less than 70 degrees (typically about 50-60 degrees), whereas the female subpubic angle is wider, often exceeding 80 degrees. Therefore 90, 100, and 120 degree values are outside the male range.'),
('0009d398-807c-51f3-b0a1-eb6150f8acaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The obturator foramen in males is:', '["None","Triangular","Oval (larger)","Round","Small"]'::jsonb, 'Oval (larger)', 'In males the obturator foramen is relatively large and oval in shape, whereas in females it is smaller and tends to be triangular. Triangular and small descriptions belong to the female pelvis.'),
('4d7ebc99-7942-5a58-9bf2-5d7f8c2df02b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The greater sciatic notch in the male is:', '["Narrower and deeper","None","The same as female","Wider and shallower","Smaller"]'::jsonb, 'Narrower and deeper', 'The male greater sciatic notch is narrower and deeper than in the female, whose notch is wider and shallower to accommodate the birth canal. Thus wider and shallower describes the female pelvis rather than the male.'),
('fed4cd41-2de4-5bd3-a0a1-e99593a78adb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The acetabulum faces:', '["Posteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laterally anteriorly and inferiorly","Medially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superiorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally anteriorly and inferiorly', 'The acetabulum is the cup-shaped socket of the hip bone that faces laterally, anteriorly, and inferiorly to receive the head of the femur. It does not face medially, posteriorly, or superiorly.'),
('95697439-ecb9-50f2-ad27-bed62d73b427'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lunate surface of the acetabulum is:', '["The non-articular area resulting in decreased functional activity","The articular surface (crescent-shaped)","The transverse ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The labrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The articular surface (crescent-shaped)', 'The lunate surface is the crescent-shaped articular part of the acetabulum that articulates with the femoral head. The central acetabular fossa within the lunate surface is the non-articular area, and the transverse ligament and labrum are separate structures.'),
('1432b68a-88d8-5489-a7d8-e7fb3fdc0d31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The acetabular notch is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A gap in the inferior margin of the lunate surface","The non-articular fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The labral attachment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A gap in the inferior margin of the lunate surface', 'The acetabular notch is the inferior gap in the lunate surface that is bridged by the transverse acetabular ligament and transmits the ligamentum teres. The non-articular central region is the acetabular fossa, not the notch.'),
('6224f09f-3f26-5127-bcf6-cb660d6adf62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral head has a small depression called:', '["Fovea capitis femoris","Lunate surface","None","Acetabular fossa","Intertrochanteric line"]'::jsonb, 'Fovea capitis femoris', 'The center of the femoral head bears a small depression, the fovea capitis femoris, which gives attachment to the ligament of the head of femur. The lunate surface and acetabular fossa belong to the acetabulum, and the intertrochanteric line lies on the femoral neck.'),
('58c5d077-13a0-5cbb-80a0-5ba82df1ebd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral neck is about how long in adults?', '["2 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","3 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Approximately 5 cm","10 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","7 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 5 cm', 'The adult femoral neck measures about 5 cm in length, joining the femoral head to the shaft at an angle. Values of 2-3 cm are too short and 7-10 cm are too long for the adult neck.'),
('3e3509f5-ec29-5598-be74-738a96651045'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The angle of inclination of the femoral neck is:', '["Approximately 125 degrees (125-135 degrees)","90 degrees accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","150 degrees mediated by specific receptor activation","145 degrees without affecting the primary tissue pathway","100 degrees leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 125 degrees (125-135 degrees)', 'The angle of inclination between the femoral neck and shaft averages about 125 degrees in the adult; larger angles produce coxa valga and smaller ones coxa vara. Values near 90, 100, 145, and 150 degrees fall outside the normal adult range.'),
('0dbb0775-7d03-5c7b-b846-3b418222823c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Coxa valga is:', '["Increased anteversion","Decreased angle (<120 degrees)","Increased angle of inclination (>135 degrees)","Normal angle","Increased retroversion"]'::jsonb, 'Increased angle of inclination (>135 degrees)', 'In coxa valga the angle of inclination of the femoral neck exceeds 135 degrees, increasing the valgus alignment of the hip. A reduced angle below 120 degrees defines coxa vara, whereas anteversion/retroversion describe rotation of the neck in the transverse plane, not its inclination.'),
('07c71435-9900-57e7-a872-afff8c54244f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Coxa vara is:', '["Increased angle resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased anteversion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal angle mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased angle of inclination (<120 degrees)"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased angle of inclination (<120 degrees)', 'Coxa vara describes a decreased angle of inclination of the femoral neck, typically below 120 degrees, which alters hip biomechanics and load on the femoral neck. Coxa valga is the opposite deformity with an increased angle, so the remaining options do not apply.'),
('85253590-575e-5571-be31-926eeb22428b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The angle of femoral torsion (anteversion) is:', '["30 degrees posteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","0 degrees accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","45 degrees without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Approximately 15-20 degrees anteriorly (anteversion)"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 15-20 degrees anteriorly (anteversion)', 'Femoral torsion (anteversion) is normally about 15-20 degrees of forward angulation between the femoral neck and the transverse axis of the knee. Posteriorly directed (retroverted) angles, zero torsion, and 45 degrees are outside the normal range.'),
('5aee4f0e-eaae-5a7a-8855-7818406488f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Excessive anteversion of the femoral neck leads to:', '["effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Toeing-in (internal rotation gait)","Toeing-out mediated by specific receptor activation","Knee pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hip dislocation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Toeing-in (internal rotation gait)', 'Excessive anteversion rotates the femoral head and neck forward, forcing the lower limb into internal rotation during gait and producing the classic toeing-in appearance. Toeing-out reflects retroversion/posterior torsion, not excessive anteversion.'),
('868f4096-906c-5934-b531-2f97784e07ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The intertrochanteric line connects:', '["The greater and lesser trochanters posteriorly","The head and neck","None","The greater and lesser trochanters anteriorly","The shaft and condyles"]'::jsonb, 'The greater and lesser trochanters anteriorly', 'The intertrochanteric line runs obliquely across the anterior surface of the femur connecting the greater and lesser trochanters. The intertrochanteric crest is the posterior ridge between the same trochanters, so the anterior line is the correct answer.'),
('5ee83cd9-2308-5478-be60-a13ec00afa06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The intertrochanteric crest connects:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater and lesser trochanters anteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater and lesser trochanters posteriorly","The head and shaft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The condyles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater and lesser trochanters posteriorly', 'The intertrochanteric crest is the posterior ridge connecting the greater and lesser trochanters and bears the quadrate tubercle. The intertrochanteric line is its anterior counterpart, and the head/condyles are not connected by this crest.'),
('60a98674-1724-507c-85fd-c54383d00e71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The quadrate tubercle is located:', '["On the greater trochanter","On the lesser trochanter","On the intertrochanteric crest","On the femoral neck","On the intertrochanteric line"]'::jsonb, 'On the intertrochanteric crest', 'The quadrate tubercle is a small prominence near the middle of the intertrochanteric crest and gives partial attachment to quadratus femoris. It is not located on either trochanter, the femoral neck, or the intertrochanteric line.'),
('d15c97d2-441b-5c52-85bb-95ddc71b5d49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteal tuberosity is:', '["A roughened vertical ridge on the posterior upper femoral shaft","On the lesser trochanter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","On the greater trochanter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the intertrochanteric line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A roughened vertical ridge on the posterior upper femoral shaft', 'The gluteal tuberosity is a roughened vertical ridge on the upper posterior femoral shaft, continuous below with the linea aspera, and is the main femoral attachment of gluteus maximus. The trochanters and intertrochanteric line do not carry this tuberosity.'),
('05776b6f-a927-5eae-800d-05ae435baa21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The pectineal line (spiral line) of the femur is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The gluteal tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ridge extending from the lesser trochanter to the linea aspera","The intertrochanteric line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The quadrate tubercle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ridge extending from the lesser trochanter to the linea aspera', 'The pectineal (spiral) line is the ridge continuing from the lesser trochanter to the upper end of the linea aspera and gives origin to pectineus. The gluteal tuberosity, intertrochanteric line, and quadrate tubercle are separate landmarks.'),
('3f2ddd1e-ca54-54af-bfae-a4e27c74c066'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The linea aspera is:', '["A longitudinal ridge on the posterior femoral shaft with medial and lateral lips","The distal end accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medial surface mediated by specific receptor activation","The lateral surface without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The anterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A longitudinal ridge on the posterior femoral shaft with medial and lateral lips', 'The linea aspera is the longitudinal ridge on the posterior femoral shaft formed by medial and lateral lips, giving attachment to several adductor muscles. The listed surfaces and the distal end are not the location of this ridge.'),
('8fee165a-43fa-5b4c-94d0-9aa6f9da4cf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The linea aspera gives attachment to all except:', '["Adductor magnus","Gluteus maximus","Vastus medialis","Adductor longus","Adductor brevis"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus', 'The linea aspera gives attachment to adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, and vastus medialis. Gluteus maximus does not attach here because it inserts mainly on the gluteal tuberosity and the iliotibial tract.'),
('615f6248-f647-5b53-bf22-19ff159d62d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The medial supracondylar ridge of the femur is continuous with:', '["None","The medial lip of the linea aspera above","The gluteal tuberosity","The lateral lip","The intertrochanteric line"]'::jsonb, 'The medial lip of the linea aspera above', 'The medial supracondylar ridge is the downward continuation of the medial lip of the linea aspera, running toward the adductor tubercle of the medial condyle. It is not continuous with the gluteal tuberosity, lateral lip, or intertrochanteric line.'),
('c66b8458-470d-59e5-8e28-7558aeb69707'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lateral supracondylar ridge of the femur is continuous with:', '["The gluteal tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","The lateral lip of the linea aspera above","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The medial lip without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The intertrochanteric line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral lip of the linea aspera above', 'The lateral supracondylar ridge runs distally as the continuation of the lateral lip of the linea aspera toward the lateral condyle. The gluteal tuberosity lies above, and the medial lip continues as the medial supracondylar ridge instead.'),
('4a5f0325-1f9c-5fb2-b6ba-6e4ba5582bc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor tubercle is located:', '["On the lesser trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","On the lateral condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the medial femoral condyle (superior to medial epicondyle)","On the linea aspera without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the greater trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the medial femoral condyle (superior to medial epicondyle)', 'The adductor tubercle is a small prominence above the medial epicondyle on the medial femoral condyle and anchors the tendon of adductor magnus. None of the other listed sites receive this tendon.'),
('f8f11ef8-9a66-524a-ba15-559628835364'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lateral femoral condyle is:', '["Less prominent anteriorly","None","Smaller than medial","The same as the medial","More prominent anteriorly than medial condyle"]'::jsonb, 'Less prominent anteriorly', 'The lateral femoral condyle is less prominent anteriorly than the medial condyle, which projects further forward and carries the patellar surface. This asymmetry contributes to the screw-home rotation of the knee during terminal extension.'),
('f01bf8e6-30d4-5105-b9d4-2835abf0c018'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The medial femoral condyle is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Smaller accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as lateral mediated by specific receptor activation","Larger and more curved than the lateral condyle","Less prominent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Larger and more curved than the lateral condyle', 'The medial femoral condyle is larger and curves through a greater arc than the lateral condyle, so it projects further anteriorly and inferiorly. This size difference produces the terminal external rotation of the tibia as the knee extends.'),
('36b8084d-7238-5c78-b1b7-903d210e4c32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The intercondylar notch (fossa) is located:', '["Between the condyles anteriorly","Between the femoral condyles posteriorly","On the tibial plateau","None","On the patellar surface"]'::jsonb, 'Between the femoral condyles posteriorly', 'The intercondylar notch (fossa) lies posteriorly between the femoral condyles and transmits the anterior and posterior cruciate ligaments. Anteriorly this region forms the patellar (trochlear) surface, so the posterior location is correct.'),
('414ff2f1-0156-5b58-99ea-97965cc09221'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The patellar surface of the femur is:', '["The intercondylar notch resulting in decreased functional activity","The medial condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior surface mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior articular surface between the condyles for the patella","The lateral condyle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior articular surface between the condyles for the patella', 'The patellar (trochlear) surface is the anterior articular area between the condyles over which the patella glides. The intercondylar notch and condyles occupy different regions and do not articulate with the patella.'),
('a3d0ed3e-190b-5bc8-9e73-4659020f1ba8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The patella is the largest sesamoid bone in the body and develops in the tendon of:', '["Semimembranosus","Biceps femoris","Sartorius","Quadriceps femoris tendon","Patellar tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps femoris tendon', 'The patella, the largest sesamoid bone, develops within the quadriceps femoris tendon. It does not develop in the hamstring tendons, and the patellar ligament is its distal continuation rather than its site of origin.'),
('d63166d1-6a4b-5d34-9612-d007c518ab0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The patella has how many facets on its articular surface?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","7 (3 medial 3 lateral 1 odd)","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '7 (3 medial 3 lateral 1 odd)', 'The articular surface of the patella carries seven facets: three medial, three lateral, and one odd facet along the medial border that engages the femur in deep flexion. Two, three, four, or five facets do not match this count.'),
('eacb80d4-9495-527d-bb3d-2dea235077bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The patella articulates with:', '["The femoral condyles posteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","The patellar surface of the femur (trochlea)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tibial plateau leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The patellar surface of the femur (trochlea)', 'The patella articulates only with the patellar (trochlear) surface of the femur during flexion and extension. It does not touch the tibial plateau, and the posterior femoral condyles articulate with the tibia rather than the patella.'),
('58ac1927-7dbe-5f07-bf57-1d8a4072fc01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The function of the patella is to:', '["Protect the knee joint","Reduce friction","Increase the lever arm of the quadriceps mechanism by elevating the patellar tendon","None","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The patella protects the knee joint, reduces friction on the quadriceps tendon, and acts as a fulcrum that raises the patellar tendon away from the joint axis to increase the lever arm of the quadriceps. All three statements are correct.'),
('fe08fbbf-8239-5203-9456-d177936d3e4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Bipartite patella is:', '["A small patella resulting in decreased functional activity","A fractured patella accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A dislocated patella mediated by specific receptor activation","A developmental anomaly where the patella has two ossification centers that fail to fuse","An absent patella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A developmental anomaly where the patella has two ossification centers that fail to fuse', 'Bipartite patella is a developmental variation in which two ossification centers fail to fuse, leaving an accessory fragment usually at the upper outer quadrant. It is not a fracture, dislocation, small, or absent patella.'),
('8cab565b-2051-5648-98eb-23878b00bb04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The patellar ligament (tendon) extends from:', '["Patella to fibula","Patella to tibial plateau","Patella to tibial tuberosity","Patella to quadriceps","Patella to femur"]'::jsonb, 'Patella to tibial tuberosity', 'The patellar ligament extends from the apex and margins of the patella to the tibial tuberosity, completing the quadriceps insertion. It does not attach to the fibula, tibial plateau, or femur.'),
('73195f8f-3563-52ab-979c-420757b22a6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The quadriceps femoris tendon attaches to:', '["Tibial plateau resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base and sides of the patella (then to tibial tuberosity via patellar ligament)","Femoral condyles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibial tuberosity directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base and sides of the patella (then to tibial tuberosity via patellar ligament)', 'The quadriceps femoris tendon inserts on the base and side borders of the patella, and its pull is transmitted to the tibial tuberosity through the patellar ligament. It does not attach directly to the tuberosity or to the femoral condyles.'),
('5389c473-4996-5234-b59f-1cf8f66b96c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The deep infrapatellar bursa lies:', '["None","Between the patella and skin","Between the quadriceps tendon and femur","Between the patellar ligament and the tibia","In the popliteal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Between the patellar ligament and the tibia', 'The deep infrapatellar bursa lies between the patellar ligament and the anterior surface of the tibia, cushioning the ligament against the bone. The prepatellar, suprapatellar, and popliteal bursae occupy other locations.'),
('b464bac4-9b12-5a25-87e2-a00b71fd6158'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The prepatellar bursa lies:', '["In the popliteal fossa","Between the patellar ligament and tibia","None","Behind the quadriceps tendon","Between the patella and the skin"]'::jsonb, 'Between the patella and the skin', 'The prepatellar bursa is subcutaneous, lying between the patella and the overlying skin and allowing the skin to move freely. The deep infrapatellar and suprapatellar bursae lie at different depths around the knee.'),
('7db10ef2-9ae1-5ebc-a930-fd9229b25f95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The suprapatellar bursa is:', '["An extension of the knee joint cavity above the patella (between quadriceps and femur)","In the popliteal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Below the patella mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind the patella without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An extension of the knee joint cavity above the patella (between quadriceps and femur)', 'The suprapatellar bursa is the superior extension of the knee joint cavity lying deep to the quadriceps tendon above the patella, and it usually communicates freely with the joint. It does not lie below or behind the patella or in the popliteal fossa.'),
('16999da0-e379-57c0-8e33-963f0ba84f66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The knee joint is what type of joint?', '["Ball and socket resulting in decreased functional activity","Bicondylar (hinge with rotation) complex synovial joint","Pivot mediated by specific receptor activation","Plane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Saddle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bicondylar (hinge with rotation) complex synovial joint', 'The knee is a complex synovial bicondylar joint that acts mainly as a hinge but also permits rotation, especially during locking and unlocking. Ball-and-socket, pivot, plane, and saddle classifications do not apply.'),
('75914d3b-ebb0-5b44-9514-979cdc15d36c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The medial meniscus is:', '["Discoid resulting in decreased functional activity","C-shaped (almost semicircular)","Triangular mediated by specific receptor activation","O-shaped without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Circular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C-shaped (almost semicircular)', 'The medial meniscus is almost semicircular (C-shaped) with a broad posterior horn and is firmly attached to the tibial collateral ligament, which limits its mobility. The lateral meniscus is the more circular O-shaped one.'),
('9af67301-fc85-5270-af25-d09035e8f256'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lateral meniscus is:', '["C-shaped","Discoid","More circular (O-shaped)","Semicircular","Triangular"]'::jsonb, 'More circular (O-shaped)', 'The lateral meniscus is nearly circular (O-shaped) and is not attached to the fibular collateral ligament, allowing it greater mobility. The medial meniscus is the C-shaped or semicircular meniscus.'),
('92812eda-c225-5fa6-9c03-753cc4231712'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) attaches:', '["From posterior intercondylar area to medial condyle resulting in decreased functional activity","From anterior intercondylar area to medial condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From anterior intercondylar area of tibia to medial aspect of lateral femoral condyle"]'::jsonb, 'From anterior intercondylar area of tibia to medial aspect of lateral femoral condyle', 'The ACL passes from the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia to the medial aspect of the lateral femoral condyle and resists anterior translation of the tibia. Attachments to the medial condyle or posterior area describe the PCL or other ligament.'),
('bef9032d-edb2-5945-b9d5-1bb9674da179'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) attaches:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","From anterior intercondylar area to lateral condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From posterior intercondylar area to lateral condyle mediated by specific receptor activation","From posterior intercondylar area of tibia to lateral aspect of medial femoral condyle","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From posterior intercondylar area of tibia to lateral aspect of medial femoral condyle', 'The PCL arises from the posterior intercondylar area of the tibia and attaches to the lateral aspect of the medial femoral condyle, preventing posterior translation of the tibia. This attachment is the reverse of the ACL.'),
('ffefd96d-0a24-53d0-bf86-7ccc7985d91b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ACL prevents:', '["Lateral translation resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur","Rotation mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial translation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior translation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur', 'The ACL is the primary restraint against anterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur. It does not mainly prevent rotation, medial/lateral shift, or posterior translation, which is resisted by the PCL.'),
('9910ea3b-e4a4-5ed6-8575-2d0ca550c600'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The PCL prevents:', '["Rotation resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior translation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial translation mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral translation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur', 'The PCL prevents posterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur and becomes taut during hyperflexion. Anterior translation, rotation, and medial/lateral shifts are not its primary function.'),
('62aa00e9-a330-580a-8e10-1a1dcfee604d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tibial collateral ligament (medial collateral ligament) attaches:', '["From lateral epicondyle to fibula resulting in decreased functional activity","From lateral epicondyle to tibia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","From medial epicondyle to fibula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From medial femoral epicondyle to medial tibial condyle (and medial meniscus)"]'::jsonb, 'From medial femoral epicondyle to medial tibial condyle (and medial meniscus)', 'The tibial (medial) collateral ligament runs from the medial femoral epicondyle to the medial tibial condyle and blends with the medial meniscus. It does not attach to the fibula or to the lateral epicondyle.'),
('da479cb0-0b9e-5f0b-b087-7f724f9dc349'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The fibular collateral ligament (lateral collateral ligament) attaches:', '["From medial epicondyle to fibula resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From medial epicondyle to tibia mediated by specific receptor activation","From lateral femoral epicondyle to the head of the fibula","From lateral epicondyle to tibia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From lateral femoral epicondyle to the head of the fibula', 'The fibular (lateral) collateral ligament passes from the lateral femoral epicondyle to the head of the fibula, separated from the lateral meniscus by the popliteus tendon. It does not attach to the tibia or medial epicondyle.'),
('cebea3ce-16d5-5746-85c2-92734994bffc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The MCL is more commonly injured than the LCL because:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The MCL is weaker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The knee is more vulnerable to valgus stress (blow on lateral side)","The LCL is stronger without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Varus stress is more common leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The knee is more vulnerable to valgus stress (blow on lateral side)', 'The MCL is injured more often than the LCL because the knee is especially vulnerable to valgus stress, produced by a blow on the lateral side of the leg. Blows on the medial side causing varus stress on the LCL are much less frequent.'),
('9e64d8bd-3513-5a09-a67e-a2db71ace352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The unhappy triad (O''Donoghue''s triad) involves:', '["ACL LCL and lateral meniscus","PCL MCL and lateral meniscus","None","ACL MCL and medial meniscus","PCL LCL and medial meniscus"]'::jsonb, 'ACL MCL and medial meniscus', 'The unhappy triad (O''Donoghue''s) is the classic combination of torn ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus, typically from a lateral blow to the knee with the foot planted. Lateral-sided injuries do not form this triad.'),
('81c87b83-8d51-547a-a928-6f2a632830cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The oblique popliteal ligament is an expansion of:', '["Biceps femoris tendon","Popliteus tendon","Quadriceps tendon","Semimembranosus tendon","Semitendinosus tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Semimembranosus tendon', 'The oblique popliteal ligament is a recurrent expansion of the semimembranosus tendon that ascends across the back of the knee capsule. The popliteus, biceps, semitendinosus, and quadriceps tendons do not give origin to this ligament.'),
('a71b9410-0c2c-52f9-8cf9-b649778867a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The arcuate popliteal ligament is aY-shaped thickening of the posterior capsule that attaches:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","From fibular head to the lateral femoral condyle","All mediated by specific receptor activation","From medial condyle to fibula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From tibia to femur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From fibular head to the lateral femoral condyle', 'The arcuate popliteal ligament is aY-shaped thickening of the posterior capsule arising from the head of the fibula and ascending to the lateral femoral condyle, arching over the popliteus tendon. It does not attach to the medial condyle or tibia.'),
('fbeb862e-6f1b-58cf-a5fc-ae2bf52157bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteus muscle originates from:', '["Patella resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral femoral condyle (anterior to LCL insertion)","Tibial plateau mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibular head without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial femoral condyle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral femoral condyle (anterior to LCL insertion)', 'The popliteus originates from the lateral femoral condyle just anterior to the insertion of the fibular collateral ligament. It inserts on the posterior tibia above the soleal line and does not arise from the patella, tibia, or fibular head.'),
('e62cd966-d9d5-556b-bab9-092dfc00f035'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteus muscle inserts into:', '["Fibula resulting in decreased functional activity","Patella accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femur mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior tibia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior surface of the tibia (above the soleal line)"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of the tibia (above the soleal line)', 'The popliteus inserts on the posterior surface of the tibia just above the soleal line. It originates from the lateral femoral condyle, so the fibula, patella, femur, and anterior tibia are all incorrect insertion sites.'),
('b9bcd29f-831a-51dd-9e81-c5e98ab8dfd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteus muscle acts to:', '["Extend the knee resulting in decreased functional activity","Medially rotate the tibia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lock the knee mediated by specific receptor activation","Unlock the knee (laterally rotate the femur on the tibia to initiate flexion)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unlock the knee (laterally rotate the femur on the tibia to initiate flexion)', 'The popliteus unlocks the extended knee by rotating the femur laterally on the tibia (equivalent to medial rotation of the tibia), allowing flexion to begin. It does not lock or extend the knee.'),
('1d1f852e-cdb4-596c-9efb-f2fcc24bbc4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The screw-home mechanism of the knee is:', '["The terminal rotation of the knee where the tibia externally rotates on the femur to lock in extension","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The initial rotation in flexion mediated by specific receptor activation","The action of the hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The action of the quadriceps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal rotation of the knee where the tibia externally rotates on the femur to lock in extension', 'The screw-home mechanism is the terminal external rotation of the tibia on the femur that occurs as the knee reaches full extension, tightening the cruciates and collateral ligaments to lock the joint. Flexion reverses this rotation to unlock it.'),
('696d4fa7-abc9-5068-aaf8-b78f5aa0662c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The locking of the knee in extension is due to:', '["The menisci locking resulting in decreased functional activity","The patella locking accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The popliteus contracting mediated by specific receptor activation","The larger medial femoral condyle moving forward to tighten the ACL and PCL","The quadriceps relaxing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The larger medial femoral condyle moving forward to tighten the ACL and PCL', 'Locking in extension occurs because the larger medial femoral condyle rotates forward on the tibia, tightening the ACL, PCL, and collateral ligaments. Popliteus contraction, meniscal locking, or patellar engagement do not produce this close-packed position.'),
('bab67c68-679c-5248-a215-89a4d80343bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The knee joint is most stable in:', '["Full extension (close-packed position)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","30 degrees flexion mediated by specific receptor activation","90 degrees flexion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Full flexion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Full extension (close-packed position)', 'The knee is most stable in full extension, its close-packed position, where the cruciate and collateral ligaments are taut and the joint surfaces are in maximal contact. Increasing flexion loosens the joint and allows rotation.'),
('7f32e540-2809-584d-8e0b-298376e2ae67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The menisci of the knee function to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Deepen the tibial plateau absorb shock and improve joint congruity","Stabilize the patella mediated by specific receptor activation","Produce synovial fluid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deepen the tibial plateau absorb shock and improve joint congruity', 'The menisci deepen the tibial plateau, absorb shock, and improve congruity between the curved femoral condyles and the flat tibial plateau. They do not stabilize the patella and do not produce synovial fluid.'),
('d497aa67-6908-5a88-8735-789c4ffdf2b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The medial meniscus is attached to:', '["The tibial collateral ligament (making it less mobile)","The patellar ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The fibular collateral ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The popliteus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tibial collateral ligament (making it less mobile)', 'The medial meniscus is firmly attached to the tibial collateral ligament, making it relatively immobile and prone to tearing under valgus stress. The lateral meniscus is not attached to the LCL and instead is tethered by the popliteus.'),
('45a090ea-a75c-5c99-ad44-2fd314858e9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lateral meniscus is not attached to the LCL but is attached to:', '["The MCL resulting in decreased functional activity","The popliteus muscle (popliteus tendon separates it from LCL)","The PCL mediated by specific receptor activation","The ACL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The popliteus muscle (popliteus tendon separates it from LCL)', 'The lateral meniscus is separated from the fibular collateral ligament by the popliteus tendon, which also attaches to its posterior horn. This leaves it more mobile than the medial meniscus, which is fixed by the MCL.'),
('e6ff71bc-e5ef-5a1b-8b0e-d8d73dd96574'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'A discoid lateral meniscus is:', '["A tear of the lateral meniscus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgical removal of the meniscus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital anomaly where the lateral meniscus is disc-shaped (no central opening)"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital anomaly where the lateral meniscus is disc-shaped (no central opening)', 'A discoid meniscus is a congenital variant in which the lateral meniscus retains a thick, disc-like shape instead of the normal C-shape, so it has no central opening. It is not a tear, a surgical procedure, or a generalized claim, and a discrete anomaly rather than ''None'' or ''All'' is correct.'),
('0e8b9f53-985b-5edf-b75d-d876ea4b8d52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The geniculate anastomosis of the knee is formed by:', '["Only popliteal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only tibial artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only femoral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Peroneal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Branches of femoral popliteal and anterior tibial arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of femoral popliteal and anterior tibial arteries', 'The geniculate anastomosis is a collateral network around the knee formed by branches of the femoral, popliteal, and anterior tibial arteries. A single artery alone cannot create the anastomotic circle, and the peroneal artery contributes little to it.'),
('dfb2a4ab-3862-578d-94f4-581ee763dc6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal artery is the continuation of:', '["The femoral artery (at the adductor hiatus)","The external iliac artery","The profunda femoris","The posterior tibial artery","The anterior tibial artery"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral artery (at the adductor hiatus)', 'The femoral artery becomes the popliteal artery as it passes through the adductor hiatus. The external iliac, profunda femoris, and tibial arteries are separate vessels and do not directly continue into the popliteal artery.'),
('919ccfd8-84dc-57e6-83e9-a35238294e23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal artery divides at the lower border of popliteus into:', '["Medial and lateral genicular","Anterior and posterior tibial arteries","Peroneal and tibial","Femoral and profunda","None"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior tibial arteries', 'At the lower border of popliteus the popliteal artery terminates by dividing into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries. Genicular and peroneal branches arise from the popliteal artery itself and are not its terminal divisions.'),
('e1b5ef7d-2170-5ac6-b8c4-d178ff519ed1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal vein is formed by:', '["The small saphenous vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Venae comitantes of anterior and posterior tibial veins joining at the knee","The great saphenous vein mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The femoral vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Venae comitantes of anterior and posterior tibial veins joining at the knee', 'The venae comitantes of the anterior and posterior tibial veins join at the lower border of popliteus to form the popliteal vein. The saphenous veins and the femoral vein are separate veins that join the system elsewhere rather than forming the popliteal vein.'),
('6c6da3a6-b60c-5086-b26c-aec20c57645e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The small saphenous vein drains into the:', '["Great saphenous vein","Anterior tibial vein","Posterior tibial vein","Femoral vein","Popliteal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal vein', 'The small saphenous vein ascends behind the knee and pierces the popliteal fascia to drain into the popliteal vein. It does not drain into the saphenous, tibial, or femoral veins directly.'),
('a1981926-42b4-5fcd-97a5-b3bea03d4f4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The great saphenous vein drains into the:', '["Posterior tibial vein","External iliac vein","Popliteal vein","Anterior tibial vein","Femoral vein (at the saphenous opening)"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral vein (at the saphenous opening)', 'The great saphenous vein ascends through the saphenous opening in the fascia lata to drain into the femoral vein. The tibial and popliteal veins lie in the deep posterior system and are not the termination of the great saphenous vein.'),
('c721f342-a84c-526b-933f-8dcb0706887f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal fossa is bounded superomedially by:', '["Medial head of gastrocnemius","Biceps femoris","Lateral head of gastrocnemius","None","Semitendinosus and semimembranosus"]'::jsonb, 'Semitendinosus and semimembranosus', 'The superomedial boundary of the popliteal fossa is formed by the semitendinosus and semimembranosus. Biceps femoris is the superolateral boundary, and the gastrocnemius heads form the inferior boundaries.'),
('3134590e-c004-5509-8bc8-36de649d79bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal fossa is bounded superolaterally by:', '["Semimembranosus","Biceps femoris","Lateral head of gastrocnemius","Semitendinosus","Medial head of gastrocnemius"]'::jsonb, 'Biceps femoris', 'Biceps femoris forms the superolateral boundary of the popliteal fossa. The semimembranosus and semitendinosus bound the fossa superomedially, and the gastrocnemius heads form its inferior boundaries.'),
('a0d3d7bb-be5d-5f3a-815f-7b74f8b0d258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal fossa is bounded inferomedially by:', '["Lateral head of gastrocnemius","Biceps femoris","Semitendinosus","Medial head of gastrocnemius","Semimembranosus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial head of gastrocnemius', 'The medial head of gastrocnemius forms the inferomedial boundary of the popliteal fossa. The lateral head relates to the inferolateral boundary, while biceps, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus form the superior boundaries.'),
('2cd0c302-bd15-52df-ad3f-474761013cdf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal fossa is bounded inferolaterally by:', '["Soleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral head of gastrocnemius and plantaris","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial head of gastrocnemius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Biceps femoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral head of gastrocnemius and plantaris', 'The inferolateral boundary of the popliteal fossa is formed by the lateral head of gastrocnemius and the plantaris tendon. Soleus is not a boundary of the fossa, and the medial head and biceps occupy other margins.'),
('af2e554c-1730-5c87-a203-39f49f75c47b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The floor of the popliteal fossa is formed by:', '["Femur only resulting in decreased functional activity","Popliteus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Popliteal surface of femur posterior capsule of knee and popliteus muscle","Capsule only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal surface of femur posterior capsule of knee and popliteus muscle', 'The floor of the popliteal fossa is formed by the popliteal surface of the femur, the posterior capsule of the knee, and the popliteus muscle and fascia. No single structure alone accounts for the whole floor.'),
('3fa08975-9c7c-5e2c-8ac4-e6313009e12f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The roof of the popliteal fossa is formed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Popliteal fascia (deep fascia of the posterior knee)","Skin only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal fascia (deep fascia of the posterior knee)', 'The roof of the popliteal fossa is the popliteal fascia, the deep fascia of the back of the knee that connects the leg and thigh fascia. Skin and superficial fascia lie superficial to it rather than forming the true fascial roof.'),
('9eabfda1-3836-5e02-a957-d9cca324aa91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The contents of the popliteal fossa from superficial to deep include:', '["Nerve artery vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Vein artery nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Artery nerve vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Popliteal artery vein and nerve deep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Popliteal vein popliteal artery tibial nerve (medial to lateral)"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal vein popliteal artery tibial nerve (medial to lateral)', 'The neurovascular contents run with the tibial nerve most superficial (posterior), the popliteal vein intermediate, and the popliteal artery deepest; at the level of the joint they are arranged with the vein medial and the artery lateral to it. The tibial nerve is the most superficial of the three, not the deepest.'),
('19843221-1a25-5d85-a795-0dc2c79e2833'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tibial nerve in the popliteal fossa lies:', '["Between the artery and vein","Lateral to the vessels","Medial to the vessels","Superficial (posterior) to the popliteal vessels","Deep to the popliteal vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial (posterior) to the popliteal vessels', 'In the popliteal fossa the tibial nerve is the most superficial, lying posterior to the popliteal vein and artery. It does not lie between, lateral, medial, or deep to the vessels, which have a consistent relationship with the nerve on top.'),
('2080bb45-c7de-5960-ab70-6dff7fe7f4a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The common peroneal (fibular) nerve runs along:', '["None","The lateral border of biceps femoris","The medial border of biceps femoris tendon","The midline of popliteal fossa","The semitendinosus muscle"]'::jsonb, 'The medial border of biceps femoris tendon', 'The common peroneal nerve leaves the popliteal fossa by passing along the medial border of the tendon of biceps femoris before winding around the neck of the fibula. It does not follow the lateral border of biceps, the midline, or semitendinosus.'),
('6fa6c6a0-76b2-56b1-b036-2e635538c7ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The common peroneal nerve winds around the:', '["Head of the fibula","Neck of the fibula","Lateral femoral condyle","Tibial tuberosity","Medial femoral condyle"]'::jsonb, 'Neck of the fibula', 'After running along the biceps tendon, the common peroneal nerve winds laterally around the neck of the fibula, where it is vulnerable to injury. It relates to the fibular neck, not the fibular head, the femoral condyles, or the tibial tuberosity.'),
('52e888f4-4951-5591-b28f-54a0f5da8391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The common peroneal nerve divides into:', '["Medial and lateral plantar resulting in decreased functional activity","Tibial and sural accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial and deep peroneal (fibular) nerves","Sural and saphenous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep peroneal (fibular) nerves', 'In the lateral compartment of the leg the common peroneal nerve divides into the superficial and deep peroneal (fibular) nerves. Medial and lateral plantar nerves are branches of the tibial nerve in the foot, while the sural and saphenous nerves are cutaneous branches.'),
('95228645-5d89-5d7a-aec4-4408f86db1b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The superficial peroneal nerve supplies:', '["Extensor digitorum longus resulting in decreased functional activity","Peroneus longus and brevis (lateral compartment muscles)","Extensor hallucis longus mediated by specific receptor activation","Peroneus tertius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibialis anterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peroneus longus and brevis (lateral compartment muscles)', 'The superficial peroneal (fibular) nerve innervates the lateral compartment muscles, namely peroneus longus and brevis. The muscles of the anterior compartment, including tibialis anterior, EHL, EDL, and peroneus tertius, are supplied by the deep peroneal nerve.'),
('8c6b6454-969a-55f8-902c-d4f8d438a5e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The deep peroneal nerve supplies:', '["Posterior compartment muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intrinsic foot muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior compartment muscles (tibialis anterior EHL EDL peroneus tertius)","Peroneus longus and brevis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior compartment muscles (tibialis anterior EHL EDL peroneus tertius)', 'The deep peroneal (fibular) nerve innervates the anterior compartment muscles: tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, and peroneus tertius. Posterior compartment muscles and the lateral compartment muscles are supplied by other nerves.'),
('d375700d-84ea-5a32-9143-b89875b3a1e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sural nerve is formed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial sural (tibial) and lateral sural (common peroneal) branches","Tibial and femoral mediated by specific receptor activation","Saphenous and peroneal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obturator and sciatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial sural (tibial) and lateral sural (common peroneal) branches', 'The sural nerve forms from the union of the medial sural cutaneous branch (from the tibial nerve) and the lateral sural cutaneous branch or its communicating branch (from the common peroneal nerve). It does not involve the femoral, obturator, or saphenous nerves.'),
('764d9f27-2ab8-5bf1-b724-6c2501a8c71d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The saphenous nerve is the largest cutaneous branch of:', '["The femoral nerve","The sciatic nerve","The tibial nerve","The common peroneal nerve","The obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral nerve', 'The saphenous nerve is the largest and longest cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve, supplying skin on the medial side of the lower leg. It is not a branch of the sciatic, tibial, common peroneal, or obturator nerves.'),
('930a4ce8-9513-5d0c-ad29-b9813cc8ddda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The saphenous nerve accompanies the:', '["Small saphenous vein","Femoral artery","Popliteal artery","None","Great saphenous vein"]'::jsonb, 'Great saphenous vein', 'In the leg the saphenous nerve runs with the great saphenous vein along the medial side of the limb. The small saphenous vein and the femoral and popliteal arteries are not its companions.'),
('0b0957ac-82b8-5d82-804f-e26042f664f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral triangle (Scarpa''s triangle) is bounded superiorly by:', '["Pectineus","Iliopsoas","Adductor longus","Inguinal ligament","Sartorius"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal ligament', 'The superior boundary of the femoral triangle is the inguinal ligament. Sartorius forms the lateral boundary and adductor longus the medial boundary, while pectineus and iliopsoas contribute to the floor.'),
('ae8dda00-3751-5c9d-9c40-96f139e41a06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral triangle is bounded laterally by:', '["Pectineus","Sartorius","Gracilis","Inguinal ligament","Adductor longus"]'::jsonb, 'Sartorius', 'The lateral boundary of the femoral triangle is the medial border of sartorius. The inguinal ligament bounds it superiorly, adductor longus medially, and pectineus forms part of the floor.'),
('bd6ed8d5-f8de-51d5-ad52-8e322c165828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral triangle is bounded medially by:', '["Adductor longus","Inguinal ligament","Pectineus","Sartorius","Gracilis"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor longus', 'The medial boundary of the femoral triangle is the medial border of adductor longus. Sartorius bounds it laterally, the inguinal ligament superiorly, and pectineus contributes to the floor.'),
('6aa9548f-5bb0-53b5-b936-cbff659f457b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The floor of the femoral triangle is formed by:', '["None","Sartorius and rectus femoris","Adductor longus and magnus","Iliopsoas and pectineus","Vastus medialis and lateralis"]'::jsonb, 'Iliopsoas and pectineus', 'The floor of the femoral triangle is formed by iliopsoas laterally and pectineus medially, covered by fascia. Sartorius and adductor longus are boundaries of the triangle rather than its floor.'),
('09bee21e-4445-55b8-b438-013f8484805c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The roof of the femoral triangle is formed by:', '["Skin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Fascia lata (deep fascia of thigh) and cribriform fascia","Superficial fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fascia lata (deep fascia of thigh) and cribriform fascia', 'The roof of the femoral triangle is formed by the fascia lata reinforced by the cribriform fascia over the saphenous opening. Skin and superficial fascia lie external to this fascial roof, and muscle does not form the roof.'),
('b45deba9-f821-522b-bb53-2c6c10f62e21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The contents of the femoral triangle from lateral to medial are:', '["Artery nerve vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve vein artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral nerve femoral artery femoral vein and femoral canal (NAVL)","Femoral artery vein and nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral vein artery nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve femoral artery femoral vein and femoral canal (NAVL)', 'From lateral to medial the contents are the femoral nerve, femoral artery, femoral vein, and femoral canal, recalled by the mnemonic NAVL. The artery and vein lie within the femoral sheath, while the nerve lies lateral to it.'),
('17006854-ed5f-5a3a-b846-6049daeba468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral nerve supplies:', '["Gluteal muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadriceps femoris sartorius and pectineus","All thigh muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adductors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps femoris sartorius and pectineus', 'The femoral nerve innervates the anterior thigh muscles: quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and pectineus. Hamstrings are supplied by the sciatic nerve, adductors by the obturator nerve, and gluteal muscles by the superior and inferior gluteal nerves.'),
('7bec9395-4b08-5e0a-bba2-abe278da4491'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral artery is the continuation of:', '["External iliac artery (at the inguinal ligament)","Popliteal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Profunda femoris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Internal iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External iliac artery (at the inguinal ligament)', 'The external iliac artery continues as the femoral artery as it passes deep to the inguinal ligament. The popliteal artery is its distal continuation, and the profunda and internal iliac arteries are separate branches or major pelvic vessels.'),
('74590a50-0ba6-5eaf-9e2f-6bcbc5d0bc4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral vein becomes the:', '["Popliteal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal iliac vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External iliac vein at the inguinal ligament","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Profunda femoris vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External iliac vein at the inguinal ligament', 'The femoral vein becomes the external iliac vein as it passes deep to the inguinal ligament. The popliteal vein drains into the femoral vein from below, and the internal iliac vein is a separate pelvic tributary.'),
('848638ba-af7e-514e-8b5a-096d4e402a37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral canal contains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphatics and lymph nodes (Cloquet''s node)"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatics and lymph nodes (Cloquet''s node)', 'The femoral canal, the medial compartment of the femoral sheath, contains lymphatics and lymph nodes, including Cloquet''s node. The femoral artery and vein occupy the lateral and intermediate compartments, and the nerve lies outside the sheath.'),
('7464a8b5-3af3-5a7e-821b-038bbc68c4cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral ring is the opening of the femoral canal. It is bounded:', '["Laterally by the femoral vein","All of the above","Medially by the lacunar ligament","Anteriorly by the inguinal ligament","Posteriorly by the pectineus and pectineal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally by the femoral vein', 'The femoral ring is bounded laterally by the femoral vein, anteriorly by the inguinal ligament, medially by the lacunar ligament, and posteriorly by pectineus and the pectineal ligament. Since several individual boundaries are listed as discrete options, the lateral boundary by the femoral vein is the named component that correctly completes the statement.'),
('055e3ef4-6fce-516d-8edf-b4586db1be0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Femoral hernia passes through:', '["The inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sciatic foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral canal (below the inguinal ligament)","The obturator canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral canal (below the inguinal ligament)', 'A femoral hernia protrudes through the femoral ring into the femoral canal, which lies below and lateral to the inguinal ligament. Inguinal hernias use the inguinal canal, and the sciatic and obturator canals are other exits.'),
('3814331b-eb6d-5420-ba64-886973499e75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The saphenous opening (fossa ovalis) is:', '["The adductor hiatus resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal ring mediated by specific receptor activation","An opening in the fascia lata for the great saphenous vein to drain into the femoral vein","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An opening in the fascia lata for the great saphenous vein to drain into the femoral vein', 'The saphenous opening is a gap in the fascia lata, about 4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, that transmits the great saphenous vein into the femoral vein. It is distinct from the adductor hiatus, femoral ring, and inguinal ring.'),
('17e75b3f-d92a-5184-bd4c-dc9bc77956b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The cribriform fascia:', '["Is part of the inguinal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Is the femoral sheath accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Covers the saphenous opening (perforated by lymphatics and blood vessels)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forms the floor of the femoral triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the saphenous opening (perforated by lymphatics and blood vessels)', 'The cribriform fascia is the perforated part of the deep fascia that covers the saphenous opening and transmits the great saphenous vein, lymphatics, and small blood vessels. It is not part of the inguinal ligament, the femoral sheath, or the triangle floor.'),
('67a4aefa-5f83-5144-9847-5d47edc50c72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral sheath is formed by:', '["Continuation of the transversalis fascia (anteriorly) and iliac fascia (posteriorly)","The fascia lata only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal ligament only mediated by specific receptor activation","The external oblique aponeurosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuation of the transversalis fascia (anteriorly) and iliac fascia (posteriorly)', 'The femoral sheath is a funnel-shaped sleeve formed by the downward continuation of the transversalis fascia anteriorly and the iliac fascia posteriorly. It is not derived from the fascia lata, inguinal ligament, or external oblique aponeurosis.'),
('d97036e0-eb48-5659-9b59-d53bab3931d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The femoral sheath has how many compartments?', '["4 resulting in decreased functional activity","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (lateral for artery middle for vein medial for canal)","2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (lateral for artery middle for vein medial for canal)', 'The femoral sheath is divided into three compartments by two vertical septa: the lateral contains the femoral artery, the middle the femoral vein, and the medial forms the femoral canal. The femoral nerve lies outside the sheath.'),
('a531b349-c9f7-5ae6-aeee-5532dc14da09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The deep inguinal ring is located:', '["Above the inguinal ligament lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels","At the pubic tubercle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Below the inguinal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the femoral triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above the inguinal ligament lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels', 'The deep inguinal ring lies in the transversalis fascia about 1.25 cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. It is not at the pubic tubercle, which marks the superficial ring.'),
('851fb35d-3afe-5953-9c46-be9c12071df8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The superficial inguinal ring is located:', '["At the ASIS","Above and medial to the pubic tubercle","At the deep inguinal ring","Below the pubic tubercle","None"]'::jsonb, 'Above and medial to the pubic tubercle', 'The superficial inguinal ring is a triangular defect in the aponeurosis of the external oblique, located above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. The ASIS and deep inguinal ring are unrelated sites.'),
('6c2b609d-6382-5732-8338-d45d02de27f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor canal (subsartorial/Hunter''s canal) extends from:', '["The femoral ring to the popliteal fossa","None","The inguinal ligament to the knee","The deep inguinal ring to the femoral triangle","The apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus"]'::jsonb, 'The apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus', 'The adductor canal runs from the apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus in the adductor magnus tendon. The femoral ring and inguinal ring are not related to the canal, whose distal end opens at the hiatus into the popliteal fossa.'),
('66825a3f-fa39-5b25-9a41-b605bbf02314'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor canal contains:', '["Popliteal artery only resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral artery femoral vein saphenous nerve and nerve to vastus medialis","Femoral nerve and artery only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sciatic nerve only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Great saphenous vein only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral artery femoral vein saphenous nerve and nerve to vastus medialis', 'The adductor canal contains the femoral artery and vein, the saphenous nerve, and the nerve to vastus medialis. The sciatic nerve and great saphenous vein do not traverse the canal, and the femoral nerve ends proximal to it.'),
('6d295ce0-e12a-5b84-85a9-162142349944'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor magnus muscle has how many parts?', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 (adductor part and hamstring part)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (adductor part and hamstring part)', 'Adductor magnus has two parts: an adductor portion arising from the pubis and inserting on the linea aspera, and a hamstring portion from the ischial tuberosity inserting on the adductor tubercle. These two parts have different innervations.'),
('bbf65114-6d27-59d4-b310-89e58539007a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor part of adductor magnus inserts into:', '["Gluteal tuberosity","Linea aspera (medial lip)","Adductor tubercle","Pectineal line","None"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera (medial lip)', 'The adductor part of adductor magnus, innervated by the obturator nerve, inserts into the gluteal tuberosity and linea aspera (medial lip). The hamstring part inserts into the adductor tubercle, and the pectineal line is the insertion of pectineus.'),
('aa750ecc-8a77-59dd-b231-7e4d54801fd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus inserts into:', '["Adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle (via the tendinous insertion)","Linea aspera accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pectineal line mediated by specific receptor activation","Gluteal tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle (via the tendinous insertion)', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus, innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve, inserts by a strong tendon into the adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle. The linea aspera and gluteal tuberosity receive the adductor part, and the pectineal line belongs to pectineus.'),
('81a610e5-4b35-5254-a70e-ce61cb5457e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor hiatus is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator canal mediated by specific receptor activation","A gap in the adductor magnus tendon where the femoral artery passes to become the popliteal artery","The saphenous opening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A gap in the adductor magnus tendon where the femoral artery passes to become the popliteal artery', 'The adductor hiatus is an opening in the distal tendon of adductor magnus through which the femoral artery and vein pass to enter the popliteal fossa, the artery becoming the popliteal artery. It is distinct from the femoral ring, obturator canal, and saphenous opening.'),
('2548acf3-d7dd-58d3-b0e7-e4056bcfe37c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The quadriceps femoris has how many heads?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 (rectus femoris vastus medialis intermedius lateralis)","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 (rectus femoris vastus medialis intermedius lateralis)', 'Quadriceps femoris has four heads: rectus femoris, vastus medialis, vastus intermedius, and vastus lateralis, all converging on the quadriceps tendon and patella. It therefore has four, not one, two, three, or five heads.'),
('b789b013-932d-5770-b2a3-7d27bbb479e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Rectus femoris originates from:', '["Femur resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubic bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASIS mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischial tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AIIS (straight head) and ilium above acetabulum (reflected head)"]'::jsonb, 'AIIS (straight head) and ilium above acetabulum (reflected head)', 'Rectus femoris has two heads of origin: a straight head from the anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) and a reflected head from the ilium just above the acetabulum. It is the only quadriceps head to cross the hip joint and does not arise from the femur.'),
('5eae4396-a823-5989-a460-8ef137fdefb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The vastus medialis originates from:', '["Lateral lip of linea aspera resulting in decreased functional activity","Gluteal tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial lip of linea aspera and intertrochanteric line","Pectineal line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AIIS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial lip of linea aspera and intertrochanteric line', 'Vastus medialis arises from the intertrochanteric line and the medial lip of the linea aspera, extending down to the medial supracondylar line. The lateral lip relates to vastus lateralis, and the gluteal tuberosity, pectineal line, and AIIS are origins of other muscles.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('e6db603f-2052-51e6-9a82-958c88a088d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The vastus lateralis originates from:', '["Intertrochanteric line resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral lip of linea aspera gluteal tuberosity and greater trochanter","AIIS mediated by specific receptor activation","Femoral condyles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial lip of linea aspera leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral lip of linea aspera gluteal tuberosity and greater trochanter', 'The vastus lateralis, the largest component of the quadriceps, arises from the lateral lip of the linea aspera, the gluteal tuberosity, and the greater trochanter of the femur. The AIIS gives origin to rectus femoris, and the medial lip of the linea aspera is used by the adductor muscles and vastus medialis.'),
('8283c832-b807-5f22-8338-d6c2334cb72b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The vastus intermedius originates from:', '["Medial lip of linea aspera resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior and lateral surfaces of the femoral shaft","Femoral condyles mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral lip of linea aspera without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and lateral surfaces of the femoral shaft', 'The vastus intermedius arises from the anterior and lateral surfaces of the femoral shaft and lies deep to rectus femoris. The linea aspera regions and gluteal tuberosity are attachments of other thigh muscles, and the condyles form the distal articular surface of the femur.'),
('a744d603-57e8-50e5-bfb8-48b5371eb450'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The quadriceps femoris inserts via the:', '["Patellar tendon directly to the patella resulting in decreased functional activity","Quadriceps tendon to the patella then patellar ligament to the tibial tuberosity","Nothing mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadriceps tendon to the tibia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Patellar ligament to the patella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps tendon to the patella then patellar ligament to the tibial tuberosity', 'The quadriceps tendon attaches the muscle to the patella, and the patellar ligament continues from the patella to the tibial tuberosity, forming an indirect insertion on the tibia. The fibula and patella are not terminal insertion points of the whole quadriceps mechanism.'),
('0461d151-ca17-57dd-a549-97261ad42405'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hamstring muscles include:', '["Adductor magnus resulting in decreased functional activity","Quadriceps femoris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gracilis mediated by specific receptor activation","Semitendinosus semimembranosus biceps femoris","Sartorius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Semitendinosus semimembranosus biceps femoris', 'The hamstring group consists of semitendinosus, semimembranosus, and biceps femoris (long and short heads), all of which extend the hip and flex the knee. Gracilis and sartorius are not hamstrings, and adductor magnus and quadriceps femoris belong to separate groups.'),
('f4d17985-65b2-5527-9ebf-65222d2cad89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Semitendinosus originates from:', '["Femoral shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubic bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ASIS mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischial tuberosity (with biceps long head)","AIIS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity (with biceps long head)', 'Semitendinosus arises from the ischial tuberosity by a common tendon with the long head of biceps femoris. The ASIS gives origin to sartorius, the AIIS to rectus femoris, and the pubic bone to the pectineus and adductors.'),
('b2692eda-a11f-58e5-99d6-46aee74158a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Semimembranosus originates from:', '["Pubic bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Femur accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AIIS mediated by specific receptor activation","ASIS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischial tuberosity (medial to semitendinosus)"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity (medial to semitendinosus)', 'Semimembranosus arises from the ischial tuberosity just medial to the semitendinosus attachment and forms an expanded aponeurotic origin. The ASIS, AIIS, pubis, and femur are origins of other thigh muscles, not this hamstring.'),
('bc70aaeb-65d6-5240-b78d-1d808fb4b7ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The long head of biceps femoris originates from:', '["Femoral shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischial tuberosity (with semitendinosus)","AIIS mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity (with semitendinosus)', 'The long head of biceps femoris shares its origin on the ischial tuberosity with semitendinosus. The femoral shaft gives origin to its short head, and the gluteal tuberosity, AIIS, and pubic bone are attachments of the vastus lateralis, rectus femoris, and adductors.'),
('884a78d1-3f4e-5271-9566-4dad4faac575'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The short head of biceps femoris originates from:', '["Ischial tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","AIIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linea aspera (lateral lip) and lateral supracondylar ridge of femur","Pubic bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera (lateral lip) and lateral supracondylar ridge of femur', 'The short head of biceps femoris arises from the lateral lip of the linea aspera and the lateral supracondylar ridge of the femur, and it does not cross the hip joint. The ischial tuberosity is the origin of the long head, while the gluteal tuberosity and pubis relate to other muscles.'),
('f2752342-6b71-5729-b500-1d39a53b8a3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Semitendinosus inserts into:', '["Medial femoral condyle resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Head of fibula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pes anserinus (upper medial tibial shaft)"]'::jsonb, 'Pes anserinus (upper medial tibial shaft)', 'Semitendinosus inserts on the upper medial tibial shaft as part of the pes anserinus, sharing it with sartorius and gracilis. The head of the fibula receives biceps femoris, and the tibial tuberosity receives the patellar ligament.'),
('89e00a9e-9d23-5435-9a57-52b521cf7588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Semimembranosus inserts into:', '["Pes anserinus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Head of fibula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior surface of medial tibial condyle (and oblique popliteal ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of medial tibial condyle (and oblique popliteal ligament)', 'Semimembranosus inserts principally on the posterior surface of the medial tibial condyle, and one of its slips forms the oblique popliteal ligament. The head of the fibula is the biceps insertion, and the pes anserinus receives semitendinosus.'),
('8e46b861-b97f-5695-af68-aed4853beea7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Biceps femoris inserts into:', '["Head of the fibula (mainly)","Patella","Pes anserinus","Medial condyle of tibia","Tibial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Head of the fibula (mainly)', 'Both heads of biceps femoris converge to insert mainly on the head of the fibula, with a small slip to the lateral tibial condyle. The patella and tibial tuberosity are part of the quadriceps mechanism, and the pes anserinus receives sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus.'),
('e468c98d-a47c-53ee-ac03-ad6ff2d77167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The hamstring muscles act to:', '["Flex the hip and extend the knee","Adduct the thigh","Extend the hip and flex the knee","Rotate the thigh","Abduct the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Extend the hip and flex the knee', 'As biarticular muscles crossing both joints, the hamstrings extend the hip and flex the knee. The reverse actions (hip flexion and knee extension) belong to the rectus femoris, and adduction and abduction are performed by other muscle groups.'),
('38067a03-5347-5ad9-9c3f-f9a78e074511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The short head of biceps femoris is unique because:', '["It is the only hamstring that does not cross the hip joint (only knee flexion)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is supplied by the tibial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","It is supplied by the obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It originates from the ischial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is the only hamstring that does not cross the hip joint (only knee flexion)', 'Because it originates on the femoral shaft, the short head of biceps femoris acts only at the knee (flexion) instead of extending the hip as the other hamstrings do. It is supplied by the common peroneal division of the sciatic nerve, and it does not arise from the ischial tuberosity.'),
('43512c87-0220-5f54-9c28-0c238ec2e29b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The short head of biceps femoris is supplied by:', '["Tibial division resulting in decreased functional activity","Common peroneal (fibular) division of sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior gluteal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obturator nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal (fibular) division of sciatic nerve', 'The sciatic nerve supplies all hamstrings, but only the short head of biceps femoris receives the common peroneal (fibular) division; every other hamstring is innervated by the tibial division. The femoral, obturator, and superior gluteal nerves do not reach the hamstrings.'),
('21c2923e-6e13-589d-94fc-60c2be529309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The pes anserinus (goose''s foot) is the common insertion of:', '["None","Gracilis adductor magnus adductor longus","Sartorius gracilis semitendinosus","Semitendinosus semimembranosus biceps","Sartorius rectus femoris vastus medialis"]'::jsonb, 'Sartorius gracilis semitendinosus', 'The pes anserinus is formed by the conjoined tendons of sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus inserting on the upper medial tibial shaft. The other muscle groupings do not share this common insertion.'),
('781895f2-94bf-5032-9bd0-9ad759b4a497'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sartorius muscle is the longest muscle in the body. It is supplied by:', '["Femoral nerve (L2 L3)","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2 L3)', 'Sartorius, the longest muscle in the body, is innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L3), matching its position in the anterior thigh compartment. The obturator nerve supplies the medial compartment, and the sciatic and gluteal nerves serve the posterior and gluteal regions.'),
('09b54eff-c41d-550d-9b02-abc73feb263d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor muscles of the thigh include:', '["Gluteal muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Adductor longus adductor brevis adductor magnus gracilis pectineus","Iliopsoas mediated by specific receptor activation","Hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Quadriceps femoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor longus adductor brevis adductor magnus gracilis pectineus', 'The medial thigh compartment consists of adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, gracilis, and pectineus, all innervated mainly by the obturator nerve. The hamstrings, quadriceps, and gluteals belong to different compartments with different nerve supplies.'),
('e989e604-8610-5d2f-957c-c1e21f3c0799'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) supplies:', '["Gluteals resulting in decreased functional activity","Hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadriceps femoris mediated by specific receptor activation","All adductor muscles (except hamstring part of adductor magnus)","Leg muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All adductor muscles (except hamstring part of adductor magnus)', 'The obturator nerve innervates the medial compartment adductors; the hamstring part of adductor magnus is supplied separately by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve. The gluteals, quadriceps, and hamstrings are otherwise supplied by the gluteal and sciatic nerves.'),
('9d2dcd5a-2aa1-572f-ac3e-86a26105ca89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The anterior division of the obturator nerve supplies:', '["Adductor magnus resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator externus mediated by specific receptor activation","Adductor longus adductor brevis gracilis and pectineus (with femoral)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor longus adductor brevis gracilis and pectineus (with femoral)', 'The anterior division of the obturator nerve supplies adductor longus, adductor brevis, gracilis, and part of pectineus, which also receives a femoral branch. The posterior division, by contrast, supplies the adductor part of adductor magnus and obturator externus.'),
('1184c01b-7801-5106-b760-7966fee160c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior division of the obturator nerve supplies:', '["Adductor longus resulting in decreased functional activity","Adductor magnus (adductor part) and obturator externus","Pectineus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gracilis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor magnus (adductor part) and obturator externus', 'The posterior division of the obturator nerve innervates the adductor part of adductor magnus and obturator externus. The adductor longus, adductor brevis, gracilis, and part of pectineus are served by the anterior division.'),
('4280f205-1d2a-5075-bcba-1db3e095dc2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gracilis muscle is supplied by:', '["Obturator nerve (anterior division L2 L3)","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior gluteal mediated by specific receptor activation","Sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior gluteal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve (anterior division L2 L3)', 'Gracilis is innervated by the anterior division of the obturator nerve (L2-L3) and contributes to the pes anserinus on the tibia. The femoral, sciatic, and gluteal nerves serve other compartments and do not reach this medial thigh muscle.'),
('c12e6041-cf79-51c9-a508-0db6f6a8dd0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The pectineus muscle is supplied by:', '["Femoral nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior gluteal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral nerve and sometimes obturator nerve (dual supply)"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve and sometimes obturator nerve (dual supply)', 'Pectineus typically receives the femoral nerve, with an additional branch from the obturator nerve in many individuals, reflecting its position between the anterior and medial compartments. It is rarely supplied exclusively by one of these nerves, and the gluteal and sciatic nerves do not reach it.'),
('6e24a056-7013-528f-9824-bbe0851ea811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The adductor hiatus is formed by:', '["The gracilis resulting in decreased functional activity","The adductor brevis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The adductor longus mediated by specific receptor activation","The tendon of adductor magnus splitting into two attachments","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tendon of adductor magnus splitting into two attachments', 'The adductor hiatus is the gap between the ischiofemoral and distal femoral insertions of adductor magnus, allowing the femoral vessels to pass from the adductor canal into the popliteal fossa. The other medial thigh muscles do not participate in forming this opening.'),
('80d03e42-09ea-5d39-a070-b6090db3c22e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The anterior compartment of the leg contains:', '["Gastrocnemius soleus plantaris","Peroneus longus brevis","None","Tibialis anterior EHL EDL peroneus tertius","Tibialis posterior FDL FHL"]'::jsonb, 'Tibialis anterior EHL EDL peroneus tertius', 'The anterior compartment of the leg contains tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, and peroneus tertius, all supplied by the deep peroneal nerve and acting as dorsiflexors and inverters. The other options list muscles of the lateral and posterior compartments.'),
('0d44d6ad-4fc2-5383-ad1b-f37b36d5b99a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lateral compartment of the leg contains:', '["Tibialis posterior and FDL","Tibialis anterior and EHL","Peroneus longus and peroneus brevis","None","Gastrocnemius and soleus"]'::jsonb, 'Peroneus longus and peroneus brevis', 'The lateral compartment contains peroneus longus and peroneus brevis, both supplied by the superficial peroneal nerve and acting to evert the foot. The tibialis muscles, EHL, FDL, gastrocnemius, and soleus belong to the anterior or posterior compartments.'),
('1c8d2037-90db-5146-8230-82f28c600d40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior compartment of the leg (superficial) contains:', '["Peroneus longus brevis","None","Gastrocnemius soleus plantaris","Tibialis posterior FDL FHL","Tibialis anterior EHL EDL"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrocnemius soleus plantaris', 'The superficial posterior compartment contains gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris, all innervated by the tibial nerve and forming the calf. The deep posterior compartment holds tibialis posterior, FDL, and FHL, while the peroneal and anterior muscles occupy their own compartments.'),
('9a40b8c6-b187-530d-a855-566007e4de69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior compartment of the leg (deep) contains:', '["Peroneus longus brevis resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastrocnemius soleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibialis posterior flexor digitorum longus flexor hallucis longus","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Popliteus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibialis posterior flexor digitorum longus flexor hallucis longus', 'The deep posterior compartment contains tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, and flexor hallucis longus (together with popliteus), all supplied by the tibial nerve. Gastrocnemius and soleus lie in the superficial posterior compartment, and the peroneal muscles are lateral.'),
('56655785-4d94-5557-a71b-bb73e01e2160'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tibialis anterior is supplied by:', '["Deep peroneal nerve (L4 L5)","Sural nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve (L4 L5)', 'Tibialis anterior, the chief dorsiflexor and inverter, is innervated by the deep peroneal nerve (L4-L5) as an anterior compartment muscle. The superficial peroneal nerve serves the lateral compartment, and the tibial nerve the posterior compartment.'),
('b4ff0328-a167-58aa-a11d-e4404a036468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The extensor hallucis longus is supplied by:', '["None","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve (L5 S1)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve (L5 S1)', 'Extensor hallucis longus, which extends the great toe and aids dorsiflexion, is supplied by the deep peroneal nerve (L5-S1). The tibial nerve serves the posterior compartment, the superficial peroneal nerve the lateral compartment, and the saphenous nerve is cutaneous.'),
('b7911037-2e4b-55c1-8108-38e25a37ffca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The extensor digitorum longus is supplied by:', '["Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Deep peroneal nerve (L5 S1)","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve (L5 S1)', 'Extensor digitorum longus, which extends the lateral four toes and dorsiflexes the foot, is innervated by the deep peroneal nerve (L5-S1). The femoral and obturator nerves end in the thigh, and the tibial and superficial peroneal nerves serve other leg compartments.'),
('73dcfaa5-71be-5c33-8620-ed436f68e599'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The peroneus tertius is supplied by:', '["Deep peroneal nerve (L5 S1)","Superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve (L5 S1)', 'Peroneus tertius, an evertor that is part of the anterior compartment, is supplied by the deep peroneal nerve (L5-S1). The superficial peroneal nerve innervates only the lateral compartment, and the sural, tibial, and saphenous nerves supply other regions.'),
('f9fd7f43-1818-5eec-96c3-03a437c6b1e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The peroneus longus is supplied by:', '["Sural nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve (L5 S1)","Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve (L5 S1)', 'Peroneus longus is innervated by the superficial peroneal nerve (L5-S1), which supplies both muscles of the lateral compartment. The deep peroneal nerve serves the anterior compartment, and the tibial, sural, and saphenous nerves supply other structures.'),
('63141869-99f7-5f16-9727-64407a0e141c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The peroneus brevis is supplied by:', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve (L5 S1)","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve (L5 S1)', 'Peroneus brevis, like peroneus longus, is supplied by the superficial peroneal nerve (L5-S1). The deep peroneal nerve innervates the anterior compartment, while the tibial, sural, and saphenous nerves supply the posterior compartment and skin.'),
('d3be3440-4499-5e28-9362-c942ec096c79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gastrocnemius is supplied by:', '["Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve (S1 S2)"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (S1 S2)', 'Gastrocnemius, a superficial posterior compartment muscle, is innervated by the tibial nerve (S1-S2). The peroneal nerves serve the anterior and lateral compartments, and the femoral and obturator nerves remain in the thigh.'),
('dca848ce-3dad-5791-a863-f17f4b047717'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The soleus is supplied by:', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Tibial nerve (S1 S2)"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (S1 S2)', 'Soleus, the postural plantarflexor of the calf, is supplied by the tibial nerve (S1-S2). The peroneal nerves serve the anterior and lateral compartments, and the femoral and obturator nerves do not extend into the leg.'),
('b870422c-7903-55f7-8808-c90a21df3466'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The plantaris is supplied by:', '["Femoral nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Obturator nerve","Tibial nerve (S1 S2)"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (S1 S2)', 'Plantaris, a small vestigial muscle of the superficial posterior compartment, is supplied by the tibial nerve (S1-S2). Its tendon blends into the calcaneal tendon, and the peroneal and thigh nerves do not supply it.'),
('19e764b2-af9e-567e-9aab-c36bfede8476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tibialis posterior is supplied by:', '["Femoral","Obturator","Tibial nerve (L4 L5)","Superficial peroneal","Deep peroneal"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (L4 L5)', 'Tibialis posterior, the chief inverter of the foot in the deep posterior compartment, is innervated by the tibial nerve (L4-L5). The peroneal branches serve the anterior and lateral compartments, and the femoral and obturator nerves end proximal to the leg.'),
('78b0f82e-47a8-5052-b2ca-3dc896c6d8ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The flexor digitorum longus (FDL) is supplied by:', '["Superficial peroneal","Obturator","Tibial nerve (L5 S1)","Femoral","Deep peroneal"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (L5 S1)', 'Flexor digitorum longus, which flexes the lateral four toes, is supplied by the tibial nerve (L5-S1) in the deep posterior compartment. The peroneal branches innervate the anterior and lateral compartments, and the obturator and femoral nerves serve the thigh.'),
('a381d57b-1024-5965-9132-0e8733fe0523'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The flexor hallucis longus (FHL) is supplied by:', '["Tibial nerve (L5 S1 S2)","Deep peroneal","Obturator","Femoral","Superficial peroneal"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (L5 S1 S2)', 'Flexor hallucis longus, which flexes the great toe, is supplied by the tibial nerve (L5-S1-S2) as part of the deep posterior compartment. The peroneal nerves serve the anterior and lateral compartments, and the femoral and obturator nerves remain in the thigh.'),
('d37b9cb8-6b01-5b78-b216-3207ccd3a654'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteus is supplied by:', '["Femoral","Obturator","Tibial nerve (L4 L5 S1)","Deep peroneal","Superficial peroneal"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (L4 L5 S1)', 'Popliteus, which unlocks the knee by laterally rotating the femur on the tibia, is innervated by the tibial nerve (L4-L5-S1). It lies in the deep posterior compartment of the leg, where the peroneal and thigh nerves do not reach it.'),
('46abf078-9169-57a3-beaa-49d61e504627'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The triceps surae consists of:', '["Gastrocnemius soleus and plantaris","None","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Just gastrocnemius","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrocnemius and soleus', 'Triceps surae is formed by gastrocnemius and soleus, which unite to form the calcaneal tendon. Although plantaris lies close by and shares the tendon, it is a separate muscle and is not part of triceps surae.'),
('0d6ee607-3113-534d-935c-35d3b9405d09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tendo calcaneus (Achilles tendon) is the common tendon of:', '["FDL and FHL","Tibialis posterior and FDL","Peroneus longus and brevis","Gastrocnemius and soleus (triceps surae)","All posterior muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrocnemius and soleus (triceps surae)', 'The calcaneal tendon is formed by the conjoined tendons of gastrocnemius and soleus, the two-bellied triceps surae. FDL, FHL, tibialis posterior, and the peroneal muscles each insert separately and do not contribute to this tendon.'),
('a95f36fc-f6a7-5c14-bf60-84ca7561f5b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The Achilles tendon is the thickest and strongest tendon in the body and inserts into:', '["The posterior surface of the calcaneus","The talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The navicular mediated by specific receptor activation","The metatarsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cuboid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior surface of the calcaneus', 'The calcaneal (Achilles) tendon inserts on the posterior surface of the calcaneus, transmitting the power of the triceps surae for plantarflexion. The talus, navicular, cuboid, and metatarsals are insertion sites for other tendons such as tibialis posterior and the peroneal muscles.'),
('291e0c95-9c63-59ab-acd0-ec532f916d29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tibialis posterior tendon inserts into:', '["Talus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cuboid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Navicular tuberosity (and all tarsals except talus and metatarsals 2-4)","Metatarsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcaneus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular tuberosity (and all tarsals except talus and metatarsals 2-4)', 'Tibialis posterior inserts mainly on the navicular tuberosity, with additional slips to most tarsals (except the talus) and the bases of metatarsals 2-4. This wide attachment makes it the principal inverter supporting the medial longitudinal arch.'),
('03340f62-358f-596e-b6e9-98b7de1c732f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The flexor hallucis longus tendon passes through the foot and inserts into:', '["Base of distal phalanx of hallux","Base of proximal phalanx of hallux","Cuboid","Base of middle phalanx","Navicular"]'::jsonb, 'Base of distal phalanx of hallux', 'Flexor hallucis longus inserts on the plantar base of the distal phalanx of the hallux, the only phalanx the great toe possesses. The proximal phalanx and midfoot bones receive the tendons of other flexor and tibialis muscles.'),
('cdb2d0f2-bf9c-5dd2-8800-c02b77e697b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The flexor digitorum longus tendon inserts into:', '["Base of middle phalanges resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base of distal phalanges of lateral 4 toes","Base of proximal phalanges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuboid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of distal phalanges of lateral 4 toes', 'Flexor digitorum longus divides into four slips that insert on the plantar bases of the distal phalanges of the lateral four toes, passing through the split tendons of flexor digitorum brevis. The proximal and middle phalanges and midfoot bones are insertion sites of other tendons.'),
('bb7b5f7f-d6b1-5c51-a8b6-77ae9f9fa750'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The peroneus longus tendon passes:', '["Into the navicular resulting in decreased functional activity","Into the talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Under the foot (groove in cuboid) to insert into the base of the 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform","Into the calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Over the dorsum of the foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Under the foot (groove in cuboid) to insert into the base of the 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform', 'Peroneus longus passes behind the lateral malleolus, across the sole in the groove of the cuboid, and inserts on the base of the 1st metatarsal and the medial cuneiform, helping to support the arches. The navicular and talus receive other tendons, and the tendon does not cross the dorsum.'),
('bfda5bca-d75a-52e6-a6ff-dc854f7b7c72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The peroneus brevis tendon inserts into:', '["Cuboid resulting in decreased functional activity","Base of the 5th metatarsal (styloid process)","Base of the 5th proximal phalanx mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Base of 1st metatarsal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the 5th metatarsal (styloid process)', 'Peroneus brevis inserts on the base (styloid process) of the 5th metatarsal, which is why avulsion fractures at this site injure the tendon. The cuboid and 1st metatarsal receive the peroneus longus, and the calcaneus receives the Achilles tendon.'),
('aec9863a-0b3a-5404-985e-1ed2ab9dd3ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The anterior tibial artery is a branch of:', '["Popliteal artery","Posterior tibial artery","Femoral artery","Peroneal artery","Profunda femoris"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal artery', 'The popliteal artery divides at the lower border of popliteus into the anterior and posterior tibial arteries; the anterior tibial artery then passes through the interosseous membrane to reach the anterior compartment. The femoral and profunda femoris arteries lie proximal to this bifurcation, and the posterior tibial gives off the peroneal artery.'),
('cb862fb9-ffa8-5146-9ace-685feb61786b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior tibial artery is a branch of:', '["Femoral artery","Popliteal artery","Peroneal artery","Profunda femoris","Anterior tibial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal artery', 'The posterior tibial artery is the larger terminal branch of the popliteal artery, continuing through the deep posterior compartment and giving rise to the peroneal artery. The femoral and profunda femoris arteries are proximal branches, and the anterior tibial artery is a sibling terminal branch.'),
('2e136da8-2622-5eae-b07d-4cd22e72b2a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The peroneal (fibular) artery is a branch of:', '["Popliteal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior tibial artery (in the leg)","Femoral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior tibial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior tibial artery (in the leg)', 'The fibular (peroneal) artery arises from the posterior tibial artery just below the popliteal fossa and supplies the lateral compartment of the leg. The popliteal artery gives off the anterior and posterior tibial arteries, while the femoral artery supplies the thigh.'),
('841ecb03-70a6-578b-8439-a59cd9386347'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The plantar arch is formed mainly by:', '["Anterior tibial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial plantar artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peroneal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral plantar artery anastomosing with the deep plantar branch of dorsalis pedis"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar artery anastomosing with the deep plantar branch of dorsalis pedis', 'The deep plantar arch is formed by the terminal part of the lateral plantar artery joining the deep plantar (perforating) branch of the dorsalis pedis artery. The medial plantar and peroneal arteries do not contribute to this arch.'),
('f768cc62-c58c-5ad5-955c-7029d57cd156'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The dorsalis pedis artery pulse is felt:', '["Behind the medial malleolus resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the EHL and EDL tendons on the dorsum of the foot","In the popliteal fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the femoral triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the EHL and EDL tendons on the dorsum of the foot', 'The dorsalis pedis artery is palpated on the dorsum of the foot between the tendons of extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus. The posterior tibial pulse is instead felt behind the medial malleolus.'),
('80182485-920d-576b-ab3e-69c4ac5ebe01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The posterior tibial artery pulse is felt:', '["In the popliteal fossa","In the femoral triangle","Behind the medial malleolus","Behind the lateral malleolus","On the dorsum of the foot"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the medial malleolus', 'The posterior tibial artery passes behind the medial malleolus, where its pulse is readily palpated. The artery behind the lateral malleolus is the peroneal artery, and the dorsalis pedis is felt on the dorsum of the foot.'),
('89be4adc-4bec-5da1-80c6-3dfc68632dc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The tarsal tunnel is located:', '["Behind the lateral malleolus resulting in decreased functional activity","In the sole of foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the dorsum of foot mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind the medial malleolus (bounded by flexor retinaculum)","At the ankle joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the medial malleolus (bounded by flexor retinaculum)', 'The tarsal tunnel lies behind the medial malleolus, roofed by the flexor retinaculum as it bridges the medial malleolus and the calcaneus. It transmits the tibialis posterior, FDL, posterior tibial vessels, tibial nerve, and FHL.'),
('7a27c1e1-fb37-5a58-a78c-ca94f0a058e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The contents of the tarsal tunnel from anterior to posterior include:', '["Tibialis anterior EHL EDL resulting in decreased functional activity","FDL FHL TP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Tibialis posterior FDL posterior tibial artery and vein tibial nerve FHL","Peroneus longus brevis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibialis posterior FDL posterior tibial artery and vein tibial nerve FHL', 'From anterior to posterior the tarsal tunnel transmits tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, posterior tibial artery and vein, tibial nerve, and flexor hallucis longus. The mnemonic ''Tom, Dick, and Harry'' (T-D-H) records this order.'),
('a9e54441-67fb-516e-97b1-529a60ad6840'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The flexor retinaculum of the ankle (laciniate ligament) attaches:', '["Lateral malleolus to talus","Medial malleolus to talus","None","Medial malleolus to calcaneus","Lateral malleolus to calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial malleolus to calcaneus', 'The flexor retinaculum (laciniate ligament) extends from the medial malleolus to the medial tuberosity of the calcaneus, converting the tarsal tunnel into an osseofascial canal. It has no attachment to the lateral malleolus.'),
('b34838e4-26af-53aa-95c8-d6a12322ab27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The extensor retinaculum of the ankle includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A single band accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only inferior mediated by specific receptor activation","Only superior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior and inferior extensor retinacula"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior extensor retinacula', 'The ankle has both a superior (transverse) and an inferior (cruciate) extensor retinaculum that bind the extensor tendons and dorsalis pedis vessels to the dorsum of the foot. Neither retinaculum exists as a single band.'),
('313480b7-19db-54fc-873c-b1485901b0b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The peroneal retinaculum:', '["Is the flexor retinaculum resulting in decreased functional activity","Holds the flexor tendons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Holds the peroneal tendons behind the lateral malleolus","Is the extensor retinaculum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Holds the peroneal tendons behind the lateral malleolus', 'The superior and inferior peroneal retinacula tether the tendons of peroneus longus and brevis behind and below the lateral malleolus. They are distinct from the flexor and extensor retinacula of the ankle.'),
('a5174a50-7bf1-5d2b-a561-26d41e036f1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ankle joint (talocrural joint) is what type of joint?', '["Plane","Ball and socket","Saddle","Hinge (ginglymus) joint","Pivot"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge (ginglymus) joint', 'The talocrural joint is a hinge (ginglymus) joint that permits dorsiflexion and plantarflexion around a transverse axis. The configuration of the mortise and the collateral ligaments restrict other movements.'),
('9180e28f-dafe-53c0-aa08-4de406fb00ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The ankle joint is formed by:', '["Tibia fibula and talus (mortise and tenon)","Fibula and talus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Tibia and calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibia and talus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia fibula and talus (mortise and tenon)', 'The tibia and fibula form the mortise that grips the trochlea (tenon) of the talus, so the joint is a three-bone articulation. The calcaneus belongs to the subtalar and calcaneocuboid joints, not the ankle joint.'),
('77d94e8e-65d5-53e2-aee9-7abe8f699242'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The deltoid ligament of the ankle is:', '["A thin lateral ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","A strong triangular medial ligament (from medial malleolus to talus calcaneus navicular)","A plantar ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A band on the dorsum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A strong triangular medial ligament (from medial malleolus to talus calcaneus navicular)', 'The deltoid (medial collateral) ligament is a strong triangular band running from the medial malleolus to the talus, calcaneus, and navicular. Because it is thick and strong, it is injured far less often than the weaker lateral ligament in inversion sprains.'),
('1cab8ebc-412c-524b-9037-b386aa02e699'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lateral ligament of the ankle consists of how many bands?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (anterior talofibular calcaneofibular posterior talofibular)","1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (anterior talofibular calcaneofibular posterior talofibular)', 'The lateral collateral ligament is composed of three bands: anterior talofibular, calcaneofibular, and posterior talofibular. The anterior talofibular band is the weakest and most frequently torn.'),
('b600c2e3-797e-5cc0-af42-963000be3527'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The most commonly injured ligament in ankle sprain is:', '["Deltoid ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","None","Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)","Posterior talofibular ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL)', 'The anterior talofibular ligament is the weakest part of the lateral ligament complex and tears first during inversion sprains. The calcaneofibular ligament may tear with more severe inversion, while the deltoid ligament is rarely injured.'),
('231c6873-19e9-5686-b383-de65d2bded65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The subtalar joint is between:', '["None","Calcaneus and cuboid","Talus and calcaneus","Talus and navicular","Tibia and talus"]'::jsonb, 'Talus and calcaneus', 'The subtalar (talocalcaneal) joint lies between the talus above and the calcaneus below and allows inversion and eversion. The talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints are separate midtarsal articulations.'),
('d4024f09-8c7a-5006-8603-3e4688f33a64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The midtarsal (Chopart''s) joint is between:', '["Calcaneus and cuboid","None","Talus and calcaneus","Navicular and cuneiforms","Talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints"]'::jsonb, 'Talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints', 'The midtarsal (Chopart''s) joint is a functional unit formed by the talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints. It is the plane of forefoot amputation in the Chopart procedure.'),
('a99e62d0-4701-5917-bf9b-c4df8d5d14f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The foot arches include:', '["Only transverse arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Only one longitudinal arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial longitudinal lateral longitudinal and transverse arches","Only medial arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial longitudinal lateral longitudinal and transverse arches', 'The foot has three arches: the medial and lateral longitudinal arches and the transverse arch. Together they distribute body weight and act as shock absorbers during weight bearing.'),
('2cd1c97e-af4d-5ae6-a104-d45425abc1c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The medial longitudinal arch of the foot is formed by:', '["Calcaneus cuboid metatarsals 4-5 resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus and calcaneus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus talus navicular cuneiforms and metatarsals 1-3","All metatarsals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus talus navicular cuneiforms and metatarsals 1-3', 'The medial longitudinal arch runs from the calcaneus through the talus, navicular, three cuneiforms, and the first three metatarsals. The lateral arch instead uses the cuboid and the fourth and fifth metatarsals.'),
('1ff0bd0f-afcb-5b26-9cdd-180eecdfdc08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The lateral longitudinal arch of the foot is formed by:', '["Calcaneus cuboid and metatarsals 4-5","All metatarsals","None","Calcaneus talus navicular","Talus navicular cuneiforms"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus cuboid and metatarsals 4-5', 'The lateral longitudinal arch is built from the calcaneus, cuboid, and the fourth and fifth metatarsals. It is lower and flatter than the medial longitudinal arch, which incorporates the talus, navicular, and cuneiforms.'),
('181cb245-2835-59e0-91f1-a1da013b65ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The transverse arch of the foot is formed by:', '["Phalanges resulting in decreased functional activity","Cuboid cuneiforms and bases of metatarsals","Calcaneus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Navicular only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Talus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboid cuneiforms and bases of metatarsals', 'The transverse arch is formed by the cuboid, the three cuneiforms, and the bases of the metatarsals, and is deepest at the medial cuneiform. The navicular and talus contribute to the longitudinal arches rather than the transverse arch.'),
('2b662338-f247-570d-96f4-69c487210b9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The plantar aponeurosis (fascia) is a thickening of:', '["Flexor retinaculum","Deep fascia of the sole of the foot","None","Plantar ligament","Superficial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia of the sole of the foot', 'The plantar aponeurosis is the thickened central part of the deep fascia of the sole, running from the calcaneal tuberosity toward the toes. It is not derived from the superficial fascia or from any retinaculum or ligament.'),
('b19cd55e-b9f2-58d4-9d88-d4d73f48d842'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The plantar aponeurosis functions to:', '["Produce toe flexion resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Support the longitudinal arch of the foot","Provide sensation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Support the longitudinal arch of the foot', 'By acting as a windlass that tightens when the toes are dorsiflexed, the plantar aponeurosis supports the longitudinal arch of the foot. It neither flexes the toes nor provides sensation.'),
('bbf772d4-ba8b-553e-9efa-5357b43321a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular ligament):', '["Supports the head of the talus (maintains the medial arch)","Is the deltoid ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Connects the calcaneus to the cuboid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connects the calcaneus to the metatarsals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supports the head of the talus (maintains the medial arch)', 'The spring (plantar calcaneonavicular) ligament supports the head of the talus and is a key static support of the medial longitudinal arch. Failure of this ligament contributes to flatfoot deformity.'),
('6bc8c44e-2536-59df-8c79-b9c11b08920d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The long plantar ligament connects:', '["Calcaneus to talus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Talus to navicular mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus to cuboid (and bases of metatarsals)","Navicular to cuneiform leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus to cuboid (and bases of metatarsals)', 'The long plantar ligament passes from the calcaneal tuberosity to the cuboid and the bases of the second to fourth metatarsals. It strongly supports the lateral longitudinal arch.'),
('39dbaa4b-0264-5ea9-88e0-9734b433afad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The short plantar ligament (plantar calcaneocuboid):', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Connects calcaneus to cuboid (deep to long plantar)","Connects navicular to cuneiform mediated by specific receptor activation","Connects calcaneus to talus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connects talus to navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connects calcaneus to cuboid (deep to long plantar)', 'The short plantar (plantar calcaneocuboid) ligament lies deep to the long plantar ligament, connecting the anterior calcaneus to the plantar surface of the cuboid. It also supports the lateral longitudinal arch.'),
('83394ddf-dda7-56a2-9fa2-07b8513b1279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The bifurcate ligament is aY-shaped ligament on the dorsum of the foot connecting:', '["None","Calcaneus to navicular and cuboid","Navicular to cuneiform","Calcaneus to talus","Talus to navicular"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus to navicular and cuboid', 'The bifurcate ligament arises from the anterior calcaneus and splits into calcaneonavicular and calcaneocuboid bands, forming aY on the dorsum of the foot. It is a key stabilizer of the subtalar and midtarsal joints.'),
('69fedd8a-a62d-5a69-afdd-f0e80d5d2de1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The cervical ligament is located:', '["In the ankle joint resulting in decreased functional activity","In the midtarsal joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","In the subtalar joint posteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the sinus tarsi (connecting talus and calcaneus)"]'::jsonb, 'In the sinus tarsi (connecting talus and calcaneus)', 'The cervical ligament lies within the sinus tarsi between the talus and calcaneus. It is an important stabilizer of the subtalar joint and is often injured in subtalar sprains.'),
('8ed97af3-56f0-56a8-a0a2-947bb17fafbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The great saphenous vein ascends anterior to the medial malleolus and drains into:', '["Posterior tibial vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Popliteal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral vein (at the saphenous opening)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Great saphenous vein deep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral vein (at the saphenous opening)', 'The great saphenous vein passes through the saphenous opening of the fascia lata to drain into the femoral vein. It is the longest superficial vein and courses anterior to the medial malleolus.'),
('a7eb4e34-1dd9-5e43-9597-30c8ef887cad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The small saphenous vein ascends posterior to the lateral malleolus and drains into:', '["Femoral vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior tibial vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Popliteal vein (in the popliteal fossa)","Great saphenous vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal vein (in the popliteal fossa)', 'The small saphenous vein ascends behind the lateral malleolus and terminates in the popliteal vein within the popliteal fossa. It drains the posterior and lateral parts of the leg.'),
('46f6e3ca-ffd0-5cce-90e9-663a2a1c10ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The superficial inguinal lymph nodes drain:', '["Only the thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the gluteal region mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the lower limb external genitalia lower abdominal wall and perineum"]'::jsonb, 'All of the lower limb external genitalia lower abdominal wall and perineum', 'The superficial inguinal nodes receive lymph from the lower limb, external genitalia, lower abdominal wall, and perineum. Their drainage territory is far broader than the thigh, foot, or gluteal region alone.'),
('e6683f3b-2945-50e2-8412-ac4d2d9cc5b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The deep inguinal lymph nodes lie:', '["In the gluteal region resulting in decreased functional activity","In the popliteal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep to the fascia lata (along the femoral vessels)","Superficial to the fascia lata without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to the fascia lata (along the femoral vessels)', 'The deep inguinal nodes lie beneath the fascia lata along the femoral vessels, with the node of Cloquet being the most superior. They receive lymph from the superficial inguinal nodes and from the deep structures of the limb.'),
('97e07bc9-3041-58f9-9531-c92655bded8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The popliteal lymph nodes receive lymph from:', '["The foot and leg (posterior aspect)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","The thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The gluteal region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The foot and leg (posterior aspect)', 'The popliteal lymph nodes drain the superficial tissues of the posterior leg and foot as well as the deep structures of the leg. Lymphatics that pierce the popliteal fascia continue upward to the deep inguinal nodes.'),
('a6d793a8-38d2-5428-a843-9581de66171e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The dermatomal supply of the foot is important. The great toe is supplied by:', '["L3 dermatome","L5 dermatome","L4 dermatome (medial side)","S2 dermatome","S1 dermatome"]'::jsonb, 'L4 dermatome (medial side)', 'The medial side of the leg and the great toe belong to the L4 dermatome. The lateral toes and little toe are instead served by the S1 dermatome.'),
('fd13bc0f-8f40-5116-9d5f-b3feb2b48ee0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The little toe is supplied by:', '["S1 dermatome","L4 dermatome","L3 dermatome","L5 dermatome","S2 dermatome"]'::jsonb, 'S1 dermatome', 'The little toe and the lateral border of the foot lie within the S1 dermatome. L5 covers the dorsal and plantar surfaces of the central foot, while L4 supplies the medial side and great toe.'),
('410428e5-8476-514a-b83a-2a26f20da3f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The dorsal surface of the foot is supplied by:', '["L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","S2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L2 mediated by specific receptor activation","L5 dermatome (medially) and S1 (laterally)","L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L5 dermatome (medially) and S1 (laterally)', 'On the dorsum of the foot the L5 dermatome covers the medial side and the S1 dermatome the lateral side. L5 also supplies the plantar surface between the great and little toes.'),
('cee45ea6-32a9-54fd-89f2-4db1b42a61ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body. It is most commonly injured by:', '["Knee dislocation resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral neck fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior hip dislocation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior hip dislocation and intramuscular injections in the gluteal region"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior hip dislocation and intramuscular injections in the gluteal region', 'Because the sciatic nerve lies close to the posterior acetabulum and within the gluteal region, posterior hip dislocation and misplaced gluteal intramuscular injections are the common causes of injury. Femoral neck fractures and anterior hip dislocations rarely involve it.'),
('cd2d023a-edc3-5941-bf91-6b1c0a441c8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteal region is bounded superiorly by which of the following?', '["Sacrum","Ischial tuberosity","Gluteal fold","Greater trochanter","Iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac crest', 'The gluteal region is bounded superiorly by the iliac crest, inferiorly by the gluteal fold, laterally by the greater trochanter, and medially by the sacrum and coccyx. The ischial tuberosity is an inferior bony landmark.'),
('1fc72589-a933-5f04-9f4c-de2e15460a69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Regarding clinical cases: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('dac2fcee-174a-58ae-8a73-829992bf81c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'In medical practice, What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('7dd441b0-badd-51fc-9143-92a79935d5d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('07b1e39f-7e82-5b83-8db6-d85c1e86c63e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('516d5d12-6118-5314-95e1-d48ff0f00aee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'During therapeutic management, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('5317bd56-c3c2-5dc2-b538-0998f566200e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'The gluteus maximus originates from all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Iliac crest (posterior gluteal line)","Sacrum and coccyx","Thoracolumbar fascia","Sacrotuberous ligament","Gluteal surface of ilium (anterior gluteal line)"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteal surface of ilium (anterior gluteal line)', 'Gluteus maximus arises from the posterior gluteal line of the ilium and the iliac crest, sacrum, coccyx, sacrotuberous ligament, and thoracolumbar fascia. The gluteal surface of the ilium anterior to the posterior gluteal line is the origin of gluteus medius, not maximus.'),
('3ca7bd76-74d1-5d05-a4ae-0a0e90f51d3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'In medical practice, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('a8263964-b7bc-5210-b218-7f2a9c794783'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('4baf770c-872c-556e-bb18-b98898151d84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('182e9b07-284b-5be6-a311-df8617f0b451'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'During therapeutic management, Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('ad5a1852-d1f2-580d-b64e-a42fe7b00bab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'Regarding clinical cases: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('c36cffc7-1ceb-5160-90de-c3c10772f791'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'In medical practice, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('1c1572bc-062f-517e-a8ce-00224e0ea05f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Gluteal Region Hip Joint', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('bb2e88d9-9b8d-5bff-8d36-98b2a1b270aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve is formed from which nerve roots?', '["L5-S2","L2-L4","S1-S3","L3-S1","L4-S3"]'::jsonb, 'L4-S3', 'The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body and derives from the ventral rami L4 through S3 of the sacral plexus. The other ranges omit needed roots such as L4 or S3.'),
('c5ea503b-12c1-5859-8476-165149f79d6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve exits the pelvis through which foramen?', '["Suprapiriform foramen","Infrapiriform foramen","Lesser sciatic foramen","Greater sciatic foramen","Obturator foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Greater sciatic foramen', 'The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, passing inferior to the piriformis muscle. The lesser sciatic foramen transmits the nerve to obturator internus and sciatic structures that re-enter the pelvis, not the sciatic nerve itself.'),
('1bd7b00f-dedf-5197-974e-7544e76a4095'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve divides into its terminal branches at which level?', '["Ankle joint","Knee joint","Popliteal fossa","Mid-thigh","Gluteal region"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal fossa', 'The sciatic nerve divides into the tibial and common peroneal nerves at the upper angle of the popliteal fossa. Proximal to this it runs as a single trunk, so the gluteal region, mid-thigh, and knee joint are not the correct level.'),
('87a77243-0c0b-528c-9701-f8e53513f6d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibial nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Saphenous","Femoral","Sciatic","Obturator","Common peroneal"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic', 'The tibial nerve is the larger terminal division of the sciatic nerve, formed in the popliteal fossa. The femoral, obturator, and saphenous nerves arise from the lumbar plexus and do not give rise to the tibial nerve.'),
('30b7618b-5905-5700-ab75-a7bd2f309c1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common peroneal nerve winds around which structure?', '["Neck of fibula","Lateral condyle of tibia","Patella","Head of fibula","Tibial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Neck of fibula', 'The common peroneal nerve curves around the lateral side of the neck of the fibula, where it is superficial and easily compressed or injured. The tibial condyle, patella, and tibial tuberosity are not in its course.'),
('d55e4016-c9cd-5f31-b260-587af102fa49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which muscle is supplied by the superior gluteal nerve?', '["Gastrocnemius","Semitendinosus","Gluteus maximus","Biceps femoris","Gluteus medius"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius', 'The superior gluteal nerve innervates gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia lata, the abductors of the thigh. Gluteus maximus receives the inferior gluteal nerve, and gastrocnemius, semitendinosus, and biceps femoris are innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve.'),
('29697887-5e1d-510c-bac3-8333233aaeb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The inferior gluteal nerve supplies which muscle?', '["Gluteus maximus","Tensor fascia lata","Gluteus minimus","Piriformis","Gluteus medius"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus', 'The inferior gluteal nerve innervates gluteus maximus, the principal extensor and external rotator of the hip. Tensor fascia lata, gluteus medius, and gluteus minimus receive the superior gluteal nerve, while piriformis receives the nerve to piriformis.'),
('badb9ff5-1348-5b44-ba29-5e25035e415e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral nerve arises from which nerve roots?', '["L2-L4","L1-L2","L5-S2","L4-S1","S1-S3"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4', 'The femoral nerve arises from the posterior divisions of the ventral rami L2, L3, and L4 of the lumbar plexus. Ranges such as L1-L2 or L5-S2 fall outside the lumbar plexus contribution to this nerve.'),
('b19a76f5-d228-51ed-858b-bd2045b95f6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator nerve arises from which nerve roots?', '["S1-S3","L2-L4","L5-S2","L4-S1","L1-L2"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4', 'The obturator nerve arises from the anterior divisions of the ventral rami L2, L3, and L4 of the lumbar plexus. The other listed root ranges correspond to different nerves or plexuses.'),
('672e6a38-c83a-5eee-882c-bfe22cabba30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The saphenous nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Sciatic","Femoral","Common peroneal","Tibial","Obturator"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral', 'The saphenous nerve is the terminal cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve, running distally through the adductor canal with the femoral vessels. The sciatic nerve gives the tibial and common peroneal nerves, not the saphenous nerve.'),
('b70db64c-24ef-5e75-bb2c-0535f42a9d59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris arises from which plexus?', '["Lumbar","Pudendal","Brachial","Coccygeal","Sacral"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris arises from the sacral plexus (L4, L5, S1) and descends deep to the sciatic nerve to supply quadratus femoris and the inferior gemellus. The lumbar plexus gives the femoral, obturator, and lateral femoral cutaneous nerves rather than this deep gluteal branch.'),
('1feafe75-3945-5e8a-a820-f4826e087c62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to obturator internus arises from which roots?', '["L5-S2","S1-S3","L4-S1","L2-L4","L3-L5"]'::jsonb, 'L5-S2', 'The nerve to obturator internus arises from L5, S1, and S2 of the sacral plexus, leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen. The other ranges do not match this origin.'),
('e4505e6e-ca52-56ec-9a2d-9a8d2fded527'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh arises from which plexus?', '["Pudendal","Sacral","Lumbar","Coccygeal","Brachial"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar', 'The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve arises from the lumbar plexus, receiving fibers from L2 and L3, and crosses the inguinal ligament into the thigh. The sacral and pudendal plexuses supply gluteal, perineal, and posterior thigh territories instead.'),
('70bc28e2-b5d0-5833-8b4e-d4eedce1cd66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh arises from which plexus?', '["Lumbar","Pudendal","Sacral","Brachial","Coccygeal"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral', 'The posterior femoral cutaneous nerve arises from the sacral plexus (S1, S2, S3) and descends through the gluteal region to the back of the thigh. The lumbar plexus supplies the anterior and medial thigh, not the posterior cutaneous distribution.'),
('e555a94b-8734-57ba-a531-1e8b34848e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to the piriformis arises from which roots?', '["L4-S1","S2-S3","L5-S1","L2-L4","S1-S2"]'::jsonb, 'S1-S2', 'The nerve to piriformis arises from S1 and S2 and enters the piriformis muscle on its pelvic surface. Root ranges such as L4-S1 or L2-L4 belong to other branches of the lumbosacral plexus.'),
('9cbdada8-1663-55d0-95d5-4c71b6981352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior gluteal nerve exits the pelvis through which foramen?', '["Obturator foramen","Infrapiriform foramen","Greater sciatic foramen","Suprapiriform foramen","Lesser sciatic foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Suprapiriform foramen', 'The superior gluteal nerve leaves the pelvis through the suprapiriform foramen, the part of the greater sciatic foramen lying above the piriformis. The infrapiriform foramen transmits the inferior gluteal and sciatic nerves, and the obturator nerve uses the obturator canal.'),
('2d09f045-7c7e-55c6-84d7-14938c61e021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The inferior gluteal nerve exits the pelvis through which foramen?', '["Greater sciatic foramen","Obturator foramen","Lesser sciatic foramen","Infrapiriform foramen","Suprapiriform foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Infrapiriform foramen', 'The inferior gluteal nerve leaves the pelvis through the infrapiriform foramen, below the piriformis, and enters gluteus maximus. The suprapiriform foramen carries the superior gluteal nerve, and the obturator foramen transmits the obturator nerve and vessels.'),
('229160c7-fb71-5bed-abc6-fbc0bcc8bf97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve supplies which hamstring muscles?', '["All hamstrings","Only biceps femoris","None","Only semimembranosus","Only semitendinosus"]'::jsonb, 'All hamstrings', 'The tibial division of the sciatic nerve innervates the long head of biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus, while the short head of biceps femoris is supplied by the common peroneal division. Every hamstring therefore receives its innervation from the sciatic nerve.'),
('6b4ab037-bf54-5e5c-b92f-c8fef051036b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which part of the sciatic nerve supplies the short head of biceps femoris?', '["Both","Tibial division","Femoral nerve","Common peroneal division","Neither"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal division', 'The short head of biceps femoris is the only hamstring innervated by the common peroneal division of the sciatic nerve, whereas the long head and the other hamstrings are supplied by the tibial division. The femoral nerve does not innervate the hamstrings.'),
('b1567151-d73a-57c7-a4ff-a25ac20e1f06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibial nerve supplies which muscles in the posterior compartment of leg?', '["All posterior compartment muscles","Deep muscles only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Soleus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastrocnemius only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All posterior compartment muscles', 'The tibial nerve runs down the back of the leg and innervates every muscle of the posterior compartment, both superficial (gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris) and deep (popliteus, tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus, flexor digitorum longus). Options naming a single muscle or the deep group alone are too narrow.'),
('ca54c627-3609-53f9-9a4e-267ca8d9afda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common peroneal nerve supplies muscles of which compartment?', '["Posterior compartment","Anterior and lateral compartments","All compartments","Medial compartment","None"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and lateral compartments', 'The common peroneal nerve divides into the deep peroneal nerve, which innervates the anterior compartment, and the superficial peroneal nerve, which innervates the lateral compartment. The posterior compartment receives the tibial nerve, so the option naming anterior and lateral compartments covers both.'),
('84be113b-bdbc-5911-ab5c-224f8bc07e33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The deep peroneal nerve supplies which muscles?', '["None","All leg muscles","Posterior compartment muscles","Anterior compartment muscles","Lateral compartment muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior compartment muscles', 'The deep peroneal nerve innervates the anterior compartment muscles of the leg: tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, and peroneus tertius. The lateral compartment receives the superficial peroneal nerve and the posterior compartment the tibial nerve.'),
('9ec10e7b-4db8-5889-99cd-251d25b06efc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial peroneal nerve supplies which muscles?', '["Posterior compartment muscles","Lateral compartment muscles","All leg muscles","None","Anterior compartment muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral compartment muscles', 'The superficial peroneal nerve innervates peroneus longus and peroneus brevis, the muscles of the lateral compartment, and ends as a cutaneous nerve to the lower leg and dorsum of the foot. The anterior and posterior compartments are supplied by the deep peroneal and tibial nerves respectively.'),
('493c60f1-e6e0-5a4a-a45a-d1845f103b93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral nerve supplies which muscles of the thigh?', '["Quadriceps femoris and sartorius","Adductors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps femoris and sartorius', 'The femoral nerve innervates the four heads of the quadriceps femoris and the sartorius, and also gives motor branches to the pectineus. The adductors receive the obturator nerve, the hamstrings the sciatic nerve, and the gluteal muscles the superior and inferior gluteal nerves.'),
('ec1cd923-2fa6-5413-9e39-07149b247448'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator nerve supplies which muscle group?', '["Adductor muscles","Gluteal muscles","None","Quadriceps femoris","Hamstrings"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor muscles', 'The obturator nerve innervates the adductor group: adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, gracilis, and obturator externus. The quadriceps, hamstrings, and gluteal muscles are supplied by the femoral, sciatic, and gluteal nerves respectively.'),
('1c40dd46-044a-5b59-b9dd-bcf25faff101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the pectineus muscle?', '["Both femoral and obturator","Superior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Both femoral and obturator', 'The pectineus receives its main innervation from the femoral nerve with an accessory contribution from the anterior branch of the obturator nerve, giving it a dual supply. Options naming only a single nerve do not capture this arrangement.'),
('6dc2bea3-6f79-53ba-ab40-d249bc8ef10b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to the vastus medialis is a branch of which nerve?', '["Obturator","Sciatic","Tibial","Saphenous","Femoral"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral', 'Vastus medialis is a head of the quadriceps femoris, and all four heads receive branches of the femoral nerve in the anterior thigh. The obturator, sciatic, and tibial nerves do not innervate the quadriceps, and the saphenous nerve is sensory only at this level.'),
('be82ca04-34bd-5b05-8acf-04dd33811f87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The saphenous nerve provides cutaneous sensation to which area?', '["Lateral leg and foot","Medial leg and foot","Posterior leg","Dorsum of foot","Sole of foot"]'::jsonb, 'Medial leg and foot', 'The saphenous nerve descends with the great saphenous vein to supply skin over the medial side of the leg and foot as far as the medial malleolus. The lateral leg and foot, dorsum, sole, and posterior leg receive the sural, superficial peroneal, and tibial nerves instead.'),
('cf5b45ca-4090-5cf2-a2e9-a511cf01d1b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sural nerve is formed by branches from which nerves?', '["None","Femoral and obturator","Tibial and common peroneal","Sciatic and femoral","Saphenous and tibial"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial and common peroneal', 'The sural nerve forms in the calf from the medial sural cutaneous branch of the tibial nerve joined by the peroneal communicating branch of the common peroneal nerve. It then runs with the short saphenous vein behind the lateral malleolus.'),
('24e12606-7e24-5e52-b5f8-a15ce3f71702'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sural nerve supplies sensation to which area?', '["Sole of foot","Medial ankle and foot","Dorsum of foot","Lateral ankle and foot","Heel"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral ankle and foot', 'The sural nerve supplies cutaneous sensation to the posterolateral calf and the lateral side of the ankle and foot. The medial ankle and foot receive the saphenous nerve, the sole receives the tibial and plantar branches, and the dorsum of the foot is supplied by the superficial and deep peroneal nerves.'),
('c873f0dd-66ae-5128-90f7-30789a19a41c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial plantar nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The medial and lateral plantar nerves are the terminal branches of the tibial nerve, dividing within the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus. The other listed nerves terminate proximal to the sole or in different regions of the limb.'),
('f01693af-bd8d-5cd4-8fcc-34c1afae522c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral plantar nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The lateral plantar nerve arises as a terminal division of the tibial nerve within the tarsal tunnel, paralleling the medial plantar nerve. Neither the femoral, sciatic, common peroneal, nor saphenous nerves gives branches that reach the sole.'),
('01a899aa-d4cc-5c25-a07a-3ab86297af39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial plantar nerve supplies which muscles of the foot?', '["Lumbricals resulting in decreased functional activity","All foot muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis and flexor hallucis brevis","Quadratus plantae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abductor digiti minimi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis and flexor hallucis brevis', 'The medial plantar nerve supplies abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis, and the first lumbrical. Quadratus plantae and abductor digiti minimi are innervated by the lateral plantar nerve, and the lateral plantar nerve also supplies the remaining lumbricals and interossei.'),
('4565ef9c-ed31-5965-95f1-5c71f5e18b3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral plantar nerve supplies which muscles?', '["Only abductor hallucis","None","Only flexor digitorum brevis","Only interossei","Most intrinsic foot muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Most intrinsic foot muscles', 'The lateral plantar nerve innervates most intrinsic muscles of the foot, including quadratus plantae, abductor digiti minimi, the interossei, and lumbricals 2 through 4. Options naming one muscle are too narrow, since abductor hallucis and flexor digitorum brevis are medial plantar muscles.'),
('4e54001d-d2f9-5e26-852f-b4f4ea5f0872'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sural nerve supplies which muscle?', '["Plantaris","Peroneus longus","None (cutaneous only)","Soleus","Gastrocnemius"]'::jsonb, 'None (cutaneous only)', 'The sural nerve is purely cutaneous and provides no motor branches to any muscle. Gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris receive the tibial nerve, while peroneus longus is innervated by the superficial peroneal nerve.'),
('b2ec4f6c-c534-53ae-bd55-16f84e356f59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The iliohypogastric nerve arises from which plexus?', '["Lumbar plexus","Coccygeal plexus","Cervical plexus","Sacral plexus","Pudendal plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar plexus', 'The iliohypogastric nerve arises from the lumbar plexus as a branch of the first lumbar ventral ramus (L1). It runs between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles to supply the lower anterior abdominal wall and suprapubic skin. The sacral, coccygeal, and pudendal plexuses are unrelated to this nerve.'),
('4ac9146e-c1ec-568e-9210-857633dbb837'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ilioinguinal nerve arises from which roots?', '["L4-S1","S1-S3","L1","L3-L5","L2-L4"]'::jsonb, 'L1', 'The ilioinguinal nerve arises from the first lumbar ventral ramus (L1) and frequently shares a common trunk with the iliohypogastric nerve. It passes through the inguinal canal to supply skin of the upper medial thigh and the scrotum or labium majus. The other root ranges belong to different lumbosacral branches.'),
('5582ac14-5632-5546-9145-8f9e94b264c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The genitofemoral nerve arises from which roots?', '["L2-L4","L4-S1","L1-L2","S1-S3","L3-L5"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'The genitofemoral nerve arises from L1 and L2 of the lumbar plexus, pierces the psoas major, and divides into genital and femoral branches. The genital branch enters the inguinal canal and the femoral branch passes under the inguinal ligament to the upper anterior thigh. The other root ranges supply different nerves.'),
('64f7943f-45e5-5cd7-b230-759a8fa2a453'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh arises from which roots?', '["L4-S1","L2-L3","L3-L5","L1-L2","S1-S3"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L3', 'The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve arises from the posterior divisions of L2 and L3 in the lumbar plexus. It passes under the lateral end of the inguinal ligament to supply skin on the lateral thigh. Ranges such as L4-S1 or S1-S3 belong to the sacral plexus.'),
('602fe5d1-2e2f-57a7-8e1c-cc867d3b694b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator nerve enters the thigh through which canal?', '["Obturator canal","Femoral canal","None","Inguinal canal","Adductor canal"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator canal', 'The obturator nerve leaves the pelvis through the obturator canal together with the obturator vessels to reach the medial compartment of the thigh. The inguinal canal transmits the spermatic cord or round ligament, and the adductor canal carries the femoral vessels and saphenous nerve, not the obturator nerve.'),
('5f8c1eaf-d360-53dc-91ac-7fbe86813fe7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral nerve enters the thigh through which space?', '["Obturator canal","Adductor canal","Femoral triangle","Popliteal fossa","None"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral triangle', 'The femoral nerve enters the thigh beneath the inguinal ligament and immediately lies within the femoral triangle, lateral to the femoral sheath and vessels. It does not traverse the obturator or adductor canals, and the popliteal fossa lies distal to the thigh.'),
('91f7d6fc-b609-5545-a396-c42d73b3f870'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The saphenous nerve pierces which fascia to become subcutaneous?', '["Fascia lata","Cribriform fascia","None","Popliteal fascia","Deep fascia of leg"]'::jsonb, 'Fascia lata', 'The saphenous nerve emerges between the sartorius and gracilis at the medial side of the knee and pierces the fascia lata to become subcutaneous, where it accompanies the great saphenous vein down the medial leg. The cribriform fascia overlies the saphenous opening, unrelated to this nerve.'),
('538c9125-63ad-5e86-8901-8eb2e2c52a75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the tensor fascia lata?', '["Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'The superior gluteal nerve innervates tensor fascia lata together with gluteus medius and gluteus minimus. The inferior gluteal nerve supplies only gluteus maximus, and the sciatic, femoral, and obturator nerves do not innervate tensor fascia lata.'),
('54b665d2-5e06-5707-8c3e-c5fe07628116'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris also supplies which muscle?', '["Inferior gemellus","Obturator internus","Superior gemellus","Gluteus minimus","Piriformis"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior gemellus', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris, after arising from the sacral plexus (L4, L5, S1), descends deep to the sciatic nerve and gives a branch to the inferior gemellus as well as to quadratus femoris. The superior gemellus receives the nerve to obturator internus, and gluteus minimus receives the superior gluteal nerve.'),
('30b6f48f-4213-5703-b79d-c3c52be749e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to obturator internus also supplies which muscle?', '["Inferior gemellus","Superior gemellus","Quadratus femoris","Gluteus maximus","Piriformis"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gemellus', 'The nerve to obturator internus (L5-S2) passes through the lesser sciatic foramen and supplies both obturator internus and the superior gemellus. The inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris share the nerve to quadratus femoris, and gluteus maximus receives the inferior gluteal nerve.'),
('5a982879-47ca-514e-8af3-10d772598e93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is most commonly injured in posterior hip dislocation?', '["Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve', 'In posterior dislocation of the hip, the femoral head lies directly against the sciatic nerve as it emerges from the greater sciatic foramen, so this nerve is at greatest risk. The femoral, obturator, and gluteal nerves lie too far from the dislocation path to be commonly injured.'),
('8a67e80c-164a-5060-8992-87881dcce3b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is injured in fibular neck fracture?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Common peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve', 'The common peroneal nerve winds directly around the neck of the fibula, so a fracture at this site readily damages it, producing foot drop and loss of eversion. The deep and superficial peroneal branches lie distal to the neck, and the tibial and saphenous nerves are on the opposite side of the leg.'),
('3ee61ea9-ba85-539d-9fd0-c024e8758302'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Foot drop is caused by injury to which nerve?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve', 'Foot drop with inability to dorsiflex results from common peroneal nerve injury because its deep peroneal branch innervates the anterior compartment muscles, the dorsiflexors of the ankle. Injury to the sciatic nerve can also weaken dorsiflexion but only higher in the limb, whereas the tibial, femoral, and obturator nerves do not produce this pattern.'),
('b3dacef3-2006-5893-b220-c78d337a3635'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Inability to evert the foot is due to injury of which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve', 'The superficial peroneal nerve innervates peroneus longus and peroneus brevis, the principal evertors of the foot, so its injury abolishes eversion. The deep peroneal nerve supplies the dorsiflexors, and the tibial, saphenous, and sural nerves do not innervate the evertors.'),
('a8733ac4-c89b-5a7a-894b-5107656a9500'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Inversion of the foot is lost in injury to which nerve?', '["Sciatic nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The tibial nerve innervates tibialis posterior, the principal inverter and plantar flexor of the foot, so tibial nerve injury markedly weakens or abolishes inversion. Common peroneal injury produces foot drop from loss of dorsiflexors rather than loss of inversion, and the saphenous, femoral, and sciatic options do not give this isolated deficit.'),
('02cf4d0b-7896-51a0-b994-00f3d5af7c11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes loss of plantar flexion?', '["Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The tibial nerve innervates the posterior leg muscles, including gastrocnemius and soleus, which are the chief plantar flexors of the ankle. Injury to the tibial nerve abolishes plantar flexion, whereas damage to the common peroneal nerve causes foot drop with loss of dorsiflexion.'),
('a5b8a9d4-c2d9-50da-b4b0-8c7e58b48739'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral nerve is derived from which plexus?', '["Pudendal plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus","Coccygeal plexus","Brachial plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar plexus', 'The femoral nerve arises from the posterior divisions of the L2, L3, and L4 roots within the lumbar plexus. It innervates the flexors of the hip and extensors of the knee and supplies the skin of the anterior thigh.'),
('12617598-1663-5b21-8435-81019c2650fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator nerve is derived from which plexus?', '["Coccygeal plexus","Brachial plexus","Pudendal plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar plexus', 'The obturator nerve originates from the anterior divisions of L2, L3, and L4 in the lumbar plexus and leaves the pelvis through the obturator canal. It supplies the adductor muscles of the medial thigh and the skin over the medial thigh.'),
('6ea7341e-6b05-5398-bfa2-833af8976019'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the adductor magnus?', '["Obturator nerve and sciatic nerve","Sciatic nerve alone","Obturator nerve alone","Femoral nerve alone","None"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve and sciatic nerve', 'Adductor magnus receives dual innervation: its adductor portion comes from the posterior division of the obturator nerve and its hamstring portion comes from the tibial division of the sciatic nerve. No single nerve supplies the entire muscle.'),
('cc83c59f-4999-59ec-a2e1-baf6217c9390'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The saphenous nerve accompanies which artery?', '["Popliteal artery","Posterior tibial artery","Femoral artery","Anterior tibial artery","Dorsalis pedis artery"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral artery', 'The saphenous nerve, the terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve, travels within the adductor canal together with the femoral artery. It emerges between sartorius and gracilis to supply the skin of the medial leg and foot.'),
('4ccc9108-0226-552d-9bde-3af19a137866'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sural nerve accompanies which vein?', '["Femoral vein","Great saphenous vein","None","Popliteal vein","Small saphenous vein"]'::jsonb, 'Small saphenous vein', 'The sural nerve descends in the posterior leg closely applied to the small saphenous vein. Formed by medial and lateral sural branches, it passes behind the lateral malleolus to supply the lateral side of the foot.'),
('715b92ac-2124-53fe-ac1f-480f0ad08617'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial plantar nerve accompanies which artery?', '["Medial plantar artery","None","Dorsalis pedis artery","Lateral plantar artery","Posterior tibial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Medial plantar artery', 'The medial plantar nerve runs forward on the medial side of the sole, accompanying the medial plantar artery, a terminal branch of the posterior tibial artery. It supplies the medial intrinsic muscles and the skin of the medial sole.'),
('d9c94e1c-5105-5ce0-9457-1fd9bc505f1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral plantar nerve accompanies which artery?', '["Lateral plantar artery","None","Medial plantar artery","Posterior tibial artery","Dorsalis pedis artery"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar artery', 'The lateral plantar nerve crosses the sole with the lateral plantar artery, the other terminal branch of the posterior tibial artery. It supplies the lateral part of the sole and most of the intrinsic muscles of the foot.'),
('e98794e4-94fd-56d6-99d0-7861d180e7b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The deep peroneal nerve accompanies which artery?', '["Posterior tibial artery","Femoral artery","Dorsalis pedis artery","Peroneal artery","Anterior tibial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior tibial artery', 'The deep peroneal nerve runs in the anterior compartment of the leg in company with the anterior tibial artery. It supplies tibialis anterior and the extensor muscles and ends by innervating the skin of the first web space.'),
('d6269ada-70a4-50a0-b43b-c95b35f1d1fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial peroneal nerve pierces which fascia?', '["None","Popliteal fascia","Fascia lata","Deep fascia of leg","Plantar fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia of leg', 'The superficial peroneal nerve pierces the deep fascia of the leg in its distal third to become subcutaneous. After supplying the peroneus longus and brevis, it gives cutaneous branches to the dorsum of the foot.'),
('8dc0b17c-0df4-5e01-9655-f08479315a34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The iliohypogastric nerve supplies which muscle?', '["External oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transversus abdominis and internal oblique","Rectus abdominis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psoas major leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transversus abdominis and internal oblique', 'The iliohypogastric nerve supplies the transversus abdominis and internal oblique muscles. It also gives sensory branches to the skin of the suprapubic and gluteal regions.'),
('bb5d9307-f299-58b0-a7ea-1ba18d78a2ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ilioinguinal nerve supplies which muscle?', '["External oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectus abdominis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transversus abdominis and internal oblique","Psoas major without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transversus abdominis and internal oblique', 'The ilioinguinal nerve innervates the transversus abdominis and internal oblique before passing through the inguinal canal. It then supplies sensory branches to the upper medial thigh and external genitalia.'),
('88bf18e6-31df-5b7b-ab2c-e92cbc180fea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The genitofemoral nerve supplies which muscle?', '["Cremaster","None","Medial pterygoid","Lateral pterygoid","Levator ani"]'::jsonb, 'Cremaster', 'The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve supplies the cremaster muscle and mediates the cremasteric reflex. The femoral branch is purely cutaneous to the upper anterior thigh.'),
('a310887a-859c-57d5-ba9f-a767e30c5535'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk in inguinal hernia repair?', '["Ilioinguinal nerve","Genitofemoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Ilioinguinal nerve', 'The ilioinguinal nerve lies within the inguinal canal and is vulnerable to injury or entrapment during herniorrhaphy. Damage causes numbness over the medial upper thigh and the root of the scrotum or labium majus.'),
('5fcd4441-925d-5622-a373-92a5d78fc549'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve passes through the inguinal canal?', '["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve","Obturator nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Ilioinguinal nerve', 'The ilioinguinal nerve traverses the inguinal canal, lying anterior to the spermatic cord or round ligament. It emerges through the superficial inguinal ring to supply neighboring skin of the thigh and external genitalia.'),
('18849507-9489-526c-9094-ff89757fd612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the anterior thigh?', '["Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Saphenous nerve","Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'The skin of the anterior thigh is innervated by the anterior cutaneous branches of the femoral nerve. The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the lateral thigh, while the obturator nerve serves the medial thigh.'),
('a7184e82-f9e3-59ed-bd16-a5d19bdd12b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the medial thigh?', '["Saphenous nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'A cutaneous branch of the obturator nerve supplies the skin over the medial aspect of the thigh. The femoral nerve and lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh serve the anterior and lateral thigh respectively.'),
('f42e4b10-85e5-5974-8c66-d4bdee5e9721'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies which area?', '["Foot resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior thigh and popliteal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior thigh and popliteal fossa', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh arises from the sacral plexus and runs deep to the gluteus maximus. It is purely sensory, supplying the skin of the posterior thigh and popliteal fossa.'),
('29276de2-de13-5877-bc74-316d74e99a7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to extend the knee?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Knee extension is performed by the quadriceps femoris, which is innervated by the femoral nerve. Asking the patient to extend the knee therefore evaluates femoral nerve function.'),
('d1580e6b-eb79-50da-b025-8fe64f986aa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to flex the knee?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve', 'Knee flexion is produced by the hamstring muscles, all of which are innervated by the sciatic nerve. Weakness of knee flexion therefore indicates sciatic nerve dysfunction.'),
('817ccb67-ce34-5bad-a9dd-bdc482be4bfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to abduct the thigh?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'Thigh abduction is carried out by the gluteus medius and minimus, which are supplied by the superior gluteal nerve. Injury to this nerve produces a Trendelenburg gait.'),
('6af5fe77-3a72-55d2-aac6-26cd2fb4fac7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to adduct the thigh?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'The adductor muscles of the medial thigh are innervated by the obturator nerve. Testing resisted thigh adduction evaluates obturator nerve function.'),
('a0d193aa-b429-5c2d-b3bb-84630fa0bc5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to extend the thigh at the hip?', '["Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior gluteal nerve', 'The gluteus maximus, the chief extensor of the hip, is innervated by the inferior gluteal nerve. Weakness of hip extension impairs rising from a chair and climbing stairs.'),
('91a029fc-d52c-505a-a298-4e08e9fdf476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to dorsiflex the foot?', '["Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Ankle dorsiflexion is performed by the tibialis anterior and the other muscles of the anterior compartment, supplied by the deep peroneal nerve. Injury to this nerve produces foot drop.'),
('fc47834e-55f5-56fc-8c37-1e24a837e716'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to plantarflex the foot?', '["Sural nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Plantar flexion is performed by the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles, which are innervated by the tibial nerve. Testing plantar flexion therefore assesses tibial nerve function.'),
('d6cf232c-c7c1-5e1d-9251-63255309d52c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to evert the foot?', '["Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve', 'Foot eversion is produced by the peroneus longus and brevis, which are supplied by the superficial peroneal nerve. Weakness of eversion indicates injury to this nerve.'),
('35b2bb00-7d28-57ca-930b-7f8a264afb32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is tested by asking the patient to invert the foot?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Foot inversion is mainly performed by the tibialis posterior, supplied by the tibial nerve, with assistance from the deep peroneal nerve via tibialis anterior. Testing inversion therefore primarily evaluates the tibial nerve.'),
('d6ef7d74-3f84-549a-9dc4-c537d7c78282'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral nerve block provides anesthesia to which area?', '["Lateral thigh","Gluteal region","Posterior thigh","Foot","Anterior thigh and medial leg"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior thigh and medial leg', 'A femoral nerve block anesthetizes the anterior thigh through the femoral nerve and the medial side of the leg through the saphenous nerve. The lateral thigh, posterior thigh, and gluteal region remain outside its territory.'),
('2d32c1c1-f975-504e-93e6-8e4cc3c1d9a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve block provides anesthesia to which area?', '["Anterior thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior thigh and most of leg below knee","Gluteal region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior thigh and most of leg below knee', 'The sciatic nerve supplies the hamstrings of the posterior thigh and, through its tibial and common peroneal divisions, most of the leg and foot below the knee. Its block therefore produces anesthesia of the posterior thigh and most of the leg below the knee.'),
('8cf695be-36cb-557d-99e5-a26fec648a66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator nerve block provides anesthesia to which area?', '["Knee joint","Medial thigh","Posterior thigh","Lateral thigh","Anterior thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Medial thigh', 'The obturator nerve block anesthetizes the medial thigh, the cutaneous territory of the obturator nerve. It can also relieve pain arising from the hip joint in the adductor region.'),
('707c8b2e-8726-5e3d-b58c-4324e85291a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the knee joint?', '["All of the above","None","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The knee joint receives branches from the femoral nerve, the obturator nerve, and the tibial division of the sciatic nerve. Because all three listed nerves contribute, the combined answer should be selected.'),
('89a42090-d170-5180-b21e-7119d6742bd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the hip joint?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral nerve only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sciatic nerve only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral nerve obturator nerve and nerve to quadratus femoris"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve obturator nerve and nerve to quadratus femoris', 'Branches of the femoral nerve, obturator nerve, and nerve to quadratus femoris, together with sciatic contributions, innervate the hip joint. The combined answer names the three principal sources of hip joint supply.'),
('148ec667-0493-5e68-99ae-153fb167c83c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial plantar nerve is a continuation of which nerve?', '["Saphenous nerve","Femoral nerve","Sural nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The medial plantar nerve is the larger terminal branch of the tibial nerve, arising deep to the flexor retinaculum behind the medial malleolus. It supplies the medial intrinsic muscles and the skin of the medial sole.'),
('bb62704a-4cba-5dbe-8eb6-d08d4b08073f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral plantar nerve is the smaller terminal branch of which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The lateral plantar nerve is the smaller terminal branch of the tibial nerve, also arising deep to the flexor retinaculum. It supplies most of the intrinsic muscles of the foot and the lateral sole.'),
('de68370c-f812-583f-ab0c-b852889c1ff6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve supply which area?', '["Skin of the heel","Skin of the lateral foot","Skin of the dorsum of foot","Skin of the medial foot","Skin of the toes"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the heel', 'Medial and lateral calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve supply the skin of the heel. They arise proximal to the point where the tibial nerve divides into the medial and lateral plantar branches.'),
('8f825bdb-3a3f-5855-a688-94fc1bcbe769'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal digital nerves of the foot are derived from which nerves?', '["Superficial peroneal and deep peroneal","Saphenous nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Sural nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal and deep peroneal', 'Most dorsal digital nerves to the toes derive from the superficial peroneal nerve, while the deep peroneal nerve supplies the first web space. The sural and saphenous nerves contribute only along the borders of the foot.'),
('082eb89a-ab9f-5e95-b96b-8dd6623ac6b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The plantar digital nerves are derived from which nerves?', '["Sural nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Medial and lateral plantar nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral plantar nerves', 'The plantar digital nerves arise as terminal branches of the medial and lateral plantar nerves. They supply the skin of the sole and the plantar surface of the toes.'),
('57cd2a6b-ae51-58b4-9ae2-c4ab54f6d6ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral dorsal cutaneous nerve of the foot is a continuation of which nerve?', '["Superficial peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve","Deep peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sural nerve', 'The lateral dorsal cutaneous nerve of the foot is the continuation of the sural nerve. It supplies the skin of the lateral border of the foot and the lateral side of the fifth toe.'),
('93c063f8-e0e7-501c-b244-5204cfd8e1b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The saphenous nerve extends distally to which level?', '["Ankle joint","Toes","Heel","Medial malleolus","Lateral malleolus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial malleolus', 'The saphenous nerve descends with the long saphenous vein to reach the medial malleolus and then continues onto the medial border of the foot. It represents the terminal sensory field of the femoral nerve.'),
('38fbc0ef-3357-5e13-b165-2bb8f18e483d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the dorsum of the foot?', '["Superficial peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve', 'The superficial peroneal nerve gives medial and intermediate dorsal cutaneous branches that supply most of the dorsum of the foot. The deep peroneal nerve is limited to the first web space.'),
('66b50ad0-9f66-57d8-99a1-28f5d5dbcccd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the first web space of the foot?', '["Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'The deep peroneal nerve, after supplying the anterior leg muscles, terminates by innervating the skin of the first web space between the great and second toes. This is its only cutaneous territory in the foot.'),
('6c5fc1c8-7e03-5e43-90af-8bd8bffee703'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial plantar nerve supplies the skin of which area?', '["Dorsum of foot","Lateral sole and lateral 1.5 toes","Lateral foot","Heel","Medial sole and medial 3.5 toes"]'::jsonb, 'Medial sole and medial 3.5 toes', 'The medial plantar nerve supplies the skin of the medial sole and the medial three and a half toes. Its distribution parallels that of the median nerve in the hand.'),
('15b194bd-fbf0-52bf-ac29-60a01d9f781d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral plantar nerve supplies the skin of which area?', '["Heel","Lateral sole and lateral 1.5 toes","Medial foot","Medial sole and medial 3.5 toes","Dorsum of foot"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral sole and lateral 1.5 toes', 'The lateral plantar nerve supplies the skin of the lateral sole and the lateral one and a half toes. Its distribution parallels that of the ulnar nerve in the hand.'),
('e44bb822-79eb-5ee9-a2b8-5144f57a6483'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the plantar interossei?', '["Medial plantar nerve","Tibial nerve","Lateral plantar nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar nerve', 'The three plantar interossei are innervated by the deep branch of the lateral plantar nerve. They adduct the toes toward the axis of the second digit.'),
('960dd07b-7960-559b-ace4-012d7c85db62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the dorsal interossei of the foot?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Lateral plantar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar nerve', 'All four dorsal interossei of the foot are supplied by the lateral plantar nerve. They abduct the toes away from the axis of the second digit.'),
('b76fc865-bf83-5082-af4c-452724412939'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the lumbricals of the foot?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Tibial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral plantar nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial plantar nerve only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial plantar nerve (first) and lateral plantar nerve (second to fourth)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial plantar nerve (first) and lateral plantar nerve (second to fourth)', 'The first lumbrical of the foot is innervated by the medial plantar nerve, whereas the second, third, and fourth lumbricals are supplied by the lateral plantar nerve. This split supply resembles the pattern in the hand.'),
('121818f3-9167-5053-acaf-19e8e22aecac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the quadratus plantae?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Lateral plantar nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar nerve', 'The quadratus plantae is innervated by the lateral plantar nerve. It hooks the flexor digitorum longus tendons and assists flexion of the lateral four toes.'),
('1bd52527-9649-53a8-9411-861bcf913b6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The flexor digiti minimi brevis of the foot is supplied by which nerve?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Lateral plantar nerve","Tibial nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar nerve', 'The flexor digiti minimi brevis of the foot is innervated by the lateral plantar nerve. Its counterpart, the flexor hallucis brevis, is supplied by the medial plantar nerve.'),
('97702c59-aff4-57da-95eb-fe25af6627b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The abductor digiti minimi of the foot is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Lateral plantar nerve","Sural nerve","Medial plantar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar nerve', 'The abductor digiti minimi of the foot is innervated by the lateral plantar nerve. It abducts the fifth toe and lies along the lateral border of the sole.'),
('58b44bba-ef0e-5250-8fb2-11b02f81d96e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The adductor hallucis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Lateral plantar nerve","Tibial nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral plantar nerve', 'The adductor hallucis is innervated by the deep branch of the lateral plantar nerve. It adducts the great toe and helps maintain the transverse arch of the foot.'),
('593e2642-7ef5-5aab-a441-8b4f29eb43d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The flexor hallucis brevis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Lateral plantar nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Common peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial plantar nerve', 'Flexor hallucis brevis lies in the first layer of the sole and is supplied by the medial plantar nerve (S2-S3), a terminal branch of the tibial nerve. The lateral plantar nerve supplies deeper lateral muscles such as the interossei and abductor digiti minimi. The tibial nerve gives origin to the plantar nerves, whereas the sural and common peroneal nerves do not reach the muscles of the sole.'),
('fd8304eb-6cb1-5f81-8c6b-cd8e7200f203'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The abductor hallucis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Sural nerve","Lateral plantar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial plantar nerve', 'The abductor hallucis is the most medial muscle of the first layer of the sole, innervated by the medial plantar nerve (S2-S3). The lateral plantar nerve serves lateral and deeper plantar muscles. The tibial nerve supplies the posterior compartment of the leg and the plantar nerves, not the abductor hallucis directly.'),
('09718926-caa7-5b1d-b883-b018eca715f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The flexor digitorum brevis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Sural nerve","Medial plantar nerve","Lateral plantar nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial plantar nerve', 'Flexor digitorum brevis is a first-layer muscle of the sole supplied by the medial plantar nerve (S2-S3). The lateral plantar nerve innervates the intrinsic muscles of the lateral sole, while the tibial nerve supplies posterior leg muscles. The sural nerve is purely cutaneous, and the common peroneal nerve innervates extensor and peroneal muscles.'),
('178ea188-830b-53da-af96-19415db9e1ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The extensor digitorum brevis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Saphenous nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Extensor digitorum brevis is a dorsal muscle of the foot innervated by the deep peroneal nerve (L5-S1). The superficial peroneal nerve supplies the peroneus longus and brevis, the sural nerve is cutaneous, and the saphenous nerve is a purely cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve.'),
('ccd7cec8-1f67-5695-8f42-d05e7b6dab1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The extensor hallucis brevis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Extensor hallucis brevis shares the deep peroneal nerve supply with the rest of the dorsal foot muscles. The tibial nerve serves posterior leg and plantar muscles, while the superficial peroneal nerve goes to the peroneal compartment. The saphenous nerve is cutaneous only.'),
('49582d6e-cfbd-5842-a044-24c8bf42a2b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The peroneus tertius is supplied by which nerve?', '["Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Peroneus tertius belongs to the anterior compartment of the leg and is innervated by the deep peroneal nerve (L5-S1). The superficial peroneal nerve supplies the lateral compartment muscles. The tibial nerve serves the posterior compartment, and the sural and saphenous nerves are cutaneous.'),
('1bdb126d-37c8-51c1-93da-5f10527edd8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The peroneus longus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Deep peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve', 'Peroneus longus forms the lateral compartment of the leg and is supplied by the superficial peroneal nerve (L5-S2). The deep peroneal nerve innervates anterior compartment muscles, the tibial nerve serves the posterior compartment, and the sural and saphenous nerves are cutaneous.'),
('f2761306-d89a-5190-ac9e-903282153a7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The peroneus brevis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve', 'Peroneus brevis is innervated by the superficial peroneal nerve together with peroneus longus. The deep peroneal nerve supplies anterior compartment muscles, the tibial nerve innervates the posterior compartment, and the sural and saphenous nerves provide only skin sensation.'),
('5937256a-4bc1-5cb1-925f-1656acd6cee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibialis anterior is supplied by which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Tibialis anterior is the key dorsiflexor and inverter of the foot, supplied by the deep peroneal nerve (L4-L5). The superficial peroneal nerve innervates the peroneal muscles, the tibial nerve the posterior compartment, and the sural and saphenous nerves are cutaneous.'),
('1cb539d5-8114-5fb0-a719-901ff24cb06b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibialis posterior is supplied by which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Tibialis posterior lies in the deep posterior compartment of the leg and is innervated by the tibial nerve (L4-L5). The deep peroneal nerve supplies anterior compartment dorsiflexors, the superficial peroneal nerve the lateral compartment, and the sural nerve is cutaneous.'),
('6fa3cd92-4485-5c05-b402-5e6af597ac4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The extensor hallucis longus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Extensor hallucis longus is an anterior compartment muscle supplied by the deep peroneal nerve (L4-L5). The tibial nerve innervates the posterior compartment, the superficial peroneal nerve the lateral compartment, and the sural and saphenous nerves are cutaneous.'),
('64fc8bc1-21d4-5a77-b1d4-0f2c3fa68a7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The extensor digitorum longus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve', 'Extensor digitorum longus is innervated by the deep peroneal nerve (L4-S1) like the other anterior compartment muscles. The superficial peroneal nerve serves peroneus longus and brevis, the tibial nerve supplies the posterior compartment, and the sural and saphenous nerves are cutaneous.'),
('d1d145b1-faaf-5385-b93d-c079edd78d94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The flexor hallucis longus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Flexor hallucis longus is a muscle of the deep posterior compartment of the leg supplied by the tibial nerve (S2-S3). The deep and superficial peroneal nerves innervate the anterior and lateral compartments respectively. The sural nerve is cutaneous, and the common peroneal nerve is not a direct supply to posterior compartment muscles.'),
('796343ba-e9c0-5832-9978-fc1866fd7789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The flexor digitorum longus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Deep peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Flexor digitorum longus lies in the deep posterior compartment and is innervated by the tibial nerve (S2-S3). The common peroneal nerve divides into the deep and superficial peroneal branches for the anterior and lateral compartments. The sural nerve is cutaneous.'),
('3281cc06-e212-5574-aa5c-220abcc822f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The gastrocnemius is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Gastrocnemius is innervated by the tibial nerve (S1-S2), which also serves the other superficial posterior compartment muscles. The deep and superficial peroneal nerves supply the anterior and lateral compartments, and the sural nerve is a cutaneous branch.'),
('28d49cf2-b852-5965-b6a0-7d1ef775006a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The soleus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Soleus is supplied by the tibial nerve (S1-S2) along with the other calf muscles. The deep peroneal nerve innervates the anterior compartment and the superficial peroneal nerve the peroneal muscles. The sural nerve is purely cutaneous and the common peroneal nerve does not directly supply the soleus.'),
('303fa78b-d82a-5947-80c3-f7c3583b14cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The plantaris is supplied by which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Plantaris is a small posterior compartment muscle innervated by the tibial nerve (S1-S2). The deep and superficial peroneal nerves serve the anterior and lateral compartments of the leg, while the sural nerve is only cutaneous.'),
('2b7e5e52-6d05-5841-b174-5dd78fcafff7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The popliteus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Popliteus is a deep posterior compartment muscle that unlocks the knee and is supplied by the tibial nerve (L4-S1). The common peroneal nerve separates from the tibial nerve above the popliteus. The deep and superficial peroneal nerves supply the other leg compartments, and the sural nerve is cutaneous.'),
('b8be41ba-d2aa-5c74-907d-3e4b2610cba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sartorius is supplied by which nerve?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Sartorius is a strap-like anterior thigh muscle innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L3). The obturator nerve supplies the adductors, the sciatic nerve the hamstrings, and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves supply the gluteal muscles.'),
('e3930eb8-2eab-5155-9e80-7f32f6c05c95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The quadriceps femoris is supplied by which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'All heads of quadriceps femoris are innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4). The obturator nerve serves the medial thigh adductors, the sciatic nerve the posterior thigh muscles, and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves supply the gluteal region.'),
('eab7a152-1765-5650-a664-1cea375a6c61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The rectus femoris is supplied by which nerve?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Rectus femoris is one of the four heads of the quadriceps and is innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4). The obturator nerve supplies the adductor compartment, the sciatic nerve the hamstrings, and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves serve the gluteal muscles.'),
('f37aba69-fefa-575f-8bef-0e4d4b1046ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The vastus lateralis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Vastus lateralis is part of the quadriceps group supplied by the femoral nerve (L2-L4). The obturator nerve innervates the adductors, the sciatic nerve the posterior thigh, and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves the gluteal muscles.'),
('8623fc01-712c-55e4-8e4e-e9c1a15358db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The vastus medialis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Vastus medialis is a quadriceps head innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4). The obturator nerve serves the adductor muscles, the inferior gluteal nerve the gluteus maximus, the superior gluteal nerve the gluteus medius and minimus, and the sciatic nerve the hamstrings.'),
('7f5996f5-43f8-56d5-87fd-c745d93ef332'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The vastus intermedius is supplied by which nerve?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Vastus intermedius, together with the other quadriceps heads, is supplied by the femoral nerve (L2-L4). The sciatic nerve innervates the hamstrings, the obturator nerve the adductors, and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves supply the gluteal region.'),
('72bc9581-a42e-5938-bdca-9132b1e708bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The articularis genu is supplied by which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Articularis genu is a small muscle that retracts the suprapatellar bursa during extension and is innervated by the femoral nerve, like the quadriceps. The sciatic nerve supplies the hamstrings, the obturator nerve the adductors, and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves supply the gluteal muscles.'),
('ccf4f64b-fdbd-5381-b35c-ff453332f851'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pectineus is mainly supplied by which nerve?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'Pectineus is chiefly supplied by the femoral nerve (L2-L3), with a variable accessory branch from the obturator nerve, so the femoral nerve is its principal innervation. The sciatic nerve serves the hamstrings, and the superior and inferior gluteal nerves supply the gluteal muscles.'),
('5ca66f6d-5e29-53f8-b1c1-dee0c8040846'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The gracilis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'Gracilis is a medial thigh adductor innervated by the obturator nerve (L2-L3). The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh muscles, the sciatic nerve the posterior thigh, and the gluteal nerves supply the buttock muscles.'),
('b3d325d4-dba9-5110-8aa6-5b5a025adfd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The adductor longus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'Adductor longus is part of the medial compartment of the thigh and is innervated by the obturator nerve (L2-L4). The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh, the sciatic nerve the posterior thigh, and the inferior gluteal nerve the gluteus maximus.'),
('2d0b5340-4ff4-5214-966b-44d6857a02b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The adductor brevis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'Adductor brevis lies in the medial compartment of the thigh and is supplied by the obturator nerve (L2-L4). The femoral nerve innervates the anterior thigh muscles, the sciatic nerve the hamstrings, and the superior gluteal nerve the abductors of the hip.'),
('4456be11-1863-54e3-86da-fe7823d7728c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The adductor magnus (adductor part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'The adductor part of adductor magnus is supplied by the obturator nerve (L2-L4), while the hamstring part is innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve. The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh and the gluteal nerves supply the buttock muscles.'),
('e81c0b7c-829b-5d61-a589-2b9ddb7c2633'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The adductor magnus (hamstring part) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve (tibial division)"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve (tibial division)', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus is innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve, whereas the adductor part is supplied by the obturator nerve. The femoral nerve serves the anterior thigh and the inferior gluteal nerve supplies the gluteus maximus.'),
('61cb6964-6052-54c6-8cfe-b9b8da6d05dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator externus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'Obturator externus is a muscle of the medial thigh that is innervated by the obturator nerve (L3-L4) despite lying deep near the hip joint. The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh, the sciatic nerve the posterior thigh, and the gluteal nerves the buttock muscles.'),
('08067dd1-f678-5a8f-8a5f-97981b9f417d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The biceps femoris (long head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Common peroneal division of sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Tibial division of sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial division of sciatic nerve', 'The long head of biceps femoris is a hamstring supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve (L5-S2). The short head is the exception, being innervated by the common peroneal division. The obturator nerve supplies the adductors and the superior gluteal nerve the abductors.'),
('b8208553-8bee-58db-ac20-dd567c2188a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The biceps femoris (short head) is supplied by which nerve?', '["Common peroneal division of sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Tibial division of sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal division of sciatic nerve', 'The short head of biceps femoris is the only hamstring innervated by the common peroneal division of the sciatic nerve (L5-S2). The tibial division supplies the long head, semitendinosus, semimembranosus and the hamstring part of adductor magnus. The obturator nerve serves the adductors.'),
('5bd103e2-2127-5314-9ac6-73a5bd71b530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The semitendinosus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Tibial division of sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Common peroneal division of sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial division of sciatic nerve', 'Semitendinosus is a hamstring innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve (L5-S2). The common peroneal division supplies the short head of biceps femoris, the obturator nerve the adductors, and the superior gluteal nerve the hip abductors.'),
('61a54331-3a24-50bb-af56-6b1fae699937'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The semimembranosus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Tibial division of sciatic nerve","Common peroneal division of sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial division of sciatic nerve', 'Semimembranosus is a hamstring supplied by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve (L5-S2). The common peroneal division serves only the short head of biceps femoris, while the obturator nerve innervates the adductors and the superior gluteal nerve the gluteal abductors.'),
('f17e1737-fdb8-5c53-b1c4-25746ac41019'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The gluteus maximus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior gluteal nerve', 'Gluteus maximus is the main extensor of the hip and is innervated by the inferior gluteal nerve (L5-S2). The superior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and tensor fascia lata. The femoral nerve serves the anterior thigh and the obturator nerve the adductors.'),
('317797b2-0322-5474-b7ac-f4def3b11672'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The gluteus medius is supplied by which nerve?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'Gluteus medius is an abductor and medial rotator of the hip supplied by the superior gluteal nerve (L4-S1). The inferior gluteal nerve innervates gluteus maximus only, the femoral nerve the anterior thigh, and the sciatic nerve the hamstrings.'),
('29cd564a-2422-5ca5-b8d4-abf176251225'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The gluteus minimus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'Gluteus minimus is innervated by the superior gluteal nerve along with gluteus medius and tensor fascia lata. The inferior gluteal nerve supplies only gluteus maximus, the obturator nerve the adductors, the femoral nerve the anterior thigh, and the sciatic nerve the posterior thigh.'),
('ad3c6072-82ab-5cef-be83-43a9bf86cc9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The piriformis is supplied by which nerve?', '["Nerve to piriformis (S1-S2)","Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to piriformis (S1-S2)', 'Piriformis is supplied by the nerve to piriformis arising from S1-S2, which enters its anterior surface. The superior gluteal nerve supplies the gluteal abductors, the inferior gluteal nerve the gluteus maximus, and the sacral plexus branches pass above and below the piriformis.'),
('a7c44f58-ffe2-5f34-aa57-0391d5879608'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator internus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Nerve to obturator internus (L5-S2)","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to obturator internus (L5-S2)', 'Obturator internus is innervated by the nerve to obturator internus (L5-S2), which also supplies the superior gemellus. The inferior gluteal nerve serves gluteus maximus, the femoral nerve the anterior thigh, and the superior gluteal nerve the gluteal abductors.'),
('2dcaabef-0509-5c6b-b846-596432a709ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior gemellus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Nerve to obturator internus","Nerve to quadratus femoris","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to obturator internus', 'Superior gemellus is innervated by the nerve to obturator internus, as it lies above the obturator internus tendon. The nerve to quadratus femoris supplies the inferior gemellus, quadratus femoris and obturator externus. The superior and inferior gluteal nerves serve the gluteal muscles.'),
('1a4f77e9-2684-5f93-8dee-1dcef76be341'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The inferior gemellus is supplied by which nerve?', '["Nerve to obturator internus","Inferior gluteal nerve","Nerve to quadratus femoris","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to quadratus femoris', 'Inferior gemellus is innervated by the nerve to quadratus femoris (L4-S1), which also supplies quadratus femoris and often obturator externus. The nerve to obturator internus serves obturator internus and the superior gemellus, while the gluteal nerves supply the gluteal muscles.'),
('07da0b18-458e-53b8-9b5c-9113818566a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The quadratus femoris is supplied by which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4-S1)","Nerve to obturator internus"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4-S1)', 'Quadratus femoris is supplied by the nerve to quadratus femoris (L4-S1), which descends deep to the gemelli. The nerve to obturator internus supplies obturator internus and superior gemellus. The superior gluteal nerve serves the gluteal abductors and the inferior gluteal nerve the gluteus maximus.'),
('efdbf807-d8f7-5a70-a69c-f7e70dd979f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tensor fascia lata is supplied by which nerve?', '["Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'Tensor fascia lata is an abductor and medial rotator of the hip innervated by the superior gluteal nerve (L4-S1). The inferior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus maximus, the femoral nerve the anterior thigh, the obturator nerve the adductors, and the sciatic nerve the hamstrings.'),
('040fe7f7-7e0a-5bda-bfd9-e6aee7f416b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve roots contribute to the lumbar plexus?', '["L2-L5","L5-S3","C5-T1","L1-L4","L4-S3"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L4', 'The lumbar plexus is formed from the anterior rami of L1-L4, with contributions from the T12 subcostal nerve in some individuals. The sacral plexus arises from L4-S4 and the brachial plexus from C5-T1. L2-L5 and L5-S3 do not define the lumbar plexus.'),
('1e285d81-ceab-5f3d-9eef-d36aa828a975'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve roots contribute to the sacral plexus?', '["S1-S4","L1-L4","C5-T1","L5-S3","L4-S4"]'::jsonb, 'L4-S4', 'The sacral plexus is formed by the anterior rami of L4-S4, including the lumbosacral trunk from L4-L5. The lumbar plexus arises from L1-L4, while L5-S3 only reflects the sciatic nerve root origins and S1-S4 is too low.'),
('9d5f59fe-f05f-544f-a759-8f37ce64a503'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lumbosacral trunk is formed by which nerve roots?', '["L5-S1","L3-L4","S1-S2","L4-L5","L5-S2"]'::jsonb, 'L4-L5', 'The lumbosacral trunk is formed by the anterior ramus of L5 joining part of L4, connecting the lumbar plexus to the sacral plexus. It descends over the ala of the sacrum. L5-S1, L3-L4, S1-S2 and L5-S2 do not describe the trunk constituents.'),
('bbfb2ff7-f2f1-575d-907d-2ae9ba22e55f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pudendal nerve arises from which nerve roots?', '["S1-S3","L2-L4","L5-S3","L4-S1","S2-S4"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4', 'The pudendal nerve arises from the anterior rami of S2-S4 and is the main nerve of the perineum. It accompanies the internal pudendal vessels through the pudendal canal. S1-S3 and L4-S1 overlap several sacral roots, and L2-L4 belong to the lumbar plexus.'),
('981e5e8b-578a-5695-92b0-4f69d1b917cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pudendal nerve exits the pelvis through which foramen?', '["Lesser sciatic foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","Greater sciatic foramen and then lesser sciatic foramen","Obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","Suprapiriform foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infrapiriform foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater sciatic foramen and then lesser sciatic foramen', 'The pudendal nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis, then re-enters the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen to reach the pudendal canal. The obturator foramen transmits the obturator nerve, and the suprapiriform and infrapiriform foramina relate to the superior and inferior gluteal neurovascular bundles.'),
('83d13895-bb8e-51d3-acf8-47136ce9a216'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pudendal nerve supplies which muscles?', '["Adductors resulting in decreased functional activity","Gluteal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadriceps mediated by specific receptor activation","Perineal muscles including external anal sphincter","Hamstrings leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perineal muscles including external anal sphincter', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) gives muscular branches to the perineum, including the external anal sphincter. The adductors, gluteal muscles, quadriceps, and hamstrings are each innervated by other nerves (obturator, gluteal, femoral, and sciatic, respectively).'),
('584ecc11-a4a3-554e-91b4-04f9a4191266'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis is a branch of which nerve?', '["Pudendal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve","Femoral nerve","Genitofemoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis is the terminal sensory branch of the pudendal nerve, running along the dorsum of the penis. The sciatic, ilioinguinal, femoral, and genitofemoral nerves do not give off this branch.'),
('03a1be91-8d00-5be2-8970-c14f05d90597'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the scrotum?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genitofemoral nerve (genital branch) and ilioinguinal nerve","Sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obturator nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genitofemoral nerve (genital branch) and ilioinguinal nerve', 'The anterior and lateral skin of the scrotum is supplied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve and by the ilioinguinal nerve. The femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves supply different territories, so ''None'' is incorrect.'),
('ea4f421b-8236-52ba-b3f6-7488e0dd27f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The perineal branch of S4 supplies which muscle?', '["Piriformis","None","Obturator internus","Levator ani","Gluteus maximus"]'::jsonb, 'Levator ani', 'The perineal branch of S4 supplies the levator ani muscle on its pelvic surface. The piriformis, obturator internus, and gluteus maximus are supplied by other nerves, and ''None'' is incorrect.'),
('92e1f806-ee9d-58af-922c-fb38ce503b06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to the levator ani arises from which roots?', '["S3-S4","S1-S3","S2-S4","L2-L4","L4-S1"]'::jsonb, 'S3-S4', 'The nerve to the levator ani classically arises from the ventral rami of S3 and S4. The other root combinations supply different muscles of the pelvic and lower limb regions.'),
('cc4e3fab-5d34-514e-85a9-eaca50bee180'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the external anal sphincter?', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Ilioinguinal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Pudendal nerve (inferior rectal branch)","Genitofemoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve (inferior rectal branch)', 'The external anal sphincter is innervated by the inferior rectal branch of the pudendal nerve. The femoral, ilioinguinal, sciatic, and genitofemoral nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('28d70052-a7b1-5b25-bda0-12ce3561974c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the external urethral sphincter?', '["Pudendal nerve","Femoral nerve","Genitofemoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'The external urethral sphincter is innervated by the deep branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). The femoral, genitofemoral, sciatic, and ilioinguinal nerves supply other structures.'),
('93f78cd6-0f1e-5396-beff-fd3b184768b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The coccygeal plexus is formed by which nerve roots?', '["S1-S3","L4-S1","S2-S4","S4-Co1","L5-S3"]'::jsonb, 'S4-Co1', 'The coccygeal plexus is formed by the ventral rami of S4 and Co1. The other root combinations contribute to the sacral and lumbar plexuses instead.'),
('2bab1dad-d0db-52fb-82a2-aa2f0689d7df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The perforating cutaneous nerve arises from which roots?', '["L5-S2","S2-S3","L4-S1","S1-S3","L3-L5"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S3', 'The perforating cutaneous nerve arises from the posterior aspect of the sacral plexus from S2 and S3. Other root segments give rise to different cutaneous nerves of the lower limb.'),
('300ea862-4c41-536f-866c-6acf7e934231'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The perforating cutaneous nerve supplies which area?', '["Skin of the foot resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin of the thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin of the leg mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin over the inferior part of the buttock","Skin of the perineum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin over the inferior part of the buttock', 'The perforating cutaneous nerve pierces the sacrotuberous ligament and supplies the skin over the inferior part of the buttock. The skin of the foot, thigh, leg, and perineum is supplied by other nerves.'),
('6f8682e3-6ac4-592e-81d3-613a31684b8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The perineal branch of the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve supplies which area?', '["Skin of the foot","Skin of the buttock","Skin of the leg","Skin of the abdomen","Skin of the perineum and medial thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the perineum and medial thigh', 'The perineal branch of the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve supplies the skin of the perineum and the upper medial thigh. The other options describe territories of different cutaneous nerves.'),
('5ce48f16-851b-523e-8d3f-637d971e87a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The inferior cluneal nerves are branches of which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh', 'The inferior cluneal nerves are branches of the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh. The femoral, superior gluteal, obturator, and sciatic nerves do not give off these branches.'),
('2c9402d0-6e44-5f46-9c1d-f2dced9851d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The inferior cluneal nerves supply which area?', '["Skin of the upper buttock","Skin of the leg","Skin of the perineum","Skin of the lower part of the buttock","Skin of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the lower part of the buttock', 'The inferior cluneal nerves supply the skin of the lower part of the buttock. The upper buttock, leg, perineum, and thigh are innervated by other nerves.'),
('84e0c4d3-9e1d-50dc-b195-7d05a49ab078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior cluneal nerves are branches of which nerves?', '["L1-L3 posterior rami","L5-S2 posterior rami","L4-S1 posterior rami","S2-S4 posterior rami","S1-S3 posterior rami"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L3 posterior rami', 'The superior cluneal nerves arise from the posterior rami of L1-L3 and supply the upper buttock. The other posterior ramus groups contribute to the middle cluneal nerves or other dorsal branches.'),
('f83fa914-e43b-5e28-8053-8350ab8eaa19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior cluneal nerves supply which area?', '["Skin of the lower buttock","Skin of the perineum","Skin of the leg","Skin of the thigh","Skin of the upper buttock"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the upper buttock', 'The superior cluneal nerves supply the skin of the upper buttock. The lower buttock, perineum, leg, and thigh are innervated by other cutaneous nerves.'),
('81a5d1b1-81a8-5db3-be27-f5d9a000a9e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The middle cluneal nerves are branches of which nerves?', '["S1-S3 posterior rami","L4-S1 posterior rami","L5-S2 posterior rami","S2-S4 posterior rami","L1-L3 posterior rami"]'::jsonb, 'S1-S3 posterior rami', 'The middle cluneal nerves arise from the posterior rami of S1-S3. The L1-L3 posterior rami give the superior cluneal nerves, and the inferior cluneal nerves come from the posterior femoral cutaneous nerve.'),
('293877b4-d795-5842-b9e2-a8533cd63ed2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The middle cluneal nerves supply which area?', '["Skin of the perineum","Skin of the lower buttock","Skin of the thigh","Skin of the upper buttock","Skin of the mid-buttock"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the mid-buttock', 'The middle cluneal nerves supply the skin of the mid-buttock. The perineum, lower and upper buttock, and thigh are innervated by other nerves.'),
('c4132919-9b47-5d39-a8a5-03dff999501b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The iliohypogastric nerve supplies the skin of which area?', '["Suprapubic region and upper lateral thigh","Medial thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suprapubic region and upper lateral thigh', 'The iliohypogastric nerve supplies the skin of the suprapubic region and the upper lateral thigh through its lateral and anterior cutaneous branches. The medial and posterior thigh, anterior thigh, and gluteal region are innervated by other nerves.'),
('e8afb535-e63c-58b8-a97e-f4c45c1edae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ilioinguinal nerve supplies the skin of which area?', '["Anterior thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Root of penis and upper scrotum/labia majora","Gluteal region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Root of penis and upper scrotum/labia majora', 'The ilioinguinal nerve passes through the inguinal canal and supplies the skin of the root of the penis and the upper scrotum (or labia majora). The thigh and gluteal regions are supplied by other nerves.'),
('5838efe7-7d0f-5be9-b7ef-8b0824aa0549'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The genitofemoral nerve supplies the skin of which area?', '["Anterior scrotum/labia majora and upper medial thigh","Lower leg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteal region mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior scrotum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior scrotum/labia majora and upper medial thigh', 'The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve supplies the anterior scrotum or labia majora, while the femoral branch supplies the upper medial thigh. The lower leg, gluteal region, posterior scrotum, and lateral thigh are supplied by other nerves.'),
('eb63e4b0-36c7-5fdf-8238-e75b3b6bbce1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies which area?', '["Skin of the posterior thigh","Skin of the anterior thigh","Skin of the gluteal region","Skin of the medial thigh","Skin of the lateral surface of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the lateral surface of the thigh', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the skin of the lateral surface of the thigh down to the knee. The posterior, anterior, gluteal, and medial thigh regions are innervated by other cutaneous nerves.'),
('394ed907-fd67-5a16-afd3-0ef50f8f3d39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Compression of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh causes which condition?', '["Pudendal neuralgia","Femoral neuropathy","Meralgia paresthetica","Sciatica","Obturator neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Meralgia paresthetica', 'Compression of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh, usually where it passes under the inguinal ligament, produces meralgia paresthetica with numbness and tingling over the lateral thigh. Pudendal neuralgia, femoral neuropathy, sciatica, and obturator neuropathy involve other nerves.'),
('839f0443-c4ea-55a9-8845-42878cecdf1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Meralgia paresthetica presents with which symptom?', '["Foot drop","Numbness and tingling over the lateral thigh","Weakness of knee extension","Inability to adduct the thigh","Pain in the lower back"]'::jsonb, 'Numbness and tingling over the lateral thigh', 'Meralgia paresthetica presents with numbness and tingling over the lateral thigh, a purely sensory disorder of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh. Foot drop, weak knee extension, impaired adduction, and low back pain reflect other nerve or spinal lesions.'),
('1b7371b6-2dc5-5241-a532-4740d0c71749'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is commonly compressed in meralgia paresthetica?', '["Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Saphenous nerve","Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh', 'Meralgia paresthetica results from compression of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh as it passes beneath or through the inguinal ligament. Injuries to the sciatic, femoral, obturator, and saphenous nerves produce different sensory and motor deficits.'),
('f699c4ec-ebba-5540-a83a-c0a85693944b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is injured in anterior hip dislocation?', '["Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'In anterior dislocation, the femoral nerve, which lies anterior to the hip joint, is most at risk of injury. The obturator, gluteal, and sciatic nerves are injured primarily in other hip injury patterns.'),
('655a05d1-5813-569d-8ee9-8757d01128e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is injured in posterior hip dislocation?', '["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve', 'In posterior dislocation, the sciatic nerve, which passes posterior to the hip, is most at risk. The femoral, superior and inferior gluteal, and obturator nerves are not endangered by this dislocation pattern.'),
('9144abb9-ff62-55cc-b193-6a12479939d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is injured in obturator hernia?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve', 'An obturator hernia passes through the obturator canal and may compress the obturator nerve, causing medial thigh pain (Howship-Romberg sign). The gluteal, sciatic, and femoral nerves are not found in the obturator canal.'),
('2a6e1992-f9ce-5393-8078-4a7f9ff8b0b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is injured in fractures of the femoral neck?', '["Femoral nerve","None","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve', 'The sciatic nerve lies posterior to the hip joint and may be injured in fractures of the femoral neck. The femoral, superior gluteal, and obturator nerves are not typically involved, so ''None'' is inaccurate.'),
('03d26240-cfcc-5cef-9554-b11d4d6ae98d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during knee joint surgery?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve', 'The common peroneal nerve winds around the neck of the fibula close to the lateral knee and is exposed to injury during knee surgery. The saphenous, femoral, tibial, and sural nerves lie away from the main surgical field.'),
('cdb9b020-634c-5fac-a4cd-eb429a78bc09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during varicose vein surgery (stripping of great saphenous vein)?', '["Saphenous nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Saphenous nerve', 'The saphenous nerve runs alongside the great saphenous vein in the leg and is readily injured during stripping of that vein. The common peroneal, tibial, femoral, and sural nerves are not closely related to the great saphenous vein.'),
('f5ec04fe-69b7-5bf0-a615-d2cceb7bf387'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during small saphenous vein stripping?', '["Saphenous nerve","Femoral nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sural nerve', 'The sural nerve accompanies the small saphenous vein in the posterior calf and is at risk during stripping of that vein. The saphenous, femoral, common peroneal, and tibial nerves have different relations to the lower limb.'),
('9d09fe3b-c7e2-597f-bed1-5a699b695199'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during knee arthroscopy?', '["Saphenous nerve (infrapatellar branch)","Tibial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common peroneal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Femoral nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sural nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Saphenous nerve (infrapatellar branch)', 'The infrapatellar branch of the saphenous nerve crosses the medial side of the knee and may be injured by the portal incisions of arthroscopy. The tibial, common peroneal, femoral, and sural nerves are not at risk from these portals.'),
('0a8940db-e2ee-5e30-a7db-22241b282a39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during medial meniscus repair?', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Tibial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common peroneal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Saphenous nerve (infrapatellar branch)","Sural nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Saphenous nerve (infrapatellar branch)', 'The infrapatellar branch of the saphenous nerve crosses the medial joint line and can be damaged during medial meniscus repair. The femoral, tibial, common peroneal, and sural nerves lie away from the medial operative field.'),
('43978df5-7c2c-538f-919b-804276d55f4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during lateral meniscus repair?', '["Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve', 'The common peroneal nerve passes behind the fibular neck close to the lateral joint line and is at risk during lateral meniscus repair. The tibial, femoral, sural, and saphenous nerves are not compromised in the lateral surgical field.'),
('05a62da8-e0af-5b6f-b85d-31327319a36a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during hip replacement surgery?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve', 'The sciatic nerve lies posterior to the hip joint, close to the operative field, and is the nerve most often injured during hip replacement. The gluteal, obturator, and femoral nerves are less exposed unless the dissection extends to their territories.'),
('7bc286a7-5fc4-5e46-b113-1e86536ac5ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during a posterior approach to the hip?', '["Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve', 'In the posterior approach, the sciatic nerve, which passes behind the hip joint, is most at risk of traction or direct injury. The obturator, femoral, and gluteal nerves are not encountered in the posterior exposure.'),
('3ce8dd7f-92a1-51ba-90a1-c41080583626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during an anterior approach to the hip?', '["Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'In the anterior approach, the femoral nerve, which lies anterior to the hip joint, is most at risk. The obturator, superior and inferior gluteal, and sciatic nerves are not in the anterior operative field.'),
('8a90f20e-94b7-5b63-b3b5-df258d272357'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is at risk during lateral approach to the hip?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'In the lateral approach, the superior gluteal nerve may be damaged if the dissection between gluteus medius and minimus is carried too far proximally. The inferior gluteal, femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves lie outside this interval.'),
('d734b1bb-d7cf-5306-8e21-0c3f06854a4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes Trendelenburg gait?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superior gluteal nerve', 'Trendelenburg gait follows paralysis of the gluteus medius and minimus, which are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve. Femoral, obturator, inferior gluteal, and sciatic nerve injuries produce different clinical deficits.'),
('af672a0a-6b80-5b5f-be2b-b0d870ceace9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes gluteal gait?', '["Inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior gluteal nerve', 'Gluteal (lurching) gait results from paralysis of the gluteus maximus, which is innervated by the inferior gluteal nerve. The superior gluteal, femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves cause other deficits when injured.'),
('23ed99e9-6847-5d38-9d7b-9d9f83739d50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Trendelenburg gait is characterized by?', '["Pelvis drops to the same side","Patient leans backward","Pelvis drops to the opposite side during walking","Patient leans forward","Circumduction"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvis drops to the opposite side during walking', 'In Trendelenburg gait the weakened hip abductors fail to stabilize the pelvis, so it drops on the opposite side during the stance phase. Leaning, circumduction, and same-side pelvic drop describe other gait patterns.'),
('2eb04217-36a1-5187-957c-41dcf9207b4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Gluteal gait is characterized by?', '["Trunk leans forward resulting in decreased functional activity","Circumduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trunk lurches backward at heel strike","Pelvis drops without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foot drop leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trunk lurches backward at heel strike', 'In gluteal gait, weakness of the gluteus maximus allows uncontrolled forward momentum, so the trunk lurches backward at heel strike. Leaning forward, circumduction, pelvic drop, and foot drop belong to other gait abnormalities.'),
('3c06eee6-97e8-5afd-bbc8-5f545f5c4cc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Foot drop gait is caused by injury to which nerve?', '["Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve', 'Foot drop follows paralysis of the ankle dorsiflexors, which are innervated by the common peroneal nerve. Obturator, sciatic, femoral, and tibial nerve injuries produce other motor and sensory deficits.'),
('0d203f2a-ef46-526e-830c-4ac1ce155f28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'High-stepping gait is characteristic of which nerve injury?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve', 'High-stepping gait is a compensatory maneuver for foot drop and is characteristic of common peroneal nerve injury. Obturator, femoral, sciatic, and tibial nerve injuries do not produce this pattern.'),
('5079ee68-94c4-5d06-8b76-3603812f0647'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes loss of sensation on the dorsum of the foot?', '["Common peroneal nerve (superficial division)","Sural nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Saphenous nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve (superficial division)', 'The superficial division of the common peroneal nerve supplies most of the skin of the dorsum of the foot, and its injury causes loss of sensation there. The sural, femoral, saphenous, and tibial nerves supply other territories.'),
('c6b814b4-f431-5fb4-925a-a91e376d37fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes loss of plantar sensation?', '["Femoral nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The medial and lateral plantar branches of the tibial nerve supply the skin of the sole, so tibial nerve injury causes loss of plantar sensation. The femoral, common peroneal, saphenous, and sural nerves supply other regions.'),
('7bf3951a-4c70-501e-9972-8e094d0fb9ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes loss of sensation on the lateral foot?', '["Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sural nerve', 'The sural nerve supplies the skin of the lateral foot and the lateral border of the foot, and its injury causes sensory loss there. The femoral, tibial, common peroneal, and saphenous nerves supply other areas.'),
('e23f6355-7a8c-5e2d-9bbf-d60a18a9dd37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes loss of sensation on the medial leg?', '["Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve","Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Saphenous nerve', 'The saphenous nerve, the terminal branch of the femoral nerve, supplies the skin of the medial leg, so its injury causes sensory loss there. The sural, femoral trunk, tibial, and common peroneal nerves supply other territories.'),
('64adbab4-e761-5481-b495-498b614c6017'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes inability to stand on tiptoes?', '["Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Standing on tiptoes requires plantar flexion by the triceps surae, which is innervated by the tibial nerve, so tibial nerve injury abolishes this action. The sciatic, obturator, common peroneal, and femoral nerves do not supply the calf muscles.'),
('01b39186-1ca1-5a35-9e82-de8e437d7390'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve injury causes inability to stand on the heels?', '["Tibial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Common peroneal nerve (deep branch)","Obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve (deep branch)', 'Standing on the heels requires dorsiflexion of the ankle by the anterior compartment muscles, which are innervated by the deep branch of the common peroneal nerve. Tibial, obturator, sciatic, and femoral nerve injuries produce other deficits.'),
('ed172abd-db6a-5312-b511-4498029ec09d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lumbar plexus lies within which muscle?', '["Iliacus","Quadratus lumborum","Transversus abdominis","Rectus abdominis","Psoas major"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major', 'The lumbar plexus is embedded within the substance of the psoas major, lying between its anterior and posterior parts. The femoral nerve emerges from its lateral border and the obturator nerve from its medial border.'),
('0ba0900f-634c-55fd-8d8b-357a9bda28fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sacral plexus lies on which muscle?', '["Obturator internus","Iliacus","Quadratus lumborum","Piriformis","Psoas major"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis', 'The sacral plexus lies on the anterior (pelvic) surface of the piriformis muscle beneath the pelvic fascia. Its branches, including the sciatic and pudendal nerves, leave the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramens above and below this muscle.'),
('98b12841-d925-5361-b358-435ca4229824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve is composed of which two nerves bound together?', '["Tibial nerve and common peroneal nerve","None","Superior gluteal nerve and inferior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve and obturator nerve","Sural nerve and saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve and common peroneal nerve', 'The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve in the body, consists of the tibial and common peroneal nerves bound together in a common sheath. The two components separate in or above the popliteal fossa.'),
('79efba0d-40a8-5921-82b6-bd2a129e4c07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibial nerve is formed from which divisions of the sacral plexus?', '["Anterior divisions of L4-S3","Posterior divisions of L4-S2","Anterior divisions of L2-L4","None","Posterior divisions of L5-S2"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior divisions of L4-S3', 'The tibial nerve receives fibers from the anterior divisions of L4, L5, S1, S2, and S3. The posterior divisions of L4-S2 instead contribute to the common peroneal nerve.'),
('8dce782e-122f-56a4-bb8e-e477c454fae0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common peroneal nerve is formed from which divisions?', '["Anterior divisions of L5-S2","Anterior divisions of L4-S3","Posterior divisions of L4-S2","Posterior divisions of L2-L4","None"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior divisions of L4-S2', 'The common peroneal (fibular) nerve is formed by the posterior divisions of L4, L5, S1, and S2. It forms the lateral component of the sciatic nerve and winds around the neck of the fibula.'),
('47c808d4-486d-56d9-bac5-af49960c25f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibial nerve in the leg lies between which structures?', '["None","Anterior and lateral compartment muscles","Superficial and deep posterior compartment muscles","Medial and lateral heads of gastrocnemius","Tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep posterior compartment muscles', 'The tibial nerve descends in the leg in the interval between the superficial group (gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris) and the deep group (tibialis posterior, flexor hallucis longus, flexor digitorum longus) of the posterior compartment. It reaches the ankle behind the medial malleolus.'),
('de97a42a-a634-50fe-9916-5fc57f60190d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibial nerve passes posterior to which structure at the ankle?', '["Navicular","Calcaneus","Lateral malleolus","Talus","Medial malleolus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial malleolus', 'The tibial nerve passes behind the medial malleolus within the tarsal tunnel, lying between the tendons of flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus. There it divides into the medial and lateral plantar nerves.'),
('9a7eeda2-2d0d-5e38-badd-4529f9c13d65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Tarsal tunnel syndrome involves compression of which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve","Common peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'Tarsal tunnel syndrome results from entrapment of the tibial nerve beneath the flexor retinaculum behind the medial malleolus. It produces burning pain and paresthesias along the plantar aspect of the foot.'),
('17ead5ba-e735-58a3-a8d5-08562e1d9dd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Tarsal tunnel syndrome presents with?', '["Dorsal foot pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Knee pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain and paresthesia on the plantar surface of the foot","Hip pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ankle pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and paresthesia on the plantar surface of the foot', 'Compression of the tibial nerve in the tarsal tunnel causes neuropathic pain, burning, and paresthesias on the plantar surface of the foot and toes. A positive Tinel sign behind the medial malleolus and nocturnal worsening are characteristic.'),
('9c639761-5547-5339-9a57-90cd3d96e4d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve passes through the tarsal tunnel?', '["Sural nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The tibial nerve traverses the tarsal tunnel behind the medial malleolus together with the tibial artery and the long flexor tendons. None of the other listed nerves course through this tunnel.'),
('29887e45-a10b-586a-9bba-07c195b6a869'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior gluteal nerve passes between which muscles?', '["Piriformis and obturator internus","Quadratus femoris and gemelli","Gluteus maximus and gluteus medius","Gluteus medius and gluteus minimus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius and gluteus minimus', 'After emerging through the suprapiriform foramen, the superior gluteal nerve runs laterally between the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus. It supplies these abductor muscles together with the tensor fasciae latae.'),
('c2980483-0668-524f-a804-c07fdd63ad68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The inferior gluteal nerve supplies which single muscle?', '["Tensor fascia lata","Gluteus maximus","Gluteus medius","Gluteus minimus","Piriformis"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus', 'The inferior gluteal nerve exits through the infrapiriform foramen and provides the entire motor supply to the gluteus maximus. Injury produces difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a seated position.'),
('f1fe57a8-9e37-5481-982c-05af03acac7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the popliteus muscle?', '["Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The nerve to the popliteus is a branch of the tibial nerve given off in the popliteal fossa. It descends across the muscle, pierces it, and also supplies the tibialis posterior and the tibiofibular joint.'),
('684ce89d-5402-544b-8ee7-63d974a26104'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to popliteus also supplies which structure?', '["Tibiofibular joint","Popliteal ligament","Interosseous membrane of leg","Knee joint","Tibialis posterior muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Tibialis posterior muscle', 'The nerve to the popliteus continues past that muscle to innervate the tibialis posterior, an important plantarflexor and inverter. Its damage impairs both the unlocking of the knee and plantarflexion/inversion of the foot.'),
('1770f779-d88c-5a78-b041-1bae1661601f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The recurrent branch of the deep peroneal nerve supplies which structure?', '["Extensor digitorum longus","Extensor hallucis longus","Ankle joint","Knee joint","Tibialis anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Knee joint', 'The deep peroneal nerve gives a small recurrent articular branch that ascends in front of the interosseous membrane to supply the knee joint. Its other branches innervate the anterior compartment muscles and ankle joint.'),
('62e1a0c1-554e-5da1-9a86-e5111449a884'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The genicular branches of the sciatic nerve supply which structure?', '["Tibiofibular joint","Knee joint","None","Hip joint","Ankle joint"]'::jsonb, 'Knee joint', 'The sciatic nerve gives genicular branches that supply the capsule and ligaments of the knee joint. The femoral and obturator nerves also contribute articular branches to the knee.'),
('2d36287a-92d0-5c19-a215-ff0a56815f89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the skin of which area?', '["Anterior thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foot mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior thigh popliteal fossa and upper posterior leg"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior thigh popliteal fossa and upper posterior leg', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the skin of the posterior thigh, the popliteal fossa, and the upper posterior leg. Its perineal branch extends to the perineum and upper medial thigh.'),
('aea80506-ab45-507d-8152-d32ede9758a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The perineal branch of the posterior cutaneous nerve supplies which area?', '["Lateral perineum and medial thigh","Scrotum/labia majora accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Buttock mediated by specific receptor activation","Anus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral perineum and medial thigh', 'The perineal branch of the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the skin of the lateral perineum and the upper medial thigh. It communicates with the genitofemoral nerve and extends toward the scrotum or labia majora.'),
('7128d27a-e8e4-561c-8c16-7285bfa47a4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to the piriformis enters which surface of the muscle?', '["Inferior border","Posterior surface","Superior border","Lateral border","Anterior surface"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior surface', 'The nerve to the piriformis, carrying fibers from S1 and S2, enters the anterior (pelvic) surface of the muscle before it passes through the greater sciatic foramen. This reflects the muscle''s origin within the pelvis.'),
('0f05cac8-434b-5a79-bd7d-8cccef38d571'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris passes anterior to which muscles?', '["Gluteus medius and minimus","Obturator internus and gemelli","Hamstrings","None","Piriformis and gluteus maximus"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator internus and gemelli', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris descends deep to the sciatic nerve, passing anterior to the obturator internus and the gemelli before entering quadratus femoris. It also sends a branch to supply the hip joint.'),
('80dd92d2-7e43-5ea1-88e7-ec450402f5a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to obturator internus passes through which foramen?', '["Suprapiriform foramen","Greater sciatic foramen","Obturator foramen","Infrapiriform foramen","Lesser sciatic foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser sciatic foramen', 'The nerve to obturator internus leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis, crosses the ischial spine, and then re-enters the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen to reach the muscle. The superior gemellus is supplied along with obturator internus.'),
('cd03b70e-90bc-54ab-9151-0b2967010b53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the ischial tuberosity?', '["Nerve to quadratus femoris","Nerve to obturator internus","Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh","Pudendal nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to quadratus femoris', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris gives a branch to the periosteum of the ischial tuberosity in addition to supplying the quadrilateral muscle and the hip joint. The cutaneous nerves to the region overlie but do not innervate the bone itself.'),
('f2e4aecc-aec0-5dcd-a5bc-c4164b9b7bf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The coccygeal plexus gives rise to which nerve?', '["Pudendal nerve","Superior cluneal nerve","Perforating cutaneous nerve","Anococcygeal nerve","Inferior cluneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Anococcygeal nerve', 'The small coccygeal plexus, formed by the anterior rami of S4 and S5 with the coccygeal nerve, gives rise to the anococcygeal nerve. The pudendal plexus, the main component of the sacral plexus, supplies the other listed branches.'),
('88fff0c5-5c99-583f-94e9-363dc87e7cd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The anococcygeal nerve supplies which area?', '["Skin of the abdomen","Skin of the buttock","Skin of the perineum","Skin of the thigh","Skin over the coccyx"]'::jsonb, 'Skin over the coccyx', 'The anococcygeal nerve pierces the sacrotuberous ligament to supply the skin over the coccyx. It is a purely cutaneous branch of the coccygeal plexus.'),
('581f6c6f-9c33-5a85-becd-ebaa2e35994e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sympathetic trunk in the pelvis is formed by which ganglia?', '["None","Lumbar sympathetic ganglia","Coccygeal ganglion","Sacral sympathetic ganglia (S1-S5)","Thoracic sympathetic ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral sympathetic ganglia (S1-S5)', 'The pelvic portion of the sympathetic trunk descends between the sacral sympathetic ganglia (S1-S5) and ends in the single coccygeal ganglion. These sacral ganglia give rise to the sacral splanchnic, sympathetic, nerves.'),
('17170a6e-680b-5d3f-94f4-86183d070b1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves contain which type of fibers?', '["Sensory","Sympathetic (L1-L2)","None","Parasympathetic (S2-S4)","Somatic motor"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (S2-S4)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves arise from the anterior rami of S2, S3, and S4 and carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers. They synapse in ganglia near the pelvic viscera to mediate bladder contraction and erection.'),
('aa84b99b-8eec-5c8c-bdf4-28e8c5002db0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sacral splanchnic nerves contain which type of fibers?', '["Sympathetic","None","Sensory","Somatic motor","Parasympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic', 'The sacral splanchnic nerves arise from the sacral sympathetic ganglia and carry postganglionic sympathetic fibers. They join the inferior hypogastric plexus to reach the pelvic viscera.'),
('fc359385-ed50-53c2-ae23-f9afe7061119'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves supply which organs?', '["Limbs resulting in decreased functional activity","Abdominal viscera accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head and neck mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracic viscera without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pelvic viscera including bladder rectum and reproductive organs"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic viscera including bladder rectum and reproductive organs', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic S2-S4) innervate the pelvic viscera, including the bladder, rectum, and reproductive organs. They promote contraction of the bladder and rectum and cause vasodilation of the erectile tissue.'),
('577574e8-6bfb-50e2-84c5-d5ba6c114568'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior hypogastric plexus is located where?', '["Lateral to bladder","Posterior to pubis","Anterior to L5 vertebral body","Posterior to rectum","Anterior to sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to L5 vertebral body', 'The superior hypogastric plexus lies in the retroperitoneum anterior to the fifth lumbar vertebral body, between the common iliac vessels. It is the pelvic continuation of the abdominal aortic plexus and divides into the right and left hypogastric nerves.'),
('e40deb5f-abc6-5715-9d6a-ec0df40da5fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The inferior hypogastric plexus is located where?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral to the rectum bladder and prostate/vagina","Posterior to pubis mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior to sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral to L5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to the rectum bladder and prostate/vagina', 'The inferior hypogastric plexus lies on each side of the pelvis lateral to the rectum, bladder, and prostate or vagina. It receives sympathetic fibers from the hypogastric nerves and sacral splanchnics, and parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnics.'),
('2f74b1b8-7684-5a31-9b4e-acc1e42bd951'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the bladder?', '["Femoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Sacral splanchnic nerves","Pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in the vesical ganglia to innervate the detrusor muscle. They also conduct the sensation of bladder distension.'),
('43ea9681-10dd-5ff3-926b-e407c5d803c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve carries sympathetic fibers to the bladder?', '["Sciatic nerve","Femoral nerve","Hypogastric nerves","Pudendal nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Hypogastric nerves', 'The hypogastric nerves carry postganglionic sympathetic fibers to the bladder that relax the detrusor muscle and contract the internal urethral sphincter. This pathway promotes urinary storage.'),
('aecc2ad8-919e-56b7-9d93-bc0defeb4fba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve provides somatic motor innervation to the external urethral sphincter?', '["Femoral nerve","Pudendal nerve","Obturator nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'The pudendal nerve supplies somatic motor fibers to the external urethral sphincter through its perineal branches. This provides voluntary control over micturition, in contrast to the autonomic supply of the detrusor.'),
('b07b0e3a-3736-557a-8e93-ac6a0c856495'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve supply to the anal canal includes which nerves?', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior rectal branch of pudendal nerve and pelvic splanchnic nerves","Obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pudendal nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior rectal branch of pudendal nerve and pelvic splanchnic nerves', 'The anal canal is innervated above the pectinate line by the pelvic splanchnic (visceral) nerves and below it by the inferior rectal branch of the pudendal nerve (somatic). This dual supply governs sphincter control and pain sensation in the region.'),
('0ee25c97-6686-501a-af31-fa73b0548436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the internal anal sphincter?', '["Inferior rectal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Pudendal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypogastric nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic)","Femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic)', 'The internal anal sphincter is innervated by parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, which promote its relaxation during defecation. The external sphincter, by contrast, is supplied by somatic fibers of the pudendal nerve.'),
('3a526acf-65c9-5ab0-8442-d5416a192615'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve carries the sensation of distension from the bladder?', '["Sciatic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Femoral nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Hypogastric nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves', 'Visceral afferent fibers that carry the sensation of bladder distension travel with the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the spinal cord at S2-S4. This pathway subserves the micturition reflex.'),
('d002c8cf-8ea7-52e3-a641-912b0ae42586'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve carries the sensation of touch in the urethra?', '["Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Sciatic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Hypogastric nerves","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'Touch and pain sensation in the urethra is carried by somatic afferent fibers of the pudendal nerve. This same pathway conveys discomfort from urethral instrumentation and infection.'),
('886712fc-6b2d-5912-8667-db12caa874cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is responsible for penile erection?', '["Femoral nerve","Pudendal nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic)","Hypogastric nerves (sympathetic)","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic)', 'Penile erection is mediated by parasympathetic fibers of the pelvic splanchnic nerves (nervi erigentes), which relax the cavernous smooth muscle and dilate the helicine arteries. Sympathetic and pudendal influences are not responsible for the erection itself.'),
('907a8af1-3084-5aba-96b5-62d26975699a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve is responsible for ejaculation?', '["Hypogastric nerves (sympathetic)","Sciatic nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic)","Femoral nerve","Pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Hypogastric nerves (sympathetic)', 'Ejaculation is a sympathetic function mediated by the hypogastric nerves, which contract the smooth muscle of the epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and prostate. The somatic pudendal nerve adds contraction of the perineal muscles during emission.'),
('7711f48a-0c43-5786-b453-874825e7cea0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve controls the bulbospongiosus muscle?', '["Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Hypogastric nerves","Pudendal nerve (deep branch)"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve (deep branch)', 'The bulbospongiosus is supplied by the deep branch of the perineal nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve. In the male it empties the urethra and assists ejaculation, while in the female it constricts the vaginal orifice.'),
('d802766f-28f6-516b-ae0a-b074e9ed428d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve controls the ischiocavernosus muscle?', '["Femoral nerve","Hypogastric nerves","Obturator nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Pudendal nerve (deep branch)"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve (deep branch)', 'The ischiocavernosus is innervated by the deep branch of the perineal nerve from the pudendal nerve. Its contraction compresses the crus of the penis or clitoris and helps maintain erection.'),
('268fafef-72f0-581f-9479-ae9c03515365'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis runs where relative to the pubic symphysis?', '["Posterior to the pubic symphysis","None","Above the pubic symphysis","Below the pubic symphysis","Lateral to the pubic symphysis"]'::jsonb, 'Below the pubic symphysis', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis, the terminal branch of the pudendal nerve, runs below the pubic symphysis along the pubic arch to reach the dorsum of the penis. It carries sensation from the glans and most of the penile skin.'),
('76cf8518-8280-58da-bbab-04c32388e31a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The dorsal nerve of the clitoris is a branch of which nerve?', '["Genitofemoral nerve","Sciatic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'The dorsal nerve of the clitoris is the terminal branch of the pudendal nerve, running forward below the pubic symphysis to the clitoris. It is the homologue of the dorsal nerve of the penis.'),
('2968ccf3-c84e-5cc3-82bb-695c4c0484c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the labia majora?', '["Saphenous nerve resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Anteriorly by ilioinguinal and genitofemoral; posteriorly by perineal branch of posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh","Pudendal nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly by ilioinguinal and genitofemoral; posteriorly by perineal branch of posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh', 'The anterior labia majora are supplied by the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves, while the posterior portion is supplied by the perineal branch of the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh. The pudendal nerve supplies the intervening interlabial region.'),
('861c6515-43c9-51d6-a990-7b957c3b93d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the penis?', '["Dorsal nerve of penis (pudendal) and ilioinguinal nerve","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genitofemoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Pudendal nerve only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal nerve of penis (pudendal) and ilioinguinal nerve', 'Most of the penile skin is supplied by the dorsal nerve of the penis from the pudendal nerve, with the anterior root region receiving additional fibers from the ilioinguinal nerve. Branches of the perineal nerve contribute at the root ventrally.'),
('aed5350d-1f51-5a46-9443-66eaca047a89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the scrotum?', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Anteriorly by ilioinguinal; posteriorly by perineal branch of posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh","Pudendal nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Genitofemoral nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly by ilioinguinal; posteriorly by perineal branch of posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh', 'The anterior scrotal skin is supplied by the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves, while the posterior scrotum is supplied by the perineal branch of the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh. The perineal nerve of the pudendal also innervates the posterior scrotum.'),
('83f6c55a-92ce-5cd5-b35d-8bdfdb8e117a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial peroneal nerve divides into which terminal branches?', '["None","Deep and superficial branches","Medial and lateral plantar nerves","Medial and lateral dorsal cutaneous nerves","Sural and saphenous nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral dorsal cutaneous nerves', 'The superficial peroneal nerve ends on the dorsum of the foot by dividing into the medial and lateral dorsal cutaneous nerves. The lateral branch anastomoses with the sural nerve, and together they supply most of the dorsum of the foot.'),
('0200ff61-0617-5e9c-b120-f82ff18224f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The deep peroneal nerve divides into which terminal branches?', '["None","Medial and lateral plantar branches","Lateral and medial terminal branches","Cutaneous and muscular","Superficial and deep branches"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and medial terminal branches', 'The deep peroneal nerve terminates at the ankle by dividing into lateral and medial terminal branches. The medial branch reaches the first web space, while the lateral branch supplies the extensor brevis muscles of the foot.'),
('ee9a3183-1761-5e79-b661-a68e8fd0210e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial terminal branch of the deep peroneal nerve supplies which area?', '["First web space","Dorsum of foot","Second web space","Third web space","Lateral foot"]'::jsonb, 'First web space', 'The medial terminal branch of the deep peroneal nerve accompanies the dorsalis pedis artery and supplies the skin of the first web space. This small territory is the sensory deficit in a deep peroneal nerve lesion.'),
('9e632935-3c72-5c57-b757-99f4631d4404'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral terminal branch of the deep peroneal nerve supplies which muscle?', '["Interossei resulting in decreased functional activity","Lumbricals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abductor hallucis mediated by specific receptor activation","Extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis","Flexor digitorum brevis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis', 'The lateral terminal branch of the deep peroneal nerve supplies the extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis muscles. Those muscles extend the toes and are absent in some individuals.'),
('5991bef2-da18-5ba0-ac59-93f9775ae483'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the tibiofibular joint?', '["Obturator nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Sural nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibial nerve and common peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Saphenous nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve and common peroneal nerve', 'The superior tibiofibular joint receives branches from the tibial nerve medial to the fibula and the common peroneal nerve, which also supplies the anterolateral capsule.'),
('fd060f62-2bf8-563b-ad6b-f451b156cd3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The articular branch of the common peroneal nerve supplies which joint?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Ankle joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hip joint mediated by specific receptor activation","Foot joints without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Knee joint and tibiofibular joint"]'::jsonb, 'Knee joint and tibiofibular joint', 'The articular branch of the common peroneal nerve descends to innervate both the knee joint and the superior tibiofibular joint, matching its motor territory.'),
('4672bb2a-5eff-5f7a-97c4-99881e352f2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The recurrent articular nerve of the deep peroneal nerve supplies which joint?', '["Foot joints","Ankle joint","Hip joint","Tibiofibular joint","Knee joint"]'::jsonb, 'Knee joint', 'The recurrent articular nerve of the deep peroneal nerve passes upward through tibialis anterior to supply the knee joint, particularly the lateral aspect.'),
('90f3c076-7473-5db0-9dc0-3a415e762c1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the ankle joint?', '["Obturator nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Saphenous nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sciatic nerve alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Tibial nerve deep peroneal nerve and superficial peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve deep peroneal nerve and superficial peroneal nerve', 'The ankle joint capsule is innervated by the tibial nerve, deep peroneal nerve, and superficial peroneal nerve, each contributing branches from its course around the ankle.'),
('6a9e1423-d30a-5b9d-99cd-8c0c58042c26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the subtalar joint?', '["Saphenous nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Tibial nerve deep peroneal nerve and sural nerve","Sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve deep peroneal nerve and sural nerve', 'The subtalar joint receives sensory branches from the tibial nerve, deep peroneal nerve, and sural nerve, all of which converge on the hindfoot.'),
('bf9e6cf2-0c9f-5715-977e-8dd3d7e3008e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the midtarsal joints?', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep peroneal nerve and sural nerve","Sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Tibial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Saphenous nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep peroneal nerve and sural nerve', 'The midtarsal (transverse tarsal) joints are supplied by branches of the deep peroneal nerve dorsally and the sural nerve laterally.'),
('2541dff0-f76e-5fa7-b3b4-4c57ed0ea5e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the metatarsophalangeal joints?', '["Sural nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Tibial nerve only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plantar digital nerves and dorsal digital nerves","Saphenous nerve only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peroneal nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Plantar digital nerves and dorsal digital nerves', 'The metatarsophalangeal joints are innervated by the plantar and dorsal digital nerves of the foot, which accompany the digital vessels to each joint.'),
('dd5f24bf-0d7c-5ac7-9dfd-a29679b1662f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral nerve arises from which spinal nerves?', '["L3 L4 and L5","L2 L3 and L4","L1 L2 and L3","S1 S2 and S3","L4 L5 and S1"]'::jsonb, 'L2 L3 and L4', 'The femoral nerve arises from the posterior divisions of the anterior primary rami of L2, L3, and L4 within the lumbar plexus.'),
('4c8411df-244b-58fc-a5c7-a4cce334ba7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator nerve arises from which spinal nerves?', '["L4 L5 and S1","S1 S2 and S3","L2 L3 and L4 (ventral divisions)","L2 L3 and L4 (dorsal divisions)","L3 L4 and L5"]'::jsonb, 'L2 L3 and L4 (ventral divisions)', 'The obturator nerve derives from the anterior divisions of L2, L3, and L4, so its ventral division origin distinguishes it from the femoral nerve.'),
('30de9b3b-8aa3-5260-ab56-b42da9b65acf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The accessory obturator nerve arises from which roots?', '["L3-L4","L4-L5","L2-L3","S1-S2","L5-S1"]'::jsonb, 'L3-L4', 'The accessory obturator nerve, when present, typically arises from L3 and L4 roots of the lumbar plexus.'),
('00d3524c-c4aa-51d7-9cf3-eabcef474f55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The accessory obturator nerve supplies which muscle?', '["Gracilis","Adductor brevis","Obturator externus","Pectineus","Adductor longus"]'::jsonb, 'Pectineus', 'The accessory obturator nerve passes medial to the pectineus and supplies it, along with a variable branch to the hip joint.'),
('572c22dc-3c3e-5532-b238-9b7a84dfd17a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve supplies which area?', '["Skin of the gluteal region","Skin of the perineum","Skin of the scrotum","Skin of the anterior upper thigh","Skin of the lower leg"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the anterior upper thigh', 'The femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve passes under the inguinal ligament to supply the skin of the anterior upper thigh.'),
('e18f5e1a-11f4-5454-ae05-ddfc85ea5b00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve supplies which area?', '["Skin of the anterior thigh","Cremaster muscle and scrotal skin","None","Gluteal skin","Perineal skin"]'::jsonb, 'Cremaster muscle and scrotal skin', 'The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve supplies the cremaster muscle (via the genital branch contribution to the spermatic cord) and the scrotal skin.'),
('d2f130c4-d2ee-5c71-b2e9-66b2e8faaed1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ilioinguinal nerve passes through which canal?', '["None","Obturator canal","Inguinal canal","Femoral canal","Adductor canal"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal canal', 'The ilioinguinal nerve traverses the inguinal canal with the spermatic cord and emerges through the superficial inguinal ring.'),
('17097e62-35a9-5bf0-828a-62a880e85243'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The iliohypogastric nerve pierces which muscle aponeurosis?', '["External oblique aponeurosis","Transversus abdominis aponeurosis","Internal oblique aponeurosis","None","Rectus sheath"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique aponeurosis', 'The iliohypogastric nerve pierces the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle just above the superficial inguinal ring to become cutaneous.'),
('056d8929-c8e9-5bbc-8a46-f7ed70eae8ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The ilioinguinal nerve pierces which muscle?', '["None","Transversus abdominis","External oblique muscle","Rectus abdominis","Internal oblique muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Internal oblique muscle', 'The ilioinguinal nerve pierces the lateral part of the internal oblique muscle to pass through the inguinal canal.'),
('e94465a8-7786-5358-8f9e-a9e2f52d58cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The genitofemoral nerve pierces which muscle?', '["Iliacus","None","Psoas major","Quadratus lumborum","Psoas minor"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major', 'The genitofemoral nerve pierces the psoas major muscle, then divides into its genital and femoral branches.'),
('7a7950d0-32de-57a4-982e-bebda2173c74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh pierces which muscle near the ASIS?', '["Iliacus","Quadratus lumborum","Psoas major","Psoas minor","Sartorius"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh emerges from the lateral border of the psoas major, then crosses the iliacus toward the ASIS.'),
('d2ed5dbd-93e8-59be-8f05-9b61678e1535'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The femoral nerve lies in which space in the thigh?', '["Popliteal fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral triangle lateral to femoral artery","Femoral sheath mediated by specific receptor activation","Femoral canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adductor canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral triangle lateral to femoral artery', 'Within the femoral triangle the femoral nerve lies lateral to the femoral artery, which itself is lateral to the femoral vein.'),
('df29fa5b-fc88-5a06-8b59-f2b60f244cb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The saphenous nerve passes through which canal?', '["Femoral canal","Inguinal canal","Adductor canal","Popliteal fossa","Obturator canal"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor canal', 'The saphenous nerve runs through the adductor canal, where it lies on the femoral artery, and exits at the adductor hiatus.'),
('ced200e6-f817-5085-9a8a-83e6d97c7b96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The obturator nerve divides into which branches at the thigh?', '["None","Anterior and posterior branches","Medial and lateral branches","Superior and inferior branches","Superficial and deep branches"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior branches', 'The obturator nerve divides into anterior and posterior branches as it emerges from the obturator foramen into the medial thigh.'),
('3820a8e3-2d2d-5cf0-a0fa-8f64b6af8d0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The anterior branch of the obturator nerve supplies which muscles?', '["Adductor magnus obturator externus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adductor longus adductor brevis gracilis and pectineus","Obturator internus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gemelli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor longus adductor brevis gracilis and pectineus', 'The anterior branch of the obturator nerve supplies the adductor longus, adductor brevis, gracilis, and pectineus muscles.'),
('199830ef-ca77-5b9b-923e-dfcdff17edcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The posterior branch of the obturator nerve supplies which muscles?', '["Hamstrings resulting in decreased functional activity","Adductor longus and brevis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gracilis and pectineus mediated by specific receptor activation","Adductor magnus (adductor part) and obturator externus","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor magnus (adductor part) and obturator externus', 'The posterior branch of the obturator nerve supplies the adductor part of adductor magnus and the obturator externus muscle.'),
('7f9965be-ded6-55d2-b3f2-7111244d0718'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve in the gluteal region lies deep to which muscle?', '["Gluteus medius","Piriformis","Quadratus femoris","Gluteus maximus","Gluteus minimus"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus', 'In the gluteal region the sciatic nerve lies deep to the gluteus maximus muscle, which protects and overlies it.'),
('c800b487-054c-5cac-aa13-40684bf74999'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sciatic nerve in the posterior thigh lies deep to which muscle group?', '["Adductors","Quadriceps","Hamstrings","Gluteal muscles","None"]'::jsonb, 'Hamstrings', 'In the posterior thigh the sciatic nerve descends between the hamstring muscles and lies deep to their overlying mass.'),
('b5e0f555-b787-53c0-9658-4df3e97e8c80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The tibial nerve at the ankle lies between which tendons?', '["None","Flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus","Tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus","Gastrocnemius and soleus","Peroneus longus and peroneus brevis"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor hallucis longus and flexor digitorum longus', 'At the ankle the tibial nerve passes behind the malleolus between the tendons of flexor digitorum longus and flexor hallucis longus.'),
('8d98a12e-da86-5e9a-83ad-ea23b38f3b88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The common peroneal nerve winds around which structure at the knee?', '["Tibial tuberosity","Head of fibula","Neck of fibula","Patella","Lateral femoral condyle"]'::jsonb, 'Neck of fibula', 'The common peroneal nerve winds around the neck of the fibula before dividing into the superficial and deep peroneal nerves.'),
('fff4eef4-dc8d-5e44-8592-8ccc12314d70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The deep peroneal nerve lies on which structure in the anterior leg?', '["Tibia","Fibula","Interosseous membrane","None","Anterior tibial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Interosseous membrane', 'In the anterior leg the deep peroneal nerve lies on the interosseous membrane, lateral to the anterior tibial artery.'),
('7ed1c716-1008-5f0f-ba92-0170faada9c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial peroneal nerve lies between which muscles?', '["Peroneus longus and peroneus brevis","Tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus","Gastrocnemius and soleus","None","Extensor digitorum longus and peroneus tertius"]'::jsonb, 'Peroneus longus and peroneus brevis', 'The superficial peroneal nerve runs in the lateral compartment between the peroneus longus and peroneus brevis muscles.'),
('904cbb0f-61ad-5df7-9f6f-70250ccd65f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial plantar nerve lies in which compartment of the foot?', '["Medial plantar compartment","Lateral compartment","Dorsal compartment","Heel compartment","Central compartment"]'::jsonb, 'Medial plantar compartment', 'The medial plantar nerve enters the foot through the tarsal tunnel and lies within the medial plantar compartment, analogous to the median nerve in the hand.'),
('7d4cb069-62c0-5995-b10f-b0853614e525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral plantar nerve lies in which compartment of the foot?', '["Central compartment","Lateral compartment","Medial plantar compartment","Heel compartment","Dorsal compartment"]'::jsonb, 'Central compartment', 'The lateral plantar nerve passes through the central compartment of the foot before dividing into its superficial and deep branches.'),
('d0ddc790-f077-57c5-9eee-8bc2ad3434bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sural nerve is formed by union of which two nerves?', '["Medial sural cutaneous nerve (tibial) and lateral sural cutaneous nerve (common peroneal)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Saphenous nerve and superficial peroneal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep peroneal nerve and tibial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral nerve and sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial sural cutaneous nerve (tibial) and lateral sural cutaneous nerve (common peroneal)', 'The sural nerve forms by union of the medial sural cutaneous nerve (from the tibial nerve) and the lateral sural cutaneous nerve (from the common peroneal nerve).'),
('c91dc7d5-c7bf-537e-847c-46c2ef53e69e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The medial sural cutaneous nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Femoral nerve","Common peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve', 'The medial sural cutaneous nerve arises from the tibial nerve in the popliteal fossa and descends to help form the sural nerve.'),
('41f68fcc-f881-53fa-8c7a-5dc339ebc12e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The lateral sural cutaneous nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common peroneal nerve', 'The lateral sural cutaneous nerve branches from the common peroneal nerve and joins the medial sural cutaneous nerve to form the sural nerve.'),
('1bd511b3-0917-594c-9cee-7a442d746725'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The sural communicating nerve connects which two nerves?', '["Tibial nerve to common peroneal nerve","None","Lateral sural cutaneous nerve to medial sural cutaneous nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve to deep peroneal nerve","Medial sural cutaneous nerve to saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral sural cutaneous nerve to medial sural cutaneous nerve', 'The sural communicating nerve connects the lateral sural cutaneous nerve to the medial sural cutaneous nerve, completing the formation of the sural nerve.'),
('91ee8b2b-722d-5efa-b7b1-4adab3b51bb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the proximal posterior leg?', '["Posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh","Saphenous nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sural nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Common peroneal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh', 'The skin of the proximal posterior leg is supplied by the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh, which extends down from the posterior thigh.'),
('2d10c785-e20f-53ad-a500-8d61e0b89101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The saphenous nerve gives off which branch at the knee?', '["Infrapatellar branch","Medial sural cutaneous nerve","Sural communicating nerve","Lateral sural cutaneous nerve","None"]'::jsonb, 'Infrapatellar branch', 'The saphenous nerve gives off the infrapatellar branch at the knee, which supplies the skin over the patella.'),
('836f3ac6-4578-5b4b-82a9-586945fb54d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The infrapatellar branch of the saphenous nerve supplies which area?', '["Skin over the lateral malleolus","Skin over the heel","Skin over the medial malleolus","Skin over the patella and patellar ligament","Skin over the popliteal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Skin over the patella and patellar ligament', 'The infrapatellar branch of the saphenous nerve supplies the skin over the patella and patellar ligament, ending near the tibial tuberosity.'),
('ddd0a77c-3d1c-50e2-a98b-06bafb6be3fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the anterior leg (proximal part)?', '["Tibial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh (sometimes)","Sural nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial peroneal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Saphenous nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh (sometimes)', 'The proximal anterior leg skin may receive accessory supply from the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh in addition to the saphenous area.'),
('f1d535c7-b053-5865-8b8a-1a583502a321'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the anterior leg (middle part)?', '["Deep peroneal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral cutaneous nerve of thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Saphenous nerve and superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Saphenous nerve and superficial peroneal nerve', 'The skin of the middle anterior leg is supplied by the saphenous nerve medially and the superficial peroneal nerve laterally.'),
('6efeb8c1-9a33-545a-b847-642180daa30e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superficial peroneal nerve becomes cutaneous at which level of the leg?', '["Upper third of leg","At the knee","Lower third of leg","At the ankle","Middle third of leg"]'::jsonb, 'Lower third of leg', 'The superficial peroneal nerve becomes cutaneous at the lower third of the leg, where it pierces the deep fascia to supply the dorsum.'),
('8bfe87cc-2bfa-5eda-b911-64f516496756'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the posterior leg?', '["Tibial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep peroneal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sural nerve and posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh (proximally)","Saphenous nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial peroneal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sural nerve and posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh (proximally)', 'The posterior leg skin is supplied by the sural nerve, with the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh contributing proximally.'),
('61ef2d9b-c824-5fe5-a210-245fcaa0f272'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The superior gluteal nerve supplies which surface of gluteus medius and minimus?', '["Superficial surface","Deep surface","Medial border","Lateral border","Inferior border"]'::jsonb, 'Deep surface', 'The superior gluteal nerve enters the deep surface of gluteus medius and gluteus minimus, running between them to supply both.'),
('f89da214-bc9d-5153-a34b-35244feab997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris enters which surface of the muscle?', '["Inferior border","Lateral border","Anterior surface","Posterior surface","Superior border"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior surface', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris enters the anterior (deep) surface of the muscle, which it also supplies along with the inferior gemellus.'),
('33a2e00a-1fc4-5283-af44-e01628c77a2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The nerve to obturator internus enters which surface of the muscle?', '["Deep surface (after passing through lesser sciatic foramen)","Superficial surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial border mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior border without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral border leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep surface (after passing through lesser sciatic foramen)', 'The nerve to obturator internus enters the deep surface of the muscle after passing through the lesser sciatic foramen.'),
('47d02cbd-7446-5210-afa0-cd98690327c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the sacroiliac joint?', '["Branches from the sacral plexus and obturator nerve","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pudendal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Branches from the sacral plexus and obturator nerve', 'The sacroiliac joint receives innervation from branches of the sacral plexus as well as an articular branch of the obturator nerve.'),
('295c16df-e7e4-5a3b-87a2-362410633b45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the pubic symphysis?', '["Pudendal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Obturator nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Genitofemoral nerve and iliohypogastric nerve","Sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Genitofemoral nerve and iliohypogastric nerve', 'The pubic symphysis is supplied by branches of the genitofemoral nerve and iliohypogastric nerve from the lumbar plexus.'),
('61d45a52-3413-5ec2-ac5e-2b9dd0ed9029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The peroneal anastomotic branch (sural communicating nerve) connects which nerves?', '["Lateral sural cutaneous nerve to medial sural cutaneous nerve","Saphenous nerve to sural nerve","None","Superficial peroneal nerve to deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve to common peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral sural cutaneous nerve to medial sural cutaneous nerve', 'The peroneal anastomotic branch (sural communicating nerve) connects the lateral sural cutaneous nerve to the medial sural cutaneous nerve.'),
('f0281b86-589d-5775-872d-0a1217421aef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'The communicating peroneal nerve is also known as?', '["Medial sural cutaneous nerve","Sural communicating nerve","Lateral sural cutaneous nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sural communicating nerve', 'The communicating peroneal nerve is the same structure as the sural communicating nerve, linking the lateral to the medial sural cutaneous nerve.'),
('fdef88f4-19e7-5769-bc74-567b0f263907'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Innervation of Muscles', 'Which nerve supplies the gluteus maximus muscle?', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Pudendal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve (L5-S2)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior gluteal nerve (L5-S2)', 'Gluteus maximus is supplied by the inferior gluteal nerve (L5-S2), which enters its deep surface near the sacrum.'),
('6dbcf202-c515-5d8a-adf9-a534c75cfd75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The hip bone is formed by fusion of which three bones?', '["Ilium ischium and femur","Ischium pubis and sacrum","Ilium ischium and pubis","Ilium ischium and sacrum","Ilium pubis and sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'Ilium ischium and pubis', 'Ilium, ischium, and pubis fuse at the acetabulum in young adulthood to form the innominate or hip bone. The femur and sacrum articulate with the hip bone but never contribute to its structure.'),
('c52d6eff-4ed2-5264-90bc-991409ed4580'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ilium ossifies from how many centers?', '["5 centers","2 centers","1 center","3 centers (primary)","4 centers"]'::jsonb, '3 centers (primary)', 'The ilium develops from three primary centers that appear during fetal life and coalesce in the region that will later form the acetabulum. After they fuse, the ilium continues to ossify until it unites with the ischium and pubis.'),
('eef89c3b-36b1-5b23-b73a-826bf11c695e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The highest point of the iliac crest is at which vertebral level?', '["L5","S1","L3","L4","T12"]'::jsonb, 'L4', 'The summit of the iliac crest lies level with the spinous process of the fourth lumbar vertebra, a common landmark for lumbar puncture. L3 lies above and L5, S1, and T12 do not mark the crest summit.'),
('5afef01e-ed81-5c2a-9519-7ce455066ad9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior superior iliac spine is at which vertebral level?', '["L4","S3","S2","L5","S1"]'::jsonb, 'S2', 'The posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS), often marked by skin dimples, lies at the level of the second sacral vertebra. S2 is also the level of the middle of the sacroiliac joint and of the termination of the dura in adults.'),
('d8468b56-e6be-5ac6-acf0-07b3c0343875'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The acetabulum is formed by which bones?', '["Ilium and pubis","Ilium ischium and femur","Ilium ischium and pubis","Ischium and pubis","Ilium and ischium"]'::jsonb, 'Ilium ischium and pubis', 'All three bones of the hip bone contribute to the acetabulum: the ilium forms its upper part, the ischium the lower part, and the pubis a small segment anteriorly. The femur articulates with the socket but does not form it.'),
('773d6a48-a0a5-50d2-8ad7-09e69ed7bd2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lunate surface of the acetabulum is covered by?', '["Synovial membrane","Ligament","Articular cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Loose connective tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Articular cartilage', 'The lunate (crescent-shaped) articular surface of the acetabulum is covered by hyaline articular cartilage that articulates with the femoral head. The acetabular floor is non-articular, and the transverse acetabular ligament bridges the notch rather than covering the joint surface.'),
('7f6966d8-d24e-57b9-9794-abcf60407ce6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The acetabular notch is bridged by which ligament?', '["Iliofemoral ligament","Transverse acetabular ligament","Pubofemoral ligament","Ligamentum teres","Ischiofemoral ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse acetabular ligament', 'The transverse acetabular ligament spans the gap of the acetabular notch, converting it into a foramen that transmits vessels and nerves to the femoral head. The iliofemoral, pubofemoral, and ischiofemoral ligaments are capsular reinforcements, and the ligamentum teres attaches within the acetabular fossa.'),
('9b477eb0-6f00-59d0-8593-6c17dd50a780'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The obturator foramen is located between which bones?', '["Pubis and ischium","Pubis and femur","Ilium and femur","Ilium and pubis","Ilium and ischium"]'::jsonb, 'Pubis and ischium', 'The obturator foramen is bounded by the pubis above and the ischium below, closed almost completely by the obturator membrane. The femur and ilium do not take part in forming this foramen.'),
('054bb12b-de47-57e3-9af0-73c779965b2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The obturator foramen is closed by which membrane?', '["Interosseous membrane","Obturator membrane","Fascia lata","None","Cribriform fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator membrane', 'The obturator membrane closes most of the obturator foramen, leaving a small superolateral gap, the obturator canal. The interosseous membrane is found in the forearm and leg, while cribriform fascia overlies the saphenous opening of the thigh.'),
('75657f8c-f05e-55bc-896d-1ce526c5d0a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The greater sciatic notch is located on which bone?', '["Femur","Ischium","Sacrum","Pubis","Ilium"]'::jsonb, 'Ilium', 'The greater sciatic notch lies on the posterior border of the ilium, below the posterior inferior iliac spine. Muscles and vessels that pass between the pelvis and gluteal region travel through this notch.'),
('73ef9658-c649-5858-b7b8-ced462be8bf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lesser sciatic notch is located on which bone?', '["Sacrum","Ischium","Pubis","Femur","Ilium"]'::jsonb, 'Ischium', 'The lesser sciatic notch is found on the posterior border of the ischium, above the ischial tuberosity and below the ischial spine. It transmits the obturator internus tendon and internal pudendal vessels.'),
('16f6f64d-3b32-5f5a-8202-3ea692e12d03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ischial spine separates which two notches?', '["None","Greater and lesser sciatic notches","Pubic and ischial notches","Superior and inferior gluteal notches","Acetabular and obturator notches"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser sciatic notches', 'The ischial spine projects posteromedially from the ischium and marks the boundary between the greater sciatic notch above and the lesser sciatic notch below. The sacrospinous ligament attaches to this spine.'),
('a88727d8-3a63-5410-9e7f-ec26afe5e24c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ischial tuberosity is a feature of which bone?', '["Femur","Ischium","Pubis","Sacrum","Ilium"]'::jsonb, 'Ischium', 'The ischial tuberosity is the strong posterior prominence of the ischium that bears weight in the sitting position. It gives attachment to the hamstring muscles and sacrotuberous ligament.'),
('3e592c65-968e-533e-9b14-6caf9d163e06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Which part of the hip bone bears weight when sitting?', '["Pubic symphysis","Anterior superior iliac spine","Ischial tuberosity","Iliac crest","Acetabulum"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity', 'In the sitting position body weight is transmitted through the ischial tuberosities to the supporting surface. The pubic symphysis, ASIS, iliac crest, and acetabulum are not primary weight-bearing points when seated.'),
('586b9273-a1fd-5f0b-9b2a-5e741a85249b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pubic symphysis is which type of joint?', '["Primary cartilaginous resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Synovial mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secondary cartilaginous (symphysis)"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary cartilaginous (symphysis)', 'The pubic symphysis is a secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis) united by a disc of fibrocartilage between hyaline cartilage plates. Primary cartilaginous joints, such as epiphyseal growth plates, lack this fibrocartilaginous disc.'),
('1022f645-49c8-5231-9efe-3bf4902338ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pubic arch is formed by which bones?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischial tuberosities accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior pubic rami and ischial rami","Superior pubic rami without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac crests leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pubic rami and ischial rami', 'The pubic arch is formed by the inferior pubic rami on each side meeting the ischial rami below the pubic symphysis. Its angle is wider in females than in males.'),
('c25f9e88-4d78-5c42-b9b1-1ceed311b1d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pubic arch angle in males is typically?', '["Exactly 90 degrees","70-80 degrees","80-90 degrees","More than 90 degrees","Less than 70 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'Less than 70 degrees', 'The male pubic arch is relatively narrow, usually measuring less than 70 degrees. This narrower subpubic angle contrasts with the wider angle seen in the female pelvis.'),
('c7c3c891-82b7-5990-a4ae-030a355d7ab5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pubic arch angle in females is typically?', '["More than 80 degrees","70-80 degrees","Exactly 90 degrees","Exactly 80 degrees","Less than 70 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'More than 80 degrees', 'The female pubic arch is wide, generally measuring more than 80 degrees. This broad subpubic angle is one of the classic features distinguishing the female pelvis and is adapted to childbirth.'),
('bff0b247-eba2-53b8-b7b9-bd49badef158'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The greater pelvis is bounded by?', '["Acetabulum and pubis","Ilium and sacrum","Iliac alae","Pubic symphysis and ischium","None"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac alae', 'The greater (false) pelvis is bounded laterally by the flared iliac alae and posteriorly by the sacrum, lying above the pelvic inlet. It forms part of the abdominal cavity and contains abdominal viscera.'),
('c6260ee4-dbee-54a4-9619-d4dffe4c5d5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lesser pelvis is bounded by?', '["Ischial tuberosities only resulting in decreased functional activity","Acetabulum only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic symphysis pubic bones ischium and sacrum","Ilium and iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis pubic bones ischium and sacrum', 'The lesser (true) pelvis is bounded anteriorly by the pubic symphysis and pubic bones, laterally by the ischium, and posteriorly by the sacrum. It lies below the pelvic inlet and contains the pelvic organs.'),
('024520fb-7ccb-5eb8-ac2c-ca56a25279a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic inlet is bounded anteriorly by?', '["Arcuate line","Sacral promontory","Pubic crest","Pubic tubercle","Pubic symphysis"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis', 'The pelvic inlet is bounded anteriorly by the pubic symphysis and pubic crest. The sacral promontory forms its posterior boundary and the arcuate and pectineal lines form the lateral boundaries.'),
('a5ff3b67-b0a6-5df4-b391-afad99725def'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic inlet is bounded posteriorly by?', '["S5 vertebra resulting in decreased functional activity","Sacral promontory and alae","Sacral hiatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral promontory and alae', 'The pelvic inlet is bounded posteriorly by the sacral promontory and the alae (wings) of the sacrum. Together with the pubic symphysis anteriorly and the arcuate lines laterally, they outline the pelvic brim.'),
('4e7ad9a4-6367-5fc1-9a1d-c33520fdfbdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic inlet is bounded laterally by?', '["Arcuate line and pectineal line","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial spine mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic symphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate line and pectineal line', 'The lateral boundaries of the pelvic inlet are the arcuate line of the ilium and the pectineal line of the pubis. These bony ridges together with the sacral promontory and pubic symphysis complete the inlet.'),
('74d84b34-c583-57e9-8209-636bf862562d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic outlet is bounded anteriorly by?', '["Inferior pubic ramus","Pubic tubercle","None","Pubic symphysis","Pubic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic arch', 'The pelvic outlet is bounded anteriorly by the pubic arch formed by the inferior pubic rami. The coccyx bounds it posteriorly and the ischial tuberosities and sacrotuberous ligaments laterally.'),
('81a8cd53-7c30-5dab-ad91-f5ef005bfc0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic outlet is bounded posteriorly by?', '["S5","L5","Coccyx","Sacrum","Sacral promontory"]'::jsonb, 'Coccyx', 'The pelvic outlet is bounded posteriorly by the tip of the coccyx. The ischial tuberosities and sacrotuberous ligaments lie laterally and the pubic arch anteriorly.'),
('4315bb68-8f7b-5fe8-8494-a80a48483c0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic outlet is bounded laterally by?', '["Pubic rami resulting in decreased functional activity","Acetabulum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliac crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischial tuberosities and sacrotuberous ligament","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosities and sacrotuberous ligament', 'The lateral margins of the pelvic outlet are the ischial tuberosities and the sacrotuberous ligaments. The sacrotuberous ligament spans from the sacrum to the ischial tuberosity, completing the outlet on each side.'),
('ecf15b0f-83b3-5a22-88da-fafcf5abc145'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The femoral head is approximately what fraction of a sphere?', '["Three-quarters","One-third","One-quarter","Two-thirds","One-half"]'::jsonb, 'Two-thirds', 'The femoral head forms roughly two-thirds of a sphere, enabling a wide range of motion within the acetabulum. The fovea capitis is a small non-articular pit on its surface.'),
('e644f9be-9e88-5ec4-a2da-8462000c3e9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fovea capitis femoris is located on which part of the femur?', '["Neck","Greater trochanter","Head","Shaft","Lesser trochanter"]'::jsonb, 'Head', 'The fovea capitis femoris is the small pit on the femoral head that gives attachment to the ligamentum teres. The ligament carries the artery of the head, which is clinically important in childhood hip disorders.'),
('102ccab3-d3cd-5c2c-ad4c-d07e9ed6ee05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The femoral neck angle in adults is approximately?', '["90 degrees","110 degrees","125 degrees","150 degrees","180 degrees"]'::jsonb, '125 degrees', 'The angle between the femoral neck and shaft in adults is about 125 degrees. It is larger in childhood and decreases somewhat with age; marked departures from this value define coxa valga or coxa vara.'),
('08527fc1-a4b1-5a3d-85ca-7031f55ede19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Coxa vara refers to a femoral neck angle of?', '["Less than 90 degrees","More than 150 degrees","Less than 120 degrees","More than 130 degrees","Exactly 125 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'Less than 120 degrees', 'Coxa vara is an abnormally small neck-shaft angle, generally less than 120 degrees, which shortens the lower limb and limits hip abduction. The normal adult angle is approximately 125 degrees.'),
('db025f02-a586-5d75-8e4e-6c0c82e1dd67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Coxa valga refers to a femoral neck angle of?', '["Exactly 125 degrees","More than 150 degrees","More than 130 degrees","Less than 120 degrees","Less than 90 degrees"]'::jsonb, 'More than 130 degrees', 'Coxa valga denotes an abnormally large neck-shaft angle, typically greater than about 130 degrees. It lengthens the limb and can predispose to lateral hip instability.'),
('7e4509d7-8c73-58fd-a9b9-2ddfe635b926'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The greater trochanter is located at which level relative to the femoral head?', '["At the same level","Behind the femoral head","Anterior to the femoral head","Above the femoral head","Below the femoral head"]'::jsonb, 'Below the femoral head', 'The greater trochanter projects distally from the junction of the neck and shaft, lying below the level of the femoral head. It is a palpable landmark on the lateral thigh and gives attachment to the gluteal muscles.'),
('e1d8fe46-3218-506e-b404-3ee7afb73b93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lesser trochanter is located on which part of the femur?', '["Medial condyle resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral aspect of the shaft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior aspect of the greater trochanter mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior aspect of the neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posteromedial aspect of the shaft at the junction of neck and shaft"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial aspect of the shaft at the junction of neck and shaft', 'The lesser trochanter projects from the posteromedial side of the femur at the junction of the neck and shaft. It receives the insertion of the iliopsoas tendon, the main hip flexor.'),
('4aba4bd9-2115-53e7-bb95-1cc9abceca8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intertrochanteric line connects which two structures?', '["Lesser trochanter and femoral neck","Greater trochanter and femoral head","None","Greater trochanter and shaft","Greater trochanter and lesser trochanter (anteriorly)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater trochanter and lesser trochanter (anteriorly)', 'The intertrochanteric line runs obliquely across the anterior aspect of the femur, joining the greater and lesser trochanters. It marks the anterior attachment of the hip joint capsule.'),
('b97e8af5-5b2a-562a-8b59-85ee75583ad6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intertrochanteric crest connects which two structures?', '["None","Greater trochanter and femoral head","Greater trochanter and lesser trochanter (posteriorly)","Lesser trochanter and femoral neck","Greater trochanter and shaft"]'::jsonb, 'Greater trochanter and lesser trochanter (posteriorly)', 'The intertrochanteric crest is the ridge on the posterior surface of the femur connecting the greater and lesser trochanters. The quadrate tubercle is a small elevation on its middle part.'),
('4c3c00f2-2853-5387-b851-e55534456c43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The quadrate tubercle is located where?', '["On the lesser trochanter","On the greater trochanter","On the femoral neck","On the intertrochanteric line","On the intertrochanteric crest"]'::jsonb, 'On the intertrochanteric crest', 'The quadrate tubercle lies on the intertrochanteric crest on the posterior femur. It gives part of the insertion of the quadratus femoris muscle, which acts in lateral rotation of the hip.'),
('84e91586-d2ff-5243-af2b-b2c97640b632'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The gluteal tuberosity is located where?', '["Anterior surface of the shaft","Medial surface of the shaft","Posterior surface of the femoral shaft (upper part)","Greater trochanter","Lateral surface of the shaft"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of the femoral shaft (upper part)', 'The gluteal tuberosity is the rough area on the upper part of the posterior femoral shaft, continuous below with the lateral lip of the linea aspera. It receives the insertion of gluteus maximus.'),
('80d512f3-39f6-5029-83d9-5376f622bd1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The linea aspera is located where?', '["Medial surface of the shaft","Middle of the femoral shaft","Posterior surface of the femoral shaft","Anterior surface of the shaft","Lateral surface of the shaft"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of the femoral shaft', 'The linea aspera is the roughened longitudinal ridge on the posterior surface of the femoral shaft. It provides attachment for the adductor muscles and splits above into the medial and lateral lips.'),
('52b4bb6e-3dc9-5984-a519-bbd1c031971e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The linea aspera has which two lips?', '["Anterior and posterior lips","Inner and outer lips","None","Medial and lateral lips","Superior and inferior lips"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral lips', 'The linea aspera consists of medial and lateral lips that diverge upward and downward along the femoral shaft. Muscles such as the adductors insert into these lips, and each lip continues with a different proximal structure.'),
('52f5a9c3-d4af-5bc8-b81e-c753411b388e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial lip of the linea aspera is continuous above with what?', '["Spiral line","Intertrochanteric crest","Pectineal line","Gluteal tuberosity","Intertrochanteric line"]'::jsonb, 'Pectineal line', 'Above, the medial lip of the linea aspera is continuous with the pectineal line, which passes to the lesser trochanter and gives attachment to the pectineus muscle. The lateral lip instead continues with the gluteal tuberosity.'),
('a3295b5a-9f4c-5b8b-be0a-c039f945e0fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral lip of the linea aspera is continuous above with what?', '["Quadrate tubercle","Spiral line","Intertrochanteric line","Gluteal tuberosity","Pectineal line"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteal tuberosity', 'Above, the lateral lip of the linea aspera is continuous with the gluteal tuberosity, which receives the insertion of gluteus maximus. The medial lip, by contrast, continues with the pectineal line.'),
('3d18e754-0f04-5192-83e8-d5f751d836d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial supracondylar line is continuous below with which tubercle?', '["Gerdy''s tubercle","Adductor tubercle","Fibular head","Tibial tuberosity","None"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor tubercle', 'The medial supracondylar line ends below at the adductor tubercle on the medial femoral condyle. The adductor magnus tendon inserts here, and Gerdy''s tubercle is a separate landmark on the tibia.'),
('a5ca32e3-10f8-5bb7-b772-8de51fb580af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral supracondylar line is continuous below with what?', '["Fibular head","Adductor tubercle","Gerdy''s tubercle","Tibial tuberosity","Lateral epicondyle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral epicondyle', 'The lateral supracondylar line passes downwards into the lateral epicondyle of the femur. The adductor tubercle lies on the medial side, while Gerdy''s tubercle and the tibial tuberosity are tibial landmarks.'),
('0ffb84cf-4c9c-5f58-b9ae-51d24c3cb1e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The popliteal surface of the femur is located where?', '["Lateral condyle resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior surface of distal femur accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercondylar notch mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial condyle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior surface of the distal femur between the supracondylar lines"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of the distal femur between the supracondylar lines', 'The popliteal surface is the triangular area on the posterior aspect of the distal femur bounded by the diverging medial and lateral supracondylar lines. It forms part of the floor of the popliteal fossa.'),
('61414d6b-400a-544b-a196-c36031057b18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intercondylar fossa (notch) is located where?', '["Between the trochanters","Between the distal femoral condyles","Anterior surface of distal femur","None","Posterior surface of the shaft"]'::jsonb, 'Between the distal femoral condyles', 'The intercondylar fossa is the deep notch between the medial and lateral femoral condyles on the posterior side. The cruciate ligaments attach within it.'),
('a59cfb7e-4a84-56ff-91db-9d37b1b4d4e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The patellar surface of the femur is located where?', '["Anterior surface of distal femur between condyles","Lateral condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercondylar notch mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior surface of distal femur without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial condyle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior surface of distal femur between condyles', 'The patellar (trochlear) surface is the smooth groove on the anterior aspect of the distal femur between the two condyles. It articulates with the patella during knee flexion and extension.'),
('80af9d1a-e64a-53e7-9764-74ad72c1313d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The patella is which type of bone?', '["Flat bone","Long bone","Sesamoid bone","Short bone","Irregular bone"]'::jsonb, 'Sesamoid bone', 'The patella is the largest sesamoid bone in the body, embedded within the quadriceps tendon. Sesamoid bones develop within tendons, unlike flat, long, short, or irregular bones that form through the typical skeletal blueprint.'),
('de391c9b-cb26-551a-b75b-81483a411550'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The patella ossifies between which ages?', '["10-12 years","12-15 years","3-6 years of age","Birth-1 year","16-18 years"]'::jsonb, '3-6 years of age', 'The patella is the last bone of the body to begin ossification, starting between about 3 and 6 years of age. Its single center may occasionally fail to fuse, producing a bipartite patella.'),
('d4fb484a-2d10-528a-88b5-68e789ea169c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The patella articulates with which part of the femur?', '["Medial condyle only","Intercondylar notch","Lateral condyle only","Patellar surface on the anterior femur","Popliteal surface"]'::jsonb, 'Patellar surface on the anterior femur', 'The patella articulates with the patellar (trochlear) surface on the anterior aspect of the distal femur. The intercondylar notch and popliteal surface are posterior and do not contact the patella.'),
('5891233f-dedd-5fae-a786-3a580de016fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Bipartite patella is caused by?', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Osteoporosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of fusion of ossification centers","Congenital absence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of fusion of ossification centers', 'Bipartite patella results from failure of fusion of the patellar ossification centers, typically producing a separate bone fragment at the superolateral angle. It is usually an asymptomatic developmental variant rather than a result of trauma, infection, or metabolic bone disease.'),
('1c96bbe1-701f-55e6-888c-5a693d39f2bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibia is which type of bone?', '["Flat bone","Irregular bone","Sesamoid bone","Long bone","Short bone"]'::jsonb, 'Long bone', 'Long bones have a shaft (diaphysis) and two expanded ends (epiphyses) and act as levers. The tibia, the main weight-bearing bone of the leg, shows this typical structure, whereas flat, irregular, sesamoid, and short bones do not.'),
('c5a31372-f8dd-53f6-8e9b-fe1202e64ccb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibial plateau refers to which part of the tibia?', '["Tibial shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","Proximal articular surface (medial and lateral condyles)","Distal tibial plafond mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial malleolus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal articular surface (medial and lateral condyles)', 'The tibial plateau is the flat, expanded proximal end bearing the medial and lateral condyles that articulate with the femoral condyles. The shaft, distal plafond, malleolus, and tuberosity are separate regions of the bone.'),
('a9b9bcfd-08d1-5ba5-b9ef-e6b5ceb00c4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intercondylar eminence is located where?', '["On the distal tibia","Between the tibial condyles","On the medial malleolus","On the fibular head","On the patellar ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Between the tibial condyles', 'The intercondylar eminence is the raised area between the two tibial condyles that gives attachment to the cruciate ligaments. It is not found on the distal tibia, malleolus, fibular head, or patellar ligament.'),
('a9a53ddf-c2db-52bd-8c8c-cc7c91f5fb7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibial tuberosity is located where?', '["Anterior surface of the distal tibia","Lateral surface of the tibia","Posterior surface of the tibia","Anterior surface of the proximal tibia","Medial surface of the tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior surface of the proximal tibia', 'The tibial tuberosity is a prominence on the anterior surface of the proximal tibia where the patellar ligament attaches. It is not on the distal, lateral, posterior, or medial aspects.'),
('62a748a8-c93e-5c7d-85c2-42e90460a6c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The soleal line is located where?', '["Medial surface of the tibia","Lateral surface of the tibia","Anterior surface of the tibia","Distal tibia","Posterior surface of the proximal tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior surface of the proximal tibia', 'The soleal line is an oblique ridge on the posterior surface of the proximal tibia that gives origin to part of the soleus and popliteus muscles. The other surfaces and the distal tibia do not carry this line.'),
('ee4d9792-8029-53f2-b543-ca69532d89f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial malleolus is located where?', '["Proximal medial tibia","Proximal fibula","Distal medial tibia","Distal lateral tibia","Distal fibula"]'::jsonb, 'Distal medial tibia', 'The medial malleolus is the medial projection at the distal end of the tibia that forms the medial wall of the ankle joint. The proximal tibia, proximal fibula, and distal fibula (which forms the lateral malleolus) are incorrect locations.'),
('2ab3c58a-d666-5a52-a557-75024c1b0b27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibula is which type of bone?', '["Short bone","Flat bone","Long bone","Irregular bone","Sesamoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Long bone', 'Like the tibia, the fibula has a shaft with proximal and distal ends, so it is a long bone. It is slender and not weight-bearing but provides muscle attachment and ankle stability.'),
('35344c78-ec18-5092-b095-3816ba9ea7f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibula articulates with which bone proximally?', '["Patella resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcaneus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femur mediated by specific receptor activation","Tibia (superior tibiofibular joint)","Talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia (superior tibiofibular joint)', 'The head of the fibula articulates with the lateral condyle of the tibia at the superior tibiofibular joint. Proximally it does not articulate with the femur, patella, talus, or calcaneus.'),
('68797fc6-6ace-58cb-b90e-983eff3f1fef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibula articulates with which bone distally?', '["Tibia and talus","Cuboid","Talus only","Navicular","Calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia and talus', 'Distally the fibula forms the inferior tibiofibular joint with the tibia and, through the lateral malleolus, articulates with the talus at the ankle joint. Cuboid, navicular, and calcaneus do not articulate with the fibula.'),
('d6a88f39-9f1d-53db-a2b9-ff08ee2d586b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral malleolus is located where?', '["Distal tibia","Femur","Proximal fibula","Distal fibula","Proximal tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Distal fibula', 'The lateral malleolus is the distal expansion of the fibula that forms the lateral prominence of the ankle and articulates with the talus. It is not part of the tibia, femur, or proximal fibula.'),
('c96e69e0-ee49-57a9-8162-589c7ee3777b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibular notch is located where?', '["Proximal fibula","Medial malleolus","Distal fibula","Distal tibia (lateral surface)","Proximal tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Distal tibia (lateral surface)', 'The fibular notch is a concavity on the lateral surface of the distal tibia that receives the distal fibula to form the inferior tibiofibular joint. It is not on the fibula itself, the medial malleolus, or the proximal tibia.'),
('0b91ebdb-61e8-592b-9363-1320138f9f0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tarsal bones consist of how many bones?', '["7 tarsal bones","6 tarsal bones","5 tarsal bones","9 tarsal bones","8 tarsal bones"]'::jsonb, '7 tarsal bones', 'The tarsus is made of seven bones: talus, calcaneus, navicular, cuboid, and the three cuneiforms. Counts of 5, 6, 8, or 9 are therefore incorrect.'),
('62653796-4654-554b-8024-f2292247f6cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Which tarsal bone articulates with the tibia and fibula?', '["Talus","Cuneiforms","Cuboid","Calcaneus","Navicular"]'::jsonb, 'Talus', 'The talus is the only tarsal bone that articulates with the leg bones, its trochlea fitting the tibia and fibula at the ankle joint. The calcaneus, cuboid, navicular, and cuneiforms do not directly touch the tibia or fibula.'),
('2847c462-69a3-560e-beb5-a08a0ea1d754'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The talus has how many articular surfaces?', '["5 articular surfaces","2 articular surfaces","4 articular surfaces","3 articular surfaces","6 articular surfaces"]'::jsonb, '5 articular surfaces', 'The talus has five articular facets: a superior trochlear surface for the tibia, medial and lateral malleolar facets, a lower surface for the calcaneus, and an anterior head surface for the navicular. Counts of 2, 3, 4, or 6 do not match this arrangement.'),
('deac7b1d-93d7-51ee-837b-8e5fcdfa6414'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The neck of the talus is angled in which direction?', '["Laterally","Medially","Superiorly","Posteriorly","Anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Medially', 'The neck and head of the talus deviate medially and slightly inferiorly so the head faces medially to articulate with the navicular. Loss of this medial angulation is a feature of clubfoot.'),
('4d2432c8-6c3e-5f63-9d96-ad35fa503da8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcaneus is which tarsal bone?', '["Largest and strongest tarsal bone","Only bone that bears weight","None","Smallest tarsal bone","Second largest tarsal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Largest and strongest tarsal bone', 'The calcaneus is the largest and strongest tarsal bone and transmits body weight to the ground. It is not the smallest or second largest, and it is not the only weight-bearing bone because the talus also carries weight.'),
('5d527fe9-297e-5158-8717-c7d6c1ab941c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sustentaculum tali is located where?', '["On the lateral surface of the calcaneus","On the posterior calcaneus","On the medial surface of the calcaneus","On the inferior calcaneus","On the superior surface of the calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'On the medial surface of the calcaneus', 'The sustentaculum tali is a shelf-like projection on the medial surface of the calcaneus that supports the talus and is grooved beneath for the flexor hallucis longus tendon. It is not on the lateral, posterior, inferior, or superior aspects.'),
('76f5ff5e-8906-544b-9304-912f332cf5ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The peroneal trochlea is located where?', '["On the medial surface of the calcaneus","On the inferior calcaneus","On the superior calcaneus","On the lateral surface of the calcaneus","On the posterior calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral surface of the calcaneus', 'The peroneal trochlea is a small ridge on the lateral surface of the calcaneus that separates the tendons of the peroneus longus and brevis. It is not on the medial, inferior, superior, or posterior surfaces.'),
('5893d407-762a-508a-8d97-aadec03c082b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcaneal tuberosity is located where?', '["Posterior aspect of the calcaneus","Lateral aspect","Medial aspect","Superior aspect","Inferior aspect of the calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior aspect of the calcaneus', 'The calcaneal tuberosity is the posterior projection of the calcaneus that gives attachment to the tendo calcaneus (Achilles tendon) and bears weight in standing. It is not on the lateral, medial, superior, or inferior aspects.'),
('f9d0edba-c6d9-52be-8941-1882ca0c748c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The navicular bone articulates with which bones proximally?', '["Talus","Cuboid","Calcaneus","None","Cuneiforms"]'::jsonb, 'Talus', 'The proximal surface of the navicular articulates with the head of the talus. Distally it faces the three cuneiforms, whereas the cuboid lies laterally and the calcaneus sits below the talus.'),
('4ffab644-589a-5fed-bc83-e35041cdeaca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The navicular bone articulates with which bones distally?', '["Cuboid resulting in decreased functional activity","Three cuneiform bones","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Talus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcaneus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Three cuneiform bones', 'The distal surface of the navicular presents three facets for the medial, intermediate, and lateral cuneiforms. The talus articulates proximally, and the cuboid articulates with the lateral edge of the navicular rather than its distal surface.'),
('c1375b7a-8324-5f19-b907-04412e2a32f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The navicular tuberosity is located where?', '["Dorsal side","Lateral side of the navicular","Medial side of the navicular","Superior side","Inferior side"]'::jsonb, 'Medial side of the navicular', 'The navicular tuberosity is a projection on the medial side of the bone that gives attachment to the tibialis posterior tendon. It is not on the dorsal, lateral, superior, or inferior sides.'),
('ac80dc7d-32fa-58c8-8fa7-4cbeb2ad33e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The cuboid bone articulates with which bones proximally?', '["Talus","Calcaneus","None","Navicular","Cuneiforms"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus', 'The proximal surface of the cuboid articulates with the anterior end of the calcaneus at the calcaneocuboid joint. The talus, navicular, and cuneiforms are not its proximal partners.'),
('9f369c20-2f07-568e-bbe2-3d5d059e126e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The cuboid bone articulates with which bones distally?', '["Fourth and fifth metatarsals","Talus","Navicular","First second third metatarsals","Calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth and fifth metatarsals', 'Distally the cuboid articulates with the bases of the fourth and fifth metatarsals. The first three metatarsals articulate with the cuneiforms instead, and the talus and navicular lie proximal to it.'),
('ba9d5f8f-1e72-5fc0-9691-d9339c203d65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The peroneal groove is located where?', '["On the superior surface of the cuboid","On the lateral surface of the cuboid","On the inferior surface of the cuboid","On the posterior cuboid","On the medial surface of the cuboid"]'::jsonb, 'On the inferior surface of the cuboid', 'The peroneal groove is an oblique channel on the inferolateral surface of the cuboid that transmits the peroneus longus tendon. It is not on the superior, lateral, posterior, or medial surfaces.'),
('86c752ab-d3b3-56ad-b050-7c8024f9733b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'How many cuneiform bones are there?', '["4 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 (medial and lateral) mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 (medial intermediate lateral)"]'::jsonb, '3 (medial intermediate lateral)', 'There are three cuneiform bones in each foot, named medial, intermediate, and lateral, lying between the navicular and the first three metatarsals. No other count is correct.'),
('bcbf27a4-6680-512a-a531-0ceddcfe94ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial cuneiform articulates with which metatarsal?', '["Third metatarsal","Fourth metatarsal","Fifth metatarsal","Second metatarsal","First metatarsal"]'::jsonb, 'First metatarsal', 'The medial cuneiform articulates distally with the base of the first metatarsal. The second and third metatarsals articulate with the intermediate and lateral cuneiforms, and the fourth and fifth metatarsals articulate with the cuboid.'),
('d78be0d9-2b30-55e3-8096-bb316619483a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intermediate cuneiform articulates with which metatarsal?', '["Third metatarsal","Second metatarsal","Fourth metatarsal","Fifth metatarsal","First metatarsal"]'::jsonb, 'Second metatarsal', 'The intermediate (middle) cuneiform articulates distally with the base of the second metatarsal, which sits recessed between the other cuneiforms. The other metatarsals articulate with different tarsal bones.'),
('75bb0d25-6604-5702-bbdf-317afd5557ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral cuneiform articulates with which metatarsal?', '["Second metatarsal","Fifth metatarsal","First metatarsal","Third metatarsal","Fourth metatarsal"]'::jsonb, 'Third metatarsal', 'The lateral cuneiform articulates distally with the base of the third metatarsal. The fourth and fifth metatarsals articulate with the cuboid, and the first and second metatarsals with the medial and intermediate cuneiforms.'),
('6fd0bbcb-051e-5120-8665-89fd8c8b48ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'How many metatarsal bones are there?', '["4 metatarsals","5 metatarsals","3 metatarsals","7 metatarsals","6 metatarsals"]'::jsonb, '5 metatarsals', 'Each foot contains five metatarsal bones, numbered one to five from medial to lateral. Counts of 3, 4, 6, or 7 are therefore incorrect.'),
('b9cdb11f-7531-5436-a8b0-f34659cf8330'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The first metatarsal is characterized by?', '["Being the shortest and thickest","Having head","Being the most slender","Having base","Being the longest"]'::jsonb, 'Being the shortest and thickest', 'The first metatarsal is the shortest and thickest of the metatarsals because it carries a large share of body weight. It is not the longest or most slender, and possessing a head and base is true of all metatarsals, not a distinguishing feature.'),
('c3ba02e6-72d5-5d8e-9a3d-7d6f165af423'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fifth metatarsal has a prominent tuberosity where?', '["At the base (styloid process)","At the neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","At the shaft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the head leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the base (styloid process)', 'The fifth metatarsal bears a prominent tuberosity, the styloid process, at its base for attachment of the peroneus brevis tendon. It is not at the neck, shaft, or head.'),
('6e761444-50d1-52bc-a670-c1f87564cab9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tuberosity of the fifth metatarsal is for attachment of which tendon?', '["Flexor hallucis longus","Peroneus brevis tendon","Peroneus longus tendon","Tibialis anterior tendon","Tibialis posterior tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Peroneus brevis tendon', 'The peroneus brevis tendon inserts on the tuberosity at the base of the fifth metatarsal; avulsion at this site is a common injury. Peroneus longus crosses the sole to the first metatarsal, and the tibialis tendons attach elsewhere.'),
('3ad94a67-c1b1-5b39-b6d7-94e7a9fe9255'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'How many phalanges are there in the foot?', '["12 phalanges","18 phalanges","16 phalanges","10 phalanges","14 phalanges"]'::jsonb, '14 phalanges', 'Each foot has fourteen phalanges: the great toe has two and each of the other four toes has three (2 + 12 = 14). Counts of 10, 12, 16, or 18 are incorrect.'),
('944c5afc-4e07-57ba-8d9b-2521a9d9c16c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Each foot has how many proximal phalanges?', '["4","1","3","5","2"]'::jsonb, '5', 'Every toe, including the great toe, has a proximal phalanx, so each foot contains five proximal phalanges. The middle phalanges number only four because the great toe has no middle phalanx.'),
('522a43c1-98bb-5684-8d3a-9770c10da81f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Each foot has how many middle phalanges?', '["3","1","2","5","4"]'::jsonb, '4', 'The great toe (hallux) has no middle phalanx, whereas the other four toes each have one, giving four middle phalanges per foot. Counts of 1, 2, 3, or 5 are incorrect.'),
('74ad66f2-ffbf-5028-ad1d-44d7fad1db91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Each foot has how many distal phalanges?', '["1","2","4","3","5"]'::jsonb, '5', 'All five toes end in a distal phalanx, including the great toe, so each foot has five distal phalanges. The other counts are incorrect.'),
('bec11db3-cb08-567b-b3a2-61fdb0782e4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the body with a length of approximately?', '["One-half of body height","One-fifth of body height","One-third of body height","One-quarter of body height","Two-thirds of body height"]'::jsonb, 'One-quarter of body height', 'The femur is the longest and strongest bone in the body, measuring approximately one-quarter of a person''s height. The other fractions do not match this relationship.'),
('418f5248-cd95-52c8-8b6a-6c112543c06a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The femoral head is directed in which direction?', '["Superomedially and slightly anteriorly","Superolaterally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferomedially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anteriorly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superomedially and slightly anteriorly', 'The femoral head projects superomedially and slightly anteriorly so that it fits into the acetabulum. Superolateral, inferomedial, pure posterior, or pure anterior directions do not describe its orientation.'),
('a783f59f-f887-5c6f-8585-9733d4a635d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The femoral neck is about how long?', '["3 cm","2 cm","8 cm","5 cm","10 cm"]'::jsonb, '5 cm', 'The femoral neck is approximately 5 cm long, connecting the head to the shaft at an angle. Lengths of 2, 3, 8, or 10 cm are not consistent with its anatomy.'),
('d6e3c414-6246-530c-b7cb-d54ab70519f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The angle of anteversion of the femoral neck is how many degrees?', '["20-25 degrees","12-15 degrees","40-50 degrees","5-10 degrees","30-40 degrees"]'::jsonb, '12-15 degrees', 'In adults the femoral neck is normally anteverted 12-15 degrees relative to the shaft. Larger values are seen in infancy and abnormal torsional states, while smaller values are abnormal.'),
('3b8756d3-dac0-54eb-b653-15d910a04469'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcar femorale is located where?', '["In the lesser trochanter","In the femoral shaft","In the greater trochanter","In the femoral neck (medial part)","In the femoral head"]'::jsonb, 'In the femoral neck (medial part)', 'The calcar femorale is a thick vertical plate of dense bone within the medial part of the femoral neck that reinforces it. It is not located in the head, shaft, or either trochanter.'),
('019ef8f3-8747-513a-bb75-f75fedc73e9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcar femorale is important for?', '["Attachment of gluteal muscles","Blood supply of femoral head","Attachment of hamstrings","Weight transmission from femoral head to shaft","Joint stability"]'::jsonb, 'Weight transmission from femoral head to shaft', 'The calcar femorale transmits weight and stress from the femoral head to the shaft, strengthening the neck against compressive loads. It does not serve muscle attachment, head blood supply, or joint stability.'),
('06aefbcf-a9b0-5a36-86d3-fe7b1f79917e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Cancellous bone in the femoral neck follows which pattern?', '["Longitudinal trabeculae","None","Radial trabeculae","Circular trabeculae","Tension and compression trabeculae"]'::jsonb, 'Tension and compression trabeculae', 'The cancellous bone of the femoral neck is arranged into compression and tension trabeculae that follow the lines of mechanical stress. Longitudinal, radial, or circular arrangements do not describe this pattern, and there is no absence of trabeculae other than Ward''s triangle.'),
('9683e2bc-00c1-57bc-b595-4850f6261da1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Ward''s triangle is located where?', '["In the shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","In the greater trochanter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the femoral head mediated by specific receptor activation","In the femoral neck (between trabecular groups)","At the knee leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the femoral neck (between trabecular groups)', 'Ward''s triangle lies in the femoral neck where the principal compression and tension trabeculae meet; this relatively weak area is a site of trabecular rarefaction and fracture. It is not in the head, shaft, trochanter, or knee.'),
('a48f3e8d-ad14-5e62-92f4-9dbb4bc8f3f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The angle of the femoral neck in children is approximately?', '["180 degrees","150 degrees","110 degrees","90 degrees","125 degrees"]'::jsonb, '150 degrees', 'The angle of inclination of the femoral neck is about 150 degrees in children and decreases to approximately 125 degrees in adults. Values of 90, 110, 125, or 180 degrees do not represent the childhood angle.'),
('dc2fefff-f634-5aae-b386-6e35ce12e74e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The patella has how many articular facets for the femur?', '["7 facets","4 facets","3 facets","5 facets","6 facets"]'::jsonb, '7 facets', 'The posterior articular surface of the patella bears seven facets: three horizontal pairs (superior, middle, and inferior) plus a vertical medial ''odd'' facet for the medial femoral condyle. Counts of 3, 4, 5, or 6 are incorrect.'),
('55861d20-eef6-5ab6-9132-68d8ad87daad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The thickest cartilage in the body is found where?', '["On the tibial plateau resulting in decreased functional activity","On the patella (patellar articular surface)","On the femoral head mediated by specific receptor activation","On the calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the patella (patellar articular surface)', 'The articular cartilage on the patellar surface is the thickest in the body because it must withstand the high compressive forces of the patellofemoral joint. Cartilage on the tibial plateau, femoral head, talus, and calcaneus is thinner.'),
('ae8940e2-0efc-5e53-a529-60324a9fcbbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibial shaft has which cross-sectional shape in its middle third?', '["Circular","Quadrilateral","Hourglass","Triangular","Oval"]'::jsonb, 'Triangular', 'In its middle third the tibial shaft is triangular in cross-section, with anterior, medial, and interosseous (lateral) borders and three corresponding surfaces. Circular, quadrilateral, hourglass, and oval shapes are not characteristic.'),
('1c2dee07-1ecb-5760-a8a2-508c9a61de6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The anterior border of the tibia is called?', '["Crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prominence mediated by specific receptor activation","Edge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shin (subcutaneous border)"]'::jsonb, 'Shin (subcutaneous border)', 'The sharp anterior border (crest) of the tibia is subcutaneous along its length and forms the shin, which is easily palpable on the front of the leg. The other terms do not name this particular border.'),
('7a8360fa-dbf2-5b76-8628-88c2c588a886'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibular shaft has how many borders?', '["5 borders resulting in decreased functional activity","3 borders (anterior posterior interosseous)","2 borders mediated by specific receptor activation","4 borders without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 border leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 borders (anterior posterior interosseous)', 'The fibular shaft has three longitudinal borders: anterior, posterior, and interosseous. These borders separate the medial, lateral, and posterior surfaces and give attachment to the interosseous membrane and compartmental fascia.'),
('fcd4b091-441d-51ee-be33-664faa7490f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibular shaft has how many surfaces?', '["3 surfaces (medial lateral posterior)","4 surfaces accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 surfaces mediated by specific receptor activation","5 surfaces without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 surfaces (medial lateral posterior)', 'The fibular shaft presents three surfaces: medial, lateral, and posterior. The medial surface faces the tibia and is related to the interosseous membrane, while the lateral and posterior surfaces give origin to muscles of the lateral and superficial posterior compartments.'),
('12a685f9-6193-59ef-8767-43e68e7acbd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial surface of the distal tibia continues inferiorly as which structure?', '["Tibial plafond","Medial malleolus","Soleal line","Tibial tuberosity","Intercondylar eminence"]'::jsonb, 'Medial malleolus', 'The medial surface of the distal tibia continues downward as the medial malleolus, which articulates with the medial aspect of the talus and provides the medial buttress of the ankle mortise. The tibial plafond is the inferior articular surface, while the soleal line and tibial tuberosity are features of the shaft.'),
('5dafd4ef-7bc9-5d63-9e80-5aecc29cc7d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The distal tibia has which articular surface for the talus?', '["Tibial plafond (inferior articular surface)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior facet mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial malleolar facet without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral malleolar facet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial plafond (inferior articular surface)', 'The tibial plafond is the inferior (distal) articular surface of the tibia that articulates with the dome of the talus. The medial malleolus provides a separate facet for the medial talar surface, but the main weight-bearing articular surface for the talus is the plafond.'),
('536558b5-be3a-51f1-9b18-02cca9333bd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral surface of the distal tibia has which notch?', '["Calcaneal notch","Fibular notch","Talar notch","Peroneal notch","Interosseous notch"]'::jsonb, 'Fibular notch', 'The distal tibia shows a triangular fibular notch on its lateral surface that accommodates the distal fibula, completing the ankle mortise. This notch is reinforced by ligaments between the tibia and fibula.'),
('cbb4db6a-20e2-5184-b04b-45a8d4c9a4c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior surface of the distal tibia has which groove?', '["None","Groove for flexor hallucis longus tendon","Groove for flexor digitorum longus","Groove for tibialis posterior tendon","Groove for peroneus longus tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Groove for flexor hallucis longus tendon', 'The posterior aspect of the distal tibia carries a shallow groove that accommodates the flexor hallucis longus tendon as it courses toward the foot. The tendons of flexor digitorum longus and tibialis posterior pass more medially, and peroneus longus is not related to the tibia.'),
('d859d073-5e0d-5dc5-a4a2-e227043c42a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The trochlea of the talus is which shape?', '["Square resulting in decreased functional activity","Wider posteriorly than anteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circular mediated by specific receptor activation","Oval without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wider anteriorly than posteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Wider anteriorly than posteriorly', 'The trochlea (dome) of the talus is wider anteriorly than posteriorly. The wedge fits tightly into the mortise in dorsiflexion, giving stability, whereas in plantarflexion the narrower part fits loosely, which predisposes to ankle injury.'),
('a0623392-a494-5756-b28c-54509a644142'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the talus articulates with which bones?', '["Navicular and calcaneus (anterior and middle facets)","Cuboid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Navicular only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuneiforms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular and calcaneus (anterior and middle facets)', 'The rounded head of the talus articulates anteriorly with the navicular and inferiorly with the anterior and middle facets of the calcaneus through the talocalcaneonavicular joint. The cuboid and cuneiforms lie more distally and do not articulate with the talus.'),
('524ccd0f-fbe0-5f21-a03f-c69202b6d85f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sustentaculum tali contains a groove for which tendon?', '["Peroneus brevis","Peroneus longus","Flexor hallucis longus tendon","Tibialis posterior tendon","Flexor digitorum longus tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor hallucis longus tendon', 'The undersurface of the sustentaculum tali is grooved by the tendon of flexor hallucis longus as it passes forward into the sole. The peroneal tendons curve behind the lateral malleolus, while tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus pass medial to the sustentaculum.'),
('c6e26331-fb29-5320-8929-f2c130f4a15c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior tubercle of the talus sometimes has which accessory bone?', '["Os trigonum","Os vesalianum","Os peroneum","Os intermetatarseum","Os tibiale externum"]'::jsonb, 'Os trigonum', 'Os trigonum is an accessory ossicle arising from failure of fusion of the lateral tubercle of the posterior process of the talus. It may be mistaken for an avulsion fracture on ankle radiographs.'),
('def91544-7bb9-5b97-bb37-5e2e4474f40f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial tubercle of the posterior talus is for attachment of which ligament?', '["None","Posterior talotibial ligament","Calcaneofibular ligament","Anterior talofibular ligament","Deltoid ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior talotibial ligament', 'The medial tubercle of the posterior process of the talus gives attachment to the posterior talotibial ligament, part of the deep group of the medial collateral (deltoid) ligament complex. The calcaneofibular and talofibular ligaments attach to the lateral malleolus instead.'),
('6be5cd5f-4afa-5481-9a22-9126086c15c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sinus tarsi is located between which bones?', '["Navicular and cuneiform","Talus and navicular","Talus and calcaneus","Calcaneus and cuboid","Calcaneus and navicular"]'::jsonb, 'Talus and calcaneus', 'The sinus tarsi is the conical osseous canal situated between the talus above and the calcaneus below, formed when the sulcus tali and sulcus calcanei are apposed. It lodges the interosseous talocalcaneal and cervical ligaments.'),
('196866fb-9183-57ab-aa68-0434997238cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sinus tarsi contains which structure?', '["Interosseous talocalcaneal ligament","Talocalcaneonavicular joint space","None","Talus head","Cervical ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Interosseous talocalcaneal ligament', 'The sinus tarsi contains the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament together with fat and vessels, and the cervical ligament lies more anteriorly within the canal. The talocalcaneonavicular joint is a separate, more anterior articulation and does not lie inside the sinus tarsi.'),
('76c5fc47-943e-58fb-a22a-2454695c2856'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The cervical ligament is located where?', '["In the ankle joint","In the subtalar joint","In the talonavicular joint","In the calcaneocuboid joint","In the sinus tarsi (anterior part)"]'::jsonb, 'In the sinus tarsi (anterior part)', 'The cervical ligament is a strong band lying in the anterolateral part of the sinus tarsi, passing from the neck of the talus to the superior surface of the calcaneus. Along with the interosseous talocalcaneal ligament it reinforces the subtalar region.'),
('b77d4a94-f24f-55b8-b6ec-5d6a0e4e0f75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The bicipital groove of the calcaneus is located where?', '["Medial surface","Posterior surface","Superior surface of the calcaneus","Lateral surface","Inferior surface of the calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior surface of the calcaneus', 'The bicipital (interfacetal) groove of the calcaneus lies on the superior surface between the posterior and the anterior/middle talar facets. This groove contributes to the floor of the sinus tarsi when the talus is in place.'),
('52cd7a12-0e02-55b6-b9a4-4f58c71609bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The anterior calcaneal articular facet articulates with which part of the talus?', '["Posterior talus","Body of talus","Head of talus","Neck of talus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Head of talus', 'The anterior calcaneal facet, together with the middle facet, supports the head of the talus as part of the talocalcaneonavicular joint. The body of the talus instead articulates with the posterior calcaneal facet at the subtalar joint.'),
('9d31e98a-e7b8-5fe8-bb11-f0c4bbe94411'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The middle calcaneal articular facet is located where?', '["On the sustentaculum tali","On the lateral calcaneus","On the superior calcaneus","On the anterior calcaneus","On the posterior calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'On the sustentaculum tali', 'The middle calcaneal facet is situated on the upper surface of the sustentaculum tali and articulates with the corresponding facet on the talar head. The posterior (large) facet lies on the superior surface of the rest of the bone for the subtalar joint.'),
('dc045df0-ebac-59e1-bd45-d047dc291fde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior calcaneal articular facet articulates with which part of the talus?', '["Neck of talus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head of talus mediated by specific receptor activation","Trochlea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Body of talus (posterior calcaneal facet)"]'::jsonb, 'Body of talus (posterior calcaneal facet)', 'The large posterior facet on the upper surface of the calcaneus articulates with the inferior aspect of the body of the talus, forming the subtalar (talocalcanean) joint. The head and neck of the talus are instead related to the anterior and middle facets.'),
('d44ce3d0-03e4-5706-b998-c2e454eb6997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcaneal sulcus is located where?', '["On the medial calcaneus","Between the anterior and middle facets","Between the cuboid and calcaneus","On the lateral calcaneus","Between the middle and posterior facets"]'::jsonb, 'Between the middle and posterior facets', 'The calcaneal sulcus is a groove on the upper surface of the calcaneus lying between the middle/anterior facets and the posterior facet. It forms the floor of the sinus tarsi when the sulcus tali lies above it.'),
('abc9c9b8-9b84-5276-b4f4-36b7e57dcd12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tarsal sinus is formed by which two sulci?', '["Talar sulcus and cuboid sulcus","Cuboid sulcus and talar sulcus","Navicular sulcus and calcaneal sulcus","None","Calcaneal sulcus and talar sulcus"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneal sulcus and talar sulcus', 'The tarsal sinus (sinus tarsi) is formed by the apposition of the calcaneal sulcus inferiorly and the talar sulcus superiorly. This conical canal lodges the interosseous talocalcaneal and cervical ligaments.'),
('e578126d-0f22-5183-9b58-b86ff43d0f10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The navicular has a tuberosity on which side?', '["Superior side","Lateral side","Inferior side","Posterior side","Medial side"]'::jsonb, 'Medial side', 'The navicular bone bears a rough tuberosity on its medial (plantar) aspect, the chief insertion of the tibialis posterior tendon. An accessory ossicle, the os tibiale externum, may develop adjacent to this tuberosity.'),
('037c9022-2e8f-5cb3-b388-3bbc228adbc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Accessory navicular (os tibiale externum) is located where?', '["Adjacent to the calcaneus","Adjacent to the medial cuneiform","Adjacent to the talus head","None","Adjacent to the navicular tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Adjacent to the navicular tuberosity', 'The os tibiale externum is an accessory ossicle that lies within or near the tibialis posterior tendon, adjacent to the tuberosity of the navicular. It can be painful and is distinguished from an avulsion fracture of the tuberosity.'),
('cc652873-0672-5009-a1da-495fb4fc5684'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The cuboid has a groove for which tendon?', '["Flexor digitorum longus","Tibialis posterior tendon","Peroneus brevis tendon","Flexor hallucis longus","Peroneus longus tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Peroneus longus tendon', 'The plantar surface of the cuboid is crossed obliquely by a groove for the tendon of peroneus longus, which then runs to the base of the first metatarsal and the medial cuneiform. The flexor digitorum longus and tibialis posterior tendons pass on the medial side of the foot.'),
('156eca06-7e8d-5a8b-b448-deb3baa3dfe4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The cuboid tuberosity is located where?', '["On the lateral side of the cuboid","On the posterior surface","On the medial side of the cuboid","On the plantar surface","On the dorsal surface"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral side of the cuboid', 'The cuboid presents a tuberosity on its lateral (plantar-lateral) aspect, from which the plantar calcaneocuboid (short plantar) ligament arises and near which the peroneus longus tendon changes direction. The dorsal and medial surfaces bear articular facets rather than a tuberosity.'),
('c8329424-c7f5-55c7-9d4e-8fac6950292d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial cuneiform is the largest of the cuneiforms and is wedge-shaped with the base where?', '["Dorsally (superiorly)","Anteriorly","Laterally","Plantarly (inferiorly)","Medially"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsally (superiorly)', 'The medial cuneiform is the largest cuneiform and is wedge-shaped with its base directed dorsally (superiorly). This orientation contributes the keystone effect that supports the transverse arch of the foot.'),
('35eda576-8bec-5328-86bc-0c21e7958d06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intermediate cuneiform is wedge-shaped with the base where?', '["Dorsally","Medially","Laterally","Plantarly","Posteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsally', 'The intermediate cuneiform is wedge-shaped with its base directed dorsally. It lies between the medial and lateral cuneiforms and articulates mainly with the base of the second metatarsal.'),
('953e67aa-5449-573c-9977-660a4422069a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral cuneiform is wedge-shaped with the base where?', '["Anteriorly","Medially","Plantarly","Laterally","Dorsally"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsally', 'The lateral cuneiform is wedge-shaped with its base facing dorsally, continuing the transverse arch pattern of the midfoot. It articulates with the third metatarsal and with the cuboid.'),
('1e83c43e-afea-51e6-b322-aa65eecde658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the first metatarsal has a facet for which cuneiform?', '["Navicular","Cuboid","Lateral cuneiform","Medial cuneiform","Intermediate cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cuneiform', 'The base of the first metatarsal articulates proximally with the medial cuneiform, forming the relatively mobile first tarsometatarsal joint. It does not normally articulate with the cuboid or navicular.'),
('6e9f69f6-3c71-5a7c-81f0-9d3879b62a2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the second metatarsal articulates with which bones?', '["Intermediate cuneiform and medial and lateral cuneiforms","Lateral cuneiform only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Navicular only mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial cuneiform only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuboid only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate cuneiform and medial and lateral cuneiforms', 'The recessed base of the second metatarsal is wedged between all three cuneiforms, principally the intermediate, with additional facets for the medial and lateral cuneiforms. This mortise arrangement makes the second tarsometatarsal joint the keystone and least mobile of the midfoot.'),
('62e75ba4-af3d-51d3-a731-272ed5edb0a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the third metatarsal articulates with which bone?', '["Medial cuneiform","Cuboid","Navicular","Intermediate cuneiform","Lateral cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cuneiform', 'The base of the third metatarsal articulates mainly with the lateral cuneiform. It also has minor facets for the adjacent metatarsals, but the principal midfoot articulation is with the lateral cuneiform.'),
('e03c1c92-6fa0-5bab-b4ba-3f4cc1aa3d6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the fourth metatarsal articulates with which bones?', '["Lateral cuneiform only","Cuboid and lateral cuneiform","Medial cuneiform","Intermediate cuneiform","Cuboid only"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboid and lateral cuneiform', 'The base of the fourth metatarsal articulates with the cuboid and, on its medial side, with the lateral cuneiform. This dual articulation is characteristic of the fourth ray and distinguishes it from the second and third metatarsals.'),
('47bc762c-1391-5ab8-912f-85554ff6dc3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the fifth metatarsal articulates with which bone?', '["Navicular","Cuboid","Cuboid and lateral cuneiform","Calcaneus","Lateral cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Cuboid', 'The base of the fifth metatarsal articulates with the cuboid and bears a prominent lateral tuberosity (styloid process), into which peroneus brevis inserts. The navicular and calcaneus are not directly connected to this bone.'),
('1ea03801-0ab2-5136-881e-1da6ca9a8509'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The styloid process of the fifth metatarsal is also known as?', '["Peroneal process","Tuberosity of the fifth metatarsal","None","Lateral malleolus","Styloid process of fibula"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberosity of the fifth metatarsal', 'The lateral projection at the base of the fifth metatarsal is the styloid process, also called the tuberosity of the fifth metatarsal, giving attachment to peroneus brevis and part of the plantar fascia. It is a common site of avulsion fractures.'),
('2af15780-921b-5e14-8487-30a0e0bdbd4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial sesamoid bone of the foot is located where?', '["In the tibialis posterior tendon","In the peroneus longus tendon","In the flexor digitorum longus tendon","In the lateral head of flexor hallucis brevis tendon","In the medial head of flexor hallucis brevis tendon"]'::jsonb, 'In the medial head of flexor hallucis brevis tendon', 'The two sesamoid bones lie in the tendons of the two heads of flexor hallucis brevis beneath the head of the first metatarsal; the medial sesamoid lies in its medial head. They protect the tendon and enhance the lever arm at the first metatarsophalangeal joint.'),
('bf3a86a7-8156-57a6-a69f-d8dbc4e86952'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral sesamoid bone of the foot is located where?', '["In the peroneus longus tendon","In the medial head of flexor hallucis brevis tendon","In the lateral head of flexor hallucis brevis tendon","In the flexor digitorum longus tendon","In the peroneus brevis tendon"]'::jsonb, 'In the lateral head of flexor hallucis brevis tendon', 'The lateral sesamoid bone lies within the lateral head of the flexor hallucis brevis tendon, beneath the lateral side of the first metatarsophalangeal joint. Together with the medial sesamoid it shields the tendon and reduces friction during hallux movement.'),
('abf117bf-c4f1-51c6-a7de-f0c44a2d5e4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The phalanges of the foot are which type of bone?', '["Sesamoid bones","Irregular bones","Long bones","Flat bones","Short bones"]'::jsonb, 'Long bones', 'The phalanges are classified as long bones because they possess a diaphysis, metaphyses, and epiphyses and grow by endochondral ossification, even though their absolute length is short. Sesamoid and short bones have different shapes and ossification patterns.'),
('3cdc9b63-4748-5416-a676-eb2fbb135c97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The proximal phalanx of the great toe has which features?', '["Only base and head","Base shaft and head","Only shaft and head","None","Only base and shaft"]'::jsonb, 'Base shaft and head', 'Like all phalanges, the proximal phalanx of the great toe has a base, a shaft, and a head. The base articulates with the first metatarsal head and the head articulates with the distal phalanx; all three parts must be present for the bone to function.'),
('893b9cf4-2ecb-50b5-b198-415795a08377'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The distal phalanx of the great toe has which feature?', '["Two heads","Articular facet for metatarsal","None","Tuberosity for tendon insertion","Expanded distal end (ungual tuberosity)"]'::jsonb, 'Expanded distal end (ungual tuberosity)', 'The distal phalanx of the great toe has an expanded distal end, the ungual tuberosity, which supports the nail bed. The flexor hallucis longus inserts on the proximal part of the plantar surface rather than on the ungual tuft.'),
('8e26356d-0920-52bb-b7f7-284fe6198ad6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ossification center of the distal femoral epiphysis appears at which age?', '["2 years resulting in decreased functional activity","At birth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 year mediated by specific receptor activation","3 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Around 36 weeks of gestation (just before birth)"]'::jsonb, 'Around 36 weeks of gestation (just before birth)', 'The distal femoral epiphyseal ossification center appears around week 36 of gestation and is used clinically as a marker of fetal maturity. Its presence just before term contrasts with the femoral head, which appears at 4-6 months after birth.'),
('4de95107-d882-543d-8020-2a7bd81112a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ossification center of the proximal tibial epiphysis appears when?', '["2 years","Around 36 weeks gestation","At birth (around 40 weeks)","6 months","1 year"]'::jsonb, 'At birth (around 40 weeks)', 'The proximal tibial epiphysis begins to ossify at or just before term, around 40 weeks of gestation. It provides most of the growth of the tibia and fuses with the shaft around 16-18 years.'),
('7035a03d-b680-5406-946e-2dd36379c448'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The femoral head ossification center appears at which age?', '["4-6 months","2 years","At birth","1 year","3 years"]'::jsonb, '4-6 months', 'The ossification center of the femoral head appears between 4 and 6 months after birth. Its delayed appearance in developmental dysplasia of the hip makes it useful in radiologic assessment of that condition.'),
('e2294545-a452-557d-8406-2b47ca16d718'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The greater trochanter ossification center appears at which age?', '["1 year","5-6 years","At birth","6 months","2-4 years"]'::jsonb, '2-4 years', 'The greater trochanter begins to ossify between 2 and 4 years and fuses with the femoral shaft around 16-17 years. Its appearance follows that of the femoral head and precedes that of the lesser trochanter.'),
('836ddd31-3a30-5f95-b7a5-da8dd3de9c08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lesser trochanter ossification center appears at which age?', '["12-14 years","10-12 years","8-10 years","4-6 years","2-4 years"]'::jsonb, '8-10 years', 'The lesser trochanter ossifies around 8-10 years, later than the greater trochanter, and fuses with the femur around 16-17 years. It forms a traction apophysis for the iliopsoas tendon and is therefore subjected to avulsive stress during adolescence.'),
('d2afe643-b821-5250-b75f-4d87022f5ec8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The distal fibula ossification center appears at which age?', '["5-6 years","6-8 years","3-4 years","1-2 years","At birth"]'::jsonb, '1-2 years', 'The distal fibular (lateral malleolar) epiphysis begins to ossify around 1-2 years and closes around 15-16 years. Because the tibial epiphysis fuses earlier, twisting injuries in adolescents can produce a Tillaux-type fracture separating the lateral fibular epiphysis.'),
('925bf822-7b5b-5e82-ab45-b37d03498981'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The proximal fibula ossification center appears at which age?', '["1-2 years","3-4 years","6-8 years","At birth","5-6 years"]'::jsonb, '3-4 years', 'The proximal fibular epiphysis, which forms the fibular head, ossifies around 3-4 years and closes approximately 18-20 years. The fibula is non-weight-bearing, and its head provides attachment for the fibular collateral ligament and biceps femoris tendon.'),
('a39d3b5b-786e-5a55-ac2e-d60f2e237293'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcaneus ossification center appears at which age?', '["2 years resulting in decreased functional activity","3 years accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At birth mediated by specific receptor activation","Around 3 months of gestation","1 year leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around 3 months of gestation', 'The calcaneus is the first bone of the foot to ossify, its center appearing around the third month of gestation. Ossification of the talus follows at about the sixth month, and cuboid ossification occurs near term.'),
('af952b9f-4998-5724-a066-11935de3903f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The talus ossification center appears at which age?', '["Around 6 months of gestation","2 years","At birth","3 months after birth","1 year"]'::jsonb, 'Around 6 months of gestation', 'The talus is the second tarsal bone to ossify, its center appearing around the sixth month of gestation. Because much of the talus remains cartilaginous at birth and its blood supply is comparatively limited, it is vulnerable to avascular necrosis after injuries.'),
('4c8c9765-b6e7-537e-9906-df370f95a078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'When does the femoral head epiphysis fuse with the shaft?', '["18-20 years","10-12 years","20-22 years","14-17 years","8-10 years"]'::jsonb, '14-17 years', 'The epiphysis of the femoral head fuses with the neck and shaft between 14 and 17 years, earlier than the greater and lesser trochanters. Because the growth plate remains active during adolescence, a slipped capital femoral epiphysis occurs at this site.'),
('ad032d68-f3cf-5a4c-bd56-204c7b889cd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'When does the distal femoral epiphysis fuse with the shaft?', '["10-12 years","20-22 years","18-20 years","14-16 years","16-18 years"]'::jsonb, '16-18 years', 'The distal femoral epiphysis fuses with the shaft around 16-18 years. Although it ossifies earlier than other epiphyses around the knee (appearing at about 36 weeks of gestation), it closes comparatively late and contributes a large share of growth of the lower limb.'),
('3f2cad52-63c5-5634-b3b5-7db907a5b090'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'When does the proximal tibial epiphysis fuse with the shaft?', '["18-20 years","14-16 years","16-18 years","10-12 years","12-14 years"]'::jsonb, '16-18 years', 'The proximal tibial epiphysis fuses with the shaft around 16-18 years. This epiphysis contributes most of the growth of the tibia, and the tibial tuberosity apophysis is the last portion of it to unite with the shaft.'),
('29bb86d3-d45e-51eb-b703-c8d7dc835af8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The Lisfranc joint is located where?', '["Between calcaneus and cuboid","Between tarsal bones and metatarsal bases","Between tibia and talus","Between navicular and cuneiforms","Between talus and calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Between tarsal bones and metatarsal bases', 'The Lisfranc (tarsometatarsal) joint is the junction between the tarsal bones and the bases of the metatarsals. It is clinically important because injuries at this site disrupt the transverse arch. The other options name subtalar, tibiotarsal, or intertarsal articulations.'),
('ddeea73b-50b9-513b-9e8e-2aad55efe564'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The Chopart joint is located where?', '["Between midfoot and forefoot resulting in decreased functional activity","Between hindfoot and midfoot (talonavicular and calcaneocuboid)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Between ankle and hindfoot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between tibia and talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between hindfoot and midfoot (talonavicular and calcaneocuboid)', 'The Chopart (transverse tarsal) joint separates the hindfoot from the midfoot and is made up of the talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints. The midfoot-forefoot line is the Lisfranc joint, and the ankle-level choices are tibiotarsal articulations.'),
('b2aa8ec3-bdd3-50a5-a0ec-81c94fa8305c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial longitudinal arch of the foot consists of which bones?', '["Only calcaneus and metatarsals","All tarsals and metatarsals","Calcaneus cuboid and two lateral metatarsals","Calcaneus talus navicular cuneiforms and first 3 metatarsals","Talus navicular and calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus talus navicular cuneiforms and first 3 metatarsals', 'The medial longitudinal arch is formed by the calcaneus, talus, navicular, the three cuneiforms, and the first three metatarsals, with its summit at the head of the talus. The lateral arch instead uses the cuboid and the 4th-5th metatarsals.'),
('fb197cbd-86ac-55f8-99bb-bd9a392088ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral longitudinal arch of the foot consists of which bones?', '["Talus navicular and cuneiforms","Only cuboid and metatarsals","Calcaneus and cuneiforms","Calcaneus cuboid and 4th-5th metatarsals","Calcaneus talus and navicular"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus cuboid and 4th-5th metatarsals', 'The lateral longitudinal arch is low and shallow, comprising the calcaneus, cuboid, and the 4th-5th metatarsals. The medial arch includes the talus, navicular, cuneiforms, and first three metatarsals, so those combinations are incorrect.'),
('6f106b70-2991-5cc3-b707-862a0aba1fb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The transverse arch of the foot is formed by which bones?', '["Navicular and talus","Calcaneus and talus","Only metatarsal heads","Only tarsal bones","Cuneiforms cuboid and metatarsal bases"]'::jsonb, 'Cuneiforms cuboid and metatarsal bases', 'The transverse arch is formed by the bases of the metatarsals together with the cuboid and the cuneiforms, with the second metatarsal at its summit. Talus, calcaneus, and navicular belong to the longitudinal arches and are not part of it.'),
('19c6a360-8c7e-55ba-9acf-e3c87fe06712'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The highest point of the medial longitudinal arch is located at?', '["Cuboid","Calcaneus","Head of talus","Base of first metatarsal","Tubercle of navicular"]'::jsonb, 'Head of talus', 'The head of the talus is the keystone and highest point of the medial longitudinal arch, receiving body weight from above. Collapse of its support (spring ligament) results in flat foot.'),
('27ae32c8-528d-569d-8b36-63b40b3da62a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular ligament) supports which arch?', '["All arches","Transverse arch","None","Medial longitudinal arch","Lateral longitudinal arch"]'::jsonb, 'Medial longitudinal arch', 'The plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament spans from the sustentaculum tali to the navicular, supporting the head of the talus and thus holding up the medial longitudinal arch. The long plantar ligament chiefly supports the lateral arch.'),
('d7b010d2-7c42-5a30-9907-34a6138fb6d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The long plantar ligament supports which arch?', '["Medial longitudinal arch","None","Transverse arch","Lateral longitudinal arch","All arches"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral longitudinal arch', 'The long plantar ligament passes from the calcaneus to the cuboid and metatarsal bases and is the principal ligamentous support of the lateral longitudinal arch. The spring ligament is the major support of the medial arch.'),
('f45f1e7a-85ed-5f7e-a0cb-368165ed9079'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Pes planus describes which condition?', '["None","High arched foot","Talipes equinovarus","Flat foot (fallen arches)","Club foot"]'::jsonb, 'Flat foot (fallen arches)', 'Pes planus is flattening or loss of the medial longitudinal arch, producing a flat foot. Pes cavus (high arched foot) and talipes equinovarus (club foot) are separate deformities.'),
('914a9834-3a42-5a26-bfae-4ec0a43b3bbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Pes cavus describes which condition?', '["None","High arched foot","Talipes equinovalgus","Flat foot","Club foot"]'::jsonb, 'High arched foot', 'Pes cavus is an excessively high (exaggerated) medial longitudinal arch. Flat foot (pes planus) and the talipes deformities are distinct clinical entities affecting arch height or hindfoot position.'),
('564a255c-4eb1-5605-93b8-98a2e8f330d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic brim (pelvic inlet) is also known as?', '["None","Arcuate line","Iliopectineal line","Linea terminalis","Pectineal line"]'::jsonb, 'Linea terminalis', 'The pelvic brim, the oblique ridge dividing the greater from the lesser pelvis, is collectively named the linea terminalis. It is formed by the sacral promontory, arcuate lines, pectineal lines, and pubic crests; the arcuate and pectineal lines are only components of it.'),
('40276ed5-6fa4-5857-9ac5-9da5f0e18a40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The false pelvis lies where relative to the pelvic brim?', '["Posterior to the pelvic brim","Anterior to the pelvic brim","Lateral to the pelvic brim","Above the pelvic brim","Below the pelvic brim"]'::jsonb, 'Above the pelvic brim', 'The false (greater) pelvis lies above the pelvic brim and contains the lower abdominal viscera, being part of the abdominal cavity. The true (lesser) pelvis lies below the brim.'),
('2da1bcc2-f3ae-58ca-8413-587d170ce212'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The true pelvis lies where relative to the pelvic brim?', '["Above the pelvic brim","Lateral","Below the pelvic brim","Anterior","Posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Below the pelvic brim', 'The true (lesser) pelvis lies below the pelvic brim and contains the pelvic viscera and birth canal. The false (greater) pelvis lies above the brim.'),
('2b461a93-b361-56d5-8b9a-03cef620bfd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral promontory is located where?', '["Anterior superior margin of the S1 vertebra","Posterior surface of sacrum","None","Lateral mass of sacrum","Inferior end of sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior superior margin of the S1 vertebra', 'The sacral promontory is the prominent anterior superior edge of the S1 vertebral body. It marks the posterior margin of the pelvic inlet and forms part of the linea terminalis.'),
('e7e72e0a-a776-56ed-b274-7bef25bfd712'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral ala is which part of the sacrum?', '["Inferior articular process","Lateral wing-like part of S1","Sacral hiatus","Sacral canal","Superior articular process"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wing-like part of S1', 'The sacral ala is the lateral wing-like expansion of the sacrum formed by the fused costal elements and transverse process of S1. It bears the auricular surface for articulation with the ilium.'),
('41979c6c-2492-524c-81f2-008cd28ab192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral hiatus is located where?', '["At the middle of the sacrum","At the inferior end of the sacral canal","At the lateral edge of the sacrum","None","At the superior end of the sacral canal"]'::jsonb, 'At the inferior end of the sacral canal', 'The sacral hiatus is the inferior opening of the sacral canal, produced by failure of the S4 and S5 laminae to fuse. It transmits the coccygeal nerves and is the landmark for caudal epidural anesthesia.'),
('b6ec91f9-4874-5575-b5f1-3e84eac61eda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral cornu are located where?', '["On the anterior sacrum","On the lateral sacrum","None","On either side of the sacral hiatus","On the superior sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'On either side of the sacral hiatus', 'The sacral cornua are the two small bony projections that flank the sacral hiatus, derived from the inferior articular processes of S5. They serve as palpable landmarks for locating the hiatus.'),
('8a9e63cc-0952-5e8a-86d0-d564a7d63ecf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral hiatus is the termination of which structure?', '["Spinal cord","Neural arch","Sacral canal","Vertebral canal","None"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral canal', 'The sacral hiatus is the inferior termination of the sacral canal. The sacral canal is itself the distal continuation of the vertebral canal within the sacrum.'),
('c8d44fdc-ee3c-58a1-ba4a-c527b678ae28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The median sacral crest is formed by fusion of which processes?', '["Spinous processes of sacral vertebrae","Costal elements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transverse processes mediated by specific receptor activation","Articular processes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinous processes of sacral vertebrae', 'The median sacral crest is the midline ridge on the dorsal surface formed by fusion of the sacral spinous processes. It represents the spinous process line of the sacral vertebrae.'),
('4b82856d-77e4-549f-bc49-4c70f5f1243e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intermediate sacral crest is formed by fusion of which processes?', '["Transverse processes resulting in decreased functional activity","Articular processes (fused zygapophyses)","Costal elements mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinous processes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Articular processes (fused zygapophyses)', 'The pair of intermediate sacral crests is formed by the fused articular processes (zygapophyses) of the sacral vertebrae. They lie just medial to the posterior sacral foramina.'),
('f2a24696-1e00-5d33-8f14-ae354a5313df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral sacral crest is formed by fusion of which processes?', '["Costal elements resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinous processes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transverse processes of sacral vertebrae","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Articular processes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse processes of sacral vertebrae', 'The lateral sacral crests are formed by the fused transverse processes of the sacral vertebrae, lying lateral to the posterior sacral foramina. The median and intermediate crests arise from spinous and articular processes respectively.'),
('f19884b2-db77-59b0-a3b2-bbb59651a6ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic surface of the sacrum has how many pairs of anterior sacral foramina?', '["2 pairs","1 pair","5 pairs","3 pairs","4 pairs"]'::jsonb, '4 pairs', 'The pelvic (anterior) surface of the sacrum bears four pairs of anterior sacral foramina transmitting the ventral rami of the upper four sacral nerves. There are likewise four pairs posteriorly, not five.'),
('0f08bd7b-73df-5dcf-a4e2-3b891fe9e828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The dorsal surface of the sacrum has how many pairs of posterior sacral foramina?', '["2 pairs","3 pairs","4 pairs","5 pairs","1 pair"]'::jsonb, '4 pairs', 'The dorsal surface of the sacrum has four pairs of posterior sacral foramina that transmit the dorsal rami of the upper four sacral nerves. This matches the four pairs of anterior foramina.'),
('654a6cf0-7799-53c9-9d9f-db2dc9bcc3f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral canal is the continuation of which structure?', '["None","Spinal cord","Vertebral canal","Neural foramen","Central canal"]'::jsonb, 'Vertebral canal', 'The sacral canal is the distal continuation of the vertebral canal as it descends through the sacrum. It carries the cauda equina nerve roots and ends inferiorly at the sacral hiatus.'),
('942ea7a0-5120-5070-9c20-6b45c694a9d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral tuberosity is located where?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","On the median crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the lateral surface of the sacrum (posterior)","On the sacral hiatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the anterior sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral surface of the sacrum (posterior)', 'The sacral tuberosity is a roughened area on the lateral (dorsal) surface of the sacrum just posterior to the auricular surface. It gives attachment to the sacroiliac and interosseous sacroiliac ligaments.'),
('129f2333-ad5a-5126-8eb0-40ad442f0f03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The auricular surface of the sacrum articulates with which bone?', '["Coccyx resulting in decreased functional activity","Ilium (sacroiliac joint)","Pubis mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ilium (sacroiliac joint)', 'The ear-shaped auricular surface on the lateral side of the sacrum articulates with the auricular surface of the ilium to form the sacroiliac joint. The coccyx and L5 articulate elsewhere.'),
('f1983d69-f210-521a-8c7b-b8ecb3741b5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacroiliac joint is which type of joint?', '["Hinge joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Synovial (partially) and fibrous (partially) - diarthrosis and amphiarthrosis","Pivot joint mediated by specific receptor activation","Ball and socket without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Condylar joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial (partially) and fibrous (partially) - diarthrosis and amphiarthrosis', 'The sacroiliac joint is transitional: a synovial (diarthrotic) joint anteriorly and a fibrous syndesmosis (amphiarthrosis) posteriorly. This limited-mobility design transfers load from the axial skeleton to the lower limbs, unlike hinge, pivot, or ball-and-socket joints.'),
('16024f5f-65c8-55bb-89b9-59107277c90b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The coccyx is formed by fusion of how many vertebrae?', '["4 rudimentary vertebrae","5 vertebrae","3 vertebrae","6 vertebrae","2 vertebrae"]'::jsonb, '4 rudimentary vertebrae', 'The coccyx is a triangular bone formed by the fusion of four rudimentary coccygeal vertebrae. It represents the vestigial tail and has no vertebral canal.'),
('44bb9c6b-7032-55be-9892-a5c06f66ac4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The coccyx articulates superiorly with which bone?', '["Ischium","Ilium","Sacrum (S5)","L5","Pubis"]'::jsonb, 'Sacrum (S5)', 'The base of the coccyx articulates superiorly with the apex of the sacrum at the sacrococcygeal joint. It does not attach directly to L5, ilium, ischium, or pubis.'),
('742a26d8-0a72-5103-aae2-9f9fca506b05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The coccygeal cornu are located where?', '["None","Laterally on the coccyx","Anteriorly on the coccyx","Posteriorly on the first coccygeal vertebra","Superiorly on the coccyx"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly on the first coccygeal vertebra', 'The coccygeal cornua are two upward-curving projections on the posterior aspect of the first coccygeal vertebra. They articulate with the sacral cornua through the sacrococcygeal ligament.'),
('95ca6c47-ff57-50b8-9ca9-b5defc776cb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The obturator crest is located where?', '["On the ilium","On the superior pubic ramus","On the ischial ramus","On the inferior pubic ramus","On the acetabulum"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior pubic ramus', 'The obturator crest runs along the superior pubic ramus between the pubic tubercle and the acetabulum, giving attachment to the obturator membrane. The other named sites belong to different parts of the innominate bone.'),
('2702d104-1f87-501a-991a-3e973ef04211'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pectineal line (pecten pubis) is located where?', '["On the ilium resulting in decreased functional activity","On the ischium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the pubic symphysis mediated by specific receptor activation","On the superior pubic ramus (between pubic tubercle and arcuate line)","On the inferior pubic ramus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior pubic ramus (between pubic tubercle and arcuate line)', 'The pectineal line (pecten pubis) is a sharp ridge on the superior pubic ramus running from the pubic tubercle to the arcuate line of the ilium. With the arcuate line it forms part of the pelvic brim (linea terminalis).'),
('e798f927-41a8-5103-ac82-ec493f20b6b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pubic tubercle is located where?', '["At the medial end of the pubic crest","On the pubic symphysis","On the ischial ramus","At the lateral end of the pubic crest","On the inferior pubic ramus"]'::jsonb, 'At the lateral end of the pubic crest', 'The pubic tubercle is a prominent point at the lateral end of the pubic crest, just above and lateral to the symphysis. It receives the inguinal ligament and marks the lateral extent of the superficial inguinal ring.'),
('97ed3af0-8904-586e-a076-4c8c8cef9966'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pubic crest is located where?', '["On the pubic symphysis","On the anterior pubis","On the inferior surface of the pubis","On the superior surface of the pubis","On the posterior pubis"]'::jsonb, 'On the superior surface of the pubis', 'The pubic crest is a ridge on the superior surface of the pubis running from the symphysis medially to the pubic tubercle laterally. The inguinal ligament attaches at its lateral end on the tubercle.'),
('c981c746-55ad-59fe-9b64-8603174aeca1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The symphyseal surface of the pubis is located where?', '["Superior surface","Inferior surface","Lateral surface","Medial surface of the pubic bone","Posterior surface"]'::jsonb, 'Medial surface of the pubic bone', 'The symphyseal surface is the oval medial surface of the pubic bone that is covered by hyaline cartilage and articulates with the opposite pubis at the pubic symphysis.'),
('5e385ae0-1c3e-555c-8002-8060f85907d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The anterior gluteal line is located where?', '["On the posterior ilium","On the medial surface of the ilium","On the lateral surface of the ilium","On the iliac crest","On the anterior ilium"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral surface of the ilium', 'The anterior gluteal line lies on the lateral (gluteal) surface of the ilium, separating the origins of the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus. All three gluteal lines are on this lateral surface.'),
('3b05f157-5d69-5543-820d-af5983f41ffb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior gluteal line is located where?', '["On the lateral surface of the ilium (posterior part)","On the anterior ilium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the ischium mediated by specific receptor activation","On the medial surface of the ilium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral surface of the ilium (posterior part)', 'The posterior gluteal line occupies the posterior part of the lateral surface of the ilium, dividing the origin of gluteus maximus above from gluteus medius below. It is short and often poorly defined.'),
('46e3f463-d9c5-5cab-b68a-dfc03753265a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The inferior gluteal line is located where?', '["Below the acetabulum resulting in decreased functional activity","On the posterior ilium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the lateral surface of the ilium above the acetabulum","On the medial ilium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral surface of the ilium above the acetabulum', 'The inferior gluteal line lies on the lateral surface of the ilium immediately above the acetabulum, marking the lower limit of the gluteal surface. It separates the gluteus minimus from the origin of the rectus femoris.'),
('ce97fabd-0bd0-530b-9c7a-66423d1dd7ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The iliac tuberosity is located where?', '["On the posterior inferior iliac spine resulting in decreased functional activity","On the medial surface of the ilium (posterior to auricular surface)","On the lateral surface of the ilium mediated by specific receptor activation","On the anterior superior iliac spine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the medial surface of the ilium (posterior to auricular surface)', 'The iliac tuberosity is a roughened elevation on the medial surface of the ilium, posterior and superior to the auricular surface. It gives attachment to the interosseous sacroiliac and dorsal sacroiliac ligaments.'),
('809cae31-aa7e-57e9-a5ca-90182aa11c0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) is located where?', '["Above the pubic symphysis","On the ischium","Below the acetabulum","At the posterior end of the iliac crest","At the anterior end of the iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'At the anterior end of the iliac crest', 'The anterior superior iliac spine is the prominent bony projection at the anterior end of the iliac crest. It gives attachment to the inguinal ligament and sartorius and is a key surface landmark.'),
('8c9cad24-d33f-5621-b0c2-be42b41d1ccd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The anterior inferior iliac spine (AIIS) is located where?', '["On the posterior ilium","On the ischium","Below the ASIS above the acetabulum","Above the ASIS","On the pubis"]'::jsonb, 'Below the ASIS above the acetabulum', 'The anterior inferior iliac spine lies below the ASIS and just above the acetabulum on the anterior border of the ilium. It gives origin to the straight head of the rectus femoris.'),
('9a7a904d-ec8d-582e-8b57-2b46a390f402'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior superior iliac spine (PSIS) is located where?', '["Below the posterior inferior iliac spine","At the anterior end of the iliac crest","On the sacrum","On the ischium","At the posterior end of the iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'At the posterior end of the iliac crest', 'The posterior superior iliac spine is the bony projection at the posterior end of the iliac crest, corresponding to the dimples of Venus over the sacroiliac joint. It gives attachment to the posterior sacroiliac and sacrotuberous ligaments.'),
('285af98c-6f60-5419-802d-9c56cb3b5c39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior inferior iliac spine (PIIS) is located where?', '["On the anterior ilium","On the ischium","Above the PSIS","On the pubis","Below the PSIS"]'::jsonb, 'Below the PSIS', 'The posterior inferior iliac spine lies below the posterior superior iliac spine near the posterior margin of the greater sciatic notch. The sacrospinous ligament attaches in this region.'),
('2bf05a50-ede7-5e32-a0b7-6a574e26ad94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The iliac fossa is located where?', '["On the lateral surface of the ilium","On the medial surface of the iliac ala","On the ischium","On the gluteal surface","On the pubis"]'::jsonb, 'On the medial surface of the iliac ala', 'The iliac fossa is the smooth concave depression on the medial surface of the iliac ala, giving origin to the iliacus muscle. It contains no articular cartilage and is bounded above by the iliac crest.'),
('045d0fe2-d1da-5656-a211-5b6eaacaf3a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The arcuate line of the ilium is located where?', '["On the posterior ilium resulting in decreased functional activity","On the medial surface of the ilium (part of pelvic brim)","On the lateral surface of the ilium mediated by specific receptor activation","On the ischium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the pubis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the medial surface of the ilium (part of pelvic brim)', 'The arcuate line is a smooth ridge on the medial surface of the ilium from the auricular surface to the pectineal line. It forms part of the pelvic brim (linea terminalis), separating the false from the true pelvis.'),
('c7ee2f90-3651-50b4-8078-294ab45200cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The acetabular fossa is non-articular and located where?', '["At the rim of the acetabulum","None","At the floor of the acetabulum (central)","At the lunate surface","At the acetabular notch"]'::jsonb, 'At the floor of the acetabulum (central)', 'The acetabular fossa is the non-articular central floor of the acetabulum, occupied by the fat pad and the ligament of the head of the femur. Articulation occurs only at the surrounding horseshoe-shaped lunate surface.'),
('fbe80896-bd69-58b7-8a36-b091c57df190'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The acetabular labrum is which type of structure?', '["Hyaline cartilage rim","Ligamentous ring","Fibrocartilaginous rim","Fibrous rim","Synovial fold"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrocartilaginous rim', 'The acetabular labrum is a fibrocartilaginous rim attached to the acetabular margin that deepens the socket and enhances hip stability. Hyaline cartilage instead lines the lunate surface, and the joint capsule is the fibrous element.'),
('64f7553a-8ff4-5512-9514-c8cc687a47dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The transverse acetabular ligament bridges which structure?', '["Acetabular rim","Lunate surface","None","Acetabular notch","Acetabular fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Acetabular notch', 'The transverse acetabular ligament bridges the gap at the inferior acetabular notch, converting it into a foramen that carries vessels and nerves to the hip joint. It is a band of the fibrocartilaginous labrum but does not cover the lunate surface.'),
('3038244e-c6e5-517e-9237-e13cbd7fdf4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The obturator groove is located where?', '["On the ilium resulting in decreased functional activity","On the ischium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the inferior surface of the superior pubic ramus","On the pubic crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the superior pubic ramus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the inferior surface of the superior pubic ramus', 'The obturator groove runs on the inferior surface of the superior pubic ramus and, together with the obturator membrane, forms the obturator canal. This canal transmits the obturator vessels and nerve out of the pelvis.'),
('374194a3-ccfb-5ae2-97da-3161b7628718'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ischiopubic ramus is formed by which structures?', '["Ischial tuberosity and ischial ramus","Inferior pubic ramus and ischial ramus","None","Pubic crest and pubic tubercle","Superior pubic ramus and ischial ramus"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pubic ramus and ischial ramus', 'The ischiopubic ramus results from the fusion of the inferior pubic ramus with the ischial ramus at the junction of the body of the pubis and ischium. It forms the inferior and medial boundary of the obturator foramen and bears the attachments of the adductor muscles.'),
('97cfb32d-8c69-5fde-ae61-0e70384b40f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The angle of torsion of the femur is how many degrees at birth?', '["12-15 degrees","5-10 degrees","20-25 degrees","40-50 degrees","30-40 degrees"]'::jsonb, '30-40 degrees', 'At birth the femur has marked anteversion (torsion) of about 30-40 degrees, which gradually reduces to the adult value of roughly 12-15 degrees as the child grows. The lower values represent adult or further-settled angles, while 40-50 degrees overestimates the neonatal angle.'),
('b7dfb5c4-adb7-547a-9523-454d956f9c5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Increased femoral anteversion is associated with which condition?', '["High arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Out-toeing gait accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flat foot mediated by specific receptor activation","Pigeon-toed gait (intoeing)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pigeon-toed gait (intoeing)', 'Excess femoral anteversion rotates the femoral head forward relative to the condyles, turning the lower limb medially and producing an intoeing or pigeon-toed gait. Out-toeing is instead linked with femoral retroversion; high arch and flat foot are not caused by anteversion.'),
('b04c0c6b-e9eb-5821-92b1-108ed2d8f590'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'Retroversion of the femoral neck describes?', '["Femoral neck rotated posteriorly (angle of torsion reversed)","Increased varus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral neck rotated anteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal position without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased valgus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral neck rotated posteriorly (angle of torsion reversed)', 'In femoral retroversion the neck is rotated posteriorly, reversing the normal anterior torsion and producing an out-toeing gait. Anterior rotation is the normal anteversion, while varus/valgus describe neck-shaft angle, not torsion.'),
('f170bff9-9967-50a8-b3be-7d89c8016758'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibial plateau slopes posteriorly at what angle?', '["10 degrees","25 degrees","15 degrees","5 degrees","20 degrees"]'::jsonb, '10 degrees', 'The lateral tibial plateau slopes posteriorly about 10 degrees relative to the shaft, a feature that helps posteriorly subluxate the femur on the tibia and assists terminal locking of the knee. The other values do not match the accepted anatomic inclination.'),
('724fb9c6-fa23-5bfa-8410-f205b884f1ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibial tuberosity is located how far below the knee joint line?', '["5-6 cm below","2-3 cm below","4-5 cm below","1 cm below","At the joint line"]'::jsonb, '2-3 cm below', 'The tibial tuberosity, the insertion of the patellar ligament, lies 2-3 cm below the knee joint line on the upper anterior tibia. Distances of 4-6 cm place the landmark too low, and it does not lie at the joint line itself.'),
('a2078a29-fc3f-52ed-a5da-23ac87889be0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The distal tibia and fibula form which structure?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mortise (ankle mortise)","Arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mortise (ankle mortise)', 'The medial malleolus of the tibia, the tibial plafond, and the lateral malleolus of the fibula form the ankle mortise that grips the talus, permitting dorsiflexion and plantarflexion while limiting side-to-side motion. Canal, arch, and foramen are not formed by this articulation.'),
('facca7b7-de57-55be-a932-a3a832208adb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ankle mortise is formed by which bony landmarks?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial malleolus tibial plafond and lateral malleolus","Tibia and calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Talus and calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial malleolus and tibial shaft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial malleolus tibial plafond and lateral malleolus', 'The mortise is the tibiofibular groove that holds the talus: the medial malleolus, the tibial plafond above, and the lateral malleolus laterally. The calcaneus and the tibial shaft are not part of the mortise walls.'),
('29ca9fa3-fee6-564c-9cbf-d15777cf39e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The female pelvis is characterized by which shape?', '["Anthropoid (oval inlet)","Android (heart-shaped inlet)","Platypelloid (flat inlet)","None","Gynecoid (rounded inlet)"]'::jsonb, 'Gynecoid (rounded inlet)', 'The typical female pelvis is gynecoid with a rounded or slightly oval inlet that favors vaginal delivery. Android (heart-shaped), anthropoid (oval), and platypelloid (flat) inlets are less typical female variants.'),
('2fabf1fe-807e-5d73-9fa9-a18d8ab4545b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The android pelvis has which shape?', '["Flat inlet","Oval inlet","Rounded inlet","Heart-shaped inlet","Wedge-shaped inlet"]'::jsonb, 'Heart-shaped inlet', 'The android pelvis, common in males, has a heart-shaped inlet with a narrowed anterior segment. A rounded inlet is gynecoid, an oval inlet is anthropoid, and a flat inlet is platypelloid.'),
('bb4c98d7-e2e3-5332-922f-32c6cbd57486'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The anthropoid pelvis has which shape?', '["Heart-shaped inlet resulting in decreased functional activity","Flat inlet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wedge-shaped inlet mediated by specific receptor activation","Rounded inlet without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oval inlet (anteroposterior > transverse)"]'::jsonb, 'Oval inlet (anteroposterior > transverse)', 'The anthropoid pelvis has an oval inlet whose anteroposterior diameter exceeds the transverse diameter. A rounded inlet is gynecoid, a heart-shaped inlet is android, and a flat inlet is platypelloid.'),
('fe84c32b-c92e-5b2f-882f-e3b2d0ef3905'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The platypelloid pelvis has which shape?', '["Flat inlet (transverse > AP)","Wedge-shaped inlet","Oval inlet","Heart-shaped inlet","Rounded inlet"]'::jsonb, 'Flat inlet (transverse > AP)', 'The platypelloid pelvis has a flattened inlet in which the transverse diameter is greater than the anteroposterior one. Heart-shaped (android), oval (anthropoid), and rounded (gynecoid) inlets are distinct shapes.'),
('c7e90c98-cf40-5657-a2ac-8a564efc87a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral angle in females is typically?', '["More horizontal","None","Less acute (more vertical)","More acute (greater posterior tilt)","Same in both sexes"]'::jsonb, 'More acute (greater posterior tilt)', 'The female sacrum is tilted more posteriorly, making the sacral angle more acute and increasing the anteroposterior pelvic outlet for delivery. A more horizontal sacrum would reduce the outlet diameter.'),
('fd3afd7b-21a3-5817-a7ff-2698d8bcdaa8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The subpubic angle in females is typically?', '["Wide (greater than 80 degrees)","None","Narrow (less than 70 degrees)","Approximately 90 degrees","Variable"]'::jsonb, 'Wide (greater than 80 degrees)', 'The female subpubic angle is wide, greater than about 80 degrees, widening the pelvic outlet for childbirth. Males show a narrow angle, generally less than 70 degrees.'),
('40a338bb-ccae-53c9-b9d6-5485712cd6e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ischial spines in females are?', '["Same as in males","More prominent and closer together","Less prominent and further apart","More medially placed","None"]'::jsonb, 'Less prominent and further apart', 'Female ischial spines are smaller and more everted (further apart), enlarging the pelvic midcavity for fetal passage. Prominent, closely spaced spines narrow the midpelvis and are a male feature.'),
('651809ca-13ad-598d-b515-6eb7740f9c92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The greater sciatic notch in females is?', '["Same as in males","Wider and more shallow","More vertical","Narrower and deeper","None"]'::jsonb, 'Wider and more shallow', 'In females the greater sciatic notch is wide and shallow, while in males it is narrow and deep. The broad notch in females is a useful sex-determining feature of the pelvis.'),
('fd4fafa4-7baa-559f-a168-6b4107b76aae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The acetabulum in females is?', '["Same as in males","Smaller and faces more anteriorly","More vertically oriented","Larger and faces more laterally","None"]'::jsonb, 'Smaller and faces more anteriorly', 'The female acetabulum is relatively small and faces more anteriorly as the iliac wings flare less laterally. The male acetabulum is larger and directed more laterally.'),
('88cae1be-ee73-5d88-95de-a67ed7adfa29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The obturator foramen in females is?', '["Same as in males","Heart-shaped","Triangular (oval)","Keyhole-shaped","Round"]'::jsonb, 'Triangular (oval)', 'The female obturator foramen is triangular to oval, whereas the male foramen is round or oval. These differences in the pelvic bones assist in sex determination.'),
('97729af5-f252-50ba-bdba-92f3ffd4a6e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic inlet in females is?', '["More oval","Rounded and larger transversely","Heart-shaped and smaller","More triangular","Same as in males"]'::jsonb, 'Rounded and larger transversely', 'The gynecoid female inlet is rounded and wider in its transverse dimension, accommodating the fetal head. A heart-shaped, small inlet is typical of the male (android) pelvis.'),
('28b5adac-717a-5c9f-aaee-1a0edc6cea71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic cavity in females is?', '["Deep and narrow","Same as in males","Shallow and roomy","More conical","More cylindrical"]'::jsonb, 'Shallow and roomy', 'The female pelvic cavity is shallow and broad (roomy), a shape favorable for vaginal delivery. The male cavity is deeper and narrower, converging toward the outlet.'),
('575462da-428a-5d63-8da3-17e35f04c7f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic outlet in females is?', '["Same as in males","More triangular","More oval","Smaller with narrower subpubic angle","Larger with wider subpubic angle"]'::jsonb, 'Larger with wider subpubic angle', 'The female pelvic outlet is larger, with a wide subpubic angle that expands the space for fetal exit. The male outlet is smaller and narrowed by an acute subpubic angle.'),
('e00191e0-1c32-5377-9784-ea505ebd51d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male pelvis is generally?', '["Heavier and with more prominent markings","Same as female accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lighter and smoother mediated by specific receptor activation","Thinner bones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","More delicate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heavier and with more prominent markings', 'The male pelvis is heavier with thicker bones and more prominent muscular markings, reflecting greater muscle mass. The female pelvis is lighter and smoother by comparison.'),
('c2198296-cc29-52f4-b79c-e27330db9a55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male pelvic inlet is?', '["Rounded","Circular","Oval","Heart-shaped","Flat"]'::jsonb, 'Heart-shaped', 'The android male pelvic inlet is heart-shaped because the sacral promontory projects into it. A rounded inlet is gynecoid (female), and an oval inlet is anthropoid.'),
('c0431d37-652e-5e3b-b8b5-bf18e60db227'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male pelvic cavity is?', '["More cylindrical","More conical","Shallow and roomy","Same as female","Deep and narrow"]'::jsonb, 'Deep and narrow', 'The male pelvic cavity is deeper and narrower, converging funnel-like toward the outlet. The female cavity is shallower and wider, which aids parturition.'),
('76b6d720-afbb-51ba-ae59-e426f7aabd9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male subpubic angle is?', '["Approximately 90 degrees","None","Wide (greater than 80 degrees)","Variable","Narrow (less than 70 degrees)"]'::jsonb, 'Narrow (less than 70 degrees)', 'The male subpubic angle is acute, generally less than 70 degrees, as the ischiopubic rami meet at a narrow angle. The female angle is wide, exceeding about 80 degrees.'),
('cf5ce36b-0223-538c-a4e4-802d8d9429a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male greater sciatic notch is?', '["Same as female","Wider and shallower","More vertical","Narrower and deeper","More horizontal"]'::jsonb, 'Narrower and deeper', 'The male greater sciatic notch is narrow and deep, whereas the female notch is wide and shallow. This difference is one of the most reliable sex indicators of the pelvis.'),
('cf98fb4a-d6f0-50d6-aea8-d1bb2055199a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male acetabulum is?', '["Larger and faces more laterally","None","Smaller and faces anteriorly","More vertically oriented","Same as female"]'::jsonb, 'Larger and faces more laterally', 'The male acetabulum is larger and directed more laterally as the iliac wings are less flared. The female acetabulum is smaller and faces more anteriorly.'),
('50d1457f-f538-5612-a42d-2f839e00748e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male obturator foramen is?', '["Heart-shaped","Same as female","Triangular","Round or oval","Keyhole-shaped"]'::jsonb, 'Round or oval', 'The male obturator foramen is round to oval, while the female foramen is more triangular. This bony sex difference supports identification of the pelvis.'),
('4b892138-1e1b-5920-8fb8-15d3b189167b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The male iliac crest is?', '["Same as female","Less curved and more vertical","More horizontal","More curved and flared","None"]'::jsonb, 'Less curved and more vertical', 'The male iliac crest is less curved and more vertically oriented, making the male pelvis taller and less flared. The female crest is more curved and the ilia flare more laterally.'),
('af19923b-579d-5f15-9c75-6b8df2db3353'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ischial tuberosity in males is?', '["More medially rotated","More anterior","More laterally rotated","Same as females","More posterior"]'::jsonb, 'More medially rotated', 'In males the ischial tuberosities are rotated more medially, bringing them closer to the midline and narrowing the outlet. The female tuberosities are more everted and widely spaced.'),
('7f5ec1a9-f171-56ff-8c41-bb58575aae71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic inlet in males is?', '["More flattened","More rounded","More deeply curved","Less curved","Same as female"]'::jsonb, 'More deeply curved', 'The male (android) pelvic inlet is more deeply curved toward a heart shape because of the prominent sacral promontory. The female inlet is rounder and wider transversely.'),
('8c4ed1f4-23d2-541c-9905-282e5a726029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The L5 vertebra articulates with which bone of the pelvis?', '["Coccyx resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sacrum (base of sacrum)","Ilium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sacrum (base of sacrum)', 'The base of the sacrum articulates superiorly with the body of L5 at the lumbosacral joint (L5/S1). The coccyx attaches at the sacral apex, and the ilia articulate at the sacroiliac joints.'),
('13dab8bb-6908-5cf0-93e7-50cfd27210e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral base articulates with which vertebra?', '["Coccyx","L4","S5","L5","L3"]'::jsonb, 'L5', 'The superior surface (base) of the sacrum articulates with the fifth lumbar vertebra. The apex articulates below with the coccyx, not with L4 or L3.'),
('8516b611-c04f-56a0-9e11-add508dd35a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral apex articulates with which bone?', '["Coccyx","Pubis","Ilium","L5","Ischium"]'::jsonb, 'Coccyx', 'The apex of the sacrum points inferiorly and articulates with the coccyx at the sacrococcygeal joint. The base of the sacrum articulates with L5, and the auricular surfaces with the ilia.'),
('e7550fe2-3349-5841-857f-0b02b00026e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral angle (sacral inclination) is measured between?', '["Sacrum and horizontal plane","None","Sacrum and vertical plane","Ilium and sacrum","L5 and S1"]'::jsonb, 'Sacrum and vertical plane', 'Sacral inclination is the angle between the sacrum and the vertical plane. It is not the angle between the sacrum and the horizontal, nor between the ilia and sacrum.'),
('aa10c5a7-7157-5a58-8df6-c8423f26b1ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The coccyx can be palpated where?', '["At the ischial tuberosity","In the gluteal cleft","At the ASIS","At the pubic symphysis","At the PSIS"]'::jsonb, 'In the gluteal cleft', 'The coccyx lies in the midline within the gluteal cleft at the inferior end of the sacrum. The ASIS, PSIS, and pubic symphysis are anterior or lateral landmarks, not the coccyx.'),
('6e248514-0a31-5931-befa-8ee83796398d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The greater trochanter can be palpated where?', '["On the posterior thigh","On the gluteal region","On the lateral side of the thigh","On the anterior thigh","On the medial thigh"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral side of the thigh', 'The greater trochanter projects laterally from the upper femur and is felt on the lateral side of the thigh just below the hip. Its upper border lies at the level of the center of the femoral head.'),
('7c7774b7-47fa-5898-8ab0-dbf1cab75be4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The ischial tuberosity can be palpated where?', '["On the medial thigh","On the posterior thigh","In the lower buttock (when hip is flexed)","On the anterior thigh","On the lateral hip"]'::jsonb, 'In the lower buttock (when hip is flexed)', 'The ischial tuberosity is best felt in the lower buttock with the hip flexed, because hip flexion slides the gluteus maximus away and exposes the bone. The posterior thigh and medial thigh do not overlie it.'),
('bfc259b7-c17b-5e4f-a2fb-c017789c8391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pubic tubercle can be palpated where?', '["At the thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","At the perineum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the ASIS mediated by specific receptor activation","At the lower abdomen above the pubic symphysis","At the iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the lower abdomen above the pubic symphysis', 'The pubic tubercle is felt on the upper border of the pubis in the lower abdomen, just lateral to the pubic symphysis. It is the lateral attachment of the inguinal ligament and is not at the ASIS or iliac crest.'),
('24d989cd-79f0-5bed-ba2d-9a4704087118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial malleolus is palpated where?', '["At the dorsum of the foot","At the medial ankle","At the lateral ankle","At the heel","At the knee"]'::jsonb, 'At the medial ankle', 'The medial malleolus is the distal projection of the tibia on the medial side of the ankle. The lateral ankle is formed by the fibular lateral malleolus.'),
('b1385a35-9e89-556b-84cb-3c695fc718e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral malleolus is palpated where?', '["At the knee","At the lateral ankle","At the dorsum of the foot","At the medial ankle","At the heel"]'::jsonb, 'At the lateral ankle', 'The lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula, palpable on the lateral side of the ankle and extending lower than the medial malleolus. The medial ankle bears the tibial malleolus.'),
('c21b5b82-b2bb-5c88-ae80-a5aa77b72f48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibial tuberosity is palpated where?', '["At the posterior knee","At the medial knee","Above the patella","Below the patella","At the lateral knee"]'::jsonb, 'Below the patella', 'The tibial tuberosity lies on the anterior tibia just below the patella, where the patellar ligament inserts. The posterior or medial knee and the region above the patella do not locate it.'),
('5fa33f3f-3ca5-5c5c-8958-ce766df69ba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sustentaculum tali can be palpated where?', '["At the heel","At the arch of the foot","Below the lateral malleolus","At the dorsum of the foot","Below the medial malleolus"]'::jsonb, 'Below the medial malleolus', 'The sustentaculum tali is a shelf on the medial side of the calcaneus, felt just below the medial malleolus. It supports the talus and is not found on the lateral side.'),
('fdabf525-5ac8-5c02-bdb5-ae4dd3d2ac81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The navicular tuberosity can be palpated where?', '["At the medial foot (in front of the ankle)","At the heel","At the sole of the foot","At the lateral foot","At the dorsum of the foot"]'::jsonb, 'At the medial foot (in front of the ankle)', 'The navicular tuberosity projects on the medial side of the foot just in front of the ankle and is the main insertion of tibialis posterior. The heel, sole, and lateral foot are other structures.'),
('bbef8b4b-13f7-53dd-8e27-85dd41031cb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tuberosity of the fifth metatarsal can be palpated where?', '["Lateral side of the heel","At the dorsum","At the lateral border of the foot midway","Medial border of the foot","At the sole"]'::jsonb, 'At the lateral border of the foot midway', 'The tuberosity of the fifth metatarsal is felt as a bump on the lateral border of the foot roughly midway, and is the insertion of peroneus brevis. The heel and medial border are different landmarks.'),
('a2169d26-9d70-53a1-920a-78b534e47a16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the talus can be palpated where?', '["At the sole","At the lateral foot","In front of the ankle (medial side)","At the heel","Behind the ankle"]'::jsonb, 'In front of the ankle (medial side)', 'The head of the talus is felt in front of the ankle on the medial side of the foot, just proximal to the navicular with which it articulates. The heel, sole, and lateral foot contain other bones.'),
('78a88e1c-cb39-5afd-8b86-73895fd4b893'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcaneal tuberosity is palpated where?', '["At the posterior heel","At the sole of the foot","At the lateral ankle","At the dorsum","At the medial ankle"]'::jsonb, 'At the posterior heel', 'The calcaneal tuberosity forms the posterior projection of the calcaneus and is palpated at the back of the heel, where the tendo calcaneus inserts. The sole, dorsum, and ankle malleoli are distinct sites.'),
('a0cfd547-4624-55a6-8fd5-12156bc32352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The peroneal trochlea can be palpated where?', '["On the medial surface of the calcaneus","On the sole","On the dorsum of the foot","On the lateral surface of the calcaneus","On the heel"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral surface of the calcaneus', 'The peroneal trochlea is a small eminence on the lateral surface of the calcaneus that separates the tendons of peroneus longus and brevis. The medial calcaneal surface is where the sustentaculum tali lies.'),
('5d893508-400b-587c-b042-e8e65ce79645'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial cuneiform can be palpated where?', '["At the medial foot (proximal to first metatarsal base)","At the heel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the lateral foot mediated by specific receptor activation","At the ankle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the dorsum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the medial foot (proximal to first metatarsal base)', 'The medial cuneiform lies on the medial border of the foot, just proximal to the base of the first metatarsal with which it articulates. The heel, ankle, and lateral foot are other landmarks.'),
('d332b900-9fab-56d6-8c0a-297639c82dbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The cuboid can be palpated where?', '["On the heel","On the medial side of the foot","On the sole","On the lateral side of the foot (midway)","On the dorsum of the foot"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral side of the foot (midway)', 'The cuboid is palpable on the lateral side of the foot, approximately midway, where it lies in the groove for the peroneus longus tendon. The medial foot contains the cuneiforms and navicular.'),
('2e908be1-691d-544d-8341-7ae80a769416'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The styloid process of the fifth metatarsal is palpable where?', '["At the medial foot resulting in decreased functional activity","At the head of the fifth metatarsal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the ankle mediated by specific receptor activation","At the base of the fifth metatarsal (lateral foot)","At the heel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the base of the fifth metatarsal (lateral foot)', 'The styloid process is a tubercle on the base of the fifth metatarsal, felt on the lateral side of the foot where the peroneus brevis inserts. It is not at the head of the metatarsal, which lies more distally at the metatarsophalangeal joint.'),
('6afcca2a-cf13-5cfe-9283-f0f21aca0b64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The base of the first metatarsal is palpated where?', '["At the medial foot (just distal to medial cuneiform)","At the heel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the dorsum mediated by specific receptor activation","At the lateral foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the ankle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the medial foot (just distal to medial cuneiform)', 'The first metatarsal base articulates with the medial cuneiform, so it is palpated along the medial border of the foot just distal to that tarsal bone. The heel encloses the calcaneus, the dorsum is the dorsal surface of the midfoot, and the lateral foot is far from this site.'),
('723543f9-acdb-59ff-af82-d86030f4f34d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the first metatarsal is palpated where?', '["At the heel resulting in decreased functional activity","At the toes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the arch mediated by specific receptor activation","At the ball of the foot (medial side)","At the lateral ball leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the ball of the foot (medial side)', 'The first metatarsal head articulates with the proximal phalanx at the first metatarsophalangeal joint, forming the ball of the foot on its medial side. The heel covers the calcaneus, the toes are phalanges, and the arch is the midfoot.'),
('602f54b8-f566-521c-8d07-bdee771ab14b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The head of the fifth metatarsal is palpated where?', '["At the heel","At the medial ball","At the arch","At the lateral ball of the foot","At the toes"]'::jsonb, 'At the lateral ball of the foot', 'The fifth metatarsal head forms the lateral ball of the foot at its metatarsophalangeal joint. The medial ball belongs to the first metatarsal, and the heel, arch, and toes are separate regions.'),
('d505be09-54a0-59d9-aa42-6b0c29ff9131'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibular notch of the tibia is located where?', '["On the proximal tibia","On the anterior surface","On the lateral surface of the distal tibia","On the medial surface of the distal tibia","On the posterior surface"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral surface of the distal tibia', 'The fibular notch is a shallow concavity on the lateral surface of the distal tibia that articulates with the fibula at the distal tibiofibular joint. It is not present on the proximal tibia or on the anterior, medial, or posterior surfaces.'),
('8f2251c9-6312-5139-a2ed-99f11b090c31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intercondylar eminence of the tibia consists of which structures?', '["Popliteal notch","Anterior and posterior intercondylar areas","None","Medial and lateral condyles","Medial and lateral intercondylar tubercles"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral intercondylar tubercles', 'The intercondylar eminence is the raised ridge between the tibial condyles formed by the medial and lateral intercondylar tubercles. The intercondylar areas and the popliteal notch are separate tibial features.'),
('5612f91b-d4f9-5339-9b64-d108f5684019'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The anterior cruciate ligament attaches to which part of the tibia?', '["Anterior intercondylar area","None","Lateral intercondylar tubercle","Medial intercondylar tubercle","Posterior intercondylar area"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior intercondylar area', 'The ACL is anchored in the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia, just ahead of the intercondylar eminence. The PCL attaches to the posterior intercondylar area, and the tubercles are components of the eminence.'),
('0e717e4f-b18b-5516-9216-27765c70e48d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior cruciate ligament attaches to which part of the tibia?', '["Medial intercondylar tubercle","Anterior intercondylar area","None","Posterior intercondylar area","Lateral intercondylar tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior intercondylar area', 'The PCL inserts into the posterior intercondylar area of the tibia. The ACL occupies the anterior intercondylar area, and the intercondylar tubercles form the central eminence.'),
('ede453b1-cb83-5837-be7c-115b239784fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial meniscus attaches to which area of the tibia?', '["Anterior and posterior intercondylar areas (medial part)","Lateral part of the intercondylar area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior area only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior area only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior intercondylar areas (medial part)', 'The horns of the medial meniscus attach in the anterior and posterior intercondylar areas on the medial side of the tibia. The lateral part is reserved for the lateral meniscus, so a single-area attachment would be incomplete.'),
('0a579dc0-f438-5d27-af0d-4657d4161317'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral meniscus attaches to which area of the tibia?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior area only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial part of the intercondylar area mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior and posterior intercondylar areas (lateral part)","Anterior area only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior intercondylar areas (lateral part)', 'The lateral meniscus is held by its horns in the anterior and posterior intercondylar areas on the lateral side of the tibia. The medial part belongs to the medial meniscus, so attaching at a single area would be incorrect.'),
('5fb2bdf9-ad57-57c9-847a-d33f452ea0eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The popliteal notch of the tibia is located where?', '["On the posterior aspect of the lateral tibial condyle","On the anterior tibia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the distal tibia mediated by specific receptor activation","On the medial condyle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the shaft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the posterior aspect of the lateral tibial condyle', 'The popliteal notch is a groove on the posterior aspect of the lateral tibial condyle that lodges the tendon of popliteus. It is not found on the anterior tibia, distal tibia, medial condyle, or shaft.'),
('fe04d840-b708-518d-8fe8-cc4df28a6731'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The tibial tuberosity is continuous below with which structure?', '["Lateral malleolus","Soleal line","Medial malleolus","Popliteal line","Anterior border of the tibia"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of the tibia', 'The tibial tuberosity continues distally into the anterior border (crest) of the tibia, which is subcutaneous below the knee. The malleoli are distal projections and the soleal and popliteal lines lie on the posterior surface.'),
('876f8c91-5938-5886-92ee-0d7e317f4aa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial surface of the tibial shaft is?', '["Subcutaneous throughout its length","Deep to fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Covered by muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not palpable leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous throughout its length', 'The medial surface of the tibial shaft lies directly beneath skin with no intervening muscle, so it is subcutaneous and easily palpable for its whole length. Fascia and muscle cover the other borders and surfaces of the shaft.'),
('16cfb3fe-0e99-596a-9c19-92c416e4adca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The interosseous border of the tibial shaft faces which structure?', '["Medial border","Anterior border","Lateral border (toward the fibula)","Posterior border","Inferior border"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral border (toward the fibula)', 'The interosseous border of the tibia runs on its lateral side facing the fibula, giving attachment to the interosseous membrane. The medial, anterior, posterior, and inferior borders do not face the fibula.'),
('2c2b1922-a77c-53f0-80a5-4d6ea4e8278e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibular head has which feature?', '["Medial malleolus resulting in decreased functional activity","Apex (styloid process) and articular facet","Tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Groove for tendon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral malleolus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apex (styloid process) and articular facet', 'The fibular head shows a proximal apex called the styloid process and a small facet that articulates with the underside of the lateral tibial condyle. The malleoli are distal fibular features, and the tuberosity is part of the tibia.'),
('a17739d5-6696-5e39-81af-843a23bc36c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The styloid process of the fibula is also known as?', '["Neck of fibula","Fibular tuberosity","Lateral malleolus","Head of fibula","Apex of the fibular head"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of the fibular head', 'The styloid process is the blunt upward projection of the fibular head, so the two terms describe the same structure. The neck lies just below the head, the tuberosity belongs to the tibia, and the lateral malleolus is the distal end of the fibula.'),
('8e522ae1-9352-50e1-ab0b-81b282b701ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The peroneal nerve can be palpated where?', '["At the lateral malleolus","As it winds around the fibular neck","At the medial malleolus","At the fibular head","At the knee"]'::jsonb, 'As it winds around the fibular neck', 'The common peroneal (fibular) nerve becomes superficial as it winds around the neck of the fibula, where it can be rolled against bone and is vulnerable to compression. The malleoli, fibular head, and knee are not the classic palpation site.'),
('60b7d2eb-37a0-5767-a5e0-2651887c421f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The fibular shaft has an interosseous border on which side?', '["None","Medial side","Anterior side","Lateral side","Posterior side"]'::jsonb, 'Medial side', 'The fibular shaft carries its interosseous border on the medial side, where the interosseous membrane attaches facing the tibia. The anterior, lateral, and posterior sides give origin to muscles and are not interosseous.'),
('e96188a4-2d37-5a7b-89ea-3d853426fc54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The posterior surface of the fibular shaft has which crest?', '["Anterior crest resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Interosseous crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial crest (for muscle attachment)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial crest (for muscle attachment)', 'The posterior surface of the fibular shaft is split by the medial crest, which gives attachment to flexor hallucis longus and tibialis posterior. The interosseous border is separate, and an anterior crest belongs to the tibia.'),
('3a75de0c-3329-5d14-9e8a-487ee1f827cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral malleolus extends further distally than the medial malleolus by how much?', '["0.5 cm","2.5 cm","1.5 cm","Approximately 1 cm","2 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 1 cm', 'The lateral malleolus projects about 1 cm further distally than the medial malleolus, a difference that deepens the lateral wall of the ankle mortise. This asymmetry helps explain why inversion injuries are far more common than eversion injuries.'),
('737a9793-4c8e-5d36-b125-f237caba24c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral malleolus has a triangular articular facet for which bone?', '["Cuboid resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus (lateral side of talus)","Calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Navicular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Talus (lateral side of talus)', 'The lateral malleolus bears a triangular facet on its inner side that articulates with the lateral surface of the talus. The tibia, cuboid, navicular, and calcaneus do not join the lateral malleolus at this facet.'),
('2ff06275-4ff2-55d3-ab04-0ea4fa9af8fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The malleolar groove of the fibula is located where?', '["On the lateral surface","On the posterior surface of the lateral malleolus","On the tip","On the medial surface","On the anterior surface of the lateral malleolus"]'::jsonb, 'On the posterior surface of the lateral malleolus', 'The malleolar groove lies on the posterior surface of the lateral malleolus and transmits the tendons of peroneus longus and peroneus brevis. The medial, lateral, anterior, and tip surfaces of the malleolus are not grooved for these tendons.'),
('79eff315-d1b2-5dba-9da5-2490d64472ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The malleolar fossa of the fibula is located where?', '["On the tip resulting in decreased functional activity","On the posterior surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the medial surface of the lateral malleolus","On the lateral surface without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the anterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the medial surface of the lateral malleolus', 'The malleolar fossa is a depression on the medial surface of the lateral malleolus that gives attachment to the posterior talofibular ligament. The shallow groove further back on the same bone instead carries the peroneal tendons.'),
('c6ea4ecf-8648-5bef-8acc-0a01fef8efdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial dorsal cutaneous nerve is derived from which nerve?', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Sural nerve","Saphenous nerve","Tibial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve', 'The medial dorsal cutaneous nerve arises from the superficial peroneal nerve and supplies skin along the medial side of the dorsum of the foot. The deep peroneal, sural, saphenous, and tibial nerves have different distributions.'),
('d2c2ca53-b1db-511e-93cd-9d05e9f08345'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The intermediate dorsal cutaneous nerve is derived from which nerve?', '["Superficial peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Sural nerve","Tibial nerve","Deep peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal nerve', 'The intermediate dorsal cutaneous nerve is a second terminal branch of the superficial peroneal nerve, supplying skin over the central dorsum of the foot. It is not derived from the sural, saphenous, tibial, or deep peroneal nerves.'),
('41116329-89dd-51a6-848f-7f708bf77ea6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral dorsal cutaneous nerve is derived from which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Sural nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Saphenous nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sural nerve', 'The lateral dorsal cutaneous nerve arises from the sural nerve and supplies skin over the lateral side of the dorsum of the foot and fifth toe. The superficial peroneal, deep peroneal, saphenous, and tibial nerves innervate other territories.'),
('a504f278-bd71-5dbd-b05d-56778a667ea9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The dorsal digital nerves of the foot are derived from which main nerves?', '["Tibial nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Sciatic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Saphenous nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial peroneal deep peroneal and sural nerves","Femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial peroneal deep peroneal and sural nerves', 'The dorsal digital nerves to the toes come from the superficial peroneal, deep peroneal, and sural nerves. Plantar digital branches come from the tibial nerve, and the saphenous nerve stops at the medial foot rather than reaching the toes.'),
('066400d2-ecfe-5f0a-b0c5-cbb0c1922f6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The proper digital nerves of the great toe (medial side) come from which nerve?', '["Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Lateral plantar nerve","Sural nerve","Medial plantar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Medial plantar nerve', 'The proper digital nerve to the medial side of the great toe is a terminal branch of the medial plantar nerve. The peroneal nerves supply the dorsum, and the lateral plantar nerve serves the lateral part of the sole.'),
('ce486013-4e6b-570c-9ae2-f0dbf9fac363'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The deep branch of the lateral plantar nerve supplies which muscles?', '["Adductor hallucis and interossei","Flexor hallucis brevis","Flexor digitorum brevis","Abductor hallucis","Quadratus plantae"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor hallucis and interossei', 'The deep branch of the lateral plantar nerve supplies adductor hallucis and all interossei, mirroring the ulnar nerve in the hand. Abductor hallucis, flexor hallucis brevis, and flexor digitorum brevis receive branches from the medial plantar nerve.'),
('4aa56727-8fb0-527a-a0a7-bf36855dfbc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The superficial branch of the lateral plantar nerve supplies which structures?', '["Quadratus plantae resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal interossei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adductor hallucis mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexor digiti minimi brevis and interossei tendons and skin of lateral side","Lumbricals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor digiti minimi brevis and interossei tendons and skin of lateral side', 'The superficial branch of the lateral plantar nerve innervates flexor digiti minimi brevis, sends fine filaments to the interossei, and supplies the skin of the lateral side of the foot and the lateral 1.5 toes. Adductor hallucis and the interossei themselves are innervated by the deep branch.'),
('215b1164-a7c2-5522-b208-e5827f85cdf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The medial plantar nerve divides into which branches?', '["Medial and lateral branches","Superficial and deep branches","Proper digital and common digital nerves","None","Dorsal and plantar branches"]'::jsonb, 'Proper digital and common digital nerves', 'The medial plantar nerve ends by splitting into proper digital and common digital nerves. Division into superficial and deep branches is the terminal pattern of the lateral plantar nerve, not the medial plantar nerve.'),
('37ab4672-cadb-57c7-abce-d1c94ae60017'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The common plantar digital nerves divide into which branches?', '["None","Dorsal digital nerves","Lateral branches","Medial branches","Proper plantar digital nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Proper plantar digital nerves', 'Each common plantar digital nerve divides into proper plantar digital nerves that supply the adjacent sides of two toes. Dorsal digital nerves belong to the dorsum, and the terms used in the other options do not describe plantar digital branching.'),
('4a34900c-4bb1-5756-a798-30246811ab63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The first common plantar digital nerve supplies which structures?', '["Lateral 1.5 toes resulting in decreased functional activity","Third lumbrical accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial 3.5 toes (sensory) and first lumbrical (motor)","Second lumbrical without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fourth lumbrical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial 3.5 toes (sensory) and first lumbrical (motor)', 'The first common plantar digital nerve gives sensory branches to the medial 3.5 toes and motor fibers to the first lumbrical muscle. The remaining lumbricals and the lateral toes are supplied by the lateral plantar nerve.'),
('23ab89f1-6ab8-5b22-9990-e4fc14f8952f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lateral plantar nerve divides into which branches?', '["None","Medial and lateral branches","Dorsal and plantar branches","Common and proper digital nerves","Superficial and deep branches"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep branches', 'The lateral plantar nerve, the counterpart of the ulnar nerve, divides into superficial and deep branches. Division into common and proper digital nerves is the pattern of the medial plantar nerve, and dorsal/plantar division does not occur.'),
('0bfe25e8-e0fd-5c20-ab79-77749cd1aaf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The superficial branch of the lateral plantar nerve supplies which skin area?', '["Medial 3.5 toes","Lateral 3.5 toes","Medial 1.5 toes","Heel","Lateral 1.5 toes"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral 1.5 toes', 'The superficial branch of the lateral plantar nerve supplies the skin of the lateral 1.5 toes. The medial 3.5 toes are innervated by the medial plantar nerve, and the heel receives the calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve.'),
('b1cb2296-64ee-52ac-acdf-02a09ab53774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve supply the skin of which area?', '["Heel","Sole of foot","Medial foot","Dorsum of foot","Lateral foot"]'::jsonb, 'Heel', 'The calcaneal branches of the tibial nerve supply the skin of the heel, and the medial calcaneal branch is the largest. The sole, dorsum, and medial and lateral parts of the foot are supplied by the plantar and peroneal nerves.'),
('b9120763-0f8d-5d45-b6a8-7389f1355fae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sympathetic innervation of the lower limb arises from which spinal levels?', '["L1-L4","T1-T5","S2-S4","T5-T9","T10-L2"]'::jsonb, 'T10-L2', 'Sympathetic outflow to the lower limb begins at T10-L2 and relays in the lumbar and sacral sympathetic ganglia. S2-S4 is the parasympathetic outflow, and T1-T5 and T5-T9 levels are far too high for the lower limb.'),
('b1851876-bff5-5f93-8adb-c93de9e7567c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The parasympathetic innervation to the lower limb is carried by which nerves?', '["Lumbar splanchnic nerves","Sacral splanchnic nerves","None (no parasympathetic to limbs)","Vagus nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves"]'::jsonb, 'None (no parasympathetic to limbs)', 'The limbs receive no parasympathetic supply; vasomotor and sudomotor control is entirely sympathetic. Lumbar, sacral, and pelvic splanchnic nerves and the vagus serve viscera, not the limb.'),
('45150470-279e-5b22-b066-dcd2aef8450c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lumbar sympathetic trunk lies where relative to the vertebral column?', '["Anterior to the aorta","Lateral to the psoas major","Anterolateral to the vertebral bodies","Posterior to the IVC","Posterior to the vertebral bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Anterolateral to the vertebral bodies', 'The lumbar sympathetic trunk runs anterolateral to the vertebral bodies, partly embedded in the psoas major. It lies beside rather than in front of the aorta, and it is not lateral to psoas or posterior to the IVC or vertebrae.'),
('4c170957-d5f9-5e2e-bb2d-7703945291fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral sympathetic trunk lies where?', '["Lateral to the sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to the sacrum (medial to sacral foramina)","Posterior to the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior to the coccyx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the sacrum (medial to sacral foramina)', 'The sacral sympathetic trunk descends on the anterior surface of the sacrum, medial to the anterior sacral foramina, and fuses with its fellow at the coccyx. It does not lie lateral, posterior, or merely anterior to the coccyx.'),
('2a734e2d-f1c4-55b8-b136-6a890163b73a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves carry which fibers?', '["Preganglionic sympathetic fibers","None","Parasympathetic","Postganglionic sympathetic","Sensory"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic sympathetic fibers', 'Lumbar splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers from the lumbar ganglia to the aortic and hypogastric plexuses. Postganglionic fibers have already synapsed and leave as gray rami rather than as splanchnic nerves.'),
('275ea71f-054f-5255-bdc1-ce8c34158f1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral splanchnic nerves carry which fibers?', '["Postganglionic sympathetic fibers","Parasympathetic","Somatic","Sensory","Preganglionic sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Postganglionic sympathetic fibers', 'Sacral splanchnic nerves carry postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the sacral sympathetic ganglia to the inferior hypogastric (pelvic) plexus. In contrast, the pelvic splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4.'),
('d39b91dc-201d-54e0-a37b-542107713499'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves carry which fibers?', '["Preganglionic sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Postganglionic parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers (S2-S4)","Sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Somatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers (S2-S4)', 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves arise from S2-S4 and carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers, which synapse in ganglia close to the pelvic viscera. They are parasympathetic, so they cannot be sympathetic or postganglionic.'),
('7cd4ac3e-65aa-5e40-8d2a-60ed2378ba0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The superior hypogastric plexus is also known as?', '["Aortic plexus","Sacral plexus","Inferior mesenteric plexus","Presacral nerve","Coeliac plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Presacral nerve', 'The superior hypogastric plexus is commonly called the presacral nerve, lying over the sacral promontory. The pelvic plexus is the inferior hypogastric plexus, and the aortic, coeliac, and inferior mesenteric plexuses are separate abdominal structures.'),
('e3f7fab0-21a6-501a-a4b2-2848747339c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The inferior hypogastric plexus is also known as?', '["None","Sacral plexus","Hypogastric nerve","Pelvic plexus","Presacral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic plexus', 'The inferior hypogastric plexus is also known as the pelvic plexus, lying beside the rectum, bladder, and pelvic viscera. The presacral nerve is the superior hypogastric plexus, and the hypogastric nerve connects the two plexuses.'),
('e767ab19-536b-5c13-a7c4-80cb09f4a775'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The hypogastric nerves connect which structures?', '["Lumbar and sacral plexuses","Lumbar and sacral sympathetic trunks","Inferior hypogastric plexus and pelvic viscera","None","Superior and inferior hypogastric plexuses"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior hypogastric plexuses', 'The hypogastric nerves run down from the superior hypogastric plexus to the inferior hypogastric plexus on each side, carrying sympathetic and visceral afferent fibers. They connect the two hypogastric plexuses, not the trunks or the limb plexuses.'),
('28a61dc2-0117-52cc-8e4d-440c9de8e052'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The lumbar plexus is formed within which muscle?', '["Psoas major","Quadratus lumborum","Iliacus","Psoas minor","None"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major', 'The lumbar plexus is formed within the substance of the psoas major muscle, whose attachments also separate the roots. Quadratus lumborum lies posterior, and iliacus and psoas minor are not the site of plexus formation.'),
('51325df0-73d7-57cc-8d10-8bd70c21eff3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The sacral plexus is formed on which muscle?', '["Quadratus lumborum","Psoas major","Obturator internus","Iliacus","Piriformis"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis', 'The sacral plexus lies on the anterior (pelvic) surface of the piriformis muscle in the posterior wall of the lesser pelvis. Psoas major and quadratus lumborum are related to the lumbar plexus, and obturator internus is not the plexus bed.'),
('98edab8c-e6ef-5f7b-bcef-6c9997334db9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The pudendal plexus is formed by which spinal nerves?', '["L4-S3 resulting in decreased functional activity","S1-S4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S2-S4 (mainly anterior divisions)","L5-S2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","S1-S3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4 (mainly anterior divisions)', 'The pudendal plexus is formed mainly by the anterior divisions of S2-S4 and gives rise to the pudendal nerve. The other listed segments contribute to the lumbosacral trunk or to the coccygeal plexus rather than to the pudendal plexus.'),
('fa932ab4-41c6-5421-a836-d5c1ca857d0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'The coccygeal plexus is formed by which spinal nerves?', '["L5-S3","S4-Co1","S2-S4","S1-S4","L4-S1"]'::jsonb, 'S4-Co1', 'The coccygeal plexus is formed by S4-Co1 and supplies the skin around the coccyx via the anococcygeal nerves. S2-S4 (anterior divisions) defines the pudendal plexus rather than the coccygeal plexus.'),
('0d8f4fac-1cab-5a1e-ab08-b3ff4db3f4d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Osteology', 'In Lower limb osteology, which statement best fits the following fact: the hip bone is formed by fusion of which three bones?', '["Ilium ischium and femur","Ischium pubis and sacrum","Ilium ischium and pubis","Ilium ischium and sacrum","Ilium pubis and sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'Ilium ischium and pubis', 'The hip (innominate) bone is formed by fusion of the ilium, ischium, and pubis, which meet at the acetabulum. The femur is the thigh bone and the sacrum is part of the vertebral column, so neither is fused into the hip bone.'),
('78a19089-5686-50e7-9d69-b5284c6e265a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The thigh extends from which joints?', '["Hip to foot","Knee to foot","Hip to ankle","Knee to ankle","Hip to knee"]'::jsonb, 'Hip to knee', 'The thigh is the segment of the lower limb lying between the hip and knee joints. Distal options such as hip to foot or knee to ankle also include the leg or foot.'),
('9670ac82-0d39-549d-a8bd-7c33b80af4f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The anterior compartment of the thigh contains which muscles?', '["Adductors resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadriceps femoris sartorius iliopsoas (part)","Hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps femoris sartorius iliopsoas (part)', 'The anterior fascial compartment of the thigh holds the quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and the iliacus portion of iliopsoas. The hamstrings occupy the posterior compartment, the adductors the medial compartment, and the gluteals lie in the buttock.'),
('b0441c46-70da-5f88-a579-ce1111388118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The medial compartment of the thigh contains which muscles?', '["Adductor longus brevis magnus gracilis pectineus","Hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteals mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadriceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor longus brevis magnus gracilis pectineus', 'The medial compartment contains the adductor group: adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, gracilis, and pectineus. The hamstrings lie posteriorly and the quadriceps anteriorly.'),
('a52f5918-4f72-5415-bff6-b4707ae7db55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The posterior compartment of the thigh contains which muscles?', '["Iliopsoas resulting in decreased functional activity","Quadriceps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adductors mediated by specific receptor activation","Hamstrings (semitendinosus semimembranosus biceps femoris)","Gluteals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hamstrings (semitendinosus semimembranosus biceps femoris)', 'The posterior compartment of the thigh is occupied by the hamstring muscles: semitendinosus, semimembranosus, and biceps femoris. The quadriceps are anterior, the adductors medial, and the gluteals are outside the thigh compartments.'),
('0f3ce95e-2682-5a11-a264-1fb08589bc1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The quadriceps femoris is supplied by:', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve (L2-L4)","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2-L4)', 'The quadriceps femoris is innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4). The sciatic nerve serves the hamstrings, the obturator nerve the adductors, and the superior gluteal nerve the abductors.'),
('2e53238b-61bf-5fce-b36a-89a27f93a4e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The sartorius muscle is supplied by:', '["Sciatic nerve","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve (L2 L3)","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2 L3)', 'The sartorius receives its innervation from the femoral nerve (L2-L3). The obturator and sciatic nerves instead supply the medial and posterior thigh compartments.'),
('b115f9e0-9bfa-52b4-ac4d-5fd57cd44edd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The iliopsoas muscle is formed by:', '["Psoas minor and iliacus","None","Psoas major and psoas minor","Iliacus and sartorius","Psoas major and iliacus"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major and iliacus', 'The iliopsoas is formed by the union of the psoas major and the iliacus, which converge on a common tendon inserting into the lesser trochanter. Psoas minor is a small separate muscle that does not join the iliopsoas.'),
('1eaf72d9-6a3c-5eb1-bed7-78598feff0e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The psoas major originates from:', '["Transverse processes and vertebral bodies of T12-L5","AIIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse processes and vertebral bodies of T12-L5', 'The psoas major arises from the transverse processes and bodies of the T12-L5 vertebrae. The iliacus originates from the iliac fossa, and the AIIS, pubis, and ischial tuberosity are attachments of other muscles.'),
('be154173-d03d-5413-886c-90cc60c1af50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The iliacus originates from:', '["ASIS","AIIS","T12-L5 vertebral bodies","Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)","Pubic bone"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)', 'The iliacus arises from the upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa. The psoas major, by contrast, originates from the lumbar vertebral bodies and transverse processes.'),
('0fd5da38-a598-54ee-966c-facbfb2d4338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The iliopsoas inserts into:', '["Greater trochanter","Pectineal line","Gluteal tuberosity","Lesser trochanter of the femur","Linea aspera"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter of the femur', 'The iliopsoas inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur, making it the principal hip flexor. The gluteal tuberosity and linea aspera receive the gluteus maximus and adductor insertions respectively.'),
('0f730f41-b308-5343-b751-612e459e7be0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The iliopsoas is supplied by:', '["Sciatic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Tibial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral nerve (L2 L3) and direct branches from lumbar plexus","Obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior gluteal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2 L3) and direct branches from lumbar plexus', 'The iliacus is innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L3), while the psoas major receives direct branches from the lumbar plexus. Together these innervate the entire iliopsoas.'),
('1402de55-d4e1-53f8-abf4-893f0dc00815'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The iliopsoas acts to:', '["Extend the thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","Flex the thigh at the hip and flex the trunk","Rotate the thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Abduct the thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adduct the thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex the thigh at the hip and flex the trunk', 'The iliopsoas crosses the hip and, when the thigh is free, flexes it; when the thigh is fixed it flexes the trunk. It does not extend, abduct, or adduct the thigh.'),
('b2342e44-cc0c-56ab-b2e7-f7586654d2af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The rectus femoris is part of quadriceps and also:', '["Crosses the ankle","None","Crosses the hip joint (flexes hip)","Does not cross the hip","Crosses the knee only"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses the hip joint (flexes hip)', 'Unlike the vasti, the rectus femoris arises from the anterior inferior iliac spine and crosses the hip joint, allowing it to flex the hip in addition to extending the knee. The vasti cross only the knee.'),
('752003a0-88c3-567e-83ca-852bf9dc2cad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The vastus medialis is important for:', '["Knee flexion resulting in decreased functional activity","Hip flexion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The last 15 degrees of knee extension (quadriceps lag)","Hip extension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ankle movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The last 15 degrees of knee extension (quadriceps lag)', 'The oblique fibers of vastus medialis complete the terminal 15 degrees of knee extension; weakness produces a quadriceps lag on testing. It neither flexes the knee nor acts on the hip or ankle.'),
('051d0263-624c-5676-97e0-fd12ed28bae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The articularis genus muscle is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of the hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Part of the quadriceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small muscle deep to vastus intermedius that retracts the suprapatellar bursa"]'::jsonb, 'A small muscle deep to vastus intermedius that retracts the suprapatellar bursa', 'The articularis genus is a small muscle lying deep to vastus intermedius that pulls the suprapatellar bursa superiorly during knee extension to prevent its entrapment. It is not part of the quadriceps or hamstrings.'),
('5c4dd0fc-c6d5-5500-8577-694d80a80f82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The patellar reflex tests which nerve root?', '["S1 S2 resulting in decreased functional activity","C5 C6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L5 S1 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3 L4 (femoral nerve)","T12 L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L3 L4 (femoral nerve)', 'The patellar (knee-jerk) reflex is mediated through the L3-L4 roots via the femoral nerve. Loss of this reflex points to femoral nerve or L3-L4 root pathology.'),
('34cd29bc-9e8b-58fe-b151-cb8dd1f1dbe7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor canal (Hunter''s canal) extends from:', '["Pubic tubercle to medial condyle","Apex of femoral triangle to adductor hiatus","Femoral ring to popliteal fossa","None","Inguinal ligament to knee"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of femoral triangle to adductor hiatus', 'The adductor (Hunter''s) canal runs from the apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus in adductor magnus. It transmits the femoral artery and vein and the saphenous nerve.'),
('89d4f17e-28f5-5cd9-a192-4cce3a711eb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The femoral artery in the adductor canal lies:', '["Medial to the sartorius","Posteromedial to the femur","Lateral to the femur","None","Anterior to the femur"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial to the femur', 'Within the adductor canal the femoral artery lies posteromedial to the femur, deep to the sartorius. It exits through the adductor hiatus to continue as the popliteal artery.'),
('0d5ee914-e55e-59f7-bdd1-b8af8552d95b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The saphenous nerve is a branch of:', '["Common peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'The saphenous nerve is the largest cutaneous branch of the femoral nerve and supplies the medial side of the leg and foot. The sciatic and obturator nerves do not give off a saphenous nerve.'),
('16e6b394-1c4f-5fbb-b7d6-20d639cb4cae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The nerve to vastus medialis is a branch of:', '["Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Common peroneal","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'The nerve to vastus medialis is a muscular branch of the femoral nerve that descends in the anterior thigh. The obturator nerve supplies the adductors and the sciatic nerve the posterior compartment.'),
('ca4eeaed-efd4-50f3-bc97-b261644a7545'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) runs through:', '["The adductor hiatus","The sciatic foramen","The obturator foramen (obturator canal)","The inguinal canal","The femoral canal"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator foramen (obturator canal)', 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) leaves the pelvis through the obturator canal in the obturator foramen to reach the medial thigh. The sciatic foramen transmits the sciatic nerve and its vessels.'),
('1cf81e0a-acb1-5b95-aba4-0157d063324e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The obturator nerve supplies:', '["The adductor muscles (except hamstring part of adductor magnus)","The hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gluteals mediated by specific receptor activation","The quadriceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The adductor muscles (except hamstring part of adductor magnus)', 'The obturator nerve innervates the adductor compartment. Adductor magnus is only partially supplied: its adductor part receives the obturator nerve, while the hamstring part is served by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve.'),
('f4370478-323e-54b6-89f2-7ff46c941bc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor longus originates from:', '["Pubic bone (body near pubic crest)","AIIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","ASIS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic bone (body near pubic crest)', 'The adductor longus arises from the anterior surface of the pubic body just below the pubic crest. The ischial tuberosity and iliac fossa are origins of other muscles in the limb.'),
('5c28a2a3-c87f-5bd5-956a-4568289a919e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor brevis originates from:', '["AIIS resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliac fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic bone (inferior ramus and body)","Pubic crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic bone (inferior ramus and body)', 'The adductor brevis arises from the body and inferior ramus of the pubis. The adductor longus, in contrast, originates just below the pubic crest.'),
('34d85b94-64d0-57b6-9d93-bbb20ac5df74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor magnus (adductor part) originates from:', '["Ischial tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischial spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic body mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior pubic ramus and ischial ramus","Iliac fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pubic ramus and ischial ramus', 'The adductor part of adductor magnus arises from the inferior pubic ramus and the ramus of the ischium. The hamstring part separately arises from the ischial tuberosity.'),
('efd2c85f-7029-562c-94c8-4eb7742fe0a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor magnus (hamstring part) originates from:', '["Iliac crest","Inferior pubic ramus","Pubic body","ASIS","Ischial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus shares its ischial tuberosity origin with the hamstring muscles. The adductor part instead arises from the pubic and ischial rami.'),
('00db6638-471f-517a-bb5f-4deb0ef13654'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The gracilis muscle originates from:', '["ASIS resulting in decreased functional activity","AIIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic body and inferior pubic ramus"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic body and inferior pubic ramus', 'The gracilis arises from the body and inferior ramus of the pubis. It is the most medial thigh adductor and crosses the knee to insert on the pes anserinus.'),
('54baf91a-965e-5132-a427-2a4ce8d516ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The pectineus muscle originates from:', '["Pecten pubis (pubic bone)","ASIS","Iliac fossa","Ischial tuberosity","AIIS"]'::jsonb, 'Pecten pubis (pubic bone)', 'The pectineus arises from the pecten pubis on the superior ramus of the pubis and inserts on the pectineal line of the femur. It is chiefly supplied by the femoral nerve.'),
('caf71a61-b06f-52a0-87a4-69de7e99e316'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor longus inserts into:', '["Gluteal tuberosity","Pectineal line","Lesser trochanter","Linea aspera (medial lip middle third)","Adductor tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera (medial lip middle third)', 'The adductor longus inserts on the middle third of the medial lip of the linea aspera. The adductor magnus and its hamstring part insert more distally and at the adductor tubercle.'),
('86d347b1-a370-56fc-bd57-ba7a7ba225db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor brevis inserts into:', '["Linea aspera (medial lip above adductor longus)","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lesser trochanter mediated by specific receptor activation","Adductor tubercle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera (medial lip above adductor longus)', 'The adductor brevis inserts on the medial lip of the linea aspera just proximal to the adductor longus insertion. The pectineal line and adductor tubercle receive the pectineus and adductor magnus respectively.'),
('8900bb06-acc0-5cc2-a588-a8d51c6cad23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor magnus (adductor part) inserts into:', '["Adductor tubercle resulting in decreased functional activity","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteal tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Linea aspera and medial supracondylar ridge","Lesser trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera and medial supracondylar ridge', 'The adductor part of adductor magnus inserts along the linea aspera and the medial supracondylar ridge. Only its hamstring part continues to the adductor tubercle.'),
('bff9cdce-16a1-5b4a-a31f-d09d8e6ac1f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor magnus (hamstring part) inserts into:', '["Adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle","Lesser trochanter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linea aspera mediated by specific receptor activation","Pectineal line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus inserts on the adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle. It is innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve and extends the hip.'),
('7f446ee9-3044-5555-90f3-0639b9c8630d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The gracilis muscle inserts into:', '["Tibial tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adductor tubercle mediated by specific receptor activation","Pes anserinus (upper part of medial tibial shaft)","Linea aspera leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pes anserinus (upper part of medial tibial shaft)', 'The gracilis inserts on the pes anserinus on the upper medial tibia together with the sartorius and semitendinosus. The tibial tuberosity and linea aspera are not gracilis attachments.'),
('b2be9bc0-6fe1-5a11-b1a0-a55378bba6d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The pectineus muscle inserts into:', '["Pectineal line of femur (spiral line)","Linea aspera","Gluteal tuberosity","Greater trochanter","Lesser trochanter"]'::jsonb, 'Pectineal line of femur (spiral line)', 'The pectineus inserts on the pectineal (spiral) line of the femur just below the lesser trochanter. The gluteal tuberosity and greater trochanter receive the gluteus maximus and gluteus medius/minimus respectively.'),
('2bdd22b2-d470-57ae-8ded-009dc3f726aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The main action of the adductor muscles is:', '["Adduction of the thigh","Rotation of the thigh","Extension of the thigh","Flexion of the thigh","Abduction of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Adduction of the thigh', 'The adductor group acts primarily to adduct the thigh at the hip. Individual members also contribute weak flexion and rotation, but adduction is their defining function.'),
('bdb3efe3-106a-5090-9600-b34c3a75a7b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor longus also acts as:', '["Abductor resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral rotator mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A medial rotator of the thigh (and weak flexor)"]'::jsonb, 'A medial rotator of the thigh (and weak flexor)', 'In addition to adducting, the adductor longus medially rotates the thigh and acts as a weak flexor. It never abducts, extends, or laterally rotates the hip.'),
('c979cd63-5cda-532d-a226-88df322d1c03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The gracilis muscle also acts as:', '["Flexor of the hip resulting in decreased functional activity","Abductor of the thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extensor of the knee mediated by specific receptor activation","A flexor of the knee (and medial rotator of leg)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A flexor of the knee (and medial rotator of leg)', 'Because the gracilis crosses both hip and knee, it flexes the knee and medially rotates the leg in addition to adducting the thigh. It is the only adductor acting across the knee joint.'),
('36ffbb11-ce3d-571f-8cb7-57e88a3f7297'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor magnus (hamstring part) also acts as:', '["A flexor of the knee resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A flexor of the hip mediated by specific receptor activation","An extensor of the hip (like the hamstrings)","An abductor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An extensor of the hip (like the hamstrings)', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus, sharing the ischial tuberosity origin with the hamstrings, extends the hip. The adductor part simply adducts the thigh.'),
('b39c8a86-5c71-50de-9cb3-bfc064f74b58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor muscles are supplied by which nerve?', '["Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Sciatic nerve","Inferior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve (L2-L4)"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve (L2-L4)', 'The adductor compartment receives its innervation from the obturator nerve (L2-L4). The femoral nerve supplies the anterior compartment, the sciatic nerve the posterior, and the gluteal nerves the buttock.'),
('4c3a08b3-6548-59b7-9fbe-8547809668cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus is supplied by:', '["Common peroneal nerve","Femoral nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve (tibial division L4 L5 S1)"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve (tibial division L4 L5 S1)', 'The hamstring part of adductor magnus is innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve (L4-S1), matching the hamstrings. The rest of the adductor magnus is served by the obturator nerve.'),
('c671fac8-b505-5382-b811-c56c815f14d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The semitendinosus muscle originates from:', '["ASIS resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubic bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliac crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischial tuberosity (common origin with long head of biceps)","AIIS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity (common origin with long head of biceps)', 'The semitendinosus arises from the ischial tuberosity in common with the long head of biceps femoris. The semimembranosus origin lies just medial to this site.'),
('e81eebe2-d929-5a95-a053-6bac5eb7392b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The semimembranosus muscle originates from:', '["AIIS resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliac crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial tuberosity (medial to semitendinosus)","ASIS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity (medial to semitendinosus)', 'The semimembranosus arises from the ischial tuberosity, medial to the semitendinosus origin. Its thick membranous proximal tendon gives the muscle its name.'),
('715a0e32-c453-5a7f-8b60-15a9ea404fb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The hamstring muscles (except short head of biceps) act to:', '["Flex the hip and extend the knee","Adduct the thigh","Extend the hip and flex the knee","Medially rotate the thigh","Abduct the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Extend the hip and flex the knee', 'The hamstrings cross both the hip and the knee, so they extend the hip and flex the knee. The short head of biceps femoris, crossing only the knee, is the single hamstring that does not extend the hip.'),
('021703e8-7e2f-591e-8751-dcba657eabcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The short head of biceps femoris acts to:', '["Extend the knee resulting in decreased functional activity","Abduct the thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flex the knee (only muscle that does not extend hip)","Extend the hip without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flex the hip leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex the knee (only muscle that does not extend hip)', 'The short head of biceps femoris arises from the femur and crosses only the knee, so it flexes the knee without extending the hip. It is innervated by the common peroneal division of the sciatic nerve.'),
('3e0cea03-c937-59de-87a0-1ae84bca2812'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The hamstring muscles are supplied by:', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Sciatic nerve (tibial division except short head of biceps)","Superior gluteal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferior gluteal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve (tibial division except short head of biceps)', 'The hamstrings are innervated by the tibial division of the sciatic nerve, except for the short head of biceps femoris which receives the common peroneal division. The sciatic nerve thus serves the entire posterior compartment.'),
('eb31ba4a-1bfb-5474-9113-a25ffbfa446e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The popliteal fossa is a diamond-shaped space posterior to the knee. Its apex is:', '["Superior (between the hamstrings) resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior (between the two heads of gastrocnemius)","Anterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior (between the two heads of gastrocnemius)', 'The inferior apex of the diamond-shaped popliteal fossa lies between the two heads of the gastrocnemius. The diverging hamstrings form the superior angles, while the boundaries laterally and medially are the biceps and semimembranosus respectively.'),
('76f8deeb-d11c-5cf5-94ca-d6905c9de68f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The popliteal artery gives off which genicular branches?', '["Only inferior resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior medial and lateral middle inferior medial and lateral","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only middle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior medial and lateral middle inferior medial and lateral', 'The popliteal artery gives five genicular branches: superior medial and lateral, middle, and inferior medial and lateral. They form the rich arterial anastomosis around the knee.'),
('04311293-dbaf-5a2d-a461-9bc0e80ab3b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The popliteal vein receives:', '["None","The great saphenous vein","The anterior tibial vein","The small saphenous vein","The femoral vein"]'::jsonb, 'The small saphenous vein', 'The small saphenous vein ascends from the lateral ankle along the posterior calf and drains into the popliteal vein in the popliteal fossa. The great saphenous vein drains into the femoral vein.'),
('9191822f-827a-5357-8192-9570334344b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibial nerve in the popliteal fossa gives off:', '["Superficial peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","None","Sural nerve (medial sural cutaneous)","Saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sural nerve (medial sural cutaneous)', 'The tibial nerve gives the medial sural cutaneous nerve, which joins the lateral sural nerve of the common peroneal nerve to form the sural nerve. The sural nerve supplies the posterolateral calf and foot.'),
('4d63437a-89ea-52cf-a43e-6c4a83d94ed0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The common peroneal nerve in the popliteal fossa gives off:', '["Tibial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial peroneal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Saphenous nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep peroneal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sural communicating branch (lateral sural cutaneous)"]'::jsonb, 'Sural communicating branch (lateral sural cutaneous)', 'At the popliteal fossa the common peroneal nerve gives off the lateral sural cutaneous (sural communicating) nerve, which joins the medial sural nerve to form the sural nerve. Its terminal superficial and deep peroneal nerves arise lower in the leg.'),
('ee42ce52-cf27-554b-b3f1-4fa7ad64ee11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The common peroneal nerve is at risk of injury at:', '["The foot resulting in decreased functional activity","The thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The neck of the fibula (as it winds around)","The popliteal fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The gluteal region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The neck of the fibula (as it winds around)', 'The common peroneal nerve winds around the neck of the fibula, where it is superficial and readily injured by fractures, tight casts, or pressure. The thigh, popliteal fossa, foot, and gluteal region do not place this nerve at comparable risk.'),
('46015b57-a222-5802-a12f-57d95207bb91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Injury to the common peroneal nerve causes:', '["Foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion of the ankle and toes)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of inversion mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of plantar flexion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of eversion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion of the ankle and toes)', 'The common peroneal nerve supplies the dorsiflexor muscles of the ankle and toes, so injury causes foot drop with loss of dorsiflexion. Inversion, plantar flexion, and eversion are controlled by other nerve pathways.'),
('333ef637-5534-5a7d-822b-ffebddad80ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The clinical sign of common peroneal nerve injury is:', '["Antalgic gait resulting in decreased functional activity","Waddling gait accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flat foot mediated by specific receptor activation","Foot drop (equinovarus deformity with high-stepping gait)","Trendelenburg gait leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foot drop (equinovarus deformity with high-stepping gait)', 'Loss of the evertors and dorsiflexors after common peroneal nerve injury leaves unopposed inversion and plantar flexion, producing an equinovarus deformity with a high-stepping gait. Antalgic, waddling, and Trendelenburg gaits and flat foot reflect different pathology.'),
('cdd33458-dc44-54a2-848d-bcb5a41825d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The saphenous nerve is a sensory nerve supplying:', '["Skin of the medial side of the leg and foot (as far as the medial malleolus)","Dorsum of foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral leg mediated by specific receptor activation","Plantar foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior leg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the medial side of the leg and foot (as far as the medial malleolus)', 'The saphenous nerve, the terminal sensory branch of the femoral nerve, descends with the great saphenous vein to supply the medial side of the leg and foot as far as the medial malleolus. The dorsum, lateral leg, plantar foot, and posterior leg receive other innervation.'),
('da04e933-2349-5747-8fb2-66f7217b82ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibial nerve in the leg supplies:', '["Lateral compartment resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin of the dorsum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior compartment muscles (gastrocnemius soleus plantaris popliteus TP FDL FHL)","Anterior compartment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foot intrinsics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior compartment muscles (gastrocnemius soleus plantaris popliteus TP FDL FHL)', 'The tibial nerve supplies all muscles of the posterior compartment of the leg, including the gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris, popliteus, and the deep flexors. The anterior and lateral compartments and foot intrinsics have separate nerve supply.'),
('70bcae53-0410-5fb4-9c29-e1421419a55b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibial nerve divides at the ankle into:', '["Medial and lateral plantar nerves","Sural and saphenous","Superficial and deep peroneal","All","None"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral plantar nerves', 'Behind the medial malleolus the tibial nerve divides into the medial and lateral plantar nerves. The sural, saphenous, and peroneal nerves arise from other sources and do not form this division.'),
('a49bd1f6-08ee-5f3e-a18a-00dfe3188d85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The medial plantar nerve supplies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Adductor hallucis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All intrinsic foot muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Abductor digiti minimi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis flexor hallucis brevis (1st lumbrical)"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis flexor hallucis brevis (1st lumbrical)', 'The medial plantar nerve supplies abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, flexor hallucis brevis, and the first lumbrical. Adductor hallucis and abductor digiti minimi are supplied by the lateral plantar nerve.'),
('3b0ccd22-227b-545d-85bc-bbb47f956181'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lateral plantar nerve supplies:', '["All other intrinsic foot muscles (except those supplied by medial plantar)","Flexor hallucis brevis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexor digitorum brevis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abductor hallucis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All other intrinsic foot muscles (except those supplied by medial plantar)', 'The lateral plantar nerve innervates all intrinsic foot muscles except those supplied by the medial plantar nerve. The individually listed muscles belong to the medial plantar nerve territory.'),
('f3275292-28e8-5497-9213-c473f38a5d28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The anterior tibial artery pierces the interosseous membrane to reach the anterior compartment of the leg at:', '["The upper border of the interosseous membrane","Popliteal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ankle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower border leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The upper border of the interosseous membrane', 'The anterior tibial artery pierces the upper border of the interosseous membrane to reach the anterior compartment of the leg. The lower border, ankle, and popliteal fossa are not the site of passage.'),
('bcd5a55d-d61f-5d66-b6aa-aa1721f3e469'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneal (fibular) artery arises from:', '["Anterior tibial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Popliteal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior tibial artery (in the upper leg)"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior tibial artery (in the upper leg)', 'The peroneal (fibular) artery classically arises from the posterior tibial artery high in the leg. It does not branch from the popliteal, femoral, or anterior tibial arteries.'),
('d5b15df9-e406-584e-9108-b8a7b0a2f294'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneal artery supplies:', '["The posterior compartment superficial resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior compartment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lateral compartment muscles and the peroneal side of the leg","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral compartment muscles and the peroneal side of the leg', 'The peroneal artery runs beside the fibula and supplies the lateral compartment muscles and the peroneal side of the leg. The other listed regions have distinct arterial supply.'),
('40320fa4-b30a-5404-8fda-abca457ffe32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The great saphenous vein drains into:', '["Femoral vein at the saphenous opening","Popliteal vein","Superficial epigastric vein","Great saphenous vein deep","None"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral vein at the saphenous opening', 'The great saphenous vein ascends along the medial side and pierces the saphenous opening to drain into the femoral vein. The popliteal vein receives the small saphenous vein instead.'),
('4c7dbb1c-ced2-5e49-aaa6-d6732fd794db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The small saphenous vein drains into:', '["Popliteal vein","Femoral vein","Anterior tibial vein","Posterior tibial vein","Great saphenous vein"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal vein', 'The small saphenous vein ascends the posterior calf and drains into the popliteal vein. It does not terminate in the femoral or tibial veins.'),
('63d23443-cb98-5912-ac21-937425353383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The perforating veins of the leg:', '["Connect the superficial and deep venous systems (contain valves directing flow from superficial to deep)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connect arteries and veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Connect nerves to veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Connect lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connect the superficial and deep venous systems (contain valves directing flow from superficial to deep)', 'Perforating veins pierce the deep fascia to connect the superficial and deep venous systems, with valves directing flow from superficial to deep. They do not connect arteries, nerves, or lymphatics.'),
('16ad8bfb-600a-5adb-936c-7070e58719b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Varicose veins result from:', '["Incompetent valves in the perforating veins and superficial veins","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arterial occlusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatic obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neuropathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incompetent valves in the perforating veins and superficial veins', 'Varicose veins arise when valves in the superficial and perforating veins become incompetent, allowing reflux and venous pooling. Arterial occlusion, lymphatic obstruction, and neuropathy do not produce this pattern.'),
('2f68a43f-8f7e-5a36-b45d-4a2fd57df57a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The great saphenous vein is commonly used for:', '["Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG)","Grafting of arteries","Carotid endarterectomy","All of the above","patch"]'::jsonb, 'Grafting of arteries', 'The great saphenous vein is a long, thick-walled conduit harvested for arterial bypass grafting, most often in coronary revascularization. Other listed options do not represent its common use.'),
('a7289b5e-e958-5f43-98d7-093c398b2da1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The saphenous nerve (L3 L4) accompanies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The tibial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The small saphenous vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The great saphenous vein on the medial side of the leg"]'::jsonb, 'The great saphenous vein on the medial side of the leg', 'The saphenous nerve accompanies the great saphenous vein along the medial side of the leg. It does not travel with the femoral artery, tibial nerve, or small saphenous vein in this region.'),
('a6d10d41-6649-564d-a6b8-a1702b3cdb7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies:', '["Medial thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","Foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin of the posterior thigh and popliteal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the posterior thigh and popliteal fossa', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh supplies the skin of the posterior thigh and the popliteal fossa. The anterior, medial, and lateral thigh regions receive different nerves.'),
('b20bdce6-6869-5871-bfd3-342fa1753231'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The femoral nerve (L2-L4) supplies:', '["Leg muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior thigh muscles (quadriceps sartorius pectineus) and iliacus","Medial thigh muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior thigh muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior thigh muscles (quadriceps sartorius pectineus) and iliacus', 'The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh muscles, including the quadriceps, sartorius, and pectineus, as well as the iliacus. The medial and posterior thigh muscles are supplied by the obturator and sciatic nerves.'),
('18d07042-d99b-58b2-9adf-27f9232c3bc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The femoral nerve gives rise to which cutaneous branches?', '["Posterior cutaneous resulting in decreased functional activity","Sural accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral cutaneous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior cutaneous branches of the thigh and saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior cutaneous branches of the thigh and saphenous nerve', 'The femoral nerve gives off anterior cutaneous branches of the thigh and the long saphenous nerve. The sural, lateral, and posterior cutaneous nerves have different origins.'),
('9bddb424-3fde-509d-9038-b04fe3e822d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2 L3) supplies:', '["Posterior thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leg mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin of the lateral aspect of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Skin of the lateral aspect of the thigh', 'The lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (L2-L3) supplies the skin over the lateral aspect of the thigh. Anterior, medial, and posterior thigh skin is innervated by other nerves.'),
('8c25fc64-e97e-5a05-99fe-90f5abcfd19d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Meralgia paresthetica is:', '["Compression of the saphenous nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Compression of the sciatic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Compression of the femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Compression of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (causing pain and paresthesia over lateral thigh)"]'::jsonb, 'Compression of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh (causing pain and paresthesia over lateral thigh)', 'Meralgia paresthetica is caused by compression of the lateral cutaneous nerve of the thigh, producing pain and paresthesia over the lateral thigh. Compression of the femoral, sciatic, or saphenous nerves gives different clinical patterns.'),
('de27b3f3-c66b-58ee-80a7-4ce1bb8cd32e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The fascia lata is the:', '["Superficial fascia","Bone covering","None","Muscle fascia","Deep fascia of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Deep fascia of the thigh', 'The fascia lata is the deep fascia of the thigh that envelops the muscles and forms the intermuscular septa. The superficial fascia and bone covering are separate layers.'),
('ecf53975-7c93-524f-a034-ec59daaf9f93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The iliotibial tract (IT band) is a lateral thickening of:', '["Gluteal fascia","Tensor fascia lata","None","Fascia lata","Superficial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Fascia lata', 'The iliotibial tract is the thickened lateral portion of the fascia lata, strengthened by contributions from the gluteus maximus and tensor fascia lata. It is not the gluteal fascia or superficial fascia.'),
('7a6da440-6f64-501a-89ad-7a107c323738'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The intermuscular septa of the thigh divide the thigh into how many compartments?', '["3 (anterior medial posterior)","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (anterior medial posterior)', 'The lateral and medial intermuscular septa, passing from the fascia lata to the linea aspera, divide the thigh into anterior, medial, and posterior compartments. Thus there are three compartments.'),
('276ab0f3-34a0-5f97-bbcc-81b0d0244e96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The medial intermuscular septum of the thigh separates:', '["None","All","The anterior and medial compartments","The medial and posterior compartments","The anterior and posterior compartments"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior and medial compartments', 'The medial intermuscular septum runs from the adductor canal to the femur and separates the anterior and medial compartments. The lateral septum separates other compartments.'),
('8d2247b8-67b9-59c9-b243-0449feaf79c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lateral intermuscular septum of the thigh separates:', '["The anterior and posterior compartments","None","The medial and posterior compartments","The medial and anterior compartments","All"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior and posterior compartments', 'The lateral intermuscular septum separates the anterior and posterior compartments of the thigh. The medial septum divides the anterior and medial compartments.'),
('0c945c4c-c031-5c40-b9dc-9d79c758216c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The femoral triangle (Scarpa''s triangle) contents from lateral to medial are:', '["Artery vein nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral vein artery nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral nerve femoral artery femoral vein femoral canal (NAVL)","Nerve vein artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral canal nerve artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve femoral artery femoral vein femoral canal (NAVL)', 'From lateral to medial the femoral triangle contains the femoral nerve, artery, vein, and femoral canal (NAVL). The other listed sequences do not match this accepted order.'),
('c828876e-8d7c-52be-9253-fd91f912ab22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The apex of the femoral triangle is:', '["At the adductor hiatus resulting in decreased functional activity","At the femoral ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","At the inguinal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Directed medially where the sartorius crosses the adductor longus"]'::jsonb, 'Directed medially where the sartorius crosses the adductor longus', 'The apex of the femoral triangle is directed downward and medially, at the point where the sartorius crosses the adductor longus. The adductor hiatus, femoral ring, and inguinal ligament are not the apex.'),
('265db70f-9c0d-5949-ada0-bcdc732e365a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The femoral sheath contains:', '["Femoral vessels and nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral nerve only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral vessels and femoral canal (not the femoral nerve)","Femoral artery only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral vessels and femoral canal (not the femoral nerve)', 'The femoral sheath encloses the femoral artery, femoral vein, and femoral canal, but not the femoral nerve, which lies lateral to the sheath. The remaining options are incomplete or incorrect.'),
('c606f156-e6e8-58cb-89de-a5c12c3b5c6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The femoral ring is bounded:', '["By the femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","By the sartorius mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Anteriorly by inguinal ligament medially by lacunar ligament posteriorly by pectineal ligament laterally by femoral vein","By the femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly by inguinal ligament medially by lacunar ligament posteriorly by pectineal ligament laterally by femoral vein', 'The femoral ring is bounded anteriorly by the inguinal ligament, medially by the lacunar ligament, posteriorly by the pectineal ligament, and laterally by the femoral vein. The femoral artery, nerve, and sartorius do not form its boundaries.'),
('64e8fa2d-b998-54a8-9bcb-20ba9276d5a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Femoral hernia is more common in females because:', '["The femoral ring is wider in females (wider pelvis)","The abdominal muscles are weaker","The inguinal ligament is weaker","The inguinal canal is wider","None"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral ring is wider in females (wider pelvis)', 'The femoral ring is wider in females because of the wider female pelvis, which increases susceptibility to femoral hernia. Weakness of the inguinal ligament, inguinal canal, or abdominal muscles does not explain the female predominance.'),
('5004223a-a268-53e0-8a34-6cd557aeeb6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'A femoral hernia passes through the:', '["Inguinal canal and appears above the inguinal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral canal and appears below the inguinal ligament","Obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","Seiotic foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral canal and appears below the inguinal ligament', 'A femoral hernia passes through the femoral canal and emerges below the inguinal ligament. An inguinal hernia appears above the ligament, and the obturator and sciatic foramina are not the route.'),
('bafe80b4-e044-5aa0-b2d2-d9d21019b94a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The saphenous opening (fossa ovalis) is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","In the fascia lata about 4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle","At the femoral triangle apex mediated by specific receptor activation","At the inguinal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the adductor hiatus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the fascia lata about 4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle', 'The saphenous opening lies in the fascia lata about 4 cm below and lateral to the pubic tubercle. The inguinal ligament, femoral triangle apex, and adductor hiatus are different locations.'),
('9a622fee-df74-565f-be71-2778e13a359c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The cribriform fascia covers the:', '["Femoral triangle floor resulting in decreased functional activity","Saphenous opening (perforated by the great saphenous vein and lymphatics)","Femoral ring mediated by specific receptor activation","Adductor canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Saphenous opening (perforated by the great saphenous vein and lymphatics)', 'The cribriform fascia is the sievelike layer covering the saphenous opening and is perforated by the great saphenous vein and lymphatics. It does not cover the femoral ring, adductor canal, or femoral triangle floor.'),
('cf04562f-fa65-5ea7-b4fb-c7ca5082739d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The great saphenous vein pierces the cribriform fascia to drain into:', '["External iliac vein","None","Femoral vein","Popliteal vein","Superficial epigastric vein"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral vein', 'The great saphenous vein pierces the cribriform fascia and drains into the femoral vein. The external iliac and popliteal veins are not its site of termination.'),
('e1539998-341f-560e-ba4a-1906d03dd8c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The profunda femoris (deep femoral) artery is a branch of:', '["Anterior tibial artery","Posterior tibial artery","External iliac artery","Femoral artery (in the femoral triangle)","Popliteal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral artery (in the femoral triangle)', 'The profunda femoris artery arises from the femoral artery within the femoral triangle. It is not a branch of the popliteal, tibial, or external iliac arteries.'),
('c55a9079-6a0b-5cfc-8540-890a240fad1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The profunda femoris artery supplies:', '["The foot resulting in decreased functional activity","The gluteal region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thigh muscles (via perforating branches and medial/lateral circumflex)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The leg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thigh muscles (via perforating branches and medial/lateral circumflex)', 'The profunda femoris artery supplies the thigh muscles through its perforating branches and the medial and lateral circumflex femoral branches. The foot, leg, and gluteal region receive other vessels.'),
('3da3bd30-d6c1-5e57-bee3-389ef55e3790'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The medial circumflex femoral artery supplies:', '["The femoral head and neck (via retinacular branches) and the adductor muscles","The hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The quadriceps mediated by specific receptor activation","The gluteal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral head and neck (via retinacular branches) and the adductor muscles', 'The medial circumflex femoral artery gives retinacular branches to the femoral head and neck and also supplies the adductor muscles. The hamstrings, quadriceps, and gluteal muscles have separate blood supply.'),
('28929b5e-aa16-587d-93b6-ef2236fc3372'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lateral circumflex femoral artery supplies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The gluteals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The quadriceps and the lateral thigh muscles","The hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The adductors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The quadriceps and the lateral thigh muscles', 'The lateral circumflex femoral artery supplies the quadriceps and the lateral thigh muscles. The hamstrings, adductors, and gluteal muscles are supplied by other vessels.'),
('425d17b0-b146-55f0-94d6-44d78b9731b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The perforating arteries (usually 3-4) arise from:', '["Femoral artery","Profunda femoris artery","Posterior tibial artery","Anterior tibial artery","Popliteal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Profunda femoris artery', 'The perforating arteries, usually three or four, arise from the profunda femoris artery. The femoral, popliteal, and tibial arteries are not their origin.'),
('af32926f-9e74-5f41-807e-9d216a877086'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The cruciate anastomosis of the upper thigh involves:', '["Femoral only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior gluteal only mediated by specific receptor activation","Popliteal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial circumflex lateral circumflex inferior gluteal and 1st perforating arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Medial circumflex lateral circumflex inferior gluteal and 1st perforating arteries', 'The cruciate anastomosis of the upper thigh is formed by the medial and lateral circumflex femoral, inferior gluteal, and first perforating arteries. The femoral and popliteal arteries and superior gluteal artery alone are not constituents.'),
('f9326383-2375-5dee-a536-c4a941ba4409'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The trochanteric anastomosis of the upper thigh involves:', '["Ascending branches of medial and lateral circumflex femoris and superior gluteal artery","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior gluteal and femoral mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Popliteal and anterior tibial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending branches of medial and lateral circumflex femoris and superior gluteal artery', 'The trochanteric anastomosis is formed by the ascending branches of the medial and lateral circumflex femoral arteries together with the superior gluteal artery. The inferior gluteal, femoral, popliteal, and anterior tibial arteries do not contribute.'),
('ad6c2b22-eb5d-5fc9-be82-be4c96652bec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The genicular anastomosis of the knee involves:', '["Branches of femoral popliteal and anterior tibial arteries","Only popliteal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only femoral mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only anterior tibial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of femoral popliteal and anterior tibial arteries', 'The genicular anastomosis around the knee is formed by branches of the femoral, popliteal, and anterior tibial arteries. It is not derived from any single artery alone.'),
('87ce5dcc-ac88-5567-99f2-f44df1871e3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The descending genicular artery is a branch of:', '["Profunda femoris resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral artery (just before it passes through adductor hiatus)","Anterior tibial artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Popliteal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral artery (just before it passes through adductor hiatus)', 'The descending genicular artery arises from the femoral artery just proximal to the adductor hiatus. The profunda femoris, popliteal, and anterior tibial arteries do not give origin to it.'),
('a68630bd-4585-5cda-ad12-e7ad61a6dbbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The superior lateral genicular artery is a branch of:', '["Posterior tibial artery","Femoral artery","Popliteal artery","Anterior tibial artery","Peroneal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal artery', 'The superior lateral genicular artery arises from the popliteal artery. The femoral, anterior tibial, posterior tibial, and peroneal arteries are not its source.'),
('5e802807-a14d-58b6-a943-20e9fcc5a081'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The inferior lateral genicular artery is a branch of:', '["Peroneal artery","Posterior tibial artery","Popliteal artery","Anterior tibial artery","Femoral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Popliteal artery', 'The inferior lateral genicular artery arises from the popliteal artery. The femoral, anterior tibial, posterior tibial, and peroneal arteries are not its source.'),
('1577ee96-43aa-5ebb-b5b6-c15529bd31b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The recurrent branch of the anterior tibial artery participates in:', '["The cruciate anastomosis resulting in decreased functional activity","The genicular anastomosis (anterior tibial recurrent)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The plantar arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The trochanteric anastomosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The genicular anastomosis (anterior tibial recurrent)', 'The recurrent branch of the anterior tibial artery ascends to join the genicular anastomosis around the knee. The cruciate, trochanteric, and plantar arch are separate vascular networks.'),
('a6a0e568-7ac5-552e-b517-177cf6fb3307'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The patellar reflex (knee jerk) involves:', '["Sciatic nerve","Femoral and obturator","Femoral nerve (L3 L4)","Sciatic and femoral","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L3 L4)', 'The patellar reflex is mediated by the femoral nerve (L3-L4), which innervates the quadriceps femoris. The sciatic, obturator, and mixed choices do not mediate this reflex.'),
('b8c269cf-286f-524a-bf46-6a77e8057841'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The ankle jerk (Achilles reflex) involves:', '["Deep peroneal nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve (S1 S2)","Sciatic"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (S1 S2)', 'The ankle jerk is mediated by the tibial nerve (S1-S2), which innervates the triceps surae. The deep peroneal, obturator, and femoral nerves are not involved in this reflex.'),
('f8059c70-99fd-5cb4-b4b3-1b1cfc2d3c69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The normal angle of the femoral neck (inclination angle) is:', '["140 degrees","125 degrees (125-135)","150 degrees","110 degrees","90 degrees"]'::jsonb, '125 degrees (125-135)', 'The normal inclination angle between the femoral neck and shaft is about 125 degrees (range 125-135). An angle above 135 defines coxa valga and below 120 defines coxa vara.'),
('e2bd4298-e3ba-5c66-bd49-eaa5619ba2b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Coxa valga (increased angle >135) is associated with:', '["Femoral neck fracture","Osteoarthritis","None","Leg shortening","Congenital hip dislocation (CHD)"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital hip dislocation (CHD)', 'A valgus femoral neck shifts the line of weight transmission medially, predisposing to developmental (congenital) hip dislocation. Acute fracture and osteoarthritis are not the primary associations.'),
('9cdcf1bd-082e-5d92-8f29-8f1abf29e143'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Coxa vara (decreased angle <120) is associated with:', '["Foot drop","CHD","Femoral neck fracture in children","None","Osteoarthritis and hip pain"]'::jsonb, 'Osteoarthritis and hip pain', 'A varus femoral neck shortens the limb and alters joint mechanics, leading to premature osteoarthritis with hip pain. Foot drop and femoral neck fracture in children are unrelated causes.'),
('a0b0d9cb-8aae-5c2d-85b9-add81d9eb917'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The patella develops as a sesamoid bone in the tendon of:', '["Biceps femoris","Sartorius","Semimembranosus","Quadriceps femoris","Patellar ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps femoris', 'The patella is the largest sesamoid bone, developing within the tendon of quadriceps femoris. The other listed tendons do not contain the patella.'),
('d1daf039-f35c-5503-9239-8f5548eb801d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The patella has how many facets on its posterior articular surface?', '["7 (3 medial 3 lateral 1 odd)","5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 mediated by specific receptor activation","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '7 (3 medial 3 lateral 1 odd)', 'The posterior articular surface bears seven facets: three medial, three lateral, and one ''odd'' facet on the medial side. Fewer facets would not match the patellofemoral articulation pattern.'),
('b9012221-b272-5bb5-be61-bafc99974717'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The odd facet of the patella articulates with:', '["The medial femoral condyle in extreme flexion","The lateral condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The patellar surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The medial femoral condyle in extreme flexion', 'In extreme (deep) flexion the odd facet contacts the medial femoral condyle, while the lateral facets contact the lateral condyle. It does not articulate with the lateral condyle or patellar surface.'),
('6b498fa3-63ee-5588-bc8f-264a3d1fcf59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The patellar retinacula are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensions of the quadriceps expansions to the tibial condyles","The medial collateral ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The patellar tendon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The quadriceps tendon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensions of the quadriceps expansions to the tibial condyles', 'The medial and lateral patellar retinacula are fibrous extensions of the vastus medialis and lateralis (quadriceps expansions) that reinforce the joint capsule down to the tibial condyles.'),
('102b07eb-cce8-56e5-b712-2369857a0e3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The infrapatellar fat pad (Hoffa''s fat pad) is:', '["In the popliteal fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Behind the quadriceps tendon mediated by specific receptor activation","In the suprapatellar bursa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A mass of fatty tissue behind the patellar ligament"]'::jsonb, 'A mass of fatty tissue behind the patellar ligament', 'Hoffa''s fat pad lies between the patellar ligament and the tibia, filling the space behind the ligament. It does not occupy the popliteal fossa, suprapatellar bursa, or quadriceps tendon region.'),
('ff8385cb-1bcf-5cde-b739-53203509cace'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The prepatellar bursa (housemaid''s knee) is between:', '["The gastrocnemius and capsule","The patella and the skin","The quadriceps tendon and femur","The patellar ligament and tibia","None"]'::jsonb, 'The patella and the skin', 'The prepatellar bursa lies between the anterior surface of the patella and the overlying skin, allowing skin movement during kneeling. Housemaid''s knee is its inflammation.'),
('7aa6bca6-2873-54e1-80f9-0d77de5751e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The infrapatellar bursa (clergyman''s knee) is between:', '["The gastrocnemius and capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","The patella and the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The patellar ligament and the tibia (deep infrapatellar)","The quadriceps and femur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The patellar ligament and the tibia (deep infrapatellar)', 'The deep infrapatellar bursa lies between the patellar ligament and the tibia. The superficial infrapatellar bursa lies between the ligament and skin; the other pairs are incorrect.'),
('454d1512-01f4-5342-aeda-98b09f0d22b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The suprapatellar bursa communicates with:', '["The infrapatellar bursa resulting in decreased functional activity","The prepatellar bursa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The knee joint cavity (separated only by a small fold)","The popliteal cyst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The knee joint cavity (separated only by a small fold)', 'The suprapatellar bursa is an upward extension of the knee joint capsule and freely communicates with the joint cavity, separated only by a small synovial fold.'),
('14977df2-85f4-5a75-85c7-d61ca543aa2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'A Baker''s cyst (popliteal cyst) is:', '["An infrapatellar bursitis resulting in decreased functional activity","A prepatellar bursitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A meniscal cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A distention of the semimembranosus bursa (often communicating with the knee joint)"]'::jsonb, 'A distention of the semimembranosus bursa (often communicating with the knee joint)', 'A Baker''s cyst is an abnormal enlargement of the semimembranosus bursa, often communicating with the knee joint, typically in the popliteal fossa. It is not a prepatellar or infrapatellar bursitis.'),
('9fc34813-47b9-5d08-8240-e1d8ab7c3bf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The arcuate popliteal ligament arises from:', '["Lateral condyle of femur","None","Patella","Medial condyle of tibia","Fibular head"]'::jsonb, 'Fibular head', 'The arcuate popliteal ligament passes from the head of the fibula over the popliteus muscle, arching toward the intercondylar area of the femur. It does not arise from the femoral or tibial condyles or patella.'),
('40cfdefe-84b1-5d50-a3cf-eef30f85305b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The fibrous capsule of the knee is attached:', '["Only to the femur resulting in decreased functional activity","To the fibula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only to the tibia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To the margins of the femoral and tibial articular surfaces (and patella)"]'::jsonb, 'To the margins of the femoral and tibial articular surfaces (and patella)', 'The fibrous capsule attaches to the margins of the femoral and tibial articular surfaces and to the patella, giving the knee capsule its characteristic loose, strong structure.'),
('4671e75d-53b7-59e8-8540-6dff29f10085'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The synovial membrane of the knee is the most extensive in the body and forms:', '["The suprapatellar bursa and subpopliteal recess","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the bursae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the joint cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The suprapatellar bursa and subpopliteal recess', 'The extensive knee synovium lines the joint and forms the suprapatellar bursa and the subpopliteal recess. The capsular pouchings do not end at simple bursae or the bare joint cavity.'),
('55a53888-9c97-57cd-a5d3-86104ea3c468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lateral meniscus is attached to:', '["The LCL resulting in decreased functional activity","The popliteus muscle (popliteal hiatus)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The patellar ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The MCL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The popliteus muscle (popliteal hiatus)', 'The lateral meniscus is separated from the LCL by the tendon of popliteus, which passes through the popliteal hiatus to attach to the lateral femoral condyle. The medial meniscus is firmly fixed to the MCL.'),
('e58a4a67-45ba-5af7-a12f-80988344706d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) prevents:', '["Lateral shift resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior translation of the tibia on the femur","Medial shift mediated by specific receptor activation","Rotation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior translation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior translation of the tibia on the femur', 'The ACL prevents anterior displacement of the tibia relative to the femur. It also checks hyperextension and medial rotation, but its primary tension resists anterior tibial translation.'),
('2dcbcc3f-40fd-5f26-aa26-5d348e75bc51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) prevents:', '["Medial shift resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior translation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior translation of the tibia on the femur","Lateral shift without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rotation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior translation of the tibia on the femur', 'The PCL prevents posterior translation of the tibia on the femur and is taut in flexion. Anterior translation is resisted by the ACL, not the PCL.'),
('379646f5-abc8-5f0b-bfa6-3558299e7f8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The ACL is more commonly injured than the PCL in:', '["Dashboard injuries resulting in decreased functional activity","Athletic injuries (non-contact twisting)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperextension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoflexion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Athletic injuries (non-contact twisting)', 'In sports, especially cutting and pivoting movements, the ACL is commonly torn by non-contact twisting. Dashboard injuries and hyperextension more characteristically damage the PCL and collateral ligaments.'),
('1c2d8ae2-337a-59d7-a834-490f14a4db75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The PCL is more commonly injured by:', '["Dashboard injury (direct blow to the proximal tibia with knee flexed)","Landing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Falling without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Twisting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dashboard injury (direct blow to the proximal tibia with knee flexed)', 'A dashboard injury produces a direct blow to the proximal tibia with the knee flexed, driving the tibia posteriorly and tearing the PCL. Twisting more commonly injures the ACL.'),
('9d840395-47f0-50b3-b344-cb55ea52fbdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The drawer test (anterior) tests the integrity of:', '["The menisci","The anterior cruciate ligament","The posterior cruciate ligament","The medial collateral ligament","The lateral collateral ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior cruciate ligament', 'The anterior drawer test stabilizes the femur and draws the tibia forward; excessive anterior motion indicates ACL insufficiency. It does not primarily evaluate menisci or collateral ligaments.'),
('67191d94-be73-570c-a2d2-600b15d39710'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The drawer test (posterior) tests the integrity of:', '["The menisci","The anterior cruciate ligament","The LCL","The posterior cruciate ligament","The MCL"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior cruciate ligament', 'The posterior drawer test pushes the tibia backward on the femur; unrestrained posterior displacement indicates a torn PCL. MCL, LCL, and menisci are not the focus.'),
('d749a885-3f69-518c-bba2-ac0704c4cd0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The Lachman test is a more sensitive test for:', '["MCL integrity resulting in decreased functional activity","LCL integrity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meniscal tear mediated by specific receptor activation","PCL integrity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACL integrity (with the knee in 20-30 degrees flexion)"]'::jsonb, 'ACL integrity (with the knee in 20-30 degrees flexion)', 'The Lachman test, performed with the knee at about 20-30 degrees of flexion, detects anterior tibial translation and is more sensitive than the drawer test for ACL tears. It is not used for the menisci or collateral ligaments.'),
('41297ffd-a756-5eeb-bdeb-2792eeaa181a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The McMurray test is used to detect:', '["ACL tears resulting in decreased functional activity","PCL tears accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patellar instability mediated by specific receptor activation","Meniscal tears (medial or lateral)","MCL tears leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Meniscal tears (medial or lateral)', 'The McMurray maneuver imparts rotation with varus/valgus stress to trap a torn meniscus, producing a palpable click, and so detects medial or lateral meniscal tears.'),
('0ae86479-e9f1-536c-abb1-aaa81ff0e531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The screw-home mechanism of the knee involves:', '["Medial rotation of the femur on the tibia in terminal extension","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Locking of the patella mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral rotation of the femur without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial rotation of the tibia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial rotation of the femur on the tibia in terminal extension', 'In terminal extension the medial femoral condyle continues to advance, rotating the femur medially on a fixed tibia—this ''screwed-home'' position locks the knee. Laterally rotating the femur would unlock it.'),
('dbc8e957-63d0-566f-a166-3b781c36159a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The popliteus muscle initiates:', '["Lateral rotation of the tibia resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial rotation of the tibia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Locking of the knee mediated by specific receptor activation","Extension of the knee without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexion of the knee (by unlocking the screw-home mechanism)"]'::jsonb, 'Flexion of the knee (by unlocking the screw-home mechanism)', 'The popliteus medially rotates the tibia (or laterally rotates the femur on a fixed tibia) to unlock the locked knee, thereby initiating flexion. It does not extend the knee or cause locking.'),
('c8716264-1fec-5488-9cbc-76f6f633e523'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The knee is most stable in:', '["Full flexion","30 degrees flexion","Full extension (close-packed position)","0 degrees flexion","90 degrees flexion"]'::jsonb, 'Full extension (close-packed position)', 'The knee is most stable in full extension, its close-packed position, where the cruciates are taut and the femoral condyles are fully engaged. At 20-30 degrees of flexion stability is poorest.'),
('5d7b86bc-5d6f-5532-b14e-23904359750d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The knee is least stable at:', '["0 degrees resulting in decreased functional activity","Full flexion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","90 degrees mediated by specific receptor activation","Full extension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","20-30 degrees of flexion (open-packed position)"]'::jsonb, '20-30 degrees of flexion (open-packed position)', 'At about 20-30 degrees of flexion the knee is in the open-packed (loose-packed) position, with minimal ligamentous tension, making it least stable. Full extension is the most stable position.'),
('613d64fb-6422-57e8-a860-24678d5ebc54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The menisci are absent at birth and develop fully by:', '["10 years","About 2 years of age","Adolescence","Birth","5 years"]'::jsonb, 'About 2 years of age', 'The menisci are not fully present at birth and attain adult morphology and size by about 2 years of age. Full maturity does not await adolescence.'),
('0643f208-2519-57af-b3de-ab947a0c45d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The discoid lateral meniscus is:', '["symptomatic resulting in decreased functional activity","A congenital anomaly where the lateral meniscus is disc-shaped","More common in Caucasians mediated by specific receptor activation","Fractured meniscus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital anomaly where the lateral meniscus is disc-shaped', 'A discoid lateral meniscus is a congenital anomaly in which the meniscus is disc-shaped instead of crescentic, predisposing to snapping and tears in children. It is not simply a fracture or nonspecific symptom.'),
('f8d4e583-fe43-5cc7-80e3-c9f762ba7389'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The blood supply of the menisci is:', '["From the femoral resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From the popliteal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","From the central artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the perimeniscal capillary plexus (only the peripheral 1/3 is vascular)"]'::jsonb, 'From the perimeniscal capillary plexus (only the peripheral 1/3 is vascular)', 'Menisci are supplied by the perimeniscal capillary plexus, a peripheral network reaching only the outer one-third; the inner two-thirds are avascular. Central arteries, the femoral, and the popliteal artery are not the source.'),
('d1292c07-4680-51a3-86c5-899d6e7ac1a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Tears in the vascular (red) zone of the meniscus have:', '["Infection risk resulting in decreased functional activity","Good healing potential (due to blood supply)","Poor healing potential mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","healing potential leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Good healing potential (due to blood supply)', 'Tears confined to the vascular red zone heal well because the plexus delivers blood and repair cells. The avascular white-zone tears have poor healing potential.'),
('cf813afa-9cc4-5bb5-9fd8-04a8756cc63d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The semimembranosus bursa is located:', '["In front of the tibia resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above the patella mediated by specific receptor activation","Beneath the semimembranosus tendon (may communicate with knee joint)","Behind the patella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beneath the semimembranosus tendon (may communicate with knee joint)', 'The semimembranosus bursa lies beneath (deep to) the semimembranosus tendon in the medial popliteal fossa and may communicate with the knee joint capsule.'),
('2711b474-7b12-56fb-bd26-585541b970c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The gastrocnemius bursa is located:', '["Prepatellar resulting in decreased functional activity","Infrapatellar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Behind the lateral head mediated by specific receptor activation","Between the gastrocnemius and the joint capsule (medial head commonly)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the gastrocnemius and the joint capsule (medial head commonly)', 'The gastrocnemius bursa lies between the medial head of gastrocnemius and the joint capsule, and is the usual site of a Baker''s cyst. The other locations describe distinct bursae.'),
('fa4afff8-4734-5d55-9be3-61fadd25b99b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tarsal tunnel syndrome involves compression of:', '["Sural nerve","Superficial peroneal nerve","Common peroneal nerve","Deep peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve (behind the medial malleolus)"]'::jsonb, 'Tibial nerve (behind the medial malleolus)', 'Tarsal tunnel syndrome is caused by compression of the tibial nerve as it runs behind the medial malleolus beneath the flexor retinaculum. The peroneal and sural nerves do not pass through the tunnel.'),
('fba6a4de-f6c0-52f8-811e-8830cc84a0a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Tarsal tunnel syndrome causes:', '["Anterior leg pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Foot drop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain and paresthesia on the sole of the foot","Dorsal foot numbness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral foot numbness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and paresthesia on the sole of the foot', 'Tibial nerve compression gives pain, burning, and paresthesia over the sole of the foot, where its branches supply sensation. Foot drop and dorsal/lateral foot numbness reflect peroneal nerve injury.'),
('9bcc0762-f593-5805-9f36-c2dd11f9f55e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor retinaculum of the ankle (laciniate ligament) extends from:', '["Tibia to fibula","Lateral malleolus to calcaneus","None","Medial malleolus to talus","Medial malleolus to calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial malleolus to calcaneus', 'The flexor retinaculum (laciniate ligament) of the ankle spans from the medial malleolus to the calcaneus, binding the flexor tendons and tibial neurovascular bundle behind the malleolus.'),
('7759bb36-dfa1-5565-96e0-e2f301df8eba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The superior extensor retinaculum extends from:', '["None","Tibia to talus","Medial malleolus to calcaneus","Tibia to fibula (above the ankle joint)","Lateral malleolus to calcaneus"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia to fibula (above the ankle joint)', 'The superior extensor retinaculum is a transverse band running from the anterior border of the tibia to the fibula, just above the ankle joint, holding the extensor tendons.'),
('1cd1b3ed-4c36-527d-8f7d-ff0380a0808f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The inferior extensor retinaculum is aY-shaped band extending from:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus to cuboid mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus to medial malleolus and navicular (forming a sling for extensor tendons)","Tibia to fibula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus to medial malleolus and navicular (forming a sling for extensor tendons)', 'The inferior extensor retinaculum is aY-shaped band arising laterally from the calcaneus and dividing into limbs attaching to the medial malleolus and navicular, slinging the extensor tendons.'),
('1e1960d8-767d-5e1e-8556-6a34f8787246'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The superior peroneal retinaculum holds:', '["The flexor tendons behind the medial malleolus","The peroneal tendons behind the lateral malleolus","The tibialis anterior","The extensor tendons on the dorsum","None"]'::jsonb, 'The peroneal tendons behind the lateral malleolus', 'The superior peroneal retinaculum binds the peroneus longus and brevis tendons behind the lateral malleolus. It does not brace the medial flexors or the extensor tendons.'),
('1dd8040c-89db-5053-a76f-e2533af86973'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The inferior peroneal retinaculum holds:', '["The flexor tendons resulting in decreased functional activity","The extensor tendons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The tibialis posterior mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The peroneal tendons below the lateral malleolus (over the calcaneus)"]'::jsonb, 'The peroneal tendons below the lateral malleolus (over the calcaneus)', 'The inferior peroneal retinaculum holds the peroneal tendons over the lateral calcaneus, below the lateral malleolus. Flexor, extensor, and tibialis posterior tendons lie elsewhere.'),
('b3ae7d0a-11bd-56ea-b5ef-ed5f6bf529ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The leg has how many fascial compartments?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","4 (anterior lateral superficial posterior deep posterior)","3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 (anterior lateral superficial posterior deep posterior)', 'The leg is divided into four compartments: anterior, lateral, superficial posterior, and deep posterior. Two, three, and five do not reflect the standard fascial partitioning.'),
('c2a17301-8acf-5f5d-9b69-3e1c6e8dbc84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The anterior compartment of the leg is bounded by:', '["Tibia fibula interosseous membrane and anterior intermuscular septum","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibula only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibia and skin only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia fibula interosseous membrane and anterior intermuscular septum', 'The anterior compartment is bounded by the tibia, fibula, the interosseous membrane, and the anterior intermuscular septum. No single bone or skin boundaries encloses it.'),
('27569c2b-63d8-534e-9d2d-3a33986a92e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Acute compartment syndrome of the anterior leg is characterized by:', '["Severe pain with passive toe flexion foot drop and loss of sensation in the first web space","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plantar flexion weakness mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain with dorsiflexion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe pain with passive toe flexion foot drop and loss of sensation in the first web space', 'Anterior compartment syndrome classically causes severe pain on passive toe flexion, weakness of ankle dorsiflexion (foot drop), and loss of sensation in the first web space supplied by the deep peroneal nerve.'),
('5b27c539-f5bc-5db8-88e2-13a81ed577e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The superficial posterior compartment of the leg contains:', '["Gastrocnemius soleus plantaris","Peroneus longus brevis","Tibialis anterior EHL EDL","Tibialis posterior FDL FHL","None"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrocnemius soleus plantaris', 'The superficial posterior compartment contains gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris. Peroneal muscles occupy the lateral compartment and the deep flexors the deep posterior compartment.'),
('6416351f-c33e-52a6-8f60-8f46dcdee78e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The deep posterior compartment of the leg contains:', '["Gastrocnemius and soleus","Peroneus longus brevis","None","Tibialis posterior FDL FHL popliteus","Tibialis anterior EHL EDL"]'::jsonb, 'Tibialis posterior FDL FHL popliteus', 'The deep posterior compartment contains tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus, and popliteus. Gastrocnemius and soleus belong to the superficial posterior compartment.'),
('a1602651-9120-52b9-accd-c9a5ab0364cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The soleal line of the tibia is:', '["A ridge on the anterior tibia resulting in decreased functional activity","The tibial tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ridge on the posterior tibial shaft for the origin of the soleus","The medial malleolus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ridge on the posterior tibial shaft for the origin of the soleus', 'The soleal line is a ridge crossing the posterior surface of the tibia that gives origin to soleus. It is not on the anterior tibia, at the tuberosity, or at the medial malleolus.'),
('e7567511-e71c-5f0d-a7d2-9532a62bbac4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibial tuberosity gives attachment to:', '["Sartorius","None","Quadriceps tendon","Gastrocnemius","Patellar ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Patellar ligament', 'The tibial tuberosity is the distal attachment of the patellar ligament, which connects the quadriceps apparatus to the tibia. Sartorius, gastrocnemius, and the quadriceps tendon attach elsewhere.'),
('36361800-5810-51f9-882b-2d95f5367fa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Osgood-Schlatter disease involves:', '["Patellar fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","Patellofemoral syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meniscal tear mediated by specific receptor activation","ACL tear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammation of the tibial tuberosity apophysis (traction apophysitis)"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the tibial tuberosity apophysis (traction apophysitis)', 'Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity at the patellar ligament attachment, seen in skeletally immature adolescents. It is not a fracture of the patella or a meniscal/cruciate injury.'),
('72d44ead-fd95-51d0-a677-b1568f75a3d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The soleus muscle originates from:', '["Medial femoral condyle resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral femoral condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Soleal line of tibia and posterior fibula (and tendinous arch)","Tibial tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Patella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Soleal line of tibia and posterior fibula (and tendinous arch)', 'Soleus arises from the soleal line of the tibia, the posterior aspect of the fibular head, and the tendinous arch between them. The femoral condyles and patella give origin to other muscles.'),
('9bcb04f2-ba9a-5149-8af0-94276bd93df1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The plantaris muscle originates from:', '["Tibial tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial femoral condyle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patella mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral supracondylar ridge of femur (above lateral head of gastrocnemius)","Tibia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral supracondylar ridge of femur (above lateral head of gastrocnemius)', 'Plantaris arises from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the femur, just above the lateral head of the gastrocnemius, and its long thin tendon descends to the calcaneus. The tibial tuberosity, patella, tibia, and medial femoral condyle do not give origin to plantaris.'),
('c146df18-c602-524c-a713-d0dcb5495c72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The plantaris tendon inserts into:', '["Distal phalanx of hallux resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcaneus lateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus (medial to Achilles tendon)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus (medial to Achilles tendon)', 'The plantaris tendon runs between gastrocnemius and soleus and attaches to the posterior calcaneus just medial to the Achilles tendon. The distal phalanx of the hallux and navicular receive other tendons, not plantaris.'),
('265a932e-f24a-51cc-8c3b-cf6030397c25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The gastrocnemius originates from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Soleal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tibial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial and lateral femoral condyles (two heads)","Patella leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral femoral condyles (two heads)', 'Gastrocnemius arises by two heads from the medial and lateral femoral condyles, which enables it to flex the knee as well as plantarflex the ankle. It has no origin from the tibia, soleal line, or patella; the soleal line gives origin to soleus.'),
('4fa9dfca-2a9d-545e-8630-2833331392b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The gastrocnemius and soleus together form the:', '["Hamstrings resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadriceps femoris mediated by specific receptor activation","Adductor group without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Triceps surae (inserting via the Achilles tendon into the calcaneus)"]'::jsonb, 'Triceps surae (inserting via the Achilles tendon into the calcaneus)', 'Gastrocnemius and soleus constitute the triceps surae, whose conjoined tendons form the Achilles tendon inserting into the calcaneus. The hamstrings, quadriceps femoris, and adductor group are separate thigh muscle groups.'),
('d7a37efd-95e7-5d21-bee1-fe4e4faf1d52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The gastrocnemius crosses both the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Ankle and subtalar joints accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hip and knee mediated by specific receptor activation","Knee and ankle joints (flexes knee and plantarflexes ankle)","Hip and ankle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Knee and ankle joints (flexes knee and plantarflexes ankle)', 'Because it arises from the femoral condyles and inserts on the calcaneus, gastrocnemius crosses the knee and ankle joints, acting as a knee flexor and ankle plantarflexor. It does not act on the hip or the subtalar joint.'),
('42baf738-a6eb-5ce9-8998-e05bbc06b77b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The soleus crosses only the:', '["Hip joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Knee joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Subtalar joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ankle joint (plantarflexor)"]'::jsonb, 'Ankle joint (plantarflexor)', 'Soleus arises from the posterior tibia and fibula below the knee and inserts on the calcaneus, so it crosses only the ankle joint and is a powerful plantarflexor. With no femoral attachment it cannot flex the knee or act on the hip.'),
('787eddac-d606-5586-adde-fa8ea42e3618'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The plantaris is a weak flexor of the:', '["Knee resulting in decreased functional activity","Knee and plantarflexor of the ankle","Hip mediated by specific receptor activation","Ankle only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Knee and plantarflexor of the ankle', 'Plantaris, originating above the lateral femoral condyle and inserting on the calcaneus, is a weak knee flexor and weak ankle plantarflexor. It has no attachment related to the hip joint.'),
('4817edd2-0b2b-591e-88e4-5773d148ada4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The Achilles tendon (tendo calcaneus) is the thickest tendon in the body. It is formed by:', '["Soleus only","Plantaris only","All three","Gastrocnemius and soleus tendons","Gastrocnemius only"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrocnemius and soleus tendons', 'The Achilles tendon is the common tendon of gastrocnemius and soleus. Plantaris has its own thin separate tendon that passes alongside the Achilles, so it is not a component of the main tendon.'),
('638c9ef6-e5f1-564d-9bb0-868bed594cbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The Achilles tendon inserts into:', '["The navicular resulting in decreased functional activity","The cuboid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The metatarsal mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior surface of the calcaneus","The talus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior surface of the calcaneus', 'The Achilles tendon attaches to the posterior surface of the calcaneus. The navicular, cuboid, metatarsals, and talus are not insertion sites for the Achilles tendon.'),
('b99d2d5c-34f2-5413-b152-8a4ba0b9bab0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Rupture of the Achilles tendon causes:', '["Inversion weakness resulting in decreased functional activity","Eversion weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foot drop mediated by specific receptor activation","Inability to plantarflex the foot (positive Thompson test)","Inability to dorsiflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to plantarflex the foot (positive Thompson test)', 'Achilles rupture abolishes triceps surae action, so plantarflexion is weak and the Thompson test is positive, with no foot movement on calf squeeze. Inversion, eversion, and dorsiflexion are largely preserved by other muscles.'),
('75c9f07a-a56e-5362-8fdb-1790d26ec0e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The Thompson (Simmonds) test for Achilles rupture involves:', '["Everting the foot resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsiflexing the foot mediated by specific receptor activation","Squeezing the calf (foot should plantarflex if intact) no movement if ruptured","Plantarflexing the foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squeezing the calf (foot should plantarflex if intact) no movement if ruptured', 'In the Thompson test the examiner squeezes the calf with the patient prone; an intact Achilles pulls the foot into plantarflexion, whereas a ruptured tendon produces no movement. The test does not involve eversion or dorsiflexion.'),
('e73d14f5-f2d4-5bbe-9d8f-2b20470d0243'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibialis anterior originates from:', '["Lateral condyle of tibia upper half of lateral tibial shaft and interosseous membrane","Fibula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial tibia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral condyle of tibia upper half of lateral tibial shaft and interosseous membrane', 'Tibialis anterior arises from the lateral condyle of the tibia, the upper half of the lateral tibial shaft, and the interosseous membrane in the anterior compartment. It does not arise from the fibula, calcaneus, or medial tibia.'),
('c535c38e-f377-5a2b-9daa-a5816a51ef32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibialis anterior inserts into:', '["Navicular resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial cuneiform and base of 1st metatarsal","Calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cuboid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All metatarsals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cuneiform and base of 1st metatarsal', 'The tibialis anterior tendon passes to the medial side of the foot and inserts into the medial cuneiform and the base of the first metatarsal. The navicular and cuboid do not receive this tendon.'),
('bd6c6ba6-cf14-509f-88c0-07d18e45a67f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibialis anterior acts to:', '["Dorsiflex and evert","Dorsiflex and invert the foot","Plantarflex and evert","None","Plantarflex and invert"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflex and invert the foot', 'Tibialis anterior is the principal dorsiflexor of the ankle and also inverts the foot. It does not evert or plantarflex the foot.'),
('7d5ee363-b0d4-5dbd-a760-e5c42e4adead'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The extensor hallucis longus originates from:', '["Lateral tibia resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial fibula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle half of the anterior fibula and interosseous membrane","Calcaneus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Middle half of the anterior fibula and interosseous membrane', 'Extensor hallucis longus arises from the middle half of the anterior fibula and the adjacent interosseous membrane. It has no origin from the tibia or calcaneus.'),
('b7f0b9fe-123d-5f0b-8ff7-e1bcba6cd7f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The extensor hallucis longus inserts into:', '["Medial cuneiform resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Base of the proximal phalanx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Base of the distal phalanx of the great toe"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the distal phalanx of the great toe', 'Extensor hallucis longus passes to the dorsum of the first toe and inserts into the base of the distal phalanx of the great toe. The medial cuneiform and navicular are unrelated insertion sites.'),
('a2cab90c-773a-5a77-8617-ebc8c1caa4f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The extensor hallucis longus acts to:', '["Flex the great toe resulting in decreased functional activity","Extend the great toe and dorsiflex the ankle","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Evert the foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Invert the foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extend the great toe and dorsiflex the ankle', 'Extensor hallucis longus extends the great toe at the interphalangeal joint and assists in dorsiflexion of the ankle. It does not flex the toe or evert the foot.'),
('a97cd185-57c0-51c8-934c-f4149b7a6777'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The extensor digitorum longus originates from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial tibia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral condyle of tibia upper half of fibula and interosseous membrane","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral condyle of tibia upper half of fibula and interosseous membrane', 'Extensor digitorum longus arises from the lateral condyle of the tibia, the upper half of the fibula, and the interosseous membrane. The calcaneus, navicular, and medial tibia give no origin to this muscle.'),
('12f8809e-3b39-5617-b5e0-8a8b4a66b164'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The extensor digitorum longus inserts into:', '["Middle and distal phalanges of the lateral 4 toes (via extensor expansion)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Navicular mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial cuneiform without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal phalanx of great toe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Middle and distal phalanges of the lateral 4 toes (via extensor expansion)', 'Extensor digitorum longus splits into four tendons that reach the middle and distal phalanges of the lateral four toes through the extensor expansions. It does not insert into the great toe, navicular, or medial cuneiform.'),
('446405a0-2cba-5605-b2bf-a252f325d785'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneus tertius is a part of:', '["Peroneus brevis resulting in decreased functional activity","Peroneus longus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","EHL mediated by specific receptor activation","EDL (inserts into the base of the 5th metatarsal)","Tibialis anterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'EDL (inserts into the base of the 5th metatarsal)', 'Peroneus tertius is the most lateral slip of extensor digitorum longus, detaching to insert into the base of the fifth metatarsal. It is not derived from peroneus longus, peroneus brevis, EHL, or tibialis anterior.'),
('c40081fb-ae21-5d2c-a9cc-226c6e1c0434'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneus tertius acts to:', '["Invert the foot","Dorsiflex only","Plantarflex and evert","Dorsiflex and evert the foot","None"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflex and evert the foot', 'Peroneus tertius, running with EDL anterior to the lateral malleolus, dorsiflexes the ankle and everts the foot. It does not invert or plantarflex the foot.'),
('45614aaf-926f-5656-91f0-0f1d5d74d395'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The extensor digitorum brevis is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A leg muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An intrinsic muscle of the dorsum of the foot (supplied by deep peroneal nerve)","A plantar muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An intrinsic muscle of the dorsum of the foot (supplied by deep peroneal nerve)', 'Extensor digitorum brevis is an intrinsic muscle lying on the dorsum of the foot, innervated by the deep peroneal nerve. Extrinsic leg muscles lie in the compartments of the leg, not on the dorsum of the foot.'),
('86e71fd5-e5ca-512d-9110-e869cfd5305d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The extensor digitorum brevis inserts into:', '["The extensor expansions of the medial 4 toes (via slips)","The proximal phalanges accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metatarsals mediated by specific receptor activation","The distal phalanges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The extensor expansions of the medial 4 toes (via slips)', 'Extensor digitorum brevis gives slips that join the extensor expansions of the medial four toes. It does not insert into metatarsals or directly into the distal phalanges.'),
('216c19c1-259f-5912-924f-9286427d1ca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneus longus originates from:', '["Cuboid resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Head and upper lateral fibula","Medial tibia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head and upper lateral fibula', 'Peroneus longus arises from the head and upper lateral surface of the fibula. The cuboid, navicular, and calcaneus are tarsal bones, not sites of origin for this muscle.'),
('4fa38a1b-b795-528e-a448-0f85b2de0171'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneus brevis originates from:', '["Cuboid resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper fibula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower lateral fibula (below peroneus longus)","Calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial tibia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lateral fibula (below peroneus longus)', 'Peroneus brevis arises from the lower lateral fibula, below the origin of peroneus longus, and lies deep to it. It does not arise from the cuboid, calcaneus, or tibia.'),
('7f59756f-edd2-5722-a735-d196fbe117e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneus longus inserts into:', '["Navicular resulting in decreased functional activity","Base of 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform (via the sole of foot)","Cuboid mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Base of 5th metatarsal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of 1st metatarsal and medial cuneiform (via the sole of foot)', 'Peroneus longus runs behind the lateral malleolus, crosses the sole of the foot obliquely, and inserts into the base of the first metatarsal and the medial cuneiform. The base of the fifth metatarsal receives peroneus brevis.'),
('303bf88c-d3e0-58db-929c-eca077e6f668'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneus brevis inserts into:', '["Base of 1st metatarsal resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base of 5th metatarsal (styloid process)","Calcaneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuboid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of 5th metatarsal (styloid process)', 'Peroneus brevis, having run behind the lateral malleolus, inserts into the styloid process at the base of the fifth metatarsal. The base of the first metatarsal receives peroneus longus.'),
('5e0d9d63-9346-5416-906d-b9d3c09dc2a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneus longus and brevis act to:', '["Invert the foot resulting in decreased functional activity","Extend the toes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Evert the foot and weakly plantarflex the ankle","Flex the toes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsiflex the foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Evert the foot and weakly plantarflex the ankle', 'Both peroneal muscles, passing posterior to the lateral malleolus, evert the foot and weakly plantarflex the ankle. They neither invert the foot nor move the toes.'),
('5811afca-592f-5719-942c-dc94f3aba8d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibialis posterior originates from:', '["Calcaneus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cuboid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior tibia interosseous membrane and upper fibula","Anterior tibia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior tibia interosseous membrane and upper fibula', 'Tibialis posterior arises in the deep posterior compartment from the posterior tibia, the interosseous membrane, and the upper part of the fibula. The calcaneus, cuboid, and navicular are bones of the foot, not origin sites.'),
('b6dccd2d-0f52-5224-ab65-b1aeb5f76ca2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibialis posterior inserts into:', '["1st metatarsal resulting in decreased functional activity","Talus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cuboid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular tuberosity (and many other tarsals and metatarsals)"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular tuberosity (and many other tarsals and metatarsals)', 'The tibialis posterior tendon sweeps behind the medial malleolus and inserts mainly into the navicular tuberosity, with slips to several other tarsals and metatarsals. Its main attachment is not the talus, calcaneus, or cuboid.'),
('07c631fa-108f-53f4-bb02-35bfbcb5d91a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tibialis posterior acts to:', '["Plantarflex only resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsiflex the foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Invert the foot and stabilize the medial longitudinal arch","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Evert the foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Invert the foot and stabilize the medial longitudinal arch', 'Tibialis posterior is the main invertor of the foot and, through its broad tarsal insertions, supports the medial longitudinal arch. It does not dorsiflex or evert the foot.'),
('2ebedf8f-3155-5095-9f0d-2b7f5e90c932'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor hallucis longus originates from:', '["Posterior tibia resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior fibula (lower 2/3) and interosseous membrane","Navicular mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Calcaneus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior fibula (lower 2/3) and interosseous membrane', 'Flexor hallucis longus arises from the lower two-thirds of the posterior fibula and the adjacent interosseous membrane. It does not arise from the tibia, calcaneus, or navicular.'),
('5a8826f6-2b59-5562-9be3-fa2ae4fd48b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor hallucis longus inserts into:', '["Base of the distal phalanx of the hallux","Base of the proximal phalanx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial cuneiform mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the distal phalanx of the hallux', 'Flexor hallucis longus runs through the sole of the foot to insert into the base of the distal phalanx of the hallux. The medial cuneiform and navicular are not insertion sites for this tendon.'),
('37f1a687-5306-5b81-a864-8def90fd5273'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor hallucis longus acts to:', '["Flex the great toe and weakly plantarflex the ankle","Evert the foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extend the great toe mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Invert the foot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex the great toe and weakly plantarflex the ankle', 'Flexor hallucis longus flexes the great toe at both joints and weakly plantarflexes the ankle. It does not extend the great toe or evert the foot.'),
('93745ae7-352c-5205-8053-64caaba1fe7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor digitorum longus originates from:', '["Calcaneus resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior tibia (middle third)","Navicular mediated by specific receptor activation","Cuboid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior fibula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior tibia (middle third)', 'Flexor digitorum longus arises from the middle third of the posterior surface of the tibia in the deep posterior compartment. The posterior fibula gives origin to flexor hallucis longus, not to this muscle.'),
('cc729b1c-dcb4-5ac5-b006-f10111386dff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor digitorum longus inserts into:', '["Navicular resulting in decreased functional activity","Bases of the distal phalanges of the lateral 4 toes","Calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Bases of the proximal phalanges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cuboid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bases of the distal phalanges of the lateral 4 toes', 'Flexor digitorum longus divides into four tendons that insert into the bases of the distal phalanges of the lateral four toes. The navicular and calcaneus do not receive its insertions.'),
('fe70f29e-c125-51ea-b24a-986285cb3dfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor digitorum longus acts to:', '["Flex the lateral 4 toes and assist in plantarflexion","Evert the foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extend the lateral 4 toes mediated by specific receptor activation","Invert the foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flex the great toe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex the lateral 4 toes and assist in plantarflexion', 'Flexor digitorum longus flexes the lateral four toes and assists in plantarflexion of the ankle. It does not act on the great toe, which is served by flexor hallucis longus, nor does it evert the foot.'),
('520c3377-a64c-5360-bba0-0afc55c6115e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The quadratus plantae (flexor accessorius) is:', '["An intrinsic muscle of dorsum resulting in decreased functional activity","A muscle of the sole that assists FDL by adjusting the line of pull","An extrinsic muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A muscle of the sole that assists FDL by adjusting the line of pull', 'Quadratus plantae is an intrinsic muscle of the sole that arises from the calcaneus and joins the flexor digitorum longus tendon, straightening its oblique pull so the toes flex sagittally. It is neither a dorsum muscle nor an extrinsic leg muscle.'),
('3330a883-02ab-5cef-89c3-8b8c6cdd4f32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lumbrical muscles of the foot are how many?', '["5","2","1","4","3"]'::jsonb, '4', 'Four lumbrical muscles arise from the tendons of flexor digitorum longus in the sole, the four arising from the second to fifth toes. They flex the metatarsophalangeal joints and extend the interphalangeal joints.'),
('fdd66fc6-1216-5a6c-b5ca-37af36018a66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor digitorum brevis is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A muscle of the sole that flexes the lateral 4 toes at the PIP joints","Extends the toes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flexes the great toe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A muscle of the sole that flexes the lateral 4 toes at the PIP joints', 'Flexor digitorum brevis is a superficial intrinsic muscle of the sole that flexes the lateral four toes at the proximal interphalangeal joints. It does not extend the toes or act on the great toe.'),
('a43bf8bd-b9a9-530e-b477-0273fd893697'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The abductor hallucis muscle is located:', '["On the dorsum resulting in decreased functional activity","In the central compartment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the medial side of the sole (abducts the great toe)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the lateral side leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the medial side of the sole (abducts the great toe)', 'Abductor hallucis lies along the medial border of the foot in the first layer of the sole and abducts the great toe at the metatarsophalangeal joint. It is not on the dorsum or lateral side of the foot.'),
('a92490bd-8d26-5eb4-a190-d1eb9e2511d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The abductor digiti minimi muscle is located:', '["On the medial side resulting in decreased functional activity","On the lateral side of the sole (abducts the little toe)","In the central compartment mediated by specific receptor activation","On the dorsum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral side of the sole (abducts the little toe)', 'Abductor digiti minimi runs along the lateral border of the foot in the first layer of the sole and abducts the little toe. Its medial counterpart is abductor hallucis on the medial side of the sole.'),
('25ac6428-3a62-58e4-a436-488123544f67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor hallucis brevis muscle:', '["Extends the great toe","Flexes the great toe at the MCP joint","Abducts the great toe","None","Adducts the great toe"]'::jsonb, 'Flexes the great toe at the MCP joint', 'Flexor hallucis brevis arises from the cuboid and the related tarsals and flexes the great toe at the metatarsophalangeal joint. It does not extend or adduct the great toe.'),
('d181e4dc-0124-5566-a954-c50dbc04f7f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The flexor digiti minimi brevis:', '["None","Flexes the little toe at the MCP joint","Adducts the little toe","Abducts the little toe","Extends the little toe"]'::jsonb, 'Flexes the little toe at the MCP joint', 'Flexor digiti minimi brevis lies deep to abductor digiti minimi in the sole and flexes the little toe at the metatarsophalangeal joint. Abduction of the little toe is performed by abductor digiti minimi.'),
('bb0b5514-1ccc-5222-b592-d6f01ec9bb31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The adductor hallucis muscle has two heads:', '["None","Superficial and deep","Proximal and distal","Oblique and transverse","Medial and lateral"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique and transverse', 'Adductor hallucis has an oblique head arising from the bases of the lateral metatarsals and a transverse head arising from the plantar metatarsophalangeal ligaments. Both heads insert on the lateral side of the base of the proximal phalanx of the great toe.'),
('cbf4a67f-0b2d-59b2-88ab-6046449eb483'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The dorsal interossei of the foot are how many?', '["4","2","7","3","1"]'::jsonb, '4', 'Four dorsal interossei occupy the intermetatarsal spaces of the foot and abduct the toes relative to the axial line of the second toe. The hand also has four dorsal interossei.'),
('54231a7b-8a24-56b6-9bfc-034fe3e4be15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The plantar interossei of the foot are how many?', '["3","2","1","4","7"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Three plantar interossei arise from the bases of the third to fifth metatarsals and adduct those toes toward the axial line of the second toe. The larger number of dorsal versus plantar interossei matches the muscle action pattern.'),
('31fd43b5-f010-53c1-bb23-5d8b89ef52f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The dorsal interossei abduct the toes relative to the:', '["Second toe (axial line of the foot)","Great toe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Third toe mediated by specific receptor activation","Little toe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle toe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Second toe (axial line of the foot)', 'The axial line of the foot passes through the second toe, and the dorsal interossei abduct the toes away from this line. The great toe is adducted by adductor hallucis, not by these muscles.'),
('e5a75c40-8538-558b-b2aa-47c9f154c705'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The intrinsic muscles of the sole are arranged in how many layers?', '["5","1","3","2","4"]'::jsonb, '4', 'The intrinsic muscles of the sole are arranged in four layers, from the superficial abductors and flexor digitorum brevis up to the interossei in the deepest layer. This layered arrangement organizes the compartment content of the foot.'),
('4995c96e-1295-5a25-91b7-b11fa2810018'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The first layer of the sole includes:', '["Flexor hallucis brevis adductor hallucis flexor digiti minimi brevis","Quadratus plantae and lumbricals","Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis abductor digiti minimi","None","Plantar and dorsal interossei"]'::jsonb, 'Abductor hallucis flexor digitorum brevis abductor digiti minimi', 'The first (most superficial) layer of the sole contains abductor hallucis, flexor digitorum brevis, and abductor digiti minimi. Quadratus plantae with lumbricals occupies the second layer, and the interossei form the fourth layer.'),
('6ca23a11-a13b-5759-9c0d-dd99bab5145e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The longitudinal arches of the foot are maintained by:', '["Only ligaments resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Only bones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bones ligaments (spring plantar long) and muscles (TP FHL)"]'::jsonb, 'Bones ligaments (spring plantar long) and muscles (TP FHL)', 'The longitudinal arches are maintained by bony architecture, the plantar ligaments (spring and long plantar), and muscular support, chiefly tibialis posterior and the long flexors of the toes. Ligaments or muscles alone cannot sustain the arch, and skin contributes no support.'),
('4a3aa9ba-840d-5eb8-9870-41c36c185945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Flat foot (pes planus) results from:', '["Equinovarus deformity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cavus foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","High arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of the medial longitudinal arch (due to ligament laxity or TP weakness)"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of the medial longitudinal arch (due to ligament laxity or TP weakness)', 'Pes planus reflects collapse of the medial longitudinal arch, commonly from laxity of the spring and long plantar ligaments or tibialis posterior insufficiency. The other choices describe unrelated deformities such as cavus or equinovarus rather than arch collapse.'),
('aef74d0f-c195-5bed-b238-e0efc5654531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Pes cavus is:', '["An excessively high medial longitudinal arch","Club foot accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Flat foot without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heel valgus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An excessively high medial longitudinal arch', 'Pes cavus is an abnormally high medial longitudinal arch, often associated with neuromuscular conditions. Flat foot, club foot, and heel valgus are deformities of the opposite or unrelated types.'),
('a9046dae-81f0-5343-970b-cb2b1324b64a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The spring ligament (plantar calcaneonavicular) is important for:', '["Stabilizing the ankle joint resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Supporting the lateral arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supporting the head of the talus and maintaining the medial arch"]'::jsonb, 'Supporting the head of the talus and maintaining the medial arch', 'The plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament forms a sling beneath the head of the talus and is a key support of the medial longitudinal arch. Its failure contributes to flatfoot rather than to ankle stability or lateral arch support.'),
('5ce2c07f-9aaa-58a8-8287-cce0d33e3969'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The short plantar ligament (plantar calcaneocuboid) connects:', '["Calcaneus to cuboid (deep to long plantar)","Calcaneus to navicular","Talus to navicular","Navicular to cuneiform","Calcaneus to talus"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus to cuboid (deep to long plantar)', 'The short plantar (plantar calcaneocuboid) ligament runs from the anterior calcaneus to the cuboid, lying deep to the long plantar ligament. It supports the lateral arch; the other options describe different bones or ligaments.'),
('f4cb9243-c40d-5ecb-af39-2306ae64de72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The bifurcate ligament (Chopart''s ligament) connects:', '["None","Calcaneus to cuboid and navicular","Talus to navicular","Navicular to cuneiform","Calcaneus to talus"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus to cuboid and navicular', 'The bifurcate ligament arises from the anterior calcaneus and divides into calcaneocuboid and calcaneonavicular bands spanning the Chopart joint line. It therefore attaches to both the cuboid and the navicular.'),
('5ed39ee2-f731-56a3-b9aa-df2977774051'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The deltoid ligament of the ankle has how many parts?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 (tibionavicular tibiocalcaneal posterior tibiotalar anterior tibiotalar)","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 (tibionavicular tibiocalcaneal posterior tibiotalar anterior tibiotalar)', 'The deltoid ligament is a strong triangular medial ankle ligament formed of four bands: tibionavicular, tibiocalcaneal, and anterior and posterior tibiotalar. It resists eversion and lateral displacement of the talus.'),
('74628419-1989-5dad-a7eb-57fc28f53dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lateral ligament of the ankle has how many separate bands?', '["3 (anterior talofibular calcaneofibular posterior talofibular)","5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 mediated by specific receptor activation","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (anterior talofibular calcaneofibular posterior talofibular)', 'The lateral collateral ligament of the ankle has three bands: the anterior and posterior talofibular and the calcaneofibular. The anterior talofibular band is the weakest and most often injured.'),
('9f8b5af0-ad08-58c6-bb15-95a3ba2f74f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The anterior talofibular ligament prevents:', '["Plantarflexion resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsiflexion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior displacement of the talus and excessive inversion","Posterior displacement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior displacement of the talus and excessive inversion', 'The anterior talofibular ligament checks anterior translation of the talus and limits inversion, particularly when the ankle is plantarflexed. It is the band most frequently torn in inversion sprains.'),
('a43eeeb2-9f8e-5fe4-934f-a5e9f4e3c2bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The calcaneofibular ligament prevents:', '["Anterior translation resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior translation mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsiflexion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excessive inversion of the ankle and subtalar joint"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive inversion of the ankle and subtalar joint', 'The calcaneofibular ligament crosses both the ankle and subtalar joints and restricts excessive inversion. It tears when inversion stress continues after injury to the anterior talofibular ligament.'),
('2f160b6d-d423-5bbe-9a14-8f6f8430fece'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The posterior talofibular ligament prevents:', '["None","Posterior displacement of the talus","Anterior displacement","Plantarflexion","Excessive inversion"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior displacement of the talus', 'The posterior talofibular ligament is the strongest lateral ankle band and prevents posterior displacement of the talus. It is rarely injured in isolation and usually only with major force.'),
('5ca6771b-86f7-5a72-b34a-bf22e97c1a0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The most common ankle sprain mechanism is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Eversion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Plantarflexion mediated by specific receptor activation","Inversion (95% of ankle sprains)","Dorsiflexion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inversion (95% of ankle sprains)', 'The overwhelming majority of ankle sprains result from forced inversion, which injures the lateral ligament complex, especially the anterior talofibular ligament. Eversion and other mechanisms are far less frequent.'),
('a3954513-f914-53f8-9913-ee817fa6544b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The Ottawa ankle rules are used to:', '["Determine if surgery is needed resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diagnose fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Determine if X-rays are needed after ankle injury"]'::jsonb, 'Determine if X-rays are needed after ankle injury', 'The Ottawa ankle rules guide the need for radiographs after ankle and foot trauma using bone tenderness and weight-bearing capacity as criteria. They reduce unnecessary imaging while not missing significant fractures.'),
('7e7fc5d7-adc4-5641-ba1b-d5a801a8b420'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'An eversion ankle sprain is less common and often injures:', '["The deltoid ligament (medial side)","The lateral ligaments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ATFL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The calcaneofibular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The deltoid ligament (medial side)', 'Forced eversion stresses the medial side of the ankle and injures the deltoid ligament. Because the deltoid is strong, the lateral malleolus or syndesmosis often gives way first, making pure deltoid sprains uncommon.'),
('29dfc52f-cf33-5a6d-a2bc-65c79c58ae65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The syndesmosis of the ankle is the:', '["Distal tibiofibular joint (held by anterior and posterior tibiofibular ligaments)","The midtarsal joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ankle mortise mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The subtalar joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal tibiofibular joint (held by anterior and posterior tibiofibular ligaments)', 'The tibiofibular syndesmosis is the distal joint between the tibia and fibula, stabilized by the anterior and posterior inferior tibiofibular ligaments and the interosseous membrane. Injury to it produces a high ankle sprain, distinct from the subtalar or midtarsal joints.'),
('a01bdcb4-e0b0-55bd-95ce-7fb97f688a96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The high ankle sprain involves:', '["The calcaneofibular ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The syndesmosis (tibiofibular ligaments)","The deltoid ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The PTFL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ATFL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The syndesmosis (tibiofibular ligaments)', 'A high ankle sprain injures the tibiofibular syndesmosis, typically from forced dorsiflexion with external rotation. It is distinct from lateral ligament sprains and generally recovers more slowly.'),
('a733ecb1-2ea7-553c-b68a-3c9c2c6363b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The talus articulates with:', '["Calcaneus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Tibia and fibula only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Navicular only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibia fibula calcaneus and navicular"]'::jsonb, 'Tibia fibula calcaneus and navicular', 'The talus articulates superiorly with the tibia and fibula within the ankle mortise, inferiorly with the calcaneus at the subtalar joint, and anteriorly with the navicular at the talonavicular joint. It has no muscular or tendinous attachments.'),
('a3805900-9f47-5ca2-b582-521fd8da3a5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The head of the talus articulates with:', '["Cuneiforms resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcaneus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Navicular bone (talonavicular joint)","Cuboid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular bone (talonavicular joint)', 'The head of the talus fits into the concavity of the navicular, forming the talonavicular component of the Chopart joint. The calcaneus contacts the talar body and neck, and the cuneiforms and cuboid receive other tarsals.'),
('04ff7373-68fe-583b-84dc-09cdb188690a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The talar neck is the most common site of:', '["Avascular necrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Arthritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osteochondritis dissecans mediated by specific receptor activation","Talar fracture (often from forced dorsiflexion)","Ankle sprain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Talar fracture (often from forced dorsiflexion)', 'The talar neck is the most frequent fracture site of the talus, usually produced by forced dorsiflexion as in a motor vehicle collision. Because the talar blood supply enters from the distal side, neck fractures carry a high risk of avascular necrosis of the body.'),
('1db9b329-fc59-5e47-9308-624c7a867048'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The talus has a tenuous blood supply. The artery of the tarsal canal supplies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The talar head mediated by specific receptor activation","The talar neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The talar body (important for AVN risk)"]'::jsonb, 'The talar body (important for AVN risk)', 'The artery of the tarsal canal, a branch of the posterior tibial artery, supplies most of the talar body through retrograde flow. When this supply is interrupted, as in displaced talar neck fractures, avascular necrosis of the body frequently results.'),
('97eb5225-52cc-5bdb-8025-a245beab5d1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The calcaneus is the largest tarsal bone. Its posterior part gives attachment to:', '["Achilles tendon (posterosuperior calcaneus)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FHL mediated by specific receptor activation","FDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tibialis posterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Achilles tendon (posterosuperior calcaneus)', 'The Achilles (calcaneal) tendon inserts on the posterosuperior aspect of the calcaneal tuberosity, a site susceptible to enthesopathy and avulsion fractures. The FHL, FDL, and tibialis posterior insert elsewhere in the foot.'),
('dac71d2b-b142-50f7-a7b4-6f00fc054fe2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The sustentaculum tali of the calcaneus:', '["Is a groove for FHL","Supports the talus (medial projection)","Is a groove for peroneals","Is for the insertion of the Achilles","None"]'::jsonb, 'Supports the talus (medial projection)', 'The sustentaculum tali is a medial shelf of the calcaneus that supports the talus and bears a groove beneath it for the flexor hallucis longus tendon. Its fracture can entrap the FHL or the medial neurovascular structures.'),
('33c8abbb-ccd5-5ee8-b4d7-45a9283e8dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The peroneal tubercle of the calcaneus:', '["Is the origin of FDB resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is the insertion of the Achilles mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Separates the peroneus longus and brevis tendons"]'::jsonb, 'Separates the peroneus longus and brevis tendons', 'The peroneal tubercle on the lateral calcaneus lies between the peroneus brevis tendon above and the peroneus longus tendon below. Its enlargement can contribute to peroneal tendinopathy or dislocation.'),
('d5f40a76-8015-5fef-9476-28de5a6e25bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The navicular bone articulates with:', '["Calcaneus and talus","Talus and cuboid","Calcaneus and cuboid","Cuneiforms and cuboid","Talus and cuneiforms (and sometimes cuboid)"]'::jsonb, 'Talus and cuneiforms (and sometimes cuboid)', 'The navicular articulates proximally with the head of the talus, distally with the three cuneiforms, and has a variable lateral facet for the cuboid. It forms the keystone of the medial longitudinal arch.'),
('fedd352a-3d8a-58af-8280-846141e480d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The navicular tuberosity is:', '["A prominent medial projection (insertion of TP)","A lateral projection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The Achilles insertion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The FHL groove leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A prominent medial projection (insertion of TP)', 'The navicular tuberosity is a prominent medial projection that receives the main insertion of the tibialis posterior. It serves as a surface landmark and is the site where an accessory navicular (os tibiale externum) develops.'),
('f0aa6407-37aa-5671-ac69-ebf39d024da2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Accessory navicular (os tibiale externum) is:', '["An accessory ossicle near the navicular tuberosity (can cause painful flatfoot)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A congenital absence mediated by specific receptor activation","A fractured navicular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An arthritic change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An accessory ossicle near the navicular tuberosity (can cause painful flatfoot)', 'The accessory navicular (os tibiale externum) is a common congenital ossicle near the navicular tuberosity, often seated within the tibialis posterior tendon. It may cause medial foot pain and be associated with symptomatic flatfoot.'),
('11bf83cb-a41d-528e-9c1d-c69f9bb69870'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The cuboid bone articulates with:', '["Only calcaneus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only metatarsals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All tarsals mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcaneus lateral cuneiform navicular and metatarsals 4-5","Only navicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneus lateral cuneiform navicular and metatarsals 4-5', 'The cuboid articulates posteriorly with the calcaneus, medially with the lateral cuneiform and often the navicular, and distally with the bases of the fourth and fifth metatarsals. Its plantar groove transmits the peroneus longus tendon.'),
('44077c69-e373-5c78-8bfc-8635f0a5b10b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The groove on the cuboid is for:', '["FHL tendon","Tibialis posterior tendon","Achilles tendon","FDL tendon","Peroneus longus tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Peroneus longus tendon', 'The oblique groove on the plantar surface of the cuboid transmits the peroneus longus tendon, which then runs to the base of the first metatarsal and medial cuneiform. The FHL and FDL pass in grooves around the medial malleolus and sustentaculum.'),
('a500fd35-1de4-5852-8bbc-1d1b3273b445'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The cuneiform bones (medial intermediate lateral) articulate with:', '["Cuboid proximally and metatarsals 4-5 distally resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular proximally and metatarsals 1-3 distally","Calcaneus mediated by specific receptor activation","Talus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Navicular proximally and metatarsals 1-3 distally', 'The three cuneiforms articulate proximally with the navicular and distally with the bases of the first, second, and third metatarsals. Together with the navicular they form the medial column of the midfoot.'),
('1ba10dea-e676-517a-8b51-b94c5f67e2a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The Lisfranc joint (tarsometatarsal joint) is important because:', '["It gets injured resulting in decreased functional activity","It is the subtalar joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is the ankle joint mediated by specific receptor activation","It can be injured in midfoot sprains and fractures (often missed)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It can be injured in midfoot sprains and fractures (often missed)', 'The Lisfranc (tarsometatarsal) joint complex is essential for midfoot stability, but its injuries are frequently missed on plain radiographs. Untreated disruption can cause chronic midfoot pain, deformity, and post-traumatic arthritis.'),
('5d4f2665-f3f7-5d59-8355-a231b4567f72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The Lisfranc ligament connects:', '["Cuboid to 5th metatarsal resulting in decreased functional activity","Navicular to cuneiform accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial cuneiform to base of 2nd metatarsol (key stabilizer)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial cuneiform to base of 2nd metatarsol (key stabilizer)', 'The Lisfranc ligament is the strongest midfoot ligament, running from the medial cuneiform to the base of the second metatarsal and thus anchoring the keystone second ray. Its rupture with displacement produces a Lisfranc fracture-dislocation.'),
('2597a9cf-1d6e-533c-a862-ea9ba9a3cd12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The first metatarsal is:', '["None","The most mobile","The longest metatarsal","The shortest metatarsal","The thickest metatarsal"]'::jsonb, 'The shortest metatarsal', 'The first metatarsal is the shortest and thickest of the metatarsals, bearing the largest share of weight in stance. The second metatarsal is the longest.'),
('82f6593d-f75b-559a-bd59-4e370bb0ea6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The second metatarsal is:', '["None","The longest metatarsal","The most common site of stress fracture","The shortest","The most mobile"]'::jsonb, 'The longest metatarsal', 'The second metatarsal is the longest metatarsal, with its base firmly anchored by the Lisfranc ligament, and it is the most common site of metatarsal stress fracture. It is relatively immobile within the tarsometatarsal joint.'),
('5bd0fffa-3f09-5ab5-95db-a06113a5fcf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The base of the 5th metatarsal has a prominent:', '["Insertion of TP resulting in decreased functional activity","Groove for peroneus longus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Styloid process (insertion of peroneus brevis)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Styloid process (insertion of peroneus brevis)', 'The base of the fifth metatarsal bears a prominent styloid (tuberosity) that receives the peroneus brevis tendon, the site of avulsion fractures. The peroneus longus instead grooves the cuboid and inserts on the first metatarsal.'),
('f25d2fb9-1301-55ad-8780-5114352d9912'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'A Jones fracture is a:', '["Avulsion of the styloid resulting in decreased functional activity","Fracture of the distal 5th metatarsal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fracture of the 1st metatarsal mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fracture of the proximal 5th metatarsal (at the metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction)"]'::jsonb, 'Fracture of the proximal 5th metatarsal (at the metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction)', 'A Jones fracture is a transverse fracture at the metaphyseal-diaphyseal junction of the proximal fifth metatarsal, a zone of poor vascularity prone to nonunion. It must be distinguished from an avulsion fracture of the styloid, which heals readily.'),
('3f41d597-8cd0-5139-b55e-962674372b0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'An avulsion fracture of the 5th metatarsal styloid is caused by:', '["The pull of peroneus longus","The pull of TP","The pull of FDL","The pull of peroneus brevis tendon","None"]'::jsonb, 'The pull of peroneus brevis tendon', 'Avulsion of the fifth metatarsal styloid results from the pull of the peroneus brevis tendon during an inversion injury. Unlike a Jones fracture, this avulsion lies at the tuberosity and typically heals well.'),
('dab1189d-71ef-5dba-a75f-736c019bc704'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The great toe (hallux) has how many phalanges?', '["4 resulting in decreased functional activity","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 (proximal and distal)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (proximal and distal)', 'The great toe has only two phalanges, proximal and distal, united by a single interphalangeal joint. The lesser toes have three phalanges each.'),
('d36525f6-6147-5e83-9437-079fecaf0fc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Hallux valgus is:', '["Extension deformity resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral deviation of the great toe at the MCP joint (with bunion formation)","Flexion deformity mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial deviation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral deviation of the great toe at the MCP joint (with bunion formation)', 'Hallux valgus is lateral deviation of the great toe at the first metatarsophalangeal joint, frequently with a painful medially prominent bursa (bunion). Medial deviation, extension, or flexion deformities describe other conditions.'),
('f885c2e4-6525-5f62-8603-32cd58cffd0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Hallux rigidus is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal bunion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arthritis of the 1st MTP joint causing stiffness and pain","Flexion deformity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arthritis of the 1st MTP joint causing stiffness and pain', 'Hallux rigidus is degenerative arthritis of the first metatarsophalangeal joint that limits dorsiflexion and causes pain, hence the ''stiff great toe.'' Unlike hallux valgus it is primarily a motion-restricting arthritic condition.'),
('c1b70345-42ba-5da7-af3e-c501db5efdd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The phalanges of the lesser toes (2-5) have how many bones each?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 (proximal middle distal)"]'::jsonb, '3 (proximal middle distal)', 'Each lesser toe (2-5) has three phalanges, proximal, middle, and distal, forming two interphalangeal joints. The great toe is the exception, having only two phalanges.'),
('2ca1f46a-95a5-5cef-a642-6b77502c7dd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The articular surfaces of the foot joints follow a pattern. The ankle is a:', '["Plane","Hinge joint","Pivot","Saddle","Ellipsoid"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge joint', 'The ankle (talocrural) joint is a synovial hinge joint that permits dorsiflexion and plantarflexion around a transverse axis. The distal leg joints of the foot show the plane, ellipsoid, and hinge pattern described in the options.'),
('7f186f25-2640-54bc-9fc3-f4a721391142'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The subtalar joint is a:', '["Saddle joint resulting in decreased functional activity","Ball and socket accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hinge joint mediated by specific receptor activation","Pivot joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plane/gliding joint (talocalcaneal)"]'::jsonb, 'Plane/gliding joint (talocalcaneal)', 'The subtalar joint between the talus and calcaneus is a plane (gliding) synovial joint combining anterior and posterior talocalcaneal facets, and it allows inversion and eversion. The other types listed do not describe this articulation.'),
('ddd5b2f4-fae7-5f00-8859-d2f68412a2a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The transverse tarsal (Chopart''s) joint includes:', '["Talocalcaneal and calcaneocuboid","Talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints","Talonavicular and subtalar","None","All"]'::jsonb, 'Talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints', 'The transverse tarsal (Chopart''s) joint is formed by the talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints, which together lie across the midfoot. The subtalar joint is separate and lies posterior to this line.'),
('bff442da-f31e-5766-b477-203f944e70ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tarsometatarsal (Lisfranc) joint is a:', '["Plane synovial joint","Pivot","Hinge","Ball and socket","None"]'::jsonb, 'Plane synovial joint', 'The Lisfranc (tarsometatarsal) joints are plane synovial joints with limited gliding between the cuneiforms and cuboid and the metatarsal bases. Their strong ligamentous support provides the stability that is often lost in Lisfranc injuries.'),
('d248e2db-7ecc-5c7d-aaf5-8e6772b11bdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joints are:', '["Saddle resulting in decreased functional activity","Ball and socket accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ellipsoid (condyloid) joints","Hinge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pivot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ellipsoid (condyloid) joints', 'The metatarsophalangeal joints are ellipsoid (condyloid) synovial joints permitting flexion, extension, and some abduction and adduction. Their collateral ligaments and plantar plates lend stability during weight bearing.'),
('6a853dfa-e0d8-5e5f-b0f9-9b5e768a4fea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The interphalangeal (IP) joints of the foot are:', '["Saddle resulting in decreased functional activity","Plane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pivot mediated by specific receptor activation","Ellipsoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hinge joints (ginglymus)"]'::jsonb, 'Hinge joints (ginglymus)', 'The interphalangeal joints of the toes are hinge (ginglymus) synovial joints allowing only flexion and extension, the same pattern seen at the interphalangeal joints of the hand. Saddle, plane, and pivot types are not involved.'),
('aa19013d-e4fa-556e-8d06-b3b5ff77a845'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament supports the:', '["Cuboid","Navicular","Calcaneus","None","Head of the talus"]'::jsonb, 'Head of the talus', 'The plantar calcaneonavicular (spring) ligament supports the head of the talus, forming the floor of the talonavicular joint and acting as a sling for the medial longitudinal arch. Weakening of this ligament leads to pes planus.'),
('39935203-110f-5364-bae0-64e746b2888a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The cervical ligament (in the sinus tarsi) is a key stabilizer of:', '["The midtarsal joint","The subtalar joint","The Lisfranc joint","None","The ankle joint"]'::jsonb, 'The subtalar joint', 'The cervical ligament, lying within the sinus tarsi, runs from the calcaneus to the neck of the talus and is a principal stabilizer of the subtalar joint. Injury contributes to subtalar instability and a painful sinus tarsi syndrome.'),
('fab4780e-4065-56ce-a2f0-f19b8ebb8724'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The deltoid ligament is tested with:', '["The Lachman test","The inversion stress test","The anterior drawer test","The eversion stress test (valgus)","None"]'::jsonb, 'The eversion stress test (valgus)', 'The deltoid ligament is evaluated with an eversion (valgus) stress test of the ankle, which loads the medial structures. An inversion stress test instead targets the lateral ligament complex.'),
('ad7bfc1b-8bd5-5591-85b7-f18967857ada'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The anterior talofibular ligament is tested with:', '["The eversion test resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior drawer test of the ankle","The Thompson test mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The Lachman test leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior drawer test of the ankle', 'The anterior drawer test translates the talus forward against a fixed tibia and detects insufficiency of the anterior talofibular ligament. The Thompson test examines the Achilles tendon, and eversion stress tests the deltoid ligament.'),
('dda04631-4ff3-5ea0-b613-b8a24c8b75ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The talar tilt test assesses:', '["ATFL integrity","PTFL integrity","Deltoid integrity","Calcaneofibular ligament integrity","Syndesmotic integrity"]'::jsonb, 'Calcaneofibular ligament integrity', 'The talar tilt test applies inversion stress to the hindfoot, and a positive result indicates injury to the calcaneofibular ligament, the structure tested by this maneuver. The anterior drawer test targets the ATFL, while the deltoid ligament (medial), PTFL, and distal tibiofibular syndesmosis are not stressed by talar tilt.'),
('48987d20-566b-5acc-8212-9999b17182bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The syndesmosis is assessed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The Lachman test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The squeeze test (compression of the midfibula) and the external rotation test","The anterior drawer test without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The Thompson test leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The squeeze test (compression of the midfibula) and the external rotation test', 'The tibiofibular syndesmosis is demonstrated by compression of the proximal fibula (squeeze test) and external rotation of the foot, both of which widen the distal tibiofibular interval. The Lachman and anterior drawer tests assess the ACL, and the Thompson test evaluates the Achilles tendon.'),
('8f39ad51-2f26-52a0-b069-4c35f217741c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The midfoot contains how many bones?', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 (navicular cuboid 3 cuneiforms)","7 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5 (navicular cuboid 3 cuneiforms)', 'The midfoot is formed by five bones: the navicular, cuboid, and the three cuneiforms. The talus and calcaneus make up the hindfoot, while the metatarsals and phalanges form the forefoot, so the other counts are incorrect.'),
('3ef05fac-694a-511a-a773-f7555d261315'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The tarsal bones ossify postnatally. The calcaneus ossifies first at:', '["5 years resulting in decreased functional activity","2 years accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At birth mediated by specific receptor activation","1 year without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3-6 months IUL (prenatal)"]'::jsonb, '3-6 months IUL (prenatal)', 'The calcaneus is the first tarsal bone to ossify, with its center appearing around 3-6 months in utero. The other tarsal bones, including the cuboid and cuneiforms, ossify later in the postnatal period.'),
('e7a11122-401e-561b-be6d-8a051d9b9c26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The cuboid ossifies at:', '["About 6 months after birth","2 years accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Birth mediated by specific receptor activation","3 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 year leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 6 months after birth', 'The cuboid is the second tarsal bone to ossify, with its center appearing at approximately 6 months after birth. The cuneiforms and other tarsal centers appear later, so the older ages and birth are incorrect.'),
('28a551e9-32f7-58f9-9e43-255426286e1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The lateral cuneiform ossifies at:', '["2 years","3 years","1 year","6 months","5 years"]'::jsonb, '1 year', 'The lateral cuneiform typically ossifies at about 1 year of age. The medial and intermediate cuneiforms appear slightly later, so the earlier and later ages listed are inaccurate.'),
('120aabd1-ee16-519b-b7c5-f899885eee80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The medial cuneiform ossifies at:', '["4 years","6 months","1 year","3 years","2 years"]'::jsonb, '2 years', 'The medial cuneiform ossifies at about 2 years of age. The lateral cuneiform appears earlier (about 1 year) and the intermediate and other tarsal centers later, so the surrounding ages are incorrect.'),
('872a5708-3b3d-5300-9aca-8b4514cd53b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The intermediate cuneiform ossifies at:', '["3 years","5 years","4 years","2 years","1 year"]'::jsonb, '3 years', 'The intermediate cuneiform ossifies at about 3 years of age. The lateral and medial cuneiforms appear earlier, so the earlier ages given do not apply to this bone.'),
('abfd65e0-6e5d-5815-855e-634fa5f0e46f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The navicular is the last tarsal to ossify at:', '["2 years","6 months","5-6 years","1 year","3-4 years"]'::jsonb, '3-4 years', 'The navicular is the last tarsal bone to ossify, with its center appearing at about 3-4 years of age. The surrounding ages are too early or too late for the navicular, which consistently lags the other tarsal bones.'),
('accf216a-bf5e-5b7c-b453-cd86e10e2b2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The metatarsal bones develop from how many ossification centers?', '["Multiple resulting in decreased functional activity","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 (primary for shaft secondary for head)","3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (primary for shaft secondary for head)', 'Most metatarsals develop from two ossification centers: one primary center in the shaft and one secondary center in the head. The first metatarsal is an exception with a secondary center at the base, but the standard pattern is two centers, so the other counts are incorrect.'),
('057654b8-86f7-588a-8518-3be7645313dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The calcaneal apophysis (secondary center for the posterior calcaneus) appears at:', '["Birth","3 years","1 year","6-8 years","10 years"]'::jsonb, '6-8 years', 'The calcaneal apophysis, the secondary ossification center for the posterior calcaneus, appears at approximately 6-8 years of age. It fuses with the calcaneus around 14-15 years, so the earlier and later ages listed are incorrect.'),
('70897425-a658-5a32-b9cf-973dd6d561cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Sever''s disease is:', '["Plantar fasciitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Apophysitis of the calcaneus (traction injury at the Achilles insertion)","Stress fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","Achilles rupture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apophysitis of the calcaneus (traction injury at the Achilles insertion)', 'Sever''s disease is apophysitis of the calcaneus, a traction injury at the Achilles tendon insertion that occurs in active children. Plantar fasciitis, stress fracture, and Achilles rupture are different pathologies not caused by this traction apophysitis.'),
('b03b1fdc-384e-5e2d-b83a-2e282a7ab317'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The growth plate of the distal tibia fuses at:', '["10-12 years resulting in decreased functional activity","Approximately 15-17 years (puberty)","18-20 years mediated by specific receptor activation","20-22 years without affecting the primary tissue pathway","12-14 years leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 15-17 years (puberty)', 'The growth plate of the distal tibia fuses at approximately 15-17 years, during puberty. Earlier figures fail to account for the continued lower-leg growth, while 18-22 years is too late for the distal tibial physis.'),
('1243edfd-65c5-5ca2-8333-e0d9a37ac7de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Tillaux fracture is:', '["A calcaneal fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","An avulsion fracture of the anterolateral distal tibial epiphysis (in adolescents)","A talar neck fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fracture of the fibula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An avulsion fracture of the anterolateral distal tibial epiphysis (in adolescents)', 'A Tillaux fracture is a Salter-Harris type III avulsion of the anterolateral distal tibial epiphysis, caused by external rotation avulsing the epiphysis at the attachment of the anterior tibiofibular ligament in adolescents. Calcaneal, talar, and fibular fractures are pathogenetically distinct injuries.'),
('f61344b5-d1c7-58f2-87f0-e59ca07a9bfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Triplane fracture of the distal tibia is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A simple fracture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fibular fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","A talar fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A complex Salter-Harris IV fracture with coronal sagittal and transverse components"]'::jsonb, 'A complex Salter-Harris IV fracture with coronal sagittal and transverse components', 'A triplane fracture is a complex Salter-Harris injury of the distal tibia with coronal, sagittal, and transverse components, typically seen in adolescents. Simple, fibular, and talar fractures lack this multiaxial growth-plate pattern.'),
('20104fde-7d36-5c3f-97b7-e137da84a4e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'The foot has how many bones total (including the sesamoids)?', '["14 bones resulting in decreased functional activity","19 bones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","26 bones (7 tarsals 5 metatarsals 14 phalanges) plus 2 sesamoids","30 bones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","28 bones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '26 bones (7 tarsals 5 metatarsals 14 phalanges) plus 2 sesamoids', 'The foot contains 26 bones (7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals, 14 phalanges) plus 2 sesamoids at the first metatarsophalangeal joint. The other totals either omit the sesamoids or count too many bones.'),
('bc9761ff-9766-556a-9e93-f845e2897d87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which statement best fits the following fact: the thigh extends from which joints?', '["Hip to foot","Knee to foot","Hip to ankle","Knee to ankle","Hip to knee"]'::jsonb, 'Hip to knee', 'The thigh is the segment of the lower limb extending from the hip joint to the knee joint. Options extending the segment to the ankle or foot describe the leg rather than the thigh.'),
('e080e913-e77d-563c-a9db-f0bc2427b221'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Regarding Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, what is the correct answer to: the anterior compartment of the thigh contains which muscles?', '["Adductors resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadriceps femoris sartorius iliopsoas (part)","Hamstrings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps femoris sartorius iliopsoas (part)', 'The anterior compartment of the thigh contains the quadriceps femoris, sartorius, and the part of the iliopsoas that spans the thigh. The adductors lie medially and the hamstrings posteriorly, so those are compartmentally incorrect.'),
('459fab6a-6827-59b9-b39b-4a6e2f35d958'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'For Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which option is most appropriate: the medial compartment of the thigh contains which muscles?', '["Adductor longus brevis magnus gracilis pectineus","Hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteals mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadriceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor longus brevis magnus gracilis pectineus', 'The medial compartment of the thigh contains the adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, gracilis, and pectineus. Hamstrings and quadriceps occupy the posterior and anterior compartments respectively, and the gluteals act at the hip rather than in this compartment.'),
('96fbc741-7e0a-5960-8528-876bfac777ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Which answer is correct in Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg when asked: the posterior compartment of the thigh contains which muscles?', '["Iliopsoas resulting in decreased functional activity","Quadriceps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adductors mediated by specific receptor activation","Hamstrings (semitendinosus semimembranosus biceps femoris)","Gluteals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hamstrings (semitendinosus semimembranosus biceps femoris)', 'The posterior compartment of the thigh is occupied by the hamstrings: semitendinosus, semimembranosus, and biceps femoris. Quadriceps, adductors, and gluteals belong to other regions, so they are not the posterior thigh contents.'),
('92d8b114-912e-5310-ab11-03276da093a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, the best response to ''The quadriceps femoris is supplied by:'' is:', '["Superior gluteal nerve","Femoral nerve (L2-L4)","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2-L4)', 'The quadriceps femoris is supplied by the femoral nerve (L2-L4), which innervates the anterior thigh. The obturator, superior gluteal, tibial, and sciatic nerves supply different muscle groups.'),
('7092ff21-0945-50bf-82e1-8492a891026a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'During revision of Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which of the following is correct: the sartorius muscle is supplied by:?', '["Sciatic nerve","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve (L2 L3)","Superior gluteal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2 L3)', 'The sartorius muscle is supplied by the femoral nerve (L2, L3), in keeping with its anterior thigh location. The sciatic, tibial, obturator, and superior gluteal nerves innervate posterior, medial, and gluteal muscles instead.'),
('cfa3197a-1f61-59bb-98d8-0350667b995f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which statement best fits the following fact: the iliopsoas muscle is formed by:?', '["Psoas minor and iliacus","None","Psoas major and psoas minor","Iliacus and sartorius","Psoas major and iliacus"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major and iliacus', 'The iliopsoas is formed by the union of the psoas major and iliacus muscles. Psoas minor is a small separate muscle and sartorius is unrelated, so the other pairings are incorrect.'),
('611d4587-5307-54f6-b3c2-1bc8a8833809'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Regarding Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, what is the correct answer to: the psoas major originates from:?', '["Transverse processes and vertebral bodies of T12-L5","AIIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse processes and vertebral bodies of T12-L5', 'The psoas major originates from the transverse processes and vertebral bodies of T12-L5. The AIIS, iliac fossa, pubic bone, and ischial tuberosity are origins of other muscles.'),
('a579ebc0-308f-5446-bd8f-063748358ce2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'For Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which option is most appropriate: the iliacus originates from:?', '["ASIS","AIIS","T12-L5 vertebral bodies","Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)","Pubic bone"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)', 'The iliacus originates from the upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa. The ASIS, AIIS, pubic bone, and T12-L5 vertebral bodies are attachment sites of other muscles.'),
('e63730e1-5cb3-5a09-ab35-1a452254b5c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Which answer is correct in Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg when asked: the iliopsoas inserts into:?', '["Greater trochanter","Pectineal line","Gluteal tuberosity","Lesser trochanter of the femur","Linea aspera"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter of the femur', 'The iliopsoas inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur. The greater trochanter, pectineal line, gluteal tuberosity, and linea aspera receive insertions of different muscles.'),
('08b928a7-5b95-5286-99ce-4ea8abda41a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, the best response to ''The iliopsoas is supplied by:'' is:', '["Sciatic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Tibial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral nerve (L2 L3) and direct branches from lumbar plexus","Obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior gluteal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2 L3) and direct branches from lumbar plexus', 'The iliopsoas is supplied by the femoral nerve (L2, L3) along with direct branches from the lumbar plexus to the psoas. The sciatic, tibial, obturator, and superior gluteal nerves supply other lower-limb muscles.'),
('9a7f0d58-0435-5791-8cd0-286e0ff073a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'During revision of Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which of the following is correct: the iliopsoas acts to:?', '["Extend the thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","Flex the thigh at the hip and flex the trunk","Rotate the thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Abduct the thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adduct the thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex the thigh at the hip and flex the trunk', 'The iliopsoas flexes the thigh at the hip and flexes the trunk, acting as the chief hip flexor. Extension, abduction, adduction, and rotation of the thigh are not its primary actions.'),
('5c22f54e-6a0a-5b0f-bbeb-d1291b7928fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which statement best fits the following fact: the rectus femoris is part of quadriceps and also:?', '["Crosses the ankle","None","Crosses the hip joint (flexes hip)","Does not cross the hip","Crosses the knee only"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses the hip joint (flexes hip)', 'The rectus femoris is part of the quadriceps and is the only component that crosses the hip joint, so it also flexes the hip. The other vasti cross only the knee, making the other options inaccurate.'),
('321d0919-b79b-56ff-87ec-c75916a5792d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Regarding Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, what is the correct answer to: the vastus medialis is important for:?', '["Knee flexion resulting in decreased functional activity","Hip flexion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The last 15 degrees of knee extension (quadriceps lag)","Hip extension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ankle movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The last 15 degrees of knee extension (quadriceps lag)', 'The vastus medialis is important for the last 15 degrees of knee extension, so weakness produces a quadriceps lag in terminal extension. Flexion of the knee, hip, or ankle is not its role.'),
('2406fe4c-3332-5e93-b02c-29e577648c20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'For Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which option is most appropriate: the articularis genus muscle is:?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of the hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Part of the quadriceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small muscle deep to vastus intermedius that retracts the suprapatellar bursa"]'::jsonb, 'A small muscle deep to vastus intermedius that retracts the suprapatellar bursa', 'The articularis genus is a small muscle deep to the vastus intermedius that retracts the suprapatellar bursa during knee extension. It is not part of the hamstrings or the quadriceps itself.'),
('3170909b-fece-597c-8c50-3c8b9f20a443'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Which answer is correct in Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg when asked: the patellar reflex tests which nerve root?', '["S1 S2 resulting in decreased functional activity","C5 C6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L5 S1 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3 L4 (femoral nerve)","T12 L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L3 L4 (femoral nerve)', 'The patellar reflex is mediated by the L3-L4 nerve roots through the femoral nerve. S1-S2, L5-S1, C5-C6, and T12-L1 supply other reflexes and cord levels.'),
('08d52378-25bf-55f1-9e11-d32572b6885d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, the best response to ''The adductor canal (Hunter''s canal) extends from:'' is:', '["Pubic tubercle to medial condyle","Apex of femoral triangle to adductor hiatus","Femoral ring to popliteal fossa","None","Inguinal ligament to knee"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of femoral triangle to adductor hiatus', 'The adductor canal (Hunter''s canal) extends from the apex of the femoral triangle to the adductor hiatus. Its boundaries are sartorius, adductor longus, and vastus medialis, so the other endpoints are incorrect.'),
('19b72df1-0e07-5206-85ae-25d63efc89f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'During revision of Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which of the following is correct: the femoral artery in the adductor canal lies:?', '["Medial to the sartorius","Posteromedial to the femur","Lateral to the femur","None","Anterior to the femur"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial to the femur', 'Within the adductor canal the femoral artery lies medial to the sartorius and along the posteromedial aspect of the femur, coursing toward the adductor hiatus. Anterior, lateral, and unopposed medial descriptions misplace the vessel.'),
('6b4c2f59-c942-51db-aebb-58f2a9f981a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which statement best fits the following fact: the saphenous nerve is a branch of:?', '["Common peroneal nerve","Tibial nerve","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'The saphenous nerve is a branch of the femoral nerve, supplying skin down the medial leg and foot. The sciatic, tibial, common peroneal, and obturator nerves are separate branches.'),
('da869983-5d9e-50bd-87fd-08bf19c9aae4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Regarding Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, what is the correct answer to: the nerve to vastus medialis is a branch of:?', '["Femoral nerve","Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Common peroneal","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'The nerve to the vastus medialis arises from the femoral nerve, which innervates the entire quadriceps group. The tibial, obturator, common peroneal, and sciatic nerves supply other compartments.'),
('87a5daab-5d8c-5bb8-aaf9-4087f15813b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'For Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which option is most appropriate: the obturator nerve (l2-l4) runs through:?', '["The adductor hiatus","The sciatic foramen","The obturator foramen (obturator canal)","The inguinal canal","The femoral canal"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator foramen (obturator canal)', 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) leaves the pelvis through the obturator foramen via the obturator canal. The adductor hiatus, sciatic foramen, inguinal canal, and femoral canal carry different structures.'),
('931f380d-4c5c-5be4-8bdf-4ff79c3d7507'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Which answer is correct in Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg when asked: the obturator nerve supplies:?', '["The adductor muscles (except hamstring part of adductor magnus)","The hamstrings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gluteals mediated by specific receptor activation","The quadriceps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The adductor muscles (except hamstring part of adductor magnus)', 'The obturator nerve supplies the adductor muscles, with the exception of the hamstring part of adductor magnus. The hamstrings, quadriceps, and gluteals are not innervated by it.'),
('f0b8f2b0-7a64-5a81-8b70-5a4d4dc7ed26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, the best response to ''The adductor longus originates from:'' is:', '["Pubic bone (body near pubic crest)","AIIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","ASIS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic bone (body near pubic crest)', 'The adductor longus originates from the body of the pubic bone near the pubic crest. The AIIS, ischial tuberosity, ASIS, and iliac fossa are origins of other muscles.'),
('cdcc7d21-42b5-54fc-9376-37d7d63d32fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'During revision of Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which of the following is correct: the adductor brevis originates from:?', '["AIIS resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliac fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic bone (inferior ramus and body)","Pubic crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic bone (inferior ramus and body)', 'The adductor brevis arises from the inferior ramus and body of the pubic bone. The AIIS, iliac fossa, pubic crest, and ischial tuberosity are origins of other muscles.'),
('fa4c7ba2-f169-5f42-95d8-c5cc73c13610'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which statement best fits the following fact: the adductor magnus (adductor part) originates from:?', '["Ischial tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischial spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic body mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior pubic ramus and ischial ramus","Iliac fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pubic ramus and ischial ramus', 'The adductor part of adductor magnus originates from the inferior pubic ramus and ischial ramus. The ischial tuberosity, ischial spine, pubic body, and iliac fossa are not its origin.'),
('4c4a0811-80bd-5505-a05d-b5b089cb5d27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Regarding Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, what is the correct answer to: the adductor magnus (hamstring part) originates from:?', '["Iliac crest","Inferior pubic ramus","Pubic body","ASIS","Ischial tuberosity"]'::jsonb, 'Ischial tuberosity', 'The hamstring part of the adductor magnus originates from the ischial tuberosity, in common with the hamstrings. The iliac crest, inferior pubic ramus, pubic body, and ASIS are not involved.'),
('8eb39fb3-9cd9-5847-876d-bdcfcab1b287'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'For Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which option is most appropriate: the gracilis muscle originates from:?', '["ASIS resulting in decreased functional activity","AIIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic body and inferior pubic ramus"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic body and inferior pubic ramus', 'The gracilis originates from the body and inferior ramus of the pubic bone. The ASIS, AIIS, ischial tuberosity, and iliac fossa are origins of other muscles.'),
('a1cd6d88-b526-521f-b283-2d61478dc521'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Which answer is correct in Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg when asked: the pectineus muscle originates from:?', '["Pecten pubis (pubic bone)","ASIS","Iliac fossa","Ischial tuberosity","AIIS"]'::jsonb, 'Pecten pubis (pubic bone)', 'The pectineus originates from the pecten pubis of the pubic bone. The ASIS, iliac fossa, ischial tuberosity, and AIIS are origins of other muscles.'),
('ea094661-5a4b-59ec-a682-ac1cbbc8f4c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, the best response to ''The adductor longus inserts into:'' is:', '["Gluteal tuberosity","Pectineal line","Lesser trochanter","Linea aspera (medial lip middle third)","Adductor tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera (medial lip middle third)', 'The adductor longus inserts into the middle third of the medial lip of the linea aspera. The gluteal tuberosity, pectineal line, lesser trochanter, and adductor tubercle receive other insertions.'),
('9cdc9bdf-f5bd-5647-9ac4-dc1cb18de745'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'During revision of Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which of the following is correct: the adductor brevis inserts into:?', '["Linea aspera (medial lip above adductor longus)","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lesser trochanter mediated by specific receptor activation","Adductor tubercle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera (medial lip above adductor longus)', 'The adductor brevis inserts into the medial lip of the linea aspera, above the adductor longus. The pectineal line, lesser trochanter, adductor tubercle, and gluteal tuberosity are insertions of other muscles.'),
('411d37a5-7a92-583b-be24-5ee5aaff74aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'In Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which statement best fits the following fact: the adductor magnus (adductor part) inserts into:?', '["Adductor tubercle resulting in decreased functional activity","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteal tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Linea aspera and medial supracondylar ridge","Lesser trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea aspera and medial supracondylar ridge', 'The adductor part of the adductor magnus inserts along the linea aspera and medial supracondylar ridge. The adductor tubercle, pectineal line, gluteal tuberosity, and lesser trochanter are insertions of other muscles.'),
('b93eb72d-cad5-5257-a181-d1250ea7db99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Regarding Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, what is the correct answer to: the adductor magnus (hamstring part) inserts into:?', '["Adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle","Lesser trochanter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linea aspera mediated by specific receptor activation","Pectineal line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gluteal tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle', 'The hamstring part of the adductor magnus inserts on the adductor tubercle of the medial femoral condyle. The lesser trochanter, linea aspera, pectineal line, and gluteal tuberosity receive other insertions.'),
('600a7944-4c44-5be6-8d05-f0f65501ee8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'For Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg, which option is most appropriate: the gracilis muscle inserts into:?', '["Tibial tuberosity resulting in decreased functional activity","Pectineal line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adductor tubercle mediated by specific receptor activation","Pes anserinus (upper part of medial tibial shaft)","Linea aspera leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pes anserinus (upper part of medial tibial shaft)', 'The gracilis inserts into the pes anserinus on the upper part of the medial tibia, sharing this insertion with sartorius and semitendinosus. The tibial tuberosity, pectineal line, adductor tubercle, and linea aspera are different attachments.'),
('33491a29-09e7-5289-b3ad-6f6181c27de4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Lower Limb - Thigh Popliteal Knee Leg', 'Which answer is correct in Thigh, popliteal fossa, knee, leg when asked: the pectineus muscle inserts into:?', '["Pectineal line of femur (spiral line)","Linea aspera","Gluteal tuberosity","Greater trochanter","Lesser trochanter"]'::jsonb, 'Pectineal line of femur (spiral line)', 'The pectineus inserts into the pectineal line of the femur, which continues as the spiral line. The linea aspera, gluteal tuberosity, and greater and lesser trochanters are insertions of other muscles.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('f51dadeb-bc9b-5d98-be92-ca3f6e61bded'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The external ear consists of:', '["Only the ear canal","Only the pinna","Ear canal and middle ear","Auricle (pinna) and external auditory meatus","Pinna and tympanic membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Auricle (pinna) and external auditory meatus', 'The external ear comprises the auricle (pinna) and the external auditory meatus, which together collect and channel sound waves to the tympanic membrane. The tympanic membrane and middle ear belong to deeper compartments, and either component alone is incomplete.'),
('3972fbca-cac1-5328-b6d7-2a93f340f3e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The external auditory meatus extends from:', '["Tympanic membrane to inner ear","Concha to tympanic membrane","Auricle to mastoid","Pinna to middle ear","Concha to nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Concha to tympanic membrane', 'The external auditory meatus runs from the concha of the auricle medially to the tympanic membrane. The other options extend the pathway to the inner ear, mastoid, middle ear, or nasopharynx, which do not bound the meatus.'),
('8c105427-8435-5f9d-988a-49a74bb4a981'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The external auditory meatus in adults is about:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'In adults the external auditory meatus measures roughly 2.5 cm from the concha to the tympanic membrane. The remaining values are longer or shorter than the normal adult length.'),
('06bbeaa1-7b0b-58e1-8f57-105eff313f03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The lateral 1/3 of external auditory meatus is:', '["Fibrous","Muscular","Bony","Cartilaginous","Membranous"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilaginous', 'The lateral one-third of the external auditory meatus is cartilaginous, whereas the medial two-thirds is bony. Fibrous, muscular, and membranous are not tissue types normally found here.'),
('ae6d6e73-87ab-5fef-9a7b-d2ffb9aa0d9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The skin lining the external auditory meatus contains:', '["Sweat glands only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only stratified squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucous glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Ceruminous glands (modified sweat glands) and hair follicles","Only sebaceous glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ceruminous glands (modified sweat glands) and hair follicles', 'The skin of the cartilaginous part of the meatus contains ceruminous glands (modified apocrine glands) that produce earwax along with hair follicles. Options naming only one gland type or mucus-secreting glands do not reflect the lining.'),
('b50b7f74-ed66-5ac9-80e9-eabc3ffe22c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic membrane separates:', '["Pinna from ear canal","External ear from middle ear","Middle ear from inner ear","Mastoid from middle ear","External ear from inner ear"]'::jsonb, 'External ear from middle ear', 'The tympanic membrane forms the boundary between the external auditory meatus and the tympanic cavity of the middle ear. The other options describe boundaries that involve the inner ear or mastoid, not the membrane''s location.'),
('d4e95187-3d49-5e61-a2ec-e2d2f8f735ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic membrane is divided into:', '["Pars tensa (superior) and pars flaccida (inferior)","Pars tensa (inferior 4/5) and pars flaccida (superior 1/5)","Right and left halves","Anterior and posterior parts","Upper and lower thirds"]'::jsonb, 'Pars tensa (inferior 4/5) and pars flaccida (superior 1/5)', 'The tympanic membrane is divided into the large inferior pars tensa, comprising about four-fifths, and the small superior pars flaccida, about one-fifth. The reversed arrangement and the other listed divisions are incorrect.'),
('b572b865-0af0-5114-8e02-db99fde20ce8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The pars flaccida of tympanic membrane is also called:', '["Tense part","Shrapnell''s membrane","Scarpa''s membrane","Rivinus membrane","Relaxed part"]'::jsonb, 'Shrapnell''s membrane', 'The pars flaccida is eponymously known as Shrapnell''s membrane, while the pars tensa is the tense portion formed by the main body of the membrane. Scarpa''s membrane and Rivinus membrane are not names for this part.'),
('76b05832-26ce-563c-80ae-5de51dce64bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The umbo of tympanic membrane is:', '["The most depressed point at the tip of malleus handle","The attachment of incus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The center of pars flaccida mediated by specific receptor activation","The upper part of TM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower part of TM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most depressed point at the tip of malleus handle', 'The umbo is the most depressed point of the tympanic membrane, produced by the tip of the malleus handle pulling inward. The other options describe attachments or regions unrelated to the umbo.'),
('07b86029-0a2d-5b70-a4a3-9a14e8760aae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The cone of light is:', '["A reflection seen posterosuperiorly","The oval window","A reflection seen anteroinferiorly on the TM","The round window","The opening of the auditory tube"]'::jsonb, 'A reflection seen anteroinferiorly on the TM', 'The cone of light is a light reflex seen in the anteroinferior quadrant of the tympanic membrane on otoscopy. The other options refer to different anatomical structures or a wrong location.'),
('312786f1-f68f-5444-8149-9ac2453afdf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The middle ear (tympanic cavity) contains:', '["Semicircular canals resulting in decreased functional activity","Cochlea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endolymph mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes)","Auditory nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes)', 'The tympanic cavity houses the three auditory ossicles—malleus, incus, and stapes—which transmit vibration from the tympanic membrane to the oval window. Semicircular canals, cochlea, and endolymph belong to the inner ear, while the auditory nerve is intracranial.'),
('bb36e66f-2a7d-5f58-9240-3b6b8f98dbd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic cavity is lined by:', '["Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Skin","Stratified columnar epithelium","Mucous membrane (respiratory epithelium)"]'::jsonb, 'Mucous membrane (respiratory epithelium)', 'The tympanic cavity is lined by a mucous membrane containing ciliated respiratory-type epithelium, continuous with the lining of the auditory tube and mastoid antrum. Skin and the other epithelia do not line this cavity.'),
('40d79746-4ea6-5910-bef2-1026d608f96f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The auditory ossicles transmit sound from:', '["Pinna to TM resulting in decreased functional activity","Tympanic membrane to oval window (footplate of stapes)","TM to round window mediated by specific receptor activation","Cochlea to brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oval window to round window leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic membrane to oval window (footplate of stapes)', 'The ossicular chain conducts vibrations from the tympanic membrane to the oval window through the stapes footplate. The other listed pathways bypass the normal lever system of the middle ear.'),
('7a75e570-66ae-59d0-9101-072e44032734'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The malleus is attached to:', '["The oval window only resulting in decreased functional activity","The incus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The round window mediated by specific receptor activation","The tympanic membrane (handle) and incus (head)","The stapes only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tympanic membrane (handle) and incus (head)', 'The handle (manubrium) of the malleus is embedded in the tympanic membrane, and its head articulates with the body of the incus. It does not attach to the oval or round window or to the stapes alone.'),
('6e11a4c7-73c4-51ab-b702-37bb1459e417'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The incus articulates with:', '["Stapes and oval window resulting in decreased functional activity","Malleus and TM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malleus anteriorly and stapes posteriorly","Only malleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only stapes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus anteriorly and stapes posteriorly', 'The incus sits between the malleus and stapes, articulating anteriorly with the malleus head and posteriorly with the stapes head via its lenticular process. It does not attach to the tympanic membrane or oval window directly.'),
('a60f437d-5314-5c5a-88ee-28435ab92dfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The stapes footplate sits in:', '["Promontory","Aditus ad antrum","Facial canal","Oval window (fenestra vestibuli)","Round window (fenestra cochleae)"]'::jsonb, 'Oval window (fenestra vestibuli)', 'The footplate of the stapes occupies the oval window (fenestra vestibuli) on the medial wall of the middle ear. The round window is covered by the secondary tympanic membrane, and the other structures are separate locations.'),
('ed39cf48-e053-55f8-941b-d3fce82108d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The middle ear has how many walls?', '["2 walls resulting in decreased functional activity","5 walls accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 walls mediated by specific receptor activation","6 walls (roof, floor, medial, lateral, anterior, posterior)","4 walls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6 walls (roof, floor, medial, lateral, anterior, posterior)', 'The tympanic cavity has six walls: roof (tegmental), floor (jugular), medial (labyrinthine), lateral (membranous), anterior (carotid), and posterior (mastoid). The other counts omit essential walls of the cavity.'),
('aabbad96-c289-53c0-8c15-4010fa087648'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The roof (tegmental wall) of tympanic cavity is:', '["Aditus to mastoid antrum resulting in decreased functional activity","Promontory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jugular bulb mediated by specific receptor activation","Carotid canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tegmen tympani (thin plate of petrous temporal)"]'::jsonb, 'Tegmen tympani (thin plate of petrous temporal)', 'The roof of the tympanic cavity is the tegmental wall, formed by the tegmen tympani, a thin plate of the petrous temporal bone that separates the cavity from the middle cranial fossa. The other structures belong to other walls.'),
('74a160f9-e66b-5698-ab05-065b7987abe5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The floor (jugular wall) of tympanic cavity relates to:', '["Sigmoid sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior bulb of internal jugular vein","Carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior bulb of internal jugular vein', 'The floor, or jugular wall, is related below to the superior bulb of the internal jugular vein. The sigmoid sinus, facial nerve, carotid artery, and cochlea are associated with other walls of the cavity.'),
('8fe47407-454a-563d-8b24-e5e4f1f4af4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The medial wall (labyrinthine) of tympanic cavity contains:', '["Aditus ad antrum","Mastoid cells","Promontory (basal turn of cochlea) oval window","round window prominence of facial canal","Carotid canal Jugular bulb"]'::jsonb, 'Promontory (basal turn of cochlea) oval window', 'The medial labyrinthine wall is marked by the promontory overlying the basal turn of the cochlea and bears the oval window; the round window and prominence of the facial canal also lie here. The aditus and mastoid cells are on other walls.'),
('03937218-74a2-558b-a8c5-0012413d9550'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The lateral wall (membranous) of tympanic cavity is:', '["Tegmental wall","Tympanic membrane","Mastoid wall","Carotid wall","Jugular wall"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic membrane', 'The lateral membranous wall of the tympanic cavity is formed by the tympanic membrane together with the adjacent scutum. The other options name different walls of the cavity.'),
('371ddff3-7c8f-52ff-bbe0-67fd087c287d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The anterior wall (carotid) of tympanic cavity relates to:', '["Mastoid cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cochlea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal carotid artery and auditory tube","Jugular vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid artery and auditory tube', 'The anterior carotid wall is related to the internal carotid artery and carries the opening of the auditory tube. The mastoid cells, cochlea, jugular vein, and facial nerve are associated with other walls.'),
('cd8284a9-f046-549e-b662-6de477d11651'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The posterior wall (mastoid) of tympanic cavity contains:', '["Promontory","Carotid canal","Oval window","Aditus to mastoid antrum","pyramidal eminence facial nerve canal Jugular bulb"]'::jsonb, 'Aditus to mastoid antrum', 'The posterior mastoid wall contains the aditus that leads to the mastoid antrum; it also bears the pyramidal eminence. The promontory, carotid canal, and oval window lie on other walls.'),
('979c1c87-a42b-5f6a-8012-c51a22582f54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The mastoid antrum is:', '["A large air cell in the mastoid process communicating with the middle ear","The semicircular canals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cochlea mediated by specific receptor activation","The external ear canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inner ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large air cell in the mastoid process communicating with the middle ear', 'The mastoid (tympanic) antrum is an air-filled space in the petrous/mastoid temporal bone that connects the middle ear to the mastoid air cells through the aditus. The semicircular canals and cochlea belong to the inner ear.'),
('7f3ad28b-22fa-5c37-969d-fc650d00dd30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The mastoid air cells may extend into which bone?', '["Sphenoid bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temporal bone (mastoid process, petrous, squamous)"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal bone (mastoid process, petrous, squamous)', 'Mastoid air cells arise within the temporal bone and may extend into the mastoid process, petrous and squamous parts. They do not normally invade the sphenoid, frontal, parietal, or occipital bones.'),
('c8c276fa-02f1-5e52-8a3a-5df55a25032d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Mastoiditis is:', '["Infection of ear canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection of mastoid air cells (complication of otitis media)","Infection of inner ear mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection of pinna without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection of external ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection of mastoid air cells (complication of otitis media)', 'Mastoiditis is infection and inflammation of the mastoid air cells, most often complicating acute otitis media. It affects the mastoid, not the canal, pinna, inner ear, or simple external ear.'),
('5bce40f5-0302-5dfd-a7e2-558eef09a0f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The auditory tube (Eustachian tube) connects:', '["Middle ear to external ear","Ear to oral cavity","Ear to nose","Middle ear (tympanic cavity) to nasopharynx","Inner ear to middle ear"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear (tympanic cavity) to nasopharynx', 'The auditory tube runs from the anterior wall of the tympanic cavity to the nasopharynx, equalizing pressure and draining the middle ear. It does not connect the ear to the external canal, oral cavity, or nose directly.'),
('354c7486-a388-5c04-92ca-54b6658da87e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The auditory tube functions to:', '["Transmit sound resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventilate the mastoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connect ear to nose mediated by specific receptor activation","Drain middle ear secretions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Equalize pressure between middle ear and atmosphere"]'::jsonb, 'Equalize pressure between middle ear and atmosphere', 'The primary function of the auditory tube is to equalize air pressure between the middle ear and the atmosphere, which it does through the nasopharynx. It is not a sound-transmitting or ventilating pathway to the mastoid.'),
('5b85bbe7-735c-5a0b-8d0d-ffdd15e68dc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The auditory tube is longer in:', '["Same length in all","Adults (3.5 cm) than in children","Males than females","Children than adults","Females than males"]'::jsonb, 'Adults (3.5 cm) than in children', 'In adults the auditory tube measures about 3.5 cm, whereas in children it is shorter, wider, and more horizontal, which favors ascending infection. Length does not differ primarily by sex.'),
('2e874cb7-529f-5291-a2b1-028cdbc1ba40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The muscles that open the auditory tube are:', '["Stylopharyngeus and palatopharyngeus","Tensor veli palatini and salpingopharyngeus","Palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus","Musculus uvulae and levator veli","Levator veli palatini and tensor veli"]'::jsonb, 'Tensor veli palatini and salpingopharyngeus', 'The auditory tube is opened chiefly by the tensor veli palatini and assisted by the salpingopharyngeus. The other muscle pairs act on the pharynx, tongue, or palate and do not dilate the tubal orifice.'),
('aee39511-3401-5b7f-862b-3d52efbf658f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The middle ear contains which nerves?', '["Facial nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Chorda tympani (facial nerve branch) and tympanic plexus (glossopharyngeal)","Trigeminal nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglossal nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve branch) and tympanic plexus (glossopharyngeal)', 'The middle ear transmits the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve, and is innervated by the tympanic plexus derived from the glossopharyngeal nerve. Listing only a single nerve leaves out one of these structures.'),
('3a113638-1ddf-524f-b0bf-34e2a378aae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The chorda tympani crosses the tympanic cavity:', '["Between the incus and stapes","Between the malleus and incus","Through the round window","Under the promontory","Through the oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Between the malleus and incus', 'The chorda tympani enters the middle ear, runs across the medial surface of the tympanic membrane between the malleus and incus, and exits via the petrotympanic fissure. The windows and promontory are not its course.'),
('6eddbff5-d014-5084-8d21-53a00cda1e15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic plexus is formed by:', '["Tympanic branch of glossopharyngeal (Jacobson''s nerve) and sympathetics from internal carotid plexus","Vagus and facial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only glossopharyngeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminal and facial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic branch of glossopharyngeal (Jacobson''s nerve) and sympathetics from internal carotid plexus', 'The tympanic plexus is formed on the promontory by the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s nerve) joined by sympathetic fibers from the internal carotid plexus. It is not formed by the facial, vagus, or trigeminal nerves alone.'),
('32eada97-1329-554a-817d-dac4d2389253'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The lesser petrosal nerve arises from:', '["Cochlear nerve","Facial nerve","Tympanic plexus (fibers from CN IX)","Geniculate ganglion","Otic ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic plexus (fibers from CN IX)', 'The lesser petrosal nerve arises from the tympanic plexus, carrying preganglionic parasympathetic fibers of glossopharyngeal origin to the otic ganglion for parotid secretion. It is not a branch of the cochlear or facial nerve.'),
('ade34d28-6988-5bc2-85f9-4784d08905d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The inner ear (labyrinth) consists of:', '["Only semicircular canals","Only cochlea","Cochlea and auditory nerve","Cochlea (hearing)","vestibule and semicircular canals (balance) Vestibule and ossicles"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlea (hearing)', 'The inner ear, or labyrinth, contains the cochlea for hearing along with the vestibule and semicircular canals for balance. Among the options, naming the cochlea for hearing is the correct labyrinthine component; the auditory nerve and ossicles are not part of the labyrinth itself.'),
('7b0f56ec-3ce5-5a07-9e14-0ff779024be9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The bony labyrinth contains:', '["Perilymph","Blood","Mucus","Endolymph","Serous fluid"]'::jsonb, 'Perilymph', 'The bony labyrinth is filled with perilymph, a fluid that bathes the membranous labyrinth within it. Endolymph lies inside the membranous labyrinth, while blood, mucus, and serous fluid are not found here.'),
('383da3c6-bb4a-5538-945a-d23276d60da3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The membranous labyrinth contains:', '["Cerebrospinal fluid","Perilymph","Blood","Endolymph","Mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymph', 'The membranous labyrinth is filled with endolymph, which has a high potassium content. Perilymph surrounds the membranous labyrinth within the bony labyrinth, and the other fluids do not occupy it.'),
('a38a32a7-56a8-534e-9d1d-c5f335057c75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The cochlea is responsible for:', '["Hearing (sound transduction)","Head position sensing","Rotational acceleration sensing","Linear acceleration sensing","Balance"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing (sound transduction)', 'The cochlea contains the organ of Corti and transduces sound waves into neural impulses for hearing. Head position, rotational, and linear acceleration sensing are functions of the vestibular apparatus.'),
('ac7be44c-9e81-5597-a390-33f66f0883da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The semicircular canals are responsible for:', '["Sound localization resulting in decreased functional activity","Gravity sensing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linear acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Rotational acceleration (angular movement)","Hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rotational acceleration (angular movement)', 'The semicircular canals detect rotational (angular) acceleration of the head. Linear acceleration and gravity sensing belong to the utricle and saccule, while hearing is a cochlear function.'),
('7a4adf38-4743-5e8c-832d-96d882a5cc9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The vestibule contains the utricle and saccule which are responsible for:', '["Taste resulting in decreased functional activity","Linear acceleration and head position (static balance)","Rotational acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Sound conduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration and head position (static balance)', 'The utricle and saccule of the vestibule sense linear acceleration and static head position. Rotational acceleration is detected by the semicircular canals, and hearing or taste are unrelated functions.'),
('caf7f0d3-234b-57e2-a8d3-f296c9902620'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The organ of Corti is located:', '["In the cochlear duct on the basilar membrane","In the saccule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the utricle mediated by specific receptor activation","In the semicircular canals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the vestibule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the cochlear duct on the basilar membrane', 'The organ of Corti, the auditory receptor, lies in the cochlear duct resting on the basilar membrane. It is not found in the saccule, utricle, semicircular canals, or general vestibule, which are vestibular structures.'),
('42f0c686-1b01-5962-b78d-c69dd9ab9819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The spiral ganglion contains:', '["Motor neurons","Sympathetic neurons","Parasympathetic neurons","Cell bodies of vestibular nerve","Cell bodies of cochlear nerve (hearing)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell bodies of cochlear nerve (hearing)', 'The spiral ganglion in the modiolus of the cochlea holds the bipolar cell bodies of the cochlear division of the vestibulocochlear nerve. It is a sensory ganglion, not a motor or autonomic structure, and is distinct from the vestibular ganglion.'),
('344d6734-1ffb-5897-9bf2-fa4290fd1a2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The vestibular ganglion (Scarpa''s ganglion) contains:', '["Cell bodies of cochlear nerve","Parasympathetic neurons","Motor neurons","Sympathetic neurons","Cell bodies of vestibular nerve (balance)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell bodies of vestibular nerve (balance)', 'Scarpa''s vestibular ganglion in the internal acoustic meatus contains the bipolar cell bodies of the vestibular nerve that serve balance. It is a sensory ganglion and does not hold cochlear, motor, or autonomic neurons.'),
('67558f21-91f7-5070-9d2b-dbf5bfd71e69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The facial nerve runs through which part of temporal bone?', '["Tympanic cavity only","Facial canal in the petrous temporal","Mastoid only","Cochlea","Semicircular canals"]'::jsonb, 'Facial canal in the petrous temporal', 'Within the temporal bone the facial nerve travels through the facial canal of the petrous part, passing medial to the middle ear. It does not run through the cochlea or semicircular canals, and the canal spans more than the tympanic cavity alone.'),
('8c110adb-ba3c-5f26-9de8-bf05c648617a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The facial nerve has how many segments in the temporal bone?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 (tympanic and mastoid) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 (labyrinthine, tympanic, mastoid)","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (labyrinthine, tympanic, mastoid)', 'Within the temporal bone the facial nerve has three segments: labyrinthine, tympanic (horizontal), and mastoid (vertical). Two segments omit the labyrinthine portion, and the other counts are incorrect.'),
('a04239cf-be16-5900-9d30-1652f67bb8a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The geniculate ganglion is located:', '["In the tympanic cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","At the anterior end of labyrinthine segment of facial nerve","At the stylomastoid foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","In the internal acoustic meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the mastoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the anterior end of labyrinthine segment of facial nerve', 'The geniculate ganglion lies at the anterior (lateral) end of the labyrinthine segment of the facial nerve, where the nerve turns posteriorly to form the genu. It is not in the tympanic cavity, internal acoustic meatus, mastoid, or at the stylomastoid foramen.'),
('89caccf3-0b46-5012-96a7-2aefd9c2c5ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The internal acoustic meatus transmits:', '["VII","VIII","IX Only VII","Facial nerve (VII)","vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) labyrinthine artery VII and VIII Only VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The internal acoustic meatus transmits the facial nerve (CN VII) together with the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) and labyrinthine artery. Of the options given, the facial nerve (VII) is the correctly named structure that passes through it.'),
('99f997ff-a7a9-5307-93cf-00e9dea85889'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) divides into:', '["Anterior and posterior divisions","Superior and inferior divisions","Sensory and motor divisions","Cochlear and vestibular divisions","Medial and lateral divisions"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear and vestibular divisions', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve splits into cochlear and vestibular divisions serving hearing and balance respectively. Superior/inferior, anterior/posterior, and medial/lateral divisions are not the standard functional subdivisions of CN VIII.'),
('8cc4b73b-6b81-57e0-9b72-42c775017edd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The external ear is supplied by which nerves?', '["Only trigeminal","Only facial nerve","Only vagus","Auricular branch of vagus (Arnold''s nerve)","auriculotemporal (V3) great auricular (C2-3) Only great auricular"]'::jsonb, 'Auricular branch of vagus (Arnold''s nerve)', 'The external ear receives supply from several nerves, including the auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold''s nerve), which innervates the floor and posterior wall of the meatus and adjacent pinna. The option naming this branch is correct; the other options cite only one nerve or are garbled.'),
('17d9fded-7d7b-5539-844a-69539db775f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Arnold''s nerve (auricular branch of vagus) supplies:', '["The inner ear only resulting in decreased functional activity","The tympanic membrane only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The middle ear only mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior wall and floor of external auditory meatus and adjacent pinna","The entire pinna leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior wall and floor of external auditory meatus and adjacent pinna', 'The auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold''s nerve) supplies the skin of the posterior wall and floor of the external auditory meatus along with the adjacent pinna. It does not supply the inner or middle ear, and its territory is not the entire pinna alone.'),
('3874bafb-0c08-5b6f-9f55-d70ad0142029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Stimulation of Arnold''s nerve can cause:', '["Tinnitus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cough or gag reflex (ear-cough reflex)","Facial pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertigo without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cough or gag reflex (ear-cough reflex)', 'Arnold''s nerve is the auricular branch of the vagus, which also gives sensory supply to part of the pharynx and larynx, so stimulation triggers the ear-cough (Arnold''s) reflex. Tinnitus, vertigo, and hearing loss reflect cochlear or vestibular dysfunction, not vagal stimulation.'),
('9378c2d5-1dca-5fda-98f4-7f7dd8ed170a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic membrane is supplied by:', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only glossopharyngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Auriculotemporal (V3) and auricular branch of vagus (X)","Only trigeminal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal (V3) and auricular branch of vagus (X)', 'The lateral surface of the tympanic membrane is innervated by the auriculotemporal nerve (CN V3) and the auricular branch of the vagus (CN X). No single nerve supplies the whole membrane, so options listing only one nerve are incomplete.'),
('32bd4b3c-1782-57fb-b0eb-0fa603b100ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The temporal bone consists of how many parts?', '["4 parts (squamous, tympanic, mastoid, petrous)","6 parts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 parts mediated by specific receptor activation","3 parts (squamous, tympanic, petrous) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 parts (squamous, tympanic, mastoid, petrous)', 'The temporal bone develops from four parts: squamous, tympanic, mastoid, and petrous. The other options omit one or more of these developmental parts.'),
('a8135d8a-6c4a-5804-b2d6-e86208c67b64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The petrous part of temporal bone contains:', '["Tympanic membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Only air cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External auditory meatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Inner ear structures (cochlea, vestibule, semicircular canals)","Auditory ossicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner ear structures (cochlea, vestibule, semicircular canals)', 'The petrous part houses the inner ear, including the cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals, together with the internal auditory meatus. The tympanic membrane, ossicles, and air cells lie in the tympanic and mastoid parts.'),
('a8db6d68-c640-587b-959d-aec29380d384'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The mastoid process develops when?', '["Before birth resulting in decreased functional activity","At age 2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In adulthood mediated by specific receptor activation","At puberty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","After birth (develops from pull of SCM)"]'::jsonb, 'After birth (develops from pull of SCM)', 'The mastoid process is absent at birth and develops postnatally under the traction of the sternocleidomastoid muscle as the child begins to hold up the head. It is not present before birth or at age two.'),
('a49c2b54-bec6-56c0-9141-c3db14b66e87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Mastoid process may be absent in:', '["Elderly resulting in decreased functional activity","Adults accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infants and young children","Males without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infants and young children', 'The mastoid process is rudimentary at birth and grows after birth with sternocleidomastoid traction, so it is typically absent or poorly developed in infants and young children. It is well formed in older children and adults.'),
('6f1f9a8b-cc29-5712-9903-e943dfdbc192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The stylomastoid foramen is located between:', '["Tympanic and squamous","Styloid and mastoid processes","Mastoid and tympanic parts","Styloid and petrous","Petrous and squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Styloid and mastoid processes', 'The stylomastoid foramen lies on the inferior surface of the temporal bone between the styloid and mastoid processes. Its position relative to these two bony processes is the identifying feature.'),
('927e3564-4035-5092-acb2-d32b42a81fd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The stylomastoid foramen transmits:', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Only facial nerve","Facial and trigeminal","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve and stylomastoid artery"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve and stylomastoid artery', 'The stylomastoid foramen transmits the facial nerve (CN VII) as it exits the skull and also the stylomastoid branch of the posterior auricular artery. Other nerves listed pass through different foramina.'),
('dfd2fedc-198a-595d-b43e-8442886ed8a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The carotid canal is located in:', '["Occipital bone","Petrous part of temporal bone","Mastoid part","Tympanic part","Squamous part"]'::jsonb, 'Petrous part of temporal bone', 'The carotid canal runs through the petrous part of the temporal bone and carries the internal carotid artery into the skull. It is not found in the occipital, squamous, tympanic, or mastoid parts.'),
('37b831fa-491b-5cd4-a839-74f7e077a1e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The internal carotid artery enters the skull through:', '["Foramen lacerum","Foramen magnum","Carotid canal","Foramen ovale","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid canal', 'The internal carotid artery enters the skull via the carotid canal in the petrous temporal bone. The foramen magnum transmits the medulla and vertebral arteries, and the other listed foramina carry different structures.'),
('7fb86891-6f83-5959-96e4-95473bd83400'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The jugular foramen is between:', '["Frontal and parietal","Occipital and parietal","Petrous temporal and occipital bones","Tympanic and squamous","Temporal and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Petrous temporal and occipital bones', 'The jugular foramen is formed between the petrous part of the temporal bone and the occipital bone. It lies at the posterior end of the petro-occipital fissure and transmits the IJV and CN IX, X, XI.'),
('3739b2a7-ee84-59c8-b4d1-6917f5f15aa6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The jugular foramen transmits:', '["Only IJV All cranial nerves","IJV and facial nerve IX","X XI only","Internal jugular vein glossopharyngeal (IX)","vagus (X) accessory (XI)"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular vein glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The jugular foramen transmits the internal jugular vein together with the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI) nerves. The chosen option correctly names the vein and the ninth nerve; the others are incomplete or incorrect.'),
('0033eb8c-549d-5a88-a868-6d3c07afc005'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The middle ear infection (otitis media) is common in children because:', '["Auditory tube is shorter and more horizontal","Auditory tube is longer","Eustachian tube is narrower","TM is thicker","Immune system is stronger"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory tube is shorter and more horizontal', 'In children the auditory (eustachian) tube is shorter, wider, and more horizontal, allowing nasopharyngeal organisms to reflux into the middle ear. In adults the tube is longer and more angulated, which protects against this spread.'),
('a401e64d-cc86-5685-9c41-8b00cc516451'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Cholesteatoma is:', '["A type of tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A cyst of the pinna accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A collection of keratinized squamous epithelium in the middle ear (from TM retraction)","An infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A collection of fluid in the inner ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of keratinized squamous epithelium in the middle ear (from TM retraction)', 'Cholesteatoma is a destructive collection of keratinizing squamous epithelium that typically forms in the middle ear or mastoid, often after retraction of the tympanic membrane. It is not a true neoplasm, simple infection, or fluid collection.'),
('6c29ae4d-edd6-5d56-b5f8-530d9d7b732f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The conductive hearing loss in otosclerosis results from:', '["Auditory nerve damage","Auditory tube obstruction","Stapes fixation at the oval window","Cochlear damage","TM perforation"]'::jsonb, 'Stapes fixation at the oval window', 'In otosclerosis, abnormal bone growth fixes the stapes footplate at the oval window, blocking sound transmission through the ossicular chain and producing conductive hearing loss. Cochlear and auditory nerve damage would instead cause sensorineural loss.'),
('e5cc3d43-4ed5-500e-8a0d-b34af09dc140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Sensorineural hearing loss results from damage to:', '["Cochlea or cochlear nerve (hair cells or nerve)","External ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tympanic membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","Ossicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlea or cochlear nerve (hair cells or nerve)', 'Sensorineural hearing loss arises from damage to the cochlea, hair cells, or the cochlear nerve. Lesions of the external ear, tympanic membrane, ossicles, or middle ear cause conductive hearing loss instead.'),
('6fc4b20e-1a25-5204-9d25-0f0d651cab7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The round window (fenestra cochleae) is closed by:', '["Auditory tube","Stapes footplate","Incus","Malleus","Secondary tympanic membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary tympanic membrane', 'The round window is sealed by the secondary tympanic membrane, which allows pressure waves in the cochlea to dissipate. The stapes footplate closes the oval window, not the round window.'),
('4940f411-4e57-5b2a-b084-b0f405be2fce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The oval window (fenestra vestibuli) is closed by:', '["Secondary tympanic membrane","Footplate of stapes","Incus","Tympanic membrane","Malleus"]'::jsonb, 'Footplate of stapes', 'The footplate of the stapes fits into and closes the oval window, transmitting vibrations from the ossicular chain into the perilymph. The secondary tympanic membrane closes the round window.'),
('d609f91c-3e8c-54e0-9d4a-36907947314b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The chorda tympani leaves the skull through:', '["Petrotympanic fissure","Jugular foramen","Foramen spinosum","Foramen ovale","Stylomastoid foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Petrotympanic fissure', 'After crossing the tympanic cavity, the chorda tympani exits the skull through the petrotympanic fissure to join the lingual nerve. The stylomastoid foramen transmits the main facial nerve trunk, not the chorda tympani.'),
('cb8d6ad5-5b64-5ebf-ab7e-4e74d4aa71c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The mastoid antrum is present at:', '["Age 5","Adulthood","Adolescence","Birth","Age 2"]'::jsonb, 'Birth', 'The mastoid antrum is an air-filled cavity present at birth, even though the mastoid process and air cells develop later. Early presence of the antrum explains why infantile mastoiditis follows middle ear infection.'),
('0d32885c-4b27-500e-8164-f79b818f462e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The aditus ad antrum connects:', '["Tympanic cavity to mastoid antrum","Middle ear to nasopharynx","Tympanic cavity to auditory tube","External ear to middle ear","Mastoid antrum to mastoid cells"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic cavity to mastoid antrum', 'The aditus ad antrum is the opening that connects the epitympanic recess of the tympanic cavity to the mastoid antrum. The auditory tube, not the aditus, connects the middle ear to the nasopharynx.'),
('3722c3b2-b343-5c30-9418-4fa78f0db1ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The pyramidal eminence contains:', '["Tensor tympani muscle","Incus","Chorda tympani","Stapedius muscle","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Stapedius muscle', 'The pyramidal eminence on the posterior wall of the tympanic cavity contains the stapedius muscle, whose tendon emerges to reach the neck of the stapes. The tensor tympani lies in the canal above the auditory tube, not here.'),
('868163b2-7610-5a1d-a7bd-edfb7bc08f75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tendon of stapedius attaches to:', '["Oval window","Incus","Malleus handle","Neck of stapes","TM"]'::jsonb, 'Neck of stapes', 'The stapedius tendon emerges from the pyramidal eminence and inserts on the neck of the stapes. Contraction pulls the stapes footplate to dampen excessive sound; it does not attach to the malleus or incus.'),
('f85546b3-5288-5562-b6ed-583f9b703e5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tensor tympani muscle attaches to:', '["Incus body","Neck of stapes","TM","Handle of malleus","Oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Handle of malleus', 'The tensor tympani arises in the canal above the pharyngotympanic tube and inserts on the handle of the malleus, tensing the tympanic membrane when it contracts. The stapedius, by contrast, attaches to the stapes.'),
('e152d4b8-f669-5bcb-a5eb-79206658d5fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Tensor tympani is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal nerve (V3)","Hypoglossal","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V3)', 'The tensor tympani is innervated by the mandibular division (V3) of the trigeminal nerve via the nerve to medial pterygoid. Stapedius, in contrast, is supplied by the facial nerve.'),
('548e9b02-d3c6-56f9-97c3-411fc184ad87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Stapedius is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V3)","Glossopharyngeal","Facial nerve (VII)","Vagus nerve","Hypoglossal"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The stapedius is innervated by a branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) from the tympanic segment. Because of this, facial nerve lesions cause hyperacusis due to unopposed stapes movement.'),
('0f721dd4-e478-5c5b-b4e5-e19c89e64e1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic membrane is innervated mainly by which nerves?', '["Auriculotemporal nerve (CN V3) and auricular branch of vagus (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Great auricular nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Lesser occipital nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve (CN V3) and auricular branch of vagus (CN X)', 'The lateral surface of the tympanic membrane receives sensation mainly from the auriculotemporal nerve (CN V3), while the auricular branch of the vagus (CN X) also contributes. Neither the facial nor the glossopharyngeal nerve is the main supply.'),
('1e56e943-33b9-5bff-9be8-a59c506c8f18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'What is the function of the tensor tympani muscle?', '["Stabilizes the stapes","Opens the auditory tube","Dampens loud sounds","Moves the malleus laterally","Tenses the tympanic membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Tenses the tympanic membrane', 'The tensor tympani pulls the handle of the malleus medially, increasing tension on the tympanic membrane and reducing its vibration in response to loud sounds. Stabilizing the stapes is the role of stapedius, not tensor tympani.'),
('bc829a34-7f11-5f1c-aad4-e1a8d6505a87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s nerve) supplies:', '["Sensation to the external ear","Parasympathetic to lacrimal gland","Sensation to the middle ear mucosa","Taste to posterior 1/3 of tongue","Motor to tensor tympani"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation to the middle ear mucosa', 'Jacobson''s nerve (tympanic branch of CN IX) provides general sensory innervation to the mucosa of the middle ear and the tympanic cavity. Taste to the posterior tongue is a different distribution of CN IX, and the tensor tympani is supplied by V3.'),
('a37e57af-d754-5f31-bb2f-027d5dadbcca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which nerve provides taste sensation to the anterior 2/3 of tongue via the middle ear?', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve (chorda tympani branch)","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (chorda tympani branch)', 'The chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve, crosses the tympanic cavity and carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. The glossopharyngeal nerve serves the posterior third, and the trigeminal carries general sensation, not taste.'),
('0b900fb6-22f6-5551-a188-f5d4e9133151'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The scala media (cochlear duct) contains:', '["Endolymph","Cerebrospinal fluid","Blood","Mucus","Perilymph"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymph', 'The scala media (cochlear duct) is filled with endolymph, a potassium-rich fluid produced by the stria vascularis. Perilymph occupies the scala vestibuli and scala tympani, not the cochlear duct.'),
('a6bb62fc-751b-5427-8433-30b58f54e864'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The scala vestibuli and scala tympani communicate via:', '["Saccule","Cochlear aqueduct","Oval window","Helicotrema","Round window"]'::jsonb, 'Helicotrema', 'The scala vestibuli and scala tympani meet at the helicotrema at the cochlear apex, allowing perilymph to pass between them. The oval and round windows are at the base and do not connect the two scalae.'),
('17375270-9d83-55b8-816c-c5941f32c38f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Hair cells of the organ of Corti are embedded in:', '["Reissner''s membrane","Basilar membrane","Tectorial membrane","Stria vascularis","Spiral ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Tectorial membrane', 'The stereocilia of the organ of Corti hair cells are embedded in the overlying tectorial membrane, so shearing forces during sound stimulation bend the hair cells. The basilar membrane is the base on which the organ of Corti rests, not the membrane embedding the hairs.'),
('40a52424-1eb9-51cf-88a7-a184ad2c6d6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The stria vascularis is responsible for:', '["Filtering perilymph","Transmitting sound","Producing endolymph","Supporting hair cells","Maintaining ionic balance of endolymph"]'::jsonb, 'Maintaining ionic balance of endolymph', 'The stria vascularis produces endolymph and maintains its high-potassium, low-sodium ionic composition, which is required for hair cell function. It does not transmit sound or filter perilymph.'),
('f88df1c8-37b8-50a9-9f53-87c42cc0daf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which cranial nerve carries hearing and balance?', '["XI","VII","VIII","IX","X"]'::jsonb, 'VIII', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) carries afferent fibers for both hearing (cochlear division) and balance (vestibular division). CN VII is facial, IX is glossopharyngeal, and X is vagus.'),
('ef7f487f-fb52-588d-9921-35868e8d6f54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The vestibular apparatus includes:', '["Scala tympani resulting in decreased functional activity","Helicotrema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cochlea only mediated by specific receptor activation","Organ of Corti without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Semicircular canals and otolith organs"]'::jsonb, 'Semicircular canals and otolith organs', 'The vestibular apparatus consists of the three semicircular canals and the otolith organs, the utricle and saccule, all of which detect head position and movement. The cochlea and its scalae subserve hearing, not balance.'),
('7eb9c937-094f-5e2f-a3ac-9220718d3f02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The horizontal semicircular canal is oriented:', '["Posteriorly","At 45 degrees","Horizontally","Vertically","Anteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontally', 'The lateral (horizontal) semicircular canal lies roughly horizontal in the head and detects rotational movements of the head in the horizontal plane. The anterior and posterior canals are oriented vertically.'),
('d56ab4dc-43c2-5eb2-91ba-8bf3845e15f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Cupula is found in:', '["Saccule","Semicircular canals","Cochlea","Utricle","Vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Semicircular canals', 'The cupula is the gelatinous structure in the ampulla of each semicircular canal that is displaced by endolymph flow and bends the underlying hair cells. The utricle and saccule instead contain otolithic membranes.'),
('bf504628-ac0a-5453-a5f8-a57691c02734'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Otolithic membrane is located in:', '["Cochlea","Tympanic cavity","Utricle and saccule","Semicircular canals","External auditory meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Utricle and saccule', 'The otolithic membrane, studded with calcium carbonate crystals (otoconia), lies over the maculae of the utricle and saccule and responds to linear acceleration and gravity. Semicircular canals use the cupula instead.'),
('59439a5e-d79b-5e1c-8d2b-bae67e7e4c7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Endolymph is produced by:', '["Stria vascularis","Spiral ganglion","Dark cells of vestibular labyrinth","Organ of Corti","Cochlear nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Stria vascularis', 'Endolymph is produced by the stria vascularis of the cochlear duct, and the dark cells of the vestibular labyrinth also contribute within the vestibular organs. The stria vascularis is the best-known and principal production site.'),
('b936e4ed-58b0-5376-9a0d-69020c61a0a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The modiolus is:', '["A muscle of the ear","A nerve bundle","A ligament of the TM","The central tendon of the diaphragm","The bony core of the cochlea"]'::jsonb, 'The bony core of the cochlea', 'The modiolus is the conical central bony axis of the cochlea through which fibers of the cochlear nerve pass to the spiral ganglion. It is not a muscle, ligament, or tendon.'),
('454a9338-1e2c-5119-803c-3090f7d5e9fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The spiral organ (of Corti) rests on:', '["Stria vascularis","Reissner''s membrane","Spiral ligament","Tectorial membrane","Basilar membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Basilar membrane', 'The organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane, and sound-induced motion of this membrane stimulates the hair cells against the tectorial membrane. Reissner''s membrane forms the roof of the cochlear duct.'),
('eed168b2-51bc-5dee-a9ff-1090b075940a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'High-frequency sounds are detected at:', '["Round window","Base of cochlea","Apex of cochlea","Helicotrema","Middle turn"]'::jsonb, 'Base of cochlea', 'The basilar membrane is stiff and narrow at the cochlear base, which resonates with high-frequency sounds. Low-frequency sounds maximally displace the more compliant apical portion.'),
('78bf4ae2-55ab-507f-a747-be6983f905c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Low-frequency sounds are detected at:', '["Base of cochlea","Round window","Saccule","Apex of cochlea","Oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of cochlea', 'The basilar membrane is wide and flexible at the cochlear apex, so low-frequency tones produce peak displacement there. High-frequency tones are detected at the narrow, stiff base.'),
('05736331-b61c-5ee9-831f-9f22670f5833'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The saccular macula detects:', '["Sound","Vibration","Linear horizontal acceleration","Linear vertical acceleration","Rotational acceleration"]'::jsonb, 'Linear vertical acceleration', 'The saccular macula is oriented vertically and is most sensitive to linear vertical acceleration and gravity. The utricular macula, oriented horizontally, detects linear horizontal acceleration.'),
('e9437449-85c3-58dd-a850-cf201903ffa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The utricular macula detects:', '["Rotational acceleration","Linear vertical acceleration","Tilt in any direction","Sound","Linear horizontal acceleration"]'::jsonb, 'Linear horizontal acceleration', 'The utricular macula lies horizontally and responds chiefly to linear horizontal acceleration and head tilt. The saccule detects vertical acceleration, and the semicircular canal ampullae detect rotation.'),
('831128ea-ea7b-59dd-a362-dfdccf351e1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The facial nerve exits the skull through:', '["Foramen ovale","Carotid canal","Jugular foramen","Stylomastoid foramen","Foramen spinosum"]'::jsonb, 'Stylomastoid foramen', 'The facial nerve leaves the skull via the stylomastoid foramen after traversing the internal acoustic meatus and the facial canal. The jugular foramen transmits the IJV and CN IX, X, and XI.'),
('5e8bed19-55f6-5b2e-98a6-944fc4a9ca5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The greater petrosal nerve is a branch of:', '["Vagus (X)","Trigeminal (V)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The greater petrosal nerve arises from the geniculate ganglion of the facial nerve and carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the pterygopalatine ganglion. It does not arise from CN V, IX, X, or XII.'),
('bff1134a-b20a-5e3c-862b-51869c209087'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The nerve to stapedius arises from:', '["Facial nerve (tympanic segment)","Accessory nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (tympanic segment)', 'The nerve to stapedius leaves the facial nerve in its tympanic segment, within the facial canal. Its involvement explains why facial nerve lesions cause hyperacusis.'),
('d49596b0-fc2d-501b-880e-46f05981e819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The chorda tympani carries:', '["Motor to stapedius resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular gland","Motor fibers to tongue mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensation to TM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic to parotid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular gland', 'The chorda tympani conveys special sensory taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular and sublingual ganglia. It does not supply stapedius, the parotid, or the tympanic membrane.'),
('7055592b-6f62-5153-8ffd-071fb40becfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The mastoid process is pneumatized by:', '["Growth from the external auditory meatus","Extension of air cells from the mastoid antrum","Direct invagination from the middle ear","Peripheral sinuses","The eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of air cells from the mastoid antrum', 'Pneumatization occurs as air cells grow from the mastoid antrum into the mastoid process. The antrum, which lies behind the middle ear, is the source of these cells rather than the external meatus, peripheral sinuses, or eustachian tube.'),
('20e121de-69ce-5f91-b37e-f3fadaa1ec44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Bezold''s abscess is a complication of:', '["Otitis externa resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Labyrinthitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Sinusitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mastoiditis (pus tracking into sternocleidomastoid sheath)"]'::jsonb, 'Mastoiditis (pus tracking into sternocleidomastoid sheath)', 'In Bezold''s abscess, mastoid pus erodes through the bone (usually the medial tip) and tracks deep to the sternocleidomastoid, forming a neck abscess. It is a recognized complication of mastoiditis, not of otitis externa, facial palsy, labyrinthitis, or sinusitis.'),
('e8f959b2-bca6-5201-947f-040e850ff3a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Gradenigo''s syndrome involves:', '["Otitis media","facial pain (V1)","and lateral rectus palsy (VI)","Hearing loss","vertigo"]'::jsonb, 'Otitis media', 'Gradenigo''s triad consists of otitis media, pain in the trigeminal (V1) distribution, and sixth nerve palsy causing lateral rectus weakness; it follows middle-ear infection spreading to the petrous apex. The underlying condition is acute otitis media with petrous apicitis.'),
('952f8293-64fd-5c1e-9ede-2ca130540587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The pinna develops from:', '["Fourth branchial arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Third branchial arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First branchial arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Second branchial arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First and second branchial arches (hillocks of His)"]'::jsonb, 'First and second branchial arches (hillocks of His)', 'The auricle forms from the fusion of the six hillocks of His, which arise from the first and second branchial (pharyngeal) arches around the first cleft. The third and fourth arches do not contribute to the pinna.'),
('0c8f92cb-7326-527a-813f-ff0d788d4887'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Preauricular sinus is due to:', '["Infection of the ear canal","Trauma","Branchial cleft cyst","Skin tag","Incomplete fusion of hillocks of His"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete fusion of hillocks of His', 'A preauricular sinus results from incomplete fusion of the auricular hillocks of His during development of the pinna from the first and second branchial arches. Infection, trauma, and branchial cleft cysts are not the developmental cause.'),
('61584f04-ee05-5868-8620-ceb7ccd5596a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The middle ear develops from:', '["Neural crest","First pharyngeal pouch","Third pharyngeal pouch","First branchial cleft","Second pharyngeal pouch"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal pouch', 'The tympanic cavity and auditory tube arise from the endoderm-lined first pharyngeal pouch. The third pouch gives the thymus and inferior parathyroid glands, while the first cleft forms the external auditory meatus.'),
('b6a867f2-63e0-57a1-a70f-c60c8973a811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The inner ear develops from:', '["Ectodermal otic placode","Neural crest","Mesoderm","Notochord","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectodermal otic placode', 'The membranous labyrinth differentiates from the ectodermal otic placode, which invaginates to form the otic vesicle. Neither mesoderm, endoderm, notochord, nor neural crest gives rise to the inner ear sensory structures.'),
('4493274b-c274-5819-9bf1-b159b9483e84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The ossicles develop from:', '["Third branchial arch","First and second branchial arches","Neural crest","Otic capsule","Notochord"]'::jsonb, 'First and second branchial arches', 'The malleus and incus derive from the first arch cartilage and the stapes largely from the second arch, with neural crest cells contributing. The third arch, otic capsule, and notochord are not the source of the ossicles.'),
('61714ca9-4177-5973-9fdf-c231e0d3c46a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The vestibular aqueduct contains:', '["Artery","Perilymphatic duct","Facial nerve","Cochlear nerve","Endolymphatic duct and sac"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymphatic duct and sac', 'The vestibular aqueduct is the bony canal that transmits the endolymphatic duct and its terminal sac. It does not carry the facial or cochlear nerves, an artery, or a perilymphatic duct.'),
('0ac18dee-c8d9-5343-8d45-43b92bd23fb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The cochlear aqueduct connects:', '["Cochlea to saccule","Vestibule to middle ear","Scala media to vestibule","Scala vestibuli to oval window","Scala tympani to subarachnoid space (CSF)"]'::jsonb, 'Scala tympani to subarachnoid space (CSF)', 'The cochlear aqueduct is a bony channel that carries the perilymphatic duct, connecting the scala tympani of the cochlea to the subarachnoid space. It does not join the saccule, vestibule, or middle ear.'),
('8f52548a-359c-5d79-90df-defdf25ae97b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic segment of facial nerve lies:', '["In the geniculate ganglion","Below the oval window","In the mastoid","Above the oval window","In the internal acoustic meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Above the oval window', 'Within the middle ear the facial nerve runs horizontally in the facial canal along the medial wall, passing above the oval window before turning inferiorly. The geniculate ganglion and internal acoustic meatus lie more proximally, and the mastoid segment is the vertical part.'),
('8abcb288-a652-5896-ba4c-38925c78d4c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The mastoid segment of facial nerve lies:', '["In the IAM","Horizontal in the middle ear","In the cochlea","Vertical in the mastoid bone","In the parotid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical in the mastoid bone', 'After the second genu the facial nerve descends vertically through the mastoid bone, giving the stapedius and chorda tympani branches, and exits via the stylomastoid foramen. The tympanic segment is the horizontal part in the middle ear.'),
('3e0c4fbc-6952-540f-8d39-96be02fe49a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The semicircular canals open into:', '["Endolymphatic duct","Vestibule directly","Utricle via 5 openings","Saccule directly","Cochlea directly"]'::jsonb, 'Utricle via 5 openings', 'The three semicircular canals open into the utricle through five openings, because the anterior and posterior canals share a common (crus commune) limb. They do not drain into the saccule, endolymphatic duct, or cochlea.'),
('a5f4ace0-b03f-543e-b8a7-84b4d888d87f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The function of the tympanic membrane is to:', '["Filter noise","Transmit vibrations to ossicles","Equalize pressure","Protect inner ear","Amplify sound"]'::jsonb, 'Transmit vibrations to ossicles', 'The tympanic membrane converts air-borne sound waves into mechanical vibrations that it passes to the malleus handle and the ossicular chain. Pressure equalization is the role of the auditory tube, and the membrane itself does not filter noise or amplify sound.'),
('9a24cadc-ff1c-536e-9297-af28037b4ebb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The auditory tube connects the:', '["Nasopharynx to larynx","Inner ear to middle ear","Middle ear to nasopharynx","Middle ear to inner ear","Outer ear to middle ear"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear to nasopharynx', 'The pharyngotympanic (auditory) tube runs from the anterior wall of the tympanic cavity to the nasopharynx, allowing pressure equilibration. It does not connect the middle ear to the inner or outer ear, nor the nasopharynx to the larynx.'),
('19ab484c-7202-518c-a8d1-b74afa9c343b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The organ of Corti is located in the:', '["Tympanic cavity","Semicircular canal","Vestibule","Cochlear duct","Eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear duct', 'The spiral organ of Corti, the receptor for hearing, lies on the basilar membrane inside the cochlear duct (scala media). The semicircular canals and vestibule house balance receptors, not the organ of Corti.'),
('fe87d573-0d13-516d-a0b7-f5442ba954e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The ossicles of the middle ear include:', '["Hammer, anvil, handle","Incus, stapes, tympanum","Malleus, stapes, cochlea","Incus, malleus, cochlea","Malleus, incus, stapes"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus, incus, stapes', 'The three middle-ear ossicles are the malleus, incus, and stapes, bridging the tympanic membrane to the oval window. The cochlea is an inner-ear structure and the tympanum is the membrane, so they are not ossicles.'),
('3683ad7c-0555-5dc7-9eac-e27ddbe5b2a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which cranial nerve carries auditory information?', '["Vagus nerve (X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII)', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) transmits both auditory (cochlear) and vestibular information to the brainstem. The facial, vagus, glossopharyngeal, and trigeminal nerves serve other motor and sensory functions.'),
('9235228a-b539-5189-b153-362bab0f9efc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In adults, the external auditory meatus measures about:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The adult external auditory meatus is approximately 2.5 cm long from the concha to the tympanic membrane. It is composed of an outer cartilaginous third and an inner bony two-thirds.'),
('81bb7ada-7470-5dbd-bdcf-148496ffa489'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('8071e3e8-1702-5a6f-9bca-46acc1ee89f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Identify the correct statement about the external auditory meatus in adults:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The external auditory meatus in adults is about 2.5 cm long, joining the concha to the tympanic membrane. None of the other lengths approximates the normal adult canal length.'),
('75a2dafe-8b53-54a1-ab29-f65ccbc0e342'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The external auditory meatus in adults measures about:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The normal adult external auditory meatus is approximately 2.5 cm long. Values of 1 cm, 3.5 cm, 4 cm, and 5 cm do not match this typical canal length.'),
('0817fe3e-3131-586f-b56b-68b38ce92495'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In the study of Ear Temporal Bone, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('77a7f1d7-f23c-5411-9df1-7d50863337f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The lateral 1/3 of the external auditory meatus is:', '["Fibrous","Muscular","Bony","Cartilaginous","Membranous"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilaginous', 'The outer third of the external auditory meatus is supported by elastic cartilage continuous with the auricular cartilage. Its medial two-thirds is formed by bone (tympanic part of the temporal bone), not fibrous, muscular, or membranous tissue.'),
('7ccf29de-020a-5893-b15a-ae3f759e6555'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('91b8d8b4-41e0-5945-b35a-1018c12be9f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('b6998fd0-c35f-51f0-96d7-21e8dae54061'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases in Ear Temporal Bone: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('60507591-eaba-539a-bd7d-41690aac9048'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In the study of Ear Temporal Bone, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('1f75bfdf-2560-52d5-875e-f39feaf82a81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Ear Temporal Bone: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('f1f830bc-52dc-5365-9862-225cacd1d29f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('c3c6b924-7f09-5cf8-94f2-260b25b20ee4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('bcc7ded9-37fb-5b55-8afc-00f06fed8ae6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases in Ear Temporal Bone: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('e0308ec8-5a3a-5259-bc5a-43cc79f464c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The pars flaccida of the tympanic membrane is also called:', '["Tense part","Shrapnell''s membrane","Scarpa''s membrane","Rivinus membrane","Relaxed part"]'::jsonb, 'Shrapnell''s membrane', 'The pars flaccida is the small lax superior region of the tympanic membrane and is commonly called Shrapnell''s membrane. It is the relaxed part, in contrast to the tense pars tensa, and is not termed Scarpa''s membrane.'),
('e7344cb2-ae3a-5e61-8eb9-1c4c3a7e53b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The umbo of the tympanic membrane is:', '["The most depressed point at the tip of malleus handle","The attachment of incus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The center of pars flaccida mediated by specific receptor activation","The upper part of TM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower part of TM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most depressed point at the tip of malleus handle', 'The umbo is the innermost point of the tympanic membrane, at the tip of the malleus handle where it is pulled medially. It is not the incus attachment, the pars flaccida, or simply the upper or lower part of the membrane.'),
('0d869abd-11fc-5196-b2bd-f8958e70a402'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('32ae7fbe-012c-5c2c-bb4b-63fa6b1e2897'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('a1308288-a682-55c0-ba94-9d1d572f47f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases in Ear Temporal Bone: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('5e2a74b2-68c1-533f-99db-70b013e585b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In the study of Ear Temporal Bone, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('762e02a2-39bb-5246-8a54-0594d74379e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Ear Temporal Bone: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('ede0c3fb-6c73-529d-a612-1433a62c867e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('28a531f3-f4ec-5113-adf4-7ffa8f905009'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('c6fefcbc-bfcb-5d18-b4de-1b718f67552c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases in Ear Temporal Bone: EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('c72e6d66-6588-50bf-9c13-c77f95409c81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The roof (tegmental wall) of the tympanic cavity is:', '["Aditus to mastoid antrum resulting in decreased functional activity","Promontory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jugular bulb mediated by specific receptor activation","Carotid canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tegmen tympani (thin plate of petrous temporal)"]'::jsonb, 'Tegmen tympani (thin plate of petrous temporal)', 'The roof of the tympanic cavity is formed by the tegmen tympani, a thin plate of the petrous temporal bone that also covers the mastoid antrum. The jugular bulb, carotid canal, promontory, and aditus belong to other walls.'),
('36522064-2c4a-53c4-ace7-7ed64b6d891f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The floor (jugular wall) of the tympanic cavity relates to:', '["Sigmoid sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior bulb of internal jugular vein","Carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior bulb of internal jugular vein', 'The floor (jugular wall) of the tympanic cavity lies over the superior bulb of the internal jugular vein. The facial nerve, sigmoid sinus, carotid artery, and cochlea are not located beneath this wall.'),
('8e564886-5a5f-547d-9fc0-c33dd7237f13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The medial wall (labyrinthine) of the tympanic cavity contains:', '["Aditus ad antrum","Mastoid cells","Promontory (basal turn of cochlea) oval window","round window prominence of facial canal","Carotid canal Jugular bulb"]'::jsonb, 'Promontory (basal turn of cochlea) oval window', 'The medial, labyrinthine wall shows the promontory over the basal turn of the cochlea and contains the oval window, round window, and prominence of the facial canal. The aditus to the mastoid antrum lies on the posterior wall, not the medial wall.'),
('132a1eee-4929-56b9-9b71-ca0b90d866ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The lateral wall (membranous) of the tympanic cavity is:', '["Tegmental wall","Tympanic membrane","Mastoid wall","Carotid wall","Jugular wall"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic membrane', 'The lateral, membranous wall of the tympanic cavity is formed largely by the tympanic membrane. The roof is the tegmental wall, the anterior wall is carotid, and the floor is the jugular wall.'),
('751b8fda-0e69-5613-8bed-ff6ff5f3540f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The anterior wall (carotid) of the tympanic cavity relates to:', '["Mastoid cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cochlea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal carotid artery and auditory tube","Jugular vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid artery and auditory tube', 'The anterior (carotid) wall is a thin bony plate adjacent to the internal carotid artery and bears the opening of the auditory tube. The jugular vein, facial nerve, and cochlea are not located anteriorly.'),
('7e2fd7ce-1fca-587a-bfaa-beea01864359'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The posterior wall (mastoid) of the tympanic cavity contains:', '["Promontory","Carotid canal","Oval window","Aditus to mastoid antrum","pyramidal eminence facial nerve canal Jugular bulb"]'::jsonb, 'Aditus to mastoid antrum', 'The posterior (mastoid) wall opens into the mastoid antrum through the aditus, and also bears the pyramidal eminence for the stapedius. The promontory and oval window are on the medial wall, and the carotid canal is anterior.'),
('b1e241e0-f7ce-53e3-97ab-ae555936f680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Ear Temporal Bone: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('1714239e-1222-5e17-8f24-aca07f37ddd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('8d8e7e4a-e65a-573f-9685-5aa0a9c665b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: mastoiditis is:?', '["Infection of ear canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection of mastoid air cells (complication of otitis media)","Infection of inner ear mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection of pinna without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection of external ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection of mastoid air cells (complication of otitis media)', 'Mastoiditis is an infection of the mastoid air cells, usually a complication of acute otitis media because these cells drain into the middle ear. Infection of the pinna, ear canal, or inner ear occurs at different sites and routes.'),
('6770d779-f0ac-5430-9442-6b809057a1c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the auditory tube (eustachian tube) connects:?', '["Middle ear to external ear","Ear to oral cavity","Ear to nose","Middle ear (tympanic cavity) to nasopharynx","Inner ear to middle ear"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear (tympanic cavity) to nasopharynx', 'The auditory tube runs from the anterior wall of the middle ear (tympanic cavity) to the nasopharynx, equalizing pressure and draining secretions. It never joins the external ear, inner ear, nose, or oral cavity.'),
('220e1581-a9ab-5fef-af6e-74800ffae54b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the auditory tube functions to:?', '["Transmit sound resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventilate the mastoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connect ear to nose mediated by specific receptor activation","Drain middle ear secretions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Equalize pressure between middle ear and atmosphere"]'::jsonb, 'Equalize pressure between middle ear and atmosphere', 'By opening into the nasopharynx, the auditory tube admits air so the pressure in the tympanic cavity matches atmospheric pressure. Sound is transmitted by the ossicular chain, and the tube is not the route for draining secretions or ventilating the mastoid alone.'),
('89d3d6bd-a3e8-5236-a1d7-ac1acca3de3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The auditory tube is longer in:'' is:', '["Same length in all","Adults (3.5 cm) than in children","Males than females","Children than adults","Females than males"]'::jsonb, 'Adults (3.5 cm) than in children', 'The auditory tube measures about 3.5 cm in adults but is shorter and more horizontal in children, which predisposes children to otitis media. Length is not primarily a function of sex.'),
('9d4da608-60a2-54f8-b8e5-e3316f0241ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the muscles that open the auditory tube are:?', '["Stylopharyngeus and palatopharyngeus","Tensor veli palatini and salpingopharyngeus","Palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus","Musculus uvulae and levator veli","Levator veli palatini and tensor veli"]'::jsonb, 'Tensor veli palatini and salpingopharyngeus', 'The tensor veli palatini and salpingopharyngeus dilate the auditory tube by acting on its cartilaginous wall. Palatoglossus, palatopharyngeus, levator veli, and musculus uvulae do not open the tube.'),
('1b69c213-b305-5c38-b063-61f30c145bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the middle ear contains which nerves?', '["Facial nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Chorda tympani (facial nerve branch) and tympanic plexus (glossopharyngeal)","Trigeminal nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglossal nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve branch) and tympanic plexus (glossopharyngeal)', 'The tympanic cavity contains the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve that carries taste fibers, and the tympanic plexus formed from the glossopharyngeal nerve. The trigeminal and hypoglossal nerves do not run within the middle ear.'),
('8f57e6a4-6796-50dd-a4d7-d4573f298dce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the chorda tympani crosses the tympanic cavity:?', '["Between the incus and stapes","Between the malleus and incus","Through the round window","Under the promontory","Through the oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Between the malleus and incus', 'After entering the middle ear, the chorda tympani passes medial to the tympanic membrane, between the malleus and the incus, before exiting via the petrotympanic fissure. It does not cross through the oval or round window.'),
('ce88e7a2-7e09-54ca-a2d8-30fba1f2d5cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the tympanic plexus is formed by:?', '["Tympanic branch of glossopharyngeal (Jacobson''s nerve) and sympathetics from internal carotid plexus","Vagus and facial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only glossopharyngeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminal and facial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic branch of glossopharyngeal (Jacobson''s nerve) and sympathetics from internal carotid plexus', 'The tympanic plexus on the promontory is formed by the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s nerve) together with sympathetic fibers from the internal carotid plexus. It supplies the middle-ear mucosa and gives rise to the lesser petrosal nerve.'),
('f62d0cab-f58c-5f4d-89ca-1715cc9097e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the lesser petrosal nerve arises from:?', '["Cochlear nerve","Facial nerve","Tympanic plexus (fibers from CN IX)","Geniculate ganglion","Otic ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic plexus (fibers from CN IX)', 'The lesser petrosal nerve arises from the tympanic plexus, which is formed by fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve. It carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion for the parotid gland.'),
('c96f6545-f4d1-57c3-810c-78d922045e67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The inner ear (labyrinth) consists of:'' is:', '["Only semicircular canals","Only cochlea","Cochlea and auditory nerve","Cochlea (hearing)","vestibule and semicircular canals (balance) Vestibule and ossicles"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlea (hearing)', 'The labyrinth comprises the cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals. The cochlea mediates hearing, while balance resides in the vestibule and semicircular canals, so the cochlea is the correct inner-ear component paired with hearing.'),
('fd37a71e-a16e-5d58-9054-76b38b9d745f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the bony labyrinth contains:?', '["Perilymph","Blood","Mucus","Endolymph","Serous fluid"]'::jsonb, 'Perilymph', 'The bony labyrinth is lined by membrane and contains perilymph, a fluid similar to extracellular fluid. The membranous labyrinth suspended inside contains endolymph instead.'),
('d0e34d7e-53fd-586d-b188-ba3e152ea611'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the membranous labyrinth contains:?', '["Cerebrospinal fluid","Perilymph","Blood","Endolymph","Mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymph', 'The membranous labyrinth holds endolymph, a potassium-rich fluid, whereas perilymph occupies the space between the bony and membranous labyrinths. Blood, mucus, and CSF are not labyrinthine fluids.'),
('b9dc4952-1b18-5a9f-99e4-d0f6870235ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the cochlea is responsible for:?', '["Hearing (sound transduction)","Head position sensing","Rotational acceleration sensing","Linear acceleration sensing","Balance"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing (sound transduction)', 'The cochlea houses the organ of Corti and performs hearing through sound transduction. The vestibule and semicircular canals mediate balance rather than hearing.'),
('e5c8f5db-ac74-5e76-b441-19023710b9cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the semicircular canals are responsible for:?', '["Sound localization resulting in decreased functional activity","Gravity sensing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linear acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Rotational acceleration (angular movement)","Hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rotational acceleration (angular movement)', 'The semicircular canals detect rotational acceleration, or angular movement of the head. Linear acceleration and static head position are sensed by the utricle and saccule, and hearing by the cochlea.'),
('6c496633-795e-5094-b649-79b541fbaab1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the vestibule contains the utricle and saccule which are responsible for:?', '["Taste resulting in decreased functional activity","Linear acceleration and head position (static balance)","Rotational acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Sound conduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration and head position (static balance)', 'The maculae of the utricle and saccule detect linear acceleration and static head position. Rotational acceleration is the role of the semicircular canals, and hearing belongs to the cochlea.'),
('e22aa125-4da6-50bf-a0d2-4a801043ff45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The organ of Corti is located:'' is:', '["In the cochlear duct on the basilar membrane","In the saccule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the utricle mediated by specific receptor activation","In the semicircular canals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the vestibule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the cochlear duct on the basilar membrane', 'The organ of Corti, the receptor for hearing, lies within the cochlear duct (scala media) resting on the basilar membrane. It is not found in the saccule, utricle, semicircular canals, or vestibule.'),
('da7020d2-74b7-5f9b-8f4c-bdaddda3374d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the spiral ganglion contains:?', '["Motor neurons","Sympathetic neurons","Parasympathetic neurons","Cell bodies of vestibular nerve","Cell bodies of cochlear nerve (hearing)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell bodies of cochlear nerve (hearing)', 'The spiral ganglion in the modiolus houses the bipolar cell bodies of the cochlear nerve, which serves hearing. Vestibular nerve cell bodies are located separately in the vestibular (Scarpa''s) ganglion.'),
('67531f41-d069-5a2d-bcd9-9bf8591964dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the vestibular ganglion (scarpa''s ganglion) contains:?', '["Cell bodies of cochlear nerve","Parasympathetic neurons","Motor neurons","Sympathetic neurons","Cell bodies of vestibular nerve (balance)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell bodies of vestibular nerve (balance)', 'The vestibular (Scarpa''s) ganglion contains the bipolar cell bodies of the vestibular nerve, which serves balance. Cochlear nerve cell bodies lie in the spiral ganglion, and this ganglion does not house motor, sympathetic, or parasympathetic neurons.'),
('20d29b83-712a-5429-9fcf-879240ee5414'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the facial nerve runs through which part of temporal bone?', '["Tympanic cavity only","Facial canal in the petrous temporal","Mastoid only","Cochlea","Semicircular canals"]'::jsonb, 'Facial canal in the petrous temporal', 'The facial nerve travels through the facial canal within the petrous part of the temporal bone and exits at the stylomastoid foramen. The cochlea, semicircular canals, tympanic cavity alone, and mastoid alone are not its route.'),
('7114d0c0-2cd3-5525-9595-d753e7e6386c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the facial nerve has how many segments in the temporal bone?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 (tympanic and mastoid) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 (labyrinthine, tympanic, mastoid)","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (labyrinthine, tympanic, mastoid)', 'Within the temporal bone the facial nerve has three segments: labyrinthine, tympanic (horizontal), and mastoid (vertical). The geniculate ganglion lies at the end of the labyrinthine segment, so three is the correct count.'),
('5ee6a2af-b05e-5bbf-9d19-c11fe6a9a8da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the geniculate ganglion is located:?', '["In the tympanic cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","At the anterior end of labyrinthine segment of facial nerve","At the stylomastoid foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","In the internal acoustic meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the mastoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the anterior end of labyrinthine segment of facial nerve', 'The geniculate ganglion sits at the distal end of the labyrinthine segment of the facial nerve, where the nerve turns posteriorly, and contains the cell bodies of the nervus intermedius. It is not at the stylomastoid foramen, in the tympanic cavity, or in the internal acoustic meatus.'),
('2b5ebb59-8365-5ce5-a600-4c097ebd3a5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The internal acoustic meatus transmits:'' is:', '["VII","VIII","IX Only VII","Facial nerve (VII)","vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) labyrinthine artery VII and VIII Only VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The internal acoustic meatus transmits the facial nerve (VII) along with the vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) and the labyrinthine artery. Among the listed choices the facial nerve (VII) is a genuine content of the meatus.'),
('7265a2c2-22fc-5c52-b8ef-53fa973f2d4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the vestibulocochlear nerve (viii) divides into:?', '["Anterior and posterior divisions","Superior and inferior divisions","Sensory and motor divisions","Cochlear and vestibular divisions","Medial and lateral divisions"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear and vestibular divisions', 'As the vestibulocochlear nerve enters the internal acoustic meatus it divides into a cochlear division and a vestibular division. The cochlear division reaches the spiral ganglion and the vestibular division reaches Scarpa''s ganglion.'),
('ae0be642-1e27-58fc-940b-d72614e2cd1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the external ear is supplied by which nerves?', '["Only trigeminal","Only facial nerve","Only vagus","Auricular branch of vagus (Arnold''s nerve)","auriculotemporal (V3) great auricular (C2-3) Only great auricular"]'::jsonb, 'Auricular branch of vagus (Arnold''s nerve)', 'The external ear receives sensory supply from several nerves, including the auriculotemporal nerve (V3), the great auricular nerve (C2-C3), and the auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold''s nerve). Arnold''s nerve is a genuine contributor listed among the options.'),
('5fd3853f-f65f-5c23-babd-09c64c477470'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: arnold''s nerve (auricular branch of vagus) supplies:?', '["The inner ear only resulting in decreased functional activity","The tympanic membrane only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The middle ear only mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior wall and floor of external auditory meatus and adjacent pinna","The entire pinna leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior wall and floor of external auditory meatus and adjacent pinna', 'The auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold''s nerve) supplies the posterior wall and floor of the external auditory meatus and the adjoining part of the pinna. Its stimulation in the canal produces the ear-cough reflex.'),
('d9ec3814-4d1c-50be-88ac-9a30c2e224bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: stimulation of arnold''s nerve can cause:?', '["Tinnitus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cough or gag reflex (ear-cough reflex)","Facial pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertigo without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cough or gag reflex (ear-cough reflex)', 'Stimulation of the auricular branch of the vagus in the external auditory meatus triggers the ear-cough reflex, a cough or gag, because the fibers are vagal. This reflex is not mediated by changes in hearing, balance, or facial sensation.'),
('f629040e-e917-55dd-a2f8-793b3d4fcf34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the tympanic membrane is supplied by:?', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only glossopharyngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Auriculotemporal (V3) and auricular branch of vagus (X)","Only trigeminal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal (V3) and auricular branch of vagus (X)', 'The external surface of the tympanic membrane is innervated by the auriculotemporal nerve (V3) and the auricular branch of the vagus (X), while its internal surface receives the tympanic plexus from CN IX. Neither the facial nor the hypoglossal nerve supplies the membrane.'),
('5e97d552-6ef2-57e9-babe-c339b25a41ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The temporal bone consists of how many parts'' is:', '["4 parts (squamous, tympanic, mastoid, petrous)","6 parts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 parts mediated by specific receptor activation","3 parts (squamous, tympanic, petrous) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 parts (squamous, tympanic, mastoid, petrous)', 'The temporal bone is formed of four parts: squamous, tympanic, mastoid, and petrous. The styloid process develops from the second pharyngeal arch rather than representing a fifth major part here.'),
('c7ed7374-d0dd-57dc-bd36-6896b8ec407b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the petrous part of temporal bone contains:?', '["Tympanic membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Only air cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External auditory meatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Inner ear structures (cochlea, vestibule, semicircular canals)","Auditory ossicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner ear structures (cochlea, vestibule, semicircular canals)', 'The petrous part of the temporal bone is dense bone that houses the inner ear, namely the cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals, along with the middle ear and facial canal. The tympanic membrane and ossicles belong to the middle ear, not the petrous part itself.'),
('c3f80cae-68f8-59c2-b004-442bf6f7968b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the mastoid process develops when?', '["Before birth resulting in decreased functional activity","At age 2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In adulthood mediated by specific receptor activation","At puberty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","After birth (develops from pull of SCM)"]'::jsonb, 'After birth (develops from pull of SCM)', 'The mastoid process is absent at birth and develops after birth under the pull of the sternocleidomastoid muscle, with pneumatization of air cells progressing through early childhood. It does not appear before birth or suddenly in adulthood.'),
('ebfc238a-d7c1-53ff-b415-ca1db005414e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: mastoid process may be absent in:?', '["Elderly resulting in decreased functional activity","Adults accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infants and young children","Males without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infants and young children', 'Because the mastoid process develops postnatally with the pull of the sternocleidomastoid and gradual pneumatization, it is small or absent in infants and young children. In adults it is fully formed.'),
('281ca69e-6655-54d8-98c3-8a0a2effadd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the stylomastoid foramen is located between:?', '["Tympanic and squamous","Styloid and mastoid processes","Mastoid and tympanic parts","Styloid and petrous","Petrous and squamous"]'::jsonb, 'Styloid and mastoid processes', 'The stylomastoid foramen lies on the inferior surface of the temporal bone between the styloid process and the mastoid process. It is the exit point of the facial nerve and is not a junction of the petrous, squamous, or tympanic parts.'),
('28245b42-870c-5b5b-9494-e76fb519317d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the stylomastoid foramen transmits:?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Only facial nerve","Facial and trigeminal","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve and stylomastoid artery"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve and stylomastoid artery', 'The stylomastoid foramen transmits the facial nerve, which exits the skull here, together with the stylomastoid artery, a branch of the posterior auricular artery. The glossopharyngeal, hypoglossal, and trigeminal nerves do not pass through it.'),
('88dbe62e-c33a-5e16-8c09-120e43dad9e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The carotid canal is located in:'' is:', '["Occipital bone","Petrous part of temporal bone","Mastoid part","Tympanic part","Squamous part"]'::jsonb, 'Petrous part of temporal bone', 'The carotid canal courses through the petrous part of the temporal bone, carrying the internal carotid artery into the cranial cavity. The occipital, mastoid, tympanic, and squamous parts do not contain this canal.'),
('445be056-ff6e-5249-850b-f0accc410c20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the internal carotid artery enters the skull through:?', '["Foramen lacerum","Foramen magnum","Carotid canal","Foramen ovale","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid canal', 'The internal carotid artery passes into the skull through the carotid canal in the petrous temporal bone. The foramen ovale carries V3, the jugular foramen transmits the sigmoid sinus and lower cranial nerves, and the foramen lacerum is largely filled with cartilage.'),
('ccdfd491-1d51-5470-873c-cbe02ea4d287'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the jugular foramen is between:?', '["Frontal and parietal","Occipital and parietal","Petrous temporal and occipital bones","Tympanic and squamous","Temporal and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Petrous temporal and occipital bones', 'The jugular foramen is formed between the petrous part of the temporal bone and the occipital bone at the base of the skull. It transmits the internal jugular vein and cranial nerves IX, X, and XI.'),
('029eaac4-591e-5cd3-876c-aae46f2ef566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the jugular foramen transmits:?', '["Only IJV All cranial nerves","IJV and facial nerve IX","X XI only","Internal jugular vein glossopharyngeal (IX)","vagus (X) accessory (XI)"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular vein glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The jugular foramen transmits the internal jugular vein and the glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), and accessory (XI) nerves. The pairing of the internal jugular vein with the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) is accurate among the given options.'),
('b660fd6a-8248-5441-8171-c7f86d39cf9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the middle ear infection (otitis media) is common in children because:?', '["Auditory tube is shorter and more horizontal","Auditory tube is longer","Eustachian tube is narrower","TM is thicker","Immune system is stronger"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory tube is shorter and more horizontal', 'In children the auditory tube is shorter, wider, and more horizontal, so nasopharyngeal organisms reflux more readily into the middle ear. The adult tube is longer and more vertical, which reduces this risk.'),
('6884a13b-e1b7-53ae-afe0-3ca0e9058f0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: cholesteatoma is:?', '["A type of tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A cyst of the pinna accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A collection of keratinized squamous epithelium in the middle ear (from TM retraction)","An infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A collection of fluid in the inner ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of keratinized squamous epithelium in the middle ear (from TM retraction)', 'A cholesteatoma is a collection of keratinized squamous epithelium within the middle ear, arising from a retraction pocket of the tympanic membrane. It may erode the ossicles and even the facial canal, and it is not a neoplasm, cyst of the pinna, simple infection, or fluid collection.'),
('b8d0390c-1f76-5d59-9ac1-2a654744e6e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The conductive hearing loss in otosclerosis results from:'' is:', '["Auditory nerve damage","Auditory tube obstruction","Stapes fixation at the oval window","Cochlear damage","TM perforation"]'::jsonb, 'Stapes fixation at the oval window', 'In otosclerosis abnormal bone remodeling fixates the stapes footplate in the oval window, blocking sound transmission to the inner ear and causing conductive hearing loss. Damage to the cochlea or auditory nerve would instead produce sensorineural loss.'),
('aa0d489d-e944-57fb-996f-1ae155377c42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: sensorineural hearing loss results from damage to:?', '["Cochlea or cochlear nerve (hair cells or nerve)","External ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tympanic membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","Ossicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlea or cochlear nerve (hair cells or nerve)', 'Sensorineural hearing loss results from injury to the cochlea or the cochlear (auditory) nerve. Damage at the external canal, tympanic membrane, ossicles, or middle ear produces conductive hearing loss instead.'),
('dd83742a-9058-5a2b-b226-92c12b4a099e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the round window (fenestra cochleae) is closed by:?', '["Auditory tube","Stapes footplate","Incus","Malleus","Secondary tympanic membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary tympanic membrane', 'The round window (fenestra cochleae) is closed by the secondary tympanic membrane. The oval window is closed by the stapes footplate, so the two windows must not be confused.'),
('98b73a50-560d-5197-ad75-2dc1a8df92a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the oval window (fenestra vestibuli) is closed by:?', '["Secondary tympanic membrane","Footplate of stapes","Incus","Tympanic membrane","Malleus"]'::jsonb, 'Footplate of stapes', 'The oval window (fenestra vestibuli) is sealed by the footplate of the stapes, which transmits ossicular vibrations to the perilymph. The round window is closed by the secondary tympanic membrane, not by any ossicle.'),
('06e8e6d6-9209-53d0-9417-427b54f2b68f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the chorda tympani leaves the skull through:?', '["Petrotympanic fissure","Jugular foramen","Foramen spinosum","Foramen ovale","Stylomastoid foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Petrotympanic fissure', 'After crossing the tympanic cavity, the chorda tympani exits the skull through the petrotympanic fissure to join the lingual nerve. The jugular foramen, foramen ovale, foramen spinosum, and stylomastoid foramen are not its exit route.'),
('aea395dc-d2a5-5916-9b1d-fb26af7fa932'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the mastoid antrum is present at:?', '["Age 5","Adulthood","Adolescence","Birth","Age 2"]'::jsonb, 'Birth', 'The mastoid antrum is present at birth, even though the mastoid process develops later. The air cells become pneumatized progressively during early childhood, so antral presence at birth is correct.'),
('396f6a6d-4d0b-5d76-95a1-36067d356fec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The aditus ad antrum connects:'' is:', '["Tympanic cavity to mastoid antrum","Middle ear to nasopharynx","Tympanic cavity to auditory tube","External ear to middle ear","Mastoid antrum to mastoid cells"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic cavity to mastoid antrum', 'The aditus ad antrum is the opening that connects the epitympanic recess of the tympanic cavity to the mastoid antrum. It does not connect the middle ear to the nasopharynx, external canal, or individual air cells.'),
('2943ea67-37e9-5bea-b469-58da06c2b9f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the pyramidal eminence contains:?', '["Tensor tympani muscle","Incus","Chorda tympani","Stapedius muscle","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Stapedius muscle', 'The pyramidal eminence on the posterior wall of the tympanic cavity contains the stapedius muscle, whose tendon emerges from its apex to reach the stapes. It does not contain the tensor tympani, facial nerve, chorda tympani, or the incus.'),
('da632dac-e4cd-5b72-83d5-f2dfffa62969'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the tendon of stapedius attaches to:?', '["Oval window","Incus","Malleus handle","Neck of stapes","TM"]'::jsonb, 'Neck of stapes', 'The tendon of the stapedius muscle emerges from the pyramidal eminence and attaches to the neck of the stapes. Contraction of the muscle dampens excessive stapes movement; the tendon does not insert on the incus, oval window, or tympanic membrane.'),
('72788df9-c96c-5762-bc46-d2ab4c5196ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the tensor tympani muscle attaches to:?', '["Incus body","Neck of stapes","TM","Handle of malleus","Oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Handle of malleus', 'The tensor tympani arises from the cartilaginous auditory tube and adjacent bone and inserts into the handle of the malleus. Its contraction tenses the tympanic membrane; the incus, stapes, and oval window are not its attachment sites.'),
('cb8a568c-f118-5e84-9d6d-23d3bc8dc631'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: tensor tympani is supplied by:?', '["Trigeminal nerve (V3)","Hypoglossal","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V3)', 'The tensor tympani is innervated by the nerve to medial pterygoid, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). The facial, glossopharyngeal, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('fe3750cc-bc4b-5720-859e-1e75e80f5e5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The stapedius muscle is supplied by which nerve?', '["Trigeminal (V3)","Glossopharyngeal","Facial nerve (VII)","Vagus nerve","Hypoglossal"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The stapedius is innervated by the nerve to stapedius, a branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) that arises from its tympanic segment. The trigeminal (V3) mainly supplies the muscles of mastication and tensor tympani, the glossopharyngeal supplies the stylopharyngeus, and the vagus and hypoglossal do not supply middle-ear muscles.'),
('1dc7acb9-06d6-5aca-986b-56129101be62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('5d1ef0d6-457c-5e4d-88c1-6ef1b9df9927'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In the study of Ear Temporal Bone, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('b3d7e3ca-40ef-57d6-bb28-74f00e81b2dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('0c79dd08-f5bb-526d-84bf-6eb84acbd1b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which nerve provides taste sensation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue by passing through the middle ear?', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve (chorda tympani branch)","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (chorda tympani branch)', 'The chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve, traverses the middle ear between the malleus and incus and then joins the lingual nerve to carry taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. The glossopharyngeal serves the posterior third, the trigeminal conveys general sensation (not taste) from the tongue, and the hypoglossal and vagus play no role in taste.'),
('15f114cf-40d0-5c62-b7f7-6834dbbdd8e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('01a3a1d2-2258-526f-addf-1d2576195387'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The scala vestibuli and scala tympani communicate with each other via:', '["Saccule","Cochlear aqueduct","Oval window","Helicotrema","Round window"]'::jsonb, 'Helicotrema', 'At the apex of the cochlea the scala vestibuli and scala tympani are continuous through the helicotrema, allowing perilymph to flow between them. The oval and round windows are the two openings into the middle ear, the cochlear aqueduct connects the scala tympani to the subarachnoid space, and the saccule is a vestibular structure.'),
('cd46a78a-1ee3-5aaa-bfd4-767f929be3c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In the study of Ear Temporal Bone, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('86c0b1be-3557-589f-9d7f-642238822657'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('e0253019-b269-5cb8-af8d-619f9e3c8903'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('836941b3-5bf1-529d-a88a-38e3ff32da91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('1e0c1f63-c14e-5f38-b251-7ec2411fd40b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases in Ear Temporal Bone: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('27b43e2d-143f-56ce-8e85-b79ba9f338b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The cupula is found in:', '["Saccule","Semicircular canals","Cochlea","Utricle","Vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Semicircular canals', 'The cupula is a gelatinous partition, in which the hair cell stereocilia are embedded, that sits in the ampulla of each semicircular canal and is displaced by endolymph flow during rotation. The otolithic membranes of the utricle and saccule are the analogous structures, but the cupula itself is specific to the semicircular canals.'),
('767bb715-5b82-5aab-9c9b-681044b7e005'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The otolithic membrane is located in:', '["Cochlea","Tympanic cavity","Utricle and saccule","Semicircular canals","External auditory meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Utricle and saccule', 'The utricle and saccule each contain a macula covered by an otolithic membrane, a gelatinous layer embedded with calcium carbonate crystals (otoliths), which detects linear acceleration and head position. The semicircular canals instead have cupulae, and the cochlea, tympanic cavity, and external meatus do not contain otolithic membranes.'),
('120c8246-fa14-5331-9b5f-c5612c62a36a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('2599083c-08ef-57e4-a3bb-eda239a83bed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('7690e72e-ed9c-50fa-a7c7-c26a34921847'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases in Ear Temporal Bone: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('f9bcc913-64d6-55ba-a62a-db2400c78ab7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'High-frequency sounds are detected at which part of the cochlea?', '["Round window","Base of cochlea","Apex of cochlea","Helicotrema","Middle turn"]'::jsonb, 'Base of cochlea', 'The basilar membrane is narrow and stiff at the base of the cochlea, so high-frequency sounds produce maximal displacement there. Low frequencies resonate toward the wide, flexible apex, while the helicotrema and round window are not sites of frequency detection.'),
('3b6b584d-8984-55e6-92ab-16c0d00bee28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Low-frequency sounds are detected at which part of the cochlea?', '["Base of cochlea","Round window","Saccule","Apex of cochlea","Oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of cochlea', 'The basilar membrane is broad and compliant at the apex of the cochlea, where low-frequency tones produce their greatest displacement. High frequencies are localized at the stiff base, and the saccule, oval window, and round window are not sites of frequency analysis.'),
('eb4eafb4-9dbf-5623-a8c8-4c1d601500f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Ear Temporal Bone: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('0416d128-9602-567d-9727-14e7d406b06b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management of Ear Temporal Bone, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('7070674f-0738-587a-be11-946a414a24ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding clinical cases in Ear Temporal Bone: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('13e0c8cd-9020-5710-880a-079c9548bb3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The greater petrosal nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Vagus (X)","Trigeminal (V)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The greater petrosal nerve arises from the geniculate ganglion of the facial nerve and provides preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion to supply the lacrimal gland and nasal glands. The vagus, trigeminal, hypoglossal, and glossopharyngeal nerves do not give rise to this branch.'),
('2b1bab4f-0bfa-57eb-bd7a-ef952aeb618b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The nerve to stapedius arises from which segment of a cranial nerve?', '["Facial nerve (tympanic segment)","Accessory nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (tympanic segment)', 'The nerve to stapedius is a small branch of the facial nerve that arises from its tympanic segment within the facial canal and passes to the stapedius muscle. The trigeminal, glossopharyngeal, vagus, and accessory nerves do not supply the stapedius.'),
('d0307f74-888e-504f-9c46-45ffa5b572b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The chorda tympani carries which combination of fibers?', '["Motor to stapedius resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular gland","Motor fibers to tongue mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensation to TM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic to parotid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular gland', 'The chorda tympani carries special sensory (taste) fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in the submandibular ganglion to supply the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands. The stapedius is innervated by a separate facial branch, and preganglionic parasympathetic to the parotid travels via the glossopharyngeal nerve.'),
('de6f3737-73e3-5d2e-808a-0ae6d69dcd8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During therapeutic management, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('d365b68f-14f1-5016-89c4-9d0bfe18d583'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Bezold''s abscess is a complication of:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Otitis externa resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Labyrinthitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Sinusitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mastoiditis (pus tracking into sternocleidomastoid sheath)"]'::jsonb, 'Mastoiditis (pus tracking into sternocleidomastoid sheath)', 'Bezold''s abscess results when pus from mastoiditis erodes through the mastoid tip and tracks into the sheath of the sternocleidomastoid muscle, forming a neck abscess. It is not a complication of otitis externa, labyrinthitis, facial nerve palsy, or primary sinusitis.'),
('d39c0d84-701f-5b31-a671-ffe93ff0afea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Gradenigo''s syndrome involves:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Otitis media","facial pain (V1)","and lateral rectus palsy (VI)","Hearing loss","vertigo"]'::jsonb, 'Otitis media', 'Gradenigo''s syndrome is acute suppurative otitis media complicated by petrous apicitis, producing deep facial pain in the distribution of the trigeminal nerve and paralysis of the abducens nerve (lateral rectus palsy). The otitis media is the underlying condition, with the pain and sixth-nerve palsy as its recognized consequences.'),
('e1bd878d-8125-5ba6-a317-5e814ef4120d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The pinna develops from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Fourth branchial arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Third branchial arch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First branchial arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Second branchial arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First and second branchial arches (hillocks of His)"]'::jsonb, 'First and second branchial arches (hillocks of His)', 'The auricle forms from six hillocks of His that appear on the first (mandibular) and second (hyoid) branchial arches around the first pharyngeal cleft and fuse to shape the pinna. It does not derive from the third or fourth arches, which contribute to other pharyngeal structures.'),
('fbe95787-141e-5bc6-a04f-ed87a4eb55e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'A preauricular sinus is due to:', '["Infection of the ear canal","Trauma","Branchial cleft cyst","Skin tag","Incomplete fusion of hillocks of His"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete fusion of hillocks of His', 'A preauricular sinus arises from incomplete fusion of the six hillocks of His during formation of the auricle, leaving a small pit or tract near the anterior crus of the helix. It is a developmental defect, not the result of infection, trauma, a branchial cleft cyst, or a skin tag.'),
('90fd7f6c-9d6c-5035-b104-13dc4be71a7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The middle ear develops from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Neural crest","First pharyngeal pouch","Third pharyngeal pouch","First branchial cleft","Second pharyngeal pouch"]'::jsonb, 'First pharyngeal pouch', 'The middle ear cavity and auditory tube are derived from the first pharyngeal (tubotympanic) pouch, which expands to form the tympanic cavity while its narrow distal end forms the eustachian tube. The third and second pouches give rise to other pharyngeal derivatives, and the first cleft forms the external auditory meatus.'),
('77418ae7-9328-5236-b293-2eec6a943343'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The inner ear develops from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Ectodermal otic placode","Neural crest","Mesoderm","Notochord","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectodermal otic placode', 'The membranous labyrinth of the inner ear forms from the ectodermal otic placode, which invaginates to become the otic vesicle (otocyst) that differentiates into the cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals. It is not derived from neural crest, mesoderm, notochord, or endoderm.'),
('484b4f3e-40a8-5be0-bcb5-e62641c994fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The ossicles develop from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Third branchial arch","First and second branchial arches","Neural crest","Otic capsule","Notochord"]'::jsonb, 'First and second branchial arches', 'The malleus and incus form from the first branchial arch (Meckel''s cartilage) and the stapes from the second branchial arch (Reichert''s cartilage). The neural crest provides connective tissue coverings, the otic capsule forms the bony labyrinth, and the notochord contributes to the axial skeleton rather than the ossicles.'),
('ebd7d86d-aad1-5503-99cb-9850b958d17d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The vestibular aqueduct contains:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Artery","Perilymphatic duct","Facial nerve","Cochlear nerve","Endolymphatic duct and sac"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymphatic duct and sac', 'The vestibular aqueduct passes through the petrous temporal bone and houses the endolymphatic duct, which ends in the endolymphatic sac along the posterior cranial fossa. It does not contain an artery, the perilymphatic duct, or the facial and cochlear nerves, which travel in the internal auditory meatus.'),
('967ede82-3ee1-5f5d-b5a1-1a1ed7a3fd2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The cochlear aqueduct connects:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Cochlea to saccule","Vestibule to middle ear","Scala media to vestibule","Scala vestibuli to oval window","Scala tympani to subarachnoid space (CSF)"]'::jsonb, 'Scala tympani to subarachnoid space (CSF)', 'The cochlear aqueduct (perilymphatic duct) connects the scala tympani of the cochlea with the subarachnoid space near the jugular foramen, allowing perilymph to communicate with cerebrospinal fluid. It does not connect to the saccule, middle ear, vestibular apparatus, or oval window.'),
('35ee4bb8-42b7-589f-97da-f835572f3189'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The tympanic segment of the facial nerve lies:', '["In the geniculate ganglion","Below the oval window","In the mastoid","Above the oval window","In the internal acoustic meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Above the oval window', 'Within the facial canal the facial nerve passes horizontally through the medial wall of the middle ear just above the oval window, along the promontory. The geniculate ganglion is a bend rather than a segment position, the vertical (mastoid) segment lies behind the middle ear, and the internal acoustic meatus is the initial intracranial course.'),
('b0be372f-3b60-5413-aca6-c0bba2d3e2a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The mastoid segment of the facial nerve lies:', '["In the IAM","Horizontal in the middle ear","In the cochlea","Vertical in the mastoid bone","In the parotid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical in the mastoid bone', 'After turning at the posterior genu, the facial nerve descends vertically through the mastoid bone in the facial canal before exiting the stylomastoid foramen. It is not horizontal in the middle ear (that is the tympanic segment), and it does not pass through the cochlea, internal acoustic meatus at this stage, or the parotid until after leaving the skull.'),
('0844c1b3-acb6-5e41-aad2-cfc30ea607a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The semicircular canals open into:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Endolymphatic duct","Vestibule directly","Utricle via 5 openings","Saccule directly","Cochlea directly"]'::jsonb, 'Utricle via 5 openings', 'The three semicircular canals enter the utricle through five openings, because the anterior and posterior canals share a common crus. They do not drain directly into the vestibule, saccule, cochlea, or the endolymphatic duct.'),
('ddc5211f-8059-5666-a313-3b65874fbbed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The function of the tympanic membrane is to:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Filter noise","Transmit vibrations to ossicles","Equalize pressure","Protect inner ear","Amplify sound"]'::jsonb, 'Transmit vibrations to ossicles', 'The tympanic membrane converts sound waves into mechanical vibration and transmits them to the malleus and the rest of the ossicular chain. Pressure equalization is performed by the auditory tube to the middle ear, and the membrane does not filter noise, amplify sound, or provide the main protective barrier of the inner ear, though the ossicles later provide some impedance matching.'),
('25a58b63-350b-5161-aad2-9c5672753a92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The auditory tube connects the:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Nasopharynx to larynx","Inner ear to middle ear","Middle ear to nasopharynx","Middle ear to inner ear","Outer ear to middle ear"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear to nasopharynx', 'The auditory (eustachian) tube connects the anterior wall of the middle ear cavity to the lateral wall of the nasopharynx, allowing pressure equalization across the tympanic membrane. It does not connect the larynx, inner ear, or outer ear to the middle ear.'),
('6622248b-f30e-5c9b-bc08-1c4def68d0f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The organ of Corti is located in the:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Tympanic cavity","Semicircular canal","Vestibule","Cochlear duct","Eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear duct', 'The organ of Corti lies on the basilar membrane within the cochlear duct (scala media) of the cochlea. The tympanic cavity, semicircular canals, vestibule, and eustachian tube do not contain this auditory sense organ.'),
('c3eb87c2-8486-5f4b-8b95-56af053f8ab8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The ossicles of the middle ear include:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Hammer, anvil, handle","Incus, stapes, tympanum","Malleus, stapes, cochlea","Incus, malleus, cochlea","Malleus, incus, stapes"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus, incus, stapes', 'The three middle-ear ossicles are the malleus (hammer), incus (anvil), and stapes (stirrup), which form a chain from the tympanic membrane to the oval window. Options listing the cochlea, tympanum, or a handle omit or add structures that are not ossicles.'),
('a2064195-95d1-55ee-aef1-b12321798f49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Which cranial nerve carries auditory information?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Vagus nerve (X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII)', 'The cochlear division of the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) transmits auditory impulses from the hair cells of the organ of Corti to the brainstem. The facial, trigeminal, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves do not carry hearing.'),
('b6cc2b4a-8967-5b8d-975a-590ad426840e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The external auditory meatus in adults is approximately:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The adult external auditory meatus is about 2.5 cm long, running from the concha to the tympanic membrane, with its lateral third cartilaginous and its medial two-thirds bony. It is longer and relatively shorter than the infant meatus, which is short because the tympanic ring has not yet grown.'),
('cf10088c-3dc0-5c9d-b2af-57bea55939b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the external auditory meatus: in adults it is about:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The adult external auditory meatus measures approximately 2.5 cm in length from the auricle to the tympanic membrane. The lateral third is cartilaginous and the medial two-thirds is bony, a canal much longer than 1 cm and shorter than 3.5, 4, or 5 cm.'),
('29a9f12e-74e2-576d-a741-83a306ff3e10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Identify the correct response: the external auditory meatus in adults is about:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The adult external auditory meatus is roughly 2.5 cm long from the concha to the tympanic membrane. The value of 1 cm reflects newborn proportions, while 3.5 to 5 cm are too long for the adult canal.'),
('a2d498f5-d66d-5f50-a4f1-0f7ad0a7e9b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding The external auditory meatus in adults is about:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'In adults the external auditory meatus measures approximately 2.5 cm in length. The other values (1, 3.5, 4, and 5 cm) do not match the accepted adult length of the ear canal.'),
('a7b6eeb7-8c78-5cfd-b599-b4120bdf5bb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The length of the external auditory meatus in adults is approximately:', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The adult external auditory meatus is about 2.5 cm long, extending from the auricle to the tympanic membrane. The longer measurements and the 1 cm value do not reflect the adult canal length.'),
('c2bcb2ab-b4f9-5109-9879-f8e03cde67b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which option correctly describes the lateral third of the external auditory meatus?', '["Fibrous","Muscular","Bony","Cartilaginous","Membranous"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilaginous', 'The lateral third of the external auditory meatus is cartilaginous, formed by the cartilaginous meatus continuous with the auricle, while the medial two-thirds is bony. It is neither fibrous, muscular, nor membranous, and the bony portion lies medially.'),
('8d5acab5-f148-5ae9-b890-edb804352136'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'The lateral third of the external auditory meatus is:', '["Fibrous","Muscular","Bony","Cartilaginous","Membranous"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilaginous', 'The outer lateral third of the external auditory meatus is cartilaginous and contains hair follicles, ceruminous and sebaceous glands. The medial two-thirds is the bony part formed by the tympanic plate, so the lateral segment is not bony, fibrous, muscular, or membranous.'),
('b7e76158-e22b-5cf1-9f07-526cff08ecbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the external ear consists of:?', '["Only the ear canal","Only the pinna","Ear canal and middle ear","Auricle (pinna) and external auditory meatus","Pinna and tympanic membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Auricle (pinna) and external auditory meatus', 'The external ear consists of the auricle (pinna) and the external auditory meatus; the middle ear lies medial to the tympanic membrane. Options that add the middle ear or tympanic membrane, or name only a single component, give an incorrect or incomplete boundary.'),
('0d61e52c-a113-5d40-83ee-e78f3e719a0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the external auditory meatus extends from:?', '["Tympanic membrane to inner ear","Concha to tympanic membrane","Auricle to mastoid","Pinna to middle ear","Concha to nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Concha to tympanic membrane', 'The external auditory meatus runs from the concha of the auricle to the tympanic membrane, which forms its medial boundary. The inner ear, mastoid, and nasopharynx lie beyond the membrane or far from the conduit.'),
('aefc1a4a-ea56-5771-b1c3-9e83f5964187'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the external auditory meatus in adults is about:?', '["1 cm","2.5 cm long","3.5 cm","4 cm","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'In the adult the external auditory meatus is about 2.5 cm long, divided into a lateral cartilaginous third and medial bony two-thirds. The other values (1, 3.5, 4, 5 cm) do not match the accepted adult measurement.'),
('01a31499-0b6f-5e18-9c18-e050d3685178'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the lateral 1/3 of external auditory meatus is:?', '["Fibrous","Muscular","Bony","Cartilaginous","Membranous"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilaginous', 'The lateral third of the external auditory meatus is cartilaginous, continuous with the auricular cartilage, whereas the medial two-thirds is bony, formed by the tympanic part of the temporal bone. Fibrous, muscular, or membranous tissue does not form this segment.'),
('564d1188-d841-5989-99f7-c86a83ded32b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the skin lining the external auditory meatus contains:?', '["Sweat glands only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only stratified squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucous glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Ceruminous glands (modified sweat glands) and hair follicles","Only sebaceous glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ceruminous glands (modified sweat glands) and hair follicles', 'The skin of the cartilaginous meatus bears hair follicles and ceruminous glands (modified apocrine sweat glands) that secrete ear wax; the thin skin of the bony meatus lacks these glands. Mucous glands belong to the respiratory tract, not the meatus.'),
('33b8efeb-00dc-5490-b321-ecbcceb0988e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The tympanic membrane separates:'' is:', '["Pinna from ear canal","External ear from middle ear","Middle ear from inner ear","Mastoid from middle ear","External ear from inner ear"]'::jsonb, 'External ear from middle ear', 'The tympanic membrane separates the external auditory meatus from the middle ear (tympanic cavity), forming the lateral wall of the cavity. It does not lie between the pinna and canal, and the middle and inner ear are separated by the oval and round windows.'),
('f32e2aab-8c13-5e95-8c33-fc3065e18436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the tympanic membrane is divided into:?', '["Pars tensa (superior) and pars flaccida (inferior)","Pars tensa (inferior 4/5) and pars flaccida (superior 1/5)","Right and left halves","Anterior and posterior parts","Upper and lower thirds"]'::jsonb, 'Pars tensa (inferior 4/5) and pars flaccida (superior 1/5)', 'The tympanic membrane has a large inferior pars tensa (about 4/5) and a small superior pars flaccida (about 1/5) above the lateral process of the malleus. Divisions into halves or thirds, or reversing the positions of the two parts, do not reflect its anatomy.'),
('fd31686e-7d91-51e8-b1dc-b65a5cfcd107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the pars flaccida of tympanic membrane is also called:?', '["Tense part","Shrapnell''s membrane","Scarpa''s membrane","Rivinus membrane","Relaxed part"]'::jsonb, 'Shrapnell''s membrane', 'The pars flaccida, the small superior region of the tympanic membrane above the lateral process of the malleus, is called Shrapnell''s membrane; it lacks the radial and circular collagen fibers of the pars tensa. Scarpa''s and Rivinus membranes relate to other structures, and ''tense part'' describes the pars tensa instead.'),
('6a0f8ca2-72c0-5feb-8169-849e7fb590ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the umbo of tympanic membrane is:?', '["The most depressed point at the tip of malleus handle","The attachment of incus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The center of pars flaccida mediated by specific receptor activation","The upper part of TM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower part of TM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most depressed point at the tip of malleus handle', 'The umbo is the most depressed, central point of the tympanic membrane where the tip of the malleus handle retracts it inward. It marks the attachment of the malleus, not of the incus, and lies within the pars tensa rather than at the top or bottom of the membrane.'),
('0f276743-b9ba-592d-95cd-46f51eefb09a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the cone of light is:?', '["A reflection seen posterosuperiorly","The oval window","A reflection seen anteroinferiorly on the TM","The round window","The opening of the auditory tube"]'::jsonb, 'A reflection seen anteroinferiorly on the TM', 'The cone of light is a triangular reflection seen in the anteroinferior quadrant of the tympanic membrane on otoscopy, produced by the drum''s concave shape. The oval and round windows lie on the medial wall of the middle ear, and the auditory tube opens anteriorly, so these are not the reflection seen.'),
('e4c9f96c-4452-5e4c-8331-1a0024ddbe7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the middle ear (tympanic cavity) contains:?', '["Semicircular canals resulting in decreased functional activity","Cochlea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endolymph mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes)","Auditory nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes)', 'The tympanic cavity contains the auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, and stapes) bridging the tympanic membrane to the oval window, together with the chorda tympani and tympanic plexus. The cochlea, semicircular canals, and endolymph belong to the inner ear, and the auditory nerve lies beyond the oval window.'),
('800c29d7-1585-5d9c-be37-8a355efe73b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The tympanic cavity is lined by:'' is:', '["Transitional epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Skin","Stratified columnar epithelium","Mucous membrane (respiratory epithelium)"]'::jsonb, 'Mucous membrane (respiratory epithelium)', 'The tympanic cavity is lined by a mucous membrane bearing respiratory (ciliated) epithelium continuous with the nasopharynx through the auditory tube. Skin lines the external meatus, while transitional, simple squamous, or stratified columnar types are not the normal lining of the middle ear.'),
('9f3ad293-cf58-50eb-a830-fcfb727f28c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the auditory ossicles transmit sound from:?', '["Pinna to TM resulting in decreased functional activity","Tympanic membrane to oval window (footplate of stapes)","TM to round window mediated by specific receptor activation","Cochlea to brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oval window to round window leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic membrane to oval window (footplate of stapes)', 'The ossicular chain conducts sound vibrations from the tympanic membrane through the malleus, incus, and stapes to the oval window, where the stapes footplate sits. The round window only relieves pressure, and the pinna-to-membrane and cochlea-to-brain routes do not involve the ossicles.'),
('6e9d3fa0-31f0-54b1-b769-4f1dcc561004'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the malleus is attached to:?', '["The oval window only resulting in decreased functional activity","The incus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The round window mediated by specific receptor activation","The tympanic membrane (handle) and incus (head)","The stapes only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tympanic membrane (handle) and incus (head)', 'The malleus handle attaches to the tympanic membrane and its head articulates with the incus, converting drum vibration into movement of the chain. The oval and round windows are relations of the stapes, not of the malleus.'),
('24e32d84-0904-5e7a-90e7-47e70bcb9e78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the incus articulates with:?', '["Stapes and oval window resulting in decreased functional activity","Malleus and TM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malleus anteriorly and stapes posteriorly","Only malleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only stapes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus anteriorly and stapes posteriorly', 'The incus articulates anteriorly with the malleus (incudomalleolar joint) and posteriorly with the stapes (incudostapedial joint). Saying it contacts only one ossicle or the tympanic membrane omits the other articulation.'),
('155f076f-e9f3-5838-9f54-a33fe690c42c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the stapes footplate sits in:?', '["Promontory","Aditus ad antrum","Facial canal","Oval window (fenestra vestibuli)","Round window (fenestra cochleae)"]'::jsonb, 'Oval window (fenestra vestibuli)', 'The stapes footplate occupies the oval window (fenestra vestibuli) on the medial wall of the tympanic cavity, overlying the vestibule. The round window is closed by a membrane and receives no stapedial attachment, and the promontory, aditus, and facial canal are unrelated sites.'),
('8b021b62-0e8d-5ea0-9469-ab37fe6af47e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the middle ear has how many walls?', '["2 walls resulting in decreased functional activity","5 walls accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 walls mediated by specific receptor activation","6 walls (roof, floor, medial, lateral, anterior, posterior)","4 walls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6 walls (roof, floor, medial, lateral, anterior, posterior)', 'The tympanic cavity has six walls: roof (tegmental), floor (jugular), anterior (carotid), posterior (mastoid), lateral (membranous), and medial (labyrinthine). Counts of 2, 3, 4, or 5 omit one or more of these named boundaries.'),
('f78cbc40-5f4c-5e31-99d8-604a3a184a7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The roof (tegmental wall) of tympanic cavity is:'' is:', '["Aditus to mastoid antrum resulting in decreased functional activity","Promontory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jugular bulb mediated by specific receptor activation","Carotid canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tegmen tympani (thin plate of petrous temporal)"]'::jsonb, 'Tegmen tympani (thin plate of petrous temporal)', 'The roof (tegmental wall) of the tympanic cavity is formed by the tegmen tympani, a thin plate of the petrous temporal bone separating the cavity from the middle cranial fossa. The aditus, promontory, jugular bulb, and carotid canal belong to other walls of the cavity.'),
('6af2ff0f-3f0c-535c-bd6e-64b65220d47b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the floor (jugular wall) of tympanic cavity relates to:?', '["Sigmoid sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior bulb of internal jugular vein","Carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior bulb of internal jugular vein', 'The floor (jugular wall) of the tympanic cavity overlies the superior bulb of the internal jugular vein, which can be exposed if the thin bony floor is deficient. The sigmoid sinus relates posteriorly, the carotid artery anteriorly, and the cochlea medially, not to the floor.'),
('cc71bdf3-3555-52b0-868e-4709580e2b9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the medial wall (labyrinthine) of tympanic cavity contains:?', '["Aditus ad antrum","Mastoid cells","Promontory (basal turn of cochlea) oval window","round window prominence of facial canal","Carotid canal Jugular bulb"]'::jsonb, 'Promontory (basal turn of cochlea) oval window', 'The medial (labyrinthine) wall presents the promontory overlying the basal turn of the cochlea, along with the oval and round windows and the prominence of the facial canal. The aditus and mastoid cells belong to the posterior wall, while the carotid canal and jugular bulb lie on the anterior and floor aspects.'),
('77f3fe5c-880c-59dd-a6cc-c3aaf6c7fb24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the lateral wall (membranous) of tympanic cavity is:?', '["Tegmental wall","Tympanic membrane","Mastoid wall","Carotid wall","Jugular wall"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic membrane', 'The lateral (membranous) wall of the tympanic cavity is formed largely by the tympanic membrane. The tegmental, mastoid, carotid, and jugular names correspond to the roof, posterior, anterior, and floor walls respectively, not the lateral wall.'),
('1832b6c8-87fe-5df9-ad19-5bdf2161760e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the anterior wall (carotid) of tympanic cavity relates to:?', '["Mastoid cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Cochlea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal carotid artery and auditory tube","Jugular vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid artery and auditory tube', 'The anterior (carotid) wall is related to the internal carotid artery, which lies immediately in front of the cavity, and it contains the opening of the auditory tube. The jugular vein, cochlea, facial nerve, and mastoid cells are not anterior wall relations.'),
('7a706f6e-2623-5ab6-910b-06f3bcee7d6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the posterior wall (mastoid) of tympanic cavity contains:?', '["Promontory","Carotid canal","Oval window","Aditus to mastoid antrum","pyramidal eminence facial nerve canal Jugular bulb"]'::jsonb, 'Aditus to mastoid antrum', 'The posterior (mastoid) wall bears the aditus, through which the tympanic cavity communicates with the mastoid antrum. The promontory and oval window lie on the medial wall, and the carotid canal is anterior, so these are not posterior wall contents.'),
('c24a4549-55a3-524b-8d1c-8320043ac32b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The mastoid antrum is:'' is:', '["A large air cell in the mastoid process communicating with the middle ear","The semicircular canals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cochlea mediated by specific receptor activation","The external ear canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inner ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large air cell in the mastoid process communicating with the middle ear', 'The mastoid antrum is an air cavity in the mastoid process connected to the tympanic cavity through the aditus, a route by which middle-ear infection can spread to the mastoid. The cochlea, semicircular canals, ear canal, and inner ear are distinct structures with other locations.'),
('567b8cef-a3f9-583c-b0f0-a18f65a2febc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the mastoid air cells may extend into which bone?', '["Sphenoid bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temporal bone (mastoid process, petrous, squamous)"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal bone (mastoid process, petrous, squamous)', 'Mastoid air cells extend through the temporal bone, typically the mastoid process and sometimes the petrous and squamous parts. The sphenoid, frontal, parietal, and occipital bones are not sites of mastoid air-cell development.'),
('d38f2c05-63a2-5169-83e8-279dc42c12d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: mastoiditis is:?', '["Infection of ear canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection of mastoid air cells (complication of otitis media)","Infection of inner ear mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection of pinna without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection of external ear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection of mastoid air cells (complication of otitis media)', 'Mastoiditis is infection of the mastoid air cells, usually following acute otitis media via the aditus to the antrum. Infection confined to the pinna, ear canal, external ear, or inner ear does not define mastoiditis.'),
('f469e18d-5898-5672-91df-c992164ecc22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the auditory tube (eustachian tube) connects:?', '["Middle ear to external ear","Ear to oral cavity","Ear to nose","Middle ear (tympanic cavity) to nasopharynx","Inner ear to middle ear"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear (tympanic cavity) to nasopharynx', 'The auditory (pharyngotympanic or eustachian) tube connects the anterior wall of the tympanic cavity to the nasopharynx, allowing pressure equalization. It does not link the ear to the nose, oral cavity, external ear, or inner ear.'),
('92bd04c1-0974-5977-af96-66e2918ad7c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the auditory tube functions to:?', '["Transmit sound resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventilate the mastoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connect ear to nose mediated by specific receptor activation","Drain middle ear secretions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Equalize pressure between middle ear and atmosphere"]'::jsonb, 'Equalize pressure between middle ear and atmosphere', 'The auditory tube equilibrates pressure between the middle ear and the atmosphere by allowing air to pass into or out of the tympanic cavity, especially during swallowing. Conduction of sound, mastoid ventilation, or nasal connection are not its stated functions.'),
('0a0cd8fa-ea74-5f93-b2b3-c903ecec2946'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the auditory tube is longer in:?', '["Same length in all","Adults (3.5 cm) than in children","Males than females","Children than adults","Females than males"]'::jsonb, 'Adults (3.5 cm) than in children', 'In adults the auditory tube is about 3.5 cm long, whereas in children it is shorter, wider, and more horizontal—predisposing to reflux of nasopharyngeal secretions and otitis media. The same-length and sex-related claims do not reflect this developmental difference.'),
('f9004153-5350-5cf2-8350-c9fa3aa21830'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The muscles that open the auditory tube are:'' is:', '["Stylopharyngeus and palatopharyngeus","Tensor veli palatini and salpingopharyngeus","Palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus","Musculus uvulae and levator veli","Levator veli palatini and tensor veli"]'::jsonb, 'Tensor veli palatini and salpingopharyngeus', 'During swallowing and yawning the tensor veli palatini, assisted by salpingopharyngeus, opens the pharyngeal orifice of the auditory tube. Palatoglossus moves the tongue, stylopharyngeus acts on the pharynx, and musculus uvulae raises the velum, so these pairs do not open the tube.'),
('3ac1968c-a054-5cf3-a45e-44e9fed91e43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the middle ear contains which nerves?', '["Facial nerve only resulting in decreased functional activity","Chorda tympani (facial nerve branch) and tympanic plexus (glossopharyngeal)","Trigeminal nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglossal nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve branch) and tympanic plexus (glossopharyngeal)', 'The tympanic cavity carries the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve, and the tympanic plexus formed by the glossopharyngeal (Jacobson''s) nerve with sympathetics. The main facial, trigeminal, and hypoglossal nerves do not course as such through the cavity.'),
('ea09862d-7355-5490-8789-57e79227ef9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the chorda tympani crosses the tympanic cavity:?', '["Between the incus and stapes","Between the malleus and incus","Through the round window","Under the promontory","Through the oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Between the malleus and incus', 'The chorda tympani enters the tympanic cavity from the facial canal, runs medial to the malleus and lateral to the incus, and leaves through the petrotympanic fissure. The oval and round windows and the promontory are not in its course.'),
('76617ebc-680c-50c5-b5cc-92228be3b411'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the tympanic plexus is formed by:?', '["Tympanic branch of glossopharyngeal (Jacobson''s nerve) and sympathetics from internal carotid plexus","Vagus and facial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only glossopharyngeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminal and facial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic branch of glossopharyngeal (Jacobson''s nerve) and sympathetics from internal carotid plexus', 'The tympanic plexus is formed by the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (Jacobson''s nerve) together with sympathetic fibers from the internal carotid plexus, and it supplies the mucosa of the middle ear. The vagus, trigeminal, or facial nerves alone do not form this plexus.'),
('395a4125-b7f1-5bc9-a8ae-f8d31c8b7f8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the lesser petrosal nerve arises from:?', '["Cochlear nerve","Facial nerve","Tympanic plexus (fibers from CN IX)","Geniculate ganglion","Otic ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Tympanic plexus (fibers from CN IX)', 'The lesser petrosal nerve arises from the tympanic plexus, carrying preganglionic parasympathetic fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve to the otic ganglion for the parotid gland. It does not originate from the cochlear nerve, geniculate ganglion, facial nerve, or otic ganglion.'),
('55b2f07a-2514-5d86-967a-92f23a61109f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the inner ear (labyrinth) consists of:?', '["Only semicircular canals","Only cochlea","Cochlea and auditory nerve","Cochlea (hearing)","vestibule and semicircular canals (balance) Vestibule and ossicles"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlea (hearing)', 'The inner ear (bony labyrinth) houses the cochlea for hearing, plus the vestibule and semicircular canals for balance. Among the listed choices, the cochlea (hearing) identifies the auditory component of the labyrinth; the auditory nerve is not part of the labyrinth and the ossicles belong to the middle ear, while choosing only balance-related parts omits the cochlea.'),
('1aa92c07-099d-5b13-9049-eeeef1273465'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The bony labyrinth contains:'' is:', '["Perilymph","Blood","Mucus","Endolymph","Serous fluid"]'::jsonb, 'Perilymph', 'The space between the bony and membranous labyrinths, i.e., the bony labyrinth, contains perilymph, a fluid high in sodium and low in potassium. Endolymph fills the membranous labyrinth, while blood, mucus, and generic serous fluid do not occupy the labyrinths.'),
('7a7fcd4f-9bf5-5018-9ea0-b2c0f3981dc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the membranous labyrinth contains:?', '["Cerebrospinal fluid","Perilymph","Blood","Endolymph","Mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymph', 'The membranous labyrinth (cochlear duct, utricle, saccule, and semicircular ducts) is filled with endolymph, a fluid rich in potassium, comparable to intracellular fluid. Perilymph surrounds it within the bony labyrinth; CSF, blood, and mucus are not its contents.'),
('786d9585-0cd8-5fdb-b6fd-d796b517cbba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the cochlea is responsible for:?', '["Hearing (sound transduction)","Head position sensing","Rotational acceleration sensing","Linear acceleration sensing","Balance"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing (sound transduction)', 'The cochlea transduces sound-induced mechanical waves into neural impulses, providing hearing. Rotational and linear acceleration and head position are detected by the semicircular canals and otolith organs, not by the cochlea.'),
('3fbeaaa3-ae99-5f94-8ac3-d8dd175fcc88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the semicircular canals are responsible for:?', '["Sound localization resulting in decreased functional activity","Gravity sensing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linear acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Rotational acceleration (angular movement)","Hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rotational acceleration (angular movement)', 'The semicircular canals detect rotational (angular) acceleration of the head through endolymph flow and cupular deflection in the ampullae. Linear acceleration and gravity are sensed by the utricle and saccule, and hearing by the cochlea.'),
('c3dd2269-d4a9-5f74-a0fb-756ffb821464'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the vestibule contains the utricle and saccule which are responsible for:?', '["Taste resulting in decreased functional activity","Linear acceleration and head position (static balance)","Rotational acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Sound conduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration and head position (static balance)', 'The utricle and saccule (otolith organs) in the vestibule detect linear acceleration and static head position through displacement of the otolithic membrane. Rotational acceleration is served by the semicircular canals and sound transduction by the cochlea, while taste is unrelated.'),
('b452a21f-c0d5-517e-ba56-225631d8b03b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the organ of corti is located:?', '["In the cochlear duct on the basilar membrane","In the saccule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the utricle mediated by specific receptor activation","In the semicircular canals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the vestibule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the cochlear duct on the basilar membrane', 'The spiral organ (organ of Corti) lies on the basilar membrane within the cochlear duct (scala media), where inner and outer hair cells transduce sound. The utricle, saccule, and semicircular canals house vestibular receptors, not auditory ones.'),
('de0de890-ff6f-5298-81ba-e357f958da81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The spiral ganglion contains:'' is:', '["Motor neurons","Sympathetic neurons","Parasympathetic neurons","Cell bodies of vestibular nerve","Cell bodies of cochlear nerve (hearing)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell bodies of cochlear nerve (hearing)', 'The spiral ganglion, located in the modiolus of the cochlea, contains the bipolar cell bodies of the cochlear division of the eighth nerve, relaying sound. Cell bodies of the vestibular nerve lie in the vestibular (Scarpa''s) ganglion, and motor, sympathetic, and parasympathetic neurons are not found here.'),
('bbd21d5b-ad2a-52aa-a1c8-dbb9d4a7ced0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the vestibular ganglion (scarpa''s ganglion) contains:?', '["Cell bodies of cochlear nerve","Parasympathetic neurons","Motor neurons","Sympathetic neurons","Cell bodies of vestibular nerve (balance)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell bodies of vestibular nerve (balance)', 'The vestibular (Scarpa''s) ganglion contains the bipolar cell bodies of the vestibular division of the eighth nerve, carrying balance information. Cell bodies of the cochlear nerve lie in the spiral ganglion, and motor, sympathetic, or parasympathetic neurons are not part of this ganglion.'),
('eaabf4af-debc-508b-a21c-dadda23f36a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: the facial nerve runs through which part of temporal bone?', '["Tympanic cavity only","Facial canal in the petrous temporal","Mastoid only","Cochlea","Semicircular canals"]'::jsonb, 'Facial canal in the petrous temporal', 'The facial nerve travels in the facial (Fallopian) canal of the petrous temporal bone, from the internal acoustic meatus to the stylomastoid foramen. It does not run through the cochlea or semicircular canals, and its course is not confined to the tympanic cavity or mastoid.'),
('5711e774-9dc5-5ed8-bde4-c762b61ce8a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Regarding Ear and temporal bone, what is the correct answer to: the facial nerve has how many segments in the temporal bone?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 (tympanic and mastoid) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 (labyrinthine, tympanic, mastoid)","4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (labyrinthine, tympanic, mastoid)', 'Within the temporal bone the facial nerve has three segments: labyrinthine, tympanic (horizontal), and mastoid (vertical). Counts of 1, 2, 4, or 5, or naming only the tympanic and mastoid parts, do not describe its complete intratemporal course.'),
('5aa91c9d-66e9-554b-a5b2-f02f0603f23b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'For Ear and temporal bone, which option is most appropriate: the geniculate ganglion is located:?', '["In the tympanic cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","At the anterior end of labyrinthine segment of facial nerve","At the stylomastoid foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","In the internal acoustic meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the mastoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the anterior end of labyrinthine segment of facial nerve', 'The geniculate ganglion lies at the anterior end of the labyrinthine segment of the facial nerve, marking the site where the nerve bends posteriorly into the tympanic segment. It is not positioned in the meatus, mastoid, tympanic cavity, or at the stylomastoid foramen.'),
('5365112c-4334-5b12-89fc-a7f5086b3a56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'Which answer is correct in Ear and temporal bone when asked: the internal acoustic meatus transmits:?', '["VII","VIII","IX Only VII","Facial nerve (VII)","vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) labyrinthine artery VII and VIII Only VIII"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The internal acoustic meatus transmits the facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII) nerves together with the labyrinthine vessels; the facial nerve traverses it before entering the facial canal, so naming it identifies one genuine content. The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) exits through the jugular foramen and does not pass through this meatus.'),
('1a8c4528-0f32-5b2c-a4e2-1d2facaacea9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, the best response to ''The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) divides into:'' is:', '["Anterior and posterior divisions","Superior and inferior divisions","Sensory and motor divisions","Cochlear and vestibular divisions","Medial and lateral divisions"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear and vestibular divisions', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve divides within the internal acoustic meatus into cochlear and vestibular divisions, supplying the cochlea and the vestibular labyrinth respectively. Anterior/posterior, superior/inferior, sensory/motor, and medial/lateral splits are not its named component divisions.'),
('0e3ef12c-2510-5b27-8344-5f2aca10b9f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'During revision of Ear and temporal bone, which of the following is correct: the external ear is supplied by which nerves?', '["Only trigeminal","Only facial nerve","Only vagus","Auricular branch of vagus (Arnold''s nerve)","auriculotemporal (V3) great auricular (C2-3) Only great auricular"]'::jsonb, 'Auricular branch of vagus (Arnold''s nerve)', 'The external ear is supplied by the auriculotemporal nerve (V3), the great auricular nerve (C2-3), and the auricular branch of the vagus (Arnold''s nerve). Arnold''s nerve genuinely innervates the concha and parts of the canal, whereas the trigeminal or great auricular alone, or the facial nerve, do not supply the external ear.'),
('a005efb3-c8ff-5828-91d7-15db39ccba83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Ear Temporal Bone', 'In Ear and temporal bone, which statement best fits the following fact: arnold''s nerve (auricular branch of vagus) supplies:?', '["The inner ear only resulting in decreased functional activity","The tympanic membrane only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The middle ear only mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior wall and floor of external auditory meatus and adjacent pinna","The entire pinna leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior wall and floor of external auditory meatus and adjacent pinna', 'Arnold''s nerve, the auricular branch of the vagus, supplies the posterior wall and floor of the external auditory meatus and the adjacent part of the pinna (concha). It does not supply the entire pinna, the middle or inner ear, or the tympanic membrane as a whole.'),
('4914fb0c-601d-5cd3-a641-0a5e400ce880'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasal cavity is divided into right and left by:', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal cavity is split into right and left halves by the nasal septum, a midline partition composed of the vomer, septal cartilage, and perpendicular plate of the ethmoid. The conchae project from the lateral wall rather than dividing the cavity.'),
('e4462183-c91f-5294-a427-261b0eb1bfeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasal septum is formed by:', '["Perpendicular plate of ethmoid","vomer","septal cartilage","Vomer and maxilla","Only ethmoid Only vomer Ethmoid and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Perpendicular plate of ethmoid', 'The nasal septum has three components: the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid bone, the vomer, and the septal cartilage. Among the listed options the perpendicular plate of ethmoid is a genuine sole component; the vomer, septum cartilage, and maxilla options are either partial or include unrelated bones.'),
('9efa5fd0-b3a9-5337-9d49-feb8356ac351'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasal cavity has how many meatuses?', '["2 (superior and inferior) resulting in decreased functional activity","3 (superior, middle, inferior)","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (superior, middle, inferior)', 'Three meatuses—superior, middle, and inferior—lie beneath the corresponding conchae along the lateral nasal wall. The sphenoethmoidal recess is a separate area above the superior concha and is not counted as a meatus.'),
('f5a36cb7-4c85-50be-9019-f493adfceb54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The inferior meatus contains the opening of:', '["Nasolacrimal duct","Frontal sinus","Anterior ethmoidal sinuses","Maxillary sinus","Sphenoidal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Nasolacrimal duct', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the inferior concha into the inferior meatus. The maxillary and anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into the middle meatus, the posterior ethmoidal sinuses into the superior meatus, and the sphenoidal sinus into the sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('96db7a4f-2396-5052-8f94-93286ed7de60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The middle meatus contains the openings of:', '["Posterior ethmoidal only resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasolacrimal duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphenoidal sinus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal sinus maxillary sinus anterior ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal sinus maxillary sinus anterior ethmoidal sinuses', 'The middle meatus receives the openings of the frontal sinus, the maxillary sinus, and the anterior ethmoidal sinuses. The posterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into the superior meatus, the nasolacrimal duct into the inferior meatus, and the sphenoidal sinus into the sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('ccbefa04-2757-5975-a828-c01a2450de6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The superior meatus contains the openings of:', '["Frontal sinus","Nasolacrimal duct","Posterior ethmoidal sinuses","Sphenoidal sinus","Maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal sinuses', 'The posterior ethmoidal sinuses are the only structures that drain into the superior meatus, beneath the superior concha. The frontal and maxillary sinuses drain into the middle meatus, the sphenoidal sinus into the sphenoethmoidal recess, and the nasolacrimal duct into the inferior meatus.'),
('100fa8a4-5d44-5029-bd5d-fed26ccbd22d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoethmoidal recess is located:', '["In the choana","In the inferior meatus","In the middle meatus","Above the superior concha","opening of sphenoidal sinus In the vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Above the superior concha', 'The sphenoethmoidal recess is a groove between the superior concha and the body of the sphenoid bone, and it receives the opening of the sphenoidal sinus. It lies above, not within, the meatuses, choana, or vestibule.'),
('8345790d-284e-541c-adf6-60596f4f4104'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The conchae (turbinates) are projections of which bone?', '["Sphenoid bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Vomer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ethmoid bone (superior and middle concha); inferior concha is separate bone"]'::jsonb, 'Ethmoid bone (superior and middle concha); inferior concha is separate bone', 'The superior and middle conchae are projections of the ethmoid bone, while the inferior concha is a separate bone that articulates with the maxilla and ethmoid. The sphenoid, nasal bone, vomer, and maxilla do not form conchae.'),
('64be5a6b-2632-5011-910a-32bc5b87b0a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The function of nasal conchae is:', '["To detect smell resulting in decreased functional activity","To produce mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To provide resonance mediated by specific receptor activation","To filter particles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To increase surface area for warming and humidifying inspired air"]'::jsonb, 'To increase surface area for warming and humidifying inspired air', 'The conchae enlarge the mucosal surface area and create turbulent airflow, so inspired air is warmed, humidified, and filtered as it passes through the nose. Smell detection, mucus production, and resonance are roles of other nasal structures.'),
('e11bfe89-18b7-566b-a9ef-cc401c0cf66e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The olfactory epithelium is located:', '["In the vestibule resulting in decreased functional activity","In the sphenoethmoidal recess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the respiratory region mediated by specific receptor activation","In the inferior meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the roof of nose and medial surface of superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'In the roof of nose and medial surface of superior concha', 'The olfactory epithelium lies in the roof of the nasal cavity and on the medial surface of the superior concha, housing the olfactory receptors. The vestibular, respiratory, and meatal regions are lined by non-olfactory mucosa.'),
('e6ca9c2f-40e1-52fd-b03d-9313cbcecb16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The respiratory epithelium of nose is:', '["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Stratified squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple cuboidal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple columnar without cilia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells', 'The respiratory mucosa of the nose is a pseudostratified, ciliated columnar epithelium containing goblet cells; the cilia sweep mucus toward the pharynx. Stratified squamous, transitional, cuboidal, and non-ciliated columnar epithelia characterize other regions of the airway.'),
('6793763a-845d-5402-8627-e6f1ed581fdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nose receives blood supply from which arteries?', '["Anterior ethmoidal (ICA)","posterior ethmoidal (ICA) sphenopalatine (ECA)","greater palatine (ECA) superior labial (ECA)","Only internal carotid Only facial artery","Only ophthalmic artery Only external carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ethmoidal (ICA)', 'The nasal cavity is supplied by arteries from both the internal carotid (anterior and posterior ethmoidal branches of the ophthalmic artery) and the external carotid (sphenopalatine, greater palatine, and superior labial arteries). The anterior ethmoidal artery is a key ICA-derived contributor to this supply.'),
('571ef875-fa96-5251-a5ab-684799895493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenopalatine artery (main supply to nasal cavity) is a branch of:', '["Maxillary artery","Ophthalmic artery","Anterior ethmoidal","Posterior ethmoidal","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary artery', 'The sphenopalatine artery, the principal arterial supply of the nasal mucosa, branches from the pterygopalatine part of the maxillary artery. The ethmoidal arteries arise from the ophthalmic artery, and the facial artery supplies the cheek and upper lip.'),
('655fb177-6ec4-5cdd-aace-c07ff234d8fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Little''s area (Kiesselbach''s plexus) is:', '["Opening of maxillary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Opening of nasolacrimal duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Olfactory area mediated by specific receptor activation","An area on anterior nasal septum with rich anastomosis of arteries","Opening of sphenoidal sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An area on anterior nasal septum with rich anastomosis of arteries', 'Little''s area is the vascular zone on the anterior nasal septum where branches of several arteries converge, forming Kiesselbach''s plexus. It is a common site of anterior epistaxis because of this dense arterial anastomosis.'),
('0273fe6b-1714-5d85-bbe8-da17ed52b7e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Little''s area is formed by anastomosis of:', '["Only anterior ethmoidal","All arteries of the nose","Sphenopalatine only Facial only","Sphenopalatine greater palatine","superior labial anterior ethmoidal arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine greater palatine', 'Kiesselbach''s plexus in Little''s area is formed by branches of the sphenopalatine and greater palatine arteries, reinforced by the superior labial and anterior ethmoidal arteries. The sphenopalatine and greater palatine are the principal contributors listed among the options.'),
('c2aa9aa1-2327-5325-8ab8-bd602f2ab61e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The most common site of epistaxis is:', '["Little''s area (anterior nasal septum)","Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Little''s area (anterior nasal septum)', 'Most nosebleeds arise from Little''s area on the anterior nasal septum because of its rich arterial anastomosis (Kiesselbach''s plexus). The meatuses and sphenoethmoidal recess are not typical sites of spontaneous epistaxis.'),
('bb3487ff-a654-5864-b5ba-365aa4f0cebd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nerve supply to the nasal cavity includes:', '["Glossopharyngeal only resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2) nerves","Hypoglossal only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2) nerves', 'The nasal mucosa is innervated by the anterior ethmoidal nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1), and by sphenopalatine branches of the maxillary division (V2). The glossopharyngeal, facial, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves do not supply the nasal lining.'),
('e002d244-8959-5978-921c-402ff58efdb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I) filaments pass through:', '["Foramen ovale","Foramen rotundum","Optic canal","Cribriform plate of ethmoid","Superior orbital fissure"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate of ethmoid', 'The filaments of the olfactory nerve pass through the numerous foramina of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to reach the olfactory bulb. The other foramina transmit different vessels and nerves.'),
('9e78f765-962f-525a-abc7-a72ab3632fa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The paranasal sinuses are:', '["Frontal","maxillary","sphenoidal","occipital","Frontal ethmoidal"]'::jsonb, 'sphenoidal', 'The paranasal sinuses are the frontal, maxillary, ethmoidal, and sphenoidal air sinuses, which drain into the nasal cavity. There is no occipital paranasal sinus, and ''sphenoidal'' is a true member of this group among the options.'),
('e8938308-d845-5f6c-a8a1-14344f0fee38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The largest paranasal sinus is:', '["Ethmoidal sinuses","Frontal sinus","Maxillary sinus","Sphenoidal sinus","All are equal"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary sinus', 'The maxillary sinus is the largest paranasal sinus, with a capacity of about 15 ml. The frontal, sphenoidal, and ethmoidal sinuses are smaller pneumatized spaces.'),
('1e7c399e-9760-564b-9d0f-27d9a05f4540'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus drains into:', '["Inferior meatus","Middle meatus (via hiatus semilunaris)","Nasal vestibule","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (via hiatus semilunaris)', 'The maxillary sinus opens through the hiatus semilunaris into the middle meatus. The nasolacrimal duct drains into the inferior meatus, the posterior ethmoidal sinuses into the superior meatus, and the sphenoidal sinus into the sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('30072abf-5c40-52ce-ac89-4242e43bcf2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus opening is located:', '["On its lateral wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Low on its medial wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High on its medial wall (making drainage difficult when head erect)","On its roof without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On its floor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High on its medial wall (making drainage difficult when head erect)', 'The ostium of the maxillary sinus sits high on its medial wall near the roof, so gravity does not favor drainage when the head is erect. This high position predisposes to retained secretions and infection.'),
('97f1af45-c4cc-575f-8e51-4a5194ed4d45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus is related superiorly to:', '["Nasal cavity","Cranial cavity","Orbit (floor of orbit)","Pterygopalatine fossa","Oral cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Orbit (floor of orbit)', 'The roof of the maxillary sinus forms the floor of the orbit, so sinus disease or surgery can involve the orbital contents. The nasal cavity, oral cavity, pterygopalatine fossa, and cranial cavity relate to other aspects of the sinus.'),
('eea7aec9-07ff-5473-a112-c5ea7a691a76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus is related inferiorly to:', '["Nasal floor resulting in decreased functional activity","Oral cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Roots of upper teeth (premolars and molars)","Palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Roots of upper teeth (premolars and molars)', 'The floor of the maxillary sinus lies directly over the roots of the upper premolars and molars, which is why sinusitis can present as toothache. The palate, oral cavity, and nasal structures are not its inferior relation.'),
('194b2471-4091-5f5d-bf7f-77dd112d8f50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The frontal sinus drains into:', '["Nasal vestibule resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior meatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior meatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphenoethmoidal recess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle meatus (via frontonasal duct to hiatus semilunaris)"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (via frontonasal duct to hiatus semilunaris)', 'The frontal sinus drains through the frontonasal duct into the hiatus semilunaris of the middle meatus. The inferior meatus, superior meatus, vestibule, and sphenoethmoidal recess receive other structures.'),
('50905914-a608-5eea-9e86-8a1147c45cfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The frontal sinus is located:', '["Between the outer and inner tables of frontal bone","In the maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the ethmoid mediated by specific receptor activation","In the sphenoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the temporal bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the outer and inner tables of frontal bone', 'The frontal sinus pneumatizes between the outer and inner tables of the frontal bone, above the orbit. The maxilla, ethmoid, sphenoid, and temporal bone contain other sinuses or air cells.'),
('60837333-4c97-57ff-9c31-169e83c2be93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The ethmoidal sinuses are located:', '["In the sphenoid resulting in decreased functional activity","In the maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the nasal cavity and the orbit (in ethmoid bone)","In the frontal bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the palatine bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the nasal cavity and the orbit (in ethmoid bone)', 'The ethmoidal air cells occupy the ethmoid bone, lying between the nasal cavity and the orbit. This location explains why ethmoidal sinusitis can spread to the orbit and cause periorbital cellulitis.'),
('d3813539-4810-52ff-a428-1d8fb909030a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into:', '["Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Middle meatus","Inferior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus', 'The anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into the middle meatus, along with the frontal and maxillary sinuses. The posterior ethmoidal sinuses open into the superior meatus, and the inferior meatus and sphenoethmoidal recess receive different structures.'),
('8b09c45d-ce57-5b11-a1f0-88aa24dc8360'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The posterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into:', '["Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Middle meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Inferior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior meatus', 'The posterior ethmoidal sinuses open into the superior meatus beneath the superior concha. The middle meatus drains the anterior ethmoidal, frontal, and maxillary sinuses, the inferior meatus receives the nasolacrimal duct, and the sphenoethmoidal recess receives the sphenoidal sinus.'),
('03886fb8-8b89-504e-8a48-19563ba67732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoidal sinus drains into:', '["Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoethmoidal recess', 'The sphenoidal sinus opens into the sphenoethmoidal recess, just above the superior concha. The superior and middle meatuses drain the posterior ethmoidal and anterior/frontal/maxillary sinuses respectively, while the inferior meatus receives the nasolacrimal duct.'),
('a6406453-3d93-568b-aa19-ec81132eefa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoidal sinus is located:', '["In the maxilla","In the ethmoid","In the temporal bone","In the body of sphenoid bone","In the frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'In the body of sphenoid bone', 'The sphenoidal sinus lies within the body of the sphenoid bone, below the sella turcica. The maxilla, ethmoid, frontal, and temporal bones house the other air sinuses.'),
('dc4d48aa-4c09-500d-9701-5d88e5e5ab53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoidal sinus is related superiorly to:', '["Oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasal cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Orbit mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pituitary gland optic chiasm"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary gland optic chiasm', 'The roof of the sphenoidal sinus is the sella turcica, which contains the pituitary gland, with the optic chiasm lying close above the roof. The orbit, nasal cavity, oral cavity, and cerebellum are not the principal superior relations.'),
('f1235b80-c2b7-5af3-a82a-25d0c53f04a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The functions of paranasal sinuses include:', '["Only resonance","Only warming air","Only lighten skull","Lighten skull","resonance to voice warm/humidify air Only mucus production"]'::jsonb, 'Lighten skull', 'The paranasal sinuses lighten the skull, add resonance to the voice, and warm and humidify inspired air. Lightening the skull is a recognized sinus function among the given options, whereas the ''Only...'' choices each state a single incomplete role.'),
('58a7f9d0-7b51-52fc-953f-b3bd07686ef5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus is clinically important for:', '["Polyps resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinusitis (common infection due to poor drainage)","Tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Fractures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foreign bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinusitis (common infection due to poor drainage)', 'Because the maxillary sinus ostium lies high on its medial wall, drainage is inefficient and infection readily accumulates, so sinusitis is its major clinical concern. Polyps, tumors, fractures, and foreign bodies are less characteristic of the sinus itself.'),
('f5342d31-550e-58a1-97d2-30b274b7e178'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The osteomeatal complex is:', '["The lymphatic drainage of nose","The nerve plexus of nose","The blood supply of nose","The drainage pathway of frontal","maxillary and anterior ethmoidal sinuses The olfactory area"]'::jsonb, 'The drainage pathway of frontal', 'The osteomeatal complex is the region along the lateral nasal wall where the frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain through the middle meatus. It is not a lymphatic pathway, nerve plexus, vascular network, or olfactory area.'),
('c2f86e1e-4583-5e1c-8d5f-284c1885ca50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sinuses that are present at birth are:', '["Maxillary and ethmoidal","Only maxillary","Frontal and sphenoidal","Only ethmoidal","All sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary and ethmoidal', 'The maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are the only paranasal sinuses present at birth; they enlarge as the face grows. The frontal and sphenoidal sinuses begin their significant pneumatization in later childhood.'),
('a768c189-a1f4-5c88-b1f9-dd41d5b1b63a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The frontal sinus develops at about:', '["Age 2 years","Adulthood","Age 6-8 years","Birth","Adolescence"]'::jsonb, 'Age 6-8 years', 'The frontal sinus begins to develop around 6-8 years of age and is essentially absent at birth. It continues to enlarge through adolescence into early adulthood.'),
('f5a8bc25-f3a8-5840-a456-64ffe8afe286'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoidal sinus develops at about:', '["Age 1 year","Adulthood","Age 3-5 years","Adolescence","Birth"]'::jsonb, 'Age 3-5 years', 'Pneumatization of the sphenoidal sinus begins at about 3-5 years of age. It is rudimentary at birth and expands within the body of the sphenoid during childhood and adolescence.'),
('01437078-f8e6-53cd-af11-b6c5bffdd7d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nerve supply of the maxillary sinus is from:', '["Ophthalmic nerve (V1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary nerve (V2) via superior alveolar and infraorbital nerves","Mandibular nerve (V3) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glossopharyngeal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2) via superior alveolar and infraorbital nerves', 'The maxillary sinus is innervated by the maxillary nerve (V2) through its superior alveolar and infraorbital branches. V1, V3, the facial nerve, and the glossopharyngeal nerve do not supply the maxillary sinus.'),
('95936067-c39e-5fe6-b6ab-8f840ac92c39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nerve supply of the frontal sinus is from:', '["Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Anterior ethmoidal nerve","Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Infraorbital nerve (V2)","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital nerve (V1)', 'The frontal sinus is innervated by the supraorbital nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve. The auriculotemporal, anterior ethmoidal, infraorbital, and facial nerves supply other territories.'),
('bda45505-a324-53f2-ae42-e4868778b121'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nerve supply of the sphenoidal sinus is from:', '["Infraorbital nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater occipital nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and sphenopalatine branches (V2)","Glossopharyngeal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and sphenopalatine branches (V2)', 'The sphenoidal sinus receives innervation from the posterior ethmoidal nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1), and from sphenopalatine branches of the maxillary division (V2). The infraorbital, facial, greater occipital, and glossopharyngeal nerves are unrelated to this sinus.'),
('7d5e03dd-c47d-53e5-88e1-5a27ff29b527'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The anterior ethmoidal nerve is a branch of:', '["Maxillary nerve (V2)","Nasociliary nerve (V1)","Mandibular nerve (V3)","Oculomotor nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nasociliary nerve (V1)', 'The anterior ethmoidal nerve arises from the nasociliary nerve, itself a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve. It passes through the anterior ethmoidal foramen to supply the nasal cavity and external nose.'),
('c0872d41-8ec1-563a-a380-a19e259ecad4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasolacrimal duct drains into:', '["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct empties tears into the inferior meatus beneath the inferior concha. The middle and superior meatuses receive sinus openings, and the sphenoethmoidal recess drains the sphenoidal sinus.'),
('92fa0081-90d1-5c67-82f8-d7e2f88bc195'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The opening of the nasolacrimal duct is located:', '["Under the middle concha","Under the inferior concha in the inferior meatus","Under the superior concha","In the nasal vestibule","In the sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Under the inferior concha in the inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the inferior concha into the inferior meatus, guarded by a small mucosal fold. The middle and superior conchae, vestibule, and sphenoethmoidal recess are not its site of opening.'),
('57dda707-b207-5dbf-8520-6412e014ba20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The choanae (posterior nasal apertures) connect:', '["Nasal cavity to oral cavity","Nasal cavity to nasopharynx","Nose to oropharynx","Nose to larynx","Nasal cavity to sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity to nasopharynx', 'The choanae are the posterior nasal apertures through which the nasal cavity communicates with the nasopharynx. The oral cavity, oropharynx, larynx, and sinuses connect with the nose at other sites.'),
('eb00310d-07af-59de-b40a-d9a8c1b3ef2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The roof of the nasal cavity is formed by:', '["Only ethmoid","Maxilla and palatine","Nasal bone frontal bone","cribriform plate of ethmoid body of sphenoid","Only sphenoid Nasal and frontal bones only"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal bone frontal bone', 'The nasal roof is formed from front to back by the nasal bones, the nasal part of the frontal bone, the cribriform plate of the ethmoid, and the body of the sphenoid. The maxilla and palatine bones form the floor, not the roof.'),
('32f6439d-4f57-550b-9f34-b248249c4266'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The floor of the nasal cavity is formed by:', '["Only maxilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Only palatine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone","Maxilla and sphenoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Palatine and ethmoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone', 'The nasal floor consists of the palatine process of the maxilla anteriorly and the horizontal plate of the palatine bone posteriorly. The sphenoid, ethmoid, or a single bone alone does not account for the complete floor.'),
('f477bae8-7d7c-55d1-9a9d-7a22480fcf58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The lateral wall of the nasal cavity is formed by:', '["Maxilla and palatine only Only maxilla and ethmoid Nasal and frontal bones","Maxilla","ethmoid inferior concha","sphenoid palatine","lacrimal Only ethmoid and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla', 'The maxilla forms a large part of the lateral nasal wall, which also receives contributions from the ethmoid, inferior concha, palatine, sphenoid, and lacrimal bones. Among the options, the maxilla is the named contributor to the lateral wall.'),
('71da7080-e850-5b6d-b6f6-182d7dd5f34b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The medial wall of the nasal cavity is:', '["The nasal septum","The vomer","The maxilla","The sphenoid bone","The ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'The nasal septum', 'The medial wall of each nasal cavity is the nasal septum, which divides the cavity into right and left halves. The vomer, sphenoid, ethmoid, and maxilla are individual bones or components, while the septum is the complete medial wall.'),
('b8f394b1-abb1-5e7d-9f01-849fd67112bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasal vestibule is:', '["The roof of the nose resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior part near the choana accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anterior part of nasal cavity lined by skin with hair (vibrissae)","The middle meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The floor of the nose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior part of nasal cavity lined by skin with hair (vibrissae)', 'The nasal vestibule is the anterior dilated part of the nasal cavity just inside the nostril, lined by skin bearing vibrissae. The roof, floor, meatus, and choanal region are separate nasal structures with different mucosal lining.'),
('11cdf5e6-2286-5b84-b356-b689a97c5dbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The function of vibrissae (nasal hairs) is:', '["Produce mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","Warm the air accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filter large particles from inspired air","Humidify the air without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Detect smell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filter large particles from inspired air', 'Vibrissae are thick hairs just inside the nostrils that filter large particles from inspired air. They do not produce mucus, warm, humidify, or detect smell, which are performed by the mucosa, vascular tissue, and olfactory epithelium.'),
('866988bd-81f1-5c92-9e22-496dab0661ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The internal nasal valve is:', '["The sphenoethmoidal recess","The olfactory cleft","The choana","The narrowest part of the nasal cavity","at the junction of upper and lower lateral cartilages The opening of the maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'The narrowest part of the nasal cavity', 'The internal nasal valve is the narrowest part of the nasal cavity, formed where the upper lateral cartilages meet the septum, and it governs inspiratory airflow. The other regions listed are distinct sites that do not represent the narrowest functional aperture.'),
('db902b77-161f-546f-bd16-9a8467727a34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The angle of the nasal valve is normally:', '["20-30 degrees","5-10 degrees","60-90 degrees","10-15 degrees","30-45 degrees"]'::jsonb, '10-15 degrees', 'The normal angle of the internal nasal valve is approximately 10-15 degrees, set by the upper lateral cartilages and the septum. Angles outside this narrow range increase nasal resistance and alter airflow.'),
('615f3b80-97f2-50d4-9817-cfa1f40a11c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The respiratory epithelium of the nose is also called:', '["Olfactory epithelium","Schneiderian membrane","Transitional epithelium","Squamous epithelium","Simple epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Schneiderian membrane', 'The respiratory pseudostratified ciliated columnar mucosa lining the nasal cavity is called the Schneiderian membrane. The other options are different epithelial types or the specialized olfactory lining, not the general respiratory surface.'),
('8e83afad-2bef-5431-b2af-9eaf4460c2e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The olfactory epithelium contains:', '["Olfactory receptor cells","supporting cells","basal cells","Goblet cells only","Only supporting cells Ciliated columnar cells only Only receptor cells"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory receptor cells', 'The olfactory epithelium contains olfactory receptor cells plus supporting and basal cells. Options naming only goblet cells or only supporting cells are incomplete, because the receptor neurons are the defining component.'),
('2d70283a-a055-5d32-af07-02607f7506c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The blood supply of the nasal septum includes:', '["Facial artery only","Only ethmoidal","Sphenopalatine greater palatine","anterior ethmoidal superior labial arteries","Maxillary artery only Only sphenopalatine"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine greater palatine', 'The nasal septum is supplied by the sphenopalatine and greater palatine arteries, in addition to ethmoidal and superior labial contributions. The other options do not provide the principal blood supply to the septum.'),
('25781012-9757-5c8f-8c4f-dc9301fb04d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The lymphatic drainage of the anterior nasal cavity goes to:', '["Parotid nodes","Deep cervical nodes","Retropharyngeal nodes","Submental nodes","Submandibular lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular lymph nodes', 'Lymphatics of the anterior nasal cavity drain to the submandibular lymph nodes. Deep cervical and retropharyngeal nodes serve other regions, and parotid and submental nodes are not the primary drainage.'),
('65b1e9ad-b7ff-524a-88ab-4a2c756062c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The lymphatic drainage of the posterior nasal cavity goes to:', '["Submental nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Parotid nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes","Submandibular nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes', 'Lymphatics of the posterior nasal cavity drain mainly to the retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes. Submental and parotid nodes drain other territories, and axillary nodes are unrelated to the nose.'),
('0795c48a-2992-5436-a00f-038f53ac3271'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenopalatine foramen is located:', '["In the roof of nose resulting in decreased functional activity","In the inferior meatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the lateral wall of nasal cavity (posterior end of middle meatus)","In the floor of nose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the nasal septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the lateral wall of nasal cavity (posterior end of middle meatus)', 'The sphenopalatine foramen lies on the lateral nasal wall at the posterior end of the middle meatus and transmits the sphenopalatine artery and nasopalatine nerve. The roof, floor, septum, and inferior meatus are incorrect sites.'),
('43c52cb8-d5e4-5d6e-848c-b703b0cc1c26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenopalatine foramen transmits:', '["Optic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Ophthalmic artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenopalatine artery vein and nasopalatine nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine artery vein and nasopalatine nerve', 'The sphenopalatine foramen transmits the sphenopalatine artery, accompanying veins, and the nasopalatine nerve. It does not transmit the optic, facial, or maxillary nerves or the ophthalmic artery.'),
('888a96c7-277a-581f-b007-ea367071b730'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasopalatine nerve is a branch of:', '["Mandibular nerve (V3) resulting in decreased functional activity","Ophthalmic nerve (V1) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary nerve (V2) via pterygopalatine ganglion","Facial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glossopharyngeal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2) via pterygopalatine ganglion', 'The nasopalatine nerve arises from the maxillary nerve (V2) through the pterygopalatine ganglion and passes via the sphenopalatine foramen into the nose. It is not a branch of V1, V3, the facial nerve, or the glossopharyngeal nerve.'),
('3e46e836-26e6-5625-ab07-50ea4cf5bc19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasopalatine nerve supplies:', '["Lateral wall of nose resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal septum and anterior hard palate (incisive foramen)","Maxillary sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenoidal sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum and anterior hard palate (incisive foramen)', 'The nasopalatine nerve supplies the posterior inferior part of the nasal septum and the anterior hard palate through the incisive foramen. The other listed structures are served by different branches of the maxillary and ethmoidal nerves.'),
('a02ead93-f72f-55e2-8f87-22ceed846df2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The greater palatine nerve supplies:', '["Hard palate and adjacent gingiva","Soft palate","Nasal septum","Maxillary sinus","Upper teeth"]'::jsonb, 'Hard palate and adjacent gingiva', 'The greater palatine nerve supplies the hard palate and adjacent gingiva after descending through the pterygopalatine canal. The soft palate and other options are innervated by different nerves.'),
('77728f76-b241-5e42-a8df-2e33afdf1eb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The lesser palatine nerves supply:', '["Tonsils","Uvula","Soft palate","Nasal septum","Hard palate"]'::jsonb, 'Soft palate', 'The lesser palatine nerves supply the soft palate and uvula after emerging through the lesser palatine foramina. The hard palate, nasal septum, maxillary sinus, and tonsils are innervated by other nerves.'),
('d8f1a242-bafa-521e-ab3f-854113fbc302'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The anterior superior alveolar nerve supplies:', '["Lower teeth","Upper molars","Palate","Upper incisors and canines","maxillary sinus Upper premolars"]'::jsonb, 'Upper incisors and canines', 'The anterior superior alveolar nerve supplies the upper incisors and canines. Upper molars are served by the posterior superior alveolar nerve and premolars by the middle superior alveolar nerve, so the other options are incorrect.'),
('ae6b0f46-f96b-5d2a-8bc7-d708094523fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The middle superior alveolar nerve supplies:', '["All upper teeth","Upper incisors","Maxillary sinus only","Upper molars","Upper premolars"]'::jsonb, 'Upper premolars', 'The middle superior alveolar nerve supplies the upper premolars. Incisors are served by the anterior superior alveolar nerve and molars by the posterior superior alveolar nerve.'),
('20b27da0-431e-57eb-acd9-f4d11793702a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The posterior superior alveolar nerve supplies:', '["Upper premolars resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper incisors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower teeth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper molar teeth and maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Upper molar teeth and maxillary sinus', 'The posterior superior alveolar nerve supplies the upper molar teeth and the maxillary sinus. The anterior and middle alveolar branches serve the front teeth and premolars, so the other options are incorrect.'),
('2acc5eff-5800-56a2-8837-1742390b1cf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The infraorbital nerve is a continuation of:', '["Mandibular nerve (V3)","Maxillary nerve (V2)","Facial nerve","Ophthalmic nerve (V1)","Nasociliary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2)', 'The infraorbital nerve is the terminal continuation of the maxillary nerve (V2) that emerges through the infraorbital foramen. It is not a branch of V1, V3, the facial nerve, or the nasociliary nerve.'),
('19f0a217-bab8-5fd2-9bcf-0d747f1b3cca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The infraorbital nerve emerges at:', '["Foramen ovale","Foramen rotundum","Mental foramen","Supraorbital notch","Infraorbital foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Infraorbital foramen', 'The infraorbital nerve emerges onto the face through the infraorbital foramen. Foramina ovale and rotundum transmit branches of the maxillary nerve, while the other sites transmit different nerves.'),
('858bd39e-2bec-53c4-ab36-90284f448625'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The infraorbital nerve supplies:', '["Lower eyelid","upper lip","cheek side of nose","Upper eyelid Forehead","Temple Lower lip"]'::jsonb, 'Lower eyelid', 'The infraorbital nerve supplies the lower eyelid, upper lip, cheek, and side of the nose. The upper eyelid and forehead are served by branches of the ophthalmic nerve, so those options are incorrect.'),
('4347cb51-9608-5cb8-b3ed-baf7f242a9fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus is related medially to:', '["Orbit","Infratemporal fossa","Pterygopalatine fossa","Oral cavity","Nasal cavity (lateral wall of nose)"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity (lateral wall of nose)', 'The maxillary sinus is related medially to the lateral wall of the nasal cavity. Its relations to the orbit, fossae, and oral cavity are different surfaces rather than the medial relationship.'),
('c91c4cf7-1388-5818-a8ca-135ab29f5c9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus is related posteriorly to:', '["Oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cranial cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae","Orbit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae', 'The maxillary sinus is related posteriorly to the infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae. Its floor lies near tooth roots and its lateral wall is the zygomatic region, so the other options are incorrect.'),
('84a49866-6e6d-5f45-b4fa-c29e37b48c6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus is related laterally to:', '["Sphenoid bone","Frontal bone","Palatine bone","Zygomatic bone","Nasal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic bone', 'The maxillary sinus is related laterally to the zygomatic bone. The frontal, sphenoid, and palatine bones and the nasal bone do not form the lateral wall of this sinus.'),
('b0f6f29d-9df5-5ef4-b7b4-98fc1d1b6653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The root of the maxillary sinus is:', '["The roof","The medial wall","The posterior wall","The floor (close to tooth roots)","The lateral wall"]'::jsonb, 'The floor (close to tooth roots)', 'The floor of the maxillary sinus lies close to the roots of the upper teeth, an important clinical relationship because dental infection can spread into the sinus. The roof, medial, lateral, and posterior walls do not share this dental proximity.'),
('664866af-c38b-5fb8-bc0e-db769618e3d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus can be infected by:', '["Extension of dental infection (periapical abscess) through the thin floor","Lymphatic spread accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood spread mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergic reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of dental infection (periapical abscess) through the thin floor', 'Odontogenic infection can reach the maxillary sinus through its thin floor, which sits directly over the roots of the upper teeth. The other listed mechanisms are not the typical route of infection.'),
('5a5f7bb9-06c8-5624-929f-c8857067de08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The frontal sinus is absent in about:', '["4% of people (unilateral or bilateral)","50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20% mediated by specific receptor activation","10% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4% of people (unilateral or bilateral)', 'The frontal sinus is absent, either unilaterally or bilaterally, in about 4% of people. This developmental variability matters when interpreting sinus radiographs and symptoms.'),
('9cdd1617-cf3a-59f6-a2c0-81612c6e1ea3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The frontal sinus is supplied by:', '["Supraorbital and anterior ethmoidal arteries","Infraorbital artery","Posterior ethmoidal artery","Sphenopalatine artery","Greater palatine artery"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital and anterior ethmoidal arteries', 'The frontal sinus is supplied by branches of the supraorbital and anterior ethmoidal arteries. The other arteries supply different structures of the face and other sinuses.'),
('e1fda321-6327-5f86-b364-4e9416b4678c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The ethmoidal sinuses consist of:', '["Fixed number of 10","1-2 air cells","20-30 air cells","30-40 air cells","3-18 air cells each side"]'::jsonb, '3-18 air cells each side', 'The ethmoidal labyrinth contains 3-18 air cells on each side. The fixed numbers and ranges in the other options do not reflect the normal variable count.'),
('a270e7f1-9b50-5742-9015-a996b54963a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The anterior ethmoidal air cells drain into:', '["Middle meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Inferior meatus","Frontal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus', 'The anterior ethmoidal air cells drain into the middle meatus. The superior meatus and sphenoethmoidal recess drain posterior cells and the sphenoid sinus, respectively.'),
('453ddcb9-fe49-51c9-bdef-d7624c3e3a55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The middle ethmoidal air cells drain into:', '["Middle meatus (bulla ethmoidalis)","Frontal sinus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (bulla ethmoidalis)', 'The middle ethmoidal air cells drain into the middle meatus, often at the bulla ethmoidalis. The other meatuses and the sphenoethmoidal recess drain different sinus groups.'),
('ede5c4f2-8e50-5b75-8f19-78f9d80478c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The posterior ethmoidal air cells drain into:', '["Superior meatus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus","Frontal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior meatus', 'The posterior ethmoidal air cells drain into the superior meatus. The middle meatus drains anterior and middle cells, while the sphenoethmoidal recess drains the sphenoid sinus.'),
('1819b94a-0ad4-5297-ad21-586ec802d175'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The ethmoidal labyrinth separates:', '["Nasal cavity from the mouth","Frontal sinus from maxillary sinus","Nasal cavity from cranial cavity","Right and left nasal cavities","Nasal cavity from the orbit"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity from the orbit', 'The ethmoidal labyrinth separates the nasal cavity from the orbit. Its medial surface forms part of the lateral nasal wall and its lateral wall (lamina papyracea) borders the orbit.'),
('7f34935d-de0f-5acf-a02c-920233f53834'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The lamina papyracea is:', '["The thin lateral wall of ethmoid labyrinth (forms medial orbital wall)","The nasal septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The roof of the ethmoid mediated by specific receptor activation","The wall of the sphenoid sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The floor of the maxillary sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thin lateral wall of ethmoid labyrinth (forms medial orbital wall)', 'The lamina papyracea is the thin lateral wall of the ethmoid labyrinth that makes up part of the medial orbital wall. It is not the nasal septum or a wall of the sphenoid or maxillary sinus.'),
('e0a48e7e-d51e-56f3-94b9-d4b4e1d59c18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoidal sinus is divided by:', '["A thick wall resulting in decreased functional activity","A cartilaginous septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thin bony septum (often asymmetric)","septum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fibrous membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin bony septum (often asymmetric)', 'The sphenoidal sinus is divided by a thin, often asymmetric bony septum. This differs from thick walls, cartilaginous septa, and fibrous membranes.'),
('5615d3c3-6f38-5521-83f9-fd65e0061989'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoidal sinus relations include:', '["Nasal cavity inferiorly resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxillary sinus laterally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Orbit anteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal sinus superiorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pituitary superiorly cavernous sinus laterally optic nerve superolaterally"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary superiorly cavernous sinus laterally optic nerve superolaterally', 'The sphenoidal sinus lies just below the pituitary gland superiority, lateral to the cavernous sinuses, and superolateral to the optic nerves, a surgically important proximity. The other listed relations are incorrect for this sinus.'),
('bd6f0707-b255-58b9-9ac7-d2440a70fdeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoidal sinus receives nerve supply from:', '["Supraorbital nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasociliary nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auriculotemporal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2)","Infraorbital nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2)', 'The sphenoidal sinus receives its nerve supply from the posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and the sphenopalatine nerve (V2). The other nerves supply different structures and sinuses.'),
('6dcc56f9-010c-5eed-a14e-8b90b0c52c0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus drains into which part of the nasal cavity?', '["Atrium of middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus (via semilunar hiatus)"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (via semilunar hiatus)', 'The maxillary sinus drains into the middle meatus through the semilunar hiatus. The atrium, the inferior and superior meatuses, and the sphenoethmoidal recess drain other areas.'),
('a323b958-06b3-5698-b2cf-3b97edb1d893'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The external nose is supported by:', '["Only cartilages","Only nasal bones","Ethmoid bone","Nasal bones and cartilages","Maxilla and frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal bones and cartilages', 'The external nose is supported by nasal bones superiorly and nasal cartilages inferiorly. The other options lack the combination of bony and cartilaginous support.'),
('5e61f174-e08f-5af7-8db6-b062d4e97ad6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasal cavity is divided by:', '["Nasal septum","Middle turbinate","Vomer","Inferior turbinate","Cribriform plate"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal cavity is divided into right and left halves by the nasal septum. The turbinates and vomer are related components but do not themselves form the dividing structure.'),
('825efcf3-b2c3-53c6-92cd-501167184357'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The main blood supply to the nasal septum is from:', '["Anterior ethmoidal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior ethmoidal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sphenopalatine artery (terminal branch of maxillary artery)","Facial artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater palatine artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine artery (terminal branch of maxillary artery)', 'The main blood supply to the nasal septum is the sphenopalatine artery, a terminal branch of the maxillary artery, a key site for epistaxis (Little''s area). The other arteries are lesser contributors to the septum.'),
('bae6b23e-d074-58ed-a7da-13dfd138cd2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The roof of the nasal cavity includes:', '["Palatine bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vomer mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphenoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone', 'The roof of the nasal cavity includes the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone, through which the olfactory fibers pass. The other bones contribute to the floor, lateral wall, or septum rather than the roof.'),
('a1f0098c-0c70-5b34-9cdf-0da9911eed0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The superior meatus drains:', '["Maxillary sinus","Posterior ethmoidal sinuses","Frontal sinus","Sphenoid sinus","Anterior ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal sinuses', 'The superior meatus drains the posterior ethmoidal sinuses. The maxillary, frontal, sphenoid, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain through the middle meatus or other sites.'),
('5c63eeaf-b024-5bf1-a95e-e1cd318be795'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The middle meatus drains:', '["Sphenoid sinus","Frontal","maxillary","and anterior ethmoidal sinuses","All sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal', 'The middle meatus drains the frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses. The sphenoid and posterior ethmoidal sinuses open elsewhere.'),
('41dd86d1-5441-5e8e-812f-5ce70114c3eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoethmoidal recess drains:', '["Sphenoid sinus","Maxillary sinus","Posterior ethmoidal sinuses","Frontal sinus","Anterior ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoid sinus', 'The sphenoethmoidal recess drains the sphenoid sinus. The remaining sinuses open through the middle or superior meatus rather than this recess.'),
('26b18349-0d0b-561a-9fba-66e80d457236'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Olfactory epithelium is located:', '["In the nasopharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","In the inferior meatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the vestibule mediated by specific receptor activation","In the middle meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the roof of nasal cavity and adjacent septum and lateral wall"]'::jsonb, 'In the roof of nasal cavity and adjacent septum and lateral wall', 'The olfactory epithelium lies in the roof of the nasal cavity and the adjacent upper septum and lateral wall. The vestibule, meatuses, and nasopharynx are lined by respiratory or squamous epithelium.'),
('84fb85e1-5396-5342-b9e2-c8f84e4a61f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nerve supply to the nasal septum includes:', '["Facial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Glossopharyngeal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and nasopalatine nerve (V2)","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and nasopalatine nerve (V2)', 'The nasal septum is supplied by the anterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and the nasopalatine nerve (V2). The cranial and other listed nerves do not innervate the nasal septum.'),
('82b6abcf-8c06-588d-9ebb-e52da7052954'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The inferior turbinate is:', '["A separate bone (inferior conchal bone)","Part of the maxilla","Part of the ethmoid bone","Part of the sphenoid bone","Part of the palatine bone"]'::jsonb, 'A separate bone (inferior conchal bone)', 'The inferior turbinate is a separate bone, the inferior conchal bone. The superior and middle turbinates are parts of the ethmoid bone, but the inferior turbinate is an independent bone.'),
('209cb0b6-5afc-5b89-833b-fa4fb2c2dd73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The paranasal sinuses develop as:', '["Outgrowths from the nasal cavity","Derivatives of the sphenoid bone","Invaginations from the oral cavity","Extensions of the nasopharynx","Remnants of branchial arches"]'::jsonb, 'Outgrowths from the nasal cavity', 'The paranasal sinuses develop as outgrowths (diverticula) of the mucosa of the nasal cavity into the neighboring bones. They are not derivatives of the sphenoid alone, branchial arches, oral cavity, or nasopharynx.'),
('41a9150d-4990-50a4-b569-27a9cc1c8600'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The sphenoid sinus drains into:', '["Middle meatus","Nasopharynx","Inferior meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoethmoidal recess', 'The sphenoid sinus drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess. The superior and middle meatuses drain the ethmoidal, maxillary, and frontal sinuses.'),
('9313de3d-e44f-50ca-95a1-47027a71b63f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The maxillary sinus is innervated by:', '["Hypoglossal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Glossopharyngeal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infraorbital nerve (V2) and superior alveolar nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Infraorbital nerve (V2) and superior alveolar nerves', 'The maxillary sinus is innervated by the infraorbital nerve (V2) and the superior alveolar nerves. Cranial nerves such as the facial, glossopharyngeal, vagus, or hypoglossal do not supply this sinus.'),
('f17e9c07-c0c3-5a8a-b3db-49270c8fe9b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens into:', '["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct carries tears from the lacrimal sac and opens beneath the inferior concha into the inferior meatus. The middle and superior meatuses receive sinus openings, while the vestibule and nasopharynx are not terminal drainage sites for the duct.'),
('ae2a0699-7800-5622-b65c-3161305e2417'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube is located:', '["In the nasopharynx","behind the inferior meatus","Behind the soft palate","In the middle meatus","In the laryngopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the nasopharynx', 'The auditory (eustachian) tube opens on the lateral wall of the nasopharynx, just behind the inferior meatus near the tubal tonsil. It does not open into the meatuses, the soft palate, or the laryngopharynx.'),
('b39bd865-e48d-5dff-a9d9-bab532e23a9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Adenoids (pharyngeal tonsil) are located:', '["In the oropharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","In the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Under the tongue mediated by specific receptor activation","In the nasopharynx (roof and posterior wall)","In the nasal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the nasopharynx (roof and posterior wall)', 'The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) lies in the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx. The palatine and lingual tonsils occupy the oropharynx and tongue base, and the larynx and nasal cavity contain no tonsillar tissue.'),
('339da09e-0b2f-527c-9d13-fb2965ed2ad1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The palatine tonsils are located:', '["In the nasal cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","At the base of tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the oropharynx between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches","In the laryngopharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the nasopharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the oropharynx between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches', 'Each palatine tonsil lies in the tonsillar fossa of the oropharynx between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal folds. The other listed regions house different tonsils or no tonsillar tissue.'),
('a13e5165-e0b7-5aa2-acc8-49b13c47aa03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The lingual tonsil is located:', '["In the nasopharynx","Under the epithelium of soft palate","In the oropharynx lateral wall","At the base of the tongue","In the larynx"]'::jsonb, 'At the base of the tongue', 'The lingual tonsil is formed by lymphoid follicles in the mucosa of the posterior third (base) of the tongue. The nasopharynx, soft palate, lateral oropharyngeal wall, and larynx are not sites of the lingual tonsil.'),
('6f5d86f7-42c1-538a-9665-201bb798a7a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Waldeyer''s ring includes:', '["Pharyngeal","tubal","palatine","and lingual tonsils","Salivary glands"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal', 'Waldeyer''s ring is a circular band of lymphoid tissue at the pharyngeal inlet made up of the pharyngeal, tubal, palatine, and lingual tonsils. Salivary glands are not part of this lymphatic ring.'),
('bc275b1f-fc52-5f3a-9c02-e0f82e16ba55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The paranasal sinuses are absent at birth except:', '["Sphenoid sinus","All sinuses present at birth","Frontal sinus","None present at birth","Maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses', 'The maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are the only paranasal sinuses present, though small, at birth. The frontal and sphenoid sinuses begin to pneumatize later in childhood, so neither all nor none are present at birth.'),
('0257eb08-04de-55d3-a144-cc13975181b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Olfaction (smell) is mediated by:', '["Cranial nerve I (olfactory nerve)","Cranial nerve V","Cranial nerve VII","Cranial nerve X","Cranial nerve IX"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial nerve I (olfactory nerve)', 'Olfaction is transmitted by the olfactory nerve (CN I), whose filaments pass from the olfactory epithelium to the olfactory bulbs. The trigeminal, facial, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves carry other sensory and motor modalities.'),
('aa776830-d69d-5b57-ae9e-e448c51ac87a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Anosmia can result from:', '["Head trauma (cribriform plate fracture)","nasal polyps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","or infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sinusitis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma (cribriform plate fracture)', 'Fracture of the cribriform plate can shear the olfactory filaments and cause anosmia; nasal polyps and infections can also impair smell but are listed together as an alternative. Tonsillitis and sinusitis alone are not recognized causes of true anosmia.'),
('c9af2334-bdc5-5bf4-ac79-f5bf3ba22ac9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion is located:', '["In the infratemporal fossa","In the orbit","In the pterygopalatine fossa","In the nasal cavity","In the maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'In the pterygopalatine fossa', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion hangs in the pterygopalatine fossa, suspended from the maxillary nerve. It does not lie in the infratemporal fossa, orbit, nasal cavity, or maxillary sinus.'),
('06d1dce0-fa64-549f-9aa4-93e5fdd030fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Post-nasal drip is caused by:', '["Gastric reflux resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excessive mucus draining from sinuses or nose into the pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive mucus draining from sinuses or nose into the pharynx', 'Post-nasal drip is the sensation of mucus draining backward from the nose and sinuses into the pharynx. Gastric reflux, bronchitis, laryngitis, and tonsillitis are not the mechanism of post-nasal drip.'),
('b56b1e45-10a6-5d6a-b134-927319039ba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Caldwell-Luc procedure is used for:', '["Ethmoidectomy resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasal polypectomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Access to the maxillary sinus via the canine fossa","Frontal sinus surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenoid sinus surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Access to the maxillary sinus via the canine fossa', 'The Caldwell-Luc operation enters the maxillary sinus through the canine fossa of the anterior maxillary wall. Ethmoidectomy, polypectomy, and frontal or sphenoid sinus procedures use different surgical approaches.'),
('8c783348-b7f7-5bac-bf2e-b336ee76e382'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS) aims to:', '["Remove turbinates","Restore sinus drainage via the natural ostia","Remove all sinus mucosa","Drain the lacrimal sac","Open the frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Restore sinus drainage via the natural ostia', 'FESS preserves the sinus mucosa while widening the natural ostia to restore sinus drainage and ventilation. It does not remove turbinates or all mucosa, and it does not involve the lacrimal sac or opening the frontal bone.'),
('78e53bb1-928c-5a11-a81f-492db0963e7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The paranasal sinuses include:', '["Maxillary, ethmoid, sphenoid, occipital","Frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, temporal","Frontal, nasal, ethmoid, sphenoid","Frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, parietal","Frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, sphenoid', 'The four paired paranasal sinuses are the frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, and sphenoid sinuses. The occipital, nasal, temporal, and parietal bones do not contain paranasal sinuses.'),
('9dd1f9df-c481-5233-8be7-2a7f33be1097'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The nasal cavity is divided into right and left halves by:', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal septum divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves. The conchae are projections of the lateral wall, and the ethmoid bone contributes to the septum but is not the division itself.'),
('9c6a62f0-2e22-5423-bbc4-1575cf494ab7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During therapeutic management of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('fa89c129-6162-5ea6-89a8-6bf64b9fecf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('78ca29c3-ef2c-529c-84c8-2f9caf3d607c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In the study of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('221ec3d9-00a2-5d51-8ce0-b9bfbf7e6b6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('3bfb5fb8-4d73-589b-a993-d2df265127ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('355369fd-4a1d-52b5-944a-3f0721d8e882'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During therapeutic management of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('bc30894a-50bf-514b-8839-06060bb7afcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('006fe897-121e-512e-90c0-02a91e51acde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In the study of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('21d929ae-95af-50e1-8048-b13aa1b096e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('b5b0c5a1-47fc-5dd6-85d1-da0d46d0efec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('2036027f-f412-52d8-b6dd-57efaac99ea4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During therapeutic management of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('c6369bbb-def9-5b6a-8b42-efba0b79806f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('a5c49ce6-94f5-5aeb-85c7-9b5108ddef6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In the study of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('2d0a7a3f-806d-5568-9413-aa2247fb5197'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('2794d5b3-e022-57bd-bfb8-21ef9a605938'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The function of the nasal conchae is:', '["To detect smell resulting in decreased functional activity","To produce mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To provide resonance mediated by specific receptor activation","To filter particles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To increase surface area for warming and humidifying inspired air"]'::jsonb, 'To increase surface area for warming and humidifying inspired air', 'The conchae enlarge the mucosal surface area and produce turbulent airflow, warming and humidifying inspired air. They do not detect smell, produce mucus, provide resonance, or primarily filter particles.'),
('90b2e844-644d-50bb-a9b2-3e121dc85d75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During therapeutic management of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('b6442ec8-3ec8-5298-9e90-88e320186467'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'The respiratory epithelium of the nose is:', '["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Stratified squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple cuboidal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple columnar without cilia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells', 'The nasal respiratory mucosa is pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium containing goblet cells. The vestibule is lined by stratified squamous epithelium, and the other listed epithelia do not match the respiratory lining.'),
('842ad1f0-4f02-510a-a29c-e144d3a98170'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In the study of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('5f9400e6-d173-5718-b1e9-10af7a84fbaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('db0b7edc-072a-540c-aea4-69f20d46daec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('0f01140b-47cb-597e-bdf0-9973b27b3376'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During therapeutic management of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('393392cc-b354-5e20-863e-97bbbd99490f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('a5b84487-b21d-5011-9583-4b7e807d7daa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In the study of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('c0157b8e-47fa-57ba-a5b5-98ee85fd6729'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('0fa26262-aa04-5293-a039-def43581c6cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('3fb7fbd1-20bd-5412-b3c2-f57d32c68136'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During therapeutic management of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('db578eb6-2ccd-555e-97f9-935144dc0a14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('0f843e72-b01e-5368-a7a6-9ad354cee786'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In medical practice, Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('e0053569-5048-51cb-8a2b-6fd76910b6a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('dac7380c-1936-576c-89d4-f2ab2e5f8dfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('ff3723f3-155c-5b07-a9fa-e8132e3be3e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During therapeutic management of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('850dcc53-84b7-502b-bf82-6196bbd40ec4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('b23f28b9-5f93-5865-82f7-cda1de985423'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In the study of Nose Paranasal Sinuses, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('3de9e19a-a682-5fa2-8eb8-eba0124d12b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Nose Paranasal Sinuses: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('9dca72dc-77a7-55c1-847b-00c621f5d4c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Nose Paranasal Sinuses: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('69fb477f-0df7-53d3-8866-c1284cda468e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the sphenoidal sinus drains into:?', '["Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoethmoidal recess', 'The sphenoidal sinus opens by a single ostium into the sphenoethmoidal recess, situated above and behind the superior concha. The meati listed drain other sinuses: the middle meatus receives the frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses, while the inferior meatus receives the nasolacrimal duct.'),
('9efd5a3a-a298-58c6-a789-8408371d17df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the sphenoidal sinus is located:?', '["In the maxilla","In the ethmoid","In the temporal bone","In the body of sphenoid bone","In the frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'In the body of sphenoid bone', 'The paired sphenoidal sinuses lie within the body of the sphenoid bone, behind the ethmoid and beneath the sella turcica. Their roof is closely related to the pituitary gland and optic chiasm.'),
('c13980f5-3b1e-55f5-922e-2a01a1e99a1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the sphenoidal sinus is related superiorly to:?', '["Oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasal cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Orbit mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pituitary gland optic chiasm"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary gland optic chiasm', 'Superiorly the sphenoidal sinus is separated by thin bone from the sella turcica containing the pituitary gland, with the optic chiasm above and behind. This close relation explains visual and endocrine complications of sphenoidal sinus disease.'),
('40afa822-75dc-5813-b3d4-857c987e03f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the functions of paranasal sinuses include:?', '["Only resonance","Only warming air","Only lighten skull","Lighten skull","resonance to voice warm/humidify air Only mucus production"]'::jsonb, 'Lighten skull', 'Paranasal sinuses reduce skull weight, add resonance to the voice, and warm and humidify inspired air. Among the choices, lightening the skull is the correctly stated function, whereas the other options are incomplete or inaccurate.'),
('886951b6-cec0-5ac4-94fb-475c8ebf9977'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the maxillary sinus is clinically important for:?', '["Polyps resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinusitis (common infection due to poor drainage)","Tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Fractures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foreign bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinusitis (common infection due to poor drainage)', 'The ostium of the maxillary sinus opens high on its medial wall, so drainage is poor and secretions accumulate, making it the most commonly infected sinus. Polyps, tumors, fractures, and foreign bodies are far less characteristic clinical associations.'),
('ed28adf0-5201-5055-8088-b004a41f046d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The osteomeatal complex is:'' is:', '["The lymphatic drainage of nose","The nerve plexus of nose","The blood supply of nose","The drainage pathway of frontal","maxillary and anterior ethmoidal sinuses The olfactory area"]'::jsonb, 'The drainage pathway of frontal', 'The osteomeatal complex is the functional unit through which the frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into the middle meatus. Obstruction of this pathway predisposes to chronic sinusitis, unlike lymphatic, neural, or vascular structures of the nose.'),
('90d0efa3-3c1d-560c-b9c7-944599ec32da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the sinuses that are present at birth are:?', '["Maxillary and ethmoidal","Only maxillary","Frontal and sphenoidal","Only ethmoidal","All sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary and ethmoidal', 'At birth only the maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are present as rudimentary cavities. The frontal sinus develops around 6-8 years and the sphenoidal sinus around 3-5 years, becoming prominent during childhood.'),
('7168e3d6-98bc-5e8d-ac12-ca2c75043b86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the frontal sinus develops at about:?', '["Age 2 years","Adulthood","Age 6-8 years","Birth","Adolescence"]'::jsonb, 'Age 6-8 years', 'The frontal sinus begins to pneumatize the frontal bone at about 6-8 years of age and reaches full development in adulthood. It is absent, unilaterally or bilaterally, in about 4% of individuals.'),
('eceabfe1-d0fe-5f9c-9286-29241751b0d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the sphenoidal sinus develops at about:?', '["Age 1 year","Adulthood","Age 3-5 years","Adolescence","Birth"]'::jsonb, 'Age 3-5 years', 'The sphenoidal sinus begins to pneumatize at about 3-5 years of age and expands within the sphenoid body thereafter. In contrast, the maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are already present at birth.'),
('0a85414b-e077-53c9-a48e-b2bf321ac5cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the nerve supply of the maxillary sinus is from:?', '["Ophthalmic nerve (V1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary nerve (V2) via superior alveolar and infraorbital nerves","Mandibular nerve (V3) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glossopharyngeal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2) via superior alveolar and infraorbital nerves', 'The maxillary sinus is innervated by branches of the maxillary nerve (V2), chiefly the infraorbital and superior alveolar nerves. This explains referred pain to the upper teeth and cheek in sinusitis; the other listed nerves do not supply the sinus.'),
('43677917-1da7-5525-9cc5-fff658fb4e09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the nerve supply of the frontal sinus is from:?', '["Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Anterior ethmoidal nerve","Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Infraorbital nerve (V2)","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital nerve (V1)', 'The frontal sinus is innervated by the supraorbital nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) that exits through the supraorbital notch. The other listed nerves supply different territories and do not reach the frontal sinus.'),
('6d79987a-6105-589a-aaaf-bdfa897d3bb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The nerve supply of the sphenoidal sinus is from:'' is:', '["Infraorbital nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater occipital nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and sphenopalatine branches (V2)","Glossopharyngeal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and sphenopalatine branches (V2)', 'The sphenoidal sinus receives innervation from the posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and branches of the sphenopalatine nerves (V2). This dual supply reflects its position between the ethmoid and sphenoid regions.'),
('32688dca-f516-5f28-9c0c-8e7ef8cc9e5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the anterior ethmoidal nerve is a branch of:?', '["Maxillary nerve (V2)","Nasociliary nerve (V1)","Mandibular nerve (V3)","Oculomotor nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nasociliary nerve (V1)', 'The anterior ethmoidal nerve arises from the nasociliary nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1). It traverses the anterior ethmoidal foramen to supply the anterior nasal cavity and the skin of the nasal tip.'),
('5c7a43fd-202a-5cb1-844f-260ced966fc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the nasolacrimal duct drains into:?', '["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior meatus beneath the inferior concha. The middle meatus receives the paranasal sinus openings, while the sphenoethmoidal recess receives the sphenoidal sinus.'),
('677411ac-4724-525f-87b1-9c5e7881d01d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the opening of the nasolacrimal duct is located:?', '["Under the middle concha","Under the inferior concha in the inferior meatus","Under the superior concha","In the nasal vestibule","In the sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Under the inferior concha in the inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the inferior concha into the inferior meatus. The middle and superior meati and the sphenoethmoidal recess are drainage sites for the sinuses, not for tears.'),
('da34487f-2300-5c89-8223-7865f21e53f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the choanae (posterior nasal apertures) connect:?', '["Nasal cavity to oral cavity","Nasal cavity to nasopharynx","Nose to oropharynx","Nose to larynx","Nasal cavity to sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity to nasopharynx', 'The choanae are the posterior nasal apertures opening from the nasal cavity into the nasopharynx. They lie behind the nasal septum and are separate from the oral cavity, oropharynx, and larynx.'),
('063c7615-ba70-54f0-9fec-f6a9ae7a62e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the roof of the nasal cavity is formed by:?', '["Only ethmoid","Maxilla and palatine","Nasal bone frontal bone","cribriform plate of ethmoid body of sphenoid","Only sphenoid Nasal and frontal bones only"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal bone frontal bone', 'The roof of the nasal cavity is formed by the nasal bone, nasal process of the frontal bone, cribriform plate of the ethmoid, and body of the sphenoid. Among the choices, the nasal and frontal bones correctly name part of this roof.'),
('6ea556b9-338b-5ff5-aa32-0a6400583bbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The floor of the nasal cavity is formed by:'' is:', '["Only maxilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Only palatine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone","Maxilla and sphenoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Palatine and ethmoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone', 'The floor of the nasal cavity, formed by the hard palate, is composed of the palatine process of the maxilla and the horizontal plate of the palatine bone. These same bones also form the roof of the oral cavity.'),
('2dc2d457-7af9-589c-a555-a612cf0bf39b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the lateral wall of the nasal cavity is formed by:?', '["Maxilla and palatine only Only maxilla and ethmoid Nasal and frontal bones","Maxilla","ethmoid inferior concha","sphenoid palatine","lacrimal Only ethmoid and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla', 'The lateral wall of the nasal cavity is formed mainly by the maxilla and ethmoid, with contributions from the palatine, sphenoid, lacrimal, and inferior concha. Among the garbled choices, the maxilla is the principal bone contributing to this wall.'),
('fffc557f-45c2-5ba0-a4ee-3304e97fd747'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the medial wall of the nasal cavity is:?', '["The nasal septum","The vomer","The maxilla","The sphenoid bone","The ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'The nasal septum', 'The medial wall of the nasal cavity is the nasal septum, formed by the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and the septal cartilage. The vomer alone is only one bony component, not the entire medial wall.'),
('20ad2362-e647-5bae-8e1d-f92ce69752d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the nasal vestibule is:?', '["The roof of the nose resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior part near the choana accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anterior part of nasal cavity lined by skin with hair (vibrissae)","The middle meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The floor of the nose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior part of nasal cavity lined by skin with hair (vibrissae)', 'The nasal vestibule is the dilated anterior part of the nasal cavity lined by skin containing hair follicles (vibrissae) and sebaceous glands. Posterior to it, the cavity is lined by respiratory or olfactory mucosa.'),
('72adb3fc-96f8-5045-9077-934840693c32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the function of vibrissae (nasal hairs) is:?', '["Produce mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","Warm the air accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filter large particles from inspired air","Humidify the air without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Detect smell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filter large particles from inspired air', 'Vibrissae trap large airborne particles, filtering them from inspired air before they reach the respiratory mucosa. Warming, humidification, mucus production, and olfaction are performed by other nasal structures.'),
('bd8bf32b-38c8-5a86-9ae0-651d9f290a19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the internal nasal valve is:?', '["The sphenoethmoidal recess","The olfactory cleft","The choana","The narrowest part of the nasal cavity","at the junction of upper and lower lateral cartilages The opening of the maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'The narrowest part of the nasal cavity', 'The internal nasal valve is the narrowest part of the nasal airway, located at the junction of the upper and lower lateral cartilages. It is the major site of nasal resistance, and its collapse can cause nasal obstruction.'),
('8fcee607-51b7-5c2e-9219-ec9e92e7df8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The angle of the nasal valve is normally:'' is:', '["20-30 degrees","5-10 degrees","60-90 degrees","10-15 degrees","30-45 degrees"]'::jsonb, '10-15 degrees', 'The angle of the internal nasal valve is normally about 10-15 degrees. A narrower angle increases nasal resistance, while a wider angle may follow excessive surgical resection.'),
('0ed940dc-2dc3-5e5f-8a21-79b27f618e6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the respiratory epithelium of the nose is also called:?', '["Olfactory epithelium","Schneiderian membrane","Transitional epithelium","Squamous epithelium","Simple epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Schneiderian membrane', 'The respiratory epithelium lining the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses is called the Schneiderian membrane. It is a pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, distinct from the olfactory epithelium in the upper nasal cavity.'),
('5f5e90eb-51aa-5dbe-b7a8-424214fcf8a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the olfactory epithelium contains:?', '["Olfactory receptor cells","supporting cells","basal cells","Goblet cells only","Only supporting cells Ciliated columnar cells only Only receptor cells"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory receptor cells', 'The olfactory epithelium contains olfactory receptor cells (bipolar neurons), supporting cells, and basal cells. The receptor cells are the defining element that detects odorants and transmits smell to the olfactory bulb.'),
('dbf29352-c545-5391-826a-db91c527b4a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the blood supply of the nasal septum includes:?', '["Facial artery only","Only ethmoidal","Sphenopalatine greater palatine","anterior ethmoidal superior labial arteries","Maxillary artery only Only sphenopalatine"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine greater palatine', 'The nasal septum is supplied posteriorly by the sphenopalatine and greater palatine arteries and anteriorly by the anterior ethmoidal and superior labial arteries. The sphenopalatine artery contributes to Kiesselbach''s plexus, the common site of anterior epistaxis.'),
('886f7bed-2e03-5e99-88df-d3b26e7a6390'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the lymphatic drainage of the anterior nasal cavity goes to:?', '["Parotid nodes","Deep cervical nodes","Retropharyngeal nodes","Submental nodes","Submandibular lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular lymph nodes', 'The anterior part of the nasal cavity and vestibule drain to the submandibular lymph nodes. The posterior nasal cavity and sinuses drain instead to the retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes.'),
('7c79bb3c-84b6-54ba-9f34-83eae6be6e13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the lymphatic drainage of the posterior nasal cavity goes to:?', '["Submental nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Parotid nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes","Submandibular nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes', 'The posterior nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses drain to the retropharyngeal and deep cervical lymph nodes. The anterior nasal cavity instead drains to the submandibular nodes, and axillary nodes do not drain the head.'),
('de9e6a88-6f90-5cbf-9219-4d240da708f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The sphenopalatine foramen is located:'' is:', '["In the roof of nose resulting in decreased functional activity","In the inferior meatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the lateral wall of nasal cavity (posterior end of middle meatus)","In the floor of nose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the nasal septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the lateral wall of nasal cavity (posterior end of middle meatus)', 'The sphenopalatine foramen lies in the lateral wall of the nasal cavity at the posterior end of the middle meatus, formed by the sphenopalatine notch of the palatine bone. It transmits the sphenopalatine vessels and the nasopalatine nerve.'),
('80f622c0-7ee6-55c4-80d4-99975704c8f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the sphenopalatine foramen transmits:?', '["Optic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Ophthalmic artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenopalatine artery vein and nasopalatine nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine artery vein and nasopalatine nerve', 'The sphenopalatine foramen transmits the sphenopalatine artery and vein and the nasopalatine nerve. This is surgically important because the sphenopalatine artery is a frequent source of refractory epistaxis.'),
('00527aec-bb6e-54a9-a50f-ff4946588ba7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the nasopalatine nerve is a branch of:?', '["Mandibular nerve (V3) resulting in decreased functional activity","Ophthalmic nerve (V1) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary nerve (V2) via pterygopalatine ganglion","Facial nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glossopharyngeal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2) via pterygopalatine ganglion', 'The nasopalatine nerve arises from the pterygopalatine (sphenopalatine) ganglion, which is associated with the maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve (V2). It passes through the sphenopalatine foramen onto the nasal septum.'),
('66ec898d-0e4e-521c-ac5d-d8cf1f551011'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the nasopalatine nerve supplies:?', '["Lateral wall of nose resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal septum and anterior hard palate (incisive foramen)","Maxillary sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenoidal sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum and anterior hard palate (incisive foramen)', 'The nasopalatine nerve descends along the nasal septum and exits through the incisive foramen to supply the anterior hard palate. Its distribution is therefore limited to the septum and anterior palate, not to the sinus walls.'),
('8263cfa6-02c8-574c-91bc-e3ea5baec301'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the greater palatine nerve supplies:?', '["Hard palate and adjacent gingiva","Soft palate","Nasal septum","Maxillary sinus","Upper teeth"]'::jsonb, 'Hard palate and adjacent gingiva', 'The greater palatine nerve passes through the greater palatine foramen to supply the mucous membrane of the hard palate and the adjacent gingiva. The soft palate is innervated by the lesser palatine nerves.'),
('1b5b919a-9d8d-5eb9-959a-c9980fc295f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the lesser palatine nerves supply:?', '["Tonsils","Uvula","Soft palate","Nasal septum","Hard palate"]'::jsonb, 'Soft palate', 'The lesser palatine nerves pass through the lesser palatine foramina to supply the soft palate. The hard palate and gingiva receive the greater palatine nerve, while the uvula is a part of the soft palate rather than a separate nerve territory.'),
('1d560f4f-23a8-54cc-80e3-bbb9dffc75fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The anterior superior alveolar nerve supplies:'' is:', '["Lower teeth","Upper molars","Palate","Upper incisors and canines","maxillary sinus Upper premolars"]'::jsonb, 'Upper incisors and canines', 'The anterior superior alveolar nerve, a branch of the infraorbital nerve, supplies the upper incisors and canines. The premolars are served by the middle superior alveolar nerve and the molars by the posterior superior alveolar nerve.'),
('a9395078-7b19-543b-805a-120ac1c2eb58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the middle superior alveolar nerve supplies:?', '["All upper teeth","Upper incisors","Maxillary sinus only","Upper molars","Upper premolars"]'::jsonb, 'Upper premolars', 'The middle superior alveolar nerve supplies the upper premolars. The incisors are served by the anterior superior alveolar nerve and the molars by the posterior superior alveolar nerve.'),
('b719b044-8ca4-5ac9-b06d-e4e3daf9e3e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the posterior superior alveolar nerve supplies:?', '["Upper premolars resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper incisors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower teeth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper molar teeth and maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Upper molar teeth and maxillary sinus', 'The posterior superior alveolar nerve supplies the upper molar teeth and the lining of the maxillary sinus. This distribution explains dental pain referred to the region in maxillary sinusitis.'),
('43419f01-035c-5a21-a687-0f2b7b17af3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the infraorbital nerve is a continuation of:?', '["Mandibular nerve (V3)","Maxillary nerve (V2)","Facial nerve","Ophthalmic nerve (V1)","Nasociliary nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2)', 'The infraorbital nerve is the direct continuation of the maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve (V2) after it traverses the pterygopalatine fossa. It emerges through the infraorbital foramen to supply the midface.'),
('b33437b5-354b-55e9-9422-a5f4653e8c55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the infraorbital nerve emerges at:?', '["Foramen ovale","Foramen rotundum","Mental foramen","Supraorbital notch","Infraorbital foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Infraorbital foramen', 'The infraorbital nerve emerges onto the face through the infraorbital foramen, about 1 cm below the inferior orbital margin. This foramen is a key landmark for local anesthetic blocks of the midface.'),
('6154710a-1413-5cdd-9099-981134f3ce5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the infraorbital nerve supplies:?', '["Lower eyelid","upper lip","cheek side of nose","Upper eyelid Forehead","Temple Lower lip"]'::jsonb, 'Lower eyelid', 'The infraorbital nerve supplies the skin of the lower eyelid, upper lip, cheek, and side of the nose. The upper eyelid and forehead are served by the ophthalmic division (supraorbital nerve), and the lower lip by the mental nerve.'),
('9cb45c15-eb1a-58c9-9b1e-774c2e971f36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The maxillary sinus is related medially to:'' is:', '["Orbit","Infratemporal fossa","Pterygopalatine fossa","Oral cavity","Nasal cavity (lateral wall of nose)"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity (lateral wall of nose)', 'Medially the maxillary sinus is related to the lateral wall of the nasal cavity, opening into the middle meatus via the hiatus semilunaris. The orbit, pterygopalatine fossa, and infratemporal fossa lie on other surfaces of the sinus.'),
('0150294d-7bf6-5555-b932-bfe357ca79b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the maxillary sinus is related posteriorly to:?', '["Oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cranial cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae","Orbit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae', 'Posteriorly the maxillary sinus is related to the infratemporal and pterygopalatine fossae. Superiorly it borders the orbit, medially the nasal cavity, and inferiorly the tooth roots and hard palate.'),
('b24d0702-1fa4-5120-9fbd-3c04b634c152'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the maxillary sinus is related laterally to:?', '["Sphenoid bone","Frontal bone","Palatine bone","Zygomatic bone","Nasal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic bone', 'Laterally the maxillary sinus is related to the zygomatic bone. The frontal, palatine, sphenoid, and nasal bones do not form the lateral boundary of this sinus.'),
('d38e13ab-c7b7-561e-8906-9cd4d6c0c02a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the root of the maxillary sinus is:?', '["The roof","The medial wall","The posterior wall","The floor (close to tooth roots)","The lateral wall"]'::jsonb, 'The floor (close to tooth roots)', 'The root (floor) of the maxillary sinus lies close to the roots of the upper teeth, sometimes separated by only a thin layer of bone. This proximity explains how dental infection can spread into the sinus.'),
('146e7433-ab40-55ed-9069-01402eed90b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the maxillary sinus can be infected by:?', '["Extension of dental infection (periapical abscess) through the thin floor","Lymphatic spread accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood spread mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergic reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of dental infection (periapical abscess) through the thin floor', 'The thin floor of the maxillary sinus overlies the upper premolar and molar roots, so a periapical abscess can erode through it and cause odontogenic sinusitis. The other listed routes are not the characteristic mechanism.'),
('ba712e14-057a-5483-9fbc-784df8aca870'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the frontal sinus is absent in about:?', '["4% of people (unilateral or bilateral)","50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20% mediated by specific receptor activation","10% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4% of people (unilateral or bilateral)', 'The frontal sinus is absent in about 4% of individuals, either unilaterally or bilaterally. Its size varies greatly, and it becomes radiologically appreciable only from about 6-8 years of age onward.'),
('0c24e45d-76fe-5147-b14f-6169b4490520'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The frontal sinus is supplied by:'' is:', '["Supraorbital and anterior ethmoidal arteries","Infraorbital artery","Posterior ethmoidal artery","Sphenopalatine artery","Greater palatine artery"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital and anterior ethmoidal arteries', 'The frontal sinus is supplied by the supraorbital artery and the anterior ethmoidal artery, both branches of the ophthalmic artery. The infraorbital, posterior ethmoidal, sphenopalatine, and greater palatine arteries serve other structures.'),
('05efa204-bf05-5a83-860a-475779ef2ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the ethmoidal sinuses consist of:?', '["Fixed number of 10","1-2 air cells","20-30 air cells","30-40 air cells","3-18 air cells each side"]'::jsonb, '3-18 air cells each side', 'The ethmoidal sinuses form a variable labyrinth of 3-18 air cells on each side, grouped into anterior and posterior divisions. Their number and size vary widely among individuals and with age.'),
('2d59cbac-507e-5150-9056-5ed8ba9a2b4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the anterior ethmoidal air cells drain into:?', '["Middle meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Inferior meatus","Frontal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus', 'The anterior ethmoidal air cells drain into the middle meatus, together with the frontal and maxillary sinuses. The posterior ethmoidal cells drain into the superior meatus, and the sphenoidal sinus drains into the sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('9b41d680-478b-5a91-8bc5-d56162f2decd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the middle ethmoidal air cells drain into:?', '["Middle meatus (bulla ethmoidalis)","Frontal sinus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (bulla ethmoidalis)', 'The middle ethmoidal air cells open through the ethmoidal bulla into the middle meatus. The anterior group also drains here, whereas the posterior cells drain into the superior meatus and the sphenoid sinus into the sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('9104d352-5f91-5681-a453-b9936052947d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the posterior ethmoidal air cells drain into:?', '["Superior meatus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus","Frontal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior meatus', 'Posterior ethmoidal air cells open into the superior meatus, the shortest of the nasal meatuses. In contrast, the middle and anterior ethmoidal cells drain into the middle meatus.'),
('65fc6af3-64e5-5c5c-b9bf-f3fc3eb75683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the ethmoidal labyrinth separates:?', '["Nasal cavity from the mouth","Frontal sinus from maxillary sinus","Nasal cavity from cranial cavity","Right and left nasal cavities","Nasal cavity from the orbit"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity from the orbit', 'The ethmoidal labyrinth forms the lateral wall of the nasal cavity and the medial wall of the orbit, so it separates the nasal cavity from the orbit. Its thin lateral surface is the lamina papyracea, which is easily fractured by trauma.'),
('0c57367e-8765-580c-bae5-ea1e4cb4b2b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The lamina papyracea is:'' is:', '["The thin lateral wall of ethmoid labyrinth (forms medial orbital wall)","The nasal septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The roof of the ethmoid mediated by specific receptor activation","The wall of the sphenoid sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The floor of the maxillary sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thin lateral wall of ethmoid labyrinth (forms medial orbital wall)', 'The lamina papyracea is the very thin lateral wall of the ethmoid labyrinth that forms most of the medial orbital wall. Because it is paper-thin, it is prone to blow-out fractures and is a route for spread of ethmoid sinus infection into the orbit.'),
('f466a1bb-f49d-5d8e-aa0e-a08b9093d8ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the sphenoidal sinus is divided by:?', '["A thick wall resulting in decreased functional activity","A cartilaginous septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thin bony septum (often asymmetric)","septum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fibrous membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin bony septum (often asymmetric)', 'The sphenoidal sinus is divided into right and left halves by a thin, often asymmetric bony septum that frequently deviates to one side. This asymmetry matters during transsphenoidal surgery.'),
('50b7d10a-8665-5f29-aed1-744f30425892'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the sphenoidal sinus relations include:?', '["Nasal cavity inferiorly resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxillary sinus laterally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Orbit anteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal sinus superiorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pituitary superiorly cavernous sinus laterally optic nerve superolaterally"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary superiorly cavernous sinus laterally optic nerve superolaterally', 'The sphenoidal sinus lies below the pituitary gland and optic chiasm, and lateral to the cavernous sinus and internal carotid artery. Disease here can therefore threaten vision and cause cavernous sinus complications.'),
('a7c5564e-e211-5beb-ad80-ebc95698cb8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the sphenoidal sinus receives nerve supply from:?', '["Supraorbital nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasociliary nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auriculotemporal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2)","Infraorbital nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2)', 'The sphenoidal sinus is supplied by the posterior ethmoidal nerve (branch of V1) and by orbital branches of the pterygopalatine (sphenopalatine) ganglion distributed via the maxillary nerve (V2).'),
('ff68caff-c3fc-5989-89f3-b8ef86f99a7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the maxillary sinus drains into which part of the nasal cavity?', '["Atrium of middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus (via semilunar hiatus)"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (via semilunar hiatus)', 'The maxillary sinus opens through the semilunar hiatus into the middle meatus. Its ostium sits high on the medial wall, which impedes dependent drainage and predisposes to sinusitis.'),
('e0b04eac-076f-5673-bae4-742b2a8334c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the external nose is supported by:?', '["Only cartilages","Only nasal bones","Ethmoid bone","Nasal bones and cartilages","Maxilla and frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal bones and cartilages', 'The external nose has a bony framework of the nasal bones and frontal processes of the maxillae, reinforced by lateral, alar, and septal cartilages, so both bone and cartilage support it.'),
('89013f67-8fee-5067-b4c5-fb063f72b0d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The nasal cavity is divided by:'' is:', '["Nasal septum","Middle turbinate","Vomer","Inferior turbinate","Cribriform plate"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal cavity is split into right and left halves by the nasal septum, made up of the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and the septal cartilage. The other structures listed are not septal or do not divide the cavity.'),
('08d62d1b-dba7-5788-9143-68c84a742ef3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the main blood supply to the nasal septum is from:?', '["Anterior ethmoidal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior ethmoidal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sphenopalatine artery (terminal branch of maxillary artery)","Facial artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater palatine artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine artery (terminal branch of maxillary artery)', 'The sphenopalatine artery, the terminal branch of the maxillary artery, is the principal blood supply of the nasal septum. Its branches help form Little''s area, the common site of anterior epistaxis.'),
('6b8c1958-d581-56fb-8bf5-f08b4faee722'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the roof of the nasal cavity includes:?', '["Palatine bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vomer mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphenoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone', 'The roof of the nasal cavity is formed chiefly by the cribriform plate of the ethmoid, through which the olfactory nerve fibers pass upward to the olfactory bulb. Fracture here can tear these fibers and cause anosmia with CSF rhinorrhea.'),
('fadbdfa2-e7f4-5bff-b6ec-95e75cf6316d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the superior meatus drains:?', '["Maxillary sinus","Posterior ethmoidal sinuses","Frontal sinus","Sphenoid sinus","Anterior ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal sinuses', 'The superior meatus, the shortest meatus, receives the openings of the posterior ethmoidal air cells. The maxillary, frontal, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into the middle meatus.'),
('68a73fb2-9bc4-5fe8-aa7a-c9e391d2eb1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the middle meatus drains:?', '["Sphenoid sinus","Frontal","maxillary","and anterior ethmoidal sinuses","All sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal', 'The middle meatus collects drainage from the frontal sinus, maxillary sinus, and anterior ethmoidal air cells via the hiatus semilunaris and ethmoidal bulla. Among the listed choices, only ''Frontal'' names a sinus that opens into the middle meatus.'),
('d2eed5c7-5615-5ba6-8a7a-a298a139da15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the sphenoethmoidal recess drains:?', '["Sphenoid sinus","Maxillary sinus","Posterior ethmoidal sinuses","Frontal sinus","Anterior ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoid sinus', 'The sphenoidal sinus opens into the sphenoethmoidal recess, the narrow pocket above the superior concha. This is why the recess is named for both the sphenoid and the ethmoid.'),
('3cc65290-16ce-5a44-bba7-27f5365edac9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''Olfactory epithelium is located:'' is:', '["In the nasopharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","In the inferior meatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the vestibule mediated by specific receptor activation","In the middle meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the roof of nasal cavity and adjacent septum and lateral wall"]'::jsonb, 'In the roof of nasal cavity and adjacent septum and lateral wall', 'Olfactory mucosa covers the roof of the nasal cavity and extends onto the adjacent upper septum and the superior concha on the lateral wall. Its receptor neurons project through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb.'),
('da0817b6-57bd-575f-9c1c-a8c209b335ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the nerve supply to the nasal septum includes:?', '["Facial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Glossopharyngeal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and nasopalatine nerve (V2)","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and nasopalatine nerve (V2)', 'The nasal septum is innervated by the anterior ethmoidal nerve (branch of V1) and the nasopalatine nerve (branch of V2 via the pterygopalatine ganglion). These are sensory branches of the trigeminal nerve, not the motor or visceral nerves listed.'),
('da2f708f-7691-5cfe-979e-2f43fe0fa82e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the inferior turbinate is:?', '["A separate bone (inferior conchal bone)","Part of the maxilla","Part of the ethmoid bone","Part of the sphenoid bone","Part of the palatine bone"]'::jsonb, 'A separate bone (inferior conchal bone)', 'The inferior nasal concha is an independent bone (inferior conchal bone) articulating with the maxilla, lacrimal, and palatine bones. This contrasts with the superior and middle conchae, which are parts of the ethmoid.'),
('1ef3c89c-b081-5740-85c5-0392a9746ef1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the paranasal sinuses develop as:?', '["Outgrowths from the nasal cavity","Derivatives of the sphenoid bone","Invaginations from the oral cavity","Extensions of the nasopharynx","Remnants of branchial arches"]'::jsonb, 'Outgrowths from the nasal cavity', 'The paranasal sinuses develop as outgrowths (diverticula) of the mucosa of the nasal cavity into the surrounding bones. This explains why each sinus maintains a drainage opening into the nasal cavity.'),
('5f884279-cf14-5953-b845-a3cbeaa47372'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the sphenoid sinus drains into:?', '["Middle meatus","Nasopharynx","Inferior meatus","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoethmoidal recess', 'The sphenoidal sinus opens through its ostium into the sphenoethmoidal recess, located in the upper posterior nasal cavity above the superior concha.'),
('b6202291-6a05-5dd8-b699-28d495e79b48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the maxillary sinus is innervated by:?', '["Hypoglossal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Glossopharyngeal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infraorbital nerve (V2) and superior alveolar nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Infraorbital nerve (V2) and superior alveolar nerves', 'The maxillary sinus is supplied by the infraorbital nerve and the superior alveolar nerves, branches of the maxillary nerve (V2). This arrangement explains referred pain from sinusitis to the upper teeth.'),
('17fd72b8-e0ee-5ac4-ae1a-71b6eee6b0e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The nasolacrimal duct opens into:'' is:', '["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the inferior meatus beneath the anterior end of the inferior concha. This is why the inferior meatus must be avoided in sinus surgery at other sites.'),
('baad405c-6afa-5068-abfd-5bb8d000644e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube is located:?', '["In the nasopharynx","behind the inferior meatus","Behind the soft palate","In the middle meatus","In the laryngopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal orifice of the auditory (eustachian) tube opens on the lateral wall of the nasopharynx, behind and above the posterior end of the inferior concha. This site allows middle ear pressure equilibration with the pharynx.'),
('542ce9b6-f9dc-5b4e-a791-b83355e9b717'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: adenoids (pharyngeal tonsil) are located:?', '["In the oropharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","In the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Under the tongue mediated by specific receptor activation","In the nasopharynx (roof and posterior wall)","In the nasal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the nasopharynx (roof and posterior wall)', 'The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) lies in the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx. When enlarged in children it can obstruct the choanae and block the auditory tube openings.'),
('68d6a3ff-851c-5bc7-b434-d56bc81d7e5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the palatine tonsils are located:?', '["In the nasal cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","At the base of tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the oropharynx between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches","In the laryngopharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the nasopharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the oropharynx between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches', 'The palatine tonsils occupy the tonsillar fossa of the oropharynx, bounded anteriorly by the palatoglossal arch and posteriorly by the palatopharyngeal arch.'),
('381af51a-0514-5776-90ce-4ca7b5a6514d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the lingual tonsil is located:?', '["In the nasopharynx","Under the epithelium of soft palate","In the oropharynx lateral wall","At the base of the tongue","In the larynx"]'::jsonb, 'At the base of the tongue', 'The lingual tonsils are collections of lymphoid tissue in the mucosa over the posterior third (base) of the tongue. They form the anterior-inferior part of Waldeyer''s ring.'),
('ed61332b-19f7-5025-8128-1b6b738adb94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: waldeyer''s ring includes:?', '["Pharyngeal","tubal","palatine","and lingual tonsils","Salivary glands"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal', 'Waldeyer''s ring is the lymphoid circle guarding the entry of the aerodigestive tract, composed of the pharyngeal, tubal, palatine, and lingual tonsils. Among the listed choices, ''Pharyngeal'' names one member of this ring.'),
('563958de-f66b-5788-bf7d-9d4b62fd1c1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The paranasal sinuses are absent at birth except:'' is:', '["Sphenoid sinus","All sinuses present at birth","Frontal sinus","None present at birth","Maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses', 'At birth only the maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are present; the sphenoid sinus begins to appear at about 2 years and the frontal sinus last, becoming visible only around 6-7 years of age.'),
('b028d2d7-dee1-5f2a-baab-cee64623caac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: olfaction (smell) is mediated by:?', '["Cranial nerve I (olfactory nerve)","Cranial nerve V","Cranial nerve VII","Cranial nerve X","Cranial nerve IX"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial nerve I (olfactory nerve)', 'Smell is carried by the olfactory nerve (CN I), whose fibers arise in the olfactory epithelium and pass through the cribriform plate to synapse in the olfactory bulb. The other cranial nerves listed have motor, sensory, or parasympathetic roles unrelated to smell.'),
('44659aae-6f0d-55c3-85bf-e9da87891794'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: anosmia can result from:?', '["Head trauma (cribriform plate fracture)","nasal polyps accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","or infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sinusitis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma (cribriform plate fracture)', 'Anosmia follows damage to the olfactory pathway, classically a cribriform plate fracture in head trauma that tears the olfactory filaments. Nasal polyps or mucosal infections can also impair smell by blocking odorant access to the olfactory epithelium.'),
('c0bdf1bd-636e-5c57-8f06-9df65064c276'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the pterygopalatine ganglion is located:?', '["In the infratemporal fossa","In the orbit","In the pterygopalatine fossa","In the nasal cavity","In the maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'In the pterygopalatine fossa', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion lies in the pterygopalatine fossa, suspended from the maxillary nerve. Its parasympathetic fibers supply the nasal mucosa, palate, and lacrimal gland.'),
('8f173952-04bc-57b9-a47d-8b29606432f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: post-nasal drip is caused by:?', '["Gastric reflux resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excessive mucus draining from sinuses or nose into the pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive mucus draining from sinuses or nose into the pharynx', 'Post-nasal drip results when excessive mucus produced in the nose or paranasal sinuses drains backward into the pharynx, commonly in rhinosinusitis or allergic rhinitis. It typically causes throat clearing and a sensation of phlegm.'),
('14aa489e-54f8-567b-8e81-1f21cb6ec7fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: caldwell-luc procedure is used for:?', '["Ethmoidectomy resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasal polypectomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Access to the maxillary sinus via the canine fossa","Frontal sinus surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenoid sinus surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Access to the maxillary sinus via the canine fossa', 'The Caldwell-Luc operation enters the maxillary sinus through an incision in the canine fossa to treat chronic maxillary sinusitis or remove antral pathology. It does not approach the ethmoid, frontal, or sphenoid sinuses.'),
('0d26b7a8-9ccf-5978-be52-cba538da7a29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''Functional endoscopic sinus surgery (FESS) aims to:'' is:', '["Remove turbinates","Restore sinus drainage via the natural ostia","Remove all sinus mucosa","Drain the lacrimal sac","Open the frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Restore sinus drainage via the natural ostia', 'FESS preserves sinus mucosa and widens the natural ostia and ostiomeatal complex to restore ventilation and drainage. It avoids removal of healthy mucosa, turbinates, or the lacrimal apparatus.'),
('dbec8322-4e05-581c-b2eb-d12f72472698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the paranasal sinuses include:?', '["Maxillary, ethmoid, sphenoid, occipital","Frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, temporal","Frontal, nasal, ethmoid, sphenoid","Frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, parietal","Frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal, maxillary, ethmoid, sphenoid', 'The four paired paranasal sinuses are the frontal, maxillary, ethmoidal, and sphenoidal sinuses, all draining into the nasal cavity. The occipital, temporal, and parietal bones do not contain paranasal sinuses.'),
('f781b2b0-532b-5af4-a249-22838fd3779c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the nasal cavity is divided into right and left by. which option is most accurate?', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal septum, formed by the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and the septal cartilage, divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves. The conchae are lateral wall structures and do not divide the cavity.'),
('48070169-1c65-5abc-93b2-c49b8ee98ede'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: choose the most accurate statement about nose paranasal sinuses: the nasal cavity is divided into right and left by?', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal cavity is divided into two halves by the nasal septum, composed of the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, vomer, and septal cartilage. The conchae project from the lateral wall and do not divide the cavity.'),
('e6e6e00d-4444-598f-a7bd-92f8069193b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: for nose paranasal sinuses, identify the correct response: the nasal cavity is divided into right and left by?', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal septum divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves, being formed by the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, vomer, and septal cartilage. The conchae are lateral wall projections, not divisional structures.'),
('0a74873e-e150-5957-ab92-f78958d5f6bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the nasal cavity is divided into right and left by. select the best answer?', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal cavity is separated into right and left halves by the nasal septum, which combines the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and septal cartilage. The conchae belong to the lateral wall and do not divide the cavity.'),
('8372115c-d7ba-54e4-b032-e481a866ab51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''Which response correctly addresses this Nose Paranasal Sinuses item: the nasal cavity is divided into right and left by'' is:', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal septum divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves, formed by the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and the septal cartilage. The conchae are lateral wall projections and do not partition the cavity.'),
('020aab78-fb6b-522b-844d-3148e87ffba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the nasal septum is formed by. which option is most accurate?', '["Perpendicular plate of ethmoid","vomer","septal cartilage","Vomer and maxilla","Only ethmoid Only vomer Ethmoid and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Perpendicular plate of ethmoid', 'The nasal septum is formed by the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and the septal cartilage. Of the listed choices, the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid is a named and genuine component of the septum.'),
('bf578b15-4f51-502d-8a6d-e0d8d2f07fe6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: choose the most accurate statement about nose paranasal sinuses: the nasal septum is formed by?', '["Perpendicular plate of ethmoid","vomer","septal cartilage","Vomer and maxilla","Only ethmoid Only vomer Ethmoid and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Perpendicular plate of ethmoid', 'The nasal septum consists of the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and the septal cartilage. Among the options, the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid is a true septal component; the maxilla and sphenoid are not part of the septum.'),
('ed4b0777-e4ec-5047-8c9e-1b5cab2d66f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the nasal cavity is divided into right and left by:?', '["Inferior concha","Nasal septum","Ethmoid bone","Middle concha","Superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal septum', 'The nasal septum, formed by the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, vomer, and septal cartilage, divides the nasal cavity into right and left halves. The conchae are lateral wall projections and do not divide the cavity.'),
('517bfe86-03a5-5fde-a0c9-6256b35cb7b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the nasal septum is formed by:?', '["Perpendicular plate of ethmoid","vomer","septal cartilage","Vomer and maxilla","Only ethmoid Only vomer Ethmoid and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Perpendicular plate of ethmoid', 'The nasal septum is made of the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid, the vomer, and the septal cartilage. Of the choices listed, the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid is an authentic component, while the maxilla and sphenoid are not.'),
('7b09a879-12e9-52c3-9e98-b746614de7ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the nasal cavity has how many meatuses?', '["2 (superior and inferior) resulting in decreased functional activity","3 (superior, middle, inferior)","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (superior, middle, inferior)', 'The lateral nasal wall bears three meatuses lying beneath the superior, middle, and inferior conchae, plus the sphenoethmoidal recess above the superior concha. Each meatus receives specific sinus or duct openings.'),
('cc198bb8-17e6-51c9-9baa-a3542d520530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The inferior meatus contains the opening of:'' is:', '["Nasolacrimal duct","Frontal sinus","Anterior ethmoidal sinuses","Maxillary sinus","Sphenoidal sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Nasolacrimal duct', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the anterior end of the inferior concha into the inferior meatus. The frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses instead drain into the middle meatus.'),
('f9128e28-6e07-5480-8a8f-f28039e7db70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the middle meatus contains the openings of:?', '["Posterior ethmoidal only resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasolacrimal duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphenoidal sinus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal sinus maxillary sinus anterior ethmoidal sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal sinus maxillary sinus anterior ethmoidal sinuses', 'The middle meatus receives the openings of the frontal sinus, maxillary sinus, and anterior ethmoidal air cells through the hiatus semilunaris and ethmoidal bulla. The posterior ethmoidal cells and sphenoid sinus drain elsewhere, and the nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior meatus.'),
('c3a2eedd-5e84-5581-8867-3be818a7754e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the superior meatus contains the openings of:?', '["Frontal sinus","Nasolacrimal duct","Posterior ethmoidal sinuses","Sphenoidal sinus","Maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal sinuses', 'The superior meatus receives the openings of the posterior ethmoidal air cells. The frontal and maxillary sinuses open into the middle meatus, the nasolacrimal duct into the inferior meatus, and the sphenoid sinus into the sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('1bd94ae8-40f0-52d4-a821-a1c1a5b46447'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the sphenoethmoidal recess is located:?', '["In the choana","In the inferior meatus","In the middle meatus","Above the superior concha","opening of sphenoidal sinus In the vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Above the superior concha', 'The sphenoethmoidal recess is the pocket in the upper posterior nasal cavity above the superior concha, where the sphenoid sinus opens. It is distinct from the meatuses and from the vestibule or choana.'),
('8ed2b4eb-ae45-55e6-895e-a1ca9c2cad59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the conchae (turbinates) are projections of which bone?', '["Sphenoid bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Vomer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ethmoid bone (superior and middle concha); inferior concha is separate bone"]'::jsonb, 'Ethmoid bone (superior and middle concha); inferior concha is separate bone', 'The superior and middle conchae are projections of the ethmoid bone, whereas the inferior concha is a separate bone. The other listed bones do not give rise to the turbinates.'),
('8b5f6b0a-cfcf-51f8-b969-723ee74e743a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the function of nasal conchae is:', '["To detect smell resulting in decreased functional activity","To produce mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To provide resonance mediated by specific receptor activation","To filter particles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To increase surface area for warming and humidifying inspired air"]'::jsonb, 'To increase surface area for warming and humidifying inspired air', 'The conchae project into the nasal cavity and greatly enlarge the mucosal surface, so inspired air is warmed and humidified as it passes over the mucosa. Smell, mucus production, and resonance relate to other parts of the nose.'),
('d20fa1df-424a-5bb6-baf3-c443139c63ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The olfactory epithelium is located:'' is:', '["In the vestibule resulting in decreased functional activity","In the sphenoethmoidal recess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the respiratory region mediated by specific receptor activation","In the inferior meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the roof of nose and medial surface of superior concha"]'::jsonb, 'In the roof of nose and medial surface of superior concha', 'Olfactory epithelium lines the roof of the nasal cavity and the medial surface of the superior concha, where odor-bearing air reaches it. The vestibule, sphenoethmoidal recess, and inferior meatus do not carry olfactory mucosa.'),
('331a5d52-9853-5b3c-9fbf-fc597ea4eb8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the respiratory epithelium of nose is:', '["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Stratified squamous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transitional mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple cuboidal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple columnar without cilia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells', 'The respiratory mucosa of the nasal cavity is pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium containing goblet cells, which move mucus posteriorly. Stratified squamous lines the vestibule, and the other epithelial types are not found in the respiratory region.'),
('80e2c1e6-3e92-5578-85ff-21fc88625fbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the nose receives blood supply from which arteries?', '["Anterior ethmoidal (ICA)","posterior ethmoidal (ICA) sphenopalatine (ECA)","greater palatine (ECA) superior labial (ECA)","Only internal carotid Only facial artery","Only ophthalmic artery Only external carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ethmoidal (ICA)', 'The nasal cavity is supplied by branches of both the internal carotid (anterior and posterior ethmoidal) and external carotid (sphenopalatine, greater palatine, superior labial) arteries. The anterior ethmoidal artery is one of the internal carotid contributions.'),
('8e510f89-9344-5de4-8aba-a44235cd657d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the sphenopalatine artery (main supply to nasal cavity) is a branch of:', '["Maxillary artery","Ophthalmic artery","Anterior ethmoidal","Posterior ethmoidal","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary artery', 'The sphenopalatine artery, the principal artery to the nasal cavity, arises from the maxillary artery within the pterygopalatine fossa and enters the nose through the sphenopalatine foramen. The other listed arteries do not give rise to this vessel.'),
('e5201b33-c149-557a-aa8e-a2aa2f926f6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: little''s area (kiesselbach''s plexus) is:', '["Opening of maxillary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Opening of nasolacrimal duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Olfactory area mediated by specific receptor activation","An area on anterior nasal septum with rich anastomosis of arteries","Opening of sphenoidal sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An area on anterior nasal septum with rich anastomosis of arteries', 'Little''s area is a zone on the anterior nasal septum where several arteries (sphenopalatine, greater palatine, superior labial, anterior ethmoidal) anastomose. It is a common site of epistaxis and has no relation to sinus or duct openings.'),
('ac8e1e0f-0d36-5c5a-89f4-765227e2cb51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: little''s area is formed by anastomosis of:', '["Only anterior ethmoidal","All arteries of the nose","Sphenopalatine only Facial only","Sphenopalatine greater palatine","superior labial anterior ethmoidal arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine greater palatine', 'Little''s area is formed by anastomosis of the sphenopalatine and greater palatine arteries together with the superior labial and anterior ethmoidal branches. These vessels converge on the anterior nasal septum.'),
('01d3b149-071a-58c2-b889-75ebcf058a5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The most common site of epistaxis is:'' is:', '["Little''s area (anterior nasal septum)","Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Little''s area (anterior nasal septum)', 'Most nosebleeds arise from Little''s area on the anterior nasal septum because of its dense arterial anastomosis. The meatuses and recess are not the usual site of spontaneous epistaxis.'),
('22a1db72-dd3a-5089-8e6b-2677de527b6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the nerve supply to the nasal cavity includes:', '["Glossopharyngeal only resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2) nerves","Hypoglossal only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ethmoidal (V1) and sphenopalatine (V2) nerves', 'General sensation to the nasal cavity is carried by the anterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and branches of the sphenopalatine nerve (V2). The glossopharyngeal, facial, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves do not provide nasal sensory supply.'),
('3fb85f79-3772-507c-a80f-88afcb161ef5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the olfactory nerve (cn i) filaments pass through:', '["Foramen ovale","Foramen rotundum","Optic canal","Cribriform plate of ethmoid","Superior orbital fissure"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate of ethmoid', 'The filaments of the olfactory nerve pass through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to enter the anterior cranial fossa and reach the olfactory bulb. The foramen ovale, foramen rotundum, optic canal, and superior orbital fissure transmit other structures.'),
('5455a7e0-adb8-54c3-90d4-c8d5a6a3247b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the paranasal sinuses are:', '["Frontal","maxillary","sphenoidal","occipital","Frontal ethmoidal"]'::jsonb, 'sphenoidal', 'The paranasal sinuses are the frontal, maxillary, ethmoidal, and sphenoidal sinuses. The occipital bone bears no paranasal sinus, and the sphenoidal sinus is one of the recognised four.'),
('006c51cc-beeb-5e8d-b336-f628ac8e3a15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the largest paranasal sinus is:', '["Ethmoidal sinuses","Frontal sinus","Maxillary sinus","Sphenoidal sinus","All are equal"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary sinus', 'The maxillary sinus is the largest paranasal sinus, lying within the body of the maxilla. The frontal, ethmoidal, and sphenoidal sinuses are smaller and do not equal it in size.'),
('8d8cc0e9-450f-5a80-83c8-81781f94319b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the maxillary sinus drains into:', '["Inferior meatus","Middle meatus (via hiatus semilunaris)","Nasal vestibule","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (via hiatus semilunaris)', 'The maxillary sinus opens into the middle meatus through the hiatus semilunaris. Its high opening contributes to poor natural drainage, and it does not drain into the inferior meatus or sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('e43033c2-3ef6-5cc1-b4fe-c8c1791f89dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The maxillary sinus opening is located:'' is:', '["On its lateral wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Low on its medial wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High on its medial wall (making drainage difficult when head erect)","On its roof without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On its floor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High on its medial wall (making drainage difficult when head erect)', 'The maxillary sinus ostium sits high on its medial wall just below the orbital floor, so natural drainage against gravity is poor when the head is upright. This anatomic feature predisposes to sinusitis.'),
('b860b645-046f-5f0b-8a2c-789489690105'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the maxillary sinus is related superiorly to:', '["Nasal cavity","Cranial cavity","Orbit (floor of orbit)","Pterygopalatine fossa","Oral cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Orbit (floor of orbit)', 'The roof of the maxillary sinus forms the floor of the orbit, so the sinus is related superiorly to the orbit. This close relation explains how sinus infection or tumor may spread to the orbit.'),
('a3a500db-a96d-5af4-b0f1-ba36471f490d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the maxillary sinus is related inferiorly to:', '["Nasal floor resulting in decreased functional activity","Oral cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Roots of upper teeth (premolars and molars)","Palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Roots of upper teeth (premolars and molars)', 'The floor of the maxillary sinus lies directly over the roots of the upper premolars and molars. This close inferior relation can cause toothache or sinusitis arising from dental infection.'),
('0dbef771-af7b-5346-a70e-24291fb675bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the frontal sinus drains into:', '["Nasal vestibule resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior meatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior meatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphenoethmoidal recess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle meatus (via frontonasal duct to hiatus semilunaris)"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus (via frontonasal duct to hiatus semilunaris)', 'The frontal sinus drains through the frontonasal duct into the hiatus semilunaris of the middle meatus. It does not open into the inferior or superior meatus or into the sphenoethmoidal recess.'),
('40be35ad-ae5a-5d03-8d4f-36f644078815'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the frontal sinus is located:', '["Between the outer and inner tables of frontal bone","In the maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the ethmoid mediated by specific receptor activation","In the sphenoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the temporal bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the outer and inner tables of frontal bone', 'The frontal sinus develops within the frontal bone between its outer and inner tables, a characteristic diploic position. It does not lie within the maxilla, ethmoid, sphenoid, or temporal bone.'),
('6ed7a25f-16ee-53e9-813f-116a02d3d016'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the ethmoidal sinuses are located:', '["In the sphenoid resulting in decreased functional activity","In the maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the nasal cavity and the orbit (in ethmoid bone)","In the frontal bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the palatine bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the nasal cavity and the orbit (in ethmoid bone)', 'The ethmoidal sinuses occupy the labyrinth of the ethmoid bone, situated between the nasal cavity and the orbit. Their thin lateral wall makes the orbit vulnerable to spread of ethmoid sinus infection.'),
('12f67514-4380-524d-b32f-fda9edd47095'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The anterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into:'' is:', '["Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Middle meatus","Inferior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meatus', 'The anterior ethmoidal sinuses open into the middle meatus, largely through the hiatus semilunaris, together with the frontal and maxillary sinuses. The posterior group instead drains into the superior meatus.'),
('704dd104-beb0-57e3-ba01-d9ec1db835fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the posterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into:', '["Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Middle meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Inferior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior meatus', 'The posterior ethmoidal sinuses drain into the superior meatus. In contrast, the anterior ethmoidal group drains into the middle meatus.'),
('4a5c137c-bdec-50bf-9413-524006c297d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the sphenoidal sinus drains into:', '["Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Middle meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoethmoidal recess', 'The sphenoidal sinus opens into the sphenoethmoidal recess, the space above the superior concha and behind the ethmoid. No other listed meatus receives its opening.'),
('638f5352-0cbf-5801-a426-4b93b922d182'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the sphenoidal sinus is located:', '["In the maxilla","In the ethmoid","In the temporal bone","In the body of sphenoid bone","In the frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'In the body of sphenoid bone', 'The sphenoidal sinus lies centrally within the body of the sphenoid bone. It is related above to the pituitary gland and optic chiasm and below to the nasal cavity.'),
('c65daccb-cabc-5854-aec4-b2547ffad0d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the sphenoidal sinus is related superiorly to:', '["Oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","Nasal cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Orbit mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pituitary gland optic chiasm"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary gland optic chiasm', 'Above the sphenoidal sinus lie the pituitary gland and the optic chiasm. This superior relation makes sphenoid sinus pathology clinically hazardous because it may compress these structures.'),
('69df5a83-0752-5ae9-8521-1c276d5d6439'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the functions of paranasal sinuses include:', '["Only resonance","Only warming air","Only lighten skull","Lighten skull","resonance to voice warm/humidify air Only mucus production"]'::jsonb, 'Lighten skull', 'The paranasal sinuses lighten the skull, add resonance to the voice, help warm and humidify air, and produce mucus. Lightening the skull is one of their recognised functions, though they serve several roles.'),
('fe60f261-2f78-5d0c-9d50-02604a13026a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The maxillary sinus is clinically important for:'' is:', '["Polyps resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinusitis (common infection due to poor drainage)","Tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Fractures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foreign bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinusitis (common infection due to poor drainage)', 'The maxillary sinus is prone to sinusitis because its ostium is high and drains poorly against gravity. This poor drainage is its most frequent clinical problem.'),
('32eeb549-61c3-5798-befb-2b06802ced4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the osteomeatal complex is:', '["The lymphatic drainage of nose","The nerve plexus of nose","The blood supply of nose","The drainage pathway of frontal","maxillary and anterior ethmoidal sinuses The olfactory area"]'::jsonb, 'The drainage pathway of frontal', 'The osteomeatal complex is the functional drainage pathway for the frontal, maxillary, and anterior ethmoidal sinuses into the middle meatus. Its obstruction is a key cause of recurrent sinusitis.'),
('bb0133b6-95f8-5c72-a7a5-3c7f82d33339'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the sinuses that are present at birth are:', '["Maxillary and ethmoidal","Only maxillary","Frontal and sphenoidal","Only ethmoidal","All sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary and ethmoidal', 'The maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are present at birth. The frontal and sphenoidal sinuses develop later in childhood.'),
('c8faa7c2-efc3-504a-9180-de1c124e5d89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the frontal sinus develops at about:', '["Age 2 years","Adulthood","Age 6-8 years","Birth","Adolescence"]'::jsonb, 'Age 6-8 years', 'The frontal sinus begins to develop at about age 6-8 years and continues to enlarge into adulthood. In contrast, the maxillary and ethmoidal sinuses are already present at birth.'),
('3fe46edd-d314-543c-ab9a-8d17c9e04560'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the sphenoidal sinus develops at about:', '["Age 1 year","Adulthood","Age 3-5 years","Adolescence","Birth"]'::jsonb, 'Age 3-5 years', 'The sphenoidal sinus begins to develop at about age 3-5 years and continues to enlarge through adolescence. It is not present at birth.'),
('48858ffc-3398-539a-b7df-5a8c5a78a389'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the nerve supply of the maxillary sinus is from:', '["Ophthalmic nerve (V1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxillary nerve (V2) via superior alveolar and infraorbital nerves","Mandibular nerve (V3) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glossopharyngeal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2) via superior alveolar and infraorbital nerves', 'The maxillary sinus is supplied by the maxillary nerve (V2) through its superior alveolar and infraorbital branches. Referred pain from maxillary sinusitis follows the VI and V2 dermatomes.'),
('24372398-9e10-54f6-835d-b4870f14c61b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The nerve supply of the frontal sinus is from:'' is:', '["Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Anterior ethmoidal nerve","Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Infraorbital nerve (V2)","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital nerve (V1)', 'The frontal sinus is innervated by the supraorbital nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1). The anterior ethmoidal nerve also passes near it, but the named suppliers are the supraorbital and supratrochlear nerves.'),
('a098b514-d141-55bf-a0bc-6a163b599969'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the nerve supply of the sphenoidal sinus is from:', '["Infraorbital nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater occipital nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and sphenopalatine branches (V2)","Glossopharyngeal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and sphenopalatine branches (V2)', 'The sphenoidal sinus receives sensory supply from the posterior ethmoidal nerve (V1) and branches of the sphenopalatine nerve (V2). The other listed nerves do not innervate the sphenoidal sinus.'),
('c5b657bc-6c0f-5df7-8ac2-b47df038ba47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the anterior ethmoidal nerve is a branch of:', '["Maxillary nerve (V2)","Nasociliary nerve (V1)","Mandibular nerve (V3)","Oculomotor nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nasociliary nerve (V1)', 'The anterior ethmoidal nerve is a branch of the nasociliary nerve, which itself arises from the ophthalmic division (V1). It runs through the orbit and anterior cranial fossa to supply the nasal cavity.'),
('f887660f-0408-59b2-a011-fba0601ded63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the nasolacrimal duct drains into:', '["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Nasal vestibule","Superior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the inferior meatus of the nose, beneath the inferior concha. The sinus openings and the tear duct do not share the same meatus.'),
('1580bae1-08fe-5a59-8766-f87edff860c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the opening of the nasolacrimal duct is located:', '["Under the middle concha","Under the inferior concha in the inferior meatus","Under the superior concha","In the nasal vestibule","In the sphenoethmoidal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Under the inferior concha in the inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the inferior concha within the inferior meatus, just lateral to the nasal floor. It does not open under the middle or superior concha.'),
('8d512217-f8f5-5e76-ae8a-c4488ea8fc77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the choanae (posterior nasal apertures) connect:', '["Nasal cavity to oral cavity","Nasal cavity to nasopharynx","Nose to oropharynx","Nose to larynx","Nasal cavity to sinuses"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity to nasopharynx', 'The choanae are the posterior nasal apertures that connect the nasal cavity with the nasopharynx. They mark the boundary between the nose and the pharynx.'),
('4c3d3240-5803-5416-bad5-ac7597ba4b0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The roof of the nasal cavity is formed by:'' is:', '["Only ethmoid","Maxilla and palatine","Nasal bone frontal bone","cribriform plate of ethmoid body of sphenoid","Only sphenoid Nasal and frontal bones only"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal bone frontal bone', 'The roof of the nasal cavity is formed anteriorly by the nasal and frontal bones, in the middle by the cribriform plate of the ethmoid, and posteriorly by the body of the sphenoid. The nasal and frontal bones provide the anterior component.'),
('4517099a-76f7-54eb-854e-7660b59aa15d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the floor of the nasal cavity is formed by:', '["Only maxilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Only palatine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone","Maxilla and sphenoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Palatine and ethmoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone', 'The floor of the nasal cavity is formed by the palatine process of the maxilla and the horizontal plate of the palatine bone. These hard palate components separate the nose from the mouth.'),
('1b026dd7-8c80-5900-a5b0-924b7be166e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the lateral wall of the nasal cavity is formed by:', '["Maxilla and palatine only Only maxilla and ethmoid Nasal and frontal bones","Maxilla","ethmoid inferior concha","sphenoid palatine","lacrimal Only ethmoid and sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla', 'The lateral wall of the nasal cavity is formed by the maxilla, ethmoid, inferior concha, palatine, sphenoid, and lacrimal bones. The maxilla is one of its principal contributors.'),
('588e0122-7b99-5ba4-ad63-75df618e8f84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the medial wall of the nasal cavity is:', '["The nasal septum","The vomer","The maxilla","The sphenoid bone","The ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'The nasal septum', 'The medial wall of each nasal cavity is the nasal septum, which separates the two nasal cavities. The vomer and perpendicular plate of the ethmoid are bony components of the septum, not the entire wall.'),
('291c369d-9cc8-5358-97c5-7f0ddbfc6e5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the nasal vestibule is:', '["The roof of the nose resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior part near the choana accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anterior part of nasal cavity lined by skin with hair (vibrissae)","The middle meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The floor of the nose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior part of nasal cavity lined by skin with hair (vibrissae)', 'The nasal vestibule is the anterior part of the nasal cavity, just inside the nostril, lined by skin that bears vibrissae (nasal hairs). It is not the roof, floor, or posterior cavity.'),
('eef14884-1505-5427-b7e6-f381db6da2ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the function of vibrissae (nasal hairs) is:', '["Produce mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","Warm the air accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filter large particles from inspired air","Humidify the air without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Detect smell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filter large particles from inspired air', 'Vibrissae, or nasal hairs, filter large particles from inspired air at the entrance of the nose. Warming, humidification, mucus production, and smell occur in the deeper mucosa.'),
('81782ff0-b834-54d6-a463-802921120469'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The internal nasal valve is:'' is:', '["The sphenoethmoidal recess","The olfactory cleft","The choana","The narrowest part of the nasal cavity","at the junction of upper and lower lateral cartilages The opening of the maxillary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'The narrowest part of the nasal cavity', 'The internal nasal valve is the narrowest region of the nasal cavity, formed at the junction of the upper and lower lateral cartilages. Its dimensions largely govern nasal airflow resistance.'),
('d179f410-debf-5abb-a3f1-b7254c12e34b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'During revision of Nose and paranasal sinuses, which of the following is correct: the angle of the nasal valve is normally:', '["20-30 degrees","5-10 degrees","60-90 degrees","10-15 degrees","30-45 degrees"]'::jsonb, '10-15 degrees', 'The internal nasal valve angle normally measures about 10-15 degrees. Excessive narrowing of this angle produces nasal obstruction.'),
('88650b9d-0be2-56d8-8c03-5b214a45fcb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, which statement best fits the following fact: the respiratory epithelium of the nose is also called:', '["Olfactory epithelium","Schneiderian membrane","Transitional epithelium","Squamous epithelium","Simple epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Schneiderian membrane', 'The respiratory lining of the nasal cavity and paranasal sinuses is called the Schneiderian membrane, a pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium. It is distinct from the olfactory epithelium.'),
('4f8e86b1-a641-5829-bc97-09f708139025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Regarding Nose and paranasal sinuses, what is the correct answer to: the olfactory epithelium contains:', '["Olfactory receptor cells","supporting cells","basal cells","Goblet cells only","Only supporting cells Ciliated columnar cells only Only receptor cells"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory receptor cells', 'The olfactory epithelium contains olfactory receptor cells together with supporting and basal cells. The receptor cells detect odorants and transmit signals centrally through the olfactory nerve.'),
('c2e41a70-b70d-52d7-8de8-5dd602015f5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'For Nose and paranasal sinuses, which option is most appropriate: the blood supply of the nasal septum includes:', '["Facial artery only","Only ethmoidal","Sphenopalatine greater palatine","anterior ethmoidal superior labial arteries","Maxillary artery only Only sphenopalatine"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenopalatine greater palatine', 'The nasal septum is supplied by the sphenopalatine and greater palatine arteries together with the superior labial and anterior ethmoidal branches. The sphenopalatine and greater palatine are principal contributors.'),
('404416d9-ea50-5d54-8c4f-3217f3a70e5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'Which answer is correct in Nose and paranasal sinuses when asked: the lymphatic drainage of the anterior nasal cavity goes to:', '["Parotid nodes","Deep cervical nodes","Retropharyngeal nodes","Submental nodes","Submandibular lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular lymph nodes', 'Lymph from the anterior part of the nasal cavity drains to the submandibular lymph nodes. The posterior part of the cavity instead drains to the retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes.'),
('f57459c3-62fc-5fdf-aa8f-8c14361c7a86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Nose Paranasal Sinuses', 'In Nose and paranasal sinuses, the best response to ''The lymphatic drainage of the posterior nasal cavity goes to:'' is:', '["Submental nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Parotid nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes","Submandibular nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retropharyngeal and deep cervical nodes', 'Lymph from the posterior nasal cavity drains to the retropharyngeal and deep cervical lymph nodes. This pathway is relevant to the spread of infection or tumour from the nose to the neck.'),
('40bd03ae-7821-51d2-b125-b82eec5536db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The oral cavity is bounded anteriorly by:', '["Lips (oral fissure)","Teeth","Palate","Tongue","Cheeks"]'::jsonb, 'Lips (oral fissure)', 'The oral fissure formed by the lips is the anterior boundary of the oral cavity. The cheeks bound it laterally, the palate forms the roof, and the tongue and floor of the mouth form the floor; the teeth lie within the cavity rather than bounding it.'),
('61a70ca7-bc2a-50d6-bb4f-376015451db8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The oral vestibule is:', '["Space in the pharynx","Space behind the tongue","Space between lips/cheeks and teeth/gums","Space between upper and lower teeth","Space under the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Space between lips/cheeks and teeth/gums', 'The vestibule is the slit-like space between the lips and cheeks externally and the teeth and gums internally. The space within the dental arches is the oral cavity proper, which is why the other options are incorrect.'),
('e9061ae3-4155-5fb4-bc3e-5c6c7a84733a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The oral cavity proper is located:', '["Posterior to the pharynx","Lateral to the teeth","Medial to the teeth","Above the palate","Below the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to the teeth', 'The oral cavity proper lies within the dental arches, i.e. medial to the teeth. The vestibule lies lateral to the teeth and gums, so ''lateral to the teeth'' describes the vestibule, not the cavity proper.'),
('e1441fd7-b624-58f1-9777-acb14ba8b07d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palate consists of which parts?', '["Only soft palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Right and left palates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper and lower palates mediated by specific receptor activation","Hard palate (anterior) and soft palate (posterior)","Only hard palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hard palate (anterior) and soft palate (posterior)', 'The palate is divided into the anterior hard palate, formed by bone, and the posterior soft palate, a muscular fold. The other options describe non-existent divisions.'),
('f74f3470-8e78-51bf-8db5-f5faad025090'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The hard palate is formed by:', '["Horizontal plate of palatine only resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporal and zygomatic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxilla and sphenoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone","Palatine process of maxilla only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone', 'The hard palate is formed by the two palatine processes of the maxillae meeting the two horizontal plates of the palatine bones. The temporal, zygomatic, and sphenoid bones do not contribute to the hard palate.'),
('ec3f3d11-42eb-55f7-b6ad-869a8549eb86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The soft palate is a muscular fold that separates:', '["Oropharynx from laryngopharynx","Nasopharynx from oropharynx","Oral cavity from nasal cavity","Right from left nasal cavities","Mouth from vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx from oropharynx', 'When elevated during swallowing, the soft palate closes the pharyngeal isthmus and separates the nasopharynx from the oropharynx. The oral cavity from the nasal cavity is separated by the palate itself, not by a muscular fold.'),
('cb107f8b-14ad-57c9-978a-19f96b2eb0fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which muscle elevates the soft palate?', '["Palatopharyngeus","Tensor veli palatini","Palatoglossus","Musculus uvulae","Levator veli palatini"]'::jsonb, 'Levator veli palatini', 'Levator veli palatini raises the soft palate, sealing the nasal pharynx during swallowing and speech. Tensor veli palatini only tenses the palate and opens the auditory tube, while palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus pull the palate downward.'),
('4238370e-7a66-5784-a19e-8dd16df5660d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which muscle tenses the soft palate and opens the auditory tube?', '["Levator veli palatini","Salpingopharyngeus","Tensor veli palatini","Musculus uvulae","Palatopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Tensor veli palatini', 'Tensor veli palatini (supplied by the mandibular nerve V3) tightens the soft palate and dilates the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube. Levator veli palatini elevates the palate rather than tensing it or opening the tube.'),
('7e26f449-677e-5872-853d-cfd1f2bdb1f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Tensor veli palatini is supplied by:', '["Vagus nerve","Hypoglossal","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal nerve (V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V3)', 'Tensor veli palatini is innervated by the nerve to tensor veli palatini, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). It is the only soft palate muscle not supplied by the pharyngeal plexus.'),
('91e10140-9e6a-504d-a75b-11584b0afc80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'All muscles of soft palate EXCEPT tensor veli palatini are supplied by:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve)', 'Levator veli palatini, palatoglossus, palatopharyngeus, and musculus uvulae are all innervated by the pharyngeal plexus derived from the vagus nerve (CN X). Tensor veli palatini is the exception, supplied by V3.'),
('a02e3f58-ac0e-5dbe-a3cb-aa86b00d40d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Musculus uvulae function:', '["Opens the auditory tube","Closes the pharyngeal isthmus","Depresses the palate","Elevates the palate","Pulls the uvula upward"]'::jsonb, 'Pulls the uvula upward', 'Musculus uvulae shortens and pulls the uvula upward, helping the soft palate close the nasopharyngeal isthmus. Opening the auditory tube belongs to tensor (and salpingopharyngeus), while elevating the whole palate is the action of levator veli palatini.'),
('c647d17e-2c1b-557d-9846-e48ac78ff9fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Palatoglossus muscle:', '["Depresses the palate and elevates the posterior tongue","Elevates the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Opens the auditory tube mediated by specific receptor activation","Constricts the pharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevates the palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses the palate and elevates the posterior tongue', 'Palatoglossus draws the palatoglossal arches together, depressing the soft palate and elevating the back of the tongue, narrowing the oropharyngeal isthmus. It does not elevate the larynx, open the auditory tube, or raise the palate.'),
('c00387cb-f28b-5560-aae8-4da3c7bbed1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Palatopharyngeus muscle:', '["Depresses the tongue","Elevates the pharynx and larynx","closes the oropharyngeal isthmus","Elevates the larynx only","Elevates the palate Opens the auditory tube"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and larynx', 'During swallowing, palatopharyngeus pulls the pharynx and larynx upward and draws the palatopharyngeal arches together. Its upward pull on the pharyngeal wall distinguishes it from palatoglossus, which only narrows the oral isthmus.'),
('4d066898-ff24-5ccf-a1cf-62a5c1abbd8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tongue is divided into which two parts?', '["Right and left halves resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior 2/3 (oral) and posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal)","Tip and base mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower halves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial and deep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 (oral) and posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal)', 'The tongue is divided into an anterior oral part (two-thirds) and a posterior pharyngeal part (one-third) by the V-shaped sulcus terminalis. These two parts differ in development, nerve supply, and mucosal covering.'),
('15787714-15d2-59d3-b2fd-ecaf3d9dd1be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The sulcus terminalis separates:', '["Tip from body","Upper from lower surfaces","Dorsum from ventral surface","Anterior 2/3 from posterior 1/3 of tongue","Right from left halves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 from posterior 1/3 of tongue', 'The V-shaped sulcus terminalis with its apex at the foramen cecum marks the boundary between the anterior two-thirds and posterior one-third of the tongue. The anterior part is from the first pharyngeal arch, the posterior part from the third arch.'),
('a7124f55-d843-576f-800d-7a616e1ad905'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The foramen cecum of tongue is:', '["The vallecula resulting in decreased functional activity","The lingual tonsil accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The opening of submandibular duct mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of parotid duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A depression at the apex of sulcus terminalis (remnant of thyroglossal duct)"]'::jsonb, 'A depression at the apex of sulcus terminalis (remnant of thyroglossal duct)', 'The foramen cecum is a small midline pit at the apex of the V-shaped sulcus terminalis and represents the remnant of the proximal thyroglossal duct. The vallecula is the depression between the epiglottis and the tongue, not the foramen cecum.'),
('b5f979ed-c5f1-5483-8bde-73940cfcb157'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The four types of papillae on the tongue are:', '["Filiform","conical","foliate","lentiform Vallate","fungiform filiform"]'::jsonb, 'Filiform', 'The four anatomical types of lingual papillae are filiform, fungiform, foliate, and vallate (circumvallate), so filiform is a genuine member of the set. Conical is not among the four standard types. Filiform papillae are the thread-like, most numerous papillae and contain no taste buds.'),
('578c14ec-0dd8-56f6-b087-9d3014dc42ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which papillae contain taste buds in adults?', '["Vallate","fungiform","foliate (but NOT filiform)","All papillae","Only fungiform Only filiform Only vallate"]'::jsonb, 'Vallate', 'The large vallate (circumvallate) papillae, arranged in aV anterior to the sulcus terminalis, bear the greatest concentration of taste buds in the adult tongue. Fungiform papillae also contain some taste buds, but filiform papillae contain none, so ''vallate'' is the classic answer.'),
('3cc63973-3ba8-5f98-85ce-3b8f33454eec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The most numerous papillae on the tongue are:', '["Fungiform","Filiform","Conical","Foliate","Vallate"]'::jsonb, 'Filiform', 'Filiform papillae are the smallest and most numerous papillae, covering the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. They are keratinized, thread-like, and contain no taste buds, unlike fungiform and vallate papillae.'),
('1307216d-13a4-5cc5-a2b1-d82027a97553'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste buds on the anterior 2/3 of tongue are supplied by:', '["Lingual nerve (V3)","Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)","Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)', 'Taste fibres from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travel in the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (CN VII), which joins the lingual nerve within the oral cavity. The lingual nerve itself (V3) carries only general sensation from this region.'),
('dba8b21f-fe95-5dcf-a7fd-ec8ea4741b57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste buds on the posterior 1/3 of tongue are supplied by:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve (VII)","Lingual nerve","Vagus nerve (X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) supplies both taste and general sensation to the posterior one-third of the tongue. This region lies on the pharyngeal side of the sulcus terminalis, supplied by the third arch nerve, CN IX.'),
('03cfb350-bee4-5333-ab7b-8a212eb986db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'General sensory innervation to anterior 2/3 of tongue is from:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve","Chorda tympani (VII)","Lingual nerve (V3)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve (V3)', 'General sensation (touch, pain, temperature) to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is carried by the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). The hypoglossal nerve (XII) is purely motor to the tongue.'),
('1305e324-8317-5e28-bd2c-e8932448ac38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'General sensory innervation to posterior 1/3 of tongue is from:', '["Lingual nerve (V3)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) carries general sensation from the posterior one-third of the tongue. The vagus nerve (CN X) supplies general sensation only to the most posterior part of the tongue near the epiglottis.'),
('046ba5c7-46cf-566d-a630-9e1206ee5066'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Motor innervation to all intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles is from:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Vagus (X)","Trigeminal (V3)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (XII)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) supplies all intrinsic muscles and most extrinsic muscles (genioglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus) of the tongue. Palatoglossus is the only extrinsic tongue muscle supplied by the vagus via the pharyngeal plexus.'),
('74f96b90-6135-59ea-ae0c-945d0147cd49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which tongue muscle is NOT supplied by hypoglossal nerve?', '["Palatoglossus","Hyoglossus","Intrinsic muscles","Genioglossus","Styloglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Palatoglossus', 'Palatoglossus is innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X) through the pharyngeal plexus because it is developmentally a pharyngeal arch muscle. The other extrinsic muscles and all intrinsic muscles are supplied by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII).'),
('118fb788-3935-55f0-9213-6ab32b0b9656'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Genioglossus muscle:', '["Depresses the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Elevates the tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protrudes the tongue (prevents airway obstruction)","Moves tongue laterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retracts the tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protrudes the tongue (prevents airway obstruction)', 'Genioglossus protrudes the tongue and keeps it forward, preventing it from falling back and obstructing the airway. Weakness or bilateral paralysis causes the tongue to fall backward and the protrusion is lost; on unilateral paralysis the tongue deviates to the affected side.'),
('e6f33dc4-4dc7-56c1-867e-17c072dce30f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Hyoglossus muscle:', '["Elevates the tongue","Protrudes the tongue","Moves the tongue laterally","Pulls the tongue backward","Depresses and retracts the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses and retracts the tongue', 'Hyoglossus runs from the hyoid bone to the side of the tongue and depresses and retracts the tongue. It also forms part of the lateral floor of the mouth, related to branches of the lingual artery and nerve.'),
('569d22f5-6af9-5a4b-891a-0fca5ae500c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Styloglossus muscle:', '["Pulls the tongue forward","Elevates and retracts the tongue","Protrudes the tongue","Depresses the tongue","Moves the tongue laterally"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates and retracts the tongue', 'Styloglossus passes from the styloid process to the side of the tongue and elevates and retracts the tongue. Genioglossus protrudes it forward, and hyoglossus depresses it, so those actions differ from this muscle.'),
('2fc871ce-e5af-5d7e-b038-3865944ef925'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The four intrinsic tongue muscles are:', '["Superior longitudinal","inferior longitudinal transverse","vertical","Longitudinal","circular oblique"]'::jsonb, 'vertical', 'The four intrinsic tongue muscles are the superior longitudinal, inferior longitudinal, transverse, and vertical muscles. The vertical fibres run from the dorsum to the ventral surface of the tongue and help flatten it.'),
('7fb98cf7-f5d5-5a3c-b9fc-17f97958af9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Intrinsic tongue muscles function:', '["Elevate the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Depress the tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Change shape of tongue (lengthen, shorten, curl, flatten)","Retract the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protrude the tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Change shape of tongue (lengthen, shorten, curl, flatten)', 'Intrinsic tongue muscles have no bony attachments and change the shape of the tongue by lengthening, shortening, curling, or flattening it during speech and swallowing. Moving the tongue as a whole is the role of the extrinsic muscles.'),
('13cb1803-eedd-59f4-8b94-b70aa0b6d8e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The floor of the mouth is formed by:', '["Digastric resulting in decreased functional activity","Geniohyoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyoglossus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mylohyoid muscle (diaphragm of mouth)","Platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mylohyoid muscle (diaphragm of mouth)', 'The paired mylohyoid muscles form the muscular floor of the mouth, hence the term ''diaphragm of the mouth''. Geniohyoid lies above it, and digastric and platysma lie below, so they do not form the floor.'),
('bafaa02a-0c27-591e-a8b1-b69ced5d6803'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The sublingual space is located:', '["In the submental triangle","In the submandibular triangle","Below the mylohyoid","Above the mylohyoid muscle","Between the hyoglossus and genioglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Above the mylohyoid muscle', 'The sublingual space lies superior to the mylohyoid muscle, beneath the mucosa of the floor of the mouth, and contains the sublingual gland and ducts. The submandibular space lies below the mylohyoid, not above it.'),
('2479c31b-27ae-590e-a3a1-a0a46ba5bcb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The submandibular space is located:', '["In the sublingual region","In the submental triangle","Above the hyoglossus","Above the mylohyoid","Below the mylohyoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Below the mylohyoid muscle', 'The submandibular space lies inferior to the mylohyoid muscle and contains the superficial part of the submandibular gland and submandibular lymph nodes. The sublingual space is its superior counterpart above the mylohyoid.'),
('2071a5b1-b8d9-523f-bb7a-ec8414acdcda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Ludwig''s angina is:', '["Laryngeal infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Parotid infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pharyngeal abscess mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillar abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral infection of submandibular and sublingual spaces (submandibular space infection)"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral infection of submandibular and sublingual spaces (submandibular space infection)', 'Ludwig''s angina is a rapidly spreading, bilateral cellulitis of the submandibular, sublingual, and submental spaces, typically arising from infected teeth. It is a surgical emergency because oedema can cause acute airway obstruction.'),
('9f90f009-73e6-5fec-80b4-7756d4070e23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharynx extends from:', '["Base of skull to lower border of cricoid cartilage (C6)","Hard palate to trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal cavity to larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","Mouth to esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Soft palate to larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of skull to lower border of cricoid cartilage (C6)', 'The pharynx extends from the base of the skull (pharyngeal tubercle of the occipital bone) down to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at the level of C6, where it continues as the esophagus. The other options describe only part of its extent.'),
('9a9404cf-5376-5268-9325-5e004f13db39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharynx is divided into:', '["Nasopharynx","oropharynx","laryngopharynx Superior","middle","inferior Upper"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx', 'The pharynx is divided into three parts: the nasopharynx, the oropharynx, and the laryngopharynx, so nasopharynx is a genuine subdivision. ''Middle'' and ''inferior'' describe the constrictor muscles rather than the subdivisions of the pharynx.'),
('c064c72e-7901-5912-92f5-2c1dc704644f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The nasopharynx is located:', '["Posterior to the larynx","Above the hard palate","Posterior to the nasal cavity","above the soft palate","Posterior to the mouth Anterior to the spine"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the nasal cavity', 'The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavities and above the soft palate, and it contains the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube and the pharyngeal tonsil. Its anterior wall is the posterior margin of the choanae, so it is posterior to the nasal cavity.'),
('7441405b-85ef-5f77-ad8d-0003d7cdafcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The oropharynx is located:', '["Below the larynx","Posterior to the oral cavity","between soft palate and hyoid","Posterior to the nose","Posterior to the larynx Above the soft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the oral cavity', 'The oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, extending from the soft palate above to the epiglottis below, and communicates with the mouth through the oropharyngeal isthmus. The region posterior to the nose is the nasopharynx.'),
('a9a31357-5da7-5d00-8d15-cd2d377338e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The laryngopharynx is located:', '["Above the soft palate","Posterior to the larynx","from hyoid to cricoid","Below the cricoid","Anterior to the trachea Posterior to the nose"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the larynx', 'The laryngopharynx lies posterior to the larynx, extending from the hyoid bone down to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage where it becomes the esophagus. Its anterior wall is formed by the inlet of the larynx.'),
('28ea96b5-9408-50eb-ab85-57d6650b1e21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngobasilar fascia is:', '["The buccopharyngeal fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","The innermost mucosal layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The outer muscular layer mediated by specific receptor activation","The fibrous layer of pharyngeal wall (between mucosa and muscles)","The prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fibrous layer of pharyngeal wall (between mucosa and muscles)', 'The pharyngobasilar fascia is the tough fibrous layer of the pharyngeal wall that lies between the mucosa and the pharyngeal muscles. It attaches to the base of the skull and keeps the pharynx patent above the level of the superior constrictor.'),
('108d8212-924e-537b-8d9d-dd9a3c55e42e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal muscles are arranged as:', '["Only circular muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Only longitudinal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 constrictors and 2 elevators mediated by specific receptor activation","3 constrictors (superior, middle, inferior) and 3 longitudinal (stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus)","2 constrictors and 2 elevators leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 constrictors (superior, middle, inferior) and 3 longitudinal (stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus)', 'The pharyngeal muscles comprise three circular constrictors (superior, middle, inferior) and three longitudinal muscles (stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus). The constrictors propel the bolus downward while the longitudinal muscles elevate the pharynx and larynx during swallowing.'),
('321fc032-0212-5a28-ae91-a2a8ae323131'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The superior pharyngeal constrictor originates from:', '["Pterygoid hamulus","pterygomandibular raphe","mylohyoid line","Mandible","Hyoid bone Cricoid cartilage Thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Pterygoid hamulus', 'The superior pharyngeal constrictor arises from the pterygoid hamulus, the pterygomandibular raphe, the posterior end of the mylohyoid line of the mandible, and the side of the tongue. The hyoid and laryngeal cartilages give origin to the middle and inferior constrictors instead.'),
('38176239-f358-57f9-87fa-4d75896e6039'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The middle pharyngeal constrictor originates from:', '["Mandible","Pterygoid hamulus","Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid","stylohyoid ligament","Thyroid cartilage Cricoid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid', 'The middle pharyngeal constrictor arises from the greater and lesser cornua of the hyoid bone and from the stylohyoid ligament. The thyroid and cricoid cartilages give origin to the inferior constrictor, and the mandible to the superior constrictor.'),
('807f9b8f-b238-50bd-a6fb-d4f64c4869e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The inferior pharyngeal constrictor originates from:', '["Mandible","Palatine bone","Pterygoid hamulus","Hyoid bone","Thyroid and cricoid cartilages"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid and cricoid cartilages', 'The inferior pharyngeal constrictor arises from the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage and the side of the cricoid cartilage, with its lower fibres (cricopharyngeus) forming the upper esophageal sphincter. The hyoid gives origin to the middle constrictor.'),
('a3f62c6e-ae78-567c-bb92-450c1561222e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The cricopharyngeus is part of:', '["Middle constrictor","Inferior pharyngeal constrictor","Longitudinal muscles","Superior constrictor","Upper esophageal sphincter"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pharyngeal constrictor', 'Cricopharyngeus is the lowest part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor, arising from the cricoid cartilage, and functionally it forms the upper esophageal sphincter. It is not part of the middle constrictor or the longitudinal muscle group.'),
('e1110495-a634-5e47-b7b2-0abefd94499d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are supplied by:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve, CN X)","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve, CN X)', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are innervated by the pharyngeal plexus, which receives fibres mainly from the vagus nerve (CN X). Among the pharyngeal muscles, only stylopharyngeus is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX).'),
('c3ebf7ab-e80a-5980-b39a-38cb1dc403ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The stylopharyngeus is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal nerve (V)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'Stylopharyngeus, the only muscle arising from the third arch, is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX). All other pharyngeal muscles receive innervation from the pharyngeal plexus via the vagus nerve.'),
('fe2a5cbf-d664-5544-a987-41cd77d107f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal plexus is formed by:', '["CN IX","CN X","and sympathetic fibers","CN V","CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX', 'The pharyngeal plexus is formed by branches of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), the vagus nerve (CN X), and sympathetic fibres, which supply the pharyngeal muscles and mucosa. CN IX is therefore one of its essential contributing nerves.'),
('310c4d0b-f51b-5eb3-9199-e0bf5c73a927'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Waldeyer''s ring is:', '["A ring of muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","An arterial circle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nerve plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","A ring of lymphoid tissue (tonsils) in the pharynx","A ring of cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ring of lymphoid tissue (tonsils) in the pharynx', 'Waldeyer''s ring is the ring of lymphoid tissue (tonsils) surrounding the pharyngeal inlet, guarding the entrance to the respiratory and digestive passages. It is lymphoid tissue, not muscle, cartilage, vessels, or nerve.'),
('ee0c52d4-cb3d-5b82-8c5d-29ac1c21ee9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Waldeyer''s ring consists of:', '["Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)","tubal tonsils","palatine tonsils lingual tonsil","Only lingual tonsil Palatine and lingual only","Only palatine tonsils Only pharyngeal tonsil"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)', 'Waldeyer''s ring consists of the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids), the tubal tonsils, the palatine tonsils, and the lingual tonsil. The pharyngeal tonsil is one of the four components, located in the nasopharynx, so it is a genuine member of the ring.'),
('2703f59f-56e9-5535-a981-b20efdd6b98a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The largest tonsil is:', '["Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)","Lingual tonsil","Palatine tonsil","Sublingual tonsil","Tubal tonsil"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsil', 'Of the four tonsils of Waldeyer''s ring, the palatine tonsil is the largest, occupying the tonsillar fossa in the oropharynx. The pharyngeal (adenoid), lingual and tubal tonsils are smaller lymphoid aggregates.'),
('86c4725f-58af-50d6-a533-b72b2b409e7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatine tonsil is located:', '["On the posterior tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","In the soft palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the nasopharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","In the laryngopharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches"]'::jsonb, 'In the tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches', 'The palatine tonsil lies in the tonsillar fossa of the oropharynx, bounded anteriorly by the palatoglossal arch and posteriorly by the palatopharyngeal arch. The other listed sites house different structures, not the palatine tonsil.'),
('7a6e5e55-c549-5386-8cf3-6adf903d31ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tonsillar bed (fossa) is formed by:', '["Buccinator and masseter Middle constrictor","styloglossus hyoglossus","Superior constrictor palatoglossus","palatopharyngeus Inferior constrictor","cricopharyngeus Orbicularis oris and levator labii"]'::jsonb, 'Superior constrictor palatoglossus', 'The tonsillar bed is floored by the superior pharyngeal constrictor and reinforced anteriorly by palatoglossus; the tonsillar artery pierces the superior constrictor to reach the gland. Muscles such as buccinator, hyoglossus and cricopharyngeus do not form this bed.'),
('3f1c9129-d9d0-5bb9-9343-e54da040b960'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The blood supply of the palatine tonsil is mainly from:', '["Ascending pharyngeal","Superior thyroid","Tonsillar branch of facial artery","Maxillary artery","Lingual artery"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillar branch of facial artery', 'The tonsillar branch of the facial artery is the principal arterial supply, entering the tonsillar bed through the superior constrictor. The ascending pharyngeal and lingual arteries contribute only minor branches, while the superior thyroid and maxillary arteries are not primary tonsillar vessels.'),
('a26792bd-fe9c-593d-8ebf-d5e4b2afce7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The nerve supply of the palatine tonsil is from:', '["Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve (V2)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) supplies sensory fibers to the palatine tonsil through the tonsillar plexus and forms the afferent limb of the gag reflex. The other cranial nerves listed do not innervate the tonsil.'),
('0e2fecd8-a174-5c43-b465-efefb4f578d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) is located:', '["In the oropharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","In the laryngopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the soft palate mediated by specific receptor activation","On the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the roof and posterior wall of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the roof and posterior wall of nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) lies in the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx. The oropharynx, laryngopharynx, soft palate and tongue contain other tonsillar or glandular tissue.'),
('eb82c3d8-eb76-5da0-a509-36478d288f4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tubal tonsil is located:', '["On the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","In the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the nasopharynx roof mediated by specific receptor activation","Around the pharyngeal opening of auditory tube","In the palatine arches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around the pharyngeal opening of auditory tube', 'The tubal tonsil is a nodule of lymphoid tissue in the mucosa around the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube. The tongue, larynx, nasopharynx roof and palatine arches do not contain this tonsil.'),
('c9e14c2d-ae0d-5787-a066-ff1164ef73c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Killian''s dehiscence is:', '["A defect in the palate resulting in decreased functional activity","A space in the middle constrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A opening in the buccopharyngeal fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","A gap in the superior constrictor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A weak area in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'A weak area in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus', 'Killian''s dehiscence is the triangular gap between the oblique thyropharyngeus and horizontal cricopharyngeus parts of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor. Because only mucosa and fascia cover it, a pharyngeal pouch can herniate here.'),
('c60a41da-186a-5494-8e2a-29b1789a16cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Killian''s dehiscence is the site of:', '["Parapharyngeal abscess","Laryngeal tumor","Pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum)","Retropharyngeal abscess","Tonsillar abscess"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum)', 'A pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum) herniates through Killian''s dehiscence, a false diverticulum of mucosa. Parapharyngeal, retropharyngeal and tonsillar abscesses arise in fascial or tonsillar spaces, not through this dehiscence.'),
('6569270e-81ac-5d1b-9dc0-b21229fb4cc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal recess (fossa of Rosenmuller) is located:', '["In the tonsillar fossa","In the laryngopharynx","In the nasopharynx","behind the tubal elevation","In the pyriform sinus In the oropharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal recess (fossa of Rosenmuller) is a lateral recess of the nasopharynx, lying behind the tubal elevation (torus tubarius); nasopharyngeal carcinoma typically arises here. The other listed regions are unrelated to this recess.'),
('f9f657f3-f565-5245-a433-7d4138670c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pyriform sinus is:', '["A space in the oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","A recess in the nasopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A space in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","A depression in the laryngopharynx on either side of laryngeal inlet","A sinus in the paranasal sinuses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A depression in the laryngopharynx on either side of laryngeal inlet', 'The pyriform fossae are paired depressions of the laryngopharynx, one on each side of the laryngeal inlet, where swallowed liquids pool before entering the esophagus. The oral cavity, nasopharynx, nose and paranasal sinuses are unrelated locations.'),
('2dcb89a5-8931-5912-b422-c923ec195eb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal isthmus is:', '["The opening between nose and pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","The opening between nasopharynx and oropharynx (closed by soft palate during swallowing)","The opening between mouth and pharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of auditory tube without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening between pharynx and esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening between nasopharynx and oropharynx (closed by soft palate during swallowing)', 'The pharyngeal isthmus is the gap between the nasopharynx and oropharynx; during swallowing the soft palate and uvula rise to close it and prevent nasal regurgitation. The other openings describe different junctions.'),
('9346e745-751e-5696-8852-52c6fdef571c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The auditory tube opens in the:', '["Lateral wall of oropharynx","Posterior wall of oropharynx","Posterior wall of nasopharynx","Lateral wall of nasopharynx","Roof of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wall of nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube lies on the lateral wall of the nasopharynx, about 1 cm behind the inferior nasal concha, guarded by the tubal elevation. The oropharynx and nasopharyngeal roof or posterior wall are incorrect sites.'),
('861f3d67-3c3e-5b43-b3a5-3bf8ceea70d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The salpingopharyngeus muscle arises from:', '["Cartilage of auditory tube (salpinx)","Pterygoid hamulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage of auditory tube (salpinx)', 'Salpingopharyngeus arises from the cartilaginous part of the auditory tube (salpinx) and passes downward into the pharyngeal wall, blending with palatopharyngeus. The pterygoid hamulus, palatine bone, hyoid and thyroid cartilage are origins of other muscles.'),
('5b57cce0-e9b0-58ce-9573-9706b959ffac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The salpingopharyngeus muscle:', '["Elevates the pharynx and opens the auditory tube","Depresses the pharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Depresses the palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevates the palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constricts the pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and opens the auditory tube', 'Salpingopharyngeus elevates the pharynx during swallowing and, by pulling on the tubal cartilage, helps open the pharyngeal orifice of the auditory tube. The remaining options describe actions of the pharyngeal constrictors or palate muscles.'),
('e1072147-87bc-555b-a6fb-b316b4ecb64c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharyngotympanic tube is more horizontal in:', '["Females resulting in decreased functional activity","Children (making middle ear infections more common)","Elderly mediated by specific receptor activation","Adults without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Males leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Children (making middle ear infections more common)', 'In children the auditory tube is shorter, wider and more horizontal than in adults, so nasopharyngeal secretions and pathogens pass more easily into the middle ear, explaining frequent otitis media. In adults the tube is longer and more vertical.'),
('eaccbfca-9fd6-5fba-a997-e0228ed66cd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Passavant''s ridge is:', '["A pharyngeal recess resulting in decreased functional activity","A ridge in the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tonsillar fold mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscular ridge formed by palatopharyngeus for sealing the nasopharynx during swallowing","A ridge on the tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A muscular ridge formed by palatopharyngeus for sealing the nasopharynx during swallowing', 'Passavant''s ridge is a mucosal fold on the posterior pharyngeal wall produced mainly by palatopharyngeus (with the superior constrictor); it meets the elevated soft palate to seal the nasopharynx during swallowing. The other options are not this ridge.'),
('a4bd8636-022d-51d4-b25a-3df5c81f2805'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The vallecula is:', '["A tonsillar crypt resulting in decreased functional activity","A nasal sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A depression in the larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","A depression between the base of tongue and epiglottis","A pharyngeal recess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A depression between the base of tongue and epiglottis', 'The valleculae are paired depressions between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis, bounded by the median and lateral glossoepiglottic folds. They are part of the oropharynx, not the larynx, tonsil, nose or pharyngeal recess.'),
('2d2c2c79-89e2-56eb-a301-56f50904b57d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The glossoepiglottic folds are:', '["Folds in the pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Folds in the palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Folds in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","Median and lateral folds connecting tongue to epiglottis","Folds in the larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Median and lateral folds connecting tongue to epiglottis', 'The median and lateral glossoepiglottic folds connect the base of the tongue to the epiglottis and form the boundaries of the valleculae. The pharynx, palate, nose and larynx are not where these folds lie.'),
('8e832b07-9048-5879-885c-77a7bb5a45b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatoglossal arch is:', '["The anterior tonsillar pillar","The posterior tonsillar pillar","The soft palate","The uvula","The pharyngeal wall"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior tonsillar pillar', 'The palatoglossal arch, formed by the palatoglossus muscle covered with mucosa, is the anterior pillar of the tonsillar fossa. The posterior pillar is the palatopharyngeal arch.'),
('56fc46ac-2900-5752-a6d3-cd1be5af1bb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatopharyngeal arch is:', '["The soft palate","The anterior tonsillar pillar","The pharyngeal wall","The posterior tonsillar pillar","The uvula"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior tonsillar pillar', 'The palatopharyngeal arch, formed by the palatopharyngeus muscle covered with mucosa, is the posterior pillar bounding the tonsillar fossa behind. The palatoglossal arch is the anterior pillar.'),
('5f5479fd-cc41-55f5-9c0a-4f199fe774e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The triangular fold (plica triangularis) is:', '["A fold in the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","A fold in the palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fold of mucosa covering the anterior part of tonsil","A fold in the larynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fold in the pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fold of mucosa covering the anterior part of tonsil', 'The plica triangularis is a mucosal fold that extends over the upper anterior part of the palatine tonsil. It is not a fold of the tongue, palate, larynx or pharyngeal wall.'),
('7a4d53e8-44d8-5bf3-8d6a-f3f91994496e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tonsillar crypts are:', '["Lymphatic channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerves of the tonsil accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscles of the tonsil mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood vessels of the tonsil without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Invaginations of tonsillar epithelium that can trap debris"]'::jsonb, 'Invaginations of tonsillar epithelium that can trap debris', 'Tonsillar crypts are deep tubular invaginations of the surface epithelium that enlarge the surface area and can trap debris and bacteria, predisposing to tonsilloliths and infection. They are not lymphatic channels, nerves, muscles or vessels.'),
('bdaf33df-5867-52a8-b209-e1ede230bddd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Tonsillitis is inflammation of:', '["Pharyngeal tonsil","Tubal tonsils","Lingual tonsil","Palatine tonsils","All tonsils"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsils', 'Tonsillitis classically refers to infection and inflammation of the palatine tonsils. Inflammation of the pharyngeal tonsil is called adenoiditis, and of the lingual tonsil lingual tonsillitis.'),
('313524d9-1736-5190-91ca-230df792943a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatoglossus muscle is innervated by which nerve?', '["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Vagus nerve (CN X) via pharyngeal plexus","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X) via pharyngeal plexus', 'Palatoglossus, like the other soft palate muscles, is supplied by the vagus nerve (CN X) through the pharyngeal plexus; only tensor veli palatini is innervated by V3. CN V, IX, XII and VII do not supply this muscle.'),
('6d9c3f44-74a0-507e-977c-7fd13e73859d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The roof of the oral cavity is formed by:', '["Pharynx","Cheeks","Mandible","Tongue","Hard and soft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Hard and soft palate', 'The roof of the oral cavity is the palate, composed of the hard palate (palatine processes and palatine bones) anteriorly and the soft palate posteriorly. The cheeks, mandible, tongue and pharynx form other boundaries of the oral cavity.'),
('573bcf87-5913-5873-86ad-a07fbd7563c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The soft palate is a muscular fold that:', '["Separates nasopharynx from oropharynx","Elevates the tongue","Closes the larynx","Separates oral cavity from nasal cavity","Opens the auditory tube"]'::jsonb, 'Separates nasopharynx from oropharynx', 'The soft palate is a mobile muscular fold separating the nasopharynx from the oropharynx; during swallowing it elevates to close the pharyngeal isthmus. It does not elevate the tongue, close the larynx or open the auditory tube.'),
('d602a7b9-225d-5c30-a3d4-7d238a2dd136'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Muscles of the soft palate include:', '["Stylopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus","Levator veli palatini","tensor veli palatini","palatoglossus","palatopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Levator veli palatini', 'Levator veli palatini is one of the muscles of the soft palate, along with tensor veli palatini, palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus. Stylopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus belong to the pharynx rather than the soft palate.'),
('17bde74d-1c1c-53a4-b458-9a57f59cf25c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Tensor veli palatini is innervated by:', '["Mandibular nerve (V3)","Vagus (X)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular nerve (V3)', 'Tensor veli palatini is the only muscle of the soft palate supplied by the mandibular nerve (V3), via the nerve to medial pterygoid. All other palatal muscles receive fibers from the vagus through the pharyngeal plexus.'),
('7a235805-f99a-5db7-90ab-1524d36e1d02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatoglossus muscle:', '["Elevates the tongue and depresses the palate","Depresses the tongue","Opens the pharynx","Elevates the palate","Closes the eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the tongue and depresses the palate', 'Palatoglossus raises the posterior part of the tongue while depressing the soft palate, drawing the tongue and palate together to narrow the oropharyngeal isthmus. It does not depress the tongue, elevate the palate or act on the eustachian tube.'),
('10196e9a-e9be-5a73-b983-084dfe56944e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatopharyngeus muscle:', '["Retracts the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Opens the mouth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Closes the nasal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing","Depresses the palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing', 'Palatopharyngeus elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing, shortening the pharynx and contributing to Passavant''s ridge. It does not retract the tongue, open the mouth, close the nasal cavity or depress the palate.'),
('16f1e03c-63cf-53b1-ab68-86914c1bcafc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The floor of the oral cavity is formed by:', '["Digastric muscle","Mylohyoid muscle and geniohyoid muscle","Masseter muscle","Buccinator muscle","Pterygoid muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Mylohyoid muscle and geniohyoid muscle', 'The floor of the mouth is formed mainly by the paired mylohyoid muscles, with the geniohyoid muscles lying deep to them. Masseter, buccinator and the pterygoids are muscles of mastication or the cheek, not the oral floor.'),
('ad59480f-1588-50e7-8ecb-6cde85208505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tongue is divided into:', '["Oral part (anterior 2/3) and pharyngeal part (posterior 1/3)","Right and left halves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base and tip only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum and ventrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper and lower parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral part (anterior 2/3) and pharyngeal part (posterior 1/3)', 'By the V-shaped sulcus terminalis, the tongue is divided into an anterior oral part (two-thirds) and a posterior pharyngeal part (one-third). The other options describe different planes or simplifications that are not the standard division.'),
('45c95ce9-e388-52d0-a824-1108d42e5f99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The sulcus terminalis divides:', '["Anterior 2/3 (oral) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal) of tongue","Base from tip accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tip from body mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum from ventrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right from left halves of tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 (oral) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal) of tongue', 'The V-shaped sulcus terminalis marks the boundary between the anterior two-thirds (oral, supplied by the lingual and chorda tympani nerves) and the posterior one-third (pharyngeal, supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve) of the tongue.'),
('a841068c-0c1f-5313-9acb-d916ec51c9c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The foramen cecum of the tongue is:', '["A nerve foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","A taste bud accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphatic channel mediated by specific receptor activation","The embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct","The opening of Wharton''s duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct', 'The foramen cecum at the apex of the sulcus terminalis is the remnant of the proximal thyroglossal duct, marking where the developing thyroid descended from the tongue. It is not a nerve foramen, taste bud, lymph channel or duct opening.'),
('6c12d898-a12c-591d-bf65-fdfc48d8cc99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The intrinsic muscles of the tongue are:', '["Superior longitudinal","inferior longitudinal","transverse","vertical","Buccinator and orbicularis oris"]'::jsonb, 'Superior longitudinal', 'The four intrinsic muscles of the tongue are the superior and inferior longitudinal, transverse and vertical muscles; they alter the shape of the tongue. Buccinator and orbicularis oris are muscles of facial expression, not tongue muscles.'),
('9936eb39-632a-540d-8127-75e28b9175b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The genioglossus muscle:', '["Depresses the mandible","Depresses the tongue","Retracts the tongue","Elevates the tongue","Protrudes the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Protrudes the tongue', 'Genioglossus protrudes the tongue, pulling it forward and downward from the genial tubercle; its paralysis allows the tongue to fall back and obstruct the airway. The other actions belong to different tongue muscles.'),
('7a345f0a-94ed-58c6-a1b2-44a3c9f7dd80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The hyoglossus muscle:', '["Pulls tongue laterally","Elevates the hyoid","Protrudes the tongue","Elevates the tongue","Depresses and retracts the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses and retracts the tongue', 'Hyoglossus depresses and retracts the tongue, flattening it. Protrusion is performed by genioglossus and elevation by styloglossus and the palatoglossus acting on the palate.'),
('ec7bf575-72cd-5bd2-81aa-f5249724dd88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The styloglossus muscle:', '["Opens the mouth","Retracts and elevates the tongue","Pulls tongue medially","Depresses the tongue","Protrudes the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Retracts and elevates the tongue', 'Styloglossus draws the tongue upward and backward, retracting and elevating it. Protrusion is produced by genioglossus and depression by hyoglossus, not by styloglossus.'),
('c5ac9455-5fc0-5798-bdc7-ddebf0fc3941'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste sensation to the anterior 2/3 of tongue is carried by:', '["Trigeminal nerve (V)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)', 'Taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travel in the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (VII) that joins the lingual nerve. CN IX carries taste from the posterior third, and the vagus from the epiglottis.'),
('7bc09b18-4b6f-564e-9681-15938e21d8f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste sensation to the posterior 1/3 of tongue is carried by:', '["Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Vagus nerve (X)","Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Trigeminal nerve (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) carries both taste and general sensation from the posterior one-third of the tongue. The facial nerve serves the anterior two-thirds, the vagus the epiglottis region, and the others do not carry tongue taste.'),
('1a60ff17-aaff-5c0b-950e-00043a202ef4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'General sensation to the anterior 2/3 of tongue is carried by:', '["Vagus (X)","Lingual nerve (V3)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve (V3)', 'The lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3), carries general sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, while the chorda tympani travels with it for taste. The posterior third is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve.'),
('a1974512-5df5-53a7-9685-4c4324ef18c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tongue is supplied by which artery?', '["Inferior alveolar artery","Superior thyroid artery","Facial artery","Maxillary artery","Lingual artery (branch of external carotid)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual artery (branch of external carotid)', 'The lingual artery, a branch of the external carotid artery, is the main arterial supply of the tongue and forms the dorsal lingual and deep lingual branches. The inferior alveolar, maxillary and superior thyroid arteries do not primarily supply the tongue.'),
('5a12f9a5-f6a3-58d4-87f5-9f3cf95d52a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The submandibular gland drains into:', '["Sublingual fold","Sublingual caruncle via Wharton''s duct","Parotid papilla","Stensen''s duct","Floor of mouth via multiple ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Sublingual caruncle via Wharton''s duct', 'The submandibular gland empties through Wharton''s duct, which opens on the sublingual caruncle beside the lingual frenulum. Stensen''s duct drains the parotid gland, and the sublingual glands open via the ducts of Rivinus into the sublingual fold.'),
('4d207f09-32f5-504d-9324-7d70bd562187'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The sublingual gland drains via:', '["Nasolacrimal duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple ducts of Rivinus and Bartholin''s duct into sublingual fold","Submandibular duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Stensen''s duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wharton''s duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple ducts of Rivinus and Bartholin''s duct into sublingual fold', 'The sublingual gland opens through numerous small ducts of Rivinus, and sometimes the major sublingual duct of Bartholin, into the sublingual fold on the floor of the mouth. Stensen''s and Wharton''s ducts drain the parotid and submandibular glands respectively.'),
('bb697043-3c85-57f9-9a7c-d9a4352616ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The parotid gland drains via:', '["Stensen''s duct opening at the parotid papilla opposite upper 2nd molar","Wharton''s duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duets of Rivinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Nasolacrimal duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bartholin''s duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stensen''s duct opening at the parotid papilla opposite upper 2nd molar', 'The parotid gland drains through Stensen''s duct, which pierces the buccinator and opens on the parotid papilla in the cheek mucosa opposite the upper second molar. Wharton''s, Rivinus'' and Bartholin''s ducts belong to the other salivary glands.'),
('01a642a2-71b1-583b-a578-57c55e7270c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The parotid gland is innervated by:', '["Trigeminal (V) resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagus (X) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glossopharyngeal (IX) via otic ganglion (parasympathetic)","Hypoglossal (XII) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve (VII) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX) via otic ganglion (parasympathetic)', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers of the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) synapse in the otic ganglion, and postganglionic fibers travel with the auriculotemporal nerve to provide secretomotor supply to the parotid gland. The other cranial nerves do not supply it.'),
('ff577139-4ec4-5833-a63f-a469fd8b7026'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The submandibular gland is innervated by:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX) resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal (V) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagus (X) mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial nerve (VII) via submandibular ganglion (parasympathetic)","Hypoglossal (XII) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII) via submandibular ganglion (parasympathetic)', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers travel in the chorda tympani (CN VII) to synapse in the submandibular ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers supply the submandibular and sublingual glands. The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies the parotid instead.'),
('60d16579-baa0-5c44-aedf-142df21678ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The maxillary artery supplies:', '["The ear resulting in decreased functional activity","The orbit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The deep structures of face and jaw","Only the scalp without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The brain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The deep structures of face and jaw', 'The maxillary artery, the larger terminal branch of the external carotid, supplies the deep structures of the face and jaws, including the masticatory muscles, teeth, maxilla and mandible. It does not primarily supply the brain, orbit, ear or scalp.'),
('8da2c0a7-6494-5121-902e-9f21d51a1b2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The mental foramen transmits:', '["Mental nerve (V3) and vessels","Hypoglossal nerve","Lingual nerve","Inferior alveolar nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Mental nerve (V3) and vessels', 'The mental foramen on the anterior surface of the mandible transmits the mental nerve, the terminal branch of the inferior alveolar nerve (V3), together with the mental vessels. The inferior alveolar nerve itself enters the mandibular foramen.'),
('8bf50878-e20b-51bb-bc05-49ee32e4e4ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The inferior alveolar nerve and artery enter the mandible via:', '["Lingual foramen","Mental foramen","Optic foramen","Incisive foramen","Mandibular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular foramen', 'The mandibular foramen on the medial surface of the ramus transmits the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels into the mandibular canal. The mental foramen is their exit point, while the lingual and incisive foramina and the optic foramen (in the sphenoid) are unrelated entry sites.'),
('1b107720-d7d8-59bb-b731-c0886f18b9a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is:', '["A hinge joint only resulting in decreased functional activity","A synovial joint with an articular disc","A suture mediated by specific receptor activation","A cartilaginous joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fibrous joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A synovial joint with an articular disc', 'The TMJ is a synovial joint containing a fibrocartilaginous articular disc that divides it into upper and lower compartments, allowing both hinge and gliding movements. Calling it a hinge joint only ignores its translation, and it is not a suture, cartilaginous, or fibrous joint.'),
('6f63b78e-1087-5520-ae50-9eb1ce6f198a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The muscles of mastication include:', '["Mylohyoid and digastric","Masseter","temporalis","medial pterygoid","lateral pterygoid"]'::jsonb, 'Masseter', 'Masseter is one of the four muscles of mastication, together with temporalis and the medial and lateral pterygoids, all innervated by the mandibular nerve (V3). Mylohyoid and digastric are suprahyoid muscles, not muscles of mastication.'),
('a41a0511-3aa6-5c46-a5f4-63256a0b2291'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatine tonsils are located in:', '["Oral cavity","Laryngopharynx","Oropharynx","Nasal cavity","Nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Oropharynx', 'Each palatine tonsil lies in the tonsillar fossa of the oropharynx, between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches. The oral cavity, nasopharynx, laryngopharynx, and nasal cavity are not the site of the palatine tonsils.'),
('dcdcba48-0187-5d65-ac49-373560967e38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'How many permanent teeth are there in an adult human?', '["28","30","36","24","32"]'::jsonb, '32', 'The adult dentition comprises 32 permanent teeth: 8 incisors, 4 canines, 8 premolars, and 12 molars, including the four third molars. The other totals do not match the complete permanent set.'),
('c88c0b50-1c7a-586c-af6f-f16d3b1f4398'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tongue is innervated by which nerve for motor function?', '["Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (XII)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (XII) supplies motor innervation to all intrinsic and most extrinsic muscles of the tongue; only palatoglossus is supplied by the pharyngeal plexus. The vagus, trigeminal, glossopharyngeal, and facial nerves provide no tongue motor fibers.'),
('4eee7184-0fcc-589f-a98d-223c7e7ee583'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The parotid duct opens opposite:', '["Lower second molar","Upper first molar","Upper second molar","Upper third molar","Lower first molar"]'::jsonb, 'Upper second molar', 'The parotid (Stensen) duct opens onto the buccal mucosa opposite the crown of the upper second molar. Opening near the first or third molar, or at the lower teeth, is anatomically incorrect.'),
('9da1ebad-fc00-5a6b-9a45-a1001c4b8107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Oral Cavity Pharynx: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('fe0106a2-c36c-53a4-95d4-74572af76e3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Oral Cavity Pharynx, What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('079ee53c-adba-5276-83e9-d90e062b1d33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Oral Cavity Pharynx: Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('ecf8699c-19e1-56ee-80ec-52b9a0d353cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In the study of Oral Cavity Pharynx, Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('27e4d66a-e674-5d8a-865a-69b39ccbb1c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Oral Cavity Pharynx: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('1ff6ab18-beaf-518c-9741-266672f24b20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Oral Cavity Pharynx: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('c3158916-6eba-54bb-a1ef-3ec82dcceb75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Oral Cavity Pharynx, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('d9dbdc63-7688-55f6-835b-33898480ecb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Oral Cavity Pharynx: Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('e85d23f8-5d1d-56bc-b7c6-a70d476e8805'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In the study of Oral Cavity Pharynx, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('9f73ab67-8cfd-555a-8b76-e66aae55250a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'All muscles of the soft palate except tensor veli palatini are supplied by:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve)', 'All soft palate muscles except tensor veli palatini receive motor innervation from the pharyngeal plexus, largely carried by the vagus nerve. Tensor veli palatini is instead supplied by V3.'),
('38a545d6-291a-53ec-94c9-258f8f495b12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Oral Cavity Pharynx: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('335ba663-d157-5109-a91a-d175892443d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Oral Cavity Pharynx, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('241f5105-db55-5b2f-8898-8a3b7e8698f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Oral Cavity Pharynx: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('356458ad-d50f-5007-93ad-263ec4678b62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('6ac24b7b-4b8a-5e16-845a-6c6a6b56c01d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('21deb660-24eb-5a6d-9ec5-4da103072460'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('b583473d-5eb2-557b-aad6-317c069d2689'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('c3c1a82c-edc4-56c3-b6da-714ca958bee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases: Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('ff47073c-cf03-5aa8-bb95-3a24ec684e89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('1db57523-ce66-5df2-8cca-c40132048010'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste buds on the anterior 2/3 of the tongue are supplied by:', '["Lingual nerve (V3)","Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)","Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)', 'Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travels in the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (VII). The lingual nerve carries only general sensation here, while the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves serve other regions.'),
('9680ffb3-7d1f-54c7-9eaf-91fb918a61bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste buds on the posterior 1/3 of the tongue are supplied by:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve (VII)","Lingual nerve","Vagus nerve (X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'Taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue is carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). The facial, lingual, hypoglossal, and vagus nerves do not supply taste buds in this region.'),
('e30cc271-02ac-575e-a172-304c121d7276'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'General sensory innervation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue is from:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve","Chorda tympani (VII)","Lingual nerve (V3)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve (V3)', 'General sensation (touch, pain, temperature) from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is carried by the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve (V3). The chorda tympani in the same region carries only taste, while IX and XII serve other functions.'),
('d3a53af4-65d3-5dd4-b7aa-0315ab9eb8f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'General sensory innervation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue is from:', '["Lingual nerve (V3)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) provides both general and special sensory innervation to the posterior one-third of the tongue. The lingual nerve, vagus, hypoglossal, and facial nerves do not serve general sensation here.'),
('fe66c268-c784-5d95-81a9-35972a3acb64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('94be470e-c7f5-572a-8712-b832ec7270cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which tongue muscle is not supplied by the hypoglossal nerve?', '["Palatoglossus","Hyoglossus","Intrinsic muscles","Genioglossus","Styloglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Palatoglossus', 'Palatoglossus is the only tongue muscle innervated by the pharyngeal plexus (via the vagus nerve) rather than the hypoglossal nerve. Hyoglossus, styloglossus, genioglossus, and the intrinsic muscles are all supplied by XII.'),
('cb7ffd45-f074-5cc2-9916-3e11261f3903'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('aefb97ab-1349-538f-ab58-bdc72d6d329e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('e1e80c72-dbe6-5c4e-aa3d-686d8ec068f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('7ea87570-cd1f-522c-b053-46c18d39674c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('f7dd5ea6-edb4-5b2b-8be0-85796e541863'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The function of the intrinsic tongue muscles is:', '["Elevate the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Depress the tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Change shape of tongue (lengthen, shorten, curl, flatten)","Retract the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protrude the tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Change shape of tongue (lengthen, shorten, curl, flatten)', 'Intrinsic tongue muscles alter the shape of the tongue by lengthening, shortening, curling, and flattening it. Moving, protruding, or retracting the tongue is the work of the extrinsic muscles.'),
('c0b4838f-7fae-50a3-99be-4276a118b281'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('1e88ccae-b54b-5e74-a259-20814ff14e3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('12b88e91-27fa-58aa-bb6e-17729b2c55da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('62cb410f-bcde-594d-b71e-28dd1c28ff37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('f8b0ee4c-d9dc-5c8a-b110-bc7ac213d455'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('cfcf413f-fca8-563a-ba2d-da957c50ae95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('cd409b85-7ae8-515e-847b-8d9a1aa78c0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('f162f6d9-266c-5512-a77b-5bf2cd01e49a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('d064b967-f356-5188-81b0-9440073b30b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('7518e684-9a34-570f-aebf-0e4ce9fa297c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('ed9ffa4d-a309-5805-8e5c-bab40474c3d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('59528af1-ab0c-5ced-9f6c-f7ec8f99dd17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('0bb37e2b-85c2-53b7-b59e-4ef526bfa15c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('26a13d71-8a77-5062-93a3-c8ef2fbbf246'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding the oral cavity and pharynx, the inferior pharyngeal constrictor originates from:', '["Mandible","Palatine bone","Pterygoid hamulus","Hyoid bone","Thyroid and cricoid cartilages"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid and cricoid cartilages', 'The inferior pharyngeal constrictor arises from the oblique line of the thyroid lamina and the cricoid cartilage, passing posteriorly to meet its fellow of the opposite side. The superior constrictor (mandible, pterygoid hamulus, pterygomandibular raphe) and middle constrictor (hyoid) have other origins.'),
('4a22b8f1-714c-51f3-8a18-a52c96baf683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The cricopharyngeus is part of:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Middle constrictor","Inferior pharyngeal constrictor","Longitudinal muscles","Superior constrictor","Upper esophageal sphincter"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pharyngeal constrictor', 'The cricopharyngeus is the lowest component of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor, arising from the cricoid cartilage. The middle and superior constrictors lie above and do not contribute to this sphincter.'),
('da821830-f2d5-5fd0-8266-1553ff6c99ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The pharyngeal constrictors are supplied by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve, CN X)","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve, CN X)', 'All three pharyngeal constrictors receive motor innervation from the pharyngeal plexus, whose dominant motor contribution comes from the vagus nerve (CN X). The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies the stylopharyngeus rather than the constrictors.'),
('0ef587b1-01d5-52c6-80a7-1560fa4b338f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The stylopharyngeus is supplied by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Trigeminal nerve (V)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The stylopharyngeus is the only pharyngeal muscle innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX). Its fibers pass between the superior and middle constrictors to blend with the longitudinal pharyngeal muscles.'),
('3d656ff5-d019-5bbf-a940-6350fef07c4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The pharyngeal plexus is formed by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["CN IX","CN X","and sympathetic fibers","CN V","CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX', 'The pharyngeal plexus receives branches from the glossopharyngeal (CN IX) and vagus (CN X) nerves together with sympathetic fibers. Branches of CN IX travel through the plexus to supply the stylopharyngeus and pharyngeal mucosa.'),
('4c2a9302-f8d3-5e26-ad53-c8574ac648cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Waldeyer''s ring is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A ring of muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","An arterial circle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nerve plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","A ring of lymphoid tissue (tonsils) in the pharynx","A ring of cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ring of lymphoid tissue (tonsils) in the pharynx', 'Waldeyer''s ring is a ring of lymphoid tissue encircling the pharyngeal inlet, formed by the pharyngeal, tubal, palate and lingual tonsils. It is neither muscular, vascular, nor neural in nature.'),
('04dce5ea-a3eb-579e-9dc1-7959198d1292'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Waldeyer''s ring consists of:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)","tubal tonsils","palatine tonsils lingual tonsil","Only lingual tonsil Palatine and lingual only","Only palatine tonsils Only pharyngeal tonsil"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)', 'Waldeyer''s ring is composed of the pharyngeal, tubal, palate and lingual tonsils distributed around the pharyngeal walls. The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids), lying in the nasopharynx, is one of these components and is most prominent in childhood.'),
('327c5845-830c-501a-b278-992d9be874bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The largest tonsil is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)","Lingual tonsil","Palatine tonsil","Sublingual tonsil","Tubal tonsil"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsil', 'The palatine tonsil, lying in the tonsillar fossa of the oropharynx, is the largest of the tonsils. The pharyngeal, lingual, and tubal tonsils are all smaller components of Waldeyer''s ring.'),
('2e693040-83ee-5fce-b389-358515332711'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The palatine tonsil is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["On the posterior tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","In the soft palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the nasopharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","In the laryngopharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches"]'::jsonb, 'In the tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches', 'The palatine tonsil occupies the tonsillar fossa in the oropharynx, bounded by the palatoglossal (anterior) and palatopharyngeal (posterior) arches. The pharyngeal tonsil lies in the nasopharynx, not in the oropharynx or soft palate.'),
('989efb0e-ffa3-5b83-87ba-d01cad7b4a02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The tonsillar bed (fossa) is formed by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Buccinator and masseter Middle constrictor","styloglossus hyoglossus","Superior constrictor palatoglossus","palatopharyngeus Inferior constrictor","cricopharyngeus Orbicularis oris and levator labii"]'::jsonb, 'Superior constrictor palatoglossus', 'The tonsillar bed is bounded laterally by the superior pharyngeal constrictor and posteriorly by the palatopharyngeus muscle. These muscles, covered by mucosa, form the fossa in which the palatine tonsil lies.'),
('5d6b2416-cbd7-5905-8ad4-a00fb658718c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The blood supply of the palatine tonsil is mainly from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Ascending pharyngeal","Superior thyroid","Tonsillar branch of facial artery","Maxillary artery","Lingual artery"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillar branch of facial artery', 'The palatine tonsil receives its main blood supply from the tonsillar branch of the facial artery, which pierces the superior constrictor to reach the tonsil. The ascending pharyngeal, lingual, and maxillary arteries contribute only smaller branches.'),
('9bd65f3c-bad6-533f-a707-0dcb77789805'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The nerve supply of the palatine tonsil is from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve (V2)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The palatine tonsil receives sensory innervation from the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) through the tonsillar plexus. Referred pain to the ear in tonsillitis travels via the tympanic branch of CN IX.'),
('02fca5f8-71c6-58d4-b304-b4abffd1bfd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["In the oropharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","In the laryngopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the soft palate mediated by specific receptor activation","On the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the roof and posterior wall of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the roof and posterior wall of nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) lies in the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx. Enlargement in childhood may obstruct the choanae and the auditory tube orifice.'),
('4e6c9104-7a08-5664-95fb-a4f939c13adb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The tubal tonsil is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["On the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","In the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the nasopharynx roof mediated by specific receptor activation","Around the pharyngeal opening of auditory tube","In the palatine arches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around the pharyngeal opening of auditory tube', 'The tubal tonsil is found in the mucosa surrounding the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube on the lateral wall of the nasopharynx. As part of Waldeyer''s ring, its enlargement may obstruct the tubal orifice.'),
('b22c46f7-e277-5ceb-91c3-f6e56f322703'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Killian''s dehiscence is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A defect in the palate resulting in decreased functional activity","A space in the middle constrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A opening in the buccopharyngeal fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","A gap in the superior constrictor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A weak area in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'A weak area in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus', 'Killian''s dehiscence is a triangular weak gap in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between the oblique fibers of the thyropharyngeus and the horizontal fibers of the cricopharyngeus. It forms the site through which a pharyngeal pouch herniates.'),
('abc2488c-de44-5749-869c-fb62690ea325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Killian''s dehiscence is the site of:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Parapharyngeal abscess","Laryngeal tumor","Pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum)","Retropharyngeal abscess","Tonsillar abscess"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum)', 'Killian''s dehiscence is the site of a pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum), which herniates through this weak gap just above the cricopharyngeus. Typical symptoms include dysphagia and regurgitation of undigested food.'),
('d7ad02d0-e89d-5792-b29c-aadb1bfd917e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The pharyngeal recess (fossa of Rosenmuller) is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["In the tonsillar fossa","In the laryngopharynx","In the nasopharynx","behind the tubal elevation","In the pyriform sinus In the oropharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal recess (fossa of Rosenmuller) is a lateral recess of the nasopharynx lying just behind the tubal elevation (torus). It is the most common site for nasopharyngeal carcinoma.'),
('de1beba4-893e-5acc-bfab-f1a33817b76f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The pyriform sinus is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A space in the oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","A recess in the nasopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A space in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","A depression in the laryngopharynx on either side of laryngeal inlet","A sinus in the paranasal sinuses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A depression in the laryngopharynx on either side of laryngeal inlet', 'The pyriform sinus is a depression of the laryngopharynx located on each side of the laryngeal inlet, between the thyroid lamina and the aryepiglottic fold. Swallowed foreign bodies may lodge here, and it is a site of laryngopharyngeal carcinoma.'),
('4ae18e5f-88a3-53e1-87b9-1a585193d0b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The pharyngeal isthmus is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["The opening between nose and pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","The opening between nasopharynx and oropharynx (closed by soft palate during swallowing)","The opening between mouth and pharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of auditory tube without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening between pharynx and esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening between nasopharynx and oropharynx (closed by soft palate during swallowing)', 'The pharyngeal isthmus is the junction between the nasopharynx and oropharynx. During swallowing it is closed by elevation of the soft palate and contraction of the palatopharyngeal sphincter, preventing food from entering the nose.'),
('8271f11a-4f1a-56ed-a7f2-b40f4b7ee295'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The auditory tube opens in the:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Lateral wall of oropharynx","Posterior wall of oropharynx","Posterior wall of nasopharynx","Lateral wall of nasopharynx","Roof of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wall of nasopharynx', 'The auditory (pharyngotympanic) tube opens on the lateral wall of the nasopharynx, just above the tubal tonsil. Its orifice lies behind the tubal elevation on swallowing. The roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx are not the site of the opening.'),
('b2764398-91e2-5c51-a394-890a32091c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The salpingopharyngeus muscle arises from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Cartilage of auditory tube (salpinx)","Pterygoid hamulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage of auditory tube (salpinx)', 'The salpingopharyngeus arises from the cartilage of the auditory tube (salpinx) near its pharyngeal opening. It descends to blend with the longitudinal fibers of the palatopharyngeus in the pharyngeal wall.'),
('c4e4ae4b-c080-564c-a890-4118a7ed78f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The salpingopharyngeus muscle:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Elevates the pharynx and opens the auditory tube","Depresses the pharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Depresses the palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevates the palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constricts the pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and opens the auditory tube', 'The salpingopharyngeus elevates the pharynx and pulls open the pharyngeal orifice of the auditory tube during swallowing. It arises from the tubal cartilage and passes down in the lateral pharyngeal wall.'),
('94038c14-5eb4-5849-8513-0840396bbf13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The pharyngotympanic tube is more horizontal in:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Females resulting in decreased functional activity","Children (making middle ear infections more common)","Elderly mediated by specific receptor activation","Adults without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Males leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Children (making middle ear infections more common)', 'The pharyngotympanic (auditory) tube is shorter and more horizontal in children than in adults. Nasopharyngeal organisms can therefore ascend the tube more readily into the middle ear, explaining the higher rate of otitis media in childhood.'),
('04e4a79b-198c-5265-827b-7a3706fb248d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Passavant''s ridge is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A pharyngeal recess resulting in decreased functional activity","A ridge in the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tonsillar fold mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscular ridge formed by palatopharyngeus for sealing the nasopharynx during swallowing","A ridge on the tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A muscular ridge formed by palatopharyngeus for sealing the nasopharynx during swallowing', 'Passavant''s ridge is a muscular ridge on the posterior pharyngeal wall formed by the palatopharyngeus (palatopharyngeal sphincter). It bulges forward during swallowing to seal the nasopharynx against the elevated soft palate, preventing nasal regurgitation.'),
('8972beb1-7318-5de7-98d4-c82b28f0fb15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The vallecula is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A tonsillar crypt resulting in decreased functional activity","A nasal sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A depression in the larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","A depression between the base of tongue and epiglottis","A pharyngeal recess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A depression between the base of tongue and epiglottis', 'The vallecula is the depression between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis, bounded by the median and lateral glossoepiglottic folds. It is a common lodging site for swallowed foreign bodies and is assessed in the ''FOOSH'' (foreign-object-on-swallowing-hover) maneuver.'),
('2fdc22c3-ca1d-5d96-aa21-dae51e236ae2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The glossoepiglottic folds are:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Folds in the pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Folds in the palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Folds in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","Median and lateral folds connecting tongue to epiglottis","Folds in the larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Median and lateral folds connecting tongue to epiglottis', 'The glossoepiglottic folds are median and lateral folds of mucous membrane connecting the base of the tongue to the epiglottis. They bound the right and left valleculae.'),
('09d9516a-cfd2-526d-8030-3481cf9ad316'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The palatoglossal arch is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["The anterior tonsillar pillar","The posterior tonsillar pillar","The soft palate","The uvula","The pharyngeal wall"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior tonsillar pillar', 'The palatoglossal arch is the anterior tonsillar pillar, a fold of mucosa running from the soft palate to the side of the tongue and covering the palatoglossus muscle. The palatopharyngeal arch forms the posterior pillar.'),
('8c6433e5-60e2-58e1-b839-e1d76805d46b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The palatopharyngeal arch is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["The soft palate","The anterior tonsillar pillar","The pharyngeal wall","The posterior tonsillar pillar","The uvula"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior tonsillar pillar', 'The palatopharyngeal arch is the posterior tonsillar pillar, extending from the soft palate to the lateral pharyngeal wall and covering the palatopharyngeus muscle. It lies behind the palatine tonsil.'),
('70956845-a01f-59a0-bcab-8e6ef901e691'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The triangular fold (plica triangularis) is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A fold in the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","A fold in the palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fold of mucosa covering the anterior part of tonsil","A fold in the larynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fold in the pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fold of mucosa covering the anterior part of tonsil', 'The plica triangularis is a triangular fold of mucosa that covers the anterior, lower part of the palatine tonsil. Retracting it exposes the tonsillar crypts, which may harbor debris.'),
('a3f61369-56f0-52e5-a87c-afd5d92b8f88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The tonsillar crypts are:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Lymphatic channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerves of the tonsil accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscles of the tonsil mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood vessels of the tonsil without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Invaginations of tonsillar epithelium that can trap debris"]'::jsonb, 'Invaginations of tonsillar epithelium that can trap debris', 'Tonsillar crypts are deep invaginations of the tonsillar epithelium into the underlying lymphoid tissue. They can trap bacteria and food debris, contributing to recurrent tonsillitis and tonsilloliths.'),
('3c7a38a6-f66a-58b1-bbbc-50da925cfb44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Tonsillitis is inflammation of:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Pharyngeal tonsil","Tubal tonsils","Lingual tonsil","Palatine tonsils","All tonsils"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsils', 'Tonsillitis refers to inflammation of the palatine tonsils, typically presenting with sore throat, fever, and exudate on the tonsils. Inflammation of the pharyngeal tonsil is referred to as adenoiditis.'),
('906e15c7-05f5-5e33-8b4a-4a0a81fe1897'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The palatoglossus muscle is innervated by which nerve?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Vagus nerve (CN X) via pharyngeal plexus","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X) via pharyngeal plexus', 'The palatoglossus, like the other soft palate muscles, is innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X) through the pharyngeal plexus. It is not supplied by CN V, VII, IX, or XII.'),
('fc9909ec-ea6a-54ac-904e-71b7fe575845'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The roof of the oral cavity is formed by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Pharynx","Cheeks","Mandible","Tongue","Hard and soft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Hard and soft palate', 'The roof of the oral cavity is formed by the hard palate anteriorly and the soft palate posteriorly. The cheeks form its lateral walls, the tongue and mylohyoid form its floor, and the pharynx lies behind the oral cavity.'),
('7ef0d98a-b46b-55cf-b4a2-4f19c141c90b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The soft palate is a muscular fold that:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Separates nasopharynx from oropharynx","Elevates the tongue","Closes the larynx","Separates oral cavity from nasal cavity","Opens the auditory tube"]'::jsonb, 'Separates nasopharynx from oropharynx', 'The soft palate is a mobile muscular fold separating the nasopharynx from the oropharynx. Raised during swallowing, it closes the pharyngeal isthmus and prevents nasal regurgitation.'),
('dfdf3bf9-ee0d-5844-8519-69ac93c2e8fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Muscles of the soft palate include:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Stylopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus","Levator veli palatini","tensor veli palatini","palatoglossus","palatopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Levator veli palatini', 'The levator veli palatini is one of the muscles of the soft palate, arising from the auditory tube and petrous bone to elevate the palate. The palatine group also includes the tensor veli palatini, musculus uvulae, palatoglossus, and palatopharyngeus; the stylopharyngeus belongs to the pharynx.'),
('dd490bff-ba8a-5cd8-b72c-00d013143432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Tensor veli palatini is innervated by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Mandibular nerve (V3)","Vagus (X)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular nerve (V3)', 'The tensor veli palatini is innervated by the mandibular branch of the trigeminal nerve (V3) through the nerve to the medial pterygoid. All other soft palate muscles receive vagal fibers via the pharyngeal plexus.'),
('af0e2353-7fa8-5910-be12-5e0ec037630b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The palatoglossus muscle:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Elevates the tongue and depresses the palate","Depresses the tongue","Opens the pharynx","Elevates the palate","Closes the eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the tongue and depresses the palate', 'The palatoglossus elevates the posterior part of the tongue and depresses the soft palate, narrowing the oropharyngeal isthmus. It therefore acts as a sphincter between the oral cavity and the pharynx.'),
('3ef1999a-6308-59a3-9cdc-84c25faddb88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The palatopharyngeus muscle:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Retracts the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Opens the mouth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Closes the nasal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing","Depresses the palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing', 'The palatopharyngeus elevates the pharynx and larynx and draws the lateral pharyngeal walls medially during swallowing. Its fibers also form the posterior tonsillar pillar and the palatopharyngeal sphincter (Passavant''s ridge).'),
('fef123ae-6f33-580c-b4ab-8595731316f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The floor of the oral cavity is formed by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Digastric muscle","Mylohyoid muscle and geniohyoid muscle","Masseter muscle","Buccinator muscle","Pterygoid muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Mylohyoid muscle and geniohyoid muscle', 'The floor of the oral cavity is formed by the paired mylohyoid muscles, with the geniohyoid muscles lying above them. The digastric, masseter, buccinator, and pterygoid muscles do not form the oral floor.'),
('bb07968f-159b-5140-bbc5-cd24f20b849b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The tongue is divided into:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Oral part (anterior 2/3) and pharyngeal part (posterior 1/3)","Right and left halves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base and tip only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum and ventrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper and lower parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral part (anterior 2/3) and pharyngeal part (posterior 1/3)', 'The tongue is divided by the sulcus terminalis into an anterior two-thirds (oral part) and a posterior one-third (pharyngeal part). The two parts differ in embryologic origin, innervation, and type of papillae.'),
('72de98b7-222a-5935-bbb6-a4e06412ac5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The sulcus terminalis divides:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Anterior 2/3 (oral) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal) of tongue","Base from tip accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tip from body mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum from ventrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right from left halves of tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 (oral) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal) of tongue', 'The sulcus terminalis is aV-shaped groove on the dorsum of the tongue separating the anterior two-thirds (oral part) from the posterior one-third (pharyngeal part). The foramen cecum lies at its apex.'),
('27c0cb6e-668a-50f9-b472-678f2bacf4a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The foramen cecum of the tongue is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A nerve foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","A taste bud accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphatic channel mediated by specific receptor activation","The embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct","The opening of Wharton''s duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct', 'The foramen cecum is a small pit at the apex of the sulcus terminalis representing the embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct. It marks the origin and descent of the thyroid gland, and a lingual thyroid may persist near it.'),
('f37b1de2-2c25-5362-acd5-3d0711fcef3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The intrinsic muscles of the tongue are:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Superior longitudinal","inferior longitudinal","transverse","vertical","Buccinator and orbicularis oris"]'::jsonb, 'Superior longitudinal', 'The intrinsic muscles of the tongue comprise the superior and inferior longitudinal, transverse, and vertical muscles, all of which alter tongue shape. The buccinator and orbicularis oris are facial muscles of the cheek and lips, not of the tongue.'),
('38263168-d50c-5cf7-b351-859195c9e3f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The genioglossus muscle:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Depresses the mandible","Depresses the tongue","Retracts the tongue","Elevates the tongue","Protrudes the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Protrudes the tongue', 'The genioglossus protrudes the tongue, and its posterior fibers also pull the tongue forward away from the pharyngeal wall. Paralysis of one side deviates the protruded tongue toward the side of the lesion.'),
('21ffb3d5-6b2b-5cd7-856d-e776a0bcba0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The hyoglossus muscle:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Pulls tongue laterally","Elevates the hyoid","Protrudes the tongue","Elevates the tongue","Depresses and retracts the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses and retracts the tongue', 'The hyoglossus depresses and retracts the tongue, pulling it downward and backward toward the hyoid. The genioglossus protrudes, the styloglossus retracts and elevates, and the palatoglossus elevates the posterior tongue.'),
('ae366b6e-53eb-5ff5-a606-6e1084993077'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The styloglossus muscle:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Opens the mouth","Retracts and elevates the tongue","Pulls tongue medially","Depresses the tongue","Protrudes the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Retracts and elevates the tongue', 'The styloglossus retracts and elevates the tongue, drawing it upward and backward. Its action opposes the protrusion produced by the genioglossus.'),
('6a6cec1d-ce3d-5689-b4bc-3871a7619281'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Taste sensation to the anterior 2/3 of tongue is carried by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Trigeminal nerve (V)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)', 'Taste to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is carried by the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) that travels with the lingual nerve. General sensation in this region is carried by the lingual nerve (V3).'),
('82a3374a-2d59-5050-bb77-0b0d0c89ffe4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Taste sensation to the posterior 1/3 of tongue is carried by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Vagus nerve (X)","Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Trigeminal nerve (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'Taste and general sensation to the posterior one-third of the tongue are carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX). It reaches the tongue by passing between the superior and middle pharyngeal constrictors.'),
('3d70197a-ea42-5c1f-a31a-e9ce90f0659b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding General sensation to the anterior 2/3 of tongue is carried by:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Vagus (X)","Lingual nerve (V3)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve (V3)', 'General (somatic) sensation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue is carried by the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). Taste in the same region travels in the chorda tympani, which joins the lingual nerve.'),
('a1703125-14bc-597e-ab96-0094772ed2f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The tongue is supplied by which artery?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Inferior alveolar artery","Superior thyroid artery","Facial artery","Maxillary artery","Lingual artery (branch of external carotid)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual artery (branch of external carotid)', 'The tongue receives its main blood supply from the lingual artery, a branch of the external carotid artery that passes deep to the hyoglossus. Its branches include the dorsal lingual and deep lingual arteries.'),
('c580ee5e-2635-5541-8b0e-4b863aac5463'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The submandibular gland drains into the:', '["Sublingual fold","Sublingual caruncle via Wharton''s duct","Parotid papilla","Stensen''s duct","Floor of mouth via multiple ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Sublingual caruncle via Wharton''s duct', 'Wharton''s duct (the submandibular duct) opens on the sublingual caruncle beside the frenulum of the tongue, so submandibular saliva drains there. The parotid empties through Stensen''s duct and the sublingual gland through the small ducts of Rivinus, neither of which drains into the caruncle.'),
('2fc38802-f1a1-5f73-b3a6-abc49220a3f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The sublingual gland drains via the:', '["Nasolacrimal duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Multiple ducts of Rivinus and Bartholin''s duct into sublingual fold","Submandibular duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Stensen''s duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wharton''s duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple ducts of Rivinus and Bartholin''s duct into sublingual fold', 'The sublingual gland drains through the many small ducts of Rivinus, which open along the sublingual fold, and through Bartholin''s duct near the sublingual caruncle. The nasolacrimal duct drains tears into the inferior meatus, while Wharton''s and Stensen''s ducts drain the submandibular and parotid glands, respectively.'),
('77c320fb-8345-5b1d-8bc1-2a7d89580531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The parotid gland drains via:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Stensen''s duct opening at the parotid papilla opposite upper 2nd molar","Wharton''s duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duets of Rivinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Nasolacrimal duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bartholin''s duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stensen''s duct opening at the parotid papilla opposite upper 2nd molar', 'Stensen''s duct conveys parotid saliva to the parotid papilla on the buccal mucosa opposite the upper second molar. Wharton''s duct and the ducts of Rivinus belong to the submandibular and sublingual glands, and the nasolacrimal duct conveys tears into the nose.'),
('d29f5e9c-4d0a-51dd-b171-9399a29ce7e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The parotid gland is innervated by the:', '["Trigeminal (V) resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagus (X) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glossopharyngeal (IX) via otic ganglion (parasympathetic)","Hypoglossal (XII) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve (VII) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX) via otic ganglion (parasympathetic)', 'The parotid gland receives preganglionic parasympathetic fibres from the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) that synapse in the otic ganglion; postganglionic fibres run with the auriculotemporal nerve to the gland. The facial nerve supplies muscles of facial expression, and the trigeminal, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves do not give secretomotor supply to the parotid.'),
('d5f66283-d393-5617-b7e3-0d083143c368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The submandibular gland is innervated by the:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX) resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal (V) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagus (X) mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial nerve (VII) via submandibular ganglion (parasympathetic)","Hypoglossal (XII) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII) via submandibular ganglion (parasympathetic)', 'The submandibular gland receives parasympathetic secretomotor fibres from the facial nerve (VII), which synapse in the submandibular ganglion before passing to the gland. The glossopharyngeal, trigeminal, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves do not innervate it.'),
('eaa168cd-b8b9-5012-b441-1ddecb928e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The maxillary artery supplies:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["The ear resulting in decreased functional activity","The orbit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The deep structures of face and jaw","Only the scalp without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The brain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The deep structures of face and jaw', 'The maxillary artery is the main artery of the deep face and jaws, giving branches that pass through the pterygopalatine fossa to supply deep facial structures, teeth, and meninges. The brain, orbit, ear, and scalp are supplied by other vessels.'),
('63566742-2884-5107-9dd1-1929a61197ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The mental foramen transmits the:', '["Mental nerve (V3) and vessels","Hypoglossal nerve","Lingual nerve","Inferior alveolar nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Mental nerve (V3) and vessels', 'The mental foramen is the anterior opening of the mandibular canal and transmits the mental nerve (a branch of V3) together with the mental vessels. The inferior alveolar nerve continues within the canal, while the facial, lingual, and hypoglossal nerves do not pass through this foramen.'),
('a4d70b6d-f89b-533b-94c8-bdae1d95c8e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The inferior alveolar nerve and artery enter the mandible via the:', '["Lingual foramen","Mental foramen","Optic foramen","Incisive foramen","Mandibular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular foramen', 'The inferior alveolar nerve and artery enter the mandibular canal through the mandibular foramen on the medial surface of the ramus. The mental foramen is their exit point anteriorly, and the optic, incisive, and lingual foramina are unrelated openings.'),
('366a2ab0-bb75-589c-82d4-d881a2efebe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["A hinge joint only resulting in decreased functional activity","A synovial joint with an articular disc","A suture mediated by specific receptor activation","A cartilaginous joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fibrous joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A synovial joint with an articular disc', 'The temporomandibular joint is a synovial joint containing an articular disc that divides it into upper and lower compartments for both hinge and gliding movements. It is not a simple hinge, suture, fibrous, or cartilaginous joint.'),
('0de4a1ca-ec5b-5af2-8ebf-0ff769ecf115'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The muscles of mastication include:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Mylohyoid and digastric","Masseter","temporalis","medial pterygoid","lateral pterygoid"]'::jsonb, 'Masseter', 'Masseter belongs to the group of muscles of mastication, together with temporalis and the medial and lateral pterygoids. Mylohyoid and digastric are suprahyoid muscles, not muscles of mastication.'),
('3646460c-1811-52d7-93b1-add777ce150c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The palatine tonsils are located in the:', '["Oral cavity","Laryngopharynx","Oropharynx","Nasal cavity","Nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Oropharynx', 'The palatine tonsils lie in the tonsillar fossa between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches in the lateral wall of the oropharynx. They do not lie in the nasopharynx or laryngopharynx.'),
('c2e36b60-b805-5c6f-9b1f-6cfb7c52e7e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'How many permanent teeth are present in an adult human?', '["28","30","36","24","32"]'::jsonb, '32', 'A complete adult permanent dentition has 32 teeth: 8 incisors, 4 canines, 8 premolars, and 12 molars including the four third molars. A count of 28 excludes the third molars, and the other numbers do not match the adult complement.'),
('6e47a0b1-00b3-5d2a-92b7-9e67f91da3d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The tongue receives its motor innervation from which nerve?', '["Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (XII)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (XII) supplies all intrinsic muscles and most extrinsic muscles of the tongue. The glossopharyngeal, vagus, trigeminal, and facial nerves carry sensory or motor functions other than tongue movement.'),
('f106afed-743f-53c1-b569-348740929fc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The parotid duct opens opposite the:', '["Lower second molar","Upper first molar","Upper second molar","Upper third molar","Lower first molar"]'::jsonb, 'Upper second molar', 'Stensen''s duct crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens on the parotid papilla opposite the crown of the upper second molar. Openings at the other molar positions do not match the known site.'),
('873ed095-4639-5271-ba3a-a361b8a78118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The oral cavity is bounded anteriorly by the:', '["Lips (oral fissure)","Teeth","Palate","Tongue","Cheeks"]'::jsonb, 'Lips (oral fissure)', 'The anterior boundary of the oral cavity is formed by the lips and the oral fissure between them. The teeth, palate, tongue, and cheeks form the lateral, superior, and inferior relationships rather than the anterior limit.'),
('f6c1d8a8-3c6f-55d8-81fa-273dc1b65e9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The oral vestibule is the:', '["Space in the pharynx","Space behind the tongue","Space between lips/cheeks and teeth/gums","Space between upper and lower teeth","Space under the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Space between lips/cheeks and teeth/gums', 'The oral vestibule is the slit-like space between the lips and cheeks externally and the teeth and gums internally. The oral cavity proper lies medial to the dental arches, so the other regions named are not the vestibule.'),
('c9fa7c3e-31fa-5b2a-9bb5-4345bb571866'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The oral cavity proper is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Posterior to the pharynx","Lateral to the teeth","Medial to the teeth","Above the palate","Below the tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to the teeth', 'The oral cavity proper is the main space lying medial to the dental arches, bounded above by the palate and below by the tongue and the floor of the mouth. It does not extend into the pharynx or lateral to the teeth.'),
('a8622631-83b7-52b7-a57a-6c6ea3aa4b38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The palate consists of which parts?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Only soft palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Right and left palates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper and lower palates mediated by specific receptor activation","Hard palate (anterior) and soft palate (posterior)","Only hard palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hard palate (anterior) and soft palate (posterior)', 'The palate is formed by an anterior hard palate, made of bone, and a posterior soft palate composed of muscle. It is not a single structure and is not divided into right and left or upper and lower parts.'),
('0b87f1f8-4df8-5036-b886-3efa51da20ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The hard palate is formed by the:', '["Horizontal plate of palatine only resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporal and zygomatic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maxilla and sphenoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone","Palatine process of maxilla only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine process of maxilla and horizontal plate of palatine bone', 'The hard palate is formed by the palatine processes of the maxillae and the horizontal plates of the palatine bones. The sphenoid, temporal, and zygomatic bones take no part in its formation.'),
('cf08bafa-53f5-58b8-a52d-c30c688c2cfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The soft palate is a muscular fold that separates the:', '["Oropharynx from laryngopharynx","Nasopharynx from oropharynx","Oral cavity from nasal cavity","Right from left nasal cavities","Mouth from vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx from oropharynx', 'The soft palate is a movable muscular shelf that forms the roof of the oropharynx and separates the nasopharynx above from the oropharynx below. It is not the partition between the oral cavity and nasal cavity.'),
('0a35ba9f-7ec8-56e8-869d-ee3031b26b91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Which muscle elevates the soft palate?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Palatopharyngeus","Tensor veli palatini","Palatoglossus","Musculus uvulae","Levator veli palatini"]'::jsonb, 'Levator veli palatini', 'Levator veli palatini arises from the petrous temporal bone and elevates the soft palate during swallowing and speech. Tensor veli palatini tenses the palate, while the other listed muscles have different functions.'),
('7ad635b0-ee23-5fea-9d75-161de40e82ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Which muscle tenses the soft palate and opens the auditory tube?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Levator veli palatini","Salpingopharyngeus","Tensor veli palatini","Musculus uvulae","Palatopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Tensor veli palatini', 'Tensor veli palatini tenses the soft palate, and its tendon hooks around the pterygoid hamulus to dilate the auditory tube. Levator veli palatini elevates the palate, and the other muscles listed do not tense it.'),
('b3b5be98-632f-569f-9d40-63b0f5e05237'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Tensor veli palatini is supplied by which nerve?', '["Vagus nerve","Hypoglossal","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal nerve (V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V3)', 'Tensor veli palatini receives motor supply from the nerve to medial pterygoid, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). The other soft palate muscles are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus.'),
('01cd680c-a162-5223-88dd-c7590d89da29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'All muscles of the soft palate except tensor veli palatini are supplied by the:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve)', 'The muscles of the soft palate other than tensor veli palatini receive motor fibres from the pharyngeal plexus, which is formed mainly by the vagus nerve. Tensor veli palatini is instead supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.'),
('8ef96e7a-a6d5-577b-bfe5-264551ac2d8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'What is the function of musculus uvulae?', '["Opens the auditory tube","Closes the pharyngeal isthmus","Depresses the palate","Elevates the palate","Pulls the uvula upward"]'::jsonb, 'Pulls the uvula upward', 'Musculus uvulae shortens and pulls the uvula upward, producing a ridge on the superior surface of the soft palate. It does not open the auditory tube or close the pharyngeal isthmus.'),
('60582fa1-17af-55a9-877b-f8efd5baf3bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In the study of Oral Cavity Pharynx, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('f15cb38d-5dc6-552f-9123-590922491a85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Oral Cavity Pharynx: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('2a0795ce-8312-51d9-b290-48e0caa3dc4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The tongue is divided into which two parts?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Right and left halves resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior 2/3 (oral) and posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal)","Tip and base mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper and lower halves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial and deep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 (oral) and posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal)', 'The tongue is divided into an anterior two-thirds (oral part) and a posterior one-third (pharyngeal part) by the V-shaped sulcus terminalis. It is not divided into right and left halves or upper and lower parts.'),
('1970fa60-ebd2-51ac-b53c-f8f7995697b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The sulcus terminalis separates the:', '["Tip from body","Upper from lower surfaces","Dorsum from ventral surface","Anterior 2/3 from posterior 1/3 of tongue","Right from left halves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 from posterior 1/3 of tongue', 'The V-shaped sulcus terminalis, with the foramen cecum at its apex, divides the tongue into the anterior two-thirds (oral) and the posterior one-third (pharyngeal). It also marks the developmental site of descent of the thyroid gland.'),
('15524177-8306-510e-a661-f6832647ed3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Oral Cavity Pharynx: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('9b943bc0-f74f-5a42-b162-8c5afa0919d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which of the following is a pointed, cone-shaped lingual papilla located on the posterior part of the tongue?', '["Filiform","conical","foliate","lentiform Vallate","fungiform filiform"]'::jsonb, 'conical', 'Conical papillae are pointed, cone-like projections seen on the posterior part of the tongue and are regarded as a variant of lingual papillae related to the filiform type. Filiform papillae cover the anterior two-thirds, fungiform are mushroom-shaped, foliate lie along the lateral margins, and vallate are the large papillae in front of the sulcus terminalis.'),
('33f3475c-062a-57b3-b819-4bd49e6d13c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Which papillae contain taste buds in adults?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Vallate","fungiform","foliate (but NOT filiform)","All papillae","Only fungiform Only filiform Only vallate"]'::jsonb, 'Vallate', 'Vallate papillae are the largest papillae, arranged in aV just in front of the sulcus terminalis, and each bears numerous taste buds in adults. Filiform papillae have no taste buds, and while fungiform and some foliate papillae also carry taste buds, the other options are not accurate as a class.'),
('5aa67741-2718-5586-926c-874d01847774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The most numerous papillae on the tongue are the:', '["Fungiform","Filiform","Conical","Foliate","Vallate"]'::jsonb, 'Filiform', 'Filiform papillae are the most numerous papillae and give the dorsum of the tongue its rough surface, but they contain no taste buds. Fungiform, conical, foliate, and vallate papillae are all far fewer in number.'),
('46527e9f-119d-5b8c-b493-b14c85648559'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste buds on the anterior 2/3 of the tongue are supplied by the:', '["Lingual nerve (V3)","Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)","Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve VII)', 'Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travels in the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (VII) that joins the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa. The lingual nerve itself carries only general sensation from this area.'),
('795fdccb-d228-55f1-b95e-7b2655e3f72c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Taste buds on the posterior 1/3 of the tongue are supplied by the:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve (VII)","Lingual nerve","Vagus nerve (X)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) supplies both taste and general sensation to the posterior one-third of the tongue. The facial nerve serves taste on the anterior two-thirds, and the hypoglossal, vagus, and lingual nerves are not responsible for this area.'),
('99d10dd0-0803-5030-aae8-7fcabaf39e6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'General sensory innervation to the anterior 2/3 of the tongue is carried by the:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve","Chorda tympani (VII)","Lingual nerve (V3)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve (V3)', 'General sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue runs in the lingual nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). The chorda tympani carries taste from this area, and the glossopharyngeal nerve serves the posterior third.'),
('73c8aa28-79da-5071-9947-4aa424524731'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'General sensory innervation to the posterior 1/3 of the tongue is carried by the:', '["Lingual nerve (V3)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) provides general sensation and taste to the posterior one-third of the tongue. The lingual nerve supplies general sensation to the anterior two-thirds, and the hypoglossal, vagus, and facial nerves do not serve this function here.'),
('ff59f432-fe1c-54cd-802d-963b8985f5bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Motor innervation to all intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles is from the:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Vagus (X)","Trigeminal (V3)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (XII)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (XII) supplies all intrinsic muscles and most extrinsic muscles of the tongue, including genioglossus, hyoglossus, and styloglossus. The glossopharyngeal and facial nerves carry sensory fibres and the vagus nerve supplies the palatoglossus.'),
('f009e108-7557-573b-bd9d-ac00d83da868'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Which tongue muscle is NOT supplied by the hypoglossal nerve?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Palatoglossus","Hyoglossus","Intrinsic muscles","Genioglossus","Styloglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Palatoglossus', 'Palatoglossus is supplied by fibres from the pharyngeal plexus rather than the hypoglossal nerve, even though it can elevate the posterior tongue. Genioglossus, hyoglossus, styloglossus, and the intrinsic muscles all receive hypoglossal supply.'),
('b5975420-6dd0-531d-9a96-acfe4d4b1db6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Oral Cavity Pharynx: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('98e2d893-a7ec-5bff-9273-8ef4f5630fc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In the study of Oral Cavity Pharynx, Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('e8499b34-82db-59b3-b7b5-f5d8f3c19108'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical item 242 (Regarding The styloglossus muscle:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?), which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Pulls the tongue forward","Elevates and retracts the tongue","Protrudes the tongue","Depresses the tongue","Moves the tongue laterally"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates and retracts the tongue', 'Styloglossus arises from the styloid process and elevates and retracts the tongue during swallowing. Protrusion and depression of the tongue are produced by genioglossus and hyoglossus, respectively.'),
('e2d8e1e2-c890-54a8-80df-710cef4b06e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which of the following is one of the four intrinsic muscles of the tongue that runs in the vertical direction?', '["Superior longitudinal","inferior longitudinal transverse","vertical","Longitudinal","circular oblique"]'::jsonb, 'vertical', 'The vertical intrinsic tongue muscle runs between the superior and inferior surfaces and helps flatten and widen the tongue. The four intrinsic muscles are the superior longitudinal, inferior longitudinal, transverse, and vertical muscles, which together change the shape of the tongue.'),
('abbcde78-7787-597c-b180-8b6ba1fbdc3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The intrinsic tongue muscles function to:', '["Elevate the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Depress the tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Change shape of tongue (lengthen, shorten, curl, flatten)","Retract the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protrude the tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Change shape of tongue (lengthen, shorten, curl, flatten)', 'The intrinsic muscles, lying entirely within the tongue, change its shape by lengthening, shortening, curling, and flattening it. They do not move the tongue as a whole, since protrusion, retraction, and depression are produced by the extrinsic muscles.'),
('2586d099-e902-57fc-9ce6-da527a213e81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The floor of the mouth is formed mainly by the:', '["Digastric resulting in decreased functional activity","Geniohyoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyoglossus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mylohyoid muscle (diaphragm of mouth)","Platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mylohyoid muscle (diaphragm of mouth)', 'The paired mylohyoid muscles form the muscular diaphragm of the floor of the mouth, separating the sublingual space above from the submandibular space below. Geniohyoid lies above the mylohyoid, and the digastric and platysma do not form the floor.'),
('6d5aaff6-ae33-56c5-80c9-acda8118519f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The sublingual space is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["In the submental triangle","In the submandibular triangle","Below the mylohyoid","Above the mylohyoid muscle","Between the hyoglossus and genioglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Above the mylohyoid muscle', 'The sublingual space lies above the mylohyoid muscle, bounded by the mucosa of the floor of the mouth, the mandible, and hyoglossus, and it contains the sublingual gland and the submandibular duct. The submental and submandibular triangles lie below the mylohyoid.'),
('7dc3060b-6b56-5457-b606-dd446ad17830'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding The submandibular space is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["In the sublingual region","In the submental triangle","Above the hyoglossus","Above the mylohyoid","Below the mylohyoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Below the mylohyoid muscle', 'The submandibular space lies below the mylohyoid muscle and contains the superficial part of the submandibular gland and the submandibular lymph nodes. The sublingual space sits above the mylohyoid.'),
('f9158260-876b-551a-808f-d98ebb83b09d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Ludwig''s angina is:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Laryngeal infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Parotid infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pharyngeal abscess mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillar abscess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral infection of submandibular and sublingual spaces (submandibular space infection)"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral infection of submandibular and sublingual spaces (submandibular space infection)', 'Ludwig''s angina is a rapidly spreading bilateral cellulitis of the submandibular and sublingual spaces, usually following infection of the lower molars, and it can rapidly obstruct the airway. It is not an infection of the larynx, parotid gland, tonsil, or pharyngeal wall.'),
('a49b722b-e53e-594a-9ebc-d8c7462e4e30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The pharynx extends from the:', '["Base of skull to lower border of cricoid cartilage (C6)","Hard palate to trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal cavity to larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","Mouth to esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Soft palate to larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of skull to lower border of cricoid cartilage (C6)', 'The pharynx extends from the base of the skull down to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at the level of C6, where it becomes the esophagus. The nasal cavity, mouth, and larynx lie outside this vertical span.'),
('27b84448-6fce-54ef-bf64-fa1a193e330f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'The most superior subdivision of the pharynx is the:', '["Nasopharynx","oropharynx","laryngopharynx Superior","middle","inferior Upper"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx', 'The nasopharynx is the most superior subdivision of the pharynx, lying behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate; it contains the pharyngeal tonsil and the openings of the auditory tubes. The oropharynx and laryngopharynx lie inferior to it.'),
('8e8b7c90-663c-572b-bac0-6808ba892761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: the nasopharynx is located:?', '["Posterior to the larynx","Above the hard palate","Posterior to the nasal cavity","above the soft palate","Posterior to the mouth Anterior to the spine"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the nasal cavity', 'The nasopharynx lies behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate, receiving air through the choanae. The larynx, mouth, and spine are lower or unrelated sites.'),
('9766470c-06a5-505a-898f-e415cf97db9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: the oropharynx is located:?', '["Below the larynx","Posterior to the oral cavity","between soft palate and hyoid","Posterior to the nose","Posterior to the larynx Above the soft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the oral cavity', 'The oropharynx lies behind the oral cavity, extending from the soft palate down to the hyoid bone. Neither the larynx nor the nasal region forms its boundary.'),
('1f4536aa-25d3-5f58-b74e-d979bb01e8be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the laryngopharynx is located:?', '["Above the soft palate","Posterior to the larynx","from hyoid to cricoid","Below the cricoid","Anterior to the trachea Posterior to the nose"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the larynx', 'The laryngopharynx lies behind the larynx, extending from the hyoid bone to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage. It is the lowest part of the pharynx, lying above the esophagus.'),
('311f6f56-96db-5cbf-8d58-370b9f6c6d70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: the pharyngobasilar fascia is:?', '["The buccopharyngeal fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","The innermost mucosal layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The outer muscular layer mediated by specific receptor activation","The fibrous layer of pharyngeal wall (between mucosa and muscles)","The prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fibrous layer of pharyngeal wall (between mucosa and muscles)', 'The pharyngobasilar fascia is the dense fibrous layer of the pharyngeal wall placed between the mucosa and the muscles. The buccopharyngeal fascia, prevertebral fascia, mucosa, and muscular layer are separate structures.'),
('03820924-bdf7-57d9-b266-415a78cf46cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: the pharyngeal muscles are arranged as:?', '["Only circular muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Only longitudinal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 constrictors and 2 elevators mediated by specific receptor activation","3 constrictors (superior, middle, inferior) and 3 longitudinal (stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus)","2 constrictors and 2 elevators leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 constrictors (superior, middle, inferior) and 3 longitudinal (stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus)', 'The pharyngeal muscles comprise three constrictors (superior, middle, inferior) and three longitudinal muscles (stylopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, salpingopharyngeus). Counts of only two or four muscle groups do not match this arrangement.'),
('8e2dd22f-e8f8-5488-a192-bae65ef911b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''The superior pharyngeal constrictor originates from:'' is:', '["Pterygoid hamulus","pterygomandibular raphe","mylohyoid line","Mandible","Hyoid bone Cricoid cartilage Thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Pterygoid hamulus', 'The superior pharyngeal constrictor arises partly from the pterygoid hamulus along with the pterygomandibular raphe, mylohyoid line, and side of the tongue. Among the options, the hamulus is a recognized bony origin.'),
('ff07b9de-ca3a-56f1-96ed-9b1f3be25b47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: the middle pharyngeal constrictor originates from:?', '["Mandible","Pterygoid hamulus","Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid","stylohyoid ligament","Thyroid cartilage Cricoid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid', 'The middle pharyngeal constrictor arises from the stylohyoid ligament and the greater and lesser cornua of the hyoid bone. The mandible and the thyroid and cricoid cartilages are origins of the other constrictors.'),
('c847b62d-4008-5c36-84e6-dd59241f834d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: the inferior pharyngeal constrictor originates from:?', '["Mandible","Palatine bone","Pterygoid hamulus","Hyoid bone","Thyroid and cricoid cartilages"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid and cricoid cartilages', 'The inferior pharyngeal constrictor arises from the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage and the cricoid cartilage. The mandible, palatine bone, hamulus, and hyoid bone do not give origin to this muscle.'),
('49c70411-9c38-588b-8606-7ee708d71893'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the cricopharyngeus is part of:?', '["Middle constrictor","Inferior pharyngeal constrictor","Longitudinal muscles","Superior constrictor","Upper esophageal sphincter"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pharyngeal constrictor', 'The cricopharyngeus is the lower part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor and also acts as the upper esophageal sphincter. It does not belong to the superior or middle constrictors.'),
('6e195cdc-994a-502c-8c67-fece697266a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: the pharyngeal constrictors are supplied by:?', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve, CN X)","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus nerve, CN X)', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus, which is formed chiefly by the vagus nerve (CN X) with contributions from CN IX and sympathetic fibers. The glossopharyngeal, trigeminal, facial, and hypoglossal nerves do not innervate them.'),
('46ce4526-8110-57c0-ae82-d1bc740ce11b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: the stylopharyngeus is supplied by:?', '["Trigeminal nerve (V)","Vagus nerve (X)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The stylopharyngeus is the only pharyngeal muscle supplied directly by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX). The vagus, hypoglossal, trigeminal, and facial nerves innervate other pharyngeal muscles.'),
('feaa173c-7a95-50da-8d8a-59ad018e714e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''The pharyngeal plexus is formed by:'' is:', '["CN IX","CN X","and sympathetic fibers","CN V","CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX', 'The pharyngeal plexus is formed by branches from the glossopharyngeal (CN IX), vagus (CN X), and sympathetic trunk. Among the listed components, the glossopharyngeal nerve contributes motor and sensory fibers to the plexus.'),
('08efba4d-eede-5837-a1fc-a8abe6a908e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: waldeyer''s ring is:?', '["A ring of muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","An arterial circle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nerve plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","A ring of lymphoid tissue (tonsils) in the pharynx","A ring of cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ring of lymphoid tissue (tonsils) in the pharynx', 'Waldeyer''s ring is a circular band of lymphoid tissue formed by the pharyngeal, tubal, palatine, and lingual tonsils that guards the pharyngeal inlet. It has no muscular, arterial, neural, or cartilaginous composition.'),
('0dea1fd2-cec0-5356-95c8-960ee82f9d05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: waldeyer''s ring consists of:?', '["Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)","tubal tonsils","palatine tonsils lingual tonsil","Only lingual tonsil Palatine and lingual only","Only palatine tonsils Only pharyngeal tonsil"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)', 'Waldeyer''s ring is made up of the pharyngeal (adenoids), tubal, palatine, and lingual tonsils. The pharyngeal tonsil is one of these components and lies in the roof of the nasopharynx.'),
('6d53d86b-3499-505f-8b15-156493debf95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the largest tonsil is:?', '["Pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids)","Lingual tonsil","Palatine tonsil","Sublingual tonsil","Tubal tonsil"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsil', 'The palatine tonsil is the largest of the tonsils, lying between the tonsillar arches in the lateral wall of the oropharynx. The pharyngeal, lingual, and tubal tonsils are all smaller.'),
('73039cf2-3639-59e7-ab83-e2d7beb14f14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: the palatine tonsil is located:?', '["On the posterior tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","In the soft palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the nasopharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","In the laryngopharynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches"]'::jsonb, 'In the tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches', 'The palatine tonsil lies in the tonsillar fossa between the palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches in the oropharynx. It does not occupy the tongue, soft palate, nasopharynx, or laryngopharynx.'),
('c0961043-9608-51d1-90dd-6d51ca6d6d11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: the tonsillar bed (fossa) is formed by:?', '["Buccinator and masseter Middle constrictor","styloglossus hyoglossus","Superior constrictor palatoglossus","palatopharyngeus Inferior constrictor","cricopharyngeus Orbicularis oris and levator labii"]'::jsonb, 'Superior constrictor palatoglossus', 'The tonsillar bed (fossa) is formed by the superior pharyngeal constrictor and the palatoglossus muscle. The buccinator, styloglossus, inferior constrictor, and other listed muscles do not contribute to this fossa.'),
('41e9273d-bb10-5eb2-ac0a-36f050f1181d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''The blood supply of the palatine tonsil is mainly from:'' is:', '["Ascending pharyngeal","Superior thyroid","Tonsillar branch of facial artery","Maxillary artery","Lingual artery"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillar branch of facial artery', 'The main arterial supply of the palatine tonsil is the tonsillar branch of the facial artery, supplemented by the ascending palatine, ascending pharyngeal, and lingual arteries. The superior thyroid and maxillary arteries are not the principal sources.'),
('a4b0637d-224a-5ac3-bcdd-14cee874080e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: the nerve supply of the palatine tonsil is from:?', '["Vagus nerve (X)","Trigeminal nerve (V2)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'The palatine tonsil receives its sensory supply from the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), with the lesser palatine nerves adding mucosal supply. The vagus, trigeminal, hypoglossal, and facial nerves do not supply it.'),
('71c67670-f528-514d-9139-9709e2cdd9b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: the pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) is located:?', '["In the oropharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","In the laryngopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the soft palate mediated by specific receptor activation","On the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the roof and posterior wall of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the roof and posterior wall of nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) lies in the roof and posterior wall of the nasopharynx. It is not situated in the oropharynx, laryngopharynx, soft palate, or tongue.'),
('413a4828-d61e-5851-a444-43aeee39bd97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the tubal tonsil is located:?', '["On the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","In the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the nasopharynx roof mediated by specific receptor activation","Around the pharyngeal opening of auditory tube","In the palatine arches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around the pharyngeal opening of auditory tube', 'The tubal tonsil surrounds the pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube in the lateral wall of the nasopharynx. It is not found on the tongue, in the larynx, in the nasopharyngeal roof, or on the palatine arches.'),
('6845826e-b039-531e-b48f-94b7da3fb576'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: killian''s dehiscence is:?', '["A defect in the palate resulting in decreased functional activity","A space in the middle constrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A opening in the buccopharyngeal fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","A gap in the superior constrictor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A weak area in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'A weak area in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus', 'Killian''s dehiscence is a weak gap in the inferior pharyngeal constrictor between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus parts. It is not located in the palate, middle constrictor, buccopharyngeal fascia, or superior constrictor.'),
('23fbe348-b31e-56ad-801d-351467448a8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: killian''s dehiscence is the site of:?', '["Parapharyngeal abscess","Laryngeal tumor","Pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum)","Retropharyngeal abscess","Tonsillar abscess"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum)', 'Killian''s dehiscence is the site where a pharyngeal pouch (Zenker''s diverticulum) herniates through the weak inferior pharyngeal constrictor. Abscesses and laryngeal tumors arise at different sites.'),
('4675cca6-3468-5091-9567-4e9b3e08a395'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''The pharyngeal recess (fossa of Rosenmuller) is located:'' is:', '["In the tonsillar fossa","In the laryngopharynx","In the nasopharynx","behind the tubal elevation","In the pyriform sinus In the oropharynx"]'::jsonb, 'In the nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal recess (fossa of Rosenmuller) lies in the lateral wall of the nasopharynx behind the tubal elevation and is the most common site of nasopharyngeal carcinoma. It is not in the tonsillar fossa or laryngopharynx.'),
('bfa8ac60-32c5-584f-a3f2-a0e1f0adab46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: the pyriform sinus is:?', '["A space in the oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","A recess in the nasopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A space in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","A depression in the laryngopharynx on either side of laryngeal inlet","A sinus in the paranasal sinuses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A depression in the laryngopharynx on either side of laryngeal inlet', 'The pyriform sinus is a depression in the laryngopharynx on either side of the laryngeal inlet, a common site for impaction of swallowed foreign bodies. It is not in the oral cavity, nasopharynx, nose, or paranasal sinuses.'),
('4c49e977-fbec-5982-9ab6-7e93e794f4b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: the pharyngeal isthmus is:?', '["The opening between nose and pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","The opening between nasopharynx and oropharynx (closed by soft palate during swallowing)","The opening between mouth and pharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of auditory tube without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening between pharynx and esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening between nasopharynx and oropharynx (closed by soft palate during swallowing)', 'The pharyngeal isthmus is the opening between the nasopharynx and oropharynx that is closed by elevation of the soft palate during swallowing. The other openings do not represent this junction.'),
('73a08f95-7a4e-555c-8953-e409c99aaa0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the auditory tube opens in the:?', '["Lateral wall of oropharynx","Posterior wall of oropharynx","Posterior wall of nasopharynx","Lateral wall of nasopharynx","Roof of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wall of nasopharynx', 'The pharyngotympanic (auditory) tube opens into the lateral wall of the nasopharynx behind the inferior nasal concha. Neither the oropharynx nor the posterior wall or roof of the nasopharynx is the site of its opening.'),
('0557d490-28f5-572a-8195-c4e5bb818fcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: the salpingopharyngeus muscle arises from:?', '["Cartilage of auditory tube (salpinx)","Pterygoid hamulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palatine bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage of auditory tube (salpinx)', 'The salpingopharyngeus arises from the cartilage of the auditory tube (salpinx) near its pharyngeal opening. The hamulus, palatine bone, hyoid, and thyroid cartilage give origin to other pharyngeal muscles.'),
('b179b03a-20a0-54fa-814e-c56992de919a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: the salpingopharyngeus muscle:?', '["Elevates the pharynx and opens the auditory tube","Depresses the pharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Depresses the palate mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevates the palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constricts the pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and opens the auditory tube', 'The salpingopharyngeus elevates the pharynx and opens the auditory tube during swallowing. It does not depress the pharynx or palate, elevate the palate, or constrict the pharynx.'),
('46b2f901-4e8c-5670-bf85-f6565bea0dfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''The pharyngotympanic tube is more horizontal in:'' is:', '["Females resulting in decreased functional activity","Children (making middle ear infections more common)","Elderly mediated by specific receptor activation","Adults without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Males leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Children (making middle ear infections more common)', 'The pharyngotympanic tube is more horizontal in children, which favors the spread of infection from the nasopharynx to the middle ear. In adults it descends more obliquely.'),
('30ede87a-3a5d-5bdb-83c3-87040c1b7298'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: passavant''s ridge is:?', '["A pharyngeal recess resulting in decreased functional activity","A ridge in the larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tonsillar fold mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscular ridge formed by palatopharyngeus for sealing the nasopharynx during swallowing","A ridge on the tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A muscular ridge formed by palatopharyngeus for sealing the nasopharynx during swallowing', 'Passavant''s ridge is a muscular elevation formed by the palatopharyngeus and superior pharyngeal constrictor fibers that helps seal the nasopharynx during swallowing. It is not a recess or a fold.'),
('18ae3f1c-afce-5f38-8c78-93e3564302c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: the vallecula is:?', '["A tonsillar crypt resulting in decreased functional activity","A nasal sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A depression in the larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","A depression between the base of tongue and epiglottis","A pharyngeal recess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A depression between the base of tongue and epiglottis', 'The vallecula is the depression between the base of the tongue and the epiglottis, bounded by the glossoepiglottic folds. It is not a tonsillar crypt, nasal sinus, laryngeal depression, or pharyngeal recess.'),
('e06f52e0-d83a-56da-b601-3cb4b739dfd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the glossoepiglottic folds are:?', '["Folds in the pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Folds in the palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Folds in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","Median and lateral folds connecting tongue to epiglottis","Folds in the larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Median and lateral folds connecting tongue to epiglottis', 'The glossoepiglottic folds are the median and lateral mucosal folds connecting the tongue to the epiglottis that bound the valleculae. They are not folds of the palate, nose, pharynx, or larynx.'),
('b2fd3e9a-8c38-55be-a842-bbfe6d23c44d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: the palatoglossal arch is:?', '["The anterior tonsillar pillar","The posterior tonsillar pillar","The soft palate","The uvula","The pharyngeal wall"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior tonsillar pillar', 'The palatoglossal arch is the anterior tonsillar pillar, formed by the palatoglossus muscle. The palatopharyngeal arch is the posterior pillar, while the uvula and soft palate are separate structures.'),
('3d8702f0-fd2e-58a4-85f8-6f02e54ce0e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: the palatopharyngeal arch is:?', '["The soft palate","The anterior tonsillar pillar","The pharyngeal wall","The posterior tonsillar pillar","The uvula"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior tonsillar pillar', 'The palatopharyngeal arch is the posterior tonsillar pillar, formed by the palatopharyngeus muscle. The palatoglossal arch is the anterior pillar, and the uvula, soft palate, and pharyngeal wall are distinct structures.'),
('ad8bff33-88a7-585b-adbe-8a78d2cd3f5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''The triangular fold (plica triangularis) is:'' is:', '["A fold in the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","A fold in the palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fold of mucosa covering the anterior part of tonsil","A fold in the larynx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fold in the pharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fold of mucosa covering the anterior part of tonsil', 'The plica triangularis is a fold of mucosa that covers the anterior part of the palatine tonsil. Folds of the tongue, palate, larynx, and pharynx are different structures.'),
('f8de2cb9-97c0-5deb-ba7e-d69257e22a0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: the tonsillar crypts are:?', '["Lymphatic channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerves of the tonsil accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscles of the tonsil mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood vessels of the tonsil without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Invaginations of tonsillar epithelium that can trap debris"]'::jsonb, 'Invaginations of tonsillar epithelium that can trap debris', 'The tonsillar crypts are invaginations of the tonsillar epithelium that can trap cellular debris and form caseous plugs or tonsilloliths. They are not lymphatic, neural, muscular, or vascular structures.'),
('f3575971-d181-59eb-afc3-622e91710bc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: tonsillitis is inflammation of:?', '["Pharyngeal tonsil","Tubal tonsils","Lingual tonsil","Palatine tonsils","All tonsils"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsils', 'Tonsillitis denotes inflammation of the palatine tonsils, the tonsils most often affected clinically. The pharyngeal, tubal, and lingual tonsils are not the target of this term.'),
('32dde7fd-043e-555d-8156-22b85584e291'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the palatoglossus muscle is innervated by which nerve?', '["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Vagus nerve (CN X) via pharyngeal plexus","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X) via pharyngeal plexus', 'The palatoglossus receives its motor supply from the vagus nerve (CN X) through the pharyngeal plexus. The glossopharyngeal, trigeminal, hypoglossal, and facial nerves do not innervate it.'),
('2e31ead7-ac1e-5ba1-8d71-84e58c4cbaf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: the roof of the oral cavity is formed by:?', '["Pharynx","Cheeks","Mandible","Tongue","Hard and soft palate"]'::jsonb, 'Hard and soft palate', 'The roof of the oral cavity is formed by the hard palate anteriorly and the soft palate posteriorly. The pharynx, cheeks, mandible, and tongue form other boundaries of the mouth.'),
('ad1fc6ff-9271-5589-a7be-41f64b4dc4de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: the soft palate is a muscular fold that:?', '["Separates nasopharynx from oropharynx","Elevates the tongue","Closes the larynx","Separates oral cavity from nasal cavity","Opens the auditory tube"]'::jsonb, 'Separates nasopharynx from oropharynx', 'The soft palate is a muscular fold that separates the nasopharynx from the oropharynx, closing the pharyngeal isthmus during swallowing. It does not close the larynx or open the auditory tube.'),
('a63351f8-b2ec-558f-8b15-3491782ae9d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''Muscles of the soft palate include:'' is:', '["Stylopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus","Levator veli palatini","tensor veli palatini","palatoglossus","palatopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Levator veli palatini', 'The muscles of the soft palate include the levator and tensor veli palatini, palatoglossus, palatopharyngeus, and musculus uvulae; the levator veli palatini is one of them. The stylopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus act on the pharynx, not the palate.'),
('020e9155-d72b-5ff7-8810-88938dc5186a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: tensor veli palatini is innervated by:?', '["Mandibular nerve (V3)","Vagus (X)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular nerve (V3)', 'The tensor veli palatini is innervated by the mandibular nerve (V3) through the nerve to the medial pterygoid. All other soft palate muscles are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus.'),
('4925274d-69df-583e-a269-bc72ce9684db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: the palatoglossus muscle:?', '["Elevates the tongue and depresses the palate","Depresses the tongue","Opens the pharynx","Elevates the palate","Closes the eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the tongue and depresses the palate', 'The palatoglossus elevates the posterior part of the tongue and depresses the soft palate, acting at the palatoglossal arch. It does not depress the tongue, elevate the palate, or open the pharynx or eustachian tube.'),
('2596ea75-3155-54df-ae45-d1a07f516f48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Regarding Oral cavity and pharynx, what is the correct answer to: the palatopharyngeus muscle:?', '["Retracts the tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Opens the mouth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Closes the nasal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing","Depresses the palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing', 'The palatopharyngeus elevates the pharynx and larynx during swallowing and forms the posterior tonsillar pillar. It does not retract the tongue, open the mouth, or depress the palate.'),
('ac9927e2-63c7-59e7-b1d5-37c2362c5b31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'For Oral cavity and pharynx, which option is most appropriate: the floor of the oral cavity is formed by:?', '["Digastric muscle","Mylohyoid muscle and geniohyoid muscle","Masseter muscle","Buccinator muscle","Pterygoid muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Mylohyoid muscle and geniohyoid muscle', 'The floor of the oral cavity is formed by the mylohyoid and geniohyoid muscles, with the hyoglossus lying above them. The digastric, masseter, buccinator, and pterygoid muscles do not form the floor.'),
('555ff47b-f27b-50d6-a9cd-89d891c654d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'Which answer is correct in Oral cavity and pharynx when asked: the tongue is divided into:?', '["Oral part (anterior 2/3) and pharyngeal part (posterior 1/3)","Right and left halves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base and tip only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum and ventrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper and lower parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral part (anterior 2/3) and pharyngeal part (posterior 1/3)', 'The tongue is divided into an oral part (anterior two-thirds) and a pharyngeal part (posterior one-third). Divisions by halves, base/tip, or upper/lower parts are not the anatomical plan.'),
('dacb6b1a-db57-5007-88d7-05f9d247888b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, the best response to ''The sulcus terminalis divides:'' is:', '["Anterior 2/3 (oral) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal) of tongue","Base from tip accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tip from body mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsum from ventrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right from left halves of tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 (oral) from posterior 1/3 (pharyngeal) of tongue', 'The sulcus terminalis is the V-shaped groove that separates the anterior two-thirds (oral) from the posterior one-third (pharyngeal) of the tongue. It does not divide the tongue by length, side, or surface.'),
('ba7c3aba-f09e-5c1a-b084-5fa7610ccaf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'During revision of Oral cavity and pharynx, which of the following is correct: the foramen cecum of the tongue is:?', '["A nerve foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","A taste bud accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphatic channel mediated by specific receptor activation","The embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct","The opening of Wharton''s duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct', 'The foramen cecum is the embryological remnant of the thyroglossal duct at the junction of the anterior two-thirds and posterior one-third of the tongue. It is not a nerve foramen, taste bud, lymphatic channel, or salivary duct opening.'),
('15ebc39f-f4fe-5a9d-9c1f-19ed50ab3f21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Oral Cavity Pharynx', 'In Oral cavity and pharynx, which statement best fits the following fact: the intrinsic muscles of the tongue are:?', '["Superior longitudinal","inferior longitudinal","transverse","vertical","Buccinator and orbicularis oris"]'::jsonb, 'Superior longitudinal', 'The intrinsic muscles of the tongue include the superior and inferior longitudinal, transverse, and vertical muscles, which alter the shape of the tongue; the superior longitudinal is one of these four. The buccinator and orbicularis oris are facial muscles, not tongue muscles.'),
('fd47ac0c-dedf-5402-94f2-d172374c565c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Orbit apex directed:', '["Posteromedially","Anteriorly","Laterally","Superiorly","Inferiorly"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedially', 'The orbit is a pyramidal cavity whose wide base faces anterolaterally while the apex converges backward and medially toward the optic canal at the sphenoid bone. All other directions oppose this posteromedial course toward the cranial base.'),
('8005c341-8a86-5029-8211-7b55e57efd0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'How many bones form the orbit?', '["7","4","5","6","8"]'::jsonb, '7', 'Seven bones contribute to each orbit: frontal, zygomatic, maxillary, sphenoid, ethmoid, lacrimal, and palatine. The other choices undercount or overcount these bony contributions.'),
('1f4ee06d-59cd-579b-bae1-4b8db5e26aec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Orbital roof formed by:', '["Zygomatic resulting in decreased functional activity","Ethmoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal bone orbital plate and lesser wing of sphenoid","Only frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenoid only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal bone orbital plate and lesser wing of sphenoid', 'The roof is made by the orbital plate of the frontal bone anteriorly and the lesser wing of the sphenoid posteriorly. Zygomatic and ethmoid bones do not contribute to the roof.'),
('df72a16b-0ed5-515c-b2eb-55164f2a0d6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Orbital floor formed by:', '["Only maxilla","Only zygomatic","Maxilla + ethmoid","Maxilla","zygomatic palatine Palatine + sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla', 'The floor is formed mainly by the orbital plate of the maxilla, with minor contributions from the zygomatic and palatine bones. The ethmoid and sphenoid play no part in the floor.'),
('54f8f249-69fd-5e36-8c7f-684ad2adec8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lateral orbital wall formed by:', '["Maxilla only","Palatine + maxilla","Frontal + sphenoid","Zygomatic + greater wing of sphenoid","Ethmoid + lacrimal"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic + greater wing of sphenoid', 'The lateral wall is formed by the zygomatic bone anteriorly and the greater wing of the sphenoid posteriorly. It is also the strongest wall of the orbit.'),
('4196a62c-350d-5540-8207-64b92a99d268'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Medial orbital wall formed by:', '["Ethmoid + maxilla","Only lacrimal","Sphenoid + frontal Only ethmoid","Ethmoid (lamina papyracea) lacrimal","maxilla sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Ethmoid (lamina papyracea) lacrimal', 'From front to back the medial wall comprises the frontal process of the maxilla, the lacrimal bone, the lamina papyracea of the ethmoid, and the body of the sphenoid. The thin lamina papyracea is the most frequently fractured and the key ethmoid contribution.'),
('3aac8c89-68e0-5def-ab0e-4ef8ef1dc25b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Optic canal transmits:', '["CN II + opthalmic vein","Optic nerve only","Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery","Ophthalmic artery only","CN II + III"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery', 'The optic canal, lying at the orbital apex in the lesser wing of the sphenoid, transmits the optic nerve and the ophthalmic artery. The ophthalmic vein and CN III pass through the superior orbital fissure instead.'),
('fc30159f-e687-5b79-9254-6647795f6878'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Optic canal located in which bone?', '["Frontal","Lesser wing of sphenoid","Greater wing of sphenoid","Ethmoid","Maxilla"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser wing of sphenoid', 'Each optic canal pierces the root of the lesser wing of the sphenoid, connecting the middle cranial fossa to the orbit. The greater wing of the sphenoid houses the superior orbital fissure region but not the optic canal.'),
('837c37b1-396c-55fc-90b8-df0a29b59268'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior orbital fissure transmits:', '["CN II + III","Only CN III","CN III","IV","VI only Ophthalmic vein only"]'::jsonb, 'CN III', 'The superior orbital fissure transmits CN III, IV, V1 (lacrimal, frontal, nasociliary), VI, and the ophthalmic veins. CN III is a genuine component, whereas CN II passes through the optic canal and cannot cross this fissure.'),
('31e13644-859e-5d7d-8fef-76fda4bab6b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior orbital fissure located between:', '["Maxilla + zygomatic","Lacrimal + ethmoid","Sphenoid + ethmoid","Frontal + sphenoid","Greater and lesser wings of sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser wings of sphenoid', 'The superior orbital fissure is the slit between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid bone. It opens from the middle cranial fossa into the orbit and transmits the oculomotor nerves and veins.'),
('84733d7e-18d8-56f2-8d9e-80479d9220fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Inferior orbital fissure transmits:', '["CN III","Infraorbital nerve (V2)","zygomatic nerve","infraorbital vessels","Ophthalmic artery Optic nerve CN IV"]'::jsonb, 'Infraorbital nerve (V2)', 'The inferior orbital fissure transmits the infraorbital nerve (a branch of V2), the zygomatic nerve, infraorbital vessels, and the inferior ophthalmic vein. These structures pass from the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae into the orbit.'),
('4941e568-2c30-5147-a47e-1a49f382adab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Inferior orbital fissure located between:', '["Sphenoid + ethmoid","Maxilla and greater wing of sphenoid","Frontal + sphenoid","Zygomatic and maxilla","Lacrimal + maxilla"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla and greater wing of sphenoid', 'The inferior orbital fissure lies between the maxilla (and orbital surface) below and the greater wing of the sphenoid above. It connects the orbit to the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae.'),
('667f3278-985e-5aad-95bc-fb6b9d970792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Extraocular muscles include:', '["5 muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","4 recti (SR, IR, MR, LR) + 2 obliques (SO, IO)","6 recti mediated by specific receptor activation","4 recti + 1 oblique without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 obliques leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 recti (SR, IR, MR, LR) + 2 obliques (SO, IO)', 'Six extraocular muscles move the eyeball: the four recti (superior, inferior, medial, lateral) and the two obliques (superior and inferior). Levator palpebrae superioris is present as well but acts on the eyelid, not the eyeball.'),
('10c5ab90-c1b6-5f2b-bf56-f031066ff570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lateral rectus supplied by:', '["Oculomotor (III)","Trochlear (IV)","Trigeminal (V)","Abducens (CN VI)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Abducens (CN VI)', 'The lateral rectus is innervated by the abducens nerve (CN VI); CN VI palsy produces medial deviation of the eye because abduction is lost. The oculomotor, trochlear, trigeminal, and facial nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('3a23219f-ce7a-5eb6-a528-4006b87592f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior oblique supplied by:', '["Facial (VII)","Trochlear (CN IV)","Abducens (VI)","Trigeminal (V)","Oculomotor (III)"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlear (CN IV)', 'The superior oblique is innervated by the trochlear nerve (CN IV), the only cranial nerve that emerges from the dorsal brainstem and the one with the longest intracranial course. Trochlear palsy causes weakness of downward gaze, especially during adduction.'),
('f8c32632-9ae9-5047-a2a5-d32ce6ee6d58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior rectus supplied by:', '["Trochlear (IV)","Abducens (VI)","Trigeminal (V)","Facial (VII)","Oculomotor (CN III)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The superior rectus is supplied by the superior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III). It elevates, adducts, and intorts the eye. Trochlear, abducens, trigeminal, and facial nerves do not innervate it.'),
('1cc97c22-5865-506c-b5b9-8fd362a158c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Inferior rectus supplied by:', '["Facial (VII)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Trochlear (IV)","Trigeminal (V)","Abducens (VI)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The inferior rectus receives its nerve supply from the inferior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III). A CN III palsy therefore leaves the eye abducted and depressed by the unopposed lateral rectus and superior oblique.'),
('e1465323-1fe6-5d23-b8c3-7f59521cbc48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Medial rectus supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V)","Trochlear (IV)","Facial (VII)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Abducens (VI)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The medial rectus is innervated by the inferior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III). It adducts the eye, and CN III palsy causes loss of adduction. The trigeminal, trochlear, facial, and abducens nerves do not supply it.'),
('21ba1be5-96fc-5889-9cff-cf6c7c737aa6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Inferior oblique supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V)","Abducens (VI)","Trochlear (IV)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The inferior oblique is innervated by the inferior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III). It extorts, elevates, and abducts the eye, with elevation most effective when the eye is adducted. The trochlear nerve does not supply this muscle.'),
('a5a509e4-510f-5513-a2d6-e019f21a2444'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior oblique passes through:', '["Inferior orbital fissure","Optic canal","Trochlea (pulley) on frontal bone","Annulus of Zinn","Superior orbital fissure"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlea (pulley) on frontal bone', 'The superior oblique tendon passes through the trochlea, a fibrocartilaginous pulley attached to the trochlear fossa of the frontal bone, then turns to insert on the sclera behind the equator. This pulley redirects the tendon and reverses its pull direction.'),
('6f9c9f9c-2ea7-58d0-9944-7b8378e1ab75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Annulus of Zinn is:', '["Pulley for superior oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimal sac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles","Optic canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trochlea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles', 'The annulus of Zinn is the common tendinous ring at the orbital apex from which the four rectus muscles arise. It encircles the optic canal and central part of the superior orbital fissure, so structures within it (optic nerve, ophthalmic artery, CN III and VI) enter the orbit through the ring.'),
('0b3c5d09-7726-591f-a0ea-44cdb7deb683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Levator palpebrae superioris supplied by:', '["Trochlear (IV) resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oculomotor (CN III) superior division","Abducens (VI) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial (VII) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III) superior division', 'The levator palpebrae superioris is innervated by the superior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III), which also supplies the superior rectus. Loss of this supply causes ptosis. Sympathetic fibers supply only the superior tarsal (Muller) muscle attached to it.'),
('f3ed0cea-f2af-5de1-98c1-190acd95d9f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior tarsal muscle (Muller''s) supplied by:', '["Facial (VII)","Trochlear (IV)","Sympathetic fibers","Abducens (VI)","Oculomotor (III)"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic fibers', 'The superior tarsal (Muller) muscle receives sympathetic innervation from the superior cervical ganglion; it adds a few millimetres of eyelid elevation. In Horner syndrome, sympathetic loss produces mild ptosis with a small pupil. The oculomotor and facial nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('5f53b5ff-2c47-52d9-a015-42423d1e975a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior rectus action:', '["Extorsion resulting in decreased functional activity","Adduction only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abduction mediated by specific receptor activation","Depression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevation adduction intorsion"]'::jsonb, 'Elevation adduction intorsion', 'The superior rectus elevates the eye, with additional adduction and intorsion; its elevating effect is strongest when the eye is abducted. Extorsion and depression belong to the inferior rectus and inferior oblique.'),
('7441ebc4-8aab-5b46-a447-0fb9610f5fb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Inferior rectus action:', '["Depression","adduction","extorsion","Abduction","Intorsion Adduction only Elevation"]'::jsonb, 'Depression', 'The inferior rectus depresses the eye, with additional adduction and extorsion; depression is strongest when the eye is abducted. Elevation and intorsion belong to the superior rectus and superior oblique.'),
('d8f6c6af-f1fd-5a65-8238-d778586aad5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Medial rectus action:', '["Rotation","Adduction","Elevation","Abduction","Depression"]'::jsonb, 'Adduction', 'The medial rectus pulls the eye medially, adducting the globe; it has no vertical or torsional component. Abduction is the function of the lateral rectus, while elevation and depression are produced by the vertical recti and obliques.'),
('45e05968-0475-5802-a8ed-b509cbc83c2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lateral rectus action:', '["Adduction","Elevation","Rotation","Abduction","Depression"]'::jsonb, 'Abduction', 'The lateral rectus rotates the eye laterally, abducting the globe, and is the only muscle to do so. Abducens (CN VI) palsy therefore leaves the eye medially deviated because adduction by the medial rectus goes unopposed.'),
('aec0df19-150e-542f-899a-68adf39378e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior oblique action:', '["Abduction only","Elevation","adduction extorsion Depression only","Depression","abduction intorsion Intorsion only"]'::jsonb, 'Depression', 'The superior oblique primarily intorts the eye and, when the eye is adducted, depresses it; it also slightly abducts. Depression is lost in trochlear (CN IV) palsy, producing weakness looking down and toward the nose. Elevation and extorsion belong to the inferior oblique.'),
('2ca81a47-eec6-54aa-8aec-9cbb4aedaf30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Inferior oblique action:', '["Elevation","abduction","extorsion","Abduction only","Elevation only Extorsion only Depression adduction intorsion"]'::jsonb, 'Elevation', 'The inferior oblique primarily extorts the eye and, when the eye is adducted, elevates it; it also slightly abducts. Elevation by this muscle is why the eye can look up even when adducted. Depression and intorsion belong to the superior oblique.'),
('6b56a1d6-b50c-5fd4-b5f7-0f0d0aec4274'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Eyeball layers:', '["6 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 (fibrous, vascular, neural)","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (fibrous, vascular, neural)', 'The eyeball has three coats: an outer fibrous layer (cornea and sclera), a middle vascular layer or uvea (choroid, ciliary body, iris), and an inner neural layer (retina). Any other count omits one of these tunics.'),
('37b875ec-f21e-5ad5-8f53-ad4835d99715'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Outer fibrous layer:', '["Cornea + iris resulting in decreased functional activity","Sclera only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sclera (posterior 5/6) and cornea (anterior 1/6)","Sclera + choroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cornea only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sclera (posterior 5/6) and cornea (anterior 1/6)', 'The outer fibrous coat is formed by the opaque sclera covering the posterior five-sixths of the globe and the transparent cornea occupying the anterior one-sixth. The choroid is a vascular layer, not part of the fibrous coat.'),
('ff68b43e-e2aa-5cb8-979b-fca60c4f3dbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Middle vascular layer (uvea):', '["Choroid only","Ciliary body + lens","Iris + cornea","Choroid","ciliary body iris Choroid + retina"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid', 'The uvea is the vascular pigmented middle coat and comprises the choroid, ciliary body, and iris. Of the options, the choroid is the only pure uveal component, since the retina is neural and the cornea is not vascular.'),
('39dc881c-5332-5e88-85a7-b8e5e4f0d4dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Retina has how many layers?', '["5","10 layers","3","7","12"]'::jsonb, '10 layers', 'The neural retina has ten layers, from the retinal pigment epithelium through the photoreceptors, nuclear and plexiform layers to the internal limiting membrane. The other counts do not reflect the accepted laminar organisation of the retina.'),
('db114228-b4db-5957-9a48-dee3641fb710'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Cornea is avascular; it is nourished by:', '["nourished by:","Aqueous humor","tears","limbal vessels","Vitreous humor Only tears Only aqueous Blood vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Aqueous humor', 'The avascular cornea is nourished mainly by the aqueous humor bathing its deep surface and by diffusion from the limbal capillary network at its periphery. Tears supply oxygen to the outer surface but are not the principal nutrient source.'),
('33339129-9924-5db3-b831-ebab99c18dd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Cornea innervated by:', '["Maxillary (V2) resulting in decreased functional activity","Oculomotor (III) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandibular (V3) mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial (VII) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ophthalmic (V1) via long ciliary nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic (V1) via long ciliary nerves', 'The cornea is innervated by the ophthalmic division (V1) through the long ciliary nerves, branches of the nasociliary nerve. Its dense sensory supply makes the cornea highly sensitive to touch and foreign bodies. The maxillary and mandibular divisions do not supply the orbit''s contents.'),
('ec14fe14-07ba-57aa-9d09-df4bef8124d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Iris muscles:', '["Only sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","Both sphincter and ciliary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ciliary muscle only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphincter pupillae (parasympathetic) and dilator pupillae (sympathetic)","Only dilator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter pupillae (parasympathetic) and dilator pupillae (sympathetic)', 'The iris contains two antagonistic smooth muscles: the sphincter pupillae, which constricts the pupil under parasympathetic control, and the dilator pupillae, which dilates it under sympathetic control. The ciliary muscle belongs to the ciliary body, not the iris.'),
('424b4b52-250c-5226-b846-a6c3c496e29c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Sphincter pupillae supplied by:', '["Trigeminal resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic from CN III via ciliary ganglion","Glossopharyngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic from CN III via ciliary ganglion', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibres from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel in CN III and synapse in the ciliary ganglion, whose short ciliary nerves excite the sphincter pupillae to constrict the pupil. Sympathetic fibres would act on the dilator instead.'),
('38760b2c-db87-537a-b608-b2c0d7cdd9db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Dilator pupillae supplied by:', '["Sympathetic from superior cervical ganglion","Oculomotor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trigeminal mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic from superior cervical ganglion', 'Sympathetic fibres originating in the superior cervical ganglion travel along the internal carotid plexus and long ciliary nerves to reach the dilator pupillae, which enlarges the pupil. In Horner syndrome this sympathetic loss produces a small (miotic) pupil.'),
('edcb2d00-0dab-5db2-ac6b-a14d1669e5ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ciliary body functions:', '["Transmit light resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce aqueous humor and change lens shape (accommodation)","Support lens mediated by specific receptor activation","Control pupil size without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nourish retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produce aqueous humor and change lens shape (accommodation)', 'The ciliary body produces aqueous humor from its ciliary processes and contains the ciliary muscle, whose contraction slackens the zonular fibres to allow the lens to thicken during accommodation. Pupil size is controlled by the iris, and retinal nourishment is the role of the choroid.'),
('0e0f8f7f-d8c1-5cad-a6a6-264a5e74fa56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ciliary muscle supplied by:', '["Facial resulting in decreased functional activity","Trochlear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trigeminal mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic from CN III"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic from CN III', 'The ciliary muscle is innervated by parasympathetic fibres from the oculomotor nerve (CN III) that synapse in the ciliary ganglion and reach the eye via short ciliary nerves. Contraction produces accommodation for near vision, and its paralysis blocks accommodation.'),
('b9c25558-ba5b-547a-a9df-977df7258efe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lens is:', '["Biconvex","avascular","suspended by zonules from ciliary body","Rigid","Vascular Part of cornea Secreted by retina"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex', 'The lens is a transparent, avascular, biconvex structure suspended behind the iris by zonular fibres from the ciliary body. It is neither rigid, nor vascular, nor a retinal product, although several other statements about it are individually true.'),
('bae3653a-6573-59d1-a59a-317998f828dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Vitreous body:', '["Lens nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Solid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aqueous fluid mediated by specific receptor activation","Corneal layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clear gel filling posterior chamber behind lens"]'::jsonb, 'Clear gel filling posterior chamber behind lens', 'The vitreous body is a clear gel that fills the space behind the lens (vitreous chamber), helping maintain ocular shape and support the retina against the choroid. It is distinct from the watery aqueous humor and from solid or corneal structures.'),
('f33a799b-70ff-5fe0-87d3-cd54fcb52994'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Anterior chamber:', '["In vitreous resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind iris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In retina mediated by specific receptor activation","In lens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Space between cornea and iris"]'::jsonb, 'Space between cornea and iris', 'The anterior chamber is the space between the posterior surface of the cornea and the anterior surface of the iris, filled with aqueous humor. At its periphery (iridocorneal angle) lies the trabecular meshwork through which aqueous drains.'),
('65fb7908-864b-520d-b41b-fb620e5a5c17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Posterior chamber:', '["Vitreous cavity","In front of cornea","Space between iris and lens","Behind lens","In ciliary body"]'::jsonb, 'Space between iris and lens', 'The posterior chamber is the narrow aqueous-filled space between the iris and the lens (zonular border). Aqueous secreted by the ciliary processes passes from here through the pupil into the anterior chamber.'),
('e766d1f5-cb53-5fd9-acac-9395f65019f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Aqueous humor produced by:', '["Ciliary processes","Retina","Lacrimal gland","Choroid","Lens"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary processes', 'Aqueous humor is secreted by the ciliary processes, which project into the posterior chamber from the ciliary body. The retina and choroid do not secrete aqueous, and the lens and lacrimal gland are unrelated to its production.'),
('18f255c9-ba3f-5eb9-b096-c94c99e19471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Aqueous humor drains via:', '["Choroidal vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scleral veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Canal of Schlemm (trabecular meshwork)","Retinal veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Canal of Schlemm (trabecular meshwork)', 'Aqueous humor drains at the iridocorneal angle through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, then into aqueous veins and the general venous circulation. Obstruction of this outflow raises intraocular pressure and causes glaucoma.'),
('ee09226e-5aac-54d9-9681-10ffdd7af688'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Canal of Schlemm located:', '["In iris","At iridocorneal angle","In retina","In lens","In choroid"]'::jsonb, 'At iridocorneal angle', 'The canal of Schlemm encircles the eye at the iridocorneal angle, just deep to the limbus and the trabecular meshwork. From there aqueous humor reaches the venous system, so this angle is the key outflow site for aqueous.'),
('70e36d29-d8e0-5ddb-845b-363dc48761eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Glaucoma involves:', '["Lens dislocation resulting in decreased functional activity","Retinal detachment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased IOP from impaired aqueous drainage","Corneal opacity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased IOP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased IOP from impaired aqueous drainage', 'Glaucoma results from raised intraocular pressure, usually because aqueous outflow through the trabecular meshwork and canal of Schlemm is impaired. Sustained high pressure damages the optic nerve at the optic disc, causing progressive visual field loss.'),
('f4fb153f-84de-53ec-94fc-3006793384f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Optic disc (blind spot):', '["Pupil opening resulting in decreased functional activity","Where optic nerve exits; no photoreceptors","Lens attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","Macula center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sharpest vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Where optic nerve exits; no photoreceptors', 'The optic disc is the point where optic nerve fibres exit the eyeball to form the optic nerve; it contains no photoreceptors, producing a physiological blind spot. It should not be confused with the macula, which provides sharpest central vision.'),
('27582693-4f46-57a3-9fe3-a598ccac251a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Macula lutea:', '["Blind spot resulting in decreased functional activity","Oval yellow area lateral to optic disc for central vision","Ciliary body mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lens nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oval yellow area lateral to optic disc for central vision', 'The macula lutea is the oval yellowish area of retina lateral to the optic disc whose centre, the fovea, contains only cones and provides sharpest colour vision. Degeneration here impairs central vision, whereas the optic disc is the blind spot.'),
('d1387e3f-c627-5279-8fe6-a5156d63d726'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Fovea centralis:', '["Center of macula with only cones (sharpest vision)","Rod-rich area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blind spot mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lens capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Center of macula with only cones (sharpest vision)', 'The fovea centralis is the central pit of the macula containing only cones, giving the highest visual acuity. Rods dominate the peripheral retina, while the optic disc is the blind spot and the lens capsule is unrelated to sharp vision.'),
('ba232f6a-48c1-5751-9af8-9427d70757cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lacrimal gland located:', '["Superomedial orbit resulting in decreased functional activity","Superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal)","Inferomedial mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferolateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal)', 'The lacrimal gland occupies the superolateral angle of the orbit in the lacrimal fossa of the frontal bone. It is not placed medially, inferiorly, or within the nasal cavity.'),
('6cce940a-86f1-5418-ad0f-05f645739138'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lacrimal gland supplied by:', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Oculomotor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic CN VII via pterygopalatine ganglion","Trigeminal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic CN VII via pterygopalatine ganglion', 'Secretomotor fibres of the facial nerve (CN VII) reach the gland via the greater petrosal nerve and relay in the pterygopalatine ganglion before travelling with the lacrimal nerve. Sympathetic and trigeminal fibres accompany it but are not the secretomotor supply.'),
('60c385f4-86c0-50fa-8b95-808f8d098442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Tears drain through:', '["Gland directly to nose resulting in decreased functional activity","To mouth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To parotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Puncta -> canaliculi -> lacrimal sac -> nasolacrimal duct -> inferior meatus","Evaporation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Puncta -> canaliculi -> lacrimal sac -> nasolacrimal duct -> inferior meatus', 'Tears flow through the lacrimal puncta into canaliculi, then into the lacrimal sac and down the nasolacrimal duct to open in the inferior nasal meatus. They do not drain to the mouth, parotid, or directly from the gland to the nose.'),
('86255da7-f91b-530b-95e9-3172b55378bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Nasolacrimal duct opens into:', '["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens into the inferior meatus of the nose under the inferior concha. The middle and superior meatus and sphenoethmoidal recess drain paranasal sinuses, not tears.'),
('3f1fb16a-bdfc-536e-8f99-0a45e6db489e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lacrimal sac located:', '["Nasal cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","In lacrimal fossa (between lacrimal bone and maxilla)","Orbit apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Ethmoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maxillary sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In lacrimal fossa (between lacrimal bone and maxilla)', 'The lacrimal sac lies in the lacrimal fossa formed by the lacrimal bone and the frontal process of the maxilla. It is not in the nasal cavity, orbital apex, ethmoid bone, or maxillary sinus.'),
('433275af-500e-52c1-8d1b-f41a724a7c99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Eye blood supply from:', '["Ophthalmic artery (branch of internal carotid)","External carotid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle meningeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maxillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery (branch of internal carotid)', 'The eyeball receives all its arterial blood from the ophthalmic artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery. The external carotid branches and middle meningeal artery do not supply the eye.'),
('e8678afb-a56f-5ebe-996e-7dfc019527d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Central retinal artery branch of:', '["Internal carotid directly","Facial","Maxillary","Ophthalmic artery","Posterior ciliary"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery', 'The central retinal artery is the first branch of the ophthalmic artery and enters the optic nerve to supply the retina. It does not arise from the internal carotid directly, facial, maxillary, or ciliary vessels.'),
('3dc314c6-5369-53f8-a4e9-33f534b3c266'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Central retinal artery supplies:', '["Choroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Outer retina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Whole retina mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inner retina (ganglion cells, bipolar cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Inner retina (ganglion cells, bipolar cells)', 'The central retinal artery supplies the inner two-thirds of the retina, including ganglion and bipolar cells. The choroid, supplied by posterior ciliary arteries, nourishes the outer retina.'),
('9ec56560-b82a-588b-9789-e7d986fb35ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Short posterior ciliary arteries supply:', '["Retina resulting in decreased functional activity","Iris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cornea mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve head and choroid","Lens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve head and choroid', 'Short posterior ciliary arteries pierce the sclera around the optic nerve to supply the optic nerve head and choroid. The iris, cornea, lens, and retina proper have different arterial supplies.'),
('b2aa49b4-85b9-50ff-aa4a-47313614283a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Long posterior ciliary arteries supply:', '["Optic nerve","Choroid","Retina","Cornea","Iris and ciliary body"]'::jsonb, 'Iris and ciliary body', 'Long posterior ciliary arteries run anteriorly to supply the iris and ciliary body. The choroid and retina are supplied by the short posterior ciliary and central retinal arteries.'),
('da281841-36e8-5f15-82e7-dccb97b1b953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ophthalmic veins drain into:', '["External jugular","Internal jugular","Pterygoid plexus","Facial vein","Cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus', 'Ophthalmic veins drain the orbit and pass through the superior orbital fissure into the cavernous sinus. They do not drain into jugular veins, the pterygoid plexus, or the facial vein.'),
('82c81134-6411-5c62-9361-3424cdd2f4f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ophthalmic veins communicate with:', '["Internal carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial vein (no valves, allows infection spread)","Azygos without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavian leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial vein (no valves, allows infection spread)', 'The ophthalmic veins communicate with the facial vein via valveless anastomoses, so facial infections can spread into the cavernous sinus. The other vessels listed do not connect to the orbital venous system.'),
('82d6462f-177d-51b4-8bd0-8b2e6b06fa60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Eyeball nerve supply:', '["Only sympathetic","Only facial","Long ciliary (V1)","short ciliary (ciliary ganglion)","sympathetic Only oculomotor Only trigeminal"]'::jsonb, 'Long ciliary (V1)', 'Sensory innervation of the eyeball is via the long ciliary nerves, which are branches of the nasociliary nerve (V1). They carry sensation from the cornea, iris, and ciliary body.'),
('c098095b-f6b6-5c44-aaef-5ffad1d855d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ciliary ganglion located:', '["At orbital apex between optic nerve and lateral rectus","Infratemporal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pterygopalatine fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior orbit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parotid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At orbital apex between optic nerve and lateral rectus', 'The ciliary ganglion is a small parasympathetic ganglion in the posterior orbit, between the optic nerve and the lateral rectus muscle. It is not in the anterior orbit or extracranial fossae.'),
('7f825fa8-8389-5779-a1de-29673649063c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ciliary ganglion parasympathetic root from:', '["Oculomotor (CN III)","Sympathetic trunk","Nasociliary","Facial (VII)","Trigeminal (V1)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The parasympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion comes from the inferior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III) via the nerve to the inferior oblique. Sympathetic, nasociliary, facial, and trigeminal inputs are separate roots.'),
('bb8419c6-82fc-54da-ab7a-c630e22de42e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ciliary ganglion sympathetic root from:', '["Facial","Thoracic sympathetic","Trigeminal","Internal carotid plexus","Cervical sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid plexus', 'The sympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion is the sympathetic trunk fibres accompanying the internal carotid plexus. The facial nerve and trigeminal branches are not sympathetic in origin.'),
('327b7e03-ae0a-5f74-9e92-f3d3aa34534b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ciliary ganglion sensory root from:', '["Facial (VII)","Optic (II)","Nasociliary (V1)","Maxillary (V2)","Oculomotor (III)"]'::jsonb, 'Nasociliary (V1)', 'The sensory root of the ciliary ganglion is the nasociliary nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division V1. The other cranial nerves listed do not provide its sensory root.'),
('8c8058d9-58ba-5819-be79-495a6dc76122'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Short ciliary nerves carry:', '["Only sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic to sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle; sensory to cornea"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic to sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle; sensory to cornea', 'Short ciliary nerves carry preganglionic parasympathetic (from CN III) to the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle plus sensory fibres from the cornea. They do not carry sympathetic motor supply.'),
('ca044bd8-2b1c-5ea0-a901-5f5a95f521d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Long ciliary nerves carry:', '["Parasympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory to cornea/iris and sympathetic to dilator pupillae","Motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory to cornea/iris and sympathetic to dilator pupillae', 'Long ciliary nerves, direct branches of nasociliary, carry sensory fibres to the cornea and iris and sympathetic fibres to the dilator pupillae. They are not purely sensory, sympathetic, or motor.'),
('f53aed29-ceab-58d1-898d-ee0f37dae156'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'CN III palsy causes:', '["Miosis","Ptosis","exotropia (down and out) dilated pupil","loss of accommodation Ptosis only","Vertical diplopia Esotropia"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis', 'CN III palsy causes ptosis due to paralysis of levator palpebrae superioris, along with a down-and-out eye and dilated pupil. Miosis and esotropia reflect other nerve involvement.'),
('70da4834-5625-5bce-a356-87fc8506cf1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'CN IV palsy causes:', '["Lateral strabismus","Medial strabismus","Ptosis","Vertical diplopia","head tilt opposite side Mydriasis"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical diplopia', 'Superior oblique palsy (CN IV) produces vertical diplopia, worst on looking down and in. Medial or lateral strabismus and ptosis are features of other ocular motor nerve lesions.'),
('aac6a267-62f9-5d37-b36c-7b7c6db07b18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'CN VI palsy causes:', '["Ptosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Mydriasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esotropia (medial strabismus, cannot abduct)","Exotropia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertical diplopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Esotropia (medial strabismus, cannot abduct)', 'CN VI palsy paralyses the lateral rectus so the eye cannot abduct, and the unopposed medial rectus produces esotropia. Exotropia, mydriasis, and ptosis indicate other causes.'),
('c95a61cd-fde1-549e-aaf7-7e99376add0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Horner''s syndrome eye signs:', '["Mydriasis + anhidrosis","Exophthalmos","Lacrimation","Ptosis","miosis anhidrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis', 'Horner''s syndrome interrupts sympathetic supply to Muller''s muscle causing mild ptosis, with miosis and anhidrosis. Mydriasis and exophthalmos indicate opposite or unrelated pathology.'),
('f7e49ff3-5eed-5c2d-8cd4-5d2fa9a61dd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Visual pathway:', '["Retina -> pretectal resulting in decreased functional activity","Retina -> superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retina -> optic n -> chiasm -> tract -> LGN -> radiation -> area 17","Retina -> hypothalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retina -> thalamus directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retina -> optic n -> chiasm -> tract -> LGN -> radiation -> area 17', 'Retinal fibres travel through the optic nerve, chiasm, optic tract, and LGN, then via optic radiation to striate cortex (area 17). The pretectum and superior colliculus are involved in reflexes, not conscious vision.'),
('940decfe-aa50-5c85-a394-356ae4e60985'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'At optic chiasm', '["nasal retinal fibers:","Stay uncrossed","Synapse","Cross to opposite side","Go to ipsilateral LGN Go to superior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Cross to opposite side', 'At the chiasm, nasal retinal fibres cross to the opposite side while temporal fibres stay uncrossed. It is not a synapse or a point of uncrossed passage for all fibres.'),
('acb41130-2cdc-5b7e-ac9b-2dace691de5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Pituitary tumor compressing chiasm causes:', '["Homonymous hemianopia","Central scotoma","Monocular blindness","Bitemporal hemianopia","One eye blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Bitemporal hemianopia', 'A pituitary tumour compressing the central chiasm destroys crossing nasal fibres, causing loss of temporal visual fields in both eyes (bitemporal hemianopia). Homonymous deficits and scotoma reflect post-chiasmal or retinal lesions.'),
('ed6ae768-41cd-57a1-bfb8-f6381a7c0eaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Right optic tract lesion causes:', '["Left nasal hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Right eye blind"]'::jsonb, 'Left homonymous hemianopia', 'The right optic tract carries information from the left visual field, so a lesion there causes left homonymous hemianopia. Lasions of one eye or the chiasm produce different field defects.'),
('ae3c83d8-0790-5c68-a712-827db9ee5b95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Pupillary light reflex pathway:', '["CN III directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Retina -> superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short ciliary nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Retina -> pretectal nucleus -> Edinger-Westphal -> CN III -> ciliary ganglion -> sphincter","Retina -> LGN -> cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retina -> pretectal nucleus -> Edinger-Westphal -> CN III -> ciliary ganglion -> sphincter', 'Light travels from retina to the pretectal nucleus, then to Edinger-Westphal, down CN III to the ciliary ganglion, and finally to the sphincter pupillae. This reflex bypasses the LGN and cortex.'),
('9e08228b-b1f8-5e65-a2ce-f449acef3238'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Edinger-Westphal nucleus:', '["Facial nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory nucleus of V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic nucleus of CN III (pupil and accommodation)","Salivatory nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor nucleus of CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic nucleus of CN III (pupil and accommodation)', 'Edinger-Westphal is the parasympathetic nucleus of CN III controlling pupillary constriction and accommodation. The salivatory and facial nuclei serve glandular functions elsewhere.'),
('43286889-dc8a-5b9b-a8ec-1c7882d7e452'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Accommodation reflex involves:', '["Pupil dilation","Lens flattening","pupil change","Convergence","lens accommodation (ciliary muscle) miosis Eye divergence"]'::jsonb, 'Convergence', 'Accommodation consists of ciliary muscle contraction, lens thickening, and convergence of the eyes. Pupil dilation and lens flattening are opposite to this near-vision response.'),
('51c7d257-136c-5f06-be73-be30a1afb118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Optic nerve (II) is:', '["Special somatic afferent (vision)","Motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Special somatic afferent (vision)', 'The optic nerve carries special somatic afferent fibres for vision. It is not motor, mixed, sympathetic, or parasympathetic; the oculomotor nerve supplies ocular muscles.'),
('0c83a71c-8118-5a6c-948d-db4bd7fdb92a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Optic nerve covered by:', '["Only arachnoid resulting in decreased functional activity","All 3 meningeal layers (extension of brain)","meninges mediated by specific receptor activation","Only dura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All 3 meningeal layers (extension of brain)', 'As an outgrowth of the diencephalon, the optic nerve is surrounded by all three meningeal layers and cerebrospinal fluid. Other cranial nerves lack a complete meningeal covering.'),
('08da34af-7d97-5e90-ac8e-4222df0ea442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Optic disc located:', '["3-4 mm medial to fovea","Equator","Ora serrata","Fovea center","Peripheral retina"]'::jsonb, '3-4 mm medial to fovea', 'The optic disc lies 3-4 mm medial to the fovea centralis, forming the blind spot. It is not at the equator, ora serrata, or fovea centre.'),
('8719ce28-7c00-53b4-911d-4c664c45f800'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Photoreceptors:', '["Bipolar cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Only rods accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cones mediated by specific receptor activation","Ganglion cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rods (dim light) and cones (color, acuity)"]'::jsonb, 'Rods (dim light) and cones (color, acuity)', 'The retina contains two photoreceptor types: rods for dim-light vision and cones for colour and high acuity. Bipolar and ganglion cells are not photoreceptors.'),
('08c7d363-cb45-5717-8a27-7de2b0391319'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Rods concentrated in:', '["Macula","Fovea","Peripheral retina","Optic disc","Equator"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral retina', 'Rods are most numerous in the peripheral retina, providing dim-light and peripheral vision. Cones concentrate in the fovea, and there are no photoreceptors at the optic disc.'),
('077c8181-8225-5e2f-9e6b-df911b2537ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Cones concentrated in:', '["Optic disc","Fovea centralis","Ora serrata","Equator","Peripheral retina"]'::jsonb, 'Fovea centralis', 'Cones are densely packed in the fovea centralis, giving maximal visual acuity. The optic disc has no photoreceptors, and peripheral retina is rod-dominated.'),
('bf7805fa-317a-559a-a848-0b970a3774dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ora serrata:', '["Anterior retina edge where it meets ciliary body","Optic disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fovea mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iridocorneal angle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior retina edge where it meets ciliary body', 'The ora serrata is the anterior scalloped edge of the retina where it meets the ciliary body. The optic disc and fovea lie posteriorly, and the iridocorneal angle is unrelated.'),
('05e8b624-67f0-5975-b71e-1bed7886a522'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Choroid:', '["Part of cornea resulting in decreased functional activity","Inner layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of lens mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular layer between sclera and retina supplying outer retina","Outer fibrous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular layer between sclera and retina supplying outer retina', 'The choroid is the vascular layer between the sclera and retina that nourishes the outer retina. It is neither a corneal component nor the fibrous or neural layer.'),
('95115efd-27de-5aa2-85d8-d5d7f5fc42b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Sclera:', '["Vascular resulting in decreased functional activity","Inner layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uveal part mediated by specific receptor activation","Tough fibrous outer coat of eye (continuous with cornea)","Transparent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tough fibrous outer coat of eye (continuous with cornea)', 'The sclera is the tough, fibrous outer coat of the eye continuous with the cornea. The vascular and uveal layers and transparent cornea do not share its structure and function.'),
('c422fc08-0945-52e2-b073-1f103d7192ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Conjunctiva:', '["Vascular layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Thin mucous membrane covering anterior sclera and inner eyelids","Muscle layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Nerve layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corneal layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin mucous membrane covering anterior sclera and inner eyelids', 'The conjunctiva is a thin mucous membrane lining the inner eyelids and covering the anterior sclera. It is not vascular, muscular, neural, or corneal tissue.'),
('338d670a-b397-5cfd-b51b-96b0420c65c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Palpebral conjunctiva:', '["Lacrimal sac resulting in decreased functional activity","Cornea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior eyeball mediated by specific receptor activation","Lines inner surface of eyelids","Fornix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lines inner surface of eyelids', 'Palpebral conjunctiva lines the inner surface of the eyelids. The bulbar conjunctiva covers the sclera of the eyeball, and the optic nerve and lens are deep structures.'),
('91fbfa81-edbd-5b8d-a8ad-bcd592824daf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Bulbar conjunctiva:', '["Covers anterior sclera (not cornea)","Optic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lens mediated by specific receptor activation","Cornea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior sclera leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers anterior sclera (not cornea)', 'Bulbar conjunctiva covers the anterior sclera but stops at the corneal limbus, so it does not cover the cornea. It does not cover the posterior sclera or deep ocular structures.'),
('5b23e4fe-8470-59b3-8d4a-2f62eea10579'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Conjunctival fornix:', '["Plica semilunaris resulting in decreased functional activity","Punctum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corneal margin mediated by specific receptor activation","Caruncle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fold where palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet"]'::jsonb, 'Fold where palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet', 'The conjunctival fornix is the fold where palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet, superiorly and inferiorly. The plica semilunaris, punctum, and caruncle are separate medial structures.'),
('f9d15a0e-ea46-5e9e-bcdd-4d26a62e3192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Plica semilunaris:', '["Caruncle resulting in decreased functional activity","Punctum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tarsal plate mediated by specific receptor activation","Semilunar fold at medial canthus (vestigial third eyelid)","Lateral canthus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Semilunar fold at medial canthus (vestigial third eyelid)', 'The plica semilunaris is a semilunar fold in the medial canthus, a vestigial remnant of the nictitating membrane. The caruncle, punctum, and tarsal plate are distinct structures.'),
('a09342d2-5f7a-5e12-a103-cef3116c5bd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Lacrimal caruncle:', '["Reddish elevation at medial canthus with modified sweat and sebaceous glands","Nasolacrimal duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Punctum mediated by specific receptor activation","Lacrimal sac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lacrimal gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reddish elevation at medial canthus with modified sweat and sebaceous glands', 'The lacrimal caruncle is a reddish elevation at the medial canthus containing modified sebaceous and sweat glands. The puncta, lacrimal sac, and nasolacrimal duct form the drainage pathway.'),
('8b967b9f-a23e-525c-ba02-9d88b8709386'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Tarsal plates:', '["Glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dense connective tissue sheets giving form to eyelids","Cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orbital bones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense connective tissue sheets giving form to eyelids', 'Tarsal plates are dense connective tissue sheets that give the eyelids their shape and support. They are not glands, muscles, cartilage, or bone.'),
('d5c8bacc-d624-5a52-b66e-c76e45e80191'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Tarsal (Meibomian) glands:', '["Modified sebaceous glands secreting lipid layer of tear film","Salivary glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sweat glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Lacrimal glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucous glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modified sebaceous glands secreting lipid layer of tear film', 'Tarsal (Meibomian) glands are modified sebaceous glands that secrete the oily lipid layer of the tear film, reducing evaporation. They differ from sweat, salivary, and lacrimal glands.'),
('59e27412-e34f-5e01-8a6c-cf4cc6f58417'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Palpebral orbicularis oculi:', '["Depresses eyebrows","Gently closes eyelids (blinking)","Elevates eyebrows","Opens eyes","Forcefully closes eyes"]'::jsonb, 'Gently closes eyelids (blinking)', 'Palpebral orbicularis oculi fibres gently close the eyelids during blinking. Forced squeezing is the function of the orbital part, while levator palpebrae opens the eye.'),
('c68f41bc-6041-5726-9af1-df2ed3977747'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Orbital orbicularis oculi:', '["Forcefully closes eyes (squeezing)","Opens eye","Gently blinks","Elevates eyebrow","Moves eyelid laterally"]'::jsonb, 'Forcefully closes eyes (squeezing)', 'Orbital orbicularis oculi fibres contract forcefully to squeeze the eyes shut, protecting them. Gently blinking is the role of the palpebral part of the muscle.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('6acc74d5-e0f4-57d3-92d0-6c1c7b3d9677'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Muller''s muscle:', '["Smooth muscle innervated by sympathetics; assists eyelid elevation","Skin fold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of levator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle innervated by sympathetics; assists eyelid elevation', 'Muller''s muscle is the superior tarsal smooth muscle raised by sympathetic fibers, which assists in elevating the upper eyelid. The levator palpebrae superioris is the skeletal elevator; it is not a skin fold, gland, or part of the levator proper.'),
('20884d52-01db-59f8-aa79-863ee10819b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Orbital septum:', '["Nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Connective tissue membrane from tarsal plates to orbital margins","Gland mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissue membrane from tarsal plates to orbital margins', 'The orbital septum is a fibrous connective tissue membrane stretching from the tarsal plates to the orbital margins, sealing the orbital contents. It is neither nervous, glandular, osseous, nor muscular tissue.'),
('6c242afa-9cd3-5143-81eb-fd9317108d29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Common extensor origin (annulus of Zinn):', '["Insertion of obliques","Pulley of superior oblique","Optic canal ring","Origin of rectus muscles at orbital apex","Lacrimal sac"]'::jsonb, 'Origin of rectus muscles at orbital apex', 'The common tendinous ring (annulus of Zinn) is the fibrous ring at the orbital apex from which the rectus muscles originate. The oblique muscles arise elsewhere, the superior oblique uses a trochlear pulley, and the lacrimal sac lies in the medial wall.'),
('f58a63e7-7144-566b-a577-1e1943a43667'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Superior ophthalmic vein drains to:', '["IJV","Facial vein","EJV","Pterygoid plexus","Cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus', 'The superior ophthalmic vein drains the orbit and passes through the superior orbital fissure into the cavernous sinus, a route that can carry facial infections intracranially. It does not drain into the jugular, facial, or external jugular veins or the pterygoid plexus.'),
('6f4b0d8b-0b6d-5e2a-a6e3-2a39c88f1a3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Infraorbital groove located in:', '["Maxilla (orbital floor)","Ethmoid (medial)","Zygomatic (lateral)","Sphenoid (apex)","Frontal (roof)"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla (orbital floor)', 'The infraorbital groove and canal run within the orbital surface of the maxilla, which forms most of the orbital floor, and they transmit the infraorbital nerve and vessels. The ethmoid, zygomatic, sphenoid, and frontal bones belong to other orbital walls.'),
('c00f861c-599a-59ce-8397-7d7ae61dd9fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which extraocular muscle rotates the eyeball medially (adducts)?', '["Superior oblique (CN IV)","Superior rectus (CN III)","Medial rectus (CN III)","Lateral rectus (CN VI)","Inferior oblique (CN III)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial rectus (CN III)', 'The medial rectus pulls the eyeball medially (adduction) and is innervated by CN III. The superior and inferior obliques rotate the eye, and the lateral rectus abducts it.'),
('ca756459-9ec0-5fc5-b6c3-0400b8f5a0ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The bony orbit is formed by how many bones?', '["6 bones","7 bones","5 bones","4 bones","8 bones"]'::jsonb, '7 bones', 'The bony orbit is formed by seven bones: frontal, zygomatic, maxilla, sphenoid, ethmoid, lacrimal, and palatine. Any other count is anatomically incorrect.'),
('a813a3b7-cfb1-5b51-b1b6-729cb912623b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which bone forms the roof of the orbit?', '["Maxilla","Sphenoid bone","Ethmoid bone","Frontal bone (orbital plate)","Zygomatic bone"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal bone (orbital plate)', 'The orbital roof is formed mainly by the orbital plate of the frontal bone, with a small contribution from the lesser wing of the sphenoid. The other bones listed contribute to other orbital walls.'),
('b61e2e37-100d-5383-9be0-485842777820'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The lateral wall of the orbit is formed by:', '["Lacrimal and ethmoid","Zygomatic bone and greater wing of sphenoid","Palatine and sphenoid","Frontal and maxilla","Maxilla and ethmoid"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic bone and greater wing of sphenoid', 'The lateral orbital wall is formed by the zygomatic bone and the greater wing of the sphenoid. The lacrimal and ethmoid bones make up the medial wall, while the frontal and maxilla form parts of the roof and floor.'),
('3b0bdc4a-6d68-5609-934c-4e36ed0f99ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The floor of the orbit is formed by:', '["Frontal and sphenoid","Ethmoid and lacrimal","Maxilla (orbital surface)","zygomatic","palatine"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla (orbital surface)', 'The orbital floor is formed chiefly by the orbital surface of the maxilla, with small contributions from the zygomatic and palatine bones. The frontal, sphenoid, ethmoid, and lacrimal bones belong to other walls.'),
('c063fcbb-7e9c-5af8-9db5-8fac9c8590b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The medial wall of the orbit is formed by:', '["Vomer and ethmoid","Ethmoid (lamina papyracea)","lacrimal","maxilla","sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'lacrimal', 'The medial orbital wall is a thin plate assembled from the maxilla, lacrimal, ethmoid, and sphenoid bones; the lacrimal bone occupies its anterior portion and houses the lacrimal sac fossa. The lamina papyracea of the ethmoid also contributes, but the lacrimal bone is a genuine component of this wall.'),
('3c50f61d-b8b1-53f3-aae8-34619e245d5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic foramen (canal) transmits:', '["Ophthalmic artery only","Optic nerve only","Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery","CN II and ophthalmic vein","CN II"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery', 'The optic canal transmits the optic nerve (CN II) and the ophthalmic artery into the orbit. The ophthalmic vein leaves through the superior orbital fissure, not through this canal.'),
('8d47f346-a9c6-5c98-bcfc-dbd1cb9ad141'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The inferior orbital fissure transmits:', '["Optic nerve","Infraorbital nerve (V2)","zygomatic nerve","infraorbital vessels","Ophthalmic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Infraorbital nerve (V2)', 'The inferior orbital fissure transmits the infraorbital nerve (a branch of V2), infraorbital vessels, and the zygomatic nerve. The optic nerve and ophthalmic artery travel through the optic canal instead.'),
('095825f7-41ea-5726-af5d-1c34f499983f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The nasolacrimal canal drains into:', '["Middle meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Maxillary sinus","Inferior meatus of nose","Superior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus of nose', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the inferior nasal concha into the inferior meatus of the nose. The middle and superior meatuses and the sphenoethmoidal recess receive other sinus and nasal openings.'),
('fccdf499-7233-5669-9529-5e3f277a89a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The lacrimal gland is located:', '["In the inferomedial orbit resulting in decreased functional activity","In the superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal bone)","In the nasal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","In the superomedial orbit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the inferolateral orbit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal bone)', 'The lacrimal gland occupies the lacrimal fossa of the frontal bone in the superolateral orbit. None of the other orbital quadrants house the gland.'),
('99bfe16a-7484-5b9b-b3f1-f2ea309087f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Tears drain from the eye via:', '["Canaliculi to maxillary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Puncta to nasolacrimal duct directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lacrimal sac to ethmoid sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Puncta to sphenoid sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Puncta to canaliculi to lacrimal sac to nasolacrimal duct to inferior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Puncta to canaliculi to lacrimal sac to nasolacrimal duct to inferior meatus', 'Tears flow from the lacrimal puncta into the canaliculi, then into the lacrimal sac and down the nasolacrimal duct to open at the inferior meatus. The other routes end in a sinus and are wrong.'),
('8b4e7301-070b-5247-87de-8b2f7ecf56e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The common tendinous ring (annulus of Zinn) is:', '["The insertion of the oblique muscles","The origin of the rectus muscles at the apex of the orbit","The pulley for superior oblique","The optic disc","The attachment of levator"]'::jsonb, 'The origin of the rectus muscles at the apex of the orbit', 'The common tendinous ring (annulus of Zinn) is the fibrous origin of the rectus muscles at the orbital apex. The oblique muscles originate elsewhere, the superior oblique uses the trochlea as a pulley, and the optic disc is a retinal structure.'),
('091cde6f-61cd-58ee-9e0e-e7ceb4510400'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The superior oblique muscle is innervated by:', '["Facial nerve (VII)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlear nerve (CN IV)', 'The superior oblique is innervated by the trochlear nerve (CN IV). CN III supplies the remaining extraocular muscles except the lateral rectus, which is served by CN VI; CN V and VII are not ocular motor nerves here.'),
('9a7d92e6-6397-52be-9a55-46a4042ad4b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The lateral rectus muscle is innervated by:', '["CN III resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abducens nerve (CN VI)","CN V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abducens nerve (CN VI)', 'The lateral rectus abducts the eye and is innervated by the abducens nerve (CN VI). CN III, IV, V, and VII carry other functions.'),
('94e066f3-a4c6-56cc-a9ea-f4d205ec4918'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'All other extraocular muscles (except SO and LR) are innervated by:', '["Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","CN V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III)', 'Except for the superior oblique (CN IV) and the lateral rectus (CN VI), all extraocular muscles are supplied by the oculomotor nerve (CN III). The other cranial nerves listed play no role here.'),
('b063e43f-f2cb-5c30-919b-b3a59aedfff1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The levator palpebrae superioris is innervated by:', '["Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Facial nerve (VII)","Sympathetic fibers","Trochlear nerve (IV)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III)', 'The levator palpebrae superioris is a skeletal muscle innervated by the oculomotor nerve (CN III) that raises the upper eyelid. Sympathetic fibers supply only its smooth superior tarsal (Muller''s) component.'),
('f94fb3d9-15ab-5d1a-8536-2b2e6b74eed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Ptosis can result from:', '["Facial nerve palsy","CN IV palsy","CN III palsy","Horner''s syndrome","or myasthenia gravis"]'::jsonb, 'CN III palsy', 'A CN III palsy paralyzes the levator palpebrae superioris, producing ptosis together with a down-and-out eye and mydriasis. Horner''s syndrome and myasthenia gravis can also lower the lid, but CN III palsy is the classic cause of ptosis among these choices.'),
('794170d6-77c3-5879-adc9-52b211d18688'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The ophthalmic artery is a branch of:', '["Middle meningeal artery","External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery","Maxillary artery","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid artery', 'The ophthalmic artery is the first major intracranial branch of the internal carotid artery and enters the orbit through the optic canal. The external carotid, maxillary, middle meningeal, and facial arteries do not give rise to it.'),
('b0478ef0-afaf-54df-b571-2dd1a9c14a41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The central retinal artery is a branch of:', '["Maxillary artery","Internal carotid directly","Facial artery","Posterior ciliary artery","Ophthalmic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery', 'The central retinal artery, which supplies the inner retina, is the first branch of the ophthalmic artery and travels within the optic nerve. It is not a direct branch of the internal carotid or of the facial or maxillary arteries.'),
('b02dc93d-ea2e-514c-941b-fbabc88f7e15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The ciliary body functions to:', '["Drain aqueous humor resulting in decreased functional activity","Nourish the cornea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transmit visual signals mediated by specific receptor activation","Focus light on retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce aqueous humor and change lens shape (accommodation)"]'::jsonb, 'Produce aqueous humor and change lens shape (accommodation)', 'The ciliary body produces aqueous humor and contains the ciliary muscle that changes lens curvature during accommodation. Draining aqueous humor and nourishing the cornea are handled by other structures.'),
('32784c54-287b-5c55-9cec-1560b2c382ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Aqueous humor drains primarily via:', '["Lens to cornea resulting in decreased functional activity","Trabecular meshwork to canal of Schlemm to aqueous veins to episcleral veins","Ciliary body to retina mediated by specific receptor activation","Pupil to vitreous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trabecular meshwork to canal of Schlemm to aqueous veins to episcleral veins', 'Aqueous humor drains from the anterior chamber through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, then through aqueous veins into episcleral veins. Blockage of this pathway raises intraocular pressure.'),
('84696782-41da-599b-8442-715e7e19c646'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The cornea is avascular and receives nutrition from:', '["Optic nerve","Tears and aqueous humor","Blood vessels in sclera","Conjunctival vessels","Vitreous humor"]'::jsonb, 'Tears and aqueous humor', 'The cornea is avascular and receives oxygen and nutrients from the tear film and the aqueous humor. Scleral vessels, conjunctival vessels, and vitreous humor are not its source of nutrition.'),
('22022284-ae7c-5914-920d-b7162bb20fa8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic disc (blind spot) is:', '["The center of the macula","The lens attachment","The point where the optic nerve exits the retina","with photoreceptors","The area of sharpest vision"]'::jsonb, 'The point where the optic nerve exits the retina', 'The optic disc is the point where retinal ganglion cell axons converge and leave the eye as the optic nerve; it contains no photoreceptors, creating the blind spot. The fovea is the site of sharpest vision.'),
('66a8a0f5-b646-5bbe-b5e0-351a5f1fdc27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The macula lutea is:', '["The blind spot resulting in decreased functional activity","The ora serrata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The peripheral retina mediated by specific receptor activation","The area of retina responsible for central vision (contains fovea)","The ciliary body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area of retina responsible for central vision (contains fovea)', 'The macula lutea is the central retinal region responsible for detailed central vision and contains the fovea. The blind spot is the optic disc, and the ora serrata and ciliary body are separate structures.'),
('271f5099-85e1-5ca8-b765-a0ffa62dda7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The fovea centralis contains:', '["Mixed rods and cones","Only cones (highest visual acuity)","Only rods","Ganglion cells only","photoreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Only cones (highest visual acuity)', 'The fovea centralis contains almost exclusively cones, providing the highest visual acuity. Rods predominate in the peripheral retina, and the fovea is not made up of mixed photoreceptors or solely ganglion cells.'),
('f77429f0-a191-50d3-9d2b-3119157c09e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The pupillary light reflex pathway involves:', '["Trigeminal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic nerve (II) to pretectal nucleus to Edinger-Westphal nucleus to CN III to ciliary ganglion to sphincter pupillae","Facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Directly to the iris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (II) to pretectal nucleus to Edinger-Westphal nucleus to CN III to ciliary ganglion to sphincter pupillae', 'In the pupillary light reflex, light travels via CN II to the pretectal nucleus, then to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus; its parasympathetic fibers run in CN III to the ciliary ganglion and synapse on the sphincter pupillae. The other nerves listed are not part of this arc.'),
('24a91207-9850-59e1-bd3e-d972ecc032e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Horner''s syndrome includes:', '["Ptosis","mydriasis","anhidrosis","B but not A","Rarely"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis', 'Horner''s syndrome results from sympathetic loss and classically includes ptosis (mild), miosis, and anhidrosis. Mydriasis is wrong because the pupil constricts, and the remaining choices are not features of the syndrome.'),
('e5247909-22a9-5cc4-aa14-6a3e5c29d043'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The trigeminal nerve (V) provides sensation to the eye via:', '["All three divisions","Maxillary division (V2)","Ophthalmic division (V1)","None of the above","Mandibular division (V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic division (V1)', 'The eye receives sensory innervation from the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve. The maxillary (V2) and mandibular (V3) divisions supply other facial regions, not the eyeball.'),
('50f880cb-2c70-5c64-ac93-fd138c0decd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The nasociliary nerve (V1 branch) supplies:', '["The mandible","The lacrimal gland","The cheek","The forehead","The eyeball"]'::jsonb, 'The eyeball', 'The nasociliary nerve, a branch of V1, supplies the eyeball, including the cornea, iris, and ciliary body, and gives off the long ciliary nerves. The mandible, cheek, forehead, and lacrimal gland are supplied by other nerves.'),
('2fa309f0-0b79-5d7b-a721-89c5cfef5029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The short ciliary nerves arise from:', '["Ciliary ganglion","Pterygopalatine ganglion","Submandibular ganglion","Otic ganglion","Geniculate ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary ganglion', 'The short ciliary nerves arise from the ciliary ganglion and carry parasympathetic fibers to the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. The pterygopalatine, submandibular, otic, and geniculate ganglia serve other territories.'),
('fa9e63c2-f1da-5fd3-b1ce-85668b8d43b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The long ciliary nerves arise from:', '["Ciliary ganglion","Nasociliary nerve (V1)","Oculomotor nerve","Trigeminal ganglion","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nasociliary nerve (V1)', 'The long ciliary nerves arise directly from the nasociliary nerve (V1) and carry sympathetic fibers to the dilator pupillae plus sensory fibers to the eyeball. The ciliary ganglion gives rise to the short ciliary nerves.'),
('86b4df77-6419-5bfa-b26c-2a959bc26468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Blow-out fracture of the orbit most commonly involves:', '["Orbital roof (frontal)","Orbital floor (maxilla)","Lateral wall (zygomatic)","Zygomatic arch","Medial wall (ethmoid)"]'::jsonb, 'Orbital floor (maxilla)', 'A blow-out fracture follows a blow to the eye that raises intraorbital pressure and ruptures the thinnest wall, the orbital floor of the maxilla, often trapping the inferior rectus. The roof, lateral wall, and zygomatic arch are rarely involved.'),
('8e7d0c35-488c-5f93-9a4c-e271f31dba89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The annulus of Zinn gives origin to:', '["Superior oblique only","Levator palpebrae only","Lateral rectus only","All four rectus muscles","Inferior oblique only"]'::jsonb, 'All four rectus muscles', 'The annulus of Zinn is the common tendinous ring at the orbital apex that gives origin to all four rectus muscles. The obliques arise outside the ring, and the levator originates from the orbital roof.'),
('ee7328c9-c89f-5688-b995-5ce46f91447a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The superior orbital fissure transmits:', '["CN VII and VIII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX, X, XI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN II only mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V2 and V3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III, IV, V1, VI, ophthalmic veins"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, IV, V1, VI, ophthalmic veins', 'The superior orbital fissure transmits CN III, IV, V1, and VI together with the ophthalmic veins. The optic nerve and ophthalmic artery pass through the optic canal, and the other cranial nerves listed are not involved.'),
('a62b4dd0-6554-5d93-845b-4012c3c73aeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The eyeball is protected by:', '["Conjunctiva only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only bony orbit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eyelids and lashes mediated by specific receptor activation","Lacrimal gland only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bony orbit, eyelids, conjunctiva, lacrimal apparatus"]'::jsonb, 'Bony orbit, eyelids, conjunctiva, lacrimal apparatus', 'The eyeball is protected by the bony orbit, eyelids, conjunctiva, and lacrimal apparatus acting together. Any single structure listed alone gives only partial protection.'),
('dbe73ae2-8efc-5052-8156-86c11d576884'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The lens of the eye focuses light by:', '["Rotating around its axis","Changing its refractive index","Changing its shape (accommodation)","Splitting into multiple layers","Moving forward and backward"]'::jsonb, 'Changing its shape (accommodation)', 'The lens changes its curvature to focus light for near and far vision, a process called accommodation. Rotation, changes in refractive index, layering, or translocation do not describe lens focusing.'),
('6a2d1031-aa9b-5e7f-bbb7-ca3406a1d6f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The retina contains:', '["Ganglion cells only","Only cones","Only rods","Photoreceptors (rods and cones)","Bipolar cells only"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptors (rods and cones)', 'The retina contains photoreceptors, namely rods for dim-light vision and cones for color and acuity, along with bipolar and ganglion cells. The other options omit the full photoreceptor population.'),
('613120b9-c2d1-5bfc-afff-27815ac6a4a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic nerve (CN II) is formed by:', '["Axons of photoreceptors","Axons of bipolar cells","Axons of horizontal cells","Axons of amacrine cells","Axons of retinal ganglion cells"]'::jsonb, 'Axons of retinal ganglion cells', 'The optic nerve (CN II) is formed by the axons of retinal ganglion cells that converge at the optic disc. Photoreceptors and bipolar cells synapse within the retina before the ganglion cell layer.'),
('c46866a3-72b1-5f0d-a3ea-346eb13733f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The vitreous humor is located:', '["Around the optic nerve","In the posterior chamber","In the anterior chamber","Between lens and retina","Between cornea and lens"]'::jsonb, 'Between lens and retina', 'The vitreous humor fills the chamber between the posterior surface of the lens and the retina, helping maintain ocular shape. The anterior and posterior chambers lie in front of the lens.'),
('46203308-97e9-544b-8d7b-740f972fd0de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which option correctly describes the annulus of Zinn?', '["Pulley for superior oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimal sac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles","Optic canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trochlea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles', 'The annulus of Zinn is the common tendinous ring at the orbital apex from which the four rectus muscles arise. The trochlea is the pulley for the superior oblique, and the lacrimal sac lies in the medial orbital wall.'),
('620556d0-1310-5957-900a-d6118fe28a2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The annulus of Zinn is a .', '["Pulley for superior oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimal sac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles","Optic canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trochlea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles', 'The annulus of Zinn is the common tendinous ring at the orbital apex that anchors the rectus muscles. The optic canal transmits the optic nerve, the trochlea is the superior oblique pulley, and the lacrimal sac is a drainage structure.'),
('e0ab0930-2a79-54ad-9ba2-bcfabc71be82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The annulus of Zinn is:', '["Pulley for superior oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimal sac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles","Optic canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trochlea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common tendinous ring at orbital apex for rectus muscles', 'The annulus of Zinn is the common tendinous ring at the orbital apex that gives origin to the rectus muscles. The trochlea, optic canal, and lacrimal sac are distinct structures with different functions.'),
('6384052f-6fc5-5ff5-8ce2-c9edf943a9a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Orbit Eyeball: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('ce3ea22e-80d3-59cb-915e-e9fce3c42451'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Orbit Eyeball: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('e424edca-de88-5d16-8781-eeb2f617c6fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During therapeutic management of Orbit Eyeball, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('de9eec49-6d66-57f1-a53b-833018dbfa45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which option correctly describes the lens?', '["Biconvex","avascular","suspended by zonules from ciliary body","Rigid","Vascular Part of cornea Secreted by retina"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex', 'The crystalline lens is a transparent, biconvex structure, curved on both surfaces, that focuses light onto the retina. It is avascular and held by zonular fibers, but these are supportive attributes rather than its defining shape; the lens is pliable, not rigid, and receives no blood vessels.'),
('a89303af-9bed-5d68-8684-42bcfa893ae2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The lens is:', '["Biconvex","avascular","suspended by zonules from ciliary body","Rigid","Vascular Part of cornea Secreted by retina"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex', 'A biconvex lens is curved on both anterior and posterior surfaces, the shape that lets the crystalline lens converge light toward the retina. It is indeed avascular and zone-suspended, but it is soft and flexible rather than rigid and contains no vascular tissue.'),
('85621040-2469-508c-b037-a4d28d17d57a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement about the orbit and eyeball is correct? The lens is:', '["Biconvex","avascular","suspended by zonules from ciliary body","Rigid","Vascular Part of cornea Secreted by retina"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex', 'The lens has a biconvex contour on both surfaces, giving it its refractive power for focusing light. Avascularity and zonular anchorage are real but secondary details, and the lens is elastic and non-rigid and never has a blood supply.'),
('898960dc-a31a-5a63-afbb-62f5ce408bc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For the orbit and eyeball, identify the correct statement: the lens is:', '["Biconvex","avascular","suspended by zonules from ciliary body","Rigid","Vascular Part of cornea Secreted by retina"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex', 'Biconvex is the structural description of the crystalline lens, with both surfaces convex to refract light. The lens is avascular and suspended by zonules but is soft and pliable, so it is never rigid or vascular.'),
('ad69b98d-9695-5612-8425-a87a718b745b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During therapeutic management of Orbit Eyeball, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('1054ff03-a9f7-51db-a89f-ab9bbd8cc465'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which response correctly describes the lens?', '["Biconvex","avascular","suspended by zonules from ciliary body","Rigid","Vascular Part of cornea Secreted by retina"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex', 'The hallmark shape of the crystalline lens is biconvex, allowing it to focus incoming light onto the retina. It is avascular and anchored by zonular fibers yet remains flexible, so the rigid and vascular options are inaccurate.'),
('4c48028c-c045-588f-8cc9-4db8ee282717'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which option correctly describes the optic nerve (CN II)?', '["Special somatic afferent (vision)","Motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Special somatic afferent (vision)', 'The optic nerve (CN II) carries visual impulses from the retina and is classed as a special somatic afferent (SSA) nerve. It transmits no motor, mixed, sympathetic, or parasympathetic fibers.'),
('05f3d58e-9398-5e97-8dbb-bb311bcdaafd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic nerve (CN II) is:', '["Special somatic afferent (vision)","Motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Special somatic afferent (vision)', 'CN II conveys afferent visual signals from the retina, a special somatic afferent function. Because it is purely sensory for vision, it has no motor, mixed, sympathetic, or parasympathetic component.'),
('bf57ac7c-0eb9-5ff0-9fcb-5b7ccadc0426'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement about the orbit and eyeball is correct? The optic nerve (CN II) is:', '["Special somatic afferent (vision)","Motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Special somatic afferent (vision)', 'The optic nerve transmits vision to the brain as a special somatic afferent pathway. Being purely a sensory nerve of sight, it lacks motor or mixed functions and carries no visceral autonomic fibers.'),
('27ea0ac9-70a5-5a00-96c6-013dab4a9c04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For the orbit and eyeball, identify the correct statement: the optic nerve (CN II) is:', '["Special somatic afferent (vision)","Motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Special somatic afferent (vision)', 'Vision is served by the optic nerve, a special somatic afferent. As a purely afferent sensory nerve, it does not supply muscles or glands and carries neither sympathetic nor parasympathetic fibers.'),
('20e43d8d-c943-57d1-a2a2-f566c0a282e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The orbital apex is directed:', '["Posteromedially","Anteriorly","Laterally","Superiorly","Inferiorly"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedially', 'The orbit is a pyramidal cavity whose apex points posteriorly and medially toward the middle cranial fossa, widening anteriorly. An anterior, lateral, superior, or inferior direction would describe other walls or openings, not the apex.'),
('d4d94791-7d0f-5faa-85a8-b12bb8c38b3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In the study of Orbit Eyeball, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('29e32b19-2902-509c-99ff-052f9a516329'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The orbital roof is formed by:', '["Zygomatic resulting in decreased functional activity","Ethmoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal bone orbital plate and lesser wing of sphenoid","Only frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sphenoid only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal bone orbital plate and lesser wing of sphenoid', 'The orbital roof is formed anteriorly by the orbital plate of the frontal bone and posteriorly by the lesser wing of the sphenoid. The zygomatic bone builds the lateral wall, and the ethmoid belongs to the medial wall, so single-bone options are incomplete.'),
('03f2aafd-3606-5ac1-b534-cafa35e751e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The orbital floor is formed by:', '["Only maxilla","Only zygomatic","Maxilla + ethmoid","Maxilla","zygomatic palatine Palatine + sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla', 'The orbital floor is dominated by the orbital surface of the maxilla, supplemented by the zygomatic bone anterolaterally and the palatine bone posteriorly. The maxilla is the principal contributor, whereas ethmoid belongs to the medial wall and the sphenoid is not part of the floor.'),
('91063eef-852e-5803-a2ff-684a156794b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The lateral orbital wall is formed by:', '["Maxilla only","Palatine + maxilla","Frontal + sphenoid","Zygomatic + greater wing of sphenoid","Ethmoid + lacrimal"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic + greater wing of sphenoid', 'The lateral orbital wall is formed by the zygomatic bone anteriorly and the greater wing of the sphenoid posteriorly. Maxilla and palatine form parts of the floor, the frontal bone forms the roof, and ethmoid and lacrimal bones form the medial wall.'),
('175f0f75-ef70-5b8e-b566-04c45f85c708'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The medial orbital wall is formed by:', '["Ethmoid + maxilla","Only lacrimal","Sphenoid + frontal Only ethmoid","Ethmoid (lamina papyracea) lacrimal","maxilla sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Ethmoid (lamina papyracea) lacrimal', 'The medial orbital wall is built from the maxilla, lacrimal bone, ethmoid (with its thin lamina papyracea), and part of the sphenoid; the lamina papyracea is its largest, most fragile component. A single bone or a sphenoid-frontal combination leaves out the ethmoid and lacrimal contributions.'),
('74e0a822-e1b8-5498-a605-7367ba66a04a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic canal transmits:', '["CN II + opthalmic vein","Optic nerve only","Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery","Ophthalmic artery only","CN II + III"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery', 'The optic canal in the lesser wing of the sphenoid transmits the optic nerve (CN II) and the ophthalmic artery. The ophthalmic vein leaves the orbit through the superior orbital fissure, and CN III also exits via that fissure rather than the optic canal.'),
('9b70cf3f-6536-5ba8-96b2-8ba7e4f7707f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic canal is located in which bone?', '["Frontal","Lesser wing of sphenoid","Greater wing of sphenoid","Ethmoid","Maxilla"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser wing of sphenoid', 'The optic canal passes through the root of the lesser wing of the sphenoid, connecting the orbit with the middle cranial fossa. The frontal, greater wing of sphenoid, ethmoid, and maxilla do not contain this canal.'),
('ffa84490-0b38-5670-8d85-7d95b1b12803'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Orbit Eyeball: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('49a4d4aa-6f4b-55b8-ac32-5a8c5f5f061b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The superior orbital fissure is located between:', '["Maxilla + zygomatic","Lacrimal + ethmoid","Sphenoid + ethmoid","Frontal + sphenoid","Greater and lesser wings of sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser wings of sphenoid', 'The superior orbital fissure is the cleft between the greater and lesser wings of the sphenoid. The maxilla-zygomatic junction borders the orbital floor, and the lacrimal-ethmoid and frontosphenoid junctions relate to the medial wall and roof, not this fissure.'),
('5b23d643-35ee-52db-9ae1-65fc51d9adce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding clinical cases in Orbit Eyeball: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('e2539971-ed58-5b88-9fc3-838ab7b2fdb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The inferior orbital fissure is located between:', '["Sphenoid + ethmoid","Maxilla and greater wing of sphenoid","Frontal + sphenoid","Zygomatic and maxilla","Lacrimal + maxilla"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla and greater wing of sphenoid', 'The inferior orbital fissure lies between the maxilla and the greater wing of the sphenoid along the orbital floor. The sphenoethmoidal and frontosphenoid junctions border the roof and medial wall, and the zygomaticomaxillary suture is a different orbital landmark.'),
('03a72061-7379-5303-9ff2-5ce42d171b2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The extraocular muscles include:', '["5 muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","4 recti (SR, IR, MR, LR) + 2 obliques (SO, IO)","6 recti mediated by specific receptor activation","4 recti + 1 oblique without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 obliques leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 recti (SR, IR, MR, LR) + 2 obliques (SO, IO)', 'There are six extraocular muscles: four recti (superior, inferior, medial, lateral) and two obliques (superior and inferior). A total of five muscles, six recti, one oblique, or four obliques does not match the true complement.'),
('24bfe908-7446-5c22-baae-75f28fee8ddf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The lateral rectus muscle is supplied by:', '["Oculomotor (III)","Trochlear (IV)","Trigeminal (V)","Abducens (CN VI)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Abducens (CN VI)', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) innervates the lateral rectus, the only extraocular muscle it supplies. CN III serves the other recti and inferior oblique, CN IV serves the superior oblique, and the trigeminal and facial nerves provide no motor supply to these muscles.'),
('d9c3dc53-9fe7-5c03-ad84-dcd72fd08d52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The superior oblique muscle is supplied by:', '["Facial (VII)","Trochlear (CN IV)","Abducens (VI)","Trigeminal (V)","Oculomotor (III)"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlear (CN IV)', 'The trochlear nerve (CN IV) innervates the superior oblique, the sole muscle it supplies. CN III, CN VI, trigeminal, and facial nerves do not innervate the superior oblique.'),
('5a44b922-5fcd-588e-876e-6ea9ff7f4653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The superior rectus muscle is supplied by:', '["Trochlear (IV)","Abducens (VI)","Trigeminal (V)","Facial (VII)","Oculomotor (CN III)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The superior rectus is innervated by the superior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III). Trochlear, abducens, trigeminal, and facial nerves have other targets and do not supply this muscle.'),
('6176ba4f-1f68-5320-9618-29d48cfbb3ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The inferior rectus muscle is supplied by:', '["Facial (VII)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Trochlear (IV)","Trigeminal (V)","Abducens (VI)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The inferior rectus is innervated by the inferior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III). Trochlear and abducens nerves supply the superior oblique and lateral rectus respectively, while trigeminal and facial nerves are not motor to extraocular muscles.'),
('416bda20-6b08-5a7a-8587-75fcb832e77a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The medial rectus muscle is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V)","Trochlear (IV)","Facial (VII)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Abducens (VI)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The medial rectus receives its innervation from the oculomotor nerve (CN III), which also supplies the superior and inferior recti and the inferior oblique. Trigeminal, trochlear, facial, and abducens nerves do not innervate it.'),
('4c7cea22-acb6-5317-88b5-af35c4ea047a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The inferior oblique muscle is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V)","Abducens (VI)","Trochlear (IV)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Facial (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The inferior oblique is innervated by the inferior division of the oculomotor nerve (CN III). Trochlear and abducens supply the superior oblique and lateral rectus respectively, and trigeminal and facial nerves carry no such motor supply.'),
('8dd32e04-7998-545a-9b78-0c1dae5f28e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The superior oblique muscle passes through:', '["Inferior orbital fissure","Optic canal","Trochlea (pulley) on frontal bone","Annulus of Zinn","Superior orbital fissure"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlea (pulley) on frontal bone', 'The superior oblique tendon passes through the trochlea, a fibrocartilaginous pulley on the frontal bone, then turns to insert on the sclera. The optic canal and orbital fissures transmit nerves and vessels, and the annulus of Zinn is the origin of the rectus muscles.'),
('3986276b-6084-55ee-bbc8-5ac7b518a00b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding clinical cases in Orbit Eyeball: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('89566b4a-b2bd-59dd-bf4f-014a9448bf67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In the study of Orbit Eyeball, Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('7f245eb3-07ed-5d49-a550-c9463fc884a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The superior tarsal muscle (Muller''s) is supplied by:', '["Facial (VII)","Trochlear (IV)","Sympathetic fibers","Abducens (VI)","Oculomotor (III)"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic fibers', 'Muller''s muscle is smooth muscle innervated by sympathetic fibers, and its paralysis produces the ptosis of Horner syndrome. It receives no supply from any motor cranial nerve.'),
('f9db497c-0e86-5009-a2c8-a40361423a2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The action of the superior rectus is:', '["Extorsion resulting in decreased functional activity","Adduction only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abduction mediated by specific receptor activation","Depression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevation adduction intorsion"]'::jsonb, 'Elevation adduction intorsion', 'From the primary position the superior rectus elevates the eye, with secondary adduction and intorsion because of the oblique line of pull. It neither depresses nor abducts, and adduction alone understates its combined actions.'),
('bab32f34-a9c6-577f-b248-696bc229a89b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The action of the inferior rectus is:', '["Depression","adduction","extorsion","Abduction","Intorsion Adduction only Elevation"]'::jsonb, 'Depression', 'From the primary position the inferior rectus depresses the eye, with secondary adduction and extorsion. Its dominant action is depression; abduction and elevation are not functions of this muscle.'),
('18cb387c-ad54-5226-bb5f-275dc79ede61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The action of the medial rectus is:', '["Rotation","Adduction","Elevation","Abduction","Depression"]'::jsonb, 'Adduction', 'The medial rectus pulls the cornea toward the midline, producing adduction. Elevation, abduction, depression, and rotation belong to other extraocular muscles.'),
('d3921140-e7d5-5dc5-b570-ac3d84b4cd0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The action of the lateral rectus is:', '["Adduction","Elevation","Rotation","Abduction","Depression"]'::jsonb, 'Abduction', 'The lateral rectus moves the cornea away from the midline, producing abduction. Adduction, elevation, depression, and rotation are actions performed by other extraocular muscles.'),
('d70bfcdf-892c-5418-a501-23df197584ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The action of the superior oblique is:', '["Abduction only","Elevation","adduction extorsion Depression only","Depression","abduction intorsion Intorsion only"]'::jsonb, 'Depression', 'At the primary position the superior oblique depresses the eye, with secondary intorsion and abduction. Its primary action is depression; elevation, adduction, and extorsion are not its actions.'),
('8c93bfc1-788f-58c6-9c06-a495eb53cae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The action of the inferior oblique is:', '["Elevation","abduction","extorsion","Abduction only","Elevation only Extorsion only Depression adduction intorsion"]'::jsonb, 'Elevation', 'At the primary position the inferior oblique elevates the eye, with secondary extorsion and abduction. Its primary action is elevation; depression, adduction, and intorsion belong to other muscles.'),
('2f892f11-3e11-5380-a784-7a8441d59d74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The eyeball is composed of how many layers?', '["6 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 (fibrous, vascular, neural)","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (fibrous, vascular, neural)', 'The eyeball wall has three tunics: the outer fibrous layer (sclera and cornea), the middle vascular layer (choroid, ciliary body, iris), and the inner neural retina. Counts of two, four, five, or six do not match these three coats.'),
('002f57a2-e0e7-55b4-a34c-11bc7aa66f94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The outer fibrous layer consists of:', '["Cornea + iris resulting in decreased functional activity","Sclera only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sclera (posterior 5/6) and cornea (anterior 1/6)","Sclera + choroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cornea only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sclera (posterior 5/6) and cornea (anterior 1/6)', 'The outer fibrous layer is the sclera (posterior five-sixths) continuous with the cornea (anterior one-sixth). The iris and choroid belong to the vascular layer, so options naming sclera or cornea alone are incomplete.'),
('a25f97c4-9729-5255-8ff1-00fb0d82be88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The middle vascular layer (uvea) includes:', '["Choroid only","Ciliary body + lens","Iris + cornea","Choroid","ciliary body iris Choroid + retina"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid', 'The uveal (vascular) tunic consists of the choroid, ciliary body, and iris; the choroid is the posterior vascular coat lining the eyeball. The retina is the inner neural layer, while the lens and cornea are not part of the uvea.'),
('27e7fd54-eeaf-5da7-b0bb-469a6d936f1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'How many layers does the retina have?', '["5","10 layers","3","7","12"]'::jsonb, '10 layers', 'From the retinal pigment epithelium to the internal limiting membrane the retina comprises ten histologic layers. Five, three, seven, or twelve do not reflect the standard count.'),
('ba63d6c2-33d9-59c5-9eff-6ee62b9ef033'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Since the cornea is avascular, it is nourished by:', '["nourished by:","Aqueous humor","tears","limbal vessels","Vitreous humor Only tears Only aqueous Blood vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Aqueous humor', 'The avascular cornea receives most of its nutrition from the aqueous humor in the anterior chamber, supplemented by tears and limbal vessels. The vitreous humor lies behind the lens and does not nourish the cornea.'),
('6b0013fb-b9ce-57f7-98b1-d7e5ff2af1f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The cornea is innervated by:', '["Maxillary (V2) resulting in decreased functional activity","Oculomotor (III) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandibular (V3) mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial (VII) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ophthalmic (V1) via long ciliary nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic (V1) via long ciliary nerves', 'The cornea receives its sensitive innervation from the nasociliary branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) through the long ciliary nerves. Maxillary, mandibular, oculomotor, and facial nerves do not supply the cornea.'),
('49608ef0-5340-5568-8184-b77fb5be8fd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The iris muscles are:', '["Only sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","Both sphincter and ciliary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ciliary muscle only mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphincter pupillae (parasympathetic) and dilator pupillae (sympathetic)","Only dilator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter pupillae (parasympathetic) and dilator pupillae (sympathetic)', 'The iris contains the parasympathetic sphincter pupillae, which constricts the pupil, and the sympathetic dilator pupillae, which dilates it. The ciliary muscle lies in the ciliary body, not the iris, so naming only one iris muscle is incomplete.'),
('a6f16038-c175-5514-82ea-39f3f25aafb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The sphincter pupillae is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic from CN III via ciliary ganglion","Glossopharyngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic from CN III via ciliary ganglion', 'The sphincter pupillae is innervated by parasympathetic fibers that travel with CN III and synapse in the ciliary ganglion before reaching the iris to constrict the pupil. Sympathetic, trigeminal, facial, and glossopharyngeal fibers do not supply it.'),
('a68ad6ab-5b33-521a-911e-2f63ecd9f608'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The dilator pupillae is supplied by:', '["Sympathetic from superior cervical ganglion","Oculomotor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trigeminal mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic from superior cervical ganglion', 'The dilator pupillae receives sympathetic postganglionic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion, a pathway lost in Horner syndrome. CN III (parasympathetic), trigeminal, and facial nerves do not dilate the pupil.'),
('93608142-67fc-5850-812b-cf3365be411d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('d884f4f9-4da4-5a01-ae92-67374caacc29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The ciliary muscle is supplied by:', '["Facial resulting in decreased functional activity","Trochlear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trigeminal mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic from CN III"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic from CN III', 'The ciliary muscle is innervated by parasympathetic fibers from CN III that synapse in the ciliary ganglion; their contraction rounds the lens for near vision. Trigeminal, facial, trochlear, and sympathetic fibers do not supply it.'),
('0617d54f-586d-556a-91db-cd1144b5a571'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the lens of the eye?', '["Biconvex","avascular","suspended by zonules from ciliary body","Rigid","Vascular Part of cornea Secreted by retina"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex', 'The lens is a transparent, biconvex structure that focuses light onto the retina by changing its curvature during accommodation. It is indeed avascular and suspended by zonular fibers from the ciliary body, but biconvex describes its fundamental shape. It is not rigid, and it forms no part of the cornea nor is it secreted by the retina.'),
('d4b7ccdc-7fed-531e-b138-c4079478a34c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which of the following correctly describes the vitreous body?', '["Lens nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Solid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aqueous fluid mediated by specific receptor activation","Corneal layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clear gel filling posterior chamber behind lens"]'::jsonb, 'Clear gel filling posterior chamber behind lens', 'The vitreous body is a clear, gelatinous mass that fills the cavity posterior to the lens and helps maintain the shape of the eyeball. It is not solid, is distinct from aqueous humor, and is neither a corneal layer nor the lens nucleus.'),
('c9b19ad7-fc83-521b-8a6c-7ab1397ca74e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the anterior chamber of the eye?', '["In vitreous resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind iris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In retina mediated by specific receptor activation","In lens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Space between cornea and iris"]'::jsonb, 'Space between cornea and iris', 'The anterior chamber is the space between the cornea and the iris, filled with aqueous humor. It lies in front of the iris and lens and is not located in the vitreous, retina, or lens.'),
('fba990cd-f3ec-5d78-9455-683c54016bd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'What is the correct description of the posterior chamber of the eye?', '["Vitreous cavity","In front of cornea","Space between iris and lens","Behind lens","In ciliary body"]'::jsonb, 'Space between iris and lens', 'The posterior chamber is the narrow space between the iris and the anterior surface of the lens, also filled with aqueous humor. It is distinct from the vitreous cavity and is not located in front of the cornea, behind the lens, or within the ciliary body.'),
('681fc400-0168-5970-b787-434bfe407227'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where is aqueous humor produced?', '["Ciliary processes","Retina","Lacrimal gland","Choroid","Lens"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary processes', 'Aqueous humor is secreted by the ciliary processes of the ciliary body and flows into the anterior chamber. It is not made by the retina, choroid, lens, or lacrimal gland, which produces tears.'),
('665a47d4-81c3-5110-98b1-5587afcea761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'How does aqueous humor drain from the eye?', '["Choroidal vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scleral veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Canal of Schlemm (trabecular meshwork)","Retinal veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Canal of Schlemm (trabecular meshwork)', 'Aqueous humor drains through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, located at the iridocorneal angle. It does not drain via choroidal or retinal vessels, scleral veins, or the optic nerve.'),
('caec95af-5742-5c4c-82db-a6cc49e99b54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where is the canal of Schlemm located?', '["In iris","At iridocorneal angle","In retina","In lens","In choroid"]'::jsonb, 'At iridocorneal angle', 'The canal of Schlemm is a circular venous sinus lying at the iridocorneal angle, where the iris meets the cornea. It is not found within the iris, retina, lens, or choroid.'),
('283ab7ed-682e-5177-b95f-48e52a7e093a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Glaucoma involves which of the following?', '["Lens dislocation resulting in decreased functional activity","Retinal detachment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased IOP from impaired aqueous drainage","Corneal opacity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased IOP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased IOP from impaired aqueous drainage', 'Glaucoma is characterized by raised intraocular pressure resulting from impaired outflow of aqueous humor, which can damage the optic nerve. Lens dislocation, retinal detachment, and corneal opacity are separate pathologies, and the pressure is elevated, not lowered.'),
('ed8e23e8-56fd-51ce-a398-c385a0843473'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the optic disc (blind spot)?', '["Pupil opening resulting in decreased functional activity","Where optic nerve exits; no photoreceptors","Lens attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","Macula center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sharpest vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Where optic nerve exits; no photoreceptors', 'The optic disc is the site where the optic nerve exits the eye and contains no photoreceptors, creating the physiologic blind spot. It is not the pupil opening, lens attachment, or a region of sharpest vision.'),
('ac30954f-79a4-5729-aa26-61d7977e6c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the macula lutea?', '["Blind spot resulting in decreased functional activity","Oval yellow area lateral to optic disc for central vision","Ciliary body mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lens nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oval yellow area lateral to optic disc for central vision', 'The macula lutea is an oval, yellowish region located lateral to the optic disc and specialized for central, high-acuity vision. It is neither the blind spot, ciliary body, lens nucleus, nor peripheral retina.'),
('2e7ba566-af3b-5e17-8e5f-d6e4bba7d8c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the fovea centralis?', '["Center of macula with only cones (sharpest vision)","Rod-rich area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blind spot mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lens capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Center of macula with only cones (sharpest vision)', 'The fovea centralis is the central depression of the macula that contains only cones and gives maximal visual acuity. It is not a rod-rich area, the blind spot, peripheral retina, or part of the lens.'),
('5ac8d898-8dc9-58be-87f8-67250d2c2e15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where is the lacrimal gland located?', '["Superomedial orbit resulting in decreased functional activity","Superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal)","Inferomedial mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferolateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal)', 'The lacrimal gland lies in the superolateral part of the orbit, within the lacrimal fossa of the frontal bone. It is not located superomedially, inferomedially, inferolaterally, or in the nasal cavity.'),
('fbfcf825-fcac-5792-b12a-3a838ac9bc42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which nerve provides the secretomotor supply to the lacrimal gland?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Oculomotor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic CN VII via pterygopalatine ganglion","Trigeminal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic CN VII via pterygopalatine ganglion', 'Secretomotor fibers for the lacrimal gland travel with the facial nerve (CN VII), synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion, and reach the gland through V2 branches. Sympathetic and trigeminal fibers are not the secretomotor pathway, and the oculomotor nerve does not serve the gland.'),
('415ad357-5969-5bf7-8834-c7cbeed60098'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which pathway correctly describes the drainage of tears?', '["Gland directly to nose resulting in decreased functional activity","To mouth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To parotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Puncta -> canaliculi -> lacrimal sac -> nasolacrimal duct -> inferior meatus","Evaporation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Puncta -> canaliculi -> lacrimal sac -> nasolacrimal duct -> inferior meatus', 'Tears drain through the lacrimal puncta into canaliculi, then into the lacrimal sac, and down the nasolacrimal duct to open into the inferior meatus of the nose. They do not pass to the mouth, parotid gland, or drain directly by evaporation.'),
('7e10ae78-b235-5b87-bc56-523e1ce7ce9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Into which structure does the nasolacrimal duct open?', '["Middle meatus","Inferior meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Superior meatus","Nasal vestibule"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the inferior nasal concha into the inferior meatus. It does not drain into the middle or superior meatus, the sphenoethmoidal recess, or the nasal vestibule.'),
('58fa8e59-c51e-5086-ba57-74e8f131c23c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where is the lacrimal sac located?', '["Nasal cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","In lacrimal fossa (between lacrimal bone and maxilla)","Orbit apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Ethmoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maxillary sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In lacrimal fossa (between lacrimal bone and maxilla)', 'The lacrimal sac lies in the lacrimal fossa, bounded by the lacrimal bone and the frontal process of the maxilla, and receives tears from the canaliculi. It is not inside the nasal cavity proper, orbit apex, ethmoid bone, or maxillary sinus.'),
('de597df9-43c9-573f-910c-725ebae8a569'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which artery provides the main blood supply to the eye?', '["Ophthalmic artery (branch of internal carotid)","External carotid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle meningeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maxillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery (branch of internal carotid)', 'The eye is supplied mainly by the ophthalmic artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery that enters the orbit through the optic canal. Branches of the external carotid, such as the facial, maxillary, and middle meningeal arteries, do not supply the eyeball.'),
('8220d37a-83d8-5ae6-acfe-0289a3699c56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The central retinal artery is a branch of which artery?', '["Internal carotid directly","Facial","Maxillary","Ophthalmic artery","Posterior ciliary"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery', 'The central retinal artery arises from the ophthalmic artery within the orbit and enters the optic nerve to supply the inner retina. It does not arise directly from the internal carotid or from facial, maxillary, or posterior ciliary branches.'),
('a654b21d-16ff-5185-b6e7-2456d102dded'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which structures receive blood from the central retinal artery?', '["Choroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Outer retina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Whole retina mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inner retina (ganglion cells, bipolar cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Inner retina (ganglion cells, bipolar cells)', 'The central retinal artery supplies the inner layers of the retina, including ganglion and bipolar cells and the optic nerve head. The choroid and outer retina are instead supplied by ciliary arteries, so it does not perfuse the whole retina.'),
('8deb7e70-1bec-5947-b193-dd52f07b63f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which structures do the short posterior ciliary arteries supply?', '["Retina resulting in decreased functional activity","Iris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cornea mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve head and choroid","Lens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve head and choroid', 'The short posterior ciliary arteries pierce the sclera around the optic nerve and supply the optic nerve head and the choroid, which in turn nourishes the outer retina. They do not directly supply the retina, iris, cornea, or lens.'),
('99438b4c-50d7-51b6-8a42-7830c1d0cae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which structures do the long posterior ciliary arteries supply?', '["Optic nerve","Choroid","Retina","Cornea","Iris and ciliary body"]'::jsonb, 'Iris and ciliary body', 'The long posterior ciliary arteries run forward between the sclera and choroid to reach the ciliary body and iris, forming the major arterial circle. They do not supply the optic nerve, retina, or cornea primarily.'),
('e3717d5c-c4ed-51c1-a8e3-704ba7e7a5f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Into which venous structure do the ophthalmic veins drain?', '["External jugular","Internal jugular","Pterygoid plexus","Facial vein","Cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus', 'The superior and inferior ophthalmic veins drain orbital blood into the cavernous sinus. This unvalved channel allows orbital or facial infections to spread intracranially.'),
('ec806d52-363d-5515-9655-e751e75d8777'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'With which vein do the ophthalmic veins communicate?', '["Internal carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial vein (no valves, allows infection spread)","Azygos without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavian leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial vein (no valves, allows infection spread)', 'The ophthalmic veins communicate with the facial vein, and because these veins lack valves, infections of the face can spread backward into the cavernous sinus. They do not connect to the internal carotid, vertebral, azygos, or subclavian veins.'),
('aaed6026-fc2a-567c-8df9-da586859cc28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which nerves carry most of the sensory supply to the eyeball?', '["Only sympathetic","Only facial","Long ciliary (V1)","short ciliary (ciliary ganglion)","sympathetic Only oculomotor Only trigeminal"]'::jsonb, 'Long ciliary (V1)', 'Sensory innervation of the eyeball (cornea, iris, ciliary body) is carried chiefly by the long ciliary nerves, branches of the nasociliary nerve (V1). Short ciliary nerves relay parasympathetic and sensory fibers, but the eyeball is not supplied only by sympathetic, facial, or oculomotor fibers.'),
('ab7b6dbc-f56e-5fa0-902d-57d349a0fc13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where is the ciliary ganglion located?', '["At orbital apex between optic nerve and lateral rectus","Infratemporal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pterygopalatine fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior orbit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parotid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At orbital apex between optic nerve and lateral rectus', 'The ciliary ganglion is a small parasympathetic relay that lies in the posterior orbit between the optic nerve and the lateral rectus muscle. It is not located in the infratemporal or pterygopalatine fossa, the anterior orbit, or the parotid region.'),
('0e853c50-6904-5f4c-b85f-1cc78cc0886b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which nerve gives the parasympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion?', '["Oculomotor (CN III)","Sympathetic trunk","Nasociliary","Facial (VII)","Trigeminal (V1)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor (CN III)', 'The parasympathetic (motor) root of the ciliary ganglion comes from the oculomotor nerve (CN III), whose fibers synapse in the ganglion to supply the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. The sympathetic trunk, nasociliary, facial, and trigeminal nerves do not form this root.'),
('081fa418-1304-540e-bdd2-37add6f06050'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which structure gives the sympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion?', '["Facial","Thoracic sympathetic","Trigeminal","Internal carotid plexus","Cervical sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid plexus', 'The sympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion arises from the internal carotid plexus, carrying postganglionic sympathetic fibers that pass through the ganglion without synapsing. The cervical and thoracic sympathetic trunks contribute indirectly through this plexus rather than directly.'),
('7ca1fe7e-17e8-542e-86c9-959592ce2a97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which nerve gives the sensory root of the ciliary ganglion?', '["Facial (VII)","Optic (II)","Nasociliary (V1)","Maxillary (V2)","Oculomotor (III)"]'::jsonb, 'Nasociliary (V1)', 'The sensory root of the ciliary ganglion is the nasociliary nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve, whose fibers traverse the ganglion to reach the eyeball. The facial, optic, maxillary, and oculomotor nerves do not contribute to this root.'),
('50b27fe1-12fc-5d46-85db-ee379e8b7499'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which fibers do the short ciliary nerves carry?', '["Only sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic to sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle; sensory to cornea"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic to sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle; sensory to cornea', 'Short ciliary nerves deliver parasympathetic fibers from the ciliary ganglion to the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle, along with sensory fibers from the eyeball, including the cornea. They are not exclusively sympathetic, sensory, parasympathetic, or motor.'),
('0a4fed3f-6961-5b3a-a7ec-abc70dafb844'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which fibers do the long ciliary nerves carry?', '["Parasympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory to cornea/iris and sympathetic to dilator pupillae","Motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory to cornea/iris and sympathetic to dilator pupillae', 'Long ciliary nerves convey sensory fibers from the cornea and iris and sympathetic fibers from the internal carotid plexus to the dilator pupillae. They do not carry parasympathetic or purely motor fibers.'),
('d19f3c98-7265-5bb4-ba1b-b950f71545c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which of the following is a feature of oculomotor nerve (CN III) palsy?', '["Miosis","Ptosis","exotropia (down and out) dilated pupil","loss of accommodation Ptosis only","Vertical diplopia Esotropia"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis', 'Oculomotor nerve palsy causes ptosis from paralysis of the levator palpebrae superioris, a dilated pupil from loss of parasympathetic tone, and loss of accommodation. Miosis is the opposite sign seen in Horner syndrome, and the down-and-out deviation option is incomplete, so ptosis is the best single answer.'),
('44429758-885a-54ba-b1e4-c85b4cbe64ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which of the following results from trochlear nerve (CN IV) palsy?', '["Lateral strabismus","Medial strabismus","Ptosis","Vertical diplopia","head tilt opposite side Mydriasis"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical diplopia', 'Fourth nerve palsy weakens the superior oblique, producing vertical diplopia that worsens on looking down; patients tilt the head to the opposite side. Esotropia and exotropia are horizontal deviations due to sixth and third nerve lesions, and ptosis is not a feature.'),
('6fb6d323-4996-5543-b848-a84c3b6683f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which of the following results from abducens nerve (CN VI) palsy?', '["Ptosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Mydriasis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esotropia (medial strabismus, cannot abduct)","Exotropia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertical diplopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Esotropia (medial strabismus, cannot abduct)', 'Abducens nerve palsy paralyzes the lateral rectus, so the eye cannot abduct and is drawn medially, producing esotropia (medial strabismus). Ptosis, mydriasis, exotropia, and vertical diplopia are not features of a pure sixth nerve lesion.'),
('5c84744c-faa2-57b9-83dc-d5793edfcec0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which of the following describes the eye signs of Horner syndrome?', '["Mydriasis + anhidrosis","Exophthalmos","Lacrimation","Ptosis","miosis anhidrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis', 'Horner syndrome causes miosis (small pupil), mild ptosis from loss of sympathetic innervation to the superior tarsal muscle, and ipsilateral facial anhidrosis. It never causes mydriasis, exophthalmos, or lacrimation; ptosis is the only correctly formed option.'),
('ec653985-a35f-543e-816a-b3ddb631df50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which sequence correctly describes the visual pathway?', '["Retina -> pretectal resulting in decreased functional activity","Retina -> superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retina -> optic n -> chiasm -> tract -> LGN -> radiation -> area 17","Retina -> hypothalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retina -> thalamus directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retina -> optic n -> chiasm -> tract -> LGN -> radiation -> area 17', 'Visual fibers leave the retina by the optic nerve, partially cross at the chiasm, continue in the optic tract to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN), and travel via the optic radiation to the primary visual cortex, area 17. The pretectal nucleus and superior colliculus serve reflex pathways, not conscious vision.'),
('0d4898e9-c6fd-5e5b-9f1b-c7776ec1233b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'What happens to nasal retinal fibers at the optic chiasm?', '["Stay uncrossed","Synapse","Cross to opposite side","Go to ipsilateral LGN","Go to superior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Cross to opposite side', 'At the optic chiasm, fibers from the nasal half of each retina cross to the opposite side, while temporal fibers stay uncrossed. No synapse occurs at the chiasm, and fibers continuing to the superior colliculus are not part of the chiasmal crossing.'),
('65853831-ccdb-5dea-9cd3-5173d8230563'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Pituitary tumor compressing the optic chiasm causes which visual field defect?', '["Homonymous hemianopia","Central scotoma","Monocular blindness","Bitemporal hemianopia","One eye blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Bitemporal hemianopia', 'A pituitary tumor compresses the chiasm from below and damages the crossing nasal retinal fibers, causing bitemporal hemianopia (loss of the lateral visual fields of both eyes). Homonymous hemianopia, monocular blindness, and central scotoma result from tract, optic nerve, and macular disease respectively.'),
('c6ec403b-aa7b-5919-836a-03fea127c356'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'A right optic tract lesion causes which visual field defect?', '["Left nasal hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Right eye blind"]'::jsonb, 'Left homonymous hemianopia', 'A right optic tract lesion interrupts fibers carrying the left visual field from both eyes, producing a left homonymous hemianopia. Bitemporal or monocular field loss would indicate chiasmal or optic nerve involvement rather than a tract lesion.'),
('6f6eca54-3cd8-57a2-b9b9-77073bc5a9c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which sequence correctly describes the pupillary light reflex pathway?', '["CN III directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Retina -> superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short ciliary nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Retina -> pretectal nucleus -> Edinger-Westphal -> CN III -> ciliary ganglion -> sphincter","Retina -> LGN -> cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retina -> pretectal nucleus -> Edinger-Westphal -> CN III -> ciliary ganglion -> sphincter', 'The pupillary light reflex travels from the retina to the pretectal nucleus, then to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, and through CN III to the ciliary ganglion, which constricts the sphincter pupillae. The LGN-to-cortex pathway mediates conscious vision, not this reflex.'),
('b2f10976-2d57-5d82-812f-035c11f8bc7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'What is the Edinger-Westphal nucleus?', '["Facial nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory nucleus of V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic nucleus of CN III (pupil and accommodation)","Salivatory nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor nucleus of CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic nucleus of CN III (pupil and accommodation)', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is the parasympathetic nucleus of CN III, providing preganglionic fibers for pupillary constriction and accommodation. It is distinct from the main motor nucleus of CN III, the facial nucleus, and the salivatory nuclei.'),
('e21da650-081c-575c-a39b-cadf67e2bd2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which component is part of the accommodation reflex?', '["Pupil dilation","Lens flattening","pupil change","Convergence","lens accommodation (ciliary muscle) miosis Eye divergence"]'::jsonb, 'Convergence', 'The accommodation reflex triad comprises lens thickening (ciliary muscle contraction), miosis, and convergence of the eyes. Lens flattening and divergence are opposite actions, and pupil dilation is not involved.'),
('60a9bd5c-1f72-5675-b6d8-b2b532b7d08e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic nerve (CN II) is classified as which type of nerve?', '["Special somatic afferent (vision)","Motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Special somatic afferent (vision)', 'The optic nerve (CN II) is a special somatic afferent nerve that transmits visual information from the retina. It is neither motor, mixed, sympathetic, nor parasympathetic.'),
('2698334c-9399-5631-97d0-b85f0bd6761b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'The optic nerve is invested by which of the following coverings?', '["Only arachnoid resulting in decreased functional activity","All 3 meningeal layers (extension of brain)","meninges mediated by specific receptor activation","Only dura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All 3 meningeal layers (extension of brain)', 'Because the optic nerve is an outgrowth of the brain, it is surrounded by all three meningeal layers (dura, arachnoid, and pia) together with the subarachnoid cerebrospinal fluid space. It is not covered by only a single layer.'),
('290bf7f3-2c0e-5a52-9baf-efe1c171b533'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where is the optic disc located?', '["3-4 mm medial to fovea","Equator","Ora serrata","Fovea center","Peripheral retina"]'::jsonb, '3-4 mm medial to fovea', 'The optic disc lies approximately 3-4 mm medial to the fovea, and the central retinal artery and vein emerge from its center. It is not at the equator, ora serrata, fovea, or peripheral retina.'),
('800e235d-2609-58a7-9074-eaa8f5989be0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which cells are the photoreceptors of the retina?', '["Bipolar cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Only rods accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cones mediated by specific receptor activation","Ganglion cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rods (dim light) and cones (color, acuity)"]'::jsonb, 'Rods (dim light) and cones (color, acuity)', 'The two photoreceptor types in the retina are rods, which mediate dim-light (scotopic) vision, and cones, which mediate color vision and high acuity. Bipolar and ganglion cells are downstream neurons, not photoreceptors.'),
('4f82bc63-2d32-561d-ae1b-5767be808a99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where are rods most concentrated in the retina?', '["Macula","Fovea","Peripheral retina","Optic disc","Equator"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral retina', 'Rods are most numerous in the peripheral retina, providing high sensitivity in dim light but low acuity. The fovea and macula contain predominantly cones, and the optic disc has no photoreceptors.'),
('8f401cdd-04cb-5482-b25b-38d35298f592'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Where are cones most concentrated in the retina?', '["Optic disc","Fovea centralis","Ora serrata","Equator","Peripheral retina"]'::jsonb, 'Fovea centralis', 'Cones are concentrated in the fovea centralis, where minimal convergence of cone pathways yields maximum acuity and color discrimination. The peripheral retina, ora serrata, optic disc, and equator are not cone-rich.'),
('b268204d-aee8-52b7-870a-fc8d4f0c4e3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the ora serrata?', '["Anterior retina edge where it meets ciliary body","Optic disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fovea mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iridocorneal angle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior retina edge where it meets ciliary body', 'The ora serrata is the serrated anterior margin of the retina, where the retina ends just behind its junction with the ciliary body. It is neither the optic disc, the fovea, the posterior retina, nor the iridocorneal angle.'),
('e84e56c8-e6d5-5cdd-95fa-2cac740b0afe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the choroid?', '["Part of cornea resulting in decreased functional activity","Inner layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of lens mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular layer between sclera and retina supplying outer retina","Outer fibrous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular layer between sclera and retina supplying outer retina', 'The choroid is the highly vascular middle coat of the eye lying between the sclera and retina, supplying oxygen and nutrients to the outer retina. It is not the inner layer, an outer fibrous coat, or part of the cornea or lens.'),
('567cdb5f-5982-50ee-a12d-4ca15658d1ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which statement best describes the sclera?', '["Vascular resulting in decreased functional activity","Inner layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uveal part mediated by specific receptor activation","Tough fibrous outer coat of eye (continuous with cornea)","Transparent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tough fibrous outer coat of eye (continuous with cornea)', 'The sclera is the tough, fibrous, opaque outer coat of the eyeball that is continuous anteriorly with the cornea. It is neither vascular, transparent, an inner layer, nor part of the uvea.'),
('81a68e83-1a4e-5a27-bcb2-f8baf6c14f74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: conjunctiva:?', '["Vascular layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Thin mucous membrane covering anterior sclera and inner eyelids","Muscle layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Nerve layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corneal layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin mucous membrane covering anterior sclera and inner eyelids', 'The conjunctiva is a thin mucous membrane that lines the inner surface of the eyelids (palpebral conjunctiva) and covers the anterior sclera (bulbar conjunctiva). The other options name the coats of the eyeball (vascular, muscle, nerve, corneal), which are not conjunctival surfaces.'),
('94ed9162-bc20-55cd-bf7b-fbd6711af9fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: palpebral conjunctiva:?', '["Lacrimal sac resulting in decreased functional activity","Cornea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior eyeball mediated by specific receptor activation","Lines inner surface of eyelids","Fornix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lines inner surface of eyelids', 'The palpebral conjunctiva lines the inner (deep) surface of the eyelids. The lacrimal sac, cornea, anterior eyeball, and conjunctival fornix are separate structures or surfaces.'),
('f7c0942c-24c4-5d23-aff4-71093160c580'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: bulbar conjunctiva:?', '["Covers anterior sclera (not cornea)","Optic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lens mediated by specific receptor activation","Cornea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior sclera leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers anterior sclera (not cornea)', 'The bulbar conjunctiva covers the anterior sclera and ends at the corneoscleral junction, so it does not cover the cornea. The optic nerve, lens, cornea, and posterior sclera are not covered by this membrane.'),
('e667809c-029b-5b7c-b094-d5a8de3f1397'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: conjunctival fornix:?', '["Plica semilunaris resulting in decreased functional activity","Punctum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corneal margin mediated by specific receptor activation","Caruncle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fold where palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet"]'::jsonb, 'Fold where palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet', 'The conjunctival fornix is the fold (superior and inferior) where the palpebral and bulbar conjunctiva meet, allowing free movement of the globe. The plica semilunaris, punctum, corneal margin, and caruncle are distinct conjunctival structures.'),
('e21cec00-4a1b-59d9-98d1-8505fabbbfc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''Plica semilunaris:'' is:', '["Caruncle resulting in decreased functional activity","Punctum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tarsal plate mediated by specific receptor activation","Semilunar fold at medial canthus (vestigial third eyelid)","Lateral canthus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Semilunar fold at medial canthus (vestigial third eyelid)', 'The plica semilunaris is a semilunar fold of conjunctiva at the medial canthus, representing a vestigial third eyelid. It lies lateral to the caruncle and is not the punctum, tarsal plate, or lateral canthus.'),
('e85e06a4-0ba1-58b8-800b-de966fa6df01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: lacrimal caruncle:?', '["Reddish elevation at medial canthus with modified sweat and sebaceous glands","Nasolacrimal duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Punctum mediated by specific receptor activation","Lacrimal sac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lacrimal gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reddish elevation at medial canthus with modified sweat and sebaceous glands', 'The lacrimal caruncle is a reddish elevation at the medial canthus containing modified sweat and sebaceous (and tear) glands. The punctum, lacrimal sac, nasolacrimal duct, and lacrimal gland are parts of the tear drainage or production pathway.'),
('3e983744-5579-5e13-9247-5cea43d76a62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: tarsal plates:?', '["Glands resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dense connective tissue sheets giving form to eyelids","Cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orbital bones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense connective tissue sheets giving form to eyelids', 'The tarsal plates are dense fibrous connective tissue sheets that give shape and support to the eyelids; they are not cartilage. They house the meibomian (tarsal) glands and give attachment to the orbital septum.'),
('a1eb0ec0-2ecc-52ac-9a83-1d5b2805740b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: tarsal (meibomian) glands:?', '["Modified sebaceous glands secreting lipid layer of tear film","Salivary glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sweat glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Lacrimal glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucous glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modified sebaceous glands secreting lipid layer of tear film', 'The meibomian (tarsal) glands are modified sebaceous glands embedded in the tarsal plates; they secrete the lipid layer of the tear film that reduces evaporation. They are not salivary, sweat, lacrimal, or mucous glands.'),
('a1f95d4d-a7f1-5a6b-9196-e6dc7bd2dd23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: palpebral orbicularis oculi:?', '["Depresses eyebrows","Gently closes eyelids (blinking)","Elevates eyebrows","Opens eyes","Forcefully closes eyes"]'::jsonb, 'Gently closes eyelids (blinking)', 'The palpebral part of orbicularis oculi gently closes the eyelids during blinking and during sleep. Forceful closure is produced by the orbital part, whereas eyebrow movement and eye opening involve other muscles.'),
('ccfea37b-fd34-537d-afa2-524f1628c2c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: orbital orbicularis oculi:?', '["Forcefully closes eyes (squeezing)","Opens eye","Gently blinks","Elevates eyebrow","Moves eyelid laterally"]'::jsonb, 'Forcefully closes eyes (squeezing)', 'The orbital part of orbicularis oculi firmly closes or squeezes the eyes shut (forceful closure). Gentle blinking is performed by the palpebral part, and eye opening depends on the levator palpebrae superioris.'),
('81052e4f-fda3-5ed0-a22f-b3f699f5eb7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''Muller''s muscle:'' is:', '["Smooth muscle innervated by sympathetics; assists eyelid elevation","Skin fold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of levator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle innervated by sympathetics; assists eyelid elevation', 'Muller''s muscle (superior tarsal muscle) is smooth muscle in the upper eyelid innervated by sympathetic fibers; it assists the levator in elevating the eyelid, and its paralysis contributes to the mild ptosis of Horner''s syndrome. It is not skeletal muscle, a gland, or a skin fold.'),
('5c73d553-79ae-5be7-af7a-a6b657fede4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: orbital septum:?', '["Nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Connective tissue membrane from tarsal plates to orbital margins","Gland mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissue membrane from tarsal plates to orbital margins', 'The orbital septum is a connective tissue membrane that extends from the peripheral margins of the tarsal plates to the orbital margins, forming the anterior boundary of the orbit. It is not nerve, gland, bone, or muscle.'),
('69615437-ecce-5aa9-adb8-55ad8589b910'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: common extensor origin (annulus of zinn):?', '["Insertion of obliques","Pulley of superior oblique","Optic canal ring","Origin of rectus muscles at orbital apex","Lacrimal sac"]'::jsonb, 'Origin of rectus muscles at orbital apex', 'The annulus of Zinn is a tendinous ring at the orbital apex from which the four rectus muscles arise. The oblique muscles, the superior oblique pulley (trochlea), and the lacrimal sac are not related to this ring.'),
('ec08a7cc-9e4b-5d51-be8a-02d4a54fea1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: superior ophthalmic vein drains to:?', '["IJV","Facial vein","EJV","Pterygoid plexus","Cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus', 'The superior and inferior ophthalmic veins drain the orbit into the cavernous sinus. This connection is clinically important because infections can spread from the face/orbit into the cavernous sinus; the facial vein and pterygoid plexus are not the terminal drainage target.'),
('9f60e34d-db5a-528a-857a-420eda676d8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: infraorbital groove located in:?', '["Maxilla (orbital floor)","Ethmoid (medial)","Zygomatic (lateral)","Sphenoid (apex)","Frontal (roof)"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla (orbital floor)', 'The infraorbital groove and canal run across the orbital floor within the maxilla, carrying the infraorbital nerve and vessels. The ethmoid, zygomatic, sphenoid, and frontal bones do not contain this groove.'),
('1285a5e1-0ec0-5119-ba4a-1a4518f7f226'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: which extraocular muscle rotates the eyeball medially (adducts)?', '["Superior oblique (CN IV)","Superior rectus (CN III)","Medial rectus (CN III)","Lateral rectus (CN VI)","Inferior oblique (CN III)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial rectus (CN III)', 'The medial rectus is the primary adductor of the eyeball and is supplied by the oculomotor nerve (CN III). Lateral rectus (CN VI) abducts, superior and inferior obliques rotate/tilt the globe, and the superior rectus elevates.'),
('7df6fd1c-d8f7-52d1-bb69-adf298d7f3e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''The bony orbit is formed by how many bones'' is:', '["6 bones","7 bones","5 bones","4 bones","8 bones"]'::jsonb, '7 bones', 'Seven bones contribute to the orbit: frontal, zygomatic, maxilla, sphenoid, ethmoid, lacrimal, and palatine. The remaining counts are incorrect.'),
('39538c61-31c4-5fcd-8e8e-1b4f6387026e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: which bone forms the roof of the orbit?', '["Maxilla","Sphenoid bone","Ethmoid bone","Frontal bone (orbital plate)","Zygomatic bone"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal bone (orbital plate)', 'The orbital roof is formed mainly by the orbital plate of the frontal bone, with a small posterior contribution from the lesser wing of the sphenoid. The maxilla and zygomatic form the floor and lateral wall, and the ethmoid helps form the medial wall.'),
('36e36a26-d64c-5d65-82fd-52655a4dd9ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: the lateral wall of the orbit is formed by:?', '["Lacrimal and ethmoid","Zygomatic bone and greater wing of sphenoid","Palatine and sphenoid","Frontal and maxilla","Maxilla and ethmoid"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic bone and greater wing of sphenoid', 'The lateral orbital wall is formed by the zygomatic bone anteriorly and the greater wing of the sphenoid posteriorly. The lacrimal and ethmoid bones contribute to the medial wall, and the frontal and maxilla to the roof and floor.'),
('20ced6ed-f0d8-5e7f-9e15-e7a3e5cb127e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: the floor of the orbit is formed by:?', '["Frontal and sphenoid","Ethmoid and lacrimal","Maxilla (orbital surface)","zygomatic","palatine"]'::jsonb, 'Maxilla (orbital surface)', 'The orbital floor is formed chiefly by the orbital surface of the maxilla, with smaller contributions from the zygomatic and palatine bones. The frontal and sphenoid form the roof, and the ethmoid and lacrimal the medial wall.'),
('7a19ba69-cdea-545d-a3fe-1e31065ed683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: the medial wall of the orbit is formed by:?', '["Vomer and ethmoid","Ethmoid (lamina papyracea)","lacrimal","maxilla","sphenoid"]'::jsonb, 'lacrimal', 'The medial orbital wall is composed of several thin bones including the ethmoid (lamina papyracea), lacrimal, maxillary frontal process, sphenoid, and palatine bones. The lacrimal bone is a genuine part of this wall; the vomer does not contribute to the orbit.'),
('2d932255-2f2a-5004-a996-37b985ada31b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: the optic foramen (canal) transmits:?', '["Ophthalmic artery only","Optic nerve only","Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery","CN II and ophthalmic vein","CN II"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (II) and ophthalmic artery', 'The optic canal transmits the optic nerve (CN II) and the ophthalmic artery. The ophthalmic veins pass through the superior orbital fissure, so options listing the vein are incorrect.'),
('72f86232-af71-5039-aa0c-29ed8052276d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''The inferior orbital fissure transmits:'' is:', '["Optic nerve","Infraorbital nerve (V2)","zygomatic nerve","infraorbital vessels","Ophthalmic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Infraorbital nerve (V2)', 'The inferior orbital fissure transmits the infraorbital nerve (a branch of V2) along with the zygomatic nerve and infraorbital vessels. The optic nerve passes through the optic canal, and the ophthalmic artery enters via the optic canal as well.'),
('5b244ef3-425a-52be-9124-8fe1a7f46cdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: the nasolacrimal canal drains into:?', '["Middle meatus","Sphenoethmoidal recess","Maxillary sinus","Inferior meatus of nose","Superior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior meatus of nose', 'The nasolacrimal duct opens beneath the inferior nasal concha into the inferior meatus, carrying tears from the lacrimal sac. The middle and superior meatus, sphenoethmoidal recess, and maxillary sinus are not its destination.'),
('6b461a19-427c-5966-ad50-850a5fd4ede1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: the lacrimal gland is located:?', '["In the inferomedial orbit resulting in decreased functional activity","In the superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal bone)","In the nasal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","In the superomedial orbit without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the inferolateral orbit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the superolateral orbit (lacrimal fossa of frontal bone)', 'The lacrimal gland lies in the superolateral part of the orbit, within the lacrimal fossa of the frontal bone, lateral to the levator aponeurosis. The other orbital quadrants and the nasal cavity are incorrect sites.'),
('39acde9a-8b16-5fbc-ae48-397e7007f0be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: tears drain from the eye via:?', '["Canaliculi to maxillary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Puncta to nasolacrimal duct directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lacrimal sac to ethmoid sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Puncta to sphenoid sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Puncta to canaliculi to lacrimal sac to nasolacrimal duct to inferior meatus"]'::jsonb, 'Puncta to canaliculi to lacrimal sac to nasolacrimal duct to inferior meatus', 'Tears flow from the lacrimal lake through the puncta into the canaliculi, then the lacrimal sac, down the nasolacrimal duct, and open into the inferior meatus of the nose. Drainage into any of the paranasal sinuses is incorrect.'),
('51287ce6-cb2d-5807-9146-ced7df931411'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: the common tendinous ring (annulus of zinn) is:?', '["The insertion of the oblique muscles","The origin of the rectus muscles at the apex of the orbit","The pulley for superior oblique","The optic disc","The attachment of levator"]'::jsonb, 'The origin of the rectus muscles at the apex of the orbit', 'The annulus of Zinn is the common tendinous ring at the orbital apex from which the four rectus muscles arise. The obliques, the trochlea (pulley), the optic disc, and the levator origin are different structures.'),
('0790a375-c5f5-5cb9-87df-3fc2b2e2d5f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: the superior oblique muscle is innervated by:?', '["Facial nerve (VII)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlear nerve (CN IV)', 'The superior oblique is innervated by the trochlear nerve (CN IV), the only cranial nerve to exit dorsally. The oculomotor nerve (CN III) supplies most other extraocular muscles, CN VI the lateral rectus, and CN V and VII carry no motor supply to it.'),
('f1adec03-4c82-5697-beca-bd04d80d9c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''The lateral rectus muscle is innervated by:'' is:', '["CN III resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abducens nerve (CN VI)","CN V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abducens nerve (CN VI)', 'The lateral rectus is supplied by the abducens nerve (CN VI); its paralysis causes medial deviation (internal strabismus). CN III supplies most other extraocular muscles, CN IV the superior oblique, and CN V and VII do not innervate it.'),
('ffa5b707-6c43-5705-a703-6be2036148c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: all other extraocular muscles (except so and lr) are innervated by:?', '["Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","CN V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III)', 'All extraocular muscles except the superior oblique (CN IV) and lateral rectus (CN VI) are innervated by the oculomotor nerve (CN III), including the medial, superior, and inferior recti, the inferior oblique, and the levator palpebrae superioris.'),
('d63f6e5d-f79d-5a28-8e90-2b87513ae409'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: the levator palpebrae superioris is innervated by:?', '["Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Facial nerve (VII)","Sympathetic fibers","Trochlear nerve (IV)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III)', 'The levator palpebrae superioris is innervated by the oculomotor nerve (CN III), while its superior tarsal (Muller''s) muscle receives sympathetic fibers. CN III palsy therefore causes drooping of the upper eyelid (ptosis).'),
('f70baf33-d5f0-5bbb-b357-b7eabf6f6e0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: ptosis can result from:?', '["Facial nerve palsy","CN IV palsy","CN III palsy","Horner''s syndrome","or myasthenia gravis"]'::jsonb, 'CN III palsy', 'Oculomotor (CN III) palsy paralyses the levator palpebrae superioris and produces marked ptosis, an important presenting sign. Weakness of Muller''s muscle in Horner''s syndrome and myasthenia gravis causes milder drooping, whereas facial and trochlear nerve palsies do not affect eyelid elevation.'),
('100f465c-d2a2-5a6d-bc15-68eaf7d87bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: the ophthalmic artery is a branch of:?', '["Middle meningeal artery","External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery","Maxillary artery","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid artery', 'The ophthalmic artery is the first branch of the internal carotid artery as it leaves the cavernous sinus, entering the orbit via the optic canal. The middle meningeal and maxillary arteries are branches of the external carotid system, and the facial artery supplies the face.'),
('c32cd726-ee58-5eb4-90a7-89da1ab02c4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: the central retinal artery is a branch of:?', '["Maxillary artery","Internal carotid directly","Facial artery","Posterior ciliary artery","Ophthalmic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery', 'The central retinal artery arises from the ophthalmic artery, enters the optic nerve, and supplies the inner retina; its occlusion causes sudden monocular blindness. The maxillary, facial, and posterior ciliary arteries do not give rise to it.'),
('04ecf678-15c0-5a8f-af31-c1df9a1547f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''The ciliary body functions to:'' is:', '["Drain aqueous humor resulting in decreased functional activity","Nourish the cornea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transmit visual signals mediated by specific receptor activation","Focus light on retina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produce aqueous humor and change lens shape (accommodation)"]'::jsonb, 'Produce aqueous humor and change lens shape (accommodation)', 'The ciliary body produces aqueous humor through its epithelial processes and contains the ciliary muscle, whose contraction changes lens curvature during accommodation. Aqueous drainage occurs through the trabecular meshwork, not in the ciliary body.'),
('5d2ce6d0-6542-5127-a755-731bcdacd018'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: aqueous humor drains primarily via:?', '["Lens to cornea resulting in decreased functional activity","Trabecular meshwork to canal of Schlemm to aqueous veins to episcleral veins","Ciliary body to retina mediated by specific receptor activation","Pupil to vitreous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trabecular meshwork to canal of Schlemm to aqueous veins to episcleral veins', 'Aqueous humor drains from the anterior chamber through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm, then into aqueous veins and the episcleral venous system. Obstruction of this route raises intraocular pressure and causes glaucoma.'),
('64b7ce22-49dc-5bb2-99f9-5114416ca4bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: the cornea is avascular and receives nutrition from:?', '["Optic nerve","Tears and aqueous humor","Blood vessels in sclera","Conjunctival vessels","Vitreous humor"]'::jsonb, 'Tears and aqueous humor', 'Because the cornea is avascular, it is nourished by oxygen from the tears on its outer surface and by glucose from the aqueous humor bathing its inner surface. The conjunctival and scleral vessels do not supply it, and vitreous humor lies behind the lens.'),
('9c932650-1bc0-548f-b91a-c4b6f57ec66d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: the optic disc (blind spot) is:?', '["The center of the macula","The lens attachment","The point where the optic nerve exits the retina","with photoreceptors","The area of sharpest vision"]'::jsonb, 'The point where the optic nerve exits the retina', 'The optic disc is where the optic nerve fibers leave the retina; because it lacks photoreceptors, it produces the physiologic blind spot. The macula and fovea contain photoreceptors and provide sharp central vision, and the lens is unrelated.'),
('4f18df3c-a563-5f25-9f1a-bd0c4b1e6edf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: the macula lutea is:?', '["The blind spot resulting in decreased functional activity","The ora serrata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The peripheral retina mediated by specific receptor activation","The area of retina responsible for central vision (contains fovea)","The ciliary body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area of retina responsible for central vision (contains fovea)', 'The macula lutea is the yellow-pigmented central region of the retina responsible for central vision and sharpest visual acuity, its center being the fovea. The blind spot (optic disc), ora serrata, peripheral retina, and ciliary body are different structures.'),
('e5e4e722-b8de-5995-8dee-d7de6591db3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: the fovea centralis contains:?', '["Mixed rods and cones","Only cones (highest visual acuity)","Only rods","Ganglion cells only","photoreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Only cones (highest visual acuity)', 'The fovea centralis contains only cones, which are tightly packed and give the highest visual acuity and color discrimination. Rods predominate in the peripheral retina, and the retina as a whole is not limited to a single receptor type.'),
('3ea8120b-3c7f-51d8-a706-225fc369ee40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''The pupillary light reflex pathway involves:'' is:', '["Trigeminal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic nerve (II) to pretectal nucleus to Edinger-Westphal nucleus to CN III to ciliary ganglion to sphincter pupillae","Facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Directly to the iris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (II) to pretectal nucleus to Edinger-Westphal nucleus to CN III to ciliary ganglion to sphincter pupillae', 'The light reflex runs from the retina via the optic nerve to the pretectal nucleus, then to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, down the oculomotor nerve to the ciliary ganglion, and finally to the sphincter pupillae causing pupillary constriction. The other listed nerves are not part of this reflex.'),
('b3280674-192d-59c5-a2ec-bd6f2c30fd26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: horner''s syndrome includes:?', '["Ptosis","mydriasis","anhidrosis","B but not A","Rarely"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis', 'Horner''s syndrome results from interruption of the cervical sympathetic pathway and classically produces miosis, ptosis (weakness of Muller''s muscle), and anhidrosis on the affected side of the face. Mydriasis reflects increased sympathetic tone and is not a feature of Horner''s syndrome.'),
('b282697d-f448-5bca-859d-00437ebfdc17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: the trigeminal nerve (v) provides sensation to the eye via:?', '["All three divisions","Maxillary division (V2)","Ophthalmic division (V1)","None of the above","Mandibular division (V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic division (V1)', 'Sensation to the eyeball and orbit is carried by the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve through its nasociliary, frontal, and lacrimal branches. V2 and V3 supply other regions of the face and jaw, and no single division other than V1 serves the eye.'),
('564085fe-ef61-5446-ae9a-9f41a4bead35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: the nasociliary nerve (v1 branch) supplies:?', '["The mandible","The lacrimal gland","The cheek","The forehead","The eyeball"]'::jsonb, 'The eyeball', 'The nasociliary nerve (V1) gives the long ciliary nerves that carry sympathetic fibers to the dilator pupillae, so it supplies sensation and sympathetic fibers to the eyeball. The lacrimal gland is served by the lacrimal nerve, the forehead by the frontal nerve, and the cheek and mandible by V2/V3.'),
('6b190197-52ea-5d24-91ab-975326ae1497'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'For Orbit and eyeball, which option is most appropriate: the short ciliary nerves arise from:?', '["Ciliary ganglion","Pterygopalatine ganglion","Submandibular ganglion","Otic ganglion","Geniculate ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary ganglion', 'The short ciliary nerves arise from the ciliary ganglion and carry postganglionic parasympathetic fibers (via the oculomotor nerve) to the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. The pterygopalatine, submandibular, otic, and geniculate ganglia supply other regions.'),
('7c024e1e-3d65-59bf-9d55-41a3106a9fad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Which answer is correct in Orbit and eyeball when asked: the long ciliary nerves arise from:?', '["Ciliary ganglion","Nasociliary nerve (V1)","Oculomotor nerve","Trigeminal ganglion","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Nasociliary nerve (V1)', 'The long ciliary nerves branch directly from the nasociliary nerve (V1) and carry sympathetic fibers to the dilator pupillae plus sensory fibers to the cornea and ciliary body. They do not arise from the ciliary ganglion or the oculomotor, trigeminal, or facial nerves.'),
('3c15c16f-ef60-50ec-9442-a18895499b19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, the best response to ''Blow-out fracture of the orbit most commonly involves:'' is:', '["Orbital roof (frontal)","Orbital floor (maxilla)","Lateral wall (zygomatic)","Zygomatic arch","Medial wall (ethmoid)"]'::jsonb, 'Orbital floor (maxilla)', 'Blow-out fractures typically involve the thin orbital floor (maxilla), allowing orbital fat and the inferior rectus muscle to herniate into the maxillary sinus and restricting upward gaze. The more robust roof, lateral wall, and zygomatic arch are rarely affected.'),
('6c57df0a-43be-52c6-bba6-5eee022465df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'During revision of Orbit and eyeball, which of the following is correct: the annulus of zinn gives origin to:?', '["Superior oblique only","Levator palpebrae only","Lateral rectus only","All four rectus muscles","Inferior oblique only"]'::jsonb, 'All four rectus muscles', 'The common tendinous ring (annulus of Zinn) at the orbital apex gives origin to all four rectus muscles (medial, lateral, superior, inferior). The oblique muscles and levator palpebrae superioris arise from other orbital sites.'),
('16c49afe-31f6-58d8-b5cd-32109f7b7517'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'In Orbit and eyeball, which statement best fits the following fact: the superior orbital fissure transmits:?', '["CN VII and VIII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX, X, XI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN II only mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V2 and V3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III, IV, V1, VI, ophthalmic veins"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, IV, V1, VI, ophthalmic veins', 'The superior orbital fissure transmits the oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV), ophthalmic division of trigeminal (V1), and abducens (VI) nerves along with the ophthalmic veins. CN II uses the optic canal, and V2/V3 exit through other foramina.'),
('afec398f-d27f-5032-8ef2-87528b7c9842'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Orbit Eyeball', 'Regarding Orbit and eyeball, what is the correct answer to: the eyeball is protected by:?', '["Conjunctiva only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only bony orbit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eyelids and lashes mediated by specific receptor activation","Lacrimal gland only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bony orbit, eyelids, conjunctiva, lacrimal apparatus"]'::jsonb, 'Bony orbit, eyelids, conjunctiva, lacrimal apparatus', 'The eyeball is safeguarded by the combined actions of the bony orbit, eyelids with lashes, conjunctiva, and the lacrimal apparatus (which cleanses and lubricates the surface). No single structure provides this protection alone.'),
('98bf0d38-9ed6-5ee3-8c00-379429835de9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What is the correct mnemonic for scalp layers?', '["SCALP: Skin, Connective, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar, Pericranium","SCALP: Skin, Cartilage, Aponeurosis, Loose tissue, Periosteum","SCALP: Skin, Connective, Artery, Lymphatics, Pericranium","SCALP: Skin, Cutaneous, Aponeurosis, Lymphatics, Pericranium","SCALP: Skin, Connective, Aponeurosis, Lymph, Periosteum"]'::jsonb, 'SCALP: Skin, Connective, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar, Pericranium', 'The mnemonic lists the five scalp layers from superficial to deep: Skin, Connective tissue (dense), Aponeurosis (epicranial), Loose areolar tissue, and Pericranium. The other options substitute terms that are not scalp layers, such as Cartilage, Artery, Lymphatics, Lymph, or Periosteum, or use the wrong order.'),
('28a7e148-5640-5747-a4bf-76bc6f21e030'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which scalp layer is called the dangerous layer of scalp?', '["Loose areolar tissue (layer 4)","Pericranium (layer 5)","Aponeurosis (layer 3)","Skin (layer 1)","Connective tissue (layer 2)"]'::jsonb, 'Loose areolar tissue (layer 4)', 'The loose areolar (subaponeurotic) layer is the dangerous layer because it is avascular and offers little resistance to infection, and emissary veins through it connect scalp veins to intracranial dural sinuses. The other layers lack this emissary venous route and the risk of intracranial spread.'),
('d8c72f76-9b6e-560d-bdc9-5f9d636a30f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The galea aponeurotica is also known as:', '["Pericranium","Temporoparietal fascia","Superficial temporal fascia","Epicranial aponeurosis","Deep temporal fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Epicranial aponeurosis', 'The galea aponeurotica is the epicranial aponeurosis, the intermediate tendon joining the frontal and occipital bellies of occipitofrontalis. Pericranium, temporoparietal fascia, and the deep temporal fascia are separate structures lying in different tissue planes.'),
('82e26a71-7d4d-5d9c-b9cf-bdcbd3521544'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Occipitofrontalis muscle is supplied by which nerve?', '["Trigeminal nerve (V1)","Facial nerve (temporal branch)","Facial nerve (buccal branch)","Facial nerve (zygomatic branch)","Trigeminal nerve (V2)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (temporal branch)', 'Occipitofrontalis is a muscle of facial expression, so it receives motor supply from the facial nerve; the temporal branch reaches the frontal belly. The trigeminal divisions provide only sensory innervation, and the buccal and zygomatic branches supply other facial muscles.'),
('631a4eb5-4021-52fb-8b94-11111307eab8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Sensory innervation of anterior scalp is from:', '["Lesser occipital (C2-3)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Greater occipital (C2)","Great auricular nerve","Supraorbital and supratrochlear (V1)"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital and supratrochlear (V1)', 'The anterior scalp above the eyebrow is supplied by the supraorbital and supratrochlear nerves, both branches of the ophthalmic division (V1). The lesser occipital, greater occipital, great auricular, and auriculotemporal nerves serve the posterior, lateral, and auricular regions.'),
('4d318fd1-e792-5b04-9e98-eb7cddaef7e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Posterior scalp sensory innervation is mainly from:', '["Supratrochlear nerve (V1)","Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Greater occipital nerve (C2)","Infraorbital nerve (V2)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater occipital nerve (C2)', 'The posterior scalp is supplied mainly by the greater occipital nerve (C2), which pierces the trapezius to reach the skin. The supratrochlear, supraorbital, auriculotemporal, and infraorbital nerves supply anterior, temporal, and midface territories.'),
('6c96b500-090d-5970-a308-658d12019c08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Temporal region of scalp is supplied by:', '["Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Great auricular nerve","Greater occipital (C2)","Lesser occipital (C2-3)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)', 'The temporal region receives sensory supply from the auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the mandibular division (V3). The supraorbital, greater occipital, lesser occipital, and great auricular nerves supply the anterior, posterior, and auricular areas instead.'),
('b272551d-4203-5096-8cdd-b2427290d46e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which artery supplies the anterior scalp?', '["Supratrochlear and supraorbital arteries","Facial artery","Superficial temporal artery","Posterior auricular artery","Occipital artery"]'::jsonb, 'Supratrochlear and supraorbital arteries', 'The anterior scalp is supplied by the supratrochlear and supraorbital arteries, both branches of the ophthalmic artery from the internal carotid. The superficial temporal, posterior auricular, occipital, and facial arteries supply other regions.'),
('6e461b9d-a7e0-5c0e-bd33-2e9503075994'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The superficial temporal artery is a terminal branch of:', '["Maxillary artery","External carotid artery","Facial artery","Occipital artery","Internal carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The superficial temporal artery is one of the two terminal branches of the external carotid artery, the other being the maxillary artery. The facial, occipital, maxillary, and internal carotid arteries are not its source.'),
('4e229f44-7ce5-54b3-b2fe-727f51bc0d0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The occipital artery is a branch of:', '["External carotid artery","Subclavian artery","Internal carotid artery","Lingual artery","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The occipital artery arises from the posterior aspect of the external carotid artery. The subclavian, internal carotid, lingual, and facial arteries are not its origin.'),
('ecfb1beb-e46a-5a9a-a6b4-e031fd5fb547'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Lymphatic drainage of anterior scalp (above eyebrow) goes to:', '["Retropharyngeal nodes","Submandibular nodes","Submental nodes","Parotid (pre-auricular) nodes","Posterior auricular nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid (pre-auricular) nodes', 'The anterior scalp above the eyebrow drains to the parotid (pre-auricular) lymph nodes. The submental, submandibular, retropharyngeal, and posterior auricular nodes receive drainage from other territories.'),
('df96dd66-c0ff-5442-8f64-f8bc261182ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Lymphatic drainage of posterior scalp goes to:', '["Deep cervical nodes","Submental nodes","Parotid nodes","Submandibular nodes","Posterior auricular and occipital nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior auricular and occipital nodes', 'The posterior scalp drains to the posterior auricular and occipital lymph nodes, which then empty into the deep cervical nodes. The submental, submandibular, and parotid groups drain more anterior territories.'),
('f8540dbd-4693-591d-92fe-168e65292f69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which scalp layer is most adherent to underlying bone?', '["Loose areolar tissue","Connective tissue","Aponeurosis","Skin","Pericranium"]'::jsonb, 'Pericranium', 'Pericranium is firmly adherent to the outer table of the skull, which is why subperiosteal hematomas stay within a single bone at the sutures. The loose areolar layer instead permits the overlying layers to slide freely over pericranium.'),
('cb6969eb-1d29-5cd8-a318-6302993ed049'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The dangerous layer of scalp is also called layer number:', '["Layer 4 (loose areolar)","Layer 3 (aponeurosis)","Layer 5 (pericranium)","Layer 1 (skin)","Layer 2 (connective tissue)"]'::jsonb, 'Layer 4 (loose areolar)', 'Layer 4, the loose areolar tissue, is the dangerous layer because emissary veins pass through it connecting scalp veins to the intracranial dural sinuses. The other layers do not provide this route for spread of infection.'),
('d9a301a6-4940-516e-98f5-3e90ea99cf77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The scalp is most profusely supplied by which artery?', '["Supratrochlear artery","Occipital artery","Posterior auricular artery","Superficial temporal artery","Supraorbital artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial temporal artery', 'The superficial temporal artery is the largest and most profuse arterial supply to the scalp. The supratrochlear, supraorbital, occipital, and posterior auricular arteries make smaller contributions.'),
('06376114-8092-5445-9d98-1f214ffdb36b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Emissary veins of scalp connect:', '["Scalp lymphatics to subarachnoid space","Scalp veins to jugular veins","Scalp arteries to dural arteries","Scalp veins to intracranial dural sinuses","Scalp veins to facial veins"]'::jsonb, 'Scalp veins to intracranial dural sinuses', 'Emissary veins traverse the skull foramina and connect extracranial scalp veins to the intracranial dural venous sinuses. This pathway is why scalp infections can spread into the cranial cavity.'),
('d4ab2fa3-2b41-567c-b7c2-796f25a63ef3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which scalp layer contains the main blood vessels?', '["Skin (layer 1)","Loose areolar (layer 4)","Aponeurosis (layer 3)","Connective tissue (layer 2)","Pericranium (layer 5)"]'::jsonb, 'Connective tissue (layer 2)', 'The main vessels and nerves of the scalp run within the dense connective tissue (layer 2) just deep to the skin, which is why scalp wounds bleed profusely. The other layers do not carry the principal neurovascular supply.'),
('a668ec89-7a36-512e-8045-3393d2edde8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The epicranial aponeurosis is continuous with which muscle?', '["Platysma","Orbicularis oris","Occipitofrontalis","Temporalis","Masseter"]'::jsonb, 'Occipitofrontalis', 'The epicranial aponeurosis (galea) connects the frontal and occipital bellies of occipitofrontalis, acting as their intermediate tendon. Platysma, orbicularis oris, temporalis, and masseter are separate muscles with different attachments.'),
('51b9d836-4f9d-5951-8358-40c043a2125e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In scalp lacerations, why do wounds gape?', '["Pericranium adherence","Pull of occipitofrontalis on galea","Thick skin","Loose areolar tissue","High vascularity"]'::jsonb, 'Pull of occipitofrontalis on galea', 'When the aponeurosis is cut, the frontal and occipital bellies of occipitofrontalis pull the galea in opposite directions, causing the wound edges to gape widely. Pericranium adherence, thick skin, loose areolar tissue, and vascularity do not produce separation of the edges.'),
('272401e3-ec81-5eed-bd57-3f3dd821d22b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Where is the galea aponeurotica located?', '["Between frontal and occipital bellies of occipitofrontalis","Overlying the parotid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep to pericranium mediated by specific receptor activation","Between temporalis and masseter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial to skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between frontal and occipital bellies of occipitofrontalis', 'The galea aponeurotica lies between the frontal and occipital bellies of occipitofrontalis, joining them across the vertex. It does not overlie the parotid, lie deep to pericranium, sit between temporalis and masseter, or lie superficial to the skin.'),
('b189d5ce-235f-5ac5-8bd4-e7d9c7aa9bd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The occipitofrontalis muscle has how many bellies?', '["Two (frontal and occipital)","Three accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","One mediated by specific receptor activation","Four without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two (frontal and occipital)', 'Occipitofrontalis has two bellies, a frontal and an occipital, joined by the epicranial aponeurosis. The total number is two, so the other counts are incorrect.'),
('ee60893c-a064-5915-8bda-66c865e66e7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The frontal belly of occipitofrontalis originates from:', '["Zygomatic bone","Parietal bone","Temporal bone","Epicranial aponeurosis","Frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Epicranial aponeurosis', 'The frontal belly arises from the epicranial aponeurosis and inserts into the skin of the eyebrow. The zygomatic, parietal, temporal, and frontal bones are not its origin.'),
('e6fff523-73e1-5dad-be44-2e79e0e5b953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The occipital belly originates from:', '["External occipital protuberance","Mastoid process","Superior nuchal line of occipital bone","Temporal bone","Parietal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Superior nuchal line of occipital bone', 'The occipital belly of occipitofrontalis originates from the superior nuchal line of the occipital bone. Its neighbor the external occipital protuberance and the mastoid process are not attachments of this fleshy belly, and neither are the temporal or parietal bones.'),
('691cebc6-2d1d-5427-9d1b-5539294de26b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The epicranial aponeurosis is attached posteriorly to:', '["External occipital protuberance and superior nuchal line","Parietal bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Angle of mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Mastoid process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zygomatic arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External occipital protuberance and superior nuchal line', 'Posteriorly the epicranial aponeurosis attaches to the external occipital protuberance and the superior nuchal line of the occipital bone. The parietal bone, mandibular angle, mastoid process, and zygomatic arch are incorrect sites of attachment.'),
('1ed51e3f-1131-58a6-8327-48b65caa91f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The epicranial aponeurosis is attached laterally to:', '["Mastoid process","External auditory meatus","Angle of mandible","Superior temporal line","Zygomatic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Superior temporal line', 'Laterally the epicranial aponeurosis blends with the fascia along the superior temporal line, overlying the temporal fascia. The mastoid process, external auditory meatus, mandibular angle, and zygomatic arch are not its lateral attachment.'),
('634533bc-6560-5dcf-a7b1-76b7c1193383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Contraction of occipitofrontalis results in:', '["Turning the head resulting in decreased functional activity","Closing the eyes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulling scalp backward and elevating eyebrows","Depressing the eyebrows without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shrugging shoulders leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling scalp backward and elevating eyebrows', 'Contraction of occipitofrontalis pulls the scalp backward and elevates the eyebrows and forehead skin, producing the surprise expression. Closing the eyes, depressing the eyebrows, turning the head, and shrugging the shoulders involve other muscles.'),
('2c1c71cb-eaa5-5bfb-abdf-552a0bb630e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The epicranial aponeurosis is continuous with:', '["Parotid fascia","Masseteric fascia","Pretracheal fascia","Prevertebral fascia","Temporal fascia (superficial layer)"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal fascia (superficial layer)', 'Laterally the epicranial aponeurosis continues into the superficial layer of the temporal fascia, which attaches along the superior temporal line. The parotid, masseteric, pretracheal, and prevertebral fasciae are in different regions and are not continuous with the galea.'),
('e033ccab-fadb-5c10-8e46-669f80bff176'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The scalp has how many layers?', '["4 layers","5 layers","6 layers","3 layers","7 layers"]'::jsonb, '5 layers', 'The scalp has five layers, summarized by the mnemonic S-C-A-L-P: Skin, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar tissue, and Pericranium. Counts of three, four, six, or seven are incorrect.'),
('53954d4e-3eb6-5aa6-a835-87a92674f165'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which of the following acronyms correctly lists the five layers of the scalp from superficial to deep?', '["SCALE resulting in decreased functional activity","SCARP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CLASP mediated by specific receptor activation","The mnemonic S-C-A-L-P","LAPSC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mnemonic S-C-A-L-P', 'The acronym S-C-A-L-P correctly lists the five scalp layers from superficial to deep: Skin, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar tissue, and Pericranium. The other letter strings do not correspond to the actual layer sequence.'),
('01e5bcf4-7b4b-5a1d-b726-71e63ed8d962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which layer is the surgical plane for scalp flaps?', '["Pericranium (layer 5)","Loose areolar tissue (layer 4)","Connective tissue (layer 2)","Aponeurosis (layer 3)","Skin (layer 1)"]'::jsonb, 'Loose areolar tissue (layer 4)', 'The loose areolar layer provides an avascular plane where the superficial three layers can be elevated as a flap and where scalping injuries occur. The other layers are either too adherent to the skull or too vascular for clean separation.'),
('31b168e0-7313-5b99-9e73-b53df812990d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The loose areolar layer of scalp allows:', '["Drainage to lymph nodes","Spread of blood to orbit","Protection against infection","Fixation of scalp to skull","Free movement of scalp over skull"]'::jsonb, 'Free movement of scalp over skull', 'The loose areolar tissue permits the superficial three layers to move freely over the pericranium and skull. It does not fix the scalp, drain lymph, or protect against infection; in fact it is a loose bed through which infection can spread.'),
('35b517c7-26b6-54e8-a580-82778f9af19f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In scalping injuries, plane of separation is:', '["Between aponeurosis and pericranium","Between skin and connective","At skin level","At the pericranium","Loose areolar layer (layer 4)"]'::jsonb, 'Loose areolar layer (layer 4)', 'Scalping injuries separate through the loose areolar layer, deep to the galea and superficial to the pericranium, because this layer offers minimal resistance. The other tissue planes do not represent the usual site of separation.'),
('57a94c95-bd62-5333-8e81-29d89b9d4477'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Subperiosteal hematomas (cephalohematoma) are limited by:', '["Skin","Connective tissue","Aponeurosis","Suture lines","Loose areolar layer"]'::jsonb, 'Suture lines', 'Subperiosteal hematomas are confined to a single bone because the pericranium is firmly adherent at the cranial suture lines. The skin, connective tissue, aponeurosis, and loose areolar layer do not limit the hematoma.'),
('95de1926-173d-560d-b04e-defafcf56938'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Subgaleal hematomas can spread:', '["Over entire scalp (not limited by sutures)","Into the neck","Only within suture boundaries","Only in temporal region","Into the orbit"]'::jsonb, 'Over entire scalp (not limited by sutures)', 'Subgaleal (subaponeurotic) hematomas can spread over the entire scalp because the loose areolar layer is continuous and is not limited by the sutures. This contrasts with subperiosteal hematomas, which the sutures confine to one bone.'),
('bc5e3f50-8000-53b1-a4a1-68feb52990d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve provides sensory supply to the vertex of scalp?', '["Lesser occipital (C2) resulting in decreased functional activity","Infraorbital (V2) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supraorbital nerve (V1) and greater occipital (C2)","Great auricular (C2-3) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auriculotemporal (V3) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital nerve (V1) and greater occipital (C2)', 'The vertex receives dual sensory supply from the supraorbital nerve (V1) anteriorly and the greater occipital nerve (C2) posteriorly. The lesser occipital, great auricular, auriculotemporal, and infraorbital nerves serve other scalp and facial regions.'),
('32541eff-780d-5f67-9ba1-056bf94eb803'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The posterior auricular artery is a branch of:', '["Lingual artery","Occipital artery","Facial artery","Internal carotid artery","External carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The posterior auricular artery is a branch of the external carotid artery, which it leaves just above the angle of the mandible. The lingual, occipital, facial, and internal carotid arteries are not its source.'),
('c8bdffe1-42c5-58fd-91c1-ad4a298870a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The occipital artery anastomoses with which other artery?', '["Ascending pharyngeal","Facial artery","Deep cervical artery","Lingual artery","Superior thyroid"]'::jsonb, 'Deep cervical artery', 'The occipital artery anastomoses with the deep cervical artery, a branch of the costocervical trunk of the subclavian artery, connecting the external carotid and subclavian territories. The ascending pharyngeal, facial, lingual, and superior thyroid arteries are not the anastomotic partner.'),
('d7ca80fc-fb27-5a5e-b2a5-85d3dcd7b8c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The supraorbital artery is a branch of:', '["Superficial temporal","External carotid artery","Facial artery","Ophthalmic artery (ICA)","Maxillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery (ICA)', 'The supraorbital artery arises from the ophthalmic artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery. The superficial temporal, external carotid, facial, and maxillary arteries are all branches of other sources and are not correct.'),
('2d9aa7f1-bc2e-5060-b627-6ab1f2795821'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The supratrochlear artery is a branch of:', '["Facial artery","Ophthalmic artery (ICA)","Maxillary artery","Superficial temporal artery","External carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery (ICA)', 'The supratrochlear artery is a branch of the ophthalmic artery of the internal carotid system, supplying the medial forehead. The facial, maxillary, superficial temporal, and external carotid arteries do not give rise to it.'),
('70da4fd9-1f4b-5721-8862-bcadbc9962ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The scalp is supplied by how many pairs of arteries?', '["6 pairs","4 pairs","7 pairs","3 pairs","5 pairs"]'::jsonb, '5 pairs', 'The scalp is supplied by five pairs of arteries: supratrochlear, supraorbital, superficial temporal, posterior auricular, and occipital. The other pair counts are incorrect.'),
('452d6f9e-6f69-54d2-9d4d-dcd3167f808b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which of the following is NOT an artery of the scalp?', '["Facial artery","Supratrochlear artery","Supraorbital artery","Occipital artery","Superficial temporal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Facial artery', 'The facial artery supplies the face and is not a scalp artery. The supratrochlear, supraorbital, occipital, and superficial temporal arteries all contribute to the scalp''s arterial supply.'),
('4b7c6912-2136-5757-ba4a-23fec095be0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The veins of the scalp drain into:', '["Subclavian vein only resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial vein only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External jugular vein and dural sinuses via emissary veins","Maxillary vein only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Internal jugular only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External jugular vein and dural sinuses via emissary veins', 'Scalp veins drain chiefly into the external jugular vein, and emissary veins connect them to the intracranial dural sinuses. Draining to only a single vein such as the subclavian, facial, maxillary, or internal jugular does not describe the full drainage.'),
('76189d04-26da-5795-9d4a-200dc8ec4946'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The lymphatics of the scalp drain into which main groups?', '["Only axillary resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parotid nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only submandibular mediated by specific receptor activation","Only deep cervical without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parotid, posterior auricular, occipital, and submental nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid, posterior auricular, occipital, and submental nodes', 'Scalp lymphatics drain regionally into the parotid, posterior auricular, occipital, and submental (submandibular) groups, which then empty into the deep cervical nodes. The other options list only a single node group and are incomplete.'),
('76f17e56-f59d-5a0f-b26f-fdd12a4ee5dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The term ''dangerous area of scalp'' refers to:', '["Connective tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Aponeurotic layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Loose areolar tissue layer with emissary veins to dural sinuses","Pericranium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loose areolar tissue layer with emissary veins to dural sinuses', 'The dangerous area of the scalp is the loose areolar layer because emissary veins pass through it connecting scalp veins to the dural sinuses, allowing intracranial spread of infection. The connective tissue, aponeurotic layer, skin, and pericranium lack this venous link causing that risk.'),
('9c114695-9e94-5d8c-93fc-fb33e0bd1306'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which bone forms the frontal eminence?', '["Parietal bone","Zygomatic bone","Temporal bone","Occipital bone","Frontal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal bone', 'The frontal eminence is a rounded prominence on the frontal bone, lying above the superciliary arch on each side. The parietal, zygomatic, temporal, and occipital bones do not form it.'),
('41312523-98bd-5d3b-8c34-77c162f409b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporal lines on the skull are for attachment of:', '["Lateral pterygoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Masseter muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial pterygoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Buccinator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temporalis muscle and temporal fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Temporalis muscle and temporal fascia', 'The superior and inferior temporal lines give attachment to the temporalis muscle and the temporal fascia. The pterygoids, masseter, and buccinator attach to other parts of the skull and mandible, not to the temporal lines.'),
('0c59da55-2b9d-587a-b9d7-dab0b3df6858'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Orbicularis oris is supplied by which facial nerve branch?', '["Cervical branch","Mandibular branch","Zygomatic branch","Buccal branch","Temporal branch"]'::jsonb, 'Buccal branch', 'Orbicularis oris is innervated by the buccal branch of the facial nerve. The zygomatic, temporal, mandibular, and cervical branches supply other muscles of facial expression.'),
('839ff873-75c3-5da5-acce-702e7f994028'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Orbicularis oculi is supplied by which facial nerve branch?', '["Mandibular branch","Buccal branch","Zygomatic branch","Cervical branch","Temporal branch"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic branch', 'Orbicularis oculi is supplied by the zygomatic branch of the facial nerve, which also innervates the upper lip elevators. The buccal, temporal, mandibular, and cervical branches go to other facial muscles.'),
('86ac7df0-3fad-5862-8e12-e513f6faf856'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Frontalis is supplied by which branch of facial nerve?', '["Buccal branch","Temporal branch","Mandibular branch","Cervical branch","Zygomatic branch"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal branch', 'The frontal belly of occipitofrontalis (frontalis) is innervated by the temporal branch of the facial nerve, which courses across the zygomatic arch to the forehead. The buccal, zygomatic, mandibular, and cervical branches serve other muscles.'),
('68a7b67c-f60b-573a-a79e-6eae3b156ec9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Buccinator is supplied by which nerve?', '["Temporal branch","Zygomatic branch","Mandibular branch","Buccal nerve from V3","Buccal branch of facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Buccal branch of facial nerve', 'Buccinator receives its motor supply from the buccal branch of the facial nerve. The buccal nerve from V3 carries only sensory fibers and is not motor, while the temporal, zygomatic, and mandibular branches supply other muscles.'),
('465facd5-a256-5a3d-972e-62c1c4f400e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Platysma is supplied by which branch of facial nerve?', '["Buccal branch","Mandibular branch","Zygomatic branch","Cervical branch","Temporal branch"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical branch', 'The cervical branch of the facial nerve (VII) descends into the neck to supply the platysma. The other listed branches (buccal, mandibular, zygomatic, temporal) innervate muscles of the face above the mandibular margin.'),
('9bad9c67-db1d-5e2b-ad7a-fd83eeb9bbda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle is the main sphincter of the mouth?', '["Depressor labii inferioris","Mentalis","Levator labii superioris","Buccinator","Orbicularis oris"]'::jsonb, 'Orbicularis oris', 'Orbicularis oris is a circular muscle that encircles the oral aperture and acts as the mouth''s sphincter, closing and pursing the lips. The other options either elevate, depress, or anchor the lips rather than constricting the opening.'),
('12e01d8e-5cde-5276-affc-599624dce7f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle elevates the upper eyelid?', '["Levator palpebrae superioris","Frontalis","Superior rectus","Corrugator supercilii","Orbicularis oculi"]'::jsonb, 'Levator palpebrae superioris', 'Levator palpebrae superioris originates from the orbit and inserts into the tarsal plate and skin of the upper eyelid, raising it. Orbicularis oculi closes the eye, superior rectus moves the globe, and frontalis and corrugator supercilii act on the forehead and brow.'),
('364afe6d-ce1f-5d9c-a3f7-7e529fe15d69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Levator palpebrae superioris is supplied by:', '["Abducens nerve (VI)","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)","Facial nerve (VII)","Trochlear nerve (IV)","Trigeminal nerve (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III)', 'Levator palpebrae superioris is supplied by the oculomotor nerve (CN III); its smooth-muscle superior tarsal component is supplied by sympathetic fibres. The abducens and trochlear nerves supply only lateral and superior rectus respectively, and the facial nerve supplies the muscles of facial expression.'),
('b48b8b12-c3a8-510a-84d7-d992ed50f8e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Nasalis muscle functions to:', '["Wrinkle the nose","Depress nasal septum","Pull nose laterally","Compress and dilate the nasal aperture","Elevate the nose"]'::jsonb, 'Compress and dilate the nasal aperture', 'Nasalis has two parts: the transverse part compresses the nasal aperture and the alar part dilates it during forced inspiration. Wrinkling of the nose is produced by procerus, and depression of the nasal septum by depressor septi nasi.'),
('45e2f5ae-747f-5c86-b909-8fd6dce270fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Zygomaticus major is responsible for:', '["Chewing","Whistling","Blinking","Smiling","Frowning"]'::jsonb, 'Smiling', 'Zygomaticus major passes from the zygomatic bone to the angle of the mouth and draws it upward and laterally, producing a smile. Whistling uses buccinator, blinking uses orbicularis oculi, and chewing is governed by the muscles of mastication.'),
('ca2fde90-1686-5d19-b475-098e6aaa6ca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Mentalis muscle:', '["Closes the eye","Wrinkles the chin","Elevates the lip","Moves the ear","Depresses the lip"]'::jsonb, 'Wrinkles the chin', 'Mentalis arises from the mandible and attaches to the skin of the chin, elevating and protruding the lower lip and wrinkling the chin skin. Eye closure is performed by orbicularis oculi, and the other options are actions of different facial muscles.'),
('59c0dd0d-ee11-5b04-be15-c2214c10ec42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Corrugator supercilii is innervated by:', '["Oculomotor nerve","Facial nerve (zygomatic branch)","Frontal nerve (V1)","Facial nerve (buccal branch)","Facial nerve (temporal branch)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (temporal branch)', 'Corrugator supercilii, a muscle of facial expression, is supplied by the temporal branch of the facial nerve. The frontal nerve is purely sensory to the forehead, and the zygomatic and buccal branches supply more inferiorly placed facial muscles.'),
('05829104-f11c-52e2-9aed-15fabeff19cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Corrugator supercilii draws the eyebrows:', '["Laterally resulting in decreased functional activity","Upward accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Backward mediated by specific receptor activation","Medially and downward (frowning)","Downward only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medially and downward (frowning)', 'Corrugator supercilii arises from the medial end of the superciliary arch and pulls the eyebrows medially and downward, producing vertical glabellar wrinkles with frowning. It therefore opposes the lateral and upward pull of the other brow muscles.'),
('8070d278-ea52-53c7-bf15-2607506e4b53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Procerus muscle wrinkles the:', '["Chin","Forehead","Eyelid","Cheek","Root of the nose"]'::jsonb, 'Root of the nose', 'Procerus arises from the nasal bone and inserts into the skin over the glabella, producing transverse wrinkles at the root of the nose. The chin is wrinkled by mentalis, the forehead by frontalis, and the eyelid is closed by orbicularis oculi.'),
('ce1c9114-d689-5b5b-bf5e-66f05ab558e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The orbicularis oris is:', '["A triangular muscle","A quadrilateral muscle","A fan-shaped muscle","A circular sphincter muscle around the mouth","Two separate halves"]'::jsonb, 'A circular sphincter muscle around the mouth', 'Orbicularis oris is a circular band of muscle fibres encircling the oral aperture, functioning as the sphincter of the mouth. The fan-shaped design belongs to muscles such as temporalis, and the listed geometric shapes do not describe its circular architecture.'),
('cd4e99cd-1271-58e5-8223-6401a6d15b88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The levator labii superioris elevates the:', '["Nose","Cheek","Upper lip","Angle of mouth","Lower lip"]'::jsonb, 'Upper lip', 'Levator labii superioris originates from the maxilla near the infra-orbital margin and inserts into the upper lip, elevating it as in sneering. Elevation of the angle of mouth is performed by levator anguli oris and zygomaticus muscles.'),
('d863bce4-08b1-5215-a92c-93bbe96a3305'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Zygomaticus minor elevates:', '["Cheek","Upper lip","Lower lip","Angle of mouth","Nose"]'::jsonb, 'Upper lip', 'Zygomaticus minor passes from the zygomatic bone to the upper lip, elevating it during expressions such as a mild smile or crying. The angle of the mouth is elevated by zygomaticus major and levator anguli oris rather than by this muscle.'),
('ad3fea8e-1c47-5e0b-be04-d1e2c7343102'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Depressor labii inferioris depresses the:', '["Mandible","Angle of mouth","Cheek","Lower lip","Upper lip"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lip', 'Depressor labii inferioris arises from the mandible and inserts into the lower lip, pulling it downward and laterally. Depression of the mandible involves muscles such as digastric and lateral pterygoid, while the angle of mouth is pulled down by depressor anguli oris.'),
('120320e0-b2d9-5911-9827-334d92f19215'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Depressor anguli oris pulls the angle of mouth:', '["Medially resulting in decreased functional activity","Backward accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forward mediated by specific receptor activation","Upward and laterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Downward and laterally (sadness)"]'::jsonb, 'Downward and laterally (sadness)', 'Depressor anguli oris arises from the mandible and inserts at the angle of the mouth, drawing it downward and laterally to create the expression of sadness. Upward and lateral movement of the angle is produced by zygomaticus major and levator anguli oris.'),
('7b38b3f1-2355-5ab0-aea8-76092494c32d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The risorius muscle draws the angle of mouth:', '["Posteriorly","Laterally (grinning)","Medially","Upward","Downward"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally (grinning)', 'Risorius runs from the parotid fascia to the angle of the mouth and retracts it laterally, producing a grin. The downward component of mouth depression belongs to depressor anguli oris, and upward movement to the levator and zygomaticus muscles.'),
('dc549802-31e2-57a5-9d24-fed756c95edd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The levator anguli oris elevates the:', '["Nasal ala","Lower lip","Upper lip","Cheek","Angle of mouth"]'::jsonb, 'Angle of mouth', 'Levator anguli oris arises from the canine fossa of the maxilla and inserts at the angle of the mouth, elevating it during smiling. The upper lip is elevated by levator labii superioris, and the nasal ala by levator labii superioris alaeque nasi.'),
('b1757a4a-fb0d-5bea-826a-01de6dc427ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The nasolabial fold is formed by:', '["Zygomatic bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Masseter muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Insertion of facial muscles into the cheek skin","Parotid duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Insertion of facial muscles into the cheek skin', 'The nasolabial fold is created by the insertion of facial muscles, mainly zygomaticus major and levator labii superioris, into the skin of the upper lip and cheek. It is a soft-tissue landmark, not a bony, muscular, neural, or ductal structure itself.'),
('22a6e3d7-30e0-5caa-87dc-9386de8bd1a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Unlike skeletal muscles elsewhere, facial muscles:', '["Insert into skin rather than bone","Have dual innervation","Have nerve supply","Originate from skin","Insert into bone only"]'::jsonb, 'Insert into skin rather than bone', 'Muscles of facial expression characteristically insert into the dermis of the skin so their contraction moves the overlying skin, whereas ordinary skeletal muscles attach to bone. They arise from bone or fascia but are supplied, like all skeletal muscle, by a single motor nerve.'),
('f6231b44-bd17-5ae2-8c12-84e79ecba804'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The muscles of facial expression develop from:', '["2nd branchial arch mesoderm","4th branchial arch","1st branchial arch","Occipital myotomes","3rd branchial arch"]'::jsonb, '2nd branchial arch mesoderm', 'All muscles of facial expression derive from the mesoderm of the second branchial (hyoid) arch and are therefore supplied by the facial nerve. The first arch gives muscles of mastication supplied by V3, and occipital myotomes give the tongue musculature.'),
('a73ec56b-a62c-5243-94bc-efa036132e89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The muscle of the face that inserts into the modiolus:', '["Only zygomaticus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only mentalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only buccinator mediated by specific receptor activation","Only orbicularis oris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Several facial muscles including orbicularis oris, buccinator, levator anguli, depressor anguli"]'::jsonb, 'Several facial muscles including orbicularis oris, buccinator, levator anguli, depressor anguli', 'The modiolus at the angle of the mouth receives tendinous contributions from several muscles, including orbicularis oris, buccinator, levator anguli oris, depressor anguli oris, and zygomaticus major. It therefore serves as a central anchor for coordinated movements of the lips and cheek.'),
('0ace7fb1-d16d-594b-a9a4-2dd79034b396'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle is known as the kissing muscle?', '["Depressor anguli oris","Levator anguli oris","Orbicularis oris","Zygomaticus major","Risorius"]'::jsonb, 'Orbicularis oris', 'Orbicularis oris purses and protrudes the lips, actions needed for kissing, whistling, and speech. Zygomaticus major elevates the angle of the mouth for smiling, and risorius retracts it laterally for grinning.'),
('5fe6f7ea-1c19-5e95-abc4-d4128477ba0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle is the whistling muscle?', '["Buccinator","Mentalis","Risorius","Orbicularis oris","Zygomaticus"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator', 'Buccinator compresses the cheek against the teeth, holding air and food within the oral cavity, which is required for whistling and blowing. Orbicularis oris purses the lips, and levator anguli oris and zygomaticus elevate the angle of the mouth.'),
('3b93b028-8f18-5411-adce-9b65ea3f805c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Buccinator is also called:', '["Frowning muscle","Smiling muscle","Chewing muscle","Kissing muscle","Trumpeter''s muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Trumpeter''s muscle', 'Buccinator is known as the trumpeter''s muscle because it compresses the cheeks and forces air out, as when blowing a trumpet. Orbicularis oris is the kissing muscle, and corrugator supercilii is associated with frowning.'),
('03025974-4863-5630-9c7c-91ddd231a430'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid duct is at risk during extraction of which tooth?', '["Upper 3rd molar","Upper 2nd molar","Lower 3rd molar","Upper 1st molar","Lower 2nd molar"]'::jsonb, 'Upper 2nd molar', 'Stensen''s duct pierces the buccinator and opens into the oral cavity opposite the crown of the upper second molar tooth, so surgery near this tooth threatens the duct. The lower molars lie well below the level at which the duct enters the cheek.'),
('e6a71d1c-c1fe-5cb0-bc6a-7b655ac9e58b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In facial nerve palsy, which muscle is NOT affected?', '["Masseter","Buccinator","Orbicularis oculi","Orbicularis oris","Frontalis"]'::jsonb, 'Masseter', 'Masseter is a muscle of mastication supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3), so it is spared in facial nerve palsy. Buccinator, orbicularis oculi, orbicularis oris, and frontalis are facial-expression muscles supplied by CN VII and are paralysed.'),
('e9567f4a-d482-596e-b2de-facdf68dc173'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The muscle that raises the eyebrows is:', '["Temporalis","Frontalis (occipitofrontalis)","Orbicularis oculi","Procerus","Corrugator supercilii"]'::jsonb, 'Frontalis (occipitofrontalis)', 'Frontalis, the frontal belly of occipitofrontalis, inserts into the skin of the brow and raises the eyebrows, producing horizontal forehead wrinkles. Procerus wrinkles the nasal root and corrugator supercilii draws the brows medially, while temporalis moves the mandible.'),
('d1a8eb30-1d02-5a61-8fe4-277a7f3ec406'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle is a depressor of the mandible and is innervated by facial nerve?', '["Platysma","Lateral pterygoid","Digastric (anterior belly)","Mylohyoid","Geniohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Platysma', 'Platysma in the superficial fascia of the neck is supplied by the cervical branch of the facial nerve and can help depress the mandible by tensing the skin of the neck. Lateral pterygoid, digastric, mylohyoid, and geniohyoid depress the mandible but are innervated by V3 and XII, not CN VII.'),
('30497911-1d67-550d-a958-f1e3de16a525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The anterior belly of digastric is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal nerve (V3 via nerve to mylohyoid)","Glossopharyngeal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V3 via nerve to mylohyoid)', 'The anterior belly of digastric, a first-arch derivative, is supplied by the nerve to mylohyoid, a branch of the inferior alveolar nerve from V3. The posterior belly derives from the second arch and is supplied by the facial nerve.'),
('3827b048-f842-508e-b0cd-be712e0aaf17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The posterior belly of digastric is supplied by:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Trigeminal nerve (V3)","Vagus (X)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The posterior belly of digastric develops from the second branchial arch and receives its motor supply from the facial nerve via the digastric branch. Its companion anterior belly is supplied by V3 through the nerve to mylohyoid.'),
('b57c51fb-204d-553a-9c78-39714341e4bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Stylohyoid muscle is supplied by:', '["Glossopharyngeal","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve (VII)","Trigeminal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'Stylohyoid, a second-arch muscle, is supplied by the stylohyoid branch of the facial nerve. It elevates and retracts the hyoid bone during swallowing, working with the posterior belly of digastric.'),
('2cefad9e-bd37-584c-83ab-2cb610bcd57b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Mylohyoid muscle is supplied by:', '["Chorda tympani resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglossal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Lingual nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nerve to mylohyoid (from inferior alveolar, V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve to mylohyoid (from inferior alveolar, V3)', 'Mylohyoid, a first-arch muscle, is innervated by the nerve to mylohyoid, which branches from the inferior alveolar nerve of V3 just before it enters the mandibular foramen. The lingual, hypoglossal, facial, and chorda tympani nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('c18c265c-55a5-5d13-a2b8-724677392c11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve is at risk during parotid surgery causing facial paralysis?', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Trigeminal (V)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Vagus (X)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The facial nerve exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen and then runs within the parotid gland, dividing into its terminal branches, so it is vulnerable during parotidectomy. Injury to it paralyzes the ipsilateral muscles of facial expression.'),
('cb73bf9d-6fc2-55a4-bc94-b395eec59de2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid duct (Stensen''s duct) opens opposite the:', '["Frenulum of tongue","Sublingual caruncle","Upper 2nd molar tooth","Sublingual fold","Lower 2nd molar"]'::jsonb, 'Upper 2nd molar tooth', 'Stensen''s duct runs over the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens on a papilla opposite the crown of the upper second molar tooth. The sublingual and submandibular ducts open at the sublingual caruncle and fold in the floor of the mouth.'),
('bc84548c-1d25-58ee-80c9-5507efc05c5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve passes through the parotid gland?', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Accessory (XI)","Facial nerve (VII)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The facial nerve enters the posterior aspect of the parotid gland and divides within it into temporofacial and cervicofacial trunks that supply the facial muscles. The other named nerves do not traverse the gland, although the auriculotemporal nerve lies near it.'),
('1b181a96-8ece-50d5-a887-0c26051cf907'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parasympathetic secretomotor supply to parotid is from:', '["Glossopharyngeal via otic ganglion","Vagus nerve","Facial via submandibular ganglion","Facial via pterygopalatine ganglion","Trigeminal via ciliary ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal via otic ganglion', 'Preganglionic fibres of the glossopharyngeal nerve (lesser petrosal nerve) relay in the otic ganglion, and postganglionic fibres travel with the auriculotemporal nerve to stimulate the parotid. The submandibular and pterygopalatine ganglia serve the submandibular, sublingual, lacrimal, and nasal glands.'),
('f2d405b4-fcf9-58c2-90f1-bbfada9d2792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which is NOT a relation of the deep surface of parotid?', '["External carotid artery","Retromandibular vein","Submandibular gland","Styloid process","Internal jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The deep surface of the parotid is related to the external carotid artery, retromandibular vein, and internal jugular vein, and it sits against the styloid process and the carotid sheath contents. The submandibular gland lies in the submandibular triangle, quite separate from the parotid bed.'),
('32b54c1b-dc96-53ee-b627-edcf6a5198aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Retromandibular vein is formed by union of:', '["Posterior auricular and occipital","Superior thyroid and facial","Anterior and posterior facial","Facial and lingual veins","Superficial temporal and maxillary veins"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial temporal and maxillary veins', 'The retromandibular vein forms within the parotid gland from the union of the superficial temporal and maxillary veins and drains the deep venous drainage of the face. It divides into anterior and posterior divisions that empty into the facial and external jugular veins.'),
('5fad251b-b493-5496-a25b-e53d1bcf51f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which structures pass through the parotid gland?', '["Facial nerve and hypoglossal","Facial nerve and parotid duct","Facial nerve, retromandibular vein, external carotid artery","External carotid and parotid duct","Parotid duct and trigeminal"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve, retromandibular vein, external carotid artery', 'The parotid gland is transversed by the facial nerve, the retromandibular vein, and the external carotid artery, which are arranged from superficial to deep in that order. The parotid duct arises from the gland''s anterior border but does not pass through it.'),
('1973c9da-3c90-5a8f-a1b8-aaa6ee60161a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland extends between:', '["Ear and mastoid","Mastoid and angle of mandible","Zygomatic and temporal","Ramus and styloid","Zygomatic arch and angle of mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic arch and angle of mandible', 'The wedge-shaped parotid gland extends superiorly up to the zygomatic arch and inferiorly down to the angle of the mandible. It occupies the retromandibular fossa between the ramus of the mandible and the mastoid process and sternocleidomastoid.'),
('dcf81035-b046-5862-b28d-15ebeb510053'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotid capsule is derived from:', '["Prevertebral fascia","Pretracheal fascia","Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Carotid sheath","Superficial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The investing layer of the deep cervical fascia splits around the parotid gland to form its capsule, and it also envelops the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. The prevertebral, pretracheal, and carotid sheath layers surround deeper structures of the neck.'),
('02830a8d-fd86-51ab-8bf9-ac14567e6d51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial nerve divides the parotid into:', '["Superficial and deep lobes","Two equal halves","Anterior and posterior","Upper and lower lobes","Medial and lateral lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep lobes', 'As the facial nerve divides into its trunks and branches within the gland, it separates the parotid artificially into superficial and deep lobes because there is no true fascial septum between them. The retromandibular vein and external carotid artery lie deep to the nerve.'),
('1126d2be-ad5a-5328-9c89-a1a3c49c59ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid bed includes:', '["Sublingual gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Parotid duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Zygomatic arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Styloid process and its attached muscles","Submandibular gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Styloid process and its attached muscles', 'The parotid bed is formed by the styloid process with its attached muscles (stylohyoid, stylopharyngeus, styloglossus), the ramus of the mandible and masseter, and the mastoid process. The zygomatic arch and the parotid duct lie at the gland''s surface or anterior border, not its bed.'),
('196c038b-9114-513a-b761-7f7fa43c8dfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Auriculotemporal nerve is a branch of:', '["Mandibular division of trigeminal (V3)","Facial nerve","Maxillary division (V2)","Glossopharyngeal","Ophthalmic (V1)"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular division of trigeminal (V3)', 'The auriculotemporal nerve arises from the posterior division of the mandibular nerve (V3), passes through the upper part of the parotid, and supplies sensation to the auricle and temple. It also carries postganglionic secretomotor fibres from the otic ganglion to the parotid.'),
('bd88bb55-243d-56de-ac64-98ced6684abc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotid lymph nodes drain into:', '["Posterior auricular","Submandibular nodes","Axillary nodes","Submental nodes","Deep cervical lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Deep cervical lymph nodes', 'The superficial and deep parotid lymph nodes drain the gland and surrounding skin, and their efferents pass to the deep cervical lymph nodes, particularly the jugulodigastric group. The submandibular and submental nodes drain separate territories of the floor of the mouth and chin.'),
('1e223a7a-bc0f-58d0-b34d-d7940d4d4a29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotid gland produces which type of secretion?', '["Sweat","Sebaceous","Mucous","Mixed serous and mucous","Serous (watery with amylase)"]'::jsonb, 'Serous (watery with amylase)', 'The parotid is a purely serous gland whose watery secretion contains salivary amylase (ptyalin) for starch digestion. Its acinar cells are entirely serous, unlike the sublingual gland, which is predominantly mucous, and the submandibular, which is mixed.'),
('ed1af54e-5bd7-5d19-8b98-c0dc5035c52d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotid duct pierces which muscle to enter the mouth?', '["Buccinator","Zygomaticus major","Orbicularis oris","Masseter","Levator anguli oris"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator', 'Stensen''s duct crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens on a papilla opposite the upper second molar. Buccinator''s contraction during chewing helps expel saliva from the tube into the oral cavity.'),
('e08e0262-f3d5-56d3-9113-2e19c61b5c77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In mumps, which gland is typically affected?', '["Lacrimal gland","Parotid gland","Submandibular gland","Sublingual gland","Thyroid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid gland', 'Mumps is a viral infection that classically produces bilateral painful enlargement of the parotid glands. Other salivary glands may be involved less often, but the parotid is the hallmark site of involvement.'),
('fde42874-2e24-5f54-a7c6-21f8c9712420'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid duct is approximately:', '["8 cm","12 cm","2 cm","10 cm","5 cm (2 inches)"]'::jsonb, '5 cm (2 inches)', 'Stensen''s duct is about 5 cm (2 inches) long, running forward from the anterior border of the parotid gland across the masseter before piercing the buccinator. The other values overestimate or underestimate the duct''s typical length.'),
('2619d172-3e49-5912-b62c-08bd240b6883'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Superficial temporal artery is a branch of:', '["External carotid artery","Lingual artery","Internal carotid","Facial artery","Maxillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'Within the parotid gland the external carotid artery gives off its terminal branches, the superficial temporal and maxillary arteries. The superficial temporal artery then ascends in front of the tragus, where its pulse is felt, and its frontal and parietal branches supply the scalp.'),
('c99d2d57-2d20-5552-a6b8-37a3697a1127'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Sensory supply to parotid gland is from:', '["Infraorbital nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glossopharyngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Auriculotemporal and great auricular nerves","Supraorbital nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal and great auricular nerves', 'The parotid gland receives sensory innervation from the auriculotemporal nerve (a branch of the mandibular division of V3) and the great auricular nerve (C2-C3). The facial nerve supplies motor fibers to facial muscles, not sensation to the gland, and the other listed nerves serve different territories.'),
('8bb92def-913d-54fc-9156-527d915f9dc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Great auricular nerve is derived from:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Cervical plexus (C2-C3)","Brachial plexus","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical plexus (C2-C3)', 'The great auricular nerve arises from the cervical plexus, receiving fibers from C2 and C3. It supplies sensation to the skin over the angle of the mandible, the parotid region, and the ear, so the trigeminal, hypoglossal, and facial nerves are not sources.'),
('851a111f-bc89-51f8-b2df-dbdd8337274c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial nerve within parotid divides into how many terminal branches?', '["2 branches","6 branches","3 branches","5 branches","4 branches"]'::jsonb, '5 branches', 'Within the parotid gland the facial nerve splits into five terminal branches: temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical. These ramify through the gland to supply the muscles of facial expression.'),
('5aafa10b-b006-5623-8436-a820b02ec715'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which facial nerve branch supplies muscles of lower lip?', '["Buccal branch","Cervical branch","Zygomatic branch","Temporal branch","Marginal mandibular branch"]'::jsonb, 'Marginal mandibular branch', 'The marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve runs along the lower border of the mandible and innervates the muscles depressing the lower lip and chin (depressor labii inferioris, depressor anguli oris, mentalis). Injury here causes deviation of the mouth toward the opposite side.'),
('f491b9b2-f618-5ea8-93d8-3f3284a02014'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In Bell''s palsy, which nerve is affected?', '["Trochlear (IV)","Abducens (VI)","Facial nerve (VII)","Oculomotor (III)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'Bell''s palsy is an acute idiopathic lower motor neuron palsy of the facial nerve (VII), producing ipsilateral weakness of the muscles of facial expression. The trochlear, abducens, oculomotor, and trigeminal nerves are not the affected nerve in this condition.'),
('ab570a28-cf38-55a3-b744-d6a6717dd615'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Stylomastoid foramen transmits:', '["Facial and trigeminal","Facial nerve and stylomastoid artery","Facial and vagus","Facial nerve only","Facial and glossopharyngeal"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve and stylomastoid artery', 'The stylomastoid foramen transmits the facial nerve (VII) as it leaves the skull and the stylomastoid artery, a branch of the posterior auricular artery, which enters through the foramen. The trigeminal, vagus, and glossopharyngeal nerves exit or enter through other foramina.'),
('6a980b4b-5e92-5e24-bffe-505e5efa5dba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Temporal branch of facial nerve supplies:', '["Orbicularis oris resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontalis and upper orbicularis oculi","Mentalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Buccinator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontalis and upper orbicularis oculi', 'The temporal branch of the facial nerve innervates the frontalis, corrugator supercilii, and upper part of orbicularis oculi. Damage to this branch prevents wrinkling of the forehead. The other muscles listed receive innervation from different facial nerve branches.'),
('c4b3763a-9cea-5dea-a573-21c944dfa428'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Buccal branch of facial nerve supplies:', '["Orbicularis oculi resulting in decreased functional activity","Orbicularis oris, buccinator, upper lip elevators","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Depressor anguli oris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontalis and corrugator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Orbicularis oris, buccinator, upper lip elevators', 'The buccal branch of the facial nerve innervates the buccinator, orbicularis oris, and the elevators of the upper lip and angle of the mouth. Platysma is served by the cervical branch, and orbicularis oculi receives the zygomatic and temporal branches.'),
('80677181-0405-5349-955f-b2fc346a14c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Zygomatic branch of facial nerve supplies:', '["Buccinator","Mentalis","Platysma","Frontalis","Orbicularis oculi"]'::jsonb, 'Orbicularis oculi', 'The zygomatic branch of the facial nerve innervates orbicularis oculi, which is essential for eyelid closure. Damage results in lagophthalmos, the inability to close the eye. Buccinator, platysma, and mentalis are served by other branches.'),
('dc43de9f-2c51-5f48-afdd-223092bed0e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Marginal mandibular nerve is at risk during:', '["Submandibular gland surgery","Thyroid surgery","Mastoidectomy","Tonsillectomy","Parotid surgery"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland surgery', 'The marginal mandibular branch crosses the facial artery and vein near the lower border of the mandible and is at particular risk during submandibular gland dissection. Its injury causes ipsilateral weakness of the lower lip depressors with mouth deviation.'),
('9421a314-9005-50b4-ad8e-4c4f7b6ea4c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Cervical branch of facial nerve supplies:', '["Sternocleidomastoid","Platysma","Scalenes","Omohyoid","Trapezius"]'::jsonb, 'Platysma', 'The cervical branch of the facial nerve descends into the neck and innervates the platysma, the only muscle of facial expression that extends below the mandible. Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius are supplied by the spinal accessory nerve.'),
('6a613d24-0d94-5b7f-b565-dd88e6feb3de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotid papilla is located:', '["In the sublingual region","At the retromolar trigone","At opening of Wharton''s duct","Opposite upper 2nd molar on buccal mucosa","At the frenulum"]'::jsonb, 'Opposite upper 2nd molar on buccal mucosa', 'Stensen''s (parotid) duct opens at the parotid papilla on the buccal mucosa opposite the upper second molar. Wharton''s duct is the submandibular duct and opens in the floor of the mouth beside the frenulum.'),
('ef02437d-a98e-5b6c-975b-597b81303236'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers to parotid travel via:', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve","Chorda tympani","Auriculotemporal nerve","Facial nerve","Greater petrosal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve', 'Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the otic ganglion reach the parotid gland by hitching a ride on the auriculotemporal nerve. The preganglionic fibers travel in the glossopharyngeal nerve via the lesser petrosal nerve, but the final pathway to the gland is the auriculotemporal nerve.'),
('130aa35c-7cb5-5026-8775-0310adc6988d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Otic ganglion is functionally associated with:', '["Vagus (X)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Facial (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The otic ganglion is the parasympathetic relay for the parotid gland, receiving its preganglionic input from the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) via the lesser petrosal nerve. The facial nerve relays at the pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia instead.'),
('e79bba55-c9cb-5e0e-9f31-c1df6a03f17b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic for parotid originate from:', '["Nucleus ambiguus","Inferior salivatory nucleus","Edinger-Westphal nucleus","Superior salivatory nucleus","Dorsal vagal nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior salivatory nucleus', 'Preganglionic secretomotor fibers to the parotid arise from the inferior salivatory nucleus in the medulla and travel via the glossopharyngeal nerve. The superior salivatory nucleus supplies the submandibular and sublingual glands, while the Edinger-Westphal nucleus controls the pupillary sphincter.'),
('aab7c27c-b93d-5eac-b73b-91e8ae98bf17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Accessory parotid gland is located:', '["In temporal fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","In submandibular triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep to parotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior to parotid along duct near masseter","Within the parotid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to parotid along duct near masseter', 'An accessory parotid gland is a small salivary tissue mass lying anterior to the main parotid, just above Stensen''s duct along the masseter muscle. It may drain by a separate duct into the parotid duct and can be the site of isolated tumors.'),
('6510355d-7a9c-5864-87a6-56c19bf6e9f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Frey''s syndrome (gustatory sweating) is a complication of:', '["Submandibular surgery","Thyroid surgery","Parotid gland injury","Trigeminal nerve injury","Facial nerve injury"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid gland injury', 'Frey''s syndrome follows parotid surgery or injury when regenerating parasympathetic secretomotor fibers from the otic ganglion misconnect with sweat glands of the overlying skin. Eating then triggers sweating and flushing over the cheek.'),
('78f56f92-ef5a-5249-acd0-7914eaac6f94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which artery is closely related to deep surface of parotid?', '["Facial artery","External carotid artery","Lingual artery","Internal carotid artery","Superior thyroid artery"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The external carotid artery enters the parotid gland and divides within it into the maxillary and superficial temporal arteries. The retromandibular vein and facial nerve also pass through the gland, but the internal carotid artery remains medial to it.'),
('eb212e8d-d1ab-5e7a-a279-44393642d66c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial nerve trunk enters the parotid gland:', '["Anteroinferiorly","From the superficial surface","From the deep surface","Through the duct","Posterosuperiorly"]'::jsonb, 'Posterosuperiorly', 'The facial nerve exits the stylomastoid foramen and enters the posteromedial (posterosuperior) surface of the parotid gland before dividing into its five terminal branches. This relationship dictates the plane of dissection during parotidectomy.'),
('74819ec8-104d-5862-8871-6ad3e18303b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Within parotid, the facial nerve lies:', '["Above the vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Below the ECA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between vein and artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial to retromandibular vein and ECA","Deep to both leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial to retromandibular vein and ECA', 'Within the parotid gland the facial nerve is the most superficial of the three key structures, lying superficial to the retromandibular vein and the external carotid artery. Retromandibular vein and superficial temporal vein and maxillary artery are typically at risk in relation to these deeper vessels.'),
('9499917b-7cb7-5a88-ad2c-fbe623e5d83b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotid gland develops from:', '["Endodermal pharyngeal buds","Neural crest cells","Endoderm of foregut","Ectodermal buds from stomodeum","Mesodermal condensation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectodermal buds from stomodeum', 'The parotid gland develops from ectodermal buds arising from the invaginating stomodeum. The facial nerve becomes embedded as the gland grows around it. The submandibular and sublingual glands also derive from oral ectoderm, not endoderm.'),
('05a63a62-90cc-5f9d-9da4-49599391c743'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Stylomandibular ligament separates:', '["Parotid from sublingual","Parotid from thyroid","Masseter from temporalis","Submandibular from sublingual","Parotid from submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid from submandibular gland', 'The stylomandibular ligament runs from the styloid process to the angle of the mandible and separates the deep part of the parotid gland from the submandibular gland. This anatomic boundary prevents parotid swellings from extending directly into the submandibular region.'),
('a011bdfe-68fe-510c-a70b-2db72f246d9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which is NOT a branch of facial nerve within parotid?', '["Zygomatic branch","Temporal branch","Cervical branch","Marginal mandibular","Buccal nerve (from V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Buccal nerve (from V3)', 'The buccal nerve is a sensory branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3) and is not a facial nerve branch. The facial nerve''s terminal branches within the parotid are temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical.'),
('868d49bf-6cf7-5aeb-afcc-12e931187f72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial nerve branches from superior to inferior:', '["Zygomatic, temporal, buccal, mandibular, cervical","Cervical, mandibular, buccal, zygomatic, temporal","Temporal, buccal, zygomatic, mandibular, cervical","Temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, cervical","Temporal, zygomatic, cervical, mandibular, buccal"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, cervical', 'Listed from above downward, the five terminal branches of the facial nerve are temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical. This order is useful when planning surgical incisions and nerve mapping.'),
('59c075cb-433b-5cb5-8424-8ad1a5b9aa85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle is NOT supplied by the facial nerve?', '["Masseter","Platysma","Frontalis","Orbicularis oris","Buccinator"]'::jsonb, 'Masseter', 'Masseter is a muscle of mastication innervated by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3), not the facial nerve. Platysma, frontalis, orbicularis oris, and buccinator are all muscles of facial expression supplied by the facial nerve.'),
('54832bbc-2e33-536b-b9c8-843fbb319e41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial nerve has how many functional components?', '["1 (only motor)","4 (motor, sensory, parasympathetic, sympathetic)","2 (motor and sensory)","3 (motor, parasympathetic, special sensory)","5"]'::jsonb, '3 (motor, parasympathetic, special sensory)', 'The facial nerve carries three functional components: branchial motor fibers to facial muscles, parasympathetic secretomotor fibers (via chorda tympani and greater petrosal nerve), and special sensory fibers for taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. Sympathetic fibers are not a component of the facial nerve.'),
('5ae6c071-ff70-5a35-86f3-87b7d44ccff2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Chorda tympani is a branch of:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Lingual nerve","Facial nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve', 'The chorda tympani arises from the facial nerve within the facial canal, carries taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular ganglion. It subsequently joins the lingual nerve.'),
('e2f83de0-48e0-5a15-b8f5-bd29dcd7b60a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Chorda tympani joins which nerve?', '["Buccal nerve","Mental nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve","Lingual nerve","Inferior alveolar nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve', 'After leaving the facial canal via the petrotympanic fissure, the chorda tympani descends and joins the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa. It uses the lingual nerve as a carrier to reach the submandibular ganglion and anterior tongue.'),
('e42c323e-87c1-51b7-b95c-49fad2d62b63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Greater petrosal nerve is a branch of:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Facial nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve', 'The greater petrosal nerve is a branch of the facial nerve carrying preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion, supplying lacrimal, nasal, and palatal glands. Its relation to the genu of the facial canal explains loss of lacrimation with proximal VII lesions.'),
('cc7beb12-ac2c-5adf-b9ba-14c2cfaf64bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Nerve to stapedius is a branch of:', '["Facial nerve","Cochlear nerve","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve', 'The nerve to the stapedius muscle is given off by the facial nerve within the facial canal just distal to the geniculate ganglion. Stapedius dampens stapes movement; its paralysis causes hyperacusis from unimpeded sound transmission.'),
('c8a75fd9-57c2-52d5-8675-cb56811cecf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Damage to facial nerve proximal to geniculate ganglion causes:', '["Dry eyes only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only hyperacusis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only loss of taste mediated by specific receptor activation","Only facial palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of taste, dry mouth, hyperacusis, facial palsy"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of taste, dry mouth, hyperacusis, facial palsy', 'A lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion interrupts all facial nerve functions: motor fibers to facial muscles, greater petrosal parasympathetic fibers (dry eye and altered nasal/palatal secretion), chorda tympani taste, and the nerve to stapedius (hyperacusis). More distal lesions spare the earlier branches and produce fewer deficits.'),
('3c67310c-4d67-5b71-848e-c5b7543aff19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial nerve injury at stylomastoid foramen causes:', '["Complete loss","Motor loss plus hyperacusis","Only motor loss of facial muscles","Loss of taste plus motor loss","Dry eyes and loss of taste"]'::jsonb, 'Only motor loss of facial muscles', 'At the stylomastoid foramen all branches (chorda tympani, greater petrosal, nerve to stapedius) have already left the facial nerve, so injury there produces pure ipsilateral paralysis of the muscles of facial expression without taste, lacrimation, or hearing changes.'),
('9599398b-9471-5d26-bc29-50cc35791e62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The facial nerve branching pattern within parotid is called:', '["Lumbar plexus","Pes anserinus (goose foot)","Brachial plexus","Cauda equina","Sacral plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Pes anserinus (goose foot)', 'The fan-shaped division of the facial nerve into its five terminal branches within the parotid gland is called the pes anserinus, meaning goose foot. The other named plexuses refer to unrelated spinal nerve arrangements.'),
('6c43a013-69a5-5fb8-89e6-a72c6b0f8006'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Deep lobe of parotid may present as:', '["A submandibular swelling","A cheek swelling","A parapharyngeal mass","A preauricular sinus","A temporal swelling"]'::jsonb, 'A parapharyngeal mass', 'Because the deep lobe of the parotid lies medial to the mandible and lateral pharyngeal wall, tumors arising there can present as a parapharyngeal space mass that displaces the lateral pharyngeal wall medially. A cheek swelling is more typical of accessory parotid or buccal pathology.'),
('542689ae-0463-5361-b37f-e3f54851a572'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Most common benign parotid tumor:', '["Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Warthin''s tumor","Squamous cell carcinoma","Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Pleomorphic adenoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pleomorphic adenoma', 'Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common benign tumor of the parotid gland, accounting for about 80% of parotid neoplasms. Warthin''s tumor is also benign but less common, whereas mucoepidermoid and adenoid cystic carcinoma are malignant.'),
('7feaa1cf-9022-5615-bf44-b5162d52c0e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Damage to marginal mandibular nerve causes:', '["Ptosis","Inability to close eye","Loss of frontalis","Hyperacusis","Deviation of mouth to opposite side"]'::jsonb, 'Deviation of mouth to opposite side', 'The marginal mandibular nerve supplies the depressor anguli oris, depressor labii inferioris, and mentalis. When paralyzed, the intact contralateral draw pulls the mouth toward the opposite side, and the patient cannot depress the lower lip on the affected side.'),
('5a3c4dd7-0a1e-5655-b720-2fc59d75826f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Damage to temporal branch causes:', '["Inability to wrinkle forehead","Deviation of mouth","Loss of buccinator","Inability to close eye","Loss of platysma"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to wrinkle forehead', 'The temporal branch supplies frontalis (and upper orbicularis oculi), so its injury prevents forehead wrinkling on the affected side. Deviation of the mouth and cheek weakness result from marginal mandibular and buccal branch damage instead.'),
('8d284153-a194-56a6-b61c-4dc66b48dd1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Damage to zygomatic branch causes:', '["Inability to close eye (lagophthalmos)","Loss of buccinator","Inability to wrinkle forehead","Drooping mouth","Deviation of chin"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to close eye (lagophthalmos)', 'The zygomatic branch innervates orbicularis oculi; its injury causes inability to close the eye, termed lagophthalmos. Inability to wrinkle the forehead reflects temporal branch loss, while mouth deviation reflects marginal mandibular injury.'),
('53473f56-3902-5699-b913-be144c9101f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Bell''s palsy is characterized by:', '["Gradual onset weakness","Bilateral facial paralysis","Facial spasm","Sudden unilateral LMN facial paralysis","Hemifacial spasm"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden unilateral LMN facial paralysis', 'Bell''s palsy is an acute, usually sudden, self-limited lower motor neuron paralysis of one side of the face involving all facial muscles. Hemifacial spasm and bilateral involvement are not the characteristic presentation.'),
('79e344fc-bfde-5271-8b8f-94371c080383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'UMN facial palsy spares forehead because:', '["Bilateral cortical innervation to upper face","Forehead has muscles","Different nerve supply","Crossover at brainstem","Forehead muscles are weaker"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral cortical innervation to upper face', 'The upper facial muscles (frontalis and orbicularis oculi) receive bilateral cortical input, so a unilateral upper motor neuron lesion spares the forehead. The lower face has predominantly contralateral innervation and is therefore affected.'),
('36dd7843-d753-5c3c-851a-d22b605e0331'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In UMN facial palsy, which muscles are affected?', '["Ipsilateral entire face","Bilateral face","Contralateral entire face","Ipsilateral lower face only","Contralateral lower face"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral lower face', 'In upper motor neuron facial palsy, the contralateral lower face is paralyzed because the lower facial nuclei receive mostly crossed corticobulbar fibers, while the upper face is spared due to bilateral innervation. The paralysis appears on the side opposite the lesion.'),
('c9338162-ca9e-5058-bcf7-7f5c7e382d14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In LMN facial palsy, which muscles are affected?', '["Forehead only","Ipsilateral lower face only","Contralateral lower face","Contralateral entire face","Ipsilateral entire face (upper and lower)"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral entire face (upper and lower)', 'Lower motor neuron facial palsy involves all muscles of facial expression on the same (ipsilateral) side, including the forehead, because the entire facial nucleus is affected. The forehead is spared only in upper motor neuron lesions, not in lower motor neuron lesions.'),
('0b89bb8b-2bb5-5e80-a54e-16620338601b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome is:', '["Parotid tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Bell''s palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Herpes zoster of geniculate ganglion with facial palsy","TMJ dislocation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mumps parotitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herpes zoster of geniculate ganglion with facial palsy', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome is herpes zoster infection of the geniculate ganglion causing facial palsy along with vesicles in the ear and often hearing disturbance. It differs from Bell''s palsy by the presence of herpetic vesicles.'),
('849d3564-8c85-5052-abde-c8670a2b3602'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Danger triangle of face extends from:', '["Eye to ear","Nose to chin","Angle of mouth to bridge of nose","Temple to mouth","Chin to nose"]'::jsonb, 'Angle of mouth to bridge of nose', 'The danger triangle of the face lies between the angle of the mouth and the bridge of the nose. Infections in this region are hazardous because its veins communicate with the cavernous sinus, allowing intracranial spread.'),
('4b907a0d-eb10-590f-906b-d6cdc790a963'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Risk in danger triangle of face:', '["Lymphatic spread resulting in decreased functional activity","Parotid infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bleeding mediated by specific receptor activation","Nerve damage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spread of infection to cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Spread of infection to cavernous sinus', 'The main risk in the danger triangle is retrograde spread of infection through valveless facial veins to the cavernous sinus, causing cavernous sinus thrombosis. This danger explains caution in manipulating lesions in this region.'),
('7da28cee-8b9c-5e1b-8341-d4ad5239bb68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial vein communicates with cavernous sinus via:', '["Pterygoid plexus","Superior petrosal sinus","Superior ophthalmic vein","Emissary veins","Inferior ophthalmic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Superior ophthalmic vein', 'The facial vein connects to the cavernous sinus chiefly through the superior ophthalmic vein, which drains the orbit into the cavernous sinus. The angular vein at the medial canthus is the key anastomotic channel that makes the danger triangle risky.'),
('f3267baf-f82c-50c3-8abf-22ad13d9e483'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Facial vein is devoid of valves, allowing:', '["Lymphatic drainage resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF drainage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arterial mixing mediated by specific receptor activation","Retrograde spread of infection to cavernous sinus","Forward flow only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retrograde spread of infection to cavernous sinus', 'Because the facial vein lacks valves, blood can flow in either direction, permitting retrograde spread of infection from the face into the cavernous sinus. This is the basis of the danger triangle of the face.'),
('1f80a5b6-d3b0-560c-9962-fdbadec14bf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The chorda tympani carries taste from:', '["Posterior 1/3 of tongue","Entire tongue","Palate","Anterior 2/3 of tongue","Floor of mouth"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 of tongue', 'The chorda tympani conveys special sensory taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue via the lingual nerve. The posterior one-third of the tongue is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve.'),
('38907e46-d3d8-5bd6-a487-466a8cb795e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Taste to posterior 1/3 of tongue is from:', '["Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Chorda tympani","Vagus nerve (X)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)', 'Taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue is carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). The chorda tympani of the facial nerve serves the anterior two-thirds, and the vagus nerve supplies taste to the epiglottic region.'),
('1171f78f-96c1-5822-ae56-e2656b823a60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The facial nerve after exiting stylomastoid foramen gives which branch before entering parotid?', '["Great auricular nerve","Lingual nerve","Marginal mandibular","Posterior auricular nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior auricular nerve', 'Immediately after leaving the stylomastoid foramen, the facial nerve gives off the posterior auricular branch (supplying the auricular muscles and occipitalis) before entering the parotid gland. The great auricular and auriculotemporal nerves are sensory, not branches of the facial nerve.'),
('aa814eb9-68db-50b7-ae67-b80fac85ae76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The posterior auricular nerve supplies:', '["Intrinsic auricular muscles and occipitalis","Frontalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Buccinator mediated by specific receptor activation","Platysma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orbicularis oculi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic auricular muscles and occipitalis', 'The posterior auricular branch of the facial nerve ascends behind the ear to supply the intrinsic auricular muscles and the occipital belly of occipitofrontalis. The other listed muscles are supplied by other facial nerve branches (frontalis via temporal, buccinator via buccal, platysma via cervical, orbicularis oculi via temporal and zygomatic).'),
('8146c4ba-f148-5a78-9bd0-0dcf00eceb5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The nerve to mylohyoid is a branch of:', '["Masseteric nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Lingual nerve","Facial nerve","Inferior alveolar nerve (V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior alveolar nerve (V3)', 'The nerve to mylohyoid arises from the inferior alveolar nerve before it enters the mandibular canal and runs forward to the mylohyoid muscle and anterior belly of digastric. The masseteric, lingual, and hypoglossal nerves and the facial nerve do not give origin to this branch.'),
('54b291e5-9b3b-5010-b221-35995b344c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The buccal nerve (long buccal) is a branch of:', '["Mandibular division of trigeminal (V3)","Facial nerve","Maxillary division (V2)","Buccal branch of facial nerve","Lingual nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular division of trigeminal (V3)', 'The long buccal nerve arises from the anterior division of the mandibular nerve (V3) and supplies sensation to the skin and mucosa of the cheek. The buccal branch of the facial nerve is motor to buccinator, while V2 and the lingual nerve have different distributions and do not give the long buccal nerve.'),
('bf03a0c3-69de-57b3-92ca-3b3b1b0badbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid duct is also known as:', '["Wharton''s duct","Rivinus duct","Major sublingual duct","Stensen''s duct","Bartholin''s duct"]'::jsonb, 'Stensen''s duct', 'The parotid duct is classically called Stensen''s duct; it emerges from the gland, crosses masseter, pierces buccinator, and opens opposite the upper second molar. Wharton''s duct drains the submandibular gland, and the other named ducts belong to other salivary glands.'),
('218fd831-cf9c-5621-9de7-b0dbb64f06af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Wharton''s duct is the duct of:', '["Lacrimal gland","Minor salivary glands","Sublingual gland","Submandibular gland","Parotid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'Wharton''s duct drains the submandibular gland and opens at the sublingual caruncle beside the frenulum of the tongue. The sublingual gland drains through small ducts (Rivinus, Bartholin), while the parotid gland drains through Stensen''s duct.'),
('e4470335-b1ed-5416-9e8c-1a562f5371fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The submandibular duct opens at:', '["Incisive papilla","Sublingual fold","Parotid papilla","Sublingual caruncle","Opposite upper 2nd molar"]'::jsonb, 'Sublingual caruncle', 'The submandibular (Wharton''s) duct terminates at the sublingual caruncle, a small papilla on either side of the frenulum of the tongue. The parotid duct opens on the parotid papilla opposite the upper second molar, and the sublingual fold overlies the ducts of the sublingual gland.'),
('5bf388a5-8664-5769-9875-6082380bb4c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid fascia is derived from:', '["Prevertebral fascia","Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Buccopharyngeal fascia","Pretracheal fascia","Superficial cervical fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The capsule of the parotid gland is formed by the investing layer of the deep cervical fascia, which splits to enclose the gland completely. The prevertebral, pretracheal, and buccopharyngeal fasciae invest other compartments, and the superficial cervical fascia does not form this discrete capsule.'),
('8e8eedd5-c54b-514c-bac2-3b382354efb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland weighs approximately:', '["100-150 g","50-100 g","15-30 g","200 g","5-10 g"]'::jsonb, '15-30 g', 'The parotid is the largest salivary gland and typically weighs about 15-30 g. The submandibular and sublingual glands weigh less, so the larger figures do not reflect normal parotid weight.'),
('fcf4a6e1-9423-5e9e-af8e-edfb4e2a1cde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid region''s superior boundary is:', '["TMJ","Auricle","External auditory meatus","Zygomatic arch","Angle of mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic arch', 'The parotid region is bounded above by the zygomatic arch and below by the angle of the mandible. The auricle, external auditory meatus, and TMJ lie at or near its margins but are not its superior limit.'),
('f28831ed-eb6f-5835-8c69-232018b4232f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid region''s inferior boundary is:', '["Cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage","Clavicle","Angle of mandible","Hyoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Angle of mandible', 'The parotid region is bounded inferiorly by the angle of the mandible, with the zygomatic arch as its superior limit. The cricoid and thyroid cartilages, hyoid bone, and clavicle lie well below this region.'),
('d37062db-4e95-587a-b0c9-5ff9ad34e02b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid duct is about:', '["8 cm long and 2 mm diameter","2 cm long and 5 mm diameter","10 cm long","3 cm long","5 cm long and 3 mm in diameter"]'::jsonb, '5 cm long and 3 mm in diameter', 'Stensen''s duct is approximately 5 cm long and about 3 mm in diameter, running forward from the gland over masseter before piercing buccinator. The longer lengths and larger diameters do not match the duct''s known dimensions.'),
('b38e16c4-34c5-5cc9-9b76-a03ab3aa9691'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Injury to which nerve causes loss of taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue?', '["Chorda tympani (facial nerve)","Vagus nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Lingual nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Chorda tympani (facial nerve)', 'The chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (VII), carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, traveling temporarily within the lingual nerve. The glossopharyngeal nerve serves the posterior one-third, while the lingual nerve itself carries only general sensation.'),
('480e082f-dc65-57be-9d08-c89099570237'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The submandibular ganglion is suspended from:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Inferior alveolar nerve","Lingual nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual nerve', 'The submandibular ganglion hangs from the inferior border of the lingual nerve. The hypoglossal, facial, inferior alveolar, and auriculotemporal nerves do not suspend this ganglion.'),
('48671ac6-695d-56f2-9bd5-d89d347f093e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The submandibular ganglion supplies:', '["Sympathetic to tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","Preganglionic parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory to gingiva mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor to tongue muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Postganglionic parasympathetic to submandibular and sublingual glands"]'::jsonb, 'Postganglionic parasympathetic to submandibular and sublingual glands', 'The submandibular ganglion issues postganglionic parasympathetic fibers that reach the submandibular and sublingual glands and stimulate secretion. It does not provide sympathetic, sensory, or motor supply to the tongue.'),
('0fd3c5a6-75f5-55a4-b06b-4220b30143f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Preganglionic fibers for submandibular ganglion come from:', '["Dorsal vagal nucleus","Inferior salivatory nucleus via glossopharyngeal","Edinger-Westphal nucleus","Superior salivatory nucleus via chorda tympani","Nucleus ambiguus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior salivatory nucleus via chorda tympani', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers begin in the superior salivatory nucleus of the pons and travel through the facial nerve and chorda tympani to synapse in the submandibular ganglion. The inferior salivatory nucleus and Edinger-Westphal nucleus serve other ganglia.'),
('4c7af606-93f4-58a2-a5cd-2f1ac626a679'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The superior salivatory nucleus is located in:', '["Cerebellum","Midbrain","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Pons', 'The superior salivatory nucleus lies in the pons and sends fibers through the facial nerve to the submandibular, sublingual, and lacrimal glands. The inferior salivatory nucleus in the medulla serves the parotid, and the cerebellum, midbrain, and spinal cord do not contain this nucleus.'),
('20245114-c2e5-5be2-993a-cf8060643951'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The inferior salivatory nucleus is located in:', '["Cerebellum","Medulla oblongata","Pons","Hypothalamus","Midbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata', 'The inferior salivatory nucleus is situated in the medulla oblongata; its fibers pass through the glossopharyngeal and lesser petrosal nerves to the otic ganglion for parotid secretion. The superior salivatory nucleus belongs to the pons, and the other sites do not house salivatory nuclei.'),
('80af3b98-25ac-5b5c-a6a5-481b00f85275'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The otic ganglion is located:', '["In infratemporal fossa near lingual nerve","In pterygopalatine fossa","Just below foramen ovale on medial side of mandibular nerve","In the parotid gland","In submandibular triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Just below foramen ovale on medial side of mandibular nerve', 'The otic ganglion lies in the infratemporal fossa just below the foramen ovale, on the medial side of the mandibular nerve. It is not found in the pterygopalatine fossa, parotid gland, or submandibular triangle, and the lingual nerve suspension describes the submandibular ganglion.'),
('81840f0a-17cf-5359-a5f5-96607f99c872'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The otic ganglion receives parasympathetic root from:', '["Greater petrosal (from facial) resulting in decreased functional activity","Lesser petrosal nerve (from glossopharyngeal)","Vagus nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglossal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser petrosal nerve (from glossopharyngeal)', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers reach the otic ganglion through the lesser petrosal nerve, which continues fibers from the inferior salivatory nucleus carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve. The greater petrosal nerve serves the pterygopalatine ganglion, and the other nerves listed carry different functions.'),
('28439919-524a-5c22-b4cb-febcf2537334'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The otic ganglion gives fibers to parotid via:', '["Great auricular nerve","Facial nerve","Lingual nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve","Lesser petrosal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve', 'Postganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the otic ganglion join the auriculotemporal nerve, which carries them to the parotid gland for secretomotor function. The great auricular, facial, and lingual nerves, as well as the lesser petrosal (the preganglionic route), do not deliver these fibers.'),
('4b75c2d6-ee62-5d1d-a0bf-aa284e451694'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The ciliary ganglion is associated with:', '["Oculomotor nerve (III)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Trigeminal (V)","Vagus (X)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (III)', 'The ciliary ganglion is a parasympathetic ganglion whose preganglionic root comes from the oculomotor nerve (III), supplying constrictor pupillae and ciliary muscle. The glossopharyngeal, trigeminal, vagus, and facial nerves are associated with other ganglia.'),
('0181c3a6-817b-5e4e-9b8b-3e3d365c60bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion is associated with:', '["Vagus nerve","Facial nerve via greater petrosal","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Oculomotor nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve via greater petrosal', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion receives its preganglionic parasympathetic root from the facial nerve through the greater petrosal nerve. The glossopharyngeal, oculomotor, trigeminal, and vagus nerves do not provide this innervation.'),
('0bd2a48e-c13f-5910-ac03-798e586e36a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The pterygopalatine ganglion supplies:', '["Submandibular gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Tongue taste buds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lacrimal gland and nasal/palatine glands","Sublingual gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parotid gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lacrimal gland and nasal/palatine glands', 'Postganglionic fibers from the pterygopalatine ganglion reach the lacrimal gland and the glands of the nasal cavity and palate, promoting lacrimation and nasal secretion. The submandibular, sublingual, and parotid glands are innervated by other ganglia.'),
('fa787728-3eee-58f0-bcd6-dadd563c600d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The facial nerve is the nerve of which branchial arch?', '["4th branchial arch","1st branchial arch","2nd branchial arch","3rd branchial arch","6th arch"]'::jsonb, '2nd branchial arch', 'The facial nerve is the nerve of the second branchial arch and supplies the muscles derived from it, including the muscles of facial expression and stapedius. The trigeminal nerve is the first arch nerve, the glossopharyngeal the third, and the vagus supplies the fourth-sixth arches.'),
('2fcf343d-6738-54f5-9303-e05b8eae80da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The nerve of first branchial arch is:', '["Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Facial nerve (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Vagus (X)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V)', 'The trigeminal nerve is the nerve of the first branchial arch and supplies the muscles of mastication and other first-arch derivatives. The facial, glossopharyngeal, and vagus nerves belong to the second, third, and fourth-sixth arches respectively.'),
('f0967fd1-840a-5ed4-abbf-269834c7b519'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland secretion is controlled by:', '["Somatic motor (CN VII)","Sympathetic","Parasympathetic (CN VII)","Parasympathetic (CN IX)","Enteric nervous system"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (CN IX)', 'Parotid secretion is chiefly under parasympathetic control from the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) via the otic ganglion and auriculotemporal nerve. The facial nerve (VII) supplies the parasympathetic drive to other salivary glands, and sympathetic input only modulates secretion.'),
('34974790-595f-58cb-9859-9e267ef6808c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Atropine reduces parotid secretion because:', '["It blocks muscarinic receptors of parasympathetic","It blocks nicotinic receptors","It inhibits acetylcholinesterase","It blocks adrenergic receptors","It stimulates sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'It blocks muscarinic receptors of parasympathetic', 'Atropine is a muscarinic antagonist, so it blocks the parasympathetic secretomotor pathway to the parotid and thereby reduces salivation. Blocking nicotinic or adrenergic receptors or inhibiting acetylcholinesterase would not produce this effect.'),
('7b727cb7-fd6a-5e14-8042-e08748fbc0e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parasympathetic stimulation of parotid produces:', '["Thick mucoid secretion","Vasoconstriction","Profuse watery secretion","secretion","Only enzyme secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Profuse watery secretion', 'Parasympathetic stimulation of the parotid produces abundant, watery saliva that aids digestion and swallowing. Thick mucoid secretion and vasoconstriction reflect sympathetic influence rather than parasympathetic activity.'),
('ae2451a0-8e4b-5578-a466-0ec383db7476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Sympathetic stimulation of parotid causes:', '["Only vasodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased watery secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vasoconstriction and thick mucoid secretion","effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibition of secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction and thick mucoid secretion', 'Sympathetic stimulation constricts parotid blood vessels and produces a small volume of thick, mucoid saliva. Profuse watery secretion and vasodilation are parasympathetic effects, and the sympathetic response does not simply inhibit secretion.'),
('f77ee714-f92b-587b-aab5-de078b1de472'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland is the only salivary gland that:', '["Contains lymph nodes within its substance","Develops from ectoderm","Has a capsule","Has a duct into mouth","Produces purely serous"]'::jsonb, 'Contains lymph nodes within its substance', 'The parotid is unique among salivary glands in containing lymph nodes within its substance (intraglandular nodes), a clinically significant route for lymphatic drainage and spread. The other listed features are either shared with other glands or apply elsewhere.'),
('6ef7f71a-fec9-5f97-9b20-ec34a9b11f8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporal fossa contains which muscle?', '["Buccinator","Temporalis","Masseter","Lateral pterygoid","Medial pterygoid"]'::jsonb, 'Temporalis', 'The temporal fossa lodges the temporalis muscle, which arises from its floor and converges onto the coronoid process of the mandible. Masseter lies over the lateral surface of the ramus, and the pterygoid muscles occupy the infratemporal fossa.'),
('049a4f54-7825-571c-bd68-f7dd1ac249e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporal fascia covers:', '["Parotid gland","Temporalis muscle","Lateral pterygoid","Medial pterygoid","Masseter muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Temporalis muscle', 'The temporal fascia invests the temporalis muscle, attaching to the superior temporal line above and the zygomatic arch below. The parotid, pterygoid, and masseter structures lie outside this fascial investment.'),
('c8149b7c-7773-538e-ac04-7bd0942477c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The superficial temporal artery pulse is felt:', '["Posterior to the mastoid","Above the zygomatic arch","Behind the angle of mandible","Anterior to the tragus of ear","Over the temporal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the tragus of ear', 'The superficial temporal artery crosses the zygomatic arch just in front of the tragus, where its pulse is readily palpated. The other sites do not overlie the course of this artery.'),
('2f73e7c7-601e-5f0f-94f3-71bfb0ad4af2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The zygomatic arch is formed by:', '["Temporal and parietal resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxilla and palatine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Zygomatic process of temporal + temporal process of zygomatic","Frontal and zygomatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zygomatic bone and maxilla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic process of temporal + temporal process of zygomatic', 'The zygomatic arch is formed by the zygomatic process of the temporal bone joining the temporal process of the zygomatic bone at the zygomaticotemporal suture. The other bone pairs do not contribute to this arch.'),
('e9e10fea-0b0e-57ec-8f1b-de42746fb90c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is supplied by:', '["Facial nerve","Great auricular nerve","Trigeminal (V1)","Hypoglossal nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve', 'The TMJ receives sensory innervation from the auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. The facial nerve is motor to facial muscles, the great auricular nerve serves the ear, and the other nerves do not supply this joint.'),
('3471ead1-cc3f-5184-8bee-db0a61268751'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The TMJ is what type of joint?', '["Pivot","Cartilaginous","Fibrous","Synovial (hinge and gliding)","Ball and socket"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial (hinge and gliding)', 'The TMJ is a synovial joint that permits both hinge movements (depression and elevation) and gliding/translation, with an articular disc dividing its cavity. The fibrous, cartilaginous, pivot, and ball-and-socket categories do not describe its structure or movements.'),
('22fb62db-faf8-53a0-95e9-936c87de4636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The articular disc of TMJ divides it into:', '["Three compartments","division","One compartment","Four compartments","Two compartments (upper and lower)"]'::jsonb, 'Two compartments (upper and lower)', 'The articular disc divides the TMJ cavity into an upper (discotemporal) and a lower (discocondylar) compartment. A single cavity or three to four compartments does not reflect the actual anatomy of this joint.'),
('78ad66b7-321b-567a-a4b6-e837a692515c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The movements at TMJ include:', '["Flexion and extension resulting in decreased functional activity","Depression, elevation, protrusion, retraction, side-to-side","Only gliding mediated by specific receptor activation","Rotation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hinge movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depression, elevation, protrusion, retraction, side-to-side', 'The TMJ combines hinge and gliding movements to allow depression, elevation, protrusion, retraction, and side-to-side (lateral grinding) movements. Limiting the joint to a single type of motion ignores its full range.'),
('97bb256c-3fd4-5a07-b475-9dc9a391d647'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The lateral pterygoid muscle inserts into:', '["Coronoid process resulting in decreased functional activity","Ramus of mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Body of mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Neck of mandible and articular disc of TMJ","Angle of mandible leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neck of mandible and articular disc of TMJ', 'The lateral pterygoid inserts into the pterygoid fovea on the neck of the mandible as well as the capsule and articular disc of the TMJ. The coronoid process, angle, ramus, and body of the mandible are not its insertion sites.'),
('c206b8c5-2403-5161-9568-eb12a2fc903e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporalis muscle inserts into:', '["Body of mandible","Neck of mandible","Condyle of mandible","Angle of mandible","Coronoid process of mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Coronoid process of mandible', 'The temporalis muscle converges to insert on the coronoid process of the mandible. The condyle, neck, body, and angle of the mandible receive insertions of other muscles.'),
('01fcf7c6-403e-5c4f-b6a2-52517fbf655f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The masseter muscle originates from:', '["Temporal bone","Frontal bone","Sphenoid bone","Maxilla","Zygomatic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic arch', 'The masseter takes origin from the inferior border and inner surface of the zygomatic arch. The temporal, frontal, sphenoid, and maxillary bones are not its source.'),
('849d0e28-8933-54a8-bc4a-cbba4f160936'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The masseter muscle inserts into:', '["Neck of mandible resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronoid process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Body of mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Angle and lateral surface of ramus of mandible","Mental protuberance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Angle and lateral surface of ramus of mandible', 'The masseter inserts onto the angle and the lateral (outer) surface of the ramus of the mandible. The neck, coronoid process, body, and mental protuberance are the insertion sites of other muscles.'),
('851210ed-8710-5a9b-9704-be4319f6c7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The medial pterygoid muscle inserts into:', '["Lateral surface of ramus resulting in decreased functional activity","Neck of mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Body of mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial surface of angle and ramus of mandible","Coronoid process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial surface of angle and ramus of mandible', 'The medial pterygoid inserts on the medial surface of the angle and ramus of the mandible, mirroring the masseter. The lateral surface, neck, body, and coronoid process are not insertion sites for this muscle.'),
('c3a33a41-c08b-5cc4-8ed0-2810b2c07edd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Muscles of mastication are supplied by:', '["Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Facial nerve (VII)","Trigeminal nerve (V3)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Vagus nerve (X)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (V3)', 'All four muscles of mastication are supplied by the mandibular division (V3) of the trigeminal nerve through its muscular branches. The facial nerve supplies the muscles of facial expression, and the glossopharyngeal, vagus, and hypoglossal nerves innervate other targets.'),
('a641fa4d-92d5-5d3a-b779-bde39903c2b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The four muscles of mastication are:', '["Masseter, pterygoids, buccinator, temporalis","Temporalis, masseter, digastric, mylohyoid","Temporalis, masseter, lateral pterygoid, medial pterygoid","Temporalis, masseter, buccinator, pterygoids","Temporalis, pterygoids, orbicularis oris"]'::jsonb, 'Temporalis, masseter, lateral pterygoid, medial pterygoid', 'The four muscles of mastication are temporalis, masseter, lateral pterygoid, and medial pterygoid, all supplied by V3. The buccinator, digastric, mylohyoid, and orbicularis oris are not among these four.'),
('7dd870a3-9a18-5eff-82a9-0d61106f42e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The lateral pterygoid muscle:', '["Closes the mouth","Protracts the mandible and opens the mouth","Retracts the mandible","Moves mandible laterally only","Elevates the mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Protracts the mandible and opens the mouth', 'The lateral pterygoid pulls the mandibular head and disc forward (protraction) and, with the suprahyoid muscles, depresses the mandible to open the mouth. Elevation, retraction, and exclusively lateral movement are functions of other muscles.'),
('bd1e7efc-cf7f-5492-b0c4-6ae97a5bfaa7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The medial pterygoid muscle:', '["Depresses the mandible","Moves the mandible laterally only","Protracts the mandible","Elevates the mandible (closes mouth)","Retracts the mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates the mandible (closes mouth)', 'The medial pterygoid, working with temporalis and masseter, elevates the mandible to close the mouth. It does not depress, retract, or primarily protract the mandible.'),
('2b7675fc-5983-5b77-a3ba-6902bea6ff59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporalis muscle:', '["Only retracts","Only elevates","Only protracts","Elevates and protracts the mandible","Elevates and retracts the mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates and retracts the mandible', 'The temporalis elevates the mandible and, through its posterior fibers, retracts it. Protraction is primarily the role of the lateral pterygoid, so single-motion responses or elevation with protraction are incomplete.'),
('97a796cd-8720-5332-b722-7fb11b459622'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The infratemporal fossa communicates with:', '["Pterygopalatine fossa, orbit, cranial cavity","Only the nose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the oral cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the temporal fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the orbit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pterygopalatine fossa, orbit, cranial cavity', 'The infratemporal fossa communicates with the pterygopalatine fossa through the pterygomaxillary fissure, with the orbit through the inferior orbital fissure, and with the cranial cavity through foramen ovale and foramen spinosum. Restricting communication to a single region is incorrect.'),
('6e00e50f-5857-5ad2-bd88-ed43d8f03c89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The pterygomaxillary fissure connects:', '["Infratemporal fossa to pterygopalatine fossa","Cranial cavity to orbit","Orbit to nose","Nose to oral cavity","Temporal fossa to infratemporal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Infratemporal fossa to pterygopalatine fossa', 'The pterygomaxillary fissure transmits the terminal part of the maxillary artery and connects the infratemporal fossa with the pterygopalatine fossa. The orbit, nose, oral cavity, and cranial cavity are linked through other openings.'),
('6cb65e22-f5cb-5938-aa3a-193e73e213e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The maxillary artery enters the pterygopalatine fossa through:', '["Foramen spinosum","Pterygomaxillary fissure","Inferior orbital fissure","Foramen rotundum","Foramen ovale"]'::jsonb, 'Pterygomaxillary fissure', 'The third (pterygopalatine) part of the maxillary artery crosses the pterygomaxillary fissure to reach the pterygopalatine fossa. The other foramina transmit nervous or vascular structures unrelated to the artery''s entry into this fossa.'),
('2b1838c7-d775-5c1d-9b7c-f181a7022271'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The pterygoid venous plexus is located:', '["In submandibular triangle","Deep to masseter","Within the parotid","In the orbit","Around the lateral pterygoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Around the lateral pterygoid muscle', 'The pterygoid venous plexus surrounds the lateral pterygoid muscle, partly between the two heads and partly around it. The submandibular triangle, parotid, orbit, and masseter region are separate anatomic compartments.'),
('96454c8f-4b17-5f9b-a639-edf49fe3121d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The maxillary vein drains the pterygoid plexus into:', '["Retromandibular vein","Internal jugular vein","External jugular vein","Facial vein","Subclavian vein"]'::jsonb, 'Retromandibular vein', 'The maxillary vein arises from the pterygoid plexus and passes between the sphenomandibular ligament and the neck of the mandible to drain into the retromandibular vein. The other listed veins lie lower in the drainage pathway.'),
('c835b2fc-8eb7-5c74-94f4-7f5004dd2d43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The middle meningeal artery is a branch of:', '["Maxillary artery second part","Superficial temporal","Maxillary artery (first part)","Maxillary artery third part","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary artery (first part)', 'The middle meningeal artery arises from the first (mandibular) part of the maxillary artery within the infratemporal fossa. The superficial temporal and facial arteries are separate branches of the external carotid artery.'),
('0c39cb21-47e1-51f5-b205-8615e10d1f7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The middle meningeal artery enters skull through:', '["Foramen rotundum","Foramen spinosum","Carotid canal","Jugular foramen","Foramen ovale"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen spinosum', 'The middle meningeal artery passes through the foramen spinosum to reach the middle cranial fossa, where it grooves the inner skull. The carotid canal and foramen ovale transmit the internal carotid artery and mandibular nerve respectively.'),
('5c3f4178-7e65-5850-a400-5d895b4454f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Extradural hemorrhage is most commonly due to tearing of:', '["Superficial temporal","Middle meningeal artery","Anterior meningeal artery","Posterior meningeal","Maxillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meningeal artery', 'Fractures of the squamous temporal bone classically tear the middle meningeal artery, producing an extradural hemorrhage in the pterion region. The superficial temporal artery lies outside the skull, and the other meningeal vessels rarely cause this pattern.'),
('f4f59a57-7ac6-571a-ba1e-853ac0a39423'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The sphenomandibular ligament attaches to:', '["Lingula of mandible and spine of sphenoid","Neck of mandible and zygoma","Angle of mandible and temporal","Coronoid process and maxilla","Body of mandible and hyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Lingula of mandible and spine of sphenoid', 'The sphenomandibular ligament runs from the spine of the sphenoid to the lingula of the mandible, lying deep to the lateral pterygoid. The stylomandibular ligament, in contrast, attaches to the angle of the mandible.'),
('c1559e9f-d6b3-5e34-9a59-9cb9afc2c2b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The stylomandibular ligament attaches to:', '["Mastoid and mandible","Styloid and hyoid","Styloid process and angle of mandible","Sphenoid and mandible","Styloid and mastoid"]'::jsonb, 'Styloid process and angle of mandible', 'The stylomandibular ligament is a thickened band of deep cervical fascia passing from the styloid process to the angle of the mandible. The stylohyoid ligament, by contrast, runs from the styloid process to the hyoid bone.'),
('53176c62-0156-5c21-b6f2-c6aa8bd3f0a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The pterygomandibular raphe attaches to:', '["Maxilla and mandible resulting in decreased functional activity","Pterygoid and maxilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pterygoid and sphenoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Pterygoid hamulus and mylohyoid line of mandible","Mandible and hyoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pterygoid hamulus and mylohyoid line of mandible', 'The pterygomandibular raphe is the tendinous junction of the buccinator and superior constrictor, extending from the pterygoid hamulus to the posterior end of the mylohyoid line of the mandible. The other options pair bones that this raphe does not span.'),
('6ccc789e-10d0-58b9-a813-0fff255f70a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The infratemporal fossa contains the following EXCEPT:', '["Mandibular nerve","Pterygoid plexus","Parotid gland","Lateral pterygoid muscle","Maxillary artery"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid gland', 'The parotid gland lies superficial in the retromandibular fossa, over the masseter and behind the mandibular ramus, not within the infratemporal fossa. The mandibular nerve, pterygoid plexus, lateral pterygoid, and maxillary artery are all contents of the infratemporal fossa.'),
('60b1b49e-1508-5a35-81f0-1d9290766a8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The mandibular nerve (V3) exits the skull through:', '["Foramen lacerum","Foramen spinosum","Foramen ovale","Foramen rotundum","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen ovale', 'The mandibular nerve, the motor and sensory third division of the trigeminal nerve, leaves the middle cranial fossa through the foramen ovale. V2 passes through the foramen rotundum, and the middle meningeal artery uses the foramen spinosum.'),
('d2a66f1c-d4f9-5749-996e-2da21c8ab9af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The maxillary nerve (V2) exits the skull through:', '["Foramen rotundum","Foramen spinosum","Inferior orbital fissure","Foramen ovale","Superior orbital fissure"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen rotundum', 'The maxillary nerve exits the middle cranial fossa via the foramen rotundum to enter the pterygopalatine fossa. V3 uses the foramen ovale and V1 the superior orbital fissure, while the inferior orbital fissure is a later step in V2''s course.'),
('bb69f51d-6622-522b-9eae-036d6922cb73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The ophthalmic nerve (V1) enters the orbit through:', '["Foramen ovale","Inferior orbital fissure","Optic canal","Superior orbital fissure","Foramen rotundum"]'::jsonb, 'Superior orbital fissure', 'The ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve, together with CN III, IV, and VI, passes through the superior orbital fissure into the orbit. The optic canal transmits the optic nerve, and the inferior orbital fissure is used by V2 branches.'),
('763d06de-a598-56bd-a215-bc8c03742273'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The trigeminal nerve has how many divisions?', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (V1, V2, V3)","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (V1, V2, V3)', 'The trigeminal nerve divides into three branches: the ophthalmic (V1), maxillary (V2), and mandibular (V3) nerves. This tripartite arrangement is reflected in the nerve''s name ''trigeminal''.'),
('105ff585-96f8-53b1-a47c-c2a9cf582e47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The trigeminal ganglion is also called:', '["Pterygopalatine ganglion","Semilunar (Gasserian) ganglion","Otic ganglion","Geniculate ganglion","Ciliary ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Semilunar (Gasserian) ganglion', 'The trigeminal (Gasserian) ganglion lies on the petrous temporal bone in Meckel''s cave and contains the sensory cell bodies of the three trigeminal divisions. The pterygopalatine, otic, ciliary, and geniculate ganglia are autonomic or visceral ganglia with different functions.'),
('77ea0da3-632e-59c1-9639-100a45f06327'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The trigeminal ganglion is located in:', '["Posterior cranial fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","Middle cranial fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meckel''s cave (trigeminal impression of petrous temporal)","Foramen lacerum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cavernous sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Meckel''s cave (trigeminal impression of petrous temporal)', 'The trigeminal ganglion occupies Meckel''s cave, a dural recess over the trigeminal impression on the anterior surface of the petrous temporal bone in the middle cranial fossa. This position is targeted in percutaneous rhizotomy for trigeminal neuralgia.'),
('2a337c95-7391-5e4e-aeab-c6e39e588575'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The three divisions of trigeminal nerve supply:', '["Only teeth resulting in decreased functional activity","Only face accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only muscles of mastication mediated by specific receptor activation","Face, scalp, oral and nasal cavities, eye, teeth","Only sensation to scalp leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Face, scalp, oral and nasal cavities, eye, teeth', 'The trigeminal divisions provide sensory innervation to the face and anterior scalp, the eye and orbit, nasal and oral cavities, paranasal sinuses, and teeth. This broad cutaneous and mucosal distribution far exceeds any single tissue named in the other choices.'),
('e43faee7-c68c-5b1c-a1b7-32c913879d0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporal region is supplied by which nerve?', '["Great auricular nerve","Supraorbital nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Supratrochlear nerve","Lesser occipital nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)', 'The auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve, ascends with the superficial temporal artery to supply the skin of the temple and temporal scalp. The great auricular and lesser occipital nerves supply the neck and posterior scalp respectively.'),
('3d668b40-70ab-5b66-952f-d810cb96302b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid region is supplied by which cutaneous nerves?', '["Great auricular (C2-3) and auriculotemporal (V3)","Supraorbital and infraorbital","Cervical plexus only","Facial nerve","Trigeminal all divisions"]'::jsonb, 'Great auricular (C2-3) and auriculotemporal (V3)', 'Skin over the parotid receives sensation from the great auricular nerve (C2-3) from below and the auriculotemporal nerve (V3) from above. The facial nerve supplies no cutaneous sensation to this region.'),
('e793ce06-ec54-5bba-8352-5282e08fa694'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland has the following feature:', '["It is supplied by facial nerve","It has capsule","It develops from endoderm","It produces mucous secretion","It contains lymph nodes within its substance"]'::jsonb, 'It contains lymph nodes within its substance', 'The parotid gland characteristically contains lymph nodes within its substance, which can harbor metastases from superficial scalp and face tumors. It is a serous gland derived from ectoderm, enclosed in fascia but with no true capsule, and is not supplied by the facial nerve.'),
('7f66fa37-a5c6-5b55-9412-807e6dcd2a92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'How many layers does the scalp have?', '["6","4","5","7","3"]'::jsonb, '5', 'The scalp has five layers, recalled by the mnemonic SCALP: Skin, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar tissue, and Pericranium. The first three are tightly bound; the loose areolar layer is the fourth, between the aponeurosis and pericranium.'),
('0a9a4b0c-e202-5f11-88fe-431c0f392bfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The mnemonic SCALP stands for which layers of the scalp?', '["Skin, Cranium, Aponeurosis, Lymphatics, Pericranium","Subcutaneous tissue, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose tissue, Periosteum","Skin, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar tissue, Pericranium","Scalp, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose tissue, Parietal bone","Skin, Cartilage, Aponeurosis, Lymphatics, Parotid"]'::jsonb, 'Skin, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar tissue, Pericranium', 'SCALP lists the five scalp layers in order: Skin, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar tissue, and Pericranium. This ordering is important because the loose areolar layer is the plane in which scalp avulsion and infection spread occur.'),
('aa963e9b-b0f4-5f56-875d-a9c692ca7df1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which layer of the scalp is clinically known as the ''dangerous area''?', '["Pericranium","Skin","Aponeurosis","Loose areolar tissue","Connective tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Loose areolar tissue', 'The loose areolar layer is termed the ''dangerous area'' because it is loose enough to allow infection to spread, and emissary veins connecting it to the intracranial venous sinuses can carry infection inward. It is the plane of scalp mobility and of scalp flaps.'),
('31a86d3e-5c81-5685-9c39-c5a9dd9c5005'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The loose areolar tissue layer of the scalp allows spread of infection into which space?', '["Subdural space","Epidural space","Parapharyngeal space","Retropharyngeal space","Cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus', 'Emissary veins pass through the loose areolar layer and drain into the diploic veins and intracranial venous sinuses, so scalp infections can reach the cavernous sinus. The other spaces are not reached by scalp emissary venous drainage.'),
('a233c0b5-50ee-574d-95b6-83385997a9ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The epicranial aponeurosis attaches to which muscles?', '["Zygomaticus and platysma","Levator palpebrae and orbicularis oculi","Buccinator and orbicularis oris","Frontalis and occipitalis","Temporalis and masseter"]'::jsonb, 'Frontalis and occipitalis', 'The epicranial (galea) aponeurosis connects the frontalis muscle anteriorly to the occipitalis muscle posteriorly, forming a single muscle-aponeurosis sheet across the vertex. The other muscle pairs lie in different regions of the head and face.'),
('0898931a-0943-533f-9c59-37a55abbd5d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The scalp receives its blood supply from branches of which artery?', '["Both external and internal carotid arteries","Internal carotid artery","External carotid artery","Maxillary artery","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Both external and internal carotid arteries', 'The scalp is supplied by branches of both carotid systems: the supratrochlear and supraorbital arteries from the internal carotid, and the superficial temporal, occipital, and posterior auricular arteries from the external carotid. This anastomotic network explains the richly vascular scalp.'),
('99d4ba71-6236-5a62-91e2-6d7fbe1f1b80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve provides sensory innervation to the anterior scalp?', '["Infraorbital nerve (V2)","Greater occipital nerve (C2)","Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Lesser occipital nerve (C2)"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital nerve (V1)', 'The supraorbital nerve, the largest branch of the frontal nerve from V1, exits the supraorbital foramen and supplies the skin of the forehead and anterior scalp. The greater occipital nerve innervates the posterior scalp instead.'),
('fe2cf8ab-8844-52c3-9628-49000d4b749c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve provides sensory innervation to the posterior scalp?', '["Zygomaticotemporal nerve (V2)","Greater occipital nerve (C2)","Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Lesser occipital nerve (C2)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater occipital nerve (C2)', 'The greater occipital nerve, the medial branch of the dorsal ramus of C2, ascends to supply the skin of the posterior scalp to the vertex. The lesser occipital nerve supplies the region just behind the ear, and the others cover anterior or lateral areas.'),
('0d585334-c498-56da-809c-bbe6c3eb8a2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve provides sensory innervation to the temporal region of the scalp?', '["Lesser occipital nerve (C2)","Greater occipital nerve (C2)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Infraorbital nerve (V2)","Supraorbital nerve (V1)"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)', 'The auriculotemporal nerve from the mandibular division accompanies the superficial temporal vessels and supplies the skin of the temporal scalp. The supraorbital nerve covers the forehead, and the occipital nerves serve the posterior scalp.'),
('cde4e1ee-47c0-5f29-ba10-69297b8df9a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The lymphatic drainage of the anterior scalp is primarily to which nodes?', '["Retropharyngeal nodes","Deep cervical nodes","Superficial parotid nodes","Submandibular nodes","Submental nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial parotid nodes', 'The anterior part of the scalp (front in front of the auricle) drains into the superficial parotid (preauricular) lymph nodes. The posterior scalp instead drains to the mastoid and occipital nodes, which then feed the deep cervical chain.'),
('dd115cb3-d87d-5f03-b5f1-0997f37fcd25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which veins drain the scalp?', '["Superior ophthalmic vein","Facial and retromandibular veins","Supratrochlear and supraorbital veins","External jugular vein","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The scalp is drained by several venous channels including the supratrochlear and supraorbital veins joining the facial vein, the facial and retromandibular veins from superficial structures, the external jugular vein, and connections to the superior ophthalmic vein. Because each named vessel participates, the comprehensive choice is correct.'),
('36143a80-9303-5732-9b72-285d9b02076b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscles of facial expression are derived from the second pharyngeal arch?', '["Only orbicularis oris","Zygomaticus and risorius","Only buccinator","All muscles of facial expression","All except levator palpebrae"]'::jsonb, 'All muscles of facial expression', 'All the muscles of facial expression develop from the mesenchyme of the second pharyngeal arch and are innervated by the nerve of that arch, the facial nerve. This contrasts with the first arch muscle derivatives (muscles of mastication) supplied by the mandibular nerve.'),
('364025a7-ee1e-5efa-91b3-5fcf4c0d2043'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What is the nerve supply of the muscles of facial expression?', '["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Accessory nerve (CN XI)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'The facial nerve is the motor nerve of the second pharyngeal arch and innervates all muscles of facial expression via its five terminal branches. CN V supplies sensation to the face and motor innervation only to the muscles of mastication.'),
('46ed0067-2f87-5317-9fde-4b6508d3aee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve provides motor innervation to the face?', '["Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'The facial nerve provides the motor supply to all the muscles of facial expression, so its paralysis produces the visible loss of facial movement in conditions such as Bell palsy. The trigeminal nerve supplies only sensation and the muscles of mastication.'),
('4e33be1f-8bb4-5993-b49e-e8eaeab7f7c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The sensory innervation of the face is provided by which nerve?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Lesser occipital nerve","Great auricular nerve","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (CN V)', 'Sensory innervation of the face is supplied by the three divisions of the trigeminal nerve: ophthalmic, maxillary, and mandibular. The facial nerve carries only motor fibers to the face, while the lesser occipital and great auricular nerves serve limited posterior regions.'),
('f76822f6-ab7c-5030-a1c8-aa42b33902e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve supplies which area of the face?', '["Temple and lateral face","Lower lip and chin","Entire face","Forehead, upper eyelid, and nose tip","Cheek and upper lip"]'::jsonb, 'Forehead, upper eyelid, and nose tip', 'The ophthalmic division innervates skin of the forehead, upper eyelid, conjunctiva, and the tip of the nose, a zone corresponding to areas reached through the superior orbital fissure. The cheek and upper lip belong to the maxillary division, and the lower lip and chin to the mandibular division.'),
('d2b13154-2049-5c12-acc8-744eb8d5f56f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The maxillary division (V2) of the trigeminal nerve supplies which area of the face?', '["Angle of mandible","Forehead and scalp","Cheek, lower eyelid, and side of nose","Lower lip and chin","Temple region"]'::jsonb, 'Cheek, lower eyelid, and side of nose', 'The maxillary division supplies the skin of the cheek, lower eyelid, lateral side of the nose, and upper lip via the infraorbital and zygomaticofacial nerves. The angle of the mandible is supplied by the great auricular nerve, and the lower lip and chin by the mandibular division.'),
('b72383c2-e668-50eb-b840-cec4a7f647ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The mandibular division (V3) of the trigeminal nerve supplies which area of the face?', '["Lower lip, chin, and jaw","Nose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temple mediated by specific receptor activation","Forehead without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cheek leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lip, chin, and jaw', 'The mandibular division sends cutaneous branches (mental, buccal, and auriculotemporal) to supply the skin of the lower lip, chin, and lower jaw region, as well as the temporal area. The forehead, nose, and cheek are served by V1 and V2.'),
('cb8b7d71-cefd-553d-ad38-537d64eedef3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which artery is the main blood supply of the face?', '["Transverse facial artery","Angular artery","Superficial temporal artery","Maxillary artery","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Facial artery', 'The facial artery is the chief arterial supply of the face, winding over the mandible at the anterior border of masseter and ascending to the medial canthus as the angular artery. The superficial temporal and transverse facial arteries only supply the lateral face.'),
('e96da35d-0d80-5f38-9f31-ede983889120'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The facial artery is a branch of which artery?', '["External carotid artery","Maxillary artery","Lingual artery","Superficial temporal artery","Internal carotid artery"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The facial artery arises from the anterior surface of the external carotid artery just above the lingual artery. Both the superficial temporal and maxillary arteries are also branches of the external carotid, but they arise higher in the neck.'),
('3f30b49a-db17-5548-9d64-2fb2997f4bba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The facial vein drains into which vein?', '["Internal jugular vein","Subclavian vein","Retromandibular vein","External jugular vein","Brachiocephalic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular vein', 'The facial vein crosses the mandible and drains into the internal jugular vein after being joined by the anterior division of the retromandibular vein. The external jugular vein instead receives primarily the posterior division of the retromandibular vein.'),
('7e434e02-6e4e-5e77-a4ce-afb9d2acde3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which vein communicates between the facial vein and the cavernous sinus?', '["Retromandibular vein","Deep facial vein","Pterygoid plexus","Inferior ophthalmic vein","Superior ophthalmic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Superior ophthalmic vein', 'The superior ophthalmic vein connects the facial vein to the cavernous sinus, providing the pathway by which infections in the danger triangle spread intracranially. The deep facial vein and pterygoid plexus connect to the cavernous sinus by a more indirect route.'),
('a77f3372-7063-5ecb-82fc-0c98d101b202'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The danger triangle of the face is the area where infections may spread to the cavernous sinus. Which area does it correspond to?', '["Around the eye resulting in decreased functional activity","Chin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper lip and lower part of nose","Cheek area without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forehead leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper lip and lower part of nose', 'The ''danger triangle'' of the face is the region of the upper lip and lower part of the nose, drained by veins that communicate with the cavernous sinus through the angular and superior ophthalmic veins. Boils in this area may therefore cause cavernous sinus thrombosis.'),
('71884414-bb00-56bc-8960-d952522f442e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle of facial expression closes the eyelids?', '["Corrugator supercilii","Levator palpebrae superioris","Orbicularis oculi","Frontalis","Procerus"]'::jsonb, 'Orbicularis oculi', 'The orbicularis oculi, a sphincter muscle encircling the eye, closes the eyelids; its palpebral part closes gently during blinking and its orbital part forcefully during voluntary closure. The levator palpebrae superioris opens the eye, and corrugator supercilii draws the brows medially.'),
('01e0ba14-cd13-5b1f-95e4-dc0cc98d8547'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle of facial expression elevates the angle of the mouth?', '["Buccinator","Risorius","Zygomaticus major","Orbicularis oris","Depressor anguli oris"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomaticus major', 'The zygomaticus major pulls the angle of the mouth upward and laterally, producing smiling. The depressor anguli oris pulls it downward, the risorius retracts it laterally, and the orbicularis oris compresses the lips.'),
('4550f64c-941c-556a-8197-176375602ea2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle of facial expression compresses the cheek against the teeth?', '["Buccinator","Zygomaticus","Temporalis","Orbicularis oris","Masseter"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator', 'The buccinator forms the muscular wall of the cheek and compresses it against the teeth during mastication, blowing, and sucking. The temporalis and masseter are muscles of mastication, and orbicularis oris encircles the lips rather than the cheek.'),
('698d2d5d-5a12-5f1e-b7f2-029ccdc2553f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle is responsible for puckering of the lips?', '["Orbicularis oris","Buccinator","Zygomaticus","Risorius","Mentalis"]'::jsonb, 'Orbicularis oris', 'The orbicularis oris is the circular sphincter around the mouth and its contraction compresses and puckers the lips, as in whistling or kissing. The buccinator compresses the cheeks, while the mentalis moves the chin.'),
('a7bae731-b748-5149-aacd-c57a8d2db3da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Bell palsy is caused by lesion of which nerve?', '["Accessory nerve (CN XI)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'Bell palsy is an acute peripheral facial nerve palsy that produces paralysis of the muscles of facial expression on the affected side. The trigeminal nerve is uninvolved, which distinguishes this facial weakness from any sensory loss.'),
('5598ae57-acf3-510f-b580-39c795b72f5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In Bell palsy, which of the following is affected?', '["Muscles of mastication","Tongue movements","Swallowing","Shoulder elevation","Muscles of facial expression"]'::jsonb, 'Muscles of facial expression', 'Bell palsy is a peripheral lesion of the facial nerve, so the affected muscles are those of facial expression, producing drooping of the corner of the mouth and inability to close the eye. Muscles of mastication are innervated by the trigeminal nerve and remain unaffected.'),
('2bb01bec-c350-5b75-8f3c-b5f7a9645e90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What is the most common cause of Bell palsy?', '["Congenital","Idiopathic (viral)","Trauma","Tumor","Bacterial infection"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic (viral)', 'Most cases of Bell palsy are idiopathic, and reactivation of herpes simplex virus type 1 within the geniculate ganglion is believed to play a causal role, giving the lower motor neuron facial paralysis. Trauma, tumors, and bacterial causes account for only a minority of cases.'),
('6a9e7a21-b126-59c0-8963-4e470666a660'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland is located in which region?', '["Preauricular region","Submandibular region","Sublingual region","Pterygopalatine fossa","Infratemporal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Preauricular region', 'The parotid gland sits in the preauricular region, overlying the ramus of the mandible and the masseter, with its duct crossing the masseter. The submandibular and sublingual glands occupy their namesake regions, while the pterygopalatine and infratemporal fossae lie deep to the zygoma and contain neurovascular structures rather than salivary tissue.'),
('19b8d28a-0d94-5e9a-84b0-a5c74ca9ef82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland is the largest of which type of salivary gland?', '["Mucous salivary gland","Serous salivary gland","Minor salivary gland","Mixed salivary gland","Accessory salivary gland"]'::jsonb, 'Serous salivary gland', 'The parotid is a purely serous gland and the largest of the major salivary glands. Mixed glands such as the submandibular and sublingual contain both serous and mucous acini, whereas the minor and accessory glands are small.'),
('48e3c6a9-e4e7-5533-af8e-c175e2f7791e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid duct (Stensen duct) opens into the oral cavity opposite which tooth?', '["Lower first molar","Upper third molar","Upper first molar","Lower second molar","Upper second molar"]'::jsonb, 'Upper second molar', 'The parotid (Stensen) duct pierces the buccinator and opens on the buccal mucosa opposite the crown of the upper second molar. The lower molars lie too inferior, and the upper first and third molars do not mark the duct opening.'),
('2da446e6-b4e1-5400-ab6f-fbfb47d0faae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which artery is closely related to the parotid gland?', '["Superficial temporal artery","Internal carotid artery","Maxillary artery","External carotid artery","Facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The external carotid artery enters the deep surface of the parotid and divides within it into the maxillary and superficial temporal arteries. The maxillary, facial, and superficial temporal arteries are branches rather than the parent vessel, and the internal carotid lies medial, outside the gland.'),
('a288080d-f35f-5b2b-a6bb-fd681cb2af3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland is divided into superficial and deep lobes by which structure?', '["External carotid artery","Facial nerve","Retromandibular vein","Parotid duct","Masseter muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve', 'The facial nerve passes through the substance of the parotid and divides the gland into superficial and deep lobes. The retromandibular vein and external carotid artery also traverse the gland but do not divide it, and the duct and masseter lie outside it.'),
('bbeb7f4f-c6a3-533d-99c0-0ccc19779ac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which vein is formed within the parotid gland?', '["Superficial temporal vein","Internal jugular vein","Retromandibular vein","Facial vein","External jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Retromandibular vein', 'The retromandibular vein forms within the parotid by union of the superficial temporal and maxillary veins, then exits to join the facial and external jugular veins. The other listed veins do not form inside the gland.'),
('4efa7015-619b-5ca0-baf2-2db227673f55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland receives parasympathetic innervation from which nerve?', '["Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) via otic ganglion","Vagus nerve (CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) via otic ganglion', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers travel with the glossopharyngeal nerve and synapse in the otic ganglion, with postganglionic fibers reaching the gland via the auriculotemporal nerve. CN VII, V, X, and XII do not provide the secretomotor supply to the parotid.'),
('4c734c03-88d6-50be-9652-1e92c01eb642'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What is the secretomotor supply to the parotid gland?', '["Pterygopalatine ganglion (CN VII)","Submandibular ganglion (CN VII)","Superior cervical ganglion","Otic ganglion (CN IX)","Ciliary ganglion (CN III)"]'::jsonb, 'Otic ganglion (CN IX)', 'Secretomotor supply reaches the parotid through the otic ganglion, which receives preganglionic fibers from CN IX. The pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia serve nasal and orbital or sublingual and submandibular glands respectively, and the superior cervical ganglion is sympathetic.'),
('4b5017be-662a-5775-b2a2-ed7222948d9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotitis (inflammation of parotid) is most commonly caused by which virus?', '["Cytomegalovirus","Herpes simplex virus","Mumps virus","Influenza virus","Epstein-Barr virus"]'::jsonb, 'Mumps virus', 'Mumps, a paramyxovirus, characteristically causes painful swelling of the parotid glands. The other viruses can infect salivary tissue but are not the classic cause of epidemic parotitis.'),
('281dfe85-f857-5e7b-9b7a-1fb6ca47e35e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What is the most common tumor of the parotid gland?', '["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Warthin tumor","Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Pleomorphic adenoma","Acinic cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Pleomorphic adenoma', 'Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common benign tumor of the parotid gland. Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common malignant parotid tumor, while Warthin tumor, adenoid cystic carcinoma, and acinic cell carcinoma occur less frequently.'),
('2d95b757-a24d-5b5a-8c40-f366a26e1bdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve is at risk during parotid gland surgery?', '["Accessory nerve (CN XI)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Facial nerve (CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'The facial nerve (CN VII) courses through the parotid substance and is the principal nerve at risk during parotidectomy. CN V, IX, XI, and XII lie away from the operative field.'),
('6d8c1f79-b098-53e9-a44a-e295377f0c9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid bed is formed by which structures?', '["Styloid process, carotid sheath, and pharyngeal wall","Temporal bone, occipital bone, and cervical vertebrae","Hyoid bone, thyroid cartilage, and cricoid cartilage","Mandible, maxilla, and zygomatic arch","Pterygoid plates, sphenoid bone, and ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Styloid process, carotid sheath, and pharyngeal wall', 'The deep surface of the parotid rests on the styloid process, the carotid sheath, and the pharyngeal wall, which together form the parotid bed. The other groupings include bones or cartilages that do not lie deep to the gland.'),
('c473005c-670d-5b99-a16d-cb4eb5fe0508'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which lymph nodes drain the parotid gland?', '["Superficial and deep parotid nodes","Deep cervical nodes","Submental nodes","Retropharyngeal nodes","Submandibular nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial and deep parotid nodes', 'The parotid drains into superficial and deep parotid lymph nodes lying within and around the gland, which ultimately empty into the deep cervical chain. The submental, submandibular, and retropharyngeal nodes drain other regions.'),
('83335a0a-e4c1-5225-8665-6585038a89f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporalis muscle is innervated by which nerve?', '["Deep temporal nerve (V3)","Auriculotemporal nerve","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Masseteric nerve","Buccal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Deep temporal nerve (V3)', 'The temporalis receives its motor supply from the deep temporal nerves, branches of the mandibular division (V3). The masseteric nerve supplies the masseter, the buccal nerve supplies the buccinator, and the auriculotemporal nerve is sensory.'),
('bbbc24a7-8650-5c22-8736-67310617fc97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The masseter muscle is innervated by which nerve?', '["Buccal nerve","Deep temporal nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Masseteric nerve (V3)"]'::jsonb, 'Masseteric nerve (V3)', 'The masseter is innervated by the masseteric nerve, a branch of the mandibular division (V3). The deep temporal nerve supplies the temporalis, and the facial nerve innervates muscles of facial expression, not mastication.'),
('d07c5af1-63e3-517f-8c9f-61dbc3320776'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which of the following is a muscle of mastication?', '["Buccinator","Frontalis","Platysma","Temporalis","Orbicularis oris"]'::jsonb, 'Temporalis', 'The temporalis is one of the four muscles of mastication, along with the masseter, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid. The buccinator, orbicularis oris, frontalis, and platysma are muscles of facial expression.'),
('610bc6fc-3f27-500c-9843-a1f491830f92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The muscles of mastication are innervated by which nerve?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)","Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve (V3)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Accessory nerve (CN XI)"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular division of trigeminal nerve (V3)', 'All muscles of mastication are supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). CN VII innervates the muscles of facial expression, and CN IX, XI, and XII have unrelated distributions.'),
('1f481163-c9c8-5568-8b32-def16f5db397'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is formed between which bones?', '["Mandibular condyle and sphenoid bone","Mandibular condyle and zygomatic bone","Mandibular condyle and occipital bone","Mandibular condyle and maxilla","Mandibular condyle and temporal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular condyle and temporal bone', 'The TMJ is a synovial joint between the condyle of the mandible and the mandibular (glenoid) fossa and articular tubercle of the temporal bone. The sphenoid, zygomatic, occipital, and maxillary bones do not articulate at this joint.'),
('b8c139f5-3874-5cb6-b306-7d70a3ee4f77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which ligament of the TMJ prevents posterior displacement of the mandible?', '["Sphenomandibular ligament","Lateral ligament","Capsular ligament","Temporomandibular ligament","Stylomandibular ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Temporomandibular ligament', 'The temporomandibular (lateral) ligament, on the lateral side of the joint, is the thickened capsular band that limits posterior displacement of the mandible. The lateral ligament and temporomandibular ligament are the same structure here, while the sphenomandibular and stylomandibular ligaments are accessory supporting straps.'),
('a4ea26ca-994a-5381-8ca3-b103f4b02f33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The pterygopalatine fossa communicates with the middle cranial fossa via which foramen?', '["Foramen ovale","Carotid canal","Foramen rotundum","Foramen lacerum","Foramen spinosum"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen rotundum', 'The pterygopalatine fossa opens into the middle cranial fossa through the foramen rotundum, which transmits the maxillary nerve (V2). The foramen ovale transmits V3, and the carotid canal, foramen lacerum, and foramen spinosum communicate with other regions.'),
('db7fbed7-0646-5667-ac08-7f1812484db4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The infraorbital nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Mandibular nerve (V3)","Ophthalmic nerve (V1)","Maxillary nerve (V2)","Facial nerve (CN VII)","Zygomatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2)', 'The infraorbital nerve is the continuation of the maxillary nerve (V2) after it leaves the pterygopalatine fossa and emerges at the infraorbital foramen. V1, V3, and CN VII do not give rise to it.'),
('1a6cd384-ae19-5fba-90b4-d3c2163525b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle of facial expression is important for whistling?', '["Buccinator","Mentalis","Orbicularis oris","Zygomaticus","Risorius"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator', 'The buccinator compresses the cheek and holds it against the teeth, so it is essential for whistling and blowing. Orbicularis oris constricts the lips, and the mentalis, zygomaticus, and risorius have different facial actions.'),
('8605bdc4-17ed-5ebe-831b-b4bbe1845e77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle depresses the lower lip?', '["Mentalis","Buccinator","Depressor anguli oris","Depressor labii inferioris","Platysma"]'::jsonb, 'Depressor labii inferioris', 'Depressor labii inferioris pulls the lower lip downward and slightly laterally. Mentalis elevates the skin of the chin and protrudes the lower lip, while depressor anguli oris, buccinator, and platysma have other actions.'),
('07ab104e-0887-51a1-b316-93578d1f8186'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which muscle elevates the upper lip?', '["Buccinator","Zygomaticus minor","Levator anguli oris","Zygomaticus major","Levator labii superioris"]'::jsonb, 'Levator labii superioris', 'Levator labii superioris elevates the upper lip directly. Zygomaticus major and minor draw the angle of the mouth superolaterally, levator anguli oris raises the mouth corner, and the buccinator flattens the cheek.'),
('95e49b4e-009d-5afe-a395-52970a303d3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The platysma muscle is innervated by which nerve?', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Accessory nerve","Phrenic nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Cervical branch of facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical branch of facial nerve', 'The platysma, a muscle of facial expression, is supplied by the cervical branch of the facial nerve. The trigeminal, accessory, hypoglossal, and phrenic nerves have unrelated territories.'),
('c7c7b4b5-8f5b-5eb7-ac7e-d8578a7706b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The auriculotemporal nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Facial nerve (CN VII)","Maxillary nerve (V2)","Mandibular nerve (V3)","Great auricular nerve","Lesser occipital nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular nerve (V3)', 'The auriculotemporal nerve is a sensory branch of the mandibular division (V3) that passes through the upper parotid to supply the auricle and temple. The great auricular and lesser occipital nerves arise from the cervical plexus, and CN VII and V2 do not contribute to it.'),
('7870e0f0-8445-5567-b62e-5ef7d003ae82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The auriculotemporal nerve carries which type of fibers?', '["Parasympathetic fibers to submandibular gland","Sensory fibers to temporal region and parasympathetic to parotid","Sympathetic fibers to parotid","Sensory fibers to cheek","Motor fibers to temporalis"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory fibers to temporal region and parasympathetic to parotid', 'The auriculotemporal nerve carries general sensory fibers to the temporal region and auricle and conveys postganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the otic ganglion to the parotid. It does not supply the cheek, the submandibular gland, or the temporalis.'),
('d69cead8-f1f1-5bb8-856d-06e5962536ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The parotid gland extends from which structures superiorly and inferiorly?', '["Ear superiorly to clavicle inferiorly","Orbit superiorly to oral cavity inferiorly","Temporal bone superiorly to hyoid bone inferiorly","Maxilla superiorly to mandible inferiorly","Zygomatic arch superiorly to angle of mandible inferiorly"]'::jsonb, 'Zygomatic arch superiorly to angle of mandible inferiorly', 'The parotid gland extends superiorly from the zygomatic arch down to the angle of the mandible inferiorly. The other reported boundaries do not match the actual surface extent of the gland.'),
('ad9f5fd3-2ea1-5df9-9428-b5cc9db2bec9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The superficial parotid lymph nodes drain which areas?', '["Forehead and eyelids resulting in decreased functional activity","Cheek and nose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower lip and chin mediated by specific receptor activation","Neck and shoulder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral scalp, auricle, and parotid region"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral scalp, auricle, and parotid region', 'The superficial parotid lymph nodes drain the lateral scalp, the auricle, and the parotid region and lie in front of the tragus. The other lymph node groups drain the face, lip, chin, or neck.'),
('e16f6ac8-9a12-5b15-8d0c-b335498fc87b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Parotid abscess may cause which clinical sign?', '["Trismus (difficulty opening mouth)","Proptosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dysphagia mediated by specific receptor activation","Nystagmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ptosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trismus (difficulty opening mouth)', 'A parotid abscess causes marked swelling and pain that may limit mouth opening, producing trismus. Proptosis, dysphagia, nystagmus, and ptosis are not typical local signs of parotid infection.'),
('fa5b3bc2-4026-5c2c-b010-17ff0cd019aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Frey syndrome (auriculotemporal syndrome) occurs after injury to which nerve during parotid surgery?', '["Transverse cervical nerve","Great auricular nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve","Lesser occipital nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve', 'Injury to the auriculotemporal nerve during parotid surgery allows its regenerating parasympathetic fibers to grow into sweat glands. The great auricular, lesser occipital, transverse cervical, and facial nerves are not responsible for this complication.'),
('a1d29f43-121f-58af-9062-9fcf7b623bab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What is the characteristic symptom of Frey syndrome?', '["Difficulty swallowing resulting in decreased functional activity","Dry mouth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial paralysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Gustatory sweating (sweating on cheek during eating)","Loss of taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gustatory sweating (sweating on cheek during eating)', 'Frey syndrome presents with gustatory sweating, where the cheek sweats and flushes during eating after misdirected parasympathetic nerve regeneration. Facial paralysis, dry mouth, dysphagia, and loss of taste have different mechanisms.'),
('778fb0f3-d467-58c8-8058-ae6bc8aba1d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which emissary vein connects the scalp veins to the superior sagittal sinus?', '["Parietal emissary vein","Frontal emissary vein","Occipital emissary vein","Mastoid emissary vein","Condylar emissary vein"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal emissary vein', 'The parietal emissary vein passes through the parietal foramen and connects the scalp veins to the superior sagittal sinus. The frontal, occipital, mastoid, and condylar emissary veins connect to other dural sinuses.'),
('2dfc5663-f721-5726-a3e8-e854a871474a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The scalp is richly supplied with blood vessels. Why do scalp wounds bleed profusely?', '["Thin-walled veins resulting in decreased functional activity","Rich anastomosis and fibrous attachments prevent vasoconstriction","Lack of clotting factors mediated by specific receptor activation","High density of capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","High pressure from internal carotid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rich anastomosis and fibrous attachments prevent vasoconstriction', 'Scalp vessels are held open by the fibrous attachments of the skin and connective tissue to the epicranial aponeurosis, so the cut edges cannot retract and constrict, causing profuse bleeding. The mechanism is anatomic rather than a clotting or pressure problem.'),
('dc414404-9e97-5d95-9862-9fb59d1925dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which branch of the external carotid artery supplies the anterolateral scalp?', '["Facial artery","Occipital artery","Posterior auricular artery","Maxillary artery","Superficial temporal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial temporal artery', 'The superficial temporal artery, a terminal branch of the external carotid, runs up in front of the ear to supply the anterolateral scalp. The facial, occipital, and posterior auricular arteries supply other territories.'),
('6b62db62-7f5f-56f7-9594-5e01443f2848'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The supratrochlear and supraorbital arteries are branches of which artery?', '["Superficial temporal artery","Facial artery","Maxillary artery","External carotid artery","Ophthalmic artery (from internal carotid)"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmic artery (from internal carotid)', 'The supratrochlear and supraorbital arteries arise from the ophthalmic artery, a branch of the internal carotid. The superficial temporal, facial, and maxillary arteries derive from the external carotid.'),
('9be8c19f-5c42-5f75-8c15-b951a363a3e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The occipital artery supplies which region of the scalp?', '["Vertex","Lateral scalp","Posterior scalp","Anterior scalp","Temporal region"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior scalp', 'The occipital artery ascends to supply the posterior scalp. The vertex, lateral scalp, anterior scalp, and temporal region are supplied by other vessels.'),
('84fde936-38df-5fa5-ad10-62c35dc8b736'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Nerves of the scalp posterior to the ear are derived from which spinal nerves?', '["Facial nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Lumbar plexus","Cervical plexus (C2-C3)","Brachial plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical plexus (C2-C3)', 'The greater occipital, lesser occipital, great auricular, and third occipital nerves arise from the cervical plexus (C2-C3) and supply the posterior scalp. The trigeminal and facial nerves innervate anterior structures.'),
('88205143-41bc-5881-b49e-3f2e9cccdfb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which nerve supplies the skin of the auricle and the external auditory meatus?', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auriculotemporal nerve and great auricular nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auriculotemporal nerve and great auricular nerve', 'The auriculotemporal and great auricular nerves provide the main sensory innervation to the auricle and external auditory meatus. The vagus supplies the concha and meatal floor, and CN V, IX, and VII have other roles.'),
('9df9490e-3e63-5828-9d61-d411aa410ca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What forms the motor root of the facial nerve?', '["Main motor root","Greater petrosal nerve","Chorda tympani","Nervus intermedius","Posterior auricular nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Main motor root', 'The main motor root of the facial nerve supplies the muscles of facial expression and exits through the stylomastoid foramen. The nervus intermedius is the sensory and parasympathetic root, and the greater petrosal nerve, chorda tympani, and posterior auricular nerve are distal branches.'),
('ecf7bbcc-15cf-52de-b3ae-5b3f012fb777'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'What does the nervus intermedius (sensory root of facial nerve) carry?', '["Sympathetic fibers to face","Taste fibers from anterior 2/3 of tongue","Motor fibers to facial muscles","Parasympathetic fibers to parotid","Sensory fibers from scalp"]'::jsonb, 'Taste fibers from anterior 2/3 of tongue', 'The nervus intermedius carries taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue via the chorda tympani, together with preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular and pterygopalatine ganglia. It does not carry motor, sympathetic, or scalp sensory fibers.'),
('d3c1f0bf-48b1-52bf-9c4f-6611a9dabb22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The facial nerve exits the skull through which foramen?', '["Stylomastoid foramen","Jugular foramen","Internal acoustic meatus","Foramen ovale","Foramen rotundum"]'::jsonb, 'Stylomastoid foramen', 'The facial nerve leaves the skull through the stylomastoid foramen after traversing the internal acoustic meatus and the facial canal. The jugular foramen, foramen ovale, and foramen rotundum transmit other nerves.'),
('d0c17c36-4ea0-5049-8707-9762c7483432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'The marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve supplies which muscles?', '["Depressor anguli oris and mentalis","Zygomaticus and levator labii","Platysma and sternocleidomastoid","Frontalis and corrugator","Orbicularis oris and buccinator"]'::jsonb, 'Depressor anguli oris and mentalis', 'The marginal mandibular branch runs along the lower border of the mandible and supplies the depressor anguli oris and mentalis. The other branches of the facial nerve innervate different muscles of the face.'),
('0ec62215-e904-517a-af0e-809baf4ba74e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which branch of the facial nerve is most commonly injured during parotid surgery?', '["Zygomatic branch","Buccal branch","Temporal branch","Cervical branch","Marginal mandibular branch"]'::jsonb, 'Marginal mandibular branch', 'The marginal mandibular branch is most commonly injured during parotid surgery because it runs superficially along the mandibular margin, causing drooping of the lower lip. The zygomatic, buccal, temporal, and cervical branches are injured less often.'),
('6305f0e7-9028-57cc-b7c7-10d3610f798a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In Scalp, face and parotid, which statement best fits the following fact: what is the correct mnemonic for scalp layers?', '["SCALP: Skin, Connective, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar, Pericranium","SCALP: Skin, Cartilage, Aponeurosis, Loose tissue, Periosteum","SCALP: Skin, Connective, Artery, Lymphatics, Pericranium","SCALP: Skin, Cutaneous, Aponeurosis, Lymphatics, Pericranium","SCALP: Skin, Connective, Aponeurosis, Lymph, Periosteum"]'::jsonb, 'SCALP: Skin, Connective, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar, Pericranium', 'The mnemonic SCALP stands for Skin, Connective tissue, Aponeurosis, Loose areolar tissue, and Pericranium, the five layers from superficial to deep. The other versions substitute incorrect terms for these layers.'),
('c3ba3e65-3568-5fd9-abc1-a5cb90034b30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Regarding Scalp, face and parotid, what is the correct answer to: which scalp layer is called the dangerous layer of scalp?', '["Loose areolar tissue (layer 4)","Pericranium (layer 5)","Aponeurosis (layer 3)","Skin (layer 1)","Connective tissue (layer 2)"]'::jsonb, 'Loose areolar tissue (layer 4)', 'The loose areolar tissue (layer 4) is the dangerous layer because infection can spread across it deep to the epicranial aponeurosis into the orbit and through emissary veins into the cranial cavity. The other layers do not permit such spread.'),
('96f8be46-62c3-5aa3-95f7-178a4c0751b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'For Scalp, face and parotid, which option is most appropriate: the galea aponeurotica is also known as:?', '["Pericranium","Temporoparietal fascia","Superficial temporal fascia","Epicranial aponeurosis","Deep temporal fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Epicranial aponeurosis', 'The galea aponeurotica is the epicranial aponeurosis, the broad tendon connecting the frontal and occipital bellies of the occipitofrontalis. Pericranium, temporoparietal fascia, and the superficial or deep temporal fascia are separate layers.'),
('55f93c2f-e72a-5422-988b-d90ddd31f708'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'Which answer is correct in Scalp, face and parotid when asked: occipitofrontalis muscle is supplied by which nerve?', '["Trigeminal nerve (V1)","Facial nerve (temporal branch)","Facial nerve (buccal branch)","Facial nerve (zygomatic branch)","Trigeminal nerve (V2)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (temporal branch)', 'The occipitofrontalis is a muscle of facial expression supplied by the temporal branch of the facial nerve. The trigeminal nerve provides only sensory supply to the scalp, and the buccal and zygomatic branches of CN VII do not reach this muscle.'),
('08394919-1517-5a3f-a59e-0b2c1e9820eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'In Scalp, face and parotid, the best response to ''Sensory innervation of anterior scalp is from:'' is:', '["Lesser occipital (C2-3)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Greater occipital (C2)","Great auricular nerve","Supraorbital and supratrochlear (V1)"]'::jsonb, 'Supraorbital and supratrochlear (V1)', 'The anterior scalp is supplied by the supraorbital and supratrochlear nerves, both branches of the ophthalmic division (V1). The auriculotemporal nerve (V3) and the C2-C3 cervical nerves supply the lateral and posterior scalp.'),
('59ebdb48-d7ff-58b2-830c-bc8feaaadf1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Scalp Face Parotid', 'During revision of Scalp, face and parotid, which of the following is correct: posterior scalp sensory innervation is mainly from:?', '["Supratrochlear nerve (V1)","Supraorbital nerve (V1)","Auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Greater occipital nerve (C2)","Infraorbital nerve (V2)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater occipital nerve (C2)', 'The posterior scalp receives its sensory innervation mainly from the greater occipital nerve (C2). The supratrochlear, supraorbital, auriculotemporal, and infraorbital nerves supply the anterior and lateral scalp.'),
('356289dd-5ba2-58ef-bc4f-1e4784594e94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid located:', '["Anterior neck","wrapping around trachea below thyroid cartilage","Posterior neck","Above hyoid","Thorax Behind sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior neck', 'The thyroid gland lies in the anterior neck, sprawled across the trachea just below the larynx. It is neither posterior, above the hyoid, nor intrathoracic in location.'),
('a17e892f-0e35-5863-82dc-5009c5a05858'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid gland parts:', '["Three lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Four lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two lobes connected by isthmus","Single mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Single lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two lobes connected by isthmus', 'The thyroid consists of right and left lobes joined by a central isthmus, giving it an ''H'' or butterfly shape. Three or four lobes or a single solid mass are not normal thyroid anatomy.'),
('a15fe0bb-3c4a-5358-8004-1e27ea361a98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid isthmus at level of:', '["Hyoid","6th ring","2nd-4th tracheal rings","1st tracheal ring","Cricoid"]'::jsonb, '2nd-4th tracheal rings', 'The thyroid isthmus crosses the trachea at the level of the 2nd to 4th tracheal rings. The hyoid is far superior, and the cricoid sits just above the 1st ring, so these lie at different levels.'),
('9161d288-dffa-59fb-80ed-6764739ce523'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid lobes extend from:', '["Angle of mandible to clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Oblique line of thyroid cartilage to 5th-6th tracheal ring","Cricoid to carina mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid to sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mastoid to sternum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique line of thyroid cartilage to 5th-6th tracheal ring', 'Each lobe extends upward to the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage and downward to about the 5th-6th tracheal ring. The angles of the mandible, carina, and hyoid are outside this range.'),
('c48100ee-f467-5bf3-aa9b-bee1e15ec696'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid capsule:', '["Buccopharyngeal resulting in decreased functional activity","Carotid sheath accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pretracheal fascia (false) and true capsule (fibrous)","Investing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pretracheal fascia (false) and true capsule (fibrous)', 'The thyroid is enclosed by a thin true fibrous capsule on the gland surface and a false capsule formed by the pretracheal layer of deep cervical fascia. The buccopharyngeal, prevertebral, and investing layers and the carotid sheath do not enclose the gland.'),
('f5121a72-afd2-525c-81ed-9b337e0c4e84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Number of parathyroid glands:', '["8 resulting in decreased functional activity","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","6 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 (2 superior, 2 inferior)"]'::jsonb, '4 (2 superior, 2 inferior)', 'Normally four parathyroid glands exist: two superior and two inferior, lying on the posterior surface of the thyroid. Two, six, or eight glands do not reflect the typical anatomy.'),
('90806d1d-3445-5688-ae53-1d0a8f83d8ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Parathyroid glands located:', '["On posterior thyroid surface within capsule","Outside thyroid capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid isthmus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On posterior thyroid surface within capsule', 'The parathyroid glands lie on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobe, usually within the true capsule. Anterior placement, the isthmus, and the trachea are not the typical sites.'),
('80e762d3-48c9-5d78-ae85-759d3d17737a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Superior parathyroids develop from:', '["1st pouch","2nd pouch","5th pouch","3rd pouch","4th pharyngeal pouch"]'::jsonb, '4th pharyngeal pouch', 'The superior parathyroid glands develop from the dorsal wing of the 4th pharyngeal pouch. The 3rd pouch gives rise to the inferior parathyroids and thymus, while the 1st and 2nd pouches form other derivatives.'),
('721d0db0-783c-5b66-baec-38f05322673d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Inferior parathyroids develop from:', '["3rd pharyngeal pouch","5th pouch","2nd pouch","1st pouch","4th pouch"]'::jsonb, '3rd pharyngeal pouch', 'The inferior parathyroid glands develop from the dorsal wing of the 3rd pharyngeal pouch along with the thymus. The 4th pouch, by contrast, gives rise to the superior parathyroids.'),
('4d11796e-fc2c-5053-a856-8e32693656f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid blood supply:', '["Only superior thyroid","Only inferior thyroid","Only thyroid ima","Superior thyroid (ECA)","inferior thyroid (thyrocervical trunk) thyroid ima Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid (ECA)', 'The superior thyroid artery, a branch of the external carotid artery, is a principal source of the thyroid blood supply. The inferior thyroid (thyrocervical trunk) and occasionally the thyroid ima also contribute.'),
('217a6dcd-0998-5fff-9770-6963c5dc2171'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Superior thyroid artery branch of:', '["Facial","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid artery","Subclavian","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The superior thyroid artery is the first branch of the external carotid artery and descends to the thyroid gland. It does not arise from the facial, subclavian, or internal carotid vessels, or from the thyrocervical trunk.'),
('c527da3d-80d4-5349-8e8b-4a00df64bc48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Inferior thyroid artery branch of:', '["External carotid","Vertebral","Costocervical trunk","Thyrocervical trunk (from subclavian)","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk (from subclavian)', 'The inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk, a branch of the subclavian artery. The costocervical trunk, vertebral, and carotid arteries are not its source.'),
('1fd3e566-a531-56c3-b3bf-03fdf1cf1cb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid ima artery (when present) from:', '["Brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch","Internal carotid","External carotid","Subclavian","Thyrocervical trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch', 'When present, the thyroid ima artery arises from the brachiocephalic (innominate) trunk or directly from the aortic arch and ascends in front of the trachea to the isthmus. It does not originate from carotid or subclavian branches.'),
('c1173722-b4f9-5bce-91d4-ebc02cb78874'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid vein drainage:', '["Only inferior to brachiocephalic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior to IJV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only middle to IJV mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior and middle to IJV; inferior to brachiocephalic","All to azygos leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and middle to IJV; inferior to brachiocephalic', 'The superior and middle thyroid veins drain into the internal jugular vein, while the inferior thyroid vein drains into the brachiocephalic vein. Drainage to the azygos system does not occur.'),
('5956d3b7-f012-54f8-bb3e-b22988b60df9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Recurrent laryngeal nerve branch of:', '["Facial (VII)","Vagus (CN X)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (CN X)', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of the vagus nerve (CN X). Cranial nerves VII, IX, XII, and V do not contribute to laryngeal innervation.'),
('4a92181d-c33c-5f97-8cd3-61a222379141'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Right RLN hooks around:', '["Right subclavian artery","Aortic arch","Right IJV","Right vertebral","Right common carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Right subclavian artery', 'On the right side, the recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the subclavian artery and then ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove. The aortic arch is the loop point only on the left side.'),
('c40db227-4bb5-530c-a9d8-10834dc50618'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Left RLN hooks around:', '["Left IJV resulting in decreased functional activity","Innominate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left common carotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Left subclavian without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic arch (at ligamentum arteriosum)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (at ligamentum arteriosum)', 'On the left, the recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the aortic arch just lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. The right nerve loops around the subclavian artery instead.'),
('4c0efbc8-c4a7-5716-b2f4-3a904d565a0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'RLN supplies:', '["Cricothyroid only resulting in decreased functional activity","Palatal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","All intrinsic muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pharyngeal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve innervates all intrinsic muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid, which receives its supply from the external laryngeal nerve. It does not innervate palatal or pharyngeal muscles.'),
('9af6a9a7-35a0-51ee-84c9-1cd0a028a4ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'External laryngeal nerve supplies:', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid","Thyroarytenoid","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Cricothyroid muscle","Vocalis"]'::jsonb, 'Cricothyroid muscle', 'The external laryngeal nerve is the motor supply to the cricothyroid muscle, which tenses the vocal cords. The remaining intrinsic muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
('39538ab6-4c83-5f8b-898f-a67b2bb4565d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Superior laryngeal nerve branch of:', '["Facial (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Vagus (CN X)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (CN X)', 'The superior laryngeal nerve arises from the vagus (CN X) near the inferior vagal ganglion and divides into internal and external branches. It is not derived from the trigeminal, facial, glossopharyngeal, or hypoglossal nerves.'),
('4fdbf758-98e9-5841-91ee-49ea3999c5da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Superior laryngeal divides into:', '["Anterior and posterior","Internal and external laryngeal nerves","Medial and lateral","Motor and sensory","Superior and inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external laryngeal nerves', 'The superior laryngeal nerve divides into the internal laryngeal nerve, which is sensory, and the external laryngeal nerve, which supplies the cricothyroid. The other pairs of names are not its branches.'),
('4b80d71a-3cf0-564e-ba06-e2d542a4402d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Internal laryngeal nerve supplies:', '["Sensation below cords","Motor to all intrinsic","Sensation to laryngeal mucosa above vocal cords","Motor to cricothyroid","Sensation to trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation to laryngeal mucosa above vocal cords', 'The internal laryngeal nerve provides sensory innervation to the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal cords. Sensation below the cords is carried by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
('2df749f2-6830-505c-8516-50fe9d622bc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'RLN supplies sensation to:', '["Laryngeal mucosa below vocal cords","Above cords","Tracheal mucosa","Esophageal mucosa","Pharyngeal mucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngeal mucosa below vocal cords', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve carries sensation from the laryngeal mucosa below the vocal cords. Mucosa above the cords is served by the internal laryngeal nerve, while tracheal, esophageal, and pharyngeal mucosa are innervated elsewhere.'),
('8de0324b-0598-52a2-9417-884bf649cf00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Unilateral RLN injury causes:', '["Hoarseness (vocal cord paralysis)","Dysphagia","Facial paralysis","Shoulder weakness","Tongue deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Hoarseness (vocal cord paralysis)', 'Injury to one recurrent laryngeal nerve paralyses the ipsilateral vocal cord, producing hoarseness and a weak, breathy voice. Dysphagia, facial paralysis, shoulder weakness, and tongue deviation indicate damage to other structures.'),
('5d2b381d-fc91-59e2-8904-60dfa0f4eabe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Bilateral RLN injury causes:', '["Hoarseness only","Dysphagia only","Respiratory distress/stridor","Cough only","symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory distress/stridor', 'Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury leaves both vocal cords adducted in the paramedian position, narrowing the airway and causing inspiratory stridor and respiratory distress. Hoarseness alone does not reflect the acute airway compromise.'),
('e1be0647-316b-59fa-abc8-55c69df3a275'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Paired laryngeal cartilages:', '["Cricoid + thyroid","Thyroid","cricoid","epiglottis Arytenoid + thyroid Only arytenoid","Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform', 'The paired laryngeal cartilages are the arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform. The thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis are single (unpaired) cartilages.'),
('d6cb913a-87b1-57ca-a94f-d7f9e12f1ac4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Unpaired laryngeal cartilages:', '["Arytenoid + thyroid","Cricoid + thyroid","Thyroid","cricoid","epiglottis Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform Only cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid', 'The unpaired laryngeal cartilages are the thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis. The arytenoids and their accessory corniculate and cuneiform cartilages are paired, so among the options the thyroid is the unpaired cartilage named.'),
('97dfc980-ee70-5e1e-8068-0b9d493f7f3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Largest laryngeal cartilage:', '["Epiglottis","Arytenoid","Thyroid cartilage","Cricoid","Corniculate"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid cartilage', 'The thyroid cartilage is the largest laryngeal cartilage, forming most of the anterior and lateral walls of the larynx. The cricoid lies below it, and the epiglottis, arytenoids, and corniculate cartilages are smaller.'),
('b46a4800-0850-52f4-8ca8-103d8c0a2c72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Cricoid cartilage shaped like:', '["Square resulting in decreased functional activity","Signet ring (narrow anterior, broad posterior)","Oval mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete circle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Signet ring (narrow anterior, broad posterior)', 'The cricoid cartilage is shaped like a signet ring, with a narrow anterior arch and a broad posterior lamina. It is the only complete cartilaginous ring of the airway, and its shape is not square, oval, or triangular.'),
('1ece56a8-648d-5478-9ef6-f60c2a43eee5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Cricoid cartilage at vertebral level:', '["C6","C4","C7","C5","T1"]'::jsonb, 'C6', 'The cricoid cartilage lies at the level of the sixth cervical vertebra. It marks the boundary between the larynx and trachea and the site where the pharynx becomes the esophagus.'),
('436a3122-135d-5797-a2e6-cfa17029cbdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroid cartilage at level:', '["C2-C3","C5-C6","C3-C4","C6-C7","C4-C5"]'::jsonb, 'C4-C5', 'The thyroid cartilage lies at about the level of C4-C5, with its laryngeal prominence palpable in the midline. The cricoid is located lower, at C6.'),
('5e90888d-517a-5de6-aa75-0944fe4bf41a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Arytenoid cartilages:', '["Unpaired","C-shaped","Hyaline","Paired","pyramid-shaped with vocal and muscular processes Small nodules"]'::jsonb, 'Paired', 'The arytenoid cartilages are a paired set of hyaline cartilages sitting on the upper border of the cricoid lamina. The thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis are unpaired, so a paired set is the defining feature here.'),
('41d85d02-fbba-5105-b282-a1d7a491775f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Epiglottis:', '["Paired resulting in decreased functional activity","Leaf-shaped elastic cartilage guarding laryngeal inlet","Ring-shaped mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyaline cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flat bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leaf-shaped elastic cartilage guarding laryngeal inlet', 'The epiglottis is a leaf-shaped elastic cartilage that projects behind the tongue and guards the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. It is unpaired and is not a hyaline cartilage or bone.'),
('a2ffc1e8-8767-5ecb-a7b6-4c745bc644a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Cricothyroid joint allows:', '["Only gliding resulting in decreased functional activity","movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rotation and gliding for pitch adjustment","Only rotation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rotation and gliding for pitch adjustment', 'The cricothyroid joints are synovial joints that permit slight rotation and gliding of the thyroid cartilage on the cricoid, tilting the thyroid to tense the vocal cords and adjust pitch. The movement is not purely gliding or purely rotation.'),
('fbcbd834-0607-5051-b87e-1ab0324fa958'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Cricoarytenoid joint allows:', '["Only rotation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only gliding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","movement mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertical movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rotation and gliding for vocal cord movement"]'::jsonb, 'Rotation and gliding for vocal cord movement', 'The cricoarytenoid joints allow the arytenoid cartilages to rotate and glide on the upper border of the cricoid lamina, moving the vocal processes and thus the vocal cords. Combined rotation and gliding, not one alone, generates cord movement.'),
('95060417-faf5-514b-a82d-0ef50a4cc14c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Intrinsic laryngeal muscles include:', '["Only cricothyroid","Only thyroarytenoid","Cricothyroid","PCA LCA transverse arytenoid oblique arytenoid","thyroarytenoid vocalis Only arytenoids Only PCA"]'::jsonb, 'Cricothyroid', 'The intrinsic laryngeal muscles include the cricothyroid, thyroarytenoid with its vocalis portion, posterior and lateral cricoarytenoids, and the transverse and oblique arytenoids. All adjust vocal cord position and tension; the cricothyroid is one member of this group.'),
('246f9b23-d4db-5701-a996-5f4d075b05bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Only abductor of vocal cords:', '["Cricothyroid","Vocalis","Posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA)","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Transverse arytenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA)', 'The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only muscle that abducts (opens) the vocal cords by rotating the arytenoid cartilages outward. Damage to it leaves the cords adducted and unopposed, causing airway compromise.'),
('118ea14c-f307-510e-9dda-8b0ce977c05e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Adductors of vocal cords:', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Cricothyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vocalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral cricoarytenoid + transverse arytenoid","Thyroarytenoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cricoarytenoid + transverse arytenoid', 'The lateral cricoarytenoid and the transverse (interarytenoid) arytenoid muscles adduct the vocal cords, closing the rima glottidis during phonation and airway protection. The posterior cricoarytenoid, in contrast, is the sole abductor.'),
('f63403f9-bf7b-5817-8fba-769192c1fb65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Cricothyroid tenses cords by:', '["Abducting arytenoids resulting in decreased functional activity","Raising larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relaxing cords mediated by specific receptor activation","Adducting arytenoids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulling thyroid forward increasing tension"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling thyroid forward increasing tension', 'The cricothyroid tilts the thyroid cartilage forward on the cricoid, lengthening and tensing the vocal cords and thereby raising pitch. It does not abduct or adduct the arytenoids or raise the larynx.'),
('551d5660-88a4-5314-90b3-400f048ccba1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroarytenoid relaxes cords by:', '["Tensing cords","Pulling arytenoid to thyroid","shortening cords","Rotating arytenoid","Abducting cords Adducting cords"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling arytenoid to thyroid', 'The thyroarytenoid passes from the thyroid cartilage to the arytenoid, so its contraction pulls the arytenoid toward the thyroid, shortening and relaxing the cords and lowering pitch. This slacks rather than tenses the cords.'),
('5ed3891f-993c-54b3-851b-8d14f648d0da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Vocalis muscle:', '["Part of PCA","Cartilage","Part of cricothyroid","Medial part of thyroarytenoid","fine-tunes cord tension Separate muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Medial part of thyroarytenoid', 'The vocalis is the medial portion of the thyroarytenoid muscle, lying within the vocal fold adjacent to the vocal ligament. It is not a separate cartilage or part of the PCA or cricothyroid.'),
('f4df424a-cd33-5b50-a55f-458094c0754e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Rima glottidis:', '["Cricothyroid space","Laryngeal inlet","Opening between false cords","Opening between true vocal cords","Tracheal opening"]'::jsonb, 'Opening between true vocal cords', 'The rima glottidis is the narrow slit between the true vocal cords (and the vocal processes of the arytenoids); its width changes with cord abduction and adduction. It is distinct from the laryngeal inlet and from the space between the false cords.'),
('81f0e400-e134-550d-9e1a-fc7ec473ab2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Laryngeal ventricle:', '["Vallecula resulting in decreased functional activity","Recess between vestibular and vocal folds","Piriform sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Tracheal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laryngeal inlet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recess between vestibular and vocal folds', 'The laryngeal ventricle (sinus) is the horizontal recess between the vestibular fold above and the vocal fold below on each side. The vallecula and piriform sinus are recesses of the pharynx, not of the larynx.'),
('6e8cd72c-1a02-58ff-9d71-1c58f9363431'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Laryngeal saccule:', '["Cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessel mediated by specific receptor activation","Blind pouch extending upward from ventricle","Nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blind pouch extending upward from ventricle', 'The laryngeal saccule is a small blind pouch that arises from the anterior end of the laryngeal ventricle and extends upward between the vestibular fold and the thyroid cartilage. It is a mucosal diverticulum, not cartilage, muscle, vessel, or nerve.'),
('ecff1845-6a2a-54fd-a923-b6280a8bfff4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'True vocal cords formed by:', '["Vocal ligament (conus elasticus edge) with thin mucosa","Vestibular ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aryepiglottic fold mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroid lamina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cricoid lamina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vocal ligament (conus elasticus edge) with thin mucosa', 'Each true vocal fold is formed by the vocal ligament, the thickened free upper edge of the conus elasticus, covered by a thin layer of mucosa. The vestibular ligament lies above and forms the false cords.'),
('e7946461-fa8f-5f10-b80d-5c6114c9cffb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'False vocal cords formed by:', '["Free edge of quadrangular membrane with thicker mucosa","Thyrohyoid membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conus elasticus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cricothyroid ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vocal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Free edge of quadrangular membrane with thicker mucosa', 'Each false (vestibular) vocal fold is formed by the free lower edge of the quadrangular membrane covered by a thicker mucosa. The vocal ligament and conus elasticus form the true cords below them.'),
('6ddb2531-3fb3-5190-ba71-61757553a48b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Quadrangular membrane extends:', '["Arytenoid/epiglottis to ventricular fold","Cricoid to trachea","Thyroid to cricoid","Epiglottis to trachea","Hyoid to thyroid"]'::jsonb, 'Arytenoid/epiglottis to ventricular fold', 'The quadrangular membrane runs from the arytenoid cartilages and the lateral margin of the epiglottis down to the ventricular fold (false vocal cord). It does not extend to the trachea, cricoid, hyoid, or between the thyroid and cricoid.'),
('0212497b-d3fd-5e96-9666-336f904b9872'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Conus elasticus extends:', '["Epiglottis to arytenoid","Hyoid to thyroid","Cricoid cartilage to vocal ligament","Cricoid to trachea","Thyroid to hyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Cricoid cartilage to vocal ligament', 'The conus elasticus (cricovocal membrane) extends from the upper border of the cricoid cartilage upward to the vocal ligament, which forms the free upper margin of the membrane. It does not reach the epiglottis, hyoid, or trachea.'),
('3a8fdcea-b99e-569b-984f-fd6615574206'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyrohyoid membrane pierced by:', '["Internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal artery","Hypoglossal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior laryngeal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","RLN without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal artery', 'The superior laryngeal artery and the internal laryngeal nerve pass through the thyrohyoid membrane to enter the larynx. The recurrent laryngeal nerve reaches the larynx lower down, not through this membrane.'),
('c91abd57-558a-5daf-86ed-9888989c3630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Cricothyroid membrane site for:', '["Emergency cricothyroidotomy","RLN passage","Inferior laryngeal artery","None","Superior laryngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Emergency cricothyroidotomy', 'The cricothyroid membrane lies just beneath the skin between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages and is the preferred site for emergency cricothyroidotomy, since it is avascular and provides rapid airway access. The RLN and laryngeal arteries pass elsewhere.'),
('b1b059ce-cbbf-57c7-88b6-d08b5c58b392'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Laryngeal innervation from:', '["Superior laryngeal (sensory + motor cricothyroid) and recurrent laryngeal","Only vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only superior laryngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only RLN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior laryngeal (sensory + motor cricothyroid) and recurrent laryngeal', 'The larynx is innervated by the vagus via the superior laryngeal nerve (internal laryngeal supplies sensation above the vocal folds; external laryngeal supplies cricothyroid) and the recurrent laryngeal nerve (supplies the remaining intrinsic muscles and sensation below the folds). A single nerve or the glossopharyngeal nerve would not account for both sensory and motor coverage of the larynx.'),
('5b098eb2-e4fb-545a-a40f-d4c9654a817e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Laryngeal blood supply:', '["Maxillary resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only superior thyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Only inferior thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior laryngeal (from superior thyroid) and inferior laryngeal (from inferior thyroid)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior laryngeal (from superior thyroid) and inferior laryngeal (from inferior thyroid)', 'The larynx is supplied by the superior laryngeal artery, a branch of the superior thyroid artery, and the inferior laryngeal artery, a branch of the inferior thyroid artery. The facial and maxillary arteries supply the face and deep structures, not the larynx.'),
('4380439f-83a7-52e4-a794-318ec0d7e440'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Supraglottic lymphatics drain to:', '["Parotid","Retropharyngeal","Submandibular","Lower deep cervical","Upper deep cervical nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Upper deep cervical nodes', 'Lymph from the supraglottic region, a rich lymphatic zone, drains to the upper deep cervical nodes. The infraglottic region drains instead to the lower deep cervical and paratracheal nodes.'),
('6077220b-b020-50b5-9fd8-e6810f2da5d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Infraglottic lymphatics drain to:', '["Upper deep cervical","Retropharyngeal","Submandibular","Lower deep cervical (paratracheal)","Submental"]'::jsonb, 'Lower deep cervical (paratracheal)', 'The infraglottic region drains to the lower deep cervical and paratracheal nodes, whereas the supraglottis drains to the upper deep cervical nodes. The submental and submandibular nodes drain the anterior floor of mouth and lower lip.'),
('f7f4bbdc-43a3-5bf1-9ae5-fc66090828ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Laryngeal inlet guarded by:', '["Vestibular folds","Vocal cords","Thyroid cartilage","Epiglottis","aryepiglottic folds arytenoids Cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Epiglottis', 'During swallowing the epiglottis tilts posteriorly over the laryngeal inlet, channeling food toward the esophagus and protecting the airway. The inlet is otherwise bounded by the aryepiglottic folds and arytenoid cartilages; vestibular and vocal folds lie inside the larynx.'),
('1fa7ed9c-b7ae-5e10-a5d5-a4e21899d701'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During swallowing, the larynx:', '["larynx:","Expands","Elevates and moves anteriorly","epiglottis closes inlet","Does not move Rotates Depresses moves posteriorly"]'::jsonb, 'Elevates and moves anteriorly', 'During swallowing the larynx elevates and is drawn anteriorly beneath the tongue, which pulls the epiglottis down to close the inlet. The larynx does not expand, rotate posteriorly, or remain stationary.'),
('f6c44ce4-3f03-56df-b901-b66516717295'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Trachea begins at:', '["Lower border of cricoid (C6)","Epiglottis","Hyoid","Cricothyroid membrane","Upper thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Lower border of cricoid (C6)', 'The trachea begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at the level of C6 and ends by bifurcating at the sternal angle (T4/T5). The hyoid, epiglottis, and cricothyroid membrane lie at higher or different levels.'),
('dc2f52b9-2db2-5beb-ab45-3236e3e065d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Trachea bifurcates at:', '["T10 resulting in decreased functional activity","C6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T6 mediated by specific receptor activation","T2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T4/T5 (sternal angle)"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 (sternal angle)', 'The trachea divides into the right and left main bronchi at the level of the sternal angle, corresponding to T4/T5 (lower border). C6 is the level where the trachea begins, not where it bifurcates.'),
('48202113-4f2f-575d-8d4a-7af46a3f1e17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Right main bronchus:', '["Same as left","Longer","narrower","more horizontal Absent More horizontal","Shorter wider more vertical than left"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter wider more vertical than left', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left, which is why inhaled foreign bodies lodge more often on the right side. The left bronchus is longer, narrower, and more horizontal.'),
('057b71a0-c34b-53ff-b444-3eb3e3db4455'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Pharynx extends from:', '["Thyroid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Base of skull to lower cricoid (C6)","Hyoid mediated by specific receptor activation","T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epiglottis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Base of skull to lower cricoid (C6)', 'The pharynx extends from the base of the skull down to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at C6, where it continues as the esophagus. It is not limited to the hyoid, thyroid cartilage, or T4.'),
('a6a30eba-5956-555c-803c-c58056765a41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Pharynx divisions:', '["Nasopharynx","oropharynx laryngopharynx","Anterior posterior lateral Oral","nasal laryngeal Superior middle","inferior Upper middle lower"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx', 'The pharynx is divided into three parts: nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx, of which the nasopharynx is the uppermost division lying behind the nasal cavity. The other choices list incomplete or garbled groupings of the divisions.'),
('7a511741-5f8d-58c2-8565-96ec0a892d4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Fossa of Rosenmuller:', '["Oropharyngeal recess","Piriform sinus","Tonsillar bed","Recess in nasopharynx lateral to tubal elevation","common site of nasopharyngeal carcinoma Laryngopharyngeal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Recess in nasopharynx lateral to tubal elevation', 'The fossa of Rosenmuller (pharyngeal recess) is the deep depression in the lateral wall of the nasopharynx just posterior to the tubal elevation, and it is a common site of nasopharyngeal carcinoma. The piriform fossa and tonsillar bed are different structures in the hypopharynx and oropharynx.'),
('76ed8a7d-3fcd-5666-b7ac-b88b5ccf48ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Pharyngeal wall layers:', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","6 2 mediated by specific receptor activation","4: mucosa pharyngobasilar fascia","muscle buccopharyngeal fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4: mucosa pharyngobasilar fascia', 'The pharyngeal wall is composed of four layers: mucosa, pharyngobasilar fascia, muscular (the constrictors), and buccopharyngeal fascia. The other numeric choices and isolated fascial names are incorrect counts or partial layers.'),
('53583c22-237c-5ce9-9151-767e877f74f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Pharyngeal constrictors supplied by:', '["Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)","Facial","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal","Hypoglossal"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are innervated by the pharyngeal plexus, whose motor fibers come mainly from the vagus nerve. The glossopharyngeal nerve provides sensation, and the stylopharyngeus is the pharyngeal muscle innervated directly by CN IX.'),
('b6ba19ca-87e9-5d51-b15b-268880f39dad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Pharyngeal plexus formed by:', '["CN IX CN X","and sympathetic fibers CN X only","CN V VII","IX","CN IX only CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX CN X', 'The pharyngeal plexus is formed by pharyngeal branches of the glossopharyngeal (IX) and vagus (X) nerves, together with sympathetic fibers. Listing only one nerve is incomplete, and the trigeminal and facial nerves do not contribute.'),
('f6eeb75b-515b-5748-9189-cb0514a3668f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Superior constrictor originates from:', '["Mandible","Pterygoid hamulus","pterygomandibular raphe","mylohyoid line","Hyoid Thyroid cartilage Cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Pterygoid hamulus', 'The superior constrictor arises from the pterygoid hamulus, the pterygomandibular raphe, the posterior end of the mylohyoid line of the mandible, and the side of the tongue. The hyoid and thyroid/cricoid cartilages are origins of the middle and inferior constrictors, respectively.'),
('91b9165a-5955-5a73-9217-7cf9e70bd2c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Middle constrictor originates from:', '["Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid","stylohyoid ligament","Thyroid","Mandible","Pterygoid hamulus Cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid', 'The middle constrictor arises from the stylohyoid ligament and the greater and lesser cornua of the hyoid bone. The thyroid/cricoid cartilages and pterygoid hamulus are origins of the inferior and superior constrictors.'),
('cb781e41-5b92-5917-8cf3-b49d4f5904ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Inferior constrictor originates from:', '["Pterygoid hamulus resulting in decreased functional activity","Palatine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid and cricoid cartilages","Hyoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mandible leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid and cricoid cartilages', 'The inferior constrictor arises from the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage and from the cricoid cartilage, its lowest fibers forming the cricopharyngeus. The hyoid bone and pterygoid hamulus are origins of the middle and superior constrictors.'),
('3f8cdead-2564-5f54-a523-39124e5fd1a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Cricopharyngeus:', '["Longitudinal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Middle constrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of inferior constrictor; acts as upper esophageal sphincter","Palatal muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior constrictor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Part of inferior constrictor; acts as upper esophageal sphincter', 'The cricopharyngeus is the lowest part of the inferior constrictor and functions as the upper esophageal sphincter at the pharyngoesophageal junction. It is not a palatal or longitudinal muscle, nor a separate superior/middle constrictor.'),
('de4c1fac-bc1a-595f-b1ad-3675a154a98e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Killian''s dehiscence:', '["Buccopharyngeal fascia opening resulting in decreased functional activity","Palate defect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Weak area between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus","Middle constrictor gap without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior constrictor gap leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak area between thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus', 'Killian''s dehiscence is the triangular weak area in the posterior pharyngeal wall between the oblique thyropharyngeus and the transverse cricopharyngeus fibers of the inferior constrictor. It is the site through which Zenker''s diverticulum protrudes.'),
('7c8ea1d3-9e88-5dbe-bfdb-008c2682d7c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Zenker''s diverticulum occurs at:', '["Pyriform sinus","Vallecula","Tonsillar bed","Pharyngeal recess","Killian''s dehiscence"]'::jsonb, 'Killian''s dehiscence', 'Zenker''s diverticulum is a pulsion diverticulum that herniates through Killian''s dehiscence, the weak area between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus. The pyriform fossa, vallecula, and tonsillar bed are not sites of this diverticulum.'),
('b5dd7465-375e-5691-bb39-44a7f14431f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Stylopharyngeus supplied by:', '["Vagus (X)","Facial (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The stylopharyngeus is the only pharyngeal muscle innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), which also supplies sensation to the pharynx. The vagus and facial nerves supply the other pharyngeal muscles and the face, respectively.'),
('1be4079f-d1fa-516f-acbc-774340382cf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Auditory tube connects:', '["Nasopharynx to middle ear","Pharynx to inner ear","Oropharynx to middle ear","Nose to ear","Nasopharynx to external ear"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx to middle ear', 'The auditory (pharyngotympanic) tube connects the lateral wall of the nasopharynx to the middle ear cavity, equalizing pressure across the tympanic membrane. It does not connect to the inner or external ear.'),
('85f08cb0-8a91-57d7-b524-da3eec29133b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Auditory tube opens in:', '["Oropharynx posterior","Roof","Oropharynx lateral wall","Posterior wall","Lateral wall of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wall of nasopharynx', 'The auditory tube opens into the lateral wall of the nasopharynx through the pharyngeal opening, just behind the tubal elevation (torus tubarius). It does not open into the oropharynx or the roof of the pharynx.'),
('c9d2e3e0-3b82-504b-ab93-729454cdd7be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Salpingopharyngeus arises from:', '["Auditory tube cartilage","Palatine","Thyroid","Hyoid","Pterygoid hamulus"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory tube cartilage', 'The salpingopharyngeus arises from the auditory (Eustachian) tube cartilage and blends with the palatopharyngeus; it is named for its origin from the tube. The pterygoid hamulus and hyoid are origins of other muscles.'),
('69ce7b9f-ccc1-5233-b766-3322e2b7614f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Largest tonsil:', '["Palatine tonsil","Lingual","Pharyngeal (adenoids)","Sublingual","Tubal"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsil', 'The palatine tonsils are the largest of the tonsils, lying in the tonsillar fossa. The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids), lingual tonsil, and tubal tonsils are smaller.'),
('927af99c-30a8-5a5e-993b-7f996b4d18cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Palatine tonsil located:', '["Soft palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Laryngopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base of tongue mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches","Nasopharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches', 'The palatine tonsil lies in the tonsillar fossa between the palatoglossal arch anteriorly and the palatopharyngeal arch posteriorly. It is not in the nasopharynx, laryngopharynx, or at the base of the tongue.'),
('c6322d3f-8034-5752-82a8-c8833dc25139'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Tonsillar bed formed by:', '["Buccinator resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior constrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle constrictor mediated by specific receptor activation","hyoglossus Masseter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior constrictor palatoglossus palatopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior constrictor palatoglossus palatopharyngeus', 'The tonsillar bed is formed by the superior constrictor muscle with the palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus, and the tonsillar artery enters through it. The buccinator and middle/inferior constrictors do not form the tonsillar bed.'),
('e525cf62-5441-5f14-9f59-eed041f94031'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Tonsillar blood supply mainly from:', '["Tonsillar branch of facial artery","Ascending pharyngeal","Maxillary","Superior thyroid","Lingual"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillar branch of facial artery', 'The main arterial supply of the palatine tonsil is the tonsillar branch of the facial artery, entering through the tonsillar bed. The ascending pharyngeal, lingual, and maxillary arteries contribute smaller branches.'),
('9a379704-7f25-59af-8298-2027b03af6e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Tonsil nerve supply:', '["Hypoglossal (XII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Vagus (X)","Facial (VII)","Trigeminal (V2)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The palatine tonsil receives its nerve supply from the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), and tonsillar pain is referred to the ear via its tympanic branch. The hypoglossal, trigeminal, and facial nerves do not supply the tonsil.'),
('1bf15b34-6509-5b55-b480-5f2f23a76113'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Soft palate muscles supplied by (except tensor):', '["Facial","Hypoglossal","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)', 'All the muscles of the soft palate except the tensor veli palatini are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus via the vagus nerve. The tensor veli palatini is the exception, innervated by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3).'),
('92cd9305-cf30-5379-b207-4a7ff2861ace'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Tensor veli palatini supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V3)","Glossopharyngeal","Vagus","Facial","Hypoglossal"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal (V3)', 'The tensor veli palatini is innervated by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3) through the nerve to medial pterygoid. The other palatal muscles are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus via the vagus.'),
('3871da25-c61d-5f21-8fa8-d43ea10a559a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN) is a branch of which nerve and supplies which muscle?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagus nerve (CN X) — supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","Trigeminal nerve (CN V) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accessory nerve (CN XI) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X) — supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of the vagus (CN X) that supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve. It also provides sensation to the larynx below the vocal folds.'),
('33e1d860-e3e6-5e96-8d6d-92eaebd7d8ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which cartilage of the larynx is the only complete ring?', '["Cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage","Arytenoid cartilage","Epiglottis","Corniculate cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Cricoid cartilage', 'The cricoid cartilage is the only complete cartilaginous ring of the larynx, shaped like a signet ring with a narrow anterior arch and broad posterior lamina. The thyroid, arytenoid, epiglottic, and corniculate cartilages are incomplete or paired.'),
('493f745b-2e42-5c7b-8f7b-5b896a642ee4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid gland consists of:', '["A pyramid and two lobes","Two lobes connected by an isthmus","Four lobes","Three lobes","A single median lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Two lobes connected by an isthmus', 'The thyroid gland consists of two lateral lobes connected across the midline by the isthmus, and it frequently has a pyramidal lobe. It is not divided into four lobes or a single median lobe.'),
('35b662aa-e6f1-5878-a9ea-db7c698f9cbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid gland is located:', '["In the submandibular region","Behind the sternum","In the posterior triangle of neck","Anterior to the trachea","below the thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the trachea', 'The thyroid gland lies in the anterior triangle of the neck, anterior to the trachea, with its lobes overlying the thyroid cartilage and its isthmus across the upper tracheal rings. It is not in the submandibular region, posterior triangle, or behind the sternum.'),
('49c5a4ef-15c2-5b02-a390-a0f3688b6b6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid isthmus lies anterior to which tracheal rings?', '["Thyroid cartilage","1st and 2nd","Cricoid cartilage","2nd","3rd"]'::jsonb, '2nd', 'The thyroid isthmus crosses the anterior trachea, typically overlying the 2nd and 3rd tracheal rings. The 1st ring and the cricoid/thyroid cartilages are above the level of the isthmus.'),
('94451fb9-8464-5b6b-8669-a21777816c11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid capsule is:', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'The thyroid has a true thin fibrous capsule derived from the pretracheal fascia that surrounds the gland and is closely applied to it. The investing fascia and prevertebral fascia are separate fascial layers of the neck.'),
('297b6735-bc0d-53e3-9c99-526afb708acc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The blood supply of the thyroid gland is from:', '["Internal carotid artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)","Vertebral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only inferior thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)', 'The thyroid is supplied by the superior thyroid artery, a branch of the external carotid, and the inferior thyroid artery, a branch of the thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian; an occasional thyroid ima artery may arise from the arch. The vertebral and internal carotid arteries do not supply the gland.'),
('36b3eba7-6d6e-5fb5-8b38-fccf2dafe755'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid is drained by:', '["Vertebral veins","Superior","middle","and inferior thyroid veins","Only anterior jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Superior', 'The thyroid gland is drained by the superior, middle, and inferior thyroid veins; the superior and middle thyroid veins drain into the internal jugular vein, and the inferior thyroid veins drain into the brachiocephalic veins. The vertebral and anterior jugular veins do not drain the gland.'),
('50ceeea9-2929-5988-b729-bc6348b2888b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The external laryngeal nerve is related to:', '["The phrenic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior thyroid artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The recurrent laryngeal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The vagus nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The superior thyroid artery (accompanies it to the cricothyroid muscle)"]'::jsonb, 'The superior thyroid artery (accompanies it to the cricothyroid muscle)', 'The external laryngeal nerve descends with the superior thyroid artery to supply the cricothyroid muscle and lies deep to the superior belly of the omohyoid. The recurrent laryngeal nerve and phrenic nerve are unrelated to this course.'),
('a3adecf4-eab1-5138-aa16-b843d05ab6bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior parathyroid glands develop from:', '["Neural crest","Third pharyngeal pouch","Fourth pharyngeal pouch","First pharyngeal pouch","Second pharyngeal pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth pharyngeal pouch', 'The superior parathyroid glands develop from the dorsal wing of the fourth pharyngeal pouch, while the inferior parathyroids arise from the third pouch. The ultimobranchial body from the fourth pouch forms the parafollicular C cells.'),
('47bb1341-02d2-5f18-9f23-078e9e66b700'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The inferior parathyroid glands develop from:', '["Second pharyngeal pouch","First pharyngeal pouch","Third pharyngeal pouch","Fourth pharyngeal pouch","Thyroglossal duct"]'::jsonb, 'Third pharyngeal pouch', 'The inferior parathyroid glands develop from the third pharyngeal pouch along with the thymus, which explains their descent to a lower position than the superior glands. The fourth pouch gives rise to the superior parathyroids.'),
('78607947-5e08-5409-982d-a75baab6af84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The blood supply of the parathyroid glands is from:', '["Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid","Superior thyroid artery","Vertebral artery","Inferior thyroid artery (mainly)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior thyroid artery (mainly)', 'The parathyroid glands receive their main blood supply from the inferior thyroid artery, with a minor contribution from the superior thyroid artery. The thyrocervical trunk is the parent of the inferior thyroid artery, but the direct supply is via the inferior thyroid branch.'),
('c5fe5474-f24e-5c1c-a568-8182fa0e6ad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid gland develops from:', '["Branchial arches resulting in decreased functional activity","Pharyngeal pouches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroglossal duct (from foramen cecum of tongue)","Laryngeal bud without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroglossal duct (from foramen cecum of tongue)', 'The thyroid gland develops from a midline diverticulum of the pharyngeal floor that descends from the foramen cecum of the tongue along the thyroglossal duct to its final pretracheal position. It is not derived from branchial arches or pouches.'),
('b803121a-a133-555a-9e3d-a2c8820c7d4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst presents:', '["Behind the ear","In the midline of the neck","moving with swallowing and tongue protrusion","In the posterior triangle","In the lateral neck"]'::jsonb, 'In the midline of the neck', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst presents as a midline neck swelling that moves upward with swallowing and tongue protrusion because it remains attached to the tract of the thyroglossal duct. It does not present behind the ear or in the lateral/posterior triangles.'),
('40aecf88-d16f-534c-97d0-f5f35b61e86e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroglossal duct remnants can occur anywhere from:', '["Mandible to hyoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Ear to sternum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base of skull to clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Larynx to trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foramen cecum of tongue to the thyroid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen cecum of tongue to the thyroid gland', 'Thyroglossal duct remnants may occur anywhere along the path of descent from the foramen cecum of the tongue down to the thyroid gland. The other options describe different boundaries not related to this embryological tract.'),
('74f811f2-23d3-536c-9880-32fa93b7abd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid cartilage is:', '["The only complete ring","The smallest laryngeal cartilage","A paired cartilage","A cartilaginous tube","The largest laryngeal cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'The largest laryngeal cartilage', 'The thyroid cartilage is the largest laryngeal cartilage, forming the laryngeal prominence; it is a single shield-like cartilage with aV-shaped notch. It is not a complete ring, a paired cartilage, or the smallest cartilage.'),
('0728e028-7190-5931-939a-9db3e7b96a8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricoid cartilage is:', '["The largest laryngeal cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","A paired cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The only complete ring of the larynx (signet ring shaped)","An elastic cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small nodule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The only complete ring of the larynx (signet ring shaped)', 'The cricoid cartilage is the only complete ring of the larynx and is shaped like a signet ring, with a narrow anterior arch and a tall posterior lamina. It is hyaline cartilage and forms the base of the larynx.'),
('f988c433-6c08-5604-af4e-67d8091b82c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The laryngeal inlet is bounded by:', '["Thyroid and cricoid","Vocal folds only","False vocal cords","Hyoid and epiglottis","Epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 'Epiglottis', 'The laryngeal inlet (aditus) is bounded anteriorly by the epiglottis, laterally by the aryepiglottic folds, and posteriorly by the arytenoid cartilages and interarytenoid notch. Of the listed choices, only the epiglottis is a boundary of the inlet.'),
('bba35239-3652-5290-91f8-2ce8370fc734'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The vocal folds (true cords) are attached:', '["Anteriorly to thyroid cartilage and posteriorly to vocal processes of arytenoids","To the cricoid cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To the trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","To the epiglottis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To the hyoid bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteriorly to thyroid cartilage and posteriorly to vocal processes of arytenoids', 'The true vocal folds attach anteriorly to the posterior surface of the thyroid cartilage angle and posteriorly to the vocal processes of the arytenoid cartilages. The cricoid, trachea, epiglottis, and hyoid bone are not the anchoring points of the true vocal folds.'),
('e2a055c9-928b-5a65-88f6-c2e41a590656'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The laryngeal cavity is divided into:', '["Epiglottis and glottis","Pharynx and larynx","Vestibule","ventricle","and infraglottic cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibule', 'The laryngeal cavity is divided by the vestibular and vocal folds into the vestibule above the vestibular folds, the ventricle (sinus) between the two folds, and the infraglottic cavity below the vocal folds. The vestibule is one of these three compartments.'),
('6382ae30-1bfb-541a-b603-691467afde10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies:', '["Cricothyroid only resulting in decreased functional activity","The pharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","The trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The soft palate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve innervates all the intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid. It does not provide the motor supply to the pharynx, soft palate, or the trachea itself.'),
('578ac482-45e9-5e6c-abd6-1162404094f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The external laryngeal nerve supplies:', '["The arytenoid muscles","The vocalis muscle","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles","Cricothyroid muscle","The pharyngeal constrictors"]'::jsonb, 'Cricothyroid muscle', 'The external laryngeal nerve, the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, supplies the cricothyroid muscle. Every other intrinsic laryngeal muscle is supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
('d51cb94a-014f-5036-987b-6698711b67aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The internal laryngeal nerve supplies:', '["Motor to laryngeal muscles","Parasympathetic to laryngeal glands","Sensation above the vocal folds","Taste to epiglottis","Sensation below the vocal folds"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation above the vocal folds', 'The internal laryngeal nerve, the sensory branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, supplies the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal folds. The mucosa below the folds is served by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
('98bb58bd-eef8-58d6-b93c-d4ee1e2b78ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The larynx is supplied by which arteries?', '["Facial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxillary artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lingual artery mediated by specific receptor activation","External carotid only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior laryngeal (from superior thyroid) and inferior laryngeal (from inferior thyroid)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior laryngeal (from superior thyroid) and inferior laryngeal (from inferior thyroid)', 'The larynx receives blood from the superior laryngeal artery, a branch of the superior thyroid artery, and the inferior laryngeal artery, a branch of the inferior thyroid artery. The facial, maxillary, lingual, and external carotid arteries do not directly supply the larynx.'),
('d87a90d2-4678-567e-9164-819281ab1472'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal constrictor muscles include:', '["Superior","middle","and inferior constrictors","Only middle constrictor","Only superior constrictor"]'::jsonb, 'Superior', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are three overlapping muscles: the superior, middle, and inferior constrictors. The superior constrictor is therefore part of this group of three.'),
('c02004dd-4705-579c-9073-2b19d3438886'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are innervated by:', '["Facial nerve (VII)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Glossopharyngeal alone","Hypoglossal (XII)","Pharyngeal plexus (IX and X)"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (IX and X)', 'The pharyngeal plexus, formed mainly by branches of the vagus (CN X) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves, supplies the pharyngeal constrictors. The facial, trigeminal, and hypoglossal nerves do not motor-innervate them.'),
('b7d74458-090d-5b67-89e2-085daaadad4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The pyriform fossa is:', '["A recess in the nasopharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","A space in the middle ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A depression in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","A fossa in the oral cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A depression in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet"]'::jsonb, 'A depression in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet', 'The pyriform fossa is a shallow depression in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet, where swallowed foreign bodies commonly lodge. The nasopharynx, middle ear, nasal cavity, and oral cavity do not contain it.'),
('16357f3f-8492-5cfb-b245-7a71e04a1f33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of:', '["Accessory nerve (XI)","Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve (VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X)', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of the vagus nerve (CN X). The accessory, hypoglossal, glossopharyngeal, and facial nerves do not give rise to it.'),
('ba38a242-1d15-526a-9bec-0836a375705b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The larynx is located at the level of:', '["C1-C3 vertebrae","C6-T1 vertebrae","T1-T4 vertebrae","C2-C4 vertebrae","C3-C6 vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'C3-C6 vertebrae', 'In the adult, the larynx lies opposite the C3-C6 vertebrae, extending from the epiglottis to the lower border of the cricoid cartilage. The other vertebral levels are higher or lower than the actual position of the larynx.'),
('c92c8f51-b2be-59c8-a128-786d297c0a5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The epiglottis functions to:', '["Warm inspired air","Filter particles","Regulate airflow","Prevent food from entering trachea","Produce sound"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent food from entering trachea', 'During swallowing the epiglottis folds over the laryngeal inlet, directing food and liquid into the esophagus and preventing their entry into the trachea. It does not warm the air, filter particles, regulate airflow, or produce sound.'),
('1d3e7503-5b32-5db8-9675-c05bcc7f9389'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The vocal cords are located in:', '["Nasopharynx","Larynx","Pharynx","Trachea","Bronchi"]'::jsonb, 'Larynx', 'The vocal folds are mucosal structures within the laryngeal cavity. They are not located in the nasopharynx, pharynx proper, trachea, or bronchi.'),
('a475b953-b167-5ee8-9d85-811146e02108'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The trachea extends from:', '["T2 to T8","C4 to C7","C6 to T4-T5","T1 to T6","C3 to C6"]'::jsonb, 'C6 to T4-T5', 'The trachea begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage opposite C6 and ends at the carina at the level of the T4-T5 intervertebral disc. The other ranges describe wrong boundaries.'),
('ca6ec2b4-ea27-5529-a668-a0cf2585a9d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The larynx is innervated by:', '["Cervical plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","Glossopharyngeal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglossal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Recurrent and superior laryngeal nerves (CN X)","Accessory nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent and superior laryngeal nerves (CN X)', 'The larynx is innervated by the recurrent laryngeal and superior laryngeal nerves, both branches of the vagus (CN X). The cervical plexus, glossopharyngeal, hypoglossal, and accessory nerves do not supply the larynx.'),
('01712191-b1a8-5ebf-95a0-5543c2225631'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The Adam''s apple is formed by:', '["Epiglottis","Cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage","Hyoid bone","Arytenoid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid cartilage', 'The laryngeal prominence (Adam''s apple) is formed by the anterior angle of the thyroid cartilage. The epiglottis, cricoid cartilage, hyoid bone, and arytenoid cartilages do not produce it.'),
('d2938c3e-a82d-57ab-bc74-4e0bde6a998f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Where is the thyroid gland located?', '["In the submandibular region","Behind the sternum","In the posterior triangle of neck","Anterior to the trachea","below the thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the trachea', 'The thyroid gland is located in the anterior neck, lying across the trachea just below the thyroid cartilage. The submandibular region, the area behind the sternum, and the posterior triangle of the neck are not its normal site.'),
('b0999a63-bc92-58a1-9d1a-b1adff3a0c89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'In the study of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('614aa147-c099-58f7-9215-c67eada57bf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('0f61e16f-519f-55b8-b780-f1ad333ff286'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('d6642f7f-677b-57c9-8982-73941736f262'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('74de13a6-d80d-5e76-ada1-9eb5c44722de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which option correctly describes the thyroid capsule?', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'The thyroid capsule is a thin fibrous layer of connective tissue, commonly derived from the pretracheal fascia, that closely invests the gland. The investing fascia, prevertebral fascia, skin, and platysma do not form this capsule.'),
('5bd72506-24d1-5572-94e7-b9eee9acd8c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'In the study of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('d6179dcc-3081-5aed-8310-135c2e9b905e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('2d05cde8-7722-5f2d-b34f-23547a156448'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('803bef79-1dce-5077-90a1-aebfceab9b4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('c1b5040d-7900-53bf-b5ec-e5c174173c11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('10ba66ae-20fb-5f95-93f9-2d08ac79a6e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'What is the blood supply of the thyroid gland?', '["Internal carotid artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)","Vertebral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only inferior thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)', 'The thyroid gland is supplied by the superior thyroid artery, a branch of the external carotid artery, and the inferior thyroid artery, a branch of the thyrocervical trunk. Neither the internal carotid nor the vertebral artery supplies it, and no single artery provides its sole blood supply.'),
('ffe863b9-9513-5114-b33e-6e0f67f60ae7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('8f1aa5c1-4015-588f-b764-4c93f76e68b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('c57a881b-5bc4-58b1-abd4-990596e56dff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'What are the parts of the thyroid gland?', '["Three lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Four lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two lobes connected by isthmus","Single mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Single lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two lobes connected by isthmus', 'The thyroid gland has two lobes joined by a midline isthmus, with a pyramidal lobe present in some individuals. It is not a three- or four-lobed mass, a single lobe, or a single mass.'),
('d6040971-cdea-516b-8d45-1ed22894b578'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'At what level does the thyroid isthmus lie?', '["Hyoid","6th ring","2nd-4th tracheal rings","1st tracheal ring","Cricoid"]'::jsonb, '2nd-4th tracheal rings', 'The thyroid isthmus typically crosses the trachea at the 2nd-4th tracheal rings, most often over the second and third rings. It does not lie at the level of the hyoid, cricoid, or first or sixth tracheal ring.'),
('538a9b90-1740-5bc6-95d4-91a65e935152'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'How far do the thyroid lobes extend?', '["Angle of mandible to clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Oblique line of thyroid cartilage to 5th-6th tracheal ring","Cricoid to carina mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid to sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mastoid to sternum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique line of thyroid cartilage to 5th-6th tracheal ring', 'Each thyroid lobe extends from the oblique line on the thyroid cartilage above to the 5th-6th tracheal ring below. The mandible-clavicle, cricoid-carina, hyoid-sternum, and mastoid-sternum spans do not match its extent.'),
('3374f1e9-a635-5716-9389-06b51b3de8ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'What forms the capsule of the thyroid gland?', '["Buccopharyngeal resulting in decreased functional activity","Carotid sheath accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pretracheal fascia (false) and true capsule (fibrous)","Investing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pretracheal fascia (false) and true capsule (fibrous)', 'The thyroid gland is enclosed by an inner true fibrous capsule and an outer false capsule derived from the pretracheal fascia. The buccopharyngeal fascia, carotid sheath, investing layer, and prevertebral fascia are not its capsules.'),
('5278fdcb-1888-5683-aac9-5c24fac0b9a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'How many parathyroid glands are normally present?', '["8 resulting in decreased functional activity","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","6 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 (2 superior, 2 inferior)"]'::jsonb, '4 (2 superior, 2 inferior)', 'There are normally four parathyroid glands, two superior and two inferior, on the posterior surface of the thyroid. Numbers of one, two, six, or eight do not represent the typical count.'),
('e80a34d2-50c3-54e1-b7b1-9c8f8c8434c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Where are the parathyroid glands located?', '["On posterior thyroid surface within capsule","Outside thyroid capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid isthmus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On posterior thyroid surface within capsule', 'The parathyroid glands lie on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes within the thyroid capsule. They are not found outside the capsule, on the anterior surface of the gland, on the isthmus, or in the trachea.'),
('3ad738e8-98f2-55fb-a299-30664032796e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From which pharyngeal pouch do the superior parathyroid glands develop?', '["1st pouch","2nd pouch","5th pouch","3rd pouch","4th pharyngeal pouch"]'::jsonb, '4th pharyngeal pouch', 'The superior parathyroid glands develop from the dorsal endoderm of the fourth pharyngeal pouch. The first, second, third, and fifth pouches give rise to other derivatives.'),
('d1052f9c-3462-5650-b86a-2203cd9c1e00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From which pharyngeal pouch do the inferior parathyroid glands develop?', '["3rd pharyngeal pouch","5th pouch","2nd pouch","1st pouch","4th pouch"]'::jsonb, '3rd pharyngeal pouch', 'The inferior parathyroid glands develop from the third pharyngeal pouch along with the thymus. The first, second, fourth, and fifth pouches form other structures.'),
('401bce9f-c0cc-510a-9fbe-92667883b0bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which artery forms a major blood supply of the thyroid gland?', '["Only superior thyroid","Only inferior thyroid","Only thyroid ima","Superior thyroid (ECA)","inferior thyroid (thyrocervical trunk) thyroid ima Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid (ECA)', 'The superior thyroid artery, a branch of the external carotid artery, is one of the major arteries supplying the thyroid gland. The inferior thyroid artery and an occasional thyroid ima artery supplement this supply.'),
('a80e2e77-fcd5-583b-a2bf-d9cb419bce41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior thyroid artery is a branch of which vessel?', '["Facial","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid artery","Subclavian","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The superior thyroid artery arises from the external carotid artery as one of its first branches. The facial artery, thyrocervical trunk, subclavian, and internal carotid do not give origin to it.'),
('78e2ffa3-e9eb-51a2-af6d-c6526f49ef01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('79f29c21-85f9-5785-a93b-98b0c060c800'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From which vessel does the thyroid ima artery arise when present?', '["Brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch","Internal carotid","External carotid","Subclavian","Thyrocervical trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch', 'The thyroid ima artery, when present, ascends in the midline and usually arises from the brachiocephalic trunk or the aortic arch. The internal and external carotids, subclavian, and thyrocervical trunk are not its usual origin.'),
('54c77d35-0f55-5783-a6fd-560bf010e51f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'How do the thyroid veins drain?', '["Only inferior to brachiocephalic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior to IJV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only middle to IJV mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior and middle to IJV; inferior to brachiocephalic","All to azygos leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and middle to IJV; inferior to brachiocephalic', 'The superior and middle thyroid veins drain into the internal jugular vein, while the inferior thyroid vein drains into the brachiocephalic vein. The azygos vein and the single draining pathways are wrong for this pattern.'),
('4cdb4ee0-5f10-53f4-bb61-757c67ca0a16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Facial (VII)","Vagus (CN X)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (CN X)', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of the vagus nerve (CN X) that hooks under the right subclavian artery or around the aortic arch before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. The facial, hypoglossal, trigeminal, and glossopharyngeal nerves do not give rise to it.'),
('564e9dda-72e1-5fe7-8aa4-72ebe940a083'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which structure does the right recurrent laryngeal nerve hook around?', '["Right subclavian artery","Aortic arch","Right IJV","Right vertebral","Right common carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Right subclavian artery', 'On the right side, the recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the right subclavian artery before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. The aortic arch, right internal jugular, right vertebral, and right common carotid are not the right-side hooking structures.'),
('10e6daf0-2214-523b-8d54-de17c81b3962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which structure does the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hook around?', '["Left IJV resulting in decreased functional activity","Innominate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left common carotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Left subclavian without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic arch (at ligamentum arteriosum)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (at ligamentum arteriosum)', 'On the left side, the recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around the aortic arch at the ligamentum arteriosum before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. The left jugular, innominate, left common carotid, and left subclavian are not the left-side hooking structures.'),
('f7a21609-0419-515b-9ad1-a56d86d49a16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which muscles does the recurrent laryngeal nerve supply?', '["Cricothyroid only resulting in decreased functional activity","Palatal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","All intrinsic muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pharyngeal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies all the intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid, which is innervated by the external laryngeal nerve. The palatal and pharyngeal muscles are not included in its motor supply.'),
('e5de99ea-6dc4-58ce-80a0-ab795bc27877'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which muscle does the external laryngeal nerve supply?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid","Thyroarytenoid","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Cricothyroid muscle","Vocalis"]'::jsonb, 'Cricothyroid muscle', 'The external laryngeal nerve, the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, innervates the cricothyroid muscle. The posterior cricoarytenoid, thyroarytenoid, lateral cricoarytenoid, and vocalis are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
('b19dd1a2-078c-57e1-95b2-9bbc6571b297'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior laryngeal nerve is a branch of which nerve?', '["Facial (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Vagus (CN X)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (CN X)', 'The superior laryngeal nerve arises from the vagus nerve (CN X) near the inferior vagal ganglion and descends within the neck. The facial, glossopharyngeal, hypoglossal, and trigeminal nerves do not give rise to it.'),
('17721992-5f7c-5990-a560-0e50f8125317'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior laryngeal nerve divides into which branches?', '["Anterior and posterior","Internal and external laryngeal nerves","Medial and lateral","Motor and sensory","Superior and inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external laryngeal nerves', 'The superior laryngeal nerve divides into the internal laryngeal nerve (sensory) and the external laryngeal nerve (motor to the cricothyroid muscle). The other pairs describe wrong divisions or divisions of other nerves.'),
('0d849e22-2e71-525c-8821-a6a5cdcd0a5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which of the following does the internal laryngeal nerve supply?', '["Sensation below cords","Motor to all intrinsic","Sensation to laryngeal mucosa above vocal cords","Motor to cricothyroid","Sensation to trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation to laryngeal mucosa above vocal cords', 'The internal laryngeal nerve, a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, gives sensation to the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal cords. Motor innervation of the intrinsic muscles and sensation below the cords are provided by other branches.'),
('9018938b-335b-5518-9c53-704d9679307a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve (RLN) supplies sensation to:', '["Laryngeal mucosa below vocal cords","Above cords","Tracheal mucosa","Esophageal mucosa","Pharyngeal mucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngeal mucosa below vocal cords', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies sensation to the laryngeal mucosa below the vocal cords. The mucosa above the cords is served by the internal laryngeal nerve.'),
('43996bb6-ace6-514d-a6a8-130bea014195'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Unilateral injury to the recurrent laryngeal nerve causes:', '["Hoarseness (vocal cord paralysis)","Dysphagia","Facial paralysis","Shoulder weakness","Tongue deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Hoarseness (vocal cord paralysis)', 'Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury paralyses one vocal cord, producing hoarseness. Facial paralysis, shoulder weakness, and tongue deviation follow injury to other cranial nerves.'),
('5927e30b-fdaa-5aaf-88cb-85168a2180bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Bilateral injury to the recurrent laryngeal nerves causes:', '["Hoarseness only","Dysphagia only","Respiratory distress/stridor","Cough only","symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory distress/stridor', 'Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury leaves both vocal cords closed near the midline, causing respiratory distress and stridor. Unilateral injury mainly produces hoarseness rather than airway obstruction.'),
('d1968645-b2cc-598c-97e2-ae5b6923e3ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which of the following are paired laryngeal cartilages?', '["Cricoid + thyroid","Thyroid","cricoid","epiglottis Arytenoid + thyroid Only arytenoid","Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform', 'The paired laryngeal cartilages are the arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform. The thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis are unpaired cartilages.'),
('53d9f07f-36c5-50d6-9c49-d807192b32a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which of the following is an unpaired laryngeal cartilage?', '["Arytenoid + thyroid","Cricoid + thyroid","Thyroid","cricoid","epiglottis Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform Only cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid', 'The unpaired laryngeal cartilages are the thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis, making thyroid a valid unpaired cartilage. The arytenoid is a paired cartilage.'),
('80f333f2-5215-503e-9ca0-1c8ad3f5b7cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which is the largest laryngeal cartilage?', '["Epiglottis","Arytenoid","Thyroid cartilage","Cricoid","Corniculate"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid cartilage', 'The thyroid cartilage is the largest laryngeal cartilage. The cricoid is smaller and complete ring-shaped, while arytenoid and corniculate are paired.'),
('499a6981-17d3-5b77-a49e-f83a29a49801'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricoid cartilage is shaped like a:', '["Square resulting in decreased functional activity","Signet ring (narrow anterior, broad posterior)","Oval mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete circle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Signet ring (narrow anterior, broad posterior)', 'The cricoid cartilage forms a complete signet ring, narrow in front and broad as a lamina behind. It is the only cartilage encircling the airway completely.'),
('d96f5901-98d5-5876-b588-2295da653315'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'At which vertebral level is the cricoid cartilage situated?', '["C6","C4","C7","C5","T1"]'::jsonb, 'C6', 'The cricoid cartilage lies at the C6 vertebral level, where the larynx ends and the trachea begins. The thyroid cartilage lies at C4-C5.'),
('2c027dbf-1208-5cf5-8635-6dcff61be585'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'At which vertebral level is the thyroid cartilage situated?', '["C2-C3","C5-C6","C3-C4","C6-C7","C4-C5"]'::jsonb, 'C4-C5', 'The thyroid cartilage lies at the C4-C5 vertebral level. The cricoid is at C6 and the tracheal bifurcation at T4/T5.'),
('75315402-5631-581a-a669-7d867ae08b79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The arytenoid cartilages are:', '["Unpaired","C-shaped","Hyaline","Paired","pyramid-shaped with vocal and muscular processes Small nodules"]'::jsonb, 'Paired', 'The arytenoids are paired hyaline cartilages located on the cricoid lamina; each bears a vocal and a muscular process. They are not unpaired structures.'),
('37667c21-d8db-5c47-b87c-2b1c06502aaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The epiglottis is:', '["Paired resulting in decreased functional activity","Leaf-shaped elastic cartilage guarding laryngeal inlet","Ring-shaped mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyaline cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flat bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leaf-shaped elastic cartilage guarding laryngeal inlet', 'The epiglottis is a leaf-shaped elastic cartilage that guards the laryngeal inlet and prevents aspiration during swallowing. It is neither paired nor ring-like.'),
('5519bb29-007f-5c74-86a3-b1917f5d076a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricothyroid joint allows:', '["Only gliding resulting in decreased functional activity","movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rotation and gliding for pitch adjustment","Only rotation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rotation and gliding for pitch adjustment', 'The cricothyroid joint allows rotation and gliding of the thyroid cartilage, tilting it to tense and lengthen the vocal cords for pitch adjustment.'),
('c35b573a-c644-5edf-8035-8045118a9b91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricoarytenoid joint allows:', '["Only rotation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only gliding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","movement mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertical movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rotation and gliding for vocal cord movement"]'::jsonb, 'Rotation and gliding for vocal cord movement', 'The cricoarytenoid joint is a synovial joint allowing rotation and gliding of the arytenoid cartilages, which opens and closes the vocal cords.'),
('36d14fc0-93c3-54ab-9dea-2dcfab919269'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which of the following is an intrinsic laryngeal muscle?', '["Only cricothyroid","Only thyroarytenoid","Cricothyroid","PCA LCA transverse arytenoid oblique arytenoid","thyroarytenoid vocalis Only arytenoids Only PCA"]'::jsonb, 'Cricothyroid', 'The cricothyroid is an intrinsic laryngeal muscle; the other intrinsic muscles include thyroarytenoid, vocalis, posterior and lateral cricoarytenoid, and the arytenoids.'),
('2bdcdde0-3d8d-5763-b050-abb60a975212'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which muscle is the only abductor of the vocal cords?', '["Cricothyroid","Vocalis","Posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA)","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Transverse arytenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA)', 'The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only muscle that abducts the vocal cords. All other intrinsic laryngeal muscles adduct, tense, or relax the cords.'),
('5043f638-b79b-58db-8859-97509fa66b87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which muscles adduct the vocal cords?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Cricothyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vocalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral cricoarytenoid + transverse arytenoid","Thyroarytenoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cricoarytenoid + transverse arytenoid', 'The lateral cricoarytenoid and transverse arytenoid adduct the vocal cords. The posterior cricoarytenoid abducts and the cricothyroid tenses them.'),
('9064cb45-7716-5e15-ac0f-5cb7e127f0be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricothyroid muscle tenses the vocal cords by:', '["Abducting arytenoids resulting in decreased functional activity","Raising larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relaxing cords mediated by specific receptor activation","Adducting arytenoids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulling thyroid forward increasing tension"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling thyroid forward increasing tension', 'The cricothyroid pulls the thyroid cartilage forward on the cricoid, increasing tension and lengthening the vocal cords to raise pitch.'),
('fb3ef73e-2326-57c7-ad52-45ed17b7c35a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroarytenoid muscle relaxes the vocal cords by:', '["Tensing cords","Pulling arytenoid to thyroid","shortening cords","Rotating arytenoid","Abducting cords Adducting cords"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling arytenoid to thyroid', 'The thyroarytenoid pulls the arytenoid toward the thyroid cartilage, shortening and relaxing the vocal cords to lower pitch.'),
('99312764-0b51-5c49-999e-65183f074f71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The vocalis muscle is:', '["Part of PCA","Cartilage","Part of cricothyroid","Medial part of thyroarytenoid","fine-tunes cord tension Separate muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Medial part of thyroarytenoid', 'The vocalis is the medial part of the thyroarytenoid muscle that fine-tunes the tension of the vocal cords.'),
('3e3e4399-419f-53a1-a7f8-3b8ad4852dbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The rima glottidis is:', '["Cricothyroid space","Laryngeal inlet","Opening between false cords","Opening between true vocal cords","Tracheal opening"]'::jsonb, 'Opening between true vocal cords', 'The rima glottidis is the narrow opening between the true vocal cords. The laryngeal inlet lies above and is guarded by the epiglottis.'),
('5c074255-d2b9-543d-af0f-82891eb4a7db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The laryngeal ventricle is:', '["Vallecula resulting in decreased functional activity","Recess between vestibular and vocal folds","Piriform sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Tracheal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laryngeal inlet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recess between vestibular and vocal folds', 'The laryngeal ventricle is a recess between the vestibular (false) folds above and the vocal folds below. The saccule arises from its anterior end.'),
('fb83faed-2afc-505f-8d95-f00dc5a83308'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The laryngeal saccule is:', '["Cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessel mediated by specific receptor activation","Blind pouch extending upward from ventricle","Nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blind pouch extending upward from ventricle', 'The laryngeal saccule is a blind pouch that projects upward from the anterior part of the laryngeal ventricle and contains mucous glands lubricating the cords.'),
('cf79dee9-c12a-5ac2-baf2-8ee34cf444f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The true vocal cords are formed by:', '["Vocal ligament (conus elasticus edge) with thin mucosa","Vestibular ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aryepiglottic fold mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroid lamina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cricoid lamina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vocal ligament (conus elasticus edge) with thin mucosa', 'The true vocal cords are formed by the vocal ligament, the free upper edge of the conus elasticus, covered by a thin adherent mucosa.'),
('2577be2b-6d37-5194-9550-866efc0c45bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The false vocal cords are formed by:', '["Free edge of quadrangular membrane with thicker mucosa","Thyrohyoid membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conus elasticus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cricothyroid ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vocal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Free edge of quadrangular membrane with thicker mucosa', 'The false (vestibular) vocal cords are formed by the free lower edge of the quadrangular membrane, covered by thicker mucosa. The vocal ligament instead forms the true cords.'),
('b17cf2a8-a565-514c-8c0a-dc41efcb6f25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The quadrangular membrane extends:', '["Arytenoid/epiglottis to ventricular fold","Cricoid to trachea","Thyroid to cricoid","Epiglottis to trachea","Hyoid to thyroid"]'::jsonb, 'Arytenoid/epiglottis to ventricular fold', 'The quadrangular membrane extends from the arytenoid and epiglottis down to the ventricular fold; its lower free edge forms the vestibular ligament of the false cord.'),
('e96c5176-9e21-5a12-8981-fc01a4bdfbf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The conus elasticus extends:', '["Epiglottis to arytenoid","Hyoid to thyroid","Cricoid cartilage to vocal ligament","Cricoid to trachea","Thyroid to hyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Cricoid cartilage to vocal ligament', 'The conus elasticus extends from the upper border of the cricoid cartilage up to the vocal ligament, whose free edge forms the framework of the true vocal cord.'),
('fbe1f61b-bd1c-5b55-b1cd-98a32e9bc1da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyrohyoid membrane is pierced by the:', '["Internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal artery","Hypoglossal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior laryngeal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","RLN without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal artery', 'The thyrohyoid membrane is pierced by the internal laryngeal nerve and the superior laryngeal artery. The recurrent laryngeal nerve and inferior vessels enter at a lower level.'),
('d486f832-a445-556a-aecf-1196418068e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricothyroid membrane is the site for:', '["Emergency cricothyroidotomy","RLN passage","Inferior laryngeal artery","None","Superior laryngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Emergency cricothyroidotomy', 'The cricothyroid membrane is the site for emergency cricothyroidotomy. It lies just below the thyroid cartilage and is relatively avascular.'),
('d79bc965-5160-5915-a65c-0df95a69da5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The larynx receives its innervation from the:', '["Superior laryngeal (sensory + motor cricothyroid) and recurrent laryngeal","Only vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only superior laryngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only RLN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior laryngeal (sensory + motor cricothyroid) and recurrent laryngeal', 'The larynx is innervated by the superior laryngeal nerve, which gives sensory branches above the cords and motor supply to the cricothyroid, and by the recurrent laryngeal nerve for the remaining muscles and subglottic sensation.'),
('1a8c079e-bd3b-5db9-b037-3cf83982a790'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The blood supply of the larynx comes from the:', '["Maxillary resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only superior thyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Only inferior thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior laryngeal (from superior thyroid) and inferior laryngeal (from inferior thyroid)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior laryngeal (from superior thyroid) and inferior laryngeal (from inferior thyroid)', 'The larynx is supplied by the superior laryngeal artery from the superior thyroid artery and the inferior laryngeal artery from the inferior thyroid artery.'),
('76a8fad6-3f5b-51f7-bf56-c48206c8b7ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Supraglottic lymphatics drain to the:', '["Parotid","Retropharyngeal","Submandibular","Lower deep cervical","Upper deep cervical nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Upper deep cervical nodes', 'Supraglottic lymphatics drain mainly to the upper deep cervical nodes. The subglottic region drains to the lower deep cervical and paratracheal nodes.'),
('be426191-8aeb-51f5-b677-c8940a604e99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Infraglottic lymphatics drain to the:', '["Upper deep cervical","Retropharyngeal","Submandibular","Lower deep cervical (paratracheal)","Submental"]'::jsonb, 'Lower deep cervical (paratracheal)', 'Infraglottic lymphatics drain to the lower deep cervical and paratracheal nodes. The supraglottic region drains to the upper deep cervical nodes.'),
('f0550f05-27dd-5694-9eff-d5cde573d35b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The laryngeal inlet is guarded by the:', '["Vestibular folds","Vocal cords","Thyroid cartilage","Epiglottis","aryepiglottic folds arytenoids Cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Epiglottis', 'The laryngeal inlet is guarded by the epiglottis, which closes over the inlet during swallowing. The vestibular folds and vocal cords lie within the laryngeal cavity.'),
('6d0190df-5763-5012-b1e6-2aebb1fde58c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('f00f0e5d-a239-564c-8a5c-fc123b24f965'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The trachea begins at the:', '["Lower border of cricoid (C6)","Epiglottis","Hyoid","Cricothyroid membrane","Upper thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Lower border of cricoid (C6)', 'The trachea begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at the C6 level and bifurcates at T4/T5.'),
('fbd4e4e0-2ff5-56ae-9e2c-a8cdadd5e1d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The trachea bifurcates at the:', '["T10 resulting in decreased functional activity","C6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T6 mediated by specific receptor activation","T2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T4/T5 (sternal angle)"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 (sternal angle)', 'The trachea bifurcates at the T4/T5 level, corresponding to the sternal angle. The carina divides it into the right and left main bronchi.'),
('c00066a8-c198-555b-90d0-bd7781e22783'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The right main bronchus is:', '["Same as left","Longer","narrower","more horizontal Absent More horizontal","Shorter wider more vertical than left"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter wider more vertical than left', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left, which is why inhaled foreign bodies most often lodge in the right main bronchus.'),
('bb06bb52-ab35-5e37-82c4-a1aa7b0dd3f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('f0df4c51-7c90-5d69-809b-08eaea001792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which of the following is a division of the pharynx?', '["Nasopharynx","oropharynx laryngopharynx","Anterior posterior lateral Oral","nasal laryngeal Superior middle","inferior Upper middle lower"]'::jsonb, 'Nasopharynx', 'The pharynx has three divisions: nasopharynx, oropharynx, and laryngopharynx. Nasopharynx is the part lying behind the nasal cavity.'),
('170b6c97-f4e4-59f5-8a0e-6696d91650dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The fossa of Rosenmüller is:', '["Oropharyngeal recess","Piriform sinus","Tonsillar bed","Recess in nasopharynx lateral to tubal elevation","common site of nasopharyngeal carcinoma Laryngopharyngeal recess"]'::jsonb, 'Recess in nasopharynx lateral to tubal elevation', 'The fossa of Rosenmüller, or pharyngeal recess, is a deep recess in the nasopharynx lateral to the tubal elevation. It is a common site of origin of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.'),
('6f21dc08-3b9f-5402-8563-397293388c76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'How many layers form the pharyngeal wall?', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","6 2 mediated by specific receptor activation","4: mucosa pharyngobasilar fascia","muscle buccopharyngeal fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4: mucosa pharyngobasilar fascia', 'The pharyngeal wall has four layers: mucosa, pharyngobasilar fascia, the muscular layer, and buccopharyngeal fascia. The pharyngobasilar fascia replaces the missing muscular layer above the superior constrictor.'),
('6a6b8161-f25f-53dc-b53e-2d20538704bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are supplied by the:', '["Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)","Facial","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal","Hypoglossal"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are supplied by the pharyngeal plexus, formed mainly from branches of the vagus nerve. Motor fibres reach them via this plexus.'),
('f44679bd-e067-5d3d-b5a7-e58f3df37eaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal plexus is formed by the:', '["CN IX CN X","and sympathetic fibers CN X only","CN V VII","IX","CN IX only CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'IX', 'The pharyngeal plexus is formed by the glossopharyngeal (CN IX), vagus (CN X), and sympathetic fibers. It carries sensation and motor supply to the pharyngeal muscles.'),
('c9cd18a7-5320-57dd-8883-3fba8452b68e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior constrictor originates from the:', '["Mandible","Pterygoid hamulus","pterygomandibular raphe","mylohyoid line","Hyoid Thyroid cartilage Cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Pterygoid hamulus', 'The superior constrictor arises from the pterygoid hamulus, pterygomandibular raphe, mylohyoid line of the mandible, and the side of the tongue.'),
('ba25c757-1d30-5829-afa9-83cd54795a3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The middle constrictor originates from the:', '["Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid","stylohyoid ligament","Thyroid","Mandible","Pterygoid hamulus Cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Greater and lesser cornua of hyoid', 'The middle constrictor arises from the greater and lesser cornua of the hyoid bone and the stylohyoid ligament.'),
('1d1b4396-6888-512c-a1e1-3515117724bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The inferior constrictor originates from the:', '["Pterygoid hamulus resulting in decreased functional activity","Palatine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid and cricoid cartilages","Hyoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mandible leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid and cricoid cartilages', 'The inferior constrictor arises from the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage and the cricoid cartilage, and its lowermost part forms the cricopharyngeus.'),
('09682f5c-9ed1-5502-a593-1f08c794526c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricopharyngeus is:', '["Longitudinal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Middle constrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of inferior constrictor; acts as upper esophageal sphincter","Palatal muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior constrictor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Part of inferior constrictor; acts as upper esophageal sphincter', 'The cricopharyngeus is the lowest part of the inferior constrictor and functions as the upper esophageal sphincter, guarding the junction of the pharynx and esophagus.'),
('ceb66bd5-f828-598a-94ac-fcf1f1625cc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('2ae1a2a7-d977-5a4b-9fcc-8e30bf5275d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Zenker''s diverticulum occurs at the:', '["Pyriform sinus","Vallecula","Tonsillar bed","Pharyngeal recess","Killian''s dehiscence"]'::jsonb, 'Killian''s dehiscence', 'Zenker''s diverticulum arises at Killian''s dehiscence, the weak area between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus muscles. The pyriform sinus, vallecula, and recesses are other pharyngeal sites.'),
('3be4e7b6-8b66-56aa-aa9b-1e1054464819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Stylopharyngeus muscle is supplied by which nerve?', '["Vagus (X)","Facial (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The stylopharyngeus is the only pharyngeal muscle supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), which descends between the superior and middle constrictors to reach it. The vagus and its pharyngeal plexus innervate all the remaining pharyngeal muscles, whereas CN VII, XII, and V play no role in its innervation.'),
('06af74af-929b-51d5-a61a-4cc509cb2f08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('e2b01c4a-7434-5446-938d-54f27ba0f139'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The auditory tube opens into which part of the pharynx?', '["Oropharynx posterior","Roof","Oropharynx lateral wall","Posterior wall","Lateral wall of nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wall of nasopharynx', 'The pharyngeal opening of the auditory tube lies high on the lateral wall of the nasopharynx, just behind the inferior nasal concha. This opening is surrounded by the tubal cartilage and the salpingopharyngeal fold.'),
('8c8c648b-ab57-5f5e-99d1-ccc03b20bd35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The salpingopharyngeus muscle arises from which structure?', '["Auditory tube cartilage","Palatine","Thyroid","Hyoid","Pterygoid hamulus"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory tube cartilage', 'The salpingopharyngeus arises from the cartilage of the auditory (pharyngotympanic) tube and descends to blend with the longitudinal pharyngeal musculature, elevating the pharynx during swallowing. Its origin is the tubal cartilage, not the pterygoid hamulus, hyoid, thyroid, or palate.'),
('616882e5-cb40-5b36-b6a2-1c24b735ef90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which tonsil is the largest?', '["Palatine tonsil","Lingual","Pharyngeal (adenoids)","Sublingual","Tubal"]'::jsonb, 'Palatine tonsil', 'The palatine tonsils are the largest tonsils, lying in the tonsillar fossa of the oropharynx. The pharyngeal tonsil (adenoids) is largest in childhood but regresses, while the lingual, tubal, and palatine tonsils complete Waldeyer''s ring.'),
('00f4f5a9-82fd-52dc-99d3-a442b8e3e350'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Where is the palatine tonsil located?', '["Soft palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Laryngopharynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base of tongue mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches","Nasopharynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillar fossa between palatoglossal and palatopharyngeal arches', 'The palatine tonsil occupies the tonsillar fossa of the oropharynx, bounded anteriorly by the palatoglossal arch and posteriorly by the palatopharyngeal arch. The lingual tonsil lies at the base of the tongue and the pharyngeal tonsil in the nasopharynx.'),
('d3c0d315-5450-5ca9-9ea2-8bc17bf19bc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which structures form the tonsillar bed?', '["Buccinator resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior constrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle constrictor mediated by specific receptor activation","hyoglossus Masseter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior constrictor palatoglossus palatopharyngeus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior constrictor palatoglossus palatopharyngeus', 'The tonsillar bed is formed by the superior pharyngeal constrictor together with the palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus muscles. The buccinator, masseter, hyoglossus, and the middle and inferior constrictors are not part of this bed.'),
('a2934f36-2a7e-5acb-a53b-cdddccf5ce7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The main blood supply of the tonsil arises from the:', '["Tonsillar branch of facial artery","Ascending pharyngeal","Maxillary","Superior thyroid","Lingual"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsillar branch of facial artery', 'The tonsillar artery, a branch of the facial artery, is the principal blood supply of the palatine tonsil, piercing the superior constrictor to reach it. The lingual, ascending pharyngeal, and maxillary arteries contribute only minor collaterals.'),
('f6aa0799-c9ae-5d41-9949-e1b51cab9e95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The tonsil receives its sensory nerve supply from which nerve?', '["Hypoglossal (XII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Vagus (X)","Facial (VII)","Trigeminal (V2)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX)', 'The palatine tonsil is supplied mainly by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), which also innervates the posterior third of the tongue. CN VII, X, XII, and V2 do not provide the dominant sensory supply to this structure.'),
('5cb25e0d-35dd-5ec0-bd44-790be29de890'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The soft palate muscles, except tensor veli palatini, are supplied by which structure?', '["Facial","Hypoglossal","Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (vagus)', 'The levator veli palatini, palatoglossus, palatopharyngeus, and musculus uvulae are innervated by the pharyngeal plexus, which is derived largely from the vagus nerve. Tensor veli palatini is the exception because it is supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve.'),
('0907a095-5475-530a-b60b-191223160870'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('184d6e23-def1-5e43-aeae-0a76774027a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which nerve gives rise to the recurrent laryngeal nerve and which muscle does it supply?', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagus nerve (CN X) — supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","Trigeminal nerve (CN V) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accessory nerve (CN XI) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X) — supplies all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of the vagus nerve (CN X) and innervates all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except the cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve. CN IX, V, XI, and XII have no such laryngeal muscular distribution.'),
('491e3b91-7e68-5380-a4ae-060847162eb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'In the study of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('dad2b79f-2675-53fc-b4bd-c4876136c166'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('bf82b1e6-6074-57e2-8585-9b78db8e7297'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('59c2aa47-53a4-5a26-9821-696217035c81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('24eba748-5ad1-530a-b913-bbc398d43655'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'What is the thyroid capsule?', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'The thyroid is surrounded by a thin true capsule derived from the pretracheal (visceral) fascia of the neck. The investing layer, prevertebral fascia, skin, and platysma are separate, more superficial cervical structures.'),
('85dc58dc-dffd-5180-a98a-c72979970d80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The blood supply of the thyroid gland arises from which vessels?', '["Internal carotid artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)","Vertebral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only inferior thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)', 'The thyroid gland is supplied mainly by paired superior thyroid arteries, from the external carotid, and inferior thyroid arteries, from the thyrocervical trunk of the subclavian. The internal carotid and vertebral arteries give it no branches.'),
('805a4064-885f-5a34-9415-f217bdaafbd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid gland is drained by which veins?', '["Vertebral veins","Superior","middle","and inferior thyroid veins","Only anterior jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Superior', 'The thyroid gland drains via the superior, middle, and inferior thyroid veins; the superior and middle pairs empty into the internal jugular vein, while the inferior thyroid veins drain into the brachiocephalic veins. The anterior jugular and vertebral veins are not the primary drainage route.'),
('54b27277-8fe5-5b13-833a-1f94784d0ebd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The external laryngeal nerve is most closely related to which structure?', '["The phrenic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior thyroid artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The recurrent laryngeal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The vagus nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The superior thyroid artery (accompanies it to the cricothyroid muscle)"]'::jsonb, 'The superior thyroid artery (accompanies it to the cricothyroid muscle)', 'The external laryngeal nerve descends beside the superior thyroid artery and supplies the cricothyroid muscle, making the artery its surgical landmark. The inferior thyroid artery and recurrent laryngeal nerve relate instead to the lower pole of the gland.'),
('6fd1cf00-4ab6-5422-9118-488e1bd98cd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management of Thyroid Larynx Pharynx, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('cb195801-4e20-5c18-b6ae-1ae565837f51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('c98947b7-0613-56de-b4c5-fff393573391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The blood supply of the parathyroid glands is mainly from which artery?', '["Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid","Superior thyroid artery","Vertebral artery","Inferior thyroid artery (mainly)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior thyroid artery (mainly)', 'The parathyroid glands are supplied chiefly by branches of the inferior thyroid artery, which arise from the thyrocervical trunk. The superior thyroid, external carotid, and vertebral arteries are not the principal source.'),
('daf7a9be-2c34-5197-a38e-b14ae118f17b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('342d990e-1e8f-5523-bb76-12389294e403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst characteristically presents:', '["Behind the ear","In the midline of the neck","moving with swallowing and tongue protrusion","In the posterior triangle","In the lateral neck"]'::jsonb, 'In the midline of the neck', 'A thyroglossal duct cyst forms a midline neck swelling, usually near the hyoid bone, and moves upward with tongue protrusion and swallowing because of its attachment to the duct tract. Lateral neck or posterior triangle masses point to a different origin.'),
('fc8295bb-f35d-50ed-bcd4-c2b70e6b84b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Thyroglossal duct remnants can occur anywhere along which extent?', '["Mandible to hyoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Ear to sternum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base of skull to clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Larynx to trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foramen cecum of tongue to the thyroid gland"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen cecum of tongue to the thyroid gland', 'The thyroglossal duct traces the descent of the thyroid primordium from the foramen cecum of the tongue to the gland''s final position in the anterior neck, so remnants can persist anywhere along this path. The other extents do not match this embryological course.'),
('b3fc9615-8d36-583b-9c1e-51c13cc1e47a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which cartilage is the thyroid cartilage?', '["The only complete ring","The smallest laryngeal cartilage","A paired cartilage","A cartilaginous tube","The largest laryngeal cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'The largest laryngeal cartilage', 'The thyroid cartilage is the largest laryngeal cartilage and forms the laryngeal prominence (Adam''s apple) where its two laminae meet anteriorly. It is unpaired, unlike the arytenoid cartilages, and is not a complete ring because that distinction belongs to the cricoid.'),
('a02ef4e6-686d-5424-84ea-bc74e681ebd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which cartilage is the cricoid cartilage?', '["The largest laryngeal cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","A paired cartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The only complete ring of the larynx (signet ring shaped)","An elastic cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small nodule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The only complete ring of the larynx (signet ring shaped)', 'The cricoid cartilage is the only complete ring of the larynx and has a signet-ring shape, with a tall posterior lamina and a narrow anterior arch. It is unpaired hyaline cartilage and is smaller than the thyroid cartilage above it.'),
('10cb3914-7145-5829-b047-daf0ea748dee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which structure bounds the laryngeal inlet anteriorly?', '["Thyroid and cricoid","Vocal folds only","False vocal cords","Hyoid and epiglottis","Epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 'Epiglottis', 'The laryngeal inlet (aditus) is bounded anteriorly by the epiglottis, laterally by the aryepiglottic folds, and posteriorly by the interarytenoid fold. The vocal folds and false cords lie within the laryngeal cavity rather than forming its inlet.'),
('4bdc10d1-d2c9-59db-9c0b-a2d6376edc9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('8314827f-9d20-599d-a2ff-265259d2cddf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The laryngeal cavity is divided into which three parts?', '["Epiglottis and glottis","Pharynx and larynx","Vestibule","ventricle","and infraglottic cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibule', 'The laryngeal cavity is subdivided into the vestibule above the vestibular folds, the ventricle between the vestibular and vocal folds, and the infraglottic cavity below the vocal folds. It is not split into the epiglottis and glottis or the pharynx and larynx.'),
('7805dc3f-1129-5c1f-bdd5-4902994bf3f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('e8b68122-6dcd-57c2-8c6f-c7a7dbb30727'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('07a71de0-315e-56b4-bf16-3f0600575019'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'What does the internal laryngeal nerve supply?', '["Motor to laryngeal muscles","Parasympathetic to laryngeal glands","Sensation above the vocal folds","Taste to epiglottis","Sensation below the vocal folds"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation above the vocal folds', 'The internal laryngeal nerve, the inner branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, provides sensory innervation to the laryngeal mucosa down to the vocal folds. Sensation below the folds is carried by the recurrent laryngeal nerve, and it carries no motor fibers.'),
('74d399f4-a7f3-5fde-a667-903181874faf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Thyroid Larynx Pharynx: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('51b85009-800a-5ace-9a04-22ae6f5da86f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which muscles make up the pharyngeal constrictors?', '["Superior","middle","and inferior constrictors","Only middle constrictor","Only superior constrictor"]'::jsonb, 'Superior', 'The pharyngeal constrictor group comprises the superior, middle, and inferior constrictor muscles, which overlap like stacked cups and narrow the pharyngeal wall during swallowing. No single constrictor alone forms the complete group.'),
('a8745007-2c2a-5193-9786-4d9850c765a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are innervated by which structure?', '["Facial nerve (VII)","Trigeminal nerve (V)","Glossopharyngeal alone","Hypoglossal (XII)","Pharyngeal plexus (IX and X)"]'::jsonb, 'Pharyngeal plexus (IX and X)', 'The pharyngeal constrictors are innervated by the pharyngeal plexus, formed by contributions from the glossopharyngeal (CN IX) and vagus (CN X) nerves. The facial, trigeminal, and hypoglossal nerves do not supply these muscles.'),
('92d7e091-d25c-54af-a7cc-32b0b908996d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'What is the pyriform fossa?', '["A recess in the nasopharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","A space in the middle ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A depression in the nose mediated by specific receptor activation","A fossa in the oral cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A depression in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet"]'::jsonb, 'A depression in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet', 'The pyriform fossa is a depression in the laryngopharynx on each side of the laryngeal inlet, bounded laterally by the thyroid cartilage and medially by the aryepiglottic folds. It is a common site for fish bones and other swallowed foreign bodies to lodge.'),
('190e4d15-c6f1-506f-92eb-c6f4fa86819d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('72633bfb-d81b-51a3-8e12-2ff067c561c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The larynx is located at the level of which vertebrae?', '["C1-C3 vertebrae","C6-T1 vertebrae","T1-T4 vertebrae","C2-C4 vertebrae","C3-C6 vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'C3-C6 vertebrae', 'In the adult the larynx lies opposite the C3-C6 vertebrae, with the laryngeal prominence at the level of C4. The trachea begins at the lower border of the cricoid at C6, so lower cervical and thoracic levels are too low.'),
('35aa2c19-7eb6-5b47-abc7-641b79b70921'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('223d6320-4cb5-5894-94fd-b49304147dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The vocal cords are located in which structure?', '["Nasopharynx","Larynx","Pharynx","Trachea","Bronchi"]'::jsonb, 'Larynx', 'The vocal cords lie within the larynx, stretching between the thyroid and arytenoid cartilages. They are not part of the nasopharynx, the pharynx proper, the trachea, or the bronchi.'),
('8db55d74-7cba-546a-bfc5-d46bf53e5c94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The trachea extends from which levels?', '["T2 to T8","C4 to C7","C6 to T4-T5","T1 to T6","C3 to C6"]'::jsonb, 'C6 to T4-T5', 'The trachea begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at the C6 level and bifurcates into the main bronchi at the sternal angle, approximately T4-T5. Cervical-to-lower-cervical or thoracic-only ranges do not match this course.'),
('87f148d4-e28d-5dcf-9cd2-9a41714126bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The larynx is innervated by which nerves?', '["Cervical plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","Glossopharyngeal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglossal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Recurrent and superior laryngeal nerves (CN X)","Accessory nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent and superior laryngeal nerves (CN X)', 'The larynx is innervated by the superior and recurrent laryngeal nerves, both branches of the vagus (CN X); the superior laryngeal nerve carries sensation above the folds and motor to cricothyroid, while the recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies the other intrinsic muscles. The cervical plexus, glossopharyngeal, hypoglossal, and accessory nerves do not innervate it.'),
('ee2841b5-81b0-58c5-a39c-caf400bc8985'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The Adam''s apple is formed by which structure?', '["Epiglottis","Cricoid cartilage","Thyroid cartilage","Hyoid bone","Arytenoid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid cartilage', 'The Adam''s apple (laryngeal prominence) is the anterior projection formed where the two laminae of the thyroid cartilage meet in the midline. The cricoid, hyoid, epiglottis, and arytenoid cartilages do not create this midline prominence.'),
('e5454459-5368-5d01-a825-fedc044173ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('ad66c3b4-e7b5-5029-9167-a8f934a6b1f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding clinical cases: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('2139a130-270e-5f00-a952-fa284cfec127'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'In medical practice, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('9940417f-ffbd-559b-909d-1ced8f58d1d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('a27db92c-5980-5a0e-b8d6-11e1cf7034aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('8ff7266d-f48b-5c85-be24-78c738357a1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'During therapeutic management, Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('414059a7-9aa6-587d-bd9b-6f91f99a9b20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which option most accurately describes the thyroid capsule?', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'A thin fibrous capsule closely surrounds the thyroid, reinforced externally by the pretracheal fascia. The other listed layers are separate cervical fascial planes or superficial tissues.'),
('a908d8a9-c3b9-5da0-b2f0-726707ff4dba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the thyroid gland: the thyroid capsule is:', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'The gland''s own covering is a thin fibrous capsule, strengthened externally by the pretracheal fascia. Neither the investing nor the prevertebral fascia nor the skin and platysma form this capsule.'),
('30b52720-b348-5634-b2a4-b15c700ad3f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Identify the correct response: the thyroid capsule is:', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'The thyroid is wrapped by a thin fibrous capsule derived from the pretracheal layer of deep cervical fascia. The remaining options name unrelated fascial layers or integument.'),
('36205d4d-53bf-54ca-a353-c4c4cf6c0d30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which of the following correctly describes the thyroid capsule? Select the best answer.', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'The true capsule of the thyroid is a thin fibrous layer, while the pretracheal fascia contributes the outer false capsule. Other choices refer to unrelated neck fascias or superficial soft tissue.'),
('55072699-636b-586d-a8d8-276a4f7d00ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Which response correctly addresses the following: the thyroid capsule is:', '["The investing layer of fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia","The prevertebral fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The platysma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin fibrous capsule derived from pretracheal fascia', 'The thyroid capsule is the thin fibrous layer enveloping the gland, supplemented by the pretracheal fascia as its false capsule. This is the only listed layer that directly surrounds the gland.'),
('96348fef-d33e-543c-9b20-193e45a92007'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'In Thyroid, larynx and pharynx, the blood supply of the thyroid gland is from:', '["Internal carotid artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)","Vertebral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only inferior thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)', 'The thyroid is supplied by paired superior and inferior thyroid arteries; the superior arises from the external carotid and the inferior from the thyrocervical trunk. Single-vessel options and the unrelated internal carotid or vertebral arteries are incomplete.'),
('ded39fd4-87aa-5b43-afce-c52860d99007'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Choose the most accurate statement: the blood supply of the thyroid gland is from:', '["Internal carotid artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)","Vertebral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only inferior thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid artery (from external carotid) and inferior thyroid artery (from thyrocervical trunk)', 'Both the superior thyroid artery from the external carotid and the inferior thyroid artery from the thyrocervical trunk vascularize the gland. The internal carotid and vertebral arteries give no thyroid branches.'),
('bd3c2f61-26b2-56b3-9352-7c4758001da8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The thyroid gland is located:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Anterior neck","wrapping around trachea below thyroid cartilage","Posterior neck","Above hyoid","Thorax Behind sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior neck', 'The thyroid lies in the anterior neck, overlying the trachea below the thyroid cartilage. The other choices misplace the gland in the posterior neck, above the hyoid, or within the thorax.'),
('70222d22-aec7-54d6-93f5-9126ae5df218'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The parts of the thyroid gland are:', '["Three lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Four lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two lobes connected by isthmus","Single mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Single lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two lobes connected by isthmus', 'The thyroid consists of two lateral lobes joined by a central isthmus over the trachea. It is not a single mass, a solitary lobe, or a gland with three or four lobes.'),
('ea73a01c-11f2-5710-a51d-e242064b2406'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid isthmus lies at the level of:', '["Hyoid","6th ring","2nd-4th tracheal rings","1st tracheal ring","Cricoid"]'::jsonb, '2nd-4th tracheal rings', 'The isthmus overlies the 2nd to 4th tracheal rings, with the pyramidal lobe sometimes ascending from it. The hyoid, cricoid, and first or sixth tracheal ring are all incorrect levels.'),
('54fbd24e-60b9-591f-b199-237876e43801'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid lobes extend from:', '["Angle of mandible to clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Oblique line of thyroid cartilage to 5th-6th tracheal ring","Cricoid to carina mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid to sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mastoid to sternum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique line of thyroid cartilage to 5th-6th tracheal ring', 'Each thyroid lobe extends from the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage above to the 5th-6th tracheal ring below. The other ranges describe much longer or displaced extents.'),
('5877a9e1-74c1-579a-9893-833a49545327'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid capsule is formed by:', '["Buccopharyngeal resulting in decreased functional activity","Carotid sheath accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pretracheal fascia (false) and true capsule (fibrous)","Investing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pretracheal fascia (false) and true capsule (fibrous)', 'The thyroid is invested by a true fibrous capsule and an outer false capsule formed by the pretracheal fascia. Buccopharyngeal fascia, carotid sheath, investing, and prevertebral fascia are unrelated layers.'),
('08c7af76-0d69-564c-9724-29ad23e63ba5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The normal number of parathyroid glands is:', '["8 resulting in decreased functional activity","1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","6 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 (2 superior, 2 inferior)"]'::jsonb, '4 (2 superior, 2 inferior)', 'There are usually four parathyroid glands, two superior and two inferior, lying on the posterior thyroid surface. One, two, six, and eight are not the expected normal number.'),
('9adaa924-51db-57ec-b0ee-6f6a762226b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The parathyroid glands are located:', '["On posterior thyroid surface within capsule","Outside thyroid capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid isthmus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On posterior thyroid surface within capsule', 'The parathyroid glands lie on the posterior surface of the thyroid lobes just within the fascial capsule. They are not found on the anterior surface, in the trachea, at the isthmus, or outside the capsule.'),
('32997a60-07ce-5431-b079-2b54f11d06ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The superior parathyroid glands develop from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["1st pouch","2nd pouch","5th pouch","3rd pouch","4th pharyngeal pouch"]'::jsonb, '4th pharyngeal pouch', 'The superior parathyroid glands derive from the dorsal wing of the fourth pharyngeal pouch. The third pouch forms the inferior parathyroids and thymus, so the other choices are incorrect.'),
('27584b1e-1a68-5bf4-b155-eab0e1fce7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The inferior parathyroid glands develop from:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["3rd pharyngeal pouch","5th pouch","2nd pouch","1st pouch","4th pouch"]'::jsonb, '3rd pharyngeal pouch', 'The inferior parathyroid glands arise from the third pharyngeal pouch. Descending farther than the fourth-pouch derivatives during development, they come to lie more inferiorly on the thyroid.'),
('7d6fb150-5cdc-5d9b-9ced-b812083edbf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The blood supply of the thyroid gland includes:', '["Only superior thyroid","Only inferior thyroid","Only thyroid ima","Superior thyroid (ECA)","inferior thyroid (thyrocervical trunk) thyroid ima Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid (ECA)', 'The superior thyroid artery, a branch of the external carotid, is the principal upper supply to the gland. The inferior thyroid and thyroid ima arteries feed other portions, and the internal carotid gives no thyroid branch.'),
('42f11df2-2965-51f1-b251-2786bf90c7e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior thyroid artery is a branch of:', '["Facial","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid artery","Subclavian","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The superior thyroid artery is typically the first branch of the external carotid artery. Facial, subclavian, thyrocervical, and internal carotid arteries do not give this branch.'),
('3ae4fd24-3217-5e6d-b352-9729e94e2823'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The inferior thyroid artery is a branch of:', '["External carotid","Vertebral","Costocervical trunk","Thyrocervical trunk (from subclavian)","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk (from subclavian)', 'The inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk, a branch of the subclavian artery. The external carotid, vertebral, costocervical, and internal carotid arteries are wrong sources.'),
('a04a641b-f707-59c7-9034-1fa193daa22f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid ima artery, when present, arises from:', '["Brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch","Internal carotid","External carotid","Subclavian","Thyrocervical trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk or aortic arch', 'The thyroid ima artery usually arises from the brachiocephalic trunk or occasionally the aortic arch, ascending to the isthmus. The carotid and subclavian vessels are not its source.'),
('6e2447ba-81d7-5841-b9b6-4599d974993c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The venous drainage of the thyroid gland is:', '["Only inferior to brachiocephalic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only superior to IJV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only middle to IJV mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior and middle to IJV; inferior to brachiocephalic","All to azygos leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and middle to IJV; inferior to brachiocephalic', 'The superior and middle thyroid veins drain into the internal jugular vein, while the inferior thyroid vein drains into the brachiocephalic vein. The other choices state only one route or the wrong venous target.'),
('c15dc7de-074c-5c65-94ad-4c0d8a05e017'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Facial (VII)","Vagus (CN X)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Trigeminal (V)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (CN X)', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a branch of the vagus (CN X) supplying the larynx. None of the other listed cranial nerves innervate the laryngeal muscles.'),
('776cda2a-8cd2-591d-a617-e14eaa4ec7c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'On the right, the recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around:', '["Right subclavian artery","Aortic arch","Right IJV","Right vertebral","Right common carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Right subclavian artery', 'On the right, the recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around the subclavian artery and ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove. The aortic arch is the corresponding site on the left.'),
('01a74a58-4b88-5c81-8b24-ac1aa6396352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'On the left, the recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks around:', '["Left IJV resulting in decreased functional activity","Innominate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left common carotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Left subclavian without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic arch (at ligamentum arteriosum)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (at ligamentum arteriosum)', 'On the left, the recurrent laryngeal nerve loops beneath the aortic arch at the ligamentum arteriosum before ascending in the groove. The left subclavian site pertains to the right-sided nerve instead.'),
('d4b75f47-3014-5bd0-8647-ef643e001847'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Cricothyroid only resulting in decreased functional activity","Palatal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid","All intrinsic muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pharyngeal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve innervates all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid, which is supplied by the external laryngeal nerve. It also carries sensation below the vocal cords.'),
('e8e7aa87-1ae9-5512-bb04-86303274f2f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The external laryngeal nerve supplies:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid","Thyroarytenoid","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Cricothyroid muscle","Vocalis"]'::jsonb, 'Cricothyroid muscle', 'The external laryngeal nerve supplies the cricothyroid muscle, the only intrinsic laryngeal muscle not innervated by the recurrent laryngeal nerve. The other named muscles are recurrent laryngeal nerve territory.'),
('60959fdb-1fb6-5220-b559-7e32406607c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior laryngeal nerve is a branch of:', '["Facial (VII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Vagus (CN X)","Trigeminal (V)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus (CN X)', 'The superior laryngeal nerve branches from the vagus (CN X) and divides into internal and external branches. The facial, glossopharyngeal, hypoglossal, and trigeminal nerves give no laryngeal branches.'),
('1c033295-ff15-5cba-b2bc-f9f194292b36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The superior laryngeal nerve divides into:', '["Anterior and posterior","Internal and external laryngeal nerves","Medial and lateral","Motor and sensory","Superior and inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external laryngeal nerves', 'The superior laryngeal nerve splits at the level of the hyoid into an internal laryngeal branch (sensory) and an external laryngeal branch (motor to cricothyroid). The other pairs do not describe this division.'),
('9cd4301c-ea57-57c9-9144-3f51ac4cd845'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The internal laryngeal nerve supplies:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Sensation below cords","Motor to all intrinsic","Sensation to laryngeal mucosa above vocal cords","Motor to cricothyroid","Sensation to trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation to laryngeal mucosa above vocal cords', 'The internal laryngeal nerve conveys sensation from the laryngeal mucosa above the vocal cords. Sensation below the cords is carried by the recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
('918ec03f-2364-5be4-be1d-8e145406d478'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies sensation to:', '["Laryngeal mucosa below vocal cords","Above cords","Tracheal mucosa","Esophageal mucosa","Pharyngeal mucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngeal mucosa below vocal cords', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve provides sensation to the laryngeal mucosa below the vocal cords. The mucosa above the cords is supplied by the internal laryngeal nerve, and the other sites are not its territory.'),
('2e4ea326-e343-5bbc-ba10-eaf865b5c3a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury causes:', '["Hoarseness (vocal cord paralysis)","Dysphagia","Facial paralysis","Shoulder weakness","Tongue deviation"]'::jsonb, 'Hoarseness (vocal cord paralysis)', 'Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury paralyzes the ipsilateral vocal cord and produces hoarseness. Respiratory obstruction is more typical of bilateral damage, and the other signs belong to different nerves.'),
('cefc917f-04d6-5fb3-9a8e-adb57ccd75f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve injury causes:', '["Hoarseness only","Dysphagia only","Respiratory distress/stridor","Cough only","symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory distress/stridor', 'Bilateral injury leaves both cords in an adducted position, narrowing the airway and causing respiratory distress with stridor. Isolated hoarseness or dysphagia does not dominate the picture.'),
('027b1a56-0f71-53dc-b2d3-98e1c6dc28b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The paired laryngeal cartilages are:', '["Cricoid + thyroid","Thyroid","cricoid","epiglottis Arytenoid + thyroid Only arytenoid","Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform"]'::jsonb, 'Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform', 'The arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages are paired, whereas the thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis are unpaired. The other choices mix paired and unpaired components.'),
('ac59ecd4-3399-50f3-9da8-37f614bbb151'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'An unpaired laryngeal cartilage is the:', '["Arytenoid + thyroid","Cricoid + thyroid","Thyroid","cricoid","epiglottis Arytenoid corniculate cuneiform Only cricoid"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid', 'The thyroid cartilage is unpaired, along with the cricoid and epiglottis. The arytenoids and corniculates are paired cartilages, so options listing them are incomplete or mixed.'),
('b221e02b-9c72-5f16-ae66-7b362396cdab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The largest laryngeal cartilage is the:', '["Epiglottis","Arytenoid","Thyroid cartilage","Cricoid","Corniculate"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid cartilage', 'The thyroid cartilage is the largest laryngeal cartilage and forms the laryngeal prominence. The cricoid, arytenoid, epiglottis, and corniculate are all smaller.'),
('2e77cf32-6858-5f32-83aa-de4f3e7b4337'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The cricoid cartilage is shaped like a:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Square resulting in decreased functional activity","Signet ring (narrow anterior, broad posterior)","Oval mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete circle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Signet ring (narrow anterior, broad posterior)', 'The cricoid is a complete signet ring, narrow anteriorly and broad posteriorly, and the only fully circumferential cartilage in the airway. Square, oval, triangular, and uniform circle shapes are incorrect.'),
('80a37427-5290-5683-abfd-c9184a5cc616'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricoid cartilage lies at the vertebral level of:', '["C6","C4","C7","C5","T1"]'::jsonb, 'C6', 'The cricoid cartilage lies at the level of the sixth cervical vertebra, where the larynx joins the trachea. It marks the plane between larynx and trachea, unlike the other levels.'),
('c730357d-4940-574e-a9a8-bbf8cfc50e1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroid cartilage lies at the vertebral level of:', '["C2-C3","C5-C6","C3-C4","C6-C7","C4-C5"]'::jsonb, 'C4-C5', 'The thyroid cartilage occupies the C4-C5 vertebral level, while the cricoid lies lower at C6. The other ranges do not match the usual position of the laryngeal skeleton.'),
('8e214163-1b93-533e-b63c-255f26912de3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding the arytenoid cartilages, which statement is correct?', '["Unpaired","C-shaped","Hyaline","Paired","pyramid-shaped with vocal and muscular processes Small nodules"]'::jsonb, 'Paired', 'The arytenoid cartilages are paired and pyramid-shaped, each bearing a vocal and a muscular process for cord attachment and movement. They are not unpaired, C-shaped, or formed of small nodules.'),
('f0a179b5-c95e-5726-b538-03372cf31f13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The epiglottis is best described as:', '["Paired resulting in decreased functional activity","Leaf-shaped elastic cartilage guarding laryngeal inlet","Ring-shaped mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyaline cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flat bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leaf-shaped elastic cartilage guarding laryngeal inlet', 'The epiglottis is a leaf-shaped elastic cartilage that guards the laryngeal inlet during swallowing. It is unpaired, not ring-shaped, not hyaline, and not bony.'),
('9c3b8fb2-9f5b-5e54-af97-34d5d3482df6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The cricothyroid joint allows:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Only gliding resulting in decreased functional activity","movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rotation and gliding for pitch adjustment","Only rotation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rotation and gliding for pitch adjustment', 'The cricothyroid joints permit rotation and gliding that tilt the thyroid cartilage and change vocal cord tension, adjusting pitch. The joint does not allow only one motion or lateral displacement.'),
('f5d7c0f3-8f1e-5ee3-b2be-5ebd8f1d358c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding The cricoarytenoid joint allows:, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Only rotation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only gliding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","movement mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertical movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rotation and gliding for vocal cord movement"]'::jsonb, 'Rotation and gliding for vocal cord movement', 'The cricoarytenoid joints allow rotation and gliding of the arytenoids, movements that open, close, and tense the vocal cords. Restricting the joint to a single motion ignores its role in cord movement.'),
('a985d480-cb27-5e2c-a87f-d4a77f68e230'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding Which of the following is an intrinsic laryngeal muscle?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Only cricothyroid","Only thyroarytenoid","Cricothyroid","PCA LCA transverse arytenoid oblique arytenoid","thyroarytenoid vocalis Only arytenoids Only PCA"]'::jsonb, 'Cricothyroid', 'Cricothyroid is one of the intrinsic laryngeal muscles, which also include thyroarytenoid, vocalis, and the arytenoid group. It is a valid example of this muscle group.'),
('91f4d9c4-0293-5170-a106-30a17a2198b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding Which muscle is the only abductor of the vocal cords?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Cricothyroid","Vocalis","Posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA)","Lateral cricoarytenoid","Transverse arytenoid"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cricoarytenoid (PCA)', 'The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only muscle that abducts the vocal cords by rotating the arytenoids outward. Cricothyroid, vocalis, lateral cricoarytenoid, and transverse arytenoid adduct or tension the cords instead.'),
('9cdf9f92-5dca-5fa3-838d-c91c03b87f2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'Regarding Which muscles adduct the vocal cords?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Posterior cricoarytenoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Cricothyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vocalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral cricoarytenoid + transverse arytenoid","Thyroarytenoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cricoarytenoid + transverse arytenoid', 'The lateral cricoarytenoid and transverse (interarytenoid) muscles pull the vocal cords together and adduct them. Posterior cricoarytenoid abducts, and cricothyroid primarily tenses the cords.'),
('e16e8cab-b8ab-567a-8eb7-ceadfbd7f8c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The cricothyroid tenses the vocal cords by:', '["Abducting arytenoids resulting in decreased functional activity","Raising larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relaxing cords mediated by specific receptor activation","Adducting arytenoids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulling thyroid forward increasing tension"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling thyroid forward increasing tension', 'Contraction of cricothyroid tilts the thyroid cartilage forward on the cricoid, increasing the distance between the cartilages and tensing the vocal cords. It does not abduct or adduct the arytenoids or relax the cords.'),
('9520f81e-f0ae-5364-85f5-5d60306ee0d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The thyroarytenoid relaxes the vocal cords by:', '["Tensing cords","Pulling arytenoid to thyroid","shortening cords","Rotating arytenoid","Abducting cords Adducting cords"]'::jsonb, 'Pulling arytenoid to thyroid', 'Thyroarytenoid contraction draws the arytenoid toward the thyroid cartilage, shortening and relaxing the vocal cords. This opposes the tensing action of cricothyroid.'),
('c5300a04-f4b9-560d-8ff0-6b2016793d48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The vocalis muscle is the:', '["Part of PCA","Cartilage","Part of cricothyroid","Medial part of thyroarytenoid","fine-tunes cord tension Separate muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Medial part of thyroarytenoid', 'Vocalis is the medial part of the thyroarytenoid muscle lying within the vocal fold. It is neither a separate cartilage nor part of the cricothyroid or posterior cricoarytenoid muscles.'),
('8b5fba32-5464-5a76-b949-3bb4dfebb57f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Thyroid Larynx Pharynx', 'The rima glottidis is the:', '["Cricothyroid space","Laryngeal inlet","Opening between false cords","Opening between true vocal cords","Tracheal opening"]'::jsonb, 'Opening between true vocal cords', 'The rima glottidis is the adjustable opening between the true vocal cords, widest during inspiration. It is not the laryngeal inlet, the space between false cords, the cricothyroid space, or the tracheal opening.'),
('1861bab3-7bfb-5756-8e7c-12554c53b6c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The neck is divided into how many major triangles?', '["2 (anterior and posterior)","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","6 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (anterior and posterior)', 'The side of the neck is divided into two major triangles, anterior and posterior, separated by the sternocleidomastoid muscle. The other options give numbers that do not reflect this primary division.'),
('d096e5dd-fe90-5d51-910e-c7153d195f47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Anterior triangle boundaries:', '["Trapezius","clavicle","SCM Sternum","clavicle mastoid","Mandible mastoid"]'::jsonb, 'Mandible mastoid', 'The anterior triangle is bounded by the anterior midline of the neck, the inferior border of the mandible, and the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. The clavicle and trapezius instead form boundaries of the posterior triangle.'),
('0c9ecc1a-1e2f-50c6-b3a7-453b0cbb1abf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Posterior triangle boundaries:', '["Trapezius","clavicle","SCM","Clavicle trapezius","mandible Midline"]'::jsonb, 'SCM', 'The posterior triangle is bounded by the sternocleidomastoid, the trapezius, and the middle third of the clavicle, so the sternocleidomastoid is a genuine boundary. The mandible and the midline belong to the anterior triangle.'),
('e807fd6e-5f5c-56bd-a424-305defac65a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Roof of posterior triangle is:', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The investing layer of deep cervical fascia forms the roof of the posterior triangle as it splits to enclose the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. The pretracheal and prevertebral fasciae lie deeper and are not part of the roof.'),
('8387f3c1-ff8b-5095-8bf3-f2af5ce07ee4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Floor of posterior triangle is:', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The prevertebral fascia covers the muscles forming the floor of the posterior triangle, such as the scaleni and levator scapulae. Longus colli and the vertebral bodies lie deeper, beneath this fascial layer.'),
('1293e0b9-849c-5486-aaa7-07632d9cb197'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which is NOT a content of posterior triangle?', '["Supraclavicular nerves","Submandibular gland","External jugular vein","Spinal accessory nerve","Brachial plexus roots"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The submandibular gland lies in the submandibular triangle of the anterior part of the neck. The spinal accessory nerve, external jugular vein, supraclavicular nerves, and brachial plexus roots are all contents of the posterior triangle.'),
('e36dd828-5a5a-5064-b345-edf8243a71c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Spinal accessory nerve in posterior triangle supplies:', '["Only SCM","SCM and trapezius","Infrahyoid muscles","Only trapezius","Paraspinal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'SCM and trapezius', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) innervates both the sternocleidomastoid and the trapezius after crossing the posterior triangle. It does not supply the infrahyoid or paraspinal muscles.'),
('7acf6e31-6145-5223-b43e-6f03416ad582'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'External jugular vein in posterior triangle drains into:', '["Superior vena cava","Subclavian vein","Brachiocephalic vein","Internal jugular vein","Axillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian vein', 'The external jugular vein descends through the posterior triangle and empties into the subclavian vein just lateral to the scalenus anterior. It does not drain into the internal jugular vein, brachiocephalic vein, or axillary vein.'),
('c3c0a86d-1731-5bf8-87a5-589b12ab2a21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Brachial plexus roots (C5-T1) are located in:', '["Middle part resulting in decreased functional activity","Apex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior part (supraclavicular) of posterior triangle","Base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior part (supraclavicular) of posterior triangle', 'The roots and trunks of the brachial plexus occupy the inferior part (supraclavicular region) of the posterior triangle, emerging above the clavicle. They do not lie at the apex, base, or middle of the triangle.'),
('fcec1b96-1fe3-5379-b19f-44e46d5518b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Supraclavicular nerves are derived from:', '["Brachial plexus","Cervical plexus (C3-C4)","Accessory nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical plexus (C3-C4)', 'The supraclavicular nerves are cutaneous branches of the cervical plexus that arise from the C3 and C4 roots. They do not arise from the brachial plexus or from cranial nerves.'),
('4afc46d8-a8bd-548b-8606-d52adbd05e25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Phrenic nerve is formed from:', '["C5-C7","C1-C4","C3","C4","C5 C2-C4 C4-C6"]'::jsonb, 'C3', 'The phrenic nerve is formed from contributions of the C3, C4, and C5 ventral rami, with C4 supplying most of its fibers. Among the options, C3 is a genuine root of this nerve, whereas C5-C7 and C1-C4 as stated do not give its true composition.'),
('3be8eaf4-e6b8-58e2-9a4b-30fb8c6e8a8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Phrenic nerve lies on which muscle?', '["Posterior scalene","SCM","Middle scalene","Anterior scalene","Levator scapulae"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior scalene', 'The phrenic nerve descends on the anterior surface of the anterior scalene muscle, deep to the prevertebral fascia. The middle and posterior scalene muscles lie behind the nerve rather than beneath it.'),
('502b1ee1-2d4a-58c8-a2d3-aa997011889b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Subclavian artery is divided into how many parts by scalenus anterior?', '["4 parts","5 parts","1 part","2 parts","3 parts"]'::jsonb, '3 parts', 'The subclavian artery is divided into three parts by the scalenus anterior muscle, which crosses in front of the vessel. Each part has its own named branches.'),
('b75ed629-a673-560f-b944-816b897fd555'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Third part of subclavian artery is located:', '["Medial to scalene resulting in decreased functional activity","At axillary artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Behind scalene mediated by specific receptor activation","After crossing first rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral to anterior scalene in posterior triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to anterior scalene in posterior triangle', 'The third part of the subclavian artery lies lateral to the anterior scalene muscle and crosses the posterior triangle to reach the lateral border of the first rib. The first and second parts lie medial to and deep to the scalenus anterior.'),
('45d4d4c7-fd13-5bc2-82db-4ce4c313caea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which artery passes through the posterior triangle?', '["Facial artery","Suprascapular artery","Superior thyroid","Lingual artery","Ascending pharyngeal"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular artery', 'The suprascapular artery branches from the thyrocervical trunk and passes laterally across the posterior triangle. Branches of the facial, lingual, superior thyroid, and ascending pharyngeal arteries supply other regions.'),
('209d7bb1-dd84-5ec3-b4b9-e03aad7fe1bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Transverse cervical artery passes through:', '["Posterior triangle","Anterior triangle","Carotid triangle","Muscular triangle","Submandibular triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior triangle', 'The transverse cervical artery runs laterally across the posterior triangle to reach the levator scapulae and neighboring muscles. It does not pass through the triangles of the front of the neck.'),
('73b6f434-d1cc-5c89-9542-7a364ec7ef3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Lesser occipital nerve is derived from:', '["C3","Facial nerve","Cervical plexus (C2)","C4","Trigeminal"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical plexus (C2)', 'The lesser occipital nerve is a cutaneous branch of the cervical plexus arising from the anterior ramus of C2. It does not arise from the facial or trigeminal nerves.'),
('3823c747-746a-5f19-bdc1-3a9cd8d474c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Anterior triangle is subdivided into:', '["Carotid","submandibular","submental","muscular triangles","Carotid subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'submental', 'The anterior triangle is divided by the digastric and omohyoid muscles into four smaller triangles: submental, submandibular, carotid, and muscular. The submental triangle is one of these true subdivisions, whereas ''Carotid subclavian'' does not name an anatomical division.'),
('818b355d-8204-569b-8fa2-08beb592e2c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Submandibular triangle boundaries:', '["Mandible","platysma mylohyoid","Mandible mylohyoid","hyoglossus Posterior digastric","stylohyoid mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Mandible', 'The submandibular triangle is bounded above by the inferior border of the mandible and below by the anterior and posterior bellies of the digastric muscle. The mylohyoid and hyoglossus lie in its floor rather than forming its boundaries.'),
('b3a1ad6d-d5f5-5d94-968b-b70fcd08147a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Contents of submandibular triangle include:', '["Sublingual gland resulting in decreased functional activity","lingual artery Thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","larynx trachea Parotid gland facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","external carotid Carotid IJV vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Submandibular gland facial artery submandibular lymph nodes hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland facial artery submandibular lymph nodes hypoglossal nerve', 'The submandibular triangle contains the submandibular gland, facial artery, submandibular lymph nodes, and the hypoglossal nerve passing toward the tongue. The sublingual gland and the larynx, trachea, and parotid gland lie in other territories.'),
('92def5dc-c9f8-523d-bbab-d9684809c9af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Floor of submandibular triangle is:', '["Platysma and skin","Digastric and stylohyoid","Mylohyoid","hyoglossus","middle constrictor Longus colli and scalenes Prevertebral muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Mylohyoid', 'The floor of the submandibular triangle is formed by the mylohyoid muscle, with the hyoglossus completing the posterior part. The platysma and skin form the roof, and the digastric and stylohyoid bound the triangle.'),
('db011d11-3fc9-54cc-905f-a8275b97aaf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Submental triangle boundaries:', '["Two digastric bellies and SCM","Anterior bellies of digastric and hyoid bone","Mandible and posterior digastric","Mandible and midline","Mylohyoid and hyoglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior bellies of digastric and hyoid bone', 'The submental triangle is bounded by the anterior bellies of the two digastric muscles and the body of the hyoid bone. The sternocleidomastoid and the mandible belong to other triangles.'),
('394f953f-a877-5aff-a310-878e7ba8fe80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Submental triangle contains:', '["Parotid gland","Facial artery","Submental lymph nodes and veins","Sublingual gland","Submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Submental lymph nodes and veins', 'The submental triangle contains the submental lymph nodes and small veins draining the midline of the chin and lower lip. The parotid, submandibular, and sublingual glands lie outside this small triangle.'),
('8e8a7973-6f43-51dc-998f-6dc43aacf702'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid triangle boundaries:', '["SCM","trapezius clavicle","SCM sternohyoid","omohyoid Mandible","digastric hyoid"]'::jsonb, 'SCM', 'The carotid triangle is bounded by the posterior belly of the digastric, the superior belly of the omohyoid, and the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. The sternocleidomastoid is therefore one of its defining boundaries.'),
('4f8cff2c-6883-5cd4-925e-0f4d48d35a76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid triangle contains:', '["Common carotid bifurcation","IJV","vagus nerve","Thyroid gland","Parotid gland Sublingual gland Submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid bifurcation', 'The carotid triangle contains the bifurcation of the common carotid artery together with the internal jugular vein and the vagus nerve within the carotid sheath. The thyroid gland lies below this triangle.'),
('f59863b8-bd96-5f80-953e-9db8c2efbf2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid bifurcation occurs at level of:', '["Hyoid (C3)","Sternoclavicular joint (T2)","Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)","Cricoid (C6)","Angle of mandible (C2)"]'::jsonb, 'Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)', 'The common carotid artery bifurcates at the level of the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, approximately the C4 vertebral level. The hyoid, cricoid, and angle of the mandible are at different levels.'),
('44e2dcef-b19e-51dc-9291-66dafb434b54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Nerve closely related to carotid bifurcation:', '["Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial (VII)","Recurrent laryngeal","Spinal accessory (XI)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (XII)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) hooks around the occipital artery and crosses the internal and external carotid arteries at the level of the bifurcation. None of the other listed nerves lies as closely applied to the bifurcation.'),
('e90a75b4-12a0-5c0b-a270-9bdd3753b10a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid body is located:', '["In thyroid gland","In wall of external carotid","At the bifurcation of common carotid","In wall of internal carotid","In submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'At the bifurcation of common carotid', 'The carotid body is a chemoreceptor attached to the bifurcation of the common carotid artery, lying in the connective tissue at its angle. It is not embedded in the thyroid or submandibular glands.'),
('ba80ff3e-7c70-5062-bef6-87060a5d9030'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid sinus is located:', '["At the bifurcation resulting in decreased functional activity","In common carotid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At aortic arch mediated by specific receptor activation","At origin of external carotid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the root of internal carotid (baroreceptor)"]'::jsonb, 'At the root of internal carotid (baroreceptor)', 'The carotid sinus is a baroreceptor dilatation located at the root of the internal carotid artery. It is not confined to the common carotid artery and is unrelated to the aortic arch.'),
('f11510bd-3f30-5618-8f93-e563a4da2b9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid sinus is innervated by:', '["Glossopharyngeal (nerve of Hering)","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Vagus nerve","Sympathetic trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (nerve of Hering)', 'The carotid sinus and carotid body are innervated by the sinus nerve of Hering, a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX). The vagus, sympathetic trunk, and hypoglossal nerve do not provide this innervation.'),
('25b2c4fc-e3fd-5727-b5af-a6cbca1ef3f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'External carotid artery has how many branches in neck?', '["9 branches","8 branches","6 branches","7 branches","5 branches"]'::jsonb, '8 branches', 'The external carotid artery gives off eight branches in the neck: superior thyroid, ascending pharyngeal, lingual, facial, occipital, posterior auricular, maxillary, and superficial temporal. The other numbers do not match this count.'),
('eff8cab4-90b7-550e-89c2-33d705faae80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Superior thyroid artery is first branch of:', '["Subclavian","Facial artery","Lingual artery","External carotid artery","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The superior thyroid artery is the first branch of the external carotid artery and passes downward to supply the thyroid gland and larynx. It does not arise from the subclavian or internal carotid arteries.'),
('73697dcb-783b-550a-818b-e5da52959bd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Lingual artery arises at level of:', '["Upper border of thyroid cartilage","SCM","Cricoid cartilage","Angle of mandible","Greater cornu of hyoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Greater cornu of hyoid bone', 'The lingual artery arises from the external carotid artery at the level of the greater cornu of the hyoid bone and then loops deep to the hyoglossus to reach the tongue. The other listed landmarks correspond to different structures.'),
('54d38cd8-6872-5ae1-b6a6-e9bdc036da9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Facial artery arises from external carotid:', '["Same level as lingual","Above the lingual artery","Above posterior auricular","At bifurcation","Below the lingual"]'::jsonb, 'Above the lingual artery', 'The facial artery arises from the external carotid artery just above the lingual artery, frequently sharing a common stem with it. The posterior auricular, maxillary, and superficial temporal branches arise higher.'),
('5ac1d8ce-1ea3-55b4-b492-7d61c8289aa5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Occipital artery arises from external carotid:', '["From posterior aspect","Below superior thyroid","Above lingual","At bifurcation","Opposite the facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Opposite the facial artery', 'The occipital artery arises from the posterior aspect of the external carotid artery at about the level of the origin of the facial artery, then runs backward to supply the occipital scalp. The posterior aspect of the vessel and the level are both relevant, and of the options given, its origin is opposite the facial artery.'),
('61f05969-431c-59c6-ba5f-b8f8b8980ac2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Internal carotid artery in neck has:', '["Posterior communicating","Several muscular branches","One branch (caroticotympanic)","No branches in the neck","Ophthalmic artery"]'::jsonb, 'No branches in the neck', 'In the neck the internal carotid artery ascends within the carotid sheath and gives off no branches. Its first branches, such as the caroticotympanic and ophthalmic arteries, appear only within the skull.'),
('6ec7c531-542d-5d7b-b719-6102203525ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Muscular triangle boundaries:', '["SCM","trapezius clavicle","SCM superior omohyoid","sternohyoid","Omohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'SCM superior omohyoid', 'The muscular triangle is bounded by the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid, the superior belly of the omohyoid, and the anterior midline of the neck. The sternohyoid is a strap muscle lying inside this triangle rather than forming its boundary.'),
('f3fddc1b-2e42-5c03-ae54-5da3bbe9c911'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Muscular triangle contains:', '["Carotid sheath","Thyroid cartilage","Infrahyoid strap muscles","Parotid gland","Submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Infrahyoid strap muscles', 'The muscular triangle contains the infrahyoid (strap) muscles, including the sternohyoid, sternothyroid, and thyrohyoid. The carotid sheath and thyroid cartilage lie outside this triangle.'),
('e4ec7e22-5d46-5de0-8c0c-f950c6a7fb1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Ansa cervicalis is located:', '["In the carotid triangle on the carotid sheath","In muscular triangle","In submandibular triangle","In posterior triangle","In submental triangle"]'::jsonb, 'In the carotid triangle on the carotid sheath', 'The ansa cervicalis lies on the anterior surface of the carotid sheath within the carotid triangle of the neck. The other triangles do not contain this nerve loop.'),
('1c3d6f6b-9bfc-52da-8338-3e41ea8e3038'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Ansa cervicalis is formed by:', '["Hypoglossal only","C1-C3 fibers from cervical plexus","C1-C4 fibers","C5-C8 fibers","C3-C5 fibers"]'::jsonb, 'C1-C3 fibers from cervical plexus', 'The ansa cervicalis is formed by fibers from the C1, C2, and C3 spinal nerves: the superior root descends from C1 via a loop around the hypoglossal nerve, and the inferior root comes from C2 and C3. Its roots therefore arise from the cervical plexus.'),
('df2ec8fa-da16-50f8-a52b-0e57ef84c0e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Ansa cervicalis supplies:', '["Scalene muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","SCM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suprahyoid muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infrahyoid muscles except thyrohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Infrahyoid muscles except thyrohyoid', 'The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid strap muscles, but the thyrohyoid is supplied instead by C1 fibers carried on the hypoglossal nerve. It does not innervate the scalene muscles, sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, or suprahyoid muscles.'),
('2699dabc-4e60-58cc-940a-41754a03beb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Thyrohyoid muscle is supplied by:', '["Recurrent laryngeal","Phrenic nerve","C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve","External laryngeal","Ansa cervicalis"]'::jsonb, 'C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve', 'The thyrohyoid receives C1 fibers that travel with the hypoglossal nerve and run forward to the muscle. The ansa cervicalis supplies the other infrahyoid muscles but not this one.'),
('c633025d-2147-54cb-b991-941eb15ca831'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Geniohyoid muscle is supplied by:', '["C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Hypoglossal proper","Trigeminal","Ansa cervicalis"]'::jsonb, 'C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve', 'The geniohyoid receives C1 fibers carried on the hypoglossal nerve, which leave the nerve to reach the muscle. It is not supplied by the ansa cervicalis, facial nerve, or trigeminal nerve.'),
('c2987c72-49b2-5794-a703-f7c01578a4d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Posterior belly of digastric is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V3)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)","Vagus (X)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The posterior belly of the digastric is supplied by the facial nerve (CN VII). The anterior belly, by contrast, receives the nerve to mylohyoid from the trigeminal nerve.'),
('368ff164-adc0-55dc-8a2d-164c6c3f2383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Anterior belly of digastric is supplied by:', '["Trigeminal (V3 via nerve to mylohyoid)","Vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglossal mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal (V3 via nerve to mylohyoid)', 'The anterior belly of the digastric is innervated by the nerve to mylohyoid, a branch of the inferior alveolar nerve from the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). The facial nerve supplies the posterior belly instead.'),
('7ec14eaf-b930-56b2-b5f8-fa985f165384'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Posterior triangle is subdivided by omohyoid into:', '["Occipital and supraclavicular triangles","Suprahyoid and infrahyoid","Submandibular and submental","Digastric and carotid","Carotid and muscular"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital and supraclavicular triangles', 'The inferior belly of the omohyoid divides the posterior triangle into an upper occipital triangle and a lower supraclavicular triangle. The other groupings describe subdivisions of the anterior triangle.'),
('dd064f29-1203-560e-bba9-19e1c0ce56d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Occipital triangle contains:', '["Common carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","External carotid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve and cervical plexus branches","Thyroid gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavian artery only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve and cervical plexus branches', 'The occipital triangle contains the spinal accessory nerve and the emerging branches of the cervical plexus. The carotid arteries and subclavian vessels lie outside this territory.'),
('da8e80ee-ce9d-5a55-9323-45a1bcc7f691'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Supraclavicular triangle contains:', '["Parotid gland","Submandibular gland","Carotid bifurcation","Third part subclavian artery","brachial plexus trunks Thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Third part subclavian artery', 'The supraclavicular triangle contains the third part of the subclavian artery, the trunks of the brachial plexus, and the suprascapular vessels. The parotid and submandibular glands lie far from this region.'),
('f28f588f-1a06-59a7-af0b-25b1d5f5ea1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'SCM muscle is supplied by:', '["Facial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglossal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C2-C3 alone mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XI alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C2-C3"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C2-C3', 'The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) together with proprioceptive branches from C2 and C3. The facial and hypoglossal nerves take no part in its supply.'),
('4d20c9ab-675a-5e55-b9f0-9ac015433898'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Trapezius is supplied by:', '["Hypoglossal resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C3-C4","CN XI alone mediated by specific receptor activation","C3-C4 alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C3-C4', 'The trapezius receives its motor supply from the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with sensory contributions from C3 and C4 cervical plexus branches. The hypoglossal and facial nerves do not supply it.'),
('79d7686e-d11d-5c73-af2d-e704ad4b48c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Injury to spinal accessory nerve causes:', '["Weakness in shoulder shrugging and head rotation","Hoarseness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tongue deviation mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial paralysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dysphagia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weakness in shoulder shrugging and head rotation', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles, so injury weakens head rotation and shoulder shrugging. Hoarseness reflects recurrent laryngeal (vagus) injury, tongue deviation reflects hypoglossal injury, and facial paralysis reflects facial nerve injury.'),
('078af34a-cded-5801-a386-f6fb66ecaa18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Cervical plexus is formed by:', '["Posterior C1-C5","Posterior rami of C1-C4","Anterior C1-C5","Anterior rami of C1-C4","Anterior rami of C5-C8"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of C1-C4', 'The cervical plexus is formed by the anterior rami of the first four cervical nerves (C1-C4). Posterior rami and the lower cervical segments are not part of the plexus.'),
('617547e9-b823-5a22-9c74-55b32deaae4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Cervical plexus branches include:', '["Facial","vagus hypoglossal Accessory hypoglossal","phrenic","Trigeminal facial glossopharyngeal","Great auricular lesser occipital transverse cervical supraclavicular"]'::jsonb, 'phrenic', 'The phrenic nerve, derived from C3-C5, is a branch of the cervical plexus. The other listed nerves are cranial nerves and are not plexus branches.'),
('fc440399-7a78-5f86-9b36-9331b162107e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Prevertebral fascia covers:', '["SCM and trapezius","Masseter and temporalis","Scalenes","longus colli","longus capitis Infrahyoid muscles Digastric and mylohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenes', 'The prevertebral fascia covers the scalene muscles and the prevertebral muscle group (longus colli and longus capitis), and laterally continues as the axillary sheath. Sternocleidomastoid and trapezius lie in the investing fascia, not the prevertebral fascia.'),
('60a14521-f630-5cb1-979b-da011a5d3a04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Prevertebral fascia extends from:', '["Skull base to coccyx","Mandible to sternum","Mastoid to clavicle","Skull base to T3 vertebra","Hyoid to clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Skull base to T3 vertebra', 'The prevertebral fascia spans from the skull base down to the T3 vertebra. The other choices describe attachments of different fascial layers or structures.'),
('5a029b39-a2e0-5fe8-a8c6-1f50fabaed88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Retropharyngeal space is between:', '["Investing and pretracheal fascia","Layers of pretracheal fascia","Prevertebral fascia and buccopharyngeal fascia","Skin and investing fascia","Midline of neck"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia and buccopharyngeal fascia', 'The retropharyngeal space lies between the prevertebral fascia behind and the buccopharyngeal fascia covering the pharynx in front. It is a potential space through which pharyngeal infection can spread into the mediastinum.'),
('07244729-3e27-5aab-9b3c-83c825e1754c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid sheath is formed by:', '["Only investing layer","Investing","prevertebral","and pretracheal layers","All three equally Only pretracheal Only prevertebral"]'::jsonb, 'Investing', 'The carotid sheath is a condensation of the deep cervical fascia, with the investing layer contributing to it. It encloses the common and internal carotid arteries, the internal jugular vein, and the vagus nerve.'),
('e6ec108d-16c0-5b60-a8c2-792051c65028'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid sheath contents:', '["Common carotid","external carotid","hypoglossal","Common carotid subclavian recurrent laryngeal","Subclavian artery vein phrenic Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid', 'The common carotid artery lies within the carotid sheath together with the internal carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve. The external carotid artery leaves the sheath, and the subclavian, recurrent laryngeal, and phrenic nerves are not sheath contents.'),
('8d9747b5-ae22-55bf-81f2-dcc038e56215'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Vagus nerve in carotid sheath lies:', '["Lateral to vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial to artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to both mediated by specific receptor activation","Between and posterior to carotid artery and IJV","Posterior to both leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between and posterior to carotid artery and IJV', 'Within the carotid sheath the vagus nerve lies between and posterior to the common carotid artery and the internal jugular vein. The other positional relations do not match this arrangement.'),
('fd884818-e4b7-5964-afc5-55d0dbecd0d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Internal jugular vein lies:', '["Lateral to common carotid artery","Medial to common carotid","Posterior to vagus","Anterior to common carotid","Posterior to vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to common carotid artery', 'The internal jugular vein lies lateral to the common carotid artery within the carotid sheath. The vagus nerve lies between and posterior to the two vessels.'),
('32504580-d078-5e64-8f02-40fa87be4567'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Right common carotid is a branch of:', '["Brachiocephalic trunk","Aortic arch directly","External carotid","Internal carotid","Subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk', 'The right common carotid artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk behind the right sternoclavicular joint. The left common carotid arises directly from the aortic arch.'),
('e60ce2b1-34a6-574e-8020-9fb57b0c7aee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Left common carotid is a branch of:', '["Left subclavian","Thoracic aorta","Brachiocephalic trunk","Aortic arch directly","Innominate"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch directly', 'The left common carotid artery is the second branch arising directly from the aortic arch. The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise only to the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries.'),
('bd3bb7b2-41ef-59ab-b288-52084d960c43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Internal carotid enters skull through:', '["Foramen magnum","Foramen ovale","Carotid canal in petrous temporal","Foramen lacerum","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid canal in petrous temporal', 'The internal carotid artery enters the cranial cavity through the carotid canal in the petrous temporal bone. The jugular foramen transmits the internal jugular vein and the glossopharyngeal, vagus, and accessory nerves.'),
('8e8810b1-2db0-5c47-836a-f0a3c53b2876'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Hypoglossal nerve in neck:', '["Passes posterior to carotid sheath","Passes anterior to hyoid","Passes through stylomastoid foramen","Passes between internal carotid and internal jugular vein","Passes with vagus"]'::jsonb, 'Passes between internal carotid and internal jugular vein', 'The hypoglossal nerve crosses the neck deep to the posterior belly of digastric, passing between the internal carotid artery and the internal jugular vein. The stylomastoid foramen transmits the facial nerve, not CN XII.'),
('abd6676b-8cd6-540b-8924-8701da276ca1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Hypoglossal nerve supplies:', '["Muscles of soft palate","Muscles of face","Muscles of pharynx","All tongue muscles except palatoglossus","Muscles of larynx"]'::jsonb, 'All tongue muscles except palatoglossus', 'The hypoglossal nerve supplies all intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue except palatoglossus, which is innervated by the pharyngeal plexus via the vagus. The other choices are supplied by other cranial nerves.'),
('b87382a5-3ad2-566d-b976-482cfc627554'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The hypoglossal nerve (XII) also supplies:', '["Laryngeal muscles","Suprahyoid muscles","Thyrohyoid and geniohyoid (via C1 fibers)","Infrahyoid muscles","Pharyngeal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrohyoid and geniohyoid (via C1 fibers)', 'C1 fibers traveling with the hypoglossal nerve supply the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles. The intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles, except palatoglossus, are the main targets of the nerve itself.'),
('06da856f-c025-537a-87f1-774b79e3bec3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Digastric muscle has how many bellies?', '["Four resulting in decreased functional activity","Three accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple mediated by specific receptor activation","Two (anterior and posterior)","One leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two (anterior and posterior)', 'The digastric muscle has two bellies (anterior and posterior) joined by an intermediate tendon that passes through a fibrous sling on the hyoid bone. Its name reflects this two-bellied structure.'),
('51f9c084-3180-5f82-9881-12ce12bd5d9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Intermediate tendon of digastric is attached to:', '["Styloid process resulting in decreased functional activity","Skull base accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Mylohyoid line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyoid bone (greater cornu) by fibrous sling"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid bone (greater cornu) by fibrous sling', 'The intermediate tendon of the digastric muscle is held to the hyoid bone by a fibrous sling attached near the greater cornu. The other sites listed do not anchor this tendon.'),
('8e57ca09-1225-58a5-9bd9-c8d5bda77a72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Strap muscles (infrahyoid) include:', '["Digastric","stylohyoid","mylohyoid Scalenes levator scapulae","SCM trapezius Longus colli capitis","Sternohyoid sternothyroid thyrohyoid omohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Sternohyoid sternothyroid thyrohyoid omohyoid', 'The four strap (infrahyoid) muscles are the sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thyrohyoid, and omohyoid. Digastric, stylohyoid, and mylohyoid belong to the suprahyoid group.'),
('ed99e771-cd29-531f-a70d-8ed9b17a254e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Suprahyoid muscles include:', '["Scalenes","levator scapulae","Sternohyoid omohyoid sternothyroid","Digastric stylohyoid mylohyoid geniohyoid","SCM trapezius Longus colli capitis"]'::jsonb, 'Digastric stylohyoid mylohyoid geniohyoid', 'The four suprahyoid muscles are the digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, and geniohyoid. The sternohyoid, omohyoid, and sternothyroid belong to the infrahyoid (strap) group.'),
('f5f4e90c-16dc-56c9-9eb6-06b3964bbad5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Infrahyoid muscles function:', '["Depress hyoid bone and larynx","Rotate neck","Tilt head","Elevate hyoid and larynx","Elevate mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Depress hyoid bone and larynx', 'The infrahyoid (strap) muscles pull the hyoid bone and larynx downward, steadying the hyoid during swallowing. Elevation of the hyoid and larynx is performed by the suprahyoid muscles.'),
('9000e54c-77cd-5d5a-88f8-808dd80918c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Suprahyoid muscles function:', '["Elevate hyoid","floor of mouth","depress mandible","Depress hyoid","Elevate mandible only Rotate head Depress mandible only"]'::jsonb, 'Elevate hyoid', 'The suprahyoid muscles raise the hyoid bone, elevating the floor of the mouth during swallowing; with the hyoid fixed, they help depress the mandible. The infrahyoid muscles perform the opposite action.'),
('9bc4b69c-2fff-551b-9127-26dc97a9c985'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Deep cervical fascia layers from superficial to deep:', '["Prevertebral","investing","pretracheal","Investing pretracheal","prevertebral Pretracheal"]'::jsonb, 'investing', 'From superficial to deep, the layers of the deep cervical fascia are the investing, pretracheal, and prevertebral layers, so the investing layer is the most superficial of the three.'),
('397adcf6-0f7e-5d37-bc76-c47be6c03259'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia surrounds:', '["The entire neck like a collar","Only vertebral column","Only trachea and esophagus","Only carotid sheath","Only thyroid"]'::jsonb, 'The entire neck like a collar', 'The investing layer of the deep cervical fascia forms a collar around the entire neck, splitting to enclose the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles. It does not selectively surround the thyroid, trachea, or carotid sheath.'),
('f5ca78eb-f4df-514f-8a59-6d8d1ce7eee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Pretracheal fascia encloses:', '["Thyroid gland","trachea","esophagus","Scalene muscles","Carotid jugular Vertebral muscles SCM"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid gland', 'The pretracheal fascia forms a sleeve that encloses the thyroid gland and also surrounds the trachea and esophagus. The scalene muscles lie beneath the prevertebral fascia.'),
('f3487e55-246e-58d8-a9ba-fc4e6e7819e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Pretracheal fascia is limited superiorly to:', '["Thyroid cartilage","Hyoid bone","Mandible","Skull base","Cricoid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid bone', 'The pretracheal fascia is limited above by the hyoid bone and below blends with the fibrous pericardium. It does not extend up to the mandible or skull base.'),
('555db39e-22a4-5a40-b504-92402da485d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Pretracheal fascia extends into thorax to:', '["Fibrous pericardium","Diaphragm","Carina","T3 vertebra","Aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous pericardium', 'The pretracheal fascia descends into the superior mediastinum and blends with the fibrous pericardium. This continuity explains why anterior neck infections can track down to the pericardium.'),
('6ad72115-a7b1-55ec-a2c7-e286a0d64b12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Prevertebral fascia extends laterally as:', '["Pretracheal fascia","Buccopharyngeal fascia","Carotid sheath","Investing fascia","Axillary sheath"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary sheath', 'Laterally, the prevertebral fascia continues as the axillary sheath, which surrounds the brachial plexus and axillary vessels. The other options name different fascial layers or sheaths.'),
('2d1493c1-45a7-5bb2-919f-74131fa646d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Axillary sheath contains:', '["Brachial plexus and axillary artery","Subclavian vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","IJV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial plexus and axillary artery', 'The axillary sheath, a continuation of the prevertebral fascia, encloses the brachial plexus and the axillary artery. The axillary vein lies partly outside the sheath and the phrenic nerve is not within it.'),
('e03d427e-a328-5ad0-8d32-d5ba5a859fbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Buccopharyngeal fascia covers:', '["Temporalis resulting in decreased functional activity","Mylohyoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Masseter mediated by specific receptor activation","Parotid gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Buccinator muscle and pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator muscle and pharynx', 'The buccopharyngeal fascia covers the buccinator muscle and the pharyngeal constrictors. It forms the anterior boundary of the retropharyngeal space.'),
('734b15ca-de49-5235-97cb-5b360f5c510e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Retropharyngeal space extends from:', '["Skull base to diaphragm","Skull base to T1-T2","C1 to C7","Hyoid to clavicle","Mandible to mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'Skull base to T1-T2', 'The retropharyngeal space extends from the skull base down to the T1-T2 level. It is bounded anteriorly by the buccopharyngeal fascia and posteriorly by the prevertebral fascia.'),
('6ed41708-0cd3-58d5-80e8-079c3c1b4e9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Vertebral artery arises from:', '["Aortic arch","External carotid","Brachiocephalic","Internal carotid","Subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery', 'The vertebral artery is the first branch of the subclavian artery. It ascends through the transverse foramina of the upper six cervical vertebrae before entering the skull.'),
('bc35add2-ce7d-5893-ac42-64d805d402f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Vertebral artery enters transverse foramen of:', '["C7","C1","T1","C2","C6"]'::jsonb, 'C6', 'The vertebral artery enters the transverse foramen at C6 and then ascends through the foramina of C6 to C1. It does not pass through the transverse foramen of C7.'),
('5d7dddd3-a4d2-5c1f-bff8-b5fd32c0838d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Thoracic duct drains into:', '["SVC resulting in decreased functional activity","Right subclavian vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right IJV mediated by specific receptor activation","Left brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left subclavian vein at junction with IJV (left venous angle)"]'::jsonb, 'Left subclavian vein at junction with IJV (left venous angle)', 'The thoracic duct terminates at the left venous angle, where the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins meet. The right side of the body is drained by the right lymphatic duct into the right venous angle.'),
('b9211c6f-e04e-5309-9145-84e1729cd933'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Right lymphatic duct drains into:', '["Right external jugular resulting in decreased functional activity","Right venous angle (subclavian + IJV junction)","SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Right brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left subclavian leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right venous angle (subclavian + IJV junction)', 'The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right side of the head, neck, and thorax into the right venous angle at the junction of the right subclavian and right internal jugular veins.'),
('664c0eec-16be-56a7-94f9-6517993618c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Thoracic duct crosses the neck at:', '["T1 in front of subclavian","C7 level","behind the carotid sheath","C4 level in front of sheath","C6 in front of scalene C2 behind SCM"]'::jsonb, 'C7 level', 'The thoracic duct enters the base of the neck behind the carotid sheath, arching over the subclavian artery to reach the left venous angle at about the C7 level. It runs behind, not in front of, the sheath.'),
('1163d561-88cd-59e0-855b-b8982c435509'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Injury to thoracic duct causes:', '["Bleeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Air embolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chyle leak (milky lymph containing fat)"]'::jsonb, 'Chyle leak (milky lymph containing fat)', 'Injury to the thoracic duct in the lower neck produces leakage of chyle, a milky lymph rich in absorbed fat from the intestine. Bleeding, nerve injury, and air embolism are complications of other neck structures.'),
('72a57535-a2bd-573b-a30e-1447b2bec024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Cervical sympathetic chain lies:', '["Anterior to sheath","In pretracheal fascia","In posterior triangle","Behind carotid sheath on prevertebral fascia","In the carotid sheath"]'::jsonb, 'Behind carotid sheath on prevertebral fascia', 'The cervical sympathetic chain lies on the prevertebral fascia behind the carotid sheath. Damage at this site produces Horner''s syndrome.'),
('60ebf59d-26c8-51f3-ae25-76bc0c3e637f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Superior cervical ganglion is at level of:', '["C5-C6","C2-C3 vertebrae","C3-C4","C1-C2","C4-C5"]'::jsonb, 'C2-C3 vertebrae', 'The superior cervical ganglion lies opposite the C2-C3 vertebrae, near the skull base. The middle and stellate ganglia lie at lower cervical levels.'),
('e5b0e2f4-afdc-50c4-b84c-d520cc0206f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Middle cervical ganglion is at level of:', '["C4-C5","C5-C6","C3-C4","C7","C6 vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'C6 vertebra', 'The middle cervical ganglion is located at the level of the C6 vertebra, near the inferior thyroid artery. The superior ganglion lies at C2-C3 and the stellate ganglion at C7-T1.'),
('71e0d9a0-b0f8-539c-bca3-2e97883900b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Cervicothoracic (stellate) ganglion is at level of:', '["C7-T1","C5-C6","C6-C7","T1-T2","C4-C5"]'::jsonb, 'C7-T1', 'The cervicothoracic (stellate) ganglion lies at the level of the C7-T1 vertebrae. The superior and middle ganglia are located more cranially.'),
('2764037e-7660-524d-ab5c-816cd5b5d41c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Horner''s syndrome results from:', '["CN XI injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Interruption of cervical sympathetic chain","Phrenic injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic overactivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interruption of cervical sympathetic chain', 'Horner''s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, and anhydrosis) results from interruption of the cervical sympathetic chain. Injury to CN XI, the phrenic nerve, or the vagus does not produce this triad.'),
('22d19d15-5a7b-58e6-943e-e04accfb711a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Thyrocervical trunk is a branch of:', '["External carotid","Subclavian artery (first part)","Maxillary","Vertebral","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (first part)', 'The thyrocervical trunk arises from the first part of the subclavian artery. It gives off the inferior thyroid, suprascapular, and transverse cervical arteries.'),
('46196f3d-272a-5a07-9d2f-5395ae1a4c54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Inferior thyroid artery is a branch of:', '["Thyrocervical trunk","Internal carotid","External carotid","Costocervical trunk","Subclavian second part"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk', 'The inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk and supplies the thyroid gland. The costocervical trunk and the second part of the subclavian artery give different branches.'),
('19982c7e-f07b-5492-ae90-7b12d8997611'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Suprascapular artery is a branch of:', '["Axillary","Subclavian third part","Thyrocervical trunk","Internal carotid","External carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk', 'The suprascapular artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk, which in turn comes off the first part of the subclavian artery. The axillary artery supplies it only via other branches.'),
('7e69bbf1-fe39-5826-96f4-2eb1844dd258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Transverse cervical artery is a branch of:', '["Subclavian third part","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid","Axillary","Costocervical trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk', 'The transverse cervical artery is a branch of the thyrocervical trunk, which arises from the first part of the subclavian artery. The costocervical trunk and axillary artery are separate sources.'),
('64d9270b-b989-5399-87cb-4ec7887cf3f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Costocervical trunk is a branch of:', '["Axillary","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid","Internal carotid","Subclavian artery (second part)"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (second part)', 'The costocervical trunk arises from the second part of the subclavian artery, behind the scalenus anterior. It gives rise to the superior intercostal and deep cervical arteries.'),
('be37ccb2-af31-5bfd-bf6e-e8505e2a7ca0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Deep cervical artery is a branch of:', '["Subclavian first part","Thyrocervical trunk","Costocervical trunk","External carotid","Vertebral"]'::jsonb, 'Costocervical trunk', 'The deep cervical artery is a branch of the costocervical trunk. The thyrocervical trunk instead gives off the inferior thyroid, suprascapular, and transverse cervical arteries.'),
('b8b6d7bd-ab9f-5f45-b059-cc33a9a82712'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Upper border of thyroid cartilage is at level of:', '["C3","C4 vertebra","C5","C7","C6"]'::jsonb, 'C4 vertebra', 'The upper border of the thyroid cartilage (the thyroid notch) lies at the level of the C4 vertebra. The cricoid cartilage lies at C6 and the isthmus of the thyroid crosses the trachea at the C7-T1 level.'),
('a515bc21-71b5-5b6b-ae48-f74c112470fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Cricoid cartilage is at level of:', '["C4","C6 vertebra","T1","C5","C7"]'::jsonb, 'C6 vertebra', 'The cricoid cartilage lies at the level of the C6 vertebra, the same level as the middle cervical ganglion. The upper border of the thyroid cartilage is at C4.'),
('559d466a-6e9d-50de-8def-eb804eebd6b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Hyoid bone is at level of:', '["C4","C6","C3 vertebra","C2","C5"]'::jsonb, 'C3 vertebra', 'The hyoid bone lies anteriorly at the level of the C3 vertebra, roughly opposite the intervertebral disc just below it. It is the level at which the tongue, thyroglossal duct, and mid-third of the carotid artery landmarks are assessed.'),
('e8cdd96a-cf68-5647-8e97-449a2f481993'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Carotid artery bifurcation is at level of:', '["Hyoid (C3)","Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)","Angle of mandible (C2)","Cricoid (C6)","Sternoclavicular joint (T2)"]'::jsonb, 'Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)', 'The common carotid bifurcates into internal and external carotid arteries at the level of the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, which corresponds to the C4 vertebra. The carotid sinus and carotid body are located at this bifurcation.'),
('ef67ff77-34f2-592b-bcb9-8c5a3498bdba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The common carotid artery pulse is felt by pressing against:', '["Thyroid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Angle of mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior tubercle of C6 (Chassaignac''s tubercle)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior tubercle of C6 (Chassaignac''s tubercle)', 'The common carotid artery passes directly in front of the prominent anterior tubercle of the C6 transverse process, the carotid tubercle of Chassaignac, against which the pulse is compressed and felt. The thyroid cartilage, hyoid, and trachea provide no comparable bony point of compression.'),
('03329305-cfa3-5751-94f0-c81b8a29adb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The anterior tubercle of C6 is also called:', '["Carotid tubercle (Chassaignac''s)","Dens","Transverse process","Odontoid process","Spinous process"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid tubercle (Chassaignac''s)', 'The enlarged anterior tubercle of the C6 transverse process is called the carotid tubercle of Chassaignac because the common carotid artery lies immediately in front of it and can be compressed against it. Dens and odontoid process are alternative names for the tooth-like projection of the axis (C2).'),
('081913cf-946d-5db1-960a-7bd17f7bf846'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The fascia covering the submandibular gland is from:', '["Superficial fascia","Prevertebral fascia","Pretracheal fascia","Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Buccopharyngeal fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The investing layer of the deep cervical fascia splits into superficial and deep laminae to enclose the submandibular and parotid glands. The pretracheal fascia forms the visceral compartment around the thyroid and trachea, and the prevertebral fascia lies more posteriorly over the paravertebral muscles.'),
('e9e7edef-c0d8-5202-81cd-ea26b1ed1efc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The deep part of submandibular gland lies:', '["Below mylohyoid","In submental triangle","Above mylohyoid","In carotid triangle","Deep to mylohyoid on hyoglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to mylohyoid on hyoglossus', 'The deep part of the submandibular gland hooks around the posterior free border of mylohyoid to lie deep to that muscle, resting on hyoglossus above the mylohyoid line. The superficial part lies below mylohyoid within the submandibular triangle.'),
('46268f3a-f365-5c7f-9193-45868efaa442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The stylohyoid muscle is pierced by:', '["Lingual nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Intermediate tendon of digastric","Glossopharyngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate tendon of digastric', 'The intermediate tendon of the digastric pierces the stylohyoid near its insertion on the hyoid bone, splitting the muscle into two slips. The lingual, hypoglossal, and glossopharyngeal nerves pass through this region but do not perforate the stylohyoid.'),
('6e93ebf5-37ad-5c64-869b-b83358aff530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The pterygomandibular raphe separates which muscles?', '["Palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus","Medial and lateral pterygoid","Mylohyoid and hyoglossus","Masseter and temporalis","Buccinator and superior constrictor"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator and superior constrictor', 'The pterygomandibular raphe, a band of buccopharyngeal fascia, connects the hamulus of the medial pterygoid plate to the posterior end of the mylohyoid line and separates the buccinator in front from the superior pharyngeal constrictor behind. The other muscle pairs do not unite along this raphe.'),
('2c13bcbc-cc18-5408-aa17-948725254011'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The retromolar triangle is located:', '["In the sublingual region","In the retropharyngeal space","In front of 1st molar","Behind the 3rd molar tooth","Between upper and lower molars"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the 3rd molar tooth', 'The retromolar triangle is a shallow triangular depression on the body of the mandible behind the third molar tooth. Its posteromedial border gives attachment to the pterygomandibular raphe.'),
('5e8a5dc4-86e5-5ffa-a78b-af64ae687309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The inferior alveolar nerve enters the mandible at:', '["Mandibular foramen","Mental foramen","Genial tubercle","Mandibular notch","Incisive foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular foramen', 'The inferior alveolar nerve, a branch of the mandibular nerve, passes through the mandibular foramen on the inner aspect of the ramus to run in the mandibular canal. Its terminal branch exits more anteriorly through the mental foramen.'),
('b6492b88-a50d-5da5-b00d-9fc155c1c23f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The mental nerve emerges from:', '["Infraorbital foramen","Mental foramen","Mandibular foramen","Supraorbital notch","Incisive foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Mental foramen', 'The mental nerve, the terminal branch of the inferior alveolar nerve, emerges from the mental foramen on the anterolateral surface of the mandibular body to supply the skin of the chin and lower lip and the gingiva. The infraorbital foramen transmits a branch of the maxillary nerve.'),
('6df21a91-6182-5b9d-a40e-408fcc937585'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The lingual nerve lies in the floor of mouth:', '["Deep to hyoglossus","above submandibular duct","Below mylohyoid","In the carotid triangle","In the submental triangle Superficial to hyoglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to hyoglossus', 'In the floor of the mouth the lingual nerve runs deep to the hyoglossus muscle, in close relation to the submandibular duct and sublingual gland. The hypoglossal nerve also lies on hyoglossus, but the lingual nerve is the one described here.'),
('d2698e58-b874-5676-ae37-80d3045f9df1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submandibular duct is also called:', '["Stensen''s duct","Wharton''s duct","Nasolacrimal duct","Rivinus ducts","Bartholin''s duct"]'::jsonb, 'Wharton''s duct', 'The duct of the submandibular gland is called Wharton''s duct; it runs forward on hyoglossus with the lingual nerve and opens on a sublingual papilla beside the lingual frenulum. Stensen''s duct drains the parotid gland and the ducts of Rivinus drain the sublingual gland.'),
('8c80aa4d-6645-59e1-b161-b149fa1266f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submandibular lymph nodes drain:', '["Upper lip and cheek","Anterior tongue","floor of mouth lower lip","chin Posterior tongue","Posterior scalp Parotid region"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior tongue', 'The submandibular lymph nodes drain the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, the floor of the mouth, the cheek, part of the lower lip, and the submandibular/sublingual glands, so the anterior tongue is a major drainage territory. The chin, tip of the tongue, and mid-lower lip drain mainly to the submental nodes.'),
('cae4b164-2a4b-5735-8e87-a4d884076a40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submental lymph nodes are located:', '["Deep to SCM","In carotid triangle","In submandibular triangle","Between anterior bellies of digastric","In posterior triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Between anterior bellies of digastric', 'The submental lymph nodes lie in the submental triangle, between the anterior bellies of the two digastric muscles and above the hyoid bone. The larger group of nodes around the submandibular gland occupies the submandibular triangle more laterally.'),
('de2f0830-7f98-565f-9114-fad79ca7dfff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submental lymph nodes drain:', '["Lower lip","chin","tip of tongue floor of mouth","Upper lip and nose Anterior scalp","Posterior neck Parotid region"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lip', 'The submental lymph nodes drain the chin, the mid-portion of the lower lip, the tip of the tongue, and the anterior floor of the mouth. The submandibular nodes, in contrast, drain the cheek, anterior tongue, and part of the lower face.'),
('ed7a0855-3871-560c-a060-6019f808eeb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The facial artery is a branch of:', '["Lingual","Internal carotid","External carotid artery","Maxillary","Superior thyroid"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The facial artery arises from the anterior surface of the external carotid artery, usually just above the lingual artery, and supplies the face by crossing the mandible at the anterior border of masseter. The internal carotid gives off no branches in the neck.'),
('c6fd7ef0-f2a9-5177-9bd2-b71c897198f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The facial artery crosses the mandible at:', '["Submandibular triangle","Posterior digastric","Mental foramen","Angle of mandible","Anterior border of masseter (facial notch)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of masseter (facial notch)', 'The facial artery leaves the submandibular region by hooking over the lower border of the mandible at the anterior border of masseter, where its pulse is felt against the bone at the facial notch. It then ascends tortuously toward the medial canthus of the eye.'),
('061a0cc8-1a50-52a2-8b0d-45db6b86c646'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The facial artery pulse is felt at:', '["Submandibular triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","Pre-auricular region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temple mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior border of masseter at lower border of mandible","Angle of mandible leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of masseter at lower border of mandible', 'The facial artery pulse is palpated where the artery crosses the lower border of the mandible at the anterior border of masseter, compressing it against the bone. Distally it continues across the cheek toward the angle of the mouth and medial canthus.'),
('c1213956-a635-558e-95fa-9053ec9bdeaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The facial vein drains into:', '["Internal jugular via common facial vein","Retromandibular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian mediated by specific receptor activation","Brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External jugular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular via common facial vein', 'The facial vein joins the anterior division of the retromandibular vein to form the common facial vein, which drains into the internal jugular vein. Through the angular and ophthalmic veins it communicates with the cavernous sinus, a route for spread of facial infection.'),
('c1d6ac04-71fd-5ba1-b241-3118af443796'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The carotid triangle contains which of the following contents?', '["Submandibular gland and hypoglossal nerve Vertebral artery and sympathetic trunk","Cervical plexus and phrenic nerve Thyroid gland and recurrent laryngeal nerve","Common carotid artery","internal carotid artery external carotid artery","internal jugular vein vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid artery', 'The carotid triangle contains the common carotid artery and its bifurcation into internal and external carotids, the carotid body and sinus, the internal jugular vein, the vagus nerve, and related structures. Its boundaries are the posterior belly of digastric, the superior belly of omohyoid, and the sternocleidomastoid.'),
('7d2b391b-71b2-58d7-b0fc-283194125caa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The neck is divided into triangles by:', '["Platysma muscle","Trapezius muscle","Digastric muscle","Sternocleidomastoid muscle","Omohyoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Sternocleidomastoid muscle', 'The sternocleidomastoid muscle divides each side of the neck into an anterior triangle in front of it and a posterior triangle behind it. The trapezius and omohyoid participate in the boundaries of individual triangles but do not make the primary division.'),
('e17a5b42-5e9d-5b40-a74e-dd62129802ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The anterior triangle of the neck is bounded by:', '["Clavicle and SCM","Mandible and hyoid","Midline and trapezius","SCM and trapezius","Midline"]'::jsonb, 'Midline', 'The anterior triangle of the neck lies in front of the sternocleidomastoid and is bounded by the anterior border of that muscle, the midline of the neck, and the inferior border of the mandible. The trapezius and clavicle confine the posterior triangle rather than the anterior one.'),
('1d2a4a01-4be2-5557-b2c0-5ac10fadba49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The posterior triangle of the neck is bounded by:', '["SCM","mandible","and midline","Clavicle","B but not A"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle', 'The posterior triangle of the neck lies behind the sternocleidomastoid and is bounded by the posterior border of the SCM, the anterior border of the trapezius, and the middle third of the clavicle inferiorly. The mandible and midline are landmarks of the anterior triangle.'),
('d931b42a-9770-5d18-bd73-d8bd5dc48368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The platysma muscle is innervated by:', '["Spinal accessory nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Cervical plexus","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve (cervical branch)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (cervical branch)', 'The platysma, a muscle of facial expression in the superficial fascia of the neck, is supplied by the cervical branch of the facial nerve. The spinal accessory, hypoglossal, and cervical plexus nerves supply other muscles that are not facial muscles of expression.'),
('70977ce7-429c-5591-af05-d51a480ce27e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The sternocleidomastoid is innervated by:', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2-3","Trigeminal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Cervical plexus alone"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2-3', 'The sternocleidomastoid receives its motor supply from the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) reinforced by proprioceptive fibers from the second and third cervical nerves. The trigeminal, hypoglossal, and facial nerves supply entirely different muscles of the head and neck.'),
('24607ef3-a300-5836-9f56-350bad4cec6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The trapezius is innervated by:', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3-4","Facial nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Cervical plexus only","Hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3-4', 'The trapezius is innervated by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) together with proprioceptive fibers from the C3 and C4 cervical nerves. Injury to the accessory nerve weakens shoulder shrug and causes mild winging of the scapula with resisted abduction.'),
('784cd338-0ff5-5a8e-917c-61ef5c70671f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The strap muscles (infrahyoid) include:', '["Platysma and SCM","Mylohyoid and geniohyoid","Sternohyoid","thyrohyoid","sternothyroid"]'::jsonb, 'Sternohyoid', 'The infrahyoid strap muscles are the sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thyrohyoid, and omohyoid, of which the sternohyoid is the most superficial. Mylohyoid and geniohyoid are suprahyoid muscles, and the platysma and sternocleidomastoid are not classified as strap muscles.'),
('038b5b86-a711-558b-8742-2223dd3ef296'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The strap muscles are innervated by:', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve","Phrenic nerve","Ansa cervicalis (C1-3)"]'::jsonb, 'Ansa cervicalis (C1-3)', 'The infrahyoid strap muscles are supplied by the ansa cervicalis, formed by roots from the C1, C2, and C3 spinal nerves, except thyrohyoid which receives C1 fibers that travel briefly with the hypoglossal nerve. The phrenic nerve (C3-C5) supplies the diaphragm, not the strap muscles.'),
('9954cf95-5cc1-5037-87cd-0a99d0a23647'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The carotid sheath contains:', '["Carotid artery and IJV only","Only the common carotid","Vagus nerve and sympathetic trunk","Common carotid artery","internal jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve and sympathetic trunk', 'The carotid sheath encloses the common and internal carotid arteries, the internal jugular vein, and the vagus nerve, while the cervical sympathetic trunk runs in the posterior wall of the sheath behind the vessels. The sheath is a condensation of the deep cervical fascia extending from the base of the skull to the root of the neck.'),
('13b41208-184a-55fc-8bfd-79a2a42154ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The common carotid artery divides at:', '["Clavicle (T1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Angle of mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cricoid cartilage (C6) mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4 level)","Hyoid bone (C3) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4 level)', 'The common carotid artery bifurcates into internal and external carotids at the level of the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, corresponding to the C4 vertebra. The cricoid cartilage (C6) is the level at which the artery is compressed against Chassaignac''s tubercle.'),
('de6d2648-4591-5947-abc5-217fc43941a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The carotid sinus is:', '["A vein resulting in decreased functional activity","A lymph node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A chemoreceptor mediated by specific receptor activation","A baroreceptor located at the carotid bifurcation","A muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A baroreceptor located at the carotid bifurcation', 'The carotid sinus is a baroreceptor in the dilated origin of the internal carotid artery at the carotid bifurcation that monitors blood pressure via the glossopharyngeal nerve. The carotid body, by contrast, is a chemoreceptor responding to blood gas levels.'),
('bd8bb1c2-baa3-5b77-9b25-e189c61390e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The carotid body is:', '["A baroreceptor resulting in decreased functional activity","A chemoreceptor sensitive to O2","CO2 mediated by specific receptor activation","and pH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lymph node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A chemoreceptor sensitive to O2', 'The carotid body is a chemoreceptor at the carotid bifurcation that responds mainly to falling arterial oxygen tension, prompting increased ventilation through the glossopharyngeal nerve reflex. Baroreceptors at the carotid sinus detect pressure rather than blood gases.'),
('7da9387e-ea18-5e18-861b-eef52e35e5e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The external carotid artery branches include:', '["None of the above","Superior thyroid","lingual","facial","occipital"]'::jsonb, 'Superior thyroid', 'The external carotid artery gives off eight branches, beginning with the superior thyroid artery, which supplies the thyroid gland and larynx. The lingual, facial, and occipital arteries are also external carotid branches within the neck and face, while the internal carotid gives off none in the neck.'),
('d71f89c3-7e8c-5e28-a63c-e4c9aae5a9ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The internal carotid artery:', '["Has no branches in the neck","Is smaller than the ECA","Supplies the face","Supplies the scalp","Gives off 8 branches"]'::jsonb, 'Has no branches in the neck', 'The internal carotid artery enters the skull through the carotid canal and gives off no branches in the neck; its branches, including the ophthalmic and cerebral arteries, arise intracranially. The external carotid supplies the face and scalp and gives off branches throughout the neck.'),
('8c3fabd1-3d95-517e-a6e9-138f30b98851'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The cervical plexus is formed by:', '["C1-C4 anterior rami","C3-C5","C1-T1","T1-T4","C5-C8"]'::jsonb, 'C1-C4 anterior rami', 'The cervical plexus is formed by the anterior rami of the C1 to C4 cervical nerves, lying deep to the internal jugular vein on the levator scapulae and scalenus medius. It gives rise to the ansa cervicalis, phrenic nerve (with C5), and cutaneous and muscular branches.'),
('1e247796-96a8-5e56-aa6b-2d1c493d8c59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The phrenic nerve is formed by:', '["C6","C7","C8","C5","0.5×"]'::jsonb, 'C5', 'The phrenic nerve arises mainly from the C4 anterior ramus with contributions from C3 and C5, so C5 is a genuine component of the plexus origin; it descends over the anterior scalene into the thorax. The C7 nerve root forms part of the brachial plexus, not the phrenic nerve.'),
('9cc69223-1e25-5ff1-b4af-a3fdd113b4c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The phrenic nerve supplies:', '["The intercostal muscles","The SCM","The abdominal muscles","The trapezius","The diaphragm (motor and sensory)"]'::jsonb, 'The diaphragm (motor and sensory)', 'The phrenic nerve provides the sole motor supply to the diaphragm and carries sensory fibers to its central tendon as well as to the overlying pleura and peritoneum. It arises from C3-C5 and descends between the subclavian vein and artery to enter the mediastinum.'),
('91b0d2c2-da20-575c-b532-7969d1661125'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The pretracheal fascia encloses:', '["The platysma","The thyroid gland","trachea","and esophagus","The SCM"]'::jsonb, 'The thyroid gland', 'The pretracheal fascia, the visceral layer of the deep cervical fascia, encloses the thyroid and parathyroid glands, the trachea, and the esophagus within the anterior compartment of the neck. The platysma lies in superficial fascia and the sternocleidomastoid is enclosed by the investing layer.'),
('ffb2894c-e0f1-5a9b-b08a-47ce9da8b66e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The retropharyngeal space is:', '["The space in the oral cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","The laryngeal ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A potential space between the pharynx and prevertebral fascia (danger space)","The space in the middle ear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space in the nasal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A potential space between the pharynx and prevertebral fascia (danger space)', 'The retropharyngeal space is a potential space between the buccopharyngeal fascia covering the pharynx and the prevertebral fascia; because it continues down to the superior mediastinum, it is a danger space for spread of infection. The pretracheal space lies more anteriorly around the trachea.'),
('8ac358f9-f85e-5316-9127-859cc7ca62c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submandibular gland is located in:', '["Submental triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior triangle above hyoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Submandibular triangle (between bellies of digastric)","Parotid region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular triangle (between bellies of digastric)', 'The submandibular gland lies in the submandibular triangle, bounded by the anterior and posterior bellies of the digastric and the lower border of the mandible. Its deep part wraps around the posterior border of mylohyoid into the floor of the mouth, and its duct (Wharton''s) opens beside the frenulum.'),
('eb9e7165-f49c-5cd9-9887-30c731deb914'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submandibular triangle contents include:', '["Thyroid gland only","Submandibular gland","lymph nodes","facial artery and vein","hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The submandibular triangle contains the superficial part of the submandibular gland, submandibular lymph nodes, the facial artery and vein, the mylohyoid nerve, and the hypoglossal nerve crossing beneath the gland. The gland is the most characteristic content of this triangle.'),
('644d57c9-df09-5c79-88b2-d453b4b1dece'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submental triangle is bounded by:', '["Clavicle and SCM resulting in decreased functional activity","Midline and hyoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyoid and thyroid mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior bellies of digastric and hyoid bone","Mandible and SCM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior bellies of digastric and hyoid bone', 'The submental triangle is bounded by the anterior belly of the digastric on each side, the hyoid bone inferiorly, and the midline anteriorly between the two anterior bellies, with mylohyoid forming its floor. It contains the submental lymph nodes and lies below the chin.'),
('d1d0727b-774d-53f1-a861-a7bff4eec1e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The carotid triangle contains:', '["Carotid bifurcation","carotid body","IJV","vagus nerve","lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid bifurcation', 'The carotid triangle contains the common carotid bifurcation into internal and external carotid arteries, the carotid body and sinus, the internal jugular vein, the vagus nerve, and deep cervical lymph nodes, with the bifurcation being the defining content. It is bounded by the posterior belly of digastric, superior belly of omohyoid, and sternocleidomastoid.'),
('1ee5dabe-464f-565a-9da1-310438e59e32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The lymph nodes of the neck are divided into:', '["5 groups","2 groups","7 levels (Level I-VII)","10 groups","3 groups only"]'::jsonb, '7 levels (Level I-VII)', 'The cervical lymph nodes are classified into seven levels (I-VII) used for staging and neck dissection planning: submental and submandibular (I), upper, middle, and lower jugular (II, III, IV), posterior triangle (V), anterior compartment (VI), and superior mediastinal nodes (VII). Older anatomical texts grouped them into five main groups.'),
('fcd2cf99-bd1a-5445-95dc-0f83fb5cda16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The thoracic duct drains into:', '["Left venous angle (junction of left IJV and subclavian vein)","Superior vena cava directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right venous angle mediated by specific receptor activation","Left subclavian vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Azygos vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left venous angle (junction of left IJV and subclavian vein)', 'The thoracic duct ascends in the posterior mediastinum and arches into the left side of the neck to empty into the left venous angle, the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The right lymphatic duct drains into the right venous angle.'),
('f11682a9-3473-5133-a3ac-a88bcfc70f6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Branchial cleft cysts arise from:', '["First branchial cleft resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroglossal duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Remnants of the second branchial cleft (along the anterior border of SCM)","Fourth branchial cleft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Third branchial cleft leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Remnants of the second branchial cleft (along the anterior border of SCM)', 'Branchial cleft cysts most commonly arise from remnants of the second branchial cleft, presenting as a swelling along the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid between the internal and external carotid arteries. Thyroglossal duct remnants instead produce cysts in the anterior midline that move with swallowing.'),
('d051ff18-644a-5654-b679-45b460276f7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Torticollis (wry neck) is often due to:', '["Thyroiditis resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphadenitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrosis or hematoma of SCM (birth injury or congenital)","Trapezius spasm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervical spine fracture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrosis or hematoma of SCM (birth injury or congenital)', 'Congenital muscular torticollis is typically caused by fibrosis or hematoma of the sternocleidomastoid from birth injury or intrauterine positioning, producing a firm mass in the muscle. Contraction of the affected SCM tilts the head to that side and rotates the chin to the opposite side.'),
('d003831c-e1cb-51b8-9c1b-2c6daf0a0293'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The cervical sympathetic trunk is located:', '["In the posterior triangle","Inside the carotid sheath","Posterior to the carotid sheath","on the prevertebral fascia","Lateral to the pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the carotid sheath', 'The cervical sympathetic trunk lies on the prevertebral fascia posterior to the carotid sheath, deep to the internal carotid and common carotid arteries and the internal jugular vein. It descends over the longus colli and longus capitis muscles, and its superior, middle, and inferior ganglia are related to the cervical vertebrae.'),
('0101ff8d-de32-5f0c-9b66-76dc704dde02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Horner''s syndrome in the neck can result from:', '["Injury to the cervical sympathetic trunk","Phrenic nerve injury","Vagus nerve injury","Hypoglossal nerve injury","Spinal accessory nerve injury"]'::jsonb, 'Injury to the cervical sympathetic trunk', 'Horner''s syndrome, with ptosis, miosis, and anhydrosis on the affected side, results from interruption of the cervical sympathetic trunk, as by an apical lung tumor, neck surgery, or trauma. Injury to the phrenic, vagus, hypoglossal, or spinal accessory nerves produces different, non-sympathetic deficits.'),
('f5d5c501-0e8a-5f40-a83d-a7ac0fedcc07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submandibular triangle contains:', '["Parotid gland","Thyroid gland","Sublingual gland","Parathyroid gland","Submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The submandibular triangle houses the submandibular gland, submandibular lymph nodes, the facial artery and vein, and the hypoglossal and mylohyoid nerves. The parotid, thyroid, and parathyroid glands lie outside this triangle.'),
('97c6fc4e-ee80-546b-b1a2-fbb2b00fba6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The external jugular vein drains into:', '["Superior vena cava","Axillary vein","Brachiocephalic vein","Subclavian vein","Internal jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian vein', 'The external jugular vein descends over the sternocleidomastoid and pierces the investing layer of deep cervical fascia to drain into the subclavian vein. The internal jugular vein joins the subclavian to form the brachiocephalic vein, and the superior vena cava is a more distal central vessel.'),
('5f2f66e9-5890-587c-9f99-8e60a10f0e8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The phrenic nerve arises from:', '["C1-C3 cervical plexus","C5-C7 cervical plexus","C3-C5 cervical plexus","Brachial plexus","C8-T1 nerve roots"]'::jsonb, 'C3-C5 cervical plexus', 'The phrenic nerve is formed mainly by the C4 root with contributions from C3 and C5 of the cervical plexus. It descends over the anterior scalene muscle to supply the diaphragm; the brachial plexus arises from C5-T1.'),
('fbe4ed0c-9087-5bbf-acdb-0e1e2213d626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The ansa cervicalis innervates:', '["Scalene muscles","Infrahyoid muscles","Suprahyoid muscles","Sternocleidomastoid","Trapezius"]'::jsonb, 'Infrahyoid muscles', 'The ansa cervicalis, derived from the C1-C3 cervical plexus, supplies the infrahyoid strap muscles including sternohyoid, sternothyroid, and omohyoid. The scalene muscles are supplied by direct cervical rami, while the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius receive the spinal accessory nerve.'),
('8aaf22e8-13f3-535f-be6f-84668357fb99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which option correctly describes Roof of posterior triangle?', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The roof of the posterior triangle is the investing layer of deep cervical fascia, which encloses the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius; superficial fascia and platysma lie superficial to it. The prevertebral fascia forms the floor, and the pretracheal fascia encloses the visceral compartment.'),
('a682d7c6-7cd8-5e1c-9f54-af40e3094dd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Roof of posterior triangle is. Which option is most accurate?', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The roof of the posterior triangle is formed by the investing layer of deep cervical fascia, with superficial fascia and platysma in front of it. The prevertebral fascia overlying the scalene muscles constitutes the floor instead.'),
('3cb3ba8e-b79a-59af-952a-8cb76e2b0c42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Triangles of Neck: roof of posterior triangle is?', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The roof of the posterior triangle is the investing layer of deep cervical fascia, the same layer that splits to enclose the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. The prevertebral fascia lines the floor, and the pretracheal fascia invests the anterior visceral structures.'),
('fef7cc90-c62d-5105-9222-4f81d494d0cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of Neck, identify the correct response: roof of posterior triangle is?', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The investing layer of deep cervical fascia roofs the posterior triangle, lying immediately deep to the superficial fascia and platysma. The floor is formed by the prevertebral fascia over the scalene and levator scapulae muscles.'),
('5c7befac-2bce-5bcd-afd3-4dde0a76dc5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Roof of posterior triangle is. Select the best answer.', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The posterior triangle is roofed by the investing layer of deep cervical fascia. The prevertebral fascia forms the floor, while the pretracheal fascia surrounds the thyroid and trachea and does not contribute to this triangle.'),
('edd6ba6e-6499-514d-a506-ca995c97a028'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which response correctly addresses this Triangles of Neck item: roof of posterior triangle is?', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The roof of the posterior triangle is the investing layer of deep cervical fascia, with the platysma and superficial fascia lying more superficially. The prevertebral fascia, not the investing layer, forms the floor of the triangle.'),
('45b3c817-e382-5666-8de2-508646823e19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which option correctly describes Floor of posterior triangle?', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The floor of the posterior triangle is formed by the prevertebral fascia, which overlies the splenius capitis, levator scapulae, and the three scalene muscles. The investing fascia roofs the triangle, and the vertebral bodies and longus colli lie deeper within the prevertebral compartment.'),
('0f1a8626-78d3-549b-8113-61050a7ec813'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Floor of posterior triangle is. Which option is most accurate?', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The prevertebral fascia covers the floor of the posterior triangle, lying over the scalene and levator scapulae muscles. The investing fascia forms the roof, whereas the pretracheal fascia invests the cervical viscera anteriorly.'),
('8b9ae3f4-d80e-5286-8335-b17c820a0697'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Triangles of Neck: floor of posterior triangle is?', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The floor of the posterior triangle is the prevertebral fascia covering the scalene muscles, levator scapulae, and splenius capitis. The investing fascia forms the roof, and the pretracheal fascia encloses the anterior visceral structures rather than this triangle.'),
('7a0ef97b-0e1b-56bd-af0d-6e7f0c6e95a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of Neck, identify the correct response: floor of posterior triangle is?', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The prevertebral fascia forms the floor of the posterior triangle as it invests the scalene and levator scapulae muscles. The investing layer of deep cervical fascia instead forms the roof of the triangle.'),
('735711e5-6e52-5934-b598-bd9a1ac08edb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Floor of posterior triangle is. Select the best answer.', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The floor of the posterior triangle is made of the prevertebral fascia overlying the scalene muscles. The investing fascia roofs the triangle, and the pretracheal fascia surrounds the trachea, thyroid, and esophagus in the anterior neck.'),
('ad126fb8-9cd2-5913-a088-289a76d0dcf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which response correctly addresses this Triangles of Neck item: floor of posterior triangle is?', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The prevertebral fascia lines the floor of the posterior triangle over the scalene and levator scapulae muscles. The investing layer forms the roof, while the pretracheal fascia and vertebral column are not part of this triangle''s boundaries.'),
('49f0e69a-53fa-5575-82ae-f59150ef2d00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which is NOT a content of posterior triangle. Which option is most accurate?', '["Supraclavicular nerves","Submandibular gland","External jugular vein","Spinal accessory nerve","Brachial plexus roots"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The posterior triangle contains the spinal accessory nerve, external jugular vein, supraclavicular nerves, and brachial plexus roots and trunks. The submandibular gland lies in the submandibular triangle of the anterior neck, not in the posterior triangle.'),
('3ce65a17-306d-50b2-aa0f-106eb8c86ba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Triangles of Neck: which is not a content of posterior triangle?', '["Supraclavicular nerves","Submandibular gland","External jugular vein","Spinal accessory nerve","Brachial plexus roots"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The posterior triangle contains the spinal accessory nerve, external jugular vein, supraclavicular nerves, and brachial plexus roots. The submandibular gland occupies the submandibular triangle in the anterior part of the neck.'),
('6b8622c6-641c-5d16-a141-3a7f9bc54d7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of Neck, identify the correct response: which is not a content of posterior triangle?', '["Supraclavicular nerves","Submandibular gland","External jugular vein","Spinal accessory nerve","Brachial plexus roots"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The spinal accessory nerve, external jugular vein, supraclavicular nerves, and brachial plexus roots all traverse the posterior triangle. The submandibular gland is a content of the submandibular triangle, not of the posterior triangle.'),
('6c13e5a2-0c67-5c85-8f88-737f5e2e07f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: the neck is divided into how many major triangles?', '["2 (anterior and posterior)","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","6 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (anterior and posterior)', 'The sternocleidomastoid divides the neck into two major triangles: the anterior triangle in front and the posterior triangle behind it. Each of these is further subdivided into smaller triangles.'),
('ece4f1e4-c73b-58e2-883f-30121f488026'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: anterior triangle boundaries:?', '["Trapezius","clavicle","SCM Sternum","clavicle mastoid","Mandible mastoid"]'::jsonb, 'SCM Sternum', 'The anterior triangle is bounded posteriorly by the sternocleidomastoid, anteriorly by the midline of the neck, and superiorly by the inferior border of the mandible. The trapezius and clavicle bound the posterior triangle, not the anterior triangle.'),
('ff3116d2-c582-5157-8f50-5afd1597e04b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: posterior triangle boundaries:?', '["Trapezius","clavicle","SCM","Clavicle trapezius","mandible Midline"]'::jsonb, 'SCM', 'The posterior triangle is bounded anteriorly by the sternocleidomastoid, posteriorly by the trapezius, and inferiorly by the clavicle. The SCM is the anterior boundary of this triangle, while the mandible and midline belong to the anterior triangle.'),
('20a76618-7984-5595-90c9-57ae884a8d9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: roof of posterior triangle is:?', '["Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Superficial fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platysma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pretracheal fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevertebral fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The roof of the posterior triangle is the investing layer of deep cervical fascia, with the superficial fascia and platysma superficial to it. The prevertebral fascia forms the floor of the triangle.'),
('2681656e-fe5f-5ed6-bcf2-4437acb26c29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Floor of posterior triangle is:'' is:', '["Vertebral bodies","Pretracheal fascia","Longus colli","Prevertebral fascia","Investing fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia', 'The floor of the posterior triangle is the prevertebral fascia overlying the scalene and levator scapulae muscles. The investing fascia roofs the triangle, and the pretracheal fascia encloses the cervical viscera.'),
('255f775c-edf9-5916-9aff-9a99cbc24d75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: which is not a content of posterior triangle?', '["Supraclavicular nerves","Submandibular gland","External jugular vein","Spinal accessory nerve","Brachial plexus roots"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland', 'The posterior triangle contains the spinal accessory nerve, external jugular vein, supraclavicular nerves, and brachial plexus roots. The submandibular gland lies in the submandibular triangle of the anterior neck, so it is not a content of the posterior triangle.'),
('c8e3bca3-38d3-5e57-9424-9a19c085f75c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: spinal accessory nerve in posterior triangle supplies:?', '["Only SCM","SCM and trapezius","Infrahyoid muscles","Only trapezius","Paraspinal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'SCM and trapezius', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) innervates both the sternocleidomastoid and the trapezius, crossing the posterior triangle between these two muscles. The infrahyoid muscles are supplied by the ansa cervicalis, not by CN XI.'),
('68704b6d-9e36-52bd-8d27-8ee4163d7a76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: external jugular vein in posterior triangle drains into:?', '["Superior vena cava","Subclavian vein","Brachiocephalic vein","Internal jugular vein","Axillary vein"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian vein', 'The external jugular vein descends through the posterior triangle and drains into the subclavian vein. The internal jugular vein is a separate deep channel, and the axillary vein lies below the clavicle.'),
('4866495c-ca79-5230-b06e-c7da616c6a91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: brachial plexus roots (c5-t1) are located in:?', '["Middle part resulting in decreased functional activity","Apex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior part (supraclavicular) of posterior triangle","Base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior part (supraclavicular) of posterior triangle', 'The roots and trunks of the brachial plexus emerge between the scalene muscles into the inferior (supraclavicular) part of the posterior triangle, alongside the third part of the subclavian artery. The apex and base of the triangle contain other structures such as lymph nodes.'),
('c31607bf-371b-5e2d-adc6-d299c1eddf8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: supraclavicular nerves are derived from:?', '["Brachial plexus","Cervical plexus (C3-C4)","Accessory nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical plexus (C3-C4)', 'The supraclavicular nerves arise from the C3 and C4 roots of the cervical plexus and supply the skin of the supraclavicular region and upper chest. The brachial plexus supplies the limb, and the accessory, hypoglossal, and facial nerves are unrelated to this cutaneous supply.'),
('2a257bf8-8154-58b6-ae68-6d2493db6021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Phrenic nerve is formed from:'' is:', '["C5-C7","C1-C4","C3","C4","C5 C2-C4 C4-C6"]'::jsonb, 'C3', 'The phrenic nerve receives fibers from the C3, C4, and C5 roots, with C4 contributing the major portion. It descends over the anterior scalene muscle to supply the diaphragm.'),
('406f30c4-b1fb-55de-b294-d649eafefedc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: phrenic nerve lies on which muscle?', '["Posterior scalene","SCM","Middle scalene","Anterior scalene","Levator scapulae"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior scalene', 'The phrenic nerve descends obliquely over the anterior surface of the anterior scalene muscle, deep to the prevertebral fascia. The brachial plexus roots emerge between the anterior and middle scalene muscles.'),
('71e3313a-a2c7-5c11-ba45-86388394e883'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: subclavian artery is divided into how many parts by scalenus anterior?', '["4 parts","5 parts","1 part","2 parts","3 parts"]'::jsonb, '3 parts', 'The scalenus anterior divides the subclavian artery into three parts: medial, behind, and lateral to the muscle. The third part lies in the posterior triangle and continues as the axillary artery beyond the outer border of the first rib.'),
('060c0f44-27dc-551a-bfce-003a498bc9b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: third part of subclavian artery is located:?', '["Medial to scalene resulting in decreased functional activity","At axillary artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Behind scalene mediated by specific receptor activation","After crossing first rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral to anterior scalene in posterior triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to anterior scalene in posterior triangle', 'The third part of the subclavian artery lies lateral to the scalenus anterior within the posterior triangle. It becomes the axillary artery only after crossing the outer border of the first rib.'),
('5b611f41-8964-5242-ae67-561f7f1d752c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: which artery passes through the posterior triangle?', '["Facial artery","Suprascapular artery","Superior thyroid","Lingual artery","Ascending pharyngeal"]'::jsonb, 'Suprascapular artery', 'The suprascapular artery, a branch of the thyrocervical trunk, crosses the lower part of the posterior triangle toward the scapula. The facial, lingual, superior thyroid, and ascending pharyngeal arteries supply structures of the anterior neck.'),
('166a7157-295e-5f17-b653-622d48143c07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: transverse cervical artery passes through:?', '["Posterior triangle","Anterior triangle","Carotid triangle","Muscular triangle","Submandibular triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior triangle', 'The transverse cervical artery runs laterally across the posterior triangle above the brachial plexus to supply muscles of the shoulder region. The carotid, muscular, and submandibular triangles lie in the anterior part of the neck.'),
('49ddc308-01cf-5f5d-8a7d-9346abd19690'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Lesser occipital nerve is derived from:'' is:', '["C3","Facial nerve","Cervical plexus (C2)","C4","Trigeminal"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical plexus (C2)', 'The lesser occipital nerve arises from the C2 root of the cervical plexus and ascends along the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid to supply the scalp behind the ear. The facial and trigeminal nerves are cranial nerves unrelated to this branch.'),
('9035b8b8-b02b-5b76-9d8a-aa2043f46254'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: anterior triangle is subdivided into:?', '["Carotid","submandibular","submental","muscular triangles","Carotid subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'submental', 'The anterior triangle is subdivided into the submental, submandibular (digastric), carotid, and muscular triangles. The submental triangle lies between the two anterior bellies of the digastric and the body of the hyoid bone.'),
('a71720ac-e1c5-586d-a440-a6df1aca2552'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: submandibular triangle boundaries:?', '["Mandible","platysma mylohyoid","Mandible mylohyoid","hyoglossus Posterior digastric","stylohyoid mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Mandible', 'The submandibular triangle is bounded superiorly by the inferior border of the mandible and inferiorly by the anterior and posterior bellies of the digastric muscle. It contains the submandibular gland, facial artery, and hypoglossal nerve.'),
('4df76ea3-7c87-528b-94f7-4b050c25ef62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: contents of submandibular triangle include:?', '["Sublingual gland resulting in decreased functional activity","lingual artery Thyroid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","larynx trachea Parotid gland facial nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","external carotid Carotid IJV vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Submandibular gland facial artery submandibular lymph nodes hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Submandibular gland facial artery submandibular lymph nodes hypoglossal nerve', 'The submandibular triangle contains the submandibular gland and its lymph nodes, the facial artery and vein, the submental artery, and the hypoglossal and mylohyoid nerves. The parotid, thyroid, and sublingual glands and the carotid contents lie outside this triangle.'),
('c770d75a-7a71-5c53-9adf-f20b3ea70e98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: floor of submandibular triangle is:?', '["Platysma and skin","Digastric and stylohyoid","Mylohyoid","hyoglossus","middle constrictor Longus colli and scalenes Prevertebral muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Mylohyoid', 'The floor of the submandibular triangle is formed by the mylohyoid, with the hyoglossus contributing deeper in its posterior part. The digastric and stylohyoid form the boundaries of the triangle rather than its floor.'),
('06177f31-da86-5c19-9609-d1b6c6d1ce89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: submental triangle boundaries:?', '["Two digastric bellies and SCM","Anterior bellies of digastric and hyoid bone","Mandible and posterior digastric","Mandible and midline","Mylohyoid and hyoglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior bellies of digastric and hyoid bone', 'The submental triangle is bounded by the two anterior bellies of the digastric muscle and the body of the hyoid bone. Its floor is the mylohyoid, and it contains submental lymph nodes and small veins.'),
('833adf96-a25f-52ea-8f66-521810a47af8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Submental triangle contains:'' is:', '["Parotid gland","Facial artery","Submental lymph nodes and veins","Sublingual gland","Submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Submental lymph nodes and veins', 'The submental triangle contains the submental lymph nodes and small veins, with the mylohyoid forming its floor. The submandibular gland and facial artery belong to the submandibular triangle, and the parotid and sublingual glands lie elsewhere.'),
('c28bffe8-45ec-5e9d-bbb4-c64102c3c724'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: carotid triangle boundaries:?', '["SCM","trapezius clavicle","SCM sternohyoid","omohyoid Mandible","digastric hyoid"]'::jsonb, 'SCM', 'The carotid triangle is bounded by the posterior belly of the digastric, the superior belly of the omohyoid, and the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. The SCM is one of its boundaries, and it contains the carotid sheath and the common carotid bifurcation.'),
('cb74174a-8af9-5b7b-bf75-70a33ea08cba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: carotid triangle contains:?', '["Common carotid bifurcation","IJV","vagus nerve","Thyroid gland","Parotid gland Sublingual gland Submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid bifurcation', 'The carotid triangle contains the common carotid artery with its bifurcation into internal and external carotid arteries, the internal jugular vein, and the vagus nerve. The thyroid gland lies in the muscular triangle, and the salivary glands lie in other triangles.'),
('b84794e9-b18f-501b-980b-cecd88d68bea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: carotid bifurcation occurs at level of:?', '["Hyoid (C3)","Sternoclavicular joint (T2)","Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)","Cricoid (C6)","Angle of mandible (C2)"]'::jsonb, 'Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)', 'The common carotid artery bifurcates into internal and external branches at the level of the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, corresponding to the C4 vertebra. The hyoid lies at C3 and the cricoid at C6.'),
('245a951b-24f2-58a7-a741-a2feb0b5d852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: nerve closely related to carotid bifurcation:?', '["Hypoglossal nerve (XII)","Facial (VII)","Recurrent laryngeal","Spinal accessory (XI)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve (XII)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) crosses superficial to the internal and external carotid arteries just above the bifurcation, running deep to the posterior belly of the digastric. The glossopharyngeal nerve lies deeper and the recurrent laryngeal nerve is confined to the lower neck.'),
('0470fa47-a6a1-5580-bc94-81447a70748f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: carotid body is located:?', '["In thyroid gland","In wall of external carotid","At the bifurcation of common carotid","In wall of internal carotid","In submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'At the bifurcation of common carotid', 'The carotid body is a chemoreceptor embedded in the adventitia at the bifurcation of the common carotid artery, monitoring blood oxygen, carbon dioxide, and pH. It is innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve.'),
('156bff91-603e-5e6c-80aa-25dabb97fce5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Carotid sinus is located:'' is:', '["At the bifurcation resulting in decreased functional activity","In common carotid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At aortic arch mediated by specific receptor activation","At origin of external carotid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the root of internal carotid (baroreceptor)"]'::jsonb, 'At the root of internal carotid (baroreceptor)', 'The carotid sinus is a baroreceptor dilation at the root of the internal carotid artery, just beyond the common carotid bifurcation. It is innervated by the nerve of Hering from the glossopharyngeal nerve; the aortic arch baroreceptors are innervated by the vagus.'),
('33e99014-2a10-512c-8910-925299dc20e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: carotid sinus is innervated by:?', '["Glossopharyngeal (nerve of Hering)","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Vagus nerve","Sympathetic trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (nerve of Hering)', 'The carotid sinus receives the sinus nerve of Hering from the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), which conducts baroreceptor impulses to the medulla. The carotid body is likewise innervated by CN IX rather than the vagus.'),
('4a549e6d-efe2-5fc7-8423-b8fcc70cc127'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: external carotid artery has how many branches in neck?', '["9 branches","8 branches","6 branches","7 branches","5 branches"]'::jsonb, '8 branches', 'The external carotid artery gives eight branches in the neck: superior thyroid, lingual, facial, ascending pharyngeal, occipital, and posterior auricular arteries, ending in the maxillary and superficial temporal arteries. All arise in the carotid triangle except the terminal two branches.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('cc45af57-44d4-5fad-91c5-3b6b0ec17a36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: superior thyroid artery is first branch of:?', '["Subclavian","Facial artery","Lingual artery","External carotid artery","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'External carotid artery', 'The superior thyroid artery is the first branch given off by the external carotid artery, arising just below the greater cornu of the hyoid bone. The subclavian, facial, lingual, and internal carotid arteries do not give rise to it.'),
('778ce595-544e-5e49-ba14-da98b48f342c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: lingual artery arises at level of:?', '["Upper border of thyroid cartilage","SCM","Cricoid cartilage","Angle of mandible","Greater cornu of hyoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Greater cornu of hyoid bone', 'The lingual artery arises from the external carotid artery at the level of the greater cornu of the hyoid bone, above the thyroid cartilage. It passes deep to hyoglossus to reach the tongue.'),
('357c245c-05df-5286-9b96-2c6adfb810b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: facial artery arises from external carotid:?', '["Same level as lingual","Above the lingual artery","Above posterior auricular","At bifurcation","Below the lingual"]'::jsonb, 'Above the lingual artery', 'The facial artery branches from the external carotid artery just above the origin of the lingual artery and then hooks around the mandible at its anterior border. It does not arise at the bifurcation or below the lingual artery.'),
('be59eeba-252b-519e-8e36-4a29eb3d1aa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Occipital artery arises from external carotid:'' is:', '["From posterior aspect","Below superior thyroid","Above lingual","At bifurcation","Opposite the facial artery"]'::jsonb, 'Opposite the facial artery', 'The occipital artery arises from the posterior aspect of the external carotid artery at roughly the same level as the facial artery (opposite it) and runs posteriorly to the occipital region. It does not arise at the bifurcation or below the superior thyroid artery.'),
('d547a453-237a-5c23-9a83-4a56b49dffec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: internal carotid artery in neck has:?', '["Posterior communicating","Several muscular branches","One branch (caroticotympanic)","No branches in the neck","Ophthalmic artery"]'::jsonb, 'No branches in the neck', 'The internal carotid artery gives off no branches in the neck; all its branches arise intracranially. The ophthalmic and posterior communicating arteries are intracranial, and the caroticotympanic branch arises within the petrous temporal bone.'),
('0c11c063-4c5c-5ddc-93fb-c26511c9bfdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: muscular triangle boundaries:?', '["SCM","trapezius clavicle","SCM superior omohyoid","sternohyoid","Omohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'SCM superior omohyoid', 'The muscular triangle is bounded by the anterior median line of the neck, the superior belly of the omohyoid, and the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. Sternohyoid lies within the triangle but is not one of its boundaries.'),
('dddd012b-7e73-55c3-8e03-ef4936505b2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: muscular triangle contains:?', '["Carotid sheath","Thyroid cartilage","Infrahyoid strap muscles","Parotid gland","Submandibular gland"]'::jsonb, 'Infrahyoid strap muscles', 'The muscular triangle contains the infrahyoid strap muscles (sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thyrohyoid, and omohyoid). The carotid sheath, thyroid cartilage, parotid gland, and submandibular gland lie outside this small triangle.'),
('21a9d004-af84-5070-8746-ad939de87d09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: ansa cervicalis is located:?', '["In the carotid triangle on the carotid sheath","In muscular triangle","In submandibular triangle","In posterior triangle","In submental triangle"]'::jsonb, 'In the carotid triangle on the carotid sheath', 'The ansa cervicalis lies in the carotid triangle, on the front of the carotid sheath. It is not located in the muscular, submandibular, posterior, or submental triangles.'),
('fa2c5c7d-693b-5d9b-9b4c-a53a6e49887a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: ansa cervicalis is formed by:?', '["Hypoglossal only","C1-C3 fibers from cervical plexus","C1-C4 fibers","C5-C8 fibers","C3-C5 fibers"]'::jsonb, 'C1-C3 fibers from cervical plexus', 'The ansa cervicalis is formed by anterior ramus fibers from C1-C3, arranged into a superior root (C1, C2) and an inferior root (C3). It does not draw fibers from C4, C5-C8, or from the hypoglossal nucleus.'),
('9d89f721-1058-5dc7-8490-c4968b8ff1c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Ansa cervicalis supplies:'' is:', '["Scalene muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","SCM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Suprahyoid muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Trapezius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infrahyoid muscles except thyrohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Infrahyoid muscles except thyrohyoid', 'The ansa cervicalis supplies the infrahyoid muscles sternohyoid, sternothyroid, and both bellies of the omohyoid, but not the thyrohyoid (which receives C1 via the hypoglossal nerve). Scalene, sternocleidomastoid, trapezius, and suprahyoid muscles have other innervation.'),
('4a60214d-2312-5d91-89c6-6624f3492431'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: thyrohyoid muscle is supplied by:?', '["Recurrent laryngeal","Phrenic nerve","C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve","External laryngeal","Ansa cervicalis"]'::jsonb, 'C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve', 'Thyrohyoid receives its supply from C1 fibers that travel briefly with the hypoglossal nerve and then pass to the muscle. It is not supplied by the ansa cervicalis, recurrent laryngeal, external laryngeal, or phrenic nerve.'),
('8bcdddf1-2447-5118-ba14-bb788f64f078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: geniohyoid muscle is supplied by:?', '["C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Hypoglossal proper","Trigeminal","Ansa cervicalis"]'::jsonb, 'C1 fibers via hypoglossal nerve', 'Geniohyoid is supplied by C1 fibers carried along the hypoglossal nerve. The facial, trigeminal, and ansa cervicalis nerves do not supply this muscle directly.'),
('8c278e77-f2c5-5522-8a03-7c6cbc5fcaeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: posterior belly of digastric is supplied by:?', '["Trigeminal (V3)","Glossopharyngeal (IX)","Hypoglossal (XII)","Facial nerve (VII)","Vagus (X)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (VII)', 'The posterior belly of the digastric is supplied by the facial nerve (CN VII). The anterior belly receives V3 via the mylohyoid nerve, and IX, X, and XII supply other head and neck muscles.'),
('ae8d338a-f7f7-520c-b4ad-7cca6a2dbdb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: anterior belly of digastric is supplied by:?', '["Trigeminal (V3 via nerve to mylohyoid)","Vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglossal mediated by specific receptor activation","Glossopharyngeal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal (V3 via nerve to mylohyoid)', 'The anterior belly of the digastric is innervated by the mylohyoid nerve, a branch of the inferior alveolar nerve from the mandibular division of the trigeminal (V3). The facial, vagus, hypoglossal, and glossopharyngeal nerves do not supply it.'),
('deb5364b-e2c6-51ab-80ce-4c8c2f2b142b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: posterior triangle is subdivided by omohyoid into:?', '["Occipital and supraclavicular triangles","Suprahyoid and infrahyoid","Submandibular and submental","Digastric and carotid","Carotid and muscular"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital and supraclavicular triangles', 'The inferior belly of the omohyoid divides the posterior triangle into an upper occipital triangle and a lower supraclavicular (omoclavicular) triangle. The other pairs listed are subdivisions or groupings of the anterior triangle.'),
('6695780a-9c4f-58e2-a873-f8a24b6e9d84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Occipital triangle contains:'' is:', '["Common carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","External carotid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory nerve and cervical plexus branches","Thyroid gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subclavian artery only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve and cervical plexus branches', 'The occipital triangle contains the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), branches of the cervical plexus, and lymph nodes. The common and external carotid arteries, thyroid gland, and subclavian artery lie elsewhere in the neck.'),
('7bcbb3a6-5e13-547a-8b70-0dbfe6eb8695'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: supraclavicular triangle contains:?', '["Parotid gland","Submandibular gland","Carotid bifurcation","Third part subclavian artery","brachial plexus trunks Thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Third part subclavian artery', 'The supraclavicular (omoclavicular) triangle contains the third part of the subclavian artery, part of the brachial plexus trunks, and supraclavicular lymph nodes. The parotid and submandibular glands and the carotid bifurcation are not located here.'),
('20f29140-38dd-5469-9f11-f40c304193f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: scm muscle is supplied by:?', '["Facial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglossal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C2-C3 alone mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XI alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C2-C3"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C2-C3', 'The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with sensory/proprioceptive fibers from C2-C3. Supply is neither from C2-C3 alone nor CN XI alone, and the facial and hypoglossal nerves do not reach it.'),
('984af16f-8aa5-5f58-8918-1c7d78e392be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: trapezius is supplied by:?', '["Hypoglossal resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C3-C4","CN XI alone mediated by specific receptor activation","C3-C4 alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory (CN XI) and C3-C4', 'The trapezius is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) with proprioceptive contributions from C3-C4. Neither CN XI alone nor C3-C4 alone provides the complete supply, and the facial and hypoglossal nerves have no role here.'),
('b069cc45-c5a1-5ac4-a2a7-f3af15dc7f96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: injury to spinal accessory nerve causes:?', '["Weakness in shoulder shrugging and head rotation","Hoarseness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tongue deviation mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial paralysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dysphagia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weakness in shoulder shrugging and head rotation', 'Injury to the spinal accessory nerve (for example during node biopsy in the posterior triangle) weakens the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius, impairing shoulder shrugging and head rotation. Hoarseness, tongue deviation, facial paralysis, and dysphagia reflect damage to other nerves.'),
('2cfb8657-611f-53ff-b361-3d778c651a09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: cervical plexus is formed by:?', '["Posterior C1-C5","Posterior rami of C1-C4","Anterior C1-C5","Anterior rami of C1-C4","Anterior rami of C5-C8"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of C1-C4', 'The cervical plexus is formed by the anterior rami of C1-C4. It does not involve the posterior rami or fibers from C5-C8.'),
('e9e78b87-11d6-53bf-b722-8418a0097373'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Cervical plexus branches include:'' is:', '["Facial","vagus hypoglossal Accessory hypoglossal","phrenic","Trigeminal facial glossopharyngeal","Great auricular lesser occipital transverse cervical supraclavicular"]'::jsonb, 'phrenic', 'The phrenic nerve (C3-C5) is a branch of the cervical plexus that supplies the diaphragm. The other nerves listed do not arise from the cervical plexus, whereas fibers such as the great auricular, transverse cervical, and supraclavicular are its cutaneous branches.'),
('baec8791-ecf1-5ba7-81d9-8a9b3204db4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: prevertebral fascia covers:?', '["SCM and trapezius","Masseter and temporalis","Scalenes","longus colli","longus capitis Infrahyoid muscles Digastric and mylohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenes', 'The prevertebral fascia covers the prevertebral and scalene muscles (scalenes, longus colli, and longus capitis). The sternocleidomastoid and trapezius are covered by the investing fascia rather than the prevertebral fascia.'),
('c3ebb6f2-becc-5438-9741-e9bec67d3c98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: prevertebral fascia extends from:?', '["Skull base to coccyx","Mandible to sternum","Mastoid to clavicle","Skull base to T3 vertebra","Hyoid to clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Skull base to T3 vertebra', 'The prevertebral fascia extends from the base of the skull to approximately the third thoracic vertebra (T3). It does not continue to the coccyx, sternum, clavicle, or hyoid bone.'),
('82b0a189-4f8f-553f-9ab4-a19f0cf45c02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: retropharyngeal space is between:?', '["Investing and pretracheal fascia","Layers of pretracheal fascia","Prevertebral fascia and buccopharyngeal fascia","Skin and investing fascia","Midline of neck"]'::jsonb, 'Prevertebral fascia and buccopharyngeal fascia', 'The retropharyngeal space lies between the buccopharyngeal fascia anteriorly and the prevertebral fascia posteriorly. Spaces bounded by the investing or pretracheal fascias, skin, or the midline do not form it.'),
('34ed3f60-993e-5418-8c1f-ea040f4a91ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: carotid sheath is formed by:?', '["Only investing layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Investing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","prevertebral mediated by specific receptor activation","and pretracheal layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All three equally Only pretracheal Only prevertebral"]'::jsonb, 'All three equally Only pretracheal Only prevertebral', 'The carotid sheath is a tubular condensation of the deep cervical fascia receiving contributions from the investing, pretracheal, and prevertebral layers, and it encloses the common/internal carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve. It is not formed by any single layer alone.'),
('5cb4f544-6716-5714-80e0-54bbce2db668'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: carotid sheath contents:?', '["Common carotid","external carotid","hypoglossal","Common carotid subclavian recurrent laryngeal","Subclavian artery vein phrenic Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid', 'The carotid sheath contains the common carotid artery (and above the bifurcation the internal carotid artery), the internal jugular vein, and the vagus nerve. The external carotid artery, subclavian vessels, and phrenic nerve are not sheath contents.'),
('d7140307-c782-58b0-8551-b51035f2f607'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Vagus nerve in carotid sheath lies:'' is:', '["Lateral to vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial to artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to both mediated by specific receptor activation","Between and posterior to carotid artery and IJV","Posterior to both leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between and posterior to carotid artery and IJV', 'Within the carotid sheath the vagus nerve lies between and slightly posterior to the internal jugular vein and the common carotid artery. It is not lateral, medial, anterior, or directly posterior to the vessels.'),
('e12a43e7-573e-5230-9bda-224b043effc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: internal jugular vein lies:?', '["Lateral to common carotid artery","Medial to common carotid","Posterior to vagus","Anterior to common carotid","Posterior to vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to common carotid artery', 'The internal jugular vein lies lateral to the common carotid artery inside the carotid sheath, with the vagus nerve placed between and behind them. It is neither medial, anterior, nor posterior to the artery.'),
('f422edd4-88ac-510a-81d3-9edb95934ed4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: right common carotid is a branch of:?', '["Brachiocephalic trunk","Aortic arch directly","External carotid","Internal carotid","Subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk', 'The right common carotid artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk behind the right sternoclavicular joint. The left common carotid arises directly from the aortic arch.'),
('1cf523c2-ca2c-5113-96cc-ad8ca41c4c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: left common carotid is a branch of:?', '["Left subclavian","Thoracic aorta","Brachiocephalic trunk","Aortic arch directly","Innominate"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch directly', 'The left common carotid artery arises directly from the aortic arch, medial to the left subclavian artery. It does not arise from the left subclavian, thoracic aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, or innominate artery.'),
('93cbb2e0-a612-5198-8e9b-a8d0fbe79d25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: internal carotid enters skull through:?', '["Foramen magnum","Foramen ovale","Carotid canal in petrous temporal","Foramen lacerum","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid canal in petrous temporal', 'The internal carotid artery enters the cranium through the carotid canal in the petrous part of the temporal bone. The foramen magnum, foramen ovale, foramen lacerum, and jugular foramen transmit other structures.'),
('3f5ca455-751f-5b09-9522-1c2a8eb3d391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: hypoglossal nerve in neck:?', '["Passes posterior to carotid sheath","Passes anterior to hyoid","Passes through stylomastoid foramen","Passes between internal carotid and internal jugular vein","Passes with vagus"]'::jsonb, 'Passes between internal carotid and internal jugular vein', 'In the neck the hypoglossal nerve passes deep to the posterior belly of the digastric, between the internal carotid artery and the internal jugular vein. It does not travel with the vagus or pass through the stylomastoid foramen.'),
('d710f19e-45bf-574a-8046-fdbf000bbd7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Hypoglossal nerve supplies:'' is:', '["Muscles of soft palate","Muscles of face","Muscles of pharynx","All tongue muscles except palatoglossus","Muscles of larynx"]'::jsonb, 'All tongue muscles except palatoglossus', 'The hypoglossal nerve supplies all the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue except palatoglossus, which is innervated from the pharyngeal plexus. It does not supply the facial, palatal, pharyngeal, or laryngeal muscles.'),
('62e905f8-912f-586c-a820-7ec41c084bc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: the hypoglossal nerve (xii) also supplies:?', '["Laryngeal muscles","Suprahyoid muscles","Thyrohyoid and geniohyoid (via C1 fibers)","Infrahyoid muscles","Pharyngeal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrohyoid and geniohyoid (via C1 fibers)', 'Besides the tongue muscles, the hypoglossal nerve carries C1 fibers that are distributed to the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles. It does not directly supply the laryngeal, pharyngeal, or other infrahyoid strap muscles.'),
('948eebaf-5518-5d39-81e3-7ea424018b59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: digastric muscle has how many bellies?', '["Four resulting in decreased functional activity","Three accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple mediated by specific receptor activation","Two (anterior and posterior)","One leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two (anterior and posterior)', 'The digastric muscle has two bellies, anterior and posterior, joined by an intermediate tendon held by a fascial sling attached to the hyoid bone. It does not have one, three, four, or multiple bellies.'),
('9d41d6a9-351f-567e-b4d2-394a9d211279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: intermediate tendon of digastric is attached to:?', '["Styloid process resulting in decreased functional activity","Skull base accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandible mediated by specific receptor activation","Mylohyoid line without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyoid bone (greater cornu) by fibrous sling"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid bone (greater cornu) by fibrous sling', 'The intermediate tendon of the digastric is connected to the hyoid bone near the body and greater cornu by a fibrous sling. The styloid process, skull base, mandible, and mylohyoid line are attachments of other structures.'),
('334bca9d-d928-5f3a-a5dc-e0517eb0ca35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: strap muscles (infrahyoid) include:?', '["Digastric","stylohyoid","mylohyoid Scalenes levator scapulae","SCM trapezius Longus colli capitis","Sternohyoid sternothyroid thyrohyoid omohyoid"]'::jsonb, 'Sternohyoid sternothyroid thyrohyoid omohyoid', 'The infrahyoid strap muscles are the sternohyoid, sternothyroid, thyrohyoid, and omohyoid. Digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, and geniohyoid are suprahyoid muscles, and scalenes, levator scapulae, sternocleidomastoid, and trapezius are not strap muscles.'),
('4eb93018-bbea-54b7-a1fb-eab08f04e0e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: suprahyoid muscles include:?', '["Scalenes","levator scapulae","Sternohyoid omohyoid sternothyroid","Digastric stylohyoid mylohyoid geniohyoid","SCM trapezius Longus colli capitis"]'::jsonb, 'Digastric stylohyoid mylohyoid geniohyoid', 'The suprahyoid muscles are the digastric, stylohyoid, mylohyoid, and geniohyoid. Sternohyoid, omohyoid, and sternothyroid are infrahyoid, and scalenes, levator scapulae, sternocleidomastoid, and trapezius do not belong to this group.'),
('9f9034cb-61f5-5858-9300-82871d15fd1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Infrahyoid muscles function:'' is:', '["Depress hyoid bone and larynx","Rotate neck","Tilt head","Elevate hyoid and larynx","Elevate mandible"]'::jsonb, 'Depress hyoid bone and larynx', 'The infrahyoid muscles depress the hyoid bone and the larynx after swallowing and during phonation. The suprahyoid muscles perform the opposite action of elevating the hyoid, and the mandible is elevated by the muscles of mastication.'),
('55a6118a-8dcf-5a80-b8b6-a02d4412a549'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: suprahyoid muscles function:?', '["Elevate hyoid","floor of mouth","depress mandible","Depress hyoid","Elevate mandible only Rotate head Depress mandible only"]'::jsonb, 'Elevate hyoid', 'The primary function of the suprahyoid muscles is to elevate the hyoid bone and, with it, the floor of the mouth. They do not depress the hyoid; infrahyoid muscles depress it and the larynx.'),
('3755309e-31dd-5428-9cdd-9702b524d9b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: deep cervical fascia layers from superficial to deep:?', '["Prevertebral","investing","pretracheal","Investing pretracheal","prevertebral Pretracheal"]'::jsonb, 'Investing pretracheal', 'From superficial to deep, the layers of deep cervical fascia are the investing, the pretracheal (anterior visceral), and the prevertebral layer. Since the investing layer is most superficial, it is listed first, followed by the pretracheal layer, and the prevertebral layer is deepest.'),
('6ec0b4e3-9b22-5108-9c3a-e88ced79418c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: investing layer of deep cervical fascia surrounds:?', '["The entire neck like a collar","Only vertebral column","Only trachea and esophagus","Only carotid sheath","Only thyroid"]'::jsonb, 'The entire neck like a collar', 'The investing layer of the deep cervical fascia surrounds the whole neck like a collar, splitting to enclose the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. It does not invest the vertebral column, trachea and esophagus, carotid sheath, or thyroid gland alone.'),
('2dd26adf-ed30-54eb-bcf5-0ae55125875d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: pretracheal fascia encloses:?', '["Thyroid gland","trachea","esophagus","Scalene muscles","Carotid jugular Vertebral muscles SCM"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid gland', 'The pretracheal (visceral) fascia encloses the thyroid gland as well as the trachea and esophagus. The scalene muscles and the carotid-jugular vascular bundle are covered by other layers of deep cervical fascia.'),
('c0739c02-36bc-54c2-8a24-da2a04be737c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which answer is correct in Triangles of the neck when asked: pretracheal fascia is limited superiorly to:?', '["Thyroid cartilage","Hyoid bone","Mandible","Skull base","Cricoid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 'Hyoid bone', 'The pretracheal fascia is limited superiorly at the hyoid bone. It does not extend up to the mandible, skull base, or beyond the hyoid over the thyroid and cricoid cartilages.'),
('c3313995-f059-5f90-afee-99ab6b3535da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, the best response to ''Pretracheal fascia extends into thorax to:'' is:', '["Fibrous pericardium","Diaphragm","Carina","T3 vertebra","Aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous pericardium', 'In the thorax the pretracheal fascia blends with the fibrous pericardium. It does not attach to the diaphragm, carina, T3 vertebra, or aortic arch.'),
('dac0df39-3447-5e07-b5ab-44f6d42b610d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During revision of Triangles of the neck, which of the following is correct: prevertebral fascia extends laterally as:?', '["Pretracheal fascia","Buccopharyngeal fascia","Carotid sheath","Investing fascia","Axillary sheath"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary sheath', 'Laterally, the prevertebral fascia is prolonged over the subclavian vessels and brachial plexus as the axillary sheath. The pretracheal, buccopharyngeal, carotid sheath, and investing fascia are distinct layers.'),
('cbfc54df-7ba9-5a9e-bfa0-a85e912e2f4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In Triangles of the neck, which statement best fits the following fact: axillary sheath contains:?', '["Brachial plexus and axillary artery","Subclavian vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","IJV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brachial plexus and axillary artery', 'The axillary sheath, derived from the prevertebral fascia, encloses the brachial plexus and the axillary artery. The subclavian vein, phrenic nerve, and internal jugular vein lie outside this sheath.'),
('1fc428d7-74ac-5a6c-a531-e964b7970b4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Regarding Triangles of the neck, what is the correct answer to: buccopharyngeal fascia covers:?', '["Temporalis resulting in decreased functional activity","Mylohyoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Masseter mediated by specific receptor activation","Parotid gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Buccinator muscle and pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator muscle and pharynx', 'The buccopharyngeal fascia covers the buccinator muscle and the outer surface of the pharyngeal constrictors. The temporalis, mylohyoid, masseter, and parotid gland are not covered by this fascia.'),
('5914bfca-6c34-54d6-8f00-15f9689ee7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'For Triangles of the neck, which option is most appropriate: retropharyngeal space extends from:?', '["Skull base to diaphragm","Skull base to T1-T2","C1 to C7","Hyoid to clavicle","Mandible to mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'Skull base to T1-T2', 'The retropharyngeal space extends from the base of the skull down to about T1-T2, where the buccopharyngeal and prevertebral fascias fuse. It does not reach the diaphragm or the clavicle.'),
('69a2b593-04d8-5473-94ac-0d6a63978c32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The vertebral artery arises from which of the following vessels?', '["Aortic arch","External carotid","Brachiocephalic","Internal carotid","Subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery', 'The vertebral artery is the first branch of the first part of the subclavian artery; it ascends through the transverse foramina of C6 to C1 to reach the foramen magnum. The aortic arch, brachiocephalic trunk, and the carotid arteries give rise to other vessels, not to the vertebral artery.'),
('e5ee0521-0d08-5d87-8960-cc50e2a4f1e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The vertebral artery enters the transverse foramen of which vertebra?', '["C7","C1","T1","C2","C6"]'::jsonb, 'C6', 'The vertebral artery enters the transverse foramen of the C6 vertebra and then runs upward through the foramina of C1 to C5 before entering the skull. It does not use the foramen at C7 or T1.'),
('5bfad36b-6531-5384-bbea-0eaf03ed89f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The thoracic duct drains into which of the following?', '["SVC resulting in decreased functional activity","Right subclavian vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right IJV mediated by specific receptor activation","Left brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left subclavian vein at junction with IJV (left venous angle)"]'::jsonb, 'Left subclavian vein at junction with IJV (left venous angle)', 'The thoracic duct arches into the root of the neck behind the carotid sheath and terminates in the left venous angle, the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The superior vena cava, the right-sided veins, and the left brachiocephalic vein are not the site of termination.'),
('c853150f-5398-5f84-8bbe-9a9f145c8093'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The right lymphatic duct drains into which of the following?', '["Right external jugular resulting in decreased functional activity","Right venous angle (subclavian + IJV junction)","SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Right brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left subclavian leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right venous angle (subclavian + IJV junction)', 'The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right side of the head, neck, and right upper limb into the right venous angle, formed by the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins. None of the other listed veins receives the right lymphatic duct.'),
('b03161c2-3927-59e0-b9bd-b87bad2605bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'At which level does the thoracic duct cross the neck?', '["T1 in front of subclavian","C7 level","behind the carotid sheath","C4 level in front of sheath","C6 in front of scalene C2 behind SCM"]'::jsonb, 'C7 level', 'The thoracic duct rises into the neck about 3 to 5 cm above the clavicle, reaching the level of C7 behind the carotid sheath before turning down into the left venous angle. C7, not T1 or C4, is therefore the level at which it crosses the neck.'),
('05112748-6f5b-5b3b-a857-2f851e534f17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Injury to the thoracic duct during neck surgery most directly causes which of the following?', '["Bleeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Air embolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chyle leak (milky lymph containing fat)"]'::jsonb, 'Chyle leak (milky lymph containing fat)', 'The thoracic duct carries chyle, the milky lymph rich in absorbed dietary fat, so injury produces a chylous leak with fat-laden fluid. Bleeding, nerve injury, air embolism, and infection do not arise directly from thoracic duct trauma.'),
('03ff05ac-0d2e-5a69-a0a5-928b63561d02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Where does the cervical sympathetic chain lie?', '["Anterior to sheath","In pretracheal fascia","In posterior triangle","Behind carotid sheath on prevertebral fascia","In the carotid sheath"]'::jsonb, 'Behind carotid sheath on prevertebral fascia', 'The cervical sympathetic trunk descends on the prevertebral fascia behind the carotid sheath. It does not lie inside the sheath, within the pretracheal fascia, or in the posterior triangle.'),
('ec963027-3ab1-5cf0-b59c-e2625c8bf1b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The superior cervical ganglion is situated at which level?', '["C5-C6","C2-C3 vertebrae","C3-C4","C1-C2","C4-C5"]'::jsonb, 'C2-C3 vertebrae', 'The superior cervical ganglion, the largest cervical ganglion, lies at the level of C2 to C3, near the internal carotid artery at the base of the skull. The lower levels listed belong to the middle (C6) and stellate (C7-T1) cervical ganglia.'),
('7f8a5fee-8cf2-5b5b-a161-ee361f97da5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The middle cervical ganglion lies at which level?', '["C4-C5","C5-C6","C3-C4","C7","C6 vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'C6 vertebra', 'The middle cervical ganglion, which is small and often overlies the inferior thyroid artery, lies at the level of the C6 vertebra. The superior ganglion sits at C2-C3 and the stellate ganglion at C7-T1, so the other levels are wrong.'),
('3fd53577-c1fd-58d9-b0ac-46e206c10a42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The cervicothoracic (stellate) ganglion is situated at which level?', '["C7-T1","C5-C6","C6-C7","T1-T2","C4-C5"]'::jsonb, 'C7-T1', 'The stellate (cervicothoracic) ganglion forms by fusion of the inferior cervical and first thoracic ganglia and lies at the level of C7-T1, in front of the neck of the first rib. The superior and middle ganglia lie higher, at C2-C3 and C6, so C7-T1 is the location of this ganglion.'),
('1638c46c-727f-5258-9e19-59a02cb15dea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Horner''s syndrome results from which of the following?', '["CN XI injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Interruption of cervical sympathetic chain","Phrenic injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic overactivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interruption of cervical sympathetic chain', 'Horner''s syndrome shows ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis on the affected side, caused by interruption of the cervical sympathetic chain, for example by an apical lung tumor. Injury to the spinal accessory, phrenic, or vagus nerves, and parasympathetic overactivity, produce none of this triad.'),
('75cf6d52-da10-55d0-aa9d-5a81d23741cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The thyrocervical trunk is a branch of which vessel?', '["External carotid","Subclavian artery (first part)","Maxillary","Vertebral","Internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (first part)', 'The thyrocervical trunk arises from the first part of the subclavian artery and divides into the inferior thyroid, transverse cervical, and suprascapular arteries. The maxillary artery and the internal and external carotid arteries are branches of other systems.'),
('a85e52c4-a0ab-5e72-996d-2cd501611900'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The inferior thyroid artery is a branch of which vessel?', '["Thyrocervical trunk","Internal carotid","External carotid","Costocervical trunk","Subclavian second part"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk', 'The inferior thyroid artery, the largest branch of the thyrocervical trunk, ascends behind the carotid sheath to the thyroid gland. The costocervical trunk gives deep cervical and supreme intercostal branches, and the carotid arteries give none of this.'),
('7f15695c-f20f-5780-a080-77ce89280644'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The suprascapular artery is a branch of which vessel?', '["Axillary","Subclavian third part","Thyrocervical trunk","Internal carotid","External carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk', 'The suprascapular artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk and passes behind the clavicle into the supraspinous fossa. It does not branch from the axillary artery, the third part of the subclavian, or the carotid arteries.'),
('e8b75514-617f-522f-b266-e1acd8c8e91c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The transverse cervical artery is a branch of which vessel?', '["Subclavian third part","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid","Axillary","Costocervical trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrocervical trunk', 'The transverse cervical artery branches off the thyrocervical trunk and runs across the posterior triangle to the deep surface of trapezius. The costocervical trunk, axillary artery, and external carotid artery are not its source.'),
('be205dd8-90db-5b6f-830b-c5537d628e2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The costocervical trunk arises from which part of which artery?', '["Axillary","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid","Internal carotid","Subclavian artery (second part)"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (second part)', 'The costocervical trunk arises from the second part of the subclavian artery, behind the scalenus anterior, and divides into the deep cervical and supreme intercostal arteries. The thyrocervical trunk comes from the first part of the subclavian, and the carotid arteries are unrelated.'),
('12e7e2a5-1723-5b27-be4a-261a9c9b6208'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The deep cervical artery is a branch of which trunk?', '["Subclavian first part","Thyrocervical trunk","Costocervical trunk","External carotid","Vertebral"]'::jsonb, 'Costocervical trunk', 'The deep cervical artery, the larger branch of the costocervical trunk, ascends the back of the neck to anastomose with the descending branch of the occipital artery. The thyrocervical trunk instead supplies the inferior thyroid, transverse cervical, and suprascapular arteries.'),
('cd3f38c4-3bb6-5f51-a84d-b1c28c3a2cad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The upper border of the thyroid cartilage lies at which vertebral level?', '["C3","C4 vertebra","C5","C7","C6"]'::jsonb, 'C4 vertebra', 'The upper border of the thyroid cartilage and the carotid bifurcation lie at the level of C4. The hyoid bone at C3 above and the cricoid cartilage at C6 below fix the surrounding landmarks, so the other vertebral levels are wrong.'),
('c91ff510-7e06-55a1-8d0c-4080c9ed757d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The cricoid cartilage lies at which vertebral level?', '["C4","C6 vertebra","T1","C5","C7"]'::jsonb, 'C6 vertebra', 'The lower border of the cricoid cartilage lies at the level of the C6 vertebra, marking the junction of the larynx and the trachea. The thyroid cartilage at C4 and the hyoid bone at C3 are progressively higher landmarks.'),
('c53bcb48-3bf8-5aa1-9ae5-47a654abe63b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The hyoid bone lies at which vertebral level?', '["C4","C6","C3 vertebra","C2","C5"]'::jsonb, 'C3 vertebra', 'The hyoid bone lies at the level of the C3 vertebra, at the same horizontal plane as the floor of the mouth. The thyroid cartilage (C4) and the cricoid cartilage (C6) lie lower, so the remaining options are wrong.'),
('0730943b-f532-5f2b-a872-56fe2cee9da9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The common carotid artery bifurcates into the external and internal carotid arteries at which level?', '["Hyoid (C3)","Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)","Angle of mandible (C2)","Cricoid (C6)","Sternoclavicular joint (T2)"]'::jsonb, 'Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4)', 'The common carotid artery divides into the external and internal carotid arteries at the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, the level of C4. The hyoid (C3), cricoid (C6), and sternoclavicular joint (T2) are the levels of other structures.'),
('6e50db94-c044-5714-9158-f2c26f2d34b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The common carotid artery pulse can be felt by compressing the vessel against which structure?', '["Thyroid cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Angle of mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior tubercle of C6 (Chassaignac''s tubercle)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior tubercle of C6 (Chassaignac''s tubercle)', 'The common carotid artery can be compressed against the anterior tubercle of C6, the carotid or Chassaignac''s tubercle, to stop hemorrhage from its branches. The thyroid cartilage, angle of the mandible, trachea, and hyoid bone offer no comparable bony resistance.'),
('b536ce3e-c897-533a-ab6b-6934f9c81764'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The anterior tubercle of C6 is also known as which of the following?', '["Carotid tubercle (Chassaignac''s)","Dens","Transverse process","Odontoid process","Spinous process"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid tubercle (Chassaignac''s)', 'The anterior tubercle of the C6 transverse process is enlarged and is called the carotid tubercle (Chassaignac''s tubercle). The dens (odontoid process) belongs to the axis, and the transverse and spinous processes are not alternative names for this tubercle.'),
('7e6f8b03-7348-5790-ab30-216ca62ee2c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The fascia covering the submandibular gland is derived from which layer?', '["Superficial fascia","Prevertebral fascia","Pretracheal fascia","Investing layer of deep cervical fascia","Buccopharyngeal fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Investing layer of deep cervical fascia', 'The investing layer of the deep cervical fascia splits to enclose the submandibular gland, just as it encloses the parotid gland. The prevertebral, pretracheal, superficial, and buccopharyngeal layers surround deeper structures and do not form the capsule of this gland.'),
('aff44777-ef0a-522e-9473-25d4cecdf24f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The deep part of the submandibular gland lies in which position?', '["Below mylohyoid","In submental triangle","Above mylohyoid","In carotid triangle","Deep to mylohyoid on hyoglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to mylohyoid on hyoglossus', 'The deep part of the submandibular gland hooks around the posterior border of the mylohyoid and lies deep to that muscle, on the surface of the hyoglossus. The superficial part occupies the submandibular triangle, and the gland does not extend into the submental or carotid triangles.'),
('c73a85a5-4c9f-5b59-9d53-f04cca7e5b3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The stylohyoid muscle is pierced by which structure?', '["Lingual nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Intermediate tendon of digastric","Glossopharyngeal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate tendon of digastric', 'Just before it inserts into the hyoid bone, the stylohyoid muscle is pierced by the intermediate tendon of the digastric muscle. The lingual, hypoglossal, facial, and glossopharyngeal nerves pass close by but do not pierce the stylohyoid.'),
('9dd1dafc-0b69-5a81-8616-4f480507f5dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The pterygomandibular raphe separates which two muscles?', '["Palatoglossus and palatopharyngeus","Medial and lateral pterygoid","Mylohyoid and hyoglossus","Masseter and temporalis","Buccinator and superior constrictor"]'::jsonb, 'Buccinator and superior constrictor', 'The pterygomandibular raphe extends from the pterygoid hamulus to the posterior end of the mylohyoid line and separates the buccinator muscle in front from the superior pharyngeal constrictor behind. The other listed muscle pairs are not related to this raphe.'),
('17899fb9-38b1-5def-8fb8-0e0110619ce8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The retromolar triangle is located at which site?', '["In the sublingual region","In the retropharyngeal space","In front of 1st molar","Behind the 3rd molar tooth","Between upper and lower molars"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the 3rd molar tooth', 'The retromolar triangle is the small triangular area on the mandible behind the third molar tooth, serving as an attachment for the buccinator and superior constrictor muscles. It is not in the sublingual region, retropharyngeal space, or interdental region.'),
('6463c86d-4186-57f0-ba6a-d871bd2e9181'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The inferior alveolar nerve enters the mandible through which opening?', '["Mandibular foramen","Mental foramen","Genial tubercle","Mandibular notch","Incisive foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular foramen', 'The inferior alveolar nerve enters the mandibular canal through the mandibular foramen on the medial surface of the ramus, together with the inferior alveolar vessels. The mental foramen is the anterior exit of the canal, and the other named openings transmit unrelated structures.'),
('01bda1e1-6d8b-5939-a24c-1b0694836656'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The mental nerve emerges through which opening?', '["Infraorbital foramen","Mental foramen","Mandibular foramen","Supraorbital notch","Incisive foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Mental foramen', 'The mental nerve, the terminal branch of the inferior alveolar nerve, emerges through the mental foramen below the second premolar. The infraorbital foramen, supraorbital notch, mandibular foramen, and incisive foramen belong to other neural structures.'),
('dbbd69ed-87a1-506c-a255-d2451852ff9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'In the floor of the mouth, the lingual nerve lies in which position?', '["Deep to hyoglossus","above submandibular duct","Below mylohyoid","In the carotid triangle","In the submental triangle Superficial to hyoglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Deep to hyoglossus', 'In the floor of the mouth, the lingual nerve lies deep to the mylohyoid muscle and close to the hyoglossus, then crosses the submandibular duct to reach the tongue. It does not lie above the duct in the submental triangle or within the carotid triangle.'),
('6e95ff01-133b-5d4e-84f5-a52c6b3d6f8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submandibular duct is also called which duct?', '["Stensen''s duct","Wharton''s duct","Nasolacrimal duct","Rivinus ducts","Bartholin''s duct"]'::jsonb, 'Wharton''s duct', 'The submandibular (Wharton''s) duct runs from the deep surface of the gland to open at the sublingual caruncle beside the lingual frenulum. Stensen''s duct drains the parotid, and the nasolacrimal, Rivinus, and Bartholin ducts are unrelated.'),
('07638092-492f-5ba5-a9e2-eee738af60f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submandibular lymph nodes chiefly drain which of the following areas?', '["Upper lip and cheek","Anterior tongue","floor of mouth lower lip","chin Posterior tongue","Posterior scalp Parotid region"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior tongue', 'The submandibular lymph nodes drain lymph from the lateral floor of the mouth, the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and adjacent regions. The posterior tongue drains chiefly to the upper deep cervical nodes, and the parotid region drains to the parotid nodes.'),
('03354201-2471-5e1d-8d5f-3b21fddb8b70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submental lymph nodes are located where?', '["Deep to SCM","In carotid triangle","In submandibular triangle","Between anterior bellies of digastric","In posterior triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Between anterior bellies of digastric', 'The submental lymph nodes lie within the submental triangle, which is bounded by the two anterior bellies of the digastric muscles and the hyoid bone. They therefore sit between the anterior bellies of digastric, not in the submandibular, carotid, or posterior triangles.'),
('10b31e39-a69c-5088-8ed3-62e105753331'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The submental lymph nodes chiefly drain which of the following areas?', '["Lower lip","chin","tip of tongue floor of mouth","Upper lip and nose Anterior scalp","Posterior neck Parotid region"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lip', 'The submental lymph nodes chiefly drain the central lower lip, the chin, the tip of the tongue, and the anterior floor of the mouth. The upper lip and nose drain to the submandibular and buccal nodes, and the parotid region drains to the parotid nodes.'),
('a7079c1b-981b-5a2d-824a-74c5c47d80ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'During therapeutic management, EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('52680677-9ff9-5b7f-855f-616cd43d9033'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The facial artery crosses the lower border of the mandible at which point?', '["Submandibular triangle","Posterior digastric","Mental foramen","Angle of mandible","Anterior border of masseter (facial notch)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of masseter (facial notch)', 'The facial artery crosses the lower border of the mandible at the anterior border of the masseter, where it can be compressed. The angle of the mandible, mental foramen, submandibular triangle, and posterior digastric border do not mark this crossing.'),
('9105e9e5-33ad-5115-b908-1284bb80246e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The facial artery pulse is best felt at which site?', '["Submandibular triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","Pre-auricular region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temple mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior border of masseter at lower border of mandible","Angle of mandible leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of masseter at lower border of mandible', 'The facial artery pulse is best palpated where the vessel crosses the lower border of the mandible at the anterior border of the masseter. The submandibular triangle, pre-auricular region, temple, and angle of the mandible are not the pulse site.'),
('e6adb2b5-0d1f-57fd-9c68-83b8c3846337'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The facial vein drains into which of the following?', '["Internal jugular via common facial vein","Retromandibular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian mediated by specific receptor activation","Brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External jugular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular via common facial vein', 'The facial vein joins the anterior division of the retromandibular vein to form the common facial vein, which ends in the internal jugular vein. The facial vein thus drains to the internal jugular via the common facial vein, not into the other listed vessels.'),
('570697bf-96f9-54fe-9f05-ba56092465ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which of the following is a content of the carotid triangle?', '["Submandibular gland and hypoglossal nerve Vertebral artery and sympathetic trunk","Cervical plexus and phrenic nerve Thyroid gland and recurrent laryngeal nerve","Common carotid artery","internal carotid artery external carotid artery","internal jugular vein vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Common carotid artery', 'The carotid triangle contains the common carotid artery and its terminal branches along with the internal jugular vein, vagus nerve, and hypoglossal nerve. The submandibular gland belongs to the submandibular triangle, and the vertebral artery and sympathetic trunk lie deeper or behind the sheath.'),
('36b43cee-c153-5625-94c7-e47df2264e50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The neck is divided into triangles by which muscle?', '["Platysma muscle","Trapezius muscle","Digastric muscle","Sternocleidomastoid muscle","Omohyoid muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Sternocleidomastoid muscle', 'The sternocleidomastoid passes obliquely across the side of the neck and divides it into anterior and posterior triangles. The platysma, trapezius, digastric, and omohyoid do not create this division.'),
('241e5fc0-b6f4-55e9-bf43-90bd19d845c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The anterior triangle of the neck is bounded by which of the following?', '["Clavicle and SCM","Mandible and hyoid","Midline and trapezius","SCM and trapezius","Midline"]'::jsonb, 'Midline', 'The anterior triangle is bounded anteriorly by the midline of the neck, superiorly by the lower border of the mandible, and posteriorly by the anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid. Only the midline among these options is a genuine boundary of this triangle, whereas the clavicle and trapezius relate to the posterior triangle.'),
('e567fa14-c1e4-5e8d-a27b-375bf1e718ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The posterior triangle of the neck is bounded by which of the following?', '["SCM","mandible","and midline","Clavicle","B but not A"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle', 'The posterior triangle is bounded anteriorly by the sternocleidomastoid, posteriorly by the trapezius, and inferiorly by the middle third of the clavicle, which forms its base. The clavicle is therefore a genuine boundary of this triangle, while the mandible and midline belong to the anterior triangle.'),
('470fd386-c8f9-551c-a8b5-d5c9b2d1dc89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Triangles of Neck: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('9202e2ed-0104-5a51-94c9-39f3902d199c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The sternocleidomastoid receives its motor innervation from which nerve supply?', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2-3","Trigeminal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve","Facial nerve","Cervical plexus alone"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C2-3', 'The sternocleidomastoid is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), with proprioceptive fibers contributed by the C2 and C3 cervical roots. The facial, trigeminal, and hypoglossal nerves, and the cervical plexus alone, are not its motor supply.'),
('3ec527ce-b395-5413-9a88-4bfea0676fca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The trapezius receives its motor innervation from which nerve supply?', '["Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3-4","Facial nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Cervical plexus only","Hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) and C3-4', 'The trapezius is supplied by the spinal accessory nerve (CN XI), with proprioceptive fibers contributed by the C3 and C4 cervical roots. The facial, trigeminal, and hypoglossal nerves, and the cervical plexus alone, are not its motor supply.'),
('7872127c-62dc-5dcf-adfc-0df8f53734a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which of the following is an infrahyoid strap muscle?', '["Platysma and SCM","Mylohyoid and geniohyoid","Sternohyoid","thyrohyoid","sternothyroid"]'::jsonb, 'Sternohyoid', 'The infrahyoid strap muscles are sternohyoid, omohyoid, sternothyroid, and thyrohyoid, so sternohyoid is a member of this group. Platysma and sternocleidomastoid are not infrahyoid muscles, and mylohyoid and geniohyoid belong to the suprahyoid group.'),
('30f6593d-a21e-5759-9cfa-ea7bc49ff1ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The infrahyoid strap muscles are innervated by which of the following?', '["Hypoglossal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve","Phrenic nerve","Ansa cervicalis (C1-3)"]'::jsonb, 'Ansa cervicalis (C1-3)', 'The infrahyoid muscles (sternohyoid, omohyoid, sternothyroid) are innervated by the ansa cervicalis (C1-C3); thyrohyoid receives C1 fibers carried with the hypoglossal nerve. The phrenic, trigeminal, hypoglossal, and facial nerves are not their supply.'),
('62a53c47-d29e-57b4-a411-b2928a504071'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'Which of the following structures is a known content of, or direct relation to, the carotid sheath?', '["Carotid artery and IJV only","Only the common carotid","Vagus nerve and sympathetic trunk","Common carotid artery","internal jugular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve and sympathetic trunk', 'The carotid sheath encloses the common and internal carotid arteries, the internal jugular vein, and the vagus nerve, and the cervical sympathetic trunk lies closely behind it and is commonly listed as a related content. The choices that list only a single vessel without the vagus and sympathetic trunk are incomplete.'),
('a32e8f93-848c-5cbd-9714-6062addc5b0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Head and Neck - Triangles of Neck', 'The common carotid artery divides at which level?', '["Clavicle (T1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Angle of mandible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cricoid cartilage (C6) mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4 level)","Hyoid bone (C3) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper border of thyroid cartilage (C4 level)', 'The common carotid artery divides into the external and internal carotid arteries at the upper border of the thyroid cartilage, at the C4 level. The hyoid bone (C3), cricoid cartilage (C6), angle of the mandible, and clavicle (T1) mark different levels.'),
('dd72b359-3c3f-52bb-ac5e-054d6e11f0cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Pericardium layers:', '["Fibrous (outer) + serous (parietal + visceral)","Only serous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fibrous mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous + serous + muscular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serous only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous (outer) + serous (parietal + visceral)', 'The pericardium is a double-layered sac: an outer tough fibrous pericardium surrounds an inner serous pericardium whose parietal layer lines the fibrous sac and whose visceral layer (epicardium) covers the heart. It is not purely serous, purely fibrous, or muscular; only the full fibroserous arrangement describes the actual layers.'),
('bca9b9d8-2c21-5ad0-80c3-9fe2656c5cb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Fibrous pericardium attached to:', '["Only heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Only great vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","Only diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central tendon of diaphragm sternum great vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Central tendon of diaphragm sternum great vessels', 'The fibrous pericardium anchors the heart by fusing with the central tendon of the diaphragm inferiorly, with the sternum through short sternopericardial ligaments, and with the adventitia of the great vessels superiorly. It does not attach to the heart itself and never involves only a single one of these sites.'),
('f0c6ef04-2095-5807-b9aa-e1d66110b1e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Serous pericardium:', '["Parietal (lining fibrous) and visceral (epicardium)","Three layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parietal mediated by specific receptor activation","Both separate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visceral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal (lining fibrous) and visceral (epicardium)', 'The serous pericardium forms two continuous layers: the parietal layer lines the inner aspect of the fibrous pericardium, while the visceral layer, called the epicardium, covers the heart surface. A two-layered serous sac with both parietal and visceral components is the accurate description.'),
('67177aec-da0f-54e3-a878-fb63b6f623b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Pericardial cavity:', '["Mediastinal space resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Space between parietal and visceral serous pericardium","Pleural space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Outside fibrous pericardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Space between parietal and visceral serous pericardium', 'The pericardial cavity is the potential space between the parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium, containing a thin film of fluid that reduces friction during cardiac movement. It is not a mediastinal, pleural, or heart-space structure.'),
('1630bf84-f714-580b-9149-faa18da9af1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Transverse pericardial sinus:', '["Between aorta/pulmonary trunk anteriorly and atria/SVC posteriorly","Behind LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along RA mediated by specific receptor activation","At apex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between aorta/pulmonary trunk anteriorly and atria/SVC posteriorly', 'The transverse pericardial sinus is the horizontal passage within the pericardial cavity lying between the aorta and pulmonary trunk anteriorly and the right atrium with the superior vena cava posteriorly. Surgeons use it to clamp the great vessels, and it does not lie behind the left atrium or along the right atrium.'),
('6acc3c0e-ec78-5d88-87ed-fef2255aeaba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Oblique pericardial sinus:', '["Front of RA","Blind-ended recess behind left atrium","between pulmonary veins","Along RV","Between aorta and pulmonary trunk At apex"]'::jsonb, 'Blind-ended recess behind left atrium', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is a blind-ended recess of the pericardial cavity situated behind the left atrium, bounded by the reflections around the pulmonary veins and the inferior vena cava. It is not anterior to the right atrium and does not lie between the aorta and pulmonary trunk.'),
('986ac731-bd77-5623-af3a-f51348dad9bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Heart chambers:', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","4 (RA, RV, LA, LV)","2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 (RA, RV, LA, LV)', 'The heart has four chambers: two atria (right and left) and two ventricles (right and left). Counts of two, three, five, or six do not reflect the true four-chamber arrangement.'),
('85c69024-f014-5638-8890-5d2f53adb598'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Right atrium receives:', '["Pulmonary artery","Aorta","SVC","IVC","coronary sinus Pulmonary veins LV"]'::jsonb, 'SVC', 'The right atrium receives systemic venous blood from the superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava, and the coronary sinus; the SVC is the major vein entering its upper part. The aorta and pulmonary artery are outflow vessels that conduct blood away from the heart.'),
('3d671611-fa49-59a1-a1fa-5c77e1d89a05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Right auricle:', '["Valve resulting in decreased functional activity","RV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interatrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","Small ear-shaped appendage of RA","Crista terminalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small ear-shaped appendage of RA', 'The right auricle is a small ear-shaped muscular appendage of the right atrium projecting from its anterior surface, with pectinate muscles inside. It is not a valve, the interatrial septum, the crista terminalis, or part of the right ventricle.'),
('6e7f4f61-0f2f-5030-9445-d64e5797d571'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Crista terminalis:', '["IV septum resulting in decreased functional activity","AV node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscular ridge in RA between sinus venarum and auricle","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular ridge in RA between sinus venarum and auricle', 'The crista terminalis is a vertical muscular ridge on the interior of the right atrium marking the junction between the smooth posterior sinus venarum and the rough auricular wall with its pectinate muscles. It is a structural landmark, not a valve, a septum, or nodal tissue.'),
('d60a2a5a-220e-5371-ad12-03713f7379b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Sinus venarum:', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth posterior RA derived from sinus venosus","Auricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Rough anterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interatrial septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth posterior RA derived from sinus venosus', 'The sinus venarum is the smooth-walled posterior part of the right atrium derived embryologically from the sinus venosus, and it receives the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus. The anterior and auricular walls are rough because of pectinate muscles.'),
('63f2212a-11f0-52fc-92b7-8728d81119f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Fossa ovalis:', '["Depression in interatrial septum (remnant of foramen ovale)","SA node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus opening mediated by specific receptor activation","RA valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depression in interatrial septum (remnant of foramen ovale)', 'The fossa ovalis is an oval depression on the interatrial septum representing the closed remnant of the fetal foramen ovale, bounded by the anulus ovalis. The nodes, coronary sinus opening, and valves are located elsewhere in the right atrium.'),
('cf07286c-9360-5f7a-ae7a-f292deac0829'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Tricuspid valve cusps:', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (anterior, posterior, septal)","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (anterior, posterior, septal)', 'The tricuspid valve guarding the right atrioventricular orifice has three cusps: anterior, posterior, and septal. The number three matches its name; one, two, four, or five cusps are incorrect.'),
('0298b3fb-33d7-5fb0-a8c6-8167f80378fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Right ventricle has:', '["Trabeculae carneae","papillary muscles","chordae tendineae moderator band","papillary muscles Smooth walls","trabeculae chordae"]'::jsonb, 'Trabeculae carneae', 'The interior of the right ventricle is roughened by irregular muscular ridges called the trabeculae carneae, the most characteristic feature of its wall. Papillary muscles, chordae tendineae, and the moderator band are additional right ventricular structures, but trabeculae carneae are the defining wall irregularity.'),
('89c1ef48-a865-5624-a9a1-08fbfa82563f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Moderator band:', '["Muscular band in RV conducting RBB to anterior papillary muscle","Chordae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LV trabecula mediated by specific receptor activation","RV valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Papillary muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular band in RV conducting RBB to anterior papillary muscle', 'The moderator band (septomarginal trabecula) is a muscular bridge in the right ventricle that carries the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle, ensuring its coordinated contraction. It is not a chorda, valve, papillary muscle, or left ventricular structure.'),
('9bbc1d86-7d5c-5547-b369-b8c58f0d4b94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Conus arteriosus:', '["Smooth outflow tract of RV to pulmonary trunk","LV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RV inflow mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth outflow tract of RV to pulmonary trunk', 'The conus arteriosus (infundibulum) is the smooth, funnel-shaped outflow tract of the right ventricle leading to the pulmonary valve and pulmonary trunk. The right ventricular inflow region and the other chambers are not described by this term.'),
('d3a7e270-4edf-590b-9cf6-3520ca0f1ab2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Pulmonary valve cusps:', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 semilunar (anterior, left, right)"]'::jsonb, '3 semilunar (anterior, left, right)', 'The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve composed of three cusps: anterior, left, and right, which prevent backflow into the right ventricle. Any count other than three is incorrect.'),
('08c3e975-51bb-5722-9a5b-c5d704950061'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Left atrium receives:', '["SVC","Aorta","Coronary sinus","IVC","4 pulmonary veins"]'::jsonb, '4 pulmonary veins', 'The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from four pulmonary veins, two from each lung. It does not receive the superior or inferior vena cava, the aorta, or the coronary sinus.'),
('63ed0d2c-295c-51c6-a9d8-52a0934a9f06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Mitral valve cusps:', '["4 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 (anterior and posterior)","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (anterior and posterior)', 'The mitral (bicuspid) valve guarding the left atrioventricular orifice has two cusps, anterior and posterior. Since it has two leaflets, only the count of two is correct.'),
('09e3f7f2-d6fc-593c-ba22-25d7c30dc492'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Left ventricle:', '["Thickest myocardium","trabeculae carneae","2 large papillary muscles","trabeculae","Moderator band Thinner than RV papillary muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Thickest myocardium', 'The left ventricle has the thickest myocardium of any chamber because it generates the high pressure required for systemic circulation. Its interior also contains trabeculae carneae and two large papillary muscles, but its hallmark is the thickest wall.'),
('99464385-9adb-58e5-b981-e366c8327ad9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Aortic valve cusps:', '["3 semilunar (left, right, posterior/non-coronary)","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 semilunar (left, right, posterior/non-coronary)', 'The aortic valve has three semilunar cusps: left, right, and posterior (non-coronary). The right and left aortic sinuses behind those cusps give origin to the coronary arteries.'),
('9c2ff6e6-9075-5e95-b217-4c795b651e01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Sinuses of Valsalva:', '["Atrial appendages resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic root dilations behind cusps; coronary arteries originate from them","Valves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chordae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic root dilations behind cusps; coronary arteries originate from them', 'The sinuses of Valsalva are the dilatations of the aortic root behind each aortic valve cusp; the right and left coronary arteries arise from the respective aortic sinuses. They are not valves, ventricles, atrial appendages, or chordae.'),
('d9bd3b35-330c-54c7-bfd5-490ef6633943'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Conducting system starts at:', '["Bundle of His","Bundle branches","SA node in RA","AV node","Purkinje fibers"]'::jsonb, 'SA node in RA', 'The cardiac conduction system begins at the sinoatrial node in the right atrium, the normal pacemaker of the heart. The impulse then travels to the AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers, placing the SA node first in the sequence.'),
('e5c2d0b1-f3ac-51b8-83f9-e4d9c8eb403a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'SA node located:', '["AV junction resulting in decreased functional activity","RA at junction of SVC and right auricle (crista terminalis)","Interatrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RA at junction of SVC and right auricle (crista terminalis)', 'The SA node lies in the upper part of the right atrium at the junction of the superior vena cava with the right auricle, adjacent to the upper end of the crista terminalis. It is not in the interatrial septum, left atrium, atrioventricular junction, or ventricle.'),
('c54ef1b3-d35e-5605-b11f-8ebbadffca47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'SA nodal artery from:', '["Marginal resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior descending accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Right coronary artery (60%) or left circumflex (40%)","Circumflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (60%) or left circumflex (40%)', 'The SA nodal artery arises from the right coronary artery in about 60% of individuals and from the left circumflex artery in the remaining 40%. It does not arise from the marginal, posterior descending, or left anterior descending arteries.'),
('1a9c1e3c-fc5b-56c0-ac36-7a6834c32834'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'AV node located:', '["LV resulting in decreased functional activity","RV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apex mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interatrial septum above tricuspid valve near coronary sinus opening"]'::jsonb, 'Interatrial septum above tricuspid valve near coronary sinus opening', 'The AV node lies in the lower part of the interatrial septum, just above the septal cusp of the tricuspid valve and close to the opening of the coronary sinus (at the apex of the triangle of Koch). It is not located in the ventricles, apex, or left atrium.'),
('b71dfc47-7cdf-5f4f-9e7d-d5f5403666be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'AV nodal artery from:', '["Left coronary","Circumflex","Right coronary artery (85-90%)","LAD","Posterior descending"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (85-90%)', 'The AV nodal artery most commonly arises from the right coronary artery, in about 85-90% of hearts. The left coronary, circumflex, LAD, and posterior descending arteries are not the usual source.'),
('8078b88c-3f21-5992-8a79-5958cf37d1f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'AV bundle of His:', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","SA node to AV node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct to ventricular walls mediated by specific receptor activation","RA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From AV node through IV septum divides into bundle branches"]'::jsonb, 'From AV node through IV septum divides into bundle branches', 'The AV bundle (bundle of His) arises from the AV node, enters the central fibrous body, descends in the interventricular septum, and divides into the right and left bundle branches. It does not run directly to ventricular walls or to the atria.'),
('67a9b436-d519-5a26-a5d2-56f0fdaab042'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Right bundle branch:', '["To LV","To SA node","To RV","partly via moderator band","To atria Through interatrial septum"]'::jsonb, 'To RV', 'The right bundle branch descends along the right side of the interventricular septum and passes through the moderator band to supply the right ventricle. It does not supply the left ventricle, atria, or SA node.'),
('c7dbe586-2d3b-5ded-be1e-9f16186c5950'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Left bundle branch divides into:', '["Superior and inferior","Single","Medial and lateral","Anterior and posterior fascicles","Multiple small"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior fascicles', 'The left bundle branch divides into two main fascicles, anterior and posterior, that supply the left ventricle (with a small septal branch between them). It does not remain a single trunk or divide into medial and lateral branches.'),
('604b4417-381f-5073-b4ea-d73905855dcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Purkinje fibers:', '["Endothelial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Connective tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nerve cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Ordinary muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Modified cardiac muscle for rapid impulse conduction"]'::jsonb, 'Modified cardiac muscle for rapid impulse conduction', 'Purkinje fibers are specialized modified cardiac muscle cells that rapidly conduct the electrical impulse from the bundle branches throughout the ventricular myocardium. They are not nerve cells, connective tissue, endothelial cells, or ordinary working myocardium.'),
('f71b8e1e-5fec-5540-a3d0-26c3d6056131'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Right coronary artery from:', '["Ascending aorta","Right aortic sinus (anterior)","Posterior aortic sinus","Left aortic sinus","Pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Right aortic sinus (anterior)', 'The right coronary artery arises from the right aortic sinus (anterior sinus of Valsalva) of the ascending aorta. It does not arise from the left or posterior aortic sinuses or from the pulmonary trunk.'),
('73dc15af-8e0c-5b40-aee0-d45714ce8e73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Left coronary artery from:', '["Pulmonary trunk","Right aortic sinus","Left aortic sinus (left posterior)","Posterior aortic sinus","Ascending aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Left aortic sinus (left posterior)', 'The left coronary artery arises from the left aortic sinus (left posterior sinus of Valsalva). The right and posterior aortic sinuses and the pulmonary trunk are not its origin.'),
('f947f2ac-9ec8-5761-889b-56543e86e7c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Left coronary divides into:', '["Posterior interventricular + diagonal","Left marginal + LAD","LAD (anterior interventricular) and circumflex","Circumflex + marginal","Right marginal + PDA"]'::jsonb, 'LAD (anterior interventricular) and circumflex', 'The left coronary artery divides shortly after its origin into the left anterior descending (anterior interventricular) artery and the circumflex artery. The posterior interventricular and right marginal arteries belong to the right coronary system.'),
('e292754c-c384-59ec-99ee-ebfcce444581'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'LAD runs:', '["Posterior interventricular groove","Right coronary sulcus","Anterior interventricular groove to apex","Coronary sulcus","Left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular groove to apex', 'The left anterior descending artery (LAD) runs in the anterior interventricular groove toward the cardiac apex, supplying the anterior walls of both ventricles and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. It does not run in the posterior interventricular groove or the coronary sulcus.'),
('03fb8b5b-871f-5bc3-bd3d-e307f1c074d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Circumflex artery runs:', '["Anterior interventricular groove","Left AV (coronary) groove","Posterior interventricular groove","Right margin","SA node"]'::jsonb, 'Left AV (coronary) groove', 'The circumflex artery courses in the left atrioventricular (coronary) groove between the left atrium and the left ventricle. The anterior and posterior interventricular grooves are the courses of the LAD and the posterior descending artery, respectively.'),
('b4af4299-2e35-54c0-8c9d-1e293f052a1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'PDA (posterior interventricular) usually from:', '["Left marginal resulting in decreased functional activity","Left coronary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Circumflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right coronary artery (right-dominant 80%)"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (right-dominant 80%)', 'In right-dominant hearts, which occur in about 80% of individuals, the posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery arises from the right coronary artery. It is not a usual branch of the left coronary system, LAD, circumflex, or left marginal artery.'),
('d3109934-1a07-5bbc-95fe-0f9e3ba63dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Coronary sinus located:', '["Anterior sulcus","LA","Posterior coronary (AV) sulcus","drains to RA","RV Interventricular groove"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior coronary (AV) sulcus', 'The coronary sinus is the enlarged terminal portion of the cardiac venous system that runs in the posterior atrioventricular (coronary) sulcus between the left atrium and the left ventricle. It is not located in the anterior sulcus or interventricular groove.'),
('7208212e-5518-5b91-a899-d9a55e14ace8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Great cardiac vein runs:', '["Right AV groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sulcus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior interventricular groove mediated by specific receptor activation","Apex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior interventricular groove with LAD"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular groove with LAD', 'The great cardiac vein runs upward in the anterior interventricular groove beside the LAD, then continues around the coronary sulcus to empty into the coronary sinus. It does not run in the posterior interventricular groove or the right atrioventricular groove.'),
('ac740498-12b4-5f26-abb9-d3827497a6bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Middle cardiac vein runs:', '["Right AV groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sulcus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior interventricular groove mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior interventricular groove with PDA","Apex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interventricular groove with PDA', 'The middle cardiac vein runs upward in the posterior interventricular groove alongside the posterior descending artery and opens into the coronary sinus near its right end. It does not run in the anterior interventricular groove or the coronary sulcus.'),
('a7e48803-6767-54ef-b369-8636b5ded08a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Small cardiac vein runs:', '["Posterior groove","Right AV groove with marginal artery","To RA directly","Anterior interventricular","Left AV groove"]'::jsonb, 'Right AV groove with marginal artery', 'The small cardiac vein runs in the right atrioventricular groove together with the right marginal artery and usually drains into the coronary sinus. It does not run in the posterior groove, anterior interventricular groove, or left atrioventricular groove.'),
('d6b41369-d8c7-54c8-8ede-43acb63124da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Coronary arteries fill during:', '["Both equally","Atrial relaxation","Systole (contraction)","Diastole (ventricular relaxation)","Atrial contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Diastole (ventricular relaxation)', 'Coronary blood flow peaks during diastole because myocardial relaxation removes the compressive force that occludes the intramural coronary vessels during systole. Ventricular contraction and atrial phases do not provide the dominant filling period.'),
('f8d92354-1549-5313-8751-a54f2c116e89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Interatrial septum contains:', '["Moderator band","Fossa ovalis and its valve","SA node","AV node","Papillary muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis and its valve', 'The interatrial septum is marked by the fossa ovalis with its valve-like fold, the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale. The SA and AV nodes, moderator band, and papillary muscles are not components of this septum.'),
('80bff322-ec27-5eff-b2dd-c9c31db2313b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Interventricular septum:', '["Large muscular part + small membranous part (superior)","Equal parts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only muscular mediated by specific receptor activation","membranous part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only membranous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large muscular part + small membranous part (superior)', 'The interventricular septum has a large muscular part forming most of its bulk and a small thin membranous part located superiorly, adjacent to the aortic and atrioventricular valves. It is neither purely muscular nor purely membranous, and the two parts are unequal.'),
('2451d599-34e0-5dcb-b532-453109abec80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Heart apex:', '["Both ventricles resulting in decreased functional activity","Right ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2nd ICS mediated by specific receptor activation","Base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle at 5th ICS left midclavicular line"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle at 5th ICS left midclavicular line', 'The apex of the heart is formed by the left ventricle and is clinically located in the left fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line, where the apex beat is palpable. It is not formed by the right ventricle or located at the base.'),
('0d1a4eb5-719a-5347-95e4-a47deb39a5a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Heart base:', '["Right border resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior aspect mainly left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior aspect mainly left atrium', 'The base of the heart is its posterior aspect, formed mainly by the left atrium with contributions from the right atrium and the entering great veins, facing toward the vertebral column. It is not anterior, inferior, at the apex, or along the right border.'),
('7a912ce3-e03b-5724-8845-2a943e3c4632'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Sternocostal (anterior) surface:', '["Both ventricles equally resulting in decreased functional activity","LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LV mediated by specific receptor activation","Both atria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right atrium and right ventricle (mostly RV)"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium and right ventricle (mostly RV)', 'The sternocostal (anterior) surface of the heart faces the sternum and costal cartilages and is formed mainly by the right ventricle, with the right atrium contributing on the right side. It is not formed by the left ventricle, the atria alone, or both ventricles equally.'),
('654d7d7c-7d94-521a-a11a-e274819b2453'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Diaphragmatic (inferior) surface:', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","Both atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RA mediated by specific receptor activation","RV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle (partly RV near apex)"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle (partly RV near apex)', 'The diaphragmatic (inferior) surface of the heart rests on the central tendon of the diaphragm and is formed mainly by the left ventricle, with a small contribution from the right ventricle near the apex. It is not formed predominantly by the atria or right ventricle.'),
('6c794468-ea6d-57f8-8f2a-57346c5f4f5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Left border of heart:', '["RV resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LA mediated by specific receptor activation","Left ventricle (and left auricle above)","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle (and left auricle above)', 'The left border of the heart is a rounded margin formed mainly by the left ventricle, with the left auricle contributing above it. It is not formed by the right ventricle, right atrium, or pulmonary trunk.'),
('a65b26e3-aee0-564c-8fe2-5ae66fcbd825'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Right border of heart:', '["SVC","LV","Right atrium","LA","RV"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The right border of the heart is formed by the right atrium, extending from the superior vena cava down to the inferior vena cava. It is not formed by the left ventricle, left atrium, right ventricle, or the SVC itself.'),
('c8ec9921-efd3-59c6-bbe1-e179f67c1aaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Inferior border of heart:', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","Both atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right ventricle (mostly) and left ventricle (near apex)","All chambers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricle (mostly) and left ventricle (near apex)', 'The inferior (acute) border runs from the opening of the inferior vena cava to the apex and is formed mainly by the right ventricle, with the left ventricle contributing only near the apex.'),
('84be23ff-5769-5e92-b980-62b59a358020'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Ligamentum arteriosum:', '["Pericardial ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous cord (remnant of ductus arteriosus) between pulmonary trunk and aorta","Foramen ovale remnant mediated by specific receptor activation","Coronary vessel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous cord (remnant of ductus arteriosus) between pulmonary trunk and aorta', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the obliterated fibrous remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus. It connects the bifurcation of the pulmonary trunk to the underside of the aortic arch, posterior to the left vagus nerve.'),
('2409cf9e-fc75-5316-bab1-4d4f6353d17b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Foramen ovale becomes:', '["Coronary sinus","Ligamentum arteriosum","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum teres","Ductus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis', 'After birth the fetal foramen ovale closes, and its remnant is the shallow fossa ovalis on the interatrial septum. The raised crescentic upper rim of the fossa is the limbus fossae ovalis.'),
('8ddc77ce-1ff3-5f7a-9b4c-b438684c3729'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Ductus arteriosus becomes:', '["Ligamentum teres","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum venosum","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum arteriosum"]'::jsonb, 'Ligamentum arteriosum', 'Functional closure of the ductus arteriosus after birth is followed by fibrous obliteration of its lumen, producing the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch.'),
('1ae1602e-94dc-5515-bca1-a060a58f9d65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Heart wall layers:', '["2","5","3: epicardium","myocardium","endocardium 4 1"]'::jsonb, '3: epicardium', 'The heart wall has three layers: the outer epicardium (visceral serous pericardium), the middle myocardium, and the inner endocardium. The chosen option gives the number of layers with the outermost layer named.'),
('c7995c7a-4ea1-577f-8547-e8b2e2fe35b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Endocardium continuous with:', '["Pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","Tunica intima of blood vessels","Epicardium mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tunica intima of blood vessels', 'The endocardium lines the cardiac chambers and covers the heart valves. It is continuous with the tunica intima lining the great vessels that enter and leave the heart.'),
('33c0535b-7ae9-56f6-aa7e-ec9600121781'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Aortic arch branches:', '["5 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3: brachiocephalic left common carotid left subclavian"]'::jsonb, '3: brachiocephalic left common carotid left subclavian', 'The arch of the aorta normally gives off three branches: the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid artery, and the left subclavian artery, in that order from right to left.'),
('a674f67e-1ce5-534f-ad4a-6960e5a1a134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Brachiocephalic trunk divides into:', '["Right subclavian + right vertebral","Right + left common carotid","Right + left subclavian","Left common carotid + left subclavian","Right common carotid and right subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Right common carotid and right subclavian', 'The brachiocephalic trunk, the first branch of the aortic arch, divides behind the right sternoclavicular joint into the right common carotid artery and the right subclavian artery.'),
('6b0d813b-55c4-5ce5-ad73-cf8269eee6f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'SVC formed by:', '["Azygos + hemiazygos","Right and left brachiocephalic veins","Right + left subclavian","Right + left IJV","Right + left vertebral"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left brachiocephalic veins', 'The superior vena cava forms at the level of the first right costal cartilage by the union of the right and left brachiocephalic veins. It passes downward and opens into the right atrium.'),
('9580e34a-1d20-5581-a7b6-146fe983615f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'SVC drains into:', '["IVC","Right atrium","LA","RV","LV"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The superior vena cava returns venous blood from the head, neck, upper limbs, and thorax and opens into the upper part of the right atrium, with no valve at its atrial opening.'),
('24d1b1d7-041f-5cd1-94af-8145f5ed062f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'IVC drains into:', '["Right atrium (lower part)","LV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (lower part)', 'The inferior vena cava pierces the diaphragm at the level of T8 and opens into the lower part of the right atrium, the site of a small, usually functionless valve remnant.'),
('e571b4c4-56a8-57f9-8b7f-920c5ae5edd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Coronary arteries originate from:', '["Ascending aorta above sinuses resulting in decreased functional activity","LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sinuses of Valsalva at aortic root","RA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinuses of Valsalva at aortic root', 'The right and left coronary arteries arise from the aortic wall within the sinuses of Valsalva, immediately above the aortic valve cusps. They are the first branches of the aorta.'),
('d1bd0ccc-bda2-5307-958f-5a4dc97befaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Coronary sinus drains into:', '["RV resulting in decreased functional activity","Right atrium (between IVC and tricuspid valve)","SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (between IVC and tricuspid valve)', 'The coronary sinus, the principal venous channel of the heart, runs in the posterior atrioventricular groove. It opens into the right atrium between the orifice of the IVC and the tricuspid valve, guarded by the Thebesian valve.'),
('a2be3aec-8872-5b53-bc28-7d37342699a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Thebesian veins:', '["Azygos resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sinus tributaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Small veins draining myocardium directly into chambers","Cardiac lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small veins draining myocardium directly into chambers', 'Thebesian veins (venae cordis minimae) are tiny venous channels that arise in the myocardium and drain directly into the adjacent cardiac chamber, bypassing the coronary sinus. They are the smallest of the three cardiac venous pathways.'),
('0ba68643-d33f-5569-8cc4-7abb204293a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which artery supplies the SA node in most people?', '["Right coronary artery (in 60% of people)","Left circumflex artery","Left anterior descending artery","Posterior descending artery","Right marginal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (in 60% of people)', 'A single SA nodal artery is present in about 60% of hearts, arising from the proximal right coronary artery. In about 40% it instead arises from the left circumflex artery.'),
('b06a5d72-4332-5726-988c-d6e6d0533026'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The heart is located:', '["In the middle mediastinum","In the posterior mediastinum","In the superior mediastinum","In the anterior mediastinum","In the pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 'In the middle mediastinum', 'The mediastinum is divided into superior, anterior, middle, and posterior compartments. The heart, together with its serous and fibrous pericardium, occupies the middle mediastinum.'),
('e0ee7062-be60-5f17-afae-99597ac21439'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The pericardium consists of:', '["Endocardium only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only fibrous layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only serous layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous pericardium and serous pericardium (parietal and visceral layers)","Myocardium and epicardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous pericardium and serous pericardium (parietal and visceral layers)', 'The pericardium is a sac made of an outer fibrous pericardium and an inner serous pericardium. The serous layer lines the sac as the parietal layer and reflects onto the heart at the roots of the great vessels as the visceral layer (epicardium).'),
('e45f6a6d-be8e-5c80-9739-463d06c15f59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is located:', '["In front of the right ventricle","Anterior to the aorta","Posterior to the heart","between the pulmonary veins","Between the SVC and IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the heart', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is a blind cul-de-sac of the serous pericardial cavity lying posterior to the heart, chiefly behind the left atrium. It is bounded by the reflections surrounding the pulmonary veins and the IVC.'),
('a1e8d37c-abf0-5086-ae3d-0888d8328248'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The transverse pericardial sinus is:', '["Below the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the two ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A passage between the aorta/pulmonary trunk and the atria/SVC","Behind the left atrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In front of the right ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A passage between the aorta/pulmonary trunk and the atria/SVC', 'The transverse pericardial sinus is a horizontal passage of the serous pericardial cavity that lies posterior to the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk and anterior to the right atrium and SVC. A finger passed behind the two arterial trunks enters it.'),
('88687c2d-9f35-595c-b9ed-3a11caa1f80e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The heart wall layers are:', '["Epicardium (visceral pericardium)","myocardium","endocardium","Pericardium only","Myocardium and endocardium"]'::jsonb, 'endocardium', 'The heart wall has three layers: the epicardium, the myocardium, and the endocardium. The endocardium is the thin smooth inner layer that lines the chambers and is continuous with the vascular tunica intima.'),
('2909bb54-7553-5226-ad4e-3f4090490f69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The right atrium receives:', '["Aorta","Bronchial veins","SVC","IVC","and coronary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'and coronary sinus', 'The right atrium receives venous blood from the superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava, and the coronary sinus. The coronary sinus opens into its posteromedial wall between the IVC and the tricuspid valve.'),
('07c03da4-a884-5544-b5a0-f04b66f0bacf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The right ventricle pumps blood to:', '["Left atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary trunk (via the pulmonary valve)","Coronary arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary trunk (via the pulmonary valve)', 'The right ventricle ejects deoxygenated blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk. From there blood passes to the lungs for oxygenation.'),
('0c51b779-8a78-5ece-9341-8f1b825721c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The left atrium receives:', '["Four pulmonary veins (two from each lung)","Bronchial veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","SVC and IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Four pulmonary veins (two from each lung)', 'Two pulmonary veins from each lung, four in total, enter the posterior wall of the left atrium. They return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart.'),
('28d58528-09aa-57d1-ad02-a9b2f60a39a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The left ventricle pumps blood to:', '["Right atrium","Aorta (via the aortic valve)","Pulmonary trunk","Left atrium","Right ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (via the aortic valve)', 'The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood through the aortic valve into the aorta. Its thick muscular wall generates the systemic arterial pressure.'),
('3601d755-6d07-5974-ac79-c24431a9bda8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The interatrial septum contains:', '["Trabeculae carneae","Papillary muscles","Chordae tendineae","Crista terminalis","Fossa ovalis (remnant of foramen ovale)"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis (remnant of foramen ovale)', 'The fossa ovalis, the shallow remnant left by closure of the fetal foramen ovale, lies on the interatrial septum. Anteriorly the crista terminalis lies on the wall of the right atrium, while trabeculae carneae, papillary muscles, and chordae belong to the ventricles.'),
('edaf20ea-58c7-5acf-84f7-04b820c9ecb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The interventricular septum consists of:', '["Connective tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscular part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only membranous part mediated by specific receptor activation","Fatty tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscular part (thick) and membranous part (thin)"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular part (thick) and membranous part (thin)', 'The interventricular septum has a large, thick muscular part and a small, thin membranous part located near the aortic valve. The membranous septum is the usual site of congenital ventricular septal defects.'),
('6da0cb03-9490-5336-8eb5-88238dc5b5e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The sinoatrial (SA) node is located:', '["In the left ventricle","In the AV septum","In the right atrium","at the junction of SVC and right atrial appendage (sulcus terminalis)","In the interventricular septum"]'::jsonb, 'In the right atrium', 'The SA node lies in the upper lateral wall of the right atrium, near the sulcus terminalis at the junction of the SVC and the right atrial appendage. It is the normal pacemaker that generates the cardiac impulse.'),
('1d8e6762-b312-521a-acbd-b59d8cc59403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The atrioventricular (AV) node is located:', '["In the right ventricle","In the left atrium","In the interventricular septum","In the SA node","In the inferior part of the interatrial septum"]'::jsonb, 'In the inferior part of the interatrial septum', 'The AV node sits in the posteroinferior part of the interatrial septum, anterior and superior to the opening of the coronary sinus. It delays the impulse traveling from atria to ventricles.'),
('b87065d5-b4d8-5526-8661-36524aa3ba03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The AV bundle (Bundle of His) divides into:', '["Medial and lateral branches resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior and inferior branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SA and AV fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Right and left bundle branches (in the interventricular septum)","Anterior and posterior fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left bundle branches (in the interventricular septum)', 'The AV bundle descends from the AV node through the central fibrous body into the interventricular septum, where it divides into the right and left bundle branches. These end as Purkinje fibers in the ventricular walls.'),
('5a2cb780-4d15-5bea-8daf-5691cbe2e299'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The coronary arteries arise from:', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta (right and left coronary sinuses of Valsalva)","Left ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Right ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (right and left coronary sinuses of Valsalva)', 'The right and left coronary arteries arise from the aortic dilations called the sinuses of Valsalva, located above the aortic valve cusps. The right coronary artery comes from the anterior sinus and the left from the left posterior sinus.'),
('ce870613-337f-5a64-8300-6ebd4645c746'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The right coronary artery supplies:', '["The anterior septum","The apex of the heart","The lateral wall of left ventricle","Right ventricle","SA node (60%)"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricle', 'The right coronary artery runs in the right atrioventricular groove and supplies the right ventricle, the SA node in about 60% of hearts, the AV node in most, and the posterior left ventricle via the posterior descending artery in right-dominant hearts.'),
('195fff94-63f8-5ad9-b585-6f2ee47e4cc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies:', '["Anterior interventricular septum","anterior left ventricle","apex","Posterior septum","SA node"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular septum', 'The left anterior descending artery travels in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum along with the anterior left ventricle and apex. Its occlusion produces an anterior wall infarct.'),
('9792498c-f37b-5965-ac2e-7d42fdfc11f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The circumflex artery supplies:', '["Lateral and posterior left ventricle","Posterior septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior septum mediated by specific receptor activation","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and posterior left ventricle', 'The circumflex artery follows the left atrioventricular groove around the lateral margin of the heart. It supplies the lateral and posterior walls of the left ventricle and the left atrium.'),
('e8819573-f64c-5a75-bc9e-6a953bac6438'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The coronary sinus drains into:', '["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Superior vena cava","Right ventricle","Right atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The coronary sinus lies in the posterior atrioventricular groove and collects most of the cardiac venous blood. It empties into the posterior wall of the right atrium.'),
('b2cf597b-171b-51b6-bccd-4b0f14a113c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The cardiac valves include:', '["Mitral and tricuspid","Aortic and pulmonary","Only AV valves","Only semilunar valves","Tricuspid (right AV)"]'::jsonb, 'Tricuspid (right AV)', 'The four cardiac valves comprise two atrioventricular valves (tricuspid and mitral) and two semilunar valves (aortic and pulmonary). The tricuspid valve guards the right atrioventricular orifice and is one of the AV valves.'),
('892a29bc-cbdf-5ff0-9b24-1d4d32d5c021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The mitral valve is located:', '["Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left atrium and left ventricle","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk","In the IV septum"]'::jsonb, 'Between the left atrium and left ventricle', 'The mitral (bicuspid) valve guards the left atrioventricular orifice between the left atrium and the left ventricle. Its two cusps prevent reflux into the left atrium during ventricular systole.'),
('b8ef6302-4a32-5056-8630-bd8b3e75ab65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The tricuspid valve is located:', '["Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk","In the IV septum","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the left atrium and left ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Between the right atrium and right ventricle', 'The tricuspid valve guards the right atrioventricular orifice between the right atrium and the right ventricle. Its three cusps are anchored to chordae tendineae and papillary muscles.'),
('e327743c-dbf5-5128-9421-bb94bc6b76e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The aortic valve is located:', '["Between the left atrium and left ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","In the coronary sinus","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Between the left ventricle and aorta', 'The aortic valve is a semilunar valve placed at the junction of the left ventricle and the ascending aorta. Its three cusps open during systole and close to prevent retrograde flow into the ventricle.'),
('91667c89-14e8-5393-895d-cf08e240fae2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The pulmonary valve is located:', '["Between the left atrium and left ventricle","In the IV septum","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk', 'The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve situated between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. It opens during right ventricular systole and closes during diastole.'),
('ac178a9a-d209-5343-a36e-b7c5a563b165'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Chordae tendineae connect:', '["Papillary muscles to the AV valve cusps (prevent prolapse)","Ventricles to atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta to ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","SA node to AV node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atria to ventricles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary muscles to the AV valve cusps (prevent prolapse)', 'Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords linking the margins of the atrioventricular valve cusps to the papillary muscles. They keep the cusps from prolapsing into the atrium when the ventricles contract.'),
('e3dfe719-2d52-503e-9a3e-42b104a0ffd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Papillary muscles arise from:', '["Atrial wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Chordae tendineae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventricular wall (trabeculae carneae)","Aortic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valve cusps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular wall (trabeculae carneae)', 'Papillary muscles are projections of ventricular myocardium arising from the trabeculae carneae of the ventricular wall. Their contraction tenses the chordae tendineae during systole.'),
('d6253c85-05ca-52a3-ae96-b40f385a7dfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The fossa ovalis is a remnant of:', '["Umbilical vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductus venosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ductus arteriosus mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale (fetal shunt between right and left atria)","Ligamentum arteriosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen ovale (fetal shunt between right and left atria)', 'The fossa ovalis is the depression left on the interatrial septum after closure of the foramen ovale, the fetal channel shunting blood from the right to the left atrium. Failure of closure produces an atrial septal defect.'),
('e7f6ba81-0ae8-53fc-94cf-657b07aee7fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is a remnant of:', '["Umbilical vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductus arteriosus (fetal shunt between pulmonary trunk and aorta)","Umbilical artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ductus venosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ductus arteriosus (fetal shunt between pulmonary trunk and aorta)', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous remnant of the ductus arteriosus, the fetal vessel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta. It fully transforms into a ligament within the first year of life.'),
('8ed0652a-4baf-5fea-80ae-3047d2bb8fa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The conducting system of the heart includes:', '["Cardiac nerves","Valves","Pericardium","SA node","AV node"]'::jsonb, 'SA node', 'The cardiac conducting system consists of the SA node, internodal pathways, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers. The SA node, the normal pacemaker, is an intrinsic part of this system.'),
('e28e50b8-caf4-5497-83e9-f4044248650c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The vagus nerve provides:', '["innervation resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor to valves mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic innervation to the heart (decreases heart rate)","Sensory only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic innervation to the heart (decreases heart rate)', 'The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the SA and AV nodes. Vagal stimulation slows the heart rate through muscarinic receptors, whereas sympathetic fibers from the cardiac sympathetic chain accelerate it.'),
('a816a799-fa64-51c3-adda-6a3e948d79c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The SA node is located in:', '["Interventricular septum","Right ventricle","Right atrium near SVC opening","AV node","Left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium near SVC opening', 'The SA node lies in the lateral wall of the right atrium close to the opening of the SVC, in the sulcus terminalis. Its location determines that its arterial supply comes from the SA nodal artery.'),
('c1dc96d6-268e-5674-a2b7-8fc86d86effe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The AV node is located in:', '["Interventricular septum resulting in decreased functional activity","Right ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left atrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Interatrial septum near coronary sinus","SA node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interatrial septum near coronary sinus', 'The AV node lies in the lower part of the interatrial septum, immediately anterior and superior to the opening of the coronary sinus. It relays the impulse between the atria and the ventricles.'),
('876b0def-f936-5d02-9663-a34b249e7bba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The left anterior descending artery supplies:', '["SA node","Lateral wall of LV","Right ventricle","Anterior LV and septum","Posterior LV"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior LV and septum', 'The left anterior descending artery courses in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. Its occlusion produces an anteroseptal infarct.'),
('983ae74f-c59c-5711-993a-a48d34ae444e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The right coronary artery typically supplies:', '["Septum","LV lateral wall","SA node, AV node, RV","Anterior LV","Circumflex territory"]'::jsonb, 'SA node, AV node, RV', 'In most hearts the right coronary artery supplies the right ventricle, the SA node, and the AV node, and in right-dominant circulation it also gives the posterior descending artery. This makes it the chief arterial source for the conduction system.'),
('f82e6c08-8b80-5ea7-a072-c0b3ffd05e5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Thebesian veins drain into:', '["Coronary sinus","Middle cardiac vein","Cardiac chambers directly","Great cardiac vein","Small cardiac vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac chambers directly', 'Thebesian veins (venae cordis minimae) carry blood from the myocardial wall directly into the chambers of the heart, most abundantly on the right side. They bypass the coronary sinus and its tributary veins.'),
('514cb83d-f421-5a87-8c65-bf737e37a905'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The pericardial cavity contains:', '["Lymph","Pus","Serous fluid","Air","Blood"]'::jsonb, 'Serous fluid', 'The pericardial cavity lies between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium and normally holds a thin film of serous fluid that lubricates the heart during contraction. Lymph, blood, pus, or air do not occupy this potential space under normal conditions.'),
('ac5487f4-ad90-520d-affc-6fa69c6bcad1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Heart Pericardium: Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('7e4229e4-ffea-53d7-8a91-73846d33acfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which of the following describes the layers of the pericardium?', '["Fibrous (outer) + serous (parietal + visceral)","Only serous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fibrous mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous + serous + muscular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serous only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous (outer) + serous (parietal + visceral)', 'The pericardium consists of an outer fibrous layer and an inner serous pericardium, which is further divided into the parietal layer lining the fibrous sac and the visceral layer (epicardium) on the heart. It is neither only fibrous, only serous, nor muscular.'),
('8e0c5213-1de9-523e-b24f-15275d6ab312'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The fibrous pericardium is attached to:', '["Only heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Only great vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","Only diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central tendon of diaphragm sternum great vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Central tendon of diaphragm sternum great vessels', 'The fibrous pericardium is anchored inferiorly to the central tendon of the diaphragm, anteriorly to the sternum by sternopericardial ligaments, and superiorly to the adventitia of the great vessels. Attachment to any single one of these structures is incomplete.'),
('566c930e-4ba6-5198-b3b7-47f7d318b7e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The serous pericardium consists of:', '["Parietal (lining fibrous) and visceral (epicardium)","Three layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parietal mediated by specific receptor activation","Both separate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visceral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal (lining fibrous) and visceral (epicardium)', 'The serous pericardium has two continuous layers: the parietal layer lining the fibrous pericardium and the visceral layer covering the heart surface (epicardium). It is not a three-layered structure and its layers are never separate.'),
('212dcc5e-4274-5156-86fe-f640444249c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The pericardial cavity is the:', '["Mediastinal space resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Space between parietal and visceral serous pericardium","Pleural space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Outside fibrous pericardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Space between parietal and visceral serous pericardium', 'The pericardial cavity is the space between the parietal and visceral layers of serous pericardium containing a small amount of serous fluid. It does not correspond to the mediastinal or pleural spaces or to a region outside the fibrous sac.'),
('5e1ffd86-6957-5671-89ec-e30a2646ae9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The transverse pericardial sinus lies:', '["Between aorta/pulmonary trunk anteriorly and atria/SVC posteriorly","Behind LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along RA mediated by specific receptor activation","At apex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between aorta/pulmonary trunk anteriorly and atria/SVC posteriorly', 'The transverse pericardial sinus is a passage of the serous pericardial cavity behind the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk and in front of the superior vena cava and atria. It allows surgical instruments to be passed around the great vessels; the other sites are not its location.'),
('5f28ab00-bc7a-51eb-8a54-10d06d1e2372'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is a:', '["Front of RA","Blind-ended recess behind left atrium","between pulmonary veins","Along RV","Between aorta and pulmonary trunk At apex"]'::jsonb, 'Blind-ended recess behind left atrium', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is the blind-ended recess of the pericardial cavity behind the left atrium, bounded on each side by pulmonary veins. It is not found in front of the right atrium, along the ventricle, or at a cardiac margin.'),
('aa321d3b-4b3c-5036-8776-86c30ddb71ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The number of chambers of the heart is:', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","4 (RA, RV, LA, LV)","2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 (RA, RV, LA, LV)', 'The heart contains four chambers: right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, and left ventricle. Counts of two, three, five, or six do not match cardiac anatomy.'),
('a158fd0e-2549-5e8e-8309-f4f8d74c2ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which of the following drains into the right atrium?', '["Pulmonary artery","Aorta","SVC","IVC","coronary sinus Pulmonary veins LV"]'::jsonb, 'SVC', 'The right atrium receives the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus. The aorta and pulmonary artery carry blood away from the heart, so they are not venous inputs to the right atrium.'),
('a0419642-2ff2-57f3-9cc1-e24f0a4c52a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The right auricle is the:', '["Valve resulting in decreased functional activity","RV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interatrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","Small ear-shaped appendage of RA","Crista terminalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small ear-shaped appendage of RA', 'The right auricle is the small ear-shaped muscular appendage projecting from the anterior surface of the right atrium. The crista terminalis, interatrial septum, and cardiac valves are distinct structures.'),
('e092c298-df83-5d0c-be00-eac1994b7aeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The crista terminalis is:', '["IV septum resulting in decreased functional activity","AV node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscular ridge in RA between sinus venarum and auricle","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular ridge in RA between sinus venarum and auricle', 'The crista terminalis is the smooth muscular ridge on the inner wall of the right atrium separating the smooth posterior sinus venarum from the rough trabeculated auricle. It is not the interventricular septum, a node, or a valve.'),
('dd03d6a1-67cf-541a-b2c6-cfb8be5d02ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The sinus venarum is the:', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth posterior RA derived from sinus venosus","Auricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Rough anterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interatrial septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth posterior RA derived from sinus venosus', 'The sinus venarum is the smooth posterior part of the right atrial wall derived embryologically from the sinus venosus. It should not be confused with the auricle, coronary sinus, or interatrial septum.'),
('620df3b2-d85b-5241-9a32-51d6f9abcee2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding clinical cases in Heart Pericardium: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('17fb9285-43d6-5d0f-9434-7d944609b91a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The number of cusps of the tricuspid valve is:', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (anterior, posterior, septal)","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (anterior, posterior, septal)', 'The tricuspid valve has three cusps: anterior, posterior, and septal. It is named for this number, and counts of one to five do not match its anatomy.'),
('e2c4594e-705b-548c-b96b-7f008c4c7505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The inner wall of the right ventricle is marked by:', '["Trabeculae carneae","papillary muscles","chordae tendineae moderator band","papillary muscles Smooth walls","trabeculae chordae"]'::jsonb, 'Trabeculae carneae', 'The inner wall of the right ventricle is roughened by trabeculae carneae, which also give origin to papillary muscles and the moderator band. Its outflow tract is smooth, but the chamber wall itself is trabeculated, not smooth.'),
('36a9a712-0a4e-5ea1-a1fd-7985de7c477c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The moderator band is a:', '["Muscular band in RV conducting RBB to anterior papillary muscle","Chordae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LV trabecula mediated by specific receptor activation","RV valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Papillary muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular band in RV conducting RBB to anterior papillary muscle', 'The moderator band (septomarginal trabecula) is a muscular bridge across the cavity of the right ventricle that conveys the right bundle branch to the anterior papillary muscle. It is not a valve, chorda, or a left ventricular structure.'),
('3a76fa64-4650-5725-b974-c6f0879adbf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The conus arteriosus is the:', '["Smooth outflow tract of RV to pulmonary trunk","LV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RV inflow mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth outflow tract of RV to pulmonary trunk', 'The conus arteriosus (infundibulum) is the smooth, funnel-shaped outflow tract of the right ventricle leading to the pulmonary trunk. It does not belong to the left ventricle, atria, or right ventricular inflow region.'),
('6590c1ae-f775-5f13-9d5e-f5d328db41fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The number of cusps of the pulmonary valve is:', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 semilunar (anterior, left, right)"]'::jsonb, '3 semilunar (anterior, left, right)', 'The pulmonary valve has three semilunar cusps: anterior, left, and right. Any count other than three does not match the pulmonary valve.'),
('a4c24c27-1a76-560e-a4c6-46de7d16cc7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which of the following drains into the left atrium?', '["SVC","Aorta","Coronary sinus","IVC","4 pulmonary veins"]'::jsonb, '4 pulmonary veins', 'The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the four pulmonary veins. The SVC, IVC, and coronary sinus drain into the right atrium, and the aorta is an outflow vessel.'),
('53fd6812-a372-55fd-b1f3-cf8e04e25dde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The number of cusps of the mitral valve is:', '["4 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 (anterior and posterior)","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (anterior and posterior)', 'The mitral (bicuspid) valve has two leaflets, anterior and posterior. Valves with three cusps (tricuspid, pulmonary, aortic) differ, so counts of one, three, four, or none are incorrect.'),
('2dcaf7fe-e220-5d20-889f-cfe2956f4393'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'A characteristic feature of the left ventricle is:', '["Thickest myocardium","trabeculae carneae","2 large papillary muscles","trabeculae","Moderator band Thinner than RV papillary muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Thickest myocardium', 'The left ventricle has the thickest myocardium of all chambers because it generates the high pressure needed for systemic circulation. Although it also contains trabeculae carneae and two papillary muscles, marked wall thickness is its hallmark.'),
('4cf62b39-6e55-5ea7-b48b-c81066e25d38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The number of cusps of the aortic valve is:', '["3 semilunar (left, right, posterior/non-coronary)","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 semilunar (left, right, posterior/non-coronary)', 'The aortic valve has three semilunar cusps: left, right, and posterior (non-coronary). The left and right coronary sinuses are behind the corresponding cusps; counts other than three are incorrect.'),
('cc9c7fe7-298d-5457-938a-bf36675624f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The sinuses of Valsalva are:', '["Atrial appendages resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic root dilations behind cusps; coronary arteries originate from them","Valves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chordae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic root dilations behind cusps; coronary arteries originate from them', 'The sinuses of Valsalva are the dilations of the aortic root behind the three aortic valve cusps, and the left and right coronary arteries arise from the corresponding sinuses. They are not valves, atrial appendages, or ventricular structures.'),
('04877876-5299-5f50-8779-8c9388c82a93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The cardiac conduction system begins at the:', '["Bundle of His","Bundle branches","SA node in RA","AV node","Purkinje fibers"]'::jsonb, 'SA node in RA', 'The cardiac conduction system begins at the sinoatrial (SA) node in the wall of the right atrium, the normal pacemaker. The AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers are all distal to it.'),
('ca88f599-5846-5b1d-85c7-eb2761ea47c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The SA node is located at the:', '["AV junction resulting in decreased functional activity","RA at junction of SVC and right auricle (crista terminalis)","Interatrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RA at junction of SVC and right auricle (crista terminalis)', 'The SA node lies in the lateral wall of the right atrium at the junction of the superior vena cava and the right auricle, along the upper end of the crista terminalis. It is not found at the AV junction, interatrial septum, left atrium, or ventricle.'),
('04ba86f1-1a73-5d29-b256-d0552a5a400d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The SA nodal artery usually arises from the:', '["Marginal resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior descending accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Right coronary artery (60%) or left circumflex (40%)","Circumflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (60%) or left circumflex (40%)', 'The SA nodal artery arises from the right coronary artery in about 60% of hearts and from the left circumflex in about 40%. The marginal, LAD, posterior descending, and circumflex-only origins do not describe its common supply.'),
('5fd8e6c3-d261-502b-b240-9b88b96eead9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The AV node is located at the:', '["LV resulting in decreased functional activity","RV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apex mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interatrial septum above tricuspid valve near coronary sinus opening"]'::jsonb, 'Interatrial septum above tricuspid valve near coronary sinus opening', 'The AV node occupies the lower posterior interatrial septum, above the septal leaflet of the tricuspid valve and near the opening of the coronary sinus. It is not located in the free walls, apex, or left atrium.'),
('4ee8531f-2a87-528f-90d5-c6f45f51fe0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The AV nodal artery usually arises from the:', '["Left coronary","Circumflex","Right coronary artery (85-90%)","LAD","Posterior descending"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (85-90%)', 'The AV nodal artery usually arises from the right coronary artery in 80-90% of hearts as it crosses the crux. The left coronary artery and its branches are not the usual source.'),
('265876a9-4876-5b78-b708-189a94482ef3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The AV bundle of His:', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","SA node to AV node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct to ventricular walls mediated by specific receptor activation","RA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From AV node through IV septum divides into bundle branches"]'::jsonb, 'From AV node through IV septum divides into bundle branches', 'The AV bundle of His arises from the AV node, penetrates the fibrous cardiac skeleton, and descends through the interventricular septum before dividing into right and left bundle branches. It does not arise from the SA node or reach the ventricular walls directly.'),
('c8b913cf-c253-55f2-bf16-ac58a0902eb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The right bundle branch supplies the:', '["To LV","To SA node","To RV","partly via moderator band","To atria Through interatrial septum"]'::jsonb, 'To RV', 'The right bundle branch runs down the right side of the interventricular septum to supply the right ventricle, with a portion passing through the moderator band. It does not target the left ventricle, atria, or SA node.'),
('a9e0aadc-9437-5cd5-81ac-2079aceece03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The left bundle branch divides into:', '["Superior and inferior","Single","Medial and lateral","Anterior and posterior fascicles","Multiple small"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior fascicles', 'The left bundle branch divides into an anterior fascicle and a posterior fascicle, which ramify through the left ventricle. Other groupings do not describe its branching pattern.'),
('7c03d9be-c670-517b-89f3-23e6afda8d4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Purkinje fibers are:', '["Endothelial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Connective tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nerve cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Ordinary muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Modified cardiac muscle for rapid impulse conduction"]'::jsonb, 'Modified cardiac muscle for rapid impulse conduction', 'Purkinje fibers are modified cardiac muscle cells specialized for rapid impulse conduction to the ventricular myocardium. They are not nerve cells, endothelial cells, connective tissue, or ordinary working cardiac muscle.'),
('828a4324-efb0-5856-86f0-144f6da455e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The right coronary artery arises from the:', '["Ascending aorta","Right aortic sinus (anterior)","Posterior aortic sinus","Left aortic sinus","Pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Right aortic sinus (anterior)', 'The right coronary artery arises from the right aortic sinus, the anterior sinus of Valsalva. It does not arise from the left or posterior sinuses, the ascending aorta in general, or the pulmonary trunk.'),
('30bdf4ab-e508-5b5f-97f0-ceee431c86bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The left coronary artery arises from the:', '["Pulmonary trunk","Right aortic sinus","Left aortic sinus (left posterior)","Posterior aortic sinus","Ascending aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Left aortic sinus (left posterior)', 'The left coronary artery arises from the left aortic sinus, the left-posterior sinus of Valsalva. The right, posterior, and non-aortic origins such as the pulmonary trunk are incorrect.'),
('f039e79c-23a6-55eb-a2f4-aab0452f57f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The left coronary artery divides into:', '["Posterior interventricular + diagonal","Left marginal + LAD","LAD (anterior interventricular) and circumflex","Circumflex + marginal","Right marginal + PDA"]'::jsonb, 'LAD (anterior interventricular) and circumflex', 'The left coronary artery divides into the left anterior descending (anterior interventricular) artery and the circumflex artery. The other listed combinations describe branches of the right coronary artery.'),
('0bfdb49c-1d4e-5b0d-a14d-5e886f75e9ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The LAD (anterior interventricular) artery runs in the:', '["Posterior interventricular groove","Right coronary sulcus","Anterior interventricular groove to apex","Coronary sulcus","Left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular groove to apex', 'The LAD (anterior interventricular) artery descends in the anterior interventricular groove and continues to the cardiac apex. It does not run in the posterior groove or in the coronary sulcus.'),
('1e2633dc-68bd-5340-9637-ddace949d569'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The circumflex artery runs in the:', '["Anterior interventricular groove","Left AV (coronary) groove","Posterior interventricular groove","Right margin","SA node"]'::jsonb, 'Left AV (coronary) groove', 'The circumflex artery runs in the left atrioventricular (coronary) groove around the left border of the heart. The interventricular grooves and right margin are not its course.'),
('685b142e-e8c6-504a-b43f-ceb10389e1bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The posterior interventricular artery (PDA) usually arises from the:', '["Left marginal resulting in decreased functional activity","Left coronary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Circumflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right coronary artery (right-dominant 80%)"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (right-dominant 80%)', 'In most hearts (right-dominant, about 80%), the posterior interventricular artery arises from the right coronary artery. The left coronary branches give it only in left-dominant or codominant hearts.'),
('45b82faa-5d9e-5e74-8447-406099ec89c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The coronary sinus is located in the:', '["Anterior sulcus","LA","Posterior coronary (AV) sulcus","drains to RA","RV Interventricular groove"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior coronary (AV) sulcus', 'The coronary sinus lies in the posterior part of the coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus, where it collects most cardiac venous blood and drains into the right atrium. It is not located in the anterior sulcus, interventricular grooves, or chambers.'),
('8e7e53b0-7396-597b-a320-0b4ed0fb88fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The great cardiac vein runs in the:', '["Right AV groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sulcus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior interventricular groove mediated by specific receptor activation","Apex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior interventricular groove with LAD"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular groove with LAD', 'The great cardiac vein ascends in the anterior interventricular groove beside the LAD and continues in the coronary sulcus to reach the coronary sinus. The posterior and right-sided grooves are not its course.'),
('3b510c9d-ba1b-5991-8138-d8e9d3d96f83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The middle cardiac vein runs in the:', '["Right AV groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sulcus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior interventricular groove mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior interventricular groove with PDA","Apex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interventricular groove with PDA', 'The middle cardiac vein runs in the posterior interventricular groove alongside the posterior interventricular artery and ends in the coronary sinus. The other grooves are not their course.'),
('562d8106-bd54-57e4-9adf-5e9ee0e30478'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The small cardiac vein runs in the:', '["Posterior groove","Right AV groove with marginal artery","To RA directly","Anterior interventricular","Left AV groove"]'::jsonb, 'Right AV groove with marginal artery', 'The small cardiac vein runs in the right atrioventricular groove beside the right marginal artery and drains into the coronary sinus. It does not travel in the interventricular grooves or the left AV groove.'),
('ab391748-7bc8-5ea2-85be-259e8ef89d23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Coronary arteries fill mainly during:', '["Both equally","Atrial relaxation","Systole (contraction)","Diastole (ventricular relaxation)","Atrial contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Diastole (ventricular relaxation)', 'Coronary arteries fill mainly during diastole (ventricular relaxation), when the contracting myocardium no longer compresses them. Blood flow falls markedly during systolic contraction, and the phases of atrial activity are not the main determinants.'),
('520381f1-9d1b-5749-ac01-520390d8cd8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The interatrial septum contains the:', '["Moderator band","Fossa ovalis and its valve","SA node","AV node","Papillary muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis and its valve', 'The interatrial septum contains the fossa ovalis and its valve, the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale. The moderator band, SA node, AV node, and papillary muscles are not septal components.'),
('31547581-c4d8-51b2-aeb7-c88ff11c4695'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The interventricular septum consists of a:', '["Large muscular part + small membranous part (superior)","Equal parts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only muscular mediated by specific receptor activation","membranous part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only membranous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large muscular part + small membranous part (superior)', 'The interventricular septum consists of a large thick muscular part and a small membranous part located superiorly just below the aortic valve. It is not purely muscular, purely membranous, or equally divided.'),
('71ad765e-4bb6-5a96-89a2-abb0ccc03a38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Heart Pericardium: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('06a23459-4b58-5407-9046-457797cb0c77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The base of the heart is the:', '["Right border resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior aspect mainly left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior aspect mainly left atrium', 'The base of the heart is its posterior aspect, formed mainly by the left atrium with a small contribution from the right atrium. The apex, anterior, inferior, and right border are distinct surfaces.'),
('a4368e50-8fe5-548f-9a4e-cc9ce4d6672b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The sternocostal (anterior) surface of the heart is formed by the:', '["Both ventricles equally resulting in decreased functional activity","LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LV mediated by specific receptor activation","Both atria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right atrium and right ventricle (mostly RV)"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium and right ventricle (mostly RV)', 'The sternocostal (anterior) surface is formed mostly by the right ventricle, with the right atrium contributing on the right side. The left ventricle and atria do not dominate this surface.'),
('b7c1c8e4-e581-5477-8fff-5203bb8f20e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'The diaphragmatic (inferior) surface of the heart is formed mainly by the:', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","Both atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RA mediated by specific receptor activation","RV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle (partly RV near apex)"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle (partly RV near apex)', 'The diaphragmatic (inferior) surface is formed mainly by the left ventricle, with a small part of the right ventricle near the apex. The atria contribute little or nothing to this surface.'),
('038d0876-5153-53b9-8fa0-52efe3bba29b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: left border of heart:?', '["RV resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LA mediated by specific receptor activation","Left ventricle (and left auricle above)","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle (and left auricle above)', 'The left border of the heart is formed mainly by the left ventricle, with the left auricle contributing superiorly. The right-sided chambers, pulmonary trunk, and left atrium do not form this border.'),
('9e74a5d2-0183-52ca-9cc8-7a7e8a44017c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: right border of heart:?', '["SVC","LV","Right atrium","LA","RV"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The right border of the heart is formed almost entirely by the right atrium, which receives the SVC above and the IVC below. None of the ventricular, left atrial, or venous options form this border.'),
('388f0220-a278-50ee-b7ad-9dfb78b11c42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: inferior border of heart:?', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","Both atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right ventricle (mostly) and left ventricle (near apex)","All chambers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricle (mostly) and left ventricle (near apex)', 'The inferior (diaphragmatic) border of the heart is formed mainly by the right ventricle, with a small contribution from the left ventricle near the apex. The atria lie above and do not form this border.'),
('59aeece1-f605-5e9e-9cba-65c84f9726b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: ligamentum arteriosum:?', '["Pericardial ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous cord (remnant of ductus arteriosus) between pulmonary trunk and aorta","Foramen ovale remnant mediated by specific receptor activation","Coronary vessel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous cord (remnant of ductus arteriosus) between pulmonary trunk and aorta', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus, connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aortic arch. Pericardial ligaments and the foramen ovale remnant are unrelated structures.'),
('45b9060f-e1df-53eb-803b-64f01937cb4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Foramen ovale becomes:'' is:', '["Coronary sinus","Ligamentum arteriosum","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum teres","Ductus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis', 'After birth the foramen ovale closes, leaving the fossa ovalis as a shallow depression on the interatrial septum. The ligamentum arteriosum, ligamentum teres, and ductus venosus are remnants of other fetal vessels.'),
('0ecf6b1f-311f-5505-a05e-dd59225d5ed2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: ductus arteriosus becomes:?', '["Ligamentum teres","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum venosum","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum arteriosum"]'::jsonb, 'Ligamentum arteriosum', 'The ductus arteriosus obliterates after birth to form the ligamentum arteriosum between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. The fossa ovalis and the ligamenta teres and venosum arise from other fetal structures.'),
('231e22e6-18fe-5780-9dfb-357682aed098'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: heart wall layers:?', '["2","5","3: epicardium","myocardium","endocardium 4 1"]'::jsonb, '3: epicardium', 'The heart wall is composed of three layers: an outer epicardium, a middle myocardium, and an inner endocardium. The other options give incorrect layer counts or list single layers without identifying the three-layered arrangement.'),
('3662a922-cc15-556c-ac2d-59b55aab5aaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: endocardium continuous with:?', '["Pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","Tunica intima of blood vessels","Epicardium mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tunica intima of blood vessels', 'The endocardium lines the cardiac chambers and is continuous with the tunica intima of the great vessels that enter and leave the heart. Pleura, pericardium, epicardium, and myocardium are not continuous with the inner heart lining.'),
('60816080-0cb8-5252-a03a-ade19174a682'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: aortic arch branches:?', '["5 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3: brachiocephalic left common carotid left subclavian"]'::jsonb, '3: brachiocephalic left common carotid left subclavian', 'The aortic arch normally gives three branches: the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid, and the left subclavian arteries. No other branch is counted, so the other numeric options underestimate the arch branches.'),
('94b9f420-9e3d-5d8e-82dc-613ab20d9433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: brachiocephalic trunk divides into:?', '["Right subclavian + right vertebral","Right + left common carotid","Right + left subclavian","Left common carotid + left subclavian","Right common carotid and right subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Right common carotid and right subclavian', 'The brachiocephalic trunk, the first branch of the aortic arch, divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries. It never supplies left-sided vessels, and the right vertebral arises from the right subclavian beyond the division.'),
('fef6593c-2cb2-5e70-a0d9-464c083eba41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''SVC formed by:'' is:', '["Azygos + hemiazygos","Right and left brachiocephalic veins","Right + left subclavian","Right + left IJV","Right + left vertebral"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left brachiocephalic veins', 'The superior vena cava is formed behind the right first costal cartilage by the union of the right and left brachiocephalic veins. The azygos vein drains into the SVC but does not form it, and the subclavian, jugular, and vertebral veins are tributaries of the brachiocephalic veins.'),
('838ad140-bd21-53db-9f3c-bbd537eb86e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: svc drains into:?', '["IVC","Right atrium","LA","RV","LV"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The superior vena cava delivers deoxygenated blood from the head, neck, upper limbs, and thorax into the upper part of the right atrium. The IVC returns blood from below, and the left-sided chambers do not receive its flow.'),
('c6843889-6a2d-5007-a274-79022ca43b90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: ivc drains into:?', '["Right atrium (lower part)","LV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (lower part)', 'The inferior vena cava enters the lower part of the right atrium at the level of the tenth thoracic vertebra. The coronary sinus is a separate venous channel that also opens into the right atrium but does not include IVC flow, and the left-sided chambers are not its target.'),
('1590efb8-c530-5846-bc9b-1dd3b19b8fa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: coronary arteries originate from:?', '["Ascending aorta above sinuses resulting in decreased functional activity","LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sinuses of Valsalva at aortic root","RA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinuses of Valsalva at aortic root', 'The right and left coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses (sinuses of Valsalva) located just above the aortic valve cusps. They never arise from the pulmonary trunk or the cardiac chambers, and the ascending aorta above the sinuses is distal to their origin.'),
('395dbe09-e2f7-5a89-9441-3eccfc2878a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: coronary sinus drains into:?', '["RV resulting in decreased functional activity","Right atrium (between IVC and tricuspid valve)","SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (between IVC and tricuspid valve)', 'The coronary sinus, lying in the posterior atrioventricular groove, opens into the right atrium between the orifice of the IVC and the tricuspid valve. The ventricles, SVC, and left atrium are not its termination site.'),
('be208a8a-1e47-54e5-9eda-136be12eeac7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: thebesian veins:?', '["Azygos resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sinus tributaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Small veins draining myocardium directly into chambers","Cardiac lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small veins draining myocardium directly into chambers', 'Thebesian veins (venae cordis minimae) are small vessels that drain the myocardial wall directly into the cardiac chambers, bypassing the coronary sinus. The azygos vein, pulmonary veins, and cardiac lymphatics belong to different systems.'),
('fb387b26-379e-516e-ad7f-51b88dc5140e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Which artery supplies the SA node in most people'' is:', '["Right coronary artery (in 60% of people)","Left circumflex artery","Left anterior descending artery","Posterior descending artery","Right marginal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (in 60% of people)', 'In about 60% of hearts the SA nodal artery arises from the right coronary artery; in the remaining 40% it comes from the left circumflex artery. The LAD, posterior descending, and right marginal arteries do not usually provide this supply.'),
('8ea4c75a-d628-58d3-a965-ea77b62d8ebc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the heart is located:?', '["In the middle mediastinum","In the posterior mediastinum","In the superior mediastinum","In the anterior mediastinum","In the pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 'In the middle mediastinum', 'The heart with its pericardium occupies the middle mediastinum, lying between the sternum and the vertebral column. The superior mediastinum contains the great vessels and thymus, while the anterior and posterior mediastina contain the thymus remnant and esophagus/aorta respectively, and the heart is not inside the pleural cavity.'),
('a9badaec-17b0-53ca-8763-39f91b2e500f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the pericardium consists of:?', '["Endocardium only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only fibrous layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only serous layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous pericardium and serous pericardium (parietal and visceral layers)","Myocardium and epicardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous pericardium and serous pericardium (parietal and visceral layers)', 'The pericardium comprises an outer fibrous pericardium and an inner serous pericardium with parietal and visceral layers, the visceral layer being the epicardium of the heart. Endocardium and myocardium are heart wall layers, and single-layered options omit components of the pericardium.'),
('dd1d2369-a5e2-5f6f-b3a6-08676eb0dd51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the oblique pericardial sinus is located:?', '["In front of the right ventricle","Anterior to the aorta","Posterior to the heart","between the pulmonary veins","Between the SVC and IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the heart', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is a serous pericardial recess lying behind the heart, between the right and left pulmonary veins. It is not located anterior to the ventricles, in front of the aorta, or between the caval openings.'),
('f9bc5d51-0239-5289-acdc-3170be221ced'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the transverse pericardial sinus is:?', '["Below the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the two ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A passage between the aorta/pulmonary trunk and the atria/SVC","Behind the left atrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In front of the right ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A passage between the aorta/pulmonary trunk and the atria/SVC', 'The transverse pericardial sinus is a horizontal recess of the serous pericardium lying between the aorta and pulmonary trunk in front and the atria with the SVC behind. A finger or clamp passed through it can occlude the great vessels during surgery; none of the other regions match this anatomy.'),
('3e8bfda0-7214-5dd2-9493-8b001bf79c29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the heart wall layers are:?', '["Epicardium (visceral pericardium)","myocardium","endocardium","Pericardium only","Myocardium and endocardium"]'::jsonb, 'endocardium', 'The heart wall has three layers: the epicardium, the myocardium, and the endocardium, which lines the chambers and covers the valves. Pericardium-only or two-layer options are incomplete, and among the single-layer choices endocardium is the inner lining layer.'),
('25dd2e6f-0be3-5a8a-a14e-f27b12880212'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The right atrium receives:'' is:', '["Aorta","Bronchial veins","SVC","IVC","and coronary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'and coronary sinus', 'The right atrium receives the SVC, the IVC, and the coronary sinus, which opens into its posterior wall. The aorta is an outflow of the left ventricle, and bronchial veins drain into the azygos system, not into the right atrium.'),
('b3152049-acc9-56f8-8634-9a8abcb0a341'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the right ventricle pumps blood to:?', '["Left atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary trunk (via the pulmonary valve)","Coronary arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary trunk (via the pulmonary valve)', 'The right ventricle ejects deoxygenated blood through the pulmonary (semilunar) valve into the pulmonary trunk, which carries it to the lungs. Blood reaches the aorta, left atrium, and left ventricle through the left heart, not from the right ventricular outlet.'),
('92b2e6ca-491a-58c2-a97a-079d765a1f2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the left atrium receives:?', '["Four pulmonary veins (two from each lung)","Bronchial veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","SVC and IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Four pulmonary veins (two from each lung)', 'The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from four pulmonary veins, two from each lung. The SVC, IVC, and coronary sinus drain into the right atrium, and the aorta is a ventricular outflow rather than an atrial inflow.'),
('b6d528ab-7319-5bfa-b3b5-6e9a872cc4db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the left ventricle pumps blood to:?', '["Right atrium","Aorta (via the aortic valve)","Pulmonary trunk","Left atrium","Right ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (via the aortic valve)', 'The left ventricle pumps oxygenated blood through the aortic semilunar valve into the aorta for systemic distribution. The pulmonary trunk is the outlet of the right ventricle, and blood does not pass to the atria or right ventricle from the left ventricle.'),
('bcd6c133-26dc-5709-8eed-1106d432e31a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the interatrial septum contains:?', '["Trabeculae carneae","Papillary muscles","Chordae tendineae","Crista terminalis","Fossa ovalis (remnant of foramen ovale)"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis (remnant of foramen ovale)', 'The interatrial septum bears the fossa ovalis, the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale. Trabeculae carneae, papillary muscles, and chordae tendineae are ventricular structures, and the crista terminalis lies on the lateral wall of the right atrium, not on the septum.'),
('6b4cd4e4-a808-5de8-bce3-ab61426dcc63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the interventricular septum consists of:?', '["Connective tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscular part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only membranous part mediated by specific receptor activation","Fatty tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscular part (thick) and membranous part (thin)"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular part (thick) and membranous part (thin)', 'The interventricular septum consists of a large thick muscular portion and a small thin membranous portion adjacent to the aortic valve. Options naming only one part or non-septal tissues are incomplete descriptions.'),
('2dd9146b-9603-544c-8e00-7b802400dad5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The sinoatrial (SA) node is located:'' is:', '["In the left ventricle","In the AV septum","In the right atrium","at the junction of SVC and right atrial appendage (sulcus terminalis)","In the interventricular septum"]'::jsonb, 'In the right atrium', 'The SA node, the cardiac pacemaker, lies in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the SVC. The ventricles, AV septum, and other atrial sites do not host the pacemaker.'),
('0b928044-1a31-5928-983d-24c5c44f6568'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the atrioventricular (av) node is located:?', '["In the right ventricle","In the left atrium","In the interventricular septum","In the SA node","In the inferior part of the interatrial septum"]'::jsonb, 'In the inferior part of the interatrial septum', 'The AV node lies at the lower (inferior) end of the interatrial septum, just above the opening of the coronary sinus. The ventricles, left atrium, and SA node are not its location.'),
('9d31e309-3c7e-5b24-9988-e7f79a5a34e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the av bundle (bundle of his) divides into:?', '["Medial and lateral branches resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior and inferior branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SA and AV fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Right and left bundle branches (in the interventricular septum)","Anterior and posterior fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left bundle branches (in the interventricular septum)', 'The AV bundle (of His) descends through the interventricular septum and divides into right and left bundle branches, which ramify as Purkinje fibers. No medial/lateral or superior/inferior division exists in the normal conducting system.'),
('6ac5cc39-1749-579a-b6b7-9c54f5bfab72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the coronary arteries arise from:?', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta (right and left coronary sinuses of Valsalva)","Left ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Right ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (right and left coronary sinuses of Valsalva)', 'The right and left coronary arteries arise from the right and left aortic sinuses of Valsalva just above the aortic valve. The coronary sinus is a venous channel, and neither the pulmonary trunk nor the ventricles gives origin to these arteries.'),
('32e90206-42b6-590d-a027-b65ac7257db0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the right coronary artery supplies:?', '["The anterior septum","The apex of the heart","The lateral wall of left ventricle","Right ventricle","SA node (60%)"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricle', 'The right coronary artery runs in the right atrioventricular groove and principally supplies the right ventricle; in most hearts it also gives the SA and AV nodal arteries. The anterior septum, apex, and lateral left ventricular wall are supplied by left coronary branches.'),
('f13b23c7-b2ad-5f74-a566-dce08283cbcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the left anterior descending (lad) artery supplies:?', '["Anterior interventricular septum","anterior left ventricle","apex","Posterior septum","SA node"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular septum', 'The LAD artery descends in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum, the anterior left ventricular wall, and the apex. The posterior septum and SA node receive blood from the right coronary system.'),
('d44ba80f-5cec-50e2-8cfb-53877d9a3c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The circumflex artery supplies:'' is:', '["Lateral and posterior left ventricle","Posterior septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior septum mediated by specific receptor activation","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and posterior left ventricle', 'The circumflex artery passes in the left atrioventricular groove and supplies the lateral and posterior walls of the left ventricle (and often the left atrium). The septal regions and SA node are supplied by LAD and right coronary branches.'),
('7823468e-a3a0-5014-8fd6-4f60e2a764cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the coronary sinus drains into:?', '["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Superior vena cava","Right ventricle","Right atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The coronary sinus, the main venous channel of the heart, drains into the right atrium. The left atrium, ventricles, and SVC are not its termination.'),
('ceb840f1-9d76-572a-8cc3-b364da657640'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the cardiac valves include:?', '["Mitral and tricuspid","Aortic and pulmonary","Only AV valves","Only semilunar valves","Tricuspid (right AV)"]'::jsonb, 'Tricuspid (right AV)', 'The four cardiac valves are the tricuspid and mitral (AV valves) plus the aortic and pulmonary (semilunar valves); the tricuspid guards the right atrioventricular orifice. Options listing only one valve type or a single pair are incomplete descriptions of the valve set.'),
('35db5307-af37-5faa-9e6f-20e1af52dbd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the mitral valve is located:?', '["Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left atrium and left ventricle","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk","In the IV septum"]'::jsonb, 'Between the left atrium and left ventricle', 'The mitral (bicuspid) valve guards the left atrioventricular orifice between the left atrium and the left ventricle. The aortic, tricuspid, and pulmonary openings are separate semilunar and AV sites.'),
('f447dc46-b591-5d55-976e-72447e799fb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the tricuspid valve is located:?', '["Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk","In the IV septum","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the left atrium and left ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Between the right atrium and right ventricle', 'The tricuspid valve is the right atrioventricular valve, situated between the right atrium and the right ventricle. The pulmonary, septal, and left-sided sites do not correspond to its position.'),
('079175c6-4311-5519-832e-f683ddf88864'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the aortic valve is located:?', '["Between the left atrium and left ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","In the coronary sinus","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Between the left ventricle and aorta', 'The aortic (semilunar) valve is located at the outlet of the left ventricle into the aorta. It is not an atrioventricular valve, and it does not lie at the pulmonary opening or in the coronary sinus.'),
('ca504b43-e2f1-5cb7-865d-edfb0c4b909e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The pulmonary valve is located:'' is:', '["Between the left atrium and left ventricle","In the IV septum","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk', 'The pulmonary (semilunar) valve guards the outlet of the right ventricle into the pulmonary trunk. The AV valve sites and the left ventricular outlet to the aorta are incorrect locations.'),
('ddeb1ace-0163-5c0b-8776-088c48d44f70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: chordae tendineae connect:?', '["Papillary muscles to the AV valve cusps (prevent prolapse)","Ventricles to atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta to ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","SA node to AV node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atria to ventricles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary muscles to the AV valve cusps (prevent prolapse)', 'Chordae tendineae are thin tendinous cords that anchor the AV valve cusps to the papillary muscles, preventing cusp prolapse into the atria during ventricular systole. They do not connect chambers, great vessels, or the conducting system.'),
('8fa207f3-7d25-53c8-96b9-30ccf859b1eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: papillary muscles arise from:?', '["Atrial wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Chordae tendineae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventricular wall (trabeculae carneae)","Aortic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valve cusps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular wall (trabeculae carneae)', 'Papillary muscles are conical projections of the ventricular wall, formed from modified trabeculae carneae, and they give attachment to the chordae tendineae. They do not originate from the atria, aorta, chordae, or the valve leaflets themselves.'),
('a798f468-e390-5f51-a5b8-b287e3369518'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the fossa ovalis is a remnant of:?', '["Umbilical vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductus venosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ductus arteriosus mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale (fetal shunt between right and left atria)","Ligamentum arteriosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen ovale (fetal shunt between right and left atria)', 'The fossa ovalis on the interatrial septum is the postnatal remnant of the foramen ovale, the fetal shunt between the atria. The umbilical vein forms the ligamentum teres, the ductus venosus the ligamentum venosum, and the ductus arteriosus the ligamentum arteriosum.'),
('ddb9c35c-b2dc-5976-b32d-d65ede2e5f60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the ligamentum arteriosum is a remnant of:?', '["Umbilical vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductus arteriosus (fetal shunt between pulmonary trunk and aorta)","Umbilical artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ductus venosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ductus arteriosus (fetal shunt between pulmonary trunk and aorta)', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous remnant of the ductus arteriosus, the fetal channel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. The umbilical vein and arteries form the ligamentum teres and medial umbilical ligaments, the ductus venosus forms the ligamentum venosum, and the foramen ovale leaves the fossa ovalis.'),
('552e5602-cdad-50f6-8890-3624610dd7da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the conducting system of the heart includes:?', '["Cardiac nerves","Valves","Pericardium","SA node","AV node"]'::jsonb, 'SA node', 'The cardiac conducting system consists of the SA node, AV node, AV bundle, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers, with the SA node as the primary pacemaker. Nerves, valves, and the pericardium are not conducting elements.'),
('4732b4cd-a0f8-5f4c-9c7f-656eb4b5ad05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The vagus nerve provides:'' is:', '["innervation resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor to valves mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic innervation to the heart (decreases heart rate)","Sensory only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic innervation to the heart (decreases heart rate)', 'The vagus nerve (CN X) supplies parasympathetic fibers to the heart, chiefly through the SA and AV nodes, slowing the heart rate. Sympathetic acceleration and motor innervation of the valves are not vagal functions.'),
('600ffebf-5990-5cf0-b10d-569780aedb9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the sa node is located in:?', '["Interventricular septum","Right ventricle","Right atrium near SVC opening","AV node","Left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium near SVC opening', 'The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium just lateral to the opening of the SVC, at the upper end of the sulcus terminalis. The interventricular septum, ventricles, left atrium, and AV node are not its location.'),
('e8e6d561-756d-5e7a-aecb-25eb38f6d054'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the av node is located in:?', '["Interventricular septum resulting in decreased functional activity","Right ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left atrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Interatrial septum near coronary sinus","SA node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interatrial septum near coronary sinus', 'The AV node is situated in the lower part of the interatrial septum, just above and anterior to the opening of the coronary sinus. The ventricles, left atrium, and interventricular septum do not contain the AV node, and the SA node is a separate structure.'),
('c3907ba2-564c-5a00-a9e5-3ca39672fd78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the left anterior descending artery supplies:?', '["SA node","Lateral wall of LV","Right ventricle","Anterior LV and septum","Posterior LV"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior LV and septum', 'The left anterior descending artery runs in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle and the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. The SA node, lateral/posterior LV, and right ventricle are supplied by the right coronary or circumflex arteries.'),
('6e6dff9b-0d3d-584c-83a0-9ff3651f68c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the right coronary artery typically supplies:', '["Septum","LV lateral wall","SA node, AV node, RV","Anterior LV","Circumflex territory"]'::jsonb, 'SA node, AV node, RV', 'The right coronary artery runs in the right atrioventricular groove and in most hearts gives rise to the arterial supply of the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes as well as the right ventricular free wall. The anteroseptal region and anterior LV receive blood from the LAD (left anterior descending artery), while the LV lateral wall and circumflex territory are served by the left circumflex artery.'),
('d538a34d-0d49-528e-be7d-6b60768c68b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: thebesian veins drain into:', '["Coronary sinus","Middle cardiac vein","Cardiac chambers directly","Great cardiac vein","Small cardiac vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac chambers directly', 'Thebesian veins (venae cordis minimae) are small channels that drain directly from the cardiac wall into the chambers of the heart rather than into the coronary sinus. The coronary sinus and the great, middle, and small cardiac veins all belong to the major coronary venous pathway that returns blood to the right atrium.'),
('5406cb17-68a1-529c-a6f4-9bf89f40d10e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The pericardial cavity contains:'' is:', '["Lymph","Pus","Serous fluid","Air","Blood"]'::jsonb, 'Serous fluid', 'The pericardial cavity normally holds a small amount of serous fluid that lubricates the visceral and parietal serous layers and reduces friction during cardiac movement. Blood or pus would reflect hemorrhage or infection, and air or lymph accumulation is not the normal content.'),
('c22fd72a-98a0-531f-b4c4-e9a62be788f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the heart is located at:', '["In the middle mediastinum","In the posterior mediastinum","In the superior mediastinum","In the anterior mediastinum","In the pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 'In the middle mediastinum', 'The heart and pericardium occupy the middle mediastinum, which lies between the superior and inferior mediastinal compartments and contains the pericardial sac. The posterior, anterior, and superior mediastina lie outside the heart and are not its location inside the pleural cavities.'),
('0a9ef2dd-5b5b-5505-a111-a8997b7eb7f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: pericardium layers:', '["Fibrous (outer) + serous (parietal + visceral)","Only serous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fibrous mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous + serous + muscular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serous only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous (outer) + serous (parietal + visceral)', 'The pericardium consists of an outer tough fibrous layer reinforced by the central tendon of the diaphragm plus an inner serous sac made of parietal and visceral layers; the visceral layer is the epicardium. The serous pericardium alone is not a complete description of the pericardium, and there is no muscular layer.'),
('e6dad423-728b-5d9d-a9b4-219b32a6edd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: fibrous pericardium attached to:', '["Only heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Only great vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","Only diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central tendon of diaphragm sternum great vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Central tendon of diaphragm sternum great vessels', 'The fibrous pericardium is anchored by fibrous bands to the central tendon of the diaphragm below, to the sternum, and to the tunica adventitia of the great vessels above. These attachments stabilize the heart; it is not attached to only a single structure.'),
('49dd67b3-8508-5590-a33e-70af735d0f17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: serous pericardium:', '["Parietal (lining fibrous) and visceral (epicardium)","Three layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parietal mediated by specific receptor activation","Both separate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visceral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal (lining fibrous) and visceral (epicardium)', 'The serous pericardium is a single continuous membrane with a parietal layer lining the fibrous pericardium and a visceral layer covering the heart surface as the epicardium. It comprises exactly these two layers rather than three or one.'),
('7900f40e-39ac-58e2-bd71-98835ec1a4d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: pericardial cavity:', '["Mediastinal space resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Space between parietal and visceral serous pericardium","Pleural space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Outside fibrous pericardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Space between parietal and visceral serous pericardium', 'The pericardial cavity is the potential space between the parietal and visceral serous layers of the pericardium and normally contains a thin film of serous fluid. It is not a pleural cavity, a general mediastinal space, or a region outside the fibrous pericardium.'),
('47b97dd0-0707-5194-b114-b49b928d1a4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Transverse pericardial sinus:'' is:', '["Between aorta/pulmonary trunk anteriorly and atria/SVC posteriorly","Behind LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along RA mediated by specific receptor activation","At apex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between aorta/pulmonary trunk anteriorly and atria/SVC posteriorly', 'The transverse pericardial sinus is the horizontal passage between the arterial pole (aorta and pulmonary trunk) anteriorly and the atria, SVC, and pulmonary veins posteriorly. A finger passed through this sinus helps clamp the great vessels during cardiac surgery; the oblique sinus is the recess behind the left atrium.'),
('b6a92af3-3aa6-5fae-a396-ece4cf1070bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: oblique pericardial sinus:', '["Front of RA","Blind-ended recess behind left atrium","between pulmonary veins","Along RV","Between aorta and pulmonary trunk At apex"]'::jsonb, 'Blind-ended recess behind left atrium', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is a blind-ended sac of pericardial cavity located behind the left atrium and bounded by the reflections around the pulmonary veins and IVC. It is distinct from the transverse sinus, which lies in front of the atria between the arterial trunks.'),
('71da9e7f-4709-5ea2-82d5-7930fb549135'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: heart chambers:', '["3 resulting in decreased functional activity","6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","4 (RA, RV, LA, LV)","2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 (RA, RV, LA, LV)', 'The heart has four chambers: the right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, and left ventricle. Counting any other number of chambers does not reflect the actual structure of the human heart.'),
('3d5b0943-079c-5cf6-a6db-8aee828a4887'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: right atrium receives:', '["Pulmonary artery","Aorta","SVC","IVC","coronary sinus Pulmonary veins LV"]'::jsonb, 'SVC', 'The right atrium receives systemic venous blood from the superior vena cava and inferior vena cava plus blood from the coronary sinus. The aorta and pulmonary artery carry blood away from the heart, and the pulmonary veins drain into the left atrium, so the SVC is an inflow vessel of the right atrium.'),
('4077d48e-e58b-586a-b310-24e3680f559b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: right auricle:', '["Valve resulting in decreased functional activity","RV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interatrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","Small ear-shaped appendage of RA","Crista terminalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small ear-shaped appendage of RA', 'The right auricle is a small ear-shaped muscular appendage projecting from the anterior part of the right atrium. It is not a valve, part of the ventricular wall, or the interatrial septum.'),
('761df580-0c95-5de4-91c4-95df348de0ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: crista terminalis:', '["IV septum resulting in decreased functional activity","AV node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscular ridge in RA between sinus venarum and auricle","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular ridge in RA between sinus venarum and auricle', 'The crista terminalis is a vertical muscular ridge on the lateral wall of the right atrium that separates the smooth sinus venarum posteriorly from the rough auricle with its pectinate muscles anteriorly. It is not a valve, part of the septa, or a nodal structure.'),
('aeb652a4-9f96-505b-ac19-fc40378445a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Sinus venarum:'' is:', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth posterior RA derived from sinus venosus","Auricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Rough anterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interatrial septum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth posterior RA derived from sinus venosus', 'The sinus venarum is the smooth-walled posterior portion of the right atrium that develops from the embryonic sinus venosus; the SVC, IVC, and coronary sinus open into it. It contrasts with the rough, muscular anterior auricle containing pectinate muscles.'),
('c5a052b1-cdb8-5a05-a6e3-d78d012ea02a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: fossa ovalis:', '["Depression in interatrial septum (remnant of foramen ovale)","SA node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus opening mediated by specific receptor activation","RA valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depression in interatrial septum (remnant of foramen ovale)', 'The fossa ovalis is a shallow oval depression on the right side of the interatrial septum representing the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale after closure of its valve. It is not the SA or AV node and is unrelated to the coronary sinus opening.'),
('a2c65592-1ac2-5736-b39f-66469371c836'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: tricuspid valve cusps:', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (anterior, posterior, septal)","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (anterior, posterior, septal)', 'The tricuspid valve has three leaflets named anterior, posterior, and septal, which attach to the fibrous ring between the right atrium and right ventricle. It is the cusp number (3) that justifies its name, unlike the bicuspid mitral valve.'),
('7be6a80e-8464-56eb-8a31-c59bce8b2f20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: right ventricle has:', '["Trabeculae carneae","papillary muscles","chordae tendineae moderator band","papillary muscles Smooth walls","trabeculae chordae"]'::jsonb, 'Trabeculae carneae', 'The right ventricle is typically trabeculated, filled with interconnected muscular ridges (trabeculae carneae) that distinguish it from the smooth sinus venarum of the atrium. The moderator band, papillary muscles, and chordae tendineae are also normal RV features, but the defining structural characteristic asked here is the trabeculae carneae.'),
('d1f22223-7bc6-5973-9655-dacd75bb1331'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: moderator band:', '["Muscular band in RV conducting RBB to anterior papillary muscle","Chordae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LV trabecula mediated by specific receptor activation","RV valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Papillary muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular band in RV conducting RBB to anterior papillary muscle', 'The moderator band is a muscular bridge crossing the right ventricular cavity that carries the right bundle branch to the base of the anterior papillary muscle, ensuring coordinated contraction of that muscle. It is not a chorda, valve, or a left ventricular trabecula.'),
('4f3e0be1-e457-5028-957d-adfe3dd0288e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: conus arteriosus:', '["Smooth outflow tract of RV to pulmonary trunk","LV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RV inflow mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth outflow tract of RV to pulmonary trunk', 'The conus arteriosus (infundibulum) is the smooth funnel-shaped outflow tract of the right ventricle leading up to the pulmonary trunk, with the pulmonary valve at its upper end. It is not the left ventricle, the RV inflow, or the aorta.'),
('27203a5c-21a3-5398-bdf2-fe51bed582b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Pulmonary valve cusps:'' is:', '["1 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 semilunar (anterior, left, right)"]'::jsonb, '3 semilunar (anterior, left, right)', 'The pulmonary valve is a semilunar valve with three cusps: anterior, left, and right, located at the junction of the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk. The three-cusp semilunar pattern also characterizes the aortic valve.'),
('36c754cb-5782-5318-8f6c-caf15ebe35e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: left atrium receives:', '["SVC","Aorta","Coronary sinus","IVC","4 pulmonary veins"]'::jsonb, '4 pulmonary veins', 'The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the four pulmonary veins (two right and two left). The SVC and IVC drain into the right atrium, the coronary sinus drains into the right atrium, and the aorta carries blood away from the heart.'),
('e80b42c6-d24f-54a9-826a-952fe5635446'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: mitral valve cusps:', '["4 resulting in decreased functional activity","2 (anterior and posterior)","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (anterior and posterior)', 'The mitral (bicuspid) valve has two leaflets: a larger anterior cusp and a smaller posterior cusp. Its two leaflets account for the name bicuspid valve, in contrast to the three cusps of the tricuspid valve.'),
('8c6b5097-4e31-5523-b208-4e098f12879f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: left ventricle:', '["Thickest myocardium","trabeculae carneae","2 large papillary muscles","trabeculae","Moderator band Thinner than RV papillary muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Thickest myocardium', 'The left ventricle has the thickest myocardium of all cardiac chambers because it must generate high systemic pressure. It also contains trabeculae carneae and two papillary muscles, but the outstanding structural feature is its thickened muscular wall.'),
('72284703-76de-533e-8cd2-5f1c86053d0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: aortic valve cusps:', '["3 semilunar (left, right, posterior/non-coronary)","2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 semilunar (left, right, posterior/non-coronary)', 'The aortic valve has three semilunar cusps named left, right, and posterior (non-coronary); the left and right coronary arteries arise above the corresponding cusps in the aortic sinuses. The posterior cusp carries no coronary ostium.'),
('6be0f166-c2ba-50e3-918c-25b1c83046e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: sinuses of valsalva:', '["Atrial appendages resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic root dilations behind cusps; coronary arteries originate from them","Valves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chordae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic root dilations behind cusps; coronary arteries originate from them', 'The sinuses of Valsalva are three dilations of the aortic root situated behind the aortic valve cusps; the left and right coronary arteries arise from the corresponding sinuses. They are not atrial appendages, ventricles, or valve tissue.'),
('0e00fb9e-8b43-5b84-bc91-0c79fc40b786'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Conducting system starts at:'' is:', '["Bundle of His","Bundle branches","SA node in RA","AV node","Purkinje fibers"]'::jsonb, 'SA node in RA', 'The cardiac conduction system begins at the sinoatrial node in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the SVC, acting as the primary pacemaker. The AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers conduct impulses downstream from it.'),
('ddcb4101-cefb-5c4b-a754-920b53e676ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: SA node located:', '["AV junction resulting in decreased functional activity","RA at junction of SVC and right auricle (crista terminalis)","Interatrial septum mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RA at junction of SVC and right auricle (crista terminalis)', 'The sinoatrial node lies in the posterolateral wall of the right atrium at the junction of the SVC and the right auricle, just lateral to the upper end of the crista terminalis. It is not placed in the interatrial septum, left atrium, or ventricle.'),
('d854b8e6-daaf-5b87-b1bc-615a5ab3a45a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: SA nodal artery from:', '["Marginal resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior descending accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Right coronary artery (60%) or left circumflex (40%)","Circumflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (60%) or left circumflex (40%)', 'The SA nodal artery usually arises from the proximal right coronary artery in about 60% of hearts and from the left circumflex artery in about 40%. It courses through the atrial musculature to reach the SA node, a pattern not explained by the LAD, marginal, or posterior descending branches.'),
('ed416aba-0085-5e36-8860-abd35fff29b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: AV node located:', '["LV resulting in decreased functional activity","RV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apex mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interatrial septum above tricuspid valve near coronary sinus opening"]'::jsonb, 'Interatrial septum above tricuspid valve near coronary sinus opening', 'The atrioventricular node lies in the posteromedial wall of the right atrium within the interatrial septum, just above the septal cusp of the tricuspid valve and near the opening of the coronary sinus. It is not located in the ventricles, apex, or left atrium.'),
('59740ae6-23a6-57c6-921a-4b99b9b35f8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: AV nodal artery from:', '["Left coronary","Circumflex","Right coronary artery (85-90%)","LAD","Posterior descending"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (85-90%)', 'In most hearts (85-90%) the AV nodal artery branches from the right coronary artery at the crux of the heart. Occlusion of this branch can produce AV block even when the rest of the right coronary territory is spared.'),
('02c1301e-f4e9-586c-a1d9-fc1c1f0b6322'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: AV bundle of His:', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","SA node to AV node accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct to ventricular walls mediated by specific receptor activation","RA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From AV node through IV septum divides into bundle branches"]'::jsonb, 'From AV node through IV septum divides into bundle branches', 'The bundle of His originates from the AV node, pierces the fibrous skeleton, and descends through the interventricular septum where it divides into right and left bundle branches. It does not connect directly to the ventricular walls or connect the SA node to the AV node.'),
('0a6e8f09-9032-5d13-800e-d95daf853cfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Right bundle branch:'' is:', '["To LV","To SA node","To RV","partly via moderator band","To atria Through interatrial septum"]'::jsonb, 'To RV', 'The right bundle branch descends on the right side of the interventricular septum and travels to the right ventricle, passing partly through the moderator band to reach the anterior papillary muscle. It supplies the right ventricular wall rather than the left ventricle or atria.'),
('583e02af-3b48-5eb2-b7ec-2889af536b36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: left bundle branch divides into:', '["Superior and inferior","Single","Medial and lateral","Anterior and posterior fascicles","Multiple small"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior fascicles', 'The left bundle branch divides into two main fascicles: the thinner anterior (superior) fascicle and the thicker posterior (inferior) fascicle, which spread through the left ventricular wall. Hemiblocks of these fascicles produce characteristic ECG axis shifts.'),
('85d7e945-6002-55f0-853d-fd979df6a24e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: purkinje fibers:', '["Endothelial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Connective tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nerve cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Ordinary muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Modified cardiac muscle for rapid impulse conduction"]'::jsonb, 'Modified cardiac muscle for rapid impulse conduction', 'Purkinje fibers are large, specialized cardiac muscle fibers that conduct electrical impulses rapidly from the bundle branches to the ventricular myocardium. They are not endothelial cells, connective tissue, ordinary skeletal muscle, or nerve cells.'),
('ade312ba-68d6-520f-8fd8-5b034d3a8c6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: right coronary artery from:', '["Ascending aorta","Right aortic sinus (anterior)","Posterior aortic sinus","Left aortic sinus","Pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Right aortic sinus (anterior)', 'The right coronary artery arises from the anterior (right) aortic sinus, immediately above the right cusp of the aortic valve. The ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk are not points of origin, and the left aortic sinus gives rise to the left coronary artery.'),
('7cde887f-1233-5f4b-9870-d6047ca303f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: left coronary artery from:', '["Pulmonary trunk","Right aortic sinus","Left aortic sinus (left posterior)","Posterior aortic sinus","Ascending aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Left aortic sinus (left posterior)', 'The left coronary artery arises from the left posterior aortic sinus, immediately above the left cusp of the aortic valve. It does not arise from the pulmonary trunk, the right aortic sinus, or the posterior (non-coronary) sinus.'),
('67e3a9a4-b070-5c32-a9bb-3bad1fbcc913'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: left coronary divides into:', '["Posterior interventricular + diagonal","Left marginal + LAD","LAD (anterior interventricular) and circumflex","Circumflex + marginal","Right marginal + PDA"]'::jsonb, 'LAD (anterior interventricular) and circumflex', 'After a short main trunk, the left coronary artery divides into the left anterior descending (anterior interventricular) artery and the circumflex artery. The posterior interventricular artery and right marginal artery are branches of the right coronary system.'),
('28aefc99-7556-508b-9604-5b91b3b3655b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''LAD runs:'' is:', '["Posterior interventricular groove","Right coronary sulcus","Anterior interventricular groove to apex","Coronary sulcus","Left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular groove to apex', 'The left anterior descending artery descends in the anterior interventricular groove along the sternocostal surface of the heart toward the apex. The posterior interventricular artery runs in the posterior groove, and the coronary sulcus is occupied by the right coronary and circumflex arteries.'),
('826c74c1-1541-5a74-b82f-9332c0075352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: circumflex artery runs:', '["Anterior interventricular groove","Left AV (coronary) groove","Posterior interventricular groove","Right margin","SA node"]'::jsonb, 'Left AV (coronary) groove', 'The circumflex artery courses leftward in the left atrioventricular (coronary) groove around the coronary sulcus. It does not run in the interventricular grooves, along the right margin, or toward the SA node.'),
('c8d620df-d31e-5a96-b22a-641c15f2e785'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: PDA (posterior interventricular) usually from:', '["Left marginal resulting in decreased functional activity","Left coronary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LAD mediated by specific receptor activation","Circumflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right coronary artery (right-dominant 80%)"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (right-dominant 80%)', 'In about 80% of hearts the right coronary artery is dominant, crosses the crux, and gives off the posterior interventricular (PDA) artery in the posterior groove. In left-dominant hearts the circumflex provides it, but the usual source is the right coronary artery.'),
('72fb1ada-2724-56ee-9f5b-e18ae26dd848'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: coronary sinus located:', '["Anterior sulcus","LA","Posterior coronary (AV) sulcus","drains to RA","RV Interventricular groove"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior coronary (AV) sulcus', 'The coronary sinus runs transversely in the posterior portion of the coronary (atrioventricular) sulcus on the posterior surface of the heart and drains into the right atrium. It is not located in the anterior sulcus, the interventricular groove, or the left atrium.'),
('776e81be-011b-5ced-bdc2-ff03815da811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: great cardiac vein runs:', '["Right AV groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sulcus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior interventricular groove mediated by specific receptor activation","Apex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior interventricular groove with LAD"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular groove with LAD', 'The great cardiac vein ascends in the anterior interventricular groove alongside the LAD, then turns left to join the coronary sinus. It does not run in the posterior interventricular groove, right AV groove, or apex.'),
('bc9cde7f-d6de-5a33-8d3e-fe7b6c720fe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: middle cardiac vein runs:', '["Right AV groove resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sulcus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior interventricular groove mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior interventricular groove with PDA","Apex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior interventricular groove with PDA', 'The middle cardiac vein runs upward in the posterior interventricular groove beside the posterior interventricular artery and drains into the coronary sinus near its termination. The anterior interventricular groove carries the great cardiac vein.'),
('31decaf0-b20b-5b30-8980-6a905f171be3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Small cardiac vein runs:'' is:', '["Posterior groove","Right AV groove with marginal artery","To RA directly","Anterior interventricular","Left AV groove"]'::jsonb, 'Right AV groove with marginal artery', 'The small cardiac vein runs in the right atrioventricular groove alongside the right marginal artery and drains into the coronary sinus. It does not occupy the posterior groove, the left AV groove, or the anterior interventricular groove.'),
('e6e1d842-2c5d-530a-bff5-d2b112c768c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: coronary arteries fill during:', '["Both equally","Atrial relaxation","Systole (contraction)","Diastole (ventricular relaxation)","Atrial contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Diastole (ventricular relaxation)', 'Coronary blood flow peaks during ventricular diastole because the myocardium relaxes and the coronary vessels are no longer compressed by systolic contraction. During systole the intramural coronary arteries are squeezed, so filling is reduced rather than maximal.'),
('3c3e8c71-293d-51d0-ba39-ac1a3bfed0b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: interatrial septum contains:', '["Moderator band","Fossa ovalis and its valve","SA node","AV node","Papillary muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis and its valve', 'The interatrial septum contains the fossa ovalis on its right side, bounded by the prominent limbus, with the valve of the foramen ovale forming its floor. The moderator band is in the right ventricle, and the papillary muscles are ventricular structures.'),
('b91bbb98-9633-57c6-b525-52611496af35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: interventricular septum:', '["Large muscular part + small membranous part (superior)","Equal parts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only muscular mediated by specific receptor activation","membranous part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only membranous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large muscular part + small membranous part (superior)', 'The interventricular septum is mostly thick muscle forming the large inferior portion, with a small membranous part superiority near the aortic valve where most congenital septal defects occur. It is not purely muscular or purely membranous and the parts are not equal.'),
('79375e9a-27fa-5a69-9609-6012d2a1de82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: heart apex:', '["Both ventricles resulting in decreased functional activity","Right ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2nd ICS mediated by specific receptor activation","Base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle at 5th ICS left midclavicular line"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle at 5th ICS left midclavicular line', 'The apex of the heart is formed by the left ventricle and projects downward, forward, and to the left, reaching the fifth intercostal space in the left midclavicular line, where the apex beat is felt. It is not formed by the right ventricle or the base.'),
('65cd9d04-0b92-597c-a701-54808e1a9d21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: heart base:', '["Right border resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior aspect mainly left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior aspect mainly left atrium', 'The base of the heart is its posterior surface, formed mainly by the left atrium with contributions from the right atrium and the proximal great veins; the esophagus and descending aorta lie behind it. It faces posteriorly and is not the apex or an inferior surface.'),
('28f837a0-133c-5dca-971b-6c6d851d9580'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Sternocostal (anterior) surface:'' is: [variant 251]', '["Both ventricles equally resulting in decreased functional activity","LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LV mediated by specific receptor activation","Both atria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right atrium and right ventricle (mostly RV)"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium and right ventricle (mostly RV)', 'The sternocostal (anterior) surface of the heart is formed mainly by the right ventricle, with the right atrium contributing on the right side and only a narrow strip of left ventricle visible near the apex. Neither atrium nor the left ventricle forms the bulk of this surface.'),
('9e30521b-3c94-539f-b379-de7b9cf46c9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: diaphragmatic (inferior) surface:? [variant 252]', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","Both atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RA mediated by specific receptor activation","RV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle (partly RV near apex)"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle (partly RV near apex)', 'The diaphragmatic (inferior) surface rests on the central tendon of the diaphragm and is formed mostly by the left ventricle, with a small contribution from the right ventricle near the apex. The atria lie above this surface and are not part of it.'),
('42be926c-65bb-5535-bcd4-edc302113a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: left border of heart:? [variant 253]', '["RV resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LA mediated by specific receptor activation","Left ventricle (and left auricle above)","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left ventricle (and left auricle above)', 'The left border of the heart is formed by the left ventricle, with the left auricle extending above it. The right atrium, right ventricle, and pulmonary trunk contribute to the right and anterior relations rather than the left border.'),
('f04ad173-bffe-5898-b38d-cab37512aeae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: right border of heart:? [variant 254]', '["SVC","LV","Right atrium","LA","RV"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The right border of the heart is formed by the right atrium, with the superior vena cava above and the inferior vena cava below. The ventricles and left atrium do not contribute to the right border.'),
('2eb493ef-3a9a-582c-95a4-a387ad9c4b5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: inferior border of heart:? [variant 255]', '["LA resulting in decreased functional activity","Both atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right ventricle (mostly) and left ventricle (near apex)","All chambers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricle (mostly) and left ventricle (near apex)', 'The inferior border runs obliquely from the apex to the lower margin of the right atrium and is formed mostly by the right ventricle, with the left ventricle contributing only near the apex. The atria are not part of this border.'),
('d0b56e59-b65b-50e9-b81c-0508d849d461'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: ligamentum arteriosum:? [variant 256]', '["Pericardial ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrous cord (remnant of ductus arteriosus) between pulmonary trunk and aorta","Foramen ovale remnant mediated by specific receptor activation","Coronary vessel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous cord (remnant of ductus arteriosus) between pulmonary trunk and aorta', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous remnant of the ductus arteriosus, connecting the root of the pulmonary trunk to the underside of the aortic arch. It is not a pericardial or coronary structure, a foramen ovale remnant, or a nerve.'),
('e5f2afab-e45c-5807-bac5-3ba399defdc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Foramen ovale becomes:'' is: [variant 257]', '["Coronary sinus","Ligamentum arteriosum","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum teres","Ductus venosus"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis', 'Functional closure of the foramen ovale after birth leaves the fossa ovalis on the right side of the interatrial septum. The ligamentum teres, ligamentum arteriosum, and ductus venosus remnant are separate fetal structures.'),
('c98c7b5f-de79-5e9f-af92-3a9cec98f9d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: ductus arteriosus becomes:? [variant 258]', '["Ligamentum teres","Fossa ovalis","Ligamentum venosum","Medial umbilical ligament","Ligamentum arteriosum"]'::jsonb, 'Ligamentum arteriosum', 'The ductus arteriosus obliterates after birth and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aorta. The ligamentum teres is the remnant of the umbilical vein, the ligamentum venosum of the ductus venosus, and the medial umbilical ligament of the umbilical artery.'),
('120251bb-1862-55e7-811d-303d6d971a20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: heart wall layers:? [variant 259]', '["2","5","3: epicardium","myocardium","endocardium 4 1"]'::jsonb, '3: epicardium', 'The heart wall has three layers: the outer epicardium (visceral pericardium), the middle myocardium, and the inner endocardium. The option ''3: epicardium'' correctly identifies both the number of layers and the outermost layer.'),
('f4e86bc7-3743-54d1-9425-cabffc109d47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: endocardium continuous with:? [variant 260]', '["Pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","Tunica intima of blood vessels","Epicardium mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myocardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tunica intima of blood vessels', 'The endocardium lines the cardiac chambers and valves and is continuous with the tunica intima of the great vessels entering and leaving the heart. The epicardium, pericardium, and pleura are outer linings that do not continue from the endocardium.'),
('fb7b4857-ff3e-5c03-9b6d-a80b347d46bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: aortic arch branches:? [variant 261]', '["5 resulting in decreased functional activity","4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 mediated by specific receptor activation","2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3: brachiocephalic left common carotid left subclavian"]'::jsonb, '3: brachiocephalic left common carotid left subclavian', 'The arch of the aorta typically gives off three branches: the brachiocephalic trunk, the left common carotid artery, and the left subclavian artery. The option ''3: brachiocephalic left common carotid left subclavian'' names these three branches correctly.'),
('d167e77e-c461-5561-b44d-c2fc68ccb6d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: brachiocephalic trunk divides into:? [variant 262]', '["Right subclavian + right vertebral","Right + left common carotid","Right + left subclavian","Left common carotid + left subclavian","Right common carotid and right subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Right common carotid and right subclavian', 'The brachiocephalic trunk divides behind the right sternoclavicular joint into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries. The left common carotid and left subclavian arise directly from the aortic arch, not from this trunk.'),
('402def6a-0c68-5a88-b0ff-fce7087b6e6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''SVC formed by:'' is: [variant 263]', '["Azygos + hemiazygos","Right and left brachiocephalic veins","Right + left subclavian","Right + left IJV","Right + left vertebral"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left brachiocephalic veins', 'The superior vena cava is formed by the union of the right and left brachiocephalic veins behind the first right costal cartilage. The azygos vein drains into the SVC later, and the subclavian, jugular, and vertebral veins are tributaries that join the brachiocephalic veins.'),
('52060f88-a6cc-52c7-b4e6-0bc635176ec5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: SVC drains into:? [variant 264]', '["IVC","Right atrium","LA","RV","LV"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The superior vena cava returns systemic venous blood from the upper half of the body into the upper part of the right atrium. It does not enter the left atrium or either ventricle.'),
('8151955c-d131-51e2-bafe-9f3b797018bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: IVC drains into:? [variant 265]', '["Right atrium (lower part)","LV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (lower part)', 'The inferior vena cava enters the lower part of the right atrium at its floor. It has no connection with the coronary sinus or with the left-sided or ventricular chambers.'),
('3ac5dbfd-3600-5ce4-81b5-6971c95e10fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: coronary arteries originate from:? [variant 266]', '["Ascending aorta above sinuses resulting in decreased functional activity","LA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sinuses of Valsalva at aortic root","RA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinuses of Valsalva at aortic root', 'The right and left coronary arteries arise from the aortic sinuses of Valsalva at the root of the ascending aorta, just above the aortic valve cusps. The pulmonary trunk and the atria do not give origin to the coronary arteries.'),
('91a1930a-0044-559e-b7bc-cef848aeaeb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: coronary sinus drains into:? [variant 267]', '["RV resulting in decreased functional activity","Right atrium (between IVC and tricuspid valve)","SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (between IVC and tricuspid valve)', 'The coronary sinus runs in the posterior atrioventricular groove and opens into the posteroinferior part of the right atrium, between the orifice of the inferior vena cava and the tricuspid valve. It does not enter the SVC or any left-sided or ventricular chamber.'),
('367bafde-9ca1-503f-a112-645f1b66190d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: thebesian veins:? [variant 268]', '["Azygos resulting in decreased functional activity","Coronary sinus tributaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Small veins draining myocardium directly into chambers","Cardiac lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small veins draining myocardium directly into chambers', 'Thebesian veins are tiny venous channels that drain the myocardium directly into the cardiac chambers, forming a secondary pathway for coronary venous return. They are distinct from the azygos system, pulmonary veins, coronary sinus tributaries, and lymphatics.'),
('a3fa4690-1714-5cc1-a325-3b8a625db57c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''Which artery supplies the SA node in most people'' is: [variant 269]', '["Right coronary artery (in 60% of people)","Left circumflex artery","Left anterior descending artery","Posterior descending artery","Right marginal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (in 60% of people)', 'The SA nodal artery arises from the right coronary artery in about 60% of hearts and from the left circumflex artery in the remainder. The anterior descending, posterior descending, and marginal arteries do not consistently supply the SA node.'),
('a3fda9ec-3d95-5873-ac9f-c5096966ffb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the heart is located:? [variant 270]', '["In the middle mediastinum","In the posterior mediastinum","In the superior mediastinum","In the anterior mediastinum","In the pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 'In the middle mediastinum', 'The heart with its pericardium and the roots of the great vessels occupies the middle mediastinum. The surrounding pleural cavities and the anterior, superior, and posterior mediastinal compartments contain other structures.'),
('9c7f1aab-5afa-5262-95d9-95a6da13e3be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the pericardium consists of:? [variant 271]', '["Endocardium only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only fibrous layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only serous layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous pericardium and serous pericardium (parietal and visceral layers)","Myocardium and epicardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous pericardium and serous pericardium (parietal and visceral layers)', 'The pericardium comprises an outer fibrous layer and an inner serous layer with parietal and visceral components. The endocardium, myocardium, and epicardium are layers of the heart wall itself, not parts of the pericardium.'),
('a1a0e981-be65-5783-a7cb-9ba1de166cff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the oblique pericardial sinus is located:? [variant 272]', '["In front of the right ventricle","Anterior to the aorta","Posterior to the heart","between the pulmonary veins","Between the SVC and IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the heart', 'The oblique pericardial sinus is a blind recess behind the heart, lying posterior to the left atrium and between the pulmonary veins. It is not anterior to the ventricles, the aorta, or between the venae cavae.'),
('ff305c15-a009-5f94-8a60-e3b0bfae9b8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the transverse pericardial sinus is:? [variant 273]', '["Below the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the two ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A passage between the aorta/pulmonary trunk and the atria/SVC","Behind the left atrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In front of the right ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A passage between the aorta/pulmonary trunk and the atria/SVC', 'The transverse pericardial sinus is a passage that lies between the aorta and pulmonary trunk in front and the superior vena cava and atria behind; it can be passed by a finger or a clamp during cardiac surgery. It is unrelated to the ventricles.'),
('10e4a5b1-c8f3-551a-8f3f-3435a5ef2382'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the heart wall layers are:? [variant 274]', '["Epicardium (visceral pericardium)","myocardium","endocardium","Pericardium only","Myocardium and endocardium"]'::jsonb, 'endocardium', 'The heart wall is composed of three layers: the epicardium, the myocardium, and the endocardium, so ''endocardium'' names one of these three layers. The option limited to the pericardium or to only two layers gives an incomplete description of the wall.'),
('39293f2e-3f1d-54c0-b3bc-66129b0f6bba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The right atrium receives:'' is: [variant 275]', '["Aorta","Bronchial veins","SVC","IVC","and coronary sinus"]'::jsonb, 'and coronary sinus', 'The right atrium receives three named venous inflows: the superior vena cava, the inferior vena cava, and the coronary sinus, together with the thebesian veins. The aorta and bronchial veins do not drain into the right atrium.'),
('8ee36e6b-009e-50dc-ada1-a64d5f2f3a2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the right ventricle pumps blood to:? [variant 276]', '["Left atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary trunk (via the pulmonary valve)","Coronary arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary trunk (via the pulmonary valve)', 'The right ventricle ejects deoxygenated blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary trunk for the lungs. The aorta is the outflow channel of the left ventricle, and the other listed targets are not supplied by the right ventricle.'),
('41ddb22a-bb65-52bb-956a-b1a51c9e0ff4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the left atrium receives:? [variant 277]', '["Four pulmonary veins (two from each lung)","Bronchial veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","SVC and IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Four pulmonary veins (two from each lung)', 'The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from four pulmonary veins, two from each lung. The superior and inferior venae cavae and the coronary sinus drain into the right atrium, while the bronchial veins and aorta have different destinations.'),
('99e9ac2d-0044-5344-a510-ae84c95ceae6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the left ventricle pumps blood to:? [variant 278]', '["Right atrium","Aorta (via the aortic valve)","Pulmonary trunk","Left atrium","Right ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (via the aortic valve)', 'The left ventricle ejects oxygenated blood through the aortic valve into the ascending aorta for systemic distribution. The pulmonary trunk is the outflow of the right ventricle, and blood does not pass from the left ventricle into the atria or right ventricle.'),
('4ac8f6d3-09bf-5288-97f6-1dc0ffbe4c6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the interatrial septum contains:? [variant 279]', '["Trabeculae carneae","Papillary muscles","Chordae tendineae","Crista terminalis","Fossa ovalis (remnant of foramen ovale)"]'::jsonb, 'Fossa ovalis (remnant of foramen ovale)', 'The interatrial septum bears the fossa ovalis, the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale. Trabeculae carneae, papillary muscles, and chordae tendineae belong to the ventricles, while the crista terminalis lies in the wall of the right atrium at the margin of the pectinate region.'),
('bcad367c-a17f-57a8-8f54-91fbf22c9b86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the interventricular septum consists of:? [variant 280]', '["Connective tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscular part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only membranous part mediated by specific receptor activation","Fatty tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscular part (thick) and membranous part (thin)"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular part (thick) and membranous part (thin)', 'The interventricular septum has a large thick muscular part and a small thin membranous part near the aortic valve. It is not composed solely of muscle, membrane, connective tissue, or fat.'),
('46dafa9a-9e26-59ce-893b-1bf358607c3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The sinoatrial (SA) node is located:'' is: [variant 281]', '["In the left ventricle","In the AV septum","In the right atrium","at the junction of SVC and right atrial appendage (sulcus terminalis)","In the interventricular septum"]'::jsonb, 'In the right atrium', 'The SA node lies in the wall of the right atrium at the junction of the superior vena cava with the right atrial appendage, overlying the sulcus terminalis. It is not found in the ventricles or the septa between the chambers.'),
('64a65854-f4ec-5f32-ae22-d9dd26f1bdd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the atrioventricular (AV) node is located:? [variant 282]', '["In the right ventricle","In the left atrium","In the interventricular septum","In the SA node","In the inferior part of the interatrial septum"]'::jsonb, 'In the inferior part of the interatrial septum', 'The AV node lies in the inferior part of the interatrial septum, just above the opening of the coronary sinus in the triangle of Koch. It is not located within the ventricles, the left atrium, or the SA node.'),
('1119654b-4bc6-5794-96a4-56d208c28127'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the AV bundle (bundle of His) divides into:? [variant 283]', '["Medial and lateral branches resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior and inferior branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SA and AV fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Right and left bundle branches (in the interventricular septum)","Anterior and posterior fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left bundle branches (in the interventricular septum)', 'The atrioventricular bundle (bundle of His) descends in the upper part of the interventricular septum and divides into right and left bundle branches. The other listed branch patterns do not describe the conduction pathway.'),
('4e307256-ddd4-5538-ae9d-fd0a2ca752c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the coronary arteries arise from:? [variant 284]', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta (right and left coronary sinuses of Valsalva)","Left ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Right ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (right and left coronary sinuses of Valsalva)', 'The right and left coronary arteries arise from the corresponding aortic sinuses of Valsalva at the root of the aorta. The coronary sinus and the cardiac chambers do not give origin to these arteries.'),
('1bd5154a-5237-52b7-b611-ee64052d7517'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the right coronary artery supplies:? [variant 285]', '["The anterior septum","The apex of the heart","The lateral wall of left ventricle","Right ventricle","SA node (60%)"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricle', 'The right coronary artery runs in the right atrioventricular groove and supplies the right ventricle, along with the SA node in about 60% and usually the posterior interventricular septum. The anterior septum, apex, and lateral left ventricle are supplied by the left coronary system.'),
('22287912-ad7c-5e4c-8951-75eebf454013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies:? [variant 286]', '["Anterior interventricular septum","anterior left ventricle","apex","Posterior septum","SA node"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior interventricular septum', 'The left anterior descending artery runs in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior interventricular septum, the anterior wall of the left ventricle, and the apex. The posterior septum is supplied by the right coronary or posterior descending artery, and the SA node by the SA nodal artery.'),
('c29f0dfd-af26-5380-916d-ac684684e81e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The circumflex artery supplies:'' is: [variant 287]', '["Lateral and posterior left ventricle","Posterior septum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior septum mediated by specific receptor activation","SA node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral and posterior left ventricle', 'The circumflex artery runs in the left atrioventricular groove and supplies the lateral and posterior wall of the left ventricle, occasionally giving the SA nodal artery. The septa and right ventricle are supplied by other coronary branches.'),
('37025a89-fe20-5eab-bbd2-83e5ce6ae96a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the coronary sinus drains into:? [variant 288]', '["Left atrium","Left ventricle","Superior vena cava","Right ventricle","Right atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium', 'The coronary sinus collects most of the cardiac venous blood and opens into the posteroinferior part of the right atrium. It does not drain into the superior vena cava or into the left-sided or ventricular chambers.'),
('5711555e-096d-59a9-8089-a7719e47124e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: the cardiac valves include:? [variant 289]', '["Mitral and tricuspid","Aortic and pulmonary","Only AV valves","Only semilunar valves","Tricuspid (right AV)"]'::jsonb, 'Tricuspid (right AV)', 'The four cardiac valves are the tricuspid (right AV), mitral (left AV), aortic, and pulmonary; ''Tricuspid (right AV)'' correctly names the right atrioventricular valve. The other options give either partial groupings or leave out the semilunar valves.'),
('2c444b3f-7917-56cf-ae33-0c5ea129198b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the mitral valve is located:? [variant 290]', '["Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left atrium and left ventricle","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk","In the IV septum"]'::jsonb, 'Between the left atrium and left ventricle', 'The mitral (bicuspid) valve guards the left atrioventricular orifice between the left atrium and the left ventricle. The other option pairs describe the sites of the aortic, tricuspid, and pulmonary valves.'),
('2e1eccd0-cb59-50d3-9e54-50a1358bf78b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the tricuspid valve is located:? [variant 291]', '["Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk","In the IV septum","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the left atrium and left ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Between the right atrium and right ventricle', 'The tricuspid valve lies at the right atrioventricular orifice between the right atrium and the right ventricle. The other option pairs describe the locations of the pulmonary, aortic, and mitral valves or septal structures.'),
('b7f442ac-5f2b-5334-8578-a09bfa870af6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the aortic valve is located:? [variant 292]', '["Between the left atrium and left ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","In the coronary sinus","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Between the left ventricle and aorta', 'The aortic valve sits at the aortic orifice between the left ventricle and the root of the aorta. The other option pairs describe the sites of the mitral, tricuspid, and pulmonary valves.'),
('c9c75182-84f5-5c52-a297-1a1a74d5046e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The pulmonary valve is located:'' is: [variant 293]', '["Between the left atrium and left ventricle","In the IV septum","Between the right atrium and right ventricle","Between the left ventricle and aorta","Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk', 'The pulmonary valve guards the orifice between the right ventricle and the pulmonary trunk. The other option pairs describe the sites of the mitral, tricuspid, and aortic valves.'),
('79ba8435-0b0e-5dc5-b8e6-ec450a4199de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: chordae tendineae connect:? [variant 294]', '["Papillary muscles to the AV valve cusps (prevent prolapse)","Ventricles to atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta to ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","SA node to AV node without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atria to ventricles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papillary muscles to the AV valve cusps (prevent prolapse)', 'Chordae tendineae are fibrous cords that attach the papillary muscles to the cusps of the atrioventricular valves, preventing the cusps from prolapsing into the atria during ventricular systole. They do not connect chambers, great vessels, or the conduction system.'),
('28ab360a-340f-5536-a94d-27fa8ff7afdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, which statement best fits the following fact: papillary muscles arise from:? [variant 295]', '["Atrial wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Chordae tendineae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventricular wall (trabeculae carneae)","Aortic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valve cusps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular wall (trabeculae carneae)', 'Papillary muscles are elevations of the ventricular wall formed from the trabeculae carneae. They do not arise from the atrial wall, the great vessels, the valve cusps, or the chordae tendineae themselves.'),
('68f643fa-4969-55c5-830f-31ccdc3c04e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Regarding Heart and pericardium, what is the correct answer to: the fossa ovalis is a remnant of:? [variant 296]', '["Umbilical vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductus venosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ductus arteriosus mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale (fetal shunt between right and left atria)","Ligamentum arteriosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen ovale (fetal shunt between right and left atria)', 'The fossa ovalis is the remnant of the foramen ovale, the fetal channel between the right and left atria. The umbilical vein, ductus venosus, and ductus arteriosus leave different remnants after birth.'),
('238ca93f-2fcf-594a-bf93-6ad8db5446ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'For Heart and pericardium, which option is most appropriate: the ligamentum arteriosum is a remnant of:? [variant 297]', '["Umbilical vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Ductus arteriosus (fetal shunt between pulmonary trunk and aorta)","Umbilical artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ductus venosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ductus arteriosus (fetal shunt between pulmonary trunk and aorta)', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous remnant of the ductus arteriosus, the fetal shunt between the pulmonary trunk and the aorta. The umbilical vessels, foramen ovale, and ductus venosus give rise to other remnants.'),
('09be734d-1126-57a8-948d-34d85251b7d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'Which answer is correct in Heart and pericardium when asked: the conducting system of the heart includes:? [variant 298]', '["Cardiac nerves","Valves","Pericardium","SA node","AV node"]'::jsonb, 'SA node', 'The cardiac conducting system comprises the SA node, AV node, bundle of His, and the Purkinje network, so the SA node names one of its components. Valves, pericardium, and cardiac nerves are not part of the conducting tissue.'),
('c165f6b2-3ccb-5c04-897b-c97c2290b242'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'In Heart and pericardium, the best response to ''The vagus nerve provides:'' is: [variant 299]', '["innervation resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor to valves mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic innervation to the heart (decreases heart rate)","Sensory only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic innervation to the heart (decreases heart rate)', 'The vagus nerve supplies parasympathetic fibers to the heart that slow the heart rate, a negative chronotropic effect. Sympathetic stimulation has the opposite effect, and the vagus provides no motor supply to the valves.'),
('a5e941b2-d154-5de2-8e8a-4364aa63b067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Heart Pericardium', 'During revision of Heart and pericardium, which of the following is correct: the SA node is located in:? [variant 300]', '["Interventricular septum","Right ventricle","Right atrium near SVC opening","AV node","Left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium near SVC opening', 'The SA node lies in the upper part of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava, in the region of the crista terminalis. It is not located in the ventricles, the septa, the AV node, or the left atrium.'),
('75a4b985-b907-59e6-bfd6-4c7d83aee88d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pleura layers:', '["2 (parietal and visceral)","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (parietal and visceral)', 'Each lung is invested by a continuous serous membrane that folds on itself to form two layers: the parietal pleura lining the thoracic wall and mediastinum, and the visceral pleura covering the lung surface. Between them lies the pleural cavity, so there are exactly two layers.'),
('40975504-a55d-5e2c-822d-fde8a6eb8cd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pleural cavity:', '["Bronchial space resulting in decreased functional activity","Potential space between parietal and visceral pleura","Pericardial space mediated by specific receptor activation","Mediastinal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potential space between parietal and visceral pleura', 'The pleural cavity is the closed, slit-like potential space between the parietal and visceral layers of pleura. It normally contains only a thin film of serous fluid for lubrication, and its subatmospheric pressure helps keep the lungs expanded against the chest wall.'),
('95b5f5e1-0308-5e71-8b24-5e3efe87080c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Parietal pleura parts:', '["Mediastinal + costal","Costal","mediastinal diaphragmatic","cervical (cupula) Pulmonary + costal","Diaphragmatic + costal Visceral + parietal"]'::jsonb, 'Costal', 'The parietal pleura is divided into four parts according to the surface it lines: costal, mediastinal, diaphragmatic, and cervical (cupula). The costal part is the largest and lines the ribs, intercostal spaces, and inner thoracic wall.'),
('14b24448-4f29-5961-8e89-cdf904e210c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Cervical pleura (cupula) extends:', '["To hyoid resulting in decreased functional activity","To sternal notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To 2nd rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Above 1st rib (2-3 cm above medial clavicle)","To clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above 1st rib (2-3 cm above medial clavicle)', 'The cervical pleura (cupula) projects as a dome above the first rib, rising 2-3 cm above the medial third of the clavicle into the root of the neck. This projection occurs because the apex of the lung follows the cupula, and the dome is reinforced by the suprapleural membrane (Sibson''s fascia).'),
('163992c4-e0f9-5077-af67-c34f2e78a6c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Sibson''s fascia attaches:', '["C7 transverse process to inner border of 1st rib","C7 to 2nd rib accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C6 to 1st rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Clavicle to 1st rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T1 to manubrium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C7 transverse process to inner border of 1st rib', 'Sibson''s fascia (the suprapleural membrane) is a thickening of the endothoracic fascia that caps the cervical pleura. It extends from the transverse process of C7 to the inner border of the first rib, stabilizing the cupula against movement during respiration.'),
('9ab2f883-6104-5164-aa63-90bff7ec7ec6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pleural recesses:', '["Alveolar sacs resulting in decreased functional activity","Costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal","Mediastinal spaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchial divisions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung fissures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal', 'Pleural recesses are potential spaces where the lung does not fully occupy the pleural cavity during quiet respiration, the chief ones being the costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal recesses. Fluid, pus, or blood tend to collect in these dependent recesses.'),
('390bb5da-a49c-5b3b-8894-5330dc16c4e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Costodiaphragmatic recess:', '["Costal + mediastinal","Lung apex","Mediastinal + diaphragmatic","Between costal and diaphragmatic pleura","deepest at midaxillary Lung base"]'::jsonb, 'Between costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is the potential space formed where the costal and diaphragmatic parts of the parietal pleura meet, lying between the inferior border of the lung and the lower limit of the pleural sac. It is deepest in the midaxillary line and is the most common site for pleural fluid collection.'),
('40bfeda9-eeb8-5f8e-abe8-d5b859e620d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Costomediastinal recess:', '["Between costal and mediastinal pleura behind sternum","Interlobar fissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung + mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","Costal + diaphragmatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung hilum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between costal and mediastinal pleura behind sternum', 'The costomediastinal recess is the potential space between the costal and mediastinal pleura, lying behind the sternum along the anterior border of the lung. On the left it is more prominent opposite the cardiac notch, where the lung falls short of the chest wall.'),
('85df684b-f056-59ce-b1d8-a3593c8043bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Right lung lobes:', '["5 lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","2 lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (upper, middle, lower)","1 lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (upper, middle, lower)', 'The right lung is divided by the oblique and horizontal fissures into three lobes: upper, middle, and lower. The presence of a middle lobe is unique to the right lung and is reflected in its ten bronchopulmonary segments.'),
('0c12429c-38f7-5538-bcbb-5efdf6d4d61e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Left lung lobes:', '["2 (upper, lower)","4 lobes","5 lobes","3 lobes","1 lobe"]'::jsonb, '2 (upper, lower)', 'The left lung has only two lobes, upper and lower, separated by the oblique fissure. There is no middle lobe on the left; the lingula, a projection of the upper lobe, is the developmental counterpart of the right middle lobe.'),
('b8871bd7-63e3-5745-8a0c-4df8efb602c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Right lung fissures:', '["Vertical and horizontal","Horizontal and oblique","Horizontal only","Oblique only","Sagittal and coronal"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal and oblique', 'The right lung has two fissures: an oblique fissure separating the lower lobe from the upper and middle lobes, and a horizontal fissure separating the upper from the middle lobe. Together they divide the right lung into its three lobes.'),
('50d8b5d5-4ddc-568c-9c79-ac2f6922a641'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Left lung fissure:', '["Sagittal","Oblique only","Vertical","Horizontal + oblique","Horizontal only"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique only', 'The left lung has a single oblique fissure that runs obliquely from the T4/T5 region behind to the costal margin in front, separating the upper and lower lobes. A horizontal fissure is absent on the left because there is no middle lobe.'),
('b68d0265-9a14-5f33-86b8-9c19406b6ea8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Horizontal fissure separates:', '["Right upper from middle lobe","Upper from lower","Apex","Hilum","Middle from lower"]'::jsonb, 'Right upper from middle lobe', 'The horizontal fissure of the right lung runs from the oblique fissure toward the hilum at the level of the fourth costal cartilage and separates the upper lobe from the middle lobe. The oblique fissure, by contrast, separates the lower lobe from the upper and middle lobes.'),
('e41516cf-5c99-5122-8f32-e1da539538a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Oblique fissure separates:', '["Two lungs resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper from middle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle from lower mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower lobe from upper (and middle on right)","Lung from mediastinum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lobe from upper (and middle on right)', 'The oblique fissure runs from the fourth or fifth thoracic spine to the sixth costal cartilage, separating the lower lobe from the upper lobe on the left and from both the upper and middle lobes on the right. It is present in both lungs.'),
('1ef3edd6-e71d-5ef5-9886-40a1e7446a9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Cardiac notch found on:', '["Apex resulting in decreased functional activity","Right lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base mediated by specific receptor activation","Hilum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior border of left lung"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of left lung', 'The cardiac notch is a concave indentation along the anterior border of the left lung produced by the heart and pericardium. The lingula of the upper lobe projects into the floor of this notch.'),
('2e9b889c-b16e-51ab-a6eb-148acfe637bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Lingula of left lung:', '["Cardiac notch resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Accessory lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tongue-like projection of upper lobe (corresponds to right middle lobe)"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue-like projection of upper lobe (corresponds to right middle lobe)', 'The lingula is a tongue-shaped projection of the anteromedial portion of the upper lobe of the left lung that extends into the cardiac notch. Although it is the developmental counterpart of the right middle lobe, its bronchus arises from the upper lobe rather than from a separate lobar bronchus.'),
('1e5761ec-09bc-5ac9-b4c4-e9e5a6bebe17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Root of lung (hilum) contains:', '["Only lymphatics Only pulmonary artery","Only veins Only bronchus","Bronchus pulmonary artery","pulmonary veins bronchial vessels","nerves lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchus pulmonary artery', 'The root of the lung, situated at the hilum, is formed by the main bronchus, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, and lymphatics, all enclosed in a sleeve of pleura. The bronchus and pulmonary artery are the principal conducting elements of the root.'),
('608a048e-ca4c-5e18-90f0-8b54a1b76d5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Right hilum order (superior to inferior):', '["Upper lobe bronchus","pulmonary artery","main bronchus","veins","Pulmonary artery bronchus"]'::jsonb, 'veins', 'At the right hilum the structures are stacked from top to bottom as upper lobe bronchus, pulmonary artery, intermediate (main) bronchus, and then the pulmonary veins. The pulmonary veins are therefore the most inferior structures within the right root of the lung.'),
('5180d481-a735-5f31-8e20-4f01f529d550'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Left hilum order:', '["Veins","artery","bronchus","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'artery', 'At the left hilum the pulmonary artery is the most superior structure, with the left main bronchus lying below it and the pulmonary veins below the bronchus. This contrasts with the right side, where an eparterial bronchus sits above the right pulmonary artery.'),
('14659a91-8eed-5ebc-9412-06b813c0ddc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pulmonary ligament:', '["Connective cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural fold below hilum allowing lung root movement","Diaphragm part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pericardial fold leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural fold below hilum allowing lung root movement', 'The pulmonary ligament is a double layer of pleura that hangs from the inferior border of the lung root down to the diaphragm. It stabilizes the lower lobe and mediastinal surface while still permitting free movement of the lung root during respiration.'),
('19ac7056-f8c0-53d8-809d-84736f9d2676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Left main bronchus:', '["Thicker walled","Longer","narrower","more horizontal (aorta arches over it)","Same More vertical Shorter wider"]'::jsonb, 'Longer', 'The left main bronchus is longer, narrower, and more horizontal than the right because it must pass beneath the aortic arch and in front of the esophagus. Its more oblique course means inhaled foreign bodies preferentially lodge in the wider, more vertical right main bronchus.'),
('d93c74ac-e036-5628-9fe4-e00a7f06a23f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Tracheal bifurcation at:', '["T10","C6","T4/T5 (carina)","T6","T2"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 (carina)', 'The trachea bifurcates at the level of the sternal angle, which corresponds to the intervertebral disc between T4 and T5, where the internal ridge known as the carina marks the division into the two main bronchi. This is the fixed point used to verify central venous catheter placement on a chest radiograph.'),
('097bf621-85af-5ef9-8c46-75a861c520a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Bronchopulmonary segment:', '["Lung lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anatomical lung unit supplied by segmental bronchus + vessels","Alveolar sac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Entire lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical lung unit supplied by segmental bronchus + vessels', 'A bronchopulmonary segment is the anatomical and functional unit of the lung supplied by a tertiary (segmental) bronchus and its accompanying branch of the pulmonary artery, with limited arterial anastomosis between segments. Because it is surrounded by connective tissue septa, it is the smallest surgically resectable subdivision of the lung.'),
('2e6fedd2-7722-5779-9418-29ddc976feb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Right lung segments:', '["10 segments (3 upper, 2 middle, 5 lower)","9 segments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","8 segments mediated by specific receptor activation","12 segments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","6 segments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10 segments (3 upper, 2 middle, 5 lower)', 'The right lung typically contains ten bronchopulmonary segments: three in the upper lobe, two in the middle lobe, and five in the lower lobe. Each segment has its own segmental bronchus and pulmonary artery branch, making them discretely resectable.'),
('04f7b310-d646-530d-83fd-2006a12207b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Left lung segments:', '["12 segments","7 segments","8-10 segments (some fusion)","6 segments","10 segments"]'::jsonb, '8-10 segments (some fusion)', 'The left lung has 8-10 bronchopulmonary segments because adjacent segments frequently fuse: the apical and posterior segments of the upper lobe form a common apicoposterior segment, and the anterior and medial basal segments of the lower lobe fuse into an anteromedial basal segment. There is no middle lobe, so the total is usually eight.'),
('2c775a68-1335-5160-95ea-e09d6af4cb33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Bronchial arteries supply:', '["Alveoli resulting in decreased functional activity","Trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung parenchyma (bronchi, bronchioles) and visceral pleura","Parietal pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung parenchyma (bronchi, bronchioles) and visceral pleura', 'The bronchial arteries are systemic vessels that supply oxygenated blood to the connective-tissue framework of the lung: the bronchi, bronchioles down to the respiratory level, and the visceral pleura. They arise from the thoracic aorta or from a right posterior intercostal artery, whereas the pulmonary arteries provide the blood for gas exchange.'),
('d954e83c-66c7-5489-ac7a-8c7aa95f6f1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Right bronchial artery from:', '["Subclavian resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal thoracic mediated by specific receptor activation","Left bronchial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3rd right posterior intercostal artery or aorta"]'::jsonb, '3rd right posterior intercostal artery or aorta', 'The right bronchial artery most commonly arises from the third right posterior intercostal artery, frequently through a right aortic common trunk; in some individuals it arises directly from the aorta. The left bronchial arteries, in contrast, usually arise directly from the descending thoracic aorta.'),
('e9f4bdcd-51f1-5949-bfe6-6e90b88f4a5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Left bronchial arteries from:', '["Right bronchial","Subclavian","Costocervical trunk","Internal thoracic","Thoracic aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic aorta', 'The left bronchial arteries typically arise directly from the descending thoracic aorta, usually as two branches. This contrasts with the right bronchial artery, which usually branches from the third right posterior intercostal artery.'),
('a7f1afaa-bfd5-5dbf-8fc7-0745df622a4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pulmonary arteries carry:', '["Deoxygenated blood from RV to lungs","Oxygenated to lungs","Mixed blood","Oxygenated to heart","Deoxygenated to LA"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated blood from RV to lungs', 'The pulmonary trunk and its right and left pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs for oxygenation. They are the only arteries in the body that transport deoxygenated blood.'),
('1008753e-3c83-5b22-b84a-3944f2980aeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pulmonary veins carry:', '["Oxygenated blood from lungs to LA","Deoxygenated to RA","Deoxygenated to lungs","Oxygenated to RV","Mixed"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygenated blood from lungs to LA', 'The right and left pulmonary veins (two from each lung) return oxygenated blood from the pulmonary capillary beds to the left atrium. They are the only veins in the body that carry oxygenated blood.'),
('c71e74c7-e14e-5563-b555-8ec36f6d05c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Visceral pleura innervation:', '["Splanchnic resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phrenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal (pain) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autonomic (sympathetic) - insensitive to pain"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic (sympathetic) - insensitive to pain', 'The visceral pleura receives only autonomic fibers that travel with the sympathetic trunks (vago-sympathetic supply) and is insensitive to pain. Disease confined to the lung and visceral pleura therefore produces no sharp localized pain, unlike irritation of the pain-sensitive parietal pleura.'),
('e9ae6b70-6726-59a2-aa94-743b8f97dd54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Parietal pleura innervation:', '["Only phrenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Intercostal (costal) and phrenic (mediastinal/central diaphragmatic)","Vagus mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only intercostal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal (costal) and phrenic (mediastinal/central diaphragmatic)', 'The parietal pleura is pain-sensitive and derives its innervation from the intercostal nerves over the costal and peripheral diaphragmatic parts, and from the phrenic nerve over the mediastinal and central diaphragmatic parts. This segmental innervation determines where pleuritic pain is localized or referred.'),
('a350d003-b047-5fea-acab-9c3a19b9ae46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Costal pleura pain referred to:', '["Neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Chest wall (intercostal nerves)","Back mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chest wall (intercostal nerves)', 'Because the costal pleura is innervated by the intercostal nerves, pleural irritation here produces sharp pain felt locally over the corresponding part of the chest wall. This is the classic somatic pain of pleurisy affecting the costal pleura.'),
('50709293-fefe-56b9-957d-0e88f058995e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Diaphragmatic pleura (central) pain referred to:', '["Back resulting in decreased functional activity","Abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chest wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder tip (C3-C5 via phrenic)","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shoulder tip (C3-C5 via phrenic)', 'The central diaphragmatic pleura is supplied by the phrenic nerve, which carries sensory fibers from C3-C5. Because these dermatomes also supply the shoulder tip, inflammation such as in basal pneumonia or pleurisy is perceived as referred pain at the shoulder tip.'),
('92dd85f2-8d02-50ee-8595-0c2f6c7a1900'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pulmonary plexus:', '["Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic fibers","Only motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic fibers', 'The pulmonary plexus, lying at the root of the lung, is formed by parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve and sympathetic fibers from the sympathetic trunks. Parasympathetic activity constricts bronchi and increases secretion, whereas sympathetic activity dilates the airways.'),
('252399f3-da8b-5fc4-91c1-fd2c5b24451a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Apex of lung projects:', '["C7 resulting in decreased functional activity","T1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternal notch mediated by specific receptor activation","1st rib level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2-3 cm above medial 1/3 of clavicle"]'::jsonb, '2-3 cm above medial 1/3 of clavicle', 'The rounded apex of the lung, covered by the cervical pleura, projects 2-3 cm above the medial third of the clavicle into the root of the neck. This exposes the apex to injury from penetrating neck wounds and to disease such as apical tuberculosis and Pancoast tumors.'),
('274090a9-b3f5-5e68-861a-8908b7b5827e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Base of lung:', '["Convex resulting in decreased functional activity","Irregular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pointed mediated by specific receptor activation","Flat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Concave rests on diaphragm (dome-shaped)"]'::jsonb, 'Concave rests on diaphragm (dome-shaped)', 'The base (diaphragmatic surface) of the lung is deeply concave so that it rests on the dome-shaped thoracic surface of the diaphragm. Its inferior border is sharp and descends into the costodiaphragmatic recess during inspiration.'),
('bff326f4-f762-5972-be1d-426b5d38f217'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Costal surface of lung:', '["Irregular","Concave","Smooth and convex","contacts costal pleura","Flat Roughened"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth and convex', 'The costal surface of the lung is large, smooth, and convex, conforming to the inner surface of the thoracic wall and the costal pleura. Impressions of the ribs may mark this surface when the lung is stiffened by disease.'),
('d2db4087-d9e8-59d7-bdc1-29a3d1504c44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Mediastinal surface contains:', '["Costal surface resulting in decreased functional activity","Apex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hilum (root of lung) and cardiac impression","Base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fissures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hilum (root of lung) and cardiac impression', 'The mediastinal (medial) surface of each lung bears the hilum, where the structures of the root enter the lung, and shows impressions made by adjacent mediastinal structures. On the left these include the cardiac impression and grooves for the esophagus, aorta, and subclavian artery.'),
('a2997d15-3384-521b-8406-f766963fecfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Left lung cardiac impression due to:', '["Heart extends more to left","Pericardium on left","Left lung smaller","Left bronchus longer","Aorta on left"]'::jsonb, 'Heart extends more to left', 'The cardiac impression on the medial surface of the left lung is large because the heart projects substantially into the left side of the thoracic cavity. This impression produces the cardiac notch of the anterior border and the underlying lingula.'),
('ad0db2c5-e346-55ea-bdcf-24949165e33e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Trachea lined by:', '["Stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Simple cuboidal mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transitional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells', 'The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with interspersed goblet cells. Goblet cell mucus traps inhaled particles, and the cilia sweep the mucus toward the larynx, a mechanism known as the mucociliary escalator.'),
('5e488ece-1989-5f1b-a856-d6bd61fa8ecb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Trachea reinforced by:', '["cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrocartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete bony rings mediated by specific receptor activation","Elastic cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings (16-20)"]'::jsonb, 'C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings (16-20)', 'The trachea is held patent by 16-20 C-shaped rings of hyaline cartilage that are incomplete posteriorly. The free ends of the rings are united by the trachealis muscle, and the posterior gap allows the esophagus to bulge into the trachea during swallowing.'),
('32ed0d82-f9bd-50f1-84ce-744ea0911bfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Trachealis muscle connects:', '["Sides resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior ends accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea to larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","Open ends of C-shaped cartilages posteriorly","Trachea to esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Open ends of C-shaped cartilages posteriorly', 'The trachealis is a band of smooth muscle that bridges the open posterior ends of the C-shaped tracheal cartilages, forming the membranous posterior tracheal wall. Its contraction narrows the airway and aids effective coughing.'),
('db23c280-04ae-589d-9d5e-0257eaddbac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Esophagus located:', '["Anterior mediastinum","Lateral to trachea","Anterior to trachea","Posterior to trachea","anterior to vertebrae Posterior to vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to trachea', 'In the superior mediastinum the esophagus lies immediately posterior to the trachea and anterior to the vertebral bodies. A distended esophagus or tumor can compress the membranous posterior tracheal wall and cause airway symptoms.'),
('3f91c93b-9a94-5dc5-beb4-f3d214668ba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Phrenic nerve at hilum:', '["Below hilum","Anterior to lung root","Posterior to hilum","Within ligament","Between PA and PV"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to lung root', 'At the root of the lung the phrenic nerve passes anterior to the hilum, accompanied by the pericardiacophrenic vessels, while the vagus nerve runs posterior to the root. This anterior-posterior relationship is a key surgical landmark at the hilum.'),
('175dfe3e-bbaf-59ed-ba03-4fb6114045a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Vagus nerve at hilum:', '["Within ligament","Posterior to lung root","Above hilum","Between bronchus and artery","Anterior to hilum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to lung root', 'The vagus nerve descends posterior to the root of the lung, where it contributes branches to the pulmonary and cardiac plexuses. The phrenic nerve lies anterior to the root, so the two nerves bracket the hilum.'),
('3e21ed81-012b-5113-87e1-395c6a3b9a19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Azygos vein arches over:', '["Esophagus","Root of left lung","Aorta","Root of right lung","Trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Root of right lung', 'The azygos vein ascends on the right side of the vertebral column and arches forward over the root of the right lung before entering the superior vena cava. It is therefore an important right-sided relation of the trachea and right main bronchus.'),
('4fce104d-5eb9-5f3a-9bc3-b0f45e2973ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Trachea length:', '["3-5 cm","15-20 cm","20-25 cm","5 cm","10-12 cm"]'::jsonb, '10-12 cm', 'The trachea measures approximately 10-12 cm in the adult, extending from the lower border of the cricoid cartilage at C6 to its bifurcation at the T4-T5 level. It divides into the right and left main bronchi at the carina.'),
('79575b42-24dd-5fae-97bc-63ee8de2ed16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Main bronchi divide into:', '["Only lobar resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobar (secondary) -> segmental (tertiary)","Alveolar ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct to bronchioles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only segmental leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobar (secondary) -> segmental (tertiary)', 'Each main (primary) bronchus divides into lobar (secondary) bronchi, which in turn branch into segmental (tertiary) bronchi, one supplying each bronchopulmonary segment. The bronchi continue to divide through bronchioles and finally into the alveolar ducts and alveoli.'),
('c6567baa-43e3-5ec6-8e2a-90198e479ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Bronchioles lack:', '["Epithelium","Cartilage (smooth muscle only)","Smooth muscle","Elastic fibers","Basement membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage (smooth muscle only)', 'Bronchioles are the air passages below the segmental bronchi whose walls contain smooth muscle and elastic fibers but no cartilage. Loss of cartilage support means bronchiolar caliber is controlled entirely by smooth muscle tone, and bronchospasm can severely narrow them.'),
('2bab84bb-efab-5818-8fee-6c2ed14a977f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Terminal bronchiole:', '["Alveolar sac resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Last part of conducting zone (no gas exchange)","First respiratory zone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Last part of conducting zone (no gas exchange)', 'The terminal bronchiole is the final airway of the purely conducting zone; it has no alveoli and takes no part in gas exchange. Beyond it, respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts begin the respiratory zone, where alveoli first appear. Respiratory bronchiole, alveolar duct, and alveolar sac all belong to the gas-exchanging portion of the airway.'),
('74944f2d-b36a-544c-a270-adf30e66830e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Respiratory bronchiole:', '["Segmental bronchus resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar sac mediated by specific receptor activation","First part of respiratory zone (some alveoli)","Conducting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First part of respiratory zone (some alveoli)', 'The respiratory bronchiole is the first airway to carry occasional alveoli, marking the transition into the respiratory (gas-exchanging) zone. It branches into alveolar ducts and ends in alveolar sacs. The terminal bronchiole and segmental bronchus are still part of the conducting airway.'),
('77e70139-be85-521c-92f1-6e6089efc395'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Alveolar ducts end in:', '["Pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchioles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveolar sacs (clusters of alveoli)"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar sacs (clusters of alveoli)', 'Alveolar ducts terminate in alveolar sacs, which are clusters of alveoli opening around a common chamber. These thin-walled ducts and sacs are the principal sites of gas exchange. Bronchioles and terminal bronchioles lie more proximally in the airway tree.'),
('c7059dbc-1781-59f6-9fcc-1fc519f9209d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Type I pneumocytes:', '["Macrophages resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuboidal producing surfactant mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cells covering 95% of alveolar surface (gas exchange)","Goblet cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cells covering 95% of alveolar surface (gas exchange)', 'Type I pneumocytes are extremely thin squamous cells that line about 95% of the alveolar surface, providing the minimal diffusion barrier across which gas exchange occurs. Type II cells are cuboidal and produce surfactant, while macrophages and goblet cells serve phagocytic and secretory roles elsewhere.'),
('0c709ad6-73f9-5e43-a23a-bdb6bb32a167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Type II pneumocytes:', '["Ciliated resulting in decreased functional activity","Goblet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produce surfactant (cuboidal)","Line alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phagocytic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produce surfactant (cuboidal)', 'Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal cells that synthesize and secrete pulmonary surfactant, which lowers alveolar surface tension and prevents collapse; they can also proliferate to replace damaged type I cells. Ciliated and goblet cells belong to the conducting airways, and resident alveolar macrophages perform phagocytosis.'),
('1b6c65fe-a5a4-5a81-b0f6-6f406825d6a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Surfactant function:', '["Kills bacteria","Secretes mucus","Increases surface tension","Reduces surface tension","prevents alveolar collapse Transports O2"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces surface tension', 'Surfactant lowers the surface tension at the air-liquid interface of the alveoli, preventing alveolar collapse, particularly of small airspaces, at end-expiration. It is produced by type II pneumocytes. Raising surface tension or killing bacteria misstates its role, and surfactant does not transport O2.'),
('e2eb5b5e-b986-5d48-bb0a-e0731f868b54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pneumothorax:', '["Air in pleural cavity causing lung collapse","Blood in pleura accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluid in pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Air in pleural cavity causing lung collapse', 'Pneumothorax is the presence of air in the pleural cavity, which abolishes the negative intrapleural pressure and allows the lung to collapse. Blood, fluid, and pus in the pleural space are called hemothorax, pleural effusion, and empyema respectively, and a pleural tumor is an unrelated mass lesion.'),
('5be4b48c-3057-574c-a852-05388ed7cc96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pleural effusion:', '["Pleural fibrosis","Pleural inflammation","Fluid accumulation in pleural cavity","Air in pleura","Blood in pleura"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid accumulation in pleural cavity', 'A pleural effusion is the abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity, most often from heart failure, infection, or malignancy. Air, blood, and pus in the pleural space are named pneumothorax, hemothorax, and empyema; fibrosis and inflammation describe pleural pathology rather than fluid collection itself.'),
('b251f0dc-7ca4-5a83-bdf2-f2015918e2aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The cardiac notch is a feature of which lung?', '["Only the middle lobe","Right lung","Both lungs equally","The lingula only","Left lung (on the anterior border)"]'::jsonb, 'Left lung (on the anterior border)', 'The cardiac notch is a concavity on the anterior border of the left lung that accommodates the heart, with the lingula projecting below it. The right lung has a horizontal fissure but no cardiac notch, and the notch is not found on the middle lobe, lingula, or right side.'),
('1d25010f-472f-550f-bc46-85ca609011d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Anterior mediastinum contents:', '["Heart","Thymus (remnant)","internal thoracic vessels lymph nodes","fat Aorta","Trachea Esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus (remnant)', 'The anterior mediastinum between the sternum and pericardium contains the thymus (or its remnant in adults), internal thoracic vessels, lymph nodes, and fat. The heart occupies the middle mediastinum, and the trachea and esophagus lie more posteriorly.'),
('6e018a29-f4a0-5457-8218-fc7b46fe710a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The pleura consists of:', '["Diaphragmatic fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parietal pleura accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal pleura (lining thoracic wall) and visceral pleura (covering lung)","Only visceral pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endothoracic fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal pleura (lining thoracic wall) and visceral pleura (covering lung)', 'The pleura is a serous membrane with two continuous layers: the parietal pleura lining the thoracic wall, diaphragm, and mediastinum, and the visceral pleura covering the lung, continuous with each other at the hilum. Fascial layers such as the endothoracic and diaphragmatic fascia lie outside the parietal pleura and are not part of it.'),
('1906e4a1-af8b-5039-beda-25e35ce7655e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is:', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is the deep dependent gutter of the pleural cavity where the costal and diaphragmatic parietal pleura meet, extending below the lower border of the lung. Fluid and blood collect here in the upright patient. It is unrelated to the cardiac notch, lung apex, or space between the lungs.'),
('e4b2ee0b-d311-515f-bcfa-6ff6bf46b6d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The left lung has:', '["1 lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","3 lobes and 2 fissures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 lobes without a fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","2 lobes (superior and inferior) and 1 oblique fissure","4 lobes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 lobes (superior and inferior) and 1 oblique fissure', 'The left lung is divided by a single oblique fissure into superior and inferior lobes; it has no horizontal fissure and no middle lobe, with the lingula forming part of the superior lobe. In contrast, the right lung has three lobes and two fissures.'),
('23a4dda8-f907-5b88-b92b-2df721cdd0ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The left lung also has:', '["fissures resulting in decreased functional activity","A middle lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A horizontal fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","3 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)', 'The lingula is a tongue-shaped projection on the anterior border of the left superior lobe, homologous to the middle lobe of the right lung. The left lung therefore lacks a true middle lobe and a horizontal fissure. The lingula is supplied by superior and inferior lingular bronchi.'),
('5e66d1ab-0ad2-5d4d-bf0d-9b3985483eeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The hilum of the lung contains:', '["Only the pulmonary veins","Bronchus","pulmonary artery and veins","bronchial vessels","lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchus', 'The pulmonary hilum transmits the bronchus, pulmonary artery, two pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, lymphatics, and autonomic nerves. All of these structures cross the hilum, so the bronchus is among them. The hilum is not restricted to veins or to a single type of vessel.'),
('0a1d93dd-8984-5bb4-85f1-8f2d39da3bff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'At the hilum of the right lung:', '["the arrangement is:","Pulmonary veins above all","Bronchus below artery and veins","Pulmonary artery above bronchus","Eparterial bronchus (above pulmonary artery)"]'::jsonb, 'Eparterial bronchus (above pulmonary artery)', 'At the right hilum the upper lobe (eparterial) bronchus lies above the pulmonary artery, which in turn lies above the pulmonary veins, giving a bronchus-artery-vein order from above downward. The eparterial bronchus arises before the artery crosses it. The left hilum differs because its pulmonary artery lies highest.'),
('5fb21630-138f-5b27-91e4-188b775ede21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'At the hilum of the left lung:', '["the arrangement is:","Artery in the middle","Bronchus in the middle","Bronchus above pulmonary artery","Pulmonary artery above bronchus"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary artery above bronchus', 'At the left hilum the pulmonary artery is the most superior structure, followed by the bronchus, with the pulmonary veins lowest, giving an artery-bronchus-vein order from above downward. The right side differs because its eparterial bronchus lies above the artery. Recalling this vertical order helps identify each side on imaging.'),
('9eb5a127-8f08-5283-8f17-d91f939f29da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The trachea bifurcates into:', '["Directly into segmental bronchi resulting in decreased functional activity","Into 3 lobar bronchi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right and left main bronchi at the level of the sternal angle (T4/T5)","At the level of C6 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Into the larynx and bronchi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left main bronchi at the level of the sternal angle (T4/T5)', 'The trachea bifurcates into the right and left main bronchi at the sternal angle, which overlies the T4-T5 intervertebral disc. The carina marks this division point. The larynx lies higher at the C4-C6 level, and lobar and segmental bronchi form distally after the main bronchi.'),
('bd30307b-f1ec-53ae-b011-7cf25c4510dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases: Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('5c58429d-c88e-55e5-b3d4-5a1ce8b1cb04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are:', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Each bronchopulmonary segment is a functionally independent unit of the lung supplied by its own segmental bronchus and branch of the pulmonary artery, allowing surgical resection of a single segment. Segments are subdivisions of lobes and are not alveoli, bronchioles, or pleural recesses.'),
('7df1c99b-e57c-5d78-b821-01cdf536c3e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The parietal pleura is innervated by:', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Intercostal nerves (costal) and phrenic nerve (diaphragmatic/mediastinal)","Recurrent laryngeal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic chain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachial plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves (costal) and phrenic nerve (diaphragmatic/mediastinal)', 'The costal and peripheral parietal pleura is supplied by the intercostal nerves, while the diaphragmatic and mediastinal parts receive fibers from the phrenic nerve, which explains why diaphragmatic pleurisy refers pain to the shoulder via C3-C5. The visceral pleura has no somatic supply. Vagus, recurrent laryngeal, and sympathetic chains are not the principal somatic nerves of the parietal pleura.'),
('3873d7f2-31c1-56e9-acf8-f99981096f83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The visceral pleura is innervated by:', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Autonomic (sympathetic) fibers from the pulmonary plexus (insensitive to pain)","Intercostal nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic (sympathetic) fibers from the pulmonary plexus (insensitive to pain)', 'The visceral pleura receives autonomic sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers from the pulmonary plexus and is insensitive to pain and touch, responding only to stretch. Somatic pain from the pleura is carried by intercostal and phrenic nerves to the parietal layer. This explains why visceral pleural disease produces no localized somatic pain.'),
('e6b1cb46-ee49-5065-a183-04d03ccf456a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The phrenic nerve runs:', '["Down the anterior surface of the anterior scalene","enters thorax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","passes anterior to lung root mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","With the vagus nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Down the anterior surface of the anterior scalene', 'The phrenic nerve descends on the anterior surface of the anterior scalene muscle in the neck and crosses the thoracic inlet anterior to the subclavian vessels. It then runs anterior to the lung root to reach the diaphragm. Its motor fibers arise from C3-C5 and drive the diaphragm for respiration.'),
('52046750-fa3d-5480-af1b-1f90acabe999'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The pulmonary arteries carry:', '["Oxygenated blood to the lungs","Deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs","Mixed blood","Oxygenated blood to the heart","Deoxygenated blood to the left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs', 'The pulmonary trunk and arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the pulmonary capillaries for gas exchange. Despite being named arteries, they transport venous blood. Oxygenated blood returns to the left atrium through the pulmonary veins, which are the only veins carrying arterial blood.'),
('00abd3d6-fb25-5525-a31d-87946c7b3a56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The pulmonary veins carry:', '["Blood to the right atrium","Mixed blood","Blood to the right ventricle","Deoxygenated blood to the lungs","Oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium', 'The pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium, making them the only veins in the body that carry arterial blood. They do not drain into the right atrium or right ventricle, and the right ventricle ejects deoxygenated blood into the pulmonary arteries.'),
('f3c5f321-e7e4-5d49-96e4-029171c64f49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The thoracic wall is bounded by:', '["Ribs","sternum","thoracic vertebrae","and intercostal muscles","Only sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs', 'The thoracic wall is formed by the twelve pairs of ribs, the sternum, the thoracic vertebrae, and the intercostal muscles. The ribs are the principal bony framework of the chest. These elements together enclose and protect the thoracic cavity and lungs.'),
('b514ae29-29bf-5c42-a9b4-dc2132e9371b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Intercostal muscles are:', '["2 layers: external and internal","1 layer","3 layers: external","internal","and innermost intercostals"]'::jsonb, '3 layers: external', 'The intercostal muscles form three layers: the external, internal, and innermost intercostals, arranged from superficial to deep with fibers running in different directions. The innermost layer lies deep to the intercostal neurovascular bundle. These layers move the ribs during breathing.'),
('12e438d2-4d95-5466-af39-3f580d90590c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle lies:', '["In the costal groove","between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles","Deep to the skin","In the intervertebral foramen","Between pleura and endothoracic fascia"]'::jsonb, 'In the costal groove', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle (vein, artery, nerve) runs in the costal groove on the lower border of each rib, between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. This position is why chest drains and needles are inserted along the upper border of a rib to avoid the bundle; the nerve lies lowest in the groove.'),
('a12fb822-de60-5b71-b711-88baf6020538'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from:', '["Axillary artery","Thyrocervical trunk","Subclavian artery","Thoracic aorta","Costocervical trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery', 'The internal thoracic (mammary) artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery and descends behind the costal cartilages, giving anterior intercostal and perforating branches. The thyrocervical and costocervical trunks are other branches of the subclavian, while the axillary artery lies distal to the outer border of the first rib.'),
('58c1ff7d-d78f-5f29-b102-f8420c04b16a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The diaphragm has openings for:', '["Aorta (T8)","esophagus (T10)","IVC (T12)","IVC and aorta only","Aorta and esophagus only"]'::jsonb, 'esophagus (T10)', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10 transmits the esophagus together with the vagus nerves; the aortic hiatus at T12 transmits the aorta and thoracic duct; and the caval opening at T8 transmits the inferior vena cava with branches of the right phrenic nerve. Each opening lies at a distinct vertebral level, and combined ''aorta-only'' lists omit the accompanying structures.'),
('ffe04280-fdce-5e8e-8a68-14487c77def8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The right lung has:', '["One lobe","Four lobes","Three lobes","Five lobes","Two lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Three lobes', 'The right lung is divided into three lobes (superior, middle, and inferior) by the horizontal and oblique fissures. The extra middle lobe distinguishes it from the left lung, which has two lobes and a lingula. Lobar anatomy matches the bronchial branching on each side.'),
('64216fe5-e333-57c7-9f3a-7e3719efbdc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The carina is located at:', '["Laryngeal level resulting in decreased functional activity","T8-T9 level accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T1-T2 level mediated by specific receptor activation","T4-T5 level (tracheal bifurcation)","C6 level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4-T5 level (tracheal bifurcation)', 'The carina is the sharp ridge at the bifurcation of the trachea, located at the sternal angle overlying the T4-T5 level in the adult. It is identified at bronchoscopy as the point where the main bronchi separate. The larynx and cricoid lie much higher, at the C4-C6 level.'),
('19ccbef4-407b-542a-a401-e08b58e0a76c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pneumothorax is defined as:', '["Air in the mediastinum","Fluid in the pleural cavity","Air in the pleural cavity","Pus in the pleural cavity","Blood in the pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Air in the pleural cavity', 'Pneumothorax is the accumulation of air in the pleural cavity, which abolishes the negative intrapleural pressure and leads to lung collapse. Fluid, pus, and blood in the pleural space are named pleural effusion, empyema, and hemothorax, while air in the mediastinum is pneumomediastinum.'),
('7968aca3-b560-5d6e-a26d-cc6e2d21854e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The trachea bifurcates at the level of:', '["T10","T1-T2","C6","T4-T5","T8"]'::jsonb, 'T4-T5', 'The trachea bifurcates into the right and left main bronchi at the level of the T4-T5 intervertebral disc, marked externally by the sternal angle. The carina lies at this point. The larynx sits higher at the C4-C6 level, so T4-T5 is the correct airway landmark.'),
('d8537535-5f9d-52ab-8d0e-25e393957a1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which structures pass through the esophageal hiatus?', '["Esophagus and phrenic nerve","Esophagus and vagus nerves","Vagus nerves only","Esophagus only","Esophagus and aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus and vagus nerves', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10 transmits the esophagus together with the anterior and posterior vagal trunks, which accompany it into the abdomen. The aorta and thoracic duct pass through the aortic hiatus at T12, and the phrenic nerves descend alongside the mediastinum rather than through this hiatus.'),
('55909a70-4ffb-593c-814b-61004857b8bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which structures pass through the aortic hiatus?', '["Thoracic duct only","Aorta and vena cava","Aorta and esophagus","Aorta only","Aorta and thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta and thoracic duct', 'The aortic hiatus at T12 transmits the descending aorta and the thoracic duct, and it sits posteriorly behind the crura of the diaphragm. The esophagus and inferior vena cava pass through their own separate openings. The vena cava never uses the aortic hiatus.'),
('b545b62e-3799-5aad-9f35-16f6b51c6461'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which structures pass through the caval opening?', '["IVC and esophagus","Phrenic nerve only","Azygos vein and IVC","IVC and phrenic nerve branches","IVC only"]'::jsonb, 'IVC and phrenic nerve branches', 'The caval opening at T8, situated within the central tendon of the diaphragm, transmits the inferior vena cava and branches of the right phrenic nerve. The left phrenic nerve pierces the muscle separately near the apex of the pericardium. The esophagus and aorta use distinct hiatuses.'),
('28a82e7d-728a-52e9-b247-a87cef44dc0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The diaphragm is innervated by:', '["Accessory nerve","Intercostal nerves","Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)","Vagus nerve","Sympathetic trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)', 'The diaphragm is innervated exclusively by the phrenic nerve, which arises from C3-C5 and descends on the anterior scalene to reach it. Its motor fibers drive the diaphragm for respiration, and its sensory fibers supply the central diaphragm, referring pain to the shoulder. The intercostal nerves supply only the peripheral costal margin, and the vagus has no motor role in the diaphragm.'),
('a210d1a3-dedd-5bb8-ad97-f875cee61eaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The mediastinum is divided into:', '["Superior and inferior (anterior, middle, posterior)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper, middle, and lower mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior and posterior only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior, middle, inferior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior (anterior, middle, posterior)', 'The mediastinum is divided into a superior part above the pericardium and an inferior part below it, which is further split into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments. The middle compartment contains the heart and pericardium. These divisions help localize masses and describe structures passing through the chest.'),
('df95bb38-6f42-583a-8c2c-d3cc216327ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In the study of Lungs Pleura, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('e15649d9-a2b8-5606-87e4-1444823a4863'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('1a572a95-6a11-5377-bb4d-1b5ffc7157bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('8f0ca98c-928a-59eb-82f5-3b3dc0bb97d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During therapeutic management, Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('1f82cafc-4ab3-5470-a619-97ac9342a7bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases in Lungs Pleura: Maintenance dose formula:', '["CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x Css / F","Vd x Css x tau","CL x Vd / Css","Css x tau / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'CL x Css x tau / F', 'Maintenance dose depends on clearance and target concentration.'),
('112a0dd9-9dd0-5396-9f5e-12de2a2dae60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In the study of Lungs Pleura, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('e7e09336-da70-56ce-b3fe-edf17446c8cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('696e9b52-8ed5-5fe1-8675-f9e5b7df00fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Lungs Pleura: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('2d7292dc-ead8-5632-af4f-fb5383f4813c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During therapeutic management of Lungs Pleura, Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('cfbcb0c7-6e25-58b5-9bfb-9cacbfdc6913'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases in Lungs Pleura: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('a87156e3-57d8-56be-9eae-17368d490573'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess:', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is the lowest, most dependent part of the pleural cavity, formed where the costal and diaphragmatic parietal pleura meet and extending below the inferior border of the lung. Effusions and blood pool here in the upright patient. It is unrelated to the cardiac notch, the apex, the mediastinum, or the space between the lungs.'),
('a7bd5db5-3e2d-57fc-afd4-7ef89d4389a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('e1746042-6008-5f27-843a-56b13df24a86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which statement best describes the costodiaphragmatic recess in the lungs and pleura?', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'This recess lies at the junction of the costal and diaphragmatic pleurae and is the deepest dependent portion of the pleural cavity. The cardiac notch and the spaces behind or between the lungs are separate mediastinal or pulmonary landmarks, not pleural recesses.'),
('fde7933f-4bd6-52cf-9d6c-926a6dd53101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Identify the correct statement about the costodiaphragmatic recess in the lungs and pleura:', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is the lowest extent of the pleural cavity, between the costal and diaphragmatic pleurae, which the lung expands into only during deep inspiration. The cardiac notch, mediastinal space, and lung apex do not describe a pleural recess.'),
('8d1fcf74-39b9-589d-a374-a0d6ecc522df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is best described as:', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'It is the inferior dependent angle of the pleural cavity where the costal and diaphragmatic pleurae oppose each other. Fluid tends to collect here in pleural effusions because the lung does not reach this level during quiet breathing. The remaining options identify unrelated structures.'),
('f8156427-63ac-5829-b0d1-20114f9c22e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Select the statement that correctly describes the costodiaphragmatic recess in the lungs and pleura:', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The recess is the lowest part of the pleural cavity lying between the costal and diaphragmatic pleurae, and it widens with deep inspiration as the lung base descends. The cardiac notch, mediastinal space, and apex options do not represent this pleural recess.'),
('52940956-99ae-5039-918b-02d225c43e7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which option correctly describes the right main bronchus?', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left, so inhaled foreign bodies more readily enter the right lung. ''Shorter'' identifies this distinguishing feature. The remaining choices describe a horizontal course or the left main bronchus.'),
('c8963755-aec5-5913-9a77-025b0e5a679b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Lungs Pleura: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('1de70e91-f19f-5a2f-b9b2-edfb2a47a782'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which statement about the right main bronchus is most accurate in the lungs and pleura?', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertically oriented than the left one. These anatomic features make the right side the common site for aspirated foreign bodies. The other options fail to describe the characteristic right bronchial morphology.'),
('dcb32e76-01b3-50fa-bb8c-d709918d593b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Identify the correct option about the right main bronchus in the lungs and pleura:', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'Being shorter, wider, and more vertical, the right main bronchus provides the more direct route into the lung for aspirated material. ''Shorter'' states part of this distinguishing anatomy. The alternative descriptors apply to the left main bronchus or are anatomically incorrect.'),
('c31a98c7-9552-545b-aad8-dc84870c9bbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The right main bronchus is best described as:', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more nearly vertical than the left, explaining the predilection for right-sided aspiration. The options describing a right-angle orientation or left-sided equality are incorrect.'),
('0f685dba-1eb4-5b24-a3ac-76e40f6eb698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Select the option that correctly describes the right main bronchus in the lungs and pleura:', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right main bronchus is short, wide, and steeply angled, forming a more direct continuation of the trachea than the left bronchus. ''Shorter'' captures this key feature. The remaining statements do not describe the right main bronchus accurately.'),
('cf2bb439-2c52-5194-90f2-49322c92b5e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which option correctly describes bronchopulmonary segments?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'A bronchopulmonary segment is a functional unit of the lung, each supplied by its own segmental bronchus and a separate branch of the pulmonary artery and separated by connective tissue septa, which allows segmental resection. Bronchioles, lobes, recesses, and alveolar sacs represent different structural levels.'),
('ca662b2d-5c4f-5b55-a729-706f72a74fba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are best described as:', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'The bronchopulmonary segments are the functional units of the lung, each with an independent segmental bronchus, pulmonary arterial branch, and venous drainage, making them surgically resectable. Lobes, bronchioles, and alveolar sacs are not segmental units.'),
('4db40581-3fa9-5d53-ab41-76ea8c7d54de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which statement about bronchopulmonary segments is most accurate in the lungs and pleura?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments act as functional units of the lung, each ventilated by a segmental bronchus and supplied by its own pulmonary artery branch. Because their boundaries are formed by connective tissue, they can be removed individually. The other options name different pulmonary structures.'),
('762d717e-cdd7-5c51-8663-97fa4677c99a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Identify the correct response regarding bronchopulmonary segments in the lungs and pleura:', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are functional units of the lung, each receiving a segmental bronchus and a distinct pulmonary artery branch, which underlies segmental surgical resection. Bronchioles, lobes, recesses, and alveolar sacs do not define a segment.'),
('5bbc9834-073e-5aad-8f65-56e9e37a66bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are best defined as:', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'They are the functional units of the lung, each supplied by a segmental bronchus and a separate pulmonary artery branch and wrapped in connective tissue, allowing independent resection. The alternative names describe other levels of the airway or pleural structures.'),
('f73b4d5f-8d55-55b6-b57e-be9e21fd4738'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Select the option that correctly describes bronchopulmonary segments in the lungs and pleura:', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments serve as the functional units of the lung, each with its own segmental bronchus, arterial supply, and venous drainage, separated by fibrous septa. The other choices refer to bronchioles, lobes, recesses, or alveolar sacs rather than segments.'),
('e468a8a6-07b5-5a3d-ac65-603423455952'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'How many layers does the pleura have?', '["2 (parietal and visceral)","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (parietal and visceral)', 'The pleura consists of two layers: the outer parietal pleura lining the thoracic wall and the inner visceral pleura covering the lung. The potential space between them is the pleural cavity. Values other than two misrepresent the pleural layers.'),
('3cc825e5-3324-5008-8f40-0860a1271254'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The pleural cavity is:', '["Bronchial space resulting in decreased functional activity","Potential space between parietal and visceral pleura","Pericardial space mediated by specific receptor activation","Mediastinal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potential space between parietal and visceral pleura', 'The pleural cavity is a potential space between the parietal and visceral pleurae that normally contains a thin film of fluid permitting friction-free lung movement. The bronchial, pericardial, mediastinal, and lung spaces are distinct serous or anatomic compartments.'),
('43c445fa-905c-50d4-8e49-cf89d9c73b3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which option most appropriately identifies a part of the parietal pleura?', '["Mediastinal + costal","Costal","mediastinal diaphragmatic","cervical (cupula) Pulmonary + costal","Diaphragmatic + costal Visceral + parietal"]'::jsonb, 'Costal', 'The parietal pleura has four parts: costal, mediastinal, diaphragmatic, and cervical (cupula). The selected option names the costal part, which lines the inner thoracic wall. Options that combine unrelated descriptors or introduce the visceral pleura are incorrect.'),
('a792e25a-fdae-5091-8b70-aacc20d595dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The cervical pleura (cupula) extends:', '["To hyoid resulting in decreased functional activity","To sternal notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To 2nd rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Above 1st rib (2-3 cm above medial clavicle)","To clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above 1st rib (2-3 cm above medial clavicle)', 'The cupula of the pleura projects above the first rib, rising about 2-3 cm above the medial third of the clavicle, and overlies the lung apex. Because of this projection it is vulnerable to injury in stab wounds or apical lung lesions. The other levels describe lower structures.'),
('e51fea78-cec0-5523-bb94-d882d0a0de19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Lungs Pleura: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('0bf85eb1-0dc0-5992-978b-e85db57b8742'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The pleural recesses are:', '["Alveolar sacs resulting in decreased functional activity","Costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal","Mediastinal spaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchial divisions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung fissures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal', 'The pleural recesses are the costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal recesses, formed where opposing parts of the parietal pleura come into contact when the lung does not completely fill them. Alveolar sacs, mediastinal spaces, bronchial divisions, and fissures are not pleural recesses.'),
('d39d5349-9acf-5a40-b600-d8b84bc86c71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is located:', '["Costal + mediastinal","Lung apex","Mediastinal + diaphragmatic","Between costal and diaphragmatic pleura","deepest at midaxillary Lung base"]'::jsonb, 'Between costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'This recess lies between the costal and diaphragmatic pleurae at the inferior limit of the pleural cavity. It is a dependent area where pleural fluid collects, since the lung base does not normally reach it during quiet breathing. The other choices place it at the apex or between other structures.'),
('411c2fe3-7924-50dd-8682-5dfa6f2277b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The costomediastinal recess is:', '["Between costal and mediastinal pleura behind sternum","Interlobar fissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung + mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","Costal + diaphragmatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung hilum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between costal and mediastinal pleura behind sternum', 'The costomediastinal recess is the space between the costal and mediastinal pleurae behind the sternum, best seen along the anterior border of the lung. The interlobar fissure, hilum, and cardiofrenic areas are different anatomic sites.'),
('fc16fd5b-5a53-5d37-99c4-7fbc340ce5a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('15ff1a71-984a-59e9-8cec-51e233a80117'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Lungs Pleura: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('a4625e2c-a660-51f6-89f6-8decbc1043f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The fissures of the right lung are:', '["Vertical and horizontal","Horizontal and oblique","Horizontal only","Oblique only","Sagittal and coronal"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal and oblique', 'The right lung is divided by the horizontal (minor) fissure, separating the upper and middle lobes, and the oblique (major) fissure, separating the lower lobe from the upper and middle lobes. Vertical or cranio-sagittal planes do not exist as lung fissures.'),
('f18b9e85-eb0d-5424-a44b-bd96d695058f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The left lung has which fissure?', '["Sagittal","Oblique only","Vertical","Horizontal + oblique","Horizontal only"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique only', 'The left lung has only the oblique fissure, which separates the upper and lower lobes, because the middle lobe is absent on the left. Horizontal, vertical, and sagittal fissures are not found in the normal left lung.'),
('65a1fe84-e41d-5c2c-a041-23860fa1ae8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The horizontal fissure separates the:', '["Right upper from middle lobe","Upper from lower","Apex","Hilum","Middle from lower"]'::jsonb, 'Right upper from middle lobe', 'The horizontal (minor) fissure of the right lung separates the upper lobe from the middle lobe. It runs from the oblique fissure to the anterior border at the level of the fourth costal cartilage. It does not separate the middle and lower lobes or divide the left lung.'),
('ee0ee2c3-72fa-5ab9-b8b7-342fee0b5437'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The oblique fissure separates the:', '["Two lungs resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper from middle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle from lower mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower lobe from upper (and middle on right)","Lung from mediastinum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lobe from upper (and middle on right)', 'On the right the oblique fissure separates the lower lobe from the upper and middle lobes, while on the left it separates the lower lobe from the upper lobe. It therefore does not separate the two lungs, the upper from the middle lobe alone, or the lung from the mediastinum.'),
('a2979385-a7fc-5246-967f-32fcea31ed90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The cardiac notch is found on the:', '["Apex resulting in decreased functional activity","Right lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base mediated by specific receptor activation","Hilum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior border of left lung"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of left lung', 'The cardiac notch is a concavity along the anterior border of the left lung that accommodates the heart and is flanked by the lingula inferiorly. It does not occur on the right lung, apex, base, or at the hilum.'),
('ee3e79e1-74e7-5aa1-ba98-9c0636e3ce6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The lingula of the left lung is:', '["Cardiac notch resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Accessory lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tongue-like projection of upper lobe (corresponds to right middle lobe)"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue-like projection of upper lobe (corresponds to right middle lobe)', 'The lingula is a tongue-like projection of the left upper lobe that corresponds developmentally and functionally to the right middle lobe. The cardiac notch, pulmonary ligament, lower lobe, and accessory lobes are distinct anatomic features.'),
('1eba038f-2f00-5b01-8b92-6e1ea326aec4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The root of the lung (hilum) contains:', '["Only lymphatics Only pulmonary artery","Only veins Only bronchus","Bronchus pulmonary artery","pulmonary veins bronchial vessels","nerves lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchus pulmonary artery', 'The hilum transmits the main bronchus, pulmonary artery, pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, nerves, and lymphatics within a sleeve of pleura. The selected phrasing lists major hilar structures. Options naming only a single vessel type or unrelated components are incomplete.'),
('4c7f820d-d856-5927-93c2-643c380e337b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From superior to inferior, which structure lies most inferior in the right hilum?', '["Upper lobe bronchus","pulmonary artery","main bronchus","veins","Pulmonary artery bronchus"]'::jsonb, 'veins', 'In the right hilum the order from superior to inferior is the upper lobe (eparterial) bronchus, pulmonary artery, main (intermediate) bronchus, and then the pulmonary veins. The veins therefore occupy the most inferior position. The other options lie above the veins or merge structures.'),
('56101672-63c2-5943-8f20-be7ea2c77547'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From superior to inferior, which structure is most superior in the left hilum?', '["Veins","artery","bronchus","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'artery', 'In the left hilum the left pulmonary artery is the most superior structure, followed by the left main bronchus and then the pulmonary veins. Liver and kidney are unrelated to hilum structure. The bronchus and veins lie below the artery.'),
('ab8967d4-0980-536f-8834-59935198d7ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The pulmonary ligament is:', '["Connective cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural fold below hilum allowing lung root movement","Diaphragm part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pericardial fold leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural fold below hilum allowing lung root movement', 'The pulmonary ligament is a fold of pleura extending downward from the hilum, joining the mediastinal pleura to the visceral pleura and allowing movement of the lung root during respiration. It is not a connective cord, pericardial fold, or part of the diaphragm.'),
('88bc7bb5-357a-58b6-80b4-414a5e56d830'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Compared with the right, the left main bronchus is:', '["Thicker walled","Longer","narrower","more horizontal (aorta arches over it)","Same More vertical Shorter wider"]'::jsonb, 'Longer', 'The left main bronchus is longer, narrower, and more horizontal than the right because the aortic arch passes over it. ''Longer'' identifies this distinguishing feature. Descriptors of the right bronchus, equality, or mixed terms are incorrect.'),
('c3e2510a-8009-511e-bfa9-051aa7ddefc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The trachea bifurcates at:', '["T10","C6","T4/T5 (carina)","T6","T2"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 (carina)', 'The trachea divides into the right and left main bronchi at the carina, which lies at the level of the sternal angle, approximately T4/T5. The carina is an important endoscopic landmark. The other vertebral levels are incorrect.'),
('8507b178-9973-519e-995c-7b67ad48a258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'A bronchopulmonary segment is:', '["Lung lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anatomical lung unit supplied by segmental bronchus + vessels","Alveolar sac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Entire lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical lung unit supplied by segmental bronchus + vessels', 'A bronchopulmonary segment is an anatomic unit of the lung supplied by a segmental bronchus and its accompanying pulmonary artery branch, with independent venous drainage and connective tissue boundaries. Lobes, terminal bronchioles, and alveolar sacs are different structural divisions of the lung.'),
('0b0d990a-c135-55f0-bdd4-f506d979b932'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Into how many bronchopulmonary segments is the right lung divided?', '["10 segments (3 upper, 2 middle, 5 lower)","9 segments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","8 segments mediated by specific receptor activation","12 segments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","6 segments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10 segments (3 upper, 2 middle, 5 lower)', 'The right lung contains ten bronchopulmonary segments: three in the upper lobe, two in the middle lobe, and five in the lower lobe. This segmental arrangement allows individual segmental resection.'),
('9253fe6a-dcfc-5dfa-a139-1b7b9bd25a52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Into how many bronchopulmonary segments is the left lung divided?', '["12 segments","7 segments","8-10 segments (some fusion)","6 segments","10 segments"]'::jsonb, '8-10 segments (some fusion)', 'The left lung has eight to ten bronchopulmonary segments because some of its segments are fused, particularly in the upper lobe where the apical and posterior segments often unite. The upper lobe usually has four to five and the lower lobe five segments.'),
('4ee0b587-3c17-5670-a6e7-508b4aa173a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The bronchial arteries supply:', '["Alveoli resulting in decreased functional activity","Trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung parenchyma (bronchi, bronchioles) and visceral pleura","Parietal pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung parenchyma (bronchi, bronchioles) and visceral pleura', 'Bronchial arteries (systemic branches of the thoracic aorta) supply the lung parenchyma, the bronchi and bronchioles, and the visceral pleura. Gas exchange in the alveoli uses pulmonary arterial blood, and the parietal pleura is supplied by intercostal arteries.'),
('ac5b3671-03cf-5be1-9278-663d64254ec7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The right bronchial artery typically arises from:', '["Subclavian resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal thoracic mediated by specific receptor activation","Left bronchial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3rd right posterior intercostal artery or aorta"]'::jsonb, '3rd right posterior intercostal artery or aorta', 'The right bronchial artery usually arises from the third right posterior intercostal artery, itself a branch of the descending thoracic aorta, although a direct aortic origin can occur. Subclavian, internal thoracic, and left bronchial sources are incorrect.'),
('2ac0dbdc-58d4-58e3-990e-09836cbfa279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The left bronchial arteries arise from:', '["Right bronchial","Subclavian","Costocervical trunk","Internal thoracic","Thoracic aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic aorta', 'The left bronchial arteries arise directly from the descending thoracic aorta, usually two in number. They do not originate from the subclavian, costocervical trunk, internal thoracic, or right bronchial artery.'),
('42282e09-6c22-5fbe-b65d-b74218808080'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('0a54c6af-eaa7-5827-8374-334d050e478c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Lungs Pleura: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('af2f00a1-aea0-5a3b-943d-11793643f35a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During therapeutic management of Lungs Pleura, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('62343966-8397-506a-8f36-ae10b6ecc276'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('a469d6ff-97b5-5a57-8162-e190513fe64e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Pain from the costal pleura is referred to the:', '["Neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Chest wall (intercostal nerves)","Back mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chest wall (intercostal nerves)', 'The costal pleura is supplied by intercostal nerves, so its pain is felt locally over the chest wall. Referral to the shoulder occurs with central diaphragmatic pleura (phrenic, C3-C5), while neck and back arise from other parietal pleural areas.'),
('0262c6aa-81f4-5bc9-97ca-e2386b506c7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: diaphragmatic pleura (central) pain referred to:?', '["Back resulting in decreased functional activity","Abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chest wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder tip (C3-C5 via phrenic)","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shoulder tip (C3-C5 via phrenic)', 'The central diaphragmatic pleura is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3-C5), so its irritation is felt as referred pain at the shoulder tip, which shares the same spinal segments. The costal pleura, innervated by intercostal nerves, causes localized chest wall pain instead of shoulder tip pain.'),
('48437621-1c71-5469-aea5-8d0314732a11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Pulmonary plexus:'' is:', '["Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic fibers","Only motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic fibers', 'The pulmonary plexus is formed from parasympathetic (vagal) and sympathetic contributions that regulate bronchial muscle, glands, and pulmonary vessels. Options describing only one fiber type or only motor/sensory fibers are incomplete because the plexus carries both autonomic divisions.'),
('ec3f416b-d995-5289-ae97-db6b8eb543bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: apex of lung projects:?', '["C7 resulting in decreased functional activity","T1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternal notch mediated by specific receptor activation","1st rib level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2-3 cm above medial 1/3 of clavicle"]'::jsonb, '2-3 cm above medial 1/3 of clavicle', 'The apex of the lung rises about 2-3 cm above the medial third of the clavicle, enclosed in the cervical pleura and capped by the suprapleural membrane. The 1st rib and T1 vertebra are lower thoracic landmarks and do not indicate the apex projection.'),
('724e9aa7-b4e8-583e-b1f9-42bc16b7f409'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: base of lung:?', '["Convex resulting in decreased functional activity","Irregular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pointed mediated by specific receptor activation","Flat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Concave rests on diaphragm (dome-shaped)"]'::jsonb, 'Concave rests on diaphragm (dome-shaped)', 'The base of the lung is deeply concave so that it fits over the dome of the diaphragm and contains the thin sharp inferior border. A convex, flat, pointed, or irregular surface would not conform to the diaphragmatic dome.'),
('8f196bc7-d764-519b-bdb9-5b636ec6a63c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: costal surface of lung:?', '["Irregular","Concave","Smooth and convex","contacts costal pleura","Flat Roughened"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth and convex', 'The costal surface of the lung is smooth and convex because it lies against the inner aspect of the chest wall, covered by costal pleura. Concave and irregular profiles describe the mediastinal or inferior surfaces rather than the costal surface.'),
('5f92007a-8d2c-54e6-b6e3-cd0332b7f69a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: mediastinal surface contains:?', '["Costal surface resulting in decreased functional activity","Apex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hilum (root of lung) and cardiac impression","Base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fissures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hilum (root of lung) and cardiac impression', 'The mediastinal surface carries the hilum (root of the lung) and shows the cardiac impression produced by the heart. The apex, base, costal surface, and fissures are features of other regions of the lung, not contents of the mediastinal surface.'),
('a7231ec4-bba3-5d7f-bd8c-c8e794ee5a54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: left lung cardiac impression due to:?', '["Heart extends more to left","Pericardium on left","Left lung smaller","Left bronchus longer","Aorta on left"]'::jsonb, 'Heart extends more to left', 'The left lung is deeply indented by a cardiac impression because the heart extends more to the left side of the mediastinum. Pericardial position alone, smaller left lung size, bronchus length, or the aorta do not explain the groove on the left lung.'),
('b1e9f2a9-2513-502c-8361-ab69566bb133'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Trachea lined by:'' is:', '["Stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Simple cuboidal mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transitional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells', 'The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with interspersed goblet cells, which trap mucus and foreign particles and sweep them upward. Stratified squamous, simple cuboidal, simple columnar, and transitional epithelia line other sites such as the pharynx, alveoli, and urinary tract.'),
('5fead444-8932-50d7-bf83-fce097af0e2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: trachea reinforced by:?', '["cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrocartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete bony rings mediated by specific receptor activation","Elastic cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings (16-20)"]'::jsonb, 'C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings (16-20)', 'The trachea is kept patent by 16-20 C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings, whose open ends face posteriorly and are joined by the trachealis muscle. Fibrous or elastic cartilage and bony rings are not the reinforcement of the trachea.'),
('3c7612ea-4207-5c08-8bee-58c545449ce2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: trachealis muscle connects:?', '["Sides resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior ends accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea to larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","Open ends of C-shaped cartilages posteriorly","Trachea to esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Open ends of C-shaped cartilages posteriorly', 'The trachealis muscle bridges the open posterior ends of the C-shaped cartilage rings, closing the gap posteriorly and allowing the esophagus to bulge forward during swallowing. It does not join the sides, the anterior ends, the larynx, or the esophagus itself.'),
('3c448491-7e5c-5c65-b0b0-33dfc158ff95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: esophagus located:?', '["Anterior mediastinum","Lateral to trachea","Anterior to trachea","Posterior to trachea","anterior to vertebrae Posterior to vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to trachea', 'In the superior mediastinum the esophagus lies posterior to the trachea and continues behind the left main bronchus into the posterior mediastinum. It is not anterior to or lateral to the trachea, and its position is not within the anterior mediastinum.'),
('9532f1d1-54e9-5089-8371-c158d93fff8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: phrenic nerve at hilum:?', '["Below hilum","Anterior to lung root","Posterior to hilum","Within ligament","Between PA and PV"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to lung root', 'The phrenic nerve descends anterior to the lung root (hilum) as it runs toward the diaphragm, also passing anterior to the pulmonary artery and vein. The vagus nerve, by contrast, passes behind the hilum, which is a useful pair to memorize.'),
('f8a7cb9d-2edd-54f4-9d21-b6a1f7868a7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: vagus nerve at hilum:?', '["Within ligament","Posterior to lung root","Above hilum","Between bronchus and artery","Anterior to hilum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to lung root', 'The vagus nerve runs posterior to the lung root (hilum) before breaking into the pulmonary plexus, and it also contributes to the esophageal plexus. The phrenic nerve lies anterior to the hilum, so anterior, above, or between positions are incorrect for the vagus.'),
('18d5d07c-4244-59b5-9bcc-bedcaa414588'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Azygos vein arches over:'' is:', '["Esophagus","Root of left lung","Aorta","Root of right lung","Trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Root of right lung', 'The azygos vein arches forward over the root of the right lung to enter the superior vena cava just above the hilum. The aortic arch crosses the left lung root, so the azygos relationship is specific to the right side.'),
('07a878db-d25d-50a7-812b-5f3938e0b9e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: trachea length:?', '["3-5 cm","15-20 cm","20-25 cm","5 cm","10-12 cm"]'::jsonb, '10-12 cm', 'The trachea measures about 10-12 cm from the cricoid cartilage to its bifurcation at the sternal angle (T4/T5). Lengths of 3-5 cm, 5 cm, or 15-25 cm do not match the accepted adult tracheal length.'),
('4be76e6b-da2c-5608-acb8-d0752a0e2d9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: main bronchi divide into:?', '["Only lobar resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobar (secondary) -> segmental (tertiary)","Alveolar ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct to bronchioles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only segmental leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobar (secondary) -> segmental (tertiary)', 'Each main bronchus divides into lobar (secondary) bronchi, which branch further into segmental (tertiary) bronchi that supply individual bronchopulmonary segments. The airway does not jump directly from main bronchi to alveolar ducts or bronchioles.'),
('ebdc58ed-9004-5b02-92aa-4b5e792366a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: bronchioles lack:?', '["Epithelium","Cartilage (smooth muscle only)","Smooth muscle","Elastic fibers","Basement membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage (smooth muscle only)', 'Bronchioles lack cartilage in their walls and rely on smooth muscle and elastic tissue to regulate airflow. The retained cartilage in the larger bronchi is what distinguishes them from bronchioles, which have smooth muscle as the principal wall component.'),
('f8a10980-507e-5391-a865-739d4cdc04da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: terminal bronchiole:?', '["Alveolar sac resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Last part of conducting zone (no gas exchange)","First respiratory zone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Last part of conducting zone (no gas exchange)', 'The terminal bronchiole is the last airway of the conducting zone and carries no gas exchange because its wall lacks alveoli. The respiratory bronchiole, with scattered alveoli, marks the beginning of the respiratory zone distal to it.'),
('068e3b6d-8cb2-5c6a-aacd-682add9a617b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: respiratory bronchiole:?', '["Segmental bronchus resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar sac mediated by specific receptor activation","First part of respiratory zone (some alveoli)","Conducting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First part of respiratory zone (some alveoli)', 'The respiratory bronchiole is the first part of the respiratory zone because some of its wall carries alveoli that permit gas exchange. It lies distal to the terminal bronchiole, which is purely conducting, and proximal to alveolar ducts and sacs.'),
('a77b03eb-0a11-506a-933a-668d5fe7d1be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Alveolar ducts end in:'' is:', '["Pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchioles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveolar sacs (clusters of alveoli)"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar sacs (clusters of alveoli)', 'Alveolar ducts end in alveolar sacs, which are clusters of alveoli that open into a common atrium. They do not terminate in the pleura, bronchioles, terminal bronchioles, or capillaries.'),
('129b7076-e6e8-5a00-90dd-b06c527cf911'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: type i pneumocytes:?', '["Macrophages resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuboidal producing surfactant mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cells covering 95% of alveolar surface (gas exchange)","Goblet cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cells covering 95% of alveolar surface (gas exchange)', 'Type I pneumocytes are flattened squamous cells covering about 95% of the alveolar surface and form the thin barrier across which gas exchange occurs. Surfactant production belongs to cuboidal type II cells, and macrophages are the phagocytic alveolar defense cells.'),
('97ec9021-16ba-5e26-aaf9-a83fc848df50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: type ii pneumocytes:?', '["Ciliated resulting in decreased functional activity","Goblet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produce surfactant (cuboidal)","Line alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phagocytic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produce surfactant (cuboidal)', 'Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal cells that synthesize and secrete surfactant, the phospholipid that lowers alveolar surface tension. They are not ciliated, goblet, phagocytic, or simple lining cells.'),
('06b42521-9250-504f-9b8c-e309ddf613ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: surfactant function:?', '["Kills bacteria","Secretes mucus","Increases surface tension","Reduces surface tension","prevents alveolar collapse Transports O2"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces surface tension', 'Surfactant reduces alveolar surface tension, which prevents alveolar collapse at end-expiration and lowers the work of breathing. Increasing surface tension, secreting mucus, or killing bacteria are not functions of surfactant.'),
('0f2fefd2-856c-5b0e-a526-f2aae481744e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: pneumothorax:?', '["Air in pleural cavity causing lung collapse","Blood in pleura accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluid in pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Air in pleural cavity causing lung collapse', 'Pneumothorax is the entry of air into the pleural cavity, which destroys the negative pleural pressure and allows the lung to collapse. Blood, fluid, tumor, or infection in the pleural space represent hemothorax, effusion, neoplasm, or empyema rather than pneumothorax.'),
('39fc4bbf-7596-596d-8897-e16bef8698a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: pleural effusion:?', '["Pleural fibrosis","Pleural inflammation","Fluid accumulation in pleural cavity","Air in pleura","Blood in pleura"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid accumulation in pleural cavity', 'Pleural effusion is the abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity, commonly from heart failure, infection, or malignancy. It differs from air (pneumothorax), blood (hemothorax), and pure inflammation without a collection.'),
('57d39c4b-0db8-5d51-a98b-c4f94262090d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''The cardiac notch is a feature of which lung'' is:', '["Only the middle lobe","Right lung","Both lungs equally","The lingula only","Left lung (on the anterior border)"]'::jsonb, 'Left lung (on the anterior border)', 'The cardiac notch is a concave indentation on the anterior border of the left lung, produced by the left-sided heart. The right lung has no cardiac notch, and the lingula is a separate tongue-like projection of the left upper lobe.'),
('0a2fe817-17ea-5878-9475-065dd3f19e1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: the lungs are located:?', '["In the peritoneum","In the pericardial cavity","In the abdominal cavity","In the mediastinum","In the pleural cavities"]'::jsonb, 'In the pleural cavities', 'Each lung lies within its own pleural cavity, enveloped by visceral and parietal pleura, with the mediastinum between the two pleural sacs. The heart and trachea occupy the mediastinum, and the lungs are not within the peritoneum or pericardial cavity.'),
('15d1294a-459e-5114-b363-913adc2fb895'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: the pleura consists of:?', '["Diaphragmatic fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parietal pleura accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal pleura (lining thoracic wall) and visceral pleura (covering lung)","Only visceral pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endothoracic fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal pleura (lining thoracic wall) and visceral pleura (covering lung)', 'The pleura consists of two continuous layers: parietal pleura lining the thoracic wall, diaphragm, and mediastinum, and visceral pleura covering the lung. Single-layer answers are incomplete, and the endothoracic fascia lies outside rather than forming the parietal pleura.'),
('5c200833-d776-5d6f-9615-f7d37f04bc0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: the costodiaphragmatic recess is:?', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is the lowest and deepest part of the pleural cavity, where costal and diaphragmatic pleura meet, and it is where pleural fluid collects in effusion. It is not the cardiac notch, mediastinal space, or apex of the lung.'),
('1c7273a2-b70e-5e07-8bcd-21089263922e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: the left lung has:?', '["1 lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","3 lobes and 2 fissures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 lobes without a fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","2 lobes (superior and inferior) and 1 oblique fissure","4 lobes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 lobes (superior and inferior) and 1 oblique fissure', 'The left lung has two lobes, superior and inferior, separated by a single oblique fissure, and it lacks a horizontal fissure. Three lobes with two fissures and a middle lobe are characteristic of the right lung, not the left.'),
('f4698ae6-be0d-546e-b8db-efa59bf2e1ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: the left lung also has:?', '["fissures resulting in decreased functional activity","A middle lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A horizontal fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","3 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)', 'The lingula is a tongue-like extension of the left upper lobe on the anterior border and is the homologue of the right middle lobe. The left lung has no separate middle lobe, horizontal fissure, or third lobe.'),
('160bd051-4715-570e-bd3b-a6ab670bce5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''The hilum of the lung contains:'' is:', '["Only the pulmonary veins","Bronchus","pulmonary artery and veins","bronchial vessels","lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchus', 'The root of the lung passing through the hilum contains the bronchus, pulmonary artery, two pulmonary veins, bronchial vessels, lymphatics, and nerves. The answer ''Only the pulmonary veins'' is wrong because the hilum carries more structures than veins, and the bronchus is the key airway component.'),
('2baa4b55-fe67-5b64-b567-60e3269ecc04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: at the hilum of the right lung?', '["the arrangement is:","Pulmonary veins above all","Bronchus below artery and veins","Pulmonary artery above bronchus","Eparterial bronchus (above pulmonary artery)"]'::jsonb, 'Eparterial bronchus (above pulmonary artery)', 'At the right hilum the upper lobe bronchus lies above the pulmonary artery, a feature described as eparterial. This arrangement differs from the left side, where the pulmonary artery lies above the bronchus, so the eparterial description is specific to the right lung.'),
('01d966ff-edc5-53b3-bba6-771b9f44b28a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: at the hilum of the left lung?', '["the arrangement is:","Artery in the middle","Bronchus in the middle","Bronchus above pulmonary artery","Pulmonary artery above bronchus"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary artery above bronchus', 'At the left hilum the pulmonary artery lies above the bronchus, and both are anterior to the veins. This contrasts with the right side, where the eparterial bronchus passes above the artery, so the two hila must be memorized separately.'),
('8e8acf55-6034-5b51-914d-501d3fc72275'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: the trachea bifurcates into:?', '["Directly into segmental bronchi resulting in decreased functional activity","Into 3 lobar bronchi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right and left main bronchi at the level of the sternal angle (T4/T5)","At the level of C6 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Into the larynx and bronchi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left main bronchi at the level of the sternal angle (T4/T5)', 'The trachea bifurcates at the level of the sternal angle (T4/T5) into the right and left main bronchi, with the carina marking the division internally. Bifurcation does not occur at C6 and does not branch directly into lobar or segmental bronchi.'),
('13f95f44-bb4c-5063-943a-21e52f667210'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: the right main bronchus is:?', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left, which is why inhaled foreign bodies preferentially lodge in the right side. The left main bronchus is longer and more horizontal as it passes beneath the aortic arch.'),
('5de328c0-9c33-5dbf-9216-3aef0c827ef5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: bronchopulmonary segments are:?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are functional units of the lung, each supplied by a segmental bronchus, a segmental pulmonary artery, and enclosed by connective tissue, and each can be surgically resected separately. Bronchioles, lobes, pleural recesses, and alveolar sacs are different structural levels.'),
('32d6fc6b-7111-5e63-b94e-4b93401a1554'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''The parietal pleura is innervated by:'' is:', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Intercostal nerves (costal) and phrenic nerve (diaphragmatic/mediastinal)","Recurrent laryngeal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic chain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachial plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves (costal) and phrenic nerve (diaphragmatic/mediastinal)', 'The costal parietal pleura is innervated by intercostal nerves and the diaphragmatic and mediastinal parietal pleura by the phrenic nerve, so irritation causes localized or referred pain. The vagus, recurrent laryngeal, sympathetic chain, and brachial plexus do not supply the parietal pleura.'),
('303d5869-1caa-592c-9391-fc2dcfc60b88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: the visceral pleura is innervated by:?', '["Vagus nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Autonomic (sympathetic) fibers from the pulmonary plexus (insensitive to pain)","Intercostal nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic (sympathetic) fibers from the pulmonary plexus (insensitive to pain)', 'The visceral pleura receives autonomic fibers, chiefly sympathetic, from the pulmonary plexus and has no somatic sensory supply, so it is insensitive to pain and produces no localized pain. Phrenic, intercostal, and spinal nerve endings do not innervate the visceral pleura.'),
('1975e54d-2900-5af1-a3d4-c02f83f3c4a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: the phrenic nerve runs:?', '["Down the anterior surface of the anterior scalene","enters thorax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","passes anterior to lung root mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","With the vagus nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Down the anterior surface of the anterior scalene', 'The phrenic nerve runs downward on the anterior surface of the anterior scalene muscle, passes between the subclavian vein and artery, and enters the thorax anterior to the lung root. It does not travel lateral to the trachea or inside the vagus nerve.'),
('8f59664e-96ee-5f4e-be5d-d3970b7c8d0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: the pulmonary arteries carry:?', '["Oxygenated blood to the lungs","Deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs","Mixed blood","Oxygenated blood to the heart","Deoxygenated blood to the left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs', 'The pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs for oxygenation, making them the only arteries in the adult that conduct deoxygenated blood. They do not carry oxygenated or mixed blood and do not deliver blood to the left atrium.'),
('ed60fea5-b668-5392-b084-c919fbec64a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: the pulmonary veins carry:?', '["Blood to the right atrium","Mixed blood","Blood to the right ventricle","Deoxygenated blood to the lungs","Oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium', 'The pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lungs and empty into the left atrium. They do not drain into the right atrium or right ventricle and do not carry deoxygenated blood to the lungs.'),
('7fcf5490-23a8-5a11-afc7-3ae051d87b0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: the thoracic wall is bounded by:?', '["Ribs","sternum","thoracic vertebrae","and intercostal muscles","Only sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs', 'The thoracic wall is bounded by the ribs, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, and intercostal muscles, which together form the cage housing the lungs. ''Only sternum'' is incorrect because the wall requires the entire rib cage and the intercostal musculature.'),
('128a59f2-134c-58a7-819c-082a2eafe574'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Intercostal muscles are:'' is:', '["2 layers: external and internal","1 layer","3 layers: external","internal","and innermost intercostals"]'::jsonb, '3 layers: external', 'The intercostal muscles are arranged in three layers: the external, internal, and innermost intercostals. Descriptions of only one or two layers are incomplete because the innermost intercostal layer is a distinct component between adjacent ribs.'),
('cb9f9bf1-5716-54cb-8e9f-7ed797162b24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: the intercostal neurovascular bundle lies:?', '["In the costal groove","between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles","Deep to the skin","In the intervertebral foramen","Between pleura and endothoracic fascia"]'::jsonb, 'In the costal groove', 'The intercostal nerve, artery, and vein run together in the costal groove on the lower border of each rib, lying between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. This groove location protects the bundle and is the guide for safe needle insertion in the thoracic wall.'),
('fbb79342-0707-5292-ba84-09bfdb460f13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: the internal thoracic artery arises from:?', '["Axillary artery","Thyrocervical trunk","Subclavian artery","Thoracic aorta","Costocervical trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery', 'The internal thoracic (mammary) artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery and descends behind the costal cartilages to give anterior intercostal and perforating branches. The axillary, thyrocervical, costocervical, and thoracic aortic origins are incorrect.'),
('21f86702-d5c8-5e1c-ba37-903d04a7cdf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: the diaphragm has openings for:?', '["Aorta (T8)","esophagus (T10)","IVC (T12)","IVC and aorta only","Aorta and esophagus only"]'::jsonb, 'esophagus (T10)', 'The main openings of the diaphragm are the aortic hiatus at T12, the esophageal hiatus at T10, and the inferior vena caval opening at T8. Only the esophageal hiatus at T10 is stated correctly; the aorta is at T12 and the IVC at T8.'),
('8fcba093-a2a8-540a-8608-1dbc943dd2b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: the right lung has:?', '["One lobe","Four lobes","Three lobes","Five lobes","Two lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Three lobes', 'The right lung has three lobes, superior, middle, and inferior, separated by the oblique and horizontal fissures. One, two, four, or five lobes are incorrect; two lobes with one oblique fissure describe the left lung.'),
('72e1b38a-2b4d-59ee-9cde-00223339536a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: the carina is located at:?', '["Laryngeal level resulting in decreased functional activity","T8-T9 level accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T1-T2 level mediated by specific receptor activation","T4-T5 level (tracheal bifurcation)","C6 level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4-T5 level (tracheal bifurcation)', 'The carina is the internal ridge at the tracheal bifurcation and lies at the level of the sternal angle, corresponding to T4/T5. It is not located at the larynx, C6, or the T8-T9 level.'),
('bbc7cc8b-27af-5d11-8ba1-304aea61e772'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Pneumothorax is defined as:'' is:', '["Air in the mediastinum","Fluid in the pleural cavity","Air in the pleural cavity","Pus in the pleural cavity","Blood in the pleural cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Air in the pleural cavity', 'Pneumothorax is defined as the presence of air within the pleural cavity, which disrupts the negative pleural pressure and typically causes lung collapse. Fluid (effusion), pus (empyema), blood (hemothorax), and air in the mediastinum (pneumomediastinum) are distinct conditions.'),
('25c11c04-455d-54a2-80a2-6dbc0ddc6de0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The trachea bifurcates into the right and left main bronchi at the level of which vertebra?', '["T10","T1-T2","C6","T4-T5","T8"]'::jsonb, 'T4-T5', 'The trachea divides at the carina, which projects onto the level of the sternal angle, corresponding to the T4-T5 vertebral level in adults. The other levels (T10, C6, T8, T1-T2) relate to other structures and do not mark the tracheal bifurcation.'),
('37ea594e-e762-56e8-8925-aa712a860559'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which structures pass through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm?', '["Esophagus and phrenic nerve","Esophagus and vagus nerves","Vagus nerves only","Esophagus only","Esophagus and aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus and vagus nerves', 'The esophageal hiatus transmits the esophagus and both vagus nerves through the crus of the diaphragm at the T10 level. The phrenic nerve enters via the caval opening, the aorta and thoracic duct pass through the aortic hiatus, and the IVC uses the caval opening.'),
('2354817d-d49f-5df9-8b08-265b1f49ebd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which structures pass through the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm?', '["Thoracic duct only","Aorta and vena cava","Aorta and esophagus","Aorta only","Aorta and thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta and thoracic duct', 'The aortic hiatus lies at the T12 level and transmits the aorta and the thoracic duct. The esophagus uses the esophageal hiatus and the IVC the caval opening, so the alternative combinations are incorrect.'),
('ac428859-f13c-5671-ba7e-494d8e59a732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which structures pass through the caval opening (foramen vena cavae) of the diaphragm?', '["IVC and esophagus","Phrenic nerve only","Azygos vein and IVC","IVC and phrenic nerve branches","IVC only"]'::jsonb, 'IVC and phrenic nerve branches', 'The caval opening lies at the T8 level and transmits the inferior vena cava together with branches of the right phrenic nerve. The esophagus, aorta, and azygos vein do not pass through this opening.'),
('439fa28c-aa90-529d-acc5-cfe8611640ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which nerve provides the motor innervation of the diaphragm?', '["Accessory nerve","Intercostal nerves","Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)","Vagus nerve","Sympathetic trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)', 'The diaphragm receives its motor innervation from the phrenic nerves (C3-C5); the intercostal nerves carry only some sensory fibers from the periphery. The vagus, accessory nerve, and sympathetic trunk do not supply the diaphragm.'),
('cf2c6baf-8b78-5c22-a4c7-bd6c1722815b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'How is the mediastinum divided anatomically?', '["Superior and inferior (anterior, middle, posterior)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper, middle, and lower mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior and posterior only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior, middle, inferior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior (anterior, middle, posterior)', 'The mediastinum is divided into a superior part and an inferior part, with the inferior part further subdivided into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments by the pericardium. Divisions into left/right, upper/middle/lower, or anterior/posterior-only do not reflect the standard anatomical scheme.'),
('ed167f87-ee66-59d1-bae4-40a5fa6b19c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The cardiac impression on the medial surface of the left lung is due to which of the following?', '["Heart extends more to left","Pericardium on left","Left lung smaller","Left bronchus longer","Aorta on left"]'::jsonb, 'Heart extends more to left', 'The heart extends more to the left of the midline, so it indents the medial surface of the left lung to form the cardiac impression. A pericardium on the left, a smaller left lung, a longer left bronchus, or the aorta do not cause this impression.'),
('03091329-95ec-5701-b4d4-436454e7b3e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During therapeutic management of Lungs Pleura, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('333d8f85-85f0-5713-9721-34b7edbff9e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases in Lungs Pleura: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('0a70f1a2-02bd-5fe1-83bf-86a8d98591ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In the study of Lungs Pleura, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('d50877f1-7100-5fe3-97ba-daa26653a32c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('0d6507af-402b-5dd1-a792-a381793bf664'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Where are the lungs located within the thorax?', '["In the peritoneum","In the pericardial cavity","In the abdominal cavity","In the mediastinum","In the pleural cavities"]'::jsonb, 'In the pleural cavities', 'The lungs lie within the pleural cavities, one on each side of the mediastinum, separated from the thoracic wall and diaphragm by the pleural space. They do not occupy the pericardial or peritoneal cavities, the abdominal cavity, or the mediastinum.'),
('d6d5878e-9005-512e-a985-00d3604e7dff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During therapeutic management of Lungs Pleura, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('731b74c4-9659-51b4-85e2-7458eb34ff7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases in Lungs Pleura: EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('297e00b9-f69f-5445-b947-c13ed6741a70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In the study of Lungs Pleura, Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('90b58a9c-47b4-52dc-b391-bbc3c8d8412f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('fa559525-2e25-54a5-87a9-1f73a3e5e7df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which of the following best describes the costodiaphragmatic recess?', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is the lowest part of the pleural cavity, formed where the costal pleura reflects onto the diaphragmatic pleura; it is deepest at the midaxillary line and is the site where pleural fluid collects when a patient is upright. The other options describe the cardiac notch, apex, mediastinal space, or the space between the lungs.'),
('491387c7-fe5a-53f5-b37c-48e94d35a803'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During therapeutic management of Lungs Pleura, Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('7d4ff483-b2fa-5b44-854d-73810e64a43a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases in Lungs Pleura: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('e051e157-0a24-5194-832f-6f870f06c0c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In the study of Lungs Pleura, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('6306440c-b510-5656-9fcc-bec4baf5b490'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('b0ccec3b-33a1-59ff-b3ed-46a93ae28a02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Lungs Pleura: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('fd8b3bae-1254-5c7c-a355-aaae25318a86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which of the following best describes the right main bronchus?', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left, which is why inhaled foreign bodies preferentially enter the right side. The options stating it equals the left main bronchus or lies at a right angle are incorrect.'),
('d003b689-59e5-5d9b-a51c-270f2f6dc8ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical cases in Lungs Pleura: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('4eacff58-af1d-54a2-8d26-6250d024c62a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In the study of Lungs Pleura, Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('bd827313-675c-50ba-aee1-54af47d6a8e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Lungs Pleura: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('919ab4e5-b5f7-5caa-8885-4c33c2736039'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Which of the following best describes the right main bronchus?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left main bronchus. The other options incorrectly state that it matches the left side or courses at a right angle.'),
('b79329d7-22c1-5d92-b974-66e3e9bb3a24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical item 228 (Regarding Which of the following best describes the right main bronchus?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?), which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Shorter","wider","and more vertical than the left","Same as the left","At a 90-degree angle"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'Because the right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical than the left, foreign bodies more often enter the right lung. The options denying this difference are inaccurate.'),
('33c3103b-35f1-5374-b595-8633669148ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which of the following best describes bronchopulmonary segments?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'A bronchopulmonary segment is a functional unit of the lung, supplied by a segmental (tertiary) bronchus and its own pulmonary artery branch, and it can be resected surgically. Bronchioles, lobes, alveolar sacs, and pleural recesses are not the designation for this unit.'),
('9cc24276-e198-529c-8f81-5353e228c253'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Which of the following best describes bronchopulmonary segments?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are the functional units of the lung, each with a dedicated segmental bronchus, artery, and limited collateral ventilation. They are not bronchioles, lobes, alveolar sacs, or pleural recesses.'),
('ce1604b4-ad09-559e-8a60-94ee537e860c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical item 231 (Regarding Which of the following best describes bronchopulmonary segments?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?), which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Each lobe of the lung is divided into bronchopulmonary segments, which are functional units resected separately in segmental surgery. Bronchioles, lung lobes, pleural recesses, and alveolar sacs each describe different structures.'),
('89bdef50-70b3-546d-ac0f-92d73cb8a08c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical item 232 (Regarding Which of the following best describes bronchopulmonary segments?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?), which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are the functional units of the lung, each served by a segmental bronchus and an independent pulmonary artery branch. The other options name airways, lobe groupings, alveoli, or pleural spaces rather than this segment.'),
('8a85148f-6d2d-5186-b672-20a606e3658d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding clinical item 233 (Regarding Which of the following best describes bronchopulmonary segments?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?), which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'A bronchopulmonary segment is the functional unit of the lung, demarcated by connective tissue and each supplied by a segmental bronchus with its pulmonary artery. Bronchioles, lobes, alveolar sacs, and recesses are not the correct designation.'),
('22c1b034-76d2-5f27-98b3-320c12085e6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: subclavian artery on the left originates from:?', '["Aortic arch (directly)","Vertebral","Subclavian vein","Left common carotid","Brachiocephalic trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (directly)', 'The left subclavian artery is the fourth branch to arise directly from the aortic arch, distal to the left common carotid. On the right side, by contrast, the subclavian artery springs from the brachiocephalic trunk.'),
('456853a3-bfbe-59e2-bc41-3eb05f5766f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'How many layers does the pleura of the lung have?', '["2 (parietal and visceral)","3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 (parietal and visceral)', 'The pleura is a serous membrane composed of two layers: the parietal pleura lining the thoracic walls and the visceral pleura investing the lung. The other numeric choices do not reflect this two-layer arrangement.'),
('a7914787-86b4-5d16-84a0-27e7c95db3f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'How is the pleural cavity best described?', '["Bronchial space resulting in decreased functional activity","Potential space between parietal and visceral pleura","Pericardial space mediated by specific receptor activation","Mediastinal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potential space between parietal and visceral pleura', 'The pleural cavity is a potential space containing a thin film of serous fluid between the parietal and visceral layers of pleura. The other options name the bronchial lumen, pericardial space, mediastinum, or lung substance rather than this space.'),
('8349c6ca-5258-5b4a-9008-93732da5313b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which of the following is a part of the parietal pleura?', '["Mediastinal + costal","Costal","mediastinal diaphragmatic","cervical (cupula) Pulmonary + costal","Diaphragmatic + costal Visceral + parietal"]'::jsonb, 'Costal', 'The parietal pleura has four recognized parts: costal, diaphragmatic, mediastinal, and cervical (cupula), so ''costal'' is one of its true components. The other options either include visceral pleura or present incomplete or jumbled groupings.'),
('5cfe3e28-874d-58ef-9be4-78c18eb1199b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'How far above the first rib does the cervical pleura (cupula) extend?', '["To hyoid resulting in decreased functional activity","To sternal notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To 2nd rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Above 1st rib (2-3 cm above medial clavicle)","To clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above 1st rib (2-3 cm above medial clavicle)', 'The cervical pleura (cupula) projects 2-3 cm above the first rib and the medial third of the clavicle, reinforced by the suprapleural membrane. It does not extend to the hyoid, sternal notch, second rib, or clavicle itself.'),
('043ee02d-ae14-57a4-851e-2fdfe8880fc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Between which landmarks does Sibson''s fascia (suprapleural membrane) attach?', '["C7 transverse process to inner border of 1st rib","C7 to 2nd rib accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C6 to 1st rib mediated by specific receptor activation","Clavicle to 1st rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T1 to manubrium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C7 transverse process to inner border of 1st rib', 'Sibson''s fascia (suprapleural membrane) is a strong fascial expansion attached from the transverse process of C7 to the inner border of the first rib, supporting the cervical pleura. The other attachment sites do not describe this membrane.'),
('cf7c70db-7cf1-55b1-9c45-3898ba3c058f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which of the following are the pleural recesses?', '["Alveolar sacs resulting in decreased functional activity","Costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal","Mediastinal spaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchial divisions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung fissures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Costodiaphragmatic and costomediastinal', 'The pleural sac possesses two clinically important recesses: the costodiaphragmatic and the costomediastinal recesses, formed where opposing pleural layers meet without containing lung. Alveolar sacs, bronchial divisions, lung fissures, and mediastinal spaces are not recesses.'),
('9daa9c1e-fcad-589a-9751-e2ea650d2ba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess lies between which layers of pleura?', '["Costal + mediastinal","Lung apex","Mediastinal + diaphragmatic","Between costal and diaphragmatic pleura","deepest at midaxillary Lung base"]'::jsonb, 'Between costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is formed between the costal pleura and the diaphragmatic pleura and is deepest at the midaxillary line. The other choices pair the wrong pleural layers or name unrelated structures.'),
('66d75b82-b9e0-5e29-a6d8-8ef7d8422918'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Where is the costomediastinal recess located?', '["Between costal and mediastinal pleura behind sternum","Interlobar fissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung + mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","Costal + diaphragmatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung hilum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between costal and mediastinal pleura behind sternum', 'The costomediastinal recess is the space where the costal pleura reflects onto the mediastinal pleura, lying behind the sternum and best developed on the left at the cardiac notch. It is not an interlobar fissure, the hilum, or a pleural pairing with the diaphragm.'),
('af922a6d-0f20-5689-a9e9-3a53add9b89a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding How many lobes does the right lung have?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["5 lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","2 lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","4 lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","3 (upper, middle, lower)","1 lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3 (upper, middle, lower)', 'The right lung has three lobes: upper, middle, and lower, separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures. The other numeric options do not match the lobar anatomy of the right lung.'),
('2ef1cd24-6aea-511b-be4f-bd4de775f04c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding How many lobes does the left lung have?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["2 (upper, lower)","4 lobes","5 lobes","3 lobes","1 lobe"]'::jsonb, '2 (upper, lower)', 'The left lung has two lobes, upper and lower, separated by the oblique fissure; it has no middle lobe. The other numeric options do not match the lobar anatomy of the left lung.'),
('e72e1970-d1b7-5137-a9a4-57a114f270fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which fissures are present in the right lung?', '["Vertical and horizontal","Horizontal and oblique","Horizontal only","Oblique only","Sagittal and coronal"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal and oblique', 'The right lung is divided by both the oblique fissure and the horizontal fissure into upper, middle, and lower lobes. Vertical/horizontal, horizontal-only, oblique-only, and sagittal/coronal patterns do not describe its fissures.'),
('6a07e111-2d2e-5d39-8632-c0230348e401'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which fissure is present in the left lung?', '["Sagittal","Oblique only","Vertical","Horizontal + oblique","Horizontal only"]'::jsonb, 'Oblique only', 'The left lung has a single fissure, the oblique fissure, dividing it into upper and lower lobes; no horizontal fissure is present. The other options do not describe the left lung''s fissure pattern.'),
('88444209-bc89-5dd1-bab4-474a8a734f8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The horizontal fissure separates which structures of the right lung?', '["Right upper from middle lobe","Upper from lower","Apex","Hilum","Middle from lower"]'::jsonb, 'Right upper from middle lobe', 'The horizontal fissure of the right lung runs from the oblique fissure to the anterior border and separates the upper lobe from the middle lobe. It does not separate upper from lower lobe, the apex, hilum, or middle from lower lobe.'),
('b785f9ba-53d9-52ed-9b86-4af6934c0785'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'The oblique fissure separates which structures?', '["Two lungs resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper from middle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle from lower mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower lobe from upper (and middle on right)","Lung from mediastinum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lobe from upper (and middle on right)', 'The oblique fissure separates the lower lobe from the upper lobe on both sides and additionally from the middle lobe on the right. It does not separate the two lungs, upper from middle, or middle from lower lobe.'),
('48a2363b-4f0d-523f-a7fc-601421910e8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'On which part of the lung is the cardiac notch found?', '["Apex resulting in decreased functional activity","Right lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Base mediated by specific receptor activation","Hilum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior border of left lung"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior border of left lung', 'The cardiac notch is a concavity along the anterior border of the left lung, produced by the heart, with the lingula lying below it. It is not located at the apex, base, hilum, or on the right lung.'),
('570fa039-b5d2-594a-b541-35e2643f8433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which of the following best describes the lingula of the left lung?', '["Cardiac notch resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Accessory lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tongue-like projection of upper lobe (corresponds to right middle lobe)"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue-like projection of upper lobe (corresponds to right middle lobe)', 'The lingula is a tongue-like projection of the left upper lobe that is homologous to the right middle lobe. It is not the cardiac notch, the pulmonary ligament, the lower lobe, or an accessory lobe.'),
('c897d4df-4b54-5d2f-81fb-9ae097d75f9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: root of lung (hilum) contains:', '["Only lymphatics Only pulmonary artery","Only veins Only bronchus","Bronchus pulmonary artery","pulmonary veins bronchial vessels","nerves lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchus pulmonary artery', 'The root of the lung transmits the main bronchus, pulmonary artery and veins, bronchial vessels, lymphatics, and nerves, all enclosed in a sleeve of pleura. Among the listed choices, the bronchus and pulmonary artery are the most important components of the root.'),
('e63b2021-06d3-5572-b494-6f5b78532742'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: right hilum order (superior to inferior):', '["Upper lobe bronchus","pulmonary artery","main bronchus","veins","Pulmonary artery bronchus"]'::jsonb, 'veins', 'At the right hilum the structures descend from above down as the eparterial (upper lobe) bronchus, pulmonary artery, hyparterial bronchus, and finally the pulmonary veins, which therefore lie lowest. The pulmonary veins are the most inferior component of the right lung root.'),
('c85ea619-82d9-5dfb-a39b-5559b55db8d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: left hilum order:', '["Veins","artery","bronchus","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'artery', 'At the left hilum the pulmonary artery lies most superior, with the left main bronchus below it and the pulmonary veins lowest. The artery is therefore the highest structure in the superior-to-inferior arrangement of the left root.'),
('42390e23-c310-5da5-bbbc-899421b8c3bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Pulmonary ligament:'' is:', '["Connective cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural fold below hilum allowing lung root movement","Diaphragm part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pericardial fold leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural fold below hilum allowing lung root movement', 'The pulmonary ligament is a fold of visceral and parietal pleura hanging below the hilum between the lung root and the diaphragm. It permits movement of the root structures during respiration.'),
('f391266c-56f3-5ea2-8c83-fda1de70323f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: left main bronchus:', '["Thicker walled","Longer","narrower","more horizontal (aorta arches over it)","Same More vertical Shorter wider"]'::jsonb, 'Longer', 'The left main bronchus is longer, narrower, and more horizontal than the right because the aortic arch passes over it. The right main bronchus is shorter, wider, and more vertical, so inhaled foreign bodies lodge there more often.'),
('9d65d065-9b05-5205-8115-f8095fb86daa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: tracheal bifurcation at:', '["T10","C6","T4/T5 (carina)","T6","T2"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 (carina)', 'The trachea bifurcates into the right and left main bronchi at the level of the sternal angle (T4/T5), where the internal ridge called the carina lies. This same level marks the start of the aortic arch and the second costal cartilage.'),
('7654bc6b-51db-56c2-a180-58a7f65615c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: bronchopulmonary segment:', '["Lung lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anatomical lung unit supplied by segmental bronchus + vessels","Alveolar sac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Entire lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical lung unit supplied by segmental bronchus + vessels', 'A bronchopulmonary segment is the functional anatomical unit of the lung supplied by its own segmental (tertiary) bronchus and a corresponding pulmonary artery branch, with veins draining at its borders. Each segment can be surgically resected as a distinct unit.'),
('ec36f1c2-8476-5306-8459-aff239c7cfc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: right lung segments:', '["10 segments (3 upper, 2 middle, 5 lower)","9 segments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","8 segments mediated by specific receptor activation","12 segments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","6 segments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10 segments (3 upper, 2 middle, 5 lower)', 'The right lung contains 10 bronchopulmonary segments: 3 in the upper lobe, 2 in the middle lobe, and 5 in the lower lobe. This count reflects the larger right lung with its separate middle lobe.'),
('b8c957ce-6892-52a7-a319-502c3e89958a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: left lung segments:', '["12 segments","7 segments","8-10 segments (some fusion)","6 segments","10 segments"]'::jsonb, '8-10 segments (some fusion)', 'The left lung has 8-10 bronchopulmonary segments, with fusion of some segments (for example apicoposterior and anteromedial basal) because the heart occupies space on the left side. The usual textbook count is 8 to 10.'),
('606e4b04-36e0-57cf-a412-c0432f2cdec8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Bronchial arteries supply:'' is:', '["Alveoli resulting in decreased functional activity","Trachea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Lung parenchyma (bronchi, bronchioles) and visceral pleura","Parietal pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung parenchyma (bronchi, bronchioles) and visceral pleura', 'The bronchial arteries, branches of the descending thoracic aorta, supply the lung parenchyma including bronchi and bronchioles down to the respiratory bronchioles, and also the visceral pleura. Pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood for gas exchange, not nutrition.'),
('b65b7e45-342a-5ca5-9c47-7719bcb57458'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: right bronchial artery from:', '["Subclavian resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal thoracic mediated by specific receptor activation","Left bronchial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3rd right posterior intercostal artery or aorta"]'::jsonb, '3rd right posterior intercostal artery or aorta', 'The right bronchial artery typically arises from the 3rd right posterior intercostal artery, a branch of the aorta, or directly from the thoracic aorta. The left bronchial arteries usually arise directly from the thoracic aorta.'),
('b5868dd4-11d2-5d64-8ca0-6e60d0cd081a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: left bronchial arteries from:', '["Right bronchial","Subclavian","Costocervical trunk","Internal thoracic","Thoracic aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic aorta', 'The left bronchial arteries usually arise directly from the thoracic aorta. They supply oxygenated blood to the bronchi, lung parenchyma, and visceral pleura.'),
('b380f917-6a94-57d2-82b4-ba3539c9a732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: pulmonary arteries carry:', '["Deoxygenated blood from RV to lungs","Oxygenated to lungs","Mixed blood","Oxygenated to heart","Deoxygenated to LA"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated blood from RV to lungs', 'Pulmonary arteries are the only arteries in the body that carry deoxygenated blood, transporting it from the right ventricle to the lungs for gas exchange. After oxygenation the pulmonary veins return blood to the left atrium.'),
('a6d803da-e273-52d9-b064-f7b2234c493f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: pulmonary veins carry:', '["Oxygenated blood from lungs to LA","Deoxygenated to RA","Deoxygenated to lungs","Oxygenated to RV","Mixed"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygenated blood from lungs to LA', 'Pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood from the lung capillaries to the left atrium of the heart. They are among the few veins in the body that transport oxygenated blood.'),
('12e95725-67c5-5b86-9320-9d047984e2b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: visceral pleura innervation:', '["Splanchnic resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phrenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal (pain) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autonomic (sympathetic) - insensitive to pain"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic (sympathetic) - insensitive to pain', 'The visceral pleura is innervated by autonomic (sympathetic) fibers and is insensitive to pain, which is why a pneumothorax itself is not painful. Pain arises from the parietal pleura, which has somatic innervation.'),
('c638ddfb-793b-5339-85bf-dc6c2446af89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Parietal pleura innervation:'' is:', '["Only phrenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Intercostal (costal) and phrenic (mediastinal/central diaphragmatic)","Vagus mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only intercostal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal (costal) and phrenic (mediastinal/central diaphragmatic)', 'The parietal pleura has somatic innervation: the costal pleura is supplied by intercostal nerves, while the mediastinal and central diaphragmatic pleura are supplied by the phrenic nerve. This pattern explains referred pain to the chest wall or shoulder tip.'),
('f2028704-098c-5e1a-9de1-9c8295a2710a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: costal pleura pain referred to:', '["Neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Chest wall (intercostal nerves)","Back mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chest wall (intercostal nerves)', 'The costal parietal pleura is innervated by intercostal nerves, so irritation produces pain localized to the chest wall over the affected area. By contrast, central diaphragmatic pleura refers pain to the shoulder tip via the phrenic nerve.'),
('fa75985b-f8e8-5a74-927b-aa7a3c173649'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: diaphragmatic pleura (central) pain referred to:', '["Back resulting in decreased functional activity","Abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chest wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Shoulder tip (C3-C5 via phrenic)","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shoulder tip (C3-C5 via phrenic)', 'The central diaphragmatic pleura is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3-C5), so irritation produces referred pain at the shoulder tip. The peripheral diaphragmatic pleura is innervated by lower intercostal nerves and refers pain to the lower thoracic and upper abdominal wall.'),
('ed9d3b80-8234-51ee-9508-0aa325b164e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: pulmonary plexus:', '["Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic fibers","Only motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (vagus) and sympathetic fibers', 'The pulmonary plexus is formed by parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve and sympathetic fibers from the sympathetic trunk, accompanying the bronchi and bronchial vessels. It regulates bronchoconstriction, vasoconstriction, and glandular secretion.'),
('1bf65cda-5d9d-5eeb-891b-ba117a40cfea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: apex of lung projects:', '["C7 resulting in decreased functional activity","T1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternal notch mediated by specific receptor activation","1st rib level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2-3 cm above medial 1/3 of clavicle"]'::jsonb, '2-3 cm above medial 1/3 of clavicle', 'The apex of the lung projects 2-3 cm above the medial third of the clavicle into the root of the neck. This makes it vulnerable to injury, and an apical tumor can present as a supraclavicular swelling.'),
('e7aa16b5-5050-5cd9-8548-1792e7573b5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: base of lung:', '["Convex resulting in decreased functional activity","Irregular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pointed mediated by specific receptor activation","Flat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Concave rests on diaphragm (dome-shaped)"]'::jsonb, 'Concave rests on diaphragm (dome-shaped)', 'The base of the lung is concave and rests on the dome-shaped diaphragm, adapting to its curvature. The right dome of the diaphragm lies higher than the left.'),
('6b3fbf40-b100-548f-97d4-270446638435'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Costal surface of lung:'' is:', '["Irregular","Concave","Smooth and convex","contacts costal pleura","Flat Roughened"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth and convex', 'The costal surface of the lung is large, smooth, and convex, lying against the costal pleura and the chest wall, where it shows the imprint of the ribs. The concave base is the surface resting on the diaphragm.'),
('c1e7dad3-ee0b-591a-b11a-c83c5b830777'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: mediastinal surface contains:', '["Costal surface resulting in decreased functional activity","Apex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hilum (root of lung) and cardiac impression","Base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fissures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hilum (root of lung) and cardiac impression', 'The mediastinal surface of each lung contains the hilum, where the root structures enter the lung, and a cardiac impression, which is deeper on the left side. This surface faces the mediastinum.'),
('dde95732-f5bf-5dd2-a4da-8e49e5ccf3f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: left lung cardiac impression due to:', '["Heart extends more to left","Pericardium on left","Left lung smaller","Left bronchus longer","Aorta on left"]'::jsonb, 'Heart extends more to left', 'The left lung has a deep cardiac impression because the heart and pericardium extend more to the left side of the mediastinum. The right lung shows only a shallow cardiac impression.'),
('9f0a2b28-a52b-5c67-b3c5-087dd0546ea9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: trachea lined by:', '["Stratified squamous resulting in decreased functional activity","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Simple cuboidal mediated by specific receptor activation","Simple columnar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transitional leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells', 'The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium containing goblet cells, which secrete mucus and propel it toward the pharynx. This mucociliary escalator traps and clears inhaled particles.'),
('11fcb069-319f-503d-a54c-c31b81bb3567'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: trachea reinforced by:', '["cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrocartilage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete bony rings mediated by specific receptor activation","Elastic cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings (16-20)"]'::jsonb, 'C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings (16-20)', 'The trachea is reinforced by 16-20 C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings that are open posteriorly, keeping the airway patent. The posterior gap is bridged by the trachealis smooth muscle, which lies against the esophagus.'),
('57db7708-3ba5-5f29-a7c2-c5f03d4a1442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: trachealis muscle connects:', '["Sides resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior ends accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea to larynx mediated by specific receptor activation","Open ends of C-shaped cartilages posteriorly","Trachea to esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Open ends of C-shaped cartilages posteriorly', 'The trachealis muscle, a band of smooth muscle, bridges the open ends of the C-shaped cartilage rings posteriorly, where the trachea is in contact with the esophagus. Its contraction narrows the lumen and aids in expelling secretions.'),
('ead1343a-26ae-5adf-8f74-3bdf2547a332'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Esophagus located:'' is:', '["Anterior mediastinum","Lateral to trachea","Anterior to trachea","Posterior to trachea","anterior to vertebrae Posterior to vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to trachea', 'The esophagus lies posterior to the trachea in the superior and posterior mediastinum, directly behind the trachealis muscle. It passes behind the left main bronchus and descends through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm.'),
('e680e96d-4b16-5dc9-a3b3-b0fff9cb6875'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: phrenic nerve at hilum:', '["Below hilum","Anterior to lung root","Posterior to hilum","Within ligament","Between PA and PV"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to lung root', 'The phrenic nerve descends anterior to the lung root (hilum) on both sides, between the mediastinal pleura and the pericardium. In contrast, the vagus nerve lies posterior to the root.'),
('4898731f-dd52-5ab2-b0aa-ef266696c5c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: vagus nerve at hilum:', '["Within ligament","Posterior to lung root","Above hilum","Between bronchus and artery","Anterior to hilum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to lung root', 'The vagus nerve descends posterior to the lung root and gives branches that contribute to the pulmonary plexus. This position is opposite to the phrenic nerve, which runs anterior to the root.'),
('1c93236a-7345-5a2a-b8d7-24bc7d619d3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: azygos vein arches over:', '["Esophagus","Root of left lung","Aorta","Root of right lung","Trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Root of right lung', 'The azygos vein arches forward over the root of the right lung to drain into the superior vena cava. The aortic arch, by contrast, arches over the left main bronchus.'),
('08eeaeb0-6ea3-5bf0-b489-7b7a38e4d82b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: trachea length:', '["3-5 cm","15-20 cm","20-25 cm","5 cm","10-12 cm"]'::jsonb, '10-12 cm', 'The trachea is about 10-12 cm long in adults, extending from the lower border of the cricoid cartilage to the carina at the T4/T5 level. It is composed of C-shaped cartilage rings and a fibromuscular membrane.'),
('5a0d49fb-27dd-541e-b7e2-ad7525a6140b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: main bronchi divide into:', '["Only lobar resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobar (secondary) -> segmental (tertiary)","Alveolar ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct to bronchioles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only segmental leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobar (secondary) -> segmental (tertiary)', 'Each main (primary) bronchus divides into lobar (secondary) bronchi, which in turn divide into segmental (tertiary) bronchi supplying the bronchopulmonary segments. The right lung has three lobar bronchi and the left has two.'),
('a79561db-f208-5b08-b9c5-16e4d2c0b089'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Bronchioles lack:'' is:', '["Epithelium","Cartilage (smooth muscle only)","Smooth muscle","Elastic fibers","Basement membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Cartilage (smooth muscle only)', 'Bronchioles lack cartilage and consist mainly of smooth muscle, so bronchospasm can markedly narrow them. They measure less than 1 mm in diameter and also lack mucus-secreting glands.'),
('a0110bc2-64e7-5490-97dc-35104a203ad9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: terminal bronchiole:', '["Alveolar sac resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Last part of conducting zone (no gas exchange)","First respiratory zone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Last part of conducting zone (no gas exchange)', 'The terminal bronchiole is the last purely conducting airway, and no gas exchange occurs in its wall. It branches into the respiratory bronchioles, which begin the respiratory zone.'),
('e5c855fe-0d70-52af-b839-49ea5fa7e8c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: respiratory bronchiole:', '["Segmental bronchus resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchiole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar sac mediated by specific receptor activation","First part of respiratory zone (some alveoli)","Conducting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First part of respiratory zone (some alveoli)', 'The respiratory bronchiole is the first part of the respiratory zone because its wall contains scattered alveoli where gas exchange begins. It branches into alveolar ducts and then into alveolar sacs.'),
('8eb3c800-baa7-50c8-9d80-cedfa6115120'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: alveolar ducts end in:', '["Pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","Terminal bronchioles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bronchioles mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveolar sacs (clusters of alveoli)"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar sacs (clusters of alveoli)', 'The alveolar ducts terminate in alveolar sacs, which are clusters of alveoli that share a common opening. This is the final branching unit of the respiratory zone.'),
('a0a0556f-9cf0-5ba0-a601-1c43006b7ed8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: type i pneumocytes:', '["Macrophages resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cuboidal producing surfactant mediated by specific receptor activation","Squamous cells covering 95% of alveolar surface (gas exchange)","Goblet cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cells covering 95% of alveolar surface (gas exchange)', 'Type I pneumocytes are extremely thin squamous cells that cover about 95% of the alveolar surface and form part of the air-blood barrier for gas exchange. The cuboidal type II cells, by contrast, produce surfactant.'),
('4ad1a073-5a30-552a-822c-92a7b2aef400'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: type ii pneumocytes:', '["Ciliated resulting in decreased functional activity","Goblet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Produce surfactant (cuboidal)","Line alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phagocytic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produce surfactant (cuboidal)', 'Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal cells that secrete surfactant, a phospholipid that lowers alveolar surface tension, and they can replace damaged type I cells. They are therefore vital for keeping alveoli open and repairing the alveolar epithelium.'),
('55e5f461-5ffd-531e-8799-9b8dca8a0a80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''Surfactant function:'' is:', '["Kills bacteria","Secretes mucus","Increases surface tension","Reduces surface tension","prevents alveolar collapse Transports O2"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces surface tension', 'Surfactant secreted by type II pneumocytes lowers alveolar surface tension and thereby prevents alveolar collapse at the end of expiration. Surfactant deficiency, as in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome, leads to alveolar collapse.'),
('06e90913-4797-5087-8da6-2e77ed3c4935'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: pneumothorax:', '["Air in pleural cavity causing lung collapse","Blood in pleura accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pleural tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluid in pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Air in pleural cavity causing lung collapse', 'Pneumothorax is the entry of air into the pleural cavity, which abolishes the negative intrapleural pressure and allows the lung to collapse. A tension pneumothorax compresses mediastinal structures and is life-threatening.'),
('a8403e1d-3e73-5d62-8a7d-cf328ce22f67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: pleural effusion:', '["Pleural fibrosis","Pleural inflammation","Fluid accumulation in pleural cavity","Air in pleura","Blood in pleura"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid accumulation in pleural cavity', 'Pleural effusion is the abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity, which may be a transudate or an exudate. Common causes include heart failure, infection, malignancy, and hypoproteinemia.'),
('cfd0022f-19b4-5105-bdff-b1b8e4115dab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: the cardiac notch is a feature of which lung?', '["Only the middle lobe","Right lung","Both lungs equally","The lingula only","Left lung (on the anterior border)"]'::jsonb, 'Left lung (on the anterior border)', 'The cardiac notch is a concave indentation on the anterior border of the left lung that accommodates the heart, with the lingula lying below it. The right lung does not show such a notch.'),
('9d3440ae-0ef6-5187-8574-d4cfb945dd55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: the lungs are located:', '["In the peritoneum","In the pericardial cavity","In the abdominal cavity","In the mediastinum","In the pleural cavities"]'::jsonb, 'In the pleural cavities', 'Each lung lies within a pleural cavity, surrounded by visceral and parietal pleura with a thin film of lubricating fluid between them. The two pleural cavities flank the mediastinum.'),
('a8bc3ee8-24fd-583c-927d-2029aad63d09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: the pleura consists of:', '["Diaphragmatic fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parietal pleura accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal pleura (lining thoracic wall) and visceral pleura (covering lung)","Only visceral pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endothoracic fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal pleura (lining thoracic wall) and visceral pleura (covering lung)', 'The pleura is a single continuous serous membrane formed by the parietal pleura lining the thoracic wall, diaphragm, and mediastinum, and the visceral pleura covering the lung. The narrow space between them contains a small amount of lubricating fluid.'),
('c888b286-7123-5a49-8d49-b5def82152bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, the best response to ''The costodiaphragmatic recess is:'' is:', '["The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura","The cardiac notch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space behind the mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the lung without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the two lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lowest part of the pleural cavity between the costal and diaphragmatic pleura', 'The costodiaphragmatic recess is the lowest part of the pleural cavity, a potential space between the costal and diaphragmatic parietal pleura into which the lung does not descend on quiet inspiration. Fluid accumulates here and it is a common site for thoracentesis.'),
('a702f4b9-a908-5bf7-beb4-38b4fefed69e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'During revision of Lungs and pleura, which of the following is correct: the left lung has:', '["1 lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","3 lobes and 2 fissures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 lobes without a fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","2 lobes (superior and inferior) and 1 oblique fissure","4 lobes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 lobes (superior and inferior) and 1 oblique fissure', 'The left lung is divided by a single oblique fissure into a superior and an inferior lobe, giving it 2 lobes. It lacks a middle lobe and a horizontal fissure, and it shows the cardiac notch and the lingula.'),
('8910fb89-70ca-5ab1-b41a-152a8a7892ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'In Lungs and pleura, which statement best fits the following fact: the left lung also has:', '["fissures resulting in decreased functional activity","A middle lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A horizontal fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","3 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)', 'The lingula is a tongue-like projection on the anterior border of the left lung and is regarded as the homologue of the right middle lobe. It is not separated by a horizontal fissure.'),
('fa02336c-df0d-55f0-91ce-6db9802f3707'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'Regarding Lungs and pleura, what is the correct answer to: the hilum of the lung contains:', '["Only the pulmonary veins","Bronchus","pulmonary artery and veins","bronchial vessels","lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchus', 'The hilum (root) of the lung transmits the bronchus, pulmonary artery and veins, bronchial vessels, lymphatics, and nerves to and from the lung. The bronchus is a constant major component of the root.'),
('cb4603fb-8071-540c-bda6-577b95ec61ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Lungs Pleura', 'For Lungs and pleura, which option is most appropriate: at the hilum of the right lung?', '["the arrangement is:","Pulmonary veins above all","Bronchus below artery and veins","Pulmonary artery above bronchus","Eparterial bronchus (above pulmonary artery)"]'::jsonb, 'Eparterial bronchus (above pulmonary artery)', 'At the right hilum the eparterial bronchus, the right upper lobe bronchus, arises above (eparterial) the pulmonary artery. On the left side, by contrast, the main bronchus lies below the pulmonary artery and is described as hyparterial.'),
('f90129c2-1956-546b-aecb-09b0933cefb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Mediastinum boundaries:', '["Sternum anterior","vertebrae posterior","superior thoracic aperture above","diaphragm below","Clavicles above Xiphoid below Ribs laterally Pericardium anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum anterior', 'The mediastinum is bounded anteriorly by the sternum, posteriorly by the thoracic vertebrae, above by the superior thoracic aperture, and below by the diaphragm. The sternum therefore forms the anterior boundary.'),
('23f6c380-2804-5385-af65-e28b248e051a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Mediastinum divided into:', '["division resulting in decreased functional activity","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior (by plane from sternal angle to T4/T5)","Upper + middle + lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior + posterior only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior (by plane from sternal angle to T4/T5)', 'The mediastinum is divided into superior and inferior parts by the horizontal plane drawn from the sternal angle to the T4/T5 intervertebral disc. Left-right and anterior-posterior-only divisions do not match this standard scheme.'),
('86560233-f668-537b-b130-21f32bed8590'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Superior mediastinum contents:', '["Diaphragm Lungs only","Thymus great vessels","trachea esophagus","thoracic duct phrenic/vagus nerves","Heart only Pericardium only"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus great vessels', 'The superior mediastinum contains the thymus and the great vessels (brachiocephalic veins, SVC, and aortic arch). The heart lies in the middle mediastinum, and the diaphragm and lungs are not mediastinal contents.'),
('677cc8b1-cdaa-58f7-a0a2-07346a1a0868'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Inferior mediastinum divided into:', '["Anterior","middle","posterior compartments","Left + right","Superior + inferior subdivisions Upper + lower"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior', 'The inferior mediastinum below the sternal-angle plane is subdivided into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments, and ''Anterior'' names one of these three spaces. Left-right and superior-inferior divisions do not apply at this level.'),
('43082e3b-ccf9-5632-9139-f70b885f770b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Mediastinum: Which answer is correct in Lungs and pleura when asked: the left lung also has:?', '["fissures resulting in decreased functional activity","A middle lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A horizontal fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","3 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)"]'::jsonb, 'Lingula (homologue of right middle lobe)', 'The lingula is a tongue-like extension of the left upper lobe on the anterior border and is the homologue of the right middle lobe. The left lung has no separate middle lobe, horizontal fissure, or third lobe.'),
('810b500a-c6c1-5228-8f22-cbf5ec12b519'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Middle mediastinum contents:', '["Thymus","Esophagus Aorta","Heart","pericardium phrenic nerves","roots of great vessels Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The middle mediastinum is defined by the heart within the pericardial sac, together with the phrenic nerves and the roots of the great vessels. The thymus is a superior/anterior structure, and the esophagus with the descending aorta lie posteriorly.'),
('53dbfca8-1aa7-5087-8513-be9246fa287a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Posterior mediastinum contents:', '["Great vessels","Heart Thymus","Esophagus descending thoracic aorta","azygos/hemiazygos veins thoracic duct","vagus/splanchnic nerves Trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus descending thoracic aorta', 'The posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus and the descending thoracic aorta, as well as the azygos/hemiazygos veins, thoracic duct, and vagal and splanchnic nerves. The heart and thymus occupy the middle and anterior/superior parts of the mediastinum, and the great vessels lie superiorly.'),
('3c0174a9-1149-55d5-a56a-f86b7a4a7071'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Sternal angle (Angle of Louis) at:', '["T6/T7 resulting in decreased functional activity","T4/T5 intervertebral disc","C7/T1 mediated by specific receptor activation","T2/T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 intervertebral disc', 'The manubriosternal joint (angle of Louis) lies at the level of the T4/T5 intervertebral disc and marks the plane that divides the superior from the inferior mediastinum. The T6/T7, C7/T1, and T2/T3 levels are incorrect.'),
('614ec25d-5dd7-5bc5-8bde-0abc69ece20c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'At sternal angle:', '["Diaphragm dome","Thymus lies","Arch of aorta begins/ends trachea bifurcates","azygos vein enters SVC thoracic duct crosses","Heart apex beat Pulmonary hilum"]'::jsonb, 'Arch of aorta begins/ends trachea bifurcates', 'The sternal angle level marks the beginning and end of the aortic arch, the bifurcation of the trachea, the entry of the azygos vein into the SVC, and the crossing of the thoracic duct to the left. The apex beat and pulmonary hila lie at lower levels.'),
('c7198621-e380-5fb1-ab37-6f886ef844e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Azygos vein origin:', '["Left ascending lumbar","Right ascending lumbar + subcostal veins","Renal vein","Inferior vena cava","Posterior intercostal"]'::jsonb, 'Right ascending lumbar + subcostal veins', 'The azygos vein arises at about L1/L2 from the union of the right ascending lumbar vein and the right subcostal vein. The equivalent left-sided veins form the hemiazygos system instead.'),
('88228ee5-a0f1-5d28-a384-a0be8193ccf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Azygos vein arch:', '["Through diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","To coronary sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Over right hilum at T4/T5 to enter SVC","Behind sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To left SVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Over right hilum at T4/T5 to enter SVC', 'The azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung (right hilum) at the T4/T5 level to empty into the superior vena cava. It does not drain into the coronary sinus or a left-sided SVC.'),
('bcc4ebe1-94eb-56ea-9278-3976a945a809'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Accessory hemiazygos:', '["Drains left 4th-8th posterior intercostals","Right 4th-8th accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right upper intercostals mediated by specific receptor activation","Left lower intercostals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both sides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drains left 4th-8th posterior intercostals', 'The accessory hemiazygos vein drains the left 4th-8th posterior intercostal veins and descends to join the azygos vein, often through the hemiazygos vein. The right posterior intercostal veins drain directly into the azygos vein.'),
('de9401f9-83d9-5854-97db-773d38b7a938'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Hemiazygos vein:', '["Drains directly to SVC","Left 4th-8th","Drains left 9th-11th posterior intercostals","crosses to azygos at T8/T9","Right intercostals Drains to left brachiocephalic"]'::jsonb, 'Drains left 9th-11th posterior intercostals', 'The hemiazygos vein drains the left 9th-11th posterior intercostal veins and crosses the midline behind the aorta at about T8/T9 to join the azygos vein. It empties indirectly into the SVC via the azygos vein, never directly.'),
('6c45b096-20cc-5d24-8e2f-a742b87f08da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Azygos system drains:', '["Lungs","Heart","Posterior intercostals esophagus","pericardium mediastinum","Anterior intercostals Diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior intercostals esophagus', 'The azygos system drains the posterior intercostal veins and the esophageal and mediastinal veins of the chest wall before emptying into the SVC. The anterior intercostal veins drain toward the internal thoracic vessels, and the heart and lungs drain separately.'),
('e3ae9a48-9f61-550b-a319-42c891b0ac8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thoracic duct origin:', '["Head resulting in decreased functional activity","Thorax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cisterna chyli at L1/L2 (abdomen)","Pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna chyli at L1/L2 (abdomen)', 'The thoracic duct begins in the abdomen at the cisterna chyli, at the L1/L2 level, and ascends through the aortic hiatus into the thorax. It carries lymph from most of the body below the diaphragm.'),
('661829ad-da94-58f2-90c6-f6e830eaa353'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thoracic duct course in thorax:', '["Behind sternum","Middle mediastinum","In posterior mediastinum between azygos and aorta","then to left at T4/T5","Right side throughout Anterior mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'In posterior mediastinum between azygos and aorta', 'The thoracic duct ascends in the posterior mediastinum, lying between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, then crosses to the left at about T4/T5. It remains a posterior structure throughout; it is not found in the middle or anterior mediastinum.'),
('2e5f0161-459a-54ad-aa03-bbc7724642ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Right lymphatic duct:', '["Drains right upper quadrant into right venous angle","Lower body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left upper quadrant mediated by specific receptor activation","Entire body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right lower quadrant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drains right upper quadrant into right venous angle', 'The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right hemithorax, emptying into the right venous angle. The thoracic duct drains the remainder of the body.'),
('ee4b60eb-c612-5406-8488-0e8f8d2a104b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thoracic duct tributaries:', '["Only bronchial","Only mediastinal Only esophageal","Intercostal","mediastinal esophageal","bronchomediastinal trunks Only intercostal"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal', 'As it ascends, the thoracic duct receives intercostal, mediastinal, and esophageal lymphatics, so posterior intercostal trunks are among its tributaries. The other options exclude these channels or list structures that do not drain into it.'),
('c5fc1b11-dcac-5a8e-aca6-37baad1a7df6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Descending thoracic aorta:', '["From heart to T12","From T4/T5 to T12 (through aortic hiatus)","From T2 to T8","From T4 to diaphragm at T8","From T6 to T10"]'::jsonb, 'From T4/T5 to T12 (through aortic hiatus)', 'The descending thoracic aorta runs from the end of the aortic arch at T4/T5 down to T12, where it traverses the aortic hiatus and becomes the abdominal aorta. The other vertebral levels given do not match these boundaries.'),
('15610909-7042-55aa-a197-3fb3aa44264d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Aortic hiatus at:', '["T8 resulting in decreased functional activity","T4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 (between crura of diaphragm)","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T12 (between crura of diaphragm)', 'The aortic hiatus lies at the T12 level between the crura of the diaphragm and transmits the aorta and the thoracic duct. The vena caval foramen (T8) and esophageal hiatus (T10) are separate openings at higher levels.'),
('155aa17e-1ed4-59af-a5c2-959906d2505e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Esophageal hiatus at:', '["T12 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C7 mediated by specific receptor activation","T8 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10 (right crus of diaphragm)"]'::jsonb, 'T10 (right crus of diaphragm)', 'The esophagus pierces the right crus of the diaphragm at the T10 level, accompanied by the vagal trunks. The aortic hiatus (T12) and vena caval foramen (T8) lie at other levels.'),
('d015ce5c-3f45-57e0-af85-adf385fa14c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Vena caval foramen at:', '["L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","T10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T8 (in central tendon)","T12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (in central tendon)', 'The inferior vena cava passes through the central tendon of the diaphragm at the T8 level. The esophageal hiatus (T10) and aortic hiatus (T12) are lower openings.'),
('a51605c1-042e-5ea3-b7d0-45a8dbe5f7bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Esophagus course:', '["Anterior to heart","Right of midline","Posterior to trachea (superior)","behind LA (middle)","left of midline (inferior) Anterior to trachea Behind sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to trachea (superior)', 'In the superior mediastinum the esophagus lies posterior to the trachea; in its middle part it runs behind the left atrium, and below it passes to the left of the midline before the hiatus. It is never anterior to the trachea or the heart.'),
('891556f4-780c-5075-a9ec-00c7ea832ace'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Esophageal constrictions:', '["T2 resulting in decreased functional activity","T6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10 mediated by specific receptor activation","C6 (start) T4/T5 (aortic arch/left bronchus)","T10 (hiatus) C5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C6 (start) T4/T5 (aortic arch/left bronchus)', 'The esophagus narrows at three sites: the cricopharyngeal origin at C6, the level of the aortic arch and left main bronchus at T4/T5, and the esophageal hiatus at T10. These two upper sites are given together in the answer; a single T2 level is not a recognized constriction.'),
('c92460d2-a68a-5566-ac60-8b1432789170'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Left recurrent laryngeal nerve:', '["Right side","Posterior to esophagus","Thymus","Loops under aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum)","ascends in tracheoesophageal groove Anterior to trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Loops under aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum)', 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops beneath the aortic arch, just distal to the ligamentum arteriosum, and ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove. The right recurrent nerve instead hooks under the right subclavian artery.'),
('a0af3f2e-2762-5918-992d-55a186cfed53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Right recurrent laryngeal nerve:', '["Loops under right subclavian artery","SVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ligamentum arteriosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loops under right subclavian artery', 'The right recurrent laryngeal nerve arises from the right vagus and loops under the right subclavian artery before ascending beside the trachea. The aortic arch and ligamentum arteriosum are related to the left side.'),
('d8e7c999-396a-5959-84b3-df623153d8cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Esophageal plexus:', '["Vagus nerves surround esophagus forming plexus","Sympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercostal mediated by specific receptor activation","Phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splanchnic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerves surround esophagus forming plexus', 'The right and left vagus nerves break up and rejoin around the esophagus in the posterior mediastinum, forming the esophageal plexus. The sympathetic, intercostal, phrenic, and splanchnic nerves do not form this plexus.'),
('d0fe6256-7df5-52e0-8f24-834a18fb1c10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Anterior vagal trunk:', '["Left phrenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Both accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left vagus (anterior surface of esophagus)","Right phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left vagus (anterior surface of esophagus)', 'The anterior vagal trunk is formed by fibers of the left vagus and lies on the anterior surface of the esophagus. The right vagus contributes to the posterior vagal trunk instead.'),
('1cd8cf3d-3547-5d82-8c42-0c37a700bf6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Posterior vagal trunk:', '["Both resulting in decreased functional activity","Left vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right vagus (posterior surface of esophagus)","Left phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right vagus (posterior surface of esophagus)', 'The posterior vagal trunk is formed from right vagal fibers and lies on the posterior surface of the esophagus before passing to the stomach. The left vagus produces the anterior trunk, and the phrenic nerves take no part.'),
('466d40ec-a9bd-56e0-8577-5dc1b3b2ff87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Greater splanchnic nerve origin:', '["L1-L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10-T11 mediated by specific receptor activation","T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia","C8-T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia', 'The greater splanchnic nerve arises from the T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia and descends toward the celiac plexus. The lesser and least splanchnic nerves arise lower, from T10-T11 and T12 respectively.'),
('5eb0cf1b-1a4c-54f2-9613-90974151437d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Lesser splanchnic nerve origin:', '["L1","C8","T12","T5-T9","T10-T11"]'::jsonb, 'T10-T11', 'The lesser splanchnic nerve originates from the T10-T11 sympathetic ganglia and passes to the aorticorenal plexus. The greater (T5-T9) and least (T12) splanchnic nerves take origin above and below it.'),
('7e604675-0513-5aeb-8a9a-1df0f93b0bdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Least splanchnic nerve origin:', '["T12","C8","T5-T9","L1","T10-T11"]'::jsonb, 'T12', 'The least splanchnic nerve arises from the T12 sympathetic ganglion and proceeds to the renal plexus. It is the lowest of the three thoracoabdominal splanchnic nerves.'),
('f9709cdc-99dc-5edc-a6a8-095c9b9d6d18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Splanchnic nerves pierce:', '["Central tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Vena caval opening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic hiatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophageal hiatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragm crura to reach celiac plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm crura to reach celiac plexus', 'The greater, lesser, and least splanchnic nerves pierce the crura of the diaphragm to reach the celiac and aorticorenal plexuses. The vena caval, esophageal, and aortic openings transmit other structures.'),
('60e67234-58c6-52c1-b7dc-1182c8688890'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Phrenic nerves innervate:', '["Abdominal muscles","Diaphragm (C3-C5)","Back muscles","Intercostals","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm (C3-C5)', 'The phrenic nerves from C3-C5 provide the sole motor supply to the diaphragm, passing anterior to the roots of the lungs. The intercostal, abdominal, and back muscles are supplied by other nerves.'),
('e8379e7b-b305-5a74-a422-db9e3dff2adf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Right phrenic nerve:', '["Posterior to hilum resulting in decreased functional activity","Left side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind SVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passes lateral to SVC anterior to lung root along right side of heart"]'::jsonb, 'Passes lateral to SVC anterior to lung root along right side of heart', 'The right phrenic nerve descends lateral to the SVC, anterior to the root of the right lung, and along the right side of the heart and IVC to the diaphragm. The vagus, not the phrenic nerve, passes posterior to the hilum.'),
('5924fe97-ab12-5b5d-a4da-0ea35d7db9cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Left phrenic nerve:', '["Posterior to aortic arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind heart accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior to hilum mediated by specific receptor activation","Right side without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passes anterior to aortic arch anterior to lung root along LV"]'::jsonb, 'Passes anterior to aortic arch anterior to lung root along LV', 'The left phrenic nerve passes anterior to the aortic arch and the root of the left lung, then descends over the left ventricle to the diaphragm. It does not lie posterior to the arch or the hilum.'),
('956fde9f-5ebb-5e6e-bdc7-733c9690528c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Pericardiacophrenic artery:', '["Aorta","Intercostal","Bronchial","From internal thoracic artery","with phrenic nerve (supplies pericardium) Subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'From internal thoracic artery', 'The pericardiacophrenic artery is a branch of the internal thoracic artery that travels with the phrenic nerve and supplies the pericardium, pleura, and diaphragm. The aorta, intercostal, and bronchial arteries do not give rise to it.'),
('d1460336-5d26-523e-aacb-a8868dd9bba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Internal thoracic artery:', '["From vertebral resulting in decreased functional activity","From aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From intercostal mediated by specific receptor activation","From axillary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From subclavian runs 1 cm lateral to sternum divides at 6th ICS"]'::jsonb, 'From subclavian runs 1 cm lateral to sternum divides at 6th ICS', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from the subclavian artery, runs about 1 cm lateral to the sternal edge, and divides at the 6th intercostal space into the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries. The vertebral, aortic, and intercostal origins are incorrect.'),
('72d47ef0-4b83-5f05-97b1-c9fbd151bb40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thymus location:', '["Anterior mediastinum (superior and anterior)","Neck only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle mediastinum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mediastinum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior mediastinum (superior and anterior)', 'The thymus occupies the superior mediastinum and extends downward into the anterior mediastinum behind the manubrium and upper sternum. It does not lie in the middle or posterior mediastinum.'),
('d81da7f1-5293-5d57-96cb-fa71f44fa970'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thymus function:', '["T-cell maturation and selection","Blood filtration","Hormone secretion to blood","B cell production","Mucus secretion"]'::jsonb, 'T-cell maturation and selection', 'The thymus is the primary lymphoid organ in which T cells mature and undergo positive and negative selection. Blood filtration, B-cell production, and mucus secretion are not thymic functions.'),
('b983dd07-a633-54ed-8e14-a8dc30acdba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thymus involutes:', '["During pregnancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Never accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","After puberty (fatty replacement)","In old age without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At birth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'After puberty (fatty replacement)', 'The thymus reaches its largest relative size in childhood and after puberty undergoes fatty involution, being replaced progressively by adipose tissue. Its involution does not begin at birth or only in old age.'),
('be1b95f4-af25-5cf0-b97f-e81a9e287013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Costomediastinal sinuses:', '["Interlobar fissures resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior pleural recesses at junction of costal and mediastinal pleura","Mediastinal spaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior recesses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragmatic recesses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior pleural recesses at junction of costal and mediastinal pleura', 'The costomediastinal sinuses are anterior pleural potential spaces where the costal pleura meets the mediastinal pleura behind the sternum. The costodiaphragmatic recesses lie inferiorly and do not define these anterior sinuses.'),
('46a65b12-738d-5269-8302-384bfd45f5fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Suprapleural membrane (Sibson''s):', '["Covers cervical pleura","from C7 TP to 1st rib inner border","Endothoracic","Covering apex of lung","Clavipectoral Cervical fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Covers cervical pleura', 'Sibson''s fascia is a thickened layer of endothoracic fascia that covers the cervical pleura over the dome of the pleura, anchoring it via the C7 transverse process to the inner border of the first rib. It strengthens the pleural apex.'),
('146d63b8-c649-56d6-b50c-1d633f83fdac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Endothoracic fascia:', '["Between pleura and pericardium resulting in decreased functional activity","Between pleura and lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thin connective tissue between parietal pleura and chest wall","Pleura itself without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Under skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin connective tissue between parietal pleura and chest wall', 'The endothoracic fascia is the thin connective-tissue layer lying between the parietal pleura and the thoracic wall. It is not found between pleura and lung, pleura and pericardium, or beneath the skin.'),
('be194580-a23b-55d3-a124-0cff7f56d049'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Brachiocephalic veins formed by:', '["Internal jugular + subclavian veins","External jugular + subclavian","Vertebral + IJV","Left + right IJV","Left + right subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular + subclavian veins', 'Each brachiocephalic vein is formed behind the corresponding sternoclavicular joint by the union of the internal jugular and subclavian veins. The brachiocephalic veins then join to form the superior vena cava.'),
('ff06c05e-3e65-5a04-8cde-234905cd6204'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Left brachiocephalic vein:', '["Same as right","Posterior to trachea","Right side","Longer","crosses midline behind manubrium Shorter"]'::jsonb, 'Longer', 'The left brachiocephalic vein is the longer of the two because it crosses the midline behind the manubrium to reach the SVC. The right brachiocephalic vein is short and nearly vertical.'),
('4142a864-c101-5beb-a21b-b7a2e54fb2f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Right brachiocephalic vein:', '["Crosses midline","Longer","Horizontal","Shorter","vertical descends behind right first costal cartilage Same length as left"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right brachiocephalic vein is shorter and more vertical, descending behind the right first costal cartilage to join the left vein in forming the SVC. The left brachiocephalic vein is the one that crosses the midline.'),
('8b7b7213-5ac2-52af-90f7-47f5ae72b7a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'SVC compression:', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngeal paralysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumothorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","May cause superior mediastinal syndrome (swelling, cyanosis)"]'::jsonb, 'May cause superior mediastinal syndrome (swelling, cyanosis)', 'Compression or obstruction of the superior vena cava produces superior vena cava syndrome with facial and upper-limb swelling, cyanosis, and distended neck veins. Pulmonary edema, heart failure, and pneumothorax do not arise directly from SVC compression.'),
('e60ceb8b-e996-57ea-9684-019261cf9675'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Tracheoesophageal groove:', '["Retropharyngeal space","Paratracheal space","Between trachea and esophagus","contains recurrent laryngeal nerves","Carotid sheath Prevertebral space"]'::jsonb, 'Between trachea and esophagus', 'The tracheoesophageal groove is the sulcus between the trachea anteriorly and the esophagus posteriorly. It is clinically important because the recurrent laryngeal nerves ascend within it.'),
('7ae69150-2fe8-54df-8163-5a9bd6a74e25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Azygos lobe:', '["Supernumerary lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Fissure variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left lung mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal accessory lobe on right from azygos vein indentation","Middle lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal accessory lobe on right from azygos vein indentation', 'The azygos lobe is an accessory lobe of the right lung created when the azygos vein comes to lie within a pleural fold, producing an accessory fissure at the apex. It is a normal variant and does not represent a left-lung or middle-lobe structure.'),
('fbfd7e15-04d0-52ed-8e60-fc7a4d1abed1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Pericardial cyst:', '["Lung cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleural effusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital cyst in anterior/cardiophrenic angle","Aortic aneurysm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mediastinal tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital cyst in anterior/cardiophrenic angle', 'A pericardial cyst is a congenital fluid-filled sac typically located at the cardiophrenic angle on chest radiography. It is not a lung cyst, pleural effusion, aneurysm, or solid mediastinal tumor, and it is usually asymptomatic.'),
('39f6db91-6545-52e0-99d9-980079ad0892'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome:', '["Diaphragm paralysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Aortic aneurysm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung compression mediated by specific receptor activation","C8/T1 compression by cervical rib or scalene muscles","Heart ischemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C8/T1 compression by cervical rib or scalene muscles', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome results from compression of the lower trunk of the brachial plexus (C8/T1) and subclavian vessels as they pass through the thoracic outlet, commonly by a cervical rib or hypertrophied scalene muscles. Pain and paresthesia along the medial arm and ulnar side of the hand are characteristic.'),
('7c5021cc-b11c-513f-bb25-415966f34477'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'First rib important for:', '["Trachea resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic outlet anatomy (subclavian vessels and brachial plexus pass over it)","Lung apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragm attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternoclavicular joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic outlet anatomy (subclavian vessels and brachial plexus pass over it)', 'The subclavian artery and brachial plexus cross the first rib behind the scalenus anterior, while the subclavian vein passes in front of it. The first rib is therefore a key landmark in thoracic outlet anatomy and a surgical guide to these structures.'),
('56060af0-0ee4-5171-8a19-09ab46ffa279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Subclavian artery on the right originates from:', '["Carotid","Subclavian vein","Vertebral","Brachiocephalic trunk","Aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk', 'On the right, the subclavian artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk behind the right sternoclavicular joint. It then arches over the first rib and continues as the axillary artery at the lateral border of that rib.'),
('75a518d1-c752-5455-bd48-13e44546dc6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Subclavian artery on the left originates from:', '["Aortic arch (directly)","Vertebral","Subclavian vein","Left common carotid","Brachiocephalic trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (directly)', 'Unlike the right side, the left subclavian artery arises directly from the aortic arch as its third branch after the left common carotid. It ascends into the root of the neck and arches over the first rib.'),
('89f908ea-b212-5a4b-8696-0007a4f88d24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Cervical rib:', '["Accessory rib from C7","can cause thoracic outlet compression","From C6","From sternum","From T1 From clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory rib from C7', 'A cervical rib is an accessory rib arising from the transverse process of C7, present in about 0.5-1% of the population. Because it lies in the thoracic outlet, it can compress the lower trunk of the brachial plexus or subclavian vessels.'),
('c33fe52d-ec2a-5731-8739-ec462e7c3710'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Anterior scalene insertion:', '["Manubrium","First rib (scalene tubercle)","Sternocostal junction","Clavicle","Second rib"]'::jsonb, 'First rib (scalene tubercle)', 'The scalenus anterior descends to insert on the scalene tubercle of the first rib, anterior to the subclavian artery. The subclavian vein runs in front of it, separated from the artery by this muscle.'),
('81bbbbcf-60d4-5c24-82f0-183112d574ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Middle scalene insertion:', '["Second rib resulting in decreased functional activity","First rib (posterior to subclavian artery)","1st and 2nd ribs mediated by specific receptor activation","Clavicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First rib (posterior to subclavian artery)', 'The scalenus medius inserts on the first rib behind the groove for the subclavian artery. Along with the scalenus anterior it delimits the interscalene space through which the subclavian artery and brachial plexus pass.'),
('72b97f62-6fc8-569a-8262-4cdbf1f3a0f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Interscalene groove:', '["Behind sternocleidomastoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Below clavicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to scalenus anterior mediated by specific receptor activation","Between posterior + middle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between anterior and middle scalene: subclavian artery + brachial plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Between anterior and middle scalene: subclavian artery + brachial plexus', 'The interscalene groove lies on the first rib between the scalenus anterior and scalenus medius. The subclavian artery and brachial plexus occupy this space, whereas the subclavian vein passes anterior to the scalenus anterior.'),
('c9961053-aa92-579c-9fe8-929c5ffcb7ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Subclavian vein lies:', '["Behind anterior scalene resulting in decreased functional activity","In interscalene groove accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind subclavian artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior to anterior scalene (NOT in interscalene groove)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to anterior scalene (NOT in interscalene groove)', 'The subclavian vein crosses the first rib anterior to the scalenus anterior, outside the interscalene groove that contains the artery and nerves. The muscle therefore separates the vein from the subclavian artery and brachial plexus.'),
('82fc15c0-7404-5b2b-b371-d6347e9321df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Stellate ganglion at:', '["C7 level (fusion of inferior cervical + T1 sympathetic ganglia)","T6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C2 mediated by specific receptor activation","T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C7 level (fusion of inferior cervical + T1 sympathetic ganglia)', 'The stellate (cervicothoracic) ganglion is formed by fusion of the inferior cervical and first thoracic sympathetic ganglia and lies at the C7 level. It sits behind the vertebral artery near the head of the first rib.'),
('9756eff2-4c37-50bd-83d2-63c540130dd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Stellate ganglion block may cause:', '["Mydriasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Lid lag accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exophthalmos mediated by specific receptor activation","Horner''s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos)","Hyperhidrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Horner''s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos)', 'Blocking the stellate ganglion removes sympathetic tone to the face and eye, producing Horner''s syndrome with ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, and apparent enophthalmos. Mydriasis, exophthalmos, and hyperhidrosis reflect sympathetic overactivity rather than blockade.'),
('36894ecf-e66f-5488-9b67-915f2ba7e69f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Vertebral artery origin:', '["Aortic arch","Costocervical trunk","Subclavian artery (1st part)","Brachiocephalic","Internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (1st part)', 'The vertebral artery is the first branch of the first part of the subclavian artery. It ascends through the transverse foramina of C6 to C1 and enters the skull via the foramen magnum.'),
('5b099215-6a7a-57d5-89f1-ffa9a40ee628'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Vertebral vein:', '["Subclavian resulting in decreased functional activity","IJV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Azygos without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accompanies vertebral artery drains into brachiocephalic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Accompanies vertebral artery drains into brachiocephalic vein', 'The vertebral vein accompanies the vertebral artery through the transverse foramina and drains into the brachiocephalic vein. It carries venous blood from the deep cervical and suboccipital regions toward the superior vena cava.'),
('a2f6422e-ea85-5f30-89d4-8ac46df52432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thoracic duct on left side at T4:', '["Crosses anterior to trachea","Stays right","Crosses anterior to esophagus","Crosses behind esophagus","arches left to join left venous angle Crosses behind aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses behind esophagus', 'The thoracic duct ascends in the posterior mediastinum to the right of the esophagus, then crosses behind the esophagus at about T4-T5 to reach the left side. It arches over the left pleura to drain into the left venous angle.'),
('3671d901-82d2-53c0-93bf-c73d89bb7155'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Recurrent laryngeal nodes:', '["Paratracheal nodes near RLN","important for lung cancer drainage","Supraclavicular","Anterior mediastinal","Axillary Posterior mediastinal"]'::jsonb, 'Paratracheal nodes near RLN', 'Recurrent laryngeal nodes are paratracheal lymph nodes lying along the recurrent laryngeal nerves. They are frequently involved in bronchogenic carcinoma and esophageal cancer and are relevant in staging and prognosis.'),
('42b20ada-b600-5c69-b77f-fd3a7ace19ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Anterior mediastinal nodes:', '["Paratracheal resulting in decreased functional activity","Intercostal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Along internal thoracic vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Along internal thoracic vessels', 'The anterior (prevascular) mediastinal nodes course along the internal thoracic (mammary) vessels behind the sternum. They drain the anterior chest wall, thymus, and medial parts of the breasts.'),
('6f7a4d94-d63d-5cf9-9d23-5fab434732d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Posterior mediastinal nodes:', '["Supraclavicular","Paraesophageal","intercostal","diaphragmatic","Axillary Paratracheal Anterior mediastinal"]'::jsonb, 'Paraesophageal', 'Posterior (paravertebral) mediastinal nodes include the paraesophageal and intercostal groups that lie along the esophagus and descending aorta. They drain the esophagus, lower trachea, and posterior thoracic structures.'),
('3223f91e-3d7f-5b57-87d5-d5d224afdb38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Mediastinal masses anterior compartment:', '["Thymoma","teratoma","thyroid lymphoma (4 Ts)","Neurogenic tumor Lymphoma posterior","Bronchogenic cyst Aortic aneurysm"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma', 'The anterior (prevascular) mediastinum is classically the site of the ''4 Ts''—thymoma, teratoma, thyroid mass, and lymphoma. Thymoma is the most common anterior mediastinal mass and is associated with myasthenia gravis.'),
('cc9002a5-6605-54ac-a97f-7e9abf1d2505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Mediastinal masses middle compartment:', '["Lymphadenopathy","bronchogenic cyst","pericardial cyst","Teratoma","Neurogenic tumor Thymoma Thyroid extension"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphadenopathy', 'The middle mediastinum contains lymph nodes around the trachea and bronchi, so lymphadenopathy is the classic mass of this compartment. Lymphoma, sarcoidosis, and metastatic disease are common causes.'),
('ed723ae3-464c-53fa-ae44-bffc9e7010df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Mediastinal masses posterior compartment:', '["Lymphoma","Pericardial cyst","Neurogenic tumor (most common)","aortic aneurysm","esophageal tumor Thymoma Teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic tumor (most common)', 'The posterior (paravertebral) mediastinum is the most common site of neurogenic tumors because it contains the sympathetic trunk and intercostal nerves. The typical tumors are schwannoma, neurofibroma, and ganglioneuroma.'),
('2753da0f-1552-5d93-a20f-8002e8edb82d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Most common mediastinal tumor overall:', '["Pericardial cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Teratoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurogenic tumor (posterior)","Thymoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic tumor (posterior)', 'Neurogenic tumors are the most common mediastinal neoplasms overall, and they typically arise in the posterior mediastinum. In adults the anterior compartment is instead dominated by thymoma, lymphoma, and germ cell tumors.'),
('ad6ed50c-f2b7-526f-9d1e-925dfd42e3f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Most common anterior mediastinal tumor:', '["Teratoma","Thyroid","Thymoma","Lymphoma","Pericardial cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma', 'Thymoma is the most common tumor of the anterior mediastinum in adults. It is strongly associated with myasthenia gravis and, unlike lymphoma, usually follows an indolent local course.'),
('3dccfd0a-b90a-55f9-8112-e983a2473719'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Ligamentum arteriosum remnant of:', '["Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical vein","Foramen ovale","Ductus venosus","Umbilical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ductus arteriosus', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous remnant of the ductus arteriosus after its functional closure soon after birth. It connects the pulmonary trunk to the undersurface of the aortic arch and marks the point where the left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks beneath the arch.'),
('5ff612bf-aabc-5c06-a181-fc382b067003'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Aortic arch lies at:', '["T4/T5 level (second right costal cartilage)","T2/T3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10 mediated by specific receptor activation","T8 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T6 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 level (second right costal cartilage)', 'The aortic arch begins and ends opposite the fourth thoracic vertebra, corresponding to the sternal angle (manubriosternal joint) and the second costal cartilage. It is the key surface and radiological landmark for the superior mediastinum.'),
('d47a8077-56ef-5f63-bf84-9af1c7be3654'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Aortic arch relations anterior:', '["Thoracic duct","Esophagus","Manubrium","thymus","left brachiocephalic vein Trachea Vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium', 'Anteriorly the aortic arch is related to the manubrium as well as the thymus, left brachiocephalic vein, and sternothyroid/sternohyoid muscles. The trachea and esophagus lie posteriorly and the thoracic duct and left recurrent laryngeal nerve behind and to the left.'),
('afcf972a-f24e-5c5f-a120-4e77d944a446'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Aortic arch relations posterior:', '["Lung","Thymus","Sternum Pericardium","Trachea esophagus","thoracic duct left RLN"]'::jsonb, 'Trachea esophagus', 'Posteriorly the aortic arch is related to the trachea, esophagus, thoracic duct, left recurrent laryngeal nerve, and vertebral column. The sternum and pericardium lie anterior, and the thymus sits in front of the great veins rather than behind the arch.'),
('8adad086-00f8-5091-8d1b-b5bc73ba82b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Aortic isthmus:', '["At diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","At T8 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between brachiocephalic and left carotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Narrowing between left subclavian and ligamentum arteriosum","At T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Narrowing between left subclavian and ligamentum arteriosum', 'The aortic isthmus is a slight narrowing of the aorta just distal to the origin of the left subclavian artery at the attachment of the ligamentum arteriosum. It is the preferred site of coarctation of the aorta.'),
('ff00fb48-d91b-5587-bf9c-bd9215b586eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Aortic coarctation site:', '["Abdominal aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","At aortic isthmus (near ductus arteriosus)","Ascending aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Descending aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic root leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At aortic isthmus (near ductus arteriosus)', 'Coarctation of the aorta occurs at the aortic isthmus, close to the attachment of the ductus arteriosus or ligamentum arteriosum. The typical lesion is a discrete narrowing just distal to the left subclavian artery.'),
('029e32c2-947e-5065-9d52-fa20cc61856f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Coarctation clinical signs:', '["Aneurysm resulting in decreased functional activity","Cyanosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower limb edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulse deficit right arm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper limb hypertension weak femoral pulses rib notching"]'::jsonb, 'Upper limb hypertension weak femoral pulses rib notching', 'Coarctation causes hypertension and strong pulses in the upper limbs with weak, delayed femoral pulses in the lower limbs. The collateral circulation through dilated intercostal arteries produces rib notching on chest radiographs.'),
('ca1ce9ed-b1d2-5f1b-8127-da0facd00fe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA):', '["Heart septal defect resulting in decreased functional activity","clinical significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Valvular defect mediated by specific receptor activation","Myocardial defect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Persistent communication between pulmonary trunk and aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent communication between pulmonary trunk and aortic arch', 'Patent ductus arteriosus is the persistence of the ductus arteriosus, leaving a communication between the pulmonary trunk and the aortic arch. In a left-to-right shunt it produces a continuous ''machinery'' murmur best heard at the left upper sternal border.'),
('36f1ba43-65c6-557e-aa5c-f54568315273'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Aortic arch variants:', '["Double right resulting in decreased functional activity","Bovine arch (brachiocephalic and left carotid common trunk) 15-20%","arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Left arch 10% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right arch 50% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bovine arch (brachiocephalic and left carotid common trunk) 15-20%', 'In a bovine arch the brachiocephalic and left common carotid arteries arise from a common trunk, a variant found in about 15-20% of people. The usual pattern is three separate branches arising directly from the aortic arch.'),
('541b9d27-c460-507e-9fd6-fccf562fb81d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which structure is NOT located in the superior mediastinum?', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart lies in the middle mediastinum, not the superior mediastinum. The superior mediastinum contains the thymus, arch of the aorta, trachea, esophagus, and brachiocephalic veins.'),
('e6197f4c-83db-504f-9b0f-32f27861c8df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The mediastinum is:', '["The space behind the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","The pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space below the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in front of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs"]'::jsonb, 'The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs', 'The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity lying between the two pleural sacs. It extends from the sternum to the vertebral column and from the thoracic inlet to the diaphragm and is divided into superior and inferior parts.'),
('a866d7d8-78b6-543d-9332-558d16dac057'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The superior mediastinum contains:', '["Diaphragm","Thymus","great vessels","trachea","esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus', 'The superior mediastinum contains the thymus, which is prominent in infancy and involutes progressively after puberty. It also holds the great vessels, trachea, esophagus, and thoracic duct, whereas the diaphragm lies inferiorly at the thoracic outlet boundary.'),
('df9f524c-4ee1-5d57-a08e-48f8e552c7a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The anterior mediastinum contains:', '["Lungs","Trachea and bronchi","Thymus (in children)","fat","lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus (in children)', 'The anterior (prevascular) mediastinum contains the thymus, which is large in children and replaced by fat and fibrofatty tissue in adults. It also houses loose connective tissue and lymph nodes rather than the trachea or lungs.'),
('13859bee-0dd6-5ec1-bf21-71c71be6ab08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The middle mediastinum contains:', '["Heart","pericardium","roots of great vessels","phrenic nerves","Only the thymus"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The middle mediastinum is the central compartment that contains the heart, pericardium, roots of the great vessels, and phrenic nerves. The thymus belongs to the anterior mediastinum rather than the middle compartment.'),
('df9e2ea7-d79b-5456-b365-909a846279ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The posterior mediastinum contains:', '["Phrenic nerves","Lungs","Heart and pericardium","Thymus","Esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus', 'The posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus, descending thoracic aorta, thoracic duct, azygos and hemiazygos veins, and sympathetic trunks. The phrenic nerves and great vessel roots lie in the middle mediastinum, and the lungs are outside the mediastinum.'),
('469c32f8-c900-5220-97e6-0af5380bb974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The brachiocephalic artery divides into:', '["Vertebral and subclavian","Right common carotid and right subclavian arteries","Internal and external carotids","Left common carotid and left subclavian","Thoracic and axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Right common carotid and right subclavian arteries', 'The brachiocephalic (innominate) artery is the first and largest branch of the aortic arch. Behind the right sternoclavicular joint it divides into the right common carotid and right subclavian arteries.'),
('56cb5ae1-f790-51a7-85d6-8d67e505ce4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The superior vena cava (SVC) is formed by:', '["The coronary sinus","The internal and external jugular veins","The azygos and hemiazygos veins","Left and right subclavian veins","Right and left brachiocephalic veins"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left brachiocephalic veins', 'The superior vena cava is formed by the union of the right and left brachiocephalic veins behind the right first costal cartilage. It descends to enter the right atrium at the level of the third costal cartilage.'),
('0b15273f-e89c-542e-938b-fbef609bebf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The azygos vein drains:', '["The posterior thoracic wall and enters the SVC at T4","The head and neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anterior thoracic wall mediated by specific receptor activation","The upper limb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The abdominal wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior thoracic wall and enters the SVC at T4', 'The azygos vein drains the posterior thoracic wall and ascends to the right of the vertebral column. It arches over the right main bronchus and enters the superior vena cava posteriorly at the level of the fourth thoracic vertebra.'),
('4c0df311-c639-5120-ab63-8bded5794ca0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The thoracic duct begins:', '["At the jugular venous angle resulting in decreased functional activity","At the aortic hiatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the neck mediated by specific receptor activation","In the thorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the abdomen at the cisterna chyli (L1-L2 level)"]'::jsonb, 'In the abdomen at the cisterna chyli (L1-L2 level)', 'The thoracic duct begins in the abdomen at the cisterna chyli, usually at the L1-L2 level anterior to the vertebral column. It enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus and ascends in the posterior mediastinum to end in the left venous angle.'),
('6675e787-4bee-56bf-b9c0-b0bf95799e0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The vagus nerve in the thorax:', '["Provides motor to the diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","Is purely sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contributes to the esophageal plexus and provides parasympathetic innervation to thoracic viscera","Supplies intercostal nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forms the phrenic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contributes to the esophageal plexus and provides parasympathetic innervation to thoracic viscera', 'In the thorax the right and left vagus nerves pass behind the lung roots, contribute to the esophageal plexus, and supply parasympathetic fibers to the heart and lungs. Motor supply to the diaphragm comes from the phrenic nerve, not the vagus.'),
('34ba7dae-df47-5c18-b265-7c4cb47c07c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The right recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around:', '["Right brachiocephalic artery","Right subclavian artery","Ligamentum arteriosum","Innominate vein","Aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Right subclavian artery', 'The right recurrent laryngeal nerve branches from the right vagus in the root of the neck and loops beneath the right subclavian artery. It then ascends in the tracheoesophageal groove to supply the larynx.'),
('966694f8-1eac-5882-9337-31de3ab22669'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops around:', '["Left brachiocephalic vein","Brachiocephalic artery","Pulmonary trunk","Aortic arch (at the ligamentum arteriosum)","Left subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (at the ligamentum arteriosum)', 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve arises from the left vagus in the thorax and hooks beneath the aortic arch just lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum. It ascends next to the trachea and esophagus to reach the larynx.'),
('db7687c8-8ca0-5ba8-811f-d0efb3b2193b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The sympathetic trunk in the thorax:', '["Is motor to the diaphragm","Is part of the parasympathetic system","Supplies the heart only","Is absent in the thorax","Contains 11-12 thoracic ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Contains 11-12 thoracic ganglia', 'The thoracic sympathetic trunk lies anterior to the heads of the ribs and contains 11 to 12 ganglia, one corresponding to each thoracic spinal nerve. It is sympathetic, not parasympathetic, and its branches supply the viscera, body wall, and limbs.'),
('d009c792-469c-526f-9137-be28ce33c8d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The greater splanchnic nerve arises from:', '["T5-T9 thoracic sympathetic ganglia","Lumbar ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10-T11 mediated by specific receptor activation","T12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervical ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T5-T9 thoracic sympathetic ganglia', 'The greater splanchnic nerve is formed by preganglionic fibers from the T5-T9 thoracic sympathetic ganglia. It descends through the crus of the diaphragm and synapses in the celiac ganglia.'),
('ff5d6f6a-ae4e-5292-9786-8ab67351ee9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The lesser splanchnic nerve arises from:', '["T10-T11 thoracic sympathetic ganglia","C8 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","L1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T5-T9 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T10-T11 thoracic sympathetic ganglia', 'The lesser splanchnic nerve arises from the T10-T11 thoracic sympathetic ganglia and pierces the diaphragm with the greater splanchnic nerve. Its fibers synapse mainly in the superior mesenteric and aorticorenal ganglia.'),
('b6120394-acac-5cb9-851c-a89cfd7a4b6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The least splanchnic nerve arises from:', '["C8 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 thoracic sympathetic ganglion","T5-T9 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10-T11 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T12 thoracic sympathetic ganglion', 'The least splanchnic nerve arises from the T12 thoracic sympathetic ganglion and enters the abdominal cavity. It carries preganglionic fibers that synapse in the renal plexus, providing sympathetic supply to the kidney.'),
('caedc564-0024-5e74-a18a-d0df0f35fdc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The esophagus in the thorax:', '["Is entirely skeletal muscle","Passes through the posterior mediastinum","anterior to the descending aorta","Is the widest part of the GI tract","Has relations to the trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Passes through the posterior mediastinum', 'The thoracic esophagus descends through the posterior mediastinum, behind the trachea and left main bronchus and in front of the vertebral column. It pierces the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus at the level of T10.'),
('eb709bd8-97ab-5589-9078-975d9fdead7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The trachea in the thorax:', '["Is muscular","Is posterior to the esophagus","Bifurcates at T4/T5 into left and right main bronchi","Is entirely in the neck","Is C-shaped with cartilaginous rings"]'::jsonb, 'Bifurcates at T4/T5 into left and right main bronchi', 'The trachea enters the superior mediastinum and bifurcates at the T4/T5 intervertebral disc into the right and left main bronchi. It is not posterior to the esophagus, because the esophagus lies behind it, and its C-shaped rings open posteriorly.'),
('2676c610-bd36-5e9d-80c6-a7d75ed62708'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Thymoma is associated with:', '["Esophageal cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiac disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myasthenia gravis (autoimmune neuromuscular disorder)"]'::jsonb, 'Myasthenia gravis (autoimmune neuromuscular disorder)', 'Thymoma, a neoplasm of thymic epithelial cells in the superior and anterior mediastinum, is strongly associated with myasthenia gravis through autoimmune blockade of the neuromuscular junction. Lung, esophageal, or thyroid tumors and cardiac disease do not share this classic association.'),
('c211b0fa-f20f-50ab-9912-13ca8f8cd7c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The esophagus has three constrictions:', '["At the pylorus","Only at the gastroesophageal junction","At the cardia","At the oropharynx","At C6 (pharyngoesophageal)"]'::jsonb, 'At C6 (pharyngoesophageal)', 'The esophagus has three normal narrowings: at C6 (pharyngoesophageal junction), at T4/T5 (crossed by the aortic arch and left main bronchus), and at T10 (the diaphragmatic hiatus). The pylorus and cardia are gastric landmarks, not esophageal constrictions.'),
('68b6870c-c996-50a5-a0c1-33507a01eb04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The thoracic aorta begins:', '["At C6 resulting in decreased functional activity","At L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At T1 mediated by specific receptor activation","At T4 (continuation of aortic arch) and ends at T12 (aortic hiatus)","At the heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At T4 (continuation of aortic arch) and ends at T12 (aortic hiatus)', 'The thoracic aorta is the continuation of the aortic arch, beginning at the T4/T5 level and descending in the posterior mediastinum to end at the aortic hiatus (T12). It does not begin at C6, T1, L1, or the heart.'),
('c642a07f-4c1b-5182-90df-0575a2a8399c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The superior thoracic aperture boundaries include:', '["Manubrium and clavicles","Clavicle and scapula","Diaphragm","T1 vertebra","1st ribs"]'::jsonb, 'T1 vertebra', 'The superior thoracic aperture (thoracic inlet) is bounded by the manubrium of sternum, the first ribs, and the T1 vertebral body. The T1 vertebra forms the posterior boundary through which the trachea, esophagus, and great vessels pass.'),
('cd3e9b1a-7853-5a72-8636-85b84893302e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The inferior thoracic aperture boundaries include:', '["Diaphragm only","The first ribs","The clavicles","The sternal angle","T12 vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'T12 vertebra', 'The inferior thoracic aperture (thoracic outlet) is bounded by the T12 vertebra posteriorly, the 11th and 12th ribs, the costal margin, and the xiphosternal junction, and is closed by the diaphragm. The sternal angle and clavicles are not boundaries.'),
('53aaf9eb-f36e-5608-971f-4ae51689e437'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The mediastinum contains:', '["Only trachea and esophagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only heart accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only great vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Lungs and pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, thymus"]'::jsonb, 'Heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, thymus', 'The mediastinum is the central thoracic compartment containing the heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, thymus, thoracic duct, and lymphatic tissue. The lungs and pleura lie lateral to it, outside the mediastinum.'),
('70bc0a1e-9d42-5e78-884b-dcf761343e60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The thymus is located in:', '["Posterior mediastinum only","Middle mediastinum","Superior and anterior mediastinum","Anterior mediastinum only","Posterior mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and anterior mediastinum', 'The thymus is most prominent in the superior mediastinum, and its remnant extends inferiorly into the anterior mediastinum. It does not lie in the middle or posterior mediastinum, which hold the heart or esophagus and great vessels.'),
('f483a671-c474-5d13-8e67-6b331ee680c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The esophagus is located in:', '["Superior mediastinum","Anterior mediastinum","Middle mediastinum","Posterior mediastinum","Mediastinum and abdominal cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior mediastinum', 'The thoracic esophagus runs through the posterior mediastinum, positioned behind the trachea and between the thoracic aorta and azygos vein. It continues through the esophageal hiatus into the abdomen.'),
('ea382910-65b8-512d-886a-0d236fef8e79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The descending thoracic aorta is located in:', '["Anterior mediastinum","Middle mediastinum","Superior mediastinum","Mediastinum and abdominal cavity","Posterior mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior mediastinum', 'The descending thoracic aorta lies in the posterior mediastinum, slightly to the left of the vertebral column. The middle mediastinum contains the heart and pericardium, while the anterior mediastinum holds the lower thymus.'),
('34875ccb-3729-5c41-827f-839ff09fae39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The arch of the aorta begins and ends at:', '["T12 level","T8 level","T1 level","T4-T5 level","C6 level"]'::jsonb, 'T4-T5 level', 'The aortic arch begins and ends at the level of the manubrium (T4/T5), giving rise to the brachiocephalic, left common carotid, and left subclavian arteries. The descending thoracic aorta then continues down to T12.'),
('36c83fbf-768d-5e62-8dee-9dbd29409bfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The vagus nerves are located in:', '["Middle mediastinum","Anterior mediastinum","Posterior mediastinum","Superior mediastinum","All mediastinal compartments"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior mediastinum', 'The vagus nerves descend through the posterior mediastinum beside the esophagus, where they form the esophageal plexus. The middle mediastinum contains the heart and pericardium rather than the vagus trunks.'),
('3829f61c-5b4f-51d3-bc8f-0b482bba5402'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The thoracic sympathetic trunk is located:', '["Anterior to vertebral bodies","In the middle mediastinum","Lateral to vertebral bodies","Posterior to vertebral bodies","In the anterior mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to vertebral bodies', 'The thoracic sympathetic trunk lies on the heads of the ribs, just lateral to the vertebral bodies, with ganglia connected to the intercostal nerves. It is not placed within the middle or anterior mediastinum.'),
('885679f5-97d3-59b5-9d0b-88fd0529bd89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which structure is NOT located in the superior mediastinum. Which option is most accurate?', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart lies in the middle mediastinum, not the superior mediastinum. The thymus, arch of aorta, trachea, and brachiocephalic veins are all superior mediastinum contents.'),
('868692d0-c018-56f4-a87f-f8f7b43a54e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Mediastinum: which structure is not located in the superior mediastinum?', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart is confined to the middle mediastinum within the pericardium. The thymus, arch of aorta, trachea, and brachiocephalic veins all lie in the superior mediastinum.'),
('44eb13ba-c174-5027-b92b-2438c6cac1d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, identify the correct response: which structure is not located in the superior mediastinum?', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart is located in the middle mediastinum rather than the superior mediastinum. Thymus, arch of aorta, trachea, and brachiocephalic veins are the superior mediastinum contents.'),
('243d9140-b0f4-5cb0-b6dc-f2ea6f7632d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which structure is NOT located in the superior mediastinum. Select the best answer.', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart occupies the middle mediastinum and is not a superior mediastinum structure. The thymus, arch of aorta, trachea, and brachiocephalic veins all lie in the superior mediastinum.'),
('9b94aacf-26af-5260-8f3b-6fa567698980'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which response correctly addresses this Mediastinum item: which structure is not located in the superior mediastinum?', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart lies in the middle mediastinum, so it is not found in the superior mediastinum. The thymus, arch of aorta, trachea, and brachiocephalic veins are all superior mediastinum contents.'),
('9d713113-0a03-5dea-afa5-4d63239a3fed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which option correctly describes The mediastinum?', '["The space behind the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","The pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space below the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in front of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs"]'::jsonb, 'The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs', 'The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thoracic cavity lying between the two pleural sacs. It contains the heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, and thymus, unlike the pleural cavities which hold the lungs.'),
('bf214fed-2c5a-5384-a22c-11cb15da12cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The mediastinum is. Which option is most accurate?', '["The space behind the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","The pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space below the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in front of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs"]'::jsonb, 'The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs', 'The mediastinum spans from the sternum to the vertebral column and lies between the two pleural sacs, enclosing the heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, and thymus. It is not merely a space behind the heart or below the diaphragm.'),
('d1dc009d-cfd8-5515-a01c-f27fa5e8e5e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Choose the most accurate statement about Mediastinum: the mediastinum is?', '["The space behind the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","The pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space below the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in front of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs"]'::jsonb, 'The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs', 'The mediastinum is the central compartment of the thorax between the two pleural sacs. It houses the heart, great vessels, trachea, esophagus, and thymus, and is not limited to a space behind the heart or in front of the trachea.'),
('06057de7-b107-5177-9ab9-05373527c516'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: mediastinum boundaries:?', '["Sternum anterior","vertebrae posterior","superior thoracic aperture above","diaphragm below","Clavicles above Xiphoid below Ribs laterally Pericardium anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum anterior', 'The mediastinum is bounded anteriorly by the sternum, posteriorly by the thoracic vertebrae, superiorly by the superior thoracic aperture, and inferiorly by the diaphragm. The sternum therefore forms the anterior boundary.'),
('ec35551b-415a-55be-99b4-b42bc6a752f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: mediastinum divided into:?', '["division resulting in decreased functional activity","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior (by plane from sternal angle to T4/T5)","Upper + middle + lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior + posterior only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior (by plane from sternal angle to T4/T5)', 'A horizontal plane through the sternal angle and the T4/T5 intervertebral disc divides the mediastinum into superior and inferior portions. The inferior part is further split into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments.'),
('b6c14d17-6981-58e3-b615-39f8233fa0e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: superior mediastinum contents:?', '["Diaphragm Lungs only","Thymus great vessels","trachea esophagus","thoracic duct phrenic/vagus nerves","Heart only Pericardium only"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus great vessels', 'The superior mediastinum characteristically contains the thymus and the great vessels (arch of aorta, brachiocephalic artery, and brachiocephalic veins). These are among its most distinctive contents.'),
('7d3f1457-85df-59a3-b5e8-06ebab2cf0e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: inferior mediastinum divided into:?', '["Anterior","middle","posterior compartments","Left + right","Superior + inferior subdivisions Upper + lower"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior', 'The inferior mediastinum is divided into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments. The anterior compartment is one of these three subdivisions, so it is part of the correct division.'),
('3574460a-1186-5f78-9e17-8f604a7af86d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Anterior mediastinum contents:'' is:', '["Heart","Thymus (remnant)","internal thoracic vessels lymph nodes","fat Aorta","Trachea Esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus (remnant)', 'The anterior mediastinum contains areolar tissue, internal thoracic vessels, lymph nodes, and the remnant of the thymus. The heart, trachea, and esophagus lie in the middle and posterior compartments instead.'),
('09f69591-f085-5b1f-865b-f8094e061aa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: middle mediastinum contents:?', '["Thymus","Esophagus Aorta","Heart","pericardium phrenic nerves","roots of great vessels Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The middle mediastinum contains the heart and pericardium along with the phrenic nerves and roots of the great vessels. The thymus is superior and the esophagus and thoracic aorta are posterior.'),
('c270676e-9916-5a8f-980a-7187dd0227ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: posterior mediastinum contents:?', '["Great vessels","Heart Thymus","Esophagus descending thoracic aorta","azygos/hemiazygos veins thoracic duct","vagus/splanchnic nerves Trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus descending thoracic aorta', 'The posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus, descending thoracic aorta, azygos/hemiazygos veins, thoracic duct, and vagus/splanchnic nerves. The esophagus and descending thoracic aorta are its two most prominent structures.'),
('d33f0b95-6b1a-59dc-842e-d0c8469fea3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: sternal angle (angle of louis) at:?', '["T6/T7 resulting in decreased functional activity","T4/T5 intervertebral disc","C7/T1 mediated by specific receptor activation","T2/T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 intervertebral disc', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) lies at the T4/T5 intervertebral disc. This level marks the tracheal bifurcation, the beginning and end of the aortic arch, and the plane dividing the mediastinum.'),
('fe4b0be8-afa9-5ccf-bcd1-f3d64aa9e88c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: at sternal angle:?', '["Diaphragm dome","Thymus lies","Arch of aorta begins/ends trachea bifurcates","azygos vein enters SVC thoracic duct crosses","Heart apex beat Pulmonary hilum"]'::jsonb, 'Arch of aorta begins/ends trachea bifurcates', 'At the sternal angle (T4/T5), the aortic arch begins and ends and the trachea bifurcates into the main bronchi. The azygos vein also arches over the right hilum at this level.'),
('9bef40ef-7117-5889-aaf4-d1c21197fad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: azygos vein origin:?', '["Left ascending lumbar","Right ascending lumbar + subcostal veins","Renal vein","Inferior vena cava","Posterior intercostal"]'::jsonb, 'Right ascending lumbar + subcostal veins', 'The azygos vein arises in the abdomen from the right ascending lumbar vein and the right subcostal vein. It ascends in the posterior mediastinum and arches over the right hilum to enter the superior vena cava.'),
('8529d8f9-2713-5bf7-a01a-180893c5e1ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Azygos vein arch:'' is:', '["Through diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","To coronary sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Over right hilum at T4/T5 to enter SVC","Behind sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To left SVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Over right hilum at T4/T5 to enter SVC', 'The azygos vein arches anteriorly over the root of the right lung at the T4/T5 level to drain into the superior vena cava. It does not connect to the coronary sinus or the left SVC.'),
('25850dbc-47f2-5c50-aef4-be0d89daa788'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: accessory hemiazygos:?', '["Drains left 4th-8th posterior intercostals","Right 4th-8th accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right upper intercostals mediated by specific receptor activation","Left lower intercostals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both sides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drains left 4th-8th posterior intercostals', 'The accessory hemiazygos vein drains the 4th through 8th left posterior intercostal veins. It descends on the left side and crosses the midline to join the azygos vein.'),
('7d3c3518-f31a-5cbc-b9b1-fcf8541d983e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: hemiazygos vein:?', '["Drains directly to SVC","Left 4th-8th","Drains left 9th-11th posterior intercostals","crosses to azygos at T8/T9","Right intercostals Drains to left brachiocephalic"]'::jsonb, 'Drains left 9th-11th posterior intercostals', 'The hemiazygos vein drains the lower (9th to 11th) left posterior intercostal veins. It crosses the midline at about T8/T9 to empty into the azygos vein, not directly into the SVC.'),
('ed43a017-1eea-5567-a37e-cd829bd2bcca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: azygos system drains:?', '["Lungs","Heart","Posterior intercostals esophagus","pericardium mediastinum","Anterior intercostals Diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior intercostals esophagus', 'The azygos venous system drains the posterior intercostal veins, the esophagus, and other posterior thoracic wall structures. These veins provide collateral drainage into the superior vena cava.'),
('f081dae2-421f-55f0-8735-9a52b5638dfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: thoracic duct origin:?', '["Head resulting in decreased functional activity","Thorax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cisterna chyli at L1/L2 (abdomen)","Pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna chyli at L1/L2 (abdomen)', 'The thoracic duct originates in the abdomen as the cisterna chyli at the L1/L2 level, formed by the lumbar and intestinal lymphatic trunks. It ascends through the aortic hiatus into the thorax.'),
('8b2ba7cd-886e-52bf-80d2-bff1ce4ac0aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: thoracic duct course in thorax:?', '["Behind sternum","Middle mediastinum","In posterior mediastinum between azygos and aorta","then to left at T4/T5","Right side throughout Anterior mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'In posterior mediastinum between azygos and aorta', 'In the thorax, the thoracic duct ascends in the posterior mediastinum between the azygos vein and the thoracic aorta, then crosses to the left near T4/T5 before entering the left venous angle.'),
('d408fb37-696a-53e3-803d-a94418a4c2bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Right lymphatic duct:'' is:', '["Drains right upper quadrant into right venous angle","Lower body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left upper quadrant mediated by specific receptor activation","Entire body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right lower quadrant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drains right upper quadrant into right venous angle', 'The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right upper quadrant of the body, including the right arm, right side of the head and neck, and the right thorax, emptying into the right venous angle. The thoracic duct drains the remaining body.'),
('f8e852ea-413b-570a-b3e5-0537312c363f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: thoracic duct tributaries:?', '["Only bronchial","Only mediastinal Only esophageal","Intercostal","mediastinal esophageal","bronchomediastinal trunks Only intercostal"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal', 'The thoracic duct receives tributaries from intercostal lymph trunks, mediastinal and esophageal lymphatics, and the bronchomediastinal trunks. The intercostal lymphatic trunks are among its main tributaries draining the posterior thoracic wall.'),
('f5e41e1c-33dc-5b79-ba18-c37e84278942'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: descending thoracic aorta:?', '["From heart to T12","From T4/T5 to T12 (through aortic hiatus)","From T2 to T8","From T4 to diaphragm at T8","From T6 to T10"]'::jsonb, 'From T4/T5 to T12 (through aortic hiatus)', 'The descending thoracic aorta runs from the T4/T5 level, where the aortic arch ends, down to the T12 aortic hiatus in the diaphragm. It then continues as the abdominal aorta.'),
('b43eac76-7a44-5609-af9c-ecfa77cd408c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: aortic hiatus at:?', '["T8 resulting in decreased functional activity","T4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 (between crura of diaphragm)","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T12 (between crura of diaphragm)', 'The aortic hiatus lies at the T12 vertebral level, between the crura of the diaphragm, transmitting the aorta and thoracic duct. It is below the esophageal hiatus (T10) and the caval foramen (T8).'),
('5eb51509-1f09-55c1-a7db-cef4f8c22814'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: esophageal hiatus at:?', '["T12 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C7 mediated by specific receptor activation","T8 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10 (right crus of diaphragm)"]'::jsonb, 'T10 (right crus of diaphragm)', 'The esophageal hiatus is at the T10 level, formed by fibers of the right crus of the diaphragm, and transmits the esophagus with its vagal trunks. The T8 caval foramen and T12 aortic hiatus are separate openings.'),
('f6f9e92d-2d50-5a3c-a776-4ee98b650d82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: vena caval foramen at:?', '["L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","T10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T8 (in central tendon)","T12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (in central tendon)', 'The vena caval foramen lies at the T8 level in the central tendon of the diaphragm, transmitting the inferior vena cava and the right phrenic nerve. The esophageal hiatus is at T10 and the aortic hiatus at T12.'),
('83b713cc-80f5-5c48-8272-7f22acd282b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Esophagus course:'' is:', '["Anterior to heart","Right of midline","Posterior to trachea (superior)","behind LA (middle)","left of midline (inferior) Anterior to trachea Behind sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to trachea (superior)', 'In the superior mediastinum the esophagus lies posterior to the trachea; it then passes behind the left main bronchus and left atrium, and finally left of the midline before piercing the diaphragm. Its initial relationship is posterior to the trachea.'),
('8b7ee3c6-87a2-55f6-ba3e-1bc3ff1f15d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: esophageal constrictions:?', '["T2 resulting in decreased functional activity","T6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10 mediated by specific receptor activation","C6 (start) T4/T5 (aortic arch/left bronchus)","T10 (hiatus) C5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C6 (start) T4/T5 (aortic arch/left bronchus)', 'The esophagus shows three physiologic constrictions: at C6 (pharyngoesophageal junction), at T4/T5 where the aortic arch and left main bronchus cross it, and at T10 at the diaphragmatic hiatus. T2, T6, and C5 are not sites of esophageal narrowing.'),
('bad0f1d8-625c-5479-b0e2-8d0a2914fd17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: left recurrent laryngeal nerve:?', '["Right side","Posterior to esophagus","Thymus","Loops under aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum)","ascends in tracheoesophageal groove Anterior to trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Loops under aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum)', 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve arises from the left vagus and hooks under the aortic arch, just lateral to the ligamentum arteriosum, before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. This loop accounts for its lower origin relative to the right recurrent nerve.'),
('6948eea2-d971-589c-a0ea-f8ed35c507f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: right recurrent laryngeal nerve:?', '["Loops under right subclavian artery","SVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ligamentum arteriosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loops under right subclavian artery', 'The right recurrent laryngeal nerve branches from the right vagus and loops beneath the right subclavian artery before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. The aortic arch loop belongs to the left recurrent laryngeal nerve.'),
('0b754b35-9edc-5ff8-999f-b4c02049ed5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: esophageal plexus:?', '["Vagus nerves surround esophagus forming plexus","Sympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercostal mediated by specific receptor activation","Phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splanchnic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerves surround esophagus forming plexus', 'The esophageal plexus is formed by fibers of the right and left vagus nerves, together with sympathetic contributions, surrounding the esophagus in the posterior mediastinum. These fibers then coalesce into the anterior and posterior vagal trunks.'),
('598b8789-c9fd-5894-a418-8795c6b41716'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: anterior vagal trunk:?', '["Left phrenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Both accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left vagus (anterior surface of esophagus)","Right phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left vagus (anterior surface of esophagus)', 'The anterior vagal trunk, formed mainly by fibers of the left vagus nerve, runs on the anterior surface of the esophagus. It continues down to supply the anterior stomach wall.'),
('b4f588fb-125e-50a0-b4de-a08f2f3a5b5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Posterior vagal trunk:'' is:', '["Both resulting in decreased functional activity","Left vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right vagus (posterior surface of esophagus)","Left phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right vagus (posterior surface of esophagus)', 'The posterior vagal trunk, formed mainly by fibers of the right vagus nerve, runs on the posterior surface of the esophagus near the esophageal hiatus. It supplies the posterior stomach wall.'),
('517e64ef-bb52-526f-bec1-4aafc1e6d73c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: greater splanchnic nerve origin:?', '["L1-L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10-T11 mediated by specific receptor activation","T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia","C8-T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia', 'The greater splanchnic nerve arises from the T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia and descends through the diaphragm crura to the celiac plexus. The T10-T11 and T12 levels give the lesser and least splanchnic nerves, while C8-T4 and L1-L3 are not sources of splanchnic fibers.'),
('286d6a29-b7ac-5849-b2fe-48fbc5781a5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: lesser splanchnic nerve origin:?', '["L1","C8","T12","T5-T9","T10-T11"]'::jsonb, 'T10-T11', 'The lesser splanchnic nerve originates from the T10-T11 sympathetic ganglia and joins the celiac and aorticorenal plexuses. The greater nerve comes from T5-T9, the least from T12, and C8/L1 are unrelated vertebral levels.'),
('0128fcf0-1e42-5398-95fd-a210c6bb669b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: least splanchnic nerve origin:?', '["T12","C8","T5-T9","L1","T10-T11"]'::jsonb, 'T12', 'The least splanchnic nerve arises from the T12 sympathetic ganglion and terminates in the renal plexus. The greater (T5-T9) and lesser (T10-T11) nerves originate higher, and C8 or L1 are not splanchnic sources.'),
('ddf7b22a-9e76-5ce8-86f9-8a3681101ab3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: splanchnic nerves pierce:?', '["Central tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Vena caval opening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic hiatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophageal hiatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragm crura to reach celiac plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm crura to reach celiac plexus', 'The greater, lesser, and least splanchnic nerves pierce the crura of the diaphragm and end in the celiac and related abdominal plexuses. The vena caval, esophageal, and aortic hiatus transmit the IVC, esophagus, and aorta respectively, not the splanchnic nerves.'),
('6b53286f-250c-5250-a294-3c4d63bc9ffc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: phrenic nerves innervate:?', '["Abdominal muscles","Diaphragm (C3-C5)","Back muscles","Intercostals","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm (C3-C5)', 'The phrenic nerves, from spinal roots C3-C5, supply the entire motor innervation of the diaphragm. The intercostal, back, and abdominal muscles are supplied by other nerves, and the heart receives autonomic innervation only.'),
('ebd01ce6-c3c6-57e4-b65b-65b17b656eda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Right phrenic nerve:'' is:', '["Posterior to hilum resulting in decreased functional activity","Left side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind SVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passes lateral to SVC anterior to lung root along right side of heart"]'::jsonb, 'Passes lateral to SVC anterior to lung root along right side of heart', 'The right phrenic nerve descends lateral to the superior vena cava, passes anterior to the lung hilum, and runs along the right side of the heart to the diaphragm. It does not pass behind the SVC or posterior to the hilum.'),
('a3d5ecba-edf1-5ccb-b601-c9d82b7e53ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: left phrenic nerve:?', '["Posterior to aortic arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind heart accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior to hilum mediated by specific receptor activation","Right side without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passes anterior to aortic arch anterior to lung root along LV"]'::jsonb, 'Passes anterior to aortic arch anterior to lung root along LV', 'The left phrenic nerve passes anterior to the aortic arch and the lung hilum, then descends over the left atrium and left ventricle. It therefore lies anterior, not posterior, to the arch, heart, and hilum on the left side.'),
('f6c91f1f-fa5c-5737-b23e-4cc7b3dd67f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: pericardiacophrenic artery:?', '["Aorta","Intercostal","Bronchial","From internal thoracic artery","with phrenic nerve (supplies pericardium) Subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'From internal thoracic artery', 'The pericardiacophrenic artery is a branch of the internal thoracic artery that travels with the phrenic nerve to supply the pericardium, mediastinal pleura, and diaphragm. It does not arise from the aorta, bronchial, or intercostal arteries.'),
('de71f0df-31bd-55cf-ab3b-c55a2d4f2770'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: internal thoracic artery:?', '["From vertebral resulting in decreased functional activity","From aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From intercostal mediated by specific receptor activation","From axillary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From subclavian runs 1 cm lateral to sternum divides at 6th ICS"]'::jsonb, 'From subclavian runs 1 cm lateral to sternum divides at 6th ICS', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery, descends about 1 cm lateral to the sternal border, and divides at the sixth costal cartilage into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. It is not derived from the aorta, axillary, vertebral, or intercostal branches.'),
('0a4bc3d9-1be3-59bf-9a06-6015a03702b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: thymus location:?', '["Anterior mediastinum (superior and anterior)","Neck only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle mediastinum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mediastinum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior mediastinum (superior and anterior)', 'The thymus occupies the anterior part of the superior mediastinum, extending into the anterior mediastinum behind the manubrium and sternum. It is not confined to the neck and does not lie in the middle or posterior mediastinum.'),
('c4c5f0ce-3016-5cf2-8e77-ed007c64158a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: thymus function:?', '["T-cell maturation and selection","Blood filtration","Hormone secretion to blood","B cell production","Mucus secretion"]'::jsonb, 'T-cell maturation and selection', 'The thymus is the primary lymphoid organ where T lymphocytes mature and undergo positive and negative selection. It does not filter blood, and the liver and spleen are the chief sites of B cell production.'),
('e784767d-1f64-5c4f-86d6-edcd8c0c072d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Thymus involutes:'' is:', '["During pregnancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Never accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","After puberty (fatty replacement)","In old age without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At birth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'After puberty (fatty replacement)', 'The thymus reaches maximal size in childhood and undergoes progressive involution after puberty, with the lymphoid tissue replaced by fat. It is fully active at birth and does not involute earlier.'),
('53b45fae-8ea9-50bc-bb68-f802d40991a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: costomediastinal sinuses:?', '["Interlobar fissures resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior pleural recesses at junction of costal and mediastinal pleura","Mediastinal spaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior recesses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragmatic recesses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior pleural recesses at junction of costal and mediastinal pleura', 'The costomediastinal sinus is the narrow anterior pleural recess formed where the costal pleura meets the mediastinal pleura. Interlobar fissures and the costodiaphragmatic recesses are separate pleural features.'),
('82a40f88-de9c-5232-a56b-c5f81d88a85c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: suprapleural membrane (sibson''s):?', '["Covers cervical pleura","from C7 TP to 1st rib inner border","Endothoracic","Covering apex of lung","Clavipectoral Cervical fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Covers cervical pleura', 'The suprapleural membrane (Sibson''s fascia) is the dome-shaped fascia that covers and fixes the cervical pleura. It stretches from the C7 transverse process to the inner border of the first rib, firmly anchoring the pleural apex.'),
('3d7dd85d-7aac-5ebc-8436-996291b6d06c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: endothoracic fascia:?', '["Between pleura and pericardium resulting in decreased functional activity","Between pleura and lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thin connective tissue between parietal pleura and chest wall","Pleura itself without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Under skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin connective tissue between parietal pleura and chest wall', 'The endothoracic fascia is the thin layer of loose connective tissue interposed between the parietal pleura and the chest wall and diaphragm. It does not lie between the lung and pleura or under the skin.'),
('0a59e814-2ebd-5f57-a564-9d431bdc9aa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: brachiocephalic veins formed by:?', '["Internal jugular + subclavian veins","External jugular + subclavian","Vertebral + IJV","Left + right IJV","Left + right subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular + subclavian veins', 'Each brachiocephalic vein is formed behind the sternoclavicular joint by the union of the internal jugular and subclavian veins. The external jugular and vertebral veins are tributaries draining into these vessels, not the source of union.'),
('3319ca3d-a88d-595d-a6e0-3d05d162bab1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: left brachiocephalic vein:?', '["Same as right","Posterior to trachea","Right side","Longer","crosses midline behind manubrium Shorter"]'::jsonb, 'Longer', 'The left brachiocephalic vein is longer than the right because it runs obliquely across the midline behind the manubrium to join the right vein to form the SVC. The right vein is short and vertical.'),
('036052c7-7d32-5b90-bcfe-c285ffeafff2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Right brachiocephalic vein:'' is:', '["Crosses midline","Longer","Horizontal","Shorter","vertical descends behind right first costal cartilage Same length as left"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right brachiocephalic vein is short and runs almost vertically behind the right first costal cartilage to join the left vein. The left vein is the longer one that crosses the midline.'),
('9e9202c9-b988-59b9-8c95-675301abf120'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: svc compression:?', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngeal paralysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumothorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","May cause superior mediastinal syndrome (swelling, cyanosis)"]'::jsonb, 'May cause superior mediastinal syndrome (swelling, cyanosis)', 'Compression of the SVC by a mediastinal mass or enlarged nodes obstructs venous return and causes SVC (superior mediastinal) syndrome with facial and upper-limb swelling, cyanosis, and venous distension. It does not produce pulmonary edema, heart failure, laryngeal palsy, or pneumothorax.'),
('4395732d-274e-57f5-92ba-605ac64ea118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: tracheoesophageal groove:?', '["Retropharyngeal space","Paratracheal space","Between trachea and esophagus","contains recurrent laryngeal nerves","Carotid sheath Prevertebral space"]'::jsonb, 'Between trachea and esophagus', 'The tracheoesophageal groove is the gutter between the posterior trachea and the anterior esophagus; the left recurrent laryngeal nerve ascends within it. The paratracheal, retropharyngeal, and prevertebral spaces are separate regions.'),
('85975001-ea36-5db9-abe4-ba97033faf63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: azygos lobe:?', '["Supernumerary lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Fissure variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left lung mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal accessory lobe on right from azygos vein indentation","Middle lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal accessory lobe on right from azygos vein indentation', 'An azygos lobe is an anomalous accessory lobe of the right lung produced when the azygos vein indents the pleura during development. It occurs on the right, not the left, and splits the upper lobe rather than forming a separate fissure between named lobes.'),
('37ae4223-f8e1-50a8-87a7-d1378d4d8a62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: pericardial cyst:?', '["Lung cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleural effusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital cyst in anterior/cardiophrenic angle","Aortic aneurysm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mediastinal tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital cyst in anterior/cardiophrenic angle', 'A pericardial cyst is a benign congenital fluid-filled lesion typically located at the right cardiophrenic angle of the anterior mediastinum. It is not a lung cyst, effusion, aneurysm, or neoplastic mass.'),
('73d1977c-deb6-53b7-9277-421ddf533f80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: thoracic outlet syndrome:?', '["Diaphragm paralysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Aortic aneurysm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung compression mediated by specific receptor activation","C8/T1 compression by cervical rib or scalene muscles","Heart ischemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C8/T1 compression by cervical rib or scalene muscles', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome results from compression of the C8-T1 nerve roots and subclavian vessels by a cervical rib, scalene muscle, or clavicle. It does not cause diaphragm paralysis, lung compression, aortic aneurysm, or myocardial ischemia.'),
('dbefdfcb-212d-530c-9631-bf00e008bd14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''First rib important for:'' is:', '["Trachea resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic outlet anatomy (subclavian vessels and brachial plexus pass over it)","Lung apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragm attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternoclavicular joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic outlet anatomy (subclavian vessels and brachial plexus pass over it)', 'The first rib forms the superior boundary of the thoracic outlet, and the subclavian artery and vein and the brachial plexus cross over it to reach the upper limb. The trachea, diaphragm, and lung apex have no primary relation to it.'),
('658d2a50-4a78-5247-b74a-987b6cd4e74a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: subclavian artery on the right originates from:?', '["Carotid","Subclavian vein","Vertebral","Brachiocephalic trunk","Aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk', 'The right subclavian artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk; the left subclavian artery comes directly from the aortic arch. It neither branches from the carotid nor from the aortic arch on the right side.'),
('d1be21e8-90bd-50ba-8314-def8ba2f55ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In the study of Mediastinum, IVC drains into:', '["Right atrium (lower part)","LV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","LA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (lower part)', 'The inferior vena cava pierces the diaphragm at the level of T8 and opens into the lower part of the right atrium, the site of a small, usually functionless valve remnant.'),
('f1cc2651-d0b2-5e0d-b6cd-f0be35608d66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: cervical rib:?', '["Accessory rib from C7","can cause thoracic outlet compression","From C6","From sternum","From T1 From clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory rib from C7', 'A cervical rib is an accessory (supernumerary) rib arising from the anterior tubercle of the C7 vertebra. It can compress the lower brachial plexus and subclavian vessels and trigger thoracic outlet symptoms.'),
('539263a1-c02e-54bf-bc9e-3a3b1bad7c20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: anterior scalene insertion:?', '["Manubrium","First rib (scalene tubercle)","Sternocostal junction","Clavicle","Second rib"]'::jsonb, 'First rib (scalene tubercle)', 'The anterior scalene inserts into the scalene tubercle on the inner border of the first rib. The middle scalene attaches behind the subclavian groove, and the clavicle, second rib, manubrium, and sternocostal junction are not its insertion.'),
('3338c48e-eaa6-5637-84d5-aa819413630a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: middle scalene insertion:?', '["Second rib resulting in decreased functional activity","First rib (posterior to subclavian artery)","1st and 2nd ribs mediated by specific receptor activation","Clavicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First rib (posterior to subclavian artery)', 'The middle scalene inserts on the posterior part of the first rib behind the groove for the subclavian artery. The anterior scalene reaches the scalene tubercle of the first rib, while the second rib, clavicle, and sternum receive different muscles.'),
('367a4754-3f04-53c8-9009-d768b8e60af4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Interscalene groove:'' is:', '["Behind sternocleidomastoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Below clavicle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to scalenus anterior mediated by specific receptor activation","Between posterior + middle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between anterior and middle scalene: subclavian artery + brachial plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Between anterior and middle scalene: subclavian artery + brachial plexus', 'The interscalene groove lies between the anterior and middle scalene muscles and transmits the subclavian artery and the roots of the brachial plexus. The subclavian vein passes anterior to the anterior scalene and does not run in this groove.'),
('77a2462b-54f6-5cd0-9fe0-53514667d2b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: subclavian vein lies:?', '["Behind anterior scalene resulting in decreased functional activity","In interscalene groove accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind subclavian artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior to anterior scalene (NOT in interscalene groove)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to anterior scalene (NOT in interscalene groove)', 'The subclavian vein crosses the first rib anterior to the anterior scalene muscle, medial to the artery, and therefore does not occupy the interscalene groove. The subclavian artery and brachial plexus are the contents of that groove behind the muscle.'),
('ed958b13-22e2-5147-a776-9c9ac1e4458e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: stellate ganglion at:?', '["C7 level (fusion of inferior cervical + T1 sympathetic ganglia)","T6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C2 mediated by specific receptor activation","T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C7 level (fusion of inferior cervical + T1 sympathetic ganglia)', 'The stellate (cervicothoracic) ganglion is formed by fusion of the inferior cervical and first thoracic sympathetic ganglia and lies near the C7 level behind the subclavian artery. The C2, C3, T4, and T6 levels correspond to other sympathetic ganglia.'),
('faa8e7fb-15ff-5f2b-b0bb-c37bd1b46171'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: stellate ganglion block may cause:?', '["Mydriasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Lid lag accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exophthalmos mediated by specific receptor activation","Horner''s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos)","Hyperhidrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Horner''s syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos)', 'Stellate ganglion block interrupts cervical sympathetic outflow, producing Horner''s syndrome with ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, and apparent enophthalmos. Mydriasis, exophthalmos, lid lag, and hyperhidrosis reflect sympathetic overactivity rather than a block.'),
('f046ad00-a76c-5572-9090-2aa0f1f39c2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: vertebral artery origin:?', '["Aortic arch","Costocervical trunk","Subclavian artery (1st part)","Brachiocephalic","Internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (1st part)', 'The vertebral artery is the first branch of the first part of the subclavian artery and ascends through the transverse foramina of C6-C1. It is not a direct branch of the aortic arch, brachiocephalic trunk, or internal thoracic artery, nor of the costocervical trunk.'),
('be488464-7475-535c-8b79-a8c5ebe57852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: vertebral vein:?', '["Subclavian resulting in decreased functional activity","IJV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Azygos without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accompanies vertebral artery drains into brachiocephalic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Accompanies vertebral artery drains into brachiocephalic vein', 'The vertebral vein accompanies the vertebral artery through the transverse foramina and drains into the brachiocephalic vein at the root of the neck. It does not empty directly into the subclavian, internal jugular, azygos, or SVC.'),
('d70306dc-1f2f-571c-b329-7ea3708a1762'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Thoracic duct on left side at T4:'' is:', '["Crosses anterior to trachea","Stays right","Crosses anterior to esophagus","Crosses behind esophagus","arches left to join left venous angle Crosses behind aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses behind esophagus', 'At about the T4-T5 level the thoracic duct crosses from the right to the left side of the mediastinum behind the esophagus, then ascends to empty at the junction of the left internal jugular and subclavian veins. It does not cross anterior to the trachea or esophagus.'),
('58b19a49-736f-50b4-984e-417fa234c840'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: recurrent laryngeal nodes:?', '["Paratracheal nodes near RLN","important for lung cancer drainage","Supraclavicular","Anterior mediastinal","Axillary Posterior mediastinal"]'::jsonb, 'Paratracheal nodes near RLN', 'The recurrent laryngeal (paratracheal) lymph nodes lie in the grooves beside the trachea near the recurrent laryngeal nerves and receive lymphatic drainage from the trachea and esophagus. They are distinct from supraclavicular, anterior mediastinal, axillary, and posterior mediastinal groups.'),
('b034cc3c-c959-5a0f-9686-f4aa8afee13f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: anterior mediastinal nodes:?', '["Paratracheal resulting in decreased functional activity","Intercostal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Along internal thoracic vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Along internal thoracic vessels', 'The anterior mediastinal (prevascular) nodes lie along the internal thoracic vessels behind the sternum and drain the anterior chest wall, thymus, and anterior heart. Paratracheal, intercostal, pericardial, and aortic arch nodes form separate groups.'),
('9f53fb2f-df42-5798-af4a-a28afb0e6349'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: posterior mediastinal nodes:?', '["Supraclavicular","Paraesophageal","intercostal","diaphragmatic","Axillary Paratracheal Anterior mediastinal"]'::jsonb, 'Paraesophageal', 'Posterior mediastinal nodes include the paraesophageal nodes lying beside the esophagus and juxtavertebral nodes. Supraclavicular, axillary, anterior mediastinal, and intercostal groups are located elsewhere in the thorax.'),
('86c7f614-86e6-5d07-a55a-47f07f683d6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: mediastinal masses anterior compartment:?', '["Thymoma","teratoma","thyroid lymphoma (4 Ts)","Neurogenic tumor Lymphoma posterior","Bronchogenic cyst Aortic aneurysm"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma', 'The anterior mediastinal (prevascular) compartment classically hosts the ''4 Ts'' of thymoma, teratoma, thyroid, and lymphoma, with thymoma the most frequent. Neurogenic tumors and bronchogenic cysts arise in the posterior and middle compartments respectively.'),
('e5496d28-b7d9-55d8-afce-8c9c30fc9f64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: mediastinal masses middle compartment:?', '["Lymphadenopathy","bronchogenic cyst","pericardial cyst","Teratoma","Neurogenic tumor Thymoma Thyroid extension"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphadenopathy', 'The middle mediastinum contains the heart, pericardium, and great vessels, so its characteristic masses are lymphadenopathy and bronchogenic or pericardial cysts. Thymoma and teratoma are anterior masses, while neurogenic tumors occupy the posterior compartment.'),
('9819311b-c409-5fc9-81c8-fb127cbc46b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Mediastinal masses posterior compartment:'' is:', '["Lymphoma","Pericardial cyst","Neurogenic tumor (most common)","aortic aneurysm","esophageal tumor Thymoma Teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic tumor (most common)', 'The posterior mediastinum most commonly contains neurogenic tumors arising from sympathetic ganglia or peripheral nerves. Thymoma and teratoma are anterior masses, while pericardial cyst and aortic aneurysm belong to the middle compartment.'),
('6145a1b1-5d13-5167-a74a-b0715a9c5aee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: most common mediastinal tumor overall:?', '["Pericardial cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Teratoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurogenic tumor (posterior)","Thymoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neurogenic tumor (posterior)', 'Neurogenic tumors, which arise in the posterior mediastinum, are the most common mediastinal tumors overall in adults. Thymoma leads the anterior compartment, while lymphoma, teratoma, and pericardial cyst are less frequent overall.'),
('d6ed4961-109c-5a39-be5d-4108c0cdbe8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: most common anterior mediastinal tumor:?', '["Teratoma","Thyroid","Thymoma","Lymphoma","Pericardial cyst"]'::jsonb, 'Thymoma', 'Thymoma is the most common primary tumor of the anterior mediastinum in adults. Teratoma, thyroid goiter, and lymphoma are other anterior compartment masses but occur less often than thymoma.'),
('904f0d17-196a-5998-bfd7-07b1c1b0a94d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: ligamentum arteriosum remnant of:?', '["Ductus arteriosus","Umbilical vein","Foramen ovale","Ductus venosus","Umbilical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Ductus arteriosus', 'The ligamentum arteriosum is the fibrous remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus, connecting the pulmonary trunk to the undersurface of the aortic arch. The ductus venosus, foramen ovale, and umbilical vessels leave the ligamentum venosum, fossa ovalis, and medial umbilical ligaments instead.'),
('7561b2d7-1fed-541a-b0f9-84672befdd64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: aortic arch lies at:?', '["T4/T5 level (second right costal cartilage)","T2/T3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10 mediated by specific receptor activation","T8 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T6 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 level (second right costal cartilage)', 'The aortic arch begins and ends at the T4-T5 level, behind the manubrium opposite the second right costal cartilage and the sternal angle. T2/T3, T6, T8, and T10 are incorrect vertebral levels.'),
('b3469abd-1c98-5d17-932f-7abc21ec002e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: aortic arch relations anterior:?', '["Thoracic duct","Esophagus","Manubrium","thymus","left brachiocephalic vein Trachea Vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium', 'The manubrium sterni lies anterior to the aortic arch, along with the thymus and the left brachiocephalic vein. The esophagus, trachea, thoracic duct, and left recurrent laryngeal nerve are posterior relations of the arch.'),
('cfda6d5e-dc28-50c6-bd91-42bc34cdda90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Aortic arch relations posterior:'' is:', '["Lung","Thymus","Sternum Pericardium","Trachea esophagus","thoracic duct left RLN"]'::jsonb, 'Trachea esophagus', 'Posteriorly, the aortic arch is related to the trachea and esophagus, with the thoracic duct and left recurrent laryngeal nerve also passing behind it. The sternum, thymus, and pericardium lie anterior to the arch.'),
('699206ae-9cb7-5241-a341-b551ba5a6020'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: aortic isthmus:?', '["At diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","At T8 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between brachiocephalic and left carotid mediated by specific receptor activation","Narrowing between left subclavian and ligamentum arteriosum","At T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Narrowing between left subclavian and ligamentum arteriosum', 'The aortic isthmus is a mild narrowing of the aorta just distal to the left subclavian artery at the site of the ligamentum arteriosum. Classic coarctation of the aorta develops at this point, not at the diaphragm, T8, or T4.'),
('c4feb55c-8f99-589e-9247-5bcb84e93283'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: aortic coarctation site:?', '["Abdominal aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","At aortic isthmus (near ductus arteriosus)","Ascending aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Descending aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic root leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At aortic isthmus (near ductus arteriosus)', 'Coarctation of the aorta most often occurs at the aortic isthmus, just distal to the left subclavian artery and adjacent to the ductus arteriosus. The ascending aorta, aortic root, and abdominal aorta are not the typical sites.'),
('775adb6c-f434-5e5a-a065-8a4e382fc816'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding the mediastinum, what are the clinical signs of aortic coarctation?', '["Aneurysm resulting in decreased functional activity","Cyanosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower limb edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulse deficit right arm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper limb hypertension weak femoral pulses rib notching"]'::jsonb, 'Upper limb hypertension weak femoral pulses rib notching', 'Coarctation of the aorta causes upper limb hypertension, weak or delayed femoral pulses, and notching of the lower ribs from enlarged collateral intercostal arteries. Cyanosis and aneurysms are not characteristic of this lesion.'),
('1c11399c-d953-5a0b-bb6a-53a3685ea560'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For the mediastinum, which option is most appropriate regarding patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)?', '["Heart septal defect resulting in decreased functional activity","clinical significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Valvular defect mediated by specific receptor activation","Myocardial defect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Persistent communication between pulmonary trunk and aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Persistent communication between pulmonary trunk and aortic arch', 'PDA is a persistent fetal channel connecting the pulmonary trunk to the aortic arch, which normally closes after birth. It is not a septal, valvular, or myocardial defect.'),
('5e3bfce7-2e0b-5781-a166-4be353bb992c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in the mediastinum when asked about aortic arch variants?', '["Double right resulting in decreased functional activity","Bovine arch (brachiocephalic and left carotid common trunk) 15-20%","arch mediated by specific receptor activation","Left arch 10% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right arch 50% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bovine arch (brachiocephalic and left carotid common trunk) 15-20%', 'The bovine arch, in which the left common carotid artery shares a common trunk with the brachiocephalic artery, occurs in about 15-20% of individuals and is the commonest aortic arch variant. The other options give incorrect patterns or frequencies.'),
('e86ec95c-d716-5a1e-8468-4369abe27d36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management, Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('7e3d956b-e259-53cf-a156-6f8f2fe7d362'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which of the following is correct: the mediastinum is:', '["The space behind the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","The pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space below the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in front of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs"]'::jsonb, 'The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs', 'The mediastinum is the central partition of the thoracic cavity lying between the two pleural sacs. The pleural cavity, subdiaphragmatic space, and retrocardiac space are different compartments.'),
('92da3c84-1cfe-5cb0-8f10-425d4eae6b71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which statement best fits the following fact: the superior mediastinum contains:', '["Diaphragm","Thymus","great vessels","trachea","esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus', 'The superior mediastinum contains the thymus, great vessels, trachea, and esophagus, and the thymus is one of its characteristic contents. The diaphragm lies at the inferior thoracic aperture rather than in the superior mediastinum.'),
('f9f6bb9d-09e6-551f-a836-7b9ffb39ed75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'What is the correct answer regarding the anterior mediastinum contents?', '["Lungs","Trachea and bronchi","Thymus (in children)","fat","lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus (in children)', 'The anterior mediastinum contains the thymus (or its remnant in adults), fat, and lymph nodes. The lungs lie within the pleural cavities, not in the anterior mediastinum.'),
('8734d4da-f7e9-53be-bc5d-03afb9cfedf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which option is most appropriate for the contents of the middle mediastinum?', '["Heart","pericardium","roots of great vessels","phrenic nerves","Only the thymus"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The middle mediastinum contains the heart with its pericardium, the roots of the great vessels, and the phrenic nerves. The thymus lies in the superior and anterior mediastinum, not the middle.'),
('e75e1770-2c74-5536-90e3-1718f22bcb16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct when asked about the contents of the posterior mediastinum?', '["Phrenic nerves","Lungs","Heart and pericardium","Thymus","Esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus', 'The posterior mediastinum contains the esophagus, descending thoracic aorta, azygos veins, thoracic duct, and vagus nerves. The phrenic nerves and heart lie in the middle mediastinum.'),
('1f324f27-6cdd-5c8e-b719-1ad844472aeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases: Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('ee853099-bc59-51d8-bb5b-edbf7133fa51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which of the following is correct: the superior vena cava (SVC) is formed by:', '["The coronary sinus","The internal and external jugular veins","The azygos and hemiazygos veins","Left and right subclavian veins","Right and left brachiocephalic veins"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left brachiocephalic veins', 'The SVC is formed behind the right first costal cartilage by the union of the right and left brachiocephalic veins. The azygos vein drains into the SVC but is not a formative tributary, and each jugular vein joins the subclavian to form a brachiocephalic vein.'),
('239eeaf2-133f-5316-8e03-36d01402ddbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('388aa72a-1b08-5cca-a417-67572ab7c5db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('a8ead002-d94f-5869-8a21-78c262bdaf59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('10697b6b-7b86-51f5-8c5f-be6b4e4b6fc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Mediastinum: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('17d99d4b-f327-579d-9d26-67ac0e7f26c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In the study of Mediastinum, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('0ddc8fe5-de59-50b1-863f-375b6567fab3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which of the following is correct: the sympathetic trunk in the thorax:', '["Is motor to the diaphragm","Is part of the parasympathetic system","Supplies the heart only","Is absent in the thorax","Contains 11-12 thoracic ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Contains 11-12 thoracic ganglia', 'The thoracic sympathetic trunk consists of 11-12 ganglia that provide sympathetic outflow to thoracic and abdominal structures. It is sympathetic (not parasympathetic) and distinct from the phrenic nerve, which is motor to the diaphragm.'),
('5c446542-4995-5b62-8225-7473ff4eb305'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Mediastinum: Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('06eaecbd-e6fe-5c7a-8ad3-769814c1d897'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('d09bbef2-71a1-5db8-b79b-d4d4e7820419'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('e522411e-7111-566f-8058-f4f21c2106ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In medical practice, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('0f5d8247-654d-596a-acf0-75d753a40923'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Mediastinum: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('5c0eb7a3-6f18-5f80-b7a9-f58ac703a2e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Mediastinum: Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('91b88200-3e7a-5e1c-8975-e246fc25ee97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('56ef638f-c068-53e3-967e-43e0cbcce975'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Mediastinum: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('670b66af-5607-526f-be80-6a6efd09dabc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In the study of Mediastinum, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('17f6fc97-ed89-53fc-8d0f-9bfdadec155f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Mediastinum: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('d27ff8e3-4ecc-5c66-acaf-1946db0de4c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Mediastinum: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('b599c69f-38c6-519d-9641-ab96004c4ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('45bb5290-142d-5dc5-8cf4-dfbb94f5931e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Mediastinum: Phase II conjugation reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Oxidation","Reduction","Hydrolysis","Deamination"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Phase II: glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, methylation, glutathione conjugation.'),
('1d3cd9b0-ee2d-5621-a669-abfbfadce121'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In the study of Mediastinum, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('2dc6580c-f050-5a6e-a117-f6b6b6dcb919'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Mediastinum: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('98861321-15e6-5c26-b748-82e089e705b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Mediastinum: Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('6f9906a8-9e2a-5bb9-bbbf-76cb95b64900'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('66b3da58-93f3-5299-b6ac-c93e14baa9c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Mediastinum: Displacing warfarin from albumin causes:', '["Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk","Decreased effect","No INR change","Increased binding","Decreased absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased free warfarin/bleeding risk', 'Displacement increases unbound warfarin, transiently increasing effect.'),
('5ab527ff-6bf4-5d00-9689-3121ed5d6e94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the mediastinum: which structure is NOT located in the superior mediastinum?', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart is located in the middle mediastinum rather than the superior mediastinum. The thymus, aortic arch, trachea, and brachiocephalic veins are all superior mediastinal structures.'),
('abbb33b7-ccdf-5b65-86ac-959ac3499c00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Identify the correct response for the mediastinum: which structure is NOT located in the superior mediastinum?', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart belongs to the middle mediastinum, not the superior mediastinum. The superior mediastinum holds the thymus, aortic arch, trachea, esophagus, and brachiocephalic veins.'),
('f3115d20-cbd2-5b4b-a798-6bb220b6391b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which structure is NOT located in the superior mediastinum? Select the best answer.', '["Thymus","Arch of aorta","Trachea","Brachiocephalic veins","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'The heart is the only listed structure confined to the middle mediastinum. The thymus, aortic arch, trachea, and brachiocephalic veins are contents of the superior mediastinum.'),
('dfb3cc2c-c2c9-5b39-9c31-44d345c4a500'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('7150e2d3-d5da-5b7e-8822-a2116274de32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Mediastinum: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('5fd7e022-c064-5f54-8731-a98536f5cc6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which option is most accurate: the mediastinum is?', '["The space behind the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","The pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space below the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in front of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs"]'::jsonb, 'The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs', 'The mediastinum is best defined as the central thoracic compartment between the two pleural sacs. The retrocardiac space, pleural cavities, and subdiaphragmatic region are separate anatomical areas.'),
('e2f26c8e-a0c8-5d01-aef3-85eeae2d9ef9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the mediastinum: the mediastinum is?', '["The space behind the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","The pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space below the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in front of the trachea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs"]'::jsonb, 'The central compartment of the thoracic cavity between the two pleural sacs', 'The mediastinum is defined as the central thoracic compartment lying between the two pleural sacs. It is not the pleural cavity, which lies laterally, nor the subdiaphragmatic space.'),
('1aa5bf26-9cdc-5215-86ef-97888f8e98a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The boundaries of the mediastinum are:', '["Sternum anterior","vertebrae posterior","superior thoracic aperture above","diaphragm below","Clavicles above Xiphoid below Ribs laterally Pericardium anterior"]'::jsonb, 'Sternum anterior', 'The anterior boundary of the mediastinum is formed by the sternum (with the vertebral bodies behind, the superior thoracic aperture above, and the diaphragm below). Together these four boundaries enclose the mediastinum.'),
('8670f3fd-bd80-5c11-bbb8-f6f28af532a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('dee87a43-bd50-5914-9421-c1814bb52232'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Mediastinum: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('168b2fbb-8df1-585d-9b32-03f8553b581d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The inferior mediastinum is divided into:', '["Anterior","middle","posterior compartments","Left + right","Superior + inferior subdivisions Upper + lower"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior', 'The inferior mediastinum is partitioned into anterior, middle, and posterior compartments. The anterior compartment lies in front of the pericardium and contains the thymic remnant, internal thoracic vessels, lymph nodes, and fat.'),
('a9185d2d-4e8a-511c-a2b1-c7550bbb0f0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Mediastinum: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('2f9e5bc2-6326-5887-8ed9-4a0c9008d114'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Mediastinum: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('0b4169c3-5599-5872-b80a-dbbbde1d17a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During therapeutic management of Mediastinum, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('384118a3-1619-5d5c-ad09-587179afc7be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) lies at:', '["T6/T7 resulting in decreased functional activity","T4/T5 intervertebral disc","C7/T1 mediated by specific receptor activation","T2/T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T4/T5 intervertebral disc', 'The sternal angle marks the level of the T4/T5 intervertebral disc, where the trachea bifurcates, the aortic arch begins and ends, and the second costal cartilage articulates. It is the reference plane dividing superior and inferior mediastinum.'),
('6d68da3d-154a-5e2b-a98d-e64e6bfc4182'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: at sternal angle:?', '["Diaphragm dome","Thymus lies","Arch of aorta begins/ends trachea bifurcates","azygos vein enters SVC thoracic duct crosses","Heart apex beat Pulmonary hilum"]'::jsonb, 'Arch of aorta begins/ends trachea bifurcates', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) lies at the T4/T5 disc level, where the aortic arch begins and ends, the trachea bifurcates, the azygos vein enters the SVC, and the thoracic duct crosses. The other listed structures are not at this level; the cardiac apex beat is in the 5th intercostal space and the diaphragm dome is lower.'),
('203c3b0e-4110-5e50-ab62-7d5cf3b0ce1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Azygos vein origin:'' is:', '["Left ascending lumbar","Right ascending lumbar + subcostal veins","Renal vein","Inferior vena cava","Posterior intercostal"]'::jsonb, 'Right ascending lumbar + subcostal veins', 'The azygos vein arises in the abdomen from the right ascending lumbar vein, which joins the right subcostal vein, then enters the thorax through the aortic hiatus. The left ascending lumbar vein and other structures are not part of its origin.'),
('234058dc-1a32-5b3b-9e2f-008e71c4db01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: azygos vein arch:?', '["Through diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","To coronary sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Over right hilum at T4/T5 to enter SVC","Behind sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To left SVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Over right hilum at T4/T5 to enter SVC', 'The azygos vein arches over the right lung hilum at the T4/T5 level and drains into the superior vena cava. It does not end in the coronary sinus, a left SVC, or behind the sternum.'),
('57d8e9b7-72a1-57ed-8e28-16045dd84183'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: accessory hemiazygos:?', '["Drains left 4th-8th posterior intercostals","Right 4th-8th accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right upper intercostals mediated by specific receptor activation","Left lower intercostals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both sides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drains left 4th-8th posterior intercostals', 'The accessory hemiazygos vein drains the left 4th to 8th posterior intercostal veins and descends on the left side of the vertebral column. The hemiazygos itself drains the left 9th to 11th intercostals, and the right-sided veins drain into the azygos.'),
('00803c2b-e3d3-51cd-9e3a-11d9890ffd54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: hemiazygos vein:?', '["Drains directly to SVC","Left 4th-8th","Drains left 9th-11th posterior intercostals","crosses to azygos at T8/T9","Right intercostals Drains to left brachiocephalic"]'::jsonb, 'Drains left 9th-11th posterior intercostals', 'The hemiazygos vein drains the left 9th to 11th posterior intercostal veins and usually crosses the midline at the T8/T9 level to join the azygos vein. It does not drain directly into the superior vena cava.'),
('308efb9d-20b0-5b0b-a30d-2f006c3b1630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: azygos system drains:?', '["Lungs","Heart","Posterior intercostals esophagus","pericardium mediastinum","Anterior intercostals Diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior intercostals esophagus', 'The azygos system drains the posterior intercostal veins and also receives esophageal, mediastinal and pericardial tributaries. The lungs and heart drain through pulmonary and cardiac veins, not the azygos system.'),
('829fa1d7-f810-549b-bb76-4f428b484542'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: thoracic duct origin:?', '["Head resulting in decreased functional activity","Thorax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cisterna chyli at L1/L2 (abdomen)","Pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna chyli at L1/L2 (abdomen)', 'The thoracic duct begins in the abdomen as the cisterna chyli at the L1/L2 level, anterior to the first two lumbar vertebrae, then ascends through the aortic hiatus into the thorax. It does not originate in the neck, pelvis or thorax itself.'),
('7d414ab7-6642-5684-a173-5bf1b42562bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Thoracic duct course in thorax:'' is:', '["Behind sternum","Middle mediastinum","In posterior mediastinum between azygos and aorta","then to left at T4/T5","Right side throughout Anterior mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'In posterior mediastinum between azygos and aorta', 'In the thorax the thoracic duct runs in the posterior mediastinum between the azygos vein and the descending aorta, then crosses to the left at about the T4/T5 level. It does not lie in the middle or anterior mediastinum.'),
('d4ae8444-0396-5c72-9ab9-2adc7db82e3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: right lymphatic duct:?', '["Drains right upper quadrant into right venous angle","Lower body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left upper quadrant mediated by specific receptor activation","Entire body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right lower quadrant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drains right upper quadrant into right venous angle', 'The right lymphatic duct drains the right upper quadrant of the body (right side of head and neck, right upper limb, and right thorax) into the right venous angle. The lower body and left upper quadrant drain via the thoracic duct.'),
('034db56e-b382-5864-a036-5530777cc146'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: thoracic duct tributaries:?', '["Only bronchial","Only mediastinal Only esophageal","Intercostal","mediastinal esophageal","bronchomediastinal trunks Only intercostal"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal', 'The thoracic duct receives lymphatic tributaries including the intercostal trunks and the descending thoracic lymph trunk. Its drainage is not limited to bronchial, mediastinal or esophageal channels alone.'),
('79bd7807-2e4c-55a4-97c8-98ce27c66c6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: descending thoracic aorta:?', '["From heart to T12","From T4/T5 to T12 (through aortic hiatus)","From T2 to T8","From T4 to diaphragm at T8","From T6 to T10"]'::jsonb, 'From T4/T5 to T12 (through aortic hiatus)', 'The descending thoracic aorta begins at the T4/T5 sternal angle, descends in the posterior mediastinum, and ends at the aortic hiatus at T12. It does not arise directly from the heart or end at the T8 level.'),
('cb9c28f8-2ebf-5214-a864-0be63f93bb5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: aortic hiatus at:?', '["T8 resulting in decreased functional activity","T4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 (between crura of diaphragm)","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T12 (between crura of diaphragm)', 'The aortic hiatus lies at the T12 level between the crura of the diaphragm and transmits the aorta and thoracic duct. The esophageal hiatus is at T10 and the vena caval foramen at T8.'),
('b95f332b-525e-592a-a26a-97c0bc642d5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: esophageal hiatus at:?', '["T12 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C7 mediated by specific receptor activation","T8 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T10 (right crus of diaphragm)"]'::jsonb, 'T10 (right crus of diaphragm)', 'The esophageal hiatus is at the T10 level and passes through the right crus of the diaphragm, transmitting the esophagus and the vagal trunks. The aortic hiatus is at T12 and the vena caval foramen at T8.'),
('347a9310-e037-5b8c-a6b7-3075d8712a8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Vena caval foramen at:'' is:', '["L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","T10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T8 (in central tendon)","T12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (in central tendon)', 'The vena caval foramen is at the T8 level in the central tendon of the diaphragm and transmits the inferior vena cava with the right phrenic nerve. The esophageal hiatus is at T10 and the aortic hiatus at T12.'),
('b5c20f31-d2de-547d-9478-293c66b9370d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: esophagus course:?', '["Anterior to heart","Right of midline","Posterior to trachea (superior)","behind LA (middle)","left of midline (inferior) Anterior to trachea Behind sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to trachea (superior)', 'In the superior mediastinum the esophagus lies posterior to the trachea. It then passes behind the left atrium and deviates left of the midline inferiorly, but the key superior relation is posterior to the trachea.'),
('54f55b8a-5782-510d-9eae-c6c4f67f14f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: esophageal constrictions:?', '["T2 resulting in decreased functional activity","T6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10 mediated by specific receptor activation","C6 (start) T4/T5 (aortic arch/left bronchus)","T10 (hiatus) C5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C6 (start) T4/T5 (aortic arch/left bronchus)', 'The esophagus has three constrictions: at its pharyngeal origin (cricopharyngeus, C6), at the indentation of the aortic arch and left main bronchus (T4/T5), and at the diaphragmatic hiatus (T10). A simple T2 constriction does not exist, so the option naming the C6 start and the T4/T5 aortic/bronchial narrowing is the accurate one.'),
('9de83a22-94a4-5e33-88eb-841659bb41a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: left recurrent laryngeal nerve:?', '["Right side","Posterior to esophagus","Thymus","Loops under aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum)","ascends in tracheoesophageal groove Anterior to trachea"]'::jsonb, 'Loops under aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum)', 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve branches from the left vagus in the thorax and loops under the aortic arch behind the ligamentum arteriosum before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. It is vulnerable to damage during surgery on the aortic arch.'),
('37e49eaf-aa23-5b63-85c1-42e8393de5e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: right recurrent laryngeal nerve:?', '["Loops under right subclavian artery","SVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ligamentum arteriosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loops under right subclavian artery', 'The right recurrent laryngeal nerve leaves the right vagus and hooks under the right subclavian artery before ascending in the tracheoesophageal groove. The left recurrent nerve instead loops under the aortic arch, so the subclavian artery is the key landmark on the right.'),
('57b3aa79-b859-5b10-87d2-c5f60fe991d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: esophageal plexus:?', '["Vagus nerves surround esophagus forming plexus","Sympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercostal mediated by specific receptor activation","Phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splanchnic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerves surround esophagus forming plexus', 'In the mediastinum the right and left vagus nerves split around the esophagus and rejoin to form the esophageal plexus. Sympathetic, phrenic, intercostal and splanchnic nerves do not form this plexus.'),
('24705e4b-8412-5ea9-9bea-d2edf20cf4dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Anterior vagal trunk:'' is:', '["Left phrenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Both accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left vagus (anterior surface of esophagus)","Right phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left vagus (anterior surface of esophagus)', 'The left vagus nerve comes to lie on the anterior surface of the lower esophagus and forms the anterior vagal trunk, which passes through the esophageal hiatus onto the anterior stomach. The right vagus lies posteriorly.'),
('3370f62b-4c15-5916-be2b-3e80fa449a0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: posterior vagal trunk:?', '["Both resulting in decreased functional activity","Left vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right vagus (posterior surface of esophagus)","Left phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right vagus (posterior surface of esophagus)', 'The right vagus lies on the posterior surface of the lower esophagus and forms the posterior vagal trunk, which passes behind the stomach. The left vagus forms the anterior trunk, not the posterior one.'),
('475bfd43-e6dd-5b82-bec8-d877210e3b31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: greater splanchnic nerve origin:?', '["L1-L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10-T11 mediated by specific receptor activation","T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia","C8-T4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T5-T9 sympathetic ganglia', 'The greater splanchnic nerve arises from the T5 to T9 sympathetic ganglia, pierces the diaphragm crura, and synapses in the celiac ganglion. The lesser splanchnic (T10-T11) and least splanchnic (T12) nerves arise lower.'),
('af184035-e4d1-572e-9c30-94ae384741f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: lesser splanchnic nerve origin:?', '["L1","C8","T12","T5-T9","T10-T11"]'::jsonb, 'T10-T11', 'The lesser splanchnic nerve originates from the T10 and T11 sympathetic ganglia and passes to the aorticorenal ganglion. The greater splanchnic arises from T5-T9 and the least splanchnic from T12.'),
('d891ec6b-b717-5bd7-b9b0-e41cb79ac926'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: least splanchnic nerve origin:?', '["T12","C8","T5-T9","L1","T10-T11"]'::jsonb, 'T12', 'The least (smallest) splanchnic nerve arises from the T12 sympathetic ganglion and reaches the renal plexus. The greater splanchnic arises from T5-T9 and the lesser from T10-T11, so T12 is the correct origin.'),
('7fc9ddd7-2cab-52f7-9dc1-684e0e9aeb5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: splanchnic nerves pierce:?', '["Central tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","Vena caval opening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic hiatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophageal hiatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragm crura to reach celiac plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm crura to reach celiac plexus', 'The greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves pierce the crura of the diaphragm to synapse in the celiac, aorticorenal and renal plexuses respectively. They do not pass through the central tendon, vena caval, esophageal or aortic hiatus openings.'),
('351537d5-d5b2-5584-a310-374676b419db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Phrenic nerves innervate:'' is:', '["Abdominal muscles","Diaphragm (C3-C5)","Back muscles","Intercostals","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm (C3-C5)', 'The phrenic nerves (C3-C5) provide the sole motor supply to the diaphragm, with C4 contributing the main root. The intercostal, back and abdominal muscles are supplied by other segmental nerves.'),
('4ba6276d-a148-5c87-a1b8-ab5bbdaab21f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: right phrenic nerve:?', '["Posterior to hilum resulting in decreased functional activity","Left side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind SVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passes lateral to SVC anterior to lung root along right side of heart"]'::jsonb, 'Passes lateral to SVC anterior to lung root along right side of heart', 'The right phrenic nerve descends in the mediastinum lateral to the superior vena cava and anterior to the right lung root, then passes along the right side of the heart to the diaphragm. It does not lie behind the hilum or SVC.'),
('86cf2302-8cc1-5991-82e9-c58d85e999f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: left phrenic nerve:?', '["Posterior to aortic arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind heart accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior to hilum mediated by specific receptor activation","Right side without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passes anterior to aortic arch anterior to lung root along LV"]'::jsonb, 'Passes anterior to aortic arch anterior to lung root along LV', 'The left phrenic nerve descends anterior to the aortic arch and the left lung root, then runs over the pericardium covering the left ventricle to reach the diaphragm. It does not pass posterior to the arch or the hilum.'),
('5a5474a9-2da2-5131-80dd-288c7b522a89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: pericardiacophrenic artery:?', '["Aorta","Intercostal","Bronchial","From internal thoracic artery","with phrenic nerve (supplies pericardium) Subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'From internal thoracic artery', 'The pericardiacophrenic artery arises from the internal thoracic artery and runs with the phrenic nerve to supply the pericardium and diaphragm. It is not a branch of the aorta, bronchial or intercostal arteries.'),
('50c30b3c-1130-5088-9529-8bbad8fdd451'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: internal thoracic artery:?', '["From vertebral resulting in decreased functional activity","From aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From intercostal mediated by specific receptor activation","From axillary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From subclavian runs 1 cm lateral to sternum divides at 6th ICS"]'::jsonb, 'From subclavian runs 1 cm lateral to sternum divides at 6th ICS', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery, descends about 1 cm lateral to the sternum, and divides at the 6th intercostal space into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. It is not a branch of the aorta, axillary, vertebral or intercostal arteries.'),
('44e38d24-fed0-5511-acfa-f65f2b5e857c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: thymus location:?', '["Anterior mediastinum (superior and anterior)","Neck only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior mediastinum mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle mediastinum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mediastinum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior mediastinum (superior and anterior)', 'The thymus is a bilobed lymphoid organ located in the anterior mediastinum, extending from the lower neck into the superior and anterior mediastinum. It does not lie in the middle or posterior mediastinum.'),
('bee0bcdb-aebe-5fdf-9b4c-6cdb11634286'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Thymus function:'' is:', '["T-cell maturation and selection","Blood filtration","Hormone secretion to blood","B cell production","Mucus secretion"]'::jsonb, 'T-cell maturation and selection', 'The thymus is the primary lymphoid organ where T lymphocytes mature and undergo positive and negative selection before entering the circulation. It does not produce B cells, filter blood, or secrete mucus.'),
('83218838-bc96-50af-8144-598f9d4e8d8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: thymus involutes:?', '["During pregnancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Never accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","After puberty (fatty replacement)","In old age without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At birth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'After puberty (fatty replacement)', 'The thymus reaches its largest relative size around puberty and then involutes, with progressive replacement of lymphoid tissue by fat. It does not disappear at birth or remain active indefinitely.'),
('2ad92200-7394-5e27-8d0e-939919be6277'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: costomediastinal sinuses:?', '["Interlobar fissures resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior pleural recesses at junction of costal and mediastinal pleura","Mediastinal spaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior recesses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragmatic recesses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior pleural recesses at junction of costal and mediastinal pleura', 'The costomediastinal sinus is the anterior pleural recess where the costal pleura meets the mediastinal pleura, mainly behind the sternum. The costodiaphragmatic recess, by contrast, lies at the inferior border of the pleural cavity near the diaphragm.'),
('bffa5130-f9e1-5c28-a637-053454bb4755'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: suprapleural membrane (sibson''s):?', '["Covers cervical pleura","from C7 TP to 1st rib inner border","Endothoracic","Covering apex of lung","Clavipectoral Cervical fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Covers cervical pleura', 'The suprapleural membrane (Sibson''s fascia) is a thick fascial sheet covering the cervical pleura, extending from the C7 transverse process to the inner border of the first rib. It strengthens the dome of the pleura over the lung apex.'),
('0bb64c05-cf33-56a0-bf80-0293a17c9ac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: endothoracic fascia:?', '["Between pleura and pericardium resulting in decreased functional activity","Between pleura and lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thin connective tissue between parietal pleura and chest wall","Pleura itself without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Under skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin connective tissue between parietal pleura and chest wall', 'The endothoracic fascia is the thin connective tissue layer lying between the parietal pleura and the chest wall (ribs and intercostal muscles). It is deep to the thoracic wall and superficial to the parietal pleura.'),
('60ae42bc-c6c6-510d-a4a9-0ffd38d90830'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: brachiocephalic veins formed by:?', '["Internal jugular + subclavian veins","External jugular + subclavian","Vertebral + IJV","Left + right IJV","Left + right subclavian"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular + subclavian veins', 'Each brachiocephalic vein forms at the venous angle by the union of the internal jugular and subclavian veins behind the sternoclavicular joint. The external jugular and vertebral veins are not direct contributors.'),
('05ce45cd-13fc-5b11-b13c-f365bd8c1e2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Left brachiocephalic vein:'' is:', '["Same as right","Posterior to trachea","Right side","Longer","crosses midline behind manubrium Shorter"]'::jsonb, 'Longer', 'The left brachiocephalic vein is longer because it must cross the midline behind the manubrium to reach the superior vena cava. The right brachiocephalic vein is short and nearly vertical.'),
('552083b7-e3f5-5082-b3de-61dfa10a53e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: right brachiocephalic vein:?', '["Crosses midline","Longer","Horizontal","Shorter","vertical descends behind right first costal cartilage Same length as left"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter', 'The right brachiocephalic vein is short and descends almost vertically behind the right first costal cartilage to join the left vein in forming the superior vena cava. The left brachiocephalic vein is longer because it crosses the midline.'),
('336689f5-4be7-5c84-af30-100f41c5b361'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: svc compression:?', '["Pulmonary edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laryngeal paralysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumothorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","May cause superior mediastinal syndrome (swelling, cyanosis)"]'::jsonb, 'May cause superior mediastinal syndrome (swelling, cyanosis)', 'Compression or obstruction of the superior vena cava, often by malignancy in the superior mediastinum, causes facial and upper-limb swelling with cyanosis, known as superior vena cava syndrome. The other options are not characteristic consequences of SVC obstruction.'),
('573c78fa-761d-5edc-b0db-888832d0b644'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: tracheoesophageal groove:?', '["Retropharyngeal space","Paratracheal space","Between trachea and esophagus","contains recurrent laryngeal nerves","Carotid sheath Prevertebral space"]'::jsonb, 'Between trachea and esophagus', 'The tracheoesophageal groove is the sulcus between the trachea and the esophagus, located in the neck and superior mediastinum, and it carries the recurrent laryngeal nerves. It is not the retropharyngeal space, paratracheal space or carotid sheath.'),
('06b6479d-93e5-510e-9d98-72364ea73dda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: azygos lobe:?', '["Supernumerary lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Fissure variant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left lung mediated by specific receptor activation","Abnormal accessory lobe on right from azygos vein indentation","Middle lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal accessory lobe on right from azygos vein indentation', 'The azygos lobe is an accessory lobe of the right upper lung created when the azygos vein indents the apex, producing an azygos fissure. It is a normal variant, not a true left-sided lobe or a supernumerary fissure.'),
('c476b619-ffef-549d-ad52-36773247d59a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: pericardial cyst:?', '["Lung cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Pleural effusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital cyst in anterior/cardiophrenic angle","Aortic aneurysm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mediastinal tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital cyst in anterior/cardiophrenic angle', 'A pericardial cyst is a congenital, fluid-filled cyst of the pericardium typically found in the anterior mediastinum at a cardiophrenic angle, most often the right. It is not a lung cyst, pleural effusion, aneurysm or solid tumor.'),
('0346eb51-ed30-5548-b0fb-1adb330cc704'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Thoracic outlet syndrome:'' is:', '["Diaphragm paralysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Aortic aneurysm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lung compression mediated by specific receptor activation","C8/T1 compression by cervical rib or scalene muscles","Heart ischemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C8/T1 compression by cervical rib or scalene muscles', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome usually results from compression of the C8/T1 roots or lower brachial plexus by a cervical rib or hypertrophied scalene muscles. It is not caused by aortic, cardiac, lung or diaphragmatic pathology.'),
('105324e9-aaa0-52a9-8baf-7fcd8e758d38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'During revision of Mediastinum, which of the following is correct: first rib important for:?', '["Trachea resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic outlet anatomy (subclavian vessels and brachial plexus pass over it)","Lung apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragm attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternoclavicular joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic outlet anatomy (subclavian vessels and brachial plexus pass over it)', 'The first rib is the key landmark of the thoracic outlet because the subclavian artery and the brachial plexus pass over it between the anterior and middle scalene muscles. It is not primarily related to the trachea, lung apex or diaphragm.'),
('436e6f7d-acf3-5d0e-afe7-7c22f76aaad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, which statement best fits the following fact: subclavian artery on the right originates from:?', '["Carotid","Subclavian vein","Vertebral","Brachiocephalic trunk","Aortic arch"]'::jsonb, 'Brachiocephalic trunk', 'On the right the subclavian artery arises from the brachiocephalic trunk, which has already given off the right common carotid artery. It does not arise directly from the aortic arch on this side.'),
('a99552a9-036c-51be-8377-81641e45e0e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Regarding Mediastinum, what is the correct answer to: subclavian artery on the left originates from:?', '["Aortic arch (directly)","Vertebral","Subclavian vein","Left common carotid","Brachiocephalic trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (directly)', 'On the left the subclavian artery arises directly from the arch of the aorta as its third branch, after the left common carotid. On the right it instead comes from the brachiocephalic trunk.'),
('3571c20f-a6ff-5305-950f-9d3c240b9d82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'For Mediastinum, which option is most appropriate: cervical rib:?', '["Accessory rib from C7","can cause thoracic outlet compression","From C6","From sternum","From T1 From clavicle"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory rib from C7', 'A cervical rib is an accessory rib arising from the C7 vertebra and may compress the lower brachial plexus or subclavian vessels, producing thoracic outlet syndrome. It does not arise from C6, T1, the sternum or the clavicle.'),
('019b1a90-fdae-57be-a46f-68ac3627fa6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'Which answer is correct in Mediastinum when asked: anterior scalene insertion:?', '["Manubrium","First rib (scalene tubercle)","Sternocostal junction","Clavicle","Second rib"]'::jsonb, 'First rib (scalene tubercle)', 'The anterior scalene muscle inserts into the scalene tubercle on the upper surface of the first rib. The subclavian vein passes in front of it, while the subclavian artery and brachial plexus pass behind it.'),
('f94a0cfe-42be-5aa8-96ba-8ee9a98fce10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Mediastinum', 'In Mediastinum, the best response to ''Middle scalene insertion:'' is:', '["Second rib resulting in decreased functional activity","First rib (posterior to subclavian artery)","1st and 2nd ribs mediated by specific receptor activation","Clavicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First rib (posterior to subclavian artery)', 'The middle scalene muscle inserts into the first rib posterior to the subclavian artery, completing the scalene triangle through which the artery and brachial plexus pass. It does not insert on the second rib, clavicle or sternum.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('33d498d0-2460-55f3-96a5-51c112e41055'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Thoracic cage consists of:', '["12 thoracic vertebrae","12 pairs of ribs","costal cartilages sternum","12 vertebrae only Sternum + ribs only","Clavicles + scapulae Ribs + costal cartilages only"]'::jsonb, '12 thoracic vertebrae', 'The thoracic cage is formed posteriorly by the 12 thoracic vertebrae, laterally by the 12 pairs of ribs, and anteriorly by the costal cartilages and sternum. The clavicles and scapulae belong to the pectoral girdle, not the thoracic cage.'),
('17d4f978-eb6d-55f2-9e5c-fc9e97a9d241'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Sternum parts:', '["Manubrium only","Manubrium + body only","Body + xiphoid only","Manubrium + gladiolus","Manubrium body xiphoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium body xiphoid process', 'The sternum consists of three parts: the manubrium, the body (gladiolus), and the xiphoid process. Any answer omitting one of these parts is incomplete.'),
('f07dd066-2de2-5344-b864-101da6238f2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Sternal angle (Louis) at:', '["Xiphisternal junction","Body-xiphoid junction","Manubriosternal junction","T4-T5","Suprasternal notch Sternoclavicular joint"]'::jsonb, 'Manubriosternal junction', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) is the ridge at the manubriosternal junction where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum. The 2nd costal cartilage and the T4 vertebral level lie at this junction.'),
('f7b66db4-976a-539e-ad4b-a7b95dc2968f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Sternal angle landmarks:', '["2nd rib","tracheal bifurcation","aortic arch ends azygos arch","Xiphoid 1st rib","3rd rib Sternal notch"]'::jsonb, '2nd rib', 'At the sternal angle the 2nd costal cartilage articulates with the sternum, making the angle a reliable surface landmark for the 2nd rib. The tracheal bifurcation and azygos arch lie at this level but are not ribs.'),
('f9a9aa47-01d8-5dc5-b33c-b12cefcc60f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'True ribs (1-7):', '["Attached directly to sternum via costal cartilage","Attached to sternum indirectly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Floating mediated by specific receptor activation","anterior attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atypical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Attached directly to sternum via costal cartilage', 'True ribs (1-7) attach to the sternum directly through their own costal cartilages. False ribs (8-10) attach indirectly, and floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment.'),
('9ab4d51a-d63e-5780-acfc-2483ee48393f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'False ribs (8-10):', '["Floating resulting in decreased functional activity","attachment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct to sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","Free without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attached to costal cartilage of rib 7"]'::jsonb, 'Attached to costal cartilage of rib 7', 'False ribs (8-10) attach to the sternum indirectly through the costal cartilage of rib 7. Their cartilages do not reach the sternum directly, and they are neither floating nor free.'),
('bdec3194-a163-5fcd-8b84-aa4158f12209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Floating ribs (11-12):', '["Attach to vertebra only","Attach to xiphoid","Direct to sternum","Attach to rib 10","No anterior attachment"]'::jsonb, 'No anterior attachment', 'Floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment; their cartilages end within the posterior abdominal wall musculature. They do not connect to the sternum or to rib 10.'),
('48d71a63-a9bb-5f1e-8f8d-f2edc666e2ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Typical rib has:', '["Head","neck","tubercle","angle","shaft costal groove"]'::jsonb, 'Head', 'A typical rib has a head, neck, tubercle, angle, shaft and costal groove. The head is the posterior expanded portion that articulates with the vertebral bodies.'),
('d014c7fc-3785-5dae-9651-7a5eaaa9d2d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Typical rib head articulates with:', '["Only vertebra above resulting in decreased functional activity","Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)","Only own vertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","Sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)', 'The head of a typical rib has two facets that articulate with the bodies of its own vertebra and the vertebra above. Rib 1 is atypical because it has only a single facet.'),
('16835cb7-5ccc-5a9b-8c60-0e1dabf2716b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Rib tubercle articulates with:', '["Sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Costal cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertebra above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process of same vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse process of same vertebra', 'The tubercle of a typical rib articulates with the transverse process of its own (same-numbered) vertebra. The head, not the tubercle, articulates with the vertebral bodies.'),
('46a3de63-24de-562d-b5c6-f1dcfd44b6f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Costal groove contains:', '["Only vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Only artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)', 'The costal groove on the inferior border of the rib shelters the intercostal neurovascular bundle. From above downward the bundle runs vein, artery, nerve (VAN).'),
('743b133c-3355-5260-aa17-6c0c28bfc4e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'First rib atypical:', '["Two facets","Single facet","scalene tubercle","subclavian grooves","Longest costal groove tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Single facet', 'The first rib is atypical: its head has a single facet for T1 only, and it carries a scalene tubercle and grooves for the subclavian vessels. Typical ribs have two facets on the head.'),
('0eacf4bc-2d90-5197-aabc-f45edf3ffc83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Scalene tubercle on first rib for:', '["Scalenus medius","SCM","Scalenus anterior","Levator scapulae","Scalenus posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenus anterior', 'The scalene tubercle on the inner border of the first rib gives attachment to the tendon of scalenus anterior. The subclavian vein passes anterior to it, while the subclavian artery and the lower trunk of the brachial plexus pass behind it.'),
('e3bea6a0-26ff-544c-adef-e2149a3370a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Intercostal spaces:', '["11 spaces between 12 ribs","9 spaces","12 spaces","10 spaces","8 spaces"]'::jsonb, '11 spaces between 12 ribs', 'The 12 pairs of ribs form 11 intercostal spaces between adjacent ribs. Each space transmits intercostal muscles and their neurovascular bundles.'),
('c534d20f-ac16-5914-a894-1b51878cb626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Intercostal muscle layers:', '["2: external + internal resulting in decreased functional activity","4 layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 layer mediated by specific receptor activation","muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3: external internal innermost"]'::jsonb, '3: external internal innermost', 'Each intercostal space contains three muscle layers: the external, internal, and innermost intercostals. The innermost layer is incomplete and blends with the transversus thoracis muscle medially.'),
('0302974d-0cd7-5fa5-a14d-501dd9b69806'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'External intercostal fibers run:', '["Vertically resulting in decreased functional activity","Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)","Circularly mediated by specific receptor activation","Downward and backward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horizontally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)', 'External intercostal fibers run obliquely downward and forward, the direction of hands placed in coat pockets. Internal intercostal fibers run at right angles to them, downward and backward.'),
('ac82a83d-d93a-5db1-bd23-d0ed12b91ea5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Internal intercostal fibers run:', '["Vertically","Downward and forward","Parallel to ribs","Horizontally","Obliquely downward and backward"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and backward', 'Internal intercostal fibers run obliquely downward and backward. This direction is opposite to the external intercostal fibers, which run downward and forward.'),
('cc2de75f-ca6c-5628-ab88-02b988cf3d52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Innermost intercostal:', '["Part of diaphragm","Same as internal","External to internal","Deepest layer","separated from internal by neurovascular bundle Absent"]'::jsonb, 'Deepest layer', 'The innermost intercostal is the deepest of the three intercostal muscle layers. The intercostal neurovascular bundle passes between it and the internal intercostal.'),
('35b39cd5-f6d3-5981-9722-770b56c5a95a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Intercostal nerve is:', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of thoracic spinal nerves T1-T11. Each supplies the intercostal muscles, overlying skin, and parietal pleura of its space.'),
('15e73d25-2f2c-55b2-b5c0-9a4d61656ac1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Intercostal bundle runs:', '["Above ribs resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep to innermost accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between internal and innermost intercostals in costal groove","Below ribs externally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial to external leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between internal and innermost intercostals in costal groove', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. It lies protected beneath the rib, which is why drains are inserted just above a rib.'),
('cc7ee8f0-8827-509a-ae59-5775125a97d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'VAN order (superior to inferior):', '["Vein","Nerve","Artery","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve', 'In the costal groove the vein lies highest, the artery in the middle, and the nerve lowest. The nerve is therefore the inferior-most element of the VAN arrangement.'),
('d8c92e26-e19d-5b9d-b142-fa2f92f0b229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Anterior intercostal artery branch of:', '["Subclavian","Aorta","Posterior intercostal","Internal thoracic artery","Axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The anterior intercostal arteries of the upper spaces arise from the internal thoracic artery, while the lower spaces receive branches from the musculophrenic artery. They supply the anterior chest wall.'),
('51e17636-3da1-59e9-92bc-33b45a32416b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Posterior intercostal artery branch of:', '["Internal thoracic resulting in decreased functional activity","Costocervical trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta (lower 9) and subclavian (upper 2 via superior intercostal)","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (lower 9) and subclavian (upper 2 via superior intercostal)', 'The posterior intercostal arteries of the lower nine spaces arise from the descending aorta, and those of the upper two spaces come from the superior intercostal artery (costocervical trunk). They supply the posterior and lateral chest wall.'),
('dc198562-9bac-5488-be2d-a2cf2f033e5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Internal thoracic artery branch of:', '["External carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian artery (first part)","Brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (first part)', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery. It descends behind the upper six costal cartilages and supplies the anterior chest wall and the medial breast.'),
('0b8a65b6-cd08-5dc7-afde-710f17c5bff5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Internal thoracic artery runs:', '["Axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Midline accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral to nipple mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 cm lateral to sternum between pleura and costal cartilages"]'::jsonb, '1 cm lateral to sternum between pleura and costal cartilages', 'The internal thoracic artery descends about 1 cm lateral to the sternal edge, between the pleura and the costal cartilages. It gives off anterior intercostal and perforating branches.'),
('e4080185-3b7d-5d27-9e42-df87f55c1ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Internal thoracic terminates as:', '["Anterior intercostals resulting in decreased functional activity","Mediastinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternal branches mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardiacophrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries', 'At the level of the 6th costal cartilage the internal thoracic artery divides into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. The superior epigastric artery continues into the anterior abdominal wall.'),
('07d0b701-834e-5533-a2f4-96dec3663b25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Intercostal veins drain to:', '["IJV resulting in decreased functional activity","Subclavian accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Azygos (right) and hemiazygos (left) systems","SVC directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Azygos (right) and hemiazygos (left) systems', 'The posterior intercostal veins drain into the azygos system on the right and the hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins on the left. These systems ultimately empty into the superior vena cava.'),
('2be017c7-67a3-5114-a41e-bfe1790e699e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Breast located:', '["Over clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Back accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","Axilla only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Over pectoral muscles 2nd-6th ribs sternum to midaxillary line"]'::jsonb, 'Over pectoral muscles 2nd-6th ribs sternum to midaxillary line', 'The breast overlies the pectoral muscles and extends from the 2nd to the 6th rib and from the sternum to the midaxillary line. Its axillary tail projects toward the axilla.'),
('a8ac163f-d7d9-586d-a581-ae63cd0f0861'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Breast composition:', '["Single lobule resulting in decreased functional activity","lobules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-10 lobules mediated by specific receptor activation","15-20 lobules in fat and connective tissue","50-100 lobules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '15-20 lobules in fat and connective tissue', 'The breast is composed of 15-20 glandular lobules embedded in fat and connective tissue. The suspensory (Cooper''s) ligaments are connective tissue septa that anchor the skin to the deep fascia.'),
('a98724f1-1d16-5803-812f-d6c662f9eb5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Each lobule drains via:', '["Multiple ducts","Lymphatics","Blood vessels","Single common duct","Lactiferous duct opening at nipple"]'::jsonb, 'Lactiferous duct opening at nipple', 'Each lobule drains through a single lactiferous duct that opens at the nipple. Near the nipple each duct expands into a lactiferous sinus.'),
('c3b76d0d-5ccd-5dbe-8453-c06d2439a782'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Lactiferous sinuses:', '["Dilations of ducts near nipple (milk reservoirs)","Blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymph nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Terminal ductules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilations of ducts near nipple (milk reservoirs)', 'Lactiferous sinuses are dilatations of the lactiferous ducts just deep to the nipple. They store milk during lactation.'),
('6d886832-b574-52a0-977e-bbfa85abe5af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Cooper''s ligaments:', '["Nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrous bands supporting breast skin to pectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous bands supporting breast skin to pectoral fascia', 'Cooper''s (suspensory) ligaments are fibrous bands passing from the skin of the breast to the deep pectoral fascia. Infiltration by tumor shortens them and produces skin dimpling.'),
('143436a9-d604-5cf6-9963-941a59cc5579'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Axillary tail of Spence:', '["Axillary muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood vessel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Projection of breast tissue into axilla","Nerve plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymph node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Projection of breast tissue into axilla', 'The axillary tail of Spence is a projection of breast tissue that extends into the axilla through the deep fascia. It can be mistaken for enlarged axillary lymph nodes.'),
('f7c801c9-8602-508a-a50f-f9386dcd25db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Breast blood supply:', '["Only lateral thoracic","Only subclavian Only intercostal","Internal thoracic","lateral thoracic intercostal","thoracoacromial Only internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic', 'The breast is supplied by the internal thoracic (via perforating branches), lateral thoracic, and intercostal arteries. The internal thoracic artery is the principal supply to the medial half of the breast.'),
('b91623b3-d14d-59bf-8239-3846543b92d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Breast lymphatics primarily to:', '["Axillary nodes (75%)","Parasternal nodes","Contralateral","Supraclavicular","Subdiaphragmatic"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nodes (75%)', 'About 75% of breast lymph drains into the axillary lymph nodes, chiefly the anterior (pectoral) group. The remainder drains to the internal mammary nodes and elsewhere.'),
('dd754515-d3ee-5be9-b18e-f05dd90daf2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Axillary lymph node groups:', '["4 groups","2 groups 6 groups","5: anterior posterior","lateral central","apical 3 groups"]'::jsonb, '5: anterior posterior', 'The axillary nodes form five groups: anterior (pectoral), posterior (subscapular), lateral, central, and apical. Lymph from the breast passes through the lower groups toward the apical nodes.'),
('5387b40f-b4c9-57c9-a5bd-395cc211919e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Pectoral (anterior) axillary nodes:', '["Apex of axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Along subscapular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along axillary vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Central fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Along lateral thoracic vessels at lower border of pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Along lateral thoracic vessels at lower border of pectoralis minor', 'The anterior (pectoral) group lies along the lateral thoracic vessels at the lower border of pectoralis minor. It is the main route of lymph drainage from the breast.'),
('165b816b-cb4c-50d5-9f69-b28dbf95c714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Apical axillary nodes:', '["Central axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior axilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above upper border of pectoralis minor near axillary vein apex","Chest wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral axilla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above upper border of pectoralis minor near axillary vein apex', 'The apical (infraclavicular) group lies above the upper border of pectoralis minor near the axillary vein at the apex of the axilla. It receives lymph from all the other axillary groups.'),
('42b624e7-1e1c-5bef-b0f3-89f16be402f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Rotter''s nodes:', '["Interpectoral nodes between pectoralis major and minor","Parasternal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Along internal thoracic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supraclavicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interpectoral nodes between pectoralis major and minor', 'Rotter''s nodes are interpectoral lymph nodes lying between pectoralis major and minor. They also drain lymph from the breast.'),
('8d551f89-7953-5464-8697-747e1adc6371'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Internal mammary nodes drain:', '["Axilla","Medial breast","anterior chest wall","upper rectus sheath","Lateral breast Axillary tail Nipple only"]'::jsonb, 'Medial breast', 'The internal mammary (parasternal) nodes drain the medial part of the breast. Tumors of the medial breast may spread to these nodes even when the axillary nodes are not involved.'),
('8ce25846-130d-5759-abfa-10812199adcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Nipple at level of:', '["3rd costal cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","4th intercostal space / 4th rib in males","6th rib mediated by specific receptor activation","5th rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2nd rib leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4th intercostal space / 4th rib in males', 'In the male the nipple lies at the level of the 4th intercostal space, over the 4th rib. The position varies somewhat in women with breast size and ptosis.'),
('ad76570f-12bf-5b39-8f5d-263aac1767fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Areola contains:', '["Sweat glands only resulting in decreased functional activity","Hair follicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymph nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Modified sebaceous glands (Montgomery) and smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Modified sebaceous glands (Montgomery) and smooth muscle', 'The areola contains modified sebaceous (Montgomery''s) glands and smooth muscle fibers. In pregnancy the sebaceous glands enlarge to form Montgomery''s tubercles.'),
('79b8fe4e-e405-5040-8ffc-98d4cfe20ec8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Pectoralis major origin:', '["Ribs only","Sternum only","Clavicle medial half sternum","costal cartilages 1-6 external oblique aponeurosis","Clavicle only Scapula only"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle medial half sternum', 'Pectoralis major has a clavicular head from the medial half of the clavicle and a sternocostal head from the sternum and upper costal cartilages. The two heads converge to insert on the humerus.'),
('58da052a-950a-5a3c-925c-1846ae0b3e9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Pectoralis major insertion:', '["Crest of greater tubercle of humerus (lateral lip)","Lesser tubercle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial humerus mediated by specific receptor activation","Coracoid process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crest of greater tubercle of humerus (lateral lip)', 'Pectoralis major inserts into the crest of the greater tubercle of the humerus (lateral lip of the intertubercular groove). It adducts and medially rotates the arm.'),
('b60334bf-fb10-5d19-8a34-0468289a6d29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Pectoralis major nerve supply:', '["Suprascapular resulting in decreased functional activity","Long thoracic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracodorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1)', 'Pectoralis major is innervated by both the lateral (C5-C7) and medial (C8-T1) pectoral nerves. The medial pectoral nerve pierces pectoralis minor to reach it.'),
('c5155f6c-fdbb-5fe8-bbe7-7f0cb7de303c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Pectoralis minor nerve supply:', '["Medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1)","Long thoracic","Axillary","Thoracodorsal","Lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1)', 'Pectoralis minor is supplied by the medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1). The lateral pectoral nerve does not innervate it directly.'),
('db479c78-8d14-5aff-a452-a94c1b83c0fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Serratus anterior nerve supply:', '["Axillary","Suprascapular","Thoracodorsal","Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7)","Medial pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7)', 'Serratus anterior is supplied by the long thoracic nerve (nerve of Bell) arising from C5-C7. The nerve runs down the superficial surface of the muscle.'),
('d71f006a-dacf-5047-bf63-3023563f6570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Winging of scapula from injury of:', '["Suprascapular resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracodorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior)', 'Injury to the long thoracic nerve paralyzes serratus anterior, allowing the medial border of the scapula to wing away from the chest wall when the arm pushes forward. The muscle normally holds the scapula against the thorax.'),
('ddeaa01b-3f51-53cf-89e1-35319f9597d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Clavipectoral fascia pierced by:', '["Brachial plexus","Cephalic vein","thoracoacromial artery lateral pectoral nerve","lymphatics Axillary vein","Only thoracoacromial Only cephalic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic vein', 'The clavipectoral fascia is pierced by the cephalic vein, the thoracoacromial artery, the lateral pectoral nerve, and lymphatics. These structures pass between the axilla and the pectoral region.'),
('eeb41d7e-4e5c-56e0-b7f3-9815cf3cd307'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Thoracic wall nerve supply:', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only accessory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only phrenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and subcostal (T12)","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and subcostal (T12)', 'The thoracic wall is segmentally supplied by the intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and the subcostal nerve (T12). Each nerve supplies the muscles and skin of its space.'),
('93fa6f4e-0433-5b17-9db5-0897cd312aad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Diaphragm nerve supply:', '["Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)","Intercostal","Vagus","Long thoracic","Sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)', 'The phrenic nerve (anterior rami of C3-C5, principally C4) provides the entire motor supply to the diaphragm. Intercostal, vagus, long thoracic and sympathetic nerves do not innervate the diaphragm; phrenic sensory fibers explain the referred shoulder-tip pain in diaphragmatic irritation.'),
('b21b43de-644b-59c4-9615-3aad3a0bc7b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Phrenic nerve arises from:', '["C4-C6","C3","C4","C5 (mainly C4)","C1-C3 C2-C4 C5-C7"]'::jsonb, 'C3', 'The phrenic nerve receives contributions from the anterior rami of C3, C4 and C5 spinal nerves, with C4 usually giving the largest component. The chosen option names C3, one of these contributing segments; fibers from all three levels unite to form the nerve.'),
('b4d96bad-5871-58bf-9415-67aafa816bd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The breast extends from which ribs vertically?', '["Rib 2 to rib 6","Rib 2 to rib 5","Rib 1 to rib 4","Rib 3 to rib 7","Rib 1 to rib 3"]'::jsonb, 'Rib 2 to rib 6', 'The breast extends vertically from the second to the sixth rib, and horizontally from the sternal edge to the midaxillary line. Its superolateral part continues toward the axilla as the axillary tail of Spence.'),
('2b6dafdb-f881-5d25-98e8-4f4a3953cbcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The thoracic cage consists of:', '["Only ribs and sternum","The clavicles and scapulae","Only vertebrae and ribs","Only the sternum","12 thoracic vertebrae"]'::jsonb, '12 thoracic vertebrae', 'The thoracic cage is formed by the 12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs with costal cartilages, and the sternum. The chosen option names the 12 thoracic vertebrae, a genuine component of the cage, whereas the clavicles and scapulae belong to the pectoral girdle.'),
('162c7e47-82fd-5c36-9eb6-49fb239863fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The sternum consists of:', '["Manubrium and body only","Body and xiphoid only","Four parts","One single bone","Manubrium"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium', 'The sternum comprises three parts: the manubrium, the body (gladiolus) and the xiphoid process. The chosen option names the manubrium, one of these genuine parts; the other choices are incomplete or inaccurate.'),
('1b2516d1-227a-5dc2-bfea-44494b264a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) is:', '["At the junction of manubrium and body of sternum","level T4/T5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the manubrial notch mediated by specific receptor activation","At T12 level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At T1 level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the junction of manubrium and body of sternum', 'The sternal angle is the transverse ridge where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum at the manubriosternal joint. It lies at the level of the T4/T5 intervertebral disc and marks the second costal cartilage.'),
('a95db255-abe9-522d-9a61-c6a6a99c03f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The xiphoid process:', '["Is the smallest part of sternum","may be bifid","ossifies later in life","Articulates with all ribs","Is the largest part of sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Is the smallest part of sternum', 'The xiphoid process is the smallest and most inferior part of the sternum, joined to the body at the xiphisternal joint. Although it may be bifid or ossify only later in life, its defining feature is its small size, and it articulates with no rib.'),
('aa2e43d5-84c0-51b8-be26-0159d9c77bfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The first rib is:', '["The rib that attaches to T12","The shortest","broadest","most curved rib with a scalene tubercle","The most mobile rib"]'::jsonb, 'The shortest', 'The first rib is the shortest, broadest and most sharply curved of all the ribs, and its inner border bears a scalene tubercle for the anterior scalene muscle. It articulates only with T1 and is not mobile.'),
('cff4ff26-57df-5e00-a2a0-86d3c17478ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The scalene tubercle on the 1st rib is:', '["For middle scalene","For serratus anterior","For attachment of anterior scalene muscle","For posterior scalene","For subclavius muscle"]'::jsonb, 'For attachment of anterior scalene muscle', 'The scalene tubercle lies on the inner border of the first rib and gives attachment to the anterior scalene muscle. The subclavian vein crosses anterior to the tubercle while the subclavian artery passes behind it.'),
('27e178ad-25e8-514d-a296-341811b82e54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The intercostal spaces are:', '["The spaces between adjacent ribs","occupied by intercostal muscles","vessels","and nerves","The spaces in the mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'The spaces between adjacent ribs', 'Intercostal spaces are the gaps between adjacent ribs, containing the intercostal muscles and neurovascular bundles. They are part of the thoracic wall, not the mediastinum.'),
('47d1719f-595e-5e61-baf6-cca019c6f4d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Intercostal nerves are:', '["Posterior rami of thoracic nerves","Branches of the brachial plexus","Cervical nerves","Sacral nerves","Anterior rami of T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves', 'The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of the T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves, while T12 continues as the subcostal nerve. They run in the intercostal spaces and supply the intercostal muscles, overlying skin and parietal pleura.'),
('0a90fd0e-a3f2-5c13-9643-4a3b087de8d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The neurovascular bundle of an intercostal space runs:', '["Deep to the pleura","In the costal groove","between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles","Superficial to the intercostal muscles","Between the skin and muscle"]'::jsonb, 'In the costal groove', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle (vein, artery and nerve) lies in the costal groove along the lower border of the rib, sheltered within the groove. It runs between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles, so injections are placed above the lower rib.'),
('eb80702e-b9b2-54e7-8287-cd39cdd7cfc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The intercostal artery arises from:', '["Facial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thoracic aorta (for posterior intercostals) and internal thoracic artery (for anterior intercostals)","External carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic aorta (for posterior intercostals) and internal thoracic artery (for anterior intercostals)', 'The posterior intercostal arteries arise from the thoracic aorta, while the anterior intercostal arteries are branches of the internal thoracic artery and its musculophrenic continuation. The facial, vertebral, external carotid and brachial arteries are not sources of these vessels.'),
('a840daf6-2eee-538f-94ad-cffab6493edf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The internal thoracic artery gives off:', '["Lumbar arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior intercostal arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior intercostal branches and the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries","Bronchial arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior intercostal branches and the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries', 'The internal thoracic artery descends behind the costal cartilages, giving off anterior intercostal branches and terminating by dividing into the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries. Posterior intercostal, bronchial, lumbar and esophageal arteries arise from other vessels.'),
('0f1fb95a-4ea1-5009-9586-0ddda5894c28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The intercostal vein drains into:', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior vena cava directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior vena cava mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Azygos/hemiazygos veins (posteriorly) and internal thoracic veins (anteriorly)"]'::jsonb, 'Azygos/hemiazygos veins (posteriorly) and internal thoracic veins (anteriorly)', 'Posterior intercostal veins drain into the azygos and hemiazygos venous systems, whereas the anterior intercostal veins drain into the internal thoracic veins, which empty into the brachiocephalic veins. The SVC, IVC and pulmonary veins do not receive them directly.'),
('a6ccb72d-643c-57a6-a43d-8fb8476ce126'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The serratus anterior muscle:', '["Protracts the scapula (punching muscle)","supplied by long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Elevates the scapula","Rotates the scapula","Supplies the arm"]'::jsonb, 'Protracts the scapula (punching muscle)', 'Serratus anterior protracts the scapula and holds it against the thoracic wall, acting when the arm is pushed forward in punching. It is supplied by the long thoracic nerve (C5-C7), and paralysis gives a winged scapula.'),
('770d28ca-e744-5259-90fb-5b0b9558ab5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The pectoralis major muscle:', '["Flexes the elbow","Extends the arm","Abducts the arm","Supinates the forearm","Adducts and medially rotates the arm"]'::jsonb, 'Adducts and medially rotates the arm', 'Pectoralis major adducts and medially rotates the arm, and its clavicular head also assists flexion of the shoulder. It does not extend, abduct, or act on the elbow or forearm.'),
('a5086288-cdf9-5183-84fc-4a2ab6833d4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The pectoralis minor muscle:', '["Elevates the shoulder","Adducts the arm","Depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula","supplied by medial pectoral nerve","Rotates the trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula', 'Pectoralis minor depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula, pulling the tip of the shoulder downward and forward. It is supplied by the medial pectoral nerve and does not move the arm or trunk.'),
('6b4ddc6e-ec49-5e4e-9bb8-0e78640bd76b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The clavicle is:', '["A sesamoid bone resulting in decreased functional activity","A flat bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not a long bone mediated by specific receptor activation","The most commonly fractured bone in the body","A short bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most commonly fractured bone in the body', 'The clavicle is the most commonly fractured bone, usually breaking at the junction of its middle and lateral thirds where it is weakest. It is a long bone that transmits forces from the upper limb to the axial skeleton.'),
('ab0b9425-ce22-5d96-91a2-5dc9ac6c0d97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The manubriosternal joint is:', '["A hinge joint resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibrous joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A synovial joint mediated by specific receptor activation","A secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis)","A primary cartilaginous joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis)', 'The manubriosternal joint is a secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis) in which a fibrocartilaginous disc unites the manubrium and the body of the sternum. It forms the sternal angle and moves slightly during respiration.'),
('9cded96f-f45b-5609-b675-d07e98c34a23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The costovertebral joints are:', '["Fibrous joints resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilaginous joints accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Synovial joints between the ribs and vertebrae","Hinge joints without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sutures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial joints between the ribs and vertebrae', 'The costovertebral joints are synovial joints between the heads of the ribs and the vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs of the thoracic spine. They allow slight gliding of the ribs during breathing.'),
('7588089b-cb35-5167-bbee-7dab4c25612a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The costotransverse joints are:', '["Synovial joints between the tubercle of rib and the transverse process of vertebra","Synarthroses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Symphyses mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous joints without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilaginous joints leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial joints between the tubercle of rib and the transverse process of vertebra', 'The costotransverse joints are synovial joints between the tubercles of the ribs and the transverse processes of the corresponding vertebrae. Together with the costovertebral joints they permit the rib movements of ventilation.'),
('2e0f8b4a-f7b4-5b53-bd0b-fe881b673279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The subcostal nerve (T12):', '["Is part of the lumbar plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","Runs below the 12th rib and supplies the lower abdominal wall","Is a branch of the brachial plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","Supplies the diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a cervical nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Runs below the 12th rib and supplies the lower abdominal wall', 'The subcostal nerve is the anterior ramus of T12, running below the twelfth rib to supply the muscles and skin of the lower abdominal wall. It is the lowest thoracic nerve and contributes a branch to the lumbar plexus.'),
('2a2ea1c2-faa1-567b-958f-f36937d0ced7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The diaphragm originates from:', '["Xiphoid process","costal cartilages of ribs 7-12","and lumbar vertebrae","Only the xiphoid","Only the vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Xiphoid process', 'The diaphragm has three parts of origin: a sternal part from the posterior surface of the xiphoid process, a costal part from the costal cartilages of ribs 7-12, and a lumbar part from the lumbar vertebrae and arcuate ligaments. The xiphoid supplies the small sternal slip.'),
('72f98a93-c317-5d22-8282-96fc2daec8c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The central tendon of the diaphragm is:', '["The aponeurotic insertion of the diaphragm","fused with fibrous pericardium","The caval opening","The aortic opening","The esophageal opening"]'::jsonb, 'The aponeurotic insertion of the diaphragm', 'The central tendon is the aponeurotic insertion of the diaphragm, onto which the sternal, costal and lumbar fibers converge. It is fused above with the fibrous pericardium and is pierced by the caval opening at T8.'),
('253910c1-5ca5-5bcc-b81b-d922423af233'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The aortic hiatus (T12) transmits:', '["Sympathetic trunk","Aorta","thoracic duct","and azygos/hemiazygos veins","Esophagus and vagus nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta', 'The aortic hiatus at T12 transmits the aorta and usually the thoracic duct, and sometimes the azygos vein. The sympathetic trunks pass behind the diaphragm near the arcuate ligaments, while the esophagus with the vagi uses the esophageal hiatus.'),
('fb76952f-f094-59b2-a7d6-755adfe1c30c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The esophageal hiatus (T10) transmits:', '["IVC and phrenic nerve","Esophagus","vagus nerves (anterior and posterior)","esophageal vessels","Only the esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10 transmits the esophagus, together with the anterior and posterior vagal trunks and small esophageal vessels. The IVC and right phrenic nerve pass through the caval opening at T8, and the aorta uses the aortic hiatus at T12.'),
('faaef25b-40af-5271-a76c-df9d8fe9ca12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The caval opening (T8) transmits:', '["The phrenic nerve alone","Aorta and thoracic duct","IVC and right phrenic nerve","Esophagus and vagus","Only the IVC"]'::jsonb, 'IVC and right phrenic nerve', 'The caval opening in the central tendon at T8 transmits the inferior vena cava and the right phrenic nerve. The aorta and thoracic duct pass through the aortic hiatus, while the esophagus and vagus nerves use the esophageal hiatus.'),
('e58ac46c-38a2-5445-a0fb-956f28afa4a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The internal intercostal muscles function in:', '["Compression of abdomen","Flexion of trunk","Forced expiration (depress ribs)","Elevation of scapula","Inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiration (depress ribs)', 'The internal intercostal muscles depress the ribs and thereby contribute to forced expiration. Quiet inspiration is driven mainly by the external intercostals and the diaphragm.'),
('5ec4366d-a52b-55ba-b090-30697b16c248'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The thoracic wall consists of:', '["Ribs and intercostal muscles only","Ribs and sternum only","Sternum and clavicle","Thoracic vertebrae and ribs","Ribs, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, intercostal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, intercostal muscles', 'The thoracic wall is formed by the 12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs with costal cartilages, the sternum and the intercostal muscles. The clavicle belongs to the pectoral girdle, not the thoracic wall.'),
('b8f9e70e-1122-5039-a738-fbc04615c97a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The true ribs are:', '["Ribs 1-5 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 11-12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 1-7 attaching directly to sternum","Ribs 1-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ribs 8-10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 1-7 attaching directly to sternum', 'True ribs (1-7) attach directly to the sternum through their own costal cartilages. Ribs 8-10 are false ribs that attach indirectly, and ribs 11-12 are floating ribs with no anterior attachment.'),
('b1687fe7-8d85-5d5f-b1d9-ea7c6cf7d9cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The false ribs are:', '["Ribs 6-10 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 1-7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 11-12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 8-10 (attach via costal cartilage)","Ribs 8-12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 8-10 (attach via costal cartilage)', 'False ribs (8-10) reach the sternum indirectly because their costal cartilages join the costal cartilage of rib 7 instead of attaching directly. Ribs 11-12 are floating ribs, which form a subset of the false ribs.'),
('891d8915-1e90-57d1-bc9e-354cdfe34d40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The floating ribs are:', '["Ribs 8-10 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 1-7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 10-12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 9-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ribs 11-12 (no anterior attachment)"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 11-12 (no anterior attachment)', 'Floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment; their costal cartilages end freely within the muscles of the posterior abdominal wall. They are not connected to the sternum or to other costal cartilages.'),
('e425e32a-f244-5ba6-86c7-b9337c731c88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The intercostal nerves are branches of:', '["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Thoracic sympathetic chain","Anterior rami of T1-T11 spinal nerves","Posterior rami of spinal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of T1-T11 spinal nerves', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of the T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves, while the anterior ramus of T12 forms the subcostal nerve. They are mixed somatic nerves, distinct from the vagus, phrenic and sympathetic structures.'),
('52d4e872-92c3-5808-892f-60c82aad9e70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The breast is located:', '["Over pectoralis minor","Over serratus anterior","Over pectoralis major, between ribs 2-6","Between ribs 1-4","Between ribs 4-8"]'::jsonb, 'Over pectoralis major, between ribs 2-6', 'The breast lies on the deep fascia over the pectoralis major and extends vertically from the second to the sixth rib. Its superolateral extension toward the axilla is the axillary tail of Spence.'),
('654d89d0-4410-5775-8424-a7f96d7f7881'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The axillary tail of Spence is:', '["A vein draining the breast","A projection of breast tissue into axilla","A lymph node in the axilla","An artery supplying the breast","A nerve innervating the breast"]'::jsonb, 'A projection of breast tissue into axilla', 'The axillary tail of Spence is the superolateral prolongation of breast glandular tissue extending toward the axilla, usually through an opening in the deep fascia. It becomes more prominent during pregnancy and may be a site of breast malignancy.'),
('a0cd02e0-62eb-5e1a-bdc5-cbdee5c96482'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Lymphatic drainage of the breast primarily goes to:', '["Cervical nodes","Mediastinal nodes","Axillary lymph nodes","Subclavian nodes","Parasternal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary lymph nodes', 'About 75% of breast lymph, especially from the lateral quadrants, drains into the axillary lymph nodes. The medial part of the breast drains mainly to the parasternal (internal mammary) nodes.'),
('541a54ab-2e26-5d0d-930d-af3c1c5a765a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Cooper ligaments of the breast:', '["Glandular tissue of breast","Nerves of breast","Blood vessels of breast","Suspensory ligaments supporting breast","Lymphatics of breast"]'::jsonb, 'Suspensory ligaments supporting breast', 'Cooper ligaments are fibrous suspensory ligaments that pass from the skin through the breast to the deep fascia over the pectoralis major, helping support the breast. Shortening of these ligaments by tumour infiltration produces skin dimpling.'),
('9a05a405-88d2-5c2d-af9b-eec130183f7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which option correctly describes the intercostal nerve?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of the T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves, supplying the intercostal spaces. They are not sympathetic, splanchnic, posterior ramus or vagal structures.'),
('e404d957-dd73-5ccf-8323-2764a7dc0a27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The intercostal nerve is best described as:', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Each intercostal nerve is the anterior ramus of a thoracic spinal nerve, running forward within its intercostal space. The posterior rami supply the back muscles, while sympathetic, splanchnic and vagus fibers have different distributions.'),
('a3906bb7-e692-5c5b-80b5-dcbcc0635e43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Choose the most accurate statement about the intercostal nerve.', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of the thoracic spinal nerves (T1-T11), carrying somatic motor and sensory fibers to the thoracic wall. The vagus, sympathetic trunk and splanchnic nerves are not intercostal nerves.'),
('240e3860-cb97-5570-aebc-622adaca836a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Identify the correct response about the intercostal nerve.', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'The intercostal nerve is the anterior ramus of a thoracic spinal nerve running in the intercostal groove. It is a mixed somatic nerve rather than a sympathetic, splanchnic, posterior ramus or vagal structure.'),
('6cc21888-abf2-5c89-abb2-08f30dd30b3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Thoracic Wall Breast: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('2cc6309b-8c5e-5778-950f-c0213fc42e32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which response best describes the intercostal nerve?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Each thoracic spinal nerve divides into a small posterior ramus and a large anterior ramus; the anterior rami of T1-T11 form the intercostal nerves. Sympathetic, splanchnic and vagus components are not intercostal nerves.'),
('7e493564-e2a3-50a4-bb4b-f6096f7959d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During therapeutic management of Thoracic Wall Breast, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('45d22bff-3bd3-510d-ae3e-feea2cd10920'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding clinical cases in Thoracic Wall Breast: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('04ad8428-baef-526e-aa9f-2afe9b8a4fee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) is located at:', '["Xiphisternal junction","Body-xiphoid junction","Manubriosternal junction","T4-T5","Suprasternal notch Sternoclavicular joint"]'::jsonb, 'Manubriosternal junction', 'The sternal angle is formed at the manubriosternal junction, lying at the level of the T4/T5 intervertebral disc. It marks the attachment of the second costal cartilage, used to identify and count the ribs.'),
('518612a2-4d9f-5db3-a1c4-f4e92dc1c1fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which landmark is associated with the sternal angle?', '["2nd rib","tracheal bifurcation","aortic arch ends azygos arch","Xiphoid 1st rib","3rd rib Sternal notch"]'::jsonb, '2nd rib', 'The sternal angle marks the attachment of the second costal cartilage, the landmark used to locate the second rib and count the ribs. It also indicates the level of tracheal bifurcation and the start and end of the aortic arch.'),
('68f7d8ad-b54c-5642-a206-dc9dda5ee9f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Thoracic Wall Breast: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('8eebe228-5485-5b29-aa91-21d33589057d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During therapeutic management of Thoracic Wall Breast, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('118f88ee-ebcb-527a-a464-42da692bef1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: floating ribs (11-12):?', '["Attach to vertebra only","Attach to xiphoid","Direct to sternum","Attach to rib 10","No anterior attachment"]'::jsonb, 'No anterior attachment', 'Floating ribs (11 and 12) have no anterior attachment; their costal cartilage tips end free within the posterior abdominal wall. Attaching to the vertebra alone, xiphoid, sternum, or rib 10 is not how these ribs behave.'),
('18a4c5b8-95ca-506e-8fdc-d93ae76d6b9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: typical rib has:?', '["Head","neck","tubercle","angle","shaft costal groove"]'::jsonb, 'Head', 'A typical rib bears a head, neck, tubercle, angle, and shaft with a costal groove; the head is the expanded proximal part carrying the articular facets. Each of the other choices names a separate component of the rib rather than the part asked.'),
('900f81d0-0309-5e8c-b9e0-32c1775da82b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: typical rib head articulates with:?', '["Only vertebra above resulting in decreased functional activity","Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)","Only own vertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","Sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)', 'The head of a typical rib carries two facets that articulate with the body of its own vertebra and the body of the vertebra immediately above. A single vertebral body, the sternum, or the transverse process are not its articulation points.'),
('d7778bd5-1509-5c51-95f6-323b55ea078d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: rib tubercle articulates with:?', '["Sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Costal cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertebra above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process of same vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse process of same vertebra', 'The rib tubercle articulates through its smooth facet with the transverse process of the same-numbered vertebra. The sternum, vertebral body, costal cartilage, or vertebra above do not receive the tubercle.'),
('556e75aa-bd79-5555-918f-7b9bb6db8fb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Costal groove contains:'' is:', '["Only vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Only artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)', 'The costal groove along the lower inner border of the rib shelters the intercostal neurovascular bundle, with the vein, artery, and nerve stacked from above down. A groove holding only one vessel, nerve, or lymphatic channel gives an incomplete picture.'),
('2afd7d03-c2a7-51e1-9958-a231e59630ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: first rib atypical:?', '["Two facets","Single facet","scalene tubercle","subclavian grooves","Longest costal groove tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Single facet', 'The first rib is atypical because its head has a single facet for the body of T1, unlike typical ribs with two facets. Two facets and a long costal groove belong to typical ribs, while the scalene tubercle and subclavian grooves are additional first-rib features, not the facet count.'),
('3439b8b3-d51f-5d5f-a876-87e007e88f51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: scalene tubercle on first rib for:?', '["Scalenus medius","SCM","Scalenus anterior","Levator scapulae","Scalenus posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenus anterior', 'The scalene tubercle on the first rib is the insertion of scalenus anterior, which passes between the subclavian vein in front and the subclavian artery and brachial plexus behind. The other scalene muscles and neck muscles attach elsewhere on the first ribs or surrounding structures.'),
('664544b2-a6b3-5a71-ad5f-e041f283caa9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: intercostal spaces:?', '["11 spaces between 12 ribs","9 spaces","12 spaces","10 spaces","8 spaces"]'::jsonb, '11 spaces between 12 ribs', 'Twelve pairs of ribs enclose 11 intercostal spaces. Any other count ignores the fixed relation between the rib pairs and the spaces they define.'),
('ba3e6f58-c58b-58b7-9e90-f357c254c7db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: intercostal muscle layers:?', '["2: external + internal resulting in decreased functional activity","4 layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 layer mediated by specific receptor activation","muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3: external internal innermost"]'::jsonb, '3: external internal innermost', 'The intercostal muscles form three layers: external, internal, and innermost. Two layers, a single layer, or four layers do not reflect the true layered arrangement of the intercostal wall.'),
('1a9dca74-c758-5443-a5d4-2b4a74fb847a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: external intercostal fibers run:?', '["Vertically resulting in decreased functional activity","Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)","Circularly mediated by specific receptor activation","Downward and backward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horizontally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)', 'External intercostal fibers run obliquely downward and forward, like hands placed in trousers pockets, and elevate the ribs during inspiration. Vertical, horizontal, circular, or downward-and-backward orientations describe other muscle layers or structures.'),
('bd7b7e43-0ad2-5867-8b5c-733668c39490'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Internal intercostal fibers run:'' is:', '["Vertically","Downward and forward","Parallel to ribs","Horizontally","Obliquely downward and backward"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and backward', 'Internal intercostal fibers run obliquely downward and backward, perpendicular to the external layer. The other directions belong to a different intercostal layer or to other thoracic muscles.'),
('df72c157-6f35-5f01-970f-6c0df45f7450'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: innermost intercostal:?', '["Part of diaphragm","Same as internal","External to internal","Deepest layer","separated from internal by neurovascular bundle Absent"]'::jsonb, 'Deepest layer', 'The innermost intercostal forms the deepest muscle layer, lying deep to the intercostal neurovascular bundle. It is not part of the diaphragm, does not replicate the internal layer, and lies deep rather than superficial to the internal intercostal.'),
('e09468ed-1820-5f17-84b3-6834f10e97e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: intercostal nerve is:?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'An intercostal nerve is the anterior ramus of a thoracic spinal nerve (T1-T11) and supplies the thoracic wall segmentally. Posterior rami, sympathetic fibers, splanchnic nerves, and the vagus are separate pathways, not the source of intercostal nerves.'),
('4d1d3d5b-44d5-59b9-96f7-dc98b8737835'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: intercostal bundle runs:?', '["Above ribs resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep to innermost accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between internal and innermost intercostals in costal groove","Below ribs externally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial to external leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between internal and innermost intercostals in costal groove', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove, sandwiched between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. A position above the ribs, deep to the innermost layer, or superficial to the external layer would not shelter the bundle within the groove.'),
('c535f243-2921-5138-be0a-ae1f2680e193'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: VAN order (superior to inferior):?', '["Vein","Nerve","Artery","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve', 'In the costal groove the vein, artery, and nerve lie from superior to inferior in that sequence, so the nerve is the lowest element of the bundle. ''Vein'' and ''artery'' sit above it, and the liver and kidney are not part of the thoracic wall.'),
('55e41028-f1cb-5e03-9a91-646d2478f0fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: anterior intercostal artery branch of:?', '["Subclavian","Aorta","Posterior intercostal","Internal thoracic artery","Axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The anterior intercostal arteries arise from the internal thoracic artery (and its musculophrenic branch) to supply the front of each intercostal space. The aorta, subclavian and axillary trunks, and the posterior intercostal arteries are not their immediate source.'),
('a2a78cce-65d8-596e-83d3-f19d6e24bc58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Posterior intercostal artery branch of:'' is:', '["Internal thoracic resulting in decreased functional activity","Costocervical trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta (lower 9) and subclavian (upper 2 via superior intercostal)","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (lower 9) and subclavian (upper 2 via superior intercostal)', 'The upper two posterior intercostal arteries come from the superior intercostal branch of the costocervical (subclavian) trunk, while the lower nine arise directly from the descending aorta. Internal thoracic, costocervical-alone, subclavian-alone, or axillary origins each give only part of the true source.'),
('92eb590f-784c-5511-becf-dc7d3d4b0369'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: internal thoracic artery branch of:?', '["External carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian artery (first part)","Brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (first part)', 'The internal thoracic artery branches from the first part of the subclavian artery and descends behind the upper costal cartilages. The external carotid, aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, and axillary artery are not its origin.'),
('030334ec-f5d9-5064-b087-56676f16907d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: internal thoracic artery runs:?', '["Axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Midline accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral to nipple mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 cm lateral to sternum between pleura and costal cartilages"]'::jsonb, '1 cm lateral to sternum between pleura and costal cartilages', 'The internal thoracic artery descends about 1 cm lateral to the sternal edge, between the costal cartilages and the parietal pleura. A midline, axillary, nipple-lateral, or intercostal-space location does not correspond to its constant course.'),
('1485188f-a9e9-5df3-8d73-6c84615957da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: internal thoracic terminates as:?', '["Anterior intercostals resulting in decreased functional activity","Mediastinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternal branches mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardiacophrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries', 'At the level of the sixth costal cartilage the internal thoracic artery divides into its terminal branches, the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. The anterior intercostal, mediastinal, sternal, and pericardiacophrenic branches are given off along its course rather than at its termination.'),
('d80c8946-97a1-5a77-b6d0-93d8882b9b20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: intercostal veins drain to:?', '["IJV resulting in decreased functional activity","Subclavian accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Azygos (right) and hemiazygos (left) systems","SVC directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Azygos (right) and hemiazygos (left) systems', 'Intercostal veins drain on the right into the azygos system and on the left into the hemiazygos system, which then empty into the superior vena cava. Direct SVC, IVC, subclavian, or internal jugular drainage does not describe their actual route.'),
('f7c6816f-1820-5c20-be2a-d6cb62d870f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: breast located:?', '["Over clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Back accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","Axilla only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Over pectoral muscles 2nd-6th ribs sternum to midaxillary line"]'::jsonb, 'Over pectoral muscles 2nd-6th ribs sternum to midaxillary line', 'The breast overlies the pectoral muscles, extending vertically from the second to the sixth rib and horizontally from the sternum to the midaxillary line. Placement on the clavicle, back, abdomen, or axilla alone is not its true surface position.'),
('dac0a3df-bc36-5de7-86af-cec9b94a3252'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Breast composition:'' is:', '["Single lobule resulting in decreased functional activity","lobules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-10 lobules mediated by specific receptor activation","15-20 lobules in fat and connective tissue","50-100 lobules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '15-20 lobules in fat and connective tissue', 'The breast contains 15-20 glandular lobules embedded in fat and connective tissue. A single lobule, 5-10 lobules, 50-100 lobules, or ''lobules'' alone give a wrong number or an incomplete description of the gland''s structure.'),
('f4e0ad96-f0b2-5ae6-9736-04cfd6f3c5f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: each lobule drains via:?', '["Multiple ducts","Lymphatics","Blood vessels","Single common duct","Lactiferous duct opening at nipple"]'::jsonb, 'Lactiferous duct opening at nipple', 'Each mammary lobule drains through a lactiferous duct, which widens near the nipple and opens as a punctum on the nipple surface. Multiple ducts, a single common duct, lymphatics, or blood vessels do not represent the lobular drainage channel.'),
('de45689f-2ee8-51bf-b48b-9e202ff087af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: lactiferous sinuses:?', '["Dilations of ducts near nipple (milk reservoirs)","Blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymph nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Terminal ductules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilations of ducts near nipple (milk reservoirs)', 'Lactiferous sinuses are dilated ampullae of the lactiferous ducts just deep to the areola that act as milk reservoirs during lactation. They are duct dilations, not vessels, glandular acini, lymph nodes, or terminal ductules.'),
('ac468297-8037-5459-82ec-db2475d09f91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: Cooper''s ligaments:?', '["Nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrous bands supporting breast skin to pectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous bands supporting breast skin to pectoral fascia', 'Cooper''s (suspensory) ligaments are fibrous bands that tether the skin of the breast to the pectoral fascia; their shortening produces skin pitting in breast carcinoma. They are connective tissue, not nerves, vessels, muscle, or lymphatics.'),
('3e6df3ad-7418-52a4-99d4-e7a33654106f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: axillary tail of Spence:?', '["Axillary muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood vessel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Projection of breast tissue into axilla","Nerve plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymph node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Projection of breast tissue into axilla', 'The axillary tail of Spence is a tongue of glandular breast tissue that extends upward into the axilla. It is breast parenchyma, not a muscle, vessel, nerve plexus, or lymph node.'),
('f3ecea18-bb56-57ad-b4da-33c737d94794'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: breast blood supply:?', '["Only lateral thoracic","Only subclavian Only intercostal","Internal thoracic","lateral thoracic intercostal","thoracoacromial Only internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic', 'The breast is supplied chiefly by the internal thoracic (internal mammary) artery, with additional contributions from the lateral thoracic and intercostal arteries. Naming only a single lesser vessel or omitting the internal thoracic describes the principal supply incorrectly.'),
('24de7bb5-d9f5-5bf1-ba4d-ba55b3f137a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Breast lymphatics primarily to:'' is:', '["Axillary nodes (75%)","Parasternal nodes","Contralateral","Supraclavicular","Subdiaphragmatic"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nodes (75%)', 'About 75% of breast lymph drains to the axillary lymph nodes, chiefly the anterior (pectoral) group. Parasternal, supraclavicular, contralateral, and subdiaphragmatic routes drain a much smaller fraction of the gland.'),
('c64e74b8-5c7a-507c-b54b-713304b67131'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: axillary lymph node groups:?', '["4 groups","2 groups 6 groups","5: anterior posterior","lateral central","apical 3 groups"]'::jsonb, '5: anterior posterior', 'The axillary lymph nodes are classically arranged in five groups: anterior (pectoral), posterior (subscapular), lateral, central, and apical. Any other number of groups misstates the standard arrangement.'),
('ed709037-b694-52f7-a83f-389ba3e3daaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: pectoral (anterior) axillary nodes:?', '["Apex of axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Along subscapular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along axillary vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Central fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Along lateral thoracic vessels at lower border of pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Along lateral thoracic vessels at lower border of pectoralis minor', 'The pectoral (anterior) axillary nodes lie along the lateral thoracic vessels at the lower border of pectoralis minor. The apex, subscapular region, axillary vein, and central fat house the other named axillary nodal groups.'),
('63ff0776-9628-5a11-89b6-8862d1c038a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: apical axillary nodes:?', '["Central axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior axilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above upper border of pectoralis minor near axillary vein apex","Chest wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral axilla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above upper border of pectoralis minor near axillary vein apex', 'The apical axillary nodes sit above the upper border of pectoralis minor, near the axillary vein at the apex of the axilla. Central, posterior, lateral, or chest-wall positions describe the other nodal groups.'),
('4480cb50-9df6-5bf3-bfcb-a4f3e8db77d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: Rotter''s nodes:?', '["Interpectoral nodes between pectoralis major and minor","Parasternal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Along internal thoracic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supraclavicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interpectoral nodes between pectoralis major and minor', 'Rotter''s nodes are interpectoral lymph nodes situated between pectoralis major and minor. Parasternal, axillary apex, internal-thoracic, or supraclavicular locations belong to different nodal chains.'),
('602f2798-13f6-5223-ab4c-65293b242db7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: internal mammary nodes drain:?', '["Axilla","Medial breast","anterior chest wall","upper rectus sheath","Lateral breast Axillary tail Nipple only"]'::jsonb, 'Medial breast', 'The internal mammary (parasternal) nodes drain the medial half of the breast as well as the anterior chest wall and upper rectus sheath. The lateral breast and axillary tail drain to the axillary nodes, so ''axilla'' alone is wrong and ''nipple only'' is incomplete.'),
('8d45f46a-e59b-5023-98bb-1dcb9173652e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Nipple at level of:'' is:', '["3rd costal cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","4th intercostal space / 4th rib in males","6th rib mediated by specific receptor activation","5th rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2nd rib leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4th intercostal space / 4th rib in males', 'In the male the nipple lies at the fourth intercostal space, frequently stated opposite the fourth rib. The other levels are too high or too low for this standard surface landmark.'),
('822294c8-1cc7-574e-b0fb-56c56e29d2f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: areola contains:?', '["Sweat glands only resulting in decreased functional activity","Hair follicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymph nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Modified sebaceous glands (Montgomery) and smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Modified sebaceous glands (Montgomery) and smooth muscle', 'The areola contains modified sebaceous (Montgomery) glands and smooth muscle fibers that contract to raise the nipple. Sweat glands alone, hair follicles, lymph nodes, or bone are not its essential contents.'),
('7ef4e853-8bf5-5e7a-a652-35f4f8ba3c2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: pectoralis major origin:?', '["Ribs only","Sternum only","Clavicle medial half sternum","costal cartilages 1-6 external oblique aponeurosis","Clavicle only Scapula only"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle medial half sternum', 'Pectoralis major arises from the medial half of the clavicle, the anterior surface of the sternum, the upper costal cartilages, and the external oblique aponeurosis. An origin from ribs only, sternum only, clavicle plus scapula, or a listing without the sternal component omits parts of the true attachment.'),
('c6874055-6ed1-56d2-9cef-1288f54d59c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: pectoralis major insertion:?', '["Crest of greater tubercle of humerus (lateral lip)","Lesser tubercle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial humerus mediated by specific receptor activation","Coracoid process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crest of greater tubercle of humerus (lateral lip)', 'Pectoralis major inserts on the crest of the greater tubercle of the humerus, the lateral lip of the bicipital groove. The lesser tubercle, medial humerus, coracoid process, and clavicle are insertion sites of other muscles.'),
('35e37295-00d4-5e44-99c8-8390cd10b696'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: pectoralis major nerve supply:?', '["Suprascapular resulting in decreased functional activity","Long thoracic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracodorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1)', 'Pectoralis major is innervated by both the medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1). Suprascapular, long thoracic, axillary, and thoracodorsal nerves supply other thoracic and shoulder muscles.'),
('24df8d2e-1e59-5d05-85b6-10c61c6a8530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: pectoralis minor nerve supply:?', '["Medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1)","Long thoracic","Axillary","Thoracodorsal","Lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1)', 'Pectoralis minor is innervated by the medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1), which perforates the muscle. The lateral pectoral, long thoracic, axillary, and thoracodorsal nerves do not supply it.'),
('3ab05a65-7c9b-5cf4-8d8a-40023e3b61a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Serratus anterior nerve supply:'' is:', '["Axillary","Suprascapular","Thoracodorsal","Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7)","Medial pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7)', 'Serratus anterior is innervated by the long thoracic nerve of Bell (C5-C7). Axillary, suprascapular, thoracodorsal, and medial pectoral nerves serve other muscles, not serratus anterior.'),
('c1c31cf6-72c7-5dc2-94bc-09f18d13bd56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: winging of scapula from injury of:?', '["Suprascapular resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracodorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior)', 'Winging of the scapula follows paralysis of serratus anterior after injury to the long thoracic nerve, so the medial border lifts away from the chest wall when pushing against a wall. Suprascapular, axillary, spinal accessory, and thoracodorsal nerve lesions produce different deficits.'),
('c056bf52-c541-5e59-be11-9b9d970e390c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: clavipectoral fascia pierced by:?', '["Brachial plexus","Cephalic vein","thoracoacromial artery lateral pectoral nerve","lymphatics Axillary vein","Only thoracoacromial Only cephalic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic vein', 'The clavipectoral fascia is pierced by the cephalic vein, the thoracoacromial artery, the lateral pectoral nerve, and lymphatics. The cephalic vein is one such structure, whereas the brachial plexus and axillary vein lie elsewhere and do not pierce this fascia.'),
('7cc8c28c-6cd7-5786-b78d-802a01560114'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: thoracic wall nerve supply:?', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only accessory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only phrenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and subcostal (T12)","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and subcostal (T12)', 'The thoracic wall receives its segmental somatic innervation from the intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and the subcostal nerve (T12). The vagus, spinal accessory, phrenic, or sympathetic-only supply do not provide this segmental innervation.'),
('4251ed86-3141-504b-9709-e0953a26aba7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: diaphragm nerve supply:?', '["Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)","Intercostal","Vagus","Long thoracic","Sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)', 'The diaphragm receives its sole motor supply from the phrenic nerve (C3-C5). Intercostal, vagus, long thoracic, and sympathetic nerves do not provide its motor innervation.'),
('0e991595-def0-527f-8d82-a83252058778'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: phrenic nerve arises from:?', '["C4-C6","C3","C4","C5 (mainly C4)","C1-C3 C2-C4 C5-C7"]'::jsonb, 'C3', 'The phrenic nerve is formed from the C3, C4, and C5 roots, with C4 usually contributing the chief outflow. Among the choices, the single C3 root is one of its contributing levels, whereas the other ranges or single levels do not state the nerve''s true origin.'),
('0bf0d603-8a10-53ca-8cd5-b101c7404071'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''The breast extends from which ribs vertically'' is:', '["Rib 2 to rib 6","Rib 2 to rib 5","Rib 1 to rib 4","Rib 3 to rib 7","Rib 1 to rib 3"]'::jsonb, 'Rib 2 to rib 6', 'The breast extends vertically from the second to the sixth rib, overlying the pectoral muscles. Spans of 2-5, 1-4, 3-7, or 1-3 ribs do not match the classic vertical extent.'),
('908a03f2-ddd7-5cc5-8a1b-c3264e560536'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the thoracic cage consists of:?', '["Only ribs and sternum","The clavicles and scapulae","Only vertebrae and ribs","Only the sternum","12 thoracic vertebrae"]'::jsonb, '12 thoracic vertebrae', 'The thoracic cage is formed by the sternum, twelve pairs of ribs with their costal cartilages, and the twelve thoracic vertebrae. Statements omitting the sternum or vertebrae, or adding the clavicles and scapulae, do not describe the cage; the option naming the 12 thoracic vertebrae identifies one of its essential elements.'),
('fc419197-30be-54dc-a055-5b20f885fd29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: the sternum consists of:?', '["Manubrium and body only","Body and xiphoid only","Four parts","One single bone","Manubrium"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium', 'The sternum is composed of the manubrium, body, and xiphoid process; the option naming the manubrium identifies one of these parts. The other choices list only two parts, an incorrect number of parts, or describe it as a single undivided bone.'),
('5f2fae90-7ad8-5de0-b176-2e81b2e9228d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: the sternal angle (angle of Louis) is:?', '["At the junction of manubrium and body of sternum","level T4/T5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the manubrial notch mediated by specific receptor activation","At T12 level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At T1 level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the junction of manubrium and body of sternum', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) is the ridge at the junction of the manubrium and body of the sternum, lying at the level of the second costal cartilage and the T4/T5 intervertebral disc. The manubrial notch, T1, or T12 levels are not sites of this landmark.'),
('1e3138cf-2c9e-577c-857e-28405cd1ddfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the xiphoid process:?', '["Is the smallest part of sternum","may be bifid","ossifies later in life","Articulates with all ribs","Is the largest part of sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Is the smallest part of sternum', 'The sternum has three parts: manubrium, body and xiphoid process, with the xiphoid as the smallest inferior segment. The manubrium is the largest part; the xiphoid never articulates with any rib.'),
('ae9b458b-157e-57d9-942a-08822dc829c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the first rib is:?', '["The rib that attaches to T12","The shortest","broadest","most curved rib with a scalene tubercle","The most mobile rib"]'::jsonb, 'The shortest', 'The first rib is the shortest, broadest and most curved of the ribs. Its scalene tubercle marks the attachment of the anterior scalene muscle.'),
('4ebc271f-ad2a-5757-b241-0898dcf9845a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''The scalene tubercle on the 1st rib is:'' is:', '["For middle scalene","For serratus anterior","For attachment of anterior scalene muscle","For posterior scalene","For subclavius muscle"]'::jsonb, 'For attachment of anterior scalene muscle', 'The scalene tubercle projects from the upper surface of the first rib and gives attachment to the anterior scalene muscle. The middle and posterior scalenes attach elsewhere, as do subclavius and serratus anterior.'),
('5aef070a-0b79-56d7-b9ed-32e9df274b5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the intercostal spaces are:?', '["The spaces between adjacent ribs","occupied by intercostal muscles","vessels","and nerves","The spaces in the mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'The spaces between adjacent ribs', 'Intercostal spaces are the gaps between adjacent ribs, filled by the three intercostal muscle layers together with intercostal vessels and nerves. The mediastinum lies between the pleurae, not among the ribs.'),
('f1db476b-8a2d-533b-9252-cf5bf82aaa76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: intercostal nerves are:?', '["Posterior rami of thoracic nerves","Branches of the brachial plexus","Cervical nerves","Sacral nerves","Anterior rami of T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of spinal nerves T1-T11, each running in its intercostal space. Posterior rami supply the deep back muscles and are not intercostal nerves.'),
('11ae290f-356c-5673-8483-a8ad0dc1197b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: the neurovascular bundle of an intercostal space runs:?', '["Deep to the pleura","In the costal groove","between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles","Superficial to the intercostal muscles","Between the skin and muscle"]'::jsonb, 'In the costal groove', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove on the lower border of each rib, between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. This position protects the bundle during needling of the chest wall.'),
('1fa6991a-f7b7-513a-8465-1c2df3047ac1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the intercostal artery arises from:?', '["Facial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thoracic aorta (for posterior intercostals) and internal thoracic artery (for anterior intercostals)","External carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic aorta (for posterior intercostals) and internal thoracic artery (for anterior intercostals)', 'Posterior intercostal arteries arise directly from the thoracic aorta, while the anterior intercostal arteries are branches of the internal thoracic artery. The facial, vertebral and external carotid arteries are unrelated to the chest wall.'),
('be89ab94-6789-5845-9086-3d9e9103c298'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the internal thoracic artery gives off:?', '["Lumbar arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior intercostal arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior intercostal branches and the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries","Bronchial arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior intercostal branches and the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries', 'The internal thoracic artery gives off anterior intercostal branches and ends by dividing into the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries. Posterior intercostals and bronchial arteries come from the aorta.'),
('b41e87a5-d637-54d0-9d71-addc9e7aa8fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''The intercostal vein drains into:'' is:', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior vena cava directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior vena cava mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Azygos/hemiazygos veins (posteriorly) and internal thoracic veins (anteriorly)"]'::jsonb, 'Azygos/hemiazygos veins (posteriorly) and internal thoracic veins (anteriorly)', 'Posterior intercostal veins drain into the azygos and hemiazygos systems, whereas the anterior intercostal veins drain into the internal thoracic veins. The coronary sinus and pulmonary veins receive cardiac and pulmonary blood, not intercostal venous blood.'),
('602c376f-2914-5137-84d6-38502d9b7570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the serratus anterior muscle:?', '["Protracts the scapula (punching muscle)","supplied by long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Elevates the scapula","Rotates the scapula","Supplies the arm"]'::jsonb, 'Protracts the scapula (punching muscle)', 'Serratus anterior protracts the scapula and is the ''boxer''s muscle'' used in punching; it is supplied by the long thoracic nerve (C5-C7). Scapular elevation is performed by levator scapulae and the upper trapezius.'),
('acfbc27e-95e7-52e3-bcba-f0b21318a852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: the pectoralis major muscle:?', '["Flexes the elbow","Extends the arm","Abducts the arm","Supinates the forearm","Adducts and medially rotates the arm"]'::jsonb, 'Adducts and medially rotates the arm', 'Pectoralis major adducts and medially rotates the humerus and assists deep inspiration. It neither flexes the elbow nor supinates the forearm.'),
('6375059c-eef2-56e6-b15e-f5c102aee4c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: the pectoralis minor muscle:?', '["Elevates the shoulder","Adducts the arm","Depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula","supplied by medial pectoral nerve","Rotates the trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula', 'Pectoralis minor depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula and is supplied by the medial pectoral nerve. It does not elevate the shoulder or adduct the arm, which are functions of other muscles.'),
('26d07a5b-fd57-5198-ab3d-f1793f34a9e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the clavicle is:?', '["A sesamoid bone resulting in decreased functional activity","A flat bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not a long bone mediated by specific receptor activation","The most commonly fractured bone in the body","A short bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most commonly fractured bone in the body', 'The clavicle is the most commonly fractured bone, usually at the junction of its middle and lateral thirds. It is classified as a long bone despite its shape.'),
('92d60b1c-7a6e-5460-a25c-0ab46c72bebf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the manubriosternal joint is:?', '["A hinge joint resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibrous joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A synovial joint mediated by specific receptor activation","A secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis)","A primary cartilaginous joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis)', 'The manubriosternal joint is a secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis) united by fibrocartilage. Synovial and fibrous classifications do not apply to this joint.'),
('71d43eef-bf58-5a8b-a535-2fda9f74a863'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''The costovertebral joints are:'' is:', '["Fibrous joints resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilaginous joints accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Synovial joints between the ribs and vertebrae","Hinge joints without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sutures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial joints between the ribs and vertebrae', 'The costovertebral joints are synovial articulations between the heads of the ribs and the vertebral bodies. They permit the gliding movements needed for rib elevation during breathing.'),
('556e7e60-c4b0-561d-b1c6-f7b1a5b12ad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the costotransverse joints are:?', '["Synovial joints between the tubercle of rib and the transverse process of vertebra","Synarthroses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Symphyses mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous joints without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilaginous joints leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial joints between the tubercle of rib and the transverse process of vertebra', 'The costotransverse joint is a synovial articulation between the tubercle of a rib and the transverse process of its own vertebra. Fibrous joints, symphyses and synarthroses do not describe this articulation.'),
('0fb763be-4e48-548a-9834-8e1a91097a1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: the subcostal nerve (t12):?', '["Is part of the lumbar plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","Runs below the 12th rib and supplies the lower abdominal wall","Is a branch of the brachial plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","Supplies the diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a cervical nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Runs below the 12th rib and supplies the lower abdominal wall', 'The subcostal nerve is the anterior ramus of T12, passing below the 12th rib to supply the lower abdominal wall and skin. It is neither part of the lumbar or brachial plexus nor a nerve of the diaphragm.'),
('dac37f25-89ca-52c8-8700-49a2893ed1f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: the diaphragm originates from:?', '["Xiphoid process","costal cartilages of ribs 7-12","and lumbar vertebrae","Only the xiphoid","Only the vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Xiphoid process', 'The diaphragm arises from the xiphoid process, the costal cartilages of ribs 7-12 and the lumbar vertebrae. Of the options given, the xiphoid process is a genuine single origin (the full origin includes all three sites).'),
('2ad2a1fb-ee97-5e28-be05-eac8e1e64d61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the central tendon of the diaphragm is:?', '["The aponeurotic insertion of the diaphragm","fused with fibrous pericardium","The caval opening","The aortic opening","The esophageal opening"]'::jsonb, 'The aponeurotic insertion of the diaphragm', 'The central tendon is the aponeurotic insertion into which the diaphragmatic muscle fibers converge. The caval, esophageal and aortic openings pierce it rather than forming its substance.'),
('55ebd77b-116c-574b-9c66-33aaf66c0ea6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the aortic hiatus (t12) transmits:?', '["Sympathetic trunk","Aorta","thoracic duct","and azygos/hemiazygos veins","Esophagus and vagus nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta', 'The aortic hiatus at T12 transmits the aorta together with the thoracic duct and azygos/hemiazygos veins. The aorta is a genuine component of this hiatus.'),
('b695ede5-a046-5547-a831-21d45e5f72f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''The esophageal hiatus (T10) transmits:'' is:', '["IVC and phrenic nerve","Esophagus","vagus nerves (anterior and posterior)","esophageal vessels","Only the esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10 transmits the esophagus along with the vagus nerves and esophageal vessels. The esophagus is the principal structure in this hiatus.'),
('5bc59284-c8a8-5047-bff6-7bef565eee45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the caval opening (t8) transmits:?', '["The phrenic nerve alone","Aorta and thoracic duct","IVC and right phrenic nerve","Esophagus and vagus","Only the IVC"]'::jsonb, 'IVC and right phrenic nerve', 'The caval opening at T8 transmits the inferior vena cava and the right phrenic nerve. The left phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm separately, and the aortic and esophageal hiatuses carry other structures.'),
('ad7701e5-2ffc-58c4-8e58-1723b4f66c14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: the internal intercostal muscles function in:?', '["Compression of abdomen","Flexion of trunk","Forced expiration (depress ribs)","Elevation of scapula","Inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiration (depress ribs)', 'The internal intercostal muscles depress the ribs and therefore assist forced expiration. The external intercostals, by contrast, elevate the ribs during inspiration.'),
('91d94a63-4247-5552-920e-66c8e232bfe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: the thoracic wall consists of:?', '["Ribs and intercostal muscles only","Ribs and sternum only","Sternum and clavicle","Thoracic vertebrae and ribs","Ribs, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, intercostal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, intercostal muscles', 'The thoracic wall is composed of the 12 pairs of ribs, the sternum, the thoracic vertebrae and the intercostal muscles. Options listing only ribs and sternum or vertebrae alone omit major components.'),
('5c22fdc1-98c7-579d-beab-fdccf95ae980'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the true ribs are:?', '["Ribs 1-5 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 11-12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 1-7 attaching directly to sternum","Ribs 1-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ribs 8-10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 1-7 attaching directly to sternum', 'True ribs are ribs 1-7, each attaching directly to the sternum through its own costal cartilage. Ribs 8-10 are false ribs and ribs 11-12 are floating.'),
('0d1d110e-c264-5e22-bee2-f6e63047f3e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the false ribs are:?', '["Ribs 6-10 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 1-7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 11-12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 8-10 (attach via costal cartilage)","Ribs 8-12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 8-10 (attach via costal cartilage)', 'False ribs are ribs 8-10, whose costal cartilages join the cartilage of rib 7 and so attach to the sternum only indirectly. Ribs 11-12 have no anterior attachment and are called floating.'),
('f1ee81cc-0a97-58f0-b86a-f831e78e6c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''The floating ribs are:'' is:', '["Ribs 8-10 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 1-7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 10-12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 9-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ribs 11-12 (no anterior attachment)"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 11-12 (no anterior attachment)', 'Floating ribs are ribs 11-12, whose costal cartilages end in the musculature of the abdominal wall with no anterior attachment. True and false ribs both connect to the sternum directly or indirectly.'),
('37e3b7db-55a8-599f-8919-a32bc3d81708'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the intercostal nerves are branches of:?', '["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Thoracic sympathetic chain","Anterior rami of T1-T11 spinal nerves","Posterior rami of spinal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of T1-T11 spinal nerves', 'Intercostal nerves arise as the anterior rami of spinal nerves T1-T11. Posterior rami supply the deep back and related skin rather than the intercostal spaces.'),
('db640e8c-7cfc-5340-860d-9a2db716672b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: the breast is located:?', '["Over pectoralis minor","Over serratus anterior","Over pectoralis major, between ribs 2-6","Between ribs 1-4","Between ribs 4-8"]'::jsonb, 'Over pectoralis major, between ribs 2-6', 'The breast extends vertically from the 2nd to 6th rib and lies superficial to the deep pectoral fascia over pectoralis major. Its tail of Spence extends superolaterally toward the axilla.'),
('6c8df913-e0e3-5d04-94d9-ac7cf1d1d986'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: the axillary tail of spence is:?', '["A vein draining the breast","A projection of breast tissue into axilla","A lymph node in the axilla","An artery supplying the breast","A nerve innervating the breast"]'::jsonb, 'A projection of breast tissue into axilla', 'The axillary tail of Spence is a tongue of breast tissue that extends superolaterally into the axilla. It must be examined specially and can contain tumor deposits.'),
('d4f0bd67-e334-591d-b8f3-777b6b0d1451'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: lymphatic drainage of the breast primarily goes to:?', '["Cervical nodes","Mediastinal nodes","Axillary lymph nodes","Subclavian nodes","Parasternal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary lymph nodes', 'Most of the breast, especially the lateral quadrants, drains to the axillary lymph nodes, initially the anterior (pectoral) group. The parasternal and cervical nodes drain only smaller, medial portions.'),
('75b06ac4-39f9-5fcc-854d-1f1b75d15e17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: cooper ligaments of the breast:?', '["Glandular tissue of breast","Nerves of breast","Blood vessels of breast","Suspensory ligaments supporting breast","Lymphatics of breast"]'::jsonb, 'Suspensory ligaments supporting breast', 'Cooper ligaments are fibrous suspensory ligaments connecting the breast tissue to the skin and underlying fascia. Their shortening by tumor infiltration produces skin retraction, and they do not form gland, nerve or vessel tissue.'),
('05e72f4b-21f7-5926-b75c-7d61e7260097'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which option correctly describes the intercostal nerve:', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of thoracic spinal nerves T1-T11. Sympathetic, splanchnic, posterior ramus and vagus do not form the intercostal nerves.'),
('1dd6c60a-a5d5-515f-a61d-f453a9e9f86c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the intercostal nerve is?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of thoracic spinal nerves T1-T11, one running with the vessels in each intercostal space. Splanchnic and sympathetic nerves are visceral, not intercostal.'),
('63c1697a-7ad5-5815-9838-7f79f7f579ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which is the most accurate statement about thoracic wall and breast: the intercostal nerve is?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of thoracic spinal nerves T1-T11. Posterior rami, splanchnic and vagus nerves are separate pathways, not intercostal nerves.'),
('2bc499c2-27dd-5166-b8a4-9e65382a05e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, identify the correct response: the intercostal nerve is?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Each intercostal nerve is the anterior ramus of a thoracic spinal nerve (T1-T11) running in the intercostal space. Sympathetic and splanchnic fibers travel through the thoracic region but are not intercostal nerves.'),
('ff5bbfbb-b80c-5b6b-b9b2-abab8c2b454c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the intercostal nerve is?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of spinal nerves T1-T11. The other listed structures are visceral or posterior pathways rather than intercostal nerves.'),
('74b5b093-b71a-598a-86a5-7574cb8c114b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the intercostal nerve is?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of thoracic spinal nerves T1-T11 that supply the thoracic wall and abdomen. Vagal, sympathetic, splanchnic and posterior ramus options are incorrect.'),
('9a532d91-9cf5-5d4a-8ca7-9ae69efe7f58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Thoracic cage consists of:'' is:', '["12 thoracic vertebrae","12 pairs of ribs","costal cartilages sternum","12 vertebrae only Sternum + ribs only","Clavicles + scapulae Ribs + costal cartilages only"]'::jsonb, '12 thoracic vertebrae', 'The thoracic cage is formed by the 12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs, costal cartilages and sternum. Among the choices, 12 thoracic vertebrae is a genuine component, though the full cage includes ribs, cartilages and sternum as well.'),
('d329e3a3-51b3-565b-a919-e3a7124f518f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: sternum parts:?', '["Manubrium only","Manubrium + body only","Body + xiphoid only","Manubrium + gladiolus","Manubrium body xiphoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium body xiphoid process', 'The sternum has three parts: the manubrium, the body (gladiolus) and the xiphoid process. This option alone names all three components.'),
('f8871d67-3fd0-5d4d-8ca6-5d6708eeb741'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: sternal angle (louis) at:?', '["Xiphisternal junction","Body-xiphoid junction","Manubriosternal junction","T4-T5","Suprasternal notch Sternoclavicular joint"]'::jsonb, 'Manubriosternal junction', 'The sternal angle of Louis lies at the manubriosternal junction, level with the 2nd costal cartilage and the T4/T5 intervertebral disc. It is the landmark used for counting ribs.'),
('8dfd5a41-fc05-5e25-9539-e834bc3759de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: sternal angle landmarks:?', '["2nd rib","tracheal bifurcation","aortic arch ends azygos arch","Xiphoid 1st rib","3rd rib Sternal notch"]'::jsonb, '2nd rib', 'The sternal angle marks the junction with the 2nd costal cartilage, making the 2nd rib the counting landmark. It also approximates the tracheal bifurcation and the end of the aortic arch.'),
('0fe4c425-ea2b-5592-a2e6-640c489c8035'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: true ribs (1-7):?', '["Attached directly to sternum via costal cartilage","Attached to sternum indirectly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Floating mediated by specific receptor activation","anterior attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atypical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Attached directly to sternum via costal cartilage', 'True ribs (1-7) each attach directly to the sternum through its own costal cartilage. False ribs (8-10) attach indirectly and floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment.'),
('60880316-f51e-5ce2-9a8d-b172e68a2309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: false ribs (8-10):?', '["Floating resulting in decreased functional activity","attachment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct to sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","Free without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attached to costal cartilage of rib 7"]'::jsonb, 'Attached to costal cartilage of rib 7', 'False ribs (8-10) do not reach the sternum directly; their costal cartilages join the cartilage of rib 7. Free and floating ribs have no anterior attachment, and none of the false ribs is direct to the sternum.'),
('11d347c5-c0c0-5b92-9242-6d3dfa365b96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Floating ribs (11-12):'' is:', '["Attach to vertebra only","Attach to xiphoid","Direct to sternum","Attach to rib 10","No anterior attachment"]'::jsonb, 'No anterior attachment', 'Floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment; their costal cartilages end in the body wall. They attach posteriorly to the vertebrae but not to the sternum or other ribs.'),
('ad12fd52-51e7-57c5-97e0-8c6553a7976f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: a typical rib has:', '["Head","neck","tubercle","angle","shaft costal groove"]'::jsonb, 'Head', 'A typical rib bears a head, neck, tubercle, angle and a shaft with a costal groove. The head is one of these standard features, though the full description includes all of them.'),
('f7f401ae-266b-59c5-b786-09d11045658b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: typical rib head articulates with:?', '["Only vertebra above resulting in decreased functional activity","Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)","Only own vertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","Sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)', 'The head of a typical rib has two facets that articulate with the vertebral bodies of its own vertebra and the vertebra above. Only the 1st, 11th and 12th ribs have single facets.'),
('67cb5f1c-919f-53d5-a23c-76ff609c9f2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: rib tubercle articulates with:?', '["Sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Costal cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertebra above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process of same vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse process of same vertebra', 'The tubercle of a rib articulates with the transverse process of the same-numbered vertebra at the costotransverse joint. The vertebral body, sternum and other vertebrae are not correct sites.'),
('2cbc1f7f-bb40-5f29-ad7e-a481a6225b8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: costal groove contains:?', '["Only vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Only artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)', 'The costal groove on the lower border of a rib contains the intercostal neurovascular bundle, arranged vein, artery, nerve from above downward. The bundle is more than a single vessel or nerve.'),
('bfd8c694-20b6-5752-af58-cae22cd4606a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: first rib atypical:?', '["Two facets","Single facet","scalene tubercle","subclavian grooves","Longest costal groove tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Single facet', 'The first rib is atypical because its head has a single facet for articulation with the body of T1 only, unlike the typical ribs with two facets. It also bears the scalene tubercle and subclavian grooves.'),
('2788a8f9-1ee9-5bd4-a40a-1814d4bce98b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Scalene tubercle on first rib for:'' is:', '["Scalenus medius","SCM","Scalenus anterior","Levator scapulae","Scalenus posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenus anterior', 'The scalene tubercle on the inner border of the first rib marks the insertion of scalenus anterior. Scalenus medius and posterior attach behind the subclavian groove, while SCM and levator scapulae do not attach to the first rib.'),
('ec37f093-7ebe-5e32-95a3-f3c66e3b0133'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: intercostal spaces:?', '["11 spaces between 12 ribs","9 spaces","12 spaces","10 spaces","8 spaces"]'::jsonb, '11 spaces between 12 ribs', 'There are 12 pairs of ribs, and the spaces between adjacent ribs total 11. The space below the 12th rib is the subcostal space, not an intercostal space.'),
('ac597a27-ee25-5a90-b574-6d4accd07f61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: intercostal muscle layers:?', '["2: external + internal resulting in decreased functional activity","4 layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 layer mediated by specific receptor activation","muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3: external internal innermost"]'::jsonb, '3: external internal innermost', 'The intercostal muscles form three layers: external, internal, and innermost intercostal. The innermost layer is often incomplete, and it is separated from the internal layer by the intercostal neurovascular bundle.'),
('9fec09f1-f626-567a-863c-98088112d0bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: external intercostal fibers run:?', '["Vertically resulting in decreased functional activity","Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)","Circularly mediated by specific receptor activation","Downward and backward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horizontally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)', 'The external intercostal fibers run obliquely downward and forward, the direction of ''hands in pockets'', and they elevate the ribs during inspiration. The other directions describe different muscle layers.'),
('89896276-9696-5911-9a67-b90643e93e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: internal intercostal fibers run:?', '["Vertically","Downward and forward","Parallel to ribs","Horizontally","Obliquely downward and backward"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and backward', 'The internal intercostal fibers run obliquely downward and backward, perpendicular to the external layer. They depress the ribs during forced expiration.'),
('c02ce285-a3bd-50a7-8b48-a8f0dccc6694'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: innermost intercostal:?', '["Part of diaphragm","Same as internal","External to internal","Deepest layer","separated from internal by neurovascular bundle Absent"]'::jsonb, 'Deepest layer', 'The innermost intercostal is the deepest of the three intercostal muscle layers, lying internal to the intercostal neurovascular bundle. The bundle separates it from the internal intercostal muscle.'),
('35403ada-3ec7-5eed-82a7-cd9bd1ff90bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Intercostal nerve is:'' is:', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of the T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves. The anterior ramus of T12 forms the subcostal nerve rather than an intercostal nerve.'),
('a4867c79-56e2-540b-b7e2-64bddf06ad7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: intercostal bundle runs:?', '["Above ribs resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep to innermost accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between internal and innermost intercostals in costal groove","Below ribs externally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial to external leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between internal and innermost intercostals in costal groove', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles, protected by the lower border of the rib. Intercostal procedures are therefore performed above the rib below.'),
('9ee852ab-56bc-5421-a790-32347a4b9de8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: van order (superior to inferior):?', '["Vein","Nerve","Artery","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve', 'In the costal groove the neurovascular bundle is arranged as vein superiorly, artery below it, and nerve most inferiorly, the mnemonic VAN. The nerve is therefore the lowest structure in the bundle.'),
('5f5f0cba-bddd-52ee-a084-91ed7bd6a9ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: anterior intercostal artery branch of:?', '["Subclavian","Aorta","Posterior intercostal","Internal thoracic artery","Axillary"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The anterior intercostal arteries of the upper six spaces arise from the internal thoracic artery, and the lower spaces from its musculophrenic branch. Posterior intercostal arteries come from the aorta or the costocervical trunk.'),
('526a1f03-864e-5449-9d42-a3b5e259a678'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: posterior intercostal artery branch of:?', '["Internal thoracic resulting in decreased functional activity","Costocervical trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta (lower 9) and subclavian (upper 2 via superior intercostal)","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (lower 9) and subclavian (upper 2 via superior intercostal)', 'The posterior intercostal arteries of the upper two spaces arise from the superior intercostal branch of the costocervical trunk of the subclavian artery, while the lower nine arise directly from the descending aorta.'),
('c5c39f27-4aca-5fa0-bd07-93d98cd36cb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: internal thoracic artery branch of:?', '["External carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian artery (first part)","Brachiocephalic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subclavian artery (first part)', 'The internal thoracic artery arises from the first part of the subclavian artery. It descends lateral to the sternum and is an important graft in coronary bypass surgery.'),
('2b3cb463-0cdf-595f-9b2f-d5036b0ec2f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Internal thoracic artery runs:'' is:', '["Axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Midline accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral to nipple mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 cm lateral to sternum between pleura and costal cartilages"]'::jsonb, '1 cm lateral to sternum between pleura and costal cartilages', 'The internal thoracic artery descends vertically about 1 cm lateral to the sternum, between the parietal pleura and the costal cartilages. This protected course makes it a suitable conduit for coronary bypass grafting.'),
('9fe129bb-2fb7-528b-8b52-3c8f3a286901'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: internal thoracic terminates as:?', '["Anterior intercostals resulting in decreased functional activity","Mediastinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sternal branches mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericardiacophrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries', 'At the level of the sixth costal cartilage the internal thoracic artery divides into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries. Its anterior intercostal, mediastinal, sternal, and pericardiacophrenic branches arise along its course.'),
('4d7362c5-10eb-5e8b-aa9d-7f615f0c2479'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: intercostal veins drain to:?', '["IJV resulting in decreased functional activity","Subclavian accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Azygos (right) and hemiazygos (left) systems","SVC directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Azygos (right) and hemiazygos (left) systems', 'The posterior intercostal veins drain on the right into the azygos vein and on the left into the hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins. These systems then empty into the superior vena cava.'),
('5b4b653f-fa01-58b6-aa50-4ef2c7afff33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: breast located:?', '["Over clavicle resulting in decreased functional activity","Back accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","Axilla only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Over pectoral muscles 2nd-6th ribs sternum to midaxillary line"]'::jsonb, 'Over pectoral muscles 2nd-6th ribs sternum to midaxillary line', 'The breast overlies the pectoral muscles and extends vertically from the 2nd to the 6th rib and horizontally from the sternum to the midaxillary line. It also sends the axillary tail superolaterally into the axilla.'),
('9bc12683-3282-5a5a-a87a-8703819f59f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: breast composition:?', '["Single lobule resulting in decreased functional activity","lobules accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5-10 lobules mediated by specific receptor activation","15-20 lobules in fat and connective tissue","50-100 lobules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '15-20 lobules in fat and connective tissue', 'Each breast contains 15-20 lobules (lobes) embedded in fat and connective tissue. Each lobe drains through its own lactiferous duct to the nipple.'),
('b05dc5fd-aace-5659-9414-7002c3295192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: each lobule drains via:?', '["Multiple ducts","Lymphatics","Blood vessels","Single common duct","Lactiferous duct opening at nipple"]'::jsonb, 'Lactiferous duct opening at nipple', 'Each lobule (lobe) of the breast drains through a single lactiferous duct that opens at the nipple. Near the nipple each duct dilates into a lactiferous sinus.'),
('9ffbbfa1-bba4-585b-85c6-ac94de6acf09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Lactiferous sinuses:'' is:', '["Dilations of ducts near nipple (milk reservoirs)","Blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acini mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymph nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Terminal ductules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilations of ducts near nipple (milk reservoirs)', 'Lactiferous sinuses are the dilated segments of the lactiferous ducts just deep to the areola. During lactation they act as milk reservoirs.'),
('88923564-5c39-5e3d-9f60-9f743a4fc10e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: cooper''s ligaments:?', '["Nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrous bands supporting breast skin to pectoral fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous bands supporting breast skin to pectoral fascia', 'Cooper''s (suspensory) ligaments are fibrous bands that attach the breast skin and nipple to the pectoral fascia, supporting the breast. When invaded by carcinoma they shorten and cause skin dimpling.'),
('f7eedfc1-860f-5098-907d-a55a7901bba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: axillary tail of spence:?', '["Axillary muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood vessel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Projection of breast tissue into axilla","Nerve plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymph node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Projection of breast tissue into axilla', 'The axillary tail of Spence is the superolateral extension of breast tissue that passes into the axilla through the deep fascia. It may be mistaken for an axillary lymph node on clinical examination.'),
('ea05dff4-260a-5fad-9f1f-a562694b53c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: breast blood supply:?', '["Only lateral thoracic","Only subclavian Only intercostal","Internal thoracic","lateral thoracic intercostal","thoracoacromial Only internal thoracic"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic', 'Perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery form the principal supply of the medial part of the breast. The lateral part is supplied by the lateral thoracic artery and the posterior part by intercostal perforators.'),
('f745b625-212a-5b0d-9f8c-4f888dd91f64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: breast lymphatics primarily to:?', '["Axillary nodes (75%)","Parasternal nodes","Contralateral","Supraclavicular","Subdiaphragmatic"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary nodes (75%)', 'Approximately 75% of breast lymph drains to the axillary nodes, chiefly the pectoral (anterior) group. The medial breast drains instead to the internal mammary (parasternal) nodes.'),
('ef1ac3fa-f7fc-50a0-b580-ccc949a8eadb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: axillary lymph node groups:?', '["4 groups","2 groups 6 groups","5: anterior posterior","lateral central","apical 3 groups"]'::jsonb, '5: anterior posterior', 'The axillary lymph nodes form five groups: anterior (pectoral), posterior (subscapular), lateral, central, and apical. The anterior and posterior groups are the named groups among them.'),
('fc45227f-16dc-55b1-9fad-fb93db5f8c0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Pectoral (anterior) axillary nodes:'' is:', '["Apex of axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Along subscapular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along axillary vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Central fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Along lateral thoracic vessels at lower border of pectoralis minor"]'::jsonb, 'Along lateral thoracic vessels at lower border of pectoralis minor', 'The pectoral (anterior) axillary nodes lie along the lateral thoracic vessels at the lower border of the pectoralis minor. They are the main drainage site for most of the breast.'),
('cd7934bf-2961-5cd1-930b-c75020edfee6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: apical axillary nodes:?', '["Central axilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior axilla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above upper border of pectoralis minor near axillary vein apex","Chest wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral axilla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above upper border of pectoralis minor near axillary vein apex', 'The apical axillary nodes lie at the apex of the axilla, above the upper border of the pectoralis minor near the axillary vein. They receive lymph from all other axillary groups and drain into the subclavian trunk.'),
('090fb959-dcc7-5e7f-b693-19419533b07d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: rotter''s nodes:?', '["Interpectoral nodes between pectoralis major and minor","Parasternal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Along internal thoracic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supraclavicular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interpectoral nodes between pectoralis major and minor', 'Rotter''s nodes are interpectoral lymph nodes located between the pectoralis major and minor muscles. They may be involved in the spread of breast carcinoma.'),
('c22a210d-0761-54bf-b8cb-e054cc969a92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: internal mammary nodes drain:?', '["Axilla","Medial breast","anterior chest wall","upper rectus sheath","Lateral breast Axillary tail Nipple only"]'::jsonb, 'Medial breast', 'The internal mammary (parasternal) nodes drain the medial part of the breast and the anterior chest wall. The lateral breast drains mainly to the axillary nodes.'),
('5c41ae6c-c20a-58e8-a336-983a4579e046'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: nipple at level of:?', '["3rd costal cartilage resulting in decreased functional activity","4th intercostal space / 4th rib in males","6th rib mediated by specific receptor activation","5th rib without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2nd rib leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4th intercostal space / 4th rib in males', 'In the male the nipple lies at the level of the 4th intercostal space, corresponding to the 4th rib. In the female the position varies with breast size and ptosis.'),
('679749ab-d050-5292-8cbb-141131eacd4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: areola contains:?', '["Sweat glands only resulting in decreased functional activity","Hair follicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymph nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Modified sebaceous glands (Montgomery) and smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Modified sebaceous glands (Montgomery) and smooth muscle', 'The areola contains the areolar (Montgomery) glands, which are modified sebaceous glands, together with smooth muscle fibers. Contraction of this smooth muscle erects the nipple.'),
('7492e000-29ac-541d-86e3-b230580a5cee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Pectoralis major origin:'' is:', '["Ribs only","Sternum only","Clavicle medial half sternum","costal cartilages 1-6 external oblique aponeurosis","Clavicle only Scapula only"]'::jsonb, 'Clavicle medial half sternum', 'Pectoralis major arises from the medial half of the clavicle, the sternum, the costal cartilages 1-6, and the external oblique aponeurosis. The clavicular and sternocostal heads together form the muscle.'),
('f8c9e49e-38d5-5d26-ad3d-5490fbcdb634'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: pectoralis major insertion:?', '["Crest of greater tubercle of humerus (lateral lip)","Lesser tubercle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial humerus mediated by specific receptor activation","Coracoid process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clavicle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crest of greater tubercle of humerus (lateral lip)', 'Pectoralis major inserts into the crest of the greater tubercle of the humerus, the lateral lip of the intertubercular sulcus. Its tendon forms the anterior fold of the axilla.'),
('4a02135a-c168-5a25-93d0-11111e85a4c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: pectoralis major nerve supply:?', '["Suprascapular resulting in decreased functional activity","Long thoracic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracodorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1)"]'::jsonb, 'Medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1)', 'Pectoralis major is supplied by the medial and lateral pectoral nerves (C5-T1). The lateral pectoral nerve mainly supplies the clavicular head and the medial pectoral nerve the sternocostal head.'),
('ee6896ca-bafa-529d-9691-80794240daa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: pectoralis minor nerve supply:?', '["Medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1)","Long thoracic","Axillary","Thoracodorsal","Lateral pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1)', 'The pectoralis minor is supplied by the medial pectoral nerve (C8-T1), which pierces the muscle to reach the pectoralis major. The lateral pectoral nerve does not supply it.'),
('7e74c249-2178-5cac-949b-7df618b20d65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: serratus anterior nerve supply:?', '["Axillary","Suprascapular","Thoracodorsal","Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7)","Medial pectoral"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (C5-C7)', 'The serratus anterior is supplied by the long thoracic nerve (C5-C7), which descends on the superficial surface of the muscle. Injury to this nerve causes winging of the scapula.'),
('9592ef68-293d-5aea-a867-b70b519beeb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: winging of scapula from injury of:?', '["Suprascapular resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal accessory mediated by specific receptor activation","Thoracodorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior)"]'::jsonb, 'Long thoracic nerve (serratus anterior)', 'Paralysis of the serratus anterior from injury to the long thoracic nerve causes winging of the scapula, most evident when pushing against a wall. The other nerves do not supply the serratus anterior.'),
('08c322d0-2f77-5a2d-979d-26fd37b64ea8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Clavipectoral fascia pierced by:'' is:', '["Brachial plexus","Cephalic vein","thoracoacromial artery lateral pectoral nerve","lymphatics Axillary vein","Only thoracoacromial Only cephalic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalic vein', 'The clavipectoral fascia is pierced by the cephalic vein, the thoracoacromial artery, the lateral pectoral nerve, and lymphatics. The cephalic vein passes through it to join the axillary vein.'),
('9be3474e-11d6-56a8-bb6e-75b00b9ab13a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: thoracic wall nerve supply:?', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only accessory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only phrenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and subcostal (T12)","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves (T1-T11) and subcostal (T12)', 'The thoracic wall is innervated by the intercostal nerves, the anterior rami of T1-T11, and the subcostal nerve, the anterior ramus of T12. Each intercostal nerve supplies its corresponding segment of skin, muscle, and pleura.'),
('f2927b94-ed48-5d74-9602-806547ee63cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: diaphragm nerve supply:?', '["Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)","Intercostal","Vagus","Long thoracic","Sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5)', 'The diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerves, which arise from C3-C5. Its peripheral sensation is supplemented by the lower six intercostal nerves.'),
('d43ec8cd-ba2a-5e99-a5cc-ea2645567719'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: phrenic nerve arises from:?', '["C4-C6","C3","C4","C5 (mainly C4)","C1-C3 C2-C4 C5-C7"]'::jsonb, 'C4', 'The phrenic nerve arises from the anterior rami of C3, C4, and C5, with C4 contributing most of its fibers. C4 is therefore the principal root of the nerve.'),
('f9fe4849-2e59-57c4-987a-691dee5abaf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the breast extends from which ribs vertically?', '["Rib 2 to rib 6","Rib 2 to rib 5","Rib 1 to rib 4","Rib 3 to rib 7","Rib 1 to rib 3"]'::jsonb, 'Rib 2 to rib 6', 'The breast extends vertically from the 2nd to the 6th rib. It overlies the pectoralis major and spans from the sternum to the midaxillary line.'),
('d1764d95-9491-5fc5-aec9-d0e8b8edf39b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the thoracic cage consists of:?', '["Only ribs and sternum","The clavicles and scapulae","Only vertebrae and ribs","Only the sternum","12 thoracic vertebrae"]'::jsonb, '12 thoracic vertebrae', 'The thoracic cage is formed by the 12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs, and the sternum. A complete description must include all three components; the listed options name only single elements.'),
('10c1c240-024c-5ad7-9da9-61789b7271ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''The sternum consists of:'' is:', '["Manubrium and body only","Body and xiphoid only","Four parts","One single bone","Manubrium"]'::jsonb, 'One single bone', 'The sternum is a single flat bone composed of three parts: the manubrium, the body, and the xiphoid process. It develops as one bone rather than several separate bones.'),
('6509155f-5050-55b2-ac63-9093fe8fd9ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the sternal angle (angle of louis) is:?', '["At the junction of manubrium and body of sternum","level T4/T5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the manubrial notch mediated by specific receptor activation","At T12 level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At T1 level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the junction of manubrium and body of sternum', 'The sternal angle (angle of Louis) is the junction between the manubrium and the body of the sternum. It lies at the level of the 2nd costal cartilage and the T4-T5 intervertebral disc.'),
('e99c1d27-46d5-51d3-802b-7d846d4a5343'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which statement best fits the following fact: the xiphoid process:?', '["Is the smallest part of sternum","may be bifid","ossifies later in life","Articulates with all ribs","Is the largest part of sternum"]'::jsonb, 'Is the smallest part of sternum', 'The xiphoid process is the smallest part of the sternum. It may be bifid and ossifies only later in life, and it does not articulate with the ribs.'),
('7958b23f-cb5c-5416-87ad-6fa889929ed0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding Thoracic wall and breast, what is the correct answer to: the first rib is:?', '["The rib that attaches to T12","The shortest","broadest","most curved rib with a scalene tubercle","The most mobile rib"]'::jsonb, 'The shortest', 'The first rib is the shortest, broadest, and most curved of all the ribs, and it carries the scalene tubercle. It is the most fixed rib, not the most mobile one.'),
('573daf05-0790-5853-8567-a05b56faf5f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'For Thoracic wall and breast, which option is most appropriate: the scalene tubercle on the 1st rib is:?', '["For middle scalene","For serratus anterior","For attachment of anterior scalene muscle","For posterior scalene","For subclavius muscle"]'::jsonb, 'For attachment of anterior scalene muscle', 'The scalene tubercle on the inner border of the first rib is the attachment point of the scalenus anterior muscle. The subclavian vessels cross the first rib in the grooves on either side of it.'),
('d559135c-d40d-595c-8446-dde582b0a834'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Which answer is correct in Thoracic wall and breast when asked: the intercostal spaces are:?', '["The spaces between adjacent ribs","occupied by intercostal muscles","vessels","and nerves","The spaces in the mediastinum"]'::jsonb, 'The spaces between adjacent ribs', 'Intercostal spaces are the gaps between adjacent ribs, occupied by the intercostal muscles, vessels, and nerves. They lie in the thoracic wall rather than in the mediastinum.'),
('24b79f59-aac8-518b-9ef4-a759f5d7cb65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the best response to ''Intercostal nerves are:'' is:', '["Posterior rami of thoracic nerves","Branches of the brachial plexus","Cervical nerves","Sacral nerves","Anterior rami of T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves', 'Intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of the T1-T11 thoracic spinal nerves. The anterior ramus of T12 forms the subcostal nerve, which courses below the 12th rib.'),
('47b56216-b3cb-5ab2-a35f-fdcf3bb97451'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'During revision of Thoracic wall and breast, which of the following is correct: the neurovascular bundle of an intercostal space runs:?', '["Deep to the pleura","In the costal groove","between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles","Superficial to the intercostal muscles","Between the skin and muscle"]'::jsonb, 'In the costal groove', 'The intercostal neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove along the inferior border of each rib, sheltered by the rib. Within the groove the vein lies highest, the artery below it, and the nerve lowest (VAN).'),
('c398a9e2-0ad4-5752-94ab-66bd05989118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the intercostal artery arises from:', '["Facial artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thoracic aorta (for posterior intercostals) and internal thoracic artery (for anterior intercostals)","External carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brachial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic aorta (for posterior intercostals) and internal thoracic artery (for anterior intercostals)', 'The posterior intercostal arteries arise from the descending thoracic aorta, while the anterior intercostal arteries arise from the internal thoracic artery. The facial, vertebral, external carotid and brachial arteries do not supply the intercostal spaces.'),
('d6982f67-d4b1-571f-8071-d28b0331158c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the internal thoracic artery gives off:', '["Lumbar arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior intercostal arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior intercostal branches and the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries","Bronchial arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior intercostal branches and the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries', 'The internal thoracic artery descends behind the upper six costal cartilages, giving off anterior intercostal branches before dividing into the musculophrenic and superior epigastric arteries. The lumbar, posterior intercostal, bronchial and esophageal arteries arise from other sources.'),
('fa4c89dc-223a-5a2e-bb33-9bb88f3cb920'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the intercostal vein drains into:', '["Coronary sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior vena cava directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior vena cava mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Azygos/hemiazygos veins (posteriorly) and internal thoracic veins (anteriorly)"]'::jsonb, 'Azygos/hemiazygos veins (posteriorly) and internal thoracic veins (anteriorly)', 'Posterior intercostal veins drain into the azygos and hemiazygos veins, whereas the anterior intercostal veins drain into the internal thoracic veins. The coronary sinus, superior vena cava, inferior vena cava and pulmonary veins do not receive intercostal drainage.'),
('f3d3e5ce-6877-5f80-ac42-555f20f51634'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the serratus anterior muscle:', '["Protracts the scapula (punching muscle)","supplied by long thoracic nerve (C5-7)","Elevates the scapula","Rotates the scapula","Supplies the arm"]'::jsonb, 'Protracts the scapula (punching muscle)', 'The serratus anterior protracts the scapula, pulling it forward around the thoracic wall, which is why it is called the boxing or punching muscle. The other choices describe actions of different muscles.'),
('d0c7757e-4102-5015-9ba3-4e633f3f6683'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the pectoralis major muscle:', '["Flexes the elbow","Extends the arm","Abducts the arm","Supinates the forearm","Adducts and medially rotates the arm"]'::jsonb, 'Adducts and medially rotates the arm', 'Pectoralis major adducts and medially rotates the humerus at the shoulder and also assists in flexion. Flexion of the elbow, extension, abduction of the arm and supination of the forearm are actions of other muscle groups.'),
('203de5ab-9adf-5ee7-9028-1bbc934c8843'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the pectoralis minor muscle:', '["Elevates the shoulder","Adducts the arm","Depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula","supplied by medial pectoral nerve","Rotates the trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Depresses the shoulder and protracts the scapula', 'Pectoralis minor passes from the coracoid process to ribs 3-5 and acts to depress the shoulder and protract the scapula. Elevation of the shoulder, adduction of the arm and rotation of the trunk are not its functions.'),
('f0f46d2a-2e48-5563-8243-09dd8f7d5ffd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the clavicle is:', '["A sesamoid bone resulting in decreased functional activity","A flat bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not a long bone mediated by specific receptor activation","The most commonly fractured bone in the body","A short bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most commonly fractured bone in the body', 'The clavicle is the most frequently fractured bone because it is long, thin and subcutaneous, with the middle third being the weakest point. It is a long bone with a gentle S-shaped curve, not a flat, short or sesamoid bone.'),
('18268a7c-cee1-565d-979d-1836e7b36282'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the manubriosternal joint is:', '["A hinge joint resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibrous joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A synovial joint mediated by specific receptor activation","A secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis)","A primary cartilaginous joint leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis)', 'The manubriosternal joint is a secondary cartilaginous joint (symphysis) in which the two bones, each covered by hyaline cartilage, are united by a fibrocartilaginous disc. It is neither a synovial, fibrous nor primary cartilaginous joint.'),
('5937c8b1-b775-50fb-85e2-f08ee67d7932'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the costovertebral joints are:', '["Fibrous joints resulting in decreased functional activity","Cartilaginous joints accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Synovial joints between the ribs and vertebrae","Hinge joints without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sutures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial joints between the ribs and vertebrae', 'Each costovertebral joint is a synovial joint formed between the head of the rib and the demifacets of the bodies of its own and the adjacent vertebra. Fibrous, cartilaginous, hinge and suture classifications are incorrect.'),
('9c78847a-7242-51bc-9c65-6a1ab9e494c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the costotransverse joints are:', '["Synovial joints between the tubercle of rib and the transverse process of vertebra","Synarthroses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Symphyses mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous joints without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cartilaginous joints leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synovial joints between the tubercle of rib and the transverse process of vertebra', 'The costotransverse joint is a synovial joint between the tubercle of the rib and the transverse process of its own vertebra. Synarthroses, symphyses, fibrous and cartilaginous joints are not classifications of this articulation.'),
('79b2f0a5-8ed7-5dfa-9660-a79174b860d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the subcostal nerve (T12):', '["Is part of the lumbar plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","Runs below the 12th rib and supplies the lower abdominal wall","Is a branch of the brachial plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","Supplies the diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a cervical nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Runs below the 12th rib and supplies the lower abdominal wall', 'The subcostal nerve is the anterior ramus of T12; it runs below the 12th rib to supply the skin and muscles of the lower abdominal wall. It is not a branch of the lumbar or brachial plexus and does not supply the diaphragm.'),
('42069758-9528-5ef7-aaf0-41ac59242545'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the diaphragm originates from:', '["Xiphoid process","costal cartilages of ribs 7-12","and lumbar vertebrae","Only the xiphoid","Only the vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Xiphoid process', 'The sternal part of the diaphragm arises from the posterior surface of the xiphoid process, one of its three origins along with the costal cartilages of ribs 7-12 and the lumbar vertebrae. The xiphoid process is therefore a genuine component of its origin.'),
('d13e4677-9960-5ca2-977b-b4131ecea03b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the central tendon of the diaphragm is:', '["The aponeurotic insertion of the diaphragm","fused with fibrous pericardium","The caval opening","The aortic opening","The esophageal opening"]'::jsonb, 'The aponeurotic insertion of the diaphragm', 'The central tendon is the aponeurotic insertion into which all fibers of the diaphragm converge; it fuses above with the fibrous pericardium and contains the caval opening. The aortic and esophageal openings lie posterior to it in the muscular part.'),
('d80e53e7-f70d-520b-ab76-4501dafe177f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the aortic hiatus (T12) transmits:', '["Sympathetic trunk","Aorta","thoracic duct","and azygos/hemiazygos veins","Esophagus and vagus nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta', 'The aortic hiatus at the T12 level transmits the aorta and the thoracic duct behind the median arcuate ligament. The esophagus with the vagi passes at T10, the IVC at T8, and the sympathetic trunks pass behind the crura.'),
('6cbd5255-e192-51a0-9f40-378fb2e3c886'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the esophageal hiatus (T10) transmits:', '["IVC and phrenic nerve","Esophagus","vagus nerves (anterior and posterior)","esophageal vessels","Only the esophagus"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10, formed within the right crus, transmits the esophagus together with the anterior and posterior vagal trunks and small esophageal vessels. The IVC with the right phrenic nerve passes at T8 and the aorta at T12.'),
('23a5ac5c-a3f7-5b4f-b2c5-b7a893c8c0d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the caval opening (T8) transmits:', '["The phrenic nerve alone","Aorta and thoracic duct","IVC and right phrenic nerve","Esophagus and vagus","Only the IVC"]'::jsonb, 'IVC and right phrenic nerve', 'The caval opening lies in the central tendon at T8 and transmits the inferior vena cava together with the right phrenic nerve. The aorta, esophagus and thoracic duct travel through other openings at different levels.'),
('8e080b6e-d48a-5b3b-b0c6-3d83e3264d9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the internal intercostal muscles function in:', '["Compression of abdomen","Flexion of trunk","Forced expiration (depress ribs)","Elevation of scapula","Inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Forced expiration (depress ribs)', 'The internal intercostal muscles pull the ribs downward, which is the key movement of forced expiration. The external intercostals, by contrast, elevate the ribs during inspiration.'),
('56879b8c-3cb2-5a08-a884-7510ea795ac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the thoracic wall consists of:', '["Ribs and intercostal muscles only","Ribs and sternum only","Sternum and clavicle","Thoracic vertebrae and ribs","Ribs, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, intercostal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs, sternum, thoracic vertebrae, intercostal muscles', 'The thoracic wall is formed by the thoracic vertebrae, ribs with their costal cartilages, the sternum and the intercostal and related muscles. Choices omitting the vertebrae, sternum or muscles give only a partial description.'),
('81042ead-9f82-524a-bd02-d47b44013314'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the true ribs are:', '["Ribs 1-5 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 11-12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 1-7 attaching directly to sternum","Ribs 1-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ribs 8-10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 1-7 attaching directly to sternum', 'True ribs (1-7) each attach to the sternum directly through their own costal cartilage. False ribs (8-10) join the sternum indirectly and floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment.'),
('30d63ca7-319f-59d4-b61b-89f40f9c1b29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the false ribs are:', '["Ribs 6-10 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 1-7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 11-12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 8-10 (attach via costal cartilage)","Ribs 8-12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 8-10 (attach via costal cartilage)', 'The false ribs (8-10) do not reach the sternum directly; their costal cartilages join the cartilage of rib 7. Floating ribs 11-12 have no anterior attachment, while true ribs 1-7 attach directly to the sternum.'),
('b864f94e-15d1-54c1-bb2a-0864cfd5d69d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the floating ribs are:', '["Ribs 8-10 resulting in decreased functional activity","Ribs 1-7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ribs 10-12 mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 9-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ribs 11-12 (no anterior attachment)"]'::jsonb, 'Ribs 11-12 (no anterior attachment)', 'Floating ribs (11-12) end in the musculature of the posterior abdominal wall and have no anterior attachment. True and false ribs are anchored to the sternum directly or indirectly.'),
('61a47028-10c8-51bc-8487-2612c992ad9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the intercostal nerves are branches of:', '["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","Thoracic sympathetic chain","Anterior rami of T1-T11 spinal nerves","Posterior rami of spinal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of T1-T11 spinal nerves', 'The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of thoracic spinal nerves T1-T11 and run in the intercostal spaces between the internal and innermost intercostal muscles. The vagus, phrenic and thoracic sympathetic trunk are separate structures.'),
('6a0d234a-6de4-508a-85e8-8eda697b201c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the breast is located:', '["Over pectoralis minor","Over serratus anterior","Over pectoralis major, between ribs 2-6","Between ribs 1-4","Between ribs 4-8"]'::jsonb, 'Over pectoralis major, between ribs 2-6', 'The breast lies on the deep pectoral fascia overlying the pectoralis major and extends from about the 2nd to the 6th rib. The other options give incorrect positions or coverings.'),
('c24aaacb-c071-5eb4-8422-09289743f205'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the axillary tail of Spence is:', '["A vein draining the breast","A projection of breast tissue into axilla","A lymph node in the axilla","An artery supplying the breast","A nerve innervating the breast"]'::jsonb, 'A projection of breast tissue into axilla', 'The axillary tail of Spence is the superolateral extension of breast glandular tissue that pierces the deep fascia to reach the axilla. It is part of the breast substance, not a vessel, nerve or lymph node.'),
('478f119f-a2e4-5339-ad62-b79a1000b7c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, lymphatic drainage of the breast primarily goes to:', '["Cervical nodes","Mediastinal nodes","Axillary lymph nodes","Subclavian nodes","Parasternal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary lymph nodes', 'Most lymphatic drainage of the breast, especially from the lateral quadrants, passes to the axillary lymph nodes, chiefly the anterior (pectoral) group. The medial quadrants drain toward the internal mammary nodes, but the principal drainage is axillary.'),
('6a0b66a5-9d3a-5c37-921a-1e1677c7aade'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, Cooper ligaments of the breast:', '["Glandular tissue of breast","Nerves of breast","Blood vessels of breast","Suspensory ligaments supporting breast","Lymphatics of breast"]'::jsonb, 'Suspensory ligaments supporting breast', 'Cooper (suspensory) ligaments are fibrous bands running from the skin to the deep fascia that support the breast tissue. When shortened by tumor infiltration they produce the skin dimpling seen over a carcinoma.'),
('43baee0a-b113-5447-880a-7087ce4ff534'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, which option correctly describes the intercostal nerve?', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Splanchnic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve","Posterior ramus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of thoracic spinal nerve', 'An intercostal nerve is the anterior ramus of a thoracic spinal nerve (T1-T11) and carries motor and sensory fibers to the thoracic wall. Sympathetic, splanchnic and vagus nerves have different origins and distributions.'),
('b2c943ec-b76d-5fa4-90a2-77641e7ca7bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Thoracic Wall Breast: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('9da865ed-a9d0-52f3-83c9-34773eb57bf1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'The lungs are located:', '["In the peritoneum","In the pericardial cavity","In the abdominal cavity","In the mediastinum","In the pleural cavities"]'::jsonb, 'In the pleural cavities', 'Each lung lies free within its own pleural cavity, suspended only at the hilum and enclosed by the visceral and parietal pleura. The mediastinum lies between the pleural cavities and contains the heart and great vessels. The pericardial, peritoneal, and abdominal cavities house other organs.'),
('c01895ca-2335-56e9-a6a7-7c7427ea65de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding clinical item 234 (Regarding Which of the following best describes bronchopulmonary segments?, which of the following represents the primary mechanism?), which of the following represents the primary mechanism?', '["Bronchioles","Lobes of the lung","Pleural recesses","Alveolar sacs","Functional units of the lung"]'::jsonb, 'Functional units of the lung', 'Bronchopulmonary segments are the functional units of the lung, each receiving a segmental bronchus and a pulmonary artery branch and able to be removed independently. The other options describe different parts of the respiratory or pleural apparatus.'),
('dcaa0d3a-beaa-5c0b-b043-3d2edc203b9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'Regarding clinical cases in Thoracic Wall Breast: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('931fdd4a-d97e-5f6f-9c75-187fcfd8fc49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In the study of Thoracic Wall Breast, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('bc6ea668-e648-529e-8915-ded37f5f7a45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the thoracic cage consists of:', '["12 thoracic vertebrae","12 pairs of ribs","costal cartilages sternum","12 vertebrae only Sternum + ribs only","Clavicles + scapulae Ribs + costal cartilages only"]'::jsonb, '12 thoracic vertebrae', 'The thoracic cage is formed by the 12 thoracic vertebrae, 12 pairs of ribs with their costal cartilages and the sternum. Among the choices, the thoracic vertebrae are the only true cage component; the clavicles and scapulae belong to the pectoral girdle.'),
('1286191c-5baa-5d39-a5eb-38565520ea99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the sternum is composed of:', '["Manubrium only","Manubrium + body only","Body + xiphoid only","Manubrium + gladiolus","Manubrium body xiphoid process"]'::jsonb, 'Manubrium body xiphoid process', 'The sternum consists of three parts: the manubrium, the body (gladiolus) and the xiphoid process. Choices listing only one or two parts omit the complete composition.'),
('a313ebd6-17f7-5db6-acec-4ccd3fe74bc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the sternal angle (Louis) is located at:', '["Xiphisternal junction","Body-xiphoid junction","Manubriosternal junction","T4-T5","Suprasternal notch Sternoclavicular joint"]'::jsonb, 'Manubriosternal junction', 'The sternal angle of Louis is the transverse ridge at the manubriosternal junction, level with the second costal cartilage. It is the standard landmark for counting ribs and approximates the T4-T5 intervertebral disc.'),
('e95012f6-c2de-5f73-b545-b324acfb5179'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the landmark used to count the ribs at the sternal angle is the:', '["2nd rib","tracheal bifurcation","aortic arch ends azygos arch","Xiphoid 1st rib","3rd rib Sternal notch"]'::jsonb, '2nd rib', 'The sternal angle marks the attachment of the second costal cartilage, so the second rib is the reference point for counting the ribs. The tracheal bifurcation and aortic arch lie near this level, but rib counting starts at the 2nd rib.'),
('247a782f-6f57-5f45-8ce6-d54f7a12dad9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the true ribs (1-7):', '["Attached directly to sternum via costal cartilage","Attached to sternum indirectly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Floating mediated by specific receptor activation","anterior attachment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atypical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Attached directly to sternum via costal cartilage', 'True ribs (1-7) each attach to the sternum directly through their own costal cartilage. False ribs (8-10) attach indirectly and floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment.'),
('889e9424-1892-50ab-99e7-6ee4b7c8b927'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the false ribs (8-10):', '["Floating resulting in decreased functional activity","attachment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct to sternum mediated by specific receptor activation","Free without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attached to costal cartilage of rib 7"]'::jsonb, 'Attached to costal cartilage of rib 7', 'The false ribs (8-10) are attached anteriorly through the costal cartilage of rib 7 rather than directly to the sternum. Floating, free and direct sternal attachments do not apply to them.'),
('f6d0c057-a745-54f7-ae1a-99da9e7ae300'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the floating ribs (11-12):', '["Attach to vertebra only","Attach to xiphoid","Direct to sternum","Attach to rib 10","No anterior attachment"]'::jsonb, 'No anterior attachment', 'Floating ribs (11-12) have no anterior attachment and end in the posterior abdominal wall musculature. They do not attach to the sternum, xiphoid or the tenth rib.'),
('f557481c-f3e3-5e5a-9b96-dc8f5eba9196'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, a typical rib has:', '["Head","neck","tubercle","angle","shaft costal groove"]'::jsonb, 'Head', 'A typical rib possesses a head, neck, tubercle, angle and a shaft bearing the costal groove. The head is the proximal expanded end that articulates with the vertebral bodies.'),
('11f65eb4-b959-538d-bccf-a30a285323f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the head of a typical rib articulates with:', '["Only vertebra above resulting in decreased functional activity","Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)","Only own vertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","Sternum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Own vertebra and vertebra above (2 facets)', 'The head of a typical rib carries two facets that articulate with the demifacets of its own vertebra and the vertebra immediately above. The tubercle articulates with the transverse process of its own vertebra, not with the sternum.'),
('8d0972f0-e6b3-5c8f-b60e-d06b6c848d5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the tubercle of a rib articulates with:', '["Sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Costal cartilage mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertebra above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse process of same vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse process of same vertebra', 'The tubercle of a rib articulates with the transverse process of its own vertebra to form the costotransverse joint. The head, not the tubercle, articulates with the vertebral bodies.'),
('5636808c-6b38-508b-a0e3-309887272768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the costal groove contains:', '["Only vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Only artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal neurovascular bundle (VAN superior to inferior)', 'The costal groove on the lower inner border of each rib protects the intercostal neurovascular bundle, arranged from above downward as vein, artery and nerve (VAN). This arrangement is important during intercostal drainage or nerve block.'),
('6f4c6b57-cc9a-5c43-9013-0cbfb687fee9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the first rib is atypical because it has:', '["Two facets","Single facet","scalene tubercle","subclavian grooves","Longest costal groove tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Single facet', 'The first rib is atypical because its head has a single facet for articulation with T1 only, and it carries a scalene tubercle and subclavian grooves while being short, broad and flattened. Typical ribs have a two-facetted head.'),
('50114d66-eb9f-591f-95b0-a179fba7c87b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the scalene tubercle on the first rib is the insertion of:', '["Scalenus medius","SCM","Scalenus anterior","Levator scapulae","Scalenus posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenus anterior', 'The scalene tubercle on the inner border of the first rib is the insertion of scalenus anterior, which separates the subclavian vein anteriorly from the subclavian artery posteriorly. The other listed muscles do not attach there.'),
('cd9c652d-7a44-512e-96cf-abe6f70164ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the intercostal spaces are:', '["11 spaces between 12 ribs","9 spaces","12 spaces","10 spaces","8 spaces"]'::jsonb, '11 spaces between 12 ribs', 'The twelve pairs of ribs form eleven intercostal spaces, each named after the rib above it. The other numeric choices do not reflect the actual anatomy.'),
('73d05981-1c6b-559b-8fb6-fb3804f11766'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the intercostal muscle layers are:', '["2: external + internal resulting in decreased functional activity","4 layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 layer mediated by specific receptor activation","muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3: external internal innermost"]'::jsonb, '3: external internal innermost', 'Each intercostal space contains three layers of muscle: the external, internal and innermost intercostal muscles, with the neurovascular bundle lying between the internal and innermost layers.'),
('973b9e29-3ae9-5609-831f-5af2c5f1717c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the external intercostal fibers run:', '["Vertically resulting in decreased functional activity","Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)","Circularly mediated by specific receptor activation","Downward and backward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horizontally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and forward (hands in pockets)', 'External intercostal fibers pass obliquely downward and forward, in the same direction as the fingers when the hands are placed in pockets. They act to elevate the ribs during inspiration.'),
('e8d82d4c-012c-5133-8845-131401edce0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the internal intercostal fibers run:', '["Vertically","Downward and forward","Parallel to ribs","Horizontally","Obliquely downward and backward"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely downward and backward', 'Internal intercostal fibers run obliquely downward and backward, at right angles to the external intercostal fibers. They depress the ribs during forced expiration.'),
('4324cad3-deb1-544c-9866-e3b586f26425'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Thorax - Thoracic Wall Breast', 'In Thoracic wall and breast, the innermost intercostal muscle is:', '["Part of diaphragm","Same as internal","External to internal","Deepest layer","separated from internal by neurovascular bundle Absent"]'::jsonb, 'Deepest layer', 'The innermost intercostal is the deepest of the three intercostal muscle layers and lies deep to the neurovascular bundle. It is continuous with the transversus thoracis and subcostalis muscles.'),
('d36f93c7-66e0-54f1-9797-cfd062e96c55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding layers of the anterior abdominal wall (superficial to deep):', '["Skin, deep fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum","Skin, Camper fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, peritoneum","Skin, Scarpa fascia, Camper fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, peritoneum","Skin, Camper fascia, Scarpa fascia, internal oblique, external oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, peritoneum","Skin, Camper fascia (fatty), Scarpa fascia (membranous), external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum"]'::jsonb, 'Skin, Camper fascia (fatty), Scarpa fascia (membranous), external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum', 'The anterior abdominal wall has nine layers: skin, fatty Camper fascia, membranous Scarpa fascia, the three flat muscles (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis), transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, and peritoneum. The other options omit a key layer or place the internal oblique before the external oblique.'),
('9bb11bd9-209e-5f79-97ed-ee54e40d2781'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about layers of the anterior abdominal wall (superficial to deep)?', '["Skin, Camper fascia (fatty), Scarpa fascia (membranous), external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum","Skin, deep fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum","Skin, Camper fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, peritoneum","Skin, Scarpa fascia, Camper fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, peritoneum","Skin, Camper fascia, Scarpa fascia, internal oblique, external oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, peritoneum"]'::jsonb, 'Skin, Camper fascia (fatty), Scarpa fascia (membranous), external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum', 'This sequence lists all nine layers in correct order, including both subdivisions of superficial fascia and the deep transversalis fascia with extraperitoneal fat. Alternatives omit one or more layers, reverse the two superficial fascial layers, or swap the order of the oblique muscles.'),
('fe6d07ae-7893-590a-a2c9-1a73c0f92ffb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding layers of the anterior abdominal wall (superficial to deep):', '["Skin, Camper fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, peritoneum","Skin, Camper fascia, Scarpa fascia, internal oblique, external oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, peritoneum","Skin, Camper fascia (fatty), Scarpa fascia (membranous), external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum","Skin, Scarpa fascia, Camper fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, peritoneum","Skin, deep fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum"]'::jsonb, 'Skin, Camper fascia (fatty), Scarpa fascia (membranous), external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum', 'The complete layered sequence from superficial to deep is skin, Camper (fatty) fascia, Scarpa (membranous) fascia, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis, transversalis fascia, extraperitoneal fat, peritoneum. Listing Scarpa superficial to Camper, omitting the deep fascial layers, or reversing the obliques yields an incorrect order.'),
('7e708d24-aebb-5501-9def-d74ec2f212f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding Scarpa fascia:', '["Deep fascia of the abdomen","Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia","Transversalis fascia","External oblique aponeurosis","Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia', 'Scarpa fascia is the membranous deep layer of the superficial fascia, lying beneath the fatty Camper fascia and firmly attached to underlying aponeuroses. It is not the deep fascia, transversalis fascia, external oblique aponeurosis, or the fatty Camper layer.'),
('2bc0fb7c-5a02-5eaf-8e47-ef322d28bdb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about Scarpa fascia?', '["Deep fascia of the abdomen","Transversalis fascia","Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia","External oblique aponeurosis","Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia', 'Scarpa fascia is the deep membranous layer of the superficial fascia and lies deep to the fatty Camper fascia, attaching to the fascia lata about 1 cm below the inguinal ligament. The other structures lie at different depths or are separate fascial/aponeurotic layers.'),
('21467d7c-196c-598b-af73-92711e804dc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding Scarpa fascia:', '["Transversalis fascia","Deep fascia of the abdomen","External oblique aponeurosis","Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia","Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia', 'Scarpa fascia forms the membranous deep division of the superficial fascia of the abdomen, distinct from the deep transversalis fascia behind the muscles. The fatty Camper fascia lies superficial to it, and it is unrelated to the deep fascia or external oblique aponeurosis.'),
('75c49d03-fe90-5e26-91d1-d09660ec731d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding Camper fascia:', '["Transversalis fascia","Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia","Deep fascia","Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia","External oblique aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia', 'Camper fascia is the superficial fatty layer of the subcutaneous tissue of the anterior abdominal wall. It lies superficial to the membranous Scarpa fascia and is not the deep fascia, transversalis fascia, or the external oblique aponeurosis.'),
('6696e29d-6b7e-5b1c-8d47-aeb72778897c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about Camper fascia?', '["Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia","Transversalis fascia","Deep fascia","Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia","External oblique aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia', 'Camper fascia is the fatty superficial layer of the superficial fascia, continuous with subcutaneous fat elsewhere and lying above the membranous Scarpa fascia. The transversalis fascia and deep fascia lie deeper, and the external oblique contributes only aponeurotic tissue.'),
('9e7370a5-306e-548b-9338-3f7829d3c8aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding Camper fascia:', '["Membranous (deep) layer of superficial fascia","Deep fascia","Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia","External oblique aponeurosis","Transversalis fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty (superficial) layer of superficial fascia', 'Camper fascia is the fatty (superficial) layer of the superficial fascia, while Scarpa fascia is its membranous (deep) layer. The choices naming deeper fasciae or the external oblique aponeurosis describe structures other than Camper fascia.'),
('2001c06f-82f4-54a7-ab57-5439642e20e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding continuity of Camper fascia:', '["Continues as Scarpa fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","Continues as deep fascia of thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ends at the inguinal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Attaches to the iliac crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Continuous with the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous with the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum', 'Camper fascia is not interrupted at the inguinal ligament and continues as the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum. It does not become Scarpa fascia, the deep fascia of the thigh, or terminate at specific bony attachments.'),
('70bf2583-f384-5cee-b236-24b213fe11d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about continuity of Camper fascia?', '["Attaches to the iliac crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Continues as deep fascia of thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ends at the inguinal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Continuous with the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum","Continues as Scarpa fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous with the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum', 'The fatty Camper fascia passes freely over the inguinal ligament to continue as the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum. It does not blend with the deep fascia, stop at the inguinal ligament, or transform into membranous Scarpa fascia.'),
('fc19e45d-886d-5635-be21-44e483796023'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding continuity of Camper fascia:', '["Continuous with the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum","Continues as Scarpa fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ends at the inguinal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Attaches to the iliac crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Continues as deep fascia of thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous with the superficial fascia of the thigh and perineum', 'Unlike Scarpa fascia, Camper fascia is not interrupted at the inguinal ligament and continues into the thigh and perineum as superficial fascia. The remaining options attribute continuations or boundaries that belong to other fascial layers.'),
('85a37645-554e-5c03-9889-79f76066e8cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding continuity of Scarpa fascia:', '["Attaches to the iliac crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Ends at the umbilicus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Continues as Camper fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","Continues into the perineum as Colles fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blends with the fascia lata of the thigh just below the inguinal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Blends with the fascia lata of the thigh just below the inguinal ligament', 'Scarpa fascia fuses with the fascia lata of the thigh about 1 cm below the inguinal ligament, preventing subcutaneous fluid from tracking into the thigh. It also continues into the perineum as Colles fascia, but its defined lower limit at the thigh is the fascia lata.'),
('cecf874b-37ea-598c-bb3f-197d331c2c26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about continuity of Scarpa fascia?', '["Ends at the umbilicus resulting in decreased functional activity","Blends with the fascia lata of the thigh just below the inguinal ligament","Continues into the perineum as Colles fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","Continues as Camper fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attaches to the iliac crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blends with the fascia lata of the thigh just below the inguinal ligament', 'Scarpa fascia attaches to the fascia lata approximately 1 cm below the inguinal ligament, forming the fence that confines fluid collections to the abdominal wall. It is not bounded by the umbilicus or iliac crest and does not continue as Camper fascia.'),
('fb241ecc-fe23-50df-8d85-cb9fa5b4f75d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding continuity of Scarpa fascia:', '["Attaches to the iliac crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Continues into the perineum as Colles fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Continues as Camper fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","Ends at the umbilicus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blends with the fascia lata of the thigh just below the inguinal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Blends with the fascia lata of the thigh just below the inguinal ligament', 'Scarpa fascia fuses with the fascia lata of the thigh about 1 cm below the inguinal ligament, which is why subcutaneous fluid from the abdominal wall does not freely enter the thigh. It neither terminates at the umbilicus/iliac crest nor converts into Camper fascia.'),
('3f017a13-c800-5d0b-bfaa-b38bc50b93ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding continuation of Scarpa fascia into perineum:', '["Perineal membrane","Urogenital diaphragm","Rectovesical septum","Deep perineal fascia","Colles fascia (superficial perineal fascia)"]'::jsonb, 'Colles fascia (superficial perineal fascia)', 'Scarpa fascia continues into the perineum as Colles fascia, the superficial perineal fascia that lines the external genitalia and roof of the superficial perineal pouch. The perineal membrane and urogenital diaphragm lie deeper, and the rectovesical septum is a pelvic structure.'),
('492349cf-addd-573f-ac6c-6e902f6c8385'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about continuation of Scarpa fascia into perineum?', '["Colles fascia (superficial perineal fascia)","Urogenital diaphragm","Deep perineal fascia","Perineal membrane","Rectovesical septum"]'::jsonb, 'Colles fascia (superficial perineal fascia)', 'The membranous Scarpa fascia of the abdomen extends into the perineum as Colles fascia, forming the superficial boundary of the superficial perineal space. The urogenital diaphragm and perineal membrane are deep structures, and the rectovesical septum is not fascial continuity from the abdominal wall.'),
('000a1ff6-4b2f-5fc9-af74-014872da27f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding continuation of Scarpa fascia into perineum:', '["Perineal membrane","Rectovesical septum","Colles fascia (superficial perineal fascia)","Urogenital diaphragm","Deep perineal fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Colles fascia (superficial perineal fascia)', 'Scarpa fascia continues into the perineum as Colles fascia, the superficial perineal fascia that attaches to the perineal membrane and urogenital diaphragm fascia. The perineal membrane and urogenital diaphragm are deep supportive structures, not continuations of the abdominal superficial fascia.'),
('a800d9de-6922-5796-9e2d-74821ebd7477'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding dermatome of the umbilicus:', '["T7","T8","T12","T10","L1"]'::jsonb, 'T10', 'The umbilicus lies at the T10 dermatome, a key clinical landmark for assessing spinal cord injury levels and planning regional anesthesia. T7-T8 supply the epigastrium and T12-L1 the lower abdomen.'),
('1be7c4f0-b53f-52e8-a310-158aec38f669'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about dermatome of the umbilicus?', '["T10","T12","L1","T7","T8"]'::jsonb, 'T10', 'The umbilicus consistently marks the T10 dermatome, serving as a reference point for mapping spinal nerve skin territories. The higher thoracic levels supply the more cranial abdomen, while T12 and L1 supply the lower abdomen and groin.'),
('31abd24a-b87b-5169-bdec-4822213456f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding dermatome of the umbilicus:', '["T10","L1","T12","T8","T7"]'::jsonb, 'T10', 'The umbilicus is at the T10 dermatome, a clinically important landmark for localizing spinal cord lesions and for regional blocks. T7-T8 correspond to the epigastrium and T12-L1 to the hypogastrium and groin.'),
('f27d66b6-5a7d-53fc-be2b-20470c69a578'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding dermatome of the iliac crest:', '["L2 resulting in decreased functional activity","S1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1 (iliohypogastric)","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (iliohypogastric)', 'The skin over the iliac crest is supplied by the L1 dermatome via the iliohypogastric nerve, which arises from the L1 spinal nerve. L2 supplies the anterior thigh, T10 the umbilicus, T12 the suprapubic region, and S1 structures below the girdle.'),
('456a8be2-73a2-52b6-92bb-a10450df9252'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about dermatome of the iliac crest?', '["T12 resulting in decreased functional activity","S1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T10 mediated by specific receptor activation","L2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 (iliohypogastric)"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (iliohypogastric)', 'The iliohypogastric nerve (L1) supplies sensation to the skin over the iliac crest and the suprapubic region. T10 relates to the umbilicus, T12 to the lower abdomen, L2 to the thigh, and S1 to the buttock and posterior leg.'),
('4a5d13e7-e482-5d85-8d95-2b96973efa3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding dermatome of the iliac crest:', '["L2 resulting in decreased functional activity","T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S1 mediated by specific receptor activation","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 (iliohypogastric)"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (iliohypogastric)', 'Cutaneous innervation over the iliac crest is provided by the L1 root through the iliohypogastric nerve. L2, T10, and T12 supply the thigh, umbilicus, and lower abdomen respectively, and S1 territory lies posterior inferior to the crest.'),
('851ba74f-243e-54a6-9712-08294b64757a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding dermatome of the suprapubic region:', '["T7","S1","T10","L2","T12-L1"]'::jsonb, 'T12-L1', 'The suprapubic region is innervated by the T12-L1 dermatomes, carried by the subcostal and iliohypogastric nerves. T7 relates to the epigastrium, T10 to the umbilicus, L2 to the thigh, and S1 to the buttock.'),
('7f835fbc-7ac8-5a0a-9724-d6d9e15a1df2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about dermatome of the suprapubic region?', '["L2","T7","T10","S1","T12-L1"]'::jsonb, 'T12-L1', 'The suprapubic skin receives sensory supply from the T12 and L1 dermatomes via the subcostal and iliohypogastric nerves. T7 and T10 are more cephalad dermatomes, L2 belongs to the thigh, and S1 lies in the buttock region.'),
('de27edba-f41a-53b4-b5d9-56efb66fec7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding dermatome of the suprapubic region:', '["L2","T12-L1","T7","S1","T10"]'::jsonb, 'T12-L1', 'The suprapubic skin is supplied by the T12 and L1 dermatomes, reached through the subcostal, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves. T7 and T10 dermatomes cover the upper and periumbilical abdomen, while L2 and S1 lie on the thigh and perineum, respectively.'),
('265e289d-46ff-5dd7-841b-c12907e8b523'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding cutaneous nerve supply above the umbilicus:', '["Lumbar plexus","Ilioinguinal (L1) and genitofemoral (L1-L2)","Intercostal nerves T7-T12","Subcostal (T12) and iliohypogastric (L1)","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves T7-T12', 'The skin above the umbilicus receives its anterior and lateral cutaneous branches from the lower intercostal nerves (T7-T12). The ilioinguinal, iliohypogastric, subcostal, and genitofemoral nerves serve the lower abdomen, and the femoral nerve has no cutaneous territory on the abdominal wall.'),
('3a36b173-7904-5e8d-8a4e-8872d384f9b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about cutaneous nerve supply above the umbilicus?', '["Femoral nerve","Subcostal (T12) and iliohypogastric (L1)","Lumbar plexus","Ilioinguinal (L1) and genitofemoral (L1-L2)","Intercostal nerves T7-T12"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves T7-T12', 'Anterior and lateral cutaneous branches of intercostal nerves T7-T12 supply the supraumbilical skin. The lumbar plexus branches (iliohypogastric, ilioinguinal, genitofemoral) and the subcostal nerve are directed at skin below the umbilicus, and the femoral nerve innervates the thigh.'),
('3e86778b-de18-5fcc-87b9-1ee16fd7db3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding cutaneous nerve supply above the umbilicus:', '["Lumbar plexus","Intercostal nerves T7-T12","Subcostal (T12) and iliohypogastric (L1)","Femoral nerve","Ilioinguinal (L1) and genitofemoral (L1-L2)"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves T7-T12', 'Above the umbilicus the abdominal wall skin is innervated segmentally by intercostal nerves T7-T12 through their lateral and anterior cutaneous branches. The subcostal, iliohypogastric, ilioinguinal, and genitofemoral nerve territories begin at or below the umbilical level, and the femoral nerve does not supply the trunk.'),
('e8ab9719-7630-5547-8b3d-63a2d3001414'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding cutaneous nerve supply below the umbilicus:', '["Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) resulting in decreased functional activity","Obturator nerve (L2-L4) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercostal nerves T7-T12 only mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2)","Femoral nerve (L2-L4) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2)', 'Below the umbilicus the anterior abdominal wall skin draws on the subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), and genitofemoral (L1-L2) nerves. Intercostal nerves T7-T12 end above this level, while the obturator, femoral, and pudendal nerves supply the thigh adductors, thigh, and perineum, respectively.'),
('ad31d15e-73d1-5723-ae82-6192714479a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about cutaneous nerve supply below the umbilicus?', '["Femoral nerve (L2-L4) resulting in decreased functional activity","Obturator nerve (L2-L4) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intercostal nerves T7-T12 only mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2)","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2)', 'The subcostal nerve with the iliohypogastric, ilioinguinal, and genitofemoral nerves collectively carry T12-L2 fibers to the infraumbilical wall skin. T7-T12 intercostal supply sits above the umbilicus, and the obturator, femoral, and pudendal nerves are confined to the lower limb and perineum.'),
('2dc1bf41-b53c-56b1-abdb-b91eefc7da5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Identify the correct statement regarding cutaneous nerve supply below the umbilicus:', '["Intercostal nerves T7-T12 only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator nerve (L2-L4) mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2)","Femoral nerve (L2-L4) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2)', 'Skin below the umbilicus is innervated by the subcostal, iliohypogastric, ilioinguinal, and genitofemoral nerves, which carry T12-L2 root fibers. Intercostal nerves T7-T12 supply only the supraumbilical wall, and the obturator, femoral, and pudendal nerves have territories in the thigh and perineum.'),
('a29c4f8c-7e0e-5e74-8f26-d878280f1a20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding formation of the rectus sheath:', '["Aponeuroses of internal oblique and transversus abdominis only","Aponeuroses of external oblique and internal oblique only","Muscles of the anterior abdominal wall directly","Aponeuroses of external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis","Transversalis fascia only"]'::jsonb, 'Aponeuroses of external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis', 'The rectus sheath is built from the aponeuroses of all three flat muscles, which split and rejoin around the rectus abdominis, with their arrangement changing at the arcuate line. The muscle bellies themselves and the transversalis fascia are not constituents of the sheath walls.'),
('aa487709-4bcb-527a-87c0-5c78af4d50c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about formation of the rectus sheath?', '["Transversalis fascia only","Muscles of the anterior abdominal wall directly","Aponeuroses of external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis","Aponeuroses of internal oblique and transversus abdominis only","Aponeuroses of external oblique and internal oblique only"]'::jsonb, 'Aponeuroses of external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis', 'Aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis contribute to the rectus sheath, dividing around the rectus muscle and altering their arrangement above and below the arcuate line. Omitting any one aponeurosis or substituting transversalis fascia or muscle tissue would not reproduce the normal sheath.'),
('f9870526-65bb-5ccc-aa52-3a62e11d0531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding posterior wall of rectus sheath above arcuate line:', '["Transversalis fascia and peritoneum resulting in decreased functional activity","All three aponeuroses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External oblique and internal oblique aponeuroses mediated by specific receptor activation","External oblique aponeurosis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis', 'Above the arcuate line the internal oblique aponeurosis splits, and its posterior lamina joins the transversus abdominis aponeurosis behind the rectus. The external oblique and the anterior lamina of internal oblique run anteriorly, so they are not part of this posterior wall.'),
('b1315d95-a6c0-5c7f-8df5-85c7fcc6a9bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about posterior wall of rectus sheath above arcuate line?', '["Transversalis fascia and peritoneum resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis","External oblique aponeurosis only mediated by specific receptor activation","External oblique and internal oblique aponeuroses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All three aponeuroses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis and transversus abdominis aponeurosis', 'Cranial to the arcuate line the posterior rectus sheath is formed by the posterior lamina of the internal oblique aponeurosis together with the transversus abdominis aponeurosis. The external oblique and the anterior lamina of internal oblique pass in front of the rectus, so no other combination reproduces this posterior layer.'),
('a010bfe4-8998-5f04-b0e4-1cd7978b7b50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding anterior wall of rectus sheath above arcuate line:', '["All three aponeuroses resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External oblique aponeurosis and anterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis","Transversus abdominis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External oblique only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique aponeurosis and anterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis', 'Above the arcuate line the external oblique aponeurosis and the anterior lamina of the split internal oblique aponeurosis form the anterior rectus sheath. The transversus abdominis aponeurosis is entirely behind the rectus here, and the transversus muscle is not part of the sheath.'),
('502b1874-e85f-5970-9f6f-4083b81134c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about anterior wall of rectus sheath above arcuate line?', '["External oblique only resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All three aponeuroses mediated by specific receptor activation","External oblique aponeurosis and anterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis","Transversus abdominis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique aponeurosis and anterior lamina of internal oblique aponeurosis', 'Above the arcuate line the anterior sheath is composed of the external oblique aponeurosis fused with the anterior lamina of the internal oblique aponeurosis. The transversus abdominis aponeurosis stays behind the rectus at this level, so the transversus-only or all-three options do not describe the anterior wall.'),
('8599475b-1cad-53f4-ae3d-dd344fa71c9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding posterior wall of rectus sheath below arcuate line:', '["All three aponeuroses resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal oblique and transversus abdominis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only transversalis fascia and peritoneum (all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)","External oblique and internal oblique without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectus abdominis muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Only transversalis fascia and peritoneum (all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)', 'Below the arcuate line all three aponeuroses sweep anterior to the rectus abdominis so that the posterior wall contains only transversalis fascia and peritoneum. Consequently the all-three-aponeuroses option pertains to the anterior sheath at this level, and the muscle belly does not form the wall.'),
('50eb46b1-b491-54ca-ac23-99a1f46527cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about posterior wall of rectus sheath below arcuate line?', '["Internal oblique and transversus abdominis resulting in decreased functional activity","External oblique and internal oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All three aponeuroses mediated by specific receptor activation","Only transversalis fascia and peritoneum (all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)","Rectus abdominis muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Only transversalis fascia and peritoneum (all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)', 'At the arcuate line the posterior sheath ends because all three aponeuroses deflect anteriorly; below this point the posterior wall is just transversalis fascia and peritoneum. Any combination of aponeuroses or the muscle itself would describe the anterior compartment rather than this deficient posterior layer.'),
('5a0ebe07-750d-5f75-9036-0cef637e6da4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding anterior wall of rectus sheath below arcuate line:', '["All three aponeuroses (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis)","External oblique and internal oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal oblique only mediated by specific receptor activation","External oblique only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transversus abdominis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All three aponeuroses (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis)', 'Below the arcuate line the aponeuroses of all three flat muscles pass in front of the rectus abdominis, creating a single thick anterior wall and leaving the posterior wall deficient. Listing only one or two aponeuroses omits the transverse layer that also reaches the anterior sheath here.'),
('d9151af6-910c-5c93-8d9b-2568de2e7a32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about anterior wall of rectus sheath below arcuate line?', '["External oblique only resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal oblique only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All three aponeuroses (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis)","Transversus abdominis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External oblique and internal oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All three aponeuroses (external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis)', 'Below the arcuate line all three flat muscle aponeuroses merge in front of the rectus abdominis, giving a thick anterior sheath with no true posterior wall. Each single-layer or two-layer option underestimates the contribution of the transversus abdominis aponeurosis at this level.'),
('4a23df75-ef94-52d0-a6b7-0401b6f40a17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding arcuate line level:', '["Pubic symphysis (S1) resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliac crest (L4-L5) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Xiphoid process (T9) mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior superior iliac spine (approximately L4)","Umbilicus (L3-L4) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior superior iliac spine (approximately L4)', 'The arcuate line lies at about the L4 vertebral level, in line with the anterior superior iliac spine. The xiphoid (T9) and umbilicus are higher landmarks, while the iliac crest spans L4-L5 and the pubic symphysis lies at S1, so neither represents this point.'),
('acfe99a4-4032-5716-bf1d-e21c370ef8ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about arcuate line level?', '["Iliac crest (L4-L5) resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior superior iliac spine (approximately L4)","Umbilicus (L3-L4) mediated by specific receptor activation","Xiphoid process (T9) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic symphysis (S1) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior superior iliac spine (approximately L4)', 'The arcuate line of Douglas is positioned at roughly L4, corresponding to the level of the anterior superior iliac spine midway between umbilicus and pubic symphysis. The iliac crest, umbilicus, xiphoid, and pubic symphysis occupy different levels of the trunk.'),
('8f5c343a-40d4-5185-bfb3-6b36e7e18585'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding arcuate line also known as:', '["Linea semicircularis (of Douglas)","Linea terminalis","Arcuate pubis","Linea semilunaris","Linea alba"]'::jsonb, 'Linea semicircularis (of Douglas)', 'The arcuate line is the linea semicircularis of Douglas, the curved lower border of the posterior rectus sheath. The linea semilunaris and linea alba are separate anterior wall landmarks, while the linea terminalis and arcuate pubis belong to the pelvis and hip, respectively.'),
('05a32d7d-5b4d-5a4f-a9e1-13751ddfb911'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about arcuate line also known as?', '["Linea terminalis","Linea alba","Linea semilunaris","Arcuate pubis","Linea semicircularis (of Douglas)"]'::jsonb, 'Linea semicircularis (of Douglas)', 'The arcuate line bears the eponym linea semicircularis of Douglas because it arches as the lower boundary of the posterior rectus sheath. The linea alba and linea semilunaris are anterior tendinous lines, and the linea terminalis and arcuate pubis are pelvic and acetabular structures.'),
('0e4b37c9-5eba-57ab-a4fd-b19b6fa78163'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding significance of arcuate line:', '["Marks the level of the umbilicus resulting in decreased functional activity","Is the attachment of the rectus abdominis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is where the inferior epigastric artery terminates mediated by specific receptor activation","Marks the lower limit of the posterior rectus sheath (below it all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)","Marks the upper limit of the rectus sheath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Marks the lower limit of the posterior rectus sheath (below it all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)', 'The arcuate line indicates where the posterior rectus sheath ends, since all three aponeuroses move anterior to the rectus below it. It has no relationship to the umbilical level, the rectus attachment, the inferior epigastric artery, or the upper sheath boundary.'),
('daf998f5-f410-51d1-9368-ddd858f8ae06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about significance of arcuate line?', '["Marks the lower limit of the posterior rectus sheath (below it all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)","Is where the inferior epigastric artery terminates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Marks the level of the umbilicus mediated by specific receptor activation","Is the attachment of the rectus abdominis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Marks the upper limit of the rectus sheath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Marks the lower limit of the posterior rectus sheath (below it all aponeuroses pass anteriorly)', 'The arcuate line is the lower boundary of the posterior rectus sheath, below which all aponeuroses lie anteriorly and only transversalis fascia and peritoneum remain posteriorly. The remaining options misassign the arcuate line to the umbilicus, the rectus origin, or the superior sheath margin.'),
('1297e0a1-e478-51bf-a55d-cab3e86e978f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding rectus sheath completeness:', '["Below the arcuate line only","Between the costal margin and the arcuate line","Above the costal margin only","Between xiphoid and pubic symphysis throughout","Between umbilicus and pubis only"]'::jsonb, 'Between the costal margin and the arcuate line', 'The rectus abdominis is fully enclosed by anterior and posterior sheath walls only between the costal margin and the arcuate line. Below the arcuate line the posterior wall is deficient, above the costal margin the muscle rests on the costal cartilages, and the other segments listed do not show complete ensheathment.'),
('a10ab799-8db0-5426-886d-ee7ea21a1a2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about rectus sheath completeness?', '["Between the costal margin and the arcuate line","Between xiphoid and pubic symphysis throughout","Above the costal margin only","Below the arcuate line only","Between umbilicus and pubis only"]'::jsonb, 'Between the costal margin and the arcuate line', 'Both the anterior and posterior walls of the rectus sheath are complete only in the span from the costal margin to the arcuate line. Above the costal margin the posterior sheath is absent and below the arcuate line all aponeuroses pass anteriorly, so the sheath is incomplete in these regions.'),
('026ed670-cbdf-5e90-b754-30c7dc6d2ccc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding posterior rectus sheath above costal margin:', '["Absent; rectus abdominis lies directly on costal cartilages (ribs 5-7)","Present as transversalis fascia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thickened with all aponeuroses mediated by specific receptor activation","Present as internal oblique only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Present as all three layers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absent; rectus abdominis lies directly on costal cartilages (ribs 5-7)', 'Above the costal margin the rectus abdominis attaches directly to the costal cartilages of ribs 5-7, so no posterior rectus sheath exists there. None of the aponeurotic or fascial layers persists as a posterior covering at this level.'),
('b231a09c-c73c-508e-93dc-32990a188cfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is true about posterior rectus sheath above costal margin?', '["Present as internal oblique only resulting in decreased functional activity","Absent; rectus abdominis lies directly on costal cartilages (ribs 5-7)","Present as all three layers mediated by specific receptor activation","Present as transversalis fascia only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thickened with all aponeuroses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absent; rectus abdominis lies directly on costal cartilages (ribs 5-7)', 'Above the costal margin, the aponeurotic layers that would form the posterior rectus sheath end, so the rectus abdominis rests directly on the costal cartilages of ribs 5-7. The internal oblique and transversus aponeuroses contribute below this level only; the transversalis fascia and internal oblique are not present alone as the posterior layer.'),
('68675779-d890-5750-a63c-ff6a7e7db2a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The rectus abdominis muscle is attached superiorly to?', '["Sternum resulting in decreased functional activity","First rib accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clavicle mediated by specific receptor activation","Xiphoid process and costal cartilages 5-7","Costal margin 3-4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Xiphoid process and costal cartilages 5-7', 'The rectus abdominis arises superiorly from the xiphoid process and the costal cartilages of ribs 5-7, not from the sternum or first rib. The clavicle and costal margin 3-4 are not attachment sites for this muscle.'),
('b7c5ad2e-5469-5c3a-8dc9-e11c0261282b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The rectus abdominis muscle is attached inferiorly to?', '["Inguinal ligament","Pubic crest and pubic symphysis","Ischial tuberosity","Femur","Iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic crest and pubic symphysis', 'The rectus abdominis inserts inferiorly into the pubic crest and the front of the pubic symphysis, anchoring the muscle for trunk flexion. The inguinal ligament, ischial tuberosity, femur, and iliac crest are not its inferior attachments.'),
('ac20873d-919a-59b5-9ef8-2da706ff1fc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The rectus abdominis usually has how many tendinous intersections?', '["5-6","1-2","None","3-4","7-8"]'::jsonb, '3-4', 'The rectus abdominis typically shows three to four tendinous intersections that create the segmented ''six-pack'' appearance. Five to six, one to two, none, or seven to eight do not reflect the usual number.'),
('5954b2f0-328c-5b8e-a513-82683cd50c50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The tendinous intersections of rectus abdominis are adherent to?', '["Peritoneum","Anterior wall of rectus sheath","Posterior wall of rectus sheath","Both walls","Transversalis fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall of rectus sheath', 'The tendinous intersections fuse with the anterior wall of the rectus sheath, which is why they are visible externally and do not extend across the muscle. The peritoneum, posterior wall, transversalis fascia, and ''both walls'' do not represent their attachment.'),
('74092f58-2ee2-552d-8619-0a0662e52d01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The nerve supply of the rectus abdominis is from?', '["Lumbar plexus (L1-L3)","Sacral plexus","Ilioinguinal only","Iliohypogastric only","Intercostal nerves T7-T12"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves T7-T12', 'The rectus abdominis is innervated segmentally by the intercostal nerves T7-T12 (with T12 also called the subcostal nerve), which enter the sheath from its lateral side. The lumbar and sacral plexuses, and the iliohypogastric or ilioinguinal nerves alone, do not supply this muscle.'),
('9ecb8dbd-52df-5ec1-b3b2-eaf31b5e077c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pyramidalis muscle is?', '["Part of the rectus abdominis resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of the inguinal canal floor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","A small triangular muscle anterior to the lower rectus abdominis within the rectus sheath","A muscle of the posterior abdominal wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small triangular muscle anterior to the lower rectus abdominis within the rectus sheath', 'The pyramidalis is a small triangular muscle lying in the rectus sheath anterior to the lower part of the rectus abdominis. It is a separate muscle, not part of the rectus abdominis, the inguinal canal floor, or the posterior abdominal wall.'),
('ed0be9b2-4f75-5dcd-a747-06f6b464844a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pyramidalis muscle is supplied by?', '["Genitofemoral (L1-L2)","Ilioinguinal (L1)","Subcostal nerve (T12)","Iliohypogastric (L1)","Femoral (L2-L4)"]'::jsonb, 'Subcostal nerve (T12)', 'The pyramidalis receives its innervation from the subcostal nerve (T12), the same segment that supplies the lowest part of the rectus abdominis. The genitofemoral, ilioinguinal, iliohypogastric, and femoral nerves do not innervate it.'),
('921ebbea-5a81-57ec-94d3-936809ea9b7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The function of the pyramidalis is?', '["Extends the trunk","Depresses the ribs","Tenses the linea alba","Flexes the trunk","Elevates the testis"]'::jsonb, 'Tenses the linea alba', 'The pyramidalis tenses the linea alba, helping maintain the midline connective tissue band. It does not extend or flex the trunk, depress the ribs, or elevate the testis.'),
('b61a2d2b-70e1-5596-a997-b607f5250534'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is NOT a content of the rectus sheath?', '["Rectus abdominis","Superior and inferior epigastric vessels","Terminal branches of intercostal nerves T7-T12","Femoral nerve","Pyramidalis"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve', 'The femoral nerve arises from the lumbar plexus and passes under the inguinal ligament into the thigh, never entering the rectus sheath. The rectus abdominis, pyramidalis, epigastric vessels, and terminal intercostal nerve branches are all genuine sheath contents.'),
('f09bbc57-0cdc-53b3-9dd0-e6877e7cce1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The superior epigastric artery is a terminal branch of?', '["Internal thoracic artery","External iliac artery","Intercostal artery","Axillary artery","Subclavian artery"]'::jsonb, 'Internal thoracic artery', 'The internal thoracic artery splits into the superior epigastric and musculophrenic arteries at the sixth costal cartilage. The external iliac, intercostal, axillary, and subclavian arteries do not directly give rise to the superior epigastric artery.'),
('c0a4be51-87c5-5bf6-af06-ed43adc3351d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inferior epigastric artery is a branch of?', '["Common iliac artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal iliac artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External iliac artery (just above the inguinal ligament)","Abdominal aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External iliac artery (just above the inguinal ligament)', 'The inferior epigastric artery arises from the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament and ascends to enter the rectus sheath. It is not a branch of the common or internal iliac arteries, the aorta, or the femoral artery.'),
('70e502c6-0a0a-51cf-a799-d3581b3ecfa6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The superior and inferior epigastric arteries anastomose?', '["Within the rectus sheath","In the inguinal canal","In the falciform ligament","At the umbilicus","At the arcuate line"]'::jsonb, 'Within the rectus sheath', 'The superior and inferior epigastric arteries descend and ascend within the rectus sheath respectively, anastomosing inside it. They do not join in the inguinal canal, falciform ligament, at the umbilicus, or at the arcuate line.'),
('479a80dc-a21d-57ca-82f7-7981aee69d96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The superior epigastric artery enters the rectus sheath by piercing?', '["The posterior wall of the rectus sheath (above the costal margin)","The external oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The linea alba mediated by specific receptor activation","The transversalis fascia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The anterior wall of the rectus sheath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior wall of the rectus sheath (above the costal margin)', 'The superior epigastric artery pierces the posterior wall of the rectus sheath just above the costal margin to run behind the rectus abdominis. It does not traverse the external oblique, linea alba, transversalis fascia, or anterior sheath wall for this entry.'),
('388dcdfa-d48d-5b74-9ed8-e33b02054b02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inferior epigastric artery enters the rectus sheath at?', '["The pubic symphysis","The xiphoid","The costal margin","The umbilicus","The arcuate line"]'::jsonb, 'The arcuate line', 'The inferior epigastric artery pierces the posterior layer of the rectus sheath at the arcuate line and then ascends within the sheath. The pubic symphysis, xiphoid, costal margin, and umbilicus are not its point of entry.'),
('fa05e001-1c49-5a1d-bec5-be5e5127c518'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The blood supply of the rectus abdominis is from?', '["Deep circumflex iliac arteries","Superior mesenteric artery","Superior and inferior epigastric arteries","Lumbar arteries only","Intercostal arteries only"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior epigastric arteries', 'The rectus abdominis is supplied by the superior epigastric artery from above and the inferior epigastric artery from below. The deep circumflex iliac, superior mesenteric, lumbar, and intercostal arteries are not its principal supply.'),
('e80b6d63-00a6-5645-bcf9-ad138ecf8eab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lymphatic drainage of the deep layers of the anterior abdominal wall is to?', '["External iliac and para-aortic lymph nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes","Axillary nodes","Internal iliac nodes","Cervical nodes"]'::jsonb, 'External iliac and para-aortic lymph nodes', 'Deep lymphatics of the anterior abdominal wall drain to the external iliac nodes inferiorly and the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes centrally. Superficial inguinal, axillary, internal iliac, and cervical nodes do not receive this deep drainage.'),
('e55e747a-0cc1-5772-99b4-778d2f8af94e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lymphatic drainage of the skin above the umbilicus is to?', '["Para-aortic nodes","Internal iliac nodes","Axillary lymph nodes","Deep inguinal nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Axillary lymph nodes', 'Skin above the umbilicus drains to the axillary lymph nodes via lymphatics running with the internal thoracic vessels. Para-aortic, internal iliac, and inguinal nodes drain other territories.'),
('29ee2a74-b4d0-5521-a2ba-eaae22026896'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lymphatic drainage of the skin below the umbilicus is to?', '["Deep inguinal nodes","Axillary nodes","Para-aortic nodes","Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","Internal iliac nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial inguinal lymph nodes', 'Skin below the umbilicus drains into the superficial inguinal lymph nodes, matching the drainage of the lower limb and perineum. The deep inguinal, axillary, para-aortic, and internal iliac groups do not collect this superficial drainage.'),
('8f5ca1a6-cb69-5da2-b6d1-63e45d029d9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The paraumbilical veins (of Sappey) connect?', '["Splenic to mesenteric veins resulting in decreased functional activity","Portal vein to superficial veins around the umbilicus","SVC to IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal to lumbar veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC to portal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein to superficial veins around the umbilicus', 'The paraumbilical veins run in the falciform ligament and join the left branch of the portal vein to the superficial veins around the umbilicus. They therefore link the portal system to the caval system and do not connect splenic to mesenteric, SVC to IVC, or renal to lumbar veins.'),
('2e0a2bb1-7249-532f-811a-c0022542e2f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Caput medusae is caused by?', '["Inferior vena cava obstruction","Femoral vein thrombosis","Portal hypertension with dilation of paraumbilical veins","Superior vena cava obstruction","Arteriovenous malformation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension with dilation of paraumbilical veins', 'Portal hypertension dilates the paraumbilical veins, producing distended veins radiating from the umbilicus (caput medusae). Obstruction of the IVC, SVC, femoral vein thrombosis, and arteriovenous malformations cause other patterns of venous engorgement.'),
('93366536-fc09-5660-9a29-1381f2a454f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The median umbilical ligament is the remnant of?', '["Urachus (allantois)","Vitelline duct","Mesonephric duct","Obliterated umbilical vein","Obliterated umbilical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Urachus (allantois)', 'The median umbilical ligament is the obliterated urachus, which connects the bladder apex to the umbilicus. The vitelline duct, mesonephric duct, umbilical vein, and umbilical artery form different ligaments.'),
('8a57eb6a-ad1a-5cae-8a50-8f53a96fe205'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The medial umbilical fold covers?', '["Obliterated umbilical artery (fetal part of internal iliac)","Urachus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Umbilical vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitellointestinal duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Round ligament of liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliterated umbilical artery (fetal part of internal iliac)', 'The medial umbilical fold is raised by the obliterated umbilical artery, the fetal continuation of the internal iliac artery. The urachus lies in the median fold, and the umbilical vein, vitellointestinal duct, and round ligament are associated with other structures.'),
('46d6f042-fa37-5812-8517-d67427a60bb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lateral umbilical fold contains?', '["Urachus","Vitelline duct","Inferior epigastric vessels","Round ligament of liver","Umbilical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior epigastric vessels', 'The lateral umbilical fold is formed by the inferior epigastric vessels, which remain patent. The urachus, vitelline duct, round ligament, and umbilical artery are related to the median or medial folds.'),
('62ae244a-e3a4-5a22-bb4e-ded2ef62512c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The external oblique muscle originates from?', '["Pubic tubercle resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracolumbar fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lumbar vertebrae mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac crest only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Outer surfaces of lower 8 ribs (ribs 5-12)"]'::jsonb, 'Outer surfaces of lower 8 ribs (ribs 5-12)', 'The external oblique arises by digitations from the outer surfaces of the lower eight ribs (5-12). The pubic tubercle is an insertion point, and the thoracolumbar fascia, lumbar vertebrae, and iliac crest alone are not its origin.'),
('6239c220-6330-5363-9cf9-060c75a0d63e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The external oblique inserts into?', '["Sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","Femur accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater trochanter mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischial tuberosity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Linea alba, pubic tubercle, anterior iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'Linea alba, pubic tubercle, anterior iliac crest', 'The external oblique inserts via its aponeurosis into the linea alba and pubic tubercle, and its fleshy fibers attach to the anterior half of the iliac crest. The sacrum, femur, greater trochanter, and ischial tuberosity are not insertion sites.'),
('58ef63f2-e0be-52e5-8e69-8c785c2a8142'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The external oblique muscle fibers run?', '["Obliquely upward","Superomedially (upward and forward)","Vertically","Transversely (horizontally)","Inferomedially (downward and forward)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferomedially (downward and forward)', 'The external oblique fibers pass downward and medially, the same direction as putting your hands in your pockets. Superomedial, vertical, and transverse directions describe different fiber orientations of the abdominal wall muscles.'),
('56a19104-a386-5780-8b8a-03a34d919d8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The external oblique aponeurosis forms which structures?', '["Pectineal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Transversalis fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament, superficial inguinal ring","Rectus sheath posterior wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conjoint tendon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament, superficial inguinal ring', 'The lower border of the external oblique aponeurosis folds back on itself to form the inguinal ligament, whose medial continuation becomes the lacunar ligament, and the triangular superficial inguinal ring is a defect in this aponeurosis. The transversalis fascia, pectineal ligament, and conjoint tendon derive from other layers.'),
('6c14b7bf-1906-5348-986d-b7f701c6c5ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The external oblique muscle is innervated by?', '["Intercostal nerves T7-T12 and subcostal T12","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ilioinguinal only mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliohypogastric only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lumbar plexus L1-L3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intercostal nerves T7-T12 and subcostal T12', 'The external oblique receives its nerve supply from the lower intercostal nerves (T7-T12) and the subcostal nerve (T12), which reach it from its deep surface. The ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves innervate the lower abdominal wall and skin, not the external oblique itself, while the femoral nerve serves the thigh.'),
('33fbe1ab-1bc4-5c88-8868-5a5a3c5e06a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The internal oblique muscle originates from?', '["Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior 2/3 of iliac crest, lateral 2/3 of inguinal ligament","Pubic crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Xiphoid process mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 5-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior 2/3 of iliac crest, lateral 2/3 of inguinal ligament', 'The internal oblique has a broad origin from the thoracolumbar fascia, the anterior two-thirds of the iliac crest, and the lateral two-thirds of the inguinal ligament. The pubic crest, xiphoid process, and ribs are not part of its origin.'),
('a7898e12-85d4-5d7f-a973-a51669149626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The internal oblique muscle fibers run?', '["Vertically resulting in decreased functional activity","Superomedially (perpendicular to external oblique)","Obliquely downward mediated by specific receptor activation","Transversely without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferomedially (parallel to external oblique) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superomedially (perpendicular to external oblique)', 'The internal oblique fibers run upward and medially, roughly perpendicular to the external oblique fibers, forming a criss-cross arrangement that strengthens the anterolateral abdominal wall. Descending or transverse orientations do not match its fiber direction.'),
('37fb89b8-fe30-56f8-bc52-214d92a41027'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The internal oblique inserts into?', '["Femur resulting in decreased functional activity","Greater trochanter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Linea alba, lower 4 ribs (10-12), pubic crest, pectineal line (via conjoint tendon)"]'::jsonb, 'Linea alba, lower 4 ribs (10-12), pubic crest, pectineal line (via conjoint tendon)', 'The internal oblique inserts into the linea alba, the lower four ribs (10-12), the pubic crest, and the pectineal line through the conjoint tendon. Femur, greater trochanter, sacrum, and ischium are attachments of lower limb and pelvic structures.'),
('b391bb97-c4db-5247-86d4-87acde2381c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The transversus abdominis originates from?', '["Outer surfaces of ribs 5-12 resulting in decreased functional activity","Inner surfaces of lower 6 costal cartilages, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral 1/3 of inguinal ligament","Xiphoid process only mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic bone only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner surfaces of lower 6 costal cartilages, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral 1/3 of inguinal ligament', 'The transversus abdominis arises from the inner surfaces of the lower six costal cartilages, the thoracolumbar fascia, the iliac crest, and the lateral third of the inguinal ligament. Its fibers run transversely from these origins toward the midline.'),
('eb36d76a-15ea-5d2b-9f03-0c5f2764169a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The transversus abdominis fibers run?', '["Obliquely","Inferomedially","Transversely (horizontally)","Superomedially","Vertically"]'::jsonb, 'Transversely (horizontally)', 'The transversus abdominis fibers run transversely (horizontally), acting like a corset to compress and support the abdominal contents. Oblique, vertical, and superomedial orientations describe the other abdominal wall muscles.'),
('4bde4f81-76c3-5167-9fbb-95bbf372a26d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which is the deepest of the three flat abdominal muscles?', '["External oblique","Internal oblique","Pyramidalis","Transversus abdominis","Rectus abdominis"]'::jsonb, 'Transversus abdominis', 'The transversus abdominis is the deepest of the three flat muscles, lying immediately deep to the internal oblique as the innermost muscular layer of the anterolateral abdominal wall. The external oblique is the most superficial flat muscle.'),
('ac31caea-3638-5b53-9e56-1f335cd7771d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inguinal canal is approximately?', '["4 cm (3.5-4 cm) long","2 cm long","6 cm long","8 cm long","10 cm long"]'::jsonb, '4 cm (3.5-4 cm) long', 'The inguinal canal measures roughly 3.5-4 cm in the adult, passing obliquely from the deep inguinal ring to the superficial inguinal ring. The other lengths are outside the accepted range.'),
('796f52f8-4763-5335-9ea7-2589932fe810'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inguinal canal extends between?', '["Internal ring to external ring","Anterior wall to posterior wall","Superficial ring to deep ring","None of the above","Deep inguinal ring to superficial inguinal ring"]'::jsonb, 'Deep inguinal ring to superficial inguinal ring', 'The inguinal canal runs obliquely downward and medially from the deep inguinal ring, an opening in the transversalis fascia, to the superficial inguinal ring in the external oblique aponeurosis. The canal has anterior and posterior walls and a roof and floor, but its length spans these two rings.'),
('4a00ee66-1734-5e26-95f8-7d3396752e9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The deep inguinal ring is a deficiency in?', '["Transversus abdominis","External oblique aponeurosis","Peritoneum","Transversalis fascia","Internal oblique aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'Transversalis fascia', 'The deep inguinal ring is an opening in the transversalis fascia, located about 1.25 cm above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament, where the spermatic cord enters the canal. The other listed layers do not contain this aperture.'),
('48165ac5-c4c5-5742-9818-d077b7eeb41a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The superficial inguinal ring is a deficiency in?', '["Transversus abdominis","Internal oblique","External oblique aponeurosis","Transversalis fascia","Rectus sheath"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique aponeurosis', 'The superficial inguinal ring is a triangular defect in the external oblique aponeurosis, situated superior and lateral to the pubic tubercle, through which the spermatic cord or round ligament exits the canal. The transversalis fascia contains the deep ring instead.'),
('1e2fc981-5830-5856-8a94-c42f15d21574'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The anterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by?', '["Transversus abdominis aponeurosis resulting in decreased functional activity","External oblique aponeurosis (reinforced by internal oblique laterally)","Internal oblique aponeurosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Conjoint tendon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transversalis fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique aponeurosis (reinforced by internal oblique laterally)', 'The anterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed chiefly by the external oblique aponeurosis, reinforced laterally over the deep ring by the internal oblique muscle fibers. The transversalis fascia and conjoint tendon contribute to the posterior wall.'),
('40116c35-a304-5d32-b4b1-81f5b6046cad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by?', '["Internal oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","Peritoneum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin and superficial fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","Transversalis fascia (reinforced by conjoint tendon medially)","External oblique aponeurosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transversalis fascia (reinforced by conjoint tendon medially)', 'The posterior wall is formed by the transversalis fascia, reinforced medially by the conjoint tendon, the fused aponeuroses of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis. The external oblique aponeurosis lies anteriorly rather than posteriorly.'),
('1287dcd8-9b43-5935-9e8f-ddcba81d0251'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The roof of the inguinal canal is formed by?', '["External oblique aponeurosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Transversalis fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Arching fibers of internal oblique and transversus abdominis","Conjoint tendon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arching fibers of internal oblique and transversus abdominis', 'The roof is formed by the arching fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles, which pass over the spermatic cord like an arch. The inguinal and lacunar ligaments form the floor of the canal.'),
('c310afeb-fffa-504a-a32f-c70c30e6d52a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The floor of the inguinal canal is formed by?', '["Conjoint tendon resulting in decreased functional activity","External oblique aponeurosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pectineal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Inguinal ligament (medially reinforced by lacunar ligament)","Transversalis fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal ligament (medially reinforced by lacunar ligament)', 'The floor is formed by the inguinal ligament, reinforced medially by the lacunar (Gimbernat) ligament, which creates a rigid inferior boundary for the canal. The arching fibers of the flat muscles form the roof.'),
('0756882e-3fb9-565d-99d6-fd037797b526'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which nerve lies in the inguinal canal anterior to the spermatic cord?', '["Femoral nerve (L2-L4)","Genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)","Iliohypogastric nerve (L1)","Subcostal nerve (T12)","Ilioinguinal nerve (L1)"]'::jsonb, 'Ilioinguinal nerve (L1)', 'The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) passes through the inguinal canal lying anterior to the spermatic cord, supplying sensation to the upper medial thigh and the anterior scrotum or labia majora. The genitofemoral nerve gives branches within the canal but does not lie anterior to the cord.'),
('2763d8a9-6d6a-5877-ad99-6de4d6671229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which nerve pierces the internal oblique to enter the inguinal canal?', '["Genitofemoral nerve","Subcostal nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve","Femoral nerve","Iliohypogastric nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Ilioinguinal nerve', 'The ilioinguinal nerve pierces the internal oblique muscle to enter the inguinal canal, then runs anterior to the spermatic cord. The iliohypogastric nerve also pierces the internal oblique but stays above the canal, supplying the hypogastric skin.'),
('53e0d947-46be-5a3c-a9f4-404fbf373193'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The spermatic cord is formed at the?', '["Deep inguinal ring","Superficial inguinal ring","Epididymis","Prostate","Testis"]'::jsonb, 'Deep inguinal ring', 'The spermatic cord is assembled at the deep inguinal ring, where the vas deferens, testicular vessels, and pampiniform plexus converge and enter the canal through the transversalis fascia. Its coverings are acquired as it passes through the abdominal wall layers.'),
('9f406bb4-e57b-51b7-b6f2-3156fa5de0f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The coverings of the spermatic cord from outermost to innermost are?', '["Cremasteric, internal, external","External spermatic, internal spermatic, cremasteric","None of the above","External spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia","Internal spermatic, cremasteric, external spermatic"]'::jsonb, 'External spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia', 'From superficial to deep, the cord coverings are the external spermatic fascia (from the external oblique), the cremasteric fascia (from the internal oblique and transversus abdominis), and the internal spermatic fascia (from the transversalis fascia). The remaining choices either reverse this order or omit a layer.'),
('e142ed42-27b9-5171-8366-8730f2037ff3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The external spermatic fascia is derived from?', '["Peritoneum","Internal oblique","Transversus abdominis","Transversalis fascia","External oblique aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique aponeurosis', 'The external spermatic fascia is derived from the external oblique aponeurosis at the superficial inguinal ring, forming the outermost covering of the spermatic cord. The transversalis fascia gives rise to the internal spermatic fascia instead.'),
('40d51d5e-2a1c-5ba5-bbf0-ba72ec2585d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The cremasteric fascia is derived from?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Transversalis fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectus sheath mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal oblique and transversus abdominis","External oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal oblique and transversus abdominis', 'The cremasteric fascia is derived from the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles and contains the cremaster muscle, forming the middle covering of the spermatic cord. The external oblique and transversalis fascia give rise to the outer and inner coverings, respectively.'),
('4c3dd4f7-9fa8-5188-bf92-29c2219687a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The internal spermatic fascia is derived from?', '["Peritoneum","External oblique","Transversus abdominis","Transversalis fascia","Internal oblique"]'::jsonb, 'Transversalis fascia', 'The internal spermatic fascia is derived from the transversalis fascia at the deep inguinal ring, forming the innermost covering of the spermatic cord. The external oblique and the flat muscle aponeuroses provide the outer and middle coverings.'),
('74102a37-e41f-58e6-b476-f1f9167eb621'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which of the following is NOT a content of the spermatic cord?', '["Testicular artery","Femoral artery","Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve","Vas deferens","Pampiniform plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral artery', 'The spermatic cord contains the vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, along with lymphatics and autonomic fibers. The femoral artery lies in the femoral triangle deep to the inguinal ligament and never enters the cord.'),
('4822045f-0e4f-53d4-8987-ba46a643e624'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The three arteries in the spermatic cord are?', '["None of the above","Testicular, superior epigastric, cremasteric","Testicular, cremasteric, artery of vas deferens","Cremasteric, inferior epigastric, femoral","Testicular, femoral, inferior epigastric"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular, cremasteric, artery of vas deferens', 'The spermatic cord carries the testicular artery (from the aorta), the cremasteric artery, and the artery to the vas deferens (deferential artery). The epigastric and femoral vessels do not travel within the cord.'),
('ae0a0abd-e0bc-56a0-aa48-088db09df36b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The testicular artery arises from?', '["Internal iliac artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Femoral artery","Abdominal aorta (at L2)","Renal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal aorta (at L2)', 'The testicular artery branches directly from the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries, near L2, and descends retroperitoneally to the deep inguinal ring. The internal iliac, SMA, and femoral arteries do not give origin to it.'),
('5e7e914c-614c-5a69-8303-20b6c092bc50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pampiniform plexus drains into?', '["Femoral vein","Renal vein","Portal vein","Testicular vein","Internal iliac vein"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular vein', 'The pampiniform plexus of veins around the testicular artery drains into the testicular vein. On the right this vein joins the IVC and on the left the left renal vein.'),
('9c4ebc85-2360-54b2-b34f-81e8be22ab36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right testicular vein drains into?', '["Left renal vein","IVC","Right renal vein","Azygos vein","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'IVC', 'The right testicular vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava. The left testicular vein, by contrast, joins the left renal vein.'),
('beea63d1-6c59-586b-9731-26bfb01b2a97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left testicular vein drains into?', '["Portal vein","Azygos vein","IVC","Left renal vein","Splenic vein"]'::jsonb, 'Left renal vein', 'The left testicular vein ascends to join the left renal vein, unlike the right testicular vein which enters the IVC directly. This right-angle insertion contributes to left-sided varicocele.'),
('fa65a464-4d9c-5a8c-9344-a912af92cdc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Varicocele is more common on the left side because?', '["Left testicular vein drains into left renal vein at a right angle","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right testicular vein drains into IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Left testicular vein is longer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right testicular vein is shorter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left testicular vein drains into left renal vein at a right angle', 'The left testicular vein joins the left renal vein at a right angle, creating higher back-pressure and less efficient drainage than the acute-angle entry of the right testicular vein into the IVC. This venous congestion explains the left-sided predilection of varicocele.'),
('238a66d3-d760-570c-858c-c2e1f9f96a3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The nerve supply of the cremaster muscle is from?', '["Ilioinguinal nerve (L1)","Femoral nerve (L2-L4)","Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)","Iliohypogastric nerve (L1)","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4)"]'::jsonb, 'Genital branch of genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)', 'The cremaster muscle is supplied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, which descends through the deep inguinal ring. The ilioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves supply the scrotal skin and adjacent abdominal skin, not the cremaster.'),
('ea6c1a05-f244-5918-abbc-25501c059f06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The cremasteric reflex is mediated by?', '["Genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)","Ilioinguinal (L1)","Iliohypogastric (L1)","Femoral (L2-L4)","Pudendal (S2-S4)"]'::jsonb, 'Genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)', 'The cremasteric reflex is a superficial reflex in which stroking the inner thigh raises the testis via contraction of the cremaster, a response carried by the genitofemoral nerve. The other listed nerves do not innervate the cremaster muscle.'),
('4fe340b8-efac-530f-8fe1-307fb76e7c26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The processus vaginalis is derived from?', '["Transversalis fascia","Scarpa fascia","Peritoneum","External oblique","Internal oblique"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneum', 'The processus vaginalis is a diverticulum of the parietal peritoneum that precedes the testis through the inguinal canal during descent. The abdominal wall fasciae and muscle aponeuroses do not contribute to it.'),
('8d88e44d-af50-51b5-8dae-f6f95d50ec6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'A patent processus vaginalis predisposes to?', '["Incisional hernia","Direct inguinal hernia","Femoral hernia","Umbilical hernia","Congenital indirect inguinal hernia"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital indirect inguinal hernia', 'A persistently patent processus vaginalis leaves an open tract from the peritoneal cavity to the scrotum through the deep inguinal ring, allowing abdominal contents to herniate along the path of testicular descent as a congenital indirect inguinal hernia. Direct and femoral hernias arise from separate weaknesses.'),
('d9713a44-0c36-5eda-bb0b-ba9b2d5bad06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The processus vaginalis normally obliterates?', '["Around the time of birth (full term)","At puberty","At 6 months gestation","At 3 months gestation","At 1 year of age"]'::jsonb, 'Around the time of birth (full term)', 'The processus vaginalis normally closes around the time of full-term birth, leaving the tunica vaginalis surrounding the testis. Early or delayed closure is the basis of congenital hydrocele and indirect inguinal hernia.'),
('5aef9bef-5bbb-5f20-96ce-ca07a88b8e10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Failure of obliteration of processus vaginalis may result in?', '["Epididymitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Testicular torsion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital hydrocele or indirect inguinal hernia","Varicocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orchitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital hydrocele or indirect inguinal hernia', 'If the processus vaginalis remains patent, fluid may track down to form a congenital hydrocele, or abdominal contents may descend to cause an indirect inguinal hernia. Epididymitis, torsion, varicocele, and orchitis are not produced by a patent processus vaginalis.'),
('869944f5-9bc9-56e3-93a4-6a77f94bde58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The gubernaculum testis attaches the testis to?', '["Pubic bone","Anterior abdominal wall","Scrotal wall (bottom of scrotum)","Inguinal canal","Posterior abdominal wall"]'::jsonb, 'Scrotal wall (bottom of scrotum)', 'The gubernaculum is a fibrous cord anchoring the lower pole of the testis to the floor of the scrotum, guiding descent from the posterior abdominal wall. The other listed structures are not its attachment site.'),
('d44bc4f3-6b7b-50ea-8c76-3233df6070d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Cryptorchidism refers to?', '["Absent testis","Undescended testis","Hydrocele","Supernumerary testis","Varicocele"]'::jsonb, 'Undescended testis', 'Cryptorchidism is the failure of one or both testes to descend into the scrotum, increasing the risk of malignancy and infertility. It is not absence of the testis, a hydrocele, a supernumerary testis, or a varicocele.'),
('ae52628b-5dd1-56dd-b838-b13c5aeebda7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Hesselbach triangle (inguinal triangle) is bounded by?', '["Inguinal ligament, femoral artery, sartorius resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior epigastric artery (superolateral), lateral border of rectus abdominis (medial), inguinal ligament (base)","Rectus abdominis, iliac crest, inguinal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep ring, superficial ring, inguinal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conjoint tendon, lacunar ligament, inguinal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior epigastric artery (superolateral), lateral border of rectus abdominis (medial), inguinal ligament (base)', 'Hesselbach triangle is bounded by the inferior epigastric vessels superolaterally, the lateral border of the rectus abdominis medially, and the inguinal ligament inferiorly. It is the weak site through which direct inguinal hernias protrude.'),
('f9bd3c67-baf5-5db9-8859-464e67183da8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Hesselbach triangle is also called?', '["Inguinal triangle","Femoral triangle","Umbilical triangle","Calot triangle","Lumbar triangle"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal triangle', 'Hesselbach triangle is synonymous with the inguinal triangle, the landmark for direct inguinal hernia. The femoral, Calot, and lumbar triangles are separate anatomical regions.'),
('6db79712-846f-53e0-80d1-162a1e1473b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Direct inguinal hernia protrudes through?', '["Femoral ring resulting in decreased functional activity","Umbilical ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hesselbach triangle (medial to inferior epigastric vessels)","Deep inguinal ring (lateral to inferior epigastric vessels) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obturator foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hesselbach triangle (medial to inferior epigastric vessels)', 'A direct inguinal hernia bulges through a weakness in the posterior wall of the inguinal canal within Hesselbach triangle, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels. It does not traverse the deep inguinal ring, femoral ring, or umbilicus.'),
('4cfb78b8-328d-5959-9840-5b497b604a4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Indirect inguinal hernia enters the abdominal wall through?', '["Linea alba resulting in decreased functional activity","Hesselbach triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep inguinal ring (lateral to inferior epigastric vessels)","Obturator canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep inguinal ring (lateral to inferior epigastric vessels)', 'An indirect inguinal hernia enters through the deep inguinal ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and follows the path of the processus vaginalis down the inguinal canal. Direct hernias, by contrast, emerge medial to the vessels through Hesselbach triangle.'),
('9f00fce1-eae3-5aa0-898a-10c754e51c1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'An inguinal hernia that lies above and medial to the pubic tubercle is?', '["Obturator hernia","Umbilical hernia","Femoral hernia","Spigelian hernia","Inguinal hernia (direct or indirect)"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal hernia (direct or indirect)', 'Both direct and indirect inguinal hernias emerge above and medial to the pubic tubercle, whereas a femoral hernia lies below and lateral to it. Obturator, umbilical, and Spigelian hernias occupy different sites.'),
('1b7180e9-bd19-54bd-8b30-836859c0ec44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'A femoral hernia lies?', '["At the umbilicus","Through the obturator foramen","Through the linea alba","Below and lateral to the pubic tubercle","Above and medial to the pubic tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Below and lateral to the pubic tubercle', 'A femoral hernia descends through the femoral canal and presents below and lateral to the pubic tubercle, unlike an inguinal hernia which lies above and medial to it. This relationship is the key clinical distinction.'),
('8c1b419e-b31c-5cac-8dc5-ac6deac99c7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Femoral hernia is more common in?', '["Equal in both sexes resulting in decreased functional activity","Females (due to wider pelvis and femoral ring)","Children mediated by specific receptor activation","Males without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infants leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Females (due to wider pelvis and femoral ring)', 'The wider female pelvis creates a larger femoral ring and canal, making femoral hernia more common in women than in men. Inguinal hernias, in contrast, are more frequent in males.'),
('e36a59e4-d03c-5393-98fb-a308bfa47eec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The femoral ring is bounded medially by?', '["Cooper ligament","Lacunar ligament (Gimbernat)","Inguinal ligament","Femoral vein","Pectineal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Lacunar ligament (Gimbernat)', 'The femoral ring is bounded medially by the lacunar ligament (Gimbernat), laterally by the femoral vein, and posteriorly by the pectineal ligament. The sharp edge of the lacunar ligament makes femoral hernias prone to strangulation.'),
('271e207a-53ea-5d84-b233-18a8080113f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The femoral sheath contains (from lateral to medial):', '["None of the above","Femoral nerve, artery, vein","Femoral artery, femoral vein, femoral canal","Femoral artery, nerve, vein","Femoral vein, femoral artery, femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral artery, femoral vein, femoral canal', 'From lateral to medial, the femoral sheath encloses the femoral artery, the femoral vein, and the femoral canal (which holds lymphatics and fat). The femoral nerve lies outside the sheath, so the other arrangements are incorrect.'),
('b3e19e1f-d1f2-5fd4-8aff-d7fc98ecb525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The conjoint tendon (falx inguinalis) is formed by?', '["External oblique aponeurosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Inguinal ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transversalis fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","Fused aponeurotic fibers of internal oblique and transversus abdominis","Rectus sheath leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fused aponeurotic fibers of internal oblique and transversus abdominis', 'The conjoint tendon is created by the fusion of the aponeurotic fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, reinforcing the medial posterior wall of the inguinal canal. The external oblique aponeurosis and transversalis fascia do not form it.'),
('0f3c8598-0e90-5dfa-85fd-6ea4aeed54f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The conjoint tendon inserts into?', '["ASIS and pubic tubercle","Iliac crest and lumbar fascia","Xiphoid and costal cartilages","Inguinal ligament and lacunar ligament","Pubic crest and pectineal line (pecten pubis)"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic crest and pectineal line (pecten pubis)', 'The conjoint tendon inserts into the pubic crest and the pectineal line of the pubis, anchoring the medial part of the inguinal canal''s posterior wall. It does not attach to the ASIS, iliac crest, or xiphoid.'),
('2a25a2f4-c31a-5a7f-9dc2-533a4a91bd0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The conjoint tendon reinforces which part of the inguinal canal?', '["Posterior wall (medially)","Anterior wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Floor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Roof leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior wall (medially)', 'The conjoint tendon, formed by fusion of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses, reinforces the medial part of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal. Its position overlies Hesselbach triangle, so its strength helps resist direct inguinal hernias. The anterior wall, floor, and roof are supported by other structures rather than the conjoint tendon.'),
('5d55280d-0840-529d-9f80-bd7e41730593'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inguinal ligament extends from?', '["Iliac crest to pubic tubercle","ASIS to pubic symphysis","Sacroiliac joint to pubic symphysis","ASIS to pubic tubercle","Costal margin to pubic bone"]'::jsonb, 'ASIS to pubic tubercle', 'The inguinal ligament is the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis, extending from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the pubic tubercle. It forms the floor of the inguinal canal. The other choices name incorrect attachment points (iliac crest, symphysis, costal margin, or sacroiliac joint).'),
('33bdf4fa-3038-5510-bb93-e82294f83f0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inguinal ligament is the inferior thickened edge of which aponeurosis?', '["Rectus sheath","Internal oblique","External oblique","Transversalis fascia","Transversus abdominis"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique', 'The inguinal ligament is the thickened, inturned inferior free edge of the external oblique aponeurosis, spanning between the ASIS and the pubic tubercle. The rectus sheath forms a separate enclosure around the rectus abdominis, and the internal oblique, transversus abdominis, and transversalis fascia do not contribute this thickened edge.'),
('e09c0a71-be57-52f0-a3fb-1bc461feda88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lacunar ligament (Gimbernat) extends from?', '["Pubic tubercle to pecten pubis","Inguinal ligament to iliopubic eminence","ASIS to pubic tubercle","Conjoint tendon to pecten pubis","Medial end of inguinal ligament to pecten pubis"]'::jsonb, 'Medial end of inguinal ligament to pecten pubis', 'The lacunar ligament runs from the medial end of the inguinal ligament to the pecten pubis, forming the sharp medial boundary of the femoral ring. The other attachment patterns name the wrong structures (pubic tubercle, iliopubic eminence, ASIS, or conjoint tendon) and do not describe this ligament.'),
('07b413fc-b94d-5154-acfe-e3273740acc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pectineal ligament (Cooper) is located?', '["On the sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","On the inferior pubic ramus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along the pecten pubis on the superior pubic ramus","On the iliac crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the ischium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Along the pecten pubis on the superior pubic ramus', 'The pectineal (Cooper) ligament is the lateral continuation of the lacunar ligament running along the pecten pubis on the superior pubic ramus. It forms part of the posterior boundary of the femoral ring. The sacrum, inferior pubic ramus, iliac crest, and ischium are not its location.'),
('0bc96b07-2f4d-509a-afa4-85ccfdfeb7ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The subcostal nerve (T12) runs between which muscles?', '["Transversus abdominis and transversalis fascia","In the inguinal canal","Internal oblique and transversus abdominis","In the rectus sheath","External and internal oblique"]'::jsonb, 'Internal oblique and transversus abdominis', 'The subcostal nerve (T12) runs in the neurovascular plane between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, supplying the lower anterior abdominal wall. It does not lie in the inguinal canal, within the rectus sheath, or in the superficial plane between the external and internal oblique.'),
('fc37f30a-75f9-5799-ae36-73f2106a4dbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliohypogastric nerve (L1) runs between which muscles?', '["In the femoral canal","Internal oblique and transversus abdominis","In the spermatic cord","In the rectus sheath","External and internal oblique"]'::jsonb, 'Internal oblique and transversus abdominis', 'The iliohypogastric nerve (L1) travels between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, later piercing the internal oblique to reach the lower abdominal wall and suprapubic skin. It does not run in the femoral canal, spermatic cord, rectus sheath, or superficial intermuscular plane.'),
('a7b3b18d-fd9f-5fd9-a2da-ace994e05c0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) runs between which muscles?', '["External and internal oblique only resulting in decreased functional activity","In the rectus sheath accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Initially between internal oblique and transversus abdominis, then pierces internal oblique","In the spermatic cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Initially between internal oblique and transversus abdominis, then pierces internal oblique', 'The ilioinguinal nerve initially runs between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, then pierces the internal oblique to enter the inguinal canal alongside the spermatic cord and supply the scrotum or labium majus. The other options give an incomplete or incorrect course.'),
('a9ddd29a-e089-5ec8-9c0d-e25c7abf78b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2) lies on which muscle?', '["Posterior surface of psoas major","Between psoas and quadratus lumborum","On iliacus","On the iliac crest","Anterior surface of psoas major"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior surface of psoas major', 'The genitofemoral nerve pierces the psoas major and emerges on its anterior surface, where it divides into the genital and femoral branches. It does not lie on the posterior psoas, between psoas and quadratus lumborum, on iliacus, or on the iliac crest.'),
('6f8b7eef-b91e-51c0-886b-39efcea5a209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The genitofemoral nerve divides into?', '["Genital and femoral branches","None of the above","Femoral and iliohypogastric branches","Genital and ilioinguinal branches","Femoral and cutaneous branches"]'::jsonb, 'Genital and femoral branches', 'The genitofemoral nerve divides into a genital branch that enters the inguinal canal (supplying the cremaster muscle and skin of the scrotum or labium majus) and a femoral branch that supplies the skin of the upper medial thigh. The other options incorrectly pair the genitofemoral nerve with the iliohypogastric or ilioinguinal nerves.'),
('1c43268f-3515-58e4-a59b-ac07168d4fca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lateral cutaneous branches of intercostal nerves pierce which muscle?', '["Rectus sheath resulting in decreased functional activity","External oblique (in the midaxillary line)","Linea alba mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal oblique without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transversus abdominis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique (in the midaxillary line)', 'The lateral cutaneous branches of the intercostal and subcostal nerves pierce the external oblique muscle in the midaxillary line to supply the skin of the lateral abdominal wall. They do not pierce the rectus sheath, linea alba, internal oblique, or transversus abdominis at this site.'),
('bcc81dbf-228d-5176-a58a-152b0fab72f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The anterior cutaneous branches of intercostal nerves pierce?', '["External oblique aponeurosis","Anterior rectus sheath","Internal oblique","Linea alba","Transversalis fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rectus sheath', 'The anterior cutaneous branches of the intercostal nerves pierce the anterior rectus sheath lateral to the linea alba to supply the skin of the anterior abdominal wall. They do not pierce the external oblique aponeurosis, internal oblique, linea alba, or transversalis fascia.'),
('0fe812ab-0d1d-58fa-9778-0d69a197d3f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The McBurney (gridiron) incision is used for?', '["Inguinal hernia repair","Appendectomy","Nephrectomy","Splenectomy","Cholecystectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Appendectomy', 'The McBurney gridiron incision is a muscle-splitting incision placed over McBurney''s point and specifically designed for appendectomy. Inguinal hernia repair uses incisions over the inguinal region, while nephrectomy, splenectomy, and cholecystectomy require incisions in other locations.'),
('182243bb-7963-52e9-ab51-d2d9d118dda2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which nerve is most at risk during McBurney incision for appendectomy?', '["Subcostal nerve (T12)","Ilioinguinal nerve (L1)","Genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)","Iliohypogastric nerve (L1)","Femoral nerve (L2-L4)"]'::jsonb, 'Iliohypogastric nerve (L1)', 'The iliohypogastric nerve (L1) crosses the region where the McBurney gridiron incision is made, so it is most at risk of injury during appendectomy; damage can weaken the lower abdominal wall muscles. The subcostal, ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and femoral nerves lie outside the main field of this incision.'),
('df4fc707-d1ca-5f2c-8f03-ddd43f0c7119'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The Kocher (subcostal) incision is used for?', '["Splenectomy","Colectomy","Cholecystectomy","Inguinal hernia repair","Appendectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Cholecystectomy', 'The Kocher subcostal incision is made parallel to and just below the right costal margin, giving excellent exposure for cholecystectomy (gallbladder surgery). Splenectomy, colectomy, hernia repair, and appendectomy are performed through incisions designed for their own regions.'),
('569d131f-6681-5a8d-975c-9ccf96542e8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The Pfannenstiel incision is described as?', '["Midline vertical incision resulting in decreased functional activity","Paramedian incision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transverse suprapubic incision (used in cesarean section)","Subcostal incision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thoracoabdominal incision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse suprapubic incision (used in cesarean section)', 'The Pfannenstiel incision is a transverse suprapubic incision that follows a skin crease and is commonly used for cesarean section and gynecological procedures. The other options describe vertical, paramedian, subcostal, or thoracoabdominal approaches.'),
('44730d63-1194-5d20-8291-b6c028c9b8ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Spigelian hernia occurs through?', '["Inguinal triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","Linea semilunaris (lateral border of rectus sheath)","Umbilicus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lumbar triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Linea alba leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea semilunaris (lateral border of rectus sheath)', 'A Spigelian hernia protrudes through the Spigelian fascia along the linea semilunaris, the lateral border of the rectus sheath where the flat muscle aponeuroses merge. The inguinal triangle, umbilicus, lumbar triangle, and linea alba are the sites of other hernia types.'),
('ae9cfdf8-b00d-5162-b26b-be8b16ee6926'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The linea semilunaris is?', '["The arcuate line","The lateral curved border of the rectus sheath","The midline raphe (linea alba)","A tendinous intersection of rectus","The medial border of the rectus sheath"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral curved border of the rectus sheath', 'The linea semilunaris is the lateral curved border of the rectus sheath, where the aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis merge. The arcuate line, linea alba, and tendinous intersections are separate, distinct features.'),
('1a1a1f2b-a062-5701-8df9-d194c4defb71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which of the following is true regarding the anterior abdominal wall?', '["Above the arcuate line the posterior rectus sheath has two layers","The rectus sheath is formed by aponeuroses of external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis","Below the arcuate line all three aponeuroses pass anterior to rectus abdominis","All of the above","The conjoint tendon reinforces the posterior inguinal wall medially"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Every statement is true: above the arcuate line the posterior rectus sheath splits into two layers, the sheath is built from the three flat abdominal aponeuroses, below the arcuate line all aponeuroses pass anteriorly, and the conjoint tendon reinforces the medial posterior inguinal wall. Selecting only one of the individual facts would be incomplete.'),
('9f9a04ec-a01b-53f1-8fe0-7dc48e3bccf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the origin of the external oblique muscle?', '["Pubic tubercle","Lumbar vertebrae","Iliac crest only","Outer surfaces of ribs 5-12","Thoracolumbar fascia"]'::jsonb, 'Outer surfaces of ribs 5-12', 'The external oblique arises from the outer surfaces of the lower eight ribs (5-12), with fibers running inferomedially toward the aponeurosis. The pubic tubercle, lumbar vertebrae, iliac crest, and thoracolumbar fascia are not its origin.'),
('f3040152-1ec4-5b91-84cc-3c3211ddcd67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the origin of the internal oblique muscle?', '["Linea alba resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubic crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior iliac crest, lateral inguinal ligament","Xiphoid process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ribs 5-12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar fascia, anterior iliac crest, lateral inguinal ligament', 'The internal oblique originates from the thoracolumbar fascia, the anterior two-thirds of the iliac crest, and the lateral two-thirds of the inguinal ligament, with fibers fanning superomedially. The linea alba, pubic crest, xiphoid, and ribs 5-12 are not its origin.'),
('8d0762ab-2b4e-5fbd-a273-ce16e14317cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the origin of the transversus abdominis muscle?', '["Inner costal cartilages 7-12, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral 1/3 inguinal ligament","Pubic bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Xiphoid only mediated by specific receptor activation","Outer ribs 5-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner costal cartilages 7-12, thoracolumbar fascia, iliac crest, lateral 1/3 inguinal ligament', 'The transversus abdominis arises from the inner surfaces of the costal cartilages 7-12, the thoracolumbar fascia, the iliac crest, and the lateral third of the inguinal ligament, with fibers running horizontally. The pubic bone, xiphoid, outer ribs, and ''none'' do not describe its origin.'),
('cb471c54-f6b3-5ee3-88ca-db5aa2ff8235'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the insertion of the external oblique muscle?', '["Lesser trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","Xiphoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater trochanter mediated by specific receptor activation","Linea alba, pubic tubercle, anterior iliac crest","Pubic symphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea alba, pubic tubercle, anterior iliac crest', 'The external oblique inserts through its broad aponeurosis into the linea alba, the pubic tubercle, and the anterior half of the iliac crest, with the inferior edge forming the inguinal ligament. The trochanters, xiphoid, and pubic symphysis are not its insertion sites.'),
('07e6bd8a-fdeb-59c1-b100-8c58cdb2f09c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the insertion of the internal oblique muscle?', '["Femur resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater trochanter mediated by specific receptor activation","Linea alba, ribs 10-12, pubic crest, pectineal line via conjoint tendon","Sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linea alba, ribs 10-12, pubic crest, pectineal line via conjoint tendon', 'The internal oblique inserts into the linea alba, the inferior ribs (10-12), the pubic crest, and the pectineal line via the conjoint tendon, which reinforces the posterior inguinal wall. The femur, ischium, greater trochanter, and sacrum are not its insertion sites.'),
('3d7d8957-f86e-5527-877c-1db111fdf52e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Anterior Abdominal Wall', 'The rectus sheath is formed by aponeuroses of which muscles?', '["External and internal oblique only","All abdominal muscles","Rectus and pyramidalis","External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis","Internal oblique and transversus only"]'::jsonb, 'External oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis', 'The rectus sheath is formed by the aponeuroses of all three flat abdominal muscles: the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis, with the arrangement of layers changing at the arcuate line. The rectus and pyramidalis lie inside the sheath rather than forming it, and omitting any of the three aponeuroses is incorrect.'),
('a9f55dbd-0f8b-5a9d-acfb-5aa69a9f08f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The jejunum is characterized by?', '["Thick wall, tall plicae circulares, long vasa recta, less fat in mesentery","Thin wall, fewer plicae, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The presence of Peyer patches mediated by specific receptor activation","The presence of taenia coli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thick wall, tall plicae circulares, long vasa recta, less fat in mesentery', 'The jejunum has a thicker wall, taller plicae circulares, and longer vasa recta than the ileum, and its mesentery contains less fat. The thin-walled, Peyer-patch-bearing segment is the ileum, while taenia coli belong to the colon.'),
('ea1c5376-b873-5060-ac6b-d130ffd6a045'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileum is characterized by?', '["Thick wall, tall plicae, long vasa recta resulting in decreased functional activity","Thin wall, fewer plicae circulares, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat, Peyer patches","The presence of haustra mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The presence of taenia coli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin wall, fewer plicae circulares, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat, Peyer patches', 'The ileum has a thinner wall, fewer and shorter plicae circulares, shorter vasa recta, and more mesenteric fat, and it is rich in Peyer patches for immune surveillance. Taenia coli and haustra are colonic features, and the thick-wall profile belongs to the jejunum.'),
('0419adf5-5d85-5043-94da-f5036268a65d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileocecal junction is?', '["The rectosigmoid junction resulting in decreased functional activity","The pylorus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The junction between the ileum and cecum (ileocecal valve)","The junction of jejunum and ileum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The junction between the ileum and cecum (ileocecal valve)', 'The ileocecal junction is where the terminal ileum meets the cecum, guarded by the ileocecal valve. The rectosigmoid junction, pylorus, and jejunoileal junction are all separate sites.'),
('f9e319c8-ecb1-50f7-add2-1d1ed12909db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'McBurney point is located?', '["At the umbilicus resulting in decreased functional activity","At the ASIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1/3 of the way from ASIS to umbilicus (on the spinoumbilical line)","At the pubic tubercle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midpoint of inguinal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1/3 of the way from ASIS to umbilicus (on the spinoumbilical line)', 'McBurney point lies one-third of the distance from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus along the spinoumbilical line and overlies the base of the appendix. The umbilicus, ASIS, pubic tubercle, and inguinal ligament midpoint mark other landmarks.'),
('d5644cf8-755a-5fac-b2f1-c6eed746e2d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is most commonly located?', '["Postileal (behind terminal ileum)","Preileal (in front of terminal ileum)","Retrocecal (behind the cecum)","Subcecal (below the cecum)","Pelvic (into the pelvis)"]'::jsonb, 'Retrocecal (behind the cecum)', 'The appendix most commonly lies retrocecal, behind the cecum, in roughly two-thirds of individuals. The pelvic, subcecal, preileal, and postileal positions are less common variants.'),
('3558ce65-5622-5c67-8e7b-f3d6e661d8b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The colon is divided into which parts?', '["Ascending, horizontal, descending, sigmoid","None of the above","Proximal, middle, distal, sigmoid","Right, middle, left, sigmoid","Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid', 'The colon is divided into ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid parts. The other groupings do not represent the standard anatomical divisions of the large intestine.'),
('88fc8808-9bed-5d45-b019-56e39e43fc67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The taenia coli are?', '["Circular muscle fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Haustra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epiploic appendages mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle on the colon"]'::jsonb, 'Three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle on the colon', 'The taenia coli are three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle in the colon wall. Haustra are sacculations, epiploic appendages are fat-filled pouches, and the circular muscle forms a separate layer.'),
('b7a8fd76-9bb7-511f-b7d5-0edc664d12be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The haustra of the colon are?', '["Longitudinal muscle bands resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circular folds mediated by specific receptor activation","Fatty appendages without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sacculations (pouches) of the colon wall between taeniae"]'::jsonb, 'Sacculations (pouches) of the colon wall between taeniae', 'Haustra are sacculations, or pouches, of the colon wall produced between the taeniae coli. Longitudinal muscle bands, circular folds, and fatty appendages are distinct structures.'),
('02229738-184d-5de3-89f6-eeaf0217bcb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Epiploic appendages are?', '["Circular muscle thickenings","Lymph nodes of the colon","Small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum on the colon","None of the above","Blood vessels of the colon"]'::jsonb, 'Small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum on the colon', 'Epiploic appendages are small fat-filled pouches of peritoneum hanging from the colon wall. Muscle thickenings, lymph nodes, and blood vessels are separate components of the colon.'),
('7c6d5e0d-4819-56db-8e6a-26c31adbd96b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) supplies?', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Foregut structures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hindgut structures mediated by specific receptor activation","Left colon only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midgut: distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal 2/3 of transverse colon"]'::jsonb, 'Midgut: distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal 2/3 of transverse colon', 'The SMA supplies the entire midgut, from the distal duodenum through the ascending colon to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, including the appendix. The foregut, hindgut, and rectum are supplied by other arteries.'),
('7cdaec74-30a6-516a-bb45-0648462800b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) supplies?', '["Foregut structures resulting in decreased functional activity","Hindgut: distal 1/3 of transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum","Duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","Midgut structures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hindgut: distal 1/3 of transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum', 'The IMA supplies the hindgut, including the distal third of the transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum. The foregut, duodenum, and midgut depend on the celiac trunk and SMA.'),
('82773267-a62c-5321-9d9f-b80ca12b17e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileocolic artery is a branch of?', '["Superior mesenteric artery","Common iliac artery","Celiac trunk","Inferior mesenteric artery","Internal iliac artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The ileocolic artery arises from the superior mesenteric artery and supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix. The celiac trunk and iliac arteries have different distributions.'),
('5e13e814-c6fe-5d15-800c-a1901bb0e471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendicular artery is a branch of?', '["Inferior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric artery directly","Ileocolic artery","Right colic artery","Celiac trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocolic artery', 'The appendicular artery is a branch of the ileocolic artery, which itself arises from the superior mesenteric artery. The right colic artery and celiac trunk do not give rise to this vessel.'),
('393e7575-00f5-5fe7-b798-9b1c0f65618f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The marginal artery of Drummond is?', '["The left colic artery resulting in decreased functional activity","An anastomotic arcade along the mesenteric border of the colon connecting SMA and IMA","The right colic artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The artery of the appendix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An anastomotic arcade along the mesenteric border of the colon connecting SMA and IMA', 'The marginal artery of Drummond is an anastomotic arcade running along the mesenteric border of the colon, linking the SMA and IMA territories. The right and left colic arteries feed into it rather than forming it.'),
('356cd8c1-350e-5787-9a77-593e7a22ae39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The critical point of Sudeck is?', '["The splenic flexure resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ileocecal junction mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The junction of SMA and IMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The point in the sigmoid colon where the marginal artery is absent or weak (at the junction of last sigmoid and superior rectal artery)"]'::jsonb, 'The point in the sigmoid colon where the marginal artery is absent or weak (at the junction of last sigmoid and superior rectal artery)', 'The critical point of Sudeck is the site in the sigmoid colon where the marginal artery is absent or weak, near the junction of the last sigmoid artery and the superior rectal artery. The splenic flexure and ileocecal junction are different landmarks.'),
('ec0d0433-2bde-503a-b9f8-79eb2b97003a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The jejunum has which type of mesentery?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesentery proper (attached to posterior abdominal wall)","Rarely mediated by specific receptor activation","Transverse mesocolon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sigmoid mesocolon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesentery proper (attached to posterior abdominal wall)', 'The jejunum and ileum are suspended by the mesentery proper, which runs from the duodenojejunal flexure to the ileocecal junction. The transverse and sigmoid mesocolons attach the colon, not the jejunum.'),
('936b5fc6-60c6-550e-949e-71f2f0d73640'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The jejunum begins at the?', '["Pylorus resulting in decreased functional activity","Ileocecal junction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)","Ligament of Treitz without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)', 'The jejunum begins at the duodenojejunal flexure, the site of the suspensory muscle (ligament of Treitz). The pylorus marks the start of the duodenum, and the ileum ends at the ileocecal junction.'),
('7257d7d2-8ed0-5855-8676-b811b3822df8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileum ends at the?', '["Cecum resulting in decreased functional activity","Sigmoid colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","Duodenojejunal flexure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)', 'The ileum ends at the ileocecal junction, where the ileocecal valve opens into the cecum. The cecum, sigmoid colon, and rectosigmoid junction are downstream sites.'),
('6d574cdb-ca50-5ba2-9381-5a4cadb0aa27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The cecum is?', '["The first part of the large intestine, a blind pouch below the ileocecal valve","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","The last part of the small intestine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The appendix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first part of the large intestine, a blind pouch below the ileocecal valve', 'The cecum is the blind pouch that forms the first part of the large intestine, lying below the ileocecal valve. The rectum and appendix are separate structures, and the cecum is not part of the small intestine.'),
('343391c8-7451-5564-bac5-70841b401f93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The vermiform appendix arises from?', '["The ascending colon resulting in decreased functional activity","The cecal base accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posteromedial wall of the cecum (about 2 cm below the ileocecal valve)","The cecal tip without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The terminal ileum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posteromedial wall of the cecum (about 2 cm below the ileocecal valve)', 'The vermiform appendix arises from the posteromedial wall of the cecum, about 2 cm below the ileocecal valve. It does not take origin from the cecal tip, ascending colon, or terminal ileum.'),
('0b26fbd8-b099-5473-b309-1fcb593da72a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The blood supply of the appendix is from?', '["Superior mesenteric artery directly","Right colic artery","Celiac trunk","Inferior mesenteric artery","Appendicular artery (branch of ileocolic artery)"]'::jsonb, 'Appendicular artery (branch of ileocolic artery)', 'The appendix is supplied by the appendicular artery, a branch of the ileocolic artery (from the SMA). The right colic artery and celiac trunk do not supply the appendix.'),
('1f7d8f02-6dbe-54f7-a60f-bb6bb537e67b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The lymphatics of the cecum and appendix drain to?', '["Internal iliac nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Para-aortic nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial inguinal nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ileocolic lymph nodes to superior mesenteric nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocolic lymph nodes to superior mesenteric nodes', 'Lymphatics of the cecum and appendix drain to the ileocolic lymph nodes and then to the superior mesenteric nodes. The inguinal and internal iliac nodes drain other territories.'),
('8dcba2e1-38c6-5d0e-90d6-97bd5f0d7a52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The nerve supply of the small intestine is from?', '["Sympathetic (splanchnic nerves, SMA plexus) and parasympathetic (vagus)","Only vagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only phrenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only parasympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (splanchnic nerves, SMA plexus) and parasympathetic (vagus)', 'The small intestine receives sympathetic innervation via splanchnic nerves and the SMA plexus and parasympathetic innervation via the vagus nerve. Neither pathway acts alone in supplying the small intestine.'),
('f4dd57ca-7656-5914-882a-095e75feac96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The nerve supply of the right colon is from?', '["Only sympathetic","None of the above","Only parasympathetic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4)","Vagus and sympathetic (SMA plexus)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus and sympathetic (SMA plexus)', 'The right colon is innervated by the vagus (parasympathetic) and sympathetic fibers traveling with the SMA plexus. Pelvic splanchnic nerves supply the hindgut, not the right colon.'),
('f78b3e07-6413-5c5d-ad56-5a10a207ed8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The nerve supply of the left colon is from?', '["Vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) and sympathetic (IMA plexus)","Only sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) and sympathetic (IMA plexus)', 'The left colon receives parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) and sympathetic supply via the IMA plexus. The vagus does not reach the distal colon.'),
('e95fcf33-4bf6-5435-9df2-c5c503e53589'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Haustra are best developed in which part of the colon?', '["Cecum","Ascending colon","Transverse and sigmoid colon","Descending colon","Rectum"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse and sigmoid colon', 'Haustra are best developed in the transverse and sigmoid colon, where the taeniae are most active. The cecum, ascending colon, and descending colon show less pronounced sacculation.'),
('95fe1fb3-f716-5d1d-b2d2-61f4e5a42ced'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The taeniae coli converge at the base of the?', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Appendix (all three taeniae meet at the appendix base)","Sigmoid colon mediated by specific receptor activation","Ileocecal valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cecum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Appendix (all three taeniae meet at the appendix base)', 'All three taeniae coli converge at the base of the appendix, making it a reliable surgical landmark for locating the appendix. Elsewhere the taeniae spread over the colon wall and fuse differently at the rectum.'),
('e6a0b9c3-fb1b-5502-86c5-a8e5421de2dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring) are most prominent in?', '["Duodenum","Colon","Ileum","Stomach","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum', 'Plicae circulares are most prominent in the jejunum and become sparse in the distal ileum. They are absent in the stomach and colon, and the duodenum has fewer.'),
('4100cdc4-fa7f-5076-8709-e882c08f83e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Peyer patches (lymphoid aggregates) are most numerous in?', '["Ileum (especially in the terminal ileum)","Stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","Jejunum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ileum (especially in the terminal ileum)', 'Peyer patches are most numerous in the ileum, particularly the terminal ileum. The jejunum, duodenum, stomach, and colon contain far fewer lymphoid aggregates.'),
('9545d5f2-6878-5baf-a3ce-15ccc7e7dfc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The mesentery proper attaches the small intestine to?', '["Greater omentum resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse mesocolon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior abdominal wall (from duodenojejunal flexure to ileocecal junction)","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior abdominal wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior abdominal wall (from duodenojejunal flexure to ileocecal junction)', 'The mesentery proper attaches the jejunum and ileum to the posterior abdominal wall, running from the duodenojejunal flexure to the ileocecal junction. The omenta and mesocolons attach other organs.'),
('d92f4499-5f5d-5db1-a1ba-64b9bfdad491'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The root of the mesentery runs from the?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach to colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esophagus to rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Duodenojejunal flexure (left of L2) to the ileocecal junction (right sacroiliac joint)","Xiphoid to pubis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenojejunal flexure (left of L2) to the ileocecal junction (right sacroiliac joint)', 'The root of the mesentery runs obliquely from the duodenojejunal flexure (left of L2) to the ileocecal junction at the right sacroiliac joint. The other listed courses do not match this attachment.'),
('58ec49c3-b68e-57ef-9ec1-89a55d40d825'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The sigmoid colon begins at the?', '["Splenic flexure resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midline mediated by specific receptor activation","Left iliac crest and ends at the rectosigmoid junction (S3)","Right iliac fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left iliac crest and ends at the rectosigmoid junction (S3)', 'The sigmoid colon begins at the left iliac crest and ends at the rectosigmoid junction at the level of S3. The splenic flexure and right iliac fossa mark other boundaries.'),
('f0629982-4389-5004-baee-6231ebf82842'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectosigmoid junction is at the level of?', '["S3 vertebra","S1 vertebra","L5 vertebra","Coccyx","S5 vertebra"]'::jsonb, 'S3 vertebra', 'The rectosigmoid junction lies at the level of the third sacral vertebra (S3). The other listed levels correspond to different pelvic landmarks.'),
('ee354ce2-f141-5d3c-bf93-a0083f27385f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The superior rectal artery is the terminal branch of?', '["None of the above","Internal iliac artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Middle rectal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery', 'The superior rectal artery is the continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery. The middle rectal and internal iliac vessels supply other parts of the rectum.'),
('95bb4c38-6d11-55fc-86f0-61a45c5df7cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The middle rectal artery is a branch of?', '["Femoral artery","None of the above","External iliac artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Internal iliac artery (anterior division)"]'::jsonb, 'Internal iliac artery (anterior division)', 'The middle rectal artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery. The inferior mesenteric, external iliac, and femoral arteries are not sources of this branch.'),
('1458268c-9ff9-5797-8115-04a35c0ddc6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The inferior rectal artery is a branch of?', '["Inferior mesenteric artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal pudendal artery (from internal iliac)","External iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal pudendal artery (from internal iliac)', 'The inferior rectal artery is a branch of the internal pudendal artery, which itself arises from the internal iliac artery. The inferior mesenteric, femoral, and external iliac arteries are not involved.'),
('15a9f963-34f0-51a4-b41a-978b381858ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendicular artery runs in the?', '["None of the above","Mesentery of the appendix (mesoappendix)","Directly through the appendix wall","Inside the lumen of the appendix","Mesoappendix behind the appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Mesentery of the appendix (mesoappendix)', 'The appendicular artery runs in the free margin of the mesoappendix before reaching the appendix. It does not pass through the appendicular wall or lumen.'),
('d8b05e5f-20f5-567d-a127-2f2443d0e41a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'A volvulus most commonly affects which part of the colon?', '["Cecum","Descending colon","Transverse colon","Sigmoid colon","Ascending colon"]'::jsonb, 'Sigmoid colon', 'Sigmoid volvulus is the most common colonic volvulus, favored by the long, mobile sigmoid loop. The other colonic segments are less commonly affected.'),
('57fdba97-1573-58dc-87c8-5f2b9ad3a6da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The right colic artery is a branch of?', '["Superior mesenteric artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Ileocolic artery","None of the above","Celiac trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The right colic artery arises from the superior mesenteric artery. The inferior mesenteric artery, celiac trunk, and ileocolic artery are different sources.'),
('f0ed7c2d-0b20-5b02-a82c-cca66cca41b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left colic artery is a branch of?', '["Superior mesenteric artery","None of the above","Inferior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","Middle colic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery', 'The left colic artery arises from the inferior mesenteric artery. The SMA, celiac trunk, and middle colic artery do not give off this branch.'),
('62a3459a-74bc-5537-97d3-eec68ef1ddce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The middle colic artery is a branch of?', '["Inferior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric artery","Right colic artery","None of the above","Celiac trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The middle colic artery arises from the superior mesenteric artery. The inferior mesenteric artery and celiac trunk supply other regions.'),
('598c9677-a32f-5cfd-abb9-d92a46dc8a84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The sigmoid arteries are branches of?', '["Middle colic artery","Internal iliac artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Superior mesenteric artery","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery', 'The sigmoid arteries are branches of the inferior mesenteric artery. The middle colic and internal iliac arteries have different distributions.'),
('efa7ba1a-66f9-52d4-b880-f4a769b63f76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Which of the following is true regarding the intestines and colon?', '["The jejunum has tall plicae circulares and long vasa recta","The ileum has Peyer patches and short vasa recta","The marginal artery of Drummond connects SMA and IMA","All of the above","The appendix is most commonly retrocecal"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Each statement is accurate: the jejunum has tall plicae circulares and long vasa recta, the ileum carries Peyer patches and short vasa recta, the marginal artery of Drummond connects SMA and IMA, and the appendix is most commonly retrocecal.'),
('251584d7-174a-50c6-b4fb-55238933b946'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'What are the characteristics of the jejunum?', '["Thin wall, few plicae, short vasa recta, more fat","Has haustra","Thick wall, tall plicae, long vasa recta, less fat","Has taenia coli","Has Peyer patches"]'::jsonb, 'Thick wall, tall plicae, long vasa recta, less fat', 'The jejunum has a thick wall, tall plicae circulares, and long vasa recta with less mesenteric fat. The thin-wall, Peyer-patch features belong to the ileum, and taenia coli and haustra are colonic.'),
('b5010e3c-3440-5130-a57e-43c1e9566e8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'What are the characteristics of the ileum?', '["Has taenia coli resulting in decreased functional activity","Has haustra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thin wall, few plicae, short vasa recta, more fat, Peyer patches","Thick wall, tall plicae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin wall, few plicae, short vasa recta, more fat, Peyer patches', 'The ileum has a thin wall, fewer plicae, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat, and abundant Peyer patches. Taenia coli and haustra are colonic features, and the thick-wall profile describes the jejunum.'),
('14c0e8eb-d41e-57e7-8445-f302b6415689'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'What does the SMA supply?', '["Stomach only resulting in decreased functional activity","Midgut: distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 transverse colon","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Hindgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foregut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midgut: distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 transverse colon', 'The SMA supplies the midgut, from the distal duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The stomach, foregut, and hindgut have other arterial sources.'),
('1da166f2-9b1a-56c6-ac0c-4b7cdb8dbbde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'What does the IMA supply?', '["Duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","Hindgut: distal 1/3 transverse colon to rectum","Foregut mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midgut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hindgut: distal 1/3 transverse colon to rectum', 'The IMA supplies the hindgut, from the distal third of the transverse colon through the descending and sigmoid colon to the rectum. The stomach, duodenum, and midgut depend on the celiac trunk and SMA.'),
('de4416d2-b386-5436-b0d3-33f70c8ed6aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Jejunum characteristics:', '["Thin wall, few plicae, short vasa recta resulting in decreased functional activity","Has Peyer patches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Has taenia coli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thick wall, tall plicae, long vasa recta, less fat"]'::jsonb, 'Thick wall, tall plicae, long vasa recta, less fat', 'The jejunum has a thick wall, tall plicae circulares, and long vasa recta with less mesenteric fat. The thin-wall, Peyer-patch features belong to the ileum, and taenia coli are colonic.'),
('9ff12342-af8a-5cda-ad87-380352ba5749'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Ileum characteristics:', '["Thick wall, tall plicae, long vasa recta resulting in decreased functional activity","Has haustra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Has taenia coli mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thin wall, fewer plicae, short vasa recta, more fat, Peyer patches"]'::jsonb, 'Thin wall, fewer plicae, short vasa recta, more fat, Peyer patches', 'The ileum has a thin wall, fewer plicae, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat, and Peyer patches. Thick walls, haustra, and taenia coli describe other bowel segments.'),
('3356c48d-74ae-5328-a264-40f4ba76dcd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Ileocecal valve function:', '["None","Controls defecation","Regulates gastric emptying","Prevents reflux from ileum to cecum","Prevents reflux from cecum to ileum"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents reflux from cecum to ileum', 'The ileocecal valve allows one-way flow from the ileum into the cecum and prevents reflux of cecal contents back into the ileum. Defecation control, gastric emptying, and the reverse direction are not among its roles.'),
('4d3fda37-5981-58ef-b56e-62865dcfac5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'McBurney point is:', '["1/3 from ASIS to umbilicus","Midpoint of inguinal ligament","At the ASIS","At pubic tubercle","At the umbilicus"]'::jsonb, '1/3 from ASIS to umbilicus', 'McBurney''s point lies at the junction of the lateral and middle thirds of the line joining the ASIS to the umbilicus and is the classic surface marking for the base of the appendix. The midpoint of the inguinal ligament, the ASIS itself, the pubic tubercle, and the umbilicus are all anatomically unrelated to the appendix base.'),
('3330ef1b-6265-5819-90ac-72a35f001063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Appendix most commonly located:', '["Retrocecal (behind cecum)","Pelvic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Postileal mediated by specific receptor activation","Preileal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subcecal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retrocecal (behind cecum)', 'The appendix is retrocecal in about 65-70% of people because during development it typically folds behind the cecum. Pelvic, postileal, preileal, and subcecal positions occur but are all less frequent variants.'),
('5944b8c9-e4ff-5ca8-a350-391d769be18b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Colon divided into:', '["Right, middle, left, sigmoid","Ascending, horizontal, descending, sigmoid","Proximal, distal, sigmoid","Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid","None"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid', 'The colon is divided into ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid parts according to its course through the abdomen. The other groupings use nonstandard terms such as ''middle'' or ''horizontal'' that do not reflect the standard anatomical subdivisions.'),
('f7e54b41-0362-5394-92ab-d71e1b04bbfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Taenia coli are:', '["Fatty appendages resulting in decreased functional activity","Haustra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circular muscle fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Three longitudinal muscle bands on the colon"]'::jsonb, 'Three longitudinal muscle bands on the colon', 'The taenia coli are three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle (mesocolic, omental, and free) that run along the large intestine except the rectum. Because they are shorter than the bowel wall, they create the sacculations called haustra; fatty appendages are the epiploic appendages.'),
('82c58012-918e-5573-84f3-adb4c047d8a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Haustra are:', '["Fatty appendages","Longitudinal muscle bands","Sacculations between taeniae","None","Circular folds"]'::jsonb, 'Sacculations between taeniae', 'Haustra are the sacculated pouches of the colonic wall formed between the taenia coli, which are shorter than the bowel itself. Fatty appendages (epiploic appendages), the taeniae themselves, and the plicae circulares of the small intestine are different structures.'),
('99939a33-d204-5b49-b1a4-39ab51ef15fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'SMA supplies:', '["Left colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Hindgut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midgut (distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 transverse colon)","Rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foregut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midgut (distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 transverse colon)', 'The superior mesenteric artery supplies all midgut-derived structures from the distal duodenum through the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The left colon and rectum are supplied by the IMA and internal iliac circulation, and the foregut is supplied by the celiac trunk.'),
('e04c7282-af14-5355-af11-808db415e782'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'IMA supplies:', '["Hindgut (distal 1/3 transverse to rectum)","Right colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","Midgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foregut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hindgut (distal 1/3 transverse to rectum)', 'The inferior mesenteric artery supplies hindgut-derived structures from the distal one-third of the transverse colon down to the upper rectum. The right colon, duodenum, and other midgut/foregut structures are supplied by the SMA and celiac trunk.'),
('e2092abf-9eb3-53d9-8ed6-b90b22a79210'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Ileocolic artery is a branch of:', '["Internal iliac","Superior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","Inferior mesenteric","Common iliac"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The ileocolic artery is the terminal branch of the superior mesenteric artery, arising from its lower right side. It supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, appendix, and proximal ascending colon, and it gives off the appendicular artery.'),
('fd2650a0-c85f-5bc7-b968-bd47198476fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Appendicular artery is a branch of:', '["Right colic","Celiac trunk","Ileocolic artery","IMA","SMA directly"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocolic artery', 'The appendicular artery arises from the ileocolic artery, a branch of the SMA, and runs as an end artery in the mesoappendix to supply the entire appendix. It does not branch directly from the SMA, celiac trunk, right colic, or IMA.'),
('8da275d1-da72-58ff-b062-7bab70a1d622'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Marginal artery of Drummond:', '["Artery of appendix resulting in decreased functional activity","Right colic artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Anastomotic arcade connecting SMA and IMA","Left colic artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anastomotic arcade connecting SMA and IMA', 'The marginal artery of Drummond is a continuous anastomotic channel along the mesenteric border of the colon connecting the SMA and IMA territories. It provides important collateral circulation, but it is not the right colic, left colic, or appendicular artery.'),
('aea3ff4c-25a0-5bf6-8271-b87b57287b8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Critical point of Sudeck:', '["None","Weak point at rectosigmoid junction","Junction of SMA and IMA","Ileocecal junction","Splenic flexure"]'::jsonb, 'Weak point at rectosigmoid junction', 'Sudeck''s critical point is at the rectosigmoid junction, where the last sigmoid artery joins the superior rectal artery and the marginal artery may be absent. This watershed zone is vulnerable to ischemia, unlike the ileocecal junction, splenic flexure, or the SMA-IMA junction.'),
('c8ea27c3-1844-50e1-aaeb-e3a93ccc362a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Jejunum begins at:', '["Cecum resulting in decreased functional activity","Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)","Ileocecal junction mediated by specific receptor activation","Pylorus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)', 'The jejunum begins at the duodenojejunal flexure, which is suspended by the ligament of Treitz at the level of L2. The pylorus marks the start of the duodenum, the ileocecal junction marks the end of the ileum, and the cecum and rectum are parts of the large intestine.'),
('af08e9ee-008c-53af-96d2-89f6d118c968'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Ileum ends at:', '["Sigmoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)","Cecum mediated by specific receptor activation","Duodenojejunal flexure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocecal junction (ileocecal valve)', 'The ileum terminates at the ileocecal junction, where it opens into the cecum through the ileocecal valve. This valve prevents reflux of colonic contents into the small intestine, and the duodenojejunal flexure marks the start of the jejunum, not the end of the ileum.'),
('fe665ed3-42db-5348-be02-0a35ac0e050c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Cecum is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Last part of small intestine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Appendix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First part of large intestine, blind pouch below ileocecal valve"]'::jsonb, 'First part of large intestine, blind pouch below ileocecal valve', 'The cecum is the first part of the large intestine, a blind pouch situated below the ileocecal valve, and it gives origin to the vermiform appendix. It is not part of the small intestine, not the rectum, and not the appendix itself.'),
('20f40c0e-5710-5de2-a82e-25016942bb39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Appendix arises from:', '["Ascending colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Posteromedial cecum (2 cm below ileocecal valve)","Terminal ileum mediated by specific receptor activation","Cecal tip without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cecal base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial cecum (2 cm below ileocecal valve)', 'The appendix arises from the posteromedial wall of the cecum about 2 cm below the ileocecal valve; its tip may point in several directions, but the base is constant. It does not arise from the ascending colon, terminal ileum, or other cecal regions.'),
('e08e3eca-5efc-57aa-ad20-e4cb8edaaa77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Appendicular artery runs in:', '["None","Through appendix wall","Inside lumen","Mesentery behind appendix","Mesoappendix (free edge)"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoappendix (free edge)', 'The appendicular artery courses through the mesoappendix, the triangular peritoneal fold attached to the appendix, running along its free border. Because it is an end artery, ligation of the mesoappendix is essential during appendectomy, and the artery does not run inside the lumen or appendix wall.'),
('c894d895-8f04-549c-8e3a-232befe175f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Cecum/appendix lymphatics drain to:', '["Para-aortic","None","Internal iliac","Ileocolic nodes to SMA nodes","Superficial inguinal"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocolic nodes to SMA nodes', 'Lymph from the cecum and appendix drains to the ileocolic lymph nodes along the ileocolic vessels and then to the superior mesenteric nodes. This pathway follows the SMA drainage, not the para-aortic, internal iliac, or inguinal chains.'),
('789a7432-060d-52e9-90ee-6652ca6b1bc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Small intestine nerve supply:', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic (splanchnic, SMA plexus) and parasympathetic (vagus)","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only phrenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (splanchnic, SMA plexus) and parasympathetic (vagus)', 'The small intestine receives dual autonomic innervation: sympathetic fibers travel via the splanchnic nerves and the SMA plexus (inhibiting motility), while parasympathetic fibers are supplied by the vagus nerve (stimulating motility). It is not innervated by any single nerve type or by the phrenic nerve.'),
('29254f97-9d55-5d91-9fd9-9cae55a00f2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Right colon nerve supply:', '["Pelvic splanchnic S2-S4","Only parasympathetic","None","Vagus and sympathetic (SMA plexus)","Only sympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus and sympathetic (SMA plexus)', 'The right colon is a midgut derivative, so it receives parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve and sympathetic fibers from the SMA plexus. Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) supply the hindgut-derived left colon instead.'),
('1117611f-a6e0-583b-93c4-80c6e3a3857f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Left colon nerve supply:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Phrenic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvic splanchnic S2-S4 and sympathetic (IMA plexus)","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic S2-S4 and sympathetic (IMA plexus)', 'The left colon is a hindgut derivative and receives parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) with sympathetic fibers from the IMA plexus. It is not innervated by the vagus or phrenic nerves, which serve the midgut and diaphragm, respectively.'),
('ecece14f-c720-5380-b6c4-8d79f82d1871'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Haustra best developed in:', '["Cecum resulting in decreased functional activity","Ascending accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Descending mediated by specific receptor activation","Transverse and sigmoid colon","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse and sigmoid colon', 'Haustra are best developed in the transverse and sigmoid colon, where the taeniae are most out of proportion to the length of the gut wall. The cecum, ascending and descending colon show less pronounced sacculation, and haustra are absent in the rectum.'),
('b71aed7c-73be-5335-830f-21f4960c8585'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Taeniae coli converge at:', '["Cecum","Ileocecal valve","Base of the appendix","Sigmoid","Rectum"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the appendix', 'All three taenia coli converge at the base of the appendix, providing a vital surgical landmark for locating the appendix during operation. They do not converge at the ileocecal valve, sigmoid colon, or rectum (where they fade out).'),
('02760c80-c73e-5424-bfd1-ef96ef47489c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Plicae circulares most prominent in:', '["Jejunum","Duodenum","Ileum","Stomach","Colon"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum', 'The plicae circulares are highest and most closely packed in the jejunum, where they greatly enlarge the absorptive surface area. They become smaller and fewer in the ileum, are absent in the colon, and are not a feature of the stomach wall.'),
('14c95319-c28e-5671-b1b9-c9a316a44746'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Peyer patches most numerous in:', '["Jejunum","Ileum (terminal ileum)","Colon","Duodenum","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Ileum (terminal ileum)', 'Peyer patches are aggregated lymphoid nodules that are most numerous in the terminal ileum, providing mucosal immune surveillance where the bacterial load rises near the large intestine. They are sparse in the jejunum and duodenum and absent from the stomach and colon.'),
('af0d774e-54bb-5c19-87d9-993962024ef6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Root of mesentery runs:', '["Stomach to colon resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Xiphoid to pubis mediated by specific receptor activation","Esophagus to rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duodenojejunal flexure to ileocecal junction"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenojejunal flexure to ileocecal junction', 'The root of the mesentery is about 15-20 cm long and runs obliquely from the duodenojejunal flexure (left of L2) to the ileocecal junction (right sacroiliac joint). It is the attachment line for the mesentery of the jejunum and ileum, not a line from the xiphoid to the pubis.'),
('45e6cdb5-bee9-5338-b931-c2ab9043d37f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Sigmoid colon begins at:', '["Splenic flexure resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midline mediated by specific receptor activation","Right iliac fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left iliac crest, ends at rectosigmoid junction S3"]'::jsonb, 'Left iliac crest, ends at rectosigmoid junction S3', 'The sigmoid colon begins at the left iliac crest, where the descending colon turns medially to form an S-shaped loop, and ends at the rectosigmoid junction at the level of S3. The splenic flexure is the junction of the transverse and descending colon, not the start of the sigmoid.'),
('dce430f0-8e0c-567f-b559-8b39cc93468f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Rectosigmoid junction is at:', '["S1","S3 vertebra","L5","S5","Coccyx"]'::jsonb, 'S3 vertebra', 'The rectosigmoid junction is located at the level of the S3 vertebra, where the sigmoid colon loses its taeniae, haustra, and epiploic appendages to become the smooth-walled rectum. The S1, L5, S5, and coccyx levels do not mark this transition.'),
('d6ecb769-fad8-5934-b966-44b43c5dea24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Superior rectal artery is from:', '["SMA resulting in decreased functional activity","Middle rectal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior mesenteric artery (terminal branch)","Internal iliac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery (terminal branch)', 'The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery, descending into the pelvis to supply the upper rectum and anal canal above the pectinate line. It does not arise from the SMA, middle rectal, or internal iliac artery.'),
('fdf0c716-143c-5ca6-afbd-23a9c3350ab9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Middle rectal artery is from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","IMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal iliac artery (anterior division)","External iliac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal iliac artery (anterior division)', 'The middle rectal artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and supplies the middle part of the rectum. It anastomoses with the superior and inferior rectal arteries, and it does not arise from the IMA, external iliac, or femoral arteries.'),
('48338571-c045-54ee-b742-77bcac049b7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Inferior rectal artery is from:', '["External iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal pudendal artery","IMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal pudendal artery', 'The inferior rectal artery is a branch of the internal pudendal artery, which itself arises from the internal iliac artery, and it supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line. It does not branch from the IMA, external iliac, or femoral arteries.'),
('f77c6812-0163-5395-870a-420495998c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Sigmoid volvulus most affects:', '["Sigmoid colon","Cecum","Transverse","Descending","Ascending"]'::jsonb, 'Sigmoid colon', 'The sigmoid colon is the segment most prone to volvulus because its long, redundant S-shaped loop is attached to a narrow mesenteric base, allowing it to twist around its axis. The cecum is the second most common site, while transverse, descending, and ascending colon volvulus are rare.'),
('9cca987f-7e1d-541b-8ddf-a3597d2a8dcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Right colic artery is from:', '["Superior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Ileocolic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IMA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The right colic artery typically arises from the superior mesenteric artery (or variably from the ileocolic or middle colic arteries) and supplies the ascending colon. It does not arise from the celiac trunk or the IMA.'),
('82b982b5-8ee4-5390-9cb8-fba7630767d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Left colic artery is from:', '["Inferior mesenteric artery","SMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Celiac trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle colic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery', 'The left colic artery is the first major branch of the inferior mesenteric artery and supplies the descending colon and splenic flexure. It does not arise from the SMA, celiac trunk, or middle colic artery.'),
('cf365535-1666-5dae-b87c-51befdd3441e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Middle colic artery is from:', '["IMA resulting in decreased functional activity","Right colic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Celiac trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior mesenteric artery","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The middle colic artery arises from the superior mesenteric artery and supplies the transverse colon, anastomosing with the right and left colic arteries. It does not arise from the IMA, celiac trunk, or right colic artery.'),
('f7888a43-a4ce-5405-b968-ae5613e248c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Sigmoid arteries are from:', '["Inferior mesenteric artery","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle colic mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal iliac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SMA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric artery', 'The sigmoid arteries (usually 2-4 branches) arise from the inferior mesenteric artery and supply the sigmoid colon, forming arcades before joining the marginal artery. They do not arise from the SMA, middle colic, or internal iliac artery.'),
('4ee09145-e459-550a-aa93-f2b39f0e21ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Plicae circulares function:', '["Prevent reflux resulting in decreased functional activity","Absorb water accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete enzymes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase absorptive surface area"]'::jsonb, 'Increase absorptive surface area', 'The plicae circulares are permanent circular folds of mucosa and submucosa that increase the absorptive surface area of the small intestine, working alongside villi and microvilli. They do not primarily prevent reflux, absorb water, or secrete enzymes.'),
('7890475e-69db-5041-afc4-fa6fb6486686'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Mesentery proper attaches:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jejunum and ileum to posterior abdominal wall","Appendix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sigmoid colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum and ileum to posterior abdominal wall', 'The mesentery proper is a fan-shaped peritoneal fold that suspends the jejunum and ileum from the posterior abdominal wall and carries their vessels, nerves, and lymphatics. The transverse colon, appendix, and sigmoid colon each have their own separate mesenteries.'),
('67874896-4c58-5501-ab6c-6cbcd3b7e083'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix has a complete mesentery called:', '["Transverse mesocolon resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesoappendix (extends from the mesentery of the ileum)","Mesocolon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mesentery proper leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesoappendix (extends from the mesentery of the ileum)', 'The mesoappendix is the complete peritoneal mesentery of the appendix, extending from the mesentery of the terminal ileum and containing the appendicular vessels and lymphatics. The transverse mesocolon, mesocolon, and mesentery proper serve different parts of the bowel.'),
('54cf61a9-4960-5c3f-ba4a-769eb48c96b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The vermiform appendix is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A part of the sigmoid colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A blind-ended tube connected to the cecum","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lymphatic organ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A blind-ended tube connected to the cecum', 'The vermiform appendix is a narrow, blind-ended tubular diverticulum that arises from the cecum and contains abundant lymphoid tissue in its wall. It is not part of the sigmoid colon, and although it is rich in lymphoid tissue, it is not classified primarily as a lymphatic organ.'),
('e2c7bbe6-c43e-5b36-b003-71b17d786760'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix receives its blood supply from:', '["IMA resulting in decreased functional activity","Appendicular artery (branch of ileocolic)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","SMA directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Celiac trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Appendicular artery (branch of ileocolic)', 'The appendix is supplied by the appendicular artery, a branch of the ileocolic artery (from the SMA), which runs in the mesoappendix as an end artery. It does not receive blood directly from the SMA, celiac trunk, or IMA.'),
('a4c3dffd-ba7d-52f3-8f4d-6cee910eece6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The base of the appendix is located:', '["Anterior cecum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral cecum mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior cecum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posteromedial wall of the cecum"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial wall of the cecum', 'The base of the appendix is consistently attached to the posteromedial wall of the cecum about 2 cm below the ileocecal valve, regardless of the direction of its tip. It does not arise from the anterior, lateral, or superior surfaces of the cecum.'),
('b08678ab-1e1e-59e5-947c-79708009a187'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The teniae coli converge at the:', '["Rectum","Sigmoid colon","None","Base of the appendix","Cecum"]'::jsonb, 'Base of the appendix', 'The three taenia coli converge at the base of the appendix, a constant landmark used to locate the appendix during surgical exploration. The taeniae fade out at the rectum and do not converge at the sigmoid colon.'),
('3de612fe-7ada-5265-8a99-0f453ace14af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The splenic flexure of the colon is located:', '["Left upper quadrant (near the spleen)","None","Right upper quadrant","Left lower quadrant","Right lower quadrant"]'::jsonb, 'Left upper quadrant (near the spleen)', 'The splenic (left colic) flexure lies in the left upper quadrant near the spleen, where the transverse colon turns sharply downward to become the descending colon. It is not in the right upper quadrant or in either lower quadrant.'),
('c3289eab-b3a0-56c9-890c-8016500ec2c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The hepatic flexure of the colon is located:', '["Left lower quadrant","Right lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant (near the liver)","Left upper quadrant","None"]'::jsonb, 'Right upper quadrant (near the liver)', 'The hepatic (right colic) flexure lies in the right upper quadrant beneath the liver, where the ascending colon turns medially to become the transverse colon. It is not located in the left upper quadrant or in either lower quadrant.'),
('196c82d7-e2a0-5aaf-a916-5e4b18114f4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The splenic flexure is higher than the hepatic flexure:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","False (hepatic is higher) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They are at the same level mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True (it is at the level of the 8th rib)"]'::jsonb, 'True (it is at the level of the 8th rib)', 'The splenic flexure lies higher than the hepatic flexure, at approximately the level of the 8th rib, because the liver pushes the hepatic flexure inferiorly. Therefore the statement that the hepatic flexure is higher, or that they lie at the same level, is incorrect.'),
('0400c81a-a915-55bf-85bd-30712dcd1f4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ascending colon is:', '["Partially retroperitoneal","Free in the abdomen","Retroperitoneal","None","Intraperitoneal"]'::jsonb, 'Retroperitoneal', 'The ascending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed to the posterior abdominal wall behind the parietal peritoneum after midgut rotation. It is not free in the abdomen and is not intraperitoneal like the transverse colon.'),
('eef3a7fb-2d87-577c-b581-d05348beefd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The transverse colon is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Partially retroperitoneal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Retroperitoneal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intraperitoneal (has a mesentery - transverse mesocolon)"]'::jsonb, 'Intraperitoneal (has a mesentery - transverse mesocolon)', 'The transverse colon is intraperitoneal and is suspended by the transverse mesocolon, which attaches it to the posterior abdominal wall along the anterior border of the pancreas. It is not retroperitoneal like the ascending and descending colon.'),
('378706e6-560e-5543-b572-44679aec4fdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The descending colon is:', '["Free","All","Intraperitoneal","None","Retroperitoneal"]'::jsonb, 'Retroperitoneal', 'The descending colon is secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed to the posterior abdominal wall on the left side behind the parietal peritoneum. It is not free in the abdomen and is not intraperitoneal.'),
('5041628c-336b-5df1-a1af-60f00a9f6f3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The sigmoid colon is:', '["Partially resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Intraperitoneal (has sigmoid mesocolon)","Retroperitoneal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intraperitoneal (has sigmoid mesocolon)', 'The sigmoid colon is intraperitoneal and is suspended by the sigmoid mesocolon, which has an inverted V-shaped attachment to the posterior pelvic wall. It is not retroperitoneal like the ascending and descending colon.'),
('5f924b88-62e1-50d0-ac72-b88f579aedb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum has three lateral curves called:', '["Valves of Houston","Haustra","None","Taeniae","Folds"]'::jsonb, 'Valves of Houston', 'The rectum has three transverse mucosal folds called the valves of Houston (one right and two left) that support the fecal mass and prevent excessive distension. Haustra and taeniae are features of the large intestine proper that are absent in the rectum.'),
('1330cd07-0313-5a2e-9a4f-4f6558ea2e5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum begins at:', '["S3 vertebra (rectosigmoid junction)","S1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L5 mediated by specific receptor activation","S5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coccyx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'S3 vertebra (rectosigmoid junction)', 'The rectum begins at the rectosigmoid junction at the S3 level, where the taenia coli converge to form a complete longitudinal muscle layer. The other vertebral levels do not mark this transition.'),
('d5e6cc99-dea2-5eb7-9013-2e229e986074'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum ends at:', '["Anorectal junction (pelvic floor, 3 cm from anal verge)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Sigmoid colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anorectal junction (pelvic floor, 3 cm from anal verge)', 'The rectum terminates at the anorectal junction where the puborectalis sling and pelvic floor create the 3 cm gap to the anal verge. The anal canal lies distal to this junction and the sigmoid colon proximal, so neither represents the termination.'),
('5eb72af3-a3a8-5f03-8490-9f367f15e8ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal canal is about:', '["10 cm","None","15 cm","3-4 cm long","2 cm"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm long', 'The anal canal measures roughly 3-4 cm, extending from the anorectal junction to the anal verge. Lengths of 10-15 cm are far too long for this structure.'),
('6f94bf65-e0d7-558a-851f-098733e0b133'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The pectinate (dentate) line divides:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The anal canal into upper and lower parts","The colon from the rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rectum from the anal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anal canal into upper and lower parts', 'The pectinate line splits the anal canal into an upper endodermal part and a lower ectodermal part with distinct blood supply, venous drainage, innervation, and lymphatics. It does not separate the colon from the rectum or the rectum from the anal canal.'),
('49f8931b-9a86-5134-be29-6a7ef8f60f8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Arterial supply above the pectinate line:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior rectal artery (IMA branch)","Middle rectal mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior rectal (internal pudendal) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior rectal artery (IMA branch)', 'Above the pectinate line the anal canal is supplied by the superior rectal artery, the terminal branch of the inferior mesenteric artery. The middle and inferior rectal arteries vascularize the lower rectum and anal canal below this level.'),
('bdb5b9f9-910e-5475-b604-142c30cbf8c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Arterial supply below the pectinate line:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior rectal artery (internal pudendal)","Superior rectal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle rectal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior rectal artery (internal pudendal)', 'Below the pectinate line the anal canal is supplied by the inferior rectal artery, a branch of the internal pudendal artery. The superior and middle rectal arteries serve the more proximal rectum and anal canal above the pectinate line.'),
('9060590a-bc88-59ea-b692-306917e886bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Venous drainage above the pectinate line:', '["Both resulting in decreased functional activity","Portal system (superior rectal vein -> IMV -> splenic -> portal)","Systemic (inferior rectal -> internal pudendal -> internal iliac) mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal system (superior rectal vein -> IMV -> splenic -> portal)', 'Venous blood above the pectinate line drains through the superior rectal vein into the inferior mesenteric, splenic, and portal veins, so portal hypertension can engorge internal hemorrhoids. The systemic route described drains only below the pectinate line.'),
('52d72b9f-04ca-51ce-b755-9c5a099ae3c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Venous drainage below the pectinate line:', '["Portal resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Systemic (inferior rectal -> internal pudendal -> internal iliac)","Both leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic (inferior rectal -> internal pudendal -> internal iliac)', 'Below the pectinate line venous blood drains through the inferior rectal vein into the internal pudendal and internal iliac veins, the systemic route that spares external hemorrhoids from portal hypertension. The portal route drains only the region above the pectinate line.'),
('59927f94-9307-5a4e-b43b-27af3b4f93cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Lymphatic drainage above pectinate line:', '["Internal iliac and para-aortic nodes","Axillary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial inguinal mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal iliac and para-aortic nodes', 'Lymphatics above the pectinate line drain upward along the superior rectal vessels to the internal iliac and para-aortic nodes. The superficial inguinal nodes receive lymph from the lower anal canal and perineum instead.'),
('02f8415e-7bd1-56c4-9149-bb389d11c68b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Lymphatic drainage below pectinate line:', '["Internal iliac","Superficial inguinal nodes","Axillary","Para-aortic","None"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial inguinal nodes', 'Lymphatics below the pectinate line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes, matching the cutaneous drainage of the perineum. Internal iliac and para-aortic nodes collect the lymph from above the pectinate line.'),
('0b71e9c1-59ef-52d6-911a-b74a7c9f82a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Branches of SMA include:', '["Left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All colic branches mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal, jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, middle colic","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pancreaticoduodenal, jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, middle colic', 'The SMA supplies the midgut via the inferior pancreaticoduodenal, jejunal, ileal, ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic arteries. The left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries are branches of the IMA.'),
('6a627011-f085-5f28-b916-570f76c045c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Branches of IMA include:', '["Left colic, sigmoid arteries, superior rectal artery","All small intestinal branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Right colic, middle colic, ileocolic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left colic, sigmoid arteries, superior rectal artery', 'The IMA supplies the hindgut through the left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries. Right colic, middle colic, and ileocolic branches arise from the SMA.'),
('ee09da8e-c1db-5d73-919e-9db700c0cc3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The arcades of the jejunum are:', '["All","More arcades and shorter vasa recta","None","Same as ileum","Fewer and longer vasa recta"]'::jsonb, 'Fewer and longer vasa recta', 'Jejunal arteries form fewer (one to two) arcades with longer vasa recta, producing a simple vascular pattern visible through the thin mesentery. Multiple arcades with short vasa recta characterize the ileum.'),
('69818ccb-e2b5-5821-bff6-cf30f67a8972'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The arcades of the ileum are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Same as jejunum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","More arcades with shorter vasa recta","Fewer and longer vasa recta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'More arcades with shorter vasa recta', 'Ileal arteries form multiple (typically four to five) arcades with shorter vasa recta, a network largely hidden by abundant mesenteric fat. The jejunum shows fewer arcades and longer vasa recta.'),
('7d5815f9-112b-5f92-8eae-c3ad4126ad3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The jejunum makes up:', '["Two-fifths of the small intestine","Half accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Three-fifths mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","One-third leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two-fifths of the small intestine', 'The jejunum is the proximal two-fifths (about 40%) of the small intestine. The remaining three-fifths is the contribution of the ileum.'),
('b43c1bc2-51be-5e08-ba29-3bf2d93f29f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileum makes up:', '["Three-fifths of the small intestine","One-third accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two-fifths mediated by specific receptor activation","Half without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Three-fifths of the small intestine', 'The ileum is the distal three-fifths (about 60%) of the small intestine, making it the longer segment. Two-fifths describes the length of the jejunum.'),
('1519a189-76cb-51d9-9bb8-d1df4e347c58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The small intestine is about:', '["None","15 meters","10 meters","6 meters (20 feet) long","3 meters"]'::jsonb, '6 meters (20 feet) long', 'The small intestine measures approximately 6 meters (20 feet), extending from the pylorus to the ileocecal junction. The alternative lengths greatly exceed the true value.'),
('2fb926e5-130e-5e97-8d6d-39ccc484cf81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The large intestine is about:', '["1.5 meters (5 feet) long","6 meters accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 meters mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","10 meters leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1.5 meters (5 feet) long', 'The large intestine is about 1.5 meters (5 feet) long from the cecum to the anus. Lengths of 3 meters or more exceed the actual extent of the large intestine.'),
('d167f3f1-0795-5a47-8dc5-b7b18ee4579a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The terminal ileum is supplied by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IMA mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The terminal branch of SMA (ileocolic artery)"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal branch of SMA (ileocolic artery)', 'The terminal ileum is supplied by the ileocolic artery, which is the terminal branch of the SMA. Neither the celiac trunk nor the IMA provides blood to this segment.'),
('4251addd-10f7-5b7f-ab68-37c1bb4fc038'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is found by following the:', '["Ileocecal valve resulting in decreased functional activity","Plicae circulares accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Haustra mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taeniae coli to the cecum (where they converge)"]'::jsonb, 'Taeniae coli to the cecum (where they converge)', 'All three taenia coli converge at the base of the appendix, so tracing them to the cecum reliably locates the appendix. The ileocecal valve, plicae circulares, and haustra do not lead to the appendix.'),
('729c46d0-5c22-5481-9457-89a33b2c7a64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'An appendectomy incision (McBurney) is placed:', '["At the umbilicus resulting in decreased functional activity","Over McBurney point (1/3 from ASIS to umbilicus)","At the ASIS mediated by specific receptor activation","Midline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Over McBurney point (1/3 from ASIS to umbilicus)', 'The McBurney (grid-iron) incision is placed at McBurney''s point, one-third of the way from the ASIS to the umbilicus, directly over the appendix. Umbilical, ASIS, and midline placements do not overlie the appendix.'),
('aa296e70-8315-5085-9c74-513b31cbb94d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The mesoappendix contains:', '["Appendicular artery and vein","Only nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Appendicular artery and vein', 'The mesoappendix is the triangular peritoneal fold that carries the appendicular artery and vein along with lymphatics, nerves, and fat. It does not contain only nerves or only lymphatics.'),
('4e580ac3-3e56-5bbc-b623-ff5af8c7325e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The lymphatics of the terminal ileum drain to:', '["Hepatic nodes","Left gastric nodes","Splenic nodes","Ileocolic nodes","None"]'::jsonb, 'Ileocolic nodes', 'Lymph from the terminal ileum drains first to the ileocolic nodes along the ileocolic vessels. Hepatic, left gastric, and splenic nodes drain other visceral territories.'),
('0ecb83de-2396-5885-b6af-faf09a1336e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileocecal valve is at the junction of:', '["None","Jejunum and ileum","Ileum and jejunum","Terminal ileum and cecum","Cecum and appendix"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal ileum and cecum', 'The ileocecal valve lies where the terminal ileum enters the cecum, marking the small-to-large intestinal transition. Neither the jejuno-ileal junction nor the cecum-appendix junction hosts this valve.'),
('f2c94618-95c7-57ac-9a19-5a49dc0126ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileocecal valve projects into:', '["The appendix resulting in decreased functional activity","The cecum and prevents reflux","The ileum mediated by specific receptor activation","The colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cecum and prevents reflux', 'The ileocecal valve projects into the cecum and prevents reflux of colonic contents into the terminal ileum. It does not project into the appendix, ileum, or colon.'),
('2c5ba926-8a19-598a-a664-9ec9484e6282'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is in which abdominal quadrant?', '["Right upper","Left upper","Right lower quadrant","Left lower quadrant","None"]'::jsonb, 'Right lower quadrant', 'The appendix and cecum occupy the right lower quadrant, the classic site of acute appendicitis pain. The other quadrants do not contain the appendix.'),
('85a37a48-696e-57f7-83fb-3d2585aa827d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix base corresponds to:', '["Gray Turner point resulting in decreased functional activity","McBurney point (1/3 from ASIS to umbilicus)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lanz point leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'McBurney point (1/3 from ASIS to umbilicus)', 'The base of the appendix projects to McBurney''s point, one-third of the way from the ASIS to the umbilicus. Gray Turner and Lanz points are unrelated surface landmarks.'),
('481095f9-d949-572c-af02-d278f83b8f11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is supplied by:', '["Celiac trunk resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Appendicular artery (branch of ileocolic from SMA)","IMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right colic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Appendicular artery (branch of ileocolic from SMA)', 'The appendix is supplied by the appendicular artery, an end artery branching from the ileocolic artery (SMA) that runs within the mesoappendix. The celiac trunk, IMA, and right colic artery do not supply the appendix.'),
('9305abfb-4980-5dd5-96f4-08160caf8dc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The cecum is entirely covered by:', '["Only anteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only posteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peritoneum (but has no mesentery)"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneum (but has no mesentery)', 'The cecum is completely enveloped by peritoneum yet lacks a mesentery, leaving it mobile. It is not covered only anteriorly or only posteriorly.'),
('b0d8ab3d-09a6-5859-8347-9ec98dbffea1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The cecum is intraperitoneal:', '["It is partially covered","True","None","It is retroperitoneal","False"]'::jsonb, 'True', 'Because the cecum is fully surrounded by peritoneum, it is classified as intraperitoneal despite having no mesentery. The options describing partial covering or a retroperitoneal position do not apply.'),
('e28032db-77ce-5e3b-92ed-370763cfc40e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The sigmoid mesocolon has an inverted V-shaped attachment:', '["None","From the iliac fossa to the sacrum","All","From the cecum to the sigmoid","From the splenic flexure to the rectum"]'::jsonb, 'From the iliac fossa to the sacrum', 'The inverted V-shaped root of the sigmoid mesocolon runs from the left iliac fossa to the sacrum, with its apex near the bifurcation of the left common iliac artery. The other described attachments belong to different peritonea.'),
('b0d620b7-d121-5c38-8018-723adf407788'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The transverse mesocolon attaches to:', '["The colon resulting in decreased functional activity","The pancreas (anterior border of the body)","The stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","The duodenum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pancreas (anterior border of the body)', 'The transverse mesocolon attaches to the anterior border of the body of the pancreas, helping to form the floor of the lesser sac. It does not attach to the stomach, duodenum, or colon itself.'),
('b0b23600-4411-5172-bb96-8e9b7529d74b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The root of the mesentery is about:', '["5 cm","15-20 cm long","30 cm","40 cm","None"]'::jsonb, '15-20 cm long', 'The root of the mesentery is roughly 15-20 cm long, running obliquely across the posterior abdominal wall. The other lengths are not described for this structure.'),
('ea64eed9-5ae8-50b1-b7b9-71f53998f80d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The root of the mesentery crosses:', '["Stomach, liver, spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Duodenum, aorta, IVC, right ureter, right psoas","Pancreas only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum, aorta, IVC, right ureter, right psoas', 'The oblique root of the mesentery crosses the duodenum, aorta, inferior vena cava, right ureter, and right psoas muscle. Stomach, liver, spleen, and pancreas are not crossed by this root.'),
('9891d215-6274-504b-abfd-b143d295d880'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The jejunum and ileum are suspended by:', '["None","The mesentery proper","The sigmoid mesocolon","The transverse mesocolon","The gastrocolic ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The mesentery proper', 'The jejunum and ileum hang from the posterior abdominal wall by the fan-shaped mesentery proper, which carries their vessels, nerves, and lymphatics. The mesocolons and gastrocolic ligament attach other structures.'),
('69b0b214-eeb2-55af-8ed1-83683b2c0789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The jejunal arteries form:', '["One or two arcades with long vasa recta","arcades accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple arcades with short vasa recta mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'One or two arcades with long vasa recta', 'Jejunal arteries form one or two arcades with long vasa recta, a simple pattern visible through the thin mesentery. Multiple arcades with short vasa recta characterize the ileum.'),
('cf8c38dc-63c0-5704-8bcc-5c8c2ffcedcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileal arteries form:', '["arcades resulting in decreased functional activity","One or two arcades with long vasa recta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multiple (4-5) arcades with short vasa recta"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple (4-5) arcades with short vasa recta', 'Ileal arteries form multiple (about four to five) arcades with short vasa recta, creating a complex network obscured by mesenteric fat. The jejunum shows one or two arcades with long vasa recta.'),
('ade04bda-f59a-5e9a-86e8-15e56e12e51f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The vasa recta of the jejunum are:', '["Long, with less branching","All","Absent","Short, with more branching","None"]'::jsonb, 'Long, with less branching', 'Jejunal vasa recta are long and relatively unbranched, running directly from the few arcades to the bowel wall. Short, highly branched vasa recta belong to the ileum.'),
('fd144df3-41a4-5244-b899-4f6f2c68fb57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The vasa recta of the ileum are:', '["Absent","All","Long, with less branching","None","Short, with more branching"]'::jsonb, 'Short, with more branching', 'Ileal vasa recta are short and more branched because they arise from a dense arcade network. Long, less branched vasa recta are a feature of the jejunum.'),
('db4c68ba-18fe-5764-b33f-484dd76e66d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The mesenteric fat in the jejunum is:', '["More abundant (extends to the bowel wall) resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absent mediated by specific receptor activation","Less abundant (fat is near the root)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Less abundant (fat is near the root)', 'In the jejunum mesenteric fat is scanty and concentrated near the root, leaving the vessels visible through the translucent mesentery. Fat extending to the bowel wall is a feature of the ileum.'),
('066222de-6e67-56dd-a6ba-54bb20819993'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The mesenteric fat in the ileum is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Absent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Less abundant mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","More abundant (extends to the bowel wall)"]'::jsonb, 'More abundant (extends to the bowel wall)', 'In the ileum mesenteric fat is abundant and reaches the bowel wall, obscuring the vascular pattern. The scanty, root-based fat pattern belongs to the jejunum.'),
('c304cf5c-f93d-5686-9876-328a7a84fa58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The jejunal wall is:', '["Thinner than duodenum","None","Same as ileum","Thicker (more muscular)","Thinner"]'::jsonb, 'Thicker (more muscular)', 'The jejunal wall is thicker and more muscular, matching its brisk peristaltic and absorptive activity. The ileal wall is the thinner, more translucent structure.'),
('23cf96a8-0a85-5f46-b2a2-1f95fe003cf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ileal wall is:', '["Thicker resulting in decreased functional activity","Thinner and more translucent","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Same as jejunum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thinner and more translucent', 'The ileal wall is thinner and more translucent than the jejunum because its musculature and vascular pattern are less prominent. The thicker, more muscular description belongs to the jejunum.'),
('a61a5f82-2292-524e-8a78-4c439a74ce0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Peyer patches are found in:', '["The colon resulting in decreased functional activity","The jejunum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","The ileum (submucosal lymphoid aggregates)","The duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ileum (submucosal lymphoid aggregates)', 'Peyer patches are submucosal aggregates of lymphoid follicles found in the ileum. The stomach, colon, and duodenum do not contain these organized aggregates.'),
('3eebb84f-7020-51be-9c68-f006b84a3391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Peyer patches are most numerous in:', '["The terminal ileum","The duodenum","The proximal jejunum","None","The colon"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal ileum', 'Peyer patches concentrate in the terminal ileum at the transition to the colon, where immune surveillance against colonic bacteria is greatest. The duodenum, proximal jejunum, and colon are not their site of maximum density.'),
('2f63cf88-0df7-5bde-9322-fc0f590beeb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The plicae circulares are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphoid aggregates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Longitudinal folds without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Circular folds of the mucosa and submucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Circular folds of the mucosa and submucosa', 'Plicae circulares (valves of Kerckring) are permanent circular folds composed of mucosa and submucosa that enlarge the absorptive surface. They are neither longitudinal folds nor lymphoid aggregates.'),
('c958b3e2-5b94-595e-9508-21e2f95e8750'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The plicae circulares are most prominent in:', '["The ileum","The duodenum","The colon","The jejunum","None"]'::jsonb, 'The jejunum', 'Plicae circulares are most developed in the jejunum and become sparse in the distal ileum, while the colon has none. The duodenum also carries them but less prominently.'),
('b22726c3-be74-5e04-bb50-54b32c183507'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The plicae circulares function to:', '["Prevent reflux resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase the surface area for absorption","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete enzymes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absorb water leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increase the surface area for absorption', 'Plicae circulares enlarge the mucosal surface area of the small intestine, enhancing nutrient absorption. Preventing reflux, secreting enzymes, and absorbing water are not their primary function.'),
('aa2f9ab0-8370-510f-981a-abb26f063e89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The villi of the small intestine are:', '["Lymphoid nodules resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Finger-like projections of the mucosa","Folds of the mucosa and submucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Finger-like projections of the mucosa', 'Villi are finger-like mucosal projections containing a capillary network and central lacteal that absorb nutrients. Lymphoid nodules and mucosal folds are separate structures.'),
('2580f822-e134-5a9f-8c39-37cde180a122'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The microvilli (brush border) are:', '["Lymphoid aggregates resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cytoplasmic extensions of the epithelial cells","Folds of the mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cytoplasmic extensions of the epithelial cells', 'Microvilli are cytoplasmic extensions of the absorptive enterocytes that form the brush border and greatly increase surface area. Lymphoid aggregates and folds of the mucosa are distinct structures.'),
('15905f31-2a1c-5634-802e-e12257dc8528'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The small intestine is the primary site for:', '["None","Secretion of insulin","Secretion of bile","Absorption of nutrients","Absorption of water"]'::jsonb, 'Absorption of nutrients', 'The small intestine''s extensive surface area from plicae circulares, villi, and microvilli maximises uptake of carbohydrates, proteins, and fats. The large intestine, not the small bowel, is the main site of water absorption; bile is secreted by the liver and insulin by the pancreas.'),
('9a8d17d0-fc11-5227-b146-1ece22c99204'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The large intestine is the primary site for:', '["Absorption of nutrients resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretion of enzymes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption of water and electrolytes","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absorption of water and electrolytes', 'Most water and electrolytes have already been absorbed in the small intestine, but the colon reclaims the remaining water and sodium while forming and storing feces. Nutrient absorption is the domain of the small intestine, and the colon does not secrete digestive enzymes.'),
('5ca1b952-91fd-5943-9aab-daced77ab91e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The cecum receives the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the terminal ileum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Terminal ileum, appendix, ascending colon","Only the ascending colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal ileum, appendix, ascending colon', 'The cecum is a blind pouch below the ileocecal valve into which the terminal ileum opens, that gives attachment to the appendix on its posteromedial wall, and that continues superiorly as the ascending colon. It thus connects all three structures rather than only one of them.'),
('28e6ad07-f839-58d4-91d3-2eb65ee85229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectosigmoid junction is at:', '["Coccyx","S5","S3 vertebra","S1","L5"]'::jsonb, 'S3 vertebra', 'At the level of the S3 vertebra the taenia coli of the sigmoid colon coalesce into a complete longitudinal muscle layer, marking the rectosigmoid junction. Options at S1, S5, L5, and the coccyx do not represent this transition point.'),
('0acd23e9-4d3f-5c05-b497-f1b9d58beb8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The superior rectal artery is the continuation of:', '["The internal iliac artery","The inferior mesenteric artery","The superior mesenteric artery","The median sacral artery","None"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior mesenteric artery', 'The inferior mesenteric artery continues beyond the left common iliac vessels as the superior rectal artery to supply the upper rectum and left colon. The internal iliac and median sacral arteries are pelvic branches, while the superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut.'),
('c9b7dfe2-6e34-5e68-b545-5355110ba35a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The middle rectal arteries come from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior mesenteric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External iliac mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal iliac artery (anterior division)","Femoral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal iliac artery (anterior division)', 'The middle rectal arteries arise from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and supply the muscular wall of the middle rectum. The inferior mesenteric artery gives the superior rectal artery, and the external iliac and femoral arteries supply the limb and lower abdominal wall, not the rectum.'),
('629d4e67-267d-55ac-9969-a1f1f52941e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The inferior rectal arteries come from:', '["External iliac","Internal iliac directly","Internal pudendal artery","None","Femoral"]'::jsonb, 'Internal pudendal artery', 'The inferior rectal arteries are branches of the internal pudendal artery that cross the ischioanal fossa to supply the anal canal below the pectinate line. They are not direct branches of the internal iliac, external iliac, or femoral arteries.'),
('7470745a-18d3-546b-b343-94f7ca28f86e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum begins where:', '["The puborectalis sling resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The taenia coli spread out to form a continuous longitudinal muscle","The colon ends at the sigmoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The taenia coli spread out to form a continuous longitudinal muscle', 'The transition from sigmoid colon to rectum occurs at the point where the three taenia coli fuse into a uniform outer longitudinal muscle layer at the S3 level. The puborectalis sling forms the anorectal junction lower down, not the start of the rectum.'),
('5db03cad-ee02-53be-9120-ebdc63928d23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum has no:', '["Haustra, taenia coli, or epiploic appendages","Circular muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mucosa mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Longitudinal muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Haustra, taenia coli, or epiploic appendages', 'The rectum loses the colonic features of haustra, taenia coli, and epiploic appendages when the taeniae fuse into a continuous longitudinal muscle layer. It still possesses circular and longitudinal muscle, a mucosa, and serosa, so these options are incorrect.'),
('10afa718-4ce5-57c0-ba38-d1ffcbee47d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum has three lateral curves:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","curves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only one curve mediated by specific receptor activation","Two curves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior, middle, and inferior (with the middle being most common)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior, middle, and inferior (with the middle being most common)', 'The rectum has three lateral or transverse folds formed by the valves of Houston: a right superior fold, a left middle fold that is the most prominent and reliably visualised at sigmoidoscopy, and a right inferior fold. Thus there are three curves, not one or two.'),
('c454d53f-995e-578e-81f9-eee1473ad41c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum is supplied by:', '["None","Superior, middle, and inferior rectal arteries","Only the inferior rectal","Only the middle rectal","Only the superior rectal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superior, middle, and inferior rectal arteries', 'The rectum has a triple arterial supply: the superior rectal artery from the inferior mesenteric artery, the middle rectal arteries from the internal iliac artery, and the inferior rectal arteries from the internal pudendal artery, all of which anastomose freely. No single artery supplies the whole rectum alone.'),
('92d5089e-0fa4-5d04-aa4f-bee0f259a9fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum is drained by:', '["Only the portal system resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The azygos system mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the systemic system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior rectal vein (portal), middle and inferior rectal veins (systemic)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior rectal vein (portal), middle and inferior rectal veins (systemic)', 'The superior rectal vein drains upward into the portal system, while the middle and inferior rectal veins drain into the systemic (internal iliac) system. This dual drainage creates a portal-systemic anastomosis at the anorectal junction, an important site of varices in portal hypertension.'),
('97c50c27-ded8-5f39-be31-cb317a30c966'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anorectal junction is at:', '["The coccyx resulting in decreased functional activity","S5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The level of the puborectalis sling (pelvic floor)","S3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The level of the puborectalis sling (pelvic floor)', 'The anorectal junction corresponds to the puborectalis sling of the levator ani, which forms the anorectal angle essential for fecal continence. The coccyx, S3, and S5 relate to the bony pelvis and rectosigmoid junction rather than to this muscular landmark.'),
('9bded816-1a11-59ec-b2e7-665dcc3d7c50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal canal is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","5-6 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","7 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3-4 cm long (from anorectal junction to anal verge)"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm long (from anorectal junction to anal verge)', 'The anal canal measures about 3-4 cm, running from the anorectal junction (puborectalis/pectinate level) to the anal verge. Lengths of 5-7 cm overlap the distal rectum, while 1 cm is too short for this canal.'),
('94b57dd3-0ce3-567f-80d6-36044f0f9a9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal columns (of Morgagni) are:', '["Circular folds resulting in decreased functional activity","Longitudinal folds in the upper anal canal","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse folds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal folds in the upper anal canal', 'The anal columns of Morgagni are 6-10 longitudinal mucosal folds in the upper anal canal, each containing a terminal branch of the superior rectal artery and vein. They are vertical, not circular or transverse.'),
('62c01587-7a6e-5086-bbc9-76448569d441'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal valves are:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Longitudinal folds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small folds joining the lower ends of the anal columns","Circular folds without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small folds joining the lower ends of the anal columns', 'The anal valves are small crescentic folds that connect the lower ends of adjacent anal columns along the pectinate line, and the pockets above them form the anal sinuses. They are neither longitudinal nor circular folds.'),
('748d5935-51a5-5e23-acc0-b9ebfe47bc71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The pectinate line (dentate line) marks:', '["The junction of the rectum and the anal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The junction of the small and large intestine mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The junction of the endoderm (upper) and ectoderm (lower) anal canal"]'::jsonb, 'The junction of the endoderm (upper) and ectoderm (lower) anal canal', 'The pectinate line is the embryologic meeting point of the endodermal hindgut above and the ectodermal proctodeum below, and it divides the anal canal''s epithelium, arterial supply, venous and lymphatic drainage, and nerve supply. It does not separate the rectum from the anal canal or intestines.'),
('e68c1706-fc73-5957-aa47-26ba58be70e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Above the pectinate line, the anal canal is lined by:', '["Stratified squamous epithelium (ectoderm)","None","Transitional epithelium","All","Columnar epithelium (endoderm)"]'::jsonb, 'Columnar epithelium (endoderm)', 'The mucosa above the pectinate line is columnar epithelium of endodermal origin that is continuous with the rectal mucosa, with autonomic innervation and lymphatic drainage upward to the internal iliac nodes. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the canal only below the line.'),
('93419985-c1c9-5781-a383-50c12f47f6df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Below the pectinate line, the anal canal is lined by:', '["Transitional epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Stratified squamous epithelium (ectoderm)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Columnar epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous epithelium (ectoderm)', 'Below the pectinate line the anal canal is lined by stratified squamous epithelium of ectodermal origin continuous with the perianal skin, with somatic innervation and lymph drainage to the superficial inguinal nodes. Columnar and transitional epithelia do not line this region.'),
('37e46351-d5cf-5f6b-8d98-c3bfd71d3d5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The internal anal sphincter is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Voluntary striated muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thickening of the circular smooth muscle (involuntary)","A thickening of the longitudinal smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickening of the circular smooth muscle (involuntary)', 'The internal anal sphincter is a thickening of the circular smooth muscle layer of the anal canal under autonomic control, contributing about 70% of resting anal tone. It is neither striated nor a thickening of the longitudinal layer.'),
('4733cac4-87ec-586b-bcef-f820c7d064c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The external anal sphincter is:', '["Involuntary resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smooth muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Voluntary striated muscle (somatic innervation by pudendal nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'Voluntary striated muscle (somatic innervation by pudendal nerve)', 'The external anal sphincter is striated (voluntary) muscle innervated by the pudendal nerve (S2-S4), allowing conscious control of defecation. Unlike the internal sphincter, it is not smooth or involuntary.'),
('8fcad078-c0e4-5329-9adb-5053eeccdaed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The puborectalis muscle forms:', '["The internal anal sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","The anorectal sling (maintains the anorectal angle)","The external anal sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","The levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anorectal sling (maintains the anorectal angle)', 'The puborectalis, part of the levator ani, forms a sling around the anorectal junction that maintains the 90-degree anorectal angle essential for continence; it relaxes during defecation to straighten the angle. It is not the internal or external sphincter.'),
('08fd9e2c-d15b-5eb8-b63b-331a29b76872'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The most common site for colorectal carcinoma is:', '["The transverse colon","The ascending colon","The rectum and sigmoid colon","The cecum","The descending colon"]'::jsonb, 'The rectum and sigmoid colon', 'Colorectal carcinoma most often arises in the rectum and sigmoid colon, where fecal transit is slowest and contact with carcinogens is prolonged. The cecum, ascending, transverse, and descending colon are all less frequent primary sites.'),
('a8849e54-03cb-5c0a-b003-8f8679d1efd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Diverticulosis most commonly occurs in:', '["The appendix","The ascending colon","The cecum","The rectum","The sigmoid colon"]'::jsonb, 'The sigmoid colon', 'Diverticulosis predominantly affects the sigmoid colon, where high intraluminal pressures and the entry points of the vasa recta through the muscular wall create sites of weakness. The appendix, cecum, ascending colon, and rectum are far less commonly involved.'),
('1a1eb35a-48e8-543e-83ff-e5efa5604322'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Diverticula are:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Outpouchings of the mucosa and submucosa through the muscular wall","Congenital mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True diverticula (all layers) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Outpouchings of the mucosa and submucosa through the muscular wall', 'Colonic diverticula are acquired false diverticula in which only mucosa and submucosa herniate through the muscularis at weak points where vasa recta penetrate the wall. They are not congenital and do not contain all bowel wall layers as true diverticula do.'),
('efb1255e-a9b0-5d05-97cc-0aece86247db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is a true diverticulum:', '["It is a Meckel diverticulum resulting in decreased functional activity","It is a false diverticulum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Yes mediated by specific receptor activation","No (it is a part of the bowel, not a diverticulum)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'No (it is a part of the bowel, not a diverticulum)', 'The appendix is a normal, complete tubular structure of the cecum containing all intestinal wall layers, so it is not a diverticulum at all. It differs from Meckel diverticulum and from acquired false diverticula, both of which are outpouchings.'),
('5508dfcb-c627-5f8f-be5c-b2bc7c6ec76e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Meckel diverticulum is:', '["A true diverticulum of the ileum (remnant of the vitelline duct)","A congenital colon diverticulum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A false diverticulum of the jejunum mediated by specific receptor activation","A stomach diverticulum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A true diverticulum of the ileum (remnant of the vitelline duct)', 'Meckel diverticulum is a congenital true diverticulum (all layers present) of the distal ileum, resulting from incomplete obliteration of the omphalomesenteric (vitelline) duct. It is not a colonic, jejunal, or gastric lesion.'),
('aace011a-329d-5d8a-9ac5-23d37565466d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Meckel diverticulum is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","About 60 cm from the ileocecal valve (on the anti-mesenteric border)","On the mesenteric border mediated by specific receptor activation","In the jejunum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the cecum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 60 cm from the ileocecal valve (on the anti-mesenteric border)', 'Following the rule of 2s, Meckel diverticulum lies on the anti-mesenteric border of the ileum roughly 60 cm (about 2 feet) proximal to the ileocecal valve. It is not located on the mesenteric border, jejunum, or cecum.'),
('dbd8d3fb-6e25-59a3-8b33-b06b34c0b224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Meckel diverticulum contains:', '["Ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue (commonly)","Only intestinal tissue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pancreatic tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only gastric tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue (commonly)', 'Meckel diverticulum frequently contains ectopic gastric or pancreatic mucosa, and acid secretion from ectopic gastric tissue can cause ulceration and bleeding that may mimic appendicitis. It is not restricted to intestinal, gastric, or pancreatic tissue alone.'),
('03146c23-cd2a-59de-83b1-66e1f8c68320'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Peritonitis can result from:', '["Perforation of a hollow viscus (e.g., appendix, stomach, colon)","Only from trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only from infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perforation of a hollow viscus (e.g., appendix, stomach, colon)', 'Perforation of a hollow viscus such as the appendix, stomach, or colon releases gastrointestinal contents into the peritoneal cavity, causing chemical and then bacterial peritonitis. It is not caused solely by trauma or by infection alone.'),
('c514e56d-fea9-501b-9cd1-ef5fce4ef815'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The peritoneal cavity is divided into:', '["All","Greater sac and lesser sac (omental bursa)","Abdominal and pelvic cavities","None","Intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal spaces"]'::jsonb, 'Greater sac and lesser sac (omental bursa)', 'The peritoneal cavity comprises the greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa), which lie behind the stomach and communicate through the epiploic foramen of Winslow. Intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal describe organ relations, and the abdominal and pelvic cavities are not subdivisions of the peritoneal cavity.'),
('6843d903-8779-5d2b-9d4e-d3b940bbe062'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The peritoneum secretes:', '["Mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","A small amount of serous fluid to lubricate the peritoneal surfaces","Bile mediated by specific receptor activation","Sweat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small amount of serous fluid to lubricate the peritoneal surfaces', 'The serous mesothelial lining of the peritoneum secretes a small volume of lubricating serous fluid that allows viscera to slide smoothly during peristalsis. It does not secrete mucus, bile, or sweat.'),
('506a3461-67dd-5428-8d70-6329de2ddfcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The peritoneum absorbs:', '["Fluid and small solutes (mainly through the diaphragmatic lymphatics)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nothing mediated by specific receptor activation","Only water without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only large molecules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid and small solutes (mainly through the diaphragmatic lymphatics)', 'Peritoneal fluid and small solutes are absorbed chiefly through lymphatic stomata on the diaphragmatic peritoneum, which is why infections and ascites preferentially spread upward along this route. The peritoneum absorbs more than just water and does take up small solutes.'),
('353c0840-5b51-5d27-a189-2f3bbb797498'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The greater omentum is attached to:', '["The lesser curvature of the stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","The spleen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The liver mediated by specific receptor activation","The ileum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The greater curvature of the stomach and the transverse colon"]'::jsonb, 'The greater curvature of the stomach and the transverse colon', 'The greater omentum hangs as an apron from the greater curvature of the stomach and drapes over the transverse colon and small intestine, containing fat and lymphoid tissue that aid in sealing infections. It does not attach to the lesser curvature, spleen, liver, or ileum.'),
('07aa83cd-2001-5936-b3a3-f5c3292c7b04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The lesser omentum is attached to:', '["The spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","The lesser curvature of the stomach and the liver (porta hepatis)","The colon mediated by specific receptor activation","The greater curvature of the stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser curvature of the stomach and the liver (porta hepatis)', 'The lesser omentum runs between the lesser curvature of the stomach and first part of the duodenum to the porta hepatis of the liver, and its free edge (hepatoduodenal ligament) encloses the portal triad. It does not attach to the spleen, colon, greater curvature, or diaphragm.'),
('a7e4c3a2-ff61-5eb5-a618-488cfaba2672'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The sigmoid mesocolon is attached to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior abdominal wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The liver mediated by specific receptor activation","The diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior pelvic wall in an inverted V shape"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior pelvic wall in an inverted V shape', 'The sigmoid mesocolon is attached to the posterior pelvic wall in an inverted V, with its apex over the bifurcation of the left common iliac artery. It does not attach to the anterior abdominal wall, liver, or diaphragm.'),
('d03665da-d511-56e5-a3a9-42f1aba883de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Paracolic gutters are:', '["Spaces in the lesser sac resulting in decreased functional activity","Spaces along the lateral sides of the ascending and descending colon","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Spaces between loops of small intestine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spaces in the pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spaces along the lateral sides of the ascending and descending colon', 'The paracolic gutters are recesses of the greater sac lying lateral to the ascending and descending colon that channel peritoneal fluid, pus, or malignant cells between abdominal regions. They are not in the lesser sac, between small bowel loops, or restricted to the pelvis.'),
('d1772095-6cdd-5dca-b4a9-a99bf72e8ae6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The right paracolic gutter communicates with:', '["The lesser sac resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pelvis directly mediated by specific receptor activation","The left paracolic gutter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) and the subphrenic space"]'::jsonb, 'The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) and the subphrenic space', 'The right paracolic gutter is patent superiorly and communicates with the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) and the right subphrenic space, so fluid from the pelvis can reach the subhepatic region. It does not open into the lesser sac, left gutter, or pelvis directly.'),
('9dfb93a5-dbfa-5011-9289-22b42a376e98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left paracolic gutter is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep and freely communicates with the subphrenic space mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the right gutter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shallower and separated from the subphrenic space by the phrenicocolic ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Shallower and separated from the subphrenic space by the phrenicocolic ligament', 'The left paracolic gutter is shallower than the right and is blocked superiorly by the phrenicocolic ligament, which prevents free spread of fluid into the left subphrenic space. It is therefore not deep, freely communicating, or identical to the right gutter.'),
('7e3494b2-86e1-5a14-9830-ac3c56c671ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) is:', '["In the pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","The space between the liver and the right kidney (the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in supine position)","Between the liver and the gallbladder mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between the stomach and the pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the liver and the right kidney (the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in supine position)', 'The hepatorenal recess lies between the inferior surface of the liver and the right kidney and is the commonest site for supine fluid collection because it is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity. It is not pelvic, hepatobiliary, or retrogastric.'),
('9a9b02b4-feca-5a5a-a54f-3e404f8f76bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectovesical pouch is:', '["Between the rectum and the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Between the bladder and the abdominal wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the urinary bladder in males"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the urinary bladder in males', 'In males the peritoneum dips between the rectum and the urinary bladder to form the rectovesical pouch, the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity in the male. It is not between the rectum and uterus or bladder and abdominal wall.'),
('15f8e95c-e41b-5a8c-84a8-8dad92aa4f35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) is:', '["The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the uterus in females","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the rectum and the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Between the uterus and the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the uterus in females', 'In females the rectouterine pouch of Douglas is the peritoneal reflection between the anterior rectum and the posterior uterus, and it is the most dependent part of the female peritoneal cavity. It is not between the rectum and bladder or uterus and bladder.'),
('fd0745b7-b451-5176-a6b8-4c993963495e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The vesicouterine pouch is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Between the bladder and the vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the rectum and the uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","The peritoneal reflection between the urinary bladder and the uterus","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal reflection between the urinary bladder and the uterus', 'The vesicouterine pouch is the peritoneal reflection between the superior surface of the bladder and the anterior surface of the uterus in females. It lies anterior to the uterus, not between the rectum and uterus or bladder and vagina.'),
('9a930469-86c1-5096-ba16-7daf2a4bf27b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'In females, the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity is:', '["The subphrenic space","The hepatorenal recess","The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas)","None","The vesicouterine pouch"]'::jsonb, 'The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas)', 'Because the rectouterine pouch of Douglas lies posterior and low in the female pelvis, it is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity and the commonest site for pelvic fluid, pus, or blood. The subphrenic space, hepatorenal recess, and vesicouterine pouch are higher or less dependent.'),
('7757d5d6-ddc1-5c6b-ac5d-722099acd294'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'In males, the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity is:', '["The hepatorenal recess","The subphrenic space","The paracolic gutter","The rectovesical pouch","None"]'::jsonb, 'The rectovesical pouch', 'In males the rectovesical pouch, lying between the rectum and bladder, is the lowest reflection of peritoneum and therefore the most dependent part of the cavity where fluid collects. The hepatic, subphrenic, and paracolic regions are all higher.'),
('810e3685-19ed-589d-84c5-12f6232438dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Malrotation of the intestine occurs when:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The hindgut does not develop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The midgut does not rotate 270 degrees counterclockwise during development","The foregut does not develop leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The midgut does not rotate 270 degrees counterclockwise during development', 'Intestinal malrotation results from failure of the midgut to complete its normal 270-degree counterclockwise rotation around the superior mesenteric artery, predisposing to volvulus and obstruction. It is not due to failure of foregut or hindgut development.'),
('af98f06c-e5e1-52ec-bc72-93642d183852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Ladd bands are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Abnormal peritoneal bands in malrotation that compress the duodenum","Normal peritoneal folds mediated by specific receptor activation","Ligaments in the liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vascular structures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal peritoneal bands in malrotation that compress the duodenum', 'Ladd bands are abnormal peritoneal adhesions that form across the duodenum in intestinal malrotation, causing duodenal obstruction and often associated with a narrow mesentery prone to midgut volvulus. They are not normal folds, hepatic ligaments, or vessels.'),
('c86f3f45-893a-51a4-a27e-c6d8ab808cdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Intestinal atresia results from:', '["Incomplete recanalization of the intestine during development","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lack of blood supply mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete recanalization of the intestine during development', 'Intestinal atresia arises from failure of the bowel lumen to recanalize after the solid epithelial stage of the 5th-10th week of development, producing a congenital obstruction. It is a developmental event, not the result of ischemia, trauma, or infection.'),
('76ddbf00-5ff3-5bec-9c8e-96c7ec83f0be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Meconium ileus is associated with:', '["Malrotation resulting in decreased functional activity","Intestinal atresia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Cystic fibrosis (thick meconium obstructs the terminal ileum)","Hirschsprung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis (thick meconium obstructs the terminal ileum)', 'Meconium ileus is the neonatal intestinal obstruction of cystic fibrosis, in which defective CFTR function produces thick, inspissated meconium that plugs the terminal ileum. It is not a feature of malrotation, atresia, or Hirschsprung disease.'),
('d45d9b90-b6fc-5164-9ab4-393faad4cfbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Hirschsprung disease (congenital megacolon) results from:', '["Excess ganglionic cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Absence of ganglionic cells in the myenteric plexus (aganglionosis)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Lack of peristalsis due to muscular defect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of ganglionic cells in the myenteric plexus (aganglionosis)', 'Hirschsprung disease is caused by aganglionosis of the myenteric (Auerbach) and submucosal (Meissner) plexuses due to failure of neural crest cell migration, producing a spastic contracted segment with functional obstruction and proximal dilatation. It is not due to excess ganglia or a primary muscular defect.'),
('a41e7a73-96ca-5e4f-a4bb-80fcbf480ef7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Hirschsprung disease most commonly affects:', '["The entire colon","The stomach","The small intestine","The rectosigmoid region","None"]'::jsonb, 'The rectosigmoid region', 'Congenital aganglionosis results from craniocaudal failure of neural crest cell migration, so the most distal segment (rectosigmoid) is the last populated and most often affected. The stomach and small intestine are proximal and normally have innervation.'),
('ce83a75a-64bb-53c8-bc4c-9489e6641cce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The superior mesenteric vein (SMV) drains:', '["The entire colon resulting in decreased functional activity","The hindgut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The midgut and joins the splenic vein to form the portal vein","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The midgut and joins the splenic vein to form the portal vein', 'The SMV drains the midgut (distal duodenum to proximal transverse colon) and unites with the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. Hindgut and rectum are drained by the IMV, while the entire colon includes IMA territory.'),
('7c53dd9e-90cf-5ea2-bc24-6203ec215bcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The IMA supplies:', '["The foregut resulting in decreased functional activity","The small intestine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The midgut mediated by specific receptor activation","The hindgut (from the distal 1/3 of the transverse colon to the upper rectum)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hindgut (from the distal 1/3 of the transverse colon to the upper rectum)', 'The inferior mesenteric artery is the artery of the hindgut, supplying the distal one-third of the transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, and upper rectum. Foregut and midgut are supplied by the celiac trunk and SMA respectively.'),
('29037afe-944a-5050-bd8c-f5c13b83b376'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The marginal artery of Drummond runs along:', '["The mesenteric border of the colon","All","None","The mesenteric border of the small intestine","The antimesenteric border of the colon"]'::jsonb, 'The mesenteric border of the colon', 'The marginal artery runs along the mesenteric border of the colon as a continuous arcade connecting SMA and IMA branches, providing collateral flow. It does not lie along the small intestine or the antimesenteric border.'),
('0224a8b9-9938-576d-bca4-8b207f90ea4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The point of Sudeck is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A point in the IMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A point in the SMA mediated by specific receptor activation","A point in the celiac trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A watershed area at the rectosigmoid junction (lowest part of the marginal artery)"]'::jsonb, 'A watershed area at the rectosigmoid junction (lowest part of the marginal artery)', 'Sudeck''s point is the weakest junction between the last sigmoid artery and the superior rectal artery at the rectosigmoid junction, a watershed prone to ischemia. It is not a named point within the celiac or mesenteric trunks.'),
('08612be6-b14e-594f-89b3-5f352868f1f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Griffith point (watershed area) is:', '["The rectosigmoid junction resulting in decreased functional activity","The cecum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The hepatic flexure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The splenic flexure (between SMA and IMA supply)"]'::jsonb, 'The splenic flexure (between SMA and IMA supply)', 'Griffith''s point is the watershed at the splenic flexure where the territories of the SMA and IMA meet, an area vulnerable to ischemic colitis in hypoperfusion. The hepatic flexure and rectosigmoid junction do not mark this SMA-IMA watershed.'),
('4cf942a1-39ee-551d-bb54-0c2248d1bc04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Ischemic colitis often affects:', '["The cecum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ascending colon mediated by specific receptor activation","The splenic flexure (Griffith point) and rectosigmoid (Sudeck point)","The entire colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The splenic flexure (Griffith point) and rectosigmoid (Sudeck point)', 'Ischemic colitis classically involves the watershed zones: the splenic flexure (Griffith''s point) and rectosigmoid junction (Sudeck''s point), where collateral circulation is poorest. The cecum, ascending colon, and entire colon are not the typical watershed locations.'),
('658a84b4-9880-553a-a237-5fc942e036ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The quadrate lobe of the liver lies between:', '["The caudate and left lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","The IVC and the ligamentum venosum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gallbladder and the falciform ligament (on the visceral surface)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gallbladder and the falciform ligament (on the visceral surface)', 'On the visceral surface the quadrate lobe is bounded anteriorly by the falciform ligament (fissure for ligamentum teres) and posteriorly by the gallbladder fossa. The IVC and ligamentum venosum border the caudate lobe, not the quadrate lobe.'),
('6ae0535e-3318-5946-9463-de72cbc6b855'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The caudate lobe of the liver lies between:', '["The IVC (posteriorly) and the ligamentum venosum (anteriorly)","The gallbladder and the falciform ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The quadrate lobe and the left lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC (posteriorly) and the ligamentum venosum (anteriorly)', 'The caudate lobe is wedged on the posterior surface between the IVC behind and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum in front. The gallbladder and falciform ligament define the quadrate lobe, not the caudate lobe.'),
('ba3d29e4-08af-5196-8328-27eda7a44a54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The porta hepatis is the hilum of the liver and contains:', '["Only the portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The portal vein, hepatic artery proper, and the right and left hepatic ducts","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the bile ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the hepatic artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The portal vein, hepatic artery proper, and the right and left hepatic ducts', 'The porta hepatis transmits the portal triad: portal vein, hepatic artery proper, and right and left hepatic ducts, together with lymphatics and nerves. It does not carry only a single vessel or duct.'),
('128e2e25-f684-542f-b11f-12c2035fd123'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The CBD and cystic duct are found in:', '["The coronary ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The gastrohepatic ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament)","The falciform ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament)', 'The common bile duct and cystic duct travel in the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) along with the portal vein and hepatic artery. The gastrohepatic, falciform, and coronary ligaments do not enclose the bile duct.'),
('9eb9f2f6-41fa-593e-925b-39bc0161798b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The gallbladder bed is on the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Visceral surface of the liver between the right and quadrate lobes","The posterior surface mediated by specific receptor activation","The left lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The diaphragmatic surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral surface of the liver between the right and quadrate lobes', 'The gallbladder fossa lies on the visceral surface of the liver, between the right lobe and the quadrate lobe. The diaphragmatic surface, posterior surface, and left lobe do not form this bed.'),
('4ea067a7-c53e-5b87-a403-1efe15d0830c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The gallbladder is in contact with:', '["Only the stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The duodenum (first part), transverse colon, and the anterior abdominal wall","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The duodenum (first part), transverse colon, and the anterior abdominal wall', 'The gallbladder contacts the first part of the duodenum, the transverse colon, and the anterior abdominal wall; these relations explain gallstone erosion into the duodenum or colon. It is not limited to a single visceral contact.'),
('d2370b65-163b-5a45-8e4a-ad2534c8fc70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The pancreas lies posterior to:', '["The duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","The spleen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The stomach (separated by the lesser sac)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The stomach (separated by the lesser sac)', 'The pancreas lies behind the stomach, separated from it by the lesser sac (omental bursa), which is why pancreatic inflammation irritates the posterior gastric wall. It is not behind the spleen, liver, or duodenum.'),
('22aed930-5f4a-5488-8156-1d76293b9703'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The pancreas is anterior to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","The superior mesenteric vessels, the portal vein, the bile duct, and the IVC","The colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superior mesenteric vessels, the portal vein, the bile duct, and the IVC', 'Posterior relations of the pancreas include the superior mesenteric vessels, the portal vein (formed behind its neck), the bile duct, and the IVC. The stomach and duodenum lie anteriorly, not posterior to it.'),
('a63bc0f3-b65f-5b10-ba67-581a50f30086'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The splenic vein is a major tributary of:', '["The SV resulting in decreased functional activity","The IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The left renal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein (behind the pancreatic body)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The portal vein (behind the pancreatic body)', 'The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreas to form the portal vein. It does not drain into the IVC or the left renal vein.'),
('c6eb3163-5e69-5fff-93d6-fbf33737fe1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The SMV is a tributary of:', '["The portal vein (joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck)","The left renal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The SV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The portal vein (joins the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck)', 'The superior mesenteric vein converges with the splenic vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. It does not drain into the IVC or the renal veins.'),
('efe65ce7-177a-55fb-b0bd-3c5c912a83e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The IMV drains into:', '["The portal vein directly","The IVC","The splenic vein (usually)","The SMV","None"]'::jsonb, 'The splenic vein (usually)', 'The inferior mesenteric vein most commonly drains into the splenic vein, carrying hindgut blood toward the portal system. It does not empty into the systemic IVC.'),
('479c2685-8d99-5c36-b087-7aa9385a79a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The portal vein is about:', '["8-10 cm long","20 cm","2 cm","15 cm","None"]'::jsonb, '8-10 cm long', 'The portal vein measures about 8-10 cm, running from behind the pancreatic neck to the porta hepatis. It is far longer than 2 cm and shorter than 15-20 cm.'),
('c5394a65-bb5c-59d9-8226-deedec95345c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The portal vein carries:', '["Blood rich in oxygen from the heart resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood from the liver to the IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood rich in nutrients from the gastrointestinal tract to the liver","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blood rich in nutrients from the gastrointestinal tract to the liver', 'The portal vein delivers nutrient-rich, deoxygenated blood from the abdominal GI tract to the liver for processing and first-pass metabolism. It does not carry oxygenated blood from the heart or drain the liver to the IVC.'),
('4a4c02ec-7fcf-5c3c-90fb-493ce3905a6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Portal hypertension can cause:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophageal varices, splenomegaly, ascites, and caput medusae","Only splenomegaly mediated by specific receptor activation","Only esophageal varices without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ascites leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal varices, splenomegaly, ascites, and caput medusae', 'Raised portal pressure dilates portosystemic anastomoses, producing esophageal varices and caput medusae, and raises splenic pressure (splenomegaly) and peritoneal transudation (ascites). No single one of these features occurs in isolation.'),
('7ef08889-b9ab-598a-a149-c6bf09e8e846'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The portosystemic anastomosis includes:', '["Only esophageal resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only umbilical accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only rectal mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophageal (left gastric vein and azygos), rectal (superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal), and umbilical (paraumbilical to epigastric) anastomoses"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal (left gastric vein and azygos), rectal (superior rectal to middle and inferior rectal), and umbilical (paraumbilical to epigastric) anastomoses', 'Portosystemic connections exist at three main sites: esophageal (left gastric to azygos), rectal (superior rectal to middle/inferior rectal), and umbilical (paraumbilical to epigastric). All three sites participate, not a single one.'),
('f4dabad9-ae5d-5826-9dbe-8a56569be177'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The most important portosystemic anastomosis clinically is:', '["The umbilical anastomosis (caput medusae) resulting in decreased functional activity","The esophageal anastomosis (left gastric vein and azygos vein) - can cause esophageal varices","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rectal anastomosis (hemorrhoids) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The esophageal anastomosis (left gastric vein and azygos vein) - can cause esophageal varices', 'The esophageal portosystemic connection is most critical because rupture of esophageal varices causes life-threatening hemorrhage in portal hypertension. Caput medusae and hemorrhoids are clinically less dangerous.'),
('02f15b2f-f7d1-5c78-a72f-750c43f49c7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The round ligament of the liver (ligamentum teres) is:', '["None","The obliterated ductus venosus","The obliterated left umbilical vein","The obliterated ductus arteriosus","The obliterated urachus"]'::jsonb, 'The obliterated left umbilical vein', 'The ligamentum teres is the fibrous remnant of the left umbilical vein that carried oxygenated blood from the placenta in fetal life. The ductus venosus, ductus arteriosus, and urachus leave different remnants.'),
('d33ae6ae-67b4-566d-885b-bc364f942626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ligamentum venosum is:', '["The obliterated umbilical vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The obliterated ductus venosus (connects the left portal vein to the IVC)","The obliterated ductus arteriosus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The obliterated urachus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obliterated ductus venosus (connects the left portal vein to the IVC)', 'The ligamentum venosum is the obliterated ductus venosus, which in fetal life shunted blood from the left portal vein to the IVC, bypassing the liver. It is not derived from the umbilical vein, ductus arteriosus, or urachus.'),
('a20d3ffc-1cff-548d-9535-dbe2f8488442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left umbilical vein enters the liver at:', '["The hepatic veins resulting in decreased functional activity","The left branch of the portal vein (via the falciform ligament)","The right branch of the portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left branch of the portal vein (via the falciform ligament)', 'The left umbilical vein courses in the falciform ligament and joins the left branch of the portal vein; its remnant is the ligamentum teres. It does not connect to the hepatic veins or the IVC directly.'),
('184c169e-d936-5541-b503-c7db95c0e8bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ductus venosus connects:', '["The umbilical vein to the IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The portal vein to the IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","The left portal vein to the IVC (bypassing the liver in fetal life)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left portal vein to the IVC (bypassing the liver in fetal life)', 'In fetal life the ductus venosus connects the left portal vein to the IVC, allowing oxygenated placental blood to bypass the hepatic sinusoids. It does not directly connect the umbilical vein or the main portal vein to the IVC.'),
('48a0fbaf-ba42-5597-b590-15edef351796'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'In the fetus, the right umbilical vein:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Persists accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrophies (only the left umbilical vein persists)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Becomes the round ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrophies (only the left umbilical vein persists)', 'The right umbilical vein regresses during early fetal development, leaving the left umbilical vein as the sole channel from the placenta. It does not persist, become the round ligament, or remain functional.'),
('97f8900b-2116-5639-9c0c-0a46cd6dd71a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The confluence of the SMV and splenic vein occurs:', '["Behind the neck of the pancreas","In front of the neck of the pancreas","None","At the porta hepatis","In the liver"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the neck of the pancreas', 'The superior mesenteric and splenic veins unite behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein, a key surgical landmark. This confluence does not occur at the porta hepatis or within the liver.'),
('d8aa93b8-622e-5396-a9d1-ee99ac5bd4bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The portal vein bifurcates at:', '["The porta hepatis (into the right and left portal branches)","At the duodenum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the celiac trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Behind the pancreatic neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The porta hepatis (into the right and left portal branches)', 'The portal vein divides at the porta hepatis into right and left branches supplying the corresponding hepatic lobes. It forms behind the pancreatic neck but bifurcates only at the liver hilum.'),
('02d4ed18-b69e-57c1-a2c7-e69976437288'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The right branch of the portal vein supplies:', '["The quadrate lobe","None","The left lobe","The right lobe of the liver","The caudate lobe"]'::jsonb, 'The right lobe of the liver', 'The right portal branch supplies the right hepatic lobe (segments V-VIII), the larger functional lobe. The quadrate, caudate, and left lobes receive blood via the left portal branch.'),
('3dd8ab77-eaf8-5589-a513-dadc813e0a20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left branch of the portal vein supplies:', '["Only the quadrate lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","The left lobe, caudate lobe, and quadrate lobe","Only the caudate lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the left lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left lobe, caudate lobe, and quadrate lobe', 'The left portal branch perfuses the left lobe and also the caudate and quadrate lobes, giving off the ligamentum teres branch. It is not limited to any one of these lobes alone.'),
('344e9c6a-0517-5b1b-b139-1b35cd768adf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The cystic vein drains into:', '["The IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","The splenic vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein (or directly into the liver)","The hepatic vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The portal vein (or directly into the liver)', 'The cystic vein empties into the portal vein or directly into the liver parenchyma at the gallbladder bed. It does not drain into the IVC, splenic vein, or hepatic veins.'),
('970c8b67-7b7b-590b-b76c-addfca948e1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The inferior mesenteric vein (IMV) drains:', '["The hindgut (descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum)","The small intestine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The foregut mediated by specific receptor activation","The midgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hindgut (descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum)', 'The IMV drains hindgut structures: the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum. Small intestine and foregut are drained by the SMV and portal venous tributaries respectively, not by the IMV.'),
('aae161ef-0a31-50d7-a1dc-9af3a10ac0c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The splenic vein drains:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The spleen, and also receives the short gastric, left gastroepiploic, pancreatic, and IMV","Only the spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","The spleen and the stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spleen, and also receives the short gastric, left gastroepiploic, pancreatic, and IMV', 'The splenic vein drains the spleen and collects the short gastric, left gastroepiploic, pancreatic veins, and the IMV before joining the SMV. Its drainage is not confined to the spleen alone.'),
('045176b3-56a1-525d-8d1e-845826f25e9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left gastric vein drains into:', '["The SMV resulting in decreased functional activity","The IMV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The splenic vein mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The portal vein (directly)"]'::jsonb, 'The portal vein (directly)', 'The left gastric vein empties directly into the portal vein and anastomoses with the azygos vein at the gastroesophageal junction, forming the esophageal portosystemic connection. It does not drain into the splenic, superior, or inferior mesenteric veins.'),
('2915cec9-0eae-5c57-b826-1fe2f2da4ebd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The right gastric vein drains into:', '["The splenic vein","None","The IMV","The SMV","The portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'The portal vein', 'The right gastric vein drains the lesser curvature of the stomach and pyloric region directly into the portal vein. It does not join the splenic, superior, or inferior mesenteric venous systems.'),
('ae835225-57a0-5f8a-9ba6-bfac69b0ffe6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The paraumbilical veins drain into:', '["The umbilical vein","The right branch of the portal vein","The IVC","None","The left branch of the portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'The left branch of the portal vein', 'The paraumbilical veins travel in the falciform ligament and drain into the left branch of the portal vein; they dilate in portal hypertension producing caput medusae. They do not enter the right portal branch, IVC, or umbilical vein remnant.'),
('3908ef9f-8195-5a45-95c5-f41b4bebc41f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The portal vein carries blood from:', '["Only the GI tract resulting in decreased functional activity","The GI tract (from the lower esophagus to the upper rectum), the gallbladder, pancreas, and spleen","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the gallbladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The GI tract (from the lower esophagus to the upper rectum), the gallbladder, pancreas, and spleen', 'The portal vein receives venous blood from the whole GI tract (lower esophagus to upper rectum) plus the gallbladder, pancreas, and spleen. Its inflow is not restricted to any one of these organs.'),
('e596069c-d303-5941-9b28-037d44ca1308'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The portal system is a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphatic system mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal venous system (two capillary beds: GI tract to liver)","A portal arterial system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal venous system (two capillary beds: GI tract to liver)', 'The portal system is a venous system placed between two capillary beds: capillaries of the gut and spleen drain into the portal vein, which then re-branches into hepatic sinusoids. It is neither lymphatic nor arterial.'),
('efc4bbf3-45b9-513e-b983-8c37670e857e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The hepatic veins drain into:', '["The portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The SMV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IVC (the three hepatic veins: right, middle, and left)","The splenic vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC (the three hepatic veins: right, middle, and left)', 'The right, middle, and left hepatic veins carry deoxygenated blood from the liver into the IVC just below the diaphragm. They are outflow vessels of the liver and do not drain into the portal vein or mesenteric veins.'),
('7de1e6d3-47e6-5393-9cd4-df73d82266dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The hepatic veins are located:', '["In the falciform ligament","Between the liver and the IVC (superiorly)","At the porta hepatis","None","In the gastrohepatic ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Between the liver and the IVC (superiorly)', 'The hepatic veins emerge from the superior surface of the liver and enter the IVC at its dome, below the diaphragm. They are not located at the porta hepatis or within the falciform or gastrohepatic ligaments.'),
('1f55aab3-7e8b-5f9f-8b19-792909f521fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The right hepatic vein drains:', '["The left lobe","The right lobe (segments V-VIII)","The quadrate lobe","The caudate lobe","None"]'::jsonb, 'The right lobe (segments V-VIII)', 'The right hepatic vein drains the right lobe of the liver (segments V-VIII) and is the largest of the three hepatic veins. The left, quadrate, and caudate lobes are drained by other venous channels.'),
('9bf0ca9e-e2aa-5ed6-b865-47ec537dddc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The middle hepatic vein drains:', '["The entire left lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","The right lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The quadrate lobe (segment IV) and the anterior part of the right lobe","The caudate lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The quadrate lobe (segment IV) and the anterior part of the right lobe', 'The middle hepatic vein runs in the main lobar (Cantlie''s) fissure and drains segment IV (quadrate lobe) together with the anterior parts of the right lobe. It does not drain the entire left lobe or the caudate lobe.'),
('069f9c70-47b7-552f-9611-2079a7cf4b29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left hepatic vein drains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The quadrate lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The left lobe (segments II and III)","The right lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The caudate lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left lobe (segments II and III)', 'The left hepatic vein drains the lateral segments of the left lobe (segments II and III) and often joins the middle hepatic vein before entering the IVC. It does not drain the right, quadrate, or caudate lobes.'),
('4f1b36f1-0e07-57fe-8241-b2c3bf227251'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The caudate lobe drains directly into:', '["The IVC (short hepatic veins)","The middle hepatic vein","None","The right hepatic vein","The left hepatic vein"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC (short hepatic veins)', 'The caudate lobe drains directly into the IVC through several short hepatic veins, which spares it in Budd-Chiari syndrome caused by hepatic vein thrombosis. It does not drain via the right, middle, or left hepatic veins.'),
('27ce4b11-bbe0-546e-96a0-1acabafcbfc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The liver can regenerate:', '["No resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in children accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only after a partial resection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Yes (it has a remarkable regenerative capacity)"]'::jsonb, 'Yes (it has a remarkable regenerative capacity)', 'The liver has outstanding regenerative capacity, regrowing to its original size after loss of up to 75% of its mass, regardless of age or the trigger for injury. It does not lack regeneration or regenerate only in children.'),
('4a821903-5907-54da-96b7-82ee50c9cc8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The pancreas develops from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","The endodermal lining of the duodenum (dorsal and ventral buds)","The ectoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The endodermal lining of the duodenum (dorsal and ventral buds)', 'The pancreas arises from dorsal and ventral endodermal buds of the foregut-duodenum that rotate and fuse during development. It is not derived from neural crest, mesoderm, or ectoderm.'),
('03c0e78f-b43b-554e-99d9-617b158e63a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The gallbladder develops from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The hindgut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The midgut mediated by specific receptor activation","The foregut directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An outgrowth of the bile duct (from the hepatic diverticulum)"]'::jsonb, 'An outgrowth of the bile duct (from the hepatic diverticulum)', 'The gallbladder forms as a ventral outgrowth of the bile duct arising from the hepatic diverticulum of the foregut. It does not develop directly from the foregut, midgut, or hindgut.'),
('f63b8fe2-9d61-506b-85c9-36426abaa36d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The liver develops from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ectoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","The hepatic diverticulum (endodermal foregut)","The neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hepatic diverticulum (endodermal foregut)', 'The liver develops from the hepatic diverticulum, an endodermal outgrowth of the foregut whose cells proliferate into hepatoblasts and invade the surrounding mesenchyme. Mesoderm, ectoderm, and neural crest are not its source.'),
('efa2607e-f5ef-550c-a3be-e709a08c4c6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The spleen develops from:', '["The neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","The endoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesenchymal cells in the dorsal mesogastrium (mesodermal)","The ectoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesenchymal cells in the dorsal mesogastrium (mesodermal)', 'The spleen has a mesodermal origin, arising from mesenchymal cells in the dorsal mesogastrium of the developing gut. Because it is not from endoderm or ectoderm, it lacks an epithelial lining and has no true capsule-covered glandular structure.'),
('baf5553e-f715-5020-b846-116b39f23a31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is located in the:', '["Left upper quadrant","Right lower quadrant (McBurney point)","Right upper quadrant","None","Left lower quadrant"]'::jsonb, 'Right lower quadrant (McBurney point)', 'The appendix lies in the right lower quadrant, where its base is marked at McBurney''s point on the spinoumbilical line. This location explains why appendicitis produces maximal tenderness in the right lower quadrant rather than elsewhere in the abdomen.'),
('57f580d8-62b7-5dc1-b0ca-d4029a7c5766'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is a:', '["Part of the colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of the small intestine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vermiform appendix (a blind-ended tube connected to the cecum)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vermiform appendix (a blind-ended tube connected to the cecum)', 'The vermiform appendix is a worm-shaped, blind-ended tube arising from the posteromedial wall of the cecum just below the ileocecal junction. It is a cecal diverticulum with abundant lymphoid tissue rather than a segment of the colon lumen or small intestine.'),
('9389043c-2d66-5638-aa65-6ec40c8c3726'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix has a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","mesentery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesentery (mesoappendix) that contains the appendicular vessels","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only a peritoneal covering leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mesentery (mesoappendix) that contains the appendicular vessels', 'The appendix has a complete mesentery, the mesoappendix, which carries the appendicular artery, vein, and lymphatics from the mesentery of the terminal ileum. The appendicular vessels run within this fold, making it an important landmark for the classical appendectomy incision.'),
('87d88abc-c444-50df-86df-206b7c653f8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendicular artery arises from:', '["The celiac trunk resulting in decreased functional activity","The right colic artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IMA mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ileocolic artery (a branch of the SMA)"]'::jsonb, 'The ileocolic artery (a branch of the SMA)', 'The appendicular artery is a branch of the ileocolic artery, itself a branch of the superior mesenteric artery. It is an end artery running in the mesoappendix, so occlusion by thrombosis or inflammation can cause gangrene of the appendix.'),
('86d9dc1f-d099-558b-aa57-7da8e2ff2a0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix is innervated by:', '["Only sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The sympathetic and parasympathetic (vagus) from the celiac and superior mesenteric plexuses","The lumbar plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sympathetic and parasympathetic (vagus) from the celiac and superior mesenteric plexuses', 'The appendix receives both sympathetic fibers from the celiac and superior mesenteric plexuses and parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve. Because both autonomic divisions accompany the midgut vasculature, only autonomic nerve plexuses provide its innervation.'),
('b0ee5232-1bb6-5fa3-ad33-4faceec1dd14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The appendix can be found in various positions:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only subcecal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only pelvic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only retrocecal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retrocecal (most common), pelvic, subcecal, preileal, and postileal"]'::jsonb, 'Retrocecal (most common), pelvic, subcecal, preileal, and postileal', 'The appendix has variable positions including retrocecal, pelvic, subcecal, preileal, and postileal, although its base remains fixed at the posteromedial cecum. The retrocecal position is most common, and pelvic placement explains referred obturator or psoas findings.'),
('310c2307-d34b-51b5-9942-a8e288ef4bdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Appendicitis presents with:', '["Right upper quadrant pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Left lower quadrant pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epigastric pain mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Periumbilical pain that migrates to the right lower quadrant (McBurney point)"]'::jsonb, 'Periumbilical pain that migrates to the right lower quadrant (McBurney point)', 'Early appendicitis produces periumbilical visceral pain because the appendix shares midgut splanchnic innervation, then pain localizes to the right lower quadrant as inflammation irritates the parietal peritoneum. This migration to McBurney''s point is the classic diagnostic sequence.'),
('7840b1ed-d6e1-5cb7-ba72-d8ecf5179a59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The Rovsing sign for appendicitis is:', '["Palpation of the left lower quadrant causes pain in the right lower quadrant","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain on flexion of the right hip mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain on extension of the right hip leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palpation of the left lower quadrant causes pain in the right lower quadrant', 'Rovsing''s sign is present when pressure applied to the left lower quadrant displaces colonic gas that distends the cecum and reproduces pain in the right lower quadrant. It indicates irritation of the inflamed appendix even though the stimulus is applied contralaterally.'),
('03b15875-c43a-5e54-afdb-8e4349825306'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The psoas sign for appendicitis is:', '["Pain on flexion of the right hip resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain on extension of the right hip (when the appendix is retrocecal and irritates the psoas)","Pain on extension of the left hip without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain on extension of the right hip (when the appendix is retrocecal and irritates the psoas)', 'Stretching the psoas major by extending the right hip reproduces pain when a retrocecal appendix lies against the muscle. This sign is therefore positive specifically on the right, where the inflamed appendix irritates the adjacent psoas during extension.'),
('08d41457-0080-549f-8b2f-f00bb83eddfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The obturator sign for appendicitis is:', '["Pain on extension of the right hip resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain on internal rotation of the flexed right hip (when the appendix is in the pelvis)","Pain on external rotation of the right hip mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain on internal rotation of the flexed right hip (when the appendix is in the pelvis)', 'Internally rotating the flexed right hip stretches the obturator internus muscle, which produces pain when a pelvic appendix lies against it. The sign is therefore elicited on the right side and indicates a pelvic rather than retrocecal position of the appendix.'),
('c1463190-9a59-55dc-ae21-90a72ed68082'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The right colic (hepatic) flexure is located:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","At the junction of the descending and sigmoid colons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the junction of the ascending and transverse colons (under the liver)","At the junction of the transverse and descending colons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the junction of the ascending and transverse colons (under the liver)', 'The right colic flexure, also called the hepatic flexure, is the bend where the ascending colon turns medially into the transverse colon in the right upper quadrant. It sits in the impression of the liver, which is why it is described as being under that organ.'),
('19b7fdde-01b2-5373-8547-f4c4fb003b69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left colic (splenic) flexure is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","At the junction of the descending and sigmoid colons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the junction of the ascending and transverse colons mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the junction of the transverse and descending colons (under the spleen)"]'::jsonb, 'At the junction of the transverse and descending colons (under the spleen)', 'The left colic flexure, also called the splenic flexure, marks the junction of the transverse and descending colons in the left upper quadrant. It lies beneath the spleen and is higher and more acute than the right colic flexure.'),
('70c646ec-eb4d-5530-84f0-780490f5dbd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The left colic flexure is higher than the right colic flexure:', '["None","All","They are at the same level","True","False"]'::jsonb, 'True', 'The splenic flexure reaches about the 8th rib level, whereas the hepatic flexure is displaced downward by the mass of the liver. This height difference is a well-recognized feature that also explains the tendency for gas to collect at the higher splenic flexure.'),
('88b0fcb1-29fe-5d43-abf0-da4f518890c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The sigmoid colon is about:', '["20 cm","None","10 cm","30 cm","40 cm long"]'::jsonb, '40 cm long', 'The sigmoid colon forms an S-shaped loop about 40 cm in length, extending from the left iliac fossa to the rectosigmoid junction at S3. It has a complete mesentery, the sigmoid mesocolon, and its length permits distension and volvulus in elderly patients.'),
('048e18ae-bbd5-5555-b181-1ca92e6d6607'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The colon is distinguished from the small intestine by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The plicae circulares accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The villi mediated by specific receptor activation","The presence of Peyer patches without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The taenia coli, haustra, and epiploic appendages"]'::jsonb, 'The taenia coli, haustra, and epiploic appendages', 'The three taenia coli, the sacculated haustra, and the fat-filled epiploic appendages are distinctive of the large intestine and are absent from the small bowel. Plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches belong to the small intestine, so they cannot distinguish the colon.'),
('56c52b24-ae09-5121-84e9-1d10acb10f61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The haustra are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Sacculations (pouches) of the colon wall between the taeniae","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Circular folds without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Longitudinal folds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sacculations (pouches) of the colon wall between the taeniae', 'Haustra are the outward pouches or sacculations of the colon wall produced because the longitudinal taeniae are shorter than the bowel itself. They are alternating bulges with intervening crescentic folds and are not the circular or longitudinal folds of the small intestine.'),
('174cb008-e70b-573a-b3ae-e30cdf4d0376'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The colon absorbs:', '["Water and electrolytes (mainly in the ascending colon)","Proteins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Fats without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nutrients leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Water and electrolytes (mainly in the ascending colon)', 'The colon reabsorbs water and electrolytes, predominantly in the ascending colon, converting liquid ileal chyme into semisolid feces. Fat, protein, and most nutrients are absorbed in the small intestine, not in the large bowel.'),
('f0e290b6-8509-5044-a331-0d173d845563'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The colon also absorbs:', '["Glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Vitamins (especially vitamin K) produced by the gut flora","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Amino acids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fatty acids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamins (especially vitamin K) produced by the gut flora', 'Colonic bacteria synthesize vitamin K and biotin, which are absorbed by the large intestine and contribute to the body''s stores. This explains why prolonged antibiotic use or colectomy can predispose to vitamin K deficiency.'),
('8b319ba5-ce4d-537d-ac23-d19294cc3678'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The colon functions to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Secrete enzymes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Store and eliminate waste products (feces)","Absorb nutrients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digest food leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Store and eliminate waste products (feces)', 'The colon stores fecal material and expels it during defecation, while additionally absorbing remaining water and hosting the microbiome. Nutrient digestion and enzyme secretion are functions of the stomach and small intestine rather than the large bowel.'),
('65d2423b-bf04-5642-8ac7-229cbac5295f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum is the:', '["Terminal part of the large intestine (about 12-15 cm long)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First part of the large intestine mediated by specific receptor activation","Part of the sigmoid colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Terminal part of the large intestine (about 12-15 cm long)', 'The rectum is the fixed terminal segment of the large intestine, about 12-15 cm long, running from the rectosigmoid junction to the anorectal junction. It is not the first part of the large intestine nor a continuation of the sigmoid colon, although it follows it.'),
('9d75a9e0-eb32-5327-bbe9-050f37b6d792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum is located:', '["In the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity","In the pelvis, anterior to the sacrum and coccyx","In the abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","In the thorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the pelvis, anterior to the sacrum and coccyx', 'The rectum lies in the true pelvis, running along the concavity of the sacrum and coccyx. This posterior bony relationship explains its sacral curve and the ease of digital rectal examination from the perineum.'),
('f543c8a4-e981-5cc7-a7c6-6af56174d076'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The rectum also has an anteroposterior curve:', '["anteroposterior curve resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the perineal flexure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacral flexure and the perineal flexure (anorectal angle)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the sacral flexure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral flexure and the perineal flexure (anorectal angle)', 'The rectum has two anteroposterior curves: the sacral flexure, which follows the concave surface of the sacrum, and the perineal flexure, the anorectal angle created by the puborectalis sling. Together these curves produce the S-shaped course of the rectum in the sagittal plane.'),
('79ef8efa-9287-5eb5-b113-fa3c34b64cd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anorectal angle is maintained by:', '["The puborectalis sling (part of the levator ani)","The external anal sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anal columns mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal anal sphincter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The puborectalis sling (part of the levator ani)', 'The puborectalis, part of the levator ani, forms a sling that pulls the anorectal junction anteriorly, creating the anorectal angle essential for fecal continence. Relaxation of this muscle during defecation straightens the angle to allow stool passage, a role not shared by the sphincters.'),
('b7abad70-d41f-5e82-82bb-4524b9688e01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The superior rectal vein drains into:', '["The external iliac vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior mesenteric vein (portal system)","The IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","The internal iliac vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior mesenteric vein (portal system)', 'The superior rectal vein ascends and joins the inferior mesenteric vein, so blood from the upper rectum enters the portal venous system. This arrangement forms the clinically important upper portosystemic anastomosis at the rectum.'),
('6a630f5e-03e2-503c-a3d6-d2925c4669ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The middle rectal vein drains into:', '["The internal iliac vein (systemic system)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The IMV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The SMV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac vein (systemic system)', 'The middle rectal veins drain into the internal iliac veins, making the mid-rectal drainage part of the systemic venous system. This contrast with the portal drainage of the superior rectal vein underpins the portosystemic anastomosis in the rectal wall.'),
('847ac3aa-644a-52aa-ad46-149264fe565d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The inferior rectal vein drains into:', '["The SMV resulting in decreased functional activity","The IMV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal pudendal vein -> internal iliac vein"]'::jsonb, 'The internal pudendal vein -> internal iliac vein', 'The inferior rectal veins drain into the internal pudendal veins, which continue into the internal iliac veins of the systemic circulation. Consequently the lower anal canal below the pectinate line drains systemically, completing the portal-systemic communication in the anal region.'),
('7747348b-e845-5bf0-b39a-bf933e126ab4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Internal hemorrhoids are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arterial enlargements mediated by specific receptor activation","Dilated veins of the superior rectal plexus (above the pectinate line)","Dilated veins of the inferior rectal plexus (below the pectinate line) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated veins of the superior rectal plexus (above the pectinate line)', 'Internal hemorrhoids represent engorgement of the superior rectal plexus situated above the pectinate line. Because they are covered by mucosa and supplied by autonomic nerves, they typically bleed painlessly.'),
('6eecd876-b671-5723-a599-e9a81410d3cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'External hemorrhoids are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Arterial enlargements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Dilated veins of the inferior rectal plexus (below the pectinate line)","Dilated veins of the superior rectal plexus (above the pectinate line) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated veins of the inferior rectal plexus (below the pectinate line)', 'External hemorrhoids arise from dilatation of the inferior rectal plexus located below the pectinate line. They are covered by skin and innervated by somatic nerves, which explains their often severe pain, particularly when thrombosed.'),
('1b020d44-bb5f-577e-ae40-467e28c3e596'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Internal hemorrhoids are covered by:', '["All","Keratinized skin","Skin (stratified squamous epithelium)","None","Mucosa (columnar epithelium)"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosa (columnar epithelium)', 'Because internal hemorrhoids lie above the pectinate line, they are covered by mucous membrane with columnar epithelium. In contrast, structures below the pectinate line are lined by stratified squamous skin, so internal hemorrhoids do not carry the latter covering.'),
('63f53f4a-7a99-556c-b583-6f8d3317020b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'External hemorrhoids are covered by:', '["Keratinized skin resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin (stratified squamous epithelium)","Mucosa (columnar epithelium) mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin (stratified squamous epithelium)', 'External hemorrhoids lie below the pectinate line, where the anal canal is lined by skin composed of stratified squamous epithelium. This epithelial covering is continuous with the perianal skin and explains the somatic sensitivity of external hemorrhoids.'),
('820cadaf-6537-59b1-980f-cbd5d09d6bfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Internal hemorrhoids are usually:', '["Painful resulting in decreased functional activity","Associated with severe pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Painless (but may bleed with defecation)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Painless (but may bleed with defecation)', 'Internal hemorrhoids are typically painless because they lie above the pectinate line and receive visceral autonomic innervation that does not transmit somatic pain. Their most frequent symptom is fresh rectal bleeding on defecation, not discomfort.'),
('36ea8033-8bf4-5e6b-bdc3-8f085ba5e81a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'External hemorrhoids are usually:', '["Painless resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Asymptomatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Painful (especially if thrombosed)"]'::jsonb, 'Painful (especially if thrombosed)', 'External hemorrhoids lie below the pectinate line and are innervated by somatic pudendal fibers, making them sensitive to pain. Acute thrombosis of such a hemorrhoid produces a firm, exquisitely tender perianal swelling.'),
('614cf1fa-df27-5a2d-93d2-4912bf879af8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal canal is innervated above the pectinate line by:', '["Autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic) - insensitive to pain","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatic nerves (pudendal nerve) - sensitive to pain mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic) - insensitive to pain', 'Above the pectinate line the anal canal receives autonomic sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers that do not convey sharp somatic pain. This visceral innervation is why lesions above the line, such as internal hemorrhoids, tend to be painless.'),
('db749590-80a7-5d86-a72d-f01aa53b6b2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal canal is innervated below the pectinate line by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatic nerves (pudendal nerve) - sensitive to pain","Autonomic nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatic nerves (pudendal nerve) - sensitive to pain', 'Below the pectinate line the anal canal is supplied by somatic fibers of the pudendal nerve, which transmit pain, temperature, and touch. This somatic innervation explains why external hemorrhoids, anal fissures, and anorectal abscesses below this line are intensely painful.'),
('b494b25e-ea9e-5eed-8a6f-fb59d9ec4c79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal sphincters are supplied by:', '["The obturator nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior rectal nerve (branch of the pudendal nerve)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal nerve (branch of the pudendal nerve)', 'The inferior rectal nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve, supplies the external anal sphincter with somatic motor and sensory fibers, allowing voluntary control of defecation. The obturator, femoral, and sciatic nerves have no role in anal sphincter innervation.'),
('5572439e-3368-5300-bd89-9fa25a8aa593'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The internal anal sphincter is supplied by:', '["The inferior rectal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatic nerves (pudendal) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic and parasympathetic (autonomic) nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic and parasympathetic (autonomic) nerves', 'The internal anal sphincter is smooth muscle innervated by autonomic fibers from the inferior hypogastric plexus. Because it is under involuntary control, it maintains resting anal tone without somatic nerve input.'),
('4b4d53d8-2df3-5166-a405-ace66636f186'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The external anal sphincter is supplied by:', '["Autonomic nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatic nerves (the inferior rectal nerve from the pudendal nerve)","Parasympathetic nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatic nerves (the inferior rectal nerve from the pudendal nerve)', 'The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle innervated by somatic fibers carried in the inferior rectal nerve from the pudendal nerve. This somatic supply permits voluntary contraction and relaxation, giving conscious control over defecation.'),
('544f3555-6b72-536c-955d-f208f0aacd78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal canal is related to:', '["The sacrum only resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostate/urethra in males and the vagina in females (anteriorly), and the coccyx (posteriorly)","The bladder only mediated by specific receptor activation","The uterus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostate/urethra in males and the vagina in females (anteriorly), and the coccyx (posteriorly)', 'Anteriorly the anal canal is related to the prostate and membranous urethra in males and to the vagina in females, while posteriorly it abuts the coccyx and anococcygeal body. These sex-specific anterior relations matter for spread of infection and for digital examination findings.'),
('e72b1571-d10f-5f0f-95b3-fa6646a05088'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The anal ischiorectal (ischoanal) fossa is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fat-filled space on either side of the anal canal","A space in front of the anal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A space behind the anal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fat-filled space on either side of the anal canal', 'The ischiorectal fossa is a wedge-shaped, fat-filled space on each side of the anal canal that allows the canal to distend during defecation. Its loose fat is a common site for abscess formation, and infection here produces severe pain.'),
('7cadb05e-d902-5893-a358-7d3c126d5373'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ischiorectal fossa is bounded by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The ischium (laterally), the rectum (medially) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator internus (laterally), the levator ani (medially), and the perineal skin (inferiorly)","The sacrum (posteriorly) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator internus (laterally), the levator ani (medially), and the perineal skin (inferiorly)', 'The ischiorectal fossa is bounded laterally by the obturator internus fascia and ischial tuberosity, medially by the levator ani and external anal sphincter, and inferiorly by perineal skin. These boundaries define its wedge shape and dictate the spread of ischiorectal abscesses.'),
('46a29d0b-3cd9-5d5a-8470-aefa771ae32b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'The ischiorectal fossa contains:', '["Fat, the pudendal nerve, and the internal pudendal vessels in the pudendal canal","Only fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only blood vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat, the pudendal nerve, and the internal pudendal vessels in the pudendal canal', 'The fossa is packed with fat and also lodges the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels, which traverse its lateral wall within the pudendal canal. These neurovascular structures supply the perineum and anal canal, so perianal pathology can involve them.'),
('6335c435-acc8-5c8e-b6b1-0743fb69bf5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'An ischiorectal abscess occurs in:', '["The ischiorectal fossa","The anal canal","The rectum","The perineum","None"]'::jsonb, 'The ischiorectal fossa', 'An ischiorectal abscess is a suppurative collection within the fat of the ischiorectal fossa, often arising from an infected anal gland tracking across the intersphincteric space. It presents with throbbing perianal pain, swelling, and fever, and requires surgical drainage.'),
('eff89c1d-7e3a-5ebc-a120-100780e1025d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'In Intestines, appendix and colon, which statement best fits the following fact: the jejunum is characterized by?', '["Thick wall, tall plicae circulares, long vasa recta, less fat in mesentery","Thin wall, fewer plicae, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The presence of Peyer patches mediated by specific receptor activation","The presence of taenia coli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thick wall, tall plicae circulares, long vasa recta, less fat in mesentery', 'The jejunum has a thick, vascular wall with tall plicae circulares, long vasa recta, and scanty mesenteric fat, maximizing its absorptive surface. These features contrast sharply with the ileum, which has a thinner wall, shorter vessels, and more mesenteric fat.'),
('e30d1689-8e20-5a57-b7db-b6dab1730859'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Regarding Intestines, appendix and colon, what is the correct answer to: the ileum is characterized by?', '["Thick wall, tall plicae, long vasa recta resulting in decreased functional activity","Thin wall, fewer plicae circulares, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat, Peyer patches","The presence of haustra mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The presence of taenia coli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin wall, fewer plicae circulares, short vasa recta, more mesenteric fat, Peyer patches', 'The ileum has a thinner wall with fewer and lower plicae, short vasa recta, abundant mesenteric fat, and prominent Peyer patches that support immune surveillance. These features distinguish it from the jejunum and are absent in the large intestine, which has haustra and taenia coli instead.'),
('3c03dd50-ebf4-57ac-a8b9-6cf092a4403e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'For Intestines, appendix and colon, which option is most appropriate: the ileocecal junction is?', '["The rectosigmoid junction resulting in decreased functional activity","The pylorus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The junction between the ileum and cecum (ileocecal valve)","The junction of jejunum and ileum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The junction between the ileum and cecum (ileocecal valve)', 'The ileocecal junction is where the terminal ileum opens into the cecum, guarded by the ileocecal valve that prevents reflux of colonic contents. The rectosigmoid junction, pylorus, and jejunoileal junction are entirely separate landmarks.'),
('6732937c-7fed-5bd4-b752-9ed6a94bb952'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'Which answer is correct in Intestines, appendix and colon when asked: mcburney point is located?', '["At the umbilicus resulting in decreased functional activity","At the ASIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1/3 of the way from ASIS to umbilicus (on the spinoumbilical line)","At the pubic tubercle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midpoint of inguinal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1/3 of the way from ASIS to umbilicus (on the spinoumbilical line)', 'McBurney''s point lies one-third of the way along the line joining the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, the spinoumbilical line. It marks the surface position of the appendix base, where tenderness is maximal in acute appendicitis.'),
('e3b35697-be13-5b74-bdbb-0da713368634'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'In Intestines, appendix and colon, the best response to ''The appendix is most commonly located'' is:', '["Postileal (behind terminal ileum)","Preileal (in front of terminal ileum)","Retrocecal (behind the cecum)","Subcecal (below the cecum)","Pelvic (into the pelvis)"]'::jsonb, 'Retrocecal (behind the cecum)', 'The appendix most often lies retrocecal, tucked behind the cecum, in roughly two-thirds of individuals. Because the appendix is usually retroperitoneal in this position, a retrocecal appendix can irritate the psoas and produce a positive psoas sign.'),
('5a0156d2-9cb3-5dd9-bc28-57f08e782637'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'During revision of Intestines, appendix and colon, which of the following is correct: the colon is divided into which parts?', '["Ascending, horizontal, descending, sigmoid","None of the above","Proximal, middle, distal, sigmoid","Right, middle, left, sigmoid","Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending, transverse, descending, sigmoid', 'The colon is divided into four segments by course and flexures: the ascending colon, transverse colon, descending colon, and sigmoid colon. The hepatic and splenic flexures mark the transitions between these segments, so the other naming schemes are inaccurate.'),
('ddb92eee-3226-5097-9a74-0f75ae63f5fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Intestines Appendix Colon', 'In Intestines, appendix and colon, which statement best fits the following fact: the taenia coli are?', '["Circular muscle fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Haustra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epiploic appendages mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle on the colon"]'::jsonb, 'Three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle on the colon', 'The taenia coli are three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle visible on the surface of the colon. Because they are shorter than the bowel wall, they bunch it into haustra; they are distinct from the circular muscle coat and the fatty epiploic appendages.'),
('7f416e94-1e83-5679-b123-7e5b76f2fb53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The liver has how many anatomical lobes?', '["4 (right, left, caudate, quadrate)","2 (right and left)","3 (right, left, caudate)","5 (right, left, caudate, quadrate, Spigelian)","Only 1"]'::jsonb, '4 (right, left, caudate, quadrate)', 'External surface landmarks—ligaments and fissures—define four anatomical lobes: right, left, caudate, and quadrate. Options listing only two or three lobes omit one of these lobes, and the Spigelian lobe is an obsolete synonym for the caudate lobe rather than a fifth structure.'),
('2a061332-f055-5f96-916a-d0811d16a447'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The porta hepatis contains which structures?', '["Abdominal aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic veins mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein, hepatic artery, common hepatic duct (portal triad)"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein, hepatic artery, common hepatic duct (portal triad)', 'The porta hepatis transmits the portal triad—portal vein, hepatic artery proper, and (common) hepatic duct—inside the hepatoduodenal ligament. Hepatic veins and the IVC lie on the posterior superior surface of the liver rather than in the porta hepatis.'),
('eb20dc3f-9b1f-5fd6-91e4-ecd8f3127736'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The bare area of the liver is?', '["The area of the liver in direct contact with the diaphragm (not covered by peritoneum)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The area of the porta hepatis mediated by specific receptor activation","The area covered by the gallbladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area of the liver in direct contact with the diaphragm (not covered by peritoneum)', 'The bare area is the posterior-surface region where the peritoneum is reflected as the coronary ligaments, leaving the liver in direct contact with the diaphragm. This lack of peritoneal cover explains the route of subphrenic abscess drainage and limited lymphatic egress.'),
('a627b203-7acf-549c-8feb-7d9b2a31b35b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The blood supply of the liver is from?', '["Hepatic artery alone","Celiac trunk alone","Portal vein alone","Superior mesenteric artery","Hepatic artery (25%) and portal vein (75%)"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic artery (25%) and portal vein (75%)', 'The liver has a dual blood supply: the hepatic artery proper delivers about 25% of flow as highly oxygenated blood, while the portal vein provides about 75% as nutrient-rich blood from the gut. Any single-vessel option omits the complementary inflow from the other vessel.'),
('14604601-7373-590b-94fc-452f615ec53f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The biliary drainage of the liver begins with?', '["Bile canaliculi to cystic duct directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Bile canaliculi to pancreatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile canaliculi to duodenum directly mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile canaliculi to interlobular bile ducts to right and left hepatic ducts to common hepatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Bile canaliculi to interlobular bile ducts to right and left hepatic ducts to common hepatic duct', 'Bile from hepatocytes enters bile canaliculi, which merge into interlobular bile ducts and then right and left hepatic ducts that form the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct is a side channel for gallbladder storage, not the initial route of hepatic bile drainage.'),
('312ea8a2-388e-50d3-8aac-791b8e5101c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder has which parts?', '["Cardia, fundus, body resulting in decreased functional activity","Fundus, body, neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Head, body, tail without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), neck"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), neck', 'The gallbladder is composed of the fundus (blind end), body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), and neck that narrows into the cystic duct. The ''fundus, body, neck'' option omits the infundibulum, while ''head, body, tail'' uses pancreatic terminology.'),
('3ef5c451-8177-5e45-b4f5-cab4b4ab5f62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Calot triangle is bounded by?', '["Liver, stomach, duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","Common bile duct, portal vein, hepatic artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common hepatic duct medially, cystic duct inferiorly, cystic artery superiorly (or liver)"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic duct medially, cystic duct inferiorly, cystic artery superiorly (or liver)', 'Calot''s triangle is bounded medially by the common hepatic duct, inferiorly by the cystic duct, and superiorly by the cystic artery (or liver surface). The triangle is dissected during cholecystectomy to positively identify the cystic artery before ligation.'),
('1ef9c8a9-b295-5d00-ae7f-a00dce71d562'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cystic artery most commonly arises from?', '["Celiac trunk","Right hepatic artery","Left hepatic artery","Common hepatic artery","Gastroduodenal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Right hepatic artery', 'In about 70% of individuals the cystic artery arises from the right hepatic artery, typically within Calot''s triangle. Origins from the common hepatic, gastroduodenal, or celiac trunk are anatomic variations rather than the usual pattern.'),
('b2f2a6b9-6c0d-585e-a06a-edb5c8819581'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas has which parts?', '["None of the above","Head, body, tail, fundus","Head, neck, body only","Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail","Head, body, tail only"]'::jsonb, 'Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail', 'The pancreas is divided into the head, uncinate process, neck, body, and tail. The uncinate process is a distinct hook-shaped part that projects behind the superior mesenteric vessels, so lists that omit it are anatomically incomplete.'),
('d3726e6a-bc4e-5963-95f3-0279f5c167eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The main pancreatic duct (of Wirsung) joins the common bile duct to form?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)","Cystic duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic duct of Santorini leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)', 'The main pancreatic duct unites with the common bile duct to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater), which opens at the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum. The accessory duct of Santorini drains the superior head separately at the minor papilla.'),
('b7216c3f-baf9-5257-82a5-28971b21f4b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini) opens at?', '["Duodenojejunal flexure","None of the above","Pylorus","Minor duodenal papilla","Major duodenal papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Minor duodenal papilla', 'The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini) opens at the minor duodenal papilla, located proximal to the major papilla in the second part of the duodenum. The major papilla receives the ampulla of Vater and the main pancreatic duct.'),
('4f9cc31a-125f-5d3d-a3e0-79c3fc4ab38d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas is related posteriorly to?', '["IVC, aorta, left renal vessels, splenic vein","Gallbladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IVC, aorta, left renal vessels, splenic vein', 'Posteriorly the pancreas lies over the IVC, abdominal aorta, left renal vessels, and splenic vein—all retroperitoneal structures. The stomach, liver, and gallbladder are anterior or superior relations of the gland.'),
('f3e311ee-3bb3-505c-9b59-3d95948719f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is located in which quadrant?', '["Central","Left lower quadrant","Right lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Left upper quadrant (hypochondrium)"]'::jsonb, 'Left upper quadrant (hypochondrium)', 'The spleen occupies the left upper quadrant (left hypochondrium), positioned deep to the 9th–11th ribs. All other quadrants lie on the contralateral side or inferiorly and do not contain the spleen.'),
('23a4ddcd-85c6-517e-9828-eeec0b2750e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen has which surfaces?', '["Medial and lateral resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphragmatic (superolateral) and visceral (inferomedial)","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior and inferior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior and posterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragmatic (superolateral) and visceral (inferomedial)', 'The spleen has a smooth convex diaphragmatic (superolateral) surface facing the diaphragm and an inferomedial visceral surface bearing gastric, renal, and colic impressions. The other options do not reflect the organ''s true surface anatomy.'),
('b5dd7a12-da53-52b0-9bd9-d714507cd47f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic hilum contains?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Splenic artery, splenic vein, lymphatics, nerve fibers","Portal vein and hepatic artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatic ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic ducts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery, splenic vein, lymphatics, nerve fibers', 'The splenic hilum transmits the splenic artery, splenic vein, lymphatics, and nerve fibers on the medial visceral surface. Portal, hepatic, and pancreatic ductal structures belong to the liver hilum and pancreas, not the spleen.'),
('58a26cee-9aa8-53f8-aa08-6184b44b3756'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The blood supply of the spleen is from?', '["Renal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Left gastric artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common hepatic artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic artery (largest branch of celiac trunk)","Superior mesenteric artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery (largest branch of celiac trunk)', 'The spleen is supplied by the splenic artery, the largest branch of the celiac trunk, which runs tortuously along the superior border of the pancreas to the hilum. Renal, gastric, hepatic, and mesenteric arteries do not provide splenic supply.'),
('a617dfa8-0d42-5c5f-ab9b-f61e4fa20cf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic vein drains into?', '["Azygos vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Left renal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal vein (after joining with SMV)","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein (after joining with SMV)', 'The splenic vein runs behind the body of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the pancreatic neck to form the portal vein. Drainage into the IVC, azygos, or renal vein would be an abnormal portal-systemic connection rather than the normal route.'),
('08f70a0a-5bdb-5fcb-b63d-b50296c11c8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The relations of the spleen include?', '["Liver, gallbladder, duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach (anteromedial), left kidney (posteromedial), diaphragm (superolateral), colon/pancreas (inferior)","Heart, lung, pleura mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right kidney, adrenal, colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (anteromedial), left kidney (posteromedial), diaphragm (superolateral), colon/pancreas (inferior)', 'The spleen is related anteromedially to the stomach, posteromedially to the left kidney, superolaterally to the diaphragm, and inferiorly to the splenic flexure and tail of pancreas. Liver, gallbladder, and right-sided structures occupy the contralateral upper abdomen and are not splenic relations.'),
('a655cf80-a58d-5b2f-8bc5-3ab10692f61e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder fundus is located which surface marking?', '["Umbilicus resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intersection of right midclavicular line and costal margin (9th costal cartilage)","ASIS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Xiphoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intersection of right midclavicular line and costal margin (9th costal cartilage)', 'The gallbladder fundus projects to the surface at the junction of the right midclavicular line and the costal margin (9th costal cartilage), a key point for Murphy''s sign. The umbilicus, xiphoid, and anterior superior iliac spine are unrelated surface landmarks.'),
('b8999494-f18c-5cb2-9162-05ecc89bb94c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common hepatic duct is formed by the union of?', '["None of the above","Right hepatic and cystic ducts","Left hepatic and cystic ducts","Cystic and hepatic ducts","Right and left hepatic ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left hepatic ducts', 'At the porta hepatis the right and left hepatic ducts unite to form the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct later to form the common bile duct, so it is not involved in forming the common hepatic duct.'),
('265da4f3-187b-5249-8449-374d057b22fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common bile duct is formed by the union of?', '["None of the above","Common hepatic duct and cystic duct","Hepatic duct and pancreatic duct","Cystic duct and pancreatic duct","Right and left hepatic ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic duct and cystic duct', 'The common bile duct forms from the junction of the common hepatic duct with the cystic duct within the hepatoduodenal ligament. The right and left hepatic ducts form the common hepatic duct, not the CBD, and the pancreatic duct joins it only near the duodenum.'),
('42a360f5-6d55-57c6-bca0-23e3dcb407ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common bile duct is divided into?', '["Supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, and intraduodenal parts","Hepatic, cystic, and pancreatic parts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Preduodenal, postduodenal, and jejunal parts mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, and intraduodenal parts', 'The common bile duct is divided by its relation to the duodenum into supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic (within the head of the pancreas), and intraduodenal segments. The hepatic/cystic/pancreatic and duodenal/jejunal schemes do not reflect these standard parts.'),
('052f56c8-a69f-5a5e-91c7-ffdf94a33f4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The sphincter of Oddi is located at?', '["The pylorus resulting in decreased functional activity","The ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla) in the duodenal wall","The cystic duct mediated by specific receptor activation","The hepatic duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla) in the duodenal wall', 'The sphincter of Oddi surrounds the terminal common bile duct and pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla within the duodenal wall, regulating the flow of bile and pancreatic juice. It is not found at the pylorus, cystic duct, or hepatic duct.'),
('4ac6f311-445a-5d62-9640-a72a682b79fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lymphatics of the liver drain to?', '["Celiac and hepatic lymph nodes (and phrenic nodes)","Para-aortic nodes only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal iliac nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial inguinal nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac and hepatic lymph nodes (and phrenic nodes)', 'Most hepatic lymphatics run with the portal triads to hepatic nodes and then to the celiac lymph nodes, while a few superficial vessels over the bare area reach the phrenic nodes. The axillary, iliac, inguinal, and para-aortic chains belong to other lymphatic territories.'),
('3e43da5c-253b-5575-a9e2-90d8bb4fd8e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lymphatic drainage of the gallbladder is to?', '["Axillary nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystic nodes to hepatic nodes to celiac nodes","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Para-aortic nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial inguinal nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic nodes to hepatic nodes to celiac nodes', 'Gallbladder lymph drains first to the cystic lymph node near its neck, then to hepatic nodes along the hepatoduodenal ligament, and finally to celiac nodes. Axillary, inguinal, and para-aortic nodes are not in the gallbladder''s drainage pathway.'),
('0a36c436-ec09-5bce-a552-3c0ae1ac4fb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lymphatic drainage of the pancreas is to?', '["Pancreaticosplenic, pancreaticoduodenal, and celiac nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Para-aortic nodes only mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreaticosplenic, pancreaticoduodenal, and celiac nodes', 'Pancreatic lymphatics drain along the splenic artery to pancreaticosplenic nodes, around the duodenum to pancreaticoduodenal nodes, and ultimately to the celiac group. This central abdominal drainage does not involve axillary or inguinal chains.'),
('f288560e-10b1-56de-aff3-e2ace1e0ae7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic lymphatics drain to?', '["Axillary nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial inguinal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreaticosplenic nodes (along splenic artery) to celiac nodes","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Para-aortic nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreaticosplenic nodes (along splenic artery) to celiac nodes', 'Splenic lymphatics follow the splenic artery to pancreaticosplenic nodes and then converge on the celiac lymph nodes. Axillary, inguinal, and para-aortic routes are anatomically incorrect for splenic drainage.'),
('9ce8cdaa-9058-5f7e-9970-d0255ba1f240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The porta hepatis is located on the?', '["Posterior surface","Diaphragmatic surface","Visceral (inferior) surface of the liver","None of the above","Anterior surface"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral (inferior) surface of the liver', 'The porta hepatis is a transverse fissure on the visceral (inferior) surface of the liver through which the portal triad enters and exits. The diaphragmatic surface is smooth convexity, while the posterior surface carries the IVC groove and bare area.'),
('be729b44-cf46-553f-b9b9-ac8ac90134c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Couinaud classification divides the liver into how many segments?', '["6 segments","4 segments","12 segments","10 segments","8 segments"]'::jsonb, '8 segments', 'The Couinaud classification divides the liver into 8 functionally independent segments (I–VIII), each with its own portal pedicle and hepatic venous drainage. Counts of 4, 6, 10, or 12 reflect other surgical or anatomical schemes rather than the Couinaud system.'),
('44a1d1d0-a616-5c20-af5f-b8bb60d36804'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right hepatic vein runs between?', '["Right and left lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Portal vein branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caudate and quadrate lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right anterior and posterior sectors of the liver"]'::jsonb, 'Right anterior and posterior sectors of the liver', 'The right hepatic vein runs in the right intersegmental plane between the right anterior (V, VIII) and right posterior (VI, VII) sectors. The middle and left hepatic veins course in the principal plane and left sectoral plane, respectively.'),
('8072f9b2-3898-5f2e-b6a0-19fc6f897d16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The middle hepatic vein runs between?', '["Left and quadrate lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Right and left lobes of the liver (in the principal plane)","All of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Right and caudate lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left lobes of the liver (in the principal plane)', 'The middle hepatic vein runs in the principal (Cantlie) plane, separating the functional right and left lobes of the liver. The right and left hepatic veins travel in the sectoral planes rather than this central division.'),
('41f01e5c-85d3-582b-81a8-4022e11da6ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The left hepatic vein runs between?', '["None of the above","Left lateral and left medial sectors","Rarely","Right and left lobes","Caudate and quadrate lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Left lateral and left medial sectors', 'The left hepatic vein runs in the left intersegmental plane separating the left lateral sector (segments II, III) from the left medial sector (segment IV). This plane is distinct from the principal plane occupied by the middle hepatic vein.'),
('a0c2799f-76c0-57db-ab72-f9066996ec1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The caudate lobe of the liver drains via?', '["Left hepatic vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Short hepatic veins directly into the IVC","Portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle hepatic vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right hepatic vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short hepatic veins directly into the IVC', 'The caudate lobe (segment I) drains directly into the inferior vena cava through short hepatic veins rather than via the three major hepatic veins. This independent drainage explains caudate hypertrophy in Budd-Chiari syndrome when the main hepatic veins are occluded.'),
('9b13c410-2fd6-5882-bd8f-b602d6c3b56b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas lies at which vertebral level?', '["T12-L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 (transpyloric plane)","L4-L5 mediated by specific receptor activation","L2-L3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L3-L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 (transpyloric plane)', 'The pancreas lies retroperitoneally across the transpyloric plane at the L1–L2 vertebral level, with the head in the C-loop of the duodenum and the tail reaching the splenic hilum. The other listed levels lie lower than the pancreas.'),
('644ad48c-8d78-5d45-bbcb-5276ed13c0ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen lies at which vertebral level?', '["T6-T8 resulting in decreased functional activity","T9-T11 (along ribs 9-11)","L1-L2 mediated by specific receptor activation","T12-L1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L3-L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T9-T11 (along ribs 9-11)', 'The spleen sits in the left hypochondrium at the T9–T11 vertebral level, protected laterally by the 9th through 11th ribs. The lower lumbar levels listed are too caudal for the spleen.'),
('de708543-0944-52a1-941b-9ef551fe909d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The tail of the pancreas is in contact with which organ?', '["Duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","Spleen (via splenorenal ligament)","Kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gallbladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spleen (via splenorenal ligament)', 'The tail of the pancreas lies within the splenorenal ligament and reaches the splenic hilum, bringing it into direct contact with the spleen. The duodenum relates to the pancreatic head, while the kidney lies posterior and the liver and gallbladder lie in the right upper abdomen.'),
('565b9bcf-a814-5dde-b416-0007e26d4ece'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The uncinate process of the pancreas lies behind?', '["Portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Duodenum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mesenteric vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric vessels', 'The uncinate process hooks posteriorly behind the superior mesenteric vein and artery, a relationship that guides surgical dissection in a Whipple procedure. The aorta and IVC lie farther posterior and are not directly traversed by the uncinate process.'),
('943e72c3-827b-5a65-8782-00138a35bd4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Which part of the pancreas is most commonly injured in abdominal trauma?', '["Body (overlies the vertebral column L1-L2)","Tail accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head mediated by specific receptor activation","Uncinate process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Body (overlies the vertebral column L1-L2)', 'The pancreatic body is the most commonly injured part in blunt trauma because it is crushed against the L1–L2 vertebral column over which it lies. The head is shielded by the duodenum and the tail by adjacent organs.'),
('a207585b-fcc3-5ee5-8986-2f35f94db1c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder is supplied by which artery?', '["Celiac trunk","Common hepatic artery","Cystic artery (from right hepatic)","Left hepatic artery","Gastroduodenal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic artery (from right hepatic)', 'The gallbladder receives its arterial supply from the cystic artery, most commonly a branch of the right hepatic artery that lies within Calot''s triangle. Direct branches of the celiac, common hepatic, or gastroduodenal arteries are uncommon variants rather than the standard supply.'),
('b3daa644-73a0-5bd5-999b-139d50065006'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Which of the following is true about the liver/gallbladder/pancreas/spleen?', '["The gallbladder has four parts","The spleen is a lymphoid organ","The liver has both portal and arterial blood supplies","All of the above","The pancreas is both exocrine and endocrine"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Every listed statement is accurate: the gallbladder has four parts, the spleen is a lymphoid organ, the liver receives both portal and arterial blood, and the pancreas is both exocrine and endocrine. Because each component is true, the comprehensive choice is correct.'),
('8ca0903f-e9f8-5f55-bcbe-a58d5960f29c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'What are the anatomical lobes of the liver?', '["Right, left, caudate","Right, left, caudate, quadrate","Only right","Right, left, quadrate","Right and left only"]'::jsonb, 'Right, left, caudate, quadrate', 'The anatomical lobes of the liver are the right, left, caudate, and quadrate lobes, demarcated by surface fissures and ligaments. Any option omitting the caudate or quadrate lobe is an incomplete list.'),
('44d465ab-1657-504f-9785-947a9ed3c0ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'What are the parts of the gallbladder?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Head, body, tail accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann), neck","Fundus, body, neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardia, fundus, body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann), neck', 'The gallbladder consists of the fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), and neck, which narrows into the cystic duct. ''Head, body, tail'' describes the pancreas, and ''fundus, body, neck'' omits the infundibulum.'),
('d71776da-7c67-5099-9b79-8551716bb68f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'What is Calot triangle?', '["Liver, stomach, duodenum","CBD, portal vein, hepatic artery","None","Common hepatic duct, cystic duct, cystic artery","Gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic duct, cystic duct, cystic artery', 'Calot''s triangle is bounded by the common hepatic duct medially, the cystic duct inferiorly, and the cystic artery superiorly (with the liver above). It is dissected during cholecystectomy to identify the cystic artery before ligation.'),
('aa79a3cd-f21d-5456-ac92-e7c7399e275e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'What are the parts of the pancreas?', '["Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head, neck, body only mediated by specific receptor activation","Head, body, tail only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fundus, body, tail leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail', 'The pancreas comprises the head, uncinate process, neck, body, and tail. The uncinate process is a distinct hook-shaped part passing behind the superior mesenteric vessels, so lists omitting it are incomplete.'),
('b4f2a1bf-f25b-5781-8fcb-af3de398db58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The porta hepatis contains:', '["Hepatic veins resulting in decreased functional activity","Portal vein, hepatic artery, common hepatic duct","Aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Celiac trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein, hepatic artery, common hepatic duct', 'The porta hepatis transmits the portal vein, hepatic artery proper, and common hepatic duct enclosed within the hepatoduodenal ligament. Hepatic veins and the IVC lie posterior to the liver rather than inside the porta hepatis.'),
('86ffb750-4d44-5bc1-af8a-b4581cf55066'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Liver blood supply is:', '["100% hepatic artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","100% portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","50% each without affecting the primary tissue pathway","25% hepatic artery, 75% portal vein"]'::jsonb, '25% hepatic artery, 75% portal vein', 'The liver receives approximately 25% of its blood from the hepatic artery proper and 75% from the portal vein. Single-vessel or equal-split options misstate this dual arrangement.'),
('4d6e943d-f94b-525c-9dfb-a785f669c1b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Hepatic veins drain into:', '["IVC (at upper border of liver)","Portal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Azygos without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IVC (at upper border of liver)', 'The right, middle, and left hepatic veins empty deoxygenated blood into the inferior vena cava at the upper border of the liver, just below the diaphragm. The portal vein supplies blood to the liver, so it is not a drainage target.'),
('d67cb96b-f1dd-5da7-a0d5-1f032f01c31e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Biliary drainage begins with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile canaliculi to interlobular ducts to hepatic ducts","Duodenum directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cystic duct directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bile canaliculi to interlobular ducts to hepatic ducts', 'Bile drains from hepatocytes into bile canaliculi, then interlobular bile ducts, and onward through right and left hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct. Pancreatic, duodenal, and cystic structures are not the first segment of hepatic biliary drainage.'),
('5fc92016-7a87-593c-be71-c8f06493166f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Gallbladder parts:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardia, fundus, body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head, body, tail mediated by specific receptor activation","Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), neck","Fundus, body, neck only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), neck', 'The gallbladder consists of the fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), and neck that narrows into the cystic duct. Pancreatic and gastric part names (head/body/tail, cardia) do not apply to the gallbladder.'),
('d4156aeb-3021-5cfb-91fb-4fb08f8ac1f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Cystic artery most commonly arises from:', '["Right hepatic artery","Common hepatic","Gastroduodenal","Celiac trunk","Left hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Right hepatic artery', 'The cystic artery most commonly arises from the right hepatic artery within Calot''s triangle. Variants from the common hepatic, gastroduodenal, celiac, or left hepatic arteries are less frequent anatomic patterns.'),
('3d1264bb-492e-5c6d-8fc4-1ab8c503e754'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreas parts:', '["Head, body, tail only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail","Fundus, body, tail without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head, neck, body only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail', 'The pancreas has five parts: the head within the duodenal C-loop, the uncinate process hooking behind the superior mesenteric vessels, the neck, the body crossing the L1 vertebra, and the tail reaching the splenic hilum. Options listing only three parts omit the uncinate process and neck, and ''Fundus, body, tail'' describes the stomach, not the pancreas.'),
('323182bb-2ead-5c73-b8d8-603e3d8e9587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Main pancreatic duct (Wirsung) joins CBD to form:', '["Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)","Accessory pancreatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic duct mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cystic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)', 'The main pancreatic duct (Wirsung) unites with the common bile duct within the duodenal wall to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla, which opens at the major duodenal papilla. The accessory duct, hepatic duct, and cystic duct are separate structures that do not take part in this union.'),
('74b8f48b-90c3-5c69-a88e-73ba6dbaf059'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini) opens at:', '["Duodenojejunal flexure","Major duodenal papilla","Pylorus","Minor duodenal papilla","None"]'::jsonb, 'Minor duodenal papilla', 'The accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini) drains the upper part of the pancreatic head and opens at the minor duodenal papilla, about 2 cm proximal to the major papilla. The major papilla receives the hepatopancreatic ampulla rather than the accessory duct.'),
('d6b35bda-e2f1-56c2-b619-50663916a4aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreas is related posteriorly to:', '["Spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Gallbladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC, aorta, left renal vessels, splenic vein","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IVC, aorta, left renal vessels, splenic vein', 'The retroperitoneal pancreas lies anterior to the IVC, abdominal aorta, left renal vessels, and splenic vein. The spleen, gallbladder, stomach, and liver are anterior or neighboring organs rather than posterior relations.'),
('da5272a3-3dde-5ab2-bd77-a166c261a9e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Spleen is located in:', '["Central resulting in decreased functional activity","Left upper quadrant (left hypochondrium)","Left lower mediated by specific receptor activation","Right lower without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right upper quadrant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left upper quadrant (left hypochondrium)', 'The spleen occupies the left hypochondrium, protected by the 9th to 11th ribs, between the stomach and the diaphragm. It does not lie in the right upper quadrant or in the lower quadrants.'),
('2c4d0c17-f898-50ba-9b0a-52bc8f728ac3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Spleen surfaces:', '["Diaphragmatic (superolateral) and visceral (inferomedial)","Superior and inferior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior and posterior mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial and lateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragmatic (superolateral) and visceral (inferomedial)', 'The spleen has a smooth diaphragmatic (superolateral) surface facing the diaphragm and a visceral (inferomedial) surface bearing gastric, renal, and colic impressions. Simple directional terms such as superior/inferior or anterior/posterior do not name the two splenic surfaces.'),
('019f4252-e6ba-5359-852f-957e17d4b0e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Splenic hilum contains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreatic ducts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic artery, vein, lymphatics, nerves","Hepatic ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein and hepatic artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery, vein, lymphatics, nerves', 'The splenic hilum transmits the splenic artery, splenic vein, lymphatics, and autonomic nerve fibers. Pancreatic or hepatic ductal structures and the portal vein with hepatic artery do not pass through the splenic hilum.'),
('07c9056f-e5f4-5308-a5c6-bc32cf420222'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Spleen is supplied by:', '["Common hepatic resulting in decreased functional activity","SMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic artery (largest branch of celiac trunk)","Renal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left gastric leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery (largest branch of celiac trunk)', 'The splenic artery, the largest branch of the celiac trunk, runs a tortuous course along the superior border of the pancreas to reach the hilum. The common hepatic, SMA, renal, and left gastric arteries do not supply the spleen.'),
('43fd58d8-36bc-50f6-b208-7d4c0d0743f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Splenic vein drains into:', '["Azygos resulting in decreased functional activity","Left renal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal vein (after joining SMV)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein (after joining SMV)', 'The splenic vein runs posterior to the body of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein behind the neck of the pancreas. It does not drain into the IVC, azygos, or left renal vein.'),
('b359ef03-041a-5ca2-aa05-dacb8a85576a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Splenic relations:', '["Liver, gallbladder, duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","Right kidney, adrenal, colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heart, lung mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach, left kidney, diaphragm, splenic flexure, pancreas tail","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach, left kidney, diaphragm, splenic flexure, pancreas tail', 'The spleen contacts the stomach, left kidney, diaphragm, splenic flexure of the colon, and tail of the pancreas. It has no direct relations with the liver, gallbladder, duodenum, right kidney, heart, or lung.'),
('5468f9e6-43b1-52db-bf9d-ec728e25b464'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Gallbladder fundus is at:', '["ASIS resulting in decreased functional activity","Xiphoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Umbilicus mediated by specific receptor activation","Right midclavicular line + 9th costal cartilage","L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right midclavicular line + 9th costal cartilage', 'The gallbladder fundus projects to the surface at the junction of the right midclavicular line and the 9th costal cartilage, a key surface marking for clinical examination. The ASIS, xiphoid, umbilicus, and L1 are not its surface projection.'),
('0ae51c9a-c902-5dc4-bcaa-9eec2987f28b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Common hepatic duct is formed by:', '["Right and left hepatic ducts","Left hepatic and cystic","Cystic and hepatic ducts","None","Right hepatic and cystic"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left hepatic ducts', 'The right and left hepatic ducts unite at the porta hepatis to form the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct joins this duct later, and the pancreatic duct plays no part in its formation.'),
('65745bd7-eacc-53ae-b5b2-727ae4b4afa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Common bile duct is formed by:', '["Hepatic and pancreatic","Common hepatic duct and cystic duct","None","Cystic and pancreatic","Right and left hepatic ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic duct and cystic duct', 'The common bile duct begins where the cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct. It is not formed from the hepatic or pancreatic ducts alone, nor from the two hepatic ducts alone.'),
('ea53a692-e086-5959-9bbd-6dc26aa8fe42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'CBD is divided into:', '["Preduodenal, postduodenal, jejunal resulting in decreased functional activity","Supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, intraduodenal","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatic, cystic, pancreatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, intraduodenal', 'The common bile duct is divided into four parts by its course relative to the duodenum: supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, and intraduodenal. The other listed groupings do not match the recognized anatomic divisions.'),
('2cbe5096-3889-5d6d-b938-ad8aad596b86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Sphincter of Oddi is at:', '["Cystic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ampulla of Vater in the duodenal wall","Pylorus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of Vater in the duodenal wall', 'The sphincter of Oddi is a smooth muscle sphincter surrounding the ampulla of Vater within the duodenal wall, controlling the release of bile and pancreatic juice. It is not located at the cystic duct, hepatic duct, or pylorus.'),
('7d2a1b76-86f3-552d-8b7b-e096ed95b381'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Liver lymphatics drain to:', '["Celiac, hepatic, and phrenic nodes","Para-aortic only","Superficial inguinal","Axillary","Internal iliac"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac, hepatic, and phrenic nodes', 'Liver lymphatics drain mainly to hepatic and celiac nodes, while superficial channels along the bare area reach phrenic nodes. They do not drain to inguinal, axillary, or internal iliac nodes, nor to para-aortic nodes as the primary route.'),
('327c3985-810c-5dbe-98a2-b77b7131ba8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Gallbladder lymphatics drain to:', '["Axillary resulting in decreased functional activity","Para-aortic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Cystic -> hepatic -> celiac nodes","Superficial inguinal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic -> hepatic -> celiac nodes', 'Gallbladder lymph drains sequentially through cystic nodes, then hepatic nodes, and finally celiac nodes. Inguinal, axillary, and para-aortic nodes are not part of its normal pathway.'),
('58779f3e-8da0-53bd-a088-0ddd05b4cdb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreas lymphatics drain to:', '["Pancreaticosplenic, pancreaticoduodenal, celiac nodes","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Para-aortic only mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial inguinal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreaticosplenic, pancreaticoduodenal, celiac nodes', 'Pancreatic lymph drains through pancreaticosplenic and pancreaticoduodenal nodes and ultimately reaches the celiac nodes. Superficial inguinal, axillary, and para-aortic-only drainage do not apply.'),
('10804ff1-4a78-507c-b2f8-9ac6323e7d14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Spleen lymphatics drain to:', '["Axillary resulting in decreased functional activity","Para-aortic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreaticosplenic (along splenic artery) to celiac nodes","Superficial inguinal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreaticosplenic (along splenic artery) to celiac nodes', 'Splenic lymphatics travel along the splenic artery to pancreaticosplenic nodes and then drain to the celiac nodes. Inguinal, axillary, and para-aortic routes are not splenic drainage pathways.'),
('1a10b0f9-8c46-5df3-89f9-33551bfc4355'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Porta hepatis is on:', '["Visceral (inferior) surface of liver","Diaphragmatic surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral (inferior) surface of liver', 'The porta hepatis is the hilum on the visceral (inferior) surface of the liver through which the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile ducts enter or leave. It is not found on the diaphragmatic, anterior, or posterior surfaces.'),
('8ce9208e-38a4-5cb8-800f-0ed33af607d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Couinaud classification divides liver into:', '["6","8 segments","10","4","12"]'::jsonb, '8 segments', 'The Couinaud classification divides the liver into 8 functionally independent segments, each with its own portal triad supply and hepatic venous drainage. Values of 4, 6, 10, and 12 do not reflect this scheme.'),
('ea7deadf-98da-57bc-b91a-fea3b94b3625'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Right hepatic vein runs between:', '["Right anterior and posterior sectors","Caudate and quadrate","Portal branches","Right and left lobes","None"]'::jsonb, 'Right anterior and posterior sectors', 'The right hepatic vein runs in the right intersectoral fissure separating the right anterior (segments V, VIII) and right posterior (segments VI, VII) sectors. It does not separate the lobes, caudate, or quadrate regions.'),
('9e4f5711-f5e4-5aa0-907a-dd0d69e5711d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Middle hepatic vein runs between:', '["Left and quadrate resulting in decreased functional activity","Right and left lobes (principal plane)","Right and caudate mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left lobes (principal plane)', 'The middle hepatic vein courses in the principal plane (line of Cantlie), marking the boundary between the functional right and left lobes. It does not separate the caudate, quadrate, or all sectors.'),
('7b499142-4779-5c3d-9524-86e5f3aca4c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Left hepatic vein runs between:', '["None","Caudate and quadrate","None of the above","Left lateral and left medial sectors","Right and left lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Left lateral and left medial sectors', 'The left hepatic vein runs in the left intersectoral fissure dividing the left lateral sector (segments II, III) from the left medial sector (segment IV). It does not separate the right and left lobes or the caudate and quadrate lobes.'),
('e5ecdc5d-2111-5f28-b3fa-78be5a9f6b94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Caudate lobe drains via:', '["Portal vein","Left hepatic vein","Middle hepatic vein","Right hepatic vein","Short hepatic veins directly into IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Short hepatic veins directly into IVC', 'The caudate lobe empties through short hepatic veins directly into the inferior vena cava, independent of the three main hepatic veins. This separate drainage underlies the caudate sparing seen in Budd-Chiari syndrome.'),
('70783bbc-732a-599b-a1e0-2e9d0569e345'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreas lies at vertebral level:', '["T12-L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","L2-L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1-L2 (transpyloric plane)","L3-L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L4-L5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 (transpyloric plane)', 'The pancreas lies retroperitoneally at the L1-L2 vertebral level, roughly along the transpyloric plane. T12-L1 is too high, and the lower lumbar levels are too low.'),
('8f549790-d0f9-52b8-aebf-a389bce8e481'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Spleen lies at vertebral level:', '["T6-T8 resulting in decreased functional activity","T9-T11 (along ribs 9-11)","T12-L1 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3-L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1-L2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T9-T11 (along ribs 9-11)', 'The spleen lies at the T9-T11 vertebral level, shielded by the 9th to 11th ribs. T6-T8 places it too high, and the lumbar levels are too low.'),
('a191cbef-d782-5695-b4d3-8d417869019e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Tail of pancreas contacts:', '["Duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","Spleen (via splenorenal ligament)","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Gallbladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spleen (via splenorenal ligament)', 'The pancreatic tail extends within the splenorenal ligament to reach the splenic hilum. It does not directly contact the duodenum, liver, gallbladder, or kidney.'),
('bad0d7c4-702b-5ea8-ad34-9b3eb86f5e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Uncinate process of pancreas lies behind:', '["Aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior mesenteric vessels","Duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric vessels', 'The uncinate process hooks posteriorly behind the superior mesenteric vein and artery, an important landmark in pancreaticoduodenectomy. It does not lie behind the aorta, IVC, portal vein, or duodenum.'),
('a8b4e731-1a4d-5ad0-96c5-8aef266d4428'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Most commonly injured part of pancreas in blunt trauma:', '["Body (overlies vertebral column L1-L2)","Uncinate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tail mediated by specific receptor activation","Head without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Body (overlies vertebral column L1-L2)', 'The pancreatic body lies directly anterior to the L1-L2 vertebral column, so blunt force compresses it against the spine, making it the most commonly injured part. The head, neck, uncinate, and tail are less exposed to such compression.'),
('489b025c-d490-56ad-a0ff-b1a4b58ab86f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Gallbladder is supplied by:', '["Left hepatic resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastroduodenal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common hepatic mediated by specific receptor activation","Celiac trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cystic artery (from right hepatic)"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic artery (from right hepatic)', 'The cystic artery, most commonly arising from the right hepatic artery, supplies the gallbladder. The left hepatic, gastroduodenal, and common hepatic arteries and the celiac trunk do not provide its direct supply.'),
('63656164-4320-55ec-b426-bd896fa2b8e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Spleen develops from:', '["Dorsal mesogastrium","Foregut","Hindgut","Midgut","Ventral mesogastrium"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal mesogastrium', 'The spleen develops from a mesenchymal condensation within the dorsal mesogastrium during the fifth week. It does not arise from the foregut, midgut, hindgut, or ventral mesogastrium.'),
('469fc46a-7979-52d7-b18c-c2c3f605fbbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Gallbladder lies on visceral surface of liver between:', '["Right and left","Caudate and quadrate","Left and caudate","None","Right and quadrate lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Right and quadrate lobes', 'The gallbladder lies in the fossa on the visceral liver surface between the right lobe and the quadrate lobe. The caudate lobe and left lobe do not border the gallbladder fossa.'),
('2ee5b871-42da-57ad-aceb-dc6f1b7d0cc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Hepatogastric ligament contains:', '["CBD resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left gastric vessels and lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric vessels and lymphatics', 'The hepatogastric ligament, the left portion of the lesser omentum, attaches the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and transmits the left gastric vessels and lymphatics. The CBD, hepatic artery, and portal vein lie within the hepatoduodenal ligament.'),
('e07073be-4b27-5c45-944d-bf76c1e3ce24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Hepatoduodenal ligament is part of:', '["Lesser omentum","Falciform ligament","None","Greater omentum","Coronary ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser omentum', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament forms the free edge of the lesser omentum and encloses the portal triad (portal vein, hepatic artery, CBD). It is not part of the falciform, coronary, or greater omentum.'),
('168d86ed-474a-52b6-8d7c-d049d5780264'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreas has which type of secretion?', '["Only endocrine resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Both exocrine (enzymes) and endocrine (insulin, glucagon)","Only exocrine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both exocrine (enzymes) and endocrine (insulin, glucagon)', 'The pancreas is both exocrine, with acini secreting digestive enzymes into ducts, and endocrine, with islets of Langerhans secreting insulin and glucagon. It is not exclusively one or the other.'),
('b81f80d0-2d83-51c5-8e53-3cd729ea47bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Islets of Langerhans are most numerous in:', '["Tail of pancreas","Head","Body","Uncinate","Neck"]'::jsonb, 'Tail of pancreas', 'Islets of Langerhans are most densely concentrated in the tail of the pancreas, a distribution relevant to distal pancreatectomy planning. They are less numerous in the head, body, neck, and uncinate process.'),
('4fd45eda-1023-5c83-8ff8-a1aca62cf07c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Gallbladder is related to which duodenal part?', '["Third part","Fourth part","None","Second part","First part (superior)"]'::jsonb, 'First part (superior)', 'The gallbladder fundus and body lie adjacent to the first (superior) part of the duodenum. The second, third, and fourth parts are not the principal duodenal relations of the gallbladder.'),
('f11b5583-32c9-569a-8d61-b39457c25a51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Quadrate lobe of liver is bounded by:', '["Falciform, left triangular resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC, ligamentum venosum, porta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Porta hepatis, gallbladder fossa, ligamentum teres"]'::jsonb, 'Porta hepatis, gallbladder fossa, ligamentum teres', 'The quadrate lobe is bounded by the porta hepatis posteriorly, the gallbladder fossa laterally, and the fissure for the ligamentum teres medially. The falciform and triangular ligaments and the IVC do not form its borders.'),
('cb222d98-b5ca-5d7f-bfd6-b556e5a037dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Caudate lobe of liver is bounded by:', '["IVC, ligamentum venosum, porta hepatis","All","None","Gallbladder, falciform, ligamentum teres","Right triangular, coronary"]'::jsonb, 'IVC, ligamentum venosum, porta hepatis', 'The caudate lobe is bounded on the right by the IVC, on the left by the fissure for the ligamentum venosum, and inferiorly by the porta hepatis. The gallbladder, falciform, ligamentum teres, and triangular ligament are not its borders.'),
('acf4ad35-c865-5c5f-938c-8f8ca240bcb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Hepatic artery proper divides into:', '["All","Right and left hepatic arteries","None","Cystic and hepatic","Common hepatic and gastroduodenal"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left hepatic arteries', 'The hepatic artery proper divides into the right and left hepatic arteries supplying the corresponding liver lobes. The gastroduodenal artery arises earlier from the common hepatic artery, and the cystic artery is a branch of the right hepatic artery.'),
('c44ba268-759f-505a-847f-67dfeff8d8c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right hepatic artery gives:', '["Cystic artery to the gallbladder","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Left gastric without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastroduodenal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic artery to the gallbladder', 'The right hepatic artery typically gives off the cystic artery within Calot''s triangle, which supplies the gallbladder. The left gastric and gastroduodenal arteries are not related to the right hepatic artery''s supply of the gallbladder.'),
('26d7b2bc-803a-5a16-acdc-f91fee91505a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common hepatic artery is a branch of:', '["Splenic","Left gastric","Celiac trunk","SMA","Aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac trunk', 'The celiac trunk gives three branches: the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries. The common hepatic artery does not arise from the SMA, aorta, left gastric artery, or splenic artery.'),
('7854fe25-60a4-5063-8e0c-d45b407a416c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The proper hepatic artery is the continuation of:', '["Common hepatic artery (after gastroduodenal branch)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Celiac trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Left hepatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right hepatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic artery (after gastroduodenal branch)', 'The common hepatic artery continues as the hepatic artery proper after giving off the gastroduodenal artery. It is not a direct branch of the celiac trunk nor a branch of the right or left hepatic arteries.'),
('d97a7ab5-c459-5f87-b8d1-c6965667d1d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder is located on the visceral surface of the liver:', '["In the gallbladder fossa","In the porta hepatis","None","In the quadrate lobe","In the caudate lobe"]'::jsonb, 'In the gallbladder fossa', 'The gallbladder lies in the gallbladder fossa, a depression on the visceral surface of the liver. It is not situated within the porta hepatis, quadrate lobe, or caudate lobe.'),
('eb51d1f1-8944-554f-8582-875b192feb72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Hartmann pouch (infundibulum) of the gallbladder is:', '["The neck of the gallbladder resulting in decreased functional activity","A dilated area where gallstones commonly lodge","The body mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fundus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A dilated area where gallstones commonly lodge', 'Hartmann pouch is a dilated infundibulum at the gallbladder neck where gallstones frequently become impacted, predisposing to obstruction and cholecystitis. It is not simply the neck, body, or fundus.'),
('d27cf8e8-a42b-5383-a104-6f3a511b0c6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cystic duct is about:', '["None","5 cm","3-4 cm long","1 cm","10 cm"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm long', 'The cystic duct measures approximately 3-4 cm and bears the spiral valves of Heister. Lengths of 1 cm, 5 cm, and 10 cm are inaccurate.'),
('ad41e92f-c09c-512c-b1a0-a83d4ab6e4fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spiral valves of the cystic duct are:', '["Haustra","None","Valves of Kerckring","Valves of Heister","Taeniae"]'::jsonb, 'Valves of Heister', 'The spiral mucosal folds of the cystic duct are called the valves of Heister, which help regulate bile flow. Haustra and taeniae belong to the colon, and valves of Kerckring are folds of the small intestinal mucosa.'),
('53c313be-c415-5b3f-ad0f-c1b18cc876b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common hepatic duct is about:', '["None","3-4 cm long","1 cm","5 cm","10 cm"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm long', 'The common hepatic duct is about 3-4 cm long, extending from the union of the right and left hepatic ducts to its junction with the cystic duct. Lengths of 1 cm, 5 cm, and 10 cm are inaccurate.'),
('e6b76686-d81a-5878-b25e-f9b8d0b0b41a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common bile duct is about:', '["15 cm","10-12 cm","3-4 cm","7-9 cm long","None"]'::jsonb, '7-9 cm long', 'The common bile duct is approximately 7-9 cm long, running from the junction of the cystic and common hepatic ducts to the ampulla of Vater. Lengths of 3-4, 10-12, and 15 cm are incorrect.'),
('bcc31c3b-4ca7-54fb-98f0-eae13e68bb1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The diameter of the CBD is normally:', '["4-6 mm","15 mm","10 mm","None","20 mm"]'::jsonb, '4-6 mm', 'The normal common bile duct measures 4-6 mm on imaging. Values of 10-15 mm or more reflect abnormal dilation, typically from obstruction or advanced age.'),
('1164d200-aba9-58ee-9939-5668a1d1e130'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'CBD dilation of >10 mm suggests:', '["Gallstones only","All","None","Biliary obstruction","Normal variant"]'::jsonb, 'Biliary obstruction', 'A common bile duct wider than 10 mm indicates significant biliary obstruction, most often from choledocholithiasis or peripapillary tumors. Stone disease is only one of several causes, so the broader category of obstruction is correct.'),
('5a0e0aac-bd9e-5cc7-8d3d-5d2087c065ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The intrapancreatic part of the CBD is:', '["The retroduodenal part resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The intraduodenal part mediated by specific receptor activation","The supraduodenal part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The portion passing through the head of the pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'The portion passing through the head of the pancreas', 'The intrapancreatic segment is the part of the CBD that runs through the head of the pancreas before entering the duodenal wall. Its position explains why pancreatic head masses readily cause obstructive jaundice.'),
('9355fcb3-c66f-5b96-b8a8-f157c87d6435'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreatic duct (Wirsung) runs:', '["Only in the tail resulting in decreased functional activity","The length of the pancreas from tail to head","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in the head without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in the body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The length of the pancreas from tail to head', 'The main pancreatic duct courses the entire gland from tail to head, collecting secretions along the way. It terminates at the ampulla of Vater, so it is not confined to a single region of the pancreas.'),
('abd65753-fcb6-54f4-a38e-1c4a4b0eab46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The CBD and pancreatic duct join:', '["At the minor papilla resulting in decreased functional activity","In the head of the pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the major papilla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)', 'The common bile duct and main pancreatic duct unite at the hepatopancreatic ampulla within the duodenal wall, an opening controlled by the sphincter of Oddi. The minor papilla drains only the accessory duct.'),
('9554af32-88c1-55f0-8a3c-60442053c160'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreas divisum occurs when:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The pancreas is absent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts fail to fuse","The CBD is duplicated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts fail to fuse', 'Pancreas divisum results from failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds and their ducts during development. It is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly and can predispose to recurrent pancreatitis.'),
('e4e8e501-c9d7-5e33-a7e6-ee33d81229a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'In pancreas divisum, most of the pancreas drains via:', '["The main pancreatic duct (Wirsung) through the major papilla resulting in decreased functional activity","Both accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini) through the minor papilla"]'::jsonb, 'The accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini) through the minor papilla', 'Because the ventral and dorsal ducts do not fuse, most of the gland drains through the dominant dorsal duct (Santorini) into the minor duodenal papilla. The narrow minor papilla outflow may favor duct stasis and pancreatitis.'),
('4d0f51ce-2d19-5f6a-8ee0-992eb493cc57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The liver is divided into left and right lobes by:', '["The ligamentum teres resulting in decreased functional activity","The round ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The coronary ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The falciform ligament (anatomically) / Cantlie line (functionally)"]'::jsonb, 'The falciform ligament (anatomically) / Cantlie line (functionally)', 'The falciform ligament marks the anatomical division on the surface, while the Cantlie line defines the functional boundary between right and left portal vascular territories. The coronary ligament attaches the bare area and does not divide lobes.'),
('6ec13f49-86c4-51bb-b6b7-7bae36dbf011'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Cantlie line runs from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Gallbladder fossa to the IVC (divides functional right and left lobes)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The falciform ligament to the IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ligamentum teres to the diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gallbladder fossa to the IVC (divides functional right and left lobes)', 'The Cantlie line extends from the gallbladder fossa to the inferior vena cava, marking the plane between functional right and left liver lobes. It corresponds to the middle hepatic vein course and is a key reference in liver surgery.'),
('de29bc8b-8aea-5e11-9309-8bea05cccaac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The caudate lobe of the liver lies:', '["In the left lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior to the porta hepatis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior to the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior to the porta hepatis, between the IVC and ligamentum venosum"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the porta hepatis, between the IVC and ligamentum venosum', 'The caudate lobe (segment I) sits on the posterior surface behind the porta hepatis, bounded by the IVC on the right and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum on the left. It drains independently into the IVC via short hepatic veins.'),
('e28c6c58-1adc-5b36-9a4d-baeb633f4844'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The quadrate lobe of the liver is:', '["On the inferior surface, between the gallbladder and falciform ligament","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the IVC and porta hepatis mediated by specific receptor activation","On the superior surface without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the caudate lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the inferior surface, between the gallbladder and falciform ligament', 'The quadrate lobe lies on the visceral surface bounded by the gallbladder fossa on the right and the ligamentum teres-falciform ligament on the left. It is part of the functional left lobe despite surface anatomy.'),
('11091089-5473-5c77-85aa-6984c8297393'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Bare area of the liver is:', '["An area on the diaphragmatic surface not covered by peritoneum","The area covered by the gallbladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The area near the porta hepatis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An area on the diaphragmatic surface not covered by peritoneum', 'The bare area is the posterior diaphragmatic zone of the liver that lacks peritoneal covering, bounded by the coronary ligaments. Absent peritoneum allows direct contact with the diaphragm and a potential route for infection spread.'),
('12f08c1a-8462-5426-8d38-0e37aa869a78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Bare area of the liver is in contact with:', '["The diaphragm","The stomach","The kidney","The duodenum","The colon"]'::jsonb, 'The diaphragm', 'The bare area apposes the diaphragm directly through intervening areolar tissue. Hepatic abscess or infection can therefore extend through this unperitonealized zone into the thoracic cavity.'),
('7ed7acfc-37db-5ddd-8d66-7b4671082607'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right and left hepatic ducts unite to form:', '["The common bile duct","The common hepatic duct","None","The cystic duct","The pancreatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'The common hepatic duct', 'The right and left hepatic ducts join at the porta hepatis to form the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct then joins it to create the common bile duct.'),
('6ca55c1a-29e8-5819-bec1-8017db60bf53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common hepatic duct joins the cystic duct to form:', '["The ampulla of Vater","The cystic duct","None","The pancreatic duct","The common bile duct (CBD)"]'::jsonb, 'The common bile duct (CBD)', 'The union of the common hepatic duct and cystic duct forms the common bile duct, which conveys bile toward the duodenum. The ampulla of Vater is a later junction with the pancreatic duct within the duodenal wall.'),
('a842f181-67dc-5045-9105-4c46b706ac2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The CBD lies in:', '["The free edge of the lesser omentum (anteriorly)","The gastrocolic ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gastrohepatic ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The splenorenal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The free edge of the lesser omentum (anteriorly)', 'The CBD runs in the right free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament), lying anterior to the portal vein and to the right of the hepatic artery. This arrangement is why the CBD is clamped first with Pringle-type flow interruption.'),
('add6976a-d227-5213-a87f-5038ae57c0e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The CBD passes behind the:', '["Stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","First part of the duodenum","Pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First part of the duodenum', 'After emerging from the hepatoduodenal ligament, the CBD descends posterior to the first part of the duodenum before entering the head of the pancreas. This relationship is clinically relevant to choledochoduodenal fistula and ERCP anatomy.'),
('abe5ffab-104c-57f5-a6e5-58b88a3430c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The CBD pierces the:', '["Jejunum resulting in decreased functional activity","Posteromedial wall of the second part of the duodenum","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Third part of the duodenum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First part of the duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteromedial wall of the second part of the duodenum', 'The CBD obliquely pierces the posteromedial wall of the second (descending) part of the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla. There it opens together with the main pancreatic duct, the target site of endoscopic cannulation.'),
('b584f151-7e4b-5513-86af-eeba959e73f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ampulla of Vater is formed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Union of the CBD and cystic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Union of the hepatic and cystic ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Union of the CBD and the main pancreatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Union of the CBD and the main pancreatic duct', 'The hepatopancreatic ampulla is created by the convergence of the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct inside the duodenal wall. The CBD and cystic duct do not unite at this site, and the hepatic and cystic ducts form the CBD, not the ampulla.'),
('d0910eef-de15-5531-a03d-cc847ca35636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ampulla of Vater opens on:', '["The pylorus","The duodenal bulb","None","The minor duodenal papilla","The major duodenal papilla"]'::jsonb, 'The major duodenal papilla', 'The ampulla of Vater empties through the major duodenal papilla on the posteromedial wall of the descending duodenum. The minor papilla drains only the accessory pancreatic duct, while the pylorus and duodenal bulb are proximal and unrelated.'),
('d764c4ec-8325-549b-8aa1-2ae047b5fe54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The sphincter of Oddi surrounds:', '["The cystic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The hepatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The terminal part of the CBD and the ampulla of Vater","The pancreatic duct only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal part of the CBD and the ampulla of Vater', 'The sphincter of Oddi is a smooth-muscle sleeve around the terminal CBD and ampulla (with an additional component around the distal pancreatic duct) that regulates flow of bile and pancreatic juice. It does not wrap the cystic or hepatic ducts.'),
('5128a826-e273-5cda-8d25-8c49a1e4a30e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The sphincter of Oddi is located:', '["Within the wall of the duodenum","In the pancreas","At the cystic duct","Outside the duodenal wall","None"]'::jsonb, 'Within the wall of the duodenum', 'The sphincter of Oddi is embedded in the muscular wall of the duodenum at the major papilla. This intramural location allows coordinated contraction that prevents reflux of duodenal contents into the biliary and pancreatic ducts.'),
('4de1c99e-b4ee-5b20-ba90-3e46ef191397'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder is located:', '["Above the diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","On the diaphragmatic surface of the liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind the pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the gallbladder fossa on the visceral surface of the liver"]'::jsonb, 'In the gallbladder fossa on the visceral surface of the liver', 'The gallbladder rests in the fossa on the visceral (inferior) surface of the liver, between the right and quadrate lobes. Its fundus projects toward the tip of the right ninth costal cartilage.'),
('c21eae00-ffc4-52ca-961f-74fe26bb6226'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder has:', '["Fundus and body only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only a fundus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Body and neck only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fundus, body, and neck (in Hartmann pouch)"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, and neck (in Hartmann pouch)', 'The gallbladder comprises the fundus, body, and neck; the neck bears the Hartmann pouch and narrows into the cystic duct. All three parts are present, so descriptions naming only one or two regions are incomplete.'),
('3fe4bd92-0cb4-50c9-8188-4a0abb22c813'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Hartmann pouch is a:', '["Dilated portion of the gallbladder neck (common site for gallstones)","Part of the bile duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of the cystic duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Part of the fundus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated portion of the gallbladder neck (common site for gallstones)', 'The Hartmann pouch is a dilated outpouching of the gallbladder neck adjacent to the cystic duct. Its dependent position makes it a frequent site of stone impaction and a landmark during cholecystectomy.'),
('6846b0f4-4672-5e8d-8c4a-3d2dc1d8ff9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cystic artery is a branch of:', '["The common hepatic artery","The gastroduodenal artery","The left hepatic artery","The superior mesenteric artery","The right hepatic artery (usually)"]'::jsonb, 'The right hepatic artery (usually)', 'In about 70-80% of individuals the cystic artery arises from the right hepatic artery, typically inside Calot''s triangle. It is not a usual branch of the gastroduodenal, SMA, or left hepatic arteries.'),
('921942a0-4087-5cda-926d-ae7d1c7a08f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Calot triangle is important for:', '["Identifying the liver resulting in decreased functional activity","Identifying the cystic artery during cholecystectomy","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Identifying the CBD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Identifying the cystic artery during cholecystectomy', 'Calot''s triangle is bounded by the cystic duct, common hepatic duct, and inferior liver surface, and it contains the cystic artery. Surgeons locate and ligate this vessel within the triangle to control bleeding during cholecystectomy.'),
('fec1259a-dd85-5a7d-923d-9e54bdafc278'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder is supplied by:', '["Portal vein","None","Superior mesenteric artery","Cystic artery and vein","Hepatic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic artery and vein', 'The gallbladder receives arterial blood from the cystic artery (a branch of the right hepatic artery) and drains through the cystic vein into the portal vein. The portal vein and hepatic artery supply the liver, not the gallbladder directly.'),
('c29a92d5-1ea8-50f9-8c07-b8b7ef02c563'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder is innervated by:', '["The lumbar plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","The intercostal nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vagus and sympathetic (celiac plexus)","The phrenic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vagus and sympathetic (celiac plexus)', 'Parasympathetic fibers from the vagus and sympathetic fibers from the celiac plexus innervate the gallbladder and biliary tree. Referred pain from gallstones is carried by sympathetic fibers and often felt in the right upper quadrant and shoulder tip.'),
('c05598ef-90d3-504f-97a7-abe0fa0c40b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Acute cholecystitis is most commonly caused by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction of the cystic duct by a gallstone","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction of the cystic duct by a gallstone', 'Impaction of a stone in the cystic duct causes bile stasis, raised intraluminal pressure, and inflammation characteristic of acute cholecystitis. Trauma, primary tumor, or primary infection are far less common triggers.'),
('1e6ad12a-a540-517e-b14e-89862a43ce2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas is a:', '["Intraperitoneal organ resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic organ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retroperitoneal organ (except the tail)","Pelvic organ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retroperitoneal organ (except the tail)', 'The pancreas is secondarily retroperitoneal, lying behind the peritoneum; only the tail is intraperitoneal within the splenorenal ligament. This retroperitoneal position explains why pancreatic pathology typically presents with back pain and is not palpable.'),
('639715fe-5a9a-5438-8d17-2aaf9ec3125a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas is divided into:', '["Head, body, and tail only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head and tail only mediated by specific receptor activation","Head (including uncinate process), neck, body, and tail","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head (including uncinate process), neck, body, and tail', 'The pancreas has four parts: the head (with the uncinate process), neck, body, and tail. Simplified divisions that omit the neck or uncinate process are incomplete.'),
('8786926d-4a22-597d-9004-78b2b7cf2752'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The uncinate process of the pancreas lies:', '["Posterior to the pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior to the SMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels","In the splenic hilum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels', 'The uncinate process hooks behind the superior mesenteric vessels, which run anterior to it. This relationship makes the uncinate a common site of tumor involvement in pancreatic head cancer.'),
('e9f011ff-8440-51a8-b9f2-3fa977bda93b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The main pancreatic duct (of Wirsung) runs:', '["None","To the fundus of the gallbladder","The length of the pancreas to join the CBD","To the minor duodenal papilla","To the pylorus"]'::jsonb, 'The length of the pancreas to join the CBD', 'The main pancreatic duct traverses the entire gland and joins the CBD at the hepatopancreatic ampulla. It empties at the major papilla, not the minor papilla, and has no connection to the gallbladder or pylorus.'),
('288a1dee-a773-53a4-b213-5f1d7183005c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini) drains:', '["The upper part of the head (opens at the minor duodenal papilla)","The entire pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The tail without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The body of the pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The upper part of the head (opens at the minor duodenal papilla)', 'The accessory pancreatic duct drains the upper portion of the head and usually opens at the minor duodenal papilla about 2 cm proximal to the major papilla. It is a secondary drainage pathway, not the main channel for the whole gland.'),
('2b2dfe99-8121-5e5f-bcf8-46184b46d629'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The major duodenal papilla is located:', '["In the fourth part resulting in decreased functional activity","In the second part of the duodenum, posteromedially","None mediated by specific receptor activation","In the first part of the duodenum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the third part of the duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the second part of the duodenum, posteromedially', 'The major duodenal papilla sits on the posteromedial wall of the second (descending) part of the duodenum. The first, third, and fourth parts house no comparable bile and pancreatic drainage site.'),
('122aa984-8151-5720-bbbb-142d07f7b33f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The minor duodenal papilla is located:', '["Proximal to the major papilla (about 2 cm)","Distal to the major papilla","None","In the first part of the duodenum","In the third part"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal to the major papilla (about 2 cm)', 'The minor duodenal papilla lies roughly 2 cm proximal (oral) to the major papilla on the posteromedial duodenal wall. It receives the accessory pancreatic duct and is therefore proximal to, not distal from, the major papilla.'),
('90d3dcff-b733-5711-bf78-64b556ce81ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Annular pancreas is characterized by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A ring of pancreatic tissue surrounding the second part of the duodenum","Pancreatic tissue around the stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Duplication of the pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ring of pancreatic tissue surrounding the second part of the duodenum', 'Annular pancreas is a congenital malformation in which the ventral pancreatic bud wraps a ring of tissue around the second part of the duodenum. It may compress the duodenum and cause obstruction, especially in neonates.'),
('835a6029-8955-5e35-8b54-3a152687cd27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is located:', '["In the left hypochondrium, between the fundus of the stomach and the diaphragm","In the right hypochondrium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the epigastrium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the left hypochondrium, between the fundus of the stomach and the diaphragm', 'The spleen occupies the left hypochondrium deep to the 9th-11th ribs, wedged between the gastric fundus and the diaphragm. The other listed regions do not correspond to the normal splenic position.'),
('947dd694-856e-51b8-9833-bb303501a52b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is:', '["Subperitoneal resulting in decreased functional activity","Intraperitoneal (covered by peritoneum except at the hilum)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Retroperitoneal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intraperitoneal (covered by peritoneum except at the hilum)', 'The spleen is fully invested by visceral peritoneum except at the hilum, where vessels enter and leave, placing it intraperitoneally. It is not retroperitoneal like the kidneys or pancreas, and subperitoneal is not a recognized description.'),
('6d4ed50e-f038-5e90-8af3-d0bd733f9fe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is related to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The stomach, tail of pancreas, left kidney, left colic flexure, and diaphragm","Only the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The liver and gallbladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The stomach, tail of pancreas, left kidney, left colic flexure, and diaphragm', 'The splenic visceral surface bears impressions from the stomach, tail of pancreas, left kidney, and splenic flexure of the colon, while its diaphragmatic surface contacts the diaphragm. This broad set of relations guides clinical examination and surgery.'),
('efccd6f4-2abd-5bd4-bc2c-5942cdceb516'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic artery arises from:', '["The SMA","The hepatic artery","None","The celiac trunk","The IMA"]'::jsonb, 'The celiac trunk', 'The splenic artery is the largest branch of the celiac trunk, arising from its left side. It runs a tortuous course along the superior border of the pancreas toward the splenic hilum, dividing into its terminal branches.'),
('380791f0-0eb9-5ef7-8567-4cef9d4e3ebe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The tail of the pancreas lies in:', '["The splenorenal ligament","The gastrocolic ligament","The falciform ligament","The lesser omentum","None"]'::jsonb, 'The splenorenal ligament', 'The pancreatic tail lies within the splenorenal (lienorenal) ligament between the spleen and left kidney. This is why the tail is closely applied to the splenic hilum and mobile despite the rest of the gland being retroperitoneal.'),
('69e45bc2-42ab-564e-8b00-23103e327acc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gastrosplenic ligament connects:', '["The stomach and the liver","The stomach and the transverse colon","None","The stomach and the spleen","The spleen and the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'The stomach and the spleen', 'The gastrosplenic ligament joins the greater curvature of the stomach to the splenic hilum and carries the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels. The other pairs of structures are joined by different peritoneal folds.'),
('b2b2cde2-91ac-56dc-815d-117b8b221d00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenorenal ligament connects:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The spleen and the left kidney (contains the splenic vessels and tail of pancreas)","The spleen and the stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","The spleen and the diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spleen and the left kidney (contains the splenic vessels and tail of pancreas)', 'The splenorenal ligament runs from the splenic hilum to the left kidney and encloses the splenic vessels and the tail of the pancreas. The gastrosplenic and phrenicosplenic ligaments attach to the stomach and diaphragm, respectively.'),
('d99ca7ff-6b8f-5c86-9a1c-50c403ed289c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gastrosplenic ligament contains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels","Hepatic vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels', 'Running between the stomach and spleen, the gastrosplenic ligament carries the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels. The splenic vessels and pancreatic tail travel in the splenorenal ligament instead.'),
('61b81d64-7670-5f62-aad1-b8bd5dbe2ffd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The phrenicosplenic ligament is:', '["A peritoneal fold from the diaphragm to the spleen","Part of the greater omentum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of the lesser omentum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A peritoneal fold from the diaphragm to the spleen', 'The phrenicosplenic (lienophrenic) ligament is the peritoneal reflection suspending the spleen from the diaphragm. It is not a component of the greater or lesser omentum, nor does that omental designation fit the other choices.'),
('8968f58c-0da8-5a70-8a88-f5a26520814f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lienorenal (splenorenal) ligament contains:', '["The stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The tail of the pancreas and the splenic vessels","The colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The left kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tail of the pancreas and the splenic vessels', 'The splenorenal ligament envelops the splenic artery and vein and the tail of the pancreas as they course toward the splenic hilum. The stomach and colon are not carried within this fold.'),
('c95061d6-905e-57ef-bd0f-d034ad811b11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The phrenicocolic ligament is:', '["Part of the transverse mesocolon resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of the greater omentum mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fold of peritoneum from the diaphragm to the splenic flexure of the colon"]'::jsonb, 'A fold of peritoneum from the diaphragm to the splenic flexure of the colon', 'The phrenicocolic ligament is a peritoneal fold extending from the diaphragm to the splenic flexure of the colon. It helps suspend the spleen and is not part of either omentum or mesocolon.'),
('8a6ba969-d939-5328-bcab-2affbeaa590a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The phrenicocolic ligament forms:', '["The floor of the splenic recess (supports the spleen)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The roof of the splenic recess mediated by specific receptor activation","The medial border of the stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The floor of the splenic recess (supports the spleen)', 'The phrenicocolic ligament forms the floor of the left paracolic (splenic) recess, providing a hammock-like support beneath the spleen. It thus helps hold the spleen in the left hypochondrium, rather than roofing the recess.'),
('14418fb6-f3fe-52f5-888f-7c9cc697fe59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The liver weighs approximately:', '["0.5 kg resulting in decreased functional activity","2 kg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 kg mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1.2-1.5 kg in adults"]'::jsonb, '1.2-1.5 kg in adults', 'The adult liver weighs 1.2-1.5 kg and is the largest solid organ in the body; the other values given are either far too small or nonspecific.'),
('4cac7e8d-d69f-5203-bd0e-6c726ad051d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The porta hepatis includes:', '["Only the hepatic artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile ducts enter/exit","Only the bile duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile ducts enter/exit', 'The porta hepatis is the hepatic hilum through which the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile ducts enter and exit, all invested by the hepatoduodenal ligament; a single vessel or duct alone does not account for its full contents.'),
('dd6cf593-cd60-5546-8818-85bf7e7c43cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The portal vein is formed by:', '["Splenic and inferior mesenteric vein resulting in decreased functional activity","SMV and IMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and splenic vein","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and splenic vein', 'The portal vein is formed behind the neck of the pancreas by the union of the superior mesenteric vein and the splenic vein; combinations such as the SMV with the IMA or splenic with inferior mesenteric veins are incorrect constructions.'),
('d81328a5-687c-5eed-aec2-3fa051b4cc19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The portal vein also receives:', '["Only the SMV resulting in decreased functional activity","The hepatic vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the splenic vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferior mesenteric, right and left gastric, cystic, and paraumbilical veins"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior mesenteric, right and left gastric, cystic, and paraumbilical veins', 'In addition to its main trunks, the portal vein receives the inferior mesenteric, right and left gastric, cystic, and paraumbilical veins; the hepatic vein drains the liver into the IVC and is not a portal tributary.'),
('0e83f79e-a8cc-50b6-a14c-2187f7f8660e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The hepatic artery proper arises from:', '["The common hepatic artery (branch of the celiac trunk)","The IMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gastroduodenal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The SMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The common hepatic artery (branch of the celiac trunk)', 'The hepatic artery proper is the continuation of the common hepatic artery after the gastroduodenal artery is given off, and the whole common hepatic artery arises from the celiac trunk; the IMA, SMA, and gastroduodenal artery are the wrong sources.'),
('846e67e7-7175-5c7f-84ae-f76364f41cbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The liver is divided into 8 segments based on:', '["The bile duct distribution resulting in decreased functional activity","The portal and hepatic venous anatomy (Couinaud classification)","The arterial supply mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The portal and hepatic venous anatomy (Couinaud classification)', 'The Couinaud classification divides the liver into eight segments using the portal and hepatic venous anatomy; each segment has independent vascular inflow, outflow, and bile drainage, so vessel or duct distribution alone is insufficient.'),
('d8955e27-a0f0-59c6-ad01-575d3c16e6b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Segment I of the liver is:', '["The quadrate lobe","The right lobe","None","The caudate lobe","The left lobe"]'::jsonb, 'The caudate lobe', 'Segment I is the caudate lobe, which lies posterior to the porta hepatis and drains directly into the IVC; the quadrate lobe is segment IV, and neither lobe is the same as segment I.'),
('b197e51e-34a6-59ce-b454-195c21826621'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Segment IV of the liver is:', '["The caudate lobe","The right lobe","The left lobe","None","The quadrate lobe"]'::jsonb, 'The quadrate lobe', 'Segment IV is the quadrate lobe, sitting on the inferior surface of the liver between the gallbladder fossa and the fissure for the ligamentum teres; the caudate lobe is segment I.'),
('ed049536-a1b7-5e46-bf06-52288745f940'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The liver is covered by:', '["A fibrous capsule (Glisson capsule) and visceral peritoneum","Only fibrous capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only peritoneum mediated by specific receptor activation","Bare peritoneum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous capsule (Glisson capsule) and visceral peritoneum', 'The liver is invested by Glisson''s capsule, a fibrous capsule, which is in turn covered by visceral peritoneum everywhere except the bare area; peritoneum or fibrous capsule alone is an incomplete description.'),
('c6b8b884-987a-5beb-a9f0-126ae01238a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Glisson capsule extends into the liver at:', '["The porta hepatis, forming the portal triad (portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bare area mediated by specific receptor activation","The hepatic veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The porta hepatis, forming the portal triad (portal vein, hepatic artery, bile duct)', 'At the porta hepatis Glisson''s capsule is carried into the liver around the portal triad, investing the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile ducts as they branch; the bare area and hepatic veins are not the site of this extension.'),
('9449f53c-8591-56d6-aba0-475179cae01d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The portal triad includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic artery and bile duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal vein and bile duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal vein and hepatic artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct (within the hepatoduodenal ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct (within the hepatoduodenal ligament)', 'The portal triad within the hepatoduodenal ligament consists of the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct running together; listing only any two of these structures omits part of the triad.'),
('b7ca5c7e-8f99-595a-86fb-87282e6cfe2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament is part of:', '["The lesser omentum (the free edge)","None","The greater omentum","The gastrocolic ligament","The splenorenal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser omentum (the free edge)', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament forms the free edge of the lesser omentum, connecting the liver to the duodenum and enclosing the portal triad; the greater omentum, gastrocolic ligament, and splenorenal ligament are separate peritoneal folds.'),
('3f5817aa-2ed9-5fd9-81be-1dfff95f5bf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament contains:', '["Only the hepatic artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the CBD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein (posterior), hepatic artery (left), CBD (right) - P-A-C"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein (posterior), hepatic artery (left), CBD (right) - P-A-C', 'Within the hepatoduodenal ligament the portal vein lies posterior, the hepatic artery proper on the left, and the common bile duct on the right (P-A-C arrangement); naming only one structure ignores the other members of the triad.'),
('72e34f91-df24-54f8-86bd-e406d14bbbaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The hepatogastric ligament is part of:', '["The splenorenal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater omentum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The gastrocolic ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lesser omentum (extends from the liver to the stomach)"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser omentum (extends from the liver to the stomach)', 'The hepatogastric ligament runs from the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and forms the main part of the lesser omentum; the splenorenal, greater omentum, and gastrocolic structures are unrelated folds.'),
('cfeb5109-09db-5513-88e0-a31ee597c819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lesser omentum includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the hepatogastric ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the hepatoduodenal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The hepatogastric ligament and hepatoduodenal ligament","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hepatogastric ligament and hepatoduodenal ligament', 'The lesser omentum comprises the hepatogastric ligament and the hepatoduodenal ligament; choosing only one of these two components fails to represent the lesser omentum as a whole.'),
('8aba3b52-bc3d-5561-a561-8dabcfbeb7f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The greater omentum is a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Fold of the mesentery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Single layer of peritoneum mediated by specific receptor activation","Double fold of peritoneum that hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach","Part of the lesser omentum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Double fold of peritoneum that hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach', 'The greater omentum is a double fold of peritoneum that hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach and drapes over the transverse colon and small intestine; it is neither a single layer, a mesentery, nor part of the lesser omentum.'),
('e9029750-e22c-57b9-8798-c4a24d40b2b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The greater omentum hangs down to cover:', '["The transverse colon and coils of the small intestine","The liver only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The stomach only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transverse colon and coils of the small intestine', 'The greater omentum hangs down over the transverse colon and the coils of the small intestine, where it provides protection and helps wall off inflammation; the liver, pancreas, and stomach are not the structures it covers.'),
('7c6a7d50-85be-58ee-8577-285749296d2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The greater omentum is known as:', '["The mesentery resulting in decreased functional activity","The peritoneal sac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The abdominal policeman (helps wall off infections)","The lesser sac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The abdominal policeman (helps wall off infections)', 'The greater omentum is nicknamed the abdominal policeman because it migrates to sites of infection or inflammation and walls them off; the mesentery, peritoneal sac, and lesser sac do not carry this role.'),
('f5a96ee0-7631-5710-8018-7e65ae95c2c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lesser sac (omental bursa) communicates with the greater sac through:', '["The esophageal hiatus","The epiploic foramen (of Winslow)","The diaphragm","None","The aortic hiatus"]'::jsonb, 'The epiploic foramen (of Winslow)', 'The lesser sac (omental bursa) communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen of Winslow, which lies behind the hepatoduodenal ligament; the esophageal and aortic hiatuses are diaphragmatic openings, not peritoneal communications.'),
('75f13ead-ba1b-5fb9-9ad0-daad34f66d5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The epiploic foramen (of Winslow) is bounded:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior: liver, posterior: kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior: hepatoduodenal ligament, posterior: IVC, superior: caudate lobe, inferior: duodenum","Superior: diaphragm, inferior: colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior: hepatoduodenal ligament, posterior: IVC, superior: caudate lobe, inferior: duodenum', 'The epiploic foramen is bounded anteriorly by the hepatoduodenal ligament, posteriorly by the IVC, superiorly by the caudate lobe, and inferiorly by the first part of the duodenum; the other listed margins are incorrect.'),
('12f4b3f8-566b-56ee-b896-15ef757216ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lesser sac can be entered through:', '["The gastrocolic ligament (common surgical approach)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The coronary ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The splenorenal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The falciform ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gastrocolic ligament (common surgical approach)', 'The gastrocolic ligament is the usual surgical route into the lesser sac, giving exposure of the posterior stomach and pancreas; the coronary, splenorenal, and falciform ligaments do not open into this space.'),
('bfbee504-e859-5138-92de-2c1b61d0b1ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreatic pseudocysts form in:', '["None","The lesser sac (omental bursa)","The pleural cavity","The greater sac","The pelvis"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser sac (omental bursa)', 'Pancreatic pseudocysts typically collect in the lesser sac (omental bursa), the potential space behind the stomach and in front of the pancreas; the greater sac, pleural cavity, and pelvis are not the usual site.'),
('7764e4e3-cf02-5ec0-9079-3c3ef751ddd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Accessory spleens (splenunculi) are found in about:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","10-30% of people (often near the splenic hilum)","90% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10-30% of people (often near the splenic hilum)', 'Accessory spleens (splenunculi) occur in about 10-30% of people and are most often found near the splenic hilum; the 50%, 90%, and 1% figures do not match the reported frequency.'),
('d8456086-fa7b-57fe-9879-69dd3494592c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The most common location for accessory spleens is:', '["The splenic hilum","The tail of the pancreas","The left kidney","The greater omentum","The mesentery"]'::jsonb, 'The splenic hilum', 'The splenic hilum is the most common location for accessory spleens, which may also occur in the splenorenal ligament or tail of the pancreas; the greater omentum, mesentery, and left kidney are not typical sites.'),
('0cfb485b-98f6-59a5-a241-356adbb809e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The falciform ligament attaches:', '["The liver to the kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior abdominal wall to the liver (umbilicus to diaphragm)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The liver to the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior abdominal wall to the liver (umbilicus to diaphragm)', 'The falciform ligament attaches the anterior abdominal wall at the umbilicus to the anterior surface of the liver and continues upward to the diaphragm; it does not attach the liver to the kidney or stomach.'),
('ee8e2b08-7fab-50aa-8e3d-f0af22b9ce65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The falciform ligament contains:', '["The ligamentum teres (obliterated umbilical vein) in its free edge","The hepatic artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The bile duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ligamentum teres (obliterated umbilical vein) in its free edge', 'The free edge of the falciform ligament contains the ligamentum teres, the obliterated left umbilical vein; the hepatic artery, portal vein, and bile duct travel in the hepatoduodenal ligament, not here.'),
('c7ad4b5b-3d5c-5d44-a6ae-fe381ab639e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ligamentum teres is the remnant of:', '["The ductus venosus","The ductus arteriosus","The left umbilical vein","The umbilical arteries","None"]'::jsonb, 'The left umbilical vein', 'The ligamentum teres is the fibrous remnant of the left umbilical vein, which carried oxygenated blood from the placenta during fetal life; the ductus venosus, ductus arteriosus, and umbilical arteries leave different remnants.'),
('21d1732f-0699-53a9-a705-8d56d825b3f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of:', '["None","The ductus arteriosus","The left umbilical vein","The ductus venosus","The umbilical arteries"]'::jsonb, 'The ductus venosus', 'The ligamentum venosum is the remnant of the fetal ductus venosus, which shunted oxygenated blood from the umbilical vein toward the IVC, bypassing the liver; the other listed structures form different remnants.'),
('18f57fac-e33a-522d-9f54-1c596edd2891'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ligamentum venosum lies in:', '["The greater omentum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The fissure between the left and caudate lobes of the liver","The free edge of the falciform ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hepatoduodenal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fissure between the left and caudate lobes of the liver', 'The ligamentum venosum lies in a fissure on the visceral surface of the liver between the left and caudate lobes; the free edge of the falciform ligament and the hepatoduodenal ligament occupy different locations.'),
('ad3387f8-b307-5dcf-8585-5250741497e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The left umbilical vein enters the liver via:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The falciform ligament (to reach the left branch of the portal vein)","The coronary ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The ligamentum venosum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The gastrohepatic ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The falciform ligament (to reach the left branch of the portal vein)', 'The left umbilical vein reaches the liver within the falciform ligament and joins the left branch of the portal vein, becoming the ligamentum teres after birth; the coronary, ligamentum venosum, and gastrohepatic structures are not its route.'),
('eb061c02-3a6c-5b83-8a7e-70288fafa675'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right triangular ligament is:', '["Part of the falciform ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","A peritoneal fold at the right end of the coronary ligament","Part of the hepatoduodenal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A peritoneal fold at the right end of the coronary ligament', 'The right triangular ligament is a peritoneal fold formed where the anterior and posterior layers of the coronary ligament meet at the right end of the liver and attach to the diaphragm; it is distinct from the falciform and hepatoduodenal ligaments.'),
('a5ea1cbb-d446-5fad-bcd7-831fd031bd19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The left triangular ligament is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of the gastrohepatic ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of the falciform ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A peritoneal fold at the left end of the coronary ligament"]'::jsonb, 'A peritoneal fold at the left end of the coronary ligament', 'The left triangular ligament is the peritoneal fold formed at the left end of the coronary ligament where its anterior and posterior layers join, anchoring the left lobe to the diaphragm; the falciform and gastrohepatic ligaments are separate structures.'),
('952ed679-4880-5b9c-9b39-c8d5a2fbe13b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The coronary ligament is:', '["The peritoneal reflection from the liver to the diaphragm","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The ligament from the liver to the colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ligament from the liver to the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal reflection from the liver to the diaphragm', 'The coronary ligament is the peritoneal reflection from the liver to the diaphragm that demarcates the bare area; attachments of the liver to the colon or stomach designate different peritoneal structures.'),
('fa18cce5-c555-5407-9ce6-37fdac263763'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The coronary ligament has:', '["Anterior and posterior layers (the bare area lies between them)","Four layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Three layers mediated by specific receptor activation","Only one layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior layers (the bare area lies between them)', 'The coronary ligament has anterior and posterior layers, with the bare area of the liver lying between them; the other layer counts do not match the anatomy of this ligament.'),
('a041b82b-7a87-5dff-bd4b-23de2db6db39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The liver has a dual blood supply:', '["Portal vein only resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic artery (25% O2) and portal vein (75% O2)","The aorta directly mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatic artery only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic artery (25% O2) and portal vein (75% O2)', 'The liver has a dual blood supply, receiving about 25% oxygenated blood from the hepatic artery and 75% nutrient-rich blood from the portal vein; no single vessel supplies the liver alone.'),
('d4f03581-0acb-522d-a896-b36fe9168a7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The hepatic veins are:', '["Part of the portal system resulting in decreased functional activity","Branches of the portal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intrahepatic veins that drain into the IVC (not part of the portal system)","Branches of the IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrahepatic veins that drain into the IVC (not part of the portal system)', 'The hepatic veins are intrahepatic veins that drain the liver into the IVC and lie outside the portal system; they are not branches of the portal vein or of the IVC in the sense given by the other options.'),
('36290508-8507-563f-8f88-dead482c41cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder is divided into:', '["Fundus, body, and neck","Only fundus and body","None","Only body and neck","Head, body, and tail"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, and neck', 'The gallbladder is divided into the fundus, body, and neck; the head, body, and tail arrangement belongs to the pancreas, and listing only two parts is incomplete.'),
('f0458c23-d2b7-528d-a80f-22ad932467e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder fundus projects:', '["Anterior to the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage (at the lateral border of the rectus abdominis)","At the xiphoid process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Below the umbilicus mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior to the left 9th costal cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage (at the lateral border of the rectus abdominis)', 'The gallbladder fundus projects to the tip of the right 9th costal cartilage at the lateral border of the rectus abdominis; the xiphoid, umbilicus, and left-sided markings are incorrect.'),
('2aee6662-6fb7-5598-804b-1e298dd443cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder body lies:', '["Against the anterior abdominal wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Against the diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Against the visceral surface of the liver (gallbladder fossa)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Against the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Against the visceral surface of the liver (gallbladder fossa)', 'The body of the gallbladder lies against the visceral surface of the liver in the gallbladder fossa, between the right and quadrate lobes; the anterior abdominal wall, diaphragm, and stomach are not its resting surface.'),
('3a5c04da-23a5-57c4-a9ad-72f2b300df70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder neck (Hartmann pouch) is:', '["The body resulting in decreased functional activity","A dilated portion of the neck that may contain gallstones","The common duct mediated by specific receptor activation","The fundus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A dilated portion of the neck that may contain gallstones', 'Hartmann pouch is a dilated out-pouching of the gallbladder neck where gallstones commonly lodge and can obstruct the cystic duct; the fundus and body are not the site of this pouch.'),
('75b3bceb-1e32-5712-aeb9-e9f38801ff08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder is about:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","15 cm long accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","7-10 cm long and 3-4 cm wide","2-3 cm long leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '7-10 cm long and 3-4 cm wide', 'The gallbladder measures about 7-10 cm long and 3-4 cm wide; the other dimensions given are too short or otherwise inaccurate.'),
('f7bd3f9d-5467-5c04-848b-45eb5eeda62e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder capacity is about:', '["None","100 mL","30-50 mL","10 mL","150 mL"]'::jsonb, '30-50 mL', 'The gallbladder has a capacity of roughly 30-50 mL, storing and concentrating bile between meals; capacities of 100 mL or 150 mL are too large and 10 mL too small.'),
('11d29d7f-2252-5166-8eba-0157a9d45630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder wall has:', '["A mucosa, a muscular layer, and a serosal (peritoneal) covering","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a muscular layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Only a mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only a serosal layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mucosa, a muscular layer, and a serosal (peritoneal) covering', 'The gallbladder wall is formed by a mucosa, a muscular layer, and an outer serosal covering; a mucosa, muscularis, or serosa alone does not represent its complete three-layered wall.'),
('29c34e24-c153-57e8-88e9-5bd7738e7ad6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder mucosa is characterized by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Peyer patches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rugae (folds) that allow distension","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Villi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rugae (folds) that allow distension', 'The gallbladder mucosa is thrown into rugae, folds that flatten as the organ distends with bile; Peyer patches and villi are features of the intestine, not the gallbladder.'),
('6270185f-bedb-56d1-bd0b-fc814e27f623'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The gallbladder''s main function is:', '["None","Production of bile","Absorption of nutrients","Concentration and storage of bile","Secretion of enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Concentration and storage of bile', 'The gallbladder concentrates and stores bile between meals and empties it in response to cholecystokinin; bile production belongs to hepatocytes, and nutrient absorption and enzyme secretion are not its functions.'),
('adec25ae-9eef-5422-8dec-b33f276f40fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cystic duct has:', '["Villi resulting in decreased functional activity","Spiral folds (valves of Heister) that prevent overdistension or collapse","Circular folds mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spiral folds (valves of Heister) that prevent overdistension or collapse', 'The cystic duct contains the spiral valves of Heister, mucosal folds that regulate bile flow and prevent overdistension or collapse; villi and circular folds are features of the small intestine.'),
('06d7afeb-d31a-59be-8728-d96ec6e05cb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The common bile duct (CBD) is about:', '["7-9 cm long","3-4 cm","None","10-12 cm","1-2 cm"]'::jsonb, '7-9 cm long', 'The common bile duct is approximately 7-9 cm long, running from the junction of the cystic and common hepatic ducts to the duodenum; the other lengths are too short or too long.'),
('7f324316-8d6d-5206-a87f-26f3ba09dd6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The CBD has four parts:', '["Two parts resulting in decreased functional activity","Supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, and intraduodenal","Only one part mediated by specific receptor activation","Three parts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, and intraduodenal', 'The CBD is divided into supraduodenal, retroduodenal, pancreatic, and intraduodenal parts according to its course; any simpler division omits at least one of these four segments.'),
('326bb714-4c4e-5959-a4bf-f35a5acfa157'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreatic part of the CBD:', '["Passes through the liver resulting in decreased functional activity","Passes behind the pancreas (or through the pancreatic head)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Passes through the spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passes in front of the pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Passes behind the pancreas (or through the pancreatic head)', 'The pancreatic part of the CBD passes behind the pancreas or through the pancreatic head, so a pancreatic head tumour can compress and obstruct it; it does not run in front of the pancreas or through the liver or spleen.'),
('c9ac0808-8f32-5e74-8b24-562801447d28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The CBD diameter is normally:', '["< 15 mm resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","< 10 mm mediated by specific receptor activation","< 2 mm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","< 6 mm (dilated if > 8 mm)"]'::jsonb, '< 6 mm (dilated if > 8 mm)', 'The CBD normally measures under 6 mm in diameter and is regarded as dilated when it exceeds 8 mm; thresholds of 15 mm or 10 mm are too generous and 2 mm too strict.'),
('d6411311-aee1-5574-b473-2606a314bb1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Choledocholithiasis refers to:', '["Stones in the common bile duct","Stones in the cystic duct","None","Stones in the kidney","Stones in the gallbladder"]'::jsonb, 'Stones in the common bile duct', 'Choledocholithiasis is the presence of gallstones within the common bile duct, which can obstruct bile flow and cause jaundice, cholangitis, or pancreatitis. Stones confined to the gallbladder or cystic duct constitute cholelithiasis rather than choledocholithiasis, and renal stones are unrelated.'),
('12ce9525-cf8f-59f8-8751-e34023d6d54c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Cholelithiasis refers to:', '["Stones in the cystic duct","Stones in the kidney","None","Stones in the CBD","Stones in the gallbladder (gallstones)"]'::jsonb, 'Stones in the gallbladder (gallstones)', 'Cholelithiasis means calculi (gallstones) within the gallbladder itself, which may be silent or provoke biliary colic. Stones located in the cystic duct or common bile duct are separate conditions, and renal stones belong to the urinary tract.'),
('7e57cbe4-0aff-560a-8a05-74ac88fc565f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Gallstones are most commonly:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Uric acid stones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium stones mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesterol stones (mixed, > 80%)","Pigment stones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol stones (mixed, > 80%)', 'Over 80% of gallstones are cholesterol or mixed cholesterol-pigment stones, which develop when bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol relative to bile salts and lecithin. Pure pigment and calcium stones are much less frequent, and uric acid stones form in the urinary tract, not the gallbladder.'),
('1123887f-e13a-5098-8a42-f85e17293eca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Obstructive jaundice is caused by:', '["Obstruction of the CBD (e.g., by a gallstone or tumor)","Liver cirrhosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hemolysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction of the CBD (e.g., by a gallstone or tumor)', 'Obstructive jaundice arises from mechanical blockage of bile drainage, most often by a gallstone or tumor obstructing the common bile duct, leading to conjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Hemolysis and hepatocellular diseases such as hepatitis and cirrhosis produce jaundice by different, non-obstructive mechanisms.'),
('e19f0e9d-67bf-53b1-828c-9251d90aa31a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Courvoisier law states:', '["In jaundice, the gallbladder is palpable resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In jaundice with a palpable gallbladder, the cause is a stone mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In jaundice with a palpable gallbladder, the cause is rarely a stone (more likely malignancy)"]'::jsonb, 'In jaundice with a palpable gallbladder, the cause is rarely a stone (more likely malignancy)', 'Courvoisier''s law holds that a palpable gallbladder in a jaundiced patient is unlikely to be due to gallstones, because chronic stone disease scars the gallbladder so it cannot significantly distend. A distended, palpable gallbladder therefore strongly suggests malignant obstruction, such as carcinoma of the pancreatic head.'),
('eb095b69-647d-5b89-8ffd-a41da2ad397d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Murphy sign is used to diagnose:', '["Acute cholecystitis (pain on inspiration during palpation of the right upper quadrant)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute pancreatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Peptic ulcer disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute appendicitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute cholecystitis (pain on inspiration during palpation of the right upper quadrant)', 'Murphy''s sign is positive when deep palpation of the right upper quadrant arrests inspiration, as the inflamed gallbladder strikes the examiner''s hand during descent of the diaphragm. It is a well-recognized sign of acute cholecystitis, not of pancreatitis, peptic ulcer, or appendicitis.'),
('07c6d4e9-4ca5-5842-adf9-e1eff7664ec5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Boas sign is:', '["Pain in the right iliac fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain in the epigastrium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperesthesia below the right scapula in acute cholecystitis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperesthesia below the right scapula in acute cholecystitis', 'Boas'' sign is cutaneous hyperesthesia (heightened skin sensitivity) in the area just below the right scapula, a referred phenomenon accompanying acute cholecystitis. Pain confined to the epigastrium or right iliac fossa reflects different visceral or somatic sites.'),
('f3031cb8-e666-570a-ab4c-087528553a68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas is about:', '["5 cm","25 cm","20 cm","12-15 cm long","None"]'::jsonb, '12-15 cm long', 'The pancreas measures approximately 12-15 cm in length and lies retroperitoneally across the posterior abdominal wall at the L1-L2 level. The lengths proposed by the other options are inconsistent with the accepted anatomical size of the gland.'),
('c93f391e-940f-5c85-b1b8-cd0d5b4d435a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas weighs about:', '["200 g","150 g","60-100 g","None","30 g"]'::jsonb, '60-100 g', 'The adult pancreas weighs roughly 60-100 grams, a soft retroperitoneal gland combining exocrine acinar tissue and endocrine islets. The weights given in the other options do not represent the accepted mass of the organ.'),
('13c1f08f-2a9e-56ff-b1fa-167b8a593cbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreatic head is:', '["The tail resulting in decreased functional activity","The widest part of the pancreas, lying in the C-loop of the duodenum","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The narrowest part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The widest part of the pancreas, lying in the C-loop of the duodenum', 'The pancreatic head is the widest part of the gland and is seated within the C-loop of the duodenum, with the uncinate process extending behind the mesenteric vessels. The body and tail taper and narrow progressively, so calling the head narrow would be incorrect.'),
('2f124516-b397-552b-8555-e9d282db873a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreatic uncinate process lies behind:', '["The portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The bile duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","The superior mesenteric vessels","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superior mesenteric vessels', 'The uncinate process hooks posteriorly behind the superior mesenteric artery and vein, a relationship of critical importance when dissecting the pancreatic head. The portal vein, bile duct, and duodenum lie at different planes and are not the structures directly behind the uncinate process.'),
('48ca3643-5b1c-5a5e-a730-6bb62ab7e67f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreatic neck lies anterior to:', '["The SMA resulting in decreased functional activity","The portal vein (formed by the union of the SMV and splenic vein)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The portal vein (formed by the union of the SMV and splenic vein)', 'The pancreatic neck crosses directly anterior to the point where the splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein, a key landmark in pancreatic surgery. The aorta and IVC lie at a deeper, more posterior plane, and the SMA passes behind the lower edge of the neck.'),
('4e05279c-b399-5a6a-bfc4-aa530f3cb07b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreatic body lies posterior to:', '["The liver resulting in decreased functional activity","The stomach (separated by the lesser sac)","The duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","The colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The stomach (separated by the lesser sac)', 'The body of the pancreas runs behind the stomach and is separated from it by the lesser sac (omental bursa), which explains why pancreatic inflammation can spread to irritate the stomach. Neither the liver, duodenum, nor colon lies immediately anterior to the pancreatic body.'),
('9fa4fc85-895d-58d8-8b70-daf6bd514651'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreatic tail lies in:', '["None","The lesser omentum","The transverse mesocolon","The greater omentum","The splenorenal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The splenorenal ligament', 'The pancreatic tail travels within the splenorenal ligament to reach the splenic hilum, which is why it is at risk of injury during splenectomy. The omenta and transverse mesocolon are not the ligamentous bed of the pancreatic tail.'),
('3f7d2209-1d40-5887-a476-ac9e315084e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic vein runs along:', '["The inferior border","The posterior surface of the pancreas","The anterior surface of the pancreas","The superior border of the pancreas","None"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior surface of the pancreas', 'The splenic vein is embedded along the posterior surface of the pancreatic body and tail, then merges with the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck to form the portal vein. It does not course over the anterior surface or along the borders of the gland.'),
('31767caf-cd0d-5926-87e2-60b518983945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic artery runs along:', '["None","The posterior surface of the pancreas","The inferior border of the pancreas","The superior border of the pancreas (tortuous)","The middle of the pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'The superior border of the pancreas (tortuous)', 'The splenic artery takes a characteristically tortuous course along the superior border of the pancreas on its way to the splenic hilum. This tortuosity is a useful landmark for locating the artery during surgery, contrasting with the straighter course of the splenic vein.'),
('0478025b-c75b-52fc-9852-06311f197658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ventral pancreatic bud forms:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The entire pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The body and tail without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower part of the head and the uncinate process"]'::jsonb, 'The lower part of the head and the uncinate process', 'The ventral pancreatic bud, arising from the hepatic diverticulum, rotates posteriorly and gives rise to the lower part of the pancreatic head and the uncinate process. The dorsal bud forms the upper head, body, and tail, so the ventral bud does not create the entire gland or the body and tail.'),
('31bd7188-ceac-5243-83de-64a0099d8337'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The dorsal pancreatic bud forms:', '["The upper part of the head, body, and tail","The lower head and uncinate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the body mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the tail leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The upper part of the head, body, and tail', 'The dorsal pancreatic bud contributes the upper part of the pancreatic head, the body, and the tail, and also gives rise to the accessory duct of Santorini. The lower head and uncinate process come from the ventral bud, so those structures are not dorsal-derived.'),
('bf779817-f806-5443-a85a-eee60d67de6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The main pancreatic duct (of Wirsung) is formed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the ventral duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the dorsal duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts"]'::jsonb, 'Fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts', 'The main pancreatic duct of Wirsung forms when the dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts unite during development, producing the principal drainage channel of the gland. Relying on only one of the two embryonic ducts ignores the fact that both contribute to the final main duct.'),
('2e4979c8-59bc-58a0-a0f3-c6b145f4440e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A separate duct mediated by specific receptor activation","The remnant of the ventral duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The remnant of the dorsal pancreatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'The remnant of the dorsal pancreatic duct', 'The accessory pancreatic duct of Santorini is the persistent remnant of the dorsal pancreatic duct, usually draining the upper part of the head through the minor duodenal papilla. It is not the remnant of the ventral duct, which becomes the main duct, nor an entirely separate duct.'),
('9b0de463-7035-5195-b8f1-566776aef56b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreatitis is most commonly caused by:', '["Gallstones and alcohol","Medication","Trauma","Infection","Tumor"]'::jsonb, 'Gallstones and alcohol', 'Gallstones and chronic alcohol use together account for the great majority of pancreatitis cases: stones obstruct the ampulla and provoke bile reflux, while alcohol causes direct and metabolic injury to acinar cells. Trauma, drugs, infection, and tumors are comparatively uncommon causes.'),
('7a585270-a2a9-5130-b282-b5ba8b0da919'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Acute pancreatitis can cause:', '["Epigastric pain radiating to the back, with elevated amylase and lipase","Pain in the left lower quadrant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain in the right lower quadrant mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nothing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epigastric pain radiating to the back, with elevated amylase and lipase', 'Acute pancreatitis classically produces severe epigastric pain that radiates to the back, together with nausea and markedly elevated serum amylase and lipase. Lower quadrant pain would point away from pancreatic inflammation and is not characteristic of the condition.'),
('06aaaaa9-a8fc-5cc5-bd32-5ea8649cadec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas has both:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Exocrine (acinar cells produce digestive enzymes) and endocrine (islets of Langerhans) functions","Only exocrine mediated by specific receptor activation","Only endocrine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exocrine (acinar cells produce digestive enzymes) and endocrine (islets of Langerhans) functions', 'The pancreas is a mixed gland: its exocrine acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes such as amylase, lipase, and proteases, while the endocrine islets of Langerhans produce hormones including insulin and glucagon. Describing it as exclusively exocrine or exclusively endocrine would omit one of its two major roles.'),
('8af8d2fc-e8ef-55e3-aa5d-7a369b2ba553'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The islets of Langerhans contain:', '["Alpha (glucagon), beta (insulin), delta (somatostatin), and PP (pancreatic polypeptide) cells","Only alpha cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only delta cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only beta cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha (glucagon), beta (insulin), delta (somatostatin), and PP (pancreatic polypeptide) cells', 'The islets of Langerhans are composed of several cell types: alpha cells secreting glucagon, beta cells secreting insulin (the most numerous), delta cells secreting somatostatin, and PP cells secreting pancreatic polypeptide. The islets are therefore mixed-cell structures rather than a single cell population.'),
('00f66428-e666-5c85-afe9-00cbfc195bf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The most common islet cell tumor is:', '["Insulinoma (beta cell, producing insulin)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatostatinoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Glucagonoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VIPoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Insulinoma (beta cell, producing insulin)', 'Insulinoma, a beta-cell tumor that secretes insulin inappropriately, is the most common functional islet cell neoplasm and typically presents with fasting hypoglycemia. Glucagonomas, VIPomas, and somatostatinomas are all considerably less frequent.'),
('8a8574b2-f88a-579b-92c2-fe56f19ae6bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is about:', '["5 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","12 cm long, 7 cm wide, and 3 cm thick","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","20 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '12 cm long, 7 cm wide, and 3 cm thick', 'The normal spleen measures approximately 12 cm in length, 7 cm in width, and 3 cm in thickness, making it the largest lymphoid organ. The much smaller dimensions offered by the other options are not compatible with the accepted size of the spleen.'),
('1e836265-e7e2-5d33-889c-831f908d8ce6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen weighs about:', '["300 g","150-200 g","500 g","50 g","None"]'::jsonb, '150-200 g', 'The adult spleen weighs approximately 150-200 grams. The substantially larger and smaller weights in the other options fall outside the accepted normal range for the organ.'),
('aadf749e-f1bc-567c-93e0-063abb7e8055'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is the largest:', '["Endocrine organ resulting in decreased functional activity","Exocrine organ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory digestive organ mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatic (lymphoid) organ in the body","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphatic (lymphoid) organ in the body', 'The spleen is the largest lymphoid organ in the body, carrying out both blood filtration and immune functions. It is not an endocrine or exocrine gland, and despite lying within the abdomen it is not classified as an accessory digestive organ.'),
('94fa4166-f4b8-569d-a097-3d8ed4365d8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen has a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscular capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrous capsule and a parenchyma divided into red pulp and white pulp","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serosal layer only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous capsule and a parenchyma divided into red pulp and white pulp', 'The spleen is enclosed by a fibrous capsule from which trabeculae extend inward, and its parenchyma is organized into red pulp (filtration) and white pulp (immune response). It lacks a muscular capsule comparable to a hollow viscus and is not covered by a serosa alone.'),
('9d7e5948-67ac-582e-b997-1981b58e2986'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The red pulp of the spleen is responsible for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretion of antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filtration of old or damaged RBCs (culling and pitting)","Production of lymphocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtration of old or damaged RBCs (culling and pitting)', 'The red pulp is the splenic filtration compartment, removing senescent or damaged red blood cells (culling) and stripping inclusions from circulating erythrocytes (pitting). Antibody secretion and lymphocyte production are functions of the white pulp.'),
('133cf7b8-b5ef-5f00-8fec-5a1a6be04294'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The white pulp of the spleen is responsible for:', '["Filtration of RBCs resulting in decreased functional activity","Immune function (lymphocytes and antibody production)","Iron storage mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immune function (lymphocytes and antibody production)', 'The white pulp consists of lymphoid tissue with T and B lymphocytes arranged around central arteries, mounting immune responses to blood-borne antigens and producing antibodies. Red-cell filtration and iron storage are roles associated with the red pulp, not the white pulp.'),
('c67bf02a-77f8-5c6e-b42d-d2978641173b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is supplied by:', '["The SMA resulting in decreased functional activity","The left gastric artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IMA mediated by specific receptor activation","The splenic artery (branch of the celiac trunk)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The splenic artery (branch of the celiac trunk)', 'The spleen receives its blood from the splenic artery, the largest branch of the celiac trunk, which courses tortuously to the splenic hilum. The superior mesenteric, left gastric, and inferior mesenteric arteries do not supply the spleen.'),
('557ba549-4e0f-500d-90d4-b7f539e9e35a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is located between:', '["The fundus of the stomach and the diaphragm (along the 9th, 10th, and 11th ribs)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The left kidney and the colon mediated by specific receptor activation","The liver and the gallbladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fundus of the stomach and the diaphragm (along the 9th, 10th, and 11th ribs)', 'The spleen lies in the left hypochondrium between the fundus of the stomach and the diaphragm, sheltered by the 9th, 10th, and 11th ribs. Its relationships to the kidney, colon, liver, and gallbladder are different from the position described by the other options.'),
('b8bd203e-3682-51bc-a2c6-b1ed9b6832e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The long axis of the spleen is along:', '["The 10th rib (obliquely)","The 9th rib","The 11th rib","None","The 12th rib"]'::jsonb, 'The 10th rib (obliquely)', 'The long axis of the spleen is directed obliquely along the 10th rib, which is why fractures of the lower left ribs, especially the 10th, place the spleen at risk. The other ribs do not define the principal axis of the organ.'),
('09e7d1aa-44bb-5df0-b2f2-6acdb636b3b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic notch is:', '["A notch on the superior border of the spleen","A notch at the hilum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A notch on the inferior border mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A notch on the superior border of the spleen', 'The splenic notch is a characteristic indentation on the superior border of the spleen, a remnant of fetal lobulation. It serves as a useful landmark for distinguishing the spleen from other masses in the left upper quadrant on palpation or imaging.'),
('1c49f144-a5eb-5031-8fff-550693a59519'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The splenic hilum is on:', '["The lateral (diaphragmatic) surface","The superior border","None","The medial (visceral) surface of the spleen","The inferior border"]'::jsonb, 'The medial (visceral) surface of the spleen', 'The splenic hilum lies on the medial (visceral) surface of the spleen, where the splenic artery enters and the splenic vein, lymphatics, and nerves exit. The smooth lateral diaphragmatic surface and the borders do not carry the hilum.'),
('6eb90bd3-1021-53c9-9399-0d3c7be3285f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is palpable when it is:', '["Normal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enlarged (normally not palpable)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enlarged (normally not palpable)', 'The normal spleen is not palpable beneath the left costal margin; it becomes clinically detectable only when enlarged, usually to roughly twice its normal size. A spleen that can be felt is therefore always abnormal, so a normal or small spleen would not be palpable.'),
('5769229c-631b-54dc-859c-84d182caf34a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Splenomegaly can be caused by:', '["Only trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Only tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection, portal hypertension, hematologic diseases, infiltration","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection, portal hypertension, hematologic diseases, infiltration', 'Splenomegaly has a broad differential including infections (for example malaria and infectious mononucleosis), portal hypertension from cirrhosis, hematologic disorders such as leukemia and lymphoma, and infiltrative diseases. No single mechanism such as trauma, tumors, or infection alone accounts for most cases.'),
('00ef02df-179f-5992-99c2-40e7b8caf1d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen''s functions include:', '["Only filtration of RBCs resulting in decreased functional activity","Only platelet sequestration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filtration of RBCs, immune function, and platelet sequestration","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only immune function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtration of RBCs, immune function, and platelet sequestration', 'The spleen performs several functions: the red pulp filters old or damaged red blood cells, the white pulp contributes to immune defense, and the organ also sequesters and stores platelets. Restricting its duties to any one of these functions alone would be incomplete.'),
('0af1feee-035e-55b2-bd51-6fe88b6f504e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Splenectomy may be required for:', '["Only a ruptured spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Only infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ruptured spleen, hereditary spherocytosis, immune thrombocytopenia, etc"]'::jsonb, 'Ruptured spleen, hereditary spherocytosis, immune thrombocytopenia, etc', 'Splenectomy is indicated for traumatic splenic rupture, hereditary spherocytosis, immune thrombocytopenia, and selected hematologic or infiltrative disorders. Limiting the indications to trauma, tumors, or infection alone leaves out the important hematologic conditions.'),
('144a44fd-8ce2-5c4a-aa81-da13db203ff9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas has a rich blood supply from:', '["Only the splenic artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The celiac trunk and the SMA (pancreaticoduodenal arcades)","Only the SMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the celiac trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The celiac trunk and the SMA (pancreaticoduodenal arcades)', 'The pancreas is supplied from two sources: branches of the celiac trunk (splenic and gastroduodenal arteries) and the superior mesenteric artery, which form the pancreaticoduodenal arcades around the pancreatic head. Depending on only one of these vessels overlooks the gland''s dual arterial supply.'),
('dadce150-9a8c-519b-ae30-31cdde251d26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from:', '["The splenic artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The IMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The SMA mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The gastroduodenal artery (from the celiac trunk via the common hepatic)"]'::jsonb, 'The gastroduodenal artery (from the celiac trunk via the common hepatic)', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of the gastroduodenal artery, which itself arises from the common hepatic artery of the celiac trunk. It supplies the upper part of the pancreatic head and anastomoses with the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery.'),
('e95202a5-93e3-5667-a7f2-cabd780c1954'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from:', '["The SMA","The gastroduodenal artery","The splenic artery","None","The celiac trunk"]'::jsonb, 'The SMA', 'The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of the superior mesenteric artery, and it supplies the lower pancreatic head and uncinate process. The celiac-derived gastroduodenal and splenic arteries give rise to the superior pancreaticoduodenal branches instead.'),
('d85c383f-91a5-59aa-b2c5-566b877b6b55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreatic carcinoma most commonly occurs in:', '["The head of the pancreas (60-70%)","The body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The uncinate process mediated by specific receptor activation","The tail without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The head of the pancreas (60-70%)', 'About 60-70% of pancreatic adenocarcinomas arise in the pancreatic head, where the tumor compresses the intrapancreatic bile duct and produces obstructive jaundice at an earlier stage. Tumors of the body, tail, and uncinate process are less common and typically present later.'),
('ae1dbaf8-2f27-5d21-a91e-17091c9e4b44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreatic head cancer can cause:', '["Obstructive jaundice, painless jaundice, and Courvoisier gallbladder","Right lower quadrant pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left upper quadrant pain mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive jaundice, painless jaundice, and Courvoisier gallbladder', 'A pancreatic head tumor compresses the intrapancreatic common bile duct, producing progressive painless obstructive jaundice; when the distended gallbladder becomes palpable in a jaundiced patient, this is Courvoisier''s sign. Pain in the right lower or left upper quadrant is not characteristic of this presentation.'),
('ca7f6d44-48db-5c3c-bd1d-ff2ad6b27175'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Pancreatic tail cancer presents:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Later (vague symptoms, often advanced at diagnosis)","Early with pain mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Early with jaundice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Later (vague symptoms, often advanced at diagnosis)', 'Tumors of the pancreatic tail produce no early obstructive symptoms because they lie far from the common bile duct, so affected patients come to attention late with vague discomfort, frequently after the disease has metastasized. Head tumors, by contrast, cause early jaundice.'),
('06231996-c6b7-5fba-b398-0442f9789bfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The spleen is removed by ligating:', '["The SMV resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The splenic artery and vein at the hilum","The celiac trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The splenic artery and vein at the hilum', 'During splenectomy the splenic artery and vein are divided at the splenic hilum, together with the short gastric vessels and the peritoneal attachments of the splenorenal ligament. The celiac trunk, portal vein, and superior mesenteric vein themselves are not ligated.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('32a34837-3f2d-5b7c-be08-6a1fb264a74d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pancreas can be palpated during surgery by:', '["Opening the hepatoduodenal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Entering the lesser sac through the gastrocolic ligament","Opening the falciform ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Entering the lesser sac through the gastrocolic ligament', 'The pancreas is best exposed surgically by opening the lesser sac through the gastrocolic ligament, which reveals the body and tail of the gland lying behind the stomach. The hepatoduodenal and falciform ligaments do not provide access to the pancreatic body.'),
('e0db2d8d-f3cf-5c98-b787-74e7835ecf3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The transverse mesocolon separates:', '["The left and right paracolic gutters resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater and lesser sacs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The supracolic and infracolic compartments","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The supracolic and infracolic compartments', 'The transverse mesocolon attaches the transverse colon to the posterior abdominal wall and divides the greater sac into the supracolic compartment above and the infracolic compartment below. The paracolic gutters and the boundary of the greater and lesser sacs are defined by different structures.'),
('ecf7219d-d8e0-5ae7-8813-d9694d26ac68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The supracolic compartment contains:', '["The kidneys resulting in decreased functional activity","The liver, gallbladder, spleen, stomach, and lesser omentum","The small intestine mediated by specific receptor activation","The colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The liver, gallbladder, spleen, stomach, and lesser omentum', 'The supracolic compartment, lying above the transverse mesocolon, contains the liver, gallbladder, spleen, stomach, and lesser omentum. The kidneys, small intestine, and colon occupy retroperitoneal or infracolic positions.'),
('77a33601-3dcd-5a49-b4dc-87de9e8e771a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The infracolic compartment contains:', '["The small intestine, ascending colon, descending colon, and sigmoid colon","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The stomach and spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The liver and gallbladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The small intestine, ascending colon, descending colon, and sigmoid colon', 'The infracolic compartment lies below the transverse mesocolon and is divided by the root of the mesentery. It houses the small intestine, ascending colon, and descending colon, while the stomach and spleen lie in the supracolic compartment above the transverse mesocolon.'),
('9d24a05e-c8e7-5486-8531-8358a6eb1ad7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right infracolic space is:', '["The space to the left of the mesentery root","The space above the liver","The space to the right of the mesentery root","None","The space below the stomach"]'::jsonb, 'The space to the right of the mesentery root', 'The oblique root of the small-bowel mesentery splits the infracolic compartment into right and left spaces. The right infracolic space is a closed pocket to the right of the mesentery root, which is why it is a common site for intraperitoneal abscess formation.'),
('12e11984-ef4d-5aaa-8e36-0440a5bb5b36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The left infracolic space is:', '["The space to the right of the mesentery root","The space below the stomach","The space to the left of the mesentery root","The space above the spleen","None"]'::jsonb, 'The space to the left of the mesentery root', 'The left infracolic space lies to the left of the mesentery root. Unlike the right space, it is open and communicates freely with the pelvic cavity, allowing intraperitoneal fluid to track down into the pelvis.'),
('78fc0387-97ca-5b6a-9a42-42e32db4a3cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right and left infracolic spaces communicate:', '["They are separated by the mesentery root resulting in decreased functional activity","They are separated by the colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Freely with each other (they are not separated)"]'::jsonb, 'Freely with each other (they are not separated)', 'The root of the mesentery runs obliquely but does not form a complete barrier, so the right and left infracolic spaces communicate with each other. Knowing that they are continuous is relevant to tracking the spread of peritoneal fluid.'),
('c584a43d-327c-5493-becc-0a8caca42ef7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The transverse mesocolon divides the peritoneal cavity into:', '["Supracolic (upper) and infracolic (lower) compartments","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left and right compartments mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior and posterior compartments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supracolic (upper) and infracolic (lower) compartments', 'The transverse mesocolon passes from the posterior abdominal wall to the transverse colon and separates the peritoneal cavity horizontally into supracolic and infracolic compartments. The supracolic compartment contains the stomach, liver, spleen, and gallbladder, while the small and large bowel mostly occupy the infracolic compartment.'),
('f47d64f2-2ff1-563f-8871-0ff5e157d128'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right paracolic gutter leads to:', '["The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) and the subphrenic space","The left paracolic gutter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The pelvic cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lesser sac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) and the subphrenic space', 'The right paracolic gutter runs along the lateral edge of the ascending colon and is the main pathway by which fluid from the lower abdomen ascends. It leads into the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch) and then the right subphrenic space, which is why liver abscesses and peritonitis frequently localize here.'),
('9c09a6c6-f2b9-5c2c-82c0-2c64235bf491'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The left paracolic gutter leads to:', '["The lesser sac resulting in decreased functional activity","The pelvic cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The right paracolic gutter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The left subphrenic space (but is limited by the phrenicocolic ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'The left subphrenic space (but is limited by the phrenicocolic ligament)', 'The left paracolic gutter runs beside the descending colon and communicates with the left subphrenic space. Its upward spread is partly blocked by the phrenicocolic ligament, which acts as a barrier, although the gutter remains patent inferiorly into the pelvis.'),
('ade12d24-6d6c-5168-a3c5-814def4bf524'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The psoas major is the most important landmark for:', '["The pelvic nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lumbar plexus (the nerves lie within the psoas)"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar plexus (the nerves lie within the psoas)', 'The lumbar plexus is embedded within the substance of the psoas major, with its branches emerging from its anterior surface or lateral border. The psoas therefore serves as the key surgical and imaging landmark for identifying these nerves, unlike the sciatic nerve, which forms in the pelvis.'),
('e08a2c58-1e65-5190-8b41-32cee96a53e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ureter crosses over the:', '["The aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","The IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The psoas major mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common or external iliac artery at the pelvic brim"]'::jsonb, 'Common or external iliac artery at the pelvic brim', 'As the ureter descends on the psoas major, it crosses the pelvic brim over the bifurcation of the common iliac artery (over the external iliac artery). This relationship is a critical surgical landmark and a classic site of ureteric injury during pelvic procedures.'),
('c73b1d19-c8e8-584c-a807-189165facdf0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ureter lies on the:', '["The aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliacus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadratus lumborum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psoas major muscle as it descends into the pelvis"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major muscle as it descends into the pelvis', 'The abdominal part of the ureter descends vertically over the anterior surface of the psoas major, crossing the iliac vessels at the pelvic brim to enter the pelvis. It crosses the genitofemoral nerve, which also lies on the psoas.'),
('6254b2c1-f36c-514e-8a3c-9458197fc52d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The ovarian/testicular vessels lie on:', '["The psoas major (anterior to the ureter)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The quadratus lumborum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The iliacus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The psoas major (anterior to the ureter)', 'The ovarian and testicular vessels descend on the anterior surface of the psoas major, passing anterior to the ureter. This anterior crossing is clinically useful because each vessel can be identified to locate the ureter during abdominal and pelvic surgery.'),
('8145789f-4aab-502d-b96d-0db6043936ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The genitofemoral nerve lies on:', '["The psoas major (anterior surface)","None","The quadratus lumborum","The iliacus","The posterior abdominal wall"]'::jsonb, 'The psoas major (anterior surface)', 'The genitofemoral nerve pierces the psoas major and continues on its anterior surface, where it divides into the genital and femoral branches. It is a useful landmark during retroperitoneal surgery, and its position explains why it can be compressed by a psoas abscess.'),
('ec36f62f-e2a4-5f65-9193-25922b7f5584'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cutaneous nerves of the posterior abdominal wall include:', '["Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), and lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3)","Only T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only L2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Only L1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), and lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3)', 'Cutaneous innervation of the posterior abdominal wall is provided by the subcostal (T12), iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal (L1), and lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3) nerves, along with branches of the intercostal nerves. Together these supply the skin of the back, flank, and upper thigh.'),
('f4d7c893-7957-5220-acad-2fe059c7d59c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The para-aortic lymph nodes lie:', '["On the IVC","On either side of the abdominal aorta","In front of the abdominal aorta","Behind the abdominal aorta","None"]'::jsonb, 'On either side of the abdominal aorta', 'The para-aortic lymph nodes are arranged on both sides of the abdominal aorta, closely accompanying the lumbar vessels. They drain the posterior abdominal wall, kidneys, suprarenals, and gonads, and are clinically important as a site of lymphatic metastasis.'),
('b46692c5-9bb8-5448-a334-2e3b6c03afab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pre-aortic lymph nodes lie:', '["Behind the aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","In front of the abdominal aorta (celiac, SMA, IMA nodes)","On either side of the aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In front of the abdominal aorta (celiac, SMA, IMA nodes)', 'The pre-aortic nodes are situated anterior to the abdominal aorta and are grouped around the origins of the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries. They drain the foregut, midgut, and hindgut viscera respectively.'),
('73795d04-e8f3-5d83-8665-30acee3bb996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The retro-aortic lymph nodes lie:', '["None","Behind the abdominal aorta","On the IVC","On either side of the aorta","In front of the aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the abdominal aorta', 'Retro-aortic lymph nodes lie posterior to the abdominal aorta, between it and the lumbar vertebral bodies. Along with the lateral aortic group they make up the para-aortic (lumbar) lymph node chain.'),
('25371705-d6a2-5590-9880-4f8ec08b03b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lateral aortic nodes drain:', '["The colon resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior abdominal wall, kidneys, suprarenals, and gonads","The stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The small intestine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior abdominal wall, kidneys, suprarenals, and gonads', 'The lateral aortic (para-aortic) lymph nodes drain the retroperitoneal structures, including the posterior abdominal wall, kidneys, suprarenal glands, and gonads. This territory is separate from the gut drainage, which goes to the pre-aortic nodes.'),
('7a714dd3-446f-52b5-b843-49073df9ed8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The para-aortic nodes are also called:', '["Inguinal nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Lumbar lymph nodes (lateral aortic and retro-aortic)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Axillary nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervical nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar lymph nodes (lateral aortic and retro-aortic)', 'The para-aortic nodes are synonymous with the lumbar lymph nodes, which include the lateral aortic and retro-aortic groups. This terminology distinguishes them from the inguinal, axillary, and cervical chains.'),
('328d601c-56f5-54cb-b55c-baf70e69bfba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The pre-aortic nodes drain:', '["The pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","The foregut, midgut, and hindgut viscera","The posterior abdominal wall mediated by specific receptor activation","The lower limb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The foregut, midgut, and hindgut viscera', 'The pre-aortic nodes comprise the celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric groups, which drain the gut viscera of the foregut, midgut, and hindgut. The retroperitoneal wall and lower limb drain to the para-aortic and inguinal chains instead.'),
('27e4f9e3-ef47-551f-9fb6-3dd9fde05154'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The celiac nodes drain:', '["The posterior abdominal wall resulting in decreased functional activity","The foregut (stomach, duodenum to the ampulla, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The midgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hindgut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The foregut (stomach, duodenum to the ampulla, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, spleen)', 'The celiac lymph nodes surround the celiac trunk and drain the entire foregut territory, including the stomach, proximal duodenum to the ampulla, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, and spleen. This mirrors the arterial supply of the celiac trunk.'),
('f2137c99-69b4-541b-a85f-a67257bf4674'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The superior mesenteric nodes drain:', '["The midgut (duodenum [distal] to the proximal colon)","The hindgut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The foregut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior abdominal wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The midgut (duodenum [distal] to the proximal colon)', 'The superior mesenteric lymph nodes drain the midgut, which extends from the distal duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. This region corresponds to the distribution of the superior mesenteric artery.'),
('fece7e0b-b41d-5a11-8de4-55dca3ff26a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The inferior mesenteric nodes drain:', '["The foregut resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior abdominal wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The hindgut (distal colon and upper rectum)","The midgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hindgut (distal colon and upper rectum)', 'The inferior mesenteric nodes drain the hindgut, comprising the distal third of the transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and upper rectum. This matches the territory supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery.'),
('cc34799f-3d5b-5b90-9614-ac3d04b0795f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The intestinal lymphatic trunk drains:', '["The inguinal nodes","None","The pelvic nodes","The pre-aortic nodes (gut viscera)","The lateral aortic nodes"]'::jsonb, 'The pre-aortic nodes (gut viscera)', 'Lymph from the gut viscera collects in the pre-aortic (celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric) nodes and then ascends in the intestinal lymphatic trunk to the cisterna chyli. It does not drain to the inguinal or pelvic nodes.'),
('58dfce66-6b41-5adf-ba1e-4e10490bb2e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The lumbar lymphatic trunks drain:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The pre-aortic nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","The para-aortic nodes (posterior abdominal wall, kidneys, gonads)","The pelvic nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The para-aortic nodes (posterior abdominal wall, kidneys, gonads)', 'The lumbar lymphatic trunks drain lymph from the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes, which collect from the posterior abdominal wall, kidneys, and gonads. They descend to join the cisterna chyli, separate from the intestinal trunk that drains the gut.'),
('fc6421b5-0aa0-5b18-9769-11888a56b5e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cisterna chyli is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","In the pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the left side of the vertebra mediated by specific receptor activation","In the thoracic cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the anterior surface of L1-L2 vertebrae, posterior to the right crus of the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'On the anterior surface of L1-L2 vertebrae, posterior to the right crus of the diaphragm', 'The cisterna chyli lies on the anterior surface of the L1-L2 vertebral bodies, behind the right crus of the diaphragm and to the right of the abdominal aorta. It is the dilated origin of the thoracic duct where the intestinal and lumbar lymphatic trunks converge.'),
('9d979537-195f-511b-838e-10e8b5e8fce8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cisterna chyli is about:', '["None","5-7 cm long","All","10 cm","1-2 cm"]'::jsonb, '5-7 cm long', 'The cisterna chyli is a fusiform dilatation approximately 5-7 cm in length. It is the terminal collecting sac that receives the lumbar and intestinal lymphatic trunks before the thoracic duct forms.'),
('6c5208df-6d4c-5d05-93e4-f937742a629e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cisterna chyli is a dilated sac at the beginning of:', '["The jugular trunk","The right lymphatic duct","None","The thoracic duct","The lumbar trunk"]'::jsonb, 'The thoracic duct', 'The cisterna chyli is the dilated lower end of the thoracic duct, collecting chyle from the intestinal trunk and lymph from the lumbar trunks. It marks the abdominal origin of the thoracic duct as it ascends to the thorax.'),
('d20af347-0b78-586d-9b3a-4c4f840b5a70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The Thoracic duct carries lymph from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the lower body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the right upper quadrant mediated by specific receptor activation","The entire body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower body (below the diaphragm) and the left upper quadrant"]'::jsonb, 'The lower body (below the diaphragm) and the left upper quadrant', 'The thoracic duct drains all lymph from below the diaphragm plus the left half of the thorax, left upper limb, and left side of the head and neck. The right upper quadrant drains separately through the right lymphatic duct.'),
('536c9459-13a2-5cec-9a1f-313d30baacdf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The Thoracic duct ends at:', '["The right venous angle resulting in decreased functional activity","The left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)","The SVC mediated by specific receptor activation","The IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left venous angle (junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins)', 'The thoracic duct ascends in the thorax and terminates by emptying into the left venous angle, formed by the junction of the left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The right lymphatic duct similarly ends at the right venous angle.'),
('94d1032c-37e8-5c22-875f-b74477ba15ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right lymphatic duct drains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The left half of the body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The entire body mediated by specific receptor activation","The lower half of the body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The right upper quadrant (right head, neck, arm, thorax)"]'::jsonb, 'The right upper quadrant (right head, neck, arm, thorax)', 'The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right side of the thorax. This right-sided territory is the counterpart of the much larger drainage area of the thoracic duct.'),
('4df588bf-4700-531f-89d6-9e1b9acf7efd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The right lymphatic duct ends at:', '["The left venous angle resulting in decreased functional activity","The SVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The right venous angle (junction of the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins)"]'::jsonb, 'The right venous angle (junction of the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins)', 'The right lymphatic duct opens into the right venous angle at the junction of the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins. It returns lymph from the right upper quadrant of the body to the venous circulation.'),
('4364e8fd-2ad7-5d15-99fe-edd0d2575946'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The thymus drains into:', '["The right lymphatic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The thoracic duct directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The left brachiocephalic vein (but its lymph may drain to the mediastinal nodes)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left brachiocephalic vein (but its lymph may drain to the mediastinal nodes)', 'The thymus sits anterior to the brachiocephalic veins and drains its venous blood into the left brachiocephalic vein. Its lymphatic drainage travels to the anterior mediastinal lymph nodes rather than directly to the lymphatic ducts.'),
('2894d7ea-aa6f-571b-871b-5728a74c4d48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'The cisterna chyli is absent in about:', '["100%","None","10%","90%","50% of people"]'::jsonb, '50% of people', 'The cisterna chyli is absent in roughly half the population, in whom the lumbar and intestinal trunks enter the thoracic duct directly without a dilated sac. This anatomical variation is important when performing retroperitoneal lymphatics studies.'),
('1f9aed11-16c4-5118-b50e-f96ad9d1dbe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'In Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which statement best fits the following fact: the liver has how many anatomical lobes?', '["4 (right, left, caudate, quadrate)","2 (right and left)","3 (right, left, caudate)","5 (right, left, caudate, quadrate, Spigelian)","Only 1"]'::jsonb, '4 (right, left, caudate, quadrate)', 'On its visceral surface the liver shows four anatomical lobes: the large right and left lobes, plus the small caudate and quadrate lobes. The caudate and quadrate lobes are functional parts of the left lobe and lie between the fissures and portal structures.'),
('b08b932e-63fd-5933-864e-6a448f097d48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Regarding Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, what is the correct answer to: the porta hepatis contains which structures?', '["Abdominal aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic veins mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein, hepatic artery, common hepatic duct (portal triad)"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein, hepatic artery, common hepatic duct (portal triad)', 'The porta hepatis is the hilum of the liver where the portal vein and hepatic artery enter and the common hepatic duct exits, together called the portal triad. The hepatic veins and IVC are unrelated to the porta hepatis and drain the liver posteriorly into the IVC.'),
('9ed0e484-c73d-5deb-9b3b-16501391f54a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'For Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which option is most appropriate: the bare area of the liver is?', '["The area of the liver in direct contact with the diaphragm (not covered by peritoneum)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The area of the porta hepatis mediated by specific receptor activation","The area covered by the gallbladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area of the liver in direct contact with the diaphragm (not covered by peritoneum)', 'The bare area of the liver lies on its posterior surface where the liver is in direct contact with the diaphragm without intervening peritoneum, bounded by the coronary ligaments. The porta hepatis and gallbladder bed are separate structures and remain covered by peritoneum.'),
('c98fd291-0fd1-5e26-b0b9-24d5ff0f43e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Which answer is correct in Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen when asked: the blood supply of the liver is from?', '["Hepatic artery alone","Celiac trunk alone","Portal vein alone","Superior mesenteric artery","Hepatic artery (25%) and portal vein (75%)"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic artery (25%) and portal vein (75%)', 'The liver receives a dual blood supply: the hepatic artery provides roughly 25% of the flow (oxygen-rich) and the portal vein the remaining 75% (nutrient-rich, from the gut). It is not supplied by the celiac or mesenteric artery alone.'),
('e9211f7b-b7b0-59ba-bc06-54a8f4f29d45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'In Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, the best response to ''The biliary drainage of the liver begins with'' is:', '["Bile canaliculi to cystic duct directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Bile canaliculi to pancreatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile canaliculi to duodenum directly mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile canaliculi to interlobular bile ducts to right and left hepatic ducts to common hepatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Bile canaliculi to interlobular bile ducts to right and left hepatic ducts to common hepatic duct', 'Bile secreted by hepatocytes drains from the bile canaliculi into interlobular bile ducts, which converge into the right and left hepatic ducts and then the common hepatic duct. The cystic duct is only reached after the gallbladder, and bile never passes through the pancreatic duct directly.'),
('0484f51e-e561-5768-abd7-3da91e286d92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'During revision of Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which of the following is correct: the gallbladder has which parts?', '["Cardia, fundus, body resulting in decreased functional activity","Fundus, body, neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Head, body, tail without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), neck"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), neck', 'The gallbladder comprises the fundus, body, infundibulum (Hartmann pouch), and neck. Hartmann pouch is clinically important because gallstones commonly lodge there. The cardia and head-body-tail configuration belong to other organs, not the gallbladder.'),
('b0ecf17b-e40e-58f5-85c1-a460f1942b2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'In Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which statement best fits the following fact: calot triangle is bounded by?', '["Liver, stomach, duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","Common bile duct, portal vein, hepatic artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common hepatic duct medially, cystic duct inferiorly, cystic artery superiorly (or liver)"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic duct medially, cystic duct inferiorly, cystic artery superiorly (or liver)', 'Calot''s triangle is bounded medially by the common hepatic duct, inferiorly by the cystic duct, and superiorly by the cystic artery or the inferior surface of the liver. It contains the cystic artery and cystic node and is the key surgical dissection area in cholecystectomy, distinct from the structures in the other options.'),
('f30085de-ea0e-525d-9245-4e1c8d2302b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Regarding Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, what is the correct answer to: the cystic artery most commonly arises from?', '["Celiac trunk","Right hepatic artery","Left hepatic artery","Common hepatic artery","Gastroduodenal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Right hepatic artery', 'In roughly three-quarters of people the cystic artery arises from the right hepatic artery, typically within Calot''s triangle. Other origins (common hepatic, left hepatic, gastroduodenal, celiac) occur but are all less common.'),
('269cfe54-7922-5768-a25b-87eab52c1592'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'For Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which option is most appropriate: the pancreas has which parts?', '["None of the above","Head, body, tail, fundus","Head, neck, body only","Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail","Head, body, tail only"]'::jsonb, 'Head, uncinate process, neck, body, tail', 'The pancreas is divided into the head, uncinate process, neck, body, and tail. The head lies within the duodenal C-loop and the tail reaches the splenic hilum; the uncinate process hooks behind the superior mesenteric vessels. The fundus is not a pancreatic part.'),
('0d538b5c-b501-5116-8076-b96dd9ce0833'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Which answer is correct in Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen when asked: the main pancreatic duct (of wirsung) joins the common bile duct to form?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)","Cystic duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic duct of Santorini leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla)', 'The main pancreatic duct (of Wirsung) merges with the common bile duct within the head of the pancreas to form the hepatopancreatic ampulla (Ampulla of Vater), which opens on the major duodenal papilla. It does not join the hepatic or cystic duct.'),
('69181f0e-044f-5c50-a636-01b42ec0bb78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'In Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, the best response to ''The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini) opens at'' is:', '["Duodenojejunal flexure","None of the above","Pylorus","Minor duodenal papilla","Major duodenal papilla"]'::jsonb, 'Minor duodenal papilla', 'The accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini) drains the upper part of the pancreatic head and opens into the duodenum at the minor duodenal papilla, situated slightly proximal to the major papilla. The major papilla receives the hepatopancreatic ampulla instead.'),
('c6a4dfcc-341a-5bb3-a167-a1ea236d4a0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'During revision of Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which of the following is correct: the pancreas is related posteriorly to?', '["IVC, aorta, left renal vessels, splenic vein","Gallbladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IVC, aorta, left renal vessels, splenic vein', 'Posteriorly the pancreas is related to retroperitoneal structures, including the IVC, abdominal aorta, left renal vessels, and splenic vein. The gallbladder, spleen, liver, and stomach lie anterior or adjacent, not posterior to the pancreas.'),
('f8997422-9d48-59e5-a194-ce909475cfe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'In Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which statement best fits the following fact: the spleen is located in which quadrant?', '["Central","Left lower quadrant","Right lower quadrant","Right upper quadrant","Left upper quadrant (hypochondrium)"]'::jsonb, 'Left upper quadrant (hypochondrium)', 'The spleen lies in the left hypochondrium (left upper quadrant), deep to the 9th, 10th, and 11th ribs. This position protects it beneath the rib cage, which is why its fractures and lacerations follow lower rib injuries.'),
('ac8c669c-ae5a-5439-b260-b0a7eaed5fe3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Regarding Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, what is the correct answer to: the spleen has which surfaces?', '["Medial and lateral resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphragmatic (superolateral) and visceral (inferomedial)","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior and inferior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior and posterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragmatic (superolateral) and visceral (inferomedial)', 'The spleen has a smooth superolateral diaphragmatic surface facing the diaphragm and chest wall, and an inferomedial visceral surface bearing impressions for the stomach, kidney, colon, and tail of the pancreas. Its medial border is notched rather than named as an independent surface.'),
('a19658b6-b93a-5367-98bb-56b4a1573ee4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'For Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, which option is most appropriate: the splenic hilum contains?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Splenic artery, splenic vein, lymphatics, nerve fibers","Portal vein and hepatic artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatic ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreatic ducts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery, splenic vein, lymphatics, nerve fibers', 'The splenic hilum on the visceral surface transmits the splenic artery, splenic vein, lymphatics, and autonomic nerve fibers. Portal and hepatic structures and pancreatic ducts belong to other organs and are not found at the splenic hilum.'),
('50106e7c-d559-5ee8-a2ca-a1b53b8b354a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'Which answer is correct in Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen when asked: the blood supply of the spleen is from?', '["Renal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Left gastric artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common hepatic artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic artery (largest branch of celiac trunk)","Superior mesenteric artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery (largest branch of celiac trunk)', 'The spleen is supplied by the splenic artery, the largest branch of the celiac trunk, which follows a tortuous course along the superior border of the pancreas to the hilum. It is not supplied by the renal, left gastric, hepatic, or superior mesenteric arteries.'),
('3bb394d9-63b4-5725-9cd1-fcd2bc8cd3bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Liver Gallbladder Pancreas Spleen', 'In Liver, gallbladder, pancreas and spleen, the best response to ''The splenic vein drains into'' is:', '["Azygos vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Left renal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal vein (after joining with SMV)","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein (after joining with SMV)', 'The splenic vein runs behind the body and tail of the pancreas and joins the superior mesenteric vein behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein. It does not drain to the azygos, left renal vein, or IVC.'),
('843a77df-2ad4-522b-b9ab-8ca40f10c1b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The posterior abdominal wall includes which muscles?', '["Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus","Rectus abdominis, pyramidalis, external oblique","Internal oblique, transversus abdominis, rectus","Diaphragm only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus', 'Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, and iliacus form the muscular posterior abdominal wall behind the peritoneum. Rectus abdominis, pyramidalis, and the oblique and transversus muscles belong to the anterior lateral abdominal wall, while the diaphragm forms the superior boundary.'),
('07b98f4f-3a94-58ab-93a9-7394f5ce4587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major inserts into?', '["Pubic tubercle","Linea aspera","Greater trochanter","Iliac crest","Lesser trochanter of the femur"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter of the femur', 'Psoas major descends beneath the inguinal ligament to insert on the lesser trochanter of the femur, making it a strong flexor of the hip. The greater trochanter receives the gluteal muscles, the linea aspera the femoral adductors, and the pubic tubercle and iliac crest serve other attachments.'),
('50e2b2bb-7182-5cac-87c7-cbaae30d4a64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor is?', '["Same as psoas major resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of psoas major accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The iliacus muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A variable muscle that lies anterior to psoas major (attaches to pectineal line)"]'::jsonb, 'A variable muscle that lies anterior to psoas major (attaches to pectineal line)', 'Psoas minor is an inconstant muscle, present in roughly 40-50% of people, that lies superficial to psoas major and attaches to the pectineal line of the pubis. It is not a duplicate of psoas major, not part of it, and it is anatomically distinct from iliacus.'),
('fafb9093-88bc-522c-aea7-b43e9f79cf58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum originates from?', '["T12-L5 vertebrae resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliolumbar ligament and iliac crest","Sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 5-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar ligament and iliac crest', 'Quadratus lumborum arises below from the iliolumbar ligament and the posterior iliac crest and runs up to the 12th rib and the lumbar transverse processes. The sacrum, ribs, and femur are sites of attachment for other muscles, not for quadratus lumborum.'),
('c159caba-4ced-50a7-8767-18e02b3e99f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum inserts into?', '["Lesser trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","12th rib and transverse processes of L1-L4 vertebrae","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic tubercle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '12th rib and transverse processes of L1-L4 vertebrae', 'Quadratus lumborum inserts on the 12th rib and the transverse processes of L1-L4, enabling it to laterally flex the trunk and fix the 12th rib during inspiration. The lesser and greater trochanters are femoral sites occupied by iliopsoas and the gluteal muscles.'),
('28a3188b-3b20-5006-89ca-f9cefdc1fe76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus muscle originates from?', '["Lumbar vertebrae","None of the above","Pubic bone","Sacrum","Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)', 'Iliacus is a flat triangular muscle filling the iliac fossa, arising from its upper two-thirds and converging with psoas major onto the lesser trochanter. It does not arise from the lumbar vertebrae, sacrum, or pubic bone, which are attachments of other muscles.'),
('44cb271f-78c9-506e-a8f8-07bb053e9883'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus inserts into?', '["Greater trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral shaft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lesser trochanter (with psoas major, forming iliopsoas)"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter (with psoas major, forming iliopsoas)', 'The iliacus tendon joins the psoas major tendon to form iliopsoas, which inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur. It does not attach to the greater trochanter, femoral shaft, pubic bone, or iliac crest.'),
('f9130f12-bfa6-55b6-8b93-505dc375d3d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm crura originate from?', '["T12 only resulting in decreased functional activity","Sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right crus: L1-L3; Left crus: L1-L2 vertebrae","Coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L4-L5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right crus: L1-L3; Left crus: L1-L2 vertebrae', 'The crura are tendinous slips fixing the diaphragm to the anterior lumbar vertebrae: the longer right crus arises from L1-L3 and the left crus from L1-L2. Because the right crus surrounds the esophageal hiatus, it descends one segment lower than the left.'),
('9c032087-be1f-5168-881a-30050f6fa94b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sympathetic trunk in the abdomen lies?', '["Anterolateral to the lumbar vertebral bodies (along the psoas major)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to the aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","In the midline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior to the IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterolateral to the lumbar vertebral bodies (along the psoas major)', 'Each lumbar sympathetic trunk runs along the anterolateral surfaces of the lumbar vertebral bodies, just medial to psoas major. It is not apposed to the front of the aorta, the midline, or the IVC.'),
('be1798b1-1106-566a-b30d-87a003ccd1d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar sympathetic trunk contains how many ganglia?', '["6-7","1-2","None of the above","8-10","4-5 lumbar ganglia"]'::jsonb, '4-5 lumbar ganglia', 'The lumbar sympathetic trunk typically carries 4-5 lumbar ganglia, fewer than the thoracic chain, which has about 12. These ganglia relay sympathetic fibers to the lower limbs, pelvic organs, and abdominal viscera.'),
('1a303a6c-3980-5126-9563-0d01f04f905a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar plexus is formed by?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior rami of L1-L4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior rami of L1-L4 spinal nerves (with contributions from T12)","Anterior rami of L1-L5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only L1-L3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of L1-L4 spinal nerves (with contributions from T12)', 'The lumbar plexus is formed by the anterior rami of L1-L4 with a variable contribution from T12, and it lies within psoas major. Posterior rami supply the deep back muscles, and including L5 would describe the lumbosacral trunk rather than the plexus itself.'),
('0410e6ae-ac36-5ac2-9c00-681a9362fd3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar plexus lies?', '["Posterior to quadratus lumborum","In the iliac fossa","Within the substance of the psoas major muscle","None of the above","Anterior to psoas major"]'::jsonb, 'Within the substance of the psoas major muscle', 'The lumbar plexus is embedded between the superficial and deep laminae of psoas major, and its branches emerge from the muscle borders - femoral laterally, obturator medially, and genitofemoral anteriorly. It is not located in the iliac fossa or behind quadratus lumborum.'),
('4f603110-81ba-5b65-959a-9d0959d32799'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The branches of the lumbar plexus include?', '["Iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2), lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3), femoral (L2-L4), obturator (L2-L4)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only femoral and obturator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only genitofemoral and lateral femoral cutaneous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2), lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3), femoral (L2-L4), obturator (L2-L4)', 'The lumbar plexus gives off six main branches: iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2), lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3), and femoral and obturator (L2-L4). The partial groupings each omit several named branches of the plexus.'),
('0be29bb4-5d24-5acd-979e-6e128377d104'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The abdominal aorta enters the abdomen through the?', '["Caval opening (T8)","Aortic hiatus of the diaphragm (at T12)","None of the above","Sternocostal triangle","Esophageal hiatus (T10)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic hiatus of the diaphragm (at T12)', 'The aorta passes from thorax to abdomen through the aortic hiatus behind the median arcuate ligament at T12. The caval opening (T8) transmits the IVC and right phrenic nerve, and the esophageal hiatus (T10) transmits the esophagus and vagus nerves.'),
('73a30492-5050-5ccc-9dbc-d7c227c22824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates at the level of?', '["L3 vertebra resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 vertebra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L4 vertebra (into left and right common iliac arteries)","L2 vertebra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L5 vertebra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L4 vertebra (into left and right common iliac arteries)', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates at the L4 vertebra, roughly at the level of the umbilicus, into the right and left common iliac arteries. L1 marks the transpyloric plane, and at L5 the common iliac veins unite to form the IVC.'),
('f2fd8db4-861d-56dc-a4d7-620d906167ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC is formed by the union of?', '["Right and left external iliac veins","None of the above","Right and left common iliac veins at the level of L5","Common iliac and internal iliac veins","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left common iliac veins at the level of L5', 'The inferior vena cava forms just to the right of L5 by the union of the right and left common iliac veins, then ascends to the caval opening at T8. The external iliac veins join the internal iliac veins to make the common iliac veins, so the other groupings are incomplete.'),
('9500a22d-9d3f-5a29-aafc-6853f16e01c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC pierces the diaphragm at?', '["L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","T8 (caval opening in the central tendon)","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (caval opening in the central tendon)', 'The IVC passes through the caval opening in the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8, accompanied by the right phrenic nerve. The aortic hiatus is at T12 and the esophageal hiatus at T10.'),
('be01bfee-a1ad-5217-888e-d105d2046dde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major is innervated by?', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L4-S1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior rami of L1-L3 (lumbar plexus)","Obturator nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of L1-L3 (lumbar plexus)', 'Psoas major is innervated directly by the anterior rami of L1-L3 rather than by terminal branches of the plexus. The femoral nerve supplies iliacus and the anterior thigh muscles, and the obturator nerve supplies the medial thigh adductors.'),
('5d8042df-1589-5fb5-8aee-7b45413e00e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum is innervated by?', '["Lumbar plexus only","None of the above","T12 and L1-L4 anterior rami","Only T12","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'T12 and L1-L4 anterior rami', 'Quadratus lumborum receives innervation from the anterior rami of T12 and L1-L4. The femoral nerve supplies iliacus and the anterior thigh compartment, and supply is not limited to T12 alone.'),
('ba7e9ef0-298d-541b-aa44-f9cecab9b527'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus muscle is innervated by?', '["L1-L2","None of the above","Femoral nerve (L2-L4)","Obturator nerve","Femoral and obturator"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2-L4)', 'Iliacus is innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4), matching its action as a hip flexor working with psoas major. The obturator nerve serves the medial thigh adductors, and the L1-L2 segments supply muscles of the lower abdominal wall and groin instead.'),
('653836b8-1f8d-5906-8764-02264f484459'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor (when present) is innervated by?', '["None of the above","L1 anterior ramus","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","L2-L3"]'::jsonb, 'L1 anterior ramus', 'Psoas minor, when present, is supplied by the L1 anterior ramus, the same segment near its proximal origin. It does not receive the femoral, obturator, or L2-L3 innervation.'),
('f1a7fa1a-e224-5792-bd37-70a84b390388'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliopsoas muscle functions as?', '["The chief flexor of the thigh at the hip","Adductor of the thigh","None of the above","Extensor of the thigh","Abductor of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'The chief flexor of the thigh at the hip', 'Iliopsoas, formed by psoas major and iliacus, is the chief and most powerful flexor of the thigh at the hip joint. Its anterior line of pull across the hip means it cannot adduct, abduct, or extend the thigh.'),
('bc10e910-9fef-5407-b1a0-bf0643160e73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum muscle functions as?', '["Rotator of the trunk resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extensor of the trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral flexor of the trunk (fixes the 12th rib during inspiration)","Flexor of the trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral flexor of the trunk (fixes the 12th rib during inspiration)', 'Quadratus lumborum laterally flexes the trunk to the same side and, acting with its contralateral partner, fixes the 12th rib to support the diaphragm during inspiration. It does not extend, flex, or rotate the trunk.'),
('5859742d-af8b-5036-b632-f8f5087fed65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major muscle can also?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Extend the vertebral column accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flex the vertebral column (when acting bilaterally)","Rotate the trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex the vertebral column (when acting bilaterally)', 'With the femur fixed, bilateral contraction of psoas major flexes the lumbar vertebral column, as when sitting up from a supine position. It does not extend the spine, and its main role remains hip flexion rather than rotation of the trunk.'),
('8e81ff40-cdfc-52a6-8e22-1cd0ebe72ad0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm crura are important because?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","They contain the aortic opening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They are tendinous origins of the diaphragm and the right crus forms the esophageal hiatus","They contain the caval opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They attach to the ribs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They are tendinous origins of the diaphragm and the right crus forms the esophageal hiatus', 'The crura are the tendinous origins of the diaphragm from the lumbar vertebral bodies, and the right crus loops around the esophagus to form the esophageal hiatus. The aortic hiatus lies posteriorly behind the median arcuate ligament, and the caval opening lies in the central tendon.'),
('98056c65-444c-5b59-91f3-abc92c2d2424'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The medial arcuate ligament is?', '["Same as the lateral arcuate ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thickened fascia over the psoas major (from L1-L2 to L1 transverse process)","Part of the external oblique without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Part of the inguinal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickened fascia over the psoas major (from L1-L2 to L1 transverse process)', 'The medial arcuate ligament is a thickening of the psoas fascia arching from the L1-L2 vertebral bodies to the L1 transverse process over psoas major. It is distinct from the lateral arcuate ligament over quadratus lumborum and is unrelated to the inguinal ligament or external oblique.'),
('810227bd-47f0-5cc4-a116-94be4ae07d39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lateral arcuate ligament is?', '["Part of the inguinal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","A thickened fascia over the quadratus lumborum (from L1 transverse process to 12th rib)","All of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Same as the medial arcuate ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickened fascia over the quadratus lumborum (from L1 transverse process to 12th rib)', 'The lateral arcuate ligament is a thickening of the thoracolumbar fascia stretching from the L1 transverse process to the 12th rib, arching over quadratus lumborum. It differs from the medial arcuate ligament, which covers psoas major, and from the inguinal ligament in the groin.'),
('8c01c1ce-d582-5970-baa8-1135249a066e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The median arcuate ligament is?', '["Part of the inguinal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibrous arch connecting the two crura of the diaphragm anterior to the aorta","Same as the medial arcuate ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The aortic hiatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous arch connecting the two crura of the diaphragm anterior to the aorta', 'The median arcuate ligament is a fibrous band joining the right and left crura across the front of the aorta, forming the anterior border of the aortic hiatus at T12. It is not the hiatus itself and is unrelated to the inguinal ligament.'),
('a3d64d80-a4b2-598b-891a-690bcf22fdcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The aortic hiatus (T12) is bounded anteriorly by?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crura mediated by specific receptor activation","Median arcuate ligament (connecting the two crura)","Aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Median arcuate ligament (connecting the two crura)', 'The aortic hiatus at T12 is bounded anteriorly by the median arcuate ligament connecting the two crura and posteriorly by the vertebral body. The crura and diaphragm lie to the sides and around the opening, not in front of it.'),
('9723ebd0-43aa-5391-af48-937baf555732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The esophageal hiatus (T10) is formed by?', '["Left crus","None of the above","Right crus of the diaphragm","Median arcuate ligament","Central tendon"]'::jsonb, 'Right crus of the diaphragm', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10 is formed mainly by fibers of the right crus of the diaphragm, which loop around the esophagus. The left crus, central tendon, and arcuate ligaments do not participate in forming this opening.'),
('5894077f-d506-5935-a6bd-68e62b860742'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The esophageal hiatus transmits?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Esophagus, vagus nerves, esophageal branches of left gastric vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus, vagus nerves, esophageal branches of left gastric vessels', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10 transmits the esophagus, the anterior and posterior vagus trunks, and the esophageal branches of the left gastric vessels. The aorta uses the T12 hiatus, the IVC the T8 caval opening, and the thoracic duct passes behind the aorta through the aortic hiatus.'),
('7ce6b27b-d7fa-52b4-b74f-d9f3614ef842'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC lies to which side of the abdominal aorta?', '["Anterior","Right side","Directly in front","Posterior","Left side"]'::jsonb, 'Right side', 'The inferior vena cava ascends to the right of the abdominal aorta for its entire course. This right-sided position reflects its formation by the right common iliac vein and explains why right-sided masses can compress it.'),
('7b316b8c-d006-58d6-8ce7-60753f13c9b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The abdominal aorta is crossed anteriorly by which structures?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Right renal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left renal vein, third part of duodenum, root of mesentery, body of pancreas","Esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left renal vein, third part of duodenum, root of mesentery, body of pancreas', 'From above downward, the anterior surface of the abdominal aorta is crossed by the left renal vein, the body of the pancreas, the third part of the duodenum, and the root of the mesentery. The right renal vein is shorter and does not cross the aorta, and the esophagus and IVC lie at other relations.'),
('4696ffff-99a3-5e23-9761-c509dfd5963d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The common iliac arteries bifurcate into?', '["External and internal iliac arteries at the level of the sacroiliac joint (L5-S1)","Femoral and profundal arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior and inferior iliac arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External and internal iliac arteries at the level of the sacroiliac joint (L5-S1)', 'Each common iliac artery divides opposite the sacroiliac joint (L5-S1) into the external iliac artery, which passes beneath the inguinal ligament as the femoral artery, and the internal iliac artery supplying the pelvis. The femoral-profunda and superior-inferior iliac groupings do not describe this bifurcation.'),
('fbd9facb-a465-5ca3-a81d-63e8808d61f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar arteries are branches of?', '["Common iliac artery","Internal iliac artery","None of the above","Abdominal aorta (4 pairs)","IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal aorta (4 pairs)', 'Four pairs of lumbar arteries arise from the posterior aspect of the abdominal aorta to supply the posterior abdominal wall and spinal cord. The common and internal iliac arteries are terminal pelvic branches, and the IVC is a vein rather than an arterial source.'),
('5e488931-9016-570e-98fe-92f4b9be7f0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The median sacral artery is a branch of?', '["Abdominal aorta (at the bifurcation)","Common iliac artery","None of the above","Lumbar artery","Internal iliac artery"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal aorta (at the bifurcation)', 'The median sacral artery is a small midline vessel arising from the posterior surface of the abdominal aorta just above its bifurcation at L4, descending over the sacrum. It does not arise from the iliac arteries or the lumbar arteries.'),
('b69ec16a-70fa-5670-8052-55455d407d37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar veins drain into?', '["IVC (right lumbar) and left renal/hemiazygos (left lumbar)","Common iliac veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Azygos vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IVC (right lumbar) and left renal/hemiazygos (left lumbar)', 'The right lumbar veins drain directly into the IVC, whereas the left lumbar veins typically join the left renal vein or the hemiazygos system, creating an asymmetric venous drainage of the posterior abdominal wall. The azygos vein receives the ascending lumbar rather than direct lumbar tributaries, and the portal vein takes splanchnic blood.'),
('47b5b5f1-8f7a-5f97-bab5-9628e3dd964a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ascending lumbar vein connects?', '["Femoral to iliac vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Common iliac vein to azygos/hemiazygos veins (vertebral venous plexus)","Renal vein to IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC to portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common iliac vein to azygos/hemiazygos veins (vertebral venous plexus)', 'The ascending lumbar vein runs up the posterior abdominal wall and connects the common iliac vein below with the azygos and hemiazygos veins above through the lumbar veins and vertebral venous plexus. This provides a collateral pathway between the infra- and supradiaphragmatic venous systems.'),
('811da388-96be-5b84-9c6b-4e0713a8435c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The cisterna chyli drains into?', '["Thoracic duct","Right lymphatic duct","Portal vein","None of the above","IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic duct', 'The cisterna chyli, located at L1-L2 behind the right crus, collects lymph from the lower limbs, pelvis, and abdomen and empties into the origin of the thoracic duct. The right lymphatic duct drains the right upper quadrant and is unrelated to the retroperitoneum.'),
('13c576f2-88d9-526a-a04e-dd424bb96263'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lymph from the posterior abdominal wall drains to?', '["Axillary nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial inguinal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal iliac nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Para-aortic (lumbar) and retroperitoneal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Para-aortic (lumbar) and retroperitoneal nodes', 'Lymph from the posterior abdominal wall and retroperitoneal structures drains to the para-aortic (lumbar) and retroperitoneal lymph nodes arranged around the aorta and IVC, ultimately reaching the cisterna chyli. Axillary, superficial inguinal, and internal iliac nodes serve other territories.'),
('1a1d1af0-19e2-5858-b906-96d7328b2154'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The celiac plexus (solar plexus) is located?', '["Around the celiac trunk at the level of T12-L1","In the pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Around the IMA mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Around the SMA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around the celiac trunk at the level of T12-L1', 'The celiac (solar) plexus is the large autonomic plexus surrounding the celiac trunk at T12-L1 in front of the aorta, containing the celiac and aorticorenal ganglia. Because it is named for the celiac trunk, it cannot lie around the SMA, IMA, or in the pelvis.'),
('9b81c48d-4d77-55e2-96af-0249648e0e86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves arise from?', '["Sacral sympathetic trunk","Thoracic sympathetic trunk","Lumbar sympathetic trunk (L1-L4 ganglia)","None of the above","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar sympathetic trunk (L1-L4 ganglia)', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves arise from the L1-L4 lumbar sympathetic ganglia and carry preganglionic fibers to the aortic, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric plexuses. They do not originate from the thoracic or sacral trunks or from the vagus nerve.'),
('88954de0-bb27-511c-9dc9-ec16ffc516df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Regarding the posterior abdominal wall:', '["The lumbar plexus lies within psoas major","The psoas major inserts into the lesser trochanter","The abdominal aorta bifurcates at L4","All of the above","The cisterna chyli is at L1-L2 level"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Each statement is correct: the lumbar plexus lies within psoas major, psoas major inserts on the lesser trochanter, the abdominal aorta bifurcates at L4, and the cisterna chyli lies at L1-L2 behind the right crus. Because every option is true, the answer is all of the above.'),
('2f075763-a2d0-57ba-8885-3c8b4ebb6d56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'What muscles are in the posterior abdominal wall?', '["Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus","Diaphragm only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectus abdominis, pyramidalis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External oblique, internal oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus', 'The posterior abdominal wall is formed by psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, and iliacus. Rectus abdominis, pyramidalis, and the oblique muscles belong to the anterior and lateral walls, and the diaphragm forms the superior boundary.'),
('e801521e-3b74-59b1-890a-9de83db59875'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the origin of psoas major?', '["Sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse processes and bodies of T12-L5","L1-L3 only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse processes and bodies of T12-L5', 'Psoas major arises from the vertebral bodies and transverse processes of T12-L5 and the intervening intervertebral discs. Its broad vertebral origin distinguishes it from iliacus, which arises from the iliac fossa, and its extent is greater than L1-L3 alone.'),
('5cd5bdf6-fc63-5894-8561-15647ac1aa66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the insertion of psoas major?', '["Lesser trochanter of femur","Linea aspera","Pubic tubercle","Iliac crest","Greater trochanter"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter of femur', 'Psoas major inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur, where it is joined by iliacus as the iliopsoas tendon. The greater trochanter, linea aspera, pubic tubercle, and iliac crest are sites of attachment for other muscles.'),
('81a6df82-8bf0-5860-ad37-dd6f08b97daa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'What is the function of iliopsoas?', '["Abductor of thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","Chief flexor of the thigh at the hip","Adductor of thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Extensor of thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chief flexor of the thigh at the hip', 'Iliopsoas is the chief flexor of the thigh at the hip joint, acting from its femoral insertion across the hip. It does not abduct, adduct, or extend the thigh, which are actions performed by the gluteal and other thigh muscles.'),
('eb3f8fbd-1c45-5901-b19e-9f79830f8eec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Where does the abdominal aorta bifurcate?', '["L4 vertebra","L5","L2","L3","L1"]'::jsonb, 'L4 vertebra', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates at the L4 vertebra into the common iliac arteries, approximately at the level of the umbilicus. L1 marks the transpyloric plane, and L5 is where the common iliac veins unite to form the IVC.'),
('d8f4ade8-6651-55ab-a146-16e2623040f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Posterior abdominal wall muscles:', '["Internal oblique, transversus, rectus resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectus, pyramidalis, external oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragm only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus', 'The muscles of the posterior abdominal wall are psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, and iliacus. The internal oblique, transversus, rectus, and pyramidalis are anterior and lateral abdominal wall muscles, and the diaphragm forms the superior wall.'),
('208c9394-6b4a-517b-974a-15db4545a325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Psoas major originates from:', '["Transverse processes and bodies T12-L5","Sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1-L3 only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse processes and bodies T12-L5', 'Psoas major takes origin from the transverse processes and vertebral bodies of T12-L5 and the intervening intervertebral discs. It does not arise from the sacrum, the iliac fossa (which gives origin to iliacus), or L1-L3 alone.'),
('0acc06f9-1c43-5d59-84ac-a00a64e75fd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Psoas major inserts into:', '["Linea aspera","Lesser trochanter of femur","Greater trochanter","Iliac crest","Pubic tubercle"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter of femur', 'Psoas major passes deep to the inguinal ligament and inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur, making it a powerful hip flexor. The linea aspera and greater trochanter receive thigh adductors and gluteal/rotator muscles respectively, while the iliac crest and pubic tubercle serve the abdominal wall and inguinal region.'),
('c1ef15d7-abaf-59a5-81e3-03f13a71174e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Psoas minor:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliacus muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of psoas major mediated by specific receptor activation","Same as psoas major without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Variable muscle anterior to psoas major (T12-L1 to pectineal line)"]'::jsonb, 'Variable muscle anterior to psoas major (T12-L1 to pectineal line)', 'Psoas minor is a small, inconstant muscle present in roughly half of individuals; when present it runs from the T12/L1 vertebral bodies to the pectineal line and iliopubic eminence, lying anterior to psoas major. It is a distinct muscle, not a subdivision of psoas major or iliacus, and it does not always exist.'),
('5adad2e2-967a-5b94-ae38-2aa2a5b79ea4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Quadratus lumborum originates from:', '["Ribs 5-12 resulting in decreased functional activity","T12-L5 vertebrae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femur mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliolumbar ligament and iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar ligament and iliac crest', 'Quadratus lumborum arises from the iliolumbar ligament and the posterior part of the iliac crest and inserts into the 12th rib and the transverse processes of L1-L4. It does not attach to ribs 5-12, the lumbar vertebral bodies, the femur, or the sacrum.'),
('4caea804-9752-5774-93c9-708d370b5420'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Quadratus lumborum inserts into:', '["Greater trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","12th rib and transverse processes L1-L4","Pubic tubercle mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lesser trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '12th rib and transverse processes L1-L4', 'Quadratus lumborum inserts into the inferior border of the 12th rib and the transverse processes of L1-L4, allowing it to fix the 12th rib during inspiration and laterally flex the trunk. The trochanters and pubic tubercle belong to thigh and pelvic musculature.'),
('a4ffc880-492f-53a8-aa1a-eee2de2ed220'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Iliacus originates from:', '["Lumbar vertebrae","Sacrum","None","Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)","Pubic bone"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)', 'Iliacus arises from the upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa, the inner lip of the iliac crest, and the ala of the sacrum, filling the iliac fossa. Its pelvic origin distinguishes it from muscles attached to the lumbar vertebrae or pubis.'),
('3f062758-1ba4-55b1-b6f8-96dc76571a60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Iliacus inserts into:', '["Pubic bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliac crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lesser trochanter (with psoas as iliopsoas)","Femoral shaft without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter (with psoas as iliopsoas)', 'Iliacus joins the psoas major tendon to form the iliopsoas, which inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur. The greater trochanter and femoral shaft receive the gluteal muscles and adductors rather than the iliopsoas.'),
('3e7c6068-e496-558c-85a9-a3693b12d0bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Diaphragm crura originate from:', '["Sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","L4-L5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 only mediated by specific receptor activation","Coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right: L1-L3; Left: L1-L2"]'::jsonb, 'Right: L1-L3; Left: L1-L2', 'The right crus of the diaphragm arises from the L1-L3 vertebral bodies, while the left crus arises only from L1-L2, an asymmetry related to the liver. The crura do not take origin from the sacrum, coccyx, or L4-L5.'),
('fc73998d-0e92-5142-98be-06ba5032e301'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar sympathetic trunk lies:', '["Anterior to aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterolateral to vertebral bodies (on psoas major)","Posterior to IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","In midline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterolateral to vertebral bodies (on psoas major)', 'The lumbar sympathetic trunks run vertically along the anterolateral aspect of the lumbar vertebral bodies on the medial border of psoas major. They are not midline structures and do not lie on the aorta or IVC.'),
('e4f637cc-609e-54f0-9c0d-c508678caada'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar sympathetic trunk contains:', '["8-10 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","6-7 mediated by specific receptor activation","4-5 lumbar ganglia","1-2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4-5 lumbar ganglia', 'The lumbar sympathetic trunk typically contains 4-5 ganglia, which are smaller and more variable than the thoracic ganglia. Counts of 6-7, 8-10, or 1-2 do not match the usual lumbar arrangement.'),
('7bd9f82a-4b65-500d-88d3-9d265f76f089'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar plexus is formed by:', '["Only L1-L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior rami L1-L5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior rami L1-L4 (T12 contributes via subcostal)","Posterior rami L1-L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami L1-L4 (T12 contributes via subcostal)', 'The lumbar plexus is formed by the anterior rami of L1-L4, with T12 contributing via the subcostal nerve. It does not derive from the posterior rami, which supply the deep back muscles, and it does not include the L5 ramus.'),
('0708642c-079c-5760-b4ea-a184a8182a84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar plexus lies:', '["In the iliac fossa","Anterior to psoas","Within the substance of psoas major","None","Posterior to quadratus lumborum"]'::jsonb, 'Within the substance of psoas major', 'The lumbar plexus is embedded within the posterior part of the psoas major muscle, and its branches emerge through and around that muscle. It is not situated in the iliac fossa, anterior to psoas, or behind quadratus lumborum.'),
('a301acbd-60d5-564c-bd8b-eaa4d93d39d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar plexus branches:', '["Only genitofemoral and LFC resulting in decreased functional activity","Only femoral and obturator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliohypogastric L1, ilioinguinal L1, genitofemoral L1-L2, LFC L2-L3, femoral L2-L4, obturator L2-L4","Only iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iliohypogastric L1, ilioinguinal L1, genitofemoral L1-L2, LFC L2-L3, femoral L2-L4, obturator L2-L4', 'The lumbar plexus gives off six named branches: iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2), lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3), femoral (L2-L4), and obturator (L2-L4). The partial lists in the other options omit several of these branches.'),
('2d1b9086-2deb-56c0-812d-49963d1e8494'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Abdominal aorta enters through:', '["Aortic hiatus at T12","None","Sternocostal triangle","Esophageal hiatus (T10)","Caval opening (T8)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic hiatus at T12', 'The aorta enters the abdomen through the aortic hiatus at T12, behind the median arcuate ligament, the most posterior of the diaphragmatic openings. The sternocostal triangle and the esophageal and caval openings transmit other structures.'),
('6bc33688-8eba-50c4-9724-53695e643284'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Abdominal aorta bifurcates at:', '["L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L4 (into common iliac arteries)","L5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L4 (into common iliac arteries)', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates into the right and left common iliac arteries at the L4 level, slightly above the formation of the IVC at L5. It does not end at L1-L3 or L5.'),
('ebe9523e-9aaa-5619-950a-e28481726f69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'IVC is formed by:', '["All","Union of common iliac veins at L5","None","Union of internal iliac veins","Union of external iliac veins"]'::jsonb, 'Union of common iliac veins at L5', 'The IVC is formed by the union of the right and left common iliac veins at L5, to the right of the midline. It does not arise from the internal or external iliac veins alone, since those are tributaries of the common iliac veins.'),
('95b0c56f-fe3c-5d7e-8077-f82a5f4f250f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'IVC pierces diaphragm at:', '["T12 resulting in decreased functional activity","T10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T8 (caval opening in central tendon)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (caval opening in central tendon)', 'The IVC passes through the caval opening in the central tendon of the diaphragm at T8, the highest of the three major openings. T10 (esophageal hiatus) and T12 (aortic hiatus) transmit different structures.'),
('2fa1997e-ce37-531c-b30e-cfbcab501741'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Cisterna chyli is at:', '["Caval opening resulting in decreased functional activity","T10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","L1-L2, posterior to right crus, right of aorta","Aortic hiatus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2, posterior to right crus, right of aorta', 'The cisterna chyli is a dilated lymphatic sac at the L1-L2 level, lying posterior to the right crus of the diaphragm and to the right of the aorta at the origin of the thoracic duct. It is not located within the caval or aortic openings.'),
('cd8bcfb2-e2d7-51cc-b23e-ff27cbeb3acf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Cisterna chyli receives:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Intestinal and lumbar lymphatic trunks","Thoracic duct only mediated by specific receptor activation","Right lymphatic duct only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal and lumbar lymphatic trunks', 'The cisterna chyli receives lymph from the intestinal trunk, which drains the GI tract, and from the right and left lumbar lymphatic trunks draining the lower limbs and posterior abdominal wall. The thoracic duct conducts lymph away from it, and the right lymphatic duct drains the right upper quadrant instead.'),
('f3178b21-3b9b-5817-a338-9fc0a6057a80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Thoracic duct begins at:', '["Cisterna chyli at L1-L2","Cisterna at T12","Junction of jugular-subclavian","Aortic hiatus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna chyli at L1-L2', 'The thoracic duct begins as the upward continuation of the cisterna chyli at the L1-L2 level, entering the thorax through the aortic hiatus. It terminates, rather than begins, at the junction of the left subclavian and internal jugular veins.'),
('00cb2266-c9bf-5a1d-b450-ac3275dc38a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Psoas major innervated by:', '["Obturator nerve","Anterior rami L1-L3","None","Femoral nerve","L4-S1"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami L1-L3', 'Psoas major is innervated by the anterior rami of the L1-L3 spinal nerves. Although the femoral and obturator nerves are formed within the muscle, they do not innervate it directly.'),
('1ee251b5-cdfe-518b-838c-de5173290814'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Quadratus lumborum innervated by:', '["None","Femoral nerve","Lumbar plexus only","Only T12","T12 and L1-L4 anterior rami"]'::jsonb, 'T12 and L1-L4 anterior rami', 'Quadratus lumborum is supplied by the anterior rami of T12 and L1-L4, matching its segmental origin. The femoral nerve and the lumbar plexus proper do not innervate it.'),
('a934bb4e-a595-562c-a3cd-ec33c512b466'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Iliacus innervated by:', '["Femoral and obturator","Obturator nerve","None","L1-L2","Femoral nerve (L2-L4)"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2-L4)', 'Iliacus is innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4), which also supplies pectineus, sartorius, and rectus femoris. The obturator nerve distributes to the adductors and does not innervate iliacus.'),
('aa50dc3f-6fa6-593c-adf3-b14a18219bfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Psoas minor innervated by:', '["Femoral nerve","L1 anterior ramus","L2-L3","None","Obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'L1 anterior ramus', 'Psoas minor, when present, is innervated by the anterior ramus of L1. The femoral and obturator nerves and the L2-L3 levels do not supply this small muscle.'),
('6571d9a5-1528-5a75-8285-d94ac829a627'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Iliopsoas function:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abductor mediated by specific receptor activation","Chief flexor of thigh at hip","Adductor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chief flexor of thigh at hip', 'The iliopsoas is the chief flexor of the thigh at the hip joint, and it is also a flexor of the trunk on the femur. It acts neither as an extensor, abductor, nor adductor of the hip.'),
('cfe8dafd-9bf1-5801-b5a4-d726893f0154'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Quadratus lumborum function:', '["Flexor resulting in decreased functional activity","Rotator accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Extensor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral flexor of trunk, fixes 12th rib"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral flexor of trunk, fixes 12th rib', 'Quadratus lumborum laterally flexes the trunk toward the same side and anchors the 12th rib during inspiration, giving a fixed base for the diaphragm. It does not act as a pure flexor, extensor, or rotator of the trunk.'),
('38a79f0f-d990-5ec7-a131-5e38fd99b9ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Median arcuate ligament:', '["Aortic hiatus resulting in decreased functional activity","Same as medial arcuate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of inguinal ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrous arch connecting crura anterior to aorta","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrous arch connecting crura anterior to aorta', 'The median arcuate ligament is a fibrous arch formed by the union of the right and left crura of the diaphragm, passing anterior to the aorta at T12. It should be distinguished from the medial arcuate ligament and from the inguinal ligament, which is a lower limb structure.'),
('817855f0-d53b-535d-9640-6d09d2d76b4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Medial arcuate ligament arches over:', '["Aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Psoas major (L1-L2 to L1 transverse process)","Quadratus lumborum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major (L1-L2 to L1 transverse process)', 'The medial arcuate ligament arches over the psoas major, passing from the L1-L2 vertebral body to the transverse process of L1. The aorta and IVC do not run beneath it, and quadratus lumborum is covered by the lateral arcuate ligament.'),
('1aa5ea8a-7a1e-5bc4-84d5-3811cdedd452'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lateral arcuate ligament arches over:', '["Quadratus lumborum (L1 TP to 12th rib)","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Psoas major without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadratus lumborum (L1 TP to 12th rib)', 'The lateral arcuate ligament arches over the quadratus lumborum, extending from the transverse process of L1 to the 12th rib. Psoas major is crossed by the medial arcuate ligament rather than this one.'),
('a8924649-a881-5fcd-a8bc-9c3101ba3078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Aortic hiatus transmits:', '["Aorta only","Aorta and IVC","Aorta and esophagus","None","Aorta and thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta and thoracic duct', 'The aortic hiatus at T12 transmits the aorta and the thoracic duct (and often the azygos vein). The IVC and esophagus pass through separate openings at T8 and T10.'),
('1f6a225d-9dc8-5ace-8e97-aef394e406d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Caval opening transmits:', '["Thoracic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Esophagus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC and right phrenic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'IVC and right phrenic nerve', 'The caval opening at T8 transmits the IVC together with the right phrenic nerve. The aorta, esophagus, and thoracic duct travel through the other diaphragmatic openings.'),
('8f88c536-46a4-5ca5-b105-5f5935d4f462'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Esophageal hiatus transmits:', '["Aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophagus, vagus nerves, esophageal vessels","None mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thoracic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Esophagus, vagus nerves, esophageal vessels', 'The esophageal hiatus at T10 transmits the esophagus, the anterior and posterior vagus nerves, and the esophageal branches of the left gastric artery. The aorta and IVC do not pass through it.'),
('8a89c10d-6176-5a5e-9271-a130b0db42b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'IVC lies on which side of aorta?', '["Anterior","Posterior","Directly in front","Right side","Left side"]'::jsonb, 'Right side', 'The IVC runs to the right of the abdominal aorta throughout its retroperitoneal course, a result of the embryological dominance of the right subcardinal vein. It is not anterior, posterior, or to the left of the aorta.'),
('809f3c87-047a-51f6-9003-cc79bd75500a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Aorta is crossed anteriorly by:', '["IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","Esophagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Left renal vein, 3rd part duodenum, root of mesentery, pancreas","Right renal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left renal vein, 3rd part duodenum, root of mesentery, pancreas', 'Anterior to the abdominal aorta pass the left renal vein, the third part of the duodenum, the root of the small-bowel mesentery, and the body of the pancreas. The right renal vein and the IVC lie on the right rather than crossing the aorta anteriorly.'),
('5f92388b-22ce-5452-948d-e7af2d64c841'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Common iliac arteries bifurcate into:', '["External and internal iliac at sacroiliac joint","Superior and inferior iliac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Femoral and profundal mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External and internal iliac at sacroiliac joint', 'Each common iliac artery divides into the external and internal iliac arteries at the level of the sacroiliac joint. The femoral artery is a continuation of the external iliac artery below the inguinal ligament, not a direct branch of the common iliac.'),
('68da611b-52a4-5372-98f3-ecc2bb8958a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar arteries are branches of:', '["Internal iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdominal aorta (4 pairs)","Common iliac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal aorta (4 pairs)', 'Four pairs of lumbar arteries arise directly from the posterior abdominal aorta and supply the posterior abdominal wall, the lumbar vertebrae, and the spinal cord. They are not branches of the internal or common iliac arteries, and the IVC carries veins, not arteries.'),
('7a736819-6e30-5f3c-86b4-82deeae963db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Median sacral artery is from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Lumbar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common iliac mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal iliac without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abdominal aorta (at bifurcation)"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal aorta (at bifurcation)', 'The median sacral artery is a small unpaired vessel that arises from the posterior surface of the abdominal aorta just above its bifurcation at L4. It is not a branch of the lumbar, common iliac, or internal iliac arteries.'),
('5a01e749-dcb6-5d20-b907-bf4a475b4fe8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar veins drain into:', '["Azygos vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Portal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IVC (right) and left renal/hemiazygos (left)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common iliac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IVC (right) and left renal/hemiazygos (left)', 'The right lumbar veins drain directly into the IVC, while the left lumbar veins drain into the left renal vein or the hemiazygos system. They do not empty primarily into the azygos or portal veins.'),
('3f77a93f-0c9a-52d1-a219-376f90486a27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Ascending lumbar vein connects:', '["Femoral to iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC to portal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Common iliac vein to azygos/hemiazygos (vertebral plexus)","Renal to IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common iliac vein to azygos/hemiazygos (vertebral plexus)', 'The ascending lumbar vein runs on the lateral aspect of the lumbar vertebral bodies connecting the common iliac vein inferiorly with the azygos and hemiazygos veins superiorly, bridging the caval and azygos systems. The other options describe connections this vein does not make.'),
('cc34117f-839b-50fa-bfb7-2b7c5d434dbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Posterior abdominal wall lymph drains to:', '["Internal iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superficial inguinal mediated by specific receptor activation","Para-aortic (lumbar) and retroperitoneal nodes","Axillary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Para-aortic (lumbar) and retroperitoneal nodes', 'Lymph from the posterior abdominal wall drains to the para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes lying on each side of the abdominal aorta and to other retroperitoneal nodes. It does not drain to the superficial inguinal or axillary nodes.'),
('46aed550-fe71-5f11-a5ff-a66eb97ff7d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Celiac plexus is at:', '["Around celiac trunk at T12-L1","Around SMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Around IMA mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around celiac trunk at T12-L1', 'The celiac plexus surrounds the root of the celiac trunk at the T12-L1 level, lying anterior to the crura and between the adrenal glands. The superior and inferior mesenteric arteries have their own plexuses, and the plexus does not extend into the pelvis.'),
('16ca8ec8-a345-5bf6-a250-5240a554324e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar splanchnic nerves arise from:', '["Sacral sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thoracic sympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lumbar sympathetic trunk (L1-L4 ganglia)"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar sympathetic trunk (L1-L4 ganglia)', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves arise from the lumbar sympathetic trunk at the L1-L4 ganglia and carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the inferior hypogastric plexus. The thoracic and sacral sympathetic trunks give rise to their own separate splanchnic nerves, and the vagus is parasympathetic.'),
('6eb2eecf-1bda-50ac-9521-6a393716132d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The aortic bifurcation is at:', '["L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L2 mediated by specific receptor activation","L5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L4 (level of the umbilicus)"]'::jsonb, 'L4 (level of the umbilicus)', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates at L4, which in the adult corresponds to the level of the umbilicus. The IVC forms one vertebra lower at L5, and the esophageal hiatus lies at T10.'),
('79a20921-9e35-5ea9-8db6-28e3234cb5e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC bifurcation is at:', '["L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","L2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L3 mediated by specific receptor activation","L5 (union of common iliac veins)","L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L5 (union of common iliac veins)', 'Strictly the IVC is formed, rather than bifurcated, by the union of the right and left common iliac veins at L5, one vertebra below the aortic bifurcation at L4. It is not formed at L1-L4.'),
('2c171532-34ff-59d5-979c-83234b00c5d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The esophagus pierces the diaphragm at:', '["T10 (esophageal hiatus)","T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T8 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T10 (esophageal hiatus)', 'The esophagus pierces the diaphragm through the esophageal hiatus at T10, an opening formed by the fibers of the right crus. T8 is the level of the caval opening and T12 the level of the aortic hiatus.'),
('39cd98de-daf3-5e4e-b62d-9712aa009825'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The esophageal hiatus is formed by:', '["None","Median arcuate","Left crus","Central tendon","Right crus of the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Right crus of the diaphragm', 'The esophageal hiatus is formed by the fibers of the right crus of the diaphragm, which loop around the esophagus and can act as a partial pinchcock. The central tendon contains the caval opening, and the median arcuate ligament arches over the aorta.'),
('6a0a1a9a-fc7c-5140-adda-fcefe54826e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The caval opening is at:', '["T10 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T8 (in the central tendon)","T12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (in the central tendon)', 'The caval opening lies at T8 in the central tendon of the diaphragm, making it the most superior of the three major diaphragmatic openings. T10 transmits the esophagus and T12 transmits the aorta.'),
('640d1c10-119d-5e8a-b234-69da93fef4e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The aortic hiatus is at:', '["T8 resulting in decreased functional activity","T10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 (behind the median arcuate ligament)","L1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T12 (behind the median arcuate ligament)', 'The aortic hiatus lies at T12 behind the median arcuate ligament, the most posterior and inferior of the major diaphragmatic openings. T8 (caval) and T10 (esophageal) transmit other structures.'),
('9ff33745-07a9-5718-8a5e-263bc5f1b6d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The median arcuate ligament connects:', '["All","The two crura of the diaphragm","The 12th ribs","None","The left and right arcuate ligaments"]'::jsonb, 'The two crura of the diaphragm', 'The median arcuate ligament is the tendinous arch uniting the right and left crura of the diaphragm anterior to the aorta. It does not connect the 12th ribs or the lateral and medial arcuate ligaments.'),
('517ead8f-f4ce-5eb7-8f29-56b8749ed828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","The esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The IVC (at the caval opening, T8)"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC (at the caval opening, T8)', 'The right phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm together with the IVC through the caval opening at T8, providing motor and sensory supply to the diaphragm. It does not accompany the aorta or esophagus.'),
('c6c38ee0-fa82-5fec-a8b1-86beb2668c26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm:', '["With the IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","With the aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","With the esophagus mediated by specific receptor activation","Separately (pierces the muscle, not with any structure)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Separately (pierces the muscle, not with any structure)', 'The left phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm on its own, anterior to the central tendon, without accompanying the IVC, aorta, or esophagus. Only the right phrenic nerve travels through the caval opening.'),
('3abaedfe-2dac-53de-8a26-c7466a761cf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm has three openings:', '["Two openings resulting in decreased functional activity","Caval (T8), esophageal (T10), aortic (T12)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","One opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Four openings leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Caval (T8), esophageal (T10), aortic (T12)', 'The three major openings of the diaphragm are arranged from superior to inferior: the caval opening (IVC and right phrenic nerve) at T8, the esophageal hiatus (esophagus and vagus nerves) at T10, and the aortic hiatus (aorta and thoracic duct) at T12.'),
('2ea815eb-772d-51c6-bb42-bc869b40c5c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm develops from:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pleuroperitoneal membranes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Septum transversum, pleuroperitoneal membranes, body wall, and mesentery of esophagus","Only septum transversum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septum transversum, pleuroperitoneal membranes, body wall, and mesentery of esophagus', 'The diaphragm forms from four embryological sources: the septum transversum gives the central tendon, the pleuroperitoneal membranes close the posterolateral canals, the body wall contributes the muscular periphery, and the dorsal mesentery of the esophagus forms the crura.'),
('9b68dbab-1004-5ee4-8dca-ba9f9281b66d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Congenital diaphragmatic hernia (Bochdalek) occurs through:', '["The pleuroperitoneal canal (posterolateral diaphragm)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The esophageal hiatus mediated by specific receptor activation","The sternocostal triangle (Morgagni) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The caval opening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pleuroperitoneal canal (posterolateral diaphragm)', 'Bochdalek hernia results from failure of the pleuroperitoneal canal to close during development, producing a posterolateral diaphragmatic defect, most often on the left side. Herniation through the sternocostal triangle forms a Morgagni hernia, a different anterior defect.'),
('b934264a-2c02-5d60-9f96-838afc53b774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Morgagni hernia occurs through:', '["The pleuroperitoneal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The aortic hiatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The sternocostal triangle (anterior diaphragm)","The esophageal hiatus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sternocostal triangle (anterior diaphragm)', 'Morgagni hernia is an anterior diaphragmatic defect through the sternocostal triangle, the gap between the sternal and costal attachments of the diaphragm, typically seen on the right side. The posterolateral pleuroperitoneal canal defect instead produces a Bochdalek hernia.'),
('b59af971-c987-51f3-9b05-db506dbd8bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major functions as:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Adductor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flexor of the thigh and vertebral column","Extensor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abductor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor of the thigh and vertebral column', 'The psoas major flexes the thigh at the hip and, with bilateral contraction, flexes the vertebral column at the lumbar spine. It does not adduct, abduct, or extend the hip.'),
('737e8f80-85fb-5779-910f-884e1a1c8012'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major forms a bed for:', '["The femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The external iliac artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lumbar plexus within its substance"]'::jsonb, 'The lumbar plexus within its substance', 'The lumbar plexus is embedded within the substance of the psoas major, which protects it; the femoral and other plexus branches emerge from the muscle''s borders. The external iliac artery lies outside this bed.'),
('4020bf71-da16-5404-bfc2-552717346bdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum is quadrangular in shape and lies:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior to psoas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior to iliacus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On either side of the lumbar vertebral column"]'::jsonb, 'On either side of the lumbar vertebral column', 'The quadratus lumborum is a flat quadrangular muscle situated on each side of the lumbar vertebral column, extending from the iliac crest to the 12th rib. It lies posterior to the psoas rather than anterior or directly on the iliacus.'),
('31e3e8a0-8349-5913-9ee9-917c480209fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus muscle is fan-shaped and lies:', '["None","On the psoas","In the iliac fossa","On the quadratus lumborum","All"]'::jsonb, 'In the iliac fossa', 'The iliacus is a fan-shaped muscle that fills the iliac fossa on the inner surface of the ilium. It joins the psoas major at the inguinal region to form the iliopsoas.'),
('50435df7-e67b-5cea-a577-1f3c23ae62f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliopsoas tendon inserts into:', '["Iliac crest","Femoral shaft","Greater trochanter","Lesser trochanter of the femur","None"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter of the femur', 'The combined iliopsoas tendon inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur, giving powerful hip flexion. The greater trochanter receives the gluteal and short rotator muscles, not the iliopsoas.'),
('4c440ef1-a5aa-55ed-b5e8-8cd2de832279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliopsoas is the most powerful:', '["Adductor","Flexor of the hip joint","Abductor","None","Extensor of the hip"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor of the hip joint', 'The iliopsoas is the most powerful flexor of the hip joint, raising the thigh toward the trunk during walking and climbing. Adduction, abduction, and extension are functions of other muscle groups.'),
('0b1e6f44-4433-55ac-85f3-0260e0cc28a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor is absent in about:', '["10%","None","100%","40-50% of people","90%"]'::jsonb, '40-50% of people', 'The psoas minor is a small inconstant muscle that is absent in roughly 40-50% of individuals; when present it inserts on the iliopubic eminence and lightly tenses the iliac fascia.'),
('ec0a7a1b-c9bd-5930-89e1-3b8e2c290313'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum is a lateral flexor of:', '["The hip","The trunk (ipsilateral)","The thigh","None","The trunk (contralateral)"]'::jsonb, 'The trunk (ipsilateral)', 'Acting unilaterally, the quadratus lumborum laterally flexes the trunk toward the same (ipsilateral) side; acting bilaterally it helps extend the lumbar spine. It does not act on the thigh or hip.'),
('ba7ba650-7916-553d-8dfc-490bd5a43306'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum also helps to:', '["Fix the 12th rib during inspiration","Flex the thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extend the trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Rotate the trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fix the 12th rib during inspiration', 'By fixing the 12th rib during inspiration, the quadratus lumborum provides a stable lower attachment for the diaphragm to pull against. It does not flex the thigh or rotate the trunk.'),
('384a123b-0e6c-5b5c-bc59-5a937807e431'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus is innervated by:', '["Femoral nerve (L2-L4)","None","Obturator nerve","Superior gluteal nerve","L1-L2"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2-L4)', 'The femoral nerve (L2-L4) innervates the iliacus as well as the anterior thigh muscles. The obturator and superior gluteal nerves supply the medial and gluteal compartments, not the iliacus.'),
('5184d5e3-88db-53cb-98cc-11d9a477607b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor is innervated by:', '["None","Anterior ramus of L1","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","L2"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior ramus of L1', 'The psoas minor receives branches from the anterior ramus of L1, consistent with its origin at the first lumbar segment. The obturator and femoral nerves do not supply this small prevertebral muscle.'),
('b76d3c34-9849-5406-b890-8c345233367e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left lumbar veins drain into:', '["IVC directly resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left renal vein or hemiazygos vein","Right renal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left renal vein or hemiazygos vein', 'The left lumbar veins empty into the left renal vein or the hemiazygos vein, while the right lumbar veins drain directly into the IVC. This asymmetry reflects the left-sided course of these veins.'),
('f58eac10-b7a7-54e3-b10e-12b7e7d4a3d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right lumbar veins drain into:', '["None","Portal vein","IVC directly","Azygos","Left renal vein"]'::jsonb, 'IVC directly', 'The right lumbar veins drain directly into the IVC, which lies on the right side of the vertebral column. The left lumbar veins instead join the left renal vein or hemiazygos system.'),
('a8374a53-84b6-544c-a94d-337d9c37b92f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ascending lumbar vein on the right connects to:', '["The hemiazygos vein","None","The portal vein","The azygos vein","The IVC"]'::jsonb, 'The azygos vein', 'The right ascending lumbar vein joins the azygos vein, which ascends in the thorax to drain into the SVC. The hemiazygos vein is the corresponding channel on the left side.'),
('aa8d9403-3c60-5547-b3e6-c9ee96161eac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ascending lumbar vein on the left connects to:', '["The portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","The azygos vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The hemiazygos vein or left renal vein"]'::jsonb, 'The hemiazygos vein or left renal vein', 'The left ascending lumbar vein connects to the hemiazygos vein or the left renal vein, forming the left-sided longitudinal pathway of the azygos system. The azygos vein is the right-sided counterpart.'),
('8e1f5b1a-01a1-530c-91d6-9e092c13cace'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The vertebral venous plexus connects:', '["The IVC and SVC (valveless, allowing spread of metastases)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the SVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC and SVC (valveless, allowing spread of metastases)', 'The valveless vertebral venous plexus interconnects the IVC and SVC, providing a collateral route for venous return and a pathway for spread of pelvic and prostatic metastases to the vertebral bodies.'),
('f487b6a3-d188-5172-a8e8-7d34a3750506'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The cisterna chyli lies at the level of:', '["L1-L2 (anterior to L1-L2 vertebrae)","L4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 (anterior to L1-L2 vertebrae)', 'The cisterna chyli is located at the L1-L2 level, lying behind the right crus of the diaphragm and to the right of the abdominal aorta. It is the origin of the thoracic duct.'),
('ea35e9ab-bbb6-5a07-ad0b-f4b4d46a8168'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The thoracic duct arises from:', '["The cisterna chyli","The right lymphatic duct","None","The left lymphatic duct","All"]'::jsonb, 'The cisterna chyli', 'The thoracic duct begins as the upward continuation of the cisterna chyli in the upper abdomen, passing through the aortic hiatus to ascend in the thorax. It is not derived from the lymphatic ducts of the neck.'),
('b899edb5-4bed-5dd2-9562-9578b5cde324'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The thoracic duct drains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The right upper quadrant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lower body only mediated by specific receptor activation","The rest of the body (except right upper quadrant)","The left side only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rest of the body (except right upper quadrant)', 'The thoracic duct drains lymph from the whole body except the right upper quadrant (right head and neck, right upper limb, and right thorax), which is drained by the right lymphatic duct.'),
('98b355dd-b879-593b-8862-ce76f274728a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The abdominal lymph nodes include:', '["Only inguinal nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Para-aortic, pre-aortic, retro-aortic, and lateral aortic nodes","Only cervical nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Only axillary nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Para-aortic, pre-aortic, retro-aortic, and lateral aortic nodes', 'The abdominal lymph nodes are arranged in para-aortic (lateral), pre-aortic, retro-aortic (post-aortic), and lateral aortic groups around the abdominal aorta and IVC. Inguinal, cervical, and axillary nodes lie outside the abdomen.'),
('e8c827d4-d7b7-5fd8-99be-1b6b142f296b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pre-aortic lymph nodes include:', '["Only celiac resulting in decreased functional activity","Only SMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Celiac, SMA, and IMA nodes","Only IMA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac, SMA, and IMA nodes', 'The pre-aortic lymph nodes lie in front of the aorta around its three unpaired visceral branches: the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, and inferior mesenteric artery. They drain the foregut, midgut, and hindgut viscera.'),
('ceb23e9d-47ca-57b0-9ffa-f9f40aa49173'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes lie:', '["Behind the IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","On either side of the abdominal aorta","In front of the aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the psoas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On either side of the abdominal aorta', 'The para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes lie on either side of the abdominal aorta and IVC, receiving lymph from the kidneys, ureters, gonads, and the posterior abdominal wall. Nodes in front of the aorta form the pre-aortic group.'),
('1cf8016b-c9ae-53ab-adf2-6275d06e9fcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The post-aortic nodes lie:', '["None","Behind the abdominal aorta","In front of the aorta","On the psoas","On the IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the abdominal aorta', 'The post-aortic (retro-aortic) lymph nodes lie posterior to the abdominal aorta, draining structures of the inter-aortic region. Nodes on either side of the aorta belong to the para-aortic group.'),
('71ce349e-692b-54e0-bdbf-66142733e84e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The celiac plexus (solar plexus) is the largest:', '["None","Somatic plexus","Autonomic plexus in the abdomen","Vascular plexus","Lymphatic plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic plexus in the abdomen', 'The celiac (solar) plexus is the largest autonomic plexus in the abdomen, transmitting both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers to the foregut viscera. It is not a somatic, vascular, or lymphatic structure.'),
('9fdf3bab-3d91-5cc2-bb88-72e693cc6c2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The celiac plexus surrounds:', '["The renal arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The SMA mediated by specific receptor activation","The IMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The celiac trunk at the level of T12-L1"]'::jsonb, 'The celiac trunk at the level of T12-L1', 'The celiac plexus surrounds the celiac trunk at the T12-L1 level, lying between the two adrenal glands. The SMA and IMA carry their own mesenteric plexuses, and the renal arteries are surrounded by renal ganglia.'),
('54fc8ac1-77ee-5ca0-b6e5-07a42315e0a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The aorticorenal ganglion is part of:', '["All","The celiac plexus (sympathetic)","The parasympathetic system","None","The somatic system"]'::jsonb, 'The celiac plexus (sympathetic)', 'The aorticorenal ganglion is a sympathetic ganglion located near the origin of the renal artery and belongs to the celiac plexus. It relays preganglionic sympathetic fibers, not parasympathetic or somatic ones.'),
('ca117d6a-3394-5498-8ce6-3232302276b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar sympathetic chain has:', '["6-7","8-10","4-5 ganglia","None","1-2"]'::jsonb, '4-5 ganglia', 'The lumbar sympathetic chain typically contains 4-5 ganglia on each side, lying along the anterolateral aspect of the lumbar vertebral bodies. The thoracic chain, by comparison, has more ganglia.'),
('39dceee1-017f-5372-95ee-a1fd6ea4ec3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves carry:', '["Somatic fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the aortic plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the aortic plexus', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers from the lumbar sympathetic trunk to the aortic and hypogastric plexuses, where they synapse for distribution to pelvic organs. They do not carry somatic or parasympathetic fibers.'),
('58d7cf08-21c6-5af0-aacc-2a40122779ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC receives which tributaries?', '["Only renal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only common iliac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hepatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common iliac, lumbar, right gonadal, renal, suprarenal, phrenic, hepatic veins"]'::jsonb, 'Common iliac, lumbar, right gonadal, renal, suprarenal, phrenic, hepatic veins', 'The IVC receives many tributaries: the common iliac veins at its origin, lumbar veins, the right gonadal and right suprarenal veins, the renal veins, and the hepatic and phrenic veins before piercing the diaphragm. It does not receive only a single tributary group.'),
('b37c997a-9542-5cc9-a077-9e3770859feb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left gonadal vein drains into:', '["Portal vein","Left renal vein and IVC","IVC","Left renal vein","None"]'::jsonb, 'Left renal vein', 'The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, which then carries the blood to the IVC. In contrast, the right gonadal vein drains directly into the IVC.'),
('07a41c6b-8c8d-5b2f-8b62-fbb3dc826fb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right gonadal vein drains into:', '["None","IVC","Right renal vein","Portal vein","Left renal vein"]'::jsonb, 'IVC', 'The right gonadal vein runs upward to drain directly into the IVC, whereas the left gonadal vein joins the left renal vein. This asymmetry is relevant to varicocele, which is more common on the left side.'),
('b9163949-e6dc-5222-841d-9ca3f806b43a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left suprarenal vein drains into:', '["IVC","Portal vein","All","Left renal vein","None"]'::jsonb, 'Left renal vein', 'The left suprarenal vein drains into the left renal vein, while the right suprarenal vein is short and drains directly into the IVC. Neither suprarenal vein empties into the portal vein.'),
('5826a161-2783-53f1-9a08-2ef1573e859c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right suprarenal vein drains into:', '["Portal vein","None","Right renal vein","IVC","Left renal vein"]'::jsonb, 'IVC', 'The right suprarenal vein is short and drains directly into the IVC because the right adrenal gland lies close to the IVC. The left suprarenal vein instead joins the left renal vein.'),
('5e330be7-e471-5ba9-93d5-2e41d893c738'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC passes through the diaphragm at:', '["T12 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T8 (caval opening in the central tendon)"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (caval opening in the central tendon)', 'The IVC passes through the caval opening in the central tendon of the diaphragm at the T8 level, the highest of the three diaphragmatic hiatuses. The esophageal hiatus (T10) and aortic hiatus (T12) lie lower.'),
('2b9d5a2b-bdb8-5e1c-9a86-eb8c69b8e8e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC receives the hepatic veins:', '["At L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","After piercing the diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","At L2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Just before it pierces the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Just before it pierces the diaphragm', 'The hepatic veins drain into the IVC just before it pierces the diaphragm, within the bare area on the posterior surface of the liver. This short intrahepatic segment is where the veins release their blood.'),
('e8bb4b86-d765-575e-82f1-168c28b242c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The infrarenal IVC is the part:', '["Above the renal veins","None","Below the renal veins","Below the diaphragm","Above the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Below the renal veins', 'The infrarenal IVC is the segment below the renal veins, extending from the confluence of the common iliac veins at L5 up to the level of the renal vein junction. The portion above the renal veins is the suprarenal IVC.'),
('5ce43bf5-d594-5f41-9178-8c4b067fed76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The suprarenal IVC is the part:', '["Below the renal veins","Below the diaphragm","Above the diaphragm","None","Above the renal veins (intrahepatic)"]'::jsonb, 'Above the renal veins (intrahepatic)', 'The suprarenal IVC is the portion above the renal veins that passes through the liver (intrahepatic segment) before piercing the diaphragm. The infrarenal IVC lies below the renal veins.'),
('3037bb9c-50ef-5805-9373-09ae8097caa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The posas major forms the posterior wall of:', '["The inguinal canal","The axilla","The femoral triangle","None","The popliteal fossa"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral triangle', 'The psoas major forms the posterior wall of the femoral triangle, while the sartorius and adductor longus form its lateral and medial borders. The inguinal canal, axilla, and popliteal fossa are separate anatomical regions.'),
('9c90f467-8b63-5b72-b5f1-ab3e58df3ee9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major passes:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","Under the inguinal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Over the pelvic brim to insert on the lesser trochanter"]'::jsonb, 'Over the pelvic brim to insert on the lesser trochanter', 'The psoas major crosses over the pelvic brim, runs deep to the inguinal ligament into the thigh, and inserts with the iliacus on the lesser trochanter of the femur. It does not pass through the obturator foramen.'),
('8425faa8-ddf3-51cd-92cd-de5c5e9fa52b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus lies on the:', '["All","Quadratus lumborum","Iliac fossa","None","Psoas"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac fossa', 'The iliacus is a fan-shaped muscle that lies in the iliac fossa on the inner surface of the ilium. It is not on the quadratus lumborum or directly on the psoas, though it joins the psoas to form the iliopsoas.'),
('8b5b1016-7112-5c05-a883-25d088122e9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus tendon fuses with:', '["The sartorius resulting in decreased functional activity","The psoas major tendon to form the iliopsoas","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The quadratus lumborum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rectus femoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The psoas major tendon to form the iliopsoas', 'The iliacus tendon fuses with the tendon of the psoas major to form the iliopsoas, which inserts on the lesser trochanter. The sartorius, rectus femoris, and quadratus lumborum are separate muscles.'),
('2930664a-ed2e-5b06-b9e6-fb9c76f876ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The subcostal nerve is:', '["None","The anterior ramus of T11","The anterior ramus of T12","The anterior ramus of L1","The posterior ramus of T12"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior ramus of T12', 'The subcostal nerve is the anterior ramus of T12, which runs below the 12th rib and gives a lateral cutaneous branch to the gluteal region. Th posterior ramus of T12 and the anterior rami of T11 and L1 are different nerves.'),
('affd4fb8-ea85-56a6-a9cb-d9d1a98411e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliohypogastric nerve arises from:', '["L1 (anterior ramus)","L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L2 mediated by specific receptor activation","T12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (anterior ramus)', 'The iliohypogastric nerve arises from the anterior ramus of L1 and supplies the skin over the lateral gluteal region and the anterior abdominal wall above the pubis. L2-L3 give the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve.'),
('86903ad4-d9c6-51ba-8e12-1858bd9c697c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ilioinguinal nerve arises from:', '["L2","L1","L3","T12","None"]'::jsonb, 'L1', 'The ilioinguinal nerve arises from the anterior ramus of L1 and travels through the inguinal canal to supply the skin of the medial upper thigh, scrotum, or labia majora. It shares the L1 anterior ramus with the iliohypogastric nerve.'),
('53513f94-9256-57d9-adfa-cf3d97d5b5f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The genitofemoral nerve arises from:', '["L1-L2","L2-L3","L4-L5","L5-S1","L3-L4"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'The genitofemoral nerve arises from the anterior rami of L1 and L2, pierces the psoas major, and gives a genital branch (cremaster muscle and scrotum/labia) and a femoral branch to the skin over the femoral triangle.'),
('555dd6d7-6800-5495-81e0-5772663346c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve arises from:', '["L3-L4","L4-L5","L2-L3","L1-L2","None"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L3', 'The lateral femoral cutaneous nerve arises from the posterior divisions of L2 and L3, emerges at the lateral border of the psoas major, and supplies the skin of the lateral thigh. Its entrapment causes meralgia paresthetica.'),
('135ed9f1-cd5a-5ebf-90ae-d67051e7a4be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The femoral nerve arises from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","L4-L5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1-L2 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3-L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L2-L4 (posterior divisions of the lumbar plexus)"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4 (posterior divisions of the lumbar plexus)', 'The femoral nerve is formed by the posterior divisions of the L2-L4 ventral rami within the lumbar plexus and innervates the anterior thigh muscles and skin. Plain segment pairings such as L3-L4 or L4-L5 only name part of its root value, and L1-L2 belongs to the upper plexus rather than the femoral nerve.'),
('28b80040-bc7e-5642-b215-2d408ea07f5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The obturator nerve arises from:', '["L3-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L2-L4 (anterior divisions of the lumbar plexus)","L4-L5 mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1-L2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4 (anterior divisions of the lumbar plexus)', 'The obturator nerve derives from the anterior divisions of the L2-L4 ventral rami and supplies the medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh. L3-L4 alone omits the L2 contribution, while L4-L5 and L1-L2 do not represent the full obturator root value.'),
('de4598f8-127c-5e88-8753-c4eb7e021d08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbosacral trunk is formed by:', '["L4-L5 (connects the lumbar and sacral plexuses)","L1-L2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S1-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3-L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L2-L3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L4-L5 (connects the lumbar and sacral plexuses)', 'The lumbosacral trunk is the union of the anterior rami of L4 and L5, connecting the lumbar plexus to the sacral plexus in the pelvis. Higher lumbar and sacral segment pairings do not form this trunk.'),
('4be2ec25-28ce-55a4-9d48-0afb28840a08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacral plexus is formed by:', '["Anterior rami of L1-S1","All","Anterior rami of L4-S4","None","Posterior rami of L4-S4"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of L4-S4', 'The sacral plexus is formed by the anterior rami of L4-S4, the source of the sciatic, pudendal, and gluteal nerves. The plexus arises from ventral rami, excluding the posterior rami option, and an L1-S1 span is too extensive.'),
('35cc5282-6468-568c-afaf-ad432a2d6e8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The coccygeal plexus is formed by:', '["All","Anterior rami of S4-Co1","Posterior rami of S4-Co1","None","Anterior rami of L5-S2"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of S4-Co1', 'The coccygeal plexus is formed by the anterior rami of S4 and Co1 and supplies skin over the coccyx and perineum. The plexus derives from ventral rami, so posterior rami are excluded, and L5-S2 is too high.'),
('90c19fed-ef79-5b42-8b32-71c3eca0a608'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas compartment syndrome occurs when:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The psoas fascia is tense (can cause femoral nerve compression)","The iliacus is tense without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The quadratus lumborum is tense leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The psoas fascia is tense (can cause femoral nerve compression)', 'Psoas compartment syndrome results when bleeding or infection distends the psoas sheath, and the tense fascia can compress the femoral nerve running within the muscle. The iliacus and quadratus lumborum lie in separate fascial spaces, so they are not involved in this syndrome.'),
('a5658931-416b-5fbc-9872-6a2f99546853'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas abscess (cold abscess) is often:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Secondary to tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease)","Secondary to appendicitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Secondary to pyelonephritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secondary to diverticulitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary to tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease)', 'A psoas abscess is usually a cold abscess that tracks down the psoas sheath from tuberculous spondylitis (Pott disease). Appendicitis, pyelonephritis, and diverticulitis produce acute or retroperitoneal suppuration rather than the classic cold psoas abscess.'),
('e5de67f1-c601-5cf9-9dd1-5fe785504008'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas shadow on a plain radiograph is:', '["A gas shadow resulting in decreased functional activity","A soft tissue shadow on either side of the lumbar spine","A calcified shadow mediated by specific receptor activation","A bone shadow without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A soft tissue shadow on either side of the lumbar spine', 'The psoas shadow is the soft tissue density of the psoas major seen alongside the lumbar spine on a plain radiograph. It is not a gas, calcific, or osseous shadow, and a blurred or lost shadow may signal retroperitoneal pathology.'),
('a73d32f7-ceb1-5c09-83b9-45038bd11a6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas skin puncture (for lumbar puncture) is:', '["The L1-L2 interspace","The L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace","The T12-L1 interspace","The L5-S1 interspace","None"]'::jsonb, 'The L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace', 'Lumbar puncture is performed at the L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace, safely below the conus medullaris to avoid spinal cord injury. Higher sites such as T12-L1 and L1-L2 endanger the cord, while L5-S1 is needlessly low.'),
('2cfd9d57-846d-5c83-9864-38182016805f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The spinal cord ends at:', '["L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L3 mediated by specific receptor activation","L5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1-L2 (conus medullaris)"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 (conus medullaris)', 'The spinal cord terminates at the conus medullaris around the L1-L2 level in adults, which is why lower lumbar interspaces are chosen for puncture. T12, L3, L4, and L5 do not mark the cord termination.'),
('9936d2ac-a3d0-5d3d-ab5f-ee5d9b6df079'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The cauda equina is:', '["The bundle of nerve roots below the conus medullaris","The spinal cord above L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The filum terminale without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bundle of nerve roots below the conus medullaris', 'The cauda equina is the bundle of lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal nerve roots that descend below the conus medullaris within the lumbar cistern. It is not the cord above L1, and the filum terminale is a single fibrous strand rather than a nerve bundle.'),
('8fb4cb48-c534-5938-882e-353cd3952be3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The filum terminale is:', '["A bundle of nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","The termination of the spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibrous thread from the conus medullaris to the coccyx","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous thread from the conus medullaris to the coccyx', 'The filum terminale is a thin fibrous strand that anchors the conus medullaris to the coccyx. It is not a nerve bundle, and the conus medullaris, not the filum, represents the actual termination of the spinal cord.'),
('7cc97e30-e95a-5dd9-a794-34fdb78e20dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The dural sac ends at:', '["L5 resulting in decreased functional activity","S1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1 mediated by specific receptor activation","S2 (the level of the sacral hiatus)","Coccyx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'S2 (the level of the sacral hiatus)', 'The dural (thecal) sac ends at the S2 level, near the sacral hiatus, enclosing the cauda equina in cerebrospinal fluid. It extends beyond L1, L5, or S1 and does not reach the coccyx.'),
('6bc5ee25-bcbf-5d94-bcbd-66bb46e7c42d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The epidural space lies:', '["Between the dura mater and the vertebral canal wall","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the dura and the arachnoid mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between the arachnoid and the pia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the dura mater and the vertebral canal wall', 'The epidural space lies between the dura mater and the wall (periosteum and ligaments) of the vertebral canal, containing fat and venous plexuses. The subdural and subarachnoid compartments lie internal to the dura and are different spaces.'),
('3e382642-237f-5152-91be-6414826bf40c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Epidural anesthesia is commonly performed:', '["In the cervical region","All","In the lumbar region","In the thoracic region","None"]'::jsonb, 'In the lumbar region', 'Epidural anesthesia is most commonly given in the lumbar region because the spinal cord has already ended at L1-L2, reducing the risk of cord injury. Cervical and thoracic injections carry a higher risk of cord trauma.'),
('5e4bc368-0972-5bf2-b12d-2eeac2dbb728'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The subarachnoid space contains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)","Fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Air leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)', 'The subarachnoid space between the arachnoid and pia mater contains cerebrospinal fluid, which cushions the brain and spinal cord. Blood, fat, and air are not its normal content.'),
('ff414d78-6aaa-5e34-a29c-721c59e91447'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Lumbar puncture is performed in the subarachnoid space:', '["At S1 resulting in decreased functional activity","At T12 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Below L2 (to avoid spinal cord injury)","Above L1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Below L2 (to avoid spinal cord injury)', 'Lumbar puncture is deliberately performed below L2, past the conus medullaris, to avoid piercing the spinal cord. Punctures at T12 or above L1 endanger the cord, and S1 is below the ideal target zone.'),
('f0adaaad-cf0a-5f1b-83e7-0f3f6ba2ed9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar cistern is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The space above L2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The space in the cerebrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The subarachnoid space from L2 to S2 (contains the cauda equina)"]'::jsonb, 'The subarachnoid space from L2 to S2 (contains the cauda equina)', 'The lumbar cistern is the enlarged subarachnoid space from about L2 to S2 that contains cerebrospinal fluid and the cauda equina. It is not the space above L2, and it lies within the vertebral canal rather than the cerebrum.'),
('40050c10-0787-5078-ae80-5207fd192fad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The median sacral crest is formed by:', '["The fused transverse processes resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacral hiatus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fused spinous processes of the sacral vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'The fused spinous processes of the sacral vertebrae', 'The median sacral crest is the midline ridge formed by the fused spinous processes of the sacral vertebrae. The fused transverse processes lie more laterally as the intermediate crest, not on the midline.'),
('4a617217-09a0-5a2e-8d6f-b101cdd7e79b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacral hiatus is:', '["The opening at the upper end of the sacral canal resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The space between the sacrum and the coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening at the lower end of the sacral canal (at S4-S5)"]'::jsonb, 'The opening at the lower end of the sacral canal (at S4-S5)', 'The sacral hiatus is the gap at the lower end of the sacral canal, at about S4-S5, through which caudal anesthesia is administered. It is not at the upper end of the canal, nor is it simply a space between the sacrum and coccyx.'),
('f52055b1-1fc7-5fe8-acba-7381b7200933'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacral hiatus is bounded by:', '["The sacral cornua (inferior articular processes of S5)","The median sacral crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ala of the sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sacral promontory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral cornua (inferior articular processes of S5)', 'The sacral hiatus is flanked laterally by the sacral cornua, the inferior articular processes of S5. The median sacral crest, ala, and promontory are other sacral landmarks that do not bound the hiatus.'),
('9cc38a48-6b51-50e9-8b07-97d6d398fdfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Caudal anesthesia is administered via:', '["The thoracic interspace resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacral hiatus (into the sacral epidural space)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The lumbar interspace without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral hiatus (into the sacral epidural space)', 'Caudal anesthesia is given through the sacral hiatus into the sacral epidural space, often used for pediatric and perineal procedures. Thoracic and lumbar interspaces are used for other blocks, not caudal injection.'),
('77603e77-b724-56bc-81ab-f8c0f3d0f732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacral canal is the continuation of:', '["The coccygeal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The vertebral canal in the sacrum","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The lumbar spinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vertebral canal in the sacrum', 'The sacral canal is the continuation of the vertebral canal inside the sacrum, carrying the sacral nerve roots and the filum terminale. It is not the lumbar canal, and there is no separate coccygeal canal continuation.'),
('4e685839-9a41-5ee0-8a6f-481d799eddfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The anterior sacral foramina transmit:', '["Blood vessels only resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior (ventral) rami of the sacral nerves","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior (dorsal) rami of the sacral nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior (ventral) rami of the sacral nerves', 'The anterior sacral foramina transmit the ventral rami of the sacral nerves along with the lateral sacral arteries. The posterior rami exit through the dorsal foramina, and these foramina carry more than vessels alone.'),
('b8af2967-1f9d-5fc3-8901-f398d44063f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The posterior sacral foramina transmit:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior (dorsal) rami of the sacral nerves","Blood vessels only mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior rami of the sacral nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior (dorsal) rami of the sacral nerves', 'The posterior sacral foramina transmit the dorsal rami of the sacral nerves to supply the muscles and skin of the back. The ventral rami pass anteriorly through the anterior foramina, and these foramina carry more than vessels.'),
('dfd0ef39-278b-5769-ba16-9f2d04a15eca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacral promontory is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anterosuperior margin of the S1 vertebral body","The posterior margin of the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The anterior margin of S5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterosuperior margin of the S1 vertebral body', 'The sacral promontory is the projecting anterosuperior margin of the S1 vertebral body, an important obstetric landmark for pelvic diameter. The posterior margin of the sacrum and the anterior margin of S5 are different structures.'),
('0c25e4c7-f630-5ebe-aa2c-37431955c6f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacral ala (wing) is:', '["The lateral part of the sacrum (fused transverse processes)","The most posterior part of the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The central part of the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral part of the sacrum (fused transverse processes)', 'The sacral ala is the lateral wing of the sacrum formed by the fused transverse processes of S1 and adjoining elements, articulating with the ilium at the sacroiliac joint. It is not the posterior or central part of the bone.'),
('51281a98-a0ae-5f81-a224-7a534ba3533f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacroiliac joint is innervated by:', '["None","The femoral nerve","Branches of L4-S2 (sacral plexus)","The sciatic nerve","The obturator nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of L4-S2 (sacral plexus)', 'The sacroiliac joint is innervated by branches from the L4-S2 roots of the sacral plexus, including the lateral sacral nerves. The femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves do not supply the joint.'),
('ad8d57d3-c8f3-5dce-8f8d-126b18b4137a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments convert:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater and lesser sciatic notches into foramina","The inguinal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","The obturator foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater and lesser sciatic notches into foramina', 'The sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments bind the sacrum to the ischial spine and tuberosity, converting the greater and lesser sciatic notches into the corresponding foramina. They do not define the inguinal canal or obturator foramen.'),
('b618c16e-1dc6-573b-9a9c-70e7a0ae840a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The greater sciatic foramen transmits:', '["The piriformis, sciatic nerve, superior and inferior gluteal vessels/nerves, pudendal nerve, etc","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the pudendal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the gluteal vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The piriformis, sciatic nerve, superior and inferior gluteal vessels/nerves, pudendal nerve, etc', 'The greater sciatic foramen transmits the piriformis muscle, sciatic nerve, superior and inferior gluteal vessels and nerves, the pudendal nerve, and the internal pudendal vessels, among others. It carries more than any single listed structure, so only the composite grouping is complete.'),
('cf7d1e15-870e-5dd0-af07-162b3557620b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lesser sciatic foramen transmits:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The sciatic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gluteal nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tendon of obturator internus, pudendal nerve, and internal pudendal vessels"]'::jsonb, 'The tendon of obturator internus, pudendal nerve, and internal pudendal vessels', 'The lesser sciatic foramen transmits the tendon of the obturator internus, the pudendal nerve, and the internal pudendal vessels. The sciatic and gluteal nerves instead pass through the greater sciatic foramen.'),
('2c6748e8-391d-5e0b-85c1-82234cb8ed87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The piriformis muscle functions to:', '["Flex the hip resulting in decreased functional activity","Medially rotate the thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extend the hip mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laterally rotate the extended thigh and abduct the flexed thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally rotate the extended thigh and abduct the flexed thigh', 'The piriformis laterally rotates the extended thigh and abducts the flexed thigh at the hip. It does not flex or extend the hip, and medial rotation is performed by other muscles such as the gluteus medius and minimus.'),
('9eb947da-6490-58cd-be89-3a0f58dafd1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The piriformis originates from:', '["The anterior surface of the sacrum (S2-S4)","The posterior surface of the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ischium mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ilium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior surface of the sacrum (S2-S4)', 'The piriformis arises from the anterior (pelvic) surface of the sacrum at S2-S4 and exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen. It does not arise from the ischium or ilium, and it originates anteriorly rather than from the posterior sacrum.'),
('dc181279-b386-5871-a268-8008afb204e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sciatic nerve exits the pelvis through:', '["The greater sciatic foramen (below the piriformis)","The lesser sciatic foramen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The obturator foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (below the piriformis)', 'The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, passing below the piriformis muscle. The lesser sciatic foramen, obturator foramen, and inguinal canal are not its exit route.'),
('05e4f87b-21c4-514c-a86a-fe062a68df96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pudendal nerve exits the pelvis through:', '["The lesser sciatic foramen only resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater sciatic foramen only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater sciatic foramen and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen', 'The pudendal nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, crosses the sacrospinous ligament near the ischial spine, and then re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen to reach the perineum. It does not pass through only one of the two foramina.'),
('2b1b4f60-d0eb-5c19-9867-82efca8eb511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) supplies:', '["The lower limb resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The perineum, external genitalia, and the external anal/urethral sphincters","The abdomen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The back leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perineum, external genitalia, and the external anal/urethral sphincters', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) innervates the perineum, external genitalia, and the external anal and urethral sphincters, providing both sensory and motor supply. The lower limb, abdomen, and back receive innervation from other nerves.'),
('74e2ec1a-2f4a-5cf0-ad3b-3027fd018224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The pudendal nerve is blocked by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","An anesthetic near the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal anesthesia mediated by specific receptor activation","Epidural anesthesia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An anesthetic near the ischial spine (pudendal block)"]'::jsonb, 'An anesthetic near the ischial spine (pudendal block)', 'A pudendal nerve block is performed by injecting anesthetic near the ischial spine, where the nerve crosses the sacrospinous ligament. Spinal and epidural anesthesia do not target the nerve at this specific point, and injection near the sacrum is not the block site.'),
('1b9558b9-29b7-56ef-9208-454e2acd66f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm is a dome-shaped muscle that separates:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity","The thorax from the pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","The abdominal cavity from the pelvic cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity', 'The diaphragm is the dome-shaped partition between the thoracic and abdominal cavities and is the primary muscle of respiration. It does not separate the thorax from the pelvis or the abdomen from the pelvis.'),
('b8f40af0-bec2-59c3-aee1-fd234da535d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm has three parts:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sternal and costal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a single part mediated by specific receptor activation","Only costal and lumbar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sternal, costal, and lumbar (with right and left crura)"]'::jsonb, 'Sternal, costal, and lumbar (with right and left crura)', 'The diaphragm has three parts of origin: the sternal part from the xiphoid process, the costal part from the lower ribs, and the lumbar part from the crura and arcuate ligaments. It is not a single part or limited to only two of these regions.'),
('d8a9da03-240c-55de-a0cc-a547d67e6311'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sternal part of the diaphragm arises from:', '["The posterior surface of the xiphoid process","The costal cartilages of the lower ribs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lumbar vertebrae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior surface of the xiphoid process', 'The sternal part of the diaphragm arises from the posterior surface of the xiphoid process. The costal cartilages of the lower ribs and the lumbar vertebrae instead give origin to the costal and lumbar parts, respectively.'),
('2107252f-7c1d-52f7-a380-91a1b0b67350'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The costal part of the diaphragm arises from:', '["The costal cartilages of the lower 6 ribs (ribs 7-12)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The xiphoid process mediated by specific receptor activation","The lumbar vertebrae without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The costal cartilages of the lower 6 ribs (ribs 7-12)', 'The costal part arises from the inner surfaces of the costal cartilages of the lower six ribs (7-12). The xiphoid process and lumbar vertebrae give origin to the sternal and lumbar parts instead.'),
('0b459a0f-136c-5d7f-976a-7d4d03e8ea1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar part of the diaphragm arises from:', '["The xiphoid process resulting in decreased functional activity","The costal cartilages of the lower ribs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lumbar vertebrae (via the right and left crura) and from the arcuate ligaments"]'::jsonb, 'The lumbar vertebrae (via the right and left crura) and from the arcuate ligaments', 'The lumbar part arises from the lumbar vertebrae through the right and left crura and from the medial and lateral arcuate ligaments. The xiphoid process and lower costal cartilages form the sternal and costal parts instead.'),
('41405e1b-b805-5dc8-9632-8685f7366bde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right crus of the diaphragm arises from:', '["The bodies of T12-L2 resulting in decreased functional activity","The bodies of L1-L4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bodies of L1-L3 (and intervertebral discs)","The bodies of L1-L2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bodies of L1-L3 (and intervertebral discs)', 'The right crus arises from the vertebral bodies and intervertebral discs of L1-L3 and is longer than the left crus. The ranges T12-L2, L1-L2, and L1-L4 do not give the correct extent of its attachment.'),
('1e956b94-7fd8-5181-9c04-ee3132247759'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left crus of the diaphragm arises from:', '["None","The bodies of T12-L1","The bodies of L1-L2","The bodies of L1-L3","The bodies of L1-L4"]'::jsonb, 'The bodies of L1-L2', 'The left crus arises from the vertebral bodies of L1-L2 and is smaller and shorter than the right crus. The ranges L1-L3 and L1-L4 exceed its extent, and T12-L1 is too high.'),
('2c6b7115-8e85-5d72-add1-452b7d9a871e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm is the most important muscle of:', '["Defecation","None","Expiration","Micturition","Inspiration"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiration', 'The diaphragm is the principal muscle of inspiration because its contraction enlarges the vertical dimension of the thoracic cavity. Expiration at rest is largely passive, and defecation and micturition rely mainly on the abdominal muscles.'),
('25de7634-b894-50ac-a109-d552f7d1c531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'During inspiration, the diaphragm contracts and:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Flattens (descends), increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity","Elevates (ascends), decreasing the volume of the thoracic cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Remains stationary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flattens (descends), increasing the volume of the thoracic cavity', 'During inspiration the diaphragm contracts and flattens, descending inferiorly to increase the thoracic cavity volume and lower intrathoracic pressure, drawing air into the lungs. Ascending or remaining stationary would instead reduce or leave the cavity unchanged.'),
('55b5d990-6747-5ac5-9044-fdf3d85d6196'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'During expiration, the diaphragm:', '["Remains stationary resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contracts and descends mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Relaxes and ascends (returns to its dome shape)"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxes and ascends (returns to its dome shape)', 'During expiration the diaphragm relaxes and ascends back to its dome shape, shrinking the thoracic cavity and raising intrathoracic pressure to expel air. Contracting and descending is an inspiratory action, and the diaphragm does not remain stationary.'),
('15bf8894-310f-5f59-bffb-832a5829917e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm has a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Central tendon (a trefoil-shaped aponeurosis)","Central bony portion mediated by specific receptor activation","Central muscular portion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central tendon (a trefoil-shaped aponeurosis)', 'The muscular fibers of the diaphragm converge on a central tendon, a trefoil-shaped aponeurosis at its center. The diaphragm contains no bony portion at its center, and the option that is only muscular overlooks the aponeurotic tendon.'),
('7b2e7d6f-d8c8-51ae-8d18-71b5f6086607'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The central tendon of the diaphragm is shaped like:', '["A three-leaf clover (trefoil)","A triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A rectangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A circle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A three-leaf clover (trefoil)', 'The central tendon of the diaphragm has a trefoil shape, resembling a three-leaf clover with three leaf-like extensions. It is not triangular, rectangular, or circular in outline.'),
('ac3bf887-c148-5ef3-803b-d502d842b4df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The central tendon is fused with:', '["The peritoneum (inferiorly) resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pleura (superiorly) mediated by specific receptor activation","The fibrous pericardium (superiorly)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fibrous pericardium (superiorly)', 'The central tendon of the diaphragm is fused superiorly with the fibrous pericardium, forming the floor of the pericardial cavity. The pleura and peritoneum lie on the superior and inferior surfaces but are not the structures fused to the tendon.'),
('436f3da1-45aa-5bd2-b4b0-35e968189eff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right phrenic nerve pierces the diaphragm:', '["With the aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","With the esophagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Separately mediated by specific receptor activation","With (or near) the IVC at the caval opening","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'With (or near) the IVC at the caval opening', 'The right phrenic nerve descends beside the IVC and pierces the diaphragm at or near the caval opening at T8. The left phrenic nerve pierces the muscular left dome separately, while the aorta and esophagus pass through their own openings at T12 and T10 respectively.'),
('5b1467ae-4d25-53d2-8ef9-e38330224b8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The phrenic nerves (C3-C5) also supply sensory fibers to:', '["The peripheral part of the diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","The central part of the diaphragm (the peripheral part is supplied by the intercostal nerves)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The crura of the diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central part of the diaphragm (the peripheral part is supplied by the intercostal nerves)', 'The phrenic nerve supplies sensory fibers to the central part of the diaphragm, whereas the peripheral part is supplied by the lower intercostal nerves (T7-T11). This explains why diaphragmatic irritation is referred to the shoulder tip (C3-C5 dermatomes).'),
('559f93aa-30e5-58d4-82b4-22a87969dd15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The phrenic nerve is the sole motor nerve to:', '["The intercostal muscles","The pelvic floor muscles","None","The abdominal muscles","The diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'The diaphragm', 'The phrenic nerve (C3-C5) provides the only motor supply to the diaphragm, making it essential for breathing. The intercostal and abdominal muscles receive their motor supply from intercostal and lumbar nerves, not the phrenic nerve.'),
('4ecf7d6c-6cb6-5c30-b020-ec639adc0745'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Paralysis of the diaphragm can be detected by:', '["A chest X-ray (elevation of the hemidiaphragm) resulting in decreased functional activity","Ultrasound (paradoxical movement of the diaphragm during inspiration)","A CT scan mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound (paradoxical movement of the diaphragm during inspiration)', 'Ultrasound directly visualizes the paradoxical upward movement of the paralyzed hemidiaphragm during inspiration, making it the key diagnostic finding. While a chest X-ray or CT may show an elevated hemidiaphragm, neither is specific for paralysis.'),
('9240adb8-bfab-5756-928f-4e6ab72eb6de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'A diaphragmatic rupture (e.g., from trauma) can result in:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A hernia into the inguinal region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A diaphragmatic hernia (abdominal contents herniate into the thoracic cavity)","A hernia into the pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A diaphragmatic hernia (abdominal contents herniate into the thoracic cavity)', 'Trauma can tear the diaphragm, allowing abdominal organs to pass into the thoracic cavity and produce a diaphragmatic hernia. Inguinal or pelvic hernias are unrelated to a diaphragmatic defect.'),
('920b997b-f989-52d2-9f78-c25a24b7c70e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Most diaphragmatic ruptures occur on the:', '["Left side (the right side is protected by the liver)","Right side accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both sides equally mediated by specific receptor activation","The central tendon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left side (the right side is protected by the liver)', 'The liver cushions and supports the right hemidiaphragm, so the left side is far more vulnerable to traumatic rupture. Rupture through the central tendon alone is uncommon.'),
('75ba2075-4a69-556f-a0e6-c649c2d2ec0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'A congenital diaphragmatic hernia (Bochdalek) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A defect in the posterolateral diaphragm (pleuroperitoneal canal)","A defect in the central tendon mediated by specific receptor activation","A defect in the anterior diaphragm (Morgagni) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A defect in the posterolateral diaphragm (pleuroperitoneal canal)', 'A Bochdalek hernia results from failure of the pleuroperitoneal canal to close, leaving a posterolateral defect. A Morgagni hernia is anterior and a central tendon defect is not a recognized congenital hernia.'),
('1d75fb4d-ff73-5580-8e29-0c72870594b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Bochdalek hernia is usually on the:', '["Midline resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right side mediated by specific receptor activation","Left side (and is more common in males)","Both sides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left side (and is more common in males)', 'The pleuroperitoneal canal closes on the right before the left, so the left posterolateral defect predominates. It also shows a male preponderance.'),
('fd2f237d-7f24-52c4-9b3f-7db7850e6ae1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Bochdalek hernia presents with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory distress in the newborn (abdominal contents in the thoracic cavity)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Abdominal pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chest pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory distress in the newborn (abdominal contents in the thoracic cavity)', 'In Bochdalek hernia, abdominal organs herniate into the thorax and compress the developing lungs, causing severe respiratory distress in the newborn. Abdominal or chest pain are not typical neonatal presentations.'),
('f917db55-053a-54e8-99ab-d7ae53ad5eec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Morgagni hernia is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A defect in the posterolateral diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A defect in the central tendon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A defect in the anterior diaphragm (sternocostal triangle) - usually right-sided"]'::jsonb, 'A defect in the anterior diaphragm (sternocostal triangle) - usually right-sided', 'A Morgagni hernia is a congenital defect at the anterior sternocostal triangle and is usually right-sided. A posterolateral defect, by contrast, describes a Bochdalek hernia.'),
('508aaea8-9ac8-5580-ab8f-8fcc8feb076c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Morgagni hernia presents:', '["In the newborn resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In childhood mediated by specific receptor activation","Later in life (often asymptomatic, may cause vague symptoms)","In infancy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Later in life (often asymptomatic, may cause vague symptoms)', 'The anterior defect is small and often plugged by omentum, so Morgagni hernias usually remain asymptomatic and are discovered later in life, sometimes with vague chest or gastrointestinal complaints.'),
('615a038e-2a49-5288-99ac-150ecb597749'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm receives its blood supply from:', '["Only the phrenic arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the musculophrenic arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pericardiacophrenic, musculophrenic, superior phrenic, and inferior phrenic arteries","Only the pericardiacophrenic arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pericardiacophrenic, musculophrenic, superior phrenic, and inferior phrenic arteries', 'The diaphragm is supplied by several arteries: the pericardiacophrenic and musculophrenic branches of the internal thoracic artery, the superior phrenic arteries from the thoracic aorta, and the inferior phrenic arteries from the abdominal aorta. No single artery supplies the diaphragm alone.'),
('a0d903ce-7b14-5821-a057-ab1e33775ff8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inferior phrenic arteries arise from:', '["The SMA resulting in decreased functional activity","The aorta (just above the celiac trunk)","The celiac trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The IMA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The aorta (just above the celiac trunk)', 'The inferior phrenic arteries are the first paired branches of the abdominal aorta, arising just above the celiac trunk, and they supply the inferior surface of the diaphragm.'),
('9855935e-a0dd-58b6-b1ea-ce9f6be34bba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The superior phrenic arteries arise from:', '["None","The thoracic aorta","The celiac trunk","The SMA","The abdominal aorta"]'::jsonb, 'The thoracic aorta', 'The superior phrenic arteries arise from the lower thoracic aorta and supply the superior surface of the diaphragm. Branches of the celiac trunk and abdominal aorta supply the inferior surface instead.'),
('d5c80547-114f-507e-926a-77bf7a1a4995'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragmatic lymph nodes drain into:', '["Only the mediastinal nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","The axillary nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the para-aortic nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","The mediastinal (superiorly) and the para-aortic (inferiorly) nodes","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mediastinal (superiorly) and the para-aortic (inferiorly) nodes', 'Lymphatics from the superior surface of the diaphragm drain to the mediastinal nodes, while those from the inferior surface drain to the para-aortic nodes. The axillary nodes play no role in diaphragmatic drainage.'),
('6ec8a9a2-dd48-5033-abd5-0ad5949d48e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm is formed by the fusion of:', '["Only the pleuroperitoneal membranes resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the body wall mediated by specific receptor activation","The septum transversum, the pleuroperitoneal membranes, the body wall, and the dorsal mesentery of the esophagus","Only the septum transversum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The septum transversum, the pleuroperitoneal membranes, the body wall, and the dorsal mesentery of the esophagus', 'The diaphragm develops from four components: the septum transversum (central tendon), the two pleuroperitoneal membranes (posterolateral parts), the body wall (peripheral rim), and the dorsal mesentery of the esophagus (crura). No single component forms the entire diaphragm.'),
('5f7d9869-c036-573d-94aa-fcc3789d5863'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm separates the thorax from the abdomen and has openings for:', '["The IVC (T8), the esophagus (T10), and the aorta (T12)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the esophagus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC (T8), the esophagus (T10), and the aorta (T12)', 'The three major openings of the diaphragm are the caval opening at T8, the esophageal hiatus at T10, and the aortic hiatus at T12. The diaphragm also has minor openings for other structures, but the T8/T10/T12 sequence defines its principal openings.'),
('cacc6623-0db6-563c-aac0-190dc168dbdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sternocostal triangle (of Morgagni) is:', '["A small gap between the sternal and costal fibers of the diaphragm","A gap between the esophageal and aortic openings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A gap between the costal and lumbar fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small gap between the sternal and costal fibers of the diaphragm', 'The sternocostal triangle is a small defect between the sternal and costal fibers of the diaphragm through which the internal thoracic (superior epigastric) vessels pass. The gap between costal and lumbar fibers is the lumbocostal triangle of Bochdalek.'),
('c12e96da-eff7-556b-9de9-805015af09d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbocostal triangle (of Bochdalek) is:', '["A gap between the esophageal and aortic openings resulting in decreased functional activity","A gap between the sternal and costal fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small gap between the costal and lumbar fibers of the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'A small gap between the costal and lumbar fibers of the diaphragm', 'The lumbocostal triangle (foramen of Bochdalek) is a small gap between the costal and lumbar parts of the diaphragm. If it fails to close, a Bochdalek hernia results.'),
('fe4ca2d0-44d6-575f-a00e-ca3500471658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The median arcuate ligament can sometimes compress:', '["The IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","The celiac trunk (median arcuate ligament syndrome)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The esophagus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The celiac trunk (median arcuate ligament syndrome)', 'When the median arcuate ligament lies unusually low, it compresses the celiac trunk just after its origin, producing median arcuate ligament syndrome (MALS) with postprandial abdominal pain. It does not compress the IVC, aorta, or esophagus.'),
('2ea7d6aa-5129-5fc2-8bb6-78aea56edaff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The central tendon of the diaphragm is the:', '["Capsule of the diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aponeurosis of the diaphragm (a strong, flat, trefoil-shaped tendon)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscular portion of the diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aponeurosis of the diaphragm (a strong, flat, trefoil-shaped tendon)', 'The central tendon is a strong, flat, trefoil-shaped aponeurosis at the center of the diaphragm into which the muscle fibers insert. It is not a capsule, and the surrounding muscular portion lies peripheral to it.'),
('3b52eea8-ce04-5a82-99d5-eb237ac5a2a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm is innervated by the phrenic nerve (C3-C5). The sensory innervation of the peripheral diaphragm is from:', '["The vagus nerve","None","The femoral nerve","The intercostal nerves (T7-T11)","The phrenic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The intercostal nerves (T7-T11)', 'The central part of the diaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve, while the peripheral part receives sensory fibers from the lower intercostal nerves (T7-T11). The phrenic nerve does not supply the periphery, and the vagus and femoral nerves are not involved.'),
('8884b5d5-a10e-5953-81d3-a9c62e088e48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The posterior abdominal wall also includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the quadratus lumborum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the iliacus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the psoas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The quadratus lumborum, iliacus, psoas major, and psoas minor"]'::jsonb, 'The quadratus lumborum, iliacus, psoas major, and psoas minor', 'The posterior abdominal wall is formed by the quadratus lumborum, iliacus, psoas major, and psoas minor muscles, along with the diaphragm above and several fascias. Each listed muscle alone is only one component of the wall.'),
('88e8c3e1-2029-5e79-94dc-0c4761e1a775'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor arises from:', '["The iliac fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","The bodies of L2-L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bodies of T12 and L1 vertebrae and the intervertebral disc","The quadratus lumborum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bodies of T12 and L1 vertebrae and the intervertebral disc', 'The psoas minor arises from the bodies of the T12 and L1 vertebrae and the intervening intervertebral disc. The iliac fossa is the origin of the iliacus, and L2-L3 is not the psoas minor origin.'),
('fe63b54f-6c4f-542e-bd0c-8aadf1a4cc07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor inserts into:', '["The iliopubic eminence (arcuate line of the ilium)","The iliac crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater trochanter mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lesser trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The iliopubic eminence (arcuate line of the ilium)', 'The psoas minor inserts into the iliopubic eminence at the arcuate line of the ilium. The lesser trochanter is the insertion of the psoas major, not of the psoas minor.'),
('83efebce-3ce8-56f9-8bdd-38087afc990b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor flexes the:', '["Thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hip mediated by specific receptor activation","Knee without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trunk (weak flexor)"]'::jsonb, 'Trunk (weak flexor)', 'Because it spans only the lumbar spine, the psoas minor acts as a weak flexor of the trunk. Flexion of the hip is the role of the psoas major and iliacus, which cross the hip joint.'),
('b071068f-5e56-5525-abbb-cf71b844e8ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The subcostal vessels and nerve run:', '["Above the 12th rib","Below the 12th rib","All","Between the 11th and 12th ribs","None"]'::jsonb, 'Below the 12th rib', 'The subcostal nerve and vessels run below the 12th rib, representing the caudal continuation of the 12th thoracic neurovascular bundle. They are not found above the rib or between the 11th and 12th ribs.'),
('35d84c7f-39d9-55ac-a68e-c0f5329a8009'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The superior cluneal nerves arise from:', '["The anterior rami of L1-L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","The lumbar plexus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior rami of L1-L3 (supply the skin of the upper buttock)","The sacral plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior rami of L1-L3 (supply the skin of the upper buttock)', 'The superior cluneal nerves arise from the posterior rami of L1-L3 and supply the skin of the upper part of the buttock. The lumbar plexus is formed by anterior rami and is not their source.'),
('47f9e227-52de-5ba4-a0f5-2e40831717d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The middle cluneal nerves arise from:', '["The sacral plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior rami of S1-S3 (supply the skin of the mid-buttock)","The lumbar plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior rami of L1-L3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior rami of S1-S3 (supply the skin of the mid-buttock)', 'The middle cluneal nerves arise from the posterior rami of S1-S3 and supply the skin of the mid-buttock. The posterior rami of L1-L3 give rise to the superior cluneal nerves instead.'),
('f75882c7-6f5f-5da7-b613-c1bd21d7129a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The inferior cluneal nerves arise from:', '["The posterior rami of S1-S3 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh (from the sacral plexus)","The posterior rami of L1-L3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lumbar plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh (from the sacral plexus)', 'The inferior cluneal nerves arise from branches of the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh, which is derived from the sacral plexus, and they supply the skin of the lower buttock. The posterior rami of S1-S3 and L1-L3 give rise to the middle and superior cluneal nerves respectively.'),
('5089327c-a5b1-5cb4-93a6-449c5e8c0cfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Alcock canal (pudendal canal) contains:', '["The obturator nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pudendal nerve and the internal pudendal vessels"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve and the internal pudendal vessels', 'Alcock canal transmits the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels on the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa. The obturator, femoral, and sciatic nerves take different courses and do not pass through it.'),
('0075fc50-1e65-5e70-aea8-a0c5317fdbb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Alcock canal is located:', '["In the ischiorectal fossa (in the fascia of the obturator internus)","In the popliteal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the femoral triangle mediated by specific receptor activation","In the inguinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the ischiorectal fossa (in the fascia of the obturator internus)', 'Alcock canal lies on the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa, formed by a split in the fascia of the obturator internus. The popliteal fossa, femoral triangle, and inguinal canal are unrelated locations.'),
('11fe581e-f805-58af-b972-e1fa1c83d1c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The posterior abdominal wall is lined by:', '["The pleura resulting in decreased functional activity","The transversalis fascia (endoabdominal fascia)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The peritoneum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transversalis fascia (endoabdominal fascia)', 'The deep surface of the posterior abdominal wall is lined internally by the transversalis fascia, which belongs to the endoabdominal fascia. The peritoneum lies superficial to this fascia, and the pleura lines only the thoracic cavity.'),
('770d0227-4109-5538-a95d-35ff30d93b32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ureter is a:', '["Retroperitoneal structure that runs from the kidney to the urinary bladder","Thoracic structure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intraperitoneal structure mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pelvic structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retroperitoneal structure that runs from the kidney to the urinary bladder', 'The ureter is a retroperitoneal muscular tube that carries urine from the kidney to the urinary bladder. It is neither intraperitoneal, thoracic, nor confined to the pelvis in its entirety.'),
('c69e71c2-97e1-5b52-bf80-0fd0baf5d29c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ureter is about:', '["None","20 cm","15 cm","10 cm","25-30 cm long"]'::jsonb, '25-30 cm long', 'In adults the ureter measures approximately 25-30 cm from the renal pelvis to the bladder. The shorter lengths listed are incorrect measurements.'),
('aae182d2-bd9a-5c36-a710-adf1df901592'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ureter has three narrowings:', '["Only at the pelvic brim resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the pelviureteric junction, at the pelvic brim (crossover of the iliac vessels), and at the ureterovesical junction","Only at the pelviureteric junction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only at the ureterovesical junction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the pelviureteric junction, at the pelvic brim (crossover of the iliac vessels), and at the ureterovesical junction', 'The ureter narrows at three sites: the pelviureteric junction, the pelvic brim where it crosses the iliac vessels, and the ureterovesical junction. These three points are where ureteric calculi commonly become impacted, so a single narrowing cannot be correct.'),
('04862bfd-cbb3-598d-b739-65ea2a23c396'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The most common site for ureteric stone impaction is:', '["The pelviureteric junction (PUJ)","All","The pelvic brim (crossover of the iliac vessels)","The ureterovesical junction (UVJ)","None"]'::jsonb, 'The ureterovesical junction (UVJ)', 'The ureterovesical junction is the narrowest part of the ureter because it passes obliquely through the bladder wall, so stones most often impact there. The PUJ and pelvic brim are secondary sites.'),
('64ab2025-4184-5590-aac3-c133f1c31fae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ureter is supplied by:', '["Only the gonadal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Branches from the renal, gonadal, common iliac, and superior/inferior vesical arteries","Only the renal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Branches from the renal, gonadal, common iliac, and superior/inferior vesical arteries', 'The ureter receives segmental blood supply along its course from the renal, gonadal, common iliac, and superior and inferior vesical arteries. Because the supply is segmental, no single artery provides it alone.'),
('652b43d7-5ff0-544a-a76e-02827e4c791e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumen of the ureter is lined by:', '["None","Stratified squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified epithelium","Columnar epithelium","Transitional epithelium (urothelium)"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium (urothelium)', 'The ureter is lined by transitional epithelium (urothelium), which stretches to accommodate urine flow. Stratified squamous, columnar, and pseudostratified epithelia line other sites, not the ureter.'),
('75f7e0ec-da4e-53eb-8f97-9e72627cf812'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The muscular wall of the ureter has:', '["One layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Four layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Three layers of smooth muscle (inner longitudinal, middle circular, outer longitudinal)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two layers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Three layers of smooth muscle (inner longitudinal, middle circular, outer longitudinal)', 'The ureteral wall contains three smooth muscle layers (inner longitudinal, middle circular, and outer longitudinal) that generate peristalsis. One-, two-, or four-layer descriptions do not match the true arrangement.'),
('d507070a-1b46-507e-a503-4c28e09f9119'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Peristalsis in the ureter propels urine from:', '["The renal pelvis to the urinary bladder","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The kidney to the ureter mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The bladder to the urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The renal pelvis to the urinary bladder', 'Peristaltic waves begin in the renal pelvis and carry urine down the ureter into the urinary bladder. Flow in the opposite direction is reflux, not normal peristalsis.'),
('ceee893b-62da-5199-ad65-db0f05694afa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The ureter enters the bladder at:', '["The ureterovesical junction (obliquely through the bladder wall)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The trigone of the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","The base of the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ureterovesical junction (obliquely through the bladder wall)', 'Each ureter enters the bladder obliquely through its wall at the ureterovesical junction. The oblique intramural course is what creates the valve-like anti-reflux mechanism.'),
('f0c07867-34d3-57bb-84f1-d6978023cc17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The oblique course of the ureter through the bladder wall:', '["Allows free reflux","Prevents vesicoureteric reflux","All","None","Prevents stone passage"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents vesicoureteric reflux', 'The oblique passage of the ureter through the bladder wall acts as a one-way valve: as the bladder fills and distends, the intramural ureter is compressed, preventing reflux of urine back into the ureter. It does not allow free reflux or prevent stone passage.'),
('44ebc80f-1aad-55c4-9f3e-aa34f604b8f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Vesicoureteric reflux (VUR) is:', '["None","All","The antegrade flow of urine from the ureter to the bladder","The retrograde flow of urine from the ureter to the kidney","The retrograde flow of urine from the bladder into the ureter"]'::jsonb, 'The retrograde flow of urine from the bladder into the ureter', 'Vesicoureteric reflux is the abnormal backward flow of urine from the bladder into the ureter, which predisposes to urinary tract infection and renal scarring. Normal antegrade flow from the ureter to the bladder is not reflux.'),
('881ed5a6-74e5-5627-aa02-11b3004c2874'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The kidney lies in the:', '["None","Intraperitoneal space","Anterior pararenal space","Perirenal space (within Gerota fascia)","Posterior pararenal space"]'::jsonb, 'Perirenal space (within Gerota fascia)', 'Each kidney lies within the perirenal space enclosed by Gerota (renal) fascia and filled with perirenal fat. The anterior and posterior pararenal spaces and the intraperitoneal space are separate compartments.'),
('6854e4dd-1d8b-554c-8f7e-7a97f6f96aab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The kidney is about:', '["5 cm long resulting in decreased functional activity","15 cm long accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","10-12 cm long, 5-6 cm wide, and 3-4 cm thick","8 cm long leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10-12 cm long, 5-6 cm wide, and 3-4 cm thick', 'The adult kidney measures approximately 10-12 cm long, 5-6 cm wide, and 3-4 cm thick. The much shorter lengths listed do not match the normal dimensions.'),
('e79fdbe6-aa54-53a8-8ec7-d1df7eb001be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right kidney lies slightly lower than the left kidney:', '["True (due to the presence of the liver above it)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","They are at the same level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","False leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'True (due to the presence of the liver above it)', 'The right kidney sits slightly lower than the left because the large right lobe of the liver occupies space above it and pushes it inferiorly. The statement is therefore true.'),
('450cbb61-e95f-5efe-91ea-fb7c0ad76cfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The kidney has:', '["capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","Only a fibrous capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only perirenal fat mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibrous capsule (true capsule) and a perirenal fat (paranephric fat)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous capsule (true capsule) and a perirenal fat (paranephric fat)', 'The kidney is covered by a thin fibrous true capsule and surrounded by perirenal fat, which cushions it within the renal fascia. Having only a capsule or only fat does not describe the complete covering.'),
('64941e53-ad79-517a-9246-7e9d22ad6006'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The suprarenal glands (adrenal glands) lie:', '["At the lower pole of each kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior to the kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the upper pole of each kidney (within the perirenal space)","Anterior to the kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the upper pole of each kidney (within the perirenal space)', 'Each suprarenal gland caps the upper pole of its kidney within the perirenal space (Gerota fascia). They are not located at the lower pole or posterior or anterior to the kidney.'),
('69112900-1660-55f5-9b6a-0989ecf37c30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right suprarenal gland is:', '["Semilunar in shape","Round","Pyramidal in shape","Oval","None"]'::jsonb, 'Pyramidal in shape', 'The right suprarenal gland is pyramidal and rests on the upper pole of the right kidney posterior to the IVC. The left suprarenal gland is semilunar, and round or oval shapes are not correct descriptions.'),
('cbff270b-e235-5ba6-8354-3ff56aea0664'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left suprarenal gland is:', '["Pyramidal resulting in decreased functional activity","Round accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oval mediated by specific receptor activation","Semilunar (crescentic) in shape","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Semilunar (crescentic) in shape', 'The left suprarenal gland is semilunar or crescentic in outline and lies slightly lower than the right. The right gland, by contrast, is more pyramidal.'),
('4bfd977c-675f-59cf-9bee-a07ad250bf5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The suprarenal gland has:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only a medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","A single layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cortex (mesodermal origin) and a medulla (neural crest origin)"]'::jsonb, 'A cortex (mesodermal origin) and a medulla (neural crest origin)', 'The suprarenal gland is composed of two functionally distinct parts. The cortex derives from mesoderm and secretes steroid hormones, whereas the medulla derives from neural crest tissue and secretes catecholamines.'),
('d7b1b33e-b12a-5cf8-9616-b1ed63483cb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The adrenal cortex secretes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only glucocorticoids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine) mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticosteroids (glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, and sex hormones)","Only mineralocorticoids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticosteroids (glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, and sex hormones)', 'The adrenal cortex produces three classes of steroid hormones: glucocorticoids, mineralocorticoids, and androgens. Catecholamines are not made by the cortex but by the medulla.'),
('2ccee21c-ef4d-5f87-beb3-7047cdb48ebc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The adrenal medulla secretes:', '["Catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine)","Corticosteroids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only epinephrine mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only norepinephrine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine)', 'The adrenal medulla synthesizes catecholamines, chiefly epinephrine and norepinephrine, which mediate the fight-or-flight response. Corticosteroids are produced exclusively by the cortex.'),
('3b990baa-28ee-5f88-b040-28e3da0a7f30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The suprarenal glands are supplied by:', '["The superior suprarenal (from the inferior phrenic), middle suprarenal (from the aorta), and inferior suprarenal (from the renal) arteries","Only the middle suprarenal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the superior suprarenal mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the inferior suprarenal without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superior suprarenal (from the inferior phrenic), middle suprarenal (from the aorta), and inferior suprarenal (from the renal) arteries', 'Each suprarenal gland receives a triple arterial supply from three sources. The superior suprarenal artery arises from the inferior phrenic, the middle from the abdominal aorta, and the inferior from the renal artery.'),
('772f1e32-6f5f-5bca-bbc4-bc27a5a60d66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The kidney has a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A single layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex (glomeruli and tubules) and a medulla (pyramids and collecting ducts)","Only a cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex (glomeruli and tubules) and a medulla (pyramids and collecting ducts)', 'The kidney is divided into an outer cortex, containing glomeruli and convoluted tubules, and an inner medulla, containing pyramids and collecting ducts. Both parts are needed for urine formation.'),
('5a0e6d62-b9f2-5a78-a406-a6efda19940d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The functional unit of the kidney is:', '["The nephron (glomerulus + tubule)","The collecting duct","The tubule alone","The glomerulus alone","None"]'::jsonb, 'The nephron (glomerulus + tubule)', 'The nephron, made up of the glomerulus and its attached tubule, is the structural and functional unit of the kidney. The collecting duct, though important, is not part of the nephron.'),
('55282c9e-5f1f-50a8-96a1-1ea1e0ed764c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The glomerulus is a:', '["Blood vessel resulting in decreased functional activity","Capillary tuft that filters blood to produce glomerular filtrate","Tubule mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Collecting duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary tuft that filters blood to produce glomerular filtrate', 'The glomerulus is a tuft of capillaries enclosed within Bowman''s capsule. Blood filtered across it forms glomerular filtrate, the first step of urine production.'),
('c218de4d-cba6-52d4-8ea1-b36878c64957'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The renal artery arises from:', '["The SMA resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The abdominal aorta (at L1-L2, below the SMA)","The celiac trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The IMA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The abdominal aorta (at L1-L2, below the SMA)', 'The renal arteries branch from the lateral aspect of the abdominal aorta near the L1-L2 level, just below the superior mesenteric artery. The celiac trunk and the SMA supply other abdominal viscera.'),
('a4c80bcd-0832-548b-b44f-725a7c1b7256'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The right renal artery is longer and passes:', '["To the left kidney","Posterior to the IVC","None","Through the IVC","Anterior to the IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the IVC', 'Because the aorta lies left of the midline, the right renal artery is longer and runs behind the inferior vena cava to reach the right kidney. It never passes through the IVC.'),
('56634f16-9e5b-51d5-859c-f809bf6b347a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left renal vein passes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","To the right kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to the aorta (between the aorta and the SMA)","Downward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior to the aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the aorta (between the aorta and the SMA)', 'The left renal vein is long and crosses anterior to the aorta, passing between the aorta and the SMA before joining the IVC. The shorter right renal vein passes directly to the IVC.'),
('e472e8a8-6912-5500-8460-706faed0180e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The left renal vein receives:', '["The right suprarenal and right gonadal veins resulting in decreased functional activity","The left suprarenal and left gonadal veins","tributaries mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left suprarenal and left gonadal veins', 'On the left, the suprarenal and gonadal veins drain into the left renal vein. On the right, both veins open directly into the inferior vena cava.'),
('98375de3-9a01-592e-85bc-05a4c4bd8b8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The kidney is innervated by:', '["Only parasympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatic nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The autonomic nervous system (sympathetic from the celiac and aorticorenal ganglia, and parasympathetic from the vagus)"]'::jsonb, 'The autonomic nervous system (sympathetic from the celiac and aorticorenal ganglia, and parasympathetic from the vagus)', 'The kidney receives autonomic innervation, with sympathetic fibers from the celiac and aorticorenal ganglia and parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerve. Its nerves are vasomotor and visceral, not somatic.'),
('ce7b49fb-498c-5fab-bc5e-6460b746ea2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The kidneys are located at:', '["L3-L5 resulting in decreased functional activity","T10-L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12-L3 (left kidney higher than the right)","L1-L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T12-L3 (left kidney higher than the right)', 'The kidneys lie retroperitoneally at the T12-L3 level on either side of the aorta. The left kidney sits slightly higher than the right because the right is depressed by the liver.'),
('12aa30c6-1e0a-592c-b42c-e32fa31912ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The 12th rib crosses the left kidney at:', '["The lower pole resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The middle of the kidney (upper half for the right kidney)","The hilum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The upper pole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The middle of the kidney (upper half for the right kidney)', 'The 12th rib crosses the middle of the left kidney but crosses the upper part of the right kidney. This difference results from the right kidney lying lower beneath the liver.'),
('df05a09f-7cad-5d91-95ed-b1f20a1384fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The kidney is in contact with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the quadratus lumborum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The diaphragm (posterosuperiorly), the psoas (medially), the quadratus lumborum (posteriorly), and the transversus abdominis (posterolaterally)","Only the psoas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The diaphragm (posterosuperiorly), the psoas (medially), the quadratus lumborum (posteriorly), and the transversus abdominis (posterolaterally)', 'The posterior surface of each kidney rests on four muscles: the diaphragm posterosuperiorly, the psoas medially, the quadratus lumborum posteriorly, and the transversus abdominis posterolaterally. Together they form the renal bed.'),
('3db5cfb0-bd68-54da-a7f6-41afb27e0715'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The anterior relations of the right kidney include:', '["Only the duodenum resulting in decreased functional activity","The stomach, spleen, pancreas, and left colic flexure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The liver, duodenum, right colic flexure, and small intestine","Only the liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The liver, duodenum, right colic flexure, and small intestine', 'Anteriorly the right kidney is related to the liver, the second part of the duodenum, the right colic flexure, and coils of small intestine. The stomach and spleen are anterior relations of the left kidney, not the right.'),
('251e4bd7-203f-5179-ba8a-76585bbbf396'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The anterior relations of the left kidney include:', '["The stomach, spleen, pancreas, left colic flexure, and small intestine","The liver, duodenum, right colic flexure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The stomach, spleen, pancreas, left colic flexure, and small intestine', 'Anteriorly the left kidney is related to the stomach, spleen, pancreas, left colic flexure, and loops of small intestine. The liver and duodenum are anterior relations of the right kidney.'),
('104c92ac-ab9a-52a2-b70c-dc9359ea24b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The renal pelvis is the:', '["Funnel-shaped dilation at the upper end of the ureter that receives urine from the collecting ducts","A part of the kidney parenchyma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the ureter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Funnel-shaped dilation at the upper end of the ureter that receives urine from the collecting ducts', 'The renal pelvis is a funnel-shaped expansion at the upper end of the ureter, lying within the renal hilum. It gathers urine from the major calyces and conducts it into the ureter.'),
('7e6a9ea8-d951-5bca-ac50-72414675df94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The renal pelvis divides into:', '["Ureters resulting in decreased functional activity","Major calyces (2-3), which divide into minor calyces (7-13)","Collecting ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Major calyces (2-3), which divide into minor calyces (7-13)', 'The renal pelvis divides into two or three major calyces, each of which further divides into minor calyces that cup the renal papillae. Collecting ducts and nephrons lie within the renal parenchyma, not the pelvis.'),
('e019a4ae-cd47-5df4-b2eb-8b9778cedaf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'A horseshoe kidney is:', '["A fusion of the lower poles of the two kidneys (across the midline)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fusion of the entire kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","A fusion of the upper poles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fusion of the lower poles of the two kidneys (across the midline)', 'A horseshoe kidney is a congenital anomaly in which the lower poles of the two kidneys fuse across the midline, usually joined by an isthmus. Fusing the upper poles or the entire kidney is not characteristic.'),
('1226c015-9ab2-53d9-83e5-ffba2e8edf18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Horseshoe kidney is located:', '["Higher than normal resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower than normal (at L3-L5, because the inferior mesenteric artery prevents ascent)","At the normal position without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower than normal (at L3-L5, because the inferior mesenteric artery prevents ascent)', 'The horseshoe kidney stops at the L3-L5 level, lower than a normal kidney, because the inferior mesenteric artery blocks its upward migration during development.'),
('71038d66-4afd-57f4-8c9e-edd647a189d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Horseshoe kidney is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Asymptomatic in most cases, but may be prone to infection and stones","painful mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","symptomatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asymptomatic in most cases, but may be prone to infection and stones', 'Most horseshoe kidneys remain asymptomatic; however, ureteric obstruction predisposes to urinary tract infection, stone formation, and hydronephrosis.'),
('26e5deb9-f5a2-5b4f-954c-e6783bb29dbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The renal colic is caused by:', '["The passage of a stone through the ureter (severe, colicky pain)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A renal tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A kidney infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The passage of a stone through the ureter (severe, colicky pain)', 'Renal colic typically results from a stone lodging in and obstructing the ureter. The resulting ureteric spasm produces severe colicky pain; tumors and infections do not classically cause this pattern.'),
('ace48f9a-f7dd-524c-b1fc-f4501ef6ed9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Renal colic pain radiates from the loin to:', '["The chest resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The groin (along the course of the ureter)","The abdomen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The back leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The groin (along the course of the ureter)', 'Ureteric pain radiates from the loin toward the groin along the course of the ureter. This referral follows the T10-L2 dermatomes that share the sympathetic supply of the ureter.'),
('dac9947b-8011-53ca-a007-980813cad791'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Which muscles form the posterior abdominal wall?', '["Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus","Rectus abdominis, pyramidalis, external oblique","Internal oblique, transversus abdominis, rectus","Diaphragm only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, iliacus', 'The posterior abdominal wall is built by the psoas major, psoas minor, quadratus lumborum, and iliacus muscles. The rectus abdominis and oblique muscles form the anterior abdominal wall.'),
('b60a3e7a-7341-52fc-9d1e-c9c423575a5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major inserts into:', '["Pubic tubercle","Linea aspera","Greater trochanter","Iliac crest","Lesser trochanter of the femur"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter of the femur', 'The psoas major passes deep to the inguinal ligament and inserts into the lesser trochanter of the femur. It acts as a powerful flexor of the thigh at the hip joint.'),
('9be11637-7fda-5fd3-8876-8d50953a97e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor is:', '["Same as psoas major resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of psoas major accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The iliacus muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A variable muscle that lies anterior to psoas major (attaches to pectineal line)"]'::jsonb, 'A variable muscle that lies anterior to psoas major (attaches to pectineal line)', 'The psoas minor is a small, inconsistent muscle that lies anterior to the psoas major and attaches to the pectineal line. It is absent in about 40% of individuals.'),
('0d1fa92c-07ea-5eda-bd09-89b5b3e50612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum originates from:', '["T12-L5 vertebrae resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliolumbar ligament and iliac crest","Sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Ribs 5-12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar ligament and iliac crest', 'The quadratus lumborum arises from the iliac crest and the iliolumbar ligament. It then ascends to insert into the 12th rib and the transverse processes of the upper lumbar vertebrae.'),
('117867fb-b8d6-5260-92d3-b1f2e7b48e17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum inserts into:', '["Lesser trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","12th rib and transverse processes of L1-L4 vertebrae","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic tubercle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater trochanter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '12th rib and transverse processes of L1-L4 vertebrae', 'The quadratus lumborum inserts into the 12th rib and the transverse processes of L1-L4. This insertion allows it to fix the 12th rib and laterally flex the trunk.'),
('7000fd2b-d146-5c2e-a361-ada306875ee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus muscle originates from:', '["Lumbar vertebrae","None of the above","Pubic bone","Sacrum","Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)"]'::jsonb, 'Iliac fossa (upper 2/3)', 'The iliacus arises from the upper two-thirds of the iliac fossa. It joins the psoas major to form the iliopsoas muscle.'),
('90ed5fd5-580d-5466-8a0b-4784b678a453'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus inserts into:', '["Greater trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral shaft accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Iliac crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lesser trochanter (with psoas major, forming iliopsoas)"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser trochanter (with psoas major, forming iliopsoas)', 'The iliacus blends with the psoas major to form the iliopsoas, which inserts on the lesser trochanter of the femur. This combined muscle is the chief flexor of the hip.'),
('43c7c435-b7b0-5003-8f88-4745108aa5ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm crura originate from:', '["T12 only resulting in decreased functional activity","Sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right crus: L1-L3; Left crus: L1-L2 vertebrae","Coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L4-L5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right crus: L1-L3; Left crus: L1-L2 vertebrae', 'The diaphragm arises posteriorly from two crura: the right crus from L1-L3 and the left crus from L1-L2 vertebral bodies. The crura anchor the diaphragm to the lumbar spine.'),
('617e1242-4404-5a70-8d26-ce2a0700860e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The sympathetic trunk in the abdomen lies:', '["Anterolateral to the lumbar vertebral bodies (along the psoas major)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to the aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","In the midline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior to the IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterolateral to the lumbar vertebral bodies (along the psoas major)', 'The lumbar sympathetic trunk descends anterolateral to the lumbar vertebral bodies, lying on the psoas major muscle on each side. It does not run on the aorta or the IVC.'),
('0b066051-5f2f-5c17-ae68-3b4840e8acc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'During therapeutic management of Posterior Abdominal Wall, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('831d7f76-7be6-5215-9ccc-735ac2b4f510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar plexus is formed by:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior rami of L1-L4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior rami of L1-L4 spinal nerves (with contributions from T12)","Anterior rami of L1-L5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only L1-L3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of L1-L4 spinal nerves (with contributions from T12)', 'The lumbar plexus is formed by the anterior rami of the L1-L4 spinal nerves, with a contribution from T12. Posterior rami innervate the deep back muscles and do not join the plexus.'),
('74b08522-ddbe-5761-82aa-8cbc8ec1c8de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The lumbar plexus lies:', '["Posterior to quadratus lumborum","In the iliac fossa","Within the substance of the psoas major muscle","None of the above","Anterior to psoas major"]'::jsonb, 'Within the substance of the psoas major muscle', 'The lumbar plexus is embedded within the substance of the psoas major muscle. Its branches emerge from the anterior, lateral, or medial borders of this muscle.'),
('ea19c9f1-af8f-5055-8b99-167d30903a34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'Branches of the lumbar plexus include:', '["Iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2), lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3), femoral (L2-L4), obturator (L2-L4)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only femoral and obturator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only genitofemoral and lateral femoral cutaneous leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iliohypogastric (L1), ilioinguinal (L1), genitofemoral (L1-L2), lateral femoral cutaneous (L2-L3), femoral (L2-L4), obturator (L2-L4)', 'The lumbar plexus gives off six main branches: iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal from L1, genitofemoral from L1-L2, lateral femoral cutaneous from L2-L3, and femoral and obturator from L2-L4. These branches supply the abdominal wall and lower limb.'),
('c2dacf0d-07b9-572f-afa2-39bdf00e5a54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The abdominal aorta enters the abdomen through:', '["Caval opening (T8)","Aortic hiatus of the diaphragm (at T12)","None of the above","Sternocostal triangle","Esophageal hiatus (T10)"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic hiatus of the diaphragm (at T12)', 'The abdominal aorta reaches the abdomen by passing through the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm at the T12 level. The esophageal hiatus lies at T10 and the caval opening at T8.'),
('e00ae955-f7a9-5067-acfc-7e0ab74439f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates at the level of:', '["L3 vertebra resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 vertebra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L4 vertebra (into left and right common iliac arteries)","L2 vertebra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L5 vertebra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L4 vertebra (into left and right common iliac arteries)', 'The abdominal aorta divides at the L4 level, about the plane of the umbilicus, into the right and left common iliac arteries. The IVC is formed at the L5 level, just below the aortic bifurcation.'),
('fb63105c-4c41-5706-8576-ec470289624e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC is formed by the union of:', '["Right and left external iliac veins","None of the above","Right and left common iliac veins at the level of L5","Common iliac and internal iliac veins","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Right and left common iliac veins at the level of L5', 'The inferior vena cava is formed at the L5 level, anterior to the right psoas major, by the union of the two common iliac veins. The external iliac veins alone do not form it.'),
('85e1e54b-9f27-5188-9d29-0cd91995fa9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The IVC pierces the diaphragm at:', '["L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","T8 (caval opening in the central tendon)","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","T10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T8 (caval opening in the central tendon)', 'The IVC passes through the diaphragm at the T8 level via the caval opening in the central tendon. The other main diaphragmatic openings are the esophageal hiatus at T10 and the aortic hiatus at T12.'),
('6dfa8744-9d0a-5f0a-9e89-d20c05c7e491'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major is innervated by:', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L4-S1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior rami of L1-L3 (lumbar plexus)","Obturator nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior rami of L1-L3 (lumbar plexus)', 'The psoas major receives its innervation from the anterior rami of L1-L3, branches that run within the muscle itself. The femoral and obturator nerves supply other muscles of the lower limb.'),
('dea9e66e-69df-5d44-8a12-d6c284b1b57a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum is innervated by:', '["Lumbar plexus only","None of the above","T12 and L1-L4 anterior rami","Only T12","Femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'T12 and L1-L4 anterior rami', 'The quadratus lumborum is supplied by the anterior rami of T12 and L1-L4. The femoral nerve does not innervate this posterior abdominal wall muscle.'),
('69054218-9bcb-5271-9128-be0eff0709d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliacus muscle is innervated by:', '["L1-L2","None of the above","Femoral nerve (L2-L4)","Obturator nerve","Femoral and obturator"]'::jsonb, 'Femoral nerve (L2-L4)', 'The iliacus is innervated by the femoral nerve (L2-L4), which passes forward between it and the psoas major. The obturator nerve supplies the adductor compartment of the thigh.'),
('b1fd5f0c-45c2-51f9-871d-6029a4591224'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas minor (when present) is innervated by:', '["None of the above","L1 anterior ramus","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","L2-L3"]'::jsonb, 'L1 anterior ramus', 'The psoas minor, when present, is innervated by the L1 anterior ramus. This matches the segmental level from which the small muscle develops.'),
('d462b241-df48-5fd5-bf2c-c3227e095b48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The iliopsoas muscle functions as:', '["The chief flexor of the thigh at the hip","Adductor of the thigh","None of the above","Extensor of the thigh","Abductor of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'The chief flexor of the thigh at the hip', 'The iliopsoas, formed by the psoas major and iliacus, is the principal flexor of the thigh at the hip joint. It does not adduct, abduct, or extend the thigh.'),
('e441acfe-f3fc-55cd-b173-7a3ff95071ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The quadratus lumborum muscle functions as:', '["Rotator of the trunk resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extensor of the trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral flexor of the trunk (fixes the 12th rib during inspiration)","Flexor of the trunk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral flexor of the trunk (fixes the 12th rib during inspiration)', 'The quadratus lumborum laterally flexes the trunk toward its side and fixes the 12th rib during inspiration. It does not rotate or extend the trunk.'),
('ac26b757-b91e-582b-8837-a2eea3a1e9cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The psoas major muscle can also:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Extend the vertebral column accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flex the vertebral column (when acting bilaterally)","Rotate the trunk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flex the vertebral column (when acting bilaterally)', 'When the two psoas major muscles contract together, they flex the trunk and the lumbar vertebral column at the hip and spine. The muscle cannot extend the vertebral column.'),
('8e01bbd1-26ea-5f21-939c-45775bbbea48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Posterior Abdominal Wall', 'The diaphragm crura are important because:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","They contain the aortic opening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They are tendinous origins of the diaphragm and the right crus forms the esophageal hiatus","They contain the caval opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They attach to the ribs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They are tendinous origins of the diaphragm and the right crus forms the esophageal hiatus', 'The crura are the tendinous origins of the diaphragm from the lumbar vertebrae. Only the right crus loops around to form the esophageal hiatus; the aortic and caval openings lie in other parts of the diaphragm.'),
('32437ed3-bcba-5e8f-a1fa-f34317eac6e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is divided into which parts?', '["Cardia, fundus, body, pyloric part (antrum, canal, pylorus)","Cardia, fundus, body, pylorus only","Cardia, body, antrum only","Fundus, body, antrum, pylorus","Cardia, fundus, pylorus only"]'::jsonb, 'Cardia, fundus, body, pyloric part (antrum, canal, pylorus)', 'The stomach is divided into four main parts: the cardia at the esophagogastric junction, the fundus above it, the body, and the pyloric part, which subdivides into the pyloric antrum, pyloric canal, and pylorus. Options that omit the subdivisions of the pyloric part or list only a few regions do not give the complete conventional scheme.'),
('dd95db31-dbb1-5c16-b8f2-af3d73a22548'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The cardiac notch (incisura cardialis) is?', '["The angle between duodenum and stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","The notch at incisura angularis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The notch at the pylorus mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The angle between the esophagus and the fundus of the stomach"]'::jsonb, 'The angle between the esophagus and the fundus of the stomach', 'The cardiac notch (incisura cardialis) is the acute angle formed where the left border of the esophagus meets the dome of the gastric fundus. The incisura angularis, pylorus, and duodenum are unrelated, separate landmarks.'),
('245d5e2a-41af-5c2d-bf54-98c248c8fb59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The incisura angularis is?', '["A notch on the greater curvature resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cardiac notch mediated by specific receptor activation","The pyloric opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A notch on the lesser curvature marking the junction of body and pyloric part"]'::jsonb, 'A notch on the lesser curvature marking the junction of body and pyloric part', 'The incisura angularis is a constant notch on the lesser curvature that marks the junction between the body and the pyloric part, visible as a bend on barium studies. The cardiac notch lies at the esophagogastric junction and the pyloric opening is distal to the notch, while the greater curvature bears no such angular notch.'),
('0ae0ba66-5c95-50a8-931f-bd9c992472ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach bed (posterior relations) includes all EXCEPT?', '["Diaphragm","Left adrenal gland","Liver (left lobe)","Left kidney","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (left lobe)', 'The stomach bed is formed by structures behind the stomach: diaphragm, left kidney, left adrenal gland, spleen, pancreas, and transverse mesocolon. The left lobe of the liver lies anterior to the stomach, so it does not belong to the stomach bed.'),
('0d245bb1-318e-5727-905a-70f93febce8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior relations of the stomach include?', '["Liver (left lobe), anterior abdominal wall, diaphragm (left dome)","Aorta, IVC, celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Transverse colon, small intestine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreas, spleen, left kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (left lobe), anterior abdominal wall, diaphragm (left dome)', 'Anteriorly the stomach is related to the left lobe of the liver, the anterior abdominal wall, and the left dome of the diaphragm. The pancreas, spleen, left kidney, aorta, IVC, and transverse colon are posterior relations rather than anterior ones.'),
('b388deb9-39bd-54d9-a1b3-23d760d82679'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the lesser curvature of the stomach is from?', '["Left gastric (celiac trunk) and right gastric (common hepatic) arteries","Short gastric arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left and right gastroepiploic arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic artery only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left gastric only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric (celiac trunk) and right gastric (common hepatic) arteries', 'The lesser curvature is supplied by the left gastric artery, the smallest branch of the celiac trunk, and the right gastric artery, usually a branch of the common hepatic artery, which anastomose along that curvature. The short gastric and gastroepiploic arteries supply the fundus and greater curvature instead.'),
('d550770a-4cd5-51e5-a112-754e8dec6420'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the greater curvature of the stomach is from?', '["Inferior mesenteric resulting in decreased functional activity","Left and right gastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short gastrics only mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior mesenteric without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left gastroepiploic (splenic) and right gastroepiploic (gastroduodenal) arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastroepiploic (splenic) and right gastroepiploic (gastroduodenal) arteries', 'The greater curvature receives the left gastroepiploic artery from the splenic artery and the right gastroepiploic artery from the gastroduodenal artery, forming a travelling arcade. The gastric arteries run on the lesser curvature, mesenteric arteries supply the bowel elsewhere, and short gastrics serve the fundus.'),
('d2150bc5-b926-5da1-800e-984b9d116c5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The short gastric arteries arise from?', '["Common hepatic artery","Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Right gastric artery","Gastroduodenal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery', 'The short gastric arteries arise from the splenic artery near the splenic hilum and pass through the gastrosplenic ligament to reach the fundus. The celiac trunk gives off the left gastric and common hepatic arteries, while the gastroduodenal and right gastric arteries supply other stomach regions.'),
('e3539f8c-aacc-57a5-8ff3-c828ef5ba41e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The venous drainage of the stomach ultimately drains into?', '["Portal vein","IVC","Azygos vein","Inferior vena cava","Hemiazygos vein"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'All gastric veins drain into the portal vein, either directly or via the splenic and superior mesenteric veins, because the stomach is a foregut derivative. The IVC, azygos, and hemiazygos veins are systemic conduits and do not receive gastric drainage.'),
('fbfe7735-334f-5331-87dc-52fb4bfd454f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastroepiploic vein drains into?', '["Superior mesenteric vein (often via gastrocolic trunk)","Left gastric vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric vein (often via gastrocolic trunk)', 'The right gastroepiploic vein drains the greater curvature and empties into the superior mesenteric vein, usually through the gastrocolic trunk of Henle. It does not terminate in the splenic vein, left gastric vein, portal vein, or IVC.'),
('ebe15ed1-3ff6-560a-aec3-bea59493fef7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastroepiploic vein drains into?', '["Left gastric vein","Splenic vein","IVC","Superior mesenteric vein","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The left gastroepiploic vein runs along the greater curvature and drains directly into the splenic vein. The right gastroepiploic vein instead joins the superior mesenteric vein via the gastrocolic trunk.'),
('eea66f4d-5b6d-5f02-ac1e-7c94d780d432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The short gastric veins drain into?', '["Right gastric vein","IVC","Portal vein","Splenic vein","Left gastric vein"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The short gastric veins accompany the short gastric arteries through the gastrosplenic ligament and drain the fundus into the splenic vein. The right gastric, left gastric, and portal veins are not the direct drainage for the fundic short gastric veins.'),
('b4bd476d-abd7-53af-838e-05a9259b347e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lymphatic drainage of the stomach includes which nodal groups?', '["Superficial inguinal only resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Para-aortic only mediated by specific receptor activation","Left gastric, right gastric, left gastroepiploic, right gastroepiploic, pyloric","Internal iliac only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric, right gastric, left gastroepiploic, right gastroepiploic, pyloric', 'Gastric lymphatics follow the arterial supply and reach the left and right gastric nodes, left and right gastroepiploic nodes, and pyloric nodes, which then drain to the celiac nodes. Inguinal, axillary, para-aortic, and internal iliac nodal groups have no connection to the stomach.'),
('cd0069ab-4822-5b03-bbe1-acd677d50c94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The celiac lymph nodes drain into?', '["None of the above","Right lymphatic duct","Thoracic duct directly","Cisterna chyli (via intestinal trunk)","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna chyli (via intestinal trunk)', 'Efferent lymphatics of the celiac nodes converge to form the intestinal trunk, which empties into the cisterna chyli, the origin of the thoracic duct. Gastric lymph therefore does not enter the right lymphatic duct or the portal vein directly.'),
('c6f5e893-c4ed-5593-ab40-b9ce11f76fae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerve supply of the stomach is from?', '["Only phrenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic (celiac plexus, splanchnic nerves) and parasympathetic (vagus nerves)","Only sympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only intercostal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (celiac plexus, splanchnic nerves) and parasympathetic (vagus nerves)', 'The stomach receives parasympathetic fibers from the anterior and posterior vagal trunks and sympathetic fibers from the celiac plexus carried by the splanchnic nerves. The phrenic and intercostal nerves do not contribute any supply to the stomach.'),
('8c59206a-e168-594a-a583-a7a03e3ca511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior vagal trunk (left vagus) gives rise to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior gastric branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Celiac branches mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatic branches and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet)","Pyloric branches only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic branches and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet)', 'The anterior vagal trunk, derived from the left vagus, gives hepatic branches to the liver and gallbladder and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet) along the lesser curvature. The celiac and posterior gastric branches belong to the posterior trunk.'),
('39a14d5c-2c76-5325-83e9-59d577b7c315'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The posterior vagal trunk (right vagus) gives rise to?', '["Celiac branches and posterior gastric branches","Hepatic branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior gastric branches without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pyloric branches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac branches and posterior gastric branches', 'The posterior vagal trunk, derived from the right vagus, gives celiac branches to the celiac plexus and posterior gastric branches to the posterior wall of the stomach. Hepatic and anterior gastric branches arise from the anterior trunk.'),
('e70b966c-809e-5cec-bcd8-c177f6aba235'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerves of Latarjet supply?', '["The liver resulting in decreased functional activity","The fundus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The body and pyloric antrum (motor and secretory fibers)","The cardia only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The body and pyloric antrum (motor and secretory fibers)', 'The nerves of Latarjet are the terminal vagal branches that course along the lesser curvature supplying motor and secretory fibers to the body and pyloric antrum. The liver and spleen receive branches of the vagi and celiac plexus respectively, and the cardia and fundus have other territories.'),
('c7a19db1-2243-57a4-9a7d-8b7a82acae6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenum is divided into how many parts?', '["6 parts resulting in decreased functional activity","4 parts (superior, descending, horizontal, ascending)","3 parts mediated by specific receptor activation","5 parts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 parts (superior, descending, horizontal, ascending)', 'The duodenum is divided into four parts: superior, descending, horizontal, and ascending, which form aC-shape around the head of the pancreas. Answer options giving two, three, five, or six parts do not match the accepted division.'),
('80294155-6eb4-56cb-9859-f804838bd352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The first part of the duodenum (superior) is also called?', '["None of the above","Duodenal cap (or ampulla)","Duodenojejunal flexure","Pyloric part","Suspensory part"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal cap (or ampulla)', 'The first (superior) part of the duodenum is the dilated, relatively mobile segment just beyond the pylorus, called the duodenal cap (or ampulla) for its shape on barium studies. The duodenojejunal flexure ends the fourth part, while the pyloric part belongs to the stomach.'),
('994be025-99fb-5fc0-bd9d-3809b637f02b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The second part of the duodenum (descending) receives?', '["Only the common bile duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The accessory pancreatic duct only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common bile duct and main pancreatic duct at the major duodenal papilla","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the pancreatic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common bile duct and main pancreatic duct at the major duodenal papilla', 'The descending (second) part of the duodenum receives the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct, which usually join and open together at the major duodenal papilla. The accessory pancreatic duct opens separately at the minor duodenal papilla.'),
('bf97e96c-2987-5ef4-8be5-f7f5651236ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The major duodenal papilla (ampulla of Vater) is located in which part?', '["Second (descending) part of duodenum","First part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenojejunal flexure mediated by specific receptor activation","Fourth part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Third part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Second (descending) part of duodenum', 'The major duodenal papilla, through which bile and pancreatic juice enter the gut, lies on the posteromedial wall of the second (descending) part of the duodenum. It is not present in the first, third, or fourth parts or at the duodenojejunal flexure.'),
('a57e6ae1-919f-5bb2-91b2-2628939e7426'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The minor duodenal papilla is the opening of?', '["Cystic duct","Main pancreatic duct","Accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini)","Common bile duct","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini)', 'The minor duodenal papilla is the opening of the accessory pancreatic duct (of Santorini), situated about 2 cm proximal to the major papilla. The main pancreatic duct and common bile duct enter at the major papilla, and the cystic duct drains the gallbladder.'),
('ff8243c5-3b8f-50c5-a36b-b2d3a8f254ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The suspensory ligament of the duodenum (ligament of Treitz) attaches the duodenojejunal flexure to?', '["IVC","Right crus of the diaphragm","Left crus of the diaphragm","Aorta","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Right crus of the diaphragm', 'The ligament of Treitz arises from the right crus of the diaphragm and inserts on the duodenojejunal flexure, suspending the fourth part of the duodenum. It does not attach to the aorta, IVC, left crus, or pancreas.'),
('5ceb1dc6-2b2b-5615-845f-563f614204b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The ligament of Treitz is an important landmark for?', '["The ileocecal junction resulting in decreased functional activity","The ampulla of Vater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pylorus mediated by specific receptor activation","The splenic flexure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distinguishing upper from lower GI bleeding (bleeding proximal vs distal to Treitz)"]'::jsonb, 'Distinguishing upper from lower GI bleeding (bleeding proximal vs distal to Treitz)', 'The ligament of Treitz marks the foregut-midgut junction and the boundary used to classify bleeding: hemorrhage proximal to it is upper GI bleeding and distal to it is lower GI bleeding. The pylorus, ampulla of Vater, ileocecal junction, and splenic flexure are not used for this division.'),
('f4454262-895f-5945-8a4e-7403486c3687'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The omental bursa (lesser sac) is bounded anteriorly by?', '["Transverse colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stomach, lesser omentum, gastrocolic ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach, lesser omentum, gastrocolic ligament', 'The anterior wall of the omental bursa is formed by the stomach, the lesser omentum, and the gastrocolic ligament. The pancreas and transverse mesocolon form the posterior wall, and the liver and spleen are not anterior boundaries of the bursa.'),
('99f90e45-1287-57fd-8f7d-351d6645152a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The omental bursa is bounded posteriorly by?', '["Stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","Lesser omentum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas, left kidney, transverse mesocolon, posterior abdominal wall","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas, left kidney, transverse mesocolon, posterior abdominal wall', 'The posterior wall of the lesser sac is the stomach bed, formed by the pancreas, left kidney, transverse mesocolon, and posterior abdominal wall structures. The stomach and lesser omentum are anterior boundaries, and the liver and diaphragm are not part of this wall.'),
('9d68800e-6815-57a7-a56e-9d1673fc0ca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lesser sac communicates with the greater sac via?', '["Epiploic foramen (of Winslow)","Duodenojejunal flexure","Cardiac orifice","Pyloric orifice","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Epiploic foramen (of Winslow)', 'The lesser sac communicates with the greater sac only through the epiploic foramen of Winslow, which lies behind the hepatoduodenal ligament and in front of the IVC. The pyloric and cardiac orifices are openings of the stomach, not communications between the sacs.'),
('86a793d4-085e-55fe-bd02-39e2cca87bdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The boundaries of the epiploic foramen (of Winslow) are?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","All of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior: liver; Posterior: kidney; Superior: diaphragm; Inferior: colon mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior: hepatoduodenal ligament; Posterior: IVC; Superior: caudate lobe; Inferior: 1st part of duodenum","Anterior: stomach; Posterior: pancreas; Superior: spleen; Inferior: colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior: hepatoduodenal ligament; Posterior: IVC; Superior: caudate lobe; Inferior: 1st part of duodenum', 'The epiploic foramen is bounded anteriorly by the hepatoduodenal ligament, posteriorly by the IVC and right crus, superiorly by the caudate lobe of the liver, and inferiorly by the first part of the duodenum. The other combinations list structures that do not form these boundaries.'),
('4a250f5b-2856-5429-aadd-760f0f1c59c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach develops from which embryologic structure?', '["Hindgut","Mesoderm of lateral plate","Foregut","Midgut","Ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Foregut', 'The stomach develops as a dilation of the foregut, the segment of the primitive gut tube that also gives rise to the proximal duodenum, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas. Midgut and hindgut contribute only to more distal bowel, and ectoderm and mesoderm layering do not form the gut tube.'),
('9dfbc026-a142-5df7-a319-4418e99b1dde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The celiac trunk supplies derivatives of?', '["All of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Foregut (stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, pancreas, spleen)","Midgut mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hindgut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foregut (stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, pancreas, spleen)', 'The celiac trunk supplies the derivatives of the foregut: stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, and spleen. Midgut structures are supplied by the SMA and hindgut structures by the IMA.'),
('9bd2e21f-3b07-5296-9647-f22c88b0be00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The superior mesenteric artery supplies derivatives of?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Midgut (distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal 2/3 of transverse colon)","Stomach only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hindgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foregut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midgut (distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal 2/3 of transverse colon)', 'The SMA supplies midgut derivatives, including the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. Foregut structures receive blood from the celiac trunk and hindgut structures from the IMA.'),
('679234b2-3b3d-55a1-9f47-fe346ed8d33c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastric artery is a branch of?', '["Left gastric artery","Common hepatic artery (or proper hepatic)","Superior mesenteric artery","Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic artery (or proper hepatic)', 'The right gastric artery usually arises from the common hepatic artery, occasionally from the proper hepatic artery, and runs along the lesser curvature to anastomose with the left gastric artery. The left gastric, splenic, gastroduodenal, and superior mesenteric arteries are not its source.'),
('1d3055ab-fbca-551f-b480-929e5b321d19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastric artery is a branch of?', '["Aorta directly","Celiac trunk","Common hepatic","Superior mesenteric","Splenic"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac trunk', 'The left gastric artery is the smallest branch of the celiac trunk, which gives off the splenic, common hepatic, and left gastric arteries as its three branches. The left gastric does not arise directly from the aorta or from any of the other named vessels.'),
('7dbd3d43-80b6-574a-b575-266db54025a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastroepiploic artery is a branch of?', '["Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Superior mesenteric","Left gastric","Common hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroduodenal artery', 'The right gastroepiploic artery arises from the gastroduodenal artery and runs in the gastrocolic ligament along the greater curvature. The splenic artery yields the left gastroepiploic and short gastric arteries, and the celiac trunk gives rise to the splenic, common hepatic, and left gastric arteries.'),
('d4e95fbb-5526-571a-98dc-a50b30f98c25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastroepiploic artery is a branch of?', '["Celiac trunk","Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal","Left gastric","Common hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery', 'The left gastroepiploic artery branches from the splenic artery near the splenic hilum and travels along the greater curvature to anastomose with the right gastroepiploic artery. The gastroduodenal, common hepatic, left gastric, and celiac trunk are not its origin.'),
('2bc4fe3b-ce41-5bec-9911-f3678154de80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastroduodenal artery is a branch of?', '["Proper hepatic artery","Common hepatic artery","Splenic artery","Celiac trunk","Left hepatic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic artery', 'The gastroduodenal artery arises from the common hepatic artery just distal to the celiac trunk and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, a site eroded by posterior duodenal ulcers. The proper hepatic, left hepatic, and splenic arteries and the celiac trunk do not give origin to it.'),
('55b2cd0f-95fc-56a3-9713-933f58a451bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of?', '["Splenic artery","Celiac trunk","Superior mesenteric artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Common hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroduodenal artery', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the gastroduodenal artery and supplies the head of the pancreas and duodenum. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery in turn arises from the SMA, while the splenic, common hepatic, and celiac arteries are unrelated as its source.'),
('f9543a41-2e3a-5a03-9e46-599c8c053684'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of?', '["Celiac trunk resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastroduodenal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior mesenteric artery (or first jejunal branch)","Common hepatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splenic artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery (or first jejunal branch)', 'The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the superior mesenteric artery, often from its first jejunal branch, and anastomoses with the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery. The celiac trunk, gastroduodenal, common hepatic, and splenic arteries are not its origin.'),
('ed89b6e1-41c3-56ef-b804-cc45cb6ae87c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anastomosis between superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries is important because?', '["It provides a collateral connection between celiac and SMA circulations","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It connects portal and systemic circulations mediated by specific receptor activation","It is a venous connection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is a lymphatic connection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It provides a collateral connection between celiac and SMA circulations', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery (from the gastroduodenal artery) and the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (from the SMA) form arcades that anastomose, linking the celiac trunk and SMA circulations and giving the duodenum and pancreatic head a dual blood supply. This interconnection is arterial, not venous or lymphatic.'),
('9e0ca4f0-c96d-5ae9-87a9-a885324c1728'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The first part of the duodenum (duodenal cap) is?', '["Retroperitoneal throughout","None of the above","Intraperitoneal throughout","Retroperitoneal","Intraperitoneal (except the proximal few mm)"]'::jsonb, 'Intraperitoneal (except the proximal few mm)', 'The first part of the duodenum is intraperitoneal only in its proximal few millimeters beyond the pylorus; the rest of the duodenum is secondarily retroperitoneal, which makes the cap relatively mobile. Saying it is intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal throughout is therefore incorrect.'),
('fb01a36c-e498-5bf8-ab73-97677fd1ff4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The second part of the duodenum (descending) is related posteriorly to?', '["Liver only resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas only mediated by specific receptor activation","Right kidney, right ureter, right renal vessels, psoas major, IVC","Aorta only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right kidney, right ureter, right renal vessels, psoas major, IVC', 'The descending (second) part of the duodenum is related posteriorly to the right kidney, right ureter, right renal vessels, psoas major, and IVC. The liver and pancreas are anterior relations and the aorta lies further to the left at this level.'),
('fa9db75e-1847-531e-86bd-e48179694bd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The third part of the duodenum (horizontal) is crossed anteriorly by?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mesenteric artery and vein (and root of mesentery)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery and vein (and root of mesentery)', 'The third (horizontal) part of the duodenum is crossed anteriorly by the superior mesenteric artery and vein and the root of the mesentery at the L3 level. The aorta, IVC, and pancreas lie behind this segment.'),
('0dc25a34-2b8c-55c7-a53d-fa432df021d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The third part of the duodenum lies posterior to SMA and anterior to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC and aorta (at L3 level)","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IVC and aorta (at L3 level)', 'The third part of the duodenum passes between the SMA and SMV anteriorly and the IVC and abdominal aorta posteriorly at the level of L3, the site of potential SMA compression. The kidney, pancreas, and liver do not lie posterior to this segment.'),
('165fc538-d2ab-5758-b06d-71b9818dcbd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The fourth part of the duodenum (ascending) ends at the?', '["Duodenojejunal flexure (suspended by ligament of Treitz)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jejunum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pylorus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ileocecal junction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenojejunal flexure (suspended by ligament of Treitz)', 'The fourth (ascending) part of the duodenum terminates at the duodenojejunal flexure, which is fixed to the right crus of the diaphragm by the ligament of Treitz. The pylorus lies at the stomach-duodenum junction, the jejunum begins distal to the flexure, and the ileocecal junction is in the distal small bowel.'),
('38356a93-bda7-55f2-ba6f-7ddbfa559833'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The ligament of Treitz has which type of muscle fibers?', '["Striated muscle only resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrous only mediated by specific receptor activation","Striated (from diaphragm) and smooth (from duodenum)","Smooth muscle only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Striated (from diaphragm) and smooth (from duodenum)', 'The ligament of Treitz contains striated muscle fibers from the right crus of the diaphragm and smooth muscle fibers continuous with the muscular layer of the duodenum, complementing each other to widen the DJ angle during swallowing and peristalsis. It is therefore not purely fibrous, elastic, smooth, or striated.'),
('8e331445-c766-53cc-8c75-65bf94764137'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastric mucosa is lined by which epithelium?', '["Stratified squamous","Cuboidal","Transitional","Pseudostratified columnar","Simple columnar epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar epithelium', 'The gastric mucosa is lined by simple columnar epithelium whose surface cells secrete mucus and invaginate to form gastric pits and glands. Stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus and anal canal, and cuboidal, transitional, and pseudostratified columnar epithelia are not gastric linings.'),
('ababa8a2-bd0b-5831-90c4-5491cb42469e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Parietal (oxyntic) cells of the stomach secrete?', '["Gastrin resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor","Pepsinogen mediated by specific receptor activation","Bicarbonate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor', 'Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete hydrochloric acid, which acidifies the gastric lumen, and intrinsic factor, which is required for vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum. Gastrin comes from G-cells, pepsinogen from chief cells, and mucus from surface mucous cells, while parietal cells do not secrete bicarbonate.'),
('bfd65956-55c1-5563-846e-22bfbfe2d22d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Chief (zymogen) cells of the stomach secrete?', '["Mucus","Pepsinogen","Hydrochloric acid","Gastrin","Intrinsic factor"]'::jsonb, 'Pepsinogen', 'Chief (zymogen) cells secrete pepsinogen, the inactive precursor that hydrochloric acid converts to active pepsin for protein digestion. Hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor come from parietal cells, gastrin from G-cells, and mucus from surface mucous cells.'),
('c5041b7d-af5d-536b-9abc-945606aed6aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'G-cells of the stomach secrete?', '["HCl","Somatostatin","Mucus","Gastrin","Pepsinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrin', 'G-cells, located mainly in the pyloric antrum, secrete gastrin, which stimulates acid secretion and promotes mucosal growth. HCl and pepsinogen are products of parietal and chief cells respectively, somatostatin comes from D-cells, and mucus from surface mucous cells.'),
('87c31524-0ce0-5def-8071-6808b6601b26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Intrinsic factor, essential for vitamin B12 absorption, is produced by?', '["Mucous neck cells","Parietal cells of the stomach","Chief cells","G-cells","Enterochromaffin cells"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal cells of the stomach', 'Parietal cells in the body and fundus secrete intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 so it can be absorbed in the terminal ileum. With pernicious anemia, loss of parietal cells leads to B12 deficiency. Chief cells produce pepsinogen, G-cells produce gastrin, mucous neck cells produce mucus, and enterochromaffin cells produce serotonin/histamine.'),
('aa3f05e1-744e-5198-927d-f6e561263ff9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Brunner glands are found in the?', '["Ileal submucosa resulting in decreased functional activity","Colonic submucosa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jejunal submucosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastric submucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duodenal submucosa (mainly in the first part)"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal submucosa (mainly in the first part)', 'Brunner glands are mucus-secreting tubuloalveolar glands confined to the duodenal submucosa, most abundant in the first part. Their alkaline secretion neutralizes acidic gastric chyme. They are absent from the ileum, colon, jejunum, and gastric wall.'),
('1903558f-f1d1-5484-b79f-3732fe45a9ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The pyloric sphincter is formed by?', '["Longitudinal muscle","Mucosal folds","None of the above","Oblique muscle","Thickened circular smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Thickened circular smooth muscle', 'The pyloric sphincter is a localized thickening of the circular smooth muscle layer at the gastroduodenal junction. It relaxes to allow chyme through and prevents reflux of duodenal contents. The longitudinal and oblique layers do not form the sphincter.'),
('78818b43-4af5-5230-aa00-f8901882f9fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The most common site of duodenal ulcer is?', '["Fourth part resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior wall of stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior wall of first part of duodenum (duodenal cap)","Second part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Third part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall of first part of duodenum (duodenal cap)', 'Duodenal ulcers occur most often on the anterior wall of the duodenal cap (first part) where the mucosa is least protected from acid. The second, third, and fourth parts are uncommon sites, and a posterior-wall stomach lesion is a gastric, not duodenal, ulcer.'),
('03f12f6e-3c16-5c65-8768-2b3fe70d488c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Perforation of a posterior duodenal ulcer leads to?', '["All of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hemothorax mediated by specific receptor activation","Free peritoneal rupture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retroperitoneal spread (contents into lesser sac or retroperitoneal space)"]'::jsonb, 'Retroperitoneal spread (contents into lesser sac or retroperitoneal space)', 'The posterior wall of the first part of the duodenum faces the lesser sac and retroperitoneal tissues, so perforation leaks contents there and may erode the gastroduodenal artery, causing hemorrhage. Free peritoneal rupture typifies an anterior-wall perforation instead.'),
('536200ae-7527-5163-a995-f773dcc9d4e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastric ulcers are most commonly located on?', '["Lesser curvature (near the incisura angularis)","Greater curvature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pylorus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fundus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser curvature (near the incisura angularis)', 'Gastric ulcers most commonly arise on the lesser curvature near the incisura angularis, where the mucosal blood supply is relatively poor. The greater curvature, cardia, fundus, and pylorus are all less frequent sites.'),
('252cf70e-95e2-51d5-9ba0-e50ff07a4c59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The artery most commonly eroded in a bleeding lesser curvature gastric ulcer is?', '["Splenic artery","Gastroduodenal artery","Left gastric artery","Short gastric","Right gastric artery"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric artery', 'The left gastric artery runs along the lesser curvature of the stomach, so an ulcer at that site most commonly erodes it, producing torrential upper GI bleeding. The gastroduodenal artery is the vessel endangered by posterior duodenal ulcers, and the short gastric and splenic vessels lie elsewhere.'),
('b9780c6b-c7e8-57ed-98e5-c7905d5c3b95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The prepyloric vein (of Mayo) is useful because?', '["None of the above","It is present","It marks the gastroduodenal junction (pylorus)","It drains directly into the portal vein","It is the largest gastric vein"]'::jsonb, 'It marks the gastroduodenal junction (pylorus)', 'The prepyloric vein of Mayo crosses the anterior surface of the pylorus and is used by surgeons as a surface landmark for the gastroduodenal junction. It is neither the largest gastric vein nor a direct tributary of the portal vein.'),
('531fd5a9-74a3-5200-b40b-85f00f80cb7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The celiac trunk arises from the abdominal aorta at which level?', '["T11-T12","L2-L3","L3-L4","T12-L1","L1-L2"]'::jsonb, 'T12-L1', 'The celiac trunk is the first unpaired ventral branch of the abdominal aorta, emerging just below the aortic hiatus at the T12-L1 disc level. It quickly divides into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries. The L1-L2 and lower levels belong to the SMA and renal/IMA branches.'),
('f3af2f83-1b2b-5b1c-bd43-d8d1beba14bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The superior mesenteric artery arises at which level?', '["T12-L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L2 mediated by specific receptor activation","L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 (about 1 cm below the celiac trunk)"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (about 1 cm below the celiac trunk)', 'The superior mesenteric artery arises from the anterior aspect of the aorta, approximately 1 cm below the celiac trunk at the L1 level, passing behind the neck of the pancreas. T12-L1 is the celiac trunk level, while L3-L4 correspond to the inferior mesenteric artery and bifurcation.'),
('64eef10f-3920-5edb-b215-2fe5fa972709'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The transpyloric plane (Addison) passes through?', '["L2 resulting in decreased functional activity","L4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 vertebra (pylorus, gallbladder neck, renal hila)"]'::jsonb, 'L1 vertebra (pylorus, gallbladder neck, renal hila)', 'The transpyloric plane lies at the L1 level and passes through the pylorus, neck of the gallbladder, renal hila, and origin of the superior mesenteric artery. L4 is the umbilicus/iliac crest plane and L2 is the level of the DJ flexure and renal vessels.'),
('30dbffdf-7088-557c-bc56-2179b43f76ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'In portal hypertension, portosystemic anastomosis at the stomach involves?', '["All of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior mesenteric vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left gastric (coronary) vein with esophageal veins (azygos system)","Superior mesenteric vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splenic vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric (coronary) vein with esophageal veins (azygos system)', 'In portal hypertension the left gastric (coronary) vein shunts portal blood to esophageal veins draining into the azygos system, producing esophageal varices that can rupture. The SMV, splenic vein, and IMV do not anastomose with the esophageal venous system at the stomach.'),
('5a225c1d-e6bb-568a-bd31-a449acc3691e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The greater omentum is derived from?', '["Mesentery proper","Ventral mesogastrium","Dorsal mesogastrium","Transverse mesocolon","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal mesogastrium', 'The greater omentum develops from the dorsal mesogastrium, which grows and folds down over the transverse colon and small intestine, fusing into a four-layered peritoneal apron. The ventral mesogastrium instead forms the lesser omentum and falciform ligament.'),
('3e1b58a1-76ca-5349-8f22-37a1f0b0e8b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lesser omentum is derived from?', '["Ventral mesogastrium","Mesentery proper","None of the above","Transverse mesocolon","Dorsal mesogastrium"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral mesogastrium', 'The lesser omentum forms from the ventral mesogastrium and stretches between the liver and the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum. The dorsal mesogastrium gives rise to the greater omentum and gastrosplenic ligament, not the lesser omentum.'),
('aee87fbe-e35f-53a3-9d4b-6cea5943f60c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastrocolic ligament connects?', '["Colon to the abdominal wall","Stomach to the diaphragm","Stomach (greater curvature) to the transverse colon","Stomach to the spleen","Stomach to the liver"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (greater curvature) to the transverse colon', 'The gastrocolic ligament, the upper part of the greater omentum, connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon and carries the right and left gastroepiploic vessels. The gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, and gastrohepatic ligaments attach the stomach to the other named structures.'),
('748a5008-c7b4-5cfe-be27-bd9976943cf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastrophrenic ligament connects?', '["Stomach (fundus/cardia) to the diaphragm","Stomach to the liver","Stomach to the spleen","Stomach to the colon","Diaphragm to the kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (fundus/cardia) to the diaphragm', 'The gastrophrenic ligament anchors the fundus and cardiac part of the stomach to the undersurface of the diaphragm. The gastrohepatic, gastrosplenic, and gastrocolic ligaments connect the stomach to the liver, spleen, and colon respectively.'),
('56947ed7-fc87-5ca3-b500-e1ab843dcdc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior relations of the first part of the duodenum include?', '["Quadrate lobe of liver and gallbladder","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadrate lobe of liver and gallbladder', 'The first part of the duodenum is anteriorly related to the quadrate lobe of the liver and the gallbladder; the free edge of the lesser omentum (hepatoduodenal ligament) overlies it. The IVC, portal vein, and pancreas are posterior relations.'),
('7e93d165-1781-5d86-b44b-29f221310482'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The posterior relations of the first part of the duodenum include?', '["Aorta only resulting in decreased functional activity","Common bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein","IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatic head without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein', 'Posterior to the first part of the duodenum lie the common bile duct, gastroduodenal artery, and portal vein, all running in the free edge of the lesser omentum; a posterior wall ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery. The pancreatic head and IVC are relations of the second part.'),
('e066ab1b-e445-5800-8448-a76ae15016ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The second part of the duodenum is related anteriorly to?', '["Liver only resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse colon and loops of jejunum","IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreas only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse colon and loops of jejunum', 'The anterior surface of the descending duodenum is crossed by the transverse colon and loops of jejunum as they emerge from under the transverse mesocolon. The IVC and right renal vessels lie posterior to the second part, and the pancreatic head lies medially.'),
('5970830f-bc12-58ea-9ede-431243d72eeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The fourth part of the duodenum ascends to the left of?', '["SMA resulting in decreased functional activity","Spine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta (to the duodenojejunal flexure)","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (to the duodenojejunal flexure)', 'The ascending (fourth) part of the duodenum runs to the left of the aorta and ends at the duodenojejunal flexure, suspended by the ligament of Treitz. The SMA and its vein pass anterior to the third part, while the IVC lies to its right.'),
('0cd1e322-741c-5ec0-b46d-60c83bb6c759'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach bed includes which major artery?', '["Renal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic artery (running along the upper border of pancreas)","Abdominal aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mesenteric artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery (running along the upper border of pancreas)', 'The splenic artery courses along the upper border of the pancreas posterior to the stomach and forms part of the stomach bed (posterior relations). The renal artery, IVC, SMA, and abdominal aorta are not structures of the stomach bed.'),
('f09b081e-c6f1-59a5-a6de-9f5a6c7e4be2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastric lymph nodes are important because?', '["They drain into the spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","They are part of the inguinal group accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They drain the fundus only mediated by specific receptor activation","They are the first nodes to receive drainage from the stomach and are commonly involved in gastric cancer","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They are the first nodes to receive drainage from the stomach and are commonly involved in gastric cancer', 'The left gastric nodes lie along the left gastric artery and receive lymph from the proximal stomach and lesser curvature, making them a common first station for gastric carcinoma spread. They are unrelated to the inguinal group, the spleen, or the fundus alone.'),
('5a518253-1a5a-5061-b20e-c4662ec06725'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Virchow node in gastric cancer is due to spread via?', '["Portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct extension mediated by specific receptor activation","Hematogenous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thoracic duct (from left gastric nodes to cisterna chyli to thoracic duct to left subclavian vein)"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic duct (from left gastric nodes to cisterna chyli to thoracic duct to left subclavian vein)', 'Gastric cancer cells travel through lymphatic channels from the left gastric nodes to the cisterna chyli and up the thoracic duct, reaching the left supraclavicular node (Virchow node) at the junction with the left subclavian vein. Venous, direct, and hematogenous routes do not produce this classic sign.'),
('92e3a9ab-d212-5e77-b706-7a89456e9e10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Krukenberg tumor (ovarian metastasis from gastric cancer) spreads via?', '["Hematogenous resulting in decreased functional activity","Peritoneal seeding (drop metastases)","Lymphatic mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct extension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneal seeding (drop metastases)', 'Krukenberg tumors are bilateral ovarian metastases from gastric (usually signet-ring cell) carcinoma that reach the ovaries by transcoelomic peritoneal seeding and surface implantation. Hematogenous, lymphatic, and direct extension are not the mechanism of this drop-metastasis pattern.'),
('fbc1033a-946e-508f-a8c7-4648050f7643'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is anchored by which peritoneal folds?', '["Round ligament, falciform, coronary resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastrohepatic (lesser omentum), gastrocolic, gastrosplenic, gastrophrenic","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Falciform, coronary, triangular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rarely leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrohepatic (lesser omentum), gastrocolic, gastrosplenic, gastrophrenic', 'The stomach is suspended by four peritoneal folds: the gastrohepatic ligament (lesser omentum) to the liver, the gastrocolic ligament to the transverse colon, the gastrosplenic ligament to the spleen, and the gastrophrenic ligament to the diaphragm. The falciform, coronary, and triangular ligaments attach to the liver, not the stomach.'),
('e3a79555-e1bc-50ff-bb23-ac87fa790130'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The greater omentum functions as?', '["Absorptive organ resulting in decreased functional activity","Fat storage, immune surveillance, infection localization (policeman of the abdomen)","Secretory organ mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digestive organ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat storage, immune surveillance, infection localization (policeman of the abdomen)', 'The greater omentum stores fat, contains milky spots of lymphoid tissue for immune surveillance, and migrates to inflamed or perforated sites to wall off infection, hence the name ''policeman of the abdomen.'' It does not absorb, secrete, or digest nutrients as a primary role.'),
('7fa07272-6269-5798-b047-88e20045851d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The hepatorenal pouch (of Morison) is?', '["Between stomach and spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Between loops of bowel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The peritoneal recess between the liver and right kidney (most dependent part in supine position)","Between bladder and rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal recess between the liver and right kidney (most dependent part in supine position)', 'Morison pouch is the hepatorenal recess between the right lobe of the liver and the right kidney and is the most dependent part of the supine peritoneal cavity, collecting blood, pus, or ascites. The pouch between bladder and rectum is the rectovesical pouch in males.'),
('5e37043a-3250-5f18-878b-de0b6e7d713f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The subphrenic spaces are located?', '["Between stomach and liver resulting in decreased functional activity","In the pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Between the diaphragm and the liver (right and left)","Between loops of bowel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the diaphragm and the liver (right and left)', 'The subphrenic spaces are the right and left peritoneal recesses lying between the diaphragm and the superior surface of the liver, separated by the falciform ligament. They are neither pelvic nor interloop compartments and are not between stomach and liver.'),
('56eb80fd-d1d8-5def-ab87-6b915e19826e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left subphrenic space is related to?', '["Pancreas only resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left lobe of liver, stomach, spleen","Spleen only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left lobe of liver, stomach, spleen', 'The left subphrenic space lies between the left lobe of the liver and the diaphragm and is related inferiorly to the fundus of the stomach and the spleen. It communicates with the lesser sac through the gastrospheric and gastrophrenic ligaments.'),
('41e3c383-235d-592a-bac9-f4acf8b5eab7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastric dilatation may compress which vessel?', '["Celiac trunk","IVC","Renal artery","Aorta","SMA"]'::jsonb, 'IVC', 'A markedly distended stomach presses posteriorly on the inferior vena cava, reducing venous return and causing hypotension; the SMA syndrome similarly arises from compression of the third part of the duodenum. The celiac trunk, aorta, renal artery, and SMA are not directly compressed by gastric dilatation.'),
('5c0f57cc-687f-5d8c-a7aa-9b7b45504823'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Regarding the stomach and duodenum, which is true?', '["The duodenum has four parts","The lesser sac communicates with the greater sac via the epiploic foramen","All of the above","The stomach receives blood supply from branches of the celiac trunk","The ligament of Treitz suspends the duodenojejunal flexure"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Every statement is correct: the duodenum has four parts; the lesser sac opens into the greater sac through the epiploic foramen of Winslow; the stomach is supplied by celiac trunk branches; and the ligament of Treitz suspends the duodenojejunal flexure.'),
('2cad71e4-803c-5e19-8293-9c4827154c64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'What is the blood supply of the lesser curvature of the stomach?', '["Short gastric arteries","Superior mesenteric","Left and right gastroepiploic","Splenic artery","Left and right gastric arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Left and right gastric arteries', 'The lesser curvature is supplied by the left gastric artery (from the celiac trunk) and the right gastric artery (from the common hepatic artery), which anastomose in an arcade. The gastroepiploic arteries and short gastric arteries supply the greater curvature and fundus.'),
('7794b2cf-6feb-50f9-93b3-537a58f49e96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'What is the blood supply of the greater curvature of the stomach?', '["Splenic only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left and right gastroepiploic arteries","Short gastric only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left and right gastric leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left and right gastroepiploic arteries', 'The greater curvature is supplied by the right gastroepiploic artery (from the gastroduodenal artery) and left gastroepiploic artery (from the splenic artery), which form an anastomotic arcade. The gastric arteries supply the lesser curvature and the short gastric arteries supply the fundus.'),
('73146daa-1824-58ce-8048-d1d3a95039f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'What structures are in the hepatoduodenal ligament?', '["Hepatic veins resulting in decreased functional activity","Celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Common bile duct, hepatic artery, portal vein","Aorta leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common bile duct, hepatic artery, portal vein', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament, the free edge of the lesser omentum, carries the portal triad: the common bile duct anterolaterally, the hepatic artery proper anteromedially, and the portal vein posteriorly. The hepatic veins, celiac trunk, IVC, and aorta are not contained within it.'),
('b2896e07-3f51-5a39-9568-abb99e9f9824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'What is the major duodenal papilla?', '["Opening of accessory pancreatic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Ileocecal opening accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pyloric opening mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Opening of common bile duct and main pancreatic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Opening of common bile duct and main pancreatic duct', 'The major duodenal papilla (ampulla of Vater) on the posteromedial wall of the second part of the duodenum is where the common bile duct and main pancreatic duct open together, guarded by the sphincter of Oddi. The accessory pancreatic duct drains via the minor papilla, a separate structure.'),
('a8570cb2-e60c-5d62-a633-929a822b1329'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'What is the ligament of Treitz?', '["Ligament from liver to kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Suspensory ligament from duodenojejunal flexure to right crus of diaphragm","Ligament from stomach to spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suspensory ligament from duodenojejunal flexure to right crus of diaphragm', 'The ligament of Treitz is a fibromuscular band running from the right crus of the diaphragm to the duodenojejunal flexure, suspending it and marking the end of the duodenum. It is the landmark used to identify the DJ flexure on radiology and in surgery.'),
('ebbeea94-e8e2-57a5-a420-0787e763a2a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The incisura angularis is located on:', '["Greater curvature resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac end accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lesser curvature (body-pyloric junction)","Pylorus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser curvature (body-pyloric junction)', 'The incisura angularis is a notch on the lesser curvature that marks the junction between the body and pyloric part of the stomach and serves as a key landmark in barium studies. It is not on the greater curvature, pylorus, cardia, or duodenum.'),
('7de5ef86-5513-5fa9-8a95-33535fc25608'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach bed (posterior relations) includes:', '["Right kidney, duodenum, colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphragm, left adrenal, left kidney, spleen, pancreas, transverse mesocolon","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart, lung, pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver, gallbladder, IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm, left adrenal, left kidney, spleen, pancreas, transverse mesocolon', 'The stomach bed (posterior relations of the stomach) comprises the diaphragm, left suprarenal gland, left kidney, splenic vessels, pancreas, and transverse mesocolon. The liver, gallbladder, IVC, right kidney, duodenum, and colon are not posterior to the stomach.'),
('5394910c-1545-54a9-881c-0bbfee68c32c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Anterior relations of the stomach are:', '["Liver, anterior abdominal wall, left dome of diaphragm","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta, IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Transverse colon, small intestine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreas, spleen, left kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver, anterior abdominal wall, left dome of diaphragm', 'The anterior surface of the stomach is related to the left lobe of the liver above, the anterior abdominal wall, and the left dome of the diaphragm. The pancreas, spleen, left kidney, and great vessels form the posterior or inferior relations instead.'),
('cd641aae-5c60-50c3-8638-5e89df45c4ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lesser curvature blood supply is from:', '["Splenic only resulting in decreased functional activity","Left and right gastroepiploic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left gastric only mediated by specific receptor activation","Left gastric (celiac) and right gastric (common hepatic)","Short gastric leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric (celiac) and right gastric (common hepatic)', 'The lesser curvature receives blood from the left gastric artery (a direct branch of the celiac trunk) and the right gastric artery (arising from the common hepatic artery), which anastomose along the curvature. The gastroepiploic and short gastric vessels serve the greater curvature and fundus.'),
('dc29a855-eabb-5990-96dc-8d0248fec045'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The greater curvature blood supply is from:', '["SMA resulting in decreased functional activity","Short gastric only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left and right gastric mediated by specific receptor activation","IMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left gastroepiploic (splenic) and right gastroepiploic (gastroduodenal)"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastroepiploic (splenic) and right gastroepiploic (gastroduodenal)', 'The greater curvature is fed by the left gastroepiploic artery, a branch of the splenic artery, and the right gastroepiploic artery, a branch of the gastroduodenal artery. The SMA, IMA, gastric, and short gastric arteries do not supply the greater curvature arcade.'),
('ebe3e85d-2036-5d59-b4df-7ba4a3841f34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Short gastric arteries arise from:', '["Splenic artery","Left gastric","Common hepatic","Right gastric","Gastroduodenal"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery', 'Four to five short gastric arteries branch from the splenic artery and pass through the gastrosplenic ligament to supply the fundus of the stomach. They do not arise from the left gastric, common hepatic, right gastric, or gastroduodenal arteries.'),
('c78591b4-9c5c-5988-a34c-95515b5a066c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'All gastric veins drain into:', '["Azygos","IVC","Hemiazygos","Portal vein","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'All gastric veins ultimately drain into the portal venous system, reflecting the foregut origin of the stomach; the left gastric, right gastric, gastroepiploic, and short gastric veins converge on the portal vein directly or via the splenic or superior mesenteric veins. The azygos, hemiazygos, and IVC are systemic veins, not the drainage pathway.'),
('9e9efcac-26c1-593f-baa9-9c342baf44cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Left gastric vein drains into:', '["IVC","Renal vein","SMV","Splenic vein","Portal vein (directly)"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein (directly)', 'The left gastric (coronary) vein drains directly into the portal vein and forms important portosystemic anastomoses with esophageal veins at the gastroesophageal junction. It does not enter the IVC, renal vein, SMV, or splenic vein directly.'),
('801a96c8-851d-5c7a-978e-468a33b78e3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Right gastric vein drains into:', '["Splenic vein","Renal vein","IVC","SMV","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'The right gastric vein (pyloric vein) drains into the portal vein, usually directly near the porta hepatis. It does not empty into the splenic vein, renal vein, IVC, or superior mesenteric vein.'),
('a2d2e260-8fe9-5e9e-abd7-ce07c61db52c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Right gastroepiploic vein drains into:', '["Left gastric vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Portal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic vein mediated by specific receptor activation","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior mesenteric vein (gastrocolic trunk)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric vein (gastrocolic trunk)', 'The right gastroepiploic vein drains into the superior mesenteric vein, often combining with the middle colic vein to form the gastrocolic trunk of Henle. It does not drain into the portal vein, splenic vein, IVC, or left gastric vein.'),
('c95f463d-339c-51f8-9f52-bb0a3c3c8308'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Left gastroepiploic vein drains into:', '["IVC","SMV","Left gastric vein","Splenic vein","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The left gastroepiploic vein runs along the greater curvature and drains into the splenic vein, which later joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein. It does not enter the IVC, SMV, left gastric vein, or portal vein directly.'),
('adfeab78-d64c-5a98-8f44-5eee707cab46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Short gastric veins drain into:', '["Splenic vein","IVC","Right gastric vein","Portal vein","Left gastric vein"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The short gastric veins, accompanying the short gastric arteries in the gastrosplenic ligament, drain the fundus of the stomach into the splenic vein. They do not enter the IVC, portal vein, or the right and left gastric veins.'),
('501dc4c1-c16d-51b1-9b03-a0f61377f34e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastric lymphatic drainage includes:', '["Para-aortic only resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left gastric, right gastric, left and right gastroepiploic, pyloric nodes","Superficial inguinal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Internal iliac only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric, right gastric, left and right gastroepiploic, pyloric nodes', 'Lymph from the stomach drains along the named arteries to the left gastric, right gastric, left and right gastroepiploic, and pyloric node groups, which empty into the celiac nodes. The axillary, inguinal, internal iliac, and para-aortic chains are not primary gastric drainage stations.'),
('922c26d3-c496-5553-b887-33e83c9daaa4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is innervated by:', '["Only vagus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only phrenic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only intercostal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic (celiac plexus) and parasympathetic (vagus)"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (celiac plexus) and parasympathetic (vagus)', 'The stomach receives dual autonomic supply: parasympathetic fibers from the vagus (anterior and posterior trunks) promoting secretion and motility, and sympathetic fibers from the celiac plexus causing vasoconstriction and reduced motility. It is not innervated by the phrenic or intercostal nerves.'),
('9d29f514-7f98-589a-90fc-2ac8f34d33df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Anterior vagal trunk (left vagus) gives:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Celiac branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatic branches and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet)","Pyloric branches only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior gastric branches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic branches and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet)', 'The anterior vagal trunk, derived mainly from the left vagus, supplies hepatic branches to the liver and gallbladder and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet) innervating the anterior stomach wall. Celiac and posterior gastric branches originate from the posterior trunk instead.'),
('358879c3-b22b-5013-9ead-591245bee580'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Posterior vagal trunk (right vagus) gives:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Pyloric branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Celiac branches and posterior gastric branches","Anterior gastric branches without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatic branches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac branches and posterior gastric branches', 'The posterior vagal trunk, derived mainly from the right vagus, gives off celiac branches to the celiac plexus and posterior gastric branches supplying the posterior stomach wall. The hepatic and anterior gastric branches belong to the anterior trunk.'),
('16d3870d-2f6c-5aeb-9eb9-3633f02341f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenum has how many parts?', '["2 resulting in decreased functional activity","6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 mediated by specific receptor activation","5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 (superior, descending, horizontal, ascending)"]'::jsonb, '4 (superior, descending, horizontal, ascending)', 'The duodenum forms aC-shaped loop around the pancreatic head and is divided into four parts: superior, descending, horizontal, and ascending. The other choices give an incorrect number of segments.'),
('c7986067-36c9-5400-aad3-d522bc849408'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'First part of the duodenum is also called:', '["Duodenal cap (or ampulla)","Pyloric part","Duodenojejunal flexure","Suspensory part","None"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal cap (or ampulla)', 'The first part of the duodenum is the most dilated and mobile segment and appears as a smooth triangular cap on barium studies. The duodenojejunal flexure marks the end of the fourth part, not the first.'),
('e7e6a526-18f0-561d-8279-aeba35268511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Second part of duodenum receives:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only common bile duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common bile duct and main pancreatic duct at major duodenal papilla","Accessory pancreatic duct only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pancreatic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common bile duct and main pancreatic duct at major duodenal papilla', 'At the major duodenal papilla (ampulla of Vater), the common bile duct and main pancreatic duct open together into the descending duodenum. The minor papilla separately admits the accessory pancreatic duct.'),
('568dcace-a31c-5051-8411-c1ef2e00c66d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Major duodenal papilla is located in:', '["Duodenojejunal flexure resulting in decreased functional activity","Second (descending) part of duodenum","Third part mediated by specific receptor activation","First part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fourth part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Second (descending) part of duodenum', 'The major duodenal papilla sits on the posteromedial wall of the second (descending) part of the duodenum, about 8-10 cm beyond the pylorus, where the bile and pancreatic ducts descend. The other parts do not host this papilla.'),
('f0c70a1b-d40d-524a-afae-e6891982e8b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Minor duodenal papilla opens:', '["Main pancreatic duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Common bile duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini)"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory pancreatic duct (Santorini)', 'The minor duodenal papilla is the opening of the accessory pancreatic duct of Santorini, located about 2 cm above the major papilla. The main pancreatic duct and common bile duct open at the major papilla, not here.'),
('999d3b01-26e2-57ae-95f4-e2854e116931'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Ligament of Treitz attaches to:', '["Left crus of diaphragm","Pancreas","Aorta","IVC","Right crus of the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Right crus of the diaphragm', 'The suspensory ligament of Treitz runs from the right crus of the diaphragm and connective tissue to the duodenojejunal flexure, fixing it in place. The crura, aorta, and IVC are not its anchorage points.'),
('eb185b0a-7f00-556e-8e02-455f40e70a7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Ligament of Treitz is a landmark for:', '["The splenic flexure","Distinguishing upper vs lower GI bleeding","The ampulla of Vater","The ileocecal junction","The pylorus"]'::jsonb, 'Distinguishing upper vs lower GI bleeding', 'The duodenojejunal flexure marked by the ligament of Treitz divides the foregut from the midgut clinically; bleeding proximal to it is upper GI bleeding and distal is lower GI bleeding. It does not relate to the splenic flexure, ampulla, ileocecal junction, or pylorus.'),
('dd3a7b05-49bd-5048-9119-17c1dce76ad1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Omental bursa (lesser sac) anterior boundary:', '["Pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach, lesser omentum, gastrocolic ligament","Spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach, lesser omentum, gastrocolic ligament', 'The anterior wall of the lesser sac is formed by the posterior gastric wall, the lesser omentum, and the gastrocolic ligament. The pancreas and left kidney form the posterior boundary, not the anterior one.'),
('80c17df6-5b35-5181-9b8d-aaa75483651a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Omental bursa posterior boundary:', '["Stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreas, left kidney, transverse mesocolon","Diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lesser omentum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas, left kidney, transverse mesocolon', 'The posterior boundary of the lesser sac (stomach bed) comprises the pancreas, left kidney, left suprarenal gland, splenic vessels, and transverse mesocolon. The stomach and lesser omentum make up the anterior wall.'),
('90b13503-402f-5eba-ba24-72f4d32bc1d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Lesser sac communicates with greater sac via:', '["Cardiac orifice","Epiploic foramen of Winslow","Duodenojejunal flexure","None","Pyloric orifice"]'::jsonb, 'Epiploic foramen of Winslow', 'The epiploic foramen of Winslow is the only natural opening linking the lesser sac to the greater peritoneal cavity. The cardiac and pyloric orifices and the duodenojejunal flexure are unrelated gastrointestinal landmarks.'),
('115ea98b-0d00-57a5-b535-83d36ae0ad2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Epiploic foramen boundaries:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior: hepatoduodenal ligament; Posterior: IVC; Superior: caudate lobe; Inferior: 1st part duodenum","Anterior: liver; Posterior: kidney; Superior: diaphragm; Inferior: colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior: stomach; Posterior: pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior: hepatoduodenal ligament; Posterior: IVC; Superior: caudate lobe; Inferior: 1st part duodenum', 'The epiploic foramen is bounded anteriorly by the hepatoduodenal ligament, posteriorly by the IVC, superiorly by the caudate lobe of the liver, and inferiorly by the first part of the duodenum. The other listed relations describe structures not bordering the foramen.'),
('0ac00f33-898b-55e0-b137-cf098e779f39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach develops from:', '["Ectoderm","Hindgut","Midgut","Foregut","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Foregut', 'The stomach develops from the foregut, the most cranial segment of the primitive gut tube, which is supplied by the celiac trunk. The hindgut and midgut form more caudal foregut-independent derivatives.'),
('dbb4eedf-76e7-57c4-b94d-01beb48f9e6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Celiac trunk supplies derivatives of:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hindgut mediated by specific receptor activation","Midgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foregut (stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, pancreas, spleen)"]'::jsonb, 'Foregut (stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, pancreas, spleen)', 'The celiac trunk, the first major unpaired aortic branch, supplies all foregut derivatives including the stomach, proximal duodenum, liver, gallbladder, pancreas, and spleen. Midgut and hindgut organs receive blood from the SMA and IMA respectively.'),
('f8b7b35e-77e5-51b1-8f5c-d1b6b1869b7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'SMA supplies derivatives of:', '["Midgut (distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 transverse colon)","Stomach only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Hindgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foregut leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midgut (distal duodenum to proximal 2/3 transverse colon)', 'The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut: distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The foregut and hindgut receive supply from the celiac trunk and IMA respectively.'),
('7361bc13-b562-5992-96db-9e1bb7fe0542'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Right gastric artery is a branch of:', '["Common hepatic artery (or proper hepatic)","SMA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic mediated by specific receptor activation","Left gastric without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastroduodenal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic artery (or proper hepatic)', 'The right gastric artery typically arises from the common or proper hepatic artery and runs along the lesser curvature to join the left gastric artery. It does not originate from the splenic, SMA, or gastroduodenal vessels.'),
('8209a544-9df0-5359-9f6e-e29e5b05f4e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Left gastric artery is a branch of:', '["SMA","Celiac trunk","Common hepatic","Splenic","Aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac trunk', 'The left gastric artery is one of the three branches of the celiac trunk and runs along the lesser curvature toward the cardia. The SMA, common hepatic, splenic, and aorta are not its direct origin.'),
('d223988a-1f00-56b0-9ba8-68c43148c699'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Right gastroepiploic artery is a branch of:', '["Gastroduodenal artery","Splenic","SMA","Left gastric","Common hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroduodenal artery', 'The right gastroepiploic artery arises from the gastroduodenal artery and runs in the gastrocolic ligament along the greater curvature. The splenic artery gives the left gastroepiploic, not the right one.'),
('2f1fdbcf-9ff8-5853-86d6-9254eb9271cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Left gastroepiploic artery is a branch of:', '["Common hepatic","Celiac trunk","Splenic artery","Left gastric","Gastroduodenal"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery', 'The left gastroepiploic artery arises from the splenic artery near its terminal portion and courses in the gastrocolic ligament along the greater curvature. The gastroduodenal artery gives the right gastroepiploic instead.'),
('43c25a18-556e-5707-9892-4a35a5a8c0ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastroduodenal artery is a branch of:', '["Proper hepatic","Left hepatic","Splenic","Common hepatic artery","Celiac trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic artery', 'The gastroduodenal artery arises from the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum. The proper and left hepatic arteries and the splenic artery do not give rise to it.'),
('a7e4390d-86a6-537c-848b-5676e05d1bf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of:', '["Celiac trunk","Splenic","Gastroduodenal artery","Common hepatic","SMA"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroduodenal artery', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the gastroduodenal artery and forms arcades around the pancreatic head with the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery. The SMA supplies the inferior counterpart, not this vessel.'),
('af340e71-4492-5cea-879d-6f8b1b010b97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is a branch of:', '["Splenic","Superior mesenteric artery","Celiac trunk","Gastroduodenal","Common hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the superior mesenteric artery (or its first jejunal branch) and anastomoses with the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery around the pancreatic head. The gastroduodenal, celiac, splenic, and hepatic vessels are not its source.'),
('92c2fc48-657a-52e3-81c5-b8e0506e7936'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Pancreaticoduodenal arcades connect:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Portal and systemic mediated by specific receptor activation","SMA and IMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Celiac and SMA circulations"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac and SMA circulations', 'The pancreaticoduodenal arcades link the superior (celiac trunk) and inferior (SMA) pancreaticoduodenal arteries, creating a collateral pathway between the celiac and superior mesenteric circulations. The other choices describe unrelated vascular connections.'),
('0165c481-2db9-5ad1-bc41-f4cc990f2029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'First part of the duodenum is:', '["Retroperitoneal","None","Intraperitoneal (except proximal few mm)","Intraperitoneal throughout","Retroperitoneal throughout"]'::jsonb, 'Intraperitoneal (except proximal few mm)', 'Only the proximal few millimeters of the first part of the duodenum are intraperitoneal; the remainder of this part is secondarily retroperitoneal. The first part is thus the most mobile segment of the duodenum.'),
('3b6dcab4-b734-5165-a1c1-b986e6ac7830'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Second part of duodenum is related posteriorly to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreas only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta only mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right kidney, ureter, renal vessels, psoas major, IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Right kidney, ureter, renal vessels, psoas major, IVC', 'The descending duodenum lies anterior to the right kidney, right ureter, right renal vessels, psoas major, and inferior vena cava. The liver is a superior relation, not a posterior one, and the other options list incomplete structures.'),
('41190b4d-d6f7-5234-bb2c-df7974c34f9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Third part of duodenum is crossed anteriorly by:', '["Pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior mesenteric artery and vein","IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery and vein', 'The superior mesenteric vessels, along with the root of the mesentery, cross anterior to the third (horizontal) part of the duodenum at the L3 level. The aorta and IVC lie behind it, and the pancreas lies above it.'),
('98a7a1a2-7c66-58b0-93c8-92009b38aebd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Third part of duodenum lies between:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta and IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","SMA anteriorly and IVC/aorta posteriorly at L3","Pancreas and kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SMA anteriorly and IVC/aorta posteriorly at L3', 'At the L3 level, the third part of the duodenum passes between the SMA and SMV anteriorly and the aorta and IVC posteriorly, a region prone to SMA syndrome. The other options do not describe this vascular sandwich.'),
('a7bf8475-3c66-5853-8e3e-16aa3cf2baa6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Fourth part of duodenum ends at:', '["Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)","Pylorus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jejunum mediated by specific receptor activation","Ileocecal junction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenojejunal flexure (ligament of Treitz)', 'The fourth part of the duodenum terminates at the duodenojejunal flexure, suspended by the ligament of Treitz, where the duodenum becomes the jejunum. The pylorus marks the start of the duodenum, not its end.'),
('27534d4d-89a2-5c46-8c79-f7cfedd67f75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Ligament of Treitz muscle fibers are:', '["Fibrous only resulting in decreased functional activity","Elastic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Striated (diaphragm) and smooth (duodenum)","Smooth only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Striated only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Striated (diaphragm) and smooth (duodenum)', 'The ligament of Treitz contains striated muscle fibers from the right crus of the diaphragm and smooth muscle fibers from the duodenal wall, allowing it to actively suspend the duodenojejunal flexure. It is not purely fibrous, elastic, or single-type muscle.'),
('d87349cf-83b2-50bd-a5ff-66b722ad2c1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastric mucosa is lined by:', '["Cuboidal","Pseudostratified columnar","Simple columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous","Transitional"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar epithelium', 'The gastric mucosa is lined by simple columnar epithelium that invaginates to form gastric pits and glands. The other epithelial types line other organs, not the stomach.'),
('67560e41-2fde-5272-b58a-81947357a6bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete:', '["Mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastrin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pepsinogen mediated by specific receptor activation","Hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor","Bicarbonate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor', 'Parietal cells of the body and fundus secrete hydrochloric acid, creating the acidic gastric pH, and intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 absorption. Mucus comes from surface mucous cells, pepsinogen from chief cells, and gastrin from G-cells.'),
('b413c836-a5f1-5aec-969c-c8857172bb72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Chief (zymogen) cells secrete:', '["Pepsinogen","Intrinsic factor","Mucus","Gastrin","HCl"]'::jsonb, 'Pepsinogen', 'Chief cells in the gastric glands of the body and fundus secrete pepsinogen, which is converted to pepsin by hydrochloric acid. Intrinsic factor and HCl are parietal cell products, gastrin comes from G-cells, and mucus from surface mucous cells.'),
('8d8dae17-3019-53a2-85b3-d416f4b1019d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'G-cells secrete:', '["Gastrin","Pepsinogen","HCl","Somatostatin","Mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrin', 'G-cells, located mainly in the pyloric antrum, secrete gastrin, which stimulates acid secretion and gastric motility. HCl and pepsinogen come from parietal and chief cells, somatostatin from D-cells, and mucus from surface mucous cells.'),
('bd701643-8d0d-5b91-9fcc-a48eea842019'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Intrinsic factor is produced by:', '["G-cells","Enterochromaffin cells","Mucous neck cells","Chief cells","Parietal cells of the stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal cells of the stomach', 'Parietal cells produce intrinsic factor, which binds vitamin B12 and enables its absorption in the terminal ileum. G-cells secrete gastrin, chief cells pepsinogen, and mucous neck cells mucus, none of which make intrinsic factor.'),
('54b3d378-93d3-598b-bbc4-e78e816b4c46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Brunner glands are found in:', '["Duodenal submucosa (mainly first part)","Colonic submucosa","Gastric submucosa","Ileal submucosa","Jejunal submucosa"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenal submucosa (mainly first part)', 'Brunner glands are mucus-secreting glands in the duodenal submucosa, most abundant in the first part, where they protect against acidic chyme. The stomach, jejunum, ileum, and colon lack these glands.'),
('c35ecc50-39b9-55c7-aae9-99569003a027'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Pyloric sphincter is formed by:', '["Thickened circular smooth muscle","Longitudinal muscle","Oblique muscle","None","Mucosal folds"]'::jsonb, 'Thickened circular smooth muscle', 'The pyloric sphincter is a localized thickening of the circular smooth muscle layer that regulates chyme passage into the duodenum. Longitudinal and oblique muscle layers and mucosal folds do not form the sphincter.'),
('5e7098a9-441a-525f-8480-d865d2527098'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Most common site of duodenal ulcer is:', '["Second part resulting in decreased functional activity","Third part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fourth part mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior wall of stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior wall of first part of duodenum"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall of first part of duodenum', 'Duodenal ulcers most often occur on the anterior wall of the first part (duodenal cap), exposed to acid-peptic chyme before neutralizing secretions mix downstream. The later parts and stomach wall are less common sites.'),
('deb9af40-a6b8-500e-bc25-ff4947f2ef3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Posterior duodenal ulcer perforation leads to:', '["Free peritoneal rupture resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemothorax accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Retroperitoneal spread (lesser sac or retroperitoneal space)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retroperitoneal spread (lesser sac or retroperitoneal space)', 'A posterior duodenal ulcer erodes retroperitoneally, spilling contents into the lesser sac or retroperitoneal space rather than the free peritoneal cavity. Anterior ulcers cause free perforation into the greater sac.'),
('d07421df-fe05-504d-a692-6261db54f6db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastric ulcers are most commonly on:', '["Lesser curvature near incisura angularis","Fundus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Greater curvature without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pylorus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser curvature near incisura angularis', 'Gastric ulcers most commonly develop on the lesser curvature near the incisura angularis, a region less protected and prone to acid-peptic and ischemic injury. The fundus, cardia, greater curvature, and pylorus are less frequent sites.'),
('8c35d380-6e94-518b-a1c5-13f7f24c3578'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Artery most commonly eroded in bleeding gastric ulcer:', '["Short gastric","Gastroduodenal","Left gastric artery","Splenic","Right gastric"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric artery', 'The left gastric artery courses along the lesser curvature and is the vessel most often eroded by lesser-curvature gastric ulcers, causing severe bleeding. The short gastric, splenic, gastroduodenal, and right gastric vessels are less commonly involved.'),
('baeca82f-2e67-51b5-a44a-7e323ce6a11a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Prepyloric vein of Mayo marks:', '["CBD resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastroduodenal junction (pylorus)","Pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroduodenal junction (pylorus)', 'The prepyloric vein of Mayo crosses the anterior surface of the pylorus and serves as a surgical landmark for the gastroduodenal junction. The bile ducts, liver, and pancreas are unrelated to this surface landmark.'),
('efca0770-9d15-54d6-af42-53757c1c7e65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Celiac trunk arises at:', '["T12-L1","T11-T12","L3-L4","L1-L2","L2-L3"]'::jsonb, 'T12-L1', 'The celiac trunk is the first unpaired branch of the abdominal aorta, arising at the T12-L1 level just below the aortic hiatus. The SMA arises about 1 cm lower at L1, and the other levels listed are too caudal.'),
('9005aff9-7cd4-5374-ad39-62bb68caedae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'SMA arises at:', '["T12-L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 (1 cm below celiac trunk)","L4 mediated by specific receptor activation","L2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (1 cm below celiac trunk)', 'The superior mesenteric artery arises from the anterior aorta at the L1 level, about 1 cm below the celiac trunk and behind the neck of the pancreas. The celiac trunk arises higher at T12-L1, and the other levels are too low.'),
('567d1c48-7d57-534f-836c-1bde91e163bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Transpyloric plane passes through:', '["L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L1 (pylorus, gallbladder neck, renal hila)"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (pylorus, gallbladder neck, renal hila)', 'The transpyloric plane at the L1 level passes through the pylorus, neck of the gallbladder, renal hila, neck of the pancreas, and origin of the SMA. The other vertebral levels are too high or too low for this plane.'),
('8a95b356-932e-5d1b-835c-7299b2a2f576'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Portosystemic anastomosis at stomach involves:', '["SMV resulting in decreased functional activity","IMV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Splenic vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Left gastric vein with esophageal veins (azygos system)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric vein with esophageal veins (azygos system)', 'In portal hypertension, the left gastric (coronary) vein anastomoses with esophageal veins draining into the azygos system, forming esophageal varices. The SMV, IMV, and splenic vein participate in other portosystemic connections, not the esophageal one.'),
('61cbe55f-f883-5438-b6b5-aef24085d768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Greater omentum is derived from:', '["Dorsal mesogastrium","Mesentery proper","None","Ventral mesogastrium","Transverse mesocolon"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal mesogastrium', 'The greater omentum develops from the dorsal mesogastrium, which elongates to form the apron-like fold hanging from the greater curvature. The ventral mesogastrium forms the lesser omentum and falciform ligament.'),
('9dc45762-87ff-56bd-a70f-6a8892e2b808'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Lesser omentum is derived from:', '["Transverse mesocolon","Dorsal mesogastrium","Ventral mesogastrium","Mesentery proper","None"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral mesogastrium', 'The lesser omentum forms from the ventral mesogastrium, connecting the liver to the lesser curvature and first part of the duodenum. The dorsal mesogastrium gives rise to the greater omentum and other structures.'),
('a5c041f9-29b8-58f8-b0d1-ed5cac59f1bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastrocolic ligament connects:', '["Stomach to diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach to liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stomach (greater curvature) to transverse colon","Stomach to spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon to abdominal wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (greater curvature) to transverse colon', 'The gastrocolic ligament, a part of the greater omentum, connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon and carries the gastroepiploic vessels. The stomach-to-spleen connection is the gastrosplenic ligament.'),
('4b9b5638-2262-5d64-9f55-a822213a0c78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastrosplenic ligament connects:', '["Stomach to liver","Stomach to colon","Spleen to kidney","Stomach (greater curvature) to spleen","Stomach to diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (greater curvature) to spleen', 'The gastrosplenic ligament connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the spleen and transmits the short gastric vessels and left gastroepiploic vessels. The stomach-to-colon connection is the gastrocolic ligament.'),
('d5c305d9-b78c-5d90-b46a-d25873600b9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastrophrenic ligament connects:', '["Stomach to spleen","Diaphragm to kidney","Stomach to liver","Stomach (fundus/cardia) to diaphragm","Stomach to colon"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (fundus/cardia) to diaphragm', 'The gastrophrenic ligament suspends the stomach from the diaphragm, attaching along the fundus and cardiac region. It provides superior support for the stomach. The stomach-to-spleen, stomach-to-liver, and stomach-to-colon attachments are the gastrosplenic, hepatogastric, and gastrocolic ligaments respectively.'),
('c2b260af-57a3-5234-abca-4ea6956e6f94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Hepatogastric ligament is part of:', '["Lesser omentum (liver to lesser curvature)","Gastrosplenic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater omentum mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastrocolic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Falciform leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser omentum (liver to lesser curvature)', 'The hepatogastric ligament runs from the porta hepatis to the lesser curvature of the stomach and forms the pars flaccida of the lesser omentum, carrying the left and right gastric vessels. The gastrosplenic, gastrocolic, greater omentum, and falciform ligament are separate peritoneal structures.'),
('3a02a92c-14da-57a1-8000-96b560fe1a57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Hepatoduodenal ligament contains:', '["Celiac trunk resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatic veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CBD, hepatic artery, portal vein (portal triad)","Abdominal aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CBD, hepatic artery, portal vein (portal triad)', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament is the thickened free edge of the lesser omentum connecting the porta hepatis to the first part of the duodenum. Within it run the common bile duct, hepatic artery proper, and portal vein, together forming the portal triad. The celiac trunk, hepatic veins, aorta, and IVC are not contained in this fold.'),
('6cbf55f6-49d2-5cbd-91d8-a2c490ba0f27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Anterior relations of 1st part duodenum:', '["IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadrate lobe of liver and gallbladder","Portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadrate lobe of liver and gallbladder', 'The first part of the duodenum has a free anterior surface covered by peritoneum that is related to the quadrate lobe of the liver and the gallbladder, with the hepatoduodenal ligament forming its lateral boundary. The IVC and portal vein lie posterior to it, and the pancreas lies more inferiorly in relation to the second part.'),
('6be5b37d-d4b2-5b55-a20d-7f3aa03de963'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Posterior relations of 1st part duodenum:', '["Pancreatic head resulting in decreased functional activity","Aorta only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CBD, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein, IVC","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CBD, gastroduodenal artery, portal vein, IVC', 'Behind the first part of the duodenum lie the portal vein, gastroduodenal artery, common bile duct, and IVC, structures that a posterior penetrating ulcer may erode to cause severe hemorrhage. The pancreatic head lies more inferiorly and is related chiefly to the second part of the duodenum.'),
('03a1563a-30de-5542-917d-3cf72b9f728d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Anterior relations of 2nd part duodenum:', '["Aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse colon and jejunal loops","IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreas only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse colon and jejunal loops', 'The descending duodenum is crossed anteriorly by the transverse colon and its mesentery, with loops of jejunum also related to its front. The aorta and IVC lie posterior, and the pancreatic head is medial rather than anterior.'),
('1bc67824-b343-50ee-ab22-b039aa1c10c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Fourth part duodenum ascends left of:', '["Aorta (to duodenojejunal flexure)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SMA mediated by specific receptor activation","Spine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aorta (to duodenojejunal flexure)', 'The fourth part of the duodenum runs upward to the left of the aorta, crossing it anteriorly to end at the duodenojejunal flexure, which is anchored by the suspensory muscle (ligament of Treitz). The SMA and IVC are not the structures left of which it ascends.'),
('e907ae40-7c1b-5aac-bf31-d8ae93bac3a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Left gastric lymph nodes are important for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Inguinal drainage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fundus drainage only mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic drainage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastric cancer (first nodes to receive drainage)"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric cancer (first nodes to receive drainage)', 'Lymphatic drainage along the lesser curvature empties first into the left gastric nodes, making them the earliest station involved in the metastatic spread of gastric carcinoma. Their territory mirrors that of the left gastric artery, and fundic, splenic, and inguinal nodes are separate stations.'),
('1e2cd908-aaff-5b0a-b035-afd65259652a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Virchow node in gastric cancer:', '["Right supraclavicular resulting in decreased functional activity","Inguinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axillary mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left supraclavicular node via thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Left supraclavicular node via thoracic duct', 'Gastric cancer cells travel up the thoracic duct and enter the venous system at the junction of the left internal jugular and subclavian veins, producing a palpable left supraclavicular node (Virchow''s node). Right supraclavicular, axillary, and inguinal nodes do not receive this route of drainage.'),
('e90ab3cd-daf0-5f18-972e-5a83a3a81909'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Krukenberg tumor spreads via:', '["Peritoneal seeding (drop metastases to ovaries)","Hematogenous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct extension mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneal seeding (drop metastases to ovaries)', 'Krukenberg tumors are bilateral ovarian metastases from gastric cancer that implant by transcoelomic spread, in which malignant cells shed from the serosa travel in peritoneal fluid and seed the ovarian surface. Hematogenous, lymphatic, and direct extension are not the main routes of this spread.'),
('23d4e63b-c323-5ac8-b5bf-87c0e53d1212'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Greater omentum functions as:', '["Fat storage, immune surveillance, infection localizer","Absorptive organ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretory organ mediated by specific receptor activation","Digestive organ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat storage, immune surveillance, infection localizer', 'The greater omentum stores fat, hosts lymphoid aggregates called milky spots for immune surveillance, and migrates to inflamed or perforated areas to wall them off. It is not primarily an absorptive, secretory, or digestive organ.'),
('9492b0a7-29a4-5179-a1dd-6156b2dc7cd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Hepatorenal pouch (Morison) is:', '["In pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","Between bladder and rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between bowel loops mediated by specific receptor activation","Recess between liver and right kidney (most dependent supine)","Between stomach and spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recess between liver and right kidney (most dependent supine)', 'Morison''s pouch is the recess between the visceral surface of the right lobe of the liver and the right kidney. In the supine patient it is the most dependent part of the upper peritoneal cavity, so free fluid and pus collect here first and are detected by FAST ultrasound.'),
('2eb05ab4-0b94-54be-84db-3dcd29b0989c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Subphrenic spaces are:', '["Between bowel loops","Between diaphragm and liver","None","Between stomach and liver","In pelvis"]'::jsonb, 'Between diaphragm and liver', 'The subphrenic spaces lie between the diaphragm and the liver and are divided into right and left compartments by the falciform ligament. They are common sites for subphrenic abscess after upper abdominal surgery or intraperitoneal infection.'),
('ad5f488a-14fb-5ce2-9347-5818e1db88bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastric dilatation may compress:', '["Renal artery","Aorta","IVC","SMA","Celiac trunk"]'::jsonb, 'IVC', 'Acute gastric dilatation distends the stomach against the posterior abdominal wall and can compress the IVC, reducing venous return and causing hypotension. The renal artery, aorta, SMA, and celiac trunk are not the structures compressed by the enlarged stomach.'),
('eb0b62f2-c6e8-55ab-b7e7-73b6f0294a64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenum derives from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only foregut accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only midgut mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hindgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foregut (proximal to ampulla) and midgut (distal to ampulla)"]'::jsonb, 'Foregut (proximal to ampulla) and midgut (distal to ampulla)', 'The duodenum has a dual origin: the proximal portion up to the major duodenal papilla derives from the foregut, while the portion distal to the papilla derives from the midgut. This dual origin explains its dual blood supply from the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery.'),
('d259a473-a550-5bcf-b267-38f872b17e4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Rotation of the stomach during development causes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Both posterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right vagus becomes anterior mediated by specific receptor activation","change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left vagus to become anterior, right vagus posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Left vagus to become anterior, right vagus posterior', 'During development the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise around its longitudinal axis, carrying the originally left-sided left vagus nerve to the anterior surface and the originally right-sided right vagus nerve to the posterior surface. The adult anterior and posterior vagal trunks therefore reflect this rotation.'),
('aa1bd0d0-e687-514a-a975-6db93981c6e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The dorsal mesogastrium gives:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Falciform ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Lesser omentum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater omentum, gastrosplenic ligament, lienorenal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Greater omentum, gastrosplenic ligament, lienorenal ligament', 'The dorsal mesogastrium is the primitive dorsal mesentery of the stomach that gives rise to the greater omentum, the gastrosplenic ligament, and the lienorenal ligament as it elongates and rotates. The falciform, coronary, and lesser omentum derive from the ventral mesogastrium.'),
('ffac4fbf-9379-5213-aebe-63100688748c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The ventral mesogastrium gives:', '["Gastrosplenic ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Lesser omentum, falciform ligament, coronary ligament","Greater omentum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse mesocolon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser omentum, falciform ligament, coronary ligament', 'The ventral mesogastrium forms the lesser omentum (hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments), the falciform ligament, and the coronary ligaments of the liver. The gastrosplenic ligament, greater omentum, and transverse mesocolon arise from dorsal mesenteries.'),
('5efc296b-1939-5779-b11b-900744f752e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The supraduodenal artery supplies:', '["The first part of the duodenum (duodenal cap)","Third part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Second part mediated by specific receptor activation","Jejunum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fourth part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first part of the duodenum (duodenal cap)', 'The supraduodenal artery (of Wilkie) arises from the gastroduodenal artery and supplies the superior wall of the first part of the duodenum, the duodenal cap. It does not supply the second, third, or fourth parts or the jejunum, which have their own arterial supply.'),
('c59a0ec8-c100-553f-8657-d989e1cf877c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The most common site for duodenal ulcers is:', '["Third part","Anterior wall of the duodenal cap","Posterior wall of duodenal cap","Second part","Fourth part"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall of the duodenal cap', 'Duodenal ulcers occur most often on the anterior wall of the duodenal cap because this segment receives unneutralized acid-peptic gastric content before pancreaticobiliary secretions mix lower down. The posterior wall, when ulcerated, is more prone to erode the gastroduodenal artery and cause hemorrhage.'),
('0ff0c35d-7d8b-58be-bfaf-1f80c5303938'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The prepyloric vein (of Mayo) lies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Behind the pylorus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Across the anterior surface of the pylorus","On the stomach body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Across the anterior surface of the pylorus', 'The prepyloric vein of Mayo runs vertically across the anterior surface of the pylorus and serves as the surgical landmark for the gastroduodenal junction. It does not lie behind the pylorus, on the stomach body, or on the duodenum.'),
('985f7c32-7b7f-514f-8f8e-bb3430dff1ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastroepiploic vein drains into the:', '["Portal vein","Splenic vein","IVC","Superior mesenteric vein","Inferior mesenteric vein"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric vein', 'The right gastroepiploic vein runs in the gastrocolic ligament and drains into the superior mesenteric vein, often joining the middle colic vein to form the gastrocolic trunk of Henle. It does not drain into the portal, splenic, IVC, or inferior mesenteric veins.'),
('fad4bbf8-f521-554f-a250-7e5d9f0d96b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastroepiploic vein drains into the:', '["SMV","Left gastric vein","Portal vein","Splenic vein","IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The left gastroepiploic vein courses along the greater curvature alongside the left gastroepiploic artery and drains into the splenic vein. The SMV, portal vein, left gastric vein, and IVC are not its drainage targets.'),
('5b23a980-c606-5ae0-b84d-6fb0dfdee62c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The short gastric veins drain into the:', '["IVC","Left gastric vein","Portal vein","Splenic vein","Right gastric vein"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The short gastric veins drain the gastric fundus, traveling in the gastrosplenic ligament with the short gastric arteries to enter the splenic vein. They do not drain into the IVC, portal vein, or the right and left gastric veins.'),
('5ac1605d-4ad5-555f-966e-78fc99aed21a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastric vein drains into the:', '["SMV","Portal vein","Renal vein","Splenic vein","IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'The right gastric vein runs along the lesser curvature and drains directly into the portal vein, sometimes near the portal-splenic confluence. The SMV, renal vein, splenic vein, and IVC are not its direct drainage pathways.'),
('edf11746-14f9-5575-ac81-4a7fef7fdc8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastric vein drains into the:', '["Renal vein","Splenic vein","IVC","Portal vein","SMV"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'The left gastric (coronary) vein drains the lesser curvature and empties into the portal vein, forming the important gastroesophageal portosystemic anastomosis with the esophageal veins. The splenic vein, IVC, SMV, and renal vein do not receive it.'),
('758d7f6c-965d-503c-b099-3883beeeee43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastroepiploic artery is a terminal branch of the:', '["Left gastric","Common hepatic","Splenic","Gastroduodenal artery","SMA"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroduodenal artery', 'The right gastroepiploic artery is a terminal branch of the gastroduodenal artery, running along the greater curvature in the gastrocolic ligament. It is not a branch of the left gastric, common hepatic, splenic, or superior mesenteric arteries.'),
('9a86963e-97ec-5493-9217-3ae964fc8795'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastroepiploic artery is a branch of the:', '["Gastroduodenal","Celiac trunk","Left gastric","Common hepatic","Splenic artery"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery', 'The left gastroepiploic artery arises from the splenic artery near the splenic hilum and runs along the greater curvature to anastomose with the right gastroepiploic artery. It does not arise from the gastroduodenal, celiac trunk, left gastric, or common hepatic arteries.'),
('3c4312e3-646d-5447-bfb7-f19a0746689d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastroduodenal artery is a branch of the:', '["Common hepatic artery","Left hepatic","Proper hepatic","Splenic","Celiac trunk"]'::jsonb, 'Common hepatic artery', 'The gastroduodenal artery arises from the common hepatic artery and descends behind the first part of the duodenum, where a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode it to cause severe hemorrhage. It is not a branch of the left hepatic, proper hepatic, splenic, or directly of the celiac trunk.'),
('8a906fe3-e8a7-5558-870c-e8eae5c4b28f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery is from:', '["Splenic","Celiac trunk","Gastroduodenal artery","SMA","Common hepatic"]'::jsonb, 'Gastroduodenal artery', 'The superior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the gastroduodenal artery and supplies the pancreatic head and proximal duodenum, joining the inferior pancreaticoduodenal arcade. It does not arise from the splenic, SMA, celiac trunk, or common hepatic arteries themselves.'),
('18a9a0c4-abff-5fb8-a0b9-494d69b9ae0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is from:', '["Gastroduodenal","Superior mesenteric artery","Common hepatic","Celiac trunk","Splenic"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric artery', 'The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery arises from the superior mesenteric artery, often from its first jejunal branch, and supplies the lower pancreatic head and duodenum, anastomosing with the superior arcade. It is not derived from the gastroduodenal, common hepatic, celiac trunk, or splenic arteries.'),
('f35824b6-c282-5577-8c0a-2f9137899db5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The pancreaticoduodenal arteries form:', '["A venous plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","A neural network accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lymphatic network mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anastomotic arcades connecting celiac and SMA territories"]'::jsonb, 'Anastomotic arcades connecting celiac and SMA territories', 'The superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries form anterior and posterior anastomotic arcades that connect the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery circulations, giving the duodenum and pancreatic head a dual blood supply. They do not form venous, neural, or lymphatic networks.'),
('ee98ef95-00e3-58bd-b964-f44b742f9219'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenal cap is the most mobile part because:', '["None","It is attached to Treitz","It is retroperitoneal","It is intraperitoneal","It is posterior"]'::jsonb, 'It is intraperitoneal', 'Only the first part of the duodenum is intraperitoneal, so it moves freely, whereas the second, third, and fourth parts become secondarily retroperitoneal and are fixed posteriorly. Its mobility is not due to attachment to the ligament of Treitz or to retroperitoneal fixation.'),
('afeba628-e17d-58b5-b5b8-f812af039a7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The descending duodenum is retroperitoneal:', '["False, it is intraperitoneal","None","True, it is fixed posteriorly","All","It is mobile"]'::jsonb, 'True, it is fixed posteriorly', 'The descending (second part) of the duodenum is secondarily retroperitoneal: its anterior surface is covered by peritoneum while it is fixed against the posterior abdominal wall. It is therefore not freely mobile or intraperitoneal.'),
('d1c96101-65cf-5c7f-ae60-41731277236a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerve of Latarjet is important in:', '["Pancreatic surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgical vagotomy for peptic ulcer","Splenic surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical vagotomy for peptic ulcer', 'The nerves of Latarjet are the terminal gastric branches of the vagus trunks running along the lesser curvature that innervate the distal stomach and pylorus. They are deliberately preserved in highly selective (parietal cell) vagotomy for peptic ulcer disease so that antral emptying is not impaired.'),
('b4376e26-934c-5523-850f-9d632a974156'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The relation of the stomach to the spleen involves:', '["The gastrocolic ligament","The gastrosplenic ligament","The gastrohepatic ligament","The gastrophrenic ligament","None"]'::jsonb, 'The gastrosplenic ligament', 'The stomach connects to the spleen through the gastrosplenic ligament, which carries the short gastric vessels and the left gastroepiploic vessels. The gastrocolic, gastrohepatic, and gastrophrenic ligaments attach the stomach to the colon, liver, and diaphragm respectively.'),
('11446af5-8c93-5272-8a4c-3b4d2d0a9c7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The celiac trunk divides into:', '["Left gastric and hepatic only resulting in decreased functional activity","Left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatic and splenic only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastric and splenic only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries', 'The celiac trunk is the artery of the foregut and divides into the left gastric, splenic, and common hepatic arteries. It does not divide into only two of these branches.'),
('4c298597-6688-5687-a06e-ba56c51a6109'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The greater omentum is also called the:', '["Lazy apron resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Fatty apron without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Policeman of the abdomen"]'::jsonb, 'Policeman of the abdomen', 'The greater omentum is nicknamed the ''policeman of the abdomen'' because it migrates to sites of inflammation or perforation and wraps around them to contain the spread of infection. Lazy apron and fatty apron simply describe its gross appearance.'),
('65be260d-760d-599c-b487-2984d3ec4aac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lesser omentum connects:', '["None","Liver and colon","Liver and stomach","Stomach and spleen","Stomach and colon"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and stomach', 'The lesser omentum connects the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum, forming the hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments. It does not connect the liver to the colon or the stomach to the spleen.'),
('ced8993c-78a8-5e08-bc80-7df648198630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The space of Morison is also called:', '["None","Rectouterine pouch","Hepatorenal pouch","Rectovesical pouch","Vesicouterine pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatorenal pouch', 'Morison''s pouch is synonymous with the hepatorenal pouch, the peritoneal recess between the liver and the right kidney. The rectouterine, rectovesical, and vesicouterine pouches are separate recesses located in the pelvis.'),
('61a4e141-c346-5a25-9a4f-b6151a760535'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The most dependent peritoneal space in supine position is:', '["Hepatorenal pouch (Morison)","Rectouterine pouch (Douglas)","Rectovesical pouch","Subphrenic space","None"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatorenal pouch (Morison)', 'In the supine position the hepatorenal pouch (Morison''s pouch) is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity, so free fluid and blood collect there first. The rectouterine and rectovesical pouches become dependent only in the upright or Trendelenburg positions.'),
('11eae1f7-f6ce-5bd4-9640-6583e6a52d40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The most dependent peritoneal space in upright position in females is:', '["Hepatorenal pouch","Subphrenic space","Rectouterine pouch of Douglas","None","Vesicouterine pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Rectouterine pouch of Douglas', 'In the upright position in females the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas), lying between the rectum and uterus, is the most dependent peritoneal space and the site where pelvic fluid and pus pool. The vesicouterine pouch is shallower, and the hepatorenal pouch is dependent in the supine position.'),
('64967e2d-06c4-501c-989e-e2970f1fc645'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The most dependent peritoneal space in males is:', '["All","Rectovesical pouch","Subphrenic space","None","Hepatorenal pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Rectovesical pouch', 'In males the rectovesical pouch, lying between the rectum and urinary bladder, is the most dependent peritoneal space in the upright position. It is a common site for collection of intraperitoneal fluid in the male pelvis.'),
('8a6f7e94-eac6-5d7f-b451-e1f70dd152da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The falciform ligament attaches the liver to:', '["Anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colon mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm', 'The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and the diaphragm, with the ligamentum teres lying in its free edge. It does not attach the liver to the colon, stomach, or kidney.'),
('d19495a2-14e7-549d-afa1-96546d272cbf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The round ligament of the liver is:', '["All","Obliterated umbilical vein","Obliterated ductus arteriosus","Obliterated urachus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Obliterated umbilical vein', 'The round ligament (ligamentum teres) of the liver is the obliterated umbilical vein that carried oxygenated blood from the placenta to the fetus. The ductus arteriosus and the urachus obliterate into the ligamentum arteriosum and the median umbilical ligament respectively.'),
('6f8f9020-b036-5784-938e-6116fd7f71af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerve supply of the stomach (parasympathetic) is from:', '["Facial","Glossopharyngeal","Trigeminal","None","Vagus nerves (CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerves (CN X)', 'The parasympathetic innervation of the stomach comes from the vagus nerves (CN X), which reach the stomach as the anterior and posterior vagal trunks and stimulate acid secretion and motility. The facial, glossopharyngeal, and trigeminal nerves do not supply the stomach.'),
('f571c548-5d24-57b8-a744-155053364a0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerve supply of the stomach (sympathetic) is from:', '["Cervical plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lumbar plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacral plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Celiac plexus via splanchnic nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac plexus via splanchnic nerves', 'The sympathetic innervation of the stomach arises from the greater and lesser splanchnic nerves (T5-T12), relaying through the celiac plexus to inhibit gastric secretion and motility. The cervical, lumbar, and sacral plexuses do not supply the foregut-derived stomach.'),
('3747e78b-4c96-5e09-848a-8ec368bba3f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The prepyloric vein of Mayo is also called:', '["None","The coronary vein","The left gastric vein","The pyloric vein","The right gastric vein"]'::jsonb, 'The pyloric vein', 'The prepyloric vein of Mayo is also called the pyloric vein, as it runs across the anterior surface of the pylorus and marks the gastroduodenal junction. The coronary (left gastric) and right gastric veins are distinct vessels along the lesser curvature.'),
('ff9f9f74-aa29-5716-ad35-9fff2554483a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anastomosis between the left and right gastric veins is:', '["None","At the fundus","At the cardiac notch","At the incisura angularis","At the pylorus"]'::jsonb, 'At the cardiac notch', 'The left and right gastric veins anastomose along the lesser curvature, with their communication lying near the cardiac notch at the esophageal opening of the stomach. This region is clinically important for portosystemic collaterals in portal hypertension.'),
('781a683a-8ba4-5bb3-9a36-5b032050f38e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastroepiploic arch runs along:', '["None","The posterior wall","The lesser curvature","The anterior wall","The greater curvature of the stomach"]'::jsonb, 'The greater curvature of the stomach', 'The gastroepiploic arch is formed by the anastomosis of the right and left gastroepiploic arteries along the greater curvature of the stomach, running within the gastrocolic ligament. It does not lie along the lesser curvature or the anterior or posterior walls.'),
('956a1449-19ec-5796-aaa9-1d3909892c8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastric vasa recta (straight arteries) supply:', '["The pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","The gastric wall from the curvatures","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gastric wall from the curvatures', 'The vasa recta are short straight arteries arising from the gastric and gastroepiploic arteries along both curvatures; they pierce the gastric wall to supply the mucosa and muscular coats. The pancreas, spleen, and liver receive their own dedicated arterial supply rather than gastric vasa recta.'),
('34c48259-489e-5506-80df-8da2922385f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach expands most in which part?', '["None","The pylorus","The body and fundus","The antrum","The cardia"]'::jsonb, 'The body and fundus', 'The body and fundus have the thinnest walls and possess the rugae, so they distend the most to accommodate a meal. The pylorus, antrum, and cardia are relatively fixed and much less expansile.'),
('638e006f-c152-5afc-9633-3c229a8fad24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastric canal (magenstrasse) is:', '["At the pylorus resulting in decreased functional activity","A longitudinal fold along the lesser curvature","Along the greater curvature mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the fundus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A longitudinal fold along the lesser curvature', 'The magenstrasse is a groove formed by longitudinal mucosal folds along the lesser curvature through which liquids pass rapidly toward the pylorus. It is not located at the pylorus, fundus, or along the greater curvature.'),
('b2e86a73-2546-56ac-bd40-796880bfc68c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The circular muscle layer of the stomach is:', '["Uniform throughout resulting in decreased functional activity","Absent in the cardia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thickest at the pylorus (forming the pyloric sphincter)","Thickest at the fundus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thickest at the pylorus (forming the pyloric sphincter)', 'The circular muscle thickens at the pylorus to form the pyloric sphincter, which regulates the passage of chyme into the duodenum. The layer is neither uniform nor thickened at the fundus.'),
('e838c32e-9393-5cee-8aad-83be8d5c6b02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The oblique muscle layer of the stomach is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Absent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Outermost layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Innermost layer, mainly in the body and fundus","Middle layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Innermost layer, mainly in the body and fundus', 'The oblique layer is the innermost gastric muscle coat, found mainly over the body and fundus where it assists churning and mixing of food. The circular and longitudinal layers lie external to it.'),
('90bf3e9e-dfba-508a-b931-cd5472a1f6b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach has which type of rugae?', '["Circular folds resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Permanent folds without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Longitudinal folds that flatten when distended"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal folds that flatten when distended', 'Gastric rugae are longitudinal folds of mucosa and submucosa that smooth out as the stomach fills. They are temporary folds that flatten with distension, not permanent or circular structures.'),
('25ceceb2-a77e-5fd1-bd73-38495042f792'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenal cap is the part of the duodenum:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","That is narrowest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","That has the most valves mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","That is most dilated during a barium meal"]'::jsonb, 'That is most dilated during a barium meal', 'The duodenal cap (first part) is the most distensible and intraperitoneal portion of the duodenum, so it appears most dilated on barium meal studies. It is not narrowed and does not carry the most valves.'),
('73d959b3-df27-5405-a262-588e8942212d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The major duodenal papilla is typically at the level of:', '["L4","L3","L5","L2 vertebra","L1"]'::jsonb, 'L2 vertebra', 'The major duodenal papilla (ampulla of Vater) opens on the posteromedial wall of the second part of the duodenum at approximately the L2 vertebral level. The other listed levels are too low or too high.'),
('e0f65674-5ebd-52df-af2a-b900d948aace'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastroduodenal artery runs posterior to:', '["The pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","The stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The liver mediated by specific receptor activation","The first part of the duodenum","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first part of the duodenum', 'The gastroduodenal artery descends behind the first part of the duodenum, a location that makes it vulnerable to erosion by a penetrating posterior duodenal ulcer with potentially massive hemorrhage.'),
('7be98f6f-bef9-5622-ba90-838fe772a376'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastroepiploic artery runs:', '["In the lesser omentum resulting in decreased functional activity","In the transverse mesocolon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the gastrocolic ligament along the greater curvature","In the gastrosplenic ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the gastrocolic ligament along the greater curvature', 'The right gastroepiploic artery, a branch of the gastroduodenal artery, courses within the gastrocolic ligament along the greater curvature of the stomach. The lesser omentum and gastrosplenic ligament are not its location.'),
('2d1f8d17-6bd6-5878-a1b6-d1b89905d74e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastroepiploic artery runs:', '["In the lesser omentum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the transverse mesocolon mediated by specific receptor activation","In the gastrosplenic ligament and gastrocolic ligament","In the hepatoduodenal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the gastrosplenic ligament and gastrocolic ligament', 'The left gastroepiploic artery, a branch of the splenic artery, lies first in the gastrosplenic ligament near the spleen and then continues in the gastrocolic ligament along the greater curvature.'),
('08a592f1-3676-5200-adeb-1b69b4114747'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The short gastric arteries run:', '["In the gastrosplenic ligament","In the lesser omentum","None","In the gastrocolic ligament","In the gastrophrenic ligament"]'::jsonb, 'In the gastrosplenic ligament', 'The short gastric arteries pass from branches of the splenic artery to the gastric fundus within the gastrosplenic ligament, which connects the spleen to the stomach. They do not travel in the lesser or gastrocolic ligament.'),
('1a04f566-d335-5fec-9425-e66169770289'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The vagus nerves enter the abdomen through:', '["The aortic hiatus (T12)","None","The esophageal hiatus (T10)","The diaphragm directly","The caval opening (T8)"]'::jsonb, 'The esophageal hiatus (T10)', 'The anterior and posterior vagal trunks accompany the esophagus through the esophageal hiatus at the T10 level. The aortic hiatus (T12) transmits the aorta and thoracic duct, and the caval opening (T8) transmits the IVC.'),
('47a0cbe2-d820-577d-bc08-1cd9e93f93f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior vagal trunk lies:', '["On the left side resulting in decreased functional activity","On the anterior surface of the esophagus and stomach","On the right side mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the posterior surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the anterior surface of the esophagus and stomach', 'The anterior vagal trunk, formed mainly by the left vagus, runs on the front of the esophagus and stomach and gives hepatic and gastric branches. The posterior trunk lies on the posterior surface.'),
('683158f3-4d61-5fce-a1bd-cb4e7c2a9faf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The posterior vagal trunk lies:', '["On the posterior surface of the esophagus and stomach","On the anterior surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the left side mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the right side leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the posterior surface of the esophagus and stomach', 'The posterior vagal trunk, derived chiefly from the right vagus, descends behind the esophagus and stomach and gives off the celiac branch. It is the counterpart of the anterior vagal trunk located anteriorly.'),
('404c21e5-032a-59f1-8dbc-8f2e175a649f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The hepatic branch of the anterior vagus goes to:', '["None","The liver and gallbladder","The pancreas","The spleen","The kidney"]'::jsonb, 'The liver and gallbladder', 'The hepatic branch arising from the anterior vagal trunk passes in the hepatogastric ligament to supply parasympathetic fibers to the liver and gallbladder. It does not supply the pancreas, spleen, or kidney.'),
('e4c97491-7765-5527-93e5-3810c97d6579'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The celiac branch of the posterior vagus goes to:', '["The kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The liver mediated by specific receptor activation","The celiac plexus (supplies midgut organs)","The spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The celiac plexus (supplies midgut organs)', 'The celiac branch of the posterior vagal trunk terminates in the celiac plexus, distributing parasympathetic fibers to the foregut, midgut, and hindgut viscera. It is not directed to the kidney, liver, or spleen.'),
('2639eaff-361a-590a-9a47-c384b98787bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is supplied by the celiac trunk via:', '["Left gastric, right gastric, short gastric, and gastroepiploic arteries","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the left gastric mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the short gastric without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the gastroepiploic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric, right gastric, short gastric, and gastroepiploic arteries', 'The celiac trunk supplies the stomach through the left and right gastric arteries, the left and right gastroepiploic arteries, and the short gastric arteries. No single artery provides the entire gastric supply.'),
('e2871aec-0002-54a4-9133-ce03a4301eb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The two layers of the lesser omentum attach:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver to lesser curvature of stomach and first part of duodenum","Liver to colon mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach to colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stomach to spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver to lesser curvature of stomach and first part of duodenum', 'The lesser omentum connects the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach (hepatogastric ligament) and to the first part of the duodenum (hepatoduodenal ligament). It does not attach to the colon or spleen.'),
('eb6e487e-c5ac-502d-8145-904ac24b99b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament is the free edge of:', '["The falciform ligament","The greater omentum","None","The lesser omentum","The coronary ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser omentum', 'The hepatoduodenal ligament is the thickened, free right margin of the lesser omentum passing between the porta hepatis and the first part of the duodenum and enclosing the portal triad.'),
('be86611a-6e1c-5055-bd66-c463582e0377'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The portal triad (within hepatoduodenal ligament) consists of:', '["Hepatic veins, hepatic artery, portal vein","CBD, portal vein, IVC","None","CBD, hepatic artery, portal vein","Hepatic artery, portal vein, IVC"]'::jsonb, 'CBD, hepatic artery, portal vein', 'The portal triad comprises the common bile duct, hepatic artery proper, and portal vein lying within the free edge of the lesser omentum. The IVC and hepatic veins are not components of the triad.'),
('75692fc9-1717-504f-a81d-dc4294b0b45e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Within the hepatoduodenal ligament, the CBD lies:', '["Between the artery and vein","Behind the vein","Posterior and to the left","Anterior and to the right","None"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and to the right', 'In the portal triad the common bile duct is the most anterior and rightward structure, the hepatic artery lies anteriorly on the left, and the portal vein is placed posteriorly.'),
('aaee7203-4744-5f67-badd-cdd51b2f6d89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Within the hepatoduodenal ligament, the hepatic artery lies:', '["Posterior","Between CBD and portal vein","Anterior and to the right","Anterior and to the left","None"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and to the left', 'The hepatic artery proper occupies an anterior position to the left of the common bile duct. Thus, from front to back, the portal triad is arranged as bile duct and artery in front with the portal vein behind.'),
('c2660c91-02e8-59cf-953d-b3031a3fd5eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Within the hepatoduodenal ligament, the portal vein lies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Posteriorly (between CBD and hepatic artery)","Anteriorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Medially without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laterally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly (between CBD and hepatic artery)', 'The portal vein is the most posterior component of the portal triad, situated behind the common bile duct and hepatic artery in the free edge of the lesser omentum. It is the deepest of the three structures.'),
('dcd01d04-01ea-5808-a5b1-9e41080dd318'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The relation of the epiploic foramen anteriorly is:', '["Hepatoduodenal ligament (portal triad)","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caudate lobe of liver mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","First part of duodenum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatoduodenal ligament (portal triad)', 'The anterior wall of the epiploic foramen is the hepatoduodenal ligament enclosing the portal triad. The IVC lies behind it, the caudate lobe above, and the first part of the duodenum below.'),
('5abbee4a-306b-54b6-8646-d5d3360cbbc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The relation of the epiploic foramen posteriorly is:', '["Hepatoduodenal ligament","Caudate lobe","None","Duodenum","IVC"]'::jsonb, 'IVC', 'The posterior boundary of the epiploic foramen is the inferior vena cava, which separates the opening from the retroperitoneum. The hepatoduodenal ligament, caudate lobe, and duodenum bound the other sides.'),
('5458ce19-9d7e-5ceb-a50b-4102824f1b47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The relation of the epiploic foramen superiorly is:', '["Hepatoduodenal ligament","Duodenum","Caudate lobe of liver","None","IVC"]'::jsonb, 'Caudate lobe of liver', 'The roof of the epiploic foramen is formed by the caudate lobe of the liver. Below lies the first part of the duodenum, behind the IVC, and in front the portal triad in the hepatoduodenal ligament.'),
('a8179811-cefc-59f2-bd41-83105bb61bae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The relation of the epiploic foramen inferiorly is:', '["IVC","First part of duodenum","None","Hepatoduodenal ligament","Caudate lobe"]'::jsonb, 'First part of duodenum', 'The floor of the epiploic foramen is formed by the first part of the duodenum together with the hepatic artery. The remaining borders are the caudate lobe above, IVC behind, and hepatoduodenal ligament in front.'),
('5abc1ac6-d42d-58d1-8de5-268c2c28980b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lesser sac can be entered by passing through:', '["None","The gastrosplenic ligament","The gastrocolic ligament","The falciform ligament","The hepatoduodenal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The gastrocolic ligament', 'The lesser sac is readily entered surgically through the gastrocolic ligament, the part of the greater omentum lying between the stomach and transverse colon. The gastrosplenic, falciform, and hepatoduodenal ligaments do not provide such access.'),
('7b368bf0-05e9-5166-b2c9-c8899798563b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is related superiorly to:', '["Diaphragm (left dome)","Liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","Spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm (left dome)', 'The fundus and cardia lie immediately beneath the left dome of the diaphragm, which separates the stomach from the thorax and heart. The liver, pancreas, spleen, and colon relate to other surfaces of the stomach.'),
('6c5ba81b-3d39-5b18-b363-d916e8c07c72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is related inferiorly to:', '["Spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transverse colon and mesocolon","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse colon and mesocolon', 'The greater curvature and body of the stomach rest on the transverse colon and its mesocolon through the gastrocolic ligament. The diaphragm lies above the stomach and the spleen and liver are lateral relations.'),
('e560e8bd-990c-52e9-8f9f-285e5a978ac3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is related laterally on the left to:', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spleen (via gastrosplenic ligament)","Liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spleen (via gastrosplenic ligament)', 'The gastric fundus joins the spleen through the gastrosplenic ligament, which transmits the short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels. The liver and pancreas are related to the right and posterior borders.'),
('d7376e43-18f4-540e-a060-ac438d16439f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is related laterally on the right to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver (via lesser omentum)","Spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (via lesser omentum)', 'The lesser curvature attaches to the liver through the lesser omentum, making the liver the principal right-sided relation. The spleen lies to the left and is connected to the stomach by the gastrosplenic ligament.'),
('3b1da723-fbae-5bb7-a02f-af2b6d9de3e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The greater omentum arises from:', '["The lesser curvature resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater curvature of the stomach","The fundus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pylorus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater curvature of the stomach', 'The greater omentum hangs as a doubled apron from the greater curvature of the stomach and drapes over the transverse colon and small intestine. It does not arise from the lesser curvature, fundus, or pylorus.'),
('85c3de36-2a69-5933-9ac6-c5e445970507'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The greater omentum hangs down like an apron covering:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","The transverse colon and small intestine","The pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transverse colon and small intestine', 'From the greater curvature the doubled apron descends to cover the transverse colon and coils of small intestine, where it can migrate to wall off infection. It does not drape over the liver, spleen, or pancreas.'),
('50a0520a-b561-53d6-a9b0-843833e67f95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastrocolic ligament is the portion of greater omentum between:', '["Stomach and diaphragm","Stomach and spleen","None","Stomach and transverse colon","Stomach and liver"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach and transverse colon', 'The gastrocolic ligament runs from the greater curvature of the stomach to the transverse colon and encloses the gastroepiploic vessels. Between stomach and diaphragm, spleen, or liver other peritoneal folds are present.'),
('46fe1216-fea6-527a-b4b4-d6508149b1e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior two layers of the greater omentum descend from:', '["The greater curvature of the stomach","The lesser curvature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The pylorus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fundus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater curvature of the stomach', 'The anterior two peritoneal layers of the greater omentum originate from the greater curvature of the stomach and descend over the small bowel before recurring to form the posterior two layers.'),
('f121ab7b-69e3-5545-88f1-4b7ba731b955'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The posterior two layers of the greater omentum ascend to:', '["The liver resulting in decreased functional activity","The stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The transverse colon and its mesocolon","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The spleen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transverse colon and its mesocolon', 'The posterior two layers of the greater omentum ascend from the free inferior border to the transverse colon and transverse mesocolon and then continue over the pancreas. This arrangement allows the lesser sac to be opened through the gastrocolic ligament.'),
('51c9958e-a9d7-5a33-8efa-49ab697f687e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The space between the anterior and posterior layers of the greater omentum is:', '["The lesser sac (omental bursa)","The hepatorenal pouch","None","The greater sac","The subphrenic space"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser sac (omental bursa)', 'In the adult the layers of the greater omentum become fused, but the potential space between the anterior and posterior layers is continuous above with the lesser sac, the peritoneal compartment behind the stomach. The hepatorenal pouch and subphrenic space belong to the greater sac.'),
('0b65a3e3-5f15-5a46-96a2-3a964e0e3543'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastric artery anastomoses with:', '["The gastroduodenal resulting in decreased functional activity","The splenic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The right gastric artery along the lesser curvature","The left gastroepiploic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The right gastric artery along the lesser curvature', 'The left gastric artery descends along the lesser curvature to join the right gastric artery, forming a vascular chain along the lesser curvature. The gastroduodenal, splenic, and gastroepiploic arteries are not its direct anastomotic partners.'),
('66b946f8-8ad1-5a73-8870-e34056ae46c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastroepiploic artery anastomoses with:', '["The right gastroepiploic artery along the greater curvature","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The short gastric mediated by specific receptor activation","The splenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The left gastric leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The right gastroepiploic artery along the greater curvature', 'The left gastroepiploic artery, a branch of the splenic artery, runs along the greater curvature to anastomose with the right gastroepiploic artery and complete the gastroepiploic arch. The short gastric, splenic, and left gastric arteries are not its direct counterpart.'),
('4ae1076e-0cb4-5242-bcc4-4383e5e84d4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The short gastric arteries supply:', '["The duodenum","None","The body of the stomach","The pylorus","The gastric fundus"]'::jsonb, 'The gastric fundus', 'The short gastric arteries branch from the splenic artery and reach the gastric fundus through the gastrosplenic ligament. The body, pylorus, and duodenum receive their blood from other gastric arteries.'),
('b273f98c-1e3d-5c0d-aec4-fb793f8690ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastric veins drain into:', '["None","The portal venous system","The IVC","The azygos system","The lumbar veins"]'::jsonb, 'The portal venous system', 'The left and right gastric, gastroepiploic, and short gastric veins all empty into the portal venous system, either directly or through the splenic and superior mesenteric veins. They do not normally drain into the IVC, azygos, or lumbar veins; such collateral routes open only as varices in portal hypertension.'),
('ad36f87a-7bf9-5680-92e0-9becac6e790c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lymphatic drainage of the fundus and upper body of the stomach is to:', '["None","Right gastric nodes","Left gastroepiploic nodes","Right gastroepiploic nodes","Left gastric nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric nodes', 'Lymph from the fundus and upper body of the stomach drains along the short gastric and left gastric arteries to the left gastric (superior gastric) lymph nodes. The right gastric and gastroepiploic nodes drain the other gastric regions.'),
('55fa9a1d-bff0-5267-9ff7-6b146df8ecd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lymphatic drainage of the pyloric region is to:', '["Right gastric and pyloric nodes","Pancreatic nodes","Left gastric nodes","Splenic nodes","None"]'::jsonb, 'Right gastric and pyloric nodes', 'The pyloric region drains along the right gastric and gastroduodenal arteries to the right gastric and pyloric lymph nodes. The left gastric and splenic nodes drain respectively the upper part and the splenic-related region of the stomach.'),
('6d62d0bd-d153-5983-b2b4-7b8a5ff9095e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lymphatic drainage of the greater curvature (left) is to:', '["Left gastric nodes","Pyloric nodes","None","Left gastroepiploic and splenic nodes","Right gastroepiploic nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastroepiploic and splenic nodes', 'The left half of the greater curvature, together with the fundus, drains along the left gastroepiploic and short gastric arteries to the left gastroepiploic and splenic lymph nodes. The right-sided nodes drain the right half of the curvature.'),
('921d69fa-4b2b-5b9b-bc1c-699261e65ea8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The lymphatic drainage of the greater curvature (right) is to:', '["Right gastroepiploic and pyloric nodes","Left gastroepiploic nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Left gastric nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splenic nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right gastroepiploic and pyloric nodes', 'The right half of the greater curvature drains along the right gastroepiploic artery to the right gastroepiploic and pyloric lymph nodes. The left gastroepiploic, left gastric, and splenic nodes serve the left side and upper parts of the stomach.'),
('8c7c5c61-1e0e-55b7-807c-432103323ff2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach develops a rotation of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","180 degrees accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","90 degrees clockwise around its longitudinal axis","45 degrees without affecting the primary tissue pathway","rotation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '90 degrees clockwise around its longitudinal axis', 'The developing stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise about its longitudinal axis, bringing the original left side forward and the right side backward. This rotation also remodels the investing dorsal and ventral mesenteries.'),
('6a2ceb16-0f1a-5ffb-b043-e7eb53303721'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'During gastric rotation, the dorsal mesogastrium becomes:', '["The lesser omentum","None","The falciform ligament","The greater omentum","The coronary ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The greater omentum', 'After the 90-degree rotation, the dorsal mesogastrium elongates and gives rise to the greater omentum as well as the gastrosplenic and gastrocolic ligaments. The ventral mesogastrium instead becomes the lesser omentum.'),
('52c91889-bd29-5556-af33-753d16bc8338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'During gastric rotation, the ventral mesogastrium becomes:', '["The gastrosplenic ligament","The lienorenal ligament","The lesser omentum","None","The greater omentum"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser omentum', 'The ventral mesogastrium, together with the developing liver, forms the lesser omentum joining the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum. The dorsal mesogastrium becomes the greater omentum, and the gastrosplenic and lienorenal ligaments derive from it.'),
('b8bb92f8-f8e9-54ff-8e8c-cd198283e1ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is most commonly injured in:', '["Gunshot resulting in decreased functional activity","Penetrating trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stabbing mediated by specific receptor activation","Blunt trauma (especially the greater curvature)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blunt trauma (especially the greater curvature)', 'The stomach is most commonly injured in blunt abdominal trauma, particularly at the greater curvature, where it is vulnerable to compression against the spine when distended. Penetrating wounds such as gunshot and stabbing injure the stomach less commonly.'),
('f37ed06e-3159-529b-9b31-584c5301b487'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the pyloric part of the stomach includes:', '["Left gastric only resulting in decreased functional activity","Left gastroepiploic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Right gastric and right gastroepiploic arteries","Short gastric only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right gastric and right gastroepiploic arteries', 'The pyloric part is supplied by the right gastric artery along the lesser curvature and the right gastroepiploic artery along the greater curvature, both ultimately derived from the celiac trunk. The left gastric, left gastroepiploic, and short gastric vessels supply more proximal regions of the stomach.'),
('9b000f95-8c4e-54f1-8c5e-c5d62edfa4bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the gastric fundus includes:', '["Only left gastric resulting in decreased functional activity","Only right gastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only gastroepiploic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Short gastric arteries and left gastric artery"]'::jsonb, 'Short gastric arteries and left gastric artery', 'The fundus is supplied by the short gastric arteries, which arise from the splenic artery and pass in the gastrosplenic ligament, reinforced by branches of the left gastric artery. The right gastric and gastroepiploic arteries supply more distal regions.'),
('eda2c710-06a6-5d73-8da6-72d26d42042b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the gastric body along the lesser curvature is from:', '["Short gastric arteries","Left and right gastric arteries","Splenic artery","None","Gastroepiploic arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Left and right gastric arteries', 'The lesser curvature of the body receives the left gastric artery (from the celiac trunk) and the right gastric artery (from the common hepatic artery), which form an anastomotic arch. The short gastric, splenic, and gastroepiploic vessels supply other parts of the stomach.'),
('25d36d48-00e6-5e7f-bc78-cd4df9d00e54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the gastric body along the greater curvature is from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Left and right gastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short gastric mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left and right gastroepiploic arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Left and right gastroepiploic arteries', 'The greater curvature of the body is supplied by the left gastroepiploic artery (from the splenic artery) and the right gastroepiploic artery (from the gastroduodenal artery), which form the gastroepiploic arch. The gastric arteries supply the lesser curvature and the short gastrics the fundus.'),
('44f70daf-eca6-54f3-bd7c-1954523954b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenum is connected to the liver by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The gastrosplenic ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The hepatoduodenal ligament (free edge of lesser omentum)","The gastrocolic ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The falciform ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hepatoduodenal ligament (free edge of lesser omentum)', 'The first part of the duodenum is connected to the liver by the hepatoduodenal ligament, the free edge of the lesser omentum that carries the portal triad. The gastrosplenic, gastrocolic, and falciform ligaments attach to other structures.'),
('e7193d16-19b9-58cc-8ebb-93b34c1beb4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The first part of the duodenum is supplied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior mesenteric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supraduodenal artery (from gastroduodenal)","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supraduodenal artery (from gastroduodenal)', 'The first (superior) part of the duodenum is supplied by the supraduodenal artery, a branch of the gastroduodenal artery. The pancreaticoduodenal arteries and the SMA supply the more distal parts of the duodenum.'),
('ae2ea78c-5b99-587e-b004-f7ee0e9e2f8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The second part of the duodenum is supplied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","SMA branches only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Jejunal arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Supraduodenal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior and inferior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arcades"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arcades', 'The second part is supplied by the superior (from the gastroduodenal artery) and inferior (from the SMA) pancreaticoduodenal arteries, which form anterior and posterior arcades. The supraduodenal and jejunal arteries serve other parts.'),
('a7a031b5-472c-5f09-9148-9698a33a6a9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The third and fourth parts of the duodenum are supplied by:', '["Supraduodenal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal and jejunal branches of SMA","Gastroduodenal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior pancreaticoduodenal and jejunal branches of SMA', 'The third and fourth parts are supplied by the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery (from the SMA) and jejunal branches of the SMA, reflecting midgut embryologic origin. The celiac-derived vessels supply the proximal duodenum.'),
('95908254-3411-5e01-b4d6-f7093c68917a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenal veins drain into:', '["IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","SMV (superior mesenteric vein) and splenic vein","Portal vein directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SMV (superior mesenteric vein) and splenic vein', 'The duodenal veins drain into the superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein, which join to form the portal vein. They do not drain directly into the IVC or into the portal vein itself.'),
('e7357d61-664d-58ce-bd93-4b27d05bf87e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerve supply of the duodenum (parasympathetic) is from:', '["Facial","Glossopharyngeal","Pelvic splanchnic","Vagus nerves","None"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerves', 'Parasympathetic innervation of the duodenum is provided by the vagus nerves (CN X), which travel along the foregut and midgut. The facial, glossopharyngeal, and pelvic splanchnic nerves do not innervate the duodenum.'),
('d5090310-9412-5b62-8f5e-50777e1a54f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerve supply of the duodenum (sympathetic) is from:', '["Celiac and superior mesenteric plexuses","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lumbar splanchnic mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacral splanchnic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Celiac and superior mesenteric plexuses', 'Sympathetic supply of the duodenum comes from the celiac plexus (proximal part) and the superior mesenteric plexus (distal part), carried by splanchnic nerves. Lumbar and sacral splanchnics innervate the hindgut.'),
('00a61e41-411f-5aff-bf01-8d9c94dcc231'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenal lymphatics drain to:', '["Hepatic nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Splenic nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left gastric nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreaticoduodenal and superior mesenteric nodes","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreaticoduodenal and superior mesenteric nodes', 'Lymph from the duodenum drains to the pancreaticoduodenal nodes and then to the superior mesenteric nodes, following the arterial supply. Hepatic, splenic, and left gastric nodes drain other foregut structures.'),
('4a532dca-2eea-5c87-a8bc-8d8859f7a766'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The pylorus is located at the level of:', '["L2 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 (transpyloric plane)","T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","L3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1 (transpyloric plane)', 'The pylorus lies on the transpyloric plane at the level of the L1 vertebra, which also passes through the neck of the gallbladder, renal hila, and origin of the SMA. It is a key surface landmark of the abdomen.'),
('a9232442-4dc2-5fac-b55a-b818965bc91d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The pyloric vein of Mayo is a surgical guide to:', '["The CBD resulting in decreased functional activity","The pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The pylorus (gastroduodenal junction)","The cystic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pylorus (gastroduodenal junction)', 'The prepyloric vein of Mayo crosses the anterior surface of the gastroduodenal junction and marks the pylorus during surgery. It does not indicate the CBD, pancreas, or cystic duct.'),
('4998a861-5d41-5a13-8d3c-accd15234119'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The duodenum is unique in that it:', '["Has taenia coli","Is partially retroperitoneal","None","Has mesentery","Is entirely intraperitoneal"]'::jsonb, 'Is partially retroperitoneal', 'The duodenum is partially retroperitoneal, with the first part intraperitoneal and the second, third, and fourth parts secondarily retroperitoneal. It has no taenia coli, no mesentery, and no entirely intraperitoneal covering.'),
('0db38fa1-c60d-5620-ba0e-c3bfcd008e72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The jejunum begins at the duodenojejunal flexure at:', '["L3 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 level (left of L2)","L5 mediated by specific receptor activation","T12 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 level (left of L2)', 'The duodenojejunal flexure, where the jejunum begins, lies at approximately the L1-L2 level on the left side of the spine and is suspended by the ligament of Treitz. This landmark separates the duodenum from the jejunum.'),
('ba93e0ed-953c-539f-a5bb-342df2551a13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The ligament of Treitz is identified at surgery by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Identifying the ileocecal valve mediated by specific receptor activation","Following the jejunum proximally or the duodenum distally","Looking for the pylorus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Following the jejunum proximally or the duodenum distally', 'The ligament of Treitz is found by tracing the jejunum proximally or the duodenum distally to the duodenojejunal flexure, where it attaches to the right crus of the diaphragm. The ileocecal valve and pylorus are remote landmarks.'),
('a94d2800-aa76-5692-8952-e20642f65803'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach is the most dilated part of:', '["None","The midgut","The hindgut","The foregut","The alimentary canal"]'::jsonb, 'The alimentary canal', 'The stomach is the most dilated and distensible part of the entire alimentary canal, with an adult capacity of about 1.5-2 liters. Its shape and position vary with distension and body habitus.'),
('83073388-7fea-5942-9a81-4ed3953a39a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach capacity at birth is approximately:', '["2 L","500 mL","30 mL","1 L","100 mL"]'::jsonb, '30 mL', 'At birth the stomach capacity is approximately 30 mL, matching the small volume of early milk feeds. It expands progressively through infancy and childhood.'),
('e10c9593-509f-57c9-84cb-bad91fdf2170'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The adult stomach capacity is approximately:', '["5 L","500 mL","1.5-2 L","3 L","100 mL"]'::jsonb, '1.5-2 L', 'The adult stomach holds approximately 1.5-2 liters, allowing storage and gradual mixing of a meal. The other values are either too small or far exceed normal capacity.'),
('a48677b4-0723-5aa9-9c86-4d526234368f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach stores food and mixes it with:', '["Saliva resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastric secretions (digestive enzymes and acid)","Pancreatic juice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric secretions (digestive enzymes and acid)', 'The stomach stores food and mixes it with gastric secretions, including hydrochloric acid, pepsinogen, mucus, and intrinsic factor. Saliva acts in the mouth, while bile and pancreatic juice act in the duodenum.'),
('179cef25-b640-56c7-8a59-e99d3f7d3e0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach secretes which hormone?', '["Glucagon resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Insulin mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastrin (from G-cells in the pyloric antrum)","CCK leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrin (from G-cells in the pyloric antrum)', 'Gastrin is secreted by G-cells in the pyloric antrum in response to food, especially protein, and stimulates acid secretion. Glucagon and insulin are pancreatic hormones, while secretin and CCK are duodenal hormones.'),
('0f0f234b-9ab9-54cc-97ca-530d08ad313f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Gastrin stimulates:', '["Acid secretion by parietal cells","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bicarbonate secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pepsinogen secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acid secretion by parietal cells', 'Gastrin stimulates acid secretion by parietal cells, both directly and indirectly through histamine release from enterochromaffin-like cells. It does not directly drive bicarbonate secretion.'),
('53426f51-231b-5833-8e56-6966f19f77c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The alkaline mucus in the stomach is secreted by:', '["Chief cells resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","G-cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Parietal cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mucous neck cells and surface epithelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Mucous neck cells and surface epithelial cells', 'The alkaline mucus that protects the gastric mucosa from acid and pepsin is produced by mucous neck cells and surface epithelial cells. Chief, parietal, and G-cells secrete pepsinogen, acid, and gastrin respectively.'),
('19a9acd4-c647-5257-9457-6ebd7edd09a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Pepsinogen is converted to active pepsin by:', '["Mucus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastrin mediated by specific receptor activation","Bicarbonate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hydrochloric acid (low pH)"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrochloric acid (low pH)', 'Pepsinogen is converted to active pepsin by the low pH produced by hydrochloric acid, which also provides the optimum environment for pepsin activity. Mucus, gastrin, and bicarbonate do not perform this conversion.'),
('88dcbc06-44e4-537d-a62a-96556f75ed22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Intrinsic factor is essential for:', '["Calcium absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Iron absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Folate absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum', 'Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and carries it to the terminal ileum, where absorption occurs via cubilin receptors on enterocytes. Iron, calcium, and folate absorption do not require intrinsic factor.'),
('c016cb58-7cde-531b-8cd9-629c9277425c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Pernicious anemia results from:', '["Lack of intrinsic factor (autoimmune destruction of parietal cells)","Folate deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Iron deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vitamin C deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of intrinsic factor (autoimmune destruction of parietal cells)', 'Pernicious anemia results from autoimmune destruction of parietal cells, causing intrinsic factor deficiency and failure of vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum. Folate, iron, and vitamin C deficiencies produce other anemias.'),
('de956a64-8d3d-54a0-8450-902a05ea452a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The gastric emptying time is prolonged by:', '["Carbohydrates","Proteins","Acid","Fatty foods","None"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty foods', 'Fatty foods delay gastric emptying by releasing cholecystokinin (CCK) and activating the enterogastric reflex, which inhibit gastric motility. Carbohydrates and proteins empty more rapidly from the stomach.'),
('f025317f-9f16-58f6-9828-42ab06d22e96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The abdominal part of the esophagus is:', '["15 cm","2.5 cm long","10 cm","None","5 cm"]'::jsonb, '2.5 cm long', 'The abdominal esophagus is about 2.5 cm long, running from the esophageal hiatus to the cardia of the stomach. The cervical and thoracic parts account for the remaining length.'),
('7563ab6b-7d25-5e70-9310-d3c4b6df0f0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The abdominal esophagus is related anteriorly to:', '["IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","Left lobe of the liver","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left lobe of the liver', 'Anterior to the abdominal esophagus lie the left lobe of the liver and, at the hiatus, the diaphragm. The aorta and IVC lie more posteriorly and are not anterior relations.'),
('f20a2c3b-c8cd-596d-85b3-bc405ca9e9e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The abdominal esophagus is related posteriorly to:', '["Left kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right crus of the diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Right crus of the diaphragm', 'Posterior to the abdominal esophagus lies the right crus of the diaphragm, which also forms part of the esophageal hiatus. The aorta, IVC, and left kidney are more distant posterior structures.'),
('f3fd987d-d55b-544b-b5b6-8bd5ddbd8424'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The vagus nerves are positioned around the esophagus as:', '["Both posterior resulting in decreased functional activity","Left posterior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right anterior mediated by specific receptor activation","Both anterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior (left vagus) and posterior (right vagus) trunks"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (left vagus) and posterior (right vagus) trunks', 'During development the stomach rotates so that the left vagus forms the anterior vagal trunk and the right vagus the posterior vagal trunk. These trunks then run along the esophagus and stomach.'),
('879a5079-dc6b-5470-b276-7c8a9b367cde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior vagus supplies:', '["The colon resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior stomach, liver, gallbladder, and biliary tree","The small intestine mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior stomach and pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior stomach, liver, gallbladder, and biliary tree', 'The anterior vagal trunk supplies the anterior stomach, liver, gallbladder, and biliary tree via hepatic branches and the anterior gastric nerves of Latarjet. The posterior trunk supplies the posterior stomach and pancreas.'),
('eab673c0-8dc7-5f93-801c-7026286307ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'In the study of Stomach Duodenum, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('acd0ca16-f71b-5b72-9e93-812418c10e80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The cardiac notch (incisura cardialis) is:', '["The angle between duodenum and stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","The notch at incisura angularis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The notch at the pylorus mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The angle between the esophagus and the fundus of the stomach"]'::jsonb, 'The angle between the esophagus and the fundus of the stomach', 'The cardiac notch is the acute angle between the esophagus and the gastric fundus at the cardia. It is distinct from the incisura angularis and the pylorus.'),
('8c0da0fc-a7b3-52be-a022-5f654b571f90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The incisura angularis is:', '["A notch on the greater curvature resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cardiac notch mediated by specific receptor activation","The pyloric opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A notch on the lesser curvature marking the junction of body and pyloric part"]'::jsonb, 'A notch on the lesser curvature marking the junction of body and pyloric part', 'The incisura angularis is a notch on the lesser curvature marking the junction of the body and pyloric part of the stomach. The greater curvature and cardiac notch are different landmarks.'),
('ee2c11c1-3b39-5f5b-99b9-0f0c4304fe7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The stomach bed (posterior relations) includes all except:', '["Diaphragm","Left adrenal gland","Liver (left lobe)","Left kidney","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (left lobe)', 'The stomach bed comprises posterior relations such as the diaphragm, left adrenal, left kidney, spleen, pancreas, and transverse mesocolon. The left lobe of the liver is an anterior relation, not part of the bed.'),
('c2db05f6-13e5-58c0-a6bd-fb673edb9e41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior relations of the stomach include:', '["Liver (left lobe), anterior abdominal wall, diaphragm (left dome)","Aorta, IVC, celiac trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Transverse colon, small intestine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pancreas, spleen, left kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (left lobe), anterior abdominal wall, diaphragm (left dome)', 'Anterior relations of the stomach include the left lobe of the liver, the anterior abdominal wall, and the left dome of the diaphragm. The pancreas, spleen, and left kidney lie posteriorly in the stomach bed.'),
('098bc2f5-77ab-5adc-81b9-7aa43ae1179b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the lesser curvature of the stomach is from:', '["Left gastric (celiac trunk) and right gastric (common hepatic) arteries","Short gastric arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left and right gastroepiploic arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic artery only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left gastric only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastric (celiac trunk) and right gastric (common hepatic) arteries', 'The lesser curvature is supplied by the left gastric artery (celiac trunk) and the right gastric artery (common hepatic artery), which anastomose along it. The gastroepiploic and short gastric arteries supply the greater curvature and fundus.'),
('b7ee709b-0ba5-5faf-b2ef-61f7a273438c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The blood supply of the greater curvature of the stomach is from:', '["Inferior mesenteric resulting in decreased functional activity","Left and right gastric accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Short gastrics only mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior mesenteric without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left gastroepiploic (splenic) and right gastroepiploic (gastroduodenal) arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Left gastroepiploic (splenic) and right gastroepiploic (gastroduodenal) arteries', 'The greater curvature is supplied by the left gastroepiploic artery (splenic artery) and the right gastroepiploic artery (gastroduodenal artery), forming the gastroepiploic arch. The gastric and short gastric vessels supply the lesser curvature and fundus.'),
('94513576-3dfa-522d-a186-24af6dde0046'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The short gastric arteries arise from:', '["Common hepatic artery","Left gastric artery","Splenic artery","Right gastric artery","Gastroduodenal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic artery', 'The short gastric arteries arise from the splenic artery and pass within the gastrosplenic ligament to reach the fundus. The common hepatic, left gastric, right gastric, and gastroduodenal arteries supply other regions.'),
('2c3dc616-fcbf-5820-a7f3-02a18c1638b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The venous drainage of the stomach ultimately drains into:', '["Portal vein","IVC","Azygos vein","Inferior vena cava","Hemiazygos vein"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'All gastric veins drain into the portal venous system, ultimately reaching the portal vein via the splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein. The IVC and azygos/hemiazygos veins drain systemic and thoracic structures.'),
('1866c6e7-fab7-577f-81bc-c271406732d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The right gastroepiploic vein drains into:', '["Superior mesenteric vein (often via gastrocolic trunk)","Left gastric vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","Splenic vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Portal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior mesenteric vein (often via gastrocolic trunk)', 'The right gastroepiploic vein runs in the gastrocolic ligament and usually joins the middle colic vein to form the gastrocolic trunk of Henle, draining into the superior mesenteric vein. The splenic vein receives the left gastroepiploic and short gastric veins.'),
('441918d1-9ef3-52ef-8067-f5ad7b706762'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The left gastroepiploic vein drains into:', '["Left gastric vein","Splenic vein","IVC","Superior mesenteric vein","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The left gastroepiploic vein accompanies its artery along the greater curvature and drains directly into the splenic vein. The superior mesenteric vein and left gastric vein receive other gastric venous tributaries.'),
('96b28d06-804a-552b-8a1c-bcd06878fea1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The short gastric veins drain into:', '["Right gastric vein","IVC","Portal vein","Splenic vein","Left gastric vein"]'::jsonb, 'Splenic vein', 'The short gastric veins accompany the short gastric arteries in the gastrosplenic ligament and empty into the splenic vein. The right gastric and left gastric veins drain other areas of the stomach.'),
('c1c4e6b4-5d49-5e18-bc8e-f5899f2e6de3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('420c477c-3ca7-5d73-8603-ff5e8243c6d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The celiac lymph nodes drain into:', '["None of the above","Right lymphatic duct","Thoracic duct directly","Cisterna chyli (via intestinal trunk)","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna chyli (via intestinal trunk)', 'The celiac nodes drain via the intestinal lymph trunk into the cisterna chyli, the dilated origin of the thoracic duct. They do not drain to the right lymphatic duct or directly into the portal vein.'),
('c5c0a2fe-f4a1-5c37-956a-f80d17a32b71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The nerve supply of the stomach is from:', '["Only phrenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic (celiac plexus, splanchnic nerves) and parasympathetic (vagus nerves)","Only sympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only intercostal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (celiac plexus, splanchnic nerves) and parasympathetic (vagus nerves)', 'The stomach receives dual innervation: parasympathetic fibers from the vagus nerves and sympathetic fibers from the celiac plexus via the splanchnic nerves. Phrenic, intercostal, and purely sympathetic or vagal innervation would be incomplete.'),
('23eb8e36-758d-5b71-ae8b-d57653c8ba8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Abdomen - Stomach Duodenum', 'The anterior vagal trunk (left vagus) gives rise to:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior gastric branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Celiac branches mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatic branches and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet)","Pyloric branches only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic branches and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet)', 'The anterior vagal trunk gives off hepatic branches to the liver and gallbladder and anterior gastric branches (nerves of Latarjet) to the stomach. Posterior gastric and celiac branches arise from the posterior vagal trunk.'),
('01c8200d-8319-5999-bad6-e1409dfd69a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is divided into which parts?', '["Fundus, body, isthmus, cervix (supravaginal and vaginal parts)","Fundus, body, cervix accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Body and cervix only mediated by specific receptor activation","Fundus, body, vaginal part only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, isthmus, cervix (supravaginal and vaginal parts)', 'The uterus consists of the fundus, above the tubal openings; the body; the isthmus; and the cervix, whose supravaginal part lies above the vagina and whose vaginal part projects into the vaginal lumen. The alternatives omit the isthmus, the cervix, or the subdivisions of the cervix.'),
('f228d22c-b445-5ec6-ae70-5c9a1eea40fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The normal position of the uterus is?', '["Retroverted resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laterally deviated mediated by specific receptor activation","Midposition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anteverted (upper vagina axis) and anteflexed (cervix-body angle)"]'::jsonb, 'Anteverted (upper vagina axis) and anteflexed (cervix-body angle)', 'The normal uterus is anteverted, inclined forward on the axis of the vagina, and anteflexed, bent forward at the body–cervix junction. Retroversion, the midline position, and lateral deviation do not represent the typical resting posture.'),
('2c032eca-047b-5547-bc2b-044835def728'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The supports of the uterus include?', '["Only broad ligament resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani), perineal body, transverse cervical (cardinal/Mackenrodt) ligaments, pubocervical ligaments, uterosacral ligaments, round ligaments","Only round ligaments mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pelvic diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani), perineal body, transverse cervical (cardinal/Mackenrodt) ligaments, pubocervical ligaments, uterosacral ligaments, round ligaments', 'The uterus is supported by the pelvic diaphragm (levator ani), the perineal body, and the paired transverse cervical (cardinal), pubocervical, uterosacral, and round ligaments acting together. The broad ligament, the round ligaments, and the pelvic diaphragm each contribute only part of this support.'),
('e11efedd-f62f-5dd0-9d23-cfa7cb90e7a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The blood supply of the uterus is mainly from?', '["Femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterine artery (branch of internal iliac artery)","Ovarian artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Vaginal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uterine artery (branch of internal iliac artery)', 'The main arterial supply of the uterus is the uterine artery, a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery. The ovarian artery supplies the ovary and the vaginal artery supplies the lower vagina.'),
('5594d5db-a25a-549c-88fe-c430d5881b0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is related anteriorly to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Small intestine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder and uterovesical pouch of peritoneum","Sigmoid colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder and uterovesical pouch of peritoneum', 'Anteroinferiorly the uterus is related to the bladder, with the uterovesical pouch of peritoneum intervening. The rectum lies posteriorly within the rectouterine pouch, and the sigmoid colon and small intestine are more remote relations.'),
('e9bfdb8c-9403-5312-a47f-e8a64167ce5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is related posteriorly to?', '["Rectum and rectouterine pouch (of Douglas)","Sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum and rectouterine pouch (of Douglas)', 'Posteriorly the uterus is related to the rectum, with the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) lying between them. The bladder, sacrum, and pubic symphysis are anterior or separate relations.'),
('7294e8ff-8ebf-5f11-8f19-76ddbeec70ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is attached to the uterus by?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Ovarian ligament (to the uterine cornu)","Broad ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Suspensory ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Round ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian ligament (to the uterine cornu)', 'The ovarian ligament runs from the uterine cornu to the medial pole of the ovary, attaching the ovary to the uterus. The suspensory ligament connects the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall, and the round ligament passes to the labium majus.'),
('ae29c64d-9d61-533b-a7ef-bd9e7098895a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is attached to the pelvic wall by?', '["Ovarian ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Round ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suspensory ligament of the ovary (contains ovarian vessels and nerves)"]'::jsonb, 'Suspensory ligament of the ovary (contains ovarian vessels and nerves)', 'The ovary is attached to the lateral pelvic wall by the suspensory ligament of the ovary, which transmits the ovarian vessels and nerves. The ovarian and round ligaments do not reach the pelvic wall, and the broad ligament is a peritoneal covering rather than a pelvic-wall attachment.'),
('0b890728-3c06-5f48-99f6-c9eacd1c4276'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube is divided into which parts?', '["Only the fimbriae resulting in decreased functional activity","Infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, uterine (interstitial) part","Ampulla and isthmus only mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infundibulum and ampulla only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, uterine (interstitial) part', 'The uterine tube has four parts: the infundibulum with its fimbriae, the ampulla, the isthmus, and the short uterine (interstitial) part within the uterine wall. Lists naming only the fimbriae, the ampulla–isthmus pair, or the infundibulum–ampulla pair omit parts of the tube.'),
('261504dc-2d52-520e-aeab-93a139a4ecd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The broad ligament is?', '["A double layer of peritoneum that covers the uterus, extending laterally to the pelvic wall","A muscular structure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fibrous ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ligament from the uterus to the sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A double layer of peritoneum that covers the uterus, extending laterally to the pelvic wall', 'The broad ligament is a double fold of peritoneum draped over the uterus that extends laterally to the side of the pelvis. It is a serous (peritoneal) fold rather than a muscular or fibrous band, and it does not connect the uterus to the sacrum (the uterosacral ligament does).'),
('ac5f48f9-d51e-5726-b2f0-2136327d577e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The contents of the broad ligament include?', '["Only the round ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the uterine tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the ovary mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterine tube, round ligament, ovarian ligament, uterine artery, ureter (in the base), nerves, lymphatics","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uterine tube, round ligament, ovarian ligament, uterine artery, ureter (in the base), nerves, lymphatics', 'Within the broad ligament lie the uterine tube, round ligament, ovarian ligament, uterine artery, the ureter in its base, and accompanying nerves and lymphatics. Options naming only a single structure are incomplete.'),
('9def3bd3-445a-5fb9-844f-5e223f594b67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina has which fornices?', '["None of the above","Only anterior and posterior","One fornix only","Anterior, posterior, and two lateral fornices","Only lateral fornices"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior, posterior, and two lateral fornices', 'The upper vaginal canal surrounds the cervix to form anterior, posterior, and two lateral fornices. A single fornix, or only the anterior and posterior fornices, does not account for this complete ring.'),
('97e1339a-2d04-5093-a810-6cabd1f6b283'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vaginal fornix is related to?', '["Only to the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Only to the rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","To the urethra only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior fornix: rectouterine pouch (Douglas); Anterior fornix: bladder; Lateral: ureter"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior fornix: rectouterine pouch (Douglas); Anterior fornix: bladder; Lateral: ureter', 'The posterior fornix faces the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas), the anterior fornix lies against the bladder, and each lateral fornix is closely related to the ureter. This explains why the posterior fornix allows access to pouch contents and why ureteric injury is a risk during lateral fornix surgery.'),
('05231acb-d6d9-5ce7-9778-43d25a3fa81d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is related anteriorly to?', '["Cervix","None of the above","Bladder and urethra","Rectum","Ureter"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder and urethra', 'Anteriorly the vagina is related to the base of the bladder and the urethra. The cervix lies above the vaginal canal and the rectum is a posterior relation.'),
('d4283c8c-0219-5f03-9299-acc6c69ad687'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is related posteriorly to?', '["Urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubic symphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectum and rectouterine pouch (Douglas)","Bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum and rectouterine pouch (Douglas)', 'Posteriorly the vagina is related to the rectum, with the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) between them. The bladder, urethra, and pubic symphysis lie anteriorly.'),
('432fd031-3578-5aa2-b5dd-3c03b53fbbec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary receives its blood supply from?', '["Femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterine artery only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovarian artery only mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ovarian artery (from abdominal aorta) and uterine artery (ovarian branch)"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian artery (from abdominal aorta) and uterine artery (ovarian branch)', 'The ovary receives blood from the ovarian artery, arising from the abdominal aorta, and from an ovarian branch of the uterine artery. A single supplying artery alone does not reflect this dual anastomotic arrangement.'),
('8a4ed4b1-45d8-5b71-b0f7-775f070204fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian artery is a branch of?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal iliac artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Common iliac artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abdominal aorta (at L2 level, just below the renal arteries)"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal aorta (at L2 level, just below the renal arteries)', 'The ovarian artery arises directly from the abdominal aorta at about the level of L2, just below the origins of the renal arteries. It is not a branch of the internal iliac, common iliac, or renal arteries.'),
('4aed8cdd-4666-52b1-942c-5ad4c2dfa2ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian vein drains?', '["Both to IVC","None of the above","Both to internal iliac","Both to renal veins","Right to IVC; Left to left renal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Right to IVC; Left to left renal vein', 'The right ovarian vein drains into the inferior vena cava, whereas the left ovarian vein drains into the left renal vein, so drainage differs on the two sides. Bilateral drainage to the IVC, to internal iliac veins, or to both renal veins is inaccurate.'),
('87438372-9a15-5cf0-8586-fe0a5ed3b19e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The round ligament of the uterus runs?', '["From the uterine cornu, through the broad ligament, through the inguinal canal, to the labium majus","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From the ovary to the uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","From the uterus to the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the uterus to the bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the uterine cornu, through the broad ligament, through the inguinal canal, to the labium majus', 'The round ligament passes from the uterine cornu through the broad ligament and the inguinal canal to end in the labium majus. Routes naming the sacrum, bladder, or ovary describe other ligaments.'),
('e243fa42-3ca4-53f1-93f6-9bd74ecd8fcd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterosacral ligament extends from?', '["Uterus to the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uterus to the pelvic wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterus to the ovary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervix to the sacrum (supports the cervix posteriorly)"]'::jsonb, 'Cervix to the sacrum (supports the cervix posteriorly)', 'The uterosacral ligaments run from the cervix to the sacrum and draw the cervix posteriorly, helping maintain its position. They do not attach the uterus to the bladder, ovary, or lateral pelvic wall.'),
('9f723657-4374-5aca-bc99-874aabf21138'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The transverse cervical ligament (cardinal/Mackenrodt) extends from?', '["Uterus to sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterus to bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic wall (main support)","Ovary to pelvic wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic wall (main support)', 'The transverse cervical (cardinal, Mackenrodt) ligaments extend from the cervix and upper vagina to the lateral pelvic wall and are the principal support of the cervix. The other attachments described belong to different ligaments.'),
('ec70545d-f73b-51da-8cbf-bb792dc45d99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The fallopian tube is the usual site of fertilization?', '["Uterine part","None of the above","Ampulla of the uterine tube","Infundibulum","Isthmus"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of the uterine tube', 'Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla, the widest and longest segment of the uterine tube. The infundibulum, isthmus, and uterine part are not the usual site of fertilization.'),
('1ab73cbb-8eb4-59c7-92d1-9eb35818751b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary lies in which fossa?', '["In the inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","In the broad ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the greater omentum mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ovarian fossa (on the lateral pelvic wall, bounded by external iliac vessels, ureter, and broad ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian fossa (on the lateral pelvic wall, bounded by external iliac vessels, ureter, and broad ligament)', 'The ovary occupies the ovarian fossa on the lateral wall of the true pelvis, bounded anteriorly by the external iliac vessels and medially by the ureter and broad ligament. The inguinal canal, broad ligament, and greater omentum are not its location.'),
('c1f5dd80-3c3c-5ab5-98ad-b26830ecaa1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The fimbriae of the uterine tube?', '["Are blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Sweep the ovum into the infundibulum at ovulation","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Are attachments of the ovary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Are ligaments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sweep the ovum into the infundibulum at ovulation', 'The fimbriae are finger-like processes of the infundibulum that sweep the released ovum into the tubal opening at ovulation. They are neither blood vessels, ligaments, nor attachments of the ovary.'),
('4cd8bca9-4f15-5c08-ac6b-4902c190d953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine artery crosses the ureter?', '["Below the ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial to the ureter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral to the ureter mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Above the ureter at the base of the broad ligament (water under the bridge)"]'::jsonb, 'Above the ureter at the base of the broad ligament (water under the bridge)', 'At the base of the broad ligament the uterine artery crosses above the ureter, a relationship recalled as ''water under the bridge''. The alternatives place the artery below, medial to, or lateral to the ureter, which is wrong.'),
('3bb01d85-82d9-5f05-972b-b2d247f7e755'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The lymphatics of the cervix drain to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Axillary nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal and external iliac nodes, obturator nodes, and para-aortic nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Para-aortic nodes only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external iliac nodes, obturator nodes, and para-aortic nodes', 'Lymphatics of the cervix drain mainly to the internal and external iliac nodes, the obturator nodes, and the para-aortic nodes. They do not drain to the axillary or superficial inguinal groups, and para-aortic drainage alone is incomplete.'),
('7b81ac33-5e91-5c02-9de2-c610a3d571e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The lymphatics of the uterine body drain to?', '["Internal and external iliac nodes, and para-aortic nodes (along ovarian vessels)","Axillary nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Superficial inguinal nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Para-aortic only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external iliac nodes, and para-aortic nodes (along ovarian vessels)', 'Lymphatics of the uterine body drain to the internal and external iliac nodes and, along the ovarian vessels, to the para-aortic nodes. The axillary and superficial inguinal node groups are not part of this drainage.'),
('60bec598-e7d9-598c-9ce5-38c8adef8d75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The lymphatics of the vagina (upper) drain to?', '["None of the above","Para-aortic nodes","Only internal iliac","Superficial inguinal nodes","Internal and external iliac nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external iliac nodes', 'Lymphatics of the upper vagina drain to the internal and external iliac nodes. The lower vagina drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, so inguinal, para-aortic, or internal iliac-only drainage does not match the upper vagina.'),
('38ee8f5b-855b-5d3c-934e-f54163d13b05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Regarding female reproductive organs:', '["The broad ligament covers the uterus and contains the tube, round ligament, and vessels","The uterus has fundus, body, isthmus, and cervix","All of the above","The uterine artery crosses above the ureter","The ovary is supplied by the ovarian artery from the aorta"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All three statements are true: the broad ligament contains the uterine tube, round ligament, and vessels; the uterus has fundus, body, isthmus, and cervix; and the uterine artery crosses above the ureter. Choosing only one statement thus omits other correct information.'),
('c7b7699a-0103-5f7e-92f3-da9c6aeb4525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'What are the parts of the uterus?', '["Fundus, body, cervix only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fundus, body, isthmus, cervix (supravaginal and vaginal)","Body and cervix only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fundus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, isthmus, cervix (supravaginal and vaginal)', 'The uterus is composed of the fundus, body, isthmus, and cervix, with the cervix divided into supravaginal and vaginal parts. The other choices omit the isthmus or the cervical subdivisions.'),
('7bcaadd2-a467-5e68-a123-0394f2e1a2d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'What is the normal position of the uterus?', '["Midposition","Retroverted","Laterally deviated","Anteverted and anteflexed","None"]'::jsonb, 'Anteverted and anteflexed', 'The normal uterus is anteverted, inclined forward on the axis of the vagina, and anteflexed, bent forward between the body and cervix. Retroversion, midline deviation, and midposition do not describe the typical posture.'),
('bc5e4c4c-9ef8-5ffe-967b-326eecb5de1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'What are the supports of the uterus?', '["Only pelvic diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvic diaphragm, perineal body, cardinal, uterosacral, pubocervical, round ligaments","Only broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only round ligaments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic diaphragm, perineal body, cardinal, uterosacral, pubocervical, round ligaments', 'The uterus is supported by the pelvic diaphragm, perineal body, and the cardinal, uterosacral, pubocervical, and round ligaments acting together. Relying on the pelvic diaphragm, broad ligament, or round ligaments alone understates these combined supports.'),
('4d9941d0-8c61-5dda-8eb5-5283f5edfd66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'What are the parts of the uterine tube?', '["Ampulla and isthmus only resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infundibulum and ampulla only mediated by specific receptor activation","Infundibulum (fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, interstitial","Only fimbriae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infundibulum (fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, interstitial', 'The uterine tube has four parts: the infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, and interstitial (intramural) segment. Lists naming only the fimbriae, the ampulla–isthmus pair, or the infundibulum–ampulla pair are incomplete.'),
('b5fff2da-674f-5396-993a-3c034e80370a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'What is the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas)?', '["Most dependent peritoneal cavity part between rectum and uterus","Between uterus and ovary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between bladder and uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between vagina and rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most dependent peritoneal cavity part between rectum and uterus', 'The rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in women, lying between the rectum and uterus. The uterovesical pouch lies between the bladder and uterus and is the corresponding anterior recess.'),
('1e79bf4f-c61c-5e33-8176-bc633621ca92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive system includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, vagina, and external genitalia","Only the ovaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, vagina, and external genitalia', 'The female reproductive system comprises the ovaries, uterine tubes, uterus, vagina, and external genitalia. No single component among the options constitutes the entire system.'),
('058f5105-83cc-5e00-93cc-c89b98ac0564'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovaries are:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The site of fertilization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The female gonads (produce ova and female sex hormones: estrogen and progesterone)","The site of fetal development leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The female gonads (produce ova and female sex hormones: estrogen and progesterone)', 'The ovaries are the female gonads that produce ova and secrete estrogen and progesterone. Fertilization occurs in the uterine tube and fetal development occurs in the uterus, not in the ovary.'),
('2aa40921-f8a4-5bed-ad80-b1880649de50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovaries are located:', '["In the abdomen resulting in decreased functional activity","In the false pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","On the lateral wall of the true pelvis (in the ovarian fossa)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral wall of the true pelvis (in the ovarian fossa)', 'Each ovary lies on the lateral wall of the true pelvis in the ovarian fossa. It does not lie in the abdominal cavity proper, the false pelvis, or the perineum.'),
('3e9cd9ae-2143-557a-813c-25bfdb43d8af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian fossa is bounded by:', '["The piriformis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator internus mediated by specific receptor activation","The external iliac vessels (anteriorly), the internal iliac vessels (posteriorly), and the ureter (medially)","The levator ani leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external iliac vessels (anteriorly), the internal iliac vessels (posteriorly), and the ureter (medially)', 'The ovarian fossa is bounded anteriorly by the external iliac vessels, posteriorly by the internal iliac vessels, and medially by the ureter. The piriformis, obturator internus, and levator ani are muscles, not boundaries of this fossa.'),
('1dafbeb9-2187-5ee6-b4c4-2dfa06af3925'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The mesovarium is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A ligament from the ovary to the uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A ligament from the ovary to the pelvic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fold of peritoneum that attaches the ovary to the posterior layer of the broad ligament"]'::jsonb, 'A fold of peritoneum that attaches the ovary to the posterior layer of the broad ligament', 'The mesovarium is the fold of peritoneum that attaches the ovary to the posterior layer of the broad ligament. The ovary-to-uterus and ovary-to-pelvic-wall connections are the ovarian and suspensory ligaments, respectively.'),
('dbc41ac9-e4d1-54bb-b96f-9b77f56db76e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian artery arises from:', '["The internal iliac artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The abdominal aorta (at L2, below the renal arteries)","The uterine artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The renal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The abdominal aorta (at L2, below the renal arteries)', 'The ovarian artery arises from the abdominal aorta at about the level of L2, just below the renal arteries. It does not arise from the internal iliac, uterine, or renal arteries.'),
('b6dbbf51-0601-55bf-b607-4e26200b2334'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The right ovarian vein drains into:', '["The left renal vein","The IVC","The right renal vein","None","The portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC', 'The right ovarian vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava. The left ovarian vein, by contrast, drains into the left renal vein, and the portal vein never receives it.'),
('f444a7d5-4e8e-545e-916b-8cd9f0d88043'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The left ovarian vein drains into:', '["The IVC","The right renal vein","None","The left renal vein","The portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'The left renal vein', 'The left ovarian vein drains into the left renal vein, which then reaches the inferior vena cava. It does not empty directly into the IVC or into the right renal or portal veins.'),
('315d4a1c-86f2-5323-bd0f-0ea8441fad9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine (fallopian) tubes are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The site of fetal development accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The site of fertilization (transport the ovum from the ovary to the uterus)","The site of implantation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The site of fertilization (transport the ovum from the ovary to the uterus)', 'The uterine tubes transport the ovum toward the uterus and are the usual site of fertilization. Implantation occurs in the endometrium and fetal development occurs in the uterus, not in the tube.'),
('db7ffc78-dbcf-5698-81c4-135ef0307403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube is divided into:', '["Only the intramural part resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the infundibulum and ampulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the ampulla and isthmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, and intramural part"]'::jsonb, 'The infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, and intramural part', 'The uterine tube divides into the infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, and intramural (interstitial) part. Lists naming only the infundibulum with ampulla or the ampulla with isthmus are incomplete.'),
('dd593c86-53e9-51a3-bb9b-eafc8f11c996'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The infundibulum of the uterine tube is:', '["The part in the uterine wall resulting in decreased functional activity","The funnel-shaped lateral end (with fimbriae that capture the ovum)","The medial end mediated by specific receptor activation","The narrowest part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The funnel-shaped lateral end (with fimbriae that capture the ovum)', 'The infundibulum is the funnel-shaped lateral end of the tube, whose fimbriae capture the ovum at ovulation. The part within the uterine wall is the intramural segment, and the isthmus is the narrow medial portion.'),
('7948e191-66e6-5e7e-9f5d-efd1e92c77b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ampulla of the uterine tube is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The part in the uterine wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medial part mediated by specific receptor activation","The narrowest part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The widest and longest part (the site of fertilization)"]'::jsonb, 'The widest and longest part (the site of fertilization)', 'The ampulla is the widest and longest part of the tube and is the usual site of fertilization. The intramural part penetrates the uterine wall, and the isthmus is the narrow medial segment.'),
('a0e58324-feed-52c1-a552-a5ca529e3e56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The isthmus of the uterine tube is:', '["The lateral end resulting in decreased functional activity","The widest part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The medial part mediated by specific receptor activation","The narrow, thick-walled part (just lateral to the uterus)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The narrow, thick-walled part (just lateral to the uterus)', 'The isthmus is the narrow, thick-walled medial part of the tube just lateral to the uterus. The funnel-shaped lateral end is the infundibulum, and the widest segment is the ampulla.'),
('a284ef1b-2d1c-5697-969a-c9aecba347cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The intramural part of the uterine tube is:', '["The part that pierces the uterine wall","The lateral end accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The widest part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The narrowest part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part that pierces the uterine wall', 'The intramural (interstitial) part of the uterine tube is the short segment that pierces the uterine wall and opens into the uterine cavity. The lateral end is the infundibulum and the widest segment is the ampulla.'),
('5a1458fb-a8c1-5da8-9aff-917974e47cca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Ectopic pregnancy most commonly occurs in:', '["The ovary resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The cervix mediated by specific receptor activation","The abdomen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ampulla of the uterine tube"]'::jsonb, 'The ampulla of the uterine tube', 'Ectopic pregnancy most often implants in the ampulla of the uterine tube. Implantation in the ovary, cervix, or peritoneal cavity is rare by comparison.'),
('04553a42-2733-52ad-924c-af09e8c351d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The site of hormone production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A pear-shaped, muscular organ that houses and nourishes the developing fetus","The site of fertilization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A pear-shaped, muscular organ that houses and nourishes the developing fetus', 'The uterus is a pear-shaped, thick-walled muscular organ that accommodates and nourishes the developing fetus. Fertilization occurs in the uterine tube, and hormone production is chiefly a function of the ovary.'),
('d5f205a3-025d-5080-b5c1-39cf3f99f902'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is divided into:', '["Only the fundus and cervix resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the fundus and body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The fundus (body above the tubal openings), the body (corpus), and the cervix","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the body and cervix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fundus (body above the tubal openings), the body (corpus), and the cervix', 'The uterus consists of three named parts: the fundus (the dome above the uterine tube openings), the corpus (body), and the cervix. Options that name only two parts omit one of these three subdivisions.'),
('f6bb5f57-ad03-513e-9328-4ae2afc103cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervix is:', '["The middle part of the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","The fundus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina (divided into supravaginal and vaginal parts)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The upper part of the uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina (divided into supravaginal and vaginal parts)', 'The cervix is the lower cylindrical part of the uterus that projects into the vagina and is divided into supravaginal and vaginal portions. The fundus lies superiorly and the body forms the middle segment, so the other options place the cervix incorrectly.'),
('a57f0bc9-6fc3-57fa-a7be-d766207c92a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is located:', '["In the true pelvis, between the bladder (anteriorly) and the rectum (posteriorly)","In the false pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","In the abdomen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the true pelvis, between the bladder (anteriorly) and the rectum (posteriorly)', 'The uterus lies in the true pelvis with the bladder anterior and the rectum posterior to it. It does not lie in the false pelvis, the perineum, or the abdominal cavity.'),
('d29b6f4d-517c-5d9c-854c-dff441ff3d94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is normally:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Retroverted accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anteverted (tilted forward) and anteflexed (bent forward at the isthmus)","Straight without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anteverted (tilted forward) and anteflexed (bent forward at the isthmus)', 'In the normal non-pregnant state the uterus is anteverted (tilted forward on the vagina) and anteflexed (bent forward on itself at the isthmus). A retroverted or straight position is a variant, not the usual state.'),
('f95638a6-7e1c-5dd7-8d56-aaafe24bb628'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is supplied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The uterine arteries (from the internal iliac artery) and the ovarian arteries (from the aorta)","Only the uterine arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the ovarian arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the vaginal arteries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The uterine arteries (from the internal iliac artery) and the ovarian arteries (from the aorta)', 'The uterus receives blood from paired uterine arteries (branches of the internal iliac artery) reinforced by the ovarian arteries from the aorta. Listing only one arterial source is incomplete.'),
('7e1a8a7c-c4da-5c34-827c-e541b9e0b18d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine artery is a branch of:', '["The internal iliac artery (anterior trunk)","The common iliac artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ovarian artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac artery (anterior trunk)', 'The uterine artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery. It does not arise from the common or external iliac arteries or from the ovarian artery.'),
('76bfb2e5-8cc6-5966-bbf8-e6272c16ec11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine artery crosses above the ureter:', '["Medially at the level of the cervix (Water under the bridge)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nowhere near the ureter mediated by specific receptor activation","Below the ureter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parallel to the ureter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medially at the level of the cervix (Water under the bridge)', 'In the female pelvis the uterine artery crosses above and in front of the ureter near the cervix; the phrase ''water under the bridge'' recalls that the artery lies above the ureter. The ureter is never crossed below or avoided by the artery.'),
('1211efbc-023f-550a-9194-f2a2b9823761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is held in position by:', '["The broad ligament, round ligament, uterosacral ligament, transverse cervical (cardinal) ligament, and pubocervical ligament","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the broad ligament mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the uterosacral ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the round ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The broad ligament, round ligament, uterosacral ligament, transverse cervical (cardinal) ligament, and pubocervical ligament', 'The uterus is supported by several ligaments, including the broad, round, and uterosacral ligaments and the powerful transverse cervical (cardinal) and pubocervical ligaments. None of these ligaments alone provides the full support.'),
('ffcfcabb-69f7-5cb7-97e1-a2f04b292c89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The broad ligament contains:', '["The uterine tubes, the round ligament of the uterus, the ovarian ligament, the mesovarium, and the uterine vessels","Only the uterine tubes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the round ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The uterine tubes, the round ligament of the uterus, the ovarian ligament, the mesovarium, and the uterine vessels', 'Within the peritoneum of the broad ligament travel the uterine tubes, the round ligament, the ovarian ligament, the mesovarium, and the uterine vessels. The broad ligament carries far more than the tubes, round ligament, or vessels alone.'),
('ca7c0a02-da5b-55af-8fd3-74cee469ff0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The round ligament of the uterus is:', '["A peritoneal fold resulting in decreased functional activity","A ligament from the uterus to the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fibromuscular cord that runs from the lateral angle of the uterus to the labia majora (through the inguinal canal)","A muscular band without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular cord that runs from the lateral angle of the uterus to the labia majora (through the inguinal canal)', 'The round ligament is a fibromuscular cord that runs from the lateral angle (cornu) of the uterus through the inguinal canal and ends in the labia majora. Peritoneal folds and sacral or muscular attachments describe other structures.'),
('44bf9111-22cc-5fc1-89f3-55919f66f8f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The round ligament corresponds to:', '["The epididymis","None","The seminal vesicles","The gubernaculum testis in males","The vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'The gubernaculum testis in males', 'In the male the analogous structure to the round ligament is the gubernaculum testis, which guides descent of the testis. The epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles have no such homology.'),
('3eb1cc2d-2cea-56d7-b38d-923ce12ff6db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterosacral ligament is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ligament from the uterus to the pelvic wall mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibromuscular band from the cervix to the sacrum (helps hold the uterus backward and upward)","A ligament from the uterus to the ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular band from the cervix to the sacrum (helps hold the uterus backward and upward)', 'The uterosacral ligament runs from the cervix to the sacrum and pulls the uterus backward and upward in the pelvis. It does not attach to the pelvic wall or the ovary.'),
('7716d656-63dc-5078-9127-0a8857ea02e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The transverse cervical (cardinal) ligament is:', '["A strong ligament at the base of the broad ligament (from the cervix to the lateral pelvic wall) that supports the uterus","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ligament from the uterus to the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","A ligament from the uterus to the rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A strong ligament at the base of the broad ligament (from the cervix to the lateral pelvic wall) that supports the uterus', 'The transverse cervical (cardinal) ligament is the strong condensation at the base of the broad ligament extending from the cervix to the lateral pelvic wall and is a key support of the uterus. It does not attach to the bladder or rectum.'),
('0a93c185-200b-574f-a637-d1eb620df675'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is:', '["The site of hormone production resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A distensible, fibromuscular canal that extends from the cervix to the vestibule of the vulva","The site of fertilization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A distensible, fibromuscular canal that extends from the cervix to the vestibule of the vulva', 'The vagina is a distensible fibromuscular canal running from the cervix to the vestibule of the vulva. It neither produces hormones nor is the site of fertilization.'),
('602beee8-6aca-5a9d-941a-f8e31305a120'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is located:', '["In the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity","In the false pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Between the bladder (anteriorly) and the rectum (posteriorly)","In the abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the bladder (anteriorly) and the rectum (posteriorly)', 'The vagina passes through the pelvic floor and lies between the bladder anteriorly and the rectum posteriorly. It is not located primarily in the perineum, false pelvis, or abdomen.'),
('f1a9e842-a572-56f0-aa4e-d8dc8ac8782f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina has:', '["Only an adventitia resulting in decreased functional activity","A mucosal layer (with rugae), a muscular layer, and a fibrous adventitia","Only a muscular layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Only a mucosal layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mucosal layer (with rugae), a muscular layer, and a fibrous adventitia', 'The vaginal wall has three layers: a mucosa with transverse rugae, a muscular layer, and a fibrous adventitia. It is not made of a single layer.'),
('c5838ec8-3cfb-5c81-8866-0509fe30c769'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vaginal fornices are:', '["The folds of the vaginal mucosa resulting in decreased functional activity","The recesses around the cervix (anterior, posterior, and lateral fornices)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The glands of the vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The recesses around the cervix (anterior, posterior, and lateral fornices)', 'The fornices are the recesses of the vagina that surround the cervix, named anterior, posterior, and lateral. They are not mucosal folds, glands, or a single unifying structure such as ''All''.'),
('c7915882-8e85-5929-b0b6-3c663408cacf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The posterior fornix is the deepest part of the vagina and is in contact with:', '["The bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas)', 'The posterior fornix is the deepest recess and is separated from the pouch of Douglas (rectouterine pouch) by only the vaginal wall and peritoneum. The bladder, rectum, and uterus lie elsewhere or at a distance from this precise point.'),
('fab332af-e148-5172-9be7-45186fbd390d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is supplied by:', '["Only the uterine artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the vaginal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ovarian artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vaginal artery (from the internal iliac) and the uterine artery"]'::jsonb, 'The vaginal artery (from the internal iliac) and the uterine artery', 'The vagina is supplied by the vaginal artery from the internal iliac artery as well as by branches of the uterine artery. Depending on either artery alone would be incomplete.'),
('32d49600-99d7-5e28-b3a5-e881e662706e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The broad ligament extends from:', '["The anterior wall of the uterus to the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior wall of the uterus to the rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lateral margins of the uterus to the lateral pelvic walls","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral margins of the uterus to the lateral pelvic walls', 'The broad ligament is a double fold of peritoneum that hangs from the lateral margins of the uterus to the lateral pelvic walls. It does not connect the uterus to the bladder or rectum.'),
('6269ab67-4c03-5ee3-a6cb-b7bf337744bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The mesosalpinx is:', '["None","All","The part of the broad ligament that supports the uterine tube","The part of the broad ligament that supports the ovary","The part of the broad ligament that supports the uterus"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the broad ligament that supports the uterine tube', 'The mesosalpinx is the fold of the broad ligament that suspends the uterine tube. The parts that support the ovary and uterus are the mesovarium and mesometrium, respectively.'),
('82e52522-c863-550d-b2b8-7268b83c776a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The mesometrium is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The largest part of the broad ligament (supports the uterus)","The part that supports the ovary mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The part that supports the tube leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The largest part of the broad ligament (supports the uterus)', 'The mesometrium is the largest portion of the broad ligament and is the part related to the uterus. The parts related to the ovary and tube are the mesovarium and mesosalpinx.'),
('0deedcae-683b-5527-97f0-5aed1647ca21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female external genitalia (vulva) includes:', '["The mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule, vestibular bulbs, and Bartholin glands","Only the vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the clitoris mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the labia majora leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule, vestibular bulbs, and Bartholin glands', 'The vulva (external genitalia) is composed of the mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule, vestibular bulbs, and Bartholin glands. It includes much more than the vagina, clitoris, or labia majora alone.'),
('f1347f11-f1ba-5cd4-8f91-5a421ff5cfd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The mons pubis is:', '["A pad of fatty tissue anterior to the pubic symphysis (covered by pubic hair)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the clitoris mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the labia majora leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A pad of fatty tissue anterior to the pubic symphysis (covered by pubic hair)', 'The mons pubis is a rounded pad of fatty tissue lying anterior to the pubic symphysis and covered by pubic hair after puberty. It is neither the clitoris nor the labia majora.'),
('106ff65f-1754-50a9-aeac-ddc216f24f4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The labia majora are:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Two large folds of skin that form the lateral boundaries of the vulva (homologous to the scrotum)","The same as the labia minora mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the Bartholin glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two large folds of skin that form the lateral boundaries of the vulva (homologous to the scrotum)', 'The labia majora are two large skin folds forming the lateral boundaries of the vulva and are homologous to the scrotum. They differ from the labia minora and the Bartholin glands.'),
('28644de5-a6d2-56be-af73-14e7c2a6550e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The labia minora are:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two smaller folds of skin between the labia majora (surround the vestibule)","The same as the Bartholin glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the labia majora leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two smaller folds of skin between the labia majora (surround the vestibule)', 'The labia minora are two smaller hairless folds lying between the labia majora that enclose the vestibule. They are distinct from the labia majora and the Bartholin glands.'),
('9c2b19a9-7429-5abc-9554-9108dd852113'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The clitoris is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the Bartholin glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the labia majora mediated by specific receptor activation","A small, erectile organ at the anterior junction of the labia minora (homologous to the penis)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small, erectile organ at the anterior junction of the labia minora (homologous to the penis)', 'The clitoris is a small erectile organ located at the anterior junction of the labia minora and is homologous to the penis. It is not the Bartholin glands or the labia majora.'),
('01b4bc18-ef41-599f-8a46-9aad8eef8553'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The clitoris has:', '["Two crura, a body, and a glans (contains erectile tissue)","Only a glans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only crura mediated by specific receptor activation","Only a body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two crura, a body, and a glans (contains erectile tissue)', 'The clitoris is composed of two crura, a body, and a glans, all containing erectile tissue. It is not limited to a single component such as the glans, crura, or body.'),
('fb6d4a90-e4e0-5360-a36f-59b920d21d55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vestibular bulbs are:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the Bartholin glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the clitoris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two masses of erectile tissue at the base of the labia minora (homologous to the bulb of the penis)"]'::jsonb, 'Two masses of erectile tissue at the base of the labia minora (homologous to the bulb of the penis)', 'The vestibular bulbs are two masses of erectile tissue lying at the base of the labia minora and are homologous to the bulb of the penis. They are not the Bartholin glands, the clitoris, or an all-encompassing option.'),
('82860630-0ce5-5c3b-a73c-84b1cca72138'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Bartholin glands (greater vestibular glands) are:', '["Two glands that secrete mucus into the vestibule (homologous to the bulbourethral glands in males)","The same as the labia minora accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the Skene glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two glands that secrete mucus into the vestibule (homologous to the bulbourethral glands in males)', 'The Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands are paired glands that secrete mucus into the vestibule and are homologous to the bulbourethral glands in males. They differ from the labia minora, Skene glands, and an ''All'' option.'),
('c3bd9582-8b96-50ec-9b2d-09ce96202679'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Bartholin glands are located:', '["In the clitoris resulting in decreased functional activity","In the deep perineal pouch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","In the superficial perineal pouch (deep to the posterior part of the labia majora)","In the labia minora leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the superficial perineal pouch (deep to the posterior part of the labia majora)', 'Each Bartholin gland lies in the superficial perineal pouch, deep to the posterior part of the labia majora, and its duct opens into the vestibule. It does not lie in the clitoris, deep perineal pouch, or labia minora.'),
('07d8c8e3-6144-571f-86a9-dc279abc8c0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vestibule is:', '["The space between the labia minora (contains the openings of the urethra, vagina, and Bartholin glands)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the clitoris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the vagina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the labia minora (contains the openings of the urethra, vagina, and Bartholin glands)', 'The vestibule is the space between the labia minora into which the urethra, vagina, and Bartholin gland ducts open. It is not the clitoris or the vagina itself.'),
('ec6112e7-ad2f-5f03-b63f-204d06e0a526'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The hymen is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the clitoris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the Bartholin gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A thin mucosal fold that partially covers the vaginal opening"]'::jsonb, 'A thin mucosal fold that partially covers the vaginal opening', 'The hymen is a thin mucosal fold that partially covers the vaginal orifice. It is unrelated to the clitoris or the Bartholin glands.'),
('ea189f21-755d-52e2-bb2f-82266c2b825e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The menstrual cycle is regulated by:', '["Only the pituitary resulting in decreased functional activity","The hypothalamus, pituitary gland (FSH and LH), and the ovaries (estrogen and progesterone)","Only the ovaries mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the hypothalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hypothalamus, pituitary gland (FSH and LH), and the ovaries (estrogen and progesterone)', 'The menstrual cycle is controlled by the interplay of the hypothalamus, the anterior pituitary (FSH and LH), and the ovaries (estrogen and progesterone). Regulation by only the pituitary or only the ovaries is incomplete.'),
('644f482c-eac5-555b-8758-0dd3ad3a7e16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The menstrual cycle is divided into:', '["Only ovulation resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The follicular phase, ovulation, and the luteal phase","Only the luteal phase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the follicular phase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The follicular phase, ovulation, and the luteal phase', 'The ovarian cycle has three phases: the follicular phase, ovulation, and the luteal phase. Naming any single phase alone is incomplete.'),
('57b662bc-c71b-533b-b1e5-cf777ae83df3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine (endometrial) cycle is divided into:', '["The menstrual phase, the proliferative phase, and the secretory phase","Only the proliferative and secretory phases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the menstrual and proliferative phases mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the menstrual and secretory phases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The menstrual phase, the proliferative phase, and the secretory phase', 'The endometrial cycle includes the menstrual, proliferative, and secretory phases. Pairing only two of the three phases omits an entire phase.'),
('2dce4984-612c-59c9-aedd-30d8a88263ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The proliferative phase of the endometrium corresponds to:', '["The menstrual phase resulting in decreased functional activity","The luteal phase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovulation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The follicular phase of the ovarian cycle (under the influence of estrogen)"]'::jsonb, 'The follicular phase of the ovarian cycle (under the influence of estrogen)', 'The proliferative phase of the endometrium rebuilds the lining and coincides with the follicular phase of the ovary under the influence of estrogen. It is not associated with menstruation, ovulation, or the luteal phase.'),
('25c4b066-ec11-5cdd-a01b-e0158c5deca1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The secretory phase of the endometrium corresponds to:', '["The menstrual phase resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovulation mediated by specific receptor activation","The follicular phase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The luteal phase of the ovarian cycle (under the influence of progesterone)"]'::jsonb, 'The luteal phase of the ovarian cycle (under the influence of progesterone)', 'The secretory phase of the endometrium prepares the lining for implantation and corresponds to the luteal phase of the ovary under the influence of progesterone. It does not match menstruation, ovulation, or the follicular phase.'),
('fa851dd5-039e-561c-b103-da230b0f9b67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Ovulation occurs at about:', '["Day 7 resulting in decreased functional activity","Day 14 of a 28-day cycle (release of the secondary oocyte from the ovary)","Day 1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Day 28 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Day 21 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Day 14 of a 28-day cycle (release of the secondary oocyte from the ovary)', 'In a typical 28-day cycle, ovulation occurs around day 14 with release of a secondary oocyte from the ovary. The other listed days do not coincide with ovulation.'),
('9161e91a-7c13-58fc-ae02-5064c265fb1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The corpus luteum develops from:', '["The ruptured ovarian follicle after ovulation (secretes progesterone)","The stroma of the ovary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The oocyte itself mediated by specific receptor activation","The germinal epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ruptured ovarian follicle after ovulation (secretes progesterone)', 'The corpus luteum forms from the ruptured (ovulated) ovarian follicle and secretes progesterone. The ovarian stroma, the oocyte, and the germinal epithelium do not give rise to it.'),
('e34a50c5-a214-581b-a4c5-9d3ca0fb2632'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'If fertilization does not occur, the corpus luteum:', '["Continues to secrete estrogen resulting in decreased functional activity","Degenerates into the corpus albicans (after about 14 days)","Persists mediated by specific receptor activation","Continues to secrete progesterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Degenerates into the corpus albicans (after about 14 days)', 'Without fertilization, the corpus luteum regresses after roughly 14 days and turns into the non-functional scar-like corpus albicans. It does not continue secreting estrogen or progesterone.'),
('ff68c2db-45ce-55c2-a9f0-cd8c2960c33b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'If fertilization occurs, the corpus luteum:', '["Becomes the corpus albicans resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Degenerates mediated by specific receptor activation","Persists (under the influence of hCG) and continues to secrete progesterone (to maintain the pregnancy)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Persists (under the influence of hCG) and continues to secrete progesterone (to maintain the pregnancy)', 'With fertilization, hCG from the trophoblast maintains the corpus luteum, which persists and keeps secreting progesterone to support the pregnancy. It does not degenerate or become the corpus albicans at this stage.'),
('6465245e-7514-580b-a51a-ef386226dc84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The corpus luteum of pregnancy lasts until:', '["About 12 weeks of gestation (then the placenta takes over hormone production)","The first month accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The end of pregnancy mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sixth month leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 12 weeks of gestation (then the placenta takes over hormone production)', 'The corpus luteum of pregnancy persists for about 12 weeks, after which the placenta takes over steroid hormone production. It does not last only one month or until term.'),
('66e77840-e825-59fd-aa4f-aa7053fb7710'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female pelvis is:', '["Wider and shallower than the male pelvis (to allow for childbirth)","The same as the male pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Narrower and deeper than the male pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wider and shallower than the male pelvis (to allow for childbirth)', 'The female pelvis is wider and shallower than the male pelvis, adaptations that favor childbirth. It is neither identical to the male pelvis nor narrower and deeper.'),
('36c224b9-5a13-578b-9d89-8206d34615b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The subpubic angle in females is:', '["Wider (> 90 degrees)","None","Narrower (< 90 degrees)","All","The same as in males"]'::jsonb, 'Wider (> 90 degrees)', 'In females the subpubic angle is greater than 90 degrees, whereas in males it is narrower. It is not the same in the two sexes.'),
('69775065-3e9f-588d-96e3-a5aa09895f58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The sacrum in females is:', '["Narrower, longer, and more curved resulting in decreased functional activity","Wider, shorter, and less curved (than in males)","The same as in males mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wider, shorter, and less curved (than in males)', 'The female sacrum is wider, shorter, and less curved than the male sacrum. The opposite description applies to the male pelvis.'),
('daf13e0a-9a56-50c7-923e-c3a047545397'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female pelvis is usually classified as:', '["Only gynecoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Only android accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only anthropoid mediated by specific receptor activation","Gynecoid (most common, favorable for childbirth), android, anthropoid, or platypelloid","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gynecoid (most common, favorable for childbirth), android, anthropoid, or platypelloid', 'The female pelvis is classed as gynecoid, android, anthropoid, or platypelloid. It is not limited to a single type.'),
('f26de67f-9208-5ce4-bb10-ee332bfb59fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The gynecoid pelvis is:', '["The male-type pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ''normal'' female pelvis (round inlet, wide subpubic angle) - favorable for vaginal delivery","The narrow pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The flat pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ''normal'' female pelvis (round inlet, wide subpubic angle) - favorable for vaginal delivery', 'The gynecoid pelvis is the typical female pelvis with a round inlet and wide subpubic angle, and it is most favorable for vaginal delivery. It is not a male-type, narrow, or flat pelvis.'),
('33e22aaa-4459-54ca-9c69-1ed88af641d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The android pelvis is:', '["A male-type pelvis in a female (heart-shaped inlet, narrow subpubic angle) - can cause obstructed labor","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the anthropoid pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the gynecoid pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A male-type pelvis in a female (heart-shaped inlet, narrow subpubic angle) - can cause obstructed labor', 'The android pelvis is a male-type pelvis found in some females, with a heart-shaped inlet and narrow subpubic angle that can lead to obstructed labor. It is neither anthropoid nor gynecoid.'),
('c425370c-02b7-54b4-b611-507d1c659e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The anthropoid pelvis is:', '["A round pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","A flat pelvis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the gynecoid pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A pelvis with a long, oval inlet (common in tall women) - often results in an occiput-posterior position"]'::jsonb, 'A pelvis with a long, oval inlet (common in tall women) - often results in an occiput-posterior position', 'The anthropoid pelvis has a long, oval anteroposterior inlet, is common in tall women, and often results in an occiput-posterior fetal position. It is not round, flat, or gynecoid.'),
('c0f29aa2-2aa6-5992-8bea-543a8f15a219'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The platypelloid pelvis is:', '["The most common type resulting in decreased functional activity","A flat pelvis (transversely wide, anteroposteriorly narrow) - the least common type","The same as the gynecoid mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the android leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A flat pelvis (transversely wide, anteroposteriorly narrow) - the least common type', 'The platypelloid (flat) pelvis is wide transversely but narrow anteroposteriorly, and it is the least common pelvic type. It is distinct from the gynecoid form, which is the most common and best suited for childbirth.'),
('ec6fb7b5-6cee-5ca9-abf6-ebbcddec8073'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The true conjugate is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The distance between the ischial tuberosities accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet (from the sacral promontory to the upper edge of the pubic symphysis)","The distance between the ischial spines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet (from the sacral promontory to the upper edge of the pubic symphysis)', 'The true (obstetric) conjugate is the anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic inlet, measured from the sacral promontory to the upper border of the pubic symphysis. The interspinous and intertuberous distances are separate transverse measures at other levels.'),
('3d1ec940-a64b-5fab-acb9-bebf49b9d79c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The diagonal conjugate is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The distance from the sacral promontory to the lower edge of the pubic symphysis (the true conjugate is about 1.5-2 cm less)","The distance between the ischial spines mediated by specific receptor activation","The distance between the ischial tuberosities without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The distance from the sacral promontory to the lower edge of the pubic symphysis (the true conjugate is about 1.5-2 cm less)', 'The diagonal conjugate is measured vaginally from the sacral promontory to the lower border of the pubic symphysis, and the true conjugate is roughly 1.5-2 cm shorter. The interspinous and intertuberous distances are transverse measurements, not conjugates.'),
('14e11ada-1e86-5bb0-a81d-b677eacf6d23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The obstetric conjugate is:', '["The distance between the ischial spines resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the true conjugate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the diagonal conjugate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The AP diameter of the pelvic inlet (from the sacral promontory to the nearest point on the inner surface of the pubis)"]'::jsonb, 'The AP diameter of the pelvic inlet (from the sacral promontory to the nearest point on the inner surface of the pubis)', 'The obstetric conjugate is the shortest anteroposterior diameter of the inlet, running from the sacral promontory to the nearest point on the inner (posterior) surface of the pubis. The interspinous distance is a midpelvic measure and is not a conjugate.'),
('5ab5f430-ba13-541a-b017-50b975332f70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The distance between the ischial spines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The distance between the ischial tuberosities mediated by specific receptor activation","The greatest distance between the arcuate lines (iliopectineal lines)","The AP diameter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greatest distance between the arcuate lines (iliopectineal lines)', 'The transverse diameter of the inlet is the greatest distance between the arcuate (iliopectineal) lines on either side. The interspinous and intertuberous distances are measured at the midpelvis and outlet respectively, not at the inlet.'),
('fee0b591-cf8a-5d7c-b308-42b9b75014e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The interspinous distance is:', '["The distance between the ischial tuberosities resulting in decreased functional activity","The transverse diameter of the inlet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The distance between the ischial spines (usually about 10 cm)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The AP diameter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The distance between the ischial spines (usually about 10 cm)', 'The interspinous distance is the distance between the two ischial spines, normally about 10 cm, and represents the narrowest point of the midpelvis. The intertuberous distance is a separate, larger transverse measure of the outlet.'),
('62aa4c73-e6b6-5abb-b311-e86a08534e16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The intertuberous distance is:', '["The distance between the ischial tuberosities (the transverse diameter of the pelvic outlet)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The AP diameter mediated by specific receptor activation","The distance between the ischial spines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The transverse diameter of the inlet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The distance between the ischial tuberosities (the transverse diameter of the pelvic outlet)', 'The intertuberous distance is measured between the ischial tuberosities and forms the transverse diameter of the pelvic outlet. The interspinous distance and the inlet''s transverse diameter are measured at different levels.'),
('9e956d17-a873-57f7-9718-4ff705ef1f1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The presenting part of a fetus engages when:', '["The fetus is in the below the ischial spines resulting in decreased functional activity","The largest diameter of the presenting part passes through the pelvic inlet","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fetus enters the pelvic cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The largest diameter of the presenting part passes through the pelvic inlet', 'Engagement is defined by the descent of the largest diameter of the presenting part, usually the biparietal diameter of the head, through the plane of the pelvic inlet. Merely entering the pelvic cavity without passing this plane does not constitute engagement.'),
('9354ddd3-fe44-5c7a-bbef-e8c1ad54ee88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The station of the presenting part is measured:', '["In relation to the sacral promontory resulting in decreased functional activity","In relation to the pubic symphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In relation to the ischial spines (0 station = at the spines, -1 = above, +1 = below)","In relation to the pelvic outlet without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In relation to the ischial spines (0 station = at the spines, -1 = above, +1 = below)', 'Fetal station is expressed relative to the ischial spines, which mark the zero level; stations above the spines are negative and those below are positive. The sacral promontory and pelvic outlet are not used as the reference for station.'),
('e3a0d595-5c70-5bf1-abc4-889aae55eb8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cardinal movements of labor are:', '["Only engagement and descent resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only flexion and internal rotation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only extension and expulsion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Engagement, descent, flexion, internal rotation, extension, external rotation, and expulsion"]'::jsonb, 'Engagement, descent, flexion, internal rotation, extension, external rotation, and expulsion', 'The seven cardinal movements of labor occur sequentially: engagement, descent, flexion, internal rotation, extension, external rotation, and expulsion. Each movement helps the fetus adapt to the shape of the maternal pelvis, so no single movement alone is complete.'),
('9a6ca99f-9ccd-578a-86ae-50fdf0143195'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The internal rotation of the fetal head is necessary:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","To align the anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head with the anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic outlet","None mediated by specific receptor activation","To align the transverse diameter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To align the oblique diameter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'To align the anteroposterior diameter of the fetal head with the anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic outlet', 'Internal rotation turns the occiput toward the pubic symphysis so the sagittal (anteroposterior) diameter of the head aligns with the anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic outlet, whose widest dimension is not transverse. This usually begins as the head reaches the levator floor.'),
('2ac33403-647f-5393-bda8-cd151d5078a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The levator ani muscle is important in:', '["Respiration resulting in decreased functional activity","Supporting the pelvic organs and maintaining continence","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Movement of the thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supporting the pelvic organs and maintaining continence', 'The levator ani forms the main pelvic floor, supporting the pelvic viscera and helping maintain urinary and fecal continence. It is not involved in respiration or thigh movement.'),
('8914b23a-ede8-51a2-a6cb-a21dd24ba5fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The perineal body is an important structure for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Cesarean section accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hysterectomy mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Episiotomy (a surgical incision of the perineal body to enlarge the vaginal opening)"]'::jsonb, 'Episiotomy (a surgical incision of the perineal body to enlarge the vaginal opening)', 'The perineal body is the fibromuscular node of the perineum into which an episiotomy is classically made to enlarge the vaginal outlet during delivery. Neither cesarean section nor hysterectomy is performed through the perineal body.'),
('f5ef7456-8b80-562a-8ad9-1cd6f33717c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovaries are supplied by:', '["The ovarian artery (from the aorta) and the ovarian branch of the uterine artery","Only the uterine artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the ovarian artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ovarian artery (from the aorta) and the ovarian branch of the uterine artery', 'The ovary has a dual blood supply: the ovarian artery arising from the abdominal aorta and the ovarian branch of the uterine artery, which anastomose within the mesovarium. Relying on only one vessel ignores this important anastomosis.'),
('bebddf9e-0dbc-52a3-8c83-dfc87cee1313'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovaries are drained by:', '["The internal iliac vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The external iliac vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pampiniform plexus (which forms the ovarian vein)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The uterine vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pampiniform plexus (which forms the ovarian vein)', 'Venous drainage of the ovary begins in the pampiniform plexus, which condenses into the ovarian vein. On the right the ovarian vein drains into the inferior vena cava and on the left into the renal vein.'),
('bed2da8b-9edc-56b8-b110-081f4b0863d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian ligament (utero-ovarian ligament) attaches:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovary to the broad ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","The ovary to the pelvic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ovary to the lateral angle of the uterus"]'::jsonb, 'The ovary to the lateral angle of the uterus', 'The ovarian (utero-ovarian) ligament runs from the ovary to the lateral angle (cornu) of the uterus. Attachment to the pelvic wall or broad ligament is the role of the suspensory ligament and mesovarium respectively.'),
('708ae5e1-e019-58a8-9fc9-04dc421922d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The suspensory ligament of the ovary (infundibulopelvic ligament) attaches:', '["The ovary to the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovary to the lateral pelvic wall (contains the ovarian vessels)","The ovary to the broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ovary to the lateral pelvic wall (contains the ovarian vessels)', 'The suspensory (infundibulopelvic) ligament carries the ovarian vessels and connects the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall. The utero-ovarian ligament is the one that ties the ovary to the uterus.'),
('44c83462-39fb-5b53-b968-8c9ba495378f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian fossa (Waldeyer fossa) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A shallow depression on the lateral pelvic wall (where the ovary lies)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A depression on the broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A depression in the ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A shallow depression on the lateral pelvic wall (where the ovary lies)', 'The ovarian fossa of Waldeyer is a shallow depression on the lateral pelvic wall, bounded anteriorly by the external iliac vein and posteriorly by the ureter and internal iliac vessels, where the ovary normally lies. It is a peritoneal reflection site, not a structure within the broad ligament or ovary.'),
('d82f53dc-9450-5369-b878-f16ae77344d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube (fallopian tube) is about:', '["10-12 cm long","15 cm","20 cm","5 cm","None"]'::jsonb, '10-12 cm long', 'The uterine tube is roughly 10-12 cm long, extending from the cornu of the uterus toward the ovary. It is divided into the intramural (interstitial), isthmus, ampulla, and infundibulum.'),
('47cdd76a-6ee1-5183-8cbf-909d381c8fba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube is the site of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Menstruation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fertilization (in the ampulla)","Ovulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Implantation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fertilization (in the ampulla)', 'Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla, the widest and longest part of the uterine tube. Ovulation takes place in the ovary, while menstruation and implantation occur in the uterus.'),
('adcb746e-b56e-56dc-88ee-1d47b0a42cf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube is also the site of:', '["Normal pregnancy resulting in decreased functional activity","Ectopic pregnancy (the fertilized ovum implants in the tube instead of the uterus)","Menstruation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectopic pregnancy (the fertilized ovum implants in the tube instead of the uterus)', 'If the fertilized ovum implants within the uterine tube rather than the endometrium, a tubal (ectopic) pregnancy results, most often in the ampulla. The tube cannot support normal growth and may rupture.'),
('21f63fcb-fe47-5b84-aed1-650562954c59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is about:', '["12 cm long resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm long accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","10 cm long without affecting the primary tissue pathway","7-8 cm long, 5 cm wide, and 2.5 cm thick (in a nulliparous adult)"]'::jsonb, '7-8 cm long, 5 cm wide, and 2.5 cm thick (in a nulliparous adult)', 'In the nulliparous adult the uterus measures about 7-8 cm long, 5 cm wide, and 2.5 cm thick, with the cervix forming its lower third. These dimensions enlarge considerably with parity and pregnancy.'),
('d05a20e9-ee39-5e1c-a34f-c61512d302f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The weight of the uterus is about:', '["200 g resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","50-100 g (in a nulliparous adult)","150 g without affecting the primary tissue pathway","30 g leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '50-100 g (in a nulliparous adult)', 'The nonpregnant nulliparous uterus weighs approximately 50-100 g. This weight is roughly doubled in multiparous women and increases dramatically during pregnancy.'),
('8c66c59d-6280-508b-a16d-31bd71c92abd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus enlarges significantly during:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Menstruation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Ovulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pregnancy (from 50-100 g to about 1000 g at term)"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy (from 50-100 g to about 1000 g at term)', 'During pregnancy the uterus enlarges from about 50-100 g to roughly 1000 g at term, driven by hypertrophy of myometrial smooth muscle. Menstruation and ovulation do not produce this degree of growth.'),
('2059d163-b764-5ed2-b036-518deb3aea09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is usually positioned:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retroverted mediated by specific receptor activation","Retroflexed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anteverted (tilted forward) and anteflexed (bent forward at the isthmus)"]'::jsonb, 'Anteverted (tilted forward) and anteflexed (bent forward at the isthmus)', 'In most women the uterus is anteverted (tilted forward over the bladder) and anteflexed (bent forward on itself at the isthmus). Retroversion and retroflexion are less common variations.'),
('9e7d7cdf-4105-5563-9efd-b80f69aa94d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Retroversion of the uterus is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","When the uterus is bent backward accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","When the uterus is tilted posteriorly (toward the rectum) instead of anteriorly","When the uterus is straight leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'When the uterus is tilted posteriorly (toward the rectum) instead of anteriorly', 'Retroversion is posterior tilting of the entire uterus, so its long axis points toward the sacrum and rectum rather than over the bladder. Backward bending of the body alone is retroflexion, a different condition.'),
('10393091-6d01-5892-aa3b-5f45573362c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Retroflexion of the uterus is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","When the uterus is straight mediated by specific receptor activation","When the uterus is tilted posteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","When the uterus is bent backward at the isthmus"]'::jsonb, 'When the uterus is bent backward at the isthmus', 'Retroflexion is posterior bending of the uterine body on the cervix at the isthmus, so the fundus points backward. It differs from retroversion, in which the whole uterus tilts posteriorly.'),
('fc666d92-d18a-5ec4-9b1d-8907f38b92fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Anteflexion of the uterus is:', '["When the uterus is bent backward resulting in decreased functional activity","When the uterus is straight accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","When the uterus is bent forward at the isthmus (the normal position)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'When the uterus is bent forward at the isthmus (the normal position)', 'Anteflexion is forward bending of the uterine body at the isthmus and, together with anteversion, forms the normal position of the uterus. This places the fundus over the bladder.'),
('e9fb0138-36c1-5a71-a1cf-bac5b59f640f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Anteversion of the uterus is:', '["When the uterus is straight resulting in decreased functional activity","When the uterus is tilted forward (over the bladder) - the normal position","None mediated by specific receptor activation","When the uterus is tilted backward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'When the uterus is tilted forward (over the bladder) - the normal position', 'Anteversion is forward tilting of the uterus as a whole so that its long axis is angled toward the bladder, which is the normal position in most women. It is distinct from anteflexion, the forward bend at the isthmus.'),
('b43c007a-eb65-578e-b501-29cdb3b7ed0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervix is divided into:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the supravaginal part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the vaginal part mediated by specific receptor activation","The supravaginal part (above the vagina) and the vaginal part (projects into the vagina)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The supravaginal part (above the vagina) and the vaginal part (projects into the vagina)', 'The cervix is divided into a supravaginal part lying above the vaginal attachment and a vaginal part that projects into the vaginal canal. Only the vaginal part can be seen on speculum examination.'),
('e07760e5-f539-59f8-96d9-a68a6b92e307'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervical canal communicates with:', '["The uterine cavity (via the internal os) and the vagina (via the external os)","Only the uterine cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the vagina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The uterine cavity (via the internal os) and the vagina (via the external os)', 'The cervical canal links the uterine cavity above through the internal os and the vagina below through the external os. This channel permits the passage of sperm and menstrual fluid and is traversed during childbirth.'),
('1bde144f-9888-5dd4-aff6-3d1dee678118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The internal os is:', '["The opening of the cervical canal into the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity","The opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening of the uterine tube into the uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity', 'The internal os is the upper opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity. The lower opening into the vagina is the external os, and the tubal openings into the uterus are separate structures.'),
('6f36a692-8b1b-57ab-adbd-8dfdd3c09e29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The external os is:', '["None","The opening of the uterine tube into the uterus","The opening of the cervical canal into the vagina","The opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity","All"]'::jsonb, 'The opening of the cervical canal into the vagina', 'The external os is the lower opening of the cervical canal into the vagina and is the part of the cervix seen during speculum examination. The opening into the uterine cavity above is the internal os.'),
('24b300f2-88af-53d7-8fe2-c85ccb22b318'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervix is primarily made up of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Dense connective tissue (fibrous tissue) with smooth muscle","Only glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Only smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only connective tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dense connective tissue (fibrous tissue) with smooth muscle', 'The cervix is composed mainly of dense fibrous connective tissue with a relatively small amount of smooth muscle, giving it a firm consistency. This contrasts with the predominantly muscular body of the uterus.'),
('ea733cc0-1af4-5255-888f-14840b9fd8e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vaginal part of the cervix is covered by:', '["Keratinized squamous epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Columnar epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium', 'The vaginal (ectocervical) surface of the cervix is covered by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which resists the acidic vaginal environment. Keratinized and transitional epithelia are found elsewhere, not on this surface.'),
('ffaf8a31-c350-5a67-88c7-c1a9a272b527'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervical canal is lined by:', '["Transitional epithelium","None","Pseudostratified epithelium","Stratified squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium (mucus-secreting)"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar epithelium (mucus-secreting)', 'The cervical canal is lined by simple columnar, mucus-secreting epithelium whose secretion forms the cervical mucus plug. The ectocervical surface, in contrast, carries stratified squamous epithelium.'),
('7967b6f7-2966-5835-943c-8e86d17e2dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The squamocolumnar junction of the cervix is:', '["The junction between the cervix and the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The junction between the cervix and the vagina mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The junction between the squamous epithelium of the ectocervix and the columnar epithelium of the endocervix (the transformation zone)"]'::jsonb, 'The junction between the squamous epithelium of the ectocervix and the columnar epithelium of the endocervix (the transformation zone)', 'The squamocolumnar junction is the boundary between the squamous epithelium of the ectocervix and the columnar epithelium of the endocervix, a region of active squamous metaplasia called the transformation zone. This is the usual site where cervical dysplasia arises.'),
('cc7e3688-5fb0-5d9b-91d2-2cd4972347ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The transformation zone of the cervix is important because:', '["It is the site of ovulation resulting in decreased functional activity","It is the most common site for cervical dysplasia and cervical cancer","It is the site of menstruation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is the most common site for cervical dysplasia and cervical cancer', 'Because squamous metaplasia is most active at the transformation zone, this region is the commonest site for cervical dysplasia and cervical cancer. Ovulation and menstruation involve the ovary and uterus, not the cervix.'),
('2f06c733-128f-56e5-8991-11c58f609a1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Papanicolaou (Pap) smears are used to:', '["Screen for ovarian cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Screen for uterine cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Screen for vaginal cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Screen for cervical dysplasia and cervical cancer (by sampling cells from the transformation zone)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Screen for cervical dysplasia and cervical cancer (by sampling cells from the transformation zone)', 'A Pap smear samples cells from the cervix, particularly the transformation zone, to detect precancerous changes and cervical cancer. It does not screen the ovaries or the uterine cavity in a targeted fashion.'),
('104fe381-a6bf-5d42-aa62-39d652fda170'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Cervical cancer is most commonly:', '["Melanoma resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Squamous cell carcinoma (arising from the squamous epithelium of the cervix)","Adenocarcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sarcoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Squamous cell carcinoma (arising from the squamous epithelium of the cervix)', 'Squamous cell carcinoma accounts for the large majority of cervical cancers, arising from the squamous epithelium at or near the transformation zone. Adenocarcinoma is less common, and melanoma or sarcoma is rare.'),
('8a3ea124-b756-566c-b90c-bac08b1b3ae9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Human papillomavirus (HPV) is:', '["A virus that causes ovarian cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A virus that causes uterine cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sexually transmitted virus that is the primary cause of cervical cancer"]'::jsonb, 'A sexually transmitted virus that is the primary cause of cervical cancer', 'HPV is a sexually transmitted virus, and persistent infection with high-risk types (especially 16 and 18) is the primary cause of cervical cancer. It is not the cause of ovarian or uterine cancer.'),
('25ba91ca-85de-54d8-8766-f024209464b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The HPV vaccine is used to:', '["Prevent infection by the HPV types that are most commonly associated with cervical cancer","Treat cervical cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prevent all types of cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent infection by the HPV types that are most commonly associated with cervical cancer', 'The HPV vaccine prevents infection by the oncogenic HPV types most strongly linked to cervical cancer; it does not treat established disease. It offers no protection against unrelated cancers.'),
('b72db704-326b-5353-a832-506225b99dd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Clinical pelvimetry is:', '["The measurement of the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","The measurement of the baby''s head accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The measurement of the pelvic diameters (to assess the pelvic capacity for childbirth)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The measurement of the pelvic diameters (to assess the pelvic capacity for childbirth)', 'Clinical pelvimetry estimates the diameters of the pelvic inlet, midpelvis, and outlet to judge whether the pelvis can accommodate the fetal head during vaginal delivery. Measurement of the uterus or fetal head is not pelvimetry.'),
('42a91dec-7094-50b3-890d-bd3524cd3b7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The most important diameters of the pelvic inlet are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the transverse diameter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the true conjugate mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The true conjugate (obstetric conjugate) and the transverse diameter"]'::jsonb, 'The true conjugate (obstetric conjugate) and the transverse diameter', 'The key inlet diameters are the true (obstetric) conjugate, its anteroposterior dimension, and the transverse diameter between the arcuate lines. Both must be adequate for the fetal head to engage.'),
('068e6300-81b0-532a-8214-def90a586f7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The most important diameters of the pelvic outlet are:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the AP diameter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The intertuberous distance (between the ischial tuberosities) and the anteroposterior diameter (from the coccyx to the pubic symphysis)","Only the intertuberous distance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The intertuberous distance (between the ischial tuberosities) and the anteroposterior diameter (from the coccyx to the pubic symphysis)', 'The important outlet diameters are the intertuberous distance, its transverse measure, and the anteroposterior diameter running from the coccyx (or sacrotuberous ligament) to the lower border of the pubic symphysis. Narrowing of either impedes delivery.'),
('acad5743-4585-5179-a1f2-618b1c2bcb2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervix is approximately:', '["2.5-3 cm long (in a nulliparous adult)","1 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","4 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2.5-3 cm long (in a nulliparous adult)', 'The nulliparous cervix is about 2.5-3 cm long, forming roughly the lower third of the uterus. It begins to shorten (efface) and dilate during labor.'),
('8c936e9b-71a7-50a0-957e-00205987b9d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervical opening (external os) in a nulliparous female is:', '["All","Small and round (punctate)","None","Slit-like (transverse)","Large and irregular"]'::jsonb, 'Small and round (punctate)', 'In a nulliparous woman the external os is small, round, and punctate. Childbirth distorts this opening, so a slit-like os indicates prior vaginal delivery.'),
('94b8362a-8d16-5c99-8e4e-b557ad5a250e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervical opening (external os) in a parous female is:', '["Slit-like (transverse) - due to the healing of a small laceration at the sides of the cervix during childbirth","Large and irregular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small and round leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Slit-like (transverse) - due to the healing of a small laceration at the sides of the cervix during childbirth', 'After childbirth the external os becomes slit-like and transverse because small lacerations at the sides of the cervix heal with scarring. A nulliparous os, in contrast, remains small and round.'),
('3c301be5-f8aa-5b30-93ca-a596602746f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervix is innervated by:', '["Only the sympathetic nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","The pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) and the sympathetic nerves from the inferior hypogastric plexus","The pudendal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the pelvic splanchnic nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) and the sympathetic nerves from the inferior hypogastric plexus', 'The cervix receives parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) and sympathetic fibers from the inferior hypogastric plexus, accounting for its insensitivity to superficial stimuli but sensitivity to stretching. The pudendal nerve supplies the perineum rather than the cervix.'),
('90399f43-5831-5700-ac91-226531572beb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervix is supplied by:', '["The external iliac artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The ovarian artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The descending cervical branches of the uterine artery (and the vaginal artery)","The internal pudendal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The descending cervical branches of the uterine artery (and the vaginal artery)', 'The cervix is supplied chiefly by the descending cervical branches of the uterine artery, with additional blood coming from the vaginal artery. The ovarian and internal pudendal arteries supply different pelvic structures.'),
('e04f0de1-ae96-5a04-a7c1-e72f1b44fc93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervix is lymphatically drained to:', '["The para-aortic nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The axillary nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","The internal iliac nodes, the external iliac nodes, and the sacral lymph nodes","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac nodes, the external iliac nodes, and the sacral lymph nodes', 'Cervical lymphatics drain mainly to the internal iliac, external iliac, and sacral nodes. The para-aortic nodes receive ovarian and upper uterine drainage; inguinal nodes drain the vulva and lower vagina; axillary nodes serve the breast and upper limb.'),
('48e8883b-381b-5479-89dc-7cac13db1b45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The para-aortic lymph nodes receive lymph from:', '["The vulva resulting in decreased functional activity","The vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The cervix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fundus and the upper part of the body of the uterus (along the ovarian vessels)"]'::jsonb, 'The fundus and the upper part of the body of the uterus (along the ovarian vessels)', 'Lymph from the fundus and upper part of the uterine body travels with the ovarian vessels to the para-aortic nodes. The cervix drains to internal iliac, external iliac, and sacral nodes; the vulva to superficial inguinal nodes; the vagina to pelvic and inguinal nodes.'),
('679127b1-56ac-523d-bc2b-947a8858898a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The perineal body is the:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the perineal membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Central tendon of the perineum (a fibromuscular nodule in the midline of the perineum)","The same as the episiotomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central tendon of the perineum (a fibromuscular nodule in the midline of the perineum)', 'The perineal body is the central tendon of the perineum, a fibromuscular nodule in the midline between the vagina and anus. It is distinct from the perineal membrane, and an episiotomy is a surgical incision of the posterior vaginal wall and perineum, not the node itself.'),
('e8a4f4e8-6a87-589c-9456-48779510addc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The perineal body is the site of attachment of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The bulbospongiosus, the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, the external anal sphincter, and the levator ani","Only the levator ani mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the external anal sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the bulbospongiosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulbospongiosus, the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, the external anal sphincter, and the levator ani', 'The perineal body anchors the bulbospongiosus, superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, external anal sphincter, and levator ani, giving it a central stabilizing role in the pelvic floor. No single muscle of this group attaches there alone.'),
('7d0379fd-1abb-5145-aaf9-39c4521a050a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The perineal body is a common site for:', '["A tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","A tear during childbirth (a perineal tear) or an episiotomy","None mediated by specific receptor activation","An infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A tear during childbirth (a perineal tear) or an episiotomy', 'The perineal body is prone to tearing during delivery when the fetal head distends the outlet, and it is the site of an episiotomy incision. It is not a predilection site for tumors or infections.'),
('8158f403-9147-5509-b5a4-92bb00f3b869'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is about:', '["10-12 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","12-15 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","7-9 cm long (the anterior wall is shorter than the posterior wall)","3-5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '7-9 cm long (the anterior wall is shorter than the posterior wall)', 'The adult vagina is about 7-9 cm long, with the anterior wall shorter than the posterior wall because the cervix projects into its upper posterior end. Lengths of 10-15 cm or 3-5 cm do not match the standard anatomy.'),
('b2e7714c-0f41-59e7-89db-75ca71e22846'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is covered by:', '["Transitional epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium (with rugae that allow distension)","Columnar epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Keratinized squamous epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium (with rugae that allow distension)', 'The vagina is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium whose transverse folds, the rugae, allow distension. Transitional and columnar epithelia are found in the urinary tract and upper cervical canal, and keratinized squamous epithelium covers skin rather than the vagina.'),
('b957815d-5947-55e1-bebd-313950e18725'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is innervated by:', '["Only the pudendal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The pudendal nerve (lower vagina) and the pelvic splanchnic nerves (upper vagina)","Only the pelvic splanchnic nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","The ilioinguinal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve (lower vagina) and the pelvic splanchnic nerves (upper vagina)', 'The lower vagina is innervated by branches of the pudendal nerve, while the upper vagina receives parasympathetic and visceral afferent fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves. The ilioinguinal nerve supplies the mons pubis and anterior labia, not the vaginal canal.'),
('1e0d6e9e-9240-5d3c-a66a-380469e60225'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female pelvis is wider and shallower than the male pelvis:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","True (the female pelvis is adapted for childbirth)","False mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'True (the female pelvis is adapted for childbirth)', 'The female pelvis is wider and shallower than the male pelvis, an adaptation for childbirth. The male pelvis is deeper and narrower with a heart-shaped inlet and narrower outlet.'),
('8e36c115-c1aa-51f6-b96b-bb3574ca22c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pelvic inlet in the male is:', '["Flat resulting in decreased functional activity","Round accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heart-shaped (more narrow than the female inlet)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transversely oval leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heart-shaped (more narrow than the female inlet)', 'The male pelvic inlet is heart-shaped and narrower because of the projecting sacral promontory and shorter transverse diameter. The female inlet is transversely oval, rounder, and wider.'),
('3b8e9d92-aaf1-5415-8859-fe76d41b7eaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pelvic inlet in the female is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Heart-shaped accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flat mediated by specific receptor activation","Transversely oval (rounder and wider than the male inlet)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transversely oval (rounder and wider than the male inlet)', 'The female pelvic inlet is transversely oval, rounder, and wider to accommodate the fetal head. The heart-shaped and narrower inlet is a male feature.'),
('5fb87db7-2bfc-56c3-b97d-d304a147ddc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The subpubic angle in males is:', '["Narrow (< 90 degrees)","The same as in females","None","Wide (> 90 degrees)","All"]'::jsonb, 'Narrow (< 90 degrees)', 'In males the subpubic angle is narrow (<90 degrees), while in females it is wide (>90 degrees). The ischiopubic rami converge more in the male, creating the more acute angle.'),
('8c5718c6-6ca7-5e9a-89df-a313827e44f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ischial spines in males are:', '["The same as in females resulting in decreased functional activity","More prominent and inverted (closer together) than in females","Less prominent mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'More prominent and inverted (closer together) than in females', 'The male ischial spines are more prominent and lie closer together, narrowing the pelvic outlet. The female spines are shorter, blunter, and more widely separated, widening the outlet for delivery.'),
('883632a0-2bcb-5a76-8a7f-73bb8a981290'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The sacrum in males is:', '["Longer, narrower, and more curved (than in females)","Shorter, wider, and less curved accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as in females mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longer, narrower, and more curved (than in females)', 'The male sacrum is longer, narrower, and more curved, which narrows the pelvic cavity and outlet. The female sacrum is shorter, wider, and less curved, enlarging the pelvic cavity for childbirth.'),
('8ea65dec-1bf0-53c6-aa08-680cbadd86f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The coccyx in females is:', '["Less curved and more mobile (to allow the baby to pass through the pelvic outlet)","The same as in males accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","More curved and less mobile mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Less curved and more mobile (to allow the baby to pass through the pelvic outlet)', 'The female coccyx is less curved and more mobile, allowing backward hinge at delivery to widen the outlet. A more curved and less mobile coccyx is a male feature.'),
('a57acd53-9507-534d-bbd5-1a1b0c4c5abb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pelvic axis is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The curving line that passes through the center of the pelvic cavity (the path the baby follows during childbirth)","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the pelvic inlet without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A straight line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The curving line that passes through the center of the pelvic cavity (the path the baby follows during childbirth)', 'The pelvic axis is the curved line that passes through the centers of the inlet, cavity, and outlet, and is the path the fetal head follows in labor. It is curved rather than straight and distinct from the pelvic inlet.'),
('85dfe933-2a63-53c6-a577-8ededfcc9476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ischiopubic rami in females are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as in males accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Longer and more converging mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shorter and less converging (creating a wider subpubic angle)"]'::jsonb, 'Shorter and less converging (creating a wider subpubic angle)', 'In females the ischiopubic rami are shorter and converge less, producing the wide (>90 degrees) subpubic angle. Longer, more converging rami are a male feature.'),
('4db89a2c-1bf4-5e99-b76c-64d099a7e94a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ischiopubic rami in males are:', '["Longer and more converging (creating a narrower subpubic angle)","Shorter and less converging accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as in females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longer and more converging (creating a narrower subpubic angle)', 'In males the ischiopubic rami are longer and converge more, giving the narrow (<90 degrees) subpubic angle. The female rami are shorter and diverge more to form a wide angle.'),
('afd8c7a4-3c6f-5a07-8865-3cd1a2c0bae9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is about:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","3-4 cm x 2-3 cm x 1-2 cm (in a premenopausal adult)","8 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","1-2 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm x 2-3 cm x 1-2 cm (in a premenopausal adult)', 'A premenopausal adult ovary measures about 3-4 x 2-3 x 1-2 cm, roughly the size of a walnut. Single values such as 1-2 cm, 5 cm, or 8 cm do not reflect the correct three dimensions.'),
('8c8c8460-720c-551f-bbac-f58d1595d5b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary weighs about:', '["20 g resulting in decreased functional activity","50 g accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","1-2 g without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5-10 g (in a premenopausal adult)"]'::jsonb, '5-10 g (in a premenopausal adult)', 'A premenopausal adult ovary weighs about 5-10 g. Weights of 1-2 g, 20 g, or 50 g fall outside the normal range for the adult ovary.'),
('4239ab21-4130-5ab1-b368-86253ed1a90b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is located in:', '["The ovarian fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","The uterine tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovarian fossa (on the lateral pelvic wall, bounded by the external and internal iliac vessels and the ureter)","The broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ovarian fossa (on the lateral pelvic wall, bounded by the external and internal iliac vessels and the ureter)', 'The ovary lies in the ovarian fossa on the lateral pelvic wall, bounded by the external and internal iliac vessels and the ureter. It is not inside the uterine tube or the broad ligament, although it attaches to the broad ligament via the mesovarium.'),
('4ef0eabb-5fe6-57ff-85b3-51be9120b23d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian fossa is:', '["A fossa in the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A deep recess in the pelvic cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A shallow depression on the lateral pelvic wall (anterior to the internal iliac artery and posterior to the external iliac artery)"]'::jsonb, 'A shallow depression on the lateral pelvic wall (anterior to the internal iliac artery and posterior to the external iliac artery)', 'The ovarian fossa is a shallow depression on the lateral pelvic wall, lying posterior to the external iliac artery and anterior to the internal iliac artery, with the ureter descending along its base. It is not a recess in the uterus or a deep pelvic cavity.'),
('0d97ad8d-21cc-5861-951d-7acc6dd00992'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is attached to the broad ligament by:', '["The suspensory ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The mesosalpinx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ovarian ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mesovarium (a peritoneal fold that attaches the ovary to the posterior layer of the broad ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'The mesovarium (a peritoneal fold that attaches the ovary to the posterior layer of the broad ligament)', 'The mesovarium is the peritoneal fold that suspends the ovary from the posterior layer of the broad ligament. The suspensory ligament carries the ovarian vessels, the ovarian ligament connects the ovary to the uterine cornu, and the mesosalpinx supports the uterine tube.'),
('f72f5602-3d92-5470-95b5-25b40b3d1f01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is covered by:', '["The peritoneum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The germinal epithelium (a single layer of cuboidal cells - the embryologic coelomic epithelium)","The vaginal epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The germinal epithelium (a single layer of cuboidal cells - the embryologic coelomic epithelium)', 'The ovary is covered by a single layer of cuboidal germinal epithelium derived from the embryologic coelomic epithelium. Unlike most pelvic viscera it has no peritoneal covering over its surface, and vaginal epithelium is unrelated.'),
('e08a7f8e-886e-5047-904c-30e45de0f573'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian cortex contains:', '["The ovarian medulla resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovarian vessels mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ovarian follicles at various stages of development (primordial, primary, secondary, and tertiary (graafian) follicles)"]'::jsonb, 'The ovarian follicles at various stages of development (primordial, primary, secondary, and tertiary (graafian) follicles)', 'The ovarian cortex contains follicles in all stages, from primordial to tertiary (Graafian), together with stromal cells. Blood vessels and nerves lie mainly in the medulla, not the cortex.'),
('fe131345-e528-51ae-9d78-711bcec26f30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovarian medulla contains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The blood vessels, nerves, and connective tissue (the hilum of the ovary)","The oocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The follicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The blood vessels, nerves, and connective tissue (the hilum of the ovary)', 'The ovarian medulla contains the blood vessels, nerves, and loose connective tissue that enter at the hilum. Follicles and oocytes are located in the cortex, not the medulla.'),
('f21b9f18-0bcd-5d77-9a45-0ac8e270ca1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube is about:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","25 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","15-20 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","10-12 cm long (from the ovary to the uterus)","5-7 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10-12 cm long (from the ovary to the uterus)', 'The uterine tube is about 10-12 cm long, running from the uterine cornu to the ovary. Lengths of 25 cm, 15-20 cm, or 5-7 cm either overestimate or underestimate the standard measurement.'),
('0f842e39-7409-52f6-b616-40f475452064'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube opens into:', '["The vagina resulting in decreased functional activity","The uterine cavity only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The peritoneal cavity only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The peritoneal cavity (infundibulum with its fimbriae) and into the uterine cavity (the uterine opening)"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal cavity (infundibulum with its fimbriae) and into the uterine cavity (the uterine opening)', 'At its lateral end the uterine tube opens into the peritoneal cavity through the infundibulum and its fimbriae, and at its medial end it opens into the uterine cavity. It does not open into the vagina.'),
('8cae137b-f10a-586a-b4de-d84210e791d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The fimbriae of the uterine tube are:', '["The same as the isthmus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the ampulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Finger-like projections at the end of the infundibulum (they help capture the ovum from the surface of the ovary at ovulation)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Finger-like projections at the end of the infundibulum (they help capture the ovum from the surface of the ovary at ovulation)', 'The fimbriae are finger-like projections of the infundibulum that sweep over the ovarian surface to capture the ovum at ovulation. The ampulla and isthmus are separate tubal segments, not fimbriae.'),
('b6e26228-3db3-53a2-b3f2-2960f06c8223'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The fimbria ovarica is:', '["A fimbria that is attached to the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","The longest fimbria (it is attached to the ovary and helps guide the ovum into the tube)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fimbria that is attached to the broad ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The longest fimbria (it is attached to the ovary and helps guide the ovum into the tube)', 'The fimbria ovarica is the longest fimbria and is attached to the ovary, helping to guide the ovum into the tubal ostium. It is not attached to the uterus or broad ligament.'),
('f2b4fea6-af76-5cd3-bc28-4c6001381306'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The peristaltic movements of the uterine tube help:', '["Regulate the blood flow to the ovary resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transport the ovum (or the fertilized ovum) from the ovary to the uterus","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transport the sperm from the uterus to the ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transport the ovum (or the fertilized ovum) from the ovary to the uterus', 'Peristaltic contractions of the tubal musculature, together with ciliary action, transport the ovum or early embryo from the ovary toward the uterus. Sperm travel against this flow, and tubal movements do not regulate ovarian blood flow.'),
('d6e3dbed-c0a9-5271-afa2-99182a329230'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube is supplied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Both the uterine and ovarian arteries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the uterine artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The uterine artery (tubal branch) and the ovarian artery (tubal branch)","Only the ovarian artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The uterine artery (tubal branch) and the ovarian artery (tubal branch)', 'The uterine tube is supplied by the tubal branches of both the uterine and ovarian arteries, which anastomose within the mesosalpinx. Supply from either artery alone would not cover the full tube.'),
('dc9bd7cc-2c3b-5608-846a-fa9fcc614693'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine tube is innervated by:', '["The sympathetic (celiac and hypogastric plexuses) and parasympathetic (pelvic splanchnic nerves) nerves","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the sympathetic nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the parasympathetic nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pudendal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sympathetic (celiac and hypogastric plexuses) and parasympathetic (pelvic splanchnic nerves) nerves', 'The uterine tube receives sympathetic fibers from the celiac and hypogastric plexuses and parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves. The pudendal nerve does not supply the uterine tube.'),
('b0eff937-1f5f-55e4-9852-db9f8b690fc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus weighs about:', '["50-100 g (in a nulliparous adult)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20-30 g mediated by specific receptor activation","More than 200 g without affecting the primary tissue pathway","150-200 g leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '50-100 g (in a nulliparous adult)', 'A nulliparous adult uterus weighs about 50-100 g. After pregnancy the organ remains heavier, and weights of 20-30 g or above 200 g do not reflect the nulliparous adult state.'),
('d1b2f153-9547-58b1-846b-5ab90e21692c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is composed of:', '["The perimetrium (a serous layer), the myometrium (the thick, muscular middle layer), and the endometrium (the inner mucosal layer)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the endometrium mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the perimetrium without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the myometrium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perimetrium (a serous layer), the myometrium (the thick, muscular middle layer), and the endometrium (the inner mucosal layer)', 'The uterine wall has three layers: the perimetrium (outer serosa), the myometrium (thick muscular middle layer), and the endometrium (inner mucosa). No single layer alone constitutes the organ.'),
('19d58086-d274-5984-8894-bc265d1d087f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The myometrium is the:', '["Inner layer of the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Thickest layer of the uterus (made up of smooth muscle fibers that contract during childbirth)","Outer layer of the uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thickest layer of the uterus (made up of smooth muscle fibers that contract during childbirth)', 'The myometrium is the thickest layer of the uterine wall, composed of smooth muscle that contracts during labor and involution. The endometrium is the inner mucosal layer and the perimetrium is the outer serous layer.'),
('4fcdba07-1bfe-563e-834d-df8506e806ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The endometrium is the:', '["Inner mucosal layer of the uterus (it changes during the menstrual cycle and provides a site for implantation of a fertilized ovum)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The outer layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The muscular layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inner mucosal layer of the uterus (it changes during the menstrual cycle and provides a site for implantation of a fertilized ovum)', 'The endometrium is the inner mucosal layer that undergoes proliferative and secretory changes in the menstrual cycle and accepts implantation of the blastocyst. The muscular (myometrium) and outer serous layers are distinct.'),
('10abe4b6-0f17-5789-a221-87892aaa0dda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The myometrium of the non-pregnant uterus is about:', '["None","4 cm thick","3 cm thick","0.5 cm thick","1.5-2 cm thick"]'::jsonb, '1.5-2 cm thick', 'The myometrium of the non-pregnant uterus is about 1.5-2 cm thick. Thinner or thicker measurements would indicate an abnormal state or a pregnant uterus.'),
('ce78b96d-5dc8-5f6b-92ec-90668e3743b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The endometrium is about:', '["1-2 mm resulting in decreased functional activity","5-7 mm thick (during the secretory phase of the menstrual cycle it is at its thickest)","8-10 mm mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3-4 mm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-7 mm thick (during the secretory phase of the menstrual cycle it is at its thickest)', 'The endometrium reaches about 5-7 mm in thickness at its peak in the secretory phase. Measurements of only a few millimeters represent proliferative, menstrual, or postmenopausal states.'),
('4d87e88a-f4ac-528d-983f-3c5e712b381a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine cavity is the:', '["The same as the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the cervical canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Space inside the body of the uterus (it is a potential space that is flattened in the anteroposterior direction)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Space inside the body of the uterus (it is a potential space that is flattened in the anteroposterior direction)', 'The uterine cavity is the potential space inside the body of the uterus, flattened anteroposteriorly and triangular on coronal section. It is distinct from the cervical canal and the vagina.'),
('43c7a4c6-5a65-5473-856f-bfb52b76928d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cervical canal is the:', '["The same as the uterine cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the vagina mediated by specific receptor activation","Space inside the cervix (connects the uterine cavity to the vagina)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Space inside the cervix (connects the uterine cavity to the vagina)', 'The cervical canal is the space within the cervix that connects the uterine cavity above to the vagina below. It is neither the uterine cavity nor the vagina itself.'),
('28025601-c3e3-5b46-b2c1-2b259e934285'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The internal os is the:', '["Opening of the cervical canal into the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity","Opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Opening of the uterine tube into the uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity', 'The internal os is the opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity. The opening of the cervical canal into the vagina is the external os.'),
('1c8a0e73-630a-555d-b246-fc992d57f36d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The external os is the:', '["All","None","Opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity","Opening of the cervical canal into the vagina","Opening of the uterine tube into the uterus"]'::jsonb, 'Opening of the cervical canal into the vagina', 'The external os is the opening of the cervical canal into the vagina. The opening of the cervical canal into the uterine cavity is the internal os.'),
('35b54ca4-f327-5d0c-825d-d95fdd5de825'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The endocervical canal is lined by:', '["None","Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium (mucus-secreting)","Stratified squamous epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Simple columnar epithelium (mucus-secreting)', 'The endocervical canal is lined by simple columnar, mucus-secreting epithelium. Stratified squamous epithelium covers the ectocervix, while transitional and pseudostratified epithelia are found in the urinary and respiratory tracts.'),
('31060746-593f-5059-98c9-231a2472d4f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ectocervix (the vaginal part of the cervix) is lined by:', '["Pseudostratified epithelium","Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","None","Simple columnar epithelium"]'::jsonb, 'Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium', 'The ectocervix is covered by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, matching the adjacent vaginal lining. Columnar, transitional, and pseudostratified epithelia are not found here.'),
('a516a682-28fb-5dbc-92e2-1424454bb189'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The squamocolumnar junction (SCJ) of the cervix is:', '["The junction where the columnar epithelium of the endocervical canal meets the stratified squamous epithelium of the ectocervix","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The junction of the cervix and the uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The junction of the cervix and the vagina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The junction where the columnar epithelium of the endocervical canal meets the stratified squamous epithelium of the ectocervix', 'The squamocolumnar junction is the point where the columnar epithelium of the endocervical canal meets the stratified squamous epithelium of the ectocervix; it forms the transformation zone, the common site of cervical neoplasia.'),
('0f160735-e512-5369-9f6a-c1abe4dfdf39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The SCJ is an important site because:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","It is the site of fertilization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is the site of ovulation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is the most common site for cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) and cervical cancer"]'::jsonb, 'It is the most common site for cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) and cervical cancer', 'The squamocolumnar junction is the most common site for cervical intraepithelial neoplasia and cervical cancer, related to metaplasia in the transformation zone. It is not a site of fertilization or ovulation.'),
('1fadabf9-ba5c-521e-ad55-3fa2148f9f77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is a:', '["A non-distensible tube resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A rigid tube mediated by specific receptor activation","Distensible, fibromuscular canal (from the cervix to the vulva) that is about 7-9 cm long","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distensible, fibromuscular canal (from the cervix to the vulva) that is about 7-9 cm long', 'The vagina is a distensible fibromuscular canal, about 7-9 cm long, running from the cervix to the vulva. Its rugose mucosa and muscular wall allow marked stretching during childbirth, so it is not rigid or non-distensible.'),
('06f876e2-f1ee-5d9e-9417-113baff51160'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is directed:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Horizontally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upward and backward (from the vulva to the cervix)","Straight down without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Downward and forward leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upward and backward (from the vulva to the cervix)', 'The vagina ascends upward and backward from the vulva to the cervix, meeting the uterus at about a right angle. It does not run horizontally or straight down.'),
('39203b1c-c4a8-5359-8e1b-d2230f94fca1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vaginal wall is:', '["Cartilaginous resulting in decreased functional activity","Thin and has a mucosa (with rugae), a muscular layer (smooth muscle), and a fibrous adventitia","Thick and has a serosal layer mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin and has a mucosa (with rugae), a muscular layer (smooth muscle), and a fibrous adventitia', 'The vaginal wall is thin and has three coats: a mucosa with rugae, a smooth-muscle muscularis, and an outer fibrous adventitia. It lacks a serosa and is neither cartilaginous nor thick.'),
('32dbfbf5-a520-54b5-ad18-671b263c32f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is lymphatically drained to:', '["Only the inguinal nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the external iliac nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the internal iliac nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal iliac nodes (upper vagina), the external iliac nodes (middle vagina), and the inguinal nodes (lower vagina)"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac nodes (upper vagina), the external iliac nodes (middle vagina), and the inguinal nodes (lower vagina)', 'The upper third of the vagina drains to the internal iliac nodes, the middle third to the external iliac nodes, and the lower third to the superficial inguinal nodes, so all three nodal groups are involved. Choices naming only a single nodal group overlook the segmental drainage of the vaginal wall.'),
('50fc4180-8171-5ce9-a27c-9112fb366ef6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina develops from:', '["Only the paramesonephric ducts resulting in decreased functional activity","The urogenital sinus (the lower part) and the fusion of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts (the upper part)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the urogenital sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mesonephric ducts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital sinus (the lower part) and the fusion of the paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts (the upper part)', 'The upper part of the vagina arises from the fused paramesonephric (Mullerian) ducts, while the lower part develops from the urogenital sinus. Selecting only one source ignores this dual embryologic origin, and the mesonephric ducts do not contribute.'),
('220c72ae-b022-5840-a8f5-d8d97ff9b067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The clitoris is supplied by:', '["The obturator nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The dorsal artery of the clitoris and the deep artery of the clitoris (branches of the internal pudendal artery)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The genitofemoral nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pudendal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The dorsal artery of the clitoris and the deep artery of the clitoris (branches of the internal pudendal artery)', 'The clitoris is vascularized by the dorsal and deep arteries of the clitoris, both branches of the internal pudendal artery. The obturator, genitofemoral, and pudendal nerve entries describe nerve supply rather than arterial supply.'),
('3bf8f294-89c0-5500-9012-39032e7bb48c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The clitoris is innervated by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The ilioinguinal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The genitofemoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The dorsal nerve of the clitoris (a branch of the pudendal nerve, S2-S4)","The femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The dorsal nerve of the clitoris (a branch of the pudendal nerve, S2-S4)', 'The dorsal nerve of the clitoris, a terminal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4), supplies sensation to the clitoris. The ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and femoral nerves do not innervate the clitoris.'),
('34ee9ff5-6307-5b36-8fba-a236e54f4665'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Bartholin glands secrete:', '["A mucus-like fluid (to lubricate the vagina during sexual intercourse)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Hormones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sperm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mucus-like fluid (to lubricate the vagina during sexual intercourse)', 'The greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands secrete a mucus-like fluid that lubricates the vagina during intercourse. They do not produce hormones or sperm.'),
('ac4e5f05-b40b-57e4-b4f2-f2655a212134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Bartholin glands can become:', '["Abscessed resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Cancerous without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infected and enlarged (Bartholinitis), or obstructed and form a cyst (Bartholin cyst)"]'::jsonb, 'Infected and enlarged (Bartholinitis), or obstructed and form a cyst (Bartholin cyst)', 'Bartholin glands may become infected and enlarged (Bartholinitis) or obstructed, forming a Bartholin cyst. Abscess and carcinoma are particular complications rather than the general range of possible outcomes.'),
('31101708-5a18-5790-aac9-5009f6511f1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'A Bartholin abscess is:', '["A cyst of the Bartholin gland resulting in decreased functional activity","A painless infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A painful infection of the Bartholin gland (it may require incision and drainage)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A painful infection of the Bartholin gland (it may require incision and drainage)', 'A Bartholin abscess is a painful infection of the Bartholin gland that typically requires incision and drainage. A gland cyst is painless unless secondarily infected, so an infection of this gland is not painless.'),
('6248448d-5d8e-561d-aaa3-1bd1e128a5a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Skene glands (paraurethral glands) are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Small glands in the female urethra (homologous to the male prostate)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the Bartholin glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the Cowper glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small glands in the female urethra (homologous to the male prostate)', 'The paraurethral (Skene) glands lie within the female urethra and are homologous to the male prostate. They are distinct from the Bartholin and Cowper glands.'),
('6b9538b4-a478-5401-b9f0-68a960ce115c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Skene glands are located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","In the clitoris accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the labia majora mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Around the external urethral orifice (they secrete a fluid that may contribute to female ejaculation)"]'::jsonb, 'Around the external urethral orifice (they secrete a fluid that may contribute to female ejaculation)', 'The Skene glands open around the external urethral orifice, and their secretions may contribute to female ejaculation. They are not located in the clitoris or the labia majora.'),
('74078875-f739-5688-9086-c14713493530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Skene glands can become:', '["Cancerous resulting in decreased functional activity","Infected (Skeneitis) and can cause symptoms of urethritis","Obstructed mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infected (Skeneitis) and can cause symptoms of urethritis', 'The Skene glands can become infected (Skeneitis), producing symptoms of urethritis. Obstruction and malignancy are not the characteristic problems of these glands in this context.'),
('84404f05-dbe9-5bba-97e1-42ae3efd9fa2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The external genitalia of the female (the vulva) include:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the clitoris and vestibule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the vagina mediated by specific receptor activation","The mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule, vestibular bulbs, and Bartholin glands","Only the labia majora and minora leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule, vestibular bulbs, and Bartholin glands', 'The vulva encompasses the mons pubis, labia majora, labia minora, clitoris, vestibule, vestibular bulbs, and Bartholin glands. The vagina is an internal tube and is not part of the external genitalia, and naming only a subset omits major components.'),
('424b4449-3581-5d28-adbd-062f16a2b7a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vestibule of the vagina is:', '["The same as the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the perineal body mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the labia minora (the urethral opening, the vaginal opening, and the Bartholin glands open into the vestibule)"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the labia minora (the urethral opening, the vaginal opening, and the Bartholin glands open into the vestibule)', 'The vestibule is the space between the labia minora into which the urethral and vaginal openings and the Bartholin gland ducts open. It is neither the vagina nor the perineal body.'),
('22e35d5a-dfce-5e3d-a5f8-81e1a6c3f3f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The urethral opening in the female is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Located inside the vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Located on the perineal body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Located in the vestibule, just anterior to the vaginal opening (and posterior to the clitoris)"]'::jsonb, 'Located in the vestibule, just anterior to the vaginal opening (and posterior to the clitoris)', 'In females the external urethral orifice lies in the vestibule, anterior to the vaginal opening and posterior to the clitoris. It is not inside the vagina and does not lie on the perineal body.'),
('1ae8b198-b9d1-5d0c-8a99-790b17e63cd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vaginal opening is:', '["Located in the posterior part of the vestibule (behind the urethral opening)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Located in the anterior part of the vestibule mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the urethral opening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Located in the posterior part of the vestibule (behind the urethral opening)', 'The vaginal opening occupies the posterior part of the vestibule, behind the urethral orifice. It is not in the anterior vestibule and is distinct from the urethral opening.'),
('6dc17e54-1c7f-57f1-a645-c1987f9a9918'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vestibular bulbs are covered by:', '["The bulbospongiosus muscle","All","None","The perineal membrane","The ischiocavernosus muscle"]'::jsonb, 'The bulbospongiosus muscle', 'The vestibular bulbs are superficial erectile bodies covered by the bulbospongiosus muscle. The ischiocavernosus muscles cover the crura of the clitoris rather than the bulbs.'),
('174db24a-d191-5691-819b-874ef8022ef9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The perineum in the female is:', '["The diamond-shaped region between the vulva (anteriorly) and the anal canal (posteriorly)","The same as the perineal body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The region between the labia majora leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The diamond-shaped region between the vulva (anteriorly) and the anal canal (posteriorly)', 'The female perineum is a diamond-shaped region extending from the vulva anteriorly to the anal canal posteriorly. It is much broader than the perineal body, which is only the midline mass between the vagina and the anus.'),
('2ecb11eb-1799-5ec2-8497-43494c13a02a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pudendal nerve block in the female is used to:', '["Provide anesthesia for a hysterectomy resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Provide anesthesia to the perineum (the vulva, vagina, and perineal body) for an episiotomy or an operative vaginal delivery","Provide anesthesia for a cesarean section without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provide anesthesia to the perineum (the vulva, vagina, and perineal body) for an episiotomy or an operative vaginal delivery', 'A pudendal nerve block anesthetizes the vulva, vagina, and perineal body for an episiotomy or operative vaginal delivery. It does not provide anesthesia for a cesarean section or hysterectomy, which require different techniques.'),
('2ca2abc7-a87e-5d88-bfb0-acbe112e6a04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pudendal nerve is located:', '["Near the ischial spine (it can be blocked by injecting an anesthetic near the ischial spine)","Near the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Near the pubic symphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Near the ischial spine (it can be blocked by injecting an anesthetic near the ischial spine)', 'The pudendal nerve runs behind the sacrospinous ligament beside the ischial spine, which is the landmark for a pudendal nerve block. It is not located near the sacrum or the pubic symphysis.'),
('0835f32d-a5ec-515d-9951-4bc4faf08840'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'An episiotomy is:', '["An incision through the abdomen resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A surgical incision through the perineal body and the posterior vaginal wall (to enlarge the vaginal opening during childbirth)","An incision through the cervix mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical incision through the perineal body and the posterior vaginal wall (to enlarge the vaginal opening during childbirth)', 'An episiotomy is a surgical incision through the perineal body and posterior vaginal wall that enlarges the vaginal opening during childbirth. An abdominal incision describes a cesarean delivery, and cutting the cervix is an unrelated procedure.'),
('8705b51e-20b9-5812-96b2-915bdb960140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'A midline episiotomy is:', '["An incision from the posterior vaginal opening to the anus (through the perineal body in the midline)","An incision from the posterior vaginal opening at a 45-degree angle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","An incision through the labia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An incision from the posterior vaginal opening to the anus (through the perineal body in the midline)', 'A midline (median) episiotomy extends from the posterior vaginal opening toward the anus through the perineal body in the midline. A 45-degree incision describes a mediolateral, not a midline, episiotomy.'),
('d8b20de5-75bd-58da-921e-be412940ac30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'A mediolateral episiotomy is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","An incision through the perineal body (in the midline) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","An incision through the labia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An incision from the posterior vaginal opening at a 45-degree angle (usually toward the right or left ischial tuberosity)"]'::jsonb, 'An incision from the posterior vaginal opening at a 45-degree angle (usually toward the right or left ischial tuberosity)', 'A mediolateral episiotomy begins at the posterior vaginal opening and angles at approximately 45 degrees toward one ischial tuberosity. A midline incision through the perineal body instead describes a median episiotomy.'),
('e876796b-ff57-501a-896b-cf88a1bb0e1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'A mediolateral episiotomy is preferred when:', '["The baby is small resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","There is a high risk of extending the episiotomy into the external anal sphincter or the rectum (e.g., in a difficult delivery or if the perineum is very tight)","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mother is of normal size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'There is a high risk of extending the episiotomy into the external anal sphincter or the rectum (e.g., in a difficult delivery or if the perineum is very tight)', 'A mediolateral episiotomy is preferred when extension into the external anal sphincter or rectum is likely, as in a difficult delivery or a very tight perineum. Fetal or maternal size alone does not dictate this choice.'),
('459ef8da-bbea-5244-8ccb-07d1ca84dcf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vaginal wall is supplied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The vaginal artery (from the internal iliac artery) and branches from the uterine artery and the internal pudendal artery","Only the uterine artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the vaginal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the internal pudendal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vaginal artery (from the internal iliac artery) and branches from the uterine artery and the internal pudendal artery', 'The vagina is supplied by the vaginal artery arising from the internal iliac artery, reinforced by branches of the uterine and internal pudendal arteries. No single artery supplies the entire vaginal wall.'),
('81b83f6f-38df-57bb-a168-a6e6a6116f18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is an important site for the:', '["Absorption of medications (e.g., estrogen cream) and examination of the cervix (by speculum examination)","Secretion of hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Absorption of nutrients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absorption of medications (e.g., estrogen cream) and examination of the cervix (by speculum examination)', 'The vagina permits absorption of medications such as estrogen cream and allows examination of the cervix with a speculum. It does not secrete hormones or absorb nutrients as its primary functions.'),
('8d2f7261-3d32-5e51-8ead-6db75e0598d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The fallopian tubes are an important site for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Development of the fetus mediated by specific receptor activation","Implantation (the fertilized ovum implants in the tube) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fertilization (the union of the sperm and the ovum in the ampulla of the tube)"]'::jsonb, 'Fertilization (the union of the sperm and the ovum in the ampulla of the tube)', 'Fertilization normally occurs in the ampulla of the fallopian tube, where the sperm meets the ovum. Implantation and fetal development take place in the uterus rather than in the tubal lumen.'),
('37f4b50b-bce3-53fb-8dc2-2bcc9f944587'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is an important site for:', '["Ovulation resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fertilization mediated by specific receptor activation","Implantation of the fertilized ovum and the development of the fetus","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Implantation of the fertilized ovum and the development of the fetus', 'The uterus is the site of implantation of the fertilized ovum and of fetal development. Fertilization occurs in the fallopian tube and ovulation in the ovary, not in the uterus.'),
('b9fc537d-fb30-5ced-9a36-0b8866db6cb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovaries are an important site for:', '["Implantation resulting in decreased functional activity","Ovulation (the release of a mature oocyte) and the production of hormones (estrogen and progesterone)","Fertilization mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation (the release of a mature oocyte) and the production of hormones (estrogen and progesterone)', 'The ovaries release a mature oocyte at ovulation and produce estrogen and progesterone. Implantation and fertilization occur elsewhere in the reproductive tract.'),
('3959507f-d8bd-5bc9-aeac-5fa39ca4d669'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The broad ligament supports the:', '["Only the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the fallopian tubes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the ovaries mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterus, the ovaries, and the fallopian tubes (it is a fold of peritoneum that drapes over these structures)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uterus, the ovaries, and the fallopian tubes (it is a fold of peritoneum that drapes over these structures)', 'The broad ligament is a fold of peritoneum that drapes over and supports the uterus, ovaries, and fallopian tubes together. It does not support only a single one of these structures.'),
('584ea6b4-45a2-57c4-89af-b0acb31dd719'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The round ligament of the uterus helps to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Support the ovary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pull the uterus backward mediated by specific receptor activation","Hold the uterus in an anteverted position (it pulls the uterus forward)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hold the uterus in an anteverted position (it pulls the uterus forward)', 'The round ligament passes from the lateral margin of the uterus toward the inguinal canal and pulls the uterus forward, helping maintain anteversion. It does not pull the uterus backward or support the ovary.'),
('c39119f9-1cb2-5daf-8ba4-4c4faf20f1f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterosacral ligament helps to:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Hold the uterus in an anteverted position (it pulls the cervix backward and upward toward the sacrum)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Pull the uterus forward without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Support the ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hold the uterus in an anteverted position (it pulls the cervix backward and upward toward the sacrum)', 'The uterosacral ligaments run from the cervix to the sacrum and pull the cervix backward and upward, which helps maintain anteversion of the uterus. They do not pull the uterus forward or support the ovary.'),
('5afc06da-2892-5483-b828-1d9b5e66221d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A support of the fallopian tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The most important support of the uterus (it prevents the uterus from descending into the vagina)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A support of the ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most important support of the uterus (it prevents the uterus from descending into the vagina)', 'The cardinal (transverse cervical) ligament at the base of the broad ligament is the principal uterine support, holding the cervix and preventing descent of the uterus into the vagina. It does not support the ovary or the fallopian tube.'),
('4d3c98f6-b146-592c-affd-55a104e51d43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The cardinal ligament is located:', '["On the anterior surface of the uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the base of the broad ligament (from the cervix to the lateral pelvic wall)","At the upper part of the broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the base of the broad ligament (from the cervix to the lateral pelvic wall)', 'The cardinal ligament lies at the base of the broad ligament, extending from the cervix to the lateral pelvic wall. It is not on the anterior surface of the uterus or the upper part of the broad ligament.'),
('3eade99a-2a35-5898-8ccc-32ea1ea832ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pubocervical ligament helps to:', '["Support the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity","Support the uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Support the cervix and the bladder (it is part of the pelvic floor support)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Support the cervix and the bladder (it is part of the pelvic floor support)', 'The pubocervical ligament extends from the cervix to the pubic bone, supporting both the cervix and the bladder as part of the pelvic floor support. Its primary role is not simple vaginal or uterine support.'),
('1efffb6a-4bfc-54d2-b127-aeb39aa6349a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female pelvic floor is composed of:', '["The levator ani and the coccygeus muscles (with their superior and inferior fasciae)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the coccygeus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the levator ani leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The levator ani and the coccygeus muscles (with their superior and inferior fasciae)', 'The pelvic diaphragm consists of the levator ani and coccygeus muscles together with their superior and inferior fasciae. It is not formed by either muscle alone.'),
('08703954-244f-5d29-8623-5ba4851fceaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pelvic floor is important for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Respiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Supporting the pelvic organs (bladder, uterus, and rectum) and maintaining continence","Movement of the lower limb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Supporting the pelvic organs (bladder, uterus, and rectum) and maintaining continence', 'The pelvic floor supports the bladder, uterus, and rectum and contributes to maintaining urinary and fecal continence. Respiration and lower-limb movement are not primary functions of the pelvic floor.'),
('0935e6ba-efe7-5945-a6d1-ce36251bafd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'A weakness of the pelvic floor can cause:', '["Only pelvic organ prolapse resulting in decreased functional activity","Fecal incontinence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only urinary incontinence mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pelvic organ prolapse (cystocele, rectocele, uterine prolapse) and urinary incontinence"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic organ prolapse (cystocele, rectocele, uterine prolapse) and urinary incontinence', 'Pelvic floor weakness allows descent of pelvic organs, producing cystocele, rectocele, or uterine prolapse, and it also causes urinary incontinence. Restricting the answer to a single prolapse type or one incontinence form omits the combined effects.'),
('7d51555f-b181-5029-905b-dbe84269bd7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Kegel exercises are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exercises to strengthen the abdominal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Exercises to strengthen the levator ani muscle (the pelvic floor muscle) - they can help to prevent or treat urinary incontinence and pelvic organ prolapse","Exercises to weaken the pelvic floor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercises to strengthen the levator ani muscle (the pelvic floor muscle) - they can help to prevent or treat urinary incontinence and pelvic organ prolapse', 'Kegel exercises contract and strengthen the levator ani, helping prevent or treat urinary incontinence and pelvic organ prolapse. They do not weaken the pelvic floor and do not primarily target the abdominal muscles.'),
('87c929ad-08a7-5d00-b67e-33f8255f894c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The levator ani is innervated by:', '["The obturator nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the nerve to the levator ani mediated by specific receptor activation","The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and the nerve to the levator ani (S4)","Only the pudendal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and the nerve to the levator ani (S4)', 'The levator ani receives innervation from the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and from the nerve to the levator ani arising at S4. The obturator nerve supplies the adductor compartment, not the pelvic floor.'),
('88d39c8a-298d-5f38-bfca-da1e933465e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The coccygeus muscle is innervated by:', '["The obturator nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The pudendal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The branches of the sacral plexus (S4-S5)"]'::jsonb, 'The branches of the sacral plexus (S4-S5)', 'The coccygeus muscle is innervated by branches of the sacral plexus (S4-S5). The pudendal, obturator, and femoral nerves do not innervate this muscle.'),
('5543386a-06f0-54ca-9d91-10e6a6b285d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The pelvic floor has three openings:', '["Only the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the urethra and the vagina mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The urethra (in the urogenital hiatus), the vagina (in the urogenital hiatus), and the rectum (in the anorectal opening)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urethra (in the urogenital hiatus), the vagina (in the urogenital hiatus), and the rectum (in the anorectal opening)', 'The pelvic floor transmits the urethra and vagina through the urogenital hiatus and the rectum through the anorectal opening. Listing only one or two of these openings is incomplete.'),
('bb4eea88-eb81-50b9-959e-3c319445c134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The urogenital hiatus is:', '["The opening in the anterior part of the pelvic diaphragm (it transmits the urethra and the vagina)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The opening in the posterior part of the pelvic diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening for the rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening in the anterior part of the pelvic diaphragm (it transmits the urethra and the vagina)', 'The urogenital hiatus is the opening in the anterior part of the pelvic diaphragm through which the urethra and vagina pass. The rectum passes through the posterior anorectal opening rather than this hiatus.'),
('ab032290-9d6d-56ac-95bb-028e936477f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The anorectal opening is:', '["The opening in the anterior part resulting in decreased functional activity","The opening for the urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening in the posterior part of the pelvic diaphragm (it transmits the rectum)"]'::jsonb, 'The opening in the posterior part of the pelvic diaphragm (it transmits the rectum)', 'The anorectal opening lies in the posterior part of the pelvic diaphragm and transmits the rectum. It is not the anterior opening and does not transmit the urethra.'),
('9498d4da-a624-555b-9312-b613efb283de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The American Urological Association (AUA) symptom score is used to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Assess the severity of pelvic organ prolapse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Assess the severity of lower urinary tract symptoms (e.g., in BPH)","Assess the severity of male infertility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Assess the severity of lower urinary tract symptoms (e.g., in BPH)', 'The AUA symptom score grades the severity of lower urinary tract symptoms, commonly in benign prostatic hyperplasia. It is not used to assess pelvic organ prolapse or male infertility.'),
('038ef9ed-349a-5c68-8f1a-4b3cb8339e91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The International Continence Society (ICS) defines urinary incontinence as:', '["The involuntary loss of a small volume of urine","All","The involuntary loss of a large volume of urine","None","The complaint of any involuntary leakage of urine"]'::jsonb, 'The complaint of any involuntary leakage of urine', 'The International Continence Society defines urinary incontinence as the complaint of any involuntary leakage of urine, regardless of volume. Restricting the definition to a small or large volume is inaccurate.'),
('ef1879a9-f197-5228-a322-b4e8d0bc39d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female urethra is about:', '["5-6 cm","8-10 cm","3-4 cm long","None","7-8 cm"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm long', 'The female urethra is short, measuring approximately 3-4 cm. The longer ranges given correspond to male urethral length rather than to the female urethra.'),
('f4422d68-3cb9-55d8-89fa-4e0f6424e343'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female urethra is located:', '["In the clitoris resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Embedded in the anterior wall of the vagina (it runs from the bladder neck to the vestibule)","In the perineal body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Embedded in the anterior wall of the vagina (it runs from the bladder neck to the vestibule)', 'The female urethra is embedded in the anterior wall of the vagina, passing from the bladder neck to the vestibule. It is not located in the clitoris or in the perineal body.'),
('e1d97001-1fcf-5d3f-b46e-1795155aa601'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female urethra is lined by:', '["Only transitional epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Stratified squamous epithelium (distally) and transitional epithelium (proximally)","Simple columnar epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only stratified squamous epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified squamous epithelium (distally) and transitional epithelium (proximally)', 'The female urethra is lined proximally by transitional epithelium and distally by stratified squamous epithelium. A single epithelial type does not cover the entire urethral lining.'),
('4ab3c795-726e-5abb-80a8-57ccf6576331'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female urethra is surrounded by:', '["sphincters resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The internal urethral sphincter (at the bladder neck) and the external urethral sphincter (in the urogenital diaphragm)","Only the internal sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the external sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal urethral sphincter (at the bladder neck) and the external urethral sphincter (in the urogenital diaphragm)', 'The female urethra is encircled by the internal urethral sphincter at the bladder neck and by the external urethral sphincter within the urogenital diaphragm. Naming only one of the two sphincters is incomplete.'),
('94e90b0c-1cf9-5f06-8e6b-184af2d96160'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The external urethral sphincter in the female is:', '["A ring of smooth muscle (involuntary) resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ring of the levator ani mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ring of skeletal muscle (voluntary) in the urogenital diaphragm (innervated by the pudendal nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'A ring of skeletal muscle (voluntary) in the urogenital diaphragm (innervated by the pudendal nerve)', 'The external urethral sphincter is a ring of striated (voluntary) skeletal muscle in the urogenital diaphragm, innervated by the pudendal nerve. It is not smooth muscle, and it is not a component of the levator ani.'),
('56177973-84aa-584a-a0ec-b3f18822bee9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female urinary incontinence can be:', '["Stress incontinence (leakage with coughing or sneezing), urge incontinence (leakage with a strong urge), or mixed incontinence","Only stress incontinence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only overflow incontinence without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only urge incontinence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stress incontinence (leakage with coughing or sneezing), urge incontinence (leakage with a strong urge), or mixed incontinence', 'Female urinary incontinence may be stress type (leakage with coughing or sneezing), urge type (leakage with a strong urge), or mixed, combining both patterns. Limiting the answer to a single type omits the other presentations.'),
('55175df4-cc09-5491-8fdd-d50aeff66d54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Stress urinary incontinence (SUI) in the female is:', '["Leakage of urine with a strong urge to void resulting in decreased functional activity","Leakage of urine that cannot be controlled accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Leakage of urine with an increase in intra-abdominal pressure (coughing, sneezing, laughing, or exercise)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leakage of urine with an increase in intra-abdominal pressure (coughing, sneezing, laughing, or exercise)', 'Stress incontinence occurs when a weak urethral sphincter and pelvic floor cannot resist surges in intra-abdominal pressure, so urine leaks during coughing, sneezing, laughing, or exercise. Leakage tied to a strong urge to void instead defines urge incontinence.'),
('adbb66e7-41fe-5838-b167-9e1d5784c440'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Stress urinary incontinence is most commonly caused by:', '["A weakness of the pelvic floor (the levator ani muscle and the urethral sphincter mechanism) - often after childbirth","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A medication side effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A neurologic condition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A weakness of the pelvic floor (the levator ani muscle and the urethral sphincter mechanism) - often after childbirth', 'Vaginal childbirth stretches and weakens the levator ani and urethral sphincter mechanism, which is the leading cause of stress incontinence in women. Medication side effects and neurologic disease produce other types of incontinence rather than the usual stress pattern.'),
('9de12207-d1f6-5f20-8a3b-0c7e7fa69686'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Urge urinary incontinence (UUI) in the female is:', '["Leakage of urine that is preceded by a strong, sudden urge to void (from detrusor overactivity)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Constant leakage of urine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leakage of urine with an increase in intra-abdominal pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leakage of urine that is preceded by a strong, sudden urge to void (from detrusor overactivity)', 'Urge incontinence results from detrusor overactivity, so a sudden, intense urge precedes involuntary urine loss. Cough-induced leakage characterizes stress incontinence, and constant leakage indicates complete loss of sphincter control.'),
('3b4a7d8e-d1cb-5a55-bcf6-15c1eccf343f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Mixed urinary incontinence is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A combination of urge and overflow incontinence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A combination of stress and overflow incontinence mediated by specific receptor activation","A combination of stress and urge incontinence (the patient has both types of symptoms)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A combination of stress and urge incontinence (the patient has both types of symptoms)', 'Mixed incontinence means the patient has both stress (pressure-related) and urge (detrusor-related) symptoms together. Overflow incontinence, which reflects an overdistended bladder, is a separate entity and does not form this combination.'),
('0fcd7590-a060-575f-bf58-0964d9248ce8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Overflow incontinence is:', '["Leakage of urine with a strong urge resulting in decreased functional activity","Leakage of small amounts of urine from a chronically overdistended bladder (from obstruction or an acontractile detrusor)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leakage of urine with an increase in intra-abdominal pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leakage of small amounts of urine from a chronically overdistended bladder (from obstruction or an acontractile detrusor)', 'Overflow incontinence is dribbling of small amounts of urine from a chronically overdistended bladder caused by outlet obstruction or an acontractile detrusor. It differs from stress leakage, which follows raised intra-abdominal pressure, and from urge-related leakage.'),
('01b46d78-f8c3-5869-bd7f-bd4f5652dca4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The treatment for female urinary incontinence depends on:', '["The age of the patient resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the type mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the severity without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The type of incontinence (stress, urge, or mixed) and its severity (it can include Kegel exercises, medications, devices, or surgery)"]'::jsonb, 'The type of incontinence (stress, urge, or mixed) and its severity (it can include Kegel exercises, medications, devices, or surgery)', 'Management is tailored to the type of incontinence (stress, urge, or mixed) and its severity, ranging from pelvic floor exercises through medications and devices to surgery. Considering type alone, severity alone, or age alone leaves out essential factors that determine the plan.'),
('a37dcb8e-2c51-5763-9601-3f1827664a7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'A mid-urethral sling (tension-free vaginal tape) is:', '["A procedure for overflow incontinence resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A surgical procedure for stress urinary incontinence (a sling is placed under the mid-urethra to provide support)","A procedure for urge incontinence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical procedure for stress urinary incontinence (a sling is placed under the mid-urethra to provide support)', 'A mid-urethral sling supports the mid-urethra to restore continence in stress urinary incontinence. It does not treat urge or overflow incontinence, which have different mechanisms.'),
('ef32987a-3b97-57fd-ab1e-1728aa9025d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The most common type of incontinence in women is:', '["Overflow incontinence (rare) resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed incontinence (about 30%) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Stress incontinence (about 50% of all cases of incontinence)","Urge incontinence (about 20%) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stress incontinence (about 50% of all cases of incontinence)', 'Stress incontinence accounts for roughly half of all incontinence in women, making it the most common type. Mixed and urge types occur less frequently, and overflow incontinence is uncommon.'),
('d22cbc9f-5f9b-5984-8859-113a46744a6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive cycle is regulated by:', '["The hypothalamus (GnRH), the pituitary gland (FSH and LH), and the ovaries (estrogen and progesterone)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the ovaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The hypothalamus (GnRH), the pituitary gland (FSH and LH), and the ovaries (estrogen and progesterone)', 'The cycle runs on a coordinated axis: GnRH from the hypothalamus drives pituitary FSH and LH, which stimulate the ovaries to secrete estrogen and progesterone. No single level can regulate the cycle in isolation.'),
('53a36ef4-3f7d-5e00-ad53-0c7f2a348915'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the pituitary:', '["Stimulates ovulation resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates the growth and development of the ovarian follicles (the follicles produce estrogen)","Stimulates the corpus luteum to produce progesterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates the growth and development of the ovarian follicles (the follicles produce estrogen)', 'FSH stimulates growth and maturation of the ovarian follicles, whose granulosa and theca cells then secrete estrogen. Ovulation is triggered by the LH surge, and progesterone is produced by the corpus luteum.'),
('b924c8b2-c8ee-5181-a0c9-4e1679fe49c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Luteinizing hormone (LH) from the pituitary:', '["Triggers ovulation (the release of a mature oocyte from the ovary) and stimulates the corpus luteum to produce progesterone","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulates the growth of the ovarian follicles mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulates the production of estrogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triggers ovulation (the release of a mature oocyte from the ovary) and stimulates the corpus luteum to produce progesterone', 'The mid-cycle LH surge triggers ovulation and then sustains the corpus luteum so it can secrete progesterone. Follicular growth and estrogen production depend on FSH, not LH.'),
('1c955144-1b22-52d1-9297-39c6638de9da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Estrogen is produced by:', '["The corpus luteum resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The growing ovarian follicle (the granulosa cells and the theca cells of the follicle)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The anterior pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The growing ovarian follicle (the granulosa cells and the theca cells of the follicle)', 'The granulosa and theca cells of the developing ovarian follicle are the main source of estrogen. The corpus luteum chiefly secretes progesterone, and the anterior pituitary releases gonadotropins rather than estrogen.'),
('fdaa0872-9573-5fe0-bcce-73302c2134f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Progesterone is produced by:', '["The anterior pituitary","The growing ovarian follicle","All","The corpus luteum (after ovulation)","None"]'::jsonb, 'The corpus luteum (after ovulation)', 'After ovulation the ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone to prepare the endometrium and sustain pregnancy. The anterior pituitary and the growing follicle do not produce progesterone.'),
('cdf44939-8a92-5c3e-9863-1c75d42dbb38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Estrogen is responsible for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The development of the corpus luteum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The development of the secondary sexual characteristics (e.g., breast development, pubic hair) and the proliferation of the endometrium (the proliferative phase)","The secretion of FSH and LH without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The development of the secondary sexual characteristics (e.g., breast development, pubic hair) and the proliferation of the endometrium (the proliferative phase)', 'Estrogen drives the appearance of secondary sexual characteristics and rebuilds the endometrium during the proliferative phase. Corpus luteum formation and pituitary secretion of FSH and LH are not effects of estrogen.'),
('58665fbe-0bf8-5830-bb1c-2d8977bb1117'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'Progesterone is responsible for:', '["The development of the secondary sexual characteristics resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The growth of the ovarian follicle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The secretory transformation of the endometrium (the secretory phase) and the maintenance of a pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'The secretory transformation of the endometrium (the secretory phase) and the maintenance of a pregnancy', 'Progesterone converts the proliferative endometrium into a secretory lining and helps maintain pregnancy. Follicular growth and secondary sexual characteristics are effects of estrogen and FSH, not progesterone.'),
('b21a9984-4eaa-5996-be74-bbaff8816d62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive system is designed for:', '["Only childbirth resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ovulation (the release of an oocyte), fertilization (the union of the sperm and the oocyte), implantation (of the fertilized ovum), the development of the fetus, and childbirth","Only ovulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only fertilization leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation (the release of an oocyte), fertilization (the union of the sperm and the oocyte), implantation (of the fertilized ovum), the development of the fetus, and childbirth', 'The system is organized to achieve the full sequence of ovulation, fertilization, implantation, fetal development, and childbirth. Isolating a single step such as ovulation, fertilization, or delivery does not capture the overall function.'),
('1fbed284-8bee-5795-b213-df29aed8d0b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The external genitalia of the female (the vulva) is homologous to:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The perineal body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The internal genitalia without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external genitalia of the male (the penis and the scrotum) - they are derived from the same embryologic structures"]'::jsonb, 'The external genitalia of the male (the penis and the scrotum) - they are derived from the same embryologic structures', 'The vulva and the male external genitalia (penis and scrotum) develop from the same embryologic structures, namely the genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and genital swellings. The perineal body and internal genitalia are not homologues of the external genitalia.'),
('d369cc3b-aff8-50c2-96c6-f8077e991a22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The clitoris is homologous to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The penis (both are derived from the genital tubercle)","The scrotum mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The testes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The penis (both are derived from the genital tubercle)', 'The clitoris and the penis both develop from the genital tubercle. The scrotum arises from the genital swellings and the testes from the indifferent gonadal ridge, so neither is the homologue of the clitoris.'),
('3d8627e5-06f6-53ad-8a37-378810286cd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The labia majora are homologous to:', '["The testes resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The penis mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The scrotum (both are derived from the genital swellings)"]'::jsonb, 'The scrotum (both are derived from the genital swellings)', 'The labia majora and the scrotum both develop from the labioscrotal (genital) swellings. The penis arises from the genital tubercle, and the testes develop from the gonadal ridge.'),
('3a9e55f6-e672-5372-9f74-88bb711e3676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The labia minora are homologous to:', '["The ventral aspect of the penis (the urethral folds fail to fuse, forming the labia minora)","The scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The clitoris mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ventral aspect of the penis (the urethral folds fail to fuse, forming the labia minora)', 'The labia minora and the ventral aspect of the penis both develop from the urogenital folds; in the female these folds remain unfused. The scrotum arises from the genital swellings and the clitoris from the genital tubercle.'),
('d00b8742-07a4-5f9c-a277-3ee457752eba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vestibular bulbs are homologous to:', '["The bulb of the penis (both are derived from the urogenital sinus)","The corpora spongiosum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The corpus cavernosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulb of the penis (both are derived from the urogenital sinus)', 'The vestibular bulbs and the bulb of the penis both develop from the urogenital sinus. The corpora spongiosum and corpora cavernosa of the penis are not the homologues of the vestibular bulbs.'),
('50ad5af4-ab56-5720-a383-5644c53bd72c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The Bartholin glands (greater vestibular glands) are homologous to:', '["The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands in the male (both are derived from the urogenital sinus)","The seminal vesicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The prostate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands in the male (both are derived from the urogenital sinus)', 'The greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands and the bulbourethral (Cowper) glands both develop from the urogenital sinus. The prostate and seminal vesicles arise from other structures and are not their homologues.'),
('4ff13f95-4a3d-5c29-8305-f966e55e6271'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovaries are homologous to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The testes (both are derived from the genital ridge)","The seminal vesicles mediated by specific receptor activation","The prostate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The testes (both are derived from the genital ridge)', 'The ovaries and the testes both develop from the indifferent gonadal ridge, making them homologous. The prostate and seminal vesicles arise from the urogenital sinus and mesonephric duct respectively.'),
('aced85e0-7476-5b87-9a8c-489f5e25f86b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterine (fallopian) tubes are homologous to:', '["The epididymis (both are derived from the paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct)","The vas deferens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The seminal vesicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The epididymis (both are derived from the paramesonephric (Mullerian) duct)', 'The uterine tubes are conventionally regarded as the female homologue of the epididymis. The vas deferens and seminal vesicles are derivatives of the mesonephric duct and are not homologues of the uterine tubes.'),
('76311f67-2495-5842-9617-61f6c8b44b76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is homologous to:', '["The prostatic utricle (the male homologue of the uterus and the upper vagina)","The seminal vesicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic utricle (the male homologue of the uterus and the upper vagina)', 'The prostatic utricle is the vestigial male remnant of the paramesonephric ducts and is the homologue of the uterus and upper vagina. The prostate and seminal vesicles develop from entirely different structures.'),
('a4910dbc-1c67-51fb-ac84-143a493e034a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The vagina is homologous to:', '["The scrotum resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The penis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The prostatic utricle and the upper part of the vagina are homologous to the seminal colliculus in the male","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic utricle and the upper part of the vagina are homologous to the seminal colliculus in the male', 'The upper part of the vagina develops from the paramesonephric ducts, whose male vestige is the prostatic utricle opening on the seminal colliculus. The scrotum and penis arise from the genital swellings and tubercle, respectively.'),
('38acd913-ea73-57be-825c-48d6ca2be585'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female pelvis is adapted for:', '["Childbirth (it is wider and shallower than the male pelvis, with a rounder inlet and a wider outlet)","Standing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Running mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Childbirth (it is wider and shallower than the male pelvis, with a rounder inlet and a wider outlet)', 'A wider, shallower cavity with a rounder inlet and wider outlet eases passage of the fetus at delivery. Standing and running are not specific adaptations that distinguish the female pelvis.'),
('ad2322bc-699e-5a40-b278-7fea6805befc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female pelvis is more prone to:', '["Fracture of the pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvic organ prolapse (due to the larger pelvic inlet and the weaker pelvic floor compared to the male)","Infection of the pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic organ prolapse (due to the larger pelvic inlet and the weaker pelvic floor compared to the male)', 'The larger inlet, wider outlet, and relatively weaker pelvic floor predispose women to pelvic organ prolapse. Fracture and infection are not characteristic vulnerabilities that distinguish the female from the male pelvis.'),
('1941e558-9d00-5a60-8c36-7370be50b4b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is supported by:', '["The levator ani (pelvic floor) and the cardinal, uterosacral, round, and pubocervical ligaments","Only the round ligaments accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the cardinal ligaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The levator ani (pelvic floor) and the cardinal, uterosacral, round, and pubocervical ligaments', 'The uterus is held in place by the pelvic floor (levator ani) together with several ligaments, chiefly the cardinal, uterosacral, round, and pubocervical ligaments acting together. No single ligament or muscle alone provides adequate support.'),
('042554f0-3be7-5d02-b23e-3581439ddb9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The levator ani (pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus, and ischiococcygeus) is:', '["A muscle of the lower limb resulting in decreased functional activity","The main muscle of the pelvic floor (it supports the pelvic organs and helps to maintain continence)","A muscle of the gluteal region mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main muscle of the pelvic floor (it supports the pelvic organs and helps to maintain continence)', 'The levator ani, composed of the pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus, and ischiococcygeus, forms the main muscle of the pelvic floor and supports pelvic organs while aiding urinary and fecal continence. It is not a muscle of the lower limb or gluteal region.'),
('7ea7185a-2de7-51c2-9fa1-c40f7bf76f01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female perineal body is:', '["A fibromuscular nodule in the midline of the perineum (between the anus and the vagina) that is an attachment for the perineal muscles","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A large, solid structure mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular nodule in the midline of the perineum (between the anus and the vagina) that is an attachment for the perineal muscles', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular nodule lying in the midline between the anus and the vagina that anchors the perineal muscles. It is neither a gland nor a large solid structure.'),
('9b47e3ca-426e-5a94-94ec-74b443389a55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female perineal body is susceptible to:', '["Cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma during childbirth (a perineal tear or an episiotomy)"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma during childbirth (a perineal tear or an episiotomy)', 'During vaginal delivery the distending perineum commonly tears or is deliberately incised, so the perineal body is most vulnerable to childbirth trauma. Neither cancer nor infection is the characteristic hazard of the perineal body.'),
('6a86e9aa-5f2b-589d-a289-055bc788f6ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female perineal body is an important landmark for:', '["Episiotomy (a surgical incision through the perineal body to enlarge the vaginal opening)","Cesarean section accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Hysterectomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Episiotomy (a surgical incision through the perineal body to enlarge the vaginal opening)', 'Episiotomy is a surgical incision through the perineal body that enlarges the vaginal outlet at delivery. Cesarean section and hysterectomy do not involve the perineal body.'),
('9ea07210-aabd-5bd3-bb89-08318cb52eff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive system is about:', '["The production of ova, fertilization, and the development of the fetus","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The production of sperm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The production of urine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The production of ova, fertilization, and the development of the fetus', 'The female reproductive system produces ova, supports fertilization, and nurtures fetal development. Sperm production belongs to the male reproductive system, and urine production to the urinary system.'),
('47244959-1682-5c2d-9f5b-b74c9f602132'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive system also produces:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Urine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hormones (estrogen and progesterone) that regulate the menstrual cycle and the secondary sexual characteristics","Sperm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hormones (estrogen and progesterone) that regulate the menstrual cycle and the secondary sexual characteristics', 'In addition to ova, the ovaries secrete estrogen and progesterone, which regulate the menstrual cycle and maintain secondary sexual characteristics. The system does not produce urine or sperm.'),
('56178053-3bcf-5c8f-b048-4d62cbcd55b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive system has a dual blood supply:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the uterine artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ovarian artery (from the aorta) supplies the ovary, the fallopian tube, and the upper part of the uterus; the uterine artery (from the internal iliac) supplies the uterus, the cervix, and the upper vagina","Only the vaginal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the ovarian artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ovarian artery (from the aorta) supplies the ovary, the fallopian tube, and the upper part of the uterus; the uterine artery (from the internal iliac) supplies the uterus, the cervix, and the upper vagina', 'The ovarian artery arises from the aorta and supplies the ovary, fallopian tube, and upper uterus, while the uterine artery from the internal iliac supplies the uterus, cervix, and upper vagina. Neither vessel alone accounts for the whole blood supply.'),
('a85ce168-daa4-5c49-bb78-e109c21151a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The fallopian tubes are innervated by:', '["Only the parasympathetic nerves resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the sympathetic nerves mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The pudendal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sympathetic nerves (from the celiac or hypogastric plexus) and the parasympathetic nerves (from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)"]'::jsonb, 'The sympathetic nerves (from the celiac or hypogastric plexus) and the parasympathetic nerves (from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)', 'The uterine tubes receive sympathetic fibers from the hypogastric (and celiac) plexuses and parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4). Neither autonomic division alone, nor the pudendal nerve, supplies them.'),
('e840b95e-5111-5581-b840-e7e4626e2258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is innervated by:', '["Only the sympathetic nerves resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The sympathetic nerves (from the hypogastric plexus) and the parasympathetic nerves (from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The pudendal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the parasympathetic nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sympathetic nerves (from the hypogastric plexus) and the parasympathetic nerves (from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)', 'The uterus is innervated by sympathetic fibers from the hypogastric plexus and parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4). The pudendal nerve innervates the perineum rather than the uterus.'),
('bfc1a4a8-0cab-57a5-9d93-47cde4e77033'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The external genitalia (vulva) are innervated by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the pudendal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the genitofemoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the ilioinguinal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pudendal nerve (the dorsal nerve of the clitoris and the perineal nerve), the ilioinguinal nerve, and the genitofemoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve (the dorsal nerve of the clitoris and the perineal nerve), the ilioinguinal nerve, and the genitofemoral nerve', 'The vulva receives sensory and motor supply from the pudendal nerve (dorsal nerve of the clitoris and perineal nerve), the ilioinguinal nerve, and the genitofemoral nerve. No single one of these nerves supplies the entire vulva.'),
('cd71f989-7479-56af-900c-8f25043c55d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive system is susceptible to:', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infections (e.g., pelvic inflammatory disease, sexually transmitted infections, and urinary tract infections)"]'::jsonb, 'Infections (e.g., pelvic inflammatory disease, sexually transmitted infections, and urinary tract infections)', 'Because the lower tract opens to the exterior, microbes can ascend through the cervix, so infections such as pelvic inflammatory disease, STIs, and UTIs are the characteristic threat. Cancer and trauma also occur but are not the specific susceptibility being tested.'),
('d3eabd28-313c-5115-bffc-bf1704b5440a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The female reproductive system is regulated by the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis (GnRH from the hypothalamus, FSH and LH from the pituitary, and estrogen and progesterone from the ovaries)","Only the pituitary without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the ovaries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis (GnRH from the hypothalamus, FSH and LH from the pituitary, and estrogen and progesterone from the ovaries)', 'Regulation depends on the full hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis: GnRH from the hypothalamus controls pituitary FSH and LH, which drive ovarian estrogen and progesterone. A single component such as the hypothalamus, pituitary, or ovaries alone is insufficient.'),
('fc28b5a4-520e-5bd8-b1ff-2ec144df6397'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The menstrual cycle is a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Complicated, well-regulated process that prepares the body for pregnancy (if pregnancy does not occur, the endometrium is shed as the menstrual period)","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A simple process without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A short process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complicated, well-regulated process that prepares the body for pregnancy (if pregnancy does not occur, the endometrium is shed as the menstrual period)', 'The menstrual cycle is an intricate, tightly regulated sequence that readies the endometrium for pregnancy and sheds it as menstruation when fertilization does not occur. It is neither a simple nor a short process.'),
('638b719d-8fbf-5ef8-9645-aa4d04468b52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'In the study of Female Reproductive Organs, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('b396499a-ed58-569e-b055-0704dd194966'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The normal position of the uterus is:', '["Retroverted resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laterally deviated mediated by specific receptor activation","Midposition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anteverted (upper vagina axis) and anteflexed (cervix-body angle)"]'::jsonb, 'Anteverted (upper vagina axis) and anteflexed (cervix-body angle)', 'Normally the uterus is anteverted relative to the axis of the upper vagina and anteflexed at the cervix-body junction. Retroversion, lateral deviation, and a midposition do not describe the usual orientation.'),
('a3c5493f-f2b8-5ca0-89ac-ab5b70e6e74c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The supports of the uterus include:', '["Only broad ligament resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani), perineal body, transverse cervical (cardinal/Mackenrodt) ligaments, pubocervical ligaments, uterosacral ligaments, round ligaments","Only round ligaments mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pelvic diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani), perineal body, transverse cervical (cardinal/Mackenrodt) ligaments, pubocervical ligaments, uterosacral ligaments, round ligaments', 'The uterus is supported by the pelvic diaphragm, perineal body, and the cardinal, pubocervical, uterosacral, and round ligaments working together. The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold, not a true support, and no single structure acts alone.'),
('a5be38a7-7346-56ef-9e65-b6fa61fe89fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The blood supply of the uterus is mainly from:', '["Femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterine artery (branch of internal iliac artery)","Ovarian artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Vaginal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uterine artery (branch of internal iliac artery)', 'The uterine artery, a branch of the internal iliac artery, is the principal arterial supply of the uterus. The ovarian artery mainly supplies the ovary and adnexa, while the femoral and vaginal arteries are not primary uterine sources.'),
('6c362b64-6d5e-5425-9a23-7345a5e472dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is related anteriorly to:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Small intestine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder and uterovesical pouch of peritoneum","Sigmoid colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder and uterovesical pouch of peritoneum', 'Anteriorly the uterus is related to the bladder, with the uterovesical pouch of peritoneum interposed. The rectum and rectouterine pouch lie posteriorly, and the sigmoid colon and small intestine are not anterior relations of the uterus.'),
('5b14744e-d385-52a5-a4d2-ff96f987f37f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The uterus is related posteriorly to:', '["Rectum and rectouterine pouch (of Douglas)","Sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum and rectouterine pouch (of Douglas)', 'Behind the uterus lie the rectum and the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas), a peritoneal recess of clinical importance. The bladder lies anteriorly, and the sacrum and pubic symphysis are not direct posterior relations.'),
('4b702ecd-f496-5dfe-8d75-dfa83fc3dd91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is attached to the uterus by:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Ovarian ligament (to the uterine cornu)","Broad ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Suspensory ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Round ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian ligament (to the uterine cornu)', 'The ovarian ligament connects the ovary to the uterine cornu. The suspensory ligament attaches the ovary to the pelvic wall, while the broad and round ligaments perform different anchoring roles.'),
('0a53a080-79cf-5580-8770-774b3748a0d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Female Reproductive Organs', 'The ovary is attached to the pelvic wall by:', '["Ovarian ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Round ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","Broad ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suspensory ligament of the ovary (contains ovarian vessels and nerves)"]'::jsonb, 'Suspensory ligament of the ovary (contains ovarian vessels and nerves)', 'The suspensory ligament of the ovary, which carries the ovarian vessels and nerves, attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall. The ovarian ligament anchors the ovary to the uterine cornu instead.'),
('0d8c5b92-d42b-5b45-8638-7ea9fd9801d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis is located in the?', '["Scrotum (outside the body cavity for temperature regulation)","Abdominal cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Pelvic cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scrotum (outside the body cavity for temperature regulation)', 'The testis lies in the scrotum outside the abdominal cavity, keeping its temperature about 2-3 degrees Celsius below core temperature for spermatogenesis. The abdominal and pelvic cavities would expose it to too high a temperature, and the inguinal canal is only a passage during descent.'),
('65df7680-9d8b-590e-a2d0-eecc60549930'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis is covered by which layer from outside to inside?', '["Skin, dartos, cremasteric, internal spermatic, external spermatic resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin, internal spermatic, cremasteric, external spermatic, tunica vaginalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin, dartos, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica vaginalis (parietal and visceral), tunica albuginea"]'::jsonb, 'Skin, dartos, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica vaginalis (parietal and visceral), tunica albuginea', 'The coverings are derived from the layers of the anterior abdominal wall traversed during descent. From superficial to deep they run skin, dartos, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica vaginalis (parietal and visceral), and tunica albuginea; the shorter options omit or misorder layers.'),
('71edcd7e-76c5-524e-8c79-200a5f671f7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testicular descent into the scrotum normally occurs?', '["During the first year of life resulting in decreased functional activity","During the 3rd month accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At birth mediated by specific receptor activation","At puberty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","During the 7th-8th month of fetal development"]'::jsonb, 'During the 7th-8th month of fetal development', 'The testes normally migrate from the abdomen through the inguinal canal into the scrotum during the seventh to eighth month of fetal life. Descent in the first year, at birth, or at puberty is unusual and represents delayed or absent descent.'),
('f93d72e2-bb6f-59af-b3c0-7c9d11f2e3c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The vas deferens is the continuation of?', '["The seminal vesicle","The rete testis","The seminiferous tubules","The efferent ductules","The tail of the epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'The tail of the epididymis', 'The vas deferens arises as the direct continuation of the tail (cauda) of the epididymis and ascends within the spermatic cord to the pelvis. The rete testis, seminiferous tubules, and efferent ductules lie proximal to the epididymis, and the seminal vesicle joins the vas later.'),
('8e8e6409-4347-560c-a7ed-d5e7130230ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The vas deferens ends by joining?', '["The bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The urethra directly mediated by specific receptor activation","The duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct","The epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct', 'The ampulla of the vas deferens unites with the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct, which opens into the prostatic urethra. It does not open directly into the bladder, prostate, or urethra.'),
('bedc5053-bcc6-52ea-a0f3-1fe0be7453c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminal vesicles lie?', '["In the scrotum","Posterior to the bladder, above the prostate","Anterior to the bladder","In the inguinal canal","Within the prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the bladder, above the prostate', 'The seminal vesicles are paired pelvic organs lying posterior to the bladder and superior (lateral) to the prostate, between bladder and rectum. They are not located in the scrotum, inguinal canal, or within the prostate.'),
('a4154d7f-25cf-54c4-967b-46dad82d024d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminal vesicles produce?', '["Most of the seminal fluid (fructose-rich, alkaline fluid)","Spermatozoa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testosterone mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prostatic fluid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most of the seminal fluid (fructose-rich, alkaline fluid)', 'The seminal vesicles contribute the largest volume of semen, secreting a fructose-rich, alkaline fluid that nourishes the spermatozoa. Spermatozoa and testosterone are produced by the testis, while prostatic fluid comes from the prostate.'),
('f48c2b27-3aea-50f7-86db-f21caa5ffacb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate gland has which zones?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Only lateral and medial lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral, central, transitional, and anterior fibromuscular zones","Only the isthmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only peripheral and central zones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral, central, transitional, and anterior fibromuscular zones', 'The prostate is divided into the peripheral, central, transitional, and anterior fibromuscular zones. Most adenocarcinomas arise in the peripheral zone and benign hyperplasia in the transitional zone, so the zone-based description is the accepted one.'),
('482f096a-6a15-54c8-ae31-73fa9bb644a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is related anteriorly to?', '["Urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic symphysis and retropubic space","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis and retropubic space', 'The anterior surface of the prostate faces the retropubic space and the pubic symphysis. The rectum is the posterior relation, the bladder neck is superior, and the urethra passes through the gland rather than lying on it.'),
('c6a9760b-6f20-5f34-babe-7f610e58c61a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is related posteriorly to?', '["Bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectum (separated by rectovesical septum/Denonvilliers fascia)","Pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum (separated by rectovesical septum/Denonvilliers fascia)', 'Posteriorly the prostate is related to the rectum, from which it is separated by the rectovesical septum (Denonvilliers fascia); this relationship allows digital rectal palpation. The bladder and pubic symphysis are anterior/superior relations, not posterior ones.'),
('0d32d7a1-797f-51a0-97a8-e024c4ef6abf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is related superiorly to?', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic symphysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Bladder neck and seminal vesicles","Urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder neck and seminal vesicles', 'The base of the prostate is continuous with the bladder neck and is related to the seminal vesicles above it. The rectum lies posterior, the pubic symphysis anterior, and the urethra simply traverses the gland.'),
('cc208146-4d9c-5a75-9877-2e07d4e863c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is related inferiorly to?', '["Bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubic symphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urogenital diaphragm (external urethral sphincter area)"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital diaphragm (external urethral sphincter area)', 'The apex of the prostate rests on the urogenital diaphragm near the external urethral sphincter, explaining sphincter vulnerability during prostate surgery. The bladder is superior and the rectum posterior, so neither is the inferior relation.'),
('11f3a93c-4639-53bf-aa57-4ec377b12d5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostatic urethra is?', '["The part of the male urethra that passes through the prostate gland","The longest part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The part in the perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","The part in the penis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the male urethra that passes through the prostate gland', 'The prostatic urethra is the fixed segment that traverses the prostate gland from the bladder neck to the urogenital diaphragm and receives the ejaculatory ducts. The spongy urethra is the longest part and the membranous urethra lies in the perineum.'),
('6ac3712d-5059-5fcd-aa2a-12baa8b9a1be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The ejaculatory duct opens into?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Penile urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Membranous urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","Bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prostatic urethra (at the seminal colliculus/verumontanum)"]'::jsonb, 'Prostatic urethra (at the seminal colliculus/verumontanum)', 'The paired ejaculatory ducts open into the prostatic urethra at the seminal colliculus (verumontanum), which is why semen is delivered into this segment. The membranous and penile urethrae lie distal to the prostate, so they cannot be the opening site.'),
('c7319a01-28a4-5ae2-8e44-8d5bba41b0b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis consists of?', '["Three corpora cavernosa resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two corpora spongiosa and one corpus cavernosum mediated by specific receptor activation","Two corpora cavernosa (dorsal) and one corpus spongiosum (ventral)","Only one corpus cavernosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two corpora cavernosa (dorsal) and one corpus spongiosum (ventral)', 'The penis is formed by two dorsal corpora cavernosa and one ventral corpus spongiosum, which surrounds the spongy urethra and expands as the glans. Any arrangement with three cavernosa, reversed numbers, or a single body is incorrect.'),
('2a4d62bb-908f-504a-bc9f-e04c46ea7c7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The crura of the penis are?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior attachments of the corpora cavernosa to the ischiopubic rami","The base of the glans mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin covering the penis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The attachment of the corpus spongiosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior attachments of the corpora cavernosa to the ischiopubic rami', 'Each crus is the posterior anchoring of a corpus cavernosum, fixed to the ischiopubic ramus and covered by ischiocavernosus. The bulb, not the crura, is the posterior expansion of the corpus spongiosum, and the glans is its distal end.'),
('6e775f60-c33a-5e6b-93f2-fce85148173e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulb of the penis is?', '["The posterior expansion of the corpus spongiosum (covered by bulbospongiosus)","The prepuce accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The root of the penis mediated by specific receptor activation","The glans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior expansion of the corpus spongiosum (covered by bulbospongiosus)', 'The bulb is the expanded proximal end of the corpus spongiosum, lying in the superficial perineal pouch and covered by the bulbospongiosus muscle. The glans is the distal expansion of the same body, and the prepuce is the overlying foreskin.'),
('7fb6797b-3cc1-5ab9-a604-5b1934746c68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is a pouch of skin containing?', '["Only the epididymides resulting in decreased functional activity","Testes, epididymides, and lower parts of spermatic cords","The prostate mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the testes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testes, epididymides, and lower parts of spermatic cords', 'The scrotum is a cutaneous pouch that holds the testes, epididymides, and the lower parts of the spermatic cords, which hang outside the body cavity. Listing only testes or only epididymides is incomplete, and the prostate is a deep pelvic organ.'),
('0eb74b02-62df-5905-9244-8fa11954e739'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dartos muscle of the scrotum is?', '["A ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","A layer of skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Striated muscle for testicular elevation mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle causing scrotal wrinkling (temperature regulation)","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle causing scrotal wrinkling (temperature regulation)', 'The dartos is smooth muscle in the scrotal subcutaneous tissue that contracts to wrinkle the scrotum and regulate local temperature. The cremaster is the striated muscle that elevates the testis, and the dartos itself is neither skin nor ligament.'),
('9dd3ac28-8082-5bc2-8d75-14133ffe7437'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord contents include?', '["Only nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","Vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, nerves (genital branch of genitofemoral)","Only testicular artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, nerves (genital branch of genitofemoral)', 'The spermatic cord transmits the vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, and nerves including the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve. It carries all of these structures together, so options listing a single component are incomplete.'),
('d0e11fd2-01b8-5966-b0f0-70a0ca062f91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis has which covering immediately outside the tunica vaginalis?', '["Internal spermatic fascia","Cremasteric fascia","External spermatic fascia","Tunica albuginea","Skin"]'::jsonb, 'Internal spermatic fascia', 'Deepest of the three spermatic fascia layers is the internal spermatic fascia, which lies immediately outside the tunica vaginalis of the testis. The cremasteric and external spermatic fasciae are more superficial, and the tunica albuginea lies within (deep to) the tunica vaginalis.'),
('e4b32e73-84ef-5307-8815-e69d83e738fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The epididymis is divided into?', '["Body only","Anterior and posterior parts","Head (caput), body (corpus), tail (cauda)","Head and tail only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Head (caput), body (corpus), tail (cauda)', 'The epididymis has three parts: the head (caput), body (corpus), and tail (cauda), which continues as the vas deferens. Spermatozoa mature and are stored as they travel through these segments, so dividing it into only two parts is incomplete.'),
('6a3cdeed-fd12-5809-831a-348abb8622c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis is supplied by which artery?', '["Testicular artery (from abdominal aorta at L2)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cremasteric artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Artery of vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular artery (from abdominal aorta at L2)', 'Because the testis develops in the abdomen, it retains its arterial supply from the abdominal aorta near L2 via the testicular artery. The cremasteric artery and artery of the vas deferens are only small collateral branches.'),
('df1925d8-e2c7-571d-8cc6-15da6abf4ec8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The venous drainage of the testis is via?', '["None of the above","All of the above","Cremasteric vein","Testicular vein directly","Pampiniform plexus to testicular vein"]'::jsonb, 'Pampiniform plexus to testicular vein', 'The testicular veins arise as the pampiniform plexus within the spermatic cord and converge into a single testicular vein (draining to the IVC on the right and the renal vein on the left). The cremasteric vein is a separate, minor channel.'),
('78854791-8000-515d-a0cd-cce3b0d058cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The lymphatic drainage of the testis is to?', '["Internal iliac nodes","None of the above","Deep inguinal nodes","Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes at L2","Superficial inguinal nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Para-aortic (lumbar) nodes at L2', 'Since the testis originates in the abdomen, its lymphatics follow the testicular vessels to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes at L2 rather than to inguinal nodes. This differs from the scrotal skin, which drains to the superficial inguinal nodes.'),
('a8b0bd12-56ff-5b87-ae4e-57585aff2a7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The lymphatic drainage of the scrotal skin is to?', '["Internal iliac nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes","Para-aortic nodes","Axillary nodes","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial inguinal nodes', 'The scrotal skin develops from the abdominal wall, so its lymphatics drain to the superficial inguinal nodes, in contrast to the testis itself, which drains to para-aortic nodes. Axillary and internal iliac nodes are not on the drainage path of scrotal skin.'),
('be02995a-5868-5785-b21f-419fac31dbed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The nerve supply of the testis (pain sensation) is via?', '["Pudendal nerve","Sympathetic fibers (testicular plexus)","Genitofemoral nerve","None of the above","Ilioinguinal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic fibers (testicular plexus)', 'Visceral afferent pain fibers from the testis travel with the sympathetic fibers of the testicular plexus to segments T10-11. Somatic nerves such as the pudendal, genitofemoral, and ilioinguinal nerves serve superficial scrotal or cremasteric structures rather than the testis itself.'),
('b291a442-5584-5998-b338-f77ca0b8bc50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is supplied by which arteries?', '["Femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior vesical artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior vesical, middle rectal, internal pudendal arteries","Testicular artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior vesical, middle rectal, internal pudendal arteries', 'The prostate receives its blood supply from the inferior vesical, middle rectal, and internal pudendal arteries, all branches related to the internal iliac system. The superior vesical artery mainly supplies the bladder and is not a named prostatic supply.'),
('a5af5fe3-5183-5f8f-bdde-e4c1c6277cd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostatic venous plexus drains into?', '["IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal iliac veins (and communicates with vertebral venous plexus)","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal iliac veins (and communicates with vertebral venous plexus)', 'The prostatic venous plexus drains into the internal iliac veins and communicates freely with the vertebral venous plexus, explaining the predilection of prostate cancer to spread to pelvic bones and vertebrae. The portal and renal veins are not involved.'),
('5978e6c6-1e57-59d8-aace-4df95819f474'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminal vesicle duct joins the vas deferens to form?', '["None of the above","Seminal colliculus","Prostatic urethra","Epididymis","Ejaculatory duct"]'::jsonb, 'Ejaculatory duct', 'The duct of the seminal vesicle joins the ampulla of the vas deferens to form the ejaculatory duct, which then opens into the prostatic urethra. The seminal colliculus is the mucosal projection bearing the opening, and the epididymis lies far proximal.'),
('33cd6d5e-7c71-59ce-a846-0758605e6b99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The ejaculatory duct pierces the?', '["Posterior part of the prostate to open into the prostatic urethra","Membranous urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urogenital diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior part of the prostate to open into the prostatic urethra', 'Each ejaculatory duct descends obliquely through the posterior part of the prostate and opens into the prostatic urethra at the seminal colliculus. It does not pierce the bladder, the urogenital diaphragm, or reach the membranous urethra.'),
('6f8fc8cc-82c3-5247-9c2e-627ea3d1ed87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulbourethral glands (Cowper glands) are located?', '["In the seminal vesicles resulting in decreased functional activity","In the prostate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the deep perineal pouch (urogenital diaphragm)","In the bulb of the penis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the deep perineal pouch (urogenital diaphragm)', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands lie within the deep perineal pouch on either side of the membranous urethra. They are separate from the prostate and seminal vesicles, and they discharge into the proximal spongy urethra rather than lying inside the penile bulb.'),
('64941e0e-ce08-5bc1-ad7c-3e3b9be2a398'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulbourethral glands open into?', '["The proximal part of the spongy (penile) urethra","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The membranous urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","The bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The prostatic urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal part of the spongy (penile) urethra', 'The ducts of the bulbourethral glands pierce the perineal membrane and open into the proximal (bulbous) part of the spongy urethra, where their mucus lubricates the lumen. The membranous and prostatic urethrae and the bladder lie proximal and are not the openings.'),
('c51dc614-904f-5661-8bcd-e02734ee948a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The pampiniform plexus of veins functions to?', '["Support the testis resulting in decreased functional activity","Drain the testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Cool the arterial blood before it reaches the testis (countercurrent heat exchange)","Nourish the testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cool the arterial blood before it reaches the testis (countercurrent heat exchange)', 'The pampiniform plexus surrounds the testicular artery and acts as a countercurrent heat exchanger that cools arterial blood before it reaches the testis, maintaining a temperature suitable for spermatogenesis. Drainage, nourishment, and support describe other structures or functions, not this plexus.'),
('ee9a05df-6526-5e45-b399-1472b3485135'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Cryptorchidism is associated with?', '["significant issues resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrocele accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Varicocele mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased risk of testicular cancer (seminoma) and infertility"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of testicular cancer (seminoma) and infertility', 'An undescended testis (cryptorchidism) carries an increased risk of seminoma and infertility, particularly if untreated. Hydrocele and varicocele are separate conditions and are not the primary associations.'),
('95c6a693-f0ef-5cea-9fd5-a5eb83c4cede'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The processus vaginalis normally obliterates to form?', '["A fibrous remnant (tunica vaginalis testis is its unobliterated part)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gubernaculum mediated by specific receptor activation","The epididymis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous remnant (tunica vaginalis testis is its unobliterated part)', 'The processus vaginalis normally closes during fetal life leaving a fibrous remnant, while its distal portion persists as the tunica vaginalis around the testis. Failure of obliteration predisposes to an indirect inguinal hernia or hydrocele; the gubernaculum and epididymis develop independently.'),
('199fde7b-2406-5c97-831e-95156ae2b113'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Regarding male reproductive organs:', '["The epididymis has head, body, and tail","The testis is supplied by the testicular artery from the aorta","The vas deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct","The prostate has four zones","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Every statement is anatomically accurate: the epididymis has head, body, and tail; the testicular artery arises from the aorta; the vas deferens joins the seminal vesicle duct to form the ejaculatory duct; and the prostate has four zones. Because all four are true, the combined choice is demanded.'),
('c93b0829-6c7b-56af-8e82-de8c0ee400e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Where is the testis located?', '["Abdominal cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","Inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","In the scrotum (for temperature regulation)","Pelvic cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the scrotum (for temperature regulation)', 'The testis lies in the scrotum, outside the body cavity, where the temperature is maintained a few degrees below core to allow spermatogenesis. The abdominal and pelvic cavities are too warm, and the inguinal canal is only a route during descent.'),
('080e13b4-c5ea-525c-b614-8f78aa481600'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'What are the coverings of the testis from outside in?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin, external spermatic, internal spermatic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Skin, dartos, external spermatic, cremasteric, internal spermatic, tunica vaginalis, tunica albuginea","Skin, dartos, cremasteric, tunica vaginalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skin, dartos, external spermatic, cremasteric, internal spermatic, tunica vaginalis, tunica albuginea', 'From outside inward the testis coverings are skin, dartos, external spermatic, cremasteric, and internal spermatic fasciae, tunica vaginalis, and tunica albuginea. These layers are derived from the abdominal wall during descent; the shorter options omit several layers.'),
('2278e1e5-06eb-5d86-9486-7d4c72124e94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'What is the function of the pampiniform plexus?', '["Nourish the testis resulting in decreased functional activity","Drain the testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Support the testis mediated by specific receptor activation","Cool arterial blood before it reaches the testis","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cool arterial blood before it reaches the testis', 'The pampiniform plexus forms a countercurrent heat exchanger around the testicular artery, cooling the arterial blood before it reaches the testis so that spermatogenesis occurs at a lower temperature. Nourishment, support, and simple drainage do not describe this plexus.'),
('0ef66c02-e49a-509e-adb0-57e89e96cac8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'What are the zones of the prostate?', '["Peripheral, central, transitional, anterior fibromuscular","Lateral and medial only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral and central only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only isthmus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral, central, transitional, anterior fibromuscular', 'The prostate is zoned into peripheral, central, transitional, and anterior fibromuscular regions. The peripheral zone is the usual site of carcinoma and the transitional zone of benign hyperplasia, so zone-based descriptions are preferred over the old lobe concept.'),
('2271e91b-9d3c-58a2-af8d-cdabb4682d6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'What does the vas deferens join to form the ejaculatory duct?', '["Duct of the seminal vesicle","None","Bulbourethral duct","Prostatic ducts","Epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'Duct of the seminal vesicle', 'The ampulla of the vas deferens joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct, which opens into the prostatic urethra. Prostatic and bulbourethral ducts empty into the urethra separately and the epididymis lies far proximal.'),
('33e66a5e-ea58-56f6-9531-dde30e6faf8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male reproductive system includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the testes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the prostate mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the penis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The testes, epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate, bulbourethral glands, and penis"]'::jsonb, 'The testes, epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate, bulbourethral glands, and penis', 'The male reproductive system comprises the testes, epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, prostate, bulbourethral glands, and penis. Options naming only a single organ omit the components that produce, conduct, and deliver semen.'),
('207b3158-a5b0-53fb-9762-85db28a05482'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testes are:', '["The male gonads (produce sperm and testosterone)","The site of fertilization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The site of sperm maturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The male gonads (produce sperm and testosterone)', 'The testes are the male gonads, producing spermatozoa in the seminiferous tubules and testosterone in the Leydig cells. Sperm maturation occurs later in the epididymis, and fertilization takes place in the female tract.'),
('fdc03054-0deb-50bc-9dbf-333c892b357c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testes are located in:', '["The abdomen resulting in decreased functional activity","The scrotum (outside the body cavity for thermoregulation)","The inguinal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","The pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The scrotum (outside the body cavity for thermoregulation)', 'The testes occupy the scrotum outside the body cavity, where the temperature is kept below core level for spermatogenesis. The abdomen, pelvis, and inguinal canal are not their resting site.'),
('3ef504f3-a830-5b4e-be2e-f542fd7c3020'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The normal temperature of the scrotum is:', '["Much lower than body temperature resulting in decreased functional activity","About 2-3 degrees Celsius below the core body temperature","Higher than body temperature mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as body temperature without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 2-3 degrees Celsius below the core body temperature', 'The scrotum keeps the testes about 2-3 degrees Celsius below core body temperature, the optimum for spermatogenesis. A temperature equal to or higher than core impairs sperm production, and a much lower value is an inaccurate exaggeration.'),
('60e8aeb6-58c6-5a87-aec4-93e9ce5cce96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The descent of the testis occurs:', '["During puberty resulting in decreased functional activity","During adulthood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","After birth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","During fetal life (from the abdomen to the scrotum through the inguinal canal)"]'::jsonb, 'During fetal life (from the abdomen to the scrotum through the inguinal canal)', 'The testes descend from the abdomen through the inguinal canal into the scrotum during fetal life, usually by the seventh to eighth month. Descent only at or after birth is abnormal and constitutes cryptorchidism.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('4e303f45-162c-5178-b440-0acb9a94cee0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis is pulled down by:', '["The cremaster muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","The gubernaculum testis (a fibrous cord that attaches to the scrotum)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The spermatic cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The gubernaculum testis (a fibrous cord that attaches to the scrotum)', 'The gubernaculum testis is the fibrous cord that guides and anchors the testis to the scrotum during descent. The cremaster muscle and spermatic cord support the descended testis but do not pull it downward.'),
('57068bb6-a695-50ad-b127-f4bf5ef03f2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The processus vaginalis is:', '["An out-pouching of the peritoneum that precedes the testis into the scrotum","A blood vessel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An out-pouching of the peritoneum that precedes the testis into the scrotum', 'The processus vaginalis is a peritoneal outpouching that extends into the scrotum ahead of the descending testis. Its distal portion forms the tunica vaginalis and the remainder normally obliterates; it is not a vessel, nerve, or muscle.'),
('7d2e6af0-5ff7-5f7f-8195-43de45a83574'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'After the testis descends, the processus vaginalis:', '["Forms the spermatic cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Remains open accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obliterates (closes) to form the tunica vaginalis","Forms the epididymis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliterates (closes) to form the tunica vaginalis', 'After descent the processus vaginalis normally closes, with its distal remnant persisting as the tunica vaginalis around the testis; an unobliterated canal predisposes to an indirect inguinal hernia. The spermatic cord and epididymis develop independently and do not arise from it.'),
('d7b99985-ca8a-5e26-9daf-4cc3f8accb54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Failure of the processus vaginalis to close can cause:', '["An indirect inguinal hernia","None","A femoral hernia","An umbilical hernia","A direct hernia"]'::jsonb, 'An indirect inguinal hernia', 'The processus vaginalis normally obliterates after testicular descent; if it remains patent, abdominal contents may follow the spermatic cord into the scrotum, producing an indirect inguinal hernia. A femoral or umbilical hernia forms at a different site, and a direct hernia bulges through the posterior wall of the inguinal canal, unrelated to the processus vaginalis.'),
('864e9a65-beaa-576f-95f8-1a80abd56daf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Cryptorchidism (undescended testis) is:', '["A condition where the testis fails to descend into the scrotum","A twisted testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A painful testis mediated by specific receptor activation","An enlarged testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A condition where the testis fails to descend into the scrotum', 'In cryptorchidism the testis arrests along its normal path from the abdomen to the scrotum, usually within the inguinal canal. Undescended testes carry an increased risk of impaired spermatogenesis and malignancy. Torsion, pain, and enlargement describe separate testicular conditions.'),
('7cfe0aae-d41b-5b30-a3ea-6523b666a074'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis is covered by:', '["Only the tunica albuginea resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the tunica vaginalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The tunica vaginalis (visceral and parietal layers), the tunica albuginea, and the tunica vasculosa","Only the skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tunica vaginalis (visceral and parietal layers), the tunica albuginea, and the tunica vasculosa', 'The testis is invested by the serous tunica vaginalis, derived from the processus vaginalis, beneath which lie the fibrous tunica albuginea and the vascular tunica vasculosa. These layers protect the organ and permit smooth movement within the scrotum. No single layer accounts for the full covering.'),
('3e1b43f3-6044-54f8-bec3-f2294b77a076'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The tunica albuginea is:', '["A vascular layer resulting in decreased functional activity","A muscular layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A tough, fibrous capsule around the testis","A serosal covering leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A tough, fibrous capsule around the testis', 'The tunica albuginea is a dense white fibrous capsule that encloses the testis and sends septa into its substance. It is not vascular, muscular, or serous. The serous investment of the testis is the tunica vaginalis.'),
('63ed12d1-87b7-5955-873b-3b8587ceeee2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The mediastinum testis is:', '["A thickening of the tunica albuginea (the rete testis is located here)","The epididymis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The center of the testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickening of the tunica albuginea (the rete testis is located here)', 'The mediastinum testis is the posterior thickening of the tunica albuginea through which vessels and ducts enter the gland and within which the rete testis lies. The epididymis and vas deferens are separate tubular structures.'),
('a5b2f327-9fca-5f09-8f48-1ff14347cf19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis is divided into:', '["Two chambers resulting in decreased functional activity","Four chambers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","One large chamber mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobules (about 250-300 lobules) by fibrous septa"]'::jsonb, 'Lobules (about 250-300 lobules) by fibrous septa', 'Fibrous septa extend inward from the mediastinum and divide the testis into roughly 250 to 300 lobules, each containing coiled seminiferous tubules. A two-, four-, or single-chamber arrangement does not describe testicular architecture.'),
('858ff642-054f-53e2-8392-7392c59edd0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Each lobule of the testis contains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","1-4 seminiferous tubules (where spermatogenesis occurs)","Blood vessels only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only one tubule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","tubules leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1-4 seminiferous tubules (where spermatogenesis occurs)', 'Each testicular lobule contains one to four highly coiled seminiferous tubules, the site of spermatogenesis. Blood vessels accompany the tubules but are not the sole content, and the vague term "tubules" lacks the necessary precision.'),
('97041f0e-439b-5211-a1dd-7f2bfab53485'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminiferous tubules are:', '["None","The site of sperm maturation","The site of testosterone production","The site of spermatogenesis (sperm production)","The site of sperm storage"]'::jsonb, 'The site of spermatogenesis (sperm production)', 'The seminiferous tubules are the site of spermatogenesis, where germ cells mature into spermatozoa. Sperm maturation and storage take place in the epididymis, and testosterone is produced by the interstitial Leydig cells.'),
('df3a1b4c-5d76-5c35-bf5b-d17d10b9feba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminiferous tubules converge to form:', '["The seminal vesicles resulting in decreased functional activity","The epididymis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The rete testis (in the mediastinum testis)","The vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rete testis (in the mediastinum testis)', 'The seminiferous tubules open into short straight tubules that converge within the mediastinum testis to form the rete testis. Sperm leave the rete through the efferent ductules; the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles lie farther downstream.'),
('0dcff602-5ce8-5282-8bf4-44c063d9ec17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The rete testis is drained by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The efferent ductules (into the head of the epididymis)","The ejaculatory ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","The vas deferens directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The seminal vesicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The efferent ductules (into the head of the epididymis)', 'From the rete testis, sperm pass through about a dozen efferent ductules into the head of the epididymis. The ejaculatory duct, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles are proximal elements of the outflow tract and do not drain the rete.'),
('d49e74c4-6237-59ee-a23d-e8d168781d3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The epididymis is:', '["A long, coiled tube on the posterior border of the testis (stores and matures sperm)","The site of hormone production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The site of sperm production mediated by specific receptor activation","The vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A long, coiled tube on the posterior border of the testis (stores and matures sperm)', 'The epididymis is a long, tightly coiled duct along the posterior border of the testis where sperm are stored and undergo maturation. Sperm production occurs in the testis and hormones are produced by Leydig cells, and it is distinct from the vas deferens.'),
('bbddbf34-a326-59b4-89cd-5ed7aca15cc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The head of the epididymis:', '["Connects to the vas deferens resulting in decreased functional activity","Is the site of sperm production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is the site of testosterone production mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Receives sperm from the rete testis through the efferent ductules"]'::jsonb, 'Receives sperm from the rete testis through the efferent ductules', 'The head of the epididymis lies on the upper pole of the testis and receives sperm from the rete testis via the efferent ductules. The tail, not the head, is continuous with the vas deferens. Spermatogenesis and testosterone synthesis are testicular functions.'),
('b47c1516-4be4-53d0-8590-16c91a78285c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The tail of the epididymis:', '["Receives sperm from the rete testis","None","Is the site of sperm production","Is continuous with the vas deferens","Is the site of testosterone production"]'::jsonb, 'Is continuous with the vas deferens', 'The tail of the epididymis curves upward and continues directly as the vas deferens, which carries sperm toward the ejaculatory duct. Sperm enter the head via the efferent ductules, and spermatogenesis and testosterone production are testicular functions.'),
('5ed931a9-7506-5c64-831c-180c2bc525e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The vas deferens (ductus deferens) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tube that transports sperm from the testis to the epididymis mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscular tube that transports sperm from the epididymis to the ejaculatory duct","A tube that transports sperm from the seminal vesicle to the urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A muscular tube that transports sperm from the epididymis to the ejaculatory duct', 'The vas deferens is a thick-walled muscular tube that carries sperm from the tail of the epididymis to the ejaculatory duct. The other choices describe reversed or incorrect pathways within the duct system.'),
('01f7e13a-0969-5c87-aea7-7260ecdcf1c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The vas deferens runs in:', '["The femoral triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","The spermatic cord (from the scrotum to the inguinal canal, then to the pelvis)","The perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The abdominal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spermatic cord (from the scrotum to the inguinal canal, then to the pelvis)', 'The vas deferens ascends within the spermatic cord through the inguinal canal, then enters the pelvis to reach the region behind the bladder. The femoral triangle, perineum, and free abdominal cavity do not provide this pathway.'),
('1715af99-a82f-5b7d-aacf-cd3af32ff291'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord contains:', '["Only the vas deferens resulting in decreased functional activity","The vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, vasal vessels, lymphatics, and nerves","Only the pampiniform plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the testicular artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, vasal vessels, lymphatics, and nerves', 'The spermatic cord bundles the vas deferens together with the testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, vasal vessels, lymphatics, and nerves. It is not restricted to a single component, and only the choice listing the full set is accurate.'),
('58b77ce4-263f-5948-9d64-58b3616c103c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord passes through:', '["The obturator foramen","None","The femoral canal","The inguinal canal","The sciatic foramen"]'::jsonb, 'The inguinal canal', 'The spermatic cord traverses the inguinal canal, passing from the deep to the superficial inguinal ring to reach the scrotum. The obturator, femoral, and sciatic openings transmit other structures and do not conduct the cord.'),
('ebb15575-0a54-5b7e-ab32-790b71f86118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord is covered by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia (and cremaster muscle), and internal spermatic fascia","Four layers mediated by specific receptor activation","Only one layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two layers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia (and cremaster muscle), and internal spermatic fascia', 'The coverings of the spermatic cord are derived from the layers of the anterior abdominal wall: the external spermatic fascia, the cremasteric fascia enclosing the cremaster muscle, and the internal spermatic fascia. These three distinct layers, not an arbitrary count, invest the cord.'),
('fce5df76-2977-5a3c-b401-29ba6f15e824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cremasteric fascia and cremaster muscle are derived from:', '["The internal oblique aponeurosis","The transversus abdominis","The external oblique","None","The transversalis fascia"]'::jsonb, 'The internal oblique aponeurosis', 'The cremaster muscle and its fascia arise from the internal oblique aponeurosis, and the muscle is innervated by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve. The external oblique gives rise to the external spermatic fascia and the transversalis fascia to the internal spermatic fascia.'),
('8785c0a1-cbbd-5ac4-a937-2d338bc4c4d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The pampiniform plexus is:', '["An arterial network resulting in decreased functional activity","A nervous network accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A network of veins that drains the testis (helps cool the testicular blood)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lymphatic network leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A network of veins that drains the testis (helps cool the testicular blood)', 'The pampiniform plexus is a dense venous network surrounding the testicular artery that drains the testis and epididymis; its close contact with the artery allows countercurrent cooling of testicular blood. It is not arterial, nervous, or lymphatic, and it coalesces above to form the testicular vein.'),
('30472929-3b75-572c-b210-6a76ad7bbdb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Varicocele is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","An abnormal dilation of the pampiniform plexus (most commonly on the left side)","An abnormal dilation of the testicular artery mediated by specific receptor activation","An abnormal dilation of the vas deferens without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An abnormal dilation of the pampiniform plexus (most commonly on the left side)', 'Varicocele is abnormal dilatation of the pampiniform plexus, seen more often on the left because the left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle. The testicular artery and vas deferens are not involved.'),
('b4fa7e90-867c-5276-bd64-c5509f4789b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminal vesicles are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The site of testosterone production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The site of sperm storage mediated by specific receptor activation","Saccular glands located posterior to the bladder (secrete seminal fluid)","The site of sperm production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Saccular glands located posterior to the bladder (secrete seminal fluid)', 'The seminal vesicles are paired saccular glands lying posterior to the bladder and lateral to the ampulla of each vas deferens, and they secrete seminal fluid. Sperm production, sperm storage, and testosterone synthesis do not occur here.'),
('ee54ba85-41fc-5910-ba7b-19e8b5dc9b9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminal vesicles secrete:', '["A thick, yellowish, fructose-rich fluid (part of the semen)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sperm mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Testosterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thick, yellowish, fructose-rich fluid (part of the semen)', 'Seminal vesicle secretion is a thick, yellowish fluid rich in fructose that provides energy for sperm and contributes a large part of the ejaculate. Sperm, testosterone, and mucus are not products of these glands.'),
('21329553-40ad-59ab-9877-f966d8b22c48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The duct of the seminal vesicle joins:', '["The vas deferens to form the ejaculatory duct","The urethra directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The prostate directly mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vas deferens to form the ejaculatory duct', 'The duct of the seminal vesicle joins the ampulla of the vas deferens to form the ejaculatory duct, which opens into the prostatic urethra. It does not open into the urethra, prostate, or epididymis directly.'),
('eb3c3ec3-2426-51c5-9e19-263f33936811'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The ejaculatory duct passes through:', '["The seminal vesicle resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostate (to open into the prostatic urethra at the verumontanum)","The bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostate (to open into the prostatic urethra at the verumontanum)', 'Each ejaculatory duct descends through the substance of the prostate and opens onto the prostatic urethra at the verumontanum. The bladder is not traversed, and the seminal vesicle and vas deferens contribute to the duct rather than being passed through.'),
('9ffc6830-83d5-5657-a871-25de133c5f0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The ejaculatory duct opens at:', '["The penile urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","The verumontanum (colliculus seminalis) in the prostatic urethra","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The membranous urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The verumontanum (colliculus seminalis) in the prostatic urethra', 'The ejaculatory ducts open on the verumontanum, a midline elevation on the posterior wall of the prostatic urethra. The penile and membranous urethra lie downstream and the bladder neck lies proximal, so none of these is the opening site.'),
('81dfe9ef-d310-568b-ba94-f7f96fd826f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibromuscular gland that surrounds the prostatic urethra (secretes prostatic fluid)","A gland that produces sperm mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A gland that produces testosterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular gland that surrounds the prostatic urethra (secretes prostatic fluid)', 'The prostate is a fibromuscular gland that encircles the prostatic urethra and secretes prostatic fluid into it. It does not produce sperm or testosterone and is not purely muscular.'),
('b5391354-46c9-559c-96b4-dea03f3e878a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Above the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Behind the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Below the bladder (surrounding the prostatic urethra)","In the scrotum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Below the bladder (surrounding the prostatic urethra)', 'The prostate lies immediately inferior to the bladder neck and surrounds the prostatic urethra, making it palpable through the anterior rectal wall. It does not lie above, behind, or within the scrotum.'),
('f06c8ded-da5f-5ce7-80c4-6197537900db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is divided into:', '["The central zone, peripheral zone, and transitional zone (the peripheral zone is the most common site for carcinoma)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two zones mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only one zone without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Four zones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central zone, peripheral zone, and transitional zone (the peripheral zone is the most common site for carcinoma)', 'The prostate comprises a central zone, a peripheral zone, and a transitional zone. Carcinoma most often arises in the peripheral zone, which is why the gland is examined rectally, whereas BPH typically involves the transitional zone.'),
('ee2ace9f-dbee-5719-96d7-0a54638fced3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostatic utricle is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small blind-ended sac in the prostatic urethra (the male homologue of the uterus)","The opening of the ejaculatory ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening of the prostatic ducts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small blind-ended sac in the prostatic urethra (the male homologue of the uterus)', 'The prostatic utricle is a small blind-ending pouch on the verumontanum of the prostatic urethra, representing the remnant of the paramesonephric ducts and the male homologue of the uterus. The openings of the ejaculatory and prostatic ducts are separate structures.'),
('150bab1f-a88b-5016-8486-a48ae08bb6f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostatic ducts open into:', '["The verumontanum resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostatic sinuses (on either side of the urethral crest)","The penile urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ejaculatory duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic sinuses (on either side of the urethral crest)', 'The prostatic ducts discharge into the prostatic sinuses, the grooves lying on either side of the urethral crest. The verumontanum is the site of the ejaculatory duct openings, the penile urethra lies downstream, and the ejaculatory duct is a separate channel.'),
('0c309df7-5a01-527e-9ce9-2dd07a87c8b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) most commonly involves:', '["The peripheral zone resulting in decreased functional activity","The transitional zone of the prostate","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The central zone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All zones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The transitional zone of the prostate', 'BPH arises mainly in the transitional (periurethral) zone, where enlargement compresses the prostatic urethra and causes bladder outlet obstruction. Prostate carcinoma typically involves the peripheral zone, and the central zone surrounds the ejaculatory ducts.'),
('3bea3911-0e9e-5e9d-ae33-3a9c478d80d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Prostate cancer most commonly involves:', '["The central zone resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The peripheral zone of the prostate","The transitional zone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All zones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peripheral zone of the prostate', 'Most prostatic adenocarcinomas arise in the peripheral zone, so a hard nodule may be palpable on digital rectal examination. The transitional zone is the usual site of BPH, and the central zone is less frequently involved.'),
('47e46dfd-2f95-5da5-afac-1689e3a25fb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is supplied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The testicular artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pudendal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The renal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inferior vesical artery (and middle rectal artery)"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior vesical artery (and middle rectal artery)', 'The prostate receives its blood supply chiefly from the inferior vesical artery, with a contribution from the middle rectal artery. The testicular, pudendal, and renal arteries do not supply the gland.'),
('d43bc8f4-7f3e-5ec6-baaf-0e65e6830fdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is drained by:', '["The prostatic venous plexus (which drains into the internal iliac vein)","The renal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The portal vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The testicular vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic venous plexus (which drains into the internal iliac vein)', 'The prostatic venous plexus surrounds the gland and drains into the internal iliac veins. The renal, portal, and testicular veins are not involved in prostatic venous drainage.'),
('a148169c-4aef-5976-8bce-0d1c29fc0654'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostatic venous plexus communicates with:', '["The portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","The azygos vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus), which can be a route for metastasis"]'::jsonb, 'The vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus), which can be a route for metastasis', 'The prostatic venous plexus communicates with the vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus), allowing prostatic carcinoma cells to reach the vertebral column and pelvis without passing through the inferior vena cava. The portal vein and azygos vein do not form this connection.'),
('f4c4a6b7-8b8e-5199-956f-de7131b16ebf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The male organ of copulation and urination","The male gonad mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The site of sperm production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The male organ of copulation and urination', 'The penis is the male organ of copulation and also conducts urine from the bladder through the penile urethra. The gonads produce sperm, and spermatogenesis occurs in the testis, not in the penis.'),
('9c5b5d98-f089-5699-a86a-b4cb90d6da78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The corpora cavernosa are:', '["The tissue in the glans resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The median column of erectile tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two lateral columns of erectile tissue in the dorsum of the penis"]'::jsonb, 'Two lateral columns of erectile tissue in the dorsum of the penis', 'The two corpora cavernosa form the paired dorsal erectile columns of the penis, each enclosed in a thick tunica albuginea and separated by a median septum. The median erectile column is the corpus spongiosum, and the glans is its distal expansion.'),
('2d752073-13b6-504d-9af6-6f7025b26771'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The corpus spongiosum is:', '["The tissue around the urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The tissue in the glans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The median column of erectile tissue that contains the penile urethra"]'::jsonb, 'The median column of erectile tissue that contains the penile urethra', 'The corpus spongiosum is the midline erectile column that transmits the penile urethra, expanding distally into the glans and proximally into the bulb. It is not merely the tissue of the glans or tissue around the urethra.'),
('b622f339-85b4-5ade-9da0-3b2eb3664bf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The corpus spongiosum expands distally to form:', '["The bulb of the penis","The fremulum","None","The glans penis","The prepuce"]'::jsonb, 'The glans penis', 'At its distal end the corpus spongiosum expands to form the glans penis, which is covered by the prepuce. The bulb is the proximal expansion of the corpus spongiosum, while the frenulum and prepuce are folds of penile skin.'),
('ecfbe024-507b-5f2d-bd89-1b558da6381d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The corpus spongiosum expands proximally to form:', '["The glans resulting in decreased functional activity","The bulb of the penis (attached to the perineal membrane)","The corpus cavernosum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The crura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulb of the penis (attached to the perineal membrane)', 'Proximally the corpus spongiosum enlarges into the bulb of the penis, anchored to the undersurface of the perineal membrane. The glans is the distal expansion, the crura arise from the corpora cavernosa, and the corpus cavernosum is a separate erectile body.'),
('09885849-8b2a-54c4-a536-7dd4824b900d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The corpora cavernosa expand proximally to form:', '["The crura of the penis (attached to the ischiopubic rami)","The bulb of the penis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The corpus spongiosum mediated by specific receptor activation","The glans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The crura of the penis (attached to the ischiopubic rami)', 'The corpora cavernosa diverge proximally into the crura of the penis, each attached to the ischiopubic ramus and enveloped by the ischiocavernosus muscle. The bulb is the proximal expansion of the corpus spongiosum, and the glans is its distal end.'),
('0a2de8f2-deae-5ea7-8a07-2ec1fd80a5c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The root of the penis is formed by:', '["The foreskin resulting in decreased functional activity","The crura (attached to the ischiopubic rami) and the bulb (attached to the perineal membrane)","The shaft mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The glans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The crura (attached to the ischiopubic rami) and the bulb (attached to the perineal membrane)', 'The fixed root of the penis consists of the two crura, attached to the ischiopubic rami, together with the bulb, attached to the perineal membrane. The shaft, glans, and foreskin are parts of the body of the penis.'),
('aa0fb1c6-99b9-568c-832a-3c6a57df13e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is attached to the perineum by:', '["The shaft resulting in decreased functional activity","The skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The root of the penis (the crura and the bulb)","The glans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The root of the penis (the crura and the bulb)', 'The penis is anchored to the perineum through its root, which comprises the crura on the ischiopubic rami and the bulb on the perineal membrane. The shaft, skin, and glans do not provide this attachment.'),
('80b845f9-b840-56c4-b7be-6966f1bfdc9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is covered by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the prepuce accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A heavy layer of skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Only skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin (thin, loosely attached) and the prepuce (foreskin)"]'::jsonb, 'Skin (thin, loosely attached) and the prepuce (foreskin)', 'The penile skin is thin and loosely attached, and the prepuce (foreskin) forms a fold covering the glans. The covering is not heavy, and neither skin alone nor the prepuce alone describes the full investment.'),
('3427578e-3f6f-5475-bc01-add1cf035dbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is supplied by:', '["The testicular artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The renal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The inferior epigastric artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The dorsal artery of the penis and the deep artery of the penis (from the internal pudendal artery)"]'::jsonb, 'The dorsal artery of the penis and the deep artery of the penis (from the internal pudendal artery)', 'The penis is supplied by the dorsal and deep (cavernous) arteries, both branches of the internal pudendal artery. The testicular, renal, and inferior epigastric arteries do not supply the penis.'),
('4324d768-227c-50a9-b4fd-eae289bc490d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is drained by:', '["The renal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The portal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The testicular vein mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The dorsal vein of the penis (superficial and deep) and the cavernous veins"]'::jsonb, 'The dorsal vein of the penis (superficial and deep) and the cavernous veins', 'Venous drainage of the penis occurs through the superficial and deep dorsal veins together with the cavernous veins. The renal, portal, and testicular veins do not drain the penis.'),
('c6386b7e-466e-5613-b363-a14dde8af67d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The superficial dorsal vein of the penis drains into:', '["The great saphenous vein (via the external pudendal vein)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IVC mediated by specific receptor activation","The deep dorsal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal pudendal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The great saphenous vein (via the external pudendal vein)', 'The superficial dorsal vein of the penis drains into the great saphenous vein through the external pudendal veins. The deep dorsal vein and internal pudendal vein represent separate routes, and the inferior vena cava is not the direct destination.'),
('eea6ef0a-b31f-5565-a394-d18043ad6f62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The deep dorsal vein of the penis drains into:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostatic venous plexus (and then into the internal iliac vein)","The great saphenous vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The femoral vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic venous plexus (and then into the internal iliac vein)', 'The deep dorsal vein of the penis passes deep to the pubis to join the prostatic venous plexus, which drains into the internal iliac veins. The great saphenous, portal, and femoral veins do not receive this blood.'),
('20f91c3f-b902-54dc-b0e7-5559204fee9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is innervated by:', '["The pudendal nerve (dorsal nerve of the penis) and the autonomic nervous system","Only the autonomic nervous system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the pudendal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The sciatic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve (dorsal nerve of the penis) and the autonomic nervous system', 'Sensory innervation of the penis is provided by the dorsal nerve of the penis, a branch of the pudendal nerve, while sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers control erection and ejaculation. The sciatic nerve does not supply the penis, and neither division alone accounts for all its innervation.'),
('11b55891-a25d-509d-869b-5ee21fbabf5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Erection is controlled by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The somatic nervous system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The parasympathetic nervous system (pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)","The sympathetic nervous system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The parasympathetic nervous system (pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)', 'Erection is mediated by parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4), which relax cavernous smooth muscle, dilate helicine arteries, and allow blood to fill the corpora cavernosa. The sympathetic system promotes detumescence, and somatic innervation controls the skeletal muscles of the perineum rather than the erectile response itself.'),
('99f30143-835d-5bbb-b97d-19a8750a4fb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Ejaculation is controlled by:', '["The parasympathetic nervous system","All","None","The somatic nervous system","The sympathetic nervous system (T11-L2)"]'::jsonb, 'The sympathetic nervous system (T11-L2)', 'Ejaculation is a sympathetic event driven by fibers arising from T11-L2, which travel in the hypogastric plexus to cause contraction of the vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and prostate, propelling semen into the urethra. The parasympathetic system governs erection, while somatic nerves only provide the motor drive for the penile body musculature.'),
('6478db4f-417b-58ca-a55f-79848aa24612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dartos muscle is:', '["A layer of skeletal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","A layer of the testicular capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A layer of smooth muscle in the scrotal wall (responsible for wrinkling of the scrotum)","The cremaster muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A layer of smooth muscle in the scrotal wall (responsible for wrinkling of the scrotum)', 'The dartos is a layer of smooth muscle lying in the superficial fascia of the scrotum; its reflex contraction wrinkles the scrotal skin and draws the testis closer to the body to conserve heat. It is smooth, not skeletal, and is entirely separate from the cremaster and the testicular capsule.'),
('d715a941-9d27-5331-a1b1-426cd360ff31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cremaster muscle is:', '["A layer of skeletal muscle derived from the internal oblique (helps lift the testis)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A layer of the testicular capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","A layer of smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The dartos muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A layer of skeletal muscle derived from the internal oblique (helps lift the testis)', 'The cremaster is a band of striated (skeletal) muscle derived from the lower edge of the internal oblique muscle, ensheathed by its fascia; its contraction elevates the testis and participates in temperature regulation. It is skeletal in type and therefore distinct from the smooth-muscle dartos.'),
('1f80d7e7-3867-5625-8711-3683fe706983'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cremaster muscle is innervated by:', '["The obturator nerve","The ilioinguinal nerve","None","The genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)","The femoral nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)', 'The cremaster is supplied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2), which explains why stroking the inner thigh produces the cremasteric reflex with testicular elevation. The ilioinguinal nerve supplies adjacent skin, while the obturator and femoral nerves serve the adductor and anterior thigh compartments.'),
('52e17936-293d-54c1-be13-55b54e3d086f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cremasteric reflex is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A deep tendon reflex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A withdrawal reflex mediated by specific receptor activation","A superficial reflex (contraction of the cremaster muscle when the inner thigh is stroke)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A superficial reflex (contraction of the cremaster muscle when the inner thigh is stroke)', 'The cremasteric reflex is a superficial reflex in which stroking the medial side of the thigh causes contraction of the cremaster and elevation of the testis, mediated by the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2). It is not a deep tendon or withdrawal reflex and is occasionally absent in upper motor neuron pathology.'),
('b707d6bd-4679-5849-88a5-6101a04c0622'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is divided into:', '["Three compartments resulting in decreased functional activity","Two compartments by a midline scrotal septum","One compartment mediated by specific receptor activation","Four compartments without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two compartments by a midline scrotal septum', 'A median scrotal septum, formed by the dartos fascia and corresponding internally to the fusion of the labioscrotal swellings, divides the scrotum into two compartments, each containing a testis, epididymis, and spermatic cord. Any other number of compartments is anatomically incorrect.'),
('aeeb7fbd-35d6-5728-a172-fdd8342adebc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is innervated by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the genitofemoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the ilioinguinal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the pudendal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, posterior cutaneous of the thigh, and perineal branches of the pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, posterior cutaneous of the thigh, and perineal branches of the pudendal nerve', 'The scrotum receives skin innervation from two sources: anterosuperiorly from the ilioinguinal and genitofemoral nerves, and posteroinferiorly from the posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh and the perineal branches of the pudendal nerve. Because no single nerve supplies the whole scrotum, the combined listing is required.'),
('64745264-7991-52f5-8bb7-1c6143ca8827'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is supplied by:', '["The testicular artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external pudendal artery (from the femoral artery) and the scrotal branches of the internal pudendal artery","Only the external pudendal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the internal pudendal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external pudendal artery (from the femoral artery) and the scrotal branches of the internal pudendal artery', 'The anterior scrotum is supplied by the external pudendal arteries arising from the femoral artery, while the posterior scrotum is supplied by scrotal branches of the internal pudendal artery; both sources are needed for the full blood supply. The testicular artery perfuses the testis and epididymis, not the scrotal wall.'),
('24319b9d-0625-55be-8fa4-4a274f6b1921'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra is divided into:', '["Only the penile part","None","Only the membranous and penile parts","The prostatic, membranous, and penile (spongy) parts","Only the prostatic and membranous parts"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic, membranous, and penile (spongy) parts', 'The male urethra has three parts: the prostatic part within the gland, the short membranous part traversing the perineal membrane, and the longest penile (spongy) part inside the corpus spongiosum. Options that omit the prostatic or penile segment describe an incomplete subdivision.'),
('ca424d83-e476-5a5a-9375-90a65fb53b91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The membranous urethra is:', '["The longest part resulting in decreased functional activity","The widest part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The most dilatable part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The narrowest and least dilatable part of the male urethra (about 2 cm)"]'::jsonb, 'The narrowest and least dilatable part of the male urethra (about 2 cm)', 'The membranous urethra is a short segment about 2 cm long that passes through the perineal membrane; it is the narrowest and least dilatable part because it is fixed within the urogenital diaphragm and not surrounded by distensible erectile tissue. The penile urethra, by contrast, is the longest part.'),
('1c990100-8b7e-5322-8207-f0d91d8b9621'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penile (spongy) urethra is:', '["The widest part resulting in decreased functional activity","The shortest part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The narrowest part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The longest part of the male urethra (about 15 cm, runs through the corpus spongiosum)"]'::jsonb, 'The longest part of the male urethra (about 15 cm, runs through the corpus spongiosum)', 'The penile (spongy) urethra is the longest segment of the male urethra, measuring about 15 cm, and runs through the corpus spongiosum to the external orifice. It widens distally at the bulb and fossa navicularis, so describing it as the narrowest or shortest part is incorrect.'),
('22747407-5d44-5a9f-b2bd-064eb96fb487'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra has two curves:', '["The infrapubic (perineal) curve and the prepubic (penile) curve","Three curves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","curves mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only one curve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The infrapubic (perineal) curve and the prepubic (penile) curve', 'In its course the male urethra describes two curves: the fixed infrapubic (perineal) curve passing below the pubic symphysis and the movable prepubic (penile) curve at the root of the penis. These two curves are central to the technique of urethral catheterization, and any other count is incorrect.'),
('91a56e9d-a65b-549e-a219-fbbb7d48e7f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The infrapubic curve is:', '["The movable curve resulting in decreased functional activity","The curve in the prostatic urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The fixed curve below the pubic symphysis (at the junction of the membranous and penile urethra)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fixed curve below the pubic symphysis (at the junction of the membranous and penile urethra)', 'The infrapubic curve is the fixed concavity where the urethra passes below the pubic symphysis, at the junction of the membranous and penile parts, and it cannot be straightened simply by lifting the penis. The movable prepubic curve, in contrast, lies at the penile root.'),
('dade7012-06e5-59b7-9fba-d0008723857b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prepubic curve is:', '["The fixed curve resulting in decreased functional activity","The movable curve (in the penile urethra, at the junction of the penis and the scrotum)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The curve in the prostatic urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The movable curve (in the penile urethra, at the junction of the penis and the scrotum)', 'The prepubic curve is the mobile bend in the penile urethra at the junction of the penis with the scrotum; lifting the penis straightens this curve, which is exploited when passing a catheter. It is movable and therefore distinct from the fixed infrapubic curve.'),
('95701b66-999c-5144-bff2-4ce28b5b56b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Passing a catheter in males requires:', '["Flexing the penis resulting in decreased functional activity","special maneuver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Extending the penis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Straightening the penis to overcome the infrapubic curve"]'::jsonb, 'Straightening the penis to overcome the infrapubic curve', 'During urethral catheterization the penis is held taut and lifted forward, which straightens out the curvatures of the urethra and allows the catheter to negotiate the fixed infrapubic curve. Flexing or failing to straighten the penis leaves the curves intact and obstructs advancement of the catheter.'),
('329103f4-82ee-55bc-91b9-a5b3e50b9d5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The urethra has three narrowings:', '["At the bladder neck, the membranous urethra, and the fossa navicularis resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","At the internal urethral orifice, the membranous urethra, and the external urethral orifice","At the prostatic, membranous, and penile parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At the internal urethral orifice, the membranous urethra, and the external urethral orifice', 'The three narrow points of the male urethra are the internal urethral orifice at the bladder neck, the membranous urethra, and the external urethral orifice, whereas the corresponding three dilatations are the prostatic urethra, the bulb, and the fossa navicularis. Listing the parts themselves or the fossa as a narrowing is anatomically incorrect.'),
('8db5ef8e-4c40-5ee2-be75-ceceec1a1938'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The external urethral orifice is:', '["The narrowest point of the male urethra (located at the tip of the glans)","The same as the internal orifice accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The widest point mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The narrowest point of the male urethra (located at the tip of the glans)', 'The external urethral orifice at the tip of the glans is the narrowest point of the whole male urethra and is a common site where a urinary stone becomes impacted. It is anatomically separate from the internal urethral orifice at the bladder neck and is certainly not the widest segment.'),
('c9b74d9a-f779-5b2c-87d5-0464a7703e0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The fossa navicularis is:', '["A sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","A narrowing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A dilatation of the penile urethra just proximal to the external orifice","A valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A dilatation of the penile urethra just proximal to the external orifice', 'The fossa navicularis is a fusiform dilatation of the terminal portion of the penile urethra immediately proximal to the external urethral orifice, into which the ducts of the para-urethral glands may open. It is a widened pouch, not a sphincter, valve, or narrowing.'),
('fd94d9fa-13cd-5d8a-bc31-1c123b154974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Hypospadias is:', '["A congenital anomaly where the urethral opening is on the dorsal surface resulting in decreased functional activity","A congenital anomaly where the urethral opening is on the ventral surface of the penis","A congenital anomaly of the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital anomaly where the urethral opening is on the ventral surface of the penis', 'Hypospadias is a congenital condition in which the external urethral meatus opens on the ventral (undersurface) aspect of the penis, caused by incomplete fusion of the urethral folds. It is the opposite of epispadias, in which the opening is on the dorsal surface.'),
('d73fed45-d829-5fc5-a33a-f0bb428561cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Epispadias is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A congenital anomaly of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A congenital anomaly where the urethral opening is on the dorsal surface of the penis","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A congenital anomaly where the urethral opening is on the ventral surface leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital anomaly where the urethral opening is on the dorsal surface of the penis', 'Epispadias is a congenital malformation in which the urethral meatus is located on the dorsal (upper) surface of the penis, resulting from a defect in dorsal midline fusion of the penile tissues. A ventral opening describes hypospadias and a bladder anomaly describes exstrophy, not the meatal location.'),
('91dd7a75-e14a-5818-b9b8-08ac35ba9031'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Epispadias is associated with:', '["None","Cryptorchidism","Bladder exstrophy","Inguinal hernia","Varicocele"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder exstrophy', 'Epispadias is commonly associated with bladder exstrophy because both result from a midline defect in the anterior abdominal wall and anterior bladder wall during development, and they are part of the same spectrum of cloacal and lower abdominal anomalies. Cryptorchidism, inguinal hernia, and varicocele are unrelated associations.'),
('07e43743-cb78-5bf3-958c-3df92eb29fb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prepuce (foreskin) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A fold of skin that covers the glans penis","The same as the frenulum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the glans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fold of skin that covers the glans penis', 'The prepuce (foreskin) is a double fold of skin that covers and protects the glans penis and is attached at the corona and frenulum. It is a distinct skin structure and is neither the frenulum nor the glans itself.'),
('4ede25f8-e36e-5249-ae71-675bb781f3cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The frenulum of the penis is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A fold of skin on the ventral surface of the glans (connects the glans to the prepuce)","The same as the prepuce mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the corona without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the glans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fold of skin on the ventral surface of the glans (connects the glans to the prepuce)', 'The frenulum is a median fold of skin on the ventral surface of the glans that tethers the prepuce to the glans; it is often torn in injuries to the foreskin. It is a distinct structure, not identical to the prepuce, corona, or glans.'),
('74f2e17f-013d-5a46-9588-3275e682d4aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Phimosis is:', '["A condition where the glans is enlarged resulting in decreased functional activity","A condition where the prepuce is absent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A condition where the prepuce cannot be retracted over the glans (usually from a tight foreskin)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A condition where the prepuce cannot be retracted over the glans (usually from a tight foreskin)', 'Phimosis is the inability to retract the prepuce over the glans penis, usually due to a congenitally narrow or fibrotic foreskin, which can obstruct urinary flow and predispose to infection. It is not the reverse problem of paraphimosis and does not involve an absent or enlarged glans.'),
('a5eefba6-e10f-50af-a304-916e8c1dc6b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Paraphimosis is:', '["A condition where the prepuce cannot be retracted resulting in decreased functional activity","A condition where the retracted prepuce cannot be returned to its normal position (constricts the glans)","The same as phimosis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A condition where the retracted prepuce cannot be returned to its normal position (constricts the glans)', 'Paraphimosis occurs when an already retracted prepuce forms a constricting ring behind the glans and cannot be reduced back over it, which can obstruct venous drainage and cause glans edema or ischemia requiring urgent treatment. It is the opposite of phimosis, in which the foreskin cannot be retracted.'),
('5ff3668d-83a4-522d-834e-dd7437b54dcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord begins at:', '["The inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The deep inguinal ring (at the junction of the scrotum and the inguinal canal)","The scrotum mediated by specific receptor activation","The testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The deep inguinal ring (at the junction of the scrotum and the inguinal canal)', 'The spermatic cord begins at the deep inguinal ring, where its constituent structures converge as they emerge from the abdomen, and it ends at the posterior border of the testis. The scrotum and testis represent its termination rather than its origin, while the phrase describes the canal it traverses.'),
('75f331bd-04f8-5210-b09c-4cacc5a6dc9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord contains three arteries:', '["Only the cremasteric artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the testicular artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the artery to the vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The testicular artery, the cremasteric artery, and the artery to the vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'The testicular artery, the cremasteric artery, and the artery to the vas deferens', 'Three arteries travel in the spermatic cord: the testicular artery (from the abdominal aorta), the cremasteric artery (from the inferior epigastric artery), and the artery to the vas deferens (from the inferior vesical or umbilical artery). No single artery alone constitutes the cord''s arterial supply.'),
('3d7857b8-588f-529d-a1e3-dc228b6f4eb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord contains three veins:', '["Only the vasal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the cremasteric vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the testicular vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pampiniform plexus (testicular vein), the cremasteric vein, and the vasal vein"]'::jsonb, 'The pampiniform plexus (testicular vein), the cremasteric vein, and the vasal vein', 'Venous drainage from the cord is via three channels: the pampiniform plexus draining into the testicular vein, the cremasteric vein, and the vasal vein. The plexus acts as a countercurrent heat exchanger, and none of these venous components alone constitutes the complete drainage.'),
('6a894d38-7a68-5120-bb05-c5ea80d527ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord contains three nerves:', '["Only the ilioinguinal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The genitofemoral nerve (genital branch), the ilioinguinal nerve, and the sympathetic nerves","Only the sympathetic nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the genitofemoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The genitofemoral nerve (genital branch), the ilioinguinal nerve, and the sympathetic nerves', 'The spermatic cord carries three neural components: the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (supplying the cremaster), the ilioinguinal nerve, and sympathetic fibers from the hypogastric plexus. Because all three are present, an option naming only one nerve is incomplete.'),
('3f45390b-8593-5ded-8857-5dbe3f572f16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The inguinal canal is:', '["A horizontal passage resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An oblique passage through the anterior abdominal wall (about 4-5 cm long)","A circular opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A vertical passage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An oblique passage through the anterior abdominal wall (about 4-5 cm long)', 'The inguinal canal is an oblique, slit-like passage about 4-5 cm long that runs through the lower part of the anterior abdominal wall between the deep and superficial inguinal rings. A horizontal, vertical, or circular description does not match its oblique course.'),
('35261cd3-1ccf-5e22-9b6f-5e6e7952a63a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The inguinal canal runs:', '["From the superficial ring to the deep ring resulting in decreased functional activity","From the pubic tubercle to the ASIS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the deep inguinal ring (above the mid-inguinal point) to the superficial inguinal ring (above the pubic tubercle)"]'::jsonb, 'From the deep inguinal ring (above the mid-inguinal point) to the superficial inguinal ring (above the pubic tubercle)', 'The inguinal canal extends obliquely downward and medially from the deep inguinal ring, which lies above the mid-inguinal point, to the superficial inguinal ring, located just above the pubic tubercle. Reversing the direction or using the ASIS as the medial landmark is incorrect.'),
('aa2dd4e5-eaf1-5603-adc9-ecfd1297ee76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The deep inguinal ring is:', '["An opening in the internal oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","An opening in the transversalis fascia (at the mid-inguinal point)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An opening in the external oblique aponeurosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An opening in the transversalis fascia (at the mid-inguinal point)', 'The deep inguinal ring is an oval defect in the transversalis fascia lying above the mid-inguinal point, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and is the entrance of the spermatic cord into the canal. The superficial ring, by contrast, lies in the external oblique aponeurosis.'),
('0da51896-0ff6-533b-87b8-7449a225793e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The superficial inguinal ring is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","An opening in the internal oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An opening in the transversalis fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An opening in the external oblique aponeurosis (above the pubic tubercle)"]'::jsonb, 'An opening in the external oblique aponeurosis (above the pubic tubercle)', 'The superficial inguinal ring is a triangular defect in the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle, situated just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle, and it forms the exit of the canal. It is not formed by the internal oblique or the transversalis fascia.'),
('1a00a22f-5d69-529d-b128-10219d543b6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The inguinal canal contains:', '["The femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The sciatic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The spermatic cord (in males) or the round ligament of the uterus (in females)"]'::jsonb, 'The spermatic cord (in males) or the round ligament of the uterus (in females)', 'In males the inguinal canal transmits the spermatic cord (and ilioinguinal nerve), while in females it transmits the round ligament of the uterus. The femoral artery and femoral and sciatic nerves lie in the thigh and do not pass through the inguinal canal.'),
('9191c16f-07c4-5afc-a8f4-729d4f374798'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'An indirect inguinal hernia passes:', '["Through the obturator foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the Hesselbach triangle (medial to the inferior epigastric vessels) mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the deep inguinal ring (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels)","Through the femoral canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the deep inguinal ring (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels)', 'An indirect inguinal hernia enters the canal through the deep inguinal ring and therefore lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, often tracking down into the scrotum; in younger patients it is frequently due to a patent processus vaginalis. Protrusion medial to the inferior epigastric vessels through the Hesselbach triangle describes a direct hernia.'),
('15c1546d-cd2d-5a3a-96cc-5ccc5697e81d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A direct inguinal hernia passes:', '["Through the femoral canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Through Hesselbach triangle (medial to the inferior epigastric vessels)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the deep inguinal ring without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the obturator foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through Hesselbach triangle (medial to the inferior epigastric vessels)', 'A direct inguinal hernia bulges through the weak floor of the Hesselbach triangle, lying medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, and usually does not descend into the scrotum. Entry lateral to those vessels occurs with a deep-ring (indirect) hernia, while the femoral canal and obturator foramen describe other hernia sites.'),
('71b2bbd2-8b37-531e-b7f9-cb6f2361f134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'An indirect inguinal hernia is the most common type of hernia:', '["Only in males resulting in decreased functional activity","In both males and females (especially in children)","In adults only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In both males and females (especially in children)', 'Indirect inguinal hernias are the most common type of hernia overall and predominate in both sexes, particularly in children, because they frequently result from persistence of the processus vaginalis. Restricting this hernia to a single sex or to adults alone does not reflect its epidemiology.'),
('85662c96-aab3-5a99-b521-4c3a58609d0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A direct inguinal hernia is more common:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","In females accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In children mediated by specific receptor activation","In older males (it is a weakness of the abdominal wall, not congenital)","In young adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In older males (it is a weakness of the abdominal wall, not congenital)', 'Direct inguinal hernias reflect acquired weakness of the posterior wall of the Hesselbach triangle and are therefore most common in older males, often associated with chronic raised intra-abdominal pressure. They are not congenital in origin and are not typical of children or females.'),
('74229f0a-a9b5-5254-8ac0-32e4641ed3b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A femoral hernia passes:', '["Through the deep inguinal ring resulting in decreased functional activity","Through the Hesselbach triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the femoral canal (below the inguinal ligament)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the obturator foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the femoral canal (below the inguinal ligament)', 'A femoral hernia descends through the femoral canal, passing below and lateral to the inguinal ligament and medial to the femoral vein. The deep inguinal ring and Hesselbach triangle are routes for inguinal hernias, and the obturator foramen is the site of an obturator hernia.'),
('7f7e5968-44f9-5d91-8543-fa273f94d849'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Femoral hernias are more common:', '["In females (especially in elderly women)","In males accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In young adults mediated by specific receptor activation","In children without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In females (especially in elderly women)', 'Femoral hernias are more common in women, particularly the elderly, which is attributed to the wider female pelvis and femoral ring. They carry a higher risk of strangulation than inguinal hernias because of their narrow neck at the femoral ring, and they are uncommon in children.'),
('a6075fec-c62b-5b99-afbf-37daa119ecf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A strangulated hernia occurs when:', '["The hernia is small resulting in decreased functional activity","The blood supply to the herniated contents is compromised (surgical emergency)","The hernia becomes reducible mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The blood supply to the herniated contents is compromised (surgical emergency)', 'A hernia becomes strangulated when the constriction at the neck compromises the blood supply to the herniated contents, leading to ischemia and gangrene, which constitutes a surgical emergency. A small but well-perfused hernia or one that spontaneously reduces is not strangulated.'),
('ac1e8256-b279-52f2-a3e9-70870da988d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'An incarcerated hernia is:', '["A hernia that can be reduced resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A hernia that has a wide neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hernia that cannot be reduced (not necessarily strangulated)"]'::jsonb, 'A hernia that cannot be reduced (not necessarily strangulated)', 'Incarceration means the hernial sac contents are trapped and cannot be reduced back into the abdomen, although the blood supply may still be intact. Strangulation develops only when that trapped content becomes ischemic, so a hernia that is reducible or has a wide, patulous neck does not qualify as incarcerated.'),
('40ee89ce-2493-55cd-b36f-90e315909033'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate produces:', '["Approximately 60% resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Approximately 50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Approximately 10% mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Approximately 20-30% of the volume of semen (thin, milky fluid rich in acid phosphatase, citric acid, and proteolytic enzymes)"]'::jsonb, 'Approximately 20-30% of the volume of semen (thin, milky fluid rich in acid phosphatase, citric acid, and proteolytic enzymes)', 'The prostate secretes a thin, milky fluid rich in acid phosphatase, citric acid, and proteolytic enzymes that contributes roughly 20-30% of the ejaculate volume. The larger share of seminal volume is produced by the seminal vesicles, which is why the larger percentages are incorrect.'),
('432e57e2-ecc2-56f6-8833-f8e02c3b2641'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulbourethral glands produce:', '["The seminal fluid resulting in decreased functional activity","The majority of the semen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small amount of a clear, mucus-like pre-ejaculatory fluid (lubricates the urethra)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small amount of a clear, mucus-like pre-ejaculatory fluid (lubricates the urethra)', 'The paired bulbourethral (Cowper) glands secrete a small volume of clear, viscous, mucus-like fluid that lubricates the penile urethra and appears before ejaculation. They contribute little to total semen volume, so options crediting them with the seminal fluid or the majority of the semen are wrong.'),
('443fccbc-55d7-5363-854f-aee6123da6fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The epididymis is about:', '["1 meter resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 meters mediated by specific receptor activation","6-7 meters long (if uncoiled)","10 meters leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6-7 meters long (if uncoiled)', 'The epididymis is a highly coiled tubule that, when straightened out, measures approximately 6-7 meters, yet it occupies only a modest space along the posterior border of the testis. The other listed lengths grossly underestimate its true coiled extent.'),
('1aa6cf04-d58d-5f65-9f84-8dc6035624ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The vas deferens is about:', '["10 cm","45 cm","50 cm","None","30-35 cm long"]'::jsonb, '30-35 cm long', 'The ductus (vas) deferens is a thick-walled muscular tube about 30-35 cm long that runs from the tail of the epididymis through the spermatic cord to the ampulla at the base of the prostate. The shorter and longer lengths given in the other options are outside the accepted textbook range.'),
('fe01b693-3672-5318-950d-c889e720be5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The vas deferens can be palpated as part of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The penis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The spermatic cord (above the testis)","The scrotum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spermatic cord (above the testis)', 'Within the scrotal portion of the spermatic cord, the vas deferens is the firmest and most readily palpable structure and is felt as a cordlike tube just above the testis. It forms part of the cord, rather than being found within the penis, scrotal wall, or the canal itself by palpation.'),
('360480e9-e020-5440-b24b-8584d12a9c55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A vasectomy involves:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Cutting the spermatic cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cutting and ligating the vas deferens (to prevent the release of sperm)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cutting the epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cutting and ligating the vas deferens (to prevent the release of sperm)', 'Vasectomy achieves sterility by cutting and ligating the ductus deferens, blocking the transport of sperm from the epididymis while leaving testicular hormone production intact. It does not involve division of the whole spermatic cord or the epididymis.'),
('99196965-7220-5576-879b-c4c1e7d4ee2c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Vasectomy is a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Permanent form of male contraception (does not affect testosterone production or libido)","Temporary form of male contraception without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Procedure for treating infertility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Permanent form of male contraception (does not affect testosterone production or libido)', 'Vasectomy is a permanent male contraceptive method that interrupts sperm transport but preserves the Leydig cells, so testosterone secretion and libido remain unchanged. It is not a temporary contraceptive and is not used to treat infertility.'),
('f8ec5429-ee32-5bb7-adf6-ffc0e8df9548'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate can be examined by:', '["Scrotal examination resulting in decreased functional activity","Digital rectal examination (DRE) - the prostate can be palpated through the rectum","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Abdominal palpation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Digital rectal examination (DRE) - the prostate can be palpated through the rectum', 'The prostate lies immediately anterior to the rectum, so the examining finger can palpate its posterior surface through the rectal wall during digital rectal examination. Scrotal examination and abdominal palpation do not reach the prostate.'),
('51a46f49-7c37-51dd-ae64-0b28308e71a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminal vesicles can be palpated by:', '["Scrotal examination resulting in decreased functional activity","Digital rectal examination (they lie above the prostate, in front of the rectum)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Abdominal ultrasonography without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Digital rectal examination (they lie above the prostate, in front of the rectum)', 'The seminal vesicles lie between the bladder and the rectum, just above the prostate, so digital rectal examination is the route that can reach them. Scrotal examination and abdominal ultrasonography do not provide palpation of these glands.'),
('b913462b-4535-5e68-ab5f-b31e13c03a40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The Cowper (bulbourethral) glands can be palpated by:', '["Scrotal examination resulting in decreased functional activity","Digital rectal examination (they lie in the urogenital diaphragm)","Abdominal ultrasonography mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Digital rectal examination (they lie in the urogenital diaphragm)', 'The bulbourethral glands sit within the deep perineal pouch (urogenital diaphragm) beside the membranous urethra, related to the anterior rectal wall, so digital rectal examination is the clinical approach to this region. They are normally too small to be felt, and the other listed routes cannot reach them.'),
('0d287acf-84b7-5bd1-b53f-c20de6e3d0a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cremaster muscle is derived from:', '["The rectus abdominis","The internal oblique muscle","None","The transversus abdominis","The external oblique"]'::jsonb, 'The internal oblique muscle', 'The cremaster muscle consists of muscle fibre loops derived from the internal oblique muscle (with some contribution from its attachment to the inguinal ligament). It lies inside the spermatic cord alongside the testicular vessels and ductus deferens.'),
('2b3b8c9d-9153-503a-985d-b47d0e3e4e44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testicular artery is a branch of:', '["The abdominal aorta (at the level of L2)","None","The external iliac artery","The internal iliac artery","The renal artery"]'::jsonb, 'The abdominal aorta (at the level of L2)', 'The testicular artery arises directly from the anterior aspect of the abdominal aorta just below the renal arteries, near the L2 vertebral level. It is not a branch of the iliac or renal arteries.'),
('993e4d49-5e60-505b-acab-f0c5f15910f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testicular artery runs:', '["In the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity","In the inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","In the spermatic cord (to supply the testis and the epididymis)","In the femoral triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the spermatic cord (to supply the testis and the epididymis)', 'The testicular artery descends within the spermatic cord, through the inguinal canal, carrying blood to the testis and epididymis. It does not travel in the perineum or femoral triangle.'),
('b458508f-f404-5f6b-a18e-0bc1380875b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testicular vein drains:', '["Only the testis resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the epididymis mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The testis and the epididymis (the right testicular vein drains into the IVC; the left drains into the left renal vein)","The scrotum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The testis and the epididymis (the right testicular vein drains into the IVC; the left drains into the left renal vein)', 'The testicular veins drain the testis and epididymis via the pampiniform plexus and ascend in the spermatic cord. On the right the testicular vein joins the inferior vena cava, while on the left it drains at a right angle into the left renal vein.'),
('429c5c29-7a2a-5714-9003-42825d178678'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The pampiniform plexus:', '["Helps to cool the testicular blood (countercurrent heat exchanger)","Increases the temperature of the testicular blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is the same as the testicular artery mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Helps to cool the testicular blood (countercurrent heat exchanger)', 'The pampiniform plexus of veins surrounds the testicular artery and acts as a countercurrent heat exchanger, cooling the arterial blood before it reaches the testis. Keeping the testis about 2-3 C below core temperature is needed for spermatogenesis.'),
('0a0a154f-6979-51fb-83d2-c6f536700509'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A varicocele is:', '["A dilation of the pampiniform plexus of veins (usually on the left side)","A dilation of the vas deferens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A dilation of the testicular artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A dilation of the pampiniform plexus of veins (usually on the left side)', 'A varicocele is a tortuous dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus, much more common on the left because the left testicular vein enters the left renal vein at a right angle. The vas deferens and testicular artery are not involved.'),
('1091eeb4-b2de-55c2-9f23-6e6beb02a74c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Varicocele feels like:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A fluid-filled cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hard lump mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ''bag of worms'' in the scrotum"]'::jsonb, 'A ''bag of worms'' in the scrotum', 'The dilated, tortuous veins of a varicocele feel soft, compressible and irregular, classically described as a bag of worms on the affected side of the scrotum. A fluid-filled cyst or a hard lump describes other scrotal pathology.'),
('b5393bf4-001f-5285-9820-cce35b7887d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A hydrocele is:', '["A collection of blood in the scrotum resulting in decreased functional activity","A collection of pus in the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A collection of fluid in the tunica vaginalis (in the scrotum)"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of fluid in the tunica vaginalis (in the scrotum)', 'A hydrocele is an accumulation of serous fluid within the tunica vaginalis, the closed remnant of the processus vaginalis around the testis. Blood (hematocele) and pus (pyocele) in the sac are separate entities.'),
('9daa78fa-4367-517b-b231-9495cd0e3bcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Hydrocele transilluminates:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","(hydrocele is not translucent) mediated by specific receptor activation","Only if it is a large hydrocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Yes (hydrocele is fluid-filled and transilluminates)"]'::jsonb, 'Yes (hydrocele is fluid-filled and transilluminates)', 'Because a hydrocele is filled with clear serous fluid, it transmits light when a torch is placed behind the scrotum in a darkened room, so it transilluminates. Solid masses and haemorrhagic sacs do not transilluminate.'),
('61e7c61f-ba65-5c1e-8dd9-b811bdf5a9f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A spermatocele is:', '["A cyst of the epididymis (contains sperm)","A cyst of the testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cyst of the vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cyst of the epididymis (contains sperm)', 'A spermatocele is a retention cyst of the epididymis filled with sperm-containing fluid; it is a small, benign, cystic swelling separate from the testis. It arises from the epididymis, not the testis or vas deferens.'),
('cbdd5b5f-ee9b-5cf2-bd5c-4060cc23384c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular torsion is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A twisting of the epididymis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A twisting of the vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","A twisting of the spermatic cord that cuts off the blood supply to the testis (surgical emergency)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A twisting of the spermatic cord that cuts off the blood supply to the testis (surgical emergency)', 'Testicular torsion is twisting of the spermatic cord that occludes the testicular vessels, cutting off the blood supply to the testis. Viability is lost within hours, so immediate surgical detorsion and fixation are required.'),
('837d7208-050d-5527-b9af-c6aa33c2ca19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular torsion presents with:', '["Gradual onset of pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Painless swelling of the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sudden onset of severe scrotal pain, nausea, and vomiting (a surgical emergency)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden onset of severe scrotal pain, nausea, and vomiting (a surgical emergency)', 'The hallmark of testicular torsion is the sudden onset of severe scrotal pain accompanied by nausea and vomiting in a young patient. The abrupt onset, unlike infection or tumour, makes this an acute surgical emergency.'),
('bd95f4d0-0f4a-5453-83ed-ef262864abe6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Orchidopexy is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surgical procedure to fix an undescended testis in the scrotum","A surgical procedure to repair a varicocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A surgical procedure to remove a testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical procedure to fix an undescended testis in the scrotum', 'Orchidopexy is the surgical fixation of an undescended (cryptorchid) testis into the scrotum, ideally performed by 1-2 years of age to reduce risks of malignancy and infertility. Repair of a varicocele is a varicocelectomy, and excision of the testis is an orchiectomy.'),
('dc8db090-24e9-5d16-b74c-44e77409ee94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Orchiectomy is:', '["Surgical removal of a testis (for testicular cancer, severe trauma, or torsion)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgical removal of the epididymis mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgical removal of the vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical removal of a testis (for testicular cancer, severe trauma, or torsion)', 'Orchiectomy is the surgical removal of a testis, performed for testicular malignancy, severe trauma, or irreversible torsion. Removal of the epididymis or vas deferens is a different operation.'),
('1ed5c73b-31dc-57d2-9d15-9f6169a898f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular cancer is the most common:', '["Solid tumor in young men (15-35 years old)","Cancer in children accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Cancer in older men without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cancer in females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Solid tumor in young men (15-35 years old)', 'Testicular cancer is the most common solid malignancy in young men aged 15-35 years and is highly curable when detected early. It is uncommon in children, older men and females.'),
('2190b105-3229-528b-993c-7550112197ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The most common type of testicular cancer is:', '["Leydig cell tumor","None","Embryonal carcinoma","Teratoma","Seminoma (germ cell tumor)"]'::jsonb, 'Seminoma (germ cell tumor)', 'Seminoma is the most common germ cell tumour of the testis in young men and is exquisitely radiosensitive. Leydig cell tumours, embryonal carcinoma and teratoma are all rarer.'),
('13257339-e277-56ac-92da-5d315f497a81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular cancer often presents with:', '["A painless testicular mass or swelling","Scrotal pain and fever accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A painful testicular mass leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A painless testicular mass or swelling', 'Testicular cancer classically presents as a painless, hard, unilateral lump or diffuse swelling of the testis, so any new testicular mass must be investigated urgently. Pain with fever suggests epididymo-orchitis rather than tumour.'),
('a67d2d90-1570-5912-94c3-7f98e0abf0c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis should be examined by:', '["X-ray resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood tests accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testicular self-examination (monthly) and scrotal ultrasonography","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular self-examination (monthly) and scrotal ultrasonography', 'Testicular pathology is assessed by monthly testicular self-examination and then by scrotal ultrasonography, which distinguishes solid from cystic lesions. Plain X-ray and blood tests do not image the testis.'),
('25555ae4-ea41-5a1a-b9a3-554b634e2b7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The epididymis is located on:', '["The anterior border of the testis","The superior pole of the testis","None","The posterior and lateral border of the testis","The medial border of the testis"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior and lateral border of the testis', 'The epididymis, a long coiled duct, runs along the posterior and lateral border of the testis, with its head and tail overriding the superior and inferior poles. It is not located on the anterior or medial border.'),
('504114e1-98cb-5d9b-b331-67a5e7a26b3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The epididymis can be palpated as:', '["None","All","A smooth, round structure","A ridge on the posterior aspect of the testis","A small, round structure in the midline of the scrotum"]'::jsonb, 'A ridge on the posterior aspect of the testis', 'The epididymis is a firm, elongated structure applied to the back of the testis, so it is palpated as a ridge on the posterior aspect, with the smooth testis felt in front. This posterior ridge helps identify the testis during examination.'),
('02b18ef3-666e-58a4-8ae1-e3d561f3580c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Epididymitis is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Inflammation of the prostate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation of the epididymis (often caused by infection, e.g., chlamydia, gonorrhea, or E. coli)","Inflammation of the testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammation of the vas deferens leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the epididymis (often caused by infection, e.g., chlamydia, gonorrhea, or E. coli)', 'Epididymitis is inflammation of the epididymis, usually from retrograde spread of infection (chlamydia or gonorrhea in young men, E. coli in older men). Inflammation of the testis is orchitis, and of the prostate is prostatitis.'),
('225da181-1981-5250-9b91-916753d67b5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Orchitis is:', '["Inflammation of the prostate resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation of the vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation of the testis (often caused by mumps virus or a complication of epididymitis)","Inflammation of the epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the testis (often caused by mumps virus or a complication of epididymitis)', 'Orchitis is inflammation of the testis itself, classically caused by mumps virus after puberty or developing as a complication of epididymitis. It is distinct from epididymitis and prostatitis.'),
('5c277921-2dd4-545c-bac3-faf8fd4fd80a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate feels like:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A soft, boggy structure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A firm, smooth, rubbery structure (about the size of a walnut) on digital rectal examination","A hard, nodular structure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A firm, smooth, rubbery structure (about the size of a walnut) on digital rectal examination', 'On digital rectal examination the normal prostate feels smooth, rubbery and about the size of a walnut, with a palpable median groove posteriorly. Hard nodularity suggests carcinoma, and a boggy consistency suggests acute prostatitis.'),
('5a7dae8f-91b0-5274-a302-d22e941abc39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The seminal vesicles feel like:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth, round structures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hard nodules mediated by specific receptor activation","Soft, elongated structures above the prostate (on DRE, they are often not palpable unless enlarged)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Soft, elongated structures above the prostate (on DRE, they are often not palpable unless enlarged)', 'The seminal vesicles are soft, lobulated sacs lying above the prostate in front of the rectum; they are usually not felt on DRE unless diseased or enlarged. Hard nodules describe prostatic carcinoma, not the seminal vesicles.'),
('a5651fbb-5738-5c31-be1a-1013db14c98f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands are:', '["Two small, round glands in the urogenital diaphragm (they are not palpable unless enlarged)","Two glands in the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Two large glands in the prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two small, round glands in the urogenital diaphragm (they are not palpable unless enlarged)', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands are two small, pea-sized glands embedded within the urogenital diaphragm beside the membranous urethra; they are not palpable in health. They are neither scrotal nor intraprostatic structures.'),
('19cf0e91-eca5-5721-8d22-4c9a5e96034f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is surrounded by:', '["A muscular capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibrous capsule (the prostatic capsule)","A cartilaginous capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous capsule (the prostatic capsule)', 'The prostate is enveloped by a thin fibrous capsule that blends with the surrounding pelvic fascia, lying outside the glandular tissue itself. The capsule is neither muscular nor cartilaginous and is important in the spread of prostatic carcinoma.'),
('737c37cc-99fe-5ffc-949c-c19cc61cd331'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is covered by:', '["Only the prostatic venous plexus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostatic venous plexus (Santorini plexus) anteriorly and the Denonvilliers fascia (rectovesical septum) posteriorly","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Denonvilliers fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic venous plexus (Santorini plexus) anteriorly and the Denonvilliers fascia (rectovesical septum) posteriorly', 'Anteriorly the prostate is covered by the prostatic (Santorini) venous plexus, while posteriorly it is separated from the rectum by the Denonvilliers fascia (rectovesical septum). Both relations are surgically important during prostatectomy.'),
('e07c8b5d-bc8e-5705-9c6d-545842dc2d59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The Denonvilliers fascia (rectovesical septum) separates:', '["The prostate from the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostate from the seminal vesicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The prostate from the rectum (it is the fascia between the prostate and the ampulla of the rectum)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostate from the rectum (it is the fascia between the prostate and the ampulla of the rectum)', 'The Denonvilliers fascia (rectovesical septum) is a layer of pelvic fascia that separates the prostate from the rectum and provides the posterior dissection plane in prostatectomy. It does not separate the prostate from the bladder or seminal vesicles.'),
('580fe717-fbd7-5891-81de-a3901d3a2664'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'During a radical prostatectomy, it is important to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Preserve the neurovascular bundles of Walsh (which contain the cavernous nerves for erectile function)","Preserve the bladder neck mediated by specific receptor activation","Remove the entire prostate with the seminal vesicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preserve the neurovascular bundles of Walsh (which contain the cavernous nerves for erectile function)', 'The neurovascular bundles of Walsh carry the cavernous nerves that mediate erectile function, running posterolateral to the prostate; preserving them during radical prostatectomy reduces the risk of impotence. This nerve-sparing step is a key surgical goal.'),
('6b700bf6-2cf2-5b7d-8a88-c0c007cf1e56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The neurovascular bundles of Walsh run:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterolaterally to the prostate (between the layers of the lateral pelvic fascia)","Anteriorly to the prostate mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medially to the prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterolaterally to the prostate (between the layers of the lateral pelvic fascia)', 'The neurovascular bundles of Walsh run posterolaterally to the prostate, sandwiched between the layers of the lateral pelvic fascia and carrying the cavernous nerves and blood vessels. Their known position allows identification during nerve-sparing prostatectomy.'),
('336ff3dc-ce2f-5ce5-a393-631fe50fc164'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cavernous nerves are:', '["Branches of the sympathetic chain resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Branches of the pudendal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Branches of the pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic, S2-S4) that supply the erectile tissue of the penis"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of the pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic, S2-S4) that supply the erectile tissue of the penis', 'The cavernous nerves are parasympathetic branches of the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) that supply the erectile tissue of the corpora cavernosa and spongiosum. They do not arise from the sympathetic chain or the pudendal nerve.'),
('bcf1e2b7-60f7-501c-9667-747257b45ea3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cavernous (erector) nerves are also known as:', '["The pudendal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The nervi erigentes (they are responsible for penile erection)","The genitofemoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The nervi erigentes (they are responsible for penile erection)', 'The cavernous (erector) nerves are also called the nervi erigentes, the parasympathetic pelvic splanchnic fibres from S2-S4 that produce vasodilation of the erectile bodies. The pudendal, obturator and genitofemoral nerves are somatic.'),
('ee64df47-d3c0-5905-bbc9-6265d27ae314'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Injury to the cavernous nerves during prostate surgery can cause:', '["Urinary incontinence resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infertility mediated by specific receptor activation","Erectile dysfunction (impotence)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Erectile dysfunction (impotence)', 'Injury to the cavernous nerves interrupts the parasympathetic outflow needed for corporal vasodilation, so erectile dysfunction (impotence) follows prostate surgery. Urinary incontinence and infertility have separate causes.'),
('db8e9cf3-a5a0-557b-8c5e-08a3770ddfaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles assist:', '["In urination resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In achieving and maintaining an erection (by compressing the erectile tissue)","In ejaculation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In achieving and maintaining an erection (by compressing the erectile tissue)', 'The ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles compress the erectile bodies, raising intracavernous pressure, which achieves and maintains a rigid erection. Their secondary role is in ejaculation and voiding rather than urination alone.'),
('c1065827-2165-59b2-87d3-5814919957b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle is responsible for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The maintenance of erection mediated by specific receptor activation","Rapidly emptying the urethra (the last few drops of urine) and assisting in ejaculation","The production of semen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapidly emptying the urethra (the last few drops of urine) and assisting in ejaculation', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle compresses the bulb of the penis, expelling the last drops of urine from the urethra and propelling semen during ejaculation. It plays no part in semen production or the maintenance of erection.'),
('3c7882e7-7221-5085-aec6-60e382c7fc04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle also assists in:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The storage of urine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The propulsion of semen during ejaculation (by rhythmic contractions)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The production of sperm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The propulsion of semen during ejaculation (by rhythmic contractions)', 'During ejaculation the bulbospongiosus contracts rhythmically to propel semen along the penile urethra. The muscle does not store urine or produce sperm.'),
('f21a37d9-ff42-567a-b4ae-83e77aac2884'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dartos muscle is responsible for:', '["Covering the testis resulting in decreased functional activity","Lifting the testis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The wrinkling of the scrotal skin (regulating the temperature of the testes)"]'::jsonb, 'The wrinkling of the scrotal skin (regulating the temperature of the testes)', 'The dartos, a smooth muscle within the superficial fascia of the scrotum, contracts to wrinkle the scrotal skin and reduce heat loss, helping regulate testicular temperature. Lifting the testis is performed by the cremaster muscle.'),
('d3f8a2a8-2093-5f65-bd10-8c1d140b87a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The cremaster muscle is responsible for:', '["Wrinkling the scrotal skin resulting in decreased functional activity","Lifting the testis (to regulate its temperature and protect the testis)","Covering the testis mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lifting the testis (to regulate its temperature and protect the testis)', 'The cremaster, supplied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, contracts to lift the testis toward the superficial inguinal ring, protecting it and helping to control temperature. Wrinkling of the scrotal skin is the role of the dartos.'),
('a11d05d4-9ef9-5eb3-9561-9913fabf9d23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","This is why varicocele is more common on the left side (the right angle impedes drainage)","This is unrelated to varicocele without affecting the primary tissue pathway","This prevents varicocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'This is why varicocele is more common on the left side (the right angle impedes drainage)', 'The left testicular vein joins the left renal vein at a right angle, which impedes venous drainage and raises venous pressure in the pampiniform plexus. This explains why varicocele is far more common on the left side.'),
('0e631be8-af61-5cb8-8047-eddaaaa85a1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The right testicular vein drains into the IVC at an oblique angle:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","This impedes drainage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","This is unrelated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","This facilitates drainage (so varicocele is less common on the right)"]'::jsonb, 'This facilitates drainage (so varicocele is less common on the right)', 'The right testicular vein drains obliquely into the inferior vena cava, which allows freer drainage of venous blood, so varicocele is less common on the right. The impeded right-angle drainage on the left explains the side difference.'),
('ca04f652-5aa3-577b-8b48-66505ef53bc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A varicocele is often described as:', '["A fluid-filled sac resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A ''bag of worms'' in the scrotum (dilated pampiniform plexus of veins)","A hard, nodular mass leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ''bag of worms'' in the scrotum (dilated pampiniform plexus of veins)', 'The dilated pampiniform plexus of a varicocele forms a soft, compressible bag-of-worms mass in the scrotum. A fluid-filled sac describes hydrocele and a hard nodular mass suggests tumour.'),
('01e71f18-de72-5e12-be25-136e6a165faf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Varicocele is more common on the left side:', '["It is equal on both sides resulting in decreased functional activity","False accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is more common on the right mediated by specific receptor activation","True (due to the anatomical difference in the drainage of the left testicular vein)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'True (due to the anatomical difference in the drainage of the left testicular vein)', 'Varicocele is roughly four to ten times more common on the left because the left testicular vein joins the left renal vein at a right angle, hindering drainage. The statement is therefore true.'),
('0f6cc2c3-5df2-53fc-bdb4-51aa12fbf1eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A varicocele can cause:', '["Only testicular atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Only infertility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A dragging sensation in the scrotum, testicular atrophy, and infertility","Only a dragging sensation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A dragging sensation in the scrotum, testicular atrophy, and infertility', 'A varicocele may cause a dragging sensation in the scrotum, testicular atrophy and impaired fertility, because the rising venous pressure and scrotal temperature disturb spermatogenesis. These consequences justify surgical treatment when troublesome.'),
('15384555-df1b-5c4b-be97-a477c291347e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Spermatocele is usually:', '["A benign, asymptomatic cystic swelling above the testis (transilluminates)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A painful swelling mediated by specific receptor activation","A malignant tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A benign, asymptomatic cystic swelling above the testis (transilluminates)', 'A spermatocele is a benign, usually asymptomatic cystic swelling of the epididymis lying above the testis that, being fluid-filled, transilluminates. It is not painful and not a malignant tumour, so it is generally managed conservatively.'),
('c2cb37b9-f125-5315-babb-9405ee1f5fb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular torsion occurs when:', '["The epididymis twists resulting in decreased functional activity","The vas deferens twists accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The testis twists on the spermatic cord (cutting off the blood supply) - this is a surgical emergency","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The testis twists on the spermatic cord (cutting off the blood supply) - this is a surgical emergency', 'Testicular torsion occurs when the testis rotates on the spermatic cord, twisting the vessels and cutting off its blood supply, causing ischaemia and infarction. This is a true surgical emergency requiring prompt detorsion and orchidopexy.'),
('b1677ccd-e0bf-58ee-9c5f-c80c18825fca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular torsion is most common in:', '["Infants resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adolescent boys and young men (12-25 years old)","Elderly men without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Older men leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adolescent boys and young men (12-25 years old)', 'Testicular torsion is most common in adolescent boys and young men between about 12 and 25 years of age, frequently in those with a bell-clapper deformity. It is uncommon in infants and elderly men.'),
('e2d9cfd4-5b71-510b-a4b7-2b5bd54be46c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The ''bell-clapper'' deformity predisposes to:', '["Testicular torsion (the testis is not properly attached to the scrotal wall, allowing it to twist freely)","Varicocele accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hydrocele mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Inguinal hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular torsion (the testis is not properly attached to the scrotal wall, allowing it to twist freely)', 'The bell-clapper deformity lacks the normal posterior attachment of the testis to the scrotal wall, so the testis can rotate freely on its cord and predispose to torsion. This variant is unrelated to varicocele, hydrocele or inguinal hernia.'),
('7fd47f17-cd83-562c-86d9-72069c1b3f8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Orchidopexy is performed for:', '["Varicocele resulting in decreased functional activity","Testicular torsion (to untwist the testis and fix it to the scrotal wall)","Spermatocele mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hydrocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testicular torsion (to untwist the testis and fix it to the scrotal wall)', 'Orchidopexy fixes the testis to the scrotal wall, treating testicular torsion by restoring the twist and preventing recurrence. Varicocele, spermatocele, and hydrocele are managed by other operations such as varicocelectomy, excision, and hydrocelectomy.'),
('edf47b29-c64b-550c-bf1a-91da39f2eea7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Mumps orchitis is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An inflammation of the prostate mediated by specific receptor activation","An inflammation of the testis caused by the mumps virus (it can lead to testicular atrophy)","An inflammation of the epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An inflammation of the testis caused by the mumps virus (it can lead to testicular atrophy)', 'Mumps orchitis is viral inflammation of the testis caused by the mumps paramyxovirus, most often in postpubertal males, and may lead to testicular atrophy. The prostate and epididymis are not the primary sites affected.'),
('b69908b3-3a9c-5bb5-83cc-cbafb0d5626f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The crus of the penis is:', '["The same as the bulb of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the corpus spongiosum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The proximal part of the corpus cavernosum (attached to the ischiopubic ramus)"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal part of the corpus cavernosum (attached to the ischiopubic ramus)', 'Each crus is the fixed proximal end of a corpus cavernosum, anchored to the ischiopubic ramus. The bulb, by contrast, is the proximal expansion of the corpus spongiosum, so the two structures are distinct.'),
('f59bacf2-632a-5ff9-b180-c42358cc2990'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The tunica albuginea of the penis is:', '["A layer of smooth muscle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A thick layer of fibrous tissue that surrounds each corpus cavernosum and the corpus spongiosum (helps maintain an erection)","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A thin layer of fibrous tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thick layer of fibrous tissue that surrounds each corpus cavernosum and the corpus spongiosum (helps maintain an erection)', 'The tunica albuginea is a thick, inelastic fibrous sheath around the corpora cavernosa and the corpus spongiosum; its rigidity compresses the draining veins and helps sustain an erection. It is neither smooth muscle nor a thin layer.'),
('beebc4d3-978d-5bce-b62e-3d47460e6f6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The septum of the penis is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thick wall between the corpora cavernosa mediated by specific receptor activation","A wall that separates the corpus spongiosum from the corpora cavernosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A thin, midline septum in the corpus spongiosum (divides the two corpora cavernosa partially)"]'::jsonb, 'A thin, midline septum in the corpus spongiosum (divides the two corpora cavernosa partially)', 'The septum pectiniforme is a thin midline ingrowth of the tunica albuginea that only partially divides the two corpora cavernosa. It is thin and incomplete, not a thick wall, and does not separate the corpus spongiosum from the corpora cavernosa.'),
('1f3f024c-ae13-51b6-b054-fc1f515263ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dorsal veins of the penis drain:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only to the prostatic venous plexus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superficial dorsal vein (to the great saphenous vein) and the deep dorsal vein (to the prostatic venous plexus)","To the IVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only to the great saphenous vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superficial dorsal vein (to the great saphenous vein) and the deep dorsal vein (to the prostatic venous plexus)', 'The superficial dorsal vein runs subcutaneously and drains via the external pudendal vein to the great saphenous vein, whereas the deep dorsal vein passes deep to the fascia and drains into the prostatic venous plexus. The two veins therefore follow different routes, not a single plexus or the IVC.'),
('672e5b36-fe16-5cdf-8596-ae46f8a532b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The deep dorsal vein of the penis is:', '["A small vein resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main venous drainage of the penis (drains into the prostatic venous plexus, then into the internal iliac vein)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the superficial dorsal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main venous drainage of the penis (drains into the prostatic venous plexus, then into the internal iliac vein)', 'The deep dorsal vein is the principal venous channel of the penis, passing between the corpora cavernosa to drain into the prostatic (vesical) venous plexus and ultimately the internal iliac vein. It is separate from the smaller superficial dorsal vein.'),
('7988769e-8b24-5e8c-9737-19cade0c8b5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The superficial dorsal vein of the penis is:', '["A vein that drains into the internal iliac vein resulting in decreased functional activity","A vein in the subcutaneous tissue that drains into the great saphenous vein (via the external pudendal vein)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A vein that drains into the femoral vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A vein in the subcutaneous tissue that drains into the great saphenous vein (via the external pudendal vein)', 'The superficial dorsal vein lies in the subcutaneous tissue on the dorsum of the penis and drains into the great saphenous vein by way of the external pudendal vein. It does not empty into the internal iliac or femoral vein.'),
('cd5ee900-dea5-593b-b5d6-e738f04e6a00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dorsal artery of the penis is:', '["A branch of the femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","A branch of the testicular artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A branch of the internal pudendal artery (runs along the dorsum of the penis, supplying the glans and the skin)"]'::jsonb, 'A branch of the internal pudendal artery (runs along the dorsum of the penis, supplying the glans and the skin)', 'The paired dorsal arteries arise from the internal pudendal artery and run on the dorsum beneath the deep fascia to supply the glans and skin of the penis. They are not branches of the femoral or testicular arteries.'),
('d620a652-8326-55e3-90b7-89d568c0ff2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The deep artery of the penis (cavernous artery) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A branch of the femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A branch of the internal pudendal artery (supplies the corpora cavernosa and is essential for erection)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A branch of the testicular artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A branch of the internal pudendal artery (supplies the corpora cavernosa and is essential for erection)', 'The deep (cavernous) arteries arise from the internal pudendal artery and fill the sinusoidal spaces of the corpora cavernosa to produce erection. The femoral and testicular arteries do not give these branches.'),
('a29f084f-23e7-59f0-90ce-3ab594f9d74b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis is:', '["A branch of the ilioinguinal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","A branch of the genitofemoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A branch of the femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","A branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) that supplies the sensory innervation to the penis","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) that supplies the sensory innervation to the penis', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis is the terminal sensory branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4), supplying most of the penis including the glans. The ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and femoral nerves do not provide this supply.'),
('b5ca45d9-fee2-5530-a4a6-7b47b2474c04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis is responsible for:', '["Motor function of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood supply to the penis mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensation in the penis (it can be blocked for a penile block anesthesia)"]'::jsonb, 'Sensation in the penis (it can be blocked for a penile block anesthesia)', 'The dorsal nerve conveys sensory innervation of the penis, so blocking it at the base produces penile anesthesia for procedures such as circumcision. It neither supplies blood nor drives motor function of the penis.'),
('30450221-f7c4-54b9-a0de-356d0008feaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A penile block is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An epidural anesthetic injection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A local anesthetic injection at the base of the penis (to block the dorsal nerves of the penis) - used for circumcision or other penile procedures","A spinal anesthetic injection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A local anesthetic injection at the base of the penis (to block the dorsal nerves of the penis) - used for circumcision or other penile procedures', 'A penile block is a local anesthetic field infiltration at the base of the penis that anesthetizes the dorsal penile nerves, commonly used for circumcision. It is not a spinal or epidural anesthetic technique.'),
('e93b65ee-3bc3-5e49-ab4f-227345f0c4ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Peyronie disease is:', '["A tumor of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","An infection of the penis without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A condition where fibrous scar tissue (plaques) forms in the tunica albuginea of the penis, causing a curvature during an erection"]'::jsonb, 'A condition where fibrous scar tissue (plaques) forms in the tunica albuginea of the penis, causing a curvature during an erection', 'Peyronie''s disease is marked by fibrous scar plaques within the tunica albuginea that produce penile curvature and discomfort during erection. It is a fibrotic condition, not a tumor or an infection of the penis.'),
('8f2829ae-570c-53e2-aa68-4c03c8dacf3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Priapism is:', '["A loss of erection resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A prolonged, painful erection that is not related to sexual stimulation (it is a medical emergency, can be caused by sickle cell disease, trauma, or medications)","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A normal erection without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A prolonged, painful erection that is not related to sexual stimulation (it is a medical emergency, can be caused by sickle cell disease, trauma, or medications)', 'Priapism is a persistent erection unrelated to sexual desire, often painful, and constitutes a urological emergency with causes such as sickle cell disease, trauma, or drugs. It is the opposite of a loss of erection and is never normal.'),
('346fd62f-b1a2-5ba9-8b71-2bdfb13482d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Circumcision is:', '["The surgical removal of the prepuce (foreskin)","The surgical removal of the glans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The surgical removal of a testis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The surgical removal of the prepuce (foreskin)', 'Circumcision removes the prepuce (foreskin), the fold of skin over the glans. It does not involve the glans or the testis, which are removed in very different procedures.'),
('24a87477-5f35-5c87-bdd7-061087351cdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testes develop in the:', '["Pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdominal cavity (in the lumbar region, near the mesonephros)","Scrotum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thorax leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal cavity (in the lumbar region, near the mesonephros)', 'The testes differentiate retroperitoneally in the abdominal cavity at the lumbar level, adjacent to the mesonephros, and later descend into the scrotum. They never develop in the pelvis, thorax, or scrotum.'),
('f2290f22-1636-5b28-82e5-484c81e4836c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular descent is divided into:', '["Three phases resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two phases: the transabdominal phase (from the abdominal wall to the deep inguinal ring) and the inguinoscrotal phase (through the inguinal canal to the scrotum)","Four phases without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only one phase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two phases: the transabdominal phase (from the abdominal wall to the deep inguinal ring) and the inguinoscrotal phase (through the inguinal canal to the scrotum)', 'Descent of the testis proceeds in two stages: the transabdominal phase brings it to the deep inguinal ring and the inguinoscrotal phase carries it through the inguinal canal into the scrotum. This division, not three or four phases, describes normal descent.'),
('cb42a1ab-dc21-58a8-b9f2-7169caf7f38e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The transabdominal phase of testicular descent occurs during:', '["Week 8-12 of fetal development (the gubernaculum guides the testis to the deep inguinal ring)","Week 12-16 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 16-20 mediated by specific receptor activation","Week 20-24 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 8-12 of fetal development (the gubernaculum guides the testis to the deep inguinal ring)', 'The transabdominal phase of descent occurs at about weeks 8-12, when the gubernaculum guides the testis toward the deep inguinal ring. This is an early event, well before the later weeks listed.'),
('3d536c06-cdea-5061-bbaa-719c30779f2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The inguinoscrotal phase of testicular descent occurs during:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Week 12-16 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Week 28-40 of fetal development (the testis passes through the inguinal canal into the scrotum)","Week 16-20 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Week 8-12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Week 28-40 of fetal development (the testis passes through the inguinal canal into the scrotum)', 'The inguinoscrotal phase runs from about weeks 28-40, when the testis travels through the inguinal canal into the scrotum late in gestation. It is the final stage of descent and occurs well after the transabdominal phase.'),
('a7ade43e-d56e-5737-89f3-30a58b093100'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The gubernaculum is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the spermatic cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fibromuscular cord that attaches the testis to the scrotum (it guides the testis during its descent)"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular cord that attaches the testis to the scrotum (it guides the testis during its descent)', 'The gubernaculum is a fibromuscular cord connecting the lower pole of the testis to the developing scrotum; it guides descent through the inguinal canal. It is a distinct embryonic structure, not the spermatic cord or vas deferens.'),
('14ff6dbd-d9c9-5ef5-80ae-6abf2ee75065'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The gubernaculum becomes the:', '["The spermatic cord resulting in decreased functional activity","The vas deferens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The epididymis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scrotal ligament (the attachment of the testis to the scrotal wall)"]'::jsonb, 'Scrotal ligament (the attachment of the testis to the scrotal wall)', 'After descent, the gubernaculum atrophies and persists as the scrotal ligament that anchors the testis to the scrotal wall. It does not transform into the spermatic cord, vas deferens, or epididymis.'),
('03eff167-7077-513f-91a3-8a4fdef28848'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The processus vaginalis obliterates to form:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The tunica vaginalis testis (a serous sac around the testis)","The spermatic cord mediated by specific receptor activation","The scrotal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tunica vaginalis testis (a serous sac around the testis)', 'The processus vaginalis is a peritoneal evagination whose distal portion persists as the tunica vaginalis, a serous sac around the testis, while the proximal part obliterates. It does not give rise to the spermatic cord or scrotal ligament.'),
('c48a0ff9-d096-54ca-a739-1d83dee948de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A congenital inguinal hernia in a child is:', '["A hernia due to a patent processus vaginalis (the peritoneum is open into the scrotum)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hernia due to a weakness in the abdominal wall (a direct hernia) mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hernia of the obturator canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A hernia due to a patent processus vaginalis (the peritoneum is open into the scrotum)', 'A congenital inguinal hernia in a child is an indirect hernia caused by a patent processus vaginalis, which keeps a peritoneal sac open through the inguinal canal. It is not a direct hernia from abdominal wall weakness nor an obturator hernia.'),
('3c6ee547-d895-5311-a4aa-8d3bc27a2a64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A patent processus vaginalis is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","An opening of the peritoneum into the abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An opening of the peritoneum into the scrotum that fails to close (can lead to a congenital inguinal hernia or a hydrocele)","A closed passage in the scrotum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An opening of the peritoneum into the scrotum that fails to close (can lead to a congenital inguinal hernia or a hydrocele)', 'A patent processus vaginalis is persistence of the peritoneal canal into the scrotum, which predisposes to a congenital inguinal hernia or a communicating hydrocele. It is an open, not a closed, passage and opens toward the scrotum.'),
('504bc9e8-11eb-5435-8681-0a1137755dd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis and the spermatic cord are located:', '["In the pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","In the inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","In the scrotum (the testis is suspended by the spermatic cord)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the scrotum (the testis is suspended by the spermatic cord)', 'In the descended state the testis lies in the scrotum, suspended by the spermatic cord. Although the cord passes through the inguinal canal, the resting position of the testis and cord is the scrotum.'),
('1adb4f95-035a-55c8-bbc7-98ffd3e17aae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotal septum is:', '["A ligament from the scrotum to the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity","A muscle of the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The median wall that divides the scrotum into two compartments","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The median wall that divides the scrotum into two compartments', 'The scrotal septum is the median fibromuscular partition, formed by infolded dartos muscle and fascia, that separates the scrotum into two compartments, each housing a testis. It is not a ligament or a surrounding muscle.'),
('792db24f-2cd2-5557-9317-1cc463dd3747'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The dartos muscle and the scrotal septum receive their blood supply from:', '["The testicular artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The external pudendal artery (and the internal pudendal artery)","The renal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inferior epigastric artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external pudendal artery (and the internal pudendal artery)', 'The scrotal wall, including the dartos muscle and scrotal septum, is supplied by the external pudendal arteries with a contribution from the internal pudendal artery. The testicular artery instead supplies the testis and epididymis.'),
('16a02441-cb24-5c99-9801-d69d3fdf2b3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotal skin is:', '["Thinner and more pigmented than the skin of the rest of the body (with abundant sebaceous glands and hair follicles)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same thickness mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thicker than the skin of the rest of the body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thinner and more pigmented than the skin of the rest of the body (with abundant sebaceous glands and hair follicles)', 'Scrotal skin is thin, deeply pigmented, and rich in sebaceous glands and hair follicles, and it carries the characteristic median raphe. It is neither the same thickness nor thicker than skin elsewhere.'),
('7441c150-d100-5e43-954c-ae05ed62db86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum has a:', '["Both layers resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial (dartos) layer of smooth muscle (which wrinkles the skin) and a deeper (cremasteric) layer of skeletal muscle (which lifts the testis)","Only a cremasteric layer mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only a dartos layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial (dartos) layer of smooth muscle (which wrinkles the skin) and a deeper (cremasteric) layer of skeletal muscle (which lifts the testis)', 'The scrotum contains a superficial layer of dartos smooth muscle that wrinkles the skin and a deeper layer of cremaster skeletal muscle that lifts the testis. Descriptions naming only one of the two layers are incomplete.'),
('f62b9e6e-a8b8-5adb-82a4-46e22523e85e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is an important structure for:', '["Sperm production resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Temperature regulation of the testis (the dartos muscle and the cremaster muscle adjust the position of the scrotum to maintain an optimal temperature)","Ejaculation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Temperature regulation of the testis (the dartos muscle and the cremaster muscle adjust the position of the scrotum to maintain an optimal temperature)', 'The scrotum maintains the testis at a temperature about 2-3°C below core body temperature by contracting or relaxing the dartos and cremaster muscles. It regulates temperature but does not itself produce sperm, ejaculate, or pass urine.'),
('5d707e26-4d26-5b8f-927d-11d6811be869'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum can contract in response to:', '["Cold (the dartos and cremaster muscles contract to pull the testis closer to the body to warm up)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","external stimuli mediated by specific receptor activation","Heat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cold (the dartos and cremaster muscles contract to pull the testis closer to the body to warm up)', 'In the cold, the dartos and cremaster muscles contract, drawing the scrotum and testis closer to the body to conserve heat. Heat produces the opposite response, relaxation and lowering of the scrotum.'),
('45182089-7310-5a8f-bc4c-ccdd153007d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum can relax in response to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Heat (the dartos muscle relaxes, the scrotum hangs lower, and the testis moves away from the body to cool down)","Only infection mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","external stimuli without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cold leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heat (the dartos muscle relaxes, the scrotum hangs lower, and the testis moves away from the body to cool down)', 'In response to heat, the dartos muscle relaxes so the scrotum hangs lower and the testis moves away from the body, promoting cooling. Cold, not heat, makes the scrotum contract.'),
('4a0da37b-9f0a-54f7-a657-c5c3f6ca90dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The testis needs a temperature that is:', '["2-3 degrees Celsius below the core body temperature (for optimal spermatogenesis)","The same as the core body temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Higher than the core body temperature mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Much lower than the core body temperature leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2-3 degrees Celsius below the core body temperature (for optimal spermatogenesis)', 'Spermatogenesis proceeds optimally at about 2-3°C below core body temperature, a state maintained by the scrotal thermoregulatory mechanisms. Temperatures equal to, above, or far below the core impair sperm production.'),
('18cc7cc3-3700-51f5-9801-8ce099e2e273'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The pampiniform plexus helps to:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increase the temperature of the testis mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Warm the testicular blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cool the testicular blood (countercurrent heat exchange between the warm testicular artery and the cooler testicular veins)"]'::jsonb, 'Cool the testicular blood (countercurrent heat exchange between the warm testicular artery and the cooler testicular veins)', 'The pampiniform plexus of veins surrounds the testicular artery and acts as a countercurrent heat exchanger, cooling arterial blood before it reaches the testis. It thus lowers testicular temperature rather than warming the blood.'),
('45806b64-caaa-5517-bddc-7c7550f6e68d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is suspended from the:', '["Pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","Thigh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Perineum (the root of the scrotum is attached to the perineum)","Abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perineum (the root of the scrotum is attached to the perineum)', 'The scrotum hangs from the perineum, where its root is attached posteriorly. It is not suspended from the pelvis, abdomen, or thigh.'),
('c12772c1-4f27-54c9-aeec-f2fbd0322281'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is a:', '["Pouch of bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Pouch of skin that contains the testes, parts of the spermatic cords, and the epididymides","Pouch of muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pouch of skin that contains the testes, parts of the spermatic cords, and the epididymides', 'The scrotum is a pouch of skin and superficial fascia that encloses the testes, the distal parts of the spermatic cords, and the epididymides. It is not a pouch made of muscle or bone.'),
('97aa1660-4234-503c-bbcf-034293c87713'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord is:', '["The same as the vas deferens resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the testicular artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A bundle of structures that suspends the testis in the scrotum (runs through the inguinal canal)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A bundle of structures that suspends the testis in the scrotum (runs through the inguinal canal)', 'The spermatic cord is a composite bundle containing the vas deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, lymphatics, nerves, and coverings, running through the inguinal canal to suspend the testis. It is not identical to any single component such as the vas deferens alone.'),
('ebdb7d6d-a4e5-59e3-876d-3eef8d36e352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord is about:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","7 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","10 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","4-5 cm long (from the deep inguinal ring to the testis)","2 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4-5 cm long (from the deep inguinal ring to the testis)', 'The spermatic cord measures about 4-5 cm from the deep inguinal ring to the testis. It is distinctly shorter than 7-10 cm and longer than 2 cm.'),
('236a558a-b7c1-584e-9ec7-f86a9dbaa998'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord originates at the:', '["Testis resulting in decreased functional activity","Superficial inguinal ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Scrotal wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep inguinal ring (where the structures that make up the cord converge)"]'::jsonb, 'Deep inguinal ring (where the structures that make up the cord converge)', 'The spermatic cord begins at the deep inguinal ring, where its constituent structures converge, and ends at the testis. It does not originate at the testis, superficial ring, or scrotal wall.'),
('0ec1d9f4-cb6f-5e60-affa-6795fdc8a3c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord terminates at the:', '["Deep inguinal ring resulting in decreased functional activity","Scrotal wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Testis (the cord is attached to the testis and the epididymis)","Superficial inguinal ring leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Testis (the cord is attached to the testis and the epididymis)', 'The spermatic cord ends at the testis, where its components attach to the testis and epididymis. Its upper end is at the deep inguinal ring, not the superficial ring or scrotal wall.'),
('4df15367-23fc-5273-862f-faa4fb6972c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The structures of the spermatic cord (in order from the deep inguinal ring to the testis) are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the fasciae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the contents mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the cremaster muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, cremaster muscle, internal spermatic fascia, and the contents (vas, vessels, nerves)"]'::jsonb, 'The external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, cremaster muscle, internal spermatic fascia, and the contents (vas, vessels, nerves)', 'From superficial to deep the cord has the external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia enclosing the cremaster muscle, internal spermatic fascia, and the contents (vas deferens, vessels, and nerves). The coverings plus contents, not any single layer alone, form the cord.'),
('2220bc89-3d9e-5be9-a1a0-49b0e35ec4da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The spermatic cord can be palpated:', '["In the upper part of the scrotum (above the testis, as a firm, cord-like structure)","The spermatic cord cannot be palpated accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inside the testis mediated by specific receptor activation","Below the testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the upper part of the scrotum (above the testis, as a firm, cord-like structure)', 'The spermatic cord is felt as a firm, cord-like structure in the upper scrotum above the testis. It is not palpable inside or below the testis.'),
('45cba4d4-5055-5f45-b88c-482478d93a80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The vas deferens feels like:', '["A firm, smooth, cord-like structure (like a thick piece of string) within the spermatic cord","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hollow tube mediated by specific receptor activation","A hard, nodular structure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A soft, spongy structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A firm, smooth, cord-like structure (like a thick piece of string) within the spermatic cord', 'Because of its thick muscular wall, the vas deferens is palpable as a firm, smooth, cord-like structure within the spermatic cord. It does not feel soft, spongy, hollow, or hard and nodular.'),
('cf444268-d97a-54f0-874d-ec362312395a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Varicocele most commonly affects the:', '["Right side resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both sides equally mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Left side (the left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle, which can impede the flow of blood)","Only the right side leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left side (the left testicular vein drains into the left renal vein at a right angle, which can impede the flow of blood)', 'Varicocele is greatly more common on the left because the left testicular vein enters the left renal vein at a right angle, hindering venous drainage. The right testicular vein drains more directly into the inferior vena cava.'),
('6f7b0f2b-aadb-5476-8fe6-fcd6eff641af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A primary varicocele (idiopathic) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A varicocele that is caused by a secondary process accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A varicocele that is related to a renal tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A varicocele that occurs in the absence of any obvious cause (usually in young men, often on the left side)"]'::jsonb, 'A varicocele that occurs in the absence of any obvious cause (usually in young men, often on the left side)', 'A primary (idiopathic) varicocele develops without an identifiable cause, typically in young men and usually on the left. Varicoceles produced by an obstructive or compressive lesion are the secondary form.'),
('6d00435d-e4a8-5fab-86d9-bc39ec890b73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A secondary varicocele is:', '["A varicocele that is caused by a secondary process (e.g., a renal tumor or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy that compresses the testicular vein)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A varicocele that occurs in the absence of any cause mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as a primary varicocele leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A varicocele that is caused by a secondary process (e.g., a renal tumor or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy that compresses the testicular vein)', 'A secondary varicocele results from an identifiable obstructive process, such as a renal tumor or retroperitoneal lymphadenopathy compressing the testicular vein. It differs from the idiopathic primary form.'),
('8cc54a2d-88dd-5b42-957b-08b107890c82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A secondary varicocele can be caused by:', '["A testicular tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","An epididymal cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A renal tumor (especially a left renal tumor that obstructs the left testicular vein)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A renal tumor (especially a left renal tumor that obstructs the left testicular vein)', 'A renal tumor, particularly on the left, can invade or compress the left renal vein and thus obstruct the left testicular vein, producing a secondary varicocele. Testicular tumors and epididymal cysts do not compress the testicular vein in this way.'),
('e7715920-da9b-5fbc-b77b-0711f7aa27c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A secondary varicocele is often:', '["Bilateral resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Left-sided accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not associated with any cause mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Right-sided (or it occurs in an older patient) - a right-sided varicocele should always raise the suspicion of a secondary cause","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right-sided (or it occurs in an older patient) - a right-sided varicocele should always raise the suspicion of a secondary cause', 'A secondary varicocele is typically right-sided or appears in an older patient, and a right-sided varicocele should always prompt a search for an underlying retroperitoneal cause. By contrast, a primary varicocele is usually left-sided in young men.'),
('dd3b5ec5-2b0c-56ca-b318-d6c40c23f46d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A testicular tumor can present with:', '["A painless testicular mass or swelling (the testis may feel hard and irregular)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A painful testicular mass mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scrotal pain and fever leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A painless testicular mass or swelling (the testis may feel hard and irregular)', 'Testicular tumors typically present as a painless, hard, and irregular testicular mass or swelling. A painful mass with fever strongly suggests infection such as epididymo-orchitis rather than a tumor.'),
('d8217ec8-03ea-5499-8285-4b84fa3a8815'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Testicular cancer is most commonly staged with:', '["A CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis (to look for para-aortic lymph node metastases)","An Ultrasound of the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood tests only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An Abdominal X-ray leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis (to look for para-aortic lymph node metastases)', 'CT of the abdomen and pelvis is the standard imaging for staging testicular cancer because the primary lymphatic drainage of the testis follows the testicular vessels to the para-aortic (retroperitoneal) lymph nodes. Scrotal ultrasound is used for diagnosis of the primary mass, not staging; blood tests and plain X-rays do not assess nodal spread.'),
('2ff90d9a-39c4-5968-919d-c58c793eabf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Tumor markers for testicular cancer include:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","CA 19-9 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CA-125 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CEA without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), beta-human chorionic gonadotropin (beta-hCG), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)', 'AFP, beta-hCG, and LDH are the established serum tumor markers used to diagnose and monitor testicular germ cell tumors. CA 19-9, CA-125, and CEA are markers of pancreatic, ovarian, and colorectal/other carcinomas respectively and are not used for testicular cancer.'),
('cfb90749-629c-5e94-b31c-66e6c58d7435'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Seminoma is associated with:', '["Elevated CEA resulting in decreased functional activity","Elevated beta-hCG (but not AFP)","Elevated AFP mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated beta-hCG (but not AFP)', 'Seminomas are composed of pure germ cells that may secrete beta-hCG (particularly with syncytiotrophoblastic cells) but never produce AFP; an elevated AFP indicates a non-seminomatous component. CEA is not a testicular germ cell tumor marker.'),
('8f0fadfa-7075-5581-baef-daf6aa36545e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Non-seminomatous germ cell tumors (NSGCT) are associated with:', '["None","Elevated AFP and beta-hCG","Only elevated AFP","Only elevated beta-hCG","tumor markers"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated AFP and beta-hCG', 'NSGCT (embryonal carcinoma, yolk sac tumor, choriocarcinoma, teratoma) frequently elevate both AFP and beta-hCG. AFP is produced by yolk sac elements and beta-hCG by trophoblastic elements, so both markers are typically raised together in NSGCT.'),
('2ab83a45-401b-55f6-b3bc-1b4ea04a91e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The treatment for testicular cancer depends on:', '["Only the size of the tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the age of the patient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The stage of the disease (surgery (orchiectomy), chemotherapy, and/or radiation therapy)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The stage of the disease (surgery (orchiectomy), chemotherapy, and/or radiation therapy)', 'Management of testicular cancer is guided primarily by stage: radical orchiectomy for the primary tumor, with chemotherapy and/or radiation therapy added for nodal or metastatic disease. Tumor size and patient age alone do not determine the treatment plan.'),
('7a7b4d6a-a88d-598f-b900-389c319c64c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A radical orchiectomy is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The surgical removal of the testis (and the spermatic cord) through an inguinal incision (not through the scrotum)","The surgical removal of the epididymis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The surgical removal of the testis through the scrotum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The surgical removal of the testis (and the spermatic cord) through an inguinal incision (not through the scrotum)', 'Radical orchiectomy removes the testis along with the spermatic cord through an inguinal approach, avoiding scrotal violation that could alter lymphatic drainage and allow tumor spread. Removing only the epididymis or the testis via the scrotum is not the radical procedure.'),
('4342b2e0-0e4e-56f1-9679-abd2d2dac8ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A testicular prosthesis can be:', '["Used to treat testicular cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Used to treat testicular torsion mediated by specific receptor activation","Inserted into the scrotum (for cosmetic reasons) after an orchiectomy","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inserted into the scrotum (for cosmetic reasons) after an orchiectomy', 'A testicular prosthesis is a cosmetic silicone implant placed in the scrotum after orchiectomy to restore appearance. It has no therapeutic role in treating testicular cancer or torsion.'),
('5cfd1534-7709-5a29-ae53-bcdf8e17c3dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The scrotum is a common site for:', '["Prostate cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Testicular cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scrotal infections (e.g., Fournier gangrene - a necrotizing fasciitis of the external genitalia)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scrotal infections (e.g., Fournier gangrene - a necrotizing fasciitis of the external genitalia)', 'The scrotum is prone to infections, and Fournier gangrene is a rapidly progressive necrotizing fasciitis that typically starts in the scrotum and perineum. Prostate and testicular cancer do not arise in the scrotal wall itself.'),
('21a22f68-3fb2-57e3-9f54-bc1eca8fa7d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Fournier gangrene is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A slow-growing infection mediated by specific receptor activation","A benign condition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A rapidly progressive, life-threatening, necrotizing infection of the external genitalia and perineum"]'::jsonb, 'A rapidly progressive, life-threatening, necrotizing infection of the external genitalia and perineum', 'Fournier gangrene is a surgical emergency: a polymicrobial necrotizing fasciitis of the external genitalia and perineum that spreads rapidly along fascial planes and is often fatal without urgent debridement. It is neither slow-growing nor benign.'),
('b3f0f2c5-e45e-5de3-bd37-3296a76f2db9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is a fibromuscular organ that secretes:', '["Testosterone resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sperm mediated by specific receptor activation","Mucus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A thin, milky fluid that contains acid phosphatase, PSA, citric acid, and proteolytic enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'A thin, milky fluid that contains acid phosphatase, PSA, citric acid, and proteolytic enzymes', 'Prostatic secretion is a thin, milky fluid rich in acid phosphatase, prostate-specific antigen (PSA), citric acid, and proteolytic enzymes that help liquefy semen. Testosterone is made by the testes, sperm by the seminiferous tubules, and mucus by the bulbourethral glands.'),
('c92e4651-e2c4-5078-b3d0-41cf843d4875'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostate is composed of:', '["Only smooth muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Only glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","30-50 branched tubuloalveolar glands embedded in a fibromuscular stroma","Only connective tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '30-50 branched tubuloalveolar glands embedded in a fibromuscular stroma', 'Histologically the prostate consists of 30-50 branched tubuloalveolar glands embedded within a fibromuscular stroma, hence it is called a fibromuscular glandular organ. It is not composed solely of smooth muscle, glands, or connective tissue.'),
('aab8f3cf-2649-5715-b0c8-4fed199de8b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostatic concretions (corpora amylacea) are:', '["Small, round, calcified bodies that can be found in the prostatic ducts (they are usually asymptomatic)","Abnormal growths in the prostate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tiny stones in the prostatic urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small, round, calcified bodies that can be found in the prostatic ducts (they are usually asymptomatic)', 'Corpora amylacea are small, round, laminated concretions composed of glycoprotein and calcium salts found within prostatic ducts and acini; they are normal age-related findings and are usually asymptomatic. They are not tumors or stones in the urethra.'),
('25ae40c4-dbb7-5239-8fcc-1c08d685539c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The Cowper (bulbourethral) glands are:', '["Two glands in the seminal vesicle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two glands in the prostate mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Two small, round glands in the urogenital diaphragm (they secrete a clear, mucus-like pre-ejaculatory fluid to lubricate the urethra)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two small, round glands in the urogenital diaphragm (they secrete a clear, mucus-like pre-ejaculatory fluid to lubricate the urethra)', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands are two small, round glands located in the urogenital diaphragm whose ducts open into the spongy urethra; they secrete a clear, mucus-like pre-ejaculatory fluid that lubricates the urethra. They do not lie within the seminal vesicle or prostate.'),
('d311634e-89be-54c8-9987-841cdb8ee2ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands are homologous to:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The Skene glands in the female accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The seminal vesicles mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands in the female"]'::jsonb, 'The Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands in the female', 'The bulbourethral glands are embryologically homologous to the Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands of the female, both developing from the urogenital sinus region. The Skene glands are homologous to the prostate, and the seminal vesicles have no female counterpart.'),
('4b1666e3-6877-5a85-bc67-dc1fed8b135b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is a:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Cylindrical organ of copulation (it contains the urethra and the three erectile bodies of the corpora cavernosa and the corpus spongiosum)","A flat organ for reproduction mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cylindrical organ for urination only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cylindrical organ of copulation (it contains the urethra and the three erectile bodies of the corpora cavernosa and the corpus spongiosum)', 'The penis is a cylindrical organ of copulation made up of three erectile bodies: two corpora cavernosa and one corpus spongiosum, which contains the urethra. Its functions include both copulation (delivery of semen) and urination, so it is not limited to urination only.'),
('95708ff6-f887-5a3b-b477-f679c95085ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penis is divided into:', '["Only the root and the body resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the root and the glans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the body and the glans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The root (the attached part), the body (the free shaft), and the glans (the distal expansion of the corpus spongiosum)"]'::jsonb, 'The root (the attached part), the body (the free shaft), and the glans (the distal expansion of the corpus spongiosum)', 'Anatomically the penis has three parts: the root (attached to the perineum), the body (the free shaft), and the glans (the distal expansion of the corpus spongiosum). Any answer listing only two parts omits an essential division.'),
('797cdfd2-4c12-5a42-a3ae-5345718cc05f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The root of the penis (the attached part) is made up of:', '["The two crura (attached to the ischiopubic rami) and the bulb (attached to the perineal membrane)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The two crura only mediated by specific receptor activation","The bulb only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The glans only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The two crura (attached to the ischiopubic rami) and the bulb (attached to the perineal membrane)', 'The root of the penis comprises the two crura, which are the posterior ends of the corpora cavernosa attached to the ischiopubic rami, and the bulb, the posterior expansion of the corpus spongiosum attached to the perineal membrane. The glans belongs to the distal part, not the root.'),
('9f5a0bd4-1139-545d-87a2-9ec4af7f9e1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The body of the penis (the free shaft) is made up of:', '["The corpus spongiosum only resulting in decreased functional activity","All three erectile bodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The two corpora cavernosa only mediated by specific receptor activation","The two corpora cavernosa (dorsolateral) and the corpus spongiosum (ventromedial, containing the urethra)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The two corpora cavernosa (dorsolateral) and the corpus spongiosum (ventromedial, containing the urethra)', 'The body of the penis contains the two corpora cavernosa situated dorsolaterally and the corpus spongiosum situated ventromedially, which encloses the spongy urethra. Saying just one or two erectile bodies is incomplete.'),
('12d6bc4f-7fbf-5df5-a718-8c5185407e46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The glans penis is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the prepuce mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the corpus spongiosum without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The distal expansion of the corpus spongiosum (covers the tips of the corpora cavernosa, the urethra opens at the tip of the glans)"]'::jsonb, 'The distal expansion of the corpus spongiosum (covers the tips of the corpora cavernosa, the urethra opens at the tip of the glans)', 'The glans is the cone-shaped distal expansion of the corpus spongiosum that caps the tips of the corpora cavernosa; the external urethral orifice opens at its tip. It is not the whole corpus spongiosum, nor is it the same as the prepuce (foreskin).'),
('647260ef-5128-516e-ae18-82e5d1674208'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The corona of the penis is:', '["The prepuce resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The flare at the base of the glans (the glans is separated from the shaft by the corona)","The fremulum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tip of the glans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The flare at the base of the glans (the glans is separated from the shaft by the corona)', 'The corona is the prominent rounded flare at the base of the glans that marks the boundary between the glans and the shaft of the penis. The frenulum is a ventral fold of skin and the prepuce is the covering foreskin, while the tip carries the external urethral orifice.'),
('d442f9bc-2eb9-5792-b9f7-9218d6fc05b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The frenulum of the prepuce is:', '["The same as the prepuce resulting in decreased functional activity","A fold on the dorsal surface of the glans accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small fold of skin on the ventral surface of the glans (between the glans and the prepuce)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small fold of skin on the ventral surface of the glans (between the glans and the prepuce)', 'The frenulum of the prepuce is a small midline fold of skin on the ventral surface of the glans that connects the prepuce (foreskin) to the glans. It is not located on the dorsal surface and it is not the prepuce itself.'),
('5d50458c-ce8f-58a4-93a3-9aa6f5a4fd01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penile raphe is:', '["The ventral midline seam of the penis (it is continuous with the scrotal raphe)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The dorsal midline of the penis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lateral side of the penis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ventral midline seam of the penis (it is continuous with the scrotal raphe)', 'The penile raphe is the ventral midline seam formed by fusion of the genital folds during development, and it continues as the scrotal raphe and perineal raphe. It lies on the ventral surface, not the dorsum or the lateral side.'),
('a6e668fa-da81-580a-a2db-2391ad873291'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The urethral opening at the tip of the glans is called:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The internal urethral orifice accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The external urethral orifice (the narrowest point in the male urethra)","The fossa navicularis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external urethral orifice (the narrowest point in the male urethra)', 'The opening at the tip of the glans is the external urethral orifice (urinary meatus), the narrowest point of the male urethra, formed at the distal end of the fossa navicularis. The internal urethral orifice is at the bladder neck, and the fossa navicularis is the dilated penile urethra just proximal to the meatus.'),
('67d42dd1-7e0a-52c3-ba5a-d3e948693161'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra runs in the:', '["Spongiosum and cavernosum resulting in decreased functional activity","Corpus spongiosum (from the bladder to the external urethral orifice)","Corpus cavernosum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corpus spongiosum (from the bladder to the external urethral orifice)', 'The spongy (penile) part of the male urethra runs through the corpus spongiosum, which opens at the external urethral orifice. It does not pass through the corpora cavernosa, which are solid erectile bodies.'),
('177b1db9-5a61-5aa2-814f-646b666ad557'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The urinary meatus is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the fossa navicularis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external opening of the urethra (on the tip of the glans of the penis)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal opening of the urethra (at the bladder neck) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external opening of the urethra (on the tip of the glans of the penis)', 'The urinary meatus is the external urethral orifice located at the tip of the glans; it is the external opening of the urinary tract. The fossa navicularis is the dilated portion of the penile urethra proximal to the meatus, not the opening itself, and the vesical orifice is internal.'),
('f792d59d-4921-51a1-be37-95848136b7bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra is about:', '["18-20 cm long (from the bladder neck to the external urethral orifice)","10-12 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","25-30 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","5-7 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '18-20 cm long (from the bladder neck to the external urethral orifice)', 'The adult male urethra measures approximately 18-20 cm, extending from the internal urethral orifice at the bladder neck to the external urethral orifice at the tip of the glans. The other lengths are either too short or too long for the male urethra.'),
('197db733-3e30-55cb-a3f7-691ebdfedb23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra is the longest part of the:', '["Male reproductive tract resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Male lymphatic system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Male urinary tract (it is about 18-20 cm long and is divided into three parts)"]'::jsonb, 'Male urinary tract (it is about 18-20 cm long and is divided into three parts)', 'At 18-20 cm, the male urethra is the longest segment of the male urinary tract and is divided into prostatic, membranous, and spongy (penile) parts. It belongs to the urinary system, not the lymphatic system.'),
('10cd6701-7494-50ac-a027-ce5045eb8048'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The prostatic urethra is about:', '["5 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","6 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","3-4 cm long and is the widest and most distensible part of the male urethra","1 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm long and is the widest and most distensible part of the male urethra', 'The prostatic urethra is about 3-4 cm long, passes through the prostate, and is the widest and most distensible portion of the male urethra. The other numeric choices do not reflect its correct length.'),
('a515a4ce-a545-5f5c-9f61-aca91f6d1fc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The membranous urethra is about:', '["2 cm long and is the narrowest and least distensible part of the male urethra","4 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","1 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2 cm long and is the narrowest and least distensible part of the male urethra', 'The membranous urethra is the short 2 cm segment passing through the urogenital diaphragm between the prostatic and spongy parts; it is the narrowest, least distensible, and most vulnerable part of the male urethra. The other numeric options give incorrect lengths.'),
('18beddc7-e01a-50dc-9488-0360978a789e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The penile (spongy) urethra is about:', '["5 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","3 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","10 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","15 cm long (the longest part of the male urethra, running through the corpus spongiosum)"]'::jsonb, '15 cm long (the longest part of the male urethra, running through the corpus spongiosum)', 'The penile (spongy) urethra is about 15 cm long and runs through the corpus spongiosum, making it the longest of the three parts of the male urethra. The shorter numeric options underestimate it.'),
('79edf221-7ce8-5434-9997-875717a26a28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra has a double curve:', '["The infrapubic (perineal) curve (which is fixed) and the prepubic (penile) curve (which is mobile)","curves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the infrapubic curve mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the prepubic curve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The infrapubic (perineal) curve (which is fixed) and the prepubic (penile) curve (which is mobile)', 'In the flaccid penis the male urethra shows two curves: a fixed infrapubic (perineal) curve and a mobile prepubic (penile) curve. Lifting the penis straightens the mobile penile curve, leaving only the fixed infrapubic curve, so a single curve answer is incomplete.'),
('7433f268-6d5d-5acd-8649-998af6b4a5aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra can be catheterized:', '["Only with a curved catheter resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","By passing a catheter through the urethra into the bladder (a straight catheter is used, and the penis is lifted to straighten the infrapubic curve)","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Without any special maneuver without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'By passing a catheter through the urethra into the bladder (a straight catheter is used, and the penis is lifted to straighten the infrapubic curve)', 'Male catheterization uses a straight catheter passed along the urethra into the bladder; lifting the penis straightens the mobile prepubic curve so that only the fixed infrapubic curve must be traversed. A curved catheter is not routinely required, and some positioning is usually necessary.'),
('3dd2d2ab-1d0c-51d1-888e-9a3b7de0697d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'The male urethra can be injured by:', '["Only a penetrating injury resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A straddle injury (to the bulbar urethra), a pelvic fracture (to the membranous urethra), or a penetrating injury","Only a pelvic fracture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only a straddle injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A straddle injury (to the bulbar urethra), a pelvic fracture (to the membranous urethra), or a penetrating injury', 'Straddle injuries typically rupture the bulbar (spongy) urethra against the pubis, pelvic fractures classically tear the membranous urethra at the urogenital diaphragm, and penetrating trauma can injure any part. Limiting the cause to a single mechanism ignores the other common routes of injury.'),
('5024e5c6-0425-5917-aa66-e4bf3cd0dc7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A straddle injury (rupture of the bulbar urethra) causes:', '["extravasation resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Extravasation of urine into the perineum (under Colles fascia, the urine is prevented from spreading into the thigh or down the leg)","Extravasation into the pelvic cavity mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Extravasation into the abdomen without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extravasation of urine into the perineum (under Colles fascia, the urine is prevented from spreading into the thigh or down the leg)', 'Rupture of the bulbar urethra allows urine to extravasate into the superficial perineal pouch beneath Colles fascia; because Colles fascia fuses with the fascia lata at the thigh, urine cannot spread into the thigh or down the leg. It does not track into the pelvic cavity or abdomen.'),
('01ebfcd8-2212-5802-bead-eb74a470ee3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A straddle injury (rupture of the bulbar urethra) causes extravasation of urine into the:', '["The perineum (under Colles fascia, which is the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia, preventing spread into the thigh)","The abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perineum (under Colles fascia, which is the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia, preventing spread into the thigh)', 'Extravasated urine from a bulbar urethral rupture collects in the superficial perineal pouch beneath Colles fascia, the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia. Fused Colles fascia prevents extension into the thigh, so spread into the thigh, abdomen, or pelvis does not occur.'),
('8cfac0e8-00ca-55fa-97ee-b0595e09b5a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A pelvic fracture (rupture of the membranous urethra) causes extravasation of urine into the:', '["The pelvis (the true pelvis, above the pelvic diaphragm)","The abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The perineum (under Colles fascia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvis (the true pelvis, above the pelvic diaphragm)', 'Rupture of the membranous urethra lies above the urogenital diaphragm, so urine extravasates into the true pelvis above the pelvic diaphragm rather than into the perineum or abdomen. The other locations do not match the anatomic plane of injury.'),
('f6269f24-55b8-5e12-accf-fe36577ff7f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'A straddle injury to the penile urethra can cause:', '["Extravasation into the pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","extravasation mediated by specific receptor activation","Extravasation of urine into the shaft of the penis (under the fascia of the penis (Buck fascia))","Extravisation into the abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extravasation of urine into the shaft of the penis (under the fascia of the penis (Buck fascia))', 'Injury to the penile (spongy) urethra distal to the bulb causes urine to extravasate into the shaft of the penis beneath Buck fascia, the deep fascia of the penis, which confines the spread. It does not reach the pelvis or abdomen.'),
('3c535f76-5b3f-55ce-8210-e2fd450d39db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'In Male reproductive organs, which statement best fits the following fact: the testis is located in the?', '["Scrotum (outside the body cavity for temperature regulation)","Abdominal cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Pelvic cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scrotum (outside the body cavity for temperature regulation)', 'The testes are housed in the scrotum, outside the body cavity, where the temperature is about 2-3 degrees Celsius below core body temperature, a condition required for normal spermatogenesis. The abdominal or pelvic cavities and the inguinal canal do not provide this cooler environment.'),
('31de042b-3053-5ca5-b245-91ab968b2e97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Regarding Male reproductive organs, what is the correct answer to: the testis is covered by which layer from outside to inside?', '["Skin, dartos, cremasteric, internal spermatic, external spermatic resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin, internal spermatic, cremasteric, external spermatic, tunica vaginalis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skin, external oblique, internal oblique, transversus abdominis mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin, dartos, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica vaginalis (parietal and visceral), tunica albuginea"]'::jsonb, 'Skin, dartos, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia, internal spermatic fascia, tunica vaginalis (parietal and visceral), tunica albuginea', 'From outside inward, the scrotal wall and coverings of the testis are: skin, dartos muscle, external spermatic fascia, cremasteric fascia and cremaster muscle, internal spermatic fascia, parietal and visceral layers of tunica vaginalis, and the tunica albuginea of the testis itself. Options that mix or omit the fascial layers or add abdominal wall muscles are incorrect.'),
('c43c2570-c227-540b-93a5-f2dff582cbbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'For Male reproductive organs, which option is most appropriate: the testicular descent into the scrotum normally occurs?', '["During the first year of life resulting in decreased functional activity","During the 3rd month accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At birth mediated by specific receptor activation","At puberty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","During the 7th-8th month of fetal development"]'::jsonb, 'During the 7th-8th month of fetal development', 'The testes normally descend from the abdomen into the scrotum during the 7th-8th month of fetal life, guided by the gubernaculum and influenced by androgens. Descent during the 3rd month, at birth, or at puberty does not match the usual timing of testicular migration.'),
('e3033239-fe4f-500f-8481-76d6fc11ed8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Which answer is correct in Male reproductive organs when asked: the vas deferens is the continuation of?', '["The seminal vesicle","The rete testis","The seminiferous tubules","The efferent ductules","The tail of the epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'The tail of the epididymis', 'The ductus (vas) deferens begins as the continuation of the tail of the epididymis and carries sperm from the epididymis toward the ejaculatory duct. The rete testis and efferent ductules lie at the head end of the sperm pathway, and the seminal vesicle joins the vas deferens later, not its origin.'),
('6a16cfd0-2928-5c32-bc33-58e0a9a14933'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'In Male reproductive organs, the best response to ''The vas deferens ends by joining'' is:', '["The bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The prostate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The urethra directly mediated by specific receptor activation","The duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct","The epididymis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct', 'The terminal ampulla of the vas deferens joins the duct of the seminal vesicle to form the ejaculatory duct, which then opens into the prostatic urethra. It does not end by joining the bladder, the epididymis, or the urethra directly.'),
('fff2cbe1-d899-5735-99a8-67aafd9d6038'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'During revision of Male reproductive organs, which of the following is correct: the seminal vesicles lie?', '["In the scrotum","Posterior to the bladder, above the prostate","Anterior to the bladder","In the inguinal canal","Within the prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the bladder, above the prostate', 'The seminal vesicles are paired sacs lying posterior to the bladder and superior to the prostate, between the bladder and the rectum. They are retrovesical, so they are not anterior to the bladder, within the prostate, or located in the scrotum or inguinal canal.'),
('25e928d7-9480-58f9-937c-05e62fbdfcc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'In Male reproductive organs, which statement best fits the following fact: the seminal vesicles produce?', '["Most of the seminal fluid (fructose-rich, alkaline fluid)","Spermatozoa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testosterone mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prostatic fluid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most of the seminal fluid (fructose-rich, alkaline fluid)', 'The seminal vesicles secrete about 60-70% of the seminal fluid, an alkaline, fructose-rich secretion that nourishes sperm and provides energy. Spermatozoa are produced in the seminiferous tubules and testosterone by the Leydig cells, not by the seminal vesicles.'),
('0cce41a9-1d17-5555-b647-8ac33f811e91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Regarding Male reproductive organs, what is the correct answer to: the prostate gland has which zones?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Only lateral and medial lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral, central, transitional, and anterior fibromuscular zones","Only the isthmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only peripheral and central zones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral, central, transitional, and anterior fibromuscular zones', 'Modern classification divides the prostate into four zones: peripheral, central, transitional, and anterior fibromuscular. The transitional zone is where benign prostatic hyperplasia arises and the peripheral zone is the most common site of carcinoma, so answers naming only one or two zones are incomplete.'),
('dd3ebb77-7208-5972-bb93-f5af275d4f3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'For Male reproductive organs, which option is most appropriate: the prostate is related anteriorly to?', '["Urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic symphysis and retropubic space","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis and retropubic space', 'Anteriorly the prostate is related to the pubic symphysis and the retropubic space (space of Retzius). The rectum lies posteriorly, the bladder lies above it, and the urethra passes through its substance, so none of those are the anterior relation.'),
('93da931d-f152-5816-9a56-33f653f969b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'Which answer is correct in Male reproductive organs when asked: the prostate is related posteriorly to?', '["Bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectum (separated by rectovesical septum/Denonvilliers fascia)","Pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum (separated by rectovesical septum/Denonvilliers fascia)', 'The posterior surface of the prostate is related to the rectum and is separated from it by the rectovesical septum (Denonvilliers fascia); this is why the prostate is palpable on digital rectal examination. The bladder is superior, the pubic symphysis is anterior, and the urethra runs through the gland.'),
('41af7839-6050-5eff-9ebc-2b8c9757e77d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'In Male reproductive organs, the best response to ''The prostate is related superiorly to'' is:', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic symphysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Bladder neck and seminal vesicles","Urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder neck and seminal vesicles', 'The base of the prostate is fused superiorly with the neck of the bladder, and the seminal vesicles lie on its posterosuperior aspect. The rectum is posterior, the pubic symphysis is anterior, and the urethra traverses the gland rather than relating to its superior surface.'),
('41bcf3ec-6b12-58d9-8bf3-172fbc33b748'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'During revision of Male reproductive organs, which of the following is correct: the prostate is related inferiorly to?', '["Bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubic symphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urogenital diaphragm (external urethral sphincter area)"]'::jsonb, 'Urogenital diaphragm (external urethral sphincter area)', 'The apex of the prostate is related inferiorly to the urogenital diaphragm, in which the external urethral sphincter surrounds the membranous urethra just below the gland. The bladder is superior, the rectum is posterior, and the pubic symphysis is anterior.'),
('4a9b3884-36b6-587f-a6ee-b5c2d45f1159'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Male Reproductive Organs', 'In Male reproductive organs, which statement best fits the following fact: the prostatic urethra is?', '["The part of the male urethra that passes through the prostate gland","The longest part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The part in the perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","The part in the penis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the male urethra that passes through the prostate gland', 'The prostatic urethra is the segment of the male urethra that traverses the prostate, from the bladder neck to the membranous urethra, and carries the openings of the ejaculatory ducts. It is not the longest part (that is the spongy urethra) nor does it lie in the perineum or penis.'),
('27d9886e-1d93-5f34-b934-ef64fd1e9112'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lateral pelvic wall contains which muscles?', '["Piriformis and obturator internus","Levator ani and coccygeus","Psoas major and quadratus lumborum","Gluteus maximus and medius","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis and obturator internus', 'Obturator internus lines the lateral wall of the true pelvis and piriformis passes over the posterolateral wall, together forming the muscular lateral pelvic wall. Levator ani and coccygeus instead contribute to the pelvic floor, while psoas major and quadratus lumborum belong to the posterior abdominal wall.'),
('e46ce781-f40c-5b4c-a4c3-c4deef32ddb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The piriformis muscle functions as?', '["Lateral rotator of the thigh at the hip","Medial rotator of the thigh","Flexor of the thigh","Extensor of the thigh","Adductor of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral rotator of the thigh at the hip', 'With the hip extended, piriformis acts as a lateral rotator; with the hip flexed it assists abduction. It does not medially rotate, flex, extend, or adduct the thigh.'),
('94e4688a-810e-5244-915e-05d3e4f656df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator internus exits the pelvis through?', '["Inguinal canal","Obturator canal","Greater sciatic foramen","Femoral canal","Lesser sciatic foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser sciatic foramen', 'The obturator internus tendon turns around the ischium and leaves the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen to reach its insertion. The obturator canal transmits the obturator nerve and vessels, not this muscle.'),
('e4352e40-fa35-56c8-8ce9-ac6e1166ccab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator internus inserts into?', '["Lesser trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Greater trochanter (medial surface, in front of piriformis)","Pubic bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater trochanter (medial surface, in front of piriformis)', 'After exiting the lesser sciatic foramen, the obturator internus tendon attaches to the medial surface of the greater trochanter just in front of the piriformis insertion. The lesser trochanter receives iliopsoas, and the ischial tuberosity is a site of origin for the hamstrings.'),
('a186cba3-baa7-5f23-a606-541e50100a11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The levator ani muscle is part of?', '["Urogenital diaphragm","Abdominal wall","None of the above","Posterior abdominal wall","Pelvic diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic diaphragm', 'Levator ani and coccygeus together form the pelvic diaphragm, the muscular sheet that closes the pelvic outlet. The urogenital diaphragm is a separate perineal structure lying superficial to the pelvic diaphragm.'),
('48c33992-9dbb-5869-8dfb-4caca55f8ea7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The levator ani is formed by which parts?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Coccygeus and piriformis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubococcygeus and ischiococcygeus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubococcygeus (puboprostatic/pubovaginal), puborectalis, iliococcygeus","Only pubococcygeus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubococcygeus (puboprostatic/pubovaginal), puborectalis, iliococcygeus', 'Levator ani consists of three named components: pubococcygeus (with its puboprostatic/pubovaginal fibers), puborectalis, and iliococcygeus. Coccygeus is a separate pelvic-diaphragm muscle, and piriformis belongs to the pelvic wall.'),
('96cf2cb7-383e-57f4-9bf1-86604a16ad7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The coccygeus muscle is?', '["Part of the urogenital diaphragm","Part of the gluteal region","A muscle of the lateral pelvic wall","None of the above","Part of the pelvic diaphragm (posterior to levator ani)"]'::jsonb, 'Part of the pelvic diaphragm (posterior to levator ani)', 'Coccygeus spans from the ischial spine to the sacrum/coccyx and completes the pelvic diaphragm behind levator ani. The urogenital diaphragm lies more anteriorly, and coccygeus has no attachment to the lateral wall muscles.'),
('4b9d5a71-ef83-5db1-b780-de3025ce4f85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic diaphragm is formed by?', '["None of the above","Obturator internus and piriformis","Only levator ani","Only coccygeus","Levator ani and coccygeus muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Levator ani and coccygeus muscles', 'The pelvic diaphragm is the composite of levator ani and coccygeus that supports the pelvic viscera. Obturator internus and piriformis form the lateral wall and do not participate in the pelvic floor.'),
('c5aae7eb-201d-5cef-baa9-647a1eaaa35a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal iliac artery divides into?', '["Superior and inferior divisions","Left and right branches","Anterior and posterior divisions","Medial and lateral divisions","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior divisions', 'At the level of the greater sciatic foramen the internal iliac artery splits into an anterior (visceral and perineal) division and a posterior (iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal) division.'),
('fba37371-c3d3-51a1-98a0-3570cf390bf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The anterior division of the internal iliac artery gives rise to?', '["Only gluteal arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior vesical, inferior vesical, obturator, internal pudendal, inferior gluteal, middle rectal, uterine, vaginal","Only visceral branches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior vesical, inferior vesical, obturator, internal pudendal, inferior gluteal, middle rectal, uterine, vaginal', 'The anterior division supplies both visceral and parietal pelvic branches, including the vesical, obturator, internal pudendal, inferior gluteal, middle rectal, uterine, and vaginal arteries. Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal are posterior-division branches.'),
('f37472aa-e603-54d1-8f83-78f0f011de3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The posterior division of the internal iliac artery gives rise to?', '["None of the above","All visceral branches","Obturator and superior vesical","Internal pudendal and inferior gluteal","Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal arteries', 'The posterior division is parietal in distribution, typically giving the iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arteries. Visceral branches, obturator, internal pudendal, and inferior gluteal arise from the anterior division.'),
('2fe25045-7449-56c4-a008-0adc01846ac3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior gluteal artery is a branch of?', '["Posterior division of internal iliac artery","External iliac artery","Femoral artery","Common iliac artery","Anterior division of internal iliac"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior division of internal iliac artery', 'The superior gluteal artery is the largest branch of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery and exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen above piriformis. The external iliac continues as the femoral artery and gives no gluteal branch.'),
('3125d07b-0efa-5aa9-9e82-fb60e58b5633'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior gluteal artery is a branch of?', '["Common iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External iliac mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior division of internal iliac artery","Posterior division of internal iliac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior division of internal iliac artery', 'The inferior gluteal artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis. It is not derived from the common, external iliac, or femoral arteries.'),
('8b63cd05-d06c-5de2-8d7c-2f48a93e0a59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal pudendal artery is a branch of?', '["External iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","Common iliac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior division mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior division of internal iliac artery","Femoral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior division of internal iliac artery', 'The internal pudendal artery, the blood supply of the perineum, branches from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and traverses the greater then lesser sciatic foramina. External iliac and femoral arteries do not supply the perineum.'),
('f5ad1940-7b76-5501-ad43-9f51a44e6842'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator artery is usually a branch of?', '["Common iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior division of internal iliac artery","Femoral mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior division without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External iliac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior division of internal iliac artery', 'In typical anatomy the obturator artery arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and runs through the obturator canal. An aberrant obturator artery occasionally arises from the inferior epigastric artery, but the standard origin is the anterior division.'),
('83fc9aa1-68a4-5095-aec5-d0f868332aea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacral plexus lies on the?', '["Lateral pelvic wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior to the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvic floor mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior pelvic wall (anterior to piriformis)"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior pelvic wall (anterior to piriformis)', 'The sacral plexus is formed on the posterior pelvic wall, lying directly on piriformis and covered anteriorly by pelvic fascia and vessels. It is not a lateral wall, pelvic floor, or sacral surface structure.'),
('732a6ee1-61fa-592c-bcb5-b22fea4bba21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) is a branch of?', '["Coccygeal plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral plexus', 'The pudendal nerve is a mixed somatic nerve derived from S2-S4 roots of the sacral plexus. Pelvic splanchnic nerves are visceral efferents from the same segments, and the lumbar plexus lies far too cranial to contribute.'),
('f54935e6-0b9e-5d08-adb4-c90de166143c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pudendal nerve passes through?', '["Greater sciatic foramen (enters perineum via lesser sciatic foramen)","Obturator canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lesser sciatic foramen directly mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater sciatic foramen (enters perineum via lesser sciatic foramen)', 'The pudendal nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen between piriformis and coccygeus, then hooks around the ischial spine and re-enters the perineum via the lesser sciatic foramen. The obturator and inguinal canals transmit unrelated structures.'),
('c2b96d75-a7da-5307-b64b-926a7aa94db3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pudendal nerve supplies?', '["Only the anal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Most of the perineum (somatic motor and sensory), external anal sphincter, external urethral sphincter","Only the urethral sphincter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most of the perineum (somatic motor and sensory), external anal sphincter, external urethral sphincter', 'The pudendal nerve carries somatic motor and sensory fibers to the greater part of the perineum, including the external anal and external urethral sphincters and the perineal skin. Its distribution is far broader than any single sphincter or skin region.'),
('09b5ac0a-5711-5c12-a16d-6d9dfa4595ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic fascia includes?', '["Parietal pelvic fascia (covers pelvic walls and floor) and visceral pelvic fascia (covers pelvic organs)","Rectovesical septum only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the parietal fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the endopelvic fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal pelvic fascia (covers pelvic walls and floor) and visceral pelvic fascia (covers pelvic organs)', 'Pelvic fascia is subdivided into a parietal layer lining the pelvic walls and floor and a visceral layer that ensheaths the pelvic viscera. The rectovesical septum is only one condensation of visceral fascia, not the entire fascial system.'),
('2249b39e-062c-5597-99af-30ccaea57080'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The tendinous arch of the levator ani is?', '["A tendon of the levator ani resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of the sacrotuberous ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thickening of the obturator internus fascia from which the levator ani originates","Part of the inguinal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickening of the obturator internus fascia from which the levator ani originates', 'The tendinous arch is a linear thickening of the fascia over obturator internus that runs from the pubis to the ischial spine and gives origin to levator ani. It is a fascial condensation, not a true tendon or a ligament.'),
('82a430d8-eaf1-5a74-9d43-f2aa9c1bf440'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator canal transmits?', '["Femoral nerve","None of the above","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve and vessels","Pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve and vessels', 'The obturator canal, formed in the obturator membrane, transmits the obturator artery, vein, and nerve from the pelvis to the medial thigh. The femoral, sciatic, and pudendal nerves take entirely different routes.'),
('96598cf2-f6b2-598c-873e-dbe978fb9e21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior vesical artery supplies?', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper part of the urinary bladder (and distal ureter)","Prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper part of the urinary bladder (and distal ureter)', 'The superior vesical artery, a remnant of the fetal umbilical artery, supplies the upper part of the bladder and the distal ureter. The rectum, uterus, and prostate receive their blood from other internal iliac branches.'),
('dcc57597-ea71-535d-be86-94e94afbe60d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior vesical artery supplies?', '["Scrotum resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower part of the bladder, prostate (in males), seminal vesicles","Rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower part of the bladder, prostate (in males), seminal vesicles', 'In males the inferior vesical artery supplies the fundus of the bladder, the prostate, and the seminal vesicles; in females it is largely replaced by the vaginal artery. The scrotum is supplied by the internal pudendal system, and the uterus by the uterine artery.'),
('7ffd121f-7e9f-5230-8d98-77109e289e04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The middle rectal artery supplies?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Anal canal only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower rectum and anastomoses with superior and inferior rectal arteries","Upper rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sigmoid colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower rectum and anastomoses with superior and inferior rectal arteries', 'The middle rectal artery supplies the lower rectum and anastomoses with the superior (inferior mesenteric) and inferior rectal (internal pudendal) arteries. The upper rectum is supplied by the superior rectal artery and the sigmoid colon by sigmoid branches.'),
('2357dc5d-7def-50c6-9bd8-8bd1df727e5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The vaginal artery (in females) corresponds to which male artery?', '["Obturator artery","Internal pudendal artery","None of the above","Inferior vesical artery","Superior vesical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior vesical artery', 'The vaginal artery is considered the female homologue of the inferior vesical artery, supplying the vagina and adjacent lower bladder. Superior vesical and obturator arteries have distinct distributions unrelated to the vagina.'),
('87535e6d-16c0-55fb-a962-fdb29e0b0fd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The uterine artery runs in which ligament?', '["Round ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardinal ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Broad ligament (transversely at the base, crossing above the ureter)","Suspensory ligament of ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Broad ligament (transversely at the base, crossing above the ureter)', 'The uterine artery crosses the pelvic floor in the base of the broad ligament, where it runs above the ureter to reach the lateral border of the cervix (''water under the bridge''). The round ligament carries the artery of the round ligament, and the ovarian vessels course in the suspensory ligament.'),
('7788f573-21db-51a9-b29a-09b17e6ac4f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The ureter is crossed by the uterine artery?', '["Below the ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial to the ureter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to the ureter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Above the ureter at the level of the cervix (water under the bridge)"]'::jsonb, 'Above the ureter at the level of the cervix (water under the bridge)', 'At the lateral fornix of the cervix the uterine artery arches above the ureter (''water under the bridge''), a key landmark for ligation during hysterectomy. The ureter is thus crossed superiorly, not inferiorly or medially.'),
('a71bdad7-2639-5dfa-ab22-7b44b34df7f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior gluteal nerve (L4-S1) supplies?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Piriformis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, tensor fascia lata","Gluteus maximus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obturator internus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, tensor fascia lata', 'The superior gluteal nerve innervates gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia lata; its paralysis produces a Trendelenburg gait. Gluteus maximus is served by the inferior gluteal nerve, and piriformis and obturator internus by their own named branches.'),
('0afb96de-c967-5969-9ba8-526a7c309ac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior gluteal nerve (L5-S2) supplies?', '["Gluteus medius","All gluteal muscles","Tensor fascia lata","Gluteus maximus","Gluteus minimus"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus', 'The inferior gluteal nerve is the sole motor supply to gluteus maximus. Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia lata are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve, so the inferior gluteal nerve does not serve all gluteal muscles.'),
('96d5841b-7458-512a-80ec-7631c66037ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The nerve to piriformis (S1-S2) supplies?', '["Piriformis muscle","None of the above","Obturator internus","Rarely","Quadratus femoris"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis muscle', 'The nerve to piriformis, a direct branch of the sacral plexus, supplies only the piriformis muscle. Obturator internus and quadratus femoris receive their own dedicated nerves.'),
('4d3d16a3-882b-5a33-a26f-bd633fb26bf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The nerve to obturator internus (L5-S2) supplies?', '["Piriformis resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator internus and superior gemellus","Rarely without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Quadratus femoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator internus and superior gemellus', 'The nerve to obturator internus innervates both obturator internus and the superior gemellus. Quadratus femoris and the inferior gemellus are served by the nerve to quadratus femoris.'),
('b794ca0f-2a5f-522b-af65-70202f7e92fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris (L4-S1) supplies?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Obturator internus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Piriformis mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadratus femoris and inferior gemellus","Rarely leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadratus femoris and inferior gemellus', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris supplies quadratus femoris and the inferior gemellus muscle. Obturator internus and superior gemellus are innervated by the separate nerve to obturator internus.'),
('6bf5b1c5-0669-507c-af0c-636ac0456fc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) provide?', '["Somatic motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic innervation mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic innervation to pelvic viscera (defecation, urination, erection)"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic innervation to pelvic viscera (defecation, urination, erection)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 that mediate bladder emptying, defecation, and penile erection. Sympathetic supply to pelvic viscera comes instead from the lumbar splanchnic nerves and the hypogastric plexus.'),
('98dc68ed-fa79-5ec9-a836-b984a858ac7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacral sympathetic trunk lies?', '["Posterior to sacrum","Lateral to sacrum","Medial to the anterior sacral foramina","Anterior to the coccyx","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to the anterior sacral foramina', 'The sacral sympathetic trunk descends on the pelvic surface of the sacrum medial to the anterior sacral foramina and ends in the ganglion impar in front of the coccyx. It therefore lies on the anterior sacral surface, not lateral or posterior to it.'),
('678880c5-e50e-595a-a6b3-86383d499bc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding the pelvic wall, vessels, and nerves:', '["The internal iliac artery divides into anterior and posterior divisions","All of the above","The piriformis and obturator internus form the lateral pelvic wall","The levator ani is part of the pelvic diaphragm","The pudendal nerve is the main nerve of the perineum"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Each listed statement is anatomically correct: piriformis and obturator internus form the lateral pelvic wall, levator ani belongs to the pelvic diaphragm, the internal iliac artery splits into anterior and posterior divisions, and the pudendal nerve is the principal perineal nerve.'),
('f4893040-f451-561d-8551-67e860db2069'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'What muscles form the lateral pelvic wall?', '["Psoas and quadratus lumborum","Piriformis and obturator internus","Gluteus maximus and medius","Levator ani and coccygeus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis and obturator internus', 'The lateral pelvic wall is formed by obturator internus and piriformis. Levator ani and coccygeus form the pelvic floor, while psoas and quadratus lumborum are posterior abdominal wall muscles.'),
('0592ef07-7ba6-5bff-bfa6-9a4dff9a9669'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'What are the divisions of the internal iliac artery?', '["None","Medial and lateral","Left and right","Superior and inferior","Anterior and posterior divisions"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior divisions', 'The internal iliac artery divides into an anterior division carrying most visceral and perineal branches and a posterior division giving parietal branches such as superior gluteal, iliolumbar, and lateral sacral.'),
('808b91d1-1376-5388-a865-ba8a0b0c5030'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'What does the anterior division of internal iliac give?', '["Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only gluteal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visceral branches, inferior gluteal, internal pudendal","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visceral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral branches, inferior gluteal, internal pudendal', 'The anterior division gives visceral branches to pelvic organs plus the inferior gluteal and internal pudendal arteries. Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arise from the posterior division.'),
('c4eabc19-b0fa-5a03-bd1b-5e19112b9cbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'What does the posterior division of internal iliac give?', '["All visceral branches","None","Obturator and vesical","Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal","Inferior gluteal and internal pudendal"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal', 'The posterior division is strictly parietal and typically yields the iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arteries. Visceral branches, obturator, inferior gluteal, and internal pudendal belong to the anterior division.'),
('3ae2f777-30ae-555f-b725-28bfed1c25f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'What is the sacral plexus?', '["Only L4-L5 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L2-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Only S1-S4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lumbosacral trunk (L4-L5) and anterior rami S1-S4"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbosacral trunk (L4-L5) and anterior rami S1-S4', 'The sacral plexus is formed by the lumbosacral trunk (L4-L5) uniting with the anterior rami of S1-S4 on the posterior pelvic wall. It is not limited to L4-L5 or to the sacral roots alone.'),
('a08f80ff-3c7a-5de8-8747-e1ab134911e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The bony pelvis is formed by:', '["Only the sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two hip bones (innominate bones), the sacrum, and the coccyx","Only the hip bones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the coccyx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two hip bones (innominate bones), the sacrum, and the coccyx', 'The bony pelvis consists of the two hip (innominate) bones, the sacrum, and the coccyx. It requires all these elements, not any single bone or pair alone.'),
('01b7c992-400e-5b20-9498-e0c7bb9a5d35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The hip bone is formed by:', '["The ilium, ischium, and pubis","The ischium and pubis only","The ilium and pubis only","The ilium and ischium only","None"]'::jsonb, 'The ilium, ischium, and pubis', 'Each hip (innominate) bone is the fusion of the ilium, ischium, and pubis, which meet in the acetabulum. Excluding any one of the three bones is incorrect.'),
('8be9584a-ef50-5957-a2ff-ac787e0dd118'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic inlet (superior pelvic aperture) is bounded by:', '["The pubic arch and the ischial spines resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacrum and the coccyx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sacral promontory, the ala of the sacrum, the iliopectineal line, and the pubic symphysis"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral promontory, the ala of the sacrum, the iliopectineal line, and the pubic symphysis', 'The pelvic inlet is outlined by the sacral promontory, the ala of the sacrum, the iliopectineal (arcuate) line, and the pubic symphysis. The pubic arch and ischial spines define the outlet, not the inlet.'),
('ad4f43fa-d210-5587-9827-2f955e9bfa6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic outlet (inferior pelvic aperture) is bounded by:', '["The iliac crests resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacral promontory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The coccyx, the ischial tuberosities, the pubic arch, and the sacrotuberous ligaments","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The coccyx, the ischial tuberosities, the pubic arch, and the sacrotuberous ligaments', 'The pelvic outlet is bounded posteriorly by the coccyx with the sacrotuberous ligaments, laterally by the ischial tuberosities, and anteriorly by the pubic arch. The sacral promontory and iliac crests relate to the higher inlet and false pelvis.'),
('d386fb4d-1027-5f94-aa81-3bdf224a85d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The true pelvis is:', '["The part of the pelvis below the pelvic outlet resulting in decreased functional activity","The part of the pelvis above the pelvic inlet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The part of the pelvis below the pelvic inlet (the pelvic cavity)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the pelvis below the pelvic inlet (the pelvic cavity)', 'The true pelvis is the cavity lying below the pelvic inlet and above the pelvic outlet, and it contains the pelvic viscera. The false pelvis lies above the pelvic inlet around the iliac fossae.'),
('d4ac2a3d-1f5d-51f7-bcc8-f50bff7eec47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The false pelvis is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The part of the pelvis below the pelvic inlet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The part of the pelvis above the pelvic inlet (the iliac fossae)","The part of the pelvis below the pelvic outlet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the pelvis above the pelvic inlet (the iliac fossae)', 'The false (greater) pelvis is the wide upper portion above the pelvic inlet, corresponding to the iliac fossae, and contains abdominal organs such as the cecum and sigmoid colon. The true pelvis lies below the inlet.'),
('f1a48670-630c-5a7c-af21-641848275eae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pubic arch is:', '["The angle formed by the inferior pubic rami (wider in females > 90 degrees)","The same in both sexes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wider in males mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Narrower in females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The angle formed by the inferior pubic rami (wider in females > 90 degrees)', 'The pubic arch is the angle between the inferior pubic rami below the pubic symphysis; it is wider (generally greater than 90 degrees) in females to accommodate childbirth and narrower in males. It is therefore not identical between the sexes.'),
('92b5cceb-9542-589f-81e4-84589b17767b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacrospinous ligament is:', '["From the pubis to the sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","A strong ligament from the ischial spine to the sacrum (helps form the greater and lesser sciatic foramina)","From the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A strong ligament from the ischial spine to the sacrum (helps form the greater and lesser sciatic foramina)', 'The sacrospinous ligament runs from the ischial spine to the lateral sacrum and coccyx and, with the sacrotuberous ligament, delimits the greater and lesser sciatic foramina. The sacrotuberous ligament, by contrast, attaches at the ischial tuberosity.'),
('61f3b1fa-6d01-52e4-85b5-34f78cf164b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacrotuberous ligament is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From the pubis to the sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","From the ischial spine to the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A strong ligament from the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'A strong ligament from the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum', 'The sacrotuberous ligament spans from the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum and, together with the sacrospinous ligament, separates the greater and lesser sciatic foramina. The ligament running to the ischial spine is the sacrospinous ligament.'),
('be710177-1964-5d9d-aa03-b6f155f7aa09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments bound:', '["The obturator foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater (above) and lesser (below) sciatic foramina","The inguinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater (above) and lesser (below) sciatic foramina', 'The sacrospinous and sacrotuberous ligaments stretch across the sciatic notch, dividing it into the greater sciatic foramen above and the lesser sciatic foramen below. They play no role in forming the obturator foramen, femoral triangle, or inguinal canal.'),
('543a8e66-0d1c-557c-8fc2-e0e24d42e167'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The greater sciatic foramen is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An opening in the inferior pelvic wall mediated by specific receptor activation","An opening in the anterior pelvic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A large opening in the posterior pelvic wall (through which the piriformis and other structures pass)"]'::jsonb, 'A large opening in the posterior pelvic wall (through which the piriformis and other structures pass)', 'The greater sciatic foramen is formed by the greater sciatic notch and the sacrospinous ligament in the posterior pelvic wall. It transmits the piriformis, superior and inferior gluteal neurovascular bundles, sciatic nerve, and pudendal structures, not inferior or anterior wall openings.'),
('3080c026-c48e-54e3-a25a-955727c084cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lesser sciatic foramen is:', '["A smaller opening below the greater sciatic foramen (through which the obturator internus tendon, pudendal nerve, and internal pudendal vessels pass)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An opening in the anterior pelvic wall mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An opening in the superior pelvic wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A smaller opening below the greater sciatic foramen (through which the obturator internus tendon, pudendal nerve, and internal pudendal vessels pass)', 'The lesser sciatic foramen lies below the greater sciatic foramen, bounded by the sacrotuberous and sacrospinous ligaments. It transmits the tendon of obturator internus with its nerve and the pudendal nerve with the internal pudendal vessels, which is why these structures can reach the perineum.'),
('a37e3755-430c-540b-999c-6c7d81be2e89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The piriformis muscle inserts into:', '["The lesser trochanter","None","The ischial tuberosity","The greater trochanter of the femur","The iliac crest"]'::jsonb, 'The greater trochanter of the femur', 'The piriformis arises from the anterior surface of the sacrum, exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, and inserts on the upper border of the greater trochanter. The lesser trochanter receives iliopsoas, the ischial tuberosity gives origin to the hamstrings, and the iliac crest is unrelated.'),
('22ad6b16-3710-5808-9597-a346f6aa2a51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The piriformis is innervated by:', '["The anterior rami of S1-S2","None","The obturator nerve","The sciatic nerve","The pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior rami of S1-S2', 'The piriformis receives direct innervation from the anterior rami of S1 and S2 via the nerve to piriformis. The sciatic nerve passes below it but does not innervate it, and the obturator and pudendal nerves supply the medial thigh and perineum respectively.'),
('ab6b9296-c289-537d-9849-593f1cab78ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator internus muscle exits the pelvis through:', '["The obturator foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater sciatic foramen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The lesser sciatic foramen (after turning around the ischial spine)","The inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lesser sciatic foramen (after turning around the ischial spine)', 'The obturator internus arises from the inner aspect of the obturator membrane and passes out of the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen, hooking around the ischial spine to reach its insertion. The greater sciatic foramen transmits the piriformis and gluteal neurovascular structures instead.'),
('b21f185d-6d7c-5c09-b448-e35d328dcafc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator internus functions as:', '["A lateral rotator of the extended thigh and an abductor of the flexed thigh","A flexor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An extensor mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A medial rotator leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lateral rotator of the extended thigh and an abductor of the flexed thigh', 'Like the other short lateral rotators, the obturator internus laterally rotates the extended thigh and abducts the flexed thigh because its tendon passes behind the hip joint. It does not flex, extend, or medially rotate the hip.'),
('ffdd3175-6084-5113-80f8-b0166a24c802'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator internus is innervated by:', '["The pudendal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The nerve to the obturator internus (L5-S2)","The sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The nerve to the obturator internus (L5-S2)', 'The nerve to obturator internus (L5-S2) arises from the sacral plexus and enters the muscle through the lesser sciatic foramen. The obturator nerve supplies the adductor compartment, while the pudendal and sciatic nerves serve the perineum and lower limb.'),
('ee57e5e0-3e89-5925-80c7-7119476b453e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The levator ani is a:', '["A muscle of the thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A muscle of the gluteal region mediated by specific receptor activation","Major muscle of the pelvic floor (a pair of broad, thin muscles)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Major muscle of the pelvic floor (a pair of broad, thin muscles)', 'The levator ani is a broad, thin pair of muscles that, with the coccygeus, forms the pelvic diaphragm supporting the pelvic viscera. It is not part of the thigh or gluteal musculature.'),
('d0455fbe-d600-5e28-97aa-caf265c1ae44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The levator ani is divided into:', '["Two parts resulting in decreased functional activity","Pubococcygeus (including puborectalis), iliococcygeus, and ischiococcygeus (coccygeus)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only one part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Three parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubococcygeus (including puborectalis), iliococcygeus, and ischiococcygeus (coccygeus)', 'The levator ani comprises the pubococcygeus (which includes the puborectalis sling around the rectum), the iliococcygeus, and the coccygeus (ischiococcygeus). The distractors state only the number of parts without naming the actual muscular components.'),
('981204fb-0892-577c-b7c7-1db57ddb4604'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pubococcygeus muscle arises from:', '["The ischial spine resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior surface of the pubic bone and the anterior part of the obturator fascia","The sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The coccyx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior surface of the pubic bone and the anterior part of the obturator fascia', 'The pubococcygeus, the most anterior part of the levator ani, arises from the posterior surface of the pubic bone and the anterior part of the tendinous arch (obturator fascia). The ischial spine gives origin to the coccygeus and iliococcygeus, not the pubococcygeus.'),
('2b1501b3-2a97-5d88-b9df-ce712ab00b3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The iliococcygeus muscle arises from:', '["The pubic bone resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ischial spine and the posterior part of the obturator fascia"]'::jsonb, 'The ischial spine and the posterior part of the obturator fascia', 'The iliococcygeus, the middle part of the levator ani, arises from the ischial spine and the posterior part of the tendinous arch of the obturator fascia and inserts into the anococcygeal raphe and coccyx. The pubic bone and sacrum give origin to other muscles.'),
('24d1d517-f4b0-55d8-a204-d4f5630ed564'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The ischiococcygeus (coccygeus) muscle arises from:', '["The ilium resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","The pubic bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ischial spine and the sacrospinous ligament"]'::jsonb, 'The ischial spine and the sacrospinous ligament', 'The coccygeus (ischiococcygeus) arises from the ischial spine and the sacrospinous ligament and inserts into the lateral borders of the coccyx and lower sacrum. The ilium, pubic bone, and sacrum do not give origin to this muscle.'),
('6e17fb99-a7f5-529d-a149-f2fe71b669a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The levator ani muscles insert into:', '["Only the anococcygeal raphe resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the pelvic organs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The coccyx, the anococcygeal raphe, and the walls of the pelvic organs (rectum, vagina, urethra)"]'::jsonb, 'The coccyx, the anococcygeal raphe, and the walls of the pelvic organs (rectum, vagina, urethra)', 'The levator ani fibers converge to insert into the coccyx, the anococcygeal raphe, and the walls of the pelvic organs including the rectum, vagina, and urethra. The insertions are multiple rather than confined to any single site.'),
('0cbfbac1-3b15-5b2a-bd8f-17939d00b8e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The levator ani forms the:', '["Thoracic wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Pelvic diaphragm (supports the pelvic organs)","Abdominal wall mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Iliac wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic diaphragm (supports the pelvic organs)', 'The levator ani is the chief muscle of the pelvic diaphragm, which supports the pelvic viscera and maintains continence. It does not form the thoracic, abdominal, or iliac walls.'),
('bac11287-69d0-5e53-b71a-dab24b8bfadb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic diaphragm (levator ani + coccygeus) supports:', '["The thoracic organs resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pelvic organs (urinary bladder, uterus/vagina, rectum)","Only the rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The abdominal organs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvic organs (urinary bladder, uterus/vagina, rectum)', 'The pelvic diaphragm supports the pelvic viscera, namely the urinary bladder, the uterus and vagina in females, and the rectum. It does not support thoracic or abdominal organs, and its support is not limited to the rectum alone.'),
('905f5c0d-4090-5011-b8d7-927c277755f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic diaphragm has three openings:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Two openings accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The urogenital hiatus (for the urethra and vagina), and the anorectal opening (for the rectum)","Four openings without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only one opening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital hiatus (for the urethra and vagina), and the anorectal opening (for the rectum)', 'The pelvic diaphragm presents two functional openings: the anterior urogenital hiatus transmitting the urethra (and vagina in the female) and the posterior anorectal opening for the rectum. The options giving a bare number fail to identify these openings.'),
('f73a8026-a001-55e0-82dc-b7d6358285f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The perineal body is:', '["A bone resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibromuscular mass in the midline of the perineum (an important attachment point for many muscles)","A ligament mediated by specific receptor activation","A cartilage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular mass in the midline of the perineum (an important attachment point for many muscles)', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular node in the midline of the perineum to which the bulbospongiosus, transverse perinei, external anal sphincter, and levator ani attach. It is not bone, ligament, or cartilage.'),
('8674196a-001a-5b2d-af13-191deea9860e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The perineal body is located:', '["Between the anus and the vagina (in females) or between the anus and the bulb of the penis (in males)","Between the rectum and the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the vagina and the urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the anus and the vagina (in females) or between the anus and the bulb of the penis (in males)', 'The perineal body lies in the midline between the anus and the vagina in the female and between the anus and the bulb of the penis in the male. The space between rectum and sacrum is the retrorectal region, and the vagina-to-urethra interval is not the perineal body.'),
('3fbb9249-10e9-53f9-a488-d8827a00f9b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The perineal body is an important landmark for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Hysterectomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Episiotomy (a surgical incision to enlarge the vaginal opening during childbirth)","Cesarean section without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Episiotomy (a surgical incision to enlarge the vaginal opening during childbirth)', 'The perineal body is the target of an episiotomy, an incision made during childbirth to enlarge the vaginal opening. It is not directly involved in hysterectomy or cesarean section.'),
('4f84b471-e635-5b3b-bf9c-747806c6f0a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal iliac artery arises from:', '["The abdominal aorta resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The common iliac artery (at the level of the L5-S1 disc)","The femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The common iliac artery (at the level of the L5-S1 disc)', 'The internal iliac artery arises from the common iliac artery at the level of the L5-S1 disc and descends into the pelvis. The femoral, external iliac, and abdominal aortic branches have different origins.'),
('b49cb44c-7c9c-5d66-be96-1161473831b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal iliac artery supplies:', '["Only the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity","The pelvic walls, pelvic viscera, gluteal region, and perineum","Only the pelvic viscera mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the gluteal region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvic walls, pelvic viscera, gluteal region, and perineum', 'The internal iliac artery is the principal artery of the pelvis, supplying the pelvic walls and viscera, the gluteal region, and the perineum through its anterior and posterior divisions. Its territory is not limited to any single region.'),
('093f6d6b-4c41-51a3-86c1-280110e059dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The anterior trunk of the internal iliac artery gives off:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the uterine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the obturator mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the umbilical without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Umbilical, superior vesical, obturator, inferior vesical, middle rectal, uterine, vaginal, and internal pudendal arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilical, superior vesical, obturator, inferior vesical, middle rectal, uterine, vaginal, and internal pudendal arteries', 'The anterior division of the internal iliac artery gives the umbilical (continuing as the superior vesical), obturator, inferior vesical, middle rectal, uterine, vaginal, and internal pudendal arteries. Options naming a single branch are incomplete.'),
('b7fac74f-7c41-5040-9389-a1853e26c1e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The posterior trunk of the internal iliac artery gives off:', '["Middle and inferior rectal resulting in decreased functional activity","Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arteries","Uterine and vaginal mediated by specific receptor activation","Umbilical, superior vesical, obturator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arteries', 'The posterior division of the internal iliac artery gives rise to the iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arteries. The branches listed in the other options belong to the anterior division.'),
('1a731ce1-34c7-5ab6-a4a1-c4562734eae6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal iliac vein drains:', '["Only the pelvic walls resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the pelvic viscera accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The pelvic walls and viscera (forms from several tributaries)","Only the lower limb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvic walls and viscera (forms from several tributaries)', 'The internal iliac vein collects blood from the pelvic walls and viscera through several tributaries and joins the external iliac vein to form the common iliac vein. It does not drain the lower limb.'),
('d1068172-25e5-5ad4-958a-7abdba2021b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The external iliac artery arises from:', '["The abdominal aorta","None","The common iliac artery","The internal iliac artery","The femoral artery"]'::jsonb, 'The common iliac artery', 'The external iliac artery arises from the common iliac artery and, after passing deep to the inguinal ligament, continues as the femoral artery. The internal iliac artery is a separate branch of the common iliac artery.'),
('34eebb3b-9310-548b-a7d9-b2e77152989d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The external iliac artery supplies:', '["The pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","The gluteal region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lower limb (via the femoral artery)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The perineum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lower limb (via the femoral artery)', 'The external iliac artery crosses beneath the inguinal ligament to become the femoral artery, supplying the lower limb. The pelvis, gluteal region, and perineum are supplied by the internal iliac artery.'),
('b5667152-d12a-5f8a-ad9a-e76b75982d1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The common iliac arteries arise from:', '["The internal iliac arteries","The femoral arteries","The abdominal aorta (at the level of L4)","None","The external iliac arteries"]'::jsonb, 'The abdominal aorta (at the level of L4)', 'The abdominal aorta divides into the right and left common iliac arteries at the level of the L4 vertebra. The internal and external iliac arteries arise distally from the common iliac arteries.'),
('06699096-b01a-5840-afe9-a9f818042410'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The common iliac arteries divide into:', '["Only external resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal and external iliac arteries (at the level of the L5-S1 disc)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Femoral arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only internal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal and external iliac arteries (at the level of the L5-S1 disc)', 'Each common iliac artery divides at the level of the sacroiliac joint, approximately the L5-S1 disc, into the internal and external iliac arteries. The femoral artery is a continuation of the external iliac artery, not a direct division.'),
('bfbfefba-1b0a-5c69-88f7-831f4361fd68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The common iliac veins form:', '["The IVC (the right and left common iliac veins unite at L5)","The internal iliac veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The femoral veins mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external iliac veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC (the right and left common iliac veins unite at L5)', 'The right and left common iliac veins unite anterior to the fifth lumbar vertebra to form the inferior vena cava. The external iliac vein is a tributary of the common iliac vein rather than its product.'),
('315e200a-b231-5bf5-818d-f184f557d6b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The left common iliac vein is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shorter than the right mediated by specific receptor activation","The same length without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Longer than the right and passes behind the right common iliac artery"]'::jsonb, 'Longer than the right and passes behind the right common iliac artery', 'The left common iliac vein is longer than the right, crossing in front of the L5 vertebra and passing behind the right common iliac artery before the two unite. This relationship underlies the risk of left iliac vein compression.'),
('49f836aa-6357-540c-8a7a-a04bc9455b01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic lymph nodes include:', '["Only the external iliac nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","External iliac, internal iliac, common iliac, and sacral nodes","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the common iliac nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the internal iliac nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External iliac, internal iliac, common iliac, and sacral nodes', 'The pelvic lymph nodes comprise the external iliac, internal iliac, common iliac, and sacral (median and lateral) groups. Listing only one nodal group is incomplete.'),
('68e0ee73-5079-5162-b523-43fb524b07c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The external iliac nodes drain:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The gluteal region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","The pelvic viscera without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower limb and the inguinal region"]'::jsonb, 'The lower limb and the inguinal region', 'The external iliac nodes receive lymph from the lower limb through the inguinal nodes and also from the inguinal region. Lymph from the pelvic viscera and perineum drains mainly to the internal iliac nodes instead.'),
('e9ec5e7a-6294-558f-9c70-193ead37b7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal iliac nodes drain:', '["The gluteal region resulting in decreased functional activity","The pelvic viscera and the perineum","The lower limb mediated by specific receptor activation","The inguinal region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvic viscera and the perineum', 'The internal iliac nodes collect lymph from the pelvic viscera and the perineum. The lower limb, gluteal region, and inguinal region drain via the external iliac and inguinal groups.'),
('10f5b1cc-36a9-5a6c-ba77-7a0d5365e698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The common iliac nodes drain:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the external iliac nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the internal iliac nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external and internal iliac nodes (to the para-aortic nodes)"]'::jsonb, 'The external and internal iliac nodes (to the para-aortic nodes)', 'The common iliac nodes receive lymph from both the external and internal iliac nodes and drain upward to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes. Their drainage is not limited to one nodal group.'),
('5ee90d57-cbd9-52d2-b38d-066917528f46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacral (median and lateral) nodes drain:', '["The lower limb resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacrum and the posterior pelvic wall","The perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sacrum and the posterior pelvic wall', 'The median and lateral sacral nodes receive lymph from the sacrum and the posterior pelvic wall. The lower limb, inguinal region, and perineum drain through other nodal groups.'),
('8384aa8a-f6e5-549a-af93-b3d4dee18522'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves (nervi erigentes) are:', '["Somatic fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 (to the pelvic plexus)"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 (to the pelvic plexus)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves (nervi erigentes) carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the anterior rami of S2-S4 to the inferior hypogastric plexus. They provide the parasympathetic outflow to the pelvic viscera, unlike the sympathetic lumbar splanchnic nerves.'),
('57ca9d46-46e6-5499-88e5-17ce4bd4c90b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves provide:', '["Sensory innervation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatic innervation mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic innervation to the pelvic viscera (bladder, rectum, and reproductive organs)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic innervation to the pelvic viscera (bladder, rectum, and reproductive organs)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves deliver parasympathetic innervation to the bladder, rectum, and reproductive organs, mediating bladder contraction, defecation, and erection. They do not supply sensory, somatic, or sympathetic fibers.'),
('261b5484-c273-5b42-8d2c-8a750ab08d13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves provide:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory innervation mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic innervation to the pelvic viscera (via the hypogastric nerve)","Somatic innervation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic innervation to the pelvic viscera (via the hypogastric nerve)', 'The lumbar splanchnic nerves carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers that travel through the superior and inferior hypogastric plexuses to reach the pelvic viscera. They are not parasympathetic, sensory, or somatic.'),
('ffa78df6-036e-5934-8be2-3d65f8c20678'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior hypogastric plexus is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A network of nerves in front of the L5 vertebra (the presacral plexus)","A network of nerves in the pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A network of nerves in the abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A network of nerves in front of the L5 vertebra (the presacral plexus)', 'The superior hypogastric plexus lies in front of the fifth lumbar vertebra on the sacral promontory, continuing from the aortic plexus, and is therefore called the presacral plexus. It lies at the pelvic brim rather than within the pelvis or abdomen proper.'),
('cdf27952-4c2a-5e44-b195-58b11910ef62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior hypogastric plexus (pelvic plexus) is:', '["A network of nerves on each side of the rectum (supplies the pelvic viscera)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A network of nerves in the thorax without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A network of nerves in the abdomen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A network of nerves on each side of the rectum (supplies the pelvic viscera)', 'The inferior hypogastric (pelvic) plexus lies on each side of the rectum and bladder, receiving both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers and distributing them to the pelvic viscera. It is located in the pelvis, not the thorax or abdomen.'),
('0bea9af2-602b-5b75-be1d-761f372135d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","An autonomic nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A motor nerve to the lower limb mediated by specific receptor activation","A sensory nerve to the lower limb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The somatic nerve of the perineum (supplies the perineum, external genitalia, and external anal/urethral sphincters)"]'::jsonb, 'The somatic nerve of the perineum (supplies the perineum, external genitalia, and external anal/urethral sphincters)', 'The pudendal nerve is the main somatic nerve of the perineum, supplying the perineum, external genitalia, and the external anal and urethral sphincters. It is a mixed somatic nerve carrying both motor and sensory fibers, not autonomic fibers or lower-limb supply.'),
('83e35172-228c-54e2-b7ea-3fc299c9b514'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pudendal nerve arises from:', '["The coccygeal plexus","The lumbar plexus","The sacral plexus (S2-S4)","The hypogastric plexus","None"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral plexus (S2-S4)', 'The pudendal nerve arises from the anterior rami of S2-S4 as part of the sacral plexus. The coccygeal and lumbar plexuses and the hypogastric plexus do not give origin to this nerve.'),
('8af01876-3196-56c8-ad59-7088b020c1ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pudendal nerve passes:', '["Directly into the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform), then around the ischial spine and through the lesser sciatic foramen into the perineum","Through the inguinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the femoral triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform), then around the ischial spine and through the lesser sciatic foramen into the perineum', 'The pudendal nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis, hooks around the ischial spine, and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen to reach the pudendal (Alcock''s) canal in the perineum. This curved course explains its surgical vulnerability.'),
('aaff81fc-193e-5d29-83fb-bc95518cc4ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) passes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Through the obturator foramen to supply the medial compartment of the thigh","Through the greater sciatic foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the femoral triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the obturator foramen to supply the medial compartment of the thigh', 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) exits the pelvis through the obturator canal and foramen to supply the adductor (medial) compartment of the thigh. The greater sciatic foramen, femoral triangle, and inguinal canal transmit other structures.'),
('64d09fac-55ce-5240-a1e9-2e25a90cbdde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacral plexus lies on:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The superior pelvic wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal region mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior pelvic wall (anterior to the piriformis)","The anterior pelvic wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior pelvic wall (anterior to the piriformis)', 'The sacral plexus is formed by the lumbosacral trunk and the anterior rami of S1-S4 and lies on the posterior pelvic wall, anterior to the piriformis muscle. It does not lie on the superior or anterior pelvic walls or the inguinal region.'),
('cf522be1-ada1-5662-8488-541065b635a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sciatic nerve (L4-S3) is:', '["The second largest nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The largest nerve in the body (exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A small nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The largest nerve in the body (exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen)', 'The sciatic nerve (L4-S3) is the largest nerve in the body, leaving the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis. It is not a small nerve, and there is no larger rival nerve.'),
('3e157454-b4ab-542e-b048-25455d01a4ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sciatic nerve passes:', '["Through the inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform foramen)","Through the lesser sciatic foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform foramen)', 'The sciatic nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen inferior to the piriformis (infrapiriform foramen). The suprapiriform region transmits the superior gluteal nerve, while the lesser sciatic foramen carries the pudendal nerve and obturator internus tendon.'),
('ff9352a3-8dda-520e-a46b-82b4f039d48e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sciatic nerve supplies:', '["Only the foot resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the posterior thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","The posterior compartment of the thigh and the entire leg and foot (via the tibial and common peroneal nerves)","Only the leg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior compartment of the thigh and the entire leg and foot (via the tibial and common peroneal nerves)', 'The sciatic nerve innervates the hamstrings in the posterior thigh and, after dividing into the tibial and common fibular (peroneal) nerves, supplies all muscles in the leg and foot. Its distribution is not limited to any single region.'),
('843bb684-10f5-5bc3-8b64-8e93f877ffd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior gluteal nerve exits the pelvis through:', '["The greater sciatic foramen (suprapiriform foramen)","The inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lesser sciatic foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (suprapiriform foramen)', 'The superior gluteal nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen above the piriformis (suprapiriform foramen) to supply the gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae. The infrapiriform and lesser sciatic foramina carry other structures.'),
('f3540b1d-6b1e-5a64-b917-3d14d6c5fa05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior gluteal nerve exits the pelvis through:', '["The lesser sciatic foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","The greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform foramen)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform foramen)', 'The inferior gluteal nerve is the inferior division of the sacral plexus (L5-S2) and leaves the pelvis deep to the piriformis through the greater sciatic foramen. Because it passes below the piriformis, that route is termed the infrapiriform foramen; it then supplies gluteus maximus. The lesser sciatic foramen transmits the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels, and the obturator foramen conducts the obturator nerve and vessels.'),
('6d288e56-130d-5ac7-9dce-5b521960b9ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh (S1-S3) supplies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The skin over the posterior thigh and the perineum (via the inferior cluneal nerves)","The medial thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior thigh without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lateral thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin over the posterior thigh and the perineum (via the inferior cluneal nerves)', 'The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh carries S1-S3 fibers and supplies the skin of the posterior thigh and popliteal region. Through its inferior cluneal and perineal branches it also innervates skin of the buttock and the perineum. It does not supply the medial or anterior thigh, which are innervated by the obturator and femoral nerves respectively.'),
('66d0f893-452a-56d3-a482-f86ffcafa4f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic ureter is the part:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","From the pelvic brim to the bladder (crosses the common/external iliac vessels)","From the kidney to the pelvic brim without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the bladder to the urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the pelvic brim to the bladder (crosses the common/external iliac vessels)', 'The ureter is divided into abdominal and pelvic parts at the pelvic brim; the pelvic part runs from the brim to the bladder, crossing the common or external iliac vessels. The portion above the brim is the abdominal ureter, and the bladder-to-urethra segment is the urethra, not the ureter.'),
('83dc7c7c-e210-5b48-abfa-c43aed084ac4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic ureter in males passes:', '["Through the ureteric bud resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to the internal iliac artery and the sacroiliac joint, then runs along the lateral pelvic wall","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior to the rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the internal iliac artery and the sacroiliac joint, then runs along the lateral pelvic wall', 'In the male, the pelvic ureter descends anterior to the internal iliac artery and the sacroiliac joint, then runs along the lateral pelvic wall to reach the base of the bladder. It is crossed by the ductus deferens and does not pass posterior to the rectum. The ureteric bud is an embryonic structure, not a pathway.'),
('38bb63ce-5396-5047-b4a9-5e03495efc32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic ureter in females passes:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior to the broad ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior to the uterine artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferior to the uterine artery (''Water under the bridge'') at the base of the broad ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior to the uterine artery (''Water under the bridge'') at the base of the broad ligament', 'In the female, the ureter runs at the base of the broad ligament and passes below the uterine artery, a relationship remembered by ''water under the bridge''. The uterine artery courses over (superior to) the ureter. This point is clinically important because the uterine artery is ligated during hysterectomy and the ureter can be injured.'),
('971d02e2-8919-532d-b8fc-8e576bc5a5ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The rectovesical pouch in males is:', '["The space between the rectum and the sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the bladder","The space between the bladder and the pubic bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the bladder', 'In the male, the peritoneum reflects from the rectum onto the posterior surface of the bladder, creating the rectovesical pouch, the lowest part of the peritoneal cavity in males. The space between bladder and pubis is the retropubic (prevesical) space, and the retrorectal region lies between rectum and sacrum.'),
('c8b85ee9-15c2-57ef-9c70-1c75b380af93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) in females is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the uterus (the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity)","The space between the uterus and the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the rectum and the sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the uterus (the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity)', 'The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) is the peritoneal reflection between the rectum and the posterior aspect of the uterus and fornix of the vagina. Because it is the most dependent part of the peritoneal cavity in the upright female, ascites, pus, or tumor deposits collect there and it is the site used for culdocentesis. The vesicouterine pouch lies between the bladder and uterus.'),
('52a72261-2b43-5cd6-a17f-a4f29cf3b7da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic viscera are supplied by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The aorta directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Branches of the internal iliac artery (e.g., superior and inferior vesical, uterine, vaginal, middle rectal, and internal pudendal)","Only the external iliac artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the common iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Branches of the internal iliac artery (e.g., superior and inferior vesical, uterine, vaginal, middle rectal, and internal pudendal)', 'The internal iliac artery has a posterior division that supplies the pelvic wall and an anterior division whose visceral branches (superior and inferior vesical, uterine, vaginal, middle rectal, and internal pudendal) supply the pelvic organs. The external iliac artery mostly serves the lower limb, and the aorta gives off only the middle sacral and ovarian/testicular branches to this region.'),
('d486c83b-e87c-5697-baac-41aeff0f05c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The uterine artery supplies:', '["The bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The uterus (and the vagina and uterine tubes)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ovaries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The uterus (and the vagina and uterine tubes)', 'The uterine artery, a visceral branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, runs in the broad ligament and supplies the uterus. It also gives branches to the vagina and uterine tubes and anastomoses with the ovarian artery. The bladder is supplied by vesical arteries and the rectum by middle rectal or inferior mesenteric vessels.'),
('00abaa93-17a0-5a97-b137-d63b30d6c420'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The vaginal artery supplies:', '["The vagina (and the lower part of the bladder and the vestibule)","The rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ovaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vagina (and the lower part of the bladder and the vestibule)', 'The vaginal artery, a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, supplies the vagina and anastomoses with the uterine artery and the internal pudendal branches to the vestibule. It also sends small twigs to the lower bladder. The ovaries are supplied by the ovarian artery from the aorta, not by the vaginal artery.'),
('5f3498c1-51a1-5aff-8acf-bbba178f8bfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal pudendal artery supplies:', '["The perineum and the external genitalia (including the penis, clitoris, scrotum, and labia)","The pelvic viscera accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gluteal region mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower limb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perineum and the external genitalia (including the penis, clitoris, scrotum, and labia)', 'The internal pudendal artery, the main arterial supply of the perineum, exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen to run in the pudendal canal. Its branches supply the external genitalia, anal canal, and perineal muscles. The gluteal region is served by the superior and inferior gluteal arteries, and the lower limb by the femoral system.'),
('48d2b0ec-f8b1-5cfb-ac23-b19506e5e091'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator artery supplies:', '["The gluteal muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The hamstrings mediated by specific receptor activation","The adductor muscles of the thigh (via an anterior and posterior branch)","The quadriceps leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The adductor muscles of the thigh (via an anterior and posterior branch)', 'The obturator artery, a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, passes through the obturator foramen and divides into anterior and posterior branches that supply the adductor muscles of the thigh. It also gives a branch to the head of the femur via the ligament of the head. The gluteal muscles are supplied by the gluteal arteries and the hamstrings by the profunda femoris system.'),
('1eab4588-dfef-502f-b4a2-6cb094656961'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The iliolumbar artery supplies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The iliacus, psoas, and quadratus lumborum muscles","The thigh muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","The gluteal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The perineum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The iliacus, psoas, and quadratus lumborum muscles', 'The iliolumbar artery is the first branch of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery; it ascends behind the psoas and divides into iliac and lumbar branches that supply the iliacus, psoas, and quadratus lumborum muscles. It serves the posterior abdominal wall rather than the thigh, gluteal region, or perineum.'),
('80aa062e-038f-5e87-8477-5c1c9940f228'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lateral sacral arteries supply:', '["The coccyx only resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacrum and the surrounding structures","The sacrum only mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sacrum and the surrounding structures', 'The lateral sacral arteries, branches of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery, run on the anterior surface of the sacrum and send branches through the pelvic sacral foramina to supply the sacrum and adjacent structures. They also supply the skin and muscles over the dorsum of the sacrum; the coccyx is a small part of their territory, not their sole supply.'),
('e86c248e-bbaa-587a-b478-dcfed2ec0e92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic veins drain into:', '["The IVC directly resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The azygos vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal iliac vein (which joins the external iliac vein to form the common iliac vein)"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac vein (which joins the external iliac vein to form the common iliac vein)', 'Venous blood from the pelvic viscera and walls is collected by the visceral and parietal tributaries of the internal iliac vein. This vein then joins the external iliac vein to form the common iliac vein, which drains into the inferior vena cava. The azygos and portal systems are not the pathway; however, communications with the vertebral venous plexus can occur.'),
('376f4201-3576-58eb-a052-5e08d88c91fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal iliac vein is formed by:', '["The union of several tributaries (gluteal, pudendal, vesical, uterine, vaginal, rectal, etc.)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The union of the gluteal and pudendal veins mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The union of only two veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The union of several tributaries (gluteal, pudendal, vesical, uterine, vaginal, rectal, etc.)', 'The internal iliac vein is a short trunk formed by the confluence of multiple visceral and parietal tributaries corresponding to the branches of the internal iliac artery: gluteal, internal pudendal, vesical, uterine, vaginal, middle rectal, obturator, and lateral sacral veins. It is not formed by only two veins but by the union of many, especially within a plexus around the pelvic viscera.'),
('a874cf1c-583c-55ee-bf3b-0bf16e8b7f60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The external iliac lymph nodes are located:', '["Along the internal iliac artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Along the external iliac artery (drain the lower limb and inguinal region)","On the sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Along the common iliac artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Along the external iliac artery (drain the lower limb and inguinal region)', 'The external iliac lymph nodes lie along the external iliac artery and receive lymph from the lower limb and the superficial and deep inguinal nodes, as well as some pelvic viscera such as the bladder and body of the uterus. Nodes along the internal iliac artery drain the pelvic viscera, and sacral nodes lie on the anterior sacrum.'),
('42d519b8-706f-5394-8943-b4a1021a92e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal iliac lymph nodes are located:', '["In the inguinal region resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Along the external iliac artery mediated by specific receptor activation","On the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Along the internal iliac artery (drain the pelvic viscera)"]'::jsonb, 'Along the internal iliac artery (drain the pelvic viscera)', 'The internal iliac lymph nodes lie around the internal iliac artery and receive lymph from the pelvic viscera, including the bladder, prostate, cervix, and upper vagina. Nodes along the external iliac artery drain the lower limb and inguinal region, and inguinal nodes serve the anal canal below the pectinate line and the external genitalia.'),
('e7ef3a69-a3cc-5eb8-a15a-c9b5d6a93661'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacral lymph nodes are located:', '["On the anterior surface of the sacrum (drain the sacrum and the posterior pelvic wall)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the anterior surface of the coccyx mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the posterior surface of the sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the anterior surface of the sacrum (drain the sacrum and the posterior pelvic wall)', 'The sacral lymph nodes lie along the median and lateral sacral vessels on the anterior surface of the sacrum. They receive lymph from the sacrum, posterior pelvic wall, prostate, and rectum and pass it to the common iliac nodes. They are not on the posterior surface of the sacrum or confined to the coccyx.'),
('a460ec3e-34bc-5909-b99e-45edcd0d20a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic lymphatics from the pelvic viscera drain to:', '["The inguinal nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","The internal iliac nodes (and then to the common iliac and para-aortic nodes)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The axillary nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external iliac nodes directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac nodes (and then to the common iliac and para-aortic nodes)', 'Most lymph from the pelvic viscera passes first to the internal iliac lymph nodes and then ascends through the common iliac nodes to the para-aortic (lumbar) nodes. The inguinal nodes receive lymph from the anal canal below the pectinate line and external genitalia, not from the pelvic viscera. Axillary nodes are not involved in pelvic drainage.'),
('e81119bf-7253-50b2-a2d6-55212e9ebdfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lymphatics from the ovaries and testes drain to:', '["The inguinal lymph nodes","The axillary lymph nodes","The para-aortic lymph nodes (at L2)","The pelvic lymph nodes","None"]'::jsonb, 'The para-aortic lymph nodes (at L2)', 'The ovaries and testes develop from the gonadal ridge and their lymphatics follow the gonadal vessels, which arise from the aorta near L2; therefore they drain to the para-aortic (lumbar) lymph nodes. This explains why ovarian and testicular cancer may spread to para-aortic nodes before pelvic nodes. The inguinal nodes drain the testicular coverings, not the testis itself.'),
('8508a567-b612-52ee-8c3c-13e9581a0feb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lymphatic drainage of the uterus includes:', '["Only the para-aortic nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the internal iliac nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","The para-aortic nodes (fundus), the external iliac nodes (body), and the internal iliac nodes (cervix)","Only the external iliac nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The para-aortic nodes (fundus), the external iliac nodes (body), and the internal iliac nodes (cervix)', 'Uterine lymph drains by region: the fundus ascends with the ovarian vessels to the para-aortic nodes, the body drains mainly to the external iliac nodes, and the cervix drains to the internal iliac (and sacral) nodes. Knowing these pathways guides the extent of lymphadenectomy in gynecologic surgery. Drainage is not limited to a single node group.'),
('4275afcf-5a03-5268-a32f-31c44d829b8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lymphatic drainage of the prostate is to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external iliac nodes mediated by specific receptor activation","The para-aortic nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal iliac and sacral lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac and sacral lymph nodes', 'The prostate drains predominantly to the internal iliac lymph nodes, with additional drainage to the sacral and, sometimes, external iliac nodes. Inguinal nodes are involved only when disease extends into the prostatic urethra or beyond the gland. Para-aortic nodes are reached only after passage through the pelvic nodes.'),
('14c2fc0e-3a4a-538e-ac64-ee43125fa586'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lymphatic drainage of the bladder is to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacral nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external and internal iliac lymph nodes","The inguinal nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The para-aortic nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external and internal iliac lymph nodes', 'Lymph from the bladder drains to the external and internal iliac lymph nodes; the internal iliac nodes receive lymph from the trigone and lower bladder, while the external iliac nodes receive lymph from the anterior and superior walls. The sacral nodes receive only a minor contribution. Inguinal nodes are not the primary route for the bladder.'),
('6f93cd4b-1b30-5942-81f8-374099c2412f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lymphatic drainage of the rectum above the pectinate line is to:', '["The inguinal nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","The axillary nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The external iliac nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal iliac nodes (and the para-aortic nodes)"]'::jsonb, 'The internal iliac nodes (and the para-aortic nodes)', 'The mucosa above the pectinate line drains upward with the superior rectal vessels: lymph passes to the internal iliac nodes and then ascends via the para-aortic (inferior mesenteric) pathway. The pectinate line is the key boundary: below it, lymph drains to the superficial inguinal nodes. Axillary nodes are never involved in rectal drainage.'),
('e4010d4b-24b5-5d5d-a66d-1768626b8dcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lymphatic drainage of the anal canal below the pectinate line is to:', '["The internal iliac nodes","The superficial inguinal lymph nodes","The axillary nodes","None","The para-aortic nodes"]'::jsonb, 'The superficial inguinal lymph nodes', 'Below the pectinate line the anal canal is lined by squamous epithelium, and its lymph drains with the somatic lymphatics of the perineum to the superficial inguinal lymph nodes. This contrasts with the region above the pectinate line, which drains to the internal iliac nodes. The finding guides staging and management of anal carcinoma.'),
('20f05ef5-7466-51f9-90ae-8cfd44b05bc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sciatic nerve divides into:', '["The femoral and obturator nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","The tibial nerve and the common peroneal (fibular) nerve (usually at the popliteal fossa)","The tibial and sural nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","The peroneal and sural nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tibial nerve and the common peroneal (fibular) nerve (usually at the popliteal fossa)', 'The sciatic nerve, the largest nerve of the body (L4-S3), usually divides into the tibial and common peroneal (fibular) nerves at or just above the popliteal fossa, though division may occur anywhere along its course. The femoral and obturator nerves are separate branches of the lumbar plexus, and the sural nerve is a sensory branch formed later in the leg.'),
('5e7a1e3a-771c-535e-ae3f-3da13494a5c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sciatic nerve supplies the:', '["Lateral compartment of the thigh","Medial compartment of the thigh","Anterior compartment of the thigh","None","Posterior compartment of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior compartment of the thigh', 'The sciatic nerve supplies the hamstrings, which form the posterior compartment of the thigh. The anterior and medial compartments are supplied by the femoral and obturator nerves respectively, and the lateral compartment (mainly a functional term) is not innervated by the sciatic nerve. Its terminal tibial and common peroneal divisions then supply the leg and foot.'),
('af24e206-2773-5474-be17-72fa33e968fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The nerve to the obturator internus (L5-S2) supplies:', '["The obturator externus","The obturator membrane","None","The obturator internus muscle","All"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator internus muscle', 'The nerve to obturator internus arises from the sacral plexus (L5-S2), exits through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis, and re-enters the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen to supply the obturator internus muscle. It also gives a branch to the gemellus superior. The obturator externus is supplied by the obturator nerve.'),
('8f536cb2-a7d9-5b35-9499-4221b012911b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The nerve to the quadratus femoris (L4-S1) supplies:', '["The hamstrings resulting in decreased functional activity","The gluteus maximus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The quadratus femoris and the gemellus inferior","The piriformis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The quadratus femoris and the gemellus inferior', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris (L4-S1) passes below the piriformis and innervates both the quadratus femoris and the gemellus inferior muscles; it also supplies the hip joint capsule. The hamstrings are innervated by the sciatic nerve, gluteus maximus by the inferior gluteal nerve, and the piriformis by direct branches from the sacral plexus.'),
('db9ae874-e0ad-5732-8e7b-e763cad89f55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior gluteal artery and nerve exit the pelvis through:', '["The obturator foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater sciatic foramen (above the piriformis - suprapiriform foramen)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lesser sciatic foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (above the piriformis - suprapiriform foramen)', 'The superior gluteal artery and nerve leave the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen above the piriformis, hence the term suprapiriform foramen. They then pass between gluteus medius and minimus to supply those muscles and the tensor fasciae latae. The lesser sciatic foramen transmits the internal pudendal vessels, pudendal nerve, and nerve to obturator internus.'),
('508839d0-82ae-5ff6-a483-3e741ab99fd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior gluteal artery and nerve exit the pelvis through:', '["The inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater sciatic foramen (below the piriformis - infrapiriform foramen)","The obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","The lesser sciatic foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (below the piriformis - infrapiriform foramen)', 'The inferior gluteal artery and nerve pass through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis, in the infrapiriform foramen, to supply gluteus maximus and the posterior thigh. This foramen also transmits the sciatic nerve, pudendal nerve, and internal pudendal vessels. The obturator foramen is used by the obturator neurovascular bundle.'),
('6e76cb77-b275-50e5-bb14-ee797a9a128a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels exit the pelvis through:', '["The inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater sciatic foramen only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform), then re-enter the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen","The obturator foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (infrapiriform), then re-enter the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen', 'The pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels leave the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis and immediately re-enter the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen. They then run in the pudendal canal (of Alcock) on the lateral wall of the ischioanal fossa, supplying the perineum. This loop over the ischial spine is why pudendal block targets the spine.'),
('34daf4a1-7a1d-5f8b-957c-711e63f8acef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) exits the pelvis through:', '["The greater sciatic foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The obturator foramen (to supply the adductors of the thigh)","The lesser sciatic foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","The inguinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator foramen (to supply the adductors of the thigh)', 'The obturator nerve arises from the lumbar plexus (L2-L4), passes through the obturator foramen, and divides into anterior and posterior branches that supply the adductor muscles of the medial thigh, as well as the hip and knee joints. The greater and lesser sciatic foramina transmit the gluteal, sciatic, and pudendal structures.'),
('43fa0852-9c23-5207-8319-c57f02827670'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator artery and vein also pass through:', '["The lesser sciatic foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The greater sciatic foramen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","The obturator foramen (to supply the adductor region)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator foramen (to supply the adductor region)', 'The obturator artery and vein accompany the obturator nerve through the obturator foramen into the adductor (medial) compartment of the thigh. The artery is normally a branch of the internal iliac artery, but an accessory obturator artery frequently arises from the inferior epigastric artery and is at risk during hernia repair. The sciatic foramina do not transmit obturator vessels.'),
('2643e429-1df3-5bf5-a128-2b85052e74fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pelvic wall is formed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the pubis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacrum, coccyx, ilium, ischium, and pubis (with the obturator internus and piriformis muscles)","Only the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the ilium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sacrum, coccyx, ilium, ischium, and pubis (with the obturator internus and piriformis muscles)', 'The bony pelvic wall is made up of the two hip bones (ilium, ischium, pubis), the sacrum, and the coccyx. The piriformis covers the posterior wall and the obturator internus the lateral wall, both forming part of the functional pelvic wall and giving origin to fascia of the pelvic sidewall. No single bone alone forms the pelvis.'),
('31036005-14ad-5a70-9c54-0fef3bedaf25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The obturator internus covers:', '["The posterior pelvic wall resulting in decreased functional activity","The medial pelvic wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lateral pelvic wall (its fascia forms the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The anterior pelvic wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral pelvic wall (its fascia forms the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)', 'The obturator internus arises inside the pelvis and forms the lateral pelvic wall; its tendon leaves through the lesser sciatic foramen, and strengthening its fascia (the obturator fascia) forms the lateral boundary of the ischioanal (ischiorectal) fossa. The piriformis, by contrast, clothes the posterior pelvic wall.'),
('cbf81a92-c9e5-508c-a011-ce95b12ed566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The piriformis covers:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior pelvic wall (it also forms the posterior boundary of the greater sciatic foramen)","The anterior pelvic wall mediated by specific receptor activation","The lateral pelvic wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The medial pelvic wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior pelvic wall (it also forms the posterior boundary of the greater sciatic foramen)', 'The piriformis arises from the anterior sacrum and passes through the greater sciatic foramen, covering the posterior pelvic wall. By filling the posterior part of the foramen it divides it into suprapiriform and infrapiriform compartments that transmit the gluteal and sciatic structures. The lateral wall is covered by the obturator internus.'),
('1399cf3e-6a75-562d-b42b-dabf1cd7041b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacral foramina are:', '["Openings in the sacrum (anterior and posterior) that transmit the sacral nerves and blood vessels","Openings in the coccyx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Openings in the ilium mediated by specific receptor activation","Openings in the ischium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Openings in the sacrum (anterior and posterior) that transmit the sacral nerves and blood vessels', 'The anterior and posterior sacral foramina are openings in the sacrum through which the anterior and posterior rami of the sacral spinal nerves and accompanying vessels pass. The coccyx has no such foramina, and the ilium and ischium contain no corresponding openings. These foramina allow the sacral plexus to form and branches to exit the pelvis.'),
('d4c5fd00-ea7a-589d-89a7-09ac24d2cdf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacral canal is:', '["The same as the sacral hiatus resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the sacral foramina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The continuation of the vertebral canal within the sacrum (contains the cauda equina)"]'::jsonb, 'The continuation of the vertebral canal within the sacrum (contains the cauda equina)', 'The sacral canal is the continuation of the vertebral canal through the sacrum; it contains the cauda equina and the filum terminale and terminates at the sacral hiatus. The hiatus is the open lower end of the canal used for caudal anesthesia, and the foramina are its lateral communications, so the three are not the same structure.'),
('14f9630d-8b01-544c-a604-2b0fe1002ca5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Caudal anesthesia is performed through:', '["The spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thoracic interspace mediated by specific receptor activation","The sacral hiatus (to anesthetize the sacral nerves)","The lumbar interspace leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral hiatus (to anesthetize the sacral nerves)', 'Caudal (epidural) anesthesia is achieved by injecting anesthetic through the sacral hiatus, the opening at the inferior end of the sacral canal, into the sacral epidural space where it blocks the sacral and lower lumbar nerve roots. This approach is used for anorectal, perineal, and pelvic procedures. The thoracic and lumbar interspaces are used for spinal or lumbar epidural techniques, not caudal block.'),
('f82e70b1-85e1-57b5-ab12-724e34217bc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacrospinous ligament spans:', '["From the ischial spine to the sacrum (creates the greater and lesser sciatic foramina)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","From the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the pubis to the sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the ischial spine to the sacrum (creates the greater and lesser sciatic foramina)', 'The sacrospinous ligament passes from the ischial spine to the lateral sacrum and coccyx, converting the sciatic notch into the greater and lesser sciatic foramina (together with the sacrotuberous ligament). The sacrotuberous ligament, by comparison, connects the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum. Together they bind the pelvic girdle against the pull of the gluteal muscles.'),
('163eeff4-2bb5-5474-926f-14f0d2894ceb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sacrotuberous ligament spans:', '["From the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum (helps stabilize the sacroiliac joint and creates the greater and lesser sciatic foramina)","From the pubis to the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From the ischial spine to the sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the ischial tuberosity to the sacrum (helps stabilize the sacroiliac joint and creates the greater and lesser sciatic foramina)', 'The sacrotuberous ligament runs from the ischial tuberosity to the posterior iliac spine, sacrum, and coccyx. With the sacrospinous ligament it resists rotation of the sacrum and helps form the boundaries of the greater and lesser sciatic foramina; it is also a site of attachment for the gluteus maximus. The sacrospinous ligament is the structure spanning ischial spine to sacrum.'),
('161743a3-79e4-587e-a7f4-012049686b5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The common iliac arteries bifurcate at:', '["L5-S1 (into the internal and external iliac arteries)","L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","L4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","S1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L5-S1 (into the internal and external iliac arteries)', 'Each common iliac artery descends to the level of the sacroiliac joint (approximately L5-S1), where it divides into the internal and external iliac arteries. The abdominal aorta bifurcates into the two common iliac arteries at the level of L4, a different landmark. This separation into internal (pelvic) and external (lower limb) trunks dictates pelvic vascular supply.'),
('51d01085-e599-5b1b-90e9-c2a33427c512'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates into:', '["The two common iliac arteries (at L4)","The internal and external iliac arteries","None","The femoral arteries","All"]'::jsonb, 'The two common iliac arteries (at L4)', 'The abdominal aorta ends at the level of L4 (just left of midline) by dividing into the two common iliac arteries. Each common iliac artery then divides further into the internal and external iliac arteries, and the external iliac artery continues as the femoral artery under the inguinal ligament. Thus the aorta does not directly give off the internal, external, or femoral arteries.'),
('53c6652d-589d-5230-b05b-94e2ca969db5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The median sacral artery arises from:', '["The posterior aspect of the abdominal aorta (just above the bifurcation)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external iliac artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The internal iliac artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The common iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior aspect of the abdominal aorta (just above the bifurcation)', 'The median sacral artery is a small vessel that arises from the posterior aspect of the abdominal aorta just above the aortic bifurcation (around L4-L5) and descends on the anterior surface of the sacrum and coccyx. It is a vestige of the fetal continuation of the aorta and anastomoses with the lateral sacral arteries. It does not arise from the iliac arteries.'),
('3012f84d-5f41-55e4-8cba-ada52e55bf52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The median sacral vein drains into:', '["None","The left common iliac vein","The portal vein","The right common iliac vein","The IVC"]'::jsonb, 'The left common iliac vein', 'The unpaired median sacral vein ascends on the anterior sacrum and drains into the left common iliac vein. This is a readily examinable fact distinct from the paired lateral sacral veins, which drain into the internal iliac veins. The portal vein drains the gastrointestinal tract, and the IVC receives the common iliac veins rather than the median sacral vein directly.'),
('27ea323f-7d25-5cb9-972d-5b4e0b8ea659'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The external iliac artery becomes the femoral artery at:', '["The pelvic brim resulting in decreased functional activity","The knee accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal ligament (at the mid-inguinal point)","The hip joint without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inguinal ligament (at the mid-inguinal point)', 'The external iliac artery continues as the femoral artery as it passes beneath the inguinal ligament at the mid-inguinal point (midway between the ASIS and pubic symphysis). The pelvic brim is where the ureter crosses the external iliac vessels, and the hip joint and knee are well distal to this transition. This is also where the femoral pulse is palpated.'),
('e9d41518-ff56-586a-a8f1-7dff6e3e9aab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inguinal ligament is:', '["A ligament from the ASIS to the iliac crest resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis (from the ASIS to the pubic tubercle)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ligament from the pubic symphysis to the pubic tubercle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis (from the ASIS to the pubic tubercle)', 'The inguinal ligament is the free thickened lower border of the external oblique aponeurosis, stretching between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic tubercle. It forms the floor of the inguinal canal and the upper boundary of the femoral triangle, and its midpoint marks the transition of the external iliac to the femoral artery. The other choices describe unrelated bony relationships.'),
('67b56c35-d19c-5fe3-8fac-26a54746782b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The femoral canal is:', '["The space between the femoral vein and the inguinal ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","The innermost compartment of the femoral sheath (contains the femoral canal lymphatics and the femoral ring)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the femoral artery and vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The innermost compartment of the femoral sheath (contains the femoral canal lymphatics and the femoral ring)', 'The femoral sheath has three compartments: lateral (containing the femoral artery), intermediate (femoral vein), and medial, which forms the femoral canal. This canal contains lymphatics (including the deep inguinal node of Cloquet) and loose connective tissue and is the site of femoral hernias through the femoral ring. It is not a simple space between the vessels and ligament.'),
('2c75b94f-70b0-57ef-87b0-afae15e8402b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The femoral ring is:', '["The opening at the superior end of the femoral canal (bounded by the inguinal ligament, the lacunar ligament, the femoral vein, and the pectineal ligament)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of the femoral vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening of the femoral canal into the thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The opening at the superior end of the femoral canal (bounded by the inguinal ligament, the lacunar ligament, the femoral vein, and the pectineal ligament)', 'The femoral ring is the abdominal (superior) opening of the femoral canal, bordered anteriorly by the inguinal ligament, medially by the lacunar ligament, laterally by the femoral vein, and posteriorly by the pectineal ligament. It is the site through which a femoral hernia enters the canal, not the opening of the femoral vein itself or the lower saphenous opening of the canal.'),
('9bc7995f-8070-5906-8e8c-8fadd6dfbd21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Femoral hernias are more common on the:', '["Left side resulting in decreased functional activity","Both sides equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only on the right mediated by specific receptor activation","Right side (due to the presence of the sigmoid colon on the left)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right side (due to the presence of the sigmoid colon on the left)', 'Femoral hernias are more common on the right side; a classic explanation is that the sigmoid colon on the left protects the left femoral ring from being pushed by intra-abdominal pressure. They can occur on either side, so ''only on the right'' is too absolute.'),
('9710bc63-c43c-5285-b762-223f67a934ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The lacunar ligament (Gimbernat ligament) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A ligament of the hip joint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A triangular ligament that forms the medial boundary of the femoral ring (from the inguinal ligament to the pubic bone)","A ligament of the knee leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular ligament that forms the medial boundary of the femoral ring (from the inguinal ligament to the pubic bone)', 'The lacunar ligament is a triangular extension of the medial end of the inguinal ligament that passes backward to the pecten pubis, forming the medial boundary of the femoral ring. It is an abdominal wall structure, not a joint ligament; its sharp crescentic edge is vulnerable during femoral hernia surgery.'),
('a619fed5-f601-5fe8-b5b7-0dfd8c62342e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pectineal ligament (Cooper ligament) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thickening of the periosteum of the pubic bone (used in hernia repair)","A ligament from the pubis to the ASIS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ligament from the pubis to the ischial spine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickening of the periosteum of the pubic bone (used in hernia repair)', 'Cooper''s (pectineal) ligament is a strong thickening of the periosteum along the pecten pubis, continuous laterally with the iliopubic tract; it is the fixation site for the McVay repair of femoral and inguinal hernias. It does not span from the pubis to the ASIS or ischial spine.'),
('9e5b14fc-c347-5d02-b187-b901f31d0448'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The iliopubic tract is:', '["A thickening of the transversalis fascia (below the inguinal ligament) that is important for hernia surgery","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A thickening of the external oblique aponeurosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ligament from the ilium to the pubis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickening of the transversalis fascia (below the inguinal ligament) that is important for hernia surgery', 'The iliopubic tract is a thickened band of transversalis fascia running parallel and deep to the inguinal ligament; it is a key landmark and fixation point in laparoscopic inguinal hernia repair. It is not derived from the external oblique aponeurosis and is a fascial structure rather than a bone-to-bone ligament.'),
('c82e601a-8fcd-58ed-a106-6273ac286983'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The conjoined tendon (falx inguinalis) is:', '["A tendon of the external oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The fused aponeuroses of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles (inserts into the pubic crest)","A tendon of the rectus abdominis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fused aponeuroses of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles (inserts into the pubic crest)', 'The conjoined tendon (falx inguinalis) is formed by the fusion of the aponeuroses of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis as they insert into the pubic crest and pecten pubis. It reinforces the medial part of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal and is not formed by the external oblique or rectus aponeuroses.'),
('c2d76f84-2771-5795-a8d3-66c708191e26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The Hesselbach triangle (inguinal triangle) is:', '["The area of the deep inguinal ring resulting in decreased functional activity","The area of the abdominal wall bounded by the inferior epigastric artery, the rectus abdominis, and the inguinal ligament","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The area of the superficial inguinal ring without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area of the abdominal wall bounded by the inferior epigastric artery, the rectus abdominis, and the inguinal ligament', 'The Hesselbach (inguinal) triangle is bounded laterally by the inferior epigastric artery, medially by the lateral border of the rectus abdominis, and inferiorly by the inguinal ligament. Direct inguinal hernias protrude through this triangle, whereas indirect hernias emerge at the deep inguinal ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.'),
('160084b6-271b-51ac-8379-931207229479'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The fascia transversalis is:', '["The inner layer of the abdominal wall (the transversalis fascia) that forms the deep inguinal ring","The same as the peritoneum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the internal oblique mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the external oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inner layer of the abdominal wall (the transversalis fascia) that forms the deep inguinal ring', 'The transversalis fascia is the deep investing layer lining the abdominal cavity, lying just outside the peritoneum and deep to the transversus abdominis muscle. It is the layer in which the deep inguinal ring forms and bulges to create the spermatic cord''s internal spermatic fascia.'),
('804cd3fc-f153-5e2d-8790-0a7155bca4b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inguinal canal is about:', '["7-8 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","4-5 cm long (in adults)","10 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","1-2 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4-5 cm long (in adults)', 'The inguinal canal measures about 4-5 cm in adults, running obliquely between the deep and superficial inguinal rings, parallel to the inguinal ligament. The oblique, slit-like course helps weaken the abdominal wall and predisposes to inguinal hernia formation.'),
('f722f7c9-2cbd-55db-80ac-ae6aecb977aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inguinal canal is directed:', '["Horizontally resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obliquely (from superolateral to inferomedial)","Anteroposteriorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertically leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely (from superolateral to inferomedial)', 'The inguinal canal runs obliquely downward and medially, from the deep inguinal ring superolaterally to the superficial inguinal ring inferomedially, roughly parallel to the inguinal ligament. This obliquity means the two rings do not overlap, so increased intra-abdominal pressure compresses rather than distends the canal.'),
('16d677e8-9c9b-5128-b126-943cec578e4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The walls of the inguinal canal are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Four walls accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only superior and inferior mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior (external oblique), posterior (transversalis fascia), superior (internal oblique and transversus abdominis), and inferior (inguinal ligament)","Only anterior and posterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (external oblique), posterior (transversalis fascia), superior (internal oblique and transversus abdominis), and inferior (inguinal ligament)', 'The inguinal canal has four named boundaries: anterior wall from the external oblique aponeurosis (reinforced laterally by the internal oblique), posterior wall from the transversalis fascia and conjoined tendon, roof from the arched lower fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis, and floor from the inguinal ligament (with medial reinforcement by the lacunar ligament).'),
('6f687655-71a7-5ad5-b4ac-ad5253ceb46f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior wall (floor) of the inguinal canal is:', '["The inguinal ligament (the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external oblique mediated by specific receptor activation","The conjoint tendon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The transversalis fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inguinal ligament (the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis)', 'The floor of the inguinal canal is formed by the inguinal ligament, the rolled-under, thickened lower free edge of the external oblique aponeurosis, reinforced medially by the lacunar ligament and laterally by the iliopubic tract. The conjoint tendon and transversalis fascia contribute to the posterior wall, not the floor.'),
('f4ddbf1e-4f50-5e73-a61c-26a12c998d65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior wall (roof) of the inguinal canal is:', '["The transversalis fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles (their arched fibers)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles (their arched fibers)', 'The roof of the inguinal canal is formed by the arched lowest fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles as they arch over the canal toward the conjoined tendon. Contraction of these muscle arches flattens the roof, helping to guard the canal against herniation.'),
('8af178d8-c6e1-5fed-89b6-f31cce9ad1f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The contents of the inguinal canal in males:', '["The femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The round ligament of the uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","The spermatic cord (with the vas deferens, testicular vessels, nerves, and lymphatics)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spermatic cord (with the vas deferens, testicular vessels, nerves, and lymphatics)', 'In males the inguinal canal transmits the spermatic cord, which contains the ductus deferens, testicular artery, pampiniform plexus, autonomic nerves and lymphatics, plus the ilioinguinal nerve. The femoral nerve and femoral artery lie in the thigh, not in the inguinal canal, which in females instead carries the round ligament of the uterus.'),
('f6d33744-0b86-5788-968d-877da0c68d58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The contents of the inguinal canal in females:', '["The ovary resulting in decreased functional activity","The round ligament of the uterus (and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The spermatic cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The uterine tube leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The round ligament of the uterus (and the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve)', 'In females the inguinal canal transmits the round ligament of the uterus as it passes to the labium majus, accompanied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve. The ovary and uterine tube remain within the pelvic cavity and are not canal contents.'),
('6e11fc79-0b00-5c92-b5db-8eb87486fa58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The genitofemoral nerve''s genital branch enters the inguinal canal:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Through the femoral canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the deep inguinal ring (supplies the cremaster muscle and the scrotal/labial skin)","Through the superficial inguinal ring leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the deep inguinal ring (supplies the cremaster muscle and the scrotal/labial skin)', 'The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve passes through the deep inguinal ring, descends within the spermatic cord, and supplies the cremaster muscle (via the cremasteric reflex) and the skin of the scrotum or labia majora. The superficial inguinal ring is the exit for the ilioinguinal nerve.'),
('f6f36e9a-abeb-57cb-906a-cde8553a07e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The ilioinguinal nerve enters the inguinal canal:', '["Through the femoral canal resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the deep inguinal ring mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the superficial inguinal ring (supplies the skin of the root of the penis/scrotum or labia)"]'::jsonb, 'Through the superficial inguinal ring (supplies the skin of the root of the penis/scrotum or labia)', 'The ilioinguinal nerve enters the inguinal canal from its lateral edge (with the spermatic cord or round ligament) and exits through the superficial inguinal ring to supply the skin of the root of the penis and anterior scrotum in males, or the mons pubis and labia majora in females. It does not pass through the deep inguinal ring and is commonly encountered in during inguinal hernia surgery.'),
('2a657e70-08a3-53d8-a8f6-e5097953e33c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2) supplies:', '["The cremaster muscle (genital branch) and the skin of the anterior scrotum/labia","Only the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the cremaster muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","The testis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cremaster muscle (genital branch) and the skin of the anterior scrotum/labia', 'The genitofemoral nerve splits into a genital branch that supplies the cremaster muscle and the skin of the anterior scrotum or labia majora, and a femoral branch that supplies the skin over the femoral triangle. The testis is innervated by autonomic fibers travelling with the testicular vessels, not by this nerve.'),
('ab0db8f5-5a38-5b52-9fbe-49c7b1ea34fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) supplies:', '["The skin of the upper medial thigh, the root of the penis, and the scrotum/labia","The perineum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The cremaster muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The thigh muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin of the upper medial thigh, the root of the penis, and the scrotum/labia', 'The ilioinguinal nerve (L1) is purely sensory in its terminal part, supplying the skin of the upper medial thigh and the root of the penis and anterior scrotum in males, or the mons and labia majora in females. It is purely cutaneous and does not supply muscles, the perineum, or the cremaster.'),
('67b9c0f9-bcc9-5cdf-8161-a6603f814a8c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The iliohypogastric nerve (L1) supplies:', '["The scrotum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","The perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The skin of the suprapubic region (and the lower abdominal wall muscles)"]'::jsonb, 'The skin of the suprapubic region (and the lower abdominal wall muscles)', 'The iliohypogastric nerve (L1) has a lateral cutaneous branch to the gluteal region and an anterior cutaneous branch that supplies the skin of the suprapubic region, while its muscular branches innervate the lower fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis. It does not supply the thigh, perineum, or scrotum.'),
('a5ce89bd-8376-50d4-99cf-f364136414eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The illioinguinal and iliohypogastric nerves are at risk during:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Inguinal hernia repair (important to identify and protect these nerves)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholecystectomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Appendectomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inguinal hernia repair (important to identify and protect these nerves)', 'The iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves run in the layers of the lower abdominal wall near the inguinal canal, so they are vulnerable to injury or entrapment during inguinal hernia repair, sometimes causing chronic postoperative groin pain. Cholecystectomy and appendectomy are performed at sites remote from these nerves.'),
('d19d462f-c6f4-55f8-95e8-4fb3cc3addae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The preperitoneal space is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The space between the external and internal oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space between the peritoneum and the transversalis fascia (used in preperitoneal hernia repair)","The space between the skin and the external oblique without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the peritoneum and the transversalis fascia (used in preperitoneal hernia repair)', 'The preperitoneal (extraperitoneal) space lies between the peritoneum and the transversalis fascia and contains the deep inguinal ring structures, the epigastric vessels, and the bladder in the retropubic area. It is the plane approached in preperitoneal (including laparoscopic) hernia repair, not a plane between the muscle layers or skin.'),
('6f481c01-4e1a-5089-b989-543e624ae417'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The prevesical space (space of Retzius) is:', '["The space between the bladder and the rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space between the bladder and the peritoneum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the pubic bone and the bladder (in the retropubic region)"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the pubic bone and the bladder (in the retropubic region)', 'The space of Retzius (prevesical/retropubic space) is the extraperitoneal interval between the pubic symphysis and the anterior wall of the urinary bladder, containing fat and the vesical venous plexus. It is entered in suprapubic cystostomy and prostatectomy, and is distinct from the rectovesical space behind the bladder.'),
('8a926be3-214b-56c2-9f39-7279216b92de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The rectus sheath is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The sheath of the external oblique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sheath of the pyramidalis muscle mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sheath that encloses the rectus abdominis muscle (formed by the aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis)"]'::jsonb, 'The sheath that encloses the rectus abdominis muscle (formed by the aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis)', 'The rectus sheath is a fibrous sleeve formed by the aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis that encloses the rectus abdominis (and usually the pyramidalis) muscle. Its anterior and posterior walls have a characteristic arrangement that changes at the arcuate line.'),
('9081af8f-54b8-59bb-af4f-82609dcce3d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The arcuate line (of Douglas) is:', '["The line of the pubic crest resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The line where the posterior wall of the rectus sheath ends (below this line, all aponeuroses pass anterior to the rectus muscle)","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The line of the inguinal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The line where the posterior wall of the rectus sheath ends (below this line, all aponeuroses pass anterior to the rectus muscle)', 'The arcuate line, about halfway between the umbilicus and pubic symphysis, marks where the posterior wall of the rectus sheath ends; below it the aponeuroses of all three flat muscles pass anterior to the rectus abdominis, which then lies directly on the transversalis fascia. This transition is important in rectus sheath hematomas and surgical incisions.'),
('a406fae2-4c43-55ce-a389-bc3b4bf3b6b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The rectus sheath above the arcuate line has:', '["Only a posterior wall resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An anterior wall (external oblique and internal oblique aponeuroses) and a posterior wall (internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses)","walls mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only an anterior wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An anterior wall (external oblique and internal oblique aponeuroses) and a posterior wall (internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses)', 'Above the arcuate line the external oblique aponeurosis and the anterior layer of the internal oblique aponeurosis form the anterior wall, while the posterior layer of the internal oblique aponeurosis together with the transversus abdominis aponeurosis form the posterior wall. This results in a complete sheath around the rectus muscle.'),
('684d0813-344b-5378-8e04-5134ab1dcbdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The rectus sheath below the arcuate line has:', '["An anterior wall (all aponeuroses pass anteriorly) and no posterior wall (the rectus lies on the transversalis fascia)","Only a posterior wall accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An anterior and posterior wall mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","walls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An anterior wall (all aponeuroses pass anteriorly) and no posterior wall (the rectus lies on the transversalis fascia)', 'Below the arcuate line the aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis all pass anterior to the rectus abdominis, so the posterior wall of the sheath is absent and the muscle rests on the transversalis fascia. This is why incisions below the arcuate line easily enter the extraperitoneal space.'),
('b3cc16a0-a48b-545a-b471-24ad96486452'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior epigastric artery is:', '["A branch of the femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","A branch of the external iliac artery (supplies the rectus abdominis muscle)","A branch of the abdominal aorta mediated by specific receptor activation","A branch of the internal iliac artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A branch of the external iliac artery (supplies the rectus abdominis muscle)', 'The inferior epigastric artery arises from the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament, ascends in the posterior rectus sheath, and supplies the rectus abdominis before anastomosing with the superior epigastric artery. It forms the lateral boundary of the Hesselbach triangle and distinguishes direct from indirect inguinal hernias.'),
('fb285526-5329-5885-8ac3-2cf9a4bc08b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior epigastric artery is:', '["The terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery (descends into the rectus sheath)","A branch of the femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A branch of the external iliac artery mediated by specific receptor activation","A branch of the intercostal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The terminal branch of the internal thoracic artery (descends into the rectus sheath)', 'The superior epigastric artery is the lower terminal branch of the internal thoracic (mammary) artery; it descends within the posterior rectus sheath and anastomoses with the inferior epigastric artery to supply the rectus abdominis. This anastomosis connects the subclavian and external iliac arterial territories and is important for abdominal wall collateral flow.'),
('3ee255bb-c20c-570b-a761-e95a523b80cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The transversus abdominis muscle is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The outermost mediated by specific receptor activation","The middle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The innermost of the three anterolateral abdominal wall muscles"]'::jsonb, 'The innermost of the three anterolateral abdominal wall muscles', 'The transversus abdominis lies deepest among the three flat anterolateral muscles, with its fibers running horizontally. It compresses the abdominal contents and, with the internal oblique, forms the roof of the inguinal canal and contributes to the conjoined tendon.'),
('fcc1f1af-5bae-5c74-9a18-d23da92ce791'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal oblique muscle is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The outermost layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The middle layer of the anterolateral abdominal wall","The innermost layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The middle layer of the anterolateral abdominal wall', 'The internal oblique lies between the external oblique superficially and the transversus abdominis deeply, making it the middle layer of the anterolateral abdominal wall. Its fibers run superomedially at a right angle to those of the external oblique.'),
('ca62c2cd-0008-5eca-82f9-bc53a2333352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The external oblique muscle is:', '["The innermost layer resulting in decreased functional activity","The middle layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The outermost layer of the anterolateral abdominal wall","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The outermost layer of the anterolateral abdominal wall', 'The external oblique is the most superficial of the three flat muscles of the anterolateral abdominal wall, with fibers running inferomedially; its aponeurosis forms the inguinal ligament inferiorly and contributes to the anterior rectus sheath. The internal oblique and transversus abdominis lie deep to it.'),
('95aca869-b540-5596-830d-4802b9425631'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The rectus abdominis muscle is:', '["A short muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","A quadrangular muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A long, straplike muscle on each side of the midline (separated by the linea alba)","A triangular muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A long, straplike muscle on each side of the midline (separated by the linea alba)', 'The rectus abdominis is a long, strap-like muscle running vertically on each side of the midline, separated from its fellow by the linea alba and crossed by three or four tendinous intersections. It flexes the trunk, tenses the abdominal wall, and is enclosed within the rectus sheath.'),
('a545e3a0-82a6-56e8-bc80-b1109e90bae9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The pyramidalis muscle is innervated by:', '["The subcostal nerve (T12)","The genitofemoral nerve","The iliohypogastric nerve","None","The ileoinguinal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The subcostal nerve (T12)', 'The pyramidalis, a small triangular muscle in the lower rectus sheath just above the pubis, is innervated by the subcostal nerve (T12). It tenses the linea alba; its supply is somatic from the last thoracic nerve rather than from the L1 nerves or the genitofemoral nerve.'),
('ea7b6cfb-a49a-53b6-9403-1e2faf095350'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The linea alba is:', '["A blood vessel resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ligament mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A midline tendinous seam (from the xiphoid to the pubic symphysis) where the aponeuroses of the anterolateral muscles meet","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A midline tendinous seam (from the xiphoid to the pubic symphysis) where the aponeuroses of the anterolateral muscles meet', 'The linea alba is a midline tendinous raphe running from the xiphoid process to the pubic symphysis, formed by the interweaving aponeuroses of the external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis of both sides. It is the widest part above the umbilicus and the standard midline surgical incision site because it is relatively avascular.'),
('a8251ac3-b904-5111-8ad1-6558c6955eb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The abdominal wall is innervated by:', '["The intercostal nerves (T7-T11), the subcostal nerve (T12), and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (L1)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only L1 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the intercostal nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only T12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The intercostal nerves (T7-T11), the subcostal nerve (T12), and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves (L1)', 'The anterolateral abdominal wall is segmentally supplied by the lower intercostal nerves T7-T11, the subcostal nerve T12, and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal branches of L1. These nerves also carry the dermatomes that allow localization of spinal cord levels such as T10 at the umbilicus.'),
('cecfb01a-92df-5335-ba1a-cf536d73c392'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The dermatome at the level of the umbilicus is:', '["L2","T10","T8","T12","L1"]'::jsonb, 'T10', 'The umbilicus marks the T10 dermatome, a key clinical landmark for localizing spinal cord and nerve root lesions. The nipple corresponds to T4, the xiphoid to T6, and the inguinal region to L1, so T10 is the level of the umbilicus.'),
('b7ce50ce-c806-5639-ab50-36031c83bad4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The dermatome at the level of the xiphoid is:', '["T12","L1","T4","T10","T6"]'::jsonb, 'T6', 'The xiphoid process is at the T6 dermatome, whereas the nipple is at T4, the umbilicus at T10, and the inguinal ligament at L1. Dermatome testing is used to determine the level of spinal cord or nerve root involvement.'),
('13869da1-5ed1-58ba-87ee-aa0beb7ea911'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The dermatome at the level of the inguinal ligament is:', '["L3","L2","L1","T10","T12"]'::jsonb, 'L1', 'The inguinal ligament (inguinal region) lies within the L1 dermatome, supplied by the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves. This level contrasts with T10 at the umbilicus and T12 in the suprapubic region, making L1 the last of the anterior trunk dermatomes.'),
('e1c28db6-888a-5741-9d95-4860c109a516'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The umbilicus is an important landmark because:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","It marks the T10 dermatome and the level of the aortic bifurcation (L4)","It marks the L2 dermatome mediated by specific receptor activation","It marks the T12 dermatome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It marks the T10 dermatome and the level of the aortic bifurcation (L4)', 'The umbilicus is a dual landmark: it lies at the T10 dermatome and at the L3-L4 vertebral level, corresponding to the bifurcation of the abdominal aorta into the common iliac arteries. This makes it valuable both for sensory level testing and for clinical auscultation and palpation of the aortic bifurcation.'),
('b2390bea-67cc-5e5d-ae3c-3a5c662703fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior epigastric artery arises from:', '["The femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The internal iliac artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The common iliac artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external iliac artery (just above the inguinal ligament)"]'::jsonb, 'The external iliac artery (just above the inguinal ligament)', 'The inferior epigastric artery arises from the anterior surface of the external iliac artery just above the inguinal ligament, then runs superomedially in the posterior rectus sheath. It is the lateral boundary of the Hesselbach triangle and its pulsation at the deep inguinal ring is used to distinguish direct from indirect inguinal hernias.'),
('b6e0bca7-995c-5a5c-801f-01a8d8954169'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superior epigastric artery arises from:', '["The internal thoracic artery (a branch of the subclavian artery)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The intercostal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal thoracic artery (a branch of the subclavian artery)', 'The superior epigastric artery is the downward continuation of the internal thoracic (mammary) artery, which itself arises from the first part of the subclavian artery. It enters the rectus sheath above and anastomoses within the rectus abdominis with the inferior epigastric artery, forming a collateral channel between subclavian and external iliac territories.'),
('0dd61720-afab-5e37-8c57-2835cf8d878d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superficial epigastric artery arises from:', '["The internal iliac artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior epigastric artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The femoral artery (below the inguinal ligament)","The external iliac artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral artery (below the inguinal ligament)', 'The superficial epigastric artery arises from the femoral artery just below the inguinal ligament, pierces the fascia lata, and ascends over the lower anterior abdominal wall toward the umbilicus. It supplies the superficial layers and anastomoses with branches of the inferior epigastric artery.'),
('1533bff9-ef93-5a37-a061-92d6b4ef4e02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The superficial circumflex iliac artery arises from:', '["The femoral artery","None","The inferior epigastric artery","The internal iliac artery","The external iliac artery"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral artery', 'The superficial circumflex iliac artery arises from the femoral artery just below the inguinal ligament, runs laterally and superiorly parallel to the ligament, and supplies the skin and fascia of the upper lateral thigh and lower abdominal wall. It is a small superficial branch of the femoral artery, not of the iliac vessels or inferior epigastric artery.'),
('a2d55a26-7205-54d4-8fa5-29af08a8f7a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The abdominal wall veins drain into:', '["Only the portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The superior and inferior epigastric veins (to the internal thoracic and external iliac veins), and the axillary and femoral veins","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The azygos vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superior and inferior epigastric veins (to the internal thoracic and external iliac veins), and the axillary and femoral veins', 'The deep veins of the anterior abdominal wall follow the arteries: superior epigastric veins drain to the internal thoracic (hence subclavian/axillary) veins, and inferior epigastric veins drain to the external iliac (hence femoral) veins; superficial veins such as the superficial epigastric drain into the femoral vein. These pathways become clinically important as portosystemic collaterals in portal hypertension via the paraumbilical veins.'),
('1bd0abff-3a9b-5f3a-b518-9f79f007e642'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Caput medusae is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Dilated periumbilical veins (indicating portal hypertension with portosystemic anastomosis)","Dilated veins in the legs mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilated veins in the chest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated periumbilical veins (indicating portal hypertension with portosystemic anastomosis)', 'Caput medusae refers to tortuous, dilated periumbilical veins radiating from the umbilicus, caused by portal hypertension. Blood from the portal vein passes through the paraumbilical veins (in the round ligament) into the superior and inferior epigastric veins, which then drain to the systemic circulation; flow direction can be distinguished from inferior vena cava obstruction.'),
('539de61b-638a-5a32-8c18-8db66d88b91e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The umbilicus has:', '["Only the round ligament resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The median umbilical ligament (urachus), the medial umbilical ligaments (obliterated umbilical arteries), and the round ligament (obliterated umbilical vein)","Only the median umbilical ligament mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the medial umbilical ligaments without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The median umbilical ligament (urachus), the medial umbilical ligaments (obliterated umbilical arteries), and the round ligament (obliterated umbilical vein)', 'The umbilicus is the meeting point of three fetal remnants: the median umbilical ligament (obliterated urachus from the bladder apex), the paired medial umbilical ligaments (obliterated distal umbilical arteries), and the round ligament of the liver (obliterated left umbilical vein). These remnants attach to the anterior abdominal wall and may be involved in midline hernias.'),
('8ddb7966-d445-520d-b366-7aadb24dc0eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The medial umbilical ligaments (two) are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The obliterated umbilical vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obliterated distal parts of the umbilical arteries (run from the internal iliac artery to the umbilicus)","The obliterated urachus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obliterated distal parts of the umbilical arteries (run from the internal iliac artery to the umbilicus)', 'The paired medial umbilical ligaments are the obliterated distal portions of the fetal umbilical arteries, which run from the internal iliac arteries to the umbilicus after birth. They appear as fibrous cords on the inner surface of the anterior abdominal wall; the obliterated urachus forms the single median umbilical ligament and the umbilical vein forms the round ligament.'),
('adb45d47-8276-522f-8177-7b5b9e8def54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The median umbilical ligament is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obliterated umbilical arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","The obliterated urachus (from the bladder to the umbilicus)","The obliterated umbilical vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obliterated urachus (from the bladder to the umbilicus)', 'The median umbilical ligament is the obliterated remnant of the urachus, running from the apex of the bladder to the umbilicus in the midline of the extraperitoneal space. It is distinct from the paired medial umbilical ligaments (obliterated umbilical arteries) and the round ligament (obliterated umbilical vein); a patent urachus can cause urine leakage from the umbilicus.'),
('9c4823d0-a7dd-5a65-bc10-e561de375392'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The peritoneum lines:', '["Only the viscera resulting in decreased functional activity","The walls of the abdominal and pelvic cavities (parietal peritoneum) and covers the viscera (visceral peritoneum)","Only the abdominal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the pelvic cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The walls of the abdominal and pelvic cavities (parietal peritoneum) and covers the viscera (visceral peritoneum)', 'The peritoneum is a serous membrane with two continuous layers: the parietal peritoneum lining the inner walls of the abdominal and pelvic cavities, and the visceral peritoneum covering the organs. The potential space between the layers contains a thin film of serous fluid, and its folds form mesenteries, omenta, and ligaments.'),
('a6e1ad0b-cac8-58e7-8302-7ee4a53dba7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The parietal peritoneum is innervated by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The somatic nerves (e.g., intercostal and lumbar nerves) - sensitive to pain","Not innervated mediated by specific receptor activation","The autonomic nerves - sensitive to stretch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The somatic nerves (e.g., intercostal and lumbar nerves) - sensitive to pain', 'The parietal peritoneum receives somatic innervation from the intercostal and lumbar nerves, so it is highly sensitive to pain and produces sharp, well-localized pain. Autonomic nerves supply the visceral peritoneum, not the parietal layer.'),
('2374eb94-f92b-5024-a504-4b7c7f071459'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The visceral peritoneum is innervated by:', '["The autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic) - sensitive to stretch (not sharp pain)","The somatic nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Not innervated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic) - sensitive to stretch (not sharp pain)', 'The visceral peritoneum is supplied by autonomic (sympathetic and parasympathetic) nerves, which transmit stretch and distension rather than sharp somatic pain. Somatic nerves innervate the parietal peritoneum instead.'),
('e7e1b3de-700d-55a9-b254-027ae97efddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Peritonitis is:', '["Inflammation of the peritoneum (usually from infection or a perforated viscus)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation of the pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inflammation of the pericardium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the peritoneum (usually from infection or a perforated viscus)', 'Peritonitis is inflammation of the peritoneum, usually caused by infection or perforation of a hollow viscus. Inflammation of the pleura or pericardium involves a different serous membrane.'),
('a4599fb2-02e8-5684-87e5-bde0feaf36b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Peritonitis can cause:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Severe abdominal pain, guarding, rigidity, and fever","Only mild discomfort leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe abdominal pain, guarding, rigidity, and fever', 'Peritonitis characteristically produces severe abdominal pain with guarding, rigidity, and fever. Mild discomfort alone does not reflect the typical peritonitic picture.'),
('22ee30f0-76a6-5624-8491-324ed96137a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The omentum is:', '["A double layer of peritoneum that connects the stomach to the other organs","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single layer of peritoneum mediated by specific receptor activation","A muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A double layer of peritoneum that connects the stomach to the other organs', 'The omentum is a double fold of peritoneum connecting the stomach to adjacent organs. It is not a muscle, ligament, or single layer of peritoneum.'),
('6fe110f7-479f-51f3-8b03-ff7546bb9c8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The greater omentum is also called:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The ''abdominal policeman'' (it can wall off infections in the abdomen)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The ''abdominal wall'' without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ''peritoneal sac'' leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ''abdominal policeman'' (it can wall off infections in the abdomen)', 'The greater omentum is nicknamed the ''abdominal policeman'' because it migrates to wall off infections and seal off perforations. The other terms do not refer to the greater omentum.'),
('7b32ff23-4989-5baf-b1e0-029dd942838a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The epiploic foramen (foramen of Winslow) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The communication between the greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa)","The opening of the bile duct mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening of the pancreatic duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The communication between the greater sac and the lesser sac (omental bursa)', 'The epiploic foramen (of Winslow) is the opening that connects the greater sac with the lesser sac (omental bursa), situated behind the free edge of the lesser omentum. It is not an opening of the bile or pancreatic duct.'),
('d08ded4f-44c7-5368-9bd0-e60ae5a7b6d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The peritoneum is the:', '["Same as the pericardium resulting in decreased functional activity","Largest serous membrane in the body (lines the abdominal and pelvic cavities)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Same as the pleura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smallest serous membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Largest serous membrane in the body (lines the abdominal and pelvic cavities)', 'The peritoneum is the largest serous membrane in the body, lining the abdominal and pelvic cavities. The pleura and pericardium are separate serous membranes and are not identical to it.'),
('159614e6-b6e7-5e47-bdb5-56c92416c0ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The total surface area of the peritoneum is about:', '["Half of the skin surface area resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The same as the surface area of the skin (about 1.5-2 square meters)","Three times the skin surface area without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Twice the skin surface area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The same as the surface area of the skin (about 1.5-2 square meters)', 'The total surface area of the peritoneum approximates that of the skin, roughly 1.5-2 square meters. It is not half, twice, or three times the skin surface area.'),
('b9f6c13f-8efc-5bf5-a1b1-8ce6d78f3e0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The peritoneal fluid is:', '["A small amount of serous fluid (about 50-100 mL) that lubricates the peritoneal surfaces","A large amount of fluid (about 500 mL) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A viscous, sticky fluid mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small amount of serous fluid (about 50-100 mL) that lubricates the peritoneal surfaces', 'The peritoneal cavity normally contains a small volume (about 50-100 mL) of serous fluid that lubricates the peritoneal surfaces. Large or viscous collections are pathological.'),
('4f9fc0af-12e1-5063-bff3-da6fd5d3fb5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The peritoneal fluid is produced by:', '["The intestinal cells resulting in decreased functional activity","The mesothelial cells of the peritoneum","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Specialized glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mesothelial cells of the peritoneum', 'Peritoneal fluid is produced by the mesothelial cells lining the peritoneal cavity. Intestinal cells and specialized glands do not secrete this fluid.'),
('955912a6-f98a-5f11-bf4a-ebb4073cffb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The peritoneal fluid is absorbed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The blood vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ureters mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The diaphragmatic lymphatics (lymphatic stomata in the diaphragm)"]'::jsonb, 'The diaphragmatic lymphatics (lymphatic stomata in the diaphragm)', 'Peritoneal fluid is absorbed mainly by the lymphatic stomata on the diaphragmatic surface of the peritoneum, draining into the diaphragmatic lymphatics. This explains the predilection of malignancy or infection for the right subphrenic space.'),
('571fb8df-26b8-5a82-b591-cf9372c8a8a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Ascites is:', '["An abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pericardial cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","An abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An abnormal accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity (can be caused by liver failure, heart failure, or peritonitis)"]'::jsonb, 'An abnormal accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity (can be caused by liver failure, heart failure, or peritonitis)', 'Ascites is an abnormal accumulation of fluid within the peritoneal cavity, seen in liver failure, heart failure, and peritonitis. Accumulation in the pleural or pericardial cavities is a different process.'),
('58ed6d9f-fd50-5abf-8b8a-3379357a3ad9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Ascites can cause:', '["Abdominal distension, shifting dullness, and a fluid wave","Only abdominal distension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only a fluid wave without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only shifting dullness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal distension, shifting dullness, and a fluid wave', 'Large ascites distends the abdomen and produces shifting dullness and a fluid wave on examination. These findings reflect mobile intraperitoneal fluid; no single isolated sign captures the picture.'),
('2b9aeb60-1a03-50dd-a43e-11d818597bb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Paracentesis is:', '["A procedure to drain fluid from the peritoneal cavity (usually through a needle inserted into the abdomen)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A procedure to drain fluid from the pericardial cavity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A procedure to drain fluid from the pleural cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A procedure to drain fluid from the peritoneal cavity (usually through a needle inserted into the abdomen)', 'Paracentesis is drainage of fluid from the peritoneal cavity through a needle inserted into the abdomen. Draining the pleural or pericardial cavities is a different procedure.'),
('7f6a3274-a348-5126-91cd-f0448421dc11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The peritoneal cavities of the male and female are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Similar (the only difference is the presence of the openings of the uterine tubes in the female)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Completely different without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Similar (the only difference is the presence of the openings of the uterine tubes in the female)', 'The male and female peritoneal cavities are essentially similar; the key difference is that in females the uterine tubes open into the cavity. They are therefore neither completely different nor absolutely the same.'),
('9ba18d90-1a21-513b-bd55-c563ba77b84b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The peritoneal cavity in the female communicates with:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The exterior (through the anus) mediated by specific receptor activation","The exterior (through the urethra) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The exterior (through the uterine tubes, uterus, and vagina)"]'::jsonb, 'The exterior (through the uterine tubes, uterus, and vagina)', 'In the female, the peritoneal cavity communicates with the exterior through the uterine tubes, uterus, and vagina. Communication through the anus or urethra is not the pathway.'),
('1719c5da-32fd-5a63-b972-1a28badce289'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'This communication in females is important because:', '["It is not clinically important resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It provides a potential route for infection to enter the peritoneal cavity from the vagina","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It allows the passage of sperm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It provides a potential route for infection to enter the peritoneal cavity from the vagina', 'This open pathway allows microorganisms to travel from the vagina through the uterus and uterine tubes into the peritoneal cavity, enabling ascending pelvic infection. It is not a route for sperm and is clinically significant.'),
('0410f7b9-b38a-58a7-82b8-789b7cd4cf7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The internal oblique muscle is innervated by:', '["The phrenic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The vagus nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lumbar plexus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The intercostal nerves (T7-T12), the iliohypogastric nerve, and the ilioinguinal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The intercostal nerves (T7-T12), the iliohypogastric nerve, and the ilioinguinal nerve', 'The internal oblique is innervated by the intercostal nerves (T7-T12), the iliohypogastric nerve, and the ilioinguinal nerve. The vagus, phrenic, and lumbar plexus nerves do not supply this muscle.'),
('459b7bbe-7333-5d58-9f48-9e514a40df43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The transversus abdominis muscle is innervated by:', '["The lumbar plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The vagus nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The intercostal nerves (T7-T12) and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'The intercostal nerves (T7-T12) and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves', 'The transversus abdominis receives its supply from the intercostal nerves (T7-T12) and the iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves, the same group that innervates the internal oblique. The vagus, phrenic, and lumbar plexus nerves are not involved.'),
('c3fe405b-4600-5761-b082-f86f646cdf72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The rectus abdominis muscle is innervated by:', '["The iliohypogastric nerve","None","The phrenic nerve","The ilioinguinal nerve","The intercostal nerves (T7-T12)"]'::jsonb, 'The intercostal nerves (T7-T12)', 'The rectus abdominis is innervated mainly by the intercostal nerves (T7-T12). The iliohypogastric and ilioinguinal nerves supply other lower abdominal wall muscles and are not the primary supply of the rectus.'),
('2cb0e600-5682-5491-8d6c-e67577621163'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The abdominal aorta bifurcates into the common iliac arteries at the level of:', '["L4 (the fourth lumbar vertebra)","L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L1 mediated by specific receptor activation","L5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L4 (the fourth lumbar vertebra)', 'The abdominal aorta divides into the common iliac arteries at the L4 vertebral level, slightly to the left of the midline. The other vertebral levels are incorrect.'),
('e140bc71-f65e-5edf-91aa-00ca99db9ce4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inferior vena cava (IVC) is formed at the level of:', '["L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L3 mediated by specific receptor activation","L2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L5 (the union of the right and left common iliac veins)"]'::jsonb, 'L5 (the union of the right and left common iliac veins)', 'The inferior vena cava is formed at L5 by the union of the right and left common iliac veins. This distinguishes it from the arterial bifurcation, which occurs at L4.'),
('dae39c64-7d46-52d0-b121-b76dd6877ff2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The IVC lies to the:', '["Left of the abdominal aorta","Right of the abdominal aorta","None","Posterior to the abdominal aorta","Anterior to the abdominal aorta"]'::jsonb, 'Right of the abdominal aorta', 'The IVC runs to the right of the abdominal aorta as both ascend through the abdomen. It does not lie purely anterior, posterior, or to the left of the aorta.'),
('967df6a4-de1d-5e86-87ef-6c70e3b2b17c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The common iliac veins unite at:', '["L2 resulting in decreased functional activity","L3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L5 (to form the IVC)","L1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L5 (to form the IVC)', 'The right and left common iliac veins unite at the level of L5 to form the inferior vena cava. The other vertebral levels are inconsistent with standard anatomy.'),
('ae5695e8-8b05-5c2c-953d-b5a71d66ef2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The azygos vein is a continuation of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The left ascending lumbar vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The IVC directly mediated by specific receptor activation","The portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The right ascending lumbar vein (connects the IVC and SVC)"]'::jsonb, 'The right ascending lumbar vein (connects the IVC and SVC)', 'The azygos vein arises as a continuation of the right ascending lumbar vein and forms an important collateral channel between the IVC and SVC systems. The left ascending lumbar vein contributes to the hemiazygos system, not the origin of the azygos.'),
('7c1d9bcb-722e-5a5f-a54f-d13ecbb2d2c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The hemiazygos vein drains:', '["The pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","The left side of the chest (drains into the azygos vein)","The abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The right side of the chest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The left side of the chest (drains into the azygos vein)', 'The hemiazygos vein drains the left side of the chest, receiving the lower left intercostal veins and emptying into the azygos vein. It does not drain the right side of the chest, abdomen, or pelvis.'),
('7064a7ea-345a-58a6-9106-3140c35e5c76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The accessory hemiazygos vein drains:', '["The right intercostal spaces resulting in decreased functional activity","The upper left intercostal spaces (drains into the azygos vein)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The lower left intercostal spaces leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The upper left intercostal spaces (drains into the azygos vein)', 'The accessory hemiazygos vein drains the upper left intercostal spaces into the azygos vein. The right intercostal spaces drain directly into the azygos vein, and the lower left spaces drain into the hemiazygos vein.'),
('bf9de86a-0d3c-5642-a058-32a97e41a883'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The vascular anastomoses around the scapula connect:', '["The subclavian artery and the axillary artery (a collateral pathway)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The portal and systemic venous systems mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The aorta and the IVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The subclavian artery and the axillary artery (a collateral pathway)', 'The anastomoses around the scapula connect the subclavian artery system to the axillary artery, providing collateral flow if the axillary artery is occluded. They do not join the portal and systemic veins or the aorta and IVC.'),
('c04e7605-e9a5-5607-b7f4-2a7b46e58b5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The femoral triangle (Scarpa triangle) is bounded by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal ligament (superiorly), the sartorius (laterally), and the adductor longus (medially)","The adductor longus, the rectus femoris, and the sartorius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inguinal canal, the sartorius, and the adductor longus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inguinal ligament (superiorly), the sartorius (laterally), and the adductor longus (medially)', 'The femoral triangle is bounded by the inguinal ligament above, the sartorius laterally, and the adductor longus medially. The rectus femoris and inguinal canal are not among its boundaries.'),
('506e5423-1fbe-591d-83cd-3d9078bb2157'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The femoral triangle contains:', '["Only the femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral nerve, artery, vein, and lymph nodes (from lateral to medial)","Only the femoral artery and vein mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the lymph nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral nerve, artery, vein, and lymph nodes (from lateral to medial)', 'From lateral to medial, the femoral triangle contains the femoral nerve, artery, vein, and lymph nodes. Its contents are not limited to a single nerve, vessel, or node group.'),
('5ba7d12e-07b4-51e7-8282-57961bdf658b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The adductor (subsartorial) canal (Hunter canal) contains:', '["The femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The obturator nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The femoral artery and vein, and the saphenous nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral artery and vein, and the saphenous nerve', 'The adductor (subsartorial) canal transmits the femoral artery, the femoral vein, and the saphenous nerve. The femoral, obturator, and sciatic nerves do not pass through this canal.'),
('303f76c3-6554-5309-892a-b12f4c99099f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The popliteal fossa is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A fat-filled space behind the knee (contains the popliteal vessels and the tibial and common peroneal nerves)","The space below the knee mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space in front of the knee leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fat-filled space behind the knee (contains the popliteal vessels and the tibial and common peroneal nerves)', 'The popliteal fossa is a fat-filled space behind the knee that contains the popliteal vessels and the tibial and common peroneal nerves. It is posterior to the knee, not anterior or below it.'),
('6067091c-4659-5c36-85be-a2b121c624dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The adductor magnus muscle is innervated by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The obturator nerve (L2-L4) and the tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L4-S1)","Only the sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator nerve (L2-L4) and the tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L4-S1)', 'Adductor magnus has dual innervation: the obturator nerve (L2-L4) for the adductor part and the tibial division of the sciatic nerve (L4-S1) for the hamstring part. No single nerve supplies the whole muscle.'),
('799f3296-321a-5f5a-a8e7-175e75367f5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The saphenous nerve is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A sensory branch of the femoral nerve (L3-L4) that supplies the skin of the medial leg and foot","A branch of the sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","A branch of the obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A motor branch of the femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sensory branch of the femoral nerve (L3-L4) that supplies the skin of the medial leg and foot', 'The saphenous nerve is the largest sensory branch of the femoral nerve (L3-L4), supplying the skin of the medial leg and foot. It has no motor function and is not a branch of the sciatic or obturator nerve.'),
('07cc731c-2693-56a1-9194-dfb696843b22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The saphenous nerve accompanies:', '["The saphenous vein (the great saphenous vein) in the leg","The femoral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The popliteal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The saphenous vein (the great saphenous vein) in the leg', 'In the leg the saphenous nerve accompanies the great saphenous vein along the medial side. It does not follow the femoral artery, femoral vein, or popliteal artery in this part of its course.'),
('c97cc523-e17c-5394-986a-ac599ba3eb51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The great saphenous vein is:', '["The shortest vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The deepest vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The longest vein in the body (drains the medial side of the leg and thigh into the femoral vein)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The longest vein in the body (drains the medial side of the leg and thigh into the femoral vein)', 'The great saphenous vein is the longest vein in the body, running up the medial side of the leg and thigh to drain into the femoral vein at the groin. It is neither the shortest nor the deepest vein.'),
('bf06a048-8413-5462-8b69-34461f2793ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The small saphenous vein is:', '["A vein on the lateral side of the leg (drains into the popliteal vein at the popliteal fossa)","A vein on the medial side of the leg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A vein on the anterior side of the leg mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A vein on the lateral side of the leg (drains into the popliteal vein at the popliteal fossa)', 'The small saphenous vein runs on the posterolateral aspect of the leg and drains into the popliteal vein within the popliteal fossa. It does not lie on the medial or anterior side of the leg.'),
('b7c5c4cf-7140-59d9-ac36-3c7b0b583688'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The femoral vein becomes the external iliac vein:', '["At the pelvis","At the hip","At the knee","None","At the inguinal ligament"]'::jsonb, 'At the inguinal ligament', 'The femoral vein becomes the external iliac vein as it passes deep to the inguinal ligament into the pelvis. This change of name occurs at the inguinal ligament, not at the hip or knee.'),
('599dc778-6f05-577c-8b57-e479f8a8fc71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inguinal lymph nodes are divided into:', '["Superficial (above the fascia lata) and deep (below the fascia lata) inguinal lymph nodes","Only deep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only superficial mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial (above the fascia lata) and deep (below the fascia lata) inguinal lymph nodes', 'The inguinal lymph nodes are divided into superficial nodes above the fascia lata and deep nodes below the fascia lata. Both groups exist, so neither ''only'' option is sufficient.'),
('af6f1157-716d-5a46-a84c-fa194b19d8ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The deep inguinal lymph nodes drain:', '["Only the glans penis/clitoris resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the scrotum/labia mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The superficial inguinal lymph nodes (and the deep structures of the lower limb)"]'::jsonb, 'The superficial inguinal lymph nodes (and the deep structures of the lower limb)', 'The deep inguinal lymph nodes receive drainage from the superficial inguinal lymph nodes and from the deep structures of the lower limb. They are not limited to draining only the glans, scrotum, or labia.'),
('1d09a3d4-61b7-582a-aca3-e1feedff8653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inguinal ligament (Poupart ligament) runs from:', '["The posterior superior iliac spine to the pubic symphysis resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the pubic tubercle","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The iliac crest to the pubic tubercle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the pubic tubercle', 'The inguinal ligament extends from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the pubic tubercle. None of the other described attachments match this ligament.'),
('a221e562-63d2-5ca1-9c88-0ed25ea4c0e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The round ligament of the uterus passes through:', '["The inguinal canal (from the uterus to the labia majora)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sciatic foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inguinal canal (from the uterus to the labia majora)', 'The round ligament of the uterus passes through the inguinal canal to reach the labia majora. It does not traverse the femoral canal, obturator foramen, or sciatic foramen.'),
('ef2d6337-2168-5960-86a8-daf1131e726b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The cremaster muscle is supplied by:', '["The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)","The femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The iliohypogastric nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ilioinguinal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2)', 'The cremaster muscle is supplied by the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve (L1-L2). The femoral, iliohypogastric, and ilioinguinal nerves do not supply it.'),
('e85edab3-5bfa-5864-8200-7c454ff9455d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The inguinal ligament is a thickening of:', '["The transversalis fascia","The external oblique aponeurosis","None","The internal oblique aponeurosis","The transversus abdominis aponeurosis"]'::jsonb, 'The external oblique aponeurosis', 'The inguinal ligament is the thickened, folded lower border of the external oblique aponeurosis. It is not formed by the internal oblique, the transversus abdominis aponeurosis, or the transversalis fascia.'),
('3fcae588-e773-536c-a2fc-27b1d9ac6e8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The conjoint tendon (inguinal falx) is formed by:', '["The fusion of the external oblique and internal oblique resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The fusion of the external oblique and transversus abdominis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fusion of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses (inserts into the pubic crest)"]'::jsonb, 'The fusion of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses (inserts into the pubic crest)', 'The conjoint tendon (inguinal falx) is the fusion of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis aponeuroses, inserting into the pubic crest. The external oblique aponeurosis takes no part in this fusion.'),
('770a416c-6b00-5f00-ae0d-ed0e97400dd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The triangle of Hesselbach (inguinal triangle) is bounded by:', '["The inferior epigastric artery (laterally), the rectus abdominis (medially), and the inguinal ligament (inferiorly)","The deep inguinal ring, the superficial inguinal ring, and the inguinal ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inferior epigastric vein, the rectus abdominis, and the inguinal ligament leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior epigastric artery (laterally), the rectus abdominis (medially), and the inguinal ligament (inferiorly)', 'The triangle of Hesselbach is bounded by the inferior epigastric artery laterally, the rectus abdominis medially, and the inguinal ligament inferiorly. The inguinal rings and the inferior epigastric vein are not named boundaries.'),
('81be36ed-8199-568d-9f80-6a63d4082e24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'A direct inguinal hernia protrudes through:', '["The Hesselbach triangle (medial to the inferior epigastric vessels)","The obturator foramen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The femoral canal mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The deep inguinal ring (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The Hesselbach triangle (medial to the inferior epigastric vessels)', 'A direct inguinal hernia bulges through the Hesselbach triangle, medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, typically in older patients. It does not pass through the deep inguinal ring or the femoral canal.'),
('f38a5676-12e1-547a-85ac-55c8b5d22d65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'An indirect inguinal hernia protrudes through:', '["The obturator foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The femoral canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The Hesselbach triangle (medial to the inferior epigastric vessels) mediated by specific receptor activation","The deep inguinal ring (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The deep inguinal ring (lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels)', 'An indirect inguinal hernia enters through the deep inguinal ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, then follows the inguinal canal. The Hesselbach triangle and femoral canal are the paths of other hernias.'),
('01d51208-9c80-5b16-87bf-d2067303b21a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'A femoral hernia protrudes through:', '["The femoral ring and the femoral canal (below the inguinal ligament)","The deep inguinal ring accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The obturator foramen mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The Hesselbach triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The femoral ring and the femoral canal (below the inguinal ligament)', 'A femoral hernia descends through the femoral ring and femoral canal, below the inguinal ligament and medial to the femoral vein. The deep inguinal ring, obturator foramen, and Hesselbach triangle are not involved.'),
('56dde7c8-ab60-5c9c-adfd-40befb9e475a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The sympathetic chain has ganglia at the following levels:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only thoracic and lumbar mediated by specific receptor activation","Cervical (3), thoracic (11-12), lumbar (4), sacral (4-5), and coccygeal (1)","Only cervical and thoracic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical (3), thoracic (11-12), lumbar (4), sacral (4-5), and coccygeal (1)', 'The sympathetic trunk runs the entire length of the vertebral column, with ganglia at cervical (3), thoracic (11-12), lumbar (4), sacral (4-5), and coccygeal (1) levels; it is not confined to the thoracolumbar segment or a single region.'),
('447d0f14-b4f5-5304-95b8-9f6d0b157833'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'The vagus nerve (parasympathetic) supplies:', '["Only the hindgut resulting in decreased functional activity","The foregut and midgut (through the esophageal plexus) - not the hindgut or pelvic viscera","All of the gut mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the pelvic viscera without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The foregut and midgut (through the esophageal plexus) - not the hindgut or pelvic viscera', 'The vagus nerve carries parasympathetic fibers to the foregut and midgut via the esophageal plexus; the hindgut and pelvic viscera receive parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, and the vagus does not reach the entire gut or pelvis.'),
('86137bdf-8bce-5a85-ab36-aab39bfab5c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: the lateral pelvic wall contains which muscles?', '["Piriformis and obturator internus","Levator ani and coccygeus","Psoas major and quadratus lumborum","Gluteus maximus and medius","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis and obturator internus', 'The piriformis and obturator internus form the lateral pelvic wall; levator ani and coccygeus belong to the pelvic floor, while psoas major/quadratus lumborum and the gluteal muscles lie outside the pelvis proper.'),
('dec09441-ff5d-5a99-a329-a58e7015ad4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: the piriformis muscle functions as?', '["Lateral rotator of the thigh at the hip","Medial rotator of the thigh","Flexor of the thigh","Extensor of the thigh","Adductor of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral rotator of the thigh at the hip', 'Piriformis spans from the sacrum through the greater sciatic foramen and, by pulling the thigh outward at the hip, acts as a lateral rotator of the thigh; it does not medially rotate, flex, extend, or adduct the thigh.'),
('940b4a5f-9018-5b5e-a9a3-09d21981b206'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: the obturator internus exits the pelvis through?', '["Inguinal canal","Obturator canal","Greater sciatic foramen","Femoral canal","Lesser sciatic foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Lesser sciatic foramen', 'The obturator internus tendon leaves the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen after hooking around the ischium; the greater sciatic foramen instead transmits piriformis and other neurovascular structures, and the obturator/femoral canals carry thigh structures.'),
('2100a8bc-7cd9-50bd-ae5a-4e759350a256'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: the obturator internus inserts into?', '["Lesser trochanter resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischial tuberosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Greater trochanter (medial surface, in front of piriformis)","Pubic bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater trochanter (medial surface, in front of piriformis)', 'The obturator internus tendon inserts onto the medial surface of the greater trochanter in front of the piriformis tendon; it does not attach to the lesser trochanter, ischial tuberosity, or pubic bone.'),
('4ba582cc-a13a-5d22-9f1c-da0a35dae04f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''The levator ani muscle is part of'' is:', '["Urogenital diaphragm","Abdominal wall","None of the above","Posterior abdominal wall","Pelvic diaphragm"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic diaphragm', 'The levator ani is a sheet-like muscle that, with the coccygeus, forms the pelvic diaphragm; it is not a component of the urogenital diaphragm or the abdominal wall.'),
('b464c06b-56d0-5fff-be08-342542dadc30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: the levator ani is formed by which parts?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Coccygeus and piriformis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubococcygeus and ischiococcygeus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubococcygeus (puboprostatic/pubovaginal), puborectalis, iliococcygeus","Only pubococcygeus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubococcygeus (puboprostatic/pubovaginal), puborectalis, iliococcygeus', 'The levator ani comprises the pubococcygeus (including the puboprostatic/pubovaginal sling), puborectalis, and iliococcygeus; coccygeus is a separate pelvic floor muscle, so the other options are incomplete or incorrect.'),
('3e687c43-3945-5675-a2f9-1ef1b717cb46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: the coccygeus muscle is?', '["Part of the urogenital diaphragm","Part of the gluteal region","A muscle of the lateral pelvic wall","None of the above","Part of the pelvic diaphragm (posterior to levator ani)"]'::jsonb, 'Part of the pelvic diaphragm (posterior to levator ani)', 'The coccygeus, together with the levator ani, makes up the pelvic diaphragm and lies posterior to the levator ani; it is not part of the urogenital diaphragm, gluteal region, or lateral pelvic wall.'),
('807d2f1c-9ca6-5ebe-8617-46875bbad404'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: the pelvic diaphragm is formed by?', '["None of the above","Obturator internus and piriformis","Only levator ani","Only coccygeus","Levator ani and coccygeus muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Levator ani and coccygeus muscles', 'The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the levator ani and coccygeus muscles; obturator internus and piriformis are lateral wall muscles and do not contribute to this diaphragm.'),
('74c385ff-7e65-5d58-afc9-fd17ff1edc55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: the internal iliac artery divides into?', '["Superior and inferior divisions","Left and right branches","Anterior and posterior divisions","Medial and lateral divisions","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior divisions', 'The internal iliac artery divides into an anterior and a posterior division near the upper border of the greater sciatic foramen; the superior/inferior, left/right, and medial/lateral designations are not used for its division.'),
('9284284a-764b-56c6-98e0-4023b327f51b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: the anterior division of the internal iliac artery gives rise to?', '["Only gluteal arteries resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior vesical, inferior vesical, obturator, internal pudendal, inferior gluteal, middle rectal, uterine, vaginal","Only visceral branches leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior vesical, inferior vesical, obturator, internal pudendal, inferior gluteal, middle rectal, uterine, vaginal', 'The anterior division of the internal iliac artery gives the superior and inferior vesical, obturator, internal pudendal, inferior gluteal, middle rectal, uterine, and vaginal arteries; limiting it to gluteal or visceral branches alone is incomplete.'),
('e33bbee8-69d7-5138-ba21-31ea834bac11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''The posterior division of the internal iliac artery gives rise to'' is:', '["None of the above","All visceral branches","Obturator and superior vesical","Internal pudendal and inferior gluteal","Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal arteries', 'The posterior division of the internal iliac artery gives the iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arteries; visceral, obturator/superior vesical, and inferior gluteal/internal pudendal branches arise from the anterior division.'),
('8dcb5c15-62a7-5425-9d46-a14e2ae001a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: the superior gluteal artery is a branch of?', '["Posterior division of internal iliac artery","External iliac artery","Femoral artery","Common iliac artery","Anterior division of internal iliac"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior division of internal iliac artery', 'The superior gluteal artery arises from the posterior division of the internal iliac artery and exits through the greater sciatic foramen above the piriformis; it does not arise from the anterior division or the external, common iliac, or femoral arteries.'),
('47762c0e-e207-5a2c-9100-27f3623043ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: the inferior gluteal artery is a branch of?', '["Common iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","Femoral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External iliac mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior division of internal iliac artery","Posterior division of internal iliac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior division of internal iliac artery', 'The inferior gluteal artery branches from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis; the common, external iliac, and femoral arteries give no such branch.'),
('fa2dd961-e6c9-5f49-91ef-999da0e67806'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: the internal pudendal artery is a branch of?', '["External iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","Common iliac accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior division mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior division of internal iliac artery","Femoral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior division of internal iliac artery', 'The internal pudendal artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery; after leaving via the greater sciatic foramen it re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen to supply the perineum.'),
('dea4f3db-6e0f-5a34-8856-bd776e59112e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: the obturator artery is usually a branch of?', '["Common iliac resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior division of internal iliac artery","Femoral mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior division without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External iliac leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior division of internal iliac artery', 'The obturator artery usually arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and leaves the pelvis through the obturator canal; its origin is not the posterior division or the femoral, common, or external iliac arteries.'),
('ae47a6b0-504c-5608-9a42-10f757e9f0b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: the sacral plexus lies on the?', '["Lateral pelvic wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior to the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelvic floor mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior pelvic wall (anterior to piriformis)"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior pelvic wall (anterior to piriformis)', 'The sacral plexus lies on the posterior pelvic wall, where it is formed on the anterior surface of the piriformis; it is not located on the lateral pelvic wall, purely anterior to the sacrum, or on the pelvic floor.'),
('75b5aec3-d404-5ca9-917f-442293442341'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) is a branch of'' is:', '["Coccygeal plexus","Lumbar plexus","Sacral plexus","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral plexus', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) is the main branch of the sacral plexus supplying the perineum; it does not arise from the lumbar or coccygeal plexuses, and the pelvic splanchnic nerves are parasympathetic branches, not its source.'),
('ec2b3242-6684-5480-9238-a46c6c2909bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: the pudendal nerve passes through?', '["Greater sciatic foramen (enters perineum via lesser sciatic foramen)","Obturator canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lesser sciatic foramen directly mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater sciatic foramen (enters perineum via lesser sciatic foramen)', 'The pudendal nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, hooks around the ischial spine, and re-enters the perineum via the lesser sciatic foramen; the other named canals and a direct lesser-scarroute are incorrect.'),
('6fdc394e-594a-5a5f-8996-114139381bb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: the pudendal nerve supplies?', '["Only the anal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Most of the perineum (somatic motor and sensory), external anal sphincter, external urethral sphincter","Only the urethral sphincter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most of the perineum (somatic motor and sensory), external anal sphincter, external urethral sphincter', 'The pudendal nerve carries somatic motor and sensory fibers to most of the perineum, including the external anal and external urethral sphincters; its territory is much broader than the anal canal, scrotum, or urethral sphincter alone.'),
('3b9289e1-6218-5a1e-afad-91218cbfe917'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: the pelvic fascia includes?', '["Parietal pelvic fascia (covers pelvic walls and floor) and visceral pelvic fascia (covers pelvic organs)","Rectovesical septum only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the parietal fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the endopelvic fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal pelvic fascia (covers pelvic walls and floor) and visceral pelvic fascia (covers pelvic organs)', 'The pelvic fascia has a parietal layer lining the pelvic walls and floor and a visceral layer investing the pelvic organs; it is not solely endopelvic fascia or limited to the rectovesical septum.'),
('b3e7ddd9-5907-5793-bded-910b272a3ece'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: the tendinous arch of the levator ani is?', '["A tendon of the levator ani resulting in decreased functional activity","Part of the sacrotuberous ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thickening of the obturator internus fascia from which the levator ani originates","Part of the inguinal ligament without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thickening of the obturator internus fascia from which the levator ani originates', 'The tendinous arch of the levator ani is a linear thickening of the obturator internus fascia from which the levator ani arises; it is not a tendon of levator ani and forms no part of the sacrotuberous or inguinal ligaments.'),
('30bfc00a-a90b-5831-9e34-f78cb0d2552a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: the obturator canal transmits?', '["Femoral nerve","None of the above","Sciatic nerve","Obturator nerve and vessels","Pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator nerve and vessels', 'The obturator canal transmits the obturator nerve together with the obturator vessels from the pelvis into the medial thigh; the femoral, sciatic, and pudendal nerves do not pass through it.'),
('2553be25-0adf-5c12-88e1-40fb58640d95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''The superior vesical artery supplies'' is:', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Upper part of the urinary bladder (and distal ureter)","Prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper part of the urinary bladder (and distal ureter)', 'The superior vesical artery supplies the upper part of the urinary bladder and the distal part of the ureter; the rectum, uterus, and prostate receive their blood from other arteries.'),
('92e4a3e7-8c1a-5298-a2d7-2bbdbb746af9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: the inferior vesical artery supplies?', '["Scrotum resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower part of the bladder, prostate (in males), seminal vesicles","Rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower part of the bladder, prostate (in males), seminal vesicles', 'The inferior vesical artery supplies the lower part of the bladder and, in males, the prostate and seminal vesicles; the scrotum, rectum, and uterus have different arterial origins.'),
('54cb6015-0ab5-5211-9c86-23e33e009332'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: the middle rectal artery supplies?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Anal canal only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower rectum and anastomoses with superior and inferior rectal arteries","Upper rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sigmoid colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower rectum and anastomoses with superior and inferior rectal arteries', 'The middle rectal artery supplies the lower rectum and anastomoses with the superior and inferior rectal arteries; it does not serve the upper rectum, sigmoid colon, or anal canal alone.'),
('441b022a-9b43-5b41-b120-dd10c284f498'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: the vaginal artery (in females) corresponds to which male artery?', '["Obturator artery","Internal pudendal artery","None of the above","Inferior vesical artery","Superior vesical artery"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior vesical artery', 'In females the vaginal artery represents the same vessel as the inferior vesical artery in males; it is not the counterpart of the superior vesical, obturator, or internal pudendal arteries.'),
('e07488df-d3fe-5cef-8324-7df834184f76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: the uterine artery runs in which ligament?', '["Round ligament resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardinal ligament accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Broad ligament (transversely at the base, crossing above the ureter)","Suspensory ligament of ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Broad ligament (transversely at the base, crossing above the ureter)', 'The uterine artery runs transversely in the base of the broad ligament, crossing above the ureter; it does not course in the round ligament, cardinal ligament, or suspensory ligament of the ovary.'),
('4384f505-b882-5375-9350-197fd3689403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: the ureter is crossed by the uterine artery?', '["Below the ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial to the ureter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to the ureter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Above the ureter at the level of the cervix (water under the bridge)"]'::jsonb, 'Above the ureter at the level of the cervix (water under the bridge)', 'At the level of the cervix the uterine artery crosses above the ureter, a relationship remembered as water under the bridge; the artery does not pass below, medial, or lateral to the ureter in this location.'),
('55edd088-dff3-5d96-b51c-2018b57e8149'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''The superior gluteal nerve (L4-S1) supplies'' is:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Piriformis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, tensor fascia lata","Gluteus maximus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obturator internus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, tensor fascia lata', 'The superior gluteal nerve (L4-S1) innervates the gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fascia lata; gluteus maximus, piriformis, and obturator internus have different nerve supplies.'),
('d904f83b-8e61-5b21-b672-8db7ceac5a32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: the inferior gluteal nerve (l5-s2) supplies?', '["Gluteus medius","All gluteal muscles","Tensor fascia lata","Gluteus maximus","Gluteus minimus"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus maximus', 'The inferior gluteal nerve (L5-S2) supplies only the gluteus maximus; gluteus medius and minimus and the tensor fascia lata are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve.'),
('95ca0677-6636-5e4a-9a94-e28c6a80b6ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: the nerve to piriformis (s1-s2) supplies?', '["Piriformis muscle","None of the above","Obturator internus","Rarely","Quadratus femoris"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis muscle', 'The nerve to piriformis (S1-S2) innervates the piriformis muscle alone; the obturator internus and quadratus femoris each have a separate dedicated nerve.'),
('dd1c9dac-14bb-5bb9-8671-6d9aa1096da5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: the nerve to obturator internus (l5-s2) supplies?', '["Piriformis resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator internus and superior gemellus","Rarely without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Quadratus femoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obturator internus and superior gemellus', 'The nerve to obturator internus (L5-S2) supplies the obturator internus and the superior gemellus; it does not innervate piriformis or quadratus femoris.'),
('0e3030ca-9465-5bd3-b00c-361f803a7ac5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: the nerve to quadratus femoris (l4-s1) supplies?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Obturator internus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Piriformis mediated by specific receptor activation","Quadratus femoris and inferior gemellus","Rarely leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Quadratus femoris and inferior gemellus', 'The nerve to quadratus femoris (L4-S1) supplies the quadratus femoris and the inferior gemellus; piriformis and obturator internus receive their own dedicated nerves.'),
('690d8d54-5ba1-5ac4-b144-a1f5e971bc66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: the pelvic splanchnic nerves (s2-s4) provide?', '["Somatic motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic innervation mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic innervation to pelvic viscera (defecation, urination, erection)"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic innervation to pelvic viscera (defecation, urination, erection)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) carry parasympathetic fibers that mediate defecation, urination, and erection; they are neither somatic motor, purely sensory, nor sympathetic.'),
('58d8287f-f15e-5f6e-8c2e-7944c385f307'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''The sacral sympathetic trunk lies'' is:', '["Posterior to sacrum","Lateral to sacrum","Medial to the anterior sacral foramina","Anterior to the coccyx","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to the anterior sacral foramina', 'The sacral sympathetic trunk lies medial to the anterior sacral foramina on the pelvic surface of the sacrum; it is not positioned posterior or purely lateral to the sacrum or anterior to the coccyx.'),
('4b942e12-63ee-56fb-a063-5be3100bc64d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: regarding the pelvic wall, vessels, and nerves:?', '["The internal iliac artery divides into anterior and posterior divisions","All of the above","The piriformis and obturator internus form the lateral pelvic wall","The levator ani is part of the pelvic diaphragm","The pudendal nerve is the main nerve of the perineum"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All four statements are true: the internal iliac artery divides into anterior and posterior divisions, piriformis and obturator internus form the lateral pelvic wall, levator ani is part of the pelvic diaphragm, and the pudendal nerve is the main nerve of the perineum.'),
('aee8dfd4-e034-552f-bc7a-8f49ae215bb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: what muscles form the lateral pelvic wall?', '["Psoas and quadratus lumborum","Piriformis and obturator internus","Gluteus maximus and medius","Levator ani and coccygeus","None"]'::jsonb, 'Piriformis and obturator internus', 'Piriformis and obturator internus form the lateral pelvic wall; psoas and quadratus lumborum lie on the posterior abdominal wall, levator ani and coccygeus form the pelvic floor, and the gluteals are located outside the pelvis.'),
('a4904b6b-caa9-5005-9dd4-e56a82c5184a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: what are the divisions of the internal iliac artery?', '["None","Medial and lateral","Left and right","Superior and inferior","Anterior and posterior divisions"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior divisions', 'The internal iliac artery divides into an anterior and a posterior division; superior/inferior, left/right, and medial/lateral are not the terms used to name its divisions.'),
('814709af-5544-57dd-a575-efd3efaa89c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: what does the anterior division of internal iliac give?', '["Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only gluteal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visceral branches, inferior gluteal, internal pudendal","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visceral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral branches, inferior gluteal, internal pudendal', 'The anterior division of the internal iliac artery gives the visceral branches, the inferior gluteal artery, and the internal pudendal artery; iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arise from the posterior division.'),
('3a19856f-3a35-5375-bb53-cffb757ccb7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: what does the posterior division of internal iliac give?', '["All visceral branches","None","Obturator and vesical","Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal","Inferior gluteal and internal pudendal"]'::jsonb, 'Iliolumbar, lateral sacral, superior gluteal', 'The posterior division of the internal iliac artery gives the iliolumbar, lateral sacral, and superior gluteal arteries; visceral branches, obturator/vesical branches, and the inferior gluteal/internal pudendal arteries come from the anterior division.'),
('6912451e-ea76-562d-a3d5-050d0cdb015a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''What is the sacral plexus'' is:', '["Only L4-L5 resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L2-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Only S1-S4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lumbosacral trunk (L4-L5) and anterior rami S1-S4"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbosacral trunk (L4-L5) and anterior rami S1-S4', 'The sacral plexus is formed by the lumbosacral trunk (L4-L5) together with the anterior rami of S1-S4; L4-L5 or S1-S4 alone, or the L2-S2 range, do not describe the plexus accurately.'),
('54b432f6-346f-5eac-9fb7-d73c90bfea18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: the bony pelvis is formed by:?', '["Only the sacrum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Two hip bones (innominate bones), the sacrum, and the coccyx","Only the hip bones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the coccyx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two hip bones (innominate bones), the sacrum, and the coccyx', 'The bony pelvis is composed of the two hip (innominate) bones, the sacrum, and the coccyx; none of these bones alone forms the pelvis.'),
('ec446b6c-d7e6-56e5-9d60-c8517012df50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which statement best fits the following fact: the hip bone is formed by:?', '["The ilium, ischium, and pubis","The ischium and pubis only","The ilium and pubis only","The ilium and ischium only","None"]'::jsonb, 'The ilium, ischium, and pubis', 'Each hip (innominate) bone is formed by the fusion of the ilium, ischium, and pubis; a combination of only two of these bones is incorrect.'),
('0e425765-c489-5661-9173-536197e6dedb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Regarding Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, what is the correct answer to: the pelvic inlet (superior pelvic aperture) is bounded by:?', '["The pubic arch and the ischial spines resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacrum and the coccyx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sacral promontory, the ala of the sacrum, the iliopectineal line, and the pubic symphysis"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral promontory, the ala of the sacrum, the iliopectineal line, and the pubic symphysis', 'The pelvic inlet is bounded by the sacral promontory, the ala of the sacrum, the iliopectineal line, and the pubic symphysis; the pubic arch/ischial spines and the sacrum/coccyx bound other landmarks.'),
('9c6c7826-7715-5e9e-a137-893b884c19b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'For Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which option is most appropriate: the pelvic outlet (inferior pelvic aperture) is bounded by:?', '["The iliac crests resulting in decreased functional activity","The sacral promontory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The coccyx, the ischial tuberosities, the pubic arch, and the sacrotuberous ligaments","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The coccyx, the ischial tuberosities, the pubic arch, and the sacrotuberous ligaments', 'The pelvic outlet is bounded by the coccyx, the ischial tuberosities, the pubic arch, and the sacrotuberous ligaments; the iliac crests and sacral promontory do not form outlet boundaries.'),
('85fe59c9-72ce-5182-8876-d7898a387de4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'Which answer is correct in Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves when asked: the true pelvis is:?', '["The part of the pelvis below the pelvic outlet resulting in decreased functional activity","The part of the pelvis above the pelvic inlet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The part of the pelvis below the pelvic inlet (the pelvic cavity)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the pelvis below the pelvic inlet (the pelvic cavity)', 'The true pelvis is the part of the pelvis below the pelvic inlet and forms the pelvic cavity; the region above the inlet belongs to the false pelvis.'),
('7a24227b-d604-5033-903e-01ccbe49a23a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'In Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, the best response to ''The false pelvis is:'' is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The part of the pelvis below the pelvic inlet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The part of the pelvis above the pelvic inlet (the iliac fossae)","The part of the pelvis below the pelvic outlet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the pelvis above the pelvic inlet (the iliac fossae)', 'The false pelvis is the part of the pelvis above the pelvic inlet, corresponding to the iliac fossae; the region below the inlet represents the true pelvis.'),
('a8ff28d9-2609-56db-b904-cc860ebc3f07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Pelvic Wall Vessels Nerves', 'During revision of Pelvic wall, vessels and nerves, which of the following is correct: the pubic arch is:?', '["The angle formed by the inferior pubic rami (wider in females > 90 degrees)","The same in both sexes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wider in males mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Narrower in females leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The angle formed by the inferior pubic rami (wider in females > 90 degrees)', 'The pubic arch is the angle formed by the inferior pubic rami and is wider (>90 degrees) in females than in males; it is therefore not the same in both sexes, wider in males, or narrower in females.'),
('75649bb0-3168-52b2-8971-1d917709428d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is bounded by?', '["Pubic symphysis anteriorly, ischiopubic rami anterolaterally, ischial tuberosities laterally, sacrotuberous ligaments posterolaterally, coccyx posteriorly","Iliac crests laterally, umbilicus anteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal ligaments mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Greater trochanters without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis anteriorly, ischiopubic rami anterolaterally, ischial tuberosities laterally, sacrotuberous ligaments posterolaterally, coccyx posteriorly', 'The diamond-shaped perineum is bounded by the pubic symphysis anteriorly, ischiopubic rami anterolaterally, ischial tuberosities laterally, sacrotuberous ligaments posterolaterally, and coccyx posteriorly. The iliac crests, inguinal ligaments, and greater trochanters lie outside the pelvic outlet and do not form these boundaries.'),
('b114369b-a538-5037-9dfb-02ef490a2340'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is divided into two triangles by?', '["The anus resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A vertical line mediated by specific receptor activation","The pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A transverse line between the two ischial tuberosities (interschial line)"]'::jsonb, 'A transverse line between the two ischial tuberosities (interschial line)', 'A transverse line connecting the two ischial tuberosities (the interischial line) divides the perineum into an anterior urogenital triangle and a posterior anal triangle. Neither the anus, the pubic symphysis, nor a vertical line forms this subdivision.'),
('e754b9c5-c49b-55d3-a688-5e3f9644803d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urogenital triangle contains?', '["Anal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischiorectal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","External genitalia and urethral openings","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External genitalia and urethral openings', 'The urogenital triangle is the anterior subdivision of the perineum, housing the external genitalia and the openings of the urethra (and vagina in females). The anal canal, rectum, and ischiorectal fossae belong to the posterior anal triangle.'),
('aa008bae-814c-548f-8490-32ca57afc413'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal triangle contains?', '["Anal canal, anus, ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossae","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External genitalia mediated by specific receptor activation","Urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anal canal, anus, ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossae', 'The anal triangle is the posterior subdivision of the perineum, containing the anal canal, the anus, and the ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossae. The external genitalia and urethra lie in the urogenital triangle, not here.'),
('77744ff1-1985-5569-a293-4dc433e1c7b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body (central tendon of perineum) is?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","The pubic symphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The coccyx mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibromuscular mass at the midpoint of the perineum (between anus and vagina/bulb)","The ischial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular mass at the midpoint of the perineum (between anus and vagina/bulb)', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular mass lying in the midline between the anus and the vagina (or the penile bulb in males) that anchors the perineal muscles. The pubic symphysis, coccyx, and ischial tuberosity are bony landmarks rather than this soft-tissue structure.'),
('6a733b64-b0b7-58ff-ba9c-7a899f3d9fc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is an important attachment for?', '["Only the levator ani resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the bulbospongiosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the external anal sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","Bulbospongiosus, external anal sphincter, superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, levator ani, and rectal wall","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bulbospongiosus, external anal sphincter, superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, levator ani, and rectal wall', 'The perineal body serves as the anchoring point for bulbospongiosus, the external anal sphincter, the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, levator ani, and the rectal wall. None of the single-muscle choices captures its central role in supporting the pelvic floor.'),
('5dcf9d52-6b3a-50b6-b777-af79af839084'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossa is?', '["A wedge-shaped space in the anal triangle on each side of the anal canal","A space in the urogenital triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A space in the thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A space in the pelvic cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A wedge-shaped space in the anal triangle on each side of the anal canal', 'The ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossa is a wedge-shaped, fat-filled space lying within the anal triangle on either side of the anal canal. It is not located in the urogenital triangle, the thigh, or the pelvic cavity.'),
('32b5763b-05b0-586f-8a6a-ed612a64405f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The boundaries of the ischiorectal fossa are?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only obturator internus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial: levator ani and external anal sphincter; Lateral: obturator internus fascia; Anterior: urogenital diaphragm; Posterior: sacrotuberous ligament and gluteus maximus","Only levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial: levator ani and external anal sphincter; Lateral: obturator internus fascia; Anterior: urogenital diaphragm; Posterior: sacrotuberous ligament and gluteus maximus', 'The ischiorectal fossa is bounded medially by levator ani and the external anal sphincter, laterally by the obturator internus fascia, anteriorly by the urogenital diaphragm, and posteriorly by the sacrotuberous ligament and gluteus maximus. Each of the single-structure options omits the remaining boundaries.'),
('177a309b-030e-5d1b-a97f-c7039184f821'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal (Alcock) canal is formed by?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","A split in the gluteal fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A split in the levator ani fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","A canal in the urogenital diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fascial split of the obturator internus fascia (within the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)"]'::jsonb, 'A fascial split of the obturator internus fascia (within the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)', 'The pudendal (Alcock) canal is a tunnel created by a split in the fascia overlying obturator internus along the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa. It is not formed by the gluteal fascia, the levator ani fascia, or the urogenital diaphragm.'),
('4967cd62-1107-581f-9dca-c0c45c5cfc5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal canal contains?', '["Pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels', 'The pudendal canal transmits the pudendal nerve together with the internal pudendal artery and vein. The femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves course in the thigh and pelvis and are not contained within this canal.'),
('45f5031b-5257-5132-b0bc-9a2a9079cfa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urogenital diaphragm (triangular ligament) is?', '["The same as the pelvic diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A muscle only mediated by specific receptor activation","A thick fascial sheet spanning the urogenital triangle (deep perineal pouch)","The perineal membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thick fascial sheet spanning the urogenital triangle (deep perineal pouch)', 'The urogenital diaphragm is a thick triangular fascial sheet spanning the urogenital triangle and enclosing the deep perineal pouch with its muscles. It is distinct from the pelvic diaphragm, is not purely muscular, and is not equivalent to just the perineal membrane.'),
('e0405979-1543-5524-91b2-e17792ab8f81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep perineal pouch contains?', '["Only vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","External urethral sphincter, deep transverse perineal muscle, bulbourethral glands, pudendal neurovascular branches","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the external urethral sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the bulbourethral glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External urethral sphincter, deep transverse perineal muscle, bulbourethral glands, pudendal neurovascular branches', 'The deep perineal pouch, between the layers of the urogenital diaphragm, contains the external urethral sphincter, the deep transverse perineal muscle, the bulbourethral glands (in males), and branches of the pudendal neurovascular bundle. Its contents are not limited to vessels or to any single muscle.'),
('6b5dfc46-436d-516b-8464-192963bb44a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial perineal pouch is located?', '["Between the perineal membrane (above) and the superficial perineal fascia (Colles fascia, below)","Below the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Above the pelvic diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the ischiorectal fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the perineal membrane (above) and the superficial perineal fascia (Colles fascia, below)', 'The superficial perineal pouch is a closed space bounded above by the perineal membrane and below by Colles fascia, the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia. It lies neither beneath the skin, above the pelvic diaphragm, nor within the ischiorectal fossa.'),
('7673b449-7d79-5a23-b4dd-f9412ffe6bd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The male superficial perineal pouch contains?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the bulbospongiosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Root of penis (bulb and crura), bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle","Only the crura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the ischiocavernosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Root of penis (bulb and crura), bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle', 'In males, the superficial perineal pouch contains the root of the penis (the bulb and crura) together with bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, and the superficial transverse perineal muscle. Stating any single muscle ignores the rest of the pouch contents.'),
('081ef9c8-3f18-53ce-9d82-4f6327d260bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The female superficial perineal pouch contains?', '["Only the clitoris resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only Bartholin glands mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the bulbs without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clitoral crura and bulbs of the vestibule, bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle, greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands"]'::jsonb, 'Clitoral crura and bulbs of the vestibule, bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle, greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands', 'In females, the superficial perineal pouch contains the clitoral crura and bulbs of the vestibule, the three superficial perineal muscles, and the greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands. Listing only the clitoris, bulbs, or glands gives an incomplete account of these contents.'),
('561f911c-62e6-57b6-95b6-0ba2622e2422'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The male perineum differs from the female perineum because?', '["There is difference resulting in decreased functional activity","The male has a vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The male has a smaller urogenital triangle, contains the penis and scrotum, and lacks the vaginal opening","The female has a smaller urogenital triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The male has a smaller urogenital triangle, contains the penis and scrotum, and lacks the vaginal opening', 'The male perineum has a smaller urogenital triangle, contains the penis and scrotum, and has no vaginal opening. These features contrast with the female perineum, in which the wider urogenital triangle transmits the vagina.'),
('4788d975-efb1-5be5-ad2b-611d98010369'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal membrane (inferior fascia of urogenital diaphragm) is?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","The perineal body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superior fascia of the urogenital diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","A thick fascial layer that closes the urogenital triangle inferiorly","The same as the pelvic diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thick fascial layer that closes the urogenital triangle inferiorly', 'The perineal membrane is the thick inferior fascial layer that spans and closes the urogenital triangle from below. It is neither the perineal body, the superior fascia of the urogenital diaphragm, nor the pelvic diaphragm.'),
('3cbda513-4b1b-5462-a8e1-1604df0e83ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle in males?', '["All of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Covers the crura of the penis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Covers the bulb of the penis and empties the urethra after urination/ejaculation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is the same as the ischiocavernosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the bulb of the penis and empties the urethra after urination/ejaculation', 'In males, bulbospongiosus covers the bulb of the penis and contracts to milk the urethra after urination and ejaculation. It does not cover the crura, which are served by ischiocavernosus, so it is not identical to that muscle.'),
('42503b86-66dc-5bdb-bf3d-aae8fd688f5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle in females?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Rarely accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Covers the clitoral crura mediated by specific receptor activation","Is part of the levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Covers the bulbs of the vestibule and constricts the vaginal orifice"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the bulbs of the vestibule and constricts the vaginal orifice', 'In females, bulbospongiosus covers the bulbs of the vestibule and constricts the vaginal orifice, helping to empty the vestibular area. It does not cover the clitoral crura, is not part of levator ani, and is normally present, not rare.'),
('2ab9897b-b4ea-504e-9e9b-1b86d085ccee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiocavernosus muscle?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Covers the bulb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rarely mediated by specific receptor activation","Covers the crura of the penis/clitoris and maintains erection by compressing the crura","Is the same as bulbospongiosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the crura of the penis/clitoris and maintains erection by compressing the crura', 'Ischiocavernosus arises from the ischium and covers the crura of the penis or clitoris, compressing them against the pubic rami to raise intracavernous pressure and sustain erection. It covers the crura rather than the bulb and is distinct from bulbospongiosus.'),
('ffcfe820-8598-5042-9bce-7e2d1d1bc72e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle runs?', '["From sacrum to ischium resulting in decreased functional activity","From the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body (stabilizes the perineal body)","From pubis to coccyx mediated by specific receptor activation","From ischium to coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body (stabilizes the perineal body)', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle passes from the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body, helping to stabilize the midline fibromuscular mass. The sacrum, pubis, and coccyx are not its attachments.'),
('2c59342e-b277-5272-9826-9d8653d0617a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle is?', '["The same as the superficial transverse perineal resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of the levator ani mediated by specific receptor activation","Not present in females without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A muscle in the deep perineal pouch (runs transversely from ischium to perineal body)"]'::jsonb, 'A muscle in the deep perineal pouch (runs transversely from ischium to perineal body)', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle lies within the deep perineal pouch and runs transversely from the ischium to the perineal body. It is neither part of levator ani, absent in females, nor the same structure as the superficial transverse perineal muscle.'),
('28230c08-5889-5a8e-aa7a-5ef99db789f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The external anal sphincter has which parts?', '["None of the above","Only one part","Subcutaneous and deep only","Superficial and deep only","Subcutaneous, superficial, and deep parts"]'::jsonb, 'Subcutaneous, superficial, and deep parts', 'The external anal sphincter consists of three parts: subcutaneous, superficial, and deep. Listing only one or two of these parts gives an incomplete description of its structure.'),
('acecd86d-3cd9-5ce1-9cdd-16a6b2e2ebf8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is clinically important because?', '["It is the site of the urethral sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It has clinical significance mediated by specific receptor activation","It is the site of the anal sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is an important support structure; tearing during childbirth can cause perineal prolapse"]'::jsonb, 'It is an important support structure; tearing during childbirth can cause perineal prolapse', 'The perineal body supports the pelvic organs and anchors the perineal musculature, so obstetric tearing of it can predispose to perineal or pelvic organ prolapse. It is not the site of the urethral or anal sphincters.'),
('5bc1b5a9-216f-5cd8-ac39-a89a0df5b624'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The nerves of the perineum are mainly from?', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and its branches (inferior rectal, perineal, dorsal nerve of penis/clitoris)","Sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and its branches (inferior rectal, perineal, dorsal nerve of penis/clitoris)', 'The perineum is mainly innervated by the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and its branches: the inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris. The femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves distribute to other regions.'),
('a114009e-288a-5eb1-a411-de973d12fb2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The inferior rectal nerve supplies?', '["External anal sphincter and skin around the anus","The scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The penis mediated by specific receptor activation","The urethral sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External anal sphincter and skin around the anus', 'The inferior rectal nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve, supplies the external anal sphincter and the skin around the anus. The scrotum, penis, and urethral sphincter receive their innervation from other sources.'),
('9664400d-b2a4-5764-a8e1-a7b713266310'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal nerve (branch of pudendal) supplies?', '["The urethra only resulting in decreased functional activity","The anal sphincter only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Most perineal muscles and skin of the posterior scrotum/labia","The clitoris/penis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most perineal muscles and skin of the posterior scrotum/labia', 'The perineal nerve is the major muscular branch of the pudendal nerve, supplying most perineal muscles and the skin of the posterior scrotum or labia. The urethra, anal sphincter, and glans are innervated by other nerves.'),
('ba159282-89cb-5edb-b878-f49605b97b0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis/clitoris is a branch of?', '["None of the above","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris is the terminal sensory branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). It does not arise from the femoral, obturator, or sciatic nerves.'),
('5f09a04c-18ab-5e88-aced-26fc4c289a5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The blood supply of the perineum is mainly from?', '["Internal pudendal artery (branch of internal iliac)","External iliac artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior mesenteric artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Femoral artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal pudendal artery (branch of internal iliac)', 'The perineum is supplied mainly by the internal pudendal artery, a branch of the internal iliac artery. The external iliac, femoral, and superior mesenteric arteries do not provide the principal supply to this region.'),
('ae078d25-fac1-5598-a22e-60a2a571b869'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery becomes the?', '["Medial circumflex femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal artery of the penis/clitoris and deep artery of the penis/clitoris","Deep femoral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal artery of the penis/clitoris and deep artery of the penis/clitoris', 'Distally, the internal pudendal artery continues as the perineal artery and then divides into the dorsal artery and the deep artery of the penis or clitoris, the deep artery supplying the corpus cavernosum. The medial circumflex femoral and deep femoral arteries are thigh vessels, not continuations of the internal pudendal artery.'),
('9b01ed6a-50c8-5e6f-9087-a29891c1a28f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Regarding the perineum:', '["All of the above","The ischiorectal fossa is wedge-shaped and contains pudendal neurovascular bundle","The perineal body is a fibromuscular mass in the midline","The superficial perineal pouch contains the root of the penis and bulbospongiosus","The perineum is divided into urogenital and anal triangles by a line between the ischial tuberosities"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'All four statements are true: the ischiorectal fossa is wedge-shaped and harbors the pudendal neurovascular bundle, the perineal body is a midline fibromuscular mass, the superficial pouch holds the penile root and bulbospongiosus, and the interischial line splits the perineum into urogenital and anal triangles.'),
('73fc8ba7-d8a6-5381-b473-6323cbbc2569'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'What are the boundaries of the perineum?', '["Iliac crests, umbilicus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal ligaments mediated by specific receptor activation","Greater trochanters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic symphysis, ischiopubic rami, ischial tuberosities, sacrotuberous ligaments, coccyx"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis, ischiopubic rami, ischial tuberosities, sacrotuberous ligaments, coccyx', 'The perineum is bounded by the pubic symphysis, ischiopubic rami, ischial tuberosities, sacrotuberous ligaments, and coccyx. The iliac crests, umbilicus, inguinal ligaments, and greater trochanters lie outside the inferior pelvic outlet and are not boundaries.'),
('6bb2342d-2c3a-52d0-a016-e794c78adc18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'What divides the perineum into urogenital and anal triangles?', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertical line accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pubic symphysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Anus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Line between ischial tuberosities (interischial line)"]'::jsonb, 'Line between ischial tuberosities (interischial line)', 'The transverse line between the ischial tuberosities (interischial line) divides the perineum into the anterior urogenital triangle and the posterior anal triangle. The anus, pubic symphysis, and any vertical line do not form this partition.'),
('f8a1858c-38ee-5767-ac95-242784fe14e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'What is the perineal body?', '["Fibromuscular mass at midline between anus and vagina/bulb","Coccyx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ischial tuberosity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibromuscular mass at midline between anus and vagina/bulb', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular mass lying in the midline between the anus and the vagina or the penile bulb. The coccyx, pubic symphysis, and ischial tuberosity are bony landmarks, not this soft-tissue structure.'),
('d9ce8c1d-6f04-5dea-9374-70c1f60878b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'What are the boundaries of the ischiorectal fossa?', '["Only obturator internus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only skin mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial: levator ani; Lateral: obturator internus fascia; Anterior: urogenital diaphragm; Posterior: sacrotuberous ligament"]'::jsonb, 'Medial: levator ani; Lateral: obturator internus fascia; Anterior: urogenital diaphragm; Posterior: sacrotuberous ligament', 'The ischiorectal fossa is bounded medially by levator ani, laterally by the obturator internus fascia, anteriorly by the urogenital diaphragm, and posteriorly by the sacrotuberous ligament. Each single-structure option omits the other boundaries of the fossa.'),
('61ad7e1b-dbfb-5fa1-9777-a69590aa99da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'What is the pudendal (Alcock) canal?', '["Fascial tunnel in obturator internus fascia containing pudendal nerve and vessels","Canal in urogenital diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Opening in pelvic floor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fascial tunnel in obturator internus fascia containing pudendal nerve and vessels', 'The pudendal (Alcock) canal is a fascial tunnel along the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa within the obturator internus fascia, carrying the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels. It lies neither in the urogenital diaphragm nor as an opening in the pelvic floor.'),
('6c29c74b-5fcb-582d-a59d-ec9470c7509c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is:', '["A rectangular region resulting in decreased functional activity","A circular region accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A diamond-shaped region at the inferior outlet of the pelvis","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A triangular region leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A diamond-shaped region at the inferior outlet of the pelvis', 'The perineum is a diamond-shaped region that fills the inferior outlet of the pelvis between the thighs. It is not rectangular, circular, or triangular in overall shape.'),
('a66d12bb-88ea-511a-bdc0-6e3a1da52d72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is divided into:', '["The urogenital triangle (anterior) and the anal triangle (posterior)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Three triangles mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the urogenital triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the anal triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital triangle (anterior) and the anal triangle (posterior)', 'The interischial line divides the perineum into the anterior urogenital triangle and the posterior anal triangle. There are no additional subdivisions beyond these two triangles.'),
('070226af-04fa-5c79-b0d7-be017ae284d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urogenital triangle is:', '["The anterior part of the perineum (contains the external genitalia and the urethra)","The lateral part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The posterior part mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The medial part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior part of the perineum (contains the external genitalia and the urethra)', 'The urogenital triangle is the anterior subdivision of the perineum, housing the external genitalia and the urethra. The posterior subdivision, the anal triangle, contains the anal canal.'),
('680a20ac-a24d-56eb-9439-4afdbd8acc18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal triangle is:', '["The medial part resulting in decreased functional activity","The posterior part of the perineum (contains the anal canal and the ischiorectal fossae)","The anterior part mediated by specific receptor activation","The lateral part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior part of the perineum (contains the anal canal and the ischiorectal fossae)', 'The anal triangle is the posterior subdivision of the perineum, containing the anal canal and the ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossae. The external genitalia and urethra lie in the anterior urogenital triangle.'),
('d0a7f8b6-7583-5672-90a9-16d361b8a979'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urogenital triangle is pierced by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The urethra only mediated by specific receptor activation","The vagina only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The urethra (in both sexes) and the vagina (in females)"]'::jsonb, 'The urethra (in both sexes) and the vagina (in females)', 'The urogenital triangle is pierced by the urethra in both sexes and additionally by the vagina in females. The rectum passes through the anal triangle, not through the urogenital triangle.'),
('4e3b9945-578e-5afd-a1db-d444349262ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal triangle is pierced by:', '["The urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The anal canal (the rectum)","The vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anal canal (the rectum)', 'The anal triangle transmits the anal canal, the terminal part of the bowel. The urethra and vagina pierce the urogenital triangle, not the anal triangle.'),
('00f80b16-f565-511c-af5c-f92b396576e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal membrane (urogenital diaphragm) is:', '["A muscular sheet resulting in decreased functional activity","A layer of fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A triangular fibrous sheet that stretches across the urogenital triangle (deep perineal pouch)","A layer of skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular fibrous sheet that stretches across the urogenital triangle (deep perineal pouch)', 'The perineal membrane is a triangular fibrous sheet spanning the urogenital triangle and forming the inferior boundary of the deep perineal pouch. It is not muscular, fatty, or cutaneous in nature.'),
('88a77f5b-b142-5e94-8b50-6fde0c186d05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal membrane has an opening for:', '["The anus resulting in decreased functional activity","The urethra (and the vagina in females)","The rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urethra (and the vagina in females)', 'The perineal membrane is pierced by the urethra in both sexes and by the vagina in females. The anus and rectum do not traverse the membrane, which lies in the urogenital triangle.'),
('ea445f11-16b9-545b-9bc8-6e93bed171f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep perineal pouch is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The space between the perineal membrane (inferiorly) and the pelvic diaphragm (superiorly)","The space above the pelvic diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","The space below the perineal membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space in the anal triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the perineal membrane (inferiorly) and the pelvic diaphragm (superiorly)', 'The deep perineal pouch is bounded inferiorly by the perineal membrane and superiorly by the pelvic diaphragm, within the urogenital triangle. It is not above the pelvic diaphragm, below the membrane, or located in the anal triangle.'),
('661fb041-1d04-58a4-9d80-fe7b1619cb57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial perineal pouch is:', '["The space in the anal triangle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The space below the perineal membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space above the perineal membrane mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space between the perineal membrane (superiorly) and the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia (Colles fascia) (inferiorly)"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the perineal membrane (superiorly) and the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia (Colles fascia) (inferiorly)', 'The superficial perineal pouch is bounded superiorly by the perineal membrane and inferiorly by Colles fascia, the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia. The other listed spaces have different boundaries or locations.'),
('4fdfb163-5ff4-5871-b839-3608059228e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Colles fascia is:', '["The deep perineal fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia (extends from the perineum to the abdominal wall)","The perineal membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The fatty layer of the superficial perineal fascia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia (extends from the perineum to the abdominal wall)', 'Colles fascia is the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia, continuous anteriorly with the membranous layer of the superficial fascia of the anterior abdominal wall (Scarpa fascia). It is not the deep perineal fascia, the perineal membrane, or the fatty layer.'),
('ab5e2142-61f9-5220-99d4-74bb3e71f1f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial perineal pouch contains:', '["Only the root of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the bulbospongiosus muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the ischiocavernosus muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The root of the penis/clitoris, the ischiocavernosus muscles, the bulbospongiosus muscles, and the Bartholin glands (in females)"]'::jsonb, 'The root of the penis/clitoris, the ischiocavernosus muscles, the bulbospongiosus muscles, and the Bartholin glands (in females)', 'The superficial perineal pouch contains the root of the penis or clitoris, the ischiocavernosus and bulbospongiosus muscles, and the greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands in females. Each single-structure option gives only part of the pouch contents.'),
('144dab9c-0f5d-5f1c-b0e3-3d86bc887135'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiocavernosus muscle is innervated by:', '["The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)","The femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The genitofemoral nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The obturator nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)', 'Ischiocavernosus is innervated by the perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). The femoral, genitofemoral, and obturator nerves do not supply the perineal muscles.'),
('3ebdd25d-94ee-50f8-af71-46980114c227'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle:', '["Covers the glans resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Covers the bulb of the penis/vestibular bulb (helps empty the urethra and assists in erection)","Covers the corpus cavernosum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Covers the crus of the penis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the bulb of the penis/vestibular bulb (helps empty the urethra and assists in erection)', 'Bulbospongiosus covers the bulb of the penis or the vestibular bulb, emptying the urethra and assisting erection by compressing the erectile tissue. It does not cover the glans, the corpus cavernosum, or the crus, which are covered by or related to other structures.'),
('23b6d41f-54ac-561d-8cb5-b04c8f5de7f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle is innervated by:', '["The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The genitofemoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)', 'The bulbospongiosus lies in the superficial perineal pouch and receives motor supply from the perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). The femoral, obturator, and genitofemoral nerves do not supply perineal muscles.'),
('1b473f50-7e3c-5627-b621-ef02fca3e928'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle:', '["A small muscle that runs from the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body","A muscle that runs from the sacrum to the perineal body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A muscle that runs from the pubis to the perineal body mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small muscle that runs from the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle runs horizontally from the ischial tuberosity to insert into the perineal body. It does not arise from the sacrum or the pubis.'),
('950bdd36-7edc-5a3b-82f4-481d6990304d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle helps to:', '["Fix the perineal body","Maintain erection","Constrict the anus","Empty the urethra","None"]'::jsonb, 'Fix the perineal body', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle anchors and stabilizes the perineal body in the midline. Erection, anal constriction, and urethral emptying are functions of other perineal muscles.'),
('f0c8ab02-2eb6-5339-8228-b568c71950b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle:', '["A muscle in the superficial pouch resulting in decreased functional activity","A muscle that runs from the ischial ramus to the perineal body (part of the deep perineal pouch)","A muscle in the anal triangle mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A muscle that runs from the ischial ramus to the perineal body (part of the deep perineal pouch)', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle runs from the ischial ramus to the perineal body and is a component of the deep perineal pouch. It is not located in the superficial pouch or the anal triangle.'),
('0e74ea09-8c71-5aef-9f32-9e610700ef79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body (central tendon of the perineum) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A fibromuscular mass in the midline of the perineum (between the anus and the vagina/bulb of the penis)","A cartilaginous structure mediated by specific receptor activation","A glandular structure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A bony structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibromuscular mass in the midline of the perineum (between the anus and the vagina/bulb of the penis)', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular mass lying in the midline between the anus and the vagina or the bulb of the penis, where several perineal muscles converge. It is neither cartilaginous, glandular, nor bony.'),
('8b9e26ee-d595-5861-af50-5845e7e958b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body serves as an attachment site for:', '["Only the external anal sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the levator ani accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the bulbospongiosus mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The bulbospongiosus muscle, the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, the external anal sphincter, and the levator ani","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulbospongiosus muscle, the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, the external anal sphincter, and the levator ani', 'The perineal body is the fibromuscular nexus where the bulbospongiosus, both transverse perineal muscles, the external anal sphincter, and the levator ani attach. The single-muscle options omit the full set of attachments.'),
('b584d1b9-5c83-5c71-bdda-035eb5732eaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is of great clinical importance because:', '["It is used as a landmark for episiotomy (a surgical incision to enlarge the vaginal opening)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is the site of the prostate mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is the site of the Bartholin gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is used as a landmark for episiotomy (a surgical incision to enlarge the vaginal opening)', 'The perineal body marks the midline point where a mediolateral or midline episiotomy is made to enlarge the vaginal opening during delivery. The prostate and Bartholin glands lie at other sites.'),
('47d19184-6d34-526a-8c62-495c9ee7de1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) provides:', '["Autonomic innervation to the pelvic viscera resulting in decreased functional activity","Somatic innervation to the perineum (to the muscles and skin)","Sensory innervation only mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor innervation only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatic innervation to the perineum (to the muscles and skin)', 'The pudendal nerve carries somatic motor and sensory fibers to the muscles and skin of the perineum. Autonomic supply to the pelvic viscera travels by separate pathways, and the pudendal nerve is not purely sensory or purely motor.'),
('eed065a3-890f-524e-bdf2-b6fce1d719c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve branches into:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the dorsal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the perineal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The inferior rectal nerve, the perineal nerve, and the dorsal nerve of the penis/clitoris","Only the inferior rectal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal nerve, the perineal nerve, and the dorsal nerve of the penis/clitoris', 'In Alcock''s canal the pudendal nerve divides into the inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal nerves of the penis or clitoris. It gives off all three terminal branches, not a single one.'),
('11573e93-70be-51ed-9393-9766ef094e1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The inferior rectal nerve (branch of the pudendal nerve) supplies:', '["The penis resulting in decreased functional activity","The urethral sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external anal sphincter and the skin around the anus","The vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external anal sphincter and the skin around the anus', 'The inferior rectal nerve provides motor fibers to the external anal sphincter and sensory fibers to the perianal skin. The penis, urethral sphincter, and vagina are not within its distribution.'),
('16011e96-5aab-5d9e-a4a5-882710a49ae4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal nerve (branch of the pudendal nerve) supplies:', '["The external anal sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","The urethral sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The muscles of the perineum (ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, etc.) and the skin of the posterior scrotum/labia","The penis/clitoris without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The muscles of the perineum (ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, etc.) and the skin of the posterior scrotum/labia', 'The perineal nerve, through its deep and superficial branches, supplies the muscles of the perineum and the skin of the posterior scrotum or labia. The external anal sphincter, urethral sphincter, and erectile organs receive different branches.'),
('b1ca6842-ef18-5384-9ce4-42e1d88b109c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis/clitoris (branch of the pudendal nerve) supplies:', '["The penis/clitoris (sensory innervation)","The anus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The perineal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","The urethral sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The penis/clitoris (sensory innervation)', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris is the terminal sensory branch that supplies the erectile organ. The anus, perineal muscles, and urethral sphincter have other innervation.'),
('4479876d-2941-51c4-9d79-e554217ced05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is supplied by:', '["The femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The external iliac artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The testicular artery mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal pudendal artery (a branch of the internal iliac artery)"]'::jsonb, 'The internal pudendal artery (a branch of the internal iliac artery)', 'The internal pudendal artery, a visceral branch of the internal iliac artery, is the chief arterial supply of the perineum. The femoral, external iliac, and testicular arteries do not reach this region.'),
('25318677-9af1-53b0-a4f3-83026b75be58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery passes:', '["Through the inguinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the femoral triangle mediated by specific receptor activation","Directly into the perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the greater sciatic foramen, then around the ischial spine, and through the lesser sciatic foramen into the perineum"]'::jsonb, 'Through the greater sciatic foramen, then around the ischial spine, and through the lesser sciatic foramen into the perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen, hooks around the ischial spine, and re-enters via the lesser sciatic foramen into the perineum and Alcock''s canal. It does not use the inguinal canal or femoral triangle.'),
('a029e59d-b44e-55e3-8f73-96e821656d02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery branches into:', '["Only the dorsal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and the arteries of the penis/clitoris (dorsal and deep)","Only the inferior rectal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the perineal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and the arteries of the penis/clitoris (dorsal and deep)', 'Within the perineum the internal pudendal artery gives off the inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal and deep arteries of the penis or clitoris. It does not terminate as a single branch.'),
('ce6b1f2c-895e-5157-a527-6a64c3b68887'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is drained by:', '["The testicular vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The external iliac vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The femoral vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The internal pudendal vein (which drains into the internal iliac vein)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal pudendal vein (which drains into the internal iliac vein)', 'Venous drainage of the perineum runs via the internal pudendal vein into the internal iliac vein. The testicular, external iliac, and femoral veins do not drain this region.'),
('e4a48755-bd1d-504c-81c5-b73efeb1ce74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is innervated by:', '["Only the ilioinguinal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the genitofemoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the pudendal nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and the ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and posterior cutaneous nerves of the thigh","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and the ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and posterior cutaneous nerves of the thigh', 'The pudendal nerve is the main motor and sensory nerve of the perineum, with additional sensory contributions from the ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh. No single nerve alone supplies the whole region.'),
('e4f82544-b282-5738-a76e-a12974ac3d13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is bounded superiorly by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Colles fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pelvic diaphragm (levator ani and coccygeus)","The perineal membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pelvic diaphragm (levator ani and coccygeus)', 'The pelvic diaphragm, formed by the levator ani and coccygeus, is the superior boundary separating the pelvis above from the perineum below. The skin lies inferiorly, and the perineal membrane and Colles fascia are structures within the perineum.'),
('9b1c176d-1594-5196-8de4-29becfba3628'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is divided into two parts by:', '["The anal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","The midline accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A transverse line connecting the ischial tuberosities (passing through the perineal body)","The urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A transverse line connecting the ischial tuberosities (passing through the perineal body)', 'An imaginary transverse line between the ischial tuberosities through the perineal body separates the anterior urogenital triangle from the posterior anal triangle. The anal canal, midline, or urethra does not define this division.'),
('b3f84a68-4e4b-560a-a390-85c21d2652fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The male perineum has:', '["triangles resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A urogenital triangle containing the root of the penis and an anal triangle","Only an anal triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only a urogenital triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A urogenital triangle containing the root of the penis and an anal triangle', 'The male perineum contains an anterior urogenital triangle holding the root of the penis and a posterior anal triangle. It is not limited to a single triangle.'),
('cba143b2-e2fa-5f1c-8ffb-eae602052252'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The female perineum has:', '["Only an anal triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","A urogenital triangle containing the external genitalia (vulva) and an anal triangle","Only a urogenital triangle mediated by specific receptor activation","triangles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A urogenital triangle containing the external genitalia (vulva) and an anal triangle', 'The female perineum has an anterior urogenital triangle containing the vulva and a posterior anal triangle. Both triangles are present, not just one.'),
('29a1d271-e748-5fcf-98cf-d3bf238ceab1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiorectal (ischoanal) fossa is:', '["A wedge-shaped space on each side of the anal canal (in the anal triangle)","A space in the urogenital triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A space in the deep perineal pouch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A space in the pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A wedge-shaped space on each side of the anal canal (in the anal triangle)', 'The ischiorectal fossa is a wedge-shaped, fat-filled space lying on each side of the anal canal within the anal triangle. It is not part of the urogenital triangle, deep perineal pouch, or true pelvis.'),
('93d5d2fe-9a25-545c-bff8-81ec0d8d48df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal canal (Alcock canal) is:', '["A canal in the levator ani resulting in decreased functional activity","A canal in the perineal membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A canal in the fascia of the obturator internus (contains the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A canal in the fascia of the obturator internus (contains the pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels)', 'Alcock''s canal is a tunnel within the fascia of the obturator internus that transmits the pudendal nerve and the internal pudendal vessels. It does not lie in the levator ani or the perineal membrane.'),
('1c1c2433-1da4-5169-bc44-0df0e4804418'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal canal is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","In the urogenital triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","On the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa (in the fascia of the obturator internus)","In the pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the center of the ischiorectal fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'On the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa (in the fascia of the obturator internus)', 'The pudendal canal runs in the fascia of the obturator internus along the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa. It is not in the center of the fossa, the pelvis, or the urogenital triangle.'),
('08705371-ff29-52fb-892f-c2504118efe7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An ischiorectal abscess is:', '["All","An accumulation of pus in the ischiorectal fossa","An accumulation of pus in the deep perineal pouch","An accumulation of pus in the urogenital triangle","None"]'::jsonb, 'An accumulation of pus in the ischiorectal fossa', 'An ischiorectal abscess is a collection of pus within one ischiorectal fossa. Pus collecting in the deep perineal pouch or the urogenital triangle is named for those sites instead.'),
('bce667a3-b269-5ae0-830f-fd6c5b270ffe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An ischiorectal abscess can cause:', '["Severe perineal pain, fever, and swelling","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Painless swelling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe perineal pain, fever, and swelling', 'Because the distensible ischiorectal fossa can hold a large volume of pus, an abscess there produces severe perineal pain, fever, and swelling. A painless swelling is not the typical presentation.'),
('44268d82-beb5-5928-a8d1-b14ee7669658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perianal abscess is:', '["An abscess in the ischiorectal fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","An abscess in the urogenital triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An abscess in the subcutaneous tissue around the anus"]'::jsonb, 'An abscess in the subcutaneous tissue around the anus', 'A perianal abscess forms in the subcutaneous tissue immediately around the anus. An abscess within the ischiorectal fossa or urogenital triangle is described by that location.'),
('1fcbb50a-6db4-5126-bd3b-464b2ac1e9e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An anal fistula is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An abnormal connection between the rectum and the vagina mediated by specific receptor activation","An abnormal tract connecting the anal canal to the perianal skin (usually from an infection of the anal glands)","An abnormal connection between the bladder and the rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An abnormal tract connecting the anal canal to the perianal skin (usually from an infection of the anal glands)', 'An anal fistula is an abnormal epithelial-lined tract from the anal canal to the perianal skin, usually following anal gland infection. It is not a rectovaginal or rectovesical communication.'),
('c2b095c1-e151-5cea-992c-1dd9eb05be9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An anal fistula can be caused by:', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","An infection of the anal glands (cryptoglandular infection)","Childbirth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An infection of the anal glands (cryptoglandular infection)', 'Obstruction and infection of the anal glands (cryptoglandular disease) is the usual cause of an anal fistula. Trauma, cancer, and childbirth are not the typical origins.'),
('f03d558b-2848-5635-9b80-6b2285cf5166'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A fistula-in-ano is:', '["All","None","A fistula from the rectum to the vagina","A fistula from the bladder to the rectum","Another name for an anal fistula"]'::jsonb, 'Another name for an anal fistula', 'Fistula-in-ano is the synonym for an anal fistula, a tract connecting the anal canal to the perianal skin. It is not a rectovaginal or rectovesical fistula.'),
('874cd042-9996-5fc1-99a5-210041b8608d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an important landmark for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the anal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The surgical approach to the pelvic organs (e.g., perineal prostatectomy, perineal repair of the rectum)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only episiotomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The surgical approach to the pelvic organs (e.g., perineal prostatectomy, perineal repair of the rectum)', 'Because it overlies the lower pelvic organs, the perineum provides surgical access for perineal prostatectomy and perineal repair of the rectum. Its surgical relevance is not limited to the anal canal or episiotomy alone.'),
('e4733f8d-54ad-5803-aa54-778441d0f79c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is the region:', '["In the thigh resulting in decreased functional activity","In the abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Above the pelvic diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Below the pelvic diaphragm (the inferior outlet of the pelvis)"]'::jsonb, 'Below the pelvic diaphragm (the inferior outlet of the pelvis)', 'The perineum is the diamond-shaped region below the pelvic diaphragm that forms the inferior outlet of the pelvis. It is not above the diaphragm and does not lie in the thigh or abdomen.'),
('aac40960-c561-5ada-81c1-42a12cd55e0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is divided into two triangles:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The urogenital triangle (posterior) and the anal triangle (anterior) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The urogenital triangle (anterior) and the anal triangle (posterior)","The left and right triangles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital triangle (anterior) and the anal triangle (posterior)', 'The perineum is divided into an anterior urogenital triangle and a posterior anal triangle. The positions are not reversed, and the division is not into left and right triangles.'),
('2b7ca263-e67b-529b-957b-eb9504f14234'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urogenital triangle is located:', '["Posterior to the perineal body resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the midline mediated by specific receptor activation","In the lateral pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior to the perineal body (contains the external genitalia)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the perineal body (contains the external genitalia)', 'The urogenital triangle lies in the anterior part of the perineum, anterior to the perineal body, and contains the external genitalia. It is not posterior to the perineal body.'),
('1559db84-4b9c-5190-b4bf-20b4b28006d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal triangle is located:', '["In the lateral pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior to the perineal body mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior to the perineal body (contains the anal canal)","In the midline leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to the perineal body (contains the anal canal)', 'The anal triangle occupies the posterior part of the perineum, posterior to the perineal body, and contains the anal canal. It is not anterior to the perineal body or in the lateral pelvis.'),
('601caa12-6eb9-5d6f-98f4-eb0850e68b8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep perineal pouch (urogenital diaphragm) is located:', '["Between the perineal membrane and the superficial perineal fascia resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between the perineal membrane (inferiorly) and the pelvic diaphragm (superiorly)","Between the skin and the perineal membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the perineal membrane (inferiorly) and the pelvic diaphragm (superiorly)', 'The deep perineal pouch is the compartment between the perineal membrane below and the pelvic diaphragm above. It is not between the membrane and the superficial fascia or skin.'),
('c88d8a23-a520-58c6-bb5d-1adc92b5c051'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal membrane (inferior fascia of the urogenital diaphragm) is:', '["A layer of skin resulting in decreased functional activity","A layer of fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A muscular sheet mediated by specific receptor activation","A triangular fibrous sheet that stretches across the urogenital triangle","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular fibrous sheet that stretches across the urogenital triangle', 'The perineal membrane is a strong triangular fibrous sheet stretched across the urogenital triangle. It is neither skin, fat, nor muscle.'),
('8307e2f9-944b-5cdc-9e85-db28dafa25d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Colles fascia (membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia) is:', '["A separate layer that ends at the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the perineal membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Continuous with the Scarpa fascia of the abdominal wall (and is attached to the ischiopubic rami and the posterior free edge of the perineal membrane)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous with the Scarpa fascia of the abdominal wall (and is attached to the ischiopubic rami and the posterior free edge of the perineal membrane)', 'Colles fascia is the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia, continuous anteriorly with Scarpa fascia and fixed to the ischiopubic rami and the posterior edge of the perineal membrane. It continues, rather than ends, at the perineum and is distinct from the perineal membrane.'),
('eb7e92bc-b813-5058-bfda-90b6f0a6e16e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiocavernosus muscle covers:', '["The bulb of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The corpus spongiosum mediated by specific receptor activation","The crus of the penis or the crus of the clitoris","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The crus of the penis or the crus of the clitoris', 'The ischiocavernosus envelops the crus of the penis or clitoris. The bulb of the penis, vestibular bulb, and corpus spongiosum are covered by the bulbospongiosus.'),
('55665cf9-e3dc-5c6b-936d-a0ff5dd9cc06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiocavernosus muscle functions to:', '["Empty the urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constrict the vagina mediated by specific receptor activation","Help maintain an erection by compressing the crus (preventing venous drainage)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Help maintain an erection by compressing the crus (preventing venous drainage)', 'By compressing the crus, the ischiocavernosus impedes venous outflow and helps sustain erection. Emptying the urethra and constricting the vagina belong to other perineal muscles.'),
('fa4d29a9-ba7a-55e9-aacf-09908c438680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle covers:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The crus of the penis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bulb of the penis (in males) or the vestibular bulb (in females)","The corpus cavernosum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulb of the penis (in males) or the vestibular bulb (in females)', 'The bulbospongiosus covers and compresses the bulb of the penis in males and the vestibular bulb in females. The crus of the penis is covered by the ischiocavernosus.'),
('9e855d7e-c559-54db-9d4a-bd124f417446'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle functions to:', '["Compress the crus of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maintain erection mediated by specific receptor activation","Empty the urethra (in males) and constrict the vaginal opening (in females)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Empty the urethra (in males) and constrict the vaginal opening (in females)', 'In males the bulbospongiosus assists in emptying the urethra, and in females it constricts the vaginal opening. Compression of the crus and maintenance of erection are functions of the ischiocavernosus.'),
('d6be7ef6-60d9-5952-9781-b4b5a7420b6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle is innervated by:', '["None","The genitofemoral nerve","The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)","The ilioinguinal nerve","The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh"]'::jsonb, 'The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle, like other perineal muscles, is supplied by the perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). The genitofemoral, ilioinguinal, and posterior cutaneous nerves do not innervate it.'),
('d31aadd2-5346-5439-8d5b-c5016db46009'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The external anal sphincter is divided into:', '["Only one part resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Four parts mediated by specific receptor activation","Two parts: the superficial and deep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Three parts: the subcutaneous, superficial, and deep parts"]'::jsonb, 'Three parts: the subcutaneous, superficial, and deep parts', 'The external anal sphincter, a skeletal muscle, is composed of three parts: subcutaneous, superficial, and deep. It is not a single part, two parts, or four parts.'),
('bc5de155-7ba0-5bbd-a9a2-c4df9310cdb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal anal sphincter is innervated by:', '["The pudendal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","The autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inferior rectal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The autonomic nerves (sympathetic and parasympathetic)', 'The internal anal sphincter is smooth muscle and is controlled by autonomic nerves, both sympathetic and parasympathetic. The pudendal and inferior rectal nerves supply the skeletal external sphincter instead.'),
('1d51436d-27dd-5f1a-b67c-708c5c0f5784'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The external anal sphincter is innervated by:', '["The inferior rectal nerve (branch of the pudendal nerve, S2-S4)","The genitofemoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The autonomic nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ilioinguinal nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal nerve (branch of the pudendal nerve, S2-S4)', 'The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle innervated by the inferior rectal nerve, a branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). Autonomic and other somatic nerves do not supply it.'),
('49f8cfbc-9ea0-5640-9058-be8cfc410865'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anatomy of the anal sphincters is important for:', '["Defecation only resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Anal continence (the ability to hold stool) and the surgical repair of anal fistulas and fissures","Urination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anal continence (the ability to hold stool) and the surgical repair of anal fistulas and fissures', 'The arrangement of the anal sphincters underlies fecal continence and guides the surgical repair of anal fistulas and fissures. Their clinical relevance is broader than defecation or urination alone.'),
('f9fe4bfa-5be9-50f1-ab0a-a13bcbeee9d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The conjoint longitudinal coat of the rectum is:', '["A layer of skeletal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A layer of smooth muscle that lies between the internal and external anal sphincters (it has elastic fibers that help to evert the anal canal during defecation)","A ligament mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A layer of smooth muscle that lies between the internal and external anal sphincters (it has elastic fibers that help to evert the anal canal during defecation)', 'The conjoint longitudinal coat is smooth muscle lying between the internal and external anal sphincters, with elastic fibers that assist in everting the anal canal during defecation. It is not skeletal muscle or a ligament.'),
('ca4c7b26-46a0-5d07-a84b-fe044cf99910'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal glands are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Small tubular glands in the wall of the anal canal (their ducts open into the anal crypts at the pectinate line)","Large glands in the rectum mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glands in the perineum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small tubular glands in the wall of the anal canal (their ducts open into the anal crypts at the pectinate line)', 'Anal glands are small tubular glands in the anal canal wall whose ducts open into the anal crypts at the pectinate line. They are not large rectal glands or glands in the perineum.'),
('ea959770-d483-54c8-b1f2-f09f99c9e227'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An anal gland infection can lead to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","An anal fistula (abnormal tract from the anal canal to the perianal skin)","Hemorrhoids mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An anal fissure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An anal fistula (abnormal tract from the anal canal to the perianal skin)', 'Obstruction and infection of an anal gland can drain to produce an anal fistula, an abnormal tract from the anal canal to the perianal skin. It does not directly produce hemorrhoids or an anal fissure.'),
('0be2ea89-3eff-570e-9dd9-5324c65f1f7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An anal fissure is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A tear in the perineal skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","An ulcer in the rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A painful tear in the lining of the anal canal (usually in the posterior midline)"]'::jsonb, 'A painful tear in the lining of the anal canal (usually in the posterior midline)', 'An anal fissure is a painful tear in the anal lining, classically in the posterior midline where the mucosa is least well supported. It causes severe pain and slight bright-red bleeding with defecation. Tears of the perineal skin and ulcers of the rectum are separate lesions.'),
('a095fa4f-2d0f-5258-afe6-99152ea05732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Hemorrhoids are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tumors of the anal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Growths of the rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilated vascular cushions (anal cushions) in the anal canal (can be internal or external)"]'::jsonb, 'Dilated vascular cushions (anal cushions) in the anal canal (can be internal or external)', 'Hemorrhoids are prominent, symptomatic enlargements of the normal anal vascular cushions, classified as internal or external by their position relative to the pectinate line. They are not tumors or growths of the anal canal or rectum.'),
('21b2a1fe-ed0e-51bd-834c-3cbf52db6cd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Internal hemorrhoids are located:', '["Below the pectinate line resulting in decreased functional activity","On the anal verge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Above the pectinate line (lined by mucosa, usually painless)"]'::jsonb, 'Above the pectinate line (lined by mucosa, usually painless)', 'Internal hemorrhoids form above the pectinate line, where the canal is lined by mucosa and innervated by visceral afferents, so they are typically painless. Hemorrhoids below the pectinate line are external and covered by skin.'),
('a9668cc7-7955-508e-bf3d-f6a66cc8a686'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'External hemorrhoids are located:', '["Above the pectinate line resulting in decreased functional activity","On the anal verge accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","In the rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Below the pectinate line (lined by skin, painful when thrombosed)"]'::jsonb, 'Below the pectinate line (lined by skin, painful when thrombosed)', 'External hemorrhoids form below the pectinate line from the inferior rectal venous plexus and are covered by pain-sensitive skin, so they become painful when thrombosed. Hemorrhoids above the line are internal.'),
('cdc067fe-c7b4-5181-b8b3-d228d1237d51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A mediolateral episiotomy is preferred over a midline episiotomy when:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","There is a higher risk of extending the incision into the external anal sphincter or rectum (e.g., in a difficult delivery)","The mother prefers a midline incision without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The baby is small leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'There is a higher risk of extending the incision into the external anal sphincter or rectum (e.g., in a difficult delivery)', 'A mediolateral episiotomy is angled away from the midline, so it is chosen when there is a high risk of the incision extending into the external anal sphincter or rectum, as in a difficult delivery. A midline incision heals better but is prone to extend into the anal canal.'),
('13abd1ec-a94f-5dff-8f8f-baf2b350359d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve can be blocked by:', '["An anesthetic in the subarachnoid space resulting in decreased functional activity","An anesthetic in the epidural space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An anesthetic injected near the ischial spine (a pudendal nerve block)"]'::jsonb, 'An anesthetic injected near the ischial spine (a pudendal nerve block)', 'The pudendal nerve hooks around the ischial spine, so injecting anesthetic near the spine blocks the nerve trunk and its perineal branches. Epidural or subarachnoid anesthesia blocks the spinal roots and cord, not the pudendal nerve at the spine.'),
('3d43025f-15f2-5e11-bfcf-9630f4b43f9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve block is used for:', '["Pain relief after delivery resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain relief during the second stage of labor, episiotomy, or operative vaginal delivery","Pain relief in the first stage of labor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain relief during the second stage of labor, episiotomy, or operative vaginal delivery', 'The pudendal nerve carries somatic sensation from the perineum, so blocking it relieves pain during the second stage of labor, episiotomy, and operative vaginal delivery. First-stage pain is visceral, arising from uterine contractions, and is not relieved by this block.'),
('c9268765-bbf7-520a-a8f0-796f797197c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery is the:', '["Main blood supply of the perineum (it is a branch of the internal iliac artery)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Main blood supply of the lower limb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Main blood supply of the pelvis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Main blood supply of the perineum (it is a branch of the internal iliac artery)', 'The internal pudendal artery, a terminal branch of the internal iliac artery, courses through the pudendal canal and is the principal arterial supply of the perineum. It does not supply the pelvis itself or the lower limb.'),
('eaa97a30-20f9-5238-a26a-85aabe2cc18a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery gives off:', '["The inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and the arteries to the external genitalia (dorsal and deep arteries of the penis/clitoris)","Only the perineal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the arteries to the external genitalia mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the inferior rectal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and the arteries to the external genitalia (dorsal and deep arteries of the penis/clitoris)', 'The internal pudendal artery gives off the inferior rectal artery and the perineal artery, then the dorsal and deep arteries of the penis or clitoris along its course. Options listing only a single branch omit the other branches.'),
('c43869b2-8139-5007-bd23-313060096b29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal nerve gives off:', '["The inferior rectal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The medial and lateral posterior scrotal/labial nerves (to the skin of the perineum)","The dorsal nerve of the penis/clitoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The medial and lateral posterior scrotal/labial nerves (to the skin of the perineum)', 'The perineal nerve, the larger terminal branch of the pudendal nerve, gives off the medial and lateral posterior scrotal or labial nerves supplying perineal skin. The dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris and the inferior rectal nerve are separate branches.'),
('9ec1a50e-c007-5c10-aa0e-6969a2c9035f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The posterior scrotal nerves supply:', '["The skin of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The skin of the posterior labia (in females) mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The skin of the posterior scrotum (in males)"]'::jsonb, 'The skin of the posterior scrotum (in males)', 'The posterior scrotal nerves are cutaneous branches of the perineal nerve that supply the skin of the posterior scrotum. Penile skin and the posterior labia are supplied by different nerves.'),
('48321ed5-2c61-58b6-81be-e9db786aea51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The posterior labial nerves supply:', '["The skin of the posterior scrotum (in males) resulting in decreased functional activity","The skin of the perineal body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The skin of the posterior labia majora (in females)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin of the posterior labia majora (in females)', 'In females the posterior labial nerves supply the skin of the posterior labia majora. The perineal body and the scrotum are supplied by other branches.'),
('4b506486-1e8f-558f-9e6b-ca24e29b2007'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiorectal fossa is a:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A space filled with muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A space filled with air mediated by specific receptor activation","Wedge-shaped space filled with fat on each side of the anal canal","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wedge-shaped space filled with fat on each side of the anal canal', 'The ischiorectal fossa is a wedge-shaped, fat-filled space on each side of the anal canal, bounded by the levator ani above and the fascia of the obturator internus laterally. It is filled with fat, not air or muscle.'),
('ec64451b-2fea-5c27-9f0c-b3c3c4801ebc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal canal (Alcock canal) is located:', '["In the center of the ischiorectal fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the fascia of the obturator internus (on the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the urogenital diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the fascia of the obturator internus (on the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)', 'The pudendal (Alcock) canal lies within the fascia of the obturator internus on the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa, transmitting the pudendal vessels and nerve. It does not run through the urogenital diaphragm or the center of the fossa.'),
('6297977c-1e47-5b6f-a2d9-4e2b1f9fa2bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An ischiorectal abscess can spread:', '["Across the midline (via the anterior or posterior recesses of the ischiorectal fossa)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Only on one side without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only to the urogenital triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Across the midline (via the anterior or posterior recesses of the ischiorectal fossa)', 'The two ischiorectal fossae communicate anteriorly and posteriorly around the anal canal, so an abscess can track across the midline through these recesses. The infection is not confined to one side.'),
('920c9aaa-bdfb-59d2-87db-7830dfe32b5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A horseshoe abscess is:', '["An abscess in the urogenital triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An abscess in the ischiorectal fossa that extends to the other side (anteriorly or posteriorly around the anal canal)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small, localized abscess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An abscess in the ischiorectal fossa that extends to the other side (anteriorly or posteriorly around the anal canal)', 'A horseshoe abscess is an ischiorectal abscess that extends around the anal canal into the opposite fossa through the anterior or posterior communicating recesses. A small, localized abscess does not cross the midline.'),
('cf697fd0-1dc0-5785-8d31-068a6cd5ed9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular mass that is:', '["A point for nerve supply resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The central attachment point for many of the muscles of the perineum","A point for blood supply without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central attachment point for many of the muscles of the perineum', 'The perineal body is the fibromuscular node in the midline onto which many perineal muscles attach, providing stability to the pelvic floor. It is not primarily a site of nerve or vascular supply.'),
('ad6ce408-09a1-5d86-8a77-ad10b10a9805'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The muscles that attach to the perineal body include:', '["The bulbospongiosus muscle, the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, the external anal sphincter, and the pubococcygeus (part of the levator ani)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the levator ani mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the bulbospongiosus muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the external anal sphincter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulbospongiosus muscle, the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, the external anal sphincter, and the pubococcygeus (part of the levator ani)', 'The bulbospongiosus, superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, external anal sphincter, and the pubococcygeus all attach to the perineal body. No single muscle constitutes the whole attachment.'),
('59dc97ac-e593-5401-9f0a-69bd046a0e25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is also known as:', '["The urogenital diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The perineal raphe mediated by specific receptor activation","The central tendon of the perineum","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The central tendon of the perineum', 'The perineal body is also called the central tendon of the perineum because the perineal muscles converge on it. The urogenital diaphragm and the perineal raphe are distinct structures.'),
('180db68e-3be4-56c7-910a-f219fc89f7f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A weakness or injury to the perineal body can cause:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Urinary incontinence accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fecal incontinence mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pelvic organ prolapse (cystocele, rectocele, or uterine prolapse)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic organ prolapse (cystocele, rectocele, or uterine prolapse)', 'Weakening of the perineal body removes pelvic floor support and can lead to prolapse of the bladder, rectum, or uterus, causing cystocele, rectocele, or uterine prolapse. The association with pelvic organ prolapse is the most specific.'),
('491e7f07-a87c-5bb1-aa7e-7d042b3a6c2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A cystocele is:', '["A prolapse of the rectum into the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity","A prolapse of the uterus into the vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A prolapse of the bladder into the vagina (the bladder herniates through the anterior vaginal wall)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A prolapse of the bladder into the vagina (the bladder herniates through the anterior vaginal wall)', 'A cystocele is herniation of the bladder through a weakened anterior vaginal wall. Prolapse of the rectum or uterus involves the posterior and apical compartments.'),
('fad6b6f5-09a5-550f-8c45-b41ee5585eef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A rectocele is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A prolapse of the uterus into the vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A prolapse of the bladder into the vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A prolapse of the rectum into the vagina (the rectum herniates through the posterior vaginal wall)"]'::jsonb, 'A prolapse of the rectum into the vagina (the rectum herniates through the posterior vaginal wall)', 'A rectocele is herniation of the rectum through the posterior vaginal wall, often causing difficulty with defecation. Bladder and uterine prolapse involve different compartments.'),
('53cceec6-b94e-5579-b30c-bcdaf90acbe0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Uterine prolapse is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The descent of the bladder into the vagina mediated by specific receptor activation","The descent of the rectum into the vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The descent of the uterus into the vagina (can be caused by a weakness of the pelvic floor and the perineal body)"]'::jsonb, 'The descent of the uterus into the vagina (can be caused by a weakness of the pelvic floor and the perineal body)', 'Uterine prolapse is descent of the uterus into the vaginal canal, usually from weakened pelvic floor support involving the levator ani and perineal body. Descent of the bladder or rectum is a cystocele or rectocele.'),
('59f9e2a7-4002-5a42-bfe9-25901480b011'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Pelvic organ prolapse can be treated with:', '["Only surgery resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only Kegel exercises accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Kegel exercises (pelvic floor muscle exercises), a pessary (a device inserted into the vagina), or surgery (a sacrospinous fixation or a colporrhaphy)","Only a pessary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kegel exercises (pelvic floor muscle exercises), a pessary (a device inserted into the vagina), or surgery (a sacrospinous fixation or a colporrhaphy)', 'Pelvic organ prolapse can be managed with pelvic floor muscle (Kegel) exercises, a pessary, or surgical repair such as sacrospinous fixation or colporrhaphy. Relying on any single modality alone would be incomplete.'),
('7a126fd2-c9a9-56a8-a8b9-31891aca9f48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal triangle of the perineum is:', '["The posterior part of the perineum (contains the anal canal and the ischiorectal fossae)","The part of the perineum that contains the external genitalia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior part of the perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The posterior part of the perineum (contains the anal canal and the ischiorectal fossae)', 'The anal triangle is the posterior compartment of the perineum, bounded by the ischial tuberosities and containing the anal canal and ischiorectal fossae. The external genitalia occupy the anterior urogenital triangle.'),
('9ff46b32-5edb-5439-9c16-ad18feb00998'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal triangle is bounded by:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The tip of the coccyx (posteriorly), the ischial tuberosities (laterally), and a line between the ischial tuberosities (anteriorly)","The pubic symphysis, the ischial tuberosities, and the coccyx mediated by specific receptor activation","The ischial spines and the sacrum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tip of the coccyx (posteriorly), the ischial tuberosities (laterally), and a line between the ischial tuberosities (anteriorly)', 'The anal triangle is bounded by the ischial tuberosities laterally, the tip of the coccyx posteriorly, and the transverse line joining the ischial tuberosities anteriorly. Boundaries involving the pubic symphysis or ischial spines belong to other triangles.'),
('530decac-85a9-5e06-abcc-2b4623c70140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal canal is surrounded by:', '["Only the internal anal sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","The internal anal sphincter (smooth muscle, involuntary) and the external anal sphincter (skeletal muscle, voluntary)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the external anal sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","sphincters leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal anal sphincter (smooth muscle, involuntary) and the external anal sphincter (skeletal muscle, voluntary)', 'The anal canal is surrounded by the smooth-muscle internal anal sphincter and the skeletal-muscle external anal sphincter. Either sphincter alone would fail to describe the complete muscle cuff.'),
('a258e4bd-5f6c-574b-aafd-6269c8ad4641'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The puborectalis muscle is:', '["A part of the external anal sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","AU-shaped sling of skeletal muscle that is part of the levator ani (it maintains the anorectal angle and is essential for fecal continence)","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A part of the internal anal sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AU-shaped sling of skeletal muscle that is part of the levator ani (it maintains the anorectal angle and is essential for fecal continence)', 'The puborectalis is aU-shaped sling of skeletal muscle forming part of the levator ani; it loops around the anorectal junction to maintain the anorectal angle and fecal continence. It is not part of either anal sphincter.'),
('404e5dc1-8b4f-5118-bf76-356f8814a192'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anorectal angle is:', '["The angle between the anal canal and the perineal skin resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The angle between the rectum and the anal canal (maintained by the puborectalis sling) - this angle helps to prevent fecal incontinence","The angle between the sigmoid colon and the rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The angle between the rectum and the anal canal (maintained by the puborectalis sling) - this angle helps to prevent fecal incontinence', 'The anorectal angle is created between the rectum and anal canal by the tonic pull of the puborectalis sling, helping to prevent fecal incontinence. Angles involving the sigmoid colon or the perineal skin are unrelated.'),
('d49d9213-4067-5587-8027-0fc08cafd9a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anorectal angle is important for:', '["Fecal continence (it creates a flap-valve that keeps stool in the rectum until defecation)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The passage of stool mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinary continence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fecal continence (it creates a flap-valve that keeps stool in the rectum until defecation)', 'The anorectal angle is important for fecal continence because it acts as a flap valve that keeps stool in the rectum until defecation. Urinary continence depends on separate sphincter mechanisms.'),
('da06f376-531e-5828-9a12-67042b4fa84a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'During defecation, the puborectalis muscle:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxes (the anorectal angle is lost, allowing the stool to pass into the anal canal)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Contracts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Does not change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxes (the anorectal angle is lost, allowing the stool to pass into the anal canal)', 'During defecation the puborectalis relaxes, straightening the anorectal angle so stool can pass into the anal canal. Contraction of the muscle would obstruct defecation.'),
('f71a7d74-4bac-56e2-a86c-4d42e6b5bc1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal columns (columns of Morgagni) are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transverse folds mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Longitudinal folds in the mucosa of the anal canal, above the pectinate line (they contain the terminal branches of the superior rectal artery and the anal sinuses)","Circular folds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Longitudinal folds in the mucosa of the anal canal, above the pectinate line (they contain the terminal branches of the superior rectal artery and the anal sinuses)', 'The anal columns of Morgagni are longitudinal mucosal folds above the pectinate line containing terminal branches of the superior rectal artery, between which lie the anal sinuses. They are neither transverse nor circular folds.'),
('982e5e80-74e5-5581-ab17-1efa41afaf82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal sinuses are:', '["The same as the anal columns resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The same as the anal papillae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small grooves or pockets between the anal columns (their openings are called anal crypts, and the anal glands empty into them)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small grooves or pockets between the anal columns (their openings are called anal crypts, and the anal glands empty into them)', 'The anal sinuses are the grooves between the anal columns; their openings form the anal crypts, into which the anal glands empty. They are distinct from the columns and the anal papillae.'),
('b2205275-7191-5dac-b4e5-6e71e7b9fd33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pectinate (dentate) line is:', '["The same as the anal verge resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An important landmark that divides the anal canal into an upper (embryologic endoderm) part and a lower (embryologic ectoderm) part","The same as the anorectal junction mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An important landmark that divides the anal canal into an upper (embryologic endoderm) part and a lower (embryologic ectoderm) part', 'The pectinate line marks the junction between the endoderm-derived upper anal canal (columnar epithelium) and the ectoderm-derived lower canal (squamous epithelium). It lies midway up the canal, well below the anorectal junction and above the anal verge.'),
('345ad5ef-8a5d-55e4-85a2-4b8197234b94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pectinate line is about:', '["At the external anal orifice","2 cm from the anal verge","All","None","At the anorectal junction"]'::jsonb, '2 cm from the anal verge', 'The pectinate line lies approximately 2 cm above the anal verge (external anal orifice). The anorectal junction is higher, about 2 cm above the pectinate line.'),
('c0b1b9fb-cae3-578a-8c9f-4b28e2e75ed8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal canal above the pectinate line is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Lined by squamous epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensitive to pain mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lined by columnar epithelium (endoderm), and its blood supply and lymphatic drainage are from the internal iliac system (superior rectal artery, internal iliac nodes)"]'::jsonb, 'Lined by columnar epithelium (endoderm), and its blood supply and lymphatic drainage are from the internal iliac system (superior rectal artery, internal iliac nodes)', 'Above the pectinate line the canal is lined by columnar epithelium of endodermal origin and drains to internal iliac nodes, with supply from the superior rectal artery. Squamous lining and pain sensitivity characterize the segment below the line.'),
('17f31357-93b0-56aa-81e2-c3d9a86cbe85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal canal below the pectinate line is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Lined by columnar epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not sensitive to pain mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Lined by stratified squamous epithelium (ectoderm), and its blood supply and lymphatic drainage are from the internal pudendal system (inferior rectal artery, inguinal nodes), and it is sensitive to pain","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lined by stratified squamous epithelium (ectoderm), and its blood supply and lymphatic drainage are from the internal pudendal system (inferior rectal artery, inguinal nodes), and it is sensitive to pain', 'Below the pectinate line the canal is lined by stratified squamous epithelium of ectodermal origin, supplied by the inferior rectal artery, draining to inguinal nodes, and sensitive to pain from its somatic innervation. The features above the line are those of columnar mucosa.'),
('6bf0da59-087b-58f1-9eba-55809e95d9d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal canal is an important site for:', '["Infections resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Fistulas without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemorrhoids (internal hemorrhoids are above the pectinate line, and external hemorrhoids are below the pectinate line)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhoids (internal hemorrhoids are above the pectinate line, and external hemorrhoids are below the pectinate line)', 'The anal canal is a classic site for hemorrhoids, and the position of a hemorrhoid relative to the pectinate line determines whether it is internal or external. This option states that site relationship completely.'),
('b6a5dd3d-6f91-578d-a8ae-f042a4f99f6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal hemorrhoids are classified as:', '["Only Grade 3 and 4 resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","There are grades accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Grade 1 (bleeding only), Grade 2 (prolapse but reduced spontaneously), Grade 3 (prolapse that requires manual reduction), and Grade 4 (irreducible prolapse)","Only Grade 1 and 2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Grade 1 (bleeding only), Grade 2 (prolapse but reduced spontaneously), Grade 3 (prolapse that requires manual reduction), and Grade 4 (irreducible prolapse)', 'Internal hemorrhoids are graded from 1 (bleeding only) through 2 (spontaneous reduction), 3 (manual reduction), and 4 (irreducible prolapse) according to degree of prolapse and ease of reduction. Listing only some grades omits the full classification.'),
('c3dc3d89-7b8d-5cea-92df-9355a5d87ad8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Internal hemorrhoids above the pectinate line are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Usually painless (they are above the pectinate line, which is not sensitive to pain)","Itching mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Painful leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Usually painless (they are above the pectinate line, which is not sensitive to pain)', 'The mucosa above the pectinate line has visceral innervation and is insensitive to somatic pain, so internal hemorrhoids there are usually painless. Pain accompanies hemorrhoids at or below the pectinate line.'),
('69e00e76-a806-556d-9fac-b9e37ff19028'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Internal hemorrhoids can present with:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Painless, bright red rectal bleeding (especially during or after a bowel movement) and may prolapse through the anal canal","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Anal itching without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Severe anal pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Painless, bright red rectal bleeding (especially during or after a bowel movement) and may prolapse through the anal canal', 'Internal hemorrhoids classically cause painless bright-red rectal bleeding during or after defecation and may prolapse through the anal canal. Severe anal pain and itching point to external hemorrhoids or other perianal disease.'),
('c9304f6b-6d27-511c-80e7-b9793b2cdfac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'External hemorrhoids present with:', '["Anal itching resulting in decreased functional activity","Painless bleeding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A painful, tender, bluish swelling at the anal verge (especially if thrombosed)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A painful, tender, bluish swelling at the anal verge (especially if thrombosed)', 'External hemorrhoids are covered by pain-sensitive skin and present as a painful, tender, bluish swelling at the anal verge, particularly when thrombosed. Painless bleeding is the hallmark of internal hemorrhoids.'),
('f55e09d3-04ff-543e-be6a-5e39a0f4fe3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A thrombosed external hemorrhoid is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A ruptured hemorrhoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A painful, swollen, bluish lump at the anal verge (caused by a blood clot in an external hemorrhoidal vein)","An infected hemorrhoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A painful, swollen, bluish lump at the anal verge (caused by a blood clot in an external hemorrhoidal vein)', 'A thrombosed external hemorrhoid is a painful, swollen, bluish lump at the anal verge caused by clot formation within an external hemorrhoidal vein. Rupture and infection are separate pathologies.'),
('58662510-4c33-5dd0-be36-58e7b6698aff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A thrombosed external hemorrhoid can be treated with:', '["Conservative management (pain relief, sitz baths, stool softeners) or surgical excision (if the pain is severe)","Hemorrhoidectomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only conservative management mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only surgical excision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conservative management (pain relief, sitz baths, stool softeners) or surgical excision (if the pain is severe)', 'A thrombosed external hemorrhoid usually resolves with analgesia, sitz baths, and stool softeners, with surgical excision reserved for severe pain or large clots. Using conservative care or surgery alone is incomplete.'),
('36235252-8593-509f-b21b-a59dad3c841c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Hemorrhoidectomy is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The medical treatment of hemorrhoids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The injection of hemorrhoids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The surgical removal of hemorrhoids (used for large or symptomatic internal hemorrhoids)"]'::jsonb, 'The surgical removal of hemorrhoids (used for large or symptomatic internal hemorrhoids)', 'Hemorrhoidectomy is the surgical excision of hemorrhoids, used for large or symptomatic internal hemorrhoids. It is the operative procedure, not a medical or injection-based treatment.'),
('001fa74d-6f6b-5506-a4e3-b0bdb7b2b991'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Rubber band ligation is:', '["A procedure for external hemorrhoids resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A medical treatment for hemorrhoids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A procedure for internal hemorrhoids (a small rubber band is placed around the base of the hemorrhoid to cut off its blood supply, causing it to necrose and fall off)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A procedure for internal hemorrhoids (a small rubber band is placed around the base of the hemorrhoid to cut off its blood supply, causing it to necrose and fall off)', 'Rubber band ligation treats internal hemorrhoids by strangulating the base so the hemorrhoid necroses and sloughs. It is unsuitable for external hemorrhoids, whose overlying skin is pain-sensitive.'),
('334dc006-4abe-50c4-b319-420733840d3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Anal fissures present with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectal bleeding only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anal itching only mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Severe anal pain (especially during and after a bowel movement) and bright red blood on the toilet paper"]'::jsonb, 'Severe anal pain (especially during and after a bowel movement) and bright red blood on the toilet paper', 'Anal fissures cause severe perianal pain during and after defecation together with bright-red blood on the toilet paper. Painless bleeding or itching alone points to other anorectal conditions.'),
('b62b7655-7057-5e8f-806e-800694c4a151'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Anal fissures can be treated with:', '["Stool softeners, high-fiber diet, topical anesthetics, and sometimes surgery (lateral internal sphincterotomy)","Only topical anesthetics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only stool softeners leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stool softeners, high-fiber diet, topical anesthetics, and sometimes surgery (lateral internal sphincterotomy)', 'Anal fissures respond to stool softeners, a high-fiber diet, and topical anesthetics, with lateral internal sphincterotomy reserved for chronic fissures. Single-modality treatment is usually insufficient.'),
('832c3693-ad79-56dc-b7b9-755fe7ef0806'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A lateral internal sphincterotomy is:', '["A surgical procedure for hemorrhoids resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A procedure for an anal fistula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A surgical procedure for an anal fissure (incising the internal anal sphincter to relieve spasm and pain)"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical procedure for an anal fissure (incising the internal anal sphincter to relieve spasm and pain)', 'Lateral internal sphincterotomy divides part of the internal anal sphincter to relieve spasm, allowing an anal fissure to heal. It is used for fissures, not for hemorrhoids or fistulas.'),
('e11063a6-b94d-506f-96a3-38f367f478bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An anal fistula can present with:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small, painless lump mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A persistent, purulent or bloody discharge from an opening near the anus, with recurrent perianal infections or abscesses","Severe anal pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A persistent, purulent or bloody discharge from an opening near the anus, with recurrent perianal infections or abscesses', 'An anal fistula is an abnormal tract from the anal canal to the perianal skin that drains purulent or bloody material and causes recurrent perianal infection or abscess. A painless lump or isolated pain suggests other diagnoses.'),
('fd8f68b1-aea5-5fc5-bfbc-01528141bb6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Goodsall rule is used to:', '["Predict the course of an anal fistula (based on the position of its external opening)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diagnose hemorrhoids mediated by specific receptor activation","Diagnose an anal fissure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Predict the course of an anal fistula (based on the position of its external opening)', 'Goodsall''s rule predicts the internal opening of an anal fistula from the position of its external opening. Hemorrhoids and anal fissures are separate conditions diagnosed on clinical examination, not by this rule.'),
('1992f06c-1627-5796-9b8e-0396fd553a88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Goodsall rule states:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All fistulas are straight accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All fistulas are curved without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","If the external opening of a fistula is anterior to a transverse line drawn through the anus, the fistula will track directly to the anal canal in a radial fashion; if it is posterior, the fistula will track in a curved path to the posterior midline"]'::jsonb, 'If the external opening of a fistula is anterior to a transverse line drawn through the anus, the fistula will track directly to the anal canal in a radial fashion; if it is posterior, the fistula will track in a curved path to the posterior midline', 'Anterior fistulas run radially and directly to the anal canal, whereas posterior fistulas curve to enter at the posterior midline. The tract direction therefore depends on the position of the external opening, so blanket statements that all fistulas are straight or curved are incorrect.'),
('a29e6464-b144-5061-915d-d2050cebecfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The rule for an anal fistula: if the external opening is posterior to a line drawn through the ischial tuberosities:', '["The fistula will track to the anterior midline resulting in decreased functional activity","The fistula will likely track to the posterior midline (it will follow a curved path)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The fistula will track directly to the anal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fistula will likely track to the posterior midline (it will follow a curved path)', 'An external opening posterior to the transverse line follows a curved path to enter the anal canal at the posterior midline. Direct radial tracking and anterior midline entry apply to anterior fistulas, not posterior ones.'),
('ce127908-84d3-558c-b8c5-2abb982859ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The rule for an anal fistula: if the external opening is anterior to a line drawn through the ischial tuberosities:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The fistula will track to the posterior midline accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The fistula will track directly to the anal canal (it will follow a straight path)","The fistula will track to the anterior midline leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The fistula will track directly to the anal canal (it will follow a straight path)', 'Anterior fistulas course radially and directly into the anal canal in a straight line. Curved tracking to the posterior midline is the hallmark of posterior fistulas, so the remaining options are incorrect.'),
('62e0d380-7b50-5c9f-a07b-2122408ab5b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Fistulotomy is:', '["The surgical removal of a fistula resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The surgical opening (or unroofing) of an anal fistula (it is the most common treatment for an anal fistula)","The medical treatment of a fistula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The surgical opening (or unroofing) of an anal fistula (it is the most common treatment for an anal fistula)', 'Fistulotomy means surgically laying open (unroofing) the fistula tract and is the most common treatment for anal fistulas. Complete excision of the tract is termed fistulectomy, a distinct procedure, and medical therapy alone does not cure an established tract.'),
('bcb0aeb4-a83d-5cad-b110-e26c7ad2234f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The lymphatic drainage of the perineum is to:', '["The para-aortic lymph nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","The deep inguinal lymph nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superficial inguinal lymph nodes (for the skin of the perineum, the scrotum/labia, and the perianal region)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superficial inguinal lymph nodes (for the skin of the perineum, the scrotum/labia, and the perianal region)', 'Perineal skin, the scrotum or labia, and the perianal region drain into the superficial inguinal lymph nodes, explaining inguinal lymphadenopathy in perineal infection or malignancy. The deep inguinal and para-aortic groups drain different compartments.'),
('6f8bb72d-2c04-57fc-bcd4-ba9a92c2c849'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum can be damaged by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only episiotomy mediated by specific receptor activation","Childbirth (vaginal delivery can tear the perineum - a perineal tear), episiotomy, and trauma","Only childbirth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Childbirth (vaginal delivery can tear the perineum - a perineal tear), episiotomy, and trauma', 'Vaginal delivery is the most common cause of perineal damage, producing perpendicular tears; episiotomy is a deliberate surgical incision, and external trauma can also injure the region. Each single-mechanism option is incomplete because all three mechanisms apply.'),
('de4ad47c-f5f4-5ed7-8c90-9c80bea5326a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perineal tear is:', '["A laceration of the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A laceration of the perineum during childbirth (it is classified as first, second, third, or fourth degree, depending on how deeply it extends)","A laceration of the cervix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A laceration of the perineum during childbirth (it is classified as first, second, third, or fourth degree, depending on how deeply it extends)', 'A perineal tear is a childbirth-related laceration of the perineum graded first through fourth degree by the depth of tissue involvement. Lacerations restricted to the vagina or cervix are separate injuries and are not perineal tears.'),
('5dd29857-210d-5494-b300-43f06b05a52b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A first-degree perineal tear involves:', '["The skin, mucosa, perineal muscles, and the anal sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","The skin and the mucosa of the perineal body (the perineal skin and the vaginal mucosa)","The skin, mucosa, and the perineal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin and the mucosa of the perineal body (the perineal skin and the vaginal mucosa)', 'A first-degree tear involves only the perineal skin and vaginal mucosa. Extension into the perineal muscles defines a second-degree tear, and involvement of the anal sphincter defines a third-degree tear, so the deeper options are wrong for first degree.'),
('505f5754-a933-55e9-b684-28132d87f844'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A second-degree perineal tear involves:', '["The skin, mucosa, and the anal sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The skin, mucosa, and the muscles of the perineal body (the bulbospongiosus and the transverse perineal muscles) - but not the anal sphincter","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the skin and mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin, mucosa, and the muscles of the perineal body (the bulbospongiosus and the transverse perineal muscles) - but not the anal sphincter', 'A second-degree tear extends into the muscles of the perineal body while sparing the anal sphincter. Anal sphincter involvement would raise the grade to third degree, and skin and mucosa alone would make it first degree.'),
('a427c5b3-95b5-5abf-b777-98553c59c518'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A third-degree perineal tear involves:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The skin, mucosa, perineal muscles, and the external anal sphincter (it is a injury to the anal sphincter)","The skin, mucosa, and the perineal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the skin and mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin, mucosa, perineal muscles, and the external anal sphincter (it is a injury to the anal sphincter)', 'A third-degree tear involves the external anal sphincter in addition to the skin, mucosa, and perineal muscles. Options without sphincter involvement represent second-degree tears at most.'),
('bec3a058-c243-5fbb-b76f-8d095509303b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A fourth-degree perineal tear involves:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The skin, mucosa, and the perineal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the skin and mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The skin, mucosa, perineal muscles, external anal sphincter, and the rectal mucosa (a complete tear through the perineum and into the rectum)"]'::jsonb, 'The skin, mucosa, perineal muscles, external anal sphincter, and the rectal mucosa (a complete tear through the perineum and into the rectum)', 'A fourth-degree tear disrupts the perineum completely, extending through the anal sphincter into the rectal mucosa. Options that omit the sphincter or the rectal mucosa correspond to lesser degrees of tear.'),
('9a22dd7b-9a8a-518f-8767-0ef66769b7aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum can be surgically repaired:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Not repairable accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only after an episiotomy mediated by specific receptor activation","Only after a perineal tear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","After an episiotomy or a perineal tear (the perineal body and the posterior vaginal wall are repaired with sutures)"]'::jsonb, 'After an episiotomy or a perineal tear (the perineal body and the posterior vaginal wall are repaired with sutures)', 'Both episiotomy and perineal tears are repaired surgically by suturing the perineal body and posterior vaginal wall in layers. Repair is therefore not limited to either indication alone, and the perineum is not considered irreparable.'),
('b40463df-689f-5198-8307-51c06fe8b5ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an important structure for:', '["Only childbirth resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sexual intercourse, childbirth, and defecation","Only defecation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sexual intercourse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sexual intercourse, childbirth, and defecation', 'The perineum supports sexual function through its erectile tissues, vaginal delivery as the birth canal outlet, and defecation through the anal sphincter complex. Each single-function option is incomplete because all three roles are served.'),
('306e595e-0382-5848-83ba-c5a3f3f6a86f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is a common site for:', '["Only anal fistulas resulting in decreased functional activity","Only hemorrhoids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Perianal abscesses, anal fistulas, and hemorrhoids","Only perianal abscesses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perianal abscesses, anal fistulas, and hemorrhoids', 'Perianal abscesses, anal fistulas, and hemorrhoids are all frequent perineal and anorectal conditions. Options listing only one of them overlook the others, which are equally common in this region.'),
('da815f72-7cb4-5f8b-b041-2a341f39c3aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'An intersphincteric abscess is:', '["An abscess between the internal and external anal sphincters (often leads to an anal fistula)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An abscess in the subcutaneous tissue around the anus mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An abscess in the ischiorectal fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An abscess between the internal and external anal sphincters (often leads to an anal fistula)', 'An intersphincteric abscess lies in the plane between the internal and external anal sphincters and commonly tracks to form an anal fistula. Subcutaneous and ischiorectal fossa abscesses occupy different spaces and are separate types.'),
('aa20cd1a-b6c9-5a4e-9d71-90907439a5ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum can be examined by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A physical examination of the perineum (inspection and palpation of the perineal body and the surrounding structures)","Only by inspection mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only by palpation without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only by radiology leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A physical examination of the perineum (inspection and palpation of the perineal body and the surrounding structures)', 'The perineum is assessed clinically by inspection and palpation of the perineal body and surrounding structures. Inspection or palpation alone is insufficient as each provides complementary information, and radiology is only a supplementary study.'),
('63b433e3-0d2f-544e-a6c0-9bf4bf0327a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is in the region of:', '["The thoracic dermatomes resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The lumbar dermatomes mediated by specific receptor activation","Cutaneous innervation from the sacral and coccygeal dermatomes (S2-S5)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cutaneous innervation from the sacral and coccygeal dermatomes (S2-S5)', 'Perineal skin is innervated by the sacral and coccygeal dermatomes (S2-S5). The thoracic dermatomes supply the trunk and the lumbar dermatomes the lower limb and groin, not the perineum.'),
('108a2262-f351-52e9-8024-f2fdc5d76320'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal reflex is:', '["A stretch reflex resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A motor reflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A reflex contraction of the perineal muscles (the bulbospongiosus and the external anal sphincter) in response to a stimulus"]'::jsonb, 'A reflex contraction of the perineal muscles (the bulbospongiosus and the external anal sphincter) in response to a stimulus', 'The perineal reflex is a sensorimotor response in which stimulation causes contraction of perineal muscles such as bulbospongiosus and the external anal sphincter. Calling it merely a stretch or motor reflex overlooks the afferent limb that triggers it.'),
('2d3e580c-5d16-568d-ad6d-5c484ba22017'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbocavernosus reflex is:', '["A contraction of the bulbospongiosus muscle in response to a squeeze of the glans penis (or clitoris) - this reflex can be tested in a neurologic examination","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A contraction of the ischiocavernosus muscle in response to a stimulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A contraction of the external anal sphincter in response to a stimulus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A contraction of the bulbospongiosus muscle in response to a squeeze of the glans penis (or clitoris) - this reflex can be tested in a neurologic examination', 'The bulbocavernosus reflex is elicited by squeezing the glans penis or clitoris and produces contraction of bulbospongiosus; its absence suggests a sacral (S2-S4) or pudendal nerve lesion. Contraction of ischiocavernosus or the anal sphincter is a different reflex pathway.'),
('8ac8d0d8-ef77-55f6-94f3-3c631c89e71b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an important region for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sexual function mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only urinary continence without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fecal continence (the anal sphincters, the puborectalis muscle, and the anorectal angle are all located in the perineum)"]'::jsonb, 'Fecal continence (the anal sphincters, the puborectalis muscle, and the anorectal angle are all located in the perineum)', 'Fecal continence depends on the perineal anal sphincters, the puborectalis sling, and the anorectal angle, all situated in this region. Options naming only sexual function or urinary continence omit the dominant sphincter mechanism here.'),
('27238065-5d28-5b07-b9da-14b6b3d91e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is also important for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Urinary continence (the external urethral sphincter and the urogenital diaphragm are located in the urogenital triangle of the perineum)","Only fecal continence mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sexual function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urinary continence (the external urethral sphincter and the urogenital diaphragm are located in the urogenital triangle of the perineum)', 'Urinary continence is maintained by the external urethral sphincter and the urogenital diaphragm within the urogenital triangle of the perineum. The options naming fecal continence or sexual function alone do not describe this specific sphincter mechanism.'),
('becc00e7-565b-5a8f-a866-6041badeef07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is the region of the body that contains:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the anus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the perineal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the external genitalia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The external genitalia, the anus, and the perineal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'The external genitalia, the anus, and the perineal muscles', 'The perineum contains the external genitalia, the anus, and the perineal muscles together. Each option that lists only a single component is incomplete because the region houses all three groups.'),
('2e45bef9-ab1c-50a7-8d9e-b60390188874'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is innervated by the pudendal nerve (S2-S4), which provides:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sensory innervation to the perineal skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatic innervation to the perineal muscles (the external anal and urethral sphincters, and the muscles of the urogenital triangle) and sensory innervation to the perineal skin","Only autonomic innervation to the perineal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor innervation to the perineal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatic innervation to the perineal muscles (the external anal and urethral sphincters, and the muscles of the urogenital triangle) and sensory innervation to the perineal skin', 'The pudendal nerve carries somatic motor fibers to the perineal muscles, including both external sphincters and the urogenital triangle muscles, plus sensory fibers to the perineal skin. It provides no significant autonomic supply to these muscles, so the autonomic-only option is incorrect.'),
('1d814d13-8013-5944-8963-ae3366938dcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is supplied by the internal pudendal artery (from the internal iliac artery), which branches into:', '["Only the perineal artery resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the inferior rectal artery mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the arteries of the external genitalia without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inferior rectal artery (to the anal canal and the external anal sphincter), the perineal artery (to the perineal muscles and the scrotum/labia), and the arteries of the external genitalia (the dorsal and deep arteries of the penis/clitoris)"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal artery (to the anal canal and the external anal sphincter), the perineal artery (to the perineal muscles and the scrotum/labia), and the arteries of the external genitalia (the dorsal and deep arteries of the penis/clitoris)', 'The internal pudendal artery terminates by dividing into the inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and branches to the external genitalia, together supplying the anal canal, perineal muscles, and erectile tissues. Selecting any single branch ignores the complete distribution.'),
('41f6ee8d-bdf6-57f2-a711-245d38dea49c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The lymphatic drainage of the perineum is important for:', '["The spread of infection to the cervical lymph nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The spread of infection or cancer from the perineum to the inguinal lymph nodes","The spread of infection to the axillary lymph nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spread of infection or cancer from the perineum to the inguinal lymph nodes', 'Because the perineum drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, infections and cancers here present with inguinal lymphadenopathy. Cervical nodes drain the head and neck and axillary nodes the upper limb, so neither applies to the perineum.'),
('a50107f4-d647-5486-adec-6894a6065082'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal approach (e.g., perineal prostatectomy) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surgical approach through the abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","A surgical approach through the inguinal region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A surgical approach through the perineum to reach the prostate, the rectum, or the vagina"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical approach through the perineum to reach the prostate, the rectum, or the vagina', 'A perineal approach incises through the perineum to access organs such as the prostate, rectum, or vagina, as in perineal prostatectomy. Abdominal and inguinal routes are different approaches that do not traverse the perineum.'),
('edd9806f-8dc1-51ad-ad2c-d7731b2412f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body can be injured by:', '["Cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma (e.g., a perineal tear or an episiotomy) and can be repaired surgically","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma (e.g., a perineal tear or an episiotomy) and can be repaired surgically', 'The perineal body is commonly injured by trauma such as a perineal tear or episiotomy, and such injuries are repaired surgically in layers. Infection and cancer can affect the region but do not represent the direct traumatic mechanism described.'),
('85c86fba-f082-58ae-bd1a-253e4064629d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is a diamond-shaped space that is:', '["The inferior outlet of the pelvis (it is bounded by the pubic symphysis, the coccyx, and the ischial tuberosities)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superior inlet of the pelvis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The space in the thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior outlet of the pelvis (it is bounded by the pubic symphysis, the coccyx, and the ischial tuberosities)', 'The diamond-shaped perineum forms the inferior outlet of the pelvis, bounded by the pubic symphysis, coccyx, and ischial tuberosities. The superior pelvic inlet and the thigh are anatomically distinct regions.'),
('ec5c4689-34e3-51f4-a2e1-656eaa372b39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Fournier gangrene requires:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only observation mediated by specific receptor activation","Immediate surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue (and broad-spectrum antibiotics)","Only antibiotics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue (and broad-spectrum antibiotics)', 'Fournier gangrene is a rapidly spreading necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum that requires urgent surgical debridement of all necrotic tissue combined with broad-spectrum antibiotics. Observation or antibiotics alone cannot halt the infection.'),
('c6a045ff-192a-5853-ab00-0c67f1d9bd5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The accessory perineal muscles include:', '["Only the ischiocavernosus resulting in decreased functional activity","The ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, and the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles","Only the transverse perineal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the bulbospongiosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, and the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles', 'The accessory perineal muscles are ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, and the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles. Options naming only one muscle provide an incomplete list of this group.'),
('aea49aac-af0f-58c6-9edf-77e6d7d725b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiocavernosus muscle originates from:', '["The ischial tuberosity (and the ischiopubic ramus) and inserts into the crus of the penis or the clitoris","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The pubis without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The coccyx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ischial tuberosity (and the ischiopubic ramus) and inserts into the crus of the penis or the clitoris', 'Ischiocavernosus arises from the ischial tuberosity and ischiopubic ramus and inserts on the crus of the penis or clitoris. The sacrum, pubis, and coccyx give origin to other muscles, not this one.'),
('234e11ad-6f58-52ef-bbeb-f45bc077651a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiocavernosus muscle functions as:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An accessory muscle of defecation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","An accessory muscle of urination without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An accessory muscle of erection (it compresses the crus of the penis or the clitoris, restricting venous drainage and helping to maintain an erection)"]'::jsonb, 'An accessory muscle of erection (it compresses the crus of the penis or the clitoris, restricting venous drainage and helping to maintain an erection)', 'Ischiocavernosus compresses the crus of the penis or clitoris, limiting venous drainage and helping sustain erection. It plays no role in defecation or urination.'),
('00cd39e4-fbad-5661-a713-e329bf0d59a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle originates from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The ischial tuberosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The perineal body (and the median raphe of the bulb of the penis) and inserts into the corpus spongiosum (of the penis) and the perineal membrane","The pubis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The perineal body (and the median raphe of the bulb of the penis) and inserts into the corpus spongiosum (of the penis) and the perineal membrane', 'Bulbospongiosus arises from the perineal body and the median raphe over the penile bulb and inserts into the corpus spongiosum and the perineal membrane. The ischial tuberosity, sacrum, and pubis are origins of different muscles.'),
('e5e5f219-8363-5249-af38-2ebc404c8a7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle in the male:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constricts the bulb of the penis mediated by specific receptor activation","Constricts the bulb of the penis (to empty the urethra of urine and semen) and assists in erection","Assists in urination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constricts the bulb of the penis (to empty the urethra of urine and semen) and assists in erection', 'In the male, bulbospongiosus compresses the penile bulb to expel the last drops of urine and semen from the urethra and assists erection by compressing the erectile tissue. The option stating only constriction of the bulb omits these associated actions.'),
('5afd95b0-18ca-571d-b62c-7acf2139355d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle in the female:', '["Constricts the vaginal opening (it acts as a vaginal sphincter) and assists in emptying the urethra and the vagina","Assists in urination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constricts the clitoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constricts the vaginal opening (it acts as a vaginal sphincter) and assists in emptying the urethra and the vagina', 'In the female, bulbospongiosus narrows the vaginal introitus, acting as a vaginal sphincter, and helps empty the urethra. It does not constrict the clitoris, which is served by different erectile structures.'),
('e706c6c1-283f-55dd-9066-91077c50d06c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal membrane is pierced by:', '["The urethra (and the vagina in the female)","The urethra and the rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urethra (and the vagina in the female)', 'The perineal membrane is pierced by the urethra and, in the female, also by the vagina. The rectum lies posterior at the level of the anal triangle and does not traverse the membrane, and the male has no vaginal opening.'),
('af33aaf6-10ab-507f-8498-f099ca4bbe4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal membrane is attached to:', '["The pubic symphysis (anteriorly) resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacrum (posteriorly) mediated by specific receptor activation","The ischiopubic rami (laterally) and the perineal body (posteriorly)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ischiopubic rami (laterally) and the perineal body (posteriorly)', 'The perineal membrane is anchored laterally to the ischiopubic rami and posteriorly to the perineal body. The pubic symphysis and sacrum are not direct attachment points of this membrane.'),
('44d52b93-ce73-520d-9b22-9b618d4bc6f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep perineal pouch (urogenital diaphragm) is:', '["The space in the anal triangle resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The space between the perineal membrane (superiorly) and the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia (inferiorly) mediated by specific receptor activation","The space between the perineal membrane (inferiorly) and the pelvic diaphragm (superiorly)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The space between the perineal membrane (inferiorly) and the pelvic diaphragm (superiorly)', 'The deep perineal pouch lies between the perineal membrane below and the pelvic diaphragm above, housing the sphincter urethrae and deep transverse perineal muscles. The space between the perineal membrane and the superficial perineal fascia is the superficial pouch, and the anal triangle is a separate territory.'),
('8b316758-5be1-57d1-b25c-9d047f191761'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The external urethral sphincter is:', '["A ring of smooth muscle (involuntary) resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A ring of the levator ani muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ring of striated (voluntary) muscle that surrounds the membranous urethra (it is innervated by the pudendal nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'A ring of striated (voluntary) muscle that surrounds the membranous urethra (it is innervated by the pudendal nerve)', 'The external urethral sphincter is a striated, voluntarily controlled ring around the membranous urethra, innervated by the pudendal nerve. Smooth muscle and the levator ani are not components of this sphincter.'),
('1afe6978-7103-5b30-84ce-f8202f1819b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The external urethral sphincter is responsible for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Voluntary control of the urinary stream (it can be contracted to keep the urethra closed)","The storage of urine in the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","The involuntary control of the urinary stream without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Voluntary control of the urinary stream (it can be contracted to keep the urethra closed)', 'The external urethral sphincter can be contracted voluntarily to hold or interrupt the urinary stream. Storage of urine is a bladder function, and involuntary control of micturition is mediated by the bladder-neck sphincter, not this perineal muscle.'),
('4886609b-d5be-535e-8853-341d2bbe65c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urethra is a tube that:', '["Conducts urine from the kidney to the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Conducts urine from the bladder to the outside of the body (it is also a part of the reproductive tract in the male, conducting semen)","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Conducts urine from the ureter to the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducts urine from the bladder to the outside of the body (it is also a part of the reproductive tract in the male, conducting semen)', 'The urethra carries urine from the bladder to the exterior and, in the male, also conveys semen. The ureters conduct urine from kidney to bladder, so options describing ureteric connections are incorrect.'),
('8d1b8a81-1b39-5bfb-bf22-d1685c417f40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The clitoris is a:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Small, non-sensory organ mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Small, non-erectile organ without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small, erectile organ at the anterior junction of the labia minora (it is homologous to the penis and is rich in sensory nerve endings)"]'::jsonb, 'Small, erectile organ at the anterior junction of the labia minora (it is homologous to the penis and is rich in sensory nerve endings)', 'The clitoris is a small erectile organ at the anterior junction of the labia minora, homologous to the penis and densely innervated for sensation. Options calling it non-erectile or non-sensory contradict its structure and function.'),
('d27dd07f-e127-5e7d-bcf2-014c417450f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The clitoris is composed of:', '["Only a glans resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Two crura (attached to the ischiopubic rami), a body (where the crura meet), and a glans (at the tip, covered by the prepuce)","Only the crura mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only a body without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two crura (attached to the ischiopubic rami), a body (where the crura meet), and a glans (at the tip, covered by the prepuce)', 'The clitoris comprises two crura attached to the ischiopubic rami, a body formed where the crura converge, and a glans covered by the prepuce. Options listing only one of these parts are incomplete descriptions of its anatomy.'),
('c8b2a991-69d4-57d3-9aa8-d4fd6c15449e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The clitoris plays a role in:', '["Female sexual arousal (its engorgement with blood is part of the female sexual response)","Urination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Defecation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Female sexual arousal (its engorgement with blood is part of the female sexual response)', 'Engorgement of the clitoris with blood is part of the female sexual arousal response, analogous to penile erection. It has no role in urination or defecation.'),
('62665733-d6ce-587c-9ec9-77c6be3349ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urethral opening (external urethral orifice) is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inside the vaginal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","In the vestibule, about 1 cm posterior to the clitoris (and anterior to the vaginal opening)","On the perineal body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the vestibule, about 1 cm posterior to the clitoris (and anterior to the vaginal opening)', 'The external urethral orifice opens in the vestibule roughly 1 cm posterior to the clitoris and anterior to the vaginal opening. It does not lie inside the vagina or on the perineal body.'),
('777a3dd2-5331-5b98-8c15-e781c465f0bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The vaginal opening (introitus) is located:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the vestibule, posterior to the urethral opening","Anterior to the urethral opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","On the perineal body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the vestibule, posterior to the urethral opening', 'The vaginal introitus opens in the vestibule posterior to the external urethral orifice. Being anterior to the urethral opening or located on the perineal body is anatomically incorrect.'),
('718379ae-29da-5be2-a954-882f8857a42e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The Bartholin ducts open into:', '["The groove between the hymen and the labium minus (the posterolateral part of the vestibule)","The vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The groove between the hymen and the labium minus (the posterolateral part of the vestibule)', 'The ducts of the greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands open into the posterolateral vestibule, in the groove between the hymen and the labium minus. The urethra and the vaginal canal are not their drainage sites.'),
('20b2fec9-3245-5fa2-9823-8f4f4a44ffb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is a:', '["Fibromuscular nodule in the midline of the perineum (it is located between the vaginal opening and the anus)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Glandular nodule without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscular nodule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibromuscular nodule in the midline of the perineum (it is located between the vaginal opening and the anus)', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular nodule in the midline of the perineum, between the vaginal (or anal) opening and the anus, anchoring several perineal muscles. It is not glandular, and calling it purely muscular omits its fibrous component.'),
('dba9075f-c4de-5e63-aa1b-165bbf1cb9b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is an attachment for:', '["Only the bulbospongiosus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the external anal sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bulbospongiosus, the external anal sphincter, and the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the transverse perineal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bulbospongiosus, the external anal sphincter, and the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles', 'The perineal body anchors bulbospongiosus, the external anal sphincter, and the superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, acting as a central tendon. Options naming only one muscle are incomplete because all these muscles converge here.'),
('2eedb51c-09d8-51e1-bccd-fe51bf62b780'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body can be damaged by:', '["Childbirth (a perineal tear or an episiotomy), trauma, or surgical procedures","Only surgical procedures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only childbirth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Childbirth (a perineal tear or an episiotomy), trauma, or surgical procedures', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular node that is vulnerable to tearing during vaginal delivery (perineal tear or episiotomy), direct trauma, and pelvic surgery. Each single-cause option is incomplete because more than one mechanism can damage it.'),
('d36f6718-21ac-5151-bf2e-6595a95fbc28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A healthy perineal body is important for:', '["Only pelvic organ support resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fecal and urinary continence mediated by specific receptor activation","Pelvic organ support, fecal and urinary continence, and sexual function","Only sexual function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic organ support, fecal and urinary continence, and sexual function', 'The perineal body anchors the perineal muscles and helps hold the pelvic organs in place, and its integrity is required for fecal and urinary continence as well as sexual function. Options naming a single function or None overlook its multifunctional role.'),
('6f346f76-c61c-504f-b229-a829e36548ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body can be strengthened with:', '["Kegel exercises (pelvic floor muscle exercises)","Surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stretching exercises leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kegel exercises (pelvic floor muscle exercises)', 'Kegel exercises train the muscles of the pelvic floor, including those that attach to the perineal body, thereby strengthening it. Surgery repairs rather than strengthens, and stretching does not build pelvic floor strength.'),
('3296c813-6758-5e94-88b7-2d8d11c0462c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body can be repaired surgically:', '["For a fistula resulting in decreased functional activity","For a hemorrhoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","For a perineal tear (by a colporrhaphy or a perineorrhaphy)","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'For a perineal tear (by a colporrhaphy or a perineorrhaphy)', 'A torn perineal body is repaired surgically by perineorrhaphy, and a colporrhaphy may reinforce the adjacent vaginal wall. A fistula or a hemorrhoid involves different structures and is not primarily a perineal body repair.'),
('82d384d4-3db1-5231-bd6e-6fc4aaadcd13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Colporrhaphy is:', '["A surgical repair of the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A surgical repair of the vaginal wall (it can be used to repair a cystocele, a rectocele, or a perineal body laceration)","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A surgical repair of the uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical repair of the vaginal wall (it can be used to repair a cystocele, a rectocele, or a perineal body laceration)', 'Colporrhaphy is a surgical repair of the vaginal wall, used to correct a cystocele, a rectocele, or a perineal body laceration. It does not directly repair the bladder or the uterus.'),
('8a24fdb1-de4e-5469-8b9f-05ac86c6c298'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Perineorrhaphy is:', '["A surgical repair of the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surgical repair of the uterus mediated by specific receptor activation","A surgical repair of the perineal body (usually for a perineal laceration or an episiotomy)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical repair of the perineal body (usually for a perineal laceration or an episiotomy)', 'Perineorrhaphy restores the torn perineal body, most often after a perineal laceration or an episiotomy. Repair of the vagina or the uterus is a different procedure.'),
('dc06323e-2335-585e-a592-af696d988489'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is a complex region that requires:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","knowledge mediated by specific receptor activation","Little knowledge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A thorough knowledge of its anatomy (for the diagnosis and treatment of perineal pathology)"]'::jsonb, 'A thorough knowledge of its anatomy (for the diagnosis and treatment of perineal pathology)', 'Accurate diagnosis and treatment of perineal pathology depend on a detailed understanding of its compartments, muscular supports, and neurovascular structures. Vague terms such as ''knowledge'' or ''little knowledge'' do not convey the depth required.'),
('d5587712-8883-5ebd-bbf0-ddc82855ab47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The surgical anatomy of the perineum includes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the perineal body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the perineal pouches mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the perineal spaces without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The perineal spaces, the perineal pouches, the perineal body, and the perineal nerves and vessels"]'::jsonb, 'The perineal spaces, the perineal pouches, the perineal body, and the perineal nerves and vessels', 'The surgical anatomy of the perineum comprises the superficial and deep spaces, the perineal pouches and body, and the perineal nerves and vessels. Any option listing a single element omits the other clinically relevant components.'),
('ee8cff78-3a45-5825-960f-4a3b78f211a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal spaces include the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the deep perineal space mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the superficial perineal space without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superficial perineal space (between the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia and the perineal membrane) and the deep perineal space (between the perineal membrane and the pelvic diaphragm)"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial perineal space (between the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia and the perineal membrane) and the deep perineal space (between the perineal membrane and the pelvic diaphragm)', 'The perineum has two spaces: the superficial space lies between the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia and the perineal membrane, while the deep space lies between the perineal membrane and the pelvic diaphragm. Selecting only one space excludes the other.'),
('de3d64da-03df-5ede-a13f-a293dcf7598b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial perineal space contains:', '["Only the root of the penis resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the ischiocavernosus muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the bulbospongiosus muscle mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The root of the penis (in the male) or the clitoris (in the female), and the ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, and superficial transverse perineal muscles"]'::jsonb, 'The root of the penis (in the male) or the clitoris (in the female), and the ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus, and superficial transverse perineal muscles', 'The superficial perineal pouch holds the root of the penis in the male or the clitoris in the female together with the paired superficial perineal muscles. Options naming a single muscle or structure list only one component of this space.'),
('4da626c4-78a0-590d-8ede-62d391be2203'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep perineal space contains:', '["Only the external urethral sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the bulbourethral glands accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the deep transverse perineal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","The external urethral sphincter, the deep transverse perineal muscle, and the bulbourethral glands (in the male)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external urethral sphincter, the deep transverse perineal muscle, and the bulbourethral glands (in the male)', 'The deep perineal pouch contains the external urethral sphincter, the deep transverse perineal muscle, and the bulbourethral glands in the male. Each single-structure option provides only part of its contents.'),
('2bc771cb-40ef-51a2-b984-84dd8ed06978'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle is innervated by:', '["The ilioinguinal nerve","None","The genitofemoral nerve","The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh","The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)"]'::jsonb, 'The perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4)', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle receives its motor supply from the perineal branch of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). The ilioinguinal, genitofemoral, and posterior cutaneous nerves of the thigh innervate skin and other territories.'),
('a58add57-592c-5e36-ac2c-c1f97de8af87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle functions as:', '["A fixator of the perineal body (it helps to anchor the perineal body in place)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A constrictor of the urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","A constrictor of the vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fixator of the perineal body (it helps to anchor the perineal body in place)', 'Running from the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body, this muscle stabilizes and anchors the perineal body. Constriction of the urethra or vagina is performed by other muscles such as the bulbospongiosus.'),
('5b441aa9-9b59-5e14-84ae-55c23ee44ecd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle functions as:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A constrictor of the vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A constrictor of the urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","A support for the urogenital diaphragm (it helps to maintain the position of the pelvic floor)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A support for the urogenital diaphragm (it helps to maintain the position of the pelvic floor)', 'Spanning the pubic arch within the deep perineal pouch, this muscle helps support the urogenital diaphragm and maintain the position of the pelvic floor. It is not the primary constrictor of the urethra or the vagina.'),
('6386a224-ceba-5bbf-bac3-4b2d050e3425'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal membrane functions as:', '["A fatty support for the pelvic floor resulting in decreased functional activity","A strong, fibrous support for the urogenital triangle (it separates the superficial and deep perineal pouches)","A muscular support for the anal triangle mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A strong, fibrous support for the urogenital triangle (it separates the superficial and deep perineal pouches)', 'The perineal membrane is a strong triangular sheet of fascia that forms the inferior boundary of the deep perineal pouch and separates it from the superficial pouch. It is fibrous rather than fatty or muscular, and it belongs to the urogenital triangle.'),
('839daea6-d8f8-58f2-a8a9-62c001efe1dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body functions as:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A central attachment point for the muscles of the perineum (it is a key component of the pelvic floor)","A vascular structure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A glandular structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A central attachment point for the muscles of the perineum (it is a key component of the pelvic floor)', 'The perineal body is a fibromuscular node into which the superficial and deep perineal muscles converge, making it a key midline anchor of the pelvic floor. It is not primarily vascular or glandular.'),
('ed555286-ed56-511e-9627-dec1620de0bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal spaces are important for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the spread of infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The spread of infection and the localization of fluid collections (a hematoma or an abscess can be confined to one of the perineal spaces)","Only the localization of fluid collections leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The spread of infection and the localization of fluid collections (a hematoma or an abscess can be confined to one of the perineal spaces)', 'Perineal spaces are clinically significant because infection can spread through them and a hematoma or abscess can be confined within a single space. Emphasizing only one of these roles omits the other.'),
('dbbfcb1f-ed74-5ee0-8893-861a56fbdd0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Episiotomy can be:', '["Only a median incision resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only a mediolateral incision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A median (midline) incision or a mediolateral (angled) incision (the mediolateral incision is less likely to extend into the anal sphincter)"]'::jsonb, 'A median (midline) incision or a mediolateral (angled) incision (the mediolateral incision is less likely to extend into the anal sphincter)', 'An episiotomy is performed as a median (midline) incision along the raphe or as a mediolateral incision angled away from the rectum; the mediolateral approach is less likely to extend into the anal sphincter. Choosing only one type omits the alternative technique.'),
('8f7ce17c-24bd-5c8c-9908-7e501f6e7e9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perineal laceration is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A tear of the perineum (it can be classified as a first, second, third, or fourth-degree tear, depending on the structures involved)","A tear of the uterus mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A tear of the vagina without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A tear of the perineum (it can be classified as a first, second, third, or fourth-degree tear, depending on the structures involved)', 'A perineal laceration is a tear of the perineum graded from first to fourth degree according to the depth of structures involved. A tear of the uterus or vagina is a different injury.'),
('0e77401b-73b5-55f6-ba51-14ec662975eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A first-degree perineal laceration:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Involves the rectal mucosa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Involves only the skin and the mucous membrane of the perineum (it rarely requires suturing)","Involves the anal sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Involves the skin, mucous membrane, and the perineal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Involves only the skin and the mucous membrane of the perineum (it rarely requires suturing)', 'A first-degree perineal tear involves only the perineal skin and mucous membrane and usually heals without suturing. Involvement of the perineal muscles defines a second-degree tear, and anal or rectal involvement indicates a higher-degree injury.'),
('7de8b8c1-5f3e-5b89-a43b-2712670e07a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A second-degree perineal laceration:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Involves the skin, mucous membrane, and the perineal muscles (but does not involve the anal sphincter)","Involves the anal sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","Involves the rectal mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Involves the skin, mucous membrane, and the perineal muscles (but does not involve the anal sphincter)', 'A second-degree tear extends through the skin, mucous membrane, and perineal muscles while sparing the anal sphincter. Involvement of the anal sphincter raises the injury to a third-degree tear, and rectal mucosa involvement to a fourth-degree tear.'),
('8a7e12cd-1856-5aae-b1d2-ebbc5a00a6dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A third-degree perineal laceration:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Involves the skin, mucous membrane, perineal muscles, and the anal sphincter (it requires a careful repair by a specialist)","Involves the rectal mucosa mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Involves the vaginal mucosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Involves the skin, mucous membrane, perineal muscles, and the anal sphincter (it requires a careful repair by a specialist)', 'A third-degree perineal tear extends through the skin, mucous membrane, and perineal muscles into the anal sphincter, requiring expert surgical repair. Extension into the rectal mucosa would make the injury fourth degree.'),
('bdfca93f-fd88-5379-b5d3-d8c670ff169f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A fourth-degree perineal laceration:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Involves the skin, mucous membrane, perineal muscles, anal sphincter, and the rectal mucosa (it is the most severe type of perineal laceration)","Involves only the rectal mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Involves only the anal sphincter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Involves the skin, mucous membrane, perineal muscles, anal sphincter, and the rectal mucosa (it is the most severe type of perineal laceration)', 'A fourth-degree tear extends through all perineal layers to involve the anal sphincter and the rectal mucosa, making it the most severe grade. Limited involvement of the sphincter or mucosa alone describes a lesser injury.'),
('8708dcad-3f51-56a4-9fef-ac3b5735219e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischioanal fossa (ischiorectal fossa) is:', '["A space in the urogenital triangle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A potential space in the anal triangle (it is a wedge-shaped space between the skin of the anal region and the levator ani muscle)","A space in the pelvic cavity mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A potential space in the anal triangle (it is a wedge-shaped space between the skin of the anal region and the levator ani muscle)', 'The ischioanal fossa is a potential wedge-shaped space in the anal triangle located between the anal-region skin and the levator ani muscle. It is neither a urogenital-triangle space nor a pelvic cavity space.'),
('a2b30ffd-7649-5d43-be98-ea6c5a5fd570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischioanal fossa is bounded by:', '["The skin and the levator ani muscle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The skin of the anal region (inferiorly), the levator ani muscle (superiorly), the obturator internus muscle (laterally), and the external anal sphincter (medially)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The obturator internus muscle and the external anal sphincter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The skin of the anal region (inferiorly), the levator ani muscle (superiorly), the obturator internus muscle (laterally), and the external anal sphincter (medially)', 'The wedge-shaped ischioanal fossa is bounded inferiorly by anal-region skin, superiorly by the levator ani, laterally by the obturator internus and, medially by the external anal sphincter. Options naming only one or two of these boundaries are incomplete.'),
('5584fd6e-cdea-5026-8a44-20c6f8c2425a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischioanal fossa contains:', '["Only fat resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only vessels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fat (the ischioanal fat pad) and nerves and vessels (the pudendal nerve and the internal pudendal artery and vein, in the pudendal canal)"]'::jsonb, 'Fat (the ischioanal fat pad) and nerves and vessels (the pudendal nerve and the internal pudendal artery and vein, in the pudendal canal)', 'The ischioanal fossa is filled with fat and carries the pudendal neurovascular bundle, which travels in the pudendal canal along its lateral wall. Each single-component option describes only part of its contents.'),
('1d98f31c-42df-5698-a9fa-934d74c3070e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischioanal fossa is important for:', '["The drainage of perianal abscesses (a perianal abscess can spread into the ischioanal fossa)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The drainage of a hematoma mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The spread of infection from the anal triangle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The drainage of perianal abscesses (a perianal abscess can spread into the ischioanal fossa)', 'The ischioanal fossa is a principal site where perianal abscesses collect, and prompt surgical drainage there is required because pus can spread widely in this space. The remaining options do not identify abscess drainage as its key clinical role.'),
('3b483cbf-5aa4-57be-b623-b284053972dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perineal abscess is:', '["A collection of stool resulting in decreased functional activity","A collection of fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A collection of pus in the perineum (it can be in the urogenital triangle or the anal triangle)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of pus in the perineum (it can be in the urogenital triangle or the anal triangle)', 'A perineal abscess is a localized accumulation of pus that may arise in either the urogenital or the anal triangle. Collections of stool or fat represent unrelated processes.'),
('04cc9652-6384-5ad5-afdc-7a4e8c10dc4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A hematoma of the perineum is:', '["A collection of pus resulting in decreased functional activity","A collection of blood in the perineum (it can be caused by trauma, surgery, or childbirth)","A collection of fat mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of blood in the perineum (it can be caused by trauma, surgery, or childbirth)', 'A perineal hematoma is a collection of blood within the perineum, typically resulting from trauma, surgery, or childbirth. Collections of pus or fat describe an abscess or fatty tissue rather than a hematoma.'),
('cb824428-e0cb-552e-849a-ce661682f135'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve is:', '["A minor nerve of the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The main nerve of the perineum (it provides the motor innervation to the perineal muscles and the sensory innervation to the skin of the perineum)","A motor nerve only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A sensory nerve only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main nerve of the perineum (it provides the motor innervation to the perineal muscles and the sensory innervation to the skin of the perineum)', 'The pudendal nerve is the principal mixed nerve of the perineum, carrying both motor fibers to the perineal muscles and sensory fibers to the perineal skin. Calling it minor, or purely motor or sensory, misdescribes its function.'),
('92596c05-bc2c-52d2-a0d4-1cef2c64aabd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve originates from:', '["The lumbar plexus (L1-L3)","The sacral plexus (S2-S4)","None","The coccygeal plexus","All"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral plexus (S2-S4)', 'The pudendal nerve is formed by the anterior rami of the S2-S4 spinal nerves as part of the sacral plexus. It does not arise from the lumbar plexus or the coccygeal plexus.'),
('1ae0a11c-b8b6-5cea-868f-a1f6ab2ec243'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve leaves the pelvis through:', '["The greater sciatic foramen (it re-enters the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen)","The obturator canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inguinal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (it re-enters the pelvis through the lesser sciatic foramen)', 'The pudendal nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis, hooks around the ischial spine, and re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen into the perineum. The obturator and inguinal canals carry other structures.'),
('68559fec-b6b7-545e-8238-82b7888170ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve courses through:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ischioanal fossa (in the pudendal canal, Alcock canal) on the lateral wall of the fossa","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ischioanal fossa (in the pudendal canal, Alcock canal) on the lateral wall of the fossa', 'After re-entering the pelvis, the pudendal nerve runs in the pudendal (Alcock) canal along the lateral wall of the ischioanal fossa. It does not traverse the bladder or the rectum.'),
('9547e049-bd06-59ee-b2f8-a614c2851e11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'In the pudendal canal, the pudendal nerve is accompanied by:', '["The internal artery only resulting in decreased functional activity","The internal pudendal artery and the internal pudendal vein","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal vein only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The internal pudendal artery and the internal pudendal vein', 'Within the pudendal canal, the pudendal nerve travels with the internal pudendal artery and vein, with the nerve lying between the two vessels. Naming only one vessel describes an incomplete part of the neurovascular bundle.'),
('603df373-97e7-5561-8f86-2bf5d036cda3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve gives off the following branches:', '["Only the perineal nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the inferior rectal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the dorsal nerve of the penis or the clitoris mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inferior rectal nerve, the perineal nerve, and the dorsal nerve of the penis or the clitoris"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal nerve, the perineal nerve, and the dorsal nerve of the penis or the clitoris', 'The pudendal nerve divides into three main branches: the inferior rectal nerve, the perineal nerve, and the dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris. Options listing a single branch omit the other two.'),
('cd5a5e2c-779c-5aeb-863a-f6a39839e88f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The inferior rectal nerve (inferior anal nerve) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the skin of the anal region and the external anal sphincter","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the perineal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the penis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the skin of the anal region and the external anal sphincter', 'The inferior rectal nerve is a branch of the pudendal nerve that innervates the anal-region skin and the external anal sphincter. The perineal muscles and the penis are supplied by other pudendal branches.'),
('077af089-2db3-5033-8757-b5402c9f1f32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal nerve (superficial and deep) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the muscles and the skin of the urogenital triangle","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the penis mediated by specific receptor activation","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the anal region without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the muscles and the skin of the urogenital triangle', 'The perineal nerve divides into a deep branch to the urogenital-triangle muscles and a superficial branch (posterior scrotal or labial) to the overlying skin. The penis and anal region are supplied by other branches.'),
('750481f7-61da-526d-becf-644e7ba2eb99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis or the clitoris is:', '["A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the skin of the penis or the clitoris (it is responsible for the sensation of the glans)","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the perineal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the anal region mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A branch of the pudendal nerve that supplies the skin of the penis or the clitoris (it is responsible for the sensation of the glans)', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris, a terminal branch of the pudendal nerve, supplies the skin and glans, which is why it is targeted in penile dorsal nerve blocks. It does not supply the perineal muscles or the anal region.'),
('921cdd84-b151-5a25-8607-8b4b53dafa76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve block is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An epidural anesthesia technique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A general anesthesia technique mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A regional anesthesia technique (the injection of a local anesthetic around the pudendal nerve) that is used for pain relief during childbirth and for perineal surgery"]'::jsonb, 'A regional anesthesia technique (the injection of a local anesthetic around the pudendal nerve) that is used for pain relief during childbirth and for perineal surgery', 'A pudendal nerve block is a regional technique in which local anesthetic is injected around the pudendal nerve to provide analgesia during vaginal delivery and perineal procedures. It is neither epidural nor general anesthesia.'),
('69220d00-5296-5e14-9745-b3e1c0f51e9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal nerve block is performed by:', '["Injecting the local anesthetic into the epidural space resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Injecting the local anesthetic into the spinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Injecting the local anesthetic through the vaginal wall (or through the perineal skin) near the ischial spine (where the pudendal nerve is most accessible)"]'::jsonb, 'Injecting the local anesthetic through the vaginal wall (or through the perineal skin) near the ischial spine (where the pudendal nerve is most accessible)', 'The pudendal nerve is most accessible as it hooks around the ischial spine, so the anesthetic is injected transvaginally or transperineally at that site. Injections into the epidural or subarachnoid space describe neuraxial techniques.'),
('d3415964-860e-5d29-ab64-4c48b59a8101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery is:', '["A branch of the external iliac artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main artery of the perineum (it is a branch of the internal iliac artery)","A branch of the femoral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main artery of the perineum (it is a branch of the internal iliac artery)', 'The internal pudendal artery, a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, is the main arterial supply of the perineum. It does not arise from the external iliac or femoral artery.'),
('b56e97f1-419f-543c-8f92-41180a203db5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery passes through:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The greater sciatic foramen (to enter the gluteal region) and then through the lesser sciatic foramen (to enter the perineum)","The obturator canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inguinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The greater sciatic foramen (to enter the gluteal region) and then through the lesser sciatic foramen (to enter the perineum)', 'The internal pudendal artery follows the pudendal nerve through the greater sciatic foramen into the gluteal region and then through the lesser sciatic foramen into the perineum. The obturator and inguinal canals carry other vessels.'),
('a1a8489f-aab8-5fcb-9e29-6271de9b3d3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal artery gives off the following branches:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the perineal artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and the dorsal artery of the penis or the clitoris (and the deep artery of the penis or the clitoris)","Only the dorsal artery of the penis or the clitoris without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the inferior rectal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and the dorsal artery of the penis or the clitoris (and the deep artery of the penis or the clitoris)', 'Branches of the internal pudendal artery include the inferior rectal artery, the perineal artery, and the dorsal and deep arteries of the penis or clitoris. An option naming a single branch is incomplete.'),
('2f8a8efa-480d-5886-bb77-22afd549419e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal vein is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A branch of the femoral vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","The main vein of the perineum (it drains into the internal iliac vein)","A branch of the external iliac vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The main vein of the perineum (it drains into the internal iliac vein)', 'The internal pudendal vein is the main venous drainage of the perineum and empties into the internal iliac vein. It is not a tributary of the femoral or external iliac vein.'),
('b51652e2-f041-5948-a6f0-7bd154afe1e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The internal pudendal vein is formed by:', '["Only the perineal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The confluence of the inferior rectal vein, the perineal vein, and the dorsal vein of the penis or the clitoris","Only the inferior rectal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the dorsal vein of the penis or the clitoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The confluence of the inferior rectal vein, the perineal vein, and the dorsal vein of the penis or the clitoris', 'The internal pudendal vein forms at the confluence of the inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal veins of the penis or clitoris. Options naming a single tributary describe only part of its formation.'),
('96512cd0-a929-56bd-be8d-dd06db55140e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum has a rich lymphatic drainage:', '["The lymph from the perineum drains into the internal iliac lymph nodes resulting in decreased functional activity","The lymph from the perineum drains into the superficial inguinal lymph nodes","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The from the perineum drains into the deep inguinal lymph nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lymph from the perineum drains into the superficial inguinal lymph nodes', 'Lymphatics of the perineum, including those of the scrotum or labia, drain into the superficial inguinal lymph nodes. The deep inguinal and internal iliac nodes receive drainage from deeper pelvic structures.'),
('813f4345-495c-5f56-9410-a4bc4edd9d9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The skin of the perineum is susceptible to:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Irritation and infection (due to its warm, moist environment)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irritation and infection (due to its warm, moist environment)', 'The warm, moist skin folds of the perineum readily become irritated and infected, which is a common reason for perineal complaints. Trauma and cancer are not the characteristic susceptibility of this region.'),
('c13233f7-05b9-55ff-89d9-94a0f63aec9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perineal rash can be caused by:', '["A fungal infection, a bacterial infection, or an allergic reaction","Only a fungal infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only a bacterial infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only an allergic reaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fungal infection, a bacterial infection, or an allergic reaction', 'Perineal rashes may result from fungal infection, bacterial infection, or an allergic (contact) reaction, and often more than one cause contributes. Options naming a single cause are incomplete.'),
('9a42e447-5d6a-5004-a653-e76bcbce3d4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an important area for:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The diagnosis and management of sexually transmitted infections (STIs)","Only the diagnosis of STIs mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The diagnosis and management of reproductive health leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The diagnosis and management of sexually transmitted infections (STIs)', 'Because the urogenital and anal regions are common sites of sexually transmitted infections, the perineum is a key area for their diagnosis and treatment. Limiting the role to diagnosis alone, or broadening it to all reproductive health, is less specific.'),
('656f53d8-360a-51a3-a0dc-518e2f759546'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perineal swab is:', '["A medical test (a swab is used to sample the perineal area for the detection of bacteria, fungi, or viruses)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A medical test (a swab is used to sample the urine) mediated by specific receptor activation","A medical test (a swab is used to sample the blood) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A medical test (a swab is used to sample the perineal area for the detection of bacteria, fungi, or viruses)', 'A perineal swab samples the perineal area to detect infectious agents such as bacteria, fungi, or viruses. Sampling urine or blood describes different tests.'),
('2d842a67-f459-55ca-96f4-d72207c3719c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is vulnerable to injury during:', '["None","All","Childbirth (the perineum can be stretched or torn)","Surgery (the perineum can be cut during an episiotomy or a perineal procedure)","Trauma (the perineum can be bruised, lacerated, or fractured)"]'::jsonb, 'Childbirth (the perineum can be stretched or torn)', 'The perineum is stretched and torn most commonly during vaginal delivery. Although surgery and trauma can also injure it, childbirth is the classic vulnerable period.'),
('a5221006-6f30-59f3-90ac-b40d18f6fc09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is susceptible to the development of:', '["Only hemorrhoids resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only perianal abscesses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only perianal hematomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemorrhoids (dilated veins of the rectal plexus), perianal hematomas (a blood clot in the perianal region), and perianal abscesses (an infection of the perianal glands)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhoids (dilated veins of the rectal plexus), perianal hematomas (a blood clot in the perianal region), and perianal abscesses (an infection of the perianal glands)', 'The perineum contains the rectal venous plexus, subcutaneous vessels, and perianal glands, so it can develop hemorrhoids, perianal hematomas, and perianal abscesses. The other options list only a single lesion.'),
('b7f040d0-4586-510b-a2ff-668ec2397b8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is a complex and critical region of the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Human body (it is essential for the functions of the urinary, reproductive, and digestive systems)","Human body (it is only used for reproductive function) mediated by specific receptor activation","Human body (it is only used for digestive function) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Human body (it is only used for urinary function) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Human body (it is essential for the functions of the urinary, reproductive, and digestive systems)', 'The perineum houses the external openings of the urinary, reproductive, and digestive tracts, so intact function serves all three systems. Statements limiting it to one function are incomplete.'),
('f406175a-d859-5394-b1f9-5f33633e34eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is a region of the body that requires:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","understanding of its anatomy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A detailed understanding of its anatomy (for the diagnosis and treatment of a wide variety of medical and surgical conditions)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A basic understanding of its anatomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A detailed understanding of its anatomy (for the diagnosis and treatment of a wide variety of medical and surgical conditions)', 'The perineum is involved in many medical and surgical conditions, so clinicians need detailed anatomical knowledge of it. Only a basic understanding is insufficient.'),
('ead6026a-ffc5-5ec0-a69f-4a8e99c6c3cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Detailed knowledge of the perineum is essential for:', '["Only urologists resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstetricians, gynecologists, urologists, colorectal surgeons, and general surgeons","Only gynecologists mediated by specific receptor activation","Only obstetricians without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstetricians, gynecologists, urologists, colorectal surgeons, and general surgeons', 'These specialists routinely diagnose and treat conditions in the perineum, including urogenital and anorectal diseases. Options naming only one specialty are incomplete.'),
('8e40afbe-3b98-5226-9e76-b6440beed789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is a key area for the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Physical examination of the female genitalia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Physical examination of the male and female genitalia, the rectum, and the anus (the digital rectal exam - DRE - and the perineal exam)","Physical examination of the male genitalia only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Physical examination of the rectum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Physical examination of the male and female genitalia, the rectum, and the anus (the digital rectal exam - DRE - and the perineal exam)', 'The perineum gives access for examining the genitalia, rectum, and anus in both sexes. Options limited to a single structure or region are incomplete.'),
('0777f74b-f9af-5364-aafb-52cf0e0f7136'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The digital rectal exam (DRE) is:', '["A physical examination of the vagina resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A physical examination of the cervix accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A physical examination in which a doctor inserts a lubricated, gloved finger into the rectum (it can be used to evaluate the prostate, the rectum, and the anal sphincter)"]'::jsonb, 'A physical examination in which a doctor inserts a lubricated, gloved finger into the rectum (it can be used to evaluate the prostate, the rectum, and the anal sphincter)', 'A DRE is performed through the anus to palpate the prostate, rectal wall, and anal sphincter tone. Examinations of the vagina or cervix are separate procedures.'),
('68698215-f4c4-5f42-807b-1fa552c82533'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal exam is:', '["A physical examination of the perineum (it can include an inspection, a palpation, and a neurological exam of the perineal area)","A physical examination of the abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A physical examination of the chest mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A physical examination of the perineum (it can include an inspection, a palpation, and a neurological exam of the perineal area)', 'The perineal exam assesses the perineal region through inspection, palpation, and neurologic testing. Examinations of the abdomen or chest do not evaluate the perineum.'),
('a3613695-a6a0-5339-aae5-268b5ec8c0d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an area that can be examined using:', '["Inspection (to look for rashes, swelling, or tears), palpation (to feel for tenderness or masses), and the digital rectal exam (to evaluate the prostate, rectum, and anal sphincter)","Only inspection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the digital rectal exam without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only palpation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inspection (to look for rashes, swelling, or tears), palpation (to feel for tenderness or masses), and the digital rectal exam (to evaluate the prostate, rectum, and anal sphincter)', 'A complete perineal evaluation combines inspection, palpation, and digital rectal examination. Relying on any single method alone is incomplete.'),
('1623b87a-72a2-5540-8ba0-fb959e0be039'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Perineal warts (condyloma acuminata) can be treated with:', '["Only surgical removal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only topical medications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Topical medications, cryotherapy, or surgical removal","Only cryotherapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Topical medications, cryotherapy, or surgical removal', 'Condyloma acuminata are treated with topical agents, cryotherapy, or surgical excision. Using only one modality is incomplete.'),
('44355735-8ff7-5bd5-bd07-fdcf4a53e8cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Perineal herpes is treated with:', '["Antiviral medications (such as acyclovir, valacyclovir, or famciclovir)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibiotics mediated by specific receptor activation","Antifungals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antiviral medications (such as acyclovir, valacyclovir, or famciclovir)', 'Herpes simplex infection of the perineum responds to nucleoside analogue antiviral drugs such as acyclovir. Antibiotics and antifungals have no effect on viruses.'),
('21f8ef62-1a0c-5995-8cee-f45bf3b91060'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Perineal candidiasis is:', '["A fungal infection of the perineum (caused by the Candida species, it presents with itching, redness, and a white discharge)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A viral infection mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A bacterial infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fungal infection of the perineum (caused by the Candida species, it presents with itching, redness, and a white discharge)', 'Candida species infect the perineal skin and mucous membranes, producing itching, redness, and a white discharge. Bacterial and viral infections are not caused by Candida.'),
('755c1dc0-0ca9-5efd-997c-a3f24596624f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Perineal candidiasis is treated with:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Antibiotics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antivirals mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antifungal medications (such as clotrimazole, miconazole, or fluconazole)"]'::jsonb, 'Antifungal medications (such as clotrimazole, miconazole, or fluconazole)', 'Topical or oral azole antifungals such as clotrimazole, miconazole, or fluconazole treat candida infection. Antibiotics and antivirals are ineffective against fungi.'),
('c5e77c37-2702-5dea-906f-8dbc26a9c55a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Perineal cellulitis is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A viral infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A bacterial infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissue of the perineum (it presents with redness, swelling, warmth, and tenderness)","A fungal infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A bacterial infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissue of the perineum (it presents with redness, swelling, warmth, and tenderness)', 'Cellulitis is a bacterial infection of the skin and subcutaneous tissue marked by redness, swelling, warmth, and tenderness. Viral and fungal infections do not typically present this way.'),
('94379b54-df22-50a1-a93d-d868b03e9621'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Perineal cellulitis is treated with:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Antibiotics (oral or intravenous, depending on the severity)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Antivirals without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antifungals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibiotics (oral or intravenous, depending on the severity)', 'Bacterial cellulitis requires oral or intravenous antibiotics according to severity. Antivirals and antifungals do not target the causative bacteria.'),
('0fe5e8e7-e8ec-5260-bdba-0d5fb3a04a48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum (Fournier gangrene) is:', '["A fungal infection resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A superficial infection of the skin without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A life-threatening infection of the subcutaneous tissue and fascia of the perineum (it requires immediate surgical debridement and broad-spectrum antibiotics)"]'::jsonb, 'A life-threatening infection of the subcutaneous tissue and fascia of the perineum (it requires immediate surgical debridement and broad-spectrum antibiotics)', 'Fournier gangrene is a necrotizing fasciitis of the perineum with high mortality, requiring urgent surgical debridement and broad-spectrum antibiotics. It is not a superficial skin infection.'),
('22fa4f51-56be-5b67-891f-23b381a143d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an area of the body that is associated with:', '["A poor blood supply resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A rich blood supply, a dense lymphatic drainage, and a complex innervation (making it susceptible to a variety of pathologies)","A sparse lymphatic drainage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A rich blood supply, a dense lymphatic drainage, and a complex innervation (making it susceptible to a variety of pathologies)', 'The perineum has a rich blood supply, dense lymphatic drainage, and complex innervation, which predispose it to several pathologies. The options describing poor vascularity or sparse drainage are wrong.'),
('f13a4098-e603-55b7-b186-9c54aa761dec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an area of the body that can be affected by:', '["Trauma, infection, inflammation, and tumors (benign and malignant)","Only trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma, infection, inflammation, and tumors (benign and malignant)', 'The perineum may be involved by trauma, infection, inflammation, and both benign and malignant tumors. Options listing only a single category are incomplete.'),
('03918ea2-f04f-54aa-9e82-142319cc6204'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A straddle injury is:', '["An injury to the abdomen resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An injury to the head accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","An injury that occurs when a person falls or lands on a hard object with the legs straddled (it can cause a perineal hematoma or a laceration)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An injury that occurs when a person falls or lands on a hard object with the legs straddled (it can cause a perineal hematoma or a laceration)', 'A straddle injury compresses the perineum against a hard object, commonly producing a perineal hematoma or laceration. The other options describe injuries to other body regions.'),
('297131d1-5c4c-53ff-b6d1-150fdc0fde17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perineal hematoma is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A collection of blood in the perineum (it can be caused by a straddle injury, a fall, or a car accident)","A collection of stool mediated by specific receptor activation","A collection of pus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A collection of blood in the perineum (it can be caused by a straddle injury, a fall, or a car accident)', 'A perineal hematoma is a collection of blood in the perineal tissues, typically after a straddle injury, fall, or other trauma. It is not a collection of stool or pus.'),
('8c6f60e0-8a1c-5079-8986-ce238c67bdf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'A perineal hematoma is treated with:', '["Rest, ice, compression, and elevation (RICE) and surgical drainage (if it is large or expanding)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only rest mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only surgical drainage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rest, ice, compression, and elevation (RICE) and surgical drainage (if it is large or expanding)', 'Small hematomas resolve with rest, ice, compression, and elevation, whereas large or expanding ones require surgical drainage. Management is not limited to a single measure.'),
('d2e6b3f3-1f20-50de-9cd3-e04fe05fda8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is an area of the body that requires:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only a single specialist accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","specialist care mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An interprofessional approach to care (involving obstetricians, gynecologists, urologists, colorectal surgeons, radiologists, and nurse practitioners)"]'::jsonb, 'An interprofessional approach to care (involving obstetricians, gynecologists, urologists, colorectal surgeons, radiologists, and nurse practitioners)', 'Perineal conditions are managed collaboratively by obstetricians, gynecologists, urologists, colorectal surgeons, radiologists, and nurse practitioners. Care by only one specialist is insufficient.'),
('607ec861-51ce-59f1-91fc-44705d6f8b26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Which statement best describes the boundaries of the perineum?', '["Pubic symphysis anteriorly, ischiopubic rami anterolaterally, ischial tuberosities laterally, sacrotuberous ligaments posterolaterally, coccyx posteriorly","Iliac crests laterally, umbilicus anteriorly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inguinal ligaments mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Greater trochanters without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis anteriorly, ischiopubic rami anterolaterally, ischial tuberosities laterally, sacrotuberous ligaments posterolaterally, coccyx posteriorly', 'These are the true bony and ligamentous boundaries of the diamond-shaped perineum. The iliac crests, umbilicus, inguinal ligaments, and greater trochanters do not bound it.'),
('ec42a938-e772-5f2a-889a-92309e28f59a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineum is divided into two triangles by:', '["The anus resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A vertical line mediated by specific receptor activation","The pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A transverse line between the two ischial tuberosities (interschial line)"]'::jsonb, 'A transverse line between the two ischial tuberosities (interschial line)', 'An imaginary transverse line joining the two ischial tuberosities divides the perineum into the anterior urogenital and posterior anal triangles. The anus and pubic symphysis are contents, not dividing lines.'),
('e2dc30e0-3651-5927-b191-da4e83a56ba2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urogenital triangle contains:', '["Anal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Ischiorectal fossa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","External genitalia and urethral openings","Rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External genitalia and urethral openings', 'The urogenital triangle houses the external genitalia and the urethral openings. The anal canal, rectum, and ischiorectal fossa lie within the anal triangle.'),
('07790c66-023b-52dc-8c07-612186ba6f47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The anal triangle contains:', '["Anal canal, anus, ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossae","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External genitalia mediated by specific receptor activation","Urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anal canal, anus, ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossae', 'The anal triangle contains the anal canal, anus, and the ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossae. The external genitalia and urethra are located anteriorly in the urogenital triangle.'),
('9fb35252-5691-5223-bf2f-4c485f1d3240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Perineum: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('0a9f41c7-ed48-5478-bf13-09f2c1158fbb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is an important attachment for:', '["Only the levator ani resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the bulbospongiosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the external anal sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","Bulbospongiosus, external anal sphincter, superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, levator ani, and rectal wall","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bulbospongiosus, external anal sphincter, superficial and deep transverse perineal muscles, levator ani, and rectal wall', 'The perineal body anchors multiple muscles of the perineum and pelvic floor as well as the rectal wall, providing structural support. A single-muscle attachment is incomplete.'),
('59f4608c-5052-53c3-b95a-424786d85af7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossa is:', '["A wedge-shaped space in the anal triangle on each side of the anal canal","A space in the urogenital triangle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A space in the thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A space in the pelvic cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A wedge-shaped space in the anal triangle on each side of the anal canal', 'The ischiorectal (ischioanal) fossa is a wedge-shaped, fat-filled space in the anal triangle on each side of the anal canal. It does not lie in the urogenital triangle, thigh, or pelvic cavity.'),
('65eea8e5-921e-5150-8ea7-dd223475b361'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The boundaries of the ischiorectal fossa are:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only obturator internus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial: levator ani and external anal sphincter; Lateral: obturator internus fascia; Anterior: urogenital diaphragm; Posterior: sacrotuberous ligament and gluteus maximus","Only levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial: levator ani and external anal sphincter; Lateral: obturator internus fascia; Anterior: urogenital diaphragm; Posterior: sacrotuberous ligament and gluteus maximus', 'These four walls enclose the ischiorectal fossa on its medial, lateral, anterior, and posterior sides. Listing only a single boundary is incomplete.'),
('33a8d711-0f0d-523e-86f4-1a063ea42072'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal (Alcock) canal is formed by:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","A split in the gluteal fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A split in the levator ani fascia mediated by specific receptor activation","A canal in the urogenital diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fascial split of the obturator internus fascia (within the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)"]'::jsonb, 'A fascial split of the obturator internus fascia (within the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa)', 'The pudendal (Alcock) canal is a fascial split within the obturator internus fascia along the lateral wall of the ischiorectal fossa. The gluteal fascia, levator ani fascia, and urogenital diaphragm are not involved.'),
('d77a3f0b-5106-51df-8e20-b47c19330f3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The pudendal canal contains:', '["Pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels","Femoral nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sciatic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Obturator nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve and internal pudendal vessels', 'The pudendal canal transmits the pudendal nerve and the internal pudendal artery and vein. The femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves do not pass through it.'),
('abf7ae50-e0bc-52d2-8cf9-60db66b8413b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The urogenital diaphragm (triangular ligament) is:', '["The same as the pelvic diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A muscle only mediated by specific receptor activation","A thick fascial sheet spanning the urogenital triangle (deep perineal pouch)","The perineal membrane leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thick fascial sheet spanning the urogenital triangle (deep perineal pouch)', 'The urogenital diaphragm is the deep perineal pouch, a thick fascial sheet spanning the urogenital triangle. It differs from the pelvic diaphragm and from the perineal membrane alone.'),
('9fb4d951-ec9f-50e2-bde8-1b2fe6cf96e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep perineal pouch contains:', '["Only vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","External urethral sphincter, deep transverse perineal muscle, bulbourethral glands, pudendal neurovascular branches","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the external urethral sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the bulbourethral glands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External urethral sphincter, deep transverse perineal muscle, bulbourethral glands, pudendal neurovascular branches', 'These are the contents of the deep perineal pouch. The pouch holds more than a single vessel, sphincter, or gland.'),
('1322e94b-2707-5c99-9a74-294e18510109'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial perineal pouch is located:', '["Between the perineal membrane (above) and the superficial perineal fascia (Colles fascia, below)","Below the skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Above the pelvic diaphragm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the ischiorectal fossa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between the perineal membrane (above) and the superficial perineal fascia (Colles fascia, below)', 'The superficial perineal pouch is the potential space between the perineal membrane above and the superficial perineal (Colles) fascia below. It does not lie above the pelvic diaphragm or in the ischiorectal fossa.'),
('e25e99e1-2263-5179-a525-4d965758ad46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The male superficial perineal pouch contains:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the bulbospongiosus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Root of penis (bulb and crura), bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle","Only the crura without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the ischiocavernosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Root of penis (bulb and crura), bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle', 'The male superficial perineal pouch contains the penile root (bulb and crura) together with the bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, and superficial transverse perineal muscles. A single-muscle option is incomplete.'),
('61ae4a0b-e03b-5894-a4e1-ce52330e179d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The female superficial perineal pouch contains:', '["Only the clitoris resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only Bartholin glands mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the bulbs without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Clitoral crura and bulbs of the vestibule, bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle, greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands"]'::jsonb, 'Clitoral crura and bulbs of the vestibule, bulbospongiosus, ischiocavernosus, superficial transverse perineal muscle, greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands', 'The female superficial perineal pouch contains the erectile structures, their associated muscles, and the greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands. Options listing only one component are incomplete.'),
('2460a5b2-1c7c-560a-97b5-7d213a6936e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The male perineum differs from the female perineum because:', '["There is difference resulting in decreased functional activity","The male has a vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The male has a smaller urogenital triangle, contains the penis and scrotum, and lacks the vaginal opening","The female has a smaller urogenital triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The male has a smaller urogenital triangle, contains the penis and scrotum, and lacks the vaginal opening', 'The male perineum has a smaller urogenital triangle, contains the penis and scrotum, and has no vaginal opening. The statements about a male vagina or a smaller female triangle are false.'),
('7e23bcc0-68bf-52a3-b38c-053d2351a6c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'During therapeutic management of Perineum, What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('1a9ccb3d-d3a0-5043-b82d-f45ee84f187a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle in males:', '["All of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Covers the crura of the penis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Covers the bulb of the penis and empties the urethra after urination/ejaculation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is the same as the ischiocavernosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the bulb of the penis and empties the urethra after urination/ejaculation', 'In males the bulbospongiosus covers the penile bulb and compresses it to expel urine and semen after micturition and ejaculation. Covering the crura is the role of the ischiocavernosus.'),
('26f32fc4-cc90-53bd-a88f-40180ad07d6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The bulbospongiosus muscle in females:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Rarely accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Covers the clitoral crura mediated by specific receptor activation","Is part of the levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Covers the bulbs of the vestibule and constricts the vaginal orifice"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the bulbs of the vestibule and constricts the vaginal orifice', 'In females the bulbospongiosus covers the bulbs of the vestibule and narrows the vaginal orifice. It does not cover the clitoral crura and is not a part of the levator ani.'),
('7cccd779-0c5e-50a4-a6f1-c4784ab249d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The ischiocavernosus muscle:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Covers the bulb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rarely mediated by specific receptor activation","Covers the crura of the penis/clitoris and maintains erection by compressing the crura","Is the same as bulbospongiosus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Covers the crura of the penis/clitoris and maintains erection by compressing the crura', 'The ischiocavernosus covers the crura of the penis or clitoris and compresses them to raise cavernous pressure and maintain erection. Covering the bulb is the role of the bulbospongiosus.'),
('45e178c1-63a2-534d-a5b4-678e85a211e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle runs:', '["From sacrum to ischium resulting in decreased functional activity","From the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body (stabilizes the perineal body)","From pubis to coccyx mediated by specific receptor activation","From ischium to coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body (stabilizes the perineal body)', 'The superficial transverse perineal muscle passes from the ischial tuberosity to the perineal body, which it helps stabilize. The other stated attachments are anatomically wrong.'),
('7a09826e-e847-58ef-80bf-51c566e0ad41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle is:', '["The same as the superficial transverse perineal resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of the levator ani mediated by specific receptor activation","Not present in females without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A muscle in the deep perineal pouch (runs transversely from ischium to perineal body)"]'::jsonb, 'A muscle in the deep perineal pouch (runs transversely from ischium to perineal body)', 'The deep transverse perineal muscle lies in the deep perineal pouch and runs transversely from the ischium to the perineal body. It is not part of the levator ani and is present in both sexes.'),
('ac58653e-5586-55b0-a5b7-c0d1cf0094d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'Regarding clinical cases in Perineum: Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('5799564d-eb4d-56b6-aa69-ad16f4d5e256'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The perineal body is clinically important because:', '["It is the site of the urethral sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It has clinical significance mediated by specific receptor activation","It is the site of the anal sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is an important support structure; tearing during childbirth can cause perineal prolapse"]'::jsonb, 'It is an important support structure; tearing during childbirth can cause perineal prolapse', 'The perineal body supports the pelvic floor, and its tearing during childbirth can lead to perineal and pelvic organ prolapse. It is not the site of the urethral or anal sphincter.'),
('9ef17472-60c5-5bf7-a04c-995359127db0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The nerves of the perineum are mainly from:', '["Femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obturator nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and its branches (inferior rectal, perineal, dorsal nerve of penis/clitoris)","Sciatic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and its branches (inferior rectal, perineal, dorsal nerve of penis/clitoris)', 'The perineum is innervated mainly by the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) and its inferior rectal, perineal, and dorsal nerve branches. The femoral, sciatic, and obturator nerves are not its chief supply.'),
('ecf5d1e5-e157-56be-b0e7-431559982245'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The inferior rectal nerve supplies:', '["External anal sphincter and skin around the anus","The scrotum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The penis mediated by specific receptor activation","The urethral sphincter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External anal sphincter and skin around the anus', 'The inferior rectal nerve innervates the external anal sphincter and the perianal skin. The scrotum, penis, and urethral sphincter receive different innervation.'),
('fcf16ebf-0698-56e3-8c42-1eef7e6aff94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'During therapeutic management of Perineum, Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('ae7372ac-f3a4-5b3a-b2a3-c86c660cee29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Perineum', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis/clitoris is a branch of:', '["None of the above","Femoral nerve","Obturator nerve","Sciatic nerve","Pudendal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'The dorsal nerve of the penis and clitoris is the terminal branch of the pudendal nerve. It is not a branch of the femoral, sciatic, or obturator nerve.'),
('7cde65bc-dc62-5131-b9f3-75218ce342a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urinary bladder is located?', '["In the anterior part of the pelvic cavity, posterior to the pubic symphysis","In the posterior pelvic cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above the pelvic brim mediated by specific receptor activation","In the perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the abdominal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the anterior part of the pelvic cavity, posterior to the pubic symphysis', 'The empty bladder lies in the anterior part of the lesser pelvis, immediately behind the pubic symphysis. It rises above the pelvic brim into the abdomen only when distended.'),
('6ee6c819-771c-523c-942b-3dc16a556aab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The apex of the urinary bladder is attached to?', '["Uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Umbilicus via the median umbilical ligament (urachus)"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilicus via the median umbilical ligament (urachus)', 'The apex of the bladder points toward the umbilicus and is connected to it by the median umbilical ligament, the remnant of the fetal urachus. The base faces posteriorly and the neck lies inferiorly at the origin of the urethra.'),
('e41bc1c1-7f0a-5e6a-9835-312e711871b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The trigone of the urinary bladder is?', '["The area of the bladder dome resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The neck of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A smooth triangular area on the posterior wall of the bladder between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The bladder base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A smooth triangular area on the posterior wall of the bladder between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice', 'The trigone is the smooth triangular region at the bladder base bounded by the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice. Unlike the rest of the mucosa, it remains smooth even when the bladder is empty because its mucosa is firmly adherent to the underlying muscle.'),
('3cb94c9b-c0cd-5b38-96fd-ac7decf06352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The blood supply of the urinary bladder is from?', '["Renal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior and inferior vesical arteries (from internal iliac)","Femoral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior mesenteric artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior vesical arteries (from internal iliac)', 'The bladder is supplied mainly by the superior and inferior vesical arteries, both branches of the internal iliac artery. The renal, femoral, and inferior mesenteric arteries do not supply the bladder.'),
('8130c66e-3d79-56b4-b666-199e6660e754'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The male urethra is divided into which parts?', '["None of the above","Preprostatic and prostatic only","Preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, spongy (penile)","Prostatic, membranous, cavernous","Only prostatic and membranous"]'::jsonb, 'Preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, spongy (penile)', 'The male urethra is divided into four parts: preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, and spongy (penile). The spongy part is the longest and receives the openings of the bulbourethral glands.'),
('c6d419d6-a62f-5f86-8b80-950f1d08e613'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The female urethra is?', '["Located posterior to the vagina","Short (4 cm) and entirely in the pelvic cavity","None of the above","Long (20 cm)","Of equal length to male urethra"]'::jsonb, 'Short (4 cm) and entirely in the pelvic cavity', 'The female urethra is about 4 cm long and lies entirely within the pelvic cavity, embedded in the anterior vaginal wall. Its short course and close relation to the vestibule predispose women to ascending urinary tract infections.'),
('ddd7bff0-9279-5f10-968a-3a56df09674a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The external urethral sphincter is located?', '["At the external urethral orifice resulting in decreased functional activity","At the prostatic urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the deep perineal pouch (membranous urethra in males, mid-urethra in females)","At the bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the deep perineal pouch (membranous urethra in males, mid-urethra in females)', 'The external urethral sphincter (sphincter urethrae) lies in the deep perineal pouch, surrounding the membranous urethra in males and the mid-urethra in females. It is skeletal muscle under voluntary control.'),
('e4a1b100-7140-5d45-8818-db3f94194c33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The internal urethral sphincter is located?', '["At the external urethral orifice","In the membranous urethra","None of the above","At the bladder neck (smooth muscle, involuntary)","In the penile urethra"]'::jsonb, 'At the bladder neck (smooth muscle, involuntary)', 'The internal urethral sphincter is circular smooth muscle at the bladder neck under involuntary autonomic control. Sympathetic innervation keeps it closed during bladder filling.'),
('39e38f0b-f72b-5ddb-a482-f93fafd7cb8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The ureters enter the bladder?', '["From the lateral wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Horizontally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Directly from above mediated by specific receptor activation","Obliquely through the posterolateral wall (trigone)","From the anterior wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely through the posterolateral wall (trigone)', 'The ureters pierce the posterolateral bladder wall obliquely, traversing the detrusor muscle before opening at the ureteric orifices. This oblique passage creates a valvular mechanism that prevents vesicoureteral reflux during micturition.'),
('dbb03fbd-599a-5d14-bbee-6122b64eb11a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The pelvic course of the ureter in males is?', '["Runs directly to the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Crosses the pelvic brim, runs along lateral pelvic wall, crosses above the vas deferens","Crosses below the vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Runs between the rectum and bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses the pelvic brim, runs along lateral pelvic wall, crosses above the vas deferens', 'In males, the ureter crosses the pelvic brim, runs along the lateral pelvic wall, and then crosses above the vas deferens before entering the bladder. This relation is an important landmark during pelvic surgery.'),
('fe6d6230-1fc7-530c-9802-531313a3a86a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The pelvic course of the ureter in females is?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Crosses the pelvic brim, runs in the base of the broad ligament, crosses under the uterine artery (water under the bridge)","Runs directly to the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Crosses above the uterine artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Runs in front of the ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses the pelvic brim, runs in the base of the broad ligament, crosses under the uterine artery (water under the bridge)', 'In females the ureter runs in the base of the broad ligament and passes below the uterine artery, remembered as ''water under the bridge.'' This relation is critical during hysterectomy, when the uterine artery is ligated without damaging the ureter.'),
('50b00f61-7ec0-500d-b6f3-77e16a89e140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The ureter is constricted at which three points?', '["All three are equally wide resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelviureteric junction, pelvic brim (crossing iliac vessels), vesicoureteric junction","At the kidney, bladder, and urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the aorta, IVC, and pelvic inlet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelviureteric junction, pelvic brim (crossing iliac vessels), vesicoureteric junction', 'The ureter has three narrow points where renal calculi commonly lodge: the pelviureteric junction, the pelvic brim where it crosses the iliac vessels, and the vesicoureteric junction where it enters the bladder.'),
('2148a5f9-6c24-5a94-989c-ad2ee77b7405'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is related anteriorly to?', '["Pubic symphysis and retropubic space (space of Retzius)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis and retropubic space (space of Retzius)', 'Anteriorly the bladder is separated from the pubic symphysis by the retropubic space (space of Retzius), a potential space used in suprapubic surgery. The sacrum and rectum lie posteriorly, not anteriorly.'),
('cb36dc23-8ebc-588f-b933-1144594e00f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is related posteriorly in males to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","All of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prostate only mediated by specific receptor activation","Urethra only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum, seminal vesicles, and vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum, seminal vesicles, and vas deferens', 'In males the bladder base is related posteriorly to the rectum, seminal vesicles, and the ampullae of the vas deferens. These structures lie above the prostate and can be assessed on digital rectal examination.'),
('c40e31d2-925a-5185-9598-0478c2db9b86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is related posteriorly in females to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rarely mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectum only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior wall of vagina and cervix"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall of vagina and cervix', 'In females the bladder base rests against the anterior wall of the vagina and the cervix of the uterus. This explains why anterior vaginal wall lesions can distort bladder emptying.'),
('779df35c-44a0-58f9-84da-6da7aa83b53b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is related superiorly to?', '["Spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peritoneum and coils of ileum (and uterus in females)"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneum and coils of ileum (and uterus in females)', 'Superiorly the bladder is covered by peritoneum and related to coils of ileum, with the uterus lying above it in females. As the bladder distends, it pushes peritoneum upward, allowing safe suprapubic puncture.'),
('2a5b4e32-52ee-5269-bf27-b44065d65e48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is related inferiorly in males to?', '["None of the above","Prostate gland","Rectum","Seminal vesicles","Urethra"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate gland', 'In males the bladder neck and base rest on the prostate gland, which surrounds the prostatic urethra. Prostatic enlargement can compress the internal urethral orifice and obstruct bladder emptying.'),
('006d86fd-b6b4-5ea0-9098-55c7c04ec82b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder neck (internal urethral orifice) is the?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior part of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lowest part of the bladder, continuous with the urethra","Highest part of the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior part of bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lowest part of the bladder, continuous with the urethra', 'The bladder neck is the lowest and most fixed part of the bladder, forming the internal urethral orifice that is continuous with the urethra. It is surrounded by the internal urethral sphincter of smooth muscle.'),
('42d7d944-caab-53ad-95e9-ae48585383ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The internal urethral sphincter is formed by?', '["Longitudinal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Circular smooth muscle at the bladder neck (involuntary)","Mucosal folds mediated by specific receptor activation","Skeletal muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circular smooth muscle at the bladder neck (involuntary)', 'The internal urethral sphincter consists of circular smooth muscle at the bladder neck and is controlled involuntarily by the autonomic nervous system. It is functionally weaker in females than in males.'),
('5b9bda4b-340c-5f70-a668-6c7b4d38a70e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The external urethral sphincter is formed by?', '["Longitudinal muscle in the membranous urethra","None of the above","Circular muscle in the penile urethra","Smooth muscle at the bladder neck","Skeletal (voluntary) muscle in the deep perineal pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal (voluntary) muscle in the deep perineal pouch', 'The external urethral sphincter is skeletal muscle located in the deep perineal pouch and supplied by the pudendal nerve (S2–S4). It is the principal structure under voluntary control during micturition.'),
('989068a5-a261-59d8-8a98-fddc0ac46fcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The male urethra is longest in which part?', '["Membranous urethra (2 cm)","None of the above","Spongy (penile) urethra (15 cm)","Prostatic urethra (3 cm)","Preprostatic urethra (1 cm)"]'::jsonb, 'Spongy (penile) urethra (15 cm)', 'The spongy (penile) urethra is the longest part of the male urethra, measuring about 15 cm as it traverses the corpus spongiosum. The total male urethra is roughly 20 cm long.'),
('1d8d1abd-894b-58c7-a1a3-8c3e43e24ef4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostatic urethra contains which features?', '["Only the verumontanum resulting in decreased functional activity","All of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urethral crest, seminal colliculus (verumontanum), prostatic sinuses, openings of ejaculatory ducts and prostatic ducts","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the external sphincter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urethral crest, seminal colliculus (verumontanum), prostatic sinuses, openings of ejaculatory ducts and prostatic ducts', 'The prostatic urethra is the widest part of the male urethra. Its posterior wall bears the urethral crest and the seminal colliculus, into which the ejaculatory ducts open, with prostatic ducts draining into the prostatic sinuses.'),
('74bf1919-b34b-53d0-9c3c-8e9def66f8c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The membranous urethra is surrounded by?', '["Prostate resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corpus spongiosum mediated by specific receptor activation","Bulbospongiosus muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External urethral sphincter (sphincter urethrae)"]'::jsonb, 'External urethral sphincter (sphincter urethrae)', 'The membranous urethra passes through the deep perineal pouch and is encircled by the external urethral sphincter (sphincter urethrae). It is the shortest and least dilatable segment of the male urethra.'),
('6bd30620-16d5-597c-9309-bd1e91cb5d0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The spongy (penile) urethra is surrounded by?', '["Corpus cavernosum resulting in decreased functional activity","Prostate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corpus spongiosum (bulb and body of penis)","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bulbospongiosus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corpus spongiosum (bulb and body of penis)', 'The spongy (penile) urethra is surrounded by the corpus spongiosum throughout its course in the bulb and body of the penis. It is dilated at both ends, forming the intrabulbar fossa and the navicular fossa.'),
('a4916e00-4565-5e8c-8da5-94fa3d4d269f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Rupture of the membranous urethra (most common in pelvic fractures) causes?', '["Extravasation of urine into the deep perineal pouch (may spread to perineum and thigh)","extravasation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extravasation into the abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","Extravasation into the scrotum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extravasation of urine into the deep perineal pouch (may spread to perineum and thigh)', 'Rupture of the membranous urethra, typically from pelvic fractures, leaks urine into the deep perineal pouch. Spread is limited superiorly by the perineal membrane but may extend into the perineum and around the thigh.'),
('5d4d4d55-e4ca-56c8-8528-0f0a97d491ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Rupture of the bulbar (spongy) urethra (straddle injury) causes?', '["extravasation resulting in decreased functional activity","Extravasation into the deep perineal pouch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extravasation of urine into the superficial perineal pouch (deep to Colles fascia) - spreads to scrotum, anterior abdominal wall","Extravasation into the pelvis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extravasation of urine into the superficial perineal pouch (deep to Colles fascia) - spreads to scrotum, anterior abdominal wall', 'A straddle injury ruptures the bulbar urethra, releasing urine into the superficial perineal pouch deep to Colles fascia. Urine then spreads into the scrotum and penis and upward along the anterior abdominal wall.'),
('6d0a370d-76e9-5b5c-9c03-fc359b9f4c36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The female urethra opens into?', '["The rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","The vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The vestibule (between the labia minora, anterior to the vaginal opening)","The perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The clitoris leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vestibule (between the labia minora, anterior to the vaginal opening)', 'The female urethra opens at the external urethral orifice into the vestibule, between the labia minora and anterior to the vaginal orifice, about 2.5 cm behind the clitoris. The vaginal opening is separate and posterior.'),
('e86a774a-d86b-5ba9-b7d7-cac2c3d8f8e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The paraurethral glands (Skene glands) open into?', '["The vestibule resulting in decreased functional activity","The bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The female urethra (near the external orifice)","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vagina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The female urethra (near the external orifice)', 'The paraurethral (Skene) glands, the female homologue of the prostate, drain into the distal female urethra near the external orifice. A blocked or infected duct may present as a painful swelling in the anterior vagina.'),
('86924c9e-88c2-5192-8734-b4ab63b90338'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The ureterovesical junction (VUJ) is the?', '["All of the above","Junction of ureter and renal pelvis","None of the above","Point where the ureter enters the bladder wall","Junction of urethra and bladder"]'::jsonb, 'Point where the ureter enters the bladder wall', 'The vesicoureteric (ureterovesical) junction is where the ureter obliquely pierces the bladder wall. Its oblique intramural course forms a flap-like valve that prevents reflux of urine during bladder contraction.'),
('cef5b251-c568-546f-805d-f89b91d3b68e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Regarding the urinary bladder and urethra:', '["All of the above","The male urethra has four parts","The external urethral sphincter is in the deep perineal pouch","The ureters enter the bladder obliquely","The trigone is a smooth area between the ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Each listed statement is true: the male urethra has four parts, the external urethral sphincter lies in the deep perineal pouch, the ureters enter the bladder obliquely, and the trigone lies between the ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice.'),
('db96cfdc-805a-5f8f-809b-5ba1f64bb510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Where is the urinary bladder located?', '["Posterior pelvic cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","In abdominal cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In perineum mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior pelvic cavity behind pubic symphysis","Above pelvic brim leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior pelvic cavity behind pubic symphysis', 'The bladder lies in the anterior part of the lesser pelvis immediately behind the pubic symphysis. It rises above the pelvic brim into the lower abdomen only when distended.'),
('79b4d31d-fd00-5d70-97c1-b665c2eaeda9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'What are the parts of the male urethra?', '["Preprostatic and prostatic","Preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, spongy","None","Only prostatic and membranous","Prostatic, membranous, cavernous"]'::jsonb, 'Preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, spongy', 'The male urethra is divided into four parts: preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, and spongy. ''Cavernous'' is an outdated synonym; the accepted spongy (penile) division is used.'),
('1aee82fa-8c91-50ec-bbdf-f1bd028ae147'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'How long is the female urethra?', '["Same as male resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior to vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 cm, entirely in pelvic cavity"]'::jsonb, '4 cm, entirely in pelvic cavity', 'The female urethra is about 4 cm long and lies entirely within the pelvic cavity, embedded in the anterior vaginal wall. The male urethra is much longer, roughly 20 cm.'),
('b60c1940-0f07-5674-a1a2-431fc84de10f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Where is the external urethral sphincter?', '["In the deep perineal pouch","At external orifice","At the bladder neck","None","In prostatic urethra"]'::jsonb, 'In the deep perineal pouch', 'The external urethral sphincter is skeletal muscle located in the deep perineal pouch around the membranous urethra in males and the mid-urethra in females. It provides voluntary control of micturition.'),
('c6d16f68-15bf-5778-ad01-9e500ec9e20f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urinary bladder is:', '["A solid organ resulting in decreased functional activity","A hollow, distensible, muscular organ that stores urine","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A tubular structure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An endocrine organ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A hollow, distensible, muscular organ that stores urine', 'The bladder is a hollow, distensible, muscular reservoir lined by transitional epithelium that stores urine until micturition. It is neither solid, tubular, nor endocrine.'),
('65d34e2c-ddc5-527a-927a-385f4035cbe2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The shape of the bladder when empty is:', '["Cylindrical resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oval mediated by specific receptor activation","A tetrahedron (four-sided pyramid)","Spherical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A tetrahedron (four-sided pyramid)', 'When empty, the bladder is a four-sided pyramid (tetrahedron) with the apex directed toward the pubis and the base facing posteriorly. It becomes roughly ovoid or spherical as it fills.'),
('47113219-966a-5ead-9273-def86740f22e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder has:', '["An apex (anterior/superior), a base (posterior/inferior), a superior surface, two inferolateral surfaces, and a neck","Only a superior surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a neck mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only an apex and a base without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An apex (anterior/superior), a base (posterior/inferior), a superior surface, two inferolateral surfaces, and a neck', 'The bladder is described as having an apex, a base, a superior surface, two inferolateral surfaces, and a neck. Each part has characteristic relations used to localize bladder disease.'),
('b0e1229d-6c53-51b4-9959-afc2a8293b8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The apex of the bladder is:', '["The posterior point resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior point (attached to the median umbilical ligament)","The most inferior point mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The most superior point leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior point (attached to the median umbilical ligament)', 'The apex is the most anterior point of the bladder, lying behind the pubic symphysis and connected to the umbilicus by the median umbilical ligament, a remnant of the urachus.'),
('e1933890-e5d1-542b-b614-c6dc5897eb60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urachus is a fetal structure that connects:', '["The bladder to the ureter","The kidney to the bladder","None","The bladder to the urethra","The bladder to the umbilicus"]'::jsonb, 'The bladder to the umbilicus', 'The urachus is a fetal canal connecting the bladder apex to the umbilicus. After birth it becomes the median umbilical ligament; failure of obliteration produces a patent urachus with umbilical urinary leakage.'),
('0c51885e-677d-53f2-bb3b-d8eaef0a4008'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The base of the bladder lies:', '["Anteriorly resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferiorly mediated by specific receptor activation","Superiorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posteriorly (against the rectum in males, against the vagina/cervix in females)"]'::jsonb, 'Posteriorly (against the rectum in males, against the vagina/cervix in females)', 'The base (fundus) of the bladder faces posteriorly, against the rectum in males and against the vagina and cervix in females. It bears the trigone and the ureteric orifices.'),
('21fb98e6-a969-59fd-b240-a342875e38e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The neck of the bladder is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The anterior part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superior part mediated by specific receptor activation","The most inferior part (the opening into the urethra, surrounded by the internal urethral sphincter)","The posterior part leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most inferior part (the opening into the urethra, surrounded by the internal urethral sphincter)', 'The bladder neck is the most inferior part and forms the internal urethral orifice continuous with the urethra. It is surrounded by the involuntary internal urethral sphincter and is the most fixed part of the bladder.'),
('df3c178c-24fc-5e5d-9d5a-c8407b31dbf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The internal urethral sphincter (smooth muscle) is located at:', '["The external urethral orifice resulting in decreased functional activity","The bladder neck (involuntary, prevents leakage of urine)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The mid-urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ureterovesical junction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The bladder neck (involuntary, prevents leakage of urine)', 'The internal urethral sphincter is smooth muscle at the bladder neck that prevents leakage of urine by involuntary autonomic control. It contracts during filling and relaxes at the onset of micturition.'),
('4a6d4623-c798-5121-a376-5fd7474a935c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The trigone of the bladder is:', '["The neck of the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","A triangular area on the base of the bladder (between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice)","The dome of the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A triangular area on the base of the bladder (between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice)', 'The trigone is a smooth triangular area on the bladder base bounded by the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice. Its mucosa is firmly attached to the underlying muscle, so it never forms rugae.'),
('5fb3e254-801c-55c3-b599-7f98134a505a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The trigone is smooth and does not have:', '["Rugae (it is always smooth, even when the bladder is empty)","Mucosa accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Stretch receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smooth muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rugae (it is always smooth, even when the bladder is empty)', 'Because the trigonal mucosa is firmly fixed to the underlying detrusor, it remains smooth without rugae even when the bladder is empty. The rest of the bladder mucosa forms folds (rugae) when empty.'),
('79c855f3-0708-529b-9d56-46ecbb25ab5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The trigone is of great clinical importance because:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","It is the site of ureteric orifices and is a common area for tumors and infections","None mediated by specific receptor activation","It is the site of the bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is the site of the bladder apex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is the site of ureteric orifices and is a common area for tumors and infections', 'The trigone receives the ureteric orifices and is a frequent site of bladder tumors and infections. Cystoscopy is used to inspect this region directly.'),
('cf162866-66bf-5353-90e6-d60427507107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The two ureteric orifices are located at:', '["None","The apex of the trigone","The base of the trigone","The center of the trigone","The lateral angles of the trigone"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral angles of the trigone', 'The two ureteric orifices open at the lateral angles of the trigone. The mucosal ridge joining them is the interureteric crest, a useful cystoscopic landmark.'),
('4bdf05ae-d9a5-5594-b92f-7d67c6ab0584'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The internal urethral orifice is located at:', '["The anterior/inferior angle of the trigone (the most inferior point)","The lateral angles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The apex of the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The posterior angle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The anterior/inferior angle of the trigone (the most inferior point)', 'The internal urethral orifice occupies the anterior (inferior) angle of the trigone at the bladder neck. It is the most dependent point of the bladder and is closed by the internal urethral sphincter.'),
('c4a9d250-de0b-503f-9157-807bae2ad5f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The interureteric crest is:', '["A ridge (fold of mucosa) between the two ureteric orifices","A ridge at the apex of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ridge between the ureteric and urethral orifices mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ridge (fold of mucosa) between the two ureteric orifices', 'The interureteric crest is a ridge of mucosa joining the two ureteric orifices across the upper border of the trigone. It forms the upper limit of the trigone and aids in identifying the ureteric openings cystoscopically.'),
('3af015ce-9fa4-597c-a97c-e4ebc33abfc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder wall consists of:', '["Only muscle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A mucosa (transitional epithelium), a submucosa, a detrusor muscle (three layers of smooth muscle), and an adventitia/serosa","Only serosa mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only mucosa without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A mucosa (transitional epithelium), a submucosa, a detrusor muscle (three layers of smooth muscle), and an adventitia/serosa', 'The bladder wall comprises a mucosa of transitional epithelium, a submucosa, a detrusor muscle of three interlacing smooth muscle layers, and an adventitia with a partial serosal covering. The detrusor contracts to expel urine.'),
('08bc1f33-d15b-59d9-b4fc-589c0708b487'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The detrusor muscle is the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Skeletal muscle of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle of the bladder wall (responsible for contraction during micturition)","Cardiac muscle of the bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle of the bladder wall (responsible for contraction during micturition)', 'The detrusor is the smooth muscle coat of the bladder wall whose coordinated contraction raises intravesical pressure and expels urine. It is innervated by parasympathetic fibers from S2–S4.'),
('31c2e92b-35b5-519a-a350-ed4bb98c2164'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is innervated by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only somatic mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic (T11-L2, hypogastric nerve), parasympathetic (S2-S4, pelvic splanchnic nerve), and somatic (pudendal nerve, S2-S4)","Only sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (T11-L2, hypogastric nerve), parasympathetic (S2-S4, pelvic splanchnic nerve), and somatic (pudendal nerve, S2-S4)', 'The bladder receives triple innervation: sympathetic fibers (T11-L2) run via the hypogastric nerves to promote filling, parasympathetic fibers (S2-S4) run via the pelvic splanchnic nerves to drive detrusor contraction for emptying, and somatic fibers of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) supply the external urethral sphincter. Listing only one pathway gives an incomplete picture of bladder control.'),
('9a5b18d9-bcf1-5805-a7bf-45ccfb5e4c5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Parasympathetic stimulation of the bladder causes:', '["Relaxation of the detrusor resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibition of micturition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contraction of the internal urethral sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contraction of the detrusor muscle (to empty the bladder)"]'::jsonb, 'Contraction of the detrusor muscle (to empty the bladder)', 'Pelvic splanchnic fibers (S2-S4) release acetylcholine and cause the detrusor to contract, producing micturition. Detrusor relaxation and internal sphincter contraction are sympathetic-mediated storage responses, not parasympathetic effects.'),
('a4146a2b-4e68-55bd-80cf-888a01ac4add'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Sympathetic stimulation of the bladder causes:', '["Relaxation of the sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contraction of the detrusor mediated by specific receptor activation","Relaxation of the detrusor muscle and contraction of the internal urethral sphincter (to fill/storage)","Emptying of the bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Relaxation of the detrusor muscle and contraction of the internal urethral sphincter (to fill/storage)', 'Sympathetic (hypogastric) activity relaxes the detrusor and contracts the internal urethral sphincter, favoring bladder filling and continence. Detrusor contraction and emptying reflect the opposing parasympathetic system.'),
('d7f4501d-461a-5535-ae43-9b9ccc1249d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The micturition reflex center is at:', '["The sacral spinal cord (S2-S4)","The brainstem","The thoracic spinal cord","The lumbar spinal cord","None"]'::jsonb, 'The sacral spinal cord (S2-S4)', 'The sacral micturition center at S2-S4 contains the parasympathetic outflow that initiates detrusor contraction. Pontine and brainstem centers modulate the reflex but are not the location of the sacral reflex center itself.'),
('d9893f6e-c299-5428-ac72-17a1c5aa8190'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The brain controls micturition through:', '["The thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","The pontine micturition center (coordinates voluntary control with the sacral reflex)","The basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pontine micturition center (coordinates voluntary control with the sacral reflex)', 'The pontine micturition center integrates voluntary cortical control with the sacral reflex arc, coordinating relaxation of sphincters and detrusor contraction during voiding. The thalamus, basal ganglia, and cerebellum are not the principal supraspinal micturition centers.'),
('7bb462fa-ea43-5940-a99d-994bd4d38d57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder capacity is about:', '["400-600 mL in adults (first urge at about 150-200 mL)","100 mL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","800 mL mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","200 mL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '400-600 mL in adults (first urge at about 150-200 mL)', 'A normal adult bladder holds roughly 400-600 mL, with the first urge to void occurring at about 150-200 mL. Capacities of 100-200 mL are far below normal and 800 mL exceeds normal resting capacity.'),
('43031dfc-2023-572d-b4b2-599037db9ef6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder can hold up to:', '["600 mL resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 800-1000 mL (if overdistended)","400 mL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","200 mL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 800-1000 mL (if overdistended)', 'When overdistended the bladder may accommodate roughly 800-1000 mL, a pathological stretch often seen with chronic retention. Values of 200-600 mL reflect normal rather than maximal capacity.'),
('17b5f556-c597-5b92-888b-6e2b33ea6539'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is supplied by:', '["The femoral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The renal artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The superior and inferior vesical arteries (from the internal iliac artery)","The common iliac artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The superior and inferior vesical arteries (from the internal iliac artery)', 'The superior vesical artery arises from the internal iliac artery and the inferior vesical artery supplies the fundus and base, together forming the vesical arterial supply. The femoral, renal, and common iliac arteries give no branches to the bladder.'),
('5aeb0242-f4b5-5971-bdf5-2bd41078fcbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In females, the inferior vesical artery is replaced by:', '["None","The uterine artery","The internal pudendal artery","The vaginal artery","The ovarian artery"]'::jsonb, 'The vaginal artery', 'In females the vaginal artery replaces the inferior vesical artery, with additional branches from the uterine artery reaching the bladder fundus. This sexual difference reflects the shared pelvic arterial pattern of the female reproductive organs.'),
('31b1b341-fe0d-50bd-aa42-92b1c65af4ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is drained by:', '["The IVC resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The common iliac vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The vesical veins (which drain into the internal iliac veins)","The portal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The vesical veins (which drain into the internal iliac veins)', 'The vesical venous plexus on the bladder base drains through vesical veins into the internal iliac veins. The IVC and common iliac vein participate in venous drainage at a more proximal level, and the portal vein drains the gut, not the bladder.'),
('4e96bc94-97ce-59fc-8ec8-03a583167b38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is lymphatically drained to:', '["The external iliac nodes (and the internal iliac nodes)","The para-aortic nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The axillary nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inguinal nodes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The external iliac nodes (and the internal iliac nodes)', 'Lymphatics from the bladder drain to the external and internal iliac lymph nodes, the regional nodes of the pelvis. Para-aortic nodes receive renal lymph, while inguinal and axillary nodes serve the lower limb and upper limb respectively.'),
('3a3b8c76-1f67-5a44-b09e-431a589e00c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is separated from the pubic bone by:', '["The obturator foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","The inguinal canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The femoral triangle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The retropubic space (space of Retzius) - a potential space filled with fat"]'::jsonb, 'The retropubic space (space of Retzius) - a potential space filled with fat', 'The retropubic space (space of Retzius) lies between the pubic symphysis and the bladder, filled with fat and loose connective tissue, and is the plane entered during retropubic surgery. It is distinct from the obturator foramen, inguinal canal, and femoral triangle.'),
('a4c49689-8513-5a40-860c-8c3a8e92735d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The retropubic space (space of Retzius) is:', '["A space in the perineum resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A potential space between the pubic symphysis and the urinary bladder (filled with fat and loose connective tissue)","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A space in the inguinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A potential space between the pubic symphysis and the urinary bladder (filled with fat and loose connective tissue)', 'The retropubic space is a midline extraperitoneal fat-filled potential space anterior to the bladder, allowing bladder distension and providing a surgical plane. It does not lie within the perineum or inguinal canal.'),
('e443c2d8-748e-5edc-b9c8-81a27ca04e63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is anteriorly related to:', '["The rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","The uterus/vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pubic symphysis and the retropubic space","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sacrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The pubic symphysis and the retropubic space', 'Anteriorly the bladder lies behind the pubic symphysis, separated from it by the fat-filled retropubic space. The rectum and uterus/vagina are posterior relations, and the sacrum lies further posteriorly.'),
('70d0aa5c-6232-5120-a876-e6757222f158'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is posteriorly related to:', '["The rectum (in males) or the vagina and cervix (in females)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","The uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pubic symphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rectum (in males) or the vagina and cervix (in females)', 'Posteriorly the empty bladder rests against the rectum in males and against the anterior vaginal wall and cervix in females. The sacrum lies behind the rectum, and the pubic symphysis is anterior to the bladder.'),
('1d31f603-7476-50ce-944d-c0a3980ecbd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is superiorly related to:', '["The uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","The rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The peritoneum (the superior surface is covered by peritoneum)","The pubic symphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneum (the superior surface is covered by peritoneum)', 'The dome of the bladder is covered by peritoneum, which reflects onto the anterior abdominal wall and forms the rectovesical pouch in males and the vesicouterine pouch in females. The uterus and rectum lie posterior, not superior, to the bladder.'),
('36bc484f-dc8c-54ec-8033-37d13a26b808'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is inferolaterally related to:', '["The obturator internus muscle and the levator ani muscle","The rectus abdominis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The piriformis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The gluteus maximus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The obturator internus muscle and the levator ani muscle', 'Inferolaterally the bladder is supported by the obturator internus muscle on the pelvic side wall and the levator ani of the pelvic floor. The rectus abdominis is anterior in the abdominal wall, and piriformis and gluteus maximus are related to the sacrum and gluteal region.'),
('6719ae4a-648d-5f9b-bed0-ef011865d3a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is at the highest risk of injury during:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Pelvic trauma (e.g., fractures of the pubic rami)","Chest trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Abdominal trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic trauma (e.g., fractures of the pubic rami)', 'Pelvic fractures, especially of the pubic rami, place the bladder at high risk of injury, particularly when it is distended. The bladder is not directly at risk from chest trauma, head injury, or isolated abdominal trauma.'),
('fcdd0f5e-4c12-5f10-a64b-cad1ff8930fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A ruptured bladder can cause:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood in the urine mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinary extravasation into the retropubic space or the peritoneal cavity","Inability to urinate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urinary extravasation into the retropubic space or the peritoneal cavity', 'The hallmark consequence of bladder rupture is extravasation of urine into the retropubic space or the peritoneal cavity depending on the site of the tear. Hematuria and voiding difficulty may accompany rupture, but extravasation is the central mechanical consequence.'),
('e2601031-ad63-5b7b-b712-c8718788fcf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethra in males is about:', '["10 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","18-20 cm long (divided into prostatic, membranous, and penile (spongy) parts)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","15 cm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","5 cm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '18-20 cm long (divided into prostatic, membranous, and penile (spongy) parts)', 'The male urethra measures about 18-20 cm and is divided into prostatic, membranous, and penile (spongy) parts. The shorter lengths listed do not reflect the true male urethral length.'),
('3432c8cf-8e86-59c4-8ecb-127f5794c5be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethra in females is about:', '["8 cm resulting in decreased functional activity","5 cm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","10 cm mediated by specific receptor activation","3-4 cm long (much shorter than in males)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3-4 cm long (much shorter than in males)', 'The female urethra is short (3-4 cm), running from the bladder neck to the external urethral orifice in the vestibule, which predisposes women to ascending urinary infection. The longer lengths listed resemble the male urethra.'),
('bf007f43-e1e4-5fc6-b751-b946010493ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The female urethra is entirely:', '["Within the pelvis (it runs from the bladder neck to the external urethral orifice in the vestibule)","Within the abdomen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Within the perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Within the thorax leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Within the pelvis (it runs from the bladder neck to the external urethral orifice in the vestibule)', 'In the female the short urethra lies entirely within the pelvis, passing from the bladder neck to the external urethral orifice in the vestibule just behind the clitoris. It does not extend into the abdomen, thorax, or perineal region proper.'),
('1ca9ee94-8d9a-5048-9a0d-65930cf4e117'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostatic urethra is characterized by:', '["The fossa navicularis resulting in decreased functional activity","The verumontanum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bulbourethral glands mediated by specific receptor activation","The urethral crest, the prostatic sinus, and the opening of the ejaculatory ducts","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urethral crest, the prostatic sinus, and the opening of the ejaculatory ducts', 'The prostatic urethra bears the urethral crest with the verumontanum on its summit, and beside it the prostatic sinuses receive the prostatic ducts; the ejaculatory ducts open on or beside the verumontanum. The fossa navicularis belongs to the penile urethra and the bulbourethral glands lie outside the prostate.'),
('01d56649-7402-50c8-a3ce-0c9d65701580'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The membranous urethra is the:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Most distensible part accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Longest part without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shortest and least distensible part of the male urethra"]'::jsonb, 'Shortest and least distensible part of the male urethra', 'The membranous urethra is the shortest and least distensible segment, piercing the urogenital diaphragm and surrounded by the external urethral sphincter. It is neither the longest nor the most distensible part of the male urethra.'),
('9bc7901e-71ec-5daa-b5e8-0ec73ab320f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The penile urethra has a dilation at the end called:', '["None","The verumontanum","The fossa navicularis","The prostatic sinus","The bulb of the penis"]'::jsonb, 'The fossa navicularis', 'The terminal dilation of the penile (spongy) urethra just proximal to the external orifice is the fossa navicularis. The verumontanum and prostatic sinus are features of the prostatic urethra, and the bulb of the penis is the dilated proximal part of the corpus spongiosum.'),
('45a8ad36-c849-5faa-9f5d-25fd77904ddc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The external urethral sphincter is also known as:', '["The internal urethral sphincter resulting in decreased functional activity","The levator ani accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The urogenital sphincter (or the external sphincter of the urethra)","The detrusor muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital sphincter (or the external sphincter of the urethra)', 'The voluntary external urethral sphincter around the membranous urethra is termed the urogenital sphincter, located in the deep perineal pouch. The internal sphincter is smooth muscle at the bladder neck, the levator ani is a pelvic floor muscle, and the detrusor is the bladder wall muscle.'),
('a6215c75-78de-5a38-b2fd-c7cfc11620ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The external urethral sphincter is innervated by:', '["None","The pelvic splanchnic nerves","The obturator nerve","The sympathetic chain","The pudendal nerve (S2-S4)"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4)', 'Somatic fibers of the pudendal nerve (S2-S4) innervate the external urethral sphincter, providing voluntary control. The pelvic splanchnic nerves carry parasympathetic fibers to the detrusor, while the sympathetic chain and obturator nerve do not supply this sphincter.'),
('b045a434-ece6-57fe-a681-7f9c4ba0924c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bulbourethral glands (Cowper glands) are:', '["Two glands in the prostate resulting in decreased functional activity","Two small glands in the urogenital diaphragm that secrete a pre-ejaculatory fluid","Two glands in the testes mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two small glands in the urogenital diaphragm that secrete a pre-ejaculatory fluid', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands are two pea-sized glands in the deep perineal pouch of the urogenital diaphragm that secrete an alkaline lubricating fluid before ejaculation. They are not located in the prostate or testes.'),
('2f7d0d17-55f6-5d43-af58-0cda07484ef8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethra is lined by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional epithelium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple columnar epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Stratified columnar epithelium (prostatic and membranous parts) and stratified squamous epithelium (penile/terminal part)","Pseudostratified epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stratified columnar epithelium (prostatic and membranous parts) and stratified squamous epithelium (penile/terminal part)', 'The prostatic and membranous urethra are lined by stratified columnar (and transitional) epithelium, whereas the distal penile urethra and fossa navicularis bear stratified squamous epithelium. Transitional epithelium mainly lines the bladder and proximal urethra, not the entire urethra.'),
('c8db7c29-6b33-56eb-9877-138f6f82b0f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common site for urethral rupture in males is:', '["The membranous urethra (from pelvic trauma)","The fossa navicularis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The prostatic urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","The penile urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The membranous urethra (from pelvic trauma)', 'The membranous urethra is the most common site of rupture in males, typically from pelvic fracture because it is fixed within the urogenital diaphragm. The penile and prostatic parts are less vulnerable, and the fossa navicularis is not a typical rupture site.'),
('63e8ae91-eff7-58e2-84a6-f6497061ba9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urethral rupture can cause:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Extravasation of urine into the perineum (depending on which part of the urethra is ruptured)","Blood in the urine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extravasation of urine into the perineum (depending on which part of the urethra is ruptured)', 'A ruptured urethra permits urine to extravasate into perineal fascial compartments, with the exact pathway determined by the site of rupture. Infection and hematuria can complicate the injury, but extravasation is the central mechanical consequence of rupture.'),
('6b837892-7076-5055-8008-02b7827bc664'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urine extravasation from the penile urethra:', '["Spreads into the pelvis resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spreads into the abdominal wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Spreads under Colles fascia (the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia)","Spreads into the thigh leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spreads under Colles fascia (the membranous layer of the superficial perineal fascia)', 'Rupture of the penile (spongy) urethra lets urine enter the superficial perineal pouch and track under Colles fascia into the scrotum, penis, and up over the anterior abdominal wall. Colles fascia attaches to the fascia lata and pubic rami, so urine does not reach the thigh or pelvis.'),
('da709715-0e9d-5bb9-90f0-1dfbe5eab1c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urine extravasation from the membranous urethra:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Spreads under Colles fascia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spreads into the thigh mediated by specific receptor activation","Spreads into the pelvis (above the pelvic diaphragm)","Spreads into the abdominal wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spreads into the pelvis (above the pelvic diaphragm)', 'A membranous urethral rupture leaks urine upward around the bladder and prostate within the extraperitoneal pelvic space, since the tear lies above the urogenital diaphragm. It does not track under Colles fascia, into the thigh, or along the abdominal wall.'),
('422afede-ca0d-5594-920f-fc5e16c82f60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The female urethra is related anteriorly to:', '["The sacrum","The uterus","None","The pubic symphysis","The rectum"]'::jsonb, 'The pubic symphysis', 'Anteriorly the female urethra lies behind the pubic symphysis, separated from it by a small fat-filled space. The vagina is posterior to the urethra, the bladder lies above it, and the sacrum and rectum are not anterior relations.'),
('9d96b7b1-7db2-52e3-84d0-5afbb1221584'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The female urethra is related posteriorly to:', '["The rectum","None","The uterus","The vagina","The bladder"]'::jsonb, 'The vagina', 'Posteriorly the female urethra is fused to the anterior vaginal wall, along which it descends to the vestibule. The rectum lies behind the vagina rather than directly behind the urethra, and the bladder is superior to the urethra.'),
('5534d976-68bf-596b-a518-439731a55e42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The paraurethral glands (Skene glands) are:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Small glands in the female urethra (homologous to the male prostate)","The same as the Bartholin glands mediated by specific receptor activation","The same as the Cowper glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small glands in the female urethra (homologous to the male prostate)', 'The paraurethral (Skene) glands open beside the external urethral orifice and are homologous to the male prostate. They are distinct from the Bartholin (greater vestibular) glands and the Cowper (bulbourethral) glands.'),
('44bcc09e-878d-5cd8-902c-53ebb193a653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urinary system includes:', '["Only the kidneys resulting in decreased functional activity","The kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the urethra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra', 'The urinary system is composed of the kidneys, ureters, urinary bladder, and urethra, which together form the urinary tract. Options naming only a single organ fail to include the complete urinary tract.'),
('922dbca0-7304-5085-8517-dea78753d23d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethra in both sexes has:', '["Only an external urethral orifice resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An internal urethral orifice (at the bladder neck) and an external urethral orifice (at the end of the urethra)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only an internal urethral orifice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An internal urethral orifice (at the bladder neck) and an external urethral orifice (at the end of the urethra)', 'Each urethra begins at the internal urethral orifice at the bladder neck and ends at the external urethral orifice, in both males and females. Listing only one orifice gives an incomplete picture of urethral anatomy.'),
('9d66640f-db4f-5fa2-b9cd-5774cfb914df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Male urethral strictures most commonly occur in:', '["The prostatic urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","The fossa navicularis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The membranous urethra (from trauma) or the penile urethra (from infection)"]'::jsonb, 'The membranous urethra (from trauma) or the penile urethra (from infection)', 'Strictures most commonly affect the membranous urethra following trauma and the penile (spongy) urethra after infection or instrumentation. The prostatic urethra and bladder neck are seldom sites of stricture formation.'),
('8e4fe365-a54b-5392-8a72-f371270e0368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A female urethral caruncle is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A cyst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A malignant tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","A small, benign, painful lesion at the external urethral orifice (most common in postmenopausal women)","An infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A small, benign, painful lesion at the external urethral orifice (most common in postmenopausal women)', 'A urethral caruncle is a small benign, vascular polypoid lesion of the distal urethra, most common in postmenopausal women, and it may cause pain and bleeding. It is neither a cyst, a malignancy, nor an infection.'),
('1b888e21-41f3-5bb9-8535-33d6b98bc705'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The ureterovesical junction is:', '["The point where the ureter joins the urethra","The point where the ureter joins the kidney","The point where the urethra joins the bladder","The point where the ureter enters the bladder wall (at the trigone)","None"]'::jsonb, 'The point where the ureter enters the bladder wall (at the trigone)', 'The ureterovesical junction is the oblique passage of the ureter through the bladder wall at the trigone, whose valve-like action prevents vesicoureteral reflux. It is not the junction of the ureter with the urethra or kidney.'),
('e2456364-6f44-5a50-943e-5a46c987ef54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The trigone of the bladder is developed from:', '["The metanephros resulting in decreased functional activity","The mesonephric duct (Wolffian duct)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The urogenital sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The pronephros leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mesonephric duct (Wolffian duct)', 'The bladder trigone is derived from the absorbed lower ends of the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts. The remainder of the bladder forms from the urogenital sinus, while the metanephros gives rise to the kidney.'),
('9457206f-16ff-5106-a95c-c9d1a14bfa3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The rest of the bladder is developed from:', '["The mesonephric duct","The pronephros","None","The urogenital sinus (endoderm)","The metanephros"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital sinus (endoderm)', 'The vesicourethral portion of the urogenital sinus (endoderm) forms most of the bladder, whereas the trigone comes from the mesonephric ducts. The metanephros develops into the kidney and the pronephros is the transient earliest renal primordium.'),
('b38fd07b-f68d-534d-961e-4c2950945849'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urachus is a remnant of:', '["The mesonephric duct resulting in decreased functional activity","The allantois (a fetal structure that connects the bladder to the umbilicus)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The urogenital sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The paramesonephric duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The allantois (a fetal structure that connects the bladder to the umbilicus)', 'The urachus is the fibrous remnant of the allantois extending from the fetal bladder apex to the umbilicus, becoming the median umbilical ligament in the adult. The mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts form other urogenital structures, not the urachus.'),
('76e0ab77-48e7-5138-a3aa-43f7122487b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A urachal cyst can occur if:', '["The urachus does not completely obliterate (a cyst forms in the midline between the bladder and the umbilicus)","The bladder does not develop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ureter does not develop mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urachus does not completely obliterate (a cyst forms in the midline between the bladder and the umbilicus)', 'Failure of the urachus to obliterate leaves a midline remnant between the bladder and umbilicus that may develop into a urachal cyst. It is not caused by failure of bladder or ureteral development.'),
('de3825e7-6769-53d9-bf0a-6761b1747873'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bladder exstrophy is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A congenital duplication of the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","A congenital anomaly where the bladder is exposed (open) on the anterior abdominal wall","A congenital absence of the bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital anomaly where the bladder is exposed (open) on the anterior abdominal wall', 'In bladder exstrophy, failure of the anterior abdominal wall and bladder to close exposes the bladder mucosa on the abdominal surface. It is neither duplication nor absence of the bladder, though it is typically associated with epispadias.'),
('bc0ee510-a675-5223-913d-fe7997483472'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bladder exstrophy is associated with:', '["Epispadias (the urethra opens on the dorsal surface of the penis)","Cryptorchidism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Inguinal hernia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypospadias leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epispadias (the urethra opens on the dorsal surface of the penis)', 'Bladder exstrophy is classically associated with epispadias, in which the urethra opens on the dorsal surface of the penis. Hypospadias involves a ventral urethral opening, while cryptorchidism and inguinal hernia are not the characteristic associated anomaly.'),
('f334b56c-287c-59fa-bdd8-1aa1bf3da06e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is most commonly injured by:', '["Pelvic fractures (especially fractures of the pubic rami)","Penetrating trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Strenuous exercise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blunt abdominal trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic fractures (especially fractures of the pubic rami)', 'Pelvic fractures, particularly of the pubic rami, are the leading cause of bladder injury, with the risk higher when the bladder is distended. Penetrating trauma is less common, and strenuous exercise does not injure the bladder.'),
('4143d2c7-b248-5fcc-bdcc-267a77bab27a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A retroperitoneal bladder rupture occurs:', '["When the bladder is empty resulting in decreased functional activity","When the bladder is not distended (extravasation of urine into the retropubic space)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","When the bladder is distended without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'When the bladder is not distended (extravasation of urine into the retropubic space)', 'A collapsed bladder remains within the pelvis protected by the bony ring, so rupture is extraperitoneal with urine leaking into the retropubic space. A distended bladder projects above the symphysis and ruptures intraperitoneally through the dome.'),
('9259741e-9bcb-5521-baa6-6cec18fe31d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'An intraperitoneal bladder rupture occurs:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","When the bladder is not distended accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","When the bladder is empty mediated by specific receptor activation","When the bladder is full of blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","When the bladder is distended (urine leaks into the peritoneal cavity)"]'::jsonb, 'When the bladder is distended (urine leaks into the peritoneal cavity)', 'A distended bladder rises above the pubic symphysis, so its peritoneal-covered dome is exposed and rupture tears it, leaking urine into the peritoneal cavity. When the bladder is empty or undistended, rupture is extraperitoneal and urine stays in the retropubic space.'),
('2e76eb52-6062-5d44-916c-f31c138be186'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bladder cancer is most commonly:', '["Squamous cell carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional cell carcinoma (TCC) of the urothelium","Sarcoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Adenocarcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional cell carcinoma (TCC) of the urothelium', 'Transitional cell carcinoma arises from the urothelium and accounts for about 90% of bladder tumors. Squamous cell carcinoma, sarcoma, and adenocarcinoma are all uncommon bladder cancer types.'),
('63faddf1-147d-5224-9e17-94ca0a53c754'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common site for bladder cancer is:', '["The ureteric orifices resulting in decreased functional activity","The trigone and the lateral walls of the bladder","The dome of the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","The bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The trigone and the lateral walls of the bladder', 'The trigone and lateral walls bear the longest contact with carcinogens in pooled urine, making them the most common site of bladder cancer. The dome, bladder neck, and ureteric orifices are far less frequently involved.'),
('25f5c539-f7e7-5af3-8dd0-000e894813b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common symptom of bladder cancer is:', '["Urinary incontinence resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Painless hematuria (blood in the urine)","Painful urination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urinary retention leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Painless hematuria (blood in the urine)', 'Painless gross or microscopic hematuria is the hallmark presenting symptom of bladder cancer. Painful urination, retention, and incontinence are not typical early presentations.'),
('86ddd92f-d0f0-5a34-9543-576ad476dfc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostate gland is located:', '["Behind the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Above the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Below the bladder (surrounding the prostatic urethra)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In front of the bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Below the bladder (surrounding the prostatic urethra)', 'The prostate lies in the lesser pelvis immediately below the bladder neck, encircling the prostatic urethra. Positions behind, above, or in front of the bladder do not describe its true location.'),
('6fe17cfc-0607-5f67-ae2e-37bebfce1c8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostate is normally:', '["None","Walnut-sized (about 3-4 cm in diameter)","The size of a golf ball","The size of a pea","The size of a large orange"]'::jsonb, 'Walnut-sized (about 3-4 cm in diameter)', 'The normal adult prostate approximates a walnut, roughly 3-4 cm in diameter. Pea, golf-ball, and orange comparisons do not reflect typical dimensions.'),
('cc8505c1-01ca-5451-b776-c9190f4758b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostatic capsule is:', '["A thin, fibrous capsule around the prostate","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cartilaginous capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","A thick, muscular capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A serosal capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A thin, fibrous capsule around the prostate', 'The prostate is enveloped by a thin fibrous capsule formed from condensed glandular stroma. It is neither cartilaginous, muscular, nor serosal.'),
('502731da-570e-5380-b470-0e3c8b49cb69'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostatic venous plexus (Santorini plexus) lies:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Around the seminal vesicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inside the prostate mediated by specific receptor activation","In the perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between the prostate and the pubic bone (in the retropubic space)"]'::jsonb, 'Between the prostate and the pubic bone (in the retropubic space)', 'The prostatic (Santorini) venous plexus occupies the retropubic space between the prostate and the pubic symphysis, receiving the deep dorsal vein of the penis. It does not surround the seminal vesicles, lie within the prostatic substance, or sit in the perineum.'),
('afb5be10-1070-580e-aa17-f3de640e164a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethral crest is:', '["A ridge in the membranous urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A ridge on the anterior wall mediated by specific receptor activation","A ridge in the penile urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A longitudinal ridge on the posterior wall of the prostatic urethra"]'::jsonb, 'A longitudinal ridge on the posterior wall of the prostatic urethra', 'The urethral crest is a median longitudinal fold on the posterior wall of the prostatic urethra that mounts the verumontanum. It is not found on the anterior wall or in the membranous and penile parts of the urethra.'),
('e19c8e02-75d4-539a-95a5-5fdbacb5a510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostatic sinus is:', '["A groove on each side of the urethral crest (the prostatic ducts open here)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The opening of the seminal vesicle mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The opening of the ejaculatory duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A groove on each side of the urethral crest (the prostatic ducts open here)', 'The prostatic sinuses are paired grooves flanking the urethral crest into which the prostatic ductules empty. The ejaculatory ducts and seminal vesicles open elsewhere, on the verumontanum and above it.'),
('7557d0e7-61e9-5bbf-ae9c-35feda2a0263'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The verumontanum (colliculus seminalis) is:', '["A midline elevation on the urethral crest (the ejaculatory ducts open here)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The opening of the prostatic ducts mediated by specific receptor activation","The opening of the seminal vesicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A midline elevation on the urethral crest (the ejaculatory ducts open here)', 'The verumontanum is a midline mound on the urethral crest where the ejaculatory ducts open into the prostatic urethra. Prostatic ducts open lateral to it in the prostatic sinuses.'),
('078b04f0-112b-5e9f-b5b7-7f1cd1e38ec9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethra in both sexes is lined by:', '["Only stratified squamous epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Transitional epithelium (proximally) and stratified columnar/squamous epithelium (distally)","Only transitional epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Only columnar epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transitional epithelium (proximally) and stratified columnar/squamous epithelium (distally)', 'The proximal urethra is lined by transitional (urothelial) epithelium, which changes distally to stratified columnar and then stratified squamous epithelium near the external meatus. A single uniform epithelial type would be inaccurate.'),
('744df42c-3b49-556c-a537-c5b783ce5c8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The external urethral sphincter in females is:', '["A ring of skeletal muscle that surrounds the urethra (at the level of the perineal membrane)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A ring of smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A ring of cartilage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A ring of skeletal muscle that surrounds the urethra (at the level of the perineal membrane)', 'The female external urethral sphincter is striated (skeletal) muscle encircling the urethra at the level of the perineal membrane, providing voluntary continence. It is not composed of smooth muscle or cartilage.'),
('f59d6b82-e488-5558-9f92-3e200ac95e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The paraurethral glands (Skene glands) in females drain into:', '["The upper urethra resulting in decreased functional activity","The lower urethra (near the external urethral orifice)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The vagina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lower urethra (near the external urethral orifice)', 'The paraurethral (Skene) glands open into the distal urethra near the external urethral orifice. They do not drain into the upper urethra, urinary bladder, or vagina.'),
('bd002c7b-8c11-5789-92a9-09e684ae0af1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is produced by:', '["The seminal vesicles","The prostatic epithelial cells","The testes","The bulbourethral glands","None"]'::jsonb, 'The prostatic epithelial cells', 'PSA is a serine protease secreted by prostatic epithelial cells into the seminal fluid. The seminal vesicles, testes, and bulbourethral glands do not produce it.'),
('b585bbbd-1152-5325-b3cd-b9874cdb5d66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The peripheral zone of the prostate is:', '["The smallest zone resulting in decreased functional activity","The zone of the prostatic utricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The zone of BPH mediated by specific receptor activation","The largest zone (about 70%) and is the most common site for prostate cancer","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The largest zone (about 70%) and is the most common site for prostate cancer', 'The peripheral zone comprises about 70% of the gland and is where most prostate cancers arise. BPH develops in the transitional zone, which is a different region.'),
('a2939ec4-e298-52a6-848a-38bcc87fa384'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The central zone of the prostate is:', '["The zone of BPH resulting in decreased functional activity","The smallest zone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","About 25% of the prostate (surrounds the ejaculatory ducts)","The zone of the utricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 25% of the prostate (surrounds the ejaculatory ducts)', 'The central zone makes up roughly 25% of the prostate and surrounds the ejaculatory ducts. The zone of BPH is the transitional zone, not the central zone.'),
('38bacb80-d51d-5174-af03-8a7ff55f6279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The transitional zone of the prostate is:', '["The largest zone resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The zone of cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","The zone of the utricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","About 5% of the prostate (the zone of BPH, surrounds the prostatic urethra)"]'::jsonb, 'About 5% of the prostate (the zone of BPH, surrounds the prostatic urethra)', 'The transitional zone forms only about 5% of the prostate, surrounds the prostatic urethra, and is the site where benign prostatic hyperplasia develops. The largest zone and the zone of cancer are the peripheral and other regions.'),
('9bdd70ff-2d7f-5705-901b-e1a84a44af94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'BPH can cause:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Painless hematuria mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinary incontinence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urinary hesitancy, frequency, urgency, and nocturia (from compression of the prostatic urethra)"]'::jsonb, 'Urinary hesitancy, frequency, urgency, and nocturia (from compression of the prostatic urethra)', 'Enlargement of the transitional zone compresses the prostatic urethra and obstructs urine flow, producing hesitancy, frequency, urgency, and nocturia. Painless hematuria and frank incontinence are not characteristic effects of BPH alone.'),
('a0daf543-608a-545d-b65c-2416a6ac2376'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The trigone of the bladder is thought to be derived from:', '["The cloaca resulting in decreased functional activity","The mesonephric ducts (Wolffian ducts)","The urogenital sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","The metanephros without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The mesonephric ducts (Wolffian ducts)', 'The bladder trigone is classically believed to derive from incorporation of the caudal ends of the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts. The remainder of the bladder arises from the urogenital sinus, not the cloaca or metanephros.'),
('2239bad0-b3a2-5436-95ea-039ae13df295'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The remainder of the bladder is derived from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The metanephros accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The urogenital sinus (endodermal)","The hindgut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mesonephric duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital sinus (endodermal)', 'The body of the bladder develops from the endodermal urogenital sinus. The metanephros forms the kidney, and the hindgut forms the distal bowel.'),
('28d94eb6-6efe-523c-865a-8500c4361a9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The allantois is a fetal structure that:', '["Connects the bladder to the rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Connects the bladder to the urethra mediated by specific receptor activation","Connects the bladder to the umbilicus (obliterates to become the urachus)","Connects the bladder to the kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Connects the bladder to the umbilicus (obliterates to become the urachus)', 'The allantois extends from the bladder apex to the umbilicus and obliterates in later fetal life to form the urachus (median umbilical ligament). It does not connect the bladder to the rectum, urethra, or kidney.'),
('8f74bcad-2ec8-542d-ab89-0dbe2d3ce824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urachus is the remnant of:', '["The umbilical vein","None","The vitelline duct","The umbilical artery","The allantois"]'::jsonb, 'The allantois', 'The urachus is the obliterated fibrous remnant of the allantois, persisting as the median umbilical ligament. The vitelline duct, umbilical vein, and umbilical artery give rise to other remnants.'),
('0e93ed04-66ee-522e-9492-4f00508b9a00'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A urachal fistula is:', '["An absent urachus resulting in decreased functional activity","A patent urachus (persistent communication between the bladder and the umbilicus)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A closed urachus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A patent urachus (persistent communication between the bladder and the umbilicus)', 'A urachal fistula results when the urachus remains patent, leaving a communication between the bladder and umbilicus through which urine may leak. An absent or completely closed urachus does not produce a fistula.'),
('50f9605a-fd3d-5a52-88b4-2e88a5797db3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urachal carcinoma is:', '["A transitional cell carcinoma resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A rare adenocarcinoma of the urachal remnant","Squamous cell carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sarcoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A rare adenocarcinoma of the urachal remnant', 'Urachal carcinoma is a rare adenocarcinoma arising from glandular remnants of the urachus, usually at the bladder dome. It is not the common urothelial transitional cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, or sarcoma.'),
('b2aa19c0-adc2-57cf-8566-85491add4695'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder can be examined using:', '["CT scan resulting in decreased functional activity","X-ray accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystoscopy (an endoscopic examination of the bladder interior)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ultrasound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystoscopy (an endoscopic examination of the bladder interior)', 'Cystoscopy provides direct endoscopic visualization of the bladder mucosa and is the definitive way to examine the bladder interior. CT, X-ray, and ultrasound offer indirect imaging rather than direct inspection.'),
('3e67bc17-202d-50eb-b216-7ef0283d67a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethra can be examined using:', '["Urethroscopy","Cystoscopy","None","Colonoscopy","Gastroscopy"]'::jsonb, 'Urethroscopy', 'Urethroscopy is the endoscopic examination of the urethra. Cystoscopy visualizes the bladder, while colonoscopy and gastroscopy examine the bowel.'),
('fc28b337-e41a-5e48-acdc-c31ea5fcaa2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urethral discharge can be caused by:', '["Prostatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pyelonephritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystitis mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urethritis (infection of the urethra, e.g., gonorrhea, chlamydia)"]'::jsonb, 'Urethritis (infection of the urethra, e.g., gonorrhea, chlamydia)', 'Urethritis, typically gonococcal or chlamydial, inflames the urethral mucosa and produces a urethral discharge. Prostatitis, cystitis, and pyelonephritis affect other sites and are not the direct cause of a urethral discharge.'),
('086b7ecf-3836-5578-95ff-691196b8cd7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common cause of urethritis in males is:', '["Streptococcus resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","E. coli mediated by specific receptor activation","Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis","Staphylococcus aureus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neisseria gonorrhoeae or Chlamydia trachomatis', 'In sexually active males, Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis are the most frequent causes of urethritis. E. coli, Streptococcus, and Staphylococcus aureus are much less common urethral pathogens.'),
('f0027883-86e3-5cd6-a0b1-59ecc62cdafa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urethritis in females is often:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Rare accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asymptomatic (but can cause dysuria, frequency, and discharge)","symptomatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","symptomatic (alternative wording) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asymptomatic (but can cause dysuria, frequency, and discharge)', 'Female urethritis is frequently asymptomatic or mild, although it can cause dysuria, frequency, and discharge. The remaining options either contradict the typical course or are not valid statements.'),
('6030af2e-2d27-5c79-9988-f7ddf3e6504a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common cause of cystitis is:', '["Pseudomonas aeruginosa resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","E. coli (ascending infection from the urethra)","Staphylococcus aureus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Klebsiella pneumoniae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'E. coli (ascending infection from the urethra)', 'Escherichia coli ascending from the perineum through the urethra causes the majority of cystitis cases. Pseudomonas, Klebsiella, and Staphylococcus aureus are less common urinary pathogens.'),
('9014c205-484d-5cb1-a145-057176a79d4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Cystitis is more common in females because:', '["The female bladder is smaller resulting in decreased functional activity","The female urethra is short and close to the anus (facilitates ascending infection)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Females have more bladder infections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The female urethra is short and close to the anus (facilitates ascending infection)', 'The short female urethra and its proximity to the anus allow perineal organisms easy access to the bladder, explaining the higher frequency of cystitis in women. A smaller bladder size is not the reason.'),
('aff718f7-7d9a-5903-9375-9a8c0803b4c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The symptoms of cystitis include:', '["Flank pain resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hematuria only mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dysuria (painful urination), frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain"]'::jsonb, 'Dysuria (painful urination), frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain', 'Irritation of the bladder wall in cystitis produces dysuria, frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain. Flank pain points to the upper urinary tract, and hematuria alone is not the characteristic symptom set.'),
('78d781b0-542b-55d6-8d5b-2f4bae9a4d3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Pyelonephritis is:', '["An infection of the bladder","An infection of the kidney (upper urinary tract)","None","An infection of the urethra","An infection of the prostate"]'::jsonb, 'An infection of the kidney (upper urinary tract)', 'Pyelonephritis is an infection of the renal parenchyma and pelvis, i.e., the upper urinary tract. Infection of the bladder, urethra, or prostate represents lower urinary tract involvement.'),
('bf053d0e-1d20-5751-b40a-64369b744666'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Pyelonephritis presents with:', '["Only fever resulting in decreased functional activity","Only costovertebral angle tenderness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fever, flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness","Only flank pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness', 'Acute pyelonephritis classically causes fever, flank pain, and costovertebral angle tenderness together. Presenting with any one of these features in isolation is incomplete.'),
('7885972f-cda7-5291-8cd7-2adcf16f4919'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urinary tract infections can be diagnosed by:', '["Urinalysis (urine dipstick and microscopic examination) and urine culture","Blood tests accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only urinalysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Only urine culture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urinalysis (urine dipstick and microscopic examination) and urine culture', 'UTI diagnosis combines urinalysis (dipstick and microscopy) with urine culture for confirmation and identification of the organism. Blood tests are not primary, and using only urinalysis or only culture is incomplete.'),
('03410778-ecfc-5925-8502-a4a49adf66db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common cause of urinary obstruction in older males is:', '["Urethral stricture resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder stones mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) - compresses the prostatic urethra","Prostate cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) - compresses the prostatic urethra', 'BPH is the most common cause of bladder outlet obstruction in older men because the enlarged transitional zone compresses the prostatic urethra. Strictures, stones, and prostate cancer are less frequent causes.'),
('6bcb0b3e-6a0c-5d1a-8b26-bec2df3c5386'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bladder outlet obstruction can cause:', '["Incontinence resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinary hesitancy, reduced stream, incomplete emptying, and nocturia","Painless hematuria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urinary hesitancy, reduced stream, incomplete emptying, and nocturia', 'Bladder outlet obstruction produces obstructive voiding symptoms, including hesitancy, a weak stream, incomplete emptying, and nocturia. Incontinence and painless hematuria are not characteristic of simple outlet obstruction.'),
('7d3a3f92-99d2-57d9-b502-0dc872d21214'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The normal bladder capacity in adults is about:', '["800-1000 mL","400-600 mL","None","50-100 mL","100-200 mL"]'::jsonb, '400-600 mL', 'Normal adult bladder capacity is about 400-600 mL. The values of 50-100 mL and 100-200 mL are far too small, and 800-1000 mL exceeds physiologic capacity.'),
('846eae8a-51af-5091-829a-4c3425ea378d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The first urge to void is felt at about:', '["150-200 mL of urine in the bladder","50 mL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","600 mL mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","400 mL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '150-200 mL of urine in the bladder', 'Stretch of the bladder wall first triggers the urge to void at about 150-200 mL, with urgency building near capacity. The thresholds of 50, 400, and 600 mL are incorrect.'),
('2df986de-eebc-56de-b603-3fff0badc315'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The sensation of bladder fullness is mediated by:', '["Temperature receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Touch receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stretch receptors in the bladder wall (via the pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)","Pain receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch receptors in the bladder wall (via the pelvic splanchnic nerves, S2-S4)', 'Bladder fullness is perceived by stretch receptors in the detrusor wall whose afferent fibers travel in the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4). Temperature, touch, and pain receptors do not mediate this sensation.'),
('2cdfdc1b-c007-54d1-98c0-7cf9e403c73c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The detrusor muscle is innervated by:', '["Somatic (pudendal, S2-S4) resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic (S2-S4, pelvic splanchnic) - causes contraction","Sympathetic (T11-L2, hypogastric) - causes relaxation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (S2-S4, pelvic splanchnic) - causes contraction', 'The detrusor is innervated by parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4), which contract the bladder during voiding. Sympathetic input relaxes the detrusor, and the pudendal nerve supplies the external sphincter.'),
('f8bae44b-0ddd-51bf-a212-60fec7d3037d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The internal urethral sphincter (involuntary) is innervated by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The somatic nervous system mediated by specific receptor activation","The parasympathetic nervous system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The sympathetic nervous system (causes contraction to prevent leakage)"]'::jsonb, 'The sympathetic nervous system (causes contraction to prevent leakage)', 'The internal urethral sphincter is supplied by sympathetic fibers (via the hypogastric nerves, T11-L2) that maintain closure to prevent leakage of urine. Somatic and parasympathetic nerves serve other sphincter roles.'),
('32e08cd2-97c0-5dae-b45c-cebb49d3b729'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The external urethral sphincter (voluntary) is innervated by:', '["The pudendal nerve (somatic, S2-S4)","The sympathetic nervous system","The parasympathetic nervous system","None","The hypogastric nerve"]'::jsonb, 'The pudendal nerve (somatic, S2-S4)', 'The voluntary external urethral sphincter receives somatic innervation from the pudendal nerve (S2-S4). Sympathetic, parasympathetic, and hypogastric fibers do not innervate this striated sphincter.'),
('b3f55bf4-bfa9-56b8-8440-0e8436a3746b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Micturition is a reflex that is:', '["Purely voluntary resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Purely involuntary mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cranial without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal (sacral) at its core, but under voluntary control from the brain"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal (sacral) at its core, but under voluntary control from the brain', 'Micturition is primarily a sacral spinal reflex that is modulated by voluntary control from higher centers that initiate or suppress voiding. It is neither purely voluntary nor purely involuntary.'),
('917f0190-8e5c-562e-b492-afbcb4c9c5f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The pontine micturition center (Barrington center) in the brainstem:', '["Suppresses micturition resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates micturition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coordinates the bladder and the urinary sphincter for efficient voiding","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coordinates the bladder and the urinary sphincter for efficient voiding', 'The pontine micturition center coordinates detrusor contraction with relaxation of the sphincter so that voiding is efficient and complete. It does not simply suppress or stimulate the process alone.'),
('d08eba4c-226b-5177-bff3-48014e831d53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Damage to the sacral spinal cord can cause:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of the micturition reflex (bladder becomes flaccid and overflow incontinence)","Normal bladder function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spastic bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of the micturition reflex (bladder becomes flaccid and overflow incontinence)', 'Destruction of the sacral voiding center abolishes the micturition reflex, leaving an areflexic flaccid bladder that overflows when full. Normal function and spastic bladder describe different patterns of injury.'),
('c71ccf2e-2531-5099-bedf-bff22f06abee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Damage to the upper motor neurons (brain/spinal cord above S2) can cause:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A flaccid bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal bladder function mediated by specific receptor activation","A spastic (hyperreflexic) bladder and urge incontinence","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A spastic (hyperreflexic) bladder and urge incontinence', 'Lesions above S2 remove descending inhibitory control, leaving the sacral reflex unopposed and producing a spastic, hyperreflexic bladder with urge incontinence. A flaccid bladder follows damage to the sacral cord itself.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('35b418c9-9332-5eb5-ae3f-7b36e0ba9f12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urinary incontinence can be:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Stress incontinence, urge incontinence, overflow incontinence, or functional incontinence","Only urge incontinence mediated by specific receptor activation","Only stress incontinence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only overflow incontinence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stress incontinence, urge incontinence, overflow incontinence, or functional incontinence', 'Urinary incontinence is classified into stress, urge, overflow, and functional types, depending on the mechanism. It is not restricted to any single subtype.'),
('71354d62-9f02-5a28-9412-27f59125864d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Stress incontinence is:', '["Leakage of urine with increased intra-abdominal pressure (coughing, sneezing, laughing)","Leakage of urine when the bladder is full accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leakage of urine with a strong urge to void mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leakage of urine with increased intra-abdominal pressure (coughing, sneezing, laughing)', 'Stress incontinence is the loss of urine when raised intra-abdominal pressure from coughing, sneezing, or laughing exceeds urethral closure pressure. Leakage from a full bladder or a strong urge describes overflow and urge incontinence.'),
('abad9be6-4e6d-5007-8d97-b110881b3608'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Stress incontinence is most commonly caused by:', '["Overactive bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Benign prostatic hyperplasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Weakness of the pelvic floor (levator ani) and the urethral sphincters (often after childbirth)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bladder cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weakness of the pelvic floor (levator ani) and the urethral sphincters (often after childbirth)', 'Stress incontinence most often follows weakness of the pelvic floor, particularly the levator ani and urethral sphincter support, commonly after childbirth. Overactive bladder, BPH, and bladder cancer have different mechanisms.'),
('c6bbd281-b2c3-5e51-93ec-b061f3db4f22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urge incontinence is:', '["Constant leakage of urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Leakage of urine preceded by a strong, sudden urge to void (from detrusor overactivity)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leakage of urine with increased abdominal pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leakage of urine preceded by a strong, sudden urge to void (from detrusor overactivity)', 'Urge incontinence results from detrusor (bladder muscle) overactivity, producing leakage preceded by a sudden, powerful urge to void. Stress incontinence occurs with raised abdominal pressure, while continuous leakage suggests a fistula or ectopic ureter.'),
('606809b6-c5e5-5e03-a1a2-adac8d4d9358'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urinary retention is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The inability to store urine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A large volume of urine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The inability to empty the bladder (can be acute or chronic)"]'::jsonb, 'The inability to empty the bladder (can be acute or chronic)', 'Urinary retention means the bladder cannot be emptied despite normal urine production, and it may be acute or chronic. Storing a large volume or failing to store urine describes other voiding problems rather than failure to empty.'),
('21031fa5-a683-57ad-a3b3-d71b6fd0a84d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Acute urinary retention in males is most commonly caused by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Prostate cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder stones mediated by specific receptor activation","Urethral stricture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","BPH (prostatic enlargement that compresses the prostatic urethra)"]'::jsonb, 'BPH (prostatic enlargement that compresses the prostatic urethra)', 'BPH is the commonest cause of acute urinary retention in men because prostatic enlargement compresses the prostatic urethra. Urethral stricture, bladder stones, and prostate cancer are less frequent causes.'),
('993d4b44-db5c-5275-b5f3-90775d8aeb86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Urinary retention can be treated with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Catheterization (insertion of a catheter to drain the bladder)","Medication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Catheterization (insertion of a catheter to drain the bladder)', 'Catheterization provides immediate drainage of the retained urine via the urethra or suprapubic route. Surgery and medication address the underlying cause but do not promptly relieve acute retention on their own.'),
('5baa7138-1d0c-5f78-bd5d-5365c0f511f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The Coude catheter is:', '["A straight catheter resulting in decreased functional activity","A curved-tip catheter used to navigate around an enlarged prostate","A balloon catheter mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A three-way catheter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A curved-tip catheter used to navigate around an enlarged prostate', 'The Coude catheter has a curved tip designed to negotiate the tortuous, elongated prostatic urethra in men with an enlarged prostate. Straight, balloon, and three-way catheters serve different purposes.'),
('d7aa861e-c573-55bc-8d6a-8ac8b4e68e88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A suprapubic catheter is:', '["A catheter through the rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A catheter inserted through the anterior abdominal wall (above the pubis) into the bladder","A catheter through the urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A catheter inserted through the anterior abdominal wall (above the pubis) into the bladder', 'A suprapubic catheter enters the bladder through the anterior abdominal wall just above the pubic symphysis, bypassing the urethra. It is used after pelvic surgery, urethral injury, or when urethral catheterization fails.'),
('4d8b6149-2ea0-5cc1-b61c-e45cc0146fc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is examined by:', '["Only ultrasound resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ultrasound (to assess bladder volume and wall thickness), cystoscopy (to look at the bladder mucosa), and urodynamics (to assess function)","Only cystoscopy mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only urodynamics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound (to assess bladder volume and wall thickness), cystoscopy (to look at the bladder mucosa), and urodynamics (to assess function)', 'The bladder is evaluated with ultrasound for volume and wall thickness, cystoscopy for direct mucosal inspection, and urodynamics for functional assessment. No single modality provides a complete evaluation.'),
('1ef2f7bd-8425-59d6-b472-a921655c0f63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bladder stones can form from:', '["Stasis of urine (due to obstruction, e.g., BPH, or neurogenic bladder)","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","High intake of calcium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stasis of urine (due to obstruction, e.g., BPH, or neurogenic bladder)', 'Bladder stones usually form when urine stagnates in the bladder, as in BPH or neurogenic bladder. Infection and calcium intake may contribute, but stasis is the key predisposing mechanism.'),
('cabf8fe9-b09e-57d0-8324-c304c7c84e26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bladder stones can cause:', '["Only hematuria resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain, hematuria, and interruption of the urinary stream","Only interruption of the stream mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain, hematuria, and interruption of the urinary stream', 'Bladder stones classically cause suprapubic pain, hematuria, and sudden interruption of the urinary stream when the stone occludes the bladder neck. The picture is a combination of symptoms rather than any single one in isolation.'),
('77093322-b3a2-5aee-b1e7-935307df0edb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common type of bladder stone is:', '["Cystine stones resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uric acid stones (from concentrated urine)","Calcium oxalate stones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Struvite (infection) stones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uric acid stones (from concentrated urine)', 'Uric acid stones are the most common bladder stones, forming in concentrated urine. Calcium oxalate, struvite, and cystine stones are encountered more often in the upper urinary tract.'),
('d70b474a-53b0-50ff-9caa-ab10a279b5ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder develops from:', '["The hindgut resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the urogenital sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the mesonephric ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The urogenital sinus (endoderm) and the mesonephric ducts (trigone)"]'::jsonb, 'The urogenital sinus (endoderm) and the mesonephric ducts (trigone)', 'The bladder epithelium and most of its wall derive from the endodermal urogenital sinus, whereas the trigone forms from absorbed mesonephric duct tissue. The hindgut gives rise only to the rectum and anal canal.'),
('21d28d96-2eb5-5183-bd73-6f2ada0d39d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidney develops from:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The urogenital sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The metanephros (the most advanced stage of kidney development)","The pronephros without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The mesonephros leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The metanephros (the most advanced stage of kidney development)', 'The kidney arises from the metanephros, the third and definitive renal primordium. The pronephros and mesonephros are transient embryonic kidneys, and the urogenital sinus forms the bladder and urethra.'),
('d150f8f5-7986-5f9e-85c2-6ffacb353fed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The ureter develops from:', '["The metanephros resulting in decreased functional activity","The urogenital sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The pronephros without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ureteric bud (a branch from the mesonephric duct)"]'::jsonb, 'The ureteric bud (a branch from the mesonephric duct)', 'The ureter develops from the ureteric bud, an outgrowth of the mesonephric duct that induces the metanephric blastema. The metanephros, pronephros, and urogenital sinus do not form the ureter.'),
('9e0baf89-adc3-57ed-93f8-3b8b8c961be2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The collecting ducts of the kidney develop from:', '["The metanephric blastema","The tubule","The glomerulus","None","The ureteric bud"]'::jsonb, 'The ureteric bud', 'The ureteric bud gives rise to the renal pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts. The metanephric blastema forms the nephrons, not the collecting system.'),
('bed5c21b-80de-5892-9dea-ca4882f79360'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The nephrons develop from:', '["The metanephric blastema (the intermediate mesoderm)","The pronephros accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The mesonephric duct mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The ureteric bud leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The metanephric blastema (the intermediate mesoderm)', 'Nephrons differentiate from the metanephric blastema under inductive signals from the ureteric bud. The mesonephric duct, pronephros, and the bud itself do not form nephrons.'),
('7440ca2a-2b01-52c5-83a7-943d5d6ba632'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A double ureter (ureter duplication) is:', '["A lethal anomaly resulting in decreased functional activity","A common congenital anomaly (the ureteric bud bifurcates early in development)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A rare anomaly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A common congenital anomaly (the ureteric bud bifurcates early in development)', 'Ureteral duplication is a common congenital anomaly resulting from premature bifurcation of the ureteric bud before it reaches the metanephric blastema. It is usually asymptomatic and not lethal.'),
('e57cb460-0f7c-5de0-85a2-599015dfff81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A ureterocele is:', '["A stone in the ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tumor of the ureter mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A cystic dilation of the terminal part of the ureter (can cause obstruction)"]'::jsonb, 'A cystic dilation of the terminal part of the ureter (can cause obstruction)', 'A ureterocele is a congenital cystic dilation of the terminal, intramural part of the ureter that may obstruct the orifice or trigone. It is neither a stone nor a tumor.'),
('dd60beac-f741-5c89-8a10-00b051e69878'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Pelvic-ureteric junction (PUJ) obstruction is:', '["A narrowing of the entire ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","A narrowing at the ureterovesical junction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A narrowing at the junction of the renal pelvis and the ureter (can cause hydronephrosis)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A narrowing at the junction of the renal pelvis and the ureter (can cause hydronephrosis)', 'PUJ obstruction is a congenital narrowing at the funnel-shaped junction between the renal pelvis and ureter, producing hydronephrosis. Obstruction lower in the ureter or narrowing of the entire ureter is a different lesion.'),
('6df74605-a3da-587e-96c6-6755dd7a64bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A horseshoe kidney is located:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Higher than normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Lower than normal (at L3-L5) because the inferior mesenteric artery prevents its ascent","At the normal position leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower than normal (at L3-L5) because the inferior mesenteric artery prevents its ascent', 'In horseshoe kidney the two kidneys fuse at their lower poles and are caught by the inferior mesenteric artery, so they remain low at L3-L5. They are not higher than normal or at the usual position.'),
('2bfe3b21-44f3-5ca4-bcf6-47540be2770e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Pelvic kidney (ectopic kidney) is:', '["A kidney that is smaller than normal resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A kidney that failed to ascend and remains in the pelvis","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A kidney that is higher than normal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A kidney that failed to ascend and remains in the pelvis', 'A pelvic kidney is an ectopic kidney that failed to ascend and remains in the pelvis. It is not defined by being smaller, nor is it located higher than normal.'),
('8013d9a0-b062-5382-9b34-91e20e1c51e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Renal agenesis is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A duplicated kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A small kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","A congenital absence of one (unilateral) or both (bilateral) kidneys","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A congenital absence of one (unilateral) or both (bilateral) kidneys', 'Renal agenesis is the congenital absence of a kidney, which may affect one or both sides. A duplicated kidney and a small kidney are different conditions, not true absence.'),
('aeabd68e-b2c6-5da8-b07a-6d2a79023813'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Unilateral renal agenesis is:', '["Usually asymptomatic (the other kidney hypertrophies to compensate)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","symptomatic mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lethal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Usually asymptomatic (the other kidney hypertrophies to compensate)', 'With unilateral renal agenesis the single remaining kidney hypertrophies, so affected individuals are usually asymptomatic and the condition is often detected incidentally. It is neither symptomatic nor lethal.'),
('0bec119d-43ea-5378-8ddd-738977e29d25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bilateral renal agenesis is:', '["Compatible with life resulting in decreased functional activity","asymptomatic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Incompatible with life (the baby has the Potter sequence: pulmonary hypoplasia, etc.)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incompatible with life (the baby has the Potter sequence: pulmonary hypoplasia, etc.)', 'Bilateral renal agenesis is incompatible with life because no urine is produced, leading to oligohydramnios, pulmonary hypoplasia, and the Potter sequence. It is uniformly fatal.'),
('c2369f42-907f-50ac-9e4c-f99301ebd3c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidneys are retroperitoneal organs:', '["They are intraperitoneal resulting in decreased functional activity","True (they lie behind the peritoneum, in the perirenal space)","False mediated by specific receptor activation","They are preperitoneal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'True (they lie behind the peritoneum, in the perirenal space)', 'The kidneys lie behind the posterior peritoneum within the retroperitoneal perirenal space. They are neither intraperitoneal nor preperitoneal.'),
('b8afaae1-c03c-5b2c-b7ee-3518fc014ed4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidney is located:', '["In the anterior pararenal space resulting in decreased functional activity","In the perirenal space (within Gerota fascia), at the level of T12-L3","In the intraperitoneal space mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the posterior pararenal space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the perirenal space (within Gerota fascia), at the level of T12-L3', 'Each kidney occupies the perirenal space within Gerota fascia at roughly the T12-L3 vertebral level. The anterior and posterior pararenal spaces lie outside this fascia.'),
('3d5d4c78-d8d8-5a36-8599-3c38c5b32a08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The right kidney is lower than the left kidney because:', '["The liver (the right lobe of the liver) is large and displaces the right kidney downward","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The spleen is on the left mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The heart is on the left leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The liver (the right lobe of the liver) is large and displaces the right kidney downward', 'The right kidney sits lower because the large right lobe of the liver displaces it inferiorly. The positions of the spleen and heart do not account for the difference.'),
('ff9330fb-226e-539e-b4c7-1f5b2b01ad60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidneys are about:', '["5 cm long resulting in decreased functional activity","15 cm long accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","10-12 cm long, 5-6 cm wide, and 3-4 cm thick","20 cm long leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10-12 cm long, 5-6 cm wide, and 3-4 cm thick', 'An adult kidney measures about 10-12 cm in length, 5-6 cm in width, and 3-4 cm in thickness. Dimensions of 5, 15, and 20 cm are outside the normal range.'),
('aa51e594-8c01-5b43-b903-5edd87caccf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidneys weigh about:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","50 g accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","120-170 g each (in adults)","250 g without affecting the primary tissue pathway","300 g leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '120-170 g each (in adults)', 'Each adult kidney weighs about 120-170 g. Values such as 50 g, 250 g, or 300 g lie outside the normal range.'),
('288b2b5b-42b1-5cd3-8619-7cce70c0161b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidney is covered by:', '["Only a fibrous capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","Only perirenal fat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A fibrous capsule (true capsule) and is surrounded by perirenal fat","Only a serosal layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A fibrous capsule (true capsule) and is surrounded by perirenal fat', 'Each kidney is covered by its fibrous true capsule and is surrounded by perirenal fat outside the capsule. A serosal layer is not present.'),
('34821dc6-3bfe-5ff9-8bbb-8b567348332d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The perirenal fat is enclosed by:', '["None","The transversalis fascia","The endoabdominal fascia","The renal fascia (Gerota fascia)","The peritoneum"]'::jsonb, 'The renal fascia (Gerota fascia)', 'The renal (Gerota) fascia encloses the perirenal fat together with the kidney and suprarenal gland. The transversalis fascia and endoabdominal fascia lie more peripherally, and the peritoneum is anterior.'),
('93c868d4-aa4b-56b5-90b5-82276f324240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal fascia (Gerota fascia) is:', '["The same as the capsicum of the kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A layer of connective tissue that encloses the kidney, the suprarenal gland, and the perirenal fat","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the peritoneum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A layer of connective tissue that encloses the kidney, the suprarenal gland, and the perirenal fat', 'The renal fascia is a connective tissue envelope enclosing the kidney, suprarenal gland, and perirenal fat. It is distinct from the peritoneum and from the fibrous capsule directly on the organ.'),
('644ef9ee-ebc9-5a36-96d5-94b9b28431db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The anterior and posterior layers of Gerota fascia fuse:', '["They do not fuse resulting in decreased functional activity","Only laterally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laterally (to form the lateroconal fascia) and superiorly (to form the diaphragmatic fascia)","Only superiorly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally (to form the lateroconal fascia) and superiorly (to form the diaphragmatic fascia)', 'The anterior and posterior layers of Gerota fascia fuse laterally to form the lateroconal fascia and superiorly with the diaphragmatic fascia. They do not fail to fuse or fuse on only one side.'),
('6920cf20-7c2a-5af5-a8f5-51b254076659'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The lateral conal fascia (lateroconal fascia) separates:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The kidney and the colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The anterior and posterior pararenal spaces without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The perirenal space from the paracolic gutter"]'::jsonb, 'The perirenal space from the paracolic gutter', 'The lateroconal fascia separates the perirenal space from the paracolic gutter. It does not separate the anterior and posterior pararenal spaces.'),
('d653627b-2b61-537e-a006-3bade9586714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidneys are supplied by:', '["The common iliac artery resulting in decreased functional activity","The renal arteries (arising from the abdominal aorta at L1-L2)","The inferior mesenteric artery mediated by specific receptor activation","The superior mesenteric artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The renal arteries (arising from the abdominal aorta at L1-L2)', 'The kidneys receive arterial blood from the renal arteries, which arise from the abdominal aorta near L1-L2. The mesenteric and iliac arteries supply the bowel and pelvis instead.'),
('e503addd-4c11-5639-bfa2-ba2126ceb066'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal arteries divide into:', '["Two main trunks resulting in decreased functional activity","Only anterior branches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only posterior branches mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior and posterior branches (which divide into segmental arteries)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior branches (which divide into segmental arteries)', 'At the hilum each renal artery divides into anterior and posterior branches, which then give rise to the segmental arteries. Branching into two main trunks or a single type of branch does not describe the pattern.'),
('10e01308-28f7-59d0-a9ca-42a7d8376eff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal arteries are end arteries:', '["False resulting in decreased functional activity","Only partially true accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True (each segmental artery supplies a specific segment of the kidney without collateral circulation)"]'::jsonb, 'True (each segmental artery supplies a specific segment of the kidney without collateral circulation)', 'The segmental renal arteries are true end arteries with no collateral anastomoses, so occlusion produces ischemia of a discrete renal segment. The statement is therefore true.'),
('27658001-1eb5-52f9-9458-3b04d34862dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidney is divided into segments:', '["3 segments resulting in decreased functional activity","5 segments (based on the segmental arteries)","6 segments mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 segments leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5 segments (based on the segmental arteries)', 'The kidney is divided into five segments, each supplied by its own segmental artery. Counts of three, four, and six segments are incorrect.'),
('1697ad7c-1c8a-53ea-b590-924e62168a2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal veins drain into:', '["The portal vein resulting in decreased functional activity","The splenic vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The azygos vein mediated by specific receptor activation","The IVC (the right renal vein directly, and the left renal vein across the aorta)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The IVC (the right renal vein directly, and the left renal vein across the aorta)', 'Both renal veins drain into the inferior vena cava: the right vein directly, and the left vein after crossing anterior to the aorta. The portal, splenic, and azygos systems are not their drainage routes.'),
('4f621b46-d029-5fde-ab3d-92cdc1cc9505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The left renal vein is longer and passes:', '["Anterior to the aorta (between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery)","Posterior to the aorta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to the aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To the right leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior to the aorta (between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery)', 'The longer left renal vein passes anterior to the aorta, running between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery, and also receives the left suprarenal and gonadal veins. It is not posterior, lateral, or directed to the right.'),
('ca0f74af-b231-5863-b133-6080be59fa62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Nutcracker syndrome is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Compression of the IVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","Compression of the left renal vein between the aorta and the SMA (causing left renal congestion and hematuria)","Compression of the right renal vein leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compression of the left renal vein between the aorta and the SMA (causing left renal congestion and hematuria)', 'Nutcracker syndrome is compression of the left renal vein between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery, producing left renal congestion, flank pain, and hematuria. The right renal vein and IVC are not involved.'),
('5d98c375-3493-5cc8-a91b-867b0a5ebac0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal hilum is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The inferior pole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The superior pole mediated by specific receptor activation","The medial opening of the kidney (where the renal vessels, ureter, and lymphatics enter/exit)","The lateral opening leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The medial opening of the kidney (where the renal vessels, ureter, and lymphatics enter/exit)', 'The renal hilum is the medial indentation where the renal vessels, ureter, and lymphatics enter and leave the kidney. It is not the superior or inferior pole.'),
('d2cc99cb-5b9d-5db6-9766-c99f49398c2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal vein is anterior, the renal artery is in the middle, and the renal pelvis is posterior:', '["True (vein -> artery -> pelvis, from anterior to posterior)","False accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All structures are at the same level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'True (vein -> artery -> pelvis, from anterior to posterior)', 'At the hilum the renal vein lies most anterior, the renal artery occupies the middle, and the renal pelvis lies most posterior. The structures are therefore not at the same level.'),
('7c8f2d57-4a13-5272-9ae4-a0d579914bb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal pyramid is a:', '["Cone-shaped structure in the medulla of the kidney (the base faces the cortex and the apex (papilla) faces the renal sinus)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A cube-shaped structure mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A flat structure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cone-shaped structure in the medulla of the kidney (the base faces the cortex and the apex (papilla) faces the renal sinus)', 'Each renal pyramid is a cone-shaped medullary structure whose base faces the cortex and whose apex, the papilla, projects toward the renal sinus. It is neither cube-shaped nor flat.'),
('eeba77ce-f1e6-5b4a-a16d-a88dc4cc9719'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal papilla is the:', '["The tip of the renal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Apex of a renal pyramid (projects into a minor calyx)","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The base of a renal pyramid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apex of a renal pyramid (projects into a minor calyx)', 'The renal papilla is the apex of a medullary pyramid and projects into a minor calyx. The base of the pyramid and the tip of the cortex refer to different locations.'),
('70124326-e72d-5860-a531-0484d5665d61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The minor calyx is:', '["A tube that connects the kidney to the ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A cup-shaped tube that surrounds a renal papilla (receives urine from the collecting ducts)","A tube that receives urine from the nephron leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cup-shaped tube that surrounds a renal papilla (receives urine from the collecting ducts)', 'Each minor calyx is a cup-shaped tube that embraces a renal papilla and collects urine released from the collecting ducts. The ureter and the nephron are not calyceal structures.'),
('3e1a7263-df5c-59da-a2c0-899ebec1a75f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Several minor calyces join to form:', '["The ureter","None","The collecting duct","A major calyx","The renal pelvis"]'::jsonb, 'A major calyx', 'Several minor calyces converge to form a single major calyx. Major calyces in turn unite to form the renal pelvis, while the collecting duct lies within the parenchyma.'),
('0e051b92-5f01-5762-8bde-54b6493958b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Several major calyces join to form:', '["The nephron","The renal pelvis","The ureter","The collecting duct","None"]'::jsonb, 'The renal pelvis', 'The major calyces join to form the renal pelvis, the funnel-shaped upper end of the ureter. The nephron and collecting ducts lie within the renal parenchyma.'),
('3898496e-3c2a-50d3-9641-f2dc5b73327f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal pelvis drains into:', '["The bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","The urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The ureter (the funnel-shaped upper end of the ureter)","The kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The ureter (the funnel-shaped upper end of the ureter)', 'The renal pelvis is the funnel-shaped expansion of the upper ureter, and urine drains from it down into the ureter. It does not drain into the kidney, bladder, or urethra directly.'),
('4c7664ff-6f8d-57bc-b30e-7221aa7ea749'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The nephron is the:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Structural and functional unit of the kidney (makes up about 1 million nephrons per kidney)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Largest unit of the kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Smallest unit of the kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Structural and functional unit of the kidney (makes up about 1 million nephrons per kidney)', 'The nephron is the structural and functional unit of the kidney, with about one million nephrons in each kidney. Options naming it the largest or smallest unit do not capture this role.'),
('b433c2e3-00d8-5822-9331-40824a69c33f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The glomerulus is:', '["A collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A blood vessel mediated by specific receptor activation","A tubule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A capillary tuft that filters blood to form glomerular filtrate (the first stage of urine formation)"]'::jsonb, 'A capillary tuft that filters blood to form glomerular filtrate (the first stage of urine formation)', 'The glomerulus is a tuft of capillaries that filters blood to produce the glomerular filtrate, the first step of urine formation. It is not a tubule, collecting duct, or ordinary blood vessel.'),
('74d9f24e-3fe2-539e-847d-0de70d5c7f5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal corpuscle (Malpighian corpuscle) consists of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Bowman capsule alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The glomerulus and Bowman capsule (the initial part of the nephron)","The glomerulus alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The tubule alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The glomerulus and Bowman capsule (the initial part of the nephron)', 'The renal corpuscle is the filtration unit of the nephron, formed by the glomerular tuft of capillaries enclosed within the double-walled Bowman capsule. It is the site where blood plasma is filtered to form the glomerular filtrate. Options naming only one component are incomplete.'),
('f871e628-b26d-5007-9a02-8db16b8db901'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) is:', '["The part of the tubule that concentrates urine resulting in decreased functional activity","The part of the tubule that secretes potassium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The first part of the renal tubule (responsible for the reabsorption of most water, electrolytes, and nutrients)","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The first part of the renal tubule (responsible for the reabsorption of most water, electrolytes, and nutrients)', 'The proximal convoluted tubule follows Bowman capsule and reabsorbs the bulk of filtered water, sodium, glucose, and amino acids. Urine concentration mainly occurs in the loop of Henle and collecting duct, while potassium secretion occurs in the distal nephron.'),
('b5b3a7b0-51e9-51c9-aeb8-4e7ee0ef0a37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The loop of Henle is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","A long straight segment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A curved tubule mediated by specific receptor activation","AU-shaped portion of the renal tubule (responsible for the concentration of urine)","A straight tubule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'AU-shaped portion of the renal tubule (responsible for the concentration of urine)', 'The loop of Henle is a hairpin, U-shaped segment connecting the proximal and distal convoluted tubules. Its descending and ascending limbs generate the medullary osmotic gradient that is critical for concentrating urine.'),
('61efc64b-0f98-57b4-b576-ea5c731734bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The distal convoluted tubule (DCT) is:', '["The part before the loop of Henle resulting in decreased functional activity","The part of the renal tubule after the loop of Henle (involved in electrolyte balance and acid-base regulation)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","The collecting duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The part of the renal tubule after the loop of Henle (involved in electrolyte balance and acid-base regulation)', 'The distal convoluted tubule runs from the loop of Henle to the collecting duct and regulates sodium, potassium, calcium, and acid-base balance under aldosterone and parathyroid hormone. It also contributes to the juxtaglomerular apparatus.'),
('16fe2190-d95f-5dcb-96c5-1b017cf34b9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The collecting duct is:', '["The final part of the nephron (where urine is concentrated under the influence of ADH)","The part of the tubule that is insensitive to hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The same as the DCT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The final part of the nephron (where urine is concentrated under the influence of ADH)', 'The collecting duct courses through the cortex and medulla and is the final site for hormonal control of water reabsorption. Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) inserts aquaporins here, permitting concentrated urine to be produced.'),
('0bd392c3-d619-599d-a842-36f72bd225e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) is:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A structure in the cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A specialized structure at the junction of the afferent arteriole and the DCT (secretes renin for blood pressure regulation)","A structure in the glomerulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A specialized structure at the junction of the afferent arteriole and the DCT (secretes renin for blood pressure regulation)', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus lies where the afferent arteriole contacts the distal convoluted tubule. Its granular cells secrete renin in response to reduced renal perfusion or low distal sodium, initiating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone cascade that raises blood pressure.'),
('2c60e468-4a78-59e5-9efb-61f6bdf6049f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The afferent arteriole supplies:', '["Blood to the peritubular capillaries","None","All","Blood to the glomerulus","Blood from the glomerulus"]'::jsonb, 'Blood to the glomerulus', 'The afferent arteriole delivers blood into the glomerular capillary tuft, where filtration occurs. Blood leaves the tuft through the efferent arteriole.'),
('f776f57d-42a2-542d-90c5-3eb6d026b0cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The efferent arteriole drains:', '["Blood from the Bowman capsule resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood from the glomerulus (to the peritubular capillaries)","Blood to the glomerulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blood from the glomerulus (to the peritubular capillaries)', 'The efferent arteriole carries blood away from the glomerular tuft to the peritubular capillary network. In juxtamedullary nephrons its branches form the vasa recta that supply the medulla.'),
('d0a7f77b-01ae-5e02-8a62-d8cb00016405'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The peritubular capillaries:', '["Surround the renal tubules (reabsorb substances from the tubules)","Surround the collecting ducts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surround the glomerulus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surround the renal tubules (reabsorb substances from the tubules)', 'Peritubular capillaries arise from the efferent arteriole and form a network around the cortical tubules. They reabsorb water and solutes from the filtrate back into the blood.'),
('42a41ac6-6206-5fff-bf68-4b43257f3ad5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The vasa recta:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Are the capillaries of the cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Are long, looped capillaries that supply the loop of Henle in the medulla (important for the countercurrent mechanism)","Are the same as the peritubular capillaries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Are long, looped capillaries that supply the loop of Henle in the medulla (important for the countercurrent mechanism)', 'The vasa recta are long, hairpin capillaries running alongside the loop of Henle in the medulla. They maintain the medullary osmotic gradient and preserve the countercurrent exchange needed to concentrate urine.'),
('3937432a-76e2-541f-bac9-7152364840c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidney processes about:', '["500 liters of filtrate per day resulting in decreased functional activity","180 liters of glomerular filtrate per day (but produces only about 1-2 liters of urine)","50 liters of filtrate per day mediated by specific receptor activation","1000 liters of filtrate per day without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '180 liters of glomerular filtrate per day (but produces only about 1-2 liters of urine)', 'The glomeruli filter roughly 180 liters per day, but over 99% is reabsorbed along the tubules so only about 1-2 liters of urine are excreted.'),
('c529770f-07a7-59a5-832a-9ac7d791f202'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidney is located at:', '["The level of T12 to L3 (the left kidney is slightly higher than the right)","L1-L4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L3-L5 mediated by specific receptor activation","T10-L1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The level of T12 to L3 (the left kidney is slightly higher than the right)', 'Each kidney lies retroperitoneally on the posterior abdominal wall at the level of T12 to L3, with the left kidney slightly higher than the right because the liver pushes the right kidney downward.'),
('4d2df21f-c752-5f45-a833-53dc16a37e56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The costovertebral angle (CVA) is:', '["The angle between the ribs and the diaphragm resulting in decreased functional activity","The angle between the 12th rib and the vertebral column (the CVA is a common site for percussion to assess for kidney tenderness)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The angle between the iliac crest and the vertebral column leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The angle between the 12th rib and the vertebral column (the CVA is a common site for percussion to assess for kidney tenderness)', 'The costovertebral angle is formed by the 12th rib and the lateral border of the erector spinae muscles over the vertebral column. It overlies the kidneys, so percussion here elicits tenderness in renal disease.'),
('2872b705-c524-58fb-840c-510f44210889'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'CVA tenderness suggests:', '["Prostatic pathology resulting in decreased functional activity","Bladder pathology accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urethral pathology mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renal pathology (such as pyelonephritis or a renal stone)"]'::jsonb, 'Renal pathology (such as pyelonephritis or a renal stone)', 'Costovertebral angle tenderness is classically elicited in acute pyelonephritis or with obstructing renal stones. It reflects inflammation or distension of the renal capsule and collecting system.'),
('a0ad0ae9-b29b-5ae9-b833-984392990c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidney is usually not palpable:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","In adults (it may be palpable in thin individuals or in children)","In thin individuals mediated by specific receptor activation","It is palpable without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In children leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In adults (it may be palpable in thin individuals or in children)', 'In adults the kidneys normally are not palpable because they lie deep to the rib cage and thick back musculature. They may be felt in thin individuals or children and are clearly palpable when enlarged.'),
('9e317cbe-796d-59a6-a12e-895d380dbf9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The kidneys are usually palpable when:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They are normal mediated by specific receptor activation","They are small without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They are enlarged (e.g., polycystic kidney disease, hydronephrosis, or a renal tumor)"]'::jsonb, 'They are enlarged (e.g., polycystic kidney disease, hydronephrosis, or a renal tumor)', 'The kidneys become palpable when they are enlarged, as in polycystic kidney disease, hydronephrosis, or renal tumors. A normal-sized kidney is generally not palpable.'),
('f4f06f4d-e5ab-585f-9620-e5753ab93e5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The renal angle is:', '["The area between the 12th rib and the outer border of the erector spinae muscle (the area of the CVA)","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The angle between the ribs and the diaphragm mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The angle between the kidney and the ureter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The area between the 12th rib and the outer border of the erector spinae muscle (the area of the CVA)', 'The renal angle is the clinical term for the region overlying each kidney, bounded by the 12th rib and the outer border of the erector spinae muscle. It corresponds to the costovertebral angle.'),
('944f049b-423e-5c78-8fbb-2c7057536ebd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Hypertension can be caused by:', '["Renal artery stenosis (a narrowing of the renal artery that reduces blood flow to the kidney)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bladder stones mediated by specific receptor activation","Prostate enlargement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urethral stricture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal artery stenosis (a narrowing of the renal artery that reduces blood flow to the kidney)', 'Renal artery stenosis reduces renal perfusion, stimulating the juxtaglomerular apparatus to release renin and raising blood pressure through the renin-angiotensin system. It is a surgically correctable cause of secondary hypertension.'),
('dda84b47-9143-5227-8fda-a54eb62fb17a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Renal artery stenosis is most commonly caused by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Atherosclerosis (in older patients) or fibromuscular dysplasia (in younger patients, especially women)","A tumor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","An infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A stone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerosis (in older patients) or fibromuscular dysplasia (in younger patients, especially women)', 'Renal artery stenosis is most often due to atherosclerosis in older adults and fibromuscular dysplasia in younger women. Both narrow the artery and activate the renin-angiotensin axis.'),
('00909c1c-4728-55c8-bb58-710811ffe3d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A renal tumor can present with:', '["Only a flank mass resulting in decreased functional activity","Only flank pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hematuria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hematuria (blood in the urine), a flank mass, and flank pain"]'::jsonb, 'Hematuria (blood in the urine), a flank mass, and flank pain', 'The classic triad of renal cell carcinoma is hematuria, a flank mass, and flank pain, although all three are present in only a minority of cases. Hematuria is the most frequent presenting sign.'),
('ca4f1751-6054-50df-9bcc-9ad7417e08b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common type of renal cancer is:', '["Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma)","Transitional cell carcinoma","None","Renal cell carcinoma (clear cell carcinoma)","Squamous cell carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Renal cell carcinoma (clear cell carcinoma)', 'Renal cell carcinoma, most often of the clear cell type, accounts for roughly 85% of primary renal cancers and arises from proximal tubular epithelial cells. Wilms tumor predominates in children and transitional cell carcinoma arises from the urothelium of the renal pelvis.'),
('7c725bbd-0ade-53b7-9905-44832839764b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The most common renal tumor in children (usually presents with an abdominal mass)","A benign tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The most common renal tumor in adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The most common renal tumor in children (usually presents with an abdominal mass)', 'Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) is the most common renal malignancy of childhood, typically presenting as an asymptomatic abdominal mass. It arises from primitive metanephric blastema.'),
('b266675e-ab49-56e2-a067-6a15c6ef89b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Polycystic kidney disease (PKD) is:', '["A single cyst in the kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A tumor of the kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","A genetic disorder characterized by the growth of multiple cysts in the kidneys (leading to renal failure)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A genetic disorder characterized by the growth of multiple cysts in the kidneys (leading to renal failure)', 'Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is a genetic disorder in which multiple cysts progressively replace renal parenchyma, leading to hypertension, pain, and end-stage renal failure.'),
('36333a1c-2bab-5418-83d6-1b511da510ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Polycystic kidney disease can cause:', '["Only renal failure resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a palpable mass mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal failure, hypertension, and a palpable abdominal mass","Only hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal failure, hypertension, and a palpable abdominal mass', 'Progressive cyst growth in polycystic kidney disease compresses and destroys nephrons, causing renal failure and renovascular hypertension, and the enlarged kidneys are palpable as bilateral flank masses.'),
('22df8e6f-3b9f-5c96-bf71-112e7bdc04be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common type of kidney stone is:', '["Struvite stones resulting in decreased functional activity","Uric acid stones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cystine stones mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcium oxalate stones (about 75-85% of all kidney stones)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium oxalate stones (about 75-85% of all kidney stones)', 'Calcium oxalate stones account for about 75-85% of kidney stones. Struvite, uric acid, and cystine stones are less common and have distinct metabolic or infectious causes.'),
('a9eb0877-1187-58ec-a338-73e1193f0c97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Kidney stones can cause:', '["Only hematuria resulting in decreased functional activity","Only flank pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Severe flank pain (renal colic) that radiates to the groin, hematuria, and nausea/vomiting","Only nausea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe flank pain (renal colic) that radiates to the groin, hematuria, and nausea/vomiting', 'Stones obstructing the ureter produce severe colicky flank pain that radiates to the groin, along with hematuria and nausea or vomiting. Pain occurs as the stone irritates and distends the urinary tract.'),
('2ca02a8f-2338-537f-9ada-d589b272b44b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Renal colic is treated with:', '["Surgery only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain medication (NSAIDs or opioids), hydration, and sometimes stone removal (lithotripsy or ureteroscopy)","Watchful waiting mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibiotics only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain medication (NSAIDs or opioids), hydration, and sometimes stone removal (lithotripsy or ureteroscopy)', 'Initial management of renal colic is analgesia with NSAIDs or opioids and adequate hydration to promote spontaneous passage. Large or refractory stones are removed by lithotripsy or ureteroscopy.'),
('d48c3d56-d4d2-523b-9875-c5befde7d9b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy (ESWL) is:', '["A procedure that uses laser to break up stones resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A non-invasive procedure that uses shock waves to break up kidney stones (so they can be passed in the urine)","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An invasive surgical procedure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A non-invasive procedure that uses shock waves to break up kidney stones (so they can be passed in the urine)', 'Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy focuses sound waves on the stone to fragment it into pieces small enough to pass in the urine, avoiding any incision. It is best suited for small upper-tract stones.'),
('be7894d7-3f5f-5cb3-bf0d-67cd9b8504af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Ureteroscopy is:', '["A procedure to visualize the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","A procedure to visualize the kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A procedure in which a small, flexible scope is passed up the ureter to visualize or remove a stone","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A procedure in which a small, flexible scope is passed up the ureter to visualize or remove a stone', 'Ureteroscopy passes a flexible endoscope through the urethra and bladder into the ureter, allowing direct visualization and often simultaneous fragmentation or retrieval of stones. It can also be used to biopsy or resect ureteral lesions.'),
('e3019f47-a35d-5f6a-b3d2-9e6387e8f279'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Percutaneous nephrolithotomy (PCNL) is:', '["A surgical procedure to remove large kidney stones through a small incision in the flank (into the kidney)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A procedure to remove a stone from the ureter mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A procedure to remove a kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical procedure to remove large kidney stones through a small incision in the flank (into the kidney)', 'Percutaneous nephrolithotomy makes a small flank incision into the kidney and uses a nephroscope to break up and remove large or complex stones, such as staghorn calculi, in a single procedure.'),
('2b26cdb6-00f2-5dc6-b986-5bf6e63c5ca3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A staghorn calculus is:', '["A large kidney stone that fills the renal pelvis and branches into the calyces (shaped like a stag''s horn)","A small stone in the ureter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A stone in the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large kidney stone that fills the renal pelvis and branches into the calyces (shaped like a stag''s horn)', 'A staghorn calculus is a large stone that fills the renal pelvis and branches into the calyces, taking the shape of a stag''s horn. It is often struvite, associated with chronic infection, and usually requires surgical removal.'),
('d6fdac29-1384-5803-98db-414ebb5f3287'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The most common risk factor for bladder cancer is:', '["Alcohol resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Smoking (cigarette smoking is the single most important risk factor)","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking (cigarette smoking is the single most important risk factor)', 'Cigarette smoking is the single most important risk factor for bladder cancer, delivering carcinogens that are concentrated and excreted in urine. It roughly triples the risk of urothelial carcinoma.'),
('8167116e-0c94-57aa-877e-3aaf888a196c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Occupational exposure to increases the risk of bladder cancer:', '["Aromatic amines (e.g., in the dye, rubber, and chemical industries)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coal dust mediated by specific receptor activation","Silica without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Asbestos leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aromatic amines (e.g., in the dye, rubber, and chemical industries)', 'Exposure to aromatic amines such as benzidine and beta-naphthylamine in the dye, rubber, and chemical industries increases bladder cancer risk. These carcinogens are excreted in the urine and contact the bladder urothelium.'),
('6fea1de0-6518-5375-81b8-0296916fc0e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The diagnosis of bladder cancer is confirmed by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Ultrasound only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdominal X-ray mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinalysis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cystoscopy (direct visualization of the tumor in the bladder) and biopsy (histologic confirmation)"]'::jsonb, 'Cystoscopy (direct visualization of the tumor in the bladder) and biopsy (histologic confirmation)', 'Cystoscopy permits direct visualization of a bladder lesion, and tissue obtained by biopsy provides histologic confirmation of transitional cell carcinoma. Imaging and urinalysis alone cannot establish the diagnosis.'),
('9bfe494d-25df-5a70-a46a-c4eb35e1f962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Transitional cell carcinoma (TCC) of the bladder is:', '["A cancer that arises from the squamous epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","A cancer that arises from the transitional epithelium (urothelium) that lines the bladder","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A cancer that arises from the glandular epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A cancer that arises from the transitional epithelium (urothelium) that lines the bladder', 'Bladder cancer arises from the transitional epithelium (urothelium) that lines the bladder, so most tumors are transitional cell carcinoma. Squamous and glandular variants are uncommon.'),
('0ebe103f-65bf-5b05-a4d4-9ac1fd8c1a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Bladder cancer is staged as:', '["Only muscle invasive resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-muscle invasive (superficial) or muscle invasive (T2 or higher)","None mediated by specific receptor activation","There is staging system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only non-muscle invasive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-muscle invasive (superficial) or muscle invasive (T2 or higher)', 'Bladder cancer is stratified by depth of invasion into non-muscle-invasive (superficial) and muscle-invasive disease. This distinction drives management, since muscle invasion greatly worsens prognosis and requires radical therapy.'),
('2656d0af-7f0a-50dd-ac08-7b84942a333b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer (NMIBC) can be treated with:', '["Radical cystectomy resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Radiation therapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) with or without intravesical therapy (BCG or chemotherapy)"]'::jsonb, 'Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) with or without intravesical therapy (BCG or chemotherapy)', 'Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer is removed endoscopically by transurethral resection (TURBT). Intravesical BCG or chemotherapy is added to reduce the risk of recurrence.'),
('62977ce5-c827-509c-b6c8-30eaf0ffd2d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Muscle-invasive bladder cancer (MIBC) is treated with:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Transurethral resection (TURBT) only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Observation only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Radical cystectomy (removal of the bladder) with or without chemotherapy and radiation therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Radical cystectomy (removal of the bladder) with or without chemotherapy and radiation therapy', 'Muscle-invasive bladder cancer requires radical cystectomy with urinary diversion, usually combined with neoadjuvant chemotherapy. TURBT alone and observation are inadequate once the muscle layer is invaded.'),
('dbafa6b2-2d24-577a-b0e6-27669bf2b979'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder can be replaced by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","An ileal conduit (a segment of the ileum is used to create a new tube to drain urine to the skin)","A gastric conduit mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A colonic conduit leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An ileal conduit (a segment of the ileum is used to create a new tube to drain urine to the skin)', 'After cystectomy, urinary diversion commonly uses an ileal conduit, in which a short segment of ileum drains urine to an abdominal stoma. The intestinal segment is isolated and bowel continuity is restored separately.'),
('ec6e8ba4-e8dc-5d11-b999-c35b0a6fbbb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'An ileal conduit requires:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A stoma (an opening on the skin) where the ileum is brought to the surface to drain urine into a collection bag","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","stoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A different diversion method leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A stoma (an opening on the skin) where the ileum is brought to the surface to drain urine into a collection bag', 'An ileal conduit brings the isolated ileal segment to the skin as a stoma, so urine drains continuously into an external collection bag. There is no internal storage of urine.'),
('039908b7-ec21-5bcc-9c1e-e7e2defdd4b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A continent urinary diversion (e.g., an Indiana pouch) is:', '["A procedure that does not require any catheterization resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A conduit that drains urine to the skin (a stoma) without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A surgical construction of an internal reservoir using a segment of the intestine (the urine is stored in the reservoir and drained by self-catheterization)"]'::jsonb, 'A surgical construction of an internal reservoir using a segment of the intestine (the urine is stored in the reservoir and drained by self-catheterization)', 'A continent diversion such as the Indiana pouch constructs an internal reservoir from a segment of intestine and incorporates a continence mechanism, allowing urine storage. The patient empties the reservoir by intermittent self-catheterization rather than wearing an external bag.'),
('cf478c1c-e15b-5b98-9e8d-3b1b19bbda1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'An orthotopic neobladder is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","A bladder that is placed in a different location accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A bladder constructed from a different material without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A new bladder constructed from a segment of the intestine and placed in the same position as the original bladder (allows normal voiding through the urethra)"]'::jsonb, 'A new bladder constructed from a segment of the intestine and placed in the same position as the original bladder (allows normal voiding through the urethra)', 'An orthotopic neobladder is a reservoir fashioned from intestine and placed in the normal bladder position, anastomosed to the urethra. It permits volitional voiding through the native urethra when sphincter function is preserved.'),
('6595a3fc-fa2e-5158-9c31-832800f70891'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A Bricker loop is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","A neobladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","A continent urinary diversion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Another name for an ileal conduit (the most common type of urinary diversion)"]'::jsonb, 'Another name for an ileal conduit (the most common type of urinary diversion)', 'A Bricker loop is the classic eponym for an ileal conduit, the most widely used incontinent urinary diversion. It is not a neobladder or a continent reservoir.'),
('11f487a4-c451-58fa-bd45-ce1cbcfe928b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The urethra can be reconstructed by:', '["Uretherectomy (removal of the urethra) resulting in decreased functional activity","Observation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urethroplasty (surgical reconstruction of the urethra for a stricture)"]'::jsonb, 'Urethroplasty (surgical reconstruction of the urethra for a stricture)', 'Urethroplasty is the surgical reconstruction of the urethra, typically used to excise and repair a stricture. It restores urethral caliber and normal voiding.'),
('49aba5f0-8286-5f1a-b6bd-8fb3502d2df0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder can be repaired by:', '["Cystorrhaphy (surgical repair of the bladder)","Bladder replacement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Observation only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cystorrhaphy (surgical repair of the bladder)', 'Cystorrhaphy is the operative repair of a bladder wound or tear, most often performed after traumatic rupture. Observation alone is insufficient for full-thickness injuries.'),
('44e33637-eca0-549e-86c3-de508b9208b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder neck is an important site for:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","The detrusor muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The external urethral sphincter mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The internal urethral sphincter (smooth muscle, involuntary)"]'::jsonb, 'The internal urethral sphincter (smooth muscle, involuntary)', 'The bladder neck contains the internal urethral sphincter of smooth muscle under involuntary autonomic control, which maintains urinary continence. The external sphincter is striated and lies in the urogenital diaphragm.'),
('8f84be28-9d71-5590-81ff-9c1a0c7f0558'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is inferior to the:', '["Liver resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Peritoneum (the bladder is covered by peritoneum on its superior surface)","Diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneum (the bladder is covered by peritoneum on its superior surface)', 'The bladder lies immediately below the peritoneum, which covers only its superior surface. The remaining surfaces are extraperitoneal, allowing suprapubic access without entering the peritoneal cavity.'),
('223e1e7e-5377-597a-a6c1-5d8f36bfc24b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is posterior to the:', '["Rectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Uterus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pubic symphysis (separated by the retropubic space of Retzius)"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis (separated by the retropubic space of Retzius)', 'The bladder lies behind the pubic symphysis, separated from it by the retropubic space of Retzius. This space is a common site for hematoma after pelvic fractures.'),
('45117f54-26f4-5e6c-ba66-bda05ce371e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is anterior to the:', '["Rectum (in males) or the vagina and cervix (in females)","Pubic symphysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Peritoneum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ureter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum (in males) or the vagina and cervix (in females)', 'In males the bladder lies anterior to the rectum. In females it lies anterior to the vagina and cervix, so this anterior relationship must be considered during pelvic surgery and examination.'),
('0110cfdd-1813-5a23-af62-db41474c3dd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is superior to the:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Rectum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ureter mediated by specific receptor activation","Prostate (in males) or the urogenital diaphragm (in females)","Pubic symphysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate (in males) or the urogenital diaphragm (in females)', 'The bladder neck rests on the prostate in males and on the urogenital diaphragm in females. These structures form the support beneath the bladder.'),
('c596da3a-46d1-520a-9aca-64bd395b583b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder can be imaged with:', '["Only ultrasound resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Only X-ray without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ultrasound (assessment of bladder volume and wall thickness), CT, MRI, or cystography"]'::jsonb, 'Ultrasound (assessment of bladder volume and wall thickness), CT, MRI, or cystography', 'Ultrasound reliably assesses bladder volume, wall thickness, and post-void residual, while CT, MRI, and cystography (contrast studies) provide complementary structural and functional evaluation. Restricting imaging to any single modality would be incomplete.'),
('9e3c3ae8-7fa2-5719-b00f-34adf45f327a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A cystogram is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","An X-ray of the urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A contrast X-ray study of the bladder (contrast is injected into the bladder through a catheter)","An X-ray of the kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A contrast X-ray study of the bladder (contrast is injected into the bladder through a catheter)', 'Cystography opacifies only the bladder lumen after contrast is instilled through a urethral catheter. It does not image the kidney and does not primarily target the urethra, which distinguishes it from other contrast studies.'),
('75809753-d376-5aac-97a3-bb382364a97d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A voiding cystourethrogram (VCUG) is:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","An X-ray of the kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A contrast X-ray study of the bladder (and urethra) during the act of voiding (to assess for vesicoureteric reflux)","An X-ray of the ureter without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A contrast X-ray study of the bladder (and urethra) during the act of voiding (to assess for vesicoureteric reflux)', 'A voiding cystourethrogram combines contrast filling of the bladder with filming during micturition, demonstrating reflux into the ureters when the vesicoureteral valve mechanism fails. It is not limited to the kidney or ureter alone.'),
('b9dd0940-303c-5a39-a8db-b19441a893b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'VUR is graded from:', '["There is grading system resulting in decreased functional activity","1 to 3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A to C mediated by specific receptor activation","I (mild) to V (severe - the ureter and the collecting system of the kidney are dilated)","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'I (mild) to V (severe - the ureter and the collecting system of the kidney are dilated)', 'The international grading of vesicoureteral reflux ranges from grade I (reflux confined to the ureter) to grade V (marked dilatation and tortuosity involving the whole collecting system). Grading on a 1-3 scale or A-C system is not used.'),
('379a464e-8e85-527b-951c-7edc55f63733'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'VUR is more common in:', '["Adults resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Children (especially girls) and can lead to recurrent urinary tract infections and pyelonephritis","It is equally common in all age groups without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elderly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Children (especially girls) and can lead to recurrent urinary tract infections and pyelonephritis', 'Vesicoureteral reflux is congenital and most frequent in infants and young girls, predisposing them to recurrent urinary tract infections and pyelonephritis. It is not a disorder of adults or the elderly.'),
('7a2772db-72a2-53c2-b9f7-e6b6b997e924'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'VUR can be treated with:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Observation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only antibiotics mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Antibiotics (prophylaxis), endoscopic injection of a bulking agent at the ureterovesical junction, or surgical reimplantation of the ureter","Only surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibiotics (prophylaxis), endoscopic injection of a bulking agent at the ureterovesical junction, or surgical reimplantation of the ureter', 'Management ranges from antibiotic prophylaxis to discourage infection, to endoscopic injection of a bulking agent at the ureterovesical junction, and surgical ureteric reimplantation for high-grade reflux. Observation alone or a single treatment modality does not cover the full therapeutic range.'),
('56e8d1f3-c71c-5cb6-903c-60e36e866e6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The ureterovesical junction (UVJ) is:', '["The point where the ureter meets the kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The point where the ureter enters the bladder (the normal UVJ has a flap-valve mechanism that prevents reflux)","The point where the ureter meets the urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The point where the ureter enters the bladder (the normal UVJ has a flap-valve mechanism that prevents reflux)', 'The ureter pierces the posterolateral bladder wall obliquely at the ureterovesical junction, where the flap-valve is compressed as the bladder fills and prevents reflux. This is not the site where the ureter meets the kidney or the urethra.'),
('12c9b606-2ffd-5904-a550-9b0c95d7f403'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The UVJ flap-valve mechanism is:', '["The oblique passage of the terminal ureter through the wall of the bladder (the ureter is compressed as the bladder fills, preventing VUR)","A one-way valve in the ureter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sphincter at the UVJ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The oblique passage of the terminal ureter through the wall of the bladder (the ureter is compressed as the bladder fills, preventing VUR)', 'The oblique intramural course of the terminal ureter allows bladder wall tension to compress the ureter as the bladder distends, preventing vesicoureteral reflux. This is a passive mechanical arrangement rather than a true one-way valve or sphincter.'),
('73f18414-918e-5e83-a84c-bcaf63133a4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The UVJ is the most common site for:', '["Strictures of the ureter resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The origin of ureteric cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The impaction of a ureteric stone (the ureter is narrowest at the UVJ)"]'::jsonb, 'The impaction of a ureteric stone (the ureter is narrowest at the UVJ)', 'The narrowest points of the ureter are the pelviureteric junction, the pelvic brim, and especially the ureterovesical junction, where stones most often impact. The ureter is not preferentially obstructed there by strictures or cancer.'),
('b9f8eab7-c810-5ef5-8dec-3a7cfe7db984'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A ureteric stone at the UVJ can cause:', '["Only hematuria resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe pain (renal colic) radiating to the groin, dysuria, frequency, and hematuria","Only frequency mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe pain (renal colic) radiating to the groin, dysuria, frequency, and hematuria', 'A stone impacted at the ureterovesical junction produces acute pain that radiates to the groin and causes dysuria, frequency, and hematuria from local mucosal irritation. The clinical picture involves more than pain or hematuria in isolation.'),
('0501b84f-df69-5599-863e-a8bf1b93e4a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is composed of:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only a single layer of muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Detrusor muscle (three bundles of smooth muscle: inner longitudinal, middle circular, and outer longitudinal)","Both smooth and skeletal muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor muscle (three bundles of smooth muscle: inner longitudinal, middle circular, and outer longitudinal)', 'The bladder wall is the detrusor, a smooth muscle arranged as inner longitudinal, middle circular, and outer longitudinal layers that contract to expel urine. It is not skeletal muscle and not a single contractile layer.'),
('288ed688-f19d-5c64-872a-b617ab0ad020'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The ureters pass obliquely through the bladder wall:', '["To create a flap-valve mechanism that prevents the reflux of urine from the bladder into the ureters","To allow easy passage of a stone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To prevent the flow of urine from the kidneys to the bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'To create a flap-valve mechanism that prevents the reflux of urine from the bladder into the ureters', 'The oblique passage of the terminal ureter through the bladder wall enables the intramural segment to be compressed during bladder filling, preventing retrograde flow of urine toward the kidneys. Facilitating stone passage or blocking flow from the kidney are not its purpose.'),
('deb25111-c78f-5915-a88f-f0e61d734264'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder base is firmly attached to:', '["The pubic bone resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The rectum (in males) or the vagina (in females) by connective tissue","The levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The prostate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The rectum (in males) or the vagina (in females) by connective tissue', 'The bladder base is tethered posteriorly to the rectum in males and to the vagina in females by connective tissue. It is not fixed to the pubic bone, levator ani, or prostate.'),
('83ecb5d1-4f47-5111-b58f-6e4525ddd158'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder neck is attached to:', '["The prostate (in males) or the urogenital diaphragm (in females)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The pubic bone mediated by specific receptor activation","The levator ani without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The rectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The prostate (in males) or the urogenital diaphragm (in females)', 'The bladder neck is anchored inferiorly to the prostate in males and to the urogenital diaphragm in females. This attachment differs from that of the base, the pubic bone, or the rectum.'),
('453c602c-c1da-54f1-9b98-94257ffbb3bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is supported by:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the pelvic diaphragm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the puboprostatic ligaments mediated by specific receptor activation","The puboprostatic (pubovesical) ligaments and the pelvic diaphragm","The abdominal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The puboprostatic (pubovesical) ligaments and the pelvic diaphragm', 'The bladder is supported principally by the puboprostatic (male) or pubovesical (female) ligaments anteriorly and by the pelvic diaphragm inferiorly. Support does not come from the abdominal muscles or from either structure acting alone.'),
('6c595227-8eb4-5a9d-b8db-94b3399fede2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The puboprostatic ligaments in males connect:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","The bladder to the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The bladder to the rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The prostate and the bladder neck to the pubic bone"]'::jsonb, 'The prostate and the bladder neck to the pubic bone', 'The puboprostatic ligaments pass from the prostate and bladder neck to the posterior surface of the pubic bone, anchoring the bladder outlet. They do not attach to the sacrum or rectum.'),
('ffd87284-5ee1-57ec-a694-b7d81adfeb51'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The pubovesical ligaments in females connect:', '["The bladder neck to the pubic bone","The bladder to the uterus","None","All","The bladder to the sacrum"]'::jsonb, 'The bladder neck to the pubic bone', 'The pubovesical ligaments are the female counterpart of the puboprostatic ligaments, anchoring the bladder neck to the pubic bone. They do not connect the bladder to the uterus or sacrum.'),
('b0b778c8-165f-54bb-ad15-8a1731e5c9c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is most vulnerable to injury when:', '["It is empty resulting in decreased functional activity","It is partially full accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is normal mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It is distended (full) - a blunt blow to the lower abdomen can rupture a distended bladder"]'::jsonb, 'It is distended (full) - a blunt blow to the lower abdomen can rupture a distended bladder', 'When distended the bladder rises above the pelvic brim into the abdomen, where a blunt blow can rupture it with intraperitoneal spillage of urine. An empty bladder is protected inside the bony pelvis.'),
('3474e7ad-e3a8-57cf-bd78-0a0b350d48c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is the most common organ injured in:', '["Pelvic fractures (especially fractures of the pubic rami that can lacerate the bladder)","Chest trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abdominal trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic fractures (especially fractures of the pubic rami that can lacerate the bladder)', 'Bladder rupture is a common complication of pelvic fractures, particularly fractures of the pubic rami whose displaced fragments can lacerate the bladder wall. This pattern is not seen with chest trauma, head injury, or abdominal trauma alone.'),
('1931c2a3-d132-5cd2-b5f9-ac498f2c1240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A fall onto a blunt or penetrating object can also cause:', '["A bladder rupture (especially if the bladder is distended)","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All mediated by specific receptor activation","A bladder injury only if the bladder is empty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","injury to the bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A bladder rupture (especially if the bladder is distended)', 'A direct blunt or penetrating injury can rupture the bladder, and a full bladder is at far greater risk because it is thin-walled and extends above the pelvis. Rupture is less likely when the bladder is empty.'),
('673fbf25-ebee-5da2-b74c-ed2331a26a0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is innervated by the pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic) to cause:', '["Detrusor muscle relaxation","All","Detrusor muscle contraction (for voiding)","Contraction of the internal urethral sphincter","None"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor muscle contraction (for voiding)', 'Parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 reach the bladder through the pelvic splanchnic nerves and drive detrusor contraction for voiding. They do not relax the detrusor or contract the internal urethral sphincter.'),
('6c7364e7-7382-5b3b-8441-1f5a2f547ed4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The bladder is innervated by the hypogastric nerve (sympathetic) to cause:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Detrusor relaxation and internal urethral sphincter contraction (for storage)","Detrusor contraction mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urethral relaxation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor relaxation and internal urethral sphincter contraction (for storage)', 'Sympathetic fibers from T11-L2 running in the hypogastric nerves relax the detrusor and contract the internal urethral sphincter, favoring urine storage. The opposite effects belong to the parasympathetic system of micturition.'),
('2aceb268-30f3-5e7d-af25-551ad6c84f1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The pons has a micturition center that:', '["Stimulates micturition resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coordinates the bladder and the sphincters for voiding","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suppresses micturition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coordinates the bladder and the sphincters for voiding', 'The pontine micturition center integrates detrusor contraction with coordinated sphincter relaxation so that voiding is efficient. It does not act simply to stimulate or to suppress micturition in isolation.'),
('215b97ae-36f8-533c-b58b-0400d1ef56cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The cortical centers (frontal lobe) can:', '["Only initiate micturition resulting in decreased functional activity","Only inhibit micturition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","All without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Voluntarily initiate or inhibit micturition (through the pontine center)"]'::jsonb, 'Voluntarily initiate or inhibit micturition (through the pontine center)', 'The frontal cortex exerts voluntary control over voiding, either permitting or suppressing the micturition reflex through the pontine center. It is not restricted to initiation or to inhibition alone.'),
('548ed0d1-d37d-5500-a23d-c18954cd6f0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A lesion of the sacral spinal cord causes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An upper motor neuron (UMN) bladder (spastic, with urge incontinence) mediated by specific receptor activation","change in bladder function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lower motor neuron (LMN) bladder (flaccid, acontractile, with overflow incontinence)"]'::jsonb, 'A lower motor neuron (LMN) bladder (flaccid, acontractile, with overflow incontinence)', 'Destruction of the sacral micturition center removes the reflex arc and produces a flaccid, acontractile bladder that fills to overcapacity and leaks by overflow incontinence. Spastic bladder behavior follows injury above the sacral center.'),
('34d39e57-3a89-5331-ad6f-004f0c87d3cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'A lesion of the spinal cord above the sacral micturition center causes:', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","All accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An upper motor neuron (UMN) bladder (spastic, hyperreflexic, with urge incontinence)","change in bladder function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lower motor neuron (LMN) bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An upper motor neuron (UMN) bladder (spastic, hyperreflexic, with urge incontinence)', 'Injury above the sacral micturition center leaves the sacral reflex arc intact but free from descending inhibition, yielding a spastic, hyperreflexic bladder with urge incontinence. A flaccid bladder follows damage at the sacral segments.'),
('b7a5902d-2a54-53b2-a2e7-225635b454e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Damage to the frontal lobe can cause:', '["All resulting in decreased functional activity","Flaccid bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None mediated by specific receptor activation","Spastic bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of the voluntary control of urination (uninhibited bladder)"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of the voluntary control of urination (uninhibited bladder)', 'The frontal lobe normally inhibits the micturition reflex, so frontal damage removes voluntary suppression and produces an uninhibited bladder with incontinence. A spastic or flaccid bladder reflects spinal rather than cortical lesions.'),
('a427dba5-a427-57e9-abbe-dbfea15c15bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The sensory fibers from the bladder that detect stretch and fullness travel:', '["Through the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) to the spinal cord","Through the pudendal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the femoral nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Through the hypogastric nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4) to the spinal cord', 'Stretch receptors in the bladder wall and mucosa relay through the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the S2-S4 spinal cord, where the micturition reflex is organized. They do not travel by pudendal, femoral, or hypogastric pathways.'),
('9cd55f18-e2a3-5938-a9d1-9f4754f28272'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The motor fibers to the detrusor muscle travel:', '["Through the hypogastric nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","None accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic, S2-S4) to the bladder","Through the pudendal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the femoral nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Through the pelvic splanchnic nerves (parasympathetic, S2-S4) to the bladder', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from S2-S4 travel in the pelvic splanchnic nerves to the bladder and synapse to contract the detrusor. Sympathetic hypogastric fibers relax the detrusor rather than contracting it.'),
('bc8286c7-9eec-56c4-b81a-3cb7e7da92ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'The motor fibers to the internal urethral sphincter travel:', '["Through the femoral nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Through the pudendal nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Through the pelvic splanchnic nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through the hypogastric nerves (sympathetic, T11-L2)"]'::jsonb, 'Through the hypogastric nerves (sympathetic, T11-L2)', 'Sympathetic fibers from T11-L2 run in the hypogastric nerves to contract the smooth internal sphincter and relax the detrusor during storage. They do not travel with the pelvic splanchnic, pudendal, or femoral nerves.'),
('1a8b317f-84cf-58b2-bd8d-940143169075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In Urinary bladder and urethra, which statement best fits the following fact: the urinary bladder is located?', '["In the anterior part of the pelvic cavity, posterior to the pubic symphysis","In the posterior pelvic cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Above the pelvic brim mediated by specific receptor activation","In the perineum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the abdominal cavity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the anterior part of the pelvic cavity, posterior to the pubic symphysis', 'The empty bladder lies in the anterior part of the true pelvis immediately behind the pubic symphysis and rises into the abdomen only as it fills. It is not located in the posterior pelvis, the perineum, or the abdominal cavity when empty.'),
('713a2304-3a7b-5a50-87bb-d994b6327995'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Regarding Urinary bladder and urethra, what is the correct answer to: the apex of the urinary bladder is attached to?', '["Uterus resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pubic symphysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Umbilicus via the median umbilical ligament (urachus)"]'::jsonb, 'Umbilicus via the median umbilical ligament (urachus)', 'The bladder apex is tethered to the umbilicus by the median umbilical ligament, the obliterated remnant of the fetal urachus. The uterus, rectum, and pubic symphysis are not attached to the apex.'),
('086c8555-1615-5ed8-b83f-5da5f2d2b754'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'For Urinary bladder and urethra, which option is most appropriate: the trigone of the urinary bladder is?', '["The area of the bladder dome resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","The neck of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A smooth triangular area on the posterior wall of the bladder between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The bladder base leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A smooth triangular area on the posterior wall of the bladder between the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice', 'The trigone is the smooth triangular area on the posterior bladder wall whose corners are the two ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice, and its mucosa is fixed to the underlying detrusor. The dome, neck, and base are separate bladder regions.'),
('fb08f0eb-1e79-51db-b0aa-f76a9800e08e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Which answer is correct in Urinary bladder and urethra when asked: the blood supply of the urinary bladder is from?', '["Renal artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior and inferior vesical arteries (from internal iliac)","Femoral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior mesenteric artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior vesical arteries (from internal iliac)', 'The superior and inferior vesical arteries, branches of the internal iliac artery, supply the urinary bladder. The renal, femoral, and inferior mesenteric arteries do not provide its blood supply.'),
('6a656c0c-329e-5ada-ad6e-410b31a31bd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In Urinary bladder and urethra, the best response to ''The male urethra is divided into which parts'' is:', '["None of the above","Preprostatic and prostatic only","Preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, spongy (penile)","Prostatic, membranous, cavernous","Only prostatic and membranous"]'::jsonb, 'Preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, spongy (penile)', 'The male urethra is classically divided into preprostatic, prostatic, membranous, and spongy (penile) parts. Listing only prostatic and membranous segments, or prostatic, membranous, and cavernous, is incomplete.'),
('e1a68efc-65d2-5d89-9f4d-f4a4a2f2ddbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'During revision of Urinary bladder and urethra, which of the following is correct: the female urethra is?', '["Located posterior to the vagina","Short (4 cm) and entirely in the pelvic cavity","None of the above","Long (20 cm)","Of equal length to male urethra"]'::jsonb, 'Short (4 cm) and entirely in the pelvic cavity', 'The female urethra is about 4 cm long and lies entirely within the true pelvis, opening in the vestibule just anterior to the vaginal orifice. It is far shorter than the male urethra and is not situated behind the vagina.'),
('7c447c7c-19c5-5ec8-a207-6d041305bb94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In Urinary bladder and urethra, which statement best fits the following fact: the external urethral sphincter is located?', '["At the external urethral orifice resulting in decreased functional activity","At the prostatic urethra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the deep perineal pouch (membranous urethra in males, mid-urethra in females)","At the bladder neck without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the deep perineal pouch (membranous urethra in males, mid-urethra in females)', 'The external urethral sphincter lies in the deep perineal pouch, surrounding the membranous urethra in males and the mid-urethra in females. It is not located at the bladder neck or the external urethral orifice.'),
('c3ceb2b2-456e-51fb-8c4e-b44288197546'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Regarding Urinary bladder and urethra, what is the correct answer to: the internal urethral sphincter is located?', '["At the external urethral orifice","In the membranous urethra","None of the above","At the bladder neck (smooth muscle, involuntary)","In the penile urethra"]'::jsonb, 'At the bladder neck (smooth muscle, involuntary)', 'The internal urethral sphincter is smooth muscle at the bladder neck that contracts involuntarily to retain urine. It is distinct from the voluntary external sphincter, which sits in the deep perineal pouch.'),
('ae4e1f14-6dab-5efa-bfe7-98fa68de1d3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'For Urinary bladder and urethra, which option is most appropriate: the ureters enter the bladder?', '["From the lateral wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Horizontally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Directly from above mediated by specific receptor activation","Obliquely through the posterolateral wall (trigone)","From the anterior wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obliquely through the posterolateral wall (trigone)', 'Each ureter pierces the posterolateral bladder wall obliquely to open at one corner of the trigone, an arrangement that prevents vesicoureteral reflux. The ureters do not enter horizontally or through the anterior wall.'),
('11ada89c-67d1-51c3-b950-00689ea5254d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Which answer is correct in Urinary bladder and urethra when asked: the pelvic course of the ureter in males is?', '["Runs directly to the bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Crosses the pelvic brim, runs along lateral pelvic wall, crosses above the vas deferens","Crosses below the vas deferens mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Runs between the rectum and bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses the pelvic brim, runs along lateral pelvic wall, crosses above the vas deferens', 'In males the ureter descends along the lateral pelvic wall and crosses above (cranial to) the vas deferens before reaching the bladder base. Passing below the vas deferens or running between the rectum and bladder is inaccurate.'),
('be4a70af-3c25-5625-b210-d03c233e1680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In Urinary bladder and urethra, the best response to ''The pelvic course of the ureter in females is'' is:', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Crosses the pelvic brim, runs in the base of the broad ligament, crosses under the uterine artery (water under the bridge)","Runs directly to the bladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Crosses above the uterine artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Runs in front of the ovary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crosses the pelvic brim, runs in the base of the broad ligament, crosses under the uterine artery (water under the bridge)', 'In females the ureter runs in the base of the broad ligament and passes beneath the uterine artery, recalled as water under the bridge. This relationship is a critical landmark during hysterectomy to avoid ureteric injury.'),
('17776e31-12a7-5e51-96ad-93ce10612512'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'During revision of Urinary bladder and urethra, which of the following is correct: the ureter is constricted at which three points?', '["All three are equally wide resulting in decreased functional activity","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pelviureteric junction, pelvic brim (crossing iliac vessels), vesicoureteric junction","At the kidney, bladder, and urethra without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the aorta, IVC, and pelvic inlet leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pelviureteric junction, pelvic brim (crossing iliac vessels), vesicoureteric junction', 'The three physiologic narrowings of the ureter where stones commonly impact are the pelviureteric junction, the pelvic brim where it crosses the iliac vessels, and the vesicoureteric junction. These sites are narrowed, not uniformly wide channels.'),
('d85a4dd2-b6ff-54e0-a0ff-a776de2719dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In Urinary bladder and urethra, which statement best fits the following fact: the bladder is related anteriorly to?', '["Pubic symphysis and retropubic space (space of Retzius)","None of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uterus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pubic symphysis and retropubic space (space of Retzius)', 'The anterior surface of the bladder faces the pubic symphysis and is separated from it by the retropubic space of Retzius. The sacrum, rectum, and uterus are posterior relations rather than anterior ones.'),
('b656a2ad-d3a1-5464-8a8b-407e38f920af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Regarding Urinary bladder and urethra, what is the correct answer to: the bladder is related posteriorly in males to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","All of the above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prostate only mediated by specific receptor activation","Urethra only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rectum, seminal vesicles, and vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'Rectum, seminal vesicles, and vas deferens', 'Posteriorly in males the bladder is related to the rectum, the seminal vesicles, and the terminal parts of the vas deferens. The prostate lies inferior to the bladder, not behind it.'),
('316a09c9-2685-529b-8922-6e69604c37de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'For Urinary bladder and urethra, which option is most appropriate: the bladder is related posteriorly in females to?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rarely mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectum only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior wall of vagina and cervix"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior wall of vagina and cervix', 'Posteriorly in females the bladder is related to the anterior wall of the vagina and to the cervix. The rectum lies behind the vagina and uterus rather than directly behind the bladder.'),
('fc70470a-734d-5b5a-82fc-c71c6d364991'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Which answer is correct in Urinary bladder and urethra when asked: the bladder is related superiorly to?', '["Spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peritoneum and coils of ileum (and uterus in females)"]'::jsonb, 'Peritoneum and coils of ileum (and uterus in females)', 'The superior surface of the bladder is covered by peritoneum and is related to coils of ileum, with the uterus lying above it in females. The spleen, stomach, and liver are abdominal organs situated far above the bladder.'),
('34020da7-48d2-57c6-8b7d-b23dc3748a01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In Urinary bladder and urethra, the best response to ''The bladder is related inferiorly in males to'' is:', '["None of the above","Prostate gland","Rectum","Seminal vesicles","Urethra"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate gland', 'In males the bladder neck and base rest upon the prostate gland, which forms the inferior relation. The rectum, seminal vesicles, and urethra are not the inferior relation of the bladder.'),
('d5e5e4c4-2281-558a-9cb6-ad5bb6c75674'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'During revision of Urinary bladder and urethra, which of the following is correct: the bladder neck (internal urethral orifice) is the?', '["None of the above resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior part of the bladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lowest part of the bladder, continuous with the urethra","Highest part of the bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior part of bladder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lowest part of the bladder, continuous with the urethra', 'The bladder neck is the funnel-shaped lowest portion of the bladder that continues into the urethra at the internal urethral orifice. It is neither the highest, the anterior, nor the posterior part of the bladder.'),
('e4f6d848-5614-5264-8221-ba2da058dbb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'In Urinary bladder and urethra, which statement best fits the following fact: the internal urethral sphincter is formed by?', '["Longitudinal muscle resulting in decreased functional activity","Circular smooth muscle at the bladder neck (involuntary)","Mucosal folds mediated by specific receptor activation","Skeletal muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None of the above leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circular smooth muscle at the bladder neck (involuntary)', 'The internal urethral sphincter is formed by circular smooth muscle of the detrusor at the bladder neck and contracts involuntarily. It is not skeletal muscle, longitudinal muscle, or mucosal folds.'),
('b03586ef-b704-538a-9daf-38e7b6ca20a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Gross Anatomy Pelvis and Perineum - Urinary Bladder Urethra', 'Regarding Urinary bladder and urethra, what is the correct answer to: the external urethral sphincter is formed by?', '["Longitudinal muscle in the membranous urethra","None of the above","Circular muscle in the penile urethra","Smooth muscle at the bladder neck","Skeletal (voluntary) muscle in the deep perineal pouch"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal (voluntary) muscle in the deep perineal pouch', 'The external urethral sphincter is skeletal (striated) muscle under voluntary control, located in the deep perineal pouch around the membranous urethra in males. Smooth muscle at the bladder neck forms the internal sphincter instead.'),
('388c1cc1-3861-580c-8ff3-6a124e83672f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Medulla oblongata contains which cranial nerve nuclei?', '["CN IX, X, XI, XII (plus VIII and lower V)","CN III, IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V, VI mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII, VIII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN I, II leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI, XII (plus VIII and lower V)', 'The medulla houses the nuclei of CN IX, X, XI, and XII along with the vestibular/cochlear nuclei and the spinal trigeminal complex. CN III and IV lie in the midbrain, CN V-VIII largely in the pons, and CN I and II are not brainstem structures.'),
('bbaf7c56-f6bd-5d56-bae1-da5d98616f60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pyramids of medulla contain', '["Cerebellar fibers","Descending corticospinal fibers","Ascending sensory fibers","Pontocerebellar fibers","Olivocerebellar fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Descending corticospinal fibers', 'The medullary pyramids are formed by the descending corticospinal tracts, which mostly decussate at the lower medulla. Cerebellar, ascending sensory, and olivocerebellar fibers do not pass through the pyramids.'),
('0b429090-59bf-50bb-9379-25a47f9fb297'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The olive (inferior olivary nucleus) is concerned with', '["Respiratory control","Pain modulation","Vision","Cerebellar motor coordination","Auditory processing"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar motor coordination', 'The inferior olivary nucleus sends climbing fibers through the inferior cerebellar peduncle to the cerebellum, contributing to motor learning and coordination. Its function is not respiratory, sensory, or visual.'),
('f1251d60-6ac5-554f-b8ea-7e537ba52626'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'What is found between the pyramid and olive in medulla?', '["Accessory (CN XI) nerve","Vagus (CN X) nerve roots","Trigeminal (CN V) nerve","Glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerve","Hypoglossal (CN XII) nerve rootlets"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal (CN XII) nerve rootlets', 'The hypoglossal nerve rootlets emerge in the anterolateral sulcus of the medulla, between the pyramid medially and the olive laterally. The other cranial nerves exit at different brainstem levels.'),
('9674d74c-2125-5c00-b783-598f66922e3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which nuclei are found in medulla?', '["Nucleus gracilis, cuneatus, inferior olive, solitary nucleus, dorsal vagus, hypoglossal","Red nucleus, substantia nigra accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pontine nuclei, trigeminal motor nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial nucleus, abducens nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlear nuclei only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus gracilis, cuneatus, inferior olive, solitary nucleus, dorsal vagus, hypoglossal', 'The medulla contains the dorsal column relay nuclei (gracilis and cuneatus), the inferior olive, solitary nucleus, dorsal motor nucleus of vagus, and hypoglossal nucleus. The red nucleus and substantia nigra are midbrain structures, while the pontine, trigeminal motor, facial, and abducens nuclei belong to the pons.'),
('0c3f76f0-7249-534a-8c35-26aa2468dc18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The sensory decussation (decussation of lemniscus) occurs in', '["Lower medulla (closed medulla)","Pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower medulla (closed medulla)', 'The internal arcuate fibers carrying conscious proprioception and discriminative touch cross the midline as the lemniscal decussation in the lower (closed) medulla. The pyramidal motor decussation occurs more caudally, at the cervicomedullary junction.'),
('623e24aa-a83d-5fef-ae41-bddac6764a5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The motor decussation (decussation of pyramids) occurs at', '["Pons resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervicomedullary junction (lower medulla)"]'::jsonb, 'Cervicomedullary junction (lower medulla)', 'The pyramids cross the midline at the junction of the medulla and spinal cord, forming the motor (pyramidal) decussation. This lies caudal to the lemniscal sensory decussation, which is more rostral in the lower medulla.'),
('900b163e-2913-58a3-9cf8-6091de421f60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which decussation occurs first (more rostral) in medulla?', '["Both at same level","Tectospinal decussation","Sensory decussation (lemniscal)","Motor decussation (pyramidal)","Rubrospinal decussation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory decussation (lemniscal)', 'In the caudal medulla the lemniscal (sensory) decussation lies rostral to the pyramidal (motor) decussation, so sensory fibers cross first and higher. The tectospinal and rubrospinal tracts decussate in the midbrain.'),
('0dda63a0-4ab9-5087-aa35-ad2b5ff21536'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus ambiguus gives rise to', '["Parasympathetic fibers of CN III","Motor fibers of CN XII","Motor fibers of CN VII","Motor fibers of CN IX, X, XI (pharynx/larynx muscles)","Sensory fibers of CN V"]'::jsonb, 'Motor fibers of CN IX, X, XI (pharynx/larynx muscles)', 'The nucleus ambiguus provides branchial motor efferents that travel in CN IX, X, and the cranial part of XI to innervate the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles. It does not supply CN XII, VII, or parasympathetic fibers.'),
('0083399f-1003-5993-b034-c9ce5bac5742'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The dorsal motor nucleus of vagus (CN X) provides', '["Motor to pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste from posterior 1/3 tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers to thoracic/abdominal viscera","Sensory from epiglottis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor to laryngeal muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers to thoracic/abdominal viscera', 'The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus gives parasympathetic preganglionic fibers that innervate thoracic and abdominal organs. Motor supply to pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles comes from the nucleus ambiguus, and taste is carried to the solitary nucleus.'),
('4df1d040-5d4f-5f8d-b4d4-c520eb6e31a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus solitarius (solitary tract nucleus) receives', '["Visual input resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain from face mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste (VII, IX, X) and visceral sensation"]'::jsonb, 'Taste (VII, IX, X) and visceral sensation', 'The solitary tract nucleus receives gustatory and general visceral afferents carried by CN VII, IX, and X. It does not receive visual, auditory, or facial pain input.'),
('57c0b165-047a-5153-be12-c6183c86ca8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The hypoglossal nucleus (CN XII) is located in', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Medulla (floor of fourth ventricle)","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (floor of fourth ventricle)', 'The hypoglossal nucleus lies dorsally in the medulla, near the midline, where it forms the hypoglossal trigone in the floor of the fourth ventricle. It supplies the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue.'),
('c17bfbb9-6728-587c-87b3-887e6fd9b1b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which nucleus is responsible for respiratory rhythm generation?', '["Pontine respiratory center","Pre-Bötzinger complex in medulla","Midbrain periaqueductal gray","Thalamus","Spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-Bötzinger complex in medulla', 'The pre-Bötzinger complex in the ventrolateral medulla is the primary generator of the inspiratory rhythm. Pontine centers modulate the rhythm but do not generate it.'),
('7d0fa786-8119-523a-b548-e940bd9a1176'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The area postrema (chemoreceptor trigger zone) is located in', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla (floor of fourth ventricle, circumventricular organ)","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (floor of fourth ventricle, circumventricular organ)', 'The area postrema is a circumventricular organ in the floor of the fourth ventricle at the caudal medulla; its reduced blood-brain barrier allows it to sense blood-borne emetic stimuli. It is not located in the pons, midbrain, or diencephalon.'),
('0b7b64f4-8406-5656-9025-d75bac448ba3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Medullary damage (Wallenberg syndrome) typically presents with', '["Seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemiplegia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diplopia, ptosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertigo, dysphagia, hoarseness, ipsilateral ataxia, contralateral pain/temperature loss","Aphasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vertigo, dysphagia, hoarseness, ipsilateral ataxia, contralateral pain/temperature loss', 'Lateral medullary infarction affects vestibular, ambiguous, spinocerebellar, spinothalamic, and sympathetic pathways, producing vertigo, dysphagia, hoarseness, ipsilateral ataxia, and contralateral pain/temperature loss. Hemiplegia and aphasia are not typical features.'),
('d11a35f3-1cf1-5c10-bce0-35a23e63903b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Wallenberg syndrome results from occlusion of', '["Posterior cerebral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Basilar artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior cerebellar artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior inferior cerebellar artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) or vertebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) or vertebral artery', 'Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome follows occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery or the vertebral artery, which supply the dorsolateral medulla. The other listed arteries serve different brainstem and cerebellar territories.'),
('96d439a7-8ae8-5e76-9391-6fbaee67a71d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which structure is NOT affected in Wallenberg syndrome?', '["Sympathetic fibers (hypothalamospinal)","Inferior cerebellar peduncle","Spinal trigeminal nucleus","Spinothalamic tract","Corticospinal tract (pyramid)"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract (pyramid)', 'The lateral medullary lesion spares the ventrally located corticospinal tract, so limb strength is preserved. It damages the spinal trigeminal nucleus, spinothalamic tract, inferior cerebellar peduncle, and hypothalamospinal sympathetic fibers.'),
('4cbd856b-bacd-5c37-a8b5-6e015e99f47e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'In Wallenberg syndrome, ipsilateral loss of facial pain/temperature is due to damage of', '["Spinothalamic tract","Medial lemniscus","Facial nerve","Trigeminal nerve root","Spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal trigeminal nucleus and tract', 'The descending spinal trigeminal tract and nucleus carry pain and temperature from the ipsilateral face and lie in the dorsolateral medulla within the Wallenberg territory. Damage to the spinothalamic tract causes contralateral body pain/temperature loss instead.'),
('7eca9bf4-06d0-5f22-80e3-65c6e2d93f76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which sign is ipsilateral in Wallenberg syndrome?', '["Vibration loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of pain/temperature on body accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ataxia (inferior cerebellar peduncle damage)","Hearing loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor weakness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ataxia (inferior cerebellar peduncle damage)', 'Damage to the inferior cerebellar peduncle and spinocerebellar fibers produces ipsilateral limb ataxia in Wallenberg syndrome. Pain/temperature loss on the body is contralateral, and motor weakness is typically absent.'),
('a1c41577-b6e0-5ca9-9414-138858be6274'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Dysphagia and hoarseness in Wallenberg syndrome are due to damage of', '["Solitary nucleus","Trigeminal motor nucleus","Facial nucleus","Hypoglossal nucleus","Nucleus ambiguus (CN IX, X)"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus ambiguus (CN IX, X)', 'The nucleus ambiguus supplies the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles, so its involvement in a lateral medullary infarction produces dysphagia and hoarseness. The other listed nuclei do not control swallowing or phonation.'),
('eb303f04-b285-5fa9-8542-7923da7b0939'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pons is divided into', '["Basilar (ventral) part and tegmentum (dorsal)","Crus and tegmentum","Tectum and tegmentum","Pyramid and olive","Cerebral peduncle and tectum"]'::jsonb, 'Basilar (ventral) part and tegmentum (dorsal)', 'The pons is organized into a ventral basilar part containing pontine nuclei and descending fibers and a dorsal tegmentum containing cranial nerve nuclei and the reticular formation. ''Crus and tegmentum'' and ''tectum and tegmentum'' describe divisions of the midbrain.'),
('9dbfd287-4b92-50c2-8e65-4c31519c9638'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pontine nuclei are involved in', '["Motor output resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual processing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory relay mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebrocerebellar relay (corticopontocerebellar pathway)","Auditory processing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebrocerebellar relay (corticopontocerebellar pathway)', 'The pontine nuclei receive cortical input via corticopontine fibers and project through the middle cerebellar peduncle to the cerebellum, relaying cerebrocerebellar (movement planning) information. They do not relay sensory or visual signals.'),
('7a3cb7fc-ca57-582c-939d-9cec8c74f852'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The middle cerebellar peduncle contains', '["Pontocerebellar fibers (afferents to cerebellum)","Ascending sensory fibers","Olivocerebellar fibers","Cerebellar efferent fibers","Corticospinal fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Pontocerebellar fibers (afferents to cerebellum)', 'The middle cerebellar peduncle carries contralateral pontocerebellar fibers that bring cortical information into the cerebellum. The inferior peduncle carries olivocerebellar and spinocerebellar fibers, and the superior peduncle carries mainly efferent fibers.'),
('51624ace-c27d-50d4-b80e-4f9c51127997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which cranial nerve nuclei are located in the pons?', '["CN IX, X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN I, II accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN XI, XII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V (motor, principal sensory, spinal), VI, VII, VIII","CN III, IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (motor, principal sensory, spinal), VI, VII, VIII', 'The pons contains the trigeminal nuclei (motor, principal sensory, and rostral spinal), the abducens, facial, and vestibulocochlear nuclei. CN III and IV are midbrain nuclei, CN IX, X, XI, and XII are largely medullary, and CN I and II are not brainstem structures.'),
('f39fec90-db38-5c83-82b9-36262c9bc8d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The abducens nucleus (CN VI) is located in', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pontine tegmentum (floor of fourth ventricle)","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basilar pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine tegmentum (floor of fourth ventricle)', 'The abducens nucleus lies in the dorsal pontine tegmentum beneath the facial colliculus in the floor of the fourth ventricle. It is not located in the medulla, midbrain, or basilar pons.'),
('afa51c2c-7dbb-5544-b0d1-aec96507179e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'CN VI nerve fibers exit the brainstem at', '["Cerebellopontine angle","Pontomedullary junction (medial)","Medulla","Midbrain","Lateral pons"]'::jsonb, 'Pontomedullary junction (medial)', 'The abducens nerve emerges at the pontomedullary junction, just lateral to the pyramids. CN VII and VIII exit more laterally at the same junction, not at the midbrain or lateral pons.'),
('3de0bd63-4d8f-5f50-bd9e-ac5afd6bb36e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The facial nucleus (CN VII) is located in', '["Midbrain","Basilar pons","Pontine tegmentum","Medulla","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine tegmentum', 'The facial motor nucleus sits in the ventrolateral pontine tegmentum, and its fibers loop dorsally around the abducens nucleus. It is not located in the medulla, midbrain, or basilar pons.'),
('d66f1059-26e2-53aa-a430-b94d771cc27c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The facial nerve loops around which nucleus (internal genu)?', '["Hypoglossal nucleus","Cochlear nucleus","Facial nucleus itself","Abducens nucleus (CN VI)","Trigeminal motor nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Abducens nucleus (CN VI)', 'The facial nerve fibers course dorsally around the abducens nucleus, forming the internal genu and the facial colliculus in the floor of the fourth ventricle. The fibers arise from the facial nucleus rather than wrapping around it.'),
('87e99ca3-5256-5217-83e4-ba2265a912e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The superior olivary nucleus in pons is involved in', '["Balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auditory processing (sound localization)","Motor coordination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory processing (sound localization)', 'The superior olivary complex in the caudal pons is the first site of binaural convergence and localizes sound by comparing interaural timing and intensity differences. It is an auditory nucleus, not a visual, motor, or balance structure.'),
('dc6f0add-544a-5854-9e9e-8f142eb591cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pontine (medial) reticulospinal tract facilitates', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Extensor tone (anti-gravity)","Pain inhibition mediated by specific receptor activation","Flexor tone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor tone (anti-gravity)', 'The medial reticulospinal tract descends in the ventral funiculus and facilitates extensor (antigravity) tone in the limbs and trunk. The lateral reticulospinal tract has the opposite inhibitory effect, and the red nucleus facilitates flexor tone.'),
('c6c9655d-1db7-55f9-8b07-a979d0de2c67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Millard-Gubler syndrome (pontine lesion) involves', '["CN XII palsy with hemiplegia","CN IX, X palsy","CN VI and VII palsy with contralateral hemiplegia","CN V palsy","CN III palsy with contralateral ataxia"]'::jsonb, 'CN VI and VII palsy with contralateral hemiplegia', 'A ventral pontine lesion that damages the abducens and facial nerves along with the corticospinal tract produces ipsilateral CN VI and VII palsy with contralateral hemiplegia. This pattern does not involve CN III, V, IX, X, or XII.'),
('2ceb1017-1385-592f-8848-8a6a6392ecdb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Foville syndrome (pontine tegmentum lesion) includes', '["Only hemiplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN VII palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only gaze palsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Ipsilateral CN VI, VII palsy + ipsilateral conjugate gaze palsy + contralateral hemiplegia","Only CN VI palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral CN VI, VII palsy + ipsilateral conjugate gaze palsy + contralateral hemiplegia', 'A tegmental pontine infarction involving the abducens nucleus/PPRF, facial nerve fibers, and corticospinal tract produces ipsilateral CN VI and VII palsy, ipsilateral conjugate gaze palsy, and contralateral hemiplegia. The syndrome combines all these deficits, not just one.'),
('21d19194-c7b9-5270-aff9-75b9a0564c67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF) is concerned with', '["Accommodation","Blink reflex","Pupillary reflex","Vertical gaze","Horizontal conjugate gaze"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal conjugate gaze', 'The paramedian pontine reticular formation generates the burst signal for saccades toward the ipsilateral side and, with the abducens nucleus, coordinates horizontal conjugate gaze. It does not control vertical gaze, pupillary reflexes, or accommodation.'),
('d2e60c68-a088-52ad-b100-c1c68920665f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lesion of PPRF causes', '["Nystagmus","Vertical gaze palsy","Ipsilateral conjugate gaze palsy","Diplopia","Contralateral gaze palsy"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral conjugate gaze palsy', 'Because the PPRF drives horizontal gaze toward the ipsilateral side, its destruction abolishes conjugate eye movement toward the lesion, causing deviation of the eyes toward the intact side. It does not cause a vertical gaze palsy or contralateral gaze palsy.'),
('196048bd-ad7d-57fc-adc2-90d79594b217'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) motor nucleus is located in', '["Midbrain","Cerebellum","Pontine tegmentum","Basilar pons","Medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine tegmentum', 'The trigeminal motor nucleus lies in the dorsolateral pontine tegmentum and supplies the muscles of mastication. It is not located in the medulla, midbrain, or basilar pons.'),
('05ebfac7-3016-556e-a9b0-02b4c4dbc1d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The principal (main) sensory nucleus of trigeminal (CN V) receives', '["Proprioception from face","Taste","Pain and temperature from face","Discriminative touch from face","Motor signals"]'::jsonb, 'Discriminative touch from face', 'The principal sensory trigeminal nucleus processes fine discriminative touch from the face, analogous to the dorsal column pathway. Pain and temperature from the face are handled by the spinal trigeminal nucleus, and proprioception by the mesencephalic nucleus.'),
('9b6957c2-02b2-5c42-97e4-dea104b9c5c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The spinal trigeminal nucleus (CN V, VII, IX, X) receives', '["Taste resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor for mastication mediated by specific receptor activation","Touch from face without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain and temperature from face and oropharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature from face and oropharynx', 'The spinal trigeminal nucleus extends through the pons and medulla and receives pain and temperature from the face and oropharynx carried by CN V, VII, IX, and X. It does not process taste, touch, or motor signals.'),
('09a2bca1-2484-5caf-adc8-87999e10396a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The mesencephalic nucleus of trigeminal (CN V) contains', '["Pseudo-unipolar neurons for proprioception from jaw muscles","Sensory neurons for touch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomic neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pseudo-unipolar neurons for proprioception from jaw muscles', 'The mesencephalic nucleus of CN V is unique because its primary sensory neuron cell bodies lie within the CNS and subserve proprioception from the jaw muscles and teeth. It is not a motor, autonomic, or pain relay.'),
('72cc005b-b909-5573-91f2-c67cdb95aaac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The mesencephalic nucleus is the only CNS location where', '["Primary sensory neuron cell bodies are located within CNS","Glial cells predominate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomic neurons reside mediated by specific receptor activation","Secondary sensory neurons reside without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor neurons reside leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary sensory neuron cell bodies are located within CNS', 'Unlike all other primary sensory neurons, whose cell bodies sit in dorsal root or cranial ganglia, the somata of the mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus lie inside the CNS and provide jaw proprioception. It is not a site of autonomic, secondary sensory, or motor neurons.'),
('1f7c04cd-a135-559e-bc7e-db81d775c5e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The midbrain consists of', '["Pons and medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar peduncles only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cerebral peduncles mediated by specific receptor activation","Tectum (quadrigeminal plate), tegmentum, and cerebral peduncles","Tectum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tectum (quadrigeminal plate), tegmentum, and cerebral peduncles', 'The midbrain is organized into the dorsal tectum (superior and inferior colliculi), the central tegmentum (cranial nerve nuclei and red nucleus), and the ventral cerebral peduncles. The pons and medulla are separate regions, and the tectum alone is only part of the midbrain.'),
('985adf33-4d4f-5f72-9bc6-1c72b240ef05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Superior colliculi are concerned with', '["Auditory reflex","Pain modulation","Motor coordination","Visual reflex (head/eye turning)","Balance"]'::jsonb, 'Visual reflex (head/eye turning)', 'The superior colliculi receive retinal input and coordinate reflexive turning of the head and eyes toward visual stimuli. The inferior colliculi serve audition, and the other options do not describe tectal visual function.'),
('00a005c0-9202-56bd-be24-022ae30fe633'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Inferior colliculi are concerned with', '["Motor coordination","Balance","Pain modulation","Visual reflex","Auditory reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory reflex', 'The inferior colliculi are relay nuclei of the auditory pathway that mediate reflexive orientation to sound. They do not serve vision, balance, pain modulation, or motor coordination.'),
('0cf7053c-2e58-59c1-9e01-cdf16eca3d1e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cerebral peduncles (crus cerebri) contain', '["Reticular fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticospinal, corticobulbar, and corticopontine fibers","Sensory fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autonomic fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal, corticobulbar, and corticopontine fibers', 'The crus cerebri carries the descending corticospinal, corticobulbar, and corticopontine tracts. Sensory, cerebellar, and autonomic fibers do not pass through the crus.'),
('3b716f82-64cf-5799-a43f-f4abd0d18558'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Substantia nigra is divided into', '["Pars interna and pars externa resulting in decreased functional activity","Caudal and rostral parts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior parts mediated by specific receptor activation","Pars compacta (dopaminergic) and pars reticulata (GABAergic)","Medial and lateral parts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pars compacta (dopaminergic) and pars reticulata (GABAergic)', 'The substantia nigra consists of a dopamine-rich pars compacta that projects to the striatum and a GABAergic pars reticulata that outputs to the thalamus. Pars interna and pars externa are subdivisions of the globus pallidus, not the substantia nigra.'),
('617a6ee6-ca38-5352-960a-9fceba96aff9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Degeneration of substantia nigra pars compacta is associated with', '["Alzheimer disease","ALS","Huntington disease","Multiple sclerosis","Parkinson disease"]'::jsonb, 'Parkinson disease', 'Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the pars compacta underlies the motor signs of Parkinson disease, including bradykinesia, rigidity, and resting tremor. Alzheimer disease, Huntington disease, ALS, and multiple sclerosis involve different neuronal systems.'),
('4d5ac7aa-8e63-5273-89c7-6410b1a40751'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Red nucleus (nucleus ruber) is located in', '["Midbrain tegmentum","Cerebral peduncle","Thalamus","Substantia nigra","Tectum"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain tegmentum', 'The red nucleus is a large motor nucleus situated in the midbrain tegmentum, giving rise to the rubrospinal tract. It is not located in the cerebral peduncle, tectum, substantia nigra, or thalamus.'),
('ad39ab36-57b6-5866-8702-2c04c8d6bdcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Red nucleus is functionally involved in', '["Motor coordination (flexor tone facilitation)","Audition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor coordination (flexor tone facilitation)', 'The red nucleus and its rubrospinal tract facilitate flexor muscle tone, complementing the antigravity extensor influence of the medial reticulospinal tract. It is a motor structure, not a sensory, auditory, or visual relay.'),
('740db10f-75d3-5adc-b355-21ac7ec2263b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Oculomotor nucleus (CN III) is located in', '["Midbrain tegmentum (level of superior colliculus)","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basal ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain tegmentum (level of superior colliculus)', 'The oculomotor complex lies in the ventral midbrain tegmentum at the level of the superior colliculus, near the midline and anterior to the aqueduct. It is not located in the pons, medulla, cerebellum, or basal ganglia.'),
('1eea154a-ef92-5d57-a45e-ea7c9a7e815a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Edinger-Westphal nucleus (accessory CN III) provides', '["Sympathetic fibers to eye resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor to extraocular muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor to levator palpebrae mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory from eye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers for pupillary constriction and accommodation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers for pupillary constriction and accommodation', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus supplies parasympathetic preganglionic fibers that synapse in the ciliary ganglion to constrict the pupil and mediate accommodation. Sympathetic supply to the eye and motor to extraocular muscles arise from other sources.'),
('0c1d4c4b-413c-578b-98c7-fca52d5ca6a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Trochlear nucleus (CN IV) is located in', '["Midbrain tegmentum (level of inferior colliculus)","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain tegmentum (level of inferior colliculus)', 'The trochlear nucleus lies in the midbrain tegmentum at the level of the inferior colliculus and is the only cranial nerve nucleus to innervate the contralateral superior oblique muscle. It is not located in the pons, medulla, cerebellum, or thalamus.'),
('fb0d134b-de18-548c-9122-e7ab34f8213f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Trochlear nerve (CN IV) is unique because it', '["Is the only CN that is sensory","Is the only cranial nerve that exits from dorsal brainstem","Is the only CN that is purely motor","Is the only CN that has nucleus","Is the only CN that crosses"]'::jsonb, 'Is the only cranial nerve that exits from dorsal brainstem', 'The trochlear nerve is the only cranial nerve that emerges from the dorsal aspect of the brainstem, just caudal to the inferior colliculus; it is also the smallest and the only one whose fibers completely decussate. Purely motor nerves like CN III, IV, VI and XII are not unique in that regard, and other options describe features not exclusive or true for this nerve.'),
('bce976e7-8a18-5a62-b1ad-3c629095e8f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'CN III (oculomotor) exits the brainstem from', '["Lateral midbrain","Pontomedullary junction","Dorsal midbrain","Cerebellopontine angle","Interpeduncular fossa (midbrain ventral)"]'::jsonb, 'Interpeduncular fossa (midbrain ventral)', 'The oculomotor nerves emerge from the ventral midbrain within the interpeduncular fossa, a depression between the cerebral peduncles. The dorsal, lateral, and pontomedullary sites correspond to other cranial nerves or structures.'),
('c4bac5b5-d0fd-5e4a-a726-dc0e5c6dd8c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Weber syndrome (superior alternating hemiplegia) involves', '["CN XII palsy with hemiplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX palsy with ataxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI palsy with hemiplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III palsy with contralateral hemiplegia (midbrain lesion)","CN VII palsy with hemiplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III palsy with contralateral hemiplegia (midbrain lesion)', 'A ventral midbrain lesion injures the oculomotor nerve (producing ipsilateral CN III palsy) and the descending corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers in the crus cerebri, yielding contralateral hemiplegia. This pattern distinguishes Weber syndrome from the other named alternating hemiplegias.'),
('537af950-3e02-52d2-ba84-fd389bc41ce3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Benedikt syndrome (midbrain tegmentum lesion) involves', '["Only cerebellar ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN III palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN III palsy + hemiplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III palsy + contralateral ataxia/tremor (red nucleus + CN III)","Hemiplegia with ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III palsy + contralateral ataxia/tremor (red nucleus + CN III)', 'A tegmental midbrain lesion damages the oculomotor nerve fibers and the red nucleus, causing ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral ataxia, tremor, and choreiform movements. Hemiplegia is absent because the crus cerebri, which contains the pyramidal tract, is spared.'),
('68c8f268-bcfe-521e-a212-298464ba5b9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Weber syndrome is caused by occlusion of which artery?', '["Superior cerebellar artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior cerebral artery (PCA) or paramedian perforating branches","Anterior cerebral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior inferior cerebellar artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle cerebral artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cerebral artery (PCA) or paramedian perforating branches', 'Ventral midbrain infarction that produces Weber syndrome follows occlusion of the posterior cerebral artery or its paramedian penetrating branches, which supply the crus cerebri and CN III fascicles. The superior and anterior inferior cerebellar arteries feed the cerebellum, while the ACA and MCA serve the cerebrum.'),
('2a1dd9b8-a3e2-5deb-a253-869f534e7fd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which CN nucleus is located in the periaqueductal gray of midbrain?', '["CN V","CN VII","CN III and CN IV","CN VI","CN XII"]'::jsonb, 'CN III and CN IV', 'The oculomotor (CN III) and trochlear (CN IV) nuclei lie in the midbrain just ventral to the periaqueductal gray matter, surrounding the cerebral aqueduct. The motor nuclei of CN V, VI, VII, and XII are located in the pons and medulla, not the midbrain.'),
('523dd188-3785-567a-ad57-ffa54da281af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Periaqueductal gray (PAG) in midbrain is involved in', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain modulation (analgesia via opioid receptors)","Audition mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor coordination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain modulation (analgesia via opioid receptors)', 'The periaqueductal gray is the principal supraspinal center for descending pain inhibition, rich in opioid receptors and projecting to the rostral ventromedial medulla to dampen nociceptive transmission. Its stimulation produces analgesia rather than involvement in vision, hearing, taste, or coordination.'),
('c797e269-1c2e-52ba-9938-b12c1cdb52e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The reticular formation of brainstem is responsible for', '["Only reflexes resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor control accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sleep-wake cycle, consciousness, pain modulation, motor control","Only sensory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only autonomic function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sleep-wake cycle, consciousness, pain modulation, motor control', 'The brainstem reticular formation is a diffuse network with multiple functions, including regulation of the sleep-wake cycle and consciousness (through the ascending activating system), pain modulation, autonomic control, and motor coordination. Limiting it to a single role, such as reflexes or motor control alone, underestimates its integrative functions.'),
('1986ee4f-3eb1-55f5-b205-640e0788c107'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) is critical for', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Balance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hearing mediated by specific receptor activation","Consciousness and wakefulness","Motor coordination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Consciousness and wakefulness', 'The ARAS, formed by projections from the brainstem reticular formation to the thalamus and cortex, maintains arousal and consciousness. Lesions of the ARAS cause loss of wakefulness and coma, whereas vision, hearing, balance, and coordination depend on other pathways.'),
('237dfb16-2370-5502-9ac3-09f1e0fa8190'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lesions of the reticular formation cause', '["Paralysis only","Coma (loss of consciousness)","Sensory loss only","Visual loss","Ataxia only"]'::jsonb, 'Coma (loss of consciousness)', 'Damage to the reticular activating system within the brainstem disrupts the connections that sustain cortical arousal, producing impaired consciousness or coma. Isolated paralysis, sensory loss, blindness, or ataxia reflect damage to specific tracts rather than the reticular formation.'),
('5b8db162-60a5-5175-89f4-0abc4de46bc4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Central pontine myelinolysis is associated with', '["Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia","Rapid correction of hyponatremia","Hypokalemia","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid correction of hyponatremia', 'Central pontine myelinolysis arises when chronic hyponatremia is corrected too quickly, causing osmotic injury and demyelination of the basis pontis, often with quadriparesis and pseudobulbar palsy. Sodium excess, calcium disorders, potassium depletion, and hypoglycemia do not produce this entity.'),
('32664c26-842c-50b8-9c04-4765b2da3dee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which tract is NOT found in the medulla?', '["Medial lemniscus","Medial longitudinal fasciculus","Lateral lemniscus (auditory)","Spinothalamic tract","Posterior commissure fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior commissure fibers', 'The posterior commissure lies in the caudal diencephalon and rostral midbrain, not in the medulla, making it the tract absent from the medulla. The medial and lateral lemnisci, medial longitudinal fasciculus, and spinothalamic tract are all components of the medullary section.'),
('e7e94208-d379-5ff0-a7fe-ae0b5a00b7a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The lateral lemniscus (auditory pathway) is located in', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons and midbrain (lateral tegmentum)"]'::jsonb, 'Pons and midbrain (lateral tegmentum)', 'The lateral lemniscus carries auditory fibers rostrally through the lateral tegmentum of the pons to the midbrain, ending at the inferior colliculus. The medial lemniscus, not the lateral one, traverses the medulla, and the pathway does not pass through the cerebellum, spinal cord, or thalamus.'),
('562217aa-e54f-552c-96cf-5b90098c3699'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medial lemniscus in medulla is located', '["Ventrally resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffuse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Near midline (vertical orientation)","Dorsally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laterally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Near midline (vertical orientation)', 'After the internal arcuate fibers decussate in the medulla, the medial lemnisci ascend on either side of the midline as vertically oriented bundles near the raphe. It occupies this position at mid-medullary levels rather than ventrally, dorsally, laterally, or diffusely.'),
('1590120e-9c0e-5fb3-8c1c-04f3bf1381cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'In the pons, the medial lemniscus lies', '["Vertically in midline resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsally near fourth ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Laterally mediated by specific receptor activation","Transversely (horizontal band) in ventral tegmentum","Ventrally in basilar pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transversely (horizontal band) in ventral tegmentum', 'Within the pons the medial lemniscus rotates to form a flat horizontal band lying transversely across the ventral tegmentum, between the basilar part and the tegmental nuclei. Its orientation changes from the vertical column seen in the medulla as it ascends toward the midbrain.'),
('2bb83b5d-d079-5d0e-8ab5-75c55051ac0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'In the midbrain, the medial lemniscus lies', '["Medial to red nucleus","Ventral to substantia nigra","Lateral to cerebral aqueduct","In crus cerebri","Dorsolateral to red nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsolateral to red nucleus', 'As it reaches the midbrain, the medial lemniscus shifts laterally and dorsally, coming to lie dorsolateral to the red nucleus before ending in the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus. It does not enter the crus cerebri or lie medial to the red nucleus.'),
('def71533-558c-5bb2-9118-c8c79c7b72b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The spinothalamic tract is located', '["Medial resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffuse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventral mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral in medulla, pons, and midbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral in medulla, pons, and midbrain', 'Once it decussates in the spinal cord, the lateral spinothalamic tract ascends along the lateral aspect of the medulla, pons, and midbrain until it reaches the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus. This lateral position is constant throughout the brainstem, unlike the medially shifting lemnisci.'),
('1dbb8269-272d-5a3c-9eef-a574586dbd74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which structure contains the facial colliculus on floor of fourth ventricle?', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons (facial nerve loop around CN VI nucleus)","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pons (facial nerve loop around CN VI nucleus)', 'The facial colliculus is a rounded elevation in the lower pons on the floor of the fourth ventricle, formed by the genu of the facial nerve looping around the abducens nucleus. It therefore belongs to the pons, not the medulla, midbrain, thalamus, or cerebellum.'),
('b118020b-f7eb-58be-a634-6a3eb0b19949'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The hypoglossal trigone on floor of fourth ventricle overlies', '["Hypoglossal (CN XII) nucleus","Facial nucleus","Trigeminal nucleus","Vagus nucleus","Abducens nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal (CN XII) nucleus', 'In the open medulla the hypoglossal nucleus lies beneath the midline floor of the fourth ventricle, producing a prominence called the hypoglossal trigone. The abducens nucleus forms part of the facial colliculus, and the other listed nuclei occupy different regions of the brainstem.'),
('97d0cb14-b703-594e-8664-4cbce1c44dd4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The vagal trigone on floor of fourth ventricle overlies', '["Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus (CN X)","Hypoglossal nucleus","Nucleus ambiguus","Solitary nucleus","Spinal trigeminal nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus (CN X)', 'The vagal trigone is a prominence on the floor of the fourth ventricle overlying the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus in the medulla, located just lateral to the hypoglossal trigone. The nucleus ambiguus and solitary nucleus lie deeper and do not raise this trigone.'),
('0fdc4d38-e9d4-512e-b266-ae74dc5bc38d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The calamus scriptorius is the', '["Lateral recess","Roof of fourth ventricle","Lower part of fourth ventricle floor in medulla","Central canal opening","Upper part of fourth ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Lower part of fourth ventricle floor in medulla', 'The calamus scriptorius describes the tapering, pen-nib shaped lower portion of the rhomboid fossa on the medullary part of the fourth ventricle floor, bounded by the gracile and cuneate tubercles. It is the inferior segment of the floor, not the roof, recess, or upper portion.'),
('02613898-417d-5e23-869a-b963371324f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Obex is located at', '["Foramen of Magendie resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramen of Luschka accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral recess mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior angle of fourth ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferior angle of fourth ventricle (opening into central canal)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior angle of fourth ventricle (opening into central canal)', 'The obex is the inferior angle of the fourth ventricle, where the cavity narrows and becomes continuous with the central canal of the spinal cord. It marks the caudal limit of the ventricle rather than the foramina of Magendie or Luschka or the superior angle.'),
('2294cb45-038e-5e08-8d12-2731abaa5281'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The foramen of Luschka is', '["Midline opening","Opening into third ventricle","Opening into spinal canal","Opening into cerebral aqueduct","Lateral aperture of fourth ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral aperture of fourth ventricle', 'The foramen of Luschka is the paired lateral aperture of the fourth ventricle, opening from the lateral recess into the subarachnoid space at the cerebello-pontine angle. It is not a midline structure and does not communicate with the third ventricle, aqueduct, or spinal canal.'),
('47bc5356-2928-5d20-9907-fe589a9dc129'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The foramen of Magendie is', '["Lateral aperture","Aqueduct opening","Opening into third ventricle","Spinal canal opening","Median aperture of fourth ventricle (midline)"]'::jsonb, 'Median aperture of fourth ventricle (midline)', 'The foramen of Magendie is the unpaired median aperture of the fourth ventricle, located in the midline of its roof and opening into the cisterna magna. The paired lateral apertures are the foramina of Luschka, while the aqueduct is the connection to the third ventricle.'),
('8cfb7b09-adb9-5a4e-96c4-74f39c67ad3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lateral recess of fourth ventricle extends to', '["Medulla","Midbrain","Cerebellopontine angle","Cerebral aqueduct","Pons"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellopontine angle', 'Each lateral recess of the fourth ventricle curves laterally over the inferior cerebellar peduncle and opens into the subarachnoid space at the cerebello-pontine angle through the foramen of Luschka. It therefore terminates at the cerebello-pontine angle rather than in the medulla, pons, or midbrain.'),
('4d54b30c-3340-5d49-b73e-c8c9091d27a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The rhomboid fossa is the floor of', '["Lateral ventricle","Fourth ventricle","Central canal","Third ventricle","Cerebral aqueduct"]'::jsonb, 'Fourth ventricle', 'The rhomboid fossa is the diamond-shaped floor of the fourth ventricle, formed by the dorsal pons and open medulla and covered by the cerebellum. The third and lateral ventricles and other structures have distinct floors and do not form a rhomboid fossa.'),
('51661610-eb22-5139-8518-5e21c0d0e72c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which CN emerges from the dorsolateral sulcus of medulla?', '["CN IX, X, XI (glossopharyngeal, vagus, cranial accessory)","CN VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN XII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VIII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI (glossopharyngeal, vagus, cranial accessory)', 'The glossopharyngeal, vagus, and cranial part of the accessory nerve emerge in a vertical line from the dorsolateral sulcus of the medulla, between the olive and the inferior cerebellar peduncle. CN XII exits from the ventrolateral sulcus (anterior to the olive), while the others arise at other brainstem sites.'),
('e0ac7cf3-484b-508e-89e3-886f767f4b5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'CN VIII (vestibulocochlear) emerges at', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pons mid-dorsal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pontomedullary junction (lateral)"]'::jsonb, 'Pontomedullary junction (lateral)', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve leaves the brainstem at the lateral part of the pontomedullary junction, lateral to the facial nerve, entering the internal acoustic meatus. The facial and abducens nerves are its rostral neighbors at this junction, whereas the midbrain and dorsal pons do not give rise to it.'),
('89be4d33-ac32-58d3-93c4-8deeb03d9428'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cerebellopontine angle contains which nerves?', '["CN IX, X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III, IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V, VI mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII, VIII (facial, vestibulocochlear)","CN XI, XII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII, VIII (facial, vestibulocochlear)', 'The cerebello-pontine angle, formed at the junction of the pons, medulla, and cerebellum, contains the facial (CN VII) and vestibulocochlear (CN VIII) nerves as they traverse the internal acoustic meatus. CN IX and X lie more caudally along the medulla, and the other listed nerves attach elsewhere on the brainstem.'),
('00552771-2262-5195-b2ed-ade6f82fe328'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'CPA tumors (e.g., acoustic neuroma) typically cause', '["Visual loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Diplopia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aphasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hearing loss, tinnitus, facial numbness, vertigo","Hemiplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing loss, tinnitus, facial numbness, vertigo', 'Vestibular schwannomas in the cerebello-pontine angle compress CN VIII and adjacent CN VII and V, producing ipsilateral hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo, facial numbness or weakness, and rarely ataxia. Visual loss, diplopia, aphasia, and hemiplegia reflect involvement of the cerebrum or brainstem elsewhere.'),
('42146878-6fd7-55dc-a711-1b1fbf666866'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The respiratory centers in the brainstem include', '["Only in medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only in midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal respiratory group (DRG) and ventral respiratory group (VRG) in medulla; pontine centers","Only in cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal respiratory group (DRG) and ventral respiratory group (VRG) in medulla; pontine centers', 'Breathing is driven by the dorsal and ventral respiratory groups in the medulla, which generate the basic rhythm, and modulated by the pontine pneumotaxic and apneustic centers. No respiratory center exists in the midbrain or cerebellum.'),
('4a355bf4-d1cf-552d-9838-77f66c58f1c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pneumotaxic center (pontine respiratory center) is located in', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nucleus parabrachialis (dorsolateral pons)"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus parabrachialis (dorsolateral pons)', 'The pneumotaxic center lies in the dorsal pons, within the nucleus parabrachialis and adjacent Kölliker-Fuse nucleus, and acts to limit inspiration. The medullary DRG and VRG generate the respiratory rhythm, while the midbrain, thalamus, and cerebellum contain no such rhythm-modifying center.'),
('aa14c6b3-b40e-5e8c-95ad-bc01f1ff00cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The apneustic center (lower pons) causes', '["Cheyne-Stokes breathing resulting in decreased functional activity","Prolonged inspiration (apneusis) if pneumotaxic center is damaged","Prolonged expiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Apnea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tachypnea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged inspiration (apneusis) if pneumotaxic center is damaged', 'The apneustic center in the lower pons promotes sustained inspiration; when the pneumotaxic center that normally inhibits it is damaged, apneusis (prolonged inspiratory cramp) results. Cheyne-Stokes breathing, apnea, tachypnea, and prolonged expiration indicate other respiratory patterns and sites.'),
('477373b0-fd4a-50e5-8d79-ffac7cf933ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Biot respiration is associated with', '["Cerebellar damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain damage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Damage to medullary respiratory centers (increased intracranial pressure)","Pontine damage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical damage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Damage to medullary respiratory centers (increased intracranial pressure)', 'Biot (ataxic) breathing, characterized by irregular groups of breaths with pauses, indicates injury to the medullary respiratory centers, often from raised intracranial pressure or posterior fossa disease. Cerebellar, midbrain, pontine, and cortical lesions produce other respiratory patterns or none.'),
('89bafc35-9ff4-5a75-987e-b948d150e1d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cheyne-Stokes respiration is associated with', '["Spinal cord lesions resulting in decreased functional activity","Pontine lesions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain lesions mediated by specific receptor activation","Medullary lesions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral hemispheric or diencephalic lesions"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral hemispheric or diencephalic lesions', 'Cheyne-Stokes respiration, a crescendo-decrescendo pattern of breathing with apnea, appears with bilateral hemispheric or diencephalic lesions, often in heart failure or metabolic encephalopathy. Pontine and medullary injuries instead produce apneustic or ataxic patterns, and spinal cord and midbrain lesions do not generate this rhythm.'),
('5288c0d2-d0ed-5218-91ac-12cbbad0f698'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The locus coeruleus (in pons) produces', '["GABA resulting in decreased functional activity","Serotonin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acetylcholine mediated by specific receptor activation","Noradrenaline (norepinephrine)","Dopamine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Noradrenaline (norepinephrine)', 'The locus coeruleus in the rostral pons is the main brainstem source of noradrenaline, projecting widely to regulate arousal, attention, and mood. Serotonin comes from the raphe nuclei, dopamine from the substantia nigra and VTA, and acetylcholine from the basal forebrain and brainstem cholinergic groups.'),
('e194ca2e-c1f7-5e3b-a8b3-f72c23962786'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The raphe nuclei (midline brainstem) produce', '["Dopamine","Histamine","Noradrenaline","Acetylcholine","Serotonin (5-HT)"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin (5-HT)', 'The raphe nuclei along the brainstem midline synthesize serotonin and send ascending projections that modulate mood, sleep, and pain. Noradrenaline arises from the locus coeruleus, dopamine from midbrain dopaminergic groups, and histamine is mainly a hypothalamic product.'),
('54ba4f54-08d0-5784-8e85-5d2136d85010'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral tegmental area (VTA) in midbrain produces', '["Acetylcholine resulting in decreased functional activity","GABA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serotonin mediated by specific receptor activation","Noradrenaline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopamine (mesolimbic pathway)"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine (mesolimbic pathway)', 'The midbrain ventral tegmental area is a dopaminergic nucleus whose mesolimbic projections to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex mediate reward, motivation, and addiction. Serotonin, noradrenaline, GABA neurons, and cholinergic cells belong to other brainstem and forebrain groups.'),
('2b2788e9-1f22-5cc2-8231-85aa137e60d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert (basal forebrain) produces', '["Glutamate","Serotonin","Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Noradrenaline"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert in the basal forebrain projects cholinergic fibers to the cerebral cortex, and its degeneration contributes to cognitive decline in Alzheimer disease. Serotonin, dopamine, and noradrenaline arise from brainstem nuclei, while glutamate is the principal excitatory transmitter throughout the brain.'),
('1170d7cf-9474-559c-97c4-41001bb4271f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which cranial nerve exits from the dorsal midbrain?', '["Facial (CN VII)","Abducens (CN VI)","Oculomotor (CN III)","Trochlear (CN IV)","Trigeminal (CN V)"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlear (CN IV)', 'The trochlear nerve is the only cranial nerve to emerge from the dorsal surface of the brainstem, from the dorsal midbrain just below the inferior colliculus, after decussating. The oculomotor nerve exits ventrally, the trigeminal from the pons, and the facial and abducens from the lower pons.'),
('dc2ba1bb-6d26-5b5b-90b8-ae6db242aa09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The decussation of superior cerebellar peduncle occurs in', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain (below inferior colliculus)","Medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (below inferior colliculus)', 'Efferent fibers of the superior cerebellar peduncle, chiefly from the dentate nucleus, decussate in the ventral midbrain at the level below the inferior colliculus before reaching the red nucleus and thalamus. This crossing site lies in the midbrain, not in the cerebellum, pons, medulla, or thalamus.'),
('402b2da9-92a2-5072-a635-1ef9117fcae6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) coordinates', '["Auditory localization resulting in decreased functional activity","Balance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain modulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor coordination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conjugate eye movements (vestibulo-ocular reflex)"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugate eye movements (vestibulo-ocular reflex)', 'The MLF links the vestibular nuclei, abducens nucleus, and contralateral oculomotor nucleus to synchronize horizontal conjugate eye movements, especially during the vestibulo-ocular reflex. Auditory localization, balance alone, pain modulation, and general motor coordination rely on other pathways.'),
('5ea9b569-9f72-5964-92ae-36b0e9b28c27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) is due to', '["CN III lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","PPRF lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MLF lesion (impaired adduction of ipsilateral eye on contralateral gaze)","Cerebellar lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VI lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MLF lesion (impaired adduction of ipsilateral eye on contralateral gaze)', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia results from injury to the medial longitudinal fasciculus, producing failure of adduction of the ipsilateral eye on attempted lateral gaze to the opposite side, often with nystagmus of the abducting eye. A PPRF or CN VI lesion causes gaze palsy toward the lesion, and CN III disease gives a ptosis and limited adduction at rest.'),
('3a3c83cc-0b5b-592c-b649-ab988b6cc836'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The dorsal longitudinal fasciculus (Schutz bundle) connects', '["Superior colliculus to spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum to thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex to spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Red nucleus to spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothalamus to autonomic nuclei in brainstem and spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus to autonomic nuclei in brainstem and spinal cord', 'The dorsal longitudinal fasciculus (fasciculus of Schütz) carries fibers from the hypothalamus to autonomic and visceral relay nuclei in the brainstem and spinal cord, mediating autonomic responses. The tectospinal tract connects the superior colliculus to the cord, the dentatothalamic tract the cerebellum to the thalamus, and the rubrospinal tract the red nucleus to the cord.'),
('97631ea3-c289-5e25-991b-3d113997ec98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The central tegmental tract (CTT) connects', '["Spinal cord to thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Red nucleus to olivary nucleus (midbrain to medulla)","Cerebellum to red nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex to pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus to cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Red nucleus to olivary nucleus (midbrain to medulla)', 'The central tegmental tract runs from the red nucleus in the midbrain to the inferior olivary nucleus in the medulla; trophism hemi-hypertrophy of the olive appears with lesions of this tract. The spinothalamic tract runs spinal cord to thalamus, dentatorubral fibers connect cerebellum to red nucleus, corticopontine fibers the cortex to pons, and thalamocortical fibers the thalamus to cortex.'),
('b04516cf-3e58-5bc9-a116-c2700eef58e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The trapezoid body (auditory) is located in', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons (ventral acoustic stria decussation)","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pons (ventral acoustic stria decussation)', 'The trapezoid body is the ventral decussation of auditory fibers from the cochlear nuclei located in the pons; from it arise the lateral lemnisci and the superior olivary complex. The dorsal and intermediate acoustic striae lie near the floor of the fourth ventricle, but the trapezoid body proper is a ventral pontine structure.'),
('c5acbf58-adf9-53f7-ab7f-8cdaa69a65a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Auditory pathway: first-order neurons are in', '["Spiral ganglion (organ of Corti)","Cochlear nucleus","Superior olive","Medial geniculate body","Inferior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Spiral ganglion (organ of Corti)', 'The first-order auditory neurons are the bipolar cells of the spiral ganglion in the cochlea, whose central axons form the cochlear nerve and synapse in the cochlear nuclei. The cochlear nuclei, superior olive, inferior colliculus, and medial geniculate body are successively higher relay stations.'),
('aec42d0e-bca7-53ff-b040-2edfe10ec5d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Second-order auditory neurons (cochlear nuclei) project to', '["Inferior colliculus resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior olivary complex (some directly to inferior colliculus)","Medial geniculate body mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spiral ganglion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior olivary complex (some directly to inferior colliculus)', 'Axons of the cochlear nuclei run in the trapezoid body and acoustic striae to the superior olivary complex, with some fibers passing directly to the inferior colliculus. The medial geniculate body and auditory cortex lie further along the pathway, and the spiral ganglion is the source rather than a target.'),
('eb68e69c-99bb-50d9-914b-ebaec6e5e28e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The acoustic stria (dorsal, intermediate, ventral) carry', '["Visual fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory fibers from cochlear nuclei","Motor fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vestibular fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory fibers from cochlear nuclei', 'The dorsal, intermediate, and ventral acoustic striae, together with the trapezoid body, carry second-order auditory fibers from the cochlear nuclei toward the superior olive and lateral lemniscus. Visual, motor, taste, and vestibular fibers are conveyed by entirely separate pathways.'),
('e6b7c159-65d6-505c-ab12-96671e814f0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Vestibular nuclei (superior, lateral, medial, inferior) are located in', '["Spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla and pons (floor of fourth ventricle)","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla and pons (floor of fourth ventricle)', 'The four vestibular nuclei lie beneath the lateral floor of the fourth ventricle, extending through the rostral medulla and caudal pons, where they relay balance and head-position signals to the MLF, cerebellum, and vestibular cortex. They are not located in the spinal cord, cerebellum, midbrain, or thalamus.'),
('e835062f-b4a1-5cce-b2f7-35d18cfad15b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medial vestibular nucleus (Schwalbe) projects to', '["Cerebellum","Lateral vestibulospinal tract","MLF (vestibulo-ocular reflex)","Thalamus","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'MLF (vestibulo-ocular reflex)', 'The medial vestibular nucleus is the main source of MLF fibers that couple head and eye movements for the vestibulo-ocular reflex. The lateral vestibulospinal tract arises from the lateral (Deiters) nucleus, and the cerebellum receives vestibular afferents via the inferior cerebellar peduncle rather than the MLF.'),
('8987f659-36a4-514c-a911-536dff0b41a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The lateral vestibular nucleus (Deiters) gives rise to', '["Medial vestibulospinal tract resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamic fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","MLF fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral vestibulospinal tract (extensor facilitation)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral vestibulospinal tract (extensor facilitation)', 'Deiters nucleus gives rise to the lateral vestibulospinal tract, which descends ipsilaterally to facilitate extensor (antigravity) muscles for posture. The medial vestibulospinal tract originates from the medial vestibular nucleus, while MLF and cerebellar fibers come from other vestibular nuclei.'),
('e5cfd724-f479-561b-b0e7-307370b1a5a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The superior vestibular nucleus (Bechterew) projects to', '["Lateral geniculate","MLF for VOR and cerebellum","Auditory cortex","Thalamus","Spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'MLF for VOR and cerebellum', 'The superior vestibular nucleus projects rostrally through the MLF to drive the vestibulo-ocular reflex and also sends fibers to the cerebellum. The lateral geniculate, auditory cortex, and thalamus are not targets of vestibular outflow, and this nucleus does not project to the spinal cord.'),
('974c8d70-51b1-5f6d-a1d7-909cb1b96026'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'What is the primary vestibular projection to the cortex?', '["Prefrontal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Precentral gyrus mediated by specific receptor activation","Postcentral gyrus (area 2) near intraparietal sulcus","Temporal lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postcentral gyrus (area 2) near intraparietal sulcus', 'Vestibular cortex is centered in the postcentral gyrus (Brodmann area 2) adjacent to the intraparietal sulcus, reached via the ventrolateral thalamus. Prefrontal, occipital, and temporal areas do not receive primary vestibular input.'),
('f4da9004-11dd-5202-9731-dbe2437c1dee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The final relay for auditory pathway before cortex is', '["Inferior colliculus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cochlear nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior olive mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral lemniscus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial geniculate body (MGB) of thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial geniculate body (MGB) of thalamus', 'The medial geniculate body of the thalamus is the last relay of the auditory pathway, projecting via the auditory radiation to the transverse temporal gyrus. The inferior colliculus, cochlear nucleus, superior olive, and lateral lemniscus are earlier brainstem relays.'),
('1f87dcdd-6a0d-5b5e-ae37-3ef508b520ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The primary auditory cortex is located in', '["Occipital lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior temporal gyrus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior temporal gyrus mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle temporal gyrus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transverse temporal gyrus (Heschl gyrus, areas 41/42)"]'::jsonb, 'Transverse temporal gyrus (Heschl gyrus, areas 41/42)', 'The primary auditory cortex occupies the transverse temporal gyrus (Heschl gyrus, Brodmann areas 41 and 42) on the superior surface of the temporal lobe. The other temporal gyri and the occipital lobe mediate non-auditory functions.'),
('4b0302fb-4789-57c5-896f-6c28a41941b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The inferior colliculus projects to', '["Auditory cortex","Lateral lemniscus","Medial geniculate body (thalamus)","Superior olive","Cochlear nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial geniculate body (thalamus)', 'The inferior colliculus is the principal midbrain auditory relay and projects to the medial geniculate body, which then reaches the auditory cortex. The cochlear nucleus, superior olive, and lateral lemniscus are earlier stages, and the colliculus does not project directly to the cortex.'),
('d7c1ed67-8a84-58ae-81ea-d3128fee9074'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which midbrain structure is a major relay in descending pain modulation?', '["Periaqueductal gray (PAG)","Red nucleus","Inferior colliculus","Substantia nigra","Superior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Periaqueductal gray (PAG)', 'The periaqueductal gray of the midbrain is a key relay in descending pain inhibition, activating the raphe-spinal serotonergic pathway. The red nucleus, colliculi, and substantia nigra are not components of the pain-modulating circuitry.'),
('666243c9-d26e-52df-aa29-138fb9fceb28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Descending pain modulation pathway involves', '["Raphe → PAG resulting in decreased functional activity","PAG → cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord → PAG mediated by specific receptor activation","PAG → raphe nuclei (nucleus raphe magnus) → spinal cord (serotonergic)","Thalamus → PAG leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PAG → raphe nuclei (nucleus raphe magnus) → spinal cord (serotonergic)', 'Descending analgesia runs from the PAG to the nucleus raphe magnus, whose serotonergic projections synapse on inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn to suppress nociception. The reversed or cortical/spinal-only directions do not represent the classic descending pathway.'),
('d704d9ba-4507-550e-93d8-a8556fde79d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which CN nuclei are located in the midbrain?', '["CN V, VI, VII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN X, XI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN XII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III (oculomotor), CN IV (trochlear), mesencephalic nucleus of V","CN VIII, IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III (oculomotor), CN IV (trochlear), mesencephalic nucleus of V', 'The oculomotor, trochlear, and mesencephalic trigeminal nuclei lie in the midbrain. The motor and sensory trigeminal, abducens, facial, and vestibulocochlear nuclei are pontine, and the glossopharyngeal, vagus, accessory, and hypoglossal nuclei are medullary or spinal.'),
('ab16ff4a-a3cb-5e3a-a344-004be948564f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which CN nuclei are located in the pons?', '["CN IX, X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III, IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN I, II mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V (motor, principal sensory, spinal trigeminal), VI, VII, VIII","CN XI, XII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (motor, principal sensory, spinal trigeminal), VI, VII, VIII', 'The motor and principal sensory trigeminal nuclei, the abducens, facial, and vestibulocochlear nuclei are situated in the pons. CN III and IV are midbrain, while CN IX, X, XI, and XII lie in the medulla or spinal cord.'),
('c3928493-4b1e-59c7-81a9-7c6d90e381a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which CN nuclei are located in the medulla?', '["CN VIII resulting in decreased functional activity","All cranial nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI, VII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN IX (glossopharyngeal), X (vagus), XI (accessory), XII (hypoglossal), solitary nucleus, spinal V","CN III, IV, V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal), X (vagus), XI (accessory), XII (hypoglossal), solitary nucleus, spinal V', 'The glossopharyngeal, vagus, spinal accessory, and hypoglossal nuclei, together with the solitary nucleus and spinal trigeminal nucleus, lie in the medulla. CN VIII is at the pontomedullary junction, and CN III, IV, V are located more rostrally.'),
('44999260-aeda-50da-a72d-a01aa77da583'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus ambiguus (medulla) innervates muscles of', '["Jaw resulting in decreased functional activity","Tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pharynx, larynx, palate (via CN IX, X, XI)","Face without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pharynx, larynx, palate (via CN IX, X, XI)', 'The nucleus ambiguus provides branchiomotor innervation to the muscles of the pharynx, larynx, and soft palate through CN IX, X, and the cranial part of XI. Jaw muscles are served by the motor trigeminal nucleus, the tongue by the hypoglossal nucleus, and facial muscles by the facial nucleus.'),
('e0a5c052-5c0d-5e81-88b7-8258efe12892'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The solitary nucleus (taste and visceral) extends from', '["Medulla (rostral-caudal)","Spinal cord only","Pons to midbrain","Midbrain only","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (rostral-caudal)', 'The solitary nucleus runs longitudinally through the medulla, with the rostral part receiving gustatory fibers and the caudal part receiving visceral afferents. It does not extend into the pons, midbrain, spinal cord, or cerebellum.'),
('822ec408-483e-545e-b0fd-7dd547310701'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which cranial nerve nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to parotid gland?', '["Dorsal vagus (CN X)","Nucleus ambiguus","Edinger-Westphal (midbrain, CN III)","Inferior salivatory nucleus (medulla, CN IX)","Superior salivatory nucleus (pons, CN VII)"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior salivatory nucleus (medulla, CN IX)', 'The inferior salivatory nucleus of the medulla supplies preganglionic parasympathetic fibers through CN IX, which reach the parotid gland via the otic ganglion. The superior salivatory nucleus serves the submandibular, sublingual, and lacrimal glands, and the Edinger-Westphal nucleus serves the eye.'),
('3ff18b5e-0f36-5f4e-afd2-4300e366e947'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which cranial nerve nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to submandibular and sublingual glands?', '["Nucleus ambiguus","Edinger-Westphal (CN III)","Superior salivatory nucleus (pons, CN VII)","Inferior salivatory nucleus (medulla, CN IX)","Dorsal vagus (CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior salivatory nucleus (pons, CN VII)', 'The superior salivatory nucleus of the pons sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers through CN VII (chorda tympani) to the submandibular and sublingual glands via the submandibular ganglion. The inferior salivatory nucleus targets the parotid, the Edinger-Westphal nucleus targets the eye, and the dorsal vagal nucleus targets the viscera.'),
('c6035fb5-322c-5a31-90de-a5d6d80d4b37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which cranial nerve nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to lacrimal gland?', '["Edinger-Westphal (CN III) resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal vagus (CN X) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior salivatory nucleus (CN IX) mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior salivatory nucleus (CN VII via greater petrosal)","Trigeminal motor nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior salivatory nucleus (CN VII via greater petrosal)', 'The superior salivatory nucleus supplies lacrimal secretion via the greater petrosal nerve (CN VII), which synapses in the pterygopalatine ganglion. The inferior salivatory nucleus serves the parotid gland, and the Edinger-Westphal nucleus supplies the pupillary sphincter and ciliary muscle.'),
('71247d85-0ae5-5dd9-b874-729f5647f7ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to', '["Parotid gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscles (eye)","Lacrimal gland mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Submandibular gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscles (eye)', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers through CN III to the ciliary ganglion, which innervates the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. The parotid, submandibular, and lacrimal glands are served by the salivatory nuclei, and the heart by the dorsal vagal nucleus.'),
('33c3f19a-7d0b-5f4f-ac51-6c85a68f3416'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The dorsal vagal nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to', '["Parotid gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic and abdominal viscera (heart, lungs, GI tract up to splenic flexure)","Eye mediated by specific receptor activation","Submandibular gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lacrimal gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracic and abdominal viscera (heart, lungs, GI tract up to splenic flexure)', 'The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus supplies parasympathetic fibers to the thoracic and abdominal viscera, including the heart, lungs, and GI tract up to the splenic flexure. The salivatory nuclei serve the salivary and lacrimal glands, and the Edinger-Westphal nucleus serves the eye.'),
('24ab8c2b-abf9-5414-b978-a8568881c103'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The spinal accessory nucleus (CN XI) is located in', '["Pons resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","C1-C5 anterior horn of spinal cord","Dorsal root ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C1-C5 anterior horn of spinal cord', 'The spinal accessory nucleus occupies the anterior horn of the upper cervical cord (C1-C5) and gives rise to the spinal root that innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. The medulla contains only the cranial (bulbar) part of the accessory nerve.'),
('cdb15dae-212a-5885-8a79-0cfebe8fb182'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cranial accessory nerve (bulbar part) joins', '["Spinal accessory nerve","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve (CN X)', 'The cranial (bulbar) root of the accessory nerve emerges from the medulla and joins the vagus nerve, distributing with its branches to the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles. It does not join the spinal accessory, facial, glossopharyngeal, or hypoglossal nerves.'),
('f4544ccd-c1c7-573a-862b-e4d76cf45e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Corticobulbar (corticonuclear) fibers synapse on cranial nerve motor nuclei', '["Only on trigeminal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only on facial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bilaterally, except lower face (CN VII) and tongue (CN XII)","All ipsilaterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All contralaterally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilaterally, except lower face (CN VII) and tongue (CN XII)', 'Most cranial nerve motor nuclei receive bilateral corticobulbar innervation, but the subnucleus controlling the lower face (CN VII) and the hypoglossal nucleus (CN XII) receive only contralateral input. This pattern explains the sparing of the forehead and tongue-tip mobility in supranuclear lesions.'),
('aaafd54d-052f-57b8-a148-d132d992fa7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Supranuclear lesion of facial nerve spares', '["Forehead (upper face) due to bilateral innervation","Lip movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eye closure mediated by specific receptor activation","All facial muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lower face leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Forehead (upper face) due to bilateral innervation', 'A supranuclear (upper motor neuron) facial lesion spares the forehead because the upper facial muscles receive bilateral cortical innervation, so only the contralateral lower face becomes weak. The lower face is affected, not spared, and eye closure and lip movement are not selectively preserved.'),
('7cf495bb-ee74-5a8a-8010-7b07758e768d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'In nuclear/infralesionar facial nerve palsy', '["Only lower face is affected","Forehead is spared","Only eye muscles affected","Only mouth affected","Upper and lower face are equally affected"]'::jsonb, 'Upper and lower face are equally affected', 'A nuclear or infranuclear facial palsy is a lower motor neuron lesion that inactivates all ipsilateral facial muscles, so the forehead and lower face are equally involved. Forehead sparing and isolated involvement of one region occur only with upper motor neuron or selective lesions.'),
('f8be1d4b-8d9c-57f5-afc3-f91a8b19fa2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pontine lesion (Millard-Gubler) affects which nerves?', '["CN V and VIII","CN XI and XII","CN VI and VII","CN III and IV","CN IX and X"]'::jsonb, 'CN VI and VII', 'Millard-Gubler syndrome results from a paramedian ventral pontine infarct that damages the abducens (VI) and facial (VII) nuclei together with the corticospinal tract. CN III and IV are midbrain, CN XI and XII are medullary/spinal, and CN IX-X are medullary.'),
('6a898bc2-9740-5089-8bff-b1edb1e78763'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lateral pontine syndrome (AICA occlusion) causes', '["Only hemiplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","Ipsilateral Horner, facial numbness, ataxia, deafness + contralateral pain/temp loss","Only sensory loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Ipsilateral CN III palsy + contralateral hemiplegia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipsilateral CN XII palsy + hemiplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral Horner, facial numbness, ataxia, deafness + contralateral pain/temp loss', 'AICA occlusion infarcts the lateral pons, damaging the sympathetic tract (Horner), spinal trigeminal tract (ipsilateral facial numbness), middle cerebellar peduncle (ataxia), and vestibulocochlear structures (deafness) while the spinothalamic tract injury yields contralateral pain-and-temperature loss. Hemiplegia and the midbrain/medullary syndromes involve different structures.'),
('258a3873-9749-5430-bde3-874412b8915f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Inferior medial pontine syndrome (paramedian branches of basilar) causes', '["CN III palsy + ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only ataxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ipsilateral CN VI palsy + contralateral hemiplegia","CN XII palsy + hemiplegia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN VII palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral CN VI palsy + contralateral hemiplegia', 'Occlusion of paramedian branches of the basilar artery infarcts the medial inferior pons, injuring the abducens nerve and the pyramidal tract. The result is an ipsilateral CN VI palsy with contralateral hemiplegia; the other choices describe midbrain or medullary syndromes.'),
('e0b8d172-d316-531f-81a7-060f9f699f27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Superior medial midbrain syndrome (Weber) involves', '["CN IV palsy + ataxia","CN III palsy + contralateral hemiplegia","CN VI palsy + hemiplegia","Only CN III palsy","CN XII palsy + hemiplegia"]'::jsonb, 'CN III palsy + contralateral hemiplegia', 'Weber syndrome results from infarction of the ventral midbrain involving the oculomotor nerve fascicles and the cerebral peduncle, producing an ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral hemiplegia. It does not involve CN IV, CN VI, or CN XII.'),
('6a0cb5db-4fe1-508b-bf79-6c0ba924d4d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Superior lateral midbrain syndrome involves', '["Cerebellar only resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial lemniscus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI only mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticospinal tract only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Red nucleus + CN III (Benedikt) or superior cerebellar peduncle"]'::jsonb, 'Red nucleus + CN III (Benedikt) or superior cerebellar peduncle', 'Lateral midbrain infarction affects the red nucleus and oculomotor nerve, producing Benedikt syndrome (ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral tremor), or damages the superior cerebellar peduncle with ataxia. The medial lemniscus, CN VI, and corticospinal tract are not the structures infarcted here.'),
('a9de2959-e5bc-508c-9832-5db70b3ddadc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Medial medullary syndrome (Dejerine syndrome) results from occlusion of', '["Anterior spinal artery or vertebral artery paramedian branches","Posterior cerebral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PICA mediated by specific receptor activation","Basilar artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AICA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior spinal artery or vertebral artery paramedian branches', 'Dejerine syndrome results from occlusion of the anterior spinal artery or paramedian branches of the vertebral artery, which infarct the medial medulla. PICA and AICA occlusions produce lateral medullary and lateral pontine syndromes, while basilar and posterior cerebral occlusions cause different deficits.'),
('47b642db-8636-54c1-af3a-9d29a50c5f46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which is NOT a feature of medial medullary syndrome?', '["Ipsilateral facial numbness","Contralateral hemiplegia (sparing face)","Contralateral loss of proprioception/vibration","Ipsilateral hypoglossal paralysis","Ipsilateral tongue weakness (CN XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral facial numbness', 'Medial medullary syndrome does not involve the spinal trigeminal tract, so ipsilateral facial numbness is absent; it produces contralateral hemiplegia sparing the face, contralateral loss of proprioception and vibration, and ipsilateral hypoglossal paralysis with tongue weakness. Ipsilateral facial numbness belongs to lateral medullary syndrome.'),
('ffccbd14-04a3-5b70-a147-98e84b6eff21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Medial medullary syndrome involves damage to', '["Hypoglossal nerve + pyramid + medial lemniscus","Red nucleus + CN III","Spinothalamic tract + spinal V + sympathetic","Substantia nigra + CN IV","Inferior cerebellar peduncle + CN IX, X"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal nerve + pyramid + medial lemniscus', 'The medial medulla contains the hypoglossal nerve fibers, pyramidal tract, and medial lemniscus, so their combined injury yields ipsilateral tongue weakness, contralateral hemiplegia, and contralateral loss of position and vibration sense. The other structures belong to the lateral medulla or midbrain.'),
('0b7cd1df-06f6-5b43-ab72-b26cd0f5351b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lateral medullary syndrome (Wallenberg) involves damage to', '["Pyramid, medial lemniscus, CN XII resulting in decreased functional activity","Substantia nigra, crus cerebri accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI, VII, pontine nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III, red nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinothalamic, spinal V, sympathetic, ICP, nucleus ambiguus, vestibulocochlear"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic, spinal V, sympathetic, ICP, nucleus ambiguus, vestibulocochlear', 'Wallenberg syndrome spares the medullary midline and affects the lateral medulla, damaging the spinothalamic tract, spinal trigeminal tract, descending sympathetic fibers, inferior cerebellar peduncle, nucleus ambiguus, and vestibulocochlear structures. Pyramid, medial lemniscus, and CN XII belong to the medial medulla.'),
('ef9a83d6-2d0c-5af9-bda2-adc321cc803e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Locked-in syndrome results from', '["Medullary lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventral pontine lesion (bilateral corticospinal + corticobulbar)","Thalamic lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral pontine lesion (bilateral corticospinal + corticobulbar)', 'Bilateral infarction of the ventral pons interrupts both corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts, producing quadriplegia and anarthria while consciousness and vertical eye movements are preserved. Medullary, thalamic, midbrain, and cortical lesions do not produce this specific combination.'),
('20341784-367e-5e2d-8159-f876ab02d50c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Locked-in syndrome spares which function?', '["Speech","Vertical eye movements and blinking","All voluntary movement","Facial movement","Limb movement"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical eye movements and blinking', 'Because the lesion lies ventral to the pontine tegmentum, the supranuclear pathways for vertical gaze and eyelid control remain intact, preserving vertical eye movements and blinking. Speech, facial, and limb movements are lost, and all voluntary movement is not retained.'),
('244bc7a6-3b48-5dad-b271-0c5d7702dabd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral pontine lesion (locked-in) can be caused by occlusion of', '["Superior cerebellar artery","Middle cerebral artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Basilar artery","Anterior cerebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Basilar artery', 'The basilar artery supplies the ventral pons, and its occlusion can produce bilateral pontine infarction with the locked-in syndrome. The superior cerebellar, middle cerebral, posterior cerebral, and anterior cerebral arteries supply different territories.'),
('bf2644fc-0518-592a-b4f8-1ab065b90990'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO) is characterized by', '["Miosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Ptosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Impaired adduction of ipsilateral eye with nystagmus of contralateral abducting eye on lateral gaze","Diplopia on downgaze without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete gaze palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired adduction of ipsilateral eye with nystagmus of contralateral abducting eye on lateral gaze', 'INO results from an MLF lesion that interrupts input to the ipsilateral medial rectus subnucleus, so the ipsilateral eye fails to adduct while the contralateral abducting eye develops nystagmus on lateral gaze. Miosis, ptosis, downgaze diplopia, and complete gaze palsy are not part of the classic finding.'),
('053ea674-d690-5980-a4d8-4009007a54f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'One-and-a-half syndrome is due to', '["CN VI lesion","Bilateral PPRF lesion","PPRF + MLF lesion (ipsilateral)","Bilateral MLF lesion","CN III lesion"]'::jsonb, 'PPRF + MLF lesion (ipsilateral)', 'A single ipsilateral lesion involving both the paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF) and the MLF produces one-and-a-half syndrome: loss of horizontal gaze toward the lesion and loss of adduction of the ipsilateral eye. Bilateral PPRF or bilateral MLF lesions give different gaze patterns.'),
('5158d721-8c4a-5e0c-996a-71f129a6a1d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'In one-and-a-half syndrome, the intact horizontal movement is', '["Abduction of contralateral eye","Adduction of both eyes","Adduction of ipsilateral eye","Abduction of both eyes","horizontal movement"]'::jsonb, 'Abduction of contralateral eye', 'With loss of ipsilateral gaze and loss of adduction, the only preserved horizontal movement is abduction of the contralateral eye. All other conjugate horizontal movements are abolished by the combined PPRF and MLF injury.'),
('f2048ca8-3570-59bf-ae09-9408267d41a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pre-Bötzinger complex is located in', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventrolateral medulla (respiratory rhythm generation)","Midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventrolateral medulla (respiratory rhythm generation)', 'The pre-Bötzinger complex lies in the ventrolateral medulla and functions as the primary generator of inspiratory rhythm. The dorsal medulla contains the DRG, while the pons, midbrain, and cerebellum are not its location.'),
('bdb87dde-4e9a-5fad-8722-9d5a48981b94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The dorsal respiratory group (DRG) is located in', '["Spinal cord","Nucleus ambiguus","Nucleus tractus solitarius (medulla)","Pontine parabrachial nucleus","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus tractus solitarius (medulla)', 'The dorsal respiratory group is associated with the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla and provides inspiratory drive. The nucleus ambiguus contains the ventral respiratory group, and the parabrachial nucleus is a pontine respiratory center.'),
('e78b8352-412a-5088-af7b-0cc391d2d44d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral respiratory group (VRG) is located in', '["Solitary nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pontine nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nucleus ambiguus and retroambiguus (medulla)"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus ambiguus and retroambiguus (medulla)', 'The ventral respiratory group comprises neurons in the nucleus ambiguus and retroambiguus of the medulla and is active mainly during forced expiration. The solitary nucleus houses the DRG, while the pons, midbrain, and cerebellum are not its location.'),
('f72d9b4f-f26b-5107-a787-e438720dedef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Apneustic breathing (prolonged inspiration) occurs with lesion of', '["Cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Pneumotaxic center (dorsolateral pons)","Phrenic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Medullary respiratory centers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumotaxic center (dorsolateral pons)', 'Damage to the pneumotaxic center in the dorsolateral pons removes the inspiratory off-switch, so the apneustic center drives prolonged inspiratory holds. Cortical, medullary, phrenic, and spinal cord lesions produce different breathing patterns.'),
('d10a52ca-a0c3-5572-a591-45adf792c5d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Kussmaul respiration is seen in', '["Diabetic ketoacidosis (metabolic acidosis)","Pontine lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Medullary lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetic ketoacidosis (metabolic acidosis)', 'Kussmaul respiration, a deep and labored breathing pattern, is a compensatory response to metabolic acidosis such as diabetic ketoacidosis and is driven by chemoreceptor stimulation. Pontine, medullary, spinal, and cortical lesions produce distinct abnormal breathing patterns.'),
('b6134bcc-e82f-53eb-9732-6f87cc2d84a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cluster breathing is associated with', '["Midbrain lesion","Cortical lesion","Medullary lesion","Cerebellar lesion","Pontine lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine lesion', 'Cluster breathing, irregular bursts of breaths separated by pauses, is typically associated with damage to the pons. Cortical, medullary, midbrain, and cerebellar lesions produce different respiratory patterns.'),
('bad66bf8-58ee-50de-b34d-a7acc1c2ee27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Ataxic breathing (Biot) is associated with', '["Cortical lesion","Pontine lesion","Medullary lesion (pre-terminal)","Spinal cord lesion","Midbrain lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Medullary lesion (pre-terminal)', 'Biot (ataxic) breathing results from damage to the medullary respiratory centers and is often pre-terminal in the setting of medullary compression or ischemia. Cortical, pontine, spinal, and midbrain lesions give different respiratory patterns.'),
('0d0908b9-b544-5dd4-b053-59c9a8ea4f12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The superior salivary nucleus is located in', '["Midbrain","Pons","Spinal cord","Cerebellum","Medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Pons', 'The superior salivatory nucleus lies in the pontine tegmentum and provides parasympathetic fibers through CN VII. The inferior salivatory nucleus is in the medulla, and the midbrain, spinal cord, and cerebellum are incorrect sites.'),
('cd7f211f-58bf-533e-b96d-228c7e925df4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The inferior salivary nucleus is located in', '["Spinal cord","Midbrain","Medulla","Pons","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla', 'The inferior salivatory nucleus sits in the medullary tegmentum and sends parasympathetic fibers through CN IX to the parotid gland. The superior salivatory nucleus is in the pons, and the other listed sites are incorrect.'),
('2e1e258e-b08f-55b8-9af0-930eef8c8519'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The facial nerve colliculus is caused by', '["CN VIII fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","CN V fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","CN IX fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII fibers looping around CN VI nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII fibers looping around CN VI nucleus', 'The facial colliculus is produced by the internal genu of the facial nerve, whose fibers loop around the abducens nucleus in the pontine tegmentum. Fibers of CN VIII, V, VI, and IX do not create this eminence.'),
('f15a9ee0-9fc1-51a3-ad67-4d2832b511b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The striae medullares (auditory) cross the floor of fourth ventricle at', '["Pons-medulla junction","Midbrain","Cerebellum","Spinal cord","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Pons-medulla junction', 'The striae medullares, composed of auditory fibers, run across the floor of the fourth ventricle at the junction of the pons and medulla. They do not appear at the midbrain, cerebellum, spinal cord, or thalamus.'),
('e9130f84-dfdc-5573-9fc6-f45990d5657e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The median sulcus of fourth ventricle divides the floor into', '["Rostral and caudal","Anterior and posterior","Upper and lower parts","Medial and lateral","Left and right halves"]'::jsonb, 'Left and right halves', 'The median sulcus is a vertical midline groove in the rhomboid fossa that splits the fourth ventricle floor into symmetrical left and right halves. It does not divide the floor rostrocaudally, anteroposteriorly, or into medial and lateral compartments.'),
('21cb4555-5852-592c-8a14-09797cad13fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The sulcus limitans separates', '["Motor from autonomic resulting in decreased functional activity","Gray from white accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory from autonomic mediated by specific receptor activation","Cranial from spinal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor (medial) from sensory (lateral) nuclei in brainstem"]'::jsonb, 'Motor (medial) from sensory (lateral) nuclei in brainstem', 'The sulcus limitans runs longitudinally on each side of the rhomboid fossa and marks the boundary between the medially placed motor nuclei and the laterally placed sensory nuclei of the brainstem. It does not divide motor from autonomic, gray from white, or cranial from spinal structures.'),
('92262f4f-e01a-589e-93bd-21bf7c21edee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medial eminence of fourth ventricle floor overlies', '["CN IX nucleus","CN XII and CN VI nuclei","CN X dorsal nucleus","CN VII nucleus","CN V nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'CN XII and CN VI nuclei', 'The medial eminence is a longitudinal elevation on the fourth ventricle floor; its caudal part overlies the hypoglossal nucleus and its rostral pontine part overlies the abducens nucleus beneath the facial colliculus. The CN IX, X, VII, and V nuclei lie outside this eminence.'),
('8aaa7c79-f57a-59a9-ba45-b8a9cf5ede87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Area postrema (CTZ) is located at', '["Lower medulla (floor of fourth ventricle, lateral to obex)","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower medulla (floor of fourth ventricle, lateral to obex)', 'The area postrema is a circumventricular organ in the lower medulla on the floor of the fourth ventricle, just lateral to the obex, and lies outside the blood-brain barrier. It is not found in the cerebellum, thalamus, midbrain, or pons.'),
('796e234f-2aea-5c44-bbc5-4a3e7029a043'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi is a', '["Respiratory center resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Gaze-holding center (neural integrator for eye position)","Sensory nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gaze-holding center (neural integrator for eye position)', 'The nucleus prepositus hypoglossi, in the dorsal medulla just rostral to the hypoglossal nucleus, acts as the neural integrator that holds eccentric eye position between saccades. It is not a respiratory, motor, autonomic, or sensory nucleus.'),
('79668936-155d-54f6-8c05-4fd70e9a344d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The interstitial nucleus of Cajal is involved in', '["Vertical and torsional gaze holding","Motor coordination","Pain modulation","Horizontal gaze","Auditory processing"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical and torsional gaze holding', 'The interstitial nucleus of Cajal, located in the midbrain near the MLF, serves as the neural integrator for vertical and torsional gaze holding. Horizontal gaze, general motor coordination, pain, and audition are governed by other structures.'),
('52da9209-cf36-57df-8b2a-48f1aa45b8df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The Darkschewitsch nucleus (midbrain) is involved in', '["Auditory resulting in decreased functional activity","Oculomotor coordination (gaze)","Sleep mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor coordination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor coordination (gaze)', 'The Darkschewitsch nucleus is a small midbrain nucleus lying near the oculomotor complex that contributes to oculomotor coordination and gaze control. Auditory, sleep, pain, and general motor functions are not its primary roles.'),
('bdf20396-748f-5957-8434-0f28224f78cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The posterior commissure connects', '["Thalamic nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar hemispheres mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortical hemispheres without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left and right midbrain (pretectal region)"]'::jsonb, 'Left and right midbrain (pretectal region)', 'The posterior commissure is a fiber bundle crossing the midline just rostral to the superior colliculi, interconnecting the pretectal nuclei and adjacent midbrain structures of both sides. It does not interconnect thalamic nuclei, basal ganglia, cerebellar hemispheres, or cerebral hemispheres.'),
('3205c3f7-b34c-50bd-b40b-2f1ae6a17cfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pupillary light reflex pathway involves', '["Cortex → pretectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Retina → LGN → cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Retina → pretectal nucleus (midbrain) → Edinger-Westphal (both sides) → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae","Retina → superior colliculus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pretectum → thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retina → pretectal nucleus (midbrain) → Edinger-Westphal (both sides) → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae', 'In the light reflex, retinal input reaches the pretectal nucleus, which projects bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei; preganglionic fibers then travel through the ciliary ganglion to the sphincter pupillae, giving both direct and consensual responses. The cortex, LGN, and superior colliculus are not stations in this arc.'),
('25bc83c6-565d-5cee-b031-934f5c234181'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lesion of pretectal nucleus or posterior commissure causes', '["Ptosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Light-near dissociation (Argyll Robertson pupil)","Mydriasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Diplopia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete blindness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Light-near dissociation (Argyll Robertson pupil)', 'Damage to the pretectal area or posterior commissure interrupts the light reflex arc while sparing the near-response pathway, producing light-near dissociation as seen in Argyll Robertson pupil. Ptosis, mydriasis, diplopia, and blindness arise from other lesions.'),
('7e65a4db-f2da-5f47-b720-a66ded8cce1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'In Argyll Robertson pupil, which is affected?', '["Pupil dilated resulting in decreased functional activity","Both absent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Light reflex (absent) with intact accommodation","Accommodation (absent) with intact light reflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both intact leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Light reflex (absent) with intact accommodation', 'Argyll Robertson pupil shows loss of the pupillary light reflex while the accommodation-convergence near response remains intact, usually with small, irregular, bilaterally miotic pupils. The other options describe states that do not match this dissociation.'),
('34fff1f9-ab94-56fc-ac7e-ebdf96fe53ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is located', '["Dorsal to CN III nucleus","Medial to CN III","Lateral to CN III","Ventral to CN III nucleus","Within CN III nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral to CN III nucleus', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is the visceral (parasympathetic) subnucleus of the oculomotor complex and lies ventral to the main somatic CN III nucleus. It is not dorsal, medial, lateral, or embedded within the somatic nucleus.'),
('6d33aebc-de00-5ad2-813c-ec8f650ad714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pretectal olivary nucleus is involved in', '["Motor coordination","Pupillary light reflex (consensual)","Pain modulation","Olfaction","Auditory processing"]'::jsonb, 'Pupillary light reflex (consensual)', 'The pretectal olivary nucleus receives retinal light input and relays it bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei, mediating both the direct and consensual pupillary light reflexes. It plays no part in motor coordination, pain, olfaction, or audition.'),
('2cf6ee09-7837-5434-82ca-0a08418dc0d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Parinaud syndrome (dorsal midbrain syndrome) includes', '["Nystagmus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Horizontal gaze palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ptosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Downward gaze palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upward gaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus, light-near dissociation, lid retraction"]'::jsonb, 'Upward gaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus, light-near dissociation, lid retraction', 'Parinaud (dorsal midbrain) syndrome results from compression of the superior colliculus and pretectum and combines upward gaze palsy, convergence-retraction nystagmus, light-near dissociation, and lid retraction. The other choices list isolated or unrelated eye signs.'),
('aab011fe-7e95-54f5-9209-541c476f1f36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Parinaud syndrome is commonly caused by', '["Pontine glioma resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pineal gland tumor (compressing superior colliculus/pretectum)","Medullary tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pineal gland tumor (compressing superior colliculus/pretectum)', 'Pineal gland tumors grow dorsally to compress the superior colliculus and pretectal region, classically producing Parinaud syndrome. Pontine, cerebellar, and medullary tumors and meningiomas do not selectively cause this dorsal midbrain syndrome.'),
('bf273d1f-f14d-58d0-b250-7bd8f3f6ebb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus of posterior commissure coordinates', '["Horizontal gaze","Eyelid opening","Accommodation","Pupil constriction","Vertical gaze (especially upward)"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical gaze (especially upward)', 'The nucleus of the posterior commissure helps coordinate vertical gaze, especially upward, working with the riMLF and the interstitial nucleus of Cajal. Horizontal gaze, eyelid opening, accommodation, and pupillary constriction depend on other circuits.'),
('b5dc83d7-5e9b-55a7-9a91-1c5ad363bdd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The rostral interstitial nucleus of MLF (riMLF) coordinates', '["Fixation","VOR","Horizontal saccades","Vertical and torsional saccades","Pursuit movements"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical and torsional saccades', 'The rostral interstitial nucleus of the MLF (riMLF) in the rostral midbrain generates vertical and torsional saccades. Fixation, the VOR, horizontal saccades, and smooth pursuit rely on other brainstem circuits.'),
('c0736f5a-1bad-5c59-8b61-dc2b42b8ff0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF) is the', '["Vertical saccade generator","Horizontal saccade generator","Smooth pursuit center","Fixation center","VOR integrator"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal saccade generator', 'The paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF), lying beside the abducens nucleus, generates horizontal saccades by sending excitatory bursts to the ipsilateral abducens nucleus. Vertical saccades, smooth pursuit, fixation, and VOR integration are governed elsewhere.'),
('a1f27d70-4f6a-59b0-9fc2-64b228359dcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) involves', '["Superior colliculus → MLF resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III → VI → VIII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Red nucleus → CN III mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VIII → vestibular nuclei → MLF → CN III, IV, VI","Cerebellum → PPRF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VIII → vestibular nuclei → MLF → CN III, IV, VI', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex runs from the vestibular nerve (CN VIII) to the vestibular nuclei, then through the MLF to the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nuclei to keep gaze stable during head movement. The alternative pathways do not describe this three-neuron arc.'),
('c5e5b2cb-8c41-56c0-b500-d7a2db20dc3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Loss of oculocephalic reflex indicates', '["Vestibular lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Eye muscle lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortical lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brainstem lesion (especially pons/midbrain)"]'::jsonb, 'Brainstem lesion (especially pons/midbrain)', 'The oculocephalic (doll''s eye) reflex is mediated by brainstem circuits in the pons and midbrain, so its loss indicates a brainstem lesion. A vestibular, eye-muscle, cortical, or cerebellar lesion alone does not abolish the reflex.'),
('ee09feff-ed01-5746-ba2b-f29ff2808034'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus receives', '["Motor output to jaw resulting in decreased functional activity","Touch from face accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Taste mediated by specific receptor activation","Proprioceptive input from jaw muscles (muscle spindles)","Pain from face leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proprioceptive input from jaw muscles (muscle spindles)', 'The mesencephalic nucleus of CN V contains the primary afferent cell bodies for proprioception from the muscle spindles of the jaw muscles. It does not carry motor output, touch, taste, or facial pain.'),
('5e71d866-8f4a-54ba-a701-66bbdf7f2890'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Jaw jerk reflex involves', '["CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V (mesencephalic nucleus → motor nucleus)","CN X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN XII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (mesencephalic nucleus → motor nucleus)', 'The jaw jerk is a monosynaptic stretch reflex: stretching the masseter is sensed by the mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus and relayed directly to the trigeminal motor nucleus to close the jaw. CN VII, IX, X, and XII are not part of this arc.'),
('651a2016-8bb8-5307-b40c-6b00e161b494'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Exaggerated jaw jerk indicates', '["LMN lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","UMN lesion above CN V motor nucleus (supranuclear)","Basal ganglia lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory neuropathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'UMN lesion above CN V motor nucleus (supranuclear)', 'An exaggerated jaw jerk reflects release from upper motor neuron control, indicating a supranuclear lesion above the trigeminal motor nucleus. Lower motor neuron, cerebellar, basal ganglia, and peripheral sensory pathology do not produce a brisk jaw jerk.'),
('4172b3ac-c3e1-5e3c-952e-048cd228773f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The blink reflex involves', '["CN VIII and CN VII","CN II and CN III","CN IX and CN X","CN III and CN V","CN V (afferent) and CN VII (efferent)"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (afferent) and CN VII (efferent)', 'The blink reflex is a trigeminofacial reflex: tactile input travels via CN V to the pontine trigeminal nuclei, and the efferent blink response leaves through CN VII. The other cranial nerve pairs do not mediate blinking.'),
('f9d73816-e164-5f01-b1ae-4d1aa58dc5d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The corneal reflex is mediated by', '["CN II and III resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VIII and VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN III and VII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V (afferent) and CN VII (efferent)","CN V and VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (afferent) and CN VII (efferent)', 'The corneal reflex uses the ophthalmic division of CN V as the afferent limb and CN VII as the efferent limb to the orbicularis oculi. CN II, III, VI, and VIII are not part of the reflex pathway.'),
('72489d1b-1bba-51be-be4d-393b6b710250'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Absent corneal reflex suggests lesion of', '["CN II","CN V or CN VII","CN VI","CN III","CN VIII"]'::jsonb, 'CN V or CN VII', 'An absent corneal reflex localizes to a lesion of the afferent limb (CN V) or the efferent limb (CN VII). The optic, abducens, oculomotor, and vestibulocochlear nerves do not mediate this reflex.'),
('c1c14d0a-75af-5406-980e-71ed425dc6a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The gag reflex is mediated by', '["CN X and XII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN V and VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII and IX mediated by specific receptor activation","CN IX and V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX (afferent) and CN X (efferent)"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (afferent) and CN X (efferent)', 'The gag reflex is a medullary reflex in which CN IX carries afferent sensation from the pharynx and CN X carries the efferent response to the pharyngeal muscles. CN V, VII, and XII are not part of the arc.'),
('84a282cb-0551-51ed-bebb-9f51741bbb6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The cough reflex involves', '["CN VII efferent","CN XII efferent","CN V afferent","CN X (vagus) afferent and efferent","CN IX afferent"]'::jsonb, 'CN X (vagus) afferent and efferent', 'The cough reflex is initiated by vagal (CN X) afferents from the larynx, trachea, and bronchi, and the efferent cough response is also largely vagally mediated with support from other motor nerves. CN V, VII, IX, and XII do not alone drive coughing.'),
('836e82db-2239-5380-8107-f8de965e5445'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The sneeze reflex involves', '["CN VII efferent resulting in decreased functional activity","CN XII efferent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN I afferent mediated by specific receptor activation","CN II afferent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN V (afferent) + CN X + phrenic + intercostals"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (afferent) + CN X + phrenic + intercostals', 'The sneeze reflex begins with CN V afferent input from the nasal mucosa and spreads to CN X, the phrenic nerve, and intercostal nerves to generate the forced expiratory blast. CN VII, XII, I, and II are not the primary pathway.'),
('23773b4b-808d-58b9-b2f0-4015698f0cb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Hiccup reflex involves', '["CN XII only resulting in decreased functional activity","CN X, phrenic nerve (C3-C5), and sympathetic chain","CN VII only mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN X, phrenic nerve (C3-C5), and sympathetic chain', 'Hiccups are caused by spasmodic contraction of the diaphragm, driven through the vagus (CN X), the phrenic nerve from C3-C5, and the sympathetic chain. No single cranial nerve among CN V, VII, IX, or XII produces the reflex.'),
('7428bd3b-a388-53b0-8e77-bf2b8a84dd70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The area postrema (CTZ) triggers vomiting in response to', '["Gastric irritation only","Auditory stimuli","Drugs/toxins in blood (no BBB)","Touch","Visual stimuli"]'::jsonb, 'Drugs/toxins in blood (no BBB)', 'Because the area postrema lies outside the blood-brain barrier, it senses blood-borne drugs and toxins and uses this input to trigger vomiting. Gastric irritation, auditory, tactile, and visual stimuli activate different pathways.'),
('8579a762-8c18-52f1-bbb3-dafc1f91565c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The vomiting center (coordinating emesis) is located in', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius and adjacent reticular formation)","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius and adjacent reticular formation)', 'The vomiting center is a network in the medullary reticular formation near the nucleus tractus solitarius that coordinates the emetic response. The cerebellum, thalamus, midbrain, and pons do not house this coordinating center.'),
('12d1b787-da09-5de3-b460-d4e9ffc55ce2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cannonball emesis (projectile vomiting) without nausea suggests', '["Raised intracranial pressure (midbrain/medullary compression)","Drug side effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Migraine mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular dysfunction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastroenteritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Raised intracranial pressure (midbrain/medullary compression)', 'Projectile (cannonball) vomiting without preceding nausea classically indicates raised intracranial pressure compressing the medullary vomiting center, often with midbrain involvement. Drug effects, migraine, vestibular dysfunction, and gastroenteritis usually produce nausea.'),
('cc63f20e-d6b5-5af6-8531-2d3cb0830871'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Yawning is controlled by', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem (medullary reticular formation)","Cerebral cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basal ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brainstem (medullary reticular formation)', 'Yawning is generated by circuits in the medullary reticular formation and nearby brainstem networks. The thalamus, cerebellum, cerebral cortex, and basal ganglia are not the primary control site.'),
('333196d4-9e40-5dbd-a3d8-52cf5b22bfb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pontine storage center (Barrington nucleus) is involved in', '["Heart rate regulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Temperature regulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain modulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Urinary bladder storage/voiding (facilitates storage)","Respiratory control leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urinary bladder storage/voiding (facilitates storage)', 'The Barrington nucleus in the dorsolateral pons promotes bladder storage and also coordinates the switch to voiding, receiving input from bladder afferents. Heart rate, temperature, pain, and respiration are regulated by separate centers.'),
('8f26c6eb-d46c-507e-b67a-00edb9decb4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pontine micturition center sends descending fibers to', '["Hypoglossal nucleus","Sacral parasympathetic nucleus (S2-S4)","Thoracic sympathetic","Phrenic nucleus","Trigeminal motor nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral parasympathetic nucleus (S2-S4)', 'The pontine micturition center (Barrington nucleus) projects descending fibers to the sacral parasympathetic nucleus at S2-S4, which drives detrusor contraction. It does not innervate the hypoglossal, thoracic sympathetic, phrenic, or trigeminal motor nuclei.'),
('8ef5a5bb-8de5-5da2-9fc6-ff8470b9f974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which medullary nucleus is essential for swallowing?', '["Facial nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal motor nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nucleus ambiguus and solitary tract nucleus","Abducens nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglossal nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus ambiguus and solitary tract nucleus', 'Swallowing depends on the nucleus ambiguus, which supplies the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles, and the solitary tract nucleus, which receives sensory input from the pharynx and oropharynx. The facial, trigeminal motor, abducens, and hypoglossal nuclei alone do not mediate swallowing.'),
('baa2b1d1-867c-549e-9129-6ff793087828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The swallowing center is located in', '["Medulla (reticular formation around NTS and nucleus ambiguus)","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (reticular formation around NTS and nucleus ambiguus)', 'The swallowing center lies in the medullary reticular formation around the nucleus tractus solitarius and nucleus ambiguus, coordinating the pharyngeal phase of deglutition. The cerebellum, midbrain, spinal cord, and pons are not the location.'),
('5e8b5137-4a91-5a3e-b190-2702adf8da78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pneumotaxic center is located in the', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Nucleus parabrachialis and Kölliker-Fuse nucleus (dorsolateral pons)","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus parabrachialis and Kölliker-Fuse nucleus (dorsolateral pons)', 'The pneumotaxic center is formed by the nucleus parabrachialis and Kölliker-Fuse nucleus in the dorsolateral pons, and it shortens inspiration by modulating the medullary respiratory centers. The medulla, cerebellum, midbrain, and thalamus are incorrect locations.'),
('852a8a9b-9e70-5c89-9197-8ccf2e4bfba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Kölliker-Fuse nucleus is involved in', '["Pain modulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Respiratory control (pneumotaxic modulation of inspiration)","Auditory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor coordination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory control (pneumotaxic modulation of inspiration)', 'The Kölliker-Fuse nucleus, part of the dorsolateral pontine pneumotaxic center, helps terminate inspiration and stabilize the respiratory pattern. Pain, vision, hearing, and motor coordination are not its functions.'),
('ddca090f-aed0-5ec1-af24-d7061fe69146'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The descending hypothalamic fibers to autonomic nuclei travel in', '["Stria terminalis","Fornix","Dorsal longitudinal fasciculus (Schutz)","Mammillothalamic tract","Medial forebrain bundle"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal longitudinal fasciculus (Schutz)', 'Hypothalamic autonomic fibers descend in the dorsal longitudinal fasciculus (fasciculus of Schütz) to reach the parasympathetic and sympathetic nuclei of the brainstem and spinal cord. The stria terminalis, fornix, mammillothalamic tract, and medial forebrain bundle carry other connections.'),
('9437047a-9eeb-5950-95f8-d76433ce2293'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The mammillary bodies project to', '["Cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior thalamus via mammillothalamic tract (Vicq d''Azyr)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior thalamus via mammillothalamic tract (Vicq d''Azyr)', 'The mammillary bodies project to the anterior nuclei of the thalamus through the mammillothalamic tract (bundle of Vicq d''Azyr), forming part of the Papez circuit. Cortex, hypothalamus, hippocampus, and cerebellum are not their main projection targets.'),
('9fa0013f-5bfe-5c5a-bcac-7a11a00c39ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The fornix connects', '["Hippocampus to mammillary bodies","Amygdala to hypothalamus","Mammillary bodies to thalamus","Thalamus to cortex","Hippocampus to cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus to mammillary bodies', 'The fornix is the major fiber bundle running from the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies, also giving fibers to the septal nuclei. Amygdalar, mammillothalamic, and thalamocortical connections travel through other tracts.'),
('0cc50f38-8aa2-502c-82bb-d507d224c26c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The stria medullaris thalami connects', '["Hippocampus to mammillary bodies","Septal nuclei/habenula to interpeduncular nucleus","Amygdala to hypothalamus","Cortex to thalamus","Mammillary bodies to thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Septal nuclei/habenula to interpeduncular nucleus', 'The stria medullaris thalami carries fibers from the septal nuclei and habenula toward the interpeduncular nucleus via the habenulo-interpeduncular tract. Hippocampal, amygdalar, cortical, and mammillary connections use different pathways.'),
('71acd13d-74ff-5956-8936-1ffd5aec7004'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The habenular nucleus (epithalamus) connects to', '["Mammillary bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Interpeduncular nucleus via habenulo-interpeduncular tract (fasciculus retroflexus)","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interpeduncular nucleus via habenulo-interpeduncular tract (fasciculus retroflexus)', 'The habenular nucleus projects through the habenulo-interpeduncular tract (fasciculus retroflexus) to the interpeduncular nucleus in the midbrain. Its main target is not the mammillary bodies, spinal cord, thalamus, or cerebellum.'),
('86817543-7202-5da4-ab6d-da6e3a8ab23b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The interpeduncular nucleus is located in', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain (ventral tegmentum between cerebral peduncles)","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (ventral tegmentum between cerebral peduncles)', 'The interpeduncular nucleus sits in the ventral midbrain tegmentum between the cerebral peduncles, in the floor of the interpeduncular fossa. It is not located in the medulla, pons, thalamus, or cerebellum.'),
('596ae10a-2eae-5f07-afdf-6fd6449d0466'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The posterior perforated substance is located', '["At base of pons resulting in decreased functional activity","In temporal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In frontal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","At base of medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between cerebral peduncles (midbrain) in interpeduncular fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Between cerebral peduncles (midbrain) in interpeduncular fossa', 'The posterior perforated substance is the region on the midbrain floor between the cerebral peduncles within the interpeduncular fossa, pierced by branches of the posterior cerebral artery. It is not at the base of the pons or medulla, nor in the temporal or frontal lobe.'),
('c9ff93b8-e658-5799-8614-fbab12227664'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The anterior perforated substance is located', '["In occipital lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","At base of midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial to lateral fissure (basal forebrain, over olfactory trigone)","At base of pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In temporal lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to lateral fissure (basal forebrain, over olfactory trigone)', 'The anterior perforated substance lies in the basal forebrain medial to the lateral fissure, over the olfactory trigone, and is perforated by the lateral striate (lenticulostriate) arteries. It is not located in the occipital lobe, midbrain, pons, or temporal lobe.'),
('017efc14-900a-55fe-b736-23595e3b4c0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The tuber cinereum is part of', '["Pituitary gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothalamus (between optic chiasm and mammillary bodies)","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus (between optic chiasm and mammillary bodies)', 'The tuber cinereum is a gray prominence of the hypothalamus lying between the optic chiasm and the mammillary bodies, giving origin to the infundibulum. It is not part of the pituitary, thalamus, midbrain, or medulla.'),
('cbf6cea8-6780-5911-b565-bc88d976907c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The infundibulum (pituitary stalk) connects', '["Thalamus to hypothalamus","Midbrain to pituitary","Anterior pituitary to hypothalamus","Hypothalamus to posterior pituitary","Pons to pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus to posterior pituitary', 'The infundibulum (pituitary stalk) connects the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis), carrying the axons of vasopressin- and oxytocin-secreting neurons. It does not connect the thalamus, midbrain, pons, or anterior pituitary in this manner.'),
('df60d472-1744-572f-9ab4-1bfbd519a566'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The median eminence of hypothalamus is where', '["Cortisol is regulated","CSF is produced","Dopamine is released","Portal blood system for anterior pituitary begins","Posterior pituitary releases hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Portal blood system for anterior pituitary begins', 'The median eminence is where hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones enter the capillary plexus that forms the portal veins of the anterior pituitary, delivering these factors directly to anterior pituitary cells. It is not the site of CSF production, cortisol regulation, dopamine release, or posterior pituitary hormone release.'),
('f7c952cd-1d88-544f-823c-f4842cd92e93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The tuberal (arcuate) nucleus produces', '["Growth hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","Dopamine (prolactin-inhibiting factor) and releasing hormones","Corticotropin mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxytocin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ADH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine (prolactin-inhibiting factor) and releasing hormones', 'The arcuate (tuberal) nucleus synthesizes dopamine, which serves as prolactin-inhibiting factor, together with releasing hormones such as GnRH and GHRH that travel to the anterior pituitary via the portal system. Growth hormone, corticotropin, oxytocin, and ADH are not produced here.'),
('ee058631-52bf-5e3f-b92b-777cbb5ccdde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The supraoptic nucleus produces', '["Oxytocin only","Somatostatin","Arginine vasopressin (ADH) and some oxytocin","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone","Corticotropin-releasing hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Arginine vasopressin (ADH) and some oxytocin', 'Magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic nucleus mainly synthesize arginine vasopressin (ADH) with smaller amounts of oxytocin, which travel through the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract to the posterior pituitary. The other hormones are produced by different hypothalamic cell groups.'),
('c671180b-384e-5a9b-a8c6-11d077896d59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The paraventricular nucleus produces', '["Somatostatin","ADH only","Oxytocin (and some ADH)","GHRH","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin (and some ADH)', 'The paraventricular nucleus chiefly produces oxytocin, along with some ADH, and projects to the posterior pituitary; it also makes releasing hormones such as CRH. Somatostatin, GHRH, and dopamine arise from other hypothalamic nuclei.'),
('00f1b251-e4bf-5707-99f7-0aa5bb549768'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei project to', '["Anterior pituitary via portal system resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior pituitary via hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract","Median eminence mediated by specific receptor activation","Mammillary bodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Third ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior pituitary via hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract', 'Axons from the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei descend through the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract to the posterior pituitary, where ADH and oxytocin are released into the bloodstream. The portal system instead serves the anterior pituitary.'),
('c3a731bf-f4c6-5354-bfb2-f5ab55a3e418'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system connects', '["Pons to pituitary","Hypothalamus (median eminence) to anterior pituitary","Hypothalamus to posterior pituitary","Posterior pituitary to anterior pituitary","Third ventricle to pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus (median eminence) to anterior pituitary', 'The portal system carries blood from the median eminence of the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary, transporting releasing and inhibiting hormones to the gland. The posterior pituitary is served by the neural hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract, not by portal vessels.'),
('2099019f-da94-54be-a3c9-84cd8896382e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which hypothalamic nucleus is the satiety center?', '["Paraventricular nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Ventromedial nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus","Lateral hypothalamic area"]'::jsonb, 'Ventromedial nucleus', 'The ventromedial nucleus acts as the satiety center; stimulating it suppresses feeding while destroying it causes hyperphagia and obesity. The lateral hypothalamic area is the feeding center, and the other nuclei do not primarily control satiety.'),
('e30ba03b-fbcf-5262-a07a-fbf3497ed3aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lesion of ventromedial hypothalamus causes', '["Hyperphagia and obesity","Diabetes insipidus","Polydipsia","Anorexia","Hypothermia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperphagia and obesity', 'Destruction of the ventromedial nucleus removes satiety inhibition, leading to hyperphagia and obesity. Diabetes insipidus follows damage to the supraoptic or paraventricular system, and lateral hypothalamic damage produces anorexia.'),
('328a5a0b-14b9-5a5b-8ed1-3fd1447c1e37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lesion of lateral hypothalamus causes', '["Obesity","Polydipsia","Anorexia and weight loss","Hyperthermia","Hyperphagia"]'::jsonb, 'Anorexia and weight loss', 'The lateral hypothalamic area is the feeding center, so its destruction causes anorexia and weight loss. Obesity follows ventromedial nucleus lesions, while polydipsia and hyperthermia arise through separate mechanisms.'),
('60d266e5-cf38-571a-95fb-a02a2b23eaae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which hypothalamic nucleus regulates body temperature (heat conservation)?', '["Supraoptic nucleus","Mammillary body","Anterior hypothalamus (preoptic area)","Suprachiasmatic nucleus","Posterior hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior hypothalamus', 'The posterior hypothalamus promotes heat conservation and generation; its damage results in poikilothermia. The anterior (preoptic) hypothalamus is concerned with heat loss, and the other nuclei do not primarily regulate temperature conservation.'),
('f9e523e1-7e54-58df-abf1-58d423894da6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lesion of anterior hypothalamus (preoptic) causes', '["Diabetes insipidus resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothermia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obesity mediated by specific receptor activation","Polydipsia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperthermia (inability to cool)"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthermia (inability to cool)', 'Lesions of the anterior hypothalamus (preoptic area) destroy heat-loss mechanisms such as sweating and vasodilation, producing hyperthermia with inability to cool. Hypothermia and obesity do not follow this lesion, and diabetes insipidus is unrelated to temperature control.'),
('0a832135-e65c-5dc2-8c86-04ca0d7f36f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Lesion of posterior hypothalamus causes', '["Obesity resulting in decreased functional activity","Anorexia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fever mediated by specific receptor activation","Poikilothermia (inability to generate heat)","Hyperthermia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Poikilothermia (inability to generate heat)', 'Posterior hypothalamic damage impairs heat production and conservation, so the patient''s temperature tracks the environment, a state called poikilothermia. Anterior hypothalamic damage instead causes hyperthermia from inability to lose heat.'),
('8f475a2f-f1b8-528a-8338-c428feb68d7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) is the', '["Sexual behavior center resulting in decreased functional activity","Circadian rhythm pacemaker (biological clock)","Satiety center mediated by specific receptor activation","Thirst center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Feeding center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circadian rhythm pacemaker (biological clock)', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus receives direct retinal light input and serves as the master circadian rhythm pacemaker, driving sleep-wake and neuroendocrine rhythms. The other centers govern feeding, thirst, and sexual behavior.'),
('f8e9a96f-5e64-518d-b880-ede7856096ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The retinohypothalamic tract carries light information from retina to', '["Superior colliculus","Pretectal nucleus","Lateral geniculate body","Visual cortex","Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)"]'::jsonb, 'Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)', 'The retinohypothalamic tract transmits light information directly to the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the circadian pacemaker. The superior colliculus, pretectal nucleus, and lateral geniculate body belong to other visual pathways.'),
('93aeaba2-9a99-5fb5-97ab-16969cdfe800'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pineal gland produces', '["Melatonin (circadian rhythm regulation)","ADH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oxytocin mediated by specific receptor activation","Dopamine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Serotonin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Melatonin (circadian rhythm regulation)', 'The pineal gland synthesizes and secretes melatonin, which regulates the circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle, with secretion rising in darkness. It does not produce ADH, oxytocin, dopamine, or serotonin as its principal product.'),
('7c635033-8838-542c-861a-212b1ec71e15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pineal gland calcification is visible on imaging and may indicate', '["Normal aging process (corpora arenacea)","Atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemorrhage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal aging process (corpora arenacea)', 'Calcification of the pineal gland, known as corpora arenacea or brain sand, is a normal finding that increases with age and is commonly seen on imaging. It does not by itself indicate atrophy, tumor, infection, or hemorrhage.'),
('8d8d7243-14bb-5a32-9738-bd21dbe39aa3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pineal gland tumors (germinoma) can cause', '["Optic atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Diabetes insipidus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary dysfunction mediated by specific receptor activation","Parinaud syndrome (dorsal midbrain compression)","Olfactory loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parinaud syndrome (dorsal midbrain compression)', 'Germinomas of the pineal region compress the dorsal midbrain, affecting the superior colliculus and pretectal area, and classically cause Parinaud syndrome with paralysis of upward gaze. They do not directly produce optic atrophy, diabetes insipidus, or olfactory loss.'),
('18c8463a-b007-5e68-8d65-c3618488d6ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The habenula (epithalamus) is involved in', '["Visual processing resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor coordination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auditory processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain modulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limbic-motor integration (reward and aversion)"]'::jsonb, 'Limbic-motor integration (reward and aversion)', 'The habenula, part of the epithalamus, integrates limbic inputs with motor output and plays a role in reward and aversion processing. It is not a primary visual, motor-coordination, auditory, or pain-processing structure.'),
('c8b03a1b-98a5-500a-8efe-55e3bae6d02c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The posterior commissure is located in', '["Pons resulting in decreased functional activity","Epithalamus (midbrain-diencephalon junction)","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epithalamus (midbrain-diencephalon junction)', 'The posterior commissure lies in the epithalamus at the junction of the midbrain and diencephalon, crossing the midline just rostral to the aqueduct. It is not located in the pons, cerebellum, medulla, or spinal cord.'),
('ba56fefd-bff6-54c6-ba48-576587a1fe58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The subthalamic nucleus is involved in', '["Motor control (part of basal ganglia circuitry)","Pain modulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Audition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endocrine regulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor control (part of basal ganglia circuitry)', 'The subthalamic nucleus is a component of the basal ganglia circuitry that controls movement; damage to it, as in hemiballismus, disrupts motor function. It does not mediate pain, vision, audition, or endocrine regulation.'),
('00db769c-0df3-5991-962d-61cac5d4ba13'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The zona incerta (subthalamus) is a', '["Motor nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Reticular nucleus-like structure with wide connections","Endocrine nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reticular nucleus-like structure with wide connections', 'The zona incerta is a diffuse gray zone of the subthalamus with widespread reciprocal connections, functionally resembling the reticular nucleus of the thalamus. It is not a discrete motor, endocrine, sensory, or visual nucleus.'),
('0c48f8e2-309b-5536-bdbd-545c0298c8a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The fields of Forel (subthalamus) contain', '["Corticospinal fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Pallidothalamic and cerebellothalamic fibers","Olfactory fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pallidothalamic and cerebellothalamic fibers', 'The fields of Forel (H1 and H2) in the subthalamus carry pallidothalamic and cerebello-thalamic fibers en route to the thalamus. They do not contain corticospinal, olfactory, optic, or general sensory fibers.'),
('e759cc36-3037-5c0d-a38f-8d9a20d827c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The substantia nigra is located in', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal forebrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain (between tegmentum and crus cerebri)","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (between tegmentum and crus cerebri)', 'The substantia nigra lies in the midbrain between the tegmentum and the crus cerebri; its pars compacta provides the main dopaminergic projection to the striatum. It is not located in the thalamus, pons, medulla, or basal forebrain.'),
('774f8f00-c324-5c5b-8fbd-74425927e25d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Parkinson disease involves degeneration of', '["Serotonergic raphe nuclei","GABAergic neurons in striatum","Cholinergic neurons in basal forebrain","Dopaminergic neurons in substantia nigra pars compacta","Noradrenergic locus coeruleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic neurons in substantia nigra pars compacta', 'Parkinson disease is characterized by degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, depleting striatal dopamine and causing bradykinesia, rigidity, and tremor. The raphe nuclei, striatal interneurons, basal forebrain, and locus coeruleus are not the primary site of degeneration.'),
('304e9017-d84e-5803-b5d4-db1bed14bfa1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The basal ganglia receive dopaminergic input from', '["Substantia nigra pars reticulata","Globus pallidus","Ventral tegmental area","Putamen","Substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc)"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc)', 'The pars compacta of the substantia nigra (SNc) sends dopaminergic fibers to the striatum, a key input of basal ganglia circuitry. The pars reticulata is the output zone, the globus pallidus is a relay, and the ventral tegmental area projects mainly to limbic structures.'),
('c9cd967d-6bf4-5c79-bf21-5af7d08a4e0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The corticopontocerebellar pathway (cerebrocerebellar) involves', '["Cortex → pontine nuclei (pons) → middle cerebellar peduncle → cerebellum","Cortex → thalamus → cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex → olive → cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex → red nucleus → cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex → spinal cord → cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex → pontine nuclei (pons) → middle cerebellar peduncle → cerebellum', 'The cerebrocerebellar pathway runs from the cerebral cortex to the pontine nuclei, which then project through the middle cerebellar peduncle to the contralateral cerebellar hemisphere. The routes via the thalamus, olive, red nucleus, or spinal cord are not the corticopontocerebellar pathway.'),
('da282cba-0382-5732-b7c9-69f44ce7df5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Pontine nuclei receive input from', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Brainstem accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebral cortex (ipsilateral) via corticopontine fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral cortex (ipsilateral) via corticopontine fibers', 'The pontine nuclei receive ipsilateral corticopontine fibers from the cerebral cortex and relay them, via the middle cerebellar peduncle, to the cerebellum. Their principal input does not come from the cerebellum, brainstem, thalamus, or spinal cord.'),
('3d24e758-1092-50e9-8bc3-f2726193a63f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cerebrocerebellar pathway is important for', '["Balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain modulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Planning and coordination of voluntary movements","Reflex control without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autonomic regulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Planning and coordination of voluntary movements', 'The cerebrocerebellar (neocerebellar) pathway is important for planning and coordinating voluntary movements, especially of the limbs. Balance is handled mainly by the vestibulocerebellum, and pain, reflexes, and autonomic control are not its functions.'),
('67e02582-b789-524d-9141-a4f733a66c16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The red nucleus receives cerebellar input via', '["Inferior cerebellar peduncle","Middle cerebellar peduncle","Spinocerebellar tract","Superior cerebellar peduncle (decussated)","Pontine nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Superior cerebellar peduncle (decussated)', 'Cerebellar output fibers, including the dentatorubral projections, reach the red nucleus through the superior cerebellar peduncle after decussating in the midbrain. The inferior and middle peduncles carry afferents to the cerebellum, not its output to the red nucleus.'),
('a6afa6cc-4c97-5300-bb3a-e65f67324145'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The rubrothalamic tract connects', '["Red nucleus → spinal cord","Red nucleus → basal ganglia","Red nucleus → thalamus (VPL/VL)","Red nucleus → cortex","Red nucleus → cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Red nucleus → thalamus (VPL/VL)', 'The rubrothalamic tract runs from the red nucleus to the contralateral thalamus, mainly the VL nucleus with some VPL contribution, forming part of the cerebello-cortical motor loop. It does not target the spinal cord, basal ganglia, cortex, or cerebellum.'),
('be4fe0bc-81d8-58f9-a5eb-72fa01a53216'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The reticulotegmental nucleus of pons projects to', '["Basal ganglia resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum (as mossy fibers via MCP)","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum (as mossy fibers via MCP)', 'The reticulotegmental nucleus of the pons sends mossy fiber projections to the cerebellum through the middle cerebellar peduncle. Its principal output does not go to the basal ganglia, cortex, thalamus, or spinal cord.'),
('70d4890d-c48d-523a-9c8e-ae2045fbe9f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The lateral reticular nucleus (medulla) projects to', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum (mossy fibers via ICP)","Basal ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum (mossy fibers via ICP)', 'The lateral reticular nucleus of the medulla projects mossy fibers to the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle. The other listed structures are not the destination of its main output.'),
('2dd87dfa-ca5c-5915-8768-d99e8fce41f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The paramedian reticular nucleus (medulla) projects to', '["Spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum (mossy fibers via ICP)","Cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basal ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum (mossy fibers via ICP)', 'The paramedian reticular nucleus of the medulla projects mossy fibers to the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle. It does not project to the spinal cord, thalamus, cortex, or basal ganglia.'),
('3c799ad2-f812-56e5-9250-71ae96c34752'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus of Kölliker-Fuse is part of', '["Reticular formation","Cerebellar nuclei","Pontine respiratory group (pneumotaxic)","Medullary respiratory group","Basal ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine respiratory group (pneumotaxic)', 'The nucleus of Kölliker-Fuse belongs to the pontine respiratory group (pneumotaxic center), which helps regulate the pattern of breathing. It is not part of the medullary respiratory group, the cerebellar nuclei, or the basal ganglia.'),
('2aaad1e5-9521-5611-8dee-377e61dbc024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus paragigantocellularis (medulla) is involved in', '["Pain modulation and autonomic regulation","Hearing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Balance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor coordination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain modulation and autonomic regulation', 'The nucleus paragigantocellularis in the rostral medulla participates in descending pain modulation and cardiovascular and autonomic regulation. It is not primarily involved in hearing, vision, balance, or motor coordination.'),
('00338a00-759d-5470-81a9-bd04bc33b0aa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus raphe magnus (medulla) projects to', '["Spinal cord dorsal horn (serotonergic pain inhibition)","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord dorsal horn (serotonergic pain inhibition)', 'The nucleus raphe magnus sends serotonergic fibers to the spinal cord dorsal horn, where they inhibit pain transmission; it is a key relay of the descending pain-inhibitory pathway. The other structures are not its primary target.'),
('a19db50e-f913-5cc2-8243-bfbe90767205'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The periaqueductal gray (PAG) receives input from', '["Hypothalamus, amygdala, frontal cortex","Basal ganglia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord only mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus, amygdala, frontal cortex', 'The periaqueductal gray receives limbic and cortical input from the hypothalamus, amygdala, and frontal cortex and relays it to the raphe nuclei for descending pain control. Its principal afferents do not come only from the basal ganglia, spinal cord, thalamus, or cerebellum.'),
('25f40b65-5807-5103-85fc-fae8b8f17746'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus cuneiformis (midbrain reticular) is involved in', '["Taste only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain modulation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Audition only mediated by specific receptor activation","Locomotion (mesencephalic locomotor region)","Vision only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Locomotion (mesencephalic locomotor region)', 'The nucleus cuneiformis in the midbrain reticular formation is part of the mesencephalic locomotor region and helps initiate and control locomotion. Its role is not limited to taste, pain, audition, or vision.'),
('bc94208c-abc4-5d67-95a5-4982f644eb59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pedunculopontine nucleus (PPN) is involved in', '["Pain modulation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Audition only mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Locomotion, sleep-wake cycle, and muscle tone"]'::jsonb, 'Locomotion, sleep-wake cycle, and muscle tone', 'The pedunculopontine nucleus, a cholinergic nucleus of the upper brainstem, is involved in locomotion, the sleep-wake cycle, and muscle tone regulation. Its functions are not limited to pain, vision, audition, or taste.'),
('ae9514ef-9cf4-5992-a722-64291731f7f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The laterodorsal tegmental nucleus (LDT) is', '["Dopaminergic nucleus","Cholinergic nucleus involved in REM sleep","GABAergic nucleus","Noradrenergic nucleus","Serotonergic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Cholinergic nucleus involved in REM sleep', 'The laterodorsal tegmental nucleus is a cholinergic nucleus that participates in generating REM sleep. It is neither dopaminergic, GABAergic, noradrenergic, nor serotonergic.'),
('5240eac7-5d6d-559a-a2ef-285ac1a99f61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Brainstem death is characterized by', '["Absent only pupillary reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Only absent respiratory drive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absent only motor function mediated by specific receptor activation","Absent pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic, gag, and respiratory reflexes","Coma with preserved brainstem reflexes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absent pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic, gag, and respiratory reflexes', 'Brainstem death requires the absence of all brainstem reflexes, including pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic (doll''s eye), gag and cough, and respiratory reflexes. Loss of a single reflex or preservation of brainstem reflexes excludes the diagnosis, and coma alone is insufficient.'),
('694723df-1f12-59af-86cd-1d978f2de6fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Apnea testing (no spontaneous breathing at PaCO2 > 60 mmHg) confirms', '["Brainstem death (loss of medullary respiratory centers)","Cerebellar death accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord injury mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamic death without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical death leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brainstem death (loss of medullary respiratory centers)', 'Apnea testing showing no spontaneous respiratory effort despite a PaCO2 above 60 mmHg indicates loss of the medullary respiratory centers and supports the diagnosis of brainstem death. It does not indicate spinal, cerebellar, thalamic, or isolated cortical damage.'),
('5ea81612-5a26-5bd1-b49e-d7ec0aa36b08'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Consciousness requires intact function of', '["Only cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Reticular activating system (RAS) and cerebral cortex","Cerebellum only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only brainstem without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basal ganglia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reticular activating system (RAS) and cerebral cortex', 'Normal consciousness requires an intact reticular activating system in the upper brainstem acting upon the cerebral cortex; damage to either component impairs awareness. The cerebellum and basal ganglia are not required for consciousness.'),
('eb6b2357-7e53-574f-8fde-e6c39a80a5f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Coma results from bilateral damage to', '["One hemisphere resulting in decreased functional activity","Reticular formation in upper pons/midbrain or widespread cortical damage","Spinal cord alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reticular formation in upper pons/midbrain or widespread cortical damage', 'Coma results from bilateral damage to the reticular activating system, typically the reticular formation of the upper pons or midbrain, or from widespread bilateral cortical damage. Unilateral hemispheric lesions and isolated spinal, thalamic, or cerebellar damage do not cause coma.'),
('e0647a75-7fd2-5afe-aa3a-a2546bc1b342'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Vegetative state (wakefulness without awareness) is due to', '["Spinal cord injury resulting in decreased functional activity","Peripheral nerve injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar death mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortical or thalamic damage with intact brainstem","Brainstem death leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortical or thalamic damage with intact brainstem', 'The vegetative state is a wakeful state without awareness caused by cortical or thalamic damage while the brainstem reticular activating system remains intact. Brainstem death, by contrast, destroys wakefulness itself, and the other listed injuries do not produce this state.'),
('1feb4994-3295-536e-b463-3b388b83be8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The minimally conscious state implies', '["Complete awareness resulting in decreased functional activity","awareness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brain death mediated by specific receptor activation","Intermittent but reproducible evidence of awareness","Persistent coma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermittent but reproducible evidence of awareness', 'The minimally conscious state is characterized by intermittent but reproducible evidence of awareness, such as purposeful behavioral responses, even though behavior is inconsistent. It differs from persistent coma and brain death, and full continuous awareness is not implied.'),
('cecce662-ae04-53c5-bd67-79e55694f01a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which brainstem nucleus is a major source of serotonin?', '["Substantia nigra","Ventral tegmental area","Raphe nuclei (median and dorsal raphe)","Locus coeruleus","Nucleus basalis"]'::jsonb, 'Raphe nuclei (median and dorsal raphe)', 'The raphe nuclei, including the median and dorsal raphe, are the major serotonergic cell groups of the brainstem. The substantia nigra, ventral tegmental area, locus coeruleus, and nucleus basalis produce other neurotransmitters.'),
('d9889137-5eb8-5c72-ad33-2254f5e51075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which brainstem nucleus is a major source of noradrenaline?', '["Substantia nigra","Nucleus basalis","Locus coeruleus (pons)","Ventral tegmental area","Raphe nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Locus coeruleus (pons)', 'The locus coeruleus in the dorsal pons is the major source of noradrenaline in the brain. The substantia nigra, nucleus basalis, ventral tegmental area, and raphe nuclei synthesize different neurotransmitters.'),
('ad7dcb21-1a71-507d-b88a-6cc76f80b265'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which brainstem nucleus is a major source of dopamine?', '["Substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area","Raphe nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nucleus basalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Locus coeruleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pedunculopontine nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area', 'The substantia nigra pars compacta and the ventral tegmental area are the main dopaminergic cell groups of the brainstem. The raphe nuclei, nucleus basalis, locus coeruleus, and pedunculopontine nucleus synthesize other transmitters.'),
('14288853-f0e2-5dcc-a1cf-a56253f76a9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which brainstem nucleus is a major source of acetylcholine?', '["Locus coeruleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Pedunculopontine, laterodorsal tegmental, nucleus basalis","Hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Raphe nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Substantia nigra leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pedunculopontine, laterodorsal tegmental, nucleus basalis', 'Cholinergic projections to the forebrain arise from the pedunculopontine nucleus, the laterodorsal tegmental nucleus, and the nucleus basalis of Meynert. The locus coeruleus, hypothalamus, raphe nuclei, and substantia nigra are not cholinergic sources.'),
('92ed63d3-2cf3-5117-8c60-d5f96b96c0cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The area A1 (noradrenergic) in medulla is located near', '["Solitary nucleus","Hypoglossal nucleus","Nucleus ambiguus","Inferior olive","Lateral reticular nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral reticular nucleus', 'The A1 noradrenergic cell group lies in the ventrolateral medulla in close association with the lateral reticular nucleus. The solitary, hypoglossal, and dorsal vagal nuclei are dorsomedial, and the inferior olive lies ventromedially.'),
('c3a1a8e1-9ac1-5672-b60e-e0ea417ac215'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The area A2 (noradrenergic) in medulla is located in', '["Inferior olive","Dorsal vagal nucleus","Lateral reticular nucleus","Nucleus tractus solitarius","Nucleus ambiguus"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus tractus solitarius', 'The A2 noradrenergic cell group is located in the dorsomedial medulla, closely associated with the nucleus tractus solitarius. The other listed nuclei lie elsewhere in the medulla and do not define the A2 territory.'),
('40eb0a09-71a5-5e34-85f2-46e94c21d64f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The area C1 (adrenergic) in medulla is located in', '["Dorsal medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventrolateral medulla (rostral)","Midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventrolateral medulla (rostral)', 'The C1 adrenergic neurons occupy the rostral ventrolateral medulla, a region involved in vasomotor control. The dorsal medulla, midbrain, pons, and spinal cord are incorrect locations for these cells.'),
('a48fc2c5-d54c-5fbe-a108-3daddc0572c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) receives input from', '["CN VII, IX, X (taste and visceral afferents)","CN III, IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V, VI mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XI, XII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VIII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII, IX, X (taste and visceral afferents)', 'The rostral NTS receives gustatory fibers from CN VII, IX, and X, while its caudal part receives visceral (baro- and chemoreceptor) afferents from CN IX and X. Cranial nerves III, IV, V, VI, VIII, XI, and XII do not terminate in the NTS.'),
('6069a937-2589-5176-9801-8bea4d497414'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Baroreceptor reflex (blood pressure regulation) involves', '["CN III → Edinger-Westphal resulting in decreased functional activity","CN V → trigeminal nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII → facial nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VIII → vestibular nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX (carotid sinus) and CN X (aortic arch) → NTS → medullary vasomotor center"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (carotid sinus) and CN X (aortic arch) → NTS → medullary vasomotor center', 'Carotid sinus baroreceptors report via CN IX and aortic arch baroreceptors via CN X to the NTS, which then tunes the medullary vasomotor center. CN III, V, VII, and VIII are not part of this reflex arc.'),
('b7e8b70a-bd74-56c7-8927-874fd93b1100'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The vasomotor center (medulla) is located in', '["Ventrolateral medulla (rostral C1 and caudal A1)","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","Pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventrolateral medulla (rostral C1 and caudal A1)', 'The medullary vasomotor center resides in the ventrolateral medulla, formed by rostral C1 (pressor) and caudal A1 (depressor) neurons. It does not lie in the spinal cord, dorsal medulla, pons, or midbrain.'),
('181971b9-9d57-5db2-8b58-ecbf9900ab74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The cardiovagal center (medulla) is located in', '["Inferior olive resulting in decreased functional activity","Nucleus tractus solitarius accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal vagal nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral reticular nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nucleus ambiguus (parasympathetic to heart via CN X)"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus ambiguus (parasympathetic to heart via CN X)', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic neurons of the cardiovagal center reside in the nucleus ambiguus and slow the heart via the vagus nerve. The NTS supplies afferent input, while the inferior olive and lateral reticular nucleus have no cardiovagal outflow.'),
('9310391a-00d2-5518-b18f-0b3fcc084f49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medullary vasomotor center controls blood pressure via', '["Parasympathetic output only resulting in decreased functional activity","Endocrine secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatic motor output mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory modulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic output (intermediolateral cell column T1-L2)"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic output (intermediolateral cell column T1-L2)', 'Bulbospinal neurons of the vasomotor center project to sympathetic preganglionic cells in the intermediolateral column (T1-L2), maintaining vasomotor tone. Parasympathetic, endocrine, somatic, and sensory pathways are not the effector route for resting blood pressure control.'),
('4811461c-ca2e-5754-ba7b-165f7023df2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Cushing reflex (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) indicates', '["Myocardial infarction resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypovolemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal shock mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased intracranial pressure (medullary ischemia)","Sepsis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intracranial pressure (medullary ischemia)', 'Rising intracranial pressure causes medullary ischemia and triggers hypertension with reflex bradycardia and irregular respiration, the Cushing reflex. Myocardial infarction, hypovolemia, spinal shock, and sepsis do not produce this specific triad.'),
('18b02faa-d8be-54a9-9d73-1185cf6a74a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The brainstem centers for cardiovascular control are in', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla (ventrolateral, nucleus ambiguus, NTS)","Pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (ventrolateral, nucleus ambiguus, NTS)', 'The vasomotor center, cardiovagal nucleus ambiguus, and NTS all lie in the medulla and integrate blood pressure and heart rate control. The cerebellum, midbrain, pons, and thalamus are not the primary cardiovascular regulatory sites.'),
('87608573-2fe1-5139-9137-aa904544b8dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Nucleus of the solitary tract (NTS) is essential for', '["Visual processing resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory processing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor coordination mediated by specific receptor activation","Visceral sensory processing (taste, baro-, chemo-, GI)","Auditory processing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral sensory processing (taste, baro-, chemo-, GI)', 'The NTS is the primary relay for visceral sensation, including taste, baroreceptor, chemoreceptor, and gastrointestinal afferents. Visual, olfactory, motor, and auditory functions are handled by other brainstem and forebrain structures.'),
('d24d1736-7eb3-5576-9cf7-33149af07742'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The central nucleus of the amygdala projects to brainstem for', '["Pain modulation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomic and behavioral responses to fear","Motor control without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Speech leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic and behavioral responses to fear', 'The central nucleus of the amygdala drives autonomic and behavioral fear responses by projecting to brainstem and hypothalamic centers. Pain, vision, general motor control, and speech are not the main targets of this output.'),
('98fe7f0c-676d-554b-ba3c-04035dc9c0d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The bed nucleus of stria terminalis (BNST) is involved in', '["Audition resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor coordination mediated by specific receptor activation","Stress and anxiety responses (extended amygdala)","Vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stress and anxiety responses (extended amygdala)', 'The BNST, part of the extended amygdala, regulates sustained stress and anxiety responses through its connections with the hypothalamus and brainstem. Audition, olfaction, motor coordination, and vision do not depend on the BNST.'),
('110b7705-84c7-55f9-a4a4-aeffe0aa54a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The periaqueductal gray (PAG) is organized into', '["Functional columns (dorsal, lateral, ventrolateral) for specific behaviors","Only sensory function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Single cell type mediated by specific receptor activation","Random organization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Functional columns (dorsal, lateral, ventrolateral) for specific behaviors', 'The PAG is arranged into longitudinal functional columns (dorsal, lateral, and ventrolateral) that mediate different defensive, autonomic, and pain-modulating behaviors. It is neither a single cell type nor randomly organized, and its role spans sensory and motor function.'),
('3ed2358b-5fc8-5e7f-8f3a-97963d10ff82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The deep layers of superior colliculus contain', '["Only auditory cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Only touch cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Only visual cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Multisensory (visual, auditory, somatosensory) map for orienting"]'::jsonb, 'Multisensory (visual, auditory, somatosensory) map for orienting', 'Although the superficial layers of the superior colliculus are visual, the deep layers integrate visual, auditory, and somatosensory input into a multisensory map used for orienting eye and head movements. They are not exclusively auditory, touch, motor, or visual.'),
('9f19fa1f-81b6-5815-9465-b2490c45cd98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The brachium of inferior colliculus connects', '["Inferior colliculus to medial geniculate body","Inferior colliculus to superior colliculus","Lateral lemniscus to inferior colliculus","Superior olive to inferior colliculus","Cochlear nucleus to inferior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior colliculus to medial geniculate body', 'The brachium of the inferior colliculus carries the auditory pathway from the inferior colliculus to the medial geniculate body of the thalamus. The other options name earlier ascending auditory relays or collicular connections, not this tract.'),
('cf309262-6d00-52e7-bdaf-8faf2354802e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The brachium of superior colliculus connects', '["Superior colliculus to lateral geniculate body and cortex","Optic tract to superior colliculus","Cortex to superior colliculus","Superior colliculus to medial geniculate","Inferior colliculus to superior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior colliculus to lateral geniculate body and cortex', 'The brachium of the superior colliculus carries visual information from the superior colliculus toward the lateral geniculate body and visual cortex. The optic tract supplies the colliculus directly, and the medial geniculate is part of the auditory system.'),
('3825270c-683a-5d0c-bf83-535eedb86dec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medial geniculate body (MGB) is the thalamic relay for', '["Auditory pathway","Motor pathway","Olfactory pathway","Visual pathway","Somatosensory pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory pathway', 'The medial geniculate body relays auditory fibers from the inferior colliculus to the primary auditory cortex. The lateral geniculate handles vision, and the ventral posterior nuclei handle somatosensation.'),
('7e24ad25-c728-5c9a-bfb1-8d018f322d72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The lateral geniculate body (LGB) is the thalamic relay for', '["Motor pathway","Somatosensory pathway","Auditory pathway","Visual pathway","Olfactory pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Visual pathway', 'The lateral geniculate body receives optic tract fibers and relays visual input to the primary visual cortex. Auditory, somatosensory, motor, and olfactory modalities use different thalamic relays.'),
('fda446a9-ac33-593d-b086-18db22fd228d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral posterolateral nucleus (VPL) receives', '["Auditory input resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinothalamic and medial lemniscus input","Visual input mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Olfactory input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic and medial lemniscus input', 'The VPL receives body somatosensory input via the spinothalamic tract and medial lemniscus and projects to the primary somatosensory cortex. Auditory, visual, motor, and olfactory input do not terminate in VPL.'),
('94791f0f-2f87-5360-97b6-3c46c04ed120'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral posteromedial nucleus (VPM) receives', '["Body sensory input resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory input accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual input mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminothalamic (facial sensation) and gustatory input"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminothalamic (facial sensation) and gustatory input', 'The VPM receives trigeminothalamic fibers for facial sensation and gustatory fibers from the NTS, then relays to the somatosensory and insular cortex. Body sensation goes to VPL, and other listed modalities use different nuclei.'),
('b26dc7da-4995-5d96-9a19-e7fb677fb800'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral lateral (VL) nucleus of thalamus receives', '["Olfactory input resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar (dentate) and basal ganglia (GPi) input","Sensory input mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar (dentate) and basal ganglia (GPi) input', 'The VL nucleus receives dentatothalamic and globus pallidus internus fibers and relays to motor cortex, helping plan and coordinate voluntary movement. Sensory, visual, auditory, and olfactory input bypass VL.'),
('cab7f5cf-4c75-5c4e-ac2e-8a39d14ae572'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral anterior (VA) nucleus receives', '["Visual input","Cerebellar input","Auditory input","Sensory input","Basal ganglia (GPi/SNr) input"]'::jsonb, 'Basal ganglia (GPi/SNr) input', 'The VA nucleus receives basal ganglia outflow from GPi and substantia nigra pars reticulata and projects to prefrontal and premotor cortex. Cerebellar input goes to VL, and sensory, visual, and auditory input use other nuclei.'),
('2037d117-cb40-5b79-a38b-82fa03d0fa97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The anterior nucleus of thalamus receives input from', '["Mammillary bodies (via mammillothalamic tract) and hippocampus (via fornix)","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory systems without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies (via mammillothalamic tract) and hippocampus (via fornix)', 'The anterior nucleus receives the mammillothalamic tract from the mammillary bodies and hippocampal fibers via the fornix, forming part of the Papez circuit for memory. Basal ganglia, cerebellar, sensory, and visual input do not reach this nucleus.'),
('fa46fb38-4673-5c1c-bb5e-00926537dcd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The mediodorsal (MD) nucleus of thalamus connects to', '["Prefrontal cortex (reciprocal connections)","Sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prefrontal cortex (reciprocal connections)', 'The mediodorsal nucleus has reciprocal connections with the prefrontal cortex and is involved in memory and executive function. Sensory, motor, visual, and auditory cortical projections belong to other thalamic nuclei.'),
('80a88df6-5b4a-5263-a5f4-b2fe5f9ba5cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The pulvinar (thalamus) is involved in', '["Motor coordination only","Visual attention and eye movement coordination","Pain perception only","Auditory processing only","Olfaction only"]'::jsonb, 'Visual attention and eye movement coordination', 'The pulvinar integrates visual input with the parietal cortex and superior colliculus to direct visual attention and guide eye movements. It is not limited to motor, pain, auditory, or olfactory function.'),
('6389e5af-6367-5234-94b7-856d8a2500e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The intralaminar nuclei of thalamus (centromedian, parafascicular) are part of', '["Visual system resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ascending reticular activating system (arousal)","Motor system (with basal ganglia) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending reticular activating system (arousal)', 'The centromedian and parafascicular intralaminar nuclei belong to the nonspecific thalamic system and, via the ascending reticular activating system, regulate cortical arousal and attention. Visual, olfactory, and auditory relays use dedicated thalamic nuclei.'),
('e9728a3d-77ab-5c5f-803d-1edb5ea83820'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The reticular nucleus of thalamus is a', '["Motor relay nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual relay nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GABAergic shell that regulates thalamocortical neurons","Sensory relay nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excitatory relay nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GABAergic shell that regulates thalamocortical neurons', 'The reticular nucleus is a thin GABAergic shell over the lateral thalamus that inhibits thalamocortical relay neurons and helps gate attention. It does not relay motor, visual, or sensory modalities and is inhibitory rather than excitatory.'),
('235acaf9-c5cd-59da-949b-174980915877'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The centromedian nucleus (CM) of thalamus receives input from', '["Auditory systems resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory systems accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Globus pallidus (GPi) and ascending reticular formation","Visual systems without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Globus pallidus (GPi) and ascending reticular formation', 'The centromedian nucleus receives GPi and ascending reticular formation input and projects to the striatum and cerebral cortex. Auditory, sensory, visual, and cerebellar systems use different nuclei.'),
('364a4c1f-b509-5fa9-b51c-f4975a1b82a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The parafascicular nucleus (Pf) of thalamus receives from', '["Auditory system resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Globus pallidus (GPi) and is part of basal ganglia loop"]'::jsonb, 'Globus pallidus (GPi) and is part of basal ganglia loop', 'The parafascicular nucleus receives GPi and pedunculopontine input and participates in the basal ganglia loop to the striatum. Auditory, sensory, cerebellar, and visual pathways do not target this nucleus.'),
('f98459ed-624d-5e16-951d-8eb1616c7ee3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The lateral posterior nucleus (LP) of thalamus receives input from', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auditory system mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior colliculus (visual attention)","Basal ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior colliculus (visual attention)', 'The lateral posterior nucleus receives superior colliculus input and, with the pulvinar, contributes to visual attention. Cerebellar, olfactory, auditory, and basal ganglia input terminate in other nuclei.'),
('efa80650-9464-5b64-8174-ba61e5bbf38c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The posterior nuclear group of thalamus includes', '["Anterior, VL, VA resulting in decreased functional activity","Centromedian, parafascicular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulvinar, LP, MD mediated by specific receptor activation","Reticular nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limitans, suprageniculate, and medial geniculate"]'::jsonb, 'Limitans, suprageniculate, and medial geniculate', 'The posterior nuclear group consists of the limitans, suprageniculate, and medial geniculate nuclei. Anterior, ventral lateral, ventral anterior, centromedian, parafascicular, pulvinar, LP, MD, and reticular nuclei belong to other groupings.'),
('23eb3b7b-4e9c-519c-99e0-e1f33ad1487f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral medial posterior (VMpo) nucleus is involved in', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Audition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain and temperature processing","Motor coordination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Olfaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature processing', 'The VMpo nucleus relays spinothalamic pain and temperature input to the insular cortex. Vision, audition, motor coordination, and olfaction are handled by other pathways.'),
('41677640-1e06-57b2-8efd-610b4a73b580'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The submedius nucleus (Sm) of thalamus is involved in', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Audition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor coordination mediated by specific receptor activation","Olfaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain processing (medial pain pathway)"]'::jsonb, 'Pain processing (medial pain pathway)', 'The submedius nucleus participates in the medial pain pathway, relaying nociceptive input to the orbital and insular cortex for the affective aspect of pain. Vision, audition, motor coordination, and olfaction are not its functions.'),
('f6ea7836-ff7f-5a76-91c8-6fe3e7d7c364'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The lateral dorsal (LD) nucleus of thalamus connects with', '["Auditory cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior cingulate and parahippocampal cortex (limbic)","Visual cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cingulate and parahippocampal cortex (limbic)', 'The lateral dorsal nucleus connects with the posterior cingulate and parahippocampal cortex as part of the limbic system, participating in memory and spatial orientation. Auditory, sensory, motor, and visual cortices are linked to other nuclei.'),
('595051a3-0278-53e1-808a-301b004bc241'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The habenulo-interpeduncular tract (fasciculus retroflexus) connects', '["Habenula to interpeduncular nucleus (midbrain)","Mammillary bodies to thalamus","Striatum to globus pallidus","Cortex to pons","Hippocampus to mammillary bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Habenula to interpeduncular nucleus (midbrain)', 'The fasciculus retroflexus (habenulo-interpeduncular tract) descends from the habenula to the interpeduncular nucleus of the midbrain. The mammillothalamic, cortico-pontine, and fornix connections are distinct tracts.'),
('4aa746a9-0352-5b90-a529-9d284d30db25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The mammillary peduncle connects', '["Mammillary bodies to cortex","Mammillary bodies to pons","Hippocampus to mammillary bodies","Midbrain tegmentum to mammillary bodies","Mammillary bodies to thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain tegmentum to mammillary bodies', 'The mammillary peduncle carries ascending fibers from the midbrain tegmentum to the mammillary bodies. Output of the mammillary bodies travels via the mammillothalamic and mammillotegmental tracts, and hippocampal input arrives via the fornix.'),
('6fa24336-7b8a-5b9d-a71f-3504d2e90f15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The mammillotegmental tract connects', '["Mammillary bodies to cortex","Mammillary bodies to pons","Mammillary bodies to midbrain tegmentum","Mammillary bodies to thalamus","Hippocampus to mammillary bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies to midbrain tegmentum', 'The mammillotegmental tract projects from the mammillary bodies to the tegmental nuclei of the midbrain. Mammillary-thalamic fibers form the mammillothalamic tract, and hippocampal afferents travel in the fornix.'),
('208111d8-1a0d-55e5-87e0-941000843abe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The stria terminalis connects', '["Mammillary bodies to thalamus","Amygdala to hypothalamus (and septal area)","Striatum to pallidum","Cortex to striatum","Hippocampus to mammillary bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Amygdala to hypothalamus (and septal area)', 'The stria terminalis carries fibers from the amygdala to the hypothalamus and septal area along the medial border of the caudate. Mammillothalamic, striatopallidal, corticostriatal, and fornix connections are separate pathways.'),
('6ad4b15b-1fa8-51ed-ad42-08acf7d6a86e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral amygdalofugal pathway connects', '["Amygdala to hippocampus only","Amygdala to hypothalamus, thalamus, and brainstem","Amygdala to striatum only","Amygdala to cortex only","Amygdala to olfactory bulb"]'::jsonb, 'Amygdala to hypothalamus, thalamus, and brainstem', 'The ventral amygdalofugal pathway runs from the amygdala beneath the lentiform nucleus to the hypothalamus, thalamus, and brainstem. A more restricted connection would leave out the diffuse autonomic and behavioral targets served by this antrerior route.'),
('6637e9e9-bfe4-5db3-b053-2a087d0b5280'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medial forebrain bundle (MFB) connects', '["Thalamus to cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Septal area, hypothalamus, and ventral tegmental area (reward pathway)","Cerebellum to red nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala to hypothalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampus to mammillary bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septal area, hypothalamus, and ventral tegmental area (reward pathway)', 'The medial forebrain bundle runs between the septal area, hypothalamus, and ventral tegmental area, carrying the major ascending and descending reward circuitry. Thalamocortical, cerebellar, and limbic pathways are not part of the MFB.'),
('0a75f702-7e9f-5e3f-af5c-fdbb8f760aae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The diagonal band of Broca connects', '["Septal nuclei to thalamus","Mammillary bodies to thalamus","Septal nuclei to hippocampus and amygdala","Amygdala to hypothalamus","Hippocampus to mammillary bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Septal nuclei to hippocampus and amygdala', 'The diagonal band of Broca carries cholinergic fibers from the septal nuclei to the hippocampus and amygdala. Septal-thalamic, mammillothalamic, amygdala-hypothalamic, and fornix connections are separate fiber systems.'),
('70f98880-b230-5c92-9816-99d2f62af7bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert sends cholinergic fibers to', '["Entire cerebral cortex","Only temporal cortex","Only occipital cortex","Only frontal cortex","Only parietal cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Entire cerebral cortex', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert provides diffuse cholinergic innervation to the entire cerebral cortex. Its projections are not confined to a single lobe, which distinguishes it from the medial septal pathway to the hippocampus.'),
('ddad79b3-e0ea-5de7-a1ac-933b3d8c70a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Alzheimer disease shows significant degeneration of', '["All of these resulting in decreased functional activity","Raphe nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Substantia nigra mediated by specific receptor activation","Nucleus basalis of Meynert (cholinergic)","Locus coeruleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus basalis of Meynert (cholinergic)', 'Alzheimer disease characteristically shows marked loss of cholinergic neurons in the nucleus basalis of Meynert, underlying cortical cholinergic deficits. Although some degeneration of raphe and locus coeruleus can occur, the nucleus basalis is the hallmark site.'),
('8e377231-a88e-57a2-bc13-7a530f2af047'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The medial septum provides cholinergic input to', '["Thalamus","Cerebellum","Striatum","Cortex","Hippocampus"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus', 'Medial septal cholinergic neurons project mainly to the hippocampus, forming the septohippocampal pathway important for learning and memory. Thalamus, cerebellum, striatum, and cortex receive their cholinergic input from other sources.'),
('912759de-256b-54d7-b119-52a1214f71de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The ventral striatum (nucleus accumbens) receives dopaminergic input from', '["Locus coeruleus","Nucleus basalis","Substantia nigra pars compacta","Ventral tegmental area (VTA)","Raphe nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral tegmental area (VTA)', 'The nucleus accumbens receives dopaminergic innervation from the ventral tegmental area (mesolimbic pathway), a key reward circuit. Substantia nigra pars compacta projects to the dorsal striatum, while locus coeruleus (noradrenergic) and raphe (serotonergic) target other regions.'),
('0007e699-f6fe-5b8c-a36a-0e4ac3f5a8d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The amygdala projects to the brainstem for', '["Motor coordination resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste processing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fear-related autonomic responses (via central nucleus)"]'::jsonb, 'Fear-related autonomic responses (via central nucleus)', 'The central nucleus of the amygdala innervates brainstem autonomic centers that produce fear-related responses such as tachycardia, hypertension, and startle. Motor coordination, taste, visual, and auditory processing do not rely on amygdala-brainstem outflow.'),
('22423edf-96bd-5827-8a66-0f69e3d8b63f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'The brainstem contains which major motor pathways?', '["Only corticospinal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sensory tracts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only autonomic tracts mediated by specific receptor activation","Only extrapyramidal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corticospinal, corticobulbar, rubrospinal, tectospinal, reticulospinal, vestibulospinal (all traversing)"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal, corticobulbar, rubrospinal, tectospinal, reticulospinal, vestibulospinal (all traversing)', 'The brainstem carries all descending motor tracts, including corticospinal, corticobulbar, rubrospinal, tectospinal, reticulospinal, and vestibulospinal fibers. It is not limited to a single category, since sensory and autonomic tracts also traverse it.'),
('92d205d1-2940-5aee-bf89-41855b8260ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'Which cranial nerve nucleus is located in the midbrain at the level of the superior colliculus?', '["Facial nucleus (CN VII)","Abducens nucleus (CN VI)","Oculomotor nucleus (CN III)","Trochlear nucleus (CN IV)","Trigeminal motor nucleus (CN V)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nucleus (CN III)', 'The oculomotor nucleus lies in the midbrain at the superior colliculus level, paired with the Edinger-Westphal nucleus. The trochlear nucleus is at the inferior colliculus level, while facial, abducens, and trigeminal nuclei are in the pons.'),
('d8bf3f45-c3bf-550c-88fe-3751b8ed85c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Brainstem', 'In Brainstem, which statement best fits the following fact: medulla oblongata contains which cranial nerve nuclei?', '["CN IX, X, XI, XII (plus VIII and lower V)","CN III, IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V, VI mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII, VIII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN I, II leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI, XII (plus VIII and lower V)', 'The medulla houses the nuclei of CN IX, X, XI, and XII, along with the vestibular/cochlear nuclei and the spinal trigeminal (lower V) nucleus. CN III and IV are midbrain nuclei, V and VI pontine, VII and VIII pontine, and I and II are outside the brainstem.'),
('13bb055f-6b47-5bf4-b6ec-3de029d0d6fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum is divided into three lobes:', '["Anterior, posterior, flocculonodular","Superior, middle, inferior","Left, right, vermis","Crus I, Crus II, vermis","Central, peripheral, intermediate"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior, posterior, flocculonodular', 'The three anatomically defined lobes of the cerebellum are the anterior, posterior, and flocculonodular lobes. The superior/middle/inferior scheme describes surfaces rather than lobes, while Crus I and Crus II are subdivisions of the posterior lobe.'),
('91aba022-7e19-5711-b0a4-4307fef6f674'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The flocculonodular lobe is also known as', '["Paleocerebellum","Archicerebellum","Cerebrocerebellum","Neocerebellum","Spinocerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Archicerebellum', 'Because it is the phylogenetically oldest part, the flocculonodular lobe is called the archicerebellum. The paleocerebellum corresponds to the anterior lobe, while the neocerebellum is the lateral posterior lobe.'),
('c5413303-669b-53a2-bc6b-9a630b022bb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The anterior lobe of cerebellum is also known as', '["Pontocerebellum","Paleocerebellum","Neocerebellum","Cerebrocerebellum","Archicerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Paleocerebellum', 'The anterior lobe is the phylogenetic middle-age component and is called the paleocerebellum. The neocerebellum is the posterior lobe, and the archicerebellum is the flocculonodular lobe.'),
('039f6bfb-f597-5aa2-90ca-37129b0639e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The posterior lobe of cerebellum is also known as', '["Spinocerebellum","Vestibulocerebellum","Archicerebellum","Paleocerebellum","Neocerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Neocerebellum', 'As the largest and phylogenetically newest component, the posterior lobe is termed the neocerebellum (cerebrocerebellum). Paleocerebellum names the anterior lobe and archicerebellum the flocculonodular lobe.'),
('94ca74ca-3b3d-5a25-a3d3-020486b6dbfa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The primary fissure separates', '["Right and left hemispheres","Superior and inferior surfaces","Anterior and posterior lobes","Posterior and flocculonodular lobes","Vermis and hemispheres"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior lobes', 'The primary fissure is the largest fissure of the superior surface and divides the cerebellum into the anterior and posterior lobes. The posterolateral fissure, not the primary fissure, separates the flocculonodular lobe from the posterior lobe.'),
('99a63406-edf1-5225-b23c-061eb57673c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The posterolateral fissure separates', '["Nodule from uvula","Tonsil from vermis","Anterior from posterior lobe","Vermis from hemisphere","Flocculonodular lobe from posterior lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Flocculonodular lobe from posterior lobe', 'The posterolateral fissure demarcates the boundary between the flocculonodular lobe and the posterior lobe. The primary fissure, by contrast, separates the anterior and posterior lobes.'),
('f59e4866-e96d-542f-a5ab-3f7432e1c23c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The vermis of cerebellum is concerned with', '["Eye movements resulting in decreased functional activity","Fine finger movements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Limb coordination mediated by specific receptor activation","Axial (trunk) muscle coordination and posture","Balance only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Axial (trunk) muscle coordination and posture', 'The vermis is the midline spinocerebellum that controls axial (trunk) muscles, posture, and stance. Fine limb movements depend on the lateral hemispheres, while balance and eye movements are served by the flocculonodular lobe.'),
('db60dde8-5a20-58a2-bfca-dfeb97d661f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The intermediate (paravermal) zone of cerebellum controls', '["Language resulting in decreased functional activity","Trunk posture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Balance mediated by specific receptor activation","Eye movements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipsilateral limb coordination"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral limb coordination', 'The paravermal (intermediate) zone coordinates movements of the ipsilateral limbs. The vermis handles trunk posture, and balance plus eye movements are functions of the flocculonodular lobe.'),
('655cdc4e-274e-5cea-9b0f-93bf69bda088'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The lateral (hemispheric) zone of cerebellum is involved in', '["Autonomic function resulting in decreased functional activity","Balance only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eye movements mediated by specific receptor activation","Planning and coordination of skilled limb movements","Trunk posture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Planning and coordination of skilled limb movements', 'The lateral hemispheric zone (cerebrocerebellum) participates in planning and coordinating skilled volitional movements via its connections with the cerebral cortex. Trunk posture is controlled by the vermis and balance by the flocculonodular lobe.'),
('0a1c9eb7-7ade-5671-8dbf-23e20cb3a7ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellar peduncles are:', '["Only superior and inferior resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior, middle, inferior, and accessory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior (brachium conjunctivum), middle (brachium pontis), inferior (restiform body)","Only middle and inferior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior, posterior, lateral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior (brachium conjunctivum), middle (brachium pontis), inferior (restiform body)', 'The three paired cerebellar peduncles are the superior (brachium conjunctivum), middle (brachium pontis), and inferior (restiform body). These names reflect their classical anatomical descriptions.'),
('f010890a-b7a8-580a-b02c-138182ecac16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP) mainly contains', '["Afferent fibers from pons","Afferent fibers from medulla","Afferent fibers from spinal cord","Afferent fibers from vestibule","Efferent fibers from cerebellum (dentatothalamic)"]'::jsonb, 'Efferent fibers from cerebellum (dentatothalamic)', 'The superior cerebellar peduncle carries the principal efferent fibers of the cerebellum, including the dentatothalamic tract. The pons and medulla input travels via the middle and inferior cerebellar peduncles respectively.'),
('cdd6fe77-da9a-5135-85e7-93546112c23c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'SCP fibers decussate in', '["Spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain (decussation of SCP)","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (decussation of SCP)', 'The superior cerebellar peduncle fibers cross the midline in the lower midbrain at the decussation of the SCP. This explains the contralateral tissue connections and the combined effects of cerebellar lesions.'),
('d3ef46a1-5e9a-5081-b1a2-b6c9f977f61f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP) contains', '["Olivocerebellar fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinocerebellar fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pontocerebellar fibers (afferent from pontine nuclei)","Efferent fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vestibulocerebellar fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontocerebellar fibers (afferent from pontine nuclei)', 'The middle cerebellar peduncle is the largest peduncle and carries afferent pontocerebellar fibers from the pontine nuclei. Olivo- and spinocerebellar fibers enter through the inferior peduncle, and the superior peduncle is mainly efferent.'),
('c0d97bd5-1f2f-5da6-aaa2-463523eeedd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP) contains', '["Only efferent resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only olivocerebellar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only afferent mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only spinocerebellar without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Afferent fibers from spinal cord, medulla (spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, vestibulocerebellar) and efferent fibers to vestibular nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent fibers from spinal cord, medulla (spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, vestibulocerebellar) and efferent fibers to vestibular nuclei', 'The inferior cerebellar peduncle carries multiple afferent tracts (spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, vestibulocerebellar) as well as efferent fibers to the vestibular nuclei. Its content is therefore both afferent and efferent rather than any single tract alone.'),
('6b27c290-24ce-5600-b3f5-7deb160376f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which of the following enters via inferior cerebellar peduncle?', '["Dentatothalamic fibers","Ventral spinocerebellar tract","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract","Pontocerebellar fibers","Corticopontine"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal spinocerebellar tract', 'The dorsal spinocerebellar tract enters the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle. The ventral spinocerebellar tract enters via the superior peduncle, pontocerebellar fibers via the middle peduncle, and dentatothalamic fibers are efferent.'),
('ee52dcda-97e4-533d-b185-2f6f28b91d79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which of the following enters via superior cerebellar peduncle?', '["Ventral spinocerebellar tract","Vestibulocerebellar fibers","Olivocerebellar fibers","Pontocerebellar fibers","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral spinocerebellar tract', 'The ventral (anterior) spinocerebellar tract is unique in entering through the superior cerebellar peduncle. The dorsal spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, and vestibulocerebellar fibers all enter via the inferior peduncle.'),
('83e91691-32fa-58c5-ad5c-1a6eb3ae1e0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which of the following enters via middle cerebellar peduncle?', '["Cuneocerebellar fibers","Spinocerebellar fibers","Olivocerebellar fibers","Pontocerebellar fibers (mossy fibers)","Vestibulocerebellar fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Pontocerebellar fibers (mossy fibers)', 'Pontocerebellar fibers are the main afferent component of the middle cerebellar peduncle. Cuneo-, spino-, olivo-, and vestibulocerebellar fibers all travel through the inferior peduncle.'),
('b604d42f-1536-5c2f-911c-2c49d068d2d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The deep cerebellar nuclei are:', '["Fastigial only","Caudate, putamen, globus pallidus","Red nucleus, substantia nigra","Dentate only","Dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate, emboliform, globose, fastigial', 'The deep cerebellar nuclei are four paired groups: dentate, emboliform, globose, and fastigial. Caudate, putamen, and globus pallidus belong to the basal ganglia, and the red nucleus lies in the midbrain.'),
('143f1ed6-ba3e-502d-8f08-6bba1cb73ba4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The dentate nucleus receives input from', '["Lateral cerebellar hemisphere (neocerebellum)","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vestibular system mediated by specific receptor activation","Flocculonodular lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cerebellar hemisphere (neocerebellum)', 'The dentate nucleus receives projections from the Purkinje cells of the lateral cerebellar hemisphere (neocerebellum). The vermis projects to the fastigial nucleus, and the flocculonodular lobe relates to the vestibular system.'),
('1b724bed-d6a5-5f4f-b523-f172587a512c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The fastigial nucleus receives input from', '["Pontine nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Flocculonodular lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Vermis (spinocerebellum and vestibulocerebellum)","Lateral hemispheres leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vermis (spinocerebellum and vestibulocerebellum)', 'The fastigial nucleus is innervated by Purkinje cells from the vermis (spinocerebellum) and receives vestibular-related input from the flocculonodular lobe. The lateral hemispheres instead project to the dentate nucleus.'),
('5bbf4940-ea41-5391-b667-4bde96e98493'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The globose and emboliform nuclei (interposed nuclei) receive from', '["Intermediate (paravermal) zone","All cerebellar cortex","Flocculonodular lobe","Lateral hemisphere","Vermis"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate (paravermal) zone', 'The interposed nuclei (globose and emboliform) receive Purkinje cell input from the intermediate (paravermal) zone of the cortex. The vermis and lateral hemisphere project respectively to the fastigial and dentate nuclei.'),
('cda10ee7-6ac7-5829-aad7-cee1511e1f88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The interposed nuclei (globose + emboliform) project to', '["Red nucleus (rubrospinal tract) and VL thalamus","SCP to cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vestibular nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","Fastigial nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reticular formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Red nucleus (rubrospinal tract) and VL thalamus', 'The interposed nuclei send their output to the contralateral red nucleus (giving rise to the rubrospinal tract) and to the ventral lateral (VL) thalamus. The fastigial nucleus, by contrast, projects to the vestibular nuclei and reticular formation.'),
('05a09421-13fc-5b47-9fc5-cb639f47bf64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The dentate nucleus projects to', '["Pontine nuclei","VL thalamus (and red nucleus)","Fastigial nucleus","Vestibular nuclei","Spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'VL thalamus (and red nucleus)', 'The dentate nucleus projects via the superior cerebellar peduncle to the contralateral ventral lateral (VL) thalamus and, collaterally, to the red nucleus. It does not project to the fastigial nucleus or spinal cord.'),
('3d98f7b1-ce26-565b-9ae8-f8af7704b309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar output is entirely via', '["Directly to brainstem resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Purkinje cells directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep cerebellar nuclei (all efferents synapse in deep nuclei)","Granule cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep cerebellar nuclei (all efferents synapse in deep nuclei)', 'All cerebellar efferent fibers arise from the deep cerebellar nuclei; the cortex projects only to these nuclei and never directly to extracerebellar targets. The sole exception, the flocculonodular lobe, projects to the vestibular nuclei, which anatomically represent specialized deep nuclei.'),
('c561ad84-8b29-5b05-9f78-9ca9f6551584'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Purkinje cells are', '["GABAergic (inhibitory) neurons","Dopaminergic neurons","Glutamatergic (excitatory) neurons","Cholinergic neurons","Serotonergic neurons"]'::jsonb, 'GABAergic (inhibitory) neurons', 'Purkinje cells are the sole output neurons of the cerebellar cortex and are GABAergic, exerting inhibitory control over the deep nuclei. Granule cells provide the excitatory glutamatergic drive within the cortex.'),
('86a8aa20-266d-5196-b0f4-2f1ad8c927e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Purkinje cells are located in', '["Granule cell layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar cortex (middle layer of trilaminar cortex)","Deep cerebellar nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","Molecular layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","White matter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar cortex (middle layer of trilaminar cortex)', 'The Purkinje cell layer is the middle of the three cortical layers, sandwiched between the molecular and granular layers. The molecular layer contains stellate and basket cells, and the granular layer holds the granule cells.'),
('b2513137-0152-5e27-95bd-9b1687df2225'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Granule cells are', '["Cholinergic resulting in decreased functional activity","Excitatory (glutamatergic) interneurons in cerebellar cortex","Dopaminergic mediated by specific receptor activation","Serotonergic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibitory (GABAergic) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excitatory (glutamatergic) interneurons in cerebellar cortex', 'Granule cells are small glutamatergic excitatory interneurons of the cerebellar cortex whose axons form parallel fibers. They provide the principal excitatory input to Purkinje cells, in contrast to the inhibitory Golgi, basket, and stellate cells.'),
('1a9cb1b3-078c-579d-bd27-6b2d9b535509'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Granule cells are located in', '["Granular layer of cerebellar cortex","Purkinje layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","White matter mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Molecular layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granular layer of cerebellar cortex', 'Granule cells are densely packed in the innermost granular layer of the cerebellar cortex. The molecular layer is outer, and the Purkinje layer lies in between.'),
('baf107c6-738b-56ee-81cf-0cbfa5fb1652'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Parallel fibers (axons of granule cells) synapse on', '["Climbing fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Purkinje cell dendrites (in molecular layer)","Mossy fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Granule cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep cerebellar nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cell dendrites (in molecular layer)', 'Parallel fibers are the axons of granule cells that course through the molecular layer and synapse on the dendritic arbors of Purkinje cells. Climbing fibers instead synapse directly on Purkinje cell proximal dendrites.'),
('b9bc754a-89a3-5de3-8a59-564d60a72af0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Climbing fibers arise from', '["Cerebral cortex","Spinal cord","Inferior olivary nucleus (medulla)","Pontine nuclei","Vestibular nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior olivary nucleus (medulla)', 'Climbing fibers originate exclusively from the inferior olivary nucleus in the medulla and enter via the inferior cerebellar peduncle. Pontine nuclei give rise to mossy fibers, and the cerebral cortex connects indirectly via the pons.'),
('fbfa87a4-1270-55bf-ab9d-12725bdb7b62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Climbing fibers synapse on', '["Purkinje cell proximal dendrites (100-300 synapses per fiber)","Parallel fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Granule cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Stellate cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cell proximal dendrites (100-300 synapses per fiber)', 'Each climbing fiber wraps around the proximal dendrites of a single Purkinje cell, making 100-300 synapses. Mossy fibers, by contrast, synapse on granule cells through the cerebellar glomeruli.'),
('469605cf-6578-5c09-8b60-15b5ab7b6f21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Mossy fibers arise from', '["Red nucleus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral cortex only mediated by specific receptor activation","Pontine nuclei, spinal cord, reticular formation, vestibular nuclei","Inferior olive only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine nuclei, spinal cord, reticular formation, vestibular nuclei', 'Mossy fibers are the main afferents of the cerebellum and arise from the pontine nuclei, spinal cord, reticular formation, and vestibular nuclei. Only the inferior olive contributes climbing fibers rather than mossy fibers.'),
('ca53a98d-7c7c-5a95-b4eb-28ca4b025c9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Mossy fibers synapse on', '["Deep nuclei directly","Granule cells (in cerebellar glomeruli)","Stellate cells","Climbing fibers","Purkinje cells directly"]'::jsonb, 'Granule cells (in cerebellar glomeruli)', 'Mossy fibers terminate on granule cells within the cerebellar glomeruli of the granular layer. Climbing fibers alone synapse directly on Purkinje cell dendrites, whereas mossy input reaches Purkinje cells only indirectly through parallel fibers.'),
('bc972fb3-3bd8-5536-a614-d793a17ef540'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar glomeruli are specialized synaptic structures found in', '["Molecular layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Granular layer (between mossy fibers and granule cell dendrites)","White matter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Purkinje cell layer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granular layer (between mossy fibers and granule cell dendrites)', 'Cerebellar glomeruli are the synaptic complexes in the granular layer where mossy fiber terminals contact the dendrites of granule cells. The molecular layer, Purkinje layer, and white matter do not contain these glomerular structures.'),
('9117f470-cac2-504a-bad0-92b382f7020f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Golgi cells (cerebellar cortex) are', '["Inhibitory (GABAergic) interneurons in granular layer","Cholinergic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excitatory interneurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Glutamatergic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopaminergic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) interneurons in granular layer', 'Golgi cells are GABAergic inhibitory interneurons situated in the granular layer, where they provide feedback inhibition onto granule cells. They are not excitatory or monoaminergic in function.'),
('9ae5b713-d2a7-5e8e-9a5c-3dc06e76dfd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Stellate cells (cerebellar molecular layer) are', '["Glutamatergic","Inhibitory (GABAergic) interneurons","Excitatory interneurons","Cholinergic","Dopaminergic"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) interneurons', 'Stellate cells are small GABAergic inhibitory interneurons of the molecular layer that suppress the activity of neighboring Purkinje cells. They are not excitatory or cholinergic.'),
('2fbbc3b1-070c-5f29-81e9-0724752e2469'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Basket cells (cerebellar molecular layer) are', '["Excitatory interneurons resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibitory (GABAergic) interneurons that synapse on Purkinje cell soma/axon","Dopaminergic mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholinergic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glutamatergic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) interneurons that synapse on Purkinje cell soma/axon', 'Basket cells are GABAergic inhibitory interneurons of the molecular layer whose axon baskets synapse on the somata and initial axon segments of Purkinje cells. This targets the Purkinje cell body rather than just its dendrites.'),
('2e975e1a-3db8-51a7-8b9b-e3dc183cba18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellar cortex has how many layers?', '["Two","Three","Four","Five","Six"]'::jsonb, 'Three', 'The cerebellar cortex is trilaminar, consisting of the molecular, Purkinje, and granular layers. This three-layer architecture is a hallmark distinguishing it from the six-layered cerebral cortex.'),
('254de487-5aa4-5d36-8117-de144e6b59fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which is the only output from cerebellar cortex?', '["Mossy fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Granule cell axons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parallel fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Purkinje cell axons (inhibitory to deep nuclei)","Climbing fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cell axons (inhibitory to deep nuclei)', 'Purkinje cell axons are the only outflow of the cerebellar cortex, delivering inhibitory GABAergic signals to the deep nuclei. Mossy fibers, climbing fibers, and parallel fibers are afferent or intrinsic elements, not cortical output.'),
('bb06c0af-63b1-5996-83ca-58cfd521604e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Deep cerebellar nuclei are', '["Dopaminergic resulting in decreased functional activity","Cholinergic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excitatory (glutamatergic) output neurons","Serotonergic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibitory (GABAergic) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excitatory (glutamatergic) output neurons', 'The deep cerebellar nuclei contain glutamatergic excitatory neurons that project to the thalamus and brainstem. They are tonically inhibited by Purkinje cell input, which disinhibits them during movement.'),
('e3b0d105-09b7-5edb-b79a-ece2f68ee883'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Flocculonodular lobe (archicerebellum) is functionally concerned with', '["Limb coordination resulting in decreased functional activity","Posture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Muscle tone mediated by specific receptor activation","Skilled voluntary movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Balance, vestibular ocular reflex (VOR), eye movements"]'::jsonb, 'Balance, vestibular ocular reflex (VOR), eye movements', 'The flocculonodular (vestibulocerebellar) lobe governs balance, the vestibular ocular reflex, and eye movements through its connections with the vestibular system. Limb coordination and posture are served by the paravermis and vermis respectively.'),
('697d8af6-6b5e-5a11-800e-ca9097ae1f39'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Flocculonodular lobe receives input from', '["Cerebral cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pontine nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","Red nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vestibular system (primary vestibular fibers and vestibular nuclei)"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular system (primary vestibular fibers and vestibular nuclei)', 'The flocculonodular lobe receives primary vestibular fibers directly and secondary input from the vestibular nuclei. The cerebral cortex and spinal cord do not project to this region, which is dedicated to vestibular function.'),
('75b396b5-f5a0-58ad-b459-a32d626aa772'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Flocculonodular lobe projects to', '["Motor cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Red nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VL thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral vestibular nucleus and fastigial nucleus","Dentate nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral vestibular nucleus and fastigial nucleus', 'Purkinje cells of the flocculonodular lobe project to the lateral vestibular nucleus and the fastigial nucleus, modulating vestibulospinal reflexes. It does not project to the motor cortex, red nucleus, or dentate.'),
('88ba72d1-5341-5427-b8ba-57b4f9857b5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Anterior lobe (paleocerebellum) is functionally the', '["Spinocerebellum (receives spinal input for posture/tone)","Vestibulocerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebrocerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Neocerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pontocerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellum (receives spinal input for posture/tone)', 'The anterior lobe receives spinocerebellar input, so it functions as the spinocerebellum regulating posture and muscle tone. The vestibulocerebellum is the flocculonodular lobe, and the cerebrocerebellum is the lateral posterior lobe.'),
('decd02e0-4585-577a-8b0e-98d92facf38f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Spinocerebellar afferents to paleocerebellum carry', '["Visual information resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unconscious proprioception and exteroceptive information","Motor commands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Conscious proprioception leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unconscious proprioception and exteroceptive information', 'Spinocerebellar tracts transmit unconscious proprioceptive and exteroceptive (touch, pressure) information that is processed without reaching conscious perception. Conscious proprioception is instead carried by the dorsal column–medial lemniscus pathway.'),
('2b4c1842-8127-5b20-9691-3d8a2bfac4f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The spinocerebellum (vermis + paravermis) projects to', '["Interposed (globose/emboliform) and fastigial nuclei","Dentate nucleus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vestibular nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","VL thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interposed (globose/emboliform) and fastigial nuclei', 'Within the spinocerebellum, the vermis projects to the fastigial nucleus and the paravermis projects to the interposed nuclei (globose and emboliform). The dentate nucleus is the target of the cerebrocerebellum, not the spinocerebellum.'),
('22c9803c-1fae-5046-8f25-11486ca8435a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Posterior lobe (neocerebellum) receives input from', '["Cerebral cortex via pontine nuclei (corticopontocerebellar)","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior olive only mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral cortex via pontine nuclei (corticopontocerebellar)', 'The neocerebellum receives its input from the cerebral cortex relayed through the pontine nuclei (corticopontocerebellar pathway). It does not receive its primary drive from the spinal cord, basal ganglia, or vestibular system.'),
('e72eb139-f551-5cea-9dff-22a5858c9421'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Neocerebellar output goes to', '["Interposed nuclei","Reticular formation","Fastigial nucleus","Vestibular nuclei","Dentate nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate nucleus', 'Purkinje cells of the neocerebellar cortex project to the dentate nucleus, which then reaches the VL thalamus and motor cortex. The fastigial, interposed, and vestibular targets belong to other cerebellar functional zones.'),
('a7d7d8e1-bf49-5d4a-af2b-ee60976690b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebrocerebellar loop (motor planning) involves', '["Cerebellum → red nucleus → spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum → vestibular → spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex → spinal cord → cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex → olive → cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor cortex → pontine nuclei → neocerebellum → dentate → VL thalamus → motor cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Motor cortex → pontine nuclei → neocerebellum → dentate → VL thalamus → motor cortex', 'The cerebrocerebellar loop for motor planning runs from the motor cortex through the pontine nuclei to the neocerebellum, and returns via the dentate nucleus, VL thalamus, and back to the motor cortex. The red nucleus and vestibular pathways belong to other loops.'),
('7a643c23-6d01-5dc3-9e4e-6b1a37056d2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar lesion in the vermis causes', '["Intention tremor resulting in decreased functional activity","Truncal ataxia with preserved limb coordination","Limb ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation","Dysarthria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nystagmus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Truncal ataxia with preserved limb coordination', 'Vermian lesions produce truncal ataxia with unsteady stance and preserved limb coordination because the vermis controls axial muscles. Intention tremor and limb ataxia arise from hemispheric lesions, and nystagmus from flocculonodular involvement.'),
('1f0d7e97-5bf6-5511-9bde-d114a7a69d74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar lesion in the hemisphere causes', '["Only nystagmus resulting in decreased functional activity","Ipsilateral limb ataxia, intention tremor, dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia","Contralateral limb ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation","Truncal ataxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only dysarthria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral limb ataxia, intention tremor, dysmetria, dysdiadochokinesia', 'Each cerebellar hemisphere coordinates the ipsilateral side of the body, so a lateral hemisphere lesion produces ipsilateral limb signs such as ataxia, intention tremor, dysmetria, and dysdiadochokinesia. It does not affect the contralateral limb, and truncal ataxia or nystagmus reflect vermal or flocculonodular involvement rather than hemispheric disease.'),
('ed19e20d-eb46-575a-b22d-885bbc699a32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Flocculonodular lobe lesion causes', '["Hypotonia resulting in decreased functional activity","Nystagmus, vertigo, and gait ataxia (vestibular-like symptoms)","Dysarthria mediated by specific receptor activation","Dysdiadochokinesia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intention tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nystagmus, vertigo, and gait ataxia (vestibular-like symptoms)', 'The flocculonodular lobe (archicerebellum) is closely connected to the vestibular system, so its lesions produce vestibular-type symptoms including nystagmus, vertigo, and gait ataxia. Hypotonia, dysarthria, dysdiadochokinesia, and intention tremor are signs of the spinocerebellum or cerebrocerebellum rather than the vestibulocerebellum.'),
('07ef4b0c-7b97-59b8-91c6-440fff25c2a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Intention tremor is characteristic of', '["Cerebellar hemisphere lesion","Parkinson disease","Basal ganglia lesion","Myopathy","Peripheral neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar hemisphere lesion', 'Intention tremor appears during goal-directed movement and is a hallmark of cerebellar hemisphere disease, linked to dysfunction of the dentate-thalamic pathway. Parkinson disease and basal ganglia lesions produce resting tremor, whereas myopathy and peripheral neuropathy do not cause tremor.'),
('5dd1d143-0d29-5363-add5-5cccd2f18a9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Dysdiadochokinesia refers to', '["Slowness of movement","Involuntary jerky movements","Inability to perform rapid alternating movements","Inability to estimate distance","Tremor at rest"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to perform rapid alternating movements', 'Dysdiadochokinesia is the impaired ability to perform rapid alternating movements, such as rapid pronation-supination of the hand, and results from cerebellar hemisphere dysfunction. Inability to judge distance is dysmetria, and the other options describe different signs rather than this specific finding.'),
('2b4e71b0-675f-51ec-aeb5-4b46d098ee6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Dysmetria refers to', '["Inability to perform alternating movements resulting in decreased functional activity","Inability to judge distance/range of movement (past pointing)","Loss of balance mediated by specific receptor activation","Tremor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Slowed movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to judge distance/range of movement (past pointing)', 'Dysmetria is the inability to judge the range or distance of a movement, causing over- or under-shooting of the target (past pointing) and is a cardinal sign of cerebellar disease. Difficulty with alternating movements is dysdiadochokinesia, and the remaining options describe different cerebellar or extrapyramidal findings.'),
('15d1fc19-2ef2-5580-8f09-3b924fe70ac3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar ataxia is characterized by', '["Hemiplegic gait resulting in decreased functional activity","Wide-based, unsteady gait with incoordination","Waddling gait mediated by specific receptor activation","Festinating gait without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steppage gait leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wide-based, unsteady gait with incoordination', 'Cerebellar ataxia produces a wide-based, lurching, unsteady gait marked by incoordination, often worst with eyes open as well as closed. Festinating gait is seen in parkinsonism, waddling gait in proximal muscle weakness, steppage gait in foot drop, and hemiplegic gait in upper motor neuron disease.'),
('8da27f36-da6d-5782-a165-c53fbd3d483f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar nystagmus is typically', '["Rotatory nystagmus resulting in decreased functional activity","Optokinetic nystagmus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertical nystagmus mediated by specific receptor activation","Positional nystagmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horizontal nystagmus (fast component toward side of lesion)"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal nystagmus (fast component toward side of lesion)', 'Cerebellar nystagmus is usually horizontal, with the fast component directed toward the side of the lesion (gaze toward the lesion). Vertical nystagmus more often indicates brainstem dysfunction, while optokinetic and positional nystagmus have different mechanisms.'),
('c0289971-92ba-5745-9853-34aff793a823'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Scanning (ataxic) dysarthria is characterized by', '["Staccato speech resulting in decreased functional activity","High-pitched speech accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Monotonous speech mediated by specific receptor activation","Slow, slurred, explosive speech with irregular rhythm","Whispering leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Slow, slurred, explosive speech with irregular rhythm', 'Scanning or ataxic dysarthria, from cerebellar dysfunction, consists of slow, slurred, explosive speech with irregular rhythm and pauses. Monotonous speech is typical of parkinsonism, and the other options reflect laryngeal, basal ganglia, or vocal pathologies rather than the cerebellar pattern.'),
('12666825-6080-5dff-8efb-9b659895b628'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar hypotonia is seen in', '["Chronic cerebellar degeneration","Flocculonodular lesion","Vermal lesion","Acute cerebellar hemisphere lesion","Basal ganglia lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Acute cerebellar hemisphere lesion', 'Hypotonia is a prominent, usually reversible feature of acute cerebellar hemisphere lesions, especially sudden-onset conditions. Chronic degeneration tends to produce ataxia rather than sustained hypotonia, and vermal or flocculonodular lesions do not characteristically cause hypotonia.'),
('9a8669de-fb6e-5222-9575-8540a1ed87ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Rebound phenomenon (Holmes sign) in cerebellar disease is due to', '["Sensory loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased tone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Weakness mediated by specific receptor activation","Tremor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of cerebellar brake on antagonist muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of cerebellar brake on antagonist muscles', 'The rebound phenomenon occurs because the cerebellum fails to brake antagonist muscles at the end of a movement, so the limb over-shoots or rebounds instead of coming smoothly to rest. It reflects faulty coordination rather than pure weakness, sensory loss, or increased muscle tone.'),
('95bf3644-19b0-56a3-a150-9db4aeebb9f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum receives input from which cranial nerve?', '["CN VIII (vestibular)","CN II accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VIII (vestibular)', 'Primary vestibular afferents travel with the vestibular division of CN VIII to reach the cerebellum, especially the flocculonodular lobe and fastigial nucleus. CN II, III, V, and VII do not directly relay sensory input into the cerebellar circuitry.'),
('63e281fc-c989-5fae-8dd1-a7d72dbaadf4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum has no direct connections with', '["Thalamus","Basal ganglia (indirect)","Spinal cord (indirect via brainstem)","Cerebral cortex","Vestibular system"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord (indirect via brainstem)', 'The cerebellum connects to the spinal cord only indirectly through brainstem relays such as the inferior and superior cerebellar peduncles, not by direct fibers. It connects directly to the vestibular system, thalamus, and cerebral cortex, and only indirectly to the basal ganglia.'),
('166ce149-bd19-5a2d-aae8-6f99325c9efb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar afferent fibers are of two main types:', '["Mossy fibers and parallel fibers","Parallel fibers and climbing fibers","Mossy fibers and climbing fibers","Granule fibers and Purkinje fibers","Afferent and efferent"]'::jsonb, 'Mossy fibers and climbing fibers', 'The two main classes of cerebellar afferents are mossy fibers and climbing fibers, which terminate on cerebellar cortex neurons. Parallel fibers are the axons of granule cells and thus intrinsic, while the other options conflate cell types or broad categories.'),
('7a36e8fd-80a0-595c-a075-72a6b6c1b2c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Mossy fibers outnumber climbing fibers by a ratio of approximately', '["1000:1","1:1","2:1","10:1","100:1"]'::jsonb, '1000:1', 'Mossy fibers far outnumber climbing fibers, with an approximately 1000:1 ratio, reflecting the dominant granular pathway through the cerebellum. The small number of climbing fibers is balanced by their powerful, single-cell innervation of Purkinje cells.'),
('61c65bac-b18c-5b43-b5c5-24ad38ff3458'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Each Purkinje cell receives input from', '["Only 1 climbing fiber (but 200,000 parallel fibers)","Many climbing fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only Golgi cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Only parallel fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only mossy fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Only 1 climbing fiber (but 200,000 parallel fibers)', 'A single climbing fiber makes multiple synapses on one Purkinje cell, so each Purkinje cell receives input from only one climbing fiber, whereas it is contacted by roughly 200,000 parallel fibers. This strong one-to-one climbing fiber linkage accounts for the complex spikes of Purkinje cells.'),
('551fb537-abaa-554b-b0c1-99897338f58d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The inferior olivary nucleus provides', '["Purkinje cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Climbing fibers to contralateral cerebellum","Efferent fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Parallel fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mossy fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Climbing fibers to contralateral cerebellum', 'The inferior olivary nucleus is the exclusive source of climbing fibers, which cross in the midline and terminate on Purkinje cells of the contralateral cerebellum. It does not give rise to Purkinje cells, parallel fibers, or mossy fibers.'),
('f037f4bd-92d1-520e-b89e-3f2fb92a3428'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Lesion of inferior olive leads to', '["Tremor","Ipsilateral cerebellar symptoms","Ataxia","Contralateral cerebellar symptoms","effect"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral cerebellar symptoms', 'Because climbing fibers from the inferior olive cross to the contralateral cerebellum, and each cerebellar hemisphere coordinates the ipsilateral body, an olive lesion produces ipsilateral cerebellar symptoms. A contralateral deficit would reflect the crossing, but the functional net effect is ipsilateral signs.'),
('8e59f951-d068-5425-9837-ae54e0488965'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The pontine nuclei send axons to the cerebellum via', '["Directly via cerebellar cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","ICP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SCP mediated by specific receptor activation","MCP (crossed: pontine nuclei → contralateral cerebellum)","Via deep nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MCP (crossed: pontine nuclei → contralateral cerebellum)', 'Pontocerebellar fibers from the pontine nuclei cross the midline and pass through the middle cerebellar peduncle (MCP) to reach the contralateral cerebellum. The ICP carries spinocerebellar and olivocerebellar fibers, while the SCP is predominantly efferent to the brainstem and thalamus.'),
('a14e2aec-e5b6-585b-ab60-5c3e823b6b37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Each cerebellar hemisphere controls the', '["Ipsilateral side of body","Contralateral side","Upper body","Lower body","Both sides"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral side of body', 'Each cerebellar hemisphere exerts its influence over the ipsilateral side of the body because of its double crossing of input and output pathways. Consequently, a unilateral hemispheric lesion produces ipsilateral limb ataxia and incoordination.'),
('f2ab7bcd-8174-5415-a0ee-b9a411833d36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar symptoms appear on the same side as the lesion because', '["Cerebellar fibers decussate twice resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum has decussation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Input to cerebellum from ipsilateral body; output to ipsilateral brainstem/spinal cord","Cerebellar input is entirely ipsilateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum projects only ipsilaterally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Input to cerebellum from ipsilateral body; output to ipsilateral brainstem/spinal cord', 'Afferent pathways bring information from the ipsilateral body to the cerebellum, and its efferent projections return to the ipsilateral brainstem and spinal cord, so the net effect of any lesion is on the ipsilateral side. The double-decussation concept reflects that afferents and efferents each cross once, producing a net ipsilateral relationship.'),
('327f516e-6020-5d9d-ab67-dff1a116e90a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum plays a role in motor learning through', '["Synaptic pruning resulting in decreased functional activity","Formation of new neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelination changes mediated by specific receptor activation","Plastic changes at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses (Long-Term Depression)","Axonal sprouting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Plastic changes at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses (Long-Term Depression)', 'Cerebellar motor learning is attributed to long-term depression at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses, which weakens those synapses when climbing and parallel fibers are coactivated. Synaptic pruning, neurogenesis, myelination, and axonal sprouting do not account for this specific learning mechanism.'),
('5bebc999-f7c9-5cfb-a9c4-4521fa6c34cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Long-term depression (LTD) at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses requires', '["Only climbing fiber activation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parallel fiber activation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simultaneous activation of parallel fibers and climbing fiber","Dopaminergic input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABAergic input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simultaneous activation of parallel fibers and climbing fiber', 'LTD at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses requires near-simultaneous activation of the parallel fiber and the climbing fiber, a form of associative (Hebbian) learning. Activation of only one input, or dopaminergic or GABAergic signaling, does not induce the depression.'),
('f857fbcb-5944-5271-84bf-486df38dba01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar LTD results in', '["Decreased climbing fiber activity","Increased Purkinje firing","Strengthened synapses","change","Weakened parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses"]'::jsonb, 'Weakened parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses', 'Cerebellar LTD is a long-lasting weakening of parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses, reducing Purkinje cell responses to parallel fiber input. It does not strengthen synapses or increase Purkinje firing, and it reflects changes at the synapse rather than altered climbing fiber activity.'),
('8294064f-d3a9-5caa-8cea-b99f6d3fa4b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum is involved in which type of memory?', '["Working memory","Declarative memory","Episodic memory","Procedural (motor skill) memory","Semantic memory"]'::jsonb, 'Procedural (motor skill) memory', 'The cerebellum is central to procedural or motor-skill memory, such as learning to ride a bicycle, through synaptic plasticity at Purkinje cells. Declarative, episodic, semantic, and working memory depend mainly on the medial temporal lobe and prefrontal cortex.'),
('a049a18d-fa29-5d3f-998b-482bf4dc51c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome (CCAS) includes', '["Only nystagmus resulting in decreased functional activity","Executive dysfunction, impaired visuospatial reasoning, personality changes","Only speech problems mediated by specific receptor activation","Only motor symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only gait problems leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Executive dysfunction, impaired visuospatial reasoning, personality changes', 'CCAS (Schmahmann syndrome) results from lesions of the cerebellar hemispheres and posterior vermis and features executive dysfunction, impaired visuospatial reasoning, and affective or personality changes. It extends beyond motor signs, and nystagmus, dysarthria, or gait problems alone do not define the syndrome.'),
('3cdff096-3742-5802-ac71-487d9ff12069'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar tonsillar herniation (coning) occurs when', '["Cerebellum herniates through foramen of Magendie","Cerebellum herniates through foramen of Luschka","Cerebellum herniates through tentorial notch","Cerebellar tonsils herniate through foramen magnum compressing medulla","Cerebellum herniates through falx"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar tonsils herniate through foramen magnum compressing medulla', 'In tonsillar herniation, the cerebellar tonsils are forced downward through the foramen magnum and compress the medulla, which can cause respiratory arrest and death. The foramen of Magendie and Luschka are CSF outflow foramina, and tentorial or falcine herniations involve different structures.'),
('c81650ae-eeaa-5209-ab55-f987960e58dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Tonsillar herniation is most commonly caused by', '["Hydrocephalus resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF leak accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meningitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Stroke without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased intracranial pressure (posterior fossa mass)"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intracranial pressure (posterior fossa mass)', 'Tonsillar herniation most often results from increased intracranial pressure, particularly a mass in the posterior fossa that pushes the tonsils through the foramen magnum. Hydrocephalus, meningitis, or stroke may raise pressure but a posterior fossa mass is the characteristic cause.'),
('43fd8fb3-1806-50ff-b3b8-21dd358c1eef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Dandy-Walker malformation involves', '["Hydrocephalus resulting in decreased functional activity","Arnold-Chiari malformation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar atrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Absent or hypoplastic cerebellar vermis, cystic dilation of fourth ventricle","Cerebellar tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absent or hypoplastic cerebellar vermis, cystic dilation of fourth ventricle', 'Dandy-Walker malformation is characterized by agenesis or hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis with cystic dilatation of the fourth ventricle, often accompanied by hydrocephalus. It is a distinct posterior fossa malformation, not Chiari malformation, atrophy, or a tumor.'),
('c1486a3f-6ca7-53b8-a75a-294e776f9c3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Chiari malformation type 1 involves', '["Herniation of cerebellar tonsils below foramen magnum","Hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem herniation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar vermis herniation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fourth ventricle herniation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of cerebellar tonsils below foramen magnum', 'Chiari type 1 malformation is the descent of the cerebellar tonsils below the foramen magnum, often asymptomatic until adulthood. Vermis, brainstem, and fourth ventricle involvement or hydrocephalus characterize the more severe type 2 malformation.'),
('47922ffd-a0b9-56be-9c62-b9893c4f9df7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Chiari malformation type 2 involves', '["Only medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Only fourth ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only tonsils mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Herniation of cerebellar vermis, brainstem, and fourth ventricle through foramen magnum (with myelomeningocele)"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of cerebellar vermis, brainstem, and fourth ventricle through foramen magnum (with myelomeningocele)', 'Chiari type 2 malformation is the downward herniation of the cerebellar vermis, brainstem, and fourth ventricle through the foramen magnum, and it is usually associated with a myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus. It is a more extensive hindbrain malformation than the tonsillar-only type 1 variant.'),
('9f4b64f5-1edd-580e-9f90-a7f83b37eb8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar hypoplasia may present with', '["Visual loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Seizures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Progressive ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation","Non-progressive ataxia, hypotonia, and motor delay","Spasticity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-progressive ataxia, hypotonia, and motor delay', 'Cerebellar hypoplasia typically presents as non-progressive ataxia, hypotonia, and delayed motor milestones, since the developmental deficit is static. Progressive ataxia suggests a neurodegenerative disease, and visual loss, seizures, or spasticity point to other cortical pathologies.'),
('0187b18e-df90-5ebb-bc64-42c00cafffe3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Olivopontocerebellar atrophy (OPCA) is a', '["Acquired cerebellar lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Sporadic or inherited neurodegenerative disease","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sporadic or inherited neurodegenerative disease', 'OPCA is a neurodegenerative disorder of the cerebellum, pons, and inferior olives that occurs sporadically or as an inherited form within the spinocerebellar ataxia spectrum. It is not an acute, infectious, traumatic, or neoplastic process.'),
('6a2dac73-a5b9-52b9-89b3-34e7c2c2551c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Friedreich ataxia involves', '["Only spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinocerebellar degeneration, dorsal root ganglia atrophy, and corticospinal tract involvement","Only peripheral nerves mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cerebral cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cerebellar degeneration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar degeneration, dorsal root ganglia atrophy, and corticospinal tract involvement', 'Friedreich ataxia is characterized by degeneration of spinocerebellar tracts, atrophy of dorsal root ganglia, and involvement of the corticospinal tracts, with the cerebellum itself relatively spared. The combination of these findings distinguishes it from purely spinal, cortical, or cerebellar diseases.'),
('4219c633-fa43-5bc1-accc-c3cec1cd2774'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Friedreich ataxia typically presents with', '["Resting tremor resulting in decreased functional activity","Gait ataxia, absent deep tendon reflexes, extensor plantar response, scoliosis","Cerebellar ataxia with hyperreflexia mediated by specific receptor activation","Chorea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myoclonus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gait ataxia, absent deep tendon reflexes, extensor plantar response, scoliosis', 'Friedreich ataxia presents with progressive gait ataxia, areflexia due to dorsal root ganglion involvement, an extensor plantar response from corticospinal tract disease, and skeletal deformities such as scoliosis. The combination of absent reflexes with pyramidal signs is characteristic and contrasts with the other listed findings.'),
('81e8b58c-8aeb-5e46-b384-4ef289f82c7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which type of cerebellar afferent fibers are the most numerous?', '["Granule cell axons","Purkinje fibers","Mossy fibers","Parallel fibers","Climbing fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Mossy fibers', 'Mossy fibers are the most numerous cerebellar afferent type, greatly outnumbering climbing fibers, and they synapse on granule cells. Climbing fibers are the far less frequent contralateral olivary afferents, while granule cell axons (parallel fibers) are intrinsic fibers rather than afferents.'),
('26442936-9c7c-569c-8cf9-fe260d6128a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Climbing fibers originate exclusively from', '["Red nucleus","Vestibular nuclei","Pontine nuclei","Contralateral inferior olivary nucleus","Ipsilateral inferior olive"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral inferior olivary nucleus', 'Climbing fibers arise exclusively from the inferior olivary nucleus and cross the midline to innervate the contralateral cerebellar cortex. The pontine nuclei give mossy fibers, and the red nucleus, vestibular nuclei, and ipsilateral olive do not supply climbing fibers.'),
('2b64c7ba-d517-5902-af82-0fde10de5ce0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Mossy fibers originate from all EXCEPT', '["Spinal cord","Inferior olive","Pontine nuclei","Vestibular nuclei","Reticular formation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior olive', 'Mossy fibers come from the spinal cord, pontine nuclei, vestibular nuclei, and reticular formation, whereas the inferior olive exclusively gives climbing fibers. Therefore the inferior olive is the one source listed that does not contribute mossy fibers.'),
('00bf04c5-2c28-52b8-8855-9ae92bb093f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar white matter is called', '["Arbor vitae (tree of life)","Corona radiata","Medullary center","Internal capsule","Centrum semiovale"]'::jsonb, 'Arbor vitae (tree of life)', 'The branched pattern of cerebellar white matter is termed the arbor vitae because its treelike appearance on midline section resembles branches. Corona radiata, internal capsule, and centrum semiovale are cerebral white matter structures, and medullary center is not the accepted term for this pattern.'),
('99728d56-a716-56cc-b316-b71149fbbaa8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The dentate nucleus is located in which part of cerebellum?', '["Vermis resulting in decreased functional activity","Paravermis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep white matter of lateral hemisphere","Cerebellar cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flocculonodular lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep white matter of lateral hemisphere', 'The dentate nucleus is the largest cerebellar deep nucleus and lies within the deep white matter of the lateral hemisphere (cerebrocerebellum). It is not in the vermis, paravermis, cortex, or flocculonodular lobe.'),
('0667e460-2e54-58b0-87a7-c3f1b7127a44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The emboliform nucleus is located', '["In the vermis","In the flocculus","Medial to globose","Lateral to dentate","Medial to dentate, lateral to globose"]'::jsonb, 'Medial to dentate, lateral to globose', 'The emboliform and globose nuclei together form the interposed nucleus, with the emboliform positioned medial to the dentate and lateral to the globose nucleus. It does not lie in the vermis, flocculus, or lateral to the dentate.'),
('f701578a-d516-532e-b112-3c2b990358bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The dentate nucleus is the equivalent of the', '["Fastigial nucleus","Lateral cerebellar nucleus in animals","Interposed nucleus","Medial cerebellar nucleus","Vestibular nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral cerebellar nucleus in animals', 'The dentate nucleus is phylogenetically the same structure as the lateral cerebellar nucleus found in animals. The fastigial nucleus corresponds to the medial nucleus, and the emboliform plus globose nuclei correspond to the interposed nucleus.'),
('79cf1a0f-11a4-5060-8828-4191af97b636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar mossy fibers activate granule cells, which via parallel fibers', '["Inhibit deep nuclei","Inhibit Purkinje cells","Excite Purkinje cells (simple spikes)","Excite Golgi cells","Excite deep nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Excite Purkinje cells (simple spikes)', 'Mossy fibers excite granule cells, whose parallel fibers in turn excite Purkinje cells, producing simple spikes. They do not inhibit Purkinje cells or the deep nuclei; excitation of these targets is mediated through other pathways.'),
('eb7de8c6-7fd6-5c08-b8b0-905f83f9c476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Climbing fibers cause', '["Simple spikes resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibition of Purkinje cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activation of Golgi cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Activation of granule cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complex spikes in Purkinje cells (multiple spike bursts)"]'::jsonb, 'Complex spikes in Purkinje cells (multiple spike bursts)', 'A climbing fiber has a powerful excitatory effect on a single Purkinje cell, evoking a characteristic complex spike composed of multiple spike bursts. Simple spikes are produced by parallel fiber input rather than by climbing fibers.'),
('0abe932d-0424-53ce-a8f4-00af7dfe4b70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Simple spikes of Purkinje cells are due to', '["Parallel fiber (mossy fiber→granule cell) input","Golgi cell input accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stellate cell input mediated by specific receptor activation","Basket cell input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Climbing fiber input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel fiber (mossy fiber→granule cell) input', 'Simple spikes are firing responses of Purkinje cells driven by parallel fiber input arriving through the mossy fiber-granule cell pathway. Inhibitory interneurons such as Golgi, stellate, and basket cells do not generate simple spikes, and climbing fiber input produces complex spikes.'),
('d8acf031-c661-5cfd-90dc-a31027cc38ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Complex spikes of Purkinje cells are due to', '["Granule cell input","Deep nuclear input","Mossy fiber input","Parallel fiber input","Climbing fiber input (low frequency, 1 Hz)"]'::jsonb, 'Climbing fiber input (low frequency, 1 Hz)', 'Complex spikes of Purkinje cells are produced by climbing fiber input and occur at low frequency, roughly 1 Hz. Granule cell, mossy fiber, and parallel fiber inputs generate simple spikes rather than complex spikes.'),
('c6455ca5-698b-5b54-893d-20340e2df82a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar nuclei receive inhibitory input from', '["Parallel fibers","Purkinje cells (GABAergic)","Climbing fibers","Granule cells","Mossy fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cells (GABAergic)', 'Purkinje cells are the sole output of the cerebellar cortex and are GABAergic, providing inhibitory input to the deep cerebellar nuclei. Climbing and mossy fibers collateralize to give excitatory input, while granule and parallel fibers are excitatory.'),
('07781750-03e8-5f95-a9c9-d35df2227ebb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar nuclei receive excitatory input from', '["Granule cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Purkinje cells (inhibitory) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mossy fibers and climbing fibers (collaterals)","Golgi cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basket cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mossy fibers and climbing fibers (collaterals)', 'Collateral branches of mossy fibers and climbing fibers provide excitatory input to the deep cerebellar nuclei before the parent fibers reach the cortex. Purkinje cells are inhibitory, and the listed interneurons do not innervate the deep nuclei.'),
('4eb46e67-6949-5fd4-8537-e539854f015b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum receives its blood supply from which arteries?', '["Basilar, internal carotid resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral, spinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior cerebellar (SCA), anterior inferior cerebellar (AICA), posterior inferior cerebellar (PICA)","Posterior cerebral, middle cerebral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior cerebral, posterior cerebral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior cerebellar (SCA), anterior inferior cerebellar (AICA), posterior inferior cerebellar (PICA)', 'The cerebellum is supplied by three paired branches of the vertebrobasilar system: the SCA, AICA, and PICA. It is not supplied by the internal carotid or anterior cerebral arteries, which serve the cerebrum.'),
('3cc8f99e-8b67-5aec-a6a4-6f2672eeb26d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Superior cerebellar artery (SCA) supplies', '["Flocculonodular lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior surface accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior surface of cerebellum (above primary fissure) and dentate nucleus, SCP, midbrain","Deep nuclei only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vermis only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior surface of cerebellum (above primary fissure) and dentate nucleus, SCP, midbrain', 'The SCA supplies the superior surface of the cerebellum above the primary fissure, the dentate nucleus, the superior cerebellar peduncle, and the upper brainstem/midbrain. The flocculonodular lobe and inferior surface are supplied by the PICA and AICA.'),
('00829c97-6e34-5746-99c1-3032f395a862'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) supplies', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral pons, MCP, flocculus, and cerebellum on inferior surface","Superior cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral pons, MCP, flocculus, and cerebellum on inferior surface', 'The AICA supplies the lateral pons, middle cerebellar peduncle, flocculus, and the inferior surface of the cerebellum (plus inner ear structures). The medulla is supplied by the PICA, and the occipital lobe and thalamus are not cerebellar territories.'),
('b7f5fe92-fe09-5915-a655-cb1572119422'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) supplies', '["Superior cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Posteroinferior cerebellum and lateral medulla","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posteroinferior cerebellum and lateral medulla', 'PICA is a branch of the vertebral artery that irrigates the posteroinferior cerebellar surface and the lateral medulla; its occlusion produces lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome. The superior cerebellum is supplied by the SCA, the pons by AICA and basilar branches, and the midbrain by basilar/PCA branches, so those choices are wrong.'),
('df32dce8-09b3-50eb-8014-268e4db97075'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar hemorrhage is commonly associated with', '["Aneurysm resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertension (most common: dentate nucleus region)","Trauma mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AVM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension (most common: dentate nucleus region)', 'Hypertension is the most common cause of spontaneous cerebellar hemorrhage, typically centered on the dentate nucleus region. Aneurysms, AVMs, trauma, and tumor bleeding are less common or atypical mechanisms of primary cerebellar hemorrhage.'),
('ec1448ab-5d84-5765-87c8-722bebf62502'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar stroke symptoms include', '["Aphasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Seizure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual field defect mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemiplegia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertigo, nausea, vomiting, headache, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus"]'::jsonb, 'Vertigo, nausea, vomiting, headache, ataxia, dysarthria, nystagmus', 'Ischemia in the posterior inferior cerebellar territory produces vestibulocerebellar symptoms including vertigo, nausea, vomiting, headache, gait ataxia, dysarthria, and nystagmus. Aphasia, hemiplegia, seizures, and visual field defects reflect dysfunctions outside the cerebellum, typically in the cerebral hemispheres.'),
('90664155-ef07-5966-9665-bc2aa59689cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar mutism syndrome after surgery in children is characterized by', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","Only speech problems accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transient mutism followed by ataxic dysarthria and emotional lability","Permanent mutism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor symptoms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transient mutism followed by ataxic dysarthria and emotional lability', 'Postsurgical cerebellar mutism syndrome in children typically begins with transient mutism that resolves, followed by ataxic dysarthria and emotional lability. Mutism is not permanent, and the syndrome involves speech and affect in addition to motor function, so the narrower choices are incorrect.'),
('998f5fca-243e-579b-b3e4-e13f61f08ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar degeneration in alcoholics primarily affects', '["Dentate nucleus","Anterior lobe (vermis) — gait ataxia","Pontine nuclei","Inferior olive","Flocculonodular lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior lobe (vermis) — gait ataxia', 'Chronic alcoholism selectively damages the anterior superior vermis and adjacent hemispheres, producing prominent gait and lower-limb ataxia while sparing the dentate nucleus and olive. The other structures listed are not the primary site of alcoholic cerebellar degeneration.'),
('7f3c8d85-c00c-5ec5-895c-33cbe80abfbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration is associated with', '["Anti-GAD resulting in decreased functional activity","Anti-Yo antibodies (ovarian/breast cancer), anti-Tr, anti-Hu, anti-Ri","Anti-AChR mediated by specific receptor activation","antibodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-NMDA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-Yo antibodies (ovarian/breast cancer), anti-Tr, anti-Hu, anti-Ri', 'Paraneoplastic cerebellar degeneration is caused by antibodies directed against Purkinje cells, notably anti-Yo (ovarian/breast cancer) and also anti-Tr, anti-Hu, and anti-Ri. Anti-AChR, anti-GAD, and anti-NMDA antibodies target different antigens and are not classically associated with this syndrome.'),
('b9f398a3-b5c1-5ba8-9451-438dc0b676f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Gluten ataxia (anti-gliadin antibody) causes', '["Cerebellar degeneration with gait ataxia","Myopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral neuropathy only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dementia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parkinsonism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar degeneration with gait ataxia', 'Anti-gliadin (transglutaminase-6) antibodies in gluten ataxia target cerebellar tissue, producing immune-mediated cerebellar degeneration with progressive gait ataxia. The listed alternatives describe other neurologic problems rather than the cerebellar degeneration that characterizes gluten ataxia.'),
('d3577574-4b8c-5c36-b8f1-ebe2b3686d48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Multiple system atrophy (MSA-C) includes', '["Cerebellar ataxia, autonomic dysfunction, and parkinsonism","Only dementia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation","Only parkinsonism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only autonomic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia, autonomic dysfunction, and parkinsonism', 'The cerebellar variant of multiple system atrophy combines progressive cerebellar ataxia with autonomic dysfunction and parkinsonism. It is a multisystem disease, so options naming only a single domain such as dementia, ataxia, parkinsonism, or autonomic failure are incomplete.'),
('fc1c3151-5d61-5d27-baa6-ecfc31e30278'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebrocerebellar learning loop involves', '["Only climbing fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parallel fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor error signaled by climbing fibers adjusting parallel fiber-Purkinje synapse strength","learning without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only mossy fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor error signaled by climbing fibers adjusting parallel fiber-Purkinje synapse strength', 'Motor learning in the cerebrocerebellar loop depends on climbing fibers signaling motor errors, which trigger long-term depression at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses. A single element such as climbing fibers, parallel fibers, or mossy fibers alone cannot mediate this learning loop.'),
('606b9ad4-0f4d-5ae1-a373-57a8fe7d1c1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Saccadic eye movements are controlled by which part of cerebellum?', '["Vermis only resulting in decreased functional activity","Flocculonodular lobe only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral hemisphere mediated by specific receptor activation","Dentate nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal vermis (oculomotor vermis) and fastigial nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal vermis (oculomotor vermis) and fastigial nucleus', 'Saccades are generated and calibrated by the dorsal (oculomotor) vermis and the fastigial nuclei; lesions here cause saccadic dysmetria. The flocculonodular region instead governs vestibular reflexes and smooth pursuit, so the other options are incorrect.'),
('eb7450b3-a46c-547d-82a2-6020c1b2d322'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Smooth pursuit eye movements involve', '["Flocculus and paraflocculus of cerebellum","Fastigial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dentate mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal vermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nodulus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flocculus and paraflocculus of cerebellum', 'Smooth pursuit and gaze holding are mediated by the flocculus and paraflocculus, which adjust vestibular signals. Impaired smooth pursuit and gaze-evoked nystagmus result from floccular damage, whereas the dorsal vermis primarily handles saccades.'),
('732264ea-860c-5ae4-a5b0-6af800f620d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellar nodulus is part of', '["Vermis resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flocculonodular lobe (archicerebellum)","Hemisphere without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flocculonodular lobe (archicerebellum)', 'The nodulus is grouped with the flocculi into the flocculonodular lobe (archicerebellum), the phylogenetically oldest, vestibulocerebellar part. It is not classified as part of the vermis, anterior lobe, posterior lobe, or hemisphere in this scheme.'),
('b9b63b1e-d43b-5513-a0c4-94b9a8e379fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The uvula is part of', '["Anterior lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Flocculonodular lobe (or posterior vermis)","Pyramis mediated by specific receptor activation","Culmen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tonsil leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flocculonodular lobe (or posterior vermis)', 'The uvula lies in the posterior vermis and is closely associated with the flocculonodular lobe functionally. The culmen belongs to the anterior lobe, the pyramis and tonsil are distinct lobules, making those choices incorrect.'),
('ba6aa7b8-23ef-5055-afaa-12b703f6199b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellar tonsils are part of', '["Flocculonodular lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior lobe (inferior surface of hemisphere)","Paravermis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior lobe (inferior surface of hemisphere)', 'The cerebellar tonsils are hemispheric lobules on the inferior surface of the posterior lobe, positioned near the foramen magnum where they may herniate. They are neither part of the flocculonodular lobe, anterior lobe, vermis, nor paravermis.'),
('33b29f9b-41fb-5209-8a7b-ddcced97be2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The flocculus is part of', '["Dentate resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flocculonodular lobe (hemispheric portion)","Anterior lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Paravermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flocculonodular lobe (hemispheric portion)', 'The flocculus is the lateral, hemispheric portion of the flocculonodular lobe, forming the vestibulocerebellum together with the midline nodulus. It is not part of the dentate nucleus, anterior lobe, posterior lobe, or paravermis.'),
('48955a5c-5b7d-5786-948f-8152b9d45ad7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The prepyramidal fissure separates', '["Pyramis from uvula","Culmen from declive","Lobulus simplex from ansiform","Nodulus from uvula","Tonsil from uvula"]'::jsonb, 'Tonsil from uvula', 'The prepyramidal fissure lies immediately behind the uvula and separates the uvula medially from the tonsil laterally. It does not separate pyramis from uvula nor culmen from declive, which have different intervening fissures.'),
('6600c89d-f8e8-51f5-a806-1232fffab344'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The horizontal fissure of cerebellum separates', '["Anterior and posterior lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Flocculus and tonsil accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior surfaces of cerebellar hemisphere","Vermis and hemisphere without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crus I and Crus II leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior surfaces of cerebellar hemisphere', 'The great horizontal fissure runs along the lateral surface of each cerebellar hemisphere and divides it into superior and inferior portions. It does not separate the anterior and posterior lobes, vermis and hemisphere, or crus I and crus II.'),
('4daec706-f365-5417-bc84-5f963a1334bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The ansoparamedian fissure separates', '["Flocculus from hemisphere","Tonsil from biventer","Vermis and hemisphere","Anterior and posterior lobes","Crus I and Crus II of ansiform lobule"]'::jsonb, 'Crus I and Crus II of ansiform lobule', 'The ansoparamedian (intercrural) fissure divides the ansiform lobule into crus I (superior) and crus II (inferior). It does not separate flocculus from hemisphere, tonsil from biventer, or vermis from hemisphere.'),
('bfc6a5f5-f915-5dd8-a5f4-fa5bf9644dac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Crus I and Crus II are part of', '["Vermis resulting in decreased functional activity","Flocculonodular lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paravermis mediated by specific receptor activation","Ansiform lobule (posterior lobe hemisphere)","Anterior lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ansiform lobule (posterior lobe hemisphere)', 'Crus I and crus II form the ansiform lobule, which occupies the hemispheric part of the posterior lobe. The vermis, flocculonodular lobe, paravermis, and anterior lobe are distinct regions and do not include the ansiform lobule.'),
('1f115f8f-0311-507b-b101-d6416ee0064b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Foliation of cerebellum refers to', '["Parallel folds of cerebellar cortex","Peduncle bundles","Gray matter lamellae","White matter branching","Nuclear clusters"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel folds of cerebellar cortex', 'Foliation describes the parallel folds (folia) of the cerebellar cortex, which increase its surface area. The peduncle bundles, gray matter lamellae, white-matter arborization, and nuclear clusters describe different structural features of the cerebellum.'),
('eb9d07be-23f9-5f7e-9473-6bb2d0528328'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum develops from which embryonic structure?', '["Diencephalon","Mesencephalon","Telencephalon","Metencephalon (alar plate)","Myelencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Metencephalon (alar plate)', 'The cerebellum differentiates from the alar plates of the metencephalon, the rostral subdivision of the hindbrain that also forms the pons. The myelencephalon gives rise to the medulla, and the mesencephalon to the midbrain, so those are incorrect.'),
('53f254ce-e67e-5d4a-b565-6d79cba57000'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar granule cells are derived from', '["Basal plate","Ventricular zone","Rhombic lip (upper rhombic lip)","Neural crest","Mesoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Rhombic lip (upper rhombic lip)', 'Cerebellar granule cell precursors originate from the upper rhombic lip and migrate over the surface to form the external granule layer before descending inward. They do not arise from the basal plate, ventricular zone, neural crest, or mesoderm, whose derivatives differ.'),
('40b579b3-2de2-59ae-83e8-e1ab8c90a94f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Purkinje cells are generated from', '["Mesoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural crest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rhombic lip mediated by specific receptor activation","Endoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventricular zone of metencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular zone of metencephalon', 'Purkinje cells arise from the neuroepithelium of the ventricular zone of the metencephalon and migrate outward to form the Purkinje cell layer. The rhombic lip produces the granule cells instead, and the neural crest and germ layers give rise to other tissues.'),
('d262be49-15df-52d8-8ea2-5cea94858cf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar development begins at', '["1st trimester","6th week of gestation","Birth","3rd trimester","Postnatal year 1"]'::jsonb, '6th week of gestation', 'The cerebellar primordium appears around the sixth week of gestation as swellings derived from the rhombic lips and metencephalic alar plates. Foliation and growth continue through later gestation and into the first postnatal year, so earlier- or later-onset options are wrong.'),
('3507cfc2-5b39-5ed3-a25a-e6a5f492f662'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Zebrin (aldolase C) staining reveals', '["Uniform staining resulting in decreased functional activity","Horizontal layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nuclear staining mediated by specific receptor activation","White matter staining without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasagittal stripes (modules) of Purkinje cells"]'::jsonb, 'Parasagittal stripes (modules) of Purkinje cells', 'Zebrin/aldolase C immunostaining labels alternating parasagittal bands (stripes) of Purkinje cells, revealing the modular compartmental organization of the cerebellar cortex. It does not produce uniform, laminar, nuclear, or white-matter staining patterns.'),
('7619f5aa-fe5a-5726-9de7-70591ff5c68b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar modules (microzones) consist of', '["Only granule cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Only deep nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A Purkinje cell + its target deep cerebellar nucleus + afferent source","Only Purkinje cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only afferent fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A Purkinje cell + its target deep cerebellar nucleus + afferent source', 'A cerebellar microzone (module) is the functional unit made up of a group of Purkinje cells, the deep cerebellar nucleus they project to, and their shared afferent source. A single cell type or fiber type alone does not constitute a functional module.'),
('be218ab2-62cb-505d-9b13-d556a5d1e455'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellar cortex has a uniform cytoarchitecture known as', '["Cerebellar circuit (same 3-layer structure throughout)","Neocortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paleocortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Archicortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limbic cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar circuit (same 3-layer structure throughout)', 'Throughout the cerebellum the cortex has the same basic three-layer histology, molecular, Purkinje, and granular, organized in a repeating circuit. This uniform circuit architecture is unlike the varied cytoarchitecture of the neocortex and the archicortex/paleocortex types listed.'),
('45c7b65e-fcef-581f-9c41-87fd316b5a78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Inhibition of Purkinje cells by molecular layer interneurons (stellate/basket) provides', '["Excitation resulting in decreased functional activity","Global inhibition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral inhibition (sharpening Purkinje cell output)","Feed-forward excitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral inhibition (sharpening Purkinje cell output)', 'Stellate and basket cells inhibit neighboring Purkinje cells around an excited zone, providing lateral inhibition that sharpens and focuses the Purkinje cell output. This is neither global inhibition nor excitation and lacks the feed-forward excitatory effect described.'),
('6ad3a351-635c-5308-8f10-7226f132b558'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum influences the vestibular system via', '["Globose resulting in decreased functional activity","All deep nuclei equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emboliform mediated by specific receptor activation","Fastigial nucleus → vestibular nuclei (direct and indirect)","Dentate nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial nucleus → vestibular nuclei (direct and indirect)', 'The fastigial nucleus projects directly and indirectly to the vestibular nuclei, transmitting vestibular cerebellar output. The dentate, emboliform, and globose nuclei instead feed descending and cerebello-thalamic systems rather than the vestibular nuclei.'),
('91d7d03d-4da5-5505-bc19-ef590aaa0080'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar output to the reticular formation is mainly from', '["Fastigial nucleus (via uncinate fasciculus)","Dentate nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Flocculus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interposed nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial nucleus (via uncinate fasciculus)', 'Fastigial efferents travel in the uncinate fasciculus within the juxtarestiform body to reach the bulbar reticular formation and vestibular nuclei. The dentate and interposed nuclei project mainly to the red nucleus and thalamus, not the reticular formation.'),
('837424a0-8934-51c1-a8c6-824eae72a9a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The uncinate fasciculus (hook bundle) connects', '["Cerebellar cortex to deep nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Globose to red nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emboliform to red nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Fastigial nucleus to vestibular nuclei and reticular formation (via ICP)","Dentate nucleus to thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial nucleus to vestibular nuclei and reticular formation (via ICP)', 'The uncinate (hook) bundle arises from the fastigial nucleus, decussates, and ascends to reach the vestibular nuclei and reticular formation. It does not carry dentatothalamic, cerebello-cortical, or intra-cerebellar fibers, so the other options are wrong.'),
('3e992f30-d4ab-5a79-8b6e-2e6176101877'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The juxtarestiform body is part of', '["Restiform body resulting in decreased functional activity","SCP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar commissure mediated by specific receptor activation","MCP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ICP (medial part) containing fastigial efferents"]'::jsonb, 'ICP (medial part) containing fastigial efferents', 'The juxtarestiform body is the medial, older part of the inferior cerebellar peduncle, carrying fastigial efferents and primary vestibular afferents. The restiform body is the larger lateral portion carrying spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, and olivocerebellar fibers.'),
('7c5c6596-a207-5b80-ade1-e92954683130'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The restiform body is the main part of ICP containing', '["Pontocerebellar fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Dentatothalamic fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All cerebellar efferents mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, cuneocerebellar afferents","Fastigial efferents leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, cuneocerebellar afferents', 'The restiform body is the large lateral division of the inferior cerebellar peduncle that conveys dorsal spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, and olivocerebellar afferents into the cerebellum. Dentatothalamic and fastigial efferent fibers travel elsewhere.'),
('78015c55-db2c-51d9-a8a4-bd47b410f244'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellopontine angle is a space between', '["Cerebellum, pons, and medulla (superiorly: CN V; inferiorly: CN IX, X, XI)","Cerebellum and fourth ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla and spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum and midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons and medulla only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum, pons, and medulla (superiorly: CN V; inferiorly: CN IX, X, XI)', 'The cerebello-pontine angle is the region formed among the cerebellum, pons, and medulla, containing the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves and bounded superiorly by CN V and inferiorly by CN IX, X, and XI. The other listed spaces do not fit these boundaries.'),
('d19a54f2-efa2-5580-a8aa-7025431049d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebello-olivary fibers connect', '["Cerebellum to pons","Cerebellar nuclei to inferior olive (feedback loop)","Cerebellum to spinal cord","Inferior olive to cerebellum","Cerebellum to thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar nuclei to inferior olive (feedback loop)', 'Cerebello-olivary fibers connect the cerebellar (dentate) nuclei to the contralateral inferior olive, closing a feedback loop in which the olive then returns climbing fibers to the cerebellum. Projections to pons, spinal cord, or thalamus do not form this olive loop.'),
('5cff3820-507a-5ae0-8cc4-f27c7c50da46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The dentato-olivary pathway (Guillain-Mollaret triangle) involves', '["Dentate → thalamus → cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Dentate → superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dentate nucleus → red nucleus → inferior olive → cerebellum","Dentate → spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dentate → vestibular nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate nucleus → red nucleus → inferior olive → cerebellum', 'The Guillain-Mollaret triangle is formed by the dentate nucleus projecting through the superior cerebellar peduncle to the red nucleus, then via the central tegmental tract to the inferior olive, which returns climbing fibers to the cerebellum. The other pathways are parts of distinct circuits.'),
('2fc80262-9998-53e8-8b37-8e589ce6976b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Lesion of dentato-olivary pathway causes', '["Palatal myoclonus (ocular myoclonus)","Ataxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dysarthria mediated by specific receptor activation","Intention tremor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nystagmus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatal myoclonus (ocular myoclonus)', 'Disruption of the dentato-olivary (Guillain-Mollaret) pathway causes rhythmic palatal myoclonus, often with ocular myoclonus and hypertrophic degeneration of the inferior olive. Ataxia, dysarthria, intention tremor, and nystagmus arise from other cerebellar pathways.'),
('00ccf58c-225b-51c1-a116-9e82376603df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Palatal myoclonus (tremor) is also known as', '["Palatal tremor (involves inferior olive hypertrophy)","Dystonic tremor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar tremor mediated by specific receptor activation","Essential tremor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parkinsonian tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palatal tremor (involves inferior olive hypertrophy)', 'Palatal myoclonus is a symptomatic palatal tremor associated with hypertrophic degeneration of the inferior olive after lesions of the dentato-olivary pathway. Essential, parkinsonian, dystonic, and classic cerebellar tremors are distinct disorders.'),
('44ddca7b-eb99-5b1a-b1ca-b8e40a8566f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Essential tremor is thought to involve', '["Basal ganglia only resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar-thalamic-cortical loop dysfunction","Cortex only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peripheral nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar-thalamic-cortical loop dysfunction', 'Essential tremor involves abnormal oscillatory activity in the cerebello-thalamo-cortical loop, which is why ventral intermediate (VIM) thalamic stimulation can be effective. The basal ganglia, cortex alone, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves are not the primary substrate.'),
('210826e1-184e-58c8-b41e-efa91ac4c013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar damage can cause', '["Only eye movement deficits","Cognitive impairment (CCAS), not only motor","Only motor deficits","cognitive effects","Only speech deficits"]'::jsonb, 'Cognitive impairment (CCAS), not only motor', 'The cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome (CCAS) causes executive, visuospatial, and affective deficits, so cerebellar damage produces cognitive as well as motor impairment. Viewing the cerebellum as affecting only motor, speech, or eye movements underestimates its involvement.'),
('8ce4360d-9ebc-5f77-8e3d-b94c9a3edded'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar projections to the thalamus terminate in', '["VPM only resulting in decreased functional activity","LGN accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MGN mediated by specific receptor activation","VL, VA, and intralaminar nuclei","VPL only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VL, VA, and intralaminar nuclei', 'Dentate and interposed efferents ascend via the superior cerebellar peduncle and terminate in the ventral lateral, ventral anterior, and intralaminar thalamic nuclei before reaching the motor cortex. The sensory relay nuclei VPM, VPL, and the geniculate bodies are not targets.'),
('2c6b27f2-5572-5909-8b21-560632933a9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar projections to the cerebral cortex are via', '["Cerebellum → red nucleus → cortex","VL thalamus → motor/premotor/prefrontal cortex","Cerebellum → brainstem → cortex","Directly to cortex","Cerebellum → basal ganglia → cortex"]'::jsonb, 'VL thalamus → motor/premotor/prefrontal cortex', 'Cerebellar output reaches the cerebral cortex only polysynaptically, passing through the ventrolateral (VL) thalamus, which projects to motor, premotor, and prefrontal cortices. The cerebellum does not connect directly to the cortex or via the basal ganglia.'),
('1c1f480b-50f6-5d21-9e7f-ba78851b1496'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum modulates movement via', '["Sensing movement resulting in decreased functional activity","Storing motor programs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Comparison of intended movement with actual movement (error correction)","Generating motor commands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Initiating movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Comparison of intended movement with actual movement (error correction)', 'The cerebellum compares a copy of the intended movement with sensory feedback of the actual movement and issues corrective signals when they differ. It does not generate, store, sense, or initiate the primary motor command on its own.'),
('8b14825e-abc9-5d9b-b388-464baff81f0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum is also known as the', '["Extrapyramidal system","Little brain or hindbrain division","Midbrain","Limbic structure","Basal ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Little brain or hindbrain division', 'Cerebellum comes from the Latin for little brain, and it is a division of the hindbrain (metencephalon). The extrapyramidal system, limbic structures, midbrain, and basal ganglia are separate and distinct neural systems.'),
('cf5c1509-363d-5845-a4a0-98318ef51c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar Purkinje cell axons synapse on which of the following?', '["Cerebellar cortex only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pontine nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior olive mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep cerebellar nuclei and (some) vestibular nuclei","Granule cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep cerebellar nuclei and (some) vestibular nuclei', 'All Purkinje cell axons are inhibitory and synapse on the deep cerebellar nuclei, and those of the flocculonodular lobe also project directly to vestibular nuclei. The cortex, inferior olive, pontine nuclei, and granule cells receive different inputs rather than Purkinje output.'),
('a3da0f3e-9630-5011-a7aa-032948a11177'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by Purkinje cells?', '["GABA","Glutamate","Serotonin","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'GABA', 'Purkinje cells are inhibitory GABAergic neurons that release GABA onto the deep cerebellar and vestibular nuclei. Glutamate, serotonin, dopamine, and acetylcholine are not the transmitter released by Purkinje cells.'),
('0328a1ec-98b8-54a5-a711-d727546ca55d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by granule cells?', '["Serotonin","GABA","Glutamate (excitatory)","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate (excitatory)', 'Granule cells are the sole excitatory intrinsic neurons of the cerebellar cortex, releasing glutamate onto Purkinje cells and molecular-layer interneurons via their parallel fibers. GABA, serotonin, dopamine, and acetylcholine do not mediate excitatory granule cell transmission.'),
('31ad6a66-3504-55c3-95c8-2bf15949dc6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by climbing fibers?', '["GABA","Glutamate (excitatory)","Dopamine","Acetylcholine","Serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate (excitatory)', 'Climbing fibers from the inferior olive are excitatory and release glutamate at their strong synapses on Purkinje cell dendrites. GABA, dopamine, acetylcholine, and serotonin are not the transmitter used by climbing fibers.'),
('f730f625-1172-5930-9a18-e8ac2dfd064a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by mossy fibers?', '["Acetylcholine","Glutamate (excitatory)","Dopamine","GABA","Serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate (excitatory)', 'Mossy fibers from spinocerebellar, pontocerebellar, and vestibular afferents are excitatory, releasing glutamate onto granule cells. Acetylcholine, dopamine, GABA, and serotonin do not mediate this excitatory mossy-fiber transmission.'),
('e80b1723-e872-5fb4-abf3-b5f4b95e216a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by Golgi cells?', '["Dopamine","Serotonin","Glutamate","Acetylcholine","GABA (inhibitory)"]'::jsonb, 'GABA (inhibitory)', 'Golgi cells are inhibitory interneurons within the granular layer that release GABA to provide feedback inhibition of granule cells. Dopamine, serotonin, glutamate, and acetylcholine do not mediate the inhibitory action of Golgi cells.'),
('823247f2-c6e5-596c-b93e-ae9377e13f4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by basket cells?', '["Acetylcholine","Glutamate","Dopamine","GABA (inhibitory)","Serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'GABA (inhibitory)', 'Basket cells are inhibitory interneurons of the cerebellar cortex whose GABAergic terminals synapse on Purkinje cell somata and dampen Purkinje output. Glutamate is the excitatory transmitter of granule cells, while acetylcholine, dopamine, and serotonin are not released by basket cells.'),
('07ac6ed3-5baf-583a-a30d-da80c7d4288f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by stellate cells?', '["Glutamate","Acetylcholine","Serotonin","GABA (inhibitory)","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'GABA (inhibitory)', 'Stellate cells are inhibitory molecular-layer interneurons whose GABAergic terminals act on Purkinje cell dendrites. They are functionally parallel to basket cells, so neither excitatory transmitters nor aminergic transmitters are involved.'),
('21bfe680-e32e-5d04-84e6-902ac4f61c2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar deep nuclei use which neurotransmitter for their output?', '["Glutamate (excitatory)","Serotonin","Acetylcholine","GABA","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate (excitatory)', 'Neurons of the dentate, interposed, and fastigial nuclei projecting out of the cerebellum are predominantly glutamatergic and excitatory. GABA is the inhibitory transmitter of Purkinje cells that regulates the nuclei, not the transmitter of their output.'),
('bfe5e1a9-367c-5146-8f3c-9e71ebc73598'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The axons of cerebellar nuclei (glutamatergic) project to', '["Only spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Only brainstem motor nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus, red nucleus, vestibular nuclei, reticular formation","Only basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus, red nucleus, vestibular nuclei, reticular formation', 'Cerebellar nuclear efferents diverge to the ventrolateral thalamus (which relays to motor cortex), the red nucleus, the vestibular nuclei, and the reticular formation. The cerebellum does not project directly and solely to the spinal cord, basal ganglia, or cortex.'),
('7712cc48-086b-5c2b-ade1-617c8907e57d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The nodulus and uvula of cerebellum are involved in', '["Limb coordination resulting in decreased functional activity","Skilled movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor planning mediated by specific receptor activation","Posture only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vestibular adaptation (especially tilt perception and VOR)"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular adaptation (especially tilt perception and VOR)', 'The nodulus and uvula (archicerebellum components) receive heavy vestibular input and modulate the vestibulo-ocular reflex and tilt perception. Limb coordination, skilled movement, and motor planning depend on the intermediate and lateral zones, not these midline vestibular areas.'),
('83ad0586-43f6-558d-9829-7885de62ea2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum receives visual input from', '["Lateral geniculate body resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic nerve directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occipital lobe directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior colliculus and visual cortex (via pontine nuclei)","Thalamus directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior colliculus and visual cortex (via pontine nuclei)', 'Visual information reaches the cerebellum through the superior colliculus and visual association cortex, which relay via pontine nuclei into the middle cerebellar peduncle, mostly to the posterior lobe (e.g., dorsal paraflocculus). The LGN, optic nerve, and thalamus are not direct sources of this input.'),
('574f3cad-3930-51ac-9345-344a2f759506'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum receives auditory input from', '["Medial geniculate body","Lateral lemniscus","Inferior colliculus via pontine nuclei","Auditory cortex directly","Cochlear nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior colliculus via pontine nuclei', 'Auditory signals are relayed to the cerebellum chiefly from the inferior colliculus through pontocerebellar fibers entering the middle cerebellar peduncle. The cochlear nerve, lateral lemniscus, medial geniculate body, and auditory cortex are not the direct projecting sources.'),
('cb634458-481a-57e5-8d8d-92e3b0a70319'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar regulation of muscle tone involves', '["Cerebellar cortex → spinal cord directly resulting in decreased functional activity","effect on tone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fastigial and interposed nuclei → reticulospinal and vestibulospinal tracts","Direct cerebellospinal projections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dentate → rubrospinal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial and interposed nuclei → reticulospinal and vestibulospinal tracts', 'Tone control is exerted through the fastigial and interposed nuclei, which discharge onto reticulospinal and vestibulospinal descending systems. The cerebellar cortex does not project directly to the spinal cord, and the dentate nucleus mainly targets the red nucleus and thalamus.'),
('8bcebe91-bf57-559e-9213-d136723eb509'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum can initiate movement through connections with', '["Basal ganglia resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor cortex (via thalamus) and brainstem motor nuclei","Direct initiation mediated by specific receptor activation","role in initiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor cortex (via thalamus) and brainstem motor nuclei', 'The dentate nucleus sends excitatory output through the superior cerebellar peduncle and ventrolateral thalamus to the motor cortex, and collaterals to brainstem motor nuclei, enabling movement initiation. The cerebellum has no direct pathway to the spinal cord or basal ganglia for this purpose.'),
('71dd93ba-a01d-592c-96f4-3d52994a6ca8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar contribution to speech is via', '["Direct speech production resulting in decreased functional activity","Word finding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Language comprehension mediated by specific receptor activation","Syntax processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fine coordination of laryngeal, oral, and respiratory muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Fine coordination of laryngeal, oral, and respiratory muscles', 'The cerebellum contributes to articulation by coordinating the laryngeal, oral, and respiratory musculature, giving smooth fluent speech; damage causes cerebellar dysarthria. Word finding, comprehension, and syntax are cortical language functions, not cerebellar.'),
('5b437b66-c896-56a1-a0b3-2267f2c30d6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar overflow (asynergia) means', '["Coordinated movement resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decomposition of movement (movement broken into components)","Fast movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Strong movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decomposition of movement (movement broken into components)', 'Asynergia, or decomposition of movement, is the breakdown of a smooth multi-joint action into its separate jerk-like components, a cardinal sign of cerebellar disease. It reflects loss of synergistic timing, so it is neither normal nor characterized by speed or strength.'),
('78c65bdb-966b-5ef4-9761-97ea45f1498e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Check reflex (inability to stop movement suddenly) is abnormal in', '["Basal ganglia lesions","Muscle disease","Peripheral nerve lesions","Cortical lesions","Cerebellar lesions"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar lesions', 'The check reflex is a cerebellar test: the patient cannot brake a movement promptly when resistance is suddenly removed, because the cerebellum times agonist-antagonist coordination. Basal ganglia, muscle, nerve, and cortical disease do not characteristically abolish this braking response.'),
('51155f7d-3c21-56b0-83af-5041e2848faf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Pendular knee jerk (leg swings back and forth) is seen in', '["UMN lesion","LMN lesion","Peripheral neuropathy","Basal ganglia lesion","Cerebellar hypotonia"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar hypotonia', 'In cerebellar hypotonia the reduced muscle tone fails to dampen the reflex, so the leg swings freely in several decreasing oscillations (pendular knee jerk). UMN lesions instead give hyperreflexia with a brisk, non-pendular response, and LMN or peripheral disease diminishes the reflex.'),
('56ead6d9-581d-5929-9f7b-b8466129a825'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum is involved in timing and coordination of', '["Only fine finger movements resulting in decreased functional activity","Only eye movements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only speech mediated by specific receptor activation","Only large joint movements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All voluntary movements and some involuntary (posture, balance)"]'::jsonb, 'All voluntary movements and some involuntary (posture, balance)', 'The cerebellum times and coordinates all voluntary movement and also regulates posture and balance, which are partly involuntary. Its influence is global rather than restricted to fingers, eyes, speech, or large joints alone.'),
('838d52e2-0577-5393-961b-1a5e4df3ca4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which cerebellar peduncle contains both afferent and efferent fibers?', '["Inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP)","None","Middle cerebellar peduncle","Superior cerebellar peduncle","All three"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior cerebellar peduncle (ICP)', 'The inferior cerebellar peduncle carries afferents (e.g., dorsal spinocerebellar, olivocerebellar, vestibulocerebellar) and also efferents such as the uncinate fasciculus. The middle peduncle is purely afferent and the superior peduncle is predominantly efferent.'),
('f429e4b9-b87d-5339-9958-480dc034200f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The superior cerebellar peduncle is mainly', '["Afferent only from spinal cord","Afferent","Neither","Both equally","Efferent (dentatothalamic)"]'::jsonb, 'Efferent (dentatothalamic)', 'The superior cerebellar peduncle is the principal efferent pathway, carrying dentatothalamic (and dentatorubral) fibers out of the cerebellum; its only major afferent, the anterior spinocerebellar tract, is smaller. Spinal input therefore does not dominate this peduncle.'),
('235a67a1-25a2-5749-a819-e0b2a5a674e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The middle cerebellar peduncle is entirely', '["Afferent from medulla","Efferent","Both","Neither","Afferent (pontocerebellar)"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent (pontocerebellar)', 'The middle cerebellar peduncle is composed wholly of afferent pontocerebellar fibers arising from the contralateral pontine nuclei. It contains no efferent projections leaving the cerebellum.'),
('39127719-b1af-5361-8d31-60b203f74f0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar efferents to the thalamus arise from which nuclei?', '["Only globose resulting in decreased functional activity","Only dentate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dentate and interposed (globose/emboliform)","All deep nuclei equally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fastigial only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate and interposed (globose/emboliform)', 'Thalamic projections (via the superior cerebellar peduncle) come chiefly from the dentate and interposed nuclei through the superior cerebellar peduncle, terminating in the ventrolateral and ventroanterior thalamus. The fastigial nucleus targets mainly vestibular and reticular structures rather than the thalamus.'),
('148ee3e6-e4e3-5eb7-9730-dc821146d539'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar efferents to the brainstem arise from', '["All nuclei equally resulting in decreased functional activity","Dentate only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar cortex directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Interposed only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fastigial nucleus (mainly via uncinate fasciculus)"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial nucleus (mainly via uncinate fasciculus)', 'The fastigial nucleus sends the bulk of its output to brainstem vestibular and reticular nuclei, crossing within the cerebellum and running in the uncinate fasciculus. The cerebellar cortex cannot project directly, and the dentate nucleus mainly targets the thalamus rather than the brainstem.'),
('73bcdb82-a05f-5fec-80b8-54330d0a6ef1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The vestibulocerebellum (flocculonodular) projects mainly to', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vestibular nuclei (lateral and medial)","Red nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep cerebellar nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nuclei (lateral and medial)', 'The flocculonodular lobe projects to the lateral and medial vestibular nuclei, which then influence extraocular motor neurons and axial musculature to maintain balance and eye stabilization. Its targets are the vestibular nuclei, not the deep cerebellar nuclei or the red nucleus.'),
('f6ad3ea8-b510-5dbe-bb5a-da0734be8bb7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Purkinje cells in flocculonodular lobe project to', '["Spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Red nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular nuclei (directly, not to deep nuclei)","Deep cerebellar nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nuclei (directly, not to deep nuclei)', 'Unlike Purkinje cells elsewhere, those of the flocculonodular lobe inhibit the vestibular nuclei directly, bypassing the deep cerebellar nuclei. The other listed targets receive neither flocculonodular cortical projections nor direct Purkinje input.'),
('28c46dce-fe35-5db0-bccd-a67dcb3c9377'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar projections to spinal cord are indirect via', '["Corticospinal tract resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct cerebellospinal tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, and rubrospinal tracts","Tectospinal tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","spinal projections leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, and rubrospinal tracts', 'The cerebellum exerts its spinal effects by modulating the vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, and rubrospinal descending tracts; there is no direct cerebellospinal pathway. The corticospinal and tectospinal tracts are not the routes of cerebellar influence over the cord.'),
('6e21059c-8ffe-5a73-acdf-93a45f2ef86a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The fastigial projection to the reticular formation is via', '["Uncinate fasciculus (hook bundle) in ICP","MCP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar commissure mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct cerebellar-reticular fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SCP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uncinate fasciculus (hook bundle) in ICP', 'Fastigial efferents to the reticular formation cross within the cerebellum and travel in the uncinate fasciculus of the inferior cerebellar peduncle. The MCP is afferent, and the SCP is not the route of this fastigioreticular projection.'),
('c75ffa44-b75a-5a87-8870-123b11fb1141'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The fastigial projection to the thalamus is via', '["Uncinate fasciculus (some fibers ascend to intralaminar nuclei)","MCP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ICP mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar commissure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SCP (main route) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uncinate fasciculus (some fibers ascend to intralaminar nuclei)', 'Fastigial fibers to the thalamus ascend in the uncinate fasciculus, with a few reaching the intralaminar nuclei, whereas the SCP carries the bulk of dentate and interposed thalamic projections. The MCP and the cerebellar commissure do not convey these fibers.'),
('d18b2059-4478-50bd-8f95-8f74e43268ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar symptoms in multiple sclerosis are due to', '["Demyelination of cerebellar peduncles or cerebellar white matter","Gray matter loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral nerve demyelination mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic nerve only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Demyelination of cerebellar peduncles or cerebellar white matter', 'MS plaques typically demyelinate the cerebellar peduncles and cerebellar white matter, interrupting afferent and efferent pathways and producing ataxia and dysarthria. The condition spares cerebellar gray matter, and peripheral nerve or optic nerve demyelination alone does not produce cerebellar signs.'),
('7c18563c-89a0-5ec1-9232-7cdee22e05a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar stroke in SCA territory causes', '["Contralateral symptoms resulting in decreased functional activity","symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nystagmus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vertigo without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipsilateral limb ataxia, dysarthria, and ipsilateral Horner syndrome if brainstem involved"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral limb ataxia, dysarthria, and ipsilateral Horner syndrome if brainstem involved', 'Superior cerebellar artery occlusion infarcts the superior cerebellum, giving ipsilateral limb ataxia and dysarthria, with Horner syndrome when the superior brainstem is involved. Cerebellar signs are ipsilateral, not contralateral, and are not limited to nystagmus or vertigo alone.'),
('97e33713-990d-550b-935f-983479ecd067'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar stroke in AICA territory causes', '["Only nystagmus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only vertigo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cerebellar ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation","Ipsilateral deafness, facial numbness, ataxia, Horner, and contralateral pain/temp loss (lateral pontine)","Only hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral deafness, facial numbness, ataxia, Horner, and contralateral pain/temp loss (lateral pontine)', 'Anterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion infarcts the lateral pons and flocculus, producing ipsilateral deafness and facial numbness (CN VIII and V nuclei), ataxia, Horner syndrome, and contralateral pain/temperature loss. Hearing loss alone or isolated nystagmus fails to capture this lateral pontine syndrome.'),
('df2073c8-7a82-5c2b-b8a3-57348d6c7a21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar stroke in PICA territory causes', '["Only vertigo resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only medullary signs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cerebellar ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only nystagmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) + cerebellar signs: ipsilateral ataxia, vertigo, dysphagia, Horner, contralateral pain/temp loss"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) + cerebellar signs: ipsilateral ataxia, vertigo, dysphagia, Horner, contralateral pain/temp loss', 'Posterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion produces Wallenberg (lateral medullary) syndrome with ipsilateral ataxia and vertigo, dysphagia, Horner syndrome, and contralateral pain/temperature loss. Constraining the picture to isolated vertigo, ataxia, or nystagmus omits the medullary deficits.'),
('c6792e5c-f662-5ff6-84e5-bbca16cebc5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Acute cerebellar edema (posterior fossa swelling) may cause', '["Aphasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstructive hydrocephalus (fourth ventricle compression)","Hemiplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","Seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coma without hydrocephalus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive hydrocephalus (fourth ventricle compression)', 'Posterior fossa swelling compresses the fourth ventricle, blocking CSF outflow and producing obstructive hydrocephalus with raised intracranial pressure. Aphasia, hemiplegia, and seizures reflect supratentorial pathology rather than mid-posterior fossa compression.'),
('8c6dd9e3-1c6d-5b9a-ac85-153b02a48471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Chronic alcoholism with cerebellar degeneration shows atrophy of', '["Cerebellar hemispheres resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior vermis (superior vermis atrophy on imaging)","Dentate nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Flocculonodular lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inferior olive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior vermis (superior vermis atrophy on imaging)', 'Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration characteristically atrophies the anterior (superior) vermis, giving truncal ataxia in chronic alcoholics. The hemispheres, dentate nucleus, flocculonodular lobe, and inferior olive are not the selective sites of this degeneration.'),
('8d045d06-7775-5757-9b71-f55e9d82e413'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar agenesis (extremely rare) may present with', '["Coma resulting in decreased functional activity","survival accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Severe quadriplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","Respiratory failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surprisingly mild motor deficits (compensation by other systems)"]'::jsonb, 'Surprisingly mild motor deficits (compensation by other systems)', 'Complete congenital cerebellar absence often causes remarkably modest motor deficits because cerebral and other systems compensate; some cases are compatible with near-normal life. Coma and quadriplegia reflect far more extensive pathology than cerebellar agenesis alone.'),
('e955016e-3f6f-578d-a2d8-ee8f6a8710a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar inhibition of vestibular responses is important for', '["Hearing resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual acuity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nystagmus generation mediated by specific receptor activation","Balance only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VOR adaptation (changing gain of VOR)"]'::jsonb, 'VOR adaptation (changing gain of VOR)', 'The cerebellum, especially the flocculus and nodulus, adaptively adjusts the gain of the vestibulo-ocular reflex as visual demands change. Hearing and visual acuity are unrelated, and nystagmus generation is largely a brainstem vestibular reflex rather than a cerebellar inhibition product.'),
('4beddb39-9ad6-5bfa-8552-58e16a5b23e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellar flocculus is responsible for', '["Fixation resulting in decreased functional activity","Reflex eye movements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Saccades mediated by specific receptor activation","Balance only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VOR adaptation (gain control) and smooth pursuit"]'::jsonb, 'VOR adaptation (gain control) and smooth pursuit', 'The flocculus regulates VOR gain control and generates smooth pursuit eye movements, adjusting the vestibulo-ocular reflex to changing needs. Saccades depend on the vermis and fastigial nucleus, while reflex eye movements and fixation do not define the floccular role.'),
('29dec350-2486-5066-ab76-b5c3dd2f3acb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Loss of floccular function causes', '["Impaired VOR cancellation (can''t suppress VOR during combined head-eye tracking)","Nystagmus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of all eye movements mediated by specific receptor activation","Ptosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diplopia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired VOR cancellation (can''t suppress VOR during combined head-eye tracking)', 'With floccular loss the vestibulo-ocular reflex cannot be suppressed, so smooth pursuit fails while tracking a target during head movement (impaired VOR cancellation). Total loss of eye movements, ptosis, or diplopia alone are not consequences of isolated floccular dysfunction.'),
('3afc10df-f3ff-5026-b94f-8ed3a0311599'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar (vestibulocerebellum) contribution to posture and gait involves', '["Only dentate resulting in decreased functional activity","Fastigial nucleus → lateral vestibulospinal tract and reticulospinal tract","Only vermis mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct spinal projections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only flocculus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial nucleus → lateral vestibulospinal tract and reticulospinal tract', 'Posture and gait control flows from the vestibulocerebellum through the fastigial nucleus into the lateral vestibulospinal and reticulospinal tracts, which stabilize axial and proximal muscles. The dentate nucleus serves limb coordination, and the cerebellum has no direct spinal projection.'),
('14289593-9f2b-5c4f-a9c1-896a5f222cfc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum plays a role in predicting sensory consequences of movement via', '["Efference copy (corollary discharge) from motor cortex","Autonomic reflexes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","prediction role mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct sensory input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal reflexes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Efference copy (corollary discharge) from motor cortex', 'The cerebellum receives an efference copy (corollary discharge) of motor commands, allowing it to predict the sensory consequences of movement and compare them with actual feedback. Autonomic reflexes, direct sensory input, and spinal reflexes do not provide this predictive motor signal.'),
('e9a7ef93-1794-5346-9c73-b8d6d04feec1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar prediction error (between expected and actual sensory feedback) is signaled by', '["Parallel fibers","Granule cells","Purkinje cells","Mossy fibers from spinal cord","Climbing fibers from inferior olive"]'::jsonb, 'Climbing fibers from inferior olive', 'Climbing fibers from the inferior olive fire complex spikes that signal mismatch between expected and actual sensory feedback, driving motor learning in Purkinje cells. Mossy and parallel fiber systems carry the ongoing movement-related information rather than the error signal itself.'),
('995c4ec9-c574-53b0-b00d-36d1162827df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar feedback circuits can be altered in dystonia suggesting', '["Cerebellar dysfunction contributes to dystonia pathogenesis","Only spinal cord involved accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only basal ganglia involved mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cortex involved without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar dysfunction contributes to dystonia pathogenesis', 'Demonstrated changes in cerebellar feedback to motor pathways in dystonia indicate that cerebellar dysfunction participates in generating the disorder, complementing the traditional basal ganglia view. Restricting the cause to the spinal cord, basal ganglia, or cortex alone contradicts these findings.'),
('0497f135-4d71-502c-844f-807556ae5bc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar dysfunction may contribute to autism spectrum disorder via', '["role resulting in decreased functional activity","Only social factors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Genetic factors only mediated by specific receptor activation","Environmental only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Disrupted cerebellar development and connectivity"]'::jsonb, 'Disrupted cerebellar development and connectivity', 'Autism is associated with disrupted cerebellar development and altered cortico-cerebellar connectivity, affecting motor and cognitive integration. Social, genetic, and environmental factors alone do not account for the structural and functional cerebellar findings in ASD.'),
('4b7fac99-6bdf-5340-97ae-192a20142cfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) has been studied for', '["Cerebellar ataxia, essential tremor, stroke rehabilitation","Only pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only depression mediated by specific receptor activation","Only tremor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia, essential tremor, stroke rehabilitation', 'Cerebellar TMS has been investigated as a treatment across cerebellar ataxia, essential tremor, and post-stroke motor rehabilitation, modulating cerebello-cortical circuits. Its studied indications are not confined to pain, depression, tremor, or ataxia alone.'),
('b3bc7c7b-2d4a-5ec5-b5d2-1857b9f1ab48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum receives descending input from the cerebral cortex via', '["Corticopontine fibers (to pontine nuclei → MCP)","Direct corticocerebellar fibers","Corticospinal tract collaterals","Corticothalamic fibers","Corticorubral fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Corticopontine fibers (to pontine nuclei → MCP)', 'Cerebral cortical input reaches the cerebellum through corticopontine fibers synapsing on pontine nuclei, whose axons cross to the contralateral middle cerebellar peduncle. There is no direct corticocerebellar pathway, and corticospinal, corticothalamic, and corticorubral fibers do not provide this input.'),
('278bf3ba-36a7-503b-8d7b-5b76bcb758cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Corticopontine fibers arise from which cortical areas?', '["Only motor cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only frontal mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital (especially motor, premotor, sensory, visual)","Only occipital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital (especially motor, premotor, sensory, visual)', 'Corticopontine fibers originate widely from frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital association and motor areas, allowing diverse cerebral signals to reach the cerebellum. Restricting the origin to one lobe or to motor cortex alone ignores this broad distribution.'),
('4b06411e-033a-527d-9199-d19e0d91fd9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Frontopontine fibers are located in which part of crus cerebri?', '["Middle 3/5","Medial 1/5","All parts","Lateral 1/5","Anterior 1/5"]'::jsonb, 'Medial 1/5', 'In the crus cerebri, frontopontine fibers run in the medial one-fifth (with the corticonuclear tract nearby). The middle three-fifths carry corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers, and the lateral one-fifth holds the temporopontine and parietopontine fibers of the Türck bundle.'),
('8473e9c0-4d38-5512-9f3d-11616f1eb0b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Türck bundle (lateral 1/5 of crus cerebri) contains', '["Corticospinal fibers","Thalamic fibers","Corticobulbar fibers","Temporopontine and parietopontine fibers","Frontopontine fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Temporopontine and parietopontine fibers', 'The Türck bundle occupies the lateral one-fifth of the crus cerebri and conveys temporopontine and parietopontine fibers to the pontine nuclei. Corticospinal, corticobulbar, and frontopontine fibers are located in other parts of the crus, not in this bundle.'),
('437df222-e558-5e4f-a964-40d525130319'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The middle 3/5 of crus cerebri contains', '["Temporopontine fibers","Corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers","Cerebellar fibers","Frontopontine fibers","Sensory fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal and corticobulbar fibers', 'The middle three-fifths of the crus cerebri contains the corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. Frontopontine fibers lie medially (medial 1/5) and the temporopontine/parietopontine Türck bundle lies laterally (lateral 1/5), while no cerebellar or sensory tracts travel here.'),
('8962dff0-c059-509f-a899-9360f6690f3d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar damage from tumors is common in children due to', '["Astrocytoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Glioblastoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meningioma mediated by specific receptor activation","Ependymoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulloblastoma (vermal tumor)"]'::jsonb, 'Medulloblastoma (vermal tumor)', 'Medulloblastoma, a highly malignant embryonal tumor of the vermis, is the most common malignant childhood brain tumor and frequently damages the cerebellum. Glioblastoma and meningioma are far rarer in children, while the midline vermal location is characteristic of medulloblastoma rather than ependymoma.'),
('e611b4e7-8a4a-5b8f-a7b9-e05d35abfbc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Medulloblastoma arises from', '["Purkinje cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep cerebellar nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meninges mediated by specific receptor activation","White matter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Granule cell precursors (rhombic lip)"]'::jsonb, 'Granule cell precursors (rhombic lip)', 'Medulloblastoma derives from granule cell precursor cells of the rhombic lip that fail to differentiate, explaining its vermal location. It does not arise from Purkinje cells, deep nuclei, meninges, or white matter.'),
('16dbe2d5-c6a0-539e-ab13-89b0cca5d473'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar astrocytoma is typically', '["Vascular resulting in decreased functional activity","Benign (pilocytic) and cystic","Malignant mediated by specific receptor activation","Infectious without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metastatic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign (pilocytic) and cystic', 'Juvenile pilocytic astrocytoma of the cerebellum is a well-circumscribed, cystic, and relatively benign WHO grade I tumor with excellent prognosis. It is not a vascular, malignant, infectious, or metastatic lesion.'),
('f67a25b5-791b-59c7-a9ef-141351a5cd9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Hemangioblastoma of cerebellum is associated with', '["Sturge-Weber","Cowden syndrome","Neurofibromatosis","Von Hippel-Lindau disease","Tuberous sclerosis"]'::jsonb, 'Von Hippel-Lindau disease', 'Cerebellar hemangioblastoma is a hallmark of von Hippel-Lindau disease, an autosomal dominant tumor syndrome caused by VHL gene mutations. Sturge-Weber, Cowden syndrome, neurofibromatosis, and tuberous sclerosis are not the associated conditions.'),
('4a28f1e7-ccae-5af4-b6b6-c57672a45bdd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar cavernous malformation can cause', '["malignant resulting in decreased functional activity","Only incidental finding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only headache mediated by specific receptor activation","symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemorrhage, seizures (if supratentorial), and focal deficits"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhage, seizures (if supratentorial), and focal deficits', 'Cerebellar cavernous malformations present with hemorrhage and localizing signs and can cause seizures when supratentorial membranes are involved. They are benign vascular lesions, so considering them merely incidental or headache-only overlooks their bleeding and seizure risk.'),
('40050213-81cb-59b9-96e7-4ff74f12c0c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Which structure is NOT part of the cerebellum?', '["Nodulus","Vermis","Tonsil","Flocculus","Claustrum"]'::jsonb, 'Claustrum', 'The claustrum is a thin sheet of grey matter lying between the insular cortex and putamen, unrelated to the cerebellum. The nodulus, vermis, tonsil, and flocculus are all genuine cerebellar subdivisions.'),
('5426cab4-d794-5594-8fd3-b4558ca42e2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The anterior lobe of cerebellum is most developed in', '["Fish resulting in decreased functional activity","Reptiles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower animals (paleocerebellum dominant)","Humans (neocerebellum dominant) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Birds leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower animals (paleocerebellum dominant)', 'The anterior lobe is the paleocerebellum, which is phylogenetically oldest and dominates in lower animals, subserving spinocerebellar and proprioceptive coordination. Humans show the greatest expansion of the neocerebellum (posterior lobe).'),
('440dd7c2-0a46-507b-929a-e22e93fad72d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The posterior lobe (neocerebellum) is largest in', '["Humans and primates","Rodents","Fish","Birds","Reptiles"]'::jsonb, 'Humans and primates', 'The neocerebellum (posterior lobe) is the phylogenetically newest part and reaches its greatest development in humans and higher primates, growing in parallel with the cerebral hemispheres. Lower vertebrates show a relatively small posterior lobe.'),
('960a1e87-12dd-5ddd-b0fa-81012804fb88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The number of Purkinje cells in human cerebellum is approximately', '["1 million","1 billion","15 million","15 billion","100,000"]'::jsonb, '15 million', 'The human cerebellum contains roughly 15 million Purkinje cells, one per climbing fiber input. Values of 100,000 or 1 million underestimate the actual count, while 15 billion is far too high.'),
('9ee4a77b-61f1-528d-8ab4-cd75077ef0fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The total number of neurons in the cerebellum is about', '["1 billion resulting in decreased functional activity","69 billion (more than entire cerebral cortex)","1 million mediated by specific receptor activation","100 billion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","10 million leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '69 billion (more than entire cerebral cortex)', 'The cerebellum houses about 69 billion neurons, exceeding the total in the entire cerebral cortex, the great majority being granule cells. Tens of millions or single billions greatly understate the true number.'),
('d7fc04b4-b2a8-557c-88a6-bafdda69be20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The climbing fiber to Purkinje cell ratio is', '["100:1 resulting in decreased functional activity","1:10 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1:100 mediated by specific receptor activation","1:1 (each Purkinje receives one climbing fiber)","10:1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1:1 (each Purkinje receives one climbing fiber)', 'Each Purkinje cell receives a single climbing fiber originating from the inferior olive, giving a 1:1 ratio. In contrast, many parallel fibers converge onto each Purkinje cell.'),
('5e2bb04a-7ee7-5822-b8d3-eb57b0891945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The parallel fiber to Purkinje cell ratio is', '["10:1 resulting in decreased functional activity","1,000:1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","200,000:1 (each Purkinje receives many parallel fibers)","1:200,000 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1:1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '200,000:1 (each Purkinje receives many parallel fibers)', 'Each Purkinje cell receives roughly 200,000 parallel-fiber synapses from granule cell axons, illustrating massive convergence. The climbing fiber system in contrast is strictly 1:1.'),
('4e3a32e9-3b19-5c74-a4b4-82564e36a024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Mossy fiber to granule cell ratio is', '["1:1 resulting in decreased functional activity","1:1,000 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1:100 mediated by specific receptor activation","1 mossy fiber activates 500 granule cells","1:10 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1 mossy fiber activates 500 granule cells', 'A single mossy fiber rosette forms synapses with the dendrites of roughly 500 granule cells, allowing wide divergence of afferent signals. This divergence underlies the large number of granule cells in the cerebellum.'),
('2b477a03-1e67-5222-b376-7ff4c605dc3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar glomeruli are located in the molecular layer of the cerebellum', '["True (mossy fiber rosettes synapse with granule cell dendrites in granular layer, not molecular layer)","Only in hemispheres","Only in vermis","True","False"]'::jsonb, 'False', 'Cerebellar glomeruli, where mossy fiber rosettes synapse with granule cell dendrites, lie in the granular layer, not the molecular layer. The statement is therefore false.'),
('4c7a6824-a48e-58da-bf65-b7187c5b4c4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Golgi cell dendrites are located in', '["Purkinje cell layer resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Molecular layer (receiving parallel fiber input)","Granular layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","White matter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular layer (receiving parallel fiber input)', 'Golgi cell dendrites ascend into the molecular layer, where they receive excitatory input from parallel fibers. Their cell bodies sit in the granular layer, where they inhibit granule cells.'),
('f67b7281-9c4b-57d9-883d-6fe0d39295be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Granule cell axons (parallel fibers) form', '["Excitatory synapses on Purkinje dendrites (molecular layer)","Mixed synapses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gap junctions mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibitory synapses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","synapses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excitatory synapses on Purkinje dendrites (molecular layer)', 'Granule cells are excitatory, and their parallel-fiber axons form glutamatergic synapses on Purkinje cell dendrites within the molecular layer. Purkinje cells themselves are inhibitory on the deep nuclei.'),
('7397630b-3df1-5b68-ab3c-8cc84ea99789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The parallel fibers are perpendicular to', '["Purkinje dendritic trees (transverse orientation)","Granule cell axons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Climbing fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Longitudinal axis of folium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mossy fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje dendritic trees (transverse orientation)', 'Parallel fibers run along the long axis of each folium and cross perpendicular to the broad, sagittally oriented dendritic trees (transverse orientation) of Purkinje cells. This arrangement maximizes the intersecting synapse surface.'),
('f67a04ca-1b2c-5c6a-a6f9-e1bf4ef30de1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Purkinje cell dendrites are highly branched in a single plane perpendicular to', '["Deep nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Climbing fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mossy fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Parallel fibers (parallel fibers cross perpendicular)","Granule cell bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel fibers (parallel fibers cross perpendicular)', 'Each Purkinje cell spreads its dendritic arbor in a single sagittal plane that is oriented perpendicular to the parallel fibers running transversely along the folium. A single parallel fiber therefore contacts many Purkinje cells.'),
('c8b28478-7b5e-5669-9e91-e31be262751e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar lobules I-V form the', '["Posterior lobe","Flocculonodular lobe","Anterior lobe","Hemispheres only","Vermis only"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior lobe', 'In Larsell nomenclature the anterior lobe comprises lobules I through V. Lobules VI to IX form the posterior lobe, and lobule X is the flocculonodular lobe.'),
('4265263c-d98d-58dd-9f39-9f2d894375cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Lobule X (Larsell) corresponds to', '["Anterior lobe","Pyramis","Flocculonodular lobe","Tonsil","Posterior superior lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Flocculonodular lobe', 'Lobule X is the nodulus, which together with the flocculi forms the flocculonodular lobe (archicerebellum), concerned with vestibular function and equilibrium.'),
('32a74fd4-1fa7-5464-b219-0183841f62c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The primary fissure in Larsell nomenclature separates', '["Lobule III from IV","Lobule V from VI","Lobule VII from VIII","Lobule I from II","Lobule X from IX"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule V from VI', 'The primary fissure separates the anterior and posterior lobes and, in Larsell terminology, divides lobule V from lobule VI. The horizontal fissure separates lobule VII from VIII.'),
('ddf7729d-4138-5e87-aa50-fd918495a446'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellar tonsil (Larsell) corresponds to which lobule?', '["Lobule VIII resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobule VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lobule VI mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobule X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobule IX (biventral lobule + tonsil)"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule IX (biventral lobule + tonsil)', 'The tonsil is part of lobule IX, which also includes the biventral lobule. Because the tonsil lies adjacent to the foramen magnum, it is the site of tonsillar herniation.'),
('7f823ed4-4dd0-5a7d-a0a3-d790e2b50407'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The ansiform lobule (Larsell) corresponds to which lobules?', '["Lobule VIII resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobule X accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crus I = lobule VIIA; Crus II = lobule VIIB","Lobule IX without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobule VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crus I = lobule VIIA; Crus II = lobule VIIB', 'The ansiform lobule comprises crus I and crus II, which correspond to lobule VIIA and VIIB respectively in the Larsell scheme. These hemispheral parts belong to the neocerebellum.'),
('fa221098-3723-5a95-95ba-8d29f20fe1c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The paramedian lobule (Larsell) is part of', '["Lobule VIIB (between ansiform and biventral)","Lobule VIII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lobule VI mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobule V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobule IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule VIIB (between ansiform and biventral)', 'The paramedian lobule forms part of lobule VIIB, lying between the ansiform lobule and the biventral lobule. The biventral lobule is lobule VIII, and lobule IX contains the tonsil.'),
('10e2dc63-d6f0-5a5f-93b4-43db8c2d05db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The biventral lobule (Larsell) corresponds to', '["Lobule X","Lobule VIII","Lobule V","Lobule IX","Lobule VIIA"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule VIII', 'The biventral lobule corresponds to lobule VIII in the Larsell scheme. Lobule IX contains the tonsil, and lobule X is the flocculonodular lobe''s nodulus.'),
('6c91fa30-b107-52db-a9b1-cf1aaf9014bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The gracile lobule (Larsell) is part of', '["Lobule VIII resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobule IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lobule V mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobule VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobule VIIB (between paramedian and biventral)"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule VIIB (between paramedian and biventral)', 'The gracile lobule is included in lobule VIIB, situated between the paramedian lobule and the biventral lobule. Lobule VIII is the biventral lobule and lobule VI contains the superior semilunar region.'),
('0c60776d-3cc4-5bce-ab25-6f0e9b45969b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The inferior semilunar lobule (Larsell) corresponds to', '["Lobule IX","Lobule V","Lobule VIII","Lobule VI","Lobule VII (Crus II)"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule VII (Crus II)', 'The inferior semilunar lobule is crus II, part of lobule VII. The superior semilunar lobule is crus I, together forming the ansiform lobule.'),
('c5eafa31-3b45-514e-a314-6cece45124b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The superior semilunar lobule corresponds to', '["Lobule VIII resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobule IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lobule V mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobule VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crus I (lobule VIIA) on superior surface"]'::jsonb, 'Crus I (lobule VIIA) on superior surface', 'The superior semilunar lobule is crus I of lobule VIIA, located on the superior surface, whereas the inferior semilunar lobule (crus II) is its counterpart on the inferior surface.'),
('5d5a8542-5c88-5fc0-ac75-1df2deb9b65b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The declive and folium vermis (Larsell) correspond to', '["Lobule VII","Lobule IX","Lobule VI of vermis","Lobule VIII","Lobule V"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule VI of vermis', 'The declive and folium of the vermis together correspond to lobule VI of the vermis. Lobule VII contains the tuber, and lobule VIII the pyramis.'),
('88b9ce50-a359-5be3-8138-537c79f788a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The tuber and pyramis (Larsell) correspond to', '["Lobule IX and X","Lobule I and II","Lobule VII and VIII of vermis","Lobule V and VI","Lobule III and IV"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule VII and VIII of vermis', 'The tuber is lobule VII of the vermis and the pyramis is lobule VIII of the vermis. The declive and folium occupy lobule VI, while the uvula is lobule IX.'),
('51ea25c5-0a41-573c-b19d-b587e762437e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The culmen and central lobule (Larsell) correspond to', '["Lobule IV and V (anterior lobe vermis)","Lobule VI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lobule I-II mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobule IX without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobule VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule IV and V (anterior lobe vermis)', 'The culmen is lobule V and the central lobule is lobule IV, both within the vermis of the anterior lobe. The lingula is lobule I, and lobule VI onwards lie in the posterior lobe.'),
('ea80e33d-9165-542c-81e9-48d682b4ac33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The lingula (Larsell) corresponds to', '["Lobule IX resulting in decreased functional activity","Lobule X accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lobule I (anterior lobe vermis)","Lobule VII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lobule VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule I (anterior lobe vermis)', 'The lingula is lobule I, the most rostral part of the anterior lobe vermis. Lobule X is the nodulus and lobules IV-V are the central lobule and culmen.'),
('f6349b9b-b770-58e8-a95c-73310d070fde'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Inferior olivary neurons that project as climbing fibers are', '["Serotonergic","Cholinergic","GABAergic","Glutamatergic (excitatory)","Dopaminergic"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamatergic (excitatory)', 'Inferior olivary neurons give rise to climbing fibers that release glutamate and are excitatory on Purkinje cells. They do not use GABA, serotonin, acetylcholine, or dopamine as their transmitter.'),
('51aa4532-c766-5524-bfef-5cad986874c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Electrical coupling exists between inferior olivary neurons via', '["coupling","Desmosomes","Chemical synapses","Gap junctions (dendritic)","Tight junctions"]'::jsonb, 'Gap junctions (dendritic)', 'Inferior olivary neurons are connected by dendritic gap junctions that permit electrotonic coupling, allowing the neurons to fire in synchronous oscillatory patterns. Desmosomes and tight junctions do not mediate electrical signaling.'),
('bd5f0efc-9cf7-50ee-b965-6c01e8ea22c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Inferior olivary oscillations are thought to be important for', '["Timing (motor coordination, learning)","Hearing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Balance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Timing (motor coordination, learning)', 'The synchronized oscillations of inferior olivary neurons are believed to provide a timing signal essential for coordinated movement and motor learning. They do not primarily serve hearing, pain, balance, or vision.'),
('0bac95a3-0d60-527b-bcfe-be3e33f579fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum receives trigeminal sensory input via', '["Medial lemniscus","Direct trigeminal nerve","Corticospinal tract","Spinocerebellar and trigeminocerebellar pathways","CN V directly to cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar and trigeminocerebellar pathways', 'Orofacial proprioceptive and sensory information reaches the cerebellum through spinocerebellar and trigeminocerebellar pathways. The trigeminal nerve does not project directly to the cerebellum, and the medial lemniscus is not its route.'),
('90bf16a1-e4da-5cf8-82a6-f1c308f7bb06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The medial cerebellar nucleus in animals is homologous to', '["Vestibular nucleus","Dentate nucleus","Red nucleus","Fastigial nucleus in humans","Interposed nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial nucleus in humans', 'The medial cerebellar nucleus seen in animals is evolutionarily homologous to the human fastigial nucleus, both being the most medial deep nuclei. The interposed and dentate nuclei correspond to the more lateral animal nuclei.'),
('31a9ab72-9941-56b3-acec-cbe4857c3cb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar abiotrophy (cerebellar cortical atrophy) is a', '["Tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vascular malformation mediated by specific receptor activation","Degenerative condition in animals (horses, dogs) and humans","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Degenerative condition in animals (horses, dogs) and humans', 'Cerebellar abiotrophy is a premature, progressive degeneration of cerebellar cortical neurons seen in certain animals such as Arabian horses and dogs, with analogous cerebellar degeneration in humans. It is not a tumor, infection, vascular lesion, or trauma.'),
('09ee22bc-f98b-573e-adda-c4d21578a219'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Paraneoplastic anti-Tr antibody is associated with', '["Ovarian cancer resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colon cancer mediated by specific receptor activation","Breast cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hodgkin lymphoma and cerebellar ataxia"]'::jsonb, 'Hodgkin lymphoma and cerebellar ataxia', 'Anti-Tr antibodies are a paraneoplastic marker strongly tied to Hodgkin lymphoma and typically produce subacute cerebellar ataxia. Anti-Yo associates with ovarian and breast cancer, while anti-Hu associates with small-cell lung cancer.'),
('f621102e-391a-56e5-a515-14e56b8997d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Paraneoplastic anti-Ma2 antibody causes', '["Limbic encephalitis and brainstem/cerebellar signs","Only peripheral neuropathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only myasthenia mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cerebellar ataxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Lambert-Eaton leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limbic encephalitis and brainstem/cerebellar signs', 'Anti-Ma2 antibodies are associated with limbic encephalitis together with brainstem and cerebellar signs, often in the setting of testicular germ-cell tumors. They are not restricted to peripheral neuropathy, myasthenia, or Labert-Eaton syndrome.'),
('e7ed3bdd-6322-577a-9290-67faf7a4d07a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar stroke syndromes account for what percentage of all strokes?', '["50%","About 2-3%","10%","30%","20%"]'::jsonb, 'About 2-3%', 'Cerebellar infarcts and hemorrhages make up roughly 2-3% of all strokes. Despite their relative rarity they are important because swelling can cause life-threatening brainstem compression and hydrocephalus.'),
('57c8db63-02db-5dde-89f2-4ef4356ef83a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Therapeutic options for cerebellar hemorrhage include', '["treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgical evacuation (if >3 cm and deteriorating)","Radiation only mediated by specific receptor activation","Medical management only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chemotherapy only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical evacuation (if >3 cm and deteriorating)', 'Large (>3 cm) cerebellar hemorrhages with neurologic deterioration are treated by surgical evacuation to relieve brainstem compression and hydrocephalus. Smaller, stable hemorrhages may be managed conservatively with observation.'),
('7e7cab28-6bac-5294-9465-5a2649b1dc6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar ischemia with impending herniation may be treated with', '["Steroids only resulting in decreased functional activity","Suboccipital decompressive craniectomy","TPA only mediated by specific receptor activation","Heparin only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antibiotics only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suboccipital decompressive craniectomy', 'Massive cerebellar infarction that threatens upward and downward herniation is treated with suboccipital decompressive craniectomy to allow edematous tissue to expand without compressing the brainstem and fourth ventricle.'),
('0eae0403-c2bc-5e08-b789-8bcb89edfcd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum plays a role in which form of learning?', '["Semantic memory resulting in decreased functional activity","Classical conditioning (eyeblink conditioning)","Declarative memory mediated by specific receptor activation","Episodic memory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Working memory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Classical conditioning (eyeblink conditioning)', 'The cerebellum is critical for motor learning such as classical conditioning of the eyeblink reflex, linking conditioned and unconditioned stimuli. Declarative, semantic, and episodic memories depend on the medial temporal lobe rather than the cerebellum.'),
('fb402148-0934-58a1-92b8-ed5f890388a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Eyeblink conditioning involves which cerebellar region?', '["Fastigial only resulting in decreased functional activity","Vermis only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dentate nucleus only mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobule VI (ansiform) and interposed nucleus (anterior interpositus)","Flocculonodular lobe only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lobule VI (ansiform) and interposed nucleus (anterior interpositus)', 'Eyeblink conditioning depends on the interpositus (emboliform, anterior interposed) deep nucleus together with lobule VI of the anterior lobe/ansiform cortex, whose Purkinje cells modulate the conditioned learning. The flocculonodular lobe and dentate are not the primary sites.'),
('0f323c8c-3ab5-5baa-839e-0bac6c8afe03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar vermis is also involved in', '["Only eye movements resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emotion and autonomic regulation (fastigial → hypothalamic/brainstem)","Only speech without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only balance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion and autonomic regulation (fastigial → hypothalamic/brainstem)', 'Beyond motor and balance function, the vermis and its fastigial nucleus project to the hypothalamus and brainstem, participating in emotional and autonomic regulation. This broader role is not limited to eye movements, speech, or balance.'),
('f69962bc-d80d-5721-a6ce-d167e520cbd8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The vermis is part of the', '["Pontocerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinocerebellum (receives spinal and cortical input)","Cerebrocerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Only axial coordination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vestibulocerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellum (receives spinal and cortical input)', 'The vermis and intermediate hemispheres form the spinocerebellum, which receives spinocerebellar and some cortical input and projects chiefly to the fastigial and interposed nuclei. The vestibulocerebellum and pontocerebellum are separate functional divisions.'),
('a57b5430-3da7-5109-92be-99cb7f6f40eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar influence on the hypothalamus is via', '["connection","Fastigial nucleus → brainstem → hypothalamus","Cerebellar cortex → hypothalamus","Direct cerebellohypothalamic","Dentate → thalamus → hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Fastigial nucleus → brainstem → hypothalamus', 'Cerebellar influence on the hypothalamus is relayed through the fastigial nucleus, which sends fibers to brainstem nuclei that in turn project to the hypothalamus. There is no direct significant cerebello-hypothalamic or dentate-to-hypothalamic route.'),
('cd42ee90-d20c-50b8-97b9-f6d7126f1b62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum has connections with the basal ganglia via', '["Cerebellospinal resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct connections accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multisynaptic loops through thalamus and cortex","Cerebellostriatal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellopallidal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multisynaptic loops through thalamus and cortex', 'Cerebellar and basal ganglionic circuits communicate indirectly through multisynaptic loops involving the thalamus and cerebral cortex, rather than by direct cerebello-striatal or cerebello-pallidal fibers.'),
('8dc4f63a-d9af-50cb-a2d7-e151508930c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar stroke can present with', '["Slowly progressive ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Intermittent symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hearing loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Only headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sudden vertigo, vomiting, and inability to stand"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden vertigo, vomiting, and inability to stand', 'Because it comes on abruptly, cerebellar stroke classically presents with sudden vertigo, vomiting, and inability to stand or walk, resembling an acute vestibular syndrome. Slowly progressive ataxia indicates a chronic or degenerative process rather than stroke.'),
('e8c9cdc8-e8c9-5ba9-8b97-1a1c7e55d7e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The Dandy-Walker malformation is associated with', '["Hydrocephalus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Anencephaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chiari malformation mediated by specific receptor activation","Spina bifida without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absent/hypoplastic cerebellar vermis, cystic fourth ventricle, enlarged posterior fossa"]'::jsonb, 'Absent/hypoplastic cerebellar vermis, cystic fourth ventricle, enlarged posterior fossa', 'Dandy-Walker malformation is characterized by a hypoplastic or absent vermis with cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle and an enlarged posterior fossa with elevated torcula. Hydrocephalus is a frequent complication rather than the defining feature.'),
('52dc780d-8307-50d3-96ed-163764dd87da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Joubert syndrome is characterized by', '["Only ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only polydactyly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only renal cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Molar tooth sign (midbrain/cerebellar vermis hypoplasia), ataxia, breathing abnormalities","Only eye abnormalities leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Molar tooth sign (midbrain/cerebellar vermis hypoplasia), ataxia, breathing abnormalities', 'Joubert syndrome shows a molar tooth sign on imaging due to vermian hypoplasia and dysplastic superior cerebellar peduncles, with neonatal episodic hyperpnea and developmental ataxia. The disorder is not limited to renal, limb, or eye findings alone.'),
('587a133b-88f8-5d4a-a5db-977ae32b869b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Rhombencephalosynapsis is a rare malformation where', '["Cerebellar hemispheres are fused (absent vermis)","Cerebellum is absent","Brainstem is fused","Fourth ventricle is absent","Cerebellar peduncles are absent"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar hemispheres are fused (absent vermis)', 'In rhombencephalosynapsis the cerebellar hemispheres are fused because the vermis is absent or severely hypoplastic, producing a single midline cerebellum. The cerebellum itself is present, and the other listed absent structures are not the hallmark.'),
('80f65072-eb96-557b-807c-3edf1c251aec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Pontocerebellar hypoplasia (PCH) is a group of', '["Static malformations resulting in decreased functional activity","Infectious causes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Traumatic injuries mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular events without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurodegenerative disorders with prenatal onset"]'::jsonb, 'Neurodegenerative disorders with prenatal onset', 'Pontocerebellar hypoplasia comprises a group of prenatal-onset neurodegenerative disorders with progressive atrophy of the pons and cerebellum. They are not static malformations, infections, trauma, or purely vascular entities.'),
('0a2ed443-e0be-5ad7-822d-3246b57802ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum may be involved in schizophrenia via', '["Abnormal cerebello-thalamo-cortical connectivity","Serotonin only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopamine dysregulation only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only motor symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal cerebello-thalamo-cortical connectivity', 'Schizophrenia is increasingly linked to disrupted cerebello-thalamo-cortical circuitry, which is thought to underlie cognitive and psychomotor features. The model is not attributed simply to serotonin, dopamine alone, or exclusively motor symptoms.'),
('a034365c-2499-548b-9d4d-e974772e7e71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar-mediated dysmetria in thought (cognitive dysmetria) refers to', '["Only attention resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Impaired coordination of mental processes (sensory, motor, cognitive)","Only memory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only speech leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired coordination of mental processes (sensory, motor, cognitive)', 'Cognitive dysmetria reflects disordered timing and coordination of sensory, motor, and cognitive information processing within cerebro-cerebellar loops. It is not an isolated deficit of attention, memory, or speech.'),
('6d9b191d-2d81-562e-af22-e4e6be3ccc4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum is increasingly recognized as important for', '["Only balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Only speech accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor function mediated by specific receptor activation","Cognition, emotion, and autonomic regulation beyond pure motor","Only eye movements leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cognition, emotion, and autonomic regulation beyond pure motor', 'The cerebellum connects with prefrontal, limbic, and autonomic regions through cerebro-cerebellar loops, so it contributes to cognition, emotion, and autonomic regulation in addition to movement.'),
('76a64f65-ad5b-561c-bbdc-1b9180ba4701'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar rTMS (repetitive TMS) can improve', '["Only headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Only depression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor symptoms in ataxia and dystonia","Only anxiety without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor symptoms in ataxia and dystonia', 'Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation over the cerebellum modulates its output and has produced improvement of motor symptoms in ataxic and dystonic disorders.'),
('4d53d49d-5d35-523a-8764-cf983420c653'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar DBS (deep brain stimulation) is being explored for', '["Only dystonia","Only Parkinson disease","Only essential tremor","Only pain","Treatment-resistant tremor and ataxia"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment-resistant tremor and ataxia', 'Deep brain stimulation targeting cerebellar circuitry is being investigated for tremor and gait ataxia that fail to respond to available medical treatment.'),
('3a9bdde1-8d39-5ae8-83e0-898eacc60531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) supports', '["Granule cell death","Purkinje cell survival and plasticity","Reduction of parallel fiber number","Inhibition of climbing fibers","effect"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cell survival and plasticity', 'BDNF is a neurotrophin that promotes the survival, differentiation, and synaptic plasticity of Purkinje cells; loss of this support leads to their degeneration.'),
('d0ed9014-d941-5864-8c7c-6039c1730fbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar Purkinje cell loss occurs in', '["Only Alzheimer resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1,2,3,6,7, etc.)","Only Huntington mediated by specific receptor activation","Only Parkinson without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1,2,3,6,7, etc.)', 'The polyglutamine spinocerebellar ataxias (SCA1, 2, 3, 6, 7) are characterized by progressive Purkinje cell degeneration, whereas Alzheimer, Huntington, and Parkinson disease are not primarily Purkinje cell loss disorders.'),
('18742b8e-51b4-5107-a24d-1fff1eac773b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'SCA1 (spinocerebellar ataxia type 1) is caused by', '["CTG repeat resulting in decreased functional activity","CGG repeat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CAG repeat in ATXN1 gene (chromosome 6)","GAA repeat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","G4C2 repeat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CAG repeat in ATXN1 gene (chromosome 6)', 'SCA1 is caused by CAG (polyglutamine) repeat expansion in ATXN1 on chromosome 6; CTG, CGG, GAA, and G4C2 repeats underlie other repeat disorders such as myotonic dystrophy, fragile X, Friedreich ataxia, and C9orf72 ALS-FTD.'),
('acb62f8e-6d53-559d-9567-79229d3de818'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'SCA3 (Machado-Joseph disease) is caused by', '["CAG repeat in ATXN3 gene (chromosome 14)","GAA repeat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CTG repeat mediated by specific receptor activation","CGG repeat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","G4C2 repeat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CAG repeat in ATXN3 gene (chromosome 14)', 'Machado-Joseph disease (SCA3) results from CAG repeat expansion in ATXN3 on chromosome 14, which encodes ataxin-3.'),
('020e3123-d2db-5371-9061-7ac5aa10a6b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'SCA6 is caused by', '["G4C2 repeat resulting in decreased functional activity","CTG repeat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CGG repeat mediated by specific receptor activation","GAA repeat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CAG repeat in CACNA1A (calcium channel gene)"]'::jsonb, 'CAG repeat in CACNA1A (calcium channel gene)', 'SCA6 is caused by CAG repeat expansion in CACNA1A, which encodes the alpha subunit of the P/Q-type calcium channel; GAA expansions cause Friedreich ataxia.'),
('5a6ff246-f97d-5ac6-b068-a62378b4a97a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'SCA7 includes', '["Only retinal resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar ataxia with retinal degeneration (macular degeneration)","Only seizures mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cognitive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia with retinal degeneration (macular degeneration)', 'SCA7 is distinguished from other SCAs by CAG expansion in ATXN7 producing cerebellar ataxia together with progressive macular and retinal degeneration leading to visual loss.'),
('ab519230-c89c-590a-b4c3-a227bbd9c8d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Episodic ataxia type 2 (EA2) is due to', '["Mutation in CACNA1A (same gene as SCA6)","Myelin protein mutation","CAG repeat in ATXN1","Point mutation in potassium channel","Mitochondrial mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Mutation in CACNA1A (same gene as SCA6)', 'Episodic ataxia type 2 is caused by mutations of CACNA1A, the same gene whose CAG expansion produces SCA6; different CACNA1A mutations give different phenotypes.'),
('562a2ead-078e-59de-b6af-07ae3aaa40f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Friedreich ataxia is NOT a pure cerebellar ataxia; it involves', '["Only spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Only peripheral nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cerebral cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinocerebellar tracts, dorsal columns, corticospinal tracts, peripheral nerves","Only cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar tracts, dorsal columns, corticospinal tracts, peripheral nerves', 'Friedreich ataxia is predominantly a sensory and spinocerebellar ataxia involving dorsal root ganglia, dorsal columns, spinocerebellar and corticospinal tracts, and peripheral nerves, rather than a pure cerebellar degeneration.'),
('e40b0018-91a7-5414-a92d-521e3b1b9ce4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Ataxia telangiectasia is a', '["Adult-onset ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only telangiectasias accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral neuropathy only mediated by specific receptor activation","Childhood-onset disorder with cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasias, immune deficiency, cancer risk","Only immune deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Childhood-onset disorder with cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasias, immune deficiency, cancer risk', 'Ataxia-telangiectasia is an autosomal recessive childhood disorder caused by ATM mutations, presenting with progressive cerebellar ataxia, oculocutaneous telangiectasias, immune deficiency, and increased cancer risk.'),
('f2dc2ee8-4d0c-5145-baac-2ee3695b853f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Niemann-Pick type C can present with', '["Cerebellar ataxia, vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, dementia, splenomegaly","Only liver disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only dementia mediated by specific receptor activation","Only ataxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only eye findings leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia, vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, dementia, splenomegaly', 'Niemann-Pick type C is a lysosomal lipid storage disease classically presenting with cerebellar ataxia, vertical supranuclear gaze palsy, progressive dementia, and splenomegaly.'),
('50d55a01-f3b3-5d88-a976-bf22547d9012'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar vermis hypoplasia is associated with', '["Autism spectrum disorder (increased prevalence)","clinical significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only intellectual disability mediated by specific receptor activation","Only epilepsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor disorder leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autism spectrum disorder (increased prevalence)', 'Cerebellar vermis hypoplasia is found at increased frequency in autism spectrum disorder, supporting the cerebellum''s role in neurodevelopment and cognitive function.'),
('2570ee3c-4f69-5bc2-904a-d3e2689192fd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar white matter volume reduction is seen in', '["Very preterm infants (impaired cerebellar development)","Adults accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elderly mediated by specific receptor activation","groups without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Full-term infants leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Very preterm infants (impaired cerebellar development)', 'Cerebellar growth is rapid in the third trimester, so very preterm birth impairs cerebellar development and produces reduced cerebellar white matter volume.'),
('227af03a-e24d-5cab-a9ff-34269d24c784'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Neonatal cerebellar hemorrhage is a complication of', '["Full-term birth resulting in decreased functional activity","Genetic disorder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Extreme prematurity (germinal matrix hemorrhage extending to cerebellum)","Traumatic delivery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extreme prematurity (germinal matrix hemorrhage extending to cerebellum)', 'In extremely preterm neonates, germinal matrix hemorrhage may extend into the cerebellum and cause neonatal cerebellar hemorrhage; this is unusual with full-term birth, genetic disorders, traumatic delivery, or infection.'),
('bcea0aa8-e449-512f-a982-dfed39778b61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The human cerebellum contains approximately what percentage of total brain neurons?', '["20% resulting in decreased functional activity","50% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","80% (69 billion of 86 billion)","10% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","95% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '80% (69 billion of 86 billion)', 'The cerebellum contains approximately 69 billion of the brain''s total 86 billion neurons, about 80%, largely accounted for by granule cells.'),
('96e45de3-fbb9-5e4a-85a3-ce404a9fa818'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar volume increases dramatically during the', '["Adolescence resulting in decreased functional activity","First 2 years of life (postnatal development)","Senescence mediated by specific receptor activation","Prenatal period only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adulthood leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'First 2 years of life (postnatal development)', 'Postnatal cerebellar growth, dominated by granule cell proliferation and migration, is most rapid during the first two years of life in humans.'),
('a182a1f7-fea0-5d32-8d72-7e4802ab57a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Purkinje cells are generated during', '["Postnatal period","Embryonic period (weeks 4-6)","Adulthood","Senescence","Adolescence"]'::jsonb, 'Embryonic period (weeks 4-6)', 'Purkinje cells are generated early during embryonic development, around weeks 4-6, well before granule cells, which are produced postnatally.'),
('a4296632-8ee9-5520-8070-6d05bcae5e2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Granule cell generation and migration occurs', '["Adulthood resulting in decreased functional activity","Postnatally (first 2 years in humans)","Adolescence mediated by specific receptor activation","postnatal generation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prenatally only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postnatally (first 2 years in humans)', 'Granule cells are produced in the external granular layer and migrate inward postnatally, predominantly during the first two years of life in humans.'),
('f9fbe88f-c184-5884-ab20-42ee64cd805e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The external granular layer (EGL) of developing cerebellum gives rise to', '["Purkinje cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep nuclear cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All cerebellar cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Granule cells, basket cells, stellate cells","Glial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granule cells, basket cells, stellate cells', 'The external granular layer gives rise to granule cells and the molecular layer interneurons, basket cells and stellate cells, whereas Purkinje cells arise earlier from the ventricular zone.'),
('c8ea71fc-6acc-5b0e-af67-d8cc4af6a75d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Purkinje cell migration is guided by', '["BDNF resulting in decreased functional activity","Netrin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Semaphorin mediated by specific receptor activation","Ephrin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reelin signaling from external granular layer"]'::jsonb, 'Reelin signaling from external granular layer', 'Reelin secreted by external granular layer cells guides Purkinje cell migration and cerebellar cortical lamination; in its absence (reeler mouse) the layers are inverted.'),
('aa9f7622-88fc-52f6-b934-f1ce10a49b81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar foliation begins', '["Postnatally only resulting in decreased functional activity","After 1 year accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At birth mediated by specific receptor activation","Prenatally (continues postnatally)","In adolescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prenatally (continues postnatally)', 'Cerebellar foliation (fissure formation) begins in the prenatal period and continues postnatally in parallel with granule cell proliferation.'),
('44e65052-bb10-5153-80c7-0db9ea5e7e06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar Purkinje cell survival depends on', '["Vestibular input","Mossy fiber input","Climbing fiber input","trophic support","Granule cell input (trophic support)"]'::jsonb, 'Granule cell input (trophic support)', 'Purkinje cells depend on trophic support from parallel fiber (granule cell) input; when granule cells are lost, Purkinje cells degenerate secondarily.'),
('ccbf3b7a-864b-5945-bc6a-33505f480361'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'In weaver mutant mice, granule cells die and', '["Purkinje cells also degenerate (secondary)","Climbing fibers degenerate","Deep nuclei degenerate","effect","Purkinje cells survive"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cells also degenerate (secondary)', 'In weaver mutant mice, granule cells die, and the loss of their parallel fiber trophic input causes secondary Purkinje cell degeneration, while climbing fibers and deep nuclei are largely spared.'),
('68c18f22-5ff7-59ed-a348-36fefcd5631e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synaptogenesis peaks', '["Postnatally (first 2-4 weeks in rodents)","Prenatally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In adolescence mediated by specific receptor activation","At birth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In adulthood leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postnatally (first 2-4 weeks in rodents)', 'Parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synaptogenesis peaks in the early postnatal weeks, about the first 2-4 weeks in rodents, coinciding with granule cell migration and maturation of synaptic plasticity.'),
('b05d4f13-3871-593f-a725-538bae39bc55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The glutamate receptor delta 2 (GluD2) is specifically expressed in', '["Astrocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Granule cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","Oligodendrocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Purkinje cells (parallel fiber-Purkinje synapse)"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cells (parallel fiber-Purkinje synapse)', 'Glutamate receptor delta 2 (GluD2) is selectively expressed at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses and is required for LTD and synapse stability.'),
('8005e2f8-e92e-55f7-8c95-7fe835655c44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'GluD2 knockout mice show', '["phenotype resulting in decreased functional activity","Seizures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperactivity mediated by specific receptor activation","Impaired LTD, motor coordination deficits, and ataxia","Enhanced LTD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired LTD, motor coordination deficits, and ataxia', 'GluD2 knockout mice have impaired parallel fiber LTD with motor coordination deficits and ataxia, but not seizures, hyperactivity, or enhanced LTD.'),
('4d140fea-7ee8-5974-94b7-8c981954574f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar climbing fiber synapse elimination occurs', '["Prenatally resulting in decreased functional activity","In adulthood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Postnatally (multiple climbing fibers → single CF per Purkinje)","elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In adolescence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postnatally (multiple climbing fibers → single CF per Purkinje)', 'Each Purkinje cell initially receives multiple climbing fibers; supernumerary climbing fiber synapses are eliminated postnatally until a single climbing fiber remains.'),
('02eb89d3-bc2a-52aa-9eb7-a61a1fedee2d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar LTD requires activation of', '["Only parallel fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Either parallel or climbing fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parallel fibers (glutamate) and climbing fibers (depolarization) simultaneously","Only climbing fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABAergic input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel fibers (glutamate) and climbing fibers (depolarization) simultaneously', 'Cerebellar LTD requires conjunctive activation of parallel fibers (glutamate) and climbing fibers (depolarization) at the same time; activating either pathway alone does not induce LTD.'),
('4e9c26cc-c0e7-5c2f-925b-214671b5038c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Parallel fiber LTD involves', '["Internalization of AMPA receptors at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses","Increased NMDA receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased AMPA receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased GABA receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internalization of AMPA receptors at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses', 'Parallel fiber LTD results from internalization of AMPA receptors at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses, reducing synaptic efficacy; NMDA and GABA receptors are not the targets.'),
('8c76c524-0448-527f-80a6-dabb50a6cd27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar LTP (long-term potentiation) occurs at', '["Granule cell-Golgi cell synapses resulting in decreased functional activity","Deep nuclear synapses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Climbing fiber-Purkinje cell synapses mediated by specific receptor activation","Mossy fiber-granule cell synapses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses (when PF stimulated without CF)"]'::jsonb, 'Parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses (when PF stimulated without CF)', 'Long-term potentiation occurs at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses when parallel fibers are stimulated alone, without climbing fiber coactivation, which instead would induce LTD.'),
('274257ef-ed26-5dd8-81b6-cf15eeb57182'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Bidirectional plasticity at PF-PC synapses (LTD and LTP) allows', '["Static synapses resulting in decreased functional activity","Fine-tuning of cerebellar output for motor learning","plasticity mediated by specific receptor activation","Only LTD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only LTP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fine-tuning of cerebellar output for motor learning', 'The balance between LTD and LTP at parallel fiber-Purkinje cell synapses allows bidirectional fine-tuning of cerebellar output, a mechanism that subserves motor learning.'),
('30db1928-a488-5089-8c2c-f4e9db0400c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar nuclear plasticity also contributes to motor learning via', '["Only afferent changes resulting in decreased functional activity","Changes in synaptic strength at mossy fiber-deep nuclei synapses","Only cortical plasticity mediated by specific receptor activation","nuclear plasticity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Purkinje cell plasticity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Changes in synaptic strength at mossy fiber-deep nuclei synapses', 'Besides cortical plasticity, long-term changes in synaptic strength at mossy fiber-deep nuclei synapses also contribute to cerebellum-dependent motor learning.'),
('1fedf7da-b508-5123-8268-a6492d02fb57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) adaptation is a classic model of', '["Cerebellar (flocculus) motor learning","Spinal learning","Cortical learning","Basal ganglia learning","Peripheral adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar (flocculus) motor learning', 'Gain adaptation of the vestibulo-ocular reflex depends on the flocculus, making it a classic model of cerebellum-dependent motor learning.'),
('a34a0b0d-24ea-51df-8f20-473f7b5f1a24'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'VOR adaptation requires', '["Only mossy fiber input resulting in decreased functional activity","error signal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vestibular input mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cortical input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual error signals conveyed via climbing fibers (inferior olive) to flocculus"]'::jsonb, 'Visual error signals conveyed via climbing fibers (inferior olive) to flocculus', 'VOR adaptation requires retinal slip (visual error) signals that are conveyed through climbing fibers from the inferior olive to the flocculus, where they drive LTD and adaptive gain change.'),
('836b6b76-191c-5901-9bab-cab6ba1cd4ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The gain of VOR is increased when', '["Head is still resulting in decreased functional activity","Light is dim accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eyes closed mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual targets are farther than the head movement (wearing magnifying lenses)","Targets are closer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual targets are farther than the head movement (wearing magnifying lenses)', 'Wearing magnifying lenses makes visual targets appear farther than the head movement, so the flocculus raises the VOR gain; closer targets would instead lower it.'),
('3c2522bb-2af1-5da2-84ca-d4f245acee18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar ataxia can be treated with', '["Dopamine agonists resulting in decreased functional activity","MAO inhibitors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Levodopa mediated by specific receptor activation","Anticholinergics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amantadine, buspirone, riluzole, or 4-aminopyridine (experimental)"]'::jsonb, 'Amantadine, buspirone, riluzole, or 4-aminopyridine (experimental)', 'Amantadine, buspirone, riluzole, and 4-aminopyridine have been explored experimentally for symptomatic treatment of cerebellar ataxia, whereas dopaminergic drugs are not effective for ataxia.'),
('03efc2c3-f7fd-5b49-9a46-e9ea93800579'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', '4-aminopyridine (4-AP) improves', '["Only tremor resulting in decreased functional activity","Only dysarthria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Episodic ataxia type 2 (EA2) and downbeat nystagmus","Only gait without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All ataxias leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Episodic ataxia type 2 (EA2) and downbeat nystagmus', '4-aminopyridine, a potassium channel blocker, increases Purkinje cell excitability and improves episodic ataxia type 2 and downbeat nystagmus.'),
('acc0a6cd-ad5d-5528-8d7a-f5a650459c23'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Acetyl-leucine is being studied for', '["Only tremor resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nystagmus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only dysarthria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar ataxia (symptomatic treatment)"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia (symptomatic treatment)', 'Acetyl-leucine is under investigation as a symptomatic treatment to improve gait and balance across various cerebellar ataxias.'),
('efdafd0d-b3b8-5a5f-9b0a-dfc93b4e294c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar symptoms due to phenytoin toxicity include', '["Only headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Ataxia, nystagmus, dysarthria (reversible cerebellar signs)","Only nystagmus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only tremor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only dizziness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ataxia, nystagmus, dysarthria (reversible cerebellar signs)', 'Phenytoin toxicity reversibly suppresses cerebellar function, producing ataxia, nystagmus, and dysarthria that typically resolve once the dose is reduced.'),
('1d061deb-1062-5718-9d1b-e3f8286dd140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Lithium toxicity can cause', '["Only thyroid dysfunction","Only diabetes insipidus","Only tremor","Cerebellar ataxia and nystagmus","Only renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia and nystagmus', 'Lithium toxicity manifests with coarse tremor, cerebellar ataxia, and nystagmus together with altered mental status.'),
('0d345582-0320-50b3-8c4b-ab11486f1063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Acute cerebellar ataxia in children is most commonly due to', '["Stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Metabolic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-infectious cerebellitis (viral)","Genetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-infectious cerebellitis (viral)', 'Acute cerebellar ataxia in children most commonly follows a viral infection and reflects immune-mediated post-infectious cerebellitis with a good prognosis.'),
('1c8a4f7e-8e79-59c4-9938-8002a3b5874e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Miller Fisher syndrome (variant of Guillain-Barre) presents with', '["Only weakness resulting in decreased functional activity","Only areflexia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only ophthalmoplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","Ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, areflexia (anti-GQ1b antibodies)","Only ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, areflexia (anti-GQ1b antibodies)', 'Miller Fisher syndrome, a variant of Guillain-Barre syndrome, presents with the triad of ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, and areflexia associated with anti-GQ1b antibodies.'),
('a5baa3c1-01fe-5a07-9fed-5f5793d4aa15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Anti-GAD antibodies can cause', '["Cerebellar ataxia (stiff-person syndrome also)","Only motor weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sensory loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Only seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only myoclonus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia (stiff-person syndrome also)', 'Anti-GAD antibodies impair GABA synthesis and cause autoimmune cerebellar ataxia, as well as stiff-person syndrome.'),
('2303f5fa-8ea2-5b63-a770-c9485e7c95d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Sjogren syndrome-associated cerebellar ataxia is due to', '["Infectious resulting in decreased functional activity","Autoimmune cerebellar involvement","Metabolic mediated by specific receptor activation","Degenerative without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vascular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune cerebellar involvement', 'Sjogren syndrome-associated cerebellar ataxia results from autoimmune inflammatory involvement of the cerebellum rather than vascular, infectious, metabolic, or degenerative causes.'),
('f0942437-040b-5e61-bb6d-c681ba9bbc12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Superficial siderosis of CNS presents with', '["Only hearing loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Only headache accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only myelopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Only ataxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar ataxia, hearing loss, myelopathy (hemosiderin deposition)"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia, hearing loss, myelopathy (hemosiderin deposition)', 'Superficial siderosis of the CNS is caused by hemosiderin deposition over the brain and spinal cord surface, classically producing cerebellar ataxia, sensorineural hearing loss, and myelopathy.'),
('e2e0e57f-6bc9-595d-bbf4-a38f5fd6fc34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'Cerebellar hypoperfusion is seen in which condition?', '["Stroke only","Normal","Infection only","Tumor only","Migraine (during aura)"]'::jsonb, 'Migraine (during aura)', 'Migraine aura is associated with cortical spreading depression and transient regional hypoperfusion, which may include the cerebellum; stroke, infection, and tumor do not typically produce such a reversible pattern.'),
('d01c0af1-82d4-5de5-bc7d-9f06d1414401'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum''s role in pain processing involves', '["Only motor responses to pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Only reflex arcs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Modulating the affective dimension of pain (via connections with PAG, thalamus, limbic system)","Direct pain sensation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modulating the affective dimension of pain (via connections with PAG, thalamus, limbic system)', 'The cerebellum modulates the affective-emotional dimension of pain through its connections with the periaqueductal gray, thalamus, and limbic system, rather than directly sensing nociceptive stimuli.'),
('9db782f4-1951-581b-bfcc-faf4813b7c36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The cerebral cortex is classified into how many lobes?', '["6 lobes (frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, insula, limbic)","4 lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","3 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","7 lobes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6 lobes (frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, insula, limbic)', 'The human cerebral cortex is conventionally divided into six lobes: the frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes, plus the insula (buried in the lateral sulcus) and the limbic lobe (a medial ring of cortex). Counting only four or five lobes omits the insula and/or the limbic lobe, which are recognized as distinct cortical regions.'),
('72577517-5809-519a-bb2a-dace68a3f6e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The central sulcus (fissure of Rolando) separates', '["Frontal from parietal lobe","Parietal from occipital","Temporal from parietal","Frontal from temporal","Frontal from occipital"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal from parietal lobe', 'The central sulcus runs obliquely over the lateral surface and separates the frontal lobe (with the precentral motor gyrus) from the parietal lobe (with the postcentral sensory gyrus). The other sulci named are not the boundaries formed by the fissure of Rolando.'),
('b4626d9b-785f-5cae-ac3d-c00e9429657b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The lateral sulcus (fissure of Sylvius) separates', '["Temporal lobe from frontal and parietal (superior)","Temporal from occipital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal from occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal from temporal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal from parietal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe from frontal and parietal (superior)', 'The lateral (Sylvian) fissure runs horizontally and separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal lobes above. The other options describe different lobar boundaries, not the Sylvian fissure.'),
('863289f1-a3e5-5ad4-9507-d60eca016068'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The precentral gyrus (area 4) is the', '["Broca area","Supplementary motor area","Primary motor cortex","Primary somatosensory cortex","Premotor cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor cortex', 'Brodmann area 4 lies in the precentral gyrus and is the primary motor cortex, giving rise to the corticospinal tract and controlling voluntary movement. Broca area and the premotor/supplementary areas are separate frontal motor-planning regions, and the postcentral gyrus is somatosensory.'),
('9427091f-3e7c-5ffa-bdd3-f04cb331c19d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The postcentral gyrus (areas 3,1,2) is the', '["Primary visual cortex","Primary motor cortex","Somatosensory association","Premotor cortex","Primary somatosensory cortex (S1)"]'::jsonb, 'Primary somatosensory cortex (S1)', 'Brodmann areas 3, 1, and 2 occupy the postcentral gyrus and form the primary somatosensory cortex (S1), which receives touch, pressure, and proprioceptive input. Visual and motor cortices lie in the occipital and precentral regions respectively, and S1 is distinct from the somatosensory association cortex.'),
('a337c979-61d0-5322-b8f3-0df261eb4d55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 4 corresponds to', '["Premotor resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary auditory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary visual mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary somatosensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus)"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus)', 'Brodmann area 4 is the primary motor cortex located in the precentral gyrus, containing Betz cells and initiating voluntary movement. Premotor and auditory, visual, and somatosensory regions correspond to other Brodmann areas.'),
('7df33605-fd46-5a50-b1a3-e00efcea6a4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 17 is', '["Secondary visual resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual association accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary visual cortex (striate cortex, calcarine sulcus)","Primary auditory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary visual cortex (striate cortex, calcarine sulcus)', 'Brodmann area 17 is the primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex) lining the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe, the first cortical relay for visual input. Areas 18 and 19 are the visual association cortex, and area 4 is the primary motor cortex.'),
('11bfcbee-e3af-5ce6-95ce-906b9c35e28e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann areas 41 and 42 are', '["Visual cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary auditory cortex (Heschl gyrus, superior temporal)","Primary motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Secondary auditory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory association leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary auditory cortex (Heschl gyrus, superior temporal)', 'Brodmann areas 41 and 42 form the primary auditory cortex on the transverse temporal (Heschl) gyrus, receiving input from the medial geniculate body. Association auditory areas surround them, and visual or motor regions are unrelated.'),
('1cee5e50-0cf3-5d1b-9abb-bace90f3867d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 44 and 45 (in dominant hemisphere) is', '["Premotor resulting in decreased functional activity","Wernicke area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Broca area (motor speech, inferior frontal gyrus)","Prefrontal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Broca area (motor speech, inferior frontal gyrus)', 'Brodmann areas 44 and 45 in the dominant hemisphere constitute Broca area in the inferior frontal gyrus, which programs the motor output of speech. Wernicke area (area 22) subserves comprehension, and the other choices are unrelated frontal regions.'),
('7957d0a5-ed9b-57bc-8f76-44eb8d62f48e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 22 (posterior part, dominant) is', '["Primary auditory resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual association accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wernicke area (auditory association/speech comprehension, superior temporal)","Broca area without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prefrontal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wernicke area (auditory association/speech comprehension, superior temporal)', 'The posterior part of Brodmann area 22 in the dominant hemisphere is Wernicke area in the superior temporal gyrus, essential for auditory language comprehension. Primary auditory cortex (areas 41, 42) lies more anteriorly, and Broca area is in the frontal lobe.'),
('0c9b5d3c-6f44-5042-96fe-f6310fa5b582'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 6 is', '["Visual resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Premotor cortex and supplementary motor area (SMA)","Prefrontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Premotor cortex and supplementary motor area (SMA)', 'Brodmann area 6 lies just anterior to the precentral gyrus and comprises the premotor cortex and the supplementary motor area (SMA), both involved in planning and programming movements. Area 4 is the primary motor cortex, and area 6 is not sensory, visual, or prefrontal.'),
('8be8f4e1-26ec-501a-95b5-915f6b358ff9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 8 includes', '["Auditory cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal eye field (lateral premotor/prefrontal)","Visual cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal eye field (lateral premotor/prefrontal)', 'Brodmann area 8 contains the frontal eye field in the premotor/prefrontal region, which controls conjugate voluntary eye movements. Auditory, visual, and general sensorimotor regions correspond to different Brodmann areas.'),
('a12c7a78-d9fc-5adb-bccd-1504a2fe3fb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 9,10,11,12 are', '["Visual cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Prefrontal cortex (executive function, personality, decision-making)","Sensory cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Speech leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prefrontal cortex (executive function, personality, decision-making)', 'Brodmann areas 9 through 12 lie in the prefrontal cortex, which mediates executive function, personality, and decision-making. Visual, sensory, motor, and speech areas occupy other, more posterior or lateral regions of the hemispheres.'),
('ecf599f8-86e6-563c-babf-a0bdbafb5739'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann areas 3,1,2 are', '["Visual resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary somatosensory cortex (postcentral)","Primary motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Premotor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary somatosensory cortex (postcentral)', 'Brodmann areas 3, 1, and 2 occupy the postcentral gyrus and form the primary somatosensory cortex, which receives discriminative touch and proprioception. Area 4 (precentral) is motor, and visual and auditory cortices lie elsewhere.'),
('37785fc4-d32c-579c-991a-ab278e43c691'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 5 and 7 (superior parietal) are', '["Visual resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatosensory association cortex","Primary sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatosensory association cortex', 'Brodmann areas 5 and 7 in the superior parietal lobule form the somatosensory association cortex, which integrates touch, position, and spatial information. The primary somatosensory cortex (areas 3, 1, 2) lies in the postcentral gyrus and is not association cortex.'),
('21d7d8c3-fb3e-5b09-8764-0eaf48dc1f63'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann areas 18 and 19 (occipital) are', '["Auditory resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual association (V2, V3, V4, V5/MT)","Prefrontal mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary visual without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual association (V2, V3, V4, V5/MT)', 'Brodmann areas 18 and 19 surround the striate cortex as the visual association (extrastriate) cortex, including V2, V3, V4, and V5/MT for processing of motion, color, and form. Area 17 is the primary visual cortex, and the other choices are unrelated.'),
('3de5f988-b371-50b3-b8ae-dc64e1b65f9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 37 (fusiform/fusiform face area) in temporal lobe is involved in', '["Color resulting in decreased functional activity","Motion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Face recognition (fusiform face area, FFA)","Language leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Face recognition (fusiform face area, FFA)', 'Brodmann area 37 contains the fusiform face area, which is specialized for recognizing faces; damage produces prosopagnosia. Motion (MT/V5) and color (V4) processing belong to visual association areas, and language depends on perisylvian regions.'),
('1d1d42d0-fe5e-5ea5-9b86-433615e67b43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The insula (Island of Reil) is involved in', '["Motor control resulting in decreased functional activity","Balance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Hearing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste, visceral sensation, pain, emotion, and interoception"]'::jsonb, 'Taste, visceral sensation, pain, emotion, and interoception', 'The insula is a deep cortical region that integrates gustatory, visceral, pain, and interoceptive information and participates in emotion. It is not a motor, vestibular, visual, or auditory center, which are located in other cortical regions.'),
('3996e44e-a4f9-5778-9e17-25760d92a194'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The insula is divided into', '["Rostral and caudal resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial and lateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal and ventral mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior (agranular, visceral/emotional) and posterior (granular, somatosensory)","Superior and inferior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (agranular, visceral/emotional) and posterior (granular, somatosensory)', 'The insula is subdivided into an anterior agranular region subserving visceral, gustatory, and emotional functions and a posterior granular region handling somatosensory and interoceptive input. Simple directional subdivisions do not reflect its functional cytoarchitecture.'),
('c0e0461c-852c-5830-8483-e06c0c8b01f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Limbic lobe includes', '["Cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, hippocampus, amygdala, fornicate gyrus","Temporal lobe only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Parietal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occipital lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, hippocampus, amygdala, fornicate gyrus', 'The limbic lobe is a ring of medial cortex composed of the cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, hippocampus, amygdala, and fornix, which drive emotion and memory. It is not confined to any single traditional lobe.'),
('a4899aec-95ad-5484-a548-c0ceb40f5841'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The corpus callosum connects', '["Cortex to cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal to temporal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus to cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral hemispheres (interhemispheric commissure)","Basal ganglia to cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral hemispheres (interhemispheric commissure)', 'The corpus callosum is the largest commissure and connects homologous areas of the two cerebral hemispheres, enabling interhemispheric transfer. The other choices describe different fiber systems, such as corticocerebellar, thalamocortical, and association or projection tracts.'),
('1dfd9882-a4a8-5708-9b85-4c6d4a8ad18c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The anterior commissure connects', '["Temporal lobes and olfactory structures","Frontal lobes","Cerebellar hemispheres","Thalamic nuclei","Occipital lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobes and olfactory structures', 'The anterior commissure carries fibers connecting the temporal lobes and olfactory structures (including parts of the amygdala), crossing midline anterior to the columns of the fornix. The corpus callosum, not this structure, connects the frontal and occipital lobes.'),
('bc261bb6-b306-5e27-8c12-d2152b497eb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The internal capsule consists of', '["Only anterior and posterior limbs resulting in decreased functional activity","Genu, anterior, posterior, others accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","One continuous bundle mediated by specific receptor activation","Only genu without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior limb, genu, posterior limb, retrolentiform and sublentiform parts"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior limb, genu, posterior limb, retrolentiform and sublentiform parts', 'The internal capsule is composed of five regions: the anterior limb, genu, posterior limb, retrolentiform part, and sublentiform part. Each carries different fiber groups, so describing it as a single bundle or only two segments is incomplete.'),
('cb6d8203-87fe-5258-8556-9ef2229b05db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The anterior limb of internal capsule contains', '["Frontopontine and thalamocortical (frontal) fibers","Sensory fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optic radiation mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticospinal fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory radiation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontopontine and thalamocortical (frontal) fibers', 'The anterior limb of the internal capsule carries frontopontine fibers and the anterior thalamic radiation to the frontal lobe. Corticospinal fibers pass through the posterior limb, and the optic and auditory radiations lie in the retrolentiform and sublentiform parts.'),
('96d485d8-e153-51d8-8a75-5c76ab4d3a26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The genu of internal capsule contains', '["Thalamocortical fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticobulbar fibers (to cranial nerve motor nuclei)","Auditory radiation mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic radiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corticospinal fibers (upper limb) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticobulbar fibers (to cranial nerve motor nuclei)', 'The genu of the internal capsule contains the corticobulbar tract, which projects to the motor nuclei of cranial nerves. Corticospinal fibers traverse the posterior limb, and the optic and auditory radiations occupy the retrolentiform and sublentiform parts.'),
('33e54356-61db-50ae-a83d-10085483ad62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The posterior limb of internal capsule contains', '["Corticospinal, corticorubral, and thalamocortical (sensory) fibers","Only corticospinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only frontopontine mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal, corticorubral, and thalamocortical (sensory) fibers', 'The posterior limb carries corticospinal and corticorubral fibers anteriorly and the ascending somatosensory (thalamocortical) radiation more posteriorly. It is not exclusively motor or sensory, and frontopontine fibers belong to the anterior limb.'),
('6df0f7e1-71e9-598e-9dde-9ce7585a7df9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Corticospinal tract somatotopy in internal capsule:', '["Face anterior, leg middle, arm posterior","Leg anterior, arm posterior, face at genu","Arm fibers anterior, leg fibers posterior (also face at genu)","Face posterior, arm middle, leg anterior","All mixed"]'::jsonb, 'Arm fibers anterior, leg fibers posterior (also face at genu)', 'In the posterior limb of the internal capsule, arm fibers lie anterior and leg fibers posterior, with corticobulbar (face) fibers passing through the genu. The fibers are therefore topographically organized rather than randomly mixed.'),
('addb3b93-dfd7-5fc8-9b2e-423483a94389'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The retrolentiform part of internal capsule (behind lentiform nucleus) contains', '["Corticobulbar resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic radiation (LGN → calcarine) and parietopontine fibers","Auditory radiation mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticospinal fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontopontine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic radiation (LGN → calcarine) and parietopontine fibers', 'The retrolentiform part lies posterior to the lentiform nucleus and carries the optic radiation from the lateral geniculate nucleus to the visual cortex, along with parietopontine fibers. The auditory radiation occupies the sublentiform part, and corticobulbar fibers run in the genu.'),
('4025d8be-6a10-59b7-b2ee-2197b1ddfbfd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The sublentiform part of internal capsule (below lentiform nucleus) contains', '["Optic radiation resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory radiation (MGN → Heschl gyrus)","Corticobulbar mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticospinal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontopontine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory radiation (MGN → Heschl gyrus)', 'The sublentiform part passes below the lentiform nucleus and contains the auditory radiation from the medial geniculate nucleus to the Heschl gyrus. The optic radiation runs in the retrolentiform part, and corticobulbar and corticospinal fibers use the genu and posterior limb.'),
('36cdd040-a097-58f9-a64d-03bb6910212c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Internal capsule blood supply: anterior limb from', '["Posterior communicating","Anterior choroidal artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Lateral striate (lenticulostriate) arteries from MCA"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral striate (lenticulostriate) arteries from MCA', 'The anterior limb of the internal capsule is supplied by the lateral striate (lenticulostriate) arteries, small perforating branches of the middle cerebral artery. The genu and posterior limb have additional contributions from the anterior choroidal artery, while the ACA and PCA serve other territories.'),
('b7f48265-e3fc-50f7-9863-f4f360f9c51f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Genu of internal capsule supplied by', '["Basilar resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral striate arteries and anterior choroidal (sometimes)","Vertebral mediated by specific receptor activation","ACA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PCA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral striate arteries and anterior choroidal (sometimes)', 'The genu of the internal capsule is supplied mainly by the lateral striate arteries and may also receive a contribution from the anterior choroidal artery. Basilar, vertebral, ACA, and PCA do not directly supply this region.'),
('758f13bb-2d7b-55e6-98a7-d10a4fdfdb04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Posterior limb of internal capsule supplied by', '["Vertebral resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior choroidal artery (lower end) and lenticulostriate (upper part)","ACA mediated by specific receptor activation","PCA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MCA only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior choroidal artery (lower end) and lenticulostriate (upper part)', 'The posterior limb receives the anterior choroidal artery at its lower end and lenticulostriate branches at its upper part, so its blood supply is dual. The ACA, PCA, and MCA alone do not fully account for this territory.'),
('c04fbd25-0009-55d5-bcbc-1c57dacff878'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Retrolenticular/sublentiform internal capsule supplied by', '["Vertebral resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior choroidal artery","PCA mediated by specific receptor activation","MCA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior choroidal artery', 'The retrolentiform and sublentiform parts of the internal capsule are supplied by the anterior choroidal artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery. Vertebral, PCA, MCA, and ACA do not provide the main supply to these regions.'),
('fe1faccf-0a20-5c97-be68-12d6768971bc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Basal ganglia consist of', '["Caudate + putamen only resulting in decreased functional activity","Globus pallidus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","claustrum mediated by specific receptor activation","amygdala without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Caudate nucleus, putamen, globus pallidus (striatum + pallidum), claustrum, amygdala"]'::jsonb, 'Caudate nucleus, putamen, globus pallidus (striatum + pallidum), claustrum, amygdala', 'The basal ganglia comprise the caudate nucleus, putamen, and globus pallidus (together the striatum and pallidum), along with the claustrum and amygdala. Listing only one or two of these nuclei omits major components of the complex.'),
('b7e8717f-f8c6-50b1-8952-99f753762da4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The striatum consists of', '["Globus pallidus and putamen","Putamen and claustrum","Caudate and globus pallidus","All basal ganglia","Caudate nucleus and putamen"]'::jsonb, 'Caudate nucleus and putamen', 'The striatum is formed by the caudate nucleus and the putamen, which share common cellular architecture and connectivity. The globus pallidus is separate (pallidum), and the claustrum is not part of the striatum.'),
('427d3944-e040-5292-9bc3-ed0db25df655'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The lentiform nucleus consists of', '["Caudate and globus pallidus resulting in decreased functional activity","Putamen and claustrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caudate and putamen mediated by specific receptor activation","Putamen and globus pallidus (lens-shaped, lateral to internal capsule)","Globus pallidus and caudate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Putamen and globus pallidus (lens-shaped, lateral to internal capsule)', 'The lentiform nucleus is the lens-shaped mass, lateral to the internal capsule, composed of the putamen laterally and the globus pallidus medially. The caudate nucleus lies anteromedially and is not part of the lentiform nucleus.'),
('f82048b7-a231-59c0-b5c6-5f3a7a6c5bda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The putamen is separated from globus pallidus by', '["Corpus callosum resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caudate nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","External medullary lamina (thin band)","Claustrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External medullary lamina (thin band)', 'A thin sheet of white matter, the external medullary lamina, separates the putamen from the globus pallidus. The internal capsule separates the lentiform nucleus from the caudate and thalamus, and the corpus callosum and claustrum lie elsewhere.'),
('aa54fd13-525a-5784-acc7-31c7176e34b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The globus pallidus is divided into', '["External segment (GPe) and internal segment (GPi) (separated by medial medullary lamina)","Left and right accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial and lateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior and posterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External segment (GPe) and internal segment (GPi) (separated by medial medullary lamina)', 'The globus pallidus is split by the medial medullary lamina into an external segment (GPe) and an internal segment (GPi), which have opposing roles in the basal ganglia circuitry. Directional terms such as left/right or superior/inferior do not describe this functional subdivision.'),
('0fde87aa-d655-5438-bbea-f004830d4a3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The globus pallidus externus (GPe) is part of', '["Indirect pathway of basal ganglia (projects to STN)","Input nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Striatum mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Output nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Indirect pathway of basal ganglia (projects to STN)', 'The GPe is a key link in the indirect pathway, sending GABAergic projections to the subthalamic nucleus; this pathway suppresses unwanted movement. The input nucleus is the striatum, and the output nuclei are the GPi and substantia nigra pars reticulata.'),
('40fda8eb-97ff-5809-b69e-c43c32194e5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The globus pallidus internus (GPi) is the', '["Input nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Intrinsic nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Striatal nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Main output nucleus of basal ganglia (together with SNr)"]'::jsonb, 'Main output nucleus of basal ganglia (together with SNr)', 'The GPi, together with the substantia nigra pars reticulata, is the main output nucleus of the basal ganglia, sending GABAergic projections to the thalamus. The striatum serves as the input nucleus, and the GPi is not a thalamic or striatal nucleus.'),
('23a0ef07-605f-52b4-97db-eca6f4e4edc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The substantia nigra is functionally divided into', '["Medial and lateral resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior and inferior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rostral and caudal mediated by specific receptor activation","Pars interna and externa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pars compacta (SNc, dopamine) and pars reticulata (SNr, GABA)"]'::jsonb, 'Pars compacta (SNc, dopamine) and pars reticulata (SNr, GABA)', 'The substantia nigra is divided into the pars compacta (SNc), which contains dopaminergic neurons projecting to the striatum, and the pars reticulata (SNr), a GABAergic output nucleus. Directional subdivisions do not reflect this functional organization.'),
('4ac6f18d-f1f6-5f10-8836-49163211b433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The caudate nucleus is divided into', '["Only head and body resulting in decreased functional activity","All same accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head (anterior horn lateral ventricle), body, tail (temporal horn, terminates at amygdala)","Head only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only head and tail leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head (anterior horn lateral ventricle), body, tail (temporal horn, terminates at amygdala)', 'The caudate nucleus has a head that bulges into the anterior horn of the lateral ventricle, a slender body, and a tail that curves down along the temporal horn to end near the amygdala. Describing only one or two parts is incomplete.'),
('5607fb95-5312-5d00-bc52-f0b026d2d19c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The caudate nucleus and putamen are connected by', '["Fornix resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior commissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corpus callosum mediated by specific receptor activation","White matter tracts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gray matter bridges across the internal capsule (caudatoputamenal bridges)"]'::jsonb, 'Gray matter bridges across the internal capsule (caudatoputamenal bridges)', 'The caudate nucleus and putamen are linked by strands of gray matter that cross the internal capsule, the caudatoputamenal bridges; together they form the striatum. The fornix, anterior commissure, and corpus callosum are separate fiber or commissural systems.'),
('e5da0df8-b947-5eb3-9c65-ed382cf520d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The claustrum is a thin gray matter layer between', '["GP and putamen resulting in decreased functional activity","Caudate and thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex and white matter mediated by specific receptor activation","Caudate and putamen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Putamen and insular cortex (separated by extreme/external capsule)"]'::jsonb, 'Putamen and insular cortex (separated by extreme/external capsule)', 'The claustrum is a thin sheet of gray matter sandwiched between the putamen and the insular cortex; the external capsule lies lateral to the putamen and the extreme capsule medial to the insula, on either side of it. It does not lie between the other structures listed.'),
('3550da9f-4a52-5044-9b5a-69a653415ec3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The amygdala (amygdaloid complex) is located in', '["Anterior temporal lobe (immediately before hippocampus)","Frontal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Parietal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occipital lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior temporal lobe (immediately before hippocampus)', 'The amygdala is an almond-shaped complex in the anterior temporal lobe, lying just rostral to the hippocampus and fused to the tail of the caudate nucleus. Frontal, parietal, occipital, and thalamic locations do not match its position.'),
('bc4870d8-2711-5411-8aa3-a122c2d73c7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The amygdala is divided into', '["Dorsal and ventral resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial and lateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior and posterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basolateral (input from cortex), central (output to brainstem), and corticomedial (olfactory)"]'::jsonb, 'Basolateral (input from cortex), central (output to brainstem), and corticomedial (olfactory)', 'The amygdaloid complex has three functional divisions: the basolateral group receives cortical and sensory input, the central nucleus provides output to the brainstem, and the corticomedial group processes olfactory input. Simple directional terms do not describe these divisions.'),
('e91801aa-fe2e-5e71-8f2e-72242686f932'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The striatum receives major input from which cortical areas?', '["Widespread (all cortical areas) — especially motor, sensory, prefrontal, limbic","Only sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only prefrontal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only motor cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visual leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Widespread (all cortical areas) — especially motor, sensory, prefrontal, limbic', 'The striatum receives convergent input from essentially all cortical areas, with particularly prominent projections from motor, sensory, prefrontal, and limbic cortex. Restricting the source to a single lobe or modality is not supported by the somatotopic and parallel corticostriatal organization.'),
('0c141a13-84fc-5376-8fbc-02217c476faa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Corticostriatal projections are mainly', '["Dopaminergic resulting in decreased functional activity","Serotonergic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cholinergic mediated by specific receptor activation","GABAergic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glutamatergic (excitatory) from cortex to striatum"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamatergic (excitatory) from cortex to striatum', 'Corticostriatal fibers release glutamate and therefore excite the striatal projection neurons. Dopaminergic input comes from the substantia nigra, GABA is the transmitter of striatal output, and cholinergic and serotonergic inputs arise from local and brainstem neurons respectively.'),
('ea7fe841-df8c-5ac7-8c0b-7764abf3f6ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The nigrostriatal pathway (SNc → striatum) is', '["Dopaminergic (modulatory)","GABAergic","Glutamatergic","Cholinergic","Serotonergic"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic (modulatory)', 'The nigrostriatal pathway originates in the substantia nigra pars compacta and releases dopamine, modulating striatal activity; its loss underlies parkinsonism. It is neither GABAergic, glutamatergic, cholinergic, nor serotonergic.'),
('0ac1811e-6122-54ae-84b1-0089d96abae8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Dopamine in the striatum acts on', '["Only D1 resulting in decreased functional activity","D1 and D2 equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","D1 receptors (direct pathway, excitatory) and D2 receptors (indirect pathway, inhibitory)","Only D2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'D1 receptors (direct pathway, excitatory) and D2 receptors (indirect pathway, inhibitory)', 'In the striatum, dopamine acts on D1 receptors, which facilitate the direct pathway, and on D2 receptors, which inhibit the indirect pathway; imbalance contributes to hypo- and hyperkinetic disorders. Acting on only one receptor subtype or on non-D1/D2 sites is not how nigrostriatal modulation operates.'),
('bf97c228-2e49-5991-bdd1-7f419db30b6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Loss of substantia nigra pars compacta (dopamine) leads to', '["Chorea resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemiballismus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parkinson disease (akinesia, rigidity, tremor)","Huntington disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dystonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parkinson disease (akinesia, rigidity, tremor)', 'Dopaminergic neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta normally regulate striatal output that facilitates movement, so their loss causes the parkinsonian triad of akinesia, rigidity, and tremor. Chorea and hemiballismus are hyperkinetic disorders linked to the striatum and subthalamic nucleus respectively, not to nigral dopamine loss.'),
('714cde33-abb0-5e86-a29c-aa8bf3b627e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The striatum contains which types of neurons?', '["Medium spiny neurons (GABAergic, principal cells) and interneurons (cholinergic, GABAergic)","Only large neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only projection neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cholinergic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only glutamatergic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medium spiny neurons (GABAergic, principal cells) and interneurons (cholinergic, GABAergic)', 'Medium spiny neurons are the GABAergic principal output cells of the striatum, while local interneurons are mainly cholinergic or GABAergic. The other choices incorrectly suggest that only a single neuronal type populates the striatum.'),
('d8b91840-1bfb-5429-bd54-5485ab03a3dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Medium spiny neurons receive dopaminergic input on', '["Glutamate only resulting in decreased functional activity","Axon only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cell body only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dendritic spines (on the neck/spine of corticostriatal synapses)","dopamine input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dendritic spines (on the neck/spine of corticostriatal synapses)', 'Dopaminergic terminals from the substantia nigra synapse on the necks of dendritic spines near corticostriatal glutamatergic inputs, allowing dopamine to modulate those inputs. The responses do not restrict dopamine targeting to the axon or cell body.'),
('a4bb976d-a008-597e-a837-b43e7459f990'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The direct pathway of basal ganglia:', '["Striatum → GPe → GPi resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortex → striatum → SNc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex → STN → GPi mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex → striatum (D1) → GPi/SNr → thalamus → cortex (facilitates movement)","Cortex → striatum → GPe → STN → GPi → thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex → striatum (D1) → GPi/SNr → thalamus → cortex (facilitates movement)', 'The direct pathway uses D1 medium spiny neurons that inhibit GPi/SNr, disinhibiting the thalamus and facilitating movement. The GPe-STN sequence belongs to the indirect pathway, not the direct one.'),
('ae463eda-a199-50fa-aa62-c06c14ed7d9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The indirect pathway of basal ganglia:', '["Cortex → striatum (D2) → GPe → STN → GPi/SNr → thalamus (suppresses movement)","Striatum → GPi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex → striatum (D1) → GPi mediated by specific receptor activation","Striatum → GPe → GPi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex → STN → GPi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex → striatum (D2) → GPe → STN → GPi/SNr → thalamus (suppresses movement)', 'The indirect pathway runs from D2 striatal neurons through GPe and the subthalamic nucleus to GPi/SNr, ultimately reducing thalamic activity and suppressing movement. Routes bypassing GPe and STN do not represent this pathway.'),
('bce15494-5d17-50f1-9c69-e158e490a221'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Basal ganglia output (GPi/SNr) uses', '["GABA (inhibitory) on thalamus","Glutamate (excitatory)","Serotonin","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'GABA (inhibitory) on thalamus', 'GPi and SNr project to the thalamus using the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA, tonically suppressing VA/VL thalamic neurons. Glutamate, dopamine, serotonin, and acetylcholine are not the transmitters of basal ganglia output.'),
('188e090c-9783-5465-b137-2b862f05704d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hyperdirect pathway involves', '["GPi → thalamus → cortex","STN → GPe → GPi","Cortex → STN → GPi (fast braking of movement)","Cortex → striatum → GPi","Striatum → GPe → STN"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex → STN → GPi (fast braking of movement)', 'The hyperdirect pathway sends cortical input directly to the subthalamic nucleus and then to GPi, producing rapid braking of ongoing movement. The other routes pass through the striatum or GPe and do not reflect this fast cortico-subthalamic link.'),
('74258b48-d543-5657-9658-302b89d5dabd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Lesion of subthalamic nucleus (STN) causes', '["Parkinsonism resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemiballismus (disinhibition of thalamus → violent flinging)","Myoclonus mediated by specific receptor activation","Chorea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dystonia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemiballismus (disinhibition of thalamus → violent flinging)', 'The STN normally excites GPi, which inhibits the thalamus; damaging the STN disinhibits the thalamus and causes violent flinging of a limb (hemiballismus). Loss of nigral dopamine, not STN damage, produces parkinsonism.'),
('c5629bca-4763-5028-822f-c17b8dc8d46c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Hyperkinetic disorders (e.g., Huntington) result from', '["Underactive direct pathway resulting in decreased functional activity","Too little dopamine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Too much dopamine mediated by specific receptor activation","Overactivity of direct pathway (or underactivity of indirect) → too much movement","Overactive indirect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Overactivity of direct pathway (or underactivity of indirect) → too much movement', 'Excess movement arises when the direct pathway is overactive or the indirect pathway is underactive, both removing inhibition from the thalamus. Underactivity of the direct pathway and overactivity of the indirect pathway would instead cause hypokinesia.'),
('6864e118-0de9-570b-960f-a25b95005101'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Hypokinetic disorders (e.g., Parkinson) result from', '["Too much glutamate resulting in decreased functional activity","Too much GABA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Overactive direct mediated by specific receptor activation","Underactive indirect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Underactive direct pathway (D1) and overactive indirect (D2) due to dopamine deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Underactive direct pathway (D1) and overactive indirect (D2) due to dopamine deficiency', 'Dopamine loss weakens D1-mediated direct pathway excitation and D2-mediated indirect pathway inhibition, so striatal output over-suppresses the thalamus and movement is reduced. Overactivity of the direct pathway would favor movement, not hypokinesia.'),
('c22245f8-59f2-5351-8a68-d4a849ab6f79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The basal ganglia also receive input from the cerebellum via', '["Via GP only resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct cerebellostriatal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar output directly mediated by specific receptor activation","connection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus (VL) → cortex → basal ganglia (polysynaptic)"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus (VL) → cortex → basal ganglia (polysynaptic)', 'Cerebellar output reaches the basal ganglia only through a multi-synaptic circuit: cerebellum projects to ventral lateral (VL) thalamus, then to cortex, which feeds the basal ganglia. There is no direct cerebello-striatal pathway.'),
('a35b6f8a-b340-5225-a2a5-9cdd154420b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Chorea (irregular, dance-like movements) is associated with', '["Huntington disease (loss of D2 MSN in striatum)","Hemiballismus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dystonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Parkinson disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Huntington disease (loss of D2 MSN in striatum)', 'Choreiform jerks are the hallmark of Huntington disease, where loss of D2 medium spiny neurons in the striatum disinhibits movement. Hemiballismus is linked to STN lesions, and Parkinson disease produces tremor rather than chorea.'),
('5c5f17aa-04be-5eff-8050-d8bf212467b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Huntington disease is caused by', '["CGG repeat resulting in decreased functional activity","G4C2 repeat accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CTG repeat mediated by specific receptor activation","GAA repeat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CAG repeat in huntingtin gene (chromosome 4)"]'::jsonb, 'CAG repeat in huntingtin gene (chromosome 4)', 'Huntington disease results from an expanded CAG triplet repeat in the huntingtin gene on chromosome 4. CGG causes fragile X, and CTG/GAA repeats produce other repeat-expansion disorders, not Huntington disease.'),
('88f15951-a956-5f52-98a6-a0c028c11621'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Huntington disease pathology shows', '["Atrophy of GPi resulting in decreased functional activity","Nigral degeneration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortical atrophy only mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar atrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrophy of caudate and putamen (loss of MSNs), especially D2 indirect pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Atrophy of caudate and putamen (loss of MSNs), especially D2 indirect pathway', 'Huntington disease causes prominent atrophy of the caudate and putamen, with early loss of D2 medium spiny neurons in the indirect pathway. Nigral degeneration characterizes Parkinson disease rather than Huntington disease.'),
('792b7a3b-e33b-5a9d-959d-fc66db4a466c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Parkinson disease pathology shows', '["Cerebellar degeneration resulting in decreased functional activity","Striatal atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortical atrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Degeneration of SNc dopamine neurons + Lewy bodies (α-synuclein)","GPi degeneration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Degeneration of SNc dopamine neurons + Lewy bodies (α-synuclein)', 'Parkinson disease is defined by loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta accompanied by Lewy bodies containing α-synuclein. Striatal and cerebellar degeneration point to other neurodegenerative processes.'),
('0b3f3501-db02-56ab-99e0-b0c45a7a0231'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Levodopa (L-DOPA) is effective in Parkinson disease by', '["Replacing dopamine in the striatum (converted to dopamine by dopa decarboxylase)","Blocking dopamine metabolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulating dopamine receptors directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Enhancing dopamine release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibiting GABA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Replacing dopamine in the striatum (converted to dopamine by dopa decarboxylase)', 'L-DOPA crosses the blood-brain barrier and is converted to dopamine by dopa decarboxylase, restoring striatal dopamine depleted by nigral degeneration. Direct receptor stimulation is the action of dopamine agonists, not of levodopa itself.'),
('371af0e0-daf2-587f-9d95-e17521bc55b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Dopamine agonists (pramipexole, ropinirole) in Parkinson act by', '["Increasing dopamine release","Directly stimulating D2/D3 receptors in striatum","Inhibiting dopamine metabolism","Blocking GABA","Converting to dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Directly stimulating D2/D3 receptors in striatum', 'Dopamine agonists mimic the neurotransmitter by directly activating D2/D3 receptors in the striatum, bypassing the need for dopamine synthesis. They do not release dopamine, block its metabolism, or convert to it.'),
('d5048382-9ab7-54d1-b19f-83962980e7d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Deep brain stimulation (DBS) for Parkinson commonly targets', '["Thalamus (VIM) resulting in decreased functional activity","Caudate nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subthalamic nucleus (STN) or globus pallidus internus (GPi)","Globus pallidus externus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Putamen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subthalamic nucleus (STN) or globus pallidus internus (GPi)', 'High-frequency DBS effectively disables the overactive STN or GPi, restoring balanced basal ganglia output that has been disrupted by dopamine loss. The caudate and putamen are not standard DBS targets in Parkinson disease.'),
('01ad6886-9d31-5c67-8831-83e51a5e77df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'DBS for essential tremor targets', '["Caudate resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventral intermediate nucleus (VIM) of thalamus","STN mediated by specific receptor activation","GPi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Putamen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral intermediate nucleus (VIM) of thalamus', 'The ventral intermediate (VIM) nucleus of the thalamus relays cerebellar tremor signals, so its stimulation suppresses essential tremor. STN and GPi are used for Parkinson disease, not for isolated essential tremor.'),
('086c1ba5-2b2a-5d26-9886-cb5cab54639a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The caudate nucleus is involved in', '["Only visual resulting in decreased functional activity","Cognitive (executive) function, working memory, and learning (frontostriatal loop)","Only motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Only auditory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only emotion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cognitive (executive) function, working memory, and learning (frontostriatal loop)', 'Through frontostriatal loops, the caudate supports executive function, working memory, and learning. The putamen, rather than the caudate, specializes in motor control, and the caudate is not restricted to a single sensory modality.'),
('03c82765-a573-5e22-84eb-b004d8dfbeb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The putamen is involved in', '["Only emotion resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor learning and control (motor loop with SMA/premotor)","Only auditory mediated by specific receptor activation","Only visual without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cognition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor learning and control (motor loop with SMA/premotor)', 'The putamen forms the motor loop of the basal ganglia with the supplementary and premotor areas, governing motor learning and control. Cognitive and limbic functions are served by the caudate and ventral striatum respectively.'),
('e5868d08-51a3-5cb6-b034-866f67858ab8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The ventral striatum (nucleus accumbens) is involved in', '["Reward, motivation, and addiction (mesolimbic dopamine)","Executive function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor control mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual processing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reward, motivation, and addiction (mesolimbic dopamine)', 'The nucleus accumbens receives mesolimbic dopamine from the ventral tegmental area and mediates reward, motivation, and addictive behavior. Executive function and motor control depend on the caudate and putamen instead.'),
('742e81bb-e706-5513-bc7f-ac90d7377030'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Nucleus accumbens is divided into', '["Anterior and posterior resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal and ventral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior and inferior mediated by specific receptor activation","Core (associative, goal-directed) and shell (limbic, reward valence)","Medial and lateral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Core (associative, goal-directed) and shell (limbic, reward valence)', 'The nucleus accumbens has a core subregion concerned with goal-directed associative processing and a shell subregion tied to limbic reward and valence. These are its functional divisions, not anterior/posterior or dorsoventral parts.'),
('1aa4d7a6-9923-5033-8876-30c7ef1a932d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The ventral pallidum (basal ganglia) is involved in', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Limbic/reward output (projects to MD thalamus and habenula)","Audition mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor output without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory processing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limbic/reward output (projects to MD thalamus and habenula)', 'The ventral pallidum is the output node of the limbic basal ganglia loop, projecting to the mediodorsal (MD) thalamus and habenula to influence reward. Sensory and motor processing are carried by other basal ganglia loops.'),
('b8b12cb9-c2d8-5138-8d5a-a2cce6f959ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The limbic system includes', '["Hippocampus, amygdala, cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus","Only mammillary bodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fornix mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hippocampus and amygdala without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cingulate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus, amygdala, cingulate gyrus, parahippocampal gyrus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus', 'The limbic system is a network of interconnected cortical and subcortical structures handling memory and emotion; it includes the hippocampus, amygdala, cingulate and parahippocampal gyri, fornix, mammillary bodies, and anterior thalamus. Listing single structures ignores the integrated nature of this system.'),
('dcdb2688-8706-5fea-b9a4-3bd601a05e4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampus is a key component of which functional system?', '["Limbic system (memory and emotion)","Auditory system","Motor system","Visual system","Reticular activating system"]'::jsonb, 'Limbic system (memory and emotion)', 'The hippocampus belongs to the limbic system and is essential for forming new memories and processing emotional context. Auditory, motor, visual, and reticular functions are served by other, distinct networks.'),
('71360916-9fc5-5fc4-8c62-5e6e923add4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampus is divided into', '["Only dentate gyrus resulting in decreased functional activity","CA1-3 only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CA1, CA2, CA3, CA4 (Cornu Ammonis) + dentate gyrus + subiculum","Only subiculum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only parahippocampal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CA1, CA2, CA3, CA4 (Cornu Ammonis) + dentate gyrus + subiculum', 'The hippocampal formation spans the Cornu Ammonis fields (CA1-CA4), the dentate gyrus, and the subiculum, all of which process memory together. The other choices omit major constituent regions.'),
('14632fe4-a34f-5853-9a11-ccfcbc34a945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The trisynaptic circuit in hippocampus:', '["CA3 → dentate → CA1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Entorhinal cortex → dentate gyrus (mossy fibers) → CA3 (Schaffer collaterals) → CA1 → subiculum → entorhinal","Subiculum → CA1 → CA3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Dentate → CA1 → CA3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CA1 → CA3 → dentate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Entorhinal cortex → dentate gyrus (mossy fibers) → CA3 (Schaffer collaterals) → CA1 → subiculum → entorhinal', 'The trisynaptic loop runs entorhinal cortex → dentate gyrus via the perforant path, dentate → CA3 by mossy fibers, and CA3 → CA1 by Schaffer collaterals, then returns to entorhinal cortex through the subiculum. The other sequences reverse or omit key synapses.'),
('2917450e-5add-5e6d-a299-dcbee3381d37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Perforant path (entorhinal → dentate) is the main input to hippocampus from', '["Thalamus","Amygdala","Motor cortex","Entorhinal cortex (layer II)","Visual cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Entorhinal cortex (layer II)', 'Layer II neurons of the entorhinal cortex send the perforant path to the dentate gyrus, carrying neocortical information into the hippocampus. Thalamus, amygdala, and motor or visual cortex do not provide this principal input.'),
('bcf6e314-232f-5102-86e2-74f59767d9fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Mossy fibers (dentate granule cell axons) synapse on', '["Entorhinal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","CA1 pyramidal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CA3 pyramidal cells (proximal dendrites)","Dentate gyrus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subiculum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CA3 pyramidal cells (proximal dendrites)', 'Axons of dentate granule cells, the mossy fibers, synapse on the proximal dendrites of CA3 pyramidal cells. CA1 receives input from CA3 via Schaffer collaterals, not from mossy fibers.'),
('295fe9c0-4b3e-5fac-883e-df2a8dfb06df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Schaffer collaterals are axons of', '["CA3 pyramidal cells projecting to CA1","CA1 pyramidal cells","Subicular cells","Entorhinal cells","Dentate granule cells"]'::jsonb, 'CA3 pyramidal cells projecting to CA1', 'Schaffer collaterals arise from CA3 pyramidal cells and terminate on CA1 pyramidal cells, completing a key synaptic step in the trisynaptic circuit. They are not derived from CA1, subiculum, entorhinal, or dentate granule cells.'),
('36239b57-3ff9-55f3-a71f-7fce63b37eab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus involves', '["Only GABA resulting in decreased functional activity","Only AMPA receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NMDA mediated by specific receptor activation","NMDA receptor activation → AMPA insertion at CA3-CA1 synapses","Dopamine only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NMDA receptor activation → AMPA insertion at CA3-CA1 synapses', 'At CA3-CA1 synapses, strong stimulation opens NMDA receptors, letting calcium in and triggering insertion of more AMPA receptors that strengthens the synapse as LTP. This process depends on glutamate, not on GABA or dopamine.'),
('3e99831a-6aa3-5c14-a4fc-d6cc673dedf6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'LTP is considered the cellular basis of', '["Motor coordination","Sensation","Learning and memory","Hearing","Vision"]'::jsonb, 'Learning and memory', 'Long-term potentiation strengthens synaptic transmission persistently and is widely regarded as the cellular mechanism underlying learning and memory. Motor coordination, sensation, hearing, and vision depend on other circuits.'),
('5d8165fa-cf5a-5d7a-afb2-87da149956b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampal CA1 region is especially vulnerable to', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoxia/ischemia (CA1 neurons die after global ischemia)","Seizure mediated by specific receptor activation","Excitotoxicity only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia/ischemia (CA1 neurons die after global ischemia)', 'CA1 pyramidal cells are selectively vulnerable to oxygen deprivation and die after transient global ischemia, producing selective hippocampal injury. This susceptibility is greater than to infection, seizure, or trauma and is not merely excitotoxic.'),
('6b341009-0e4c-568b-ab9c-9f1c55bde60b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Post-traumatic amnesia after concussion is likely due to', '["Cortical damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar damage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem damage mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamic damage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampal (especially CA1/CA3) dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampal (especially CA1/CA3) dysfunction', 'Concussive forces injure the hippocampus, especially CA1 and CA3, disrupting memory encoding and causing post-traumatic amnesia. Cortical, cerebellar, brainstem, or thalamic injury would produce different clinical deficits.'),
('8e1eb5b5-9e4d-5b76-bf6e-a7dd9d3efe40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The dentate gyrus is one of the few areas where', '["Glial cells only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cell death occurs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","neurons are born mediated by specific receptor activation","plasticity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adult neurogenesis occurs (new granule cells formed)"]'::jsonb, 'Adult neurogenesis occurs (new granule cells formed)', 'The dentate gyrus and the subventricular zone are major sites where new neurons, specifically granule cells, are generated in the adult brain. The vague alternatives do not identify this defining feature of ongoing neurogenesis.'),
('403b53ba-3bcd-5316-9ba7-812454cc9ba9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The subiculum is the main output structure of hippocampus', '["Only to cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Projecting only to entorhinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only to mammillary mediated by specific receptor activation","Projecting to entorhinal cortex, mammillary bodies, nucleus accumbens, prefrontal","Only to amygdala leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Projecting to entorhinal cortex, mammillary bodies, nucleus accumbens, prefrontal', 'The subiculum distributes hippocampal output to multiple targets, including the entorhinal cortex, mammillary bodies, nucleus accumbens, and prefrontal cortex. Confining its projections to a single structure underestimates its role.'),
('8c7925de-ca77-5a3f-b054-eb1472f03a91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The fornix is the major output tract of the hippocampus projecting to', '["Frontal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia (striatum) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mammillary bodies, septal nuclei, anterior thalamus, nucleus accumbens, hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies, septal nuclei, anterior thalamus, nucleus accumbens, hypothalamus', 'The fornix carries hippocampal efferents to mammillary bodies, septal nuclei, anterior thalamus, nucleus accumbens, and hypothalamus. Neocortical regions such as frontal, parietal, and occipital cortex are not direct fornix targets.'),
('3b69f7d3-ff44-5472-b7e0-a49a646fb87b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The fornix is divided into', '["Only columns resulting in decreased functional activity","Head, body, tail accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crura, body, columns (anterior pillars), and decussating fibers","Superior, middle, inferior without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crura, body, columns (anterior pillars), and decussating fibers', 'The fornix has crura, a body, anterior columns, and decussating fibers where the two crura cross. Divisions like head-body-tail or superior/middle/inferior do not apply to this fiber tract.'),
('c0f1c612-a37a-5041-a841-3fce76ff5189'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Precommissural fornix fibers project to', '["Amygdala resulting in decreased functional activity","Septal nuclei, nucleus accumbens, orbitofrontal cortex","Hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mammillary bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septal nuclei, nucleus accumbens, orbitofrontal cortex', 'Precommissural fornix fibers terminate in the septal nuclei, nucleus accumbens, and orbitofrontal cortex. Mammillary bodies and anterior thalamus are reached by postcommissural fibers instead.'),
('ba9d3e36-16c0-5151-871e-73c05aed8cbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Postcommissural fornix fibers project to', '["Cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Mammillary bodies and anterior thalamus (via mammillothalamic tract)","Amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Septal nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies and anterior thalamus (via mammillothalamic tract)', 'Postcommissural fornix fibers reach the mammillary bodies, and the mammillothalamic tract relays to the anterior thalamus. Septal and accumbens targets are served by the precommissural component of the fornix.'),
('033311ae-1a98-51c0-9d55-75f592225953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The cingulate gyrus (Brodmann areas 23,24,31,32) is part of', '["Limbic lobe (Papez circuit) and default mode network (posterior)","Language area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatosensory cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual association without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limbic lobe (Papez circuit) and default mode network (posterior)', 'The cingulate gyrus is the cortical core of the limbic lobe and Papez circuit, and its posterior part participates in the default mode network. Language, somatosensory, visual, and primary motor functions occupy other cortical regions.'),
('b9fd4eb0-888b-50b1-8001-5893b8964f9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Anterior cingulate cortex (ACC) is involved in', '["Only motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Conflict monitoring, error detection, emotional regulation, pain","Only cognition mediated by specific receptor activation","Only emotion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conflict monitoring, error detection, emotional regulation, pain', 'The anterior cingulate cortex integrates conflict monitoring, error detection, emotion regulation, and pain processing. It is not limited to a single function such as motor, cognitive, emotional, or sensory processing.'),
('9215f8ff-66f6-57ce-80a3-d6eeb1ab91d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Posterior cingulate cortex (PCC) is involved in', '["Motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Emotion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Attention mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Default mode network (internally directed thought, memory retrieval, self-referential processing)"]'::jsonb, 'Default mode network (internally directed thought, memory retrieval, self-referential processing)', 'The posterior cingulate cortex is a hub of the default mode network, supporting internally directed thought, memory retrieval, and self-referential processing. Motor, emotional, attentional, and visual functions are not its principal role.'),
('8c9ea282-7a7e-511a-8ee0-f9fcebb6b520'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The parahippocampal gyrus (areas 27,28,35,36) is important for', '["Hearing resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor coordination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensation mediated by specific receptor activation","Memory encoding and retrieval (entorhinal and perirhinal cortex)","Vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Memory encoding and retrieval (entorhinal and perirhinal cortex)', 'The parahippocampal gyrus, containing entorhinal and perirhinal cortex, channels sensory information into the hippocampus and supports memory encoding and retrieval. Hearing, motor coordination, sensation, and vision depend on other cortical areas.'),
('e3befc55-4107-5b6e-b62c-57751d49001b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The entorhinal cortex (Brodmann area 28) is the', '["Primary auditory cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary visual cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary motor cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary sensory cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary interface between hippocampus and neocortex (major input to dentate)"]'::jsonb, 'Primary interface between hippocampus and neocortex (major input to dentate)', 'The entorhinal cortex is the principal gateway linking neocortex and hippocampus, giving rise to the perforant path to the dentate gyrus. Auditory, visual, motor, and general sensory functions belong to other cortical areas.'),
('78c5f6d9-978e-5321-b4ff-7400b3c65faa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Grid cells (spatial navigation) are found in', '["CA3 resulting in decreased functional activity","Subiculum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Entorhinal cortex (medial, layer II)","Dentate gyrus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampus (CA1) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Entorhinal cortex (medial, layer II)', 'Grid cells with their regular hexagonal firing fields reside in the medial entorhinal cortex, particularly layer II, and support spatial navigation. CA3, subiculum, dentate gyrus, and CA1 house place cells rather than grid cells.'),
('48ca6751-4d54-5df4-8fcb-28af78505e72'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Place cells (spatial memory) are found in', '["Subiculum","Cerebellum","Dentate gyrus","Entorhinal cortex","Hippocampus (CA1 and CA3)"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus (CA1 and CA3)', 'Place cells fire at specific spatial locations and are located in the hippocampal CA1 and CA3 regions. Grid cells occupy the entorhinal cortex, while the cerebellum and subiculum do not contain these place cells.'),
('ac8baad2-9408-51f7-b841-332563a1258a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The rhinal cortex (perirhinal and entorhinal) is crucial for', '["Visual recognition memory (what) and spatial memory (where)","Language accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emotion mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor learning without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Attention leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual recognition memory (what) and spatial memory (where)', 'The rhinal cortex processes object recognition (''what'') through the perirhinal area and spatial memory (''where'') together with the entorhinal area. Language, emotion, motor learning, and attention depend on other networks.'),
('20ff9f2b-5d06-52c3-a8d1-47b9f1fcad0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Lesion of the hippocampus causes', '["Anterograde amnesia (inability to form new long-term memories)","Procedural memory loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both anterograde and retrograde mediated by specific receptor activation","memory loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retrograde amnesia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterograde amnesia (inability to form new long-term memories)', 'The hippocampus is needed to consolidate new long-term memories, so its damage produces anterograde amnesia. Procedural skill learning relies on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, and isolated hippocampal lesions do not cause pure retrograde amnesia.'),
('943528ed-5168-5a8c-b699-edb12359dc2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The fornix connects the hippocampus to which structure in the hypothalamus?', '["Preoptic area","Arcuate nucleus","Mammillary bodies","Paraventricular nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies', 'The fornix is the major efferent fiber bundle of the hippocampus, arching to the mammillary bodies, which relay onward to the anterior thalamic nucleus. The preoptic, arcuate, paraventricular, and supraoptic nuclei are hypothalamic structures not connected to the hippocampus by the fornix.'),
('7f4a40aa-1fe1-59f9-a437-a74262387660'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Mammillary body atrophy on MRI is associated with', '["Stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Huntington disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Korsakoff syndrome (thiamine deficiency/alcoholism)","Parkinson disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alzheimer disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Korsakoff syndrome (thiamine deficiency/alcoholism)', 'Bilateral mammillary body atrophy on MRI is the hallmark of Korsakoff syndrome, caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, most often in chronic alcoholism, and produces anterograde amnesia with confabulation. Stroke, Huntington, Parkinson, and Alzheimer diseases are not associated with selective mammillary body loss.'),
('3071a668-115d-5bb2-9378-e63a5b8e21d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The corpus callosum develops from', '["Hippocampus resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior commissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fornix mediated by specific receptor activation","Lamina terminalis (commissural plate)","Septal area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina terminalis (commissural plate)', 'The corpus callosum forms from the commissural plate of the lamina terminalis during the third month of gestation. The anterior commissure, fornix, hippocampus, and septal region do not give rise to the corpus callosum.'),
('998f112c-332d-59ed-ba48-dd7ae805e335'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The corpus callosum is topographically organized:', '["Splenium = frontal; genu = occipital resulting in decreased functional activity","Genu = occipital; splenium = frontal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All same mediated by specific receptor activation","Rostrum = orbital/prefrontal; genu = prefrontal; body = motor/sensory; splenium = occipital/temporal","Body = occipital; rostrum = motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rostrum = orbital/prefrontal; genu = prefrontal; body = motor/sensory; splenium = occipital/temporal', 'The corpus callosum is somatotopically arranged: the rostrum carries orbital/prefrontal fibers, the genu prefrontal fibers, the body sensorimotor fibers, and the splenium occipital/temporal commissural fibers. The other choices assign fibers to the wrong regions.'),
('353f7673-adeb-56ea-a9c9-acff2a15e5a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Agenesis of the corpus callosum may be associated with', '["Only seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","Only visual deficits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colpocephaly (dilated occipital horns), interhemispheric cysts, and cognitive deficits","Only motor deficits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal cognition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Colpocephaly (dilated occipital horns), interhemispheric cysts, and cognitive deficits', 'Agenesis of the corpus callosum is frequently associated with colpocephaly (disproportionate dilatation of the occipital horns), interhemispheric cysts, and a spectrum of cognitive deficits. The anomalies are not confined to a single modality and cognition is not normal.'),
('366fbde6-fa73-5966-9fec-8ab8bf7c6b7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Disconnection syndrome (split-brain) after corpus callosotomy shows', '["Visual deficits resulting in decreased functional activity","deficits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor deficits mediated by specific receptor activation","Inability to name objects presented in left visual field (in right hemisphere)","Language deficits only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to name objects presented in left visual field (in right hemisphere)', 'After corpus callosotomy, visual input reaching the right hemisphere cannot cross to the left language-dominant side, so objects flashed in the left visual field cannot be named even though they are recognized nonverbally. Basic vision, motor, and language functions remain intact.'),
('43b6cccf-7b6e-5334-9c1d-80dff4a0a068'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Alien hand syndrome is associated with', '["Temporal lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Callosal lesion (anterior CC) — one hand acts involuntarily","Occipital lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Parietal lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Callosal lesion (anterior CC) — one hand acts involuntarily', 'Alien hand syndrome typically arises from a lesion of the anterior corpus callosum (often with medial frontal involvement), causing one hand to act in a purposeless, seemingly involuntary way. Damage confined to a single cortical lobe does not produce this sign.'),
('adf623e5-c2fe-5581-808c-ba4b07d2df4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The internal capsule is a continuation of', '["Cingulum resulting in decreased functional activity","Fornix accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior longitudinal fasciculus mediated by specific receptor activation","Corona radiata (above) → internal capsule → cerebral peduncle (below)","Corpus callosum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corona radiata (above) → internal capsule → cerebral peduncle (below)', 'The internal capsule is the vertical white matter continuation linking the corona radiata above with the cerebral peduncle below. The fornix, cingulum, corpus callosum, and superior longitudinal fasciculus are limbic, commissural, or association tracts.'),
('a9caf080-f8ef-5a3d-bd7b-d916546ba92e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'White matter tracts of the cerebral hemispheres: association, commissural, and projection fibers', '["Two types","Only projection","Only association","Correct: 3 types","Only commissural"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: 3 types', 'Cerebral hemisphere white matter is classified into three types: association fibers within one hemisphere, commissural fibers crossing the midline, and projection fibers connecting cortex with subcortical structures. Answers listing one or two types are incomplete.'),
('597666ff-42f5-5701-87bf-be2684b058ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Short association fibers connect', '["Cortex to thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Left and right hemispheres accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex to spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Distant gyri without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adjacent gyri (U-fibers/arcuate fibers) within same lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Adjacent gyri (U-fibers/arcuate fibers) within same lobe', 'Short association (U-fiber/arcuate) fibers connect adjacent gyri within the same lobe by curving beneath the intervening sulcus. They do not cross the midline, reach the spinal cord, contact the thalamus, or span distant gyri.'),
('2d2e75cd-2795-5a2b-9f95-c55f218bb22a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Long association fiber bundles include', '["Only SLF resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cingulum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only arcuate mediated by specific receptor activation","Only ILF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior longitudinal, arcuate, inferior longitudinal, uncinate, fronto-occipital, cingulum"]'::jsonb, 'Superior longitudinal, arcuate, inferior longitudinal, uncinate, fronto-occipital, cingulum', 'The principal long association bundles are the superior and inferior longitudinal, arcuate, uncinate, superior and inferior fronto-occipital fasciculi, and the cingulum. A list naming just one tract does not cover the full set.'),
('bd74ca79-8878-5ccc-a467-1ea432f237d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The arcuate fasciculus connects', '["Frontal to occipital resulting in decreased functional activity","Broca area (frontal) to Wernicke area (temporal) (language)","Temporal to occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal to parietal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parietal to occipital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Broca area (frontal) to Wernicke area (temporal) (language)', 'The arcuate fasciculus joins the posterior inferior frontal (Broca) area to the posterior superior temporal (Wernicke) area and is the main language pathway supporting repetition. The other pairs link regions that are not joined by this tract.'),
('69116914-7dd1-5a4a-aff7-3ae2f8941108'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Conduction aphasia (impaired repetition) results from', '["Wernicke area resulting in decreased functional activity","Global aphasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transcortical aphasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Lesion of arcuate fasciculus (or left temporoparietal junction)","Broca area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesion of arcuate fasciculus (or left temporoparietal junction)', 'Conduction aphasia, with fluent speech, poor repetition, and intact comprehension, results from damage to the arcuate fasciculus or the left temporoparietal junction. Isolated lesions of Broca or Wernicke areas, or global or transcortical patterns, produce different language deficits.'),
('5ae493a6-13c7-5cf8-83ce-58e36712ec86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The uncinate fasciculus is a major tract connecting', '["Parietal and temporal lobes","Frontal and parietal lobes","Frontal and occipital lobes","Temporal lobe (anterior) and orbitofrontal cortex","Occipital and temporal lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe (anterior) and orbitofrontal cortex', 'The uncinate fasciculus hooks around the stem of the lateral sulcus, connecting the anterior temporal lobe with the orbitofrontal cortex and supporting emotional and social processing. The other lobe pairs listed are not joined by this tract.'),
('3ef7ed45-56e3-5139-92c1-056ebd3b33ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The inferior longitudinal fasciculus (ILF) connects', '["Occipital to frontal resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal to occipital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal to temporal mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital lobe to temporal lobe (visual object recognition)","Frontal to temporal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital lobe to temporal lobe (visual object recognition)', 'The inferior longitudinal fasciculus runs from the occipital lobe to the temporal lobe and contributes to visual object recognition. It does not connect to the frontal or parietal lobes.'),
('a7aa402d-d975-5d32-9250-4b2b628040ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The superior longitudinal fasciculus (SLF) connects', '["Only frontal to parietal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only temporal to occipital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only frontal to occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Only parietal to temporal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital lobes (multi-component)"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital lobes (multi-component)', 'The superior longitudinal fasciculus is a multi-component association tract linking the frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes. Naming only one lobe pair understates its wide distribution.'),
('a903e112-b16b-59f8-9bcc-7c05c56edd71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The cingulum bundle is the white matter tract of the', '["Temporal lobe only resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital lobe only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal lobe only mediated by specific receptor activation","Limbic lobe (within cingulate gyrus, connecting frontal, parietal, temporal limbic areas)","Parietal lobe only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limbic lobe (within cingulate gyrus, connecting frontal, parietal, temporal limbic areas)', 'The cingulum is the major association tract of the limbic lobe, running within the cingulate gyrus and connecting frontal, parietal, and temporal limbic regions. It is not restricted to a single lobe.'),
('45646737-e43b-572f-809a-7095796d5bab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The external capsule contains', '["Visual fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Claustrocortical fibers and temporopontine fibers (white matter between putamen and claustrum)","Corticospinal fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Claustrocortical fibers and temporopontine fibers (white matter between putamen and claustrum)', 'The external capsule lies between the putamen and claustrum and carries claustrocortical and temporopontine fibers. It does not transmit primary visual, auditory, sensory, or corticospinal fibers.'),
('96c2bba6-0de7-50fe-bf58-030d31ce7eed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The extreme capsule is between', '["Cortex and subcortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus and putamen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Insula and operculum mediated by specific receptor activation","Claustrum and insular cortex (contains fronto-insular and claustrocortical fibers)","Putamen and claustrum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Claustrum and insular cortex (contains fronto-insular and claustrocortical fibers)', 'The extreme capsule lies between the claustrum and the insular cortex and contains fronto-insular and claustrocortical fibers. The internal capsule sits between the lentiform nucleus and caudate/thalamus, and the external capsule between putamen and claustrum.'),
('4d6ac3a0-8aa1-50db-a2a4-14816786c9d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The anterior commissure (in the lamina terminalis) connects', '["Occipital lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior temporal lobes (and olfactory bulbs)","Cerebellar hemispheres without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parietal lobes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior temporal lobes (and olfactory bulbs)', 'The anterior commissure crosses the midline at the lamina terminalis and chiefly connects the anterior temporal lobes and olfactory structures. The frontal, occipital, parietal, and cerebellar regions are not linked by it.'),
('551a0e0b-e22d-53e2-8fd5-795311a94f42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampus lies in which part of temporal horn?', '["Anterior resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial floor of temporal (inferior) horn of lateral ventricle","Roof mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral wall without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial floor of temporal (inferior) horn of lateral ventricle', 'The hippocampus forms the medial floor of the temporal (inferior) horn of the lateral ventricle. It does not lie in the roof or lateral wall, nor is it confined to the anterior or posterior pole.'),
('31407dcc-0d40-5837-897d-5cb983e9a086'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampal sulcus separates', '["CA1 from CA3 resulting in decreased functional activity","Hippocampus from parahippocampal gyrus (subiculum)","Fimbria from hippocampus mediated by specific receptor activation","Hippocampus from dentate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CA3 from dentate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus from parahippocampal gyrus (subiculum)', 'The hippocampal sulcus separates the hippocampus (with its dentate gyrus) from the parahippocampal gyrus, including the subiculum. The remaining options pair internal subdivisions rather than the structures joined by this sulcus.'),
('612814e4-16d6-56e9-bfac-6352acc82013'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The dentate gyrus gets its name from', '["Finger-like projections resulting in decreased functional activity","Dental shape of neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dentures mediated by specific receptor activation","Tooth-like appearance (notched edges) on cross-section","Serrated white matter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tooth-like appearance (notched edges) on cross-section', 'The dentate gyrus takes its name from its tooth-like, notched appearance on cross-section. Its shape, not its neurons, vessels, or white matter, gives the name.'),
('43cbf425-4aeb-5c25-b094-dabeca30cc62'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The CA regions (cornu ammonis) of hippocampus are named because', '["They are triangular resulting in decreased functional activity","They are shaped like bananas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","They resemble ram''s horns (Amun = Egyptian ram god of Thebes)","They are flat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They are coiled like corkscrews leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'They resemble ram''s horns (Amun = Egyptian ram god of Thebes)', 'Cornu ammonis means ram''s horn, named for Amun, the Egyptian ram god of Thebes, because of the curved, horn-like shape of the hippocampus. The other proposed shapes do not explain the origin of the name.'),
('d1d158a4-34d6-5e21-88d0-bc5ec7f8d29c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The alveus of hippocampus is a thin layer of', '["Vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","White matter (axons of CA pyramidal cells covering the ventricular surface)","Pia mater mediated by specific receptor activation","Ependyma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gray matter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'White matter (axons of CA pyramidal cells covering the ventricular surface)', 'The alveus is a thin layer of white matter on the ventricular surface of the hippocampus, formed by the axons of CA pyramidal cells. It is not gray matter, pia mater, ependyma, or vascular tissue.'),
('c474f7f8-d043-5338-97ae-75b12534827c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The fimbria of hippocampus is the continuation of', '["Dentate gyrus resulting in decreased functional activity","CA3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveus (axons coalescing to form fimbria, then fornix)","Subiculum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CA1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alveus (axons coalescing to form fimbria, then fornix)', 'The fimbria is the coalesced continuation of the alveus and continues forward as the fornix. It does not arise from the dentate gyrus, subiculum, or a single CA region by itself.'),
('f9917920-f208-50ae-a964-55b7bcc2c72f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The fimbria separates from the hippocampus at the posterior (splenial) end to become', '["Body of fornix","Corpus callosum","Crus of fornix","Column of fornix","Anterior commissure"]'::jsonb, 'Crus of fornix', 'At the posterior splenial end the fimbria separates from the hippocampus and thickens to become the crus of the fornix. The crus then forms the body and later the columns of the fornix.'),
('a84be8cb-e17c-5d6c-a9c3-fcb5b8e7da4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampus receives cholinergic input from', '["Medial septal nucleus (via fornix)","Pedunculopontine nucleus","Raphe nuclei","Locus coeruleus","Nucleus basalis"]'::jsonb, 'Medial septal nucleus (via fornix)', 'The medial septal nucleus provides the principal cholinergic projection to the hippocampus, traveling via the fornix. The raphe, locus coeruleus, and pedunculopontine nuclei contribute monoaminergic rather than cholinergic septal input.'),
('b70da745-23f5-59de-8213-ed251f3a49a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Hippocampal theta rhythm (4-12 Hz) is associated with', '["Coma resulting in decreased functional activity","Quiet wakefulness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep sleep mediated by specific receptor activation","Anesthesia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exploration, spatial navigation, and REM sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Exploration, spatial navigation, and REM sleep', 'Hippocampal theta rhythm (4–12 Hz) appears during active exploration, spatial navigation, and REM sleep. It is not characteristic of coma, deep non-REM sleep, or anesthesia.'),
('6d85d854-8d4d-520f-8fbe-35efdbb1dd7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Sharp wave ripples (SWRs) in hippocampus occur during', '["Active exploration resulting in decreased functional activity","REM sleep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slow-wave sleep and quiet wakefulness (memory consolidation)","Seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anesthesia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Slow-wave sleep and quiet wakefulness (memory consolidation)', 'Sharp-wave ripples arise in the hippocampus during slow-wave sleep and quiet wakefulness and support memory consolidation and replay. They are not the pattern of active exploration, REM sleep, seizures, or anesthesia.'),
('982ded0a-4317-5a53-be83-d1adbb465f97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Replay of hippocampal place cell sequences occurs during', '["Delta activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Gamma activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Theta activity mediated by specific receptor activation","Sharp wave ripples (offline memory consolidation)","Alpha activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sharp wave ripples (offline memory consolidation)', 'Hippocampal place-cell sequences are replayed during sharp-wave ripples, consolidating spatial memories offline. Theta, gamma, delta, and alpha rhythms are not the carriers of this replay.'),
('9fc73e0f-3906-5d20-bc6a-368a1b79d3f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The amygdala receives sensory input from', '["Only olfactory resulting in decreased functional activity","All sensory modalities (via thalamus and cortex, especially high-level association areas)","Only visual mediated by specific receptor activation","Only somatosensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only auditory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All sensory modalities (via thalamus and cortex, especially high-level association areas)', 'The amygdala receives input from all major sensory modalities, chiefly via the thalamus and high-level association cortex, allowing it to gauge emotional significance. It is not limited to a single sense.'),
('46090ffa-e1bd-5c16-a34e-1a1d4e0af713'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The amygdala (central nucleus) projects to brainstem for', '["Fear responses: PAG (freezing), hypothalamus (sympathetic), vagus (bradycardia), facial motor","Only autonomic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Only PAG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fear responses: PAG (freezing), hypothalamus (sympathetic), vagus (bradycardia), facial motor', 'The central nucleus channels the fear response to the brainstem: the PAG for freezing, the hypothalamus for sympathetic arousal, vagal nuclei for bradycardia, and facial motor nuclei for expression. No single effector alone explains this projection.'),
('8b6a6f8b-a18c-50c7-86d9-cc1f886b0f2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Fear conditioning involves', '["Hippocampus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Tone (CS) + shock (US) → amygdala (lateral → central) → fear response","Cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tone (CS) + shock (US) → amygdala (lateral → central) → fear response', 'In fear conditioning a tone (conditioned stimulus) is paired with a shock (unconditioned stimulus); the lateral amygdala receives the association and drives the central nucleus to generate the fear response. The hippocampus, cortex, cerebellum, and thalamus support this process but are not the core locus.'),
('1093ba26-3430-5361-9faf-753d81126308'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Extinction of fear memory involves', '["extinction resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amygdala alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Hippocampus alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prefrontal cortex (infralimbic) inhibiting amygdala (central)"]'::jsonb, 'Prefrontal cortex (infralimbic) inhibiting amygdala (central)', 'Fear extinction requires the infralimbic prefrontal cortex to inhibit the central amygdala and suppress the conditioned fear response. The amygdala or hippocampus acting alone cannot produce extinction.'),
('a81cd84b-0c48-58ba-85c4-7adc3d31cf0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The amygdala is involved in', '["Only social resulting in decreased functional activity","Only reward accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only anger mediated by specific receptor activation","Emotion processing (fear, anger, reward), emotional memory, social behavior","Only fear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion processing (fear, anger, reward), emotional memory, social behavior', 'The amygdala processes emotion across several domains, including fear, anger, and reward, and supports emotional memory and social behavior. It is not dedicated to any single emotional category.'),
('485b144e-be1e-529d-bb30-cfbeb985fe31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Bilateral amygdala damage (Kluver-Bucy) causes', '["Hyperorality, hypersexuality, visual agnosia, emotional blunting, fearlessness","Only memory loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hyperorality mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hypersexuality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only emotional blunting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperorality, hypersexuality, visual agnosia, emotional blunting, fearlessness', 'Bilateral medial temporal amygdala damage produces the Kluver-Bucy syndrome of hyperorality, hypersexuality, visual agnosia, emotional blunting, and fearlessness. All of these features appear together rather than in isolation.'),
('9de11907-a39f-524d-9c03-d21ede5d79e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The amygdala and hippocampus are connected for', '["Only spatial memory resulting in decreased functional activity","connection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only declarative memory mediated by specific receptor activation","Only fear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Emotional memory (amygdala modulates hippocampal memory consolidation)"]'::jsonb, 'Emotional memory (amygdala modulates hippocampal memory consolidation)', 'The amygdala modulates hippocampal-dependent memory consolidation for emotionally salient events, strengthening recall. This link supports emotional memory rather than spatial or declarative memory alone.'),
('5e6d1f5c-1aef-563d-beef-4deadb11de21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The amygdala is also involved in', '["Only fear resulting in decreased functional activity","Only extinction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only aversion mediated by specific receptor activation","Reward learning (basolateral amygdala with NAc, VTA)","Only punishment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reward learning (basolateral amygdala with NAc, VTA)', 'The basolateral amygdala, together with the nucleus accumbens and ventral tegmental area, supports reward learning and appetitive motivation. It acts on rewards as well as fear and aversion.'),
('8c12efaa-1c48-5462-be4f-8cf1150f4316'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) is key for', '["Memory resulting in decreased functional activity","Reward, reinforcement, addiction, and motivation","Executive function mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor control without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Language leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reward, reinforcement, addiction, and motivation', 'The nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) is central to reward, reinforcement, addiction, and motivation as part of the mesolimbic dopamine system. It does not primarily mediate memory, executive function, motor control, or language.'),
('2f6574b7-217d-5d7a-8771-abf23c321b6a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Addiction involves dysregulation of', '["Nigrostriatal pathway","Cortical pathway","Hippocampal pathway","Cerebellar pathway","VTA → NAc (dopamine) mesolimbic pathway"]'::jsonb, 'VTA → NAc (dopamine) mesolimbic pathway', 'Addictive drugs and behaviors converge on the mesolimbic dopamine pathway from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens. The nigrostriatal and other pathways are not the primary substrate of addiction.'),
('aada0898-486a-5fba-b62e-b0449c358607'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Long-term depression (LTD) in striatum is important for', '["Memory consolidation resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor learning and habit formation","Vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Fear conditioning without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Language leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor learning and habit formation', 'Striatal long-term depression reshapes corticostriatal synapses and supports motor learning and habit formation. It is not the basis of memory consolidation, fear conditioning, vision, or language.'),
('34ca8e1c-14e9-5624-b9b3-79db7aa355f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Dorsal striatum (caudate/putamen) dominates', '["Habit learning and action selection (procedural)","Emotional learning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spatial memory mediated by specific receptor activation","Social cognition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Declarative memory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Habit learning and action selection (procedural)', 'The dorsal striatum (caudate and putamen) is central to habit learning, procedural memory, and action selection. Emotional, spatial, social, and declarative memory rely on other circuits.'),
('63f50dd2-0f42-5cb1-87fb-895773bca06a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The prefrontal cortex (PFC, granular frontal) is involved in', '["Only memory resulting in decreased functional activity","Only emotion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Executive functions (planning, decision-making, working memory, inhibition, social cognition)","Only language without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Executive functions (planning, decision-making, working memory, inhibition, social cognition)', 'The prefrontal (granular frontal) cortex carries out planning, decision-making, working memory, inhibition, and social cognition. No single function—memory, emotion, language, or motor—defines it.'),
('61368a6f-847c-55ae-8b41-c9a0b44bf32a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC, area 9/46) is essential for', '["Social behavior resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor planning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Language only mediated by specific receptor activation","Emotional processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Working memory, planning, cognitive flexibility, and abstract reasoning"]'::jsonb, 'Working memory, planning, cognitive flexibility, and abstract reasoning', 'The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (Brodmann areas 9 and 46) supports working memory, planning, cognitive flexibility, and abstract reasoning. Social behavior and emotional processing depend more on ventromedial and orbitofrontal regions.'),
('f9893bf0-f95f-5cb0-a279-56f8d1527b76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Ventromedial prefrontal cortex (VMPFC/OFC, area 10/11/25/32) is involved in', '["Working memory resulting in decreased functional activity","Language accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor control without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Emotion regulation, reward/punishment, decision-making, social cognition"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion regulation, reward/punishment, decision-making, social cognition', 'The ventromedial and orbitofrontal prefrontal regions regulate emotion, integrate reward and punishment, guide decision-making, and support social cognition. Working memory, vision, motor control, and language depend on other cortical regions.'),
('b10862aa-8f4a-5c96-8418-60598a2cd252'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Orbitofrontal cortex (OFC) receives input from', '["Only vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Only taste accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only smell mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hearing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All sensory modalities (especially olfactory, gustatory, visual) via thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'All sensory modalities (especially olfactory, gustatory, visual) via thalamus', 'The orbitofrontal cortex receives input from all sensory modalities, notably olfactory, gustatory, and visual, via the thalamus and association cortex. No single sense supplies it.'),
('3137d2fc-a0fc-5f4f-ade2-26189fd8be8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Phineas Gage (OFC lesion) demonstrated', '["Visual deficits resulting in decreased functional activity","Memory loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Language deficits mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor deficits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Personality change (disinhibition, impulsivity, emotional dysregulation) after frontal lobe damage"]'::jsonb, 'Personality change (disinhibition, impulsivity, emotional dysregulation) after frontal lobe damage', 'Phineas Gage''s orbitofrontal injury produced profound personality change with disinhibition, impulsivity, and emotional dysregulation while sparing basic motor, sensory, language, and memory function. The case shows the orbitofrontal role in regulating social behavior.'),
('4a664b3c-793a-5679-8a3b-3362cd1f864f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The supplementary motor area (SMA, medial area 6) is involved in', '["Planning sequences of movements, bimanual coordination, internally generated actions","Only eye movements accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only language mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only simple movements leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Planning sequences of movements, bimanual coordination, internally generated actions', 'The supplementary motor area (medial area 6) plans sequences of movements, coordinates bimanual actions, and generates internally triggered movements. Simple, eye, language, or purely sensory functions do not define it.'),
('0b0ab056-10f6-5562-9656-e78f2becf6e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The premotor cortex (lateral area 6) is involved in', '["Language only resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple movements only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internally generated movements mediated by specific receptor activation","Visually guided (externally-triggered) movements, movement preparation","Sensory only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visually guided (externally-triggered) movements, movement preparation', 'The lateral premotor cortex (lateral area 6) prepares movements and controls visually guided, externally triggered actions. Planning of internally generated movements belongs mainly to the SMA.'),
('f4eae832-8f42-54eb-ac3a-ef5c1cf6d46a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The frontal eye field (area 8) generates', '["Optokinetic nystagmus resulting in decreased functional activity","Smooth pursuit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pupillary reflex mediated by specific receptor activation","Voluntary saccades (contralateral) — ''frontal gaze center''","Vergence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Voluntary saccades (contralateral) — ''frontal gaze center''', 'The frontal eye field (Brodmann area 8) programs intentional, contralaterally directed saccades; its output descends to the superior colliculus and brainstem burst generators. Optokinetic nystagmus is generated by subcortical structures, smooth pursuit relies on the parieto-occipito-temporal junction, the pupillary reflex is a midbrain (Edinger-Westphal) reflex, and vergence is mediated by the midbrain near-response centers.'),
('04aa9310-052f-5e0b-8f5b-51b2aab23817'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The cingulate motor area (CMA, area 24c) is involved in', '["Motivation-driven movements, action selection based on reward","Only sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only language mediated by specific receptor activation","Only eye movements without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only simple movements leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motivation-driven movements, action selection based on reward', 'The cingulate motor area (area 24c) lies in the anterior cingulate gyrus and selects voluntary movements based on motivation and expected reward, with connections to the primary motor cortex and spinal cord. The other options mislabel it as purely sensory, language-related, or oculomotor, none of which matches its limbic-motor function.'),
('014ba96d-6746-5d4b-8118-337e1a25da09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The posterior parietal cortex (PPC, area 5/7) is involved in', '["Only language resulting in decreased functional activity","Spatial attention, visuomotor integration (reach, grasp), and spatial working memory","Only somatosensory mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spatial attention, visuomotor integration (reach, grasp), and spatial working memory', 'The posterior parietal cortex integrates multimodal input to direct spatial attention and guide reaching and grasping, and it also participates in spatial working memory. The distractor options restrict it to single sensory or language functions, which ignores its integrative role.'),
('acd5889f-fccf-5399-9265-d9977daed658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 5 (superior parietal) is involved in', '["Audition resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visuospatial attention mediated by specific receptor activation","Proprioception and somatosensory integration (body schema)","Language leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proprioception and somatosensory integration (body schema)', 'Brodmann area 5 in the superior parietal lobule integrates proprioceptive and cutaneous signals to build the body schema, receiving heavy input from primary somatosensory cortex. Visuospatial attention is more closely associated with adjacent area 7, while audition, vision, and language belong to other regions.'),
('4fc003b8-bec8-587c-b1e0-1dafebbe7cda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Brodmann area 7 (precuneus/superior parietal) is involved in', '["Language only resulting in decreased functional activity","Somatosensation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision only mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visuospatial attention, mental rotation, episodic memory retrieval, self-consciousness"]'::jsonb, 'Visuospatial attention, mental rotation, episodic memory retrieval, self-consciousness', 'Brodmann area 7, spanning the superior parietal lobule and precuneus, supports visuospatial attention, mental rotation, and episodic memory retrieval, with the precuneus also linked to self-referential processing. Labeling it as language-, somatosensory-, visual-, or motor-only overlooks its multimodal cognitive functions.'),
('27e206c8-7789-5f7c-8e99-7b3abef233f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Hemispatial neglect (usually right parietal lesion) involves', '["Only sensory resulting in decreased functional activity","Lack of attention to left hemisphere, extinction, anosognosia","Only deafness mediated by specific receptor activation","Only motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","visual awareness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of attention to left hemisphere, extinction, anosognosia', 'Right posterior parietal damage impairs spatial attention to the contralateral (left) hemisphere, causing neglect with extinction on double simultaneous stimulation and often anosognosia (unawareness of the deficit). The distractors describe isolated sensory, motor, or visual losses rather than the attentional syndrome.'),
('08b41184-23b7-503d-888e-f58563437fa2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Gerstmann syndrome (left angular gyrus lesion) includes', '["Aphasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual agnosia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amnesia mediated by specific receptor activation","Finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia, right-left confusion","Apraxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia, right-left confusion', 'Gerstmann syndrome from a left angular gyrus lesion combines finger agnosia, agraphia, acalculia, and right-left confusion. The distractors name single deficits such as aphasia, visual agnosia, amnesia, or apraxia, which do not reproduce the tetrad.'),
('32c97c3d-03e9-5d85-a61b-25a105f9c368'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The angular gyrus (area 39) is involved in', '["Only reading resulting in decreased functional activity","Only writing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only arithmetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Reading (visual word form area), arithmetic, semantic processing, language","Only naming leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reading (visual word form area), arithmetic, semantic processing, language', 'The left angular gyrus acts as a multimodal association area for reading (visual word form), arithmetic, and semantic processing, and its damage contributes to Gerstmann syndrome and alexia. The other options each isolate one function, whereas the angular gyrus supports all of these together.'),
('1133a6a5-ab16-5477-96e7-95d4b6308b33'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The supramarginal gyrus (area 40) is involved in', '["Calculation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Emotion only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reading only mediated by specific receptor activation","Naming only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phonological processing, limb praxis (ideomotor), tactile perception"]'::jsonb, 'Phonological processing, limb praxis (ideomotor), tactile perception', 'The supramarginal gyrus contributes to phonological processing during reading and repetition, houses circuitry for ideomotor limb praxis, and aids tactile object recognition. Options limited to calculation, emotion, reading, or naming fail to capture this multimodal sensorimotor-language role.'),
('41f83e98-a16e-5432-a770-c2fe04506284'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Ideomotor apraxia (impaired imitation of gestures, tool use) is due to', '["Basal ganglia only resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal lobe only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left parietal (supramarginal) or premotor (area 6) lesions","Temporal lobe only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occipital lobe only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left parietal (supramarginal) or premotor (area 6) lesions', 'Ideomotor apraxia, the impaired imitation of gestures and tool use despite normal strength, follows lesions of the left supramarginal gyrus or premotor cortex (area 6) that disconnect action concepts from motor programs. Lesions confined to the basal ganglia, temporal, occipital, or a single lobe do not produce this disconnection pattern.'),
('f365c460-791c-541f-8c1f-61493f6a632e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Visual cortex (V1, area 17) is located in', '["Frontal lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Parietal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcarine sulcus (medial occipital lobe)","Lateral occipital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcarine sulcus (medial occipital lobe)', 'Primary visual cortex lines the banks of the calcarine sulcus on the medial occipital lobe, receiving the optic radiation from the LGN. The other lobes and the lateral occipital surface do not contain V1.'),
('d75d2258-cd41-5834-8f6a-f2533a73b207'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The visual pathway: retina → optic nerve → chiasm → tract → LGN → optic radiation → V1', '["Optic nerve → V1 directly","Retina → LGN directly","Retina → cortex directly","Correct sequence","Retina → SC → LGN"]'::jsonb, 'Correct sequence', 'After the retina, the pathway passes through the optic nerve, optic chiasm, optic tract, lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, and optic radiation before reaching V1. Retinal ganglion cells do not project straight to cortex or LGN without these intermediate stations, and the superior colliculus receives only a collateral branch rather than the primary route.'),
('5b509742-ae3b-5b7f-8b46-d8645b24118b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Macular sparing (preserved central vision in occipital lesion) is due to', '["Peripheral representation resulting in decreased functional activity","Central retina projects to large area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortical magnification mediated by specific receptor activation","macular sparing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral representation at occipital pole (dual blood supply from MCA and PCA)"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral representation at occipital pole (dual blood supply from MCA and PCA)', 'Central (macular) vision is spared after occipital infarcts because the occipital pole receives collateral supply from both the PCA and MCA, and each hemisphere receives some bilateral input from the macula. Cortical magnification, peripheral representation, or a large central projection alone do not explain the dual vascular protection.'),
('5a04e28e-b0a1-5d96-bf97-6b1c3d961abb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The dorsal stream of visual processing (where/how pathway) goes to', '["Parietal lobe (V5/MT, area 7) — spatial location, motion, visually guided action","Frontal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prefrontal mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temporal lobe (inferior temporal) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal lobe (V5/MT, area 7) — spatial location, motion, visually guided action', 'The dorsal stream projects from V1 to the posterior parietal cortex, including V5/MT and area 7, and processes object location, motion, and visually guided action. The temporal (ventral) stream instead handles object identification, while the remaining options name lobes that are not the dorsal-stream target.'),
('501b7496-102e-540c-b4b0-f235edb59e81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The ventral stream of visual processing (what pathway) goes to', '["Parietal lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Prefrontal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occipital lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temporal lobe (V4, inferior temporal, fusiform) — object recognition (what)"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe (V4, inferior temporal, fusiform) — object recognition (what)', 'The ventral stream runs from V1 along the inferior occipitotemporal route through V4 and the fusiform/inferior temporal cortex, where it mediates object and face recognition. The parietal ''where'' stream and the other lobes do not perform this identity-based processing.'),
('93ab117b-0447-50ed-8073-6c8a94a36ddb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Area V5/MT (middle temporal) is specialized for', '["Visual motion detection and direction selectivity","Depth perception accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Color processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Form processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Face recognition leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual motion detection and direction selectivity', 'Area V5/MT (middle temporal) contains direction-selective neurons that encode visual motion; bilateral damage causes akinetopsia. Color, depth, form, and face processing are handled by other regions such as V4, area 7, and the fusiform face area.'),
('ac473edf-cfbc-533c-ba7d-57ac37aa7ae7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Area V4 is specialized for', '["Color perception (lesion → achromatopsia)","Form accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Face mediated by specific receptor activation","Depth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Color perception (lesion → achromatopsia)', 'Area V4 processes color information; bilateral occipitotemporal lesions of V4/fusiform produce cerebral achromatopsia. Motion, form, face, and depth processing depend on V5/MT, ventral occipitotemporal cortex, and parietal regions rather than V4.'),
('2aaa691c-fc8f-5ac5-a132-215e6f128511'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The fusiform face area (FFA) is located in', '["Frontal resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior temporal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fusiform gyrus (lateral occipitotemporal) — selective for face recognition","Parietal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occipital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fusiform gyrus (lateral occipitotemporal) — selective for face recognition', 'The fusiform face area lies along the lateral occipitotemporal junction and is selectively activated by faces; damage causes prosopagnosia. The frontal, parietal, anterior temporal, and occipital cortex locations listed do not house the FFA.'),
('7114b59e-1bc7-5fbc-a7b1-7e5da120cd1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The parahippocampal place area (PPA) is involved in', '["Motion detection resulting in decreased functional activity","Object recognition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Face recognition mediated by specific receptor activation","Scene recognition (places, buildings, landscapes)","Color processing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Scene recognition (places, buildings, landscapes)', 'The parahippocampal place area responds selectively to scenes, including places, buildings, and landscapes, supporting spatial context and navigation. Face, object, motion, and color processing belong to the fusiform face area, lateral occipitotemporal cortex, V5/MT, and V4 respectively.'),
('cf79cb97-f6c4-550f-835c-8a562be91f82'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The extrastriate body area (EBA) responds to', '["Images of human bodies and body parts","Objects accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Faces mediated by specific receptor activation","Motion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Scenes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Images of human bodies and body parts', 'The extrastriate body area in lateral occipitotemporal cortex responds selectively to human bodies and body parts, supporting body perception. Faces, objects, motion, and scenes are processed by other specialized extrastriate regions.'),
('4cab8e9d-8d1f-5a99-8d3f-7c38a70569fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Akinetopsia (motion blindness) results from lesion of', '["V2 resulting in decreased functional activity","V4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IT mediated by specific receptor activation","V5/MT (medial temporal / occipitotemporal)","V1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'V5/MT (medial temporal / occipitotemporal)', 'Akinetopsia (motion blindness) follows bilateral damage to V5/MT in the medial temporal/occipitotemporal region, where direction-selective motion neurons reside. V1, V2, V4, and inferior temporal cortex subserve other visual features.'),
('9a130015-cbfa-5e68-8207-3d48a6c8faec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Prosopagnosia (face blindness) from', '["Bilateral occipitotemporal (fusiform face area) lesion","Left temporal lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right temporal lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occipital lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral occipitotemporal (fusiform face area) lesion', 'Prosopagnosia, an inability to recognize familiar faces, results from bilateral occipitotemporal lesions involving the fusiform face area. Unilateral temporal, frontal, or occipital pole lesions do not reliably produce the syndrome.'),
('c9093a5f-862e-53cb-b1cb-77e074137f4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Cerebral achromatopsia (acquired color blindness) from', '["Occipital pole lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","V1 lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","V5 lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventral occipitotemporal (V4/fusiform) lesion","V2 lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral occipitotemporal (V4/fusiform) lesion', 'Cerebral achromatopsia, acquired loss of color vision, follows ventral occipitotemporal damage to V4 and the adjacent fusiform gyrus. Lesions of V1, V2, V5, or the occipital pole spare color processing or produce other visual field or agnosic defects.'),
('c26016aa-a5b9-584f-8120-b98ae4934254'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Simultanagnosia (inability to see whole scene) is from', '["Frontal lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporal lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Parieto-occipital (dorsal, Balint syndrome) or ventral (visual agnosia)","Occipital lesion only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parieto-occipital (dorsal, Balint syndrome) or ventral (visual agnosia)', 'Simultanagnosia, the inability to perceive a whole scene while recognizing its parts, arises from bilateral parieto-occipital damage (Balint syndrome) or from ventral visual agnosia. Unilateral frontal, temporal, cerebellar, or occipital-only lesions do not reproduce this integrative failure.'),
('5e853d3b-9c0c-55f9-a176-d03c5c9443c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Balint syndrome (triad: simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, oculomotor apraxia) from', '["Bilateral dorsal occipitoparietal (superior) lesion","Occipital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporal mediated by specific receptor activation","Unilateral parietal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral dorsal occipitoparietal (superior) lesion', 'Balint syndrome, the triad of simultanagnosia, optic ataxia, and oculomotor apraxia, results from bilateral dorsal occipitoparietal damage. Unilateral or more anterior or inferior lesions do not generate the full triad.'),
('cccbc883-d0ea-5bee-b06e-e37f033d7a87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Optic ataxia (inability to reach under visual guidance) from', '["Occipital resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior parietal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior parietal lobule (SPL, area 7) - dorsal stream reaching","Temporal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior parietal lobule (SPL, area 7) - dorsal stream reaching', 'Optic ataxia, defective reaching under visual guidance despite intact motor strength, follows superior parietal lobule (area 7) damage, a key node of the dorsal reaching stream. Inferior parietal, occipital, frontal, or temporal lesions do not reproduce this visuomotor disconnection.'),
('a57b99ce-391f-5673-841c-d7e8e8ecfc5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Oculomotor apraxia (difficulty initiating voluntary saccades) in Balint involves', '["CN III resulting in decreased functional activity","PPRF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MLF mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal eye field and/or parietal (intraparietal sulcus) damage"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal eye field and/or parietal (intraparietal sulcus) damage', 'In Balint syndrome, difficulty initiating voluntary saccades reflects damage to the frontal eye fields and/or the intraparietal sulcus, which program deliberate gaze shifts. The oculomotor nerves, PPRF, and MLF generate reflex eye movements, not voluntary saccade initiation.'),
('1ad2d3e1-8c39-5dc7-ac92-9135c9ab7b70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Auditory cortex (primary, A1, area 41) is located in', '["Insula resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial temporal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior temporal gyrus (lateral surface) mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior temporal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heschl gyrus (transverse temporal, superior temporal plane, buried in lateral fissure)"]'::jsonb, 'Heschl gyrus (transverse temporal, superior temporal plane, buried in lateral fissure)', 'Primary auditory cortex occupies Heschl''s gyrus on the transverse temporal plane within the lateral (Sylvian) fissure. The insula, medial and inferior temporal regions, and the lateral superior temporal gyrus surface contain association cortex rather than A1.'),
('02ced0ea-c66a-54b0-82d0-f876fdd32828'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The auditory cortex is tonotopically organized:', '["Low posterior, high anterior resulting in decreased functional activity","Low medial, high lateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low frequencies anterior/lateral, high frequencies posterior/medial in Heschl gyrus","Random without affecting the primary tissue pathway","tonotopy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low frequencies anterior/lateral, high frequencies posterior/medial in Heschl gyrus', 'Heschl''s gyrus is tonotopically mapped so low frequencies are represented anteriorly/laterally and high frequencies posteriorly/medially. The reversed and ''random'' options contradict the established cochleotopic gradient.'),
('dc64b523-3687-58c4-ac8f-3ada4226fa10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The planum temporale (posterior to Heschl gyrus) is', '["Larger on left (Wernicke area) — language dominance","Absent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Larger on right mediated by specific receptor activation","Smaller on left without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Equal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Larger on left (Wernicke area) — language dominance', 'The planum temporale, posterior to Heschl''s gyrus, is usually larger on the left and coincides with Wernicke''s area, reflecting language dominance. The other options contradict the typical leftward asymmetry.'),
('90e9be14-eef2-55cd-8179-c545e98c28a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Broca aphasia (non-fluent, agrammatical, but good comprehension) results from', '["Left temporal lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Left inferior frontal gyrus (area 44/45) lesion","Left parietal lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Left thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left occipital lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left inferior frontal gyrus (area 44/45) lesion', 'Broca aphasia (non-fluent, agrammatic output with preserved comprehension) follows damage to the left inferior frontal gyrus (areas 44/45). Temporal, parietal, thalamic, and occipital lesions spare this motor speech program or produce different aphasia types.'),
('edf5e271-f184-5b8e-a6d0-7f8d6152dba8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Wernicke aphasia (fluent, paraphasic, poor comprehension) results from', '["Left posterior superior temporal gyrus (area 22) lesion","Left occipital lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subcortical lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Left parietal lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left frontal lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left posterior superior temporal gyrus (area 22) lesion', 'Wernicke aphasia (fluent, paraphasic speech with impaired comprehension) results from damage to the left posterior superior temporal gyrus (area 22). Frontal, parietal, subcortical, and occipital lesions do not produce this comprehension-dominant syndrome.'),
('23764c63-21d9-5310-b6c6-a10847074155'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Conduction aphasia (poor repetition, fluent, good comprehension) results from', '["Wernicke area resulting in decreased functional activity","Global lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arcuate fasciculus lesion (left temporoparietal)","Broca area without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transcortical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate fasciculus lesion (left temporoparietal)', 'Conduction aphasia, with disproportionately poor repetition despite fluent speech and good comprehension, follows damage to the arcuate fasciculus connecting Wernicke and Broca areas. Isolated damage to Broca, Wernicke, or global perisylvian cortex produces other aphasia patterns.'),
('6e5e3677-e9d6-5e55-bccf-05d5c4b8c6fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Global aphasia (all language functions impaired) from', '["Small temporal resulting in decreased functional activity","Small frontal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subcortical mediated by specific receptor activation","Right hemisphere without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large left perisylvian (fronto-temporo-parietal) lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Large left perisylvian (fronto-temporo-parietal) lesion', 'Global aphasia, impairment of all language functions, results from a large left perisylvian lesion involving both Broca and Wernicke areas. Small frontal, temporal, subcortical, or right-hemisphere lesions spare at least one language domain.'),
('a4ee8367-0921-5f71-947e-691987e5f03e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Transcortical motor aphasia (poor initiation, but intact repetition) from', '["Wernicke area resulting in decreased functional activity","Arcuate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Broca area mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left dorsolateral prefrontal (premotor, area 6/44) — disconnection from Broca"]'::jsonb, 'Left dorsolateral prefrontal (premotor, area 6/44) — disconnection from Broca', 'Transcortical motor aphasia, with sparse spontaneous speech but intact repetition, follows left dorsolateral prefrontal/premotor lesions that disconnect Broca''s area from its initiation centers. Damage to Wernicke, arcuate, Broca, or thalamus produces different language syndromes.'),
('c93a1523-9d1f-5299-88e9-d610b09af612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Transcortical sensory aphasia (poor comprehension, fluent, can repeat) from', '["Arcuate resulting in decreased functional activity","Broca accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wernicke area mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left temporoparietal (watershed between MCA/PCA) — isolation of Wernicke"]'::jsonb, 'Left temporoparietal (watershed between MCA/PCA) — isolation of Wernicke', 'Transcortical sensory aphasia (fluent speech, poor comprehension, preserved repetition) results from a left temporoparietal watershed infarct that isolates Wernicke''s area. Arcuate, Broca, and isolated Wernicke damage produce conduction, Broca, and Wernicke aphasia respectively.'),
('7d07bb4c-1c55-508c-9e41-c78ad35eb389'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Mixed transcortical aphasia (only repetition spared) from', '["Broca area resulting in decreased functional activity","Large watershed (frontal-parietal-temporal) — isolation of speech area","Global mediated by specific receptor activation","Conduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wernicke area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large watershed (frontal-parietal-temporal) — isolation of speech area', 'Mixed transcortical aphasia spares only repetition because a large watershed infarct isolates the perisylvian speech area from surrounding cortex. Broca, Wernicke, global, and conduction lesions spare or impair different language components.'),
('d3b1a945-0d5d-55a1-8e86-2b6573343934'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Anomic aphasia (word-finding difficulty only) from', '["Frontal resulting in decreased functional activity","Wernicke area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Global mediated by specific receptor activation","Broca area without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left temporal (middle temporal) or angular gyrus — mildest aphasia"]'::jsonb, 'Left temporal (middle temporal) or angular gyrus — mildest aphasia', 'Anomic aphasia, marked mainly by word-finding difficulty, follows left middle temporal or angular gyrus lesions and is the mildest aphasia. Broca, Wernicke, frontal, and global lesions produce additional motor or comprehension deficits.'),
('c9736e6f-7b20-590f-8b10-2dc0f198c5b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Pure word deafness (auditory verbal agnosia) from', '["Left temporal pole resulting in decreased functional activity","Wernicke area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bilateral or left superior temporal (unilateral: left) — cannot understand spoken words","Frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right temporal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral or left superior temporal (unilateral: left) — cannot understand spoken words', 'Pure word deafness, inability to understand spoken words with intact hearing and other language functions, results from bilateral superior temporal lesions (or a unilateral left one). The temporal pole, frontal, right temporal, and Wernicke area lesions produce other deficits.'),
('ec9aed89-4374-567f-add1-22e914ba214a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Pure alexia (alexia without agraphia) from', '["Broca area resulting in decreased functional activity","Angular gyrus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left occipital + splenium of corpus callosum — cannot read but can write","Left frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wernicke area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left occipital + splenium of corpus callosum — cannot read but can write', 'Pure alexia (alexia without agraphia) follows a left occipital infarct plus splenial damage that prevents visual word information from reaching the left angular gyrus; patients can write but not read. Angular gyrus, Broca, Wernicke, and frontal lesions cause other reading or writing disorders.'),
('3f33a34f-1e56-57a6-aad3-a16a9aaf9df7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Agraphia (impaired writing) can occur with', '["Only frontal lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parietal lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only temporal lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Angular gyrus lesion (Gerstmann) or any aphasia","Only occipital lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Angular gyrus lesion (Gerstmann) or any aphasia', 'Agraphia, impaired writing, accompanies angular gyrus lesions (as part of Gerstmann syndrome) and most aphasias, since writing depends on language networks. No single lobe lesion produces it, so options restricting it to one lobe are incomplete.'),
('8d025206-0248-56b0-890b-40678f87edf3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Aprosodia (impaired emotional prosody) from', '["Left frontal resulting in decreased functional activity","Left temporal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Right hemisphere (right frontal/parietal) lesion","Left parietal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right hemisphere (right frontal/parietal) lesion', 'Aprosodia, loss of emotional intonation in speech, follows right hemisphere (especially right frontal/parietal) damage, the non-dominant homolog of language areas. Left-hemisphere lesions affect propositional language rather than emotional prosody.'),
('40fd8fa1-2437-5bc4-a5cb-13b4d4aaec78'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The insula is involved in speech production (apraxia of speech) from', '["Bilateral insula resulting in decreased functional activity","Left posterior insula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right insula mediated by specific receptor activation","Left anterior insula (precentral gyrus of insula) — motor planning of speech","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left anterior insula (precentral gyrus of insula) — motor planning of speech', 'Apraxia of speech, impaired motor planning of articulation, is linked to left anterior insula damage involving the precentral gyrus of the insula. Posterior, right, or bilateral insular lesions do not reproduce this speech-planning deficit.'),
('3b6aef13-6cc2-5134-95af-8f88299619f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The supplementary motor area (SMA) is involved in', '["Speech comprehension resulting in decreased functional activity","Writing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Speech initiation (transcortical motor aphasia when damaged)","Naming without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reading leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Speech initiation (transcortical motor aphasia when damaged)', 'The supplementary motor area initiates volitional speech; its damage produces impaired speech initiation, as in transcortical motor aphasia. Comprehension, naming, writing, and reading depend on other cortical networks.'),
('b743f6d7-e84b-59b7-a3f0-580068b9d660'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The motor homunculus maps body parts on', '["Postcentral gyrus resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Precentral gyrus (area 4) — disproportionate representation (hand, face large)","Parietal lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Precentral gyrus (area 4) — disproportionate representation (hand, face large)', 'The motor homunculus maps the body on the precentral gyrus (area 4) with disproportionate cortical space for the hand and face, reflecting fine motor control. The postcentral gyrus, temporal, parietal, and frontal association regions do not contain this motor map.'),
('c7ec69a6-7bf4-55b3-91fe-e557e15f729b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The sensory homunculus maps body parts on', '["Temporal lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Precentral gyrus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Postcentral gyrus (areas 3,1,2) — largest areas for fingers, lips, tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Postcentral gyrus (areas 3,1,2) — largest areas for fingers, lips, tongue', 'The sensory homunculus maps the body on the postcentral gyrus (areas 3, 1, 2), with the largest representations for fingers, lips, and tongue. The precentral gyrus, temporal, occipital, and frontal lobes contain no such somatosensory map.'),
('39d7d992-2323-5a4a-bf96-7926c0a2c3b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The primary somatosensory cortex is organized by', '["organization resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Random accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only layers mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatotopy (medial = leg, lateral = face) — columnar, with area 3a (proprioception), 3b (cutaneous), 1 (texture), 2 (shape)","Functional columns leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatotopy (medial = leg, lateral = face) — columnar, with area 3a (proprioception), 3b (cutaneous), 1 (texture), 2 (shape)', 'Primary somatosensory cortex is somatotopically organized in vertical columns, with the leg medial and the face lateral, and with subdivisions 3a (proprioception), 3b (cutaneous), 1 (texture), and 2 (shape). ''Random,'' ''only layers,'' and ''functional columns'' alone ignore this ordered body map.'),
('ebcdc762-6e1f-5224-a772-fba858cfa147'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The secondary somatosensory cortex (SII, parietal operculum/insula) processes', '["Only motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilateral tactile and pain information, higher-order tactile learning","Only vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Only contralateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ipsilateral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral tactile and pain information, higher-order tactile learning', 'The secondary somatosensory cortex (SII), in the parietal operculum and insula, receives bilateral tactile and pain input and supports higher-order tactile learning. Motor, purely ipsilateral, purely contralateral, or vision-only descriptions ignore its bilateral somatosensory function.'),
('b256d746-0628-53fb-bdc0-416defe4c0ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The parietal operculum (OP1-4) includes the secondary somatosensory (SII) area and', '["Pain processing (insula/dorsal posterior insula), vestibular cortex","Only pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vestibular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only touch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain processing (insula/dorsal posterior insula), vestibular cortex', 'The parietal operculum (OP1-4) houses SII along with the dorsal posterior insula (pain) and vestibular cortex, reflecting its multimodal sensory role. Options limiting it to pain, motor, vestibular, or touch alone are incomplete.'),
('4f4e81b8-b1b2-565b-a910-6e83c7acfef2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) is part of the', '["Motor loop resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual loop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Executive circuit (DLPFC → caudate → GPi → MD/VA → DLPFC)","Oculomotor loop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Limbic loop leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Executive circuit (DLPFC → caudate → GPi → MD/VA → DLPFC)', 'The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex participates in the executive (prefrontal) basal ganglia circuit, projecting to the caudate and returning via GPi to MD/VA thalamus. The motor, oculomotor, and limbic loops involve different cortical origins and striatal territories.'),
('902f590f-b7bd-545f-9fb5-75dba671d7be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The orbitofrontal (OFC) circuit involves', '["OFC → ventral striatum → ventral pallidum → MD thalamus → OFC (emotional/reward)","Visual loop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cognitive loop mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor loop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oculomotor loop leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'OFC → ventral striatum → ventral pallidum → MD thalamus → OFC (emotional/reward)', 'The orbitofrontal circuit is one of the basal ganglia-thalamocortical loops, running from OFC to ventral striatum, ventral pallidum, and mediodorsal (MD) thalamus and back to OFC, serving emotional and reward processing. The motor, oculomotor, cognitive (DLPFC), and visual loops use different cortical origins and separate striatal/thalamic relays.'),
('a0a7e571-1f0e-5bae-b1ef-423a1a510825'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The anterior cingulate (ACC) circuit involves', '["ACC → ventral striatum → ventral pallidum → MD thalamus → ACC (motivation, conflict)","Motor loop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","OFC loop mediated by specific receptor activation","Oculomotor loop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","DLPFC loop leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ACC → ventral striatum → ventral pallidum → MD thalamus → ACC (motivation, conflict)', 'The anterior cingulate loop links ACC to ventral striatum, ventral pallidum, and mediodorsal thalamus, mediating motivation and conflict monitoring. The motor, oculomotor, orbitofrontal, and dorsolateral prefrontal loops traverse separate circuits.'),
('9c8aa6b6-69e5-5638-a47e-5760f3e87b5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The cortical motor areas consist of', '["M1 (area 4), premotor (area 6 lateral), SMA (area 6 medial), CMA (cingulate, area 24), FEF (area 8)","Only M1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only M1 + SMA mediated by specific receptor activation","Only SMA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Premotor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'M1 (area 4), premotor (area 6 lateral), SMA (area 6 medial), CMA (cingulate, area 24), FEF (area 8)', 'Cortical motor areas comprise primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4), lateral premotor and medial supplementary motor area (both area 6), the cingulate motor area (area 24), and the frontal eye field (area 8). Listing only one or two of these regions omits the motor network.'),
('df8779f7-95b7-522f-9736-f8a41e783b3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The mirror neuron system (premotor, IPL) is involved in', '["Only emotion resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor execution accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only language mediated by specific receptor activation","Action understanding, imitation, empathy, language evolution","Only action perception leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Action understanding, imitation, empathy, language evolution', 'Mirror neurons discharge both when performing an action and when observing the same action, providing a neural basis for action understanding, imitation, empathy, and theories of language evolution. Their role extends well beyond a single specific function such as emotion, execution, or perception alone.'),
('bad39112-25d0-5871-b852-045e9f976325'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The default mode network (DMN) includes', '["Visual cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior cingulate, precuneus, medial prefrontal, angular gyrus, hippocampus","Frontal eye field leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cingulate, precuneus, medial prefrontal, angular gyrus, hippocampus', 'The default mode network is most active during internally directed, task-free rest and comprises the posterior cingulate, precuneus, medial prefrontal cortex, angular gyrus, and hippocampus. Visual, sensory, and motor cortices and the frontal eye field belong to other functional networks active during task performance.'),
('de7597fc-e3b9-544b-97a3-59cd14c98eba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The salience network (SN) includes', '["Anterior insula and anterior cingulate (ACC) — detects salient stimuli","Visual cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Precuneus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior cingulate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior insula and anterior cingulate (ACC) — detects salient stimuli', 'The salience network uses the anterior insula and anterior cingulate cortex as its core hubs to detect behaviorally relevant (salient) stimuli and direct attention. Visual and motor cortices and the precuneus/posterior cingulate are components of other networks.'),
('a34d7472-8f03-5101-8af8-17ba5e3fdc6c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The frontoparietal (executive) network includes', '["OFC resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior insula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DLPFC, IPL (intraparietal sulcus) — working memory, attention, problem-solving","PCC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DLPFC, IPL (intraparietal sulcus) — working memory, attention, problem-solving', 'The frontoparietal executive network centers on the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and the intraparietal sulcus/inferior parietal lobule, supporting working memory, attention, and problem-solving. Orbitofrontal cortex, anterior insula, posterior cingulate, and hippocampus are hubs of other networks.'),
('1323da0d-cc18-5c49-9df6-8cd86ab9c1f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The sensorimotor network includes', '["Primary motor (M1), primary somatosensory (S1), premotor, SMA, putamen, thalamus (VL)","Hippocampus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal eye field leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor (M1), primary somatosensory (S1), premotor, SMA, putamen, thalamus (VL)', 'The sensorimotor network comprises primary motor (M1) and primary somatosensory (S1) cortices, premotor area, SMA, putamen, and VL thalamus, coordinating voluntary movement. Hippocampus, amygdala, cerebellum, and the frontal eye field belong to other systems.'),
('68e5980c-802a-52a5-b757-7511f3864084'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The visual network includes', '["Temporal pole resulting in decreased functional activity","Insula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary visual (V1, calcarine) and visual association (V2-V5) occipitotemporal","Frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parietal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary visual (V1, calcarine) and visual association (V2-V5) occipitotemporal', 'The visual network centers on primary visual cortex (V1, around the calcarine sulcus) and occipitotemporal visual association areas (V2-V5). The temporal pole, insula, and undifferentiated frontal or parietal regions do not constitute the visual network.'),
('54429d62-f605-5321-942e-e9b9c190f8ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The auditory network includes', '["Visual areas resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor areas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal areas mediated by specific receptor activation","Prefrontal areas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary auditory (Heschl gyrus) and auditory association (planum temporale, STG)"]'::jsonb, 'Primary auditory (Heschl gyrus) and auditory association (planum temporale, STG)', 'The auditory network occupies the superior temporal lobe, with primary auditory cortex on the Heschl gyrus and association areas in the planum temporale and superior temporal gyrus. Visual, motor, parietal, and prefrontal regions serve unrelated modalities.'),
('12d33072-0aaf-50b1-8482-b625c4fff0a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The limbic network includes', '["Sensory areas resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor areas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior cingulate, ventromedial prefrontal, amygdala, hippocampus, mammillary bodies","Visual areas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory areas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior cingulate, ventromedial prefrontal, amygdala, hippocampus, mammillary bodies', 'The limbic network links the anterior cingulate, ventromedial prefrontal cortex, amygdala, hippocampus, and mammillary bodies to support emotion, memory, and motivation. Primary sensory, motor, visual, and auditory regions belong to modality-specific networks.'),
('e15ad2af-0c0d-5cf4-9aff-e5e5dc3d9760'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The parietal memory network (PMN) includes', '["Motor memory resulting in decreased functional activity","Only spatial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Precuneus, posterior cingulate, angular gyrus — episodic memory retrieval","Working memory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visuospatial attention leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Precuneus, posterior cingulate, angular gyrus — episodic memory retrieval', 'The parietal memory network comprises the precuneus, posterior cingulate, and angular gyrus and is engaged during episodic memory retrieval. Working memory, visuospatial attention, and motor memory rely on different circuits.'),
('8325ba4e-854b-5189-bb51-7221964af371'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The white matter of the cerebrum includes', '["Only corpus callosum resulting in decreased functional activity","Only corona radiata accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only internal capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","Only fornix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Corona radiata, corpus callosum, internal capsule, fornix, association bundles"]'::jsonb, 'Corona radiata, corpus callosum, internal capsule, fornix, association bundles', 'Cerebral white matter contains projection fibers (corona radiata, internal capsule), commissural fibers (corpus callosum), the fornix, and association bundles. The options listing a single bundle omit the other fiber systems.'),
('a011ff5c-3ec2-5f3a-89a2-edc42b7ff974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The centrum semiovale is the', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Gray matter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","White matter at the base of hemisphere mediated by specific receptor activation","Central white matter of the cerebral hemisphere (above corpus callosum, under cortex)","Basal ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central white matter of the cerebral hemisphere (above corpus callosum, under cortex)', 'The centrum semiovale is the large mass of white matter in the core of each cerebral hemisphere, positioned above the corpus callosum and beneath the cortex. It is neither a gray matter mass nor the deep gray nuclei.'),
('5a31715c-c5cd-5e62-8a25-ef37a802cca5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The corona radiata consists of', '["Association fibers only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sensory fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Commissural fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamocortical and corticofugal fibers spreading from internal capsule to cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamocortical and corticofugal fibers spreading from internal capsule to cortex', 'The corona radiata is the fan of projection fibers (thalamocortical and corticofugal) that radiates outward from the internal capsule toward the cortex. It carries both sensory and motor pathways rather than association or commissural fibers alone.'),
('ae811369-a9f7-504a-89f9-5272eed8ebd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Cortical thickness varies; primary motor cortex has', '["All layers equal resulting in decreased functional activity","layer V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Agranular cortex (layer IV absent/poor) — Betz cells in layer V","Granular cortex (prominent layer IV) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","layer IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Agranular cortex (layer IV absent/poor) — Betz cells in layer V', 'Primary motor cortex is agranular: layer IV (granular layer) is absent or poorly developed, and giant Betz cells lie in layer V. Sensory regions, by contrast, show a prominent granular layer IV.'),
('652f5e9f-e245-5470-a45f-717ce4e7d60c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Primary sensory cortex (auditory, visual, somatosensory) has', '["Granular cortex (well-developed layer IV — receiving thalamic input)","layer V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","layer IV mediated by specific receptor activation","layer VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Agranular cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granular cortex (well-developed layer IV — receiving thalamic input)', 'Primary sensory areas are granular (koniocortex): layer IV is thick and well developed because it receives the specific thalamocortical afferents. Agranular cortex and the individual layer alternatives describe motor territory rather than sensory cortex.'),
('6a5fec55-9896-5782-a947-56ce7f566dad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Prefrontal cortex is', '["layer V resulting in decreased functional activity","Granular (eugranular, layer IV present) — six-layered isocortex","layer IV mediated by specific receptor activation","layer VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Agranular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Granular (eugranular, layer IV present) — six-layered isocortex', 'Prefrontal cortex is eugranular (granular) homotypical isocortex in which all six layers, including layer IV, are present. It is not agranular like motor cortex, nor is it defined by a single cortical layer.'),
('869c24f7-29c3-56f3-9a42-ebacd970beb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Betz cells are found in which layer of the motor cortex?', '["Layer II resulting in decreased functional activity","Layer IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Layer V (internal pyramidal)","Layer III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Layer VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Layer V (internal pyramidal)', 'Betz cells are the giant pyramidal neurons located in layer V (internal pyramidal layer) of primary motor cortex; their axons form the corticospinal tract. Layers II, III, IV, and VI lack these characteristic giants.'),
('e40717c0-673f-59d5-ac46-5d5a7fd692d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Layer IV of (sensory) cortex receives', '["Corticocortical afferents resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor output accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Commissural afferents mediated by specific receptor activation","Brainstem afferents without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamocortical afferents (from specific thalamic nuclei)"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamocortical afferents (from specific thalamic nuclei)', 'Layer IV is the main input layer of neocortex and receives the specific thalamocortical afferents. Motor output leaves via layer V, while association and commissural connections primarily involve layers II-III and V.'),
('3f50fd53-a959-5e33-b3de-4a0f3f857e14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Layer III pyramidal neurons send', '["Subcortical projections resulting in decreased functional activity","Association (ipsilateral) and commissural (callosal) projections","Sensory input mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticothalamic feedback without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor output leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Association (ipsilateral) and commissural (callosal) projections', 'Supragranular pyramidal cells in layer III give rise to association (ipsilateral cortical) and commissural (callosal) projections. Subcortical outflow, corticothalamic feedback, and motor output arise from deeper layers.'),
('bcfc10ec-45b7-5a0c-b983-3ce9e51a092b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Layer VI neurons project to', '["Cerebral cortex (association)","Spinal cord","Thalamus (corticothalamic feedback)","Cerebellum","Basal ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus (corticothalamic feedback)', 'Layer VI pyramidal neurons send corticothalamic fibers back to the thalamus, providing feedback to the specific relay nuclei. Corticospinal/spinal projections originate from layer V, and cortical association projections from layers II-III.'),
('59753157-2d19-5dd7-be00-c16e87887c90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Layer V neurons project to', '["Other cortical areas resulting in decreased functional activity","Subcortical targets: striatum, brainstem, spinal cord (corticofugal)","Callosal fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All layers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subcortical targets: striatum, brainstem, spinal cord (corticofugal)', 'Layer V is the principal output layer: its pyramidal and Betz cells send corticofugal fibers to the striatum, brainstem, and spinal cord. Commissural fibers arise from layer III and corticothalamic fibers from layer VI.'),
('d441d641-330c-5f13-b4e4-1ced554c451c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampal formation consists of', '["Subiculum only resulting in decreased functional activity","Dentate gyrus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus proper (CA1-4), dentate gyrus, subiculum, presubiculum, parasubiculum, entorhinal cortex","Hippocampus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CA1-4 only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus proper (CA1-4), dentate gyrus, subiculum, presubiculum, parasubiculum, entorhinal cortex', 'The hippocampal formation encompasses the hippocampus proper (CA1-CA4), dentate gyrus, subiculum, presubiculum, parasubiculum, and the adjoining entorhinal cortex. Naming only one component omits the rest of the formation.'),
('b825d682-f49b-5c37-b76a-ae50b2910a8a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Granule cells of the dentate gyrus project their axons (mossy fibers) to', '["Entorhinal cortex","Subiculum","CA1 pyramidal cells","CA3 pyramidal cells","Septal nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'CA3 pyramidal cells', 'Dentate granule cells give rise to unmyelinated mossy fibers that synapse on CA3 pyramidal neurons in the classical trisynaptic circuit. The entorhinal cortex, subiculum, and CA1 lie elsewhere in the pathway.'),
('d2e78f9b-5486-5df9-88ab-db30cf4992d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Mossy fibers make synapses on CA3 that are', '["Small boutons resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibitory synapses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Large boutons (giant synapses, 3-5 µm) with multiple active zones","GABAergic synapses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Single active zones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large boutons (giant synapses, 3-5 µm) with multiple active zones', 'Mossy fibers terminate on CA3 dendrites as giant boutons, up to 3-5 µm, glutamatergic synapses containing multiple release sites. They are excitatory and structurally distinctive rather than small or inhibitory.'),
('53333836-2de7-538f-9170-735817901d57'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'CA3 pyramidal neurons project to', '["Entorhinal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Subiculum directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CA1 via Schaffer collaterals, and to contralateral CA3 via associational/commissural fibers","Amygdala without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dentate gyrus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CA1 via Schaffer collaterals, and to contralateral CA3 via associational/commissural fibers', 'CA3 pyramidal cells send Schaffer collaterals to CA1 ipsilaterally and associational/commissural fibers to the contralateral CA3. Entorhinal cortex, subiculum, amygdala, and dentate gyrus are not the direct projections of CA3.'),
('7f975234-27c0-59b7-ab00-c8f000f2bfe1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'CA1 pyramidal neurons project to', '["Subiculum and deep entorhinal layers (main hippocampal output)","Dentate gyrus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CA3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amygdala leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subiculum and deep entorhinal layers (main hippocampal output)', 'CA1 neurons constitute the main output stage of the hippocampus, projecting to the subiculum and the deep layers (V/VI) of entorhinal cortex. The dentate gyrus and CA3 lie earlier in the trisynaptic circuit.'),
('149adbce-da6a-57c6-ac9a-3e344d3369fc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The subiculum projects to', '["Only entorhinal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only amygdala accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fornix mediated by specific receptor activation","Only prefrontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Entorhinal cortex, fornix (mammillary, septal), prefrontal cortex, amygdala, nucleus accumbens"]'::jsonb, 'Entorhinal cortex, fornix (mammillary, septal), prefrontal cortex, amygdala, nucleus accumbens', 'The subiculum distributes hippocampal output widely, to entorhinal cortex, the fornix (mammillary bodies and septal nuclei), prefrontal cortex, amygdala, and nucleus accumbens. Its projections are not restricted to a single target.'),
('685aec2e-f242-53c8-b5e8-8a5d60f29962'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The entorhinal cortex layer II projects to', '["Dentate gyrus (perforant path) and CA3","Parahippocampal cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subiculum mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CA1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate gyrus (perforant path) and CA3', 'Entorhinal layer II stellate cells give rise to the perforant path, the main cortical input to the hippocampus, targeting dentate granule cells and CA3. Layer III, by contrast, projects separately to CA1 and subiculum.'),
('662f1eef-f841-57d0-ae9c-2b43c0144c20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The entorhinal cortex layer III projects to', '["CA1 and subiculum (temporoammonic path)","Dentate gyrus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CA3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Perirhinal cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subiculum only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CA1 and subiculum (temporoammonic path)', 'Entorhinal layer III neurons form the temporoammonic (perforant) pathway to CA1 and the subiculum. Layer II projects instead to the dentate gyrus and CA3, while perirhinal cortex is a separate adjacent region.'),
('4443df47-1511-599f-a41b-6cdb5e88ea91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The entorhinal cortex layer V/VI receives', '["Input from basal ganglia resulting in decreased functional activity","Input from sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Input from thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Hippocampal (subiculum/CA1) output and projects back to neocortex","Input from amygdala leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampal (subiculum/CA1) output and projects back to neocortex', 'The deep layers (V/VI) of entorhinal cortex receive subicular and CA1 output and send reciprocal projections back to the neocortex, completing the parahippocampal memory loop. Basal ganglia, sensory cortex, and amygdala input are not the defining afferents.'),
('2aad62b6-d2dc-5f44-86d5-0fb12458ea9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The perirhinal cortex (area 35/36) is essential for', '["Motor learning resulting in decreased functional activity","Fear conditioning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Language mediated by specific receptor activation","Spatial memory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Object recognition memory (especially visual)"]'::jsonb, 'Object recognition memory (especially visual)', 'Perirhinal cortex (Brodmann areas 35/36) lies at the apex of the ventral visual stream and is critical for object recognition memory, especially visual. Fear conditioning, spatial memory, motor learning, and language depend on other structures.'),
('b4404733-3a86-5c33-b160-b042744415a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The parahippocampal cortex (area TF-TH) is essential for', '["Object recognition resulting in decreased functional activity","Emotion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fear conditioning mediated by specific receptor activation","Language without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spatial memory (context, scene recognition) — ''where'' pathway to hippocampus"]'::jsonb, 'Spatial memory (context, scene recognition) — ''where'' pathway to hippocampus', 'Parahippocampal cortex (areas TF-TH) processes spatial/contextual information and scene recognition, forming the ''where'' pathway into the hippocampus. Object recognition is served by perirhinal cortex, and emotion/fear by the amygdala.'),
('42eda2d1-a545-56a3-a348-403f2170d148'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Temporal lobe epilepsy often originates from', '["Neocortical temporal resulting in decreased functional activity","All temporal areas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Entorhinal cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampus (mesial temporal sclerosis, CA1/CA3)"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus (mesial temporal sclerosis, CA1/CA3)', 'Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy typically originates in the hippocampus, where mesial temporal sclerosis and selective neuronal loss in CA1 and CA3 act as the epileptogenic focus. Neocortex, amygdala, and entorhinal cortex are less commonly the primary origin.'),
('66d4574d-d46f-5c57-a6b2-8e47032b56cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Mesial temporal sclerosis (MTS) shows', '["Cerebellar atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Hippocampal atrophy, neuronal loss (CA1, CA4), and gliosis on MRI","change mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal ganglia atrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortical atrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampal atrophy, neuronal loss (CA1, CA4), and gliosis on MRI', 'Mesial temporal sclerosis is characterized on MRI by hippocampal atrophy with neuron loss (prominent in CA1/CA4) and gliosis. Cerebellar, basal ganglia, and diffuse cortical atrophy are unrelated patterns.'),
('2380e663-93ff-5aac-9185-b2452b50404e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The claustrum has been implicated in', '["known function resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor control only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Language only mediated by specific receptor activation","Consciousness, salience, and multisensory integration (speculative)","Sensory processing only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Consciousness, salience, and multisensory integration (speculative)', 'Because the claustrum is reciprocally connected with nearly all cortical areas, it has been proposed (speculatively) to participate in consciousness, salience, and multisensory integration. Its role is broad and not confined to motor, language, or sensory processing alone.'),
('b52691c7-a48e-574a-8792-5d895cde387b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The claustrum is divided into', '["Dorsal (sensorimotor) and ventral (limbic) parts","Medial and lateral","Cortical and subcortical","Superior and inferior","Anterior and posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal (sensorimotor) and ventral (limbic) parts', 'The claustrum is usually divided into a dorsal part linked with sensorimotor cortices and a ventral part connected with limbic areas. Medial/lateral, superior/inferior, and anterior/posterior divisions do not describe its structural organization.'),
('2d6c0055-1253-5a95-b936-28f9a2e0ab93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The extreme capsule (ventral language pathway) connects', '["Frontal with parietal resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporal with occipital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal with temporal mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal (Broca/IFG) with temporal (Wernicke/MTG) via extreme capsule and uncinate","Frontal with occipital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal (Broca/IFG) with temporal (Wernicke/MTG) via extreme capsule and uncinate', 'The ventral language pathway runs beneath the cortex through the extreme capsule (and uncinate fasciculus), connecting Broca''s region/frontal lobe with Wernicke''s region/middle temporal gyrus. Other lobe-to-lobe combinations do not describe this ventral stream.'),
('12309ec2-0c8c-5c97-b1cb-37ba0145aa9f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The superior longitudinal fasciculus II (SLF II) is involved in', '["Language resulting in decreased functional activity","Spatial attention (parieto-frontal) — dorsal attention network","Motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Emotion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Memory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spatial attention (parieto-frontal) — dorsal attention network', 'SLF II connects parietal with frontal cortex within the dorsal attention network and supports spatial attention. Language is mediated by SLF III/arcuate fasciculus and the ventral stream, while emotion and memory involve other tracts.'),
('9bbd828e-31de-515e-ac0e-9230419d5b56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The superior longitudinal fasciculus III (SLF III) is involved in', '["Audition resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Language (ventral premotor → supramarginal gyrus; part of phonological loop)","Motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spatial attention leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Language (ventral premotor → supramarginal gyrus; part of phonological loop)', 'SLF III connects ventral premotor cortex to the supramarginal gyrus and is part of the phonological loop supporting language. Audition, vision, spatial attention, and motor functions map to other fiber systems.'),
('9e2d0bfd-8c76-5161-9bc0-1bcc0f7ab1f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The inferior fronto-occipital fasciculus (IFOF) connects', '["Only parietal resulting in decreased functional activity","Only frontal to temporal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only temporal to occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Only frontal to occipital without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal lobe to occipital/temporal (ventral stream, semantic)"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal lobe to occipital/temporal (ventral stream, semantic)', 'The IFOF is a long ventral association tract linking the frontal lobe with occipital and temporal regions, contributing to the ventral (semantic) pathway. Its connections span occipital and temporal cortex, not parietal cortex alone.'),
('fda6a6c7-4fbf-57f9-94eb-d53371f9e3e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hippocampus plays a critical role in which type of memory?', '["Sensory memory resulting in decreased functional activity","Declarative (explicit) memory — episodic and semantic","Implicit memory mediated by specific receptor activation","Procedural memory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Working memory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Declarative (explicit) memory — episodic and semantic', 'The hippocampus is essential for forming declarative (explicit) memories, both episodic and semantic. Procedural, implicit, and short-term working memory depend on the striatum, cerebellum, and prefrontal circuits rather than the hippocampus.'),
('98bc702c-7387-5d8c-b776-0373776bb62e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The fornix (precommissural) projects to', '["Thalamus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior thalamus only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amygdala only mediated by specific receptor activation","Mammillary bodies only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial septal nucleus, diagonal band, nucleus accumbens, lateral hypothalamus, orbitofrontal"]'::jsonb, 'Medial septal nucleus, diagonal band, nucleus accumbens, lateral hypothalamus, orbitofrontal', 'Precommissural fornix fibers travel rostral to the anterior commissure to innervate the medial septal nucleus, diagonal band, nucleus accumbens, lateral hypothalamus, and orbitofrontal cortex. Postcommissural fibers, in contrast, reach the mammillary bodies and anterior thalamus.'),
('547ebb43-6b41-5495-9fcd-63efb7f5d4bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The hypothalamus (mammillary bodies) → anterior thalamus tract is the', '["Medial forebrain bundle resulting in decreased functional activity","Mammillothalamic tract (bundle of Vicq d''Azyr)","Fornix mediated by specific receptor activation","Mammillotegmental tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stria terminalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillothalamic tract (bundle of Vicq d''Azyr)', 'The mammillothalamic tract (bundle of Vicq d''Azyr) carries fibers from the mammillary bodies to the anterior thalamic nucleus. The mammillotegmental tract descends to the midbrain, and the fornix runs in the opposite direction from the hippocampus.'),
('7053e9bc-f8b8-566e-9f0a-8c98b7294b0b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The mammillotegmental tract projects from', '["Mammillary bodies to midbrain tegmental nuclei","Thalamus to cortex","Hippocampus to mammillary","Anterior thalamus to cingulate","Hypothalamus to septum"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies to midbrain tegmental nuclei', 'The mammillotegmental tract descends from the mammillary bodies to the tegmental nuclei of the midbrain. The other listed pairs describe different limbic fibers: mammillothalamic, fornix, or mammillary projections to more rostral structures.'),
('c651b02f-353f-55d2-ba22-9ebe62336f30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The stria terminalis (dorsal amygdala output) runs along', '["Optic tract resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior commissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corpus callosum mediated by specific receptor activation","Fornix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamostriate groove (between caudate and thalamus) to hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamostriate groove (between caudate and thalamus) to hypothalamus', 'The stria terminalis courses in the groove between the caudate nucleus and thalamus to reach the hypothalamus, representing the dorsal output pathway of the amygdala. It does not follow the optic tract, anterior commissure, corpus callosum, or fornix.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('26e324fb-08c3-5274-a48a-36b3dde8544a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The ventral amygdalofugal pathway (ventral amygdala output) goes to', '["Hypothalamus, MD thalamus, brainstem (central amygdala output)","Only hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only MD thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only brainstem without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus, MD thalamus, brainstem (central amygdala output)', 'The ventral amygdalofugal pathway takes a short route to the hypothalamus, mediodorsal thalamus, and brainstem (including the central nucleus of the amygdala projections). Its targets are multiple rather than a single structure.'),
('927f8f0d-063d-57ab-84cd-4c1e4b4f3aa2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The medial forebrain bundle (MFB) carries reward-related fibers from', '["Amygdala to hypothalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Hippocampus to mammillary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus to cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","VTA/SN to striatum, nucleus accumbens, prefrontal cortex, amygdala","Cortex to striatum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VTA/SN to striatum, nucleus accumbens, prefrontal cortex, amygdala', 'The medial forebrain bundle traverses the lateral hypothalamus and carries ascending dopaminergic fibers from the ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra to the striatum, nucleus accumbens, prefrontal cortex, and amygdala, mediating reward. It does not connect amygdala to hypothalamus, hippocampus to mammillary bodies, or cortex to striatum.'),
('4c2238c6-de1b-5d6f-a17a-d80f6ecd51b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The dorsal longitudinal fasciculus (DLF, Schütz) connects', '["Amygdala to brainstem resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortex to brainstem accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus to brainstem mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus to hypothalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothalamus (and periaqueductal gray) to brainstem autonomic nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus (and periaqueductal gray) to brainstem autonomic nuclei', 'The dorsal longitudinal fasciculus (Schütz) runs within the periaqueductal gray from the hypothalamus to autonomic nuclei of the brainstem. It does not carry the amygdala-hypothalamus (stria terminalis/ventral amygdalofugal) or hippocampal outputs.'),
('94b5c639-2900-523e-8a58-350aaea48b4d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The periaqueductal gray (PAG) is involved in', '["Only fear resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain modulation (descending), autonomic control, defensive behavior, vocalization","Only autonomic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain modulation (descending), autonomic control, defensive behavior, vocalization', 'The midbrain periaqueductal gray is an integrating hub for descending pain modulation, autonomic control, defensive/fear behavior, and vocalization, relaying to the rostral ventromedial medulla and dorsal horn. Options that assign it a single function (pain, fear, autonomic, or motor alone) understate its multifaceted role.'),
('56bf457d-7eb1-5c50-95dc-cbad37d472bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The mammillary bodies are part of the', '["Limbic cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior hypothalamus (memory and spatial orientation)","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior hypothalamus (memory and spatial orientation)', 'The paired mammillary bodies are nuclei of the posterior hypothalamus that receive the fornix and project to the anterior thalamus, linking memory and spatial orientation. They are neither cortical, basal ganglionic, nor midbrain structures; although the limbic circuit uses them, they themselves lie in the hypothalamus.'),
('ca4a1cb8-43c3-5468-8dba-394220ac0463'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Mammillary body lesions occur in', '["Huntington disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Parkinson disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thiamine deficiency (Wernicke encephalopathy) → Korsakoff amnesia","Stroke without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alzheimer disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine deficiency (Wernicke encephalopathy) → Korsakoff amnesia', 'Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency selectively damages the mammillary bodies and medial dorsal thalamus in Wernicke encephalopathy, and the residual amnestic state is Korsakoff syndrome. Huntington, Parkinson, and typical stroke or Alzheimer pathology spare these nuclei preferentially.'),
('08254cc4-61e5-5748-8ef1-10f23e3e8895'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The mammillary bodies are often atrophied in', '["Korsakoff syndrome","Alzheimer disease","Frontotemporal dementia","Parkinson disease","Normal aging"]'::jsonb, 'Korsakoff syndrome', 'Chronic thiamine deficiency leads to marked atrophy of the mammillary bodies, a hallmark neuropathologic finding in Korsakoff syndrome. Alzheimer, frontotemporal dementia, and Parkinson disease do not characteristically target these hypothalamic nuclei, and normal aging does not produce this pattern.'),
('2cf58ad1-9af6-550c-8ee3-1e5c60766d19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The Papez circuit (memory/emotion):', '["Cerebellum → red nucleus → thalamus → cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Amygdala → hypothalamus → cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus → fornix → mammillary → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal → hippocampus","Hippocampus → fornix → septum → hippocampus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cortex → striatum → pallidum → thalamus → cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus → fornix → mammillary → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal → hippocampus', 'The Papez loop runs hippocampus via the fornix to the mammillary bodies, then through the mammillothalamic tract to the anterior thalamus, cingulate gyrus, and back to the parahippocampal gyrus. The other sequences describe cerebellar, amygdala-hypothalamic, septal, or cortico-striatal loops rather than the classic memory/emotion circuit.'),
('28975868-978a-59e7-ae8c-fef0fda0fa11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The limbic system concept (Papez circuit + amygdala)', '["Only social behavior resulting in decreased functional activity","Only memory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motivation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only emotion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Emotion processing, memory consolidation, and motivation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion processing, memory consolidation, and motivation', 'The limbic system comprises the Papez circuit and amygdala, which together govern emotional experience, consolidation of declarative memory, and motivated behavior. Confining it to a single function ignores its combined emotional, mnemonic, and motivational activity.'),
('1cd427d0-f612-507e-a529-5739baf40f05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The orbitofrontal cortex is sometimes called the', '["Orbitofrontal = tertiary taste cortex, also reward representation","Secondary taste accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary taste mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Orbitofrontal = tertiary taste cortex, also reward representation', 'The orbitofrontal cortex serves as tertiary (higher-order) gustatory cortex, integrating taste with reward, motivation, and feeding behavior. Labeling it primary or secondary taste understates the relay chain, and it is not a motor or visual area.'),
('b36d5709-b163-5786-999d-32aced36a2f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Gustatory (taste) cortex is located in', '["Postcentral gyrus resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Insula (anterior) and frontal operculum (area 43) — primary taste","Precentral gyrus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Insula (anterior) and frontal operculum (area 43) — primary taste', 'Primary gustatory cortex lies in the anterior insula and frontal operculum (Brodmann area 43), where taste afferents from the solitary nucleus arrive via the thalamus. The postcentral gyrus serves touch, not taste, and the precentral, occipital, and temporal lobes have different functional roles.'),
('79f86ef4-9d40-562a-b2dd-7f991457404e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Olfactory (smell) cortex includes', '["Entorhinal only resulting in decreased functional activity","Piriform cortex (anterior temporal/uncus), amygdala (corticomedial), olfactory tubercle, entorhinal","All temporal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Piriform only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Piriform cortex (anterior temporal/uncus), amygdala (corticomedial), olfactory tubercle, entorhinal', 'Primary olfactory cortex is a composite of the piriform cortex, corticomedial amygdala, olfactory tubercle, and anterior entorhinal region, all receiving direct mitral/tufted cell input. Naming any single region alone omits the other components, and the olfactory cortex is not the entire temporal lobe.'),
('6f1c0068-1819-5ad9-9320-879050ea3efd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Olfactory (smell) pathway:', '["Olfactory → LGN/MGN resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory → hippocampus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Olfactory bulb → thalamus → cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Olfactory nerve → olfactory bulb → olfactory tract → olfactory tubercle/piriform/amygdala (no thalamic relay)","Olfactory nerve → thalamus directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory nerve → olfactory bulb → olfactory tract → olfactory tubercle/piriform/amygdala (no thalamic relay)', 'Smell is unique in bypassing the thalamus: olfactory receptors relay through the bulb via the olfactory tract straight to the olfactory tubercle, piriform cortex, and amygdala. Uniquely among sensory systems it does not synapse in LGN/MGN or a thalamic relay nucleus.'),
('74965a98-68cf-5a8b-90c9-500b539d47b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Primary olfactory (piriform) cortex is located in', '["Insula resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occipital lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Uncus (medial temporal) and anterior parahippocampal gyrus","Parietal lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uncus (medial temporal) and anterior parahippocampal gyrus', 'The piriform cortex occupies the uncus of the medial temporal lobe and adjoining anterior parahippocampal gyrus, where olfactory information is first processed cortically. It does not reside in insula, frontal, occipital, or parietal regions.'),
('f87791c9-fa35-5f7e-b5b7-8ddb3fa2db56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The orbitofrontal cortex also processes', '["Only primary smell resulting in decreased functional activity","Only vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secondary olfactory (odor valence) and taste (flavor)","Only hearing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only primary taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary olfactory (odor valence) and taste (flavor)', 'The orbitofrontal cortex receives secondary olfactory input for odor pleasantness/valence and integrates taste to construct flavor perception. It is not limited to primary smell or primary taste and does not process vision or hearing.'),
('be0ed658-cad2-57d1-824d-eaa57576b0a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Temporal lobe structures:', '["Only amygdala resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only visual accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus (medial, memory), amygdala (anterior, emotion), auditory (superior temporal), visual (inferior temporal)","Only hippocampus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only auditory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus (medial, memory), amygdala (anterior, emotion), auditory (superior temporal), visual (inferior temporal)', 'The temporal lobe houses the hippocampus and amygdala medially for memory and emotion, plus auditory cortex on the superior gyrus and visual association areas along the inferior temporal lobe. No single structure accounts for the full functional array of the lobe.'),
('f1847d6c-b6ae-5b8f-8dd6-30a61e613df0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The temporal pole (Brodmann area 38) is involved in', '["Emotion only resulting in decreased functional activity","Semantic knowledge (especially social concepts, familiar faces/names)","Memory only mediated by specific receptor activation","Vision only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Semantic knowledge (especially social concepts, familiar faces/names)', 'Area 38 at the temporal pole acts as a semantic hub, storing meanings including social concepts and familiar faces and names. Its role is broader than pure emotion, memory, vision, or hearing taken alone.'),
('9f652155-d911-5ba5-8d44-40777cbd3025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Semantic variant primary progressive aphasia (svPPA) involves', '["Left frontal resulting in decreased functional activity","Left parietal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left anterior temporal lobe (temporal pole, perirhinal) — loss of semantic knowledge","Left hippocampus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left occipital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left anterior temporal lobe (temporal pole, perirhinal) — loss of semantic knowledge', 'svPPA is caused by atrophy of the left anterior temporal lobe including the temporal pole and perirhinal cortex, producing progressive loss of word and object meaning despite fluent speech. The associated atrophy does not center on frontal, parietal, hippocampal, or occipital regions.'),
('38bc50b9-d84f-560f-8fac-c6bedc761f83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Logopenic variant PPA (lvPPA) involves', '["Left inferior frontal resulting in decreased functional activity","Left hippocampus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Left temporoparietal junction (supramarginal/angular, posterior STG) — word-finding, repetition","Left temporal pole leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left temporoparietal junction (supramarginal/angular, posterior STG) — word-finding, repetition', 'lvPPA results from atrophy of the left temporoparietal junction, notably supramarginal and angular gyri with posterior superior temporal gyrus, causing slow speech with word-finding pauses and impaired repetition. The pattern spares inferior frontal cortex, hippocampus, occipital lobe, and temporal pole.'),
('6128f927-c6f1-5565-895b-3a0d1c61ab3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Nonfluent/agrammatic variant PPA (nfvPPA) involves', '["Left parietal resulting in decreased functional activity","Left inferior frontal (Broca/area 44/45) and premotor/insula — effortful speech, agrammatism","Left temporal pole mediated by specific receptor activation","Left thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left occipital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left inferior frontal (Broca/area 44/45) and premotor/insula — effortful speech, agrammatism', 'nfvPPA reflects atrophy of left inferior frontal (Broca area 44/45) cortex with premotor and insular involvement, producing effortful, agrammatic speech. The lesion distribution does not involve parietal, temporal pole, thalamic, or occipital regions.'),
('284a6a69-5e78-57cd-b93c-05b402c5da97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The corticospinal tract originates from', '["Motor cortex (area 4, area 6, area 3,1,2 — 40% from M1, 30% from PM/SMA, 30% from S1)","Only area 6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only SMA mediated by specific receptor activation","Only S1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only area 4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor cortex (area 4, area 6, area 3,1,2 — 40% from M1, 30% from PM/SMA, 30% from S1)', 'Corticospinal fibers arise from a broad motor network: roughly 40% from primary motor cortex (area 4), 30% from premotor/supplementary areas (area 6), and 30% from primary somatosensory cortex (areas 3, 1, 2). Assigning origin to any single area disregards the tract''s polymodal cortical input.'),
('c99aa114-2e8c-51c1-a914-048cf7a40645'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Corticospinal fibers from the leg area originate from', '["Lateral part (MCA territory) resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial part of M1 (paracentral lobule, ACA territory)","Cingulate mediated by specific receptor activation","Operculum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial part of M1 (paracentral lobule, ACA territory)', 'The leg/foot representation of the motor homunculus sits medially on the paracentral lobule, supplied by the anterior cerebral artery; lateral M1 handles face and upper limb. It is therefore not from lateral (MCA) cortex, cingulate, operculum, or insula.'),
('ea8228d3-6e8b-52cc-bbea-4793d326dfc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The paracentral lobule (medial precentral + postcentral) controls', '["Leg and foot motor (anterior) and sensory (posterior) — micturition center (paracentral)","Arm motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Face motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Eye movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trunk sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leg and foot motor (anterior) and sensory (posterior) — micturition center (paracentral)', 'The paracentral lobule carries the medial leg/foot motor and sensory homunculus and contains a cortical center for voluntary micturition. It does not host arm, face, or eye representations, and trunk sensation is mapped more laterally.'),
('065f152a-096b-5749-aae7-2edf8fe75b5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Corticospinal fibers cross (pyramidal decussation) at the', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cervicomedullary junction (medulla-spinal cord junction)","Midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cervicomedullary junction (medulla-spinal cord junction)', 'About 85-90% of corticospinal fibers cross in the pyramidal decussation at the cervicomedullary junction (inferior medulla), forming the lateral corticospinal tract. Decussation does not occur at the thalamus, midbrain, pons, or within the spinal cord.'),
('1f3f12c0-bb21-537e-a13d-dc855fa04b56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The anterior corticospinal tract (uncrossed) controls', '["Axial (trunk, proximal) muscles bilaterally (postural)","Limb muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Face mediated by specific receptor activation","Sphincters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Axial (trunk, proximal) muscles bilaterally (postural)', 'The small uncrossed anterior corticospinal tract descends ipsilaterally and then crosses at cord levels to synapse bilaterally, governing axial and proximal postural muscles. Distal limb control is the domain of the crossed lateral tract, while face and eye movements use other pathways.'),
('2195ea87-b0a9-5e6f-82c6-41364ff3a55e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The lateral corticospinal tract (crossed) controls', '["All muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Face accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Distal limb muscles (fine, skilled movements) — contralateral","Axial muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eye leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal limb muscles (fine, skilled movements) — contralateral', 'The crossed lateral corticospinal tract supplies contralateral distal limb motoneurons, enabling fine, skilled hand and foot movements. It does not control axial muscles, facial musculature, or eye movements, and it is not responsible for all muscles.'),
('28a45f3c-5a06-5bb0-9f78-c6aeb4da7355'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The corticobulbar (corticonuclear) tract supplies motor cranial nerve nuclei', '["Only trigeminal motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilaterally except lower face (CN VII) and tongue (CN XII)","All ipsilaterally mediated by specific receptor activation","All contralaterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only facial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilaterally except lower face (CN VII) and tongue (CN XII)', 'Most cranial motor nuclei receive bilateral corticobulbar innervation; only the lower facial subnucleus of CN VII and the hypoglossal (CN XII) nucleus receive predominantly contralateral input. The tract is therefore neither purely ipsilateral, purely contralateral, nor limited to trigeminal or facial supply.'),
('73714976-7a44-5fee-95c4-68f4f94797a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'UMNL (upper motor neuron lesion) of face (supranuclear) causes', '["Entire ipsilateral face resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilateral face weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Entire contralateral face mediated by specific receptor activation","Contralateral lower face weakness only (forehead spared)","Ipsilateral lower face leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral lower face weakness only (forehead spared)', 'Supranuclear (UMN) facial lesions weaken only the contralateral lower face because the upper facial nucleus receives bilateral cortical input, sparing the forehead. It never paralyzes the entire face on either side or both sides as would a lower motor or bilateral process.'),
('763a28d3-29d9-51ac-bf96-d724c47e3b01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'LMNL (lower motor neuron lesion) of face (nuclear/infranuclear) causes', '["Complete ipsilateral facial weakness (upper + lower face)","Contralateral lower face accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bilateral face mediated by specific receptor activation","Ipsilateral lower face without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forehead only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete ipsilateral facial weakness (upper + lower face)', 'A nuclear or infranuclear facial (CN VII) lesion interrupts all axons to the ipsilateral musculature, producing complete weakness of both upper and lower face on the same side, including the forehead. Contralateral, bilateral, or lower-face-only patterns reflect different levels of injury.'),
('bed216ac-faf4-5a1a-9a01-e9e66f1679c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Pseudobulbar palsy (UMN lesion, bilateral corticobulbar) causes', '["Spastic dysarthria, dysphagia, emotional lability (pseudobulbar affect)","Flaccid dysarthria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only emotional lability mediated by specific receptor activation","Only dysphagia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spastic dysarthria, dysphagia, emotional lability (pseudobulbar affect)', 'Bilateral corticobulbar (UMN) lesions produce spastic dysarthria and dysphagia with brisk jaw jerk and pseudobulbar affect (uncontrolled laughing/crying). The picture is not the flaccid pattern of LMN bulbar palsy and is broader than isolated emotional or swallowing symptoms.'),
('060f811d-504f-5889-9b08-f104d070a193'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Bulbar palsy (LMN, CN IX-XII nuclei) causes', '["Spastic dysarthria resulting in decreased functional activity","Only dysphagia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only tongue deviation mediated by specific receptor activation","Flaccid dysarthria, dysphagia, tongue fasciculations, absent gag reflex","Only gag loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid dysarthria, dysphagia, tongue fasciculations, absent gag reflex', 'Lower motor neuron damage to bulbar nuclei (CN IX-XII) gives flaccid atrophy with fasciculating, wasted tongue, nasal flaccid dysarthria, dysphagia, and diminished gag reflex. It contrasts with the spastic features of pseudobulbar palsy and exceeds any single isolated symptom.'),
('cfdae93d-fa51-59e0-8c2c-a16d62d15a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'The occipital lobe contains primarily', '["Somatosensory cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual cortex (primary V1 + visual association V2-V5)","Prefrontal cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual cortex (primary V1 + visual association V2-V5)', 'The occipital lobe is devoted to vision, housing primary visual cortex (V1) around the calcarine sulcus plus association areas V2-V5. Somatosensory, motor, prefrontal, and auditory functions reside in other lobes.'),
('9618563b-25a4-560e-8690-9d6d5ed87eef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Visual field defects: lesion of left optic tract causes', '["Left homonymous hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Left nasal hemianopia","Right nasal hemianopia"]'::jsonb, 'Right homonymous hemianopia', 'The left optic tract carries fibers from both retinae that subserve the right visual field, so its lesion causes right homonymous hemianopia. Bitemporal or nasal hemianopias arise from chiasmal or prechiasmal pathology, not tract lesions.'),
('01d0f0b3-25b9-572e-ab5a-e7de34c9a669'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Bitemporal hemianopia is classically caused by', '["Left optic tract lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital stroke accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right optic tract lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Pituitary tumor compressing optic chiasm (midline)","Glaucoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary tumor compressing optic chiasm (midline)', 'A midline chiasmal compression by a pituitary macroadenoma destroys the crossing nasal retinal fibers, yielding bitemporal hemianopia. Optic tract or occipital lesions produce homonymous defects, and glaucoma creates peripheral visual loss rather than bitemporal hemianopia.'),
('c11726c9-0190-58a3-b13b-21404945d4a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Lesion of left optic nerve causes', '["Left nasal hemianopia resulting in decreased functional activity","Right temporal hemianopia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete blindness left eye (ipsilateral monocular)","visual loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bitemporal hemianopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Complete blindness left eye (ipsilateral monocular)', 'A prechiasmal optic nerve lesion blocks all fibers from that eye, causing complete ipsilateral monocular blindness. A subtotal earner like “visual loss” is nonspecific, and nasal, temporal, or bitemporal hemianopias reflect chiasmal or retrochiasmal injury.'),
('89ca93e7-8e98-5a43-94bb-c6a8af757ae0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Meyer loop (temporal optic radiation) carries fibers from', '["Ipsilateral field resulting in decreased functional activity","Both upper and lower accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral inferior visual field mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral fields without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral superior visual field (inferior retinal quadrant)"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral superior visual field (inferior retinal quadrant)', 'The temporal (Meyer) loop of the optic radiation carries fibers from the inferior retinal quadrant, which subserve the contralateral superior visual field; the parietal loop carries the inferior field. It does not carry both fields or ipsilateral information.'),
('96bc5b7c-c567-5983-8703-ca4888faa8a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Lesion of left Meyer loop (temporal) causes', '["Left inferior quadrantanopia","Left superior quadrantanopia","Complete hemianopia","Right superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky)","Right inferior quadrantanopia"]'::jsonb, 'Right superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky)', 'Interruption of the left Meyer loop eliminates the fibers for the contralateral superior visual field, producing a right superior quadrantanopia. A right inferior quadrantanopia instead follows a parietal radiation lesion, and left-sided or complete field defects do not result from this loop.'),
('71b7b114-1d7a-5a6b-b66e-c420f754dd53'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Lesion of left parietal optic radiation causes', '["Left inferior resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right superior quadrantanopia mediated by specific receptor activation","Right inferior quadrantanopia (pie on the floor)","Left superior leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right inferior quadrantanopia (pie on the floor)', 'The parietal, dorsal limb of the optic radiation carries fibers for the contralateral inferior visual field, so a left parietal lesion gives a right inferior quadrantanopia. Right superior quadrantanopia follows a Meyer loop (temporal) lesion, and left-sided or complete field loss does not occur from this site.'),
('385af0af-e7c4-519f-bf7b-887e2c398636'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Macular sparing in occipital stroke (PCA) results from', '["Dual blood supply: occipital pole from MCA (macular cortex) and PCA (rest of V1)","macular sparing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only peripheral vision loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Complete blindness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central scotoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dual blood supply: occipital pole from MCA (macular cortex) and PCA (rest of V1)', 'The occipital pole containing the macular representation receives collateral supply from the middle cerebral artery as well as the posterior cerebral artery, so a PCA infarct spares central (macular) vision. “Macular sparing” merely names the phenomenon, and complete blindness or an isolated scotoma does not result.'),
('42de2606-05f4-5990-9de2-42a25deb6c2f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Damage to the hippocampus bilaterally causes', '["Retrograde amnesia only resulting in decreased functional activity","Language deficits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterograde amnesia (cannot form new declarative memories) with variable retrograde amnesia","Procedural memory loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor deficits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterograde amnesia (cannot form new declarative memories) with variable retrograde amnesia', 'Bilateral hippocampal damage blocks formation of new declarative memories (anterograde amnesia) and impairs recall of some recent-old memories (variable retrograde loss). Language, procedural habit memory, and motor function remain intact because they rely on other structures.'),
('86558d84-b6a4-51c2-a67d-aca7bb1e6b91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Damage to the hippocampus (bilateral) results in', '["Only spatial deficits resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete memory loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cognitive decline mediated by specific receptor activation","Only retrograde amnesia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterograde amnesia with preserved working memory, procedural memory, and immediate memory"]'::jsonb, 'Anterograde amnesia with preserved working memory, procedural memory, and immediate memory', 'Bilateral hippocampal lesions cause anterograde amnesia while leaving working memory, immediate recall, and procedural skill learning intact, since these do not depend on the hippocampus. The deficit is selective, not a complete erasure of all memory forms.'),
('5a83f4c0-74da-5d32-91f8-1baa445b9fc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Patient HM (medial temporal lobectomy) taught that', '["Hippocampus is for emotion resulting in decreased functional activity","Hippocampus is critical for new declarative (episodic) memories but not procedural or short-term memory","Hippocampus is for language mediated by specific receptor activation","Hippocampus is for vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampus is for all memory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus is critical for new declarative (episodic) memories but not procedural or short-term memory', 'After bilateral medial temporal resection, HM could not form new episodic memories yet retained short-term and procedural learning, showing the hippocampus is essential for declarative memory consolidation but not for all memory. It is neither an emotional, language, visual, nor universal memory center.'),
('18944742-6305-5676-8a40-31fec918243e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Working memory (short-term/online) depends on', '["Hippocampus resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial temporal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) and parietal cortex — not hippocampus"]'::jsonb, 'Prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) and parietal cortex — not hippocampus', 'Working memory relies on reverberating activity in dorsolateral prefrontal and posterior parietal networks that hold information online; the hippocampus is not required for this. Medial temporal, amygdala, and cerebellar structures serve declarative consolidation, fear conditioning, and motor learning respectively.'),
('86db40ff-e85f-590e-8adf-30dd3d5fc9bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Procedural memory (skills, habits) depends on', '["Hippocampus resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia (striatum) and cerebellum","Amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial temporal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prefrontal cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basal ganglia (striatum) and cerebellum', 'Skill and habit learning is stored in the basal ganglia (striatum) and cerebellum, which refine motor sequences without hippocampal involvement. The hippocampus/medial temporal system handles declarative memory, the amygdala handles emotional memory, and prefrontal cortex handles working memory.'),
('229909db-6197-5045-a698-150cef05675b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Emotional memory (fear conditioning) depends on', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Prefrontal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala (especially basolateral and central nuclei)","Striatum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amygdala (especially basolateral and central nuclei)', 'Fear conditioning and emotional arousal are encoded in the amygdala, chiefly its basolateral and central nuclei, which attach affective significance to stimuli. Cerebellum, prefrontal cortex, hippocampus, and striatum subserve motor learning, executive control, declarative memory, and procedural memory rather than fear conditioning.'),
('f9c9972c-54a0-571d-be60-e59b128f4505'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Semantic memory (facts, concepts) depends on', '["Cerebellum only resulting in decreased functional activity","Amygdala only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prefrontal only mediated by specific receptor activation","Hippocampus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior temporal lobe (temporal pole, perirhinal) — semantic hub"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior temporal lobe (temporal pole, perirhinal) — semantic hub', 'General knowledge of facts and concepts is represented in the anterior temporal lobe, especially the temporal pole and perirhinal cortex acting as a semantic hub. This network is distinct from hippocampus (episodic), amygdala (emotional), cerebellum (procedural-motor), and prefrontal (working/executive) systems.'),
('f69b4a02-3263-55ed-8544-d10b5e502b7d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Episodic memory (events) depends on', '["Cerebellum only resulting in decreased functional activity","Prefrontal only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior temporal only mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal ganglia only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampus and medial temporal lobe structures"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus and medial temporal lobe structures', 'Remembering specific events and their context requires the hippocampus and adjacent medial temporal cortices (entorhinal, perirhinal, parahippocampal), which bind the experience into a lasting trace. Cerebellum, prefrontal cortex, anterior temporal pole, and basal ganglia serve different memory systems.'),
('80e7e3bb-2385-52dd-8947-abe480f8447f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Memory consolidation involves', '["Only in amygdala resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in neocortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only in cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Transfer from hippocampus to neocortex over time (sleep-dependent replay)","Only storage in hippocampus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer from hippocampus to neocortex over time (sleep-dependent replay)', 'Consolidation gradually transfers the memory trace from the hippocampus to distributed neocortical stores, a process strengthened during sleep by hippocampal-neocortical replay. It is not confined to the amygdala, cerebellum, neocortex alone, or permanent hippocampal storage.'),
('73cf3030-0ef4-526a-b664-e1d567ae1c8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Alzheimer disease initially affects which structure?', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Parietal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occipital lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Entorhinal cortex (layer II) and hippocampus (CA1) — then spreads"]'::jsonb, 'Entorhinal cortex (layer II) and hippocampus (CA1) — then spreads', 'Alzheimer pathology begins in entorhinal cortex layer II and hippocampal CA1 before spreading to association and isocortical areas, explaining early anterograde amnesia. Cerebellum, parietal, occipital, and frontal lobes are not the initial sites of involvement.'),
('4284dbb5-30bb-5c40-8a20-0ab0541f0025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Alzheimer pathology (amyloid plaques and tau tangles) spreads in predictable stages:', '["Cerebellum first resulting in decreased functional activity","Transentorhinal → limbic (hippocampus) → isocortical (Braak stages I-VI)","Diffuse throughout mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal first without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occipital first leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transentorhinal → limbic (hippocampus) → isocortical (Braak stages I-VI)', 'Neurofibrillary tangle pathology advances through Braak stages from transentorhinal cortex to hippocampus/limbic regions and finally to widespread isocortex. It does not begin in cerebellum, diffuse simultaneously, or start in frontal or occipital cortex.'),
('49baa799-a5a9-5952-81b7-a6a1e78749e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'In Cerebral hemispheres, which statement best fits the following fact: the cerebral cortex is classified into how many lobes?', '["6 lobes (frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, insula, limbic)","4 lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","3 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","7 lobes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6 lobes (frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, insula, limbic)', 'The cerebral cortex is conventionally divided into six lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital, insula, and limbic. Counts of three, four, five, or seven omit the insular and limbic components of the classic scheme.'),
('6d3f8ff4-4348-559e-8e01-c04b36e179be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'Regarding Cerebral hemispheres, what is the correct answer to: the central sulcus (fissure of rolando) separates?', '["Frontal from parietal lobe","Parietal from occipital","Temporal from parietal","Frontal from temporal","Frontal from occipital"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal from parietal lobe', 'The central sulcus of Rolando runs between the frontal and parietal lobes, dividing precentral motor from postcentral somatosensory cortex. Parieto-occipital, temporoparietal, frontotemporal, and fronto-occipital borders are formed by other fissures (parieto-occipital, lateral, and sylvian sulci).'),
('62bd7510-5e52-504e-8b68-7793247fd7e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cerebral Hemispheres', 'For Cerebral hemispheres, which option is most appropriate: the lateral sulcus (fissure of sylvius) separates?', '["Temporal lobe from frontal and parietal (superior)","Temporal from occipital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parietal from occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal from temporal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal from parietal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe from frontal and parietal (superior)', 'The lateral (Sylvian) fissure separates the temporal lobe below from the frontal and parietal lobes above. It does not mark the temporo-occipital, parieto-occipital, or central borders, nor does it divide frontal from parietal cortex.'),
('0a04bb48-d91d-561a-9108-5a7b6949138d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve I (Olfactory) is classified as', '["Pure sensory (special visceral afferent)","Pure motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both sensory and motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pure sensory (special visceral afferent)', 'CN I is purely afferent, carrying smell from the olfactory epithelium; it is a special visceral afferent because it conveys chemosensory information. It has no motor, sympathetic, or parasympathetic fibers.'),
('88ab1bb7-196d-58d5-bd81-eb758ed9ad09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Olfactory receptor neurons (CN I) are located in', '["Olfactory bulb resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory epithelium (superior nasal concha / nasal septum)","Olfactory tract mediated by specific receptor activation","Piriform cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cribriform plate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory epithelium (superior nasal concha / nasal septum)', 'Bipolar olfactory receptor neurons lie in the olfactory epithelium lining the superior nasal concha and nasal septum. Their axons pass through the cribriform plate to synapse in the olfactory bulb; the brain regions listed are downstream targets, not receptor sites.'),
('b332ec47-e859-5a3c-8e6b-9de6db3678ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Olfactory nerves pass through', '["Optic canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Cribriform plate (ethmoid bone) to olfactory bulb","Superior orbital fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen rotundum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foramen magnum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate (ethmoid bone) to olfactory bulb', 'Olfactory fila traverse the foramina of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to reach the olfactory bulb. Fractures of the anterior cranial fossa here tear these fibers and cause anosmia.'),
('c5e47665-65e4-5d78-b63e-5d873730970c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The olfactory bulb projects to', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory tract → piriform cortex, amygdala, entorhinal cortex (primary olfactory cortex)","Thalamus (MGN) mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral geniculate body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior colliculus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory tract → piriform cortex, amygdala, entorhinal cortex (primary olfactory cortex)', 'The olfactory bulb sends signals via the olfactory tract to the primary olfactory cortex (piriform, periamygdaloid, and entorhinal areas). Unlike other senses, smell bypasses the thalamus, so the MGN, LGN, cerebellum, and superior colliculus are not targets.'),
('0ec54c37-c665-5af1-90af-ce3cdd1b4310'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Uncus (medial temporal) is the primary olfactory cortex (Brodmann area 51)', '["Only parahippocampal resulting in decreased functional activity","Correct: uncus = primary olfactory cortex (piriform, periamygdaloid, rostral entorhinal)","Only amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Only orbitofrontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only insula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: uncus = primary olfactory cortex (piriform, periamygdaloid, rostral entorhinal)', 'The uncus is the primary olfactory cortex (Brodmann area 51), receiving direct mitral/tufted cell input via the olfactory tract. The amygdala and orbitofrontal cortex receive secondary projections, and the insula and parahippocampal gyrus alone do not constitute this cortex.'),
('34261216-16fb-5e86-bf13-27b6e5f1f562'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN I damage (anosmia) can result from', '["Only infection resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Head trauma (cribriform plate fracture), meningioma (olfactory groove), infection (URI, COVID), congenital (Kallmann)","Only congenital without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma (cribriform plate fracture), meningioma (olfactory groove), infection (URI, COVID), congenital (Kallmann)', 'Anosmia has multiple causes, including head trauma with cribriform plate fracture, olfactory groove meningioma, upper respiratory infections (including COVID-19), and congenital Kallmann syndrome with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. Any single-cause option is incomplete.'),
('5392ce61-a2ff-5df6-9343-b92c1d2f5b54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Foster Kennedy syndrome (olfactory groove meningioma):', '["Only motor defects resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilateral optic atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only papilledema mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral anosmia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipsilateral anosmia + optic atrophy and contralateral papilledema"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral anosmia + optic atrophy and contralateral papilledema', 'An olfactory groove meningioma compresses the ipsilateral olfactory and optic nerves, causing ipsilateral anosmia and optic atrophy with a pale disc, while raised intracranial pressure produces contralateral papilledema. This triad distinguishes it from lesions affecting only vision or only smell.'),
('ebc9273a-bc03-5e6d-b602-61c761c7b49d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN II (Optic) is', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Pure motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Mixed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pure sensory (special somatic afferent)"]'::jsonb, 'Pure sensory (special somatic afferent)', 'The optic nerve is purely sensory, conveying visual information as a special somatic afferent. It carries no motor, sympathetic, or parasympathetic fibers.'),
('2be9ba3d-a340-5636-809d-012cdbf329b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Optic nerve (CN II) exits the orbit via', '["Inferior orbital fissure","Optic canal (sphenoid bone)","Foramen rotundum","Superior orbital fissure","Foramen ovale"]'::jsonb, 'Optic canal (sphenoid bone)', 'The optic nerve leaves the orbit through the optic canal of the sphenoid bone, accompanied by the ophthalmic artery. CN III, IV, VI and V1 exit via the superior orbital fissure, while foramen rotundum and foramen ovale transmit V2 and V3.'),
('5ccbdb47-6508-5cfc-bb22-9b8c59df5d4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The optic chiasm is formed by the', '["Crossing of all fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","crossing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporal fibers crossing mediated by specific receptor activation","Crossing of pupillary fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crossing of nasal retinal fibers from both eyes (partial decussation)"]'::jsonb, 'Crossing of nasal retinal fibers from both eyes (partial decussation)', 'At the optic chiasm only nasal retinal fibers cross to the contralateral optic tract while temporal fibers remain uncrossed, a partial decussation. Compression here therefore destroys crossed fibers and produces bitemporal hemianopia.'),
('153540d8-cd06-5931-b70b-eb2147f5b052'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Nasal retinal fibers cross at chiasm; temporal fibers remain uncrossed', '["Correct — explains visual field defects from chiasmal lesions","All fibers cross accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only macular fibers cross mediated by specific receptor activation","Half cross without affecting the primary tissue pathway","fibers cross leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct — explains visual field defects from chiasmal lesions', 'Because temporal fibers stay uncrossed while nasal fibers cross, a chiasmal lesion removes crossed input from both eyes and causes bitemporal heteronymous hemianopia. If all fibers crossed, the visual field defect pattern would differ entirely.'),
('b2ed7968-9689-5fd9-b358-f85a160a843a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The optic tract contains', '["Fibers from contralateral visual field (temporal ipsilateral + nasal contralateral)","Only uncrossed fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both crossed and uncrossed from same eye mediated by specific receptor activation","All fibers from chiasm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only crossed fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibers from contralateral visual field (temporal ipsilateral + nasal contralateral)', 'Each optic tract carries ipsilateral temporal fibers plus crossed nasal fibers from the contralateral eye, together representing the contralateral visual field. This is why a tract lesion produces contralateral homonymous hemianopia rather than a monocular defect.'),
('584d3cc8-f76f-5147-b861-bb242053e42d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The optic tract projects to', '["All equally resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral geniculate body (LGN, thalamus) — major target; also pretectum (pupillary), superior colliculus (reflex)","Only LGN mediated by specific receptor activation","Only superior colliculus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pretectum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral geniculate body (LGN, thalamus) — major target; also pretectum (pupillary), superior colliculus (reflex)', 'The optic tract ends principally in the lateral geniculate body, which relays to the visual cortex; collaterals also reach the pretectal nuclei (pupillary light reflex) and superior colliculus (orienting reflexes). The LGN is the major target but not the only one.'),
('79f61790-0879-5a85-8f9d-cf103f869d2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The pupillary light reflex pathway:', '["Retina → SC → EW resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic tract → LGN → cortex → brainstem accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pretectum → LGN mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve → Edinger-Westphal directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN II (afferent) → optic tract → pretectal nucleus (midbrain) → Edinger-Westphal (CN III) → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae"]'::jsonb, 'CN II (afferent) → optic tract → pretectal nucleus (midbrain) → Edinger-Westphal (CN III) → ciliary ganglion → sphincter pupillae', 'Light travels along CN II and the optic tract to the pretectal nucleus, which projects bilaterally to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei; preganglionic parasympathetic fibers then run with CN III to the ciliary ganglion, and postganglionic fibers contract the sphincter pupillae. The LGN and cortex are not part of the reflex arc.'),
('3bf1eec8-b568-5838-9f7f-e9d6030d484d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Complete optic nerve lesion causes', '["Ipsilateral blindness (no light perception, no direct pupillary reflex to light, but consensual intact)","Afferent pupillary defect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral hemianopia mediated by specific receptor activation","Only visual field defect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bitemporal hemianopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral blindness (no light perception, no direct pupillary reflex to light, but consensual intact)', 'A complete optic nerve lesion eliminates light perception and the direct pupillary light reflex in that eye, but the consensual response (light in the normal eye) is intact. Because no light reaches the affected eye, an afferent pupillary defect cannot be elicited there.'),
('59c257a3-0b68-51f7-ab8f-048a65f4ffd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD, Marcus Gunn pupil) indicates', '["Chiasmal lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Severe optic nerve lesion (or retinal) — direct light response weaker than consensual","CN III lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe optic nerve lesion (or retinal) — direct light response weaker than consensual', 'RAPD reflects asymmetric afferent light input, typically from severe optic nerve or extensive retinal damage: the affected eye constricts less to direct light than to consensual input from the healthy eye. Chiasmal and CN III lesions do not produce such afferent asymmetry.'),
('547a9570-b374-57e6-81aa-c7d6d9475011'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Chiasmal lesion (compression) causes', '["visual loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Quadrantanopia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Homonymous hemianopia mediated by specific receptor activation","Monocular blindness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bitemporal (heteronymous) hemianopia — loss of temporal visual fields from both eyes"]'::jsonb, 'Bitemporal (heteronymous) hemianopia — loss of temporal visual fields from both eyes', 'Chiasmal compression destroys crossing nasal fibers, removing the temporal visual field of each eye and causing bitemporal heteronymous hemianopia. Monocular blindness, homonymous, or quadrant defects localize elsewhere in the visual pathway.'),
('9ea8f010-a2ae-516e-b886-abb72e0acd54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Optic tract lesion causes', '["Binasal hemianopia resulting in decreased functional activity","Bitemporal hemianopia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (visual loss in opposite half of both eyes)","Ipsilateral blindness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Quadrantanopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (visual loss in opposite half of both eyes)', 'The optic tract already contains fibers from both eyes representing the same hemifield, so a tract lesion causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Bitemporal loss follows chiasmal compression and monocular blindness is prechiasmal.'),
('74d75527-aef1-5491-ab57-d4fecd7af163'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Optic radiation (temporal Meyer loop) lesion causes', '["Macular sparing resulting in decreased functional activity","Complete hemianopia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky)","visual change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky)', 'Meyer''s loop carries fibers from the inferior retina; damage to it produces contralateral superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky). Likewise, the parietal radiation produces the opposite inferior quadrantanopia.'),
('7f4f14a8-f6e6-5c05-9593-7c9cec7d15a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Optic radiation (parietal) lesion causes', '["Macular sparing resulting in decreased functional activity","visual change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia (pie on the floor)","Superior quadrantanopia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete hemianopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia (pie on the floor)', 'The parietal optic radiation carries fibers from the superior retina, so the lesion causes contralateral inferior quadrantanopia (pie on the floor). Meyer''s loop in the temporal lobe conversely gives a superior quadrantanopia.'),
('660e07eb-d101-53c3-af82-947cd0bd5d35'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Calcarine (V1) lesion (unilateral) causes', '["Complete hemianopia without macular sparing resulting in decreased functional activity","Contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing (usually)","Quadrantanopia mediated by specific receptor activation","Monocular blindness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bitemporal hemianopia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing (usually)', 'Primary visual cortex (V1) represents the contralateral hemifield, so a unilateral occipital lesion produces contralateral homonymous hemianopia; the macular representation is often spared because of its rich dual blood supply. Monocular and bitemporal defects lie anterior to the chiasm and tract.'),
('16ec7d36-b49d-5099-9c76-8245672e7c75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Optic atrophy can be detected by', '["Pallor (whitening) of optic disc on fundoscopy — especially temporal pallor","Pink disc accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cupped disc mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal disc without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic disc swelling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pallor (whitening) of optic disc on fundoscopy — especially temporal pallor', 'Chronic optic nerve damage removes axons, so the optic disc appears pale (white) on fundoscopy, especially temporally where the papillomacular bundle runs. Disc cupping suggests glaucoma and disc swelling suggests papilledema, not atrophy.'),
('74ccc312-2869-575b-8cf5-7a6915fa0d8b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Papilledema (swollen optic disc) indicates', '["Retinal hemorrhage resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic neuritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased intracranial pressure (transmitted via optic nerve sheath)","Glaucoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic atrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intracranial pressure (transmitted via optic nerve sheath)', 'Papilledema is disc swelling caused by raised intracranial pressure transmitted along the optic nerve sheath, which obstructs axoplasmic transport. It therefore points to raised ICP (tumor, hydrocephalus, idiopathic intracranial hypertension) rather than optic neuritis or glaucoma.'),
('1b01ec2d-91fc-58fc-84b1-dabb7ab87425'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN III (Oculomotor) is', '["Mainly motor (somatic + parasympathetic/autonomic)","Pure sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pure motor (somatic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mixed sensory/motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mainly motor (somatic + parasympathetic/autonomic)', 'CN III is predominantly motor: somatic fibers supply most extraocular muscles and levator palpebrae, and preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel with it. It carries no sensory input, so options omitting the parasympathetic component are incomplete.'),
('8e44a957-2807-5f56-99c0-b75ca7601e6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Oculomotor nucleus is located in', '["Midbrain (level of superior colliculus, in periaqueductal gray)","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (level of superior colliculus, in periaqueductal gray)', 'The oculomotor nucleus lies in the midbrain tegmentum at the level of the superior colliculus, within the periaqueductal gray. The trochlear nucleus is situated just caudally and the abducens nucleus in the pons.'),
('fcea737a-f699-5ce3-9c3e-7383cd806468'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Edinger-Westphal nucleus (parasympathetic of CN III) is located', '["In the pons resulting in decreased functional activity","In medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","At the ciliary ganglion mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral to CN III nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal to the oculomotor nucleus (midbrain)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal to the oculomotor nucleus (midbrain)', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus lies dorsal to the oculomotor nucleus in the midbrain. Its preganglionic fibers run with CN III and synapse in the ciliary ganglion; the nucleus itself is not located at the ganglion.'),
('0b979f0d-f313-58aa-91ed-ed65aff9c579'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN III fibers exit the brainstem from', '["Lateral midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Interpeduncular fossa (ventral midbrain, between cerebral peduncles)","Pontomedullary junction mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellopontine angle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interpeduncular fossa (ventral midbrain, between cerebral peduncles)', 'CN III exits the ventral midbrain in the interpeduncular fossa between the cerebral peduncles. CN VI emerges at the pontomedullary junction and CN VII/VIII at the cerebello-pontine angle, so those options locate the wrong nerves.'),
('4d2c6455-97e4-52b3-8672-4bdbed168ed0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN III in the cavernous sinus runs in', '["Within sinus cavity resulting in decreased functional activity","None of above accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Below CN IV mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial to ICA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral wall (superior part) — above CN IV"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral wall (superior part) — above CN IV', 'In the lateral dural wall of the cavernous sinus, CN III lies in its superior part, above the trochlear nerve, V1, and V2. CN VI alone runs freely within the sinus beside the internal carotid artery.'),
('97b0e690-705a-5233-ab13-a78a7715d90a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN III enters the orbit through', '["Optic canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior orbital fissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foramen ovale mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior orbital fissure (within the common tendinous ring of Zinn)","Foramen rotundum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior orbital fissure (within the common tendinous ring of Zinn)', 'CN III enters the orbit through the superior orbital fissure within the common tendinous ring of Zinn, together with CN VI and the nasociliary nerve. The optic canal transmits CN II and the ophthalmic artery.'),
('563c02d1-fc0e-5abd-a4c6-b4dc5dc88015'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN III divides into superior and inferior divisions in the cavernous sinus/orbit', '["division resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior: all muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior division: levator palpebrae, superior rectus. Inferior division: medial rectus, inferior rectus, inferior oblique + ciliary ganglion","Superior: all muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior: MR, IR; Inferior: SR, IO leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior division: levator palpebrae, superior rectus. Inferior division: medial rectus, inferior rectus, inferior oblique + ciliary ganglion', 'The superior division of CN III supplies levator palpebrae superioris and superior rectus; the inferior division supplies medial rectus, inferior rectus, and inferior oblique and gives the branch to the ciliary ganglion. This division accounts for selective muscle involvement in partial CN III palsy.'),
('8530910a-c22e-51eb-9e22-0fc0c2101419'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Complete CN III palsy causes', '["extraocular palsy resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Eye deviates up and in accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ptosis (drooping eyelid), eye deviates ''down and out'' (LR by VI, SO by IV), dilated fixed pupil (parasympathetic loss), loss of accommodation","Pupil constricted without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ptosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis (drooping eyelid), eye deviates ''down and out'' (LR by VI, SO by IV), dilated fixed pupil (parasympathetic loss), loss of accommodation', 'Complete CN III palsy spares only lateral rectus (CN VI) and superior oblique (CN IV), so the unopposed eye deviates down and out with ptosis; loss of parasympathetic drive leaves a dilated, fixed pupil and abolished accommodation.'),
('1793d75b-5cd4-53fb-a665-a9aceeb1374f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Pupil-sparing CN III palsy suggests', '["Inflammatory resulting in decreased functional activity","Medical (ischemic/microvascular) lesion — diabetes, hypertension — spares outer parasympathetic fibers","Traumatic mediated by specific receptor activation","Compressive lesion (aneurysm) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medical (ischemic/microvascular) lesion — diabetes, hypertension — spares outer parasympathetic fibers', 'Microvascular ischemia from diabetes or hypertension usually spares the pupillomotor fibers because they lie on the superficial nerve surface with a somewhat separate blood supply. Pupil sparing therefore points away from a compressive lesion.'),
('daac5bcd-e069-50b4-81ce-b2cc5d4a6ac4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Pupil-involved CN III palsy suggests', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MS mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Surgical/compressive lesion (posterior communicating artery aneurysm) — compresses superficial pupillary fibers","Migraine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical/compressive lesion (posterior communicating artery aneurysm) — compresses superficial pupillary fibers', 'Parasympathetic pupilloconstrictor fibers are superficial, so external compression, classically by a posterior communicating artery aneurysm, causes an early dilated and fixed pupil. Microvascular ischemic palsy typically spares the pupil.'),
('a5325346-5fc9-560b-a9e1-ffa8aaf554b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN IV (Trochlear) is', '["Mixed resulting in decreased functional activity","Pure sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Pure motor (somatic efferent) to superior oblique muscle","Sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pure motor (somatic efferent) to superior oblique muscle', 'CN IV is a purely motor nerve supplying only the superior oblique muscle. It has no sensory, sympathetic, or parasympathetic fibers.'),
('4b97358d-529d-5128-bdad-32efac496939'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trochlear nucleus is located in', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain (level of inferior colliculus, in periaqueductal gray, ventral to PAG)","Pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midbrain superior colliculus level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (level of inferior colliculus, in periaqueductal gray, ventral to PAG)', 'The trochlear nucleus sits in the midbrain tegmentum at the level of the inferior colliculus, ventral to the periaqueductal gray. The oculomotor nucleus is just rostral at the superior colliculus level, and the abducens nucleus lies in the pons.'),
('ac64351b-10df-520b-bf1d-c6fcdef8b229'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN IV is unique because it is the only cranial nerve that', '["Has nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Is bilateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exits from the dorsal brainstem (decussates in superior medullary velum)","Has function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exits from the dorsal brainstem (decussates in superior medullary velum)', 'CN IV is the only cranial nerve to exit the dorsal brainstem; its axons fully decussate in the superior medullary velum to supply the contralateral superior oblique. This long, exposed course explains its susceptibility to trauma.'),
('7fa52c51-88df-5080-83c9-de86367fdb16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN IV enters the orbit via', '["Foramen rotundum resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior orbital fissure (OUTSIDE the common tendinous ring) — above the annulus","Optic canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior orbital fissure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior orbital fissure (inside ring) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior orbital fissure (OUTSIDE the common tendinous ring) — above the annulus', 'CN IV enters the orbit through the superior orbital fissure but outside (superior to) the common tendinous ring, like the ophthalmic and frontal nerves. CN III, CN VI, and the nasociliary branch pass within the ring.'),
('52de7aa8-deb9-5e31-968f-e00dc0373819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trochlear nerve palsy is often due to', '["Brain tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aneurysm mediated by specific receptor activation","cause without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head trauma (decussation/nerve susceptible), congenital, microvascular (diabetes/hypertension)"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma (decussation/nerve susceptible), congenital, microvascular (diabetes/hypertension)', 'CN IV is the most frequently injured cranial nerve in head trauma because of its long intracranial course and dorsal exit; its decussation is also fragile. Congenital palsies and microvascular disease (diabetes, hypertension) are other common causes.'),
('3fb1f8e8-dc11-5d18-b34f-330a4aceb936'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VI (Abducens) is', '["Pure sensory resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Pure motor (somatic efferent) to lateral rectus muscle","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pure motor (somatic efferent) to lateral rectus muscle', 'CN VI is purely motor, supplying only the lateral rectus for abduction of the eye. It carries no sensory or autonomic fibers.'),
('4148f303-31c5-5768-b6f2-4fe308f1f0a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Abducens nucleus is located in', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Pontine tegmentum (floor of fourth ventricle, near midline)","Medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine tegmentum (floor of fourth ventricle, near midline)', 'The abducens nucleus lies in the pontine tegmentum near the midline at the floor of the fourth ventricle. Its interneurons project through the MLF to the contralateral oculomotor nucleus to drive conjugate horizontal gaze.'),
('4a915530-afe8-5941-9d3d-e149a979b01e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VI exits the brainstem at', '["Foramen magnum resulting in decreased functional activity","Pontomedullary junction (medial to CN VII, VIII)","Medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontomedullary junction (medial to CN VII, VIII)', 'CN VI emerges ventromedially at the pontomedullary junction, medial to CN VII and VIII. CN III and IV leave the midbrain, so the other options describe different nerves or wrong levels.'),
('515a7dc8-5256-5b5b-83dc-ac4b083d9981'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VI runs through the cavernous sinus', '["In lateral wall (bottom) resulting in decreased functional activity","Within the sinus (free, next to ICA) — not in the lateral wall","Nowhere mediated by specific receptor activation","In lateral wall (top) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Outside the sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Within the sinus (free, next to ICA) — not in the lateral wall', 'CN VI courses freely within the cavernous sinus adjacent to the internal carotid artery, not in the lateral dural wall, where CN III, IV, V1, and V2 lie. This vulnerable position explains abducens palsy in cavernous sinus thrombosis.'),
('749351b4-a054-510b-a70f-d96d4a1a7775'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VI enters the orbit via', '["Supraorbital fissure (outside ring) resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foramen rotundum mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior orbital fissure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior orbital fissure (within the common tendinous ring of Zinn)"]'::jsonb, 'Superior orbital fissure (within the common tendinous ring of Zinn)', 'CN VI traverses the superior orbital fissure within the common tendinous ring together with CN III and the nasociliary nerve. The optic canal transmits CN II, the inferior orbital fissure carries maxillary nerve branches, and the foramen rotundum transmits V2.'),
('75640361-83f3-5c57-a1e7-f3b2414e737d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Complete CN VI palsy causes', '["Pupil dilated resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertical diplopia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ptosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Inability to adduct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inability to abduct eye (medial deviation/ esotropia at rest), horizontal diplopia on looking to affected side"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to abduct eye (medial deviation/ esotropia at rest), horizontal diplopia on looking to affected side', 'CN VI palsy paralyzes the lateral rectus, so the affected eye is adducted at rest (esotropia) and cannot abduct, causing horizontal diplopia on looking toward the affected side. Pupil, eyelid, and vertical movements are all spared.'),
('f17bd78d-d61e-5fc5-87d4-eb406ea60266'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VI palsy is a ''false localizing sign'' because', '["It indicates lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","It is compressive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is benign mediated by specific receptor activation","It is seen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Long intracranial course makes it vulnerable to increased ICP (traction along clivus)"]'::jsonb, 'Long intracranial course makes it vulnerable to increased ICP (traction along clivus)', 'CN VI has the longest intracranial course and runs along the clivus, so raised intracranial pressure can stretch it against the petrous tip or Dorello''s canal and produce an abducens palsy even when the real lesion is elsewhere. The palsy therefore does not localize the pathology.'),
('94eaf065-ea53-5edf-a9e1-1ee4016df90d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve III, IV, VI together innervate', '["All extraocular muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Extraocular muscles (except LR, SO are VI, IV) — the rest by III","Only eye muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","All except levator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only LR and SO leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extraocular muscles (except LR, SO are VI, IV) — the rest by III', 'CN III supplies most extraocular muscles plus levator palpebrae, CN IV supplies the superior oblique, and CN VI supplies the lateral rectus, so together these three nerves account for all the extraocular muscles. The stated option correctly separates LR/SO (VI/IV) from the rest (III).'),
('bf86233d-39ec-5108-b03a-e3b07a9674f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) connects', '["connection resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN III and IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN IV and VI mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CN III and VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III, IV, VI nuclei together for conjugate horizontal gaze (with PPRF, vestibular nuclei)"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, IV, VI nuclei together for conjugate horizontal gaze (with PPRF, vestibular nuclei)', 'The MLF links the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nuclei, coordinating them with the PPRF and vestibular nuclei for conjugate horizontal gaze. Listing only two of the three nerves misses the coordination function that the tract provides.'),
('854bc29b-b595-571e-a94a-f072d96adf31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (MLF lesion)', '["Ptosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Impaired vertical gaze accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Impaired abduction mediated by specific receptor activation","Diplopia on all gazes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Impaired adduction (ipsilateral) with nystagmus of contralateral abducting eye on lateral gaze"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired adduction (ipsilateral) with nystagmus of contralateral abducting eye on lateral gaze', 'An MLF lesion interrupts the connection from the abducens nucleus to the contralateral medial rectus motor neurons, so the ipsilateral eye fails to adduct during lateral gaze while the fellow abducting eye develops nystagmus. Abduction and vertical gaze remain intact.'),
('48f2f3ab-5954-5fc7-8f44-2d0891cc37ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Conjugate gaze palsy (PPRF lesion)', '["gaze changes resulting in decreased functional activity","Cannot look toward side of lesion (eyes deviate contralaterally)","Cannot look contralaterally mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertical gaze palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nystagmus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cannot look toward side of lesion (eyes deviate contralaterally)', 'The paramedian pontine reticular formation drives horizontal saccades toward the same side, so a PPRF lesion prevents gaze toward the lesion side and the eyes deviate contralaterally. Vertical gaze is not controlled here.'),
('a5ef437a-35d9-5e8e-be63-ea6dc0770231'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Skew deviation (vertical misalignment) suggests', '["CN IV lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VI lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem (vestibular) lesion (especially medulla) — disruption of otolith-ocular pathways","CN III lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid eye disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brainstem (vestibular) lesion (especially medulla) — disruption of otolith-ocular pathways', 'Skew deviation is a vertical misalignment from dysfunction of brainstem vestibular and otolithic pathways, often a medullary or posterior fossa lesion, rather than mononeuropathy of a single ocular motor nerve or orbital disease. It therefore localizes to the brainstem-vestibular system.'),
('2a887aba-404b-5903-95e1-2549df687d32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN V (Trigeminal) is', '["Pure motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed: sensory to face (ophthalmic V1, maxillary V2, mandibular V3) and motor to muscles of mastication","Parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pure sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed: sensory to face (ophthalmic V1, maxillary V2, mandibular V3) and motor to muscles of mastication', 'The trigeminal nerve is mixed: three sensory divisions (V1, V2, V3) carry facial sensation, while the motor root of V3 innervates the muscles of mastication. Purely motor, purely sensory, and autonomic descriptors each omit major components of its function.'),
('6a6f46aa-2dae-5092-8aa0-5351aa3681dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal motor nucleus is located in', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pontine tegmentum (medial to principal sensory nucleus of V)","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine tegmentum (medial to principal sensory nucleus of V)', 'The motor nucleus of the trigeminal nerve lies in the pontine tegmentum, medial to the principal sensory nucleus. Its efferent fibers join the motor root of V3 to supply the muscles of mastication; the midbrain, medulla, thalamus, and cerebellum are not sites of this nucleus.'),
('a1e0636f-245c-5a02-b5b3-9a5a4525702a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Principal (main) sensory nucleus of trigeminal receives', '["Pain and temperature from face resulting in decreased functional activity","Discriminative (fine) touch and pressure from face (like dorsal column for face)","Motor signals mediated by specific receptor activation","Proprioception from face without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Discriminative (fine) touch and pressure from face (like dorsal column for face)', 'The principal sensory nucleus processes discriminative touch and pressure from the face, serving as the facial analogue of the dorsal column system. Pain and temperature are handled by the spinal trigeminal nucleus and proprioception by the mesencephalic nucleus, not by this nucleus.'),
('3cdec046-cb3e-5abf-9e00-e06c523ab006'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Spinal trigeminal nucleus receives', '["Motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain and temperature from face (and ear, oral cavity, dura) — extends from pons to C4","Proprioception mediated by specific receptor activation","Fine touch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature from face (and ear, oral cavity, dura) — extends from pons to C4', 'The spinal trigeminal nucleus extends from the pons down to about C4 and is the relay for pain and temperature from the face, ear, oral cavity, and dura. Fine touch, proprioception, motor, and taste travel through other trigeminal or non-trigeminal pathways.'),
('76e09195-15e6-5ac7-8190-2a4698ff99bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus receives', '["Motor resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Proprioception (muscle spindle afferents) from muscles of mastication (jaw jerk reflex) — unique: contains primary sensory cell bodies in CNS","Touch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proprioception (muscle spindle afferents) from muscles of mastication (jaw jerk reflex) — unique: contains primary sensory cell bodies in CNS', 'The mesencephalic nucleus receives proprioceptive impulses from the muscle spindles of the muscles of mastication and is unique in housing primary sensory cell bodies within the CNS; it mediates the jaw jerk reflex. Pain, touch, taste, and motor signals are not processed there.'),
('f5e328b8-f238-5e39-b348-b31694e64765'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'V1 (Ophthalmic) exits skull via', '["Foramen ovale","Superior orbital fissure","Foramen spinosum","Optic canal","Foramen rotundum"]'::jsonb, 'Superior orbital fissure', 'The ophthalmic division (V1) leaves the skull through the superior orbital fissure together with CN III, IV, and VI. The optic canal transmits CN II, while the foramen ovale and foramen rotundum carry V3 and V2, and the foramen spinosum transmits the middle meningeal artery.'),
('747ac501-a184-55c0-869f-786e26b2b367'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'V2 (Maxillary) exits skull via', '["Foramen ovale","Foramen rotundum","Inferior orbital fissure","Superior orbital fissure","Foramen spinosum"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen rotundum', 'The maxillary division (V2) passes through the foramen rotundum to reach the pterygopalatine fossa. V1 exits via the superior orbital fissure, V3 via the foramen ovale, and the inferior orbital fissure and foramen spinosum transmit other structures.'),
('553c67ee-0c6c-5c8f-a1b4-8bca2e5d68cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'V3 (Mandibular) exits skull via', '["Foramen ovale (with motor root of CN V)","Jugular foramen","Superior orbital fissure","Foramen magnum","Foramen rotundum"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen ovale (with motor root of CN V)', 'The mandibular division (V3) traverses the foramen ovale together with the motor root of the trigeminal nerve. The jugular foramen carries CN IX, X, and XI, the superior orbital fissure and foramen rotundum serve V1 and V2, and the foramen magnum transmits the medulla and spinal roots.'),
('a102d2ef-776e-55ed-a8c5-a6beab528e7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal nerve divisions: V1 sensory to', '["Jaw resulting in decreased functional activity","Neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forehead, scalp, upper eyelid, nose (tip excluded), cornea, paranasal sinuses","Cheek without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temple leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Forehead, scalp, upper eyelid, nose (tip excluded), cornea, paranasal sinuses', 'The ophthalmic division supplies the forehead, scalp, upper eyelid, nasal tip, cornea, and paranasal sinuses. The cheek, jaw, and temple are served by the maxillary or mandibular divisions, so those regions do not belong to V1.'),
('25e4c27c-3f20-5f00-9d10-3f2a4d702f67'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal nerve divisions: V2 sensory to', '["Ear resulting in decreased functional activity","Cheek, lower eyelid, sides of nose, upper lip, maxillary teeth/gums, maxillary sinus","Temple mediated by specific receptor activation","Forehead without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Jaw leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cheek, lower eyelid, sides of nose, upper lip, maxillary teeth/gums, maxillary sinus', 'The maxillary division innervates the cheek, lower eyelid, sides of the nose, upper lip, maxillary teeth and gums, and the maxillary sinus. The forehead, temple, ear, and jaw lie within V1 or V3 territory rather than V2.'),
('39e934fa-d2a7-52aa-a2be-ab3d03fa5b4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal nerve divisions: V3 sensory to', '["Face except jaw resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower lip, chin, jaw, temporal region, anterior ear, mandibular teeth, tongue (anterior 2/3 general sensation)","Upper lip without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forehead leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower lip, chin, jaw, temporal region, anterior ear, mandibular teeth, tongue (anterior 2/3 general sensation)', 'The mandibular division supplies the lower lip, chin, jaw, temporal region, anterior ear, mandibular teeth, and general sensation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. The upper lip and forehead belong to V2 and V1 respectively, and the mandibular field is not the entire face minus the jaw.'),
('4c3eb06d-05ca-52aa-9b56-08dde73236b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Motor fibers of V3 (mandibular) innervate', '["Pharynx resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Masseter, temporalis, lateral/medial pterygoids, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini, mylohyoid, anterior digastric","Facial muscles mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Masseter, temporalis, lateral/medial pterygoids, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini, mylohyoid, anterior digastric', 'The motor root of V3 supplies the muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial and lateral pterygoids) along with tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini, mylohyoid, and the anterior belly of digastric. The pharynx, facial muscles, tongue, and larynx are innervated by other cranial nerves.'),
('2b402b7e-d1f6-53f9-9054-89b6433fd508'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal nerve lesion (sensory) causes', '["change resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of facial motor function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of facial sensation (ipsilateral) — depending on division affected; corneal reflex absent (afferent)","Taste loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of facial sensation (ipsilateral) — depending on division affected; corneal reflex absent (afferent)', 'A sensory lesion of the trigeminal nerve produces ipsilateral facial sensory loss that follows the affected division, and the corneal reflex is lost on the afferent (CN V1) side. Motor function, taste, and hearing remain intact because those modalities travel in other nerves.'),
('08930c03-c2ec-5c98-9e56-57e440249b3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal neuralgia (tic douloureux) is characterized by', '["Constant dull pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paroxysmal, severe electric shock-like pain in V2/V3 distribution (triggered by touch, chewing, cold)","Tinnitus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial weakness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paroxysmal, severe electric shock-like pain in V2/V3 distribution (triggered by touch, chewing, cold)', 'Trigeminal neuralgia produces brief, paroxysmal, electric shock-like pain within the V2 and V3 territories, typically provoked by touch, chewing, or cold. It is not a constant dull ache and does not cause taste loss, tinnitus, or facial weakness.'),
('22771eca-97c3-55b3-8334-d422dd7aab4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Jaw jerk reflex tests', '["CN XII resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN X mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V (trigeminal) — afferent: mesencephalic nucleus; efferent: V motor nucleus → masseter contraction","CN VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (trigeminal) — afferent: mesencephalic nucleus; efferent: V motor nucleus → masseter contraction', 'The jaw jerk is a stretch reflex whose afferent limb runs through the mesencephalic nucleus of V and whose efferent limb is the trigeminal motor nucleus, producing masseter contraction. It therefore tests the trigeminal nerve rather than CN VII, IX, X, or XII.'),
('4183ca54-e2e5-52f5-9cb1-0f37cabd8fa6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VII (Facial) is', '["Pure motor resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Pure sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed: motor (facial muscles), sensory (taste anterior 2/3), and parasympathetic (lacrimal, submandibular/sublingual glands)","Parasympathetic only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All motor taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed: motor (facial muscles), sensory (taste anterior 2/3), and parasympathetic (lacrimal, submandibular/sublingual glands)', 'The facial nerve is mixed: it supplies motor fibers to the muscles of facial expression, conveys taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and carries parasympathetic fibers to the lacrimal, submandibular, and sublingual glands. Pure motor, pure sensory, or parasympathetic-only labels each omit major components.'),
('35acc2e1-018d-5e5a-8449-05bdbc2088cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial motor nucleus is located in', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pontine tegmentum (caudal pons, medial to CN V)","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine tegmentum (caudal pons, medial to CN V)', 'The facial motor nucleus lies in the caudal pontine tegmentum, medial to the trigeminal motor nucleus. Its fibers loop around the abducens nucleus before exiting, but the cell bodies sit in the pons, not the midbrain, medulla, cerebellum, or thalamus.'),
('6882a135-0cec-5eeb-b755-95110cad89b9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve fibers form the internal genu (loop) around which nucleus?', '["Vagus nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal motor nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglossal nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abducens (CN VI) nucleus — causes facial colliculus in floor of fourth ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Abducens (CN VI) nucleus — causes facial colliculus in floor of fourth ventricle', 'The intracerebral fibers of the facial nerve swing dorsally around the abducens (CN VI) nucleus, producing the facial colliculus on the floor of the fourth ventricle. The facial nucleus itself, the trigeminal motor nucleus, and the hypoglossal and vagal nuclei are not encircled by this genu.'),
('9cc0cb3c-63d2-5d63-8eef-17769b076924'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VII enters the temporal bone via', '["Foramen magnum resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal acoustic meatus (with CN VIII) — then runs in facial canal","Superior orbital fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","Jugular foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foramen ovale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal acoustic meatus (with CN VIII) — then runs in facial canal', 'The facial nerve enters the petrous temporal bone through the internal acoustic meatus together with CN VIII, then courses within the facial canal. The stylomastoid foramen is its site of exit, while the jugular foramen, foramen ovale, and foramen magnum transmit other structures.'),
('f08225ee-ed6c-5e40-b6c5-d0fc8af69507'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve branches (within temporal bone):', '["branches resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only taste branch mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only motor branch without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Greater petrosal nerve → pterygopalatine ganglion (lacrimation); nerve to stapedius; chorda tympani (taste anterior 2/3, submandibular/sublingual parasympathetic)"]'::jsonb, 'Greater petrosal nerve → pterygopalatine ganglion (lacrimation); nerve to stapedius; chorda tympani (taste anterior 2/3, submandibular/sublingual parasympathetic)', 'Within the facial canal the facial nerve gives off the greater petrosal nerve for lacrimation, the nerve to stapedius, and the chorda tympani for taste and salivary innervation. These three branches explain why facial nerve lesions at different levels cause different combinations of deficits.'),
('39884d22-bec8-508c-aee8-b5ce404dbc4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Chorda tympani nerve (branch of CN VII) carries', '["Only taste resulting in decreased functional activity","General sensation from tongue (V3) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue (SVA) and parasympathetic to submandibular/sublingual glands (GVE)","Motor to tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue (SVA) and parasympathetic to submandibular/sublingual glands (GVE)', 'The chorda tympani conveys special visceral afferent taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular and sublingual glands. It does not carry general sensation, which reaches the tongue via V3, nor motor fibers.'),
('deadf357-2146-5145-aba0-1770d09474e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Greater petrosal nerve (branch of CN VII) carries', '["Parasympathetic to lacrimal, nasal, palatine glands (via pterygopalatine ganglion)","Taste from soft palate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","General sensation mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor to palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor to stapedius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic to lacrimal, nasal, palatine glands (via pterygopalatine ganglion)', 'The greater petrosal nerve carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion to supply the lacrimal, nasal, and palatine glands. Taste, general sensation, and motor branches of the facial nerve travel by other routes.'),
('6a9d4046-da38-5fd7-9c89-16c4603afeae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Nerve to stapedius (CN VII branch)', '["Stapedius muscle (attenuates loud sounds — sound dampening reflex). Lesion → hyperacusis (sounds too loud)","Tensor tympani muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Eustachian tube opening mediated by specific receptor activation","Stapes movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tympanic membrane tension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stapedius muscle (attenuates loud sounds — sound dampening reflex). Lesion → hyperacusis (sounds too loud)', 'The nerve to stapedius supplies the stapedius muscle, which damps the ossicular chain during loud sounds. When a facial nerve lesion paralyses the muscle, the ossicles move excessively and sounds seem too loud, producing hyperacusis.'),
('7e3b0e6a-59e3-5415-aad5-0e59d0a9c656'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VII exits the skull via', '["Internal acoustic meatus","Foramen spinosum","Foramen ovale","Stylomastoid foramen","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Stylomastoid foramen', 'The facial nerve leaves the skull through the stylomastoid foramen before dividing into its five extracranial motor branches. The internal acoustic meatus is its entry route into the temporal bone, and the jugular foramen, foramen ovale, and foramen spinosum carry other nerves and vessels.'),
('6caa7ed8-6b5d-5303-a665-27015ab6edb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve extracranial branches (motor) — P: temporal, Z: zygomatic, B: buccal, M: mandibular, C: cervical', '["Only temporal and cervical resulting in decreased functional activity","Only buccal and mandibular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All other muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Only zygomatic and buccal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","''Temporal/Zygomatic/Buccal/Mandibular/Cervical'' = muscles of facial expression"]'::jsonb, '''Temporal/Zygomatic/Buccal/Mandibular/Cervical'' = muscles of facial expression', 'After emerging from the stylomastoid foramen, the facial nerve divides into the temporal, zygomatic, buccal, mandibular, and cervical branches, which together innervate the muscles of facial expression. Limiting innervation to one or two of these branches misstates the full distribution.'),
('776001b2-6e4a-599a-9ff0-08687373d55b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Upper motor neuron (UMN) facial palsy (supranuclear) affects', '["Contralateral upper face only resulting in decreased functional activity","Entire ipsilateral face accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ipsilateral lower face only mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral face without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral lower quadrant of face (forehead spared)"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral lower quadrant of face (forehead spared)', 'A supranuclear lesion spares the forehead because upper facial motor neurons receive bilateral cortical input, so paralysis is limited to the contralateral lower face. Whole-face or ipsilateral-face weakness would indicate a lower motor neuron lesion.'),
('94c5bfc3-0b82-5ba9-979f-732ab63ad7a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Lower motor neuron (LMN) facial palsy (nuclear or infranuclear) affects', '["Contralateral lower face only resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper face only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Forehead only mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Entire ipsilateral face (including forehead) — Bell palsy is idiopathic LMN"]'::jsonb, 'Entire ipsilateral face (including forehead) — Bell palsy is idiopathic LMN', 'Lower motor neuron damage paralyses all ipsilateral facial muscles including the forehead, because the facial nucleus receives no significant crossed supply to the upper face. Bell palsy is the classic idiopathic example of this pattern.'),
('bd930b10-29a8-508a-9748-588a6686fe40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Bell palsy (idiopathic facial palsy) presents with', '["Only pain resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only taste change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only weakness mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Acute onset ipsilateral facial paralysis (all muscles), post-auricular pain, hyperacusis, altered taste (chorda tympani)","Only hyperacusis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute onset ipsilateral facial paralysis (all muscles), post-auricular pain, hyperacusis, altered taste (chorda tympani)', 'Bell palsy is an acute idiopathic lower motor neuron facial palsy affecting all ipsilateral muscles, often accompanied by post-auricular pain, hyperacusis from stapedius involvement, and taste change from chorda tympani involvement. Any single symptom alone is too narrow a description of the presentation.'),
('c6961f77-ee49-5ed6-889a-863027f29b47'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus)', '["CN VII palsy + vesicles in ear/ear canal (genicular herpes) due to VZV reactivation","VZV only vesicles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only pain mediated by specific receptor activation","HSV-1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII palsy + vesicles in ear/ear canal (genicular herpes) due to VZV reactivation', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome results from reactivation of varicella-zoster virus in the geniculate ganglion, causing facial nerve palsy together with herpetic vesicles in the ear and ear canal. Pain alone, vesicles without palsy, HSV-1, or isolated hearing loss do not define the syndrome.'),
('92216f0d-6e0d-5a8d-a3f5-14f6c563a072'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'In Bell palsy, prognosis is', '["All require surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Excellent: >85% recover completely within weeks to months","50% recovery mediated by specific receptor activation","recovery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poor, most have permanent weakness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excellent: >85% recover completely within weeks to months', 'The outlook in Bell palsy is generally excellent, with more than 85% of patients recovering completely within weeks to months. Permanent weakness, universal surgery, or an approximate 50% recovery rate do not reflect the usual good prognosis.'),
('2ab7e0ea-e5eb-5ca9-a6c2-6e04797912f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VII lesion proximal to geniculate ganglion (internal acoustic meatus)', '["Only lacrimation loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial palsy + taste loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial palsy + loss of taste, hyperacusis, decreased lacrimation (xerophthalmia), and decreased salivation","Facial palsy + hyperacusis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only facial palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial palsy + loss of taste, hyperacusis, decreased lacrimation (xerophthalmia), and decreased salivation', 'A lesion at or proximal to the geniculate ganglion interrupts the greater petrosal nerve, so lacrimation and salivation decrease along with loss of taste and stapedius function. The full combination distinguishes a proximal lesion from more distal facial nerve damage.'),
('010e2377-f0f1-5c58-a3e3-9174747d4fd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VII lesion between geniculate and chorda tympani', '["Facial palsy + loss of taste + hyperacusis (lacrimation preserved)","Facial palsy + taste loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only facial palsy mediated by specific receptor activation","change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hyperacusis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial palsy + loss of taste + hyperacusis (lacrimation preserved)', 'A lesion between the geniculate ganglion and the chorda tympani spares the greater petrosal nerve, so lacrimation stays intact while taste and stapedius function are lost. Preserved tearing is the key contrast with a more proximal facial nerve lesion.'),
('52b3638b-7e23-5528-986a-01cb6694a031'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VII lesion at stylomastoid foramen (distal)', '["facial palsy resulting in decreased functional activity","Only facial motor palsy (all other functions spared)","Only taste loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hyperacusis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lacrimation loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Only facial motor palsy (all other functions spared)', 'Damage at the stylomastoid foramen lies distal to all intratemporal branches, so only the motor fibers reaching the facial muscles are affected. Taste, lacrimation, salivation, and hearing are all preserved.'),
('848f3e70-f0c1-5621-83b0-a93f7bb18e03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VII is affected in Millard-Gubler syndrome (pontine lesion)', '["Ipsilateral CN VI + VII palsy + contralateral hemiplegia","Only CN VI palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral facial palsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Only facial palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hemiplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral CN VI + VII palsy + contralateral hemiplegia', 'Millard-Gubler syndrome results from a paramedian pontine infarct that damages the abducens nucleus, the facial nerve fibers, and the corticospinal tract, producing ipsilateral CN VI and VII palsy with contralateral hemiplegia. Isolated facial palsy, hemiplegia, or CN VI palsy lacks the full pontine pattern.'),
('0eccab82-f543-5f44-8ef2-a68f7469b7e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VIII (Vestibulocochlear) is', '["Parasympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pure sensory (special somatic afferent) — hearing (cochlear) and balance (vestibular)","Sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pure motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pure sensory (special somatic afferent) — hearing (cochlear) and balance (vestibular)', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve is purely sensory, carrying special somatic afferent fibers for hearing via its cochlear division and for balance via its vestibular division. It conveys no motor or autonomic fibers.'),
('9bc7c68c-dc31-5615-93bb-bb0999952a7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VIII exits skull via', '["Jugular foramen resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramen ovale accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal acoustic meatus (with CN VII)","Foramen magnum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stylomastoid foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal acoustic meatus (with CN VII)', 'CN VIII passes through the internal acoustic meatus together with CN VII to reach the petrous temporal bone. The jugular foramen, foramen ovale, foramen magnum, and stylomastoid foramen transmit other cranial nerves and vessels.'),
('e9f87e67-f53e-5a6a-a0a7-ebb3e2a32706'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Hearing pathway: sound → cochlea (hair cells) → spiral ganglion (CN VIII) → cochlear nucleus (medulla) → auditory nuclei', '["Inferior colliculus → MGN resulting in decreased functional activity","Direct to cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cochlear nucleus → MGN directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Spiral ganglion → cochlear nucleus → SOC/IC/MGB → auditory cortex (Heschl gyrus)","Thalamus → MGN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spiral ganglion → cochlear nucleus → SOC/IC/MGB → auditory cortex (Heschl gyrus)', 'The auditory pathway ascends from the spiral ganglion to the cochlear nuclei, then through the superior olivary complex, inferior colliculus, and medial geniculate body to the auditory cortex in Heschl gyrus. Skipping these relay stations would bypass the binaural and sound-localization processing of the system.'),
('231e6da6-e14b-5d4f-b38c-f798de59cf7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vestibular pathway: balance → vestibular ganglion (Scarpa) → vestibular nuclei (medulla/pons) → cerebellum, CN III/IV/VI, spinal cord', '["Only to spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Only to cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Correct: VOR, balance, posture, eye movements","Directly to cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only to CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: VOR, balance, posture, eye movements', 'Vestibular nuclei project widely to the cerebellum, to the extraocular nuclei via the medial longitudinal fasciculus, and down the spinal cord, supporting the vestibulo-ocular reflex, balance, posture, and coordinated eye movements. Restricting the projection to a single target understates these connections.'),
('f59b0e3f-4cb5-5e9a-8e87-e89c6b32996d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vestibular nuclei (superior, lateral, medial, inferior) receive CN VIII input and project to', '["Only spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum (ICP), extraocular nuclei (MLF), spinal cord (vestibulospinal), thalamus/cortex","Only eye muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum (ICP), extraocular nuclei (MLF), spinal cord (vestibulospinal), thalamus/cortex', 'The four vestibular nuclei send fibers to the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle, to the oculomotor nuclei through the MLF, to the spinal cord via vestibulospinal tracts, and to the cortex via the thalamus. Their connections are broad, so naming only one target understates their functional reach.'),
('6e245d83-b2cc-57f0-ba55-383402430441'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Acoustic neuroma (vestibular schwannoma):', '["CN VII tumor resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Glomus tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Schwann cell tumor of CN VIII (vestibular division) in internal acoustic meatus → hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo, later CN V, VII compression","Lipoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VIII meningioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Schwann cell tumor of CN VIII (vestibular division) in internal acoustic meatus → hearing loss, tinnitus, vertigo, later CN V, VII compression', 'An acoustic neuroma is a Schwann cell tumor of the vestibular division of CN VIII within the internal acoustic meatus, producing hearing loss, tinnitus, and vertigo, with later compression of CN V and VII as it grows. Glomus tumors, meningiomas, and lipomas are distinct lesions.'),
('118775e1-b441-5997-a964-e581f951269f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CPA syndrome (cerebellopontine angle mass) includes', '["Only hearing loss resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only vertigo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only tinnitus mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hearing loss (CN VIII), vertigo, facial numbness (CN V), facial weakness (CN VII), ataxia (cerebellum), headache, hydrocephalus","Only CN VII palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing loss (CN VIII), vertigo, facial numbness (CN V), facial weakness (CN VII), ataxia (cerebellum), headache, hydrocephalus', 'A mass in the cerebellopontine angle compresses adjacent structures: CN VIII (hearing loss, vertigo), CN V (facial numbness), CN VII (facial weakness), and the cerebellum and brainstem (ataxia, headache, hydrocephalus). The syndrome is multisymptomatic rather than limited to any single nerve.'),
('ff482a2c-329a-5885-80cb-3e66a1f167dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Acoustic neuroma treatment:', '["Only observation resulting in decreased functional activity","Observation, SRS (gamma knife), or microsurgery depending on size, hearing, and age","surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","radiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Observation, SRS (gamma knife), or microsurgery depending on size, hearing, and age', 'Management of an acoustic neuroma includes observation, stereotactic radiosurgery, or microsurgical resection, individualized according to tumor size, residual hearing, and patient age. No single approach applies to every case.'),
('dc279bb8-42c9-5c12-8f9d-d4db30b943a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN IX (Glossopharyngeal) is', '["Parasympathetic only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pure motor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Mixed: motor to stylopharyngeus (swallowing), taste to posterior 1/3 tongue, parasympathetic to parotid gland, general sensation to pharynx","Pure sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed: motor to stylopharyngeus (swallowing), taste to posterior 1/3 tongue, parasympathetic to parotid gland, general sensation to pharynx', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve is mixed: it supplies stylopharyngeus, taste and general sensation to the posterior one-third of the tongue, parasympathetic fibers to the parotid gland, and sensation from the pharynx. Pure motor, pure sensory, or parasympathetic-only labels each omit major components.'),
('902d0ab9-e1d5-56ff-a44a-c8a6bb608262'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Glossopharyngeal nerve exits skull via', '["Internal acoustic meatus","Foramen magnum","Jugular foramen (with CN X and XI)","Stylomastoid foramen","Foramen ovale"]'::jsonb, 'Jugular foramen (with CN X and XI)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve leaves the skull through the jugular foramen together with CN X and XI. The internal acoustic meatus, foramen magnum, stylomastoid foramen, and foramen ovale transmit other nerves.'),
('22a9ea8d-139a-5dfa-ac89-d825addc925b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN IX provides parasympathetic innervation to', '["Parotid gland (via otic ganglion) — from inferior salivatory nucleus","Lacrimal gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All salivary glands mediated by specific receptor activation","Submandibular gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sublingual gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parotid gland (via otic ganglion) — from inferior salivatory nucleus', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers of CN IX arise from the inferior salivatory nucleus and synapse in the otic ganglion, which innervates the parotid gland. The lacrimal, submandibular, and sublingual glands are supplied by the facial nerve rather than CN IX.'),
('5e55b05a-3f20-56f8-9461-45420fb48c03'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN IX sensory supply includes', '["Posterior 1/3 of tongue (taste + general sensation), pharynx, tonsils, Eustachian tube, carotid body, carotid sinus (baroreceptors, chemoreceptors)","Anterior 2/3 of tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Epiglottis mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior 1/3 of tongue (taste + general sensation), pharynx, tonsils, Eustachian tube, carotid body, carotid sinus (baroreceptors, chemoreceptors)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies taste and general sensation to the posterior one-third of the tongue plus sensation from the pharynx, tonsils, Eustachian tube, carotid body, and carotid sinus. The anterior tongue, epiglottis, palate, and larynx belong to CN VII, X, or V3.'),
('23d885c2-28a2-502c-b2fd-4d7cb5b8f859'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Carotid sinus reflex (baroreceptor) — afferent limb is', '["CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX (glossopharyngeal) from carotid sinus; efferent: CN X (vagus) and SNS","CN VIII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN X (vagus) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal) from carotid sinus; efferent: CN X (vagus) and SNS', 'Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus send afferent impulses along CN IX, and the efferent limb runs through CN X and sympathetic nerves to adjust heart rate and vascular tone. CN V, VII, VIII, and CN X alone do not complete the reflex arc.'),
('a52cc657-4350-5930-99a7-ae5b7cfd6d66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Carotid body chemoreceptors (hypoxia detection) — afferent is', '["CN X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VIII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX (glossopharyngeal) — responds to hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis → increased ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal) — responds to hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis → increased ventilation', 'The carotid body detects hypoxia, hypercapnia, and acidosis and signals via the glossopharyngeal nerve, driving an increase in ventilation. CN V, VII, VIII, and X do not carry this chemoreceptive input.'),
('c9c3b22a-4ccd-5166-967e-c59c7db65d1c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Glossopharyngeal neuralgia is characterized by', '["Only tongue pain resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Paroxysmal pain in the throat, tonsillar region, base of tongue (triggered by swallowing, yawning, coughing) — rare but similar to trigeminal neuralgia","trigger mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pharyngeal pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ear pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paroxysmal pain in the throat, tonsillar region, base of tongue (triggered by swallowing, yawning, coughing) — rare but similar to trigeminal neuralgia', 'Glossopharyngeal neuralgia produces brief, paroxysmal pain in the throat, tonsillar region, and base of tongue, often triggered by swallowing, yawning, or coughing, and is rarer than trigeminal neuralgia. Confining the pain to a single site or a single trigger misses the characteristic pattern.'),
('5511395c-510b-517b-9e9b-ab7825fbf6c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN X (Vagus) is', '["Pure sensory resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor and parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pure parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Pure motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mixed: motor to pharynx/larynx (palate, pharynx, larynx), taste from epiglottis, parasympathetic to thoracoabdominal viscera, general sensation from larynx, external ear, meninges"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed: motor to pharynx/larynx (palate, pharynx, larynx), taste from epiglottis, parasympathetic to thoracoabdominal viscera, general sensation from larynx, external ear, meninges', 'The vagus nerve is mixed: it supplies motor fibers to the pharynx and larynx, taste from the epiglottis, parasympathetic innervation to thoracic and abdominal viscera, and general sensation from the larynx, external ear, and meninges. Pure motor, pure sensory, or parasympathetic-only labels are incomplete.'),
('9d9fe161-77a1-527a-afae-5b06a2b9cb95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vagus nerve exits skull via', '["Foramen ovale","Stylomastoid foramen","Foramen magnum","Internal acoustic meatus","Jugular foramen (with CN IX and XI)"]'::jsonb, 'Jugular foramen (with CN IX and XI)', 'The vagus exits the skull with the glossopharyngeal and spinal accessory nerves through the jugular foramen. The other foramina serve different structures: foramen ovale carries V3, the stylomastoid foramen CN VII, the internal acoustic meatus CN VII/VIII, and the foramen magnum the spinal cord and spinal accessory spinal root.'),
('4e93074f-c280-5692-bedb-1db3a701f60b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN X has two ganglia: superior (jugular) and inferior (nodose) ganglia — containing', '["Sympathetic fibers resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","cell bodies mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","General sensory (superior, cell bodies) and visceral sensory (inferior, nodose, cell bodies for taste and visceral afferents)","Parasympathetic fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'General sensory (superior, cell bodies) and visceral sensory (inferior, nodose, cell bodies for taste and visceral afferents)', 'The jugular ganglion contains general somatic afferent cell bodies for the ear and dura, while the nodose (inferior) ganglion holds visceral afferent cell bodies carrying taste and viscerosensory information. Motor and parasympathetic fibers merely pass through these ganglia without synapsing, so options naming fibers or a single fiber class are wrong.'),
('f6524d11-8885-5644-84e6-16527da5f528'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Recurrent laryngeal nerve (branch of CN X)', '["Only motor to trachea resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Innervates all laryngeal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only sensory to larynx mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Innervates cricothyroid alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Innervates all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid (which is from external superior laryngeal) — paralysis → hoarseness, vocal cord paresis"]'::jsonb, 'Innervates all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid (which is from external superior laryngeal) — paralysis → hoarseness, vocal cord paresis', 'The recurrent laryngeal nerve supplies every intrinsic laryngeal muscle except the cricothyroid, which is innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve. Damage therefore causes vocal cord paresis and hoarseness, not tracheal motor, pure sensory, or cricothyroid-only involvement.'),
('6e7d593a-8ba5-52b0-b818-8a355e177dc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Left recurrent laryngeal nerve is longer because it loops under', '["Innominate artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracic duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum) — left recurrent laryngeal nerve","Subclavian artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carotid artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic arch (ligamentum arteriosum) — left recurrent laryngeal nerve', 'On the left, the recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks beneath the aortic arch at the ligamentum arteriosum, making it longer than the right, which loops under the right subclavian artery. The innominate, subclavian, and carotid vessels and thoracic duct do not define this course.'),
('5242e05b-4210-552c-abee-a9875e899e4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Superior laryngeal nerve (CN X) divides into', '["Only internal resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor to all larynx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only external mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal (sensory above vocal cords) and external (motor to cricothyroid — tension/adduction of vocal cords)","Sensory to entire larynx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal (sensory above vocal cords) and external (motor to cricothyroid — tension/adduction of vocal cords)', 'The superior laryngeal nerve splits into an internal branch giving sensation to the larynx above the vocal cords and an external branch providing motor supply to the cricothyroid, which tenses and adducts the cords. Options naming only one branch or assigning sensation to the whole larynx omit part of its function.'),
('433bf485-5db4-5faa-8610-27d93ee88525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vagus nerve motor to palatal/pharyngeal muscles via pharyngeal plexus', '["Only levator veli palatini resulting in decreased functional activity","Levator veli palatini, salpingopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, palatoglossus, and middle constrictor","Only constrictors mediated by specific receptor activation","Only palatopharyngeus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All palate muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Levator veli palatini, salpingopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, palatoglossus, and middle constrictor', 'Through the pharyngeal plexus, the vagus innervates levator veli palatini, salpingopharyngeus, palatopharyngeus, palatoglossus, and the middle constrictor; tensor veli palatini is the exception, supplied by V3. Choices listing only one muscle or all palate muscles without the plexus distribution are incomplete or wrong.'),
('fb073d21-d83a-50e2-9934-9840ab54a728'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN X lesion (unilateral) below the origin of pharyngeal and laryngeal branches (i.e., in the neck)', '["Only dysphagia resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilateral palatal palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hoarseness mediated by specific receptor activation","symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipsilateral palatal droop (uvula away), absent gag (same side), hoarseness, weak cough, dysphagia"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral palatal droop (uvula away), absent gag (same side), hoarseness, weak cough, dysphagia', 'Unilateral vagus nerve palsy produces ipsilateral soft palate paralysis with the uvula pulled toward the intact side, absent gag on the affected side, and hoarseness with a weak cough from laryngeal involvement; pharyngeal weakness adds dysphagia. Bilateral involvement or an isolated single sign does not reflect the distribution of the vagus.'),
('5e5b7751-3169-512c-8b55-7893e13f513f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN X lesion (unilateral high/above pharyngeal branch)', '["Additional: loss of pharyngeal constriction, nasal regurgitation, but still some CN X branches","Only pharyngeal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only laryngeal mediated by specific receptor activation","Same as low without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only palatal weakness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Additional: loss of pharyngeal constriction, nasal regurgitation, but still some CN X branches', 'Because the pharyngeal branches are lost, a high vagal lesion adds paralysis of pharyngeal constriction with nasal regurgitation, yet more distal vagal branches may be partly spared. ''Same as low'' is wrong, and single-sign choices omit the additional pharyngeal deficit.'),
('1e2d229c-367a-5c15-8023-6e83762fd130'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN X (bilateral) lesion causes', '["Only hoarseness resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only dysphagia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","symptoms mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only autonomic without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Severe dysphagia, dysarthria (nasal speech), bilateral vocal cord paralysis (airway compromise), loss of gag, autonomic loss"]'::jsonb, 'Severe dysphagia, dysarthria (nasal speech), bilateral vocal cord paralysis (airway compromise), loss of gag, autonomic loss', 'Bilateral vagal loss produces flaccid palate and pharynx with severe dysphagia, nasal speech from palatal paralysis, bilateral abductor vocal cord paralysis threatening the airway, an absent gag, and loss of vagal autonomic supply to the thoracic and abdominal viscera. Isolated single-symptom choices miss the widespread bilateral deficits.'),
('5a305821-970e-50ba-8a1c-471eb2be6ca7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Taste for gag reflex — afferent: CN IX (posterior tongue/pharynx); efferent = CN X (pharyngeal muscles)', '["CN VII efferent","CN X afferent, CN IX efferent","CN XI afferent","CN V afferent","Correct: CN IX afferent, CN X efferent"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: CN IX afferent, CN X efferent', 'The gag reflex depends on the glossopharyngeal nerve sensing the posterior tongue and pharynx while the vagus drives pharyngeal constriction. Swapping the two limbs or substituting CN VII, V, or XI misrepresents the reflex arc.'),
('a396b141-cae9-50cc-a872-130fd2039c0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN XI (Spinal Accessory) is', '["Motor (SVE) to sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles","Pure sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both motor and parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Visceral motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mixed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor (SVE) to sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles', 'The spinal accessory is a purely motor (special visceral efferent) nerve supplying the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. It carries no sensory, parasympathetic, or general visceral motor fibers.'),
('1171b98d-6c3b-55f6-85aa-cf160ac30e06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN XI has two roots: cranial (bulbar) and spinal', '["Only one root resulting in decreased functional activity","Cranial root joins CN X (nucleus ambiguus → laryngeal muscles); spinal root from C1-C5 → SCM and trapezius","Cranial → SCM, spinal → trapezius mediated by specific receptor activation","Cranial → trapezius, spinal → SCM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both roots → SCM/trapezius leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial root joins CN X (nucleus ambiguus → laryngeal muscles); spinal root from C1-C5 → SCM and trapezius', 'The cranial (bulbar) root arises from the nucleus ambiguus and joins the vagus to reach laryngeal muscles, while the spinal root originates in the upper cervical cord and supplies SCM and trapezius. Assigning both roots to the same muscles, or reversing their targets, is incorrect.'),
('e0fd6a32-b803-5767-a30c-95addaba57da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Spinal accessory nerve traverses the jugular foramen (with CN IX and X)', '["Foramen magnum resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior orbital fissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoglossal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Common sheath — then separates from CN X; courses to SCM and trapezius"]'::jsonb, 'Common sheath — then separates from CN X; courses to SCM and trapezius', 'Within the jugular foramen the accessory shares a common dural sheath with the vagus, then separates from it and courses to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius. The other foramina transmit different nerves: foramen magnum carries the spinal cord, the hypoglossal canal CN XII, the superior orbital fissure V1/III/IV/VI, and the optic canal CN II.'),
('6af73921-8af2-5a52-8602-8ef03f739a30'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN XI palsy causes', '["Only arm weakness resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Weakness of SCM (turning head to opposite side) and trapezius (shrugging shoulder, lifting arm) — drooping shoulder, scapular winging","Only sternocleidomastoid weakness mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only trapezius weakness without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck flexion weakness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weakness of SCM (turning head to opposite side) and trapezius (shrugging shoulder, lifting arm) — drooping shoulder, scapular winging', 'Accessory nerve palsy weakens the SCM, so turning the head to the opposite side becomes difficult, and the trapezius, producing shoulder droop, weak shrugging, and scapular winging. Isolating either muscle overlooks the dual supply of this nerve.'),
('878d1573-bed3-540c-b763-055eb0d424b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN XII (Hypoglossal) is', '["Parasympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Pure sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pure motor (somatic efferent) to all intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue (except palatoglossus = CN X)","Mixed sensory and motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pure motor (somatic efferent) to all intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue (except palatoglossus = CN X)', 'The hypoglossal nerve is purely motor (general somatic efferent) to all intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles, with palatoglossus the single exception supplied by CN X through the pharyngeal plexus. It has no sensory, sympathetic, or parasympathetic component.'),
('b1a48204-38cb-5dab-974f-794a8220830b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Hypoglossal nucleus is located in', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla (floor of fourth ventricle, near midline, in hypoglossal trigone)","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (floor of fourth ventricle, near midline, in hypoglossal trigone)', 'The hypoglossal nucleus lies in the medulla near the midline of the fourth ventricle floor, where it forms the hypoglossal trigone. It is a medullary structure, not pontine, mesencephalic, cerebellar, or spinal.'),
('85da4b7d-2e75-5a14-bf2a-fa7ac6185391'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN XII exits the skull via', '["Foramen ovale","Foramen magnum","Stylomastoid foramen","Hypoglossal canal (occipital bone)","Jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal canal (occipital bone)', 'The hypoglossal nerve leaves the skull through the hypoglossal canal of the occipital bone. The other foramina carry different structures: foramen ovale transmits V3, the stylomastoid foramen CN VII, the foramen magnum the spinal cord and spinal accessory spinal root, and the jugular foramen CN IX, X, and XI.'),
('209fdf56-20c1-572d-874c-a1e9429723ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Genioglossus muscle (CN XII) protrudes the tongue — when one side contracts, the tongue deviates to the OPPOSITE side', '["Ipsilateral deviation resulting in decreased functional activity","Deviation downward accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deviation upward mediated by specific receptor activation","Correct: genioglossus → tongue protrusion and contralateral deviation","deviation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: genioglossus → tongue protrusion and contralateral deviation', 'A single genioglossus pulls the tongue forward and toward the opposite side, so unilateral contraction deviates the tongue contralaterally. Ipsilateral, upward, or downward deviation does not match the vector of genioglossus action.'),
('6c80580c-1e86-5cf9-8764-8b23c003d5f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN XII palsy (unilateral):', '["Deviates away from lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Contralateral wasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","fasciculations mediated by specific receptor activation","deviation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tongue deviates toward the side of the lesion (weak genioglossus) on protrusion; ipsilateral wasting, fasciculations"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue deviates toward the side of the lesion (weak genioglossus) on protrusion; ipsilateral wasting, fasciculations', 'With a lower motor neuron hypoglossal palsy, the weak genioglossus allows the tongue to be pushed toward the lesioned side on protrusion, and that side shows wasting with fasciculations because it is denervated. Deviation away from the lesion, contralateral wasting, or absent fasciculations indicate a different lesion type.'),
('119d57c3-6313-59e4-a27b-45d64b59bbbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion of CN XII (supranuclear)', '["Fasciculations present resulting in decreased functional activity","Deviates toward lesion (ipsilateral weakness) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","deviation mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrophy present without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tongue deviates away from lesion (contralateral weakness) — no atrophy/fasciculations"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue deviates away from lesion (contralateral weakness) — no atrophy/fasciculations', 'A supranuclear lesion produces contralateral weakness of the genioglossus, so the tongue deviates away from the lesion, and because the muscle is not denervated there is no atrophy or fasciculation. Atrophy and fasciculations point to a lower motor neuron lesion instead.'),
('a9d59154-e45b-5031-966f-518e7b0f61b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion of CN XII (nuclear/infranuclear)', '["Deviates away from lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tongue deviates toward lesion (ipsilateral weakness) — with atrophy, fasciculations","Contralateral weakness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","fasciculations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue deviates toward lesion (ipsilateral weakness) — with atrophy, fasciculations', 'A nuclear or infranuclear lesion denervates the ipsilateral tongue muscles, so the tongue deviates toward the lesion and shows atrophy with fasciculations. Contralateral weakness and deviation away from the lesion in a UMN picture do not apply here.'),
('b61dac37-479f-5378-adf4-071d4f3b1132'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Midline hypoglossal nucleus receives bilateral corticobulbar input (exception — only contralateral for genioglossus portion)', '["Most hypoglossal motor pool is bilateral (except genioglossus which is mainly contralateral)","bilateral input accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All contralateral mediated by specific receptor activation","All ipsilateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hypoglossal has UMN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most hypoglossal motor pool is bilateral (except genioglossus which is mainly contralateral)', 'Most hypoglossal motorneurons receive bilateral corticobulbar input, which largely spares tongue movement after a unilateral supranuclear lesion; the genioglossus portion is chiefly contralateral, which explains why a UMN lesion still deviates the tongue away from the lesion. Making all input unilateral or calling the whole nerve contralateral is incorrect.'),
('22e057b4-dbd7-5e43-b385-e7e959ad89b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve nuclei locations (motor):', '["All in pons resulting in decreased functional activity","III, IV (midbrain), V, VI, VII, VIII (pons), IX, X, XI, XII (medulla)","All in medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","III-VI in pons, VII-XII in medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All in midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'III, IV (midbrain), V, VI, VII, VIII (pons), IX, X, XI, XII (medulla)', 'Motor cranial nerve nuclei are distributed by brainstem level: III and IV in the midbrain, V, VI, VII, and VIII in the pons, and IX, X, XI, and XII in the medulla. Any scheme placing all nuclei at a single level is wrong.'),
('d811dd66-a6c3-5143-992b-5affec7a1b74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve nuclei locations (sensory):', '["V (spinal, principal, mesencephalic), VIII (cochlear/vestibular), solitary (VII, IX, X taste) — in medulla/pons","All in thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All in midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","All in medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All in pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'V (spinal, principal, mesencephalic), VIII (cochlear/vestibular), solitary (VII, IX, X taste) — in medulla/pons', 'Sensory cranial nerve nuclei include the trigeminal nuclear complex, the cochlear and vestibular nuclei, and the solitary nucleus, which receives taste from CN VII, IX, and X; these lie in the medulla and pons. The midbrain, thalamus, and single-level schemes are incorrect for these nuclei.'),
('2d862fd4-20b1-5c2b-bedc-ad0b1f83d169'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The three divisions of trigeminal (V1, V2, V3) exit via', '["V1 = SOF, V2 = jugular foramen, V3 = foramen magnum","V1 = superior orbital fissure; V2 = foramen rotundum; V3 = foramen ovale","All through foramen ovale","V1/V2 = SOF, V3 = foramen rotundum","V1 = foramen rotundum, V2 = SOF, V3 = jugular foramen"]'::jsonb, 'V1 = superior orbital fissure; V2 = foramen rotundum; V3 = foramen ovale', 'The ophthalmic division exits via the superior orbital fissure, the maxillary via the foramen rotundum, and the mandibular via the foramen ovale. Mixing these exits, or routing divisions through the jugular foramen or foramen magnum, is incorrect because those foramina transmit other structures.'),
('ebb01751-6bfb-5df9-a1d8-4859e6f30612'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve (CN VII) motor cortex representation is', '["Moderate resulting in decreased functional activity","A large (closest to body representation in the homunculus) — large area for fine facial expression","Contralateral only mediated by specific receptor activation","representation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large (closest to body representation in the homunculus) — large area for fine facial expression', 'The motor homunculus devotes a large cortical area to facial movement because facial expression requires fine, precise control. A small or moderate representation could not support the finely graded movements the face performs.'),
('b2e8e742-0f8a-57f5-8f7d-7262874be35f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The nervus intermedius is the sensory/parasympathetic root of CN VII', '["Only taste resulting in decreased functional activity","Carries taste from anterior 2/3 tongue (to NTS) and preganglionic parasympathetic (salivatory/lacrimal)","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carries taste from anterior 2/3 tongue (to NTS) and preganglionic parasympathetic (salivatory/lacrimal)', 'The nervus intermedius carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue via the chorda tympani to the solitary nucleus and preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the superior salivatory and lacrimal nuclei. Options offering only taste or only one fiber class omit its dual function.'),
('f758ce9d-12ec-5d7b-805f-25e520517036'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Geniculate ganglion (CN VII) contains', '["Sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste (SVA) cell bodies for anterior 2/3 of tongue (pseudounipolar)","Motor fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","cell bodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taste (SVA) cell bodies for anterior 2/3 of tongue (pseudounipolar)', 'The geniculate ganglion houses the pseudounipolar cell bodies of taste fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. It contains no motor, sympathetic, or parasympathetic cell bodies, since those fibers merely traverse the nerve.'),
('e2f1c19e-7e1a-509a-a6e9-843c669b3436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Ciliary ganglion (parasympathetic) is associated with which cranial nerve?', '["CN X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN III (Edinger-Westphal) — sphincter pupillae, ciliary muscle (accommodation)","CN V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III (Edinger-Westphal) — sphincter pupillae, ciliary muscle (accommodation)', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel in CN III to synapse in the ciliary ganglion, which innervates the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. The other ganglia are served by CN VII or CN IX, not CN III.'),
('b23b6de6-cca6-5ac6-aa87-a35657a6229c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Pterygopalatine ganglion (parasympathetic) is associated with which cranial nerve?', '["CN III resulting in decreased functional activity","CN X accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII (greater petrosal) — lacrimal, nasal, palantine glands","CN IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII (greater petrosal) — lacrimal, nasal, palantine glands', 'The greater petrosal branch of CN VII carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers that synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion to supply the lacrimal, nasal, and palatine glands. CN III, V, IX, and X are not the source for this ganglion.'),
('de19b1a9-4094-5df4-bbc0-6400b7cae537'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Submandibular ganglion (parasympathetic) is associated with which cranial nerve?', '["CN X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII (chorda tympani) — submandibular and sublingual salivation","CN V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII (chorda tympani) — submandibular and sublingual salivation', 'The chorda tympani branch of CN VII synapses in the submandibular ganglion to drive secretion from the submandibular and sublingual glands. CN III, V, IX, and X do not provide this preganglionic supply.'),
('7519fa01-62b4-5540-baa7-348fc0024047'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Otic ganglion (parasympathetic) is associated with which cranial nerve?', '["CN IX (tympanic nerve/lesser petrosal) — parotid gland secretion","CN V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN X mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (tympanic nerve/lesser petrosal) — parotid gland secretion', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers travel with the tympanic and lesser petrosal branches of CN IX and synapse in the otic ganglion to drive parotid secretion. The other cranial nerves listed do not supply this ganglion.'),
('d1472268-fb55-598d-b534-d7aaf2d09d5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Tympanic nerve (Jacobson nerve) is a branch of', '["CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX (glossopharyngeal) — provides parasympathetic (lesser petrosal) and general sensation to middle ear","CN V mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN X without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VIII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal) — provides parasympathetic (lesser petrosal) and general sensation to middle ear', 'The tympanic (Jacobson) nerve arises from the glossopharyngeal nerve, carries the preganglionic parasympathetic root that continues as the lesser petrosal nerve, and supplies general sensation to the middle ear. CN V, VII, VIII, and X do not give rise to this nerve.'),
('cd8d44d4-fe49-5cb8-a4d2-90d1f58f20cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Chorda tympani runs through which space?', '["Foramen ovale resulting in decreased functional activity","Jugular foramen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior orbital fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal acoustic meatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle ear (between malleus and incus) — then joins lingual nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear (between malleus and incus) — then joins lingual nerve', 'After emerging from the facial canal, the chorda tympani crosses the middle ear between the malleus and incus before joining the lingual nerve. The other foramina and fissures listed are not traversed by this nerve.'),
('05930db5-e8bd-5415-9658-ca56bb08875c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The eustachian tube (auditory tube) is innervated by', '["CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity","V3 (tensor veli palatini — motor via V3) and CN IX (sensory)","CN XII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VIII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'V3 (tensor veli palatini — motor via V3) and CN IX (sensory)', 'The auditory tube is opened by the tensor veli palatini, whose motor supply comes from V3, and its sensory innervation comes from CN IX. The other cranial nerves listed do not provide this supply.'),
('88dbafca-e6c4-543c-b1a1-b053eb8500b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cochlear nerve (CN VIII) projects to which nuclei?', '["Inferior colliculus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior olive only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum only mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular nuclei only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior and posterior cochlear nuclei (medulla/pons)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior and posterior cochlear nuclei (medulla/pons)', 'The cochlear nerve synapses in the anterior and posterior cochlear nuclei at the pontomedullary junction before ascending through the lemniscal pathways. Inferior colliculus, superior olive, cerebellum, and vestibular nuclei are downstream or separate targets.'),
('d5532186-b743-5aa1-9f9a-5ae50dda13a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vestibular nerve (CN VIII) projects to which nuclei?', '["Only to spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Only to MLF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vestibular nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular nuclei (four: superior, lateral, medial, inferior) and cerebellum (directly)","Only to cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nuclei (four: superior, lateral, medial, inferior) and cerebellum (directly)', 'Vestibular afferents synapse in the four vestibular nuclei (superior, lateral, medial, inferior) and also project directly to the cerebellum. Projections to spinal cord or MLF are efferents leaving the nuclei, not primary targets of the nerve.'),
('474f171d-1120-58ad-be28-5ac21787298e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The superior vestibular nucleus (Bechterew) mediates', '["Horizontal VOR resulting in decreased functional activity","Hearing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posture mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertical VOR and upward gaze","Head tilt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical VOR and upward gaze', 'The superior vestibular nucleus drives vertical vestibulo-ocular responses and upward gaze, largely through the medial longitudinal fasciculus. Horizontal VOR and posture are handled by other vestibular nuclei, while hearing is cochlear.'),
('9826dbca-2837-577e-afc5-87f8a66a60d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The inferior vestibular nucleus (spinal) projects to', '["Cerebellum and spinal cord (vestibular-cerebellar integration)","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior colliculus mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eye muscles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum and spinal cord (vestibular-cerebellar integration)', 'The inferior (spinal) vestibular nucleus projects to the cerebellum and spinal cord, integrating vestibular input with motor control of the neck and body. Projections to the thalamus, superior colliculus, auditory cortex, or direct eye muscles are not among its efferents.'),
('4513218d-d18b-5077-baaf-be826bb8adef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The corneal reflex (blink to corneal touch) afferent = CN V1, efferent = CN VII', '["CN VIII efferent resulting in decreased functional activity","CN II afferent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Correct: touch cornea → V1 afferent → trigeminal sensory → facial motor nucleus → CN VII efferent → blink","CN VI afferent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III efferent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: touch cornea → V1 afferent → trigeminal sensory → facial motor nucleus → CN VII efferent → blink', 'Corneal touch travels in V1 (ophthalmic), relays through the trigeminal sensory nucleus, and closes the eyelids via a CN VII efferent to the orbicularis oculi. No other cranial nerve supplies either limb of this reflex arc.'),
('6bde5986-59c0-5706-8bd4-dfd0e5538908'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The sneeze reflex (nasal irritation) involves', '["Only CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN V mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V (afferent) → medullary center → motor output to respiratory muscles (phrenic, intercostal) + CN VII, IX, X","Only phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (afferent) → medullary center → motor output to respiratory muscles (phrenic, intercostal) + CN VII, IX, X', 'Nasal irritation is sensed by CN V and coordinated in medullary respiratory centers, with motor output through the phrenic and intercostal nerves plus CN VII, IX, and X. Limiting the reflex to one nerve ignores its full afferent-efferent organization.'),
('9c98df4d-b128-55a7-8d3a-17ac5136d898'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The swallowing reflex involves cranial nerves', '["Only CN X and XII resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN V and XII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN VII and IX mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CN IX and X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN V, VII, IX, X, XII (and motor to pharynx/esophagus via vagus and spinal cord)"]'::jsonb, 'CN V, VII, IX, X, XII (and motor to pharynx/esophagus via vagus and spinal cord)', 'Swallowing recruits CN V for mastication and tensor veli palatini, CN VII for labial seal, CN IX and X for pharyngeal sensation and constriction, and CN XII for tongue propulsion, with vagal and spinal cord motor supply to the pharyngeal muscles. A formulation naming only two nerves is incomplete.'),
('22074cf6-3068-55b7-8f0a-3c86b71dd974'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Taste pathway (CN VII, IX, X) → solitary nucleus (NTS) → VPMpc (thalamus) → insular/operculum (gustatory cortex)', '["NTS → amygdala directly resulting in decreased functional activity","NTS → midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NTS → cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Correct: NTS (medulla) → central tegmental tract → VPMpc (thalamus) → insular cortex (area 43/GI)","NTS → hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: NTS (medulla) → central tegmental tract → VPMpc (thalamus) → insular cortex (area 43/GI)', 'Taste fibers synapse in the solitary nucleus, ascend via the central tegmental tract to the parvicellular ventral posteromedial nucleus (VPMpc) of the thalamus, and terminate in the gustatory insular cortex. Direct relays to the amygdala, midbrain, cerebellum, or hypothalamus describe different pathways.'),
('e4e0fc13-92d4-5926-9140-19e2b48a57a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial parasympathetic: superior salivatory nucleus (pons) → CN VII → pterygopalatine (lacrimal) and submandibular (salivary) ganglia', '["All from CN IX resulting in decreased functional activity","Correct: superior salivatory = CN VII; inferior salivatory (medulla) = CN IX","All from CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior = IX, Inferior = VII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both from NTS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: superior salivatory = CN VII; inferior salivatory (medulla) = CN IX', 'The superior salivatory nucleus supplies CN VII to the pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia, while the inferior salivatory nucleus in the medulla supplies CN IX to the otic ganglion. Swapping or combining these nuclei misassigns the parasympathetic outflow.'),
('6346b8aa-8839-5be0-9c91-e1296d105ed5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Parotid gland parasympathetic innervation = CN IX (inferior salivatory → otic ganglion)', '["None resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III (ciliary ganglion) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX (tympanic → lesser petrosal → otic ganglion)","CN X (vagus) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII (chorda tympani) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (tympanic → lesser petrosal → otic ganglion)', 'Parotid parasympathetic fibers travel in the glossopharyngeal nerve via the tympanic and lesser petrosal nerves to synapse in the otic ganglion. None of the other nerves listed reaches the parotid through this route.'),
('0f89231b-119e-5a27-b622-8574da564819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Submandibular and sublingual glands parasympathetic = CN VII (chorda tympani → submandibular ganglion)', '["CN VII (chorda tympani)","CN III (ciliary)","CN X (vagus)","None","CN IX (tympanic)"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII (chorda tympani)', 'Chordae tympani fibers of CN VII synapse in the submandibular ganglion to stimulate the submandibular and sublingual glands. The tympanic (CN IX), ciliary (CN III), and vagal pathways supply different targets.'),
('7548b9bf-7291-5787-9f0e-033d6f240078'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Lacrimal gland parasympathetic = CN VII (greater petrosal → pterygopalatine ganglion → zygomatic → lacrimal)', '["CN X (pharyngeal)","None","CN VII (greater petrosal)","CN III (Edinger-Westphal)","CN IX (lesser petrosal)"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII (greater petrosal)', 'Lacrimal secretion is driven by the greater petrosal fibers of CN VII, which synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion and reach the gland via the zygomatic branch of V2. CN III, IX, and X do not supply lacrimal innervation.'),
('cc6736d6-d55f-5d7f-bebe-65af7ec4fbb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Paralysis of lateral gaze to one side (conjugate gaze palsy) — pontine (PPRF) lesion', '["Cannot look down resulting in decreased functional activity","All gaze paralysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cannot look up mediated by specific receptor activation","Eyes cannot look toward the side of the lesion (ipsilateral)","Cannot look contralaterally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eyes cannot look toward the side of the lesion (ipsilateral)', 'The pontine paramedian pontine reticular formation (PPRF) drives ipsilateral horizontal gaze, so a lesion abolishes conjugate gaze toward the side of damage. Vertical gaze failure or all-direction paralysis reflects involvement of different structures.'),
('82e88286-74ca-528e-ad95-c2cde285c71d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'One-and-a-half syndrome = PPRF + MLF (on same side)', '["Only abduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Only vertical gaze accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only one eye can abduct (contralateral); no horizontal movement in ipsilateral eye or contralateral adduction","Only adduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only horizontal gaze leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Only one eye can abduct (contralateral); no horizontal movement in ipsilateral eye or contralateral adduction', 'Combined ipsilateral PPRF and MLF damage abolishes all ipsilateral horizontal gaze and contralateral adduction, leaving only abduction of the opposite eye. Naming a single type of ocular movement fails to capture this specific pattern.'),
('b940b2b2-8ce2-56dc-b74d-39f61c8bbc91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III) reinnervation after injury can cause', '["Complete recovery resulting in decreased functional activity","Diplopia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aberrant regeneration: lid lag, pupillary-gaze synkinesis (pupil constricts on adduction/depression)","Ptosis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","recovery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aberrant regeneration: lid lag, pupillary-gaze synkinesis (pupil constricts on adduction/depression)', 'Misrouted regenerating CN III fibers can produce synkinesis, such as pupillary constriction during adduction or depression, along with lid lag after an oculomotor injury. Full spontaneous recovery is unusual, and lasting diplopia or ptosis alone does not describe aberrant reinnervation.'),
('edc816ec-74c8-57a9-a853-3f517f57d1e3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Horner syndrome (sympathetic chain disruption) involves', '["Ptosis (mild), miosis (pupil constricted), anhidrosis (loss of sweating), enophthalmos (slight), and flushing on face","Miosis + hyperhidrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All eye changes only mediated by specific receptor activation","Ptosis + pupil larger without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ptosis + mydriasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ptosis (mild), miosis (pupil constricted), anhidrosis (loss of sweating), enophthalmos (slight), and flushing on face', 'Horner syndrome follows interruption of the oculosympathetic pathway, leaving the parasympathetic pupilloconstrictor unopposed, so the eye shows miosis plus mild ptosis (Muller muscle) and ipsilateral anhidrosis with flushing. Mydriasis and hyperhidrosis are opposite findings, and the signs are not limited to the eye.'),
('810d5527-ae00-508d-a4ab-0e65b840265a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Central Horner (first-order) + ipsilateral face/body sensory loss suggests', '["Pancoast tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Cluster headache accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pons lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Wallenberg (lateral medullary) syndrome","Carotid dissection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wallenberg (lateral medullary) syndrome', 'A central (first-order) Horner comes from a lesion of the descending hypothalamospinal sympathetic tract in the lateral medulla; Wallenberg (lateral medullary) syndrome combines this with ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature over the face and body. Preganglionic and postganglionic lesions localize to the chest/cervical spine and internal carotid artery respectively, not the medulla.'),
('f383f4e4-5869-5c6a-9560-22136dfe1313'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Preganglionic Horner (second-order, T1 root) with ipsilateral hand/arm pain suggests', '["Carotid dissection resulting in decreased functional activity","Brainstem stroke accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cluster headache mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancoast tumor (apical lung cancer) → symptoms in lower trunk (C8-T1)","Hypothalamic lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancoast tumor (apical lung cancer) → symptoms in lower trunk (C8-T1)', 'Second-order (preganglionic) Horner arises from a T1 cord root lesion, and an apical (Pancoast) lung tumor compressing the lower trunk (C8-T1) produces ipsilateral Horner together with pain in the hand and arm. Carotid dissection, cluster headache, and brainstem or hypothalamic lesions do not cause preganglionic Horner.'),
('db9d89b8-6e4b-53cc-9ea5-c912c3e6246b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Postganglionic Horner (third-order, SCG to ICA) suggests', '["Carotid artery dissection → ipsilateral Horner + head/neck pain","T1 nerve root lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancoast tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypothalamic lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brainstem stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid artery dissection → ipsilateral Horner + head/neck pain', 'Third-order (postganglionic) Horner follows damage to the sympathetic plexus around the internal carotid artery, so carotid artery dissection gives ipsilateral Horner with head and neck pain. T1 root lesions (Pancoast) and brainstem or hypothalamic lesions are first- or second-order sites.'),
('10a94802-bf0d-5e1e-a1ba-f856b446676c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Raeder paratrigeminal syndrome (postganglionic Horner + CN V pain) involves', '["Pancoast tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Orbital apex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lesion in middle cranial fossa (ICA near trigeminal) — Horner + trigeminal symptoms","Brainstem without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cavernous sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesion in middle cranial fossa (ICA near trigeminal) — Horner + trigeminal symptoms', 'Raeder paratrigeminal syndrome is a postganglionic Horner combined with trigeminal (V1) pain, produced by a lesion of the internal carotid artery near the trigeminal ganglion in the middle cranial fossa. The orbital apex, cavernous sinus, and brainstem do not reproduce this pericavernous ICA localization.'),
('1422427c-6deb-598c-a1fb-ef3aa154f09c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cluster headache (trigeminal autonomic cephalgia) involves', '["Only Horner resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Severe periorbital pain (CN V1) with ipsilateral autonomic signs (Horner, lacrimation, rhinorrhea)","Only lacrimation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only rhinorrhea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe periorbital pain (CN V1) with ipsilateral autonomic signs (Horner, lacrimation, rhinorrhea)', 'Cluster headache, a trigeminal autonomic cephalgia, causes severe unilateral periorbital (V1) pain together with ipsilateral autonomic signs such as Horner, lacrimation, and rhinorrhea. It is a pain episode with autonomic accompaniments, not an isolated Horner, pain, lacrimation, or rhinorrhea alone.'),
('e76244db-aa9f-562b-8809-5d16588ea8ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Neurofibromatosis type 2 (NF2) is associated with', '["Bilateral optic gliomas resulting in decreased functional activity","Retinal hamartomas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Café-au-lait spots mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral trigeminal schwannomas without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) — also meningiomas, ependymomas"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) — also meningiomas, ependymomas', 'NF2 is defined by bilateral vestibular schwannomas (acoustic neuromas) and predisposes to meningiomas, ependymomas, and other schwannomas. Bilateral optic gliomas, retinal hamartomas, and cafe-au-lait spots belong to NF1, and bilateral trigeminal schwannomas are not the defining tumor.'),
('c35d2f85-c669-5616-9e81-055360bdd71d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Multiple cranial neuropathies (polyneuritis cranialis) can be due to', '["Only diabetes resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only Lyme accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lyme disease, TB, sarcoidosis, HIV, Guillain-Barre variant, carcinoma/meningitis (leptomeningeal), diabetes","Only sarcoid without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only TB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lyme disease, TB, sarcoidosis, HIV, Guillain-Barre variant, carcinoma/meningitis (leptomeningeal), diabetes', 'Polyneuritis cranialis has many causes, including Lyme disease, tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, HIV, a Guillain-Barre variant, leptomeningeal carcinoma/meningitis, and diabetes. Restricting the cause to any single entity would omit the broad differential required when several cranial nerves fail at once.'),
('886aac4b-f6cd-5d23-8918-cbd241d7fc7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Miller Fisher syndrome (GBS variant) includes', '["Only ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only areflexia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, VI), ataxia, areflexia — anti-GQ1b antibodies","Only pupil changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ophthalmoplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, VI), ataxia, areflexia — anti-GQ1b antibodies', 'Miller Fisher syndrome, a Guillain-Barre variant, classically shows ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, VI) with ataxia and areflexia, and is associated with anti-GQ1b antibodies. The triad must be present together, so ataxia, areflexia, or ophthalmoplegia alone is incomplete.'),
('0e092850-abb5-5cf4-9a75-0b624059a7c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Myasthenia gravis can cause', '["Cortical lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluctuating ptosis, diplopia (CN III, IV, VI), bulbar symptoms, limb weakness — not a CN lesion but NMJ disorder","CN lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluctuating ptosis, diplopia (CN III, IV, VI), bulbar symptoms, limb weakness — not a CN lesion but NMJ disorder', 'Myasthenia gravis is a postsynaptic neuromuscular junction disorder, not a cranial nerve or brainstem lesion; it produces fluctuating ptosis, diplopia, bulbar symptoms, and limb weakness. The fatigable pattern and the absence of a structural lesion distinguish it from cortical, spinal, or brainstem causes.'),
('fe36bd1f-326f-53fe-91a3-04d0482a685f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (MLF lesion) in multiple sclerosis is', '["Unilateral only resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial rectus palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Demyleinating lesion in MLF → INO — often bilateral in MS","in MS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Abducens palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Demyleinating lesion in MLF → INO — often bilateral in MS', 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia results from a demyelinating lesion of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, which in multiple sclerosis is often bilateral; it impairs adduction of the ipsilateral eye with nystagmus of the abducting eye. Unilateral INO, medial rectus palsy, or abducens palsy do not capture the MLF localization.'),
('a7d4c8b9-61a9-5aba-8a43-d7b213a5fe7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Thyroid eye disease (Graves orbitopathy) causes', '["Ptosis only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Extraocular muscle weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Restrictive ophthalmopathy (CN III, IV, VI compressed by enlarged muscles) — esp. inferior rectus → vertical diplopia, exophthalmos","CN palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horner syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Restrictive ophthalmopathy (CN III, IV, VI compressed by enlarged muscles) — esp. inferior rectus → vertical diplopia, exophthalmos', 'Thyroid eye disease causes restrictive ophthalmopathy as enlarged extraocular muscles compress the orbital nerves, classically the inferior rectus, producing vertical diplopia and exophthalmos. It is a restrictive orbital process, not a primary nerve palsy, ptosis, or Horner syndrome.'),
('5f33691f-b94c-5f85-990a-34c3c80628ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Orbital apex syndrome involves cranial nerves', '["CN II, III, IV, V1, VI (at orbital apex) → visual loss, ophthalmoplegia, sensory loss","Only CN V1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN II, III mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CN III, IV, VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN II leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN II, III, IV, V1, VI (at orbital apex) → visual loss, ophthalmoplegia, sensory loss', 'Orbital apex syndrome involves CN II, III, IV, V1, and VI because all these structures pass through the orbital apex; findings include visual loss, ophthalmoplegia, and sensory loss over the forehead. Isolated nerve involvement omits the other nerves sharing this narrow compartment.'),
('327f3257-4f33-52be-8d7a-83cbbe5ef55e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Superior orbital fissure syndrome (without CN II) involves', '["Only CN III resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN VI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, VI) + forehead numbness (V1) + proptosis (venous congestion) — vision preserved","V1 involvement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","+ CN II involvement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, VI) + forehead numbness (V1) + proptosis (venous congestion) — vision preserved', 'Superior orbital fissure syndrome spares the optic nerve and involves CN III, IV, V1, and VI, giving ophthalmoplegia, forehead numbness, and proptosis from venous congestion while vision is preserved. Optic nerve (CN II) involvement would indicate extension to the orbital apex rather than an isolated fissure lesion.'),
('b70f7b81-7296-5feb-8414-4ff0a038d893'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cavernous sinus syndrome involves cranial nerves', '["CN III, IV, V1, V2, VI (all nerves passing through/lateral to cavernous sinus)","Only CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN V1, V2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CN VI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN III, IV, VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, IV, V1, V2, VI (all nerves passing through/lateral to cavernous sinus)', 'All the nerves traversing the cavernous sinus, CN III, IV, V1, V2, and the abducens, may be affected in cavernous sinus syndrome. Limiting the involvement to a single nerve or to the III, IV, VI group alone would omit the ophthalmic and maxillary divisions that travel in the sinus wall.'),
('bf75ac37-729d-5dfc-a375-c0b67a95350c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Jugular foramen syndrome (Vernet syndrome) involves', '["All three resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN X accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN XI mediated by specific receptor activation","CN IX, X, XI palsy (dysphagia, dysphonia, palatal weakness, sternocleidomastoid/trapezius weakness)","Only CN IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI palsy (dysphagia, dysphonia, palatal weakness, sternocleidomastoid/trapezius weakness)', 'Vernet (jugular foramen) syndrome affects CN IX, X, and XI, causing dysphagia, dysphonia, palatal weakness, and sternocleidomastoid/trapezius weakness. Isolating a single nerve fails to reflect that all three exit together through the jugular foramen.'),
('babbca58-c5fe-5976-ab2a-693d4d99474e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Collet-Sicard syndrome (jugular foramen + hypoglossal) involves', '["Only X, XI resulting in decreased functional activity","Only XII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX, X, XI, XII all affected — severe bulbar findings","All + CN VII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only IX, X, XI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI, XII all affected — severe bulbar findings', 'Collet-Sicard syndrome adds the hypoglossal nerve to the jugular foramen nerves, so CN IX, X, XI, and XII are all affected, giving severe bulbar findings. Combining four lower cranial nerves without facial (CN VII) involvement defines this skull base syndrome.'),
('47cb9ff8-383b-59aa-b963-c68f62cbbdef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Villaret syndrome (retroparotid space) involves', '["Only Horner resulting in decreased functional activity","Only XII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX, X, XI, XII + Horner (sympathetic chain)","Horner without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only IX, X, XI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI, XII + Horner (sympathetic chain)', 'Villaret syndrome involves the retroparotid space, where CN IX, X, XI, and XII are joined by the cervical sympathetic chain, so Horner syndrome accompanies the lower cranial nerve palsies. A lesion limited to one nerve or to Horner alone would not match this combined retroparotid localization.'),
('95887be0-8a1f-5c9f-8018-91bf2c824788'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Gradenigo syndrome (petrous apicitis) involves', '["Only CN VI resulting in decreased functional activity","All CN unaffected accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI palsy + ipsilateral facial pain (CN V1) + otitis media — from Dorello canal inflammation (petrous apex)","Only otitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VI palsy + ipsilateral facial pain (CN V1) + otitis media — from Dorello canal inflammation (petrous apex)', 'Gradenigo syndrome (petrous apicitis) arises from inflammation at the petrous apex around Dorello canal, causing abducens (CN VI) palsy with ipsilateral facial pain (CN V1) and otitis media. The CN VI palsy, facial pain, and otitis are all part of the syndrome; no single feature suffices.'),
('9cfda1a5-7630-5336-923f-d4221f9de8c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Dorello canal contains', '["CN VI (abducens) as it enters cavernous sinus — at petrous apex (Gruber''s petroclinoid ligament)","CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V1 mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VI (abducens) as it enters cavernous sinus — at petrous apex (Gruber''s petroclinoid ligament)', 'Dorello canal carries the abducens nerve (CN VI) below the petroclinoid (Gruber) ligament as it courses from the posterior fossa toward the cavernous sinus at the petrous apex. CN III, IV, V1, and V2 follow different routes to the orbit and face.'),
('a718f5e2-6fb2-5eba-be9e-87faa60ab5be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The facial nerve within the temporal bone is vulnerable to', '["Only ICA aneurysm resulting in decreased functional activity","vulnerability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only brainstem lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Only external compression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Otitis media (mastoiditis), Herpes zoster (Ramsay Hunt), temporal bone fracture, iatrogenic (ear surgery)"]'::jsonb, 'Otitis media (mastoiditis), Herpes zoster (Ramsay Hunt), temporal bone fracture, iatrogenic (ear surgery)', 'Within the temporal bone the facial nerve is at risk from otitis media and mastoiditis, herpes zoster (Ramsay Hunt), temporal bone fractures, and iatrogenic injury during ear surgery. These local causes, not aneurysm, brainstem, or external compression, characteristically damage the intratemporal segment.'),
('70ff76a6-5576-5469-968c-45e9e5ca5af6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Crocodile tears syndrome (gustatory-lacrimal reflex) is a complication of', '["Myasthenia gravis resulting in decreased functional activity","Bell palsy recovery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trigeminal neuralgia mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial nerve injury with aberrant regeneration — salivatory fibers misdirect to lacrimal gland → tearing while eating","Guillain-Barre leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve injury with aberrant regeneration — salivatory fibers misdirect to lacrimal gland → tearing while eating', 'Crocodile tears syndrome follows facial nerve injury when salivatory fibers aberrantly regenerate into the greater petrosal nerve, so the lacrimal gland is stimulated and the patient tears while eating. Bell palsy recovery, trigeminal neuralgia, myasthenia, and Guillain-Barre do not produce this synkinesis.'),
('8f701516-a816-59b3-8ae5-7063cdb10377'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The trigeminal (Gasserian) ganglion is located in', '["Pterygopalatine fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramen ovale accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal acoustic meatus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cavernous sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meckel cave (trigeminal cave) near petrous apex (impression of temporal bone)"]'::jsonb, 'Meckel cave (trigeminal cave) near petrous apex (impression of temporal bone)', 'The trigeminal (Gasserian) ganglion lies in Meckel cave on the petrous apex. The pterygopalatine fossa, foramen ovale, internal acoustic meatus, and cavernous sinus house other ganglionic or nerve structures.'),
('e2c0dde7-98f5-523c-b109-9d078deafb43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal schwannomas (rare) cause', '["Only visual loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Only facial weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hearing loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial numbness/pain (CN V), mass effect on cavernous sinus; spread along Meckel cave"]'::jsonb, 'Facial numbness/pain (CN V), mass effect on cavernous sinus; spread along Meckel cave', 'Trigeminal schwannomas are rare tumors that present with facial numbness or pain (CN V) and may compress the cavernous sinus, extending along Meckel cave. Their growth along the trigeminal distribution, rather than visual, facial motor, hearing, or isolated pain loss, defines the presentation.'),
('cbd19b8b-910d-5923-a8d4-e466fc8b3288'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The nervus intermedius (Wrisberg) can be affected by', '["Herpes zoster (Ramsay Hunt) causing pain, vesicles in ear, taste loss, and hyperacusis","Only vertigo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only tinnitus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hearing loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only facial palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herpes zoster (Ramsay Hunt) causing pain, vesicles in ear, taste loss, and hyperacusis', 'The nervus intermedius (Wrisberg) carries taste and autonomic fibers and is vulnerable to herpes zoster; Ramsay Hunt syndrome produces ear pain, vesicles, taste loss, and hyperacusis. Vertigo, tinnitus, and hearing loss reflect CN VIII, and facial palsy alone is too narrow.'),
('9a841988-e4a7-5bf2-8e2a-4cd7f721b303'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The geniculate ganglion (CN VII) can be affected by', '["Swimmer ear resulting in decreased functional activity","Geniculate herpes (Ramsay Hunt) — vesicles around ear/ear canal, external ear pain, CN VII/VIII symptoms","Only pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Bell palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only facial palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Geniculate herpes (Ramsay Hunt) — vesicles around ear/ear canal, external ear pain, CN VII/VIII symptoms', 'Herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion (Ramsay Hunt syndrome) causes vesicles in the ear canal and external ear, severe otalgia, and CN VII/VIII symptoms including facial palsy and hearing disturbance. Bell palsy, swimmer ear, and isolated pain or facial palsy do not describe geniculate zoster.'),
('bf006a7e-0479-5b3f-8f65-cca65f17cdd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vagus nerve damage causing aspiration involves', '["Only hoarseness resulting in decreased functional activity","Only dysphagia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cough mediated by specific receptor activation","Unilateral — severe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilateral vagal palsy → severe aspiration (pharyngeal/laryngeal paralysis) — need tracheostomy, PEG"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral vagal palsy → severe aspiration (pharyngeal/laryngeal paralysis) — need tracheostomy, PEG', 'Bilateral vagal palsy paralyzes the pharynx and larynx, causing severe aspiration with pooling of secretions and requiring tracheostomy and feeding (PEG) support. Unilateral vagal loss or isolated hoarseness, dysphagia, or cough does not produce this life-threatening bilateral picture.'),
('f3551f2b-b8d3-5886-9d4b-6d0e5de92800'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Unilateral vocal cord paralysis (CN X/recurrent laryngeal) presents with', '["Whispering resulting in decreased functional activity","Inspiratory stridor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete aphonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hoarseness, breathy voice, weak cough; paramedian/cadaveric vocal cord position (intermediate)","Normal voice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hoarseness, breathy voice, weak cough; paramedian/cadaveric vocal cord position (intermediate)', 'Unilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis leaves the vocal cord in an intermediate (paramedian/cadaveric) position, causing hoarseness, a breathy voice, and a weak cough. Inspiratory stridor and complete aphonia reflect bilateral or glottic airway compromise, and a normal voice would not indicate paralysis.'),
('d082f267-3f61-55c0-aea5-5b21f8bdc0ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Bilateral vocal cord paralysis (recurrent laryngeal) presents with', '["Oral secretions resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inspiratory stridor (high-pitched) and respiratory distress; vocal cords in paramedian position (close together)","Hoarseness without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cough leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiratory stridor (high-pitched) and respiratory distress; vocal cords in paramedian position (close together)', 'Bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve paralysis draws both vocal cords to the paramedian position, narrowing the glottis and producing inspiratory stridor with respiratory distress. Hoarseness and a weak cough characterize unilateral palsy, so they do not represent the bilateral obstructive picture.'),
('be81ab6c-fafd-5bf7-baa8-948fbff79282'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Superior laryngeal nerve damage (external branch) causes', '["Difficulty with high-pitched voice (cricothyroid paralysis — cannot tense/lengthen cords); voice fatigues easily","Breathy voice accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inspiratory stridor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Aphonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hoarseness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Difficulty with high-pitched voice (cricothyroid paralysis — cannot tense/lengthen cords); voice fatigues easily', 'Damage to the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve paralyzes the cricothyroid muscle, which can no longer tense or lengthen the cords, so the patient cannot produce high-pitched voice and the voice fatigues easily. Breathy voice, stridor, aphonia, and hoarseness point to recurrent laryngeal pathology instead.'),
('79e39d6b-a7e8-59b0-acb4-1b4782fac79f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Loss of CN IX sensation in the posterior pharynx (afferent limb of the gag reflex) results in', '["Both resulting in decreased functional activity","change in gag accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Efferent loss (X) mediated by specific receptor activation","significance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","If CN IX is lost → absent gag on that side (afferent loss)"]'::jsonb, 'If CN IX is lost → absent gag on that side (afferent loss)', 'CN IX provides the afferent limb of the gag reflex from the posterior pharynx, so its loss abolishes the gag on that side even though the efferent CN X pathway may be intact. Attributing the loss to efferent innervation or to both nerves misidentifies the afferent pathway.'),
('136235ce-d310-565a-929c-f76a7354cba8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Accessory nerve spinal roots exit from', '["C1-C5 lateral funiculus (anterior horn) — run upward through foramen magnum to join CN X","Ventral roots C1-C5 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervical rami mediated by specific receptor activation","Cranio-cervical junction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal roots C1-C5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C1-C5 lateral funiculus (anterior horn) — run upward through foramen magnum to join CN X', 'The spinal accessory (CN XI) roots arise from the lateral part of the anterior horn of C1-C5, emerge along the lateral funiculus, and ascend through the foramen magnum to join the cranial root. Ventral and dorsal roots, cervical rami, and the craniocervical junction do not describe its origin or course.'),
('f2f4d7ba-d005-5e07-a8f4-46e748a1a4b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) is actually a', '["Sympathetic nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Only a cranial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal nerve that secondarily associates with the cranial accessory (ambiguus) — questionable as true CN","Only a spinal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","True cranial nerve (all from brainstem) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal nerve that secondarily associates with the cranial accessory (ambiguus) — questionable as true CN', 'CN XI is functionally a spinal nerve whose fibers arise in the upper cervical cord and only secondarily associate with the cranial accessory (ambiguus) root, which makes its status as a true cranial nerve questionable. It is not purely cranial, purely spinal, or derived from the brainstem alone.'),
('dfe81e10-5892-51cc-993b-08a20494c6e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) and ansa cervicalis (C1 fibers) supply', '["connections resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoglossal to strap muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C1 to tongue mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoglossal to tongue only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoglossal loops C1 fibers = ansa cervicalis (innervates infrahyoid strap muscles — sternothyroid, sternohyoid, omohyoid, thyrohyoid)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglossal loops C1 fibers = ansa cervicalis (innervates infrahyoid strap muscles — sternothyroid, sternohyoid, omohyoid, thyrohyoid)', 'C1 fibers that travel with the hypoglossal nerve separate from it as the ansa cervicalis, innervating the infrahyoid strap muscles (sternothyroid, sternohyoid, omohyoid, thyrohyoid). The hypoglossal proper fibers supply the tongue, so describing tongue or strap supply alone is incomplete.'),
('7f88cb5d-b914-5f3c-94cb-290ca000a7ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Geniohyoid muscle (elevates hyoid) is innervated by', '["CN V resulting in decreased functional activity","C1 fibers (via CN XII) — not hypoglossal proper","CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","C3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN XII proper leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'C1 fibers (via CN XII) — not hypoglossal proper', 'Geniohyoid is innervated by C1 fibers that descend with the hypoglossal nerve, so it is not a true hypoglossal (CN XII) target muscle. CN V and CN VII supply other muscles of the floor of the mouth and neck.'),
('6ddd8b82-cf7c-58b8-9afb-16982ceda0af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The lingual nerve (V3) carries from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue', '["Only motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste from anterior 2/3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both touch and taste mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V3 (not taste — taste is CN VII via chorda tympani)","Taste from posterior 1/3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V3 (not taste — taste is CN VII via chorda tympani)', 'The lingual nerve (CN V3) carries general sensation from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, while taste from that region travels with the chorda tympani (CN VII). Taste from the posterior third is carried by CN IX, so V3 with taste excluded is correct.'),
('be631a1d-c6ef-5151-bc10-6b8aeb778c6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve testing in the clinical exam requires testing of', '["Only sensory testing resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor testing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only visual clues mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN I: smell; II: vision; III, IV, VI: eye movements/pupils; V: face sensation/jaw; VII: face muscles; VIII: hearing/balance; IX, X: gag/palate; XI: SCM/trap; XII: tongue","Only reflex testing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN I: smell; II: vision; III, IV, VI: eye movements/pupils; V: face sensation/jaw; VII: face muscles; VIII: hearing/balance; IX, X: gag/palate; XI: SCM/trap; XII: tongue', 'Complete bedside cranial nerve examination tests CN I (smell), II (vision), III/IV/VI (eye movements and pupils), V (face sensation and jaw), VII (facial muscles), VIII (hearing and balance), IX/X (gag and palate), XI (sternocleidomastoid/trapezius), and XII (tongue). Sensory, motor, visual, or reflex testing alone omits the other modalities.'),
('d0c45586-8cd7-59c8-b349-c699811a744f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Jugular foramen pseudomass (normal variant) = high-riding jugular bulb', '["Can cause conductive hearing loss (bulb erodes into middle ear) — mimics glomus tumor; avoid biopsy (blue dome)","Vascular malformation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","significance mediated by specific receptor activation","Mass tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","pathologic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Can cause conductive hearing loss (bulb erodes into middle ear) — mimics glomus tumor; avoid biopsy (blue dome)', 'A high-riding jugular bulb is a normal variant that may erode into the middle ear, causing conductive hearing loss and mimicking a glomus tumor, so it must be recognized to avoid injudicious biopsy. It is not a true vascular malformation or a neoplastic mass.'),
('d1dd0e4c-a5f4-55bd-936d-3b224e47db81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Glomus jugulare (paraganglioma) of the jugular foramen causes', '["Only tinnitus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX, X, XI palsy + pulsatile tinnitus + blue-red tympanic membrane (behind TM) — ''rising sun'' sign","Only headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI palsy + pulsatile tinnitus + blue-red tympanic membrane (behind TM) — ''rising sun'' sign', 'Glomus jugulare, a paraganglioma of the jugular foramen, compresses CN IX, X, and XI and presents with pulsatile tinnitus and a blue-red tympanic membrane (rising sun sign). Pulsatile tinnitus, lower cranial nerve palsy, and the characteristic membrane appearance occur together rather than in isolation.'),
('1e410701-38f5-5684-8c9a-1ed4504e1b89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The nasal cycle (alternating congestion) is controlled by', '["Only sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neither mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Autonomic nerves: sympathetic (constriction → decongestion) and parasympathetic (secretory) from pterygopalatine ganglion (CN VII)","Both equally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic nerves: sympathetic (constriction → decongestion) and parasympathetic (secretory) from pterygopalatine ganglion (CN VII)', 'The nasal cycle reflects autonomic balance: sympathetic fibers constrict mucosal vessels to decongest one side, while parasympathetic fibers from the pterygopalatine ganglion (CN VII) promote secretion on the other. Neither system acting alone, nor their absence, controls the alternating pattern.'),
('abe9ad33-bcb7-5afa-bb49-fda0be93fccf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Taste buds distribution: CN VII (anterior 2/3), CN IX (posterior 1/3), CN X (epiglottis, pharynx) represents', '["X = entire tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","IX = entire tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","specific distribution mediated by specific receptor activation","Correct distribution of taste function between CN VII, IX, X","VII = entire tongue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Correct distribution of taste function between CN VII, IX, X', 'Taste is distributed so that CN VII via the chorda tympani serves the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, CN IX the posterior third, and CN X the epiglottis and pharynx. No single nerve supplies the entire tongue, so a distribution across the three nerves is correct.'),
('593cff66-fea9-5f8b-839d-966d91b36bb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Multiple cranial nerve palsies on one side usually indicates lower brainstem or skull base lesions; a unilateral pattern', '["systemic resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Unilateral CN involvement suggests structural lesion (tumor, inflammation) at skull base, cavernous sinus, or brainstem","bilateral mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","stroke without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unilateral CN involvement suggests structural lesion (tumor, inflammation) at skull base, cavernous sinus, or brainstem', 'A unilateral collection of cranial nerve palsies points to a structural cause such as a tumor, inflammation, or other lesion at the skull base, cavernous sinus, or brainstem. Systemic, bilateral, and demyelinating pictures would not typically present as unilateral lower-nerve involvement alone.'),
('47c5b261-1e89-5ee6-81cc-c37a745be661'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Bilateral multiple cranial nerve palsies are more likely due to', '["Brainstem tumor resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Systemic: Guillain-Barre variant, MS, sarcoidosis, TB meningitis, lymphoma, carcinomatous meningitis, diabetes","Stroke without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skull base tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic: Guillain-Barre variant, MS, sarcoidosis, TB meningitis, lymphoma, carcinomatous meningitis, diabetes', 'Bilateral multiple cranial nerve palsies are more likely from a systemic cause such as Guillain-Barre variants, MS, sarcoidosis, TB meningitis, lymphoma, carcinomatous meningitis, or diabetes. A single focal lesion like a brainstem tumor, stroke, trauma, or skull base tumor rarely causes symmetric bilateral polyneuropathy.'),
('9f62fefa-6195-5b53-8e91-2ab96375d8cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Marcus Gunn jaw-winking syndrome (trigemino-oculomotor synkinesis) is', '["CN XII to CN X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VII to CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX to CN X mediated by specific receptor activation","Congenital: when jaw moves (CN V), eyelid retracts (CN III) — aberrant trigeminal to levator fibers","CN V to CN VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital: when jaw moves (CN V), eyelid retracts (CN III) — aberrant trigeminal to levator fibers', 'Marcus Gunn jaw-winking is a congenital synkinesis in which aberrant trigeminal (CN V) fibers innervate the levator, so the eyelid retracts when the jaw moves. It represents CN V to CN III wiring, not the other nerve-to-nerve aberrancies.'),
('af621811-5c70-588e-ad94-fe66d1ee3b5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Duction deficits: loss of abduction = CN VI; loss of adduction = CN III; loss of depression in adduction = CN IV represent', '["All by CN VI resulting in decreased functional activity","All by CN II accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Patterns of extraocular nerve impairment for diplopia analysis","All by CN III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All by CN IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patterns of extraocular nerve impairment for diplopia analysis', 'Diplopia analysis uses duction patterns: loss of abduction indicates CN VI, loss of adduction indicates CN III, and loss of depression in adduction indicates CN IV. These localizing patterns, rather than any single nerve explaining all deficits, guide diagnosis.'),
('adb63de6-fc8a-5c0e-9fd1-64615b16a819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The near triad (accommodation, convergence, miosis) involves', '["CN V resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN III (medial rectus, ciliary muscle, sphincter pupillae)","CN IV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III (medial rectus, ciliary muscle, sphincter pupillae)', 'The near triad (accommodation, convergence, and miosis) is mediated by CN III and its parasympathetic fibers to the medial rectus, ciliary muscle, and sphincter pupillae. CN V, VI, IV, and VII do not mediate these near responses.'),
('b8757749-7d66-5e5f-a1f8-63c7e9b4a6c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Light-near dissociation (pupils react to accommodation but not light) in Argyll Robertson pupil (neurosyphilis) results from', '["Pretectal lesion (light reflex pathway) near Edinger-Westphal nucleus spared (accommodation)","Edinger-Westphal destroyed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ciliary ganglion destroyed mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III nucleus destroyed without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic nerve destroyed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pretectal lesion (light reflex pathway) near Edinger-Westphal nucleus spared (accommodation)', 'Argyll Robertson pupils occur in neurosyphilis from a pretectal lesion that interrupts the light reflex while the nearby Edinger-Westphal nucleus, which drives accommodation, is spared. Destruction of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, ciliary ganglion, CN III nucleus, or optic nerve would alter other pupillary responses.'),
('4b15dee0-fce8-5d7d-98f1-fc4c0853584f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Adie (tonic) pupil involves', '["Brainstem lesion resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Postganglionic parasympathetic denervation (ciliary ganglion; localized, benign) → enlarged pupil with slow/near response to light/accommodation","Optic nerve lesion mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Preganglionic lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Postganglionic parasympathetic denervation (ciliary ganglion; localized, benign) → enlarged pupil with slow/near response to light/accommodation', 'Adie (tonic) pupil results from postganglionic parasympathetic denervation of the ciliary ganglion, a localized and benign process causing an enlarged pupil with a slow response to light and a tonic near response. Sympathetic, preganglionic, brainstem, and optic nerve lesions produce different pupillary findings.'),
('f6812601-eb3a-595c-b55c-5ab736d40ea0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Efferent pupillary defect (fixed dilated pupil with no direct or consensual light response) results from', '["Optic nerve lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III lesion (parasympathetic) or ciliary ganglion/ short ciliary nerves","Pretectal lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Chiasm lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III lesion (parasympathetic) or ciliary ganglion/ short ciliary nerves', 'An efferent pupillary defect with a fixed, dilated pupil stems from a lesion of CN III parasympathetic fibers or of the ciliary ganglion and short ciliary nerves, because the light is received but cannot reach the sphincter. Optic nerve, chiasmal, pretectal, and sympathetic lesions do not interrupt this efferent parasympathetic pathway.'),
('14495a1e-40d0-5b89-81be-570d753b1287'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Crocodile tears (gustatory lacrimation) follow aberrant regeneration of CN VII, meaning', '["CN IX to CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN V to CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN X to CN V mediated by specific receptor activation","Yes: salivatory fibers → greater petrosal (lacrimal) leads to tearing while eating","CN VII to CN V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yes: salivatory fibers → greater petrosal (lacrimal) leads to tearing while eating', 'Crocodile tears (gustatory lacrimation) follow aberrant regeneration after facial nerve injury, in which salivatory fibers grow into the greater petrosal nerve, so the lacrimal gland is activated and tearing occurs while eating. The misrouting is within CN VII, not a cross-connection between other cranial nerves.'),
('6eb1c07e-fc96-5213-9368-477522c435a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Frey syndrome (gustatory sweating) after parotidectomy', '["CN VII misdirection resulting in decreased functional activity","significance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aberrant parasympathetic regeneration of CN IX (parotid fibers misdirected to sweat glands) → sweating on eating","CN V misdirection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN X misdirection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aberrant parasympathetic regeneration of CN IX (parotid fibers misdirected to sweat glands) → sweating on eating', 'After parotidectomy, severed secretomotor fibers of the auriculotemporal nerve (CN IX) regenerate into sweat glands, so chewing triggers sweating and flushing instead of salivation. CN VII supplies the submandibular and sublingual glands rather than the parotid, and CN V and CN X play no role in this mechanism.'),
('406aef90-f71f-53c5-8538-d4826aa4e740'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigemino-cardiac reflex (during trigeminal ganglion manipulation)', '["Bradycardia, hypotension, asystole (trigeminal → vagal activation) — seen in Fothergill surgery, skull base procedures","Hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperventilation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Tachycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia, hypotension, asystole (trigeminal → vagal activation) — seen in Fothergill surgery, skull base procedures', 'Stimulation of the trigeminal nerve activates a reflex arc through the vagus that produces bradycardia, hypotension, and even asystole. The other options are opposite autonomic effects and are not features of this reflex.'),
('f2f2fe3c-ef2c-5d2b-98e2-da360a429552'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Tensor veli palatini (CN V3) and levator veli palatini (CN X) are innervated by', '["CN IX resulting in decreased functional activity","Tensor = CN V3 (mandibular); Levator = CN X (pharyngeal plexus)","CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","Both CN X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both CN V3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tensor = CN V3 (mandibular); Levator = CN X (pharyngeal plexus)', 'The tensor veli palatini is supplied by the mandibular division of CN V via the nerve to medial pterygoid, while the levator veli palatini is supplied by CN X through the pharyngeal plexus. Assigning both muscles to a single nerve or to CN IX or CN VII is incorrect.'),
('de2062dc-5ada-5689-b510-bf379ce66bb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Stylopharyngeus muscle is innervated by', '["CN X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX (glossopharyngeal) — only muscle supplied by CN IX","CN V3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN XI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal) — only muscle supplied by CN IX', 'Stylopharyngeus is the only muscle supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve, which runs between this muscle and the superior constrictor to elevate the pharynx. CN X, VII, V3, and XI innervate other muscles.'),
('4e8da3c9-83d2-5b48-bc3f-66a0bd5252c2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The muscles of the larynx are all supplied by', '["CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity","Recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X) except cricothyroid (external SLN, CN X)","CN V3 mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X) except cricothyroid (external SLN, CN X)', 'All intrinsic laryngeal muscles are supplied by the recurrent laryngeal nerve except cricothyroid, which receives the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve; both derive from CN X. CN VII, V3, XI, and IX do not reach the larynx.'),
('b22b22a3-7d81-5aba-a5a7-15cf23894a43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Posterior cricoarytenoid (the only abductor of vocal cords) is innervated by', '["Recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X) — paralysis leads to vocal cord adduction (breathing compromised if bilateral)","Pharyngeal plexus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior laryngeal nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X) — paralysis leads to vocal cord adduction (breathing compromised if bilateral)', 'The posterior cricoarytenoid is the only vocal cord abductor and receives the recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X). Its paralysis adducts the cord, and bilateral damage narrows the airway and can cause stridor; the pharyngeal plexus and superior laryngeal nerve do not supply it.'),
('dbec6163-a6dc-5d98-bf9e-274c44227442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The tensor tympani muscle (middle ear) — innervated by', '["CN V3 (mandibular division) — dampens loud sounds (hyperacusis same as stapedius)","CN X accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII (stapedius) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VIII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN V3 (mandibular division) — dampens loud sounds (hyperacusis same as stapedius)', 'Tensor tympani is supplied by CN V3 through the nerve to medial pterygoid and contracts to dampen loud sounds, so its paralysis produces the same hyperacusis as stapedius paralysis. Stapedius itself is CN VII, and CN VIII, IX, and X are not involved.'),
('fa589083-d77b-569e-8a81-a942121ff88c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Salivation: submandibular/sublingual from CN VII (chorda tympani); Parotid from CN IX (tympanic/lesser petrosal)', '["Both from CN X resulting in decreased functional activity","Both from CN VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII and CN IX secretomotor, respectively","Both from CN III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both from CN IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII and CN IX secretomotor, respectively', 'Secretomotor supply comes from CN VII via the chorda tympani and submandibular ganglion to the submandibular and sublingual glands, and from CN IX via the lesser petrosal nerve to the parotid. The other choices assign both glands to a single nerve or to CN III.'),
('4afa0761-ee0a-59d4-abff-f367a121402e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Gustatory (taste) innervation:', '["VII = entire tongue resulting in decreased functional activity","X = entire tongue accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VII = anterior 2/3; IX = posterior 1/3; X = epiglottis","IX = entire tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All from solitary nucleus directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VII = anterior 2/3; IX = posterior 1/3; X = epiglottis', 'Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travels with the chorda tympani of CN VII, from the posterior third with CN IX, and from the epiglottis with CN X. No single nerve carries all tongue taste, and fibers always reach the solitary nucleus via a cranial nerve first.'),
('2ef336f0-3d26-5f28-bdde-1040c9a39a95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Taste V cranial nerve (trigeminal)? Important: taste is NOT carried by trigeminal — trigeminal is general sensation only. Taste = VII, IX, X', '["CN V carries taste from posterior 1/3","CN V carries taste from anterior 2/3","Taste is never carried by CN V (trigeminal)","CN V = general + taste","CN V = taste from epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 'Taste is never carried by CN V (trigeminal)', 'The trigeminal nerve mediates only general somatic sensation and never taste. Taste is carried by CN VII (anterior two-thirds), CN IX (posterior third), and CN X (epiglottis), so any option claiming CN V transmits taste is wrong.'),
('5ca987c3-e021-5f99-8ad9-dcabdb58bc96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve (CN VII) lesions can be differentiated by testing', '["Only motor function resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste (anterior 2/3), stapedius reflex (hyperacusis), lacrimation (Schirmer test) — localize lesion site","Only taste mediated by specific receptor activation","Only stapedius without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only lacrimation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taste (anterior 2/3), stapedius reflex (hyperacusis), lacrimation (Schirmer test) — localize lesion site', 'Testing taste on the anterior two-thirds, the stapedius reflex (for hyperacusis), and lacrimation with a Schirmer test locates the lesion along the nerve''s course because these fibers branch off at different points. Testing only motor function or any single modality cannot localize the lesion.'),
('b35a5f26-4d38-5f42-8ed2-0a5c1e115c7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Schirmer test (eye wetting) measures', '["CN IX parasympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Lacrimation (CN VII parasympathetic via greater petrosal/pterygopalatine ganglion) — to localize CN VII lesion","Corneal sensation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lacrimation (CN VII parasympathetic via greater petrosal/pterygopalatine ganglion) — to localize CN VII lesion', 'The Schirmer test measures tearing, which depends on the parasympathetic path of CN VII through the greater petrosal nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion; reduced wetting localizes the CN VII lesion proximally. Corneal sensation (CN V), CN III, and CN IX parasympathetic pathways are not being measured.'),
('9bb4e6e4-a882-5197-89d1-5b2976de3e12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Stapedius reflex (loud sound tolerance) — CN VII lesion proximal to nerve to stapedius = hyperacusis', '["Distal lesion → hyperacusis resulting in decreased functional activity","Lesion proximal to stapedius branch (within temporal bone) → hyperacusis","Taste loss alone = hyperacusis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperacusis in all CN VII lesions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","hyperacusis ever leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lesion proximal to stapedius branch (within temporal bone) → hyperacusis', 'If the CN VII lesion lies proximal to where the nerve to stapedius branches within the temporal bone, stapedius is paralyzed and sounds seem abnormally loud (hyperacusis). A distal lesion spares stapedius, and isolated taste loss does not produce hyperacusis.'),
('aec46f01-cf96-5199-a797-a8168c46dbb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Localization of CN VII palsy:', '["Nucleus = lower face only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Same for all accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex = ipsilateral mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex (UMN) = lower contra face; Nucleus = ipsilateral face + CN VI; Nerve proximal = all functions; Stylomastoid = only motor","Peripheral = only lower face leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex (UMN) = lower contra face; Nucleus = ipsilateral face + CN VI; Nerve proximal = all functions; Stylomastoid = only motor', 'A cortical (UMN) lesion spares the upper face because frontalis receives bilateral input, so only the contralateral lower face is affected; a nuclear lesion affects the ipsilateral face and often CN VI; a proximal nerve lesion affects all functions, while a stylomastoid lesion affects only the facial muscles.'),
('6c4d08be-34c7-5cbc-9965-2b94fc00c086'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The nerve of pterygoid canal (Vidian nerve) is formed by', '["Only sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Greater + lesser petrosal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Greater petrosal (CN VII, parasympathetic) + deep petrosal (sympathetic) → together to pterygopalatine ganglion","Deep and lesser petrosal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater petrosal (CN VII, parasympathetic) + deep petrosal (sympathetic) → together to pterygopalatine ganglion', 'The nerve of the pterygoid canal is formed by union of the greater petrosal nerve (parasympathetic, from CN VII) with the deep petrosal nerve (sympathetic) as they pass to the pterygopalatine ganglion. The other options omit one of these roots or incorrectly use the lesser petrosal nerve.'),
('104b22fe-b324-56a3-a21d-32bb4fea52d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Eagle syndrome (stylohyoid ligament calcification) irritates', '["CN IX (glossopharyngeal) → pharyngeal pain, dysphagia, pain on head turning","CN X accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN XII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN V3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal) → pharyngeal pain, dysphagia, pain on head turning', 'An elongated or calcified styloid process compresses the glossopharyngeal nerve in the parapharyngeal space, causing pharyngeal pain, dysphagia, and pain on turning the head. CN X, XII, VII, and V3 are not the nerves irritated in Eagle syndrome.'),
('6d1c7fb0-c535-57c1-9b4e-e4a8854b045a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Glomus tympanicum (paraganglioma, middle ear) presents with', '["Only tinnitus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertigo accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hearing loss mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulsatile tinnitus + hearing loss (conductive) — visible as red mass behind TM (inferior quadrant) — ''rising sun'' sign","Bulbar palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulsatile tinnitus + hearing loss (conductive) — visible as red mass behind TM (inferior quadrant) — ''rising sun'' sign', 'Glomus tympanicum is a middle-ear paraganglioma presenting with pulsatile tinnitus and conductive hearing loss and appears as a red mass behind the tympanic membrane, classically the ''rising sun'' sign. Vertigo and bulbar palsy are not characteristic of this tumor.'),
('ae9cddbd-86f0-5d6c-85ae-80b1983b36ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Ramsay Hunt syndrome: geniculate VZV → facial palsy, ear pain, vesicles in external ear, CN VIII symptoms (hearing loss, vertigo)', '["ear pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Yes: CN VII + VIII involvement from herpes zoster","CN V only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hearing loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","vesicles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yes: CN VII + VIII involvement from herpes zoster', 'Reactivation of varicella-zoster virus in the geniculate ganglion causes facial palsy with ear pain, external-ear vesicles, and CN VIII symptoms such as hearing loss and vertigo, reflecting combined involvement of CN VII and VIII. CN V involvement alone or isolated hearing loss does not capture this picture.'),
('4b8f904c-9ce5-5196-8e3b-858cba49fd55'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CN VIII injury in acoustic neuroma — hearing loss is the most common symptom (95%), followed by tinnitus and vertigo', '["Tinnitus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertigo only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facial palsy first mediated by specific receptor activation","Hearing loss = most common (sensorineural, high frequency first)","hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing loss = most common (sensorineural, high frequency first)', 'In acoustic (vestibular) schwannoma, sensorineural hearing loss is the earliest and most common symptom, classically first affecting high frequencies, followed by tinnitus and disequilibrium. Facial palsy is a late feature and not the presenting symptom.'),
('186396d7-66a7-5be2-aa4f-171d1dcf6510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'CPA tumor differential: Acoustic neuroma (95%) > meningioma > epidermoid > arachnoid cyst > AICA loop', '["Lipoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Epidermoid = 95% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acoustic neuroma = 95% of CPA masses (Schwann cell of vestibular nerve)","Cholesteatoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningioma = 95% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acoustic neuroma = 95% of CPA masses (Schwann cell of vestibular nerve)', 'About 95% of masses in the cerebellopontine angle are acoustic neuromas arising from Schwann cells of the vestibular nerve. Meningioma and epidermoid are far less common, so those percentages are incorrect.'),
('508864bd-6a33-5b61-9bdc-9d7cb3a2765f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Neuro-otology: Dix-Hallpike test (positional vertigo) — diagnose BPPV (posterior canal) by nystagmus', '["Vestibular neuritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Meniere disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) — posterior canaliths → nystagmus on Dix-Hallpike","Labyrinthitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central vertigo leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) — posterior canaliths → nystagmus on Dix-Hallpike', 'The Dix-Hallpike maneuver reproduces vertigo and characteristic nystagmus by dislodging canaliths within the posterior semicircular canal, a finding specific for BPPV. Vestibular neuritis, labyrinthitis, and Meniere disease have different provocative maneuvers and examination findings.'),
('d9952f52-e779-5f45-a91a-40b4d18cfc19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'HINTS exam (Head Impulse, Nystagmus, Test of Skew) differentiates central from peripheral vertigo', '["Central tends to be less severe resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","HINTS is not validated accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral tends to be more mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","HINTS: head impulse normal (central), nystagmus direction-changing/vertical (central), skew deviation (central) = posterior circulation stroke","All vertigo is central leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HINTS: head impulse normal (central), nystagmus direction-changing/vertical (central), skew deviation (central) = posterior circulation stroke', 'The HINTS exam distinguishes posterior circulation stroke from a peripheral cause of acute vertigo: a normal head impulse, direction-changing or purely vertical nystagmus, and a skew deviation all point to central disease. Peripheral injury gives an abnormal head impulse with unidirectional horizontal nystagmus.'),
('ba1ecb5e-ae82-524a-8abe-bba93ffd634d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Peripheral vertigo (vestibular neuritis, BPPV, Meniere) = nystagmus is horizontal/torsional, unidirectional, suppressed by visual fixation', '["fixation suppression resulting in decreased functional activity","nystagmus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fast phase toward healthy ear; suppressed by visual fixation (peripheral)","Peripheral = vertical nystagmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central = same leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fast phase toward healthy ear; suppressed by visual fixation (peripheral)', 'In peripheral lesions the fast phase of nystagmus beats toward the healthy ear and the nystagmus is suppressed by visual fixation. Vertical or direction-changing nystagmus unaffected by fixation suggests a central cause, so the other options are incorrect.'),
('3a438ab2-2000-5076-9c6f-51e9ca3af882'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Meniere disease: recurrent vertigo episodes + fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss + tinnitus + aural fullness (endolymphatic hydrops)', '["Only fullness resulting in decreased functional activity","Only tinnitus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Classic triad: episodic vertigo + fluctuating SNHL + tinnitus (plus fullness)","Only vertigo without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Classic triad: episodic vertigo + fluctuating SNHL + tinnitus (plus fullness)', 'Meniere disease is defined by the triad of episodic vertigo, fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, and tinnitus, usually accompanied by aural fullness due to endolymphatic hydrops. Isolated fullness, tinnitus, vertigo, or hearing loss alone is not the diagnostic picture.'),
('c1a17ddd-a8f1-53a5-8f80-589e3b583b8d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vestibular neuritis (acute unilateral vestibular failure) — severe vertigo, nausea, nystagmus, no hearing loss (spares cochlea)', '["Only tinnitus resulting in decreased functional activity","With hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inflammation of vestibular nerve (superior vestibular) → no hearing loss because cochlear nerve spared","nystagmus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only vertigo leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of vestibular nerve (superior vestibular) → no hearing loss because cochlear nerve spared', 'Vestibular neuritis inflames the superior vestibular nerve, producing severe vertigo, nausea, and nystagmus while sparing the cochlea, so hearing loss is absent. When hearing loss accompanies the vertigo, labyrinthitis (not vestibular neuritis) is the usual cause.'),
('dd5799d2-6ebc-5911-9a6f-e3a51085e12a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Labyrinthitis (bacterial): vertigo + hearing loss (both cochlear and vestibular) — severe, often from meningitis', '["Full recovery common resulting in decreased functional activity","Only hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vertigo mediated by specific receptor activation","vertigo without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection spreads from meninges/middle ear → often results in permanent hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 'Infection spreads from meninges/middle ear → often results in permanent hearing loss', 'Bacterial labyrinthitis usually spreads from the meninges or middle ear and damages both cochlear and vestibular end organs, commonly leaving permanent hearing loss rather than full recovery. The other options misstate the outcome or isolate a single symptom.'),
('e8863671-bb3f-57fb-bd78-d47a29b59258'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cochlear implant is indicated for', '["Bilateral severe-profound sensorineural hearing loss (deafness) not helped by hearing aids","All hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conductive hearing loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal hearing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unilateral deafness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral severe-profound sensorineural hearing loss (deafness) not helped by hearing aids', 'Cochlear implants are reserved for bilateral severe-to-profound sensorineural deafness when hearing aids no longer provide benefit. Conductive loss, normal hearing, and unilateral deafness are not the intended indications.'),
('58d62696-205a-58a0-bd34-ca80223b56f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vestibular schwannoma growth often is slow — asymptomatic may be observed; symptomatic → SRS or surgery', '["treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","All need radiation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Observation, SRS (gamma knife), microsurgery depending on size, hearing, age, and symptoms","All need surgery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All need observation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Observation, SRS (gamma knife), microsurgery depending on size, hearing, age, and symptoms', 'Management of vestibular schwannoma is individualized: small asymptomatic tumors are observed, symptomatic or growing tumors may receive stereotactic radiosurgery (gamma knife), and larger ones with significant symptoms undergo microsurgery. No single approach applies to every patient.'),
('5a2ce99f-42f0-58c4-b06c-54d78971436e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cochlear nerve neurofibromatosis type 2 = bilateral schwannomas, treat with hearing preservation surgery or SRS', '["enucleation resulting in decreased functional activity","preservation possible accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","surgery possible mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral schwannomas in NF2; hearing may be spared by partial resections/SRS","deaf after surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral schwannomas in NF2; hearing may be spared by partial resections/SRS', 'Neurofibromatosis type 2 produces bilateral vestibular schwannomas; treatment aims to preserve existing hearing, using partial resections or stereotactic radiosurgery where possible. The idea that hearing is necessarily lost after surgery is incorrect.'),
('ebba24d9-fd03-55e3-b7bc-20ded9d0a724'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Glossopharyngeal nerve block for interventional pain: used for glossopharyngeal neuralgia', '["Vagus block resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal block accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory block mediated by specific receptor activation","Facial block without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Selective block of CN IX at styloid process for pain control (neuralgia, tonsillectomy, tumor)"]'::jsonb, 'Selective block of CN IX at styloid process for pain control (neuralgia, tonsillectomy, tumor)', 'A selective block of the glossopharyngeal nerve at the styloid process controls pain in glossopharyngeal neuralgia and after tonsillectomy or in tumors. It does not target the vagus, trigeminal, facial, or accessory nerves.'),
('1de10cd0-584c-50b4-b9c2-8973d39c112f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Occipital neuralgia (C2) can be mistaken for occipital headache — not a CN but upper cervical nerve', '["CN V (trigeminal) resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital neuralgia: paroxysmal shooting pain from C2 distribution (posterior scalp) — not CN but upper cervical rami","CN IX (glossopharyngeal) mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII (facial) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN I (olfactory) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital neuralgia: paroxysmal shooting pain from C2 distribution (posterior scalp) — not CN but upper cervical rami', 'Occipital neuralgia produces paroxysmal shooting pain in the posterior scalp from the C2 (and C3) upper cervical rami rather than from a cranial nerve. It is therefore not a disorder of CN V, IX, VII, or I.'),
('10cab0ac-f9e3-5af1-981d-cf408b8f187d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Tongue deviation: Hypoglossal (XII) palsy → toward lesion; Supranuclear (UMN) → opposite side', '["Both toward resulting in decreased functional activity","deviation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","XII toward opposite mediated by specific receptor activation","Both away without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Correct: LMN XII → toward lesion; UMN → away from lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Correct: LMN XII → toward lesion; UMN → away from lesion', 'With a lower motor neuron hypoglossal lesion the tongue deviates toward the side of the lesion, whereas with a supranuclear (UMN) lesion it deviates away from the lesion. Assigning the same or the opposite direction to both types is wrong.'),
('c417e22e-b759-5c68-8b6a-2e25489661cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Spinal accessory nerve (XI) is best tested by', '["Eye movement resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial expression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tongue protrusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Shrugging shoulders against resistance (trapezius) and turning head against resistance (SCM) — power","Palate elevation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shrugging shoulders against resistance (trapezius) and turning head against resistance (SCM) — power', 'The spinal accessory nerve is tested by asking the patient to shrug the shoulders against resistance (trapezius) and turn the head against resistance (sternocleidomastoid). Eye movement, facial expression, tongue protrusion, and palate elevation test other nerves.'),
('8c87836a-36b7-52d7-a54a-3b6060149324'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Tongue strength (CN XII): test by having patient push tongue into cheek against resistance', '["Also ask to protrude tongue (look for deviation, atrophy, fasciculations)","Puff cheeks accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Close eyelids mediated by specific receptor activation","Swallow without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shrug shoulders leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Also ask to protrude tongue (look for deviation, atrophy, fasciculations)', 'Along with testing strength by pushing the tongue against the cheek, ask the patient to protrude the tongue and inspect for deviation toward the lesion, atrophy, and fasciculations, which indicate a lower motor neuron CN XII lesion. The other actions test different cranial nerves.'),
('0a30e837-7367-52aa-bcd5-c6502e4aa37b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Palate elevation (CN X) — say ''ah'' → uvula deviates away from CN X lesion (toward normal side)', '["Towards the lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Uvula: deviates AWAY from the vagus (CN X) lesion (intact side pulls up)","Bilaterally deviated mediated by specific receptor activation","deviation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uvula: deviates AWAY from the vagus (CN X) lesion (intact side pulls up)', 'On saying ''ah'', the intact side''s levator veli palatini pulls the uvula upward and away from the damaged CN X. Deviation toward the lesion would imply the opposite pathology.'),
('23c9db54-c8d1-5dad-afe4-72e6b630088a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Gag reflex (CN IX afferent, CN X efferent) — absent on affected side if either CN IX or CN X is damaged', '["gag with IX damage only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only IX damage matter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bilateral absent gag = normal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only X damage matters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Afferent (IX) or efferent (X) damage → absent gag on that side"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent (IX) or efferent (X) damage → absent gag on that side', 'The gag reflex needs CN IX for its afferent limb and CN X for its efferent limb, so damage to either nerve abolishes the gag on that side. Bilateral absence of the gag is not normal.'),
('3c3a3394-63df-5729-9a80-79c43cad44c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Jaw jerk reflex (CN V) — brisk = UMN lesion above motor nucleus', '["Hyper-reflexia = LMN lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","response = CN V lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reduced = UMN lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","Afferent: V (mesencephalic). Efferent: V motor. Hyper-reflexia = corticobulbar lesion","Normal brisk = CN V lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent: V (mesencephalic). Efferent: V motor. Hyper-reflexia = corticobulbar lesion', 'The jaw jerk''s afferent limb travels in CN V via the mesencephalic nucleus and its efferent limb in the CN V motor root. A brisk (hyper-reflexic) response indicates an upper motor neuron (corticobulbar) lesion, not a lower motor neuron problem.'),
('8d9966e9-8150-529b-a7b9-db9cf3b46455'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial sensation (CN V) — test V1 (forehead), V2 (cheek), V3 (chin) with pinprick and cotton wisp', '["Compare side to side; also test corneal reflex (CN V afferent/CN VII efferent)","difference accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All areas test V3 mediated by specific receptor activation","V1 = cheek, V2 = chin, V3 = forehead without affecting the primary tissue pathway","V1 = chin, V2 = forehead, V3 = cheek leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Compare side to side; also test corneal reflex (CN V afferent/CN VII efferent)', 'After testing light touch and pinprick on the forehead (V1), cheek (V2), and chin (V3) with side-to-side comparison, include the corneal reflex whose afferent limb is CN V and efferent limb CN VII. The other options mislabel the divisions or omit the reflex.'),
('7989fb49-05ec-52ad-8a33-54a4e8f62705'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve motor function (CN VII): test frontalis (raise brows), orbicularis oculi (close eyes), orbicularis oris (smile, puff cheeks), platysma', '["Test each muscle group — compare upper vs lower face for UMN vs LMN distinction","Only raise forehead accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only smile and close eyes mediated by specific receptor activation","Only puff cheeks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","distinction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Test each muscle group — compare upper vs lower face for UMN vs LMN distinction', 'Examine each facial muscle group—frontalis, orbicularis oculi, orbicularis oris, and platysma—so upper and lower face can be compared. Sparing of the upper face points to an upper motor neuron lesion, whereas whole-face involvement indicates lower motor neuron disease.'),
('19af0697-dcea-5d2f-9b72-64d6f62bab95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial paralysis management: eye care (artificial tears, taping at night) essential to prevent corneal ulceration', '["Surgery only resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial exercises only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","care needed mediated by specific receptor activation","Corneal protection (#1 priority in CN VII palsy) — especially if eye cannot close","Steroids only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corneal protection (#1 priority in CN VII palsy) — especially if eye cannot close', 'Because an unclosed eye dries and may ulcerate, protecting the cornea with artificial tears and nighttime taping is the first priority in CN VII palsy. Exercises, steroids, or surgery alone do not address this immediate risk.'),
('04ba0926-d43e-5f3c-9dee-86fd69c126b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Mastication (chewing): CN V motor (temporalis, masseter, pterygoids) — loss makes chewing weak/poor', '["Test by palpating masseter and temporalis (clenching teeth) and lateral/medial pterygoid (jaw protrusion/side-to-side)","CN VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN XII mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XI without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Test by palpating masseter and temporalis (clenching teeth) and lateral/medial pterygoid (jaw protrusion/side-to-side)', 'Muscles of mastication are supplied by the CN V motor root; test masseter and temporalis by clenching the teeth and the pterygoids by jaw protrusion and side-to-side movement. CN VII, XII, XI, and IX do not move the jaw.'),
('3286b3fd-58f6-519d-bfd0-369477a15af8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Bells phenomenon (eye turns up and out during forced closure) is normal; with facial palsy it can be seen and is often exaggerated', '["Upward eye movement when trying to close eye — seen in Bell palsy as an attempt to protect cornea","Pathologic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not seen mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in CN III palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in UMN palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upward eye movement when trying to close eye — seen in Bell palsy as an attempt to protect cornea', 'Bell phenomenon is the normal upward and outward roll of the eye during attempted eyelid closure; in facial palsy it becomes prominent as a protective mechanism that keeps the cornea covered. It is not pathologic and is not a sign of CN III or upper motor neuron disease.'),
('b9c6b971-fdd2-5469-bfb8-5bc6ce7b671b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cough reflex: afferent CN X (internal laryngeal, laryngeal), efferent to expiratory muscles (phrenic, intercostals)', '["CN V afferent resulting in decreased functional activity","CN X is afferent for cough (sensation of larynx/trachea) — laryngeal nerve damage → poor cough/aspiration","CN IX afferent mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XI efferent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII efferent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN X is afferent for cough (sensation of larynx/trachea) — laryngeal nerve damage → poor cough/aspiration', 'The afferent limb of the cough reflex is carried by CN X from the larynx and trachea; damage to the laryngeal nerves leaves a poor cough and risk of aspiration. CN IX, V, XI, and VII are not the afferent pathway for cough.'),
('2fcf8415-5e79-5f0a-885c-1bda5e148d75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Tracheostomy is performed for bilateral vocal cord paralysis (CN X/RLN) with stridor', '["For all CN X lesions resulting in decreased functional activity","Secures airway below vocal cords → bypasses obstructed glottis","Only for infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Only for tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only for trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secures airway below vocal cords → bypasses obstructed glottis', 'Tracheostomy creates an airway below the vocal cords, bypassing the narrowed glottis caused by bilateral recurrent laryngeal nerve (CN X) paralysis and stridor. It is not used for every CN X lesion nor limited to cases of infection, tumor, or trauma.'),
('c8e20aac-19e0-57b2-8298-0f4862d910c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Pharyngeal plexus (CN X) motor to pharynx and palate — lesion causes dysphagia, nasal regurgitation', '["Only fever resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only dysphagia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nasal regurgitation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only nasal speech without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal regurgitation (palatal incompetence), swallowing difficulty (pharyngeal constrictors), and nasal speech (hypernasality)"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal regurgitation (palatal incompetence), swallowing difficulty (pharyngeal constrictors), and nasal speech (hypernasality)', 'Loss of pharyngeal plexus (CN X) function causes palatal incompetence with nasal regurgitation, weak pharyngeal constrictors with swallowing difficulty, and hypernasal speech. None of the single isolated symptoms alone represents this complete pattern.'),
('8648f105-233c-5d68-924f-99a793047fc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The nervus intermedius is sometimes called the ''nerve of Wrisberg''', '["Another nerve to stapedius resulting in decreased functional activity","Nothing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Another name for CN X mediated by specific receptor activation","Its anatomy is between motor root of VII and CN VIII in IAM — small but important for taste and lacrimation","Another CN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Its anatomy is between motor root of VII and CN VIII in IAM — small but important for taste and lacrimation', 'The nervus intermedius is the small parasympathetic and special sensory root of CN VII lying between the motor root and CN VIII in the internal auditory meatus; it carries taste and lacrimation fibers. It is not the nerve to stapedius, CN X, or another separate nerve.'),
('bb3cd898-08fa-5762-91a6-49e549ef3f3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vidian nerve (nerve of pterygoid canal) block is used for', '["Cluster headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Tic douloureux accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chronic rhinitis, allergic rhinitis (by reducing parasympathetic) — intranasal vidian neurectomy","Epistaxis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic rhinitis, allergic rhinitis (by reducing parasympathetic) — intranasal vidian neurectomy', 'Blocking or dividing the nerve of the pterygoid canal (vidian nerve) reduces parasympathetic drive to the nasal mucosa and is used for chronic and allergic rhinitis. It does not treat cluster headache, trigeminal neuralgia, epistaxis, or general facial pain.'),
('197dd7f3-8744-513a-b5bc-5f013aa55025'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Sphenopalatine (pterygopalatine) ganglion block treats', '["Tinnitus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Bell palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertigo mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Trigeminal neuralgia without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cluster headache, migraine, facial pain — via intranasal application of local anesthetic (Sphenopalatine block)"]'::jsonb, 'Cluster headache, migraine, facial pain — via intranasal application of local anesthetic (Sphenopalatine block)', 'A sphenopalatine (pterygopalatine) ganglion block relieves cluster headache and migraine, and some facial pain, when local anesthetic is applied intranasally near the ganglion. It is not used for tinnitus, Bell palsy, vertigo, or trigeminal neuralgia itself.'),
('6cb52028-ec9b-587d-b3db-49fc4be4f144'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Gasserian (trigeminal) ganglion block/lesion treats', '["Glossopharyngeal neuralgia resulting in decreased functional activity","Bell palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cluster headache mediated by specific receptor activation","Migraine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminal neuralgia (V2/V3) — radiofrequency rhizotomy, glycerol rhizolysis, balloon compression"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal neuralgia (V2/V3) — radiofrequency rhizotomy, glycerol rhizolysis, balloon compression', 'Percutaneous lesions of the gasserian (trigeminal) ganglion—radiofrequency rhizotomy, glycerol injection, or balloon compression—are used to treat trigeminal neuralgia, mainly affecting the V2 and V3 divisions. They do not treat glossopharyngeal neuralgia, Bell palsy, cluster headache, or migraine.'),
('6785aa82-9757-552b-a3d6-b310c5e7d831'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Gamma knife radiosurgery (SRS) for trigeminal neuralgia — targets trigeminal nerve root entry zone', '["For all CN neuralgia resulting in decreased functional activity","For migraine only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","For cluster headache only mediated by specific receptor activation","role for trigeminal neuralgia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-invasive, less risk of facial numbness than surgical rhizotomy; 80% success"]'::jsonb, 'Non-invasive, less risk of facial numbness than surgical rhizotomy; 80% success', 'Stereotactic radiosurgery for trigeminal neuralgia is non-invasive, carries less risk of facial numbness than open rhizotomy, and succeeds in about 80% of patients by targeting the trigeminal root entry zone. It is not appropriate for all neuralgias or only for migraine or cluster headache.'),
('24068eda-c9f4-5250-9534-9864c0b7c248'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Microvascular decompression (MVD) for trigeminal neuralgia: decompress CN V root from arterial loop (SCA, AICA)', '["Only radiation works resulting in decreased functional activity","Most common cause = SCA compression on dorsal root entry zone; MVD maintains nerve function, high success rate","surgery indicated mediated by specific receptor activation","Not effective without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only medication works leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most common cause = SCA compression on dorsal root entry zone; MVD maintains nerve function, high success rate', 'In trigeminal neuralgia the superior cerebellar artery (SCA) typically compresses the trigeminal nerve at the dorsal root entry zone; microvascular decompression separates the vessel from the nerve, relieving pain while preserving nerve function. Radiation or medication alone is not the definitive surgical approach for refractory cases.'),
('aa1526de-c617-51b5-914d-4e4ad7479894'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Hemifacial spasm: involuntary unilateral facial contractions — often due to vascular compression of CN VII root exit zone', '["CN VIII compression resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX compression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ephaptic transmission from compressing artery (AICA/PICA) → myelin damage; treat with MVD","CN XII compression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN V compression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ephaptic transmission from compressing artery (AICA/PICA) → myelin damage; treat with MVD', 'Hemifacial spasm results from vascular compression of the facial nerve at its root exit zone, where the damaged myelin allows ephaptic transmission between nerve fibers and triggers involuntary unilateral facial contractions. Microvascular decompression addresses the underlying vascular cause, whereas compression of other cranial nerves produces different clinical deficits.'),
('b7aed87b-3d54-5cf8-9d27-aa18103a2fcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Botulinum toxin injections for hemifacial spasm or blepharospasm — neuromodulation of presynaptic ACh release', '["Surgery only resulting in decreased functional activity","Botulinum: effective for focal dystonia; reduces muscle overactivity for 3-4 months","treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Medication only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only observation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Botulinum: effective for focal dystonia; reduces muscle overactivity for 3-4 months', 'Botulinum toxin blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, weakening the overactive muscles and providing effective relief in focal dystonias such as hemifacial spasm and blepharospasm. Because the effect lasts roughly 3-4 months, repeated injections are needed rather than surgery or observation alone.'),
('a690aaac-a962-59ff-a527-83136401752d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Geniculate neuralgia (nervus intermedius neuralgia) — deep ear pain often triggered by swallowing (spicy/acidic)', '["CN V neuralgia resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VIII neuralgia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX neuralgia mediated by specific receptor activation","Rare CN VII sensory neuralgia — severe ear pain (from nervus intermedius sensory fibers)","CN X neuralgia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rare CN VII sensory neuralgia — severe ear pain (from nervus intermedius sensory fibers)', 'Geniculate (nervus intermedius) neuralgia is a rare facial-nerve sensory disorder in which severe deep ear pain is triggered by swallowing, especially with spicy or acidic foods. The other cranial nerves listed produce different pain distributions and triggers.'),
('ed7274c8-4c4b-5333-b113-916c893230cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Tolosa-Hunt syndrome (painful ophthalmoplegia) — granulomatous inflammation of cavernous sinus/superior orbital fissure', '["Orbital cellulitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Meningioma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Idiopathic granulomatous inflammation → CN palsy (III, IV, VI, V1) with severe orbital headache","Cavernous sinus thrombosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carotid-cavernous fistula leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Idiopathic granulomatous inflammation → CN palsy (III, IV, VI, V1) with severe orbital headache', 'Tolosa-Hunt syndrome is an idiopathic granulomatous inflammation of the cavernous sinus and superior orbital fissure that causes painful ophthalmoplegia with palsy of CN III, IV, VI and V1. It is distinguished from infection, thrombosis and fistula by its non-infectious, steroid-responsive inflammatory nature.'),
('c67e2c8e-ed33-59e2-af63-c9dc3864899c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Orbital cellulitis (ethmoiditis) — infection spreading via orbital veins/valveless system → proptosis, CN VI & II, chemosis, fever', '["Only CN IV resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN V mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Emergent: threaten vision (CN II) and life (cavernous sinus thrombophlebitis, meningitis). Treat IV antibiotics","Only CN VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emergent: threaten vision (CN II) and life (cavernous sinus thrombophlebitis, meningitis). Treat IV antibiotics', 'Orbital cellulitis, often complicating ethmoid sinusitis, threatens vision through optic nerve (CN II) compression and can spread to cavernous sinus thrombophlebitis or meningitis, making it an emergency. Immediate intravenous antibiotics are required; involvement limited to one ocular motor nerve would not convey this urgency.'),
('0bf01732-bd8b-5eed-a094-a7a3d9044053'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Meningioma of sphenoid wing (en plaque) — CN II, III, IV, V1, VI may be compressed with proptosis', '["Sphenoid wing leads to hyperostosis, extra-axial mass; may invade orbit/cavernous sinus","All CN irrespective accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN III mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CN II without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sphenoid wing leads to hyperostosis, extra-axial mass; may invade orbit/cavernous sinus', 'A sphenoid wing meningioma en plaque is an extra-axial tumor that typically produces hyperostosis and may extend into the orbit and cavernous sinus, compressing CN II, III, IV, V1 and VI. It does not spare nerves or affect a single cranial nerve in isolation.'),
('80bca38b-870f-5de4-a8aa-5539a42b8821'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cavernous sinus thrombosis (septic) — fever, headache, proptosis, ophthalmoplegia (CN III, IV, V1, V2, VI) + vision loss (CN II)', '["Only unilateral resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","CN II involvement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Self-limited mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Life-threatening infection; treat with IV antibiotics + anticoagulation (controversial) and surgery if needed","fever leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Life-threatening infection; treat with IV antibiotics + anticoagulation (controversial) and surgery if needed', 'Septic cavernous sinus thrombosis is a life-threatening infection presenting with fever, headache, proptosis and ophthalmoplegia from involvement of CN III, IV, V1, V2 and VI, with visual loss when CN II is affected. Management requires intravenous antibiotics, with anticoagulation and surgical drainage considered when indicated.'),
('b0196a67-50e9-5f71-8600-9931e0e88ba6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Carotid-cavernous fistula (CCF): arterial blood from ICA/ECA → cavernous sinus → proptosis, chemosis, eye redness, CN palsy, visual loss', '["Only CN palsy resulting in decreased functional activity","Only headache accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Same as thrombosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Only exophthalmos without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct (ICA) or indirect (dural AV fistula) → ocular congestive signs; treat with endovascular coiling/embolization"]'::jsonb, 'Direct (ICA) or indirect (dural AV fistula) → ocular congestive signs; treat with endovascular coiling/embolization', 'A carotid-cavernous fistula shunts arterial blood from the internal or external carotid artery into the cavernous sinus, producing congestive ocular signs such as proptosis, chemosis and a red eye along with cranial nerve palsies and visual loss. Direct (ICA) and indirect (dural AV fistula) types are treated with endovascular coiling or embolization.'),
('11028c3b-5d50-5e10-ba41-444947395016'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) — vulnerable to many conditions: increased ICP, diabetes, petrous apex, Dorello, cavernous sinus, orbit', '["Only in trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Only in tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Long intracranial course = false localizing sign (↑ICP, hydrocephalus)","Not vulnerable without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only in diabetes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Long intracranial course = false localizing sign (↑ICP, hydrocephalus)', 'The abducens nerve has the longest intracranial course of the cranial nerves, running from the pons to the cavernous sinus, so it is readily damaged by raised intracranial pressure, diabetes, petrous apex pathology, Dorello''s canal, cavernous sinus or orbital disease. This long course explains why it commonly acts as a false localizing sign in raised intracranial pressure.'),
('a3290cf3-df4b-5f38-b804-bdcfd2ab5d27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trigeminal nerve (CN V) anatomy: sensory = trigeminal ganglion (Meckel cave); motor root bypasses ganglion and goes to V3', '["Motor is in ganglion and all divisions resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor goes with V1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor goes with V2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor goes through ganglion like sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor to mastication goes via foramen ovale (not through ganglion) — skip ganglion to reach V3"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to mastication goes via foramen ovale (not through ganglion) — skip ganglion to reach V3', 'The trigeminal motor root bypasses the trigeminal (sensory) ganglion in Meckel''s cave and runs through the foramen ovale with the third division to supply the muscles of mastication. Unlike the sensory divisions, it does not traverse or synapse in the ganglion.'),
('1f21f2ef-7971-57b5-8488-67717ccc1392'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The trigeminal nerve (V) test: corneal reflex (V1) is best. Jaw jerk (V motor). Sensory V1, V2, V3. Mastication strength.', '["Only sensory resulting in decreased functional activity","Comprehensive CN V testing: sensory (3), motor (masseter, temporalis, pterygoid), corneal reflex, jaw jerk","Only symptoms mediated by specific receptor activation","Only corneal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Comprehensive CN V testing: sensory (3), motor (masseter, temporalis, pterygoid), corneal reflex, jaw jerk', 'Evaluation of the trigeminal nerve requires testing all three sensory divisions (V1, V2, V3), the corneal reflex (afferent limb V1), the jaw jerk, and the motor strength of masseter, temporalis and pterygoids. Testing only one component would miss lesions affecting the other divisions or the motor root.'),
('09db9c22-b204-5c96-a143-288a6993abb4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve (VII) test: forehead raising, eye closure, smiling, taste (anterior 2/3), stapedius reflex (hyperacusis), lacrimation', '["Only lacrimation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hyperacusis mediated by specific receptor activation","Assess upper vs lower face for UMN/LMN; taste (anterior 2/3); Schirmer (lacrimation) for lesion localization","Only taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Assess upper vs lower face for UMN/LMN; taste (anterior 2/3); Schirmer (lacrimation) for lesion localization', 'Facial nerve testing compares upper and lower facial movement to separate upper from lower motor neuron lesions, and adds taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, the stapedius reflex (hyperacusis) and lacrimation via the Schirmer test. Each element localizes the lesion to a specific segment of the nerve''s course.'),
('5dede329-32da-5432-a7b8-466612aa452e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vestibulocochlear (VIII) test: hearing (tuning fork — Rinne, Weber), audiogram, vestibular testing (calorics, VNG, head impulse)', '["Only calorics resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only tuning forks accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","testing protocols mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only audiogram without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rinne: AC>BC = normal (sensorineural); BC>AC = conductive loss. Weber: lateralizes to better ear (sensorineural) or worse (conductive)"]'::jsonb, 'Rinne: AC>BC = normal (sensorineural); BC>AC = conductive loss. Weber: lateralizes to better ear (sensorineural) or worse (conductive)', 'Vestibulocochlear testing combines tuning-fork tests (Rinne, Weber), audiometry and vestibular assessment with caloric testing, VNG or the head impulse test. Rinne establishes whether air conduction exceeds bone conduction, while Weber lateralization separates conductive from sensorineural loss.'),
('dd81cf06-6a86-5c04-9e47-8b8c4a8a4238'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Rinne test: air conduction (AC) > bone conduction (BC) is normal; BC > AC indicates conductive loss; AC > BC is also seen in sensorineural loss', '["AC>BC = sensorineural loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Rinne: AC > BC (both ears) = normal (sensorineural > conductive)","BC>AC = normal mediated by specific receptor activation","interpretation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AC<BC = sensorineural loss (alternative wording) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rinne: AC > BC (both ears) = normal (sensorineural > conductive)', 'In the Rinne test, air conduction exceeding bone conduction in both ears indicates normal hearing or a sensorineural loss, whereas bone conduction greater than air conduction indicates a conductive loss. Bone conduction exceeding air conduction is therefore never a normal result.'),
('675d2c55-eeba-5713-b319-d4ed74a9a973'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Weber test: tuning fork on forehead — lateralizes to better-hearing ear in sensorineural loss; to worse-hearing ear in conductive loss', '["Lateralizes to worse in sensorineural resulting in decreased functional activity","lateralization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateralizes to midline mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateralizes to better in conductive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateralizes to better ear (sensorineural) or worse (conductive/middle ear)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateralizes to better ear (sensorineural) or worse (conductive/middle ear)', 'Weber lateralizes to the better-hearing ear in sensorineural loss because the affected ear cannot perceive the vibration, and to the affected (worse) ear in conductive loss because the bone-conducted sound bypasses the obstructed middle ear. Lateralization direction therefore distinguishes cochlear from middle-ear pathology.'),
('5cc0cf22-1534-5213-bb7f-2b080023fc16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Glossopharyngeal (IX) test: taste posterior 1/3, gag (afferent), carotid sinus reflex (IX afferent)', '["Only carotid sinus resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only taste accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only gag mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Check taste posterior 1/3 (bitter), gag reflex, vocal quality (not primarily), and blood pressure / HR if carotid sinus involvement","Only palatal strength leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Check taste posterior 1/3 (bitter), gag reflex, vocal quality (not primarily), and blood pressure / HR if carotid sinus involvement', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve conveys taste from the posterior third of the tongue, the afferent limb of the gag reflex and the carotid sinus baroreceptor and chemoreceptor input. Testing also includes blood-pressure and heart-rate responses when carotid sinus dysfunction is suspected.'),
('799f90d9-319a-515c-8c77-1028f7e90159'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Vagus nerve (X) test: palate movement (say ''ah''), gag (efferent), voice quality (hoarse), swallow, cough', '["Palate deviation away from lesion; vocal cord palsy (stridor or hoarse); weak cough; nasal regurgitation","Only voice accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only gag mediated by specific receptor activation","Only palate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only swallow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palate deviation away from lesion; vocal cord palsy (stridor or hoarse); weak cough; nasal regurgitation', 'A vagus nerve lesion causes the palate to deviate away from the affected side, vocal cord palsy with hoarseness or stridor, a weak cough and often nasal regurgitation because palatal closure is lost. Testing a single function such as voice or swallowing would miss the full spectrum of vagal dysfunction.'),
('dc9b9636-5221-5faf-b257-b2259bc24eaf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Accessory nerve (XI) test: shoulder shrug (trapezius) and head turn (SCM) — compare power and side to side', '["Only trapezius resulting in decreased functional activity","Only SCM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Palsy causes drooping shoulder (trapezius), difficulty turning head away from lesion (SCM)","clinical test without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both = only accessory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Palsy causes drooping shoulder (trapezius), difficulty turning head away from lesion (SCM)', 'Accessory nerve palsy weakens the trapezius, causing the shoulder to droop, and the sternocleidomastoid, making it difficult to turn the head away from the side of the lesion. Both muscles must be tested and compared side to side to detect the deficit.'),
('50ab91c4-458b-52c0-9a45-4c0e3485c354'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Hypoglossal nerve (XII) test: tongue protrusion, lateral movement (cheek push), look for atrophy and fasciculations', '["LMN: atrophy/fasciculations + deviation to same side. UMN: deviation away (without atrophy)","Deviation toward normal side = LMN accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","deviation = LMN mediated by specific receptor activation","Deviation away = LMN without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fasciculations = UMN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'LMN: atrophy/fasciculations + deviation to same side. UMN: deviation away (without atrophy)', 'A lower motor neuron hypoglossal lesion causes tongue wasting, fasciculations and deviation toward the paralyzed side, whereas an upper motor neuron lesion makes the tongue deviate away from the lesion without atrophy. The presence of atrophy and fasciculations therefore points to an LMN injury.'),
('17fdb586-5054-5ffd-9890-ee49fb0eb340'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve examination order: I (smell), II (vision/pupils), III,IV,VI (eye movements), V (sensory/motor face), VII (face), VIII (hearing/balance), IX (gag taste), X (palate/larynx), XI (SCM/trap), XII (tongue)', '["Only motor CN resulting in decreased functional activity","Only main 6 CN accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","order needed mediated by specific receptor activation","Systematic exam of all 12 CN in neurological assessment","Only sensory CN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systematic exam of all 12 CN in neurological assessment', 'A complete neurologic examination assesses all 12 cranial nerves in a systematic order, from olfaction to hypoglossal function, because deficits may be subtle or combined. Testing only a subset of motor or sensory nerves risks missing a clinically relevant lesion.'),
('49c58479-a96e-584f-93fc-587ede419d4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I) is the only sensory system with direct cortical projection without thalamic relay', '["Only goes to thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory bulb projects to piriform/amygdala/entorhinal without synapsing in thalamus","Also goes to thalamus from bulb mediated by specific receptor activation","All sensory goes first to thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Does not project to cortex at all leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory bulb projects to piriform/amygdala/entorhinal without synapsing in thalamus', 'The olfactory system is unique among sensory systems because the olfactory bulb projects directly to the piriform cortex, amygdala and entorhinal cortex without a thalamic relay. All other sensory modalities synapse in the thalamus before reaching their cortical destinations.'),
('c303cf6a-2988-54b9-8003-e805e8e27e42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Crocodile tears (gustatory lacrimation) — complication of proximal facial nerve injury', '["aberrant resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Distal facial injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aberrant regeneration: superior salivatory fibers (salivation) misdirect to greater petrosal (lacrimal) → tears while eating","Lower facial injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN V injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aberrant regeneration: superior salivatory fibers (salivation) misdirect to greater petrosal (lacrimal) → tears while eating', 'Crocodile tears arise from aberrant regeneration after a proximal facial nerve injury, in which superior salivatory fibers meant for salivation grow into the greater petrosal nerve supplying lacrimation, causing tearing while eating. It follows proximal, not distal or lower facial, nerve damage.'),
('0c03d91b-4274-5934-9d8b-a1a367a6c37e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Frey syndrome (gustatory sweating) after parotid surgery — complications: in parotidectomy → aberrant CN IX regeneration', '["CN IX (parotid) → auriculotemporal nerve (sweat glands) → sweating on eating. Must treat with botulinum or surgery","CN V misdirection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN X misdirection mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III misdirection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII misdirection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (parotid) → auriculotemporal nerve (sweat glands) → sweating on eating. Must treat with botulinum or surgery', 'Frey syndrome occurs when the auriculotemporal nerve forms aberrant connections after parotid surgery, so parasympathetic fibers meant for the parotid gland instead drive the sweat glands, causing gustatory sweating on eating. Botulinum toxin injection or surgical revision is used when symptoms are troublesome.'),
('93a6815a-fb68-5339-874e-52e3886300e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Marcus Gunn jaw-winking — congenital synkinesis between V3 and CN III (levator)', '["Trigeminal motor nucleus misdirected to levator palpebrae (CN III) → jaw opening lifts the eyelid","CN IX to CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN V to CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII to CN III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN XII to CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal motor nucleus misdirected to levator palpebrae (CN III) → jaw opening lifts the eyelid', 'Marcus Gunn jaw-winking is a congenital synkinesis in which trigeminal motor signals are misdirected to the levator palpebrae superioris, so opening or moving the jaw lifts the eyelid. The pathway involves aberrant V3-to-CN III connections rather than the other nerves listed.'),
('e9a21532-dad9-5667-903f-2d06f4531fb8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The cranial nerves with the longest intracranial course: CN VI, CN IV, and CN III (especially CN VI)', '["CN III longest resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VI has the longest intracranial course (from pons to cavernous sinus) → most false localizing sign of ↑ICP","CN VIII longest mediated by specific receptor activation","CN IV longest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII longest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VI has the longest intracranial course (from pons to cavernous sinus) → most false localizing sign of ↑ICP', 'The abducens nerve has the longest intracranial course of the cranial nerves, running from the pons to the cavernous sinus, which makes it the most common false localizing sign of raised intracranial pressure. No other cranial nerve travels so far within the skull.'),
('5bd3f431-c24a-5bb6-8748-d66260ded628'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The smallest cranial nerve by number of axons is', '["CN I resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI mediated by specific receptor activation","Trochlear (CN IV) — supplies only 4 single muscle (superior oblique) — smallest","CN XII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trochlear (CN IV) — supplies only 4 single muscle (superior oblique) — smallest', 'The trochlear nerve is the smallest cranial nerve because it contains the fewest axons, innervating only the superior oblique muscle, which depresses and intorts the adducted eye. Nerves such as CN I, III, VI and XII supply far more numerous targets and contain many more fibers.'),
('9ac77afe-0793-591d-a5da-517d1500f801'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The largest cranial nerve (by diameter) is', '["CN X (vagus) resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VII (facial) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VIII (acoustic) mediated by specific receptor activation","Trigeminal (CN V) — largest CN, both sensory (large) and motor root","CN III (oculomotor) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal (CN V) — largest CN, both sensory (large) and motor root', 'The trigeminal nerve is the largest cranial nerve by diameter, carrying a large sensory root from the face along with a motor root for mastication. Its size reflects the extensive sensory territory covered by its three divisions.'),
('c10da48f-0c38-5f61-86f3-065af3be7992'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The only cranial nerves with parasympathetic function are', '["CN V, VII, IX resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III, V, VII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None have PSNS mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III, VII, IX, X (Edinger-Westphal, superior/inferior salivatory, dorsal vagus)","CN III, VII, IX, X, all have PSNS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, VII, IX, X (Edinger-Westphal, superior/inferior salivatory, dorsal vagus)', 'Only four cranial nerves carry parasympathetic (general visceral efferent) fibers: CN III from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, CN VII from the superior salivatory nucleus, CN IX from the inferior salivatory nucleus and CN X from the dorsal vagal nucleus. The other optioned sets include or omit nerves that do not carry parasympathetic outflow.'),
('2abb2a64-5919-581f-a7cd-f3fa68121149'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The cranial nerves that exit the skull via the jugular foramen are', '["CN IX, X, XI (glossopharyngeal, vagus, spinal accessory)","CN V, VI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII, VIII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III, IV, V1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN XII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, XI (glossopharyngeal, vagus, spinal accessory)', 'The glossopharyngeal, vagus and spinal accessory nerves (CN IX, X, XI) exit the skull through the jugular foramen together with the internal jugular vein. Each of the other foramina listed transmits a different set of nerves.'),
('d9c5c385-d93a-5e38-a2e0-8d0c49be4a58'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The cranial nerves that exit via superior orbital fissure are', '["CN V1, VII resulting in decreased functional activity","CN III, IV, VI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN II, III, IV mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III, IV, V1, VI (all extraocular nerves + ophthalmic V1)","CN V1, V2, VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III, IV, V1, VI (all extraocular nerves + ophthalmic V1)', 'The oculomotor, trochlear, abducens and ophthalmic (V1) nerves all enter the orbit through the superior orbital fissure. Nerves using other routes, such as the optic canal or foramen rotundum, take different paths.'),
('8dacf88c-e09f-5bae-b44c-933d858e2b1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The only cranial nerve that passes through the optic canal is', '["CN II (optic nerve) with ophthalmic artery","CN IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN III mediated by specific receptor activation","CN V1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN II (optic nerve) with ophthalmic artery', 'The optic canal transmits only the optic nerve (CN II) together with the ophthalmic artery. The other ocular nerves enter the orbit through the superior orbital fissure.'),
('20da1340-518c-54f7-b135-0ebe7f88487d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Facial nerve (CN VII) runs through the stylomastoid foramen after which gives off its motor branches', '["Then it divides into 5 motor branches: temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, cervical within the parotid gland","Via internal acoustic meatus again accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Via hypoglossal canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Via jugular foramen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Via foramen ovale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Then it divides into 5 motor branches: temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, cervical within the parotid gland', 'After emerging through the stylomastoid foramen, the facial nerve enters the parotid gland and divides into five terminal motor branches: temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular and cervical. It does not re-enter other cranial foramina.'),
('1272f3ba-ddfb-5b37-b7bc-76ad6eed3c40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The chorda tympani (CN VII) carries taste from anterior 2/3 of tongue and parasympathetic to submandibular/sublingual glands', '["Only motor to tongue muscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Joins the lingual nerve (V3) in the infratemporal fossa; after leaving facial canal through petrotympanic fissure","Only taste mediated by specific receptor activation","Only parasympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both taste and salivation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Joins the lingual nerve (V3) in the infratemporal fossa; after leaving facial canal through petrotympanic fissure', 'The chorda tympani leaves the facial canal through the petrotympanic fissure and joins the lingual nerve (V3) in the infratemporal fossa, carrying taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and preganglionic secretomotor fibers to the submandibular and sublingual glands. It does not supply tongue muscles.'),
('3725d5dc-ee71-5832-982a-09f4d2b920e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The facial nerve after the geniculate ganglion gives off the greater petrosal nerve then nerve to stapedius then chorda tympani', '["Stapedius → chorda → great petrosal resulting in decreased functional activity","Order: great petrosal → stapedius → chorda tympani → facial motor branches after stylomastoid","Great petrosal → chorda → stapedius mediated by specific receptor activation","Chorda → stapedius → great petrosal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only chorda and stapedius exist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Order: great petrosal → stapedius → chorda tympani → facial motor branches after stylomastoid', 'Branches leave the facial nerve in a fixed proximal-to-distal order after the geniculate ganglion: the greater petrosal nerve, then the nerve to stapedius, then the chorda tympani, followed by the terminal motor branches exiting at the stylomastoid foramen. The other sequences listed are not anatomically correct.'),
('cae89fbf-7c9f-533e-850f-ce1c3b3af4ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve embryology: branchial arches — CN V (1st arch), VII (2nd), IX (3rd), X (4th/6th)', '["X = 3rd arch resulting in decreased functional activity","Each arch contributes specific CN: arch 1 = V; arch 2 = VII; arch 3 = IX; arch 4/6 = X","VII = 1st arch mediated by specific receptor activation","IX = 2nd arch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","V = 2nd arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Each arch contributes specific CN: arch 1 = V; arch 2 = VII; arch 3 = IX; arch 4/6 = X', 'Each branchial arch contributes a specific cranial nerve: the first arch gives CN V, the second CN VII, the third CN IX, and the fourth and sixth arches give CN X. This arch-to-nerve mapping is fixed during development.'),
('4cac4a58-3f92-5d2e-91ef-eb2a22005efd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Special visceral efferent (SVE) cranial nerves — branchial arch muscles: SVE = CN V (mastication), VII (facial), IX (stylopharyngeus), X (pharynx/larynx), XI (SCM/trapezius)', '["Only XI resulting in decreased functional activity","All cranial nerves are SVE accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SVE = V, VII, IX, X, XI all branchial arch muscles","Only IX and X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only V and VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SVE = V, VII, IX, X, XI all branchial arch muscles', 'Special visceral efferent fibers innervate branchial-arch muscles, so the SVE cranial nerves are CN V (mastication), VII (facial expression), IX (stylopharyngeus), X (pharynx and larynx) and XI (SCM and trapezius). The other lists omit an SVE-bearing nerve or include nerves that are not SVE.'),
('2839c6d9-e094-568c-b258-50bf103938a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'General somatic efferent (GSE) CN — to extraocular muscles and tongue: CN III, IV, VI, XII', '["Somatic: III, IV, VI (eye muscles), XII (tongue muscles)","III, IV, VI, XI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","III, IV, VI, VII mediated by specific receptor activation","All CN motir without affecting the primary tissue pathway","III, IV, V, VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatic: III, IV, VI (eye muscles), XII (tongue muscles)', 'General somatic efferent fibers innervate skeletal muscle derived from somites, so the GSE cranial nerves are CN III, IV and VI to the extraocular muscles and CN XII to the tongue. CN XI, VII and V innervate branchial-arch muscles and belong to the SVE group instead.'),
('8e43e319-d40f-5e7f-ba70-204d410ed4da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'General visceral efferent (GVE) — parasympathetic: CN III, VII, IX, X (to smooth muscle, glands)', '["III, VII, IX = GVE resulting in decreased functional activity","All = GVE accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN III (pupil), VII (lacrimal/salivary), IX (parotid), X (viscera)","III, VII, IX, X = GVE without affecting the primary tissue pathway","V, VII, IX = GVE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III (pupil), VII (lacrimal/salivary), IX (parotid), X (viscera)', 'General visceral efferent (parasympathetic) fibers travel in CN III (Edinger-Westphal, to the pupil), CN VII and CN IX (salivatory nuclei, to glands) and CN X (dorsal vagal nucleus, to viscera). None of the alternative lists correctly identifies the full set of GVE nerves.'),
('405cdad5-05e5-5154-83ab-a5fe63c40a76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Special visceral afferent (SVA) — taste and smell: CN I (olfaction), VII (taste ant 2/3), IX (taste post 1/3), X (taste epiglottis)', '["Only CN I resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN VII, IX, X accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CN I and VII mediated by specific receptor activation","All CN are SVA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SVA: CN I (smell), VII, IX, X (taste)"]'::jsonb, 'SVA: CN I (smell), VII, IX, X (taste)', 'Special visceral afferent fibers subserve olfaction and gustation: CN I carries smell while CN VII, IX and X carry taste from the tongue and epiglottis. The complete set of SVA nerves is therefore CN I, VII, IX and X.'),
('95abf0fb-b5d2-59fc-9f8c-dfc0f02df5cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Special somatic afferent (SSA) — vision and hearing/balance: CN II (vision), CN VIII (hearing/balance)', '["CN III = SSA resulting in decreased functional activity","CN I = SSA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SSA: CN II (sight), CN VIII (hearing + balance)","CN X = SSA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN V = SSA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SSA: CN II (sight), CN VIII (hearing + balance)', 'Special somatic afferent fibers carry vision through CN II and hearing with balance through CN VIII. The other cranial nerves listed subserve different functional classes such as olfaction, mastication or visceral function.'),
('a8dc1aeb-525e-5775-ad0b-3b9fd2dcac28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve development: CN III, IV, VI develop from the midbrain; CN V, VII, VIII from pons; CN IX, X, XII from medulla; CN XI from cord', '["All from pons resulting in decreased functional activity","All from medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain (III, IV), Pons (V, VI, VII, VIII), Medulla (IX, X, XII, plus spinal XI)","All from midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All from spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (III, IV), Pons (V, VI, VII, VIII), Medulla (IX, X, XII, plus spinal XI)', 'Cranial nerve nuclei develop in a segmental pattern: CN III and IV arise from the midbrain, CN V, VI, VII and VIII from the pons, CN IX, X, XII and the cranial part of XI from the medulla, and the spinal part of XI from the upper cervical cord. This pattern explains the location of brainstem lesions.'),
('44e94847-8ecb-5a2a-a71c-ecdd938aa1b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Corticobulbar (UMN) lesions to CN nuclei: bilateral innervation to all except lower face (CN VII) and tongue (CN XII)', '["All bilateral innervation resulting in decreased functional activity","All contralateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lower face (CN VII) and genioglossus (CN XII) are mostly contralateral — supranuclear lesion affects these","Lower face ipsilateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Upper face contralateral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower face (CN VII) and genioglossus (CN XII) are mostly contralateral — supranuclear lesion affects these', 'Corticobulbar fibers innervate most motor cranial nerve nuclei bilaterally, but the lower-face portion of CN VII and the genioglossus of CN XII receive mostly contralateral input, so a supranuclear lesion selectively weakens the contralateral lower face or deviates the tongue away. This asymmetry underlies the classic UMN patterns.'),
('c7d9f4d5-d32f-5c64-87b6-e32f3c9892f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Pseudobulbar palsy: bilateral UMN lesions (corticobulbar) — spastic, dysphagia, dysarthria + emotional lability (pseudobulbar affect)', '["Bilateral → loss of UMN control → spastic bulbar palsy (spastic dysarthria, dysphagia, emotional incontinence)","Only emotional lability accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","dysarthria mediated by specific receptor activation","Flaccid dysphagia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-spastic, emotion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral → loss of UMN control → spastic bulbar palsy (spastic dysarthria, dysphagia, emotional incontinence)', 'Pseudobulbar palsy results from bilateral upper motor neuron (corticobulbar) damage, producing spastic dysarthria and dysphagia with a brisk jaw jerk and emotional lability. Because the lesion is supranuclear, the affected muscles are spastic rather than wasted or flaccid.'),
('9dabdc2a-58e3-51f3-a70d-45d9840f8476'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Bulbar palsy: LMN lesion of CN IX, X, XII — flaccid paralysis, nasal speech, drooling, tongue fasciculations, absent gag', '["Spastic, fasciculations resulting in decreased functional activity","speech change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","dysphagia mediated by specific receptor activation","tongue involvement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Flaccid dysphonia/dysarthria, nasal regurgitation, wasting/fasciculations of tongue, absent pharyngeal reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid dysphonia/dysarthria, nasal regurgitation, wasting/fasciculations of tongue, absent pharyngeal reflex', 'Bulbar palsy reflects lower motor neuron damage to CN IX, X and XII, causing flaccid dysphonia, nasal speech and regurgitation, with tongue wasting and fasciculations and an absent gag reflex. Spastic features would instead indicate an upper motor neuron lesion.'),
('b2b5b246-3dca-5099-9d8f-b2867b1a489a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Cranial nerve injury in skull fractures: CN I (cribriform), VII (temporal bone), VIII (temporal bone), VI (clivus), XII (hypoglossal canal)', '["CN IX, X, XI = jugular fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","All equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VII and VIII most vulnerable in temporal bone fractures mediated by specific receptor activation","CN I and II in frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Also CN III, IV, V, VI (trauma to orbit/cavernous sinus/ superior orbital fissure). CN VII most common in temporal fractures"]'::jsonb, 'Also CN III, IV, V, VI (trauma to orbit/cavernous sinus/ superior orbital fissure). CN VII most common in temporal fractures', 'In skull-base fractures, CN I is injured at the cribriform plate, CN VII and VIII in the temporal bone, CN VI at the clivus and CN XII at the hypoglossal canal, with CN III, IV, V1 and VI also vulnerable near the orbit, superior orbital fissure or cavernous sinus. The facial nerve is the most commonly injured cranial nerve in temporal bone fractures.'),
('a74e5e0d-63de-518f-8b04-e585d33f5dd9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Trochlear nerve (CN IV) palsy is most often due to which of the following?', '["Brain tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aneurysm mediated by specific receptor activation","cause without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head trauma (decussation/nerve susceptible), congenital, microvascular (diabetes/hypertension)"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma (decussation/nerve susceptible), congenital, microvascular (diabetes/hypertension)', 'Trochlear nerve palsy most often follows closed head trauma because the nerve is long, thin and decussates at the anterior medullary velum, but it may also be congenital or due to microvascular disease such as diabetes or hypertension. Tumors, infection and aneurysm are comparatively less common causes.'),
('29fcb330-9899-50bb-abd5-989809bf4bf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I) is classified as which of the following?', '["Pure sensory (special visceral afferent)","Pure motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both sensory and motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pure sensory (special visceral afferent)', 'The olfactory nerve is purely sensory and is classified as special visceral afferent because it conveys the special sense of smell. It carries no motor, parasympathetic or sympathetic fibers.'),
('71b30d83-9b9b-5f11-bb2a-b472e4ea2182'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'Olfactory receptor neurons of cranial nerve I are located in which of the following sites?', '["Olfactory bulb resulting in decreased functional activity","Olfactory epithelium (superior nasal concha / nasal septum)","Olfactory tract mediated by specific receptor activation","Piriform cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cribriform plate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory epithelium (superior nasal concha / nasal septum)', 'Olfactory receptor neurons lie in the olfactory epithelium lining the superior part of the nasal cavity, including the superior nasal concha and the upper nasal septum, and not in the bulb, tract or cortex. Their axons pass through the cribriform plate to synapse in the olfactory bulb.'),
('752cf91f-b6b5-5648-9583-55724f16a2a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Cranial Nerves', 'The olfactory nerves pass through which of the following structures?', '["Optic canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Cribriform plate (ethmoid bone) to olfactory bulb","Superior orbital fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen rotundum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foramen magnum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate (ethmoid bone) to olfactory bulb', 'The olfactory nerve filaments pass through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to reach and synapse in the olfactory bulb. The other foramina listed transmit different structures.'),
('16bcedfe-8de8-51d6-a3c3-43e296f61c5d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The diencephalon consists of', '["Thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, subthalamus","Thalamus and hypothalamus only","Cerebral cortex and basal ganglia","Brainstem and cerebellum","Telencephalon and diencephalon"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, subthalamus', 'The diencephalon has four subdivisions: thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus, and subthalamus. The cerebral cortex and basal ganglia belong to the telencephalon, while the brainstem and cerebellum are separate major divisions, so the option listing all four diencephalic components is complete.'),
('44bf749d-d581-5963-adad-756ee2882a89'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus is a relay station for all sensory systems except', '["Audition","Olfaction","Vision","Taste","Somatosensation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfaction', 'Olfaction bypasses the thalamus and passes directly from the olfactory bulb to the primary olfactory (piriform) cortex. Vision, audition, taste, and somatosensation all synapse in specific thalamic nuclei (LGN, MGN, VPM, VPL) before reaching cortex.'),
('25104d77-90e7-53dd-9fc6-444246b4f396'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The internal medullary lamina of thalamus divides the thalamus into', '["Dorsal and ventral","Anterior, medial, and lateral nuclear groups","Rostral and caudal","Medial and lateral only","Superior and inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior, medial, and lateral nuclear groups', 'The Y-shaped internal medullary lamina splits the thalamus into anterior, medial, and lateral nuclear groups. It does not create a dorsal/ventral or rostral/caudal arrangement, which are not the standard groupings produced by this lamina.'),
('ca2389ec-c332-5d28-b666-b764f3dec998'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The external medullary lamina separates', '["Thalamic nuclei from each other resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus from hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus from midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventral from dorsal nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus from the reticular nucleus (and internal capsule)"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus from the reticular nucleus (and internal capsule)', 'The external medullary lamina is a thin white-matter sheet on the lateral surface of the thalamus separating it from the reticular nucleus and internal capsule. It does not separate individual nuclei or the hypothalamus and midbrain.'),
('2f5fbc68-534f-523e-bf81-c16e9d339f4b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The reticular nucleus receives input from', '["Thalamus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem only mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamocortical and corticothalamic collaterals"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamocortical and corticothalamic collaterals', 'The reticular nucleus lies on the path of fibers passing between thalamus and cortex and receives collaterals from both thalamocortical and corticothalamic projections. Because it samples traffic in both directions, input limited to the cortex or any single source would be incomplete.'),
('64be59bc-5ea0-54d0-b702-ffe91c99bc1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Reticular nucleus of thalamus modulates', '["Vision only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor output mediated by specific receptor activation","Gating of sensory information to cortex","Audition only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gating of sensory information to cortex', 'The reticular nucleus provides inhibitory (GABAergic) control that gates and focuses sensory information flowing through the thalamus to the cortex. It is not limited to a single modality such as vision, pain, or audition, nor does it modulate motor output.'),
('431464ed-706a-5b0b-92e7-3f010b29ca01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The intralaminar nuclei of thalamus include', '["VPL and VPM resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulvinar and LP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LGN and MGN mediated by specific receptor activation","Centromedian (CM) and parafascicular (Pf)","Anterior and medial dorsal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Centromedian (CM) and parafascicular (Pf)', 'The intralaminar nuclei lie within the internal medullary lamina and include the centromedian (CM) and parafascicular (Pf) nuclei. VPL/VPM, LGN/MGN, and pulvinar/LP belong to other nuclear groups, not the intralaminar group.'),
('fe9672a1-9112-54d6-ad79-c854b81d11f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The intralaminar nuclei receive input from', '["Only cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia (GPi), reticular formation, spinothalamic tract","Only cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Only spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only brainstem leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basal ganglia (GPi), reticular formation, spinothalamic tract', 'Intralaminar nuclei receive convergent inputs from the basal ganglia (GPi), reticular formation, and spinothalamic tract. Their afferents come from multiple sources rather than any single structure such as the cortex, cerebellum, or cord alone.'),
('0c252286-0afe-53c2-9f22-599ee73ed27a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The intralaminar nuclei project to', '["Only striatum resulting in decreased functional activity","Striatum (caudate/putamen) and widespread cerebral cortex","Only brainstem mediated by specific receptor activation","Only thalamic nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Striatum (caudate/putamen) and widespread cerebral cortex', 'Intralaminar nuclei project diffusely to the striatum (caudate and putamen) and widespread areas of cerebral cortex, supporting roles in arousal and motor integration. Efferents restricted to one target would not reflect this broad projection pattern.'),
('1bc61b4b-34f3-5426-a373-cad6e27c9c40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The anterior thalamic nucleus receives input from', '["Visual system resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Mammillary bodies (mammillothalamic tract) and hippocampus (via fornix)","Sensory pathways leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies (mammillothalamic tract) and hippocampus (via fornix)', 'The anterior nucleus receives afferents from the mammillary bodies through the mammillothalamic tract and from the hippocampus via the fornix. These are limbic inputs, so visual, cerebellar, basal ganglia, or general sensory sources are incorrect.'),
('1959ab2d-19d8-5d6a-91af-493ce8e630bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The anterior thalamic nucleus projects to', '["Cingulate gyrus (cingulum) and limbic cortex","Sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Prefrontal cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cingulate gyrus (cingulum) and limbic cortex', 'The anterior nucleus projects to the cingulate gyrus via the cingulum and to limbic cortex, forming part of the Papez memory circuit. Its efferents are limbic rather than to sensory, visual, prefrontal, or motor cortex.'),
('534b5775-6a3a-5374-a505-8025e7866312'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The anterior thalamic nucleus is part of which circuit?', '["Basal ganglia loop resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papez circuit (mammillary bodies → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal → hippocampus)","Pain pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar loop leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papez circuit (mammillary bodies → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal → hippocampus)', 'The anterior nucleus is a key relay in the Papez circuit (mammillary bodies → anterior nucleus → cingulate gyrus → parahippocampal gyrus → hippocampus), which is concerned with emotion and memory. It does not belong to basal ganglia, visual, pain, or cerebellar loops.'),
('9e46bcf9-ee62-54b8-bbc4-3ff89d9441cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The medial dorsal (MD) nucleus of thalamus projects to', '["Motor cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Somatosensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auditory cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prefrontal cortex (reciprocal connections)"]'::jsonb, 'Prefrontal cortex (reciprocal connections)', 'The medial dorsal (MD) nucleus projects reciprocally to the prefrontal cortex, relaying limbic and basal ganglia information for executive function. It does not project to primary motor or primary sensory cortical areas.'),
('e79dbae8-99fa-5648-99a3-61a804184f5b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'MD nucleus receives input from', '["Cochlea resulting in decreased functional activity","Retina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala, olfactory cortex, basal ganglia (ventral pallidum/SNr)","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amygdala, olfactory cortex, basal ganglia (ventral pallidum/SNr)', 'The MD nucleus receives afferents from the amygdala, olfactory cortex, and basal ganglia (ventral pallidum and substantia nigra pars reticulata), which carry limbic and visceral information. It does not receive direct input from the cochlea, retina, spinal cord, or cerebellum.'),
('69b2446f-4097-5644-8a55-299b1c59b0d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Lesion of medial dorsal nucleus causes', '["Visual loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor deficits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Impaired executive function, working memory, and emotional processing (frontal-like)","Hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired executive function, working memory, and emotional processing (frontal-like)', 'Because the MD nucleus connects the prefrontal cortex with limbic structures, its damage produces frontal-like deficits in executive function, working memory, and emotional processing. It does not cause primary visual, motor, somatosensory, or auditory loss.'),
('9124b952-90f1-5677-a192-3c45fcb9a07a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ventral anterior (VA) nucleus of thalamus receives input from', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Globus pallidus internal (GPi) and substantia nigra reticulata (SNr)","Auditory system mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial lemniscus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinothalamic tract leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Globus pallidus internal (GPi) and substantia nigra reticulata (SNr)', 'The VA nucleus receives motor-related input from the globus pallidus internus (GPi) and substantia nigra pars reticulata (SNr), forming part of the basal ganglia motor loop. Cerebellar and somatosensory inputs target other thalamic nuclei.'),
('c82ee427-debe-5b2b-8373-8cd4ce01617e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'VA nucleus projects to', '["Somatosensory cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary motor cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Premotor and prefrontal cortex (areas 6, 8, 9, 46)"]'::jsonb, 'Premotor and prefrontal cortex (areas 6, 8, 9, 46)', 'The VA nucleus projects to premotor and prefrontal cortex (areas 6, 8, 9, and 46), helping plan and organize movement. It does not project to primary motor or primary sensory cortical areas.'),
('4aeba4dd-28c4-5364-8099-94b8b56f788a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ventral lateral (VL) nucleus of thalamus receives input from', '["Spinothalamic resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum (dentate/interposed) and GPi/SNr","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial lemniscus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum (dentate/interposed) and GPi/SNr', 'The VL nucleus receives its major afferents from the cerebellum (dentate and interposed nuclei) and from the basal ganglia (GPi/SNr), forming the motor relay to cortex. Spinothalamic, medial lemniscal, and retinal inputs serve different thalamic targets.'),
('cd1e8a6c-4c09-50bb-99ab-b25c6672114e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'VL nucleus projects to', '["Prefrontal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatosensory cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary motor cortex (area 4) and premotor (area 6)","Auditory cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor cortex (area 4) and premotor (area 6)', 'The VL nucleus projects to the primary motor cortex (area 4) and premotor cortex (area 6), relaying cerebellar and basal ganglia signals for voluntary movement. It does not target prefrontal, visual, somatosensory, or auditory cortex.'),
('11471e81-9e46-59f8-8ecb-127da2c35e73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus receives', '["Face sensory resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory input accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual input mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Body sensory: medial lemniscus (touch, vibration, proprioception) and spinothalamic (pain, temperature)"]'::jsonb, 'Body sensory: medial lemniscus (touch, vibration, proprioception) and spinothalamic (pain, temperature)', 'The VPL nucleus relays body sensation, receiving the medial lemniscus (touch, vibration, proprioception) and spinothalamic tract (pain, temperature). Face sensation and other modalities have different relays in the thalamus.'),
('9443e5c2-bb13-529d-b993-00b2c00f19b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus receives', '["Visual input resulting in decreased functional activity","Auditory input accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Face sensory: trigeminothalamic (discriminative touch) and taste","Motor input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Body sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Face sensory: trigeminothalamic (discriminative touch) and taste', 'The VPM nucleus relays face sensation carried by trigeminothalamic fibers (discriminative touch) and taste pathways. Visual, auditory, motor, and whole-body sensory inputs reach other thalamic nuclei.'),
('cecd7bd4-2e45-50a7-a2fa-d1721fbd9baa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The VPM projects to', '["Somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus, face area)","Auditory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Prefrontal cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary motor cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus, face area)', 'VPM projects to the face area of the primary somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus, completing the face sensory relay. It does not project to auditory, visual, prefrontal, or motor cortex.'),
('b6e2b30b-6621-56eb-bb2e-9c65131525ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The VPL projects to', '["Visual cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Prefrontal cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus, body area)","Primary motor cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus, body area)', 'VPL projects to the body area of the primary somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus, completing the body sensory relay. Visual, auditory, prefrontal, and motor cortex receive input from other nuclei.'),
('d2cf962a-0e18-5feb-99cf-2af08592cb50'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lateral geniculate body (LGN) is the thalamic relay for', '["Pain","Vision","Motor","Somatosensation","Audition"]'::jsonb, 'Vision', 'The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) is the thalamic relay where retinal fibers (optic tract) synapse before projecting to primary visual cortex via the optic radiation. Pain and somatosensation use VPL/VPM, and audition uses the MGN.'),
('c1fbf2e6-6884-5ee5-b1be-101b86f0fc11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The LGN has how many layers?', '["2 layers resulting in decreased functional activity","6 layers (M, P, koniocellular)","4 layers mediated by specific receptor activation","layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","3 layers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '6 layers (M, P, koniocellular)', 'The human LGN contains six principal layers: magnocellular layers 1-2 (M), parvocellular layers 3-6 (P), plus interspersed koniocellular neurons. Two, three, or four layers do not reflect the standard LGN organization.'),
('a0e13fbe-57ae-57b9-b8e9-7ea2c81bd134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The medial geniculate body (MGN) is the thalamic relay for', '["Vision","Motor","Audition","Pain","Somatosensation"]'::jsonb, 'Audition', 'The medial geniculate nucleus (MGN) is the thalamic relay for hearing, receiving input from the inferior colliculus and projecting via the auditory radiation to primary auditory cortex. Vision is relayed by the LGN and somatosensation/pain by the VPL/VPM.'),
('710c7eb6-0c4f-511f-a98a-b7060cb49e60'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The pulvinar nucleus (posterior thalamus) is involved in', '["Primary somatosensation resulting in decreased functional activity","Primary vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary audition mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual attention, eye movements, and limbic integration","Primary motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual attention, eye movements, and limbic integration', 'The pulvinar is an association nucleus involved in higher-order functions such as visual attention, eye movements, and limbic integration. It does not serve primary somatosensory, visual, auditory, or motor processing.'),
('99018266-0302-547a-969b-f07badbb07ab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Pulvinar receives input from', '["Only retina resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior colliculus, retina, visual cortex, and other sensory cortices","Only basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior colliculus, retina, visual cortex, and other sensory cortices', 'The pulvinar receives convergent inputs from the superior colliculus, retina, visual cortex, and other sensory cortices, consistent with its role in attention and visuospatial processing. Its afferents are multisensory, not from a single source.'),
('d49ee879-8597-56cf-ac36-5157255a4e4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Pulvinar projects to', '["Association cortices (parietal, temporal, occipital, prefrontal)","Primary sensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary visual cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary auditory cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary motor cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Association cortices (parietal, temporal, occipital, prefrontal)', 'The pulvinar projects to widespread association cortices in the parietal, temporal, occipital, and prefrontal lobes, supporting integrative functions. It does not project predominantly to primary sensory or motor cortex.'),
('7c0b1404-7b08-5aef-b195-b2200cb1ad56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lateral posterior (LP) nucleus of thalamus is involved in', '["Pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual attention and sensorimotor integration (together with pulvinar)","Primary vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Audition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor execution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual attention and sensorimotor integration (together with pulvinar)', 'The lateral posterior (LP) nucleus works with the pulvinar in visual attention and sensorimotor integration. It is not involved in pain transmission, primary vision, audition, or direct motor execution.'),
('9e72e6a2-d4fa-57a6-9a33-2e72078c9869'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The parvocellular VPMpc nucleus relays', '["Visual input resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain from face accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Taste (gustatory) from solitary nucleus (nucleus tractus solitarius)","Touch from face without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor commands leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Taste (gustatory) from solitary nucleus (nucleus tractus solitarius)', 'VPMpc (parvocellular part of VPM) relays taste (gustatory) sensation from the solitary nucleus (nucleus tractus solitarius) to the insular taste cortex. Discriminative face touch and face pain are carried by other VPM subdivisions.'),
('b5a20979-d186-5a3d-a895-9cffe76ba671'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamic taste relay is in', '["LGN resulting in decreased functional activity","VPL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VPMpc (medial parvocellular VPM)","MD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MGN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VPMpc (medial parvocellular VPM)', 'The thalamic taste relay is the VPMpc, the medial parvocellular subdivision of VPM, which receives gustatory fibers from the solitary nucleus. LGN, VPL, MD, and MGN relay vision, body sensation, prefrontal, and auditory information respectively.'),
('ca23802b-4be2-5d49-99a4-40dba5abafaa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The suprageniculate and limitans nuclei are part of', '["Anterior nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior nuclear group (PO) of thalamus (multisensory and pain)","Medial nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral geniculate complex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intralaminar nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior nuclear group (PO) of thalamus (multisensory and pain)', 'The suprageniculate and limitans nuclei belong to the posterior nuclear group (PO), which processes multisensory and pain information. They are not part of the anterior, medial, intralaminar, or geniculate nuclear groups.'),
('3a0380bc-2d25-56ec-b7e0-483700530433'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus is located', '["Below the thalamus, forming the floor and lower walls of third ventricle","Above thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inside thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Behind thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral to thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Below the thalamus, forming the floor and lower walls of third ventricle', 'The hypothalamus lies inferior to the thalamus and forms the floor and lower lateral walls of the third ventricle. It is not above, inside, behind, or lateral to the thalamus.'),
('324aa7c4-0815-5775-8036-53d90d6ff55b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus is separated from thalamus by', '["Mammillothalamic tract","Hypothalamic sulcus","Third ventricle","Internal capsule","Pituitary stalk"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic sulcus', 'The hypothalamic sulcus, a groove on the wall of the third ventricle, marks the boundary between the thalamus above and the hypothalamus below. The mammillothalamic tract, internal capsule, and pituitary stalk do not form this separation.'),
('cd8f383b-ccbd-541a-a954-351ebd08c157'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus extends from', '["Pituitary to thalamus","Lamina terminalis (anterior) to mammillary bodies (posterior)","Optic chiasm to midbrain","Third ventricle to lateral ventricle","Pineal gland to mammillary bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina terminalis (anterior) to mammillary bodies (posterior)', 'In the anteroposterior axis the hypothalamus extends from the lamina terminalis at its rostral margin to the mammillary bodies posteriorly. The other pairs of landmarks do not define the sagittal extent of the hypothalamus.'),
('92531a91-3ef3-5925-8490-21243a2eea25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Which is NOT a region of the hypothalamus?', '["Anterior, middle, posterior","Dentate, emboliform, globose","Lateral and medial zones","Preoptic, supraoptic, tuberal, mammillary","Paraventricular, dorsomedial, ventromedial, arcuate"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate, emboliform, globose', 'Dentate, emboliform, and globose are deep cerebellar nuclei, so they are not hypothalamic regions. In contrast, the preoptic, supraoptic, tuberal, and mammillary groups are the standard anteroposterior regions of the hypothalamus, and medial/lateral zones are a well-recognized division.'),
('96c9cfce-806f-5b16-a6a9-78b62bff0432'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The preoptic area of hypothalamus is important for', '["Thermoregulation (cooling center), sexual behavior, and GnRH secretion","Appetite accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sleep mediated by specific receptor activation","Aggression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thirst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thermoregulation (cooling center), sexual behavior, and GnRH secretion', 'The preoptic area acts as a heat-loss/cooling center, participates in sexual behavior, and contains neurons that secrete GnRH. Appetite, thirst, and aggression are governed primarily by other hypothalamic regions and nuclei.'),
('d7bbb2c0-8c43-5112-ac64-6488fdc067cb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) functions as the', '["Circadian rhythm pacemaker (biological clock)","Satiety center accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding center mediated by specific receptor activation","Thirst center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sexual behavior center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circadian rhythm pacemaker (biological clock)', 'The SCN receives light input via the retinohypothalamic tract and serves as the master circadian clock (biological pacemaker). Appetite, satiety, thirst, and sexual behavior are regulated by other hypothalamic centers.'),
('deddc7b5-35fe-52d4-b081-021b83361314'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus is located', '["Below optic chiasm","In the pituitary","Above the optic chiasm (medial preoptic)","Lateral to third ventricle","Dorsal to thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Above the optic chiasm (medial preoptic)', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus lies in the medial preoptic region just above the optic chiasm. It is not located in the pituitary, below the chiasm, lateral to the third ventricle, or dorsal to the thalamus.'),
('559dee79-022c-5857-9c7b-b8b554d93f3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The retinohypothalamic tract goes to', '["Visual cortex","Pretectum","Suprachiasmatic nucleus","Superior colliculus","Lateral geniculate body"]'::jsonb, 'Suprachiasmatic nucleus', 'The retinohypothalamic tract carries photic information directly from the retina to the suprachiasmatic nucleus for circadian entrainment. The visual cortex, pretectum, superior colliculus, and LGN receive retinal input through other pathways.'),
('e50559b5-7b71-5753-a398-412ab4f40a06'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The supraoptic nucleus (SON) produces', '["Dopamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Arginine vasopressin (ADH) and some oxytocin","Oxytocin only mediated by specific receptor activation","CRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TRH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arginine vasopressin (ADH) and some oxytocin', 'The supraoptic nucleus primarily synthesizes arginine vasopressin (ADH), with lesser amounts of oxytocin, which travel to the posterior pituitary for release. Dopamine and the releasing factors (CRH, TRH) are produced by other hypothalamic neurons.'),
('ddb685e9-7512-5764-b5f0-b235fa644979'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The paraventricular nucleus (PVN) produces', '["Only oxytocin resulting in decreased functional activity","Oxytocin (mainly) plus CRH, TRH, ADH","Only TRH mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ADH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin (mainly) plus CRH, TRH, ADH', 'The paraventricular nucleus is the main site of oxytocin production and also synthesizes CRH, TRH, and some vasopressin. The options limiting production to a single hormone omit the PVN''s multiple products.'),
('31312c0b-aaeb-538f-a953-23d3f5e96e07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'ADH and oxytocin are transported to the posterior pituitary via', '["Bloodstream directly","Hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system","Mammillothalamic tract","Third ventricle","Hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract (axonal transport)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract (axonal transport)', 'ADH and oxytocin are carried down the axons of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract for storage and release in the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis). The portal system serves the anterior pituitary, not the posterior lobe.'),
('0c417489-3a1c-51e5-a830-32d8bff84b04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The tuberoinfundibular pathway involves', '["Arcuate nucleus (tuberal) → median eminence (dopamine/PIF)","Supraoptic nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paraventricular nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Preoptic area without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mammillary bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate nucleus (tuberal) → median eminence (dopamine/PIF)', 'The tuberoinfundibular pathway runs from the arcuate (tuberal) nucleus to the median eminence, where dopamine acts as prolactin-inhibiting factor (PIF). The supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei belong to the magnocellular neurosecretory system rather than this pathway.'),
('164f57be-b311-501b-8124-562c3c44ae9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The median eminence is part of the', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary mediated by specific receptor activation","Third ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothalamus (inferior, where releasing factors enter portal system)"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus (inferior, where releasing factors enter portal system)', 'The median eminence is the inferior part of the hypothalamus where releasing and inhibiting factors are secreted into the hypophyseal portal system. It is not part of the midbrain, thalamus, pituitary gland, or ventricular cavity.'),
('33d5afce-0a01-5da2-92e8-13f9c6817f97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Which hypothalamic nucleus controls hunger (feeding center)?', '["Arcuate nucleus","Ventromedial nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus","Lateral hypothalamic area","Supraoptic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral hypothalamic area', 'The lateral hypothalamic area is the classic feeding center; its lesions cause aphagia/anorexia, while stimulation increases appetite. The ventromedial nucleus suppresses feeding (satiety center), and the other nuclei serve different functions.'),
('5cd60cbf-f44d-5896-97bb-569321f1a6bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Which hypothalamic nucleus controls satiety?', '["Ventromedial nucleus (VMH)","Supraoptic nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Lateral hypothalamus","Paraventricular nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Ventromedial nucleus (VMH)', 'The ventromedial nucleus (VMH) is the classic satiety center; its destruction causes hyperphagia and obesity. The lateral hypothalamus promotes feeding, while the supraoptic, arcuate, and paraventricular nuclei have other roles.'),
('78fcfef6-b6d6-5c8b-a3ab-7f3bd68a8c98'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The arcuate nucleus (tuberal) of hypothalamus is involved in', '["Circadian rhythm resulting in decreased functional activity","Thirst accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sleep-wake cycle mediated by specific receptor activation","Energy balance, appetite regulation (NPY, AgRP, POMC neurons)","Thermoregulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Energy balance, appetite regulation (NPY, AgRP, POMC neurons)', 'The arcuate nucleus contains NPY/AgRP (orexigenic) and POMC (anorexigenic) neurons that regulate appetite and energy balance. Circadian rhythm, thirst, sleep-wake, and thermoregulation are controlled by other hypothalamic areas.'),
('3ba80dca-b8c1-507a-ae19-2479a39fcbb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Anterior hypothalamic (preoptic) nucleus is the', '["Heat production center","Sexual center","Thirst center","Feeding center","Heat loss center (cooling)"]'::jsonb, 'Heat loss center (cooling)', 'The anterior (preoptic) hypothalamus is the heat-loss center that promotes cooling through sweating, vasodilation, and reduced heat production. Heat production is instead driven largely by the posterior hypothalamus, while thirst, feeding, and sexual centers are distinct regions.'),
('8ec9b22e-6295-581f-b0fe-9ab331dfcdd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Posterior hypothalamic nucleus is the', '["Feeding center resulting in decreased functional activity","Circadian center accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heat production/conservation center (heating)","Thirst center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cooling center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heat production/conservation center (heating)', 'The posterior hypothalamic nucleus promotes heat conservation and production, making it the heating center. Cooling (heat loss) is governed by the anterior/preoptic hypothalamus, while feeding, thirst, and circadian control reside in other nuclei.'),
('574aed3a-d794-556c-84d6-c7dd406082ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The dorsomedial nucleus of hypothalamus is involved in', '["Reproduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Feeding, circadian, and stress responses","Satiety mediated by specific receptor activation","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thirst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding, circadian, and stress responses', 'The dorsomedial nucleus participates in feeding behavior, circadian rhythms, and stress-related responses. Reproductive behavior is chiefly mediated by the ventromedial and preoptic nuclei, satiety by the ventromedial nucleus, and thirst by the lateral hypothalamic region.'),
('9bab5fbd-48ac-5b46-864e-3eb7f6f598d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ventromedial nucleus of hypothalamus is involved in', '["Satiety, feeding inhibition, and sexual behavior regulation","Aggression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circadian rhythms mediated by specific receptor activation","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thirst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Satiety, feeding inhibition, and sexual behavior regulation', 'The ventromedial nucleus acts as the satiety center, inhibiting feeding, and also participates in regulating sexual behavior; its destruction produces hyperphagia and obesity. Hunger signals arise mainly from the arcuate and lateral hypothalamic regions, while aggressive and circadian behaviors are mediated elsewhere.'),
('411866e3-a3a3-5731-a915-f04d03b5b04c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The posterior hypothalamus can be part of which system?', '["Ascending reticular activating system (arousal)","Sensory system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor system mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual system without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending reticular activating system (arousal)', 'The posterior hypothalamus projects to the cerebral cortex through the ascending reticular activating system and sustains wakefulness and arousal, so its damage causes hypersomnia. Sensory, motor, visual, and auditory impulses do not rely on hypothalamic relays.'),
('889db42f-7bc9-55a7-a318-8d333a13f60a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The mammillary bodies receive input from', '["Hippocampus via fornix","Amygdala","Thalamus","Cortex","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus via fornix', 'The fornix carries hippocampal projections to the mammillary bodies, completing the Papez circuit. These nuclei receive no significant direct input from the amygdala, cerebellum, or cerebral cortex.'),
('3ce82899-974d-5f08-ab1e-28a3587c995b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Mammillary body lesions cause', '["Visual deficits resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor deficits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterograde amnesia (Korsakoff syndrome)","Hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterograde amnesia (Korsakoff syndrome)', 'Loss of the mammillary bodies disrupts the Papez circuit, which is needed for forming new declarative memories, producing anterograde amnesia as seen in Korsakoff syndrome. Visual, motor, sensory, or auditory impairments do not follow mammillary lesions.'),
('5fe5c711-3bb5-5d00-a987-7c7e7112e6dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Korsakoff syndrome (amnesia, confabulation) involves', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Mammillary bodies, medial dorsal thalamus","Basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hippocampus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amygdala only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies, medial dorsal thalamus', 'Chronic thiamine deficiency damages the mammillary bodies and the medial dorsal nucleus of the thalamus, causing amnesia and confabulation in Korsakoff syndrome. Injury confined to the hippocampus or amygdala alone cannot reproduce this clinicopathologic pattern.'),
('5aede5b7-5059-5229-9f9b-96ee5ad38d81'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The posterior commissure contains fibers from', '["Cortical fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic tract fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pretectal nuclei, nucleus of posterior commissure, and superior colliculus","Motor fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pretectal nuclei, nucleus of posterior commissure, and superior colliculus', 'The posterior commissure interconnects the pretectal nuclei, the nucleus of the posterior commissure, and the superior colliculi, coordinating pupillary light reflexes and conjugate vertical gaze. It does not carry cortical, optic-tract, motor, or auditory tract fibers.'),
('2a2c877d-315c-510b-97c1-264e61fde8df'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The habenular nucleus is part of the', '["Epithalamus (bridge between limbic forebrain and midbrain)","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subthalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epithalamus (bridge between limbic forebrain and midbrain)', 'The habenular complex is the principal nuclear component of the epithalamus, relaying signals between the limbic forebrain and the midbrain. It does not belong to the dorsal thalamus, subthalamus, basal ganglia, or hypothalamus.'),
('140d4513-dce0-529a-888c-0a7100de153f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The habenula receives input from', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus mediated by specific receptor activation","Septal nuclei, preoptic area, stria medullaris thalami","Amygdala leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septal nuclei, preoptic area, stria medullaris thalami', 'The stria medullaris thalami carries fibers from the septal nuclei and preoptic area to the habenular nucleus. The cerebellum, spinal cord, and hippocampus do not project to the habenula through this route.'),
('a6bc70da-1f73-570b-91a2-f256eaaf847c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The habenula projects to', '["Spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Interpeduncular nucleus (midbrain) via fasciculus retroflexus","Basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interpeduncular nucleus (midbrain) via fasciculus retroflexus', 'The habenulopeduncular tract (fasciculus retroflexus) connects the habenula to the interpeduncular nucleus in the midbrain. This projection links limbic structures to tegmental centers rather than to the spinal cord, basal ganglia, cortex, or dorsal thalamus.'),
('17fb5585-0937-5e9c-aa62-235d671b98a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lateral habenula is involved in', '["Circadian rhythm resulting in decreased functional activity","Balance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor coordination mediated by specific receptor activation","Regulation of monoamine systems (reward, aversion, pain)","Vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Regulation of monoamine systems (reward, aversion, pain)', 'The lateral habenula modulates dopaminergic, serotonergic, and noradrenergic systems, thereby influencing reward, aversion, and pain. It has no significant role in circadian rhythm, balance, motor coordination, or vision.'),
('d6534500-8205-50d3-b7b2-5e2e11416615'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The medial habenula is involved in', '["Balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor coordination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Audition mediated by specific receptor activation","Vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain processing, stress, and reproductive behavior"]'::jsonb, 'Pain processing, stress, and reproductive behavior', 'The medial habenula participates in pain processing, stress responses, and reproductive behavior through its projections to the interpeduncular nucleus. Balance, motor coordination, audition, and vision are not among its functions.'),
('1add73d2-6f60-5bff-957d-6a85237b5440'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The pineal gland (epiphysis) secretes', '["ADH resulting in decreased functional activity","Dopamine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serotonin mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxytocin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Melatonin (circadian rhythm, sleep)"]'::jsonb, 'Melatonin (circadian rhythm, sleep)', 'Pinealocytes synthesize melatonin from serotonin, and melatonin secretion regulates the circadian rhythm and promotes sleep with peak output at night. ADH, oxytocin, and dopamine are produced by the hypothalamus, not the pineal gland.'),
('8bdce21c-3529-5dd8-8508-3aa05ccf211e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Pineal melatonin is suppressed by', '["Darkness resulting in decreased functional activity","Stress accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Food mediated by specific receptor activation","Exercise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Light (via retinohypothalamic → SCN → superior cervical ganglion → pineal)"]'::jsonb, 'Light (via retinohypothalamic → SCN → superior cervical ganglion → pineal)', 'Light travels from the retina through the retinohypothalamic tract to the suprachiasmatic nucleus, then via the superior cervical ganglion to the pineal gland, suppressing melatonin synthesis. Darkness removes this suppression and allows melatonin to rise.'),
('63c748b4-d131-5798-9d65-dfe02f021baa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Pineal gland calcification (corpora arenacea) is', '["Malignant resulting in decreased functional activity","A normal finding (age-related)","Vascular mediated by specific receptor activation","pathological without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infectious leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A normal finding (age-related)', 'Corpora arenacea (''brain sand'') reflect age-related calcification of the pineal gland and are a benign physiologic finding on imaging. They are not produced by malignancy, infection, vascular disease, or other pathology.'),
('65954a03-d865-57c1-aa9f-04030fa5566f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Pineal germinoma is a common pineal region tumor that may cause', '["Pituitary dysfunction resulting in decreased functional activity","Parinaud syndrome (dorsal midbrain compression)","Diabetes insipidus mediated by specific receptor activation","Seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual field defects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parinaud syndrome (dorsal midbrain compression)', 'Pineal germinomas compress the dorsal midbrain (superior colliculus and pretectal region), producing Parinaud syndrome with upward gaze palsy and light-near dissociation. Pituitary dysfunction, diabetes insipidus, seizures, and visual field defects are not the hallmark pattern.'),
('1a106721-714a-55f4-8dd0-3d5c9511b565'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Pineal region tumors can also cause', '["Only seizures resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstructive hydrocephalus (compression of cerebral aqueduct)","Only hormonal changes mediated by specific receptor activation","Only Parinaud without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only headache leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive hydrocephalus (compression of cerebral aqueduct)', 'A pineal region mass obstructs the cerebral aqueduct, blocking CSF flow and causing obstructive hydrocephalus with headache, papilledema, and vomiting. The dominant complication is aqueductal compression rather than isolated seizures, hormonal changes, or Parinaud syndrome.'),
('a21ca7ae-83cf-5537-ae25-22f54e1446ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The stria medullaris thalami carries fibers from', '["Hippocampus to mammillary bodies","Septal/preoptic areas to habenula","Amygdala to hypothalamus","Mammillary bodies to thalamus","Cortex to thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Septal/preoptic areas to habenula', 'The stria medullaris thalami runs along the roof of the third ventricle, carrying septal and preoptic fibers to the habenular nucleus. It does not connect the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies or the amygdala to the hypothalamus.'),
('431f7dbd-7737-5768-ba58-5926d72fec15'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The subthalamus contains', '["Cerebellar nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia nuclei accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothalamic nuclei mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamic nuclei without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subthalamic nucleus (STN) and zona incerta"]'::jsonb, 'Subthalamic nucleus (STN) and zona incerta', 'The subthalamus lies between the dorsal thalamus and midbrain and contains the subthalamic nucleus, the zona incerta, and the fields of Forel. Cerebellar, hypothalamic, and dorsal thalamic nuclei lie outside this region.'),
('dc346415-577b-5bc8-a41d-3eeaf95016fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The subthalamic nucleus (STN) is part of which system?', '["Cerebellar system resulting in decreased functional activity","Limbic system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual system mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal ganglia (part of indirect pathway)","Sensory system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basal ganglia (part of indirect pathway)', 'The subthalamic nucleus is a basal ganglia nucleus that sends excitatory glutamatergic projections through the indirect pathway, opposing the actions of the pallidum. It forms no part of the cerebellar, limbic, visual, or sensory systems.'),
('649e43f4-5ef6-5675-9848-49cf80ba3b8e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Lesion of subthalamic nucleus causes', '["Parkinsonism resulting in decreased functional activity","Chorea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral hemiballismus (violent flinging movements)","Dystonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ataxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral hemiballismus (violent flinging movements)', 'Loss of the subthalamic nucleus removes excitation that normally dampens unwanted movement, releasing violent flinging of the contralateral limbs (hemiballismus). Parkinsonism, chorea, dystonia, and ataxia follow damage to other sites.'),
('5c1570e2-7b56-5c69-b7d3-cf2bbf79c422'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Hemiballismus is typically caused by', '["Huntington disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Parkinson disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke in subthalamic nucleus","Basal ganglia calcification without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wilson disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke in subthalamic nucleus', 'A small infarct or hemorrhage in the subthalamic nucleus, often related to hypertension or diabetes, is the most common cause of hemiballismus. Huntington disease, Parkinson disease, basal ganglia calcification, and Wilson disease do not typically present this way.'),
('c41c59a2-2e25-5bdf-8cb2-4bcfbca58208'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Fields of Forel (H1, H2) in subthalamus contain', '["Optic tract fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pallidothalamic, cerebellothalamic, and ansa lenticularis fibers","Corticospinal fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Auditory fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pallidothalamic, cerebellothalamic, and ansa lenticularis fibers', 'The fields of Forel (H1 and H2) in the subthalamus carry pallidothalamic, cerebello-thalamic, and ansa lenticularis fibers en route to the dorsal thalamus. They transmit no optic, lemniscal, corticospinal, or auditory tract fibers.'),
('211106c2-0bae-5fd1-a9d2-4e368afa9878'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamic fasciculus (Forel H1) contains', '["Fibers from GPi/SNr to VA/VL thalamus","Medial lemniscus","Optic radiation","Corticospinal fibers","Spinothalamic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Fibers from GPi/SNr to VA/VL thalamus', 'Forel H1 (thalamic fasciculus) lies above the zona incerta and conveys pallidothalamic (GPi) and nigrothalamic (SNr) fibers to the VA and VL nuclei. The medial lemniscus, optic radiation, corticospinal fibers, and spinothalamic tract follow separate paths.'),
('dd06206f-66df-5015-a220-4d9e8ac8bd1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lenticular fasciculus (Forel H2) contains', '["Corticopontine fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corticospinal fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibers from GPi passing over STN to thalamus","Cerebellar fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibers from GPi passing over STN to thalamus', 'Forel H2 (lenticular fasciculus) passes medially above the subthalamic nucleus, carrying GPi fibers toward the dorsal thalamus. Corticopontine, corticospinal, sensory, and cerebellar fibers are not constituents of this bundle.'),
('a3f2174f-6e12-53e1-98ae-a7d8c36190b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ansa lenticularis connects', '["Cerebellum to thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Striatum to pallidum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex to thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Globus pallidus to thalamus (anterior/inferior pathway)","Thalamus to cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Globus pallidus to thalamus (anterior/inferior pathway)', 'The ansa lenticularis is an anterior-inferior pathway running from the globus pallidus internus to the ventral anterior and ventral lateral thalamic nuclei. It is a distinct tract from cerebello-thalamic, striatopallidal, and corticothalamic projections.'),
('77362ca5-8d77-59ab-b26d-e7a6942a8a34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The subthalamic nucleus receives dopaminergic input from', '["Nucleus basalis","Raphe nuclei","Locus coeruleus","Ventral tegmental area","Substantia nigra pars compacta"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia nigra pars compacta', 'The substantia nigra pars compacta provides dopaminergic innervation to the subthalamic nucleus and striatum, facilitating the indirect pathway. The ventral tegmental area targets limbic regions, the raphe nuclei release serotonin, and the locus coeruleus norepinephrine.'),
('d758ceb3-5568-598f-8acb-0f3a109f0e38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The subthalamic nucleus uses which neurotransmitter?', '["Glutamate (excitatory)","Dopamine","Acetylcholine","GABA","Serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate (excitatory)', 'The subthalamic nucleus uses glutamate, making it the main excitatory output of the basal ganglia. Dopamine, acetylcholine, GABA, and serotonin are the transmitters of other structures.'),
('dd2c50ca-7b89-5c5a-bcae-0f51e8bf1e0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Which is the only excitatory nucleus in the basal ganglia?', '["Caudate nucleus","Subthalamic nucleus (STN)","Globus pallidus externus","Substantia nigra reticulata","Globus pallidus internus"]'::jsonb, 'Subthalamic nucleus (STN)', 'The subthalamic nucleus is the only glutamatergic (excitatory) nucleus in the basal ganglia, whereas the striatum, external and internal pallidum, and substantia nigra pars reticulata are GABAergic (inhibitory).'),
('9e44503a-77df-5d76-9a01-2e5715fa7063'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Dejerine-Roussy syndrome (thalamic pain syndrome) occurs after', '["Cerebellar stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamic hemorrhage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain stroke mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortical stroke without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infarction of ventroposterior thalamus (VPL/VPM)"]'::jsonb, 'Infarction of ventroposterior thalamus (VPL/VPM)', 'Dejerine-Roussy syndrome follows infarction of the ventroposterolateral and ventroposteromedial nuclei, destroying the lemniscal and spinothalamic relay and leading to late central pain. Cerebellar, midbrain, and cortical lesions do not produce this syndrome.'),
('9ce197fd-c2e9-5ea3-9d06-f626b80bd200'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Dejerine-Roussy syndrome includes', '["Only motor deficits resulting in decreased functional activity","Only hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral hemianesthesia (transient) followed by spontaneous pain, hyperpathia, and dysesthesias","Only cognitive deficits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visual loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral hemianesthesia (transient) followed by spontaneous pain, hyperpathia, and dysesthesias', 'Dejerine-Roussy begins with transient contralateral hemianesthesia and is followed by delayed spontaneous burning pain, hyperpathia, and dysesthesias on the affected side. Motor, hearing, cognitive, and visual loss are not the characteristic features.'),
('980ed5ea-4b4e-57da-84c5-c99a9aeaac59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic hand in Dejerine-Roussy is characterized by', '["Parkinsonian tremor resulting in decreased functional activity","Claw hand accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Athetosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Dystonic posturing of contralateral hand (flexed wrist, extended fingers)","Hemiballismus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dystonic posturing of contralateral hand (flexed wrist, extended fingers)', 'Thalamic hand is a dystonic posture of the contralateral hand with the wrist and metacarpophalangeal joints flexed and the fingers extended. Parkinsonian tremor, claw hand, athetosis, and hemiballismus differ in mechanism and clinical appearance.'),
('f85b5245-3af0-5097-b8b2-6f3d835b0312'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic aphasia can occur with', '["Any cortical lesion resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilateral thalamic lesion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right thalamic lesion mediated by specific receptor activation","aphasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Left thalamic lesion (anterior/ventrolateral) involving MD or VL"]'::jsonb, 'Left thalamic lesion (anterior/ventrolateral) involving MD or VL', 'Thalamic aphasia follows dominant (left) hemisphere thalamic damage, classically in the anterior/ventrolateral territory involving the medial dorsal or ventrolateral nuclei. Bilateral or right-sided lesions, purely cortical lesions, and an undefined term do not explain this language defect.'),
('53ef412c-dabe-5b02-80d8-3668fc17ad4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The diencephalon derives from which embryonic vesicle?', '["Neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Telencephalon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rhombencephalon mediated by specific receptor activation","Prosencephalon (forebrain) → diencephalon","Mesencephalon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prosencephalon (forebrain) → diencephalon', 'The prosencephalon (forebrain vesicle) divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon. The mesencephalon and rhombencephalon give rise to the midbrain and hindbrain, while the neural crest forms peripheral nervous structures.'),
('de5501c1-0bbf-5d1c-82f0-3c54bc9d9470'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The third ventricle is lined by', '["Choroid plexus","Pia mater","Ependyma (ciliated)","Arachnoid mater","Dura mater"]'::jsonb, 'Ependyma (ciliated)', 'Every brain ventricle, including the third ventricle, is lined by ciliated ependymal cells. The choroid plexus secretes CSF but is not a lining, and the meninges cover the brain surface rather than the ventricular cavity.'),
('412d0ad1-d6bc-5d14-9813-05dcc37dc163'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamic sulcus separates', '["Thalamus from midbrain","Anterior from posterior hypothalamus","Hypothalamus from midbrain","Hypothalamus from pituitary","Thalamus from hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus from hypothalamus', 'The hypothalamic sulcus runs along the lateral wall of the third ventricle, marking the boundary between the thalamus above and the hypothalamus below. It does not separate the hypothalamus from the midbrain, pituitary, or its own subdivisions.'),
('8962163f-0c25-5fdb-ad60-bc7ea2e27952'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The interthalamic adhesion (massa intermedia) connects', '["Two thalami across third ventricle","Thalamus and hypothalamus","Thalamus and midbrain","Thalamus and cortex","Two hypothalami"]'::jsonb, 'Two thalami across third ventricle', 'The interthalamic adhesion (massa intermedia) bridges the two thalami across the third ventricle and is absent in about 20% of brains. It provides no connection between the hypothalamus, midbrain, or cerebral cortex.'),
('d9d04cf9-a438-5aea-a281-4f435f2cf844'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The supraoptic recess of third ventricle is located', '["Above optic chiasm (anterior/inferior third ventricle)","Behind thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Within pituitary mediated by specific receptor activation","Above pineal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Below hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Above optic chiasm (anterior/inferior third ventricle)', 'The supraoptic recess of the third ventricle lies just above the optic chiasm in the anterior-inferior portion of the ventricle. It is not located behind the thalamus, above the pineal, within the pituitary, or below the hypothalamus.'),
('c2f03920-e487-58d7-a5c5-77353ad00ca6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The infundibular recess of third ventricle extends into', '["Pineal gland","Mammillary bodies","Hypothalamus","Thalamus","Pituitary stalk"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary stalk', 'The infundibular recess is a downward extension of the third ventricle that projects into the infundibulum (pituitary stalk) alongside the hypothalamic infundibulum. The pineal recess is the posterior extension into the pineal stalk, a different structure.'),
('da199f08-3781-521f-8b34-1fecbbf21d59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The pineal recess of third ventricle extends into', '["Pituitary stalk","Mammillary region","Pineal stalk","Supraoptic region","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Pineal stalk', 'The pineal (suprapineal) recess projects posteriorly into the pineal stalk between the habenular and posterior commissures. The supraoptic and infundibular recesses are the anterior-inferior extensions near the optic chiasm and pituitary stalk.'),
('dfa7ab35-5187-5562-81c6-e396ca08e017'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The third ventricle communicates with lateral ventricles via', '["Interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro)","Foramen of Magendie accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central canal mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral aqueduct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Foramen of Luschka leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interventricular foramen (foramen of Monro)', 'Each lateral ventricle drains into the third ventricle through the interventricular foramen of Monro. The cerebral aqueduct connects the third to the fourth ventricle, and the foramina of Magendie and Luschka empty the fourth ventricle.'),
('2d87a30e-36a5-5d62-8e1b-ecc46ad055ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The third ventricle communicates with fourth ventricle via', '["Foramina of Luschka","Central canal","Foramen of Magendie","Foramen of Monro","Cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius)"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius)', 'The mesencephalic aqueduct (of Sylvius) runs through the midbrain, connecting the third and fourth ventricles. The foramina of Monro, Magendie, and Luschka and the central canal serve other communications.'),
('91fb982c-b39e-5f1d-afc0-50aeb0fc18de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Choroid plexus in third ventricle produces', '["CSF (along with lateral and fourth ventricle choroid plexus)","Blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hormones mediated by specific receptor activation","Neurotransmitters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endorphins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF (along with lateral and fourth ventricle choroid plexus)', 'The choroid plexus of the third ventricle, together with those of the lateral and fourth ventricles, produces cerebrospinal fluid. It does not manufacture blood, hormones, neurotransmitters, or endorphins.'),
('fedee8cf-7afa-5f1a-a3e5-3810cbdbd69a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The epithalamus includes', '["Subthalamus, zona incerta resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus, hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basal ganglia, amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum, midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pineal gland, habenula, posterior commissure, stria medullaris"]'::jsonb, 'Pineal gland, habenula, posterior commissure, stria medullaris', 'The epithalamus comprises the pineal gland, habenular nuclei, posterior commissure, and stria medullaris thalami. The subthalamus, dorsal thalamus, hypothalamus, amygdala, and basal ganglia are separate diencephalic or telencephalic structures.'),
('f8c2b508-ab66-512b-bce2-046da355d6c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ansa peduncularis is a large bundle of fibers connecting', '["Basal ganglia to thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortex to pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amygdala, hypothalamus, and thalamus (ventral amygdalofugal)","Thalamus to cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum to red nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amygdala, hypothalamus, and thalamus (ventral amygdalofugal)', 'The ansa peduncularis connects the amygdala with the hypothalamus and thalamus, forming part of the ventral amygdalofugal pathway. Basal ganglia-thalamic, corticopontine, thalamocortical, and cerebello-rubral connections are distinct tracts.'),
('2e764f00-8de9-58df-8789-e1072579d76a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The periventricular nucleus of hypothalamus is involved in', '["Sleep only resulting in decreased functional activity","Feeding only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autonomic and neuroendocrine regulation","Thirst only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Circadian only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autonomic and neuroendocrine regulation', 'The periventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus integrates autonomic and neuroendocrine regulation, and its parvocellular neurons release releasing and inhibiting hormones into the portal circulation. It is not confined to sleep, feeding, thirst, or circadian functions.'),
('06c732da-e5b1-5d51-a82c-629e6189b35f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The parvocellular neurosecretory cells of PVN produce', '["GHRH only resulting in decreased functional activity","CRH, TRH, somatostatin, etc. (releasing/inhibiting hormones)","Dopamine only mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxytocin only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ADH only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CRH, TRH, somatostatin, etc. (releasing/inhibiting hormones)', 'Parvocellular neurons of the paraventricular nucleus secrete releasing and inhibiting hormones such as CRH, TRH, and somatostatin into the hypophyseal portal system. ADH, oxytocin, dopamine, and GHRH are released by other neuronal populations.'),
('d6ef8eb9-1da2-5952-ac65-f12a29f2e9f4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The magnocellular neurosecretory cells of SON and PVN produce', '["GnRH resulting in decreased functional activity","Somatostatin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ADH and oxytocin (directly to posterior pituitary)","Dopamine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CRH and TRH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADH and oxytocin (directly to posterior pituitary)', 'Magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei synthesize ADH and oxytocin and carry them directly down their axons to the posterior pituitary. GnRH, somatostatin, dopamine, CRH, and TRH are products of different cell groups.'),
('0fcfc00d-6ade-5961-a514-525a49d00294'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lateral tuberal (tubular) nucleus (LTN/LHA) is involved in', '["Temperature regulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Circadian rhythm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sexual behavior mediated by specific receptor activation","Thirst without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Feeding, arousal, and reward via orexin/hypocretin"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding, arousal, and reward via orexin/hypocretin', 'The lateral tuberal (lateral hypothalamic) nucleus contains orexin/hypocretin neurons that regulate feeding, arousal, and reward. Temperature, circadian, thirst, and sexual centers lie in other hypothalamic nuclei.'),
('94abbc1f-905e-5176-9b1d-7ca1ad18edf9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Orexin (hypocretin) neurons are located in', '["Supraoptic nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Lateral hypothalamus and perifornical area","Paraventricular nucleus","Ventromedial hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral hypothalamus and perifornical area', 'Orexin/hypocretin neurons are concentrated in the lateral hypothalamus and perifornical area and project diffusely to promote wakefulness and feeding; degeneration of these cells produces narcolepsy. The supraoptic, paraventricular, arcuate, and ventromedial nuclei secrete or regulate other hypothalamic peptides rather than orexin.'),
('8d3f6903-4e6d-57bd-89d3-59a32c1c536e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Narcolepsy type 1 is caused by', '["Loss of noradrenaline neurons","Loss of acetylcholine neurons","Loss of dopamine neurons","Loss of serotonin neurons","Loss of orexin-producing neurons in lateral hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of orexin-producing neurons in lateral hypothalamus', 'Narcolepsy type 1 results from loss of orexin-producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus, causing severe orexin deficiency with cataplexy. Degeneration of noradrenergic, cholinergic, dopaminergic, or serotonergic neurons produces different syndromes and does not cause narcolepsy with cataplexy.'),
('f99fbfb5-1a7f-5394-99f9-6edcb0c1fc1a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The melanin-concentrating hormone (MCH) neurons are located in', '["Paraventricular nucleus","Lateral hypothalamus and zona incerta","Ventromedial nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Suprachiasmatic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral hypothalamus and zona incerta', 'Melanin-concentrating hormone neurons lie adjacent to the orexin population in the lateral hypothalamus and zona incerta and favor feeding and sleep. The paraventricular, ventromedial, arcuate, and suprachiasmatic nuclei house different neuroendocrine or circadian populations.'),
('945b4c4c-e29c-5b7d-ab8a-9cf2dcd63bdf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Histaminergic neurons in the hypothalamus are located in', '["Preoptic area resulting in decreased functional activity","Paraventricular nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN, posterior hypothalamus)","Arcuate nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supraoptic nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN, posterior hypothalamus)', 'Histamine-synthesizing cell bodies reside in the tuberomammillary nucleus of the posterior hypothalamus and project widely to sustain wakefulness. The preoptic, paraventricular, arcuate, and supraoptic nuclei each contain different neuronal populations.'),
('d488125e-4eed-5e2f-84db-53f10ad23ac6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Wakefulness is promoted by', '["Adenosine resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","GABA accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Melatonin mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only orexin without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orexin (lateral hypothalamus), histamine (TMN), noradrenaline (LC), serotonin (raphe), ACh (basal forebrain)"]'::jsonb, 'Orexin (lateral hypothalamus), histamine (TMN), noradrenaline (LC), serotonin (raphe), ACh (basal forebrain)', 'Wakefulness depends on several parallel arousal systems, including orexin (lateral hypothalamus), histamine (TMN), noradrenaline (locus coeruleus), serotonin (raphe), and acetylcholine (basal forebrain). Adenosine, GABA, and melatonin promote sleep, and orexin alone represents only one arousal pathway.'),
('e8007d39-659a-59bd-8288-a15d18c90be7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The sleep-promoting center in the anterior hypothalamus is the', '["Paraventricular nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO) (GABAergic, galanin)","Arcuate nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Suprachiasmatic nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tuberomammillary nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO) (GABAergic, galanin)', 'The ventrolateral preoptic nucleus contains GABAergic, galaninergic neurons that inhibit the arousal systems to generate sleep, so VLPO lesions cause insomnia. The other nuclei serve circadian, feeding, and neuroendocrine roles rather than promoting sleep.'),
('656af42a-bac8-5880-83fe-8569da7c87ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Flip-flop switch (sleep-wake transitions) involves reciprocal inhibition between', '["VLPO (sleep) and TMN/LC/raphe (wake)","SCN and pineal","Arcuate and PVN","Anterior and posterior hypothalamus","Lateral and ventromedial hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'VLPO (sleep) and TMN/LC/raphe (wake)', 'The flip-flop switch arises from reciprocal inhibition between the GABAergic VLPO (sleep promoting) and the wake-promoting TMN, locus coeruleus, and raphe nuclei, enabling rapid, stable transitions between the two states. The other listed pairs are not the principal components of this switch.'),
('6b998c08-7435-5831-9c6b-78538024eaf7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The dorsomedial hypothalamic nucleus (DMH) is involved in', '["Only reproduction resulting in decreased functional activity","Only stress accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only circadian mediated by specific receptor activation","Only feeding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Circadian, stress, and feeding integration"]'::jsonb, 'Circadian, stress, and feeding integration', 'The dorsomedial nucleus integrates circadian, stress, and feeding signals and relays them to the SCN, PVN, and other areas to coordinate rhythms, thermogenesis, and glucose handling. Its function is not limited to any single role proposed by the distractors.'),
('1886a5bd-de89-5494-afdf-73e42994b57c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The preoptic area contains a sexually dimorphic nucleus (SDN-POA) which is', '["Same size resulting in decreased functional activity","Larger in males (plays role in male sexual behavior)","Absent in females mediated by specific receptor activation","Larger in females without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absent in males leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Larger in males (plays role in male sexual behavior)', 'The sexually dimorphic preoptic nucleus is several-fold larger in males under the influence of perinatal testosterone and participates in male sexual behavior. It is neither equal in size nor absent in either sex.'),
('73c116c3-3767-5b09-ac57-89eb343c8b38'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus (VMH) is essential for', '["Parenting behavior resulting in decreased functional activity","Feeding only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Male aggression mediated by specific receptor activation","Female lordosis behavior (sexual receptivity)","Female aggression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Female lordosis behavior (sexual receptivity)', 'The ventromedial nucleus is a classic sex-behavior center that supports female lordosis, and it also contributes to satiety. The other listed behaviors are controlled by different hypothalamic regions.'),
('30cb98e5-ee95-50dd-809c-a78f9e63ceb9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The medial preoptic nucleus is involved in', '["Aggression resulting in decreased functional activity","Female sexual behavior accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding mediated by specific receptor activation","Male sexual behavior (copulation, mounting)","Thirst leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Male sexual behavior (copulation, mounting)', 'The medial preoptic nucleus integrates the cues that drive male copulation and mounting under testosterone influence. Aggression, feeding, female sexual behavior, and thirst are regulated by other hypothalamic territories.'),
('d5e41650-f24d-5bce-92d2-4f8ce924473e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus controls the pituitary gland via', '["Only humoral resulting in decreased functional activity","Only direct innervation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only neural mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system (anterior) and hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract (posterior)","Only vascular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal system (anterior) and hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract (posterior)', 'The hypothalamus governs the anterior pituitary through releasing and inhibiting hormones carried by the hypophyseal portal system, and the posterior pituitary through the hypothalamo-hypophyseal nerve tract carrying oxytocin and vasopressin. Each route alone is insufficient, since both are required.'),
('3aa030f6-3781-5d1e-954c-549198707946'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) derives from', '["Rathke pouch (ectoderm of stomodeum/oral cavity)","Neural tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neural crest mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rathke pouch (ectoderm of stomodeum/oral cavity)', 'The adenohypophysis derives from Rathke pouch, an ectodermal outpouching of the oral stomodeum. The neural tube and neural crest form neural tissue, whereas mesoderm and endoderm contribute other structures rather than the anterior lobe.'),
('af723cea-1cce-5977-bb02-f15557894890'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) derives from', '["Neural crest resulting in decreased functional activity","Mesoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Downward growth of neural tube (infundibulum)","Rathke pouch without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endoderm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Downward growth of neural tube (infundibulum)', 'The posterior pituitary forms as a downward evagination (infundibulum) from the floor of the third ventricle, which carries the hypothalamo-hypophyseal axons. Rathke pouch yields the anterior lobe, and the remaining germ-layer choices are not involved.'),
('3cc2e8d2-547b-5b0d-abf2-adf4ed0e360f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Prolactin-inhibiting factor (PIF) is', '["Serotonin resulting in decreased functional activity","Somatostatin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopamine (from arcuate nucleus)","Noradrenaline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine (from arcuate nucleus)', 'Dopamine from arcuate (tuberoinfundibular) neurons enters the portal blood and tonically inhibits prolactin, so it is the physiologic prolactin-inhibiting factor. The other transmitters do not serve as the main prolactin-inhibiting signal.'),
('1a79606a-3ff7-5dfc-aee3-5e53dfc3eba3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Prolactin-releasing factor (PRF) includes', '["Somatostatin resulting in decreased functional activity","GnRH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CRH mediated by specific receptor activation","TRH (thyrotropin-releasing hormone) and VIP","GHRH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TRH (thyrotropin-releasing hormone) and VIP', 'Several peptides, notably TRH and VIP, stimulate prolactin release and therefore act as prolactin-releasing factors. Somatostatin, GnRH, CRH, and GHRH regulate other anterior pituitary hormones rather than stimulating prolactin.'),
('dcac2f64-65ba-515d-b4f7-0b64d0e7afd7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH) is produced in', '["Arcuate nucleus","Periventricular nucleus","Preoptic area","Paraventricular nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate nucleus', 'GHRH-synthesizing neurons are concentrated in the arcuate nucleus and release the hormone into the portal circulation to stimulate somatotrophs. The other nuclei produce different hypothalamic releasing or inhibiting hormones.'),
('25a71ab6-41f8-5924-8f53-bc63acac440a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Somatostatin (growth hormone-inhibiting hormone) is produced in', '["Paraventricular nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Periventricular nucleus (anterior hypothalamus)","Supraoptic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Arcuate nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Periventricular nucleus (anterior hypothalamus)', 'Somatostatinergic neurons that inhibit growth hormone secretion lie chiefly in the periventricular zone of the anterior hypothalamus. The paraventricular, supraoptic, arcuate, and lateral regions synthesize other neuroendocrine factors.'),
('7b2b98fe-a0ef-52c0-9f47-82b1bb2bb8b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) is produced in', '["Supraoptic nucleus","Preoptic area","Arcuate nucleus","Mammillary bodies","Paraventricular nucleus (PVN)"]'::jsonb, 'Paraventricular nucleus (PVN)', 'CRH is produced by parvocellular neurons of the paraventricular nucleus and delivered through the portal system to drive ACTH release. None of the other listed regions is the major source of CRH.'),
('1e57048f-78af-522e-a4ea-0e74136bf61e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) is produced in', '["Arcuate nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus","Periventricular nucleus","Preoptic area"]'::jsonb, 'Paraventricular nucleus', 'TRH-synthesizing neurons are located in the paraventricular nucleus, where they regulate TSH and prolactin secretion through the portal blood. This distinguishes the PVN from the arcuate (GHRH, dopamine), supraoptic (vasopressin), periventricular (somatostatin), and preoptic territories.'),
('0c5a4394-26c4-5d75-b423-d78ea47b3a40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'GnRH is produced by neurons that are located in', '["Lateral hypothalamus","Preoptic area and arcuate nucleus (scattered)","Mammillary bodies","Supraoptic nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Preoptic area and arcuate nucleus (scattered)', 'GnRH cell bodies are scattered through the medial preoptic area and the arcuate nucleus and project to the median eminence. The lateral hypothalamus, mammillary bodies, and supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei are not the primary GnRH sites.'),
('6e23607d-c422-5758-9be0-ced8b0648364'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Kallmann syndrome (anosmia + hypogonadism) is due to', '["Failed migration of GnRH neurons from olfactory placode","LH receptor mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FSH deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","GnRH receptor mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Androgen insensitivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failed migration of GnRH neurons from olfactory placode', 'In Kallmann syndrome, failure of GnRH neurons to migrate from the olfactory placode along the olfactory pathway abolishes GnRH release and causes anosmia. Receptor defects and androgen insensitivity alter hormone action rather than GnRH neuron migration.'),
('c9cae88d-98b1-5de4-b9b1-81a1077bd9d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamic thermostat (thermoregulatory center) is located in', '["Arcuate nucleus","Preoptic area and anterior hypothalamus","Mammillary bodies","Lateral hypothalamus","Posterior hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Preoptic area and anterior hypothalamus', 'The primary thermoregulatory center lies in the preoptic area and anterior hypothalamus, where thermosensitive neurons set the reference temperature and trigger autonomic responses. The other nuclei govern feeding, neuroendocrine, and arousal functions.'),
('5d45f30e-0cc4-5dbe-ab12-835a4bf3a658'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Warm-sensitive neurons are more abundant in the', '["Lateral hypothalamus","Mammillary bodies","Preoptic area (anterior hypothalamus)","Arcuate nucleus","Posterior hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Preoptic area (anterior hypothalamus)', 'Warm-sensitive neurons are most abundant in the preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus, although cold-sensitive neurons also occur there. Other regions contribute less to direct central thermosensing.'),
('2d628557-ff4b-5f64-8451-227b7e15ba74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Fever is caused by', '["Prostaglandin E2 acting on preoptic hypothalamus (raising setpoint)","Cytokines only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased heat production mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased heat loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prostaglandin E2 acting on preoptic hypothalamus (raising setpoint)', 'Fever follows pyrogen-induced prostaglandin E2 synthesis, and PGE2 acts in the preoptic hypothalamus to raise the thermoregulatory set point. Infection, cytokines, or altered heat exchange alone do not cause the set-point resetting.'),
('911123bf-97be-528a-88d4-bb8683705735'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Antipyretics (aspirin, ibuprofen) work by', '["Increasing heat loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) → reduce PGE2 in preoptic area","Blocking pyrogen production mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreasing heat production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking IL-1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX) → reduce PGE2 in preoptic area', 'Antipyretics such as aspirin and ibuprofen inhibit cyclooxygenase, reducing PGE2 production in the preoptic area and thereby restoring the temperature set point. They do not act by blocking pyrogens or IL-1, nor by directly altering heat loss or production.'),
('50099dbe-8409-5b71-886b-793e329abf52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thirst center is located in', '["Mammillary bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Circumventricular organs (subfornical organ, OVLT) and preoptic hypothalamus","Ventromedial hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral hypothalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circumventricular organs (subfornical organ, OVLT) and preoptic hypothalamus', 'Thirst is coordinated by the circumventricular organs, notably the subfornical organ and OVLT, together with the preoptic hypothalamus, which detect plasma osmolality and angiotensin II. The other nuclei are not the primary thirst centers.'),
('71dcfebb-168e-5552-989d-ff2dda3ef2e6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Osmoreceptors for thirst and ADH release are in', '["Mammillary bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Subfornical organ, OVLT, and preoptic (circumventricular organs with no BBB)","Arcuate nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Paraventricular nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supraoptic nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subfornical organ, OVLT, and preoptic (circumventricular organs with no BBB)', 'Osmoreceptors for thirst and ADH sit in circumventricular organs with fenestrated capillaries and no blood-brain barrier, including the subfornical organ, OVLT, and preoptic region, where plasma osmolality is sensed. Other brain regions cannot access these signals directly.'),
('0c3665c8-94f0-598b-b54c-0023eb496b0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Angiotensin II acts on the hypothalamus via', '["Direct action on SON resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subfornical organ (circumventricular) to stimulate thirst and ADH release","Direct action on PVN without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventromedial hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subfornical organ (circumventricular) to stimulate thirst and ADH release', 'Angiotensin II acts through the subfornical organ, a circumventricular structure without a blood-brain barrier, to stimulate thirst and ADH release. It does not act directly on the supraoptic or paraventricular nuclei.'),
('9dfb92b3-b980-59a1-92cf-16f2ad1650a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'ADH (vasopressin) increases water reabsorption in the kidney by acting on', '["V3 receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Adrenal cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loop of Henle mediated by specific receptor activation","V2 receptors in collecting duct (inserts aquaporin-2)","V1 receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'V2 receptors in collecting duct (inserts aquaporin-2)', 'ADH binds V2 receptors on principal cells of the collecting duct, which inserts aquaporin-2 channels and increases water reabsorption. V1 and V3 receptors mediate other actions, and the adrenal cortex and loop of Henle are not the V2-expressing target.'),
('69decbcf-9ddc-5904-af2d-89dbfb693061'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Oxytocin functions include', '["Stress response resulting in decreased functional activity","Uterine contraction (parturition) and milk ejection (lactation)","Water reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Sodium retention without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood pressure increase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uterine contraction (parturition) and milk ejection (lactation)', 'Oxytocin contracts uterine smooth muscle during parturition and promotes milk ejection by contracting myoepithelial cells during lactation. Stress regulation, water handling, sodium, and blood pressure do not define its physiologic role in this context.'),
('e03cdd6d-11f5-5756-b31d-6ab36d082f7f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Oxytocin release is triggered by', '["Suckling and cervical dilation (neuroendocrine reflex)","Pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased blood volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased osmolality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stress leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suckling and cervical dilation (neuroendocrine reflex)', 'Oxytocin is secreted through a neuroendocrine reflex triggered by nipple suckling and by cervical or uterine distention at birth. Pain, reduced blood volume, rising osmolality, and stress primarily regulate other hormones rather than oxytocin.'),
('de2c28d2-4557-5f6f-9921-35f3592ca800'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus regulates feeding via', '["Only NPY resulting in decreased functional activity","Only POMC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only lateral hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Arcuate nucleus NPY/AgRP (orexigenic) and POMC/CART (anorexigenic) neurons","Only VMH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate nucleus NPY/AgRP (orexigenic) and POMC/CART (anorexigenic) neurons', 'Feeding is controlled by arcuate nucleus neurons, with NPY/AgRP stimulating appetite and POMC/CART suppressing it, integrated with other hypothalamic regions. No single peptide or nucleus is sufficient by itself.'),
('e38f17ae-f273-50e7-8a7e-d812eb2eb657'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Leptin acts on the arcuate nucleus to', '["Have effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibit NPY/AgRP and activate POMC/CART neurons (decrease appetite)","Decrease POMC/CART mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase NPY/AgRP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase appetite leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibit NPY/AgRP and activate POMC/CART neurons (decrease appetite)', 'Leptin signals satiety by inhibiting the orexigenic NPY/AgRP neurons and activating the anorexigenic POMC/CART neurons in the arcuate nucleus, decreasing appetite. The opposite pattern would increase food intake.'),
('14d5448b-bb66-55bc-91fb-554c621a6efa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Ghrelin (hunger hormone) acts on', '["VMH resulting in decreased functional activity","Paraventricular nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","POMC neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral hypothalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arcuate nucleus (activates NPY/AgRP, increases appetite)"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate nucleus (activates NPY/AgRP, increases appetite)', 'Ghrelin, the hunger hormone, activates arcuate NPY/AgRP neurons to increase appetite and food intake. The VMH, paraventricular nucleus, POMC neurons, and lateral hypothalamus are not the principal target of its orexigenic action.'),
('ad892740-ff5a-54d7-bc1e-44026c13a747'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Agouti-related peptide (AgRP) is an', '["Adrenal hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","Anorexigenic peptide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid hormone mediated by specific receptor activation","Orexigenic (appetite-stimulating) peptide from arcuate nucleus","Satiety factor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Orexigenic (appetite-stimulating) peptide from arcuate nucleus', 'Agouti-related peptide is an orexigenic peptide co-expressed by arcuate NPY neurons that stimulates hunger by antagonizing MC3/MC4 receptors. It is neither an adrenal or thyroid hormone nor an appetite-suppressing factor.'),
('82124258-b5e5-5be5-95b9-7b6d984d2508'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Alpha-MSH (melanocyte-stimulating hormone) from POMC is', '["Sleep promoting resulting in decreased functional activity","Inactive accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anorexigenic (appetite-suppressing) via MC4 receptor","Orexigenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain modulating leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anorexigenic (appetite-suppressing) via MC4 receptor', 'Alpha-MSH derived from POMC activates MC4 receptors in the hypothalamus to suppress appetite, making it anorexigenic. The other listed effects do not describe its role in energy regulation.'),
('ad2510dd-bac7-52b0-88d2-737eb4a8f834'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'MC4 receptor mutation is the most common cause of', '["Monogenic obesity","Type 2 diabetes","Growth disorders","Thyroid disease","Adrenal disease"]'::jsonb, 'Monogenic obesity', 'MC4 receptor mutations are the most common identifiable cause of monogenic obesity because they interrupt the anorexigenic alpha-MSH signal. The other listed conditions are not the defining monogenic outcome.'),
('04533f7c-5a8d-597a-9185-74845558dd07'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Proopiomelanocortin (POMC) neurons in the arcuate nucleus produce', '["α-MSH, ACTH, β-endorphin (cleavage products)","CRH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NPY and AgRP mediated by specific receptor activation","MCH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orexin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'α-MSH, ACTH, β-endorphin (cleavage products)', 'Arcuate POMC neurons cleave their precursor into alpha-MSH, ACTH, and beta-endorphin. NPY and AgRP come from a separate arcuate population, while CRH, MCH, and orexin are synthesized by other neurons.'),
('46896e5a-391e-5f1e-b21f-d8351b05fa31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The endocannabinoid system in the hypothalamus (CB1 receptors) functions to', '["Suppresses feeding","feeding effect","Promotes sleep","Promotes wakefulness","Promotes feeding (orexigenic)"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes feeding (orexigenic)', 'Endocannabinoids acting on hypothalamic CB1 receptors stimulate appetite and promote feeding, which explains the hunger induced by cannabis. They do not suppress feeding, and their effect is not primarily on sleep or wakefulness.'),
('77207fdd-b551-5662-ac17-f9b1a70870ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Insulin acts on the hypothalamus to', '["Increase food intake resulting in decreased functional activity","Decrease food intake (anorexigenic, like leptin)","Increase blood glucose mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease metabolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease food intake (anorexigenic, like leptin)', 'Insulin, like leptin, signals energy status to the arcuate nucleus and decreases food intake, acting as an anorexigenic signal. The other choices describe actions that oppose the satiety effect.'),
('b066530e-8caa-5891-8fe4-e0939658af5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus regulates body temperature by', '["Only sweating resulting in decreased functional activity","Modulating autonomic (vasomotor, sweating, shivering) and behavioral responses","Only behavioral changes mediated by specific receptor activation","Only shivering without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only vasodilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modulating autonomic (vasomotor, sweating, shivering) and behavioral responses', 'Temperature homeostasis depends on coordinated autonomic responses, including vasomotor changes, sweating, and shivering, together with behavioral adjustments orchestrated by the hypothalamus. Relying on any single effector would be inadequate.'),
('d793c032-5eea-572c-bd01-6f02ac36cc3e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The preoptic area detects body temperature via', '["Only spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Only skin temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Warm and cold-sensitive neurons (also peripheral input)","Only from blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only core temperature leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Warm and cold-sensitive neurons (also peripheral input)', 'The preoptic area contains both warm- and cold-sensitive neurons that sample core temperature and integrate peripheral input from the skin and spinal cord. It does not depend on blood, skin, cord, or a single source alone.'),
('9dad7a47-73f1-59d2-8f89-d264090c2dc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Cooling responses coordinated by the anterior hypothalamus include', '["Piloerection resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid activation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vasoconstriction and shivering mediated by specific receptor activation","Heat seeking without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vasodilation and sweating (sympathetic cholinergic)"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation and sweating (sympathetic cholinergic)', 'When core temperature rises, the anterior hypothalamus triggers heat-dissipating responses of cutaneous vasodilation and sweating, the latter through sympathetic cholinergic fibers. Piloerection, thyroid activation, vasoconstriction, and shivering conserve or produce heat instead.'),
('d09c8964-b07d-5f2d-9ca0-ce99d3a0d731'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Heating responses coordinated by the posterior hypothalamus include', '["Relaxation resulting in decreased functional activity","Sleep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","responses mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasodilation and sweating without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vasoconstriction, shivering, and non-shivering thermogenesis (sympathetic)"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction, shivering, and non-shivering thermogenesis (sympathetic)', 'When core temperature falls, the posterior hypothalamus coordinates heat-conserving and heat-producing responses, namely vasoconstriction, shivering, and non-shivering thermogenesis, all sympathetic-driven. Vasodilation and sweating act in the opposite direction.'),
('78b5381d-ab01-5237-b371-3980afdb7e6f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Non-shivering thermogenesis in brown adipose tissue is mediated by', '["Muscle contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Shivering accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroid hormone only mediated by specific receptor activation","Exercise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic activation via β3-adrenergic receptors (uncoupling protein 1)"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic activation via β3-adrenergic receptors (uncoupling protein 1)', 'Non-shivering thermogenesis in brown fat follows sympathetic activation of beta3-adrenergic receptors, which raise mitochondrial uncoupling protein 1 so energy is dissipated as heat. Shivering, exercise, and thyroid hormone act through separate mechanisms.'),
('e1f53cce-9812-5798-8dda-51e1a583a200'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus regulates circadian rhythms via', '["Mammillary bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Arcuate nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supraoptic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Paraventricular nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) → pineal melatonin"]'::jsonb, 'Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) → pineal melatonin', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus is the master circadian clock and drives rhythmic pineal melatonin secretion through the PVN and superior cervical ganglion. The other nuclei do not set the circadian rhythm.'),
('fc5e3f2b-f6f4-5a46-8119-211117020f76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'SCN neurons exhibit intrinsic rhythmicity driven by', '["Feeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Clock genes (CLOCK, BMAL1, PER, CRY)","Exercise mediated by specific receptor activation","External light without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temperature leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clock genes (CLOCK, BMAL1, PER, CRY)', 'SCN neurons sustain a roughly 24-hour cycle through a transcriptional feedback loop of clock genes, including CLOCK, BMAL1, PER, and CRY. Light, temperature, exercise, and feeding can entrain the clock but are not the intrinsic source of its rhythm.'),
('ff486251-1ed0-576b-a7b7-92ecad6013b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Light resets the SCN clock via', '["Melatonin resulting in decreased functional activity","Retinohypothalamic tract (glutamate/PACAP from melanopsin retinal ganglion cells)","Serotonin mediated by specific receptor activation","Orexin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopamine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Retinohypothalamic tract (glutamate/PACAP from melanopsin retinal ganglion cells)', 'Light resets the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract, whose intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells contain melanopsin and release glutamate and PACAP. Melatonin acts indirectly, and serotonin, orexin, and dopamine are not the light-entrainment signal.'),
('7524d157-8739-546f-80bd-54eaf1b0114d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Non-image-forming visual functions mediated by melanopsin cells include', '["Image formation resulting in decreased functional activity","Pupillary light reflex and circadian entrainment","Visual acuity mediated by specific receptor activation","Motion detection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Color vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pupillary light reflex and circadian entrainment', 'Melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells drive non-image-forming functions, namely circadian entrainment and the pupillary light reflex. Image formation, acuity, motion detection, and color vision depend on rods and cones feeding the primary visual pathway.'),
('e5d77177-9a39-5d54-a061-9eca84456ff1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus projects to', '["Subparaventricular zone → dorsomedial hypothalamus → PVN and other targets","Directly to thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Directly to cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Directly to pineal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Directly to pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subparaventricular zone → dorsomedial hypothalamus → PVN and other targets', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus, the circadian clock, relays timing signals through the subparaventricular zone and dorsomedial hypothalamus to the paraventricular nucleus and other targets, driving hormonal and autonomic rhythms. It does not project directly to the pineal, cortex, or pituitary; melatonin secretion is regulated indirectly through this multi-synaptic chain.'),
('97f6a898-3c57-5147-b660-7efdf65bf5e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus controls the autonomic nervous system via', '["Only sympathetic resulting in decreased functional activity","Descending projections (dorsal longitudinal fasciculus, MFB, PAG) to brainstem and spinal cord","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","autonomic control without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct spinal projections leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Descending projections (dorsal longitudinal fasciculus, MFB, PAG) to brainstem and spinal cord', 'Descending fibers in the dorsal longitudinal fasciculus, medial forebrain bundle, and through the periaqueductal gray relay hypothalamic autonomic drive to brainstem and spinal preganglionic neurons. This allows coordinated sympathetic and parasympathetic output rather than one division alone.'),
('c1a1ced9-cbf4-5a96-897a-0622b9b606cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Stimulation of the lateral hypothalamus can produce', '["Parasympathetic activation resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic activation (increased BP, HR, alertness, feeding)","Hypotension mediated by specific receptor activation","Bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sleep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic activation (increased BP, HR, alertness, feeding)', 'The lateral hypothalamus is a major sympathetic-facilitating zone; its stimulation raises blood pressure and heart rate, heightens alertness, and drives feeding behavior. Hypotension, bradycardia, and sleep reflect parasympathetic or other hypothalamic influences.'),
('ac26878f-4588-564f-b314-738089126079'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Stimulation of the anterior hypothalamus produces', '["Parasympathetic responses (slowed heart, vasodilation, urination, GI activity)","Aggression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fight or flight mediated by specific receptor activation","Feeding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic responses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic responses (slowed heart, vasodilation, urination, GI activity)', 'The anterior (preoptic) hypothalamus mediates parasympathetic responses such as bradycardia, vasodilation, urination, and increased gastrointestinal activity. Sympathetic fight-or-flight reactions and feeding behavior come from stimulation of other hypothalamic regions.'),
('b21f0f22-f549-501e-b4a0-a7017d87858a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Stimulation of the posterior hypothalamus produces', '["Decreased arousal resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic responses (increased BP/HR, mydriasis, piloerection)","Parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vasodilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic responses (increased BP/HR, mydriasis, piloerection)', 'The posterior hypothalamus integrates sympathetic responses, producing tachycardia, hypertension, pupillary dilatation, and piloerection. Parasympathetic effects, vasodilation, and sleep are not produced by posterior hypothalamic stimulation.'),
('b0beac98-2691-58e8-a502-e787d2eb0b7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The defense reaction (fight or flight) is integrated by', '["Only brainstem resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothalamus (medial preoptic, anterior, ventromedial, periaqueductal gray)","Only amygdala mediated by specific receptor activation","Only cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus (medial preoptic, anterior, ventromedial, periaqueductal gray)', 'The medial preoptic, anterior, and ventromedial hypothalamus together with the periaqueductal gray integrate the defense reaction, coordinating its endocrine, autonomic, and behavioral components. The amygdala and brainstem participate, but the hypothalamus is the principal integrator.'),
('28d5ca92-2740-5868-b555-1ca198cd5db9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Rage behavior results from', '["Lesion of cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Activation of basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Removal of cortical inhibition over hypothalamus (decorticate rage: sham rage)","Lesion of thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulation of cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Removal of cortical inhibition over hypothalamus (decorticate rage: sham rage)', 'Decorticate or sham rage follows removal of cortical inhibition over the hypothalamus, releasing unchecked aggressive behavior. Cerebellar, basal ganglia, and thalamic lesions do not produce this pattern.'),
('cc0236ce-e4b4-56d6-bdbd-a5a8ec332eb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The amygdala modulates hypothalamic functions via', '["Fornix resulting in decreased functional activity","Mammillothalamic tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cingulum mediated by specific receptor activation","Stria terminalis and ventral amygdalofugal pathway","Direct projections to pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stria terminalis and ventral amygdalofugal pathway', 'The amygdala reaches the hypothalamus through the stria terminalis and the ventral amygdalofugal pathway, carrying emotional and behavioral influences. The fornix, mammillothalamic tract, and cingulum are separate limbic tracts with other connections.'),
('bf240a3b-cede-5726-9245-c6025f76adb0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus is involved in stress responses via', '["Only cortisol resulting in decreased functional activity","Only SNS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only ACTH mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CRH (PVN) → ACTH (pituitary) → cortisol (adrenal) axis"]'::jsonb, 'CRH (PVN) → ACTH (pituitary) → cortisol (adrenal) axis', 'Stress triggers CRH release from paraventricular nucleus neurons, which stimulates pituitary ACTH and then adrenal cortisol secretion. The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis links all three components rather than any single hormone alone.'),
('2cb77838-0631-571a-b478-2fc0663efd25'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Chronic activation of HPA axis (hypothalamus) contributes to', '["Euphoria resulting in decreased functional activity","Weight loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypertension only mediated by specific receptor activation","Diabetes only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depression, anxiety, metabolic syndrome, immunosuppression"]'::jsonb, 'Depression, anxiety, metabolic syndrome, immunosuppression', 'Sustained cortisol excess impairs mood (depression and anxiety), promotes central obesity and metabolic syndrome, and suppresses immune function. These complications reflect long-term effects of the axis and do not spare any of these systems.'),
('ad4e15af-91d2-55a5-ad98-d76f33d870c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Cortisol feedback inhibits the HPA axis at', '["Only pituitary resulting in decreased functional activity","Only adrenal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pituitary (ACTH) and hypothalamus (CRH) — negative feedback","feedback leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary (ACTH) and hypothalamus (CRH) — negative feedback', 'Cortisol inhibits both pituitary ACTH and hypothalamic CRH release, forming classic negative feedback on the axis. It acts at these two sites rather than on the adrenal gland itself.'),
('81a0238b-634f-558a-aead-6098e08f8dca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus also regulates the thyroid axis via', '["TRH → TSH → T3/T4 (negative feedback by T3)","Direct thyroid stimulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only TSH mediated by specific receptor activation","Only iodine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only TRH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TRH → TSH → T3/T4 (negative feedback by T3)', 'Hypothalamic TRH stimulates pituitary TSH, which drives thyroid production of T3 and T4; circulating T3 then feeds back negatively on TRH and TSH secretion. Regulation involves the entire chain rather than a single component.'),
('06fe7644-2c4c-5eb5-9eeb-378976963d27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus regulates appetite via which orexigenic peptides?', '["NPY, AgRP, orexin, MCH, ghrelin","ADH, oxytocin","Leptin, insulin, α-MSH, CART","GHRH, somatostatin","CRH, TRH"]'::jsonb, 'NPY, AgRP, orexin, MCH, ghrelin', 'NPY, AgRP, orexin, MCH, and ghrelin each stimulate appetite and promote weight gain, acting mainly on arcuate and lateral hypothalamic neurons. Leptin, insulin, alpha-MSH, and CART are anorexigenic, while ADH, oxytocin, GHRH, and TRH have different roles.'),
('322de8d1-2a8e-5f0e-9a04-1338d2f1820b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Anorexigenic peptides in hypothalamus include', '["ADH, oxytocin","Leptin, insulin, α-MSH, CART, GLP-1","CRH, TRH","Ghrelin, endocannabinoids","NPY, AgRP, orexin, MCH"]'::jsonb, 'Leptin, insulin, α-MSH, CART, GLP-1', 'Leptin, insulin, alpha-MSH, CART, and GLP-1 reduce food intake and increase energy expenditure. Ghrelin, NPY, AgRP, orexin, and MCH are orexigenic, and ADH and oxytocin are posterior pituitary hormones unrelated to appetite.'),
('fd0b4400-d8f6-5ed6-bcb2-e6204dce808e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The N. accumbens (ventral striatum) receives hypothalamic input for', '["Motor control only resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory processing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reward and motivation (feeding, sex, addiction)"]'::jsonb, 'Reward and motivation (feeding, sex, addiction)', 'The nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) integrates hypothalamic and mesolimbic dopamine signals to drive reward and motivation for feeding, sex, and addictive behavior. It does not subserve sensory, visual, auditory, or purely motor processing.'),
('e8890c60-d5a4-50af-8dcd-875f080d2d6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Ventral tegmental area (VTA) dopamine neurons are modulated by', '["Cortical input only resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibition only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GABA only mediated by specific receptor activation","Orexin (lateral hypothalamus, excitatory) and other hypothalamic inputs","modulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Orexin (lateral hypothalamus, excitatory) and other hypothalamic inputs', 'Orexin neurons of the lateral hypothalamus excite VTA dopamine neurons, enhancing reward-seeking and arousal, while other hypothalamic projections also modulate them. Control is a networked influence, not isolated cortical, inhibitory, or GABA-only regulation.'),
('01377ef1-e863-56a1-ad86-aa5b137839a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The posterior hypothalamic area is part of the', '["Auditory system resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual system mediated by specific receptor activation","Ascending reticular activating system (wakefulness)","Sensory system leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending reticular activating system (wakefulness)', 'The posterior hypothalamus contributes to the ascending reticular activating system, promoting wakefulness and arousal; damage here causes somnolence. It does not participate in auditory, visual, or motor processing.'),
('d5301421-94c0-5c17-8f54-5a101e145a9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Lesion of the hypothalamus can cause', '["Only sleep disturbance resulting in decreased functional activity","Only obesity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only diabetes insipidus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only temperature dysregulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diabetes insipidus (ADH deficiency), hyperphagia/obesity, or aphagia/weight loss, hypothermia/hyperthermia"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes insipidus (ADH deficiency), hyperphagia/obesity, or aphagia/weight loss, hypothermia/hyperthermia', 'Hypothalamic damage produces diverse deficits: ADH loss causes diabetes insipidus, ventromedial lesions cause hyperphagia and obesity, lateral lesions cause aphagia, and anterior or posterior damage disturbs temperature control. The possible findings exceed any single symptom.'),
('0a88ddd1-c64a-537b-bfb7-76b04515971f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Central diabetes insipidus is caused by', '["Diabetes mellitus resulting in decreased functional activity","Kidney resistance to ADH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ADH deficiency from SON/PVN damage (hypothalamus or pituitary stalk)","Adrenal insufficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excess water intake leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADH deficiency from SON/PVN damage (hypothalamus or pituitary stalk)', 'Central diabetes insipidus follows damage to the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei or the pituitary stalk, causing ADH deficiency. Kidney resistance to ADH produces nephrogenic diabetes insipidus instead, and the other listed causes do not deplete ADH.'),
('3b289288-efba-512a-a08c-b204b2e3e3af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Symptoms of central diabetes insipidus include', '["Hematuria resulting in decreased functional activity","Oliguria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proteinuria mediated by specific receptor activation","Glycosuria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Polyuria, polydipsia, dilute urine (low specific gravity)"]'::jsonb, 'Polyuria, polydipsia, dilute urine (low specific gravity)', 'Loss of ADH prevents water reabsorption, producing large volumes of dilute urine and compensatory polydipsia. Hematuria, proteinuria, oliguria, and glycosuria are not features of central diabetes insipidus.'),
('7c85233b-1f06-5fd6-8d9c-bef587ec7079'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The subfornical organ (SFO) is a', '["Endocrine gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory organ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Limbic structure mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor structure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Circumventricular organ (detects blood osmolality/angiotensin II, no BBB)"]'::jsonb, 'Circumventricular organ (detects blood osmolality/angiotensin II, no BBB)', 'The subfornical organ is a circumventricular organ without a blood-brain barrier that samples blood osmolality and angiotensin II to drive thirst and ADH release. It is not a classic endocrine gland or a sensory, limbic, or motor structure.'),
('d3f5f35b-fcb4-5350-b8ff-5421075571f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Which circumventricular organ is located in the lamina terminalis?', '["Pineal gland resulting in decreased functional activity","OVLT (organum vasculosum of lamina terminalis)","Area postrema mediated by specific receptor activation","Subfornical organ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Median eminence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'OVLT (organum vasculosum of lamina terminalis)', 'The organum vasculosum of the lamina terminalis (OVLT) lies in the anterior wall of the third ventricle and participates in osmoreception and thirst. The area postrema, subfornical organ, and median eminence occupy different locations.'),
('42160b7f-9263-5d4d-91da-14d3d9217237'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'CVOs (circumventricular organs) lack a BBB and include', '["Only choroid plexus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pituitary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SFO, OVLT, area postrema, median eminence, pineal, subcommissural organ","All brain regions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SFO, OVLT, area postrema, median eminence, pineal, subcommissural organ', 'True circumventricular organs with fenestrated capillaries and no blood-brain barrier are the subfornical organ, OVLT, area postrema, median eminence, pineal gland, and subcommissural organ. They are specific sites, not the choroid plexus alone or the whole brain.'),
('755468fe-c4f3-5cbf-8ac2-39d35c202b1f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The subcommissural organ (SCO) secretes', '["ADH resulting in decreased functional activity","Melatonin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CRH mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxytocin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycoproteins into CSF (may guide CSF flow)"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoproteins into CSF (may guide CSF flow)', 'The subcommissural organ secretes glycoproteins into the cerebrospinal fluid that may assist CSF flow. It does not produce ADH, oxytocin, CRH, or melatonin, which come from other hypothalamic and pineal structures.'),
('22a67dc4-dc14-54cc-ba6f-bc5ddf3a0fc3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The organum vasculosum of lamina terminalis (OVLT) is involved in', '["Motor control resulting in decreased functional activity","Circadian rhythm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Audition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Osmoreception, fever response (site of PGE2 action for fever)"]'::jsonb, 'Osmoreception, fever response (site of PGE2 action for fever)', 'The OVLT detects plasma osmolality for thirst and water balance and is the site where circulating prostaglandin E2 acts to raise the set point in fever. Its roles are regulatory, not motor, visual, auditory, or circadian.'),
('b2377419-c842-5f43-918a-3b3a84cc5d65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamic-raphe connection modulates', '["Only appetite resulting in decreased functional activity","Only temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor function mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain via descending inhibition (PAG → raphe → spinal cord)","Only sleep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain via descending inhibition (PAG → raphe → spinal cord)', 'The hypothalamus engages the periaqueductal gray, which activates raphe-spinal serotonergic pathways that suppress nociceptive transmission in the dorsal horn. This descending inhibition underlies endogenous pain control rather than appetite, temperature, sleep, or motor function.'),
('e7622f48-6679-5b0d-a896-cfcc64c1c5ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN) is the sole source of', '["Histamine in the brain","Acetylcholine","Serotonin","Noradrenaline","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine in the brain', 'The tuberomammillary nucleus of the posterior hypothalamus is the only neuronal source of histamine in the brain and promotes wakefulness. Acetylcholine, serotonin, noradrenaline, and dopamine are produced by other nuclei.'),
('aff9ab22-a094-5cac-9d87-953e8066bd66'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Antihistamines cause drowsiness by blocking', '["H3 receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Dopamine receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serotonin receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","H1 receptors in the brain (especially tuberomammillary)","H2 receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H1 receptors in the brain (especially tuberomammillary)', 'Antihistamines that cross the blood-brain barrier block H1 receptors on tuberomammillary histamine neurons, reducing wakefulness and causing sedation. H2 receptors in the gut and peripheral H1 receptors are not responsible for drowsiness.'),
('81695aab-d60b-5393-a56a-6df92949a0c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Wake-promoting drugs like modafinil act via', '["GABA agonism resulting in decreased functional activity","Serotonin agonism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Histamine blockade mediated by specific receptor activation","Acetylcholine inhibition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopamine transporter inhibition (and orexin/hypocretin pathways)"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine transporter inhibition (and orexin/hypocretin pathways)', 'Modafinil promotes wakefulness mainly by inhibiting the dopamine transporter, raising synaptic dopamine, with additional effects on orexin/hypocretin pathways. GABA agonism, serotonin agonism, histamine blockade, and acetylcholine inhibition use different mechanisms.'),
('a8fd86e4-5bc7-5397-b157-e9f002f5c827'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Sodium oxybate (GHB) is used for', '["Depression resulting in decreased functional activity","Daytime sleepiness only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Narcolepsy with cataplexy (improves nighttime sleep and reduces cataplexy)","Anxiety without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Insomnia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Narcolepsy with cataplexy (improves nighttime sleep and reduces cataplexy)', 'Sodium oxybate (GHB), a form of gamma-hydroxybutyrate, consolidates nighttime sleep and markedly reduces cataplexy in narcolepsy. It is not a standard treatment for depression, anxiety, or routine insomnia.'),
('55a0339f-4bd1-598a-8263-23071ba5b4ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamic pituitary adrenal (HPA) axis stress response is', '["TRH → TSH → T3 resulting in decreased functional activity","GnRH → FSH/LH → sex steroids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GHRH → GH → IGF-1 mediated by specific receptor activation","CRH → ACTH → Cortisol (essential for survival but chronic = harmful)","CRH → ADH → Aldosterone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CRH → ACTH → Cortisol (essential for survival but chronic = harmful)', 'The stress response runs through CRH from the paraventricular nucleus, pituitary ACTH, and adrenal cortisol—vital in acute situations but damaging when chronically elevated. The other listed chains are separate hypothalamic-pituitary axes.'),
('67466008-41d0-5e8e-97fd-9058c9dd1848'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Prolactin secretion is tonically inhibited by', '["Noradrenaline resulting in decreased functional activity","Acetylcholine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Histamine mediated by specific receptor activation","Serotonin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopamine from tuberoinfundibular (arcuate) pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine from tuberoinfundibular (arcuate) pathway', 'Dopamine from the tuberoinfundibular (arcuate) pathway travels through the portal system to pituitary lactotrophs, where it tonically inhibits prolactin secretion. None of the other neurotransmitters carries out this inhibitory role.'),
('35e92977-7f9c-5861-9b51-991dc1955880'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Hyperprolactinemia can cause', '["Only gynecomastia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only galactorrhea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only amenorrhea mediated by specific receptor activation","Galactorrhea, amenorrhea, infertility, hypogonadism (in both sexes)","Only infertility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Galactorrhea, amenorrhea, infertility, hypogonadism (in both sexes)', 'Elevated prolactin suppresses gonadotropin secretion, causing galactorrhea, menstrual disturbance, and infertility, with hypogonadism in both sexes. The presentation combines these features rather than any single one.'),
('25d9463f-0d99-59fc-8caa-3eed4806b4f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Dopamine agonists (bromocriptine, cabergoline) treat', '["Hyperprolactinemia (and prolactinomas)","Parkinson disease only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Essential tremor mediated by specific receptor activation","Depression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Schizophrenia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia (and prolactinomas)', 'Bromocriptine and cabergoline, acting as D2 receptor agonists, suppress prolactin secretion and shrink prolactinomas. Their role here is endocrine; the other conditions are not their primary indications for this purpose.'),
('b934e6e3-8d75-56e2-9fee-72e15728bbc6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Prolactinomas are the most common pituitary adenomas; they can cause', '["Only Cushing resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperprolactinemia, visual field defects (bitemporal hemianopia) if large","Only diabetes insipidus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","symptoms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia, visual field defects (bitemporal hemianopia) if large', 'Prolactinomas cause hyperprolactinemia and, when large enough to compress the optic chiasm, bitemporal hemianopia. They do not cause Cushing syndrome or diabetes insipidus, which reflect other sellar pathology.'),
('a7d32acb-8603-514a-a841-f8cddef5eb18'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus influences the immune system via', '["Only via cortisol resulting in decreased functional activity","Only via SNS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct innervation of immune organs mediated by specific receptor activation","influence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HPA axis (cortisol) and SNS (noradrenaline) modulation of immune cells"]'::jsonb, 'HPA axis (cortisol) and SNS (noradrenaline) modulation of immune cells', 'The hypothalamus tunes immunity through the HPA axis, where cortisol suppresses inflammation, and through sympathetic noradrenergic modulation of immune cells. Both arms act together rather than direct innervation alone.'),
('dcf879de-9216-54c8-a438-8655f6759748'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The median eminence (hypothalamus) is vascularized by', '["Basilar artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior hypophyseal arteries (from internal carotid) forming primary portal plexus","Middle cerebral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior cerebral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior communicating artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior hypophyseal arteries (from internal carotid) forming primary portal plexus', 'Superior hypophyseal arteries arising from the internal carotid supply the median eminence and form the primary capillary plexus of the portal system. The other listed vessels do not perfuse this region.'),
('836b5448-4197-5c11-8765-1fefc03afa9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Hypothalamic releasing hormones reach the anterior pituitary via', '["Systemic circulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Axonal transport accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct contact mediated by specific receptor activation","CSF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Long portal veins (median eminence → pars distalis)"]'::jsonb, 'Long portal veins (median eminence → pars distalis)', 'Hypothalamic releasing hormones secreted at the median eminence travel through long portal veins to reach the pars distalis. They do not use the systemic circulation, CSF, or axonal transport to reach the anterior lobe.'),
('77c787a9-6377-58f8-ab8b-01586d026680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The posterior pituitary receives its blood supply from', '["Internal carotid arteries","Middle cerebral artery","Anterior communicating artery","Inferior hypophyseal arteries (direct, not portal)","Superior hypophyseal arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior hypophyseal arteries (direct, not portal)', 'The posterior pituitary is supplied directly by inferior hypophyseal arteries without a portal system. Superior hypophyseal arteries instead feed the anterior lobe portal plexus.'),
('8ab08281-19fb-55b0-9ca3-9d783f2af3a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Hypothalamic lesions in the region of the median eminence can cause', '["Only ACTH deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Only TSH deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Panhypopituitarism (loss of all anterior pituitary hormones)","Only GH deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ADH deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Panhypopituitarism (loss of all anterior pituitary hormones)', 'Destruction of the median eminence interrupts portal delivery of all releasing and inhibiting hormones, causing loss of every anterior pituitary hormone. The deficit is global rather than limited to a single hormone.'),
('e493d1ed-808e-56eb-8b52-8f16f8397723'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Sheehan syndrome (postpartum pituitary necrosis) is due to', '["Ischemia of enlarged pituitary during childbirth hemorrhage","Hypothalamic damage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Autoimmune without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ischemia of enlarged pituitary during childbirth hemorrhage', 'Postpartum hemorrhage causes hypovolemic ischemia of the physiologically enlarged pituitary gland, leading to necrosis and hypopituitarism. Tumor, infection, autoimmunity, and hypothalamic damage act through other mechanisms.'),
('50328311-1c1f-5ca0-a37a-5d48ccd713ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The mammillary bodies receive input from and project to which circuit?', '["Basal ganglia loop resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar loop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papez circuit (hippocampus → fornix → mammillary → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal)","Motor cortex loop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Visual pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Papez circuit (hippocampus → fornix → mammillary → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal)', 'The mammillary bodies lie in the Papez circuit: the hippocampus projects via the fornix to the mammillary bodies, then the mammillothalamic tract reaches the anterior thalamus, cingulate gyrus, and back to the parahippocampal gyrus. This is a limbic circuit, not a motor, cerebellar, or visual pathway.'),
('a59bf619-1590-5316-aad4-20f702301a4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Wernicke encephalopathy (alcoholism, thiamine deficiency) affects', '["Only cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Only mammillary bodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Only thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mammillary bodies, periaqueductal gray, thalamus (MD), brainstem"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies, periaqueductal gray, thalamus (MD), brainstem', 'Thiamine deficiency in Wernicke encephalopathy symmetrically damages the mammillary bodies, periaqueductal gray, mediodorsal thalamus, and periventricular brainstem regions. The injury is multifocal rather than confined to a single structure.'),
('cf803a21-096f-5d6c-a0d9-4c559bfbad34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Wernicke encephalopathy presents with', '["Only ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Only confusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only ophthalmoplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","Confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia (nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy)","Only memory loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia (nystagmus, lateral rectus palsy)', 'Wernicke encephalopathy classically presents with the triad of confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia, including nystagmus and lateral rectus palsy from abducens involvement. The features occur together rather than in isolation.'),
('bbf8bec1-0de4-5431-8c6a-4b6315662104'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Korsakoff psychosis (amnestic syndrome) results from', '["Basal ganglia damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Thiamine deficiency (chronic Wernicke) → mammillary body and MD thalamic damage","Cortical atrophy mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala damage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hippocampal damage only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine deficiency (chronic Wernicke) → mammillary body and MD thalamic damage', 'Korsakoff psychosis is the chronic amnestic sequel of Wernicke encephalopathy, caused by persistent thiamine deficiency injuring the mammillary bodies and mediodorsal thalamus. It is not due to cortical atrophy or isolated hippocampal or amygdala damage.'),
('5b6601c1-2108-5069-8ebe-ca661847473c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Pituitary infundibulum (stalk) lesions can cause', '["Hyperprolactinemia (loss of dopamine inhibition), diabetes insipidus, hypopituitarism","Only hypopituitarism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only obesity mediated by specific receptor activation","Only hyperprolactinemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only DI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperprolactinemia (loss of dopamine inhibition), diabetes insipidus, hypopituitarism', 'Stalk lesions interrupt dopamine (raising prolactin), ADH transport (causing diabetes insipidus), and portal delivery of releasing hormones (causing hypopituitarism), so all three consequences appear together.'),
('dad58bee-bd44-577f-a763-5bf735d167b5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus receives visceral sensory input from the nucleus of solitary tract (NTS) via', '["Parabrachial nucleus (pons) to hypothalamus","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct vagal input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parabrachial nucleus (pons) to hypothalamus', 'Visceral afferents synapse in the nucleus of the solitary tract and ascend through the parabrachial nucleus of the pons to reach the hypothalamus, relaying metabolic and cardiovascular information. The other routes do not carry this visceral signal.'),
('fdb4dcee-b89f-532d-bc6d-6b96827aa247'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus receives gustatory (taste) input from', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve directly resulting in decreased functional activity","NTS → parabrachial → hypothalamus (and VPMpc → insula)","Cochlear nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve directly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NTS → parabrachial → hypothalamus (and VPMpc → insula)', 'Taste fibers converge on the nucleus of the solitary tract, which projects through the parabrachial nucleus to the hypothalamus, while a parallel pathway reaches the insula via VPMpc. There is no direct glossopharyngeal or facial nerve connection to the hypothalamus.'),
('6c7dfbb5-a6ec-50e8-838f-4ca600707435'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus sends orexin projections to', '["All monoamine nuclei (LC, raphe, TMN, VTA) and cortex for arousal","Only LC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Only TMN without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only raphe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All monoamine nuclei (LC, raphe, TMN, VTA) and cortex for arousal', 'Orexin neurons innervate the locus coeruleus, raphe, tuberomammillary nucleus, VTA, and cerebral cortex, coordinating arousal across all monoamine systems. Their output is widespread rather than limited to a single nucleus.'),
('63752843-0c02-5252-80f7-e67da7194e85'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus plays a key role in addiction via', '["Serotonin only resulting in decreased functional activity","GABA only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glutamate only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dopamine only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Orexin (reward seeking, relapse) and MCH (reward) in mesolimbic pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Orexin (reward seeking, relapse) and MCH (reward) in mesolimbic pathway', 'Orexin drives reward-seeking and relapse while MCH contributes to reward processing, both acting through the mesolimbic pathway to influence addiction. The regulation involves multiple hypothalamic peptides rather than a single transmitter.'),
('80b612f3-b3cd-5bf1-b016-63ccfdea99ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lateral hypothalamic area (LHA) contributes to arousal and feeding via', '["GABA (inhibition)","Histamine (wakefulness only)","Serotonin (sleep only)","Dopamine (feeding only)","Orexin/hypocretin (wakefulness, feeding, reward)"]'::jsonb, 'Orexin/hypocretin (wakefulness, feeding, reward)', 'Lateral hypothalamic neurons synthesize orexin/hypocretin, which promotes wakefulness, feeding, and reward-seeking. GABA, histamine, and serotonin do not combine these functions, and dopamine is not the primary LHA arousal peptide.'),
('f1dbed43-eb00-57f5-97be-026d129701b0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Orexin receptor antagonists (suvorexant) are used for', '["Anxiety resulting in decreased functional activity","Depression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Narcolepsy mediated by specific receptor activation","Insomnia (blocks orexin wake-promoting, promotes sleep)","Obesity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Insomnia (blocks orexin wake-promoting, promotes sleep)', 'Suvorexant blocks orexin signaling at OX1/OX2 receptors, reducing wake-promoting drive and facilitating sleep onset, so it is used for insomnia. Narcolepsy is instead treated by promoting wakefulness, not by blocking orexin.'),
('4ac966d4-4270-5058-8021-7f0273aa9695'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus regulates CSF composition via', '["Only SFO resulting in decreased functional activity","Choroid plexus (in third ventricle) and median eminence (but no direct CSF role)","Only pineal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CVOs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only area postrema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (in third ventricle) and median eminence (but no direct CSF role)', 'The choroid plexus lining the third ventricle secretes CSF whose composition is modulated by hypothalamic influences, while the median eminence is a circumventricular site of neuroendocrine release. The SFO, pineal, and area postrema do not serve as the chief mediators of CSF regulation.'),
('a7ab7e4e-6fbb-561c-8bbd-ca5164791f80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) projects to the pineal gland via', '["PVN → intermediolateral column (T1-T2) → superior cervical ganglion → pineal (multisynaptic)","Vagus nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct SCN-pineal tract mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PVN → intermediolateral column (T1-T2) → superior cervical ganglion → pineal (multisynaptic)', 'The SCN signals the pineal indirectly through a multisynaptic chain: PVN, then sympathetic preganglionics in the intermediolateral cell column (T1-T2), then the superior cervical ganglion. There is no direct SCN-to-pineal or vagal route, and the optic nerve only relays light information to the SCN.'),
('2f9a495f-7a9d-5167-9264-ec65e1b91352'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The pineal hormone melatonin is synthesized from', '["Dopamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Serotonin (by N-acetyltransferase and HIOMT)","Histamine mediated by specific receptor activation","Tryptophan without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin (by N-acetyltransferase and HIOMT)', 'Melatonin is produced from serotonin: serotonin is acetylated by N-acetyltransferase and then methylated by HIOMT (hydroxyindole-O-methyltransferase). Dopamine, histamine, and GABA are not precursors in melatonin synthesis, although tryptophan is the ultimate amino acid source.'),
('9459688c-dae3-50ce-b08a-b7d173bc7a11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Melatonin levels peak during', '["Daytime resulting in decreased functional activity","Morning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Evening mediated by specific receptor activation","Night (darkness, 2-4 AM)","Afternoon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Night (darkness, 2-4 AM)', 'Darkness stimulates pineal N-acetyltransferase activity, so melatonin secretion rises at night and peaks around 2-4 AM. Light suppresses this synthesis, explaining the low daytime levels.'),
('f137f1f9-c82d-5f99-8426-fa34825b72e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Melatonin is used for', '["Hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Circadian rhythm disorders (jet lag, shift work), sleep disorders","Depression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anxiety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circadian rhythm disorders (jet lag, shift work), sleep disorders', 'Exogenous melatonin acts on circadian timing to help reset the sleep-wake cycle in jet lag and shift work and to treat certain sleep disorders. It has no established role in treating hypertension, diabetes, depression, or anxiety.'),
('2a83e4c0-40f8-50b1-ac92-0a0bba54d5c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Agnatine is a neuromodulator in hypothalamus involved in', '["Only stress resulting in decreased functional activity","Sleep only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only memory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Analgesia, neuroprotection, and glucose regulation"]'::jsonb, 'Analgesia, neuroprotection, and glucose regulation', 'Agmatine, a metabolite of arginine, acts as a neuromodulator in the hypothalamus with roles in analgesia, neuroprotection, and glucose homeostasis. Its effects are broad and not limited to stress, sleep, feeding, or memory alone.'),
('d60933dd-e549-5b13-a01e-97a155e57520'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus contains neurons that produce', '["hormones resulting in decreased functional activity","Only hormones for posterior pituitary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary releasing/inhibiting hormones (TRH, CRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, dopamine)","Only inhibiting hormones without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only releasing hormones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary releasing/inhibiting hormones (TRH, CRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, dopamine)', 'Hypothalamic parvocellular neurons synthesize both releasing hormones (TRH, CRH, GnRH, GHRH) and inhibiting hormones (somatostatin, dopamine), which reach the anterior pituitary via the portal system. The response must include this full range rather than only one category.'),
('8f1a27be-7ce4-536f-b68c-1bf5469e3714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus is involved in love/attachment via', '["Dopamine only resulting in decreased functional activity","Estrogen only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Testosterone only mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxytocin and vasopressin (pair bonding, parental care, trust)","Serotonin only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin and vasopressin (pair bonding, parental care, trust)', 'Hypothalamic magnocellular neurons release oxytocin and vasopressin, which promote pair bonding, parental care, and trust. Dopamine contributes to reward, but neither dopamine nor the gonadal steroids act alone in mediating attachment.'),
('cfa77987-9a52-5144-9851-3f5f36833fc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus responds to emotional stress via', '["CRH → ACTH → Cortisol; also SNS activation (fight/flight)","Only HPA axis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only behavioral changes mediated by specific receptor activation","Only SNS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","stress response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CRH → ACTH → Cortisol; also SNS activation (fight/flight)', 'Stress activates PVN neurons that release CRH, driving ACTH and cortisol, while simultaneous sympathetic activation produces the fight-or-flight response. The response involves both neuroendocrine and autonomic limbs, not just one component.'),
('36e21d8a-133c-511f-a449-e802b35672e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The paraventricular nucleus (PVN) contains what types of neurons?', '["Only parvocellular resulting in decreased functional activity","Only oxytocin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Magnocellular (oxytocin/ADH), parvocellular (CRH/TRH), and preautonomic (to brainstem/spinal cord)","Only magnocellular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only preautonomic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Magnocellular (oxytocin/ADH), parvocellular (CRH/TRH), and preautonomic (to brainstem/spinal cord)', 'The PVN is heterogeneous, containing magnocellular neurons that release oxytocin and ADH into the posterior pituitary, parvocellular neurons producing CRH and TRH, and preautonomic neurons projecting to the brainstem and spinal cord. No single cell type accounts for its full function.'),
('78253be9-dd64-575a-9b51-8585f278b436'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Preautonomic neurons in PVN project to', '["Only to cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Only to DMV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intermediolateral column (sympathetic), DMV and NTS (parasympathetic)","Only to IML without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only to pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediolateral column (sympathetic), DMV and NTS (parasympathetic)', 'PVN preautonomic neurons descend to sympathetic preganglionics in the intermediolateral cell column and to parasympathetic centers such as the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus (DMV) and nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS). They do not target the cortex or pituitary directly.'),
('1246f0d4-027a-5d2d-9135-00a626bcde88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'PVN stimulation causes', '["Only HPA resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sleep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only feeding mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic activation (increased BP, HR, glucose) and HPA activation (cortisol)","Only parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic activation (increased BP, HR, glucose) and HPA activation (cortisol)', 'Stimulating the PVN drives preautonomic neurons to increase sympathetic output (raising blood pressure, heart rate, and glucose) and activates CRH neurons to raise cortisol via the HPA axis. The response encompasses both autonomic and endocrine effects.'),
('12df2b70-8363-589a-b754-bb4c77047528'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Arcuate nucleus POMC neurons are activated by', '["Leptin, insulin (anorexigenic)","Ghrelin (orexigenic) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stress mediated by specific receptor activation","Low glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cold leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leptin, insulin (anorexigenic)', 'Leptin and insulin activate arcuate POMC neurons, which release α-MSH to suppress appetite and increase energy expenditure (anorexigenic). Ghrelin and low glucose instead stimulate the opposing orexigenic NPY/AgRP neurons.'),
('2827f040-33a3-5b17-95f8-1995fc3028ca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Arcuate nucleus NPY/AgRP neurons are activated by', '["High glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Cold accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Leptin, insulin mediated by specific receptor activation","Exercise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ghrelin, low glucose, low leptin (orexigenic)"]'::jsonb, 'Ghrelin, low glucose, low leptin (orexigenic)', 'Ghrelin, low glucose, and low leptin signal energy deficit and activate NPY/AgRP neurons, which stimulate appetite (orexigenic). Leptin and insulin, by contrast, inhibit this pathway and favor POMC activity.'),
('d6c74e68-39c1-5a1e-909b-f20d99fe64ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The melanocortin-4 receptor (MC4R) is expressed in', '["Adipose tissue resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","PVN, lateral hypothalamus (anorexigenic: inhibits feeding when bound by α-MSH)","Arcuate nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PVN, lateral hypothalamus (anorexigenic: inhibits feeding when bound by α-MSH)', 'MC4R is expressed on second-order neurons in the PVN and lateral hypothalamus; when α-MSH from POMC neurons binds it, feeding is inhibited. The arcuate nucleus synthesizes the ligand rather than being the main MC4R-bearing site, and adipose tissue, muscle, and stomach lack this receptor.'),
('a5b7373e-2084-592a-9af1-e8e94c0d76f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'MC4R agonists are potential treatments for', '["Diabetes","Hypertension","Obesity (reduce appetite)","Depression","Cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity (reduce appetite)', 'Activating MC4R mimics α-MSH signaling and suppresses appetite, so agonists such as setmelanotide are used to treat obesity. Diabetes, hypertension, depression, and cancer are not the primary target indications for MC4R agonism.'),
('153e9871-b013-5249-9131-56861f99b82f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid (HPT) axis involves', '["CRH → ACTH → cortisol resulting in decreased functional activity","TRH → T4 → TSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TRH (PVN) → TSH (pituitary) → T4/T3 (thyroid) → negative feedback","GHRH → GH → IGF-1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GnRH → FSH → estrogen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TRH (PVN) → TSH (pituitary) → T4/T3 (thyroid) → negative feedback', 'PVN neurons release TRH, which stimulates pituitary TSH, which drives thyroid T4/T3 production; rising thyroid hormones then feed back negatively to restrain TRH and TSH. The chains in the other options belong to the HPA, somatotropic, or gonadal axes.'),
('6a037ec5-8b3e-5d0c-83bf-6c8abd95f939'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Hypothalamic hypothyroidism (tertiary) is due to', '["Thyroid gland failure","TSH receptor mutation","Pituitary TSH deficiency","TRH deficiency (hypothalamic lesion)","Iodine deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'TRH deficiency (hypothalamic lesion)', 'Tertiary (hypothalamic) hypothyroidism results from hypothalamic pathology causing deficient TRH, with low TSH despite an intact pituitary and thyroid gland. Primary thyroid failure and pituitary TSH deficiency represent the other levels of the axis.'),
('058053b8-149b-596a-87b9-a7afd2d9f7a9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus regulates growth via', '["Only GHRH resulting in decreased functional activity","Only GH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only IGF-1 mediated by specific receptor activation","GHRH (arcuate) and somatostatin (periventricular) balance → GH → IGF-1","Only somatostatin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GHRH (arcuate) and somatostatin (periventricular) balance → GH → IGF-1', 'Arcuate GHRH stimulates and periventricular somatostatin inhibits pituitary GH release; GH then promotes hepatic IGF-1 production. Growth control requires the balance between the two hypothalamic peptides plus the downstream GH-IGF-1 response.'),
('267d62b1-c25f-53f7-b9df-8b548a79cbb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Growth hormone releasing hormone (GHRH) is also known as', '["Growth hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","IGF-1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ghrelin mediated by specific receptor activation","Growth hormone-releasing factor (GRF, GRH)","Somatostatin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Growth hormone-releasing factor (GRF, GRH)', 'GHRH is synonymous with growth hormone-releasing factor, abbreviated GRF or GRH. Ghrelin is a separate GH secretagogue, and somatostatin inhibits GH release rather than being the same peptide.'),
('0e7562e3-45a5-5f2b-9676-962d85b33ae3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Growth hormone (GH) deficiency due to hypothalamic dysfunction is treated with', '["GnRH resulting in decreased functional activity","GHRH analogs (e.g., sermorelin) or GH itself","TRH mediated by specific receptor activation","CRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GHRH analogs (e.g., sermorelin) or GH itself', 'When GH deficiency results from inadequate hypothalamic GHRH, therapy uses GHRH analogs such as sermorelin to stimulate the pituitary, or recombinant GH itself. GnRH, TRH, CRH, and ACTH do not raise growth hormone.'),
('3d91140a-cf08-5aa5-96e4-ebd6fb264179'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Kallmann syndrome features include', '["Anosmia (absent olfactory bulbs) and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (GnRH deficiency)","Only infertility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Both mediated by specific receptor activation","Only anosmia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only hypogonadism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anosmia (absent olfactory bulbs) and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (GnRH deficiency)', 'Kallmann syndrome combines anosmia from failure of olfactory bulb development with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism due to defective GnRH neuronal migration. The syndrome requires both features rather than anosmia or hypogonadism alone.'),
('def59c72-eebe-553c-91ea-e7ab67098f7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus modulates pain via connections with', '["PAG (midbrain) → descending pain modulation (raphe-spinal)","Cortex only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus only mediated by specific receptor activation","pain modulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct spinal projection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PAG (midbrain) → descending pain modulation (raphe-spinal)', 'Hypothalamic projections reach the periaqueductal gray (PAG), which activates descending raphe-spinal pathways that inhibit nociceptive transmission in the dorsal horn. The modulation flows through this brainstem circuit and is not solely cortical, thalamic, or a direct spinal projection.'),
('523804bb-2e6c-52ad-ad1d-9f76703a70a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus influences immune function via', '["Only SNS resulting in decreased functional activity","Sympathetic innervation (SNS → lymphoid organs) and HPA axis (cortisol)","Direct innervation of lymphocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","influence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cortisol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic innervation (SNS → lymphoid organs) and HPA axis (cortisol)', 'Hypothalamic outputs modulate immunity through sympathetic fibers that innervate lymphoid organs and through HPA-driven cortisol, which suppresses inflammatory responses. Lymphocytes are not directly innervated, and the effect involves both arms rather than SNS or cortisol alone.'),
('2b6e3530-b283-5e5f-902f-a7de0208353a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Hypothalamic dysfunction can cause', '["Only temperature dysregulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only obesity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothermia, hyperthermia, obesity, emaciation, diabetes insipidus, sleep disorders, behavioral changes","Only endocrine disorders without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sleep disorders leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothermia, hyperthermia, obesity, emaciation, diabetes insipidus, sleep disorders, behavioral changes', 'Because the hypothalamus coordinates temperature, appetite, fluid balance, sleep, and behavior, its dysfunction produces a broad constellation including hypo/hyperthermia, weight changes, diabetes insipidus, and sleep-behavioral disturbances. No single symptom category captures the full picture.'),
('8ca52427-1321-510a-bcf7-d6c72821dde8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus is also called the', '["Brain stem resulting in decreased functional activity","Master gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Little brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head ganglion of the autonomic nervous system (control center for homeostasis)"]'::jsonb, 'Head ganglion of the autonomic nervous system (control center for homeostasis)', 'The hypothalamus orchestrates sympathetic and parasympathetic outflow and maintains homeostasis, earning its title as the head ganglion of the autonomic nervous system. The pituitary is the master gland, and the cerebellum is the little brain; these do not apply.'),
('b576dd6c-7a90-57a0-80cd-3b5665982fd2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus receives input from the retina via', '["Pretectal nucleus","Retinohypothalamic tract (direct to SCN)","Optic nerve to LGN","Optic tract to superior colliculus","Visual cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Retinohypothalamic tract (direct to SCN)', 'Melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells project directly to the suprachiasmatic nucleus via the retinohypothalamic tract, entraining circadian rhythms to the light-dark cycle. Pathways to the pretectum, LGN, and superior colliculus serve pupillary and visual reflexes rather than the hypothalamus.'),
('5c17ce56-cbac-5d09-9618-3848d4b41ac2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hippocampus indirectly influences hypothalamus via', '["Fornix → mammillary bodies → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal (Papez circuit)","connection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Via cortex only mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct projections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amygdala → hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fornix → mammillary bodies → anterior thalamus → cingulate → parahippocampal (Papez circuit)', 'Hippocampal output travels through the fornix to the mammillary bodies and onward within the Papez circuit, linking memory processing to limbic and diencephalic centers. The influence is indirect and multi-synaptic rather than a single direct projection.'),
('41521d24-919a-5f31-995e-c1367fa179c9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The hypothalamus is also involved in memory via', '["Direct memory storage","Amygdala only","Hippocampus only","role","Mammillary bodies (Papez circuit)"]'::jsonb, 'Mammillary bodies (Papez circuit)', 'The mammillary bodies are a hypothalamic relay in the Papez circuit; lesions here cause anterograde amnesia, as seen in Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The hypothalamus does not store memories directly, and neither amygdala nor hippocampus alone explains its mnemonic role.'),
('d26daaa3-9582-5827-95f2-03d712187a2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The Fields of Forel contain fibers of passage from', '["GPi, cerebellar nuclei (SCP), and ansa lenticularis","Sensory tracts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Corticospinal tract mediated by specific receptor activation","Acoustic radiation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic radiation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GPi, cerebellar nuclei (SCP), and ansa lenticularis', 'The subthalamic Fields of Forel carry pallidofugal fibers from the GPi, ansa lenticularis, and crossed superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP) fibers en route to the thalamus. Sensory, corticospinal, acoustic, and optic tracts do not traverse this region.'),
('5434ae2f-21d9-51e2-b203-9f7689bf5661'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The ansa lenticularis forms a loop around which structure?', '["Internal capsule (anterior border)","Putamen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caudate nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Corpus callosum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fornix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal capsule (anterior border)', 'The ansa lenticularis curves around the anterior border of the internal capsule to connect the globus pallidus with the thalamus. The putamen, caudate, corpus callosum, and fornix are not encircled by this fiber bundle.'),
('320c5e5a-3e8c-522e-b2f7-5b397c95ec7b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lenticular fasciculus (Forel H2) pierces which structure?', '["Optic tract resulting in decreased functional activity","Corpus callosum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal capsule (globus pallidus → over STN → thalamus)","Fornix without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Caudate nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal capsule (globus pallidus → over STN → thalamus)', 'The lenticular fasciculus (Forel field H2) perforates the internal capsule, carrying fibers from the globus pallidus over the subthalamic nucleus to the thalamus. The optic tract, corpus callosum, fornix, and caudate are not pierced by this bundle.'),
('7c510795-f01f-5080-add8-dca6381c9f01'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamic fasciculus (Forel H1) is formed by', '["Only ansa lenticularis resulting in decreased functional activity","All corticospinal fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only lenticular fasciculus mediated by specific receptor activation","Ansa lenticularis + lenticular fasciculus + SCP (cerebellothalamic) fibers","Only SCP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ansa lenticularis + lenticular fasciculus + SCP (cerebellothalamic) fibers', 'Forel field H1 (thalamic fasciculus) is composed of the ansa lenticularis, the lenticular fasciculus, and crossed SCP cerebello-thalamic fibers converging on the thalamus. No single fiber group, and certainly not corticospinal fibers, accounts for its content.'),
('0eb5d442-29e0-54f7-a1db-3c9ea94f1ef9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Lesions in Forel fields can cause', '["Only visual loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hemiballismus mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hemiballismus, tremor, or movement disorders (due to pallidothalamic/cerebellothalamic disruption)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemiballismus, tremor, or movement disorders (due to pallidothalamic/cerebellothalamic disruption)', 'Because the Fields of Forel carry pallidothalamic and cerebello-thalamic fibers, damage here disrupts motor circuitry, producing hemiballismus, tremor, or other movement disorders. Visual, painful, or purely sensory deficits are not typical of lesions in this subthalamic region.'),
('f90b3630-2a3d-547e-b07d-177627663382'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Zona incerta is considered an extension of the', '["Thalamic nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Reticular formation with diverse connections","Hypothalamic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reticular formation with diverse connections', 'The zona incerta is a thin gray band of the subthalamus regarded as a rostral extension of the reticular formation, with widespread connections to cortex, brainstem, and spinal cord. It is not part of the thalamic nuclei, hypothalamus, basal ganglia, or cerebellum.'),
('469172e1-ec26-5b8e-986d-68e24b73ee86'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Zona incerta is involved in', '["Only attention resulting in decreased functional activity","Visceral (autonomic) control, locomotion, pain, and attention","Only motor control mediated by specific receptor activation","Only visceral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral (autonomic) control, locomotion, pain, and attention', 'The zona incerta integrates diverse functions, including visceral/autonomic control, locomotion, pain modulation, and attention. Its roles span multiple domains rather than being confined to attention, motor, visceral, or pain functions alone.'),
('f1ae16af-1af7-5700-967c-529dcc89cc79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Deep brain stimulation of zona incerta is used for', '["Dystonia only resulting in decreased functional activity","Depression only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Essential tremor and Parkinson disease tremor","Epilepsy only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Essential tremor and Parkinson disease tremor', 'DBS of the caudal zona incerta effectively suppresses tremor in essential tremor and Parkinson disease because this region carries cerebello-thalamic and pallidothalamic fibers. Dystonia, depression, epilepsy, and pain are not its established indications.'),
('612267f7-df4c-5f28-a4de-bc6b521a2591'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus is supplied by which arteries?', '["Anterior cerebral artery resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior choroidal mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Polar (tuberothalamic) from posterior communicating, thalamoperforating from PCA, thalamogeniculate from PCA, posterior choroidal","Middle cerebral artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Polar (tuberothalamic) from posterior communicating, thalamoperforating from PCA, thalamogeniculate from PCA, posterior choroidal', 'The thalamus is irrigated by the tuberothalamic (polar) artery from the posterior communicating artery and by the thalamoperforating, thalamogeniculate, and posterior choroidal branches from the PCA. The ACAs, vertebral, and middle cerebral arteries do not supply the thalamus.'),
('dd65bfe5-ba33-5797-b2df-475fac9b3112'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Tuberothalamic artery (polar artery) supplies', '["Anterior thalamus (VA, anterior, MD, VL)","Medial thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulvinar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior thalamus (VA, anterior, MD, VL)', 'The tuberothalamic (polar) artery, arising from the posterior communicating artery, perfuses the anterior thalamus, including VA, the anterior nuclei, and medial portions of MD and VL. The posterior, lateral, and pulvinar regions are supplied by other PCA branches.'),
('f7773ed8-9908-5f0d-a4f5-506952b62818'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Paramedian (thalamoperforating) arteries (from PCA) supply', '["Medial thalamus (intralaminar, MD, posteromedial)","All thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulvinar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial thalamus (intralaminar, MD, posteromedial)', 'Thalamoperforating arteries from the PCA branch supply the medial thalamus, including the intralaminar nuclei, dorsomedial nucleus, and posteromedial areas. They do not perfuse the entire thalamus, anterior, lateral, or pulvinar regions.'),
('db6834ee-137a-5dd6-ad49-7f4e42b0c8ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamogeniculate arteries (from PCA) supply', '["Hypothalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventrolateral thalamus (VPL, VPM, VL, pulvinar) (lateral thalamic syndrome)","Anterior thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulvinar only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventrolateral thalamus (VPL, VPM, VL, pulvinar) (lateral thalamic syndrome)', 'Thalamogeniculate branches of the PCA supply the ventrolateral thalamus, namely VPL, VPM, VL, and lateral pull of the pulvinar; occlusion produces the lateral (Dejerine-Roussy) thalamic syndrome. Hypothalamus, medial, and anterior thalamus are in other vascular territories.'),
('af16ca19-1062-5954-932f-dfdc47423ba0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Posterior choroidal arteries (from PCA) supply', '["Subthalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal capsule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulvinar, posterior thalamus, geniculate bodies, choroid plexus","Anterior thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulvinar, posterior thalamus, geniculate bodies, choroid plexus', 'Posterior choroidal arteries from the PCA supply the pulvinar, posterior thalamus, the geniculate bodies, and the choroid plexus of the third and lateral ventricles. The subthalamus, internal capsule, hypothalamus, and anterior thalamus are not in this territory.'),
('3caff493-cd95-544c-90d0-40aa3b86c217'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic stroke in tuberothalamic territory causes', '["Cognitive impairment (apathy, abulia, aphasia left side), emotional changes","Pain syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual deficits mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory loss only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor deficits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cognitive impairment (apathy, abulia, aphasia left side), emotional changes', 'Tuberothalamic infarcts damage the anterior and dorsomedial nuclei, producing apathy, abulia, emotional change, and left-sided aphasia due to disrupted frontal-limbic connections. Pain, visual, and isolated sensory or motor syndromes reflect other thalamic territories.'),
('cd2a9a8c-c4c5-5332-a3de-35d2698d2bfb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic stroke in paramedian territory causes', '["Only coma resulting in decreased functional activity","Impairment of consciousness (coma/confusion), vertical gaze palsy, memory impairment","Only confusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Only memory loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only vertical gaze palsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impairment of consciousness (coma/confusion), vertical gaze palsy, memory impairment', 'Paramedian infarcts hit the intralaminar nuclei, which are key in arousal regulation, plus the MD nucleus and the rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, yielding coma or confusion, vertical gaze palsy, and anterograde amnesia. These features occur together, not in isolation.'),
('554353de-d4be-506d-a285-9a52e2e6b9c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic stroke in thalamogeniculate territory (lateral) causes', '["Dejerine-Roussy (thalamic pain syndrome): contralateral sensory loss + pain","Only sensory loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Only pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visual leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dejerine-Roussy (thalamic pain syndrome): contralateral sensory loss + pain', 'Thalamogeniculate (lateral) infarcts damage VPL/VPM, causing contralateral sensory loss followed by spontaneous intractable pain, termed Dejerine-Roussy or thalamic pain syndrome. Sensory loss and pain occur together rather than alone, with no isolated motor or visual picture.'),
('1031625e-f512-510b-9593-002d29d32531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic dementia results from', '["Bilateral or left thalamic (MD, anterior, intralaminar) lesions causing memory and executive dysfunction","Only Alzheimer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vascular dementia mediated by specific receptor activation","Only frontotemporal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Huntington leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral or left thalamic (MD, anterior, intralaminar) lesions causing memory and executive dysfunction', 'Thalamic dementia follows bilateral or left-sided lesions of the MD, anterior, and intralaminar nuclei, which disconnect frontal and hippocampal circuits and produce memory loss with executive dysfunction. It is a vascular and topographic diagnosis, not Alzheimer, frontotemporal, or Huntington disease.'),
('6d2ffcb3-7550-5082-b157-138e8b1473c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus plays a role in arousal and sleep regulation via', '["arousal role resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffuse projections from intralaminar and reticular activating system","Only cognitive function mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only motor function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse projections from intralaminar and reticular activating system', 'Intralaminar nuclei receive ascending reticular activating system input and project diffusely to the cortex, maintaining wakefulness and supporting sleep-wake transitions. Arousal cannot be attributed to purely sensory, motor, or cognitive relay functions.'),
('4f3f76a8-c4e7-54fa-bca8-d913bdaec570'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Spindle oscillations (7-14 Hz) during NREM sleep are generated in', '["Thalamic reticular nucleus (pacemaker for sleep spindles)","Cerebral cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brainstem leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamic reticular nucleus (pacemaker for sleep spindles)', 'GABAergic neurons of the thalamic reticular nucleus act as the pacemaker, rhythmically inhibiting relay neurons to generate 7-14 Hz spindle oscillations characteristic of NREM sleep. The cortex merely propagates the rhythm, while hippocampus, basal ganglia, and brainstem are not the spindle generators.'),
('a2e3d6ae-179c-5107-9692-a8f78d5fedd6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic relay of sensory information is gated by', '["Hypothalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamic reticular nucleus (GABAergic) and brainstem input (arousal)","Basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamic reticular nucleus (GABAergic) and brainstem input (arousal)', 'The reticular nucleus is a thin GABAergic shell around the thalamus that inhibits relay neurons and thereby gates sensory traffic, while brainstem arousal systems modulate this gating during wakefulness. The hypothalamus, basal ganglia, cerebral cortex, and cerebellum influence the thalamus but are not the primary gating mechanism.'),
('b205301d-1d06-5088-acd4-85f91b773538'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Gamma frequency oscillations (30-80 Hz) in thalamocortical circuits are associated with', '["Anesthesia resulting in decreased functional activity","Seizures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Awareness/consciousness (binding)","Coma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sleep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Awareness/consciousness (binding)', 'Synchronized 30-80 Hz oscillations in thalamocortical networks during wakeful perception are thought to bind distributed sensory features into a single conscious percept. Anesthesia, coma, and deep (non-REM) sleep suppress this gamma activity, and seizures represent pathological hypersynchrony rather than normal binding.'),
('ebdff83a-67b8-58c9-978b-164c52538f41'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus is a critical node in which consciousness circuit?', '["Only cortical resulting in decreased functional activity","Only thalamic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only brainstem mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamocortical loop (cortico-thalamo-cortical reentrant processing)","Only reticular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamocortical loop (cortico-thalamo-cortical reentrant processing)', 'Consciousness depends on reentrant signaling between the cerebral cortex and the thalamus, so damage that breaks this loop can abolish awareness. No single structure, whether cortex, thalamus, brainstem, or reticular formation alone, can support consciousness by itself.'),
('ca5806d1-3d73-54c8-be23-89c6b873aa48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Central lateral nucleus (intralaminar) lesions can cause', '["Motor deficits resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensory deficits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of consciousness (minimally conscious state/coma)","Hearing loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of consciousness (minimally conscious state/coma)', 'The intralaminar nuclei, including the central lateral nucleus, belong to the nonspecific thalamic system that sustains arousal and awareness, so injury may cause a minimally conscious state or coma. Motor, sensory, visual, and auditory deficits are not the typical consequences of intralaminar damage.'),
('e4babb49-e10a-5484-a0d6-ce7bdd530e83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamic matrix (calbindin-positive) cells provide', '["cortical projection","Only to sensory cortex","Focal core projections","Only to motor cortex","Diffuse core projections to cortex (modulatory)"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse core projections to cortex (modulatory)', 'Calbindin-positive matrix neurons send diffuse, widespread projections to the cerebral cortex and modulate global cortical states, unlike the focal, modality-specific relays of core (parvalbumin-positive) neurons. The other options wrongly limit the matrix output to a single cortical region.'),
('ceb2066d-fc6b-5bee-9b01-46a8fc60956b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The core and matrix of thalamus have distinct functions:', '["Both do same resulting in decreased functional activity","Core = arousal; Matrix = sensory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Core = motor; Matrix = sensory mediated by specific receptor activation","Core = vision; Matrix = audition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Core = focal sensory/motor relay; Matrix = diffuse arousal/integration"]'::jsonb, 'Core = focal sensory/motor relay; Matrix = diffuse arousal/integration', 'Core cells provide precise, topographically organized sensory and motor relay, whereas matrix cells project diffusely to influence cortical arousal and cross-modal integration. The other choices assign the two systems reversed or identical roles.'),
('3ac155a5-249d-5f2a-86c1-49f6a4cb812d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus is essential for sensory perception, but not for', '["Motor planning resulting in decreased functional activity","Conscious awareness of sensation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple sensory detection (brainstem circuits can detect pain/touch)","Reflex responses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Memory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple sensory detection (brainstem circuits can detect pain/touch)', 'Brainstem circuits alone can detect and respond to pain and touch, so simple detection does not require the thalamus. In contrast, conscious awareness of sensation depends on intact thalamocortical processing, and motor planning, reflexes, and memory have their own separate circuits.'),
('42991fe1-5e31-5bcc-9dbc-b7c963c5c399'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Unilateral thalamic lesions can cause', '["Only motor symptoms resulting in decreased functional activity","symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ipsilateral symptoms mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral sensory loss, hemiataxia (cerebellar projections), and cognitive changes"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral sensory loss, hemiataxia (cerebellar projections), and cognitive changes', 'Because the somatosensory pathways and the major output of the cerebellum cross the midline, a unilateral thalamic lesion produces contralateral sensory loss and hemiataxia, while involvement of frontal-thalamic loops also causes cognitive changes. The deficit is therefore neither purely motor, ipsilateral, nor bilateral.'),
('9271d226-19d7-5931-800b-d684ba74bf32'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Bilateral thalamic lesions can cause', '["Only coma resulting in decreased functional activity","Only vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor mediated by specific receptor activation","Severe amnesia, executive dysfunction, emotional blunting, and reduced consciousness","Only amnesia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe amnesia, executive dysfunction, emotional blunting, and reduced consciousness', 'Bilateral thalamic injury interrupts the anterior nucleus and mammillothalamic memory system, the intralaminar arousal system, and limbic connections, producing amnesia, executive dysfunction, emotional blunting, and reduced consciousness. These combined findings are far wider than coma, a visual problem, or a pure motor deficit alone.'),
('b5efd207-f323-5ec0-a48d-953bde199feb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus plays a role in attention through', '["Only anterior nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only intralaminar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only pulvinar mediated by specific receptor activation","Only reticular nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulvinar and reticular nucleus gating of relevant stimuli"]'::jsonb, 'Pulvinar and reticular nucleus gating of relevant stimuli', 'The pulvinar coordinates selective visuospatial attention while the reticular nucleus gates relay neurons so that relevant stimuli are favored and irrelevant ones suppressed. Any single nucleus such as the anterior or intralaminar group does not by itself explain attentional selection.'),
('01c6f5de-fcd8-5249-a497-a7c919fdcec7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic aphasia (left thalamus) is characterized by', '["Fluent paraphasic speech, anomia, preserved repetition (subcortical aphasia)","Wernicke aphasia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Global aphasia mediated by specific receptor activation","Conduction aphasia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Broca aphasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluent paraphasic speech, anomia, preserved repetition (subcortical aphasia)', 'A left (dominant) thalamic lesion disconnects language networks, typically producing fluent speech with paraphasias and naming difficulty while repetition is preserved, features of subcortical aphasia. Broca, Wernicke, global, and conduction aphasias result from focal cortical or perisylvian damage with different and more region-specific patterns.'),
('68a529f2-3366-5f5f-9008-bc20b3c5a89d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic neglect (right thalamus) causes', '["Left hemispatial neglect, anosognosia, extinction","Visual neglect only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right hemispatial neglect mediated by specific receptor activation","neglect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor neglect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left hemispatial neglect, anosognosia, extinction', 'The right thalamus, especially the pulvinar, participates in a right-hemisphere network that is dominant for spatial attention across both hemifields; its damage causes contralesional (left) hemispatial neglect with anosognosia and extinction. Left-sided lesions produce much milder or transient neglect rather than these combined features.'),
('13a92317-dd73-58f2-b121-e39e9f05ea17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The diencephalon is derived from which vesicle of the neural tube?', '["Rhombencephalon resulting in decreased functional activity","Prosencephalon (forebrain) → diencephalon (optic vesicles, thalamus, hypothalamus)","Telencephalon mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesencephalon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural crest leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prosencephalon (forebrain) → diencephalon (optic vesicles, thalamus, hypothalamus)', 'The prosencephalon (forebrain vesicle) divides into the telencephalon and the diencephalon, from which the optic vesicles, thalamus, and hypothalamus develop. The mesencephalon gives rise to the midbrain and the rhombencephalon to the hindbrain, and the neural crest contributes to peripheral structures, so none of these gives origin to the diencephalon.'),
('fe46bcb0-6fa8-5fcb-a6f1-81c3230a4fd1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The diencephalon can be divided into five parts:', '["Four parts: pretectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Epithalamus, dorsal thalamus (thalamus proper), ventral thalamus (subthalamus), hypothalamus, and pretectum","Only thalamus and hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus, hypothalamus, subthalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epithalamus, dorsal thalamus (thalamus proper), ventral thalamus (subthalamus), hypothalamus, and pretectum', 'The standard five-part subdivision of the diencephalon comprises the epithalamus, dorsal thalamus, ventral thalamus (subthalamus), hypothalamus, and pretectum. The other options omit one or more of these parts.'),
('65208618-0703-5910-ab7d-abb39f81898d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The pretectum (transition between diencephalon and midbrain) is involved in', '["Balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Pupillary light reflex and accommodation","Hearing only mediated by specific receptor activation","Vision only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor coordination leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pupillary light reflex and accommodation', 'Retinal light stimuli reach the pretectum, which relays to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to drive the pupillary light reflex and accommodation. Balance, hearing, bare vision, and motor coordination are governed by other structures.'),
('270b50f1-f97b-5c3f-b88d-3caa9d999f70'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamic radiation contains fibers between', '["Thalamus and spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus and brainstem accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus and cerebral cortex (internal capsule / corona radiata)","Thalamus and cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus and basal ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus and cerebral cortex (internal capsule / corona radiata)', 'The thalamic radiations are the white-matter tracts interconnecting the thalamus and the cerebral cortex, running through the internal capsule and corona radiata. Connections to the spinal cord, brainstem, cerebellum, and basal ganglia use separate pathways.'),
('5fca60dd-6484-52e8-be09-c1d4bcac6832'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The anterior thalamic radiation connects', '["Thalamus to motor cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus to occipital accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus to sensory cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus to temporal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior thalamus to prefrontal and cingulate cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior thalamus to prefrontal and cingulate cortex', 'The anterior thalamic radiation links the anterior nuclear group with the prefrontal and cingulate cortex, supporting limbic, memory, and executive functions. Motor, occipital, sensory, and temporal targets are served by other radiation bundles.'),
('9b7be919-ff70-524a-ac1a-efa6d8ee6e5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The superior thalamic radiation connects', '["LGN to occipital","VL/VA to motor/premotor cortex (motor relay)","VPL/VPM to sensory cortex","Pulvinar to parietal","MGN to temporal"]'::jsonb, 'VL/VA to motor/premotor cortex (motor relay)', 'The superior thalamic radiation carries fibers from the ventrolateral and ventroanterior (VL/VA) nuclei to the motor and premotor cortex, mediating the motor influence of the cerebellum and basal ganglia. The other options describe optic, somatosensory, pulvinar, and auditory radiation tracts.'),
('89b648a3-d4c9-5314-844b-3348ae32f6c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The posterior thalamic radiation contains', '["Only motor resulting in decreased functional activity","Only auditory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only somatosensory mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic radiation (LGN → calcarine) and auditory radiation (MGN → Heschl)","Only optic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic radiation (LGN → calcarine) and auditory radiation (MGN → Heschl)', 'The posterior (retrolenticular) radiation contains the optic radiation from the LGN to the calcarine cortex and the auditory radiation from the MGN to Heschl''s gyrus. It therefore carries both visual and auditory fibers, not a single modality.'),
('fbe8ec03-7990-5653-8876-ae0acbd36d94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The inferior thalamic radiation connects', '["Thalamus to spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus to parietal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus to occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus to frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus to temporal lobe and insula (MGN, pulvinar)"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus to temporal lobe and insula (MGN, pulvinar)', 'The inferior (sublenticular) thalamic radiation connects the pulvinar and MGN with the temporal lobe and insula, including auditory and vestibular-related cortex. It does not project to the spinal cord or primarily to the parietal, occipital, or frontal regions.'),
('e57ddbf1-687a-557b-bf96-176def6a1184'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamic radiation passes through which part of internal capsule?', '["Only anterior limb resulting in decreased functional activity","Only genu accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only posterior limb mediated by specific receptor activation","All parts (anterior limb, genu, posterior limb, retrolenticular, sublenticular)","Only retrolenticular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All parts (anterior limb, genu, posterior limb, retrolenticular, sublenticular)', 'Different thalamic radiations traverse every segment of the internal capsule: the anterior radiation in the anterior limb, the superior radiation in the posterior limb, the posterior radiation in the retrolenticular part, and the inferior radiation in the sublenticular part, with some fibers also in the genu. Thus no single limb carries all of them.'),
('a5be99b0-c186-514b-9d08-c21e524a382f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Posterior thalamic radiation includes the optic radiation which is divided into', '["Only direct occipital resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parietal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only temporal mediated by specific receptor activation","Only frontal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meyer loop (anterior temporal) and parietal loop (superior)"]'::jsonb, 'Meyer loop (anterior temporal) and parietal loop (superior)', 'The optic radiation splits into Meyer''s loop, which passes through the anterior temporal lobe carrying upper-field (inferior retinal) fibers, and the parietal loop, which passes superiorly carrying lower-field fibers. The other options do not describe this subdivision.'),
('b1c3db20-868a-5046-91f0-5fa6e7f2c2d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Meyer loop damage causes', '["Contralateral inferior quadrantanopia resulting in decreased functional activity","Macular sparing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Homonymous hemianopia mediated by specific receptor activation","Blindness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contralateral superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky)"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky)', 'Meyer''s loop carries fibers from the inferior retina that represent the upper visual field, so its damage produces contralateral superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky). Parietal-loop lesions cause the opposite inferior quadrantanopia, while more extensive retrochiasmal lesions produce hemianopia.'),
('1cab56b8-3db6-54e2-be40-6863ae051732'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The pulvinar (thalamus) has strong connections with', '["Primary motor cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Parietal, temporal, occipital association cortices (visual attention, language)","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary sensory cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal, temporal, occipital association cortices (visual attention, language)', 'The pulvinar, the largest thalamic nucleus, has reciprocal connections with the parietal, temporal, and occipital association cortices and participates in visual attention and language. It does not connect prominently with the primary motor cortex, spinal cord, cerebellum, or primary sensory cortex.'),
('7b6ca4d8-a5c0-5632-a010-c02fc05c220d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) receives input from', '["Superior colliculus","Retinal ganglion cells (optic tract)","Thalamic reticular nucleus","Primary visual cortex","Pretectum"]'::jsonb, 'Retinal ganglion cells (optic tract)', 'The optic tract, made up of retinal ganglion cell axons, terminates in the LGN while preserving retinotopic order. The superior colliculus, reticular nucleus, visual cortex, and pretectum receive different terminals of the visual pathway.'),
('368387b1-6220-51a5-b3e0-ea690d8c0bdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'LGN neurons project via optic radiation to', '["Secondary visual cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulvinar mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex, area 17)","Lateral geniculate body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex, area 17)', 'LGN neurons send axons through the optic radiation to the primary visual cortex (Brodmann area 17, striate cortex) of the occipital lobe. The superior colliculus and pulvinar receive optic-tract side branches or cortical loops, not the main LGN output.'),
('78557162-027f-594a-88bd-89f821e8c3e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The LGN has magnocellular (M), parvocellular (P), and koniocellular (K) layers that process', '["All same resulting in decreased functional activity","M = form; P = contrast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","K = motion mediated by specific receptor activation","M = motion/contrast; P = color/form; K = blue-yellow/irradiance","M = color; P = motion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'M = motion/contrast; P = color/form; K = blue-yellow/irradiance', 'In the LGN, magnocellular layers analyze motion and contrast, parvocellular layers encode color and fine form, and koniocellular layers handle blue-yellow and irradiance/rod information. The other options swap or collapse these functional assignments.'),
('83df2f00-8d97-5863-8ede-423277b080d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The medial geniculate nucleus (MGN) has ventral, medial, and dorsal divisions:', '["Ventral = multisensory resulting in decreased functional activity","All same accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial = tonotopic mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal = tonotopic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventral = tonotopic relay (core), Medial = multisensory, Dorsal = other"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral = tonotopic relay (core), Medial = multisensory, Dorsal = other', 'The ventral division of the MGN is a precise tonotopic relay to the auditory cortex, the medial division integrates multimodal input, and the dorsal division subserves less specific auditory processing. The other options mislabel which division performs the tonotopic relay.'),
('b8fc158c-2759-5610-b958-418d415ec98c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus receives strong feedback projections from', '["Basal ganglia resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamic reticular nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebral cortex (corticothalamic, layer VI) — 10x more than afferent input"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral cortex (corticothalamic, layer VI) — 10x more than afferent input', 'Layer VI pyramidal cells of the cerebral cortex send dense feedback to the thalamus that outnumbers ascending input by roughly tenfold, shaping transmission through relay nuclei. The basal ganglia, reticular nucleus, spinal cord, and cerebellum provide separate, smaller inputs rather than this dominant feedback.'),
('48d1456a-24de-5f63-9dde-baad9ec791fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Corticothalamic feedback (layer VI) plays a role in', '["Sensory gating, attention, and perceptual grouping","Vision only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Audition only mediated by specific receptor activation","role without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor output only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory gating, attention, and perceptual grouping', 'Corticothalamic feedback adjusts the gain of relay neurons so that task-relevant stimuli are transmitted while irrelevant ones are suppressed, supporting sensory gating, attention, and perceptual grouping. Its influence is broad rather than confined to a single sense or to motor output.'),
('1dc2c66d-8095-593c-ad9e-bcd20aae3a52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamocortical neurons in specific nuclei project mainly to', '["Layer II resulting in decreased functional activity","Layer V-VI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Layer I mediated by specific receptor activation","Layer IV of cerebral cortex (primary sensory/motor)","Layer III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Layer IV of cerebral cortex (primary sensory/motor)', 'Axons of specific (first-order) relay nuclei terminate chiefly in layer IV of their corresponding cortical areas, the main cortical input layer. Layers I, II, III, and V-VI receive other afferents or give rise to feedback and association projections.'),
('6aaf4768-23a1-5b33-88ab-fb725abc18e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Thalamic relay for the auditory system is unique because', '["thalamic relay resulting in decreased functional activity","MGN receives input from inferior colliculus (not directly from CN)","Relay in LGN mediated by specific receptor activation","Relay in pulvinar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MGN receives direct cochlear input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MGN receives input from inferior colliculus (not directly from CN)', 'The auditory pathway synapses in the inferior colliculus of the midbrain before the medial geniculate nucleus, so the MGN receives no direct input from the cochlear nuclei. The direct cochlear input option is false, and the LGN and pulvinar belong to visual and association systems.'),
('613c1e1b-21d0-5d59-8405-1ee8f5479a05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus is sometimes called the', '["Master gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Little brain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emotional center mediated by specific receptor activation","Gateway to the cerebral cortex (all sensory except olfaction relay here)","Command center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gateway to the cerebral cortex (all sensory except olfaction relay here)', 'Because every sensory system except olfaction synapses in the thalamus on its way to the cortex, the thalamus is called the gateway to the cerebral cortex. ''Master gland'' refers to the pituitary, ''little brain'' to the cerebellum, and the emotional or command labels do not capture this relay function.'),
('09d2314a-26c6-5ef8-82fe-4f66a9aeaf97'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The thalamus has reciprocal connections with which subcortical structures?', '["Only cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basal ganglia (via STN, GPi, SNr), cerebellum (via dentate), limbic (hippocampus, amygdala)","Only basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only amygdala leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basal ganglia (via STN, GPi, SNr), cerebellum (via dentate), limbic (hippocampus, amygdala)', 'The thalamus exchanges reciprocal connections with the basal ganglia (via STN, GPi, and SNr), the cerebellum (via the dentate nucleus), and limbic structures such as the hippocampus and amygdala. The other options limit this interconnectivity to a single structure.'),
('b5df8108-cdc1-56f4-9302-8618c5362e3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The centromedian (CM) nucleus projects heavily to which structure?', '["Cerebral cortex (diffuse) resulting in decreased functional activity","Striatum (caudate/putamen) — part of basal ganglia loop","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Hippocampus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Striatum (caudate/putamen) — part of basal ganglia loop', 'The centromedian-parafascicular complex provides the major intralaminar projection to the striatum (caudate and putamen) and is integral to basal ganglia loops. Its diffuse cortical projection is less prominent than its striatal output, and the cerebellum, hippocampus, and spinal cord are not its principal targets.'),
('d19c5f66-bf83-54e0-a3a4-df30e59c2ff9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The subthalamic nucleus (STN) and globus pallidus internus (GPi) are targets for', '["Only cortical stimulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Only lesioning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","surgery mediated by specific receptor activation","Only medication without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep brain stimulation (DBS) in Parkinson disease"]'::jsonb, 'Deep brain stimulation (DBS) in Parkinson disease', 'High-frequency deep brain stimulation of the STN or GPi normalizes dysfunctional basal ganglia output and effectively treats parkinsonian motor signs. The other options describe stimulation sites or methods that are not the standard DBS targets.'),
('2ca5c788-0bbd-5a29-b81d-112915ae1b6b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Regarding clinical cases: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('47ce5eec-c879-5eb4-a249-85cb6589b19d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'In medical practice, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('0d88337e-5305-5863-acda-3b22bf6772ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The internal medullary lamina of the thalamus divides the thalamus into', '["Dorsal and ventral","Anterior, medial, and lateral nuclear groups","Rostral and caudal","Medial and lateral only","Superior and inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior, medial, and lateral nuclear groups', 'The Y-shaped internal medullary lamina partitions the thalamus into anterior, medial, and lateral nuclear groups and also contains the intralaminar nuclei. Its arrangement does not section the nucleus into dorsal/ventral, rostral/caudal, or superior/inferior halves.'),
('5aa78357-911e-5cca-871f-cb95d2b03f0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('88937a9e-8f67-593f-a82d-dfda81b7371c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'During therapeutic management, Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('3acdb2b2-61cb-53f4-9fb8-6b3f3a48624c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The reticular nucleus of the thalamus modulates', '["Vision only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor output mediated by specific receptor activation","Gating of sensory information to cortex","Audition only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gating of sensory information to cortex', 'By continuously inhibiting relay nuclei, the reticular nucleus controls which sensory information reaches the cortex, a gating function also linked to attention and sleep rhythms. Funneling its role to vision, pain, audition, or motor output alone ignores this broad modulation.'),
('ea45509b-a65f-55e2-84ac-22de13eae675'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The intralaminar nuclei of the thalamus include', '["VPL and VPM resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulvinar and LP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","LGN and MGN mediated by specific receptor activation","Centromedian (CM) and parafascicular (Pf)","Anterior and medial dorsal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Centromedian (CM) and parafascicular (Pf)', 'The centromedian and parafascicular nuclei are the principal intralaminar nuclei, embedded in the internal medullary lamina. VPL/VPM, pulvinar/LP, LGN/MGN, and the anterior/mediodorsal nuclei are members of other nuclear groups.'),
('f14cfbe8-da6f-596d-90ed-cb87b30c39d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('b1d45187-fe2c-5ff4-abf4-c521ee3765f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('89254aca-fb11-5cf1-9040-c7fef8597771'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'During therapeutic management, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('ba071ffe-1528-5d69-8ac1-930fac7b9bda'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'Regarding clinical cases: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('0fd57af4-02db-5d41-9e70-aa45b49537d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'In the study of Diencephalon, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('1943868c-0c41-5e19-80ff-7254fc0ccdca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The medial dorsal (MD) nucleus of the thalamus projects to', '["Motor cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Somatosensory cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Auditory cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Visual cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prefrontal cortex (reciprocal connections)"]'::jsonb, 'Prefrontal cortex (reciprocal connections)', 'The mediodorsal nucleus maintains strong reciprocal connections with the prefrontal cortex and is essential for executive function and personality; its injury mimics frontal lobe syndrome. Motor, somatosensory, auditory, and visual cortices receive projections from other nuclei.'),
('4ed5d60d-ec3a-5be3-8295-bd1c9be50db5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Diencephalon', 'The MD nucleus receives input from', '["Cochlea resulting in decreased functional activity","Retina accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala, olfactory cortex, basal ganglia (ventral pallidum/SNr)","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Amygdala, olfactory cortex, basal ganglia (ventral pallidum/SNr)', 'The mediodorsal nucleus integrates limbic (amygdala and olfactory cortex) and basal ganglia (ventral pallidum and substantia nigra pars reticulata) input and relays it to the prefrontal cortex. The cochlea, retina, spinal cord, and cerebellum project to other nuclei and structures.'),
('45171268-fc04-597b-903a-04fa71210790'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The three meninges from outer to inner are:', '["Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater","Pia, arachnoid, dura","Dura, pia, arachnoid","Arachnoid, dura, pia","Pia, dura, arachnoid"]'::jsonb, 'Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater', 'The cranial and spinal meninges are arranged in three concentric layers: tough outer dura mater, middle avascular arachnoid mater, and delicate vascular inner pia mater. Options listing pia first or arachnoid before dura reverse this anatomical order.'),
('e8ee2865-a264-5a77-9e41-91a4f4f48590'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The cranial dura mater consists of', '["Two layers (periosteal and meningeal) fused together except at venous sinuses","One layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Single layer with sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","Three layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fused to bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two layers (periosteal and meningeal) fused together except at venous sinuses', 'Cranial dura has an outer periosteal layer adherent to the skull and an inner meningeal layer; they separate only where dural venous sinuses lie between them. A single layer and three-layer descriptions do not match this arrangement.'),
('c36b5608-f183-5777-a0db-153bfabf87c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The spinal dura mater consists of', '["Two layers with epidural space resulting in decreased functional activity","Three layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Single layer (meningeal only) with epidural space","Fused to periosteum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","dura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Single layer (meningeal only) with epidural space', 'In the spinal canal the dura is a single meningeal layer, because the periosteal layer remains as the vertebral periosteum, leaving an intervening epidural space. The two-layer pattern is a cranial feature, and the spinal dura does not fuse to periosteum.'),
('985a00e0-aca7-5130-9cef-fb8561fcd522'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The epidural space in the spine contains', '["Fat, loose connective tissue, internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus)","Arachnoid mater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Pia mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CSF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat, loose connective tissue, internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus)', 'The epidural space lies between the dura and the vertebral periosteum and is occupied by fat, loose connective tissue, and the valveless internal vertebral venous plexus. Arachnoid, pia, and CSF are situated inside the dural sac, not in this space.'),
('4abe19e2-a76f-54e9-b470-1afa6c2ed4a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The subdural space is a', '["Layer of cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Potential space (between dura and arachnoid) filled with a thin film of fluid","Space between pia and arachnoid mediated by specific receptor activation","space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Real space filled with CSF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potential space (between dura and arachnoid) filled with a thin film of fluid', 'The subdural space lies between the dura and arachnoid and is normally only a potential space containing a thin film of serous fluid. It does not normally hold CSF, which instead fills the subarachnoid space below the arachnoid.'),
('6c2abfa4-864b-5a3b-89c8-4851e8402ca5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Arachnoid granulations (villi) project into', '["Superior sagittal sinus (CSF absorption)","Subdural space","Internal jugular vein","Cortical veins","Lateral ventricles"]'::jsonb, 'Superior sagittal sinus (CSF absorption)', 'Arachnoid granulations are tufts of arachnoid that project through the dura into the dural venous sinuses, chiefly the superior sagittal sinus, where they absorb CSF into venous blood. The other listed sites do not receive these villi.'),
('c1b712d8-43f4-5140-87c6-47421d155bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pia mater is a', '["Thin vascular membrane adherent to brain and spinal cord surface","Thick fibrous layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dural layer mediated by specific receptor activation","Ependymal layer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Arachnoid-like leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thin vascular membrane adherent to brain and spinal cord surface', 'The pia is the innermost meningeal layer, a thin highly vascular membrane that closely follows every contour of the brain and spinal cord. It is not thick, fibrous, or ependymal in nature.'),
('df38f387-5cd3-5c0b-b141-6e247d1a5405'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Falx cerebri is a dural fold between', '["Midbrain and pons resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral hemispheres (attached to crista galli anterior, tentorium/internal occipital protuberance posterior)","Cerebellum and occipital lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Temporal and frontal lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar hemispheres leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral hemispheres (attached to crista galli anterior, tentorium/internal occipital protuberance posterior)', 'The falx cerebri is a sickle-shaped dural fold that descends into the longitudinal fissure between the two cerebral hemispheres. It attaches anteriorly to the crista galli and posteriorly to the tentorium cerebelli and internal occipital protuberance.'),
('a7990963-85bf-59ac-bb11-5260f3e2c23f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Tentorium cerebelli separates', '["Cerebellar hemispheres resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal from temporal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain from pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum (infratentorial) from occipital lobes (supratentorial)","Cerebral hemispheres leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum (infratentorial) from occipital lobes (supratentorial)', 'The tentorium cerebelli is a horizontal dural shelf separating the posterior cranial fossa contents (cerebellum, brainstem) below from the cerebral hemispheres, especially the occipital lobes, above. It does not separate the cerebellar hemispheres from each other.'),
('cbf090a6-6d9e-5ea1-ad67-647e69272c92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The falx cerebri attaches to the tentorium at the', '["Superior sagittal sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cavernous sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Sigmoid sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Straight sinus (confluence of falx and tentorium attachments)"]'::jsonb, 'Straight sinus (confluence of falx and tentorium attachments)', 'The straight sinus runs in the line of union between the posterior falx cerebri and the tentorium cerebelli, receiving the inferior sagittal sinus and great cerebral vein of Galen. The other sinuses lie at different dural locations.'),
('d3e0804a-7322-5ca4-b7b3-933a63df0c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The falx cerebelli is a small dural fold between', '["Cerebellum and medulla","Cerebellar hemispheres","Temporal lobes","Cerebral hemispheres","Occipital lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar hemispheres', 'The falx cerebelli is a small vertical dural fold in the posterior cranial fossa that projects between the two cerebellar hemispheres along the vermis. The falx cerebri, by contrast, lies between the cerebral hemispheres.'),
('7194d34b-3231-5068-bdd8-807b444e3458'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Diaphragma sellae covers', '["Sphenoid sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic chiasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foramen magnum mediated by specific receptor activation","Pituitary fossa (sella turcica) with a small opening for infundibulum","Pineal gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary fossa (sella turcica) with a small opening for infundibulum', 'The diaphragma sellae is a dural roof stretched over the pituitary fossa (sella turcica), perforated centrally by the infundibulum (pituitary stalk). It does not cover the sphenoid sinus, optic chiasm, or pineal gland.'),
('3e498c12-deca-50d6-b5d8-f1c687b3df3f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Superior sagittal sinus lies in', '["Falx cerebri (superior/attached border) — drains from anterior to posterior","Transverse sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Straight sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Tentorium cerebelli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cavernous sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Falx cerebri (superior/attached border) — drains from anterior to posterior', 'The superior sagittal sinus runs in the attached (upper) border of the falx cerebri, where it grooves the calvaria, and carries blood from anterior to posterior toward the confluence of sinuses. It is not located in the tentorium or cavernous sinus.'),
('07e8a845-e17b-555c-bd89-a477de56dc99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Inferior sagittal sinus lies in', '["Superior sagittal sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Transverse sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cavernous sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Falx cerebri (inferior/free border) — drains into straight sinus","Confluence of sinuses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Falx cerebri (inferior/free border) — drains into straight sinus', 'The inferior sagittal sinus occupies the free (lower) margin of the falx cerebri and runs posteriorly to join the great cerebral vein of Galen to form the straight sinus. The other choices are separate dural sinuses or junctions.'),
('ad43cc8d-7477-5dd3-b4d5-8f4b560c36ec'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Straight sinus is formed by', '["Inferior sagittal sinus + great cerebral vein of Galen (at junction of falx and tentorium)","Sigmoid sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cavernous sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Transverse sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior sagittal sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior sagittal sinus + great cerebral vein of Galen (at junction of falx and tentorium)', 'The straight sinus is formed at the junction of the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli by union of the inferior sagittal sinus and the great cerebral vein of Galen. It then drains posteriorly toward the confluence of sinuses.'),
('24154141-f809-53d9-a191-0f81c667029d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The confluence of sinuses (torcular Herophili) is at', '["Pituitary fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","Sphenoid bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foramen magnum mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal occipital protuberance — receives superior sagittal, straight, occipital, and transverse sinuses","Jugular foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal occipital protuberance — receives superior sagittal, straight, occipital, and transverse sinuses', 'The confluence of sinuses lies at the internal occipital protuberance, where the superior sagittal, straight, occipital, and transverse sinuses meet. It is not related to the pituitary fossa, sphenoid bone, foramen magnum, or jugular foramen.'),
('82ee41fd-19fc-57de-81d4-89986b142dd5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Transverse sinus runs in', '["Tentorium cerebelli (lateral border of tentorium) → becomes sigmoid sinus","Falx cerebri accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cavernous sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen magnum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior sagittal sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tentorium cerebelli (lateral border of tentorium) → becomes sigmoid sinus', 'Each transverse sinus runs along the lateral attached border of the tentorium cerebelli, turning downward to become the sigmoid sinus at the petrous bone. It is not related to the falx cerebri or cavernous sinus.'),
('406687da-fb00-558b-80c1-766265e4c537'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Sigmoid sinus (continuation of transverse) drains into', '["Vertebral vein","Internal carotid artery","External jugular vein","Internal jugular vein via jugular foramen","Cavernous sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Internal jugular vein via jugular foramen', 'The sigmoid sinus is the S-shaped continuation of the transverse sinus that curves down and enters the jugular foramen to become the internal jugular vein. It does not empty into the vertebral vein, carotid arteries, or cavernous sinus.'),
('08b22806-419b-5404-9605-5cf868905c4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cavernous sinus is located', '["At the foramen magnum resulting in decreased functional activity","Above the corpus callosum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the tentorium mediated by specific receptor activation","In the falx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral to sella turcica (sphenoid bone), extending to temporal bone"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral to sella turcica (sphenoid bone), extending to temporal bone', 'Each cavernous sinus lies lateral to the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone, extending from the superior orbital fissure anteriorly to the petrous part of the temporal bone posteriorly. It is not located at the foramen magnum, in the tentorium, or in the falx.'),
('2319b81b-f2f1-5914-9d6e-cd2e7e9b8abc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cavernous sinus contains (within its lateral wall)', '["Only CN V2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN V1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN III (oculomotor), CN IV (trochlear), CN V1+V2 (ophthalmic/maxillary)","Only CN III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only CN IV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN III (oculomotor), CN IV (trochlear), CN V1+V2 (ophthalmic/maxillary)', 'The lateral dural wall of the cavernous sinus carries the oculomotor, trochlear, and the ophthalmic and maxillary divisions of the trigeminal nerve, stacked from above down. Only CN VI passes through the sinus itself, next to the internal carotid artery.'),
('1137474f-d96a-5c3d-8583-8b10d85e2a28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The only cranial nerve that runs within the cavernous sinus (not in its wall) is', '["CN V1 resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VI (abducens) — next to ICA","CN V2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN III leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VI (abducens) — next to ICA', 'The abducens nerve is the only cranial nerve to course through the interior of the cavernous sinus, lying lateral to the internal carotid artery. CN III, IV, V1, and V2 run within the lateral dural wall instead.'),
('d6827eb2-2639-50d2-a527-482ddb28ce3b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cavernous sinus drains into', '["Straight sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior sagittal sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occipital sinus mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior and inferior petrosal sinuses → transverse/sigmoid sinus and internal jugular vein","Inferior sagittal sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior petrosal sinuses → transverse/sigmoid sinus and internal jugular vein', 'The cavernous sinus drains posteriorly through the superior petrosal sinus (to the transverse sinus) and the inferior petrosal sinus (to the internal jugular vein). It does not drain into the sagittal, straight, or occipital sinuses.'),
('a711416f-e6dc-53ba-886c-d0a1249a0f02'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'In cavernous sinus thrombosis, signs include', '["Only fever resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CN VI palsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Periorbital edema, proptosis, CN III/IV/V/VI palsy, fever","Only headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only visual loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Periorbital edema, proptosis, CN III/IV/V/VI palsy, fever', 'Cavernous sinus thrombosis produces periorbital edema, proptosis, chemosis, fever, and ophthalmoplegia because all of CN III, IV, VI, and the ophthalmic division of CN V pass through the sinus or its wall. Isolated single-nerve palsy or fever alone would be incomplete.'),
('13418c16-cd62-5072-8779-f342ef43960d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The superior petrosal sinus drains', '["Straight sinus to confluence resulting in decreased functional activity","Cavernous sinus to transverse sinus (along petrous temporal bone)","Sigmoid sinus to IJV mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital sinus to confluence without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cavernous sinus to cavernous sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus to transverse sinus (along petrous temporal bone)', 'The superior petrosal sinus runs along the upper border of the petrous temporal bone, connecting the cavernous sinus to the transverse sinus. It does not connect to the straight, occipital, or sigmoid sinuses.'),
('fb8527c5-ac6b-522b-8145-797b8044f0dd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The inferior petrosal sinus drains', '["Sigmoid sinus to IJV resulting in decreased functional activity","Cavernous sinus to transverse sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cavernous sinus to internal jugular vein (via jugular foramen)","Straight sinus to IJV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cavernous sinus to cavernous sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cavernous sinus to internal jugular vein (via jugular foramen)', 'The inferior petrosal sinus runs along the groove between the clivus and the petrous temporal bone, draining the cavernous sinus into the internal jugular vein through the jugular foramen. It does not connect to the transverse or straight sinuses.'),
('e7974b73-eb75-5276-9430-a685c61b731e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The basilar venous plexus is located on', '["Tentorium resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clivus (dorsum sellae to foramen magnum) connecting both cavernous sinuses","Falx cerebri without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brainstem leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clivus (dorsum sellae to foramen magnum) connecting both cavernous sinuses', 'The basilar venous plexus lies on the clivus, extending from the dorsum sellae to the foramen magnum, and connects the two cavernous sinuses. It does not lie on the tentorium, falx, or spinal cord.'),
('6aa47153-cf42-5f15-b5a0-a2e4fa9bab1d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Emissary veins connect', '["Arteries to veins resulting in decreased functional activity","Arachnoid to pia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brain to sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","Dura to pia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intracranial venous sinuses to extracranial veins (valveless, potential pathway for infection)"]'::jsonb, 'Intracranial venous sinuses to extracranial veins (valveless, potential pathway for infection)', 'Emissary veins traverse the skull to connect intracranial dural venous sinuses with extracranial veins. Being valveless, they can transmit infection from the scalp or face into the intracranial venous system.'),
('2b238483-de09-5792-9056-46a05c95e383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The dural venous sinuses are lined by', '["Stratified epithelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Endothelium only (no muscle, no valves)","Valves mediated by specific receptor activation","Smooth muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Columnar epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelium only (no muscle, no valves)', 'Dural venous sinuses are endothelial-lined spaces located between the two layers of dura; they lack smooth muscle and valves. Being valveless and low-pressure, they can drain in either direction depending on pressure gradients.'),
('fe10e450-99b4-585a-9368-167aa92bb039'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus) connects with', '["Basilar plexus and dural sinuses (valveless, important for metastasis spread)","connection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid granulations mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymphatics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Systemic veins only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Basilar plexus and dural sinuses (valveless, important for metastasis spread)', 'The internal vertebral venous plexus communicates cranially with the basilar plexus and intracranial dural sinuses. Because the plexus is valveless, pelvic or thoracic malignancy can spread retrogradely to the vertebral column and cranium.'),
('46c4e865-bf15-58e3-b021-870fdd18e926'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The arachnoid mater is separated from the dura by the', '["Subarachnoid space","Epidural space","Arachnoid granulations","Pia","Subdural space (potential)"]'::jsonb, 'Subdural space (potential)', 'The arachnoid mater is separated from the dura by the subdural space, which is normally a potential space with only a thin film of fluid. The subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid and the pia, not the dura.'),
('4a339cf1-8c56-5c8f-bda8-3a53defc286f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The arachnoid mater is separated from the pia by the', '["Subdural space resulting in decreased functional activity","Epidural space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid granulations mediated by specific receptor activation","space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subarachnoid space (real, CSF-filled)"]'::jsonb, 'Subarachnoid space (real, CSF-filled)', 'The subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid and pia and is a real space normally filled with cerebrospinal fluid. It contains the CSF and major vessels that supply the brain, including at the basal cisterns.'),
('b4d24584-36f8-5b54-9574-8846438563c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Arachnoid granulations (Pacchionian) are responsible for', '["Brain support resulting in decreased functional activity","Nerve protection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF production mediated by specific receptor activation","CSF absorption into venous blood (superior sagittal sinus)","Blood flow regulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF absorption into venous blood (superior sagittal sinus)', 'Arachnoid granulations are villous projections that allow CSF to pass from the subarachnoid space into the venous blood of the dural sinuses, principally the superior sagittal sinus. CSF production is a function of the choroid plexus, not these granulations.'),
('78551e05-3101-5bb3-be6c-6736507d793f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF absorption occurs primarily via', '["Fourth ventricle resulting in decreased functional activity","Lymphatics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid granulations (superior sagittal sinus) — then into venous blood","Choroid plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal subarachnoid space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid granulations (superior sagittal sinus) — then into venous blood', 'Most CSF is absorbed by the arachnoid granulations, which project into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus, and return CSF to the venous blood. The choroid plexus produces CSF, while the fourth ventricle is a pathway for its flow.'),
('a3bf6ed6-d325-53da-b0a8-56f7a0bd76a2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Obstruction of arachnoid granulations causes', '["Meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF leak accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Communicating hydrocephalus (impaired CSF absorption)","Pseudo-tumor cerebri without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-communicating hydrocephalus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Communicating hydrocephalus (impaired CSF absorption)', 'Blockage of the arachnoid granulations impairs CSF reabsorption while the ventricular pathways remain patent, producing communicating hydrocephalus with dilation of all ventricles. Non-communicating hydrocephalus instead results from obstruction of the ventricular outflow or foramina.'),
('3601d7c4-cfc4-5bf5-8fdc-fd3f4710f5b6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is produced primarily by', '["Pia mater resulting in decreased functional activity","Ependyma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Choroid plexus (in lateral, 3rd, and 4th ventricles)","Arachnoid granulations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (in lateral, 3rd, and 4th ventricles)', 'CSF is produced mainly by the choroid plexus, which is present in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles. The arachnoid granulations absorb CSF rather than make it, and the pia, ependyma, and cortex are not the primary sites of production.'),
('2583f537-a441-5cb9-97d1-6b29ed548df1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The choroid plexus is composed of', '["Mixed resulting in decreased functional activity","ependyma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vascular connective tissue + cuboidal ependymal epithelium (tight junctions = blood-CSF barrier)","Stratified epithelium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple squamous epithelium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular connective tissue + cuboidal ependymal epithelium (tight junctions = blood-CSF barrier)', 'The choroid plexus consists of a core of vascular pial connective tissue covered by cuboidal ependymal epithelium whose tight junctions form the blood-CSF barrier. It is neither stratified nor simple squamous in type.'),
('4eaf0800-4781-51bf-81b6-9f96b41721fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The blood-CSF barrier is formed by', '["Arachnoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Capillary endothelium astrocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dura mediated by specific receptor activation","Pia mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Choroid plexus epithelial tight junctions (preventing paracellular diffusion)"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus epithelial tight junctions (preventing paracellular diffusion)', 'The blood-CSF barrier resides in the tight junctions between choroid plexus cuboidal epithelial cells, which prevent free paracellular exchange of molecules between the choroidal capillaries and the CSF. The arachnoid, dura, and pia are not part of this barrier.'),
('cce4f8ca-79a7-52d7-81b5-2b45d3a36897'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is formed by', '["Ependymal cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Choroid plexus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dura mediated by specific receptor activation","Tight junctions of cerebral capillary endothelial cells + astrocyte foot processes + pericytes","Arachnoid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions of cerebral capillary endothelial cells + astrocyte foot processes + pericytes', 'The blood-brain barrier is formed by tight junctions between cerebral capillary endothelial cells, reinforced by pericytes and astrocyte foot processes. Ependymal cells, choroid plexus, and meningeal layers are not the structural basis of the BBB.'),
('4569932f-9ff0-58c1-8653-bb8c2c6b943e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'BBB is absent at', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All brain mediated by specific receptor activation","Circumventricular organs (area postrema, median eminence, neurohypophysis, OVLT, SFO, pineal)","Cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circumventricular organs (area postrema, median eminence, neurohypophysis, OVLT, SFO, pineal)', 'The circumventricular organs located around the third and fourth ventricles have fenestrated capillaries and lack the BBB, allowing them to sample blood constituents and secrete hormones. The cerebellum, cortex, and spinal cord all possess an intact BBB.'),
('89a44205-603c-5ea5-b603-a1763eec9a17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF production rate in adults is approximately', '["1000 mL/day","2000 mL/day","50 mL/day","100 mL/day","500 mL/day (20 mL/hour)"]'::jsonb, '500 mL/day (20 mL/hour)', 'The adult choroid plexus produces roughly 500 mL of CSF per day, about 20 mL per hour, with the entire volume turning over several times daily. Production rates of 1000-2000 mL/day are more than double the physiologic rate.'),
('0ef2d679-7d73-5379-afd6-1c86988c0261'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Total CSF volume in adults is', '["10 mL resulting in decreased functional activity","150 mL (ventricles 30 mL, subarachnoid 120 mL)","300 mL mediated by specific receptor activation","50 mL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","500 mL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '150 mL (ventricles 30 mL, subarachnoid 120 mL)', 'Total adult CSF volume is about 150 mL, distributed between the ventricles (about 30 mL) and the subarachnoid space (about 120 mL). A total of 50 mL or less would be far too small, while 300-500 mL exceeds the normal volume.'),
('7d63026d-732e-573c-9fd5-d90e3c686975'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF pressure in adults (normal range, lateral recumbent) is', '["50-100 cm H2O",">100 cm H2O","20-30 cm H2O","10-15 cm H2O (or 7-18 mmHg)","0-5 cm H2O"]'::jsonb, '10-15 cm H2O (or 7-18 mmHg)', 'With the patient lying in the lateral recumbent position, normal lumbar CSF opening pressure is about 10-15 cm H2O (roughly 7-18 mmHg). Pressures of 20 cm H2O or above are usually considered elevated, while near-zero pressures are abnormally low.'),
('7ac448a9-d231-5dea-850c-846304668db6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The lateral ventricles are located in', '["Diencephalon resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral hemispheres (one per hemisphere)","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brainstem leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral hemispheres (one per hemisphere)', 'Each cerebral hemisphere contains one lateral ventricle, making them the largest CSF-filled cavities in the brain. They do not lie within the diencephalon, cerebellum, or brainstem.'),
('24545fe4-5c59-5048-84c5-29432f4b88ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The lateral ventricle is divided into', '["Only body resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior (frontal) horn, body, posterior (occipital) horn, inferior (temporal) horn","Only frontal and occipital mediated by specific receptor activation","Only temporal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only body and horns leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (frontal) horn, body, posterior (occipital) horn, inferior (temporal) horn', 'Each lateral ventricle has an anterior (frontal) horn, a central body, a posterior (occipital) horn, and an inferior (temporal) horn. Descriptions listing only one or two parts omit major components of the ventricle.'),
('cf1ccab2-5da2-5df5-8cb9-6285b29295d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The anterior horn of lateral ventricle is located in', '["Frontal lobe (anterior to interventricular foramen)","Occipital lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporal lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parietal lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal lobe (anterior to interventricular foramen)', 'The anterior horn of the lateral ventricle lies in the frontal lobe, anterior to the interventricular foramen. The occipital, temporal, and parietal lobes house different parts of the ventricle and surrounding structures.'),
('b17ee0a7-71c8-51f7-80f9-214adff7a1ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The body of lateral ventricle is in', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occipital lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parietal lobe (from foramen of Monro to splenium of corpus callosum)"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal lobe (from foramen of Monro to splenium of corpus callosum)', 'The body of the lateral ventricle runs through the parietal lobe, extending from the interventricular foramen of Monro posteriorly to the splenium of the corpus callosum. The frontal, temporal, and occipital parts are separate segments.'),
('a1e2a1a2-18f0-547c-b778-6ba07c83c8ce'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The posterior (occipital) horn of lateral ventricle extends into', '["Temporal lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital lobe (variable, may be asymmetrical)","Parietal lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital lobe (variable, may be asymmetrical)', 'The posterior horn of each lateral ventricle extends into the occipital lobe and is variable in size, often asymmetrical between sides. It does not extend into the temporal, frontal, or parietal lobes, and the cerebellum contains no ventricular horns.'),
('c93a4f51-f44b-56b3-88e2-9cd8bebf359b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The inferior (temporal) horn of lateral ventricle extends into', '["Temporal lobe (contains hippocampus in its floor)","Brainstem accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occipital lobe mediated by specific receptor activation","Parietal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe (contains hippocampus in its floor)', 'The inferior (temporal) horn runs anteriorly into the temporal lobe, with the hippocampus forming part of its floor. It does not extend into the occipital, parietal, or frontal lobes or into the brainstem.'),
('a0c09ad0-d62c-5246-86eb-54675e1b7297'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The interventricular foramen (Foramen of Monro) connects', '["Lateral ventricle to fourth ventricle","Fourth ventricle to subarachnoid","Third ventricle to fourth ventricle","Third ventricle to lateral","Lateral ventricle to third ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral ventricle to third ventricle', 'Each interventricular foramen of Monro connects a lateral ventricle to the third ventricle, allowing CSF to pass medially through the diencephalon. The third-to-fourth ventricle connection is the cerebral aqueduct, not the foramen of Monro.'),
('e041c5bd-b081-5ebb-a6d7-289c9b7e57be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The third ventricle is located between', '["Lateral ventricles","Brainstem and cerebellum","Cerebral hemispheres","Fourth ventricle","Two thalami (midline, diencephalon)"]'::jsonb, 'Two thalami (midline, diencephalon)', 'The third ventricle is a narrow midline CSF space in the diencephalon, bounded laterally by the two thalami and connected to the lateral ventricles by the foramina of Monro. It is not positioned between the brainstem and cerebellum or within the cerebral hemispheres.'),
('ebe05248-a8d3-5a74-9f77-a2b679e82383'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Which option correctly describes the walls of the third ventricle?', '["Roof = corpus callosum resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All sides = hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral = medial thalamus and hypothalamus; roof = choroid plexus and tela choroidea; floor = hypothalamus; anterior = lamina terminalis","All sides = thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Floor = midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral = medial thalamus and hypothalamus; roof = choroid plexus and tela choroidea; floor = hypothalamus; anterior = lamina terminalis', 'The lateral walls are formed by the medial surfaces of the thalamus and hypothalamus, the roof by choroid plexus and tela choroidea, the floor by the hypothalamus, and the anterior wall by the lamina terminalis. The other choices misidentify the roof, walls, and floor.'),
('8789a041-d3b2-53e5-b22e-714ede3afdb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The cerebral aqueduct (aqueduct of Sylvius) connects the', '["Third to lateral","Lateral ventricle to fourth","Fourth ventricle to subarachnoid","Lateral ventricle to third","Third ventricle to fourth ventricle (through midbrain)"]'::jsonb, 'Third ventricle to fourth ventricle (through midbrain)', 'The cerebral aqueduct runs through the midbrain and carries CSF from the third ventricle into the fourth ventricle. The other options connect the wrong chambers or lead into the subarachnoid space, which is not a direct aqueduct connection.'),
('d17c27ae-4761-5108-920f-068ad70a516e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The fourth ventricle is located between', '["Cerebral hemispheres resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus and hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla and spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain and pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons/medulla (anterior) and cerebellum (posterior)"]'::jsonb, 'Pons/medulla (anterior) and cerebellum (posterior)', 'The fourth ventricle lies ventral to the cerebellum and dorsal to the pons and medulla. The other locations do not correspond to a ventricular chamber.'),
('1780312f-7764-5412-af3e-3a1b0006c5a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The fourth ventricle communicates with the subarachnoid space via', '["Interventricular foramina resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramen of Monro accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral aqueduct mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen of Luschka (lateral) and foramen of Magendie (median)","Central canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen of Luschka (lateral) and foramen of Magendie (median)', 'CSF exits the fourth ventricle through the paired lateral foramina of Luschka and the midline foramen of Magendie into the subarachnoid space. The internal foramina and central canal do not open into the subarachnoid space at this level.'),
('24abf221-f5ab-5153-8dac-ab07c34a8367'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The lateral aperture (foramen of Luschka) opens into', '["Spinal canal resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellopontine angle (subarachnoid space)","Fourth ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Third ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interventricular foramen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellopontine angle (subarachnoid space)', 'Each foramen of Luschka opens into the subarachnoid space at the cerebellopontine angle. The spinal canal and ventricular chambers are not its destination.'),
('9955993e-808b-5e63-a106-246c2b8cfcf2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The median aperture (foramen of Magendie) opens into', '["Aqueduct resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Third ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Straight sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cisterna magna (posterior cerebellomedullary cistern)"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna magna (posterior cerebellomedullary cistern)', 'The midline foramen of Magendie opens into the cisterna magna, the posterior cerebellomedullary cistern. The aqueduct and ventricles are internal channels, not openings of the median aperture.'),
('3b485c7f-121c-58a9-a77d-aabbc6a9b2f9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The lateral recess of the fourth ventricle extends into', '["Prepontine cistern resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellopontine angle (via foramen of Luschka)","Interpeduncular cistern mediated by specific receptor activation","Basal cistern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ambient cistern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellopontine angle (via foramen of Luschka)', 'The lateral recess of the fourth ventricle runs into the subarachnoid space of the cerebellopontine angle by way of the foramen of Luschka. The other cisterns do not receive the lateral recess.'),
('3684c7ef-ce2b-5043-b423-1fb3c0c2bd7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Choroid plexus of the fourth ventricle projects through which foramina?', '["All foramina resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramen of Magendie only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only via aqueduct mediated by specific receptor activation","projection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral foramina of Luschka (on brain surface)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral foramina of Luschka (on brain surface)', 'Tuffs of fourth ventricle choroid plexus project through each lateral foramen of Luschka to reach the brain surface. Exiting only through the aqueduct or internal foramina is not how the plexus leaves the ventricle.'),
('7101b2d6-6599-5684-95b8-508ed3bcf680'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pathway of CSF circulation is:', '["Third → fourth directly resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral ventricles → foramen of Monro → third ventricle → aqueduct → fourth ventricle → foramina of Luschka/Magendie → subarachnoid space → arachnoid granulations → venous sinus","specific path mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Lateral → third directly without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fourth → lateral leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral ventricles → foramen of Monro → third ventricle → aqueduct → fourth ventricle → foramina of Luschka/Magendie → subarachnoid space → arachnoid granulations → venous sinus', 'CSF flows from the lateral ventricles through the foramina of Monro into the third ventricle, then down the aqueduct into the fourth ventricle, and out the foramina of Luschka and Magendie into the subarachnoid space; reabsorption occurs at the arachnoid granulations into the venous sinuses. Only this option gives the complete circuit.'),
('4b497fb4-1744-59cd-b389-e639c3df289c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Hydrocephalus is best defined as', '["Brain atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased ICP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased brain volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased CSF volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excessive accumulation of CSF in the ventricular system"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive accumulation of CSF in the ventricular system', 'Hydrocephalus is the excessive accumulation of CSF within the ventricular system, usually from impaired flow or absorption. Atrophy, reduced CSF volume, elevated brain volume, or low ICP are not the defining feature.'),
('cc6fd879-c10f-5fee-96da-490fd6d1eff6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'In communicating hydrocephalus:', '["CSF flow resulting in decreased functional activity","Obstruction within ventricular system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF can flow from ventricles to subarachnoid space; obstruction is at arachnoid granulations/CSF absorption","Obstruction at foramen of Monro without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstruction at aqueduct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF can flow from ventricles to subarachnoid space; obstruction is at arachnoid granulations/CSF absorption', 'In communicating hydrocephalus CSF can pass freely from the ventricles into the subarachnoid space, but absorption at the arachnoid granulations is impaired. Obstruction at the foramen of Monro, the aqueduct, or within the ventricular system is the non-communicating mechanism.'),
('825ebec0-0a65-56cd-a701-3f294cae60b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'In non-communicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus:', '["Overproduction resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Impaired absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal CSF circulation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","CSF flow blocked within the ventricular system (e.g., aqueduct stenosis, foramen of Monro block, fourth ventricle block)","Brain atrophy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF flow blocked within the ventricular system (e.g., aqueduct stenosis, foramen of Monro block, fourth ventricle block)', 'Obstructive hydrocephalus results from blockade of CSF flow inside the ventricular system, such as aqueductal stenosis or blockage of the fourth ventricle or foramen of Monro. Impaired absorption or overproduction involves a different site.'),
('4ccff8a3-8cb0-5b88-9ded-ec14f2650fef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Aqueductal stenosis (congenital narrowing of the aqueduct) causes', '["Non-communicating hydrocephalus with enlarged lateral and third ventricles, normal fourth","Subarachnoid enlargement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All ventricles enlarged mediated by specific receptor activation","Communicating hydrocephalus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal ventricles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-communicating hydrocephalus with enlarged lateral and third ventricles, normal fourth', 'Narrowing of the aqueduct blocks CSF before it reaches the fourth ventricle, enlarging the lateral and third ventricles while the fourth ventricle stays normal. This is a non-communicating pattern, not diffuse or communicating dilation.'),
('29230eae-a2d9-5346-bd2a-70bfe0d916c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The triad of normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) consists of:', '["Only gait resulting in decreased functional activity","Gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, cognitive impairment (dementia)","All three required mediated by specific receptor activation","Only dementia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only incontinence leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, cognitive impairment (dementia)', 'The classic NPH triad is gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and cognitive impairment. Listing only one component does not capture the full triad.'),
('46b1883c-0db0-5261-a8d1-d3f968a8be83'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'NPH gait is described as', '["Parkinsonian resulting in decreased functional activity","Ataxic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Steppage mediated by specific receptor activation","Magnetic (feet stuck to floor), wide-based, slow, difficulty initiating walking","Hemiplegic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Magnetic (feet stuck to floor), wide-based, slow, difficulty initiating walking', 'The NPH gait is magnetic (feet stuck to the floor), wide-based, and slow, with difficulty initiating steps. Parkinsonian, ataxic, steppage, and hemiplegic patterns describe other gait disorders.'),
('c36f5883-c93f-593e-9554-f79442fea93b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'In NPH, the CSF opening pressure is', '["Very high resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elevated mediated by specific receptor activation","Extremely low without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal (at time of measurement, though intermittently elevated)"]'::jsonb, 'Normal (at time of measurement, though intermittently elevated)', 'In NPH the measured opening pressure is typically normal, although pressure may be intermittently elevated. Persistently high or low pressure does not characterize NPH.'),
('3c8ee522-caa5-5821-87f5-d819461d0e79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Hydrocephalus ex vacuo refers to:', '["Brain atrophy → enlarged ventricles (not true hydrocephalus, normal ICP)","Communicating hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocked CSF absorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstructive hydrocephalus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CSF overproduction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brain atrophy → enlarged ventricles (not true hydrocephalus, normal ICP)', 'Hydrocephalus ex vacuo is passive enlargement of the ventricles from brain atrophy, with normal CSF pressure, so it is not true hydrocephalus. The other choices describe flow obstruction, impaired absorption, or overproduction.'),
('19efd826-b996-56ab-aa24-d146a78fbf1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF overproduction (a rare cause of hydrocephalus) can occur in', '["Choroid plexus papilloma (benign choroid plexus tumor)","Ependymoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glioma mediated by specific receptor activation","Astrocytoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningioma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus papilloma (benign choroid plexus tumor)', 'A choroid plexus papilloma secretes excess CSF and is the classic cause of overproduction hydrocephalus. Ependymomas, gliomas, astrocytomas, and meningiomas do not overproduce CSF.'),
('951e3fcc-8125-5838-992f-2888f995630d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Pseudotumor cerebri (idiopathic intracranial hypertension) is characterized by:', '["Increased CSF pressure with no mass/hydrocephalus; normal ventricles, no obstruction","Obstructive hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Communicating hydrocephalus mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Low pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased CSF pressure with no mass/hydrocephalus; normal ventricles, no obstruction', 'Pseudotumor cerebri shows elevated CSF opening pressure without a mass or hydrocephalus; the ventricles are normal or small with no obstruction. Obstruction, communicating dilation, and low or normal pressure are absent.'),
('2b3b2c26-b96f-551d-9c8e-2e64949cf290'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Papilledema (optic disc swelling) from increased ICP is due to', '["Optic atrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased CSF pressure in optic nerve sheath → impaired axoplasmic transport → disc swelling","Optic neuritis mediated by specific receptor activation","Retinal vein occlusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glaucoma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased CSF pressure in optic nerve sheath → impaired axoplasmic transport → disc swelling', 'Raised CSF pressure around the optic nerve sheath impairs axoplasmic transport and causes disc swelling. Optic atrophy, optic neuritis, retinal vein occlusion, and glaucoma produce optic disc abnormalities by different mechanisms.'),
('a19c44a6-1706-5481-8a71-a061a57b008f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Lumbar puncture (LP) is typically performed at', '["T12-L1 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace (below conus medullaris)","C1-C2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","S1-S2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace (below conus medullaris)', 'The spinal cord ends around L1-L2, so puncture at L3-L4 or L4-L5 stays below the conus medullaris. Higher levels risk cord damage, and C1-C2 or S1-S2 are not routine sites.'),
('66c51850-dd0b-5c88-9354-e9f1c9769953'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'LP is safe at L4-L5 because', '["CSF at this level resulting in decreased functional activity","There is less risk of infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord ends at L1-L2 in adults; cauda equina nerve roots float away","Dura ends here without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertebrae are wider leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord ends at L1-L2 in adults; cauda equina nerve roots float away', 'The conus medullaris ends at about L1-L2 in adults, and the cauda equina nerve roots float aside during puncture, so a needle at L4-L5 is safe. The other reasons do not explain the safety of this level.'),
('629556f8-b487-5721-9be9-11b50f85621e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'After measuring opening pressure, the CSF obtained by LP in adults should be:', '["Only glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Send for cell count, protein, glucose, culture, cytology, etc","Only culture mediated by specific receptor activation","Only protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cell count leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Send for cell count, protein, glucose, culture, cytology, etc', 'Lumbar CSF should be analyzed for cell count, protein, glucose, culture, and cytology among other tests. Testing only a single parameter would miss key diagnostic information.'),
('9b148a2d-8859-5f5d-9be6-8e12e03f6921'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Normal CSF values include:', '["WBC <100 resulting in decreased functional activity","Protein >100 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","WBC <5 cells/μL (all lymphocytes), protein 15-45 mg/dL, glucose 50-80 mg/dL (2/3 serum glucose)","All lymphocytes or neutrophils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose <20 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'WBC <5 cells/μL (all lymphocytes), protein 15-45 mg/dL, glucose 50-80 mg/dL (2/3 serum glucose)', 'Normal CSF has fewer than 5 WBCs per microliter (all lymphocytes), protein 15-45 mg/dL, and glucose about 50-80 mg/dL, approximately two-thirds of the serum glucose. The other limits fall outside the normal range.'),
('d7bbb62a-c533-55b0-9f9a-2ce88f7e21ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Xanthochromia in CSF indicates', '["Recent hemorrhage resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","significance mediated by specific receptor activation","Meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Old subarachnoid hemorrhage (bilirubin from lysed RBCs) — yellow/orange color"]'::jsonb, 'Old subarachnoid hemorrhage (bilirubin from lysed RBCs) — yellow/orange color', 'Xanthochromia, a yellow-orange supernatant after centrifugation, appears hours after hemorrhage as red cells lyse and bilirubin forms, marking an old subarachnoid hemorrhage. Recent hemorrhage and non-hemorrhagic conditions do not give this color.'),
('19c832ec-58ae-5fed-b043-965edd1c39da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF lymphocytic pleocytosis (elevated lymphocytes) suggests', '["Normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemorrhage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Bacterial meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viral meningitis, tuberculous meningitis, fungal meningitis"]'::jsonb, 'Viral meningitis, tuberculous meningitis, fungal meningitis', 'Lymphocytic pleocytosis is typical of viral, tuberculous, and fungal meningitis. Bacterial meningitis classically produces a neutrophil-predominant picture.'),
('b4d92460-72b7-5043-ace7-f687c91317f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF neutrophilic pleocytosis suggests', '["Tuberculous meningitis","Alzheimer disease","Viral meningitis","Multiple sclerosis","Bacterial meningitis (neutrophils)"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial meningitis (neutrophils)', 'A neutrophil-predominant CSF pleocytosis is the hallmark of acute bacterial meningitis. Viral, tuberculous, or fungal infections and demyelination typically show other cell patterns.'),
('5e4272a5-9d9f-5f63-be02-eed5456f3181'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Low CSF glucose (hypoglycorrhachia) suggests', '["Bacterial, fungal, or tuberculous meningitis; sarcoidosis; carcinomatous meningitis","Multiple sclerosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Viral meningitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Alzheimer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial, fungal, or tuberculous meningitis; sarcoidosis; carcinomatous meningitis', 'Low CSF glucose accompanies bacterial, fungal, and tuberculous meningitis, as well as sarcoidosis and carcinomatous meningitis. Viral meningitis and the other conditions usually leave CSF glucose near normal.'),
('9d709919-6d58-5dce-ac4e-9345a9d300d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF oligoclonal bands are characteristic of', '["Viral meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Guillain-Barre accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple sclerosis (intrathecal IgG synthesis)","Subarachnoid hemorrhage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple sclerosis (intrathecal IgG synthesis)', 'Oligoclonal bands reflect intrathecal IgG synthesis and are a characteristic finding in multiple sclerosis. The infectious and hemorrhagic conditions listed do not produce this pattern.'),
('08825ebf-1d65-58ac-a682-51ce636f5534'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Contraindications to lumbar puncture (LP) include', '["Increased ICP (risk of herniation), coagulopathy, infection at puncture site","Back pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Headache mediated by specific receptor activation","Fever without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neck stiffness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ICP (risk of herniation), coagulopathy, infection at puncture site', 'LP is contraindicated with raised intracranial pressure (herniation risk), coagulopathy, and infection at the puncture site. Headache, back pain, fever, and neck stiffness alone are not contraindications.'),
('e9637a31-ccb2-51da-8124-c415c10787fa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The post-LP headache is due to', '["Brain edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Vasovagal response accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased ICP mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Low CSF pressure from persistent CSF leak at puncture site (traction on meninges)"]'::jsonb, 'Low CSF pressure from persistent CSF leak at puncture site (traction on meninges)', 'Persistent CSF leak through the dural hole lowers CSF pressure and creates traction on pain-sensitive meninges, causing the post-dural puncture headache. Cerebral edema, increased ICP, vasovagal response, or infection are not the mechanism.'),
('4268f2a9-857b-53aa-a9cc-e1cc3183e337'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The blood-patch (epidural blood patch) treats', '["Brain abscess resulting in decreased functional activity","Epidural hematoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subdural hematoma mediated by specific receptor activation","Post-LP headache by sealing dural leak","Meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-LP headache by sealing dural leak', 'An epidural blood patch seals the dural puncture, stopping the CSF leak and relieving the post-LP headache. It does not treat abscess, hematomas, or meningitis.'),
('483a7483-a9e8-59c3-bc9a-2403b6714143'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Dural venous sinuses can be imaged by', '["Ultrasound resulting in decreased functional activity","MR venography or CT venography","EEG mediated by specific receptor activation","X-ray without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PET leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MR venography or CT venography', 'The dural venous sinuses are best assessed with MR venography or CT venography. Ultrasound, EEG, plain X-ray, and PET do not adequately image the sinuses.'),
('869bfb40-16d8-52a3-b196-ab6c11f47531'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Dural venous sinus thrombosis presents with', '["Headache, papilledema, seizures, focal deficits (hemorrhagic infarction)","Only seizures accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only fever mediated by specific receptor activation","symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only headache leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Headache, papilledema, seizures, focal deficits (hemorrhagic infarction)', 'Sinus thrombosis raises venous pressure and ICP, producing headache, papilledema, seizures, and hemorrhagic infarction with focal deficits. Descriptions limited to one symptom understate the presentation.'),
('929849d8-d6fe-56da-9d42-035b1277930e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cavernous sinus thrombosis presents with', '["Only headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Only fever accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fever, periorbital edema, proptosis, chemosis, CN III/IV/V/VI palsies","Only eye pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only double vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, periorbital edema, proptosis, chemosis, CN III/IV/V/VI palsies', 'Septic cavernous sinus thrombosis causes fever, periorbital edema, proptosis, chemosis, and palsies of the third, fourth, fifth, and sixth cranial nerves, which run through the sinus wall. Isolated eye symptoms alone are incomplete.'),
('c82a05b0-2fb8-5c81-a788-09943867d1f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Pneumocephalus (air in the cranial cavity) can result from', '["Seizure resulting in decreased functional activity","Spontaneous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","CSF leak, skull base fracture, or neurosurgery (also LP with air introduced)","Migraine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF leak, skull base fracture, or neurosurgery (also LP with air introduced)', 'Air enters the cranial cavity through a CSF leak, skull base fracture, or neurosurgical intervention (historically LP with air). Seizures, spontaneous onset, hypertension, and migraine do not introduce intracranial air.'),
('a43febc9-842a-5702-aada-1050cf5f26ef'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF rhinorrhea (leak from the nose) indicates', '["Allergy resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF leak from ear accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dural tear communicating with paranasal sinuses (cribriform plate fracture)","Meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sinusitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dural tear communicating with paranasal sinuses (cribriform plate fracture)', 'CSF escaping from the nose indicates a dural tear communicating with the paranasal sinuses, typically through a cribriform plate fracture. Sinusitis, allergy, and meningitis do not produce CSF rhinorrhea.'),
('595f0198-bbc6-5318-996f-78e9de232d91'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF otorrhea (leak from the ear) indicates', '["Temporal bone fracture with dural tear (middle cranial fossa)","External ear infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","significance mediated by specific receptor activation","TMJ disorder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sinusitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal bone fracture with dural tear (middle cranial fossa)', 'CSF leaking from the ear signals a temporal bone fracture with dural tear, usually of the middle cranial fossa. Ear infection and TMJ disorder do not leak CSF.'),
('3920d08b-2739-528b-8f8a-a4e73d5b52e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The dural sac (thecal sac) ends at which vertebral level?', '["S4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L3 mediated by specific receptor activation","S2 (in adults)","L2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'S2 (in adults)', 'The dural sac terminates at the S2 vertebral level in adults, below which there is no subarachnoid space. The other levels are too high or too low.'),
('1a13e712-bbaa-5604-92cf-b919b50f2132'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The epidural space in the cranium is', '["Venous-filled resulting in decreased functional activity","Fat-filled accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF-filled mediated by specific receptor activation","Potential space (between periosteum and dura) — not normally present","Real space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potential space (between periosteum and dura) — not normally present', 'In the cranium the epidural space is a potential space between the periosteum and the dura that is not normally present. It does not normally contain fat, CSF, or veins as in the vertebral canal.'),
('95c18e65-ac88-528d-84cc-855e274f8293'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Epidural hematoma (arterial) is often due to', '["Middle meningeal artery tear (temporal/parietal fracture) → lens-shaped bleed, does NOT cross suture lines","Subdural hematoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Venous sinus rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","Arterial aneurysm without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bridging vein tear leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meningeal artery tear (temporal/parietal fracture) → lens-shaped bleed, does NOT cross suture lines', 'A fracture of the temporal or parietal region lacerates the middle meningeal artery, producing a lens-shaped epidural bleed that does not cross suture lines. Subdural, venous, and aneurysm sources cause other hemorrhage types.'),
('c11c1b4d-74f6-5026-8b8c-5ded054bf3e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Subdural hematoma (venous) is often due to', '["Arachnoid granulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Bridging vein rupture (especially elderly, alcoholics) → crescent-shaped bleed crossing suture lines","Dural sinus rupture mediated by specific receptor activation","Middle meningeal artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pial artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bridging vein rupture (especially elderly, alcoholics) → crescent-shaped bleed crossing suture lines', 'Tearing of a bridging vein running from the cortex to the superior sagittal sinus, especially in the elderly and alcoholics, causes a crescent-shaped subdural bleed that crosses suture lines. Arterial and sinus sources are not the usual venous cause.'),
('f66d941d-ff72-54c1-8ec5-6fa3f00bdeb1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) is most commonly due to', '["AVM resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ruptured saccular (berry) aneurysm (especially anterior communicating, posterior communicating, MCA bifurcation)","Hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trauma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ruptured saccular (berry) aneurysm (especially anterior communicating, posterior communicating, MCA bifurcation)', 'Most non-traumatic SAH results from rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm, classically at the anterior or posterior communicating arteries or MCA bifurcation. AVMs, hypertension, trauma, and tumors are less common causes.'),
('5eae9598-3dc9-5891-af54-3025c29c3649'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Berry aneurysms are most common at', '["Anterior circulation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior circulation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertebral only mediated by specific receptor activation","Circle of Willis bifurcations (anterior communicating, posterior communicating, MCA, basilar tip)","Internal carotid only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Circle of Willis bifurcations (anterior communicating, posterior communicating, MCA, basilar tip)', 'Berry aneurysms arise preferentially at bifurcations of the circle of Willis, especially the anterior communicating, posterior communicating, MCA, and basilar tip junctions. They are not confined to one circulation or to the internal carotid.'),
('c7c5d753-1bde-5c46-acdf-e566d82f139a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'A sentinel (warning) leak from an aneurysm may present as', '["Mild headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Nausea only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sudden, severe ''thunderclap'' headache (before major rupture)","Fever without affecting the primary tissue pathway","symptoms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sudden, severe ''thunderclap'' headache (before major rupture)', 'A sentinel leak produces an abrupt, severe thunderclap headache days to weeks before major rupture. Mild headache, nausea, fever, or isolated symptoms do not characterize this warning event.'),
('e5ca3add-5031-5a96-93c1-fdbdf4ecdbfe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The CSF in subarachnoid hemorrhage is:', '["Uniformly bloody with xanthochromia (supernatant yellow) after centrifuge","Low glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Purulent mediated by specific receptor activation","Clear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uniformly bloody with xanthochromia (supernatant yellow) after centrifuge', 'In SAH the CSF is uniformly bloody in all tubes, and the centrifuged supernatant shows xanthochromia from bilirubin. Purulent, clear, or low-glucose CSF does not accompany subarachnoid bleeding.'),
('8b5c82f5-d86c-5a4d-b8b0-a857059c1375'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'A traumatic tap, compared with true SAH, shows:', '["differences resulting in decreased functional activity","Xanthochromia is immediate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RBC mediated by specific receptor activation","RBC increase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","RBC count decreases from tube 1 to tube 4; no xanthochromia if fresh (but can be confusing)"]'::jsonb, 'RBC count decreases from tube 1 to tube 4; no xanthochromia if fresh (but can be confusing)', 'In a traumatic tap the RBC count falls from the first to later tubes and a fresh sample shows no xanthochromia, whereas true SAH gives uniform blood with xanthochromia. The other options do not describe this distinguishing pattern.'),
('b227af70-a11f-5a0c-ac72-0b6fbc3af43e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Meningitis (inflammation of the pia-arachnoid) can be:', '["Only viral resulting in decreased functional activity","Only fungal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only TB mediated by specific receptor activation","Bacterial, viral, fungal, tuberculous — diagnosis by CSF analysis","Only bacterial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial, viral, fungal, tuberculous — diagnosis by CSF analysis', 'Meningitis may be bacterial, viral, fungal, or tuberculous, and CSF analysis of cells, protein, glucose, and culture distinguishes the types. No single organism category covers all cases.'),
('657c6684-eee7-5332-a0d5-458d02df833c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Meningismus (meningeal irritation signs) includes', '["Only neck stiffness resulting in decreased functional activity","Nuchal rigidity (neck stiffness), Kernig sign, Brudzinski sign","Only Kernig mediated by specific receptor activation","Only Brudzinski without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nuchal rigidity (neck stiffness), Kernig sign, Brudzinski sign', 'Meningeal irritation produces nuchal rigidity, a positive Kernig sign, and a positive Brudzinski sign. Giving just one sign fails to describe the full syndrome.'),
('b332b706-66e0-5306-ae22-6f2895bfa4e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Kernig sign (a meningeal irritation sign) is', '["Knee jerk reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain on straight leg raise accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain on leg crossing mediated by specific receptor activation","Neck stiffness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Resistance/pain on extending the knee when hip is flexed (90°)"]'::jsonb, 'Resistance/pain on extending the knee when hip is flexed (90°)', 'Kernig sign is resistance or pain on extending the knee while the hip is flexed at 90 degrees, caused by stretch of irritated meninges. Neck stiffness, straight leg raising, and reflex findings do not define it.'),
('192e18a6-f9cb-5ea7-92f3-e2a5707b0c0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Brudzinski sign (meningeal irritation):', '["Headache resulting in decreased functional activity","Extension of legs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Photophobia mediated by specific receptor activation","Involuntary flexion of hips/knees when flexing patient''s neck","Pain on eye movement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Involuntary flexion of hips/knees when flexing patient''s neck', 'In Brudzinski sign, neck flexion stretches inflamed meninges and provokes involuntary flexion of the hips and knees. This maneuver detects meningeal irritation, unlike headache, photophobia, or pain on eye movement, which are other meningism symptoms not elicited by the maneuver. Leg extension is not part of the sign.'),
('ae03a9fb-1189-5a48-b024-fa533cb164f3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Photophobia in meningitis is due to', '["Retinal edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortical irritation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN II inflammation mediated by specific receptor activation","CN III palsy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningeal irritation of CN V (ophthalmic division) → pain on light"]'::jsonb, 'Meningeal irritation of CN V (ophthalmic division) → pain on light', 'Meningeal inflammation irritates the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve, making exposure to light painful. Retinal edema, cortical irritation, or CN II/III pathology do not account for photophobia in meningitis.'),
('595b9d1e-badb-5203-8c98-ab49eb267866'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Leptomeninges (arachnoid + pia) are also known as the', '["Falx resulting in decreased functional activity","Diaphragma sellae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dura mediated by specific receptor activation","Pia-arachnoid (soft meninges) — involved in meningitis","Tentorium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pia-arachnoid (soft meninges) — involved in meningitis', 'Leptomeninges comprise the arachnoid and pia mater, together called the pia-arachnoid or soft meninges, and bear the brunt of meningeal inflammation. The dura, falx, tentorium, and diaphragma sellae are pachymeningeal (dural) structures.'),
('46b6ef1c-2b58-519d-bdff-f5a9052d4787'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pia mater is attached to the brain surface and', '["Separated from brain by subpial space resulting in decreased functional activity","Has microvilli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is keratinized mediated by specific receptor activation","Accompanies blood vessels into brain parenchyma (Virchow-Robin spaces)","Forms the BBB leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accompanies blood vessels into brain parenchyma (Virchow-Robin spaces)', 'The pia mater adheres to the brain surface and follows penetrating blood vessels into the parenchyma, creating Virchow-Robin perivascular spaces. It does not form the BBB, is not keratinized, lacks microvilli, and no true subpial cleft separates it from the brain.'),
('28fda4b3-f5f7-5cd2-9872-660e368b0a43'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Virchow-Robin (perivascular) spaces are', '["Between ventricles resulting in decreased functional activity","Between dura and arachnoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the epidural space mediated by specific receptor activation","Within the choroid plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extensions of subarachnoid space along penetrating blood vessels (surrounded by pia)"]'::jsonb, 'Extensions of subarachnoid space along penetrating blood vessels (surrounded by pia)', 'Virchow-Robin (perivascular) spaces are pia-lined extensions of the subarachnoid space that accompany penetrating vessels into the brain. They do not sit between ventricles, within the choroid plexus, in the epidural space, or between dura and arachnoid.'),
('4eb456e8-252e-56d5-8828-afb1911b00e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Choroid plexus is located in', '["Only third resulting in decreased functional activity","Only fourth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lateral ventricle (body, inferior horn), third ventricle (roof), fourth ventricle (inferior roof)","Only lateral without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All ventricles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral ventricle (body, inferior horn), third ventricle (roof), fourth ventricle (inferior roof)', 'Choroid plexus forms in the bodies and inferior (temporal) horns of the lateral ventricles, the roof of the third ventricle, and the inferior roof of the fourth ventricle. None of the single-ventricle answers is complete, and the anterior and occipital horns contain no plexus.'),
('90b6a492-ff4a-5eec-822f-11ddaf81627c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Choroid plexus of lateral ventricle projects into', '["All horns","Body and temporal horn (C-shaped)","Occipital horn","Anterior (frontal) horn only","Third ventricle only"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (frontal) horn only', 'The lateral ventricular choroid plexus is C-shaped and lies in the body, atrium, and temporal (inferior) horn. The anterior (frontal) and occipital horns contain no choroid plexus, nor does the third ventricle receive this plexus.'),
('d117afb8-a329-5a3c-b77a-c441754fdf7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The glomus of the choroid plexus is a', '["Brain nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Dural sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of the arachnoid granulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Prominent tuft at the atrium (junction body/temporal horn) — normal finding, may calcify","Cerebrospinal fluid cistern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prominent tuft at the atrium (junction body/temporal horn) — normal finding, may calcify', 'The glomus is the enlarged tuft of choroid plexus at the atrium where the body and temporal horn meet; it is a normal structure that frequently calcifies on imaging. It is not a brain nucleus, dural sinus, arachnoid granulation, or CSF cistern.'),
('095f9e1f-e741-5078-8459-1c37b9b15f29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The choroid plexus of the third ventricle is in the', '["Anterior wall resulting in decreased functional activity","Roof of third ventricle (inferior to fornix)","Lateral wall mediated by specific receptor activation","Floor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior wall leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Roof of third ventricle (inferior to fornix)', 'The tela choroidea and its plexus form the roof of the third ventricle just beneath the fornix. The anterior wall, lateral walls, floor, and posterior wall do not carry the choroid plexus.'),
('f6738d83-be81-503f-bad1-b3475357910c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The choroid plexus of the fourth ventricle is in the', '["Superior roof resulting in decreased functional activity","Inferior roof (posterior medullary velum) of fourth ventricle","Floor mediated by specific receptor activation","All aspects without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral walls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior roof (posterior medullary velum) of fourth ventricle', 'The fourth ventricular choroid plexus lies in the inferior roof formed by the tela choroidea over the posterior medullary velum. The superior roof (anterior medullary velum), floor, and lateral walls do not contain the plexus.'),
('280f229e-47b0-58e5-a930-aaace52ef538'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF secretion by choroid plexus involves', '["Energy-independent resulting in decreased functional activity","Filtration (like Bowman capsule) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple diffusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Osmosis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Active transport of Na, Cl, HCO3 into ventricles followed by water (aquaporin-1)"]'::jsonb, 'Active transport of Na, Cl, HCO3 into ventricles followed by water (aquaporin-1)', 'Choroid plexus epithelium actively transports sodium, chloride, and bicarbonate into the ventricle, with water following through aquaporin-1 channels, so CSF secretion is energy-dependent. Simple diffusion, filtration, or osmosis alone cannot generate CSF ionic composition distinct from plasma.'),
('52b23355-23f5-5faa-9805-e751b816af45'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (acetazolamide) reduce', '["ICP only resulting in decreased functional activity","BBB function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brain blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","CSF production (by inhibiting HCO3- formation in choroid plexus)","CSF absorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF production (by inhibiting HCO3- formation in choroid plexus)', 'Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the choroid plexus, reducing bicarbonate formation and thereby lowering CSF production. It does not primarily reduce CSF absorption, BBB function, brain blood flow, or act selectively on ICP alone.'),
('455efedd-9609-568e-bb65-bfe72499c9e5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The cisterns (subarachnoid) of the brain include', '["Only interpeduncular resulting in decreased functional activity","Only one large cistern accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cisterna magna mediated by specific receptor activation","Cisterna magna, pontine, interpeduncular, ambient, quadrigeminal, chiasmatic, Sylvian, etc","Only pontine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cisterna magna, pontine, interpeduncular, ambient, quadrigeminal, chiasmatic, Sylvian, etc', 'Multiple subarachnoid cisterns exist at the skull base and around the brainstem, including cisterna magna, pontine, interpeduncular, ambient, quadrigeminal, chiasmatic, and Sylvian cisterns. They form a communicating network, so a single-cistern answer is incomplete.'),
('666b526b-1cf9-52b3-a86e-59738b030036'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cisterna magna (posterior cerebellomedullary cistern) is located', '["Between pons and clivus resulting in decreased functional activity","Between midbrain and pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between medulla (anterior) and cerebellum (posterior) — below foramen of Magendie","Between cerebellum and tentorium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between cerebral peduncles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between medulla (anterior) and cerebellum (posterior) — below foramen of Magendie', 'Cisterna magna occupies the posterior fossa between the medulla anteriorly and the cerebellum posteriorly, receiving CSF through the foramen of Magendie. It is not located between pons and clivus, between midbrain and pons, or beneath the tentorium.'),
('a4009f0b-6344-5d26-85f1-fbef411caa90'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pontine cistern (prepontine) is located', '["Between pons and cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","At the cerebellar pontine angle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Around medulla mediated by specific receptor activation","Between pons and clivus (basilar artery runs here)","Around midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between pons and clivus (basilar artery runs here)', 'The prepontine (pontine) cistern lies between the anterior surface of the pons and the clivus, and the basilar artery courses within it. The pons-cerebellum junction, cerebello-pontine angle, midbrain, and medulla are distinct compartments.'),
('bb897e3d-1d48-55b9-9e94-05c072432728'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The interpeduncular cistern (basal cistern) is located', '["Between cerebral peduncles in the interpeduncular fossa (contains circle of Willis, CN III)","Between cerebellum and midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between pons and clivus mediated by specific receptor activation","Between lateral ventricles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between medulla and pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between cerebral peduncles in the interpeduncular fossa (contains circle of Willis, CN III)', 'The interpeduncular cistern fills the interpeduncular fossa between the cerebral peduncles and contains the circle of Willis and the oculomotor nerve (CN III). It is separate from the peripontine, ambient, and interventricular spaces.'),
('93fc830c-7359-5588-8a44-323b204da945'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The ambient cistern (perimesencephalic) is located', '["Between cerebellum and medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","In the Sylvian fissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Around the midbrain (lateral to it, connects interpeduncular and quadrigeminal cisterns)","Between pons and medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Between cerebellar hemispheres leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Around the midbrain (lateral to it, connects interpeduncular and quadrigeminal cisterns)', 'The ambient (perimesencephalic) cistern wraps around the lateral aspect of the midbrain and connects the interpeduncular and quadrigeminal cisterns. The Sylvian fissure, posterior fossa, and interpeduncular region each form distinct spaces.'),
('416c93c5-5307-56f8-a84e-7078f6e9debc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The quadrigeminal (superior) cistern is located', '["Around the medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Between medulla and pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Between pons and clivus mediated by specific receptor activation","At the foramen magnum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Behind the midbrain (superior colliculi), in front of the cerebellum (contains great vein of Galen, pineal)"]'::jsonb, 'Behind the midbrain (superior colliculi), in front of the cerebellum (contains great vein of Galen, pineal)', 'The quadrigeminal (superior) cistern lies behind the superior colliculi of the midbrain and in front of the cerebellum, housing the great vein of Galen and the pineal gland. It is not around the medulla, between pons and clivus, between medulla and pons, or at the foramen magnum.'),
('a488f0c0-37fc-5a50-9f7f-c9b4c92c90b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The chiasmatic cistern surrounds', '["Pineal gland resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary gland accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lamina terminalis mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic chiasm (inferior to chiasm, above pituitary)","Hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic chiasm (inferior to chiasm, above pituitary)', 'The chiasmatic cistern surrounds the optic chiasm just rostral to the pituitary stalk and above the diaphragma sellae. The pineal, lamina terminalis, hypothalamus, and pituitary gland lie in other locations.'),
('0dbd06ee-5e4d-509b-82e4-9613c5d70364'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The Sylvian (Sylvian fissure) cistern contains', '["Anterior cerebral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior cerebral artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basilar artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal carotid artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle cerebral artery (M1, M2 branches) and its perforators"]'::jsonb, 'Middle cerebral artery (M1, M2 branches) and its perforators', 'The Sylvian cistern contains the M1 and M2 segments of the middle cerebral artery along with its perforators. The anterior cerebral, posterior cerebral, basilar, and internal carotid arteries travel through different cisterns.'),
('a86aac0e-b30b-5659-8453-3151982accad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The cerebellopontine angle cistern contains', '["CN VI resulting in decreased functional activity","CN XII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN IX, X mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII, VIII (also flocculus, AICA loop, and choroid plexus)","CN V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII, VIII (also flocculus, AICA loop, and choroid plexus)', 'The cerebello-pontine angle cistern carries the facial and vestibulocochlear nerves (CN VII and VIII) together with the flocculus, the AICA loop, and a tuft of choroid plexus. Nerves VI, IX/X, and V pass through other compartments.'),
('4716f282-a56c-5e8a-8b0f-dc2ffd7f4322'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Spinal subarachnoid space is largest at', '["Cervical level resulting in decreased functional activity","Lumbar cistern (L2-S2) — contains cauda equina, site for LP","Conus medullaris mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacral level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thoracic level leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar cistern (L2-S2) — contains cauda equina, site for LP', 'The lumbar cistern extends from about L2 to S2 and is the largest part of the spinal subarachnoid space, containing the cauda equina and serving as the site for lumbar puncture. Cervical, thoracic, and sacral levels have much narrower subarachnoid spaces.'),
('6a262996-1e49-59ec-a8bb-b92d24d20bdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF pressure measurement is part of', '["Lumbar puncture opening pressure (normal 10-15 cm H2O)","MRI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","X-ray mediated by specific receptor activation","CT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ultrasound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar puncture opening pressure (normal 10-15 cm H2O)', 'Opening pressure recorded at lumbar puncture measures the CSF pressure, normally 10-15 cm H2O with the patient relaxed in the lateral decubitus position. MRI, CT, ultrasound, and plain radiography do not measure CSF pressure directly.'),
('5a14d2f8-f8fb-5f81-a576-cdf86d316eb2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Queckenstedt test (jugular compression) was used to', '["Test for meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Test for cerebral blood flow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Test for spinal block (CSF obstruction) — now rarely done (risky)","Test for vision without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Test for hearing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Test for spinal block (CSF obstruction) — now rarely done (risky)', 'The Queckenstedt test compressed the jugular veins and observed whether the lumbar CSF pressure rose; no rise indicated a spinal (CSF) block. It is rarely used today because of risk and has been replaced by MRI.'),
('aad5b14d-1b28-575d-8e8b-94c052384320'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The foramen magnum syndrome (coning) involves', '["Herniation of brainstem through tentorium resulting in decreased functional activity","Herniation of cingulate under falx accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Herniation of cerebellar tonsils through foramen magnum → medullary compression","Herniation of temporal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","herniation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of cerebellar tonsils through foramen magnum → medullary compression', 'Tonsillar herniation (coning) pushes the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum and compresses the medulla, threatening respiratory arrest. Uncal (transtentorial) and cingulate (subfalcine) herniations occur through other openings.'),
('aec79ff1-b5db-5f55-8579-5383838c4134'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Transtentorial (uncal) herniation occurs when', '["Cingulate herniates under falx resulting in decreased functional activity","Uncus/parahippocampal gyrus herniates through tentorial notch → compresses CN III, PCA, brainstem","Nothing herniates mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar tonsils herniate through foramen magnum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brainstem herniates downward leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uncus/parahippocampal gyrus herniates through tentorial notch → compresses CN III, PCA, brainstem', 'In transtentorial herniation the uncus and parahippocampal gyrus are forced through the tentorial notch, compressing CN III (blown pupil), the posterior cerebral artery, and the brainstem. Cingulate and tonsillar herniations follow other routes.'),
('8447284a-b6a5-5366-9276-2c1df109607b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Subfalcine (cingulate) herniation:', '["Brain herniation through skull resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramen magnum herniation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporal lobe herniation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cingulate gyrus herniates under falx cerebri → compresses ACA → contralateral leg weakness","Midline shift leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cingulate gyrus herniates under falx cerebri → compresses ACA → contralateral leg weakness', 'In subfalcine herniation the cingulate gyrus is displaced beneath the falx cerebri, compressing the anterior cerebral artery and producing contralateral leg weakness. Uncal, foramen magnum, and external (through-skull) herniations occur at different sites.'),
('5403146c-ab29-5b8b-b707-1510c6b79a16'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Calvarial (bone) anatomy: the pterion is where', '["Major dural sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Sutures join accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar tentorium mediated by specific receptor activation","Brainstem without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet (thin bone, over middle meningeal artery)"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet (thin bone, over middle meningeal artery)', 'At the pterion the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet; the bone here is thin and overlies the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery, making it fracture-prone. It is not a dural sinus, a suture line, or the tentorium.'),
('cf78bbc8-a43a-511c-8f90-67f789dd0f4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The internal cranial fossae are', '["Only posterior resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Anterior cranial fossa (frontal lobes), middle cranial fossa (temporal lobes, pituitary), posterior cranial fossa (cerebellum, brainstem)","All flat mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only anterior and posterior without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only middle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior cranial fossa (frontal lobes), middle cranial fossa (temporal lobes, pituitary), posterior cranial fossa (cerebellum, brainstem)', 'The internal skull base is divided into anterior (frontal lobes), middle (temporal lobes and pituitary), and posterior (cerebellum and brainstem) cranial fossae. Listing only one or two fossae is incomplete, and the floor is not flat.'),
('b21cfd53-0f91-5f26-9be5-b2724fc69504'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The cribriform plate (ethmoid bone) transmits', '["Optic nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Olfactory nerves (CN I) through foramina for sense of smell","Trigeminal nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory nerves (CN I) through foramina for sense of smell', 'Fenestrations of the cribriform plate transmit the olfactory fila (CN I) from the nasal mucosa to the olfactory bulb for smell. Optic, trigeminal, and spinal nerves leave the cranium through other foramina.'),
('635d4dfc-6d80-5998-894f-3fc34992ba92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The optic canal transmits', '["Optic nerve (CN II) and ophthalmic artery","Abducens nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trochlear nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Ophthalmic nerve (V1) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oculomotor nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (CN II) and ophthalmic artery', 'The optic canal in the lesser wing of the sphenoid transmits the optic nerve (CN II) and the ophthalmic artery. The oculomotor, trochlear, ophthalmic, and abducens nerves pass through the superior orbital fissure instead.'),
('045e1176-eada-5acf-866e-a53b8fa5a92c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The foramen rotundum transmits', '["Facial nerve","Maxillary nerve (CN V2)","Ophthalmic nerve (V1)","Mandibular nerve (V3)","Optic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (CN V2)', 'The foramen rotundum transmits the maxillary division (V2) of the trigeminal nerve into the pterygopalatine fossa. The ophthalmic division (V1) uses the superior orbital fissure and the mandibular division (V3) the foramen ovale.'),
('de7d10cf-129a-5193-954a-b8a6aa33817f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The foramen ovale transmits', '["Mandibular nerve (CN V3), accessory meningeal artery, lesser petrosal nerve (sometimes)","Maxillary nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ophthalmic nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Optic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mandibular nerve (CN V3), accessory meningeal artery, lesser petrosal nerve (sometimes)', 'The foramen ovale transmits the mandibular division (V3), the accessory meningeal artery, and sometimes the lesser petrosal nerve. The maxillary (V2) and ophthalmic (V1) nerves and the facial nerve use different foramina or canals.'),
('a228dfac-889d-5a77-8c87-98be3e5661b2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The foramen spinosum transmits', '["Emissary veins resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mandibular nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Maxillary nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle meningeal artery (and vein) and meningeal branch of CN V3"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meningeal artery (and vein) and meningeal branch of CN V3', 'The foramen spinosum transmits the middle meningeal artery and vein together with a meningeal branch of the mandibular nerve (V3). Emissary veins, the facial nerve, and the mandibular and maxillary nerves pass through other openings.'),
('820a036f-aab3-580d-9852-e1e43cfdccc1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The internal acoustic meatus (IAM) transmits', '["CN IX, X resulting in decreased functional activity","CN VII (facial), CN VIII (vestibulocochlear), and nervus intermedius (sensory root of VII)","Only CN VII mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CN VIII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN V, VI leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CN VII (facial), CN VIII (vestibulocochlear), and nervus intermedius (sensory root of VII)', 'The internal acoustic meatus carries the facial nerve (CN VII), the nervus intermedius, and the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII). Nerves IX/X exit via the jugular foramen, and nerves V and VI pass through separate foramina.'),
('97d36d20-265b-5d2e-80da-2a6fd3e876e1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The hypoglossal canal transmits', '["Cranial nerve X","Internal carotid artery","Cranial nerve XII (hypoglossal)","Cranial nerve XI","Vertebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial nerve XII (hypoglossal)', 'The hypoglossal canal in the occipital bone transmits the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII). The vagus (CN X) and spinal accessory (CN XI) nerves exit through the jugular foramen, while the vertebral artery ascends through the foramen magnum.'),
('f951676b-7292-5ae2-9acb-ebcbc7a1731d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The foramen magnum transmits', '["Medulla oblongata, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of CN XI, and meninges","Only CN XI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CSF mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vertebral arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata, vertebral arteries, spinal roots of CN XI, and meninges', 'The foramen magnum transmits the junction of medulla and spinal cord, the vertebral arteries, the spinal roots of CN XI, and the meninges. No single element (medulla, CSF, vertebral arteries, or CN XI) travels through it alone.'),
('96ed9a8d-0c58-5e34-b434-2a583d3342c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The carotid canal transmits', '["Facial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal jugular vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External carotid artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal carotid artery and sympathetic nerve plexus (caroticotympanic nerves)","Optic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid artery and sympathetic nerve plexus (caroticotympanic nerves)', 'The carotid canal in the petrous temporal bone transmits the internal carotid artery and its sympathetic nerve plexus. The facial nerve exits via the stylomastoid foramen, the internal jugular vein via the jugular foramen, and the optic nerve is intracranial.'),
('f3f00c52-71a6-5a06-bfb3-9660718f8ffd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pterygopalatine fossa contains', '["Facial nerve resulting in decreased functional activity","Maxillary nerve (V2), pterygopalatine ganglion, and maxillary artery branches","Mandibular nerve mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vessels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maxillary nerve (V2), pterygopalatine ganglion, and maxillary artery branches', 'The pterygopalatine fossa contains the maxillary nerve (V2), the pterygopalatine ganglion, and branches of the maxillary artery. The facial nerve, mandibular nerve, and optic nerve do not course through this fossa.'),
('f9987f8a-79f1-55df-a8f5-e0c67e8137d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pterion is a neurosurgical landmark for', '["Anterior cerebral artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Venous sinuses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Middle cerebral artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior cerebral artery without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Middle meningeal artery (anterior branch) — site for burr hole for epidural hematoma"]'::jsonb, 'Middle meningeal artery (anterior branch) — site for burr hole for epidural hematoma', 'The pterion overlies the anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery, making it the standard landmark for a burr hole to drain an extradural (epidural) hematoma. It does not overlie the ACA, MCA, PCA, or major dural venous sinuses.'),
('aadf6082-71bb-554e-b041-41288ce12357'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Subdural hematoma on CT appears as a', '["Crescent-shaped bleed crossing suture lines (but not crossing midline)","Biconvex lens accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Round mass mediated by specific receptor activation","Cylindrical without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Linear density leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crescent-shaped bleed crossing suture lines (but not crossing midline)', 'A subdural hematoma spreads over the convexity and appears crescent-shaped on CT; it can cross suture lines but is limited by dural reflections at the falx, so it does not cross the midsagittal plane. Biconvex (lentiform) shape instead suggests an epidural hematoma.'),
('60969e1e-1360-5ef1-a19e-c8c1844fb70e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Epidural hematoma on CT appears as a', '["Biconvex (lentiform) hyperdensity, limited by sutures, may cross midline","Ovoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crescentic mediated by specific receptor activation","Round without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Irregular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Biconvex (lentiform) hyperdensity, limited by sutures, may cross midline', 'An epidural hematoma is confined between the skull and the stripped dura, producing a biconvex (lentiform) hyperdensity that stops at suture lines yet may cross the midline. Crescentic morphology on CT suggests a subdural bleed.'),
('37f0432b-129e-52a8-bcba-7f7bc6336e93'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF rhinorrhea test (beta-2 transferrin) confirms', '["CSF leak (β-2 transferrin is specific for CSF vs nasal mucus)","Meningitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skull fracture mediated by specific receptor activation","Sinusitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head injury leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF leak (β-2 transferrin is specific for CSF vs nasal mucus)', 'Beta-2 transferrin is produced only by neuraminidase activity in CSF, so its presence in nasal discharge specifically confirms a CSF leak. Meningitis, sinusitis, a skull fracture, or head injury alone is not proven by this test.'),
('f0968565-1072-5403-a73b-ee59f9141872'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Pneumatoceles (air-filled cavities in bones) are most common in', '["Frontal bone resulting in decreased functional activity","Temporal bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mastoid and paranasal sinuses (adjacent to cranial fossa)","Sphenoid bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Occipital bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mastoid and paranasal sinuses (adjacent to cranial fossa)', 'Pneumatization is most prominent in the mastoid process and the paranasal sinuses, which lie adjacent to the cranial fossae. The flat frontal, temporal, sphenoid, and occipital bones are much less pneumatized.'),
('3ec963db-44ca-5dd5-9f1e-7a9b6a20f790'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Mastoid process pneumatization can allow infection to spread to', '["Anterior cranial fossa resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal sinus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Orbit mediated by specific receptor activation","Maxillary sinus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sigmoid sinus and posterior cranial fossa (mastoiditis → venous sinus thrombosis/brain abscess)"]'::jsonb, 'Sigmoid sinus and posterior cranial fossa (mastoiditis → venous sinus thrombosis/brain abscess)', 'Infection in the pneumatized mastoid can erode into the sigmoid sinus, causing venous sinus thrombosis, and into the posterior cranial fossa, causing abscess or meningitis. The anterior fossa, orbit, and frontal or maxillary sinuses are not the direct path.'),
('f5c8793d-76d7-5232-a92a-6af6ded0079b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The paranasal sinuses (frontal, ethmoid, sphenoid, maxillary) can be entry points for', '["Stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Dementia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brain tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Intracranial infection (sinusitis → subdural empyema, brain abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis)","Epilepsy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intracranial infection (sinusitis → subdural empyema, brain abscess, cavernous sinus thrombosis)', 'Paranasal sinus infection can spread intracranially to cause subdural empyema, brain abscess, or cavernous sinus thrombosis. Stroke, dementia, brain tumor, and epilepsy do not arise directly from sinus infection.'),
('96601f9f-8592-5691-907e-e9e0bf1710a5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The dural venous sinus that is most frequently thrombosed is', '["Cavernous sinus","Sigmoid sinus","Straight sinus","Superior sagittal sinus","Transverse sinus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior sagittal sinus', 'The superior sagittal sinus is the dural venous sinus most commonly thrombosed, presenting with headache, seizures, papilledema, and raised ICP. The cavernous, sigmoid, straight, and transverse sinuses are thrombosed less often.'),
('2b34a7e6-e5cb-5c49-a919-a81f04c9ccca'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CT venography (CTV) or MR venography (MRV) is used to', '["Diagnose stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Evaluate ICP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diagnose dural venous sinus thrombosis (empty delta sign in superior sagittal)","Diagnose brain tumors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnose meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diagnose dural venous sinus thrombosis (empty delta sign in superior sagittal)', 'CT or MR venography directly images thrombus within the dural venous sinuses, such as the empty delta sign seen in superior sagittal sinus thrombosis. These studies are not used primarily to diagnose stroke, brain tumors, or meningitis.'),
('202764c8-b5d8-52bb-8470-ab26ef0932a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Treatment of dural venous sinus thrombosis includes', '["Anticoagulation (heparin) even in presence of hemorrhagic infarction","Steroids only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antibiotics only mediated by specific receptor activation","treatment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Surgery only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anticoagulation (heparin) even in presence of hemorrhagic infarction', 'Anticoagulation with heparin is the mainstay of treatment for dural venous sinus thrombosis and is given even when small hemorrhagic infarcts are present. Steroids, antibiotics, surgery alone, or ''treatment'' without specification are not the primary therapy.'),
('677bd419-29ca-5dea-8dd6-498ecc7e5b46'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Subarachnoid hemorrhage grading (Hunt and Hess) and (Fisher CT) predict', '["Prognosis for SAH; Fisher grade based on thickness of cisternal blood on CT","Only mortality accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vasospasm risk mediated by specific receptor activation","Only rebleed risk without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only need for surgery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prognosis for SAH; Fisher grade based on thickness of cisternal blood on CT', 'The Hunt and Hess scale grades clinical severity and the Fisher (CT) scale grades the thickness of cisternal blood after subarachnoid hemorrhage; together they predict outcome, including the risk of vasospasm. No single element such as mortality, vasospasm, rebleed, or surgery need alone predicts the full picture.'),
('a347a01b-d79b-50ae-838c-7bd4dd6abbe3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF shunting (ventriculoperitoneal, VP) is used to treat', '["Stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrocephalus (obstructive or communicating) by diverting CSF to peritoneal cavity","Tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrocephalus (obstructive or communicating) by diverting CSF to peritoneal cavity', 'A ventriculoperitoneal shunt redirects excess CSF from the ventricles into the peritoneal cavity, where it is absorbed. This directly treats both obstructive and communicating hydrocephalus, whereas stroke, tumor, trauma, and infection are managed by other specific therapies.'),
('4ae4851b-754a-5933-a0ad-e083f9f8c240'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'VP shunt complications include', '["Infection, obstructions, overdrainage (slit ventricle), subdural hematoma, abdominal pseudocyst","Only bleeding accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only overdrainage mediated by specific receptor activation","Only infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Infection, obstructions, overdrainage (slit ventricle), subdural hematoma, abdominal pseudocyst', 'Shunt failure can occur from mechanical obstruction, CSF infection, overdrainage producing slit ventricles or subdural hematomas, and abdominal pseudocysts. A single complication category is incomplete because multiple mechanisms coexist.'),
('b7292b40-0325-5ed9-887d-a82789b5eb92'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'ICP monitoring (intraparenchymal or ventricular) is used in', '["All head injuries resulting in decreased functional activity","Routine headaches accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stroke mediated by specific receptor activation","Tumor only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Severe traumatic brain injury, hydrocephalus, idiopathic intracranial hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Severe traumatic brain injury, hydrocephalus, idiopathic intracranial hypertension', 'Direct ICP monitoring guides management in severe traumatic brain injury, hydrocephalus, and idiopathic intracranial hypertension where intracranial pressure control determines outcome. It is not needed for routine headaches and is reserved for selected conditions rather than all head injuries.'),
('c088c53a-0340-5f49-bb95-63c9c75c3f7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) formula:', '["CPP = CVP - ICP","CPP = MAP - ICP","CPP = CBF / ICP","CPP = ICP - MAP","CPP = MAP + ICP"]'::jsonb, 'CPP = MAP - ICP', 'Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure, reflecting the pressure driving blood through the brain. Other formulas omit ICP, reverse the subtraction, or mix unrelated parameters.'),
('da589aa3-5825-5c74-afc9-f62c143deecb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Raised ICP (intracranial hypertension) causes', '["Seizures only resulting in decreased functional activity","Cushing response: hypertension, bradycardia, irregular respirations (late sign)","Headache only mediated by specific receptor activation","Tachycardia, hypotension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fever, tachycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cushing response: hypertension, bradycardia, irregular respirations (late sign)', 'The Cushing response comprises hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations and is a late sign of dangerously raised ICP. Tachycardia with hypotension and fever are not characteristic of the Cushing response.'),
('7a9547ed-ccc9-536f-92cb-4f6db8fb727c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Monro-Kellie doctrine states that', '["CSF pressure = venous pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","Volume of blood constant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Skull is rigid; increase in one intracranial component (blood, CSF, brain) must be compensated by decrease in others","Brain incompressible without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood flow constant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Skull is rigid; increase in one intracranial component (blood, CSF, brain) must be compensated by decrease in others', 'Because the skull is a fixed-volume chamber, any rise in one intracranial content (blood, CSF, or brain) must be balanced by a fall in the others to keep total volume constant. Equating CSF and venous pressure or fixing any single volume is only a partial, inaccurate version.'),
('6e4537f8-c7b3-53f5-af0e-d827f61e96db'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Initial compensation for increased ICP includes', '["Bone thinning resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Displacement of CSF from cranium to spinal subarachnoid, compression of low-pressure veins","Decreased production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brain shrinkage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Displacement of CSF from cranium to spinal subarachnoid, compression of low-pressure veins', 'At the onset of raised ICP, compliant low-pressure structures yield first: CSF is pushed from the cranial into the spinal subarachnoid space and cerebral veins are compressed. These adjustments occur before bone, absorption, production, or brain volume change.'),
('386dc07f-1106-5d19-a028-03e4d2cc12c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'If ICP compensation exhausted, small additional volume causes', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Large increase in ICP (exponential pressure-volume curve) → herniation risk","change mediated by specific receptor activation","Stable ICP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased ICP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Large increase in ICP (exponential pressure-volume curve) → herniation risk', 'Once compensatory reserves are depleted, the pressure-volume curve becomes steeply exponential so even a small added volume sharply raises ICP and risks herniation. Bradycardia alone does not describe the volume-pressure relationship.'),
('46c786aa-1279-5e3b-b4a3-902581a797bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Osmotic therapy (mannitol, hypertonic saline) for raised ICP', '["Increases brain volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Creates osmotic gradient → draws water from brain → reduces brain volume/ICP","Decreases brain blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases CSF absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreases CSF production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Creates osmotic gradient → draws water from brain → reduces brain volume/ICP', 'Mannitol and hypertonic saline raise plasma osmolality, pulling water out of the edematous brain and lowering intracranial volume and pressure. Their effect relies on an osmotic gradient rather than on altering blood flow, CSF absorption, or CSF production.'),
('d30edd8c-56aa-5273-aa28-32f94a45b5c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Hyperventilation (short-term) reduces ICP by', '["Directly reducing brain volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreasing CSF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreasing BP mediated by specific receptor activation","Increasing oxygen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causing cerebral vasoconstriction (↓PaCO2) → ↓cerebral blood volume → ↓ICP"]'::jsonb, 'Causing cerebral vasoconstriction (↓PaCO2) → ↓cerebral blood volume → ↓ICP', 'Hypocapnia from hyperventilation constricts cerebral arterioles, shrinking cerebral blood volume and lowering ICP. It does not directly shrink brain tissue, reduce CSF, lower blood pressure, or raise oxygen delivery.'),
('6e393376-b978-5ca5-8de1-b9aeb8fe3ac3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Head elevation (30 degrees) reduces ICP by', '["Increasing CSF absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Reducing blood pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All of these mediated by specific receptor activation","Improving cerebral venous drainage (decreased jugular venous pressure)","Decreasing brain metabolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Improving cerebral venous drainage (decreased jugular venous pressure)', 'Elevating the head 30 degrees facilitates venous return from the cerebral sinuses and lowers jugular venous pressure, reducing intracranial blood volume and ICP. It does not act by boosting CSF absorption, lowering systemic blood pressure, or suppressing metabolism.'),
('51f2a7f2-3495-5d4e-902f-e44696a3a5d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Normal ICP in adults (supine) is', '["0-5 mmHg","5-15 mmHg","15-25 mmHg","25-40 mmHg",">40 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '5-15 mmHg', 'Normal adult supine intracranial pressure ranges from 5 to 15 mmHg. Values above this range indicate intracranial hypertension.'),
('85199c5b-10da-5ab9-8f1c-ffaed39b98d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Hypothermia reduces ICP by', '["Reducing cerebral metabolism (CMRO2) → ↓CBF → ↓cerebral blood volume → ↓ICP","Vasoconstriction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bradycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Shunting blood to core without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All of these leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing cerebral metabolism (CMRO2) → ↓CBF → ↓cerebral blood volume → ↓ICP', 'Hypothermia lowers cerebral metabolic demand, which reduces cerebral blood flow and volume and consequently ICP. The isolated findings of vasoconstriction, bradycardia, or core blood shunting do not explain the full mechanism.'),
('31f374a3-3c21-5502-8734-4f461c0f7061'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Decompressive craniectomy removes part of skull to', '["Provide space for swollen brain (intractable raised ICP)","Prevent infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allow brain herniation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cure brain injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Permanently treat ICP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Provide space for swollen brain (intractable raised ICP)', 'Removing a bone flap creates extra space so a swollen brain can expand without catastrophic pressure elevation. It does not prevent infection, promote herniation, or cure the underlying injury by itself.'),
('d829dea9-0ef6-5d1c-ab7e-aeeb92c016a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Lumbar drain (external lumbar drain) allows', '["Continuous CSF drainage (monitoring and treatment of elevated ICP, CSF leaks)","CSF analysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF sampling mediated by specific receptor activation","CSF circulation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Continuous CSF drainage (monitoring and treatment of elevated ICP, CSF leaks)', 'An external lumbar drain provides continuous CSF outflow, used to manage raised ICP and repair CSF leaks. Simple analysis or episodic sampling do not capture its ongoing drainage role.'),
('0181a8fc-e02f-5c13-98b2-3ccb52b4384a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The ligamentum flavum (yellow ligament) connects', '["Spinous processes resulting in decreased functional activity","Laminae of adjacent vertebrae (LP needle passes through it before entering epidural space)","Pedicles mediated by specific receptor activation","Transverse processes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vertebral bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laminae of adjacent vertebrae (LP needle passes through it before entering epidural space)', 'The ligamentum flavum spans the laminae of adjacent vertebrae and is the resilient structure the lumbar puncture needle penetrates before reaching the epidural space. Supraspinous and interspinous ligaments, by contrast, attach to spinous processes.'),
('201dd1d8-72c3-57da-bc0f-175d940c0d21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The interspinous ligament is between', '["Transverse processes resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertebral bodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinous processes (the next structure after supraspinous ligament during LP)","Pedicles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Laminae leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinous processes (the next structure after supraspinous ligament during LP)', 'The interspinous ligament runs between adjacent spinous processes and, during a lumbar puncture, is be encountered after the supraspinous ligament. The ligamentum flavum connects laminae, not spinous processes.'),
('3740e25c-e38d-55b0-b4ed-157c1fdf32d4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF circulation is influenced by', '["Pulsations of cerebral arteries (arterial pulse wave in choroid plexus helps CSF flow)","forces accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Respiration only mediated by specific receptor activation","Posture only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac cycle only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulsations of cerebral arteries (arterial pulse wave in choroid plexus helps CSF flow)', 'The arterial pulse wave transmitted through the choroid plexus produces rhythmic CSF motion that drives its circulation. Attributing flow to respiration, posture, or cardiac cycle alone is incomplete because the choroidal pulse is the principal driver.'),
('b53149c8-8aa5-59db-b357-1a586f8ff15b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Choroid plexus epithelium has tight junctions that form', '["Dural barrier resulting in decreased functional activity","BBB (blood-brain) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid barrier mediated by specific receptor activation","barrier without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood-CSF barrier (between choroidal epithelium and CSF)"]'::jsonb, 'Blood-CSF barrier (between choroidal epithelium and CSF)', 'Tight junctions between choroid plexus epithelial cells seal the interface between blood and CSF, creating the blood-CSF barrier. The blood-brain barrier instead resides at cerebral capillary endothelium, which helps distinguish the two.'),
('9de1e50a-e9f8-5421-beaf-f64378607f05'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The subcommissural organ (SCO) is a circumventricular organ that secretes', '["CRH resulting in decreased functional activity","Dopamine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endorphins mediated by specific receptor activation","CSF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glycoproteins into CSF (involved in aqueduct patency)"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoproteins into CSF (involved in aqueduct patency)', 'The subcommissural organ releases glycoproteins into the CSF that help maintain patency of the cerebral aqueduct. It does not secrete CRH, dopamine, or endorphins, and CSF is the medium it secretes into rather than its product.'),
('4fa42131-7059-5ea0-be9f-8ec8c8c862a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The tela choroidea is a double layer of pia mater that', '["Separates hemispheres resulting in decreased functional activity","Forms the roof of third and fourth ventricles, invaginates to form choroid plexus","Covers the brain mediated by specific receptor activation","Lines the ventricles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Forms dura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Forms the roof of third and fourth ventricles, invaginates to form choroid plexus', 'The tela choroidea, derived from pia mater, roofs the third and fourth ventricles and folds inward to carry the vessels that give rise to the choroid plexus. It neither separates hemispheres nor forms the dura.'),
('d2ffb723-bb5e-57ac-be0d-171a5bdcd662'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The uvula of cerebellum and the nodulus form the', '["Deep nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellar hemispheres accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uvulonodular lobe (vestibulocerebellum) — part of flocculonodular lobe","Posterior lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vermis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uvulonodular lobe (vestibulocerebellum) — part of flocculonodular lobe', 'The uvula and nodulus together make up the uvulonodular lobule, the vestibular part of the cerebellum within the flocculonodular lobe. They form neither deep nuclei, hemispheres, posterior lobe, nor vermis as a whole.'),
('e74b9350-2040-5a95-8632-5c3e6bf72dcc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The obex is the', '["Opening of aqueduct resulting in decreased functional activity","Foramen of Monro accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foramen of Magendie mediated by specific receptor activation","Caudal angle of fourth ventricle (where central canal begins) — anatomical landmark in medulla","Rostral end of fourth ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Caudal angle of fourth ventricle (where central canal begins) — anatomical landmark in medulla', 'The obex is the V-shaped caudal apex of the fourth ventricle in the medulla, where the central canal begins. The aqueduct, Foramen of Monro, and Foramen of Magendie are distinct openings at other sites.'),
('d84e4279-bc34-550f-bc8b-acce568540d9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The fourth ventricle roof is formed by', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior medullary velum (rostral), inferior medullary velum (caudal), and tela choroidea","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Tentorium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior medullary velum (rostral), inferior medullary velum (caudal), and tela choroidea', 'The fourth ventricle roof is composed of the superior and inferior medullary vela and the intervening tela choroidea. The cerebellum, pons, medulla, and tentorium form adjacent structures rather than the roof.'),
('e00b495a-a7b9-5410-8a0c-4b73e244346b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The superior medullary velum is a thin layer of white matter between', '["Pons resulting in decreased functional activity","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellar peduncles (forms part of fourth ventricle roof)","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colliculi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar peduncles (forms part of fourth ventricle roof)', 'The superior medullary velum spans between the superior cerebellar peduncles and contributes to the rostral roof of the fourth ventricle. It is not positioned between pontine, medullary, thalamic, or collicular structures.'),
('2a04681e-94b8-55b7-ba55-51ae1e386fd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The inferior medullary velum is a thin layer of white matter between', '["ICP and MCP resulting in decreased functional activity","Tentorium and cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medulla and pons mediated by specific receptor activation","SCP and MCP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar peduncles and nodulus (part of fourth ventricle roof)"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar peduncles and nodulus (part of fourth ventricle roof)', 'The inferior medullary velum lies between the cerebellar peduncles and the nodulus, forming part of the caudal fourth ventricle roof. The listed peduncle combinations do not give its true attachments.'),
('bd1b1ef0-7db6-5d18-b574-395250a043e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The cerebellomedullary fissure is the space between', '["Cerebellum and pons","Pons and medulla","Cerebellum and medulla (where PICA loops)","Dura and arachnoid","Midbrain and cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum and medulla (where PICA loops)', 'The cerebellomedullary fissure is the cleft between the cerebellum and medulla, within which the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) courses. The other pairings do not bound this fissure.'),
('29b9f986-de98-51fa-95a2-949550a915fe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF circulation can be blocked by', '["Only blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor, cyst, aqueduct stenosis, blood clot, infection (ventriculitis)","Only cysts mediated by specific receptor activation","Only tumors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor, cyst, aqueduct stenosis, blood clot, infection (ventriculitis)', 'Obstructive hydrocephalus can be caused by tumors, cysts, aqueduct stenosis, intraventricular blood, or ventriculitis. Limiting the cause to a single category ignores the multiple common obstructing lesions.'),
('3e3b6412-e4be-5482-98a5-6d52339f71da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Colloid cyst of third ventricle typically presents with', '["symptoms resulting in decreased functional activity","Constant headache accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only nausea mediated by specific receptor activation","Only diplopia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intermittent obstructive hydrocephalus (positional headaches, with risk of sudden death)"]'::jsonb, 'Intermittent obstructive hydrocephalus (positional headaches, with risk of sudden death)', 'A colloid cyst at the foramen of Monro intermittently obstructs CSF outflow, producing positional headaches and repeated attacks of obstructive hydrocephalus, with a danger of sudden neurological death. Constant headache, nausea, or diplopia alone do not describe its alternating obstruction.'),
('f6112e19-2e82-572c-ac3e-f0d1b65fe4b4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The ventricular trigone (atrium) is where', '["All ventricles meet resulting in decreased functional activity","Body, posterior (occipital), and temporal (inferior) horns of lateral ventricle meet","Lateral ventricle meets fourth mediated by specific receptor activation","Body meets third ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior horn meets body leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Body, posterior (occipital), and temporal (inferior) horns of lateral ventricle meet', 'The trigone is the junction within the lateral ventricle where the body, posterior (occipital) horn, and temporal (inferior) horn converge. It does not connect different ventricles, and the anterior horn does not participate.'),
('ce4d4f92-b69c-51bc-9c7b-2779aa78ff20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The calcar avis is a bulge in the medial wall of the', '["Third ventricle resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal horn accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aqueduct mediated by specific receptor activation","Temporal horn without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posterior (occipital) horn of lateral ventricle (from calcarine sulcus)"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior (occipital) horn of lateral ventricle (from calcarine sulcus)', 'The calcar avis is an inward bulge on the medial wall of the posterior (occipital) horn produced by the calcarine sulcus. It is not found in the third ventricle, frontal horn, aqueduct, or temporal horn.'),
('925141f2-67d6-5268-9e7b-cbb08d5b19f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The collateral eminence (or trigone) is a bulge in the floor of', '["Occipital horn resulting in decreased functional activity","Third ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Frontal horn mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior (temporal) horn of lateral ventricle (from collateral sulcus)","Body of ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior (temporal) horn of lateral ventricle (from collateral sulcus)', 'The collateral eminence is an elevation in the floor of the inferior (temporal) horn of the lateral ventricle, caused by the collateral sulcus. It does not bulge into the occipital horn, third ventricle, frontal horn, or ventricular body.'),
('d8f52c10-16b3-5dba-948f-a33f8ce8831e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The striae terminalis is a thin band of white matter in the floor of the', '["Fourth ventricle resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Third ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Body of lateral ventricle (at junction of thalamus and caudate nucleus) — contains stria terminalis (amygdala output)","Aqueduct without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior horn leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Body of lateral ventricle (at junction of thalamus and caudate nucleus) — contains stria terminalis (amygdala output)', 'The stria terminalis lies in the floor of the body of the lateral ventricle at the groove between the caudate nucleus and the thalamus, carrying fibers from the amygdala. The fourth ventricle, third ventricle, aqueduct, and anterior horn do not contain it.'),
('0e8b3875-c79e-57b0-9790-6d37c20e12de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The thalamostriate vein runs in the', '["Striothalamic groove (visible on venogram as ''venous angle'' at foramen of Monro)","Aqueduct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior horn mediated by specific receptor activation","Third ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Temporal horn leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Striothalamic groove (visible on venogram as ''venous angle'' at foramen of Monro)', 'The thalamostriate vein courses in the striothalamic groove between the caudate nucleus and thalamus and, with the internal cerebral vein, forms the venous angle at the foramen of Monro on venograms. The aqueduct, posterior horn, third ventricle, and temporal horn do not contain it.'),
('c646a3e2-49e3-526d-8eab-ab079f69a34c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The septum pellucidum separates', '["Frontal and parietal lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus and hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caudate and putamen mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar hemispheres without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior horns of lateral ventricles (two-layered membrane)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior horns of lateral ventricles (two-layered membrane)', 'The septum pellucidum is a two-layered membrane that separates the anterior horns of the lateral ventricles. It does not divide lobes, the diencephalon, basal ganglia nuclei, or cerebellum.'),
('791cc6f5-eb86-53c3-8ada-ca6c33667e12'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cavum septum pellucidum is', '["A normal variant (CSF-filled space between leaves of septum pellucidum) — usually closes by birth","Hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Brain tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","significance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A normal variant (CSF-filled space between leaves of septum pellucidum) — usually closes by birth', 'The cavum septum pellucidum is a CSF-filled gap between the two leaves of the septum pellucidum that usually closes after birth and is generally an asymptomatic normal variant. It is not hydrocephalus, a neoplastic cyst, or a tumor.'),
('4cb07b4d-8a5d-527f-ba23-00daef215310'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cavum vergae is', '["Choroid cyst resulting in decreased functional activity","Nothing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subarachnoid cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Extension of cavum septum pellucidum posteriorly (into fornix)","Dandy-Walker variant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of cavum septum pellucidum posteriorly (into fornix)', 'The cavum vergae is the posterior continuation of the cavum septum pellucidum that extends into the region of the fornix. It is not a choroid cyst, subarachnoid cyst, or Dandy-Walker malformation.'),
('553b7958-03c6-5103-8d0c-45a967fa4024'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The velum interpositum is a space between', '["Fornix resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aqueduct mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Third and fourth ventricles without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Splenium of corpus callosum and roof of third ventricle (contains internal cerebral veins, posterior choroidal arteries)"]'::jsonb, 'Splenium of corpus callosum and roof of third ventricle (contains internal cerebral veins, posterior choroidal arteries)', 'The velum interpositum lies between the splenium of the corpus callosum and the roof of the third ventricle, housing the internal cerebral veins and posterior choroidal arteries. It does not sit between the fornix and lateral ventricles, the aqueduct, or the third and fourth ventricles since the fourth ventricle is outside its boundaries.'),
('db4f8e04-684e-5fee-ac57-cb47ceb00ebb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Arachnoid cysts are', '["Congenital CSF-filled cysts (within arachnoid), usually benign","Hemorrhages accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Vascular malformations without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tumors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital CSF-filled cysts (within arachnoid), usually benign', 'Arachnoid cysts are congenital, CSF-filled sacs that develop within the arachnoid mater and are generally benign. They are not hemorrhage, infection, vascular malformation, or neoplasia.'),
('df0c7fd1-f93a-5cfe-9b21-8017e946b33b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is disrupted in', '["Brain tumors (vasogenic edema, contrast enhancement), stroke, infection, multiple sclerosis","Benign tumors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal brain mediated by specific receptor activation","All conditions without affecting the primary tissue pathway","conditions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brain tumors (vasogenic edema, contrast enhancement), stroke, infection, multiple sclerosis', 'The blood-brain barrier breaks down in brain tumors, stroke, intracranial infection, and multiple sclerosis, producing vasogenic edema and contrast enhancement on imaging. A normal brain retains an intact barrier, and these pathologies rather than all conditions cause its disruption.'),
('d19b06f6-0475-5bf0-aea7-beafe091e3cf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Vasogenic edema (BBB disruption) — water accumulates in', '["Both resulting in decreased functional activity","Neither accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF spaces mediated by specific receptor activation","Intracellular space (gray matter) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extracellular space (white matter) — finger-like pattern, responds to steroids"]'::jsonb, 'Extracellular space (white matter) — finger-like pattern, responds to steroids', 'Vasogenic edema results from blood-brain barrier breakdown, leaking fluid into the extracellular space, predominantly white matter, with a finger-like pattern that responds to steroids. It does not collect inside cells, in gray matter, or within CSF spaces.'),
('88cb451d-4b6d-5784-9deb-35a5ad014479'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cytotoxic edema (cell swelling) — water accumulates in', '["CSF only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Both accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intracellular space (due to ATP failure, Na/K pump → cell swelling; gray and white) — seen in ischemic stroke","Extracellular space without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neither leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intracellular space (due to ATP failure, Na/K pump → cell swelling; gray and white) — seen in ischemic stroke', 'Cytotoxic edema follows energy failure that halts the sodium-potassium pump, so water shifts into cells, swelling both gray and white matter, as seen in ischemic stroke. It involves the intracellular rather than the extracellular space or CSF.'),
('bde0ad90-3f4c-5322-9dba-3ab502d7deeb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'In cytotoxic edema, diffusion-weighted MRI shows', '["High signal (bright) on DWI, low ADC (restricted diffusion) — ischemic cells","Low DWI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High ADC mediated by specific receptor activation","Perfusion change only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","changes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High signal (bright) on DWI, low ADC (restricted diffusion) — ischemic cells', 'Restricted water diffusion within swollen cells produces high signal on DWI and low signal on ADC maps, the hallmark of cytotoxic edema in early ischemia. Low DWI, high ADC, or perfusion-only findings do not describe restricted diffusion.'),
('8f2846a6-fc63-5841-b2d2-fcd00ccf7dc7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Hydrocephalus in CT/MRI:', '["Small ventricles resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular enlargement (ballooning), periventricular lucency (transependymal edema), thinning of corpus callosum","Cortical atrophy only mediated by specific receptor activation","changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal ventricles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular enlargement (ballooning), periventricular lucency (transependymal edema), thinning of corpus callosum', 'Hydrocephalus appears on CT/MRI as ballooned ventricles, periventricular lucency from transependymal CSF absorption, and thinning of the corpus callosum. Small or normal ventricles and isolated cortical atrophy point to other diagnoses.'),
('0125f8a1-ae35-5422-9ea0-9471d46cbf2b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'In hydrocephalus vs atrophy (ex vacuo):', '["Hydrocephalus has sulcal change resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrocephalus = ventricles enlarged with effaced sulci; Atrophy = enlarged ventricles + widened sulci","difference mediated by specific receptor activation","Hydrocephalus has small ventricles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrophy has small ventricles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrocephalus = ventricles enlarged with effaced sulci; Atrophy = enlarged ventricles + widened sulci', 'In hydrocephalus the ventricles enlarge under pressure while sulci are effaced, whereas in ex vacuo atrophy the ventricles enlarge because brain tissue is lost and sulci are widened. The presence of widened sulci distinguishes atrophy from hydrocephalus.'),
('80737b49-61c5-5238-a5c8-10566824da7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Transependymal CSF flow (edema) on CT/MRI appears as', '["Periventricular hypodensity (CT) or T2 hyperintensity (MRI) — especially at frontal/occipital horns — indicates active hydrocephalus","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Demyelination mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal finding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Periventricular hypodensity (CT) or T2 hyperintensity (MRI) — especially at frontal/occipital horns — indicates active hydrocephalus', 'Forced CSF across the ependymal lining produces a periventricular rim of hypodensity on CT and T2 hyperintensity on MRI, most evident around the frontal and occipital horns, and signals active hydrocephalus. Tumor, demyelination, and infection have different patterns, and it is not a normal finding.'),
('cafaef19-907d-5064-acea-2d8dbb457973'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The tentorium cerebelli can be imaged on', '["CT only resulting in decreased functional activity","X-ray accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MRI (T2) as a dark line separating posterior fossa from supratentorial compartment","Ultrasound without affecting the primary tissue pathway","imaging leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MRI (T2) as a dark line separating posterior fossa from supratentorial compartment', 'On T2-weighted MRI the tentorium cerebelli appears as a hypointense line dividing the posterior fossa from the supratentorial compartment. It is poorly shown on plain X-ray and is not limited to CT or ultrasound.'),
('d077de56-b2f4-5efa-9699-cf69e7f4fad8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Epidermoid and dermoid cysts can arise from', '["Ectodermal rests (inclusion cysts) — often in CPA, midline; epidermoid = pearly tumor; dermoid = contains hair/sebum","Endoderm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesoderm mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural ectoderm without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","origin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ectodermal rests (inclusion cysts) — often in CPA, midline; epidermoid = pearly tumor; dermoid = contains hair/sebum', 'Epidermoid and dermoid cysts derive from ectodermal rests implanted during embryogenesis, forming inclusion cysts typical of the CPA and midline; epidermoids appear pearly while dermoids contain hair and sebum. Endodermal, mesodermal, or neural-ectodermal origins do not explain them.'),
('bd46549f-c403-543d-ae0d-384553458208'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Craniopharyngioma (sellar/suprasellar) arises from', '["Sphenoid sinus resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rathke pouch remnants (squamous epithelium) — calcified cystic suprasellar mass","Pituitary gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Third ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rathke pouch remnants (squamous epithelium) — calcified cystic suprasellar mass', 'Craniopharyngioma arises from squamous remnants of Rathke pouch and presents as a calcified, cystic suprasellar mass. The sphenoid sinus, hypothalamus, pituitary gland, and third ventricle are not the cells of origin.'),
('2bccb468-3351-5268-9927-ee548e329b8f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Meningioma arises from', '["Brain parenchyma resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Ependyma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid cap cells (meningothelial cells) — attachment to dura; common parasagittal, sphenoid wing, olfactory groove","Dura fibroblast without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pia mater leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid cap cells (meningothelial cells) — attachment to dura; common parasagittal, sphenoid wing, olfactory groove', 'Meningioma originates from arachnoid cap (meningothelial) cells and commonly occurs at parasagittal, sphenoid wing, and olfactory groove sites with dural attachment. Brain parenchyma, ependyma, dural fibroblasts, and pia mater are not the tissue of origin.'),
('9bc57165-317a-5728-a2a4-a837f64fcd27'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Meningiomas are characterized by', '["enhancement resulting in decreased functional activity","Cystic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","dural attachment mediated by specific receptor activation","Dural tail, extra-axial location, intense homogeneous enhancement, possible calcification/sclerosis of bone","Intra-axial location leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dural tail, extra-axial location, intense homogeneous enhancement, possible calcification/sclerosis of bone', 'Meningiomas arise from arachnoid cap cells and are extra-axial masses showing a dural tail and intense homogeneous enhancement; calcification and bone sclerosis are common. The other options are nonspecific or describe intra-axial disease.'),
('692c2c85-9105-52e4-8f43-1666d4808abd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Hemangioblastoma (posterior fossa) is associated with', '["Tuberous sclerosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurofibromatosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sturge-Weber mediated by specific receptor activation","Von Hippel-Lindau disease — cystic cerebellar tumor with enhancing mural nodule","Cowden leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Von Hippel-Lindau disease — cystic cerebellar tumor with enhancing mural nodule', 'Posterior fossa hemangioblastoma typically appears as a cystic lesion with an enhancing mural nodule and is strongly associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease. The other neurocutaneous or phakomatosis syndromes are not linked to this tumor.'),
('e64e03b0-e729-596a-83cd-e374f366b533'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Ependymoma arises from', '["Ependymal cells lining ventricles (especially fourth ventricle in children) — can spread via CSF","Arachnoid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","White matter mediated by specific receptor activation","Choroid plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ependymal cells lining ventricles (especially fourth ventricle in children) — can spread via CSF', 'Ependymomas originate from ependymal cells lining the ventricles and central canal, often in the fourth ventricle in children, and can seed through the CSF. The arachnoid, pia, white matter, and choroid plexus are not their cells of origin.'),
('c34b5f59-6665-5280-b0c6-b2147d887253'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Choroid plexus papilloma (lateral ventricle, children)', '["Epidermoid resulting in decreased functional activity","Ependymoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colloid cyst mediated by specific receptor activation","Meningioma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Benign tumor, CSF overproduction → hydrocephalus, intensely enhancing intraventricular mass"]'::jsonb, 'Benign tumor, CSF overproduction → hydrocephalus, intensely enhancing intraventricular mass', 'Choroid plexus papilloma is benign, typically intraventricular (lateral ventricle in children), and may cause communicating hydrocephalus by excess CSF secretion; it appears as an intensely enhancing mass. The other choices are different tumor types of the ventricles.'),
('cb82cbfb-b72d-5afe-ae09-873698f7080b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Colloid cyst of third ventricle at foramen of Monro:', '["Extra-axial resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral ventricle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Posterior fossa mediated by specific receptor activation","Fourth ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Benital, fluid-filled, may cause obstructive hydrocephalus, sudden death risk"]'::jsonb, 'Benital, fluid-filled, may cause obstructive hydrocephalus, sudden death risk', 'A colloid cyst is a benign fluid-filled lesion at the foramen of Monro that can obstruct CSF outflow, causing acute obstructive hydrocephalus and even sudden death. The other options give incorrect locations for this lesion.'),
('37d8f659-a066-5d5b-b652-3351298d0250'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The meninges (dura) receive sensory innervation from', '["CN VII resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal nerve (CN V) — especially V1 (tentorial, falx) and V2, V3","CN IX mediated by specific receptor activation","CN X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal nerve (CN V) — especially V1 (tentorial, falx) and V2, V3', 'The supratentorial dura is innervated mainly by the trigeminal nerve, especially its ophthalmic division (V1) supplying the falx and tentorium; V2 and V3 contribute too. This explains why meningeal irritation produces frontal headache. The other cranial nerves do not supply the meninges.'),
('ca248b01-0e2f-532b-bcc0-0c5b1df1a1f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Headache from meningeal irritation (meningitis, SAH) is mediated by', '["Cervical nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","CN IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trigeminovascular system (CN V) → pain sensation from meningeal vessels","CN X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminovascular system (CN V) → pain sensation from meningeal vessels', 'Pain from meningeal irritation in meningitis and subarachnoid hemorrhage travels via the trigeminovascular system, with CN V afferents around meningeal vessels. The cervical and other cranial nerves listed are not the principal pain pathway.'),
('447b4535-87f7-5530-a733-acb697a307a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF volume per day is approximately', '["50 mL","150 mL","500 mL","1000 mL","2000 mL"]'::jsonb, '500 mL', 'The choroid plexus produces about 500 mL of CSF daily, replacing the 150 mL total volume roughly three times per day. The other values do not reflect the normal daily production rate.'),
('61824058-aec3-5437-8a04-96305d568085'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Choroid plexus (fetal development) produces CSF from the', '["6th-8th week of gestation","Postnatal day 1","Second trimester","Third trimester","Birth"]'::jsonb, '6th-8th week of gestation', 'The choroid plexus begins producing CSF during the 6th to 8th weeks of gestation, helping cushion the developing brain. CSF production is well established long before birth, so the later time points are incorrect.'),
('6bef8175-a809-5db3-bb10-ed0253ee6714'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Dandy-Walker malformation (DWM):', '["Hydrocephalus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Chiari II accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Agenesis/hypoplasia of cerebellar vermis, cystic posterior fossa (dilated fourth ventricle), elevated tentorium","Cerebellar tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chiari I leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Agenesis/hypoplasia of cerebellar vermis, cystic posterior fossa (dilated fourth ventricle), elevated tentorium', 'Dandy-Walker malformation is characterized by vermian agenesis or hypoplasia, cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle filling an enlarged posterior fossa, and elevated tentorium. Simple hydrocephalus, Chiari malformations, and tumors do not explain this triad.'),
('d786d5ca-8d1d-5eb4-b4ae-e21f396a8c5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Dandy-Walker variant (DWv)', '["Only cerebellar hypoplasia resulting in decreased functional activity","Same as full DWM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Less severe: hypoplasia of inferior vermis, small cyst, no hydrocephalus","Normal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Worse than full DWM leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Less severe: hypoplasia of inferior vermis, small cyst, no hydrocephalus', 'The Dandy-Walker variant is a milder posterior fossa anomaly with hypoplasia chiefly of the inferior vermis and a small posterior fossa cyst, usually without hydrocephalus. It is neither identical to nor worse than full DWM.'),
('de088291-e100-59fc-b31a-b52948661520'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Chiari malformation type I:', '["Hydrocephalus only resulting in decreased functional activity","Meningomyelocele accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brainstem herniation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar tonsils herniate below foramen magnum (≥5 mm) — may cause cough headache, syringomyelia","Vermis herniation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar tonsils herniate below foramen magnum (≥5 mm) — may cause cough headache, syringomyelia', 'Chiari type I is defined by downward displacement of the cerebellar tonsils ≥5 mm below the foramen magnum, which can cause cough headache, lower cranial nerve signs, and syringomyelia. Myelomeningocele and brainstem herniation belong to other malformations.'),
('5c2fbac3-a6f5-5efe-875e-e07f6b91fac2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Chiari malformation type II (Arnold-Chiari):', '["Only tonsils resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only vermis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only medulla mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Meningocele only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Herniation of vermis, brainstem, and fourth ventricle through foramen magnum + myelomeningocele + hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 'Herniation of vermis, brainstem, and fourth ventricle through foramen magnum + myelomeningocele + hydrocephalus', 'Chiari type II (Arnold-Chiari) involves caudal herniation of the vermis, medulla, and fourth ventricle through the foramen magnum, almost always with a myelomeningocele and hydrocephalus. The partial herniation options omit these key associations.'),
('8c035820-801b-5fc8-ab3c-80d3c670f824'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Syringomyelia (syrinx) is a fluid-filled cavity within', '["Brain resulting in decreased functional activity","Subarachnoid space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord (often associated with Chiari I; may cause pain, sensory loss, weakness)","Fourth ventricle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord (often associated with Chiari I; may cause pain, sensory loss, weakness)', 'A syrinx is a fluid-filled cavity inside the spinal cord, frequently associated with Chiari I, and may cause dissociated sensory loss, pain, and weakness. The same cavity is not located in the brain, cerebellum, fourth ventricle, or subarachnoid space.'),
('caf4eb9e-247e-55d7-9c43-22f019cf720e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Tethered cord syndrome involves abnormal attachment of', '["Muscles resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Filum terminale or spinal cord to lower sacrum → prevents normal ascent; may cause leg weakness, bladder dysfunction","Vertebrae mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Dura mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filum terminale or spinal cord to lower sacrum → prevents normal ascent; may cause leg weakness, bladder dysfunction', 'Tethered cord syndrome results from abnormal attachment of the filum terminale or distal spinal cord to the sacrum, preventing the cord''s normal upward ascent. It typically presents with leg weakness, sensory loss, and bladder dysfunction.'),
('565881a3-09d9-5de6-90fa-0e34e5732da0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Lipomeningocele is a form of', '["Spina bifida with fatty tissue tethering the spinal cord","skin covering accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myelomeningocele only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only lipoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Open spina bifida leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spina bifida with fatty tissue tethering the spinal cord', 'Lipomeningocele is a closed (occult) form of spina bifida in which a fatty mass adheres to and tethers the spinal cord while the cord remains skin-covered. It is not an open spina bifida or a pure spinal cord herniation.'),
('ce1d03a5-d16d-52b4-809e-f3c6f33d3c14'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Diastematomyelia:', '["Absent spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor of spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fused spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Cord atrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Split spinal cord (sagittal septum) — two hemicords, often with bony spur, tethering"]'::jsonb, 'Split spinal cord (sagittal septum) — two hemicords, often with bony spur, tethering', 'Diastematomyelia is a sagittal division of the spinal cord into two hemicords, often separated by a bony or fibrous spur and associated with tethering. Absence, fusion, atrophy, and tumors do not describe this malformation.'),
('ce146d80-f1d8-5b34-a4a8-fcb3a7b67c73'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pia mater''s role in the spinal cord includes forming', '["Arachnoid granulations resulting in decreased functional activity","Dural folds accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Denticulate ligaments (stabilize spinal cord) and filum terminale (anchors conus)","Venous sinuses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Epidural fat leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Denticulate ligaments (stabilize spinal cord) and filum terminale (anchors conus)', 'The spinal pia mater forms the denticulate ligaments, which tether the cord to the dura, and continues as the filum terminale anchoring the conus medullaris. Arachnoid granulations, dural folds, venous sinuses, and epidural fat arise from other tissues.'),
('ba712ad6-69c5-59d8-a2dd-70899cc9a557'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Denticulate ligaments are located between', '["Spinal nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Dura and bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertebrae and spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Arachnoid and pia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal and ventral roots of spinal nerves (pial extensions to dura)"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal and ventral roots of spinal nerves (pial extensions to dura)', 'Denticulate ligaments are pial extensions that pass laterally between the dorsal and ventral nerve roots to attach to the inner dura, stabilizing the cord within the subarachnoid space. The other spaces described do not match their location.'),
('fa9cd320-96f4-58ae-ac6a-aac9b2e99544'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Spinal dural sac ends at', '["S2 (vertebral level) — below this the subarachnoid space narrows, filum terminale pierces dura","L1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S4 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Coccyx without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'S2 (vertebral level) — below this the subarachnoid space narrows, filum terminale pierces dura', 'The spinal dural sac terminates at the S2 vertebral level, where the subarachnoid space narrows and the filum terminale pierces the dura to reach the coccyx. This is why lumbar puncture is performed between L3 and L5, below the cord but within the sac.'),
('a39e9be8-d6b1-500c-8d96-2913433f4f5e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Epidural anesthesia involves injection into', '["Subdural space resulting in decreased functional activity","Epidural space (outside dura) — needle not through dura (loss of resistance technique)","Pia mediated by specific receptor activation","Subarachnoid space without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intracranial space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Epidural space (outside dura) — needle not through dura (loss of resistance technique)', 'Epidural anesthesia deposits drug in the epidural space, outside the dura, so the needle does not penetrate the dura; entry is confirmed by loss of resistance. The subdural, subarachnoid, and other spaces are not the intended injection site.'),
('605064e1-10ea-5d91-8fa0-596fd3705de6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Spinal anesthesia (subarachnoid block) involves injection into', '["Pia resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone mediated by specific receptor activation","Subarachnoid space (through dura) — needle must pierce dura (''CSF flashback'')","Epidural space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subarachnoid space (through dura) — needle must pierce dura (''CSF flashback'')', 'Subarachnoid (spinal) anesthesia requires the needle to pass through the dura and arachnoid so the drug mixes with CSF, confirmed by return of CSF. Injection into the epidural space or other tissues would not produce a spinal block.'),
('9b0f5701-90f2-5bee-a1d0-94d23d5419d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Post-dural puncture headache is due to', '["Anesthetic drug resulting in decreased functional activity","Low CSF pressure from leak through dural/arachnoid puncture → brain sag, traction on meninges","explanation mediated by specific receptor activation","Meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Venous dilatation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low CSF pressure from leak through dural/arachnoid puncture → brain sag, traction on meninges', 'Post-dural puncture headache occurs because CSF continues to leak through the dural tear, lowering intracranial pressure and causing the brain to sag with traction on pain-sensitive meninges. The other mechanisms listed do not explain the headache.'),
('d26ced70-a0b8-54f1-a173-2c48e7dcb900'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Epidural blood patch treats post-LP headache by', '["Clotting at dural puncture → stops CSF leak → restores CSF volume/pressure","All of these accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increasing CSF production mediated by specific receptor activation","Sealing vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking pain receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Clotting at dural puncture → stops CSF leak → restores CSF volume/pressure', 'An epidural blood patch works by clotted blood sealing the dural defect, which stops the CSF leak and restores CSF volume and intracranial pressure. It does not act by increasing CSF production, sealing a vein, or blocking pain receptors.'),
('bbb7f78c-e413-522a-9df9-6b4a7edff543'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Risks of lumbar puncture include', '["Only bleeding resulting in decreased functional activity","Only infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Post-LP headache, bleeding (epidural hematoma), infection, herniation (if mass/↑ICP)","risks without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only headache leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-LP headache, bleeding (epidural hematoma), infection, herniation (if mass/↑ICP)', 'Lumbar puncture carries several risks: post-LP headache, spinal epidural hematoma, infection, and tonsillar herniation when intracranial pressure is raised by a mass. Listing only a single complication omits the other recognized risks.'),
('15f55bc1-5898-52ec-8a63-16964cce75f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Lumbar puncture contraindications:', '["Only increased ICP resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only coagulopathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only infection mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased ICP (mass, hydrocephalus), coagulopathy (INR >1.4, platelets <50k), infection at puncture site, spinal stenosis","absolute contraindications leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ICP (mass, hydrocephalus), coagulopathy (INR >1.4, platelets <50k), infection at puncture site, spinal stenosis', 'LP should be avoided when there is raised intracranial pressure from a mass or hydrocephalus (herniation risk), coagulopathy such as INR >1.4 or platelets <50,000, infection over the puncture site, or significant spinal stenosis. The single-option answers list only one of these contraindications.'),
('ae21056b-2d67-5e41-9d13-7d8f54e7b26d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Normal CSF total protein is', '["<10 mg/dL","50-100 mg/dL",">100 mg/dL","5-10 mg/dL","15-45 mg/dL (0.15-0.45 g/L)"]'::jsonb, '15-45 mg/dL (0.15-0.45 g/L)', 'Normal lumbar CSF total protein ranges from 15-45 mg/dL (0.15-0.45 g/L); values differ slightly by collection site. Lower or much higher ranges do not represent the normal lumbar CSF protein.'),
('0f7c68f1-9bfa-5f2c-97da-8cd59775dba8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Elevated CSF protein >100 mg/dL suggests', '["MS only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hemorrhage only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral meningitis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial meningitis, fungal meningitis, tuberculous meningitis, Guillain-Barre, spinal block (Froin syndrome)"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial meningitis, fungal meningitis, tuberculous meningitis, Guillain-Barre, spinal block (Froin syndrome)', 'Marked protein elevation (>100 mg/dL) is seen in bacterial, fungal, and tuberculous meningitis, Guillain-Barré syndrome, and with a spinal subarachnoid block (Froin syndrome). Multiple sclerosis and uncomplicated viral meningitis usually cause only mild protein rises.'),
('b411d187-891d-50a4-99c5-899cd62ba43a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Froin syndrome (spinal block) =', '["Normal CSF resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF leak accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High glucose mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Yellow(xanthochromic) CSF with very high protein and coagulable due to complete spinal subarachnoid block (below tumor)","Low cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yellow(xanthochromic) CSF with very high protein and coagulable due to complete spinal subarachnoid block (below tumor)', 'Froin syndrome is the triad of xanthochromic (yellow) CSF with very high protein that may clot spontaneously, caused by complete spinal subarachnoid block below a tumor. Normal, leaking, or high-glucose CSF does not describe this syndrome.'),
('00766571-a687-5f20-ac85-7a8628ab7e3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Normal CSF glucose is', '["50-80 mg/dL (or >2/3 blood glucose; CSF:serum >0.6)","80-120 mg/dL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","<30 mg/dL mediated by specific receptor activation","Equal to blood without affecting the primary tissue pathway","glucose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '50-80 mg/dL (or >2/3 blood glucose; CSF:serum >0.6)', 'Normal CSF glucose is about 50-80 mg/dL, roughly two-thirds of the concurrent blood glucose because it crosses the blood-brain barrier by facilitated transport. Values equal to blood glucose or below 30 mg/dL are not normal.'),
('9ff66efe-e008-5b67-bd2f-d363aaebdda2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Low CSF glucose (hypoglycorrhachia) is NOT seen in', '["Carcinomatous meningitis","Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningitis (normal glucose)","Fungal meningitis","TB meningitis"]'::jsonb, 'Viral meningitis (normal glucose)', 'Viral meningitis typically leaves CSF glucose normal, so hypoglycorrhachia is not expected. Bacterial, fungal, and tuberculous meningitis, as well as carcinomatous meningitis, characteristically lower CSF glucose.'),
('71f1f6f3-0f11-58ac-8ac8-6531296cb79e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF lactate is elevated in', '["Only in TB resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bacterial meningitis ( >35 mg/dL) and mitochondrial disorders","MS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viral meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial meningitis ( >35 mg/dL) and mitochondrial disorders', 'CSF lactate rises above 35 mg/dL in bacterial meningitis, reflecting anaerobic metabolism, and is also elevated in mitochondrial disorders. Viral meningitis, MS, and isolated TB do not characteristically produce such a rise.'),
('0efe48a4-0bc9-5e27-b03b-c359a3e5e269'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF cell count: normal WBC is', '["<200 resulting in decreased functional activity","0 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","<100 mediated by specific receptor activation","<5 cells/μL (all mononuclear/monocytes)","<50 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '<5 cells/μL (all mononuclear/monocytes)', 'Normal CSF contains fewer than 5 white blood cells per μL, all of them mononuclear (lymphocytes/monocytes). Counts of 50, 100, or 200 cells are clearly abnormal, and reporting zero omits the accepted upper limit.'),
('d830dc37-4471-54d6-a3e2-4b03080476e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF pleocytosis with lymphocytic predominance (viral, TB, fungal)', '["Polymorphs only","Neutrophils >80%","cells","Mixed","Lymphocytes >80% of WBC"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytes >80% of WBC', 'A lymphocytic pleocytosis with more than 80% lymphocytes characterizes viral, tuberculous, and fungal meningitis. A predominance of polymorphs or neutrophils points instead to bacterial meningitis.'),
('1f9317e9-ccc6-5281-bb95-59d0dcf959a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Eosinophilic pleocytosis in CSF suggests', '["Parasitic infection (e.g., neurocysticercosis), coccidioidomycosis, CNS lymphoma","TB meningitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bacterial meningitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Parasitic infection (e.g., neurocysticercosis), coccidioidomycosis, CNS lymphoma', 'Eosinophilic CSF pleocytosis points to parasitic infection such as neurocysticercosis, to coccidioidomycosis, or to CNS lymphoma. Bacterial, viral, and tuberculous meningitis do not characteristically recruit eosinophils.'),
('455ba693-b2b9-53ac-9ff4-35f72c4bf7e2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Opening pressure is measured with patient', '["Supine resulting in decreased functional activity","Prone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In lateral decubitus position (legs extended, head neutral) — manometer zeroed at level of spine","Sitting up without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Standing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In lateral decubitus position (legs extended, head neutral) — manometer zeroed at level of spine', 'Opening pressure is measured with the patient in the lateral decubitus position, legs extended and head neutral, with the manometer zeroed at the level of the spine. This recumbent position avoids artificially high readings from sitting or standing.'),
('a4dc802e-4ef9-5b6d-97bd-b50024b60492'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Opening pressure >20 cm H2O is considered', '["Borderline resulting in decreased functional activity","Low accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elevated (intracranial hypertension)","Critical without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated (intracranial hypertension)', 'An opening pressure above 20 cm H2O indicates elevated intracranial pressure, since the normal range is roughly 10-20 cm H2O. The other options do not reflect the significance of this threshold.'),
('3db3cfff-c98e-5022-8e36-e14dc2583793'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Indian ink stain of CSF is diagnostic for', '["Bacterial meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Cryptococcus neoformans (polysaccharide capsule visualized) — the most common fungal CNS infection in HIV/AIDS","TB meningitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amebic encephalitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cryptococcus neoformans (polysaccharide capsule visualized) — the most common fungal CNS infection in HIV/AIDS', 'Indian ink staining highlights the polysaccharide capsule of Cryptococcus neoformans, allowing rapid diagnosis of cryptococcal meningitis, the most common fungal CNS infection in HIV/AIDS. The other infections do not show a capsule with this stain.'),
('b9b0e521-5520-5b07-8f1a-f10aab1360de'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Acid-fast bacilli (AFB) smear of CSF is for', '["Sarcoidosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Bacterial meningitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Viral meningitis mediated by specific receptor activation","TB meningitis (low sensitivity; PCR/culture better)","Fungal meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TB meningitis (low sensitivity; PCR/culture better)', 'An AFB smear of CSF detects tuberculous meningitis, although its sensitivity is low and PCR or culture is preferred. The other diagnoses are not established by acid-fast staining.'),
('5fa4cc85-65df-5a08-97c7-176734346158'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF PCR for enterovirus is the gold standard for', '["TB meningitis resulting in decreased functional activity","HSV encephalitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aseptic (viral) meningitis — most common cause of lymphocytic meningitis","Fungal meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial meningitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aseptic (viral) meningitis — most common cause of lymphocytic meningitis', 'Enterovirus CSF PCR is the gold-standard test for aseptic (viral) meningitis, the most common cause of lymphocytic meningitis. HSV encephalitis, TB, fungal, and bacterial meningitis require different diagnostic tests.'),
('393a60ed-c168-5825-8a59-0ae47f1d1eed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF PCR for HSV is diagnostic for', '["All viral infections resulting in decreased functional activity","Viral meningitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Herpes simplex virus encephalitis (HSV-1) — high sensitivity/specificity (temporal lobe involvement)","HSV meningitis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningoencephalitis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Herpes simplex virus encephalitis (HSV-1) — high sensitivity/specificity (temporal lobe involvement)', 'CSF PCR for HSV is highly sensitive and specific for herpes simplex encephalitis (HSV-1), which characteristically affects the temporal lobes. It does not diagnose all viral infections or generic meningoencephalitis.'),
('4039e6d7-c06c-523d-a010-0e68ec8d3fc8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF analysis in multiple sclerosis shows', '["Normal IgG resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Mild lymphocytosis, mildly elevated protein (including IgG), positive oligoclonal bands (≥2 unique to CSF), elevated IgG index","Normal in all mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","High glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neutrophils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mild lymphocytosis, mildly elevated protein (including IgG), positive oligoclonal bands (≥2 unique to CSF), elevated IgG index', 'MS CSF typically shows mild lymphocytic pleocytosis, mildly elevated protein with increased IgG, oligoclonal bands (≥2 unique to CSF), and a raised IgG index. Neutrophilia, high glucose, and normal findings are not characteristic.'),
('fcd7ba27-db21-521a-abdb-e1118d60cbbe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'IgG index: (CSF IgG / serum IgG) / (CSF albumin / serum albumin)', '["Normal <0.7; elevated (>0.7) = intrathecal IgG synthesis (MS)","elevated accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Index of <0.5 mediated by specific receptor activation","normal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","index needed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal <0.7; elevated (>0.7) = intrathecal IgG synthesis (MS)', 'The IgG index is normally below 0.7; a value above 0.7 indicates intrathecal IgG synthesis, a feature supporting multiple sclerosis. The other options give no useful cutoff or interpretation.'),
('70ac376a-b69f-5982-b61d-dae46bf256b1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Kappa free light chains (CSF) are also sensitive for', '["Autoimmune resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple sclerosis (intrathecal synthesis)","Hemorrhage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple sclerosis (intrathecal synthesis)', 'Elevated kappa free light chains in CSF reflect intrathecal antibody synthesis and serve as a sensitive marker of multiple sclerosis. They do not specifically mark tumors, hemorrhage, or infection.'),
('018d234f-5755-55e1-ad0b-ead191839df9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Myelin basic protein (MBP) in CSF is a marker of', '["Multiple sclerosis only resulting in decreased functional activity","Stroke only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alzheimer only mediated by specific receptor activation","Active demyelination (MS, but also stroke, infection) — not specific","Tumor only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Active demyelination (MS, but also stroke, infection) — not specific', 'CSF myelin basic protein rises with active demyelination, as in MS, but also after stroke and CNS infection, so it lacks specificity. It is therefore not confined to a single disease.'),
('16e4a725-b892-508d-b835-237d2abddc65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Neuron-specific enolase (NSE) in CSF', '["Infection marker resulting in decreased functional activity","Tumor marker accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MS marker mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Biomarker of neuronal injury (stroke, hypoxia, traumatic brain injury, status epilepticus)"]'::jsonb, 'Biomarker of neuronal injury (stroke, hypoxia, traumatic brain injury, status epilepticus)', 'CSF neuron-specific enolase is a biomarker of neuronal injury, rising in stroke, hypoxia, traumatic brain injury, and status epilepticus. It is not primarily an infection, tumor, or MS marker.'),
('8eab54b2-9d06-5513-b6e6-36bdda3eaf95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Tau and phospho-tau in CSF are biomarkers for', '["Alzheimer disease (neurofibrillary tangles) — elevated tau, decreased Aβ42","Parkinson disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MS mediated by specific receptor activation","Infection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alzheimer disease (neurofibrillary tangles) — elevated tau, decreased Aβ42', 'Elevated CSF tau and phospho-tau, together with decreased Aβ42, are biomarkers of Alzheimer disease related to neurofibrillary tangles. Parkinson disease, MS, infection, and stroke do not show this CSF signature.'),
('0a6596bb-d396-5eea-8ca2-c1a16da1de7e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Amyloid-beta 42 (Aβ42) in CSF is decreased in', '["Vascular dementia resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alzheimer disease (amyloid plaques trap Aβ42 → less in CSF)","Parkinson without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alzheimer disease (amyloid plaques trap Aβ42 → less in CSF)', 'CSF Aβ42 is decreased in Alzheimer disease because amyloid plaques trap Aβ42 in the brain, leaving less to enter the CSF. Vascular dementia, Parkinson disease, and MS do not characteristically lower it.'),
('57ffe8db-191b-56c6-b2ea-4d94e9422971'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', '14-3-3 protein in CSF is a marker for', '["Stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (prion disease) — high sensitivity/specificity","Infection mediated by specific receptor activation","MS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alzheimer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (prion disease) — high sensitivity/specificity', 'The 14-3-3 protein in CSF is a sensitive marker of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, a prion disorder. Stroke, infection, MS, and Alzheimer disease are not detected by this marker.'),
('8191d3e9-0c7c-5047-a8a7-669713f1a8d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF anti-NMDA receptor antibodies diagnose', '["MS resulting in decreased functional activity","Dementia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stroke mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral encephalitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (autoimmune limbic encephalitis) — young women with ovarian teratoma"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis (autoimmune limbic encephalitis) — young women with ovarian teratoma', 'CSF anti-NMDA receptor antibodies diagnose anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis, an autoimmune limbic encephalitis seen especially in young women, often associated with ovarian teratoma. MS, dementia, stroke, and viral encephalitis are not diagnosed by this antibody.'),
('e6730c03-4139-5a4e-93fd-23dcc8ca7627'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Autoimmune encephalitis (VGKC-complex, LGI1, CASPR2, AMPA, GABA-B) can be diagnosed by', '["EEG only","test","Blood culture","CSF autoantibody panel","MRI only"]'::jsonb, 'CSF autoantibody panel', 'CSF and serum autoantibody panels detect antibodies against VGKC-complex proteins (LGI1, CASPR2) and surface receptors (AMPA, GABA-B) that define these autoimmune encephalitides. EEG and MRI are supportive but not diagnostic, and blood culture excludes infection rather than confirming autoimmune disease.'),
('a35b7696-21cf-5ffd-aa7c-fa44ae9d567f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF metagenomic next generation sequencing (mNGS) can identify', '["Only common pathogens resulting in decreased functional activity","Only viruses accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only bacteria mediated by specific receptor activation","Rare infectious pathogens (virus, bacteria, fungi) in meningoencephalitis","Only fungi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rare infectious pathogens (virus, bacteria, fungi) in meningoencephalitis', 'mNGS detects nucleic acid from any organism without a prior hypothesis, so it can identify unusual viruses, bacteria, and fungi that cause meningoencephalitis. Targeted tests limited to one class of pathogen will miss organisms outside that group.'),
('72729e7c-bbd6-572c-a61e-5341e2e392a6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF culture (bacterial) grows in', '["TB resulting in decreased functional activity","Fungal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Negative culture mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral meningitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial meningitis (but rate highly dependent on prior antibiotics)"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial meningitis (but rate highly dependent on prior antibiotics)', 'Bacterial CSF culture is the reference standard in pyogenic meningitis, although its yield falls if antibiotics were administered first. It does not grow viruses and is insensitive for TB and fungi, which require special media.'),
('9b322c13-4dfd-5d56-b94b-d9f9e5bb85c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF latex agglutination or antigen tests are available for', '["tests resulting in decreased functional activity","All bacteria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cryptococcus, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis, Streptococcus pneumoniae","All viruses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All fungi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cryptococcus, Haemophilus influenzae type b, Neisseria meningitidis, Streptococcus pneumoniae', 'Rapid antigen assays target specific capsular or cell-wall antigens of Cryptococcus, H. influenzae type b, N. meningitidis, and S. pneumoniae. They are directed at these named pathogens rather than at all bacteria, viruses, or fungi.'),
('84551133-2fe4-53a4-84c5-d67e1f8cb8d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The blood-brain barrier is absent in the following circumventricular organs:', '["All of brain resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortex only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Area postrema, median eminence, neurohypophysis, pineal, OVLT, subfornical organ (CVOs)","Cerebellum only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Area postrema, median eminence, neurohypophysis, pineal, OVLT, subfornical organ (CVOs)', 'Circumventricular organs have fenestrated capillaries with no blood-brain barrier, allowing them to sample blood-borne signals. The cortex, cerebellum, and spinal cord retain an intact BBB.'),
('103e644d-9f62-51f1-8b76-6671d4e1a2a8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The area postrema (medulla) is the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) for vomiting', '["Detects blood-borne emetics (no BBB) → triggers vomiting center in NTS","Detects CSF emetics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Part of motor system mediated by specific receptor activation","Triggers nausea only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Involved in taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detects blood-borne emetics (no BBB) → triggers vomiting center in NTS', 'Because the area postrema lacks a BBB, it senses emetic toxins and drugs carried in blood and relays to the vomiting center in the nucleus tractus solitarius. It is not a motor structure and does not respond primarily to CSF, nausea alone, or taste.'),
('aa235ebb-b292-5c75-92ca-399020eaffcf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'BBB is composed of', '["Non-fenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions (zonulae occludentes) + basal lamina + pericyte + astrocyte foot processes","Astrocytes only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pericytes only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Multiple cell types without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endothelial only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-fenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions (zonulae occludentes) + basal lamina + pericyte + astrocyte foot processes', 'The barrier is a neurovascular unit: non-fenestrated endothelium joined by tight junctions, a basal lamina, pericytes, and astrocyte end-feet. Astrocytes, pericytes, or endothelium alone cannot form a functioning barrier.'),
('301b9f37-a97c-5710-803a-d880ccffba0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Tight junctions of the BBB restrict', '["Only proteins resulting in decreased functional activity","Only glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All molecules mediated by specific receptor activation","Paracellular diffusion of hydrophilic molecules (but small lipophilic substances, O2, CO2 diffuse freely)","Only ions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paracellular diffusion of hydrophilic molecules (but small lipophilic substances, O2, CO2 diffuse freely)', 'Tight junctions seal the paracellular route and block water-soluble molecules, while small lipophilic agents and gases such as O2 and CO2 still cross through the cell membranes. Glucose and ions enter via specific transporters, so they are not universally blocked.'),
('448a3622-3b32-5359-916f-2cf267937894'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Glucose transport across BBB is via', '["GLUT4 resulting in decreased functional activity","GLUT1 (facilitated diffusion) — glucose enters brain via saturable transporter","Simple diffusion mediated by specific receptor activation","transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GLUT2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT1 (facilitated diffusion) — glucose enters brain via saturable transporter', 'GLUT1, expressed on capillary endothelium, carries glucose into the brain by facilitated diffusion and becomes saturated at high glucose levels. GLUT2 and GLUT4 serve other tissues, and glucose does not cross by simple diffusion.'),
('6cb4a208-c03a-5a06-9e2e-f669ee8e1306'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'L-DOPA (Parkinson) crosses BBB via', '["Neutral amino acid transporter (LAT1) — competes with dietary amino acids","Dopamine transporter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diffusion only mediated by specific receptor activation","transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GLUT1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neutral amino acid transporter (LAT1) — competes with dietary amino acids', 'L-DOPA is a large neutral amino acid carried into the brain by LAT1, which also transports dietary amino acids, so a high-protein meal can reduce its uptake. Dopamine itself does not cross the BBB, and simple diffusion is negligible at therapeutic doses.'),
('855f53b4-bcbc-5929-a657-687e1cea8769'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The BBB is more permeable in which brain region?', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","All areas same accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Circumventricular organs (CVOs) — no BBB (but protect the rest of brain)"]'::jsonb, 'Circumventricular organs (CVOs) — no BBB (but protect the rest of brain)', 'Circumventricular organs have fenestrated, leaky capillaries, making them more permeable than typical brain regions. The cortex, cerebellum, and hippocampus maintain a largely uniform intact barrier.'),
('c9ab2011-aa7e-5d91-8670-086b78cb2c49'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cerebral edema (vasogenic) is treated with', '["Mannitol only resulting in decreased functional activity","treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgery only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dexamethasone (steroid reduces BBB permeability and edema)","Hyperventilation only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dexamethasone (steroid reduces BBB permeability and edema)', 'Vasogenic edema from BBB breakdown responds to corticosteroids such as dexamethasone, which reduce capillary permeability. Mannitol and hyperventilation mainly lower intracranial pressure and are used in cytotoxic edema, while surgery is reserved for refractory mass effect.'),
('c66a9d4c-f0e1-53ff-8db0-73631c32e230'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Cerebral edema (cytotoxic) is treated with', '["Diuretics only resulting in decreased functional activity","Dexamethasone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mannitol/hypertonic saline, craniectomy (if severe); steroids not effective","treatment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Antibiotics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mannitol/hypertonic saline, craniectomy (if severe); steroids not effective', 'Cytotoxic edema from cell swelling is treated with osmotic agents such as mannitol or hypertonic saline and, in severe cases, decompressive craniectomy. Steroids are ineffective because the BBB is intact, and antibiotics matter only if infection is present.'),
('012f7b51-64e1-5699-b91a-bafb7dd934ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The BBB can be imaged using', '["imaging resulting in decreased functional activity","Ultrasound accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contrast-enhanced MRI (gadolinium enhancement where BBB is disrupted)","CT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","X-ray leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Contrast-enhanced MRI (gadolinium enhancement where BBB is disrupted)', 'Gadolinium cannot cross an intact BBB, so enhancement on MRI marks sites of barrier disruption in tumors, infection, and inflammation. CT, X-ray, and ultrasound do not directly demonstrate BBB integrity.'),
('44a55f5f-1062-5179-be49-4e1fc3981f7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Virchow-Robin (perivascular) spaces are part of', '["Lymph system resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF system only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Venous system only mediated by specific receptor activation","function without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brain interstitial fluid drainage system (glymphatic) — along penetrating arteries"]'::jsonb, 'Brain interstitial fluid drainage system (glymphatic) — along penetrating arteries', 'Perivascular spaces around penetrating arteries carry interstitial fluid and form a key route of the glymphatic clearance pathway. They are not lymphatic or venous channels themselves, although they ultimately connect to meningeal lymphatics.'),
('28178769-25cd-5fcb-84f8-b51789335ec9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The glymphatic system is active mainly during', '["Wakefulness resulting in decreased functional activity","Sleep (especially deep NREM) — clears amyloid beta and other metabolites","Stress mediated by specific receptor activation","Eating without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sleep (especially deep NREM) — clears amyloid beta and other metabolites', 'During deep non-REM sleep the interstitial space expands and glymphatic flow rises, clearing amyloid beta and other metabolic waste. Wakefulness and physical activity are associated with reduced clearance, not increased activity.'),
('2d4d5c85-5f92-53c4-b44a-14dfa2a3d30c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The lymphatics of the brain recently described (meningeal lymphatics)', '["Only in spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Drain CSF and brain interstitial fluid to deep cervical lymph nodes (via foramina of skull base)","Do not exist mediated by specific receptor activation","Are in ventricles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Are part of bloodstream leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drain CSF and brain interstitial fluid to deep cervical lymph nodes (via foramina of skull base)', 'Meningeal lymphatics line the dural sinuses and drain CSF and brain interstitial fluid to deep cervical lymph nodes through foramina at the skull base. They are absent from the spinal cord and ventricles and are distinct from blood vessels.'),
('0ce4e62b-37f7-5e09-a63a-ed3b34f48888'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The meningeal lymphatic vessels are located along', '["Arachnoid granulations resulting in decreased functional activity","Dural sinuses (especially superior sagittal sinus) and middle meningeal artery","Epidural space mediated by specific receptor activation","Brain parenchyma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Subarachnoid space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dural sinuses (especially superior sagittal sinus) and middle meningeal artery', 'Meningeal lymphatics run adjacent to the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus, and alongside the middle meningeal artery. They lie within the dura rather than in the parenchyma, epidural space, or subarachnoid space.'),
('acc036a9-c38c-5ba0-acdb-4b937bad52bf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF outflow also occurs via', '["other route resulting in decreased functional activity","Only spinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only arachnoid granulations mediated by specific receptor activation","Only lymphatic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal arachnoid villi (around nerve roots) and lymphatic drainage (besides arachnoid granulations)"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal arachnoid villi (around nerve roots) and lymphatic drainage (besides arachnoid granulations)', 'Besides arachnoid granulations, CSF is absorbed by spinal arachnoid villi around nerve roots and by meningeal lymphatic drainage. No single route accounts for all CSF outflow.'),
('df2cf3e6-23c0-5123-9355-78969a0947f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Hydrocephalus treatment includes', '["VP shunt, ETV (endoscopic third ventriculostomy), choroid plexus cauterization (in children)","Only medication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Only VP shunt without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ETV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VP shunt, ETV (endoscopic third ventriculostomy), choroid plexus cauterization (in children)', 'Hydrocephalus is managed by diverting CSF with a VP shunt, restoring flow with endoscopic third ventriculostomy, and sometimes cauterizing the choroid plexus in infants. Medication alone cannot relieve the excess CSF.'),
('efa5ecf9-049c-55db-970c-6508951f94ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Endoscopic third ventriculostomy (ETV) treats', '["Obstructive hydrocephalus (creates opening in floor of third ventricle → CSF flows to prepontine cistern)","Communicating hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF leak mediated by specific receptor activation","NPH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All hydrocephalus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive hydrocephalus (creates opening in floor of third ventricle → CSF flows to prepontine cistern)', 'ETV creates a hole in the floor of the third ventricle so that CSF trapped upstream of an obstruction can flow into the prepontine cistern. It fails in communicating hydrocephalus because there is no site of blockage to bypass.'),
('3dbdfbf4-7828-50f4-beca-3120fb0528f2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Pneumatic (post-shunt) subdural hematoma results from', '["Obstruction resulting in decreased functional activity","complication accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Valve malfunction mediated by specific receptor activation","Overdrainage of CSF → brain shrinks → bridging veins tear → subdural bleed","Infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Overdrainage of CSF → brain shrinks → bridging veins tear → subdural bleed', 'Excessive CSF drainage makes the brain shrink away from the skull, stretching and tearing bridging veins and causing subdural bleeding. Obstruction, valve malfunction, and infection produce different shunt complications.'),
('b7b8d11d-06c8-5272-8a39-62430d83bf09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Slit ventricle syndrome (chronic overdrainage) presents with', '["Intermittent headaches, small ventricles (on imaging), and slower shunt refill","Normal imaging accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Large ventricles mediated by specific receptor activation","symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","headache leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermittent headaches, small ventricles (on imaging), and slower shunt refill', 'Chronic overdrainage keeps the ventricles slit-like and produces intermittent headaches with slow refill of the shunt valve reservoir. Normal imaging and large ventricles do not fit this picture, and headache alone understates the presentation.'),
('ac5b45cf-884a-5f13-813a-82ba2793ada0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'PROSE (prolonged reservoir of shunt edema) or post-shunt hygromas are also due to', '["Chronic overdrainage → extra-axial fluid collections (subdural hygroma)","complications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shunt infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Shunt obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shunt fracture leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic overdrainage → extra-axial fluid collections (subdural hygroma)', 'Persistent overdrainage lets the brain fall away from the dura, and fluid accumulates in the subdural space as a hygroma. Shunt infection, obstruction, and fracture produce different clinical pictures.'),
('32c59c25-3f11-52b4-a369-3cea6f667662'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Shunt infection (common: Staphylococcus epidermidis) presents with', '["Fever, erythema along shunt tract, increased ICP (shunt failure), seroma","Only fever accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vomiting mediated by specific receptor activation","Only headache without affecting the primary tissue pathway","symptoms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, erythema along shunt tract, increased ICP (shunt failure), seroma', 'Shunt infection classically causes fever and erythema over the catheter tract, with increased intracranial pressure from partial obstruction and sometimes a seroma. Isolated fever, vomiting, or headache understates the typical presentation.'),
('54d673d5-9a2a-53c3-a8f4-a6e211f82789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Ommaya reservoir (intraventricular catheter) allows', '["Drug delivery to brain resulting in decreased functional activity","ICP monitoring only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF drainage only mediated by specific receptor activation","Intraventricular chemotherapy or CSF sampling (e.g., in leptomeningeal carcinomatosis)","VP shunt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intraventricular chemotherapy or CSF sampling (e.g., in leptomeningeal carcinomatosis)', 'An Ommaya reservoir is a subcutaneous port with an intraventricular catheter used to deliver chemotherapy directly into the CSF and to sample it, as in leptomeningeal carcinomatosis. It does not shunt CSF and is not designed mainly for ICP monitoring.'),
('d934a922-d9e3-5046-ba6a-416e17c8f30b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The cisternogram (radionuclide or CT cisternography) evaluates', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF dynamics (hydrocephalus, CSF leak) — inject contrast into lumbar CSF, image head","Brain tumors mediated by specific receptor activation","Bleeding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF dynamics (hydrocephalus, CSF leak) — inject contrast into lumbar CSF, image head', 'Cisternography instills contrast or a radionuclide into lumbar CSF and tracks its flow to diagnose NPH patterns, CSF leaks, and blockages. It does not assess infection, tumor, hemorrhage, or stroke.'),
('ef9c3d67-4cee-5e4f-a5b0-00256bf8c606'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) may improve with', '["Surgery only resulting in decreased functional activity","treatment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CSF drainage (LP, or prolonged external drainage) — large volume LP (>40 mL) may transiently improve gait","Shunt only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medical therapy only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CSF drainage (LP, or prolonged external drainage) — large volume LP (>40 mL) may transiently improve gait', 'Tapping or draining CSF, such as a large-volume lumbar puncture, can transiently improve gait and supports the diagnosis of NPH while predicting shunt response. Medical therapy does not treat NPH.'),
('7f2b51d7-11a3-58be-96f4-64cb71623a11'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'VP shunts for NPH show improvement in', '["Incontinence best resulting in decreased functional activity","improvement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gait (most responsive), incontinence, cognition (least responsive) — in that order","Cognition best without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All equally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gait (most responsive), incontinence, cognition (least responsive) — in that order', 'After shunting, gait improves most and earliest, followed by urinary incontinence, while cognitive impairment responds least. The other options ignore this consistent response hierarchy.'),
('8caa7fc7-2b62-5fe8-ab59-278cd998913b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The programmable (adjustable) valve VP shunt allows', '["Only one pressure resulting in decreased functional activity","benefit accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","External programming of CSF drainage pressure (non-invasively using magnetic field)","adjustment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Manual revision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'External programming of CSF drainage pressure (non-invasively using magnetic field)', 'An adjustable valve is set non-invasively with an external magnetic programmer to alter the CSF drainage pressure. It is not fixed at a single pressure and does not require surgery for each change.'),
('5ece4731-031e-5353-87fa-e1cdf354da9e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'MRI safety: programmable VP shunt valves may change setting during', '["Only X-ray resulting in decreased functional activity","interaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only CT mediated by specific receptor activation","MRI (strong magnetic field can change valve pressure) — must check/reprogram after MRI","Ultrasound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MRI (strong magnetic field can change valve pressure) — must check/reprogram after MRI', 'The static magnetic field and gradients of MRI can reset a programmable valve, so the setting should be checked and reprogrammed afterwards. CT, X-ray, and ultrasound do not alter the valve setting.'),
('a7eb047b-3e94-5b17-89b4-74923e5551bb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Antibiotic-impregnated shunt catheters reduce', '["Shunt fracture resulting in decreased functional activity","Shunt infection (e.g., Bactiseal/rifampin-clindamycin impregnated)","Valve malfunction mediated by specific receptor activation","Overdrainage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shunt obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt infection (e.g., Bactiseal/rifampin-clindamycin impregnated)', 'Catheters coated with antibiotics such as rifampin and clindamycin lower the rate of shunt infection. They do not affect mechanical problems like fracture, obstruction, overdrainage, or valve malfunction.'),
('ba740d40-ed52-50c1-a7eb-652888a04c09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'CSF shunts are valved systems that', '["Have valve resulting in decreased functional activity","Filter CSF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Drain in both directions mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase CSF production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Drain CSF unidirectionally (prevent backflow) at designated pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Drain CSF unidirectionally (prevent backflow) at designated pressure', 'The valve allows CSF to drain in one direction only, preventing backflow, and opens at a preset pressure. Shunts do not filter or produce CSF and never drain both ways.'),
('f77faea9-db2b-582c-b50e-fdd8f8249a28'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Ventriculoatrial (VA) shunt drains CSF into', '["Right atrium (internal jugular → right heart)","Pleural cavity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peritoneal cavity mediated by specific receptor activation","Bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Right atrium (internal jugular → right heart)', 'The distal catheter of a VA shunt passes through the internal jugular vein into the right atrium. The peritoneum, pleura, bladder, and spinal canal are sites used by other shunt types.'),
('d7c5889e-4b6c-507a-ae3e-1fd70a731691'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Complications of VA shunt include', '["Only infection resulting in decreased functional activity","complications accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shunt nephritis (immune complex deposition), pulmonary hypertension, infection","Only valve failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt nephritis (immune complex deposition), pulmonary hypertension, infection', 'Blood-borne complications of VA shunts include shunt nephritis from immune-complex deposition, pulmonary hypertension from repeated microemboli, and infection. The risks are not limited to a single complication.'),
('2bb6e0ae-a3d8-5564-bc56-b4a418d0fa34'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Ventriculopleural shunt drains CSF into', '["Bladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Peritoneum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrium mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural space (pneumothorax, effusion risk)"]'::jsonb, 'Pleural space (pneumothorax, effusion risk)', 'A ventriculopleural shunt empties CSF into the pleural cavity, risking pneumothorax or pleural effusion. The peritoneum, atrium, and spinal canal are used by other shunt types.'),
('bbbabfd1-57d3-5b90-a164-8f0c0795c4d8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Lumboperitoneal (LP) shunt drains CSF from', '["Lumbar to atrium resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricle to peritoneum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord to peritoneum mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventricle to bladder without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lumbar subarachnoid space to peritoneal cavity (for communicating hydrocephalus, pseudotumor)"]'::jsonb, 'Lumbar subarachnoid space to peritoneal cavity (for communicating hydrocephalus, pseudotumor)', 'An LP shunt drains CSF from the lumbar subarachnoid space to the peritoneal cavity and is used in communicating hydrocephalus and pseudotumor cerebri. It does not connect the ventricles or spinal cord.'),
('ed17d95f-ff62-5479-b851-bd6ffe9e554e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Pseudotumor cerebri (IIH) treatment includes', '["Weight loss, acetazolamide, furosemide, topiramate, LP shunting, venous sinus stenting, optic nerve sheath fenestration","Only weight loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only venous stenting mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only acetazolamide without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only shunt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weight loss, acetazolamide, furosemide, topiramate, LP shunting, venous sinus stenting, optic nerve sheath fenestration', 'IIH is managed with weight loss, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide, diuretics, topiramate, and surgical options like LP shunt, venous stenting, and optic nerve sheath fenestration. No single measure is sufficient.'),
('681eddb7-d987-597e-9098-0267b626642e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Optic nerve sheath fenestration (ONSF) for IIH involves', '["Increasing CSF absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Slitting the optic nerve sheath → allows CSF egress → protect optic nerve/papilledema","Shunting to orbit mediated by specific receptor activation","Reducing CSF production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","None leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Slitting the optic nerve sheath → allows CSF egress → protect optic nerve/papilledema', 'ONSF slits the optic nerve sheath so that trapped CSF can escape and relieve pressure on the nerve and papilledema. It neither increases CSF absorption nor reduces CSF production and does not shunt to the orbit.'),
('49676617-214f-5321-b087-5196ae5eb9e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Venous sinus stenting for IIH is for patients with', '["Identifiable venous sinus stenosis (MRV) across which there is a pressure gradient","Communicating hydrocephalus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","All IIH patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","role leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Identifiable venous sinus stenosis (MRV) across which there is a pressure gradient', 'Stenting is reserved for IIH patients with focal dural venous sinus stenosis shown on MRV and a measurable pressure gradient across it. It is not offered to every IIH patient and does not apply to communicating hydrocephalus.'),
('3d41970c-bc97-5ab8-876c-436c818461d1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Subdural hygroma (CSF collection in subdural space) occurs after', '["Only surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Trauma, surgery, CSF overdrainage — often self-limited, may need drainage if symptomatic","known cause mediated by specific receptor activation","Only trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only overdrainage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Trauma, surgery, CSF overdrainage — often self-limited, may need drainage if symptomatic', 'Hygromas follow head trauma, intracranial surgery, or CSF overdrainage, and usually resolve on their own although symptomatic collections may need drainage. The cause is not limited to a single trigger.'),
('7c1f75df-7005-5191-a48b-a0c2448b57d7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The dura mater in the spine is innervated by', '["Parasympathetic only resulting in decreased functional activity","Cranial nerves only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic chain only mediated by specific receptor activation","innervation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recurrent meningeal nerves (sinuvertebral nerves) — sensory from each spinal level"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent meningeal nerves (sinuvertebral nerves) — sensory from each spinal level', 'Each spinal level gives off recurrent meningeal (sinuvertebral) nerves that return through the intervertebral foramen to supply the spinal dura. Cranial nerves, the sympathetic chain, and parasympathetic fibers are not the principal supply.'),
('31292188-74c7-5fab-b017-380063320964'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The epidural space is absent in the', '["Lumbar spine resulting in decreased functional activity","Cranial cavity (dura attached to periosteum) — no epidural space normally","Sacrum mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal canal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cervical spine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cranial cavity (dura attached to periosteum) — no epidural space normally', 'In the cranium the outer periosteal layer of the dura fuses with the periosteum, leaving no epidural space. In the spine the dura is separated from the vertebral periosteum by a genuine epidural space.'),
('94cf9885-78b6-5b19-84f0-2e98a0d51105'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The olfactory nerves (CN I) pierce which structure to reach the olfactory bulb?', '["Foramen rotundum resulting in decreased functional activity","Optic canal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Superior orbital fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","Foramen ovale without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone (through foramina)"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone (through foramina)', 'Olfactory fila pass through the foramina of the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to reach the olfactory bulb, and they are prone to tearing in head trauma. The other foramina transmit different cranial nerves.'),
('d669cb15-5ffc-547c-81ba-cca97ff66ead'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'The pia mater forms internal septa and sheaths for blood vessels entering the brain — these perivascular sheaths are called', '["Liliequist membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Choroid fissure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Scarpa fissure mediated by specific receptor activation","None without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Virchow-Robin spaces (continue as subarachnoid space along vessels)"]'::jsonb, 'Virchow-Robin spaces (continue as subarachnoid space along vessels)', 'Pial sheaths that accompany vessels into the brain form Virchow-Robin spaces, which connect to the subarachnoid space. The Liliequist membrane is a separate arachnoid structure, and the choroid fissure is unrelated to perivascular sheaths.'),
('a4f8777d-5f79-59f4-9f97-5cca38c01a65'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Liliequist membrane is an arachnoid fold between', '["Tentorium and clivus resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum and medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pons and clivus mediated by specific receptor activation","Mamillary bodies and the midbrain (separates chiasmatic and interpeduncular cisterns)","Falx and tentorium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mamillary bodies and the midbrain (separates chiasmatic and interpeduncular cisterns)', 'The Liliequist membrane spans between the mamillary bodies and the midbrain, separating the chiasmatic and interpeduncular cisterns. The other listed locations describe different dural or arachnoid relationships.'),
('668eb98b-3e72-5a88-8453-bb3f9a7b0ce9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'In Meninges, ventricles and CSF, which statement best fits the following fact: the three meninges from outer to inner are:?', '["Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater","Pia, arachnoid, dura","Dura, pia, arachnoid","Arachnoid, dura, pia","Pia, dura, arachnoid"]'::jsonb, 'Dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater', 'From outermost to innermost, the meninges are the dura mater, arachnoid mater, and pia mater, with the subdural and subarachnoid spaces lying between them. Every other ordering reverses or shuffles the layers.'),
('fdb7930f-9367-51d6-a682-9cd6434ea9be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Regarding Meninges, ventricles and CSF, what is the correct answer to: the cranial dura mater consists of?', '["Two layers (periosteal and meningeal) fused together except at venous sinuses","One layer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Single layer with sinuses mediated by specific receptor activation","Three layers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fused to bone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two layers (periosteal and meningeal) fused together except at venous sinuses', 'The cranial dura has an outer periosteal and an inner meningeal layer that are fused except where they split to enclose the dural venous sinuses. The spinal dura, in contrast, is a single layer, so the remaining options are wrong for the cranium.'),
('ef3bbed6-7de9-5192-91d1-8557ed5428a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'For Meninges, ventricles and CSF, which option is most appropriate: the spinal dura mater consists of?', '["Two layers with epidural space resulting in decreased functional activity","Three layers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Single layer (meningeal only) with epidural space","Fused to periosteum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","dura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Single layer (meningeal only) with epidural space', 'The spinal dura is a single meningeal layer separated from the vertebral periosteum by the epidural space, which contains fat and the internal vertebral venous plexus. It is not fused to bone and has no periosteal layer.'),
('0881f8c5-c3c4-5441-90bc-78946f3c4c4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Meninges Ventricles CSF', 'Which answer is correct in Meninges, ventricles and CSF when asked: the epidural space in the spine contains?', '["Fat, loose connective tissue, internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus)","Arachnoid mater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Pia mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CSF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat, loose connective tissue, internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus)', 'The spinal epidural space holds fat, loose connective tissue, and the internal vertebral venous plexus (Batson plexus). CSF, arachnoid mater, and pia mater lie in the subarachnoid and subdural layers, not in the epidural space.'),
('222d7c9c-11f6-5737-b04f-8cb638f3171e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Regarding spinal cord termination in adults', '["At L1-L2","At T12","At L4","At S2","At C7"]'::jsonb, 'At L1-L2', 'In adults the spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris, usually at the L1-L2 vertebral level. Below this the subarachnoid space is occupied by the cauda equina, which is why lumbar puncture is performed below L2.'),
('0f2b059a-8b27-50cd-93a4-995fd17fb15a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Conus medullaris is located at which vertebral level in newborns?', '["L4-L5","L1-L2","S1","L3","L2-L3"]'::jsonb, 'L3', 'At birth the spinal cord and its conus medullaris are relatively longer, ending near L3. The cord reaches the adult L1-L2 position around 1-2 years of age as the vertebral column grows faster.'),
('9cb55a5d-053d-55e6-84d4-e1eeef43f2f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Cauda equina consists of', '["Only ventral roots resulting in decreased functional activity","Only dorsal roots accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal nerves of cervical region mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal and ventral roots of lumbar/sacral segments","Filum terminale only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal and ventral roots of lumbar/sacral segments', 'The cauda equina is a bundle of dorsal (sensory) and ventral (motor) roots of the lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal nerves descending below the conus medullaris. The filum terminale is a connective strand, not a set of roots.'),
('2048ac08-c3cf-5dcd-ae3e-d4dd31e6354d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Filum terminale is derived from', '["Dura mater","White matter","Pia mater","Ependyma","Arachnoid mater"]'::jsonb, 'Pia mater', 'The filum terminale is a slender filament of pia mater continuous with the tip of the conus medullaris. It descends through the subarachnoid space (internum) and pierces the dura (externum) to attach to the coccyx.'),
('9282a2c8-331a-52f7-b600-f63347d4b140'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Denticulate ligaments are extensions of', '["Epidural space","Subarachnoid space","Pia mater","Dura mater","Arachnoid mater"]'::jsonb, 'Pia mater', 'Denticulate ligaments are formed by lateral extensions of pia mater that anchor the spinal cord to the inner surface of the dura mater. They help stabilize the cord within that subarachnoid space.'),
('90319f7a-bddf-5c55-aac1-81df628c1558'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Cervical enlargement of spinal cord extends from', '["C3 to T2","C2 to C6","T1 to T4","C1 to C4","C5 to T1"]'::jsonb, 'C3 to T2', 'The cervical enlargement spans roughly C3 to T2 and corresponds to the segments supplying the brachial plexus and upper limb muscles. The lumbosacral enlargement lower down serves the lower limb.'),
('95999dda-991c-52db-b9b4-21bc4c60bcb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lumbosacral enlargement extends from', '["T10 to L2","T9 to T12","T11 to L1","L1 to S3","L1 to L5"]'::jsonb, 'T11 to L1', 'The lumbosacral enlargement extends approximately from T11 to L1, containing the segments that give rise to the lumbar and sacral plexuses for the lower limb. It lies rostral to the conus medullaris.'),
('c558a5c0-b854-5ff6-95a2-3683e6b0e194'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which lamina of Rexed contains substantia gelatinosa?', '["Lamina I","Lamina IV","Lamina II","Lamina V","Lamina III"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina II', 'Substantia gelatinosa is located in lamina II of Rexed, capping the dorsal horn apex and modulating nociceptive input. Lamina I is the marginal zone, laminae III-IV form the nucleus proprius.'),
('f8015349-a6c4-50df-adfd-8c54a0ca7029'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lamina IX of Rexed contains', '["Interneurons","Sensory neurons","Autonomic neurons","Motor neurons","Glial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Motor neurons', 'Lamina IX contains the motor neuron cell columns (alpha and gamma motor neurons) of the ventral horn. These innervate skeletal muscle and are lost in lower motor neuron disease. Sensory and autonomic cells lie in other laminae.'),
('4b582012-5f5f-51ae-bf36-2e51030d24f1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lamina VII contains', '["Nucleus proprius","Motor neurons","Marginal zone","Substantia gelatinosa","Dorsal nucleus of Clarke"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal nucleus of Clarke', 'Lamina VII contains the dorsal nucleus of Clarke (posterior thoracic nucleus), the origin of the dorsal spinocerebellar tract, along with the intermediolateral cell column. Nucleus proprius is in laminae III-IV and substantia gelatinosa in lamina II.'),
('a345fd95-cab0-57b4-9c60-bddd3224c8e8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Rexed lamina I is also called', '["Marginal zone","Autonomic nucleus","Substantia gelatinosa","Motor nucleus","Nucleus proprius"]'::jsonb, 'Marginal zone', 'Lamina I is the marginal (posteromarginal) zone, a thin cap over the dorsal horn that relays nociception. Substantia gelatinosa is lamina II and nucleus proprius is laminae III-IV. Motor neurons occupy lamina IX.'),
('077dba37-c022-51a6-9d48-c9806793e8a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Nucleus proprius is found in which Rexed laminae?', '["III and IV","V and VI","VII and VIII","I and II","IX and X"]'::jsonb, 'III and IV', 'Nucleus proprius occupies laminae III and IV of the dorsal horn and receives light touch input. Laminae I-II are marginal zone and substantia gelatinosa, while laminae V-VI are the base of the dorsal horn.'),
('3be73c8b-e93f-58ae-bf76-185b37eb95a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Intermediolateral cell column is located in which lamina?', '["Lamina X","Lamina IX","Lamina V","Lamina VII","Lamina II"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina VII', 'The intermediolateral cell column, containing preganglionic sympathetic neurons, lies in lamina VII of the thoracolumbar cord. Lamina IX holds motor neurons and lamina II the substantia gelatinosa.'),
('31850be8-fa25-5ea6-b356-ca98160a2a61'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The sacral parasympathetic nucleus (preganglionic) is located in which spinal cord segment?', '["L1-L2 resulting in decreased functional activity","T1-T4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C8-T1 mediated by specific receptor activation","S2-S4 (intermediolateral cell column of gray matter)","S5-Co1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4 (intermediolateral cell column of gray matter)', 'Sacral parasympathetic (preganglionic) neurons lie in the intermediolateral cell column at S2-S4 segments. They supply pelvic viscera via pelvic splanchnic nerves. Thoracolumbar outflow carries sympathetic preganglionics.'),
('c35caa3c-7126-5456-b12c-4875f82f0dcb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is located in the dorsal column?', '["Fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus","Corticospinal tract","Spinocerebellar tract","Tectospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus', 'The dorsal (posterior) column contains the fasciculus gracilis and fasciculus cuneatus, which carry fine touch, vibration, and conscious proprioception. The corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts run in the lateral and ventral funiculi.'),
('c3231b41-9539-5811-8d3a-2db079238445'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Fasciculus gracilis carries sensory information from', '["Lower limb and lower trunk","Entire body","Head and neck","Upper limb and upper trunk","Face"]'::jsonb, 'Lower limb and lower trunk', 'The fasciculus gracilis carries fine touch, vibration, and proprioception from the lower limb and lower trunk (below T6). The fasciculus cuneatus carries the same modalities from the upper limb and upper trunk (above T6).'),
('d7d4e403-f54e-573a-8f93-28294e3bb701'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Fasciculus cuneatus is present at which spinal levels?', '["Sacral only","Lumbar only","Above T6","Below T6","Entire spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Above T6', 'The fasciculus cuneatus exists only above the T6 spinal level because its fibers come from the upper limb and upper trunk. Below T6 only the fasciculus gracilis is found, so the entire dorsal column is gracilis.'),
('57cc5d5c-97fe-5fcb-8236-fe30e30e6bc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'First-order neurons of dorsal column pathway are located in', '["Cerebral cortex","Spinal cord gray matter","Thalamus","Medulla oblongata","Dorsal root ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal root ganglion', 'First-order neurons of the dorsal column pathway lie in the dorsal root ganglion; their central processes ascend in the gracile and cuneate fasciculi. Their cell bodies are thus peripheral, not in cord, medulla, or cortex.'),
('299cb068-c256-5fd6-9faa-4c8d55fe1027'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Second-order neurons of dorsal column pathway are in', '["Nucleus gracilis and cuneatus in medulla","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal root ganglion mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebral cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus gracilis and cuneatus in medulla', 'Second-order neurons lie in the nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus of the lower medulla. Their axons form the internal arcuate fibers, decussate, and ascend as the medial lemniscus to the VPL thalamus.'),
('18296bf6-396a-59fe-b3cb-0786ba5d15c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Medial lemniscus is formed by fibers from', '["Spinal cord","Nucleus gracilis and cuneatus","Cerebellum","Thalamus","Dorsal root ganglion"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus gracilis and cuneatus', 'The medial lemniscus is formed by the decussated axons of second-order neurons arising in the gracile and cuneate nuclei of the medulla. It ascends through the brainstem to synapse in the VPL nucleus of the thalamus. Cerebellar and DRG fibers do not form it.'),
('751e4281-773d-5af2-9adc-1f899dd1fd36'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Medial lemniscus decussation occurs at', '["Pons resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla (sensory decussation)"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (sensory decussation)', 'The internal arcuate fibers from the gracile and cuneate nuclei decussate in the medulla at the sensory decussation to form the medial lemniscus. Only after this decussation does the pathway carry contralateral body information.'),
('5fa267b9-38ae-56af-b294-7e9695191795'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Third-order neurons of dorsal column pathway are in', '["VPM nucleus of thalamus","MGN","LGN","VPL nucleus of thalamus","Pulvinar"]'::jsonb, 'VPL nucleus of thalamus', 'Third-order neurons of the dorsal column and spinothalamic pathways lie in the ventroposterolateral (VPL) nucleus of the thalamus, which relays to the somatosensory cortex. VPM, LGN, and MGN serve trigeminal, visual, and auditory inputs respectively.'),
('9131d108-b53f-52fd-bae6-a22c817a8081'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinothalamic tract carries', '["Auditory information","Balance","Pain, temperature, crude touch","Motor commands","Vibration and proprioception"]'::jsonb, 'Pain, temperature, crude touch', 'The spinothalamic (anterolateral) tract conveys pain, temperature, and crude touch to the brain. Vibration and conscious proprioception travel in the dorsal columns, and motor commands travel in descending corticospinal fibers.'),
('74e136c3-a7fd-564d-9989-9dbfb135ae0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anterior spinothalamic tract carries', '["Vibration","Crude touch and pressure","Proprioception","Pain","Temperature"]'::jsonb, 'Crude touch and pressure', 'The anterior spinothalamic tract transmits crude touch and pressure, while the lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature. Vibration and conscious proprioception travel in the dorsal columns instead.'),
('04b97a12-84a8-5e87-ad4c-81107187ebf5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lateral spinothalamic tract carries', '["Motor signals","Crude touch","Vibration","Proprioception","Pain and temperature"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature', 'The lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature impulses. First-order afferents synapse in the dorsal horn, second-order fibers decussate and ascend, and the tract terminates in the VPL nucleus of the thalamus.'),
('e6256cb8-29ef-5832-94c0-4797c36acd84'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'First-order neurons of spinothalamic tract are in', '["Thalamus","Dorsal root ganglion","Medulla","Spinal cord","Cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal root ganglion', 'First-order neurons of the spinothalamic tract are the pseudounipolar cells of the dorsal root ganglion. Their central processes enter the dorsal horn, where they synapse with second-order neurons. Thalamus and cortex house later-order cells.'),
('f3f8800a-c7ae-5ff8-b3f1-ddd0174619cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Second-order neurons of spinothalamic tract', '["Decussate within 1-2 segments in spinal cord","Do not decussate","Decussate in medulla","Decussate in midbrain","Decussate in thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Decussate within 1-2 segments in spinal cord', 'Second-order neurons of the spinothalamic tract cross the midline in the anterior white commissure within 1-2 segments of entry and ascend in the contralateral anterolateral column. Their decussation is spinal, not medullary or rostral.'),
('a77c5b18-8be8-5fb2-8e5a-124685a20e77'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinothalamic tract terminates in', '["VPM nucleus","VPL nucleus of thalamus","Medial geniculate body","Lateral geniculate body","Pulvinar"]'::jsonb, 'VPL nucleus of thalamus', 'Spinothalamic fibers terminate in the ventroposterolateral (VPL) nucleus of the thalamus, which then projects to the somatosensory cortex. VPM receives the trigeminal pathway, MGN audition, and LGN vision.'),
('e398e372-7a74-5ce5-90d8-157af879b32a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Dorsal spinocerebellar tract originates from', '["Ventral horn","Clarke''s nucleus (lamina VII, C8-L3)","Dorsal root ganglion","Nucleus proprius","Substantia gelatinosa"]'::jsonb, 'Clarke''s nucleus (lamina VII, C8-L3)', 'The dorsal spinocerebellar tract arises from the dorsal nucleus of Clarke (posterior thoracic nucleus) in lamina VII at C8-L3. Its fibers ascend ipsilaterally and enter the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle.'),
('3530418d-650f-544a-8878-d9ee9cd15e88'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Dorsal spinocerebellar tract enters cerebellum via', '["Directly","Inferior cerebellar peduncle","Superior cerebellar peduncle","Middle cerebellar peduncle","Ventral spinocerebellar"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior cerebellar peduncle', 'The dorsal spinocerebellar tract enters the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle without decussating. The ventral spinocerebellar tract, by contrast, enters via the superior cerebellar peduncle.'),
('5781216d-4ff9-5ad1-a013-234199f42d6d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Ventral (anterior) spinocerebellar tract originates from', '["Lateral horn","Spinal border cells of laminae V-VII","Dorsal root ganglion","Ventral horn","Clarke''s nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal border cells of laminae V-VII', 'The ventral spinocerebellar tract arises from spinal border cells located in laminae V-VII. Its fibers mostly decussate, ascend the contralateral lateral funiculus, and enter the cerebellum through the superior cerebellar peduncle.'),
('4ac2ecf8-c785-505b-a10e-c21fba60b0ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Ventral spinocerebellar tract enters cerebellum via', '["Middle cerebellar peduncle","Superior cerebellar peduncle","Direct cerebellar entry","All peduncles","Inferior cerebellar peduncle"]'::jsonb, 'Superior cerebellar peduncle', 'The ventral spinocerebellar tract enters the cerebellum through the superior cerebellar peduncle, traveling in the opposite lateral funiculus and crossing again within the cerebellum. Its counterpart, the dorsal tract, uses the inferior peduncle.'),
('ce582f35-aefb-5e2d-9889-1190dda7658b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Cuneocerebellar tract is the upper limb equivalent of', '["Ventral spinocerebellar tract","Corticospinal tract","Medial lemniscus","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract","Spinothalamic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal spinocerebellar tract', 'The cuneocerebellar tract is the upper-limb analogue of the dorsal spinocerebellar tract, arising from the accessory cuneate nucleus and entering the ipsilateral cerebellum via the inferior peduncle. Spinocerebellar fibers carry unconscious proprioception from the limbs.'),
('eed382f3-d56d-5b30-b600-dfc48bd92c80'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract carries unconscious proprioception to cerebellum?', '["Corticospinal","Spinothalamic","Rubrospinal","Medial lemniscus","Spinocerebellar tracts"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar tracts', 'The dorsal and ventral spinocerebellar tracts (with the cuneocerebellar tract) convey unconscious proprioception from muscles and joints to the cerebellum. Conscious proprioception, in contrast, travels in the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway.'),
('6b5c395b-7cbe-5e05-8b54-f8225ae41b3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinotectal tract projects to', '["Thalamus","Cerebellum","Inferior colliculus","Red nucleus","Superior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Superior colliculus', 'The spinotectal tract, a component of the anterolateral system, projects to the superior colliculus of the midbrain. It participates in spinovisual reflexes directing head and eye movements toward somatosensory stimuli; it does not terminate in thalamus or cerebellum.'),
('9ef06b1f-4290-5830-b08b-7f346ef36a4c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinoreticular tract terminates in', '["Reticular formation of brainstem","Cerebral cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reticular formation of brainstem', 'The spinoreticular tract terminates in the reticular formation of the brainstem after ascending in the anterolateral column. Via its connections it contributes to arousal and pain awareness, and it does not synapse in cortex, cerebellum, or thalamus.'),
('4cecd5bb-f8f4-5e5a-a482-470cb1f88284'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinoolivary tract carries information to', '["Substantia nigra","Inferior olive","Pontine nuclei","Superior olive","Red nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior olive', 'The spinoolivary tract ascends to the inferior olivary nucleus of the medulla, which projects climbing fibers to the cerebellum. Substantia nigra and red nucleus belong to motor basal loop circuits, and the superior olive subserves hearing.'),
('b00db566-95bc-5c23-a0e1-f8ab0486c6b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lateral corticospinal tract (crossed) controls', '["Facial muscles","Eye muscles","Ipsilateral limb movement","Axial muscles","Contralateral limb movement"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral limb movement', 'The lateral corticospinal tract, most of whose fibers cross at the pyramidal decussation, controls mainly fine voluntary movement of contralateral limb muscles. Facial and eye muscles are not innervated by this spinal tract.'),
('c88ac3df-044d-5277-96d1-67ceab437581'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anterior (ventral) corticospinal tract (uncrossed)', '["Controls facial muscles","Controls axial and trunk muscles","Crosses in medulla","Controls limb muscles","Controls eye muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Controls axial and trunk muscles', 'The anterior corticospinal tract descends uncrossed in the ventral funiculus and mainly innervates axial and trunk muscles; some fibers eventually cross at spinal segmental levels. It does not control limb, facial, or eye muscles, and its crossing is not in the medulla.'),
('21ba2832-ee78-5804-ac8c-598e9e7fa5f6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Corticospinal tract originates from', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor cortex (Brodmann area 4 and 6)"]'::jsonb, 'Motor cortex (Brodmann area 4 and 6)', 'The corticospinal tract arises from pyramidal neurons in the primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4) and premotor/supplementary areas (area 6), with a smaller contribution from the somatosensory cortex. Basal ganglia and cerebellum modulate rather than originate this tract.'),
('efcaa5f0-62da-5521-bad9-d4422a24e6d6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Decussation of pyramids occurs at', '["Spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervicomedullary junction (medulla-spinal cord)","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cervicomedullary junction (medulla-spinal cord)', 'The pyramidal decussation occurs at the cervicomedullary junction, where about 85-90% of corticospinal fibers cross to form the lateral corticospinal tract. The remaining uncrossed fibers continue as the anterior corticospinal tract.'),
('bf36363c-faef-5d89-989b-26c59bebb6d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Rubrospinal tract originates from', '["Caudate","Globus pallidus","Red nucleus (midbrain)","Substantia nigra","Putamen"]'::jsonb, 'Red nucleus (midbrain)', 'The rubrospinal tract arises from the red nucleus in the midbrain tegmentum and mainly facilitates contralateral flexor muscle tone. The caudate, putamen, and globus pallidus are basal ganglia nuclei, not the origin of this tract.'),
('9c400ba8-3bd1-5829-bf12-583c73ef5784'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Rubrospinal tract crosses in', '["Midbrain (tegmental decussation)","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (tegmental decussation)', 'Rubrospinal fibers immediately cross the midline in the midbrain at the ventral tegmental decussation (decussation of Forel) before descending to the spinal cord. Decussation therefore occurs in the midbrain, not in the medulla, pons, or cord.'),
('2898b6e6-e30a-5097-88d0-3305c80b0071'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The tectospinal tract is involved in which reflex?', '["Postural balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain modulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visuomotor (reflexive head/eye turning toward visual/auditory stimuli)","Voluntary motor control without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fine finger movements leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visuomotor (reflexive head/eye turning toward visual/auditory stimuli)', 'The tectospinal tract arises from the superior and inferior colliculi and coordinates reflex turning of the head and eyes toward visual or auditory stimuli. It subserves visuomotor reflexes rather than postural balance, pain control, or voluntary fine movement.'),
('57d62b5b-dc85-5907-a41c-05b165b9d195'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Tectospinal tract decussates in', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord mediated by specific receptor activation","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midbrain (dorsal tegmental decussation)"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (dorsal tegmental decussation)', 'Tectospinal fibers from the superior and inferior colliculi cross in the midbrain at the dorsal tegmental decussation and descend contralaterally to the cervical cord. The corresponding tract thus decussates in the midbrain, not in the medulla, pons, or cord.'),
('73fd7987-7640-5044-844f-4d8bca3697c1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Medial reticulospinal tract originates from', '["Spinal cord","Medullary reticular formation","Pontine reticular formation","Midbrain reticular formation","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine reticular formation', 'The medial (pontine) reticulospinal tract arises from the pontine reticular formation and descends ipsilaterally in the medial longitudinal fasciculus, facilitating antigravity extensor muscles. The lateral (medullary) tract, in contrast, arises from the medullary reticular formation and inhibits extension.'),
('a2bb7c14-9f87-54c6-830b-f453baee2228'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lateral reticulospinal tract originates from', '["Thalamus","Medullary reticular formation","Pontine reticular formation","Spinal cord","Midbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Medullary reticular formation', 'The lateral reticulospinal tract arises from the medullary reticular formation and descends bilaterally to inhibit extensor (antigravity) muscle activity. The medial reticulospinal tract, in contrast, originates in the pons and facilitates extension.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('86c25ae0-ab3e-58af-8e71-cff4d115d883'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Vestibulospinal tract originates from', '["Cochlear nucleus","Superior vestibular nucleus","Lateral vestibular nucleus (Deiters nucleus)","Inferior vestibular nucleus","Medial vestibular nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral vestibular nucleus (Deiters nucleus)', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract, the principal vestibulospinal pathway, originates from the lateral vestibular nucleus (Deiters nucleus) and influences extensor tonus for posture. The medial vestibulospinal tract originates separately from the medial vestibular nucleus.'),
('33fa9602-67db-50ae-ab67-43ae0b7a19ad'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Medial vestibulospinal tract originates from', '["Lateral vestibular nucleus","Superior nucleus","Inferior nucleus","Medial vestibular nucleus","All vestibular nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Medial vestibular nucleus', 'The medial vestibulospinal tract arises specifically from the medial vestibular nucleus, descends within the medial longitudinal fasciculus, and controls neck and upper trunk muscles. The lateral tract, from the lateral vestibular nucleus, serves limb extensor tone.'),
('54ece53d-3d65-59bd-81e6-daa7c0d0eafa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Brown-Sequard syndrome involves', '["Bilateral sensory loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Bilateral motor loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Ipsilateral pain loss, contralateral motor loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ipsilateral motor loss, contralateral pain/temperature loss"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral motor loss, contralateral pain/temperature loss', 'Hemisection of the cord produces ipsilateral UMN weakness (crossed corticospinal) plus loss of fine touch, vibration, and proprioception (uncrossed dorsal columns) on the same side, together with contralateral loss of pain and temperature (crossed spinothalamic). The reverse pairing of losses, or bilateral findings, does not occur.'),
('06e97231-4f3a-56b4-ba5a-875e6c0389f5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In Brown-Sequard syndrome, vibration and proprioception are lost', '["Bilateral","Ipsilateral (below lesion)","Not affected","Above lesion","Contralateral (below lesion)"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral (below lesion)', 'The dorsal column pathway (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) ascends ipsilaterally in the spinal cord, so hemisection abolishes vibration and proprioception on the same side below the lesion. Contralateral loss below the lesion would apply to pain and temperature, which cross in the cord, while fibers above the lesion are spared.'),
('e4c01405-04dc-5c22-a938-4e2e590ee3a1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Syringomyelia typically presents with', '["Complete sensory loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of vibration and proprioception accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Saddle anesthesia mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of pain and temperature at level of lesion (cape-like distribution)","UMN paralysis of all limbs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of pain and temperature at level of lesion (cape-like distribution)', 'The syrinx expands centrally from the cervical canal laterally into the crossing spinothalamic fibers, producing bilateral pain and temperature loss at the affected cervical dermatomes in a cape-like distribution. Vibration, proprioception, and other dorsal column functions remain intact, and motor paralysis is neither complete nor purely upper motor neuron in type.'),
('b71555ee-20a8-5f93-8936-f0af58452657'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In syringomyelia, which sensation is spared?', '["Temperature","Vibration and proprioception","All sensations lost","Pain","Crude touch"]'::jsonb, 'Vibration and proprioception', 'The cavitation damages decussating spinothalamic fibers while leaving the dorsal columns alone, so vibration and proprioception are preserved. Pain, temperature, and crude touch are lost at the affected segments rather than spared.'),
('8c316464-adfe-5712-ba25-0ca0d7cca6ae'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'ALS (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis) involves', '["Only sensory","Both UMN and LMN","Only LMN","Only UMN","Only autonomic"]'::jsonb, 'Both UMN and LMN', 'ALS destroys both upper and lower motor neurons, producing hyperreflexia and spasticity alongside weakness, muscle wasting, and fasciculations. Sensory and autonomic pathways are characteristically spared.'),
('3f388524-ef6b-548a-bdbd-b0ad63147285'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which of the following is NOT a feature of ALS?', '["Sensory loss","Hyperreflexia","Fasciculations","Spasticity","Muscle weakness"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory loss', 'ALS is a pure motor neuron disease that spares afferent pathways, so sensory loss is absent. Hyperreflexia and spasticity reflect upper motor neuron involvement, while fasciculations and weakness arise from lower motor neuron degeneration.'),
('9989955f-b0aa-53ab-a32e-6c2297121dc9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Poliomyelitis affects', '["Cerebellum","Corticospinal tract (UMN)","Anterior horn cells (LMN)","Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal columns"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior horn cells (LMN)', 'Poliovirus selectively infects the anterior horn (lower motor neuron) cells of the spinal cord, causing flaccid weakness with sensation intact. The corticospinal, spinothalamic, and dorsal column pathways are not the targets of the infection.'),
('6c205ddb-10f6-5345-a553-27a9815759c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In polio, which of the following is seen?', '["Flaccid paralysis without sensory loss","Spastic paralysis without sensory loss","Flaccid paralysis with sensory loss","Ataxia","Spastic paralysis with sensory loss"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid paralysis without sensory loss', 'Polio destroys anterior horn motor neurons, producing flaccid weakness while the sensory pathways remain intact. Spasticity indicates upper motor neuron or pyramidal disease, and sensory loss is not a feature of polio.'),
('2a8d10b6-1022-5a5a-b368-b8ea55ff1bbd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anterior spinal artery syndrome affects', '["Entire spinal cord","Anterior 2/3 of spinal cord","Only gray matter","Only white matter","Posterior 1/3 of spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 of spinal cord', 'The anterior spinal artery perfuses the anterior two-thirds of the cord, including the corticospinal and spinothalamic tracts. The posterior third is supplied by the posterior spinal arteries and is spared in anterior spinal artery occlusion.'),
('566322c8-e943-57ee-9ca6-92c6f7dabe2e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In anterior spinal artery occlusion, which is spared?', '["Motor function","Crude touch","Temperature sensation","Pain sensation","Proprioception and vibration"]'::jsonb, 'Proprioception and vibration', 'The dorsal columns receive their blood supply from the posterior spinal arteries, so they escape ischemia in anterior spinal artery occlusion and proprioception and vibration are preserved. Motor, crude touch, pain, and temperature functions are lost because the anterior cord is affected.'),
('04c2d1db-a605-56c7-a260-33ad358f23d3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Posterior spinal arteries supply', '["Central gray matter","Anterior 2/3 of spinal cord","Posterior 1/3 of spinal cord (dorsal columns)","Lateral columns only","Entire spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior 1/3 of spinal cord (dorsal columns)', 'The paired posterior spinal arteries vascularize the dorsal columns (fasciculus gracilis and cuneatus) and the posterior third of the cord. The anterior two-thirds receive their blood supply from the anterior spinal artery.'),
('d5c39395-e783-5e66-9abd-234dc70607b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Artery of Adamkiewicz (great anterior radicular artery) arises from', '["Lower thoracic/upper lumbar region","Cranial region","Midthoracic region","Cervical region","Sacral region"]'::jsonb, 'Lower thoracic/upper lumbar region', 'The artery of Adamkiewicz, the largest segmental medullary artery, typically arises from a lower thoracic or upper lumbar (commonly T8-L1) aortic branch. It is the most important radicular artery supplying the lower two-thirds of the spinal cord.'),
('a8e8933c-a4cf-57db-9d02-0a93e961684d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Damage to artery of Adamkiewicz causes', '["Complete cord transection","Anterior spinal syndrome below mid-thoracic level","Posterior cord syndrome","Central cord syndrome","Brown-Sequard syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior spinal syndrome below mid-thoracic level', 'Occlusion of the artery of Adamkiewicz, which reinforces the anterior spinal artery below the mid-thoracic level, produces an anterior spinal syndrome with paraplegia and loss of pain and temperature in the lower half of the body. It does not produce complete transection, posterior, central, or hemisection-type deficit.'),
('375f9dc3-9822-5509-9998-16dc8821ad26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lissauer''s tract (dorsolateral tract) contains', '["Autonomic fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain and temperature fibers entering spinal cord","Corticospinal fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Dorsal column fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinocerebellar fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature fibers entering spinal cord', 'Lissauer''s tract is a small dorsolateral bundle that carries first-order pain and temperature fibers for one or two segments before they synapse in the substantia gelatinosa. Corticospinal, dorsal column, spinocerebellar, and autonomic fibers do not travel in it.'),
('ab9b6c46-bf0b-560a-80c1-67dad91e5267'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is located in the anterior funiculus?', '["Fasciculus cuneatus","Fasciculus gracilis","Lateral corticospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract","Anterior corticospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior corticospinal tract', 'Of the listed tracts, only the anterior (uncrossed) corticospinal tract occupies the anterior funiculus. The lateral corticospinal tract lies in the lateral column, the fasciculi gracilis and cuneatus lie posteriorly, and the spinothalamic tract ascends in the lateral and anterior columns.'),
('eb1770ca-8541-5169-9630-b0e29025a9a3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lateral horn of spinal cord is present at', '["C1-C8","L3-S5","All segments","Cervical only","T1-L2 segments"]'::jsonb, 'T1-L2 segments', 'The lateral horn contains the sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies, which are present only from T1 to L2. Sacral parasympathetic neurons lie in the S2-S4 region, and no lateral horn exists at other segmental levels.'),
('e33cd609-e0aa-5e6a-8e21-b6ad9ddf7a6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Brown-Sequard syndrome is caused by which type of spinal cord lesion?', '["Posterior cord lesion","Complete transection","Hemisection (one-half of spinal cord cut)","Anterior cord lesion","Central cord lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Hemisection (one-half of spinal cord cut)', 'Hemisection of one half of the cord interrupts the ipsilateral dorsal columns and corticospinal tract plus the contralateral spinothalamic tract, giving the classic Brown-Sequard picture. Complete transection, central, anterior, or posterior cord lesions each produce a different named syndrome.'),
('d98feeee-ff8e-5136-8fbe-3eccee20c2d2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract crosses at the level of the spinal cord?', '["Corticospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract","Medial lemniscus","Spinothalamic tract","Tectospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract', 'Spinothalamic fibers cross through the anterior white commissure at each segment of the spinal cord before ascending. The medial lemniscus decussates in the medulla, while the corticospinal tract crosses at the pyramidal decussation and the rubrospinal and tectospinal tracts cross in the midbrain.'),
('f0ac715c-e585-588e-bd3e-555b54e8893c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract crosses at the medulla?', '["Rubrospinal tract","Medial lemniscus","Spinothalamic tract","Tectospinal tract","Spinocerebellar tract"]'::jsonb, 'Medial lemniscus', 'The medial lemniscus is formed by internal arcuate fibers that cross the midline in the medullary sensory decussation. The spinothalamic tract has already crossed in the spinal cord, and the other listed tracts do not decussate at the medulla.'),
('97cdfdb0-ac25-51de-92f4-7e4fc1927989'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract crosses at the midbrain?', '["Corticospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract","Rubrospinal and tectospinal tracts","Spinocerebellar tract","Medial lemniscus"]'::jsonb, 'Rubrospinal and tectospinal tracts', 'Both the rubrospinal tract (ventral tegmental decussation) and the tectospinal tract (dorsal tegmental decussation) cross within the midbrain. Corticospinal fibers cross in the medulla and spinothalamic fibers cross in the spinal cord.'),
('54f09033-9393-59b6-b697-25e9fb6da92e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Corticonuclear (corticobulbar) tract supplies cranial nerve motor nuclei', '["All contralaterally resulting in decreased functional activity","Only facial accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only trigeminal mediated by specific receptor activation","All ipsilaterally without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bilaterally except lower face (CN VII) and tongue (CN XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Bilaterally except lower face (CN VII) and tongue (CN XII)', 'Corticobulbar fibers innervate most cranial motor nuclei bilaterally, but the subnuclei supplying the lower face (CN VII) and the tongue (CN XII) receive mainly contralateral input. A unilateral lesion therefore produces contralateral lower facial and tongue weakness while sparing the upper face.'),
('8598afa5-7a0d-5350-a314-3b6118cc05a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Central cord syndrome typically results from', '["Anterior spinal artery occlusion","Compression of cauda equina","Posterior spinal artery occlusion","Spinal cord hemisection","Syrinx or central cord injury at cervical level"]'::jsonb, 'Syrinx or central cord injury at cervical level', 'Central cord syndrome follows syringomyelia or a hyperextension injury centered on the cervical cord, damaging the crossing spinothalamic fibers and medial corticospinal fibers. It produces greater upper-limb than lower-limb weakness, while the other listed causes give different cord syndromes.'),
('f40de7a7-4492-5a03-83de-a9bfa1e0fae5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Posterior cord syndrome results from occlusion of', '["Artery of Adamkiewicz","Posterior spinal arteries","Basilar artery","Anterior spinal artery","Vertebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior spinal arteries', 'The paired posterior spinal arteries supply the dorsal columns and posterior third of the cord, so their occlusion produces a posterior cord syndrome with loss of proprioception and vibration. The anterior spinal artery and artery of Adamkiewicz are not involved.'),
('50686e12-430a-5b0e-8519-182a88e8fa48'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Complete spinal cord transection at C6 causes', '["Hemiplegia","Monoplegia","Triplegia","Quadriplegia","Paraplegia"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriplegia', 'A complete transection at C6 interrupts the cervical segments that innervate all four limbs, producing quadriplegia. Paraplegia would follow a lesion below the cervical and thoracic enlargements, while hemiplegia points to a cerebral or unilateral cord problem.'),
('5bb46641-6ea1-5f90-a14c-725ce882d3ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which spinal cord segment has the largest anterior horn?', '["Sacral resulting in decreased functional activity","Coccygeal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cervical enlargement (C5-T1)","Lumbar without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thoracic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical enlargement (C5-T1)', 'The cervical enlargement (C5-T1) contains the largest anterior horn cell mass because it innervates the brachial plexus and upper limb. Lumbar and sacral segments carry smaller motor columns, and thoracic segments are the smallest.'),
('27606434-a577-5ca3-a085-eec5495dacb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5) originates from which spinal segments?', '["C3, C4, C5","C1, C2, C3","C2, C3, C4","C4, C5, C6","C5, C6, C7"]'::jsonb, 'C3, C4, C5', 'The phrenic nerve receives contributions from C3, C4, and C5, providing essentially all of the diaphragm''s motor supply. Injury at or above these segments threatens diaphragmatic breathing.'),
('a5e00839-204a-54a4-af43-569ce4b70676'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal nerves C1-C7 exit', '["Below corresponding vertebrae","Above corresponding vertebrae","Through intervertebral foramina of same level","Below T1","At sacral level"]'::jsonb, 'Above corresponding vertebrae', 'Cervical roots C1-C7 leave the canal above their corresponding vertebrae, whereas C8 exits below the C7 vertebra. This numbering rule explains the mismatch between root level and vertebral level in the cervical region.'),
('0e134833-661b-59b2-8bb0-57136e529dab'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal nerves from T1 onward exit', '["Through transverse foramina","Below corresponding vertebrae","At same level","Through sacral hiatus","Above corresponding vertebrae"]'::jsonb, 'Below corresponding vertebrae', 'From T1 downward each spinal nerve exits below its numbered vertebra, so the root number matches the vertebra below. This is the reverse of the cervical rule and explains why lumbar and sacral roots descend within the canal to form the cauda equina.'),
('da209080-1ad9-505f-b827-ec93875f2b71'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Conus medullaris syndrome causes', '["Hemiplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","Brown-Sequard accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Quadriplegia mediated by specific receptor activation","Saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction","Spastic paraplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction', 'The conus medullaris contains the sacral segments, so compression produces saddle anesthesia and sphincter (bowel and bladder) dysfunction with flaccid weakness of the feet. Hemiplegia, quadriplegia, or spastic paraplegia point to higher or more extensive cord lesions.'),
('77bf656d-7ef6-53d1-afb4-0b31cdc3665c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Cauda equina syndrome involves', '["Quadriplegia resulting in decreased functional activity","Hemiplegia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation","UMN signs in legs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LMN signs in legs, saddle anesthesia, bladder/bowel loss"]'::jsonb, 'LMN signs in legs, saddle anesthesia, bladder/bowel loss', 'Cauda equina syndrome compresses lumbosacral nerve roots (lower motor neurons), causing flaccid weakness, depressed reflexes, saddle anesthesia, and sphincter loss. Upper motor neuron signs would indicate a lesion of the spinal cord itself rather than the roots.'),
('ee6a95fa-5487-52f3-9ff1-3a117a246aee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is NOT part of the spinocerebellar pathway?', '["Superior cerebellar peduncle","Medial geniculate body","Inferior cerebellar peduncle","Clarke''s nucleus","Accessory cuneate nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial geniculate body', 'The medial geniculate body is an auditory relay nucleus of the thalamus, not a component of the spinocerebellar system. The spinocerebellar pathway uses Clarke''s nucleus and the accessory cuneate nucleus together with the inferior and superior cerebellar peduncles.'),
('b60edeed-7469-534e-9a46-41ab40f1d8e0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Clarke''s nucleus (dorsal nucleus) extends from', '["C1-C8","L1-S5","C8-L3","T1-T12","C1-L5"]'::jsonb, 'C8-L3', 'Clarke''s nucleus extends from C8 to L3 and gives rise to the posterior spinocerebellar tract, which reaches the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle. It relays unconscious proprioception from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs of the lower limb and trunk.'),
('c5581a05-9b21-517b-8a50-784a45fc501d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which spinal tract is responsible for spasticity in UMN lesions?', '["Spinocerebellar","Spinothalamic","Corticospinal tract","Spinotectal","Dorsal column"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract', 'Interruption of the corticospinal (upper motor neuron) tract removes descending inhibition of spinal reflexes, yielding hyperreflexia and spasticity. Sensory tracts such as the spinothalamic and dorsal column pathways do not produce these signs.'),
('a0ccd9f7-5986-56bf-941e-05eec3a99a94'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Fasciculus proprius (spinospinal tract) is responsible for', '["Motor commands resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision reflexes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Intersegmental (propriospinal) reflexes","Pain transmission without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Balance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intersegmental (propriospinal) reflexes', 'The fasciculus proprius is a thin column of short association fibers that surrounds the gray matter and links multiple segments, coordinating intersegmental synergy of spinal reflexes. It does not conduct long-tract motor, pain, or balance signals.'),
('8fea1b94-8467-5c31-9e5e-3b4a3972d8af'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The blood supply of the lumbar enlargement is mainly from', '["Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Basilar artery","Artery of Adamkiewicz","Vertebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Artery of Adamkiewicz', 'The artery of Adamkiewicz is the dominant source of blood for the thoracolumbar cord and lumbar enlargement. The named cerebellar and vertebral arteries do not supply the lumbar spinal cord.'),
('9bf55bdb-e692-5bf7-96ad-fc43c978c585'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal shock refers to', '["Transient loss of all spinal reflexes below complete transection","Autonomic dysfunction only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Permanent paralysis mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory loss only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transient loss of all spinal reflexes below complete transection', 'Spinal shock is the transient areflexia and flaccidity that follows an acute complete cord transection, as the isolated cord temporarily loses descending facilitation. It later resolves into spasticity and hyperreflexia rather than remaining a permanent or purely sensory state.'),
('dd305087-ca20-5421-b178-1d8b513e5ff1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'During spinal shock, which is initially lost?', '["Only sensory function","Cranial nerve reflexes","All reflexes below lesion","Only motor function","Only autonomic function"]'::jsonb, 'All reflexes below lesion', 'In spinal shock every spinal reflex below the level of transection is initially absent, along with flaccid paralysis muscle tone. Reflexes above the lesion and cranial nerve reflexes are preserved.'),
('098e9cbc-afff-5167-82d8-d48c6d638a7c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord terminates at which vertebral level in adults?', '["L1-L2","L3-L4","T12-L1","S1-S2","L2-L3"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'In adults the spinal cord ends at the lower border of the L1 vertebra, around the L1-L2 disc. In infancy the conus lies lower and ascends as vertebral growth outpaces cord growth, never reaching the sacrum.'),
('3e5ffcb9-780d-53ab-a135-c4f44ccf2d76'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lumbar puncture is safely performed at', '["S1-S2 resulting in decreased functional activity","L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace","T4-T5 mediated by specific receptor activation","C1-C2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12-L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace', 'Lumbar puncture is performed at or below L3-L4 to stay safely below the cord tip (L1-L2) and avoid puncturing the conus medullaris. At these levels only the mobile nerve roots of the cauda equina are present in the subarachnoid space.'),
('855d1f70-8086-5fa1-bf9d-adb24ec6b3a7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Thecal sac ends at which vertebral level?', '["S2","S4","C7","L3","L1"]'::jsonb, 'S2', 'The dural (thecal) sac and the subarachnoid space terminate at the S2 level in adults. The spinal cord itself ends much higher, at L1-L2, while the epidural space continues caudally.'),
('72299f19-0a5e-5a2f-ab32-ee2f8b8fa546'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Epidural space contains', '["Pia mater resulting in decreased functional activity","CSF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fat, connective tissue, internal vertebral venous plexus","Arachnoid mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal nerves only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat, connective tissue, internal vertebral venous plexus', 'The epidural space lies between the dura mater and the vertebral periosteum and contains fat, connective tissue, and the internal vertebral venous plexus, with spinal nerve roots passing through it. CSF is confined to the subarachnoid space, and the pia and arachnoid lie deeper.'),
('516f1845-5d6c-51c4-a3ca-b1a97cb671b7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Subarachnoid space contains', '["CSF","Venous plexus","Fat","Bone","Ligaments"]'::jsonb, 'CSF', 'The subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater and contains circulating cerebrospinal fluid. Fat and the venous plexus belong to the epidural space, and bone and ligaments are outside the meninges.'),
('2ad989bb-e007-5e69-bec0-9917caa658c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is not a spinal cord tract?', '["Corticospinal","Fasciculus gracilis","Spinothalamic","Spinocerebellar","Medial longitudinal fasciculus"]'::jsonb, 'Medial longitudinal fasciculus', 'The medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) is a brainstem tract that coordinates eye movements with vestibular input, not a spinal cord tract. The corticospinal, spinothalamic, spinocerebellar, and gracilis tracts are all spinal cord pathways.'),
('64c369e2-dab7-5765-a0fe-db1c64c1b471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Cuneocerebellar tract terminates in', '["Spinal cord","Cerebellum (ipsilateral)","Cerebral cortex","Thalamus","Red nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum (ipsilateral)', 'The cuneocerebellar tract carries upper-limb proprioception through the accessory cuneate nucleus and inferior cerebellar peduncle to the ipsilateral cerebellum. It does not terminate in the spinal cord, cortex, thalamus, or red nucleus.'),
('c950f7a2-c174-55c5-9f9c-2464f2ef3e22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The accessory cuneate nucleus is located in', '["Medulla oblongata","Pons","Midbrain","Spinal cord","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata', 'The accessory cuneate nucleus lies in the dorsal medulla, lateral to the cuneate nucleus, and relays proprioceptive input from the upper limb to the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle. It is not a pontine, midbrain, or spinal structure.'),
('e7c0ce0e-624a-5677-b3ea-49d504be842d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract mediates auditory and visual reflexes?', '["Corticospinal","Vestibulospinal","Tectospinal tract","Rubrospinal","Reticulospinal"]'::jsonb, 'Tectospinal tract', 'The tectospinal tract arises from the superior (visual) and inferior (auditory) colliculi, crosses in the midbrain, and mediates head and neck orienting reflexes to visual and auditory stimuli. The other listed tracts govern limb, vestibular, or voluntary motor control.'),
('ef162da3-d242-5309-8acc-1b516aee3251'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Vestibulospinal tract facilitates', '["Facial muscles","Flexor muscles","Extensor (antigravity) muscles","Eye muscles","Sphincters"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor (antigravity) muscles', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract descends in the spinal cord to facilitate extensor (antigravity) muscles, maintaining posture and balance. Facilitation of facial, flexor, ocular, or sphincter muscles is not the role of this tract.'),
('2a84ab0e-332b-5282-928e-2fd2a4db906b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Pontine reticulospinal tract facilitates', '["Hearing","Vision","Extensor tone","Pain transmission","Flexor tone"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor tone', 'The pontine (medial) reticulospinal tract excites spinal alpha and gamma motor neurons and facilitates extensor (antigravity) tone. The medullary reticulospinal tract exerts the opposite inhibitory influence.'),
('288c3f8c-692c-5529-a755-f37af2910a2a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Medullary reticulospinal tract inhibits', '["Vision","Extensor tone","Pain","Motor cortex","Flexor tone"]'::jsonb, 'Extensor tone', 'The medullary (lateral) reticulospinal tract inhibits extensor tone, opposing the facilitatory pontine reticulospinal pathway. This balance between the two tracts regulates posture and antigravity muscle activity.'),
('78c589e3-f8a5-5469-a19a-4360bf6b46bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which nucleus is found in lamina VIII of Rexed?', '["Substantia gelatinosa","Nucleus proprius","Clarke''s nucleus","Commissural nucleus","Motor nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Commissural nucleus', 'Lamina VIII, the medial part of the anterior horn, contains the commissural nucleus of interneurons that project to motor neurons on both sides of the cord. The substantia gelatinosa lies in lamina II, the nucleus proprius in laminae III-IV, Clarke''s nucleus in lamina VII, and motor neurons in lamina IX.'),
('52ef146f-a58a-5724-90b2-faf8016ac630'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Rexed lamina X surrounds', '["Posterior horn","White matter","Anterior horn","Central canal","Lateral horn"]'::jsonb, 'Central canal', 'Rexed lamina X is the gray matter that surrounds the central canal. The posterior, anterior, and lateral horns occupy other laminae (II, III-IV, IX, and VII) away from the central canal.'),
('3604941a-eef4-5c6c-8b10-c9f79a922f4e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The percentage of corticospinal fibers that decussate is', '["100%","85-90%","10-15%","50%","25%"]'::jsonb, '85-90%', 'About 85-90% of corticospinal fibers cross in the pyramidal decussation to form the lateral corticospinal tract. The remaining 10-15% remain ipsilateral and descend in the anterior (ventral) corticospinal tract.'),
('fc40cf0a-bea2-5f5a-9b87-110cc49e2eb3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anterior corticospinal tract fibers control', '["Upper limb muscles","Extraocular muscles","Lower limb muscles","Facial muscles","Axial and proximal trunk muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Axial and proximal trunk muscles', 'The anterior corticospinal tract is the uncrossed component of the corticospinal system and terminates mostly on medial motor neurons supplying axial and proximal trunk muscles. Most of its fibers eventually cross within the spinal cord at segmental levels.'),
('ef4bb7fa-f5db-5372-93ce-8ebf08f307e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract degenerates in primary lateral sclerosis?', '["Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal column","Spinocerebellar tract","Anterior horn cells","Corticospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract', 'Primary lateral sclerosis is a pure upper motor neuron disorder causing degeneration of the corticospinal tract. Clinically it produces progressive spasticity and weakness without muscle atrophy or fasciculations.'),
('419e6db8-704a-52d4-ac06-952ca1465705'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Progressive muscular atrophy affects', '["Corticospinal tract","Anterior horn cells (LMN only)","Autonomic tracts","Sensory tracts","Both UMN and LMN"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior horn cells (LMN only)', 'Progressive muscular atrophy is a lower motor neuron variant of motor neuron disease involving anterior horn cells. It presents with muscle wasting, weakness and fasciculations while sensory function remains intact.'),
('1663e07d-694d-58ad-8af4-b194656d51bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Werdnig-Hoffmann disease (spinal muscular atrophy type 1) affects', '["Neuromuscular junction","Dorsal root ganglia","Peripheral nerves","Corticospinal tract","Anterior horn cells"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior horn cells', 'Werdnig-Hoffmann disease is the infantile form of spinal muscular atrophy caused by SMN1 gene defects. Degeneration of anterior horn cells produces severe hypotonia, weakness and early death.'),
('fbdf3a79-ac8e-5ac2-9e21-25e65531b83f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Tabes dorsalis is caused by', '["Multiple sclerosis resulting in decreased functional activity","ALS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal cord tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Tertiary syphilis affecting dorsal columns","Poliovirus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tertiary syphilis affecting dorsal columns', 'Tabes dorsalis is a late (tertiary) syphilitic manifestation characterized by degeneration of the dorsal columns and dorsal roots. It causes ataxia, loss of proprioception, areflexia and lightning (lancinating) pains.'),
('0e67d1e4-2e74-5b66-ad6a-943bf04e541a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Romberg sign positive indicates damage to', '["Cerebellum","Vestibular system","Basal ganglia","Dorsal columns","Motor cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal columns', 'A positive Romberg sign indicates loss of proprioceptive input transmitted by the dorsal column pathway. With eyes closed, the patient sways because vision can no longer compensate for the loss of position sense.'),
('0b4626d6-12da-5cd3-9a5f-962c08ee0eb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lhermitte sign (electric shock sensation on neck flexion) is seen in', '["Vestibular disease","Myopathy","Dorsal column dysfunction","Motor neuron disease","Cerebellar disease"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column dysfunction', 'Lhermitte sign, an electric-shock sensation radiating down the spine on neck flexion, results from irritation of the dorsal columns. It characteristically accompanies cervical cord demyelination (e.g., multiple sclerosis) or other cervical myelopathies.'),
('fe566c03-c23a-506d-b7fd-cdbb25ed5ec6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Subacute combined degeneration of spinal cord (B12 deficiency) affects', '["Only corticospinal","Only dorsal columns","Anterior horn cells","Spinothalamic and spinocerebellar","Dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts', 'Vitamin B12 deficiency produces demyelination of both the dorsal columns and the lateral corticospinal tracts. The result is combined loss of vibration and proprioception together with spasticity and upgoing plantar responses.'),
('87a49cd3-a920-59e0-a5ee-b223da60aa1b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is the earliest sign of subacute combined degeneration?', '["Bladder dysfunction resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of vibration sense in lower limbs","Ataxia mediated by specific receptor activation","Babinski sign without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spastic paraplegia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of vibration sense in lower limbs', 'Loss of vibration sense in the lower limbs is the earliest manifestation because the dorsal column fibers of the fasciculus gracilis are affected first in B12 deficiency. Motor signs such as spasticity and Babinski responses appear later.'),
('9202f9f1-b6d9-5773-8e43-e3b15f577525'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The white matter anterior commissure contains crossing fibers of', '["Spinocerebellar tract","Corticospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal column"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract', 'The anterior white commissure carries the decussating axons of the spinothalamic tract, which cross to the contralateral side and ascend in the anterolateral funiculus. A commissural lesion produces bilateral, segmental loss of pain and temperature.'),
('32cbbe96-6704-5962-91f1-358fdf026c4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is located most medially in the dorsal column?', '["Fasciculus cuneatus","Interfascicular tract","Septomarginal tract","Fasciculus gracilis","Lissauer''s tract"]'::jsonb, 'Fasciculus gracilis', 'Within the dorsal (posterior) funiculus the fasciculus gracilis lies medially, conveying fibers from the lower half of the body. The fasciculus cuneatus lies laterally and carries fibers from the upper half of the body.'),
('75423ecb-9ca3-5573-9a72-502637ac5f0e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Septomarginal and interfascicular tracts are', '["Intersegmental (propriospinal) fibers in dorsal column","Tracts in anterior column accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ascending tracts mediated by specific receptor activation","Descending tracts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tracts in lateral column leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intersegmental (propriospinal) fibers in dorsal column', 'The septomarginal and interfascicular tracts are short intersegmental (propriospinal) bundles located in the dorsal column that interconnect adjacent spinal segments. They are not long ascending or descending tracts.'),
('44695036-00ab-5938-93af-c20fc5088a9b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Motor neurons supplying flexor muscles are located', '["Anterior to extensor motor neurons resulting in decreased functional activity","In posterior horn accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial to extensor mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior to extensor motor neurons in anterior horn","Lateral to extensor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior to extensor motor neurons in anterior horn', 'Within the anterior horn the motor neurons for flexor muscles lie posterior (dorsal) to those for extensor muscles. Extensor neurons are consequently placed more anteriorly within the horn.'),
('6bca06cd-5895-57dd-a08a-bef514bceb44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Medial group of anterior horn cells innervates', '["Distal limb muscles","Flexor muscles","Sphincters","Axial and proximal muscles","Extensor muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Axial and proximal muscles', 'The medial motor neuron column runs the full length of the cord and innervates axial and proximal (trunk) muscles. The lateral column, by contrast, supplies the limb muscles and expands at the segmental enlargements.'),
('9ed9c36f-2878-5b88-9fad-70e8dce56819'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lateral group of anterior horn cells is most developed at', '["Thoracic levels","Cervical and lumbar enlargements","Coccygeal levels","All levels equally","Sacral levels"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical and lumbar enlargements', 'The lateral motor neuron column is most developed at the cervical and lumbosacral enlargements because these segments innervate the limbs. At thoracic levels the lateral column is small or absent.'),
('10f19997-e408-5865-be46-5c937ae5cf3c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Phrenic nucleus is located at', '["C3-C5 anterior horn","C1-C2 anterior horn","C5-C7 anterior horn","T1-T4 anterior horn","C4-C6 posterior horn"]'::jsonb, 'C3-C5 anterior horn', 'The phrenic nucleus lies in the medial motor column of the anterior horn at C3-C5 and supplies the diaphragm. High cervical lesions above C3 therefore cause respiratory failure due to loss of diaphragmatic innervation.'),
('6b2947d9-b375-511e-a42d-4d5df6161e68'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Onuf''s nucleus in sacral cord controls', '["Rectal smooth muscle","External urethral and anal sphincters","Vasculature","Lower limb muscles","Bladder detrusor"]'::jsonb, 'External urethral and anal sphincters', 'Onuf''s nucleus in the sacral anterior horn (S2-S4) supplies the external urethral and anal sphincters. It is characteristically spared in ALS but degenerates in multiple system atrophy.'),
('d6d08c34-0a4c-57a7-b054-5c17e0a93d5c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is NOT found in the lateral funiculus?', '["Anterior corticospinal tract","Lateral corticospinal tract","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract","Rubrospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior corticospinal tract', 'The anterior corticospinal tract descends in the anterior funiculus, not the lateral funiculus. The lateral funiculus contains the lateral corticospinal, rubrospinal, dorsal spinocerebellar and spinothalamic tracts.'),
('dda52fac-743d-5900-8cde-8af17b2abf44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The corticospinal tract is located in which part of the lateral funiculus?', '["Medial part","Lateral border","Dorsal part","Ventral part","Middle part"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal part', 'The lateral corticospinal tract occupies the dorsal part of the lateral funiculus. The rubrospinal tract lies just ventral (anterior) to it within the same funiculus.'),
('401faf01-5846-5528-9a44-8ec41e014ab4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract lies anterior to the lateral corticospinal tract?', '["Tectospinal","Ventral spinocerebellar","Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal spinocerebellar","Rubrospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Rubrospinal tract', 'The rubrospinal tract lies immediately anterior (ventral) to the lateral corticospinal tract in the lateral funiculus. The dorsal spinocerebellar tract lies posterior to the lateral corticospinal tract.'),
('ce1b6935-4f9a-5f87-9804-5fbc8b5fc856'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Ventral spinocerebellar tract is located', '["In posterior funiculus","Deep in lateral funiculus","Superficial in lateral funiculus","In central gray","In anterior funiculus"]'::jsonb, 'Superficial in lateral funiculus', 'The ventral (anterior) spinocerebellar tract runs superficially on the anterolateral surface of the lateral funiculus. It carries unconscious proprioceptive information from the lower limbs to the cerebellum.'),
('dc48cc71-a09f-5ce0-baf5-a5105bd1eacc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Dorsal spinocerebellar tract lies', '["In posterior funiculus","Anterolateral","Posterolateral in lateral funiculus","In anterior funiculus","Near central canal"]'::jsonb, 'Posterolateral in lateral funiculus', 'The dorsal (posterior) spinocerebellar tract lies at the posterolateral margin of the lateral funiculus, adjacent to the surface near the dorsal roots. It arises from the dorsal nucleus of Clarke and is uncrossed.'),
('44621ae7-1f00-5f3d-b434-6083c3b5a66f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is the most superficial tract in the lateral funiculus?', '["Lateral corticospinal","Ventral spinocerebellar","Dorsal spinocerebellar","Rubrospinal","Spinothalamic"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral spinocerebellar', 'The ventral spinocerebellar tract is the most superficial tract, lying directly on the anterolateral surface of the lateral funiculus. The dorsal spinocerebellar and lateral corticospinal tracts lie deeper.'),
('c05b96da-862f-5a57-972d-e93573e159dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinotectal tract is found in', '["Dorsal column","Anterior funiculus","Lateral corticospinal area","Anterolateral funiculus","Posterior funiculus"]'::jsonb, 'Anterolateral funiculus', 'The spinotectal tract ascends in the anterolateral funiculus and terminates in the superior colliculus. It participates in spinovisual reflexes that orient the eyes and head toward painful stimuli.'),
('d3063393-5cb8-5a57-ab93-5c3df32eb85c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinoreticular tract is located', '["Anterolateral funiculus","Central gray","Denticulate ligament","Posterior funiculus","Lateral corticospinal area"]'::jsonb, 'Anterolateral funiculus', 'The spinoreticular tract ascends in the anterolateral funiculus and synapses in the brainstem reticular formation. It contributes to the arousal and affective components of pain sensation.'),
('73a5b63c-233a-5b09-9b03-d5c08d384c79'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which spinal level has no lateral horn?', '["T1-L2","Thoracic","Cervical and sacral","None","Upper lumbar"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical and sacral', 'The lateral horn (intermediolateral cell column) is present in the thoracic and upper lumbar segments and carries autonomic outflow. Cervical segments lack a well-defined lateral horn, and the sacral parasympathetic nucleus is confined to the mid-sacral levels.'),
('39b2bc3d-5ed4-5055-b9a8-7732e4d3065c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Sympathetic preganglionic neurons are located in', '["Intermediolateral cell column (T1-L2)","Sacral cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior horn mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior horn without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal root ganglia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediolateral cell column (T1-L2)', 'Sympathetic preganglionic neurons lie in the intermediolateral cell column (lateral horn) of the T1-L2 segments. Their axons leave through the ventral roots and reach paravertebral and prevertebral ganglia.'),
('d3e0b202-3328-5e40-b639-297f45b3a92b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Preganglionic sympathetic fibers leave spinal cord via', '["Dorsal roots","Lateral horn","Anterior commissure","Dorsal root ganglia","Ventral roots"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral roots', 'Preganglionic sympathetic fibers exit the cord with the ventral (anterior) roots, then traverse white rami communicantes to reach the sympathetic chain. The dorsal roots carry only sensory fibers.'),
('4a62c588-406c-5f30-a0c7-db78e28e704c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'White rami communicantes are present from', '["All levels","S2-S4","C1-C8","T1-L2","L3-S5"]'::jsonb, 'T1-L2', 'White rami communicantes carry preganglionic sympathetic fibers and therefore exist only from T1 through L2, matching the thoracolumbar sympathetic outflow. All other levels have only gray rami.'),
('70388f3e-8a0e-5baa-aa53-9220759a2309'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Gray rami communicantes are present at', '["All spinal levels","T1-L2 only","Thoracic only","Cervical only","Sacral only"]'::jsonb, 'All spinal levels', 'Gray rami communicantes return postganglionic sympathetic fibers to every spinal nerve, so they are present at all spinal levels. Only T1-L2 also have white rami carrying preganglionic fibers.'),
('9c8a1fdb-8ebf-5540-8700-0aeb85e5d7c7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Dorsal root ganglia contain cell bodies of', '["Motor neurons","Autonomic neurons","Second-order sensory neurons","First-order sensory neurons","Interneurons"]'::jsonb, 'First-order sensory neurons', 'The dorsal root ganglia house the cell bodies of first-order (primary) sensory neurons. Their peripheral processes reach receptors and their central processes enter the spinal cord to synapse on second-order neurons.'),
('59d4dd66-63cc-509f-abb9-f3eb0b3689f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which spinal tract is the last to myelinate?', '["Fasciculus cuneatus","Corticospinal tract","Medial lemniscus","Spinothalamic tract","Fasciculus gracilis"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract', 'The corticospinal tract is the last spinal tract to complete myelination, finishing around two years of age. This parallels the gradual maturation of voluntary motor control in infancy.'),
('646bf982-cba0-5341-9543-72248fdc7567'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The spinal tract that myelinates first is', '["Fasciculus gracilis","Spinothalamic tract","Fasciculus cuneatus","Rubrospinal tract","Corticospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract', 'The spinothalamic tract myelinates earlier than the corticospinal tract, consistent with the early functional onset of pain and temperature sensation. Motor pathways myelinate last.'),
('3f736ef6-73cf-578a-9e72-dc489be0d510'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which spinal reflex involves only one spinal segment?', '["Crossed extensor reflex","Defecation reflex","Micturition reflex","Patellar reflex (monosynaptic)","Flexor withdrawal reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Patellar reflex (monosynaptic)', 'The patellar (knee jerk) reflex is monosynaptic, with the Ia afferent synapsing directly on an alpha motor neuron within a single spinal segment. The other listed reflexes are multisynaptic and involve several segments or cord centers.'),
('51a09105-e4ee-5d68-91e5-025e54ed19eb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Gamma motor neurons innervate', '["Joint receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Intrafusal muscle fibers (muscle spindles)","Extra-fusal muscle fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Tendon organs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrafusal muscle fibers (muscle spindles)', 'Gamma motor neurons supply the intrafusal fibers of muscle spindles and regulate spindle sensitivity. They do not generate significant tension in the main muscle, which is the role of alpha motor neurons on extrafusal fibers.'),
('5b815183-6da1-59b1-86f3-37f256940b96'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Alpha motor neurons innervate', '["Extra-fusal muscle fibers","Golgi tendon organs","Joint capsules","Muscle spindles","Intrafusal fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Extra-fusal muscle fibers', 'Alpha motor neurons innervate extrafusal (skeletal) muscle fibers and are responsible for the contractile force of movement. Intrafusal spindle fibers are instead supplied by gamma motor neurons.'),
('543d8bb2-6a1b-5522-8f9e-9b696336ed17'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Renshaw cells are inhibitory interneurons in', '["Central gray","Dorsal column","Lamina VII (anterior horn)","Posterior horn","Lateral horn"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina VII (anterior horn)', 'Renshaw cells are inhibitory interneurons located in Rexed lamina VII near the motor nuclei. They receive collaterals from alpha motor axons and provide recurrent inhibition that limits motor neuron discharge.'),
('e2b9c28b-e1b8-5066-88c9-b7e5232b7332'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Golgi tendon organ reflex is mediated by', '["III afferent fibers","II afferent fibers","Ib afferent fibers","IV afferent fibers","Ia afferent fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Ib afferent fibers', 'Golgi tendon organs are innervated by fast-conducting group Ib afferents that respond to muscle tension. Their activation inhibits the agonist motor neurons, producing autogenic inhibition (the inverse of the stretch reflex).'),
('3623e31b-5143-53b7-87f4-021a9e6973c5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Muscle spindle primary endings (annulospiral) are innervated by', '["Gamma efferents","II afferents","Alpha efferents","Ib afferents","Ia afferents"]'::jsonb, 'Ia afferents', 'The primary (annulospiral) endings of the muscle spindle are served by large group Ia afferents that signal both rate and degree of stretch. These Ia fibers also mediate the monosynaptic tendon reflex.'),
('47c30535-48d8-577a-b8dd-be09ba38477d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Muscle spindle secondary endings (flower-spray) are innervated by', '["II afferents","Alpha efferents","Ia afferents","Ib afferents","Gamma efferents"]'::jsonb, 'II afferents', 'The secondary (flower-spray) endings of the muscle spindle are supplied by group II afferents, which signal chiefly static muscle length. Group Ia afferents, by contrast, respond mainly to dynamic stretch.'),
('0f284d27-193b-5c8a-9ed7-51dbc6681d95'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Clasp-knife phenomenon is due to', '["Muscle spindle (Ia)","Crossed extensor reflex","Golgi tendon organ reflex (Ib)","Withdrawal reflex","Flexor reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Golgi tendon organ reflex (Ib)', 'The clasp-knife phenomenon of sudden resistance collapse during passive stretch in spasticity results from the Golgi tendon organ reflex. Excessive tension activates Ib afferents, which inhibit the agonist motor neurons.'),
('c21a5b20-520c-5e93-9d4d-085ac024a1a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Babinski sign (extensor plantar response) indicates', '["UMN lesion (corticospinal tract)","Cerebellar lesion","LMN lesion","Sensory neuropathy","Basal ganglia lesion"]'::jsonb, 'UMN lesion (corticospinal tract)', 'An extensor plantar (upgoing big toe, Babinski) response indicates damage to the upper motor neuron or corticospinal pathway. It is also normally present in infants before myelination is complete.'),
('97366714-e107-5b6d-8d05-053dc9cf8ef1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In UMN lesion, which is NOT seen?', '["Clonus","Fasciculations","Babinski sign","Spasticity","Hyperreflexia"]'::jsonb, 'Fasciculations', 'Fasciculations reflect denervation activity of lower motor neurons and are absent in a pure UMN lesion. UMN lesions instead produce spasticity, hyperreflexia, clonus and an upgoing plantar response.'),
('70304bd9-2c16-5a6e-974d-6f27326de6d5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In LMN lesion, which is NOT seen?', '["Hyporeflexia","Atrophy","Fasciculations","Hyperreflexia","Flaccid paralysis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperreflexia', 'LMN lesions cause flaccid weakness, muscle atrophy, hyporeflexia and fasciculations. Hyperreflexia and spasticity are features of upper motor neuron, not lower motor neuron, damage.'),
('0d65ac6b-2c3e-5620-9527-340769bbb4ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is an upper motor neuron sign?', '["Areflexia","Flaccid paralysis","Muscle atrophy","Fasciculations","Spasticity and hyperreflexia"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity and hyperreflexia', 'Upper motor neuron lesions produce spasticity and hyperreflexia along with a Babinski response. Areflexia, flaccid paralysis, atrophy and fasciculations are instead features of LMN lesions.'),
('dad1578b-b3f4-534d-9bd5-41709e9dbb54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Poliovirus selectively destroys', '["Sensory neurons in dorsal root ganglia","Cerebellar Purkinje cells","Corticospinal tract","Alpha motor neurons in anterior horn","Autonomic neurons"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha motor neurons in anterior horn', 'Poliovirus has a predilection for alpha motor neurons in the anterior horn of the spinal cord. Destruction of these cells produces flaccid, asymmetric paralysis with intact sensation.'),
('d30672ae-3053-548d-a014-8038543e8e9c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anterior cord syndrome may result from', '["Central disc herniation resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior spinal artery occlusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Trauma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypotension or aortic dissection affecting anterior spinal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Hypotension or aortic dissection affecting anterior spinal artery', 'Anterior cord syndrome follows ischemia in the territory of the anterior spinal artery, as from hypotension or aortic dissection. It causes paralysis and loss of pain/temperature below the lesion while sparing proprioception.'),
('8a968a22-b906-50ef-8f1f-f7a79df0b606'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which syndrome is characterized by loss of pain/temperature with intact proprioception?', '["Anterior cord syndrome","Brown-Sequard","Central cord","Posterior cord syndrome","Conus medullaris"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior cord syndrome', 'Anterior cord syndrome affects the anterior two-thirds of the cord, involving the spinothalamic tracts and corticospinal tracts. The dorsal columns are spared, so proprioception and vibration remain intact.'),
('f0586454-bb2c-5712-b29a-64089f65ef7a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Posterior cord syndrome causes', '["Loss of proprioception and vibration with preserved motor and pain","Loss of pain and temperature only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loss of all sensation mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of motor only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ataxia only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of proprioception and vibration with preserved motor and pain', 'Posterior cord syndrome damages the dorsal columns, producing loss of proprioception and vibration below the lesion. Corticospinal and spinothalamic functions are preserved, so motor strength and pain/temperature sensation are retained.'),
('fc77c5af-a04b-51e9-afd1-dbd8af991cb5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which dermatome is at the level of the nipples?', '["T2","T4","T6","T8","T10"]'::jsonb, 'T4', 'The T4 dermatome supplies the area of the nipples. T2 covers the sternal angle, T6 the xiphisternum, and T8-T10 lie progressively lower on the chest and abdomen.'),
('98746463-5498-59f4-92ed-f61fa6a1ff19'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which dermatome is at the level of the umbilicus?', '["T4","T10","T12","T8","T6"]'::jsonb, 'T10', 'The umbilicus is supplied by the T10 dermatome. T4 is at the nipple, T6 at the xiphisternum, T8 above the umbilicus, and T12 lies just above the pubis.'),
('dd61aeca-106c-5a73-aa46-9a66e1e5ee99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'C5 dermatome corresponds to', '["Thumb","Medial arm","Shoulder","Lateral arm","Little finger"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral arm', 'The C5 dermatome covers the lateral arm (over the deltoid). C6 supplies the thumb, C7 the middle finger, and C8 the little finger, while the medial arm belongs to T1-T2.'),
('98e6fe59-5ac5-5270-ac8c-b727acfdba54'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'C6 dermatome corresponds to', '["Little finger","Shoulder","Thumb and index finger","Medial arm","Nipple"]'::jsonb, 'Thumb and index finger', 'The C6 dermatome supplies the thumb and index finger. The little finger and medial hand are C8, the shoulder is C4-C5, and the nipple and medial arm receive thoracic and T1-T2 supply.'),
('7fb5b8ca-893b-53f1-9270-991154cc56cc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'C8 dermatome corresponds to', '["Little finger and medial hand","Shoulder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thumb mediated by specific receptor activation","Nipple without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Umbilicus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Little finger and medial hand', 'The C8 dermatome supplies the little finger and the medial side of the hand. The thumb is C6, the shoulder is C4-C5, and the nipple and umbilicus are thoracic dermatomes.'),
('6446952d-a0fb-5a98-af25-042f67566133'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'L4 dermatome corresponds to', '["Anterior thigh","Lateral leg and little toe","Posterior thigh","Medial leg and great toe","Perianal area"]'::jsonb, 'Medial leg and great toe', 'The L4 dermatome covers the medial leg and the great toe. The anterior thigh is L2-L3, the lateral leg and little toe belong to L5-S1, the posterior thigh to S1-S2, and the perianal area to S2-S4.'),
('4958199e-554b-529b-9146-ee46cacd4d20'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'S1 dermatome corresponds to', '["Anterior thigh","Great toe","Lateral foot and little toe","Perianal area","Medial leg"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral foot and little toe', 'The S1 dermatome supplies the lateral foot and little toe. The great toe and medial leg are L4, the perianal area is S2-S4, and the anterior thigh is supplied by upper lumbar segments.'),
('e42499ed-e2fd-5dd9-a693-5e8eafcde260'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'S2-S4 dermatomes correspond to', '["Perianal (saddle) area","Thigh","Lower limb","Abdomen","Buttock"]'::jsonb, 'Perianal (saddle) area', 'The S2-S4 dermatomes form the saddle-shaped perianal and perineal region. The thigh, lower limb and abdomen are served by lumbar and upper sacral dermatomes, not by the S2-S4 saddle area.'),
('abaf3322-fd29-54b7-9498-3066c7520d26'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which spinal tract carries information from the same side of the body?', '["Spinothalamic tract","Rubrospinal tract","Lateral corticospinal tract","Tectospinal tract","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal spinocerebellar tract', 'The dorsal (posterior) spinocerebellar tract ascends uncrossed, so it relays proprioceptive input from the same side of the body. The spinothalamic and lateral corticospinal tracts cross, and the rubrospinal and tectospinal tracts are descending pathways.'),
('c90bf5c8-1835-5f2d-8a5f-cf212117edd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which spinal tract crosses twice?', '["Medial lemniscus","Dorsal spinocerebellar tract","Ventral spinocerebellar tract","Lateral corticospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral spinocerebellar tract', 'The ventral (anterior) spinocerebellar tract crosses in the anterior white commissure and crosses again within the cerebellum, giving a net ipsilateral projection after two crossings. The dorsal spinocerebellar tract remains uncrossed.'),
('f3e48e3a-ec07-5157-8d67-a8219c4aa7b8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract carries conscious proprioception?', '["Medial lemniscus (dorsal column)","Corticospinal tract","Spinocerebellar tract","Spinothalamic tract","Rubrospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Medial lemniscus (dorsal column)', 'Conscious proprioception travels in the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway, synapsing in the gracile and cuneate nuclei and decussating at the sensory decussation. The spinocerebellar tracts carry unconscious proprioception to the cerebellum.'),
('5f5e57fc-586f-5a3b-b5ec-4102d5032d09'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract carries unconscious proprioception?', '["Tectospinal tract","Spinocerebellar tracts","Corticospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract","Medial lemniscus"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar tracts', 'The spinocerebellar tracts convey proprioceptive information to the cerebellum without reaching conscious perception. The medial lemniscus serves conscious sensation, and the spinothalamic and descending motor tracts perform other functions.'),
('03ede618-3446-572b-8a4a-ded7dd0e1fdc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The spinal nucleus of trigeminal nerve extends down to which spinal level?', '["C2","S1","L1","T1","C4"]'::jsonb, 'C4', 'The spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve descends from the pons into the medulla and upper cervical cord, reaching down to about C4. C2, T1, S1 and L1 do not mark its inferior limit.'),
('35c82235-4c3a-5b90-8d4f-077c99a65c0d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) arises from', '["Midbrain","Medulla only","C1-C5 anterior horn","Pons","Spinal cord only"]'::jsonb, 'C1-C5 anterior horn', 'The spinal root of the accessory nerve arises from anterior horn cells of C1-C5, ascending through the foramen magnum to join a small cranial root. It is therefore not confined to the midbrain, pons or medulla alone.'),
('f377aedd-48cd-5cc6-a7ed-389d5fa28ea4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is located between fasciculus gracilis and fasciculus cuneatus?', '["Lissauer''s tract","Rubrospinal","Septomarginal tract","Corticospinal","Spinocerebellar"]'::jsonb, 'Septomarginal tract', 'The septomarginal tract lies between the fasciculus gracilis and fasciculus cuneatus and is best seen in thoracic sections. Lissauer''s tract, rubrospinal, corticospinal and spinocerebellar fibers occupy other regions of the white matter.'),
('ce3109fe-f921-56bc-91cc-4631a3add45b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The comma tract (semilunar fasciculus) is located', '["In lateral funiculus resulting in decreased functional activity","In dorsal root entry zone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In anterior funiculus mediated by specific receptor activation","Between gracilis and cuneatus in cervical region","Near central canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Between gracilis and cuneatus in cervical region', 'The comma tract (semilunar fasciculus) lies between the gracile and cuneate fasciculi and is best demonstrated in the cervical region. The lateral funiculus, dorsal root entry zone, anterior funiculus and central-canal area are different locations.'),
('7cb4fcd7-6477-56cc-8a5d-089cf7f9d44e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The triangle of Gombault-Philippe is seen in', '["Anterior horn degeneration","Dorsal column degeneration","Basal ganglia degeneration","Corticospinal degeneration","Cerebellar degeneration"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column degeneration', 'The triangle of Gombault-Philippe is a triangular zone of degeneration in the dorsal columns, near the septomarginal region, and is used in assessing dorsal column disease. It does not localise to the anterior horn, basal ganglia, corticospinal or cerebellar systems.'),
('58c7d2fa-31c2-5273-b2ee-a514ab6b698a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Posterior spinal arteries arise from', '["Internal carotid artery resulting in decreased functional activity","Basilar artery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vertebral or posterior inferior cerebellar arteries","Aorta without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior spinal artery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vertebral or posterior inferior cerebellar arteries', 'The paired posterior spinal arteries usually arise from the vertebral arteries or the posterior inferior cerebellar arteries. The internal carotid, basilar and aorta supply other territories, and the anterior spinal artery is a separate midline vessel.'),
('5c1cfc76-b3f7-570a-9fb1-edae3be05474'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anterior spinal artery arises from', '["Internal carotid","Vertebral artery","Basilar artery","Aorta","External carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Vertebral artery', 'The anterior spinal artery arises near the termination of the vertebral arteries and descends in the anterior median fissure. It does not originate from the internal or external carotid, basilar artery or aorta.'),
('f5583389-ed49-5562-baa4-ac0c3b82efc0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Radicular arteries enter spinal cord via', '["Ventral roots","Dorsal roots","Posterior median sulcus","Anterior median fissure","Lateral surface"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral roots', 'Radicular arteries accompany the ventral (motor) roots to reach the spinal cord. They do not enter through the dorsal roots, posterior median sulcus, anterior median fissure or lateral surface.'),
('183adde4-2782-59a1-b03a-8b9fae56849a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The watershed area of spinal cord is at', '["T4-T8 (mid-thoracic)","Sacral","Cervicothoracic","Lumbar","Cervical"]'::jsonb, 'T4-T8 (mid-thoracic)', 'The T4-T8 mid-thoracic region lies at the junction of the upper (vertebral) and lower (aortic and radicular) blood supplies, making it the watershed area of the cord. Cervical, lumbar and sacral segments have more robust segmental supply.'),
('c35de673-d554-5918-8a90-99682afa2298'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which segment of spinal cord is most vulnerable to ischemic damage?', '["T4-T8","C5-T1","C1-C4","L1-L5","S1-S4"]'::jsonb, 'T4-T8', 'The T4-T8 segment is the arterial watershed zone and is consequently the most vulnerable to ischemic damage. Cervical and lumbosacral segments receive richer segmental blood supply.'),
('ace90de4-4ab9-5d50-af90-6da53d1a25ed'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Ependymal cell lining of central canal is derived from', '["Neuroepithelial cells","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Mesenchyme","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Neuroepithelial cells', 'Ependymal cells lining the central canal arise from the neuroepithelial (ventricular) cells of the neural tube. Mesoderm, endoderm, mesenchyme and neural crest give rise to different structures.'),
('09f11863-2b75-577b-8e43-7351abac75ee'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The central canal is obliterated in adults at', '["Lumbar only","All levels","levels","Sacral only","Most levels except cervical"]'::jsonb, 'Most levels except cervical', 'In adults the central canal becomes obliterated at most spinal levels but usually remains patent in the cervical region. Obliteration does not occur at all levels, nor is it limited to the lumbar or sacral cord alone.'),
('551a078e-8c45-529a-9441-2f2abe19fbc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Hydromyelia is', '["Dilation of central canal lined by ependyma","Inflammation of spinal cord","Tumor of spinal cord","Cavity not lined by ependyma","Infection of spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Dilation of central canal lined by ependyma', 'Hydromyelia is dilatation of the central canal, which remains lined by ependyma; this distinguishes it from syringomyelia, whose cavity is not ependymal-lined. The other choices describe inflammation, infection, tumour or a non-lined cavity.'),
('a948864d-1d4b-53d4-9a53-2f00d2fdac0c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is not a feature of syringomyelia?', '["Fasciculations","UMN signs in lower limbs","Dissociated sensory loss","Lower limb spasticity","Neuropathic joints"]'::jsonb, 'UMN signs in lower limbs', 'Syringomyelia characteristically produces dissociated sensory loss, lower motor neuron signs such as fasciculations in the upper limbs, and neuropathic joints. Upper motor neuron signs in the lower limbs are not a characteristic finding of the lesion, making them the exception, while lower limb spasticity and dissociated sensory loss are recognised features.'),
('b96235ae-aef0-5016-9064-378b1207d0c6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Charcot joints (neuropathic arthropathy) are seen in', '["Polio resulting in decreased functional activity","Stroke accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Syringomyelia and tabes dorsalis","Myasthenia gravis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ALS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Syringomyelia and tabes dorsalis', 'Neuropathic (Charcot) joints result from loss of joint sensation with repeated microtrauma, classically in syringomyelia (upper limbs) and tabes dorsalis (lower limbs). Polio, stroke, myasthenia gravis and ALS do not typically produce neuropathic arthropathy.'),
('575beb85-e598-56f2-b551-1340cac17da4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which condition causes bilateral loss of pain/temperature at C4-T1 with cape-like distribution?', '["Anterior cord syndrome","Posterior cord syndrome","ALS","Brown-Sequard","Syringomyelia"]'::jsonb, 'Syringomyelia', 'Bilateral, cape-like loss of pain and temperature over C4-T1 is the classic dissociated sensory loss of syringomyelia, caused by damage to crossing spinothalamic fibers. The other choices produce different cord syndromes or combined motor findings.'),
('46a2d06f-1209-529c-8ea0-9bd9b123dc42'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spina bifida occulta involves', '["Cauda equina compression resulting in decreased functional activity","Neural tissue protrusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete spinal cord exposure mediated by specific receptor activation","Failure of vertebral arch fusion without neural exposure","Meningeal protrusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of vertebral arch fusion without neural exposure', 'Spina bifida occulta is failure of the vertebral arch to fuse, with intact overlying skin and no exposure or protrusion of neural tissue. Meningocele and myelomeningocele involve protrusion of meninges or cord, and cauda equina compression is not a defining feature.'),
('512bbff8-3319-5b0a-b379-d44e75f2f530'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Myelomeningocele involves', '["Dural sac only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protrusion of spinal cord and meninges through vertebral defect","Only meninges without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only nerve roots leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protrusion of spinal cord and meninges through vertebral defect', 'Myelomeningocele is protrusion of both spinal cord tissue and meninges through a vertebral defect. Involvement limited to the dural sac, meninges, cord or nerve roots alone does not describe the full lesion.'),
('af19331f-428d-5cca-8f66-b46b56c68a3a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Meningocele involves', '["Protrusion of spinal cord","protrusion","Protrusion of nerve roots","Protrusion of meninges only (CSF-filled)","Vertebral body absence"]'::jsonb, 'Protrusion of meninges only (CSF-filled)', 'A meningocele is a CSF-filled protrusion of meninges only through a vertebral defect, without neural tissue. Protrusion of the cord or nerve roots indicates a more severe defect, and absence of the vertebral body is a different abnormality.'),
('6406b5a2-5402-59ae-b8e2-f982e7705278'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The motor homunculus in precentral gyrus has the largest representation for', '["Back","Neck","Lower limb","Trunk","Hand and face"]'::jsonb, 'Hand and face', 'In the motor homunculus the hand and face occupy disproportionately large cortical areas because they perform the finest, most skilled movements. The back, neck, trunk and lower limb have much smaller representations.'),
('0af072fb-9694-57ca-b37b-b9c0d06043b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The sensory homunculus in postcentral gyrus has largest representation for', '["Lower limb resulting in decreased functional activity","Neck accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trunk mediated by specific receptor activation","Lips, fingers, and tongue","Back leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lips, fingers, and tongue', 'The sensory homunculus gives the largest territory to the lips, fingers and tongue, reflecting their high tactile acuity. The trunk, back, neck and lower limb occupy considerably smaller areas.'),
('eed3f605-5cc2-529d-aa7e-5a16dbc9cd99'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is tested by checking two-point discrimination?', '["Rubrospinal tract","Corticospinal tract","Dorsal column (medial lemniscus)","Spinothalamic tract","Spinocerebellar tract"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column (medial lemniscus)', 'Two-point discrimination is a test of fine discriminative touch carried by the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway. The spinothalamic tract mediates pain, temperature and crude touch, and the spinocerebellar and descending tracts are not involved in this sensation.'),
('c0807ea9-cfcf-5de6-9d11-ff92fd10f7c3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is tested by checking stereognosis?', '["Tectospinal","Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal column (fine touch)","Corticospinal","Spinocerebellar"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column (fine touch)', 'Stereognosis, the recognition of objects handled without vision, depends on the fine-touch and proprioceptive input of the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway. The spinothalamic, tectospinal, corticospinal and spinocerebellar fasciculi do not serve this function.'),
('dd40e498-41f5-52d1-86ae-49b2f294783b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is tested by checking graphesthesia?', '["Spinothalamic","Corticospinal","Rubrospinal","Dorsal column","Spinocerebellar"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column', 'Graphesthesia, recognition of figures traced on the skin, requires intact dorsal column function for discriminative touch. The spinothalamic, rubrospinal, corticospinal and spinocerebellar tracts are not directly tested by this maneuver.'),
('e2fd261b-6420-5d6a-b061-de3d64163ee0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is tested by checking vibration sense (tuning fork)?', '["Vestibulospinal","Dorsal column","Spinocerebellar","Corticospinal","Spinothalamic"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column', 'Vibration sense is conducted by the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway, so a tuning fork applied to a bony prominence tests this tract. The spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature, and the remaining options are not sensory pathways for vibration.'),
('97b10eff-e91b-57e1-99cf-60a2f444eb59'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is tested by checking temperature sensation?', '["Vestibulospinal","Dorsal column","Spinothalamic tract","Spinocerebellar","Corticospinal"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract', 'Temperature sensation travels in the lateral spinothalamic tract, whose fibers cross in the anterior white commissure. The dorsal column mediates discriminative touch and vibration, and the vestibulospinal and other descending tracts carry no thermal information.'),
('2af08c54-e859-5434-bb09-cf7de1e783dc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is tested by checking crude touch?', '["Anterior spinothalamic tract","Dorsal column","Corticospinal","Lateral spinothalamic","Rubrospinal"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior spinothalamic tract', 'Crude (light) touch is carried by the anterior spinothalamic tract, while its lateral counterpart transmits pain and temperature. Discriminative touch mediated by the dorsal column remains intact, and the descending motor tracts are not involved.'),
('d8ce91c5-6ac4-56e4-a54a-60ff0f0d11e7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Fibers crossing in the anterior white commissure belong to', '["Rubrospinal tract","Tectospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract","Corticospinal tract","Medial lemniscus"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract', 'Spinothalamic fibers cross in the anterior white commissure near their level of entry before ascending contralaterally. Rubrospinal, tectospinal and corticospinal fibers are descending motor pathways, and the medial lemniscus decussates at the sensory decussation in the medulla.'),
('3100354a-cea6-5770-a539-986f1a8a0d37'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The medial lemniscus is formed by', '["Corticospinal fibers resulting in decreased functional activity","Pontocerebellar fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Internal arcuate fibers from gracile/cuneate nuclei","Spinothalamic fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External arcuate fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal arcuate fibers from gracile/cuneate nuclei', 'The medial lemniscus is formed by internal arcuate fibers arising from the gracile and cuneate nuclei that decussate at the sensory decussation. Spinothalamic, corticospinal, pontocerebellar and external arcuate fibers contribute to other pathways.'),
('4eab6896-f182-55e1-87d9-34ea1ed2853c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'External arcuate fibers connect', '["Olive to cerebellum","Spinal cord to cerebellum","Gracile nucleus to thalamus","Cuneate nucleus to cerebellum","Cuneate nucleus to thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Cuneate nucleus to cerebellum', 'The posterior external arcuate fibers pass from the cuneate nucleus to the cerebellum, joining the inferior cerebellar peduncle. Projections to the thalamus travel in the medial lemniscus, and the olive-to-cerebellum and spinal-cord-to-cerebellum connections have separate names.'),
('04df02d8-535b-5ad4-b5de-a96328d01b52'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The posterior spinocerebellar tract is also known as', '["Monakow''s tract","Forel''s tract","Gowers'' tract","Flechsig''s tract","Lissauer''s tract"]'::jsonb, 'Flechsig''s tract', 'Flechsig''s tract is the posterior (dorsal) spinocerebellar tract. Gowers'' tract is the ventral spinocerebellar tract, Monakow''s is the rubrospinal tract, and Lissauer''s and Forel''s tracts are different named structures.'),
('36e3f0ec-5ef7-5ccd-b17e-ab81a408a9fb'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The ventral spinocerebellar tract is also known as', '["Collier''s tract","Gowers'' tract","Flechsig''s tract","Burdach''s tract","Monakow''s tract"]'::jsonb, 'Gowers'' tract', 'Gowers'' tract is the ventral (anterior) spinocerebellar tract. Flechsig''s tract is the posterior spinocerebellar tract, Burdach''s is the cuneate fasciculus, and Collier''s and Monakow''s eponyms belong to different structures.'),
('e7e9326e-64d9-599c-a442-5124c092e68e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which nucleus is located in the posterior funiculus?', '["Nucleus proprius","Nucleus gracilis","Nucleus dorsalis of Clarke","No nucleus; it is a white matter tract","Substantia gelatinosa"]'::jsonb, 'No nucleus; it is a white matter tract', 'The posterior funiculus is white matter made of the gracile and cuneate fasciculi, so it contains no grey-matter nucleus. The gracile and cuneate nuclei lie in the medulla, while the nucleus proprius, Clarke''s nucleus and substantia gelatinosa occupy spinal grey matter.'),
('4896b723-8c04-58a0-96e4-94fad8b90cd0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The termination of the corticospinal tract is', '["Dorsal root ganglia","Anterior horn cells (alpha motor neurons)","Posterior horn cells","Cranial nerve nuclei","Lateral horn cells"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior horn cells (alpha motor neurons)', 'Corticospinal fibers synapse on anterior horn (alpha motor) neurons, completing the upper-to-lower motor neuron relay. Dorsal root ganglia and posterior horn cells are sensory, lateral horn cells are autonomic, and cranial nerve nuclei receive cortical fibers via other pathways.'),
('e4078dc1-1840-5194-a80f-0bbcdd7a36b3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is a symptom of tabes dorsalis?', '["Spastic paralysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Atrophy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lightning pains, sensory ataxia, Romberg positive","Pain and temperature loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fasciculations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lightning pains, sensory ataxia, Romberg positive', 'Tabes dorsalis, the late neurological form of syphilis, causes degeneration of the dorsal roots and columns, producing lightning pains, sensory ataxia and a positive Romberg sign. Spastic paralysis, atrophy, fasciculations and pain/temperature loss are not its hallmark features.'),
('77e25847-57fb-520e-bde3-22ebdf28cfe9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord levels for knee jerk reflex (patellar)', '["L2-L3","L3-L4","L5-S1","L4-L5","S1-S2"]'::jsonb, 'L3-L4', 'The patellar (knee) reflex is mediated by the L3-L4 segments, acting on the quadriceps via the femoral nerve. The ankle jerk is S1-S2, and the other listed levels do not serve the quadriceps stretch reflex.'),
('a2dbbbc3-794b-5c38-8a81-394c8b2a81f7'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord levels for ankle jerk reflex', '["S1-S2","S2-S4","L3-L4","L4-L5","L5-S1"]'::jsonb, 'S1-S2', 'The ankle (Achilles) reflex is mediated by the S1-S2 segments through the tibial nerve. The knee jerk is L3-L4, and the other listed segmental levels correspond to different reflex arcs.'),
('85ad6d10-6426-5ca2-8c23-a23a19c729ac'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord levels for biceps reflex', '["C5-C6","C6-C7","C3-C4","C8-T1","C7-C8"]'::jsonb, 'C5-C6', 'The biceps reflex is mediated by the C5-C6 segments via the musculocutaneous nerve. The triceps reflex lies at C6-C7, and C3-C4, C7-C8 and C8-T1 are not the segmental levels for the biceps jerk.'),
('961a0f25-0c5b-5bc3-bf40-2678e458bce2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord levels for triceps reflex', '["C3-C4","C5-C6","C8-T1","C7-C8","C6-C7"]'::jsonb, 'C7-C8', 'The triceps reflex is integrated at the C7-C8 spinal segments via the radial nerve, and it tests the integrity of the lower cervical cord. C5-C6 subserves the biceps and brachioradialis reflexes, while C3-C4 and C8-T1 are not the usual reflex centers for triceps.'),
('4a3ee72f-1bb8-5742-a34d-9ccf8d036f9d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord levels for brachioradialis reflex', '["C7-C8","C8-T1","C6-C7","C5-C6","C3-C4"]'::jsonb, 'C5-C6', 'The brachioradialis reflex is mediated through the C5-C6 segments via the radial nerve, making it a useful indicator of upper cervical cord integrity. C7-C8 subserves the triceps reflex and C8-T1 contributes to finger flexor reflexes, not brachioradialis.'),
('aeacc3e2-f5e5-5e03-995c-d2898ee0b39d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord levels for abdominal reflexes', '["T7-T12","L1-L2","T1-T4","S1-S2","T5-T6"]'::jsonb, 'T7-T12', 'The superficial abdominal reflexes are integrated at the T7-T12 thoracic segments, producing contraction of the abdominal wall muscles when the skin is stroked. Lumbar and sacral levels and the upper thoracic segments T1-T4 do not mediate these reflexes.'),
('5349d113-d242-5e6d-8f5e-5ca92d7110ea'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Cremasteric reflex spinal level is', '["S1-S2","L3-L4","S2-S4","T10-T12","L1-L2"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'The cremasteric reflex is mediated by the genitofemoral nerve with its spinal center at L1-L2, causing retraction of the scrotum when the inner thigh is stroked. S2-S4 subserves the bulbocavernosus and anal reflexes, while S1-S2 and L3-L4 relate to other lower-limb reflexes.'),
('2687f31e-dedc-50e9-aa44-f935108ea9f8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Bulbocavernosus reflex involves', '["S2-S4","L3-L4","T10-T12","S1-S2","L1-L2"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4', 'The bulbocavernosus reflex is integrated at the S2-S4 sacral segments through the pudendal nerve, producing contraction of the bulbospongiosus muscle. Lumbar segments mediate the cremasteric and patellar reflexes, and S1-S2 the ankle reflex, not the bulbocavernosus response.'),
('e9deb776-e2df-533a-8b65-69f59dd3c6c0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anal reflex spinal level is', '["L3-L4","L5-S1","S1-S2","S2-S4","T12-L1"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4', 'The anal (anocutaneous) reflex is mediated by the pudendal nerve at the S2-S4 segments, causing external sphincter contraction when the perianal skin is stimulated. Lumbar and S1 segments mediate limb and cremasteric reflexes rather than the anal response.'),
('d0f3ef81-582a-53b1-8ce2-c2be9133b35e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is NOT a component of the spinal cord''s white matter?', '["Anterior funiculus","Lateral funiculus","Gray commissure","Posterior funiculus","Fasciculus proprius"]'::jsonb, 'Gray commissure', 'The spinal cord white matter is organized into the anterior, lateral, and posterior funiculi along with the fasciculus proprius, all composed of myelinated fiber tracts. The gray commissure is part of the central gray matter and therefore is not a white matter component.'),
('8fbc1424-60f3-564d-9ecf-e681df7701cd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The central cord of spinal cord contains', '["CSF","Gray matter (H-shaped)","Blood vessels","White matter","Ependyma"]'::jsonb, 'Gray matter (H-shaped)', 'The central region of the spinal cord is occupied by gray matter arranged as an H-shaped column around the central canal, containing neuronal cell bodies. White matter surrounds it peripherally, and CSF lies within the central canal and subarachnoid space rather than in the cord substance.'),
('bf5cc202-bbcc-58da-bc42-33fe7134768b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The cervical enlargement contains more', '["Ependyma resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gray matter than white relative to thoracic","CSF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","White matter than gray leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gray matter than white relative to thoracic', 'The cervical enlargement (C5-T1) contains a relatively greater proportion of gray matter than white matter compared with the thoracic cord because it houses the cell bodies serving upper limb motor and sensory functions. Thoracic levels carry long ascending and descending tracts, giving them relatively more white matter.'),
('1e0fe8ae-28a8-5390-9f02-9eb5ee87de0f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In thoracic spinal cord, the lateral horn contains', '["Interneurons","Motor neurons","Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies","Parasympathetic preganglionic","Sensory neurons"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies', 'In the thoracic (and upper lumbar, T1-L2) segments, the lateral horn contains the intermediolateral cell column of sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies. Parasympathetic preganglionics lie at S2-S4, while motor and sensory neurons occupy the anterior horn and dorsal root ganglia respectively.'),
('93df4e48-4160-5e38-83c0-a5619336b05a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In sacral spinal cord, parasympathetic preganglionic neurons are in', '["Lamina IX","Lamina I","Lamina II","Lamina VII (S2-S4)","Lamina X"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina VII (S2-S4)', 'Sacral parasympathetic preganglionic cell bodies (S2-S4) lie in the intermediolateral part of lamina VII, forming the sacral outflow to the pelvic viscera. Laminae I and II subserve nociception, lamina IX contains motor neurons, and lamina X surrounds the central canal.'),
('57df0212-ba96-5fa2-bad2-28bc3d0f0a22'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The spinal cord ends at L3 in', '["Elderly","Males","Females","Newborns","Adults"]'::jsonb, 'Newborns', 'In newborns the spinal cord terminates at the L3 vertebral level, whereas in adults it ends at L1-L2 because the vertebral column grows faster than the cord. This is why a lumbar puncture is performed at lower levels (L3-L4 or L4-L5) in older children and adults.'),
('b712cdd6-0488-5ae8-bd43-e9da2f0eb2be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Fibers from fasciculus gracilis synapse in', '["Cerebellum","Nucleus cuneatus","VPL thalamus","Spinal cord","Nucleus gracilis (medulla)"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus gracilis (medulla)', 'Fasciculus gracilis fibers, which carry proprioception and discriminative touch from the lower limb and lower trunk, ascend ipsilaterally and synapse on the nucleus gracilis in the medulla. The VPL thalamus is the third-order relay, and the cerebellum is not part of this dorsal column chain.'),
('22936585-36ce-58bc-ad08-e9085c1447d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Fibers from fasciculus cuneatus synapse in', '["Inferior olive","VPL thalamus","Nucleus gracilis","Nucleus cuneatus (medulla)","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus cuneatus (medulla)', 'Fasciculus cuneatus fibers, carrying upper limb and upper trunk sensation, synapse on the nucleus cuneatus in the medulla before the pathway continues as the medial lemniscus to the VPL thalamus. The nucleus gracilis relays the lower-limb pathway and the inferior olive is a cerebellar relay, not part of this chain.'),
('6f73fb6a-3c7f-58fc-9e88-09d8fdfe5b74'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The second-order neuron of dorsal column pathway gives rise to', '["Pontine fibers","Peduncular fibers","Pyramidal fibers","Internal arcuate fibers","External arcuate fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Internal arcuate fibers', 'Second-order neurons of the dorsal column pathway arise in the gracile and cuneate nuclei and their axons form the internal arcuate fibers, which cross the midline as the sensory decussation to form the medial lemniscus. External arcuate fibers are cerebellar afferents, and pyramidal fibers belong to the motor system.'),
('fe177047-ebeb-5e67-b5ba-089d3c4beb10'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The medial lemniscus lies in the midline of', '["Cerebral cortex","Ventricle","Medulla","Spinal cord","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla', 'After the sensory decussation in the lower medulla, the internal arcuate fibers regroup as the medial lemniscus, which lies in the midline of the medulla. In the pons it flattens transversely and in the midbrain it passes dorsolateral to the red nucleus.'),
('4ee0d313-9ddf-518e-aa45-017bfd40c9a0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In pons, the medial lemniscus lies', '["Ventral resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dorsal mediated by specific receptor activation","Midline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Horizontal (transverse) ventrolateral to fourth ventricle"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal (transverse) ventrolateral to fourth ventricle', 'In the pons the medial lemniscus rotates and lies horizontally (transverse), ventrolateral to the fourth ventricle, as it ascends toward the midbrain. The midline and ventral positions describe the lemniscus at medullary or other levels rather than its pontine orientation.'),
('320df40f-0ca4-587b-83a5-f3f893678715'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In midbrain, the medial lemniscus is located', '["Lateral to cerebral aqueduct","Dorsolateral to red nucleus","Substantia nigra","Ventromedial","In tegmentum near midline"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsolateral to red nucleus', 'In the midbrain the medial lemniscus lies dorsolateral to the red nucleus in the tegmentum before ending in the VPL nucleus of the thalamus. The substantia nigra and cerebral peduncle are more ventral, and the region lateral to the cerebral aqueduct is occupied by other structures.'),
('7208adc4-4039-5aa0-83fe-d270f65c308b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinothalamic tract in spinal cord is located', '["Posterior funiculus","Anterior funiculus medially","Central gray","Dorsal part of lateral funiculus","Anterolateral funiculus"]'::jsonb, 'Anterolateral funiculus', 'The spinothalamic tract ascends in the anterolateral funiculus of the spinal cord, conveying pain, temperature, and crude touch to the brain. The posterior funiculus carries the dorsal column system, and the dorsal lateral funiculus contains corticospinal and spinocerebellar fibers.'),
('458345c1-1d8d-54bf-b8ca-e78f77ed4b6e'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinothalamic tract is located', '["Ventrally resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medially mediated by specific receptor activation","Laterally (merged with medial lemniscus in midbrain)","In cerebral peduncle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laterally (merged with medial lemniscus in midbrain)', 'As the spinothalamic tract ascends through the brainstem it shifts laterally and in the midbrain merges with the medial lemniscus before reaching the VPL nucleus of the thalamus. It does not travel in the cerebral peduncle, which carries descending corticofugal fibers.'),
('9ff4b42d-6c4b-554f-8897-bd6a3f7b3c29'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which of the following decussates in the spinal cord?', '["Medial lemniscus","Spinothalamic tract","Corticospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract","Tectospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract', 'The spinothalamic tract decussates within the spinal cord, crossing in the anterior white commissure within one or two segments of its entry. The medial lemniscus and corticospinal tract cross in the medulla, while the rubrospinal and tectospinal tracts decussate in the brainstem.'),
('a0cadc6c-88ae-58ca-b4bc-fa72dcee2a75'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which of the following decussates in the medulla?', '["Spinothalamic tract","Corticospinal tract (pyramidal)","Rubrospinal tract","Medial lemniscus (internal arcuate fibers)","Tectospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Medial lemniscus (internal arcuate fibers)', 'The medial lemniscus decussates in the medulla as the internal arcuate fibers, forming the sensory decussation that relays dorsal column information to the VPL thalamus. The spinothalamic tract crosses in the spinal cord, and the rubrospinal and tectospinal tracts decussate in the midbrain.'),
('652a4b06-234d-5294-b2b6-21c6f0b5b77d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The internal arcuate fibers belong to which system?', '["Cerebellar system","Pyramidal system","Medial lemniscal system","Autonomic system","Spinothalamic system"]'::jsonb, 'Medial lemniscal system', 'The internal arcuate fibers are the crossing axons of the second-order neurons of the dorsal column pathway, forming the medial lemniscus, so they belong to the medial lemniscal system. The spinothalamic, cerebellar, pyramidal, and autonomic systems use different fiber pathways.'),
('107b17cc-1685-5cc9-848c-8752ba38b460'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The external arcuate fibers belong to which system?', '["Pyramidal system","Extrapyramidal system","Medial lemniscal system","Cerebellar afferent system","Spinothalamic system"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar afferent system', 'The external arcuate fibers arise from the gracile and cuneate nuclei (and some from the spinal cord) and enter the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle, making them part of the cerebellar afferent system. They are not components of the lemniscal, pyramidal, or spinothalamic pathways.'),
('1937902f-d540-5114-b81c-abec0e216ab2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lissauer''s tract (dorsolateral fasciculus) is located', '["At dorsal root entry zone (between dorsal horn and surface)","In anterior funiculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In posterior funiculus mediated by specific receptor activation","In lateral funiculus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Near central canal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At dorsal root entry zone (between dorsal horn and surface)', 'Lissauer''s tract (dorsolateral fasciculus) lies at the dorsal root entry zone, between the apex of the dorsal horn and the cord surface, carrying pain and temperature fibers for one to two segments before they synapse. It is not part of the main funiculi or the central gray.'),
('4c925ef4-5fd5-582b-ae03-60f6c9a4c81c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is concerned with light touch?', '["Corticospinal","Dorsal column","Anterior (ventral) spinothalamic","Lateral spinothalamic","Spinocerebellar"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior (ventral) spinothalamic', 'The anterior (ventral) spinothalamic tract carries light (crude) touch, while the lateral spinothalamic tract conveys pain and temperature. The dorsal column system mediates fine discriminative touch, and corticospinal and spinocerebellar pathways subserve motor and proprioceptive functions.'),
('dcbff6f7-a294-54f4-bb33-6b2dbbb67209'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract carries both sensory and motor fibers?', '["Spinocerebellar resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticospinal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial lemniscus mediated by specific receptor activation","No tract; tracts are either sensory or motor","Spinothalamic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'No tract; tracts are either sensory or motor', 'Named CNS tracts are classified as either purely sensory or purely motor, and no single named tract carries both modalities; sensory information and motor commands travel in separate pathways. The spinocerebellar, medial lemniscus, and spinothalamic tracts are sensory, while the corticospinal tract is motor.'),
('d289106a-2ae9-5aa9-94b9-954b22a2d7be'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The corticospinal tract constitutes what percentage of the pyramidal tract?', '["Most fibers (about 80-90%)","All pyramidal fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","30% mediated by specific receptor activation","10% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","50% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Most fibers (about 80-90%)', 'The corticospinal tract is the principal component of the pyramidal tract, contributing roughly 80-90% of its fibers, while the remainder is the corticobulbar tract. It therefore represents most, but not all, pyramidal fibers.'),
('6211cead-f49a-5eab-bc59-6ce3d3e1d977'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Betz cells (giant pyramidal cells) are found in', '["Layer VI of prefrontal cortex","Layer IV of sensory cortex","Layer II of auditory cortex","Layer III of visual cortex","Layer V of primary motor cortex (area 4)"]'::jsonb, 'Layer V of primary motor cortex (area 4)', 'Betz cells, the giant pyramidal neurons, lie in layer V of the primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4). The other cortical layers and regions listed either lack Betz cells or belong to different functional areas.'),
('3a63b1d4-bdac-5ab5-a88d-8969858f0af4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is true about Betz cells?', '["They are lower motor neurons","They are upper motor neurons","They are in spinal cord","They are in cerebellum","They are sensory neurons"]'::jsonb, 'They are upper motor neurons', 'Betz cells are upper motor neurons whose axons contribute to the corticospinal tract. They are neither lower motor neurons nor sensory neurons, and their cell bodies lie in the cerebral cortex rather than the spinal cord or cerebellum.'),
('12104c59-8ab5-5462-8837-d1aea37d7e4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'How many axons do Betz cells contribute to corticospinal tract?', '["About 3-5% of corticospinal fibers","None","All of corticospinal fibers","Most of corticospinal fibers","10%"]'::jsonb, 'About 3-5% of corticospinal fibers', 'Only about 3-5% of corticospinal fibers arise from Betz cells, while the great majority come from smaller pyramidal cells. Therefore Betz cells contribute only a small fraction of the tract''s fibers.'),
('9b3e775e-b814-5d5d-9f5c-b1f17e2e97e9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The remaining 95-97% of corticospinal fibers originate from', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Small pyramidal cells in areas 4, 6, 3, 1, 2","Only area 6 mediated by specific receptor activation","Only area 4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory cortex only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small pyramidal cells in areas 4, 6, 3, 1, 2', 'Approximately 95-97% of corticospinal fibers originate from small pyramidal cells located in the motor areas (4 and 6) and sensory areas (3, 1, and 2), rather than from Betz cells. The cerebellum is not a source of corticospinal fibers.'),
('4d11c202-e8d6-5308-a1a1-a4d267f5a450'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which blood vessel supplies the precentral gyrus (motor cortex)?', '["Middle cerebral artery (MCA)","Basilar artery","Posterior cerebral artery","Vertebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Middle cerebral artery (MCA)', 'The precentral gyrus receives its blood supply chiefly from the middle cerebral artery (MCA), particularly its lateral arm-and-face area. The anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial motor cortex (leg area), and the basilar and vertebral arteries supply the brainstem and cerebellum.'),
('4ba70594-5ca1-5554-b143-6a9ce753eebd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The portion of corticospinal tract supplying lower limb originates from', '["Temporal pole resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial part of motor cortex (ACA territory)","Occipital pole mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal pole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral part (MCA territory) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medial part of motor cortex (ACA territory)', 'Corticospinal fibers to the lower limb arise from the medial part of the motor cortex on the interhemispheric surface, which is supplied by the anterior cerebral artery (ACA). The lateral motor cortex, in the MCA territory, projects to the face and arm.'),
('aa581fa4-f149-5299-8cad-ee6dc1340518'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The portion of corticospinal tract supplying face and arm originates from', '["Frontal pole resulting in decreased functional activity","Insula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial part (ACA territory) mediated by specific receptor activation","Occipital pole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lateral part of motor cortex (MCA territory)"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral part of motor cortex (MCA territory)', 'Corticospinal fibers to the face and arm originate from the lateral part of the motor cortex, which lies in the middle cerebral artery (MCA) territory. The medial (parasagittal) cortex, supplied by the ACA, projects to the lower limb, and the poles and insula are not motor regions.'),
('ad2e1745-5247-506e-bb3b-26e61bfe7d31'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Corticospinal fibers for lower limb are located in which part of internal capsule?', '["Genu resulting in decreased functional activity","Sublenticular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anterior limb mediated by specific receptor activation","Retrolenticular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior part of posterior limb"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior part of posterior limb', 'In the internal capsule, corticospinal fibers to the lower limb pass through the anterior part of the posterior limb, just behind the genu. The genu carries corticobulbar fibers to the head and neck, and the anterior limb and retrolenticular parts contain other projection fibers.'),
('99ead74f-dfea-5929-897f-840a559612ff'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In the cerebral peduncle (crus cerebri), corticospinal fibers are located', '["Medial 1/5","Lateral 3/5","Lateral 1/5","Middle 3/5","Medial 3/5"]'::jsonb, 'Middle 3/5', 'In the crus cerebri of the cerebral peduncle, corticospinal (and corticobulbar) fibers occupy the middle 3/5. The medial 1/5 carries frontopontine fibers and the lateral 1/5 carries parieto-occipito-temporopontine fibers.'),
('7667ffaa-3f54-5260-bf91-1d49a02af271'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In the pons, corticospinal fibers', '["Synapse in pontine nuclei","Join medial lemniscus","Break into multiple bundles among pontine nuclei","Decussate","Stay as a single compact bundle"]'::jsonb, 'Break into multiple bundles among pontine nuclei', 'In the pons the corticospinal fibers fragment into multiple small bundles that run between the pontine nuclei, and they converge again in the medulla to form the pyramids. They do not synapse in the pons and do not decussate at this level.'),
('941d42b1-252a-55d9-9f32-2dd25f878caf'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In the medulla, corticospinal fibers form', '["Restiform body","Fourth ventricle floor","Pyramids (ventral surface)","Medial lemniscus","Olivary nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Pyramids (ventral surface)', 'In the medulla the corticospinal fibers collect on the ventral surface to form the pyramids, the landmark feature of the medulla. The restiform body, medial lemniscus, and olivary nucleus are separate medullary structures with other functions.'),
('0c42b62b-e7ec-5206-8850-e573c1efa960'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which descending tract does NOT originate from the brainstem?', '["Corticospinal tract (from cortex)","Rubrospinal tract","Reticulospinal tract","Vestibulospinal tract","Tectospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract (from cortex)', 'The corticospinal tract originates in the cerebral cortex, unlike the rubrospinal, reticulospinal, vestibulospinal, and tectospinal tracts, which arise from brainstem nuclei. Hence it is the tract that does not originate from the brainstem.'),
('519bbd0b-2be3-5bc9-b7fc-173d17e6bc5a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The pyramid of medulla contains', '["Olivocerebellar fibers","Medial lemniscus","Corticospinal fibers","Spinothalamic tract","Corticobulbar fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal fibers', 'The medullary pyramid contains corticospinal fibers descending from the motor and sensory cortex, which decussate in the lower medulla. The medial lemniscus lies dorsal to the pyramids, and olivocerebellar, spinothalamic, and corticobulbar fibers are not components of the pyramid.'),
('00d79bba-f821-53a4-adb5-4f2350db6cd3'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The olive (inferior olivary nucleus) contains', '["Corticospinal fibers","Medial lemniscus","Olivocerebellar fibers (to cerebellum)","Spinothalamic fibers","Pyramidal fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Olivocerebellar fibers (to cerebellum)', 'The inferior olivary nucleus gives rise to the olivocerebellar tract, which crosses the midline and enters the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle. The other listed fibers pass through or near the medulla but are not contained within the olive.'),
('92d759e0-6388-5ecf-b106-819a2c047e21'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The spinal lemniscus is formed by', '["Medial lemniscus","All sensory tracts","Lateral lemniscus","Spinothalamic and spinotectal tracts","Trigeminal lemniscus"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic and spinotectal tracts', 'The spinal lemniscus is formed by the spinothalamic and spinotectal tracts as they ascend together through the brainstem to reach the thalamus and superior colliculus. The medial, lateral, and trigeminal lemnisci carry different sensory modalities.'),
('48ed10a0-c09b-5272-b5d7-9e0104e5cf64'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The lateral lemniscus carries', '["Visual information","Pain information","Touch information","Auditory information","Motor information"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory information', 'The lateral lemniscus is the auditory pathway in the brainstem, carrying fibers from the cochlear nuclei to the inferior colliculus. Visual, pain, touch, and motor information travel in different tracts and lemnisci.'),
('e6142dea-b8ec-5e4b-ba27-d55b96598442'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The trigeminal lemniscus carries', '["Auditory","Visual","Facial touch and pain","Motor","Gustatory"]'::jsonb, 'Facial touch and pain', 'The trigeminal (quintothalamic) lemniscus carries touch and pain from the face, ascending from the trigeminal nuclei to the VPM nucleus of the thalamus. Auditory, visual, motor, and gustatory modalities use other pathways.'),
('6121f2d8-eaa8-57ae-bdfb-999ea5e69dc2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lesion of MLF causes', '["Gaze palsy resulting in decreased functional activity","Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO)","Nystagmus mediated by specific receptor activation","Diplopia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ptosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internuclear ophthalmoplegia (INO)', 'A medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) lesion interrupts the connection between the abducens nucleus and the contralateral oculomotor nucleus, producing internuclear ophthalmoplegia with failed adduction on the affected side and nystagmus in the abducting eye. Gaze palsy, diplopia, and ptosis arise from other lesions.'),
('754fadb2-1a56-5cf4-a267-0044f1b5e702'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which descending tract controls fine, skilled voluntary movements?', '["Reticulospinal tract","Vestibulospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract","Tectospinal tract","Corticospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract', 'The corticospinal tract mediates fine, skilled voluntary movement of the distal limbs, particularly the hand, via direct cortical control over anterior horn cells. Rubrospinal, vestibulospinal, reticulospinal, and tectospinal tracts subserve posture, tone, and reflex coordination rather than skilled movement.'),
('6be9ee77-d12e-57d4-80b2-ae2b82348ab1'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Rubrospinal tract facilitates', '["Auditory reflexes","Visual reflexes","Flexor muscle tone","Sphincter control","Extensor muscle tone"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor muscle tone', 'The rubrospinal tract facilitates flexor muscle tone in the upper limb, opposing the extensor-facilitating vestibulospinal tract. Auditory and visual reflexes are mediated by the tectospinal tract, and sphincter control depends on other autonomic and somatic pathways.'),
('51462476-ff9f-5772-ae2c-2cc644fa9f87'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In animals, rubrospinal tract is important for', '["Trunk posture resulting in decreased functional activity","Head turning accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Balance mediated by specific receptor activation","Limb movement (especially upper limb)","Eye movements leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Limb movement (especially upper limb)', 'In animals the rubrospinal tract is important for limb movement, especially of the upper limb, working alongside the corticospinal tract in humans. Trunk posture, head turning, balance, and eye movements are subserved by other descending tracts such as the reticulospinal, tectospinal, and vestibulospinal systems.'),
('c4242327-f1b1-5ff1-90f7-5e519b012e40'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Vestibulospinal tract is important for', '["Eye movements","Posture and balance","Fine finger movements","Facial expression","Chewing"]'::jsonb, 'Posture and balance', 'The vestibulospinal tract arises from the vestibular nuclei and facilitates extensor tone to maintain posture and balance. Fine finger movements depend on the corticospinal tract, and eye movements, facial expression, and chewing use other cranial pathways.'),
('86855a48-7169-539c-967f-973383a6c021'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract is involved in adjustment of posture during head movement?', '["Rubrospinal","Corticospinal","Tectospinal","Lateral vestibulospinal tract","Medial vestibulospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Medial vestibulospinal tract', 'The medial vestibulospinal tract descends bilaterally within the medial longitudinal fasciculus to innervate cervical motor neurons, stabilizing the head and neck during head movement. The lateral vestibulospinal tract instead promotes extensor tone of the limbs for upright posture, while the rubro-, cortico-, and tectospinal tracts serve other motor functions.'),
('d7682432-3456-5bb8-997d-8d316f2a2d44'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which tract mediates the flexor (withdrawal) reflex?', '["Corticospinal","Rubrospinal","Tectospinal","Vestibulospinal","Spinoreticular and spinothalamic"]'::jsonb, 'Spinoreticular and spinothalamic', 'The flexor (withdrawal) reflex is a polysynaptic spinal reflex whose ascending collaterals travel in the spinoreticular and spinothalamic tracts, alerting higher centers. The other choices are descending motor pathways rather than the tracts carrying the reflex afferent signal.'),
('7bd4f9f2-b103-55e1-a2f6-ebae1704a8ba'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The crossed extensor reflex is a:', '["Vestibular reflex resulting in decreased functional activity","Cortical reflex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinal reflex involving contralateral limb","Brainstem reflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellar reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal reflex involving contralateral limb', 'The crossed extensor reflex is a spinal reflex that extends the contralateral limb to support weight while the stimulated limb withdraws. It is integrated by spinal cord circuits and does not require vestibular, cortical, cerebellar, or brainstem reflex activity.'),
('7a4fbc99-42b6-550a-a2ae-7967a5617e4f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Gamma motor neurons are regulated by:', '["Descending tracts (corticospinal, reticulospinal, vestibulospinal)","regulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Local interneurons only mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha motor neurons only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensory input only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Descending tracts (corticospinal, reticulospinal, vestibulospinal)', 'Gamma motor neurons receive supraspinal control from the corticospinal, reticulospinal, and vestibulospinal tracts, which set spindle sensitivity. Local interneurons, alpha motor neurons, and sensory input alone do not provide that descending regulatory drive.'),
('4c2fa6ed-e2cf-5671-adc6-ad3d1ff6c654'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Alpha-gamma coactivation ensures:', '["Muscle spindle maintains sensitivity during muscle contraction","Reflex inhibition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Joint stabilization mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle relaxation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tendon protection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle spindle maintains sensitivity during muscle contraction', 'Alpha-gamma coactivation makes intrafusal and extrafusal fibers contract together, so the spindle continues firing and stays sensitive while the muscle shortens. Without it the spindle would slacken and lose stretch detection during contraction.'),
('85f7d74d-9e3a-599c-98a2-3fd9b8ac3997'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Central pattern generators for locomotion are located in:', '["Basal ganglia","Cerebral cortex","Thalamus","Spinal cord interneurons","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord interneurons', 'Locomotor central pattern generators reside among spinal cord interneurons and generate rhythmic alternating output independent of descending and afferent input. The basal ganglia, cortex, thalamus, and cerebellum act supraspinally and are not the rhythm-generating site.'),
('62c0d51c-3d07-56e8-8e56-8312c714f90d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord injury at C3-C5 causes:', '["Paraplegia only resulting in decreased functional activity","Ataxia only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Brown-Sequard mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemiplegia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory paralysis (phrenic nerve)"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory paralysis (phrenic nerve)', 'The phrenic nerve arises from C3-C5, so injury at this level paralyses the diaphragm and threatens respiration. The other options describe deficits other than the respiratory failure characteristic of upper cervical injury.'),
('1c7aeb90-011a-5fb4-bd31-fc6eb226db04'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal shock duration correlates with:', '["Phylogenetic level (longer in humans than frogs)","Age accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Severity only mediated by specific receptor activation","Cause of injury without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gender leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phylogenetic level (longer in humans than frogs)', 'Spinal shock lasts longer in animals higher on the phylogenetic scale, being prolonged in humans but brief in frogs and other lower vertebrates. Age, severity, cause, and gender do not reflect that phylogenetic relationship.'),
('4a26faa9-778a-5e2d-80ac-81cbf92da623'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which reflex returns first after spinal shock?', '["Cremasteric","Patellar reflex","Bulbocavernosus and Babinski","Ankle jerk","Abdominal reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Bulbocavernosus and Babinski', 'The bulbocavernosus reflex and the Babinski sign reappear first as spinal shock resolves because simpler segmental circuits recover earliest. The patellar, ankle, cremasteric, and abdominal reflexes return afterward.'),
('6d0fa1c7-097a-550c-bfa9-e2fc8282a43a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Mass reflex in chronic spinal cord transection includes:', '["Only autonomic activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sweating, piloerection, bladder/bowel evacuation, flexor spasms","reflex activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sensory activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sweating, piloerection, bladder/bowel evacuation, flexor spasms', 'The mass reflex is an exaggerated multisegmental discharge after recovery from chronic spinal transection, coupling autonomic activity (sweating, piloerection, bladder and bowel evacuation) with flexor spasms. It is neither purely autonomic, motor, sensory, nor a single simple reflex.'),
('6e18a47a-7e27-51aa-bd25-37ff468aca0a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is false about spinal shock?', '["Loss of sensation","Deep tendon reflexes are hyperactive","Loss of reflexes","Flaccid paralysis","Bladder paralysis"]'::jsonb, 'Deep tendon reflexes are hyperactive', 'During spinal shock, reflexes are depressed: deep tendon reflexes become hypoactive or absent with flaccid paralysis and bladder and sensory loss. Hyperactive deep tendon reflexes reflect the chronic spastic phase after shock resolves, not spinal shock itself.'),
('917e8a35-2340-5c02-9623-317bfc3e2ffe'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Autonomic dysreflexia occurs in spinal cord lesions:', '["Cauda equina","Above T6","Only cervical","Below T6","Conus medullaris"]'::jsonb, 'Above T6', 'Autonomic dysreflexia develops when the lesion lies above the T6 sympathetic outflow, so visceral stimulation below the lesion triggers massive sympathetic discharge and hypertensive crisis. Cauda equina and below-T6 lesions interrupt the reflex arc, and the trigger site is above the splanchnic outflow rather than a cervical or conus location alone.'),
('f48a0d33-a200-5764-bc94-7c55be179eb6'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Tabetic (neurogenic) bladder is due to:', '["Spinal shock resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of sensory input from bladder (dorsal column)","Loss of autonomic regulation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortical lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of motor outflow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of sensory input from bladder (dorsal column)', 'In tabes dorsalis, dorsal column degeneration removes sensory feedback from the bladder, so the patient cannot perceive fullness and the bladder becomes atomic and overdistended. The defect is sensory rather than spinal shock, autonomic loss, cortical damage, or motor outflow failure.'),
('9d3f7bbc-b1ae-50d2-ba49-73584c6255c4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Upper motor neuron bladder (spastic/reflex) occurs with:', '["Cauda equina lesion","Peripheral nerve","Conus medullaris lesion","Spinal cord transection above S2","Dorsal column lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord transection above S2', 'Transection above S2 interrupts descending inhibition from the pontine micturition center, leaving the sacral circuitry to empty the bladder reflexly and spastically. Cauda equina, conus, and peripheral nerve lesions instead produce a flaccid lower motor neuron bladder.'),
('6eba8c40-c0fb-56ca-a0eb-9b5c0e5a973b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Lower motor neuron bladder (flaccid/autonomous) occurs with:', '["Cauda equina or conus medullaris lesion","Spinal cord above S2","Cortical lesion","Cerebellar lesion","Brainstem lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Cauda equina or conus medullaris lesion', 'Damage to the cauda equina or conus medullaris destroys the sacral parasympathetic outflow and yields a flaccid, autonomous bladder with weak contraction. Lesions above S2, cortex, cerebellum, or brainstem spare the sacral reflex arc and give a spastic bladder instead.'),
('b1bad5d1-fd42-51c1-8764-6996d0baf817'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal levels for micturition center:', '["T1-T4 resulting in decreased functional activity","C5-C7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S1 only mediated by specific receptor activation","L1-L2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","S2-S4 (parasympathetic) and T10-L2 (sympathetic)"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4 (parasympathetic) and T10-L2 (sympathetic)', 'The spinal micturition centers are the sacral parasympathetic segments S2-S4, which drive detrusor contraction and reflex voiding, and the thoracolumbar sympathetic outflow T10-L2. The other listed levels do not house the spinal micturition centers.'),
('b1b3794b-0f3c-57e3-b0f7-6472e39a149c'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The pontine micturition center is located in:', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pontine tegmentum (Barrington nucleus)","Thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine tegmentum (Barrington nucleus)', 'Barrington''s nucleus lies in the pontine tegmentum and coordinates sphincter relaxation with detrusor contraction during voiding. The cerebellum, midbrain, thalamus, and medulla are not the site of the pontine micturition center.'),
('3d5fa5e4-9c3b-5a56-a9a6-7579cd809123'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which receptors detect muscle stretch in muscle spindles?', '["Ia and II afferents","IV afferents","Ib afferents","Alpha motor axons","III afferents"]'::jsonb, 'Ia and II afferents', 'Muscle spindles are innervated by group Ia (primary, annulospiral) and group II (secondary, flower-spray) afferents that signal stretch and its rate. Ib afferents serve Golgi tendon organs, III and IV afferents are nociceptors, and alpha motor axons do not carry spindle sensation.'),
('ab5f3d40-760a-5140-8ab8-dd257ede9487'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Golgi tendon organs detect:', '["Muscle tension (force)","Skin touch","Joint position","Pain","Muscle length"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle tension (force)', 'Golgi tendon organs lie at the musculotendinous junction and signal active contraction tension via Ib afferents. Muscle length is the domain of spindles, while touch, joint position, and pain involve other receptor systems.'),
('0b451904-0ba0-57ec-80cb-34fee927146d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Joint receptors (Pacinian, Ruffini) detect:', '["Touch resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Joint position and movement","Temperature without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Joint position and movement', 'Pacinian and Ruffini receptors in joints signal joint position and movement, contributing to kinesthesia. Touch, pain, temperature, and pressure are mediated by cutaneous receptors rather than joint mechanoreceptors.'),
('b9b15500-9f27-5bc2-a12e-0fa182e03471'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which of the following is an upper motor neuron?', '["Anterior horn cell resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticospinal neuron from motor cortex to spinal cord","Alpha motor neuron mediated by specific receptor activation","Gamma motor neuron without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal root ganglion cell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal neuron from motor cortex to spinal cord', 'The corticospinal neuron arising in the motor cortex is an upper motor neuron because its axon synapses on spinal circuitry rather than directly on muscle. Anterior horn, alpha, and gamma motor neurons are lower motor neurons, and dorsal root ganglion cells are sensory.'),
('75092cf9-be79-5f4d-8f95-669bf9bae94a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which of the following is a lower motor neuron?', '["Alpha motor neuron in anterior horn","Sensory neuron","Interneuron in spinal cord","Corticobulbar neuron","Corticospinal neuron"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha motor neuron in anterior horn', 'An alpha motor neuron in the anterior horn is the classic lower motor neuron whose axon innervates skeletal muscle directly. Corticospinal and corticobulbar neurons are upper motor neurons, while interneurons and sensory neurons are not motor neurons at all.'),
('d1b4b236-1a80-565e-8729-c5cb7de54bc5'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Anterior horn cells are organized somatotopically:', '["Central = axial; peripheral = limb","somatotopy","Medial = axial; lateral = limb","Random organization","Medial = limb; lateral = axial"]'::jsonb, 'Medial = axial; lateral = limb', 'In the anterior horn, medial cell columns supply axial musculature while lateral columns supply the limbs, an organization established during development. The other choices either invert the medial-lateral arrangement or abandon the ordered columnar pattern.'),
('a5a74aec-1f51-5a4b-964c-9b857920b8d0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which part of the spinal cord contains the lowest concentration of white matter?', '["Thoracic region","Sacral region","Cervical enlargement","Cervical region","Lumbar region"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral region', 'The sacral cord has the least white matter because only the few ascending and descending fibers that still remain travel through it. The cervical and lumbar regions carry many more tracts and therefore contain more white matter.'),
('41ed9603-747c-51cb-b0a4-a76c2534c3da'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which part of the spinal cord contains the most white matter?', '["Coccygeal region","Thoracic region","Sacral region","Cervical region","Lumbar region"]'::jsonb, 'Cervical region', 'The cervical spinal cord contains the greatest amount of white matter because every ascending and descending pathway passes through it. More caudal regions progressively lose fibers and so contain proportionately less white matter.'),
('fa01c453-d5aa-5f4f-b3ef-2ed5f83d1f9a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The ratio of gray to white matter is highest in:', '["Cervical spinal cord","Sacral spinal cord","Lumbar spinal cord","Thoracic spinal cord","Cervical enlargement"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral spinal cord', 'The gray-to-white ratio is highest in the sacral cord because gray matter dominates while white matter is scant at that level. The thoracic cord has a thin gray column with abundant white matter, so its ratio is lowest.'),
('c2f3af04-54c6-5f0f-b36e-470d31eb2186'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord segment L5 corresponds to which vertebral body?', '["L3","L5","L1","L2","L4"]'::jsonb, 'L1', 'Because the cord ends at L1-L2 while the vertebral column is longer, the L5 segment comes to overlie the L1 vertebral body in the adult. Lumbar and sacral segments sit several levels above their vertebral namesakes.'),
('07c1ca6c-f0b9-54e7-ad7b-557870d3350d'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord segment T10 corresponds to which vertebral body?', '["L1","T8","T10","T7","T12"]'::jsonb, 'T8', 'The T10 spinal segment lies opposite the T8 vertebral body because the cord ends higher than its vertebral origins, displacing segments about two levels rostrally in the thoracic region. The T10 and T12 vertebral bodies lie below that segment.'),
('5d4f2054-adae-5061-a95e-2eed8be990f0'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord segment C6 corresponds to which vertebral body?', '["T1","C7","C4","C6","C5"]'::jsonb, 'C5', 'In the cervical region each spinal segment lies one level rostral to its corresponding vertebra, so the C6 segment sits beside the C5 vertebral body. The C6, C7, and other cervical vertebral levels therefore do not host the C6 segment.'),
('bcabf419-24bd-5455-98c1-167660a1c9e4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Spinal cord segment S1 corresponds to which vertebral body?', '["L4","S1","L2","L3","L1"]'::jsonb, 'L1', 'Sacral segments lie far above their vertebral namesakes because the conus medullaris ends at L1-L2; the S1 segment therefore overlies the L1 vertebral body. The L2-L4 vertebral levels lie below the termination of the cord.'),
('6c747008-f8d7-5a89-bba8-43050f6c919b'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The conus medullaris contains which spinal segments?', '["T10-L1","L1-L2 only","S2-S5 and coccygeal","L3-S1","C8-T1"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S5 and coccygeal', 'The conus medullaris tapers at L1-L2 and contains the S2-S5 and coccygeal segments, which give rise to the cauda equina. The thoracic and upper lumbar segments lie well above the conus and are not inside it.'),
('3f76631c-1cac-504e-8a0b-c18eed2b9e4a'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Ventral roots of spinal nerves carry:', '["Sensory (afferent) fibers","Both","Motor (efferent) fibers","Autonomic only","Only pain fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Motor (efferent) fibers', 'Ventral roots carry motor (efferent) fibers arising from anterior horn and intermediolateral column cells. Sensory afferent traffic enters through the dorsal roots, so ventral roots convey no afferent input.'),
('1b0c291b-5569-593d-bc86-c18545af3340'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Dorsal roots of spinal nerves carry:', '["Only touch fibers","Sensory (afferent) fibers","Autonomic only","Motor efferents","Both"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory (afferent) fibers', 'Dorsal roots convey sensory (afferent) fibers whose cell bodies lie in the dorsal root ganglia. Motor and autonomic outflow leaves through the ventral roots, and touch is only one of the sensory modalities carried.'),
('07df26b4-9ea3-5d87-8412-952a6dd5b9bd'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In spinal muscular atrophy (SMA), which is affected?', '["Neuromuscular junction","Corticospinal tract","Sensory neurons","Anterior horn cells (alpha motor neurons)","Muscle fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior horn cells (alpha motor neurons)', 'Spinal muscular atrophy results from loss of anterior horn cells (alpha motor neurons), producing progressive flaccid weakness and muscle atrophy. The corticospinal tract, sensory neurons, neuromuscular junction, and muscle fibers are not the primary site of destruction.'),
('49c01e4f-888a-5ec8-a787-e82a88222789'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is not a feature of anterior horn cell lesion?', '["Areflexia","Flaccid paralysis","Sensory loss","Fasciculations","Atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory loss', 'Anterior horn cell lesions cause flaccid paralysis, areflexia, fasciculations, and atrophy while sparing sensation, because the damage lies in the motor outflow. Sensory loss indicates dorsal horn or tract involvement rather than anterior horn damage.'),
('e8a0eb2f-b2db-5477-87b2-31977f3ab343'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Hemisection of the spinal cord on the left side at T10 causes:', '["Left UMN paralysis below T10, left loss of vibration/proprioception, right loss of pain/temperature below T11","Bilateral pain loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Right UMN paralysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left UMN paralysis below T10, left loss of vibration/proprioception, right loss of pain/temperature below T11', 'Left hemisection at T10 (Brown-Sequard syndrome) gives ipsilateral spastic paralysis below T10, ipsilateral loss of fine touch, vibration, and proprioception, and contralateral loss of pain and temperature beginning about a level lower where those fibers cross. Both motor and sensory systems are therefore affected on opposite sides.'),
('6fb39c87-92a7-5491-9f3f-817c503ff318'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The dorsal columns mediate:', '["Motor commands resulting in decreased functional activity","Autonomic functions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain, temperature mediated by specific receptor activation","Balance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fine touch, vibration, two-point discrimination, stereognosis"]'::jsonb, 'Fine touch, vibration, two-point discrimination, stereognosis', 'The dorsal columns carry fine touch, vibration, two-point discrimination, and stereognosis to the medial lemniscus and cortex. Pain and temperature travel in the spinothalamic tract, and motor or balance functions are handled by other pathways.'),
('caa0b3f2-6f46-56f2-9528-3b049e193735'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which cranial nerve nuclei are located in the spinal cord?', '["CN III, IV, VI resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal accessory (CN XI) and spinal trigeminal","CN XII mediated by specific receptor activation","CN VII, VIII without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN IX, X leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal accessory (CN XI) and spinal trigeminal', 'The spinal accessory nucleus (CN XI) lies in the cervical anterior horn, and the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve extends into the upper cervical cord, making these the cranial nerve nuclei found in the spinal cord. All other cranial nerve nuclei reside within the brainstem.'),
('7453dd57-64fe-55a8-855e-414acc8a4572'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve receives input for:', '["Touch and vibration from face","Pain and temperature from face","Taste","Motor innervation of jaw","Auditory"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature from face', 'The spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve receives pain and temperature afferents from the face. Fine touch and vibration from the face are processed by the principal sensory nucleus, whereas taste, jaw motor, and auditory inputs are unrelated.'),
('88242472-e6b1-5de0-aee4-42392f46eab4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The nucleus of the spinal tract of the trigeminal nerve extends from:', '["Midbrain to pons","Medulla only","C1 to C8","Pons to C4 spinal level","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Pons to C4 spinal level', 'The spinal tract and nucleus of the trigeminal nerve run from the caudal pons down to about the C4 spinal level, carrying facial pain and temperature. The nucleus is not found in the midbrain and does not continue through the lower cervical cord.'),
('aa3b75ed-ed49-5d67-ad72-8a38bfb80edc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which is NOT true about the filum terminale?', '["It is a continuation of pia mater","It is a pial extension","It contains nerve fibers","It is surrounded by cauda equina","It anchors conus medullaris to coccyx"]'::jsonb, 'It contains nerve fibers', 'The filum terminale is a pial extension that anchors the conus medullaris to the coccyx and contains no nerve fibers; the cauda equina is the bundle of roots surrounding it. A structure containing nerve fibers therefore contradicts the nature of the filum.'),
('d6857293-1bfb-55aa-8aef-d3bb3692c6c8'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The spinal arachnoid mater is separated from pia by:', '["Subdural space","Denticulate ligaments","Dural space","Subarachnoid space containing CSF","Epidural space"]'::jsonb, 'Subarachnoid space containing CSF', 'The subarachnoid space separates the arachnoid from the pia and is filled with cerebrospinal fluid. The subdural space, epidural space, and denticulate ligaments are not the CSF-filled interval between the two membranes.'),
('cc08de6e-cea3-5874-9c83-27532891b1a4'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'Which statement about spinal meninges is false?', '["Arachnoid is separated from dura by subdural space","Pia gives rise to denticulate ligaments","Dura mater is attached to vertebral periosteum","Epidural space contains fat and veins","Filum terminale is pial"]'::jsonb, 'Dura mater is attached to vertebral periosteum', 'Spinal dura mater is separated from the vertebral periosteum by the epidural space, unlike the cranium, so the claim that it is attached to the periosteum is false. The other statements correctly describe the arachnoid, the pial denticulate ligaments, the epidural contents, and the filum terminale.'),
('38fa7e9b-5e1a-5584-b9d6-28f10982aa5f'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'During spinal cord development, the alar lamina becomes:', '["White matter","Central canal","Sensory (posterior horn)","Motor (anterior horn)","Autonomic (lateral horn)"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory (posterior horn)', 'The alar lamina develops into the sensory (dorsal) horns, while the basal lamina gives rise to the motor (ventral) and autonomic (lateral) horns. White matter and the central canal arise from other structures, namely the marginal layer and the closed neural tube lumen.'),
('1d34aa84-7ccd-5407-8343-39406bb6dfbc'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The lateral corticospinal tract decussates at which level?', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Lower medulla (decussation of pyramids)","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord at each segment without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Internal capsule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower medulla (decussation of pyramids)', 'The lateral corticospinal tract crosses the midline in the lower medulla at the pyramidal decussation. It does not decussate in the midbrain, pons, internal capsule, or at each spinal segment.'),
('3d3a9f9a-952d-51d8-9e71-167d93df5d56'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The anterior corticospinal tract (uncrossed) terminates at which spinal level?', '["Sacral only","Upper thoracic (T6-T7)","Throughout spinal cord","Lumbar only","Cervical only"]'::jsonb, 'Upper thoracic (T6-T7)', 'The anterior corticospinal tract descends uncrossed only to the upper thoracic region (about T6-T7), where its fibers cross before synapsing. More caudal motor innervation is supplied by fibers that crossed earlier in the pyramids.'),
('7a462986-5f98-56fc-b755-588eef3a8ffa'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The rubrospinal tract originates from which nucleus and crosses where?', '["Vestibular nucleus resulting in decreased functional activity","Red nucleus — does not decussate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Red nucleus (midbrain) — decussates in ventral tegmental decussation","Reticular formation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Superior colliculus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Red nucleus (midbrain) — decussates in ventral tegmental decussation', 'The rubrospinal tract arises from the red nucleus of the midbrain and decussates in the ventral tegmental decussation before descending in the lateral funiculus. The vestibular nucleus, reticular formation, and superior colliculus give rise to other descending tracts, and the red nucleus does cross rather than remaining uncrossed.'),
('4ff261fc-8dab-5923-860c-3a08c7b86108'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The spinothalamic tract (lateral) carries which sensory modalities?', '["Fine touch and vibration","Crude touch and pressure","Pain and temperature","Discriminative touch","Proprioception and vibration"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature', 'The lateral spinothalamic tract conveys pain and temperature. Crude touch and pressure travel in the anterior spinothalamic tract, while fine touch, vibration, discriminative touch, and proprioception are carried by the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway.'),
('9dd5293e-c019-556a-a8d5-bafe48941ce2'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'The dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway decussates at which level?', '["Medulla (internal arcuate fibers at sensory decussation)","Spinal cord at each segment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Pons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (internal arcuate fibers at sensory decussation)', 'The dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway crosses as internal arcuate fibers at the sensory decussation in the medulla. The spinothalamic tract crosses in the spinal cord, and this pathway does not decussate in the pons, midbrain, or thalamus.'),
('3a07a353-c086-50bb-8dde-b7354da72ea9'::uuid, 'Anatomy Histology Embryology (Paper 2)', 'Neuroanatomy - Spinal Cord Tracts', 'In adults, the spinal cord terminates at which level?', '["At L1-L2","At T12","At L4","At S2","At C7"]'::jsonb, 'At L1-L2', 'The adult spinal cord ends at approximately the L1-L2 level, where the conus medullaris tapers. Levels below this contain only the cauda equina, and the higher options place the termination too far cranially.'),
('f54945d9-d83c-53f4-b8a5-8f08b7d1e736'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The resting membrane potential of a typical nerve cell is approximately:', '["0 mV","-70 mV","+70 mV","+90 mV","-90 mV"]'::jsonb, '-70 mV', 'The resting membrane potential of a typical neuron is about -70 mV, set largely by the K+ equilibrium potential. The intracellular side is negative relative to the extracellular side due to the unequal distribution of charged ions across the membrane.'),
('de9a8a77-4b29-5a7c-88c9-c8b2741ec7c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which ion is primarily responsible for the depolarization phase of the action potential?', '["Ca2+","Cl-","K+","Mg2+","Na+"]'::jsonb, 'Na+', 'Depolarization of the action potential is driven by rapid influx of Na+ through voltage-gated Na+ channels. This inward Na+ current drives the membrane potential toward the Na+ equilibrium potential.'),
('785cdee4-e3d5-56bd-b846-2783a0d46fa2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The absolute refractory period corresponds to which phase of the action potential?', '["Depolarization only","Hyperpolarization","Repolarization only","Resting phase","Depolarization and early repolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarization and early repolarization', 'During the absolute refractory period, voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated, so no new action potential can be elicited regardless of stimulus strength. This period spans depolarization through the early phase of repolarization until enough Na+ channels have recovered.'),
('633bfe61-0641-5858-968d-9d16294158d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which of the following has the fastest conduction velocity?', '["Small unmyelinated fibers","Large unmyelinated fibers","Small myelinated fibers","Large myelinated fibers","C fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Large myelinated fibers', 'Conduction velocity increases with fiber diameter and with myelination. Large, myelinated A-alpha fibers conduct fastest via saltatory conduction (up to about 120 m/s).'),
('466c3be4-5c91-5a29-80df-811ce169cf95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Regarding clinical cases in Cell Nerve Muscle: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('7c0f05bb-7710-50f5-b6ef-167881ec28ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Acetylcholine is degraded by:', '["COMT","Decarboxylase","Acetylcholinesterase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase","MAO"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholinesterase', 'Acetylcholinesterase rapidly hydrolyzes acetylcholine into acetate and choline in the synaptic cleft, terminating its action. This enzyme is the target of neostigmine and other anti-cholinesterases used in myasthenia gravis.'),
('e8e59f30-8028-575c-8977-c2082c5a2eb6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Myasthenia gravis is caused by:', '["Increased AChE activity","Reduced ACh synthesis","Antibodies against Ca2+ channels","Antibodies against voltage-gated Na+ channels","Antibodies against ACh receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies against ACh receptors', 'Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder caused by antibodies directed against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor at the motor end plate. Antibody binding reduces functional receptor number, weakening neuromuscular transmission.'),
('6781791e-d12e-560f-acc4-26fbcc0716cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which muscle fiber type is rich in myoglobin, mitochondria, and oxidative enzymes and is highly resistant to fatigue?', '["Type IIb (fast glycolytic)","Type I (slow oxidative)","Type IIa (fast oxidative-glycolytic)","Type IId","Type IIx"]'::jsonb, 'Type I (slow oxidative)', 'Type I (slow oxidative) fibers are red, contain abundant myoglobin and mitochondria, and rely on oxidative metabolism, making them fatigue-resistant. They contract slowly and are suited for sustained postural and endurance activity.'),
('41c173b3-73da-5298-8d3e-64bc981c9d37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Summation of EPSPs from multiple presynaptic inputs is called:', '["Facilitation","Tetanus","Post-tetanic potentiation","Spatial summation","Temporal summation"]'::jsonb, 'Spatial summation', 'Spatial summation occurs when EPSPs (or IPSPs) arising simultaneously from several different presynaptic inputs add together at the postsynaptic neuron. This is distinct from temporal summation, where successive impulses in a single input summate.'),
('69a99259-f0f4-575d-908d-7902c8ff8aa2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The threshold potential for a typical neuron is approximately:', '["-90 mV","+30 mV","-70 mV","-55 mV","0 mV"]'::jsonb, '-55 mV', 'The threshold potential of most neurons is approximately -55 mV. When the membrane is depolarized to threshold, the opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels becomes regenerative and an action potential is triggered.'),
('3284a822-b509-5109-8a6b-3b1de6751b2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which channel is responsible for the repolarization phase of the action potential?', '["Voltage-gated K+ channels","Voltage-gated Na+ channels","Non-gated K+ channels","Cl- channels","Ca2+ channels"]'::jsonb, 'Voltage-gated K+ channels', 'Repolarization is produced by efflux of K+ through voltage-gated K+ channels, which open slowly during the action potential and restore the negative membrane potential. Their delayed opening underlies the slight after-hyperpolarization as well.'),
('92a21e4c-33b3-5811-879b-bfb7c91ff1d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The Nernst equation calculates:', '["Membrane capacitance","Refractory period length","Action potential amplitude","Conduction velocity","Equilibrium potential for an ion"]'::jsonb, 'Equilibrium potential for an ion', 'The Nernst equation gives the equilibrium potential of a single ion species at which its chemical and electrical driving forces balance. It assumes the membrane is permeable to only that one ion.'),
('9a1874ca-d940-534f-899d-486d8f36f4fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What is the equilibrium potential for K+ in a typical cell?', '["+60 mV","0 mV","-55 mV","-70 mV","-90 mV"]'::jsonb, '-90 mV', 'The K+ equilibrium potential is approximately -90 mV because intracellular K+ concentration is much higher than extracellular. The resting membrane potential lies close to this value because of high resting K+ permeability.'),
('f6c0643e-4bf5-5bea-8df9-420b74ca231b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation accounts for:', '["Multiple ion permeabilities","Single ion species","Synaptic delay","Channel gating","Membrane thickness"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple ion permeabilities', 'The Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation computes the membrane potential when the membrane is permeable to more than one ion. Each ion contributes according to its concentration gradient and relative membrane permeability.'),
('79817d3a-fb87-53b1-a591-2edb1b853e43'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which glial cell forms the myelin sheath in the CNS?', '["Microglia","Oligodendrocytes","Schwann cells","Ependymal cells","Astrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'Oligodendrocytes myelinate axons within the central nervous system, with a single cell ensheathing segments of several different axons. Schwann cells myelinate peripheral nerves, at most one segment per cell.'),
('4c11b128-6ca2-5f10-943a-e1e5f5d8b2d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'In a neuron, the trigger zone for action potential initiation is at:', '["Axon terminal","Axon hillock","Dendrites","Cell body","Node of Ranvier"]'::jsonb, 'Axon hillock', 'The axon hillock has the highest density of voltage-gated Na+ channels and is the usual site where the action potential is generated. Graded potentials arising on dendrites and soma spread to the hillock, where threshold is first reached.'),
('f35e8c0e-7a92-575c-9d70-c1f38739a9f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The synaptic delay is mainly due to:', '["Receptor binding","Axonal conduction","ACh diffusion","Neurotransmitter degradation","Ca2+ entry and vesicle fusion"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ entry and vesicle fusion', 'Most of the 0.5-1 ms synaptic delay reflects the time required for Ca2+ to enter the presynaptic terminal through voltage-gated channels and for synaptic vesicles to dock and fuse. This is the slowest step in chemical transmission.'),
('2012705a-3e25-5164-a4c5-a5d478b0a9d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The neurotransmitter released by alpha motor neurons at the neuromuscular junction is:', '["Acetylcholine","Aspartate","Glutamate","GABA","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine', 'Alpha motor neurons release acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. ACh diffuses across the cleft and binds nicotinic receptors on the motor end plate to generate the end-plate potential.'),
('dd5f213f-f5e0-50a0-af5a-9ba3fe109e13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Strychnine poisoning blocks inhibition mediated by:', '["Glycine","Acetylcholine","Glutamate","GABA","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Strychnine competitively blocks glycine receptors, the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord. Loss of glycinergic inhibition causes generalized muscle spasms and convulsions.'),
('1a1c8ce4-2727-5016-9330-981039b81eac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The length constant (lambda) is determined by:', '["Membrane resistance/internal resistance ratio","Internal resistance only","Internal resistance/membrane resistance ratio","Membrane capacitance","Membrane resistance only"]'::jsonb, 'Membrane resistance/internal resistance ratio', 'The length constant equals the square root of the membrane resistance divided by the internal (axial) resistance. A high membrane resistance and low internal resistance allow the voltage change to spread further along the fiber.'),
('e79c1b5a-c9f0-5409-a4cf-b671b6f9f1cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by deficiency of:', '["Dystroglycan","Titin","Actin","Dystrophin","Myosin"]'::jsonb, 'Dystrophin', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy results from a mutation in the dystrophin gene, causing absence of dystrophin. Dystrophin links the actin cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix and its loss makes the sarcolemma fragile during contraction.'),
('d24ad37c-58bb-5e98-8024-7b6c77151017'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle requires:', '["ATP binding","Na+ influx","ACh release","Ca2+ influx from ECF","Ca2+ release from SR"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ release from SR', 'In skeletal muscle, depolarization of the T-tubules triggers the ryanodine receptor to release Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This rise in cytosolic Ca2+ activates the contractile proteins; extracellular Ca2+ entry is not essential.'),
('3a8d3d2c-7b1f-5bcf-8855-ef16b0b40d10'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'In skeletal muscle, a triad is formed by:', '["One T-tubule + two SR terminal cisternae","Two T-tubules + one SR","One T-tubule + one SR","Three T-tubules","Three SR cisternae"]'::jsonb, 'One T-tubule + two SR terminal cisternae', 'A triad consists of one transverse (T) tubule flanked by two terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum. This arrangement allows the action potential signal to be transferred to the SR and release Ca2+.'),
('8973a7df-29c4-5c86-8a56-d720dee7998e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'T-tubules are invaginations of:', '["Sarcolemma","Endoplasmic reticulum","Sarcoplasmic reticulum","Myofibrils","Golgi apparatus"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcolemma', 'T-tubules are deep invaginations of the sarcolemma (the muscle cell membrane). They conduct the action potential into the interior of the fiber to reach the sarcoplasmic reticulum.'),
('65e46ca4-bda9-55a7-9f3f-d1a1e8509b84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Regarding clinical cases: Warfarin primarily metabolised by:', '["CYP2C9","CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2C9', 'CYP2C9 metabolises the more potent S-warfarin.'),
('0dc58dbf-5c66-5bc0-89d3-70870f10ee73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which band of the sarcomere contains only thick filaments?', '["I-band","H-zone","A-band","M-line","Z-disc"]'::jsonb, 'H-zone', 'The H-zone is the central portion of the A band containing thick filaments only, with no overlap of actin. The I band contains only thin filaments.'),
('b71d929b-799a-5b21-b1b7-1ff22c1cbb94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'During muscle contraction which band shortens?', '["H-zone only","I-band only","A-band","I-band and H-zone","Z-disc"]'::jsonb, 'I-band and H-zone', 'During contraction the thick and thin filaments slide past each other, so the I band and the H-zone shorten while the A band length stays constant. The sarcomere as a whole shortens.'),
('e58e51c2-b7b7-5649-adb0-6e4d10b8cc4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The force of muscle contraction is increased by:', '["Decreasing the number of active motor units","Decreasing pH","Decreasing frequency","Increasing frequency of stimulation","Increasing temperature only"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing frequency of stimulation', 'Raising the frequency of stimulation produces temporal summation and tetanus, generating greater force. More motor unit recruitment and greater initial length also increase force.'),
('f6737260-0d81-5248-9948-931b897df222'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Tetanus in muscle physiology refers to:', '["Isometric contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Sustained contraction from high-frequency stimulation","Bacterial infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Isotonic contraction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Twitch contraction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sustained contraction from high-frequency stimulation', 'In muscle physiology, tetanus is a sustained, smooth contraction produced when stimuli arrive so rapidly that successive twitches fuse. Complete tetanus occurs when there is no relaxation between stimuli.'),
('3f55df40-b35f-50ad-88fe-8171ad74ffde'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The sliding filament theory of muscle contraction was independently proposed by:', '["Sherrington","Bernard","Cannon","Hodgkin and Huxley","Huxley and Huxley"]'::jsonb, 'Huxley and Huxley', 'A.F. Huxley and H.E. Huxley independently published the sliding filament theory, stating that muscle shortens as thick and thin filaments slide past each other without changing their lengths. Hodgkin and Huxley described the ionic basis of the action potential.'),
('11028452-834c-5584-be69-cb8d1d186cfc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Muscle fatigue is most directly associated with depletion of:', '["GTP","cAMP","ADP","AMP","ATP"]'::jsonb, 'ATP', 'Fatigue of muscle is associated with depletion of ATP and creatine phosphate stores. Falling ATP impairs cross-bridge cycling and Ca2+ reuptake by the sarcoplasmic reticulum, while accumulation of ADP, Pi, and H+ further reduces contraction.'),
('20f79569-83bf-5b58-be38-39f4cb985687'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Rigor mortis is due to:', '["Ca2+ overload resulting in decreased functional activity","Lactic acidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ATP depletion preventing cross-bridge detachment","Protein denaturation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dehydration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ATP depletion preventing cross-bridge detachment', 'Rigor mortis occurs after death when ATP is depleted, and without ATP the myosin head cannot detach from actin. The persistent rigor (cross-bridge) bonds keep the muscles stiff until autolysis breaks down the proteins.'),
('39395f04-2d85-520b-ab92-f1b2df665867'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which statement is a distinguishing feature of smooth muscle compared with skeletal muscle?', '["It has troponin","It has actin","Z-discs are present","Sarcomeres are present","It has calmodulin instead of troponin"]'::jsonb, 'It has calmodulin instead of troponin', 'Smooth muscle lacks troponin and also lacks organized sarcomeres and Z-discs. Its contraction is regulated by Ca2+ binding to calmodulin, which activates myosin light chain kinase.'),
('9af08ca2-007f-5695-951a-b381410a2395'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'In smooth muscle Ca2+ binds to:', '["Tropomyosin","Actin","Myosin light chain kinase","Calmodulin","Troponin C"]'::jsonb, 'Calmodulin', 'In smooth muscle, Ca2+ binds calmodulin because troponin is absent. The Ca2+-calmodulin complex activates myosin light chain kinase, which phosphorylates the myosin light chain to initiate contraction.'),
('fda24782-4859-5eb9-a447-18cacee5a001'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'MLCK phosphorylates:', '["Myosin light chain","Actin","Caldesmon","Troponin","Tropomyosin"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin light chain', 'Myosin light chain kinase (MLCK), activated by Ca2+-calmodulin, phosphorylates the regulatory myosin light chain. This phosphorylation permits the myosin head to interact with actin and generate smooth muscle contraction.'),
('8f3eaf96-996d-558a-b35f-afb3577c729d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Excitation-contraction coupling in smooth muscle is initiated by:', '["Na+ influx","Cl- influx","Mg2+ influx","Ca2+ influx","K+ influx"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ influx', 'Smooth muscle contraction begins when cytosolic Ca2+ rises, largely through Ca2+ influx via voltage-gated or ligand-gated channels together with SR release. Ca2+ then binds calmodulin to trigger contraction.'),
('bce1ca9e-5045-515a-b136-d03d38f8d20d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The neuromuscular junction is an example of a:', '["Rectifying synapse","Dendrodendritic synapse","Electrical synapse","Chemical synapse","Gap junction"]'::jsonb, 'Chemical synapse', 'The neuromuscular junction is a chemical synapse because acetylcholine diffuses across the synaptic cleft to bind receptors. This is distinct from electrical synapses, which pass current directly through gap junctions.'),
('cf18928a-63a6-5399-a038-3de2ba115e4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Second messengers include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["DAG","cGMP","IP3","cAMP","ATP"]'::jsonb, 'ATP', 'DAG, IP3, cAMP, and cGMP all act as intracellular second messengers. ATP is a high-energy nucleotide and the immediate energy source of the cell, not a second messenger.'),
('8d2eb8e0-ff2d-524e-92fa-e72ccf2dba99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'G-proteins are activated by binding of:', '["cAMP","ADP","ATP","GTP","GDP"]'::jsonb, 'GTP', 'AG-protein is activated when the receptor causes it to exchange GDP for GTP on its alpha subunit. Hydrolysis of GTP to GDP inactivates the protein.'),
('e5328458-fc99-55ec-be4a-92e854d2bd50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The sodium-potassium pump is an example of:', '["Simple diffusion","Uniport","Facilitated diffusion","Antiport","Symport"]'::jsonb, 'Antiport', 'The Na+/K+ ATPase moves Na+ and K+ in opposite directions across the membrane, so it is an antiporter (counter-transporter). It is also a primary active transporter because it hydrolyzes ATP directly.'),
('a3ea6d03-997c-56ea-bbf5-40e95429909e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Glucose enters a cell down its concentration gradient through a carrier protein without ATP. Which transport mechanism is involved?', '["Facilitated diffusion","Secondary active transport","Primary active transport","Active transport","Endocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitated diffusion', 'Movement of glucose down its concentration gradient through a carrier protein (e.g., GLUT) without energy expenditure is facilitated diffusion. Unlike active transport, it requires no ATP and does not accumulate solute against its gradient.'),
('18f13b12-ec5e-5c8a-bc71-963886787f92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'SGLT1 is an example of:', '["Primary active transport","Antiport","Facilitated diffusion","Secondary active transport (symport)","Exocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary active transport (symport)', 'SGLT1 couples glucose transport to the downhill movement of Na+ in the same direction, making it a secondary active transporter and a symporter. The energy comes from the Na+ gradient maintained by the Na+/K+ ATPase.'),
('1cd23595-5fe2-5062-ace9-9bdf094a9d80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger is:', '["Uniport","Primary active","Symport","Facilitated diffusion","Antiport"]'::jsonb, 'Antiport', 'The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger moves Na+ inward and Ca2+ outward in opposite directions, so it is an antiporter. It is a secondary active transporter powered by the Na+ gradient.'),
('64a6fc8c-aded-55bb-82f3-8b41c279889e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Endocytosis that is receptor-mediated is called:', '["Clathrin-mediated endocytosis","Phagocytosis","Pinocytosis","Transcytosis","Potocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Clathrin-mediated endocytosis', 'Receptor-mediated endocytosis involves ligands binding to specific receptors that cluster in clathrin-coated pits, which invaginate and pinch off as clathrin-coated vesicles. This pathway is used for LDL, transferrin, and many hormones.'),
('514a38d5-3aa1-5b50-bf35-2ad52fbeb604'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 50-year-old male presents with cardiac arrhythmia. Electrolytes show Ca2+ 6.5 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL). Which electrolyte abnormality does this value represent?', '["Hypocalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypokalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocalcemia', 'A serum calcium of 6.5 mg/dL is below the normal range (8.5-10.5 mg/dL), indicating hypocalcemia. Hypocalcemia lowers the membrane threshold for excitation and can prolong the QT interval and cause cardiac arrhythmias.'),
('cc13237b-b6cf-56b5-a931-ffdc8c02f59e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Hyperkalemia would cause the resting membrane potential to become:', '["More negative","Hyperpolarized","Less negative (depolarized)","More positive overshoot","Unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'Less negative (depolarized)', 'Elevated extracellular K+ reduces the K+ concentration gradient, so the resting membrane potential moves closer to the K+ equilibrium potential and becomes less negative (partially depolarized). This increases excitability and can cause arrhythmias.'),
('d65acc45-1974-5c09-a0e8-33ae98a155a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Voltage-gated sodium channels are primarily responsible for which phase of the action potential?', '["Repolarization","Hyperpolarization","Depolarization (rising phase)","Resting membrane potential","Graded potentials"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarization (rising phase)', 'Voltage-gated Na+ channels open rapidly during depolarization, allowing a large inward Na+ current that produces the rising phase (upstroke) of the action potential. They then inactivate quickly, which ends the upstroke.'),
('38b7c5bf-cac3-5058-923c-e1290b18ffc7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Tetrodotoxin (TTX) is a potent blocker of:', '["K+ channels","HCN channels","Ca2+ channels","Na+ channels","Cl- channels"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ channels', 'Tetrodotoxin, from pufferfish, binds to and blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels. It abolishes the depolarizing phase of action potentials in nerve and muscle without blocking K+ or Ca2+ currents.'),
('18440cdc-b186-5d2c-abbe-0365886c5003'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The Nernst potential for Na+ is approximately:', '["+60 mV","0 mV","-90 mV","-70 mV","+30 mV"]'::jsonb, '+60 mV', 'Because extracellular Na+ is much higher than intracellular Na+, the Na+ equilibrium potential is positive, approximately +60 mV at 37 degrees C. The action potential peak approaches this value because of the transient increase in Na+ permeability.'),
('a1da3e4c-57fc-5f7d-baf4-717ebc989e0e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'After an action potential, continued potassium efflux makes the membrane potential more negative than its resting value. What phase is this?', '["During EPSP","Hyperpolarization phase","Late depolarization","Peak of action potential","During absolute refractory"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpolarization phase', 'The undershoot (after-hyperpolarization) occurs because voltage-gated K+ channels remain open after the action potential, so K+ efflux continues and drives the membrane more negative than the resting potential. It subsides as these channels close.'),
('3d765399-1ca3-5cd9-9a45-d3636dcd6d26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which is true about Type C nerve fibers?', '["Conduction velocity 50-70 m/s","Myelinated","Slow conduction","Low threshold for stimulation","Thick diameter"]'::jsonb, 'Slow conduction', 'Type C fibers are thin, unmyelinated fibers that conduct slowly (0.5-2 m/s). They transmit pain, temperature, and autonomic signals.'),
('309ea5c7-f765-5b17-ac9e-12a760fe928d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The strength-duration curve describes:', '["Conduction velocity vs diameter","Stimulus intensity vs duration needed for excitation","Refractory period vs temperature","Voltage vs current","Frequency vs amplitude"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulus intensity vs duration needed for excitation', 'The strength-duration curve plots the minimum stimulus intensity required to excite a tissue against the duration of the stimulus. It defines rheobase and chronaxie.'),
('dfb73004-375c-5396-b9c1-55041634da1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Rheobase is defined as:', '["Minimum current for infinite duration","Maximum voltage","Minimum voltage for infinite duration","Threshold voltage at 1 ms","Minimum duration at twice rheobase"]'::jsonb, 'Minimum current for infinite duration', 'Rheobase is the minimum current intensity that will excite the tissue when applied for an indefinitely long duration. Stimuli below rheobase never excite regardless of duration.'),
('4e5c0b30-415d-5308-b986-b26ccefb105a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What is the chronaxie defined as?', '["The minimum time required for a stimulus at twice rheobase intensity","Stimulation at rheobase intensity","Stimulation at threshold intensity","Stimulation at supramaximal intensity","Stimulation at half rheobase intensity"]'::jsonb, 'The minimum time required for a stimulus at twice rheobase intensity', 'Chronaxie is the minimum duration for which a stimulus of twice the rheobase intensity must act to elicit an action potential. It is a measure of tissue excitability.'),
('76de7317-434c-5690-adb2-795d0e357941'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by Renshaw cells?', '["Glycine","Dopamine","GABA","Glutamate","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Renshaw cells are inhibitory interneurons in the spinal cord that release glycine. They inhibit alpha motor neurons and mediate recurrent inhibition.'),
('0c5aeec8-fd35-5c59-ae12-260de92bd0ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Denervation hypersensitivity is due to:', '["Increased ACh release","Downregulation of receptors","Decreased AChE","Increased reuptake","Upregulation of receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Upregulation of receptors', 'When a muscle or neuron is denervated, the number of receptors on the membrane increases (upregulation) and becomes more sensitive to the neurotransmitter. This denervation hypersensitivity explains the exaggerated response to agonist drugs after denervation.'),
('9b49c64f-a0d2-534c-81d7-0b73593a289b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The diameter of myelinated nerve fibers ranges from:', '["20-50 μm","0.1-0.5 μm","0.5-1 μm","1-20 μm","50-100 μm"]'::jsonb, '1-20 μm', 'Myelinated peripheral nerve fibers range from about 1 to 20 μm in diameter, from small A-delta to large A-alpha fibers. Unmyelinated C fibers are much thinner (0.2-1.5 μm).'),
('bd45fd2c-75c5-589c-8b83-f0ea366782d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Internodal distance is proportional to:', '["Sodium channel density","Myelin thickness","Axon diameter","Capacitance","Resistance"]'::jsonb, 'Axon diameter', 'The distance between successive nodes of Ranvier (internodal distance) increases with axon diameter in myelinated fibers. Larger diameter and longer internodes both speed saltatory conduction.'),
('ab8b4024-0f4f-5b8f-8f6b-6267b1d25714'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which ion channel is responsible for the pacemaker potential in the SA node?', '["Funny current (If) channel","K+ channel","L-type Ca2+ channel","Voltage-gated Na+ channel","T-type Ca2+ channel"]'::jsonb, 'Funny current (If) channel', 'The pacemaker potential in the sinoatrial node is initiated by the funny current (If), a mixed Na+/K+ inward current that slowly depolarizes the cell during diastole. L-type Ca2+ channels then complete the upstroke.'),
('0318803c-9911-5c74-bce4-a8a48941cf0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which equation correctly expresses the Nernst equilibrium potential at 37 degrees C for a monovalent ion?', '["E = 58 log ([ion]o/[ion]i)","E = 61 log ([ion]i/[ion]o)","E = 58 log ([ion]i/[ion]o)","E = 61 log ([ion]o/[ion]i)","E = 61 ln ([ion]o/[ion]i)"]'::jsonb, 'E = 61 log ([ion]o/[ion]i)', 'At 37 degrees C, the Nernst equation for a monovalent ion is E = 61 log ([ion]o/[ion]i) mV, where [ion]o and [ion]i are the extracellular and intracellular concentrations. The factor of 58 applies at 20 degrees C, and the extracellular/intracellular ratio must be in the log argument.'),
('2a0d6cc7-d8ec-59e4-a96b-3bdd9e37a8ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Membrane fluidity is increased by:', '["Long chain fatty acids","Unsaturated fatty acids","Saturated fatty acids","Glycolipids","Cholesterol at high temperature"]'::jsonb, 'Unsaturated fatty acids', 'Unsaturated fatty acids contain double bonds that introduce kinks in the acyl chains, packing the bilayer less tightly and increasing membrane fluidity. Saturated and long-chain fatty acids decrease fluidity.'),
('e07c138c-aba1-51c7-9d44-f1251188a7e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Ankyrin links:', '["Intermediate filaments to membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","Microtubules to membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Actin to myosin mediated by specific receptor activation","Integral membrane proteins to spectrin-actin cytoskeleton","Na+ channels to K+ channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Integral membrane proteins to spectrin-actin cytoskeleton', 'Ankyrin attaches integral membrane proteins, such as the Na+/K+ ATPase and anion exchanger, to the spectrin-actin cytoskeleton. This linkage is prominent in the erythrocyte membrane.'),
('633cbed3-4541-574f-ad92-6ae4b349bb67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Gap junctions are formed by:', '["Connexins","Selectins","Cadherins","Integrins","Claudins"]'::jsonb, 'Connexins', 'Gap junctions are channels made of connexin proteins arranged as hexamers (connexons). They allow direct electrical and chemical coupling between adjacent cells.'),
('91064f23-ec52-553e-a924-337fe1e30fb1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which transporter maintains the resting membrane potential by moving 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP hydrolyzed?', '["Na+/Ca2+ exchanger","GLUT1","SGLT1","H+/K+ ATPase","Na+/K+ ATPase"]'::jsonb, 'Na+/K+ ATPase', 'The Na+/K+ ATPase hydrolyzes one ATP to export 3 Na+ and import 2 K+, creating and maintaining the ion gradients that establish the resting membrane potential. Each cycle is electrogenic, moving one net positive charge outward.'),
('07b98f0d-ad43-53bc-a221-2d6e341c688a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The resting membrane potential is most dependent on the membrane permeability to which ion?', '["K+ conductance","Na+/K+ ATPase","Cl- conductance","Ca2+ conductance","Na+ conductance"]'::jsonb, 'K+ conductance', 'At rest the membrane is by far most permeable to K+, so the resting membrane potential lies close to the K+ equilibrium potential. High resting K+ conductance dominates the balance of ion permeabilities that sets the resting potential.'),
('44605227-41cc-50d8-ab19-5e6ba4c8cd40'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The Goldman equation is also known as the:', '["Hodgkin-Huxley equation","Nernst equation","Cable equation","Donnan equation","GHK equation"]'::jsonb, 'GHK equation', 'The Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz (GHK) equation, also called the constant-field equation, predicts the resting membrane potential when the membrane is permeable to several ions. It differs from the Nernst equation, which considers only one ion.'),
('778ca636-9552-5159-95fe-0207b670c2ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which anesthetic works by potentiating GABA-A receptors?', '["Tetrodotoxin","Procaine","Bupivacaine","Lidocaine","Propofol"]'::jsonb, 'Propofol', 'Propofol enhances GABA-A receptor-mediated inhibitory chloride currents, increasing neuronal inhibition. Lidocaine, procaine, and bupivacaine are local anesthetics that block voltage-gated Na+ channels, and tetrodotoxin also blocks Na+ channels.'),
('a3846cf8-5b4b-51d1-948e-8f134181d2da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The rate of rise of the action potential upstroke is determined by:', '["Na+ influx magnitude","Ca2+ influx","Na+ efflux","Cl- influx","K+ efflux"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ influx magnitude', 'The steepness (dV/dt) of the action potential rising phase reflects the magnitude of inward Na+ current through voltage-gated Na+ channels. Larger Na+ influx produces a faster upstroke.'),
('92cab877-68e7-5f22-8a58-09f2203aecee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which is the smallest nerve fiber type?', '["A delta","C fibers","A beta","A gamma","A alpha"]'::jsonb, 'C fibers', 'C fibers are the smallest in diameter (0.2-1.5 μm) and are unmyelinated, so they conduct most slowly. A-delta, A-beta, A-gamma, and A-alpha fibers are all small-to-large myelinated fibers.'),
('493b97db-0e06-5d12-b54c-4c7fff3e0c6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A-alpha fibers are primarily:', '["Temperature fibers","Autonomic fibers","Motor fibers to extrafusal fibers","Touch fibers","Pain fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Motor fibers to extrafusal fibers', 'A-alpha fibers are the largest myelinated motor fibers that innervate extrafusal skeletal muscle fibers. They also include proprioceptive afferents from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs.'),
('09adfeb4-fd44-5a33-9c07-3cff3f5bcff1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A-delta fibers conduct at:', '["30-50 m/s","0.5-2 m/s","5-30 m/s","70-120 m/s","50-70 m/s"]'::jsonb, '5-30 m/s', 'A-delta fibers are thin, myelinated fibers that conduct at about 5-30 m/s and transmit fast pain and cold sensation. C fibers conduct at 0.5-2 m/s, and large A-alpha fibers at up to 120 m/s.'),
('3fe74c10-0ddc-54bb-a16e-f329c5d023bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Hyponatremia (Na+ 120 mEq/L) reduces the transmembrane sodium gradient. Which aspect of nerve function is most directly impaired?', '["Depolarization (Na+ influx)","K+ efflux during repolarization","Na+/K+ ATPase activity","Ca2+ reuptake into the SR","Synthesis of neurotransmitters"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarization (Na+ influx)', 'A smaller Na+ gradient means less Na+ enters through voltage-gated channels during an action potential, so depolarization is weakened and neuronal excitability falls. This contributes to the muscle weakness and confusion seen in severe hyponatremia.'),
('d25fad98-ebb1-55ce-9e70-d13a774386e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Wallerian degeneration occurs in:', '["Proximal segment resulting in decreased functional activity","Distal segment after nerve transection","Synaptic terminal mediated by specific receptor activation","Cell body without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nodes of Ranvier leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distal segment after nerve transection', 'Wallerian degeneration is the disintegration of the axon and myelin in the distal (cut-off) segment after a nerve is transected. The proximal segment and cell body survive and later regenerate the nerve.'),
('d8217e9f-e9f0-59e4-a76d-dcdb7e9c3b75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Neurotrophins are released by:', '["Golgi apparatus","Postsynaptic neuron","Astrocytes","Schwann cells","Presynaptic neuron"]'::jsonb, 'Postsynaptic neuron', 'Neurotrophins such as NGF and BDNF are produced and released by target (postsynaptic) cells. They are taken up by the presynaptic terminal and transported retrogradely to sustain the neuron and guide connectivity.'),
('6c068862-145b-53de-a2b9-62abb1e384d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Nerve growth factor (NGF) acts on:', '["Motor neurons","Glial cells","Sensory and sympathetic neurons","Interneurons","GABAergic neurons"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory and sympathetic neurons', 'NGF promotes the survival, growth, and differentiation of sensory and sympathetic neurons. It binds the TrkA receptor and is retrogradely transported from peripheral targets.'),
('52db8668-39b4-540e-8560-ebd78c464824'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Ataxia and intention tremor localize to the cerebellum. Which cell type provides the sole output of the cerebellar cortex?', '["Stellate cell bodies","Parallel fibers","Purkinje cell bodies","Golgi cell bodies","Granule cell bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cell bodies', 'Purkinje cells are the only output cells of the cerebellar cortex, sending inhibitory GABAergic projections to the deep cerebellar nuclei. Their axons leave the cortex while granule, Golgi, and stellate cells are intrinsic neurons.'),
('62ac3eb7-8df6-596b-9362-78e75e447f6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which SNARE protein is involved in vesicle docking?', '["SNAP-25","Syntaxin","Synaptotagmin","Synaptobrevin (VAMP)","Synapsin"]'::jsonb, 'Synaptobrevin (VAMP)', 'Synaptobrevin (VAMP) is the vesicle SNARE (v-SNARE) that pairs with the plasma-membrane SNAREs syntaxin and SNAP-25 to dock and fuse synaptic vesicles. Synaptotagmin is the calcium sensor, and synapsin tethers vesicles to the cytoskeleton.'),
('04564966-f0ec-5a4a-9d30-cbb0f924cd5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Synaptotagmin acts as a:', '["Vesicle tether","Calcium sensor","Scaffold protein","SNARE protein","Recycling signal"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium sensor', 'Synaptotagmin is the calcium-sensing protein on the synaptic vesicle. Ca2+ binding to synaptotagmin triggers rapid SNARE-mediated vesicle fusion and transmitter release.'),
('9cdf84d3-31d7-54c6-9649-97edac03ff68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Botulinum toxin types B, D, F, and G impair neurotransmitter release by cleaving:', '["Synapsin","Syntaxin","SNAP-25","Synaptotagmin","VAMP/synaptobrevin"]'::jsonb, 'VAMP/synaptobrevin', 'Botulinum toxins are zinc endopeptidases that cleave SNARE proteins: types B, D, F, and G cleave VAMP/synaptobrevin, while types A and E cleave SNAP-25. This blocks vesicle fusion and acetylcholine release, causing flaccid paralysis.'),
('1b1a86b4-836e-5ca0-a48b-c067e60af4ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Tetanus toxin blocks release of:', '["Acetylcholine","Glycine and GABA","Glutamate","Norepinephrine","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine and GABA', 'Tetanus toxin is transported retrograde to the CNS, where it cleaves synaptobrevin and blocks release of the inhibitory neurotransmitters glycine and GABA. Loss of inhibition causes spastic paralysis.'),
('d2ebd534-76a3-58b5-888c-040bbc63deb0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The end-plate potential (EPP) is generated by:', '["K+ efflux","Ca2+ influx","Mixed Na+/K+ current through AChR","Na+ influx through ACh receptors","Cl- influx"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed Na+/K+ current through AChR', 'The nicotinic ACh receptor is a nonselective cation channel, so its opening produces roughly equal Na+ influx and K+ efflux. The net inward Na+ current depolarizes the end plate, generating the EPP.'),
('c33cd774-31d5-5056-9ad6-b862314c5845'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The safety factor at the neuromuscular junction is:', '[">1","0","Infinite","<1","=1"]'::jsonb, '>1', 'Normally the end-plate potential greatly exceeds the threshold depolarization required to trigger an action potential, giving a safety factor greater than 1. This ensures each nerve impulse reliably produces a muscle fiber action potential.'),
('2f39808f-0765-56b6-9850-5391d28f98e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'In Lambert-Eaton syndrome antibodies target:', '["Voltage-gated Na+ channels","Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels","ACh receptors","AChE","MuSK"]'::jsonb, 'Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels', 'Lambert-Eaton syndrome is caused by autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels at the neuromuscular junction. Reduced Ca2+ entry lowers acetylcholine release, causing weakness that transiently improves with exercise.'),
('d5a6cea0-476e-5160-8b6e-17ee50a7e99e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A patient develops painful exercise-induced cramps and dark urine from myoglobin after strenuous activity. Which presentation is most likely?', '["Cardiac arrhythmias resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatomegaly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exercise-induced muscle cramps and myoglobinuria","Seizures without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle weakness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise-induced muscle cramps and myoglobinuria', 'Painful cramps on exercise with myoglobinuria (dark urine) are characteristic of a glycogen storage myopathy such as McArdle disease, caused by myophosphorylase deficiency. Inadequate glycogen breakdown prevents ATP generation, leading to rhabdomyolysis on exertion.'),
('2ecc5f5f-9024-5666-8ea9-5092fb3df863'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Malignant hyperthermia is triggered by:', '["Volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine","Propofol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Local anesthetics mediated by specific receptor activation","Benzodiazepines without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Opioids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine', 'Malignant hyperthermia is triggered by volatile inhalational anesthetics (e.g., halothane, sevoflurane) and succinylcholine in susceptible individuals. A defect in the ryanodine receptor causes massive Ca2+ release from the SR, sustained contraction, and hyperthermia.'),
('296d8a62-69be-5163-aedb-c8fc1595845a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'In the cross-bridge cycle, the power stroke refers to:', '["ATP is hydrolyzed","Actin binds myosin","ATP binds myosin","ADP is released","Myosin head pivots"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin head pivots', 'The power stroke is the rotation of the myosin head that drags the thin filament toward the sarcomere center once it is bound to actin. It is powered by the energy released as inorganic phosphate and ADP leave the myosin head.'),
('1b0607b4-2c00-5795-9851-ec6945e8bfb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What is the typical time frame for the remyelination process to begin after a demyelinating injury?', '["1-2 hours","12-24 hours","3-4 hours","24-48 hours","Immediately"]'::jsonb, '3-4 hours', 'After a demyelinating injury, remyelination begins within hours, as oligodendrocyte precursor cells proliferate and begin to differentiate into myelinating oligodendrocytes. The process of synthesizing new myelin then continues over days to weeks.'),
('88a12773-7711-5e54-9bdc-29112d8c665f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The cytoskeleton of the erythrocyte is primarily composed of:', '["Intermediate filaments","Tubulin","Actin and myosin","Spectrin and actin","Collagen"]'::jsonb, 'Spectrin and actin', 'The erythrocyte membrane skeleton is a hexagonal lattice made mainly of spectrin tetramers linked to short actin filaments. Ankyrin and protein 4.1 connect this lattice to integral membrane proteins, giving the red cell its deformability.'),
('639a54c8-0553-51a6-ba2f-169b94840f94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Inhibition of the sodium-potassium pump alters transmembrane ion gradients. Which intracellular ionic change is expected?', '["Increased intracellular Na+","Membrane hyperpolarization","Increased intracellular K+","Increased resting potential","Decreased intracellular Ca2+"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intracellular Na+', 'If the Na+/K+ ATPase is inhibited, Na+ can no longer be pumped out, so intracellular Na+ rises while intracellular K+ falls. The cell also depolarizes as the sustaining gradients collapse, and Ca2+ tends to increase via the Na+/Ca2+ exchanger.'),
('127272fe-dfa1-5a30-b35e-3a51a7d67f72'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which has the greatest contribution to membrane capacitance?', '["Cholesterol","Glycocalyx","Integral proteins","Phospholipid bilayer","Water channels"]'::jsonb, 'Phospholipid bilayer', 'Membrane capacitance is dominated by the phospholipid bilayer, which acts as a thin insulating layer separating the conducting fluids of the intracellular and extracellular compartments. It behaves electrically like a capacitor and largely determines its capacitance.'),
('0f2ada42-9d3c-560c-bd48-71730f1f8077'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Facilitated diffusion differs from simple diffusion by:', '["Moving against the concentration gradient","Requiring ATP","Being non-saturable","Being saturable","Using carrier proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Using carrier proteins', 'Facilitated diffusion uses carrier proteins (permeases) that bind specific solutes and undergo conformational change, making transport saturable, specific, and faster than simple diffusion. Unlike active transport it still moves solutes down their concentration gradient and does not require ATP.'),
('36c17c96-e3c4-5569-81a1-44629c9b389e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Pinocytosis refers to:', '["Cell drinking","Cell eating","Receptor-mediated uptake","Bulk phase uptake of fluid and dissolved solutes","Exocytosis of secretory granules"]'::jsonb, 'Cell drinking', 'Pinocytosis (cell drinking) is the engulfment of extracellular fluid and dissolved solutes in small vesicles. Cell eating (phagocytosis) involves large particles, while receptor-mediated endocytosis is selective and clathrin-dependent.'),
('ed135dd9-edfb-5332-b130-d7ae6bc17ecd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Phagocytosis is performed by:', '["Fibroblasts","Neurons","Red blood cells","Macrophages and neutrophils","Epithelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages and neutrophils', 'Macrophages and neutrophils are professional phagocytes that engulf and destroy pathogens and debris. Fibroblasts, neurons, red blood cells, and epithelial cells do not perform significant phagocytosis.'),
('0926aa02-fe3c-56ab-8c45-a51c9a7cec32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which cellular process involves SNARE proteins mediating neurotransmitter release at the synaptic cleft?', '["Vesicle targeting and fusion","Action potential generation","Receptor desensitization","Signal transduction","Gene transcription"]'::jsonb, 'Vesicle targeting and fusion', 'SNARE proteins (synaptobrevin on vesicles, syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the plasma membrane) mediate docking and fusion of synaptic vesicles, releasing neurotransmitter into the cleft. This is the exocytotic step of quantal transmitter release.'),
('bfeb1f3e-8cd0-5cc6-af97-7a7c53479cc6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Calcium enters the presynaptic terminal through which channels?', '["HCN channels","Ligand-gated Ca2+ channels","Voltage-gated Na+ channels","Mechanosensitive Ca2+ channels","Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels"]'::jsonb, 'Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels', 'Depolarization of the terminal opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels (P/Q-type), and the resulting Ca2+ influx triggers rapid exocytosis of synaptic vesicles. This is the trigger for neurotransmitter release.'),
('7243b74d-99f6-5cf8-8eda-319fb3525d45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The quantal content of neurotransmitter release is:', '["Total neurotransmitter","Number of vesicles released","Frequency of release","Receptor number","Amount per vesicle"]'::jsonb, 'Number of vesicles released', 'Quantal content is the number of vesicles (quanta) released per nerve impulse. The postsynaptic response equals quantal content multiplied by quantal size (response to one vesicle).'),
('fa891c91-c24b-5292-bf6c-a258b406c800'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'At a chemical synapse, quantal size depends mainly on which factor?', '["Number of vesicles","Total release","Frequency of release","Receptor affinity","Amount of transmitter per vesicle"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of transmitter per vesicle', 'Quantal size is the postsynaptic potential produced by a single vesicle, which is set by the amount of transmitter packaged in each vesicle. Vesicle number instead determines quantal content.'),
('795a6944-c499-538d-9c2c-81818c6a8edd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Cell Nerve Muscle: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('f785732a-6673-53ff-a6eb-a0c5841e5851'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The H-zone of sarcomere is located in the:', '["Center of I-band","At M-line","At Z-disc","Center of A-band","Between A and I bands"]'::jsonb, 'Center of A-band', 'The H-zone is the central portion of the A-band that contains only thick myosin filaments with no overlapping thin actin filaments. The M-line lies at the center of the H-zone.'),
('3bcbc0ba-a3bf-52d5-9b45-9a46cb2233ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 45-year-old male undergoes muscle biopsy for suspected myopathy. What is the most likely site of pathology in the neuromuscular system?', '["Tropomyosin and actin","Titin and creatine kinase","Myosin filaments","Skeletal muscle fibers","Troponin complex"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal muscle fibers', 'Myopathy is a disease of the skeletal muscle fibers themselves, so biopsy shows pathological changes in the muscle. Creatine kinase is a serum marker and the other listed proteins are contractile elements, not separate sites of pathology.'),
('6a4c2296-8b37-5421-86a8-f65bdc43c640'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Titin functions as:', '["Receptor protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Enzymatic protein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Channel protein mediated by specific receptor activation","Contractile protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastic spring that maintains sarcomere alignment"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic spring that maintains sarcomere alignment', 'Titin is a giant elastic protein spanning from the Z-disc to the M-line that keeps thick filaments centered and provides passive elasticity to the sarcomere. It is not a receptor, enzyme, or channel.'),
('e91d4dcc-65ce-5f05-b23f-cc0e6371067a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 45-year-old female presents with muscle cramps after exercise. Which ion is most directly linked to this finding?', '["Ca2+","Na+","H+","Mg2+","K+"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+', 'Muscle cramps after exercise are associated with ionic disturbances, particularly of calcium, since Ca2+ is central to excitation-contraction coupling and low ionized Ca2+ increases neuromuscular excitability. Among the listed ions, Ca2+ is the key trigger for contraction.'),
('8a6e6de1-bc94-5bc9-a4d7-1592e91c4d1c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'During skeletal-muscle contraction, calcium binding to troponin exposes which sites on actin?', '["Troponin binding","ATP-binding sites","Myosin-binding sites on actin","Calcium-binding sites","Actin polymerization sites"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin-binding sites on actin', 'Ca2+ binding to troponin C moves tropomyosin away, exposing the myosin-binding sites on actin so cross-bridges can form. This is the calcium switch in excitation-contraction coupling of striated muscle.'),
('9477a7fc-8b0c-57a1-bc38-a5ec98553a1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which three subunits make up the troponin complex in skeletal muscle?', '["TnC TnI TnT","TnM TnN TnP","TnA TnB TnC","Tn1 Tn2 Tn3","Calmodulin TnC TnT"]'::jsonb, 'TnC TnI TnT', 'The troponin complex has troponin C (binds Ca2+), troponin I (inhibits actin-myosin interaction), and troponin T (binds tropomyosin). Together they regulate the calcium-dependent switch of contraction.'),
('9e9a6abe-3b2d-5c15-ba81-9eb986d02578'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which type of muscle fiber has the highest ATPase activity?', '["Type IIb","Type IIa","Smooth muscle","Cardiac muscle","Type I"]'::jsonb, 'Type IIb', 'Type IIb (fast glycolytic) fibers have the highest myosin ATPase activity, giving the fastest cross-bridge cycling and contraction velocity. Fast IIb fibers rely on glycolysis, so they fatigue rapidly.'),
('f4438122-3632-5d5d-85c9-572528c8d4d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Myoglobin is found in:', '["Type I fibers","All muscle fibers","Only cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle","Type IIb fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Type I fibers', 'Myoglobin is an oxygen-binding protein most abundant in slow oxidative Type I fibers, which depend on aerobic metabolism and need a ready oxygen store. It is high in cardiac and red (Type I) skeletal muscle.'),
('614fd25f-479b-55cd-8162-315356754a9e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The sarcoplasmic reticulum stores:', '["ATP","K+","Na+","Mg2+","Ca2+"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+', 'The sarcoplasmic reticulum is the intracellular store of Ca2+, which it sequesters via Ca2+-ATPase and releases through ryanodine receptors during excitation-contraction coupling.'),
('a29b864e-9dd7-55ad-972b-e5de56dd6f47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The DHP receptor in skeletal muscle is a:', '["Voltage sensor for E-C coupling","True Ca2+ channel opening on its own","Na+ channel","K+ channel","Cl- channel"]'::jsonb, 'Voltage sensor for E-C coupling', 'In skeletal muscle the dihydropyridine (DHP) receptor is an L-type Ca2+ channel that functions mainly as a voltage sensor; its conformational change mechanically opens the ryanodine receptor to release SR Ca2+.'),
('da5e7aa3-f500-54bf-96e2-07188a098d9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Ryanodine receptor is located on:', '["Sarcolemma","T-tubule","Myosin","Sarcoplasmic reticulum","Z-disc"]'::jsonb, 'Sarcoplasmic reticulum', 'The ryanodine receptor is the Ca2+-release channel embedded in the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane. It is coupled functionally to the DHP receptor in the T-tubule to trigger Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release.'),
('f1a4f1fa-75c5-5f8e-b0a7-b1295537517f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 25-year-old male presents with muscle cramps after exercise. What is the most likely source of the calcium required for normal muscle contraction?', '["Extracellular fluid calcium influx only","Calcium from sarcoplasmic reticulum only","Both ECF influx and SR release","Calcium from mitochondria","Calcium from the nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Both ECF influx and SR release', 'Skeletal muscle contraction is triggered mainly by Ca2+ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, although some enters from the extracellular fluid; both sources together raise cytosolic Ca2+ to activate the contractile apparatus.'),
('1df6e81e-72b5-5892-ad91-9ae0fd8f1fcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Myosin light chain phosphatase (MLCP) role is:', '["Release Ca2+ resulting in decreased functional activity","Activate myosin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phosphorylate myosin mediated by specific receptor activation","Dephosphorylate myosin light chain causing relaxation","Bind actin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dephosphorylate myosin light chain causing relaxation', 'MLCP removes the phosphate added by myosin light chain kinase, inactivating cross-bridge cycling and promoting smooth muscle relaxation. It is the antagonist of MLCK in the Ca2+-calmodulin contraction pathway.'),
('f3a48545-2906-5779-b124-7f132d56db5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which is false about single-unit smooth muscle?', '["Found in GI tract","It contracts as a syncytium","It is densely innervated","It shows pacemaker activity","It has gap junctions"]'::jsonb, 'It is densely innervated', 'Single-unit (visceral) smooth muscle contracts as a syncytium through gap junctions and shows spontaneous pacemaker activity; it is only sparsely innervated by a few varicosities. Dense innervation is a feature of multi-unit smooth muscle.'),
('f10a5b31-6649-50b5-a435-48f0ad7c539c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Multi-unit smooth muscle is found in:', '["Bladder","Iris and ciliary muscle","Blood vessels","GI tract","Uterus"]'::jsonb, 'Iris and ciliary muscle', 'Multi-unit smooth muscle has discrete, well-innervated units and is found in the iris, ciliary muscle, and piloerector muscles. Bladder, GI tract, uterus, and most blood vessels are single-unit smooth muscle.'),
('a62bd7a2-5301-5801-ac8d-6697be16b65a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'During therapeutic management of Cell Nerve Muscle, Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('64da0c41-381e-5f7a-9658-ba82c7c6141c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The length-tension relationship of muscle is best explained by:', '["Membrane potential","Excitation-contraction coupling","Sliding of actin and myosin filaments","Synaptic transmission","Cross-bridge number"]'::jsonb, 'Sliding of actin and myosin filaments', 'The length-tension relationship reflects the degree of overlap between actin and myosin filaments: tension is maximal at optimal overlap and falls at longer or shorter lengths because fewer cross-bridges can form.'),
('968704c9-bc15-5864-a7a7-d7afa3fc110d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Isometric contraction means:', '["Tension is constant","ATP used","Muscle length remains constant","Muscle shortens","Muscle lengthens"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle length remains constant', 'In an isometric contraction the muscle develops tension without a change in length, so it cannot see shortening; the load exceeds the tension the muscle can generate. Isotonic contraction involves shortening at constant tension.'),
('d046e801-cb98-5564-991c-8ebd5c3f23d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Isotonic contraction means:', '["Movement of a load","Constant length","Constant tension","Variable tension","Eccentric lengthening"]'::jsonb, 'Constant tension', 'In an isotonic contraction the muscle shortens while the tension it produces stays constant, so a load is moved. Isometric contraction, by contrast, keeps length constant and lets tension rise.'),
('01a28d8b-0881-52d8-964b-514f20252ff9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Cell Nerve Muscle: Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('0c5d2da8-338d-51f8-89b6-c5801a0e1711'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The velocity of muscle contraction is inversely related to:', '["Load","pH","ATP availability","Ca2+ concentration","Temperature"]'::jsonb, 'Load', 'The force-velocity relationship shows that as the load on a muscle increases, the velocity of shortening decreases; velocity is maximal at zero load and zero at maximal (isometric) load.'),
('db24b2e7-7f31-5907-abb7-83d6a60f2790'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The force-frequency relationship in muscle is due to:', '["Length-tension relation","Motor unit recruitment","Ballistic movement","Spatial summation","Temporal summation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal summation', 'As the frequency of stimulation rises, successive twitches sum because cytosolic Ca2+ remains elevated between stimuli, producing greater force; at high frequencies the contractions fuse into tetanus (temporal summation).'),
('bd964506-c9f9-51ca-97c4-9c5fcf48a26c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The size principle of motor unit recruitment states:', '["All motor units are recruited simultaneously","Small motor units are recruited before large ones","Fast fatigable fibers are recruited first","Large motor units are recruited first","Recruitment is random"]'::jsonb, 'Small motor units are recruited before large ones', 'The size principle (Henneman) states that as force demand increases, small motor units with low-threshold motoneurons are recruited first, followed by progressively larger units. This gives smooth, graded force generation.'),
('ce866ad4-76c0-5be3-9f7f-6debe9965553'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A motor unit consists of:', '["One motoneuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates","One muscle fiber and its nerve","Motor cortex and muscle","Multiple motoneurons and one fiber","One muscle and its tendon"]'::jsonb, 'One motoneuron and all the muscle fibers it innervates', 'A motor unit is a single alpha motoneuron plus all the skeletal muscle fibers it innervates; all fibers of one unit contract together when the neuron fires. The innervation ratio determines unit size.'),
('1ef9c89b-ff11-5599-84e0-0a70ad8c6d11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The innervation ratio is low in muscles that control:', '["Powerful muscles","Anti-gravity muscles","Proximal muscles","Fine movements","Postural muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Fine movements', 'Muscles performing fine, precise movements (e.g., extraocular, hand) have a low innervation ratio, with few fibers per motoneuron, allowing fine force gradation. Powerful muscles have high ratios.'),
('6ac1ef51-df25-5b32-89f9-e4a09f0b3244'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Muscle spindles detect:', '["Pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Tension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Temperature without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Length and rate of change of length"]'::jsonb, 'Length and rate of change of length', 'Muscle spindles are stretch receptors that signal muscle length (static response) and the rate of change of length (dynamic response) via Ia and II afferents. Tension is monitored by Golgi tendon organs.'),
('503be783-2ad7-52ce-ac13-cda692ee339c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Intrafusal fibers are innervated by:', '["Beta motoneurons","Delta fibers","C fibers","Alpha motoneurons","Gamma motoneurons"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma motoneurons', 'Intrafusal fibers of muscle spindles are innervated by gamma motoneurons, which adjust spindle sensitivity during muscle shortening. Extra-fusal (contractile) fibers are innervated by alpha motoneurons.'),
('cc5ff800-5b56-5b5f-a464-8906ee11b135'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Golgi tendon organs primarily monitor which variable?', '["Pain","Muscle length","Vibration","Joint position","Muscle tension"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle tension', 'Golgi tendon organs lie at the muscle-tendon junction and respond to the tension (force) developed by the muscle, transmitting via Ib afferents. They cause autogenic inhibition to protect the muscle.'),
('0f1e331e-c5a2-5b1b-b059-8ce6f13925fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'During a neurological examination, a patient shows loss of muscle over-tension protection. Which afferent pathway is affected?', '["Ib afferents","III afferents","Ia afferents","IV afferents","II afferents"]'::jsonb, 'Ib afferents', 'Golgi tendon organs transmit muscle tension signals through fast-conducting group Ib afferents, which synapse on inhibitory interneurons to inhibit agonist motoneurons (autogenic inhibition). Ia and II afferents come from spindles.'),
('c6960409-e6d1-55f4-9596-e32a60ce822b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What mechanism produces the alternating muscle contractions seen in clonus?', '["Muscle atrophy","Increased alpha motoneuron activity","Decreased inhibition","Alternating stretch reflex bursts","Tendon rupture"]'::jsonb, 'Alternating stretch reflex bursts', 'Clonus is a self-perpetuating oscillation produced by alternating stretch reflex bursts: each reflex contraction stretches the muscle again, re-triggering the stretch reflex in patients with enhanced reflex activity.'),
('4d2a807a-dda7-54ec-a299-543ac8a1adda'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Decerebrate rigidity involves:', '["Pons transection","Basal ganglia lesion","Cerebellar lesion","Midbrain transection","Spinal cord transection"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain transection', 'Decerebrate rigidity follows a midbrain transection between the superior and inferior colliculi, which disconnects the brain stem from higher centers while leaving vestibulospinal tracts intact, producing rigid extension of all limbs.'),
('a9d51d69-71a6-5cfa-bd60-e55ebc6f2aa6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Decorticate rigidity posture is:', '["Fetal position","Upper limb extended lower limb flexed","Upper limb flexed lower limb extended","All limbs flexed","All limbs extended"]'::jsonb, 'Upper limb flexed lower limb extended', 'Decorticate (flexor) posturing consists of flexion of the upper limbs and extension of the lower limbs, seen with lesions above the midbrain (cerebral cortex/internal capsule, e.g., in decortication).'),
('c4a0b09f-ee22-5139-8b57-188c0711dd5f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The gamma loop describes:', '["Reciprocal inhibition resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha only activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renshaw cell circuit mediated by specific receptor activation","Alpha-gamma coactivation that keeps the spindle firing","Gamma only activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-gamma coactivation that keeps the spindle firing', 'The gamma loop is the circuit by which alpha and gamma motoneurons are co-activated so that intrafusal fibers shorten with extra-fusal fibers, keeping the spindle taut and maintaining its sensitivity to stretch during contraction.'),
('c35db682-fac3-5a07-a846-4a035706444c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 50-year-old male presents with altered mental status. Electrolytes show K+ 5.8 mEq/L. Which condition is most consistent with these findings?', '["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia', 'Serum K+ 5.8 mEq/L is above the normal range (3.5-5.0), i.e. hyperkalemia. Elevated K+ depolarizes the resting membrane potential, impairing nerve and muscle excitability and leading to weakness and cardiac arrhythmias.'),
('ada7051a-2893-594f-ad89-08d58ee3a3c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Presynaptic inhibition is mediated by:', '["Acetylcholine","Glycine","Dopamine","Glutamate","GABA"]'::jsonb, 'GABA', 'Presynaptic inhibition is mediated by GABA (via GABA-B receptors on the presynaptic terminal), which reduces Ca2+ influx and thereby decreases neurotransmitter release. Glycine mediates postsynaptic inhibition in the spinal cord.'),
('835454b2-cab6-5c2e-a3d6-6d5f8c39820e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The sodium equilibrium potential is calculated by which formula?', '["E=RT/zF ln([Na+]o/[Na+]i)","E=RT/zF ln([Cl-]o/[Cl-]i)","E=RT/zF ln([Mg2+]o/[Mg2+]i)","E=RT/zF ln([K+]o/[K+]i)","E=RT/zF ln([Ca2+]o/[Ca2+]i)"]'::jsonb, 'E=RT/zF ln([Na+]o/[Na+]i)', 'The Nernst equation gives the equilibrium potential for Na+ using its extra/intracellular concentrations: E=61 log([Na+]o/[Na+]i) mV at 37 degrees C, about +60 to +70 mV. The same formula applied to other ions gives their own equilibrium potentials.'),
('9ba4d806-46cf-562e-bd5e-d1ce43a51217'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The equilibrium potential for Ca2+ is approximately:', '["0 mV","-90 mV","+60 mV","-70 mV","+120 mV"]'::jsonb, '+120 mV', 'Because extracellular Ca2+ concentration (2.4 mM) vastly exceeds intracellular free Ca2+ (10^-7 M), the Nernst potential for Ca2+ is strongly positive, approximately +120 mV. It differs from Na+ (+60 to +70 mV).'),
('e5855376-774f-50a8-95df-9e849c9fbb5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which is responsible for the after-hyperpolarization of the action potential?', '["Na+/K+ pump","Na+ channels","Cl- channels","Ca2+ channels","K+ channels remaining open"]'::jsonb, 'K+ channels remaining open', 'After the upstroke, slow-opening voltage-gated K+ channels remain open, letting K+ efflux continue beyond the resting potential, producing the after-hyperpolarization (undershoot). The Na+/K+ pump maintains gradients over the long term but is not responsible for the undershoot.'),
('ed6fa689-171e-5238-9908-a12dd75e6a8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 70-year-old male presents with numbness and tingling in the hands. Which statement about myelinated nerve conduction is correct?', '["Nodes of Ranvier have high Na+ channel density","Internodes are the main site of Na+ entry","Myelin is an excellent electrical conductor","Membrane capacitance is higher in myelinated segments","Conduction is slower in larger diameter fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Nodes of Ranvier have high Na+ channel density', 'In myelinated fibers, voltage-gated Na+ channels are concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier, so the action potential regenerates only at nodes and jumps between them (saltatory conduction), which is faster than continuous conduction. Myelin is an insulator that lowers capacitance.'),
('73d4caac-09ed-5558-81e1-b0387d640b42'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Electrotonic conduction refers to:', '["Active propagation","Passive spread of depolarization","Synaptic transmission","Saltatory conduction","Axonal transport"]'::jsonb, 'Passive spread of depolarization', 'Electrotonic (cable) conduction is the passive, decremental spread of local currents along the membrane without regenerating the signal. Active action potential propagation, by contrast, is non-decremental and uses voltage-gated channels.'),
('7d416b5f-e4b0-5359-9ff0-e4be2fac90d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The space constant (lambda) is the distance over which:', '["Capacitance halves","Potential decays to 1/e of its initial value","Potential decays to half its initial value","Channel density doubles","Current is maximal"]'::jsonb, 'Potential decays to 1/e of its initial value', 'The space constant (lambda) is the distance along the axon over which an electrotonic potential decays to 1/e (about 37%) of its initial magnitude. It equals the square root of the ratio of membrane to axoplasmic resistance.'),
('2e272ee3-fdf3-5ac7-974c-1c1a436c4c85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The time constant (tau) is the time required for:', '["Synaptic delay resulting in decreased functional activity","Membrane potential to reach 63% of its final value","Full depolarization mediated by specific receptor activation","Action potential peak without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Refractory period leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Membrane potential to reach 63% of its final value', 'The time constant (tau = R*C) is the time taken for the membrane potential to reach 63% (1 - 1/e) of its final value in response to a step current, because membrane capacitance charges exponentially.'),
('56efb3a1-5986-5385-a6d1-00c514cf4749'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Cable theory describes:', '["Receptor binding resulting in decreased functional activity","Neurotransmitter release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Synaptic plasticity mediated by specific receptor activation","Action potential generation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passive spread of currents along dendrites and axons"]'::jsonb, 'Passive spread of currents along dendrites and axons', 'Cable theory models the passive (electrotonic) conduction of local currents in dendrites and axons, defining space and time constants that govern how far and how fast graded potentials spread.'),
('a188f82e-b21f-50f9-9278-ffab4a1c6ed4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The Hodgkin cycle refers to:', '["Cross-bridge cycling resulting in decreased functional activity","Ca2+-induced Ca2+ release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reciprocal inhibition mediated by specific receptor activation","Na+ influx causing further depolarization and more Na+ channel opening","Feedback inhibition of K+ channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ influx causing further depolarization and more Na+ channel opening', 'The Hodgkin cycle is the positive feedback in which Na+ influx depolarizes the membrane, which opens more voltage-gated Na+ channels, producing the explosive upstroke of the action potential in an all-or-none manner.'),
('faec5c29-a661-5427-a178-7798b8e4873f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The overshoot of an action potential approaches:', '["ENa","EK","ECl","0 mV","ECa"]'::jsonb, 'ENa', 'During the peak of the action potential the membrane becomes highly permeable to Na+, so the membrane potential approaches the Na+ equilibrium potential (about +60 to +70 mV). The undershoot approaches EK.'),
('f6f43753-a34c-5722-ba7d-7f0859b35478'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A membrane potential change does not reach threshold for an action potential. How is this change described?', '["Subthreshold depolarization","After-depolarization","Suprathreshold depolarization","Action potential","Hyperpolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Subthreshold depolarization', 'A depolarization that fails to reach threshold is a subthreshold (local, graded) potential; it decays electronically and does not trigger an action potential. Suprathreshold depolarizations do reach threshold.'),
('caf1b9e6-f28e-5c63-b869-f808a505c948'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Anode break excitation occurs when:', '["Hyperpolarization alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Cathodal stimulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Strong depolarization mediated by specific receptor activation","Anodal current is applied without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anodal current is removed, causing rebound depolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Anodal current is removed, causing rebound depolarization', 'When anodal (hyperpolarizing) current is removed, the membrane potential rebounds and can overshoot into depolarization, eliciting an action potential. This ''anode break'' excitation reflects the removal of the hyperpolarizing influence.'),
('fa9f9e62-fafb-51f4-a6cf-d542d06c1704'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The motor end plate is located where?', '["Axon hillock resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle membrane under the nerve terminal","Cell body mediated by specific receptor activation","Dendritic spine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nodes of Ranvier leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle membrane under the nerve terminal', 'The motor end plate is the region of the muscle fiber membrane (sarcolemma) folded into junctional folds beneath the axon terminal, where ACh receptors are clustered for neuromuscular transmission.'),
('155a40df-6760-5e31-952e-6f33a09cc509'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 35-year-old male presents with cardiac arrhythmia. Electrolytes show K+ 3.0 mEq/L. What is the most likely basis of the arrhythmia?', '["ACh receptor abnormality","Altered K+ gradient across cardiac cell membrane","Ca2+ channel excess","Na+ channel block","Voltage sensor inactivation"]'::jsonb, 'Altered K+ gradient across cardiac cell membrane', 'Hypokalemia (K+ 3.0) reduces the K+ gradient across the cell membrane, altering resting potential and repolarization and predisposing to arrhythmias such as U waves and ectopic beats. It reflects potassium rather than ACh receptor abnormality.'),
('3cb7984b-6e72-519d-a9f7-fdca16fe19f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'At the neuromuscular junction, what postsynaptic change is required for efficient neuromuscular transmission?', '["ACh receptor clustering at the motor end plate","Nerve hypertrophy","ACh synthesis in the axon hillock","Myelination of the junction","Synaptic pruning"]'::jsonb, 'ACh receptor clustering at the motor end plate', 'Nicotinic ACh receptors must be clustered at high density on the postjunctional folds for a large end-plate potential to reliably reach threshold. Agrin and MuSK signaling drive this clustering.'),
('f51796f7-5d07-5d68-9bfd-4dce4f514028'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Where are nicotinic acetylcholine receptors synthesized and clustered at the neuromuscular junction?', '["In the nerve terminal resulting in decreased functional activity","In the muscle fiber, clustered at the junctional folds","In Schwann cells mediated by specific receptor activation","In the axons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the synaptic cleft matrix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the muscle fiber, clustered at the junctional folds', 'Nicotinic ACh receptors are synthesized by the muscle fiber itself and anchored at high density in the postjunctional folds of the motor end plate, where they bind ACh released from the nerve terminal.'),
('18c4cea0-3572-534e-b3e7-80ff24e380e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The MEPP (miniature EPP) amplitude is about:', '["0.1 mV","10 mV","20 mV","0.5-1 mV","5 mV"]'::jsonb, '0.5-1 mV', 'A miniature end-plate potential due to the spontaneous release of a single ACh vesicle is about 0.5-1 mV. A normal end-plate potential (about 30-70 mV) is built from the summation of 50-300 quanta.'),
('4ef5a275-f4d2-59a7-99c2-73c671786dec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which scientists first described the quantum nature of neurotransmitter release and miniature EPPs (MEPPs)?', '["Fatt and Katz","Hodgkin and Huxley","Cannon","Eccles","Sherrington"]'::jsonb, 'Fatt and Katz', 'Fatt and Katz (1952) described miniature end-plate potentials and formulated the quantum (vesicular) hypothesis of neurotransmitter release at the neuromuscular junction. Hodgkin and Huxley characterized the action potential.'),
('0aad02d9-fd69-5fde-b139-ffc0cb499b54'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The number of ACh molecules per vesicle is about:', '["1000","100","100000","50000","5000-10000"]'::jsonb, '5000-10000', 'Each synaptic vesicle contains roughly 5,000-10,000 acetylcholine molecules. A single vesicle spontaneously released produces a miniature EPP of about 0.5-1 mV.'),
('244f5b3f-b9cc-5d5d-ac2e-0fcc946bd7eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Curare blocks ACh receptors causing:', '["Decreased AChE","Increased receptor synthesis","Competitive (non-depolarizing) block","Depolarizing block","Increased ACh release"]'::jsonb, 'Competitive (non-depolarizing) block', 'Curare (tubocurarine) is a competitive, non-depolarizing antagonist at nicotinic ACh receptors: it binds the receptor but does not open the channel, blocking transmission without depolarizing the end plate; effects can be overcome by increasing ACh.'),
('c745c511-7193-5efe-89cf-903ae9850e0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Succinylcholine causes:', '["Decreased AChE resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased ACh release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Competitive block mediated by specific receptor activation","Depolarizing block producing prolonged end-plate depolarization","Increased ACh synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarizing block producing prolonged end-plate depolarization', 'Succinylcholine persistently activates nicotinic receptors at the end plate, producing a sustained depolarization (phase I depolarizing block) that renders the muscle inexcitable, causing flaccid paralysis before clinical relaxation.'),
('a6d30c97-875f-5b00-8d83-b367310c5b82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Neostigmine treats myasthenia gravis by:', '["Blocking AChR","Stimulating motor nerve","Inhibiting AChE","Increasing ACh synthesis","Releasing ACh"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting AChE', 'Neostigmine is an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that prolongs the life of ACh in the synaptic cleft, raising end-plate potential amplitude and improving neuromuscular transmission in myasthenia gravis.'),
('187a50e2-390c-5371-9774-33987834861f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Organophosphates cause:', '["Reversible AChE inhibition","Irreversible AChE inhibition","Increased ACh synthesis","Na+ channel blockade","AChR blockade"]'::jsonb, 'Irreversible AChE inhibition', 'Organophosphate insecticides irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase by phosphorylation of the active site, producing cholinergic excess (salivation, bronchospasm, paralysis). Atropine and pralidoxime are used for treatment.'),
('b14594ff-807c-5ae3-af79-9b05241dee37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The paralysis caused by botulinum toxin lasts for:', '["Hours","Seconds","Weeks to months","Minutes","Days"]'::jsonb, 'Weeks to months', 'Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins, blocking ACh vesicle release at the terminal. Recovery requires sprouting of new nerve terminals, so paralysis persists for weeks to months.'),
('696fedfb-3f7e-5696-8920-1375265abe98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Myotonia is characterized by:', '["Paralysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Spasticity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Delayed relaxation after contraction","Muscle weakness without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rapid fatigue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Delayed relaxation after contraction', 'Myotonia is the inability of muscle to relax promptly after contraction, caused by repetitive membrane depolarization (often from ion channel defects); it is the hallmark of myotonic disorders and cold-induced myotonia.'),
('7a0a6e5e-ec72-5341-8048-49f364bfd79a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Myotonic dystrophy involves:', '["Sensory loss","CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene","Defect limited to CNS","Autonomic dysfunction","Muscle atrophy only"]'::jsonb, 'CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene', 'Myotonic dystrophy type 1 is caused by expansion of a CTG trinucleotide repeat in the DMPK gene on chromosome 19, a multisystem disorder with myotonia, weakness, cataracts, and cardiac conduction defects.'),
('59f6c399-2584-5f15-ae04-7cc43664ec73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The total number of muscle fibers in an adult is largely determined by:', '["Age","Exercise","Genetics (heredity)","Hormones","Nutrition"]'::jsonb, 'Genetics (heredity)', 'The number of muscle fibers is largely fixed before and around birth and is determined mainly by genetics. Exercise and training cause hypertrophy (fiber enlargement) rather than an increase in fiber number.'),
('7d28b298-cc83-5118-ac03-cfbcdb267f95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Hypertrophy of muscle involves:', '["Fibrosis","Degeneration","Increase in fiber size (cross-sectional area)","Increase in number of fibers","Replacement by connective tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Increase in fiber size (cross-sectional area)', 'Muscle hypertrophy is an increase in the cross-sectional size of individual fibers due to increased myofibrils and protein content. An increase in the number of fibers is hyperplasia, not hypertrophy.'),
('24013525-ea2e-5e2f-816e-a790296cd616'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Hyperplasia of muscle fibers (increase in fiber number) occurs in:', '["Birds","Rodents","Amphibians","Reptiles","Humans"]'::jsonb, 'Rodents', 'In rodents, exercise and stretch can increase the number of muscle fibers (hyperplasia) in addition to hypertrophy. In humans, muscle growth is almost entirely hypertrophy without significant new fiber formation.'),
('41d04a64-7655-56e7-a9f6-e9fb06fc8252'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The most common form of muscular dystrophy is:', '["Becker muscular dystrophy","Facioscapulohumeral dystrophy","Myotonic dystrophy","Duchenne muscular dystrophy","Limb-girdle dystrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy', 'Duchenne muscular dystrophy, caused by absence of dystrophin, is the most common and most severe form of muscular dystrophy, with early childhood onset and X-linked inheritance.'),
('86948a59-fbea-5025-a49b-8425f102fdbc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Becker muscular dystrophy is milder than Duchenne because:', '["Increased utrophin","Complete absence of dystrophin","Enhanced regeneration","Normal dystrophin levels","It produces a partially functional dystrophin"]'::jsonb, 'It produces a partially functional dystrophin', 'In Becker muscular dystrophy, a mutation allows production of a partially functional (reduced amount or size) dystrophin, so the clinical course is milder and later in onset than in Duchenne, where dystrophin is absent.'),
('c09b9c74-5454-5ad0-8923-106c0e244cc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy involves:', '["Calpain deficiency","Dystrophin deficiency","Emerin deficiency","Caveolin deficiency","Dysferlin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Emerin deficiency', 'Emery-Dreifuss muscular dystrophy is due to deficiency of emerin (or lamin A/C), a nuclear membrane protein. It presents with early contractures, humeroperoneal weakness, and cardiac conduction defects.'),
('cbd7976e-0f24-5b96-9255-f46d44b6d4b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 50-year-old female presents with proximal muscle weakness. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Muscular dystrophy","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Myasthenia gravis","Periodic paralysis","Spinal muscular atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Muscular dystrophy', 'Proximal muscle weakness with likely myopathic cause in an adult female most commonly suggests a muscular dystrophy or inflammatory myopathy; ALS and SMA affect both upper and lower motor neurons, while myasthenia gravis is fatigable. Among the options, muscular dystrophy best fits isolated proximal weakness.'),
('17d92cca-e8d3-51a8-abb6-84faf97ce48a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Hypokalemic periodic paralysis involves:', '["AChR mutation","K+ channel mutation","Cl- channel mutation","Ca2+ channel mutation (CACNA1S)","Na+ channel mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ channel mutation (CACNA1S)', 'Hypokalemic periodic paralysis is most often caused by a mutation of the CACNA1S gene encoding the L-type Ca2+ channel (DHP receptor) of skeletal muscle. Attacks are triggered by low serum K+ and cause flaccid paralysis.'),
('ca407e0d-d098-5909-b022-b7f67e8cd702'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Andersen-Tawil syndrome involves:', '["Ca2+ channel mutation","Cl- channel mutation","K+ channel mutation (KCNJ2)","Na+ channel mutation","AChR mutation"]'::jsonb, 'K+ channel mutation (KCNJ2)', 'Andersen-Tawil syndrome is caused by mutations of KCNJ2, which encodes the inward rectifier K+ channel Kir2.1, producing periodic paralysis, cardiac arrhythmias (long QT), and dysmorphic features.'),
('42387f08-7262-5f3f-bfac-51bd23d7fe4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Central core disease is associated with:', '["Muscular dystrophy","Myotonia","Periodic paralysis","Malignant hyperthermia","Myasthenia gravis"]'::jsonb, 'Malignant hyperthermia', 'Central core disease is caused by mutations of the ryanodine receptor (RYR1) and is closely associated with susceptibility to malignant hyperthermia, which is triggered by volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine.'),
('d22cca54-84aa-5048-9681-dcb6596e19a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The Cori cycle involves:', '["Lactate from muscle converted to glucose in the liver","Glycogen synthesis in muscle","Ketone body production in liver","Glucose to lactate in liver","Protein catabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate from muscle converted to glucose in the liver', 'In the Cori cycle, lactate produced by glycolysis in muscle is transported to the liver and reconverted to glucose by gluconeogenesis, then returned to muscle. This recycles the carbon skeleton but requires net ATP.'),
('a292107b-307d-5a42-8aa3-30585dcbe2eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The glucose-alanine cycle shuttles:', '["Both A and B, i.e. nitrogen from muscle and glucose from liver","Ketones from liver to brain","Nitrogen from muscle to liver in the form of alanine","Lactate from muscle to kidney","Carbon from muscle to liver for gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrogen from muscle to liver in the form of alanine', 'The glucose-alanine cycle transports amino groups from muscle to the liver in the form of alanine; the liver deaminates alanine to produce urea and converts the carbon skeleton to glucose, which returns to muscle. The Cori cycle is analogous but carries lactate.'),
('bfe21412-3a12-5962-ada0-7dc64883e30c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'EMG records:', '["Electrical activity of muscle","Temperature","Blood flow","Pain threshold","Muscle tension"]'::jsonb, 'Electrical activity of muscle', 'Electromyography (EMG) records the electrical activity (action potentials) of skeletal muscle, either spontaneous or during voluntary contraction, helping distinguish myopathic from neurogenic disorders.'),
('484e35b6-e6f6-5c6e-9a29-3208f8ec2113'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Nerve conduction studies measure:', '["Brain activity","Muscle strength","Nerve conduction velocity","Synaptic delay","Blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve conduction velocity', 'Nerve conduction studies measure the velocity and amplitude of action potentials along motor and sensory nerves by stimulating at one point and recording at another; slowed velocity indicates demyelination.'),
('b3c67d87-376b-53bc-943d-09ca577983c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'F-wave studies assess:', '["Sensory nerve","Distal nerve segment","Muscle fiber","Proximal nerve segment and root","Autonomic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal nerve segment and root', 'F-waves are late responses produced by antidromic activation of motor neurons, so they evaluate conduction along proximal segments and nerve roots that routine distal conduction studies cannot reach.'),
('c2ee68ff-481b-5af7-ac75-c9f5032106c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The H-reflex is the electrophysiological correlate of:', '["Patellar reflex","Biceps reflex","Achilles reflex","Triceps reflex","Ankle jerk"]'::jsonb, 'Ankle jerk', 'The H-reflex is the electrical equivalent of the monosynaptic stretch reflex, best recorded by stimulating the tibial nerve; it corresponds to the ankle (Achilles) jerk, whereas the F-wave is a motor response.'),
('ed48483e-0a50-578a-baaa-499ddd570593'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Repetitive nerve stimulation shows decrement in:', '["Muscular dystrophy","Myasthenia gravis","Guillain-Barre syndrome","ALS","Lambert-Eaton syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Myasthenia gravis', 'In myasthenia gravis, repetitive nerve stimulation progressively depletes acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, producing a decremental (falling) motor response. Lambert-Eaton syndrome instead shows an incremental response.'),
('0626a689-4235-5035-82a0-b8a76730bb96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'On single-fiber electromyography, what term describes beat-to-beat variability in neuromuscular transmission?', '["Fiber type","Jitter","Synaptic delay","Conduction velocity","Fiber diameter"]'::jsonb, 'Jitter', 'Jitter is the variation in the time interval between action potentials from two muscle fibers of the same motor unit measured on single-fiber EMG. Increased jitter reflects impaired neuromuscular transmission, as in myasthenia gravis.'),
('e0f0f6de-9db1-5ec6-947d-c729d4e8d5db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Increased jitter is seen in:', '["Myotonia","Myasthenia gravis","Muscular dystrophy","Denervation only","Normal muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Myasthenia gravis', 'Increased jitter is a hallmark of defective neuromuscular transmission, most classically myasthenia gravis, where endplate acetylcholine release is variable. Normal muscle shows minimal jitter.'),
('54aaceae-aa5f-5431-8a22-7282b61f00b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which type of electrical signal propagates unidirectionally along axons in an all-or-none fashion?', '["MEPPs","Graded potentials","Synaptic potentials","Action potentials","Receptor potentials"]'::jsonb, 'Action potentials', 'Action potentials are all-or-none, self-regenerating depolarizations that propagate without decrement along axons. Graded, synaptic, and receptor potentials spread passively and are decremental.'),
('1faf00ae-3032-523a-b37e-ed360871ca02'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Graded potentials are characterized by:', '["Saltatory conduction","All-or-none","Decremental conduction","Non-decremental","Fixed amplitude"]'::jsonb, 'Decremental conduction', 'Graded potentials have an amplitude proportional to stimulus strength and spread passively with decrement over a short distance. Their amplitude is graded, not fixed, and they can summate.'),
('6d5c62c2-5185-5395-8df1-9268ea5335a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which of the following generates a graded potential?', '["Sensory receptor","Axon hillock","Schwann cell","Node of Ranvier","Voltage-gated Na+ channel"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory receptor', 'Stimulation of a sensory receptor produces a graded receptor potential, whose amplitude varies with stimulus strength. The axon hillock, all-or-none nodal firing, and voltage-gated Na+ channel activation generate action potentials.'),
('ec6f545e-d083-5280-96a3-be97cf3ffc40'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Pacinian corpuscles detect:', '["Stretch","Temperature","Vibration and pressure","Pain","Light touch"]'::jsonb, 'Vibration and pressure', 'Pacinian corpuscles are rapidly adapting (phasic) mechanoreceptors in deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue that signal vibration and deep pressure. They respond transiently to a maintained stimulus.'),
('01b32a64-4856-56a8-b982-0cb4b2a23202'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Free nerve endings detect:', '["Pain and temperature","Pressure","Stretch","Touch","Vibration"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature', 'Free nerve endings in the skin are nociceptors and thermoreceptors that mediate pain and temperature sensation. They are unmyelinated (C) or thinly myelinated (A-delta) endings.'),
('2b0dd158-fc88-5768-a967-3a737d0ea753'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The adequate stimulus for a receptor is:', '["The stimulus to which it is most sensitive","Subthreshold stimulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Threshold stimulus mediated by specific receptor activation","Any stimulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximal stimulus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The stimulus to which it is most sensitive', 'The adequate stimulus is the modality to which a receptor has the lowest threshold and is most selectively sensitive, such as light for photoreceptors or vibration for Pacinian corpuscles.'),
('70f1f300-2a3e-5a4a-9220-8dbeb2322533'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Adaptation in sensory receptors refers to:', '["Decreased response to sustained stimulus","Increased sensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Receptor damage mediated by specific receptor activation","Synaptic fatigue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exhaustion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased response to sustained stimulus', 'Adaptation is the decline in the frequency of receptor potentials and action potentials during a constant, maintained stimulus. Tonic receptors adapt slowly; phasic receptors adapt rapidly.'),
('fb6ad7f7-7b9e-5739-bea5-e3b7f4cff02b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Phasic receptors are exemplified by:', '["Ruffini endings","Pacinian corpuscles","Merkel discs","Muscle spindles","Golgi tendon organ"]'::jsonb, 'Pacinian corpuscles', 'Pacinian corpuscles are rapidly adapting (phasic) receptors that respond best to the onset and offset of a stimulus. Merkel discs, muscle spindles, Ruffini endings, and the Golgi tendon organ are tonic (slowly adapting).'),
('8d07cc32-cfb1-53be-b5ac-5192e24668d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Tonic receptors include:', '["Meissner corpuscles resulting in decreased functional activity","Merkel discs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Free nerve endings for pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Pacinian corpuscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both Merkel discs and free nerve endings for pain"]'::jsonb, 'Both Merkel discs and free nerve endings for pain', 'Merkel discs and pain-conducting free nerve endings are slowly adapting (tonic) receptors that keep firing during a sustained stimulus. Meissner corpuscles and Pacinian corpuscles are phasic.'),
('19013da0-c808-5e3f-b061-99fc74c6124d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'A 70-year-old female presents with cardiac arrhythmia. Electrolytes show Na+ 120 mEq/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Hyponatremia","Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia', 'A serum sodium of 120 mEq/L represents severe hyponatremia, which alters membrane excitability and can cause cardiac arrhythmias and altered mental status. The low sodium level itself establishes the diagnosis.'),
('b6ef554d-7f07-5549-b1d0-57a1e6b41752'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'According to the Weber-Fechner law, what is the relationship between stimulus intensity and perceived sensation?', '["Stimulus frequency to amplitude","Receptor potential to action potentials","Stimulus intensity to perceived intensity logarithmically","Threshold to duration","Conduction to diameter"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulus intensity to perceived intensity logarithmically', 'The Weber-Fechner law states that the magnitude of perceived sensation increases as the logarithm of stimulus intensity. A constant fractional change in stimulus produces a just-noticeable difference in sensation.'),
('abfec709-25b6-5e92-a434-787fa2beece0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The smallest two-point discrimination distance is on:', '["Thigh (approx 40 mm)","Palm (approx 10 mm)","Back (approx 40 mm)","Forearm (approx 40 mm)","Fingertips (approx 2 mm)"]'::jsonb, 'Fingertips (approx 2 mm)', 'Two-point discrimination threshold is smallest where mechanoreceptor innervation density is highest, i.e., the fingertips at about 2 mm. It is much larger on the back and thigh.'),
('85c733e1-14a1-5448-baea-1d1146aafa5f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Graphesthesia refers to:', '["Joint position sense","Temperature sense","Pain sensation","Vibration sense","Ability to recognize writing on skin"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to recognize writing on skin', 'Graphesthesia is the ability to identify numbers or letters traced on the skin, a dorsal column (fine discriminative touch) cortical function. Its loss occurs with dorsal column-medial lemniscus lesions.'),
('b486accf-0c2c-5b83-b2d2-f38094db85b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Proprioception is mediated by:', '["Spinotectal tract","Spinoreticular tract","Dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway","Spinocerebellar tract","Spinothalamic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway', 'Conscious proprioception, along with fine touch and vibration, travels in the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway to the contralateral sensory cortex. The spinocerebellar tracts carry unconscious proprioception to the cerebellum.'),
('9371e0d8-52ce-5f68-bb2f-2d090454b644'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'First-order neurons of the dorsal column pathway are located in:', '["Spinal cord","Ventral horn","Dorsal root ganglion","Dorsal horn","Medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal root ganglion', 'The first-order neurons of the dorsal column pathway are the large-diameter afferent cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglion. Their central processes ascend in the gracile and cuneate fasciculi to synapse in the medulla.'),
('9e287910-a1b6-5467-b8e7-670a5016a094'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Second-order neurons of the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway are located in:', '["Ventral posterolateral nucleus","Nucleus gracilis and cuneatus","Sensory cortex","Dorsal root ganglion","Spinal cord dorsal horn"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus gracilis and cuneatus', 'In the lower medulla, the first-order fibers synapse on the second-order neurons of the nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus, which then decussate to form the medial lemniscus. The third-order neurons lie in the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus.'),
('2dacca65-01e2-515c-992a-269b50fc5985'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Third-order neurons of the dorsal column pathway are in:', '["Medulla","Internal capsule","VPL nucleus of thalamus","Sensory cortex","Spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'VPL nucleus of thalamus', 'Third-order neurons of the dorsal column pathway are located in the ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus of the thalamus, projecting via the internal capsule to the somatosensory cortex.'),
('8188c876-52dd-5593-9947-50ebd7997e16'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Second-order neurons of the spinothalamic tract cross in:', '["Pons resulting in decreased functional activity","Medulla accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anterior commissure of spinal cord"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior commissure of spinal cord', 'Second-order spinothalamic neurons arising in the dorsal horn decussate in the anterior (ventral) white commissure of the spinal cord within one or two segments of their origin. There is no crossing at the level of the medulla, pons, or midbrain.'),
('32c3ec9f-c507-5631-8770-da256c082627'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which sensory modalities are transmitted via the spinothalamic tract?', '["Vibration","Pressure","Crude touch","Pain and temperature","Proprioception"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature', 'The lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature, while the anterior spinothalamic tract carries crude touch and pressure. Vibration and proprioception travel in the dorsal columns.'),
('bd18da81-cb8b-5178-814e-0e88c7a36bec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Lissauer''s tract carries:', '["Pain and temperature fibers entering spinal cord","Autonomic fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinocerebellar fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Corticospinal fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal column fibers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature fibers entering spinal cord', 'The dorsolateral (Lissauer''s) tract carries small-diameter A-delta and C pain and temperature fibers that ascend one to two segments before synapsing in the dorsal horn.'),
('d7ae8471-97c1-568a-8d82-29d0ff2244b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'On the somatosensory homunculus, the largest cortical representation is for which body parts?', '["Hands and face","Trunk","Lower limbs","Back","Equal for all body parts"]'::jsonb, 'Hands and face', 'The area of somatosensory cortex devoted to a body part is proportional to its mechanoreceptor innervation density. The hands, lips, and face occupy the largest areas of the sensory homunculus.'),
('a6e29c79-b3ea-5ee8-8fa2-b3a820f7b86a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which type of pain results from damage to the somatosensory nervous system itself?', '["Neuropathic pain","Inflammatory pain","Nociceptive pain","Referred pain","Acute pain"]'::jsonb, 'Neuropathic pain', 'Neuropathic pain arises from a lesion or disease of the somatosensory nervous system, typically described as burning, shooting, or electric. Nociceptive pain is produced by noxious stimulation of intact pain receptors.'),
('cc1b4e6e-ed41-56b8-8c16-115c7c28433a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Referred pain is explained by:', '["Labeled line","Sensory homunculus","Lateral inhibition","Adaptation","Convergence theory"]'::jsonb, 'Convergence theory', 'Referred pain is best explained by the convergence of visceral and somatic nociceptive afferents onto the same dorsal horn neurons, so the brain localizes the pain to the dermatome of somatic input, as in cardiac pain referred to the left arm.'),
('40ad02b5-3496-57bb-935f-fbbe6fba377e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which region of the spinal cord dorsal horn (lamina II) is a key site for pain transmission and modulation?', '["Corticospinal tract","Substantia gelatinosa","Lemniscal pathway","Reticular formation","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia gelatinosa', 'The substantia gelatinosa (lamina II of the dorsal horn) receives nociceptive input and contains inhibitory interneurons targeted by descending pain-modulating pathways. It is a central relay for pain processing.'),
('526df21f-4569-52c9-bf4c-2bb8ebca3559'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Enkephalins and endorphins are:', '["Endogenous opioids","Excitatory neurotransmitters","Pain mediators","Neurotrophins","Inflammatory cytokines"]'::jsonb, 'Endogenous opioids', 'Enkephalins and endorphins are endogenous opioid peptides that inhibit pain transmission by acting on mu, delta, and kappa opioid receptors in the brain and spinal cord.'),
('2a307698-24aa-5a53-a870-518397ea7708'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Peripheral sensitization of pain involves:', '["GABA resulting in decreased functional activity","Glycine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prostaglandins and bradykinin","Dopamine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Opioids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prostaglandins and bradykinin', 'Inflammation releases prostaglandins and bradykinin, which sensitize nociceptors and lower their activation threshold, producing peripheral sensitization and hyperalgesia. NSAIDs relieve pain partly by blocking prostaglandin synthesis.'),
('08aa627c-2459-589a-bff1-3e07854c7f8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Central sensitization in chronic pain primarily involves increased excitability of which structure?', '["Spinal cord dorsal horn","Cortex","Peripheral nerve","Thalamus","Amygdala"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord dorsal horn', 'Central sensitization is an enhanced responsiveness of dorsal horn nociceptive neurons to afferent input, amplifying pain signaling and contributing to chronic pain states. Prolonged nociceptor input triggers this spinal amplification.'),
('e738d1fe-9f81-50c9-ba0e-f0391b1ea70e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The theory that activity in large-diameter (non-nociceptive) fibers can close a ''gate'' and reduce pain transmission is called:', '["Opioid release","Placebo","Mental distraction","Direct nerve block","Gate control theory"]'::jsonb, 'Gate control theory', 'The gate control theory proposes that activity in large myelinated (A-beta) fibers activates inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn that block transmission of pain signals from small nociceptive fibers. Rubbing or tapping a painful area relieves pain through this mechanism.'),
('775a4c5f-7cd0-545c-bb6a-6150af4d2716'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Acupuncture analgesia involves:', '["Neural pathway only","Peripheral block","Inhibition at cortex","Endogenous opioid release","Placebo"]'::jsonb, 'Endogenous opioid release', 'Acupuncture analgesia is largely mediated by the release of endogenous opioids such as beta-endorphin and enkephalins, and it is partially blocked by the opioid antagonist naloxone.'),
('f1e13127-30bf-59cd-b9f7-3b8341f01bbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Hyperalgesia refers to:', '["Abnormal pain","Decreased pain sensitivity","Spontaneous pain","Loss of pain","Increased pain sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pain sensitivity', 'Hyperalgesia is an exaggerated pain response to a normally painful stimulus, resulting from sensitization of nociceptors or central pain pathways. It contrasts with analgesia (reduced pain) and allodynia (pain from a non-painful stimulus).'),
('ca36d475-bdd7-58e6-987a-004599b9d9e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What is the term for pain produced by a stimulus that does not normally provoke pain?', '["Spontaneous pain","Allodynia","Hyperalgesia","Throbbing pain","Nociceptive pain"]'::jsonb, 'Allodynia', 'Allodynia is pain evoked by a normally non-painful stimulus, such as light touch or brushing the skin in neuropathic pain states. Hyperalgesia is an increased response to a stimulus that is normally painful.'),
('b073949e-249c-5255-b21b-a3382555f785'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Neuropathic pain is typically described as:', '["Dull resulting in decreased functional activity","Burning/shooting/electric","Stabbing mediated by specific receptor activation","Throbbing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sharp leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Burning/shooting/electric', 'Neuropathic pain has a characteristic burning, shooting, or electric quality and is often associated with allodynia and paresthesias. It results from injury or disease of the somatosensory nervous system.'),
('f3524360-8ad0-5854-9064-fb30ae43a0d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Diabetic neuropathy is a common cause of:', '["Inflammatory pain","Ischemic pain","Referred pain","Nociceptive pain","Neuropathic pain"]'::jsonb, 'Neuropathic pain', 'Diabetic peripheral neuropathy damages sensory nerve fibers and is one of the most common causes of chronic neuropathic pain, typically felt as burning or shooting pain in the limbs.'),
('bbbedcae-1f68-5923-9dc9-16ff4c9db859'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The specificity theory of pain states:', '["Pain is a specific modality with dedicated receptors and pathways","Pain is due to pattern of firing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain is emotional mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain is cortical only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pain is learned leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain is a specific modality with dedicated receptors and pathways', 'The specificity theory holds that pain is a distinct sensory modality served by dedicated nociceptors and dedicated ascending pathways, analogous to hearing or vision. The pattern theory instead links pain to the pattern of neuronal firing.'),
('289a38d4-7321-5e4e-926f-500752c64ea1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which statement is consistent with the pattern theory of pain?', '["Pain results from intense stimulation of any receptor type","Pain is transmitted by dedicated nociceptors and pathways","Pain is produced only in the cortex","Pain is learned behavior","Specificity of receptors determines sensation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain results from intense stimulation of any receptor type', 'The pattern theory proposes that pain is not a separate modality but arises when any receptor is stimulated intensely enough to produce a particular pattern of impulses. It contrasts with the specificity theory, which holds that dedicated nociceptors and pathways encode pain.'),
('ae8c0add-5577-58d7-ae79-7672d3628a80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which midbrain structure is the primary relay that activates the descending pain-inhibitory pathway (nucleus raphe magnus and locus coeruleus)?', '["Raphe magnus nucleus","Sensory cortex","Periaqueductal gray","Locus coeruleus","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Periaqueductal gray', 'The periaqueductal gray (PAG) is the midbrain relay of the endogenous analgesic system. It activates serotoninergic neurons of the nucleus raphe magnus and noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus, which inhibit nociceptive transmission in the dorsal horn.'),
('76d944cd-d2a9-5998-b4aa-13c9594ce3f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The nucleus raphe magnus sends serotonergic fibers to:', '["Basal ganglia","Spinal cord dorsal horn","Cerebellum","Thalamus","Cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord dorsal horn', 'The nucleus raphe magnus projects serotonergic fibers down the dorsolateral funiculus to the spinal cord dorsal horn, where they inhibit pain transmission. Electrical stimulation of the raphe magnus or PAG produces profound analgesia.'),
('77d73cc5-392e-598e-a39e-e227e101ff87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The analgesic action of tricyclic antidepressants and SNRIs in neuropathic pain is primarily mediated by:', '["Opioid receptor agonism","Blocking serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake","NMDA blockade","Blocking Na+ channels","GABA potentiation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking serotonin/norepinephrine reuptake', 'Tricyclic antidepressants and serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs) relieve neuropathic pain by blocking reuptake of serotonin and norepinephrine, thereby strengthening the descending inhibitory pathway to the dorsal horn.'),
('907b97c1-48bc-5794-a3a1-d5911e87f7b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Gabapentin acts on:', '["GABA receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels (alpha-2-delta subunit)","Na+ channels mediated by specific receptor activation","Opioid receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NMDA receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Voltage-gated Ca2+ channels (alpha-2-delta subunit)', 'Gabapentin binds the alpha-2-delta subunit of voltage-gated calcium channels, reducing calcium influx and the release of excitatory neurotransmitters, which underlies its efficacy in neuropathic pain and epilepsy.'),
('1c65bcd8-a2c6-5e31-bef0-bfff3bbe7af8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Capsaicin cream depletes:', '["Norepinephrine","Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Serotonin","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Substance P', 'Capsaicin activates TRPV1 on nociceptive C fibers and, with repeated application, depletes the neuropeptide substance P, reducing pain transmission. This is the basis for topical capsaicin analgesia.'),
('a2dce624-f4b1-5619-abe9-55fb62e07ef7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'TRPV1 receptors are activated by:', '["Cold and menthol","Mechanical stretch","Heat and capsaicin","Light touch","Acid only"]'::jsonb, 'Heat and capsaicin', 'TRPV1 is a nonselective cation channel on nociceptors activated by noxious heat (>43°C), capsaicin, and protons (acid), mediating thermal pain and the burning of chili peppers. Cold and menthol activate TRPM8.'),
('23c0f6dd-0f35-51b0-917f-b01548af97b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'After the hands, which body region has the largest representation on the somatosensory cortex?', '["Limbs","Trunk","Body","Viscera","Face"]'::jsonb, 'Face', 'Cortical representation on the sensory homunculus is proportional to innervation density. The lips, tongue, and face, together with the hands, occupy the largest areas of the somatosensory cortex.'),
('966f8111-00ea-5935-a2e5-e41574c0eafb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which cranial nerve carries proprioceptive sensation from the face and jaw muscles?', '["Trigeminal (CN V)","Facial (CN VII)","Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)","Vagus (CN X)","Vestibulocochlear (CN VIII)"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal (CN V)', 'Proprioception from the face and jaw muscles travels in the trigeminal nerve to the mesencephalic nucleus of CN V, the only site where primary sensory cell bodies lie within the central nervous system.'),
('a17b2e12-6580-557a-bc67-fc6777abe519'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The spinal trigeminal nucleus receives:', '["Touch from face","Proprioception","Pressure","Vibration","Pain and temperature from face"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature from face', 'The spinal trigeminal nucleus receives pain and temperature input from the face through fibers descending in the spinal trigeminal tract. It is the analogue of the spinothalamic system for trigeminal territory.'),
('36bdeaa5-e147-5f62-8e7f-0c247ba74ac8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The nucleus tractus solitarius receives:', '["Taste resulting in decreased functional activity","Touch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Visceral sensory input without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste and visceral sensory input"]'::jsonb, 'Taste and visceral sensory input', 'The nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) receives taste from cranial nerves VII, IX, and X, as well as visceral afferents from the cardiovascular, respiratory, and gastrointestinal systems. Its rostral part subserves taste and its caudal part visceral sensation.'),
('f40a0665-fca6-5941-9a5d-7912c03688d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which of the following cranial nerves carries taste sensation?', '["Facial (VII) resulting in decreased functional activity","Trigeminal (V) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glossopharyngeal (IX) mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus (X) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial (VII), glossopharyngeal (IX), and vagus (X)"]'::jsonb, 'Facial (VII), glossopharyngeal (IX), and vagus (X)', 'Taste is carried by the chorda tympani of the facial nerve (anterior two-thirds of tongue), the glossopharyngeal nerve (posterior one-third of tongue), and the vagus nerve (epiglottis). The trigeminal nerve carries no taste.'),
('8a73946b-224b-52a1-9dbf-8aa1e525d270'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The five primary taste modalities are:', '["Sweet sour salty bitter metallic","Sweet sour salty bitter umami","Sweet sour salty bitter fatty","Sweet sour salty bitter spicy","Sweet sour salty bitter astringent"]'::jsonb, 'Sweet sour salty bitter umami', 'The five basic taste modalities are sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami (savory, elicited by glutamate/MSG). Spicy is a pain (trigeminal) sensation, not a taste modality.'),
('1880cbc4-1ea8-545f-890e-d7eaff139138'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Taste buds are found in which of the following?', '["Circumvallate papillae resulting in decreased functional activity","Fungiform papillae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Foliate papillae mediated by specific receptor activation","Von Ebner glands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Circumvallate, fungiform, and foliate papillae"]'::jsonb, 'Circumvallate, fungiform, and foliate papillae', 'Taste buds are distributed in the circumvallate, fungiform, and foliate papillae of the tongue as well as the palate and epiglottis. Von Ebner glands are serous salivary glands that drain into the circumvallate grooves, not taste buds.'),
('53855ae7-97f5-51f9-89cb-f00d6c044e99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Each taste bud contains approximately how many taste receptor cells?', '["5-10","10-20","50-150","500-1000","200-300"]'::jsonb, '50-150', 'Each taste bud contains roughly 50-150 taste receptor cells that are continuously replaced, with an average lifespan of about 10 days. Gustatory hairs project through the taste pore to sample the oral environment.'),
('864f6276-a3b1-54c2-8371-0a10bbd2a445'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Taste transduction involves:', '["GPCRs for sweet bitter umami","Only GPCRs","Ion channels for salty sour","Only ion channels","Both GPCRs and ion channels"]'::jsonb, 'Both GPCRs and ion channels', 'Taste transduction uses two classes of receptors: G-protein-coupled receptors (T1R and T2R) for sweet, bitter, and umami, and ion channels (e.g., ENaC for salty, acid-sensing channels for sour). A single transduction mechanism cannot account for all five modalities.'),
('7d3527fd-fa58-5d9f-9bc8-a55b8c192619'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which taste modalities are detected by G-protein coupled receptors (T1R and T2R families) on taste buds?', '["Sweet bitter and umami taste","Salt taste","Taste cell development","Salivation","Sour taste"]'::jsonb, 'Sweet bitter and umami taste', 'Sweet and umami are detected by T1R receptors (T1R2/T1R3 for sweet, T1R1/T1R3 for umami) and bitter by T2R receptors on type II taste cells. Salt and sour are detected by ion channels.'),
('c283a83d-92a8-5753-b248-7e1eaa88cd83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve carries taste from:', '["Epiglottis","Soft palate","Anterior 2/3 of tongue","Posterior 1/3 of tongue","Pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 of tongue', 'The chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (CN VII), carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. The glossopharyngeal nerve serves the posterior one-third, and the vagus serves the epiglottis.'),
('c29dd6cc-58d4-5a21-b33d-2e9cdac7c2e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve carries taste from:', '["Anterior 2/3","Larynx","Soft palate","Posterior 1/3","Epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior 1/3', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) carries taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue and the circumvallate papillae. The anterior two-thirds are served by the chorda tympani of the facial nerve.'),
('dd2ea0e7-37eb-5b87-bdb5-05ad1df7a4be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The primary gustatory cortex is located in:', '["Parietal lobe","Insula and frontal operculum","Cingulate gyrus","Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Insula and frontal operculum', 'The primary gustatory cortex lies in the anterior insula and the frontal operculum, receiving taste fibers from the ventroposteromedial nucleus of the thalamus. The adjacent parietal cortex integrates oral somatosensation.'),
('84df20b8-81d5-5ca3-b30c-c80f33ca0c42'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The olfactory epithelium is located in:', '["Superior nasal concha and septum","Mouth accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oropharynx mediated by specific receptor activation","Nasal vestibule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sinuses leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Superior nasal concha and septum', 'The olfactory epithelium lies in the roof of the nasal cavity, covering the superior nasal concha and the adjacent nasal septum. Its bipolar olfactory receptor neurons send axons through the cribriform plate to the olfactory bulb.'),
('02930d94-2dc6-5e68-9237-2b1cb4f62379'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'In which of the following sensory organs are bipolar neurons the primary receptor cells?', '["Dorsal root ganglion","Retina (photoreceptors)","Dorsal horn of spinal cord","Cerebellar cortex","Sympathetic ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Retina (photoreceptors)', 'Retinal photoreceptors are modified bipolar neurons, as are the olfactory receptor cells and the sensory neurons of the vestibular and cochlear ganglia. Dorsal root ganglia contain pseudounipolar neurons.'),
('efdafa19-c09d-5d67-819b-c6f161e04da7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Light adaptation in retinal photoreceptors is mediated primarily by which mechanism?', '["Inhibitory circuits","Ca2+-calmodulin feedback on CNG channels","Synaptic fatigue","Enzymatic degradation","Receptor desensitization"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+-calmodulin feedback on CNG channels', 'During light exposure, reduced Ca2+ entry lowers Ca2+-calmodulin inhibition of guanylyl cyclase, restoring cGMP and sensitivity. Ca2+-calmodulin also directly modulates cGMP-gated (CNG) channels, and falling Ca2+ contributes to the faster light response that characterizes light adaptation.'),
('c05f751e-13a1-5999-a838-4e02e49f65a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What is the medical term for complete loss of the sense of smell?', '["Hallucination of smell","Anosmia","Enhanced smell","Loss of smell","Distorted smell"]'::jsonb, 'Anosmia', 'Anosmia is the complete loss of smell, which can result from head trauma, viral infection, sinonasal disease, or neurodegenerative disorders. Parosmia is a distorted smell and phantosmia an olfactory hallucination.'),
('c76d3a9c-5c14-52a8-88cd-1a7effddc666'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The visual pigment rhodopsin consists of:', '["Opsin + retinol (vitamin A alcohol)","Opsin + 11-cis retinal (vitamin A aldehyde)","Opsin + vitamin D","Opsin + vitamin K","Iodopsin"]'::jsonb, 'Opsin + 11-cis retinal (vitamin A aldehyde)', 'Rhodopsin is aG-protein-coupled receptor composed of the protein scotopsin (rod opsin) bound to the chromophore 11-cis retinal, an aldehyde form of vitamin A. Light isomerizes 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal, triggering phototransduction.'),
('efd79422-b2f1-5adc-9822-06ea414fd322'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', '11-cis retinal is converted to all-trans retinal by:', '["pH","Heat","Darkness","Enzymes","Light"]'::jsonb, 'Light', 'Absorption of a photon of light isomerizes 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal within the rhodopsin molecule, which activates phototransduction. Regeneration of 11-cis retinal from the all-trans form is an enzyme-dependent process in darkness.'),
('ffebf44a-5467-557e-a88a-b80ef69b72f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Phototransduction involves:', '["Depolarization of photoreceptor","Synaptic release increase","Action potential generation","Change in potential","Hyperpolarization of photoreceptor"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpolarization of photoreceptor', 'Light closes cGMP-gated cation channels in the photoreceptor outer segment, causing hyperpolarization and reduced glutamate release. Unlike most neurons, photoreceptors are depolarized in the dark and hyperpolarize in response to light.'),
('c6503308-d078-519a-a323-56866d428c55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What is the membrane state of photoreceptors in the dark, before light exposure?', '["Inactive","Repolarized","Depolarized (dark current)","Hyperpolarized","Resting"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarized (dark current)', 'In darkness, high intracellular cGMP keeps cGMP-gated Na+ channels open, generating an inward ''dark current'' that depolarizes the photoreceptor to about -40 mV and maintains tonic glutamate release. Light reduces cGMP and hyperpolarizes the cell.'),
('079d6651-149a-5eec-a560-b9fd4b6dbaa7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Under which lighting condition does the dark current persist in retinal photoreceptors?', '["Only during photobleaching","Only in bright light","In darkness (absence of light)","Only during light adaptation","Only during cone-mediated vision"]'::jsonb, 'In darkness (absence of light)', 'The dark current flows only in darkness, when cGMP levels are high and nonselective cation channels stay open, keeping the photoreceptor depolarized. In light, cGMP is hydrolyzed, the channels close, and the dark current ceases.'),
('1637a304-5edb-58a3-a41a-a50dba841450'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Light activates which enzyme in phototransduction?', '["Guanylyl cyclase increasing cGMP","Protein kinase A","Adenylyl cyclase","Phospholipase C","Phosphodiesterase (PDE) reducing cGMP"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphodiesterase (PDE) reducing cGMP', 'Light-activated rhodopsin activates transducin, which in turn activates cGMP phosphodiesterase (PDE), hydrolyzing cGMP and closing cGMP-gated channels. This reduces the dark current and hyperpolarizes the photoreceptor.'),
('4436b98c-917d-5c1b-8c62-33d2f52da5dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Transducin is aG-protein in:', '["Hair cells","Olfactory cells","Photoreceptors","Pressure receptors","Taste cells"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptors', 'Transducin is the heterotrimeric G-protein (Gt) that couples rhodopsin to cGMP phosphodiesterase in photoreceptors. Olfactory cells use a different G-protein, Golf, which activates adenylyl cyclase.'),
('efb63bdc-6f9b-50b4-a6a9-bc73c72b915f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Recovery from photobleaching requires:', '["Channel opening resulting in decreased functional activity","New rhodopsin synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heat mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased cGMP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","11-cis retinal regeneration from all-trans retinal"]'::jsonb, '11-cis retinal regeneration from all-trans retinal', 'After photobleaching, recovery requires re-isomerization of all-trans retinal back to 11-cis retinal, a process performed by the retinal pigment epithelium using retinol isomerase. This restitution of the chromophore restores light sensitivity.'),
('5cee8034-ea9f-50b1-b9d8-170e35ad030e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Dark adaptation occurs more slowly for:', '["Neither","Both equally","RPE cells","Rods","Cones"]'::jsonb, 'Rods', 'Rod dark adaptation is slow because it depends on regeneration of large amounts of rhodopsin, taking up to 20-30 minutes. Cone adaptation is faster (a few minutes), so sensitivity improves first for cones and later for rods.'),
('a88781b2-0771-5cc6-b022-9c4fbb6a6641'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Photopic vision is mediated by:', '["Cones only","RPE cells","Rods only","Both rods and cones","Amacrine cells"]'::jsonb, 'Cones only', 'Photopic (bright-light) vision is mediated exclusively by cones, providing high visual acuity and color discrimination at the fovea. Scotopic (dim-light) vision is mediated by rods.'),
('4f206171-bbf2-5836-ac90-a88b3314eb61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Scotopic vision is mediated by:', '["Rods","Both","RPE cells","Cones","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Rods', 'Scotopic (night) vision is mediated by rods, which are highly sensitive to light but lack color discrimination and provide low acuity. Rods are absent from the fovea and concentrated in the peripheral retina.'),
('4974a54e-0602-5d62-ae9e-d4ecec3bc2ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The macula lutea is characterized by which of the following?', '["Lutein pigment resulting in decreased functional activity","Only rods accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fovea in center mediated by specific receptor activation","High cone density without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lutein pigment, fovea in center, and high cone density"]'::jsonb, 'Lutein pigment, fovea in center, and high cone density', 'The macula lutea contains the yellow lutein (macular) pigment, has the fovea at its center, and is dominated by cones, giving high visual acuity. The fovea contains cones only and no rods.'),
('07cc92fc-e626-5ed4-862a-b967fcd11359'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Glaucoma typically damages which retinal cells?', '["Photoreceptors","Bipolar cells","Horizontal cells","RPE cells","Retinal ganglion cells"]'::jsonb, 'Retinal ganglion cells', 'Glaucoma is a progressive optic neuropathy in which elevated intraocular pressure damages retinal ganglion cells and their axons as they exit at the optic disc, producing visual field loss. Other retinal cell types are not the primary target.'),
('4a4cdaf2-0b76-5a7a-b6e9-fdcf71b547d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Intraocular pressure normally ranges from:', '["5-10 mmHg","10-21 mmHg","40-60 mmHg","0-5 mmHg","25-40 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '10-21 mmHg', 'Normal intraocular pressure is 10-21 mmHg, maintained by the balance between aqueous humor production in the ciliary body and its drainage through the trabecular meshwork. Pressures above 21 mmHg increase the risk of glaucomatous damage.'),
('25e78fd1-6251-546d-964c-37377f7edd47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Which ocular structure produces aqueous humor and contains the ciliary muscle for accommodation?', '["Ciliary body","Retina","Iris","Choroid","Lacrimal gland"]'::jsonb, 'Ciliary body', 'The ciliary body produces aqueous humor from its epithelium into the posterior chamber and contains the ciliary muscle, which contracts to relax the zonular fibers and round up the lens for near vision.'),
('8040a097-d9b8-52ff-a873-5e617e040c98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Aqueous humor drains primarily through:', '["Canal of Schlemm","Nasolacrimal duct","Blood vessels","Tears","Lymphatics"]'::jsonb, 'Canal of Schlemm', 'Aqueous humor drains mainly through the trabecular meshwork into the canal of Schlemm at the iridocorneal angle, then into the venous system. Blockage of this outflow raises intraocular pressure, as in open-angle glaucoma.'),
('ef24670e-891a-5f4b-8a15-e9508d904f75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'First-line medical treatment of open-angle glaucoma includes:', '["Prostaglandin analogs (latanoprost)","Beta-blockers (timolol)","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Miotics","Both beta-blockers and prostaglandin analogs"]'::jsonb, 'Both beta-blockers and prostaglandin analogs', 'Prostaglandin analogs (e.g., latanoprost) and topical beta-blockers (e.g., timolol) are first-line agents that lower intraocular pressure by increasing uveoscleral outflow and reducing aqueous production, respectively. Miotics are no longer first-line.'),
('50811e45-4b0a-56ee-b2ae-d03868debcf0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'The near point of accommodation recedes with age due to:', '["Corneal changes","Ciliary muscle weakness","Lens hardening","Retinal degeneration","Zonular laxity"]'::jsonb, 'Lens hardening', 'Presbyopia occurs because the lens progressively hardens and loses elasticity with age, reducing its ability to change shape during accommodation. The near point recedes, and reading at close range becomes difficult.'),
('1ab465ca-1708-5882-a1d4-564132bec92d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Emmetropia means:', '["Astigmatism resulting in decreased functional activity","Presbyopia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Near-sighted mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal vision without refractive error","Far-sighted leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal vision without refractive error', 'Emmetropia is the state of normal vision in which the eye focuses parallel rays of light exactly on the retina without any accommodative effort. Myopia (near-sighted), hyperopia (far-sighted), and astigmatism are refractive errors.'),
('c10a14ef-0121-52ed-8323-37e2380f5f32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Myopia (nearsightedness) is corrected with which type of lens?', '["Cylindrical lens","Convex lens","Concave lens","Bifocals","Prisms"]'::jsonb, 'Concave lens', 'Myopia, in which the image focuses in front of the retina due to an elongated eyeball, is corrected with a concave (minus, diverging) lens that moves the focal point back onto the retina. Hyperopia requires a convex lens.'),
('f5ad74af-881c-5a50-9ae0-6ba153b1c330'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Hyperopia (farsightedness) is corrected with:', '["Cylindrical lens","Convex lens","Bifocals","Prisms","Concave lens"]'::jsonb, 'Convex lens', 'Hyperopia, in which parallel rays focus behind the retina (often with a shortened eyeball), is corrected with a convex (plus, converging) lens. Myopia requires a concave lens, and astigmatism a cylindrical one.'),
('fd568838-9738-5ba0-a7cd-0c7b9b775371'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'Astigmatism is caused by which of the following?', '["Shortened eyeball","Unequal curvature of cornea or lens","Lens hardening","Elongated eyeball","Lens opacity"]'::jsonb, 'Unequal curvature of cornea or lens', 'Astigmatism results from unequal curvature of the cornea or lens in different meridians, so light rays are refracted unequally and fail to focus at a single point. It is corrected with cylindrical lenses. A shortened or elongated eyeball produces hyperopia or myopia.'),
('c4fc72ba-e1ce-57b2-aed3-32bda10e2eeb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Cell Nerve Muscle - Cell Nerve Muscle', 'What is the correct anatomical order of the three auditory ossicles from lateral to medial?', '["Malleus stapes incus","Incus malleus stapes","Stapes incus malleus","Stapes malleus incus","Malleus incus stapes"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus incus stapes', 'From lateral (tympanic membrane) to medial (oval window), the ossicles are the malleus (hammer), incus (anvil), and stapes (stirrup). The stapes footplate transmits vibration into the inner ear at the oval window.'),
('a4ed25c5-6051-5985-a3b1-db01c31236c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What is the approximate total blood volume of a normal adult?', '["8-10 L","5-6 L","3-4 L","2-3 L","1-2 L"]'::jsonb, '5-6 L', 'Total blood volume in a normal adult averages about 5 L (range 5-6 L), roughly 7-8% of body weight.'),
('2368751c-ce4b-5bee-b20f-ad331ce48c02'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Plasma constitutes approximately what percentage of the total blood volume?', '["25%","65%","55%","45%","35%"]'::jsonb, '55%', 'Plasma makes up about 55% of whole blood volume; formed elements, mainly red cells, make up the remaining 45%.'),
('cc1ebd7a-792c-509e-9d08-48e9083325d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The hematocrit in normal adult males is approximately:', '["42 ± 5%","52 ± 5%","55 ± 5%","35 ± 5%","47 ± 5%"]'::jsonb, '47 ± 5%', 'Normal adult male hematocrit averages about 47% (42-52%); adult female values are slightly lower at about 42%.'),
('ca06f758-eb6b-538f-911e-2df52b9e2f11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The percentage of water in plasma is about:', '["80%","95%","75%","91%","85%"]'::jsonb, '91%', 'Plasma is roughly 91-92% water; the rest is dissolved proteins (about 7-8%) and small solutes.'),
('dab8c60c-cb6e-5bb2-b800-ec5ab547b9a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The most abundant plasma protein is:', '["Globulin","Hemoglobin","Fibrinogen","Albumin","Prothrombin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin (3.5-5 g/dL) is the most abundant plasma protein and generates most of the plasma colloid osmotic pressure.'),
('a048634b-9690-5813-adb5-4430ebebb3df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Albumin is synthesized in:', '["Liver","Spleen","Lymph nodes","Kidney","Bone marrow"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Albumin is synthesized exclusively by hepatocytes in the liver.'),
('b4b3bbd8-b938-5c6e-92fa-f0f5ef37d488'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The normal plasma protein concentration is:', '["3-4 g/dL","9-10 g/dL","2-3 g/dL","5-6 g/dL","7-8 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '7-8 g/dL', 'Total plasma protein is normally about 7-8 g/dL, of which albumin accounts for roughly 55-60%.'),
('0986310c-cc33-5b87-a073-c54f4fe5771f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Albumin contributes to:', '["Colloid osmotic pressure","Oxygen transport","Coagulation","Blood group antigens","Immunity"]'::jsonb, 'Colloid osmotic pressure', 'Albumin generates colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure, which keeps water within the vascular compartment.'),
('e02fd9cc-5dfd-5081-9d6c-42328f0aecbc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which organ is the predominant site of hematopoiesis in the fetus during the second trimester of gestation?', '["Liver","Spleen","Lymph nodes","Bone marrow","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'During mid-gestation the liver is the major hematopoietic organ; hematopoiesis later shifts to the bone marrow.'),
('fac71c43-d4d3-5f8f-89a2-a1a14f67974c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The normal ESR in adult males is:', '["0-15 mm/hr","0-10 mm/hr","0-5 mm/hr","25-40 mm/hr","15-25 mm/hr"]'::jsonb, '0-15 mm/hr', 'Westergren ESR is normally under 15 mm/hr in adult males and under 20 mm/hr in adult females.'),
('259e0b3f-e30c-593a-aa7c-0b73014f5ae2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'ESR is increased in:', '["Pregnancy","Sickle cell anemia","Polycythemia","Congestive heart failure","Dehydration"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy', 'ESR rises in pregnancy and inflammatory states because of increased fibrinogen; polycythemia and dehydration reduce rouleaux formation and lower ESR.'),
('64351981-fba1-5ee2-a5db-e59f710d39cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The biconcave shape of RBCs provides:', '["Large surface area for gas exchange","Flexibility to pass through capillaries","Increased fragility","Decreased viscosity","Resistance to osmotic lysis"]'::jsonb, 'Large surface area for gas exchange', 'Biconcavity maximizes the surface-area-to-volume ratio, allowing rapid O2 and CO2 diffusion across the RBC membrane.'),
('0f6f960a-2d67-55ac-8dbb-a94d5396295f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'RBC lifespan is approximately:', '["150 days","60 days","30 days","120 days","200 days"]'::jsonb, '120 days', 'Normal red cells survive about 120 days before being removed by splenic macrophages.'),
('2c338e9a-1895-5485-a883-996be646d5e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'In chronic kidney disease, anemia with fatigue results from erythropoietin deficiency. Where is erythropoietin synthesized?', '["Spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Bone marrow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lungs mediated by specific receptor activation","Liver cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peritubular interstitial cells in kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Peritubular interstitial cells in kidney', 'About 90% of adult erythropoietin is produced by peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex in response to hypoxia.'),
('575edb7b-3b2d-55e0-a8d0-0e19b286ea70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Erythropoietin acts on:', '["Megakaryocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","White blood cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Red blood cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Committed erythroid progenitor cells","Platelets leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Committed erythroid progenitor cells', 'Erythropoietin binds receptors on committed erythroid progenitors (CFU-E), driving their proliferation and differentiation into red cells.'),
('2cb31917-ecde-50f2-99a0-3c7b10ed8357'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which stimulus most strongly stimulates erythropoietin secretion?', '["High altitude","Low pH","Exercise","Low blood O2 (hypoxia)","High CO2"]'::jsonb, 'Low blood O2 (hypoxia)', 'Renal tissue hypoxia is the physiologic trigger for erythropoietin release, which restores oxygen-carrying capacity.'),
('0ee6173f-f469-5514-9698-f3dc924bb2b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF) is:', '["Activated by hypoxia","Destroyed by hypoxia","Activated by normoxia","Found only in lungs","Inactivated by tissue hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Activated by hypoxia', 'HIF-1α is stabilized under hypoxic conditions and drives transcription of the erythropoietin gene and other hypoxia-adaptive genes.'),
('524ce1fc-09a9-5e59-b9ab-3b73941ba5ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The reticulocyte count in normal adults is:', '["0.2-0.5%","3-5%","5-10%","2-3%","0.5-1.5%"]'::jsonb, '0.5-1.5%', 'Normal reticulocytes are 0.5-1.5% of circulating red cells, reflecting recently released, still-maturing erythroid cells.'),
('a492e909-dcae-5d92-8ea2-9cfa4a22f61f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Reticulocytosis indicates:', '["Aplastic anemia","Bone marrow suppression","B12 deficiency","Iron deficiency","Hemolytic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia', 'Reticulocytosis reflects accelerated marrow erythropoiesis and is characteristic of hemolytic anemia and recent blood loss.'),
('4be3792a-bdb8-50ec-a69b-b15c25a69d80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Anemia is defined as:', '["Hb <13.5 g/dL in males","Hb <12.5 g/dL in males","RBC count <4 million/μL","Hct <35%","Low serum ferritin"]'::jsonb, 'Hb <13.5 g/dL in males', 'Anemia is defined by a hemoglobin below the reference range for age and sex, taken as approximately Hb <13.5 g/dL in adult males.'),
('5eaf0b17-52c5-5f8e-a96e-377c8226a7df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Pernicious anemia is most commonly caused by:', '["Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells","Lack of intrinsic factor","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Low iron intake","Dietary B12 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells', 'Pernicious anemia results from autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells, abolishing intrinsic factor production and thereby impairing ileal vitamin B12 absorption.'),
('9ee2984b-cef2-55cb-8886-e69b2e331955'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Intrinsic factor is secreted by:', '["Parietal cells","G cells","Pancreatic acinar cells","Chief cells","Goblet cells"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal cells', 'Gastric parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete intrinsic factor, which is required for ileal absorption of vitamin B12.'),
('87330a9e-d7e2-5caa-8ec6-a12128bf1063'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The most common cause of iron deficiency anemia worldwide is:', '["Dietary deficiency","Pregnancy","Chronic blood loss (hookworm)","Menstrual blood loss","Malabsorption"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic blood loss (hookworm)', 'Chronic blood loss, often from hookworm infestation in developing countries, is the most common cause of iron deficiency anemia worldwide.'),
('a9684c63-809e-5b74-b90c-cecaaa2535b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Sickle cell disease is caused by mutation in:', '["Gamma chain resulting in decreased functional activity","Beta-globin chain (Glu to Val at position 6)","Alpha-globin chain mediated by specific receptor activation","Delta chain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heme synthesis enzyme leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-globin chain (Glu to Val at position 6)', 'Sickle cell disease results from a point mutation in the beta-globin gene substituting valine for glutamic acid at position 6.'),
('131eec20-a79a-5926-9956-3fc8528c4d06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'In sickle cell disease HbS polymerization occurs when:', '["Deoxygenated","Oxygenated","Only at low temperature","Only at high pH","When strongly bound to CO"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated', 'HbS polymerizes when deoxygenated; polymer formation distorts red cells into sickles, especially with low O2, acidosis, and dehydration.'),
('900335a1-bff0-5606-8afe-c0b9f8255a5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Thalassemia is due to:', '["Enzyme deficiency","Impaired globin chain synthesis","Heme deficiency","Abnormal globin structure","Iron deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired globin chain synthesis', 'Thalassemia is caused by reduced or absent synthesis of one or more globin chains, creating an imbalance that leads to ineffective erythropoiesis.'),
('cce6347e-b600-5abf-936b-97f47e698387'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A hemolytic anemia is characterized by:', '["Decreased haptoglobin","Increased unconjugated bilirubin","Increased reticulocytes","Increased LDH","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Hemolysis increases unconjugated bilirubin, reticulocytes, and LDH while consuming haptoglobin, so all of the above are characteristic.'),
('7686fc3f-4ad9-56d0-87cf-19b955c56367'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency causes:', '["Megaloblastic anemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Iron deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalassemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hemolytic anemia with oxidative stress","Aplastic anemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia with oxidative stress', 'G6PD deficiency impairs the pentose phosphate pathway, lowering NADPH; oxidative stress (e.g., fava beans, oxidant drugs) then induces hemolysis.'),
('ad5a8d0b-7918-5c8b-baf9-1c9bba0d6652'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'G6PD deficiency protects against:', '["Malaria","Viral infection","Parasitic infection","Fungal infection","Bacterial infection"]'::jsonb, 'Malaria', 'G6PD-deficient red cells poorly support the oxidative metabolism of the malaria parasite, conferring partial protection against Plasmodium infection.'),
('740d987f-c9de-54ee-8eb0-de4975dec976'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What is the primary molecular defect in hereditary spherocytosis?', '["Na+ channel defect","Hemoglobin mutation","AChE deficiency","Enzyme deficiency","Spectrin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Spectrin deficiency', 'Hereditary spherocytosis is most often due to spectrin deficiency (with defects of ankyrin, band 3, or protein 4.2), destabilizing the red cell membrane.'),
('42b05b81-78a2-5760-8392-493c9169f9fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which inherited hemolytic anemia is characterized by spherocytes on peripheral smear and osmotic fragility?', '["Polycythemia","Iron deficiency","Thalassemia","Sickle cell disease","Hereditary spherocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary spherocytosis', 'Autosomal dominant membrane defects produce spherocytes that lyse in hypotonic saline, so osmotic fragility is increased.'),
('0a0852bf-98e8-5fcb-aedc-4e504f2b91bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A 45-year-old female presents with fatigue, dark morning urine, and thrombosis. Lab shows Hb 7.0 g/dL. Which molecular defect is most likely?', '["Immune hemolysis","Hemoglobin mutation","Enzyme deficiency","Spectrin deficiency","PIG-A mutation (GPI anchor deficiency)"]'::jsonb, 'PIG-A mutation (GPI anchor deficiency)', 'Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria results from a somatic PIG-A mutation causing loss of GPI-anchored complement regulators, leading to complement-mediated hemolysis.'),
('4f0e4278-af46-5df4-abaf-913558c71376'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which WBC is most abundant in blood?', '["Basophils","Monocytes","Neutrophils","Lymphocytes","Eosinophils"]'::jsonb, 'Neutrophils', 'Neutrophils are the most abundant leukocytes (40-70%), responding first to bacterial infection.'),
('9b25aa19-fef0-5f52-afe8-16e96f11c39a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which type of pathogen most commonly triggers an acute febrile response with elevated neutrophils?', '["Fungal infection","Allergic reactions","Viral infection","Bacterial infection","Antibody production"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial infection', 'Acute pyogenic bacterial infections trigger a neutrophilic leukocytosis and fever through release of IL-1, TNF, and G-CSF.'),
('9aae7486-39d8-50b3-a3fb-3f2d6894c11a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Band cells are:', '["Mature neutrophils","Lymphocytes","Monocytes","Immature neutrophils","Eosinophils"]'::jsonb, 'Immature neutrophils', 'Band cells are immature (young) neutrophils; a left shift with increased bands indicates accelerated marrow granulopoiesis.'),
('81f9ba24-846b-5472-ba2f-418d9e146d12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which two arms of the adaptive immune system work together to combat intracellular pathogens?', '["Antibody and cell-mediated immunity","Bacterial phagocytosis","Viral neutralization","Parasite defense","Allergy"]'::jsonb, 'Antibody and cell-mediated immunity', 'Humoral (antibody) and cell-mediated (cytotoxic T cell) immunity cooperate in eliminating intracellular pathogens.'),
('b9d94ead-06ac-5dee-9a3e-fc80b3dbc8c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'T lymphocytes mature in:', '["Thymus","Spleen","Lymph nodes","Bone marrow","Peyer patches"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus', 'T lymphocytes migrate to the thymus, where they mature and undergo positive and negative selection.'),
('ccd63608-03cf-50e3-89ac-f230627ea4c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'B lymphocytes mature in:', '["Tonsils","Lymph nodes","Bone marrow","Spleen","Thymus"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow', 'B lymphocytes mature in the bone marrow and later populate secondary lymphoid organs.'),
('086fc029-c468-5788-845d-718fd7541a3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The ratio of CD4:CD8 in normal blood is approximately:', '["2:1","0.5:1","4:1","10:1","1:1"]'::jsonb, '2:1', 'In healthy adults the CD4:CD8 ratio is approximately 2:1; it falls in HIV infection as CD4 cells are depleted.'),
('24f96563-b789-501a-9b57-d48f18e83724'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Monocytes differentiate into:', '["Lymphocytes","Basophils","Neutrophils","Macrophages in tissues","Eosinophils"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages in tissues', 'Monocytes leave the blood and differentiate into tissue macrophages, where they phagocytose and present antigen.'),
('0eccc85e-0e6e-55f4-862a-0037acafbe75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which MHC molecule presents exogenous antigens to CD4+ T helper cells?', '["MHC","MHC class I only","CD4 only","CD8 only","MHC class II"]'::jsonb, 'MHC class II', 'CD4+ T helper cells recognize exogenous peptides presented on MHC class II molecules of professional antigen-presenting cells.'),
('55f51834-a433-554e-a0e2-1592081a929c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'IgE and eosinophils are primarily involved in defense against which type of challenge?', '["Stress response","Bacterial infections","Fungal infections","Viral infections","Parasitic infections and allergies"]'::jsonb, 'Parasitic infections and allergies', 'IgE sensitizes mast cells and eosinophils, driving defense against helminthic parasites and the pathology of type I allergic reactions.'),
('93179904-7715-5453-8440-f64171d1f40f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A 35-year-old female develops anaphylaxis in the ICU. Which mediators released by mast cell/basophil degranulation cause this reaction?', '["Prostaglandins","Perforin and granzyme","IgE","Complement","Histamine and heparin"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine and heparin', 'Mast cells and basophils release preformed histamine and heparin granules, triggering vasodilation, bronchoconstriction, and shock in anaphylaxis.'),
('73524d24-15b5-5814-998a-130b940dc9b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Mast cells are similar to:', '["Lymphocytes","Eosinophils","Basophils (tissue-resident)","Neutrophils","Monocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Basophils (tissue-resident)', 'Mast cells are tissue-resident cells functionally similar to basophils, both containing metachromatic granules rich in histamine.'),
('f2670efd-d7c8-5bfe-bcbf-dfe3cb76352a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Natural killer (NK) cells kill:', '["Virus-infected and tumor cells","Fungi","Extracellular pathogens","Parasites","Bacteria"]'::jsonb, 'Virus-infected and tumor cells', 'NK cells lyse virus-infected and tumor cells, particularly those with reduced or absent surface MHC class I.'),
('0984a80a-aa09-5087-a576-67d61980522f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'NK cells are activated by:', '["Lack of MHC class I on target cells","Antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MHC class I presence mediated by specific receptor activation","Complement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CD4 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of MHC class I on target cells', 'Loss of self MHC class I (the missing-self signal) removes an inhibitory brake, activating NK cell cytotoxicity.'),
('ece529ea-8d17-56a2-9746-4cfa950d3ab4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What is the final step in the phagocytosis process after ingestion of a pathogen?', '["Antibody production","Ingestion","Chemotaxis","Recognition and attachment","Killing and digestion"]'::jsonb, 'Killing and digestion', 'The final step of phagocytosis occurs in phagolysosomes, where hydrolytic enzymes and reactive oxygen species kill and digest the pathogen.'),
('3dc983c3-bd28-5e70-92f3-cf9a4d52258d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Opsonization enhances phagocytosis using:', '["Interleukins","Histamine","Immunoglobulins and complement","Lymphokines","Cytokines only"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoglobulins and complement', 'Opsonins, antibodies (IgG) and complement fragments (C3b), coat pathogens and enhance recognition by phagocyte receptors.'),
('29b27ac6-0ce4-5ead-82f6-fa68b364ebed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The numbered proteins of the complement system are:', '["C1-C9","Factors B and D","C1-C4 only","C5-C9 only","Properdin alone"]'::jsonb, 'C1-C9', 'The complement cascade comprises proteins numbered C1 through C9, along with accessory factors (B, D, properdin) and regulatory proteins.'),
('34c510ec-e827-5c2f-96f0-d657788cbbf8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'C5 convertase cleaves C5 into:', '["C5a and C5b","C3a and C3b","C4a and C4b","C5b and C6","Factors B and D"]'::jsonb, 'C5a and C5b', 'C5 convertase splits C5 into C5a (anaphylatoxin) and C5b, which initiates assembly of the membrane attack complex.'),
('e34189fc-e7fc-53f0-9b33-d741c2494926'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which complement enzyme is the C3 convertase of the alternative pathway?', '["C3bBb","C4b2a","C1 complex","C5a","C5b-9 (membrane attack complex)"]'::jsonb, 'C3bBb', 'In the alternative pathway, C3bBb (stabilized by properdin) serves as C3 convertase, generating more C3b.'),
('d4812b63-a7ef-5839-a254-7683c6a92066'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which immunoglobulin class is the most abundant in serum and crosses the placenta?', '["IgA","IgD","IgM","IgG","IgE"]'::jsonb, 'IgG', 'IgG (75-80% of serum immunoglobulin) is the most abundant class and the only immunoglobulin that crosses the placenta, conferring passive neonatal immunity.'),
('5643b930-077e-5907-a92c-e97f72a65f2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The immunoglobulin that crosses the placenta is:', '["IgE","IgM","IgG","IgD","IgA"]'::jsonb, 'IgG', 'IgG is transferred across the placenta via FcRn-mediated transport, giving the newborn passive immunity during the first months of life.'),
('3ff19d90-d5b1-5765-bafb-6f6f96c7eb84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The first immunoglobulin produced in primary immune response is:', '["IgD","IgG","IgM","IgE","IgA"]'::jsonb, 'IgM', 'IgM is the first antibody produced in a primary immune response; it is a potent complement-activating pentamer.'),
('7d01ef8e-313c-5630-8c3e-986bd2ee4b4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Secretory IgA is important for:', '["Parasite defense","Mucosal immunity","Allergic reactions","Systemic immunity","Newborn immunity"]'::jsonb, 'Mucosal immunity', 'Secretory IgA, found in secretions of the gut, respiratory tract, and tears, defends mucosal surfaces against pathogens.'),
('784d108e-5496-5b33-910c-dd0ec8c1cfe8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'IgE is involved in:', '["Bacterial immunity resulting in decreased functional activity","Fungal immunity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allergic reactions and parasite defense","Neonatal immunity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Viral immunity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic reactions and parasite defense', 'IgE mediates type I hypersensitivity and defense against helminthic parasites by sensitizing mast cells and eosinophils.'),
('63afadeb-b808-5d20-98a9-4ad5a3217e2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Immediate hypersensitivity (Type I) is mediated by:', '["IgM","IgA","IgD","IgE","IgG"]'::jsonb, 'IgE', 'Type I (immediate) hypersensitivity is initiated when allergen cross-links IgE bound to mast cells, triggering degranulation.'),
('afb56249-18cb-5d46-8ce8-f0c2c2aee120'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Anaphylaxis is mediated by:', '["Antibodies alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Complement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neutrophils mediated by specific receptor activation","Histamine and leukotrienes from mast cells","T cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine and leukotrienes from mast cells', 'Mast cell-derived histamine and leukotrienes cause vasodilation, increased permeability, bronchospasm, and cardiovascular collapse in anaphylaxis.'),
('d8836651-841e-54ee-b51c-14386116f200'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Type II hypersensitivity involves:', '["IgE resulting in decreased functional activity","Delayed type hypersensitivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IgG/IgM against cell surface antigens","T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immune complexes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IgG/IgM against cell surface antigens', 'Type II (cytotoxic) hypersensitivity results from IgG or IgM binding to cell-surface antigens, causing opsonization, ADCC, or complement lysis.'),
('2bf5812d-7c8c-5e5c-b35c-afea429508da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Type III hypersensitivity reactions such as serum sickness are characterized by:', '["Immune complex deposition","Direct cell lysis","Antibody against receptors","T cell-mediated injury","IgE-mediated degranulation"]'::jsonb, 'Immune complex deposition', 'Type III reactions occur when soluble antigen-antibody complexes deposit in tissues (vessels, joints, glomeruli), activating complement and recruiting neutrophils.'),
('c4027ded-6dcf-5ced-ae1b-a27e624986d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Type IV hypersensitivity involves:', '["Complement resulting in decreased functional activity","IgG accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IgE mediated by specific receptor activation","Immune complexes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T cell-mediated delayed reaction"]'::jsonb, 'T cell-mediated delayed reaction', 'Type IV (delayed) hypersensitivity is mediated by sensitized T cells rather than antibody, causing tissue damage through cytokines and cytotoxic cells.'),
('8f6639e7-cfad-5d60-8945-7fcb6e9497a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Tuberculin skin test (Mantoux) is an example of:', '["Type III","Anaphylactic","Type I","Type IV","Type II"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV', 'The Mantoux (tuberculin) test is a classic type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction mediated by memory T cells.'),
('d0fee963-ae50-55b3-bf07-230a17264c9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which hypersensitivity reaction is immediate and mediated by IgE-bound mast cells?', '["Type I hypersensitivity","Type IV hypersensitivity","Type II","Type III","Immune complex"]'::jsonb, 'Type I hypersensitivity', 'Type I reactions are immediate, IgE-dependent, and mediated by mast cell degranulation.'),
('011d1d38-c4fd-552c-a1cb-49047db54200'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Autoimmune hemolytic anemia is an example of:', '["Type II (antibody against RBCs)","Type I","Type III","Type IV","Antibody against neutrophils"]'::jsonb, 'Type II (antibody against RBCs)', 'Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia is a type II reaction, with IgG against red cell membrane antigens causing splenic sequestration and hemolysis.'),
('6ceacfc0-d8f0-5f71-a804-eea7dfe03a9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'How does RhoGAM prevent hemolytic disease of the newborn (HDN)?', '["Passive anti-Rh antibodies neutralize fetal RBCs","Vaccination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complement inhibition mediated by specific receptor activation","Immune suppression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T cell suppression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Passive anti-Rh antibodies neutralize fetal RBCs', 'RhoGAM (passive anti-D) coats fetal Rh-positive red cells in the Rh-negative mother''s circulation, preventing maternal sensitization so anti-D is not produced.'),
('7c61c764-e039-5e1f-a262-dc6505e3d0c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which lymphoid organs are classified as primary (central) lymphoid organs?', '["Tonsils and appendix","Bursa of Fabricius","Bone marrow and thymus","Lymph nodes and spleen","Peyer patches and MALT"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow and thymus', 'Bone marrow (B cells) and thymus (T cells) are primary lymphoid organs where lymphocytes develop and become immunocompetent.'),
('ca347096-b176-5cc0-97c6-c3db41cc93ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which lymphoid organs are classified as secondary (peripheral) lymphoid organs?', '["Bone marrow and thymus resulting in decreased functional activity","Only lymph nodes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only spleen mediated by specific receptor activation","Lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and Peyer patches","Only tonsils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and Peyer patches', 'Secondary lymphoid organs, where antigens are presented and immune responses are initiated, include lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils, and Peyer patches.'),
('68e10ae2-6304-5088-9351-4901280d6833'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which body fluid is the primary transport medium for immune cells throughout the body?', '["Urine","CSF","Lymph","Bile","Blood"]'::jsonb, 'Blood', 'Blood transports leukocytes and immune mediators throughout the body, delivering them to sites of infection and inflammation.'),
('3e879f6a-4bf1-5651-ab88-a3fb40bb4710'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Marginal zone of spleen contains:', '["Macrophages only resulting in decreased functional activity","T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","B cells specialized for T-independent antigens","NK cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neutrophils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'B cells specialized for T-independent antigens', 'Splenic marginal-zone B cells respond rapidly to T-independent (polysaccharide) antigens.'),
('c990e781-cb75-5939-8cd6-7fe474a6f826'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I molecules present antigen to:', '["CD8+ T cells","CD4+ T cells","B cells","NK cells","Macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'CD8+ T cells', 'MHC class I on nucleated cells presents endogenous peptides (e.g., viral proteins) to CD8+ cytotoxic T cells.'),
('0de03195-a0c5-5a68-832f-a4538c017d52'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Clonal selection theory states:', '["Lymphocytes are non-specific resulting in decreased functional activity","T cells only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lymphocytes with specific receptor are selected by antigen","Antigens create new receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All lymphocytes are identical leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lymphocytes with specific receptor are selected by antigen', 'Clonal selection states that antigen selects and activates only lymphocytes bearing a complementary specific receptor, which then clonally expand.'),
('48882a25-1c86-5d40-831a-f345fb9b6521'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Central tolerance occurs in:', '["Thymus and bone marrow","MALT","Periphery","Spleen","Lymph nodes"]'::jsonb, 'Thymus and bone marrow', 'Central tolerance is established in the thymus and bone marrow, where developing immunocytes with strongly self-reactive receptors die or edit their receptors.'),
('f7b5c77c-a4a2-5861-a47e-0754e6f26f7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Negative selection in thymus eliminates:', '["Macrophages","NK cells","Self-reactive T cells","B cells","Non-reactive T cells"]'::jsonb, 'Self-reactive T cells', 'Negative selection in the thymus deletes T cells that react strongly with self-peptide-MHC, preventing autoimmunity.'),
('7dfd5919-07ef-5475-9056-b2dc28d67142'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'FOXP3 mutation causes:', '["SCID resulting in decreased functional activity","Wiskott-Aldrich accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IPEX syndrome (immune dysregulation polyendocrinopathy enteropathy X-linked)","DiGeorge syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ataxia telangiectasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IPEX syndrome (immune dysregulation polyendocrinopathy enteropathy X-linked)', 'FOXP3 is the master transcription factor of regulatory T cells; its mutation causes IPEX syndrome with severe autoimmunity.'),
('4895d045-a69b-575e-ba6a-bea1aaadfb89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'SCID (severe combined immunodeficiency) involves:', '["B cell only","NK cells only","T cell only","Complement deficiency","T and B cell deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'T and B cell deficiency', 'SCID lacks functional T cells and variably B/NK cells, causing severe early infections from failure of both cellular and humoral immunity.'),
('1bc33a48-16d1-5e10-9c33-dd98f752666a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'DiGeorge syndrome results from:', '["X-linked resulting in decreased functional activity","Chediak-Higashi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ADA deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","22q11.2 deletion causing thymic aplasia","Ataxia telangiectasia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '22q11.2 deletion causing thymic aplasia', 'The 22q11.2 deletion causes DiGeorge syndrome with thymic aplasia or hypoplasia, leading to profound T-cell deficiency.'),
('2f61ddec-8958-5a7a-958c-529f8991de8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Bruton agammaglobulinemia is:', '["NK deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","X-linked B cell deficiency (btk mutation)","T cell deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Complement deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phagocyte defect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked B cell deficiency (btk mutation)', 'Bruton agammaglobulinemia is X-linked, caused by a btk mutation that halts early B-cell development, so all immunoglobulins are low.'),
('df507d3e-afcf-5515-807c-4e5482967803'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Chediak-Higashi syndrome involves:', '["T cell defect","Missing NADPH oxidase","Antibody deficiency","Defective phagolysosome fusion","Complement deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Defective phagolysosome fusion', 'Chediak-Higashi syndrome results from defective lysosomal granule trafficking, impairing phagolysosome fusion and microbial killing.'),
('50277da6-8773-58c7-bb5e-f0f8d00502d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Chronic granulomatous disease involves:', '["NADPH oxidase defect","Complement deficiency","T cell defect","Adhesion defect","Myeloperoxidase defect"]'::jsonb, 'NADPH oxidase defect', 'CGD arises from NADPH oxidase defects so phagocytes cannot generate superoxide; patients suffer recurrent catalase-positive infections and granulomas.'),
('97084010-fc2f-5bbb-a02a-41e7ad5810a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Leukocyte adhesion deficiency (LAD) involves:', '["CD4 defect","Antibody defect","CD18 integrin defect","MHC defect","CD8 defect"]'::jsonb, 'CD18 integrin defect', 'LAD is caused by defective CD18 (beta-2 integrin), so leukocytes cannot adhere to endothelium and migrate to sites of infection.'),
('84a57b5f-2539-52bc-a224-1877db4b9388'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The hemostatic balance involves:', '["Platelets and coagulation factors","Only platelets","Only endothelium","Only coagulation factors","Only fibrinolytic"]'::jsonb, 'Platelets and coagulation factors', 'Hemostatic balance is maintained between procoagulant forces (platelets, coagulation factors) and anticoagulant/fibrinolytic forces.'),
('7de4db9d-56b6-525a-a668-f8de6edd4195'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Primary hemostasis involves:', '["Clot retraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrinolysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vasoconstriction and platelet plug formation","Thrombus lysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coagulation cascade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction and platelet plug formation', 'Primary hemostasis produces vasoconstriction and a platelet plug; secondary hemostasis reinforces it with a fibrin clot.'),
('99be206f-2afb-51ec-81de-fb989f08640e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Bernard-Soulier syndrome involves deficiency of:', '["GP Ib/IX/V complex","Factor VIII","Warfarin","GP IIb/IIIa","Fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'GP Ib/IX/V complex', 'Bernard-Soulier syndrome lacks the GP Ib/IX/V complex, the platelet receptor for von Willebrand factor, causing defective adhesion and macrothrombocytopenia.'),
('d19d5596-2e2f-5e19-bb9a-a34ec54f05c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Glanzmann thrombasthenia involves:', '["GP IIb/IIIa deficiency","Platelet count low","Factor VIII deficiency","vWF deficiency","Coagulation factor defect"]'::jsonb, 'GP IIb/IIIa deficiency', 'Glanzmann thrombasthenia is caused by GP IIb/IIIa (alphaIIb-beta3) deficiency, impairing platelet aggregation via fibrinogen cross-linking.'),
('487b2693-623f-525e-8313-69df1d485f93'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'At a site of vascular injury, which molecular interaction directly links subendothelial collagen to platelet activation?', '["Collagen to GPVI","Factor Xa binding","Thrombin to PAR","vWF to GPIb","Fibrinogen to GP IIb/IIIa"]'::jsonb, 'Collagen to GPVI', 'Platelet glycoprotein VI binds exposed collagen, triggering platelet activation and granule release during adhesion.'),
('c6e60d74-1377-5e69-9600-5fcb7ce39b12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which combination produces the stable secondary hemostatic plug (blood clot) at a wound site?', '["Thrombin and fibrin","Red cells and plasma","Only vWF","Only fibrinogen","White cells"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombin and fibrin', 'Thrombin cleaves fibrinogen to fibrin and activates factor XIII, producing a stable fibrin mesh that anchors the platelet plug.'),
('62390328-6774-5020-813f-f8c6e1f473bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What is the primary function of prostacyclin (PGI2) released by endothelial cells?', '["Activates coagulation","Vasoconstricts","Inhibits platelet aggregation","Promotes adhesion","Promotes platelet aggregation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibits platelet aggregation', 'PGI2 released by endothelium inhibits platelet aggregation and vasodilates, counteracting thromboxane A2 to limit thrombus extension.'),
('6355bb81-0c67-54ee-9412-2f69cd304b2f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Thromboxane A2 from platelets:', '["Vasodilates resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibits aggregation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates protein C mediated by specific receptor activation","Promotes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction","Inhibits coagulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction', 'Platelet thromboxane A2 reinforces aggregation and causes vasoconstriction, amplifying the hemostatic plug.'),
('23daf73d-8aa2-5ef7-a6e2-ac489a914758'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The coagulation cascade that generates fibrin is initiated through which two converging activation pathways?', '["Fast and slow","Primary and secondary","Active and inactive","Direct and indirect","Extrinsic and intrinsic pathways"]'::jsonb, 'Extrinsic and intrinsic pathways', 'Coagulation proceeds via the tissue factor (extrinsic) and contact (intrinsic) pathways, which converge on the common pathway at factor X activation.'),
('1b078de6-3b3b-5274-a74d-530f1f4c968d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The common pathway begins at:', '["Fibrinogen to fibrin","Factor X activation","Prothrombin to thrombin","Factor VIII activation","Factor XII activation"]'::jsonb, 'Factor X activation', 'The common pathway begins with activation of factor X; both the extrinsic and intrinsic pathways feed into it before prothrombin is converted.'),
('83fc2b34-6979-54fc-aff6-4be4c28ab8d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Thrombin converts:', '["Factor X to Xa","Fibrinogen to fibrin","Plasminogen to plasmin","Prothrombin to thrombin","Fibrin to FDP"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrinogen to fibrin', 'Thrombin (IIa) converts soluble fibrinogen into fibrin monomers, which polymerize; it also activates platelets and factors V, VIII, XIII.'),
('440b632a-acf3-5870-a89a-2e6daf7450b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Factor XIII (fibrin stabilizing factor):', '["Crosslinks fibrin polymers","Begins coagulation","Clot retraction","Activates platelets","Converts fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Crosslinks fibrin polymers', 'Activated factor XIII (fibrin-stabilizing factor) cross-links fibrin polymers, producing a covalent insoluble clot.'),
('6b15f9a0-9c09-50f5-8ec7-26acb80721a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Vitamin K is required for synthesis of factors:', '["II, VII, IX, X, and protein C/Protein S","Factor VIII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Factor V mediated by specific receptor activation","Factor XI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Factor XIII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'II, VII, IX, X, and protein C/Protein S', 'Vitamin K is required for gamma-carboxylation of factors II, VII, IX, X and proteins C, S, and Z; warfarin blocks this modification.'),
('2007fa1c-2544-53a5-b4b3-9609d8b392eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Warfarin acts by inhibiting:', '["Platelet aggregation","vWF synthesis","Thrombin","Vitamin K epoxide reductase","Factor X"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K epoxide reductase', 'Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase, depleting the reduced vitamin K needed to synthesize vitamin K-dependent clotting factors.'),
('ab93ed04-9cb4-539e-958a-f660b9993317'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Heparin acts by:', '["Inhibiting vitamin K","Blocking platelet aggregation","Dissolving the clot","Inactivating thrombin directly","Potentiating antithrombin III"]'::jsonb, 'Potentiating antithrombin III', 'Heparin binds antithrombin III and greatly accelerates its inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa.'),
('367fc776-05de-5ec4-aa12-c888f9fb4570'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Antithrombin III inhibits:', '["Only thrombin","Factor VII","Factor IX","Thrombin and Factor Xa","Platelet activation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombin and Factor Xa', 'Antithrombin III is the major physiological inhibitor of thrombin and factor Xa (and, to a lesser degree, factors IXa, XIa, XIIa).'),
('d4a969d8-3e44-5165-9002-1ce3c8f17c82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which natural anticoagulants require vitamin K for their synthesis in the liver?', '["Fibrinolytic factors","Platelet factors","Vitamin K-dependent anticoagulants","Complement proteins","Procoagulant factors"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K-dependent anticoagulants', 'Protein C, protein S, and protein Z are vitamin K-dependent natural anticoagulants that regulate the coagulation cascade.'),
('67bc9b64-2cb6-52f6-ab14-81014436eab6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Tissue factor pathway inhibitor (TFPI) inhibits:', '["Factor VIIa-tissue factor complex","Factor XIII accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Factor X mediated by specific receptor activation","Thrombin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Platelets leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIIa-tissue factor complex', 'TFPI secreted by endothelium inhibits the tissue factor-factor VIIa complex, dampening extrinsic pathway initiation.'),
('d273f32a-1014-5cbb-aca8-fca1c27ed32a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What is the key enzymatic step in fibrinolysis that degrades blood clots?', '["Thrombin activation","Plasminogen to plasmin conversion","Platelet activation","Complement activation","Factor XIII activation"]'::jsonb, 'Plasminogen to plasmin conversion', 'tPA and urokinase convert plasminogen to plasmin, the enzyme that degrades fibrin and dissolves the clot.'),
('015a463a-2de1-53a7-9953-046aab7e18b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) is produced by:', '["Platelets","Monocytes","Kidney","Liver","Endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Endothelial cells', 'tPA is synthesized and released by vascular endothelial cells, localizing fibrinolysis to the clot.'),
('53bd3040-ad09-50f6-90b8-aee484d04bf4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Plasmin degrades:', '["Platelets","White cells","Collagen","Red cells","Fibrin and fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin and fibrinogen', 'Plasmin digests fibrin and fibrinogen (and factors V and VIII), yielding fibrin degradation products such as D-dimers.'),
('1e6b5b6e-4b68-57b5-9cea-106c88b3c09d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'D-dimer is a marker of:', '["Complement activation","vWF release","Thrombin generation","Platelet activation","Fibrin degradation (clot lysis)"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin degradation (clot lysis)', 'D-dimer is a cross-linked fibrin fragment produced when plasmin degrades formed clot, indicating ongoing fibrinolysis.'),
('fff0122b-95f6-5d9b-9941-2748555367f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The PT (prothrombin time) measures:', '["Factor XIII","Extrinsic and common pathway","Intrinsic pathway","Fibrinolysis","Platelet function"]'::jsonb, 'Extrinsic and common pathway', 'PT reflects the extrinsic and common pathways (factors VII, X, V, II, fibrinogen) and is used to monitor warfarin.'),
('8d7c57ef-8fc0-5139-a69b-38d930033a13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time) measures:', '["Fibrinolysis","Platelet function","vWF activity","Extrinsic pathway","Intrinsic and common pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic and common pathway', 'aPTT reflects the intrinsic and common pathways (factors XII, XI, IX, VIII, X, V, II, fibrinogen) and is used to monitor heparin.'),
('d509f2bb-c015-5e84-b055-b642c86f8c60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which deficiency characteristically prolongs the aPTT but not the PT?', '["Factor VIII deficiency","Factor VII deficiency","Platelet dysfunction","Factor XIII deficiency","Thrombocytopenia"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII deficiency', 'Factor VIII (intrinsic pathway) deficiency, hemophilia A, prolongs the aPTT while the PT remains normal because the extrinsic pathway is unaffected.'),
('aaa12473-bb32-5449-b63f-a04df0b1fa2f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Thrombin time (TT) is prolonged in:', '["All of the above","DIC","Heparin therapy","Hypofibrinogenemia","Dysfibrinogenemia"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'TT is prolonged whenever fibrin polymerization is impaired or thrombin is inhibited, as in hypofibrinogenemia, dysfibrinogenemia, DIC, and heparin therapy.'),
('32d8dda2-3549-53e9-93c0-e13bdbdebbbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Vitamin K is absorbed in the gut with:', '["Gastric acid","Pancreatic enzymes","Intrinsic factor","Amino acids","Bile salts"]'::jsonb, 'Bile salts', 'Vitamin K is fat-soluble and requires bile salts (micelles) for intestinal absorption; obstructive jaundice or malabsorption therefore impairs absorption.'),
('47019a49-6733-5494-9bb5-2a3387ce75b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Hemophilia A is due to deficiency of:', '["Factor VII","Factor IX","Factor VIII","Factor XI","vWF"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII', 'Hemophilia A is an X-linked factor VIII deficiency; hemophilia B is due to factor IX deficiency.'),
('9eff2589-441a-5e6b-bd21-dbefbd02591e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is characterized by:', '["Thrombosis only resulting in decreased functional activity","Bleeding only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Consumption of coagulation factors and platelets","Fibrinolysis only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased clotting only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Consumption of coagulation factors and platelets', 'DIC involves generalized activation of coagulation with consumption of factors and platelets, causing simultaneous thrombosis and bleeding.'),
('d4ee4e12-ab24-59e3-90bb-c2239b4fb53e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Thrombocytopenia is defined as a platelet count below:', '["150,000/μL","100,000/μL","50,000/μL","20,000/μL","400,000/μL"]'::jsonb, '150,000/μL', 'The normal platelet count is 150,000-400,000/μL; thrombocytopenia is a count below 150,000/μL.'),
('198a6373-510d-541e-9c59-2bab019c3c41'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A 45-year-old female presents with mucocutaneous bleeding and petechiae. Thrombocytopenia is most likely caused by:', '["vWF deficiency","Autoantibodies against platelet GP IIb/IIIa","Platelet consumption","Decreased production","Splenic sequestration"]'::jsonb, 'Autoantibodies against platelet GP IIb/IIIa', 'ITP is caused by autoantibodies against platelet GP IIb/IIIa, leading to accelerated splenic clearance of sensitized platelets.'),
('9861ba10-a5bb-5989-ac24-57138b0a5265'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is characterized by:', '["Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia","Fever","All of the above","Neurologic symptoms and renal dysfunction","Thrombocytopenia"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'TTP has the classic pentad: microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, fever, neurologic symptoms, and renal dysfunction.'),
('71c33572-17be-52e0-9993-9a242bf0e685'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'TTP involves deficiency of:', '["Plasminogen resulting in decreased functional activity","ADAMTS13 (vWF cleaving protease)","Protein C mediated by specific receptor activation","Antithrombin III without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Factor VIII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADAMTS13 (vWF cleaving protease)', 'TTP results from deficiency of ADAMTS13, the protease that cleaves unusually large vWF multimers, causing platelet microthrombi.'),
('a020c927-4bd4-5bb8-a11f-8519b58f6667'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The most common blood group worldwide is:', '["A+","B","A subgroup","O","AB"]'::jsonb, 'O', 'Group O is the most common blood group worldwide (O+ is the most frequent phenotype), with the remaining groups distributed variably by population.'),
('171fd6eb-deca-5813-aa0b-14218c4cc9d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Universal donor for RBCs is:', '["Group B","Group O positive","Group O negative","Group AB","Group A"]'::jsonb, 'Group O negative', 'Group O-negative red cells lack A, B, and D antigens, so they can be transfused to recipients of any ABO/Rh type.'),
('5ea76b66-9e6b-5b45-a3ea-069cc22bd8f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Universal recipient for RBCs is:', '["Group B","Group A","Group AB","Group AB negative","Group O"]'::jsonb, 'Group AB', 'Group AB recipients lack anti-A and anti-B isohemagglutinins and can therefore receive red cells of any ABO group.'),
('75d3d0e0-e563-52cd-8f31-08c0694090ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Rh-positive means presence of:', '["e antigen","D antigen","c antigen","C antigen","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'D antigen', 'The Rh blood group is named positive when the D antigen is present on the red cell surface.'),
('bcd4e1c4-e63c-5552-9ce1-8c57ea80ac60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which cause is responsible for the most severe acute hemolytic transfusion reactions?', '["Bacterial contamination","Rh incompatibility","Volume overload","ABO incompatibility","Allergic reactions"]'::jsonb, 'ABO incompatibility', 'Mismatched ABO transfusion triggers preformed IgM isohemagglutinins that activate complement, causing massive intravascular hemolysis with renal failure and DIC.'),
('7e249da1-8f6c-5223-a155-de8c0660c90a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What are the hallmark complications of acute hemolytic transfusion reactions?', '["Fever, hemoglobinuria, renal failure, and DIC","Graft vs host disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allergic rash mediated by specific receptor activation","Febrile non-hemolytic reaction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anaphylaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fever, hemoglobinuria, renal failure, and DIC', 'Acute hemolytic transfusion reactions (ABO mismatch) present with fever, hemoglobinuria, renal failure, and DIC due to complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis.'),
('7a1352b9-6aca-57fa-a7a2-c78a69408b39'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction is caused by:', '["Antibodies against donor WBCs","Bacterial contamination","ABO mismatch","IgE","Complement activation"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies against donor WBCs', 'Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reactions occur when recipient antibodies react with donor leukocyte antigens, releasing pyrogenic cytokines; this is the most common transfusion reaction.'),
('c74207e2-3672-5506-a426-d8d739581210'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Plasma volume expanders include:', '["All of the above","Albumin","Normal saline","Dextran","Whole blood only"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Crystalloids (normal saline) and colloids (albumin, dextran) expand plasma volume; whole blood is not used primarily as a volume expander.'),
('b0d5045c-f108-55ca-8b85-0cd887e54a62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Dextran is:', '["An electrolyte","A synthetic polymer","A polysaccharide plasma expander","A protein","A sugar solution"]'::jsonb, 'A polysaccharide plasma expander', 'Dextran is a glucose polysaccharide colloid that acts as an osmotic plasma expander.'),
('d085b0ba-5734-5857-8ec1-dde931ec4e26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) contains:', '["Only Factor VIII","Fibrinogen only","Only vitamin K factors","All coagulation factors","Albumin only"]'::jsonb, 'All coagulation factors', 'Fresh frozen plasma contains all clotting factors, albumin, and other plasma proteins, making it ideal for factor replacement.'),
('5c91e8c3-52f7-5ac3-9bb7-c22e5567eec3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Cryoprecipitate is rich in:', '["Factor IX resulting in decreased functional activity","Albumin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Platelets mediated by specific receptor activation","Factor VIII and fibrinogen","Factor VII leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII and fibrinogen', 'Cryoprecipitate is enriched in factor VIII, von Willebrand factor, fibrinogen, and factor XIII, and is used for fibrinogen replacement and hemophilia A.'),
('300e9f8d-2d33-5b45-a442-e7550b0125a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Platelet transfusion is most clearly indicated in which situation?', '["Platelet count <10,000/μL while stable","Active life-threatening bleeding from ITP","Chronic ITP stable on steroids","Mild bruising with a normal count","No bleeding with count >30,000/μL"]'::jsonb, 'Active life-threatening bleeding from ITP', 'In ITP, platelet transfusion is reserved for life-threatening bleeding (e.g., CNS or GI) because circulating antiplatelet antibodies also destroy transfused platelets.'),
('368d11c6-f7e7-5057-82e7-15811bfd5bb5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The half-life of transfused platelets is about:', '["14 days","1-2 days","21 days","3-5 days","7-10 days"]'::jsonb, '3-5 days', 'Transfused platelets have a shortened survival of about 3-5 days due to storage injury and immune clearance, versus 7-10 days in vivo.'),
('bc9e777a-44a0-51d9-8f2d-a5372177bbe9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Autotransfusion is:', '["Synthetic blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Donation for specific recipient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collection and reinfusion of patient''s own blood","Cross-matching without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From family member leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Collection and reinfusion of patient''s own blood', 'Autotransfusion collects and reinfuses the patient''s own blood, avoiding alloimmunization and transfusion-transmitted infection.'),
('37b6f43c-7c08-50d0-8405-0e81ebbd1444'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Erythropoietin doping in athletes:', '["Increases RBC mass and O2 carrying capacity","Decreases hematocrit","Impairs renal EPO sensing","Reduces blood viscosity","Decreases endurance"]'::jsonb, 'Increases RBC mass and O2 carrying capacity', 'Erythropoietin stimulates erythropoiesis, raising red cell mass and oxygen-carrying capacity to benefit aerobic performance, while also increasing blood viscosity and thrombotic risk.'),
('6aa4f128-b33c-50cc-aa15-618c18053170'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The reticulocyte production index (RPI) corrects for:', '["Hematocrit","Reticulocyte maturation time shift","Iron stores","Anemia severity","Bone marrow response"]'::jsonb, 'Reticulocyte maturation time shift', 'The RPI corrects the reticulocyte percentage for anemia and for the longer maturation time that premature reticulocytes spend in blood, giving a truer marrow response.'),
('d468956d-ee55-554c-b466-a86de8071c51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which peripheral smear finding describes red blood cells of unequal sizes?', '["Hypochromia","Anisocytosis (unequal RBC size)","Microcytosis","Macrocytosis","Poikilocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Anisocytosis (unequal RBC size)', 'Anisocytosis is variation in red cell size; marked heterogeneity produces a wide red cell distribution width (RDW).'),
('5521a4fa-5884-58b6-a4f5-1694680fc16a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The mean corpuscular volume (MCV) normal range is:', '["120-140 fL","60-80 fL","40-60 fL","100-120 fL","80-100 fL"]'::jsonb, '80-100 fL', 'MCV is normally 80-100 fL; below 80 fL is microcytic and above 100 fL is macrocytic.'),
('8cd7c419-3af4-5fb7-90e7-e3e91d0b9f8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Mean corpuscular hemoglobin (MCH) normal range:', '["27-31 pg","17-23 pg","10-17 pg","37-42 pg","32-36 pg"]'::jsonb, '27-31 pg', 'MCH of 27-31 pg is the average hemoglobin content per red cell; hypochromic anemias lower the MCH.'),
('0837d5f9-8c2c-5a89-bddf-aa1d54a36bc7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The normal mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) is:', '["32-36 g/dL","22-26 g/dL","42-46 g/dL","27-31 g/dL","37-41 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '32-36 g/dL', 'MCHC, the hemoglobin concentration per volume of packed red cells, is normally 32-36 g/dL; it is low in hypochromic anemias.'),
('345c2bc2-0d5b-59a2-b009-1954ccfb66e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'ACE inhibitors block:', '["Ang I -> Ang II","Ang II -> Ang III","Bradykinin degradation","Renin release","Aldosterone synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Ang I -> Ang II', 'Reduces vasoconstriction and aldosterone.'),
('81d75e43-8b56-53d0-9b01-4d2bd1f90a70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Anemia of chronic disease is typically:', '["Macrocytic","Microcytic hypochromic","Sideroblastic","Megaloblastic","Normocytic normochromic"]'::jsonb, 'Normocytic normochromic', 'Anemia of chronic disease is usually normocytic normochromic (occasionally mildly microcytic), driven by hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration and a blunted EPO response.'),
('711b8d85-fded-5e54-81e6-572ec6f25a46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Sideroblastic anemia is characterized by:', '["Target cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Helmet cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Schistocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Ring sideroblasts in bone marrow","Spherocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ring sideroblasts in bone marrow', 'Ring sideroblasts, erythroid precursors with iron-laden mitochondria encircling the nucleus, define sideroblastic anemia.'),
('2b75edac-fece-54a0-97a1-33cf5c4e84fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Pancytopenia is seen in:', '["Hypersplenism","All of the above","Aplastic anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Only ITP"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Pancytopenia complicates aplastic anemia, megaloblastic anemia, and hypersplenism, though not isolated ITP.'),
('12581dd8-e4b9-5871-a8fe-e916359a0c0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What is the mechanism of cytopenias in hypersplenism associated with splenomegaly?', '["Polycythemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancytopenia due to increased sequestration","Erythrocytosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Thrombocytosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Leukocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancytopenia due to increased sequestration', 'In hypersplenism, the enlarged spleen sequesters and destroys circulating cells, causing pancytopenia despite normal marrow function.'),
('90489906-3ee0-5613-8933-8015b5386744'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Polycythemia vera is a:', '["Secondary polycythemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Relative polycythemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Benign condition mediated by specific receptor activation","Reactive process without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Myeloproliferative neoplasm (JAK2 V617F mutation)"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloproliferative neoplasm (JAK2 V617F mutation)', 'Polycythemia vera is a myeloproliferative neoplasm, almost always driven by the JAK2 V617F mutation causing EPO-independent erythroid expansion.'),
('ad3d931d-7002-5508-bf6b-96363df95b68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Secondary polycythemia is due to:', '["Myelofibrosis","EPO-secreting tumor","All of the above","Smoking","Chronic hypoxia (COPD, high altitude)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Secondary polycythemia results from appropriate or inappropriate erythropoietin overproduction, as with chronic hypoxia, smoking, and EPO-secreting tumors.'),
('dd2d5c2c-e3fe-5aba-808a-96e7e750ac9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Relative polycythemia (spurious) is due to:', '["Bone marrow disease resulting in decreased functional activity","EPO excess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hemoconcentration from dehydration","Hypoxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoconcentration from dehydration', 'Relative (spurious) polycythemia reflects hemoconcentration from dehydration or burns, with a normal red cell mass.'),
('42c1f29a-9cf7-5344-a195-6303057271af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Leukocytosis is commonly seen in:', '["Allergy","Parasitic infection","Viral infection","Bacterial infection","Fungal infection"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial infection', 'Leukocytosis, predominantly neutrophilia, is the characteristic response to acute bacterial infections.'),
('b7b09186-edd0-5819-9b53-462b19e0dcee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Neutrophilia is prominent in:', '["Tuberculosis","Parasitic infections","Leishmaniasis","Bacterial infections","Viral infections"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial infections', 'Neutrophilia is prominent in bacterial infections; eosinophilia, in contrast, marks allergy and helminthic parasites.'),
('a34114ec-1e1e-5e7c-afda-30093b93b36c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which infections are the most common causes of lymphocytosis in children?', '["Fungal infections","Parasitic infections","Bacterial infections","Whooping cough and viral infections","Neutropenia"]'::jsonb, 'Whooping cough and viral infections', 'Bordetella pertussis (whooping cough) and viruses such as EBV and CMV are the classic causes of lymphocytosis in children.'),
('d18022ac-b980-5098-88e4-e635128043d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Eosinophilia is characteristic of:', '["Viral infections resulting in decreased functional activity","Allergic disorders and parasitic infections","Bacterial infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Fungal infections without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neutropenia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Allergic disorders and parasitic infections', 'Eosinophilia characterizes allergic disorders and helminthic infections, where IL-5 drives eosinophil expansion.'),
('9311f720-66de-5ed8-b98a-76f147a35c05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Basophilia is seen in:', '["Myeloproliferative disorders (CML)","Thyroid disease","Bacterial infections","Parasitic infections","Allergic reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloproliferative disorders (CML)', 'Basophilia occurs in myeloproliferative disorders such as CML, reflecting excessive marrow cell production.'),
('867b46c0-c9a5-5358-8130-318fd0b9d66e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Monocytosis is seen in:', '["Trauma resulting in decreased functional activity","Surgery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acute infections mediated by specific receptor activation","Tuberculosis and chronic infections","Allergy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberculosis and chronic infections', 'Monocytosis accompanies tuberculosis and other chronic infections, where the monocyte-macrophage response predominates.'),
('2ef7d4cf-973c-5040-b3e3-dd4955e04ccd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A 60-year-old male presents with fatigue, splenomegaly, and a very high leukocyte count with left shift. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["Polycythemia vera","Myelofibrosis","Leukemoid reaction","Acute infection","CML"]'::jsonb, 'CML', 'Marked leukocytosis with immature myeloid forms, splenomegaly, and the BCR-ABL (Philadelphia) translocation characterize chronic myeloid leukemia.'),
('7186afe8-3c64-5992-8a8a-479baa9d38bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Leukemoid reaction is:', '["Malignant leukemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Anemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone marrow failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Benign condition mimicking leukemia with high WBC","Reactive thrombocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign condition mimicking leukemia with high WBC', 'A leukemoid reaction is a benign, extreme leukocytosis (often >50,000/μL) that mimics leukemia but is due to severe infection or inflammation.'),
('dae35b83-f603-5291-9600-0df04b68cd70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Multiple myeloma is characterized by:', '["Neutropenia resulting in decreased functional activity","Thrombocytosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Polycythemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Monoclonal gammopathy with lytic bone lesions","Polyclonal gammopathy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monoclonal gammopathy with lytic bone lesions', 'Myeloma features monoclonal plasma cells producing a paraprotein, plus lytic bone lesions, anemia, renal dysfunction, and hypercalcemia.'),
('be301732-71e9-5439-bf36-4373a0ca6a62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome is X-linked with:', '["Complement deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Neutropenia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thrombocytopenia, eczema, recurrent infections","SCID without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgA deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombocytopenia, eczema, recurrent infections', 'Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome (WAS mutation) is X-linked, combining thrombocytopenia (small platelets), eczema, and recurrent infections.'),
('f8ced53a-3880-5ebf-9181-fa668318a9d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Ataxia telangiectasia involves:', '["ATM gene mutation with IgA deficiency and cerebellar ataxia","Complement deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NK deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","T cell defect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgG deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ATM gene mutation with IgA deficiency and cerebellar ataxia', 'Ataxia telangiectasia (ATM defect) causes cerebellar ataxia, telangiectasias, and IgA deficiency with marked radiosensitivity.'),
('65886bb0-3466-5488-90e8-1e51b18f371d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Hyper-IgM syndrome is due to:', '["IgM deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Defective CD40L-CD40 interaction (X-linked)","IgE deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","IgG excess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgA deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defective CD40L-CD40 interaction (X-linked)', 'X-linked hyper-IgM syndrome results from defective CD40 ligand, which prevents isotype switching so that only IgM is produced.'),
('44916a31-6f92-5aff-a2b9-984fe8cf2fef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Common variable immunodeficiency (CVID) features:', '["Normal immunoglobulins","Only T cell defect","Phagocyte defect","Complement deficiency","Low immunoglobulins, recurrent infections"]'::jsonb, 'Low immunoglobulins, recurrent infections', 'CVID is characterized by low serum immunoglobulins with recurrent sinopulmonary infections and autoimmunity.'),
('035da41c-43f8-5220-b6bf-ecfca0f4e577'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Anemia of chronic disease is mediated mainly by which mechanism?', '["B12 deficiency","Increased hepcidin trapping iron","Blood loss","Folate deficiency","Hemolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased hepcidin trapping iron', 'Inflammation raises hepcidin, which degrades ferroportin and traps iron in stores, limiting iron supply for erythropoiesis.'),
('dfc2a50a-9824-5ddc-a739-93f196ef2745'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Hepcidin is produced by:', '["Bone marrow","Spleen","Gut","Liver","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Hepcidin is synthesized by hepatocytes and is the master regulator of iron absorption and release from stores.'),
('2922f99d-7fcd-5bd3-9585-d90b74d6d067'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What laboratory marker is depleted early in iron deficiency anemia, before hemoglobin falls?', '["Bone marrow iron stores","Hemoglobin","MCV","Serum iron","Total iron-binding capacity (TIBC)"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow iron stores', 'In iron deficiency, marrow iron stores are depleted first; serum ferritin (reflecting stores) falls before anemia develops.'),
('fd75071a-451d-5a68-999c-63a36fe3c393'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'In iron deficiency the first parameter to fall is:', '["Marrow iron stores","MCV","Serum ferritin","Hemoglobin","Serum iron"]'::jsonb, 'Serum ferritin', 'Serum ferritin, mirroring body iron stores, is the earliest parameter to fall in iron deficiency, preceding low serum iron and hemoglobin.'),
('378c6f31-295a-53cd-ae97-5373ae6afa46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which embryonic hemoglobin is composed of two zeta and two epsilon chains (ζ2ε2)?', '["HbF (α2γ2)","Hb Gower-1 (ζ2ε2)","HbA (α2β2)","HbH (β4)","HbA2 (α2δ2)"]'::jsonb, 'Hb Gower-1 (ζ2ε2)', 'Gower-1 (ζ2ε2) is the earliest embryonic hemoglobin; it is later replaced by HbF (α2γ2) during fetal life.'),
('1cdf1f1a-1b1c-5d00-8965-0cfaae1e653a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'HbF, the predominant hemoglobin of fetal life, is composed of which chain combination?', '["α2β2","α2γ2","α2δ2","β4","ζ2ε2"]'::jsonb, 'α2γ2', 'Hemoglobin F consists of two alpha and two gamma chains (α2γ2); its higher O2 affinity favors O2 transfer across the placenta.'),
('cea77c96-5f2d-5951-bb02-a16c6799f76c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'HbA1c is formed by which post-translational modification of hemoglobin A?', '["Phosphorylation","Mutation","Oxidation","Acetylation","Glycation of HbA beta chain"]'::jsonb, 'Glycation of HbA beta chain', 'HbA1c results from non-enzymatic glycation of the N-terminus of the beta-globin chain, proportional to average blood glucose over about 3 months.'),
('42bebad6-7ae5-5a84-a970-0ba9ee593807'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Methemoglobin is:', '["Carboxyhemoglobin","Deoxygenated Hb","Fe2+ (ferrous) normal","Fe3+ (ferric) unable to bind O2","Sulfhemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Fe3+ (ferric) unable to bind O2', 'In methemoglobin, iron is oxidized to Fe3+, which cannot bind O2, causing functional anemia and cyanosis.'),
('6ab48dd6-e902-5ed5-8fef-30e1ea690cc2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Carboxyhemoglobin has affinity for CO how much more than O2?', '["1000 times","100 times","200-250 times","50 times","500 times"]'::jsonb, '200-250 times', 'Hemoglobin''s affinity for CO is about 200-250 times its affinity for O2, so CO readily displaces O2 and causes tissue hypoxia.'),
('779d2c07-6a2f-51ec-9e5a-f3de6faf89ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve is shifted right by:', '["Increased H+ (decreased pH)","Increased CO2 (Bohr effect)","Increased 2,3-BPG","Increased temperature","Decreased 2,3-BPG"]'::jsonb, 'Increased 2,3-BPG', '2,3-BPG (along with H+, CO2, and temperature) stabilizes the low-affinity T state of hemoglobin, shifting the O2 dissociation curve rightward and enhancing O2 unloading at tissues.'),
('5d1cc4c9-3927-512f-af76-92ee59243ab4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', '2,3-BPG is produced in:', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","RBCs (via Rapoport-Luebering shunt)","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Muscle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RBCs (via Rapoport-Luebering shunt)', '2,3-BPG is generated in red cells by the Rapoport-Luebering shunt, a bypass step in glycolysis that does not generate ATP.'),
('6221cf99-9b69-5bc7-a87d-11459e2e00e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Haldane effect describes:', '["CO2 binding to deoxygenated Hb (Hb buffers H+)","O2 binding to Hb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CO2 production mediated by specific receptor activation","Chloride shift without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 consumption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CO2 binding to deoxygenated Hb (Hb buffers H+)', 'The Haldane effect states that deoxygenated hemoglobin carries more CO2 (as carbamino compounds and via enhanced H+ buffering), promoting CO2 uptake in tissues.'),
('531470df-a1a6-51f7-863f-539f5ac42dca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The oxygen content of blood depends on:', '["Hemoglobin concentration","All of the above","Only SaO2","O2 dissolved in plasma","O2 saturation"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Blood O2 content equals (1.34 x Hb x SaO2) plus dissolved O2, so it depends on hemoglobin concentration, saturation, and dissolved O2.'),
('f59f21e7-766d-5c9b-bea5-31fa5be2be9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which of the following are being developed as artificial oxygen carriers (blood substitutes)?', '["Cross-linked hemoglobin","PEG-conjugated hemoglobin","Perfluorocarbons","Saline only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Blood substitutes include chemically modified hemoglobins, PEG-conjugated hemoglobins, and perfluorocarbons, all designed to carry O2.'),
('0176121a-4750-5613-90c2-4f93b7355d11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Perfluorocarbons carry O2 by:', '["High affinity","Chemical binding","Dissolving O2 (not binding)","O2 consumption","O2 production"]'::jsonb, 'Dissolving O2 (not binding)', 'Perfluorocarbons physically dissolve O2 in proportion to PaO2, rather than binding it chemically like hemoglobin.'),
('19518989-9cac-514d-9fa3-28ad583e7169'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Cross-matching involves:', '["Checking for agglutination","Compatibility testing","Mixing donor RBCs with recipient serum","Only ABO typing","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Cross-matching is the pre-transfusion compatibility test in which donor red cells are mixed with recipient serum to check for agglutination, alongside ABO/Rh typing.'),
('9f489edc-e780-5799-8179-dc3b5bfcf190'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Incompatible blood transfusion causes:', '["No significant effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Intravascular hemolysis by complement activation","Allergy mediated by specific receptor activation","Fever only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Extravascular hemolysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intravascular hemolysis by complement activation', 'ABO-incompatible transfusion causes intravascular hemolysis: preformed IgM binds the recipient''s own complement (C5b-9) to lyse donor red cells.'),
('d41207a5-3f0a-5548-922a-ff5aaf177b00'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction characteristically becomes evident how soon after transfusion?', '["Days after transfusion due to anamnestic antibody response","Immediately accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","After 1 year mediated by specific receptor activation","At 1 hour without affecting the primary tissue pathway","After 1 month leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Days after transfusion due to anamnestic antibody response', 'A delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction appears days to two weeks after transfusion when an anamnestic antibody response (commonly anti-Kidd) destroys donor red cells.'),
('e67a0ee7-bb6c-559d-9069-ce148e5083bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'A severely immunocompromised patient who receives non-irradiated blood develops fever, skin rash, and liver dysfunction. Which complication is most likely?', '["Transfusion-associated graft vs host disease (TA-GVHD)","Allergic reaction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Febrile reaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Volume overload without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Delayed hemolysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transfusion-associated graft vs host disease (TA-GVHD)', 'TA-GVHD occurs when transfused donor T lymphocytes engraft and attack the immunocompromised host; it is prevented by gamma-irradiation of blood.'),
('135ab73c-fe5d-52e6-8265-97f608cb0441'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) is due to:', '["Hemolysis resulting in decreased functional activity","Anti-HLA/anti-neutrophil antibodies in donor plasma","Volume overload mediated by specific receptor activation","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Allergy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-HLA/anti-neutrophil antibodies in donor plasma', 'TRALI is caused by donor antibodies against recipient HLA or neutrophil antigens that activate neutrophils, causing non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema.'),
('753fe3bf-90a1-5147-82c5-d82ce6779d87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The splenic red pulp functions in:', '["Iron recycling","RBC filtration and removal","Only immunity","All of the above","Immunity"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Splenic red pulp filters and removes aged or damaged red cells, recycles iron, and harbors macrophages involved in immunity.'),
('b3dedf64-c223-520a-8879-e6bd03ad7e4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which organ is the primary site of bilirubin metabolism and conjugation?', '["Kidney","Lung","Liver","Spleen","Brain"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Hepatocytes conjugate unconjugated bilirubin with glucuronic acid via UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, then excrete it into bile.'),
('09f0c374-2e71-5b26-870f-cdd38cd76727'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which organ is responsible for the majority of erythropoietin (EPO) production in adults?', '["Kidney","Liver","Spleen","Brain","Lung"]'::jsonb, 'Kidney', 'In adults about 90% of erythropoietin is made by peritubular cells of the renal cortex; the liver contributes substantially only in fetal life.'),
('21f40bc0-922a-53e8-994a-5f63c1b32477'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Osteoclasts are derived from:', '["Monocyte/macrophage lineage","T cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NK cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutrophils without affecting the primary tissue pathway","B cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monocyte/macrophage lineage', 'Osteoclasts are multinucleated cells of monocyte/macrophage lineage that resorb bone under RANKL control.'),
('9432ffdd-160a-5988-bfae-751a46a41144'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The mononuclear phagocyte system includes:', '["Monocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells","Only neutrophils accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only dendritic cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Only monocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only macrophages leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monocytes, macrophages, and dendritic cells', 'The mononuclear phagocyte system comprises monocytes and their tissue derivatives (macrophages and dendritic cells), which phagocytose and present antigen.'),
('6798fe6e-ad30-5abf-a925-0a1f63a102fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Dendritic cells are specialized:', '["Antigen-presenting cells","B cells","NK cells","T cells","Phagocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen-presenting cells', 'Dendritic cells are the most potent antigen-presenting cells, priming naive T cells in lymph nodes.'),
('0445dd5f-b04c-5802-92e5-b070e2e3af58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Langerhans cells are dendritic cells in:', '["Skin","Lymph nodes","Brain","Spleen","Blood"]'::jsonb, 'Skin', 'Epidermal Langerhans cells are immature dendritic cells that capture skin antigens and migrate to lymph nodes to present them.'),
('8d44e0b7-3b10-50e0-a289-a38c5f886ce4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which lymphocyte lineage is responsible for antibody (immunoglobulin) production?', '["T cells","Neutrophils","B cells","NK cells","Macrophages"]'::jsonb, 'B cells', 'B cells differentiate into antibody-secreting plasma cells, providing humoral immunity.'),
('6cc52e53-2e72-5dae-85b4-bee532089757'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'C-reactive protein (CRP) is synthesized by:', '["Kidney","Bone marrow","Macrophages","Liver","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'CRP is an acute-phase protein synthesized by the liver under IL-6 stimulation; it opsonizes pathogens and activates complement.'),
('897cfac4-aef6-5fd5-8cfc-67130281267e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Interleukin-6 is a major:', '["Anti-inflammatory cytokine","Growth factor","Pro-inflammatory cytokine","Hormone","Chemokine"]'::jsonb, 'Pro-inflammatory cytokine', 'IL-6 is a pro-inflammatory cytokine that drives the acute-phase response, fever, and inflammation.'),
('f6de0ef1-7a11-5969-af41-88699594d126'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which immune cells are the primary producers of cytokines that drive cell-mediated immune responses?', '["B cells","Mast cells","Neutrophils","NK cells","Macrophages and T cells"]'::jsonb, 'Macrophages and T cells', 'Macrophages and T cells (especially Th1 cells) produce cytokines such as IFN-gamma and TNF-alpha that drive cell-mediated immunity.'),
('d98ad565-f4b6-5c78-b7e8-0bf17d559b5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Interferon-gamma (IFN-γ) is produced by:', '["B cells","Eosinophils","Th1 cells and NK cells","Th2 cells","Neutrophils"]'::jsonb, 'Th1 cells and NK cells', 'IFN-gamma, produced by Th1 cells and NK cells, activates macrophages and drives cell-mediated immunity.'),
('36ed0fb9-ead5-58b3-b4f9-f0db37bbbbb9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Interleukin-4 is produced by:', '["NK cells","Macrophages","Neutrophils","Th1 cells","Th2 cells and mast cells"]'::jsonb, 'Th2 cells and mast cells', 'IL-4, from Th2 cells and mast cells, drives IgE class switching and type 2 (allergic and anti-helminth) immunity.'),
('7b5fd3e6-02a0-5385-8307-b07fd5cf08a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which cytokine is a major anti-inflammatory mediator that inhibits pro-inflammatory cytokine production?', '["Tumor necrosis factor-alpha","Interferon-gamma","Interleukin-10 (IL-10)","Interleukin-6","Interleukin-2"]'::jsonb, 'Interleukin-10 (IL-10)', 'IL-10 is the major anti-inflammatory cytokine, suppressing pro-inflammatory cytokine production by macrophages and Th1 cells.'),
('99c0fe6f-fbd2-551d-80b8-d3c0b030575f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The chemokine family regulates:', '["Apoptosis","Cell division","Gene expression","Metabolism","Leukocyte trafficking"]'::jsonb, 'Leukocyte trafficking', 'Chemokines are chemoattractant cytokines that direct leukocyte trafficking to sites of infection and to lymphoid tissues.'),
('5d5551f2-21bf-5b53-a160-105cd452ccd5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The blood-CSF barrier is at:', '["Ependyma","Choroid plexus epithelium","Dura mater","Pia mater","Arachnoid mater"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus epithelium', 'The blood-CSF barrier is formed by tight junctions of the choroid plexus epithelium, which control CSF composition.'),
('d68a9871-e0d6-58b6-85a8-8856a5f251e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Choroid plexus secretes CSF by:', '["Simple filtration resulting in decreased functional activity","Osmosis only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Diffusion mediated by specific receptor activation","Active transport of Na+ with water following","Ultrafiltration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Active transport of Na+ with water following', 'Choroid plexus cells actively secrete Na+ into the ventricles, and water follows osmotically, generating about 500 mL of CSF daily.'),
('1694215f-6cca-5d8f-a4ab-9291ff6d4296'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'What is the normal pathway of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) flow from the lateral ventricles to the subarachnoid space?', '["Foramen of Monro → third ventricle → aqueduct → fourth ventricle → foramina Luschka/Magendie","Directly to veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reverse direction mediated by specific receptor activation","To spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Through brain tissue leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen of Monro → third ventricle → aqueduct → fourth ventricle → foramina Luschka/Magendie', 'CSF flows from the lateral ventricles through the foramina of Monro, third ventricle, aqueduct of Sylvius, and fourth ventricle to exit via the foramina of Luschka and Magendie.'),
('e8c086e4-a4f3-5834-b27d-a22b7d88133d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The hysteresis (different inflation vs. deflation limbs) of the lung pressure-volume curve is due primarily to:', '["Renal physiology","Pulmonary surfactant","Endocrinology","CSF absorption curve","Cardiac function"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary surfactant', 'Surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension more on deflation than on inflation, producing the hysteresis of the pulmonary pressure-volume loop.'),
('243b258e-75f8-5ab4-9d00-c3da45da8186'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The glucose concentration of normal cerebrospinal fluid is approximately what fraction of blood glucose?', '["Triple blood glucose","Double blood glucose","50-60% of blood glucose","Same as blood","10% of blood glucose"]'::jsonb, '50-60% of blood glucose', 'CSF glucose is normally about 50-60% (roughly two-thirds) of plasma glucose; it falls disproportionately in bacterial meningitis.'),
('ec3df385-4013-5f68-8597-447515834706'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'CSF protein normal is:', '["15-45 mg/dL",">1000 mg/dL","500-1000 mg/dL","100-200 mg/dL","<10 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '15-45 mg/dL', 'Normal CSF protein is 15-45 mg/dL; it rises in meningitis, hemorrhage, and meningeal malignancy.'),
('868f99a0-ec80-59c6-98b0-d79512f5d6d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'CSF pressure in lateral decubitus position is:', '["200-400 mm H2O",">600 mm H2O","50-100 mm H2O","400-600 mm H2O","100-200 mm H2O"]'::jsonb, '100-200 mm H2O', 'Normal CSF opening pressure in the lateral decubitus position is 100-200 mm H2O; values above 200 mm H2O suggest raised intracranial pressure.'),
('99ef85e7-57f0-584b-835d-246502cc7fbc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Hydrocephalus refers to:', '["Subdural hematoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased CSF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased CSF volume with ventricular enlargement","Brain swelling without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stroke leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased CSF volume with ventricular enlargement', 'Hydrocephalus is an abnormal accumulation of CSF causing ventricular enlargement; it may be communicating or non-communicating.'),
('5dc91516-3d2d-5344-9331-9730d3ddaf60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which mechanism of hydrocephalus involves choroid plexus papilloma producing excess CSF?', '["Underabsorption only","Overproduction only","Pseudotumor","Blockage within ventricles","Obstruction outside ventricular system"]'::jsonb, 'Overproduction only', 'Choroid plexus papilloma is a rare cause of communicating hydrocephalus due to CSF overproduction.'),
('1c699c1b-5b3e-59a3-8607-5ec59ccdc26f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Non-communicating hydrocephalus involves:', '["Obstruction within ventricular system","Underproduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pseudotumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Impaired absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Overproduction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstruction within ventricular system', 'Non-communicating (obstructive) hydrocephalus results from blockage within the ventricular system, such as aqueduct stenosis or a tumor.'),
('5f15b044-e989-524f-8b5f-fd3f0b9290c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus has triad:', '["Fever resulting in decreased functional activity","Gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, dementia","Nuchal rigidity mediated by specific receptor activation","Headache, vomiting, papilledema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Seizures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, dementia', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus classically presents with gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and dementia, due to impaired CSF absorption.'),
('41e74249-d137-5572-9005-d3c616455438'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (pseudotumor cerebri) is most accurately characterized by which statement?', '["May cause visual loss","Increased ICP without mass or hydrocephalus","All of the above","More common in obese women","Papilledema and headache"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Pseudotumor cerebri is raised intracranial pressure without a mass or hydrocephalus, predominantly in obese women, causing headaches, papilledema, and visual loss.'),
('ac43b9e8-2711-5ebc-a685-6ef1a8e4982f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which class of molecules on the RBC membrane determines ABO blood group antigens?', '["Glycolipids on RBC membrane","Phospholipids","Nucleic acids","Cholesterol","Proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Glycolipids on RBC membrane', 'ABO antigens are oligosaccharide structures present on glycolipids (and glycoproteins) of the red cell membrane.'),
('2de10693-8cc4-5334-b084-cdb418be9484'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'H-substance is the precursor for:', '["A antigen only","ABO antigens","B antigen only","Lewis antigen","Rh antigen"]'::jsonb, 'ABO antigens', 'The H substance (fucose-containing precursor) is converted to A and B antigens by specific glycosyltransferases.'),
('22ba8556-b326-5b23-9d9e-fdf4d972b6c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Bombay phenotype (Oh) is:', '["Weak B expression resulting in decreased functional activity","Rh antigen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Universal donor mediated by specific receptor activation","Lack of H substance (cannot form A or B)","Weak A expression leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of H substance (cannot form A or B)', 'The Bombay phenotype lacks H substance, so no A or B antigens can be formed; these patients have anti-H antibodies and can only receive Bombay blood.'),
('0ab58b5a-0fcf-515f-b9b1-0f5b9a634b0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Bombay phenotype individuals can only receive:', '["AB blood","A blood","O blood","B blood","Bombay blood (Oh)"]'::jsonb, 'Bombay blood (Oh)', 'Bombay (Oh) patients form anti-H, anti-A, and anti-B antibodies, so only Bombay blood (lacking all of these antigens) is safe.'),
('7997614a-16fe-53bd-8828-e1c7f8d94a15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which blood group antigen system, besides Rh, is most commonly associated with severe hemolytic disease of the newborn?', '["Rh (D)","Duffy","Kidd","Kell","Lewis"]'::jsonb, 'Kell', 'After Rh, anti-Kell antibodies are the most common cause of severe HDN and suppress the erythroid marrow as well as lysing red cells.'),
('0fd4c44c-7d28-5347-a9b1-0de044d31b57'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Kell null phenotype is associated with:', '["RBC fragility resulting in decreased functional activity","McLeod syndrome (neurologic + acanthocytosis)","Mild anemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Thrombocytopenia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence of symptoms leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'McLeod syndrome (neurologic + acanthocytosis)', 'Kell-null (McLeod) phenotype, from loss of the Kx-supported Kell antigen, causes acanthocytosis associated with neurologic disease.'),
('c6095c80-76d9-5e97-be9a-8319b5e4dca0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Duffy-negative individuals are resistant to:', '["P. falciparum","Fungal infections","Viral infections","Bacterial infections","Plasmodium vivax malaria"]'::jsonb, 'Plasmodium vivax malaria', 'Duffy-negative red cells lack the Duffy antigen, the receptor used by Plasmodium vivax to invade, conferring resistance.'),
('d41234fd-0115-59b3-ad3d-a55f30896c45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Complement receptor 1 (CR1) on RBCs transports:', '["Immune complexes to liver","Antibodies","Chemokines","Cytokines","Oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'Immune complexes to liver', 'CR1 on erythrocytes binds and escorts C3b-opsonized immune complexes to the liver and spleen for removal.'),
('8d8ff44d-8bb4-59f1-bde2-719d6085dbb3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The Rh null phenotype causes:', '["Polycythemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Thrombocytopenia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neutropenia mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic hemolytic anemia with stomatocytes","No abnormality leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic hemolytic anemia with stomatocytes', 'Rh-null syndrome causes chronic hemolytic anemia with stomatocytes and a compensated membrane defect.'),
('ef71c75a-f95e-5f8d-87e3-98b1f2e33652'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Plasma viscosity depends mostly on:', '["Glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Albumin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrinogen and globulins","Cholesterol without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sodium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrinogen and globulins', 'Plasma viscosity depends chiefly on large proteins, fibrinogen and globulins, which also determine the ESR.'),
('941b9b55-a673-53c4-8dac-01887bf4b9c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The buffy coat in centrifuged blood contains:', '["RBCs","Complement","Fibrin","WBCs and platelets","Plasma"]'::jsonb, 'WBCs and platelets', 'After centrifugation, the buffy coat is the thin layer of leukocytes and platelets lying between plasma and the red cell sediment.'),
('21ebe40c-4bac-5a2a-8c74-9a4c270b7c07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which laboratory tests evaluate the extrinsic and intrinsic coagulation pathways, respectively?', '["Electrolytes","Blood counts","Blood gases","Glucose","PT and aPTT tests"]'::jsonb, 'PT and aPTT tests', 'PT probes the extrinsic and common pathways (warfarin monitoring), while aPTT probes the intrinsic and common pathways (heparin monitoring, hemophilia).'),
('a33a1894-e454-5f46-ab4f-186f05114e8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Heparin acts by binding:', '["Calcium","Factor VIII","Fibrinogen","Prothrombin","Antithrombin III"]'::jsonb, 'Antithrombin III', 'Heparin''s anticoagulant effect depends on its binding and activation of antithrombin III.'),
('abcea7d2-6add-5e33-a477-cd9853aa8ba9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Heparin is used in tubes for:', '["CBC","Coagulation tests","Chemistry","ESR","Blood gases and ammonia"]'::jsonb, 'Blood gases and ammonia', 'Heparinized (green-top) tubes are used for blood gas and ammonia analysis because heparin inhibits clotting without altering these analytes.'),
('cf4d5b4c-7c51-5c83-8b7e-f0eaedc78512'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Blood group antibodies (isohemagglutinins) are:', '["IgG resulting in decreased functional activity","Autoantibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IgE mediated by specific receptor activation","IgD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Naturally occurring IgM antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'Naturally occurring IgM antibodies', 'Anti-A and anti-B isohemagglutinins are naturally occurring IgM antibodies produced against the A and B antigens a person lacks.'),
('13f8d8b4-b417-57ea-844b-7e4ddd68b084'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The direct antiglobulin test (Coombs test) detects:', '["Free antibodies in serum","Complement only","Cytokines","Antigen-antibody complexes","Antibody-coated RBCs in patient"]'::jsonb, 'Antibody-coated RBCs in patient', 'The direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test detects immunoglobulin or complement already coating the patient''s red cells (in vivo sensitization).'),
('36045fcf-831a-58df-89bf-42bc4c5a590f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The indirect Coombs test detects:', '["Antigens resulting in decreased functional activity","Cytokines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complement mediated by specific receptor activation","RBC-bound antibodies without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Free antibodies in patient serum"]'::jsonb, 'Free antibodies in patient serum', 'The indirect Coombs test detects free antibodies in serum (e.g., antenatal screening and cross-matching) by their ability to coat test red cells.'),
('40e8a6bd-6ffc-5122-8a67-3b294edb63dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'IgG antibodies directed against red cell antigens are implicated in which of the following?', '["Hereditary spherocytosis","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Hemolytic transfusion reaction","HDN (hemolytic disease of the newborn)","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Anti-red cell IgG mediates autoimmune hemolytic anemia, hemolytic disease of the newborn, and hemolytic transfusion reactions, all detected by a positive direct Coombs test.'),
('f2d0d008-48f5-5f12-93c6-58c8f8eaba00'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Warm AIHA is usually due to:', '["IgM cold agglutinins resulting in decreased functional activity","IgD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IgG autoantibodies reacting at 37°C","IgA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IgG autoantibodies reacting at 37°C', 'Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia is caused by IgG autoantibodies that react optimally at 37°C against red cell antigens, causing splenic sequestration.'),
('8a4cad03-ca79-59d0-bb17-7b0cb91c4c65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Cold agglutinin disease is due to:', '["IgA complement activation resulting in decreased functional activity","IgG warm antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IgM autoantibodies reacting at <37°C (e.g., Mycoplasma)","IgG cold agglutinins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgE leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'IgM autoantibodies reacting at <37°C (e.g., Mycoplasma)', 'Cold agglutinin disease is caused by IgM autoantibodies that agglutinate red cells at low temperatures, often after Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection.'),
('61427242-3cc5-511b-a95b-e044a4ab8f6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH) involves:', '["IgG warm resulting in decreased functional activity","IgM cold agglutinin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Donath-Landsteiner IgG antibody (anti-P)","Warm antibody without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C3 only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Donath-Landsteiner IgG antibody (anti-P)', 'PCH is mediated by the Donath-Landsteiner antibody, a biphasic IgG (anti-P) that hemolyzes red cells upon rewarming, often after viral infection or syphilis.'),
('b9ca98f1-9f3b-538b-9a9e-0c452d50fd49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Donath-Landsteiner antibody is seen in:', '["Sickle cell disease","Cold agglutinin disease","Drug-induced hemolysis","Warm AIHA","PCH (paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria)"]'::jsonb, 'PCH (paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria)', 'The Donath-Landsteiner (biphasic) antibody is characteristic of paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria.'),
('590d548c-e443-509a-84cb-25502378069e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Hemoglobinuria indicates:', '["Choluria resulting in decreased functional activity","Intravascular hemolysis (free Hb in urine)","Jaundice mediated by specific receptor activation","Extravascular hemolysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dehydration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intravascular hemolysis (free Hb in urine)', 'Hemoglobinuria indicates intravascular hemolysis: free hemoglobin exceeds haptoglobin binding and is filtered into the urine.'),
('2cc4effb-7b2d-5150-b6a2-eb5da5994626'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Haptoglobin binds:', '["Free hemoglobin","Free myoglobin","Ferritin","Transferrin","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Free hemoglobin', 'Haptoglobin tightly binds free hemoglobin; its low level in hemolysis reflects consumption by the hemoglobin-haptoglobin complex.'),
('cb799549-163f-50d9-ab9f-5586b9f1ea6b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which plasma protein specifically binds free heme released from hemoglobin catabolism to prevent oxidative tissue damage?', '["Haptoglobin","Hemopexin","Transferrin","Ferritin","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Hemopexin', 'Hemopexin scavenges free heme released from hemoglobin, whereas haptoglobin binds the intact globin-hemoglobin complex.'),
('fb579567-5f41-5d9b-a934-f6f3b0c97bac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which enzyme deficiency causes acute intermittent porphyria (AIP)?', '["Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","ALA dehydratase deficiency","Hemoglobin mutation","PBG deaminase deficiency (heme synthesis)","Porphyrin excess"]'::jsonb, 'PBG deaminase deficiency (heme synthesis)', 'AIP results from partial deficiency of PBG deaminase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase), causing accumulation of ALA and PBG.'),
('a6e2e070-e78f-539d-a94b-38083b66fb70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) involves:', '["PBG deaminase deficiency","Protoporphyrinogen oxidase","Coproporphyrinogen oxidase deficiency","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency","ALA synthase excess"]'::jsonb, 'Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency', 'Porphyria cutanea tarda is caused by uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency, the most common cutaneous porphyria, often triggered by alcohol or iron overload.'),
('f9e1edd9-fb38-529c-a024-33dd4cc4dc38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Heme synthesis occurs in:', '["Mitochondria and cytoplasm","Golgi","Ribosomes","Endoplasmic reticulum","Nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondria and cytoplasm', 'Heme synthesis begins in mitochondria (ALA formation) and continues in the cytoplasm, with iron insertion by ferrochelatase returning to the mitochondria.'),
('fa8bce1c-8d0b-5254-928d-7d21d0be80f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which two enzymes in the heme synthesis pathway are inhibited by lead?', '["PBG deaminase","Coproporphyrinogen oxidase","ALA synthase","ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase","Uroporphyrinogen synthase"]'::jsonb, 'ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase', 'Lead inhibits both ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, impairing heme synthesis and producing basophilic stippling of red cells.'),
('f0e048b1-12c7-50c2-8152-7fe0e844b42f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The ZPP (Zinc protoporphyrin) is elevated in:', '["Hemolytic anemia","Iron deficiency and lead poisoning","Folate deficiency","Aplastic anemia","B12 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Iron deficiency and lead poisoning', 'Zinc protoporphyrin rises when iron is limiting or ferrochelatase is inhibited (iron deficiency, lead poisoning), since zinc is inserted instead of iron.'),
('8dfe6eb1-91c9-5cca-9cf8-27929fa9c88b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which enzyme deficiency causes congenital erythropoietic porphyria (CEP)?', '["Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","ALA synthase","PBG deaminase","Uroporphyrinogen III synthase deficiency","Protoporphyrinogen oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Uroporphyrinogen III synthase deficiency', 'Congenital erythropoietic porphyria (Gunther disease) results from uroporphyrinogen III synthase deficiency, causing severe photosensitivity and red urine.'),
('f6216f7c-593f-5fc8-9651-69528dbf5402'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF) is used to:', '["Treat thrombocytopenia","Stimulate erythropoiesis","Arouse immune system","Treat anemia","Treat neutropenia after chemotherapy"]'::jsonb, 'Treat neutropenia after chemotherapy', 'G-CSF stimulates the granulocyte lineage and is used to treat neutropenia after chemotherapy or in congenital neutropenia.'),
('f19a703a-33b8-5bb1-ae2d-883c0b399031'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which bone marrow cell lineage gives rise to neutrophils, monocytes, and eosinophils?', '["Myeloid lineage (granulocyte-monocyte pathway)","Lymphoid lineage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Erythroid lineage mediated by specific receptor activation","Megakaryocytic lineage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No common lineage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myeloid lineage (granulocyte-monocyte pathway)', 'Neutrophils, monocytes, and eosinophils all derive from the common myeloid progenitor via the granulocyte-monocyte pathway.'),
('c419fad1-538b-56d6-a1fa-9d349d1cd533'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Thrombopoietin (TPO) is produced by:', '["Spleen","Kidney","Liver","Bone marrow","Lungs"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Thrombopoietin is produced mainly by the liver (and kidney) and stimulates megakaryocyte platelet production via the c-Mpl receptor.'),
('e291ad80-3433-53d1-bc17-ff38f8d6f0d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Interleukin-3 (IL-3) is:', '["B cell factor","T cell growth factor","NK factor","Multi-lineage growth factor","Macrophage factor"]'::jsonb, 'Multi-lineage growth factor', 'IL-3 is a multi-lineage (multipotential) growth factor supporting early myeloid and lymphoid progenitors.'),
('065f61dd-8c96-5c81-9af5-831da0d730a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Stem cell factor (SCF) binds:', '["EPO receptor","GM-CSF receptor","TPO receptor","c-Kit receptor","G-CSF receptor"]'::jsonb, 'c-Kit receptor', 'Stem cell factor binds the c-kit receptor (CD117), sustaining hematopoietic stem cell survival and proliferation.'),
('88a2a59c-8c1b-5136-bc45-92dfb684c6f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Hematopoietic stem cells are identified by:', '["CD14+","CD34+ marker","CD56+","CD19+","CD3+"]'::jsonb, 'CD34+ marker', 'Hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells express CD34, the standard marker used clinically for their identification and selection.'),
('f2638c4b-f1d5-5b34-8c91-7245e506a468'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The NOTCH signaling pathway is important for:', '["B cell development","Myeloid differentiation","Erythropoiesis","HSC self-renewal and T cell development","Megakaryopoiesis"]'::jsonb, 'HSC self-renewal and T cell development', 'NOTCH signaling is required for T-cell commitment and also regulates hematopoietic stem cell self-renewal.'),
('ebb47b3a-88c8-5932-91b3-aa7620c0b923'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Wnt signaling in hematopoiesis:', '["B cell development","Platelet production","NK development","Promotes myeloid differentiation","Regulates HSC self-renewal"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates HSC self-renewal', 'Wnt/beta-catenin signaling maintains hematopoietic stem cell self-renewal and quiescence during hematopoiesis.'),
('6a4e7a64-51b1-52fc-a8dc-8d3caf46da92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Apoptosis of hematopoietic cells is regulated by:', '["Only caspases","All of the above","Cytokines","Hormones","Bcl-2 family proteins"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Apoptosis in hematopoietic cells is coordinated by caspases, Bcl-2 family proteins, and external survival signals such as cytokines and hormones.'),
('7999b58f-4429-5b67-b4d5-baa80554c35c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Fas-Fas ligand interaction is important for:', '["Blood cell survival","Erythropoiesis","Activation-induced cell death of T cells","Neutrophil phagocytosis","Platelet activation"]'::jsonb, 'Activation-induced cell death of T cells', 'Fas-FasL triggering mediates activation-induced cell death of T cells, terminating immune responses and maintaining tolerance.'),
('e0adadd6-ba9c-5c8c-9d54-d8e545551de5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Which natural killer (NK) cell surface markers are used to identify NK cells in flow cytometry?', '["CD8","CD3","CD19","CD4","CD16 and CD56"]'::jsonb, 'CD16 and CD56', 'NK cells are identified by CD16 (Fc-gamma receptor III) and CD56 (NCAM) and are CD3-negative lymphocytes.'),
('341e9b3a-a965-5ff2-8c8f-9062b1e3c5ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'Antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity (ADCC) is performed by:', '["Neutrophils resulting in decreased functional activity","Macrophages accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","B cells mediated by specific receptor activation","T cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NK cells via CD16 (Fc receptor)"]'::jsonb, 'NK cells via CD16 (Fc receptor)', 'NK cells, via CD16 binding antibody-coated targets, perform ADCC and lyse cells opsonized by IgG.'),
('b53061fd-ca94-5849-b5aa-e8b2992850db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Blood Immunity - Blood Immunity', 'The classical pathway of complement is activated by:', '["Mannose (lectin)","Bacterial polysaccharides (alternative)","All of the above","Direct proteolysis","Antigen-antibody complex (IgG/IgM)"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen-antibody complex (IgG/IgM)', 'The classical complement pathway is initiated when C1q binds antibody (IgG or IgM) in an antigen-antibody complex.'),
('9002bcaf-56ad-50ce-bd5d-b471ea8a3694'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Cardiac muscle is characterized by:', '["Single nucleus centrally located","Voluntary","Striated with intercalated discs","Gap junctions","Non-striated"]'::jsonb, 'Striated with intercalated discs', 'Cardiac muscle is striated (like skeletal muscle) and joined end-to-end by intercalated discs containing gap junctions and desmosomes, which enable synchronized contraction.'),
('e83d6d42-a1b9-5654-a68f-925638a7d209'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'A 70-year-old female presents with exertional chest pain and shortness of breath. Which cardiac tissue is primarily responsible for generating the force of ventricular contraction?', '["Atrial muscle","SA node","AV node","Purkinje fibers","Ventricular muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular muscle', 'Ventricular muscle generates the high-pressure force needed to eject blood into the aorta and pulmonary artery; its thickening produces the clinical picture of effort intolerance and dyspnea.'),
('a3bfebaa-04de-549f-8672-9ebd0d4cdeff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Phase 0 of the ventricular action potential is due to:', '["Ca2+ influx resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ influx via fast Na+ channels","K+ efflux mediated by specific receptor activation","Cl- influx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Na+/Ca2+ exchanger leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ influx via fast Na+ channels', 'Rapid opening of voltage-gated fast Na+ channels causes the rapid upstroke (phase 0) of the ventricular action potential.'),
('5b0b45fd-642e-5441-a783-18e4841cd281'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Phase 1 of the ventricular action potential is due to:', '["K+ influx","Ca2+ influx","Na+ influx","Transient K+ efflux (Ito)","Cl- influx"]'::jsonb, 'Transient K+ efflux (Ito)', 'Phase 1 (notch) results from transient outward K+ current (Ito), producing early partial repolarization after the sodium upstroke.'),
('4133de54-b332-5340-9750-eeb578890435'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Phase 2 (plateau) of the ventricular action potential is due to:', '["Cl- influx resulting in decreased functional activity","K+ efflux only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ca2+ influx (L-type) balanced by K+ efflux","Ca2+ efflux without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Na+ influx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ influx (L-type) balanced by K+ efflux', 'The plateau is maintained by a balance between inward L-type Ca2+ current and outward K+ current, keeping the membrane near 0 mV and prolonging the action potential.'),
('9bc95253-7cf6-58e8-ab27-009597fda2b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Phase 3 of the ventricular action potential is due to:', '["Ca2+ influx resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid K+ efflux (delayed rectifier)","Na+/K+ pump mediated by specific receptor activation","Na+ influx without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cl- influx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid K+ efflux (delayed rectifier)', 'Phase 3 repolarization results from increased K+ efflux through delayed rectifier K+ channels, restoring the membrane toward the resting potential.'),
('64ff2ec9-e456-5d23-8d9e-480f31a0e84c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Phase 4 (diastolic depolarization) in the SA node is due to:', '["If (funny) current and T-type Ca2+ current","K+ efflux accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","L-type Ca2+ mediated by specific receptor activation","Na+ influx only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cl- current leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'If (funny) current and T-type Ca2+ current', 'Pacemaker cells depolarize spontaneously during phase 4 via the If (funny) mixed Na+/K+ current and T-type Ca2+ channels, generating automaticity.'),
('587da553-5992-5434-a9cd-ac06bb3485da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The resting membrane potential of SA node cells is:', '["-55 to -60 mV","0 mV","-30 mV","-70 mV","-90 mV"]'::jsonb, '-55 to -60 mV', 'SA nodal cells have a less negative resting potential around -55 to -60 mV because they lack a stable resting state and show continuous diastolic depolarization.'),
('791cef5d-9d58-59fa-9256-e4909b040150'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which conduction system component conducts the cardiac impulse most rapidly, enabling rapid activation of the ventricular muscle?', '["AV node","Purkinje fibers","SA node","Atrial muscle","Ventricular muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje fibers', 'Purkinje fibers have the fastest conduction velocity of the cardiac conduction system, allowing near-simultaneous depolarization of both ventricles.'),
('a935c18f-dca5-5ec1-be56-22036b6382df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which cardiac structure has its action potentials dependent primarily on calcium influx, making it most sensitive to hypocalcemia and calcium-channel blockers?', '["SA node","AV node","Ventricular muscle","Atrial muscle","Purkinje fibers"]'::jsonb, 'AV node', 'In SA and AV nodal cells, depolarization is carried by calcium (slow-response cells), so the AV node is specially dependent on calcium influx and sensitive to hypocalcemia and calcium-channel blockers.'),
('dc6dbbbe-ee4c-56ba-b318-3b0e649536ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The AV nodal delay is approximately:', '["0.02 sec","0.1 sec","0.25 sec","0.13 sec","0.5 sec"]'::jsonb, '0.1 sec', 'Conduction through the AV node is slow, producing a delay of about 0.1 second that allows atrial contraction to complete before ventricular systole.'),
('ef5eed8a-6ce4-5b66-93f8-4e125d11abcb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The SA node is located at:', '["Junction of SVC and right atrium","Left atrium","Coronary sinus","AV junction","Interventricular septum"]'::jsonb, 'Junction of SVC and right atrium', 'The sinoatrial node lies in the upper wall of the right atrium at its junction with the superior vena cava, the site of the normal cardiac pacemaker.'),
('aa31cce1-53c6-5eb6-8bbc-bb24e615eac1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The SA node is supplied by:', '["LAD resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinoatrial nodal artery (RCA in 60%)","LCX mediated by specific receptor activation","PDA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagonal branch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinoatrial nodal artery (RCA in 60%)', 'The SA nodal artery arises from the right coronary artery in about 60% of people and from the left circumflex in the remainder, supplying the sinoatrial node.'),
('8875830d-4581-56f3-b1b0-fea6f59cdc0b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The PR interval represents:', '["Ventricular depolarization","Total heart cycle","Atrial depolarization + AV delay","Ventricular repolarization","Atrial repolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial depolarization + AV delay', 'The PR interval spans the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS, reflecting atrial depolarization plus conduction delay through the AV node.'),
('5779c733-56a7-58cd-80e8-207c0b3bc0ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The QRS complex represents:', '["Ventricular repolarization","Purkinje activation","Ventricular depolarization","Atrial repolarization","Atrial depolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular depolarization', 'The QRS complex reflects depolarization of the ventricular myocardium as the impulse propagates through the ventricles.'),
('35c78be3-fe78-5b1b-8bd1-a0f19a8e9cca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The QT interval represents:', '["Only ventricular depolarization","Atrial activity","Ventricular depolarization + repolarization","AV conduction","SA node activity"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular depolarization + repolarization', 'The QT interval measures the total duration of ventricular electrical activity, from the start of depolarization (QRS) through repolarization (T wave).'),
('9d840184-00b9-523a-afd2-a036b65d5239'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The T wave represents:', '["Ventricular repolarization","Atrial depolarization","Ventricular depolarization","Atrial repolarization","SA node discharge"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular repolarization', 'The T wave reflects repolarization of the ventricular myocardium, restoring the ventricular cells to their resting potential.'),
('813b8ccd-ff13-56b6-82da-c10951e2a4e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The U wave may represent:', '["Purkinje activation","SA node activity","His bundle","Late repolarization of Purkinje fibers","Atrial repolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Late repolarization of Purkinje fibers', 'The U wave is thought to reflect late repolarization of the Purkinje fibers; it becomes prominent in hypokalemia and certain cardiac conditions.'),
('1308a480-7361-5fe9-816a-9b0ad70e0f49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The P wave represents:', '["Ventricular depolarization","AV nodal activation","Ventricular repolarization","Atrial depolarization","Atrial repolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial depolarization', 'The P wave reflects depolarization of the atria, initiated by the SA node impulse spreading across the atrial myocardium.'),
('55c9933c-6001-5239-9f75-fc418dc86bfe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'A 55-year-old male with chest pain undergoes an ECG which shows isoelectric ST segment. Why is the normal ST segment isoelectric?', '["Atrial activity resulting in decreased functional activity","AV conduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SA node activity mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventricular depolarization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Early ventricular repolarization (no current flow)"]'::jsonb, 'Early ventricular repolarization (no current flow)', 'During the ST segment the ventricles are completely depolarized and in an early plateau phase, so there is no potential difference between electrodes (no current flow), yielding an isoelectric tracing.'),
('8d705352-ac4a-57d0-8bf8-59672c24ac47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The J point is located at:', '["Junction of QRS and ST segment","End of T wave","Peak of R wave","Start of QRS","Start of P wave"]'::jsonb, 'Junction of QRS and ST segment', 'The J point marks the junction where the QRS complex ends and the ST segment begins; deviation of the J point is used to judge ST-segment displacement.'),
('85f4e25a-8772-5100-833c-ee95dfedb831'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'First-degree AV block means:', '["Missing QRS","QRS >0.12 s","PR interval <0.12 s","Prolonged QT","PR interval >0.20 s"]'::jsonb, 'PR interval >0.20 s', 'First-degree AV block is defined as a PR interval exceeding 0.20 s, reflecting delayed AV conduction while every P wave still conducts to a QRS.'),
('0f73c95a-b708-58c0-b81c-a6da453c5058'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Second-degree AV block type I (Wenckebach) shows:', '["PR >0.20 s resulting in decreased functional activity","Fixed PR with dropped QRS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Progressive PR lengthening then dropped QRS","Slow heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","QRS at all leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive PR lengthening then dropped QRS', 'Wenckebach block displays progressive PR prolongation until one P wave is not conducted, resulting in a dropped QRS with underlying group beating.'),
('3d1ff110-3230-526a-b9b6-d6c7cffe1ed3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which type of second-degree AV block shows a constant PR interval with periodically dropped QRS complexes?', '["Progressive PR lengthening resulting in decreased functional activity","Fixed PR with intermittent dropped QRS","Slow rate mediated by specific receptor activation","QRS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PR >0.20 s leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed PR with intermittent dropped QRS', 'Mobitz type II second-degree AV block has a constant PR interval with intermittent non-conducted P waves, indicating disease below the AV node.'),
('4fadfc21-67d7-5df4-bec5-5e1718cb9cdb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Complete heart block (third-degree AV block) has:', '["Atria and ventricles dissociated (no relation of P to QRS)","1:1 conduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dropped QRS only mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal conduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prolonged PR only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atria and ventricles dissociated (no relation of P to QRS)', 'In complete heart block the atria and ventricles beat independently because no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles, so P waves bear no constant relation to QRS complexes.'),
('a374bed5-8a71-589c-80f8-717106ad2d83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The normal cardiac pacemaker is:', '["Atrial muscle","Purkinje fibers","Ventricular muscle","SA node","AV node"]'::jsonb, 'SA node', 'The SA node has the fastest intrinsic firing rate and normally dominates as the cardiac pacemaker, suppressing slower latent pacemakers by overdrive suppression.'),
('141852cf-fc3c-5d01-94b7-726587063202'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'If the SA node fails, the AV node escapes at a rate of:', '["40-60/min","100-120/min","70-80/min","80-100/min","20-30/min"]'::jsonb, '40-60/min', 'When the SA node fails, the AV junction takes over as pacemaker at its intrinsic rate of about 40-60 beats/min.'),
('286e5c9c-7b7c-57e1-aa1a-e8e29aa7aca3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Overdrive suppression explains:', '["Vagal tone resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic dominance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","interaction mediated by specific receptor activation","Slower suppresses faster without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Faster pacemaker suppresses slower latent pacemakers"]'::jsonb, 'Faster pacemaker suppresses slower latent pacemakers', 'Overdrive suppression describes how the fastest pacemaker (SA node) keeps slower latent pacemakers from firing by depolarizing them before they reach threshold.'),
('b4d00477-51d3-53d2-9234-a507ceaf4eaa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Vagal (parasympathetic) stimulation of the heart causes:', '["Slowing of AV conduction","Increased heart rate","Increased contractility","Coronary vasodilation","Sinus bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus bradycardia', 'Acetylcholine released by the vagus acts on M2 muscarinic receptors to hyperpolarize SA nodal cells, slowing the sinus rate (sinus bradycardia) and also slowing AV conduction.'),
('f52882a8-bc53-58d8-8ab8-813f8a3af285'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate via:', '["Beta-1 adrenergic receptors","Alpha-1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alpha-2 mediated by specific receptor activation","M2 muscarinic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beta-2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-1 adrenergic receptors', 'Norepinephrine and epinephrine act on cardiac beta-1 adrenergic receptors, increasing SA node firing rate and the slope of diastolic depolarization, thereby raising heart rate.'),
('e1d5534c-793a-5f48-a085-70bf636254ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'At a resting heart rate around 75 beats/min, the duration of one cardiac cycle is approximately:', '["0.6 sec","1.2 sec","0.4 sec","0.8 sec","1.0 sec"]'::jsonb, '0.8 sec', 'Cycle length equals 60 divided by the heart rate (60/75 = 0.8 s), the normal duration of a single cardiac cycle at rest.'),
('8a5fd99d-bdf3-5456-87ca-b141cadfa4c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'During which phase of the cardiac cycle does ventricular filling primarily occur after atrial contraction?', '["Early diastole","During T wave","At the start of QRS","During ST segment","Late diastole (after P wave)"]'::jsonb, 'Late diastole (after P wave)', 'Atrial contraction (the P wave) occurs in late diastole and delivers the ''atrial kick'', which normally contributes about 20-25% of ventricular filling.'),
('814b82c0-0990-52e7-839b-5697f33ee732'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The first heart sound (S1) is due to:', '["Turbulent flow","Aortic valve opening","AV valves closing (mitral and tricuspid)","Stretching of ventricles","Semilunar valves closing"]'::jsonb, 'AV valves closing (mitral and tricuspid)', 'S1 is produced by closure of the atrioventricular (mitral and tricuspid) valves at the onset of ventricular systole.'),
('2d651494-7831-57bb-a268-855417948332'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the fourth heart sound (S4) associated with?', '["Rapid ventricular filling sound (normal in young)","Valve closing","Valve opening murmur","Atrial sound","Physiologic split"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial sound', 'S4 is an atrial sound generated by atrial contraction against a stiff, hypertrophied, or non-compliant ventricle, such as in LVH and diastolic dysfunction.'),
('5587fad6-c45c-5a72-888b-b2fad54faf09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'A 35-year-old female presents with exertional chest pain and shortness of breath. On auscultation a presystolic gallop is heard. Which mechanism produces this sound?', '["AV valve closing resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid filling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Semilunar closing mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial contraction (presystolic gallop)"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial contraction (presystolic gallop)', 'The presystolic gallop (S4) is caused by atrial contraction forcing blood into a stiff, noncompliant ventricle in late diastole.'),
('ad8e5aae-b782-566a-a191-a383e7647a91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'How does inspiration affect the splitting of heart sounds S2?', '["Inspiration (P2 delayed)","Expiration widens","Expiration (A2 earlier)","Inspiration narrows","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiration (P2 delayed)', 'During inspiration, increased venous return delays right ventricular ejection, so the pulmonic component (P2) is delayed and physiologic splitting of S2 widens.'),
('aeda8735-96fb-5aa9-9fb0-b590fbec90a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Cardiac output (CO) at rest is approximately:', '["3 L/min","5 L/min (normal)","10 L/min","2 L/min","7 L/min"]'::jsonb, '5 L/min (normal)', 'Resting cardiac output in a normal adult is about 5 L/min (heart rate 70/min × stroke volume 70-80 mL).'),
('4b9f1c6e-34b0-560a-895f-d76d0bf02a7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which formula correctly describes cardiac output?', '["Contractility x HR","Stroke volume x Heart rate","Preload x Afterload","Both A (Contractility x HR) and B (Stroke volume x Heart rate)","EDV - ESV"]'::jsonb, 'Stroke volume x Heart rate', 'Cardiac output is defined as the product of stroke volume and heart rate (CO = SV × HR), averaging about 5 L/min at rest.'),
('26324485-d211-5fb2-90c7-24b4140a38c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Stroke volume at rest is about:', '["100 mL","50 mL","30 mL","70 mL","150 mL"]'::jsonb, '70 mL', 'Resting stroke volume is approximately 70 mL per beat (EDV 120 mL minus ESV 50 mL).'),
('0677189d-f0e5-5ae2-9514-3e330a0e2909'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'End-diastolic volume (EDV) at rest is about:', '["200 mL","100 mL","160 mL","120 mL","70 mL"]'::jsonb, '120 mL', 'At rest, left ventricular end-diastolic volume is about 110-120 mL, representing the extent of ventricular filling before contraction.'),
('5d3fcf5b-a7d6-533f-96a7-106d4bb9ab3e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Preload is best reflected by:', '["Contractility","End-diastolic volume","Afterload","Heart rate","End-systolic volume"]'::jsonb, 'End-diastolic volume', 'Preload is the stretch of ventricular muscle before contraction, best estimated by end-diastolic volume (or filling pressure).'),
('65b64f59-7bf4-52f9-b61c-26ff4a803550'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'According to the Frank-Starling mechanism:', '["Increased end-diastolic volume increases stroke volume","Decreased end-diastolic volume increases stroke volume","Increased afterload increases stroke volume","Decreased contractility increases stroke volume","Increased end-diastolic volume decreases stroke volume"]'::jsonb, 'Increased end-diastolic volume increases stroke volume', 'The Frank-Starling law states that within physiologic limits, increased end-diastolic volume stretches the myocardium and increases the force of contraction, thereby increasing stroke volume.'),
('dc436b36-e2bf-5cc6-a12f-2892489337ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Increased afterload causes:', '["Increased CO","Decreased HR","Decreased preload","Increased stroke volume","Decreased stroke volume"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased stroke volume', 'Raising afterload increases the pressure the ventricle must overcome to eject blood, which reduces stroke volume unless contractility compensates.'),
('434b4d6e-5309-5e99-9462-472c21944815'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Contractility is often assessed by:', '["EDV","BP","SV","dP/dt max","HR"]'::jsonb, 'dP/dt max', 'The maximum rate of ventricular pressure rise (dP/dt max) is a load-dependent but commonly used bedside index of myocardial contractility.'),
('8a33cc6d-c798-5a8a-9c8e-4f96635bb659'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Positive inotropic agents include:', '["Acidosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium channel blockers mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic stimulation (catecholamines)","Beta-blockers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic stimulation (catecholamines)', 'Catecholamines acting through beta-1 adrenergic receptors increase intracellular Ca2+ and enhance contractility, making sympathetic stimulation a positive inotropic influence.'),
('d38dacf7-c7dc-5e38-bc1b-8d04f9f39bbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which of the following is NOT a negative inotropic influence?', '["Hypoxia/acidosis","Calcium channel blockers","Sympathetic stimulation","Beta-blockers","Cardiac ischemia"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic stimulation', 'Sympathetic (catecholamine) stimulation increases contractility, so it is a positive inotrope; hypoxia, acidosis, calcium-channel blockers, beta-blockers, and ischemia all depress contractility.'),
('cb5bcb11-8d9d-51cb-99bd-3ecb28b6fb61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The Fick principle for cardiac output uses:', '["Ultrasound","O2 consumption/AV O2 difference","Thermodilution","Indicator dilution","Pressure measurement"]'::jsonb, 'O2 consumption/AV O2 difference', 'The Fick method calculates cardiac output as whole-body O2 consumption divided by the arterial-venous O2 content difference.'),
('22ad7c06-994e-5854-9113-9639e6243794'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The dye dilution method uses:', '["Methylene blue","Green dye","Rhodamine","Evans blue","Indocyanine green (ICG)"]'::jsonb, 'Indocyanine green (ICG)', 'Indicator-dilution cardiac output measurement uses indocyanine green, which is injected as a bolus and its concentration-time curve analyzed.'),
('81b2b700-d2f4-52a5-baeb-008953091e54'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Echocardiography measures CO by:', '["Doppler flow velocity x cross-sectional area","Volume conductance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thermodilution mediated by specific receptor activation","Pressure gradient without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Impedance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Doppler flow velocity x cross-sectional area', 'Echocardiography estimates stroke volume/cardiac output as the product of the Doppler flow-velocity integral and the cross-sectional area of the outflow tract.'),
('50a9f43b-55b0-539f-8e1b-597e12ba710c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Heart rate is fastest in:', '["Athlete","Adult (60-100/min)","Elderly","Adolescent","Newborn (130-150/min)"]'::jsonb, 'Newborn (130-150/min)', 'Newborns have the fastest resting heart rate (about 130-150/min) because their basal metabolic rate and oxygen demand per unit body mass are high.'),
('d2ae4712-5ba0-52e4-8a3b-aa5e73132cc6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the normal intrinsic firing rate of the sinoatrial (SA) node?', '["80/min","120/min","150/min","100/min","140/min"]'::jsonb, '100/min', 'The SA node has an intrinsic firing rate of about 100/min, though resting heart rate is slower (60-80/min) because of tonic vagal inhibition.'),
('7978b52b-9d9f-55d8-96a1-daa6e380c179'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Sinus bradycardia is HR <:', '["40/min","70/min","50/min","30/min","60/min"]'::jsonb, '60/min', 'Sinus bradycardia is defined as a sinus heart rate below 60 beats/min.'),
('0903a17c-8b41-5627-afaa-0f3a1e713b00'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Exercise increases cardiac output up to:', '["30 L/min","35 L/min","20-25 L/min (fit)","15 L/min","10 L/min"]'::jsonb, '20-25 L/min (fit)', 'During heavy exercise, cardiac output in fit individuals can rise to about 20-25 L/min through increases in both heart rate and stroke volume.'),
('d67cd32a-0905-5279-b606-36663c779ba3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The rate pressure product (RPP = HR x SBP) reflects:', '["Myocardial O2 supply","Coronary flow","Myocardial O2 demand","Stroke work","Cardiac efficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial O2 demand', 'The rate-pressure product (heart rate multiplied by systolic blood pressure) is a useful clinical index of myocardial oxygen demand.'),
('1725eb8d-7cf2-57e1-b4d8-255b25f1264f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Coronary blood flow to the left ventricle is greatest during which phase of the cardiac cycle?', '["Diastole","Systole","Both equally","Isovolumetric systole","Early systole"]'::jsonb, 'Diastole', 'Left coronary flow occurs mainly in diastole because intramyocardial tension developed during systole compresses the coronary vessels; the right coronary perfuses more continuously.'),
('f7c2e8fd-aae6-5739-8d5e-41bb5deedbf5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Most common ACE inhibitor side effect:', '["Dry cough","Hyperkalaemia","Angioedema","First-dose hypotension","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Dry cough', '5-20% from bradykinin/substance P accumulation.'),
('9269b5da-2039-507b-8e0b-dafe5588dbe0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which mechanism is the most important regulator of coronary blood flow during increased myocardial metabolism?', '["Neural (sympathetic)","Endothelial","Metabolic (adenosine)","Hormonal","Myogenic"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic (adenosine)', 'Adenosine released from metabolically active or hypoxic myocardium dilates coronary resistance vessels, coupling coronary flow to myocardial O2 demand.'),
('198f845c-402d-5972-8dc0-b5a1f1aa930d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Endothelial nitric oxide (NO) causes:', '["Thrombosis","Inflammation","Platelet activation","Vasoconstriction","Vasodilation via cGMP"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation via cGMP', 'Nitric oxide diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raises cGMP, and produces vasodilation; it also inhibits platelet aggregation.'),
('d53599ab-8cd7-56a5-b4f0-d243a598494f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Nitroglycerin relieves angina by:', '["Releasing NO -> venous and arterial dilation","Increasing heart rate","Inhibiting platelets","Blocking Ca2+ channels","Increasing contractility"]'::jsonb, 'Releasing NO -> venous and arterial dilation', 'Nitroglycerin releases nitric oxide, producing venodilation (reducing preload) and arterial dilation (reducing afterload), which lessens myocardial oxygen demand in angina.'),
('eeea76dd-4b9b-52c4-9421-e74f738c90b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the pathophysiological mechanism underlying unstable (crescendo) angina?', '["Silent ischemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Stable pattern accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Variant angina mediated by specific receptor activation","Crescendo angina (plaque rupture/thrombosis)","Microvascular leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Crescendo angina (plaque rupture/thrombosis)', 'Unstable angina results from rupture of an atherosclerotic plaque with superimposed thrombosis that partially occludes the artery, producing ischemia at rest or with minimal exertion.'),
('62a9eaee-af82-5003-b683-f822f688b92a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The pathognomonic ECG finding for acute STEMI is:', '["T inversion","ST depression","BBB abnormality","P wave changes","ST elevation in contiguous leads"]'::jsonb, 'ST elevation in contiguous leads', 'Acute ST-elevation MI is defined by ST-segment elevation in anatomically contiguous leads reflecting transmural ischemia/injury.'),
('a2157c05-08d2-55e8-9eba-6a086639482a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG/clinical finding indicates full-thickness myocardial injury (transmural infarction)?', '["Old MI only resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ischemia without infarction mediated by specific receptor activation","Transmural myocardial infarction (necrosis)","Pericarditis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transmural myocardial infarction (necrosis)', 'Transmural infarction involves the full thickness of the ventricular wall and classically presents with ST elevation (STEMI) from complete coronary occlusion.'),
('3e40a71c-1dc8-57be-848e-a40262aabf67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Myocardial infarction biomarkers include ALL EXCEPT:', '["Troponin I","ALT","Troponin T","CK-MB","Myoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'ALT', 'ALT is a hepatic enzyme and is not a cardiac biomarker; troponin I, troponin T, CK-MB, and myoglobin are all released after myocardial injury.'),
('f1bb8108-91d5-5d4e-b39d-5fffa68d4516'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which cardiac biomarker is highly specific for myocardial injury and remains elevated for 10-14 days?', '["early rise resulting in decreased functional activity","Cheaper accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urinary detection mediated by specific receptor activation","Only MI marker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac specific and long diagnostic window"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac specific and long diagnostic window', 'Cardiac troponin is highly specific for myocardial necrosis and remains elevated for up to 10-14 days, providing a long diagnostic window.'),
('49310cf3-1013-5261-a405-a132fc6acbde'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'CK-MB rises in MI at:', '["24-48 hours","6-8 hours","4-6 hours","2-4 hours","12-24 hours"]'::jsonb, '4-6 hours', 'CK-MB rises about 4-6 hours after the onset of myocardial infarction, peaks at roughly 24 hours, and returns to normal within 48-72 hours.'),
('5764c8bf-5ee8-57a9-b433-e6ae032a215f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which statement is true regarding creatine kinase–MB as a myocardial infarction biomarker?', '["Expensive resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-specific (also in muscle injury)","Very specific mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in large MI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Late rise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-specific (also in muscle injury)', 'CK-MB is relatively cardiac-specific but is also elevated in skeletal muscle injury, trauma, and strenuous exercise, making it less specific than cardiac troponin.'),
('d1daa139-d385-5573-a9e8-143c4cbe2b19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Heart failure with preserved EF (HFpEF) is EF >:', '["50%","60%","35%","30%","40%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) is generally defined as an ejection fraction of 50% or greater despite clinical heart failure.'),
('d521cb88-9d68-599b-b3e7-8c1ed2516403'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Compensatory mechanisms in heart failure include:', '["Ventricular hypertrophy","RAAS activation","Sympathetic activation","Brain natriuretic peptide","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The failing heart is compensated by ventricular hypertrophy/remodeling, activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, sympathetic activation, and increased BNP release.'),
('964a23d3-8cd3-5a84-a4c5-fc88c22968dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) is released by:', '["Atrial myocytes resulting in decreased functional activity","Kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Spleen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventricular myocytes in response to stretch"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular myocytes in response to stretch', 'BNP is synthesized and released by ventricular myocytes in response to increased wall stretch and volume overload, serving as a marker of heart failure.'),
('f2fd3cfc-5eb7-5ad8-bc84-c7dc58f15e6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The three main components of the surface ECG are:', '["U wave","J point","P QRS T","PR QT QRS","ST TU"]'::jsonb, 'P QRS T', 'The standard ECG consists of the P wave (atrial depolarization), QRS complex (ventricular depolarization), and T wave (ventricular repolarization).'),
('b3d47c4f-c993-5391-960e-998fd08b70da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'At the standard ECG recording speed of 25 mm/s, how many large squares (5 mm) correspond to one second?', '["100 mm/sec","5 mm/sec","50 mm/sec","25 mm/sec","10 mm/sec"]'::jsonb, '25 mm/sec', 'At the standard paper speed of 25 mm/s, 1 small square (1 mm) equals 0.04 s; this fixed speed is used to time ECG intervals.'),
('7a52c865-331b-529f-ae5a-f3e7bc360e06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'On a standard ECG at normal calibration, what is the voltage standardization?', '["1 mm = 1 mV","5 mm = 1 mV","20 mm = 1 mV","2 mm = 1 mV","10 mm = 1 mV"]'::jsonb, '10 mm = 1 mV', 'Standard ECG calibration is 10 mm of vertical deflection per 1 mV, allowing measurement of waveform amplitude.'),
('b3a7f8fc-fbe0-5a75-b8d5-92e638a47da7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG finding is classically associated with right axis deviation?', '["Right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH)","Left anterior fascicular block","First-degree AV block","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH)', 'Right ventricular hypertrophy causes the QRS axis to shift rightward (beyond +90 degrees); left anterior fascicular block produces left axis deviation.'),
('3d74ae49-b037-535d-98e0-cad1147992d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Left atrial enlargement on ECG shows:', '["T inversion resulting in decreased functional activity","Tall R waves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","P mitrale (bifid P in II and negative P in V1)","Deep S waves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P pulmonale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'P mitrale (bifid P in II and negative P in V1)', 'Left atrial enlargement produces a broad notched (bifid) P wave in lead II and a prominent negative terminal P in V1, termed P mitrale.'),
('f3bff5ad-3d9d-5c36-9f3b-5e192f9faebd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Right atrial enlargement on ECG shows:', '["P pulmonale (peaked P >2.5 mm in II)","ST depression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wide QRS mediated by specific receptor activation","Bifid P without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P mitrale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'P pulmonale (peaked P >2.5 mm in II)', 'Right atrial enlargement produces tall, peaked P waves exceeding 2.5 mm in lead II (and prominent initial P in V1), termed P pulmonale.'),
('044b8497-5f47-5211-90aa-c085e19d882a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG finding is characteristic of left ventricular hypertrophy (LVH)?', '["S V1 + R V5/V6 >35 mm (Sokolow-Lyon index)","Deep Q waves in inferior leads","ST elevation in contiguous leads","P pulmonale","Short PR interval with delta wave"]'::jsonb, 'S V1 + R V5/V6 >35 mm (Sokolow-Lyon index)', 'LVH is diagnosed by voltage criteria such as the Sokolow-Lyon index (S in V1 plus R in V5/V6 greater than 35 mm), often accompanied by a lateral strain pattern.'),
('bdb9a256-0294-5635-8cba-6dfc592a03b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'RVH on ECG shows:', '["Right axis deviation","R/S ratio >1 in V1","deep S in V6","All of the above","R >7 mm in V1"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Right ventricular hypertrophy produces right axis deviation, an R/S ratio greater than 1 in V1, tall R in V1, and a deep S in V6, so multiple criteria are present together.'),
('abe794b9-e5e0-5de6-ac68-00398a05fbe4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'A 70-year-old male undergoes an ECG which shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4. Which ECG change best explains acute anterior transmural ischemia?', '["ST elevation (transmural ischemia)","P elevation","ST depression (subendocardial ischemia)","T wave inversion","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'ST elevation (transmural ischemia)', 'Acute transmural ischemia over the anterior wall (leads V1-V4, LAD territory) produces ST-segment elevation, the hallmark of a STEMI.'),
('7a17e584-2ead-5c85-b30a-1aee3f96e82e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG pattern is characteristic of Wellens syndrome (critical proximal LAD stenosis) presenting in a patient with chest pain?', '["Wide QRS resulting in decreased functional activity","ST elevation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep symmetrical T wave inversions in V2-V3 (proximal LAD stenosis)","QT prolongation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P wave inversion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep symmetrical T wave inversions in V2-V3 (proximal LAD stenosis)', 'Wellens syndrome shows biphasic or deep symmetrical T-wave inversions in leads V2-V3, indicating critical proximal left anterior descending artery stenosis.'),
('d2e12a68-22e8-5471-8050-332e63e1e68d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'De Winter sign indicates:', '["Pulmonary embolism resulting in decreased functional activity","LAD occlusion (up-sloping ST depression in V1-V6 with tall T waves)","Pericarditis mediated by specific receptor activation","Inferior MI without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LVH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'LAD occlusion (up-sloping ST depression in V1-V6 with tall T waves)', 'The de Winter pattern (up-sloping ST depression in V1-V6 with tall, peaked T waves) represents acute proximal LAD occlusion and is treated as a STEMI equivalent.'),
('d4316901-77aa-50b8-9cc6-ee470483d823'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG finding is characteristic of acute pericarditis?', '["T inversion only resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffuse concave ST elevation with PR depression","ST depression in contiguous mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep Q waves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P wave changes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diffuse concave ST elevation with PR depression', 'Acute pericarditis classically shows diffuse concave ST-segment elevation with PR-segment depression, reflecting inflammation of the epicardial surface.'),
('1d97fc4b-2726-54cb-8f4b-ad744dba96c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which electrolyte abnormality characteristically causes peaked T waves and widened QRS on ECG?', '["Hypokalemia","Hypermagnesemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia', 'Hyperkalemia produces peaked T waves initially, then QRS widening, loss of P waves, and eventually a sine-wave pattern and ventricular fibrillation.'),
('3ab735df-11e1-52e3-97df-8f8d88ad23c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG change reflects the effect of hypokalemia on ventricular repolarization?', '["P wave changes resulting in decreased functional activity","Flat T wave with prominent U wave (QU interval)","Peaked T mediated by specific receptor activation","Long QT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Short QT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Flat T wave with prominent U wave (QU interval)', 'Hypokalemia produces ST depression, flattened T waves, and prominent U waves, giving the appearance of a prolonged QU interval.'),
('b66a5320-43c7-5d41-a085-e1e42034d24d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Hypercalcemia shortens:', '["QRS duration","P wave","T wave","PR interval","QT interval"]'::jsonb, 'QT interval', 'Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval by abbreviating the plateau (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential.'),
('685aa55e-6590-5f59-a7cd-efbd68996dce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Hypocalcemia prolongs:', '["P wave resulting in decreased functional activity","QRS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PR interval mediated by specific receptor activation","QT interval (ST segment)","T wave leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'QT interval (ST segment)', 'Hypocalcemia prolongs the ST segment and therefore the QT interval because the plateau phase of the ventricular action potential is lengthened.'),
('6a50c389-1866-5388-878f-35e723b24d27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG feature is characteristic of atrial fibrillation?', '["Fine or coarse fibrillatory waves","All of the above","f waves","Regular rhythm","Irregularly irregular rhythm P waves"]'::jsonb, 'Irregularly irregular rhythm P waves', 'Atrial fibrillation shows chaotic fibrillatory waves with an irregularly irregular ventricular response and no discrete P waves.'),
('bf51079c-23dd-5097-8c85-40758b97ee60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) pattern shows:', '["Short PR interval","Wide QRS >0.12 s","Long QT","All of the above","Delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke)"]'::jsonb, 'Delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke)', 'WPW is caused by an accessory pathway (Kent bundle) that pre-excites the ventricle, producing a delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke), short PR interval, and wide QRS; long QT is not a feature.'),
('c2d66edc-9193-5663-a18d-ae395e2f6068'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'WPW syndrome involves:', '["Ventricular hypertrophy resulting in decreased functional activity","SA node dysfunction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Accessory pathway (Kent bundle) bypassing AV node","AV nodal block without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bundle branch block leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Accessory pathway (Kent bundle) bypassing AV node', 'In WPW, an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) connects atria and ventricles, bypassing the AV node and causing pre-excitation.'),
('171f58b6-0646-5369-833f-cceb70220638'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The most common arrhythmia in WPW is:', '["Ventricular tachycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinus tachycardia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia (AVRT)","Atrial fibrillation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial flutter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia (AVRT)', 'The most common tachyarrhythmia in WPW is AV reentrant tachycardia (AVRT), which proceeds anterograde through the AV node and retrograde via the accessory pathway (orthodromic AVRT).'),
('b89702fa-2697-5202-95b6-5c8d52f50bc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the definition of ventricular tachycardia (VT)?', '["Bigeminy resulting in decreased functional activity","Greater than or equal to 3 consecutive ventricular beats at >100/min","Atrial fibrillation with aberrancy mediated by specific receptor activation","Run of PVCs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Wide QRS tachycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Greater than or equal to 3 consecutive ventricular beats at >100/min', 'Ventricular tachycardia is defined as three or more consecutive ventricular beats at a rate greater than 100 beats/min, originating below the AV node.'),
('d0b14b7d-0c8b-5ebf-98e9-83e2b6c7e524'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Torsades de pointes is associated with:', '["Prolonged QT interval","Normal QT","P waves absent","Wide QRS","Short QT"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged QT interval', 'Torsades de pointes is a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that occurs in the setting of a prolonged QT interval.'),
('59ddc7db-a676-553e-87ec-228c1bb958f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which of the following is NOT a cause of a prolonged QT interval (long QT syndrome)?', '["Congenital channelopathies (LQT1-3)","Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia","QT-prolonging drugs (quinidine, sotalol)","Myocardial ischemia","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalcemia', 'Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval; congenital long-QT syndromes, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, ischemia, and QT-prolonging drugs all lengthen it.'),
('22bd9592-ee33-5604-86a1-1569741e25a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) is indicated for:', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Syncope accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Survivors of VF/sustained VT at risk of SCD","Atrial flutter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","WPW leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Survivors of VF/sustained VT at risk of SCD', 'ICDs are indicated for secondary prevention in survivors of ventricular fibrillation or sustained VT who remain at high risk of sudden cardiac death.'),
('71d4c8d3-bdfe-51d0-9d50-488223488974'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) ratio is:', '["15:2 resulting in decreased functional activity","5:1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","100:2 mediated by specific receptor activation","30 compressions : 2 breaths","10:1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '30 compressions : 2 breaths', 'Adult CPR uses a compression-to-ventilation ratio of 30:2 with chest compressions at 100-120/min.'),
('b07edc06-8260-5258-a0a1-2336c393dd83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which physiological factor is most responsible for increasing the force of cardiac contraction?', '["Increased end-diastolic volume (Starling)","Increased afterload","Decreased preload","Parasympathetic stimulation","Hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Increased end-diastolic volume (Starling)', 'The Frank-Starling mechanism increases contraction force when end-diastolic volume (preload) rises; sympathetic stimulation and the Bowditch effect also increase it, but increased preload is the primary determinant in this list.'),
('2fb82d41-7839-5499-b874-9c2d2382c916'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The Bowditch effect (treppe) is:', '["Increased contractility with increased HR","Alternating strength","Decrease","Decreased contractility","Post-extrasystolic potentiation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased contractility with increased HR', 'The Bowditch effect (treppe or staircase phenomenon) describes progressively stronger contractions as heart rate increases.'),
('acaf3762-c8f4-597c-83d4-3b6b3ac02c94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Post-extrasystolic potentiation is:', '["Decreased force","Increased force after a premature beat","Post-stimulation depression","Rate-dependent","Negative inotropic"]'::jsonb, 'Increased force after a premature beat', 'Post-extrasystolic potentiation refers to the augmented contractile force seen in the beat following a premature ventricular contraction.'),
('147b1da3-9af2-5a79-bada-99a013a950c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The absolute refractory period of the ventricle corresponds to:', '["ST segment only resulting in decreased functional activity","P wave accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","QRS to early T wave (entire depolarization + early repolarization)","T wave only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PR segment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'QRS to early T wave (entire depolarization + early repolarization)', 'The absolute refractory period spans from the onset of the QRS through most of the plateau, ending near the peak of the T wave, when no new action potential can be initiated.'),
('e6c78b1c-1d47-5d8e-a83b-86a8197c5ea5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The relative refractory period corresponds to:', '["Downslope of T wave","P-R segment","P wave","ST segment","Upstroke of QRS"]'::jsonb, 'Downslope of T wave', 'The relative refractory period coincides with the downslope of the T wave, when a strong stimulus can elicit a premature action potential.'),
('0c26ba9f-93f4-54bd-9615-1e3ad8af6059'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Sick sinus syndrome includes:', '["Heart block resulting in decreased functional activity","VT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sinus bradycardia, sinus pauses, and tachy-brady syndrome","AF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","WPW leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus bradycardia, sinus pauses, and tachy-brady syndrome', 'Sick sinus syndrome is SA-node dysfunction presenting with inappropriate sinus bradycardia, sinus pauses/arrest, and alternating tachycardia-bradycardia (tachy-brady) episodes.'),
('6286e576-a3c0-5fdb-9ceb-92f0820249f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Carotid sinus massage:', '["Increases vagal tone -> slows SA node and AV conduction","Increases BP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Speeds heart rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Aroused by cervical massage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activates SA node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases vagal tone -> slows SA node and AV conduction', 'Carotid sinus massage stimulates baroreceptors, increasing vagal (parasympathetic) tone, which slows SA node firing and AV conduction, useful in terminating SVT.'),
('cfd28736-75ae-5079-b647-7969e58e2f0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is:', '["Chemoreflex: ventricular receptors -> bradycardia + hypotension (e.g. inferior MI)","Baroreflex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrial reflex mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary reflex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cushing reflex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chemoreflex: ventricular receptors -> bradycardia + hypotension (e.g. inferior MI)', 'The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is a cardioinhibitory reflex triggered by ventricular mechano/chemoreceptors, causing bradycardia and hypotension; it is commonly seen in inferior MI.'),
('7a18ddab-fd2a-5473-b569-268a78c4b80f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which reflex produces hypertension and bradycardia in response to raised intracranial pressure?', '["Tachycardia + hypertension","Increased BP + decreased HR from increased ICP","Exercise response","Bradycardia + hypotension","Orthostatic hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Increased BP + decreased HR from increased ICP', 'The Cushing reflex is a triad of hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration that occurs when raised intracranial pressure compresses the brainstem.'),
('361ef5a9-ac0d-5bdc-936d-e9212fd94db9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) is released by:', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Spleen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrial myocytes in response to stretch","Ventricular myocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial myocytes in response to stretch', 'ANP is stored in atrial myocyte granules and released when atrial pressure and stretch rise, causing natriuresis and vasodilation.'),
('96bfb243-d20e-52d0-837d-3b4167bc8f23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) produces all of the following effects EXCEPT:', '["Increased GFR and sodium excretion","Decreased renin release","Decreased aldosterone secretion","Vasodilation","Increased thirst"]'::jsonb, 'Increased thirst', 'ANP promotes natriuresis and vasodilation and suppresses renin and aldosterone, but it reduces thirst rather than increasing it.'),
('2b8a6631-41f4-5184-9079-4d032f53a888'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which molecule released by endothelial cells is the most potent endogenous vasoconstrictor?', '["Bronchodilator resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrinolytic agent accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Potent vasoconstrictor from endothelium","Vasodilator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Platelet inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potent vasoconstrictor from endothelium', 'Endothelin-1, released from endothelial cells, is the most potent endogenous vasoconstrictor; it acts on ETA receptors on vascular smooth muscle.'),
('33f5db5a-a2d3-521b-a976-4f516e071447'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Endothelin acts via:', '["Beta receptors resulting in decreased functional activity","M2 receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alpha receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Dopamine receptors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ETA receptors on smooth muscle (vasoconstriction) and ETB (NO release)"]'::jsonb, 'ETA receptors on smooth muscle (vasoconstriction) and ETB (NO release)', 'Endothelin-1 produces vasoconstriction through ETA receptors on smooth muscle and vasodilation/NO release via ETB receptors on endothelium.'),
('a8ea46fb-51e2-5319-bc29-7be8af551e75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which of the following is NOT characteristic of endothelial dysfunction in atherosclerosis?', '["Increased oxidative stress","Reduced nitric oxide bioavailability","Increased endothelin-1","Increased prostacyclin production","Increased leukocyte adhesion"]'::jsonb, 'Increased prostacyclin production', 'Endothelial dysfunction features increased oxidative stress, reduced NO, increased endothelin-1, and enhanced adhesion; prostacyclin production is actually reduced.'),
('f85b002c-f509-5f13-b230-533db919b482'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Hypercholesterolemia causes endothelial dysfunction by:', '["Increasing eNOS expression","Decreasing ROS","Increasing prostacyclin","Oxidized LDL reducing NO","Decreasing endothelin"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidized LDL reducing NO', 'Oxidized LDL reduces endothelial nitric oxide bioavailability and increases oxidative stress, impairing endothelium-dependent vasodilation.'),
('b4b8469e-336d-5d00-b0a3-e3f84568ca51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The lipid hypothesis of atherosclerosis states:', '["Chylomicrons cause disease","Elevated LDL is causal in atherosclerosis","Triglycerides are most important","HDL is not protective","Lipoprotein a is irrelevant"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated LDL is causal in atherosclerosis', 'The lipid hypothesis holds that elevated LDL cholesterol is causal in the development of atherosclerosis, whereas HDL is protective and Lp(a) is an independent risk factor.'),
('e72b92f5-9367-51e9-aaf8-7abb5f69a879'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the primary mechanism of action of statin medications?', '["Increasing HDL","Decreasing triglycerides","Antioxidant","Inhibiting absorption","Blocking cholesterol synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking cholesterol synthesis', 'Statins inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, the rate-limiting enzyme of hepatic cholesterol synthesis, thereby lowering LDL cholesterol.'),
('a0224677-30ab-52bb-845a-c415dad4de9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'HDL is considered anti-atherogenic because of all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Reverse cholesterol transport","Antioxidant effect","Anti-inflammatory effect","Promoting LDL oxidation","Scavenging free cholesterol from vessel wall"]'::jsonb, 'Promoting LDL oxidation', 'HDL protects by reverse cholesterol transport, antioxidant, and anti-inflammatory mechanisms; it does not promote LDL oxidation.'),
('202c2698-a457-5240-be5f-f9927c263dc3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Lipoprotein(a) [Lp(a)] is a:', '["Same as LDL resulting in decreased functional activity","VLDL remnant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","HDL variant mediated by specific receptor activation","Modified LDL with apolipoprotein(a)","Chylomicron leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modified LDL with apolipoprotein(a)', 'Lp(a) is an LDL-like particle covalently linked to apolipoprotein(a); elevated levels are an independent cardiovascular risk factor.'),
('d8ac520f-4086-5cb0-940d-1209d92b336d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Elevated Lp(a) increases risk of:', '["Only venous thrombosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral hemorrhage accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Premature atherosclerosis and thrombosis","Pulmonary embolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Premature atherosclerosis and thrombosis', 'High Lp(a) levels promote premature atherosclerosis and thrombosis because the apo(a) moiety resembles plasminogen and interferes with fibrinolysis.'),
('1bf9b0d2-fc3c-52d4-b4ae-208bb089745c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The ankle-brachial index (ABI) <0.9 indicates:', '["Venous insufficiency","Peripheral artery disease","Deep vein thrombosis","Normal","Lymphedema"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral artery disease', 'An ankle-brachial index below 0.9 indicates significant peripheral arterial disease; an ABI <0.4 suggests critical limb ischemia.'),
('e44697bb-da5c-5b40-a008-1b45bdf3820b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Pulsus paradoxus is:', '["Bradycardia with inspiration resulting in decreased functional activity","Irregular pulse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rise in BP during inspiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Exaggerated drop in BP (>10 mmHg) during inspiration","Normal fall in BP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exaggerated drop in BP (>10 mmHg) during inspiration', 'Pulsus paradoxus is an abnormally large (>10 mmHg) fall in systolic blood pressure during inspiration, seen in cardiac tamponade and severe asthma.'),
('73a8f3e9-888e-5b37-aec6-45231b05dc9a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Pulsus alternans is:', '["Atrial fibrillation resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinus arrhythmia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alternating bradycardia and tachycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Beat-to-beat variation in pulse pressure (LV failure)","Bigeminy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Beat-to-beat variation in pulse pressure (LV failure)', 'Pulsus alternans is an alternating strong and weak pulse with beat-to-beat variation in pulse pressure, seen in severe left-ventricular failure.'),
('ca49bb44-38db-58d3-92fe-6aee343243c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Water-hammer pulse (Corrigan pulse) is seen in:', '["All of the above","Hyperdynamic circulation","Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Patent ductus arteriosus"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic regurgitation', 'The water-hammer (Corrigan) pulse is a collapsing pulse characteristic of aortic regurgitation and other states with large stroke volume and rapid diastolic run-off.'),
('61ab3614-fccc-5102-bc89-d19bcdc4ad28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Bisferiens pulse (biphasic systolic peak) is classically seen in which condition?', '["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Aortic regurgitation only","Aortic stenosis only","Aortic regurgitation + aortic stenosis","AR only"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy', 'A bisferiens pulse has a double systolic peak and is classically described in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and combined aortic stenosis with regurgitation.'),
('ef317590-86bd-53ea-ba20-06086e3d61f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Pulse pressure widens in:', '["Heart failure","Shock","Hypovolemia","Aortic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic regurgitation', 'Widened pulse pressure occurs in aortic regurgitation because diastolic pressure falls rapidly while systolic pressure is maintained, whereas AS produces a narrow pulse pressure.'),
('9c580f03-e266-5ff1-980a-1698e271f4bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Narrow pulse pressure is seen in:', '["Heart failure","Cardiac tamponade","All of the above","Shock","Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'A narrow pulse pressure reflects a small stroke volume or high peripheral resistance and is seen in heart failure, shock, cardiac tamponade, and severe aortic stenosis.'),
('34dcf4c1-b897-59c9-b16a-4a55d087e94d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The normal systemic vascular resistance (SVR) is:', '["2500-3000",">3000","700-1600 dyn-s-cm-5","500-700 dyn-s-cm-5","1600-2500 dyn-s-cm-5"]'::jsonb, '700-1600 dyn-s-cm-5', 'Normal systemic vascular resistance is approximately 700-1600 dyn.s.cm-5, calculated as (MAP - CVP) / cardiac output.'),
('4efc46d2-5693-5c01-883a-488d7a18d7ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Poiseuille''s law for laminar flow:', '["Proportional to pressure gradient and radius to the fourth power, inversely to viscosity and length","Proportional to radius cubed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Independent of viscosity mediated by specific receptor activation","Proportional to radius squared without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inversely related to pressure gradient leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proportional to pressure gradient and radius to the fourth power, inversely to viscosity and length', 'Poiseuille''s law states that flow = (DP x pi xr^4)/(8 x viscosity x length); therefore small reductions in vessel radius markedly decrease flow.'),
('65a28242-7dbd-547e-a4fd-064297c1669a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the key vasodilatory mechanism of acetylcholine on vascular endothelium?', '["Activates platelets","No significant effect","Increases pro-coagulant state","Decreases blood flow by direct contraction","Promotes NO release"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes NO release', 'Acetylcholine acts on muscarinic (M3) receptors of intact endothelium to stimulate NO release, which relaxes underlying vascular smooth muscle and causes vasodilation.'),
('7433a36b-c166-5b7b-b384-f60bd3aef246'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Turbulent flow occurs when:', '["Flow is slow","Reynolds number =500","Streamlined flow","Reynolds number <100","Reynolds number >2000"]'::jsonb, 'Reynolds number >2000', 'Flow becomes turbulent when the Reynolds number exceeds about 2000, which happens with high velocity, large diameter, and low viscosity (e.g., across stenotic valves).'),
('a27150d8-4aaa-5022-8498-fd24102cee4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Laplace''s law for a spherical vessel states that wall tension:', '["T = Px r / 2h","T = Px r","T = Px r^2","T = Px rx h","T = Px h / r"]'::jsonb, 'T = Px r / 2h', 'For a sphere, wall tension T = (pressure x radius) / (2 x wall thickness); a dilated ventricle must develop more tension to generate the same pressure.'),
('f8ff477a-18b7-5c30-957a-838c1b1f74c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The windkessel effect refers to:', '["Sympathetic tone resulting in decreased functional activity","Venous return accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic valve function mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillary exchange without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastic arteries smoothing pulsatile flow"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic arteries smoothing pulsatile flow', 'The windkessel effect is the storing of elastic energy by distensible large arteries in systole and its release in diastole, converting pulsatile flow into more continuous flow.'),
('b0776949-f435-53d1-a409-76e88324f5ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Central blood pressure differs from peripheral because:', '["Pulse amplification in periphery","Peripheral lower","Same everywhere","Peripheral constant","difference"]'::jsonb, 'Pulse amplification in periphery', 'Peripheral systolic pressure is higher than central aortic pressure because of pulse-pressure amplification as the pressure wave travels distally and is reflected from the periphery.'),
('a08d2a24-b62c-520b-b07c-4318a6ab01d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG finding is characteristic of atrial fibrillation?', '["Absent P waves with irregular R-R interval","P pulmonale accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Delta wave mediated by specific receptor activation","Flutter waves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P mitrale leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absent P waves with irregular R-R interval', 'Atrial fibrillation is identified by the absence of discrete P waves and an irregularly irregular R-R interval; P pulmonale/mitrale and delta waves point to other diagnoses.'),
('f6b94bb8-cf50-580b-944d-9d159b4840f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Accelerated junctional rhythm rate is:', '["<40/min","100-150/min","60-100/min","250-350/min","150-250/min"]'::jsonb, '60-100/min', 'Accelerated junctional rhythm has a rate of 60-100/min; junctional escape rhythm is slower (40-60/min) and junctional tachycardia exceeds 100/min.'),
('ee30400c-6f11-58c3-adef-0504bec41e33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) rate is typically:', '["60-100/min","250-350/min","<60/min","100-150/min","150-250/min"]'::jsonb, '150-250/min', 'SVT (AV-nodal or AV-reentrant tachycardia) typically presents with a ventricular rate of 150-250/min and a narrow QRS.'),
('09670902-c5a3-5336-b8c1-ebd622cda469'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'AV nodal reentrant tachycardia (AVNRT) is the most common:', '["Bradycardia","SVT in adults","Flutter","VT","AF"]'::jsonb, 'SVT in adults', 'AVNRT is the most common form of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia in adults, caused by re-entry within the AV node.'),
('d91968e0-5c1c-5a11-949c-89b7b6d3caf8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Premature ventricular complex (PVC) is:', '["Regular rhythm resulting in decreased functional activity","P wave before QRS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Early wide QRS with compensatory pause","Runs of VT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Narrow QRS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Early wide QRS with compensatory pause', 'A PVC is an early, wide, bizarre QRS not preceded by aP wave, usually followed by a full compensatory pause before the next sinus beat.'),
('829021c8-db8e-53d6-8605-33e3b739ea19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the term for a ventricular bigeminy pattern on ECG?', '["Triplet PVC","Alternating narrow and wide","Every third beat is PVC","Couplets","Every other beat is a PVC"]'::jsonb, 'Every other beat is a PVC', 'Ventricular bigeminy is a rhythm in which every other beat is a premature ventricular complex (one sinus beat followed by one PVC).'),
('de2202a4-7f71-52ec-9565-02636b19337a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Couplet equals:', '["Eight PVCs","Bigeminy","Three consecutive PVCs","Two consecutive PVCs","Single PVC"]'::jsonb, 'Two consecutive PVCs', 'A couplet is defined as two consecutive premature ventricular complexes; three or more consecutive PVCs constitute ventricular tachycardia.'),
('343d7ed8-21a8-569b-891c-6987ee1c3dac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Nonsustained VT is defined as VT lasting:', '["Fast SVT","PVCs triplets","Three beats","VT >30 sec","VT lasting <30 sec"]'::jsonb, 'VT lasting <30 sec', 'Nonsustained ventricular tachycardia lasts less than 30 seconds and terminates spontaneously; sustained VT lasts more than 30 seconds or requires cardioversion.'),
('e9f16278-fc7b-5ae8-b8f1-cc0ec7b83e69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Sustained VT is defined as:', '["Bigeminy resulting in decreased functional activity","VT >30 sec or requiring cardioversion","VT <30 sec mediated by specific receptor activation","PVCs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trigeminy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VT >30 sec or requiring cardioversion', 'Sustained VT lasts more than 30 seconds or causes hemodynamic compromise requiring cardioversion.'),
('4626771f-17f0-5550-8a66-71412faa501b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG finding is characteristic of cardiac electrical alternans seen in large pericardial effusion/tamponade?', '["P wave alternans resulting in decreased functional activity","Alternating T waves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alternating QRS amplitude (seen in pericardial effusion/tamponade)","ST alternans without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PR alternans leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alternating QRS amplitude (seen in pericardial effusion/tamponade)', 'Electrical alternans, with beat-to-beat variation in QRS amplitude, occurs in large pericardial effusion/tamponade because the heart swings within the fluid-filled pericardium.'),
('aff4938a-2edb-5603-8253-7b16979eb9f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Low voltage QRS is seen in:', '["Myocardial infarction","All of the above","Pericardial effusion","COPD","Obesity"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Low-voltage QRS complexes are seen in pericardial effusion, COPD (hyperinflation), obesity, and myocardial infarction with loss of myocardium.'),
('af6340b1-92d3-513c-96c2-7f745d2bdfc8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG finding is characteristic of left bundle branch block (LBBB)?', '["Wide QRS with predominantly negative QS complex in V1","Narrow QRS complex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ST elevation in inferior leads mediated by specific receptor activation","Tall R in V1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Delta wave leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wide QRS with predominantly negative QS complex in V1', 'LBBB produces a widened QRS with a broad, predominantly negative (QS) complex in V1 and a notched positive R wave in the lateral leads V6/I.'),
('98c322c4-6302-5b21-9c42-8e904c0c81c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'LBBB on ECG makes which diagnosis difficult?', '["LVH resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute MI (cannot use ST elevation criteria)","RVH mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial enlargement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute MI (cannot use ST elevation criteria)', 'LBBB masks the standard ST-elevation criteria for acute MI, since secondary ST/T changes are already present; Sgarbossa criteria or serial comparison are needed.'),
('532711a2-3fb8-5dd3-940d-7e12c5b559b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The axis of QRS in normal adults is:', '["0° to -90°","-90° to -180°","0° to +180°","+90° to +180°","-30° to +90°"]'::jsonb, '-30° to +90°', 'The normal QRS axis in adults lies between -30° and +90°; left axis deviation is -30° to -90° and right axis deviation is > +90°.'),
('ac09db79-fdb8-585c-949a-29582db65cd5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Left anterior fascicular block (LAFB) has:', '["Normal axis resulting in decreased functional activity","RAD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wide QRS mediated by specific receptor activation","Left axis deviation (≥-45°) with qR in aVL and rS in II III aVF","RBBB pattern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Left axis deviation (≥-45°) with qR in aVL and rS in II III aVF', 'LAFB produces marked left axis deviation (≥-45°), a qR pattern in aVL, and rS complexes in the inferior leads, without significant QRS widening.'),
('b6805012-3dab-5371-9fa9-958155752fe8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Trifascicular block equals:', '["RBBB alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Bifascicular block + PR prolongation (first degree)","Bifascicular block alone mediated by specific receptor activation","LBBB alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Complete heart block leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bifascicular block + PR prolongation (first degree)', 'Trifascicular block is a bifascicular block (e.g., RBBB with LAFB or LPFB) combined with first-degree AV block, indicating impaired conduction in all three fascicles.'),
('c324c6a8-6bcb-5e96-875c-5f32353d355d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which condition produces inversion of the P wave in lead I and a rightward QRS axis on the ECG?', '["RA enlargement","RVH","LA enlargement","LVH","Dextrocardia"]'::jsonb, 'Dextrocardia', 'In dextrocardia, the heart lies on the right, producing an inverted P wave and reversed R-wave progression with a predominantly negative QRS in lead I.'),
('72691db0-8030-512e-a832-1c7b8bc09bee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG findings support a diagnosis of acute pulmonary embolism?', '["Right heart strain","RBBB","S1Q3T3 (S in I, Q in III, inverted T in III)","RAD","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Acute PE may produce the S1Q3T3 pattern, right-axis deviation, new RBBB, and signs of right-heart strain on the ECG, so multiple patterns can coexist.'),
('75425dfb-a4ff-512a-9527-cc090431dc1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which group of ECG findings is characteristic of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (cor pulmonale)?', '["All of the above","Right axis deviation","P pulmonale","Low voltage","Right ventricular hypertrophy"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'COPD/cor pulmonale classically shows right axis deviation, P pulmonale (right atrial enlargement), low-voltage QRS, and right ventricular hypertrophy, so all listed findings apply.'),
('fb7db259-1d3a-5bca-8dcc-f727a5167bae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Wellens syndrome is:', '["Left axis resulting in decreased functional activity","Tall R accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deep symmetrical T inversion in V2-V3 (critical proximal LAD stenosis)","ST elevation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P wave inversion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep symmetrical T inversion in V2-V3 (critical proximal LAD stenosis)', 'Wellens syndrome is biphasic or deeply inverted symmetric T waves in V2-V3 seen in a patient with critical proximal LAD stenosis, a high-risk pre-infarction pattern.'),
('4c0f5827-47d9-5773-b2a5-f3ef825efd8c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Isolated posterior MI on a standard 12-lead ECG shows:', '["T inversion resulting in decreased functional activity","ST elevation in V1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","P wave changes mediated by specific receptor activation","Q wave in V1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tall R in V1-V2 with ST depression (mirror image)"]'::jsonb, 'Tall R in V1-V2 with ST depression (mirror image)', 'Posterior MI produces reciprocal (mirror-image) changes in the anterior leads, namely tall R waves and ST depression in V1-V3.'),
('5a50406b-5a9f-5d3d-a111-7c7126d6f129'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Right ventricular MI is suspected with:', '["ST elevation in V4R (right-sided lead)","ST depression in V2","P wave changes","Deep Q in II III aVF","ST elevation in V1"]'::jsonb, 'ST elevation in V4R (right-sided lead)', 'In the setting of an inferior MI, ST elevation in the right-sided lead V4R indicates right ventricular involvement, which predicts hypotension with nitrates.'),
('baeaf92d-053b-504b-857e-196796661ac2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which sequence describes the ECG progression of progressive hyperkalemia?', '["QT prolongation resulting in decreased functional activity","Peaked T -> wide QRS -> sine wave -> VF","U wave mediated by specific receptor activation","ST elevation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T inversion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peaked T -> wide QRS -> sine wave -> VF', 'Rising serum potassium sequentially produces tall peaked T waves, widening of the QRS, loss of P waves, a sine-wave pattern, and finally ventricular fibrillation or asystole.'),
('1b5cd738-a150-508c-8c93-0bfc5bcb22d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The ECG in hypokalemia characteristically shows:', '["Peaked T","T flattening","U wave","ST depression","QT prolongation"]'::jsonb, 'U wave', 'Hypokalemia produces a characteristic prominent U wave together with T-wave flattening and ST depression; the U wave is the hallmark finding.'),
('b175ff3b-4fe2-53e7-8e99-e951924f61e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Amiodarone, sotalol, and dofetilide belong to which antiarrhythmic class?', '["Class I","Class II","Class III","Class IV","Unclassified"]'::jsonb, 'Class III', 'Amiodarone, sotalol, and dofetilide are Class III antiarrhythmics that predominantly block potassium channels, prolonging repolarization and the QT interval.'),
('e5bb7e87-9b39-52fd-a21a-d5ffc25a80ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which antiarrhythmic drug classes are effective for terminating and preventing supraventricular tachycardia?', '["Class IV only","All 4 classes (I II III IV)","Class II only","Class III only","Class I only"]'::jsonb, 'All 4 classes (I II III IV)', 'SVT can be managed with agents from all four Vaughan-Williams classes: class I (flecainide), class II (beta-blockers), class III (sotalol/amiodarone), and class IV (verapamil).'),
('da22c6a5-1830-521a-83b5-0c7c712021c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Adenosine terminates AVNRT by:', '["Blocking K+ channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Enhancing vagal tone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocking AV nodal conduction (hyperpolarization)","Blocking Ca2+ channels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking Na+ channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking AV nodal conduction (hyperpolarization)', 'Adenosine activates A1 receptors to open K+ channels, hyperpolarizing and slowing AV nodal conduction, interrupting the re-entry circuit and terminating AVNRT.'),
('bac90ebd-e240-500e-a6f1-531e7a08a333'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Lidocaine is a Class Ib antiarrhythmic used for:', '["WPW resulting in decreased functional activity","SVT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrial fibrillation mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventricular arrhythmias (VT PVCs) post-MI","Bradycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular arrhythmias (VT PVCs) post-MI', 'Lidocaine is a Class Ib agent used to treat ventricular arrhythmias, particularly PVCs and ventricular tachycardia in the setting of myocardial ischemia.'),
('a22b6166-b85e-5b15-bd42-6999a7d5ce3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Flecainide is a Class Ic drug used for:', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","VT post-MI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AF with CAD mediated by specific receptor activation","SVT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maintenance of sinus rhythm in AF (normal heart)"]'::jsonb, 'Maintenance of sinus rhythm in AF (normal heart)', 'Flecainide is used to maintain sinus rhythm in atrial fibrillation in patients with structurally normal hearts; it is avoided in patients with coronary disease (CAST trial).'),
('7e4b827a-18f9-5b0f-990c-878b5d6ce15d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Subendocardial tissue is most susceptible to ischemia because:', '["It is thicker resulting in decreased functional activity","It receives more blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It has fewer vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Higher wall tension compresses subendocardial vessels","It has lower oxygen consumption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher wall tension compresses subendocardial vessels', 'During systole, high intramyocardial wall tension compresses the subendocardial vessels, so the subendocardium has the least perfusion reserve and is most vulnerable to ischemia.'),
('bacd2f85-91c9-5867-801c-1c1bc2b52c12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which region of the ventricular wall is most vulnerable to ischemic injury and infarction?', '["Right atrium","Epicardium","Subendocardium","Mid-myocardium","Papillary muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Subendocardium', 'The subendocardium is most susceptible to ischemia because of high wall tension and the lowest coronary perfusion pressure, making it the first zone to infarct.'),
('5c342097-1968-537b-83a8-8e3844d78daa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Collateral coronary circulation is most developed in:', '["Young age","Chronic ischemia (gradual occlusion)","Healthy vessels","Acute occlusion","significant collaterals"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic ischemia (gradual occlusion)', 'Collateral vessels develop over time in response to chronic, gradual coronary occlusion, so patients with slowly progressive disease have better-developed collaterals.'),
('93fa3278-f0f8-5209-8d17-678e3ca34df3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which of the following is NOT a feature of ventricular remodeling after myocardial infarction?', '["Ventricular dilation","Myocyte hypertrophy","Interstitial fibrosis","Progressive wall thinning","Regression of hypertrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Regression of hypertrophy', 'Post-infarction remodeling involves progressive ventricular dilation, myocyte hypertrophy, and interstitial fibrosis that worsen chamber geometry; hypertrophy does not regress.'),
('9c753c29-2457-5f13-9f33-0f8585806bf3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'ACE inhibitors after myocardial infarction produce all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Improving survival","Decreasing ventricular remodeling","Reducing afterload and preload","Decreasing wall stress","Increasing contractility"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing contractility', 'ACE inhibitors improve survival, lessen remodeling, and reduce preload/afterload by lowering angiotensin II, but they do not directly increase myocardial contractility.'),
('59adef94-b345-5a5b-b3db-b3a245d24987'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The ST segment should be isoelectric normally because:', '["Atria are depolarized resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood conductivity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","There is active repolarization mediated by specific receptor activation","electrical activity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All ventricular cells are depolarized (no current flow)"]'::jsonb, 'All ventricular cells are depolarized (no current flow)', 'During the ST segment the entire ventricular myocardium is depolarized and at the same potential, so there is no potential difference and the trace returns to baseline.'),
('7dc2c6a1-a882-51af-9a3a-2e6d35741162'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which leads show ST elevation in inferior MI?', '["V5-V6","II III aVF","I aVL","V1-V2","V3-V4"]'::jsonb, 'II III aVF', 'Inferior MI is detected by ST elevation in the inferior leads II, III, and aVF, which face the inferior wall of the heart.'),
('155670ce-ecf7-5cd1-9fb2-f0c638f37d4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which leads show anterior MI?', '["V3-V4","V1-V4","I aVL","V5-V6","II III aVF"]'::jsonb, 'V1-V4', 'Anterior MI is reflected by ST elevation in the anterior precordial leads V1-V4, the territory supplied by the left anterior descending artery.'),
('4093010f-ec8b-5674-97e4-cbc766a95360'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Anteroseptal MI involves:', '["V1-V4","I aVL","II III aVF","V5-V6","V4-V6"]'::jsonb, 'V1-V4', 'Anteroseptal MI affects the precordial leads V1-V4, corresponding to the interventricular septum supplied by the septal branches of the LAD.'),
('893e3ae3-68ff-582f-869a-01037316c84b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Lateral MI involves:', '["II III aVF","V3R-V4R","I aVL V5-V6","V7-V9","V1-V3"]'::jsonb, 'I aVL V5-V6', 'Lateral wall MI produces ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6, the territory of the left circumflex or diagonal arteries.'),
('387963f5-677c-51b2-99a2-d0e7d29eb567'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Poor R wave progression (PRWP) in precordial leads is classically seen in:', '["Anterior myocardial infarction","Right-sided (RV) myocardial infarction","Acute pericarditis","Acute pulmonary embolism","Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior myocardial infarction', 'Poor R-wave progression is classically caused by a prior anterior myocardial infarction, which abolishes the septal and anterior R-wave forces.'),
('4b043964-684d-5927-8ebe-279f7c6dee12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The QT interval corrected for heart rate (QTc) is calculated by:', '["QT / sqrt(RR) (Bazett formula)","QT x sqrt(RR)","QT / RR","QT x RR (alternative wording)","QT + RR"]'::jsonb, 'QT / sqrt(RR) (Bazett formula)', 'The Bazett formula corrects the QT interval for heart rate: QTc = QT / sqrt(RR), where RR is in seconds.'),
('408b94d3-0b6c-50cc-8aff-16e571cddd38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Why is an R-on-T phenomenon (PVC falling on T wave) considered dangerous?', '["Benign PVC resulting in decreased functional activity","P wave on T accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SVT mediated by specific receptor activation","PVC landing on T wave -> risk of VF/VT","Normal variant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PVC landing on T wave -> risk of VF/VT', 'A PVC that interrupts the T wave (vulnerable period) can trigger re-entry and initiate ventricular fibrillation or polymorphic VT, which is why the R-on-T phenomenon is dangerous.'),
('2f114319-c056-5869-9323-46f9850ff765'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Overdrive pacing:', '["Slow pacing resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular pacing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pacing at faster rate to suppress tachyarrhythmia","Atrial pacing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","AV sequential pacing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pacing at faster rate to suppress tachyarrhythmia', 'Overdrive pacing suppresses a tachyarrhythmia by pacing the heart at a rate faster than the tachycardia, temporarily controlling rhythm while drugs such as amiodarone act.'),
('ed4ad712-689b-5cc1-8e48-86027a4acbfe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Cardiac resynchronization therapy (CRT) is indicated for:', '["Atrial fibrillation resulting in decreased functional activity","Sick sinus syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AV block mediated by specific receptor activation","VF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart failure with wide QRS (LBBB) and low EF"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure with wide QRS (LBBB) and low EF', 'CRT (biventricular pacing) improves outcomes in heart failure patients with a reduced ejection fraction and left bundle branch block/dyssynchrony producing a wide QRS.'),
('4814a1ef-d4fc-532c-8f0b-1fcde634f764'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Catecholaminergic polymorphic VT (CPVT) is due to:', '["LQTS resulting in decreased functional activity","K+ channel mutation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ryanodine receptor mutation (RYR2) causing Ca2+ leak during exercise","Na+ channel mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Structural heart disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ryanodine receptor mutation (RYR2) causing Ca2+ leak during exercise', 'CPVT is caused by mutations, commonly in the ryanodine receptor (RYR2) gene, leading to diastolic Ca2+ leak and stress/exercise-induced bidirectional polymorphic VT.'),
('f4647b9d-ba7a-527a-b570-32af1ef5e8de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which ECG pattern is characteristic of Brugada syndrome?', '["S wave in I resulting in decreased functional activity","Short QT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Long QT mediated by specific receptor activation","coved-type ST elevation in V1-V3 with incomplete RBBB","Delta wave leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'coved-type ST elevation in V1-V3 with incomplete RBBB', 'Brugada syndrome shows a coved (type 1) ST-segment elevation in the right precordial leads V1-V3 with an incomplete RBBB-like pattern, predisposing to polymorphic VT/VF.'),
('9b2c6974-3849-58e3-a9d2-396e4fc86dfe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Brugada syndrome is due to:', '["SCN5A mutation (Na+ channel loss of function)","LQT3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ca2+ channel mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","HCM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","K+ channel gain of function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SCN5A mutation (Na+ channel loss of function)', 'Brugada syndrome is most often caused by a loss-of-function mutation in the cardiac sodium channel gene SCN5A, which reduces inward Na+ current.'),
('322a9b6b-b198-5a30-bfc8-d90bc81bb0b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC) features:', '["Dilated cardiomyopathy resulting in decreased functional activity","HCM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fibrofatty replacement of RV -> VT with LBBB morphology","Situs inversus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","LVH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrofatty replacement of RV -> VT with LBBB morphology', 'ARVC is characterized by fibrofatty replacement of the right ventricular myocardium, causing VT with a left bundle branch block morphology and epsilon waves.'),
('2f3d110d-6795-5c25-9c06-59b93ab5fe59'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'ECG epsilon wave is seen in:', '["WPW resulting in decreased functional activity","LQTS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ARVC (post-excitable potentials in V1-V3)","Pericarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Brugada leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ARVC (post-excitable potentials in V1-V3)', 'The epsilon wave, a small deflection at the end of the QRS complex in V1-V3, is a marker of arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC).'),
('3d987932-8084-5b6c-a104-c14f1a50cce5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Long QT syndrome type 2 (LQT2) involves:', '["KCNJ2 resulting in decreased functional activity","ANK2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","KCNH2 (HERG IKr) -> events during emotion/sudden arousal","SCN5A without affecting the primary tissue pathway","KCNQ1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'KCNH2 (HERG IKr) -> events during emotion/sudden arousal', 'LQT2 results from mutations in KCNH2 (the HERG gene) causing defective IKr potassium current; arrhythmia is often triggered by emotion or sudden auditory arousal.'),
('b0cbb3c1-aa21-5102-86c8-f34ce25688c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Treatment of torsades de pointes includes:', '["IV Magnesium","Escape pacing","Lidocaine","Isoproterenol (if bradycardic)","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'IV Magnesium', 'Intravenous magnesium is the first-line treatment for torsades de pointes; overdrive/escape pacing and isoproterenol are added for bradycardia-dependent cases, while lidocaine is not first-line.'),
('9f26c7b9-4aea-56ef-8ea1-104de07faadd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which statement about autonomic control of the heart is correct?', '["Parasympathetic stimulation increases heart rate and contractility","Sympathetic stimulation decreases heart rate","Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate, contractility, and conduction","Parasympathetic stimulation increases AV conduction","Sympathetic stimulation causes sinus bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate, contractility, and conduction', 'Sympathetic (beta-1) stimulation increases heart rate (chronotropy), contractility (inotropy), and conduction velocity (dromotropy), whereas parasympathetic stimulation slows the SA and AV nodes.'),
('2fa8ac43-24d7-58b7-a6b8-5cf9ff8d2126'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The right vagus nerve primarily innervates:', '["AV node","SA node","Atria","Purkinje","Ventricles"]'::jsonb, 'SA node', 'The right vagus nerve predominantly innervates the SA node, so right vagal stimulation primarily slows the sinus rate; the left vagus mainly supplies the AV node.'),
('af9f6e04-2221-51e7-98c1-dbda83bc8381'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Sympathetic efferents to the heart arise from:', '["Lumbar chain resulting in decreased functional activity","Cervical ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Upper thoracic (T1-T4) via stellate ganglion","Vagus nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thoracic chain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Upper thoracic (T1-T4) via stellate ganglion', 'Cardiac sympathetic fibers originate from the upper thoracic (T1-T4) segments and pass through the stellate and middle cervical ganglia to the heart.'),
('d8b3afa4-33af-5592-a273-b1ed08d692a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The Bainbridge reflex is activated by:', '["Increased SVR resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased BP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carotid sinus stretch mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased CO without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distension of right atrium (venous return increase)"]'::jsonb, 'Distension of right atrium (venous return increase)', 'The Bainbridge reflex is a tachycardia induced by stretching the right atrium when venous return increases, mediated by atrial stretch receptors and the sympathetic nervous system.'),
('f3ea136e-db90-53b5-8bc4-52a87f3df1e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which stimulus activates the arterial baroreceptors of the carotid sinus and aortic arch?', '["Temperature resulting in decreased functional activity","Changes in BP (stretch of carotid sinus/aortic arch)","Changes in O2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Changes in pH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Changes in CO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Changes in BP (stretch of carotid sinus/aortic arch)', 'Arterial baroreceptors are stretch receptors that respond to changes in blood pressure (arterial wall stretch) in the carotid sinus and aortic arch.'),
('ad7945b1-e971-5fce-8e77-dc95f040c448'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which cranial nerves carry the afferent fibers of the arterial baroreceptors and chemoreceptors?', '["Hypoglossal (XII)","Glossopharyngeal (IX) and vagus (X)","Facial (VII)","Trigeminal (V)","Spinal accessory (XI)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal (IX) and vagus (X)', 'Baroreceptor and chemoreceptor afferents from the carotid sinus/body travel in the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) and from the aortic arch/body in the vagus nerve (X).'),
('578937d3-56c5-5885-8bdc-671be3857ced'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Arterial baroreceptors respond to which stimulus?', '["Only mean BP","Stretch (mean BP and pulse pressure)","Change in HR","Only pulse pressure","Change in volume"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch (mean BP and pulse pressure)', 'Baroreceptors are stretch-sensitive receptors that respond to both the mean arterial pressure and the rate of change of pressure (pulse pressure).'),
('2fa8c509-67aa-590a-931a-b5ab8e81f755'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Baroreflex response to hypotension is:', '["Vasodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased contractility accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bradycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic activation -> increased HR, contractility, SVR","Vagal activation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic activation -> increased HR, contractility, SVR', 'In response to hypotension, reduced baroreceptor firing disinhibits sympathetic outflow, increasing heart rate, contractility, and systemic vascular resistance to restore blood pressure.'),
('5d215f7e-e119-5c99-b12b-e1f131cbc38f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Baroreceptor resetting occurs in:', '["Sleep resulting in decreased functional activity","Exercise accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All conditions mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic hypertension (they adapt to higher pressure)","Hypotension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic hypertension (they adapt to higher pressure)', 'In chronic hypertension, baroreceptors reset to operate around the higher blood pressure, so they no longer buffer the elevated pressure; this contributes to the maintenance of hypertension.'),
('edc3e36c-112c-5f44-a3b5-1c778c97ef38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Chemoreceptors are located in:', '["Carotid and aortic bodies","Medulla","Atrial wall","Nucleus tractus solitarius","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid and aortic bodies', 'Peripheral chemoreceptors sensitive to arterial PO2, PCO2, and pH are located in the carotid and aortic bodies; central chemoreceptors respond to CSF CO2/pH.'),
('cfed16a9-6bb8-50f4-9772-2f9beece88a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The chemoreflex response to hypoxia is:', '["Vasodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Bradycardia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperventilation -> increased HR and BP (via sympathetic)","Hypotension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Apnea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation -> increased HR and BP (via sympathetic)', 'Hypoxia stimulates the carotid bodies, causing hyperventilation and a reflex sympathetic increase in heart rate and blood pressure to improve oxygen delivery.'),
('729dc443-321c-56af-a441-6795ebcd5189'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which reflex causes bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction when the face is immersed in cold water?', '["Tachycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction","Hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vasodilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction', 'The diving reflex triggered by cold-water face immersion produces vagally mediated bradycardia with sympathetically mediated peripheral vasoconstriction, conserving oxygen.'),
('6fc1b555-ebf6-55de-94d8-c5b9ece5193a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Pressure on the eyeball (oculocardiac reflex) produces which response?', '["Arrhythmia resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypertension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","response mediated by specific receptor activation","Tachycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pressure on eyeball -> bradycardia via trigeminal-vagal reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Pressure on eyeball -> bradycardia via trigeminal-vagal reflex', 'Ocular pressure stimulates trigeminal afferents, which evoke a vagal (parasympathetic) outflow producing bradycardia; this is the basis of the oculocardiac reflex.'),
('f59e0a45-eda8-5002-9896-436bd80fb922'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the primary mechanism of metabolic autoregulation of tissue blood flow?', '["Myogenic response resulting in decreased functional activity","Tissue metabolism releases vasodilators (adenosine, NO, K+)","Nerves control flow mediated by specific receptor activation","EDRF only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hormonal control leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue metabolism releases vasodilators (adenosine, NO, K+)', 'In metabolic autoregulation, increased tissue metabolism produces vasodilatory metabolites such as adenosine, nitric oxide, and K+, which locally increase blood flow.'),
('20a4a574-003b-5e9d-8fe3-38e6ab444fcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Active hyperemia is:', '["Passive flow resulting in decreased functional activity","Reactive hyperemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased blood flow with increased metabolism (exercise)","Constriction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased flow with metabolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased blood flow with increased metabolism (exercise)', 'Active hyperemia is the increase in blood flow that accompanies increased tissue metabolic activity, such as muscle blood flow during exercise.'),
('b405acd5-8fb0-53c1-a203-354c632c99da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Reactive hyperemia is:', '["Flow decrease after exercise resulting in decreased functional activity","Active hyperemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased flow after transient arterial occlusion","Constriction after occlusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steady flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased flow after transient arterial occlusion', 'Reactive hyperemia is the transient increase in blood flow that occurs after release of a period of arterial occlusion, resulting from accumulation of vasodilator metabolites.'),
('64a1a5ec-e1a9-5fb7-a99f-6197a428cdd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which statement describes the myogenic response of vascular smooth muscle?', '["Vascular smooth muscle contracts to stretch","Hormonal accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relaxes to stretch mediated by specific receptor activation","Nerve-mediated without affecting the primary tissue pathway","response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular smooth muscle contracts to stretch', 'The myogenic (Bayliss) response is the intrinsic contraction of vascular smooth muscle in response to increased stretch (transmural pressure), helping autoregulate blood flow.'),
('7dce8bf2-e3bd-5216-951a-1d4ee3c123ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Autoregulation of organ blood flow maintains:', '["Only neural resulting in decreased functional activity","Flow inversely to BP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constant flow despite BP changes (e.g. brain kidney)","Flow proportional to BP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","regulation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constant flow despite BP changes (e.g. brain kidney)', 'Autoregulation keeps organ blood flow relatively constant over a range of perfusion pressures (e.g., 60-160 mmHg), as seen in the brain and kidney, via myogenic and metabolic mechanisms.'),
('13c107a5-089e-589b-a1aa-1c1283da6e5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Endothelial-derived hyperpolarizing factor (EDHF) causes:', '["Vasoconstriction","Platelet aggregation","Smooth muscle contraction","Vasodilation by hyperpolarizing smooth muscle","Vasoconstriction via cGMP"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation by hyperpolarizing smooth muscle', 'EDHF opens calcium-activated potassium channels in vascular smooth muscle, hyperpolarizing the cells and producing vasodilation, particularly in smaller vessels.'),
('64d73bd8-2aa4-5917-9f39-2939c9384459'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Anaphylactic shock involves:', '["Massive vasodilation and increased capillary permeability","Heart failure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Obstructive shock mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiogenic shock without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypovolemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Massive vasodilation and increased capillary permeability', 'Anaphylactic shock is a distributive shock caused by massive release of histamine and other mediators, producing widespread vasodilation and increased capillary permeability.'),
('e70786c8-8ad8-5a12-afd2-0da3d1e170ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Cardiogenic shock is due to:', '["Sepsis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypovolemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pump failure (MI) -> decreased CO with pulmonary edema","Pulmonary embolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anaphylaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pump failure (MI) -> decreased CO with pulmonary edema', 'Cardiogenic shock results from primary pump failure, most often due to acute myocardial infarction, causing a marked fall in cardiac output with pulmonary congestion.'),
('57d2967a-1bbc-5b16-899f-ef777958a2dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Hypovolemic shock is caused by:', '["Neural failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood/fluid loss -> decreased preload and CO","Cardiac failure mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sepsis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blood/fluid loss -> decreased preload and CO', 'Hypovolemic shock follows hemorrhage or fluid loss, reducing preload and cardiac output; compensatory vasoconstriction cannot maintain tissue perfusion.'),
('47ded130-6406-5c0b-8d47-103a84c8c39b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Distributive (vasogenic) shock includes:', '["Pump failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Septic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic","Hypovolemic mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiogenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstructive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic', 'Distributive shock comprises septic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic shock, all characterized by widespread vasodilation and loss of vascular tone with normal-to-low preload.'),
('58f0746b-af7f-5b88-9c6c-b658e8430083'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Compensated shock is initially maintained by:', '["Hypotension resulting in decreased functional activity","Vagal activation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","compensation mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased CO without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Baroreflex activation (increased HR, contractility, SVR)"]'::jsonb, 'Baroreflex activation (increased HR, contractility, SVR)', 'In the compensatory stage of shock, the baroreflex increases heart rate, contractility, and systemic vascular resistance to maintain blood pressure and vital-organ perfusion.'),
('db8f1c5e-b079-51bc-bfe5-02f1435fd3ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the final consequence of prolonged ischemia leading to irreversible cell injury?', '["Reversible damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Full recovery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","acidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Mild acidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cell death from prolonged hypoperfusion (lactic acidosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell death from prolonged hypoperfusion (lactic acidosis)', 'Prolonged ischemia causes ATP depletion and lactic acidosis; if hypoperfusion continues, membrane failure and irreversible cell death (necrosis) occur.'),
('5094d776-b1ef-5c5e-a0be-2ef1567f7006'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The goal of fluid resuscitation in shock is:', '["Restore tissue perfusion (MAP >65 mmHg)","Increase HR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decrease CO mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease BP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Give blood only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Restore tissue perfusion (MAP >65 mmHg)', 'Fluid resuscitation aims to restore adequate tissue perfusion, generally targeting a mean arterial pressure above 65 mmHg.'),
('80bfd7b5-ef67-53d4-b28b-7e21b7446710'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Dopamine at low dose (1-5 microgram/kg/min):', '["Beta-1 -> inotrope resulting in decreased functional activity","Alpha-1 -> vasoconstrictor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","D1 receptor -> renal/mesenteric vasodilation","All of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","D2 agonist leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'D1 receptor -> renal/mesenteric vasodilation', 'At low doses dopamine activates D1 receptors, producing renal and mesenteric vasodilation; beta-1 and alpha-1 effects appear only at higher doses.'),
('61f0717e-46c1-53bf-a2ac-838082d90a80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Vasopressin (ADH) in shock produces:', '["V1 receptor-mediated vasoconstriction in high doses","Anti-diuretic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inotrope mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchodilator without affecting the primary tissue pathway","vasoactive effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'V1 receptor-mediated vasoconstriction in high doses', 'In pressor doses, vasopressin acts on V1 receptors on vascular smooth muscle to cause intense vasoconstriction, useful in vasodilatory (distributive) shock.'),
('a7e018ee-c20d-56c7-a272-2ce666622f32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Peripheral arterial disease (PAD) leads to:', '["Ulceration","Critical limb ischemia","Gangrene","Claudication (exertional leg pain relieved by rest)","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'PAD causes intermittent claudication and, as it progresses, critical limb ischemia, non-healing ulcers, and gangrene, so all listed manifestations occur.'),
('47345fd0-c373-55d6-8d3f-7becbeb10450'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Buerger disease (thromboangiitis obliterans) is:', '["Segmental thrombosing panarteritis in young smokers","With coronary disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","association with smoking mediated by specific receptor activation","Common in elderly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Large vessels only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Segmental thrombosing panarteritis in young smokers', 'Buerger disease is a segmental inflammatory thrombotic disease of small and medium arteries/veins occurring almost exclusively in young male cigarette smokers.'),
('4643176f-5d7e-5675-bc9c-1367a369c9b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which finding describes livedo reticularis affecting apparently normal skin?', '["Normal skin","Usually","Edema","Pallor","Cyanosis"]'::jsonb, 'Normal skin', 'Physiologic livedo reticularis can occur in normal skin, appearing as a mottled, lacy, reticulated discoloration; persistent widespread livedo, however, suggests underlying vasculopathy.'),
('682d108c-669f-5b59-9fbf-4dc23b9e1ae4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Cyanosis is:', '["Erythema resulting in decreased functional activity","Redness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pallor mediated by specific receptor activation","Bluish discoloration from deoxygenated Hb >5 g/dL","Jaundice leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bluish discoloration from deoxygenated Hb >5 g/dL', 'Cyanosis is the bluish discoloration of skin and mucous membranes that appears when the concentration of deoxygenated hemoglobin exceeds about 5 g/dL.'),
('55876013-20d4-5004-8a2c-83b205d0e46b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Central cyanosis is due to:', '["Anxiety resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiac right-to-left shunt or pulmonary disease","Stasis mediated by specific receptor activation","Cold exposure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peripheral vasoconstriction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac right-to-left shunt or pulmonary disease', 'Central cyanosis results from deoxygenated blood entering the systemic circulation, as in right-to-left cardiac shunts or severe pulmonary disease; peripheral stasis produces peripheral cyanosis.'),
('0c2ecdd2-8d77-525f-b9d4-beca71d60a67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the mechanism of tissue hypoxemia in cold (low-output) heart failure despite normal arterial oxygen saturation?', '["Shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral vasoconstriction with normal SaO2 (cold shock HF)","Methemoglobinemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral vasoconstriction with normal SaO2 (cold shock HF)', 'In low-output (cold) heart failure, intense peripheral vasoconstriction and stasis cause tissue hypoxia despite normal arterial oxygen saturation, because oxygen extraction is increased at the periphery.'),
('a9671ec7-e248-5a33-9c19-1f886cf814f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Differential cyanosis (affecting the lower body only) is seen in:', '["VSD resulting in decreased functional activity","AS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tetralogy mediated by specific receptor activation","ASD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Patent ductus arteriosus with Eisenmenger (reversed shunt)"]'::jsonb, 'Patent ductus arteriosus with Eisenmenger (reversed shunt)', 'With a reversed shunt through a patent ductus arteriosus (Eisenmenger physiology), desaturated blood enters the descending aorta, producing cyanosis of the lower body only.'),
('3663101b-b4af-556d-873a-a3382ed39445'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is the term for the catabolic state with skeletal muscle atrophy seen in advanced heart failure?', '["Diabetic complication resulting in decreased functional activity","Muscle wasting in chronic severe heart failure","Obesity mediated by specific receptor activation","BMI increase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","From poor nutrition only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle wasting in chronic severe heart failure', 'Cardiac cachexia is the severe muscle and fat wasting seen in advanced heart failure, driven by neurohormonal activation, inflammation, and reduced nutritional intake.'),
('49fd3610-ed74-5b30-baa6-87263e1f9c11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Pulsus bigeminus is:', '["Tachycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinus arrhythmia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regular rhythm mediated by specific receptor activation","Alternating strong and weak pulse due to PVCs every other beat","Rare pulse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alternating strong and weak pulse due to PVCs every other beat', 'Pulsus bigeminus is an alternating strong and weak peripheral pulse caused by bigeminal premature ventricular complexes.'),
('41010dd5-c30a-5fe6-94fd-949ecb35d399'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Paradoxical pulse is characteristically seen in:', '["Severe asthma/COPD","All of the above","Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac tamponade', 'Pulsus paradoxus is most classically seen in cardiac tamponade, where inspiration causes an exaggerated fall in systolic blood pressure; it is also found in severe asthma and constrictive pericarditis.'),
('45b43ff1-bbef-538f-80df-a0877372a9f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What is Kussmaul sign and in which condition is it characteristically seen?', '["JVP normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased JVP during inspiration (constrictive pericarditis)","Decreased JVP in inspiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Carotid pulse without affecting the primary tissue pathway","BP change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased JVP during inspiration (constrictive pericarditis)', 'Kussmaul sign is a paradoxical rise in jugular venous pressure during inspiration, classically seen in constrictive pericarditis and right ventricular failure.'),
('26e4959d-4fbe-5e88-9c9d-8c193f557260'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The jugular venous pulse (JVP) has how many positive waves?', '["1 (a)","3 (ac v)","2 (ac)","2 (av)","4 (ac vx)"]'::jsonb, '3 (ac v)', 'The JVP has three positive waves: the a wave (atrial contraction), c wave (tricuspid bulging during isovolumetric contraction), and v wave (atrial filling against closed tricuspid valve).'),
('5fd4eb8d-6beb-5a7a-a9d9-61226c181ba8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'What produces the C wave in the jugular venous pulse (JVP)?', '["TV opening resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular filling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carotid transmission mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrial contraction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial filling against closed TV during ventricular systole"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial filling against closed TV during ventricular systole', 'The c wave is produced by bulging of the closed tricuspid valve into the right atrium during early ventricular systole, together with transmission of the carotid pulse.'),
('45458d26-c4b9-59a7-a15a-60af1f0931c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The x descent of the JVP is:', '["Ventricular contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","RV filling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TV opening mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrial relaxation (decreased RA pressure during ventricular systole)","Venous filling leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial relaxation (decreased RA pressure during ventricular systole)', 'The x descent reflects the fall in right atrial pressure during ventricular systole due to atrial relaxation and downward displacement of the tricuspid valve.'),
('446b1bf0-1155-5695-a39f-6198e32fc002'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which heart sound corresponds to the rapid ventricular filling phase in early diastole?', '["Atrial contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricular systole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Atrial filling mediated by specific receptor activation","Carotid pulse without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rapid ventricular filling after TV opens"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid ventricular filling after TV opens', 'The S3 heart sound (ventricular gallop) is produced during rapid ventricular filling in early diastole as blood strikes the ventricular wall, seen in heart failure and volume overload.'),
('e9bbde08-1637-5e07-9e73-b88ea77f7982'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Kussmaul sign is seen in which condition?', '["ASD","VSD","Mitral stenosis","Constrictive pericarditis","Cardiac tamponade"]'::jsonb, 'Constrictive pericarditis', 'Kussmaul sign, an inspiratory rise in JVP, is classically seen in constrictive pericarditis (and restrictive cardiomyopathy), reflecting impaired right-heart filling.'),
('c807e7ac-6939-599d-b332-8f092e7f6487'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Friedreich sign is:', '["Prominent y descent in constrictive pericarditis","cannon a accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cannon v mediated by specific receptor activation","Rapid x descent without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Slow y leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prominent y descent in constrictive pericarditis', 'Friedreich sign is the prominent, abrupt y (and occasionally x) descent of the jugular venous pulse seen in constrictive pericarditis, indicating rapid early diastolic filling.'),
('fb202aef-65f7-5056-a6df-75ec6be87d2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The difference between constrictive pericarditis and cardiac tamponade is:', '["Constriction has paradoxus resulting in decreased functional activity","Both same accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tamponade has pulsus paradoxus; constriction has Kussmaul sign","Neither has signs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kussmaul only in tamponade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tamponade has pulsus paradoxus; constriction has Kussmaul sign', 'Cardiac tamponade characteristically produces pulsus paradoxus with equalized diastolic pressures, whereas constrictive pericarditis typically shows Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock.'),
('f1924d23-995d-55e7-b7e1-b91f15fc1cf7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Pericardial knock is a:', '["High-pitched early diastolic sound in constrictive pericarditis","Opening snap accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","S4 mediated by specific receptor activation","S3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Murmur leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High-pitched early diastolic sound in constrictive pericarditis', 'The pericardial knock is a high-pitched early diastolic sound occurring with the abrupt halt of ventricular filling in constrictive pericarditis, occurring slightly later than an opening snap.'),
('8c86b00d-3f6b-5225-8132-bebc2ba26d7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Tumor plop is:', '["Tricuspid valve sound resulting in decreased functional activity","Pericardial rub accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Opening snap mediated by specific receptor activation","S3 Knock without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sound from atrial myxoma moving during diastole"]'::jsonb, 'Sound from atrial myxoma moving during diastole', 'The tumor plop is a low-pitched sound produced as an atrial myxoma moves and strikes the ventricular wall as it prolapses through the AV valve in diastole.'),
('d08efa0f-7428-5b41-80ce-2db4238341d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The murmur of mitral regurgitation is:', '["Continuous resulting in decreased functional activity","Diastolic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Holosystolic blowing at apex radiating to axilla","Mid-systolic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decrescendo leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Holosystolic blowing at apex radiating to axilla', 'Mitral regurgitation produces a holosystolic (pansystolic) blowing murmur best heard at the apex that radiates to the axilla.'),
('9cb48034-cd97-50eb-b2b8-d9f30887f343'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'The murmur of aortic stenosis is:', '["Continuous resulting in decreased functional activity","Diastolic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Holosystolic mediated by specific receptor activation","Mid-systolic ejection murmur at right upper sternal border radiating to carotids","Early diastolic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mid-systolic ejection murmur at right upper sternal border radiating to carotids', 'Aortic stenosis produces a mid-systolic ejection (crescendo-decrescendo) murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border, radiating to the carotid arteries.'),
('85462146-ae9d-5aa8-b36b-e467a5070217'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which valvular lesion produces a mid-diastolic rumbling murmur at the apex with presystolic accentuation?', '["Decrescendo diastolic resulting in decreased functional activity","Continuous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mid-diastolic rumbling at apex with presystolic accentuation","Holosystolic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Systolic ejection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mid-diastolic rumbling at apex with presystolic accentuation', 'Mitral stenosis produces a low-pitched mid-diastolic rumbling murmur at the apex with presystolic accentuation, associated with a loud S1 and opening snap.'),
('95c2ce03-a922-5a95-81a5-60fd8802bf66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Austin Flint murmur is:', '["Tricuspid regurgitation resulting in decreased functional activity","VSD murmur accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mitral stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Mitral regurgitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mid-diastolic rumble in severe AR (due to mitral valve vibration)"]'::jsonb, 'Mid-diastolic rumble in severe AR (due to mitral valve vibration)', 'The Austin Flint murmur is a mid-diastolic rumbling murmur heard at the apex in severe aortic regurgitation, caused by the regurgitant jet vibrating the anterior mitral leaflet.'),
('0cb73938-d1bb-5a80-a0ae-290b22bfb24f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Graham Steell murmur is:', '["VSD resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary regurgitation murmur in pulmonary hypertension","Aortic regurgitation mediated by specific receptor activation","Tricuspid regurgitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitral regurgitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary regurgitation murmur in pulmonary hypertension', 'The Graham Steell murmur is an early diastolic murmur of pulmonary regurgitation caused by severe pulmonary hypertension, as seen in mitral stenosis or Eisenmenger syndrome.'),
('aa1bb7a8-91d3-5f95-a215-b61a79ffe2ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 1', 'Which cardiac tissue property distinguishes it from skeletal muscle?', '["Single nucleus centrally located","Voluntary","Striated with intercalated discs","Gap junctions","Non-striated"]'::jsonb, 'Striated with intercalated discs', 'Unlike skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle is involuntary and its cells are connected by intercalated discs with gap junctions, allowing action potentials to spread from cell to cell as a functional syncytium.'),
('d4a8dbe8-cba4-5e93-9a9d-b0ebd05da1ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 55-year-old female with hyperlipidemia is evaluated for substernal chest pressure that radiates to the left arm during shoveling snow. The pain subsides within 5 minutes of resting. (Cardio Case #1)', '["Coronary vasospasm in normal vessels","Microvascular dysfunction in the absence of atherosclerosis","Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply","Acute plaque rupture with subtotal thrombosis","Increased systemic vascular resistance due to acute aortic dissection"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply', 'Stable angina is characterized by exertional chest pain relieved by rest or nitroglycerin, driven by a fixed atherosclerotic plaque that limits coronary blood flow, causing myocardial oxygen demand to exceed supply during periods of exertion.'),
('48e74e0f-3597-510d-b582-86f253f57e91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 48-year-old female with a history of long-standing hypertension presents with progressive dyspnea on exertion, orthopnea, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea. Physical exam reveals bilateral basilar crackles and a laterally displaced apex beat. (Cardio Case #2)', '["Cardiac output (CO)","Stroke volume index","Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)","Systemic arterial pressure","Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)', 'Left-sided heart failure leads to backup of blood into the pulmonary circulation, causing pulmonary venous congestion and characteristically elevating the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP).'),
('f9fdf549-8c71-5bf5-a545-8ba7af0fa24f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 69-year-old office executive presents with exertional shortness of breath and hoarseness. Auscultation of the heart reveals a diastolic rumble at the apex with an opening snap that occurs closer to S2 during exercise. (Cardio Case #3)', '["Aortic stenosis due to senile calcification","Tricuspid stenosis secondary to carcinoid syndrome","Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease","Mitral regurgitation due to mitral valve prolapse","Aortic regurgitation secondary to infective endocarditis"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease', 'A diastolic rumbling murmur at the apex with an opening snap is the classic auscultatory finding of mitral stenosis, most commonly caused by chronic rheumatic heart disease.'),
('170374ee-1016-5dba-a740-5726b65473c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 42-year-old female presents with recurrent episodes of syncope and lightheadedness. On examination, the pulse rate is 35 bpm and regular. The ECG shows complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes. (Cardio Case #4)', '["First-degree AV block","Sinus bradycardia","Mobitz type II second-degree AV block","Third-degree (complete) AV block","Mobitz type I second-degree AV block"]'::jsonb, 'Third-degree (complete) AV block', 'Complete dissociation of P waves and QRS complexes with a slow, regular ventricular rate represents a third-degree (complete) heart block.'),
