('578c8953-b65c-5bdc-8fc8-734af1cf1415'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 55-year-old construction worker presents with exertional shortness of breath and hoarseness. Auscultation of the heart reveals a diastolic rumble at the apex with an opening snap that occurs closer to S2 during exercise. (Cardio Case #71)', '["Aortic stenosis due to senile calcification","Mitral regurgitation due to mitral valve prolapse","Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease","Aortic regurgitation secondary to infective endocarditis","Tricuspid stenosis secondary to carcinoid syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease', 'A diastolic rumbling murmur at the apex with an opening snap is the classic auscultatory finding of mitral stenosis, most commonly caused by chronic rheumatic heart disease.'),
('92575f79-6287-56e6-8867-b943da2084f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 48-year-old female presents with recurrent episodes of syncope and lightheadedness. On examination, the pulse rate is 35 bpm and regular. The ECG shows complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes. (Cardio Case #72)', '["Sinus bradycardia","First-degree AV block","Mobitz type II second-degree AV block","Mobitz type I second-degree AV block","Third-degree (complete) AV block"]'::jsonb, 'Third-degree (complete) AV block', 'Complete dissociation of P waves and QRS complexes with a slow, regular ventricular rate represents a third-degree (complete) heart block.'),
('dc616780-7823-5923-9f8c-57ae6ed52dcc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 55-year-old male with hyperlipidemia is evaluated for substernal chest pressure that radiates to the left arm during gardening. The pain subsides within 5 minutes of resting. (Cardio Case #73)', '["Acute plaque rupture with subtotal thrombosis","Increased systemic vascular resistance due to acute aortic dissection","Microvascular dysfunction in the absence of atherosclerosis","Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply","Coronary vasospasm in normal vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply', 'Stable angina is characterized by exertional chest pain relieved by rest or nitroglycerin, driven by a fixed atherosclerotic plaque that limits coronary blood flow, causing myocardial oxygen demand to exceed supply during periods of exertion.'),
('3e522ba0-db93-5716-a2c0-f100d19ea436'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 62-year-old office executive presents with shortness of breath when lying flat, coughing up pink frothy sputum, and a history of ischemic heart disease. Examination shows bilateral rales and an S3 gallop. (Cardio Case #74)', '["Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2)","Cardiac output (CO)","Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)","Stroke volume index","Systemic arterial pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)', 'Left-sided heart failure leads to backup of blood into the pulmonary circulation, causing pulmonary venous congestion and characteristically elevating the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP).'),
('9f0d0053-2fb1-59d6-8306-700fb141f03c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 69-year-old female originally from a developing country presents with progressive dyspnea and hemoptysis. Physical exam reveals a low-pitched, rumbling diastolic murmur loudest at the apex, preceded by an opening snap. (Cardio Case #75)', '["Tricuspid stenosis secondary to carcinoid syndrome","Aortic stenosis due to senile calcification","Aortic regurgitation secondary to infective endocarditis","Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease","Mitral regurgitation due to mitral valve prolapse"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease', 'A diastolic rumbling murmur at the apex with an opening snap is the classic auscultatory finding of mitral stenosis, most commonly caused by chronic rheumatic heart disease.'),
('de59071b-90be-5780-8154-c16cec9124ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 42-year-old female presents with recurrent episodes of syncope and lightheadedness. On examination, the pulse rate is 35 bpm and regular. The ECG shows complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes. (Cardio Case #76)', '["Third-degree (complete) AV block","First-degree AV block","Sinus bradycardia","Mobitz type II second-degree AV block","Mobitz type I second-degree AV block"]'::jsonb, 'Third-degree (complete) AV block', 'Complete dissociation of P waves and QRS complexes with a slow, regular ventricular rate represents a third-degree (complete) heart block.'),
('959e2f57-80c8-5a69-953f-7e000e0f099d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 62-year-old homemaker reports retrosternal chest discomfort that consistently occurs during climbing stairs and is completely relieved by rest or sublingual nitroglycerin. (Cardio Case #77)', '["Coronary vasospasm in normal vessels","Acute plaque rupture with subtotal thrombosis","Microvascular dysfunction in the absence of atherosclerosis","Increased systemic vascular resistance due to acute aortic dissection","Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply', 'Stable angina is characterized by exertional chest pain relieved by rest or nitroglycerin, driven by a fixed atherosclerotic plaque that limits coronary blood flow, causing myocardial oxygen demand to exceed supply during periods of exertion.'),
('81a25cdc-3617-5ada-84f0-cf9a0ed31f6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 55-year-old office executive presents with shortness of breath when lying flat, coughing up pink frothy sputum, and a history of ischemic heart disease. Examination shows bilateral rales and an S3 gallop. (Cardio Case #78)', '["Cardiac output (CO)","Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)","Systemic arterial pressure","Stroke volume index","Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2)"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)', 'Left-sided heart failure leads to backup of blood into the pulmonary circulation, causing pulmonary venous congestion and characteristically elevating the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP).'),
('88317e7a-ada4-5bd7-a32e-8ed26ac04fcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 69-year-old software engineer presents with exertional shortness of breath and hoarseness. Auscultation of the heart reveals a diastolic rumble at the apex with an opening snap that occurs closer to S2 during exercise. (Cardio Case #79)', '["Tricuspid stenosis secondary to carcinoid syndrome","Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease","Aortic regurgitation secondary to infective endocarditis","Mitral regurgitation due to mitral valve prolapse","Aortic stenosis due to senile calcification"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral stenosis secondary to rheumatic heart disease', 'A diastolic rumbling murmur at the apex with an opening snap is the classic auscultatory finding of mitral stenosis, most commonly caused by chronic rheumatic heart disease.'),
('ad87764b-2a64-5959-9317-1c0d938879ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 48-year-old construction worker is brought to the clinic after fainting. The ECG reveals a regular atrial rate of 80 bpm and a regular ventricular escape rate of 32 bpm, with no correlation between them. (Cardio Case #80)', '["Mobitz type II second-degree AV block","Mobitz type I second-degree AV block","Sinus bradycardia","Third-degree (complete) AV block","First-degree AV block"]'::jsonb, 'Third-degree (complete) AV block', 'Complete dissociation of P waves and QRS complexes with a slow, regular ventricular rate represents a third-degree (complete) heart block.'),
('a9157686-185f-5f4e-8823-f48f1eb7a6cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 55-year-old female with hyperlipidemia is evaluated for substernal chest pressure that radiates to the left arm during jogging. The pain subsides within 5 minutes of resting. (Cardio Case #81)', '["Increased systemic vascular resistance due to acute aortic dissection","Coronary vasospasm in normal vessels","Acute plaque rupture with subtotal thrombosis","Microvascular dysfunction in the absence of atherosclerosis","Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply"]'::jsonb, 'Fixed coronary artery obstruction leading to myocardial oxygen demand exceeding supply', 'Stable angina is characterized by exertional chest pain relieved by rest or nitroglycerin, driven by a fixed atherosclerotic plaque that limits coronary blood flow, causing myocardial oxygen demand to exceed supply during periods of exertion.'),
('1b689475-87f8-5e2d-904d-d0ab50bc9915'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 35-year-old female has a blood pressure of 155/92 mmHg on three readings. Under which threshold does this qualify as hypertension?', '["SBP ≥130 or DBP ≥80 mmHg (ACC/AHA 2017)","≥130/90 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","≥160/100 mediated by specific receptor activation","≥140/90 (JNC 7) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","≥150/90 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SBP ≥130 or DBP ≥80 mmHg (ACC/AHA 2017)', 'Per the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline, hypertension is defined as a sustained systolic BP ≥130 mmHg or diastolic BP ≥80 mmHg, so 155/92 meets the diagnostic threshold for stage 1 or 2 hypertension.'),
('5409dbd9-fc67-507d-878b-5cdb8b58d056'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Secondary causes of hypertension include:', '["Renal artery stenosis","Renal parenchymal disease","Pheochromocytoma","Primary aldosteronism","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Renovascular disease, chronic kidney/parenchymal disease, pheochromocytoma, and primary aldosteronism are all recognized secondary causes of hypertension; together they represent the complete correct set.'),
('eadeab32-9871-56e6-ae44-9452a9bd4d58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Renovascular hypertension is due to:', '["Essential HTN resulting in decreased functional activity","Parenchymal disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renal artery stenosis → ↑renin → ↑Ang II → ↑aldosterone","Coarctation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary aldosteronism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal artery stenosis → ↑renin → ↑Ang II → ↑aldosterone', 'Stenosis of a renal artery reduces renal perfusion pressure, triggering renin release and consequent angiotensin II and aldosterone formation, which raise blood pressure; this is the classic mechanism of renovascular hypertension.'),
('1e99679c-0449-5fab-bc2a-e93223de78b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Heart failure with preserved EF (HFpEF) is EF >:', '["50%","60%","35%","30%","40%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) is generally defined as an ejection fraction of 50% or greater despite clinical heart failure.'),
('43e498d9-6642-5e58-9bf5-ec3c96957989'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: Heart failure with preserved EF (HFpEF) is EF >:', '["50%","60%","35%","30%","40%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) is generally defined as an ejection fraction of 50% or greater despite clinical heart failure.'),
('784d1dfb-4ec3-5742-a377-a2d0dff61b21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pheochromocytoma is a:', '["Tumor of adrenal medulla secreting catecholamines (paroxysmal HTN)","Pituitary tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pancreatic tumor mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal tumor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenal cortex tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tumor of adrenal medulla secreting catecholamines (paroxysmal HTN)', 'Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of the adrenal medullary chromaffin cells that secretes catecholamines (epinephrine/norepinephrine), classically causing paroxysmal hypertension, palpitations, headache, and sweating.'),
('809ea1e1-5797-5f3d-bc49-c6b98aa76db6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome) features:', '["HTN with hypokalemia","Metabolic alkalosis","Low renin","Hypotension","HTN with hyperkalemia"]'::jsonb, 'HTN with hypokalemia', 'Aldosterone excess increases sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion, causing hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with suppression of renin; these findings characterize primary aldosteronism.'),
('e46df032-492f-5f4b-a3b7-c09424fbabf5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CVS Pt2: Which cardiac biomarker is highly specific for myocardial injury and remains elevated for 10-14 days?', '["early rise resulting in decreased functional activity","Cheaper accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urinary detection mediated by specific receptor activation","Only MI marker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac specific and long diagnostic window"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac specific and long diagnostic window', 'Cardiac troponin is highly specific for myocardial necrosis and remains elevated for up to 10-14 days, providing a long diagnostic window.'),
('6286cd25-c02d-5f86-a84a-362f7a1f2ddb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Which of the following drug classes is recommended as routine first-line antihypertensive therapy?', '["Thiazide diuretics","Beta-blockers","Alpha-1 blockers (prazosin)","Direct vasodilators (hydralazine)","Centrally acting agents (clonidine)"]'::jsonb, 'Thiazide diuretics', 'Thiazide diuretics, along with ACE inhibitors/ARBs and calcium channel blockers, are first-line antihypertensive agents. Beta-blockers, alpha-1 blockers, direct vasodilators, and centrally acting agents are not routine first-line choices absent a compelling indication.'),
('1da2c540-20fb-5283-bb29-daf7bd548198'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 55-year-old male presents to the ED with acute substernal chest pain radiating to the jaw and left arm, accompanied by diaphoresis. An ECG reveals ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded in this patient?', '["Left main coronary artery","Left circumflex artery (LCx)","Right coronary artery (RCA)","Left anterior descending artery (LAD)","Left marginal artery"]'::jsonb, 'Right coronary artery (RCA)', 'ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF indicates an acute inferior wall myocardial infarction. In approximately 85% of individuals (right-dominant circulation), the inferior wall is supplied by the posterior descending artery, which arises from the right coronary artery (RCA).'),
('8b9e81e8-69b5-59ba-8b12-7baf593dfbf4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, The normal systemic vascular resistance (SVR) is:', '["2500-3000",">3000","700-1600 dyn-s-cm-5","500-700 dyn-s-cm-5","1600-2500 dyn-s-cm-5"]'::jsonb, '700-1600 dyn-s-cm-5', 'Normal systemic vascular resistance is approximately 700-1600 dyn.s.cm-5, calculated as (MAP - CVP) / cardiac output.'),
('d2bd99da-7abc-586e-8329-27fd6461696e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'What is the primary mechanism of action of non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem)?', '["Only diuretic resulting in decreased functional activity","Block L-type Ca2+ channels in heart → ↓HR ↓contractility","Only β-blocker mediated by specific receptor activation","Only vasodilate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only block Na+ channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Block L-type Ca2+ channels in heart → ↓HR ↓contractility', 'Non-dihydropyridine CCBs (verapamil, diltiazem) act preferentially on L-type Ca2+ channels in cardiac tissue, reducing sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodal activity and myocardial contractility (negative chronotropy and inotropy).'),
('3fc5e251-94b7-571e-803f-9583d91e8313'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CVS Pt2: The ECG in hypokalemia characteristically shows:', '["Peaked T","T flattening","U wave","ST depression","QT prolongation"]'::jsonb, 'U wave', 'Hypokalemia produces a characteristic prominent U wave together with T-wave flattening and ST depression; the U wave is the hallmark finding.'),
('80591b40-3eb9-5c27-8564-71d1e7add26a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Positive inotropic agents include:', '["Acidosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypoxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcium channel blockers mediated by specific receptor activation","Sympathetic stimulation (catecholamines)","Beta-blockers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic stimulation (catecholamines)', 'Catecholamines acting through beta-1 adrenergic receptors increase intracellular Ca2+ and enhance contractility, making sympathetic stimulation a positive inotropic influence.'),
('2ac311fa-1233-5c75-8d55-afe9bd5f490c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pseudohypertension is:', '["Malignant HTN resulting in decreased functional activity","Falsely high BP reading due to non-compressible arteries (elderly with atherosclerosis)","Orthostatic HTN mediated by specific receptor activation","Labile HTN without affecting the primary tissue pathway","White coat HTN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Falsely high BP reading due to non-compressible arteries (elderly with atherosclerosis)', 'Pseudohypertension is an artifactually elevated cuff blood pressure in elderly patients with stiff, non-compressible, heavily calcified arteries; the cuff reading may overestimate the true intra-arterial pressure (Osler''s sign).'),
('059364d9-187b-5f72-92dd-b27d00f36194'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Isolated systolic hypertension in the elderly is due to:', '["Increased CO resulting in decreased functional activity","Aortic stiffness (decreased compliance) → widened pulse pressure","High renin mediated by specific receptor activation","High aldosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypervolemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aortic stiffness (decreased compliance) → widened pulse pressure', 'Age-related loss of large-artery compliance raises systolic pressure and lowers diastolic pressure, widening pulse pressure; since stroke volume is ejected into a stiff aorta, systolic (and pulse) pressure rises in isolated systolic hypertension.'),
('04888d0e-88a6-5497-9ed3-a3f80e65c0cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The concept of total peripheral resistance (TPR):', '["MAP / CO","Dynes/sec/cm-5","800-1200 dyn·s·cm-5 normally","All of the above","CO / MAP"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'TPR equals mean arterial pressure divided by cardiac output, is expressed in dyn·s·cm-5 (or Wood units), and has a normal value of roughly 800-1200 dyn·s·cm-5; these are all valid statements describing TPR.'),
('f2bbf31e-fde1-5efc-941d-b959f7667ffa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pulse wave velocity increases with:', '["Low BP resulting in decreased functional activity","Compliance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elasticity mediated by specific receptor activation","Arterial stiffness (age atherosclerosis)","Young age leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial stiffness (age atherosclerosis)', 'Pulse wave velocity increases in stiffer arteries (age, atherosclerosis) because the pulse wave travels faster through a rigid tube; higher PWV is an index of arterial stiffness and cardiovascular risk.'),
('c220baaa-8dea-5cdc-a9bb-26a08014dddf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In stiff arteries, reflected pressure waves return earlier and are amplified; this phenomenon is:', '["SVR resulting in decreased functional activity","HR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Wave reflection amplification (increases with arterial stiffness)","CO without affecting the primary tissue pathway","BP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wave reflection amplification (increases with arterial stiffness)', 'In stiff vessels the reflected wave from peripheral sites returns during systole instead of diastole, amplifying systolic pressure and widening pulse pressure; this wave reflection amplification worsens as arterial stiffness increases.'),
('0d12169d-9e36-5ba5-a61f-75778e0cd18f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Orthostatic hypotension due to reduced baroreceptor sensitivity is most common in:', '["Children resulting in decreased functional activity","Males accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Females mediated by specific receptor activation","Elderly (stiff arteries → reduced baroreceptor sensitivity)","Young adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elderly (stiff arteries → reduced baroreceptor sensitivity)', 'In the elderly, arterial stiffening reduces baroreceptor compliance and sensitivity, impairing the reflex compensation (tachycardia and vasoconstriction) on standing and predisposing them to orthostatic hypotension.'),
('c81525cf-7c4a-5d37-bb6d-5dace015359c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'What is the kidney''s dominant mechanism for long-term blood pressure regulation?', '["Increased Na+ reabsorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased Na+ excretion with increased BP (pressure natriuresis)","Increased renin mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased GFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased Na+ excretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased Na+ excretion with increased BP (pressure natriuresis)', 'Pressure natriuresis is the key long-term controller: a rise in arterial pressure directly increases renal sodium and water excretion, lowering blood volume and pressure; the kidney''s pressure-natriuresis curve sets the chronic BP level.'),
('dea9392e-389a-5fcb-b502-df23fe472b23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: What is the final consequence of prolonged ischemia leading to irreversible cell injury?', '["Reversible damage resulting in decreased functional activity","Full recovery accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","acidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Mild acidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cell death from prolonged hypoperfusion (lactic acidosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Cell death from prolonged hypoperfusion (lactic acidosis)', 'Prolonged ischemia causes ATP depletion and lactic acidosis; if hypoperfusion continues, membrane failure and irreversible cell death (necrosis) occur.'),
('5063a7f6-0bbc-5988-a1e0-fa37bff8270d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CVS Pt2: Peripheral arterial disease (PAD) leads to:', '["Ulceration","Critical limb ischemia","Gangrene","Claudication (exertional leg pain relieved by rest)","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'PAD causes intermittent claudication and, as it progresses, critical limb ischemia, non-healing ulcers, and gangrene, so all listed manifestations occur.'),
('3086f2aa-3f38-5eaf-8514-188ed45ac963'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Phase 2 (plateau) of the ventricular action potential is due to:', '["Cl- influx resulting in decreased functional activity","K+ efflux only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ca2+ influx (L-type) balanced by K+ efflux","Ca2+ efflux without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Na+ influx leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ influx (L-type) balanced by K+ efflux', 'The plateau is maintained by a balance between inward L-type Ca2+ current and outward K+ current, keeping the membrane near 0 mV and prolonging the action potential.'),
('6ca923a4-046a-5e30-a9a9-c4cd2c5161df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: Pulsus bigeminus is:', '["Tachycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinus arrhythmia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regular rhythm mediated by specific receptor activation","Alternating strong and weak pulse due to PVCs every other beat","Rare pulse leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alternating strong and weak pulse due to PVCs every other beat', 'Pulsus bigeminus is an alternating strong and weak peripheral pulse caused by bigeminal premature ventricular complexes.'),
('90db42b8-5c31-58dd-930d-2c9abd6e3ea3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Salt-sensitive hypertension is more common in:', '["Diabetes","CKD","All of the above","African-Americans","Elderly"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Salt sensitivity is more common in African-Americans, the elderly, and patients with diabetes or chronic kidney disease; these groups are particularly prone to blood pressure elevation from dietary sodium.'),
('7eb1d134-7b90-51c1-abf5-88fcb50ed98f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: Distributive (vasogenic) shock includes:', '["Pump failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Septic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic","Hypovolemic mediated by specific receptor activation","Cardiogenic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstructive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Septic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic', 'Distributive shock comprises septic, anaphylactic, and neurogenic shock, all characterized by widespread vasodilation and loss of vascular tone with normal-to-low preload.'),
('cac0d230-ed07-55eb-a66b-6e1cd64d39b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (GRA):', '["ENaC mutation resulting in decreased functional activity","Chimeric gene (CYP11B1/B2) → ACTH-regulated aldosterone synthesis → HTN","Coarctation mediated by specific receptor activation","Mineralocorticoid receptor mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Renin tumor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Chimeric gene (CYP11B1/B2) → ACTH-regulated aldosterone synthesis → HTN', 'GRA results from a chimeric CYP11B1/CYP11B2 gene so that aldosterone synthase is expressed under the control of ACTH in the zona fasciculata; aldosterone secretion is therefore glucocorticoid-suppressible and causes early-onset hypertension.'),
('e0c1cf6b-2ed4-5a7c-8ad3-1acf18aa3a64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 45-year-old male presents with hypertension, hypokalemia, and weakness. Accumulation of which steroid explains the blood pressure elevation?', '["↓Cortisol → ↑ACTH → ↑11-deoxycortisol + ↑deoxycorticosterone (DOC) → HTN","Pheochromocytoma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renin excess mediated by specific receptor activation","Aldosteronoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ENaC mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↓Cortisol → ↑ACTH → ↑11-deoxycortisol + ↑deoxycorticosterone (DOC) → HTN', 'In 11β-hydroxylase deficiency, cortisol synthesis is impaired, raising ACTH and shunting precursors to 11-deoxycortisol and the mineralocorticoid deoxycorticosterone (DOC); DOC excess causes hypertension and hypokalemia.'),
('d7c1255a-f006-588e-9c3c-ff6ecf974609'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: The SA node is supplied by:', '["LAD resulting in decreased functional activity","Sinoatrial nodal artery (RCA in 60%)","LCX mediated by specific receptor activation","PDA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagonal branch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sinoatrial nodal artery (RCA in 60%)', 'The SA nodal artery arises from the right coronary artery in about 60% of people and from the left circumflex in the remainder, supplying the sinoatrial node.'),
('1f9d7817-fedf-5a5b-a40e-a9c10c24a6c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME) is due to:', '["Liddle resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperaldosteronism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renin excess mediated by specific receptor activation","GRA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","11β-HSD2 deficiency → cortisol occupies MR → HTN"]'::jsonb, '11β-HSD2 deficiency → cortisol occupies MR → HTN', 'In AME, deficiency of 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 in the kidney allows cortisol to act on mineralocorticoid receptors, causing sodium retention, hypokalemia, and hypertension with low renin and aldosterone.'),
('f1c9cc75-87a1-5311-b16e-41c90ce72248'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: The SA node is located at:', '["Junction of SVC and right atrium","Left atrium","Coronary sinus","AV junction","Interventricular septum"]'::jsonb, 'Junction of SVC and right atrium', 'The sinoatrial node lies in the upper wall of the right atrium at its junction with the superior vena cava, the site of the normal cardiac pacemaker.'),
('ef9c8d60-bfbb-57aa-86c5-cc5b86dcd3ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The microcirculation (the site of fluid and nutrient exchange) consists of:', '["Only arterioles","Sinusoids","Only venules","Arterioles capillaries venules","Only capillaries"]'::jsonb, 'Arterioles capillaries venules', 'The microcirculation comprises the terminal arterioles, capillaries, and venules; the capillaries are the principal exchange vessels where fluid, gases, and solutes move between blood and interstitial fluid.'),
('7a07d512-c8c6-5073-bbac-0d94938eb2db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Which hemodynamic variable primarily determines the rate of blood flow into individual capillaries?', '["Central venous pressure","Arteriolar resistance and precapillary sphincter tone","Lymphatic drainage pressure","Interstitial fluid hydrostatic pressure","Venous compliance"]'::jsonb, 'Arteriolar resistance and precapillary sphincter tone', 'Blood flow into a capillary bed is governed by arteriolar resistance and the tone of precapillary sphincters, which regulate how much blood enters each capillary and distribute flow among different capillary beds.'),
('77866343-3ec8-5ee7-acee-5eed65d8f6f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: Laplace''s law for a spherical vessel states that wall tension:', '["T = Px r / 2h","T = Px r","T = Px r^2","T = Px rx h","T = Px h / r"]'::jsonb, 'T = Px r / 2h', 'For a sphere, wall tension T = (pressure x radius) / (2 x wall thickness); a dilated ventricle must develop more tension to generate the same pressure.'),
('0d11389e-f393-5ba8-93e2-e78912f482b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, The chemoreflex response to hypoxia is:', '["Vasodilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Bradycardia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperventilation -> increased HR and BP (via sympathetic)","Hypotension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Apnea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation -> increased HR and BP (via sympathetic)', 'Hypoxia stimulates the carotid bodies, causing hyperventilation and a reflex sympathetic increase in heart rate and blood pressure to improve oxygen delivery.'),
('bd241de3-3df4-50e7-9e16-f7cea99a4353'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Fenestrated capillaries are found in:', '["Skin resulting in decreased functional activity","Kidney glomerulus intestinal villi endocrine glands","Muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Brain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kidney glomerulus intestinal villi endocrine glands', 'Fenestrated capillaries have endothelial pores that permit rapid exchange of fluid and small molecules; they are found in the renal glomerulus, intestinal villi, and endocrine glands.'),
('0b1fdeb1-05b3-58f9-b828-4dce7a296faf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The glycocalyx on capillary endothelium:', '["Attracts platelets","Promotes coagulation","Regulates permeability and mechanotransduction","Functions in transport of fats","Barrier to O2"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates permeability and mechanotransduction', 'The endothelial glycocalyx is a carbohydrate-rich layer that regulates capillary permeability, senses shear stress (mechanotransduction) for nitric oxide release, and protects the endothelium; its disruption increases permeability and edema.'),
('2a1afd0b-cf14-53fb-9dad-e14656d7f266'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'How much interstitial fluid can accumulate before clinically visible edema appears?', '["Osmotic compensation resulting in decreased functional activity","Immediate edema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only 0.5 L mediated by specific receptor activation","The tissue can accumulate 2-3 L of fluid before visible edema (lymphatic reserve)","Reserve absent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The tissue can accumulate 2-3 L of fluid before visible edema (lymphatic reserve)', 'The lymphatic system has a large reserve capacity, so about 2-3 L of excess interstitial fluid can accumulate before lymphatic flow is overwhelmed and clinically detectable edema develops.'),
('e974f498-5e41-50ff-8c4a-4a6467947e4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Alveolar edema develops when:', '["Hydrostatic pressure exceeds 25 mmHg","Interstitial pressure rises","Lymph flow increases","Capillary damage occurs","Oncotic pressure rises"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrostatic pressure exceeds 25 mmHg', 'When pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure rises above the plasma oncotic pressure (25 mmHg), filtration exceeds reabsorption and fluid floods the interstitium and alveoli, producing alveolar pulmonary edema.'),
('3ffa3381-fcaf-5638-9ebe-0494532bad77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 50-year-old female recently traveled to high altitude and presents with headache, dyspnea, and fatigue. This is most consistent with:', '["Neurogenic resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiogenic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oxygen toxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Re-expansion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Non-cardiogenic (exaggerated HPV → overperfusion of some capillaries → leak)"]'::jsonb, 'Non-cardiogenic (exaggerated HPV → overperfusion of some capillaries → leak)', 'High-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE) is a non-cardiogenic edema in which uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction overperfuses some capillary regions, raising pressure and causing a protein-rich leak into the alveoli.'),
('72246ead-1c27-5fa0-9dc2-fdf49a29e95a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 70-year-old female presents with chronic cough and dyspnea. Which etiological factor is primarily responsible for neurogenic pulmonary edema?', '["Left heart failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Aspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Massive sympathetic discharge → pulmonary vasoconstriction + increased permeability","Sepsis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Valve disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Massive sympathetic discharge → pulmonary vasoconstriction + increased permeability', 'Neurogenic pulmonary edema follows a surge of sympathetic (catecholamine) discharge, as in head injury or seizures, which causes pulmonary vasoconstriction, capillary stress, and increased permeability, leading to edema.'),
('a3778123-2923-55a9-9733-49b526aba604'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Re-expansion pulmonary edema occurs after:', '["Any lung surgery resulting in decreased functional activity","Slow re-expansion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collapse alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Lobectomy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rapid drainage of large pleural effusion/pneumothorax"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid drainage of large pleural effusion/pneumothorax', 'Rapid evacuation of a large pleural effusion or pneumothorax can precipitate ipsilateral re-expansion pulmonary edema, likely due to sudden stretch and reperfusion injury of the collapsed lung.'),
('038abc04-6663-5083-b8b7-75f54de1e1a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pulmonary edema treatment includes:', '["Diuresis (furosemide) vasodilators (nitrates) O2 positive pressure","Only fluids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only inotropes mediated by specific receptor activation","Only O2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only diuretics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diuresis (furosemide) vasodilators (nitrates) O2 positive pressure', 'Cardiogenic pulmonary edema is treated by reducing preload and afterload with diuretics (furosemide) and vasodilators (nitrates), plus supplemental oxygen and positive airway pressure; fluids are generally avoided.'),
('634a43aa-0ba6-5e76-8d4b-877bf0547dad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The Frank-Starling curve shifts downward in:', '["Increased preload resulting in decreased functional activity","Exercise accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Athlete mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart failure (↓contractility → lower SV for same EDV)","Normal heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure (↓contractility → lower SV for same EDV)', 'In heart failure, reduced contractility flattens and shifts the Frank-Starling curve downward and to the right, so stroke volume is lower for any given end-diastolic volume compared with a normal or exercised heart.'),
('87320af3-1647-59d3-8f35-d44fbbcf0ca1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A 40-year-old female presents with progressive dyspnea, orthopnea, and peripheral edema. Which condition best fits compensated (stable) heart failure?', '["Decompensated resulting in decreased functional activity","Reserve lost accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximal output mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased output without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stable symptoms with normal cardiac output at rest but reduced reserve"]'::jsonb, 'Stable symptoms with normal cardiac output at rest but reduced reserve', 'In compensated heart failure, compensatory mechanisms maintain a normal cardiac output at rest, but cardiac reserve is reduced, so symptoms such as dyspnea appear with exertion; decompensation occurs when resting output falls and congestion develops.'),
('f6bb24c3-2930-56a0-af5d-788f5189dbcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Decompensated heart failure has:', '["Symptoms only resulting in decreased functional activity","High output accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Worsening symptoms low CO pulmonary edema","Stable without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Improving leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Worsening symptoms low CO pulmonary edema', 'Decompensated heart failure is characterized by worsening symptoms, low cardiac output, and congestion (pulmonary/peripheral edema) because compensatory mechanisms can no longer maintain adequate cardiac performance.'),
('76429385-8046-5a0c-8668-6a9cad739f6f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'High-output heart failure causes include:', '["Thyrotoxicosis beriberi anemia Paget AV fistula","Valvular heart disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiomyopathy mediated by specific receptor activation","Ischemic heart disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrotoxicosis beriberi anemia Paget AV fistula', 'High-output heart failure arises from conditions that raise cardiac output demands, including thyrotoxicosis, beriberi (thiamine deficiency), severe anemia, Paget disease of bone, and arteriovenous fistulas.'),
('702155ce-deda-5829-b14d-2f8fc8d4f147'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Cardiac myocytes respond to stretch and volume overload by secreting:', '["Thyroid hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","Catecholamines accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ADH mediated by specific receptor activation","Renin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ANP and BNP (natriuretic peptides)"]'::jsonb, 'ANP and BNP (natriuretic peptides)', 'Atrial myocytes secrete atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) in response to stretch, and ventricular myocytes release B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) during volume/pressure overload; BNP is a useful marker for heart failure.'),
('9e084ed1-f7c1-591b-837f-67e6a53a559b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Natriuretic peptide receptors (NPR-A/B) produce their effects via:', '["Ca2+ resulting in decreased functional activity","IP3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","cGMP (vasodilation natriuresis)","DAG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","cAMP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'cGMP (vasodilation natriuresis)', 'Natriuretic peptides bind NPR-A/B receptors that have guanylyl cyclase activity, generating cGMP, which produces vasodilation, natriuresis, diuresis, and inhibition of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system.'),
('9dc2e305-8809-581c-9365-e212c194da9a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, How does inspiration affect the splitting of heart sounds S2?', '["Inspiration (P2 delayed)","Expiration widens","Expiration (A2 earlier)","Inspiration narrows","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiration (P2 delayed)', 'During inspiration, increased venous return delays right ventricular ejection, so the pulmonic component (P2) is delayed and physiologic splitting of S2 widens.'),
('788fb622-fe05-5b57-a11c-7d640cb8b883'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Hydralazine + isosorbide dinitrate in heart failure:', '["Vasodilator combination (especially beneficial in African Americans)","ARB accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","β-blocker mediated by specific receptor activation","Aldosterone antagonist without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACEi leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilator combination (especially beneficial in African Americans)', 'The hydralazine-isosorbide dinitrate combination provides balanced arterial and venous vasodilation and improves outcomes in heart failure, with particular benefit shown in African American patients; it is used when ACE inhibitors/ARBs are not tolerated or as add-on therapy.'),
('637336ad-385f-5452-826f-2bcb1498b55d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Function of pericardial fluid:', '["Lubrication reduces friction during heart beats","Protection from trauma","Cooling of the heart","Oxygenation of myocardium","Shock absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Lubrication reduces friction during heart beats', 'The small volume of pericardial fluid lubricates the visceral and parietal pericardial surfaces, reducing friction between the moving heart and surrounding structures during each cardiac cycle.'),
('efe1659f-61fd-5867-a3b2-0c38a6f19638'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Cardiac tamponade physiology:', '["No significant effect resulting in decreased functional activity","↑CO accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bradycardia mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasodilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pericardial effusion → compression of all chambers → ↓filling → ↓CO"]'::jsonb, 'Pericardial effusion → compression of all chambers → ↓filling → ↓CO', 'Accumulation of pericardial fluid raises intrapericardial pressure, compressing all cardiac chambers, reducing ventricular filling, stroke volume, and cardiac output; signs include hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and distended neck veins.'),
('39ce049e-d96f-5c64-bd42-92ec86264d8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pulsus paradoxus mechanism in cardiac tamponade:', '["Systolic only resulting in decreased functional activity","Inspiration → increased RV filling → pushes septum left → ↓LV filling → ↓LV SV → ↓SBP","Drop in BP with expiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Decrease in pulse without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only expiration effect leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inspiration → increased RV filling → pushes septum left → ↓LV filling → ↓LV SV → ↓SBP', 'During inspiration in tamponade, increased right ventricular filling pushes the interventricular septum leftward (ventricular interdependence), reducing left ventricular filling, stroke volume, and systolic blood pressure—a fall >10 mmHg on inspiration is pulsus paradoxus.'),
('e6a4d6af-c541-5917-a58d-18d4fb3c33b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pericardiocentesis is treatment for:', '["MI","Cardiac tamponade (drains fluid)","Pleural effusion","Pericarditis without effusion","Pneumothorax"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac tamponade (drains fluid)', 'Pericardiocentesis is needle aspiration of pericardial fluid and is the definitive emergency treatment for cardiac tamponade, promptly relieving chamber compression and restoring cardiac output.'),
('8935e87f-1444-5519-a6f7-c503dbee7b4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Myocardial contractility can be measured by:', '["CO resulting in decreased functional activity","SV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","EF mediated by specific receptor activation","dP/dt max (rate of pressure rise)","EDV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'dP/dt max (rate of pressure rise)', 'The maximum rate of left ventricular pressure rise during isovolumic contraction (dP/dt max) is an index of contractility that is relatively independent of preload and afterload, though it is not completely load-independent.'),
('3b29ef60-4c63-599b-a2e1-453700cc1b71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The preload recruitable stroke work (PRSW):', '["CO resulting in decreased functional activity","EF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Load-independent measure of contractility (slope of stroke work vs EDV)","EDV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Load-independent measure of contractility (slope of stroke work vs EDV)', 'PRSW is the slope of the relationship between stroke work and end-diastolic volume; because it is a linear, preload- and afterload-insensitive index, it is considered a relatively load-independent measure of contractility.'),
('99ed0436-11c7-5bc0-8e4d-f987ab6cf80a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, A 35-year-old female presents with exertional chest pain and shortness of breath. On auscultation a presystolic gallop is heard. Which mechanism produces this sound?', '["AV valve closing resulting in decreased functional activity","Rapid filling accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Semilunar closing mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic opening without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial contraction (presystolic gallop)"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial contraction (presystolic gallop)', 'The presystolic gallop (S4) is caused by atrial contraction forcing blood into a stiff, noncompliant ventricle in late diastole.'),
('84900799-822f-58c0-9bf5-c48e4dd01a7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: The windkessel effect refers to:', '["Sympathetic tone resulting in decreased functional activity","Venous return accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Aortic valve function mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillary exchange without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elastic arteries smoothing pulsatile flow"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic arteries smoothing pulsatile flow', 'The windkessel effect is the storing of elastic energy by distensible large arteries in systole and its release in diastole, converting pulsatile flow into more continuous flow.'),
('16fdd336-2851-54ff-aa15-535794fee1e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The end-diastolic pressure-volume relationship (EDPVR) represents:', '["Ventricular compliance (diastolic function)","Preload accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Afterload mediated by specific receptor activation","Heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contractility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricular compliance (diastolic function)', 'The EDPVR plots left ventricular end-diastolic pressure against end-diastolic volume; its slope reflects ventricular compliance, so it is an index of diastolic function—stiff ventricles have a steeper (shifted up) EDPVR.'),
('77a55027-ac67-57f2-b53a-a51733f31067'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Increased ventricular stiffness (decreased compliance) is seen in:', '["Hypertrophy fibrosis ischemia (HFpEF)","Normal young accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Volume overload mediated by specific receptor activation","Exercise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Athlete leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertrophy fibrosis ischemia (HFpEF)', 'Ventricular hypertrophy, fibrosis, and ischemia reduce chamber compliance (increased stiffness), producing diastolic dysfunction with a steeper EDPVR; this underlies heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF).'),
('799f25a7-fd77-50e4-ab12-7dde5dda12df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CVS Pt2: Carotid sinus massage:', '["Increases vagal tone -> slows SA node and AV conduction","Increases BP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Speeds heart rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Aroused by cervical massage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activates SA node leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases vagal tone -> slows SA node and AV conduction', 'Carotid sinus massage stimulates baroreceptors, increasing vagal (parasympathetic) tone, which slows SA node firing and AV conduction, useful in terminating SVT.'),
('d5147907-4288-5667-81c9-db65bbbed88b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Right heart catheterization criteria for pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension include:', '["LV disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-capillary PH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only secondary PH mediated by specific receptor activation","Group 2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pre-capillary PH (mPAP >25 PCWP <15 PVR >3 Wood)"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-capillary PH (mPAP >25 PCWP <15 PVR >3 Wood)', 'Pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension is defined as a mean pulmonary artery pressure >25 mmHg with a normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (<15 mmHg) and an elevated pulmonary vascular resistance (>3 Wood units); it is seen in PAH, lung disease, and CTEPH.'),
('be532b69-ccd6-5d52-8d75-970e368f61ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: The jugular venous pulse (JVP) has how many positive waves?', '["1 (a)","3 (ac v)","2 (ac)","2 (av)","4 (ac vx)"]'::jsonb, '3 (ac v)', 'The JVP has three positive waves: the a wave (atrial contraction), c wave (tricuspid bulging during isovolumetric contraction), and v wave (atrial filling against closed tricuspid valve).'),
('5093ade2-334b-52d5-b5f7-39d23df9da5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, Compensatory mechanisms in heart failure include:', '["Ventricular hypertrophy","RAAS activation","Sympathetic activation","Brain natriuretic peptide","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The failing heart is compensated by ventricular hypertrophy/remodeling, activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, sympathetic activation, and increased BNP release.'),
('08a12860-cc47-5c32-ab60-ed346ba0ae2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: Most common ACE inhibitor side effect:', '["Dry cough","Hyperkalaemia","Angioedema","First-dose hypotension","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Dry cough', '5-20% from bradykinin/substance P accumulation.'),
('b96668d8-0777-596e-95e4-667b6538ec03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The normal peripheral arterial pressure waveform has:', '["Sharp systolic upstroke dicrotic notch (incisura) diastolic runoff","Notch only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Systolic only mediated by specific receptor activation","Monophasic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diastolic only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sharp systolic upstroke dicrotic notch (incisura) diastolic runoff', 'A normal peripheral arterial pulse waveform consists of a sharp systolic upstroke, a dicrotic notch (incisura) representing aortic valve closure, and gradual diastolic runoff; the contour changes in aortic stenosis and other conditions.'),
('a8e769d1-d97a-5826-b5ed-1fefeabee31d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Which heart sound is produced by closure of the aortic and pulmonic (semilunar) valves?', '["Lung inflation","RV ejection","S2 (second heart sound)","Ventricular filling","Mitral valve opening"]'::jsonb, 'S2 (second heart sound)', 'The second heart sound (S2) is produced by closure of the aortic valve (A2) followed by the pulmonic valve (P2); it marks the end of systole and signals the onset of isovolumic relaxation.'),
('2037d7dd-a1aa-51e7-b263-fc28e5af3f13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The PaO2/FiO2 ratio (P/F ratio) is used to:', '["Assess severity of hypoxemia (normal >400; ARDS <300)","Assess CO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measure cardiac function mediated by specific receptor activation","Evaluate kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Assess liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Assess severity of hypoxemia (normal >400; ARDS <300)', 'The P/F ratio quantifies oxygenation adjusted for inspired oxygen; normal values exceed 400, and a ratio of 300 or less (200 or less in severe disease) is a key criterion for the diagnosis and severity grading of ARDS.'),
('4c6ef465-2985-5d11-9b6a-f99fb3f63ae0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'What is the diagnostic criteria for acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) according to the Berlin definition?', '["Cardiogenic edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute onset bilateral infiltrates P/F ≤300 not fully from cardiogenic","Normal chest X-ray mediated by specific receptor activation","Unilateral infiltrates without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P/F >400 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute onset bilateral infiltrates P/F ≤300 not fully from cardiogenic', 'The Berlin definition of ARDS requires acute onset, bilateral radiographic infiltrates not fully explained by effusion, atelectasis, or cardiogenic edema, and aP/F ratio ≤300 with a minimum PEEP/CPAP (mild ≤200 mmHg, moderate ≤100, severe criteria by PaO2/FiO2).'),
('c28e74b1-402c-541f-ba61-2cbb184fda4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Which Starling force opposes filtration at the capillary level?', '["Plasma colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure","Interstitial oncotic pressure","Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Capillary permeability"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure', 'The plasma colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure, generated mainly by albumin, pulls water into the capillary and is the principal Starling force opposing filtration; it is offset by the capillary hydrostatic pressure that drives fluid out.'),
('04d669f2-f08d-5d29-80d7-2bd99f61fdd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'What is the approximate capillary hydrostatic pressure at the arterial end that drives filtration?', '["13 mmHg (filtration)","-7 mmHg","0 mmHg","25 mmHg","35 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '35 mmHg', 'Capillary hydrostatic pressure is about 35 mmHg at the arterial end and falls to roughly 15-17 mmHg at the venous end; the 35 mmHg at the arterial end is the force driving fluid out of the capillary.'),
('7f9af090-fe94-5854-89a6-a1e369a10c7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'At the venous end of a capillary the net pressure is:', '["+13 mmHg","+25 mmHg","-7 mmHg (reabsorption)","0 mmHg","-13 mmHg (alternative wording)"]'::jsonb, '-7 mmHg (reabsorption)', 'At the venous end the fall in capillary hydrostatic pressure allows the plasma oncotic pressure to dominate, producing a net reabsorptive pressure of about -7 mmHg that pulls fluid back into the capillary.'),
('e4424907-bd40-5fdf-a128-e8eac6d12900'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: Accelerated junctional rhythm rate is:', '["<40/min","100-150/min","60-100/min","250-350/min","150-250/min"]'::jsonb, '60-100/min', 'Accelerated junctional rhythm has a rate of 60-100/min; junctional escape rhythm is slower (40-60/min) and junctional tachycardia exceeds 100/min.'),
('30b84255-64c5-52a1-ab69-30c5ef6140aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure leads to:', '["Decreased lymph","Increased reabsorption","Decreased filtration","Decrease in flow","Increased filtration (edema)"]'::jsonb, 'Increased filtration (edema)', 'Raising capillary hydrostatic pressure increases the net filtration pressure and drives more fluid out of the capillary, promoting interstitial fluid accumulation and edema, as occurs with venous congestion or heart failure.'),
('9986c4db-7f2a-5187-8946-dc6f028dfebd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Decreased plasma oncotic pressure causes:', '["Decreased lymph","Decrease in filtration","Decreased filtration","Increased reabsorption","Increased filtration (edema)"]'::jsonb, 'Increased filtration (edema)', 'A fall in plasma oncotic pressure (e.g., hypoalbuminemia) weakens the opposing force that pulls water into the capillary, so net filtration increases and fluid accumulates in the interstitium, producing edema.'),
('8c7260e7-e86b-569e-8071-860c660aef2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'A major function of the lymphatic system is:', '["Hormone transport resulting in decreased functional activity","O2 transport accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CO2 removal mediated by specific receptor activation","Return of filtered protein and excess fluid to circulation","Clotting factor transport leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Return of filtered protein and excess fluid to circulation', 'Lymphatic capillaries collect filtered proteins and excess interstitial fluid that cannot be reabsorbed at the venous end and return them to the bloodstream, preventing edema and maintaining plasma protein balance.'),
('af0435fa-7acb-5b29-805b-b5a8e4c9bbbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Pulsus paradoxus is:', '["Bradycardia with inspiration resulting in decreased functional activity","Irregular pulse accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rise in BP during inspiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Exaggerated drop in BP (>10 mmHg) during inspiration","Normal fall in BP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exaggerated drop in BP (>10 mmHg) during inspiration', 'Pulsus paradoxus is an abnormally large (>10 mmHg) fall in systolic blood pressure during inspiration, seen in cardiac tamponade and severe asthma.'),
('63f7ade0-d8dc-50a7-9e3d-18eea29da55a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The interstitial gel matrix (hyaluronan and proteoglycans):', '["Decreases filtration","Only in joints","Resists fluid movement (buffers edema)","Increases filtration","No significant effect"]'::jsonb, 'Resists fluid movement (buffers edema)', 'The gel matrix of hyaluronan and proteoglycans in the interstitium immobilizes fluid, increasing resistance to its movement and thereby buffering the rapid accumulation of edema fluid.'),
('37119514-31ea-55a6-b06b-6330468ca69c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The capillary filtration coefficient (Kf):', '["Unmeasurable resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conductance × surface area = filtration per unit pressure","Only conductance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conductance × surface area = filtration per unit pressure', 'Kf is the product of capillary hydraulic conductance and exchange surface area, representing the volume of fluid filtered per unit of net filtration pressure; it quantifies how easily fluid moves across the capillary wall.'),
('73c3ac12-d7b1-5cbc-a038-eca2e60f7206'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The vasomotor center that maintains tonic sympathetic vasoconstrictor outflow is located in the:', '["Midbrain","Medulla oblongata","Cerebellum","Hypothalamus","Pons"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata', 'The vasomotor center (pressor RVLM and depressor CVLM areas) lies in the medulla oblongata and provides tonic sympathetic discharge that maintains arteriolar and venous tone, thereby regulating arterial pressure.'),
('d03e9776-aa79-5ddf-b41b-7ce72cc957a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The arterial (high-pressure) baroreceptors are located in the:', '["Ventricles only resulting in decreased functional activity","Atria only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carotid sinus (Hering) and aortic arch","Coronary arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary vessels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid sinus (Hering) and aortic arch', 'Arterial baroreceptors are stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus (innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve via Hering''s nerve) and the aortic arch (innervated by the vagus); they monitor arterial pressure beat to beat.'),
('66bb25d9-0f6c-56ec-a26b-c41275390ddf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Baroreceptors are stimulated (increase firing) by:', '["Increased arterial pressure (stretch)","Hypoxia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypercapnia mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreased pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decrease in stretch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased arterial pressure (stretch)', 'Baroreceptors are mechanoreceptors that fire in proportion to arterial wall stretch; a rise in arterial pressure stretches the carotid sinus and aortic arch, increasing afferent discharge and reflexly lowering heart rate and peripheral resistance.'),
('439495f9-1158-5eac-a792-521671048025'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The baroreceptor reflex response to hypotension is:', '["Bradycardia + vasoconstriction","Tachycardia + vasoconstriction","Tachycardia + vasodilation","Bradycardia + vasodilation","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia + vasoconstriction', 'Falling blood pressure reduces baroreceptor firing, releasing the medulla from inhibition and increasing sympathetic outflow; the result is tachycardia and vasoconstriction, which restore blood pressure (compensatory response to hypotension).'),
('169f7113-3ca7-5395-ae33-faf011066396'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) pattern shows:', '["Short PR interval","Wide QRS >0.12 s","Long QT","All of the above","Delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke)"]'::jsonb, 'Delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke)', 'WPW is caused by an accessory pathway (Kent bundle) that pre-excites the ventricle, producing a delta wave (slurred QRS upstroke), short PR interval, and wide QRS; long QT is not a feature.'),
('b287828b-b069-591b-b42e-78637ac5d40e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Tachycardia in response to increased right atrial pressure (stretch) is known as:', '["Decrease in heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Tachycardia in response to increased right atrial pressure (stretch)","Only hypotension mediated by specific receptor activation","Bradycardia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only bradycardia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia in response to increased right atrial pressure (stretch)', 'The Bainbridge reflex is the reflex tachycardia produced by increased filling of the right atrium and venae cavae; atrial stretch receptors send vagal afferents that increase sympathetic outflow, raising heart rate to match venous return.'),
('5d324264-a25e-578a-a1e5-1da8f4d1bf65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Mean arterial pressure (MAP) equals:', '["Systolic/Diastolic","Diastolic - Systolic","Systolic + Diastolic","Diastolic + 1/3(Systolic - Diastolic)","Systolic - Diastolic"]'::jsonb, 'Diastolic + 1/3(Systolic - Diastolic)', 'MAP is calculated as diastolic pressure plus one-third of the pulse pressure, because diastole occupies about two-thirds of the cardiac cycle; this approximates the time-averaged arterial pressure.'),
('14dbeff0-49a3-5576-bab2-9040ce72549d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pulse pressure depends primarily on:', '["Heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood viscosity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TPR mediated by specific receptor activation","Stroke volume and arterial compliance","Venous return leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stroke volume and arterial compliance', 'Pulse pressure equals systolic minus diastolic pressure and is determined mainly by stroke volume and arterial compliance: a larger stroke volume or a stiffer (less compliant) aorta widens the pulse pressure.'),
('08bf8441-f34e-5729-8eb0-3c6a41c1fdfc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Cardiac output is equal to:', '["SV × TPR","SV × BP","HR × SV","HR × TPR","HR × BP"]'::jsonb, 'HR × SV', 'Cardiac output is the product of heart rate and stroke volume (CO = HR × SV), normally about 5 L/min; it represents the volume of blood pumped by the heart per minute.'),
('afdcc48a-7dd2-594c-a97b-ca392d56bb6b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, Which mechanism is the most important regulator of coronary blood flow during increased myocardial metabolism?', '["Neural (sympathetic)","Endothelial","Metabolic (adenosine)","Hormonal","Myogenic"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic (adenosine)', 'Adenosine released from metabolically active or hypoxic myocardium dilates coronary resistance vessels, coupling coronary flow to myocardial O2 demand.'),
('1b4d6a1a-0e8b-506a-bd31-dc7c7a687a04'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: A 55-year-old male with chest pain undergoes an ECG which shows isoelectric ST segment. Why is the normal ST segment isoelectric?', '["Atrial activity resulting in decreased functional activity","AV conduction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SA node activity mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventricular depolarization without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Early ventricular repolarization (no current flow)"]'::jsonb, 'Early ventricular repolarization (no current flow)', 'During the ST segment the ventricles are completely depolarized and in an early plateau phase, so there is no potential difference between electrodes (no current flow), yielding an isoelectric tracing.'),
('2f61f173-74f1-583d-a25d-15f9c148513c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Essential (primary) hypertension is best described as:', '["Secondary to endocrine resulting in decreased functional activity","Multifactorial (genetic + environmental). No single cause","Only genetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Only dietary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secondary to renal disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multifactorial (genetic + environmental). No single cause', 'Essential hypertension, which accounts for about 90-95% of cases, has no single cause; it results from an interaction of genetic predisposition and environmental factors such as high sodium intake, obesity, and stress.'),
('ab6dcf17-728c-5bbc-b3de-9ced80a76efa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Renovascular hypertension is a particularly important cause of secondary hypertension in:', '["Pheochromocytoma resulting in decreased functional activity","Renal artery stenosis (fibromuscular dysplasia in young adults)","Primary hyperaldosteronism mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Coarctation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Renal artery stenosis (fibromuscular dysplasia in young adults)', 'In young adults, especially women, renovascular hypertension is commonly due to fibromuscular dysplasia of the renal artery, whereas in older patients it is usually atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis.'),
('00edf084-80f7-5c21-b3ab-968023f7749d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Orthostatic (postural) hypotension is defined as:', '["Stroke resulting in decreased functional activity","↓CO accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arrhythmia mediated by specific receptor activation","Fall in BP ≥20/10 mmHg on standing → inadequate compensatory reflex","Cardiac disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fall in BP ≥20/10 mmHg on standing → inadequate compensatory reflex', 'Orthostatic hypotension is a drop in systolic blood pressure ≥20 mmHg (or diastolic ≥10 mmHg) within 3 minutes of standing, caused by failure of the baroreflex to compensate for blood pooling in the lower limbs.'),
('9ea655a7-ac8f-556f-8989-be26de084a60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, Collateral coronary circulation is most developed in:', '["Young age","Chronic ischemia (gradual occlusion)","Healthy vessels","Acute occlusion","significant collaterals"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic ischemia (gradual occlusion)', 'Collateral vessels develop over time in response to chronic, gradual coronary occlusion, so patients with slowly progressive disease have better-developed collaterals.'),
('92633249-fda4-5221-8ce9-41d6a8e3b251'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: Flecainide is a Class Ic drug used for:', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","VT post-MI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","AF with CAD mediated by specific receptor activation","SVT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maintenance of sinus rhythm in AF (normal heart)"]'::jsonb, 'Maintenance of sinus rhythm in AF (normal heart)', 'Flecainide is used to maintain sinus rhythm in atrial fibrillation in patients with structurally normal hearts; it is avoided in patients with coronary disease (CAST trial).'),
('e3f56efd-035a-519c-b3ad-efe585934a8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CVS Pt2: Turbulent flow occurs when:', '["Flow is slow","Reynolds number =500","Streamlined flow","Reynolds number <100","Reynolds number >2000"]'::jsonb, 'Reynolds number >2000', 'Flow becomes turbulent when the Reynolds number exceeds about 2000, which happens with high velocity, large diameter, and low viscosity (e.g., across stenotic valves).'),
('d56c56c2-bf52-52cc-a32c-9e53bd3b5fbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Obstructive shock includes:', '["Massive PE resulting in decreased functional activity","Volume loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiac tamponade mediated by specific receptor activation","Tension pneumothorax (obstruction to flow → ↓CO) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Massive PE, cardiac tamponade, and tension pneumothorax (obstruction to flow → ↓CO)"]'::jsonb, 'Massive PE, cardiac tamponade, and tension pneumothorax (obstruction to flow → ↓CO)', 'Obstructive shock results from physical obstruction of ventricular filling or outflow; causes include massive pulmonary embolism, cardiac tamponade, and tension pneumothorax, all of which reduce cardiac output despite a normal pump.'),
('fd0007dc-c693-5641-950a-a443e627612b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Irreversible shock is characterized by:', '["Full recovery resulting in decreased functional activity","Reversible dysfunction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cell death and organ failure despite correction of the cause","Transient hypotension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Organ failure despite correction of the cause leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cell death and organ failure despite correction of the cause', 'In irreversible shock, tissue ischemia is so prolonged that cellular and organ damage persist even after the initiating cause is corrected; cell death and multiple organ failure ensue and the condition is no longer reversible.'),
('cfa735ab-582d-58f3-96a9-0d5b0d333e2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Aldosterone acts on the distal nephron to increase:', '["H+ excretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ excretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+ reabsorption and K+ excretion in collecting duct (ENaC)","K+ reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Water excretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ reabsorption and K+ excretion in collecting duct (ENaC)', 'Aldosterone upregulates the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in collecting-duct principal cells, increasing sodium reabsorption and potassium (and H+) secretion; this explains the hypokalemia and alkalosis seen with aldosterone excess.'),
('3e6955e9-1636-5d6c-b135-9056a8efad98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Vasopressin (ADH) is synthesized in neurons whose cell bodies lie in the:', '["Adrenal cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Posterior pituitary (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei)","Pancreas mediated by specific receptor activation","Anterior pituitary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior pituitary (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei)', 'ADH is synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus and transported down axons to the posterior pituitary, where it is stored and released into the circulation.'),
('348dd255-1946-5946-95b8-cc52e8630152'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CVS Pt2: During which phase of the cardiac cycle does ventricular filling primarily occur after atrial contraction?', '["Early diastole","During T wave","At the start of QRS","During ST segment","Late diastole (after P wave)"]'::jsonb, 'Late diastole (after P wave)', 'Atrial contraction (the P wave) occurs in late diastole and delivers the ''atrial kick'', which normally contributes about 20-25% of ventricular filling.'),
('728b9188-6af6-552c-afe0-7311b2554f03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The natriuretic peptides (ANP, BNP) act to:', '["Cause natriuresis","Vasodilation","↓renin","Cause Na+ retention","↑aldosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Cause natriuresis', 'ANP and BNP increase renal sodium excretion (natriuresis), promote vasodilation, and inhibit renin and aldosterone release; together these actions lower blood volume and blood pressure.'),
('0baa6bbe-6ba9-59ec-af88-d1f7bfd14130'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) is released from:', '["Ventricles (in response to stretch/volume overload → diagnostic for heart failure)","Atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coronary arteries mediated by specific receptor activation","Kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricles (in response to stretch/volume overload → diagnostic for heart failure)', 'BNP is synthesized and released by ventricular myocytes in response to wall stretch and volume overload; its elevated plasma level is used as a diagnostic marker for heart failure.'),
('9a2ea12d-4149-5a64-ab06-80c170957d82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Cerebral blood flow (CBF) autoregulation keeps flow constant over a mean arterial pressure range of:', '["80-120 mmHg","40-80 mmHg","60-150 mmHg","20-60 mmHg","100-200 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '60-150 mmHg', 'Cerebral autoregulation maintains cerebral blood flow relatively constant over a mean arterial pressure range of approximately 60-150 mmHg; beyond these limits flow becomes pressure-dependent, risking ischemia or hyperemia.'),
('8afa6df8-597a-50a4-b0d8-3fdc344a0e1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The most potent regulator of cerebral blood flow is:', '["PaCO2 (CO2 causes vasodilation)","Sympathetic activity","pH changes","PaO2","Blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'PaCO2 (CO2 causes vasodilation)', 'Arterial CO2 is the strongest regulator of cerebral blood flow: rising CO2 (hypercapnia) produces vasodilation and increased CBF, whereas hypocapnia causes vasoconstriction and reduced CBF.'),
('b9f6cb5b-e1fc-560a-a577-dd15a6f37862'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Cerebral blood flow is increased by:', '["Hyperventilation","Hypercapnia and hypoxia","Alkalosis","Hypocapnia","Hyperoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercapnia and hypoxia', 'Both hypercapnia (via increased tissue H+) and hypoxia stimulate cerebral vasodilation and increase cerebral blood flow, whereas hypocapnia, alkalosis, and hyperoxia cause vasoconstriction and decreased flow.'),
('c2451704-acce-551c-a4d1-42751313363f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Blood flow to the left ventricular myocardium occurs mainly during:', '["Both equally resulting in decreased functional activity","At end-systole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","None of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","Systole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diastole (due to LV compression during systole)"]'::jsonb, 'Diastole (due to LV compression during systole)', 'During systole the contracting myocardium compresses the intramural coronary vessels, impeding flow; most left coronary flow therefore occurs in diastole, when the myocardium is relaxed and coronary resistance is lowest.'),
('2b0fec13-d329-5958-97af-a6877a7b5d8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) is:', '["Only in hyperoxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Vasoconstriction in response to alveolar hypoxia (diverts blood from poorly ventilated areas)","Vasodilation in hypoxia mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in exercise without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vasodilation in hyperoxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction in response to alveolar hypoxia (diverts blood from poorly ventilated areas)', 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is the constriction of pulmonary arterioles serving poorly ventilated (hypoxic) alveoli, redirecting blood flow to better-ventilated regions and improving ventilation-perfusion matching.'),
('47719110-02e1-53d9-ad43-2b7669995226'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, Which electrolyte abnormality characteristically causes peaked T waves and widened QRS on ECG?', '["Hypokalemia","Hypermagnesemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia', 'Hyperkalemia produces peaked T waves initially, then QRS widening, loss of P waves, and eventually a sine-wave pattern and ventricular fibrillation.'),
('4f1ce494-713f-59ed-966f-121a81e5e6af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The normal resting ejection fraction of the left ventricle is approximately:', '["75%","50%","25-30%","5%","10%"]'::jsonb, '50%', 'The ejection fraction is stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume; the normal resting value is about 55-70%, so 50% is the most appropriate choice among these options. Values below 40% indicate reduced systolic function.'),
('a6241f85-3ee2-592d-b300-c265b0747b39'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Skin blood flow is regulated mainly for:', '["Thermoregulatory (sympathetic: vasoconstriction in cold; vasodilation in heat)","Metabolic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CO2 control mediated by specific receptor activation","BP maintenance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 delivery leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thermoregulatory (sympathetic: vasoconstriction in cold; vasodilation in heat)', 'Cutaneous blood flow varies widely (from about 20 mL/min to up to 3000 mL/min) and is controlled primarily by sympathetic nerves for thermoregulation—constricting in cold to conserve heat and dilating in heat to dissipate it.'),
('cbf3d60a-3a6e-54a6-99be-61d8deb27754'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Exercise increases cardiac output up to:', '["30 L/min","35 L/min","20-25 L/min (fit)","15 L/min","10 L/min"]'::jsonb, '20-25 L/min (fit)', 'During heavy exercise, cardiac output in fit individuals can rise to about 20-25 L/min through increases in both heart rate and stroke volume.'),
('1b65ca65-32e6-5cd8-9918-93a56f23926d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: At the standard ECG recording speed of 25 mm/s, how many large squares (5 mm) correspond to one second?', '["100 mm/sec","5 mm/sec","50 mm/sec","25 mm/sec","10 mm/sec"]'::jsonb, '25 mm/sec', 'At the standard paper speed of 25 mm/s, 1 small square (1 mm) equals 0.04 s; this fixed speed is used to time ECG intervals.'),
('a7c7ca9b-2005-51c3-a4aa-d6bbba9a0c7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The direct Fick method is the:', '["Gold standard for measuring CO (but invasive: requires PA catheter)","Most common method accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not used mediated by specific receptor activation","Non-invasive without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only an estimate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gold standard for measuring CO (but invasive: requires PA catheter)', 'The direct Fick method is the reference standard for cardiac output measurement, but it is invasive because it requires sampling mixed venous blood from the pulmonary artery and measuring oxygen consumption.'),
('494c1122-5977-548e-92ef-7c220644c16d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: Which of the following is NOT a cause of a prolonged QT interval (long QT syndrome)?', '["Congenital channelopathies (LQT1-3)","Hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia","QT-prolonging drugs (quinidine, sotalol)","Myocardial ischemia","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalcemia', 'Hypercalcemia shortens the QT interval; congenital long-QT syndromes, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, ischemia, and QT-prolonging drugs all lengthen it.'),
('e46313aa-8555-5470-90ad-a8f62d72430f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CVS Pt2: What is the mechanism of tissue hypoxemia in cold (low-output) heart failure despite normal arterial oxygen saturation?', '["Shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral vasoconstriction with normal SaO2 (cold shock HF)","Methemoglobinemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral vasoconstriction with normal SaO2 (cold shock HF)', 'In low-output (cold) heart failure, intense peripheral vasoconstriction and stasis cause tissue hypoxia despite normal arterial oxygen saturation, because oxygen extraction is increased at the periphery.'),
('96e81e64-9dc2-5c25-afbf-baca6b17ac94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The cardiac index is calculated as:', '["CO / HR","CO / weight","CO × BSA","CO / BSA (2.5-4 L/min/m²)","HR / BSA"]'::jsonb, 'CO / BSA (2.5-4 L/min/m²)', 'The cardiac index is cardiac output indexed to body surface area (CI = CO/BSA), with a normal value of about 2.5-4.0 L/min/m²; it is a useful measure of cardiac performance independent of body size.'),
('5b6f7f8a-baa0-5193-bc20-09936a68009a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Ejection fraction is calculated as:', '["EDV / ESV","SV / ESV","HR / SV","SV / EDV (normal 55-70%)","CO / EDV"]'::jsonb, 'SV / EDV (normal 55-70%)', 'Ejection fraction equals stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume (EF = SV/EDV), normally about 55-70%; it is the most widely used index of global systolic function.'),
('f68c8469-97f9-55d9-9df4-c2139a565f6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Afterload is best estimated by:', '["Preload resulting in decreased functional activity","HR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","SV mediated by specific receptor activation","Contractility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) or arterial pressure during ejection"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) or arterial pressure during ejection', 'Afterload is the load against which the ventricle must eject; it is approximated clinically by systemic vascular resistance or by the systolic wall stress/arterial pressure during ejection.'),
('cefdc6db-9466-5b55-8664-f2619b832e2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Myocardial contractility is increased by:', '["None of these resulting in decreased functional activity","↓Na+ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic stimulation (β1)","↑Ca2+ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digitalis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic stimulation (β1)', 'Sympathetic stimulation of β1-adrenoceptors increases intracellular Ca2+ and is the principal physiologic positive inotropic mechanism; increased extracellular Ca2+ and digitalis also raise contractility but the key sympathetic mediator is the β1 effect.'),
('ce42fa5f-be3e-5bea-84de-960d21dc41bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Spironolactone NOT contraindicated in:', '["Hypothyroidism","Heart failure","Ascites","Primary aldosteronism","Resistant HTN"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothyroidism', 'Aldosterone antagonist for HF, ascites, resistant HTN.'),
('ecc2265f-fa85-5481-bea6-f5e8c3db8b69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Increased afterload causes:', '["Increased CO","Decreased HR","Decreased preload","Increased stroke volume","Decreased stroke volume"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased stroke volume', 'Raising afterload increases the pressure the ventricle must overcome to eject blood, which reduces stroke volume unless contractility compensates.'),
('5f70e0ef-32ba-5eef-87d4-48b5d020abc3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The major determinant of myocardial oxygen consumption is:', '["Heart rate (tension-time index)","Wall tension","Preload only","SV only","Contractility"]'::jsonb, 'Wall tension', 'Myocardial oxygen consumption is determined mainly by wall tension (which is a function of systolic pressure and chamber radius per the Law of Laplace), along with heart rate and contractility; among these, wall tension is generally the largest contributor.'),
('af6d5411-83a5-5e21-8c74-25bac946a1f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Coronary steal phenomenon:', '["Only constricts vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Improves blood flow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","No significant effect mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasodilators (dipyridamole/adenosine) dilate normal vessels → divert flow away from stenotic vessels","Depends only on dilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilators (dipyridamole/adenosine) dilate normal vessels → divert flow away from stenotic vessels', 'Coronary steal occurs when a potent vasodilator (adenosine, dipyridamole) maximally dilates normal vessels, lowering resistance downstream of a stenosis and diverting collateral flow away from the ischemic territory, worsening its perfusion.'),
('dfc2a3bd-463f-51a5-9c7b-d2b1210c69dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Ventricular contractility is affected by the vagus nerve:', '["Major effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Only on atria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only on HR mediated by specific receptor activation","Strong effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minimal direct effect (sparse parasympathetic innervation of ventricles)"]'::jsonb, 'Minimal direct effect (sparse parasympathetic innervation of ventricles)', 'The ventricles receive sparse vagal (parasympathetic) innervation compared with the atria and SA/AV nodes, so vagal activity has little direct effect on ventricular contractility; its main influence is on heart rate and atrial function.'),
('f4bc1de8-4e08-524d-afd0-7504bfab5613'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Ventricular pressure-volume loop analysis:', '["Two phases as ejection and filling","Four phases: isovolumic contraction","Ejection only","Filling only","Only isovolumic relaxation"]'::jsonb, 'Four phases: isovolumic contraction', 'The cardiac cycle on a pressure-volume loop consists of four phases: ventricular filling, isovolumic contraction, ejection, and isovolumic relaxation; the area enclosed by the loop equals stroke work.'),
('b01ebf1b-d71f-5bcf-b200-029141c3082e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, The difference between constrictive pericarditis and cardiac tamponade is:', '["Constriction has paradoxus resulting in decreased functional activity","Both same accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tamponade has pulsus paradoxus; constriction has Kussmaul sign","Neither has signs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kussmaul only in tamponade leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tamponade has pulsus paradoxus; constriction has Kussmaul sign', 'Cardiac tamponade characteristically produces pulsus paradoxus with equalized diastolic pressures, whereas constrictive pericarditis typically shows Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock.'),
('26d6d071-1a37-505b-b38a-96aed5aacd1c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: First-degree AV block means:', '["Missing QRS","QRS >0.12 s","PR interval <0.12 s","Prolonged QT","PR interval >0.20 s"]'::jsonb, 'PR interval >0.20 s', 'First-degree AV block is defined as a PR interval exceeding 0.20 s, reflecting delayed AV conduction while every P wave still conducts to a QRS.'),
('5218ea43-8766-527c-901a-e1bba5b9a667'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Ventricular-arterial coupling is best described as:', '["Ratio of Ea (arterial elastance) to Ees (ventricular elastance). Optimal when Ea/Ees 1","Only for ventricles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not important mediated by specific receptor activation","Only for arteries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fixed value leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ratio of Ea (arterial elastance) to Ees (ventricular elastance). Optimal when Ea/Ees 1', 'Ventricular-arterial coupling is the ratio of arterial elastance (Ea, afterload) to end-systolic ventricular elastance (Ees, contractility); energy transfer is optimal when Ea/Ees is near 1, and this ratio quantifies ventriculo-arterial interaction.'),
('f3dab269-b4ad-5f49-b927-dccf48e15d1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'What is the thermoregulatory cardiovascular response to cold exposure?', '["Vasoconstriction (↓skin flow) + ↑BP + possible ↑metabolic heat production","Only shivering accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only ↑HR mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasodilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓BP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction (↓skin flow) + ↑BP + possible ↑metabolic heat production', 'Cold exposure triggers sympathetic-mediated cutaneous vasoconstriction, which reduces skin blood flow and conserves heat while raising blood pressure; shivering may additionally increase metabolic heat production.'),
('ab78e9a3-bcc4-503b-8a62-2ed592a5e5c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The cardiovascular response to heat stress is:', '["No change resulting in decreased functional activity","Cutaneous vasodilation + ↑sweating + ↑HR (↑CO to skin for heat dissipation)","↓HR mediated by specific receptor activation","Vasoconstriction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sweating leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cutaneous vasodilation + ↑sweating + ↑HR (↑CO to skin for heat dissipation)', 'Heat stress causes sympathetic cholinergic vasodilation of skin vessels, increased sweating, and a rise in heart rate and cardiac output so that blood is redirected to the skin for heat dissipation.'),
('b0758b45-d3ca-5faf-ad18-a8b61b072e1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'What are the four phases of the blood pressure response during the Valsalva maneuver?', '["Continuous drop resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Phase I: ↑BP (↑thoracic pressure → mechanical). Phase II: ↓BP (↓venous return) then ↑BP (compensation). Phase III: ↓BP (release). Phase IV: overshoot","Only one phase mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Indeterminate without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only overshoot leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phase I: ↑BP (↑thoracic pressure → mechanical). Phase II: ↓BP (↓venous return) then ↑BP (compensation). Phase III: ↓BP (release). Phase IV: overshoot', 'The Valsalva maneuver has four pressure phases: I (brief rise from thoracic compression), II (fall from reduced venous return followed by compensatory rise), III (fall on release), and IV (overshoot from continued vasoconstriction), which is used to test autonomic integrity.'),
('4e44cfdf-e09a-5a19-a9c3-0aed3805d420'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'What is the normal ankle-brachial index (ABI) and what does a value <0.9 indicate?', '["Only for legs resulting in decreased functional activity","Only for veins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ratio of ankle SBP to brachial SBP. Normal >0.9. <0.9 → PAD","Only for arms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal >1.5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ratio of ankle SBP to brachial SBP. Normal >0.9. <0.9 → PAD', 'The ABI is the ratio of ankle to brachial systolic blood pressure; normal is between 0.9 and 1.3, and a value below 0.9 indicates peripheral arterial disease from atherosclerotic stenosis of the leg arteries.'),
('ab8a3cd9-77a7-5d35-9d60-36135d8340f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Pulse wave velocity (PWV):', '["Measures BP resulting in decreased functional activity","Measures HR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measures SV mediated by specific receptor activation","Measures arterial stiffness (faster PWV → stiffer arteries)","Measures CO leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measures arterial stiffness (faster PWV → stiffer arteries)', 'Pulse wave velocity is the speed at which the arterial pressure wave travels; higher PWV reflects stiffer, less compliant arteries (aging, hypertension, atherosclerosis) and predicts cardiovascular risk.'),
('577bc861-44eb-5bee-8864-2ad1eacb323c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Low-pressure (volume) receptors located in the atria and great veins act to:', '["Only regulate arterial tone resulting in decreased functional activity","Low-pressure receptors (atria/venae cavae) → regulate blood volume via ADH","Only control HR mediated by specific receptor activation","Only renal sympathetic nerves without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only ANP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low-pressure receptors (atria/venae cavae) → regulate blood volume via ADH', 'Low-pressure receptors in the atrial and vena caval walls sense blood volume (via stretch) and regulate body fluid volume, chiefly by modulating ADH secretion—reduced stretch increases ADH and water retention to restore blood volume.'),
('cc7e6fd5-260e-5737-9fdf-6ab384649b47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Which physiologic adaptation of pregnancy explains the increased stroke volume and cardiac output through enhanced preload?', '["↓SVR","↑Plasma volume (40-50%)","Hypotension in supine","↑CO (30-50%)","↑HR"]'::jsonb, '↑Plasma volume (40-50%)', 'During pregnancy, plasma volume expands by about 40-50%, increasing venous return and preload; this raises stroke volume and cardiac output (which overall increases 30-50%) via the Frank-Starling mechanism.'),
('d456291e-2a1a-50f1-af2e-1c43c88a75b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, What is the mechanism of tissue hypoxemia in cold (low-output) heart failure despite normal arterial oxygen saturation?', '["Shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Peripheral vasoconstriction with normal SaO2 (cold shock HF)","Methemoglobinemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral vasoconstriction with normal SaO2 (cold shock HF)', 'In low-output (cold) heart failure, intense peripheral vasoconstriction and stasis cause tissue hypoxia despite normal arterial oxygen saturation, because oxygen extraction is increased at the periphery.'),
('2b7ba50e-0e82-5115-af54-2c0d0b83420b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Oxygen pulse equals:', '["VO2 / SV resulting in decreased functional activity","SV / HR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VO2 / HR (reflects SV × arteriovenous O2 difference)","VO2 × HR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","HR / VO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 / HR (reflects SV × arteriovenous O2 difference)', 'Oxygen pulse is the oxygen consumed per heartbeat (VO2 ÷ HR); by the Fick principle it equals stroke volume times the arteriovenous oxygen content difference, so it tracks stroke volume and oxygen extraction during exercise.'),
('34d11221-e93c-5671-b209-e9b3b7e7bdd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, What is the term for a ventricular bigeminy pattern on ECG?', '["Triplet PVC","Alternating narrow and wide","Every third beat is PVC","Couplets","Every other beat is a PVC"]'::jsonb, 'Every other beat is a PVC', 'Ventricular bigeminy is a rhythm in which every other beat is a premature ventricular complex (one sinus beat followed by one PVC).'),
('13ff4e0c-b2c6-5adf-9c1e-c5aca44d09c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: LBBB on ECG makes which diagnosis difficult?', '["LVH resulting in decreased functional activity","Acute MI (cannot use ST elevation criteria)","RVH mediated by specific receptor activation","Pericarditis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial enlargement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute MI (cannot use ST elevation criteria)', 'LBBB masks the standard ST-elevation criteria for acute MI, since secondary ST/T changes are already present; Sgarbossa criteria or serial comparison are needed.'),
('4f89e898-a397-519a-8a05-93f0d151df15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Total blood volume in a normal adult is approximately:', '["10 L resulting in decreased functional activity","3 L accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 L (70 mL/kg in adults)","7 L without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1.5 L leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5 L (70 mL/kg in adults)', 'Blood volume in an average adult is about 70 mL per kilogram of body weight, i.e., roughly 5 L total; acute loss of a large fraction can precipitate hypovolemic shock.'),
('e4d841f8-a791-5650-8103-1576e8bd00fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) produces all of the following effects EXCEPT:', '["Increased GFR and sodium excretion","Decreased renin release","Decreased aldosterone secretion","Vasodilation","Increased thirst"]'::jsonb, 'Increased thirst', 'ANP promotes natriuresis and vasodilation and suppresses renin and aldosterone, but it reduces thirst rather than increasing it.'),
('031788db-af1c-5af3-aa5c-4bc8487accfe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: Cardiac muscle is characterized by:', '["Single nucleus centrally located","Voluntary","Striated with intercalated discs","Gap junctions","Non-striated"]'::jsonb, 'Striated with intercalated discs', 'Cardiac muscle is striated (like skeletal muscle) and joined end-to-end by intercalated discs containing gap junctions and desmosomes, which enable synchronized contraction.'),
('b71389c2-7207-5915-b3d0-c6d4c555927f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'The QRS duration is normally:', '["<0.16 seconds","<0.20 seconds","<0.12 seconds","<0.04 seconds","<0.08 seconds"]'::jsonb, '<0.12 seconds', 'The QRS complex, representing ventricular depolarization, normally lasts <0.12 seconds in the intact heart; a prolonged QRS indicates bundle branch block or ventricular conduction delay.'),
('c08ce7ef-953e-5fb6-bfd4-17832fc0d31d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CVS Pt2: Austin Flint murmur is:', '["Tricuspid regurgitation resulting in decreased functional activity","VSD murmur accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mitral stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Mitral regurgitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mid-diastolic rumble in severe AR (due to mitral valve vibration)"]'::jsonb, 'Mid-diastolic rumble in severe AR (due to mitral valve vibration)', 'The Austin Flint murmur is a mid-diastolic rumbling murmur heard at the apex in severe aortic regurgitation, caused by the regurgitant jet vibrating the anterior mitral leaflet.'),
('c75ad8d6-d8b1-543d-8da4-22ace171b92d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Which force promotes fluid movement OUT of capillaries?', '["Plasma oncotic pressure","Venous pressure","Capillary hydrostatic pressure","Interstitial oncotic pressure","Interstitial hydrostatic pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary hydrostatic pressure', 'Capillary hydrostatic pressure is the Starling force that pushes water out of the capillary, driving filtration into the interstitium; plasma oncotic pressure opposes this filtration.'),
('e460286b-a338-5475-977f-70b616073a37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CVS Pt2, Cardiogenic shock is due to:', '["Sepsis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypovolemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pump failure (MI) -> decreased CO with pulmonary edema","Pulmonary embolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anaphylaxis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pump failure (MI) -> decreased CO with pulmonary edema', 'Cardiogenic shock results from primary pump failure, most often due to acute myocardial infarction, causing a marked fall in cardiac output with pulmonary congestion.'),
('2c4db81b-c330-5ebc-aa83-d9b1c669c258'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CVS Pt2: The pathognomonic ECG finding for acute STEMI is:', '["T inversion","ST depression","BBB abnormality","P wave changes","ST elevation in contiguous leads"]'::jsonb, 'ST elevation in contiguous leads', 'Acute ST-elevation MI is defined by ST-segment elevation in anatomically contiguous leads reflecting transmural ischemia/injury.'),
('27e5f245-b0db-5c7a-a8b7-de79660ac43e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Cardiovascular System - CVS Part 2', 'In the study of CVS Pt2, Which physiological factor is most responsible for increasing the force of cardiac contraction?', '["Increased end-diastolic volume (Starling)","Increased afterload","Decreased preload","Parasympathetic stimulation","Hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Increased end-diastolic volume (Starling)', 'The Frank-Starling mechanism increases contraction force when end-diastolic volume (preload) rises; sympathetic stimulation and the Bowditch effect also increase it, but increased preload is the primary determinant in this list.'),
('5789d945-064d-5913-b786-362e617517a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Pulmonary ventilation is defined as:', '["Movement of air in and out of lungs","Gas exchange across alveolar membrane","O2 consumption","CO2 production","Transport of gases in blood"]'::jsonb, 'Movement of air in and out of lungs', 'Pulmonary ventilation is the bulk movement of air into and out of the lungs, achieved by inspiration and expiration. Gas exchange across the alveolar membrane is a separate process that occurs by diffusion.'),
('27c0ca62-3b3e-59d6-a2b8-d72a60e80e06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal tidal volume (VT) at rest is approximately:', '["300 mL","1000 mL","500 mL","200 mL","700 mL"]'::jsonb, '500 mL', 'Tidal volume is the air inspired or expired with each normal breath, averaging about 500 mL (≈6-8 mL/kg) in healthy adults at rest. Larger or smaller values suggest altered breathing patterns or disease.'),
('0eb71b49-927b-5c74-a948-0a10870cd718'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Alveolar ventilation (VA) is the volume of fresh air that reaches the gas-exchanging regions of the lungs each minute and equals:', '["VT × RR (minute ventilation)","(VT − VD) × RR","FRC × RR","VT alone","VD × RR"]'::jsonb, '(VT − VD) × RR', 'Alveolar ventilation is the fraction of minute ventilation that actually reaches the alveoli and participates in gas exchange. It equals tidal volume minus dead space, multiplied by the respiratory rate: VA = (VT − VD) × RR.'),
('87da73e0-08a4-5c62-9df2-5fd3ab084e88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Dead space in the respiratory system includes:', '["Only the bronchi resulting in decreased functional activity","Anatomical (conducting airways) + alveolar (non-perfused alveoli) + physiological","Only the trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the nose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical (conducting airways) + alveolar (non-perfused alveoli) + physiological', 'Dead space is the portion of each breath that does not participate in gas exchange. Physiological dead space is the sum of anatomical dead space (conducting airways) and alveolar dead space (ventilated alveoli that are not perfused).'),
('7f500ba7-a563-5152-baa3-713cb1e601dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Anatomical dead space is approximately:', '["50 mL","200 mL","150 mL (≈2 mL/kg)","250 mL","100 mL"]'::jsonb, '150 mL (≈2 mL/kg)', 'Anatomical dead space is the volume of the conducting airways where no gas exchange occurs, about 150 mL (≈2 mL/kg ideal body weight) in adults. It roughly equals the physiological dead space in a normal healthy lung.'),
('13a79e9e-1ec5-52aa-bd93-8bdd2b0b5970'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal physiological dead space to tidal volume ratio (VD/VT) is:', '["0.2-0.3 (20-30%)","0.5-0.6","<0.1","0.7-0.8","0.1-0.15"]'::jsonb, '0.2-0.3 (20-30%)', 'In health, dead space accounts for roughly 20-30% of each tidal volume (VD/VT ≈ 0.2-0.3). A ratio above about 0.3 indicates abnormally increased dead space ventilation, as seen in pulmonary embolism.'),
('a8c29f50-5295-5c53-b9c5-046546713e4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the normal intrapleural pressure at functional residual capacity (FRC)?', '["Positive","Equal to alveolar pressure","Atmospheric","Subatmospheric (-5 cmH2O at FRC)","Equal to mouth pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Subatmospheric (-5 cmH2O at FRC)', 'Intrapleural pressure is normally subatmospheric because the elastic recoil of the lung pulls inward while the chest wall pulls outward. At FRC it is about -5 cmH2O, and swinging more negative during inspiration keeps the lungs inflated.'),
('8b5db714-1069-55af-a58a-6f297f5fe54c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Compliance of the lung is defined as:', '["Volume / resistance resulting in decreased functional activity","Volume × pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Change in volume per unit change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP)","Pressure / flow without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ΔP/ΔV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Change in volume per unit change in pressure (ΔV/ΔP)', 'Compliance measures the distensibility of the lung, defined as the change in lung volume produced per unit change in transpulmonary pressure (ΔV/ΔP). It is reduced in restrictive disease and increased in emphysema.'),
('6c10f79f-c7be-52ee-85a2-d807212e74ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 35-year-old male presents with chronic dry cough and exertional dyspnea. Spirometry shows reduced vital capacity with a normal FEV1/FVC ratio. Which diagnosis is most likely?', '["COPD resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary fibrosis (restrictive disease)","Normal lung mediated by specific receptor activation","Emphysema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise-induced bronchospasm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis (restrictive disease)', 'Reduced lung volumes with a preserved (normal or increased) FEV1/FVC ratio is the hallmark of restrictive lung disease such as pulmonary fibrosis. Restriction results from decreased lung compliance, unlike the low FEV1/FVC seen in obstructive disease.'),
('61fb5d3e-23e8-5aa8-841e-b43032c7e131'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The compliance of the lung is increased in:', '["Atelectasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary edema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Emphysema (loss of elastic recoil)"]'::jsonb, 'Emphysema (loss of elastic recoil)', 'Emphysema destroys alveolar septa and reduces elastic recoil, making the lung easier to distend, so lung compliance is increased. Fibrosis, edema, atelectasis, and pneumonia all stiffen the lung and lower compliance.'),
('51a186f8-7338-57d5-8e7b-40462aa4d286'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The chest wall compliance is approximately:', '["400 mL/cm H2O","100 mL/cm H2O","300 mL/cm H2O","200 mL/cm H2O","50 mL/cm H2O"]'::jsonb, '200 mL/cm H2O', 'Chest wall compliance is roughly 200 mL/cm H2O, similar to lung compliance. Because they behave in series, total respiratory compliance is about half of either component (≈100 mL/cm H2O).'),
('5203fa48-e1fe-599e-9bb4-2078336448d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Total respiratory compliance (lung + chest wall) is about:', '["100 mL/cm H2O (1/Ctotal = 1/Clung + 1/Ccw)","300 mL/cm H2O accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","200 mL/cm H2O mediated by specific receptor activation","50 mL/cm H2O without affecting the primary tissue pathway","400 mL/cm H2O leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '100 mL/cm H2O (1/Ctotal = 1/Clung + 1/Ccw)', 'Lung and chest wall compliances act like two springs in series, so 1/Ctotal = 1/Clung + 1/Ccw. With each component near 200 mL/cm H2O, total respiratory compliance is about 100 mL/cm H2O.'),
('f9e5fb49-0068-54a9-abe1-e15d4251b7c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Surface tension in the alveoli is reduced by:', '["Blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surfactant (dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine - DPPC)","Water without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Edema fluid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant (dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine - DPPC)', 'Pulmonary surfactant, rich in dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC), is secreted by type II alveolar cells and lowers alveolar surface tension, especially at low lung volumes. This stabilizes alveoli and prevents collapse.'),
('a6fe1e89-0b58-5fdf-ab91-31c83d5eabe1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'According to Laplace''s law for a spherical alveolus, the pressure required to keep the alveolus open is:', '["P = T/2r","P = 4T/r","P = 2T/r","P = T/r","P = 2T²/r"]'::jsonb, 'P = 2T/r', 'Laplace''s law for a sphere states P = 2T/r, so the distending pressure is proportional to wall tension and inversely proportional to radius. Surfactant lowers T so smaller alveoli do not develop excessive pressure and collapse.'),
('749d9574-a4a6-51ea-b8c7-100519925552'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'How does pulmonary surfactant prevent alveolar collapse (atelectasis)?', '["Only in large alveoli resulting in decreased functional activity","No significant effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","By increasing tension in small alveoli mediated by specific receptor activation","Only during expiration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","By reducing surface tension more in smaller alveoli (variable effect)"]'::jsonb, 'By reducing surface tension more in smaller alveoli (variable effect)', 'Surfactant molecules are spread more densely over the smaller alveolar surface, lowering its surface tension preferentially. This equalizes pressures between large and small alveoli and prevents collapse of the smaller ones.'),
('fab6f1c3-73c0-5223-abcc-48622b897350'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the term for the collapse of alveoli due to loss of surfactant or airway obstruction?', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Atelectasis (alveolar collapse)","Edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atelectasis (alveolar collapse)', 'Atelectasis is the collapse of alveoli, which can result from surfactant deficiency (as in ARDS or neonatal RDS), airway obstruction, or external compression. It causes ventilation-perfusion mismatch and hypoxemia.'),
('9df321fe-c7b6-566e-8c00-84b8cd776be4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Airway resistance is primarily determined by:', '["Viscosity of gas resulting in decreased functional activity","Flow rate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Length of airways mediated by specific receptor activation","Radius of airways (Poiseuille''s law: R ∝ 1/r⁴)","Density of gas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radius of airways (Poiseuille''s law: R ∝ 1/r⁴)', 'By Poiseuille''s law, resistance is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the airway radius (R ∝ 1/r⁴). Because of this steep relationship, small changes in airway caliber dramatically alter resistance, as in asthma.'),
('4d7bc91e-8d91-5e68-af1c-92a475b0a659'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The major site of airway resistance is:', '["Nose resulting in decreased functional activity","Alveoli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Trachea mediated by specific receptor activation","Terminal bronchioles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medium-sized bronchi (generations 4-8)"]'::jsonb, 'Medium-sized bronchi (generations 4-8)', 'Although thousands of small airways exist, most of the total airway resistance lies in the medium-sized bronchi (approximately generations 4-8). Beyond this, the large number of parallel small airways lowers overall resistance.'),
('e85c7e61-940f-55a6-aa9d-8c68c378f043'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Airway resistance is increased in:', '["Normal lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Asthma (bronchoconstriction and inflammation)","Hyperinflation mediated by specific receptor activation","Emphysema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma (bronchoconstriction and inflammation)', 'In asthma, bronchospasm, mucosal edema, and secretions narrow the airways, raising resistance. Because resistance is inversely proportional to the fourth power of radius, even mild narrowing markedly impairs airflow.'),
('6c76f7a7-8a24-5278-8cf5-18368ca22281'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal FEV1/FVC ratio is:', '["0.3-0.5","0.5-0.6","0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)","1.0","0.9-1.0"]'::jsonb, '0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)', 'In healthy adults, FEV1 is 75-85% of the forced vital capacity, with a lower limit of normal near 0.7. A ratio below 0.7 indicates airflow obstruction, typical of asthma or COPD.'),
('9df47e3a-5423-54ba-91f5-8d261862f2f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which pulmonary function test pattern is characteristic of obstructive lung diseases like asthma and COPD?', '["Obstructive pattern (↓FEV1/FVC, normal or ↓FVC, ↑RV/TLC)","Restrictive pattern accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal mediated by specific receptor activation","Low compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased diffusion capacity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive pattern (↓FEV1/FVC, normal or ↓FVC, ↑RV/TLC)', 'Obstructive disease reduces the FEV1/FVC ratio (<0.7) because airways limit the rate of emptying; FVC is often near normal or slightly reduced and residual volume rises from air trapping. Restrictive disease instead shows reduced volumes with a normal ratio.'),
('6652fea6-4b96-5237-b244-8088c21c86c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'FEV1/FVC >0.8 (preserved ratio) together with a reduced FVC indicates:', '["Normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Asthma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emphysema mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Restrictive lung disease (fibrosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Restrictive lung disease (fibrosis)', 'A preserved or increased FEV1/FVC ratio with a low FVC (and low TLC) is the spirometric hallmark of restriction, such as pulmonary fibrosis. In obstruction the ratio falls, not rises.'),
('f1d582fa-470a-5843-80d5-073943d3585b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Total lung capacity (TLC) in a normal adult is approximately:', '["3 L","7 L","5 L","6 L","4 L"]'::jsonb, '6 L', 'Total lung capacity is the volume of air in the lungs after a maximal inspiration, averaging about 6 L in a healthy adult. It is reduced in restrictive disease and often increased in obstructive disease.'),
('110184dc-912b-5d95-b4f5-c0b998869498'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In a normal adult, vital capacity (VC) is approximately:', '["100% of TLC","90% of TLC","60% of TLC","50% of TLC","80% of TLC (≈4.8 L)"]'::jsonb, '80% of TLC (≈4.8 L)', 'Vital capacity is the maximum volume of air that can be exhaled after a maximal inspiration, roughly 80% of TLC (about 4.8 L in adults). The residual volume makes up the remaining 20% of TLC.'),
('4a601d7a-eaa2-5282-a1ac-978ee4a6f3f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Functional residual capacity (FRC) in a normal adult is about:', '["0.5 L resulting in decreased functional activity","4 L accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 L mediated by specific receptor activation","2.2-2.5 L (40% of TLC)","1 L leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '2.2-2.5 L (40% of TLC)', 'FRC is the lung volume at the end of a normal expiration, the sum of ERV and RV, about 2.2-2.5 L (≈40% of TLC). At FRC the inward elastic recoil of the lung exactly balances the outward recoil of the chest wall.'),
('f60cd697-ac84-5308-b0ca-e0100ee7bc89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Residual volume (RV) in a normal adult is approximately:', '["1.2 L (20% of TLC)","2 L accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","3 L mediated by specific receptor activation","0.2 L without affecting the primary tissue pathway","0.5 L leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1.2 L (20% of TLC)', 'Residual volume is the air remaining in the lungs after a maximal forced expiration, about 1.2 L (≈20% of TLC). It cannot be measured by simple spirometry and prevents complete alveolar collapse between breaths.'),
('99f766ea-33bc-5497-9050-227394376599'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) in a normal adult male is approximately:', '["4 L","3 L (male)","0.5 L","2 L","1 L"]'::jsonb, '3 L (male)', 'Inspiratory reserve volume is the additional air that can be inspired above a normal tidal breath, about 3 L in men and 1.9 L in women. It contributes the largest component of inspiratory capacity and vital capacity.'),
('92db8baa-aa12-5e9d-a6e2-27a5ce0bada7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Inspiratory capacity (IC) equals:', '["TV only","TV + IRV (≈3.5 L)","TLC − IRV","FRC + TV","VC + RV"]'::jsonb, 'TV + IRV (≈3.5 L)', 'Inspiratory capacity is the maximum volume that can be inhaled from the resting end-expiratory level, so IC = TV + IRV (about 3.5 L). It is one of the two components that together make up vital capacity (IC + ERV).'),
('7c295b51-97a6-59f1-b5c9-2e9ba459afb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Forced vital capacity (FVC) is:', '["IRV resulting in decreased functional activity","Maximum volume forcibly exhaled after full inspiration","Tidal volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Volume at rest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minute volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum volume forcibly exhaled after full inspiration', 'FVC is the total volume of air that can be forcefully expired after a maximal inspiration. It is a key spirometric measurement used together with FEV1 to distinguish obstructive from restrictive disease.'),
('d56ea840-2313-542b-920e-b18b095f5f2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) is:', '["VC resulting in decreased functional activity","FEV1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Minute ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Volume at rest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum expiratory flow rate (used in asthma monitoring)"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum expiratory flow rate (used in asthma monitoring)', 'PEFR is the highest flow achieved during a forced expiration, approaching 400-600 L/min in healthy adults. It is inexpensive to measure with a peak-flow meter and is widely used to monitor asthma control.'),
('64f6de67-b86d-5461-be50-1b24783eec4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Maximal voluntary ventilation (MVV) is:', '["MV resulting in decreased functional activity","Resting minute ventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FEV1 mediated by specific receptor activation","PEFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum volume of air breathed per minute (≈120-170 L/min)"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum volume of air breathed per minute (≈120-170 L/min)', 'MVV measures the maximum volume of air that can be breathed in and out over one minute (by extrapolating a 12-15 second effort), roughly 120-170 L/min in adults. It reflects overall respiratory muscle strength and airway function.'),
('c764ec2f-a800-5cd0-8e36-ec2a143a9401'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'MVV is approximately related to FEV1 by:', '["PEFR × 2","FRC × RR","FEV1 × 10","TV × RR × 60","FEV1 × 35-40"]'::jsonb, 'FEV1 × 35-40', 'Because both MVV and FEV1 are effort-dependent, forced-flow measurements, MVV in liters per minute is approximately 35-40 times the FEV1 in liters. This relationship is a helpful bedside check of test quality.'),
('3f118270-e29b-5da4-b13c-1e91821ada72'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which flow-volume loop pattern is characteristic of obstructive airway disease?', '["Square shape resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal shape accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Concave (scooped-out) expiratory limb with reduced peak flow","Increased peak flow without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Straight line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Concave (scooped-out) expiratory limb with reduced peak flow', 'In obstruction, airways narrow during forced expiration, so the expiratory limb shows a characteristic ''scooped-out'' concavity with a reduced peak flow and low flows at lower lung volumes. Restrictive disease instead produces a small, narrow loop.'),
('07e1dc07-2fae-5daa-bd2a-db4d897054be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What spirometry pattern is characteristic of restrictive lung disease?', '["Increased PEFR resulting in decreased functional activity","Scooped expiratory limb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Incomplete emptying mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduced volume (narrow base) with normal shape","Convex shape leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced volume (narrow base) with normal shape', 'Restrictive disease shows globally reduced lung volumes, producing a small, narrow (tall) flow-volume loop whose shape remains relatively normal. The FEV1/FVC ratio is preserved, unlike the scooped pattern of obstruction.'),
('89d23bd0-d298-58e8-8a12-4b11af1fd417'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which lung volumes can be measured by body plethysmography but not by spirometry?', '["FEV1 resulting in decreased functional activity","FVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VC mediated by specific receptor activation","PEFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TLC and RV (using Boyle''s law: P1V1 = P2V2)"]'::jsonb, 'TLC and RV (using Boyle''s law: P1V1 = P2V2)', 'Spirometry cannot measure the gas that remains in the lungs (RV), so TLC, FRC, and RV require body plethysmography or gas-dilution techniques. Plethysmography uses Boyle''s law (P1V1 = P2V2) to estimate intrathoracic gas volume.'),
('7b810e2a-dddd-5cc2-aecc-ee0c77a7e9a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The helium dilution method measures:', '["VC resulting in decreased functional activity","TLC directly accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FRC by equilibration of helium (helium dilution)","FEV1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Airway resistance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'FRC by equilibration of helium (helium dilution)', 'The subject rebreaths a known volume of helium-containing gas; because helium is insoluble and does not leave the lungs, the fall in helium concentration reflects the FRC into which it diluted. It measures gas that communicates with the airways.'),
('c4cc6562-61c2-532b-9ce6-a464f5db7eb6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The nitrogen washout method for measuring FRC:', '["Patient breathes 100% O2 and exhaled N2 is collected; the amount washed out is used to calculate FRC","Uses helium dilution only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uses body plethysmography mediated by specific receptor activation","Measures only tidal volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uses simple spirometry alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Patient breathes 100% O2 and exhaled N2 is collected; the amount washed out is used to calculate FRC', 'During nitrogen washout, the patient breathes 100% oxygen while all expired gas is collected. The total nitrogen washed out of the lungs divided by the initial alveolar N2 concentration gives the initial lung volume (FRC).'),
('3eec7ff5-74d8-52a2-ab1e-3898a77242cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The diffusing capacity of the lung (DLCO) measures:', '["Ventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Perfusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transfer of CO across the alveolar-capillary membrane (CO diffuses similarly to O2)","CO2 diffusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 diffusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer of CO across the alveolar-capillary membrane (CO diffuses similarly to O2)', 'DLCO quantifies how readily gas moves from the alveoli into the blood, estimated using carbon monoxide because its uptake is limited by diffusion rather than perfusion. It is reduced in emphysema, fibrosis, and anemia.'),
('8dc1115e-4ab1-5864-9bc1-7cb357b8637f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'DLCO is decreased in:', '["All of the above","Anemia (↓Hb)","Pulmonary embolism (↓capillary volume)","Emphysema (↓surface area)","Pulmonary fibrosis (membrane thickening)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'DLCO falls whenever the effective alveolar-capillary surface or the available hemoglobin is reduced, or the membrane is thickened. Anemia reduces the sink for CO, emphysema destroys surface area, PE removes perfused capillaries, and fibrosis thickens the membrane.'),
('75c0d289-8c06-5df6-aa73-0e4ec6ce3bf7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'DLCO is increased in:', '["Pulmonary hemorrhage (RBCs in alveoli bind CO)","Polycythemia","Exercise (↑capillary volume)","Left-to-right shunt","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'DLCO rises when more blood is present to take up CO, including polycythemia and increased pulmonary capillary volume during exercise or a left-to-right shunt. In pulmonary hemorrhage, red cells in the alveoli themselves bind additional CO.'),
('fa41e060-fa87-5dc9-ba92-58c03dd28c7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which equation gives the partial pressure of oxygen in the alveoli (PAO2)?', '["O2 consumption","PAO2 − PaCO2","Alveolar ventilation","PaCO2","PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/RQ)"]'::jsonb, 'PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/RQ)', 'The alveolar gas equation estimates PAO2 from the inspired PO2 minus the oxygen consumed relative to CO2 produced: PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/RQ), where RQ is the respiratory quotient (0.8). It links alveolar oxygen to ventilation.'),
('89a2b5ac-4147-523e-83b2-c23c284eca8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient on room air is about:', '[">20 mmHg","5-15 mmHg","25-40 mmHg","<5 mmHg","50 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '5-15 mmHg', 'The normal A-a gradient on room air is roughly 5-15 mmHg when breathing 21% O2, and it widens with age. A normal A-a gradient indicates hypoxemia from alveolar hypoventilation, whereas a widened gradient reflects V/Q mismatch, shunt, or diffusion impairment.'),
('01773687-3c55-5faf-aa64-121623bfa108'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A widened alveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient indicates:', '["A diagnostic problem resulting in decreased functional activity","Compensation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Impaired gas exchange (shunt, V/Q mismatch, or diffusion impairment)","Hypoventilation alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Low inspired FiO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired gas exchange (shunt, V/Q mismatch, or diffusion impairment)', 'A widened A-a gradient means oxygen is not equilibrating normally between alveoli and arterial blood, due to shunt, V/Q mismatch, or diffusion impairment. Pure alveolar hypoventilation and low inspired FiO2 leave the A-a gradient normal.'),
('c1b6cf31-f13a-5d3b-a124-d70c15f6ee5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 25-year-old presents with breathing difficulty and the arterial PO2 on 100% oxygen fails to rise above 200 mmHg. Which pattern of gas exchange abnormality is present?', '["Normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Severe hypoxemia due to a large right-to-left shunt","Mild hypoxemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperoxia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Respiratory alkalosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hypoxemia due to a large right-to-left shunt', 'In a right-to-left shunt, venous blood bypasses ventilated alveoli, so even breathing 100% oxygen cannot fully correct the arterial hypoxemia (PaO2 often stays below 200 mmHg). This contrasts with V/Q mismatch, which improves considerably on high oxygen.'),
('de68e71a-487e-5646-88b0-24d9bc720af1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The oxygen content of arterial blood (CaO2) is calculated as:', '["1.34 × Hb resulting in decreased functional activity","0.003 × PaO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PaO2 only mediated by specific receptor activation","SaO2 only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1.34 × Hb × SaO2 + 0.003 × PaO2"]'::jsonb, '1.34 × Hb × SaO2 + 0.003 × PaO2', 'Arterial oxygen content is dominated by hemoglobin-bound oxygen (1.34 mL O2 per gram of Hb × saturation) plus a small dissolved fraction (0.003 mL/dL per mmHg of PaO2). CaO2 drives oxygen delivery (DO2 = CO × CaO2).'),
('e0427583-e0ee-55fa-b68b-519b19de5151'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve is right-shifted (decreased O2 affinity) by:', '["All of: 3-BPG, ↑CO2 (Bohr effect), ↑H+ (↓pH), and ↑Temperature","3-BPG accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↑2 mediated by specific receptor activation","↑CO2 (Bohr effect) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↑H+ (↓pH) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'All of: 3-BPG, ↑CO2 (Bohr effect), ↑H+ (↓pH), and ↑Temperature', 'Hemoglobin releases O2 more readily when its affinity falls, which is promoted by increased 2,3-BPG, elevated CO2 (the Bohr effect), acidosis (↓pH), and fever. A right shift lowers P50 and improves oxygen unloading to tissues.'),
('9e926981-75bc-5fb7-befe-99715ae4c3b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A left shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve indicates:', '["Increased O2 affinity (less unloading to tissues)","More unloading of O2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rightward shift mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased P50 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased affinity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased O2 affinity (less unloading to tissues)', 'A left shift reflects increased hemoglobin affinity for oxygen (lower P50), so hemoglobin binds O2 more avidly and releases less to tissues. It is caused by opposite factors to a right shift, such as low temperature, alkalosis, and reduced 2,3-BPG.'),
('295b507f-bbd7-598e-91e5-98d0d95a4af0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The P50 (the PaO2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated) is normally:', '["20 mmHg","30 mmHg","40 mmHg","36 mmHg","26-27 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '26-27 mmHg', 'P50, the partial pressure at which hemoglobin is half-saturated, is about 26-27 mmHg in normal adult blood. A low P50 indicates increased O2 affinity (left shift); a high P50 indicates reduced affinity (right shift).'),
('ba8d3bb3-836f-59bd-8773-2a6f207c1bd0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has:', '["Cooperative oxygen binding","Higher O2 affinity than HbA (left-shifted curve)","Lower O2 affinity than HbA","Bohr effect only","Same O2 affinity as HbA"]'::jsonb, 'Higher O2 affinity than HbA (left-shifted curve)', 'HbF has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult hemoglobin because it binds 2,3-BPG less avidly, shifting the dissociation curve left. This allows the fetus to extract oxygen across the placenta from maternal blood.'),
('5d63cfb1-36b8-5980-b806-7a66c3f6af8c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning:', '["O2 content is increased resulting in decreased functional activity","Unloading of O2 is increased accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The ODC is right-shifted mediated by specific receptor activation","There is no significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO binds Hb with 200-250× the affinity of O2, causing a left shift and ↓O2 content"]'::jsonb, 'CO binds Hb with 200-250× the affinity of O2, causing a left shift and ↓O2 content', 'CO binds hemoglobin with about 200-250 times the affinity of O2, occupying sites that would otherwise carry oxygen and shifting the dissociation curve left. The result is markedly reduced oxygen content and impaired tissue oxygenation despite a normal PaO2.'),
('9b9f5cd0-e956-5d24-8186-92878cba9c24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the treatment of choice for carbon monoxide poisoning?', '["Intubation alone","Mechanical ventilation","100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen","CPAP","Breathing room air"]'::jsonb, '100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen', '100% oxygen competitively displaces CO from hemoglobin and shortens its half-life; hyperbaric oxygen further accelerates dissociation and is used in severe cases. High FiO2 is the cornerstone of therapy because CO has an enormous affinity advantage otherwise.'),
('05113fc1-b373-5844-9c0f-222298cc2085'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio for the whole lung is:', '["0.8-1.0","2.0","<0.5","1.5","0.5"]'::jsonb, '0.8-1.0', 'Total alveolar ventilation (4 L/min) and cardiac output (5 L/min) give an overall V/Q ratio near 0.8-1.0. Regional V/Q varies, being high at the apex (3) and low at the base (0.6).'),
('6a0063ac-9047-5949-aa3d-34d493ae7b6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In ideal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching:', '["V/Q = 1 (perfect match)","V/Q < 1","V/Q = 0","V/Q is infinite","V/Q > 1"]'::jsonb, 'V/Q = 1 (perfect match)', 'Ideal matching means alveolar ventilation exactly equals capillary perfusion in proportion, so V/Q = 1. In reality the whole-lung V/Q is 0.8, and regional matching is imperfect, producing the normal A-a gradient.'),
('1e2ff9ce-f066-5d7c-b61a-ca8760ef543b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A shunt (V/Q = 0) means:', '["Perfusion without ventilation, so deoxygenated blood enters the left heart (like a right-to-left shunt)","High V/Q accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal V/Q mediated by specific receptor activation","Low V/Q without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventilation without perfusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perfusion without ventilation, so deoxygenated blood enters the left heart (like a right-to-left shunt)', 'In a shunt, blood flows past unventilated alveoli (V/Q = 0) and returns to the arterial side without being oxygenated. Because this blood never sees alveolar gas, the resulting hypoxemia is poorly corrected by 100% oxygen.'),
('9997e0f1-bd7c-58e1-ae53-65bc10e27299'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 30-year-old with pulmonary embolism develops sudden dyspnea and evidence of increased dead space ventilation. The most likely explanation is:', '["Shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal V/Q accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Low V/Q without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventilation without perfusion (wasted ventilation)"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation without perfusion (wasted ventilation)', 'A pulmonary embolus occludes a pulmonary artery, so ventilated alveoli receive no blood flow (V/Q approaches infinity). This creates alveolar dead space and wasted ventilation, raising physiologic dead space and widening the A-a gradient.'),
('8a9a7303-3042-5827-9f3a-4eabfe7b01c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the definition of physiologic dead space ventilation?', '["Hyperoxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Dead space (wasted ventilation that does not participate in gas exchange)","Hypoxemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal gas exchange without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shunt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dead space (wasted ventilation that does not participate in gas exchange)', 'Physiologic dead space is the portion of each breath that ventilates gas-exchanging regions but does not take part in gas exchange, including anatomical dead space plus alveolar dead space. It is elevated when VD/VT exceeds about 0.3.'),
('c9563dc1-daab-5dd5-899f-a24dce3aa377'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 25-year-old presents with chronic cough and dyspnea attributed to regional hypoxia in the lung. The local response that redirects blood away from poorly ventilated alveoli is:', '["Decreased ventilation","Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV)","Vasodilation","Increased blood flow","Bronchodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV)', 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction constricts vessels supplying poorly ventilated (hypoxic) alveoli and diverts blood to better-ventilated regions. This optimizes V/Q matching, although when widespread it causes pulmonary hypertension.'),
('68f923e3-e6b3-533d-a719-e5b161d699a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In the upright lung, ventilation is greatest at the:', '["Equal throughout","Posterior regions","Apex","Lung bases (gravity-dependent)","Middle regions"]'::jsonb, 'Lung bases (gravity-dependent)', 'Because of gravity, the intrapleural pressure is less negative (more compressed) at the lung bases, so they undergo a greater volume change with breathing and receive more ventilation. Perfusion is also greatest at the bases.'),
('5251494b-7eec-5eec-be44-9c304aff01bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 70-year-old with suspected pulmonary embolism undergoes aV/Q scan. Which of the following normally has the highest ventilation and perfusion, providing the best match?', '["Posterior regions","Apex of the lung","Lung bases (gravity-dependent)","Middle lung zones","Equal everywhere"]'::jsonb, 'Lung bases (gravity-dependent)', 'Gravity makes both ventilation and perfusion greatest at the lung bases, where the change in pleural pressure and the driving pressure for blood flow are largest. Although V/Q is more uniform there, the base still receives the most blood and air.'),
('40a9b3c1-c435-5413-b7ce-85ec87c90fa9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The regional ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio from apex to base:', '["Is lowest at the apex","Is constant throughout","Is highest at the apex (3)","Is highest at the base","Is zero at the apex"]'::jsonb, 'Is highest at the apex (3)', 'Both ventilation and perfusion decrease toward the apex, but perfusion falls more steeply, so the V/Q ratio is highest at the apex (3) and lowest at the base (0.6). The base has the most blood flow despite the lower ratio.'),
('ca1d20ac-1067-572e-a762-430614b06ac0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 55-year-old with COPD has morning headaches and confusion. Physiologically, CO2 diffuses across the alveolar-capillary membrane how many times faster than O2?', '["100× resulting in decreased functional activity","50× accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","20× (higher solubility in plasma)","2× without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Same as O2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '20× (higher solubility in plasma)', 'The diffusion coefficient of a gas is proportional to its solubility and inversely proportional to the square root of its molecular weight. Because CO2 is far more soluble than O2, it diffuses about 20 times faster across the membrane.'),
('3aac70b6-c9da-5fb4-a7ca-232ed63b948d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The pulmonary capillary transit time at rest is:', '["2 seconds","1.5 seconds","0.5 seconds","0.25 seconds","0.75 seconds"]'::jsonb, '0.75 seconds', 'At rest, blood takes about 0.75 seconds to cross the pulmonary capillary, which is ample for gas equilibration. During heavy exercise the transit time shortens toward 0.3-0.5 seconds, which is why diffusion-limited impairment worsens with exercise.'),
('384ac259-80e0-573f-b234-44a49966889c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal fraction of cardiac output that bypasses gas exchange (physiologic shunt fraction) is:', '["<5% of total blood flow","20-25% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","15-20% mediated by specific receptor activation","10-15% without affecting the primary tissue pathway",">25% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '<5% of total blood flow', 'In healthy subjects, a small amount of mixed venous blood (including thebesian and bronchial drainage) bypasses oxygenation, amounting to less than 5% of cardiac output. Larger shunt fractions produce clinically significant hypoxemia.'),
('675fc00f-fecf-50f9-911b-d1ee6875ac9a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Venous admixture is a concept in which:', '["O2 is added to the blood resulting in decreased functional activity","CO2 is added to the blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed venous blood enters the arterial system (from shunt and low V/Q units)","Arterial blood enters the veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No mixing occurs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed venous blood enters the arterial system (from shunt and low V/Q units)', 'Venous admixture describes the effect of blood from shunt or low-V/Q units joining oxygenated blood, lowering the arterial PO2. It is quantified as the anatomical shunt fraction by the (CcO2 − CaO2)/(CcO2 − CvO2) equation.'),
('72b1e801-5b6e-5ad5-9482-4e1a59832c3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The respiratory exchange ratio (R) is defined as:', '["VO2 / VCO2","VE / VCO2","PaCO2 / PAO2","VT / FEV1","VCO2 / VO2"]'::jsonb, 'VCO2 / VO2', 'The respiratory exchange ratio is the rate of CO2 production divided by the rate of O2 consumption (VCO2/VO2). On a typical mixed diet R is about 0.8, and it exceeds 1.0 transiently at the ventilatory (lactate) threshold.'),
('a9d88ba6-74a9-5ee9-a1a2-a9803102e313'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The Bohr effect states that:', '["CO2 increases O2 binding to Hb resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alkalinity decreases Hb affinity for O2 mediated by specific receptor activation","O2 increases CO2 binding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acidity (H+ and CO2) decreases Hb affinity for O2 (right shift)"]'::jsonb, 'Acidity (H+ and CO2) decreases Hb affinity for O2 (right shift)', 'The Bohr effect describes how increased H+ and CO2 stabilize the deoxy (T) conformation of hemoglobin, lowering its O2 affinity and right-shifting the curve. This promotes O2 unloading in the acidic, CO2-rich tissues.'),
('0b4b58a0-b364-5ff2-8ffb-6b148706777b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The Bohr and Haldane effects work together to:', '["Facilitate both O2 unloading and CO2 loading at the tissues","Prevent O2 uptake accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prevent CO2 release mediated by specific receptor activation","Slow gas transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce gas exchange leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitate both O2 unloading and CO2 loading at the tissues', 'At the tissues, acidity released by CO2 cuts hemoglobin''s O2 affinity (Bohr), while deoxygenation raises hemoglobin''s affinity for CO2 and H+ (Haldane). Together they promote O2 delivery to muscles and CO2 pickup for transport to the lungs.'),
('bf5970bb-f4aa-5280-b394-e66d0ae054d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Where does the majority of CO2 conversion to bicarbonate occur in the body?', '["Endothelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Plasma only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RBCs (carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO2 + H2O ↔ H2CO3 ↔ HCO3− + H+)","Alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RBCs (carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO2 + H2O ↔ H2CO3 ↔ HCO3− + H+)', 'Carbonic anhydrase, concentrated inside red blood cells, rapidly hydrates CO2 to carbonic acid, which dissociates to HCO3− and H+. This makes the RBC the principal site where CO2 is converted to bicarbonate during transport.'),
('a3d588d4-58e8-52e4-a8fa-c726eef8ac03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Most CO2 is transported in the blood as:', '["Carbamino compounds (23%)","Dissolved CO2 (7%)","Bicarbonate (HCO3−) (70%)","Carbonic acid","Bound to hemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Bicarbonate (HCO3−) (70%)', 'Roughly 70% of CO2 is carried as bicarbonate, about 23% as carbamino compounds bound to hemoglobin, and about 7% dissolved in plasma. The bicarbonate is generated in red cells and exchanged for chloride by the chloride (Hamburger) shift.'),
('391110f6-5f42-585d-a07f-684c98d2b0bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is carbaminohemoglobin and how is CO2 transported in this form?', '["CO2 dissolving in plasma resulting in decreased functional activity","HCO3− formation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CO2 binding to terminal amine groups of proteins (mainly Hb)","CO2 excreted by the kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO2 binding to lipids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CO2 binding to terminal amine groups of proteins (mainly Hb)', 'Carbaminohemoglobin forms when CO2 binds directly to the free amino groups of globin chains. This reaction accounts for about 20-30% of CO2 transport, and deoxygenated hemoglobin binds more CO2 (the Haldane effect).'),
('2875d572-d946-54c6-80a0-65d82775c580'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'How does hemoglobin function as a buffer during CO2 transport?', '["Proteins resulting in decreased functional activity","HCO3− only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Buffering mediated by specific receptor activation","Phosphate only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hb buffers H+ produced from carbonic acid, minimizing pH change"]'::jsonb, 'Hb buffers H+ produced from carbonic acid, minimizing pH change', 'Each CO2 converted to bicarbonate releases one H+. Hemoglobin, mainly through its reduced (deoxy) form, binds these protons, so blood pH barely changes as CO2 is transported. This is the isohydric (Haldane) buffering of carriage.'),
('7b3502ce-697f-517e-ad5c-377198f538f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The pulmonary capillary endothelium contains high concentrations of:', '["Na+ channels resulting in decreased functional activity","Water channels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","K+ channels mediated by specific receptor activation","H+ pumps without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carbonic anhydrase (accelerates HCO3− → CO2 conversion)"]'::jsonb, 'Carbonic anhydrase (accelerates HCO3− → CO2 conversion)', 'Like the red cell, the pulmonary capillary endothelium contains carbonic anhydrase, which accelerates the dehydration of HCO3− back to CO2 so it can diffuse into the alveoli. This helps offload CO2 in the lung.'),
('55ab1231-1aad-5254-87d6-688718d201fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The amount of O2 delivered to the tissues each minute (DO2) equals:', '["PaO2 × CO","CO × CaO2","CO × CvO2","VO2 × CO","Hb × SaO2"]'::jsonb, 'CO × CaO2', 'Oxygen delivery is the product of cardiac output and arterial oxygen content: DO2 = CO × CaO2. Under basal conditions this is roughly 250 mL O2/min per 5 L/min of flow with a CaO2 of about 20 mL/dL.'),
('97d68a70-3460-551f-b1c3-ae5e7e9bab81'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Oxygen consumption (VO2) at rest is approximately:', '["1000 mL/min","750 mL/min","250 mL/min","100 mL/min","500 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '250 mL/min', 'Basal whole-body oxygen consumption in an adult is about 250 mL/min, equivalent to an oxygen extraction ratio near 25% of the 1000 mL/min delivered. During maximal exercise VO2 can rise more than tenfold.'),
('eb131ca3-9744-5f4d-9e88-66142bcd561c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The oxygen extraction ratio (O2ER) is defined as:', '["CaO2 − CvO2 resulting in decreased functional activity","CaO2 / CvO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VO2 × DO2 mediated by specific receptor activation","VO2 / DO2 (equals (CaO2 − CvO2)/CaO2)","DO2 / VO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 / DO2 (equals (CaO2 − CvO2)/CaO2)', 'The O2 extraction ratio is the fraction of delivered oxygen that the tissues consume, VO2/DO2, equivalent to (CaO2 − CvO2)/CaO2. It is normally about 25% and rises when delivery falls below the critical DO2.'),
('c901a087-6259-5491-9c1e-e4444b6e5418'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The critical DO2 is:', '["The anaerobic threshold resulting in decreased functional activity","VO2 max accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The DO2 below which VO2 becomes supply-dependent (300-400 mL/min)","Resting VO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximal exercise VO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The DO2 below which VO2 becomes supply-dependent (300-400 mL/min)', 'Normally VO2 is independent of delivery, but below a critical DO2 (≈300-400 mL/min) oxygen consumption falls with delivery and anaerobic metabolism begins. This supply-dependence marks the onset of tissue dysoxia.'),
('bec9c349-d3d4-59d0-8d7b-015517e2fdaf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Arterial hypoxemia (low PaO2) can cause:', '["Cyanosis if deoxyHb > 5 g/dL","Reduced O2 content","Stimulation of peripheral chemoreceptors","All of the above","Pallor"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Low PaO2 lowers arterial O2 content and, when reduced hemoglobin exceeds about 5 g/dL, produces cyanosis. It also stimulates the carotid and aortic bodies, which drive the hypoxic ventilatory response, especially when PaO2 is below about 60 mmHg.'),
('d2dcee6b-8562-5c90-9bf0-4c907c745154'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What are the five main mechanisms of hypoxemia?', '["Only right-to-left shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","Only diffusion impairment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoventilation, diffusion impairment, V/Q mismatch, right-to-left shunt, and low inspired O2 (FiO2)","Only hypoventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only low FiO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoventilation, diffusion impairment, V/Q mismatch, right-to-left shunt, and low inspired O2 (FiO2)', 'Hypoxemia arises through five mechanisms: alveolar hypoventilation, diffusion impairment, ventilation-perfusion mismatch, right-to-left shunting, and reduced inspired oxygen (as at high altitude). The A-a gradient and the response to 100% O2 help distinguish them.'),
('8a179569-14e5-5da3-aac1-44a0d13c0c72'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 65-year-old with muscle weakness has arterial blood gas showing hypoxemia with an elevated PaCO2 and a normal A-a gradient. The mechanism of hypoxemia is:', '["↑PaCO2 with normal A-a gradient (alveolar hypoventilation)","↓PaCO2 with widened A-a gradient","Normal PaCO2 and widened A-a gradient","Right-to-left shunt","Alveolar dead space from PE"]'::jsonb, '↑PaCO2 with normal A-a gradient (alveolar hypoventilation)', 'Alveolar hypoventilation lowers alveolar and arterial PO2 while raising PaCO2, but the A-a gradient stays normal because gas exchange itself is preserved. Weakness of the respiratory muscles is a classic cause. All other mechanisms of hypoxemia widen the A-a gradient.'),
('50da7a19-b2b8-5392-a5fc-6dbc4fd6f12d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 35-year-old recently traveled to high altitude and presents with headache and dyspnea. The expected arterial blood gas pattern is:', '["↑A-a gradient with hypercapnia","Right shift of the dissociation curve","Normal blood gases","Hypoxemia with ↓PaCO2 due to hyperventilation","Hypercapnic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia with ↓PaCO2 due to hyperventilation', 'At high altitude the inspired PO2 is low, producing hypoxemia, but the resulting hypoxic ventilatory drive overventilates the patient, lowering PaCO2. The A-a gradient is normal because the defect is a low inspired O2 rather than lung disease.'),
('374e29c6-7d5e-5e53-9057-431a334e8e95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Diffusion impairment causes significant hypoxemia especially during:', '["Supine position resulting in decreased functional activity","Rest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exercise (reduced capillary transit time)","Breath holding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sleep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise (reduced capillary transit time)', 'At rest the 0.75-second capillary transit time usually suffices for equilibration even with a thickened membrane. During exercise, the shorter transit time may not allow full equilibration, so diffusion-limited hypoxemia becomes evident with exertion.'),
('83a0f247-f706-51db-8033-862e096c9b6f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Hypoxemia due to ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch is characterized by:', '["Improvement with supplemental O2","No change with O2","Hypercapnia","Failure to correct with O2","Worsening with O2"]'::jsonb, 'Improvement with supplemental O2', 'In V/Q mismatch, extra inspired oxygen can enter poorly ventilated units and raise arterial PO2 substantially, often above 500 mmHg on 100% O2. This is in contrast to true right-to-left shunt, where hypoxemia is refractory to oxygen.'),
('74170a4c-c6c2-5e2a-9e0e-bce6c7a65462'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A right-to-left shunt is suggested by:', '["Improvement of hypoxemia with O2 resulting in decreased functional activity","Low A-a gradient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Failure of hypoxemia to correct with 100% O2 (PaO2 usually <200 mmHg)","Normal PaO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypercapnia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Failure of hypoxemia to correct with 100% O2 (PaO2 usually <200 mmHg)', 'Blood shunted past the lungs never contacts alveolar gas, so breathing 100% oxygen raises the PaO2 little, usually remaining below about 200 mmHg in a significant shunt. V/Q mismatch and diffusion impairment both correct considerably on high inspired O2.'),
('b8c7769d-6a1d-5258-9904-4bc5dfb3528e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Pulmonary vasoconstriction in response to alveolar hypoxia is:', '["Only in utero resulting in decreased functional activity","Irreversible (necrotic) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reversible (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction)","Always benign without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Permanent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reversible (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction)', 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction constricts segmental vessels within minutes of regional hypoxia and reverses when oxygenation improves. It diverts flow to better-ventilated alveoli, and only when chronic and widespread does it lead to fixed pulmonary hypertension.'),
('ff8cdd11-cbfc-57ae-b11a-fe129e3e2f0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The ventilatory response to hypercapnia is:', '["Apnea resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased ventilation (CO2 is the main ventilatory driver)","Decreased ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchoconstriction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No change leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation (CO2 is the main ventilatory driver)', 'PaCO2 is the dominant controller of ventilation; a rise in arterial CO2 briskly increases minute ventilation, mediated mainly by central chemoreceptors sensing CSF H+. The response is steep and linear over the physiologic range.'),
('c6571356-6f19-5b9d-8fb5-0ec24c1248c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The chemoreceptors that detect arterial CO2 are located:', '["Central (medullary) and peripheral (carotid/aortic)","Only central accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only peripheral mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial walls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central (medullary) and peripheral (carotid/aortic)', 'CO2 is sensed both centrally, by medullary chemoreceptors that respond to CSF H+, and peripherally, by the carotid and aortic bodies. Central receptors mediate about 80% of the ventilatory response to CO2.'),
('60f7c737-d88d-5855-9851-3c4f5b151d60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'How do central chemoreceptors in the medulla sense arterial CO2?', '["Na+ concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","H+ in CSF (from CO2 diffusing across the blood-brain barrier)","O2 directly mediated by specific receptor activation","K+ concentration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO2 binding directly to receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+ in CSF (from CO2 diffusing across the blood-brain barrier)', 'CO2 diffuses freely across the blood-brain barrier and, under the action of carbonic anhydrase, generates H+ in the CSF. Central chemoreceptors on the ventral medullary surface are stimulated by this CSF H+, which closely tracks arterial PCO2.'),
('ed849f2f-86f7-5501-9da2-20388bd74e3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The blood-brain barrier is relatively impermeable to:', '["H+ and HCO3− (but CO2 freely diffuses)","CO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urea mediated by specific receptor activation","O2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+ and HCO3− (but CO2 freely diffuses)', 'Hydrogen and bicarbonate ions cross the blood-brain barrier poorly, whereas CO2 diffuses freely. As a result, central chemoreceptors sample the H+ that CO2 generates in the CSF, and rapid correction of CSF pH depends on bicarbonate transport.'),
('cc11186f-c86a-5850-8e4a-a033ea84f2be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The ventilatory response to hypoxia is mediated mainly by:', '["Decreased ventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies)","Central chemoreceptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercapnia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Apnea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies)', 'Hypoxia stimulates the peripheral chemoreceptors, chiefly the carotid bodies, which increase ventilation through the glossopharyngeal nerve. The response becomes prominent only when PaO2 falls below about 60 mmHg.'),
('33988ce8-b103-58fb-a7d3-161195ae5c26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In the lung, the Haldane effect promotes:', '["CO2 unloading (oxygenation of Hb reduces its CO2 affinity)","Release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","O2 loading mediated by specific receptor activation","O2 unloading without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO2 loading leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CO2 unloading (oxygenation of Hb reduces its CO2 affinity)', 'When hemoglobin becomes oxygenated in the pulmonary capillaries, it binds less CO2 and less H+, favoring CO2 release into the alveolus and conversion of bicarbonate back to CO2. This is the pulmonary half of the Haldane effect.'),
('374c7812-df66-5d8c-9239-a5501b97b179'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In which condition do ventilated alveoli receive no perfusion, producing wasted ventilation (increased dead space)?', '["Pulmonary embolism (no perfusion of ventilated alveoli)","Normal lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asthma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary embolism (no perfusion of ventilated alveoli)', 'An embolus obstructs a pulmonary artery, so alveoli distal to it are ventilated but not perfused, creating alveolar dead space (V/Q extremely high). The wasted ventilation raises physiologic dead space and worsens gas exchange.'),
('b5d286bc-d0d1-5f30-8c9a-35c0e253cadb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Alveolar ventilation (VA) equals:', '["TV × RR (minute ventilation)","VE − VD only","IC × RR","VT × RR − (VD × RR)","FRC × RR"]'::jsonb, 'VT × RR − (VD × RR)', 'Alveolar ventilation is the minute ventilation minus the ventilation wasted on dead space: VA = (VT − VD) × RR = VT × RR − VD × RR. Because PaCO2 is inversely proportional to VA, doubling VA halves PaCO2.'),
('f64d1083-c071-5c6b-b37a-64b5ed26be98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The relationship between PaCO2 and alveolar ventilation (VA) is:', '["PaCO2 ∝ VA resulting in decreased functional activity","PaCO2 ∝ O2 consumption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PaCO2 ∝ VCO2 / VA (inverse relationship)","No relationship without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PaCO2 ∝ VCO2 alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PaCO2 ∝ VCO2 / VA (inverse relationship)', 'Alveolar PCO2 is set by the ratio of CO2 production to alveolar ventilation: PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA. Thus increasing alveolar ventilation lowers PaCO2, and hypoventilation raises it proportionately.'),
('9d5bf93f-faeb-5bc5-8454-76cb27981618'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What are the three components of the total work of breathing?', '["Elastic work + resistive work + inertial work","Only resistive work","Only elastic work","Only inertial work","Inspiratory plus expiratory"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic work + resistive work + inertial work', 'Total work of breathing is the sum of elastic work to stretch lung and chest wall, resistive (frictional) work to move air through the airways, and a small inertial component to accelerate gases. Elastic work dominates in normal quiet breathing.'),
('c4d0d9a6-390b-5060-8fba-594c8f3349f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'J (juxtapulmonary capillary) receptors:', '["Signal pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Respond to stretch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sense temperature mediated by specific receptor activation","Near capillaries, respond to pulmonary congestion/edema → rapid shallow breathing","Detect CO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Near capillaries, respond to pulmonary congestion/edema → rapid shallow breathing', 'J receptors lie in the alveolar walls close to the pulmonary capillaries and are activated by interstitial congestion, edema, and microemboli. Stimulation produces rapid shallow breathing, dyspnea, and bradycardia, as in pulmonary edema or pneumonia.'),
('98f2da24-d83b-570b-92a4-32062a24cfef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which receptors mediate reflex bronchoconstriction and cough in response to inhaled irritants?', '["Peripheral chemoreceptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Central chemoreceptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Airway stretch receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Irritant (rapidly adapting) receptors responding to dust, cold, and chemicals","J receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irritant (rapidly adapting) receptors responding to dust, cold, and chemicals', 'Irritant receptors are rapidly adapting receptors located in the airway epithelium that are stimulated by mechanical and chemical irritants such as dust, smoke, and cold air. They trigger cough, bronchoconstriction, and mucus secretion.'),
('5e081b4e-5626-5fc9-9552-48a1a5b78bd5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Type J (C-fiber) receptors in the lung are stimulated by:', '["Pulmonary congestion, microemboli, and edema","Airway stretch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temperature mediated by specific receptor activation","CO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary congestion, microemboli, and edema', 'The juxtacapillary (J) C-fiber receptors in the alveolar interstitium are activated by pulmonary congestion, edema, and microembolism. Their activation produces rapid shallow breathing, dyspnea, and cough, particularly in interstitial fluid accumulation.'),
('8993fe2e-2eb7-53c9-a75f-57c4746e71de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Parasympathetic (vagal) tone to the airways causes:', '["Decreased secretions resulting in decreased functional activity","Bronchoconstriction and increased secretions","Bronchodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased heart rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchoconstriction and increased secretions', 'Vagal (cholinergic) stimulation constricts bronchial smooth muscle via M3 receptors and increases mucus secretion. This is the dominant bronchomotor influence at rest and is antagonized by sympathetic β2 stimulation.'),
('4a3a3894-e014-542a-8354-05957928b4c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Sympathetic stimulation of airway β2 receptors causes:', '["Bronchodilation","Constriction","Increased secretions","No effect","Mucus plugging"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchodilation', 'Activation of β2-adrenergic receptors relaxes bronchial smooth muscle, producing bronchodilation. This is why β2 agonists (e.g., salbutamol, albuterol) are the mainstay of acute asthma treatment.'),
('40c44065-a3fa-5c1c-83e6-4efac57107a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The pathophysiologic triad of asthma is:', '["Only airway inflammation resulting in decreased functional activity","Infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only bronchoconstriction mediated by specific receptor activation","Bronchoconstriction, airway inflammation, and mucus plugging","Only mucus hypersecretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchoconstriction, airway inflammation, and mucus plugging', 'Asthma involves variable airway obstruction from bronchospasm, chronic airway inflammation, and mucus plugging all together. The inflammation is usually allergic (eosinophilic, IgE-mediated), and obstruction is characteristically reversible.'),
('6f4caf09-27e9-57b4-9d86-2fb73bba756d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The FEV1 improves after an inhaled bronchodilator in:', '["Fibrosis","Normal lung","Asthma (reversible obstruction)","Emphysema (irreversible)","Bronchiectasis with scarring"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma (reversible obstruction)', 'A brisk improvement in FEV1 after bronchodilator indicates reversible airflow obstruction, the defining feature of asthma. In emphysema and fixed COPD the obstruction is largely irreversible, though a small degree of reversibility is sometimes seen.'),
('da17352a-0c84-537d-b8d5-e63a41fa362a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Small airways disease in COPD produces:', '["Emphysema only resulting in decreased functional activity","Remodeling only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","No significant effect mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased resistance in bronchioles <2 mm, early airway closure, and air trapping","Surfactant deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased resistance in bronchioles <2 mm, early airway closure, and air trapping', 'The bronchioles smaller than 2 mm contribute little normal resistance but become the dominant site of airflow limitation in COPD. Inflammation and fibrosis narrow them, causing early dynamic airway closure, air trapping, and hyperinflation.'),
('67cf6a4e-b370-5151-b04f-c8b0eff4d5a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Dynamic hyperinflation (auto-PEEP) in COPD results from:', '["Incomplete exhalation before the next breath → positive end-expiratory pressure","Decreased airway resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal expiration mediated by specific receptor activation","Full exhalation with each breath without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Incomplete exhalation before the next breath → positive end-expiratory pressure', 'Because of high expiratory resistance, COPD patients cannot empty the lungs fully before the next breath begins. Lung volume and end-expiratory pressure rise progressively (auto-PEEP or intrinsic PEEP), increasing the work of breathing and impairing cardiac filling.'),
('e504d5aa-77db-59e5-bf83-3ea40e653403'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The most sensitive test for small airways disease is:', '["FEV1/FVC resulting in decreased functional activity","FEV1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MEF25-75 (forced expiratory flow between 25-75% of FVC)","FVC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PEFR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MEF25-75 (forced expiratory flow between 25-75% of FVC)', 'MEF25-75 (mid-expiratory flow) measures flow at relatively low lung volumes during forced expiration and is the most sensitive spirometric index for detecting small airways disease. FEV1 and the FEV1/FVC ratio are later, less sensitive findings.'),
('9e60021c-6568-58ef-af30-427709e307d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Closing volume (CV) is the:', '["Volume at full exhalation resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung volume at which small airways in the dependent lung zones close","TLC mediated by specific receptor activation","Volume at full inspiration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FRC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung volume at which small airways in the dependent lung zones close', 'Closing volume is the lung volume (measured from residual volume) at which small airways, particularly in dependent regions, begin to close during expiration. When closing capacity exceeds FRC, the airways close during tidal breathing, impairing gas exchange.'),
('ca443c51-872f-5344-9ff5-03ba918f2fc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Closing capacity (CC) equals:', '["CV + RV","TLC","TV","VC","FRC"]'::jsonb, 'CV + RV', 'Closing capacity is the lung volume at which airway closure begins, computed as closing volume plus residual volume (CC = CV + RV). Clinically, one asks whether CC encroaches on FRC, which happens with aging and in smokers.'),
('b0a031f6-ab83-5dc0-9ef9-4568da72654e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Closing volume increases with:', '["Tall stature","Normal aging and smoking","Female sex","Regular exercise","Young age"]'::jsonb, 'Normal aging and smoking', 'Closing volume increases with advancing age and with cigarette smoking because loss of elastic recoil and small airways disease cause earlier airway closure. When it exceeds FRC (which falls with age), small airways close during tidal breathing.'),
('8ccd1f6c-ac13-5b20-9c86-6d19b1b5bbde'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What does the single-breath nitrogen washout test measure?', '["Compliance resulting in decreased functional activity","Closing volume only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Uniformity of ventilation (an increased slope indicates V/Q inequality)","TLC only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dead space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Uniformity of ventilation (an increased slope indicates V/Q inequality)', 'In the single-breath nitrogen test the patient inhales pure oxygen then exhales while alveolar nitrogen concentration is recorded; a rising phase-3 slope indicates uneven ventilation distribution. It also allows closing volume to be identified from the rising phase-4.'),
('9dcb8ad9-6340-5d85-8537-5165d3b956a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which grouping correctly lists the muscles that participate in respiration?', '["Diaphragm (most important), intercostals, abdominals, and accessory muscles","Only the abdominal muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only the intercostal muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the scalenes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only the diaphragm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm (most important), intercostals, abdominals, and accessory muscles', 'The diaphragm is the main inspiratory muscle, assisted by the external intercostals; the internal intercostals and abdominals are expiratory, and the scalenes and sternocleidomastoid act as accessory inspiratory muscles during heavy breathing.'),
('3fa6e290-61ee-5de0-95a6-416171e508cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Paralysis of the diaphragm leads to:', '["Normal breathing resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cough impairment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paradoxical movement (upward during inspiration) and reduction of VC by 50%","No significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paradoxical movement (upward during inspiration) and reduction of VC by 50%', 'With a paralyzed diaphragm, the contracting abdominal wall pushes the flaccid diaphragm upward during inspiration, producing paradoxical movement. Vital capacity falls by as much as half because the diaphragm normally generates the largest part of the volume change.'),
('23f7a7d3-d728-59f7-80a7-027ff8c2595d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which muscles act as accessory inspiratory muscles when ventilation is increased (e.g., during exercise or distress)?', '["Abdominal muscles","Pelvic floor muscles","Rectus abdominis","Internal intercostals (interosseous)","Scalenes and sternocleidomastoid"]'::jsonb, 'Scalenes and sternocleidomastoid', 'The scalenes and sternocleidomastoid elevate the upper ribs and sternum and are recruited during increased ventilatory demand. They are not ordinarily active during quiet breathing, so their visible use signals respiratory distress.'),
('81a28d46-2b08-5324-ab55-e0482c776300'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'During normal quiet breathing, expiration is:', '["Forceful resulting in decreased functional activity","Only diaphragmatic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Passive (due to elastic recoil of lungs and chest wall)","Muscular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Active leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Passive (due to elastic recoil of lungs and chest wall)', 'Relaxation of inspiratory muscles at the end of inspiration allows the elastic recoil of the lungs (and chest wall) to expel air passively. Expiratory muscles are recruited only during forced expiration, as in exercise or obstruction.'),
('23c7f56e-c0e5-5ac2-bf31-ec53500b2440'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Maximum inspiratory pressure (MIP) measures:', '["Lung volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Airway resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DLCO mediated by specific receptor activation","Respiratory muscle strength (normal 100 cmH2O)","Compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory muscle strength (normal 100 cmH2O)', 'MIP (or negative inspiratory force) is measured against an occluded airway at residual volume and reflects inspiratory muscle strength, normally about -100 cmH2O. It is reduced in neuromuscular weakness such as myasthenia gravis or phrenic palsy.'),
('37ea67a3-6c06-55dc-ac90-112c3bb28169'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the normal maximum expiratory pressure (MEP) and what does it assess?', '["Airflow resulting in decreased functional activity","Inspiratory strength accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Expiratory muscle strength (cough effectiveness; normal 150 cm H2O)","Lung volumes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Expiratory muscle strength (cough effectiveness; normal 150 cm H2O)', 'MEP is measured at total lung capacity against an occlusion and reflects expiratory (abdominal and internal intercostal) muscle strength, normally about 150 cm H2O. It is a key predictor of effective cough, especially in neuromuscular disease.'),
('e4d711d8-bbf8-52e1-8c1d-1fd045a7248a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which lung volume equals vital capacity plus residual volume?', '["TV + IRV","VC + RV","VC + TV","FRC + IRV","RV + TV"]'::jsonb, 'VC + RV', 'Vital capacity is the volume expired from TLC to RV, so adding the residual volume reconstructs the total lung capacity: TLC = VC + RV. Residual volume is the part of TLC that cannot be measured by spirometry.'),
('a2600023-2c02-5d56-92ae-82ee2eb756d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Vital capacity (VC) equals:', '["TV + RV","IRV + ERV","FRC + IRV","IRV + TV + ERV","TLC − TV"]'::jsonb, 'IRV + TV + ERV', 'Vital capacity is the sum of inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume, and expiratory reserve volume (IRV + TV + ERV). Equivalently it is TLC minus residual volume, spanning the volumes between maximal inspiration and maximal expiration.'),
('607e9eac-4a5b-5585-8a06-087307b700c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the definition and approximate normal value of residual volume (RV)?', '["Maximal inspiration volume","Volume remaining after maximal exhalation (1.2 L)","Volume after a normal inspiration","Total lung capacity","Volume after a normal exhalation"]'::jsonb, 'Volume remaining after maximal exhalation (1.2 L)', 'Residual volume is the air that remains in the lungs after a maximal forced exhalation, about 1.2 L in adults. It cannot be exhaled and therefore cannot be measured by spirometry; it helps keep alveoli open between breaths.'),
('3509076d-9737-5036-8c17-b9925fdeb223'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The FEV1/FVC ratio is normally:', '["0.3-0.5",">1.0","0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)","0.5","0.9-1.0"]'::jsonb, '0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)', 'In healthy adults FEV1 is about 75-85% of FVC (ratio ≥0.7). Values below 0.7 indicate obstructive airflow limitation, whereas restrictive disease preserves or even raises the ratio while lowering FVC.'),
('ce906b6e-bd81-50bd-8d69-65e1d4633934'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What spirometry findings are characteristic of COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease)?', '["Normal pattern resulting in decreased functional activity","↑FEV1/FVC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↓FEV1/FVC (<0.7), FVC may be normal or ↓ (hyperinflation), and ↑RV","↑TLC only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Normal FEV1/FVC with ↑FVC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↓FEV1/FVC (<0.7), FVC may be normal or ↓ (hyperinflation), and ↑RV', 'COPD is defined spirometrically by a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.7. Expiratory flow limitation, early airway closure, and air trapping raise residual volume, and hyperinflation increases TLC while FVC may fall.'),
('cf618102-7f43-5a21-9a2f-42fe3795a021'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing shows:', '["↓FEV1/FVC resulting in decreased functional activity","↑TLC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↓TLC, FEV1/FVC normal or ↑, and ↓FVC","Normal pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstructive pattern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↓TLC, FEV1/FVC normal or ↑, and ↓FVC', 'Restrictive disease lowers total lung capacity and vital capacity while the FEV1/FVC ratio is preserved or even increased. This pattern is seen in pulmonary fibrosis, chest wall restriction, and neuromuscular weakness, and differs from the low ratio of obstruction.'),
('a94fff05-0246-52fc-820f-521b59353656'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Helium dilution measures FRC by:', '["Closed-circuit rebreathing of a He/O2 mixture; it measures the communicating gas volume and may underestimate FRC with air trapping","Direct measurement of TLC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nitrogen washout mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Body plethysmography without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple open-circuit spirometry leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Closed-circuit rebreathing of a He/O2 mixture; it measures the communicating gas volume and may underestimate FRC with air trapping', 'Helium dilution uses a closed circuit in which insoluble helium equilibrates with the FRC; the fall in helium concentration gives the gas volume that communicates with the airways. In emphysema and air trapping, non-communicating pockets make this underestimate the true volume measured by plethysmography.'),
('6900a48d-5cef-508c-ab5b-8994156a22b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Closing volume (CV) and closing capacity (CC):', '["Only relate to diffusion resulting in decreased functional activity","CV is the lung volume at which small airways in dependent zones begin to close; CC = CV + RV","Only relate to airway resistance mediated by specific receptor activation","Only relate to compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Have no clinical use leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CV is the lung volume at which small airways in dependent zones begin to close; CC = CV + RV', 'Closing volume marks the point during expiration when dependent small airways start to close; adding residual volume gives closing capacity (CC = CV + RV). When CC exceeds FRC, airways close during quiet tidal breathing, as in the elderly and smokers.'),
('520643ed-93c8-56ad-bc1b-8920b2cc460b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Maximal voluntary ventilation (MVV) is measured by:', '["Breathing at rest resulting in decreased functional activity","FVC × 40 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TV × RR mediated by specific receptor activation","FEV1 × 10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Breathing maximally for 12-15 seconds and extrapolating to one minute (120-170 L/min)"]'::jsonb, 'Breathing maximally for 12-15 seconds and extrapolating to one minute (120-170 L/min)', 'MVV measures the greatest volume of air that can be ventilated per minute, obtained by a maximal 12-15 second effort extrapolated to 60 seconds, normally about 120-170 L/min. It integrates airway resistance, respiratory muscle strength, and coordination.'),
('ab00bfb9-fb56-5a6b-a7c6-e83af77919ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'LABAs + monotherapy in asthma:', '["Increased exacerbation risk","Ineffective","Tolerance","Expensive","Addiction"]'::jsonb, 'Increased exacerbation risk', 'FDA black box: always combine with ICS.'),
('c9a9b51b-7513-5c11-911f-70dd808587dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The flow-volume loop in restrictive lung disease shows:', '["A broad concave loop resulting in decreased functional activity","A scoop in the expiratory limb accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Normal shape mediated by specific receptor activation","A narrow, tall loop with small area (↓TLC but relatively preserved peak flow)","A convex inspiratory limb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A narrow, tall loop with small area (↓TLC but relatively preserved peak flow)', 'Because restrictive disease reduces lung volumes without proportional loss of airway flow, the loop is small and narrow (tall) with a relatively preserved peak flow. This contrasts with obstruction, which produces a scooped-out expiratory limb.'),
('e47e097f-bb50-5130-95d5-eb83f82bc808'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Dead space is the volume of:', '["Alveoli only resulting in decreased functional activity","Parenchyma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Conducting airways (no gas exchange); anatomical dead space ≈150 mL (2 mL/kg)","Capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Conducting airways (no gas exchange); anatomical dead space ≈150 mL (2 mL/kg)', 'Dead space is the air that occupies the conducting airways (nose to terminal bronchioles), where no gas exchange occurs; it totals about 150 mL (≈2 mL/kg). Physiological dead space adds any ventilated non-perfused alveoli to this anatomical component.'),
('06e92dd9-71fa-5976-8755-40bdb76a66ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 70-year-old with pulmonary embolism has sudden dyspnea and increased dead space ventilation. The affected alveoli are best described as:', '["Ventilated without perfusion (V/Q → ∞), producing wasted ventilation","Anatomic dead space accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Perfused without ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Collapsed alveoli leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilated without perfusion (V/Q → ∞), producing wasted ventilation', 'A pulmonary embolus prevents blood from reaching normally ventilated alveoli, pushing their V/Q ratio toward infinity and creating alveolar dead space. The wasted ventilation raises physiologic dead space and widens the A-a gradient.'),
('846c99c9-a738-5170-8f15-f7a439604ab1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Physiologic dead space (VDphys) equals:', '["Tidal volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Anatomical dead space only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Residual volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar dead space only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anatomical dead space + alveolar dead space (VDanat + VDalv)"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical dead space + alveolar dead space (VDanat + VDalv)', 'Physiological dead space is the sum of anatomical dead space (conducting airways) and alveolar dead space (ventilated but unperfused alveoli). In health alveolar dead space is negligible, so physiologic and anatomical dead space are nearly equal (150 mL).'),
('3f4b856e-25ec-5480-a280-7d7e7a005e1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 70-year-old with COPD has increasing morning headache and confusion. PaCO2 is primarily determined by which relationship?', '["Dead space only resulting in decreased functional activity","Tidal volume only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar ventilation (VA) and CO2 production (VCO2); PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA","Respiratory rate alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 consumption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar ventilation (VA) and CO2 production (VCO2); PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA', 'PaCO2 is set by the ratio of CO2 production to alveolar ventilation: PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA. In COPD, reduced alveolar ventilation from dead-space load and poor ventilation raises PaCO2, contributing to morning headache from overnight CO2 retention.'),
('b6f1e57a-0c19-5e65-876a-a38f87e87d9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The alveolar gas equation is:', '["PIO2 × R resulting in decreased functional activity","R × PaCO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PaO2 − PaCO2 mediated by specific receptor activation","PIO2 + PaCO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/R) (+ a small correction)"]'::jsonb, 'PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/R) (+ a small correction)', 'The alveolar gas equation calculates PAO2 from the inspired PO2 minus the oxygen consumed during CO2 production: PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/R). It is used to derive the A-a gradient and detect impaired gas exchange.'),
('2a527abd-f0e4-5c64-88c1-6e667d9873f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal alveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient on room air is approximately:', '[">100 mmHg resulting in decreased functional activity","20-30 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","0 mmHg mediated by specific receptor activation","5-15 mmHg on room air (increases with age)","40-50 mmHg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '5-15 mmHg on room air (increases with age)', 'On room air the A-a gradient is normally about 5-15 mmHg and slowly widens with age, roughly PaO2 (mmHg) = 104 − (age/3) minus 10. A larger gradient indicates shunt, V/Q mismatch, or diffusion impairment.'),
('709673cf-1cb8-5681-9a5f-58e299cd3d17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A widened A-a gradient that improves substantially with supplemental oxygen (PaO2 rising above 500 mmHg on 100% O2) is most characteristic of:', '["Right-to-left shunt","V/Q mismatch","Hypoventilation alone","Low inspired O2 (high altitude)","Decreased cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'V/Q mismatch', 'In V/Q mismatch, extra oxygen can reach poorly ventilated alveoli, so PaO2 rises dramatically on 100% O2 (often >500 mmHg). True right-to-left shunt is refractory to oxygen, and pure hypoventilation or low FiO2 leaves the A-a gradient normal.'),
('f8e4958e-b2d2-5bb3-8b27-d6f8c872fc55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The physiological shunt equation (Qs/Qt) is:', '["Qs/Qt = (CcO2 − CaO2) / (CcO2 − CvO2); normal < 5%","Qs/Qt = (CaO2 − CvO2) / (CcO2 − CaO2)","Qs/Qt = CaO2 / (CcO2 − CvO2)","Qs/Qt = (CcO2 − CvO2) / (CcO2 − CaO2)","Qs/Qt = (CvO2 − CaO2) / CcO2"]'::jsonb, 'Qs/Qt = (CcO2 − CaO2) / (CcO2 − CvO2); normal < 5%', 'The shunt fraction compares the deficit between ideal capillary (CcO2) and actual arterial (CaO2) oxygen content to the total mixed-venous gradient. It is normally below 5% of cardiac output, and rises with right-to-left shunting.'),
('5691b4ed-64f8-5b8e-a233-6ae9b0c4ae2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which statement correctly describes the West zones of pulmonary blood flow?', '["Only zone 1 exists in the normal lung resulting in decreased functional activity","Zone 1: PA > Pa > Pv (apex, minimal perfusion); Zone 2: Pa > PA > Pv; Zone 3: Pa > Pv > PA; Zone 4: interstitial pressure exceeds alveolar","All zones have equal blood flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Blood flow is equal at apex and base without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Zone 1 receives the greatest perfusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Zone 1: PA > Pa > Pv (apex, minimal perfusion); Zone 2: Pa > PA > Pv; Zone 3: Pa > Pv > PA; Zone 4: interstitial pressure exceeds alveolar', 'In West''s model, alveolar pressure (PA), arterial (Pa), and venous (Pv) pressures determine regional flow. Zone 3 at the base has continuous flow (Pa > Pv > PA), which is why perfusion is greatest there; zone 1 normally occurs only with very low pressures, e.g., on ventilator PEEP.'),
('4b9d80d0-5561-5570-830b-c73c4c818998'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Positive pressure ventilation (e.g., with PEEP) can cause which hemodynamic effect?', '["Increased venous return","Decreased left ventricular afterload","Reduced cardiac output due to decreased venous return","Increased pulmonary perfusion","Increased cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced cardiac output due to decreased venous return', 'Positive intrathoracic pressure compresses the great veins, reducing venous return and therefore cardiac output, particularly in hypovolemic patients. It also compresses zone-1 alveoli, increasing alveolar dead space.'),
('618675ac-458a-5ce4-9cdc-1b72802a2966'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The physiological benefit of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is that it:', '["Causes bronchoconstriction resulting in decreased functional activity","Has no significant effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vasodilates pulmonary vessels mediated by specific receptor activation","Redirects blood from hypoxic alveoli to better-ventilated areas, optimizing V/Q matching","Shunts blood away from the right heart leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Redirects blood from hypoxic alveoli to better-ventilated areas, optimizing V/Q matching', 'By constricting vessels in poorly ventilated (hypoxic) regions, HPV diverts perfusion toward better-ventilated alveoli, minimizing venous admixture. When generalized and chronic, however, it produces pulmonary hypertension and right-heart strain.'),
('5a670c91-b163-5811-9f2c-126bf59328dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Gas exchange across the alveolocapillary membrane occurs by:', '["Facilitated diffusion resulting in decreased functional activity","Bulk flow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple diffusion (passive, down the partial pressure gradient)","Active transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pinocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Simple diffusion (passive, down the partial pressure gradient)', 'O2 and CO2 cross the respiratory membrane passively by simple diffusion along their partial-pressure gradients; no energy is consumed. Their rates follow Fick''s law and are normally perfusion-limited rather than diffusion-limited.'),
('1e02b3a2-6475-521e-a0c4-e92f6df55de8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Fick''s law of diffusion states that the rate of gas diffusion is:', '["Proportional to membrane thickness","Proportional to molecular weight","Inversely proportional to surface area","Proportional to (Area × Solubility × ΔP) / (Thickness × √MW)","Inversely proportional to the pressure difference"]'::jsonb, 'Proportional to (Area × Solubility × ΔP) / (Thickness × √MW)', 'Per Fick''s law, diffusion rate is directly proportional to surface area, gas solubility, and the partial pressure difference, and inversely proportional to membrane thickness and the square root of molecular weight. CO2 diffuses about 20 times faster than O2.'),
('6ceb5ef7-9956-5d25-ba25-37510e59c9b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The diffusing capacity of the lung measured with CO (DLCO):', '["Measures gas transfer from alveoli to blood; CO is used because its uptake is diffusion-limited, not perfusion-limited","Measures helium concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measures CO2 production mediated by specific receptor activation","Measures O2 consumption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measures N2 washout leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measures gas transfer from alveoli to blood; CO is used because its uptake is diffusion-limited, not perfusion-limited', 'Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin so avidly that its pulmonary uptake is limited by diffusion across the membrane and into the red cells. DLCO therefore measures the effective surface area and thickness of the alveolar-capillary membrane.'),
('84f09ee4-532a-5140-817f-4308b1780afc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The membrane component of diffusing capacity (Dm) can be separated from:', '["Capillary blood volume (Vc) by measuring DLCO at varying oxygen tensions","DLCO alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary capillary length mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the membrane resistance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Alveolar surface area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Capillary blood volume (Vc) by measuring DLCO at varying oxygen tensions', 'Because CO competes with oxygen for hemoglobin, measuring DLCO at different O2 tensions separates the two components: the membrane diffusing capacity (Dm) and the capillary blood volume (Vc). This is the classic Roughton-Forster approach.'),
('dc9484ae-1408-53d8-9e56-b031d3cd4b08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In arterial blood, oxygen is transported mostly as:', '["97% bound to hemoglobin (oxyhemoglobin)","Entirely dissolved in plasma","50% bound to Hb and 50% dissolved","25% bound and 75% dissolved","70% bound to Hb and 30% dissolved"]'::jsonb, '97% bound to hemoglobin (oxyhemoglobin)', 'Only about 3% of arterial oxygen is physically dissolved; the remaining 97% is carried bound to hemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin. This is why severe anemia can reduce oxygen content markedly even when PaO2 is normal.'),
('3ff89586-5a78-5df5-9cf1-1a1aa41b3c9e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A rightward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve (e.g., from increased 2,3-BPG, H+, temperature, or CO2):', '["Promotes O2 loading in the lungs; caused by ↓pH","Promotes O2 unloading to the tissues; caused by ↑H+ (Bohr effect)","Decreases O2 delivery by left-shifting the curve","Increases O2 affinity and reduces unloading","Has no effect on O2 delivery"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes O2 unloading to the tissues; caused by ↑H+ (Bohr effect)', 'A right shift lowers hemoglobin''s O2 affinity (higher P50), so oxygen is released to the tissues more easily, particularly in the acidic, hypercapnic, warm exercising muscle (Bohr effect). It aids tissue oxygenation at the expense of slightly less lung loading.'),
('209a5fda-f352-5fdd-a369-77767607344c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The Haldane effect refers to the fact that:', '["Deoxygenated Hb binds more CO2 and H+ than oxygenated Hb, facilitating CO2 transport","Oxygenated Hb binds more CO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Deoxygenated Hb binds less CO2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CO affects Hb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only O2 transport is affected leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated Hb binds more CO2 and H+ than oxygenated Hb, facilitating CO2 transport', 'Reduced (deoxygenated) hemoglobin binds CO2 and H+ more avidly than oxyhemoglobin. This lets the tissues load CO2 and buffer H+, while in the lungs oxygenation releases both, promoting CO2 excretion.'),
('bd18cb40-c85d-5814-bdbd-3972017101ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which statement about the forms of CO2 transport in blood is correct?', '["Almost all CO2 is carried as carbaminohemoglobin resulting in decreased functional activity","CO2 is carried entirely dissolved in plasma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 7-10% of CO2 is transported dissolved in plasma, with the remainder bound to Hb or carried as bicarbonate","CO2 is carried only as carbonic acid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO2 is carried only as bicarbonate within the RBC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 7-10% of CO2 is transported dissolved in plasma, with the remainder bound to Hb or carried as bicarbonate', 'CO2 is carried in three forms: roughly 70% as bicarbonate (formed in the RBC and chloride-shifted into plasma), about 23% as carbamino compounds (mainly on hemoglobin), and about 7-10% dissolved. No single form accounts for all of transport.'),
('a7467cb9-55e0-524c-9605-c7de6317a3a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the chloride shift and where does it occur?', '["Occurs only in the stomach resulting in decreased functional activity","Occurs only in the kidneys accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occurs only at the alveolar membrane mediated by specific receptor activation","HCO3− leaves the RBC in exchange for Cl− to maintain electroneutrality; occurs in systemic capillaries","Occurs only in the lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'HCO3− leaves the RBC in exchange for Cl− to maintain electroneutrality; occurs in systemic capillaries', 'As CO2 enters red cells in the systemic capillaries, carbonic anhydrase generates HCO3−, which exits via the band-3 (AE1) exchanger in exchange for chloride to keep the cell electrically neutral — the chloride (Hamburger) shift. The reverse occurs in the lungs.'),
('c021da5b-69d0-5f40-ae12-d7f37cf7d6e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Carbonic anhydrase is located:', '["In the airways resulting in decreased functional activity","In the alveoli accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","In RBCs (and various tissues), catalyzing CO2 + H2O ↔ H2CO3","In the interstitial fluid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In RBCs (and various tissues), catalyzing CO2 + H2O ↔ H2CO3', 'Carbonic anhydrase is concentrated in red blood cells and also present in capillary endothelium, renal tubules, and gastric mucosa. It catalyzes the rapid hydration of CO2 to carbonic acid, essential for CO2 transport and acid-base regulation.'),
('138a847d-be1d-55f5-b9c5-16bc10acc68d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The bicarbonate/CO2 buffer system:', '["Is important only in the kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Is the most important extracellular buffer; pH = 6.1 + log([HCO3−]/(0.03 × PaCO2))","Acts only intracellularly mediated by specific receptor activation","Does not buffer plasma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acts only within the RBC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is the most important extracellular buffer; pH = 6.1 + log([HCO3−]/(0.03 × PaCO2))', 'The HCO3−/CO2 pair is the dominant buffer of the extracellular fluid because its two components (HCO3− and the volatile CO2) can be regulated by the kidneys and lungs. Its pH is given by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation with pKa 6.1.'),
('157c9afe-3a01-5fd0-b35b-de12ad739c4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The sequence of primary change and compensation in respiratory alkalosis is:', '["↑H+ resulting in decreased functional activity","↓pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Respiratory acidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","↓PaCO2 (hyperventilation) → ↑pH → compensation by ↓HCO3− (renal)","Metabolic acidosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↓PaCO2 (hyperventilation) → ↑pH → compensation by ↓HCO3− (renal)', 'Respiratory alkalosis begins with hyperventilation, which lowers PaCO2 and raises pH. The kidney compensates by excreting bicarbonate, lowering serum HCO3− over days. Causes include hypoxia, anxiety, and stimulation of the respiratory centers.'),
('1099dff6-3275-54a3-8c1a-7cfa2367f6c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Respiratory acidosis is characterized by:', '["↓PaCO2 (hyperventilation) resulting in decreased functional activity","Metabolic acidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Respiratory alkalosis mediated by specific receptor activation","No change in pH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↑PaCO2 (hypoventilation) → ↓pH → compensation by ↑HCO3− (renal)"]'::jsonb, '↑PaCO2 (hypoventilation) → ↓pH → compensation by ↑HCO3− (renal)', 'Respiratory acidosis results from CO2 retention when ventilation is inadequate, lowering pH. The kidneys compensate by retaining bicarbonate, which only partially corrects the acidosis. It is seen in COPD, sedative overdose, and neuromuscular weakness.'),
('4aeef8bd-031d-5ceb-9e79-ad63f00692cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 60-year-old with uncontrolled diabetes has ABG: pH 7.25, pCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3− 12 mEq/L. The most likely interpretation is:', '["Metabolic alkalosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Respiratory acidosis without compensation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↑HCO3− with alkalemia mediated by specific receptor activation","↑pH from hyperventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓HCO3− → ↓pH (metabolic acidosis) with respiratory compensation (↓PaCO2, Kussmaul breathing)"]'::jsonb, '↓HCO3− → ↓pH (metabolic acidosis) with respiratory compensation (↓PaCO2, Kussmaul breathing)', 'The low bicarbonate and low pH indicate metabolic (keto) acidosis, and the reduced PaCO2 represents appropriate respiratory compensation, as in the deep Kussmaul breathing of diabetic ketoacidosis. Winters'' formula can confirm the expected PaCO2.'),
('87bedda0-cb6e-53be-99a3-26511313b411'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Winters'' formula is used to:', '["Assess metabolic alkalosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Assess respiratory acidosis alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Assess respiratory alkalosis alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Predict the pH in metabolic acidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Estimate the expected PaCO2 in metabolic acidosis: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × [HCO3−]) + 8 ± 2"]'::jsonb, 'Estimate the expected PaCO2 in metabolic acidosis: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × [HCO3−]) + 8 ± 2', 'Winters'' formula predicts the appropriate respiratory (PaCO2) compensation for a metabolic acidosis, allowing one to detect mixed acid-base disorders when the measured PaCO2 deviates from this expected value.'),
('7d035519-5c87-52ca-8580-aeaec299cae0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Metabolic alkalosis is characterized by:', '["Respiratory alkalosis resulting in decreased functional activity","↑HCO3− → ↑pH → compensation by ↑PaCO2 (hypoventilation)","↓pH mediated by specific receptor activation","No compensation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓HCO3− leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↑HCO3− → ↑pH → compensation by ↑PaCO2 (hypoventilation)', 'Metabolic alkalosis starts with bicarbonate retention or acid loss, raising pH. The ventilatory response is hypoventilation, which raises PaCO2 and blunts the alkalemia. Causes include vomiting, diuretics, and hyperaldosteronism.'),
('7851bb71-c9db-5fb2-86b7-33dac2fd508c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the anion gap and how is it calculated?', '["Na+ − (Cl− + HCO3−); normal 8-12 mEq/L","Na+ + K+ + Cl− accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+ − K+ mediated by specific receptor activation","Na+ + Cl− + HCO3− without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cl− + HCO3− leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ − (Cl− + HCO3−); normal 8-12 mEq/L', 'The anion gap is the difference between the main measured cation (Na+) and the main measured anions (Cl− + HCO3−), normally 8-12 mEq/L. An elevated gap points to unmeasured anions such as ketones, lactate, or uremic acids in metabolic acidosis.'),
('827ba318-1cd9-54af-a776-38e72055d3d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which hemodynamic parameter reflects left ventricular filling pressure and is estimated by pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP)?', '["Left atrial pressure (normal 8-12 mmHg)","Pulmonary artery pressure","Mean arterial pressure","Central venous pressure","Right atrial pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Left atrial pressure (normal 8-12 mmHg)', 'With the balloon wedged and the downstream static column, PCWP reflects left atrial pressure, normally about 8-12 mmHg, and estimates left ventricular end-diastolic (filling) pressure. It helps distinguish cardiogenic from non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema.'),
('5341608c-db88-5478-9b19-0a6ea896afd4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The bronchial circulation:', '["Is part of the systemic circulation (1-2% of cardiac output) supplying the conducting airways","Is part of the pulmonary circulation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Supplies the alveoli directly mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a venous network draining into the vena cava without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supplies only the visceral pleura leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is part of the systemic circulation (1-2% of cardiac output) supplying the conducting airways', 'The bronchial arteries arise from the systemic circulation and supply the conducting airways, walls of the vessels, and pleura, carrying about 1-2% of cardiac output. Their deoxygenated drainage contributes slightly to the normal physiologic shunt.'),
('93087d21-a16e-5827-8cd9-e671a884a66f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the principal role of the pulmonary lymphatics?', '["Transport gases between alveoli and blood resulting in decreased functional activity","Drain fluid from the interstitium toward hilar lymph nodes, helping prevent pulmonary edema","Supply oxygen to bronchial walls mediated by specific receptor activation","Maintain alveolar surface tension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Provide blood to pulmonary capillaries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Drain fluid from the interstitium toward hilar lymph nodes, helping prevent pulmonary edema', 'Pulmonary lymphatics remove excess interstitial fluid, returning it toward the hilar lymph nodes. This drainage is a major safety factor that prevents pulmonary edema when capillary filtration rises, and it pumps vigorously during exercise.'),
('e53f8a8c-2715-5c55-bcd3-27b5f79c7254'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Pulmonary edema caused by increased alveolar-capillary permeability, as in sepsis, presents as:', '["ARDS (exudative edema, high protein, normal PCWP)","Pulmonary edema from left heart failure","Pneumonia only","Aspiration only","Transudative edema with raised PCWP"]'::jsonb, 'ARDS (exudative edema, high protein, normal PCWP)', 'ARDS is a high-permeability (exudative) pulmonary edema in which protein-rich fluid leaks into the alveoli despite a normal wedge pressure. This distinguishes it from cardiogenic edema, which is transudative and driven by an elevated left atrial pressure.'),
('2ca96f9b-4e76-50d1-a817-9dd5886b664d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which of the following is a major safety factor that prevents pulmonary edema?', '["Pulmonary lymphatic drainage","Low plasma oncotic pressure","Increased capillary permeability","High left atrial pressure","Edema fluid in the alveoli"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary lymphatic drainage', 'The pulmonary lymphatics actively pump excess interstitial fluid away and are the principal mechanism that keeps the alveoli dry. Low oncotic pressure, high capillary permeability, and raised left atrial pressure all promote edema rather than prevent it.'),
('707dd509-374c-5c07-b837-1b6c3ee74e0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the primary function of pulmonary surfactant?', '["Prevents alveolar collapse by lowering surface tension, especially at low lung volumes","Increases surface tension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Promotes alveolar fluid secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreases lung compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Raises surface tension during expiration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents alveolar collapse by lowering surface tension, especially at low lung volumes', 'Surfactant lowers the surface tension of the alveolar fluid lining, with a disproportionately large effect on small alveoli (Laplace''s law, P = 2T/r). This stabilizes alveolar size, increases compliance, and prevents atelectasis.'),
('b4cfc8fe-493d-568d-9d0b-19a0c785c08e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Surfactant deficiency in a premature infant leads to:', '["Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), with alveolar collapse and hyaline membranes","Emphysema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary embolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pleural fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), with alveolar collapse and hyaline membranes', 'In preterm infants, surfactant deficiency causes progressive atelectasis, exudation of protein (hyaline membranes), stiff lungs, and severe hypoxemia — the respiratory distress syndrome. Antenatal steroids and postnatal surfactant replacement reduce the risk.'),
('d58d4c30-6246-54ae-9d20-d7f57aa8d56d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which therapy is given antenatally to accelerate fetal lung surfactant production in threatened preterm delivery?', '["Thyroid hormone resulting in decreased functional activity","Prophylactic antibiotics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Antenatal corticosteroids (e.g., betamethasone)","Prostaglandin inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Catecholamines leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Antenatal corticosteroids (e.g., betamethasone)', 'Corticosteroids such as betamethasone or dexamethasone, given to the mother before preterm delivery, induce maturation of type II pneumocytes and surfactant synthesis. This lowers the incidence and severity of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome.'),
('42feda0f-8408-57bc-8d05-5ded39012e9e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex is characterized by:', '["Stimulation of deep inspiration resulting in decreased functional activity","Effects limited to expiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibition of inspiration by airway stretch receptors when tidal volume is large; weak in adults but important in neonates","Bronchospasm on inflation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence in all mammals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of inspiration by airway stretch receptors when tidal volume is large; weak in adults but important in neonates', 'Slowly adapting stretch receptors in the airways discharge as the lungs inflate and inhibit the inspiratory centers, terminating inspiration and prolonging expiration (the Hering-Breuer reflex). It is weak in adults but helps limit tidal volume, especially in newborns.'),
('d4962a34-b89b-59bc-9b55-0e854a59b9d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'J (juxtacapillary) receptors are located and stimulated as follows:', '["In the nasal mucosa; respond to inhaled irritants resulting in decreased functional activity","Respond only to airway stretch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Near pulmonary capillaries; respond to ↑interstitial fluid (congestion, edema, inflammation) → rapid shallow breathing and dyspnea","Respond to arterial pH changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the medulla; respond to CSF pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Near pulmonary capillaries; respond to ↑interstitial fluid (congestion, edema, inflammation) → rapid shallow breathing and dyspnea', 'J receptors lie in the alveolar interstitium close to the pulmonary capillaries and are activated by interstitial congestion, edema, and inflammation. Their stimulation produces rapid shallow breathing, dyspnea, and cough in conditions such as pulmonary edema and pneumonia.'),
('595878a8-a370-59da-b2b0-8e4b4afb9241'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which reflex is mediated by receptors in the nasal mucosa carried by the trigeminal nerve?', '["Cough (laryngeal receptors) resulting in decreased functional activity","Sneeze (nasal receptors via the trigeminal nerve → forceful expiration through the nose)","Hiccups (phrenic nerve) mediated by specific receptor activation","Yawning without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gag reflex (glossopharyngeal nerve) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sneeze (nasal receptors via the trigeminal nerve → forceful expiration through the nose)', 'The sneeze reflex begins with irritation of nasal mucosal receptors whose afferents travel in the trigeminal nerve (V1/V2). Its efferent limb produces a strong, coordinated expiration that expels air and irritants through the nose.'),
('c5aa5a50-d311-5ade-bac3-c862aa4539c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which component constitutes the largest fraction of the work of breathing at rest?', '["Resistive work (28%, overcoming airway resistance)","Inertial work (7%, accelerating gas)","Elastic work (65%, expanding the lungs and chest wall)","Frictional work of the abdominal contents","Resistive work of the chest wall"]'::jsonb, 'Elastic work (65%, expanding the lungs and chest wall)', 'At rest, most of the work is elastic — stretching the lungs and chest wall against their recoil — accounting for roughly two-thirds of total work. Resistive (airway) work is about 28% and inertial work only about 7%; their proportions change with breathing pattern.'),
('6eff6f6e-497c-5771-8ae4-3dde9ba422e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The minimal total work of breathing occurs at:', '["A normal respiratory rate (12-16/min) with a normal tidal volume (500 mL) — the optimal balance between elastic and resistive work","All breathing patterns equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A very high rate with large tidal volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Very rapid shallow breathing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very slow deep breathing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A normal respiratory rate (12-16/min) with a normal tidal volume (500 mL) — the optimal balance between elastic and resistive work', 'Fast shallow breathing minimizes elastic work but increases resistive work, whereas slow deep breaths do the reverse. The body selects the rate that minimizes total work, normally about 12-16 breaths/min with a tidal volume near 500 mL.'),
('6e9869e0-1f52-5870-90fb-906bd920c15a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The static pressure-volume curve of the lung shows:', '["Only a deflation curve resulting in decreased functional activity","A linear relationship over all volumes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hysteresis: inflation and deflation follow different curves, and compliance is lower at very low and very high lung volumes","Compliance that increases steadily with volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Identical inflation and deflation pressures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hysteresis: inflation and deflation follow different curves, and compliance is lower at very low and very high lung volumes', 'Because surface tension and tissue forces differ during inflation versus deflation, the P-V curve shows hysteresis, with deflation resting above inflation. Compliance is greatest mid-range and falls at both very low and very high volumes (the sigmoid shape).'),
('2a736359-7492-547c-bab1-576e7a60b8ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Regional distribution of ventilation in the upright lung:', '["Is equal in all zones resulting in decreased functional activity","Is greatest in dependent zones (bases) due to the pleural pressure gradient","Occurs only in the apex mediated by specific receptor activation","Is independent of gravity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is greater at the apex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is greatest in dependent zones (bases) due to the pleural pressure gradient', 'Gravity makes the intrapleural pressure less negative toward the bases, so the dependent lung starts inhalation from a more compressed state and undergoes a larger volume change. Ventilation is therefore greatest at the bases in the upright position.'),
('d95d825c-cf78-5f4a-b203-64f813ca0432'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The intrapleural pressure at rest (FRC) is:', '["−5 cmH2O (negative), due to opposing recoil of the lung and chest wall","−10 cmH2O accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","0 cmH2O mediated by specific receptor activation","+10 cmH2O without affecting the primary tissue pathway","+5 cmH2O leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '−5 cmH2O (negative), due to opposing recoil of the lung and chest wall', 'The inward elastic recoil of the lung and the outward recoil of the chest wall pull in opposite directions, creating a subatmospheric intrapleural pressure of about −5 cmH2O at FRC. This negative pressure keeps the lung expanded.'),
('c8709ffc-859d-5611-8b40-fbda56fd85a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The equal pressure point (EPP) is the:', '["Point along the airway where intraluminal pressure equals pleural pressure; downstream, airways are compressed","Point that exists only during inspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Point that exists only during relaxed breathing mediated by specific receptor activation","Point that exists only in the bronchi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Point never reached in normal subjects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Point along the airway where intraluminal pressure equals pleural pressure; downstream, airways are compressed', 'During forced expiration, upstream airway pressure falls until it equals pleural pressure at the equal pressure point. Beyond this point the airways are dynamically compressed, which is why maximum expiratory flow cannot be increased by greater effort.'),
('1a492dad-9f67-5336-91f7-7db5970a39b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Maximum expiratory airflow is limited by:', '["Only lung volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscle strength accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Effort-independent mechanisms (lung elastic recoil and airway resistance set the maximal achievable flow)","Only the heart''s output without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only effort leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Effort-independent mechanisms (lung elastic recoil and airway resistance set the maximal achievable flow)', 'During forced expiration, flow becomes effort-independent once dynamic airway compression occurs, so maximal flow is set by lung elastic recoil and airway resistance rather than by further effort. This underlies the airflow limitation of obstructive disease.'),
('6c9f787a-d8db-5bb6-bf2e-4574912cfc5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which factor is the primary determinant of maximum expiratory flow, such that its loss in emphysema lowers flow?', '["Lung elastic recoil","Arterial oxygen saturation","Effort alone (since maximal flow is effort-dependent)","Respiratory rate","Blood hemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Lung elastic recoil', 'Maximum expiratory flow depends on the pressure driving expiration (elastic recoil) and airway resistance. In emphysema, destroyed elastic tissue reduces recoil, so the driving pressure falls and maximal flow declines despite increased lung compliance.'),
('caf1819e-f06a-5ddf-811b-f15741f3c8eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Isovolume pressure-flow curves demonstrate that, at a fixed lung volume, expiratory flow:', '["Increases continuously in proportion to effort","Rises to a maximum and then plateaus (effort-independent flow)","Always decreases with effort","Is the same at all lung volumes","Is turbulent at every volume"]'::jsonb, 'Rises to a maximum and then plateaus (effort-independent flow)', 'Plotting flow against pleural pressure at a constant lung volume shows flow rising with effort only up to a plateau; beyond that, further effort does not increase flow. The plateau reflects dynamic airway compression, and its height falls as lung volume decreases.'),
('235bf76b-f37d-5df5-ac7a-98a9f9dedb9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In obstructive lung disease, the flow-volume loop characteristically shows:', '["A concave (scooped-out) expiratory limb with a reduced peak flow","A convex expiratory limb with elevated peak flow","A normal loop with preserved peak flow","A square-shaped expiratory limb","A linear expiratory curve"]'::jsonb, 'A concave (scooped-out) expiratory limb with a reduced peak flow', 'Obstructive disease reduces maximum expiratory flow at mid and low lung volumes, producing a concave ''scooped-out'' expiratory limb with a low peak flow. The loop also reveals air trapping, with a large residual volume at the end of the curve.'),
('09983049-f595-5456-b13e-4b8a2351af91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'On spirometry, restrictive lung disease characteristically shows:', '["Reduced vital capacity with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio (peak flow preserved relative to lung volume)","A reduced FEV1/FVC ratio below 0.7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased total lung capacity mediated by specific receptor activation","A concave expiratory loop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Air trapping with increased RV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced vital capacity with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio (peak flow preserved relative to lung volume)', 'Restriction shrinks all lung volumes, lowering FVC and TLC, while the FEV1/FVC ratio stays normal or even rises because emptying is not disproportionately slow. This distinguishes it from obstruction, where the ratio falls.'),
('3189c41e-be44-5c0a-a1af-42c7274c963d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Normal ventilation-perfusion matching in the lung depends on:', '["Uniform ventilation and uniform perfusion everywhere resulting in decreased functional activity","Local regulation, including intact hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) that diverts blood from poorly ventilated regions","Absence of any regional gradient mediated by specific receptor activation","Constriction of the alveolar ducts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Systemic bronchial vasodilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Local regulation, including intact hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) that diverts blood from poorly ventilated regions', 'Because regional ventilation is not uniform, matching depends on local control; HPV constricts vessels feeding poorly ventilated (hypoxic) alveoli and redirects flow to better-ventilated ones. Without this, venous admixture and hypoxemia would be far worse.'),
('96954fd7-ed1b-5fe0-a650-172fb0470aed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which cause of hypoxemia characteristically produces a high PaCO2 with a normal A-a gradient?', '["V/Q mismatch","Low inspired FiO2 (high altitude)","Alveolar hypoventilation","Right-to-left shunt","Diffusion impairment"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar hypoventilation', 'Pure alveolar hypoventilation lowers PAO2 and raises PaCO2 proportionately, but because gas exchange is intact the A-a gradient remains normal. All other mechanisms of hypoxemia (shunt, V/Q mismatch, diffusion limitation) widen the A-a gradient.'),
('58cdb7d3-53cc-5d81-b1c6-c4a23b6a569c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The response to breathing 100% oxygen helps distinguish:', '["Shunt from V/Q mismatch (in pure shunt, PaO2 rises little; in V/Q mismatch, PaO2 rises toward >500 mmHg)","All diagnoses equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoventilation from low inspired O2 only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the shunt fraction quantitatively without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nothing clinically useful leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt from V/Q mismatch (in pure shunt, PaO2 rises little; in V/Q mismatch, PaO2 rises toward >500 mmHg)', 'Blood that perfuses unventilated alveoli (shunt) never contacts supplemental oxygen, so PaO2 rises only slightly on 100% O2 (usually <200 mmHg). In V/Q mismatch and diffusion impairment, extra oxygen reaches the affected units and PaO2 climbs well above 500 mmHg.'),
('c5b20c64-f4e5-5280-9d2b-d251f845dcfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Venous admixture from low V/Q units produces hypoxemia that:', '["Is partially correctable with 100% O2 (unlike a true shunt)","Is never correctable with oxygen","Is identical to a true anatomical shunt","Corrects only with hyperbaric oxygen","Is unrelated to V/Q mismatch"]'::jsonb, 'Is partially correctable with 100% O2 (unlike a true shunt)', 'Low V/Q units are ventilated, just poorly, so breathing 100% O2 raises their alveolar PO2 and largely corrects the resulting hypoxemia. A true shunt, by contrast, bypasses ventilation entirely and remains hypoxemic despite 100% O2.'),
('772b57ed-56ce-5ca2-83d2-a730a8a59ef4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The ventilatory response to CO2 is characterized by:', '["↑PaCO2 → ↑minute ventilation, mediated mainly by central chemoreceptors (sensitivity ≈ 2-3 L/min per mmHg rise)","No ventilatory response to CO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a peripheral hypoxic response mediated by specific receptor activation","Identical to the hypoxic response without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓ventilation with rising PaCO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↑PaCO2 → ↑minute ventilation, mediated mainly by central chemoreceptors (sensitivity ≈ 2-3 L/min per mmHg rise)', 'Rising arterial CO2 strongly stimulates ventilation, chiefly through central chemoreceptors sensing CSF H+, with a normal gain of about 2-3 L/min per mmHg increase in PaCO2. This response is blunted in chronic CO2 retainers.'),
('0f466466-697c-5a46-ba8e-8508072969f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The ventilatory response to hypoxia is:', '["Mediated by peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies) and becomes significant mainly when PaO2 < 60 mmHg","Mediated by central chemoreceptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Largely mediated by the aortic bodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Most marked when PaO2 is above 100 mmHg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unimportant even in severe hypoxemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mediated by peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies) and becomes significant mainly when PaO2 < 60 mmHg', 'Hypoxia is sensed chiefly by the carotid bodies, which raise ventilation through the glossopharyngeal nerve. Because the response accelerates only below about 60 mmHg, it is a backup mechanism compared with the brisk CO2 drive, but it becomes critical in chronic CO2 retainers.'),
('546a717f-5e56-5ab1-9e06-c93ca8cacaa7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The interaction between the hypoxic and hypercapnic ventilatory responses is:', '["Independent resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Synergistic (hypoxia and hypercapnia potentiate each other, so combined stimuli produce a greater response than the sum)","Antagonistic mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Additive only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Without any interaction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synergistic (hypoxia and hypercapnia potentiate each other, so combined stimuli produce a greater response than the sum)', 'Hypoxia and hypercapnia multiply each other''s ventilatory effects rather than merely adding. This synergy is why a patient with both hypoxemia and CO2 retention can develop a very large ventilatory drive.'),
('d103c54e-53c5-531f-8b4c-beb8fca0d2f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The ventilatory response to progressive exercise is:', '["Present only at the start of exercise resulting in decreased functional activity","Present only at maximal effort accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A linear increase in VE with VCO2/VO2; at high workloads VE rises disproportionately above the VCO2 inflection (lactate threshold)","Constant throughout without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fall in ventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A linear increase in VE with VCO2/VO2; at high workloads VE rises disproportionately above the VCO2 inflection (lactate threshold)', 'During mild-moderate exercise, ventilation rises linearly in step with oxygen consumption and CO2 production. Above the lactate threshold, bicarbonate buffering of lactic acid adds extra CO2, so ventilation increases more steeply relative to VO2.'),
('759bb7e8-a68b-5473-9eab-aa3d6e2536a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the lactate threshold and why does the VCO2/VO2 ratio increase at this point?', '["It occurs only at rest resulting in decreased functional activity","It coincides with maximum heart rate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is unrelated to blood lactate mediated by specific receptor activation","The point during exercise when lactate production exceeds clearance; bicarbonate buffering increases VCO2 and raises the VCO2/VO2 ratio","It reflects only muscle mass leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The point during exercise when lactate production exceeds clearance; bicarbonate buffering increases VCO2 and raises the VCO2/VO2 ratio', 'At the lactate (anaerobic) threshold, lactate production outstrips clearance and the accumulating H+ is buffered by bicarbonate, generating extra CO2. This extra CO2 raises VCO2 disproportionately, so the respiratory exchange ratio climbs above 1.0.'),
('d1e60779-7b40-5b06-8e69-3f4404f2c77e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which parameter is the standard measure of aerobic (cardiorespiratory) capacity during progressive exercise testing?', '["Heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VO2 (oxygen consumption, e.g., VO2 max)","VCO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minute ventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 (oxygen consumption, e.g., VO2 max)', 'Oxygen consumption rises linearly with work rate and plateaus at VO2 max, the accepted index of aerobic capacity. It integrates cardiac output, hemoglobin, and muscle oxygen extraction during exercise.'),
('8d909c21-fd68-54f4-b791-4343e004b30c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The VE/VCO2 slope during exercise:', '["Is raised in pulmonary hypertension (increased dead space ventilation) resulting in decreased functional activity","Is raised in pulmonary embolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is a measure of ventilatory efficiency (normal <30) and is elevated in heart failure","Is measured only at rest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Has no clinical use leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is a measure of ventilatory efficiency (normal <30) and is elevated in heart failure', 'The VE/VCO2 slope quantifies how much ventilation is needed to clear each liter of CO2; a steeper slope indicates wasted (dead-space) ventilation. A normal value is below about 30, and it rises in heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, and PE, aiding prognosis.'),
('81271edf-cb14-52f7-af2f-edc3f9606c5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The oxygen pulse (VO2/HR) is best described as:', '["A ratio used only at rest resulting in decreased functional activity","A measure of ventilatory efficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A surrogate for stroke volume during exercise; a ↓O2 pulse suggests cardiac limitation (reduced SV)","A measure of muscle strength without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A measure of diffusion capacity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A surrogate for stroke volume during exercise; a ↓O2 pulse suggests cardiac limitation (reduced SV)', 'Because VO2 = stroke volume × heart rate × arteriovenous O2 difference, the oxygen pulse (VO2/HR) approximates stroke volume times oxygen extraction. A flattening or fall in the O2 pulse during exercise suggests an inadequate stroke-volume response, as in heart failure.'),
('921eb659-9684-529f-8db1-1e7774c27591'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal systolic pulmonary artery pressure is approximately:', '["120/80 mmHg (systemic values)","90 mmHg","50 mmHg","25 mmHg","10 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '25 mmHg', 'The normal pulmonary artery systolic pressure is about 25 mmHg (diastolic 8 mmHg, mean 15 mmHg), only about one-fifth of the systemic pressure. Pulmonary hypertension is diagnosed when the mean pressure exceeds 20-25 mmHg at rest.'),
('fb20d01f-b8fc-5107-ac64-fcf1edd35262'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, a key Starling force in the lung, is approximately:', '["8-10 mmHg","75 mmHg (systemic capillary pressure)","120 mmHg (systemic arterial)","0 mmHg (no hydrostatic gradient)","50 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '8-10 mmHg', 'The pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure is only about 8-10 mmHg because pulmonary vascular resistance and pressures are low. This low pressure, together with plasma oncotic pressure (25 mmHg) and lymphatic drainage, keeps the alveoli normally dry.'),
('8ac198af-9898-59d5-8582-248fd87c6d28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) is calculated as:', '["CO only resulting in decreased functional activity","BP only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PA pressure only mediated by specific receptor activation","PCWP − RAP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","(PAP − PCWP) / CO; normal ≈100-250 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵"]'::jsonb, '(PAP − PCWP) / CO; normal ≈100-250 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵', 'PVR equals the pressure drop across the pulmonary circulation (mean PAP minus left atrial/PCWP pressure) divided by cardiac output. The normal value is about 100-250 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵, far lower than systemic vascular resistance.'),
('6d32a82b-22c7-5d9b-92b8-98909e80d7bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In a patient with pulmonary hypertension, a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) of ≤15 mmHg indicates:', '["Pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension (normal PCWP, from pulmonary vasoconstriction and arterial remodeling)","Post-capillary pulmonary hypertension (elevated PCWP) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low pulmonary vascular resistance mediated by specific receptor activation","Aortic stenosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mitral stenosis with raised left atrial pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension (normal PCWP, from pulmonary vasoconstriction and arterial remodeling)', 'A normal PCWP with elevated pulmonary artery pressure localizes the resistance to the precapillary pulmonary vessels (WHO group 1 PAH, or hypoxic, or chronic thromboembolic). Post-capillary causes such as left heart failure or mitral stenosis raise the PCWP instead.'),
('85be7f1c-e4dc-51cf-bba7-ba249ce94646'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Pulmonary hypertension secondary to left heart disease (e.g., left ventricular failure or mitral stenosis) is classified as:', '["Chronic thromboembolic disease","Idiopathic pulmonary hypertension","Hypoxic (COPD-related) disease","Post-capillary pulmonary hypertension (elevated PCWP)","Exercise-induced"]'::jsonb, 'Post-capillary pulmonary hypertension (elevated PCWP)', 'Left heart disease raises the left atrial pressure, which is transmitted backward into the pulmonary veins and capillaries, producing post-capillary pulmonary hypertension with an elevated PCWP. Treating the left-heart cause is the primary therapy.'),
('464ee5b7-b688-5299-9046-4575674612ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A 45-year-old who recently traveled to high altitude develops headache, dyspnea, and fatigue with crackles on auscultation. The most likely mechanism of pulmonary edema is:', '["Leakage from left heart failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Cardiogenic edema with high PCWP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction raising pulmonary capillary pressure → non-cardiogenic leak","Alveolar surfactant excess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bronchospasm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction raising pulmonary capillary pressure → non-cardiogenic leak', 'At high altitude, generalized hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction increases pulmonary arterial pressure, and in susceptible individuals it causes uneven, over-vasoconstricted regions to leak fluid — high-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE). The wedge pressure is normal, distinguishing it from cardiogenic edema.'),
('7958d374-4070-5611-90d9-079f5d4a73c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Acute mountain sickness (AMS):', '["Occurs above 2500 m with headache, nausea, fatigue, and dizziness; generally self-limited","Is a chronic irreversible condition accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Affects only the CNS permanently mediated by specific receptor activation","Causes only pulmonary edema without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is always fatal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occurs above 2500 m with headache, nausea, fatigue, and dizziness; generally self-limited', 'AMS is a headache-predominant syndrome developing within hours of ascent above about 2500 m, from cerebral vasodilation and mild edema. Most cases resolve with rest, hydration, analgesics, and/or descent; progression to HAPE or HACE is serious and requires descent.'),
('5a8a0410-cb6f-5342-acc7-05bead99f343'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which is the most rapid physiological acclimatization response to high-altitude hypoxia?', '["Increased ventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response within minutes to days)","Increased capillary density (weeks)","Increased Hb mass via erythropoietin (weeks)","Increased 2,3-BPG right-shifting the ODC (days)","Decreased ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response within minutes to days)', 'The immediate acclimatization response is an increase in ventilation driven by the peripheral chemoreceptors, occurring within minutes to hours and intensifying over days as CSF bicarbonate is lost. Erythropoiesis, capillary growth, and 2,3-BPG changes develop more slowly.'),
('043e6779-c201-5d12-ada0-e5262dcd93d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the pathophysiological mechanism of pulmonary oxygen toxicity?', '["Reactive oxygen species (ROS) damage the alveolar epithelium, producing ARDS-like lung injury","Pneumothorax from barotrauma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vagal overactivity mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct CO2 retention without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased surfactant synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive oxygen species (ROS) damage the alveolar epithelium, producing ARDS-like lung injury', 'High inspired oxygen tensions generate reactive oxygen species that overwhelm antioxidant defenses and injure type I and type II pneumocytes, causing alveolar-capillary leak and an ARDS-like picture. CNS seizures and retrolental fibroplasia occur with prolonged hyperoxia in other settings.'),
('bfdd998f-065d-5dd3-8267-3c4b98989f78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'A diver experiences joint pain and neurological symptoms after a rapid ascent. The physiological disturbances of increased ambient pressure that predispose to this include:', '["Central redistribution of blood → ↑CVP/PCWP with a diuretic response, and increased work of breathing from dense gas","Decreased work of breathing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral pooling of blood with hypotension mediated by specific receptor activation","Reduced pulmonary blood volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No changes until 100 m depth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central redistribution of blood → ↑CVP/PCWP with a diuretic response, and increased work of breathing from dense gas', 'Immersion and increased pressure shift blood centrally, raising CVP/PCWP and causing a pressure-diuresis, while the high gas density increases the work of breathing. On ascent, nitrogen that dissolved in tissues can form bubbles (decompression sickness), causing joint pain and neurological symptoms.'),
('df1c4fbc-b3a9-5b1d-a70e-f461fdc606d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Decompression sickness (DCS) is caused by:', '["Nitrogen bubbles forming in tissues during rapid ascent → joint pain (the bends)","Oxygen toxicity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only skin mottling mediated by specific receptor activation","Only CNS symptoms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No known mechanism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrogen bubbles forming in tissues during rapid ascent → joint pain (the bends)', 'During rapid ascent, nitrogen that had dissolved in tissues under pressure comes out of solution as bubbles, obstructing vessels and causing the joint pain of ''the bends'', as well as pulmonary (chokes), cutaneous, and neurological syndromes. Slow staged ascent or in-water recompression prevents it.'),
('88e38795-8762-571e-a219-0854359fcca4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Arterial gas embolism (AGE):', '["Gas bubbles in the arterial circulation causing stroke or loss of consciousness, often from pulmonary barotrauma (breath-holding during ascent)","Venous bubbles that are always harmless accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A condition that is never serious mediated by specific receptor activation","Only local skin changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Caused by oxygen toxicity alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gas bubbles in the arterial circulation causing stroke or loss of consciousness, often from pulmonary barotrauma (breath-holding during ascent)', 'If a diver ascends while breath-holding, expanding intrapulmonary gas can rupture alveoli and force bubbles into the pulmonary veins and left heart. These arterial gas emboli travel to the brain and cause stroke-like deficits or loss of consciousness; immediate hyperbaric recompression is required.'),
('9480d10b-6399-58ac-ba0d-113ccec4162c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Pulmonary barotrauma in divers results from:', '["Air embolism only resulting in decreased functional activity","A minor complaint accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mediastinal emphysema alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Overinflation of the lungs due to breath-holding during ascent → pneumothorax (and possible air embolism)","A benign finding leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Overinflation of the lungs due to breath-holding during ascent → pneumothorax (and possible air embolism)', 'As ambient pressure falls during ascent, gas within the lungs expands; if the diver cannot exhale (e.g., breath-holding), the lungs overinflate and may rupture. The resulting air can produce pneumothorax, mediastinal/subcutaneous emphysema, or arterial gas embolism.'),
('31263cc8-5bc7-553d-9668-c1660f6c4410'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Central chemoreceptors are located in the:', '["Carotid bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Aortic bodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal mucosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulla oblongata (ventral surface near the exit of CN IX-X), responding to H+ in CSF","Peripheral nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata (ventral surface near the exit of CN IX-X), responding to H+ in CSF', 'Central chemoreceptors lie near the ventral surface of the medulla and are stimulated by H+ in the CSF. Because CO2 diffuses freely across the blood-brain barrier and is converted to H+, these receptors mediate the dominant ventilatory response to CO2.'),
('fc33cb91-d6c2-5ee9-a26a-ccb6f1f5c941'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Peripheral chemoreceptors consist of:', '["Carotid bodies (CN IX) and aortic bodies (CN X), responding primarily to ↓PaO2","Only carotid bodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only aortic bodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Only medullary neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nasal receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carotid bodies (CN IX) and aortic bodies (CN X), responding primarily to ↓PaO2', 'The carotid bodies (innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve) and aortic bodies (by the vagus) respond mainly to low arterial PO2, and secondarily to ↑PaCO2 and ↑H+. The carotid bodies dominate the hypoxic ventilatory response.'),
('8a62a62e-bccc-5301-b5b2-2945b6d9a253'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The ventilatory response to CO2 is mediated primarily by:', '["Neither central nor peripheral chemoreceptors","Peripheral chemoreceptors only","Central chemoreceptors only","Central chemoreceptors (80%) with a smaller peripheral contribution (20%)","Equally by central and peripheral receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Central chemoreceptors (80%) with a smaller peripheral contribution (20%)', 'The central medullary chemoreceptors, sensing CSF H+ generated from CO2, account for about 80% of the ventilatory response to hypercapnia, while the carotid and aortic bodies contribute roughly 20%. The peripheral component is relatively more important for hypoxia.'),
('d8de4185-0838-562a-98fe-2a46f5f9f7fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Chronic CO2 retention in COPD leads to:', '["An increased CO2 response resulting in decreased functional activity","Only an O2 response accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A normal CO2 response mediated by specific receptor activation","Blunting of the ventilatory response to CO2, with reliance on the hypoxic drive","Permanent apnea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blunting of the ventilatory response to CO2, with reliance on the hypoxic drive', 'Chronic hypercapnia desensitizes the central chemoreceptors, so ventilation is increasingly driven by the peripheral hypoxic response. Giving high-flow oxygen to such patients can remove this drive and dangerously worsen CO2 retention.'),
('a5f4ef21-2eb9-5439-bbe5-347599f34805'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In carbon monoxide poisoning, the pathophysiology includes:', '["CO binds Hb with about 250× the affinity of O2 → ↓O2 capacity and a left shift of the ODC (impaired unloading) → tissue hypoxia","Right shift of the ODC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased O2 capacity mediated by specific receptor activation","No significant effect on O2 transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased CO affinity for Hb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CO binds Hb with about 250× the affinity of O2 → ↓O2 capacity and a left shift of the ODC (impaired unloading) → tissue hypoxia', 'CO competitively binds hemoglobin at 250 times the affinity of O2, reducing oxygen-carrying capacity and left-shifting the dissociation curve so that remaining oxygen is unloaded poorly. The result is tissue hypoxia out of proportion to the measured PaO2.'),
('797c75a0-eaa8-5718-b24b-9f11615f9161'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Methemoglobinemia is characterized by:', '["Oxidation of Hb Fe2+ to Fe3+ (which cannot bind O2) → functional anemia and a left shift; cyanosis with a normal PaO2, caused by nitrates, dapsone, and benzocaine","Increased O2-carrying capacity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binding of CO with 250× the affinity of O2 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A right shift with greater O2 unloading without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inability of Hb to carry CO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation of Hb Fe2+ to Fe3+ (which cannot bind O2) → functional anemia and a left shift; cyanosis with a normal PaO2, caused by nitrates, dapsone, and benzocaine', 'Oxidizing drugs convert the ferrous iron of hemoglobin to ferric methemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen and increases the affinity of the remaining hemes for O2. Patients appear cyanotic with chocolate-brown blood but a normal arterial PO2; methylene blue is the antidote.'),
('a111fb37-0c82-5fe6-972e-208a5b953a0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which genetic deficiency causes early-onset panacinar emphysema with lower lobe predominance?', '["Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ genotype)","Cystic fibrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asthma mediated by specific receptor activation","Marfan syndrome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Systemic sclerosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ genotype)', 'Alpha-1 antitrypsin normally inhibits neutrophil elastase in the lung; its deficiency (especially the PiZZ genotype) allows elastase to destroy alveolar walls, producing panacinar emphysema at an early age that predominates in the lower lobes. Smoking accelerates the damage.'),
('bf68ca4e-32f6-5574-9002-63c40a91319f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Budesonide (ICS) works by:', '["Reduces airway inflammation via gene transcription","Dilates bronchi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocks histamine mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits leukotrienes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocks muscarinic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces airway inflammation via gene transcription', 'Binds glucocorticoid receptors.'),
('c80dd371-8524-5c25-ae79-66df3b4944bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Phrenic nerve injury leads to:', '["No significant effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal diaphragm movement accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only minor weakness mediated by specific receptor activation","Diaphragmatic paralysis with an elevated hemidiaphragm on CXR and paradoxical movement on the sniff test","Hyperinflation of the lung leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragmatic paralysis with an elevated hemidiaphragm on CXR and paradoxical movement on the sniff test', 'Unilateral phrenic palsy paralyzes one hemidiaphragm, which appears elevated on chest radiograph and moves paradoxically upward during a sniff on fluoroscopy/ultrasound. Vital capacity falls, especially in the supine position, and bilateral palsy causes respiratory failure.'),
('d8a78839-077a-5935-8c99-6ed5b7294313'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The diaphragm contributes what percentage of quiet-breathing tidal volume?', '["75%","30%","50%","90%","100%"]'::jsonb, '75%', 'During quiet breathing the diaphragm is the dominant inspiratory muscle, accounting for roughly 75% of the tidal volume change by increasing the vertical dimension of the thorax. The external intercostals contribute the remaining 25%.'),
('6f21c735-2ed8-55f1-855d-cc82e5068f28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The external intercostal muscles:', '["Are primarily expiratory resulting in decreased functional activity","Elevate the ribs during inspiration, increasing the anteroposterior and lateral thoracic diameter","Compress the abdomen mediated by specific receptor activation","Act only in maximal expiration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depress the ribs and reduce lung volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elevate the ribs during inspiration, increasing the anteroposterior and lateral thoracic diameter', 'The external intercostals slope downward and forward, so their contraction elevates the ribs, enlarging the thoracic cage in both anteroposterior and lateral directions. They are active inspiratory muscles that assist the diaphragm during normal and deeper breathing.'),
('9a0a6ea9-a47b-5452-a6b1-9a4279dd6a0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Regarding the internal intercostal muscles:', '["The intercartilaginous (parasternal) portion elevates the ribs during inspiration, while the interosseous portion is expiratory","All portions are purely expiratory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The interosseous portion is inspiratory mediated by specific receptor activation","The parasternal portion is expiratory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","They have no respiratory function leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The intercartilaginous (parasternal) portion elevates the ribs during inspiration, while the interosseous portion is expiratory', 'The internal intercostals have two parts with opposite actions: the intercartilaginous (parasternal) segment is inspiratory and elevates the ribs, whereas the interosseous segment pulls the ribs down and is active during forced expiration.'),
('c5d2ecf4-e5c3-5498-9036-407953e00780'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which medullary groups constitute the primary rhythm-generating centers that drive automatic breathing?', '["Apneustic center only resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsal respiratory group (DRG) and ventral respiratory group (VRG) in the medulla","Basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumotaxic center only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal respiratory group (DRG) and ventral respiratory group (VRG) in the medulla', 'Automatic breathing is generated in the medullary DRG and VRG, which produce the inspiratory ramp and coordinate inspiratory-expiratory neuron firing. The pontine pneumotaxic and apneustic centers modulate the pattern, while the cerebral cortex provides voluntary control.'),
('a53eec0f-fd5e-5739-9e85-e2ea7f57c852'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The respiratory quotient (RQ = VCO2/VO2) on a typical mixed diet is approximately:', '["1.2","2.0","0.8","0.5","1.0"]'::jsonb, '0.8', 'The respiratory quotient equals the ratio of CO2 produced to O2 consumed and averages about 0.8 on a mixed diet (1.0 for pure carbohydrate, 0.7 for fat). It is used in the alveolar gas equation to relate PAO2 to PaCO2.'),
('626d8f8d-5b1f-53c0-a399-f5dc4be6a102'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'In a healthy adult, the normal physiological (anatomical) dead space volume is approximately:', '["1000 mL","300 mL","500 mL","150 mL","50 mL"]'::jsonb, '150 mL', 'The dead space is the portion of each breath that does not participate in gas exchange, about 150 mL (≈2 mL/kg) in adults, nearly all of it anatomical in health. Because of the alveolar gas equation, PaCO2 falls when dead space is bypassed by intubation.'),
('582f24ca-bd2b-5a36-9700-bf89213b29b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the normal intrapleural pressure at rest?', '["+10 cm H2O","0 cm H2O","−5 cm H2O","−10 cm H2O","+5 cm H2O"]'::jsonb, '−5 cm H2O', 'At rest the intrapleural pressure is subatmospheric, about −5 cm H2O, because the lung''s inward elastic recoil opposes the chest wall''s outward recoil. It becomes more negative during inspiration (−8 to −10 cm H2O) and less negative during expiration.'),
('8b9d7402-3b8d-5a67-9b9e-ba1a7105c13f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Peripheral chemoreceptors are most sensitive to:', '["PaO2 (hypoxia)","PaCO2 (hypercapnia)","Arterial HCO3− (pH)","O2 content","Decreased cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'PaO2 (hypoxia)', 'The carotid and aortic bodies respond primarily to the arterial partial pressure of oxygen, sensing hypoxia rather than total O2 content. They are also stimulated by ↑PaCO2 and ↑H+, but their distinctive role is driving the hypoxic ventilatory response below a PaO2 of about 60 mmHg.'),
('56030e6b-9e23-58e0-b526-4cb9e444e33d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Which muscles are the primary muscles of inspiration during normal quiet breathing?', '["Only the external intercostals","Only the diaphragm","Diaphragm and external intercostals","Internal intercostals and abdominal muscles","Scalenes and sternocleidomastoid"]'::jsonb, 'Diaphragm and external intercostals', 'During quiet breathing the diaphragm (≈75% of the tidal volume) and the external intercostals are the active inspiratory muscles. The internal intercostals and abdominals are expiratory, and the scalenes and sternocleidomastoid are accessory muscles used when ventilation is increased.'),
('168d180a-a5a8-5040-93ee-909e9583175a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Most important ICS local side effect:', '["Oral candidiasis","Systemic immunosuppression","Adrenal suppression","Growth retardation","Osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 'Oral candidiasis', 'Mouth rinsing reduces risk.'),
('ec143582-36b6-5dce-a7b3-d16ce2111f77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the term for the bulk flow of air between the atmosphere and the alveoli?', '["Pulmonary ventilation","Gas exchange across the alveolar membrane","O2 consumption","CO2 production","Transport of gases in the blood"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary ventilation', 'The bulk flow of air between the atmosphere and the alveoli is pulmonary ventilation, produced by the respiratory muscles changing the thoracic volume. Diffusion-based gas exchange across the membrane is a distinct step.'),
('822521b5-45a9-53c8-ab54-d06b8dc0bcd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the normal tidal volume (VT) at rest?', '["300 mL","1000 mL","500 mL","200 mL","700 mL"]'::jsonb, '500 mL', 'At rest the normal tidal volume is approximately 500 mL in an adult. Larger or smaller values indicate altered breathing depth and help characterize restrictive or obstructive patterns.'),
('9fe20c06-4cab-514e-80b9-b31985876b5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'Minute ventilation (VE) equals:', '["TV / RR","RR / TV","TV × FiO2","TV + RR","TV × RR"]'::jsonb, 'TV × RR', 'Minute ventilation is the total volume of air moved per minute, the product of tidal volume and respiratory rate: VE = VT × RR. At a VT of 500 mL and RR of 12/min this is about 6 L/min.'),
('4b8a75af-00c5-5b8d-b029-e27e363104e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What does minute ventilation (VE) equal?', '["TV / RR","RR / TV","TV × FiO2","TV + RR","TV × RR"]'::jsonb, 'TV × RR', 'Minute ventilation is the product of tidal volume and respiratory frequency (VE = VT × RR), normally about 6 L/min at rest. Alveolar ventilation is obtained by subtracting dead-space ventilation from VE.'),
('4142f718-e5a4-574c-92f4-c9da75e9ba88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'What is the approximate anatomical dead space volume in a healthy adult?', '["50 mL","200 mL","150 mL (≈2 mL/kg)","250 mL","100 mL"]'::jsonb, '150 mL (≈2 mL/kg)', 'The anatomical dead space is the air residing in the conducting airways between the nose/mouth and the respiratory bronchioles, roughly 150 mL or about 2 mL/kg. This gas does not participate in gas exchange.'),
('adcaa827-5420-59e9-ad8b-9f35e03332fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 1', 'The normal VD/VT ratio is:', '["0.2-0.3 (20-30%)","0.5-0.6","<0.1","0.7-0.8","0.1-0.15"]'::jsonb, '0.2-0.3 (20-30%)', 'The normal dead space fraction of the tidal volume is 0.2-0.3 (20-30%). Because dead-space ventilation is wasted, an elevated VD/VT ratio impairs CO2 elimination and reflects increased alveolar dead space.'),
('34601003-0982-59e6-92e5-504b6f17aa98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 68-year-old truck driver presents with chest tightness and cough that worsens at night. PFTs show an obstructive pattern that is reversible with a bronchodilator. (Respiratory Case #1)', '["Emphysema","Chronic bronchitis","Sarcoidosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma', 'Asthma is characterized by a reversible obstructive airway pattern, showing >12% improvement in FEV1 post-bronchodilator.'),
('6497cdbe-0f73-5868-963e-59dab5033253'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 29-year-old female presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after a long-distance flight. ABG shows hypoxemia, hypocapnia, and a respiratory alkalosis. (Respiratory Case #2)', '["Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt","Alveolar dead space only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anatomic shunt only mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypoventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diffusion limitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt', 'A pulmonary embolism causes V/Q mismatch and shunt, leading to hypoxemia.'),
('fd5088fb-afd0-5541-9eaf-2bf84be80c0e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 45-year-old construction worker presents with chest tightness and cough that worsens at night. PFTs show an obstructive pattern that is reversible with a bronchodilator. (Respiratory Case #3)', '["Sarcoidosis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Chronic bronchitis","Asthma","Emphysema"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma', 'Asthma is characterized by a reversible obstructive airway pattern, showing >12% improvement in FEV1 post-bronchodilator.'),
('1807eead-4b14-5bef-85b9-ac5293e8dd91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 61-year-old school teacher develops sudden breathlessness and tachycardia post-surgery. V/Q scan shows a ventilation-perfusion mismatch with normal ventilation but no perfusion. (Respiratory Case #4)', '["Alveolar dead space only resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt","Hypoventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Diffusion limitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anatomic shunt only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt', 'A pulmonary embolism causes V/Q mismatch and shunt, leading to hypoxemia.'),
