('d37fef40-b808-5f01-a788-88478b18c93f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 36-year-old female presents with sudden-onset pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea after a long-distance flight. ABG shows hypoxemia, hypocapnia, and a respiratory alkalosis. (Respiratory Case #84)', '["Alveolar dead space only resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt","Anatomic shunt only mediated by specific receptor activation","Diffusion limitation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypoventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt', 'A pulmonary embolism causes V/Q mismatch and shunt, leading to hypoxemia.'),
('a91cbb1a-678f-54a8-8662-11cdb0d8bd48'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 29-year-old truck driver presents with chest tightness and cough that worsens at night. PFTs show an obstructive pattern that is reversible with a bronchodilator. (Respiratory Case #85)', '["Chronic bronchitis","Sarcoidosis","Emphysema","Asthma","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma', 'Asthma is characterized by a reversible obstructive airway pattern, showing >12% improvement in FEV1 post-bronchodilator.'),
('634f7b31-2f34-5084-8aa1-ea8700345adf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 45-year-old truck driver develops sudden breathlessness and tachycardia post-surgery. V/Q scan shows a ventilation-perfusion mismatch with normal ventilation but no perfusion. (Respiratory Case #86)', '["Diffusion limitation resulting in decreased functional activity","Alveolar dead space only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt","Hypoventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anatomic shunt only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt', 'A pulmonary embolism causes V/Q mismatch and shunt, leading to hypoxemia.'),
('897bb94a-b4ad-5f88-882e-c5b08019aa66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 29-year-old school teacher presents with chest tightness and cough that worsens at night. PFTs show an obstructive pattern that is reversible with a bronchodilator. (Respiratory Case #87)', '["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Emphysema","Asthma","Sarcoidosis","Chronic bronchitis"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma', 'Asthma is characterized by a reversible obstructive airway pattern, showing >12% improvement in FEV1 post-bronchodilator.'),
('7f349387-fb70-5682-8b2c-e71030e01ab5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A 22-year-old school teacher develops sudden breathlessness and tachycardia post-surgery. V/Q scan shows a ventilation-perfusion mismatch with normal ventilation but no perfusion. (Respiratory Case #88)', '["Hypoventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Diffusion limitation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar dead space only mediated by specific receptor activation","Anatomic shunt only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch and shunt', 'A pulmonary embolism causes V/Q mismatch and shunt, leading to hypoxemia.'),
('fbf0bae9-5338-5304-ba69-ccf60838e12c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: Forced vital capacity (FVC) is:', '["IRV resulting in decreased functional activity","Maximum volume forcibly exhaled after full inspiration","Tidal volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Volume at rest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minute volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum volume forcibly exhaled after full inspiration', 'FVC is the total volume of air that can be forcefully expired after a maximal inspiration. It is a key spirometric measurement used together with FEV1 to distinguish obstructive from restrictive disease.'),
('c851e92f-144e-5c87-b2de-925e2eefa522'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, Closing capacity (CC) equals:', '["CV + RV","TLC","TV","VC","FRC"]'::jsonb, 'CV + RV', 'Closing capacity is the lung volume at which airway closure begins, computed as closing volume plus residual volume (CC = CV + RV). Clinically, one asks whether CC encroaches on FRC, which happens with aging and in smokers.'),
('e5427dbd-fbfc-589b-a5ee-3ccfc2d20261'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Which equation gives the partial pressure of oxygen in the alveoli (PAO2)?', '["O2 consumption","PAO2 − PaCO2","Alveolar ventilation","PaCO2","PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/RQ)"]'::jsonb, 'PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/RQ)', 'The alveolar gas equation estimates PAO2 from the inspired PO2 minus the oxygen consumed relative to CO2 produced: PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/RQ), where RQ is the respiratory quotient (0.8). It links alveolar oxygen to ventilation.'),
('13a5bf19-3344-5d02-80fa-ed5000037073'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'How is the breathing reserve (BR) at peak exercise calculated?', '["MVV - peak exercise VE (normally >20-25 L/min)","Peak exercise VE alone","Maximum heart rate minus resting heart rate","VO2max divided by body weight","MVV alone"]'::jsonb, 'MVV - peak exercise VE (normally >20-25 L/min)', 'Breathing reserve is the difference between the maximal voluntary ventilation (MVV) and the minute ventilation reached at peak exercise. A normal reserve is >20-25 L/min or >30% of MVV; a low reserve indicates a ventilatory limitation to exercise.'),
('3b04a5a9-f433-590b-9f4a-e39d09c85e4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What does the Borg scale measure?', '["Severity of anxiety resulting in decreased functional activity","Arterial pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain severity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rating of perceived exertion/dyspnea (6-20 scale)"]'::jsonb, 'Rating of perceived exertion/dyspnea (6-20 scale)', 'The Borg scale is a 6-20 rating scale of perceived exertion or breathlessness (dyspnea) during exercise. It allows standardized subjective quantitation of effort and symptoms in CPET and rehabilitation.'),
('140f72f4-70dd-545a-baf2-6ad18f7a5f82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What does the modified Medical Research Council (mMRC) dyspnea scale assess?', '["Anxiety level resulting in decreased functional activity","COPD exacerbation history accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dyspnea severity (0-4) for daily activities","Pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cough frequency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dyspnea severity (0-4) for daily activities', 'The mMRC scale grades breathlessness from 0 (breathless only on strenuous exercise) to 4 (too breathless to leave the house), reflecting how dyspnea limits daily activities. It is used with the CAT score to assess COPD symptom burden.'),
('7ca6339b-3858-58a0-ac07-04db43d7ce1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, Closing volume (CV) is the:', '["Volume at full exhalation resulting in decreased functional activity","Lung volume at which small airways in the dependent lung zones close","TLC mediated by specific receptor activation","Volume at full inspiration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FRC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lung volume at which small airways in the dependent lung zones close', 'Closing volume is the lung volume (measured from residual volume) at which small airways, particularly in dependent regions, begin to close during expiration. When closing capacity exceeds FRC, the airways close during tidal breathing, impairing gas exchange.'),
('03097d30-d1e3-5e56-a90a-a1ada74983f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the role of non-invasive ventilation (NIV) in an acute exacerbation of COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure?', '["BiPAP for acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (↓intubation)","Used for every COPD exacerbation regardless of severity","Only for mild isolated hypoxemia","Reserved exclusively for ventilated patients","A replacement for all bronchodilator therapy"]'::jsonb, 'BiPAP for acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (↓intubation)', 'NIV with BiPAP is first-line for acute hypercapnic (type II) respiratory failure in COPD exacerbation because it reduces work of breathing, improves gas exchange, and decreases the need for intubation and mortality. It is indicated for acute respiratory acidosis, not mild hypoxia alone.'),
('6350ba5f-d0a3-555b-8a3f-d420fea399ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, The chemoreceptors that detect arterial CO2 are located:', '["Central (medullary) and peripheral (carotid/aortic)","Only central accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only peripheral mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atrial walls leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central (medullary) and peripheral (carotid/aortic)', 'CO2 is sensed both centrally, by medullary chemoreceptors that respond to CSF H+, and peripherally, by the carotid and aortic bodies. Central receptors mediate about 80% of the ventilatory response to CO2.'),
('b6b38d6c-b349-5f0c-a30c-018f45dddd75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the mechanism by which pursed-lip breathing helps patients with COPD?', '["Provides inspiratory assistance resulting in decreased functional activity","Deliberately increases airway resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prolongs expiration → prevents airway collapse (↑intra-airway pressure)","Encourages breath holding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Improves inspiratory muscle strength leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prolongs expiration → prevents airway collapse (↑intra-airway pressure)', 'Pursed-lip breathing creates back pressure that raises intra-airway pressure during expiration, counterbalancing the compressive pleural pressure and preventing premature small-airway collapse. This reduces air trapping and dyspnea in COPD.'),
('9b5eb99e-fde9-5aa9-b650-c9193a5bb7e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'An incentive spirometer is best described by which of the following?', '["A machine that mechanically ventilates the patient resulting in decreased functional activity","A spirometer that measures FEV1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A device that encourages deep breathing (prevents atelectasis post-surgery)","A device that measures lung compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An oxygen delivery device leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A device that encourages deep breathing (prevents atelectasis post-surgery)', 'An incentive spirometer provides visual feedback to encourage slow, sustained deep inspirations, preventing and reversing postoperative atelectasis. It does not measure lung function or deliver ventilation or oxygen.'),
('8fc6f1eb-3a0f-5c09-b160-d0145b168ee6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the mechanism of action of a positive expiratory pressure (PEP) device?', '["Applies constant negative pressure to the airway","Breathing out against resistance → splints airways open (mobilizes secretions)","Delivers high-frequency oscillatory flow","Provides inspiratory resistance only","Functions as continuous positive airway pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Breathing out against resistance → splints airways open (mobilizes secretions)', 'A PEP device applies expiratory resistance so the patient exhales against a positive pressure, which keeps airways splinted open, reduces gas trapping, and helps mobilize secretions through pressure-equalization and collateral ventilation.'),
('edfe8c88-29ac-5043-b8ee-f9f598bfe1cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Airway resistance is primarily determined by:', '["Viscosity of gas resulting in decreased functional activity","Flow rate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Length of airways mediated by specific receptor activation","Radius of airways (Poiseuille''s law: R ∝ 1/r⁴)","Density of gas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Radius of airways (Poiseuille''s law: R ∝ 1/r⁴)', 'By Poiseuille''s law, resistance is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the airway radius (R ∝ 1/r⁴). Because of this steep relationship, small changes in airway caliber dramatically alter resistance, as in asthma.'),
('7e6120b4-2b18-548b-a86a-4745f2909f5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, Vital capacity (VC) equals:', '["TV + RV","IRV + ERV","FRC + IRV","IRV + TV + ERV","TLC − TV"]'::jsonb, 'IRV + TV + ERV', 'Vital capacity is the sum of inspiratory reserve volume, tidal volume, and expiratory reserve volume (IRV + TV + ERV). Equivalently it is TLC minus residual volume, spanning the volumes between maximal inspiration and maximal expiration.'),
('6890bac7-0e0a-5fc6-9385-2f4d12674f3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: The Bohr effect states that:', '["CO2 increases O2 binding to Hb resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alkalinity decreases Hb affinity for O2 mediated by specific receptor activation","O2 increases CO2 binding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acidity (H+ and CO2) decreases Hb affinity for O2 (right shift)"]'::jsonb, 'Acidity (H+ and CO2) decreases Hb affinity for O2 (right shift)', 'The Bohr effect describes how increased H+ and CO2 stabilize the deoxy (T) conformation of hemoglobin, lowering its O2 affinity and right-shifting the curve. This promotes O2 unloading in the acidic, CO2-rich tissues.'),
('4f0efa35-9b13-57b3-83d8-119bb57c7007'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: What is the treatment of choice for carbon monoxide poisoning?', '["Intubation alone","Mechanical ventilation","100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen","CPAP","Breathing room air"]'::jsonb, '100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen', '100% oxygen competitively displaces CO from hemoglobin and shortens its half-life; hyperbaric oxygen further accelerates dissociation and is used in severe cases. High FiO2 is the cornerstone of therapy because CO has an enormous affinity advantage otherwise.'),
('e02cc0bf-a233-5b98-8cf0-af5d0ca96089'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, What is the normal intrapleural pressure at functional residual capacity (FRC)?', '["Positive","Equal to alveolar pressure","Atmospheric","Subatmospheric (-5 cmH2O at FRC)","Equal to mouth pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Subatmospheric (-5 cmH2O at FRC)', 'Intrapleural pressure is normally subatmospheric because the elastic recoil of the lung pulls inward while the chest wall pulls outward. At FRC it is about -5 cmH2O, and swinging more negative during inspiration keeps the lungs inflated.'),
('fbe04f8b-741c-5596-acf2-1293c42fc5b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Stridor developing shortly after extubation most likely indicates which of the following?', '["Laryngeal edema (treat with racemic epinephrine/Heliox) or reintubation","Normal physiologic variation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Residual bronchospasm only mediated by specific receptor activation","Silent atelectasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Recurrent infection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Laryngeal edema (treat with racemic epinephrine/Heliox) or reintubation', 'Post-extubation stridor reflects upper airway obstruction from laryngeal edema caused by the endotracheal tube. It is managed with racemic epinephrine and heliox, and reintubation is required if it progresses, as the airway may be critically narrowed.'),
('7d8e7379-0627-5339-97f2-ee0fcb1dca87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, The chest wall compliance is approximately:', '["400 mL/cm H2O","100 mL/cm H2O","300 mL/cm H2O","200 mL/cm H2O","50 mL/cm H2O"]'::jsonb, '200 mL/cm H2O', 'Chest wall compliance is roughly 200 mL/cm H2O, similar to lung compliance. Because they behave in series, total respiratory compliance is about half of either component (≈100 mL/cm H2O).'),
('bd4bb46e-ab5c-547d-9153-4d7ab56b010e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: In the upright lung, ventilation is greatest at the:', '["Equal throughout","Posterior regions","Apex","Lung bases (gravity-dependent)","Middle regions"]'::jsonb, 'Lung bases (gravity-dependent)', 'Because of gravity, the intrapleural pressure is less negative (more compressed) at the lung bases, so they undergo a greater volume change with breathing and receive more ventilation. Perfusion is also greatest at the bases.'),
('b692216f-4de3-5b93-9b86-a8740e2880b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'How does heliox (a helium-oxygen mixture) reduce the work of breathing?', '["Work of breathing (lower density → ↓turbulent flow resistance)","By increasing pulmonary blood flow","By reducing dead space","By suppressing airway inflammation","By increasing O2 consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Work of breathing (lower density → ↓turbulent flow resistance)', 'Helium is much less dense than nitrogen, so a helium-oxygen mixture markedly reduces turbulent-flow airway resistance and thereby the work of breathing. It is used as a temporizing measure in upper airway obstruction and severe asthma.'),
('c62bbae4-3294-5fab-a970-5a0110b14feb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the first-line bronchodilator therapy for an acute asthma exacerbation?', '["Aminophylline infusion alone resulting in decreased functional activity","β2 agonists (albuterol) + anticholinergics (ipratropium) nebulized","Systemic steroids alone as monotherapy mediated by specific receptor activation","Aggressive intravenous hydration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Supplemental oxygen only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'β2 agonists (albuterol) + anticholinergics (ipratropium) nebulized', 'Repeated inhaled short-acting β2-agonists (e.g., albuterol) combined with an anticholinergic (ipratropium) provide rapid bronchodilation and are the first-line treatment of acute asthma; corticosteroids are added to treat inflammation but do not relieve bronchospasm immediately.'),
('1b3b6cd1-2205-50c4-80bc-f0180b7b18f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Maximum inspiratory pressure (MIP) measures:', '["Lung volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Airway resistance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DLCO mediated by specific receptor activation","Respiratory muscle strength (normal 100 cmH2O)","Compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory muscle strength (normal 100 cmH2O)', 'MIP (or negative inspiratory force) is measured against an occluded airway at residual volume and reflects inspiratory muscle strength, normally about -100 cmH2O. It is reduced in neuromuscular weakness such as myasthenia gravis or phrenic palsy.'),
('c72b885b-038f-5ccb-b202-3cfe0ae8910d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which two types of chemoreceptors regulate ventilation in response to changes in blood gases?', '["Only central chemoreceptors","Voluntary motor cortex control","Only peripheral chemoreceptors","Central and peripheral chemoreceptors","Airway mechanoreceptors"]'::jsonb, 'Central and peripheral chemoreceptors', 'Central chemoreceptors on the ventrolateral medulla respond primarily to CSF H+ (i.e., PaCO2), while peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies respond mainly to arterial PO2, as well as to pH and PaCO2. Together they set the level of ventilation.'),
('7061b243-05cb-535b-80a5-f227caf235a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes the dorsal respiratory group (DRG)?', '["Located in nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS); primarily inspiratory neurons; integrates afferent input","Located in the pons; purely expiratory accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contains only expiratory motor neurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Controls only voluntary breathing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Located in the spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Located in nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS); primarily inspiratory neurons; integrates afferent input', 'The DRG lies in the nucleus tractus solitarius of the medulla and contains mostly inspiratory neurons that integrate stretch receptor, chemoreceptor, and other afferent input to generate rhythmic inspiration.'),
('3d3b5a8f-2383-582a-b909-1c68a73fb57d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: Anatomical dead space is approximately:', '["50 mL","200 mL","150 mL (≈2 mL/kg)","250 mL","100 mL"]'::jsonb, '150 mL (≈2 mL/kg)', 'Anatomical dead space is the volume of the conducting airways where no gas exchange occurs, about 150 mL (≈2 mL/kg ideal body weight) in adults. It roughly equals the physiological dead space in a normal healthy lung.'),
('9b921095-ac8c-58a1-9b27-93957518f727'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, A 70-year-old with suspected pulmonary embolism undergoes aV/Q scan. Which of the following normally has the highest ventilation and perfusion, providing the best match?', '["Posterior regions","Apex of the lung","Lung bases (gravity-dependent)","Middle lung zones","Equal everywhere"]'::jsonb, 'Lung bases (gravity-dependent)', 'Gravity makes both ventilation and perfusion greatest at the lung bases, where the change in pleural pressure and the driving pressure for blood flow are largest. Although V/Q is more uniform there, the base still receives the most blood and air.'),
('534421eb-dae9-5637-a378-fe1a1aa5eaec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: The ventilatory response to CO2 is characterized by:', '["↑PaCO2 → ↑minute ventilation, mediated mainly by central chemoreceptors (sensitivity ≈ 2-3 L/min per mmHg rise)","No ventilatory response to CO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only a peripheral hypoxic response mediated by specific receptor activation","Identical to the hypoxic response without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓ventilation with rising PaCO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↑PaCO2 → ↑minute ventilation, mediated mainly by central chemoreceptors (sensitivity ≈ 2-3 L/min per mmHg rise)', 'Rising arterial CO2 strongly stimulates ventilation, chiefly through central chemoreceptors sensing CSF H+, with a normal gain of about 2-3 L/min per mmHg increase in PaCO2. This response is blunted in chronic CO2 retainers.'),
('891fcf15-16f1-58d6-8cf3-1a5c6c240b4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, Central chemoreceptors are located in the:', '["Carotid bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Aortic bodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Nasal mucosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulla oblongata (ventral surface near the exit of CN IX-X), responding to H+ in CSF","Peripheral nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata (ventral surface near the exit of CN IX-X), responding to H+ in CSF', 'Central chemoreceptors lie near the ventral surface of the medulla and are stimulated by H+ in the CSF. Because CO2 diffuses freely across the blood-brain barrier and is converted to H+, these receptors mediate the dominant ventilatory response to CO2.'),
('8fde7c1d-6ece-581c-9345-5dd84ab77e09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes the Hering-Breuer inflation reflex?', '["Vagal afferent from stretch receptors → inhibits inspiration (promotes expiration); more important in infants, weak in adults","It stimulates deeper inspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It is active only during voluntary hyperventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","It operates through sympathetic efferents only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It increases respiratory rate in infants only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagal afferent from stretch receptors → inhibits inspiration (promotes expiration); more important in infants, weak in adults', 'The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex is the vagally mediated inhibition of inspiration caused by pulmonary stretch receptor discharge at high lung volumes. It promotes expiration and is significant in infants but weak in resting adult breathing.'),
('cead486a-1788-5623-9726-19e6384ed727'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: The ventilatory response to hypoxia is:', '["Mediated by peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies) and becomes significant mainly when PaO2 < 60 mmHg","Mediated by central chemoreceptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Largely mediated by the aortic bodies mediated by specific receptor activation","Most marked when PaO2 is above 100 mmHg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unimportant even in severe hypoxemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mediated by peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid bodies) and becomes significant mainly when PaO2 < 60 mmHg', 'Hypoxia is sensed chiefly by the carotid bodies, which raise ventilation through the glossopharyngeal nerve. Because the response accelerates only below about 60 mmHg, it is a backup mechanism compared with the brisk CO2 drive, but it becomes critical in chronic CO2 retainers.'),
('ec61b246-7812-5eb5-a0b4-eda6519c3c6d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Where is the respiratory and cardiovascular control integrated in the brainstem?', '["In the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), which integrates baroreceptor + chemoreceptor afferents","In the cerebral cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the limbic forebrain mediated by specific receptor activation","In the spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the carotid bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), which integrates baroreceptor + chemoreceptor afferents', 'The NTS is the first central relay for baroreceptor, chemoreceptor, and other viscerosensory afferents and integrates signals that coordinate ventilatory and cardiovascular responses, such as during hypoxia, exercise, and hemorrhage.'),
('7e271b81-625b-5cdb-acad-685cdfb2b915'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: The physiological shunt equation (Qs/Qt) is:', '["Qs/Qt = (CcO2 − CaO2) / (CcO2 − CvO2); normal < 5%","Qs/Qt = (CaO2 − CvO2) / (CcO2 − CaO2)","Qs/Qt = CaO2 / (CcO2 − CvO2)","Qs/Qt = (CcO2 − CvO2) / (CcO2 − CaO2)","Qs/Qt = (CvO2 − CaO2) / CcO2"]'::jsonb, 'Qs/Qt = (CcO2 − CaO2) / (CcO2 − CvO2); normal < 5%', 'The shunt fraction compares the deficit between ideal capillary (CcO2) and actual arterial (CaO2) oxygen content to the total mixed-venous gradient. It is normally below 5% of cardiac output, and rises with right-to-left shunting.'),
('be6f7a03-e116-5e7b-8449-61c4e6c01482'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What does indirect calorimetry measure and for which purpose is it used?', '["Only tidal volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Resting energy expenditure (REE) from VO2 and VCO2 (Weir equation) for nutritional assessment","Only body temperature mediated by specific receptor activation","Only heart rate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only respiratory rate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Resting energy expenditure (REE) from VO2 and VCO2 (Weir equation) for nutritional assessment', 'Indirect calorimetry measures oxygen consumption and CO2 production to compute energy expenditure using the Weir equation. It quantifies resting energy expenditure and the respiratory quotient, guiding nutritional support in critically ill patients.'),
('89cdd52e-6ad2-59c5-a78c-3997534d0cef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A patient has ventilation increased in excess of metabolic needs, with hypocapnia and respiratory alkalosis. Which term best describes this disorder?', '["Alveolar hyperventilation","Alveolar hypoventilation","Appropriate compensatory ventilation","Isolated hypoxemia","Hypercapnic respiratory failure"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar hyperventilation', 'When ventilation exceeds CO2 production, alveolar ventilation is excessive, PaCO2 falls, and a respiratory alkalosis results. This is alveolar hyperventilation, seen with anxiety, fever, sepsis, pain, or Cheyne-Stokes disease-related drive; in the steady state it eventually abates as CO2 reaccumulates.'),
('5ad8df5f-a47d-50b7-8966-8b4f69a5b93e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: FEV1/FVC >0.8 (preserved ratio) together with a reduced FVC indicates:', '["Normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Asthma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Emphysema mediated by specific receptor activation","Obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Restrictive lung disease (fibrosis)"]'::jsonb, 'Restrictive lung disease (fibrosis)', 'A preserved or increased FEV1/FVC ratio with a low FVC (and low TLC) is the spirometric hallmark of restriction, such as pulmonary fibrosis. In obstruction the ratio falls, not rises.'),
('4ffe5687-5cb0-5e3f-81ac-4fa739104d3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following best characterizes obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS)?', '["Obesity (BMI >30) with daytime hypercapnia (PaCO2 >45), sleep-disordered breathing, hypoxemia, and pulmonary HTN","Normal weight with nocturnal desaturations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Isolated snoring without gas abnormality mediated by specific receptor activation","Restrictive disease without hypercapnia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Central apnea due to a brainstem lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity (BMI >30) with daytime hypercapnia (PaCO2 >45), sleep-disordered breathing, hypoxemia, and pulmonary HTN', 'Obesity hypoventilation (Pickwickian) syndrome requires obesity plus daytime hypercapnia with sleep-disordered breathing, hypoxemia, and often pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale. Its mechanism combines mechanical loading, leptin resistance, and blunted ventilatory drive.'),
('8ef8e95e-225f-5b73-8274-ee7d3e067337'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes obstructive sleep apnea (OSA)?', '["Recurrent pharyngeal collapse during sleep → apneas/hypopneas with hypoxia and arousal","Loss of central respiratory drive with no effort accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea mediated by specific receptor activation","Isolated snoring without apnea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Trepopnea during sleep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent pharyngeal collapse during sleep → apneas/hypopneas with hypoxia and arousal', 'OSA is characterized by repetitive pharyngeal collapse during sleep causing apneas/hypopneas despite continued respiratory effort, with oxygen desaturation and arousal responses. Consequences include daytime sleepiness, hypertension, and cardiovascular disease.'),
('25c698fa-64ac-5be1-972f-6a0241634a44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes agonal breathing?', '["Cheyne-Stokes periodic breathing resulting in decreased functional activity","Gasping pattern (a last-resort brainstem reflex) that precedes respiratory arrest","Deep sighing Kussmaul pattern mediated by specific receptor activation","Rapid shallow tachypnea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regular rhythmic breathing with normal depth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gasping pattern (a last-resort brainstem reflex) that precedes respiratory arrest', 'Agonal breathing is a slow, deep, gasping pattern produced by the terminal brainstem reflex during severe hypoxia or brainstem dysfunction. It often appears just before respiratory arrest and should not be mistaken for adequate ventilation; it may still occur early in cardiac arrest.'),
('2e3618d8-489d-5130-b0a3-fa84ae6f4060'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: Which lung volume equals vital capacity plus residual volume?', '["TV + IRV","VC + RV","VC + TV","FRC + IRV","RV + TV"]'::jsonb, 'VC + RV', 'Vital capacity is the volume expired from TLC to RV, so adding the residual volume reconstructs the total lung capacity: TLC = VC + RV. Residual volume is the part of TLC that cannot be measured by spirometry.'),
('1597ab1c-b345-54ae-9f12-710767409c11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: The relationship between PaCO2 and alveolar ventilation (VA) is:', '["PaCO2 ∝ VA resulting in decreased functional activity","PaCO2 ∝ O2 consumption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PaCO2 ∝ VCO2 / VA (inverse relationship)","No relationship without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PaCO2 ∝ VCO2 alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PaCO2 ∝ VCO2 / VA (inverse relationship)', 'Alveolar PCO2 is set by the ratio of CO2 production to alveolar ventilation: PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA. Thus increasing alveolar ventilation lowers PaCO2, and hypoventilation raises it proportionately.'),
('b189690f-0dc3-5d29-86ad-1553304f99ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following defines trepopnea?', '["Dyspnea worse when lying on one side, often with unilateral lung disease, better lying on the healthy side","Dyspnea equal in all lateral positions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Breathlessness only when prone mediated by specific receptor activation","Constant upright dyspnea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise-induced breathlessness leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dyspnea worse when lying on one side, often with unilateral lung disease, better lying on the healthy side', 'Trepopnea is positional dyspnea that is worse in one lateral decubitus position and improved in the other. In unilateral pulmonary disease, lying on the affected side worsens V/Q mismatch and work of breathing, so patients prefer lying on the unaffected side.'),
('bdfd6bcd-b201-5d07-8dd5-36bd7185d04b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following correctly defines a COPD exacerbation?', '["Acute worsening of symptoms beyond normal day-to-day variation, typically due to infection","A gradual 5-year decline in FEV1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A one-time episode of chest discomfort mediated by specific receptor activation","Improvement in spirometry after oxygen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A stable pattern of daily cough leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute worsening of symptoms beyond normal day-to-day variation, typically due to infection', 'A COPD exacerbation is an acute, sustained increase in cough, sputum, and dyspnea beyond usual day-to-day variation, most often triggered by viral or bacterial respiratory infection (or pollution). Exacerbations accelerate lung function decline and raise mortality.'),
('9d5cdd0b-6f2f-5f6c-ac2c-2d2b8b0558b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, Pulmonary edema caused by increased alveolar-capillary permeability, as in sepsis, presents as:', '["ARDS (exudative edema, high protein, normal PCWP)","Pulmonary edema from left heart failure","Pneumonia only","Aspiration only","Transudative edema with raised PCWP"]'::jsonb, 'ARDS (exudative edema, high protein, normal PCWP)', 'ARDS is a high-permeability (exudative) pulmonary edema in which protein-rich fluid leaks into the alveoli despite a normal wedge pressure. This distinguishes it from cardiogenic edema, which is transudative and driven by an elevated left atrial pressure.'),
('1d3517ec-a278-5a18-9a2e-b602968b910d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: The diffusing capacity of the lung (DLCO) measures:', '["Ventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Perfusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transfer of CO across the alveolar-capillary membrane (CO diffuses similarly to O2)","CO2 diffusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 diffusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer of CO across the alveolar-capillary membrane (CO diffuses similarly to O2)', 'DLCO quantifies how readily gas moves from the alveoli into the blood, estimated using carbon monoxide because its uptake is limited by diffusion rather than perfusion. It is reduced in emphysema, fibrosis, and anemia.'),
('0852c2e2-e1d4-5bf6-be2f-8194dab40f82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: Maximum expiratory airflow is limited by:', '["Only lung volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscle strength accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Effort-independent mechanisms (lung elastic recoil and airway resistance set the maximal achievable flow)","Only the heart''s output without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only effort leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Effort-independent mechanisms (lung elastic recoil and airway resistance set the maximal achievable flow)', 'During forced expiration, flow becomes effort-independent once dynamic airway compression occurs, so maximal flow is set by lung elastic recoil and airway resistance rather than by further effort. This underlies the airflow limitation of obstructive disease.'),
('25ddf42c-0519-5d61-be40-b4c67c140f5f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, Most CO2 is transported in the blood as:', '["Carbamino compounds (23%)","Dissolved CO2 (7%)","Bicarbonate (HCO3−) (70%)","Carbonic acid","Bound to hemoglobin"]'::jsonb, 'Bicarbonate (HCO3−) (70%)', 'Roughly 70% of CO2 is carried as bicarbonate, about 23% as carbamino compounds bound to hemoglobin, and about 7% dissolved in plasma. The bicarbonate is generated in red cells and exchanged for chloride by the chloride (Hamburger) shift.'),
('baa94273-c565-58fa-8bad-ec3c9eaf64a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: What does minute ventilation (VE) equal?', '["TV / RR","RR / TV","TV × FiO2","TV + RR","TV × RR"]'::jsonb, 'TV × RR', 'Minute ventilation is the product of tidal volume and respiratory frequency (VE = VT × RR), normally about 6 L/min at rest. Alveolar ventilation is obtained by subtracting dead-space ventilation from VE.'),
('5a293756-3887-5d94-8b02-04a7c0047974'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'How is pulmonary hypertension (PH) defined, and how are its hemodynamic categories distinguished?', '["Mean PAP >20 mmHg at right heart catheterization; pre-capillary (PCWP ≤15) vs post-capillary (PCWP >15)","Mean PAP >40 mmHg at rest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Estimated by transthoracic echo only mediated by specific receptor activation","Mean PAP <15 mmHg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A systolic PAP above 10 mmHg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mean PAP >20 mmHg at right heart catheterization; pre-capillary (PCWP ≤15) vs post-capillary (PCWP >15)', 'Pulmonary hypertension is defined hemodynamically as a resting mean pulmonary artery pressure >20 mmHg confirmed by right heart catheterization. It is pre-capillary when the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is ≤15 mmHg and post-capillary when PCWP is >15 mmHg (as in left heart disease).'),
('7c38a8af-1ab7-5ac2-bd73-935d2bced33c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which set of findings is consistent with the Berlin definition of ARDS?', '["Inflammatory (non-cardiogenic) pulmonary edema: bilateral infiltrates + P/F ≤300, excluding cardiogenic causes, with diffuse alveolar damage and hyaline membranes","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema with an elevated PCWP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unilateral lung consolidation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal chest imaging with mild hypoxemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","P/F ratio >300 plus bilateral crackles leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammatory (non-cardiogenic) pulmonary edema: bilateral infiltrates + P/F ≤300, excluding cardiogenic causes, with diffuse alveolar damage and hyaline membranes', 'ARDS is acute non-cardiogenic inflammatory pulmonary edema defined by bilateral opacities, P/F ≤300 on ≥5 cmH2O PEEP, and no evidence of primary volume overload or left-heart failure. Early-phase pathology is diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes.'),
('3a83bb89-698c-5df6-b87e-07e1d9c0f51b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: A left shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve indicates:', '["Increased O2 affinity (less unloading to tissues)","More unloading of O2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rightward shift mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased P50 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased affinity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased O2 affinity (less unloading to tissues)', 'A left shift reflects increased hemoglobin affinity for oxygen (lower P50), so hemoglobin binds O2 more avidly and releases less to tissues. It is caused by opposite factors to a right shift, such as low temperature, alkalosis, and reduced 2,3-BPG.'),
('f339c557-d0ca-50bd-95f5-4ef8f8677edb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes high-frequency oscillatory ventilation (HFOV)?', '["Very small tidal volumes (1-4 mL/kg) at very high rates (3-15 Hz) maintaining recruitment while minimizing VILI","Conventional large tidal volumes at normal rates accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pressure support breaths only mediated by specific receptor activation","Zero mean airway pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Low-frequency mandatory ventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Very small tidal volumes (1-4 mL/kg) at very high rates (3-15 Hz) maintaining recruitment while minimizing VILI', 'HFOV delivers very small tidal volumes (often below dead space) at high frequencies (3-15 Hz) with a constant mean airway pressure. Gas exchange occurs by convection and diffusion, maintaining lung recruitment while minimizing cyclic stretch and ventilator-induced lung injury.'),
('0eee936e-e108-58f9-a195-c3f1f38334c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the role of extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO)?', '["Venovenous (VV) for respiratory failure: blood oxygenated externally → returned to vein; venoarterial (VA) for cardiorespiratory failure","Pulmonary artery catheterization accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A conventional mechanical ventilator mode mediated by specific receptor activation","Delivery of high-flow oxygen by mask without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intravenous fluid resuscitation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Venovenous (VV) for respiratory failure: blood oxygenated externally → returned to vein; venoarterial (VA) for cardiorespiratory failure', 'VV ECMO removes deoxygenated venous blood, oxygenates it in an external circuit, and returns it to the venous system, supporting gas exchange in severe respiratory failure (e.g., refractory ARDS). VA ECMO returns oxygenated blood to the arterial system and additionally supports cardiac output.'),
('598827cb-eb1e-5c16-847c-60369805bb3e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which statement correctly explains the Haldane effect?', '["DeoxyHb carries more CO2 as carbamino and buffers more H+ → facilitates CO2 transport from tissues","Oxyhemoglobin binds CO2 more avidly than deoxyhemoglobin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CO2 competes with O2 at the heme iron mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxygenation of Hb always decreases blood pH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deoxygenation has no effect on CO2 carriage leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DeoxyHb carries more CO2 as carbamino and buffers more H+ → facilitates CO2 transport from tissues', 'The Haldane effect describes how deoxygenated hemoglobin has a higher capacity to carry CO2 as carbaminohemoglobin and to buffer H+. At the tissues, deoxygenation therefore increases CO2 uptake, while at the lungs oxygenation releases it for excretion.'),
('d707888f-156a-53d4-94fe-833fba962213'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: Maximum expiratory airflow is limited by:', '["Only lung volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Only muscle strength accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Effort-independent mechanisms (lung elastic recoil and airway resistance set the maximal achievable flow)","Only the heart''s output without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only effort leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Effort-independent mechanisms (lung elastic recoil and airway resistance set the maximal achievable flow)', 'During forced expiration, flow becomes effort-independent once dynamic airway compression occurs, so maximal flow is set by lung elastic recoil and airway resistance rather than by further effort. This underlies the airflow limitation of obstructive disease.'),
('b4282cf6-ea13-586a-a333-a71538e8998e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following is the appropriate treatment for carbon monoxide poisoning?', '["100% normobaric O2 (CO half-life 5 h → 1 h); hyperbaric O2 shortens it to 20 min and limits neurological sequelae","Room air and observation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperbaric air at 2 ATA mediated by specific receptor activation","Methylene blue infusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exchange transfusion in all cases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '100% normobaric O2 (CO half-life 5 h → 1 h); hyperbaric O2 shortens it to 20 min and limits neurological sequelae', 'High inspired PO2 competitively displaces CO from hemoglobin, shortening its half-life from about 5 hours on room air to about 1 hour on 100% oxygen, and to 20 min with hyperbaric oxygen. Hyperbaric oxygen is used in severe poisoning to reduce neurological injury.'),
('bf5c5957-373c-5103-adce-47b37cf5ff24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following correctly describes oxygen delivery by a nasal cannula?', '["Delivers 1-6 L/min with FiO2 24-44% (each L/min raises FiO2 4%); comfortable","Delivers a fixed FiO2 independent of flow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Can deliver 100% FiO2 at low flow mediated by specific receptor activation","Requires a reservoir bag without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Provides negative airway pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Delivers 1-6 L/min with FiO2 24-44% (each L/min raises FiO2 4%); comfortable', 'A nasal cannula delivers 1-6 L/min of oxygen; each liter per minute increases inspired FiO2 by about 3-4%, giving approximately 24-44% FiO2. The actual FiO2 is variable because it depends on the patient''s inspiratory flow rate.'),
('7715789a-2209-5c92-ac74-28464e18c295'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: In a healthy adult, the normal physiological (anatomical) dead space volume is approximately:', '["1000 mL","300 mL","500 mL","150 mL","50 mL"]'::jsonb, '150 mL', 'The dead space is the portion of each breath that does not participate in gas exchange, about 150 mL (≈2 mL/kg) in adults, nearly all of it anatomical in health. Because of the alveolar gas equation, PaCO2 falls when dead space is bypassed by intubation.'),
('eb46ef13-9d65-5b33-953d-f79c1d70118f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, A 70-year-old with COPD has increasing morning headache and confusion. PaCO2 is primarily determined by which relationship?', '["Dead space only resulting in decreased functional activity","Tidal volume only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar ventilation (VA) and CO2 production (VCO2); PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA","Respiratory rate alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 consumption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Alveolar ventilation (VA) and CO2 production (VCO2); PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA', 'PaCO2 is set by the ratio of CO2 production to alveolar ventilation: PaCO2 ∝ VCO2/VA. In COPD, reduced alveolar ventilation from dead-space load and poor ventilation raises PaCO2, contributing to morning headache from overnight CO2 retention.'),
('cd700d08-8de7-5dc8-9e31-4b98eb98a774'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: The restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing shows:', '["↓FEV1/FVC resulting in decreased functional activity","↑TLC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↓TLC, FEV1/FVC normal or ↑, and ↓FVC","Normal pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstructive pattern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↓TLC, FEV1/FVC normal or ↑, and ↓FVC', 'Restrictive disease lowers total lung capacity and vital capacity while the FEV1/FVC ratio is preserved or even increased. This pattern is seen in pulmonary fibrosis, chest wall restriction, and neuromuscular weakness, and differs from the low ratio of obstruction.'),
('fd791e50-d822-5453-982a-f78c55dfc106'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following correctly describes a Venturi mask?', '["High-flow device delivering precise FiO2 (24-50%) via the Bernoulli principle; used in COPD to avoid O2-induced hypercapnia","A low-flow device with variable FiO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Delivers 100% FiO2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Provides oxygen only in children without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Delivers FiO2 that is not adjustable leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High-flow device delivering precise FiO2 (24-50%) via the Bernoulli principle; used in COPD to avoid O2-induced hypercapnia', 'The Venturi mask entrains a fixed proportion of room air via the Bernoulli/Venturi effect, delivering a precise and stable FiO2 (usually 24-50%) even at high inspiratory flows. This accuracy is important in CO2 retainers with COPD to avoid oxygen-induced hypercapnia.'),
('a394cae9-3569-5ad0-937b-f247679aedc4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (NPPV)?', '["Positive pressure ventilation via a mask (CPAP or BiPAP), avoiding intubation, used in COPD exacerbation with hypercapnic failure","Invasive ventilation through an endotracheal tube accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oxygen by nasal cannula only mediated by specific receptor activation","High-flow nasal oxygen only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mechanical ventilation requiring a tracheostomy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Positive pressure ventilation via a mask (CPAP or BiPAP), avoiding intubation, used in COPD exacerbation with hypercapnic failure', 'NPPV delivers ventilatory support through a tight-fitting mask without an artificial airway, providing pressure support (BiPAP) or continuous pressure (CPAP). It is first-line for acute hypercapnic respiratory failure in COPD exacerbation and cardiogenic pulmonary edema, reducing intubation and mortality.'),
('5b363a3a-6880-5df6-b09e-7e50e144a85b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Where does the majority of CO2 conversion to bicarbonate occur in the body?', '["Endothelium resulting in decreased functional activity","Plasma only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RBCs (carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO2 + H2O ↔ H2CO3 ↔ HCO3− + H+)","Alveoli without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidney leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'RBCs (carbonic anhydrase catalyzes CO2 + H2O ↔ H2CO3 ↔ HCO3− + H+)', 'Carbonic anhydrase, concentrated inside red blood cells, rapidly hydrates CO2 to carbonic acid, which dissociates to HCO3− and H+. This makes the RBC the principal site where CO2 is converted to bicarbonate during transport.'),
('bbe73b59-4019-5570-882c-93a8e6af3ebc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, The minimal total work of breathing occurs at:', '["A normal respiratory rate (12-16/min) with a normal tidal volume (500 mL) — the optimal balance between elastic and resistive work","All breathing patterns equally accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A very high rate with large tidal volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Very rapid shallow breathing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very slow deep breathing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A normal respiratory rate (12-16/min) with a normal tidal volume (500 mL) — the optimal balance between elastic and resistive work', 'Fast shallow breathing minimizes elastic work but increases resistive work, whereas slow deep breaths do the reverse. The body selects the rate that minimizes total work, normally about 12-16 breaths/min with a tidal volume near 500 mL.'),
('0014c213-4f24-530a-9848-00b794eb43c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: In arterial blood, oxygen is transported mostly as:', '["97% bound to hemoglobin (oxyhemoglobin)","Entirely dissolved in plasma","50% bound to Hb and 50% dissolved","25% bound and 75% dissolved","70% bound to Hb and 30% dissolved"]'::jsonb, '97% bound to hemoglobin (oxyhemoglobin)', 'Only about 3% of arterial oxygen is physically dissolved; the remaining 97% is carried bound to hemoglobin as oxyhemoglobin. This is why severe anemia can reduce oxygen content markedly even when PaO2 is normal.'),
('6f92dd18-6bcd-530e-9509-a9c5739dd872'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: Which statement about the forms of CO2 transport in blood is correct?', '["Almost all CO2 is carried as carbaminohemoglobin resulting in decreased functional activity","CO2 is carried entirely dissolved in plasma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","About 7-10% of CO2 is transported dissolved in plasma, with the remainder bound to Hb or carried as bicarbonate","CO2 is carried only as carbonic acid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CO2 is carried only as bicarbonate within the RBC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'About 7-10% of CO2 is transported dissolved in plasma, with the remainder bound to Hb or carried as bicarbonate', 'CO2 is carried in three forms: roughly 70% as bicarbonate (formed in the RBC and chloride-shifted into plasma), about 23% as carbamino compounds (mainly on hemoglobin), and about 7-10% dissolved. No single form accounts for all of transport.'),
('c8cb8045-2c01-5fde-b9ad-d88c11dd4883'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, The FEV1/FVC ratio is normally:', '["0.3-0.5",">1.0","0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)","0.5","0.9-1.0"]'::jsonb, '0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)', 'In healthy adults FEV1 is about 75-85% of FVC (ratio ≥0.7). Values below 0.7 indicate obstructive airflow limitation, whereas restrictive disease preserves or even raises the ratio while lowering FVC.'),
('d88b424b-983f-5681-9c8f-48bfe19469d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the goal of inspiratory muscle training (using a threshold loading device)?', '["Strength training for inspiratory muscles, improving maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP) and exercise tolerance","Training of the expiratory muscles accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Immediate bronchodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Replacing bronchodilator therapy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increasing lung compliance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Strength training for inspiratory muscles, improving maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP) and exercise tolerance', 'Inspiratory muscle training uses a threshold-loading device that the patient must overcome on each inspiration, strengthening the diaphragm and accessory muscles. It raises maximal inspiratory pressure and improves dyspnea and exercise tolerance, particularly in weak or hyperinflated patients.'),
('278c7c56-dde7-5898-a7ee-d4eb4919eb10'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following correctly describes measures of respiratory muscle strength?', '["MIP (maximal inspiratory pressure) reflects inspiratory strength; MEP (maximal expiratory pressure) reflects expiratory strength; SNIP (sniff nasal inspiratory pressure) is a related test","Only spirometric flow rates are used accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only diffusing capacity is measured mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only lung volumes are measured without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only airway resistance is measured leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'MIP (maximal inspiratory pressure) reflects inspiratory strength; MEP (maximal expiratory pressure) reflects expiratory strength; SNIP (sniff nasal inspiratory pressure) is a related test', 'Respiratory muscle strength is assessed by maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP) and maximal expiratory pressure (MEP) measured against an occluded airway, along with sniff nasal inspiratory pressure (SNIP). A MIP less negative than about -20 cmH2O indicates inspiratory weakness and predicts weaning difficulty.'),
('7327fd04-e86f-526f-9f9d-94859018abc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: The normal VD/VT ratio is:', '["0.2-0.3 (20-30%)","0.5-0.6","<0.1","0.7-0.8","0.1-0.15"]'::jsonb, '0.2-0.3 (20-30%)', 'The normal dead space fraction of the tidal volume is 0.2-0.3 (20-30%). Because dead-space ventilation is wasted, an elevated VD/VT ratio impairs CO2 elimination and reflects increased alveolar dead space.'),
('b0ba92ab-4384-52f9-8f16-98be46399289'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: A 35-year-old recently traveled to high altitude and presents with headache and dyspnea. The expected arterial blood gas pattern is:', '["↑A-a gradient with hypercapnia","Right shift of the dissociation curve","Normal blood gases","Hypoxemia with ↓PaCO2 due to hyperventilation","Hypercapnic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia with ↓PaCO2 due to hyperventilation', 'At high altitude the inspired PO2 is low, producing hypoxemia, but the resulting hypoxic ventilatory drive overventilates the patient, lowering PaCO2. The A-a gradient is normal because the defect is a low inspired O2 rather than lung disease.'),
('42a32024-d5fa-5ec3-8209-b62939a860f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, The static pressure-volume curve of the lung shows:', '["Only a deflation curve resulting in decreased functional activity","A linear relationship over all volumes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hysteresis: inflation and deflation follow different curves, and compliance is lower at very low and very high lung volumes","Compliance that increases steadily with volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Identical inflation and deflation pressures leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hysteresis: inflation and deflation follow different curves, and compliance is lower at very low and very high lung volumes', 'Because surface tension and tissue forces differ during inflation versus deflation, the P-V curve shows hysteresis, with deflation resting above inflation. Compliance is greatest mid-range and falls at both very low and very high volumes (the sigmoid shape).'),
('279cccae-d516-5c10-9350-2ed3670ed0b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: The alveolar gas equation is:', '["PIO2 × R resulting in decreased functional activity","R × PaCO2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PaO2 − PaCO2 mediated by specific receptor activation","PIO2 + PaCO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/R) (+ a small correction)"]'::jsonb, 'PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/R) (+ a small correction)', 'The alveolar gas equation calculates PAO2 from the inspired PO2 minus the oxygen consumed during CO2 production: PAO2 = PIO2 − (PaCO2/R). It is used to derive the A-a gradient and detect impaired gas exchange.'),
('39a93bab-8abc-5647-b628-1787c453c972'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A postoperative patient develops hypoxemia from collapse of lung units after upper abdominal surgery. Which mechanism best explains this?', '["Perioperative atelectasis producing V/Q mismatch and hypoxemia, common after abdominal/thoracic surgery","Acute pulmonary embolism accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spontaneous pneumothorax mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Perioperative atelectasis producing V/Q mismatch and hypoxemia, common after abdominal/thoracic surgery', 'After upper abdominal or thoracic surgery, pain and splinting reduce sighing and deep breaths, causing alveolar collapse (atelectasis), which produces V/Q mismatch and hypoxemia. Incentive spirometry and early mobilization prevent and reverse it.'),
('e5ac13e7-1653-57c1-8d62-4b02d4cb23ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Why do hypercapnia and hypoxemia develop in cardiogenic or hemorrhagic shock?', '["Hypoperfusion (shock) → ↓respiratory muscle perfusion → ↓VE → hypercapnia + hypoxemia","Primary diffusion abnormality of the alveolar membrane accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pulmonary venous thrombosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of pulmonary surfactant without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated bronchoconstriction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoperfusion (shock) → ↓respiratory muscle perfusion → ↓VE → hypercapnia + hypoxemia', 'In cardiogenic or hemorrhagic shock, reduced perfusion of respiratory muscles impairs their oxygen supply and may reduce ventilation, leading to CO2 retention while hypoperfusion and impaired gas exchange produce hypoxemia. This is an example of type II failure of perfusion-related muscle failure rather than primary lung disease.'),
('bea5db0f-8f07-527c-bae1-cf44799bf07c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the physiologic shunt (Qs/Qt) and its characteristic feature?', '["Blood bypassing ventilated alveoli, producing hypoxemia that does NOT correct with 100% O2; normally <5%","Blood flowing through high V/Q regions accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypoxemia that corrects with 100% oxygen mediated by specific receptor activation","Dead space ventilation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventilation in excess of perfusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blood bypassing ventilated alveoli, producing hypoxemia that does NOT correct with 100% O2; normally <5%', 'Shunt is the fraction of cardiac output reaching systemic blood without passing through ventilated alveoli. Because shunted (mixed venous) blood never sees alveolar oxygen, the resulting hypoxemia cannot be corrected by breathing 100% O2 — unlike V/Q mismatch, which does correct.'),
('9d3ce46a-30b3-5c60-a5aa-4a8d41d8abca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Sudden cessation of breathing that mandates immediate airway support is termed:', '["Hypercapnia","Respiratory muscle fatigue","Airway protection","Apnea","Tachypnea"]'::jsonb, 'Apnea', 'Apnea is the complete cessation of respiratory airflow, an absolute indication for immediate airway management and ventilatory support. The other terms describe conditions predisposing to or resulting from respiratory failure rather than the absence of breathing itself.'),
('387971a8-621e-536d-8174-f4445168a419'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Physiologic dead space (VDphys) equals:', '["Tidal volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Anatomical dead space only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Residual volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar dead space only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anatomical dead space + alveolar dead space (VDanat + VDalv)"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical dead space + alveolar dead space (VDanat + VDalv)', 'Physiological dead space is the sum of anatomical dead space (conducting airways) and alveolar dead space (ventilated but unperfused alveoli). In health alveolar dead space is negligible, so physiologic and anatomical dead space are nearly equal (150 mL).'),
('2b8a21a3-1b3e-5c81-8b57-b62bd2a78531'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, A rightward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve (e.g., from increased 2,3-BPG, H+, temperature, or CO2):', '["Promotes O2 loading in the lungs; caused by ↓pH","Promotes O2 unloading to the tissues; caused by ↑H+ (Bohr effect)","Decreases O2 delivery by left-shifting the curve","Increases O2 affinity and reduces unloading","Has no effect on O2 delivery"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes O2 unloading to the tissues; caused by ↑H+ (Bohr effect)', 'A right shift lowers hemoglobin''s O2 affinity (higher P50), so oxygen is released to the tissues more easily, particularly in the acidic, hypercapnic, warm exercising muscle (Bohr effect). It aids tissue oxygenation at the expense of slightly less lung loading.'),
('bc8b7623-1c94-5efb-b6fb-3d66ae047a4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: The normal FEV1/FVC ratio is:', '["0.3-0.5","0.5-0.6","0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)","1.0","0.9-1.0"]'::jsonb, '0.75-0.85 (≥0.7)', 'In healthy adults, FEV1 is 75-85% of the forced vital capacity, with a lower limit of normal near 0.7. A ratio below 0.7 indicates airflow obstruction, typical of asthma or COPD.'),
('809f578d-93f3-5943-8563-cd929178c845'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'How is a spontaneous breathing trial (SBT) performed?', '["T-piece (disconnected from the ventilator) or low-level pressure support/CPAP for 30-120 min to assess weaning readiness","A single forced vital capacity maneuver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximum voluntary ventilation for 12 s mediated by specific receptor activation","An arterial blood gas drawn at rest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Immediate extubation without a trial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'T-piece (disconnected from the ventilator) or low-level pressure support/CPAP for 30-120 min to assess weaning readiness', 'An SBT allows the patient to breathe without meaningful ventilator assistance, via aT-piece or minimal pressure support/CPAP, for 30-120 minutes. Tolerance of the trial (stable gas exchange and hemodynamics, no distress) predicts successful extubation.'),
('cc31c7a8-69ab-513b-8fbe-ed3c828da524'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, The FEV1 improves after an inhaled bronchodilator in:', '["Fibrosis","Normal lung","Asthma (reversible obstruction)","Emphysema (irreversible)","Bronchiectasis with scarring"]'::jsonb, 'Asthma (reversible obstruction)', 'A brisk improvement in FEV1 after bronchodilator indicates reversible airflow obstruction, the defining feature of asthma. In emphysema and fixed COPD the obstruction is largely irreversible, though a small degree of reversibility is sometimes seen.'),
('d0d99763-3b6a-5ea4-aa20-6197ded79b84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following is an advantage of a tracheostomy over a translaryngeal endotracheal tube?', '["Lower airway resistance (shorter and relatively larger tube)","Increases dead space","Increases the risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia","Requires the patient to be awake","Prevents all airway secretions"]'::jsonb, 'Lower airway resistance (shorter and relatively larger tube)', 'A tracheostomy is shorter and wider than a translaryngeal ETT, reducing airway resistance and work of breathing, and it bypasses the upper-airway dead space, lowering dead-space ventilation. Other advantages include reduced VAP risk, easier suctioning, and better oral hygiene.'),
('caecc641-e1ca-569e-b358-7a9ff1a9ba62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, Diffusion impairment causes significant hypoxemia especially during:', '["Supine position resulting in decreased functional activity","Rest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Exercise (reduced capillary transit time)","Breath holding without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sleep leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Exercise (reduced capillary transit time)', 'At rest the 0.75-second capillary transit time usually suffices for equilibration even with a thickened membrane. During exercise, the shorter transit time may not allow full equilibration, so diffusion-limited hypoxemia becomes evident with exertion.'),
('f1e71405-7e11-5e19-a3dc-ac272044719a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A patient with apparent asthma has normal spirometry and exhibits inspiratory stridor. Which condition most likely explains this picture?', '["Paradoxical adduction of the vocal cords during inspiration → inspiratory stridor (vocal cord dysfunction)","Bronchospasm with wheezing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Expiratory stridor from subglottic stenosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Silent aspiration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastroesophageal reflux without airway signs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paradoxical adduction of the vocal cords during inspiration → inspiratory stridor (vocal cord dysfunction)', 'Vocal cord dysfunction (paradoxical vocal fold motion) is the inappropriate adduction of the vocal cords during inspiration, producing inspiratory stridor and dyspnea that mimics asthma but with normal spirometry between episodes. It does not respond to bronchodilators.'),
('bef3e512-34e9-57ea-9215-c8f44f06754e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: In carbon monoxide poisoning, the pathophysiology includes:', '["CO binds Hb with about 250× the affinity of O2 → ↓O2 capacity and a left shift of the ODC (impaired unloading) → tissue hypoxia","Right shift of the ODC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased O2 capacity mediated by specific receptor activation","No significant effect on O2 transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreased CO affinity for Hb leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CO binds Hb with about 250× the affinity of O2 → ↓O2 capacity and a left shift of the ODC (impaired unloading) → tissue hypoxia', 'CO competitively binds hemoglobin at 250 times the affinity of O2, reducing oxygen-carrying capacity and left-shifting the dissociation curve so that remaining oxygen is unloaded poorly. The result is tissue hypoxia out of proportion to the measured PaO2.'),
('cb99015f-647f-5961-87c7-fde714f07791'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In an obese patient with respiratory symptoms, weight loss produces which respiratory mechanical improvement?', '["Weight loss → ↑chest wall compliance","Weight loss → ↓chest wall compliance","Weight loss → ↓FVC with more restriction","Weight loss → ↑A-a gradient","Weight loss → ↓ERV with air trapping"]'::jsonb, 'Weight loss → ↑chest wall compliance', 'In obesity, the chest wall is loaded by adipose tissue, reducing chest wall and total respiratory compliance. Sustained weight loss decreases this load and increases chest wall compliance, improving lung volumes (especially ERV) and reducing dyspnea.'),
('f6f187bc-97fe-5294-a55d-807ac806c5a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: The diffusing capacity of the lung (DLCO) measures:', '["Ventilation resulting in decreased functional activity","Perfusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Transfer of CO across the alveolar-capillary membrane (CO diffuses similarly to O2)","CO2 diffusion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","O2 diffusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer of CO across the alveolar-capillary membrane (CO diffuses similarly to O2)', 'DLCO quantifies how readily gas moves from the alveoli into the blood, estimated using carbon monoxide because its uptake is limited by diffusion rather than perfusion. It is reduced in emphysema, fibrosis, and anemia.'),
('5ab2ba92-8691-5826-bbbd-b64e2c3a617f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, On spirometry, restrictive lung disease characteristically shows:', '["Reduced vital capacity with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio (peak flow preserved relative to lung volume)","A reduced FEV1/FVC ratio below 0.7 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased total lung capacity mediated by specific receptor activation","A concave expiratory loop without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Air trapping with increased RV leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced vital capacity with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio (peak flow preserved relative to lung volume)', 'Restriction shrinks all lung volumes, lowering FVC and TLC, while the FEV1/FVC ratio stays normal or even rises because emptying is not disproportionately slow. This distinguishes it from obstruction, where the ratio falls.'),
('a06c87cb-66ac-5967-986f-1516e0f15459'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: The normal pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, a key Starling force in the lung, is approximately:', '["8-10 mmHg","75 mmHg (systemic capillary pressure)","120 mmHg (systemic arterial)","0 mmHg (no hydrostatic gradient)","50 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '8-10 mmHg', 'The pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure is only about 8-10 mmHg because pulmonary vascular resistance and pressures are low. This low pressure, together with plasma oncotic pressure (25 mmHg) and lymphatic drainage, keeps the alveoli normally dry.'),
('c6477c2f-bbfc-5587-ab3f-e09cdd38121d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: Closing capacity (CC) equals:', '["CV + RV","TLC","TV","VC","FRC"]'::jsonb, 'CV + RV', 'Closing capacity is the lung volume at which airway closure begins, computed as closing volume plus residual volume (CC = CV + RV). Clinically, one asks whether CC encroaches on FRC, which happens with aging and in smokers.'),
('33696fa4-6ac6-5628-9cbd-2ec522fbbe44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the primary mechanism of pulmonary oxygen toxicity at high FiO2?', '["Reactive oxygen species (ROS) directly injuring cells and causing inflammation; tracheobronchitis → ARDS → fibrosis; risk rises with FiO2 >0.5 for >24-48 h","Nitrogen narcosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carbon dioxide retention alone mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of surfactant without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduced mitochondrial function only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reactive oxygen species (ROS) directly injuring cells and causing inflammation; tracheobronchitis → ARDS → fibrosis; risk rises with FiO2 >0.5 for >24-48 h', 'High inspired PO2 increases the production of reactive oxygen species that overwhelm antioxidant defenses, causing tracheobronchitis and alveolar injury that can progress to ARDS and fibrosis. The risk rises with FiO2 >0.5 for more than 24-48 hours, so clinicians use the lowest FiO2 that maintains safe saturation.'),
('321b817a-5164-530c-9e4f-2b82ce7f9212'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following correctly describes the mechanism of retinopathy of prematurity (ROP)?', '["O2-induced vasoconstriction → vaso-obliteration of developing retinal vessels → subsequent neovascularization → retinal detachment","Intraocular infection in the newborn accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital cataract formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Inadequate surfactant production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retinal arterial embolism in adults leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'O2-induced vasoconstriction → vaso-obliteration of developing retinal vessels → subsequent neovascularization → retinal detachment', 'Excess supplemental oxygen in premature infants causes retinal arteriolar vasoconstriction and vaso-obliteration of the developing vessels; subsequent relative hypoxia drives uncontrolled neovascularization that can bleed, scar, and lead to retinal detachment and blindness.'),
('fbaf73b7-825a-53cb-bccc-d2800c255e4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the effect of positive-pressure ventilation on cardiac function?', '["↓Venous return (↑intrathoracic pressure) → ↓RV preload → ↓LV preload → ↓CO, more pronounced in hypovolemia","Increases venous return accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases left ventricular stroke volume directly mediated by specific receptor activation","No effect on cardiac output without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Raises cardiac output in hypovolemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↓Venous return (↑intrathoracic pressure) → ↓RV preload → ↓LV preload → ↓CO, more pronounced in hypovolemia', 'Positive intrathoracic pressure compresses the great veins and raises right atrial pressure, reducing venous return and RV preload, which in sequence lowers LV preload and cardiac output. This effect is greatest in hypovolemic states, and it is the basis of the physiologic pulse-pressure variation.'),
('35210ca1-d52d-55e9-8908-5b729cc4c71d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What are pulse pressure variation (PPV) and stroke volume variation (SVV) used for?', '["Dynamic indices of fluid responsiveness during mechanical ventilation; PPV >12-15% suggests the patient is likely fluid responsive","Static CVP-guided assessment of volume status accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Estimates of dead space mediated by specific receptor activation","Measures of vasopressor need only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diagnostic markers of sepsis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dynamic indices of fluid responsiveness during mechanical ventilation; PPV >12-15% suggests the patient is likely fluid responsive', 'In mechanically ventilated, curarized patients, positive-pressure breaths cyclically reduce preload; a large resulting variation in pulse pressure or stroke volume (PPV/SVV >12-15%) indicates the patient is on the steep part of the Frank-Starling curve and is likely to respond to a fluid bolus. Static CVP is a poor predictor.'),
('4162e9d4-610e-5aca-a2ee-ea337c2be354'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'How is the passive leg raise (PLR) test used, and what does a positive response indicate?', '["Legs raised to 30-45° increase venous return; if cardiac output rises >10%, the patient is fluid responsive","Legs raised to test venous thrombosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A test requiring vasopressor withdrawal mediated by specific receptor activation","An unreliable static assessment of CVP without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A maneuver that always lowers cardiac output leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Legs raised to 30-45° increase venous return; if cardiac output rises >10%, the patient is fluid responsive', 'Passive leg raising transiently autotransfuses 150-300 mL of blood from the legs into the central circulation, effectively acting as an endogenous fluid challenge. A rise in cardiac output of >10% within the maneuver predicts that subsequent fluid boluses will also increase cardiac output.'),
('e17d9a41-80ff-56b1-aa9c-d3537b1973bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes the concept of fluid responsiveness?', '["Only about 50% of hemodynamically unstable patients are fluid responsive (CO rises with a fluid bolus); dynamic indices predict response better than static CVP","All unstable patients respond to fluid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Static CVP is the best predictor of response mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluid responsiveness can be predicted with certainty without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cardiac output never changes with fluid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Only about 50% of hemodynamically unstable patients are fluid responsive (CO rises with a fluid bolus); dynamic indices predict response better than static CVP', 'Only about half of hemodynamically unstable patients increase their cardiac output in response to a fluid challenge; the rest are preload-independent. Dynamic parameters (PPV, SVV, PLR) predict this response far better than static measurements such as CVP.'),
('76c678b5-938f-5e2d-ba30-96100a564b27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What are the physiologic events in acute cor pulmonale caused by a massive pulmonary embolism?', '["Acute PE → ↑PAP → RV pressure overload → RV dilation → ↓RV CO → interventricular septum shifts left → ↓LV filling → ↓CO; signs include distended neck veins","Pulmonary vasodilation with normal RV afterload accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Right ventricular contractility increases without strain mediated by specific receptor activation","The septum shifts right, increasing LV filling without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No change in RV afterload leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acute PE → ↑PAP → RV pressure overload → RV dilation → ↓RV CO → interventricular septum shifts left → ↓LV filling → ↓CO; signs include distended neck veins', 'A massive PE abruptly raises pulmonary vascular resistance, causing acute RV pressure overload and dilation; the interventricular septum shifts leftward, impairing LV filling, while reduced RV output and the high PAP lower LV preload and cardiac output. Clinical signs reflect RV failure (distended neck veins, RV heave, tricuspid regurgitation).'),
('de09be3a-1f95-5843-9b65-cde5c2047b45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH)?', '["Organized PE → chronic obstruction of pulmonary arteries → ↑PVR → progressive PH → RV failure; a treatable cause of pulmonary hypertension","An acute single embolic event accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Congenital absence of the pulmonary artery mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary hypertension from left-heart disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diffuse alveolar damage from ARDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Organized PE → chronic obstruction of pulmonary arteries → ↑PVR → progressive PH → RV failure; a treatable cause of pulmonary hypertension', 'CTEPH arises when unresolved pulmonary emboli organize into chronic obstructive thromboembolic material in the proximal pulmonary arteries, causing progressive pulmonary hypertension and eventually right heart failure. It is the only potentially curable form of pulmonary hypertension (via pulmonary thromboendarterectomy).'),
('091cab5c-6ed9-517c-8426-88b49507e41e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: Arterial hypoxemia (low PaO2) can cause:', '["Cyanosis if deoxyHb > 5 g/dL","Reduced O2 content","Stimulation of peripheral chemoreceptors","All of the above","Pallor"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Low PaO2 lowers arterial O2 content and, when reduced hemoglobin exceeds about 5 g/dL, produces cyanosis. It also stimulates the carotid and aortic bodies, which drive the hypoxic ventilatory response, especially when PaO2 is below about 60 mmHg.'),
('3d73f7c5-e330-5bd8-b1bf-7f1965366074'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which histologic pattern characterizes idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF)?', '["Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) with honeycombing, associated with traction bronchiectasis and poor prognosis","Non-caseating granulomas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alveolar filling with proteinaceous material mediated by specific receptor activation","Reversible bronchial inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Silica-induced nodular fibrosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) with honeycombing, associated with traction bronchiectasis and poor prognosis', 'IPF is an idiopathic progressive fibrosing ILD histologically and radiologically showing usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP): patchy fibrosis, fibroblastic foci, and honeycombing with traction bronchiectasis. It is largely irreversible and carries a poor prognosis.'),
('39cb338e-aa5c-59b5-b8b1-e54f6f7be519'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, A 30-year-old with pulmonary embolism develops sudden dyspnea and evidence of increased dead space ventilation. The most likely explanation is:', '["Shunt resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal V/Q accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Low V/Q without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ventilation without perfusion (wasted ventilation)"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation without perfusion (wasted ventilation)', 'A pulmonary embolus occludes a pulmonary artery, so ventilated alveoli receive no blood flow (V/Q approaches infinity). This creates alveolar dead space and wasted ventilation, raising physiologic dead space and widening the A-a gradient.'),
('a8090781-a6ac-5bd6-a345-79613c1ea833'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: The equal pressure point (EPP) is the:', '["Point along the airway where intraluminal pressure equals pleural pressure; downstream, airways are compressed","Point that exists only during inspiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Point that exists only during relaxed breathing mediated by specific receptor activation","Point that exists only in the bronchi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Point never reached in normal subjects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Point along the airway where intraluminal pressure equals pleural pressure; downstream, airways are compressed', 'During forced expiration, upstream airway pressure falls until it equals pleural pressure at the equal pressure point. Beyond this point the airways are dynamically compressed, which is why maximum expiratory flow cannot be increased by greater effort.'),
('05601e4d-dbf4-56d0-b28b-e34ac2fe3dd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the most common postoperative pulmonary complication?', '["Atelectasis","Pulmonary embolism","Aspiration pneumonia","Pleural effusion","Bronchopleural fistula"]'::jsonb, 'Atelectasis', 'Atelectasis is the most frequent pulmonary complication after surgery, particularly after upper abdominal and thoracic operations, due to splinting, hypoventilation, and retained secretions. It produces fever and hypoxemia and is prevented with early mobilization and incentive spirometry.'),
('6804b877-4b11-5b74-83ab-d3bbc590feb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What does the 6-minute walk distance (6MWD) measure, and what finding indicates a poor prognosis?', '["Distance walked in 6 minutes (normal 400-600 m by age/sex); a fall in SpO2 ≥4% during the test indicates poor prognosis","Total lung capacity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maximal oxygen uptake mediated by specific receptor activation","Static compliance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peak flow variability leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distance walked in 6 minutes (normal 400-600 m by age/sex); a fall in SpO2 ≥4% during the test indicates poor prognosis', 'The 6MWD quantifies functional exercise capacity and is widely used in pulmonary hypertension and chronic lung disease. Desaturation of ≥4% during the walk indicates significant V/Q deterioration and predicts worse outcomes, while a longer distance indicates better functional status.'),
('e502ae4c-1db8-5b9c-9369-7694a9024c04'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes the incremental (shuttle) walk test?', '["An incremental, externally paced walking test with progressively faster beeps, providing a maximal estimate of VO2max","A non-incremental constant-pace test accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A test identical to the 6-minute walk mediated by specific receptor activation","A submaximal test without pacing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A test that measures only desaturation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An incremental, externally paced walking test with progressively faster beeps, providing a maximal estimate of VO2max', 'The shuttle walk test requires the patient to walk between two markers at a speed dictated by progressively shortening audio beeps, so it is externally paced and incremental, driving most patients to a maximal, symptom-limited effort. It provides a better estimate of VO2max than the self-paced 6MWT.'),
('b951743c-c141-54e1-9336-82f21288901c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, Arterial gas embolism (AGE):', '["Gas bubbles in the arterial circulation causing stroke or loss of consciousness, often from pulmonary barotrauma (breath-holding during ascent)","Venous bubbles that are always harmless accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A condition that is never serious mediated by specific receptor activation","Only local skin changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Caused by oxygen toxicity alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gas bubbles in the arterial circulation causing stroke or loss of consciousness, often from pulmonary barotrauma (breath-holding during ascent)', 'If a diver ascends while breath-holding, expanding intrapulmonary gas can rupture alveoli and force bubbles into the pulmonary veins and left heart. These arterial gas emboli travel to the brain and cause stroke-like deficits or loss of consciousness; immediate hyperbaric recompression is required.'),
('975ccac2-6ba1-5927-a6d9-e12b4dea21b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which parameter is the single best overall indicator of cardiorespiratory fitness obtained during CPET?', '["VO2max (maximal oxygen uptake)","Peak heart rate","Resting minute ventilation","VCO2 at rest","Oxygen pulse at rest"]'::jsonb, 'VO2max (maximal oxygen uptake)', 'VO2max, the highest rate of oxygen uptake reached during maximal incremental exercise, is the accepted single measurement of cardiorespiratory fitness. It integrates cardiac output, oxygen extraction, and ventilation, and is the benchmark used to grade fitness and disability.'),
('e35e6a5f-8a0a-5070-a62e-db36c7bdc711'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the anaerobic threshold (AT) detected during cardiopulmonary exercise testing?', '["The VO2 where lactate rises so that the VCO2/VO2 ratio increases (V-slope method); normally >40% of predicted VO2max and reduced in heart failure","The peak heart rate reached accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The respiratory exchange ratio below 1 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","The highest VO2 obtained without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A value unrelated to lactate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The VO2 where lactate rises so that the VCO2/VO2 ratio increases (V-slope method); normally >40% of predicted VO2max and reduced in heart failure', 'The anaerobic (ventilatory) threshold is the VO2 above which anaerobic lactate production rises; buffering of lactate generates extra CO2 that increases VCO2 relative to VO2 (V-slope). A normal AT exceeds 40% of predicted VO2max and is characteristically reduced in heart failure and deconditioning.'),
('0788a5eb-09e5-5156-b6ab-5bc3e493f893'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: Alveolar ventilation (VA) equals:', '["TV × RR (minute ventilation)","VE − VD only","IC × RR","VT × RR − (VD × RR)","FRC × RR"]'::jsonb, 'VT × RR − (VD × RR)', 'Alveolar ventilation is the minute ventilation minus the ventilation wasted on dead space: VA = (VT − VD) × RR = VT × RR − VD × RR. Because PaCO2 is inversely proportional to VA, doubling VA halves PaCO2.'),
('f8c24681-fc28-57f3-b0dd-fd200084f070'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What does the oxygen uptake efficiency slope (OUES) reflect?', '["The slope of VO2 versus log ventilation (VE), reflecting ventilatory efficiency and cardiovascular function; it is constant across exercise intensity and less effort-dependent than VO2max","The ratio of HR to VO2 at rest accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The peak minute ventilation mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A static measure of dead space without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A value that rises steeply only at peak effort leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The slope of VO2 versus log ventilation (VE), reflecting ventilatory efficiency and cardiovascular function; it is constant across exercise intensity and less effort-dependent than VO2max', 'OUES is the linear slope relating oxygen uptake to the logarithm of minute ventilation across submaximal and maximal exercise. An abnormal (flat) slope reflects cardiopulmonary inefficiency, and because it is effort-independent it is useful when the patient cannot achieve a true maximal effort.'),
('53bff03d-8bb3-599d-978b-b648b7b11c92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'How does tidal volume (VT) change during incremental exercise?', '["VT increases from 0.5 L to 2-3 L at peak by using the inspiratory reserve volume; further increases come from the respiratory rate once VT approaches 60% of VC","VT always stays constant accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VT falls as exercise intensifies mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only the respiratory rate changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","VT depends only on the dead space leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VT increases from 0.5 L to 2-3 L at peak by using the inspiratory reserve volume; further increases come from the respiratory rate once VT approaches 60% of VC', 'During exercise, tidal volume rises progressively, drawing on the inspiratory reserve volume to reach 2-3 L; once VT approaches about 50-60% of vital capacity, further increases in minute ventilation come mainly from a faster respiratory rate. This maximizes alveolar ventilation efficiently.'),
('9d02b782-b131-5886-89be-3f04658263da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following is the primary parenchymal change underlying the altered lung mechanics seen with aging?', '["Increased chest wall compliance","Reduced surfactant production","Loss of elastic recoil from the aging lung","Increased alveolar surface area","Reduced airway tone only"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of elastic recoil from the aging lung', 'Aging reduces lung elastic recoil and increases lung compliance, while chest wall compliance falls with age. These changes (sometimes called ''senile emphysema'') lead to increased RV and functional residual capacity, premature airway closure, and a decline in FEV1 and gas exchange.'),
('6158b195-ec7d-590c-9860-4e7bb77eff52'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the most important spirometric benefit of smoking cessation in a patient with established COPD?', '["Slows the rate of FEV1 decline to the normal age-related rate (25-30 mL/year vs 60 mL/year in continuing smokers) with some improvement in cough","Restores lung function to normal immediately accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stops all further FEV1 loss entirely mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases the rate of FEV1 decline without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Eliminates the need for bronchodilator therapy leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Slows the rate of FEV1 decline to the normal age-related rate (25-30 mL/year vs 60 mL/year in continuing smokers) with some improvement in cough', 'The chief benefit of smoking cessation in COPD is that FEV1 decline slows to the normal age-related trajectory (about 25-30 mL/year) instead of the accelerated 60 mL/year seen in continuing smokers. It also reduces cough and sputum, although established airflow obstruction does not reverse.'),
('cc3d2e6c-c30f-5714-a595-6b905546f847'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What does the BODE index combine to predict in COPD, and how is it scored?', '["A prognostic score in COPD combining BMI (B), Obstruction (FEV1, O), Dyspnea (mMRC, D) and Exercise capacity (6MWD, E); a higher score predicts worse survival","A weaning score based on the RSBI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A grading score for asthma severity mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","A pneumonia severity score (CURB-65) without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A pulmonary arterial pressure measurement leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A prognostic score in COPD combining BMI (B), Obstruction (FEV1, O), Dyspnea (mMRC, D) and Exercise capacity (6MWD, E); a higher score predicts worse survival', 'The BODE index integrates four prognostic dimensions of COPD — body mass index, airflow obstruction (FEV1), dyspnea (mMRC), and exercise capacity (6MWD) — into a single 0-10 score. Higher BODE scores predict significantly worse survival and are better than FEV1 alone.'),
('13505ee1-cdbb-5ce8-a25d-2941d1d08fe7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: The bicarbonate/CO2 buffer system:', '["Is important only in the kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Is the most important extracellular buffer; pH = 6.1 + log([HCO3−]/(0.03 × PaCO2))","Acts only intracellularly mediated by specific receptor activation","Does not buffer plasma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acts only within the RBC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is the most important extracellular buffer; pH = 6.1 + log([HCO3−]/(0.03 × PaCO2))', 'The HCO3−/CO2 pair is the dominant buffer of the extracellular fluid because its two components (HCO3− and the volatile CO2) can be regulated by the kidneys and lungs. Its pH is given by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation with pKa 6.1.'),
('c9bbbfe3-f4db-5520-ab3a-c0f7658e26c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the mechanism of hypoxia at high altitude?', '["Reduced barometric pressure → ↓PIO2 → hypoxemia; the lower gas density also transiently raises peak expiratory flow rate","A thicker diffusion barrier accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased pulmonary shunt mediated by specific receptor activation","Elevated barometric pressure raising alveolar PO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of hypoxic ventilatory drive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced barometric pressure → ↓PIO2 → hypoxemia; the lower gas density also transiently raises peak expiratory flow rate', 'At altitude, the fraction of oxygen is unchanged but barometric pressure (and thus inspired PO2) falls, lowering alveolar and arterial PO2 and producing hypoxic hypoxia. The reduced gas density also transiently increases PEFR and MVV, while the drop in PO2 stimulates ventilation.'),
('4ea87558-5c6c-52cf-b14a-0b21286349de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Maximal voluntary ventilation (MVV) is:', '["MV resulting in decreased functional activity","Resting minute ventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FEV1 mediated by specific receptor activation","PEFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximum volume of air breathed per minute (≈120-170 L/min)"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum volume of air breathed per minute (≈120-170 L/min)', 'MVV measures the maximum volume of air that can be breathed in and out over one minute (by extrapolating a 12-15 second effort), roughly 120-170 L/min in adults. It reflects overall respiratory muscle strength and airway function.'),
('5a7cd9bc-b115-595b-be2b-d15ba99d5341'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'The multiple inert gas elimination technique (MIGET) classifies lung units by ventilation-perfusion ratio. Which category represents units that are ventilated but hardly perfused, as in pulmonary embolism?', '["Shunt (V/Q <0.005)","Low V/Q (0.005-0.1)","Normal V/Q (0.1-10)","Dead space (V/Q >100)","High V/Q (10-100)"]'::jsonb, 'Dead space (V/Q >100)', 'MIGET partitions the lung into shunt (V/Q <0.005), low V/Q (0.005-0.1), normal V/Q (0.1-10), high V/Q (10-100), and dead space (V/Q >100). Pulmonary embolism creates predominantly dead-space units (ventilated but unperfused), which are classified as V/Q >100.'),
('1d008907-af10-591f-87ab-27cfb1e99fed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: Which flow-volume loop pattern is characteristic of obstructive airway disease?', '["Square shape resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal shape accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Concave (scooped-out) expiratory limb with reduced peak flow","Increased peak flow without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Straight line leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Concave (scooped-out) expiratory limb with reduced peak flow', 'In obstruction, airways narrow during forced expiration, so the expiratory limb shows a characteristic ''scooped-out'' concavity with a reduced peak flow and low flows at lower lung volumes. Restrictive disease instead produces a small, narrow loop.'),
('0e936c2c-e369-5dcf-b4ab-954a2db8686e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Using the mathematical model of V/Q distribution, what pattern is characteristic of COPD?', '["A broad bimodal distribution combining low V/Q (from airway obstruction) and high V/Q (from emphysematous capillary destruction), with little shunt or dead space","Predominant shunt accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single normal V/Q peak mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Dead space only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A shift of all units to V/Q <0.005 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A broad bimodal distribution combining low V/Q (from airway obstruction) and high V/Q (from emphysematous capillary destruction), with little shunt or dead space', 'COPD produces a bimodal V/Q distribution: obstructed units create low (but not zero) V/Q areas, while destroyed capillary beds create high V/Q (near-dead-space) areas. Because few units are truly unperfused or unventilated, true shunt and dead space remain small; the mixed pattern explains the marked response to supplemental oxygen.'),
('0c9f7273-bbc2-5990-b70c-a5323e8d7943'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'How does the hypoxemia of a true shunt differ from that of a low V/Q mismatch in its response to breathing 100% oxygen?', '["Shunt hypoxemia does not correct with increasing FiO2, whereas low V/Q hypoxemia does because alveolar PO2 can be raised enough","Both correct fully with oxygen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neither corrects with oxygen mediated by specific receptor activation","Low V/Q hypoxemia never corrects with O2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The two behave identically in every way leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Shunt hypoxemia does not correct with increasing FiO2, whereas low V/Q hypoxemia does because alveolar PO2 can be raised enough', 'In low V/Q units, raising inspired PO2 increases alveolar PO2 enough to fully saturate the equilibrating blood, so hypoxemia corrects with 100% O2. In a true shunt, mixed venous blood bypasses ventilated alveoli entirely and never equilibrates with alveolar gas, so even 100% inspired oxygen leaves the arterial PO2 lower than expected.'),
('20833e06-728d-546a-a5ad-2c264aa62992'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Most important ICS local side effect:', '["Oral candidiasis","Systemic immunosuppression","Adrenal suppression","Growth retardation","Osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, 'Oral candidiasis', 'Mouth rinsing reduces risk.'),
('cf8bc6d3-bc89-5335-84fd-6c7410f23025'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What physiologic mechanisms underlie the severe hypoxemia with relatively preserved breathing effort (silent hypoxemia) seen in COVID-19 pneumonia?', '["Severe V/Q mismatch plus severe shunt, with impaired hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction; intravascular microthrombosis contributes","Pure central hypoventilation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only diffusion limitation without shunt mediated by specific receptor activation","Absence of any V/Q abnormality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fixed right-to-left cardiac shunt leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe V/Q mismatch plus severe shunt, with impaired hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction; intravascular microthrombosis contributes', 'COVID-19 pneumonia produces a mixed V/Q mismatch and shunt (with regions of microvascular thrombosis), and impairment of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction may blunt the usual redistribution of flow. This combination produces profound hypoxemia out of proportion to respiratory distress, the so-called silent hypoxemia.'),
('46da4b5e-adc2-5ec9-998b-d1bce26a230d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: The normal fraction of cardiac output that bypasses gas exchange (physiologic shunt fraction) is:', '["<5% of total blood flow","20-25% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","15-20% mediated by specific receptor activation","10-15% without affecting the primary tissue pathway",">25% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '<5% of total blood flow', 'In healthy subjects, a small amount of mixed venous blood (including thebesian and bronchial drainage) bypasses oxygenation, amounting to less than 5% of cardiac output. Larger shunt fractions produce clinically significant hypoxemia.'),
('25d29beb-f39f-5a94-a0b0-88d4f20c2fbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which type of hypoxia results from a reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood (e.g., severe anemia or carbon monoxide poisoning)?', '["Hypoxic hypoxia","Stagnant hypoxia","Histotoxic hypoxia","Cytopathic hypoxia","Anemic hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Anemic hypoxia', 'Anemic hypoxia is caused by a reduced hemoglobin concentration or altered hemoglobin (COHb, metHb) so that oxygen content is low even though arterial PO2 is normal. In contrast, hypoxic hypoxia has a low PaO2, stagnant hypoxia has low flow, and histotoxic/cytopathic hypoxia reflect impaired tissue use of oxygen.'),
('0a4dcca4-26b3-5f97-bdac-dda444c8e10e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Why does the Haldane effect increase CO2 transport?', '["Deoxygenated Hb carries more CO2 as carbamino compounds","Oxyhemoglobin displaces CO from hemoglobin","CO2 forms carbonic acid in plasma only","Oxygen binds hemoglobin cooperatively","Oxygen causes pulmonary vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxygenated Hb carries more CO2 as carbamino compounds', 'By the Haldane effect, deoxygenated hemoglobin has a higher affinity for CO2 (forming carbaminohemoglobin) and binds H+ more avidly. At the tissues, deoxygenation therefore enhances CO2 uptake and buffering, while at the lungs oxygenation reverses it to promote CO2 excretion.'),
('6d1c3d44-516d-5ee0-9547-7ae6c39a1987'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Which parameter is the standard measure of aerobic (cardiorespiratory) capacity during progressive exercise testing?', '["Heart rate resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","VO2 (oxygen consumption, e.g., VO2 max)","VCO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minute ventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 (oxygen consumption, e.g., VO2 max)', 'Oxygen consumption rises linearly with work rate and plateaus at VO2 max, the accepted index of aerobic capacity. It integrates cardiac output, hemoglobin, and muscle oxygen extraction during exercise.'),
('af23b299-cb14-5b1f-9b66-fbb5663aa92b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which arterial blood gas pattern defines type II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure?', '["Hypoxemia (PaO2 <60 mmHg) with a normal or high PaCO2","Isolated respiratory alkalosis","Pure hypercapnia without hypoxemia","Normal blood gases","Polycythemia without gas abnormality"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia (PaO2 <60 mmHg) with a normal or high PaCO2', 'Type II respiratory failure combines hypoxemia with hypercapnia (PaCO2 >45 mmHg) from alveolar hypoventilation. Unlike type I failure, where PaCO2 is normal/low, hypoventilation reduces alveolar PO2 while raising PaCO2, as seen in neuromuscular weakness, severe COPD, and sedative overdose.'),
('3a552da8-e226-5c6b-826f-a1efb045e522'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following is the functional consequence of a rightward shift of the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve?', '["Increased Hb affinity for O2","A shift to the left","A decrease in P50","Decreased Hb affinity for O2 (increased P50)","Increased oxygen loading at the lungs"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased Hb affinity for O2 (increased P50)', 'A right shift lowers hemoglobin''s oxygen affinity, increasing the P50, so oxygen is released to tissues more readily at a given PO2. The opposite (left shift) raises affinity during oxygen loading but impairs unloading at the tissues.'),
('f14f51da-69fc-5dfe-815f-e11ca3080bd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following pulmonary conditions typically produces arterial hypoxemia on room air?', '["Pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema","Anemia with normal lung function","Cyanide poisoning (histotoxic hypoxia)","Methemoglobinemia alone","Decompression sickness"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema', 'Pulmonary fibrosis causes hypoxemia through diffusion limitation and V/Q mismatch, and emphysema through widespread V/Q mismatch; both lower arterial PO2 on room air. Anemia and methemoglobinemia lower the oxygen content but not the PaO2, and cyanide impairs tissue utilization rather than arterial oxygenation.'),
('0cfcb478-9370-5558-990d-9681d2387bfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, The compliance of the lung is increased in:', '["Atelectasis resulting in decreased functional activity","Pulmonary edema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pneumonia mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Emphysema (loss of elastic recoil)"]'::jsonb, 'Emphysema (loss of elastic recoil)', 'Emphysema destroys alveolar septa and reduces elastic recoil, making the lung easier to distend, so lung compliance is increased. Fibrosis, edema, atelectasis, and pneumonia all stiffen the lung and lower compliance.'),
('edecf27e-ca76-5f14-bfd5-54359ef061bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which pH range is considered normal for arterial blood gas analysis?', '["7.35-7.45","7.0-7.35","7.45-7.55","7.2-7.4","7.5-7.6"]'::jsonb, '7.35-7.45', 'Arterial blood pH is normally 7.35-7.45. Acid-base disturbances are classified by whether pH falls below or rises above this normal range, in combination with the direction of change in PaCO2 and HCO3-.'),
('7110edb1-4f21-5ff5-8dc4-d40176ced594'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'The pH of arterial blood in a healthy person at rest lies within which range?', '["7.35-7.45","7.0-7.35","7.45-7.55","7.2-7.4","7.5-7.6"]'::jsonb, '7.35-7.45', 'Resting arterial pH is normally between 7.35 and 7.45. This narrow range is defended by chemical buffers and by the lungs and kidneys modulating PCO2 and bicarbonate.'),
('a967dde8-c6e6-51b6-bf01-1117b01338ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What range of arterial pH defines acidemia versus the normal state?', '["7.35-7.45","7.0-7.35","7.45-7.55","7.2-7.4","7.5-7.6"]'::jsonb, '7.35-7.45', 'A pH below 7.35 defines acidemia, above 7.45 alkalemia, and 7.35-7.45 is the normal arterial pH range. The pH is set primarily by the ratio of HCO3- to dissolved CO2 according to the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship.'),
('29d80096-36ac-5f65-b79b-3bbb6d0d9774'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following represents the normal arterial blood pH range?', '["7.35-7.45","7.0-7.35","7.45-7.55","7.2-7.4","7.5-7.6"]'::jsonb, '7.35-7.45', 'The normal arterial pH is 7.35-7.45. Disorders of acid-base balance disturb this range, and interpretation of the pH together with PaCO2 and HCO3- points to the primary respiratory or metabolic disturbance.'),
('a926680b-89e6-5c62-850d-4ec6635dc407'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following correctly describes the normal arterial PaO2 on room air?', '["120-140 mmHg","60-80 mmHg","80-100 mmHg","50-70 mmHg","100-120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-100 mmHg', 'Arterial PaO2 is normally 80-100 mmHg breathing room air at sea level in a young adult. It decreases with age, and values below 60 mmHg define hypoxemia (respiratory failure).'),
('e3abb806-e209-5394-8e8b-37d6559b9ccd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: The normal tidal volume (VT) at rest is approximately:', '["300 mL","1000 mL","500 mL","200 mL","700 mL"]'::jsonb, '500 mL', 'Tidal volume is the air inspired or expired with each normal breath, averaging about 500 mL (≈6-8 mL/kg) in healthy adults at rest. Larger or smaller values suggest altered breathing patterns or disease.'),
('d43a2cf7-b5b0-5691-a674-5b77c3aa6d16'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, The Bohr and Haldane effects work together to:', '["Facilitate both O2 unloading and CO2 loading at the tissues","Prevent O2 uptake accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prevent CO2 release mediated by specific receptor activation","Slow gas transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduce gas exchange leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitate both O2 unloading and CO2 loading at the tissues', 'At the tissues, acidity released by CO2 cuts hemoglobin''s O2 affinity (Bohr), while deoxygenation raises hemoglobin''s affinity for CO2 and H+ (Haldane). Together they promote O2 delivery to muscles and CO2 pickup for transport to the lungs.'),
('cb276bc6-dd17-59a2-b34c-bb80db60d2f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which value range represents the normal resting arterial oxygen tension (PaO2)?', '["120-140 mmHg","60-80 mmHg","80-100 mmHg","50-70 mmHg","100-120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-100 mmHg', 'The normal resting arterial PaO2 is 80-100 mmHg on room air. Hypoxemia is defined by a PaO2 below 60 mmHg, and the expected PaO2 decreases progressively with age.'),
('cef996b7-07e7-5f3a-bf13-d80460041639'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'What is the normal PaO2 range on room air in a healthy person?', '["120-140 mmHg","60-80 mmHg","80-100 mmHg","50-70 mmHg","100-120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-100 mmHg', 'Healthy individuals maintain a PaO2 of roughly 80-100 mmHg breathing room air at sea level. Because of normal shunting and V/Q inequalities, the arterial PO2 is slightly lower than the alveolar PO2.'),
('5b7af9ab-b74a-5efa-80e4-657ad51a96c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which of the following is the normal range of arterial oxygen partial pressure?', '["120-140 mmHg","60-80 mmHg","80-100 mmHg","50-70 mmHg","100-120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-100 mmHg', 'Normal arterial PaO2 is 80-100 mmHg. Values in older adults are lower, and the normal A-a gradient widens with age; PaO2 below 60 mmHg defines significant hypoxemia.'),
('b74ebaaf-0bef-5f7d-9ab9-90fc9581006f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has:', '["Cooperative oxygen binding","Higher O2 affinity than HbA (left-shifted curve)","Lower O2 affinity than HbA","Bohr effect only","Same O2 affinity as HbA"]'::jsonb, 'Higher O2 affinity than HbA (left-shifted curve)', 'HbF has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult hemoglobin because it binds 2,3-BPG less avidly, shifting the dissociation curve left. This allows the fetus to extract oxygen across the placenta from maternal blood.'),
('be4ccd26-4ce0-52a0-95ac-b2d1575c831a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, Physiologic dead space (VDphys) equals:', '["Tidal volume resulting in decreased functional activity","Anatomical dead space only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Residual volume mediated by specific receptor activation","Alveolar dead space only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Anatomical dead space + alveolar dead space (VDanat + VDalv)"]'::jsonb, 'Anatomical dead space + alveolar dead space (VDanat + VDalv)', 'Physiological dead space is the sum of anatomical dead space (conducting airways) and alveolar dead space (ventilated but unperfused alveoli). In health alveolar dead space is negligible, so physiologic and anatomical dead space are nearly equal (150 mL).'),
('a3c12e82-c5af-5b1e-bcf4-5dd0d80fd3b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: Which receptors mediate reflex bronchoconstriction and cough in response to inhaled irritants?', '["Peripheral chemoreceptors resulting in decreased functional activity","Central chemoreceptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Airway stretch receptors mediated by specific receptor activation","Irritant (rapidly adapting) receptors responding to dust, cold, and chemicals","J receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Irritant (rapidly adapting) receptors responding to dust, cold, and chemicals', 'Irritant receptors are rapidly adapting receptors located in the airway epithelium that are stimulated by mechanical and chemical irritants such as dust, smoke, and cold air. They trigger cough, bronchoconstriction, and mucus secretion.'),
('fc73b324-64cd-5533-92ba-154143a60c86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'A patient with chronic diarrhea has ABG: pH 7.25, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 12 mEq/L. Which statement is most consistent with this acid-base picture?', '["PaCO2 = HCO3- + 20 is the appropriate relationship","PaCO2 = HCO3- × 2 is expected","PaCO2 = 40 - (24 - HCO3-) is the expected compensation","Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × [HCO3-]) + 8 ± 2","PaCO2 = HCO3- + 15"]'::jsonb, 'Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × [HCO3-]) + 8 ± 2', 'The ABG shows a metabolic acidosis (low pH, low HCO3-) with respiratory compensation (PaCO2 30 mmHg). Winter''s formula predicts an expected PaCO2 of about 26 ± 2 mmHg for HCO3- 12, so the measured value indicates appropriate compensation rather than a superimposed disorder.'),
('5d8ba723-3238-550c-a77d-a40235aa0cbf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which formula is used to check the adequacy of respiratory compensation in a metabolic acidosis?', '["PaCO2 = HCO3- + 20","PaCO2 = HCO3- × 2","PaCO2 = 40 - (24 - HCO3-)","Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × [HCO3-]) + 8 ± 2","PaCO2 = HCO3- + 15"]'::jsonb, 'Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × [HCO3-]) + 8 ± 2', 'Winter''s formula, PaCO2 = 1.5 × HCO3- + 8 ± 2, tests whether a metabolic acidosis is appropriately compensated. If the measured PaCO2 matches the predicted value, the respiratory response is adequate; if not, a mixed respiratory disorder is present.'),
('af8c5c00-302f-5e41-8418-533cb407d3fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Respiratory Pt2: The restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing shows:', '["↓FEV1/FVC resulting in decreased functional activity","↑TLC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↓TLC, FEV1/FVC normal or ↑, and ↓FVC","Normal pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Obstructive pattern leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↓TLC, FEV1/FVC normal or ↑, and ↓FVC', 'Restrictive disease lowers total lung capacity and vital capacity while the FEV1/FVC ratio is preserved or even increased. This pattern is seen in pulmonary fibrosis, chest wall restriction, and neuromuscular weakness, and differs from the low ratio of obstruction.'),
('5215525b-c68f-5639-b53c-6126e4b13a74'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, The critical DO2 is:', '["The anaerobic threshold resulting in decreased functional activity","VO2 max accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The DO2 below which VO2 becomes supply-dependent (300-400 mL/min)","Resting VO2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maximal exercise VO2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The DO2 below which VO2 becomes supply-dependent (300-400 mL/min)', 'Normally VO2 is independent of delivery, but below a critical DO2 (≈300-400 mL/min) oxygen consumption falls with delivery and anaerobic metabolism begins. This supply-dependence marks the onset of tissue dysoxia.'),
('e151efc8-41aa-5818-9fa1-b9fa9222f185'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which blood gas finding best characterizes respiratory acidosis?', '["Normal pH low PaCO2","High pH low HCO3-","pH <7.35 PaCO2 >45 mmHg","Low pH low HCO3-","High pH high PaCO2"]'::jsonb, 'pH <7.35 PaCO2 >45 mmHg', 'Respiratory acidosis results from inadequate alveolar ventilation with CO2 retention, giving a low pH and high PaCO2. The low HCO3- pattern instead suggests a metabolic acidosis.'),
('fc06233b-e1d8-5d89-a32e-9dbc4c84bd56'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Respiratory Pt2: Paralysis of the diaphragm leads to:', '["Normal breathing resulting in decreased functional activity","Only cough impairment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Paradoxical movement (upward during inspiration) and reduction of VC by 50%","No significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperventilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Paradoxical movement (upward during inspiration) and reduction of VC by 50%', 'With a paralyzed diaphragm, the contracting abdominal wall pushes the flaccid diaphragm upward during inspiration, producing paradoxical movement. Vital capacity falls by as much as half because the diaphragm normally generates the largest part of the volume change.'),
('3a2f87f1-ff20-5909-a7ff-b2f6d3af098d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, J (juxtacapillary) receptors are located and stimulated as follows:', '["In the nasal mucosa; respond to inhaled irritants resulting in decreased functional activity","Respond only to airway stretch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Near pulmonary capillaries; respond to ↑interstitial fluid (congestion, edema, inflammation) → rapid shallow breathing and dyspnea","Respond to arterial pH changes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the medulla; respond to CSF pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Near pulmonary capillaries; respond to ↑interstitial fluid (congestion, edema, inflammation) → rapid shallow breathing and dyspnea', 'J receptors lie in the alveolar interstitium close to the pulmonary capillaries and are activated by interstitial congestion, edema, and inflammation. Their stimulation produces rapid shallow breathing, dyspnea, and cough in conditions such as pulmonary edema and pneumonia.'),
('d8e1e4e7-9085-51b9-8ff7-1128dc80ef36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which combination of pH and PaCO2 is seen in respiratory alkalosis?', '["Low pH high HCO3-","High pH high PaCO2","pH >7.45 PaCO2 <35 mmHg","Low pH low PaCO2","pH <7.35 PaCO2 >45"]'::jsonb, 'pH >7.45 PaCO2 <35 mmHg', 'In respiratory alkalosis, hyperventilation blows off CO2, producing a high pH with a low PaCO2. This pattern is classically seen in anxiety, hypoxia, fever, and ventilated-overdrive states.'),
('0f544637-7fe1-5a54-b5c4-bb8ab8a39e1c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, Positive pressure ventilation (e.g., with PEEP) can cause which hemodynamic effect?', '["Increased venous return","Decreased left ventricular afterload","Reduced cardiac output due to decreased venous return","Increased pulmonary perfusion","Increased cardiac output"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced cardiac output due to decreased venous return', 'Positive intrathoracic pressure compresses the great veins, reducing venous return and therefore cardiac output, particularly in hypovolemic patients. It also compresses zone-1 alveoli, increasing alveolar dead space.'),
('a12af9b2-a5d6-5a92-beda-816bdde20344'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: In ideal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching:', '["V/Q = 1 (perfect match)","V/Q < 1","V/Q = 0","V/Q is infinite","V/Q > 1"]'::jsonb, 'V/Q = 1 (perfect match)', 'Ideal matching means alveolar ventilation exactly equals capillary perfusion in proportion, so V/Q = 1. In reality the whole-lung V/Q is 0.8, and regional matching is imperfect, producing the normal A-a gradient.'),
('8c7912c2-0ceb-5eba-8b6a-53daa0455256'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which acid-base disturbance produces a pH >7.45 combined with a PaCO2 <35 mmHg?', '["Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory alkalosis', 'A high pH with a low PaCO2 defines respiratory alkalosis from alveolar hyperventilation. It is the opposite pattern to respiratory acidosis (low pH, high PaCO2).'),
('e89e622b-5926-5864-8e3c-ab2b1c253b6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex is characterized by:', '["Stimulation of deep inspiration resulting in decreased functional activity","Effects limited to expiration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibition of inspiration by airway stretch receptors when tidal volume is large; weak in adults but important in neonates","Bronchospasm on inflation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence in all mammals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of inspiration by airway stretch receptors when tidal volume is large; weak in adults but important in neonates', 'Slowly adapting stretch receptors in the airways discharge as the lungs inflate and inhibit the inspiratory centers, terminating inspiration and prolonging expiration (the Hering-Breuer reflex). It is weak in adults but helps limit tidal volume, especially in newborns.'),
('60021ddd-6a91-5afd-82d3-ee9fc63a0f4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which acid-base pattern indicates metabolic acidosis?', '["Normal pH low PaCO2","pH <7.35 HCO3- <22 mmHg","Low pH high PaCO2","High pH low HCO3-","pH >7.45 HCO3- >26"]'::jsonb, 'pH <7.35 HCO3- <22 mmHg', 'Metabolic acidosis is a low pH with a reduced HCO3- (<22 mEq/L), reflecting a primary loss of bicarbonate or gain of acid. The PaCO2 typically falls as a compensatory hyperventilation.'),
('46128847-2a0c-5b5a-928c-4cd09fab2fac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, Isovolume pressure-flow curves demonstrate that, at a fixed lung volume, expiratory flow:', '["Increases continuously in proportion to effort","Rises to a maximum and then plateaus (effort-independent flow)","Always decreases with effort","Is the same at all lung volumes","Is turbulent at every volume"]'::jsonb, 'Rises to a maximum and then plateaus (effort-independent flow)', 'Plotting flow against pleural pressure at a constant lung volume shows flow rising with effort only up to a plateau; beyond that, further effort does not increase flow. The plateau reflects dynamic airway compression, and its height falls as lung volume decreases.'),
('ba3eaab5-d2c2-510e-83a5-49768e358485'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Respiratory Pt2: In which condition do ventilated alveoli receive no perfusion, producing wasted ventilation (increased dead space)?', '["Pulmonary embolism (no perfusion of ventilated alveoli)","Normal lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Asthma mediated by specific receptor activation","Pneumonia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pulmonary edema leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary embolism (no perfusion of ventilated alveoli)', 'An embolus obstructs a pulmonary artery, so alveoli distal to it are ventilated but not perfused, creating alveolar dead space (V/Q extremely high). The wasted ventilation raises physiologic dead space and worsens gas exchange.'),
('216adcbf-8a4e-5988-9f87-c498ebef2f4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which acid-base pattern corresponds to a pH 7.50, PaCO2 48 mmHg, and HCO3- 35 mEq/L?', '["Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation","Respiratory acidosis with renal compensation","Respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation","Mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation', 'A high pH and high bicarbonate point to metabolic alkalosis, and the compensatory rise in PaCO2 reflects the expected respiratory compensation (alveolar hypoventilation). This CO2 retention should not be mistaken for respiratory acidosis.'),
('08d04f2e-5937-5e85-a5b7-fece662e1817'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Respiratory Pt2, The respiratory quotient (RQ = VCO2/VO2) on a typical mixed diet is approximately:', '["1.2","2.0","0.8","0.5","1.0"]'::jsonb, '0.8', 'The respiratory quotient equals the ratio of CO2 produced to O2 consumed and averages about 0.8 on a mixed diet (1.0 for pure carbohydrate, 0.7 for fat). It is used in the alveolar gas equation to relate PAO2 to PaCO2.'),
('00b4ac0c-2481-545b-b8ff-e8d87e2c24e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'Which combination of pH and HCO3- is characteristic of metabolic alkalosis?', '["High pH low HCO3-","pH >7.45 HCO3- >26","Low pH low HCO3-","Low pH high HCO3-","Normal pH high HCO3-"]'::jsonb, 'pH >7.45 HCO3- >26', 'In metabolic alkalosis the pH rises above 7.45 and HCO3- exceeds 26 mEq/L. It is the converse of metabolic acidosis (low pH, low HCO3-) and is maintained by chloride depletion and hypokalemia.'),
('096b3ee7-13e4-5c48-a103-d2383ab7b49a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Respiratory System - Respiratory Part 2', 'In the study of Respiratory Pt2, In ideal ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching:', '["V/Q = 1 (perfect match)","V/Q < 1","V/Q = 0","V/Q is infinite","V/Q > 1"]'::jsonb, 'V/Q = 1 (perfect match)', 'Ideal matching means alveolar ventilation exactly equals capillary perfusion in proportion, so V/Q = 1. In reality the whole-lung V/Q is 0.8, and regional matching is imperfect, producing the normal A-a gradient.'),
('8f8fce4e-07ee-5eb9-b7fa-2b86ff21f546'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The enteric nervous system is also called:', '["The second brain (gut brain)","Autonomic nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Central nervous system","Peripheral nervous system"]'::jsonb, 'The second brain (gut brain)', 'The enteric nervous system is the intrinsic neural network of the gut wall and is widely called the second brain (gut brain) because it contains about 100 million neurons and can function independently of the CNS.'),
('3c4a5479-9bfb-5e20-949f-56f7312b168b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What are the two major plexuses of the enteric nervous system?', '["Circular and longitudinal","Vagus and splanchnic","Sympathetic and parasympathetic","Only one plexus","Myenteric (Auerbach) and submucosal (Meissner)"]'::jsonb, 'Myenteric (Auerbach) and submucosal (Meissner)', 'The enteric nervous system consists of two principal plexuses: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the longitudinal and circular muscle layers and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus in the submucosa.'),
('615b8039-7f4d-5dd1-8d64-9af807fe6948'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Parasympathetic supply to the distal colon and rectum is via:', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'The vagus supplies the gut down to the proximal colon; the distal colon and rectum receive parasympathetic innervation from the pelvic splanchnic nerves arising from S2-S4.'),
('c4e88206-1010-5f22-befc-28525d8ed27c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Sympathetic supply to the GI tract is via:', '["Cranial nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)","Pelvic mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)', 'Sympathetic innervation of the gastrointestinal tract is provided by preganglionic fibers from the thoracolumbar outflow (T5-L2), which synapse in the prevertebral ganglia and travel in the splanchnic nerves.'),
('092ec54d-ebe7-55b8-8dcc-46f8410c802f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The major excitatory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system is:', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is the principal excitatory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system, stimulating GI smooth muscle contraction and secretion through muscarinic receptors.'),
('6dfd70fc-f8b8-53b1-8b55-93bb430847af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The major inhibitory neurotransmitter acting on the enteric nervous system is:', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine, which acts on enteric neurons and smooth muscle to inhibit GI motility and secretion.'),
('d45755dd-a471-5ad7-b2e2-9e825c8e4b8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which inhibitory neurotransmitters mediate relaxation of GI smooth muscle?', '["Serotonin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP', 'Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) are the principal non-adrenergic, non-cholinergic (NANC) inhibitory neurotransmitters that relax GI smooth muscle.'),
('2cd8c66f-601a-5acd-95ae-9db8481d35dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'In the peristaltic reflex of the intestine, local stimulation produces:', '["Only secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Only relaxation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only contraction mediated by specific receptor activation","Contraction limited to the stimulus site without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulation anywhere → contraction above relaxation below"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulation anywhere → contraction above relaxation below', 'The peristaltic reflex produces contraction orally (above) and relaxation aborally (below) the point of stimulation, propelling contents distally.'),
('23231dbd-a6dd-5cd3-94d9-2a7a267f2cf7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The migrating motor complex (MMC) is:', '["Defecation resulting in decreased functional activity","Satiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)","Postprandial pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vomiting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)', 'The MMC is a cyclic pattern of GI contractions during fasting that sweeps undigested material distally, acting as a housekeeper.'),
('1501d509-cbf6-589e-baac-0e03f9248879'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The migrating motor complex (MMC) is disrupted by:', '["Medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Fasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)', 'Feeding replaces the fasting MMC with a continuous, irregular postprandial pattern of contraction.'),
('30e81e6e-1ad6-5af5-b456-96dffe6e4df4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The primary function of segmentation contractions is:', '["Sweeping resulting in decreased functional activity","Storing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixing and churning (smooth muscle segmental contractions)","Emptying without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Propulsion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixing and churning (smooth muscle segmental contractions)', 'Segmentation contractions are stationary, ring-like contractions that mix and churn intestinal contents, promoting digestion and absorption.'),
('79a01aa2-a232-533d-86d9-81cebeb397e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the normal lower esophageal sphincter (LES) pressure relative to gastric pressure?', '["5-10 mmHg resulting in decreased functional activity","25-30 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","30-40 mmHg mediated by specific receptor activation","15-25 mmHg above gastric pressure","0-5 mmHg leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '15-25 mmHg above gastric pressure', 'The LES normally maintains a pressure of about 15-25 mmHg above intragastric pressure, forming the barrier that prevents gastroesophageal reflux.'),
('43ee9bf9-483b-5c56-8baf-c5fa61923f9c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Receptive relaxation of the proximal stomach during swallowing is mediated by:', '["Sympathetic activation (norepinephrine)","Local myenteric reflex of the antrum","Histamine release from ECL cells","Inspiration","Swallowing (via vagal NANC inhibition - NO/VIP)"]'::jsonb, 'Swallowing (via vagal NANC inhibition - NO/VIP)', 'Swallowing triggers a vagovagal reflex that releases the NANC inhibitory transmitters NO and VIP, which relax the proximal stomach (receptive relaxation).'),
('09e3f97f-5af0-5845-9728-29fe030dc0b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the principal cause of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)?', '["High LES pressure","Increased frequency of normal swallow-related relaxations","Normal LES pressure","Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone","Absence of gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone', 'GERD results mainly from transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) not triggered by swallowing, sometimes combined with a low resting LES tone.'),
('4278905c-1156-56b2-910d-89f25cd4b345'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The rate of gastric emptying depends on:', '["All of the above","Duodenal feedback","Volume and composition of gastric contents","Caloric density","Only volume"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Gastric emptying is regulated by the volume and composition of gastric contents, caloric density, and inhibitory feedback from the duodenum.'),
('c267e6f2-b000-5b8f-80d8-35c379f2f3c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Contractions of the gastric body are best described as:', '["Segmental resulting in decreased functional activity","Sweeping accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propulsive mediated by specific receptor activation","Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)","Strong leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)', 'The gastric body and fundus produce weak, sustained contractions that regulate intragastric tone and mediate receptive relaxation; strong peristaltic waves occur in the antrum.'),
('85ee548b-c0bc-5327-960a-1eb4bf0dc2be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which statement best describes antral peristalsis?', '["MMC resulting in decreased functional activity","Peristalsis in fundus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Segmentation mediated by specific receptor activation","Emptying without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Strong contraction (3/min) → trituration of solids to <2 mm particles"]'::jsonb, 'Strong contraction (3/min) → trituration of solids to <2 mm particles', 'Antral peristaltic waves occur at about 3 per minute and are strong enough to grind and sieve food, reducing solids to particles smaller than 2 mm.'),
('12f85ca6-6ed7-5327-9f5c-86e9e0ac2b5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The enterogastric (duodenal) reflex is characterized by:', '["Fat and acid in duodenum → ↑cholecystokinin (CCK) secretin GIP → ↓gastric emptying","No significant effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↑appetite mediated by specific receptor activation","↑emptying without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↑secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fat and acid in duodenum → ↑cholecystokinin (CCK) secretin GIP → ↓gastric emptying', 'Fat and acid in the duodenum release CCK, secretin and GIP, which inhibit gastric emptying via the enterogastric reflex.'),
('580813f5-4e8b-539a-b213-6ad7d10c9296'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Vomiting is coordinated by:', '["Diencephalon resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Vomiting center in medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius + central pattern generator)","Intestine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vomiting center in medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius + central pattern generator)', 'The vomiting center in the medulla, centered on the nucleus tractus solitarius with a central pattern generator, coordinates the emetic response.'),
('9ae05f7f-8323-5633-865d-c99529c53664'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the correct sequence of events in vomiting?', '["Diarrhea","Immediate projectile vomiting","Nausea → retching → vomiting (expulsion)","Retching only","Retching followed directly by expulsion without nausea"]'::jsonb, 'Nausea → retching → vomiting (expulsion)', 'Vomiting proceeds through the sequence of nausea, then retching, and finally expulsion of gastric contents.'),
('e4472938-c9f6-5b10-8600-739f77fa29ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the sequence of events during the vomiting (emesis) reflex?', '["Only abdominal contraction resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only gastric contraction accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only cough mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Deep breath → closure of glottis → contraction of abdominals/diaphragm → ↑intra-abdominal pressure → gastric relaxation → expulsion","Only esophageal relaxation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deep breath → closure of glottis → contraction of abdominals/diaphragm → ↑intra-abdominal pressure → gastric relaxation → expulsion', 'Emesis begins with a deep breath and glottis closure; abdominal and diaphragmatic contraction raises intra-abdominal pressure, and the stomach relaxes as contents are expelled.'),
('a7660474-1edf-5846-b568-0ee37bb50bea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Postprandial satiation is mediated by:', '["Gastric distension (stretch receptors) + CCK (CCK-1 receptors)","Low blood glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Empty stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Low insulin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ghrelin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric distension (stretch receptors) + CCK (CCK-1 receptors)', 'Meal-induced satiation is driven by gastric distension acting on stretch receptors together with CCK acting on CCK-1 receptors.'),
('8349361d-ef50-5f2c-a9ba-4d4ebcf61396'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Appetite regulation involves:', '["GI tract only resulting in decreased functional activity","Arcuate nucleus (hypothalamus) integrating leptin ghrelin NPY AgRP POMC CART","Cortex only mediated by specific receptor activation","Vomiting center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CTZ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate nucleus (hypothalamus) integrating leptin ghrelin NPY AgRP POMC CART', 'The arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus integrates peripheral signals such as leptin and ghrelin with NPY/AgRP (orexigenic) and POMC/CART (anorexigenic) neurons.'),
('daa24f06-2638-518a-a5bb-4e1d8b60edf1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Ghrelin is an:', '["Enzyme resulting in decreased functional activity","Orexigenic hormone (appetite stimulant) from stomach (P/D1 cells)","Anorectic mediated by specific receptor activation","Inert without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurotransmitter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Orexigenic hormone (appetite stimulant) from stomach (P/D1 cells)', 'Ghrelin, secreted by P/D1 cells of the gastric fundus, is an orexigenic hormone that rises before meals and stimulates appetite.'),
('401db382-a510-52a7-92d5-93d404576e3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric acid secretion is regulated by:', '["Histamine (paracrine from ECL cells)","All three stimulate acid","Gastrin (hormone from G cells)","Only histamine","ACh (vagal)"]'::jsonb, 'All three stimulate acid', 'Acid secretion is stimulated by three agonists: ACh from vagal nerves, histamine released paracrinally from ECL cells, and gastrin from G cells; together they are the principal physiologic stimulants.'),
('f4493bd0-b1fb-5651-b11d-e2907cd5c9f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which substances are secreted by parietal cells in the stomach?', '["Pepsinogen","Intrinsic factor only","HCl only","Mucus","HCl and intrinsic factor"]'::jsonb, 'HCl and intrinsic factor', 'Parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid, which activates pepsinogen and kills bacteria, and intrinsic factor, which is required for vitamin B12 absorption.'),
('813656aa-da95-5dfb-80f7-b21fc8f8d69c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'H2-receptor antagonists (e.g., ranitidine) block:', '["Gastric distension resulting in decreased functional activity","ACh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Histamine-induced acid secretion from parietal cells","Prostaglandin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastrin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine-induced acid secretion from parietal cells', 'H2-receptor antagonists competitively block histamine H2 receptors on parietal cells, thereby reducing histamine-stimulated acid secretion.'),
('ff02881f-980d-5a24-b9fe-4079f7ce3e24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which paracrine factor is the most potent stimulator of gastric acid secretion from parietal cells?', '["Serotonin resulting in decreased functional activity","ACh accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastrin mediated by specific receptor activation","Histamine (stimulates parietal cell acid secretion)","Somatostatin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Histamine (stimulates parietal cell acid secretion)', 'Histamine released from ECL cells is the most potent paracrine stimulator of acid secretion, acting through H2 receptors on parietal cells.'),
('1d2c6500-d117-5269-aece-204a58b13379'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastrin is released from:', '["G cells in gastric antrum (in response to peptides amino acids vagal GRP)","Parietal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ECL cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Chief cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","D cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'G cells in gastric antrum (in response to peptides amino acids vagal GRP)', 'Gastrin is secreted by G cells in the gastric antrum in response to peptides and amino acids and to vagal stimulation via gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP).'),
('911157a9-7bbb-53b6-b12b-2ead34152a1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Somatostatin is released from:', '["Chief cells resulting in decreased functional activity","ECL cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","G cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Parietal cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","D cells of stomach and pancreas (inhibitory paracrine hormone)"]'::jsonb, 'D cells of stomach and pancreas (inhibitory paracrine hormone)', 'Somatostatin is released by D cells in the stomach and pancreatic islets and acts as a universal inhibitory paracrine hormone.'),
('83abf262-1640-55a8-b204-d4f6ebff9847'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The cephalic phase of gastric secretion is characterized by:', '["Sight/smell/taste of food → vagal activation → 30-50% of acid output","Oral phase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastrin release from antral G cells in response to peptides mediated by specific receptor activation","Swallowing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chewing leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sight/smell/taste of food → vagal activation → 30-50% of acid output', 'The cephalic phase is initiated by the sight, smell or thought of food through vagal activation and contributes about 30-50% of total acid output.'),
('80984539-1e43-5be8-8d37-f6c86a3e333e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The gastric phase of secretion is characterized by:', '["10% resulting in decreased functional activity","Distension + chemical (protein/amino acids) → gastrin + local reflexes → 50-60% of acid","100% mediated by specific receptor activation","80% without affecting the primary tissue pathway","30% leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distension + chemical (protein/amino acids) → gastrin + local reflexes → 50-60% of acid', 'The gastric phase is the largest phase of acid secretion (50-60%), triggered by gastric distension and protein/amino acids via gastrin and local reflexes.'),
('cb4d315e-4c9a-53f4-85d3-dc48fbfb7800'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The intestinal phase of secretion is characterized by:', '["50% resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","100% accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","80% mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","30% without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small intestinal factors (distension amino acids) → weak stimulation or inhibition (5-10% of acid)"]'::jsonb, 'Small intestinal factors (distension amino acids) → weak stimulation or inhibition (5-10% of acid)', 'The intestinal phase contributes only 5-10% of acid output, produced by weak stimulatory or inhibitory influences from the small intestine.'),
('5b3929c2-5b45-544f-9aac-47f2ddd8f121'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Protective mechanisms against gastric acid include:', '["Mucus-bicarbonate barrier (surface mucus + HCO3-)","All of the above","Blood flow","Tight junctions","Prostaglandins (PGE2)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The gastric mucosa is protected by the mucus-bicarbonate barrier, mucosal blood flow, tight junctions and prostaglandins such as PGE2.'),
('a71e18ed-c1e8-5ea7-a78c-a0ab2e2c9483'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Mucus is secreted by:', '["Parietal cells resulting in decreased functional activity","G cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Goblet cells (surface mucous cells) and neck mucous cells","ECL cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chief cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Goblet cells (surface mucous cells) and neck mucous cells', 'Surface mucous (goblet) cells and neck mucous cells secrete mucus, which traps bicarbonate and forms a protective layer over the mucosa.'),
('55d4677f-2c5e-5903-a708-13065f9d2bb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Helicobacter pylori survives in the stomach by:', '["Adhesins","Urease → produces NH3 → neutralizes acid around it","Mucus secretion","Flagella","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'H. pylori uses adhesins and flagella to colonize the gastric mucus layer and produces urease, which generates ammonia that neutralizes local acid.'),
('8a29ddc0-223f-551f-9ab2-e8adf4627c33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Peptic ulcer disease (PUD) is most commonly caused by:', '["H. pylori infection + NSAIDs (most common)","Steroids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alcohol mediated by specific receptor activation","Stress alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spicy food leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H. pylori infection + NSAIDs (most common)', 'H. pylori infection and NSAID use are the two most common causes of peptic ulcer disease; alcohol, stress and diet are only minor contributors.'),
('e4a9b430-67f0-5421-a671-a63cd03081c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'How do gastric ulcers differ from duodenal ulcers?', '["Gastric: normal/low acid; pain worsens with food. Duodenal: high acid; pain relieved by food","Gastric higher acid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenal has worse pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Duodenal ulcers typically have lower acid secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastric pain worse fasting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric: normal/low acid; pain worsens with food. Duodenal: high acid; pain relieved by food', 'Gastric ulcers are associated with normal or low acid and pain that worsens with meals, whereas duodenal ulcers occur with high acid and pain relieved by food.'),
('6c1b6fa8-6bfa-50ce-a6e7-20d758f1117d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Intrinsic factor secreted by parietal cells is essential for:', '["Iron absorption","Calcium absorption","Vitamin B12 absorption in terminal ileum","Potassium absorption","Folate absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 absorption in terminal ileum', 'Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 so that the IF-B12 complex can be absorbed in the terminal ileum.'),
('12f79bf8-2482-51dc-a670-df12b6716c56'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'A 35-year-old male presents with fatigue and pallor. Which is the most probable underlying cause?', '["Marrow failure resulting in decreased functional activity","Folate deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lack of intrinsic factor (autoimmune destruction of parietal cells)","Iron deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","B12 deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of intrinsic factor (autoimmune destruction of parietal cells)', 'Fatigue and pallor suggest anemia; autoimmune destruction of gastric parietal cells causing intrinsic factor deficiency produces pernicious anemia with impaired B12 absorption.'),
('a3dc8465-aed4-52fd-967a-a15ec6fea315'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The exocrine pancreas secretes:', '["Insulin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Bile only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Digestive enzymes (trypsinogen amylase lipase) and HCO3-","Enzymes only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucagon only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Digestive enzymes (trypsinogen amylase lipase) and HCO3-', 'The exocrine pancreas secretes digestive enzymes (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, amylase, lipase) and a bicarbonate-rich fluid.'),
('16d264a4-9270-5a29-b677-6031cf4f0456'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Pancreatic amylase digests:', '["Protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Nucleic acids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Starch (glycogen/amylose → maltose maltotriose dextrins)","Fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fiber leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Starch (glycogen/amylose → maltose maltotriose dextrins)', 'Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes starch, glycogen and amylose into maltose, maltotriose and alpha-limit dextrins.'),
('095975dd-104c-598a-968c-1065c43815b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Pancreatic lipase digests:', '["Protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Triglycerides → monoglycerides + fatty acids","Starch mediated by specific receptor activation","DNA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fiber leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triglycerides → monoglycerides + fatty acids', 'Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides at the 1 and 3 positions to yield monoglycerides and free fatty acids.'),
('57c2ad47-c37b-5288-a2c3-035244d571b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Colipase is:', '["Emulsifier resulting in decreased functional activity","Bile acid accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Digestive enzyme mediated by specific receptor activation","Cofactor for lipase (binds bile salts to prevent lipase denaturation)","Lipase inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cofactor for lipase (binds bile salts to prevent lipase denaturation)', 'Colipase is a cofactor that binds bile salts, anchoring pancreatic lipase to the lipid-water interface and preventing its inactivation.'),
('0c764791-91a3-5b8c-90f8-3d873e2ee1bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Pancreatic proteases (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase) are:', '["Activated in pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Only one active accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activated in duodenum (trypsinogen → trypsin via enterokinase)","Secrete active without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activated in the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated in duodenum (trypsinogen → trypsin via enterokinase)', 'Pancreatic proteases are secreted as inactive zymogens and activated in the duodenum; enterokinase (enteropeptidase) converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates the others.'),
('f9c148b9-94a7-59be-ac05-89478fb78180'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Pancreatic secretory trypsin inhibitor (PSTI):', '["Protects pancreas from premature trypsin activation","Bile cofactor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates trypsin mediated by specific receptor activation","Digests trypsin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inactivates trypsin in the gut lumen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protects pancreas from premature trypsin activation', 'PSTI, present in pancreatic juice, inhibits trypsin inside the pancreas and pancreatic duct, preventing premature activation of other proteases and autodigestion.'),
('35827b09-3d4d-5254-b3ef-ad2a2527351b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Secretin stimulates:', '["Insulin resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreatic HCO3- and water secretion (from S cells in duodenum in response to acid)","Enzyme secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Acid secretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gallbladder contraction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic HCO3- and water secretion (from S cells in duodenum in response to acid)', 'Acid in the duodenum stimulates S cells to release secretin, which promotes secretion of bicarbonate-rich pancreatic fluid to neutralize the acid.'),
('1ce8cc46-bc7c-54dc-b45f-9129dc25095d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which intestinal cells secrete cholecystokinin (CCK) in response to fat and protein in the duodenum?', '["G cells of the gastric antrum","S cells of the duodenum","K cells of the duodenum","L cells of the distal ileum","I cells of duodenum in response to fat and protein"]'::jsonb, 'I cells of duodenum in response to fat and protein', 'CCK is released by I cells in the duodenum and jejunum in response to fat and protein, stimulating gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion.'),
('c4105502-0249-54f7-a650-41869dac3542'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile:', '["Concentrates 2× resulting in decreased functional activity","Stores only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Concentrates bile 5-10× by absorbing water (maximum storage 50-100 mL)","Concentrates 15-20× without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilutes bile leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Concentrates bile 5-10× by absorbing water (maximum storage 50-100 mL)', 'The gallbladder concentrates bile 5-10 fold by absorbing water and can store approximately 50-100 mL.'),
('57030d93-610f-5e80-8f38-497a70aa8bff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Primary bile acids include:', '["Deoxycholic and lithocholic resulting in decreased functional activity","Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid (synthesized from cholesterol in hepatocytes)","Lithocholic acid only mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesterol esters without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile salts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid (synthesized from cholesterol in hepatocytes)', 'The primary bile acids, cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid, are synthesized from cholesterol in hepatocytes; deoxycholic and lithocholic are secondary bile acids.'),
('6d710dca-df99-5391-9b3b-9d3b918a2674'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Secondary bile acids (deoxycholic and lithocholic) are formed by:', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gallbladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Bacterial dehydroxylation in colon → deoxycholic (from cholic) and lithocholic (from CDCA)","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial dehydroxylation in colon → deoxycholic (from cholic) and lithocholic (from CDCA)', 'Colonic bacteria dehydroxylate primary bile acids: cholic acid becomes deoxycholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid becomes lithocholic acid.'),
('9f511abe-448e-5849-b5b1-10d28a059cc6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Functions of bile salts include:', '["Emulsify fat (detergent action)","Promote absorption of fat-soluble vitamins","Form mixed micelles for absorption","Activate (facilitate) pancreatic lipase","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Bile salts emulsify fat, form mixed micelles that carry lipids and fat-soluble vitamins to the enterocyte, and facilitate pancreatic lipase activity.'),
('30d901ac-85f2-5669-b336-1b1551dc7483'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The enterohepatic circulation of bile acids involves:', '["Not recycled resulting in decreased functional activity","Stored in gallbladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile acids reabsorbed in terminal ileum → portal vein → liver → resecreted in bile (recycles 6-10×/day)","Recycled once without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Excreted in stool leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bile acids reabsorbed in terminal ileum → portal vein → liver → resecreted in bile (recycles 6-10×/day)', 'Bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, return to the liver via the portal vein, and are resecreted into bile, recycling about 6-10 times per day.'),
('699b2512-969e-5c32-aa04-5f797fc4cc06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The daily bile acid pool is approximately:', '["10 g resulting in decreased functional activity","3-5 g (recycles 6-10 times/day)","20 g mediated by specific receptor activation","15 g without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 g leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '3-5 g (recycles 6-10 times/day)', 'The bile acid pool is about 3-5 g and recirculates 6-10 times each day through the enterohepatic circulation.'),
('8e07671d-1519-5185-b760-d412e213a3be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the most common type of gallstone?', '["Pigment stones (black with hemolysis; brown with infection) and mixed stones","Pigment stones are the most common type","Mixed stones are the most common type","Cholesterol stones (80%)","Pure calcium stones are the most common type"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol stones (80%)', 'Approximately 80% of gallstones are cholesterol stones; pigment stones (black with hemolysis, brown with infection) and mixed stones are less common.'),
('ae0182b8-0f29-56fc-96d3-83b0302a155b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Fat digestion occurs mainly in the:', '["Small intestine (duodenum and jejunum)","Esophagus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colon mediated by specific receptor activation","Mouth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Small intestine (duodenum and jejunum)', 'Most dietary fat is digested in the duodenum and proximal jejunum by pancreatic lipase aided by bile salts.'),
('308f852e-2a35-5f81-a267-33a386954d1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Protein digestion begins in the:', '["Colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Mouth only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stomach (pepsin) and continues in small intestine (trypsin chymotrypsin carboxypeptidase)","Esophagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Small intestine only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (pepsin) and continues in small intestine (trypsin chymotrypsin carboxypeptidase)', 'Protein digestion begins in the stomach with pepsin and continues in the small intestine through pancreatic trypsin, chymotrypsin and carboxypeptidase.'),
('6b1119d6-9c2d-54cc-8efc-43a2543da8a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Carbohydrate digestion begins in the:', '["Esophagus","Small intestine","Mouth (salivary amylase)","Stomach","Colon"]'::jsonb, 'Mouth (salivary amylase)', 'Carbohydrate digestion begins in the mouth, where salivary amylase hydrolyzes starch into shorter polysaccharides before pancreatic amylase continues the process.'),
('88dc48b3-4958-5c6a-ad44-a7f5f47aab8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Lactose intolerance is due to:', '["Lipase deficiency resulting in decreased functional activity","Amylase deficiency accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protease deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Lactase deficiency → undigested lactose → osmotic diarrhea + gas (bloating)","Sucrase deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lactase deficiency → undigested lactose → osmotic diarrhea + gas (bloating)', 'Lactase deficiency leaves lactose undigested; the lactose is fermented in the colon, producing osmotic diarrhea and gas with bloating.'),
('f93d840c-7e90-558f-bf27-13eb31f6e84d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Monosaccharide absorption in the small intestine:', '["All by GLUT5 resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only active accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All by SGLT1 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only passive without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose and galactose: SGLT1 (Na+ dependent secondary active transport). Fructose: GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion)"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose and galactose: SGLT1 (Na+ dependent secondary active transport). Fructose: GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion)', 'Glucose and galactose are absorbed by the Na+-dependent SGLT1 transporter, while fructose is absorbed by the facilitated-diffusion transporter GLUT5.'),
('4b60e325-304d-5ff5-81db-1ad2ec6751da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the underlying defect in glucose-galactose malabsorption?', '["Defective GLUT5 transporter resulting in decreased functional activity","Diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A defective SGLT1 sodium-glucose cotransporter","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IBD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A defective SGLT1 sodium-glucose cotransporter', 'Glucose-galactose malabsorption is caused by mutations in the SGLT1 sodium-glucose cotransporter, producing severe diarrhea after glucose or galactose ingestion; fructose absorption is unaffected.'),
('10f92fa6-b67b-5b17-b7ad-e6a9b1b6a643'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Fructose absorption uses:', '["SGLT1 resulting in decreased functional activity","GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion at brush border) and GLUT2 (basolateral)","GLUT4 mediated by specific receptor activation","Active transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sodium dependent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion at brush border) and GLUT2 (basolateral)', 'Fructose enters the enterocyte by facilitated diffusion through apical GLUT5 and exits basolaterally via GLUT2.'),
('360b4ae4-2763-5881-a2f3-125b4661e242'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Dipeptides and tripeptides are absorbed via:', '["Facilitated diffusion resulting in decreased functional activity","Pinocytosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GLUT5 mediated by specific receptor activation","PEPT1 (H+-coupled cotransporter at brush border)","SGLT1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PEPT1 (H+-coupled cotransporter at brush border)', 'Di- and tripeptides are taken up across the brush border by the H+-coupled cotransporter PEPT1 and then hydrolyzed intracellularly.'),
('2a14eff8-1706-5a87-b36a-da1bf2835eb3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The small intestine has the largest surface area due to:', '["Microvilli (brush border)","Circular folds (valves of Kerckring)","Only microvilli","Villi","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Circular folds, villi and microvilli progressively amplify the small intestinal surface area for absorption.'),
('58dc993f-5aab-5466-87bc-56b205598f27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Na+ absorption in the small intestine:', '["Na+/H+ exchange (NHE3) resulting in decreased functional activity","ENaC (colon) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple mechanisms: Na+/glucose cotransport","Only one mechanism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Electroneutral Na+Cl- leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple mechanisms: Na+/glucose cotransport', 'In the small intestine sodium is absorbed by several mechanisms, including Na+-glucose cotransport (SGLT1), Na+/H+ exchange (NHE3) and electroneutral NaCl absorption.'),
('d6108420-0838-5284-abc3-c1599f4d37fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Water absorption across the intestinal epithelium occurs by:', '["Active transport resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple diffusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osmotic (follows actively absorbed solutes)","Filtration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pinocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osmotic (follows actively absorbed solutes)', 'Water moves passively across the intestinal epithelium by osmosis, following the osmotic gradient created by actively absorbed solutes such as sodium and glucose.'),
('9ec1d797-becd-565d-ba45-92068ca8a9b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which mechanism accounts for chloride absorption in the ileum and colon?', '["CFTR (secretory)","Only paracellular","Na+-Cl- cotransport","Only passive","In ileum/colon: Cl-/HCO3- exchanger"]'::jsonb, 'In ileum/colon: Cl-/HCO3- exchanger', 'In the ileum and colon, chloride is absorbed in exchange for bicarbonate via the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger, with the remaining chloride absorbed paracellularly.'),
('656c5aae-1560-5b87-8ecb-a9459c2998f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) is a:', '["Water channel resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ channel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bicarbonate pump mediated by specific receptor activation","K+ channel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","cAMP-activated Cl- channel (secretory defects in CF cause thick secretions)"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP-activated Cl- channel (secretory defects in CF cause thick secretions)', 'CFTR is a cAMP-activated chloride channel; defective secretion of Cl- and water in cystic fibrosis produces thick, dehydrated secretions.'),
('26b087b2-a019-509f-98ed-308d6aaa0483'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Oral rehydration solution (ORS) containing glucose is effective in secretory diarrhea because:', '["Killing bacteria resulting in decreased functional activity","Slowing secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binding toxin mediated by specific receptor activation","SGLT1 (Na+/glucose cotransport) drives Na+ and water absorption despite CFTR activation","Neutralizing toxin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SGLT1 (Na+/glucose cotransport) drives Na+ and water absorption despite CFTR activation', 'Glucose in ORS is absorbed with sodium via SGLT1, which remains functional in secretory diarrhea and drives coupled water absorption even while CFTR is activated.'),
('e3b02222-4171-5c2f-b2a3-066a35c78d67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Colonic functions include:', '["All of the above","Water and electrolyte absorption","Storage of feces","Formation and expulsion of stool","Microbiota fermentation (SCFA production)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The colon absorbs water and electrolytes, stores and expels feces, and hosts microbiota that ferment fiber to short-chain fatty acids.'),
('90655976-3402-57f0-98f0-3343404d346f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The main short-chain fatty acids produced by colonic fermentation of fiber are:', '["Vitamins resulting in decreased functional activity","Amino acids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acetate propionate butyrate (energy source for colonocytes + systemic effects)","Bile acids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholesterol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acetate propionate butyrate (energy source for colonocytes + systemic effects)', 'Fermentation of dietary fiber by colonic bacteria yields acetate, propionate and butyrate, which serve as the main fuel for colonocytes.'),
('e497806a-fcb2-5e80-b6bd-0927371d4ee8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which sequence correctly describes the defecation reflex?', '["Somatic only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stool in rectum → stretch receptors → parasympathetic (pelvic) → internal anal sphincter relaxes + rectal contraction → external sphincter voluntarily relaxed","Brain only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Voluntary only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stool in rectum → stretch receptors → parasympathetic (pelvic) → internal anal sphincter relaxes + rectal contraction → external sphincter voluntarily relaxed', 'Rectal filling activates stretch receptors, triggering pelvic parasympathetic impulses that contract the rectum and relax the internal sphincter while the external sphincter relaxes voluntarily.'),
('6d5b2391-2309-59b0-a73e-7512f56577fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt1: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('dd0ea03f-9af4-5dd5-8e71-771fe3aa90cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The rectoanal inhibitory reflex (rectal sampling) is characterized by:', '["Internal sphincter contracts on distension resulting in decreased functional activity","External sphincter contracts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reverse of the normal sequence mediated by specific receptor activation","Rectal distension → internal sphincter relaxes (sampling of stool content)","Loss of voluntary control leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rectal distension → internal sphincter relaxes (sampling of stool content)', 'Rectal distension transiently relaxes the internal anal sphincter, allowing the rectal mucosa to sample the stool contents.'),
('e19dd4cd-6a90-50ea-b179-7179dfe15f5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionosis) is characterized by:', '["Absence of ENS ganglia in distal colon → tonic contraction → obstruction hypersegmentation","Diarrhea alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only vomiting mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperganglionosis of the distal colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilated colon only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of ENS ganglia in distal colon → tonic contraction → obstruction hypersegmentation', 'In Hirschsprung disease, congenital absence of enteric ganglia in the distal colon prevents relaxation, causing tonic contraction, functional obstruction and proximal megacolon.'),
('f9a6e6a1-7b1f-5b08-af9a-7b806c4998eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The migrating motor complex (MMC) is characterized by:', '["Bursts of contractions during fasting (interdigestive)","Continuous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occurs only during sleep mediated by specific receptor activation","Irregular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Postprandial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bursts of contractions during fasting (interdigestive)', 'The MMC is a cyclic, interdigestive (fasting) pattern of contractions that sweeps the gut clean between meals.'),
('650105c0-ebb6-554f-86e4-1298065e8ab9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which cells in the gastric fundus produce ghrelin, the orexigenic (hunger-stimulating) hormone?', '["P/D1 cells of gastric fundus (orexigenic)","Pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ileum mediated by specific receptor activation","Duodenum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'P/D1 cells of gastric fundus (orexigenic)', 'Ghrelin is produced by P/D1 cells in the gastric fundus, and its secretion rises before meals to stimulate appetite.'),
('122baaa8-2e93-54db-b202-2010f4b4a14f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Obestatin is:', '["From liver resulting in decreased functional activity","From colon accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Same as ghrelin mediated by specific receptor activation","A peptide derived from preproghrelin (appetite suppressant)","Inactive leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A peptide derived from preproghrelin (appetite suppressant)', 'Obestatin is a peptide cleaved from preproghrelin that suppresses appetite, in contrast to ghrelin.'),
('3acad988-6547-5276-a666-73ecdbbd120b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide) is secreted by:', '["G cells resulting in decreased functional activity","S cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","I cells mediated by specific receptor activation","K cells of duodenum in response to glucose/fat → insulinotropic (incretin)","L cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'K cells of duodenum in response to glucose/fat → insulinotropic (incretin)', 'GIP is released by K cells of the duodenum and proximal jejunum in response to glucose and fat, and it potentiates glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (incretin effect).'),
('dc7bd5c3-b69b-5221-8ea3-e019b62663e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'GLP-1 is secreted by:', '["Pancreatic α cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastric G cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Duodenal K cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatic delta cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L cells of distal ileum/colon in response to nutrients → incretin"]'::jsonb, 'L cells of distal ileum/colon in response to nutrients → incretin', 'GLP-1 is released by L cells of the distal ileum and colon in response to nutrients and acts as an incretin that augments insulin secretion.'),
('c8ee846e-7977-5783-9515-e63d12ec44ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric emptying of liquids is best described as:', '["Only with solids","Same as solids","Speed up only","Slower than solids","Rapid (exponential - T1/2 20 min)"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid (exponential - T1/2 20 min)', 'Liquids empty from the stomach rapidly along an exponential curve with a half-time of about 20 minutes.'),
('14dcdb01-b89b-5f4d-90c9-fd1d941728f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric emptying of solids is characterized by:', '["Only exponential emptying","No lag phase","Lag phase (trituration) → linear emptying phase","Only linear emptying","Emptying occurs immediately after ingestion"]'::jsonb, 'Lag phase (trituration) → linear emptying phase', 'Solids first undergo a lag phase during which they are triturated to small particles, followed by a linear emptying phase.'),
('d42b3c4c-d75e-5c22-b6ae-b03099dd0b33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The presence of acid in the duodenum triggers:', '["Motilin resulting in decreased functional activity","Acid → secretin release → inhibits gastric acid secretion and slows emptying","GIP mediated by specific receptor activation","CCK without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ghrelin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acid → secretin release → inhibits gastric acid secretion and slows emptying', 'Duodenal acid stimulates S cells to release secretin, which inhibits gastric acid secretion and slows gastric emptying.'),
('355cad12-fd64-5969-8eb4-5dbf4fe58d45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Enterokinase (enteropeptidase):', '["Digests fat resulting in decreased functional activity","Digests starch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates trypsinogen to trypsin (crucial first step in pancreatic enzyme activation)","Digests disaccharides without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Digests protein in the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activates trypsinogen to trypsin (crucial first step in pancreatic enzyme activation)', 'Enterokinase on the duodenal brush border converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the other pancreatic proteases.'),
('6af8b9bf-e41e-5fd3-b961-291cc045b3ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Carbohydrates are absorbed predominantly as:', '["Monosaccharides (glucose galactose fructose)","Polysaccharides accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Oligosaccharides mediated by specific receptor activation","Disaccharides without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Starch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Monosaccharides (glucose galactose fructose)', 'Only monosaccharides are absorbed; disaccharides and larger carbohydrates are first hydrolyzed to glucose, galactose and fructose by brush-border enzymes.'),
('caeeb668-4fef-50b1-b2f0-974eef8caee0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'After absorption, long-chain fatty acids are transported from enterocytes:', '["To colon resulting in decreased functional activity","To liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","To stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Directly to portal vein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Enterocytes → secreted into lacteals (lymphatics)"]'::jsonb, 'Enterocytes → secreted into lacteals (lymphatics)', 'Long-chain fatty acids are re-esterified into triglycerides, packaged into chylomicrons, and secreted into the lacteals (lymphatics).'),
('09b13670-8e19-5305-a2d7-e87f9ee00e86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'ApoB48 is specific to:', '["LDL resulting in decreased functional activity","VLDL (from liver) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","IDL mediated by specific receptor activation","HDL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chylomicrons (from small intestine)"]'::jsonb, 'Chylomicrons (from small intestine)', 'ApoB48 is found exclusively on chylomicrons synthesized by the small intestine, whereas the liver produces ApoB100 on VLDL and LDL.'),
('2ab10e8a-67b1-5cf0-8f42-44c9cab60d43'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Whipple disease is caused by:', '["Crohn disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Malabsorption only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tropheryma whipplei infection → malabsorption + arthralgias + neurologic symptoms","Fat only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Celiac-like leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tropheryma whipplei infection → malabsorption + arthralgias + neurologic symptoms', 'Whipple disease is a systemic infection by Tropheryma whipplei causing malabsorption, arthralgias, weight loss and neurologic symptoms.'),
('b530e2b9-592c-5dc5-b573-a62a4fb2774e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) includes:', '["Only spleen resulting in decreased functional activity","Peyer patches (ileum) + intraepithelial lymphocytes + lamina propria lymphocytes + mesenteric lymph nodes","Only tonsils mediated by specific receptor activation","Only lymph nodes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only appendix leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peyer patches (ileum) + intraepithelial lymphocytes + lamina propria lymphocytes + mesenteric lymph nodes', 'GALT comprises Peyer patches of the ileum, intraepithelial lymphocytes, lamina propria lymphocytes and mesenteric lymph nodes.'),
('6daac747-8b63-5782-8cd3-cd76ae479b0e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Secretory IgA (sIgA):', '["IgG resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","IgM accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","B cells → plasma cells → dimeric IgA + secretory component → released at mucosa → neutralizes pathogens","IgE without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'B cells → plasma cells → dimeric IgA + secretory component → released at mucosa → neutralizes pathogens', 'Mucosal plasma cells produce dimeric IgA that is transported across enterocytes with a secretory component, then released at the mucosa to neutralize pathogens.'),
('83470c95-b47f-5ef3-9b7f-e3ee486b21d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Oral tolerance is:', '["Systemic immune unresponsiveness to fed antigens (prevents food allergy)","Intolerance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Allergy to food mediated by specific receptor activation","Anaphylaxis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sensitivity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Systemic immune unresponsiveness to fed antigens (prevents food allergy)', 'Oral tolerance is the active suppression of systemic immune responses to dietary antigens, preventing food allergy.'),
('93e12574-7603-526c-8cbb-b6537de2c0e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which of the following is a product of colonic microbial fermentation of dietary fiber?', '["Ferment indigestible fiber (SCFAs)","Short-chain fatty acids","Regulate metabolism","Synthesize vitamins (K B12 biotin)","Influence immune system"]'::jsonb, 'Short-chain fatty acids', 'Fermentation of dietary fiber by the colonic microbiota produces short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate), the main fuel for colonocytes.'),
('bbecc571-dd90-5eaa-b507-d3efbec57a96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Prebiotics are:', '["Probiotics resulting in decreased functional activity","Non-digestible fibers (inulin FOS GOS) that stimulate beneficial bacteria growth","Digestive enzymes mediated by specific receptor activation","Proteins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Minerals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Non-digestible fibers (inulin FOS GOS) that stimulate beneficial bacteria growth', 'Prebiotics are non-digestible fibers such as inulin, FOS and GOS that promote the growth of beneficial gut bacteria.'),
('124feb99-178a-58ba-9a28-b8f5fd685b9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Synbiotics are:', '["Enzymes resulting in decreased functional activity","Antibiotics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Combination of prebiotics + probiotics (synergistic)","Only probiotics without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only prebiotics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Combination of prebiotics + probiotics (synergistic)', 'Synbiotics combine prebiotics and probiotics to produce a synergistic effect on the gut microbiome.'),
('0fc0e4e1-c46a-5097-a7a0-d06ded1a3a99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The primary fuels for colonocytes are:', '["Ketone bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Short-chain fatty acids (especially butyrate)","Glucose mediated by specific receptor activation","Amino acids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fatty acids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Short-chain fatty acids (especially butyrate)', 'Colonocytes preferentially oxidize short-chain fatty acids, particularly butyrate, as their primary energy source.'),
('eec2fbbe-d838-5374-8d1d-ea91f15b0f18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('ff45135d-f754-5428-a04d-ff73a00eea82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The function of the ileocecal sphincter is to:', '["Prevents reflux from colon to ileum + regulates flow from ileum to cecum","Only regulates flow accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stores chyme in the ileum mediated by specific receptor activation","Only prevents reflux without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prevents all passage of contents leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents reflux from colon to ileum + regulates flow from ileum to cecum', 'The ileocecal sphincter prevents reflux of colonic contents into the ileum and regulates the passage of chyme into the cecum.'),
('5141ea69-195d-5804-a6a0-d55e6cdb3e18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Receptive relaxation of the proximal stomach is mediated by:', '["CCK resulting in decreased functional activity","Histamine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gastrin mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagovagal reflex (NO/VIP → proximal stomach relaxation upon swallowing)","Acetylcholine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vagovagal reflex (NO/VIP → proximal stomach relaxation upon swallowing)', 'Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which the swallowing reflex releases NO and VIP, relaxing the proximal stomach.'),
('a28de827-f026-5558-944c-a66cda931218'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric accommodation is:', '["Secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contraction mediated by specific receptor activation","Absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Maintained relaxation of proximal stomach during meal (allows large volume with low pressure)"]'::jsonb, 'Maintained relaxation of proximal stomach during meal (allows large volume with low pressure)', 'Gastric accommodation is the sustained relaxation of the proximal stomach during a meal that allows large volumes to be stored at low pressure.'),
('fa457d0f-12de-5efd-92b3-c1ac5960671b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The migrating motor complex (MMC) consists of:', '["Phase III (regular contractions). Cycle 90-110 min","Phase I only","Phase II (irregular)","Phases I, II, and III in a repeating sequence","Phase III only"]'::jsonb, 'Phases I, II, and III in a repeating sequence', 'Each MMC cycle has three phases: phase I (quiescence), phase II (irregular contractions) and phase III (intense regular contractions), repeating every 90-110 minutes during fasting.'),
('5f0adf78-7f4e-59c8-84d9-c9f0ef3f2d09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Motilin:', '["CCK release resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretin release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slow gastric emptying mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Gastrin release without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Peptide hormone from duodenal M cells. Released during fasting. Stimulates MMC phase III. Erythromycin is motilin agonist"]'::jsonb, 'Peptide hormone from duodenal M cells. Released during fasting. Stimulates MMC phase III. Erythromycin is motilin agonist', 'Motilin is released from duodenal M cells during fasting and stimulates MMC phase III; erythromycin is a motilin receptor agonist.'),
('e0c10961-bece-5733-b470-574592247fd8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Ghrelin is:', '["An appetite-suppressing hormone from the liver resulting in decreased functional activity","A hormone that increases after meals accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A hormone synthesized in the pancreatic beta cells mediated by specific receptor activation","An enzyme involved in starch digestion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hunger hormone from gastric fundus (X/A-like cells). Increases before meals. Stimulates appetite"]'::jsonb, 'Hunger hormone from gastric fundus (X/A-like cells). Increases before meals. Stimulates appetite', 'Ghrelin is the hunger hormone secreted by X/A-like cells of the gastric fundus; its levels rise before meals and it stimulates appetite.'),
('326fae3f-8ac5-5d38-adee-21e432dbe79b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The pylorus:', '["Regulates emptying of liquids only resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretes intrinsic factor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretes digestive enzymes mediated by specific receptor activation","Is a purely passive valve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Regulates gastric emptying of solids (grinding to <2 mm). Tonic contraction + phasic pressure. Relaxes with NO/VIP"]'::jsonb, 'Regulates gastric emptying of solids (grinding to <2 mm). Tonic contraction + phasic pressure. Relaxes with NO/VIP', 'The pylorus regulates emptying of solids by grinding them to particles smaller than 2 mm, using tonic and phasic contraction, and relaxes under NO/VIP influence.'),
('0c83cac1-6f01-5cef-8cc9-844f95cbf995'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric emptying of liquids is best characterized by:', '["Rapid (T1/2 20 min). Fundal pressure gradient. Regulated by duodenal feedback","Slow and controlled by antral peristalsis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slow (T1/2 2 h) mediated by specific receptor activation","Only semi-solids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dependent on particle size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid (T1/2 20 min). Fundal pressure gradient. Regulated by duodenal feedback', 'Liquids empty rapidly (half-time about 20 min) driven by the fundal pressure gradient and are regulated by duodenal feedback.'),
('d2af773e-a790-58c2-a4e6-ba558e9208fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric emptying of solids is best characterized by:', '["Rapid emptying without a lag phase resulting in decreased functional activity","Emptying independent of particle size accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lag phase (grinding <2 mm) then linear emptying. T1/2 2-3h. Regulated by particle size","Faster than liquids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Controlled solely by gastric volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lag phase (grinding <2 mm) then linear emptying. T1/2 2-3h. Regulated by particle size', 'Solids show a lag phase during grinding to <2 mm, then empty linearly over about 2-3 hours, with emptying regulated by particle size.'),
('1be0a663-0d3f-5b97-8a65-4ae09b619754'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which nutrient is the most potent inhibitor of gastric emptying?', '["Fat","Acid","Hypertonic solutions","Amino acids","Fiber"]'::jsonb, 'Fat', 'Fat in the duodenum is the most potent inhibitor of gastric emptying, acting through CCK and the enterogastric reflex.'),
('c3718cdc-83a7-5deb-b72f-7379fad2cd4b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Functional dyspepsia is defined as:', '["Upper GI symptoms without organic cause (normal endoscopy). EPS vs PDS","Symptoms with an identifiable organic cause","Predominantly lower GI symptoms","Requires abnormal endoscopy findings","Always associated with weight loss"]'::jsonb, 'Upper GI symptoms without organic cause (normal endoscopy). EPS vs PDS', 'Functional dyspepsia is upper GI discomfort without an organic cause on endoscopy, subdivided into epigastric pain syndrome (EPS) and postprandial distress syndrome (PDS).'),
('e4ecac44-a4fe-5798-8808-27966e540736'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Cholecystokinin (CCK):', '["Only contracts the gallbladder resulting in decreased functional activity","Increases gastric acid secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibits pancreatic enzyme secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Released from I cells by fat/protein. Increases enzyme secretion (acinar). Contracts gallbladder","Decreases bile flow leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Released from I cells by fat/protein. Increases enzyme secretion (acinar). Contracts gallbladder', 'CCK is released from duodenal I cells by fat and protein, stimulates pancreatic enzyme (acinar) secretion and contracts the gallbladder.'),
('58757613-fd03-597a-9952-7d07255baebb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The major regulator of pancreatic enzyme secretion is:', '["CCK (major)","Secretin","Somatostatin","ACh (vagal)","GRP, with negative feedback via trypsin degradation of CCK-releasing factor"]'::jsonb, 'CCK (major)', 'CCK is the major hormonal stimulator of pancreatic enzyme (acinar) secretion, with vagal ACh and GRP as secondary regulators.'),
('386606f5-669c-52a6-9070-af6e233ec7da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Approximately 90% of nutrient absorption occurs in the:', '["Duodenum and proximal jejunum. 90% of absorption","Stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Rectum mediated by specific receptor activation","Ileum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Colon leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum and proximal jejunum. 90% of absorption', 'About 90% of nutrient absorption takes place in the duodenum and proximal jejunum.'),
('af4cc92f-7de2-5bae-9f2e-2e2c3a44d539'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Sucrase-isomaltase deficiency:', '["Results in normal carbohydrate digestion","Is caused by a defective SGLT1 transporter","Congenital AR. Diarrhea","A benign condition with no clinical consequences","Only affects fat digestion"]'::jsonb, 'Congenital AR. Diarrhea', 'Sucrase-isomaltase deficiency is an autosomal recessive disorder causing osmotic diarrhea when sucrose and starch are ingested.'),
('0cd233e5-18b2-503d-b497-cf045df10bbf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which statement correctly describes the pathways of carbohydrate digestion and absorption?', '["GLUT5 for fructose resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","All carbohydrates are absorbed as intact polysaccharides accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Starch to amylase to maltose/maltotriose/limit dextrins to brush border to glucose. Lactose to lactase: glucose+galactose. Sucrose to sucrase: glucose+fructose. SGLT1 for glucose/galactose","SGLT1 transports fructose without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose is absorbed by simple diffusion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Starch to amylase to maltose/maltotriose/limit dextrins to brush border to glucose. Lactose to lactase: glucose+galactose. Sucrose to sucrase: glucose+fructose. SGLT1 for glucose/galactose', 'Starch is digested by amylase to maltose/maltotriose and limit dextrins, then brush-border enzymes yield monosaccharides; glucose and galactose are absorbed via SGLT1.'),
('a448f185-f8b6-5007-beeb-f23ee8b12122'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'GLUT5 is:', '["Only galactose resulting in decreased functional activity","Only glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fructose is transported by SGLT1 mediated by specific receptor activation","GLUT5 is located on the basolateral membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fructose-specific apical transporter in jejunum. Upregulated by fructose. Fructose malabsorption: gas"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose-specific apical transporter in jejunum. Upregulated by fructose. Fructose malabsorption: gas', 'GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter in the jejunum, upregulated by dietary fructose; unabsorbed fructose causes gas and diarrhea.'),
('eb8f271a-9050-575c-85d4-bec87020c67d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Protein digestion is initiated in the stomach by:', '["Pepsin (pH 2-3) → polypeptides","Trypsin → amino acids","Amylase → maltose","Lipase → fatty acids","Enterokinase → peptides"]'::jsonb, 'Pepsin (pH 2-3) → polypeptides', 'Pepsin, active at pH 2-3, begins protein digestion by cleaving dietary protein into large polypeptides.'),
('0e295813-453c-5da1-967c-bd41887d730c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which statement best summarizes the pathway of dietary fat digestion?', '["Lingual and gastric lipases act mainly on short- and medium-chain triglycerides resulting in decreased functional activity","Only pancreatic lipase is involved accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lingual/gastric lipase (short/medium chain). Pancreatic lipase (long-chain TG+colipase). Bile salt emulsification. Products: 2-MG + FA","Bile salts directly hydrolyze triglycerides without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lipid digestion yields free glycerol and three fatty acids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lingual/gastric lipase (short/medium chain). Pancreatic lipase (long-chain TG+colipase). Bile salt emulsification. Products: 2-MG + FA', 'Lingual and gastric lipases digest short- and medium-chain triglycerides, while pancreatic lipase with colipase and bile salts digests long-chain triglycerides to 2-monoglycerides and fatty acids.'),
('c0e6e32c-834f-54ea-bba9-225d3731d8a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the primary role of colipase in the digestion of dietary fats?', '["Emulsification of large fat droplets","Hydrolysis of triglycerides into monoglycerides","Anchoring pancreatic lipase to the lipid surface in the presence of bile salts","Transport of fatty acids across the enterocyte membrane","Synthesis of chylomicrons"]'::jsonb, 'Anchoring pancreatic lipase to the lipid surface in the presence of bile salts', 'Colipase anchors pancreatic lipase to the lipid droplet surface in the presence of bile salts, which would otherwise displace lipase from the oil-water interface.'),
('065a818a-38c3-5b3d-a4dc-9c39cbbe6e80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Bile salts enhance fat digestion primarily by:', '["Emulsify fat (increase surface area for lipase)","Digesting triglycerides directly","Neutralizing gastric acid","Activating enterokinase","Transporting fat to the liver"]'::jsonb, 'Emulsify fat (increase surface area for lipase)', 'Bile salts emulsify dietary fat into small droplets, increasing the surface area available for pancreatic lipase.'),
('b68c1fb7-1af7-50c1-940c-6adc28c28865'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Chylomicrons are best described as:', '["ApoB100 containing resulting in decreased functional activity","Secreted directly into the portal vein accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Lipoprotein transporting dietary lipids. ApoB48. Secreted into lacteals","Secreted into the hepatic sinusoids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Transporting endogenous liver triglycerides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lipoprotein transporting dietary lipids. ApoB48. Secreted into lacteals', 'Chylomicrons carry dietary (exogenous) lipids, contain ApoB48, and are secreted into the lacteals and lymphatic system.'),
('c7cff9c1-0180-5622-8d16-76eaaeee8b5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) are absorbed:', '["Identically to long-chain triglycerides resulting in decreased functional activity","Via the lymphatic system as chylomicrons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","As chylomicrons via the lacteals mediated by specific receptor activation","Exclusively through the lymphatic route without affecting the primary tissue pathway","MCTs (C8-C12) absorbed directly into portal vein. No chylomicron/micelle needed. Water-soluble"]'::jsonb, 'MCTs (C8-C12) absorbed directly into portal vein. No chylomicron/micelle needed. Water-soluble', 'Medium-chain triglycerides are water-soluble and are absorbed directly into the portal blood without requiring micelles or chylomicron formation.'),
('3471f578-d91a-5806-b663-77cc8f3acdeb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Bile acids are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum via:', '["ASBT in the terminal ileum","NTCP in the liver","Passive diffusion in the stomach","GLUT5 in the jejunum","SGLT1 in the duodenum"]'::jsonb, 'ASBT in the terminal ileum', 'Bile acids are actively reabsorbed in the terminal ileum by the apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter (ASBT), then carried to the liver via the portal vein.'),
('cf3392e0-9311-5aa8-9ab9-6e2435256283'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Bile acid malabsorption (e.g., after ileal resection) causes diarrhea because:', '["Excess BA in colon stimulates water/electrolyte secretion and motility: watery diarrhea. Ileal resection","Bile acids in the colon inhibit water secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile acids are absorbed in the duodenum mediated by specific receptor activation","Bile acids decrease colonic motility without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile acids are not reabsorbed in the terminal ileum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excess BA in colon stimulates water/electrolyte secretion and motility: watery diarrhea. Ileal resection', 'When the terminal ileum is resected, excess bile acids reach the colon, where they stimulate water and electrolyte secretion and motility, producing watery diarrhea.'),
('3bfb6cf6-1706-546e-8db3-b08595085705'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The release of vitamin B12 from R-binder (haptocorrin) in the duodenum is brought about by:', '["Pancreatic protease","Gastric acid","Intrinsic factor","Bile salts","Cubilin"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic protease', 'Pancreatic proteases degrade R-binder in the duodenum, freeing vitamin B12 to bind intrinsic factor for ileal absorption.'),
('3fed608a-ceab-57f9-9859-843c0033a902'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Dietary folate (polyglutamate) is absorbed after:', '["Brush-border conjugase converts polyglutamate to monoglutamate, absorbed via PCFT in the duodenum/jejunum","Reduction by dihydrofolate reductase before absorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Passive diffusion across the colonic mucosa mediated by specific receptor activation","Absorption occurs in the terminal ileum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Folate is absorbed as polyglutamate without modification leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Brush-border conjugase converts polyglutamate to monoglutamate, absorbed via PCFT in the duodenum/jejunum', 'Brush-border conjugase hydrolyzes folate polyglutamate to monoglutamate, which is absorbed by the proton-coupled folate transporter (PCFT) in the duodenum and jejunum.'),
('cd2da960-e73e-508f-ac79-50fbe42acc21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Iron is absorbed mainly in the:', '["Colon resulting in decreased functional activity","Only non-heme iron is absorbed in the stomach accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption occurs only in the colon mediated by specific receptor activation","Stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Duodenum/jejunum. Heme via HCP1. Non-heme Fe3+ to DcytB to Fe2+ to DMT1. Ferroportin basolateral to transferrin"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum/jejunum. Heme via HCP1. Non-heme Fe3+ to DcytB to Fe2+ to DMT1. Ferroportin basolateral to transferrin', 'Iron is absorbed in the duodenum and proximal jejunum: heme via HCP1 and non-heme iron reduced by DcytB and taken up by DMT1, with basolateral efflux via ferroportin.'),
('22054103-539a-5c31-8f87-7b7c0d13be85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which statement correctly describes hepcidin?', '["Increases intestinal iron absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Synthesized in the kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Key iron regulator. Reduces intestinal Fe absorption + macrophage Fe release. IL-6 increases (anemia of chronic disease)","Decreases in chronic inflammation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secreted by intestinal enterocytes into the gut lumen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Key iron regulator. Reduces intestinal Fe absorption + macrophage Fe release. IL-6 increases (anemia of chronic disease)', 'Hepcidin is the key iron-regulatory hormone that reduces intestinal iron absorption and macrophage iron release; IL-6 raises hepcidin in anemia of chronic disease.'),
('91597b14-d706-58a5-ad6c-097de2f4b2a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Phosphate is absorbed in the small intestine by:', '["NaPi-IIb (SLC34A2) active transport in the duodenum/jejunum","Only passive diffusion","Via the Na+/glucose cotransporter","In the colon via ASBT","Regulated only by dietary calcium"]'::jsonb, 'NaPi-IIb (SLC34A2) active transport in the duodenum/jejunum', 'Phosphate is actively absorbed by the NaPi-IIb (SLC34A2) transporter in the duodenum and jejunum; 1,25(OH)2D upregulates and FGF23 downregulates it.'),
('f3519969-6a7c-5750-b33c-8cee61a27e01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The majority of magnesium is absorbed:', '["Exclusively in the stomach","Jejunum/ileum. Paracellular passive 80%","Colon only","Entirely via transcellular TRPM6/7 active transport","Entirely via paracellular route in the colon"]'::jsonb, 'Jejunum/ileum. Paracellular passive 80%', 'About 80% of magnesium is absorbed passively via the paracellular route in the jejunum and ileum, with a smaller regulated transcellular component.'),
('55f6e199-1ae9-5a9f-b573-96b70a1e0275'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Zinc is absorbed primarily in the:', '["Duodenum/jejunum. ZIP4 apical (up by Zn deficiency)","Solely via basolateral ZnT1","In the colon only","Through passive diffusion in the stomach","Regulated exclusively by dietary iron"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum/jejunum. ZIP4 apical (up by Zn deficiency)', 'Zinc is absorbed in the duodenum and jejunum by apical ZIP4, which is upregulated by zinc deficiency, with basolateral efflux via ZnT1 and metallothionein regulation by MTF-1.'),
('d43c39b9-fdf7-5cca-84a1-fa0dc37e36a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which statement best describes the link between innate immunity and Crohn disease?', '["Crohn disease affects only the colon","Nod2 mutations increase defensin production","Nod2 mutations reduce alpha-defensins","Nod2 mutations enhance the intestinal barrier","Nod2 mutations increase bacterial adherence"]'::jsonb, 'Nod2 mutations reduce alpha-defensins', 'NOD2 mutations reduce alpha-defensin production by Paneth cells, impairing bacterial clearance and predisposing to ileal Crohn disease.'),
('b4488bf8-c898-5e27-9194-7aa7b99d9035'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Secretory IgA is best described as:', '["Monomeric IgA in serum resulting in decreased functional activity","Dimeric IgA + secretory component (poly-IgR). Produced by lamina propria plasma cells","Only IgM mediated by specific receptor activation","Only IgG without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produced by intraepithelial lymphocytes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dimeric IgA + secretory component (poly-IgR). Produced by lamina propria plasma cells', 'Secretory IgA is dimeric IgA produced by lamina propria plasma cells and transported across enterocytes via the poly-Ig receptor, acquiring a secretory component.'),
('ef076608-fc61-5724-99b0-357936bc887e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Tuft cells (chemosensory cells) of the intestinal epithelium:', '["Secrete pepsinogen resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Produce mucus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Detect protists via SUCNR1 (succinate receptor). Initiate type 2 immunity (IL-25 to ILC2). Express bitter taste receptors","Phagocytose bacteria without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absorb vitamin B12 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detect protists via SUCNR1 (succinate receptor). Initiate type 2 immunity (IL-25 to ILC2). Express bitter taste receptors', 'Tuft cells sense protists through the succinate receptor SUCNR1 and secrete IL-25 to activate ILC2 cells, initiating type 2 immunity; they also express bitter taste receptors.'),
('30f1973e-8227-5c42-86f2-55d123e5605c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Enteroendocrine cells of the gut:', '["Constitute approximately 10% of the intestinal epithelium","Are the body''s single largest endocrine organ","Secrete hormones directly into the gut lumen only","Scattered GI epithelium. Secrete hormones to luminal contents. 1% epithelial cells","Are located exclusively in the pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Scattered GI epithelium. Secrete hormones to luminal contents. 1% epithelial cells', 'Enteroendocrine cells are scattered throughout the GI epithelium, comprise about 1% of epithelial cells, and secrete hormones in response to luminal contents.'),
('6f416920-2789-5af5-85b6-966857acb1da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The incretin effect refers to:', '["Insulin response is greater after IV than oral glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Insulin response is identical for oral and IV glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Insulin response is greater after oral than IV glucose mediated by specific receptor activation","Oral > IV glucose insulin response (same load). GIP (K cells) + GLP-1 (L cells). 50-70% postprandial insulin","The effect is mediated entirely by neural reflexes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oral > IV glucose insulin response (same load). GIP (K cells) + GLP-1 (L cells). 50-70% postprandial insulin', 'For the same glucose load, oral glucose produces a greater insulin response than IV glucose because the incretins GIP and GLP-1 enhance insulin secretion; they mediate 50-70% of postprandial insulin release.'),
('456c0b61-caa7-5148-bb58-3fa786837762'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4):', '["Synthesizes GLP-1 resulting in decreased functional activity","Enzyme degrading GLP-1 and GIP (half-life 1-2 min). DPP-4 inhibitors increase active GLP-1","Degrades only GIP mediated by specific receptor activation","Is activated by DPP-4 inhibitors without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a receptor for GLP-1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme degrading GLP-1 and GIP (half-life 1-2 min). DPP-4 inhibitors increase active GLP-1', 'DPP-4 is the enzyme that degrades GLP-1 and GIP, giving them half-lives of 1-2 minutes; DPP-4 inhibitors raise active GLP-1 levels.'),
('89a31dfb-1a92-5e0a-b7b0-d6a578c881be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which adaptive change occurs in the remaining small bowel after massive resection?', '["No adaptive changes occur resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased villus height accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Malabsorption after massive small bowel resection (<200 cm). Adaptation: increased villus height","Decreased crypt depth without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shortened intestinal transit time leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Malabsorption after massive small bowel resection (<200 cm). Adaptation: increased villus height', 'After massive resection (<200 cm of bowel remaining), the bowel adapts by increasing villus height and crypt depth and slowing transit; parenteral nutrition is often needed initially.'),
('a845a8fa-9e3f-53d2-b193-5d7af01670c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) is best defined as:', '["Decreased bacterial counts in the small bowel","Bacterial overgrowth limited to the colon",">10^5 CFU/mL in small bowel. Malabsorption","A purely motor disorder without microbial involvement","Bacterial overgrowth of the stomach only"]'::jsonb, '>10^5 CFU/mL in small bowel. Malabsorption', 'SIBO is the presence of more than 10^5 CFU/mL of bacteria in the small bowel, causing malabsorption with diarrhea, bloating and B12 deficiency.'),
('95393236-fb65-5ed7-b651-1e949f406ad9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Steatorrhea is best defined as:', '["Carbohydrate malabsorption producing watery stools","Protein malabsorption","Fat malabsorption without any change in stool","Normal stool appearance","Fat malabsorption: bulky"]'::jsonb, 'Fat malabsorption: bulky', 'Steatorrhea is fat malabsorption producing bulky, greasy, pale and foul-smelling stools.'),
('1b477c77-a7a9-563f-b2ef-ef832214da5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Steatorrhea in chronic pancreatitis results from:', '["Bile duct obstruction","Inadequate enzymes: maldigestion of fat (steatorrhea)","Inadequate brush border disaccharidases","Reduced intestinal motility","Excess bile salt secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Inadequate enzymes: maldigestion of fat (steatorrhea)', 'In chronic pancreatitis, inadequate pancreatic lipase causes maldigestion of fat and steatorrhea.'),
('a58561ae-ef3a-5432-af7b-6f6bffc44fb5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT):', '["Lipase 30-50k units/meal. Enteric-coated microspheres survive gastric acid","Enteric-coated microspheres dissolve in the stomach","Taken on an empty stomach","Requires concomitant histamine administration","Enzymes are given as plain, uncoated powders"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase 30-50k units/meal. Enteric-coated microspheres survive gastric acid', 'PERT provides 30,000-50,000 units of lipase per meal in enteric-coated microspheres that resist gastric acid and release enzymes in the duodenum.'),
('de9f38ab-bd34-54b1-9b6b-c12849b3a149'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is caused by:', '["Gastrin deficiency","Atypical ulcers without acid hypersecretion","Reduced acid secretion with recurrent ulcers","Gastrinoma (duodenum/pancreas): hypersecretion of acid","Hypersecretion of pepsinogen alone"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrinoma (duodenum/pancreas): hypersecretion of acid', 'A gastrinoma of the duodenum or pancreas secretes excess gastrin, producing severe acid hypersecretion with refractory, atypical peptic ulcers and diarrhea.'),
('8b22db63-3fe7-5f34-8ea9-ddc314b222a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Celiac disease is best described as:', '["IgE-mediated food allergy resulting in decreased functional activity","An infectious enteritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Autoimmune enteropathy triggered by gluten (gliadin) in HLA-DQ2/DQ8. Villous atrophy","Villous hypertrophy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Caused by lactose intolerance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune enteropathy triggered by gluten (gliadin) in HLA-DQ2/DQ8. Villous atrophy', 'Celiac disease is an autoimmune enteropathy triggered by gluten (gliadin) in HLA-DQ2/DQ8 carriers, causing villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytosis.'),
('9752226e-403f-5d6e-9b97-f6724fd4ea58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Non-celiac gluten sensitivity is characterized by:', '["Positive celiac serology and villous atrophy","Villous atrophy on biopsy","Symptoms occur only after FODMAP ingestion","Autoimmune enteropathy in HLA-DQ2/DQ8","Gluten-related symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Gluten-related symptoms', 'Non-celiac gluten sensitivity produces gluten-related symptoms with negative celiac serology and a normal biopsy; FODMAPs may contribute to symptoms.'),
('3a1bdb32-62cd-5fa6-a050-17e39393910a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Abetalipoproteinemia is caused by:', '["Increased chylomicron synthesis","Defective SGLT1 transporter","Absent ApoB (MTP mutation). Reduced chylomicron synthesis","ApoE mutation","Increased apolipoprotein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Absent ApoB (MTP mutation). Reduced chylomicron synthesis', 'A microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) mutation causes absent ApoB, preventing chylomicron assembly and producing fat malabsorption, acanthocytosis and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency.'),
('0500ee46-7669-505b-a01a-5ac84400baa7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Chylomicron retention disease is caused by:', '["SAR1B mutation: chylomicrons retained in ER","Absence of ApoB","Defective microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP)","Defective lacteal development","Defective bile acid synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'SAR1B mutation: chylomicrons retained in ER', 'A SAR1B mutation prevents chylomicron export from the enterocyte endoplasmic reticulum, causing fat malabsorption that differs from abetalipoproteinemia.'),
('c04bb6f3-110d-5006-81d8-5d113aa764ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Lacteals are best described as:', '["Blood capillaries in the villi resulting in decreased functional activity","Specialized venules of the portal system accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blind-ended lymphatic capillaries in villi. Chylomicrons enter via paracellular spaces","Lymphatic vessels draining only the colon without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sites of bile acid synthesis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Blind-ended lymphatic capillaries in villi. Chylomicrons enter via paracellular spaces', 'Lacteals are blind-ended lymphatic capillaries in the villi into which chylomicrons pass, eventually draining long-chain fats into the thoracic duct.'),
('99dfb884-a542-5edb-9eea-90e11f064908'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Enteric glial cells:', '["Are neurons that generate pacemaker activity","Support cells of ENS (like CNS astrocytes). Maintain barrier","Phagocytic cells of the gut lumen","Produce the migrating motor complex","Generate slow waves in smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Support cells of ENS (like CNS astrocytes). Maintain barrier', 'Enteric glial cells are the support cells of the enteric nervous system, analogous to CNS astrocytes, and help maintain the mucosal barrier.'),
('0951039d-724a-5c3b-87ce-35853be8db0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC):', '["Effector cells that secrete HCl resulting in decreased functional activity","Principal motor neurons of the ENS accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Generate slow waves only in skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Are identical to tissue macrophages without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pacemaker cells: generate slow waves. Myenteric border ICC-MY. c-Kit+. Gap junctions to smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Pacemaker cells: generate slow waves. Myenteric border ICC-MY. c-Kit+. Gap junctions to smooth muscle', 'Interstitial cells of Cajal are c-Kit-positive pacemaker cells at the myenteric border that generate slow waves and transmit them to smooth muscle via gap junctions.'),
('a6dcac5d-d765-5da1-a41f-76288978d4ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The peristaltic reflex is characterized by:', '["Contraction of the entire gut segment resulting in decreased functional activity","Relaxation of the entire gut segment accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stretch to proximal contraction + distal relaxation. Ascending contraction + descending relaxation. Enteric reflex","Contraction only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A reflex requiring only extrinsic nerves leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch to proximal contraction + distal relaxation. Ascending contraction + descending relaxation. Enteric reflex', 'The peristaltic reflex is an intrinsic enteric reflex in which stretch causes contraction above (ascending) and relaxation below (descending) the stimulus.'),
('9a67e4d3-a22b-5557-a8c7-0b89c7242b58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The law of the intestine states that local stimulation produces:', '["Contraction of the entire segment resulting in decreased functional activity","Contraction only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Relaxation only mediated by specific receptor activation","Relaxation below the stimulus only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Contraction above and relaxation below the stimulus; the basis of peristalsis via the intrinsic myenteric plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Contraction above and relaxation below the stimulus; the basis of peristalsis via the intrinsic myenteric plexus', 'The law of the intestine states that stimulation produces contraction above and relaxation below the stimulus, the basis of peristalsis mediated by the intrinsic myenteric plexus.'),
('856548d3-3453-54f6-a4b6-2550687d13c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The gastroenteric reflex refers to:', '["Ileal distension increasing gastric motility","Gastric distension decreasing ileal motility","Increased gastric motility with ileal distension","Gastric distension increases ileal motility (prepares distal gut for meal)","A reflex that is abolished by fasting"]'::jsonb, 'Gastric distension increases ileal motility (prepares distal gut for meal)', 'The gastroenteric reflex increases ileal motility in response to gastric distension, preparing the distal gut to receive the meal.'),
('b269c3a1-ee06-5a83-af5c-0a6139cc4fb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The intestino-intestinal reflex is characterized by:', '["Overdistension/irritation of one segment causes relaxation of rest of intestine. Protective","Overdistension of one segment causes contraction of the entire intestine","A reflex mediated only by the vagus","Overdistension of one segment stimulates propulsion in all segments","A reflex that is lost after vagotomy only"]'::jsonb, 'Overdistension/irritation of one segment causes relaxation of rest of intestine. Protective', 'The intestino-intestinal reflex is protective: overdistension or irritation of one bowel segment inhibits motility in the rest of the intestine.'),
('b19a5761-b0a8-503a-b009-bb22e54b4ef6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Postvagotomy diarrhea is attributed to:', '["Truncal vagotomy denervates proximal bowel: rapid gastric emptying","Delayed gastric emptying accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constipation mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal gastric emptying without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduced intestinal motility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Truncal vagotomy denervates proximal bowel: rapid gastric emptying', 'Truncal vagotomy denervates the proximal bowel, causing rapid gastric emptying and reduced bile acid absorption, which contribute to postvagotomy diarrhea.'),
('9c9d7d57-aac4-5584-9442-1320531ebdba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Postoperative ileus is best described as:', '["Generalized hypomotility after surgery. Sympathetic hyperactivity + inflammation. Stomach 1-2d","Localized hypomotility of the colon only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypermotility of the small intestine mediated by specific receptor activation","Transient hypercontractility of the stomach without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perforation-induced generalized contraction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Generalized hypomotility after surgery. Sympathetic hyperactivity + inflammation. Stomach 1-2d', 'Postoperative ileus is generalized GI hypomotility after surgery driven by sympathetic hyperactivity and inflammation; the stomach recovers in 1-2 days and the colon in 3-5 days.'),
('79705e6b-eb69-5b96-93d3-1bcf85a49359'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Acute hyperglycemia (blood glucose >200 mg/dL) affects GI motility by:', '["Acute BG >200 delays gastric emptying","Hastening gastric emptying","Increasing antral contractions","Increasing MMC frequency","Decreasing pyloric tone"]'::jsonb, 'Acute BG >200 delays gastric emptying', 'Acute hyperglycemia delays gastric emptying by reducing antral contractions, disrupting the MMC and increasing pyloric tone; hypoglycemia increases motility.'),
('f3078032-ad0f-5ea8-8d01-104feb912230'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric electrical stimulation for gastroparesis:', '["Uses low-frequency high-energy stimulation resulting in decreased functional activity","Implantable device high-frequency (14Hz) low-energy to antrum. Improves gastroparesis symptoms. Afferent mechanism (vagal)","Works via an efferent sympathetic mechanism mediated by specific receptor activation","Is ineffective for nausea and vomiting without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is administered by mouth leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Implantable device high-frequency (14Hz) low-energy to antrum. Improves gastroparesis symptoms. Afferent mechanism (vagal)', 'Gastric electrical stimulation uses an implanted device delivering high-frequency (14 Hz) low-energy stimulation to the antrum, improving gastroparesis symptoms through a vagal afferent mechanism.'),
('4ac02bcd-658f-56d1-9d6a-290649950fb3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The brain-gut axis is best described as:', '["Unidirectional signals from brain to gut only","Bidirectional CNS-ENS communication. Stress alters motility","A reflex mediated only through the spinal cord","Communication solely via circulating hormones","A purely local enteric phenomenon"]'::jsonb, 'Bidirectional CNS-ENS communication. Stress alters motility', 'The brain-gut axis is bidirectional communication between the CNS and the enteric nervous system; stress alters motility, permeability, secretion and sensation.'),
('dc921f36-a67c-507c-9539-8a104f8b75d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The effect of food consistency on gastric emptying is:', '["Liquids: rapid emptying. Solids: need trituration (<2mm). Semi-solids: intermediate","All foods empty at the same rate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Solids empty faster than liquids mediated by specific receptor activation","Liquids empty slower than solids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Semi-solids empty faster than liquids leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liquids: rapid emptying. Solids: need trituration (<2mm). Semi-solids: intermediate', 'Liquids empty rapidly, solids require trituration to particles below 2 mm before emptying, and semi-solids empty at an intermediate rate.'),
('70aae38f-2f40-5619-9beb-e58ce59563c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric sieving refers to:', '["The pylorus allows all particle sizes to pass","Emptying of particles independent of size","Retention of all liquids in the stomach","Pylorus allows liquids and small particles <2mm","The pylorus allows only solids to pass"]'::jsonb, 'Pylorus allows liquids and small particles <2mm', 'Gastric sieving means the pylorus allows liquids and particles smaller than 2 mm to pass while retaining larger particles for further grinding.'),
('bb8406a7-d76b-5de2-9df9-bf86acf8cf44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The cephalic phase of digestion is triggered by:', '["Release of gastrin from antral G cells","Sight/smell/taste/thought of food: vagal activation","Presence of chyme in the duodenum","Intraluminal distension of the stomach","Absence of vagal tone"]'::jsonb, 'Sight/smell/taste/thought of food: vagal activation', 'The cephalic phase is initiated by the sight, smell, taste or thought of food acting through vagal activation.'),
('79240f91-296f-5b45-a43d-4e8e01b3eb8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which of the following initiates the gastric phase of acid secretion?', '["Emptying of the stomach","Sight and smell of food","Fat in the duodenum","Distension of the rectum","Food in stomach: distension"]'::jsonb, 'Food in stomach: distension', 'The gastric phase is initiated by food in the stomach causing distension and chemical (protein/amino acid) stimuli, which release gastrin and drive 50-60% of acid output.'),
('bbf3d08f-354e-56bf-8dd6-825a5fde3379'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastric restitution refers to:', '["A slow process requiring cell division over weeks resulting in decreased functional activity","Scar formation with fibrosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Recruitment of neutrophils to the defect mediated by specific receptor activation","Rapid repair: cells migrate from pits to cover denuded area (minutes). Needs microvasculature","Formation of a permanent scar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid repair: cells migrate from pits to cover denuded area (minutes). Needs microvasculature', 'Gastric restitution is rapid repair in which surviving cells migrate from the pits to cover the denuded surface within minutes, requiring an intact microvasculature.'),
('cb129568-c1b4-5ae4-b16d-739ff295eae3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The first-line treatment of H. pylori-positive gastric MALT lymphoma is:', '["Radiotherapy as first choice resulting in decreased functional activity","Chemotherapy as first choice accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Surgery as first choice mediated by specific receptor activation","H pylori-driven gastric B cell lymphoma. Antibiotic eradication leads to regression 70-80%","No treatment required leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H pylori-driven gastric B cell lymphoma. Antibiotic eradication leads to regression 70-80%', 'Gastric MALT lymphoma is aH. pylori-driven B cell lymphoma; antibiotic eradication of H. pylori is first-line treatment and leads to regression in 70-80% of cases.'),
('899aeb52-f779-5cd1-8fb1-a06f71e9c0f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The principal excitatory neurotransmitter released by enteric motor neurons onto GI smooth muscle is:', '["ACh (excitatory)","NE (inhibitory)","Dopamine","VIP (inhibitory)","Nitric oxide"]'::jsonb, 'ACh (excitatory)', 'Acetylcholine is the principal excitatory neurotransmitter of enteric motor neurons, contracting GI smooth muscle via muscarinic receptors.'),
('cfd0a940-25ea-5c78-b5c4-807d64fd5158'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the primary metabolic pathway for tryptophan in the body?', '["The serotonin (5-HT) pathway is the primary route","Tryptophan is not metabolized in the body","Tryptophan is metabolized only in the liver","Tryptophan is converted mainly to indoles by the microbiome","95% via kynurenine (IDO)"]'::jsonb, '95% via kynurenine (IDO)', 'About 95% of tryptophan is metabolized through the kynurenine pathway by indoleamine-2,3-dioxygenase (IDO); only about 5% is converted to serotonin.'),
('fb8606a0-d28d-5788-af4a-91e073cc0b1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The human gut microbiota is dominated by which two phyla?', '["Bacteroidetes and Firmicutes","Proteobacteria only","Actinobacteria and Verrucomicrobia","Verrucomicrobia exclusively","Fungi and archaea only"]'::jsonb, 'Bacteroidetes and Firmicutes', 'The adult gut microbiota is dominated by the Firmicutes and Bacteroidetes phyla, which together account for most of the 10^14 bacteria and about 150 times more genes than the human genome.'),
('411f921f-708c-5402-aea0-e9128d40c87f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Akkermansia muciniphila:', '["Is the dominant Firmicutes species","Mucin-degrader in mucus layer. Associated with reduced obesity","Degrades brush border enzymes","Increases intestinal inflammation","Is associated with increased obesity"]'::jsonb, 'Mucin-degrader in mucus layer. Associated with reduced obesity', 'Akkermansia muciniphila degrades mucin in the mucus layer and is associated with reduced obesity and improved metabolic health.'),
('3d5dd5b0-aff7-56b8-a78f-814059ed6da6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Lactobacilli are classified as:', '["Strict aerobes","Gram-negative bacteria","Sulfate-reducing bacteria","Spore-forming pathogens","Lactic acid bacteria (LAB)"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acid bacteria (LAB)', 'Lactobacilli are lactic acid bacteria that ferment sugars to lactate, produce bacteriocins, and include probiotic strains such as L. acidophilus and L. rhamnosus.'),
('a9c7bf65-833a-5098-948b-28d11b05d299'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'A high-fiber diet promotes which gut microbe?', '["A high-fiber diet reduces microbial diversity","A high-fat diet promotes Bifidobacterium","A high-fiber diet promotes Proteobacteria","A high-fiber diet decreases SCFA production","High fiber: Bifido"]'::jsonb, 'High fiber: Bifido', 'A high-fiber diet promotes beneficial bacteria such as Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus, increasing the production of short-chain fatty acids.'),
('c358c589-80ab-5f44-bf1d-44d38970fc4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Farnesoid X receptor (FXR), the nuclear bile acid receptor:', '["Activates CYP7A1 to increase bile acid synthesis","Nuclear BA receptor. Reduces CYP7A1 via SHP","Is located only in the colon","Increases bile acid synthesis","Is a membrane-bound receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear BA receptor. Reduces CYP7A1 via SHP', 'FXR is a nuclear bile acid receptor that reduces CYP7A1 via SHP, inhibiting bile acid synthesis; ileal FXR activation induces FGF19, which also reduces hepatic CYP7A1.'),
('89661e30-037d-5174-afc8-f39fa7751484'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the primary mechanism of action of bile acid sequestrants such as cholestyramine?', '["Direct inhibition of cholesterol synthesis","Binding bile acids in the intestine to interrupt enterohepatic circulation","Increasing HDL cholesterol levels","Reducing intestinal cholesterol absorption via NPC1L1 inhibition","Stimulating LDL receptor expression on hepatocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Binding bile acids in the intestine to interrupt enterohepatic circulation', 'Cholestyramine binds bile acids in the gut, interrupting enterohepatic circulation; the liver then uses cholesterol to synthesize new bile acids, upregulating LDL receptors and lowering LDL.'),
('290d5619-02f8-5c57-8ebb-f6d2414defe2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Pancreatic polypeptide (PP):', '["Secreted by alpha cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Stimulates pancreatic exocrine secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inhibits pancreatic exocrine secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","PP cells (gamma) in islets uncinate. Released after meals (vagal). Inhibits pancreatic exocrine","Increases after prolonged fasting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PP cells (gamma) in islets uncinate. Released after meals (vagal). Inhibits pancreatic exocrine', 'Pancreatic polypeptide is secreted by PP (gamma) cells in the islets'' uncinate process, released after meals via vagal stimulation, and inhibits pancreatic exocrine secretion.'),
('c138c468-7263-5165-9e92-6114ef622697'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Amylin (IAPP):', '["Secreted by alpha cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Co-secreted with insulin from beta cells (same granules). Slows gastric emptying","Accelerates gastric emptying mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases food intake without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Raises blood glucose leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Co-secreted with insulin from beta cells (same granules). Slows gastric emptying', 'Amylin is co-secreted with insulin from pancreatic beta cells, slows gastric emptying, suppresses glucagon and reduces food intake.'),
('3c4a9e5f-3c6c-5c95-85ee-97e681964781'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'A source of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the GI tract is:', '["Antioxidant enzymes are the main source","Glutathione peroxidase generates ROS","Superoxide dismutase produces ROS","ROS from digestion","ROS are produced only in the liver"]'::jsonb, 'ROS from digestion', 'Reactive oxygen species are generated as by-products of oxidative metabolism during digestion; antioxidant enzymes such as SOD, catalase and GPx defend against them.'),
('22198af7-5d92-5b91-a40d-0143d9afafbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which gasotransmitter is the principal mediator of NANC inhibitory relaxation of GI smooth muscle?', '["CO (heme oxygenase)","H2S (cystathionine-gamma-lyase)","NO","Oxygen","Nitrous oxide"]'::jsonb, 'NO', 'Nitric oxide (NO) is the principal non-adrenergic, non-cholinergic (NANC) inhibitory neurotransmitter relaxing GI smooth muscle; CO and H2S are additional gasotransmitters.'),
('757f7641-72c8-55f1-b116-631a008baaca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'In irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), which neuropeptide is increased in the remodeled gut innervation?', '["Neural remodeling is absent in IBS","Substance P is decreased in IBS","Nerve remodeling: increased SP","VIP is increased in IBS","Acetylcholine is reduced in IBS remodeling"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve remodeling: increased SP', 'IBS is associated with nerve remodeling in which substance P is increased, contributing to visceral hypersensitivity and pain.'),
('91ae4ad5-69f8-5478-928c-8bb6ecd3ca64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Mast cells contribute to IBS pain by:', '["Mast cells near enteric nerves. Stress CRF to mast cell degranulation: histamine","Stress suppresses mast cell degranulation","Mast cells are located far from enteric nerves","Mast cells destroy enteric nerves","Mast cell products do not affect nociception"]'::jsonb, 'Mast cells near enteric nerves. Stress CRF to mast cell degranulation: histamine', 'Mast cells lie close to enteric nerves; stress triggers CRF-mediated mast cell degranulation, releasing histamine, tryptase, prostaglandins and 5-HT that activate nerves and cause pain.'),
('07b36f50-b089-5b09-b355-03863b1ff9b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Diamine oxidase (DAO):', '["Enzyme that synthesizes histamine resulting in decreased functional activity","Located on the basolateral membrane of crypt cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enzyme degrades histamine in gut. Enterocyte villus tip. Reduced DAO: histamine accumulation","Inhibited by histamine ingestion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increased DAO causes food intolerance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme degrades histamine in gut. Enterocyte villus tip. Reduced DAO: histamine accumulation', 'Diamine oxidase at the enterocyte villus tip degrades dietary histamine; reduced DAO leads to histamine accumulation and symptoms of food intolerance.'),
('a4ea4932-d5ed-583a-96ee-5c0aae2ddc47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which cells serve as the intestinal stem cells at the crypt base, and which signal maintains them?', '["Enterocytes serve as the intestinal stem cells","LGR5+ CBC stem cells at crypt base. Paneth cells: Wnt","Paneth cells are the intestinal stem cells","BMP from the crypt base maintains stem cells","Goblet cells are the intestinal stem cells"]'::jsonb, 'LGR5+ CBC stem cells at crypt base. Paneth cells: Wnt', 'LGR5+ crypt base columnar (CBC) cells are the intestinal stem cells, maintained by Wnt signals supplied by adjacent Paneth cells.'),
('333cb2b4-eeba-529d-8cc0-93ed68a704bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'In the intestinal crypt, Wnt signaling:', '["Degradation of beta-catenin","APC mutation inactivates Wnt signaling","Wnt from Paneth/stromal to Frizzled/LRP","APC loss inhibits proliferation","Wnt signaling inhibits proliferation"]'::jsonb, 'Wnt from Paneth/stromal to Frizzled/LRP', 'Wnt ligands from Paneth and stromal cells bind Frizzled/LRP receptors, stabilizing beta-catenin and driving TCF/LEF-mediated proliferation; APC mutation causes constitutive Wnt signaling.'),
('688f686a-95f7-5e51-829b-51148b025bbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Notch signaling from Paneth cells:', '["Notch inhibition decreases goblet cells","Notch promotes secretory differentiation","Notch drives differentiation into enterocytes only","Paneth cells express Notch receptors and receive signals","DLL1/4 from Paneth to stem cell Notch. Promotes stem/progenitor"]'::jsonb, 'DLL1/4 from Paneth to stem cell Notch. Promotes stem/progenitor', 'DLL1/4 ligands from Paneth cells activate Notch on stem cells, promoting the stem/progenitor state and inhibiting secretory differentiation; Notch inhibition increases goblet cells.'),
('1d37b86c-f9f8-5bea-abed-fb5f44b20810'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'In the intestinal crypt-villus axis, BMP signaling:', '["Promotes Wnt signaling","BMP is highest at the crypt base","BMP from mesenchyme","BMP promotes stem cell self-renewal","BMP is secreted by Paneth cells at the crypt base"]'::jsonb, 'BMP from mesenchyme', 'BMP from the mesenchyme and villus epithelium inhibits Wnt and promotes differentiation; BMP is low at the crypt base, preserving stem cells. BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis.'),
('48c8ace3-ca4c-5a19-a7df-d9390c98b36f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT):', '["Epithelial cells gain apical-basal polarity","EMT is induced by loss of TGF-beta","EMT involves gain of E-cadherin","EMT decreases cell motility","Epithelial lose polarity"]'::jsonb, 'Epithelial lose polarity', 'In EMT, epithelial cells lose polarity and E-cadherin and gain mesenchymal features (vimentin); TGF-beta induces EMT, which contributes to wound healing, fibrosis and metastasis.'),
('80e731e4-f0c1-5b6d-8831-edebf357c878'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Lubiprostone treats chronic idiopathic constipation by:', '["Inhibiting ClC-2 chloride channels","Blocking CFTR","ClC-2 Cl- channel activator: Cl- rich fluid secretion","Reducing intestinal fluid secretion","Stimulating water absorption"]'::jsonb, 'ClC-2 Cl- channel activator: Cl- rich fluid secretion', 'Lubiprostone activates ClC-2 chloride channels, increasing Cl- and fluid secretion into the intestinal lumen and promoting transit.'),
('df533071-4ce1-5725-a6df-6383413b7f7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Linaclotide acts by:', '["Blocking guanylate cyclase-C","Decreasing cGMP","Acting on ClC-2 channels","GC-C agonist: increases cGMP","Increasing visceral pain"]'::jsonb, 'GC-C agonist: increases cGMP', 'Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase-C (GC-C) agonist that increases cGMP, promoting fluid secretion and reducing visceral pain in IBS-C.'),
('43789ce0-8a58-54c2-ac4d-3d791028dbe2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The migrating motor complex is best described as:', '["Segmentation resulting in decreased functional activity","Cyclic interdigestive motility pattern that sweeps gut clean","Peristalsis only mediated by specific receptor activation","Tonic contraction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Continuous digestive motility leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic interdigestive motility pattern that sweeps gut clean', 'The MMC is a cyclic interdigestive (fasting) motility pattern that sweeps the gut clean between meals.'),
('928ad1a1-a472-5582-82c7-b3cb8600e7b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the function of the ileocecal sphincter?', '["Site of digestive enzyme secretion","Site of vitamin B12 absorption","Site of bile storage","Site of water absorption","Sphincter preventing backflow from colon to ileum"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter preventing backflow from colon to ileum', 'The ileocecal sphincter prevents backflow of colonic contents into the ileum and regulates the passage of chyme into the cecum.'),
('4a5aaa4a-d52c-5ca6-b0cb-c44f5ba5b6d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which of the following is NOT primarily synthesized by the liver?', '["Clotting factors","Urea","Albumin","Immunoglobulins","Bile acids"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoglobulins', 'The liver synthesizes clotting factors, urea, albumin and bile acids, but immunoglobulins are produced by plasma cells, not the liver.'),
('26afacd3-00e5-5526-8868-0ffcf61120af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastrin is produced by:', '["ECL cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Parietal cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","D cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Chief cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","G cells in the gastric antrum"]'::jsonb, 'G cells in the gastric antrum', 'Gastrin is produced by G cells in the gastric antrum and stimulates acid secretion from parietal cells.'),
('fca9dd7f-c4cb-59ef-8069-8cf9ded344e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Cholecystokinin (CCK) is produced by:', '["G cells in the antrum","S cells in the duodenum","Parietal cells","I cells in the duodenum","ECL cells"]'::jsonb, 'I cells in the duodenum', 'CCK is produced by I cells in the duodenum and jejunum in response to fat and protein.'),
('2a78c199-aa6e-54ac-8796-1c0e7fb4d6ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the enteric nervous system commonly called?', '["The second brain (gut brain)","Autonomic nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Central nervous system","Peripheral nervous system"]'::jsonb, 'The second brain (gut brain)', 'The enteric nervous system is commonly called the second brain or gut brain.'),
('9eacfaa1-6609-564a-8f3a-7b6a5ef0b30d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which name is commonly given to the enteric nervous system?', '["The second brain (gut brain)","Autonomic nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Central nervous system","Peripheral nervous system"]'::jsonb, 'The second brain (gut brain)', 'The enteric nervous system is commonly given the nickname the second brain (gut brain).'),
('461e4e8b-a105-540d-abe0-2d7213b71303'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The enteric nervous system is also known as:', '["The second brain (gut brain)","Autonomic nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Central nervous system","Peripheral nervous system"]'::jsonb, 'The second brain (gut brain)', 'The enteric nervous system is also known as the second brain (gut brain) due to its extensive intrinsic neuronal network.'),
('e4940695-34e7-50ac-bcce-d16dbf712d92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'A common nickname for the enteric nervous system is:', '["The second brain (gut brain)","Autonomic nervous system","Somatic nervous system","Central nervous system","Peripheral nervous system"]'::jsonb, 'The second brain (gut brain)', 'The enteric nervous system is nicknamed the second brain (gut brain).'),
('3e0a287a-23f4-538d-afff-2dcd884744f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Parasympathetic innervation to the distal colon and rectum is via:', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'The vagus supplies the gut to the proximal colon; the distal colon and rectum receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4).'),
('73e02702-01e1-5f96-ace4-bee748a0360d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which nerves provide parasympathetic supply to the distal colon and rectum?', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves arising from S2-S4 carry parasympathetic innervation to the distal colon and rectum.'),
('c9bfcec7-f3c6-5453-a9e0-78d18fbfbcc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The parasympathetic innervation of the left colon and rectum arises from:', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'Parasympathetic fibers to the left colon and rectum arise from the sacral parasympathetic outflow via the pelvic splanchnic nerves.'),
('c2a442fc-d85f-5a1e-bd23-449ec2c262e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Pelvic organs and the distal colon receive parasympathetic fibers from:', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'Pelvic organs and the distal colon receive parasympathetic innervation from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4).'),
('622546ef-9e69-51af-87cc-9adcf7f55e65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which nerve group carries parasympathetic fibers to the distal colon and rectum?', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'Parasympathetic fibers to the distal colon and rectum travel in the pelvic splanchnic nerves from the sacral cord.'),
('c67aaeba-93b1-5949-84b4-c0a65b34b8f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which nerves provide sympathetic innervation to the abdominal organs?', '["Cranial nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)","Pelvic mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)', 'The abdominal organs receive sympathetic innervation through the thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2).'),
('e2b5f4a7-0c8d-536b-98ad-3ba511b8d3af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Sympathetic innervation of the gut arises from:', '["Cranial nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)","Pelvic mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)', 'Sympathetic fibers to the gut originate from the thoracolumbar outflow (T5-L2) and travel in the splanchnic nerves.'),
('ec764dae-3e05-534e-b0d0-7b543402a4ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The sympathetic fibers to the gastrointestinal tract travel in:', '["Cranial nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)","Pelvic mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)', 'Sympathetic fibers to the GI tract travel in the thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves.'),
('235a06b9-8d49-5548-8ca9-5029776c04e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Sympathetic supply to the abdominal viscera is conveyed by:', '["Cranial nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)","Pelvic mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)', 'The abdominal viscera receive sympathetic supply via the thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2).'),
('ccc75ace-bc0b-5a23-8d02-393632907798'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which neurotransmitter is the principal excitatory transmitter in the enteric nervous system?', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is the principal excitatory neurotransmitter in the enteric nervous system.'),
('479fb0a1-4bea-5de5-91ee-c4926b1bfa14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The primary parasympathetic postganglionic neurotransmitter is:', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is released at parasympathetic postganglionic endings and is the major excitatory transmitter acting on enteric neurons and GI smooth muscle.'),
('1d29590f-2102-556f-94da-ae12de4aadb3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which neurotransmitter is released at parasympathetic postganglionic endings?', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Parasympathetic postganglionic fibers release acetylcholine, the major excitatory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system.'),
('c806852d-e91a-504a-8213-2d798cf03805'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The chief excitatory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system is:', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is the chief excitatory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system.'),
('12a02b5e-016b-5b5f-a557-a53704a16f60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which of the following is the principal excitatory neurotransmitter of the gut?', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is the principal excitatory neurotransmitter of the gut, promoting motility and secretion.'),
('8396f596-24cd-5d3f-a8c3-57070702f498'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The major inhibitory neurotransmitter acting on the gut is:', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Norepinephrine released from postganglionic sympathetic fibers is the major inhibitory influence on the gut, reducing motility and secretion.'),
('55482dbf-230d-53d8-a64e-c48cc086e4f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which neurotransmitter is the principal inhibitory transmitter acting on the gut?', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Norepinephrine is the principal inhibitory transmitter released by sympathetic postganglionic fibers onto the gut.'),
('7360aa24-b591-502e-bb9b-31efced6ab9c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The primary sympathetic postganglionic neurotransmitter is:', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine, which inhibits GI motility and secretions.'),
('99f5544e-f58c-57b0-a20b-dcaae326519d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which neurotransmitter is released at sympathetic postganglionic endings to inhibit the gut?', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Sympathetic postganglionic endings release norepinephrine, which inhibits GI motility.'),
('7417917b-8a03-54ac-b65c-772b68735db6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system is:', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'The major inhibitory neurotransmitter influencing the enteric nervous system is norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers.'),
('d084ac37-244d-5057-b1d0-84f6d055baf8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which of the following is the principal inhibitory neurotransmitter of the gut?', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Norepinephrine from sympathetic postganglionic fibers is the principal inhibitory neurotransmitter of the gut.'),
('307d66b2-6a39-591e-837f-beea4fec2c1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which inhibitory neurotransmitters mediate smooth muscle relaxation in the GI tract?', '["Serotonin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP', 'NO and VIP mediate inhibitory (NANC) relaxation of GI smooth muscle.'),
('9213b593-1dae-52ee-9128-6cb949a2240b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Non-adrenergic, non-cholinergic (NANC) inhibitory relaxation of GI smooth muscle is mediated by:', '["Serotonin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP', 'NANC inhibitory relaxation of GI smooth muscle is mediated chiefly by nitric oxide and VIP.'),
('3e6cded5-9835-5ae3-b52f-0d97bd266fa8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Smooth muscle relaxation in the GI tract is brought about by:', '["Serotonin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP', 'Smooth muscle relaxation in the GI tract is produced by the inhibitory transmitters NO and VIP.'),
('de9a3ed5-4e7a-5f05-8a3e-56ae11f92a14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which of the following are the principal inhibitory neurotransmitters of the gut?', '["Serotonin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP', 'The principal inhibitory neurotransmitters of the gut are nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP).'),
('0aa4187b-0363-5173-9bf6-e87340b7c02e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The migrating motor complex (MMC) is best described as:', '["Defecation resulting in decreased functional activity","Satiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)","Postprandial pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vomiting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)', 'The MMC is best described as a cyclic fasting pattern of contraction with a housekeeper function.'),
('3c6d9e26-133c-5bcb-b171-11744ec1181c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which statement best describes the migrating motor complex (MMC)?', '["Defecation resulting in decreased functional activity","Satiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)","Postprandial pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vomiting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)', 'The MMC is a cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting that acts as the housekeeper of the gut.'),
('8783c548-5603-52ec-88b6-ae50f8001e8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt1: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('9f8bed9e-8197-59d7-9ae5-e8bb1b4e958d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'During fasting, the gut shows a cyclical pattern of contraction known as:', '["Defecation resulting in decreased functional activity","Satiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)","Postprandial pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vomiting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)', 'The cyclical fasting pattern of contraction is the migrating motor complex, the gut''s housekeeper.'),
('540da1a0-6bf0-532b-aa07-3d641e29af31'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which of the following interrupts the migrating motor complex (MMC)?', '["Medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Fasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)', 'Feeding interrupts the MMC by producing a continuous postprandial contraction pattern.'),
('2091326c-27c1-5b4e-9ca9-a1be2268f9d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The postprandial pattern of continuous contraction replaces the MMC after:', '["Medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Fasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)', 'After feeding, the fasting MMC is replaced by a continuous postprandial pattern of contraction.'),
('2b2f95e9-469f-5f09-aee5-04172e9ff97a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'MMC is disrupted by:', '["Medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Fasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)', 'MMC is disrupted by feeding, which induces the continuous postprandial pattern.'),
('b0ac5000-a894-5a81-91ba-097f566d7e7d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The fasting migrating motor complex (MMC) is abolished by:', '["Medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Fasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)', 'The fasting MMC is abolished by feeding, which produces a continuous postprandial pattern.'),
('e5cccf26-271f-5f0a-ae37-c7bb5594667c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is due to:', '["High LES pressure","Increased frequency of normal swallow-related relaxations","Normal LES pressure","Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone","Absence of gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone', 'GERD results from transient LES relaxations with or without low basal LES tone, allowing reflux of gastric contents.'),
('6c38390d-2825-5a2b-9ba0-aafaec1f0a84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Which mechanism underlies gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)?', '["High LES pressure","Increased frequency of normal swallow-related relaxations","Normal LES pressure","Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone","Absence of gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone', 'GERD is caused by transient LES relaxations (TLESRs), often with low basal LES tone, permitting reflux.'),
('ec898c97-1b53-59e6-909c-22c432f87e73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'The pathogenesis of GERD is best explained by:', '["High LES pressure","Increased frequency of normal swallow-related relaxations","Normal LES pressure","Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone","Absence of gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone', 'The pathogenesis of GERD involves transient LES relaxations and reduced basal LES tone.'),
('4579ee47-3551-5e9c-baca-e6b084aad7dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Reflux of gastric contents into the esophagus occurs because of:', '["High LES pressure","Increased frequency of normal swallow-related relaxations","Normal LES pressure","Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone","Absence of gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone', 'Reflux occurs during transient LES relaxations (TLESRs), often combined with low resting LES tone.'),
('d30c14b9-83eb-5655-bcf4-fabb018ce962'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Peristalsis in the gastric body is best described as:', '["Segmental resulting in decreased functional activity","Sweeping accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propulsive mediated by specific receptor activation","Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)","Strong leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)', 'The gastric body produces weak peristaltic contractions mainly for tone regulation and receptive relaxation; strong waves occur in the antrum.'),
('2a49969f-cce0-5f6b-af64-d90c9bdb9797'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Peristalsis in the stomach body is:', '["Segmental resulting in decreased functional activity","Sweeping accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propulsive mediated by specific receptor activation","Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)","Strong leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)', 'Peristalsis in the stomach body is weak and functions in tone regulation and receptive relaxation.'),
('df43d4af-36e2-5fb4-b41d-1732bc161045'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'What is the tone of the fundic/body stomach during receptive relaxation?', '["Segmental resulting in decreased functional activity","Sweeping accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propulsive mediated by specific receptor activation","Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)","Strong leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)', 'During receptive relaxation the fundus and body show weak tone, allowing the stomach to accommodate food at low pressure.'),
('b802a71f-9b8f-5a3d-9107-434f797d1092'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 1', 'Contractions of the gastric body during a meal are:', '["Segmental resulting in decreased functional activity","Sweeping accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propulsive mediated by specific receptor activation","Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)","Strong leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)', 'During a meal the gastric body contracts weakly, regulating tone and accommodating the meal.'),
('2e2f82ce-0b16-5c95-99bb-57171b8caeea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'A 30-year-old clerk complains of food getting stuck in the chest, regurgitation, and weight loss. Manometry reveals aperistalsis and high resting LES pressure. (GIT Case #1)', '["Diffuse esophageal spasm","Achalasia","GERD","Esophageal web","Zenker diverticulum"]'::jsonb, 'Achalasia', 'Achalasia is caused by the loss of inhibitory neurons in the LES, leading to high resting tone and failure to relax upon swallowing.'),
