('97c73ee2-4ea6-51de-9055-f2fc82df8d22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'A 30-year-old male presents with progressive dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Barium swallow reveals a dilated esophagus with distal narrowing (''bird''s beak''). (GIT Case #64)', '["Achalasia","Diffuse esophageal spasm","GERD","Zenker diverticulum","Esophageal web"]'::jsonb, 'Achalasia', 'Achalasia is caused by the loss of inhibitory neurons in the LES, leading to high resting tone and failure to relax upon swallowing.'),
('831b01d7-9d9d-5db5-89a4-1aa988d618aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'A 30-year-old male presents with progressive dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Barium swallow reveals a dilated esophagus with distal narrowing (''bird''s beak''). (GIT Case #65)', '["Zenker diverticulum","Esophageal web","GERD","Diffuse esophageal spasm","Achalasia"]'::jsonb, 'Achalasia', 'Achalasia is caused by the loss of inhibitory neurons in the LES, leading to high resting tone and failure to relax upon swallowing.'),
('6e0ca12b-9b7c-5ac5-b083-39fa79e273e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the first-line treatment for primary biliary cholangitis?', '["Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) improving liver biochemistry and transplant-free survival","Primary sclerosing cholangitis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alcoholic hepatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","NAFLD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gilbert syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) improving liver biochemistry and transplant-free survival', 'UDCA is the first-line therapy for PBC, improving cholestatic liver biochemistry and transplant-free survival.'),
('a49aab9c-2cf2-5932-910c-ccca4b58cc44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, In irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), which neuropeptide is increased in the remodeled gut innervation?', '["Neural remodeling is absent in IBS","Substance P is decreased in IBS","Nerve remodeling: increased SP","VIP is increased in IBS","Acetylcholine is reduced in IBS remodeling"]'::jsonb, 'Nerve remodeling: increased SP', 'IBS is associated with nerve remodeling in which substance P is increased, contributing to visceral hypersensitivity and pain.'),
('101196b8-6776-511d-8983-c5235e23cab5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the role of FGF21 (fibroblast growth factor 21)?', '["Neurotransmitter in the gut resulting in decreased functional activity","Hepatokine that regulates energy metabolism (increasing insulin sensitivity and ketogenesis)","Incretin mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatic hormone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adipocyte hormone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatokine that regulates energy metabolism (increasing insulin sensitivity and ketogenesis)', 'FGF21 is a hepatokine that improves insulin sensitivity and promotes ketogenesis, regulating systemic energy metabolism.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('e700d846-3962-599d-bc6b-95595a6a669f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement is correct regarding octreotide, a somatostatin analogue?', '["Used to treat neuroendocrine tumors","Causes splanchnic vasoconstriction reducing portal pressure in variceal bleeding","Used to treat acromegaly","All of the above","Increases portal pressure"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Octreotide reduces splanchnic blood flow and portal pressure in variceal bleeding and is also used to treat acromegaly and neuroendocrine tumors.'),
('9c462ed5-b726-5286-815b-74e843fb5c90'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Gastric varices are most commonly located in which part of the stomach?', '["Body","Antral","Esophageal","Pyloric","Fundus"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus', 'Unlike esophageal varices, gastric varices most commonly develop in the gastric fundus.'),
('200d7e7e-3286-551f-b0f4-c4dd3afac84e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the function of a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS)?', '["Tumor treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver biopsy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Creates an artificial channel between portal and hepatic veins to decrease portal pressure (for varices/ascites)","Biliary drainage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hepatic resection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Creates an artificial channel between portal and hepatic veins to decrease portal pressure (for varices/ascites)', 'TIPS creates a shunt between the portal and hepatic veins, lowering portal pressure and treating refractory variceal bleeding and ascites.'),
('888c2e61-337e-53c0-8a57-c21b5778ee0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)?', '["Viral infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Tubercular infection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fungal infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Chemical irritation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bacterial infection of ascitic fluid in cirrhosis with no apparent source (gut translocation)"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial infection of ascitic fluid in cirrhosis with no apparent source (gut translocation)', 'SBP is bacterial infection of ascitic fluid in cirrhosis without an obvious intra-abdominal source, arising from translocation of gut bacteria.'),
('4fa83c1f-7223-5a23-91c2-0ecd33775be2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is hepatorenal syndrome (HRS)?', '["Functional renal failure in cirrhosis (splanchnic vasodilation causing renal vasoconstriction and decreased GFR)","Pre-renal azotemia from hypovolemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renal parenchymal disease mediated by specific receptor activation","Acute tubular necrosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Post-renal obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Functional renal failure in cirrhosis (splanchnic vasodilation causing renal vasoconstriction and decreased GFR)', 'HRS is a functional, potentially reversible renal failure in cirrhosis caused by intense renal vasoconstriction from splanchnic vasodilation, with no structural renal damage.'),
('515ce085-be9b-514e-bf7e-46bcd0829a2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, The parasympathetic innervation of the left colon and rectum arises from:', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'Parasympathetic fibers to the left colon and rectum arise from the sacral parasympathetic outflow via the pelvic splanchnic nerves.'),
('e21586e4-d891-52c6-a2cf-12ffbb15573a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Pulmonary hypertension occurring in the setting of portal hypertension is termed what?', '["Interstitial lung disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Portopulmonary hypertension (pulmonary hypertension in portal hypertension, similar to PAH)","Hepatopulmonary syndrome mediated by specific receptor activation","Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension without affecting the primary tissue pathway","COPD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Portopulmonary hypertension (pulmonary hypertension in portal hypertension, similar to PAH)', 'Portopulmonary hypertension is a form of pulmonary arterial hypertension associated with portal hypertension, classified as pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension.'),
('05bab150-7aa1-58b6-a989-08f0835af5ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Pancreatic secretory trypsin inhibitor (PSTI):', '["Protects pancreas from premature trypsin activation","Bile cofactor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates trypsin mediated by specific receptor activation","Digests trypsin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inactivates trypsin in the gut lumen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Protects pancreas from premature trypsin activation', 'PSTI, present in pancreatic juice, inhibits trypsin inside the pancreas and pancreatic duct, preventing premature activation of other proteases and autodigestion.'),
('5f3c51b4-997e-5502-b711-e8a1b9b0e04a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Steatorrhea in chronic pancreatitis results from:', '["Bile duct obstruction","Inadequate enzymes: maldigestion of fat (steatorrhea)","Inadequate brush border disaccharidases","Reduced intestinal motility","Excess bile salt secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Inadequate enzymes: maldigestion of fat (steatorrhea)', 'In chronic pancreatitis, inadequate pancreatic lipase causes maldigestion of fat and steatorrhea.'),
('d3c2dbf4-c239-56e9-9865-444adb5ab072'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which mediator is responsible for most of the growth-promoting effects of growth hormone?', '["GH itself resulting in decreased functional activity","IGF-binding protein 3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All of the above mediated by specific receptor activation","GH receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IGF-1 (somatomedin C) mediating most growth-promoting effects of GH"]'::jsonb, 'IGF-1 (somatomedin C) mediating most growth-promoting effects of GH', 'IGF-1 (somatomedin C), produced mainly by the liver in response to GH, mediates most of the growth-promoting effects of growth hormone.'),
('0fe22713-27a0-5095-bd5f-e4ab88d7c067'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'The liver is a major site of production of which hormone precursor?', '["ADH production resulting in decreased functional activity","Angiotensinogen production (precursor of Ang I)","Aldosterone production mediated by specific receptor activation","Renin production without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACE production leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Angiotensinogen production (precursor of Ang I)', 'The liver synthesizes angiotensinogen, which renin cleaves to angiotensin I as the first step of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system.'),
('2a63e85c-6355-5cef-a4f3-243eb1e76256'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: The cephalic phase of digestion is triggered by:', '["Release of gastrin from antral G cells","Sight/smell/taste/thought of food: vagal activation","Presence of chyme in the duodenum","Intraluminal distension of the stomach","Absence of vagal tone"]'::jsonb, 'Sight/smell/taste/thought of food: vagal activation', 'The cephalic phase is initiated by the sight, smell, taste or thought of food acting through vagal activation.'),
('cf072602-144f-5cf2-8d24-3e293a9dfa26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Which sequence correctly describes the defecation reflex?', '["Somatic only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stool in rectum → stretch receptors → parasympathetic (pelvic) → internal anal sphincter relaxes + rectal contraction → external sphincter voluntarily relaxed","Brain only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Voluntary only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stool in rectum → stretch receptors → parasympathetic (pelvic) → internal anal sphincter relaxes + rectal contraction → external sphincter voluntarily relaxed', 'Rectal filling activates stretch receptors, triggering pelvic parasympathetic impulses that contract the rectum and relax the internal sphincter while the external sphincter relaxes voluntarily.'),
('751026a2-4973-5fc7-ae5f-61fda1609411'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Which of the following are the principal inhibitory neurotransmitters of the gut?', '["Serotonin","Histamine","Acetylcholine","Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide (NO) and VIP', 'The principal inhibitory neurotransmitters of the gut are nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP).'),
('b9d284b8-1196-521b-bc61-dd0dcf2089e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Through which anatomical structure do bile and pancreatic juice enter the duodenum?', '["Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla), regulated by the sphincter of Oddi","Pyloric sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ileocecal valve mediated by specific receptor activation","Cystic duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gastroesophageal junction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of Vater (hepatopancreatic ampulla), regulated by the sphincter of Oddi', 'Bile and pancreatic juice enter the duodenum through the hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater), whose outflow is controlled by the sphincter of Oddi.'),
('152401ab-0678-5dcc-80b2-247942fec5b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is sphincter of Oddi dysfunction characterized by?', '["Recurrent pancreatitis resulting in decreased functional activity","Dilated CBD (diagnosed by manometry) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal spasm causing biliary-type pain","GERD without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IBS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal spasm causing biliary-type pain', 'Sphincter of Oddi dysfunction is an abnormal spasm or stenosis of the sphincter producing biliary-type pain and sometimes pancreatitis, diagnosed by manometry.'),
('bb9853a1-c7d6-544c-86f5-dd48a7045a27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the principal constituent of bile by volume?', '["Bile salts","Cholesterol","Water (97%)","Phospholipids","Bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Water (97%)', 'Bile is approximately 97% water, with bile salts, phospholipids, cholesterol, and bilirubin making up the remainder.'),
('7236d6c2-68bc-5daf-87da-70c4674c5d6f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement about bile acid synthesis is correct?', '["Primary bile acids (cholic, chenodeoxycholic) are synthesized from cholesterol via CYP7A1 and conjugated with glycine or taurine","Secondary bile acids are formed in the liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile acids are synthesized in the gallbladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Bile acids are excreted unchanged in the urine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bile acids are not reabsorbed in the ileum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary bile acids (cholic, chenodeoxycholic) are synthesized from cholesterol via CYP7A1 and conjugated with glycine or taurine', 'The liver synthesizes primary bile acids (cholic and chenodeoxycholic) from cholesterol via CYP7A1 and conjugates them with glycine or taurine; bacteria form secondary bile acids in the colon.'),
('10f9fd73-3143-5f60-af7e-d5e10095d6ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Vomiting is coordinated by:', '["Diencephalon resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stomach mediated by specific receptor activation","Vomiting center in medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius + central pattern generator)","Intestine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vomiting center in medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius + central pattern generator)', 'The vomiting center in the medulla, centered on the nucleus tractus solitarius with a central pattern generator, coordinates the emetic response.'),
('b7a79607-ce2a-5f8f-a89d-75a5e30d852e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes the transport of unconjugated bilirubin in the blood?', '["Albumin-bound in blood, lipophilic, not urine-excretable; hepatocyte uptake via OATP1B1/1B3","Freely water-soluble in plasma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excreted directly in urine mediated by specific receptor activation","Albumin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","OATP1B1/1B3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin-bound in blood, lipophilic, not urine-excretable; hepatocyte uptake via OATP1B1/1B3', 'Unconjugated bilirubin is lipophilic, travels bound to albumin, cannot be excreted in urine, and is taken up by hepatocytes via OATP1B1/1B3.'),
('36ec18f3-3783-550b-b13b-bc27d966fcb3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Receptive relaxation of the proximal stomach during swallowing is mediated by:', '["Sympathetic activation (norepinephrine)","Local myenteric reflex of the antrum","Histamine release from ECL cells","Inspiration","Swallowing (via vagal NANC inhibition - NO/VIP)"]'::jsonb, 'Swallowing (via vagal NANC inhibition - NO/VIP)', 'Swallowing triggers a vagovagal reflex that releases the NANC inhibitory transmitters NO and VIP, which relax the proximal stomach (receptive relaxation).'),
('3fd8223d-30d7-5ee7-a692-cd3aefbb63e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: Peristalsis in the gastric body is best described as:', '["Segmental resulting in decreased functional activity","Sweeping accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Propulsive mediated by specific receptor activation","Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)","Strong leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Weak (tone regulation - receptive relaxation)', 'The gastric body produces weak peristaltic contractions mainly for tone regulation and receptive relaxation; strong waves occur in the antrum.'),
('2c74b91d-6098-523d-ac80-a021786dfa11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which features characterize pre-hepatic (hemolytic) jaundice?', '["Increased bilirubin production (hemolysis) with unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, no bilirubinuria, and normal liver enzymes","Elevated conjugated bilirubin with bilirubinuria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Abnormal liver synthetic function mediated by specific receptor activation","Dark urine due to conjugated bilirubin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Impaired hepatocyte uptake of bilirubin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased bilirubin production (hemolysis) with unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, no bilirubinuria, and normal liver enzymes', 'Pre-hepatic jaundice results from increased bilirubin production in hemolysis, causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia without bilirubinuria and with normal liver enzymes.'),
('bdfa84f7-4bc5-5af7-af37-43243bb0f150'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Monosaccharide absorption in the small intestine:', '["All by GLUT5 resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only active accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All by SGLT1 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only passive without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose and galactose: SGLT1 (Na+ dependent secondary active transport). Fructose: GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion)"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose and galactose: SGLT1 (Na+ dependent secondary active transport). Fructose: GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion)', 'Glucose and galactose are absorbed by the Na+-dependent SGLT1 transporter, while fructose is absorbed by the facilitated-diffusion transporter GLUT5.'),
('60ceb165-0a45-533c-ae27-8b09e1f4aba5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes the Crigler-Najjar syndromes?', '["UGT1A1 mutation; type 1 is complete absence (severe unconjugated >20 mg/dL, kernicterus), type 2 is partial deficiency (phenobarbital-responsive)","Type 1 has partial enzyme activity accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type 2 causes kernicterus in all cases mediated by specific receptor activation","Both types present with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Both types are responsive to phenobarbital leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'UGT1A1 mutation; type 1 is complete absence (severe unconjugated >20 mg/dL, kernicterus), type 2 is partial deficiency (phenobarbital-responsive)', 'Crigler-Najjar type 1 is complete UGT1A1 deficiency with severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia and kernicterus, while type 2 is partial and phenobarbital-responsive.'),
('ad052b4f-04d1-5618-b700-f563f2628ae0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement about aspartate aminotransferase (AST) is correct?', '["Found in liver, muscle, heart, kidney, and brain; less specific for liver than ALT; AST:ALT ratio aids differential diagnosis","Present exclusively in hepatocytes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elevated only in biliary obstruction mediated by specific receptor activation","More specific for hepatocellular injury than ALT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Undetectable in healthy individuals leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Found in liver, muscle, heart, kidney, and brain; less specific for liver than ALT; AST:ALT ratio aids differential diagnosis', 'AST is present in many tissues, so it is less specific for the liver than ALT; the AST:ALT ratio helps distinguish alcoholic liver disease and other hepatopathies.'),
('58d01f84-7414-5266-a887-a348da9fec6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement about aminotransferase patterns in liver disease is correct?', '["ALT and AST are equally elevated in all liver diseases","In hepatocellular injury, both ALT and AST are markedly elevated","ALP and GGT are markedly elevated in hepatocellular injury","Isolated bilirubin elevation without enzyme changes is impossible","Cholestatic injury shows a predominantly ALT/AST elevation"]'::jsonb, 'In hepatocellular injury, both ALT and AST are markedly elevated', 'Hepatocellular injury produces marked elevation of both ALT and AST, whereas cholestatic injury predominantly raises ALP and GGT.'),
('2b8145f6-f870-505d-a511-418c1fe65e88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: The gallbladder stores and concentrates bile:', '["Concentrates 2× resulting in decreased functional activity","Stores only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Concentrates bile 5-10× by absorbing water (maximum storage 50-100 mL)","Concentrates 15-20× without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilutes bile leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Concentrates bile 5-10× by absorbing water (maximum storage 50-100 mL)', 'The gallbladder concentrates bile 5-10 fold by absorbing water and can store approximately 50-100 mL.'),
('0b307b95-cd63-53e3-a23d-82b196ffb8d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What are the synthesis rate, half-life, and clinical significance of serum albumin?', '["Synthesized exclusively in the liver (12-15 g/day), half-life 20 days; low in chronic liver disease (not acute) and nephrotic syndrome","A good marker of acute liver injury accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Half-life of approximately 2 days mediated by specific receptor activation","Synthesized mainly in the kidneys without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Elevated in cirrhosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesized exclusively in the liver (12-15 g/day), half-life 20 days; low in chronic liver disease (not acute) and nephrotic syndrome', 'Albumin is made only in the liver (12-15 g/day) with a half-life of about 20 days, so it is low in chronic liver disease and nephrotic syndrome, not in acute liver injury.'),
('7f2a47ab-2830-55c8-884d-0a7c7d7f5861'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) is best defined as:', '["Decreased bacterial counts in the small bowel","Bacterial overgrowth limited to the colon",">10^5 CFU/mL in small bowel. Malabsorption","A purely motor disorder without microbial involvement","Bacterial overgrowth of the stomach only"]'::jsonb, '>10^5 CFU/mL in small bowel. Malabsorption', 'SIBO is the presence of more than 10^5 CFU/mL of bacteria in the small bowel, causing malabsorption with diarrhea, bloating and B12 deficiency.'),
('b0f482cb-6084-599e-bc1a-4580cb258c70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Whipple disease is caused by:', '["Crohn disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Malabsorption only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tropheryma whipplei infection → malabsorption + arthralgias + neurologic symptoms","Fat only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Celiac-like leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tropheryma whipplei infection → malabsorption + arthralgias + neurologic symptoms', 'Whipple disease is a systemic infection by Tropheryma whipplei causing malabsorption, arthralgias, weight loss and neurologic symptoms.'),
('6fb1d302-3198-5d8a-8b22-ea5cdcfa57e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'How does the liver dispose of ammonia from amino acid catabolism and gut bacteria?', '["Urea cycle in periportal hepatocytes (CPS1) plus glutamine synthetase in perivenous hepatocytes; gut ammonia arises from bacterial urease","Glutaminase in the kidneys only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct excretion of ammonia in bile mediated by specific receptor activation","No role for the urea cycle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ammonia is stored as urea in muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urea cycle in periportal hepatocytes (CPS1) plus glutamine synthetase in perivenous hepatocytes; gut ammonia arises from bacterial urease', 'Periportal hepatocytes convert ammonia to urea via the urea cycle (CPS1), perivenous hepatocytes scavenge residual ammonia as glutamine, and gut bacteria produce ammonia through urease.'),
('7bb042da-41fc-51a2-9870-b3cd137e0742'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Fructose absorption uses:', '["SGLT1 resulting in decreased functional activity","GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion at brush border) and GLUT2 (basolateral)","GLUT4 mediated by specific receptor activation","Active transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sodium dependent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion at brush border) and GLUT2 (basolateral)', 'Fructose enters the enterocyte by facilitated diffusion through apical GLUT5 and exits basolaterally via GLUT2.'),
('5900b345-bc64-55a7-afcf-94cda11163f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Gastric emptying of liquids is best characterized by:', '["Rapid (T1/2 20 min). Fundal pressure gradient. Regulated by duodenal feedback","Slow and controlled by antral peristalsis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Slow (T1/2 2 h) mediated by specific receptor activation","Only semi-solids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dependent on particle size leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid (T1/2 20 min). Fundal pressure gradient. Regulated by duodenal feedback', 'Liquids empty rapidly (half-time about 20 min) driven by the fundal pressure gradient and are regulated by duodenal feedback.'),
('c745064a-3729-54eb-9765-fa3c5d2f8ec1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Pancreatic proteases (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase) are:', '["Activated in pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Only one active accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activated in duodenum (trypsinogen → trypsin via enterokinase)","Secrete active without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Activated in the stomach leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Activated in duodenum (trypsinogen → trypsin via enterokinase)', 'Pancreatic proteases are secreted as inactive zymogens and activated in the duodenum; enterokinase (enteropeptidase) converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates the others.'),
('f928d521-f63b-5363-a0c6-e19332b4a048'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Which of the following is NOT primarily synthesized by the liver?', '["Clotting factors","Urea","Albumin","Immunoglobulins","Bile acids"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoglobulins', 'The liver synthesizes clotting factors, urea, albumin and bile acids, but immunoglobulins are produced by plasma cells, not the liver.'),
('be009772-e721-583b-83be-77f76be07b41'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes the 2023 MASLD nomenclature?', '["The 2023 nomenclature replaces NAFLD; MASLD requires at least one cardiometabolic risk factor and falls under the steatotic liver disease (SLD) umbrella","MASLD is identical to NAFLD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MASLD requires no cardiometabolic risk factor mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","MetALD includes alcohol as the sole cause without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ALD is classified under MASLD leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The 2023 nomenclature replaces NAFLD; MASLD requires at least one cardiometabolic risk factor and falls under the steatotic liver disease (SLD) umbrella', 'MASLD replaced NAFLD in 2023, requiring at least one cardiometabolic risk factor, and is classified under the umbrella term steatotic liver disease (SLD).'),
('75357d2c-347d-5f51-8efa-38981541bda4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: The primary function of segmentation contractions is:', '["Sweeping resulting in decreased functional activity","Storing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixing and churning (smooth muscle segmental contractions)","Emptying without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Propulsion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixing and churning (smooth muscle segmental contractions)', 'Segmentation contractions are stationary, ring-like contractions that mix and churn intestinal contents, promoting digestion and absorption.'),
('4232d7ab-b460-5cdd-9be2-40af97177e73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the first histological change in alcoholic liver disease?', '["Fatty change (steatosis), which is reversible","Cirrhosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Alcoholic hepatitis mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatocellular carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholestasis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty change (steatosis), which is reversible', 'Reversible steatosis (fatty change) is the earliest histological lesion of alcoholic liver disease, preceding alcoholic hepatitis and cirrhosis.'),
('b9938eb3-1cd8-5138-9ee0-e47fd8cd9e51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Water absorption across the intestinal epithelium occurs by:', '["Active transport resulting in decreased functional activity","Simple diffusion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Osmotic (follows actively absorbed solutes)","Filtration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pinocytosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osmotic (follows actively absorbed solutes)', 'Water moves passively across the intestinal epithelium by osmosis, following the osmotic gradient created by actively absorbed solutes such as sodium and glucose.'),
('ec4fb706-5b43-592a-9539-605c018095e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which autoimmune liver disease is characterized by chronic cholestasis, bile duct fibrosis with strictures, and association with ulcerative colitis?', '["Autoimmune hepatitis","Primary biliary cholangitis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Wilson disease","Hemochromatosis"]'::jsonb, 'Primary sclerosing cholangitis', 'Primary sclerosing cholangitis causes fibrosing strictures of intra- and extrahepatic bile ducts with chronic cholestasis and is strongly associated with ulcerative colitis.'),
('7cccddc0-6f4c-5d6f-8303-7ea49e4b3711'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is due to:', '["High LES pressure","Increased frequency of normal swallow-related relaxations","Normal LES pressure","Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone","Absence of gastric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) +/- low basal tone', 'GERD results from transient LES relaxations with or without low basal LES tone, allowing reflux of gastric contents.'),
('1ff63bb4-acd7-50dc-a486-32c8a6e8ce58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: The exocrine pancreas secretes:', '["Insulin only resulting in decreased functional activity","Bile only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Digestive enzymes (trypsinogen amylase lipase) and HCO3-","Enzymes only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucagon only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Digestive enzymes (trypsinogen amylase lipase) and HCO3-', 'The exocrine pancreas secretes digestive enzymes (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, amylase, lipase) and a bicarbonate-rich fluid.'),
('93ec5b3c-7194-588f-96c3-e016f5ceffee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, What is the primary mechanism of action of bile acid sequestrants such as cholestyramine?', '["Direct inhibition of cholesterol synthesis","Binding bile acids in the intestine to interrupt enterohepatic circulation","Increasing HDL cholesterol levels","Reducing intestinal cholesterol absorption via NPC1L1 inhibition","Stimulating LDL receptor expression on hepatocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Binding bile acids in the intestine to interrupt enterohepatic circulation', 'Cholestyramine binds bile acids in the gut, interrupting enterohepatic circulation; the liver then uses cholesterol to synthesize new bile acids, upregulating LDL receptors and lowering LDL.'),
('e574ca6b-3898-56c9-b594-b66c3c36a655'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Pelvic organs and the distal colon receive parasympathetic fibers from:', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'Pelvic organs and the distal colon receive parasympathetic innervation from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4).'),
('a1ea8a70-9a1e-5f92-8352-db82b962e5c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Liver regeneration after partial hepatectomy occurs primarily through which mechanism?', '["Hepatocyte proliferation, driven mainly by HGF (from stellate cells) with priming by TNF-alpha and IL-6","Dedifferentiation of bile duct cells only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Regeneration only in children mediated by specific receptor activation","Scar formation without hepatocyte division without affecting the primary tissue pathway","No regeneration after 30% resection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocyte proliferation, driven mainly by HGF (from stellate cells) with priming by TNF-alpha and IL-6', 'Restoration of hepatic mass relies on proliferation of mature hepatocytes, driven by HGF from stellate cells and priming cytokines including TNF-alpha and IL-6.'),
('c9623026-d193-5c88-ae83-713fb6286139'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes metabolic zonation of the liver acinus?', '["Periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes preferentially perform gluconeogenesis, while perivenous (zone 3) cells perform glycolysis (and express CYP2E1)","Zone 1 preferentially performs glycolysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CYP2E1 is expressed mainly in zone 1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Zone 3 has the best oxygen supply without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All zones have identical metabolic activity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes preferentially perform gluconeogenesis, while perivenous (zone 3) cells perform glycolysis (and express CYP2E1)', 'Zone 1 (periportal) hepatocytes predominate in gluconeogenesis and the urea cycle, while zone 3 (perivenous) cells favor glycolysis and express CYP2E1.'),
('14eb9da8-91b6-5150-9521-f273d331bcf9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'A patient with cirrhosis and easy bruising has prolonged bleeding after tooth extraction. Deficiency of which clotting factors is the cause?', '["Factor X resulting in decreased functional activity","Factor IX accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Factor VII mediated by specific receptor activation","Factors II, VII, IX, X (vitamin K-dependent)","Factor V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Factors II, VII, IX, X (vitamin K-dependent)', 'Advanced liver disease reduces synthesis of the vitamin K-dependent factors II, VII, IX, and X, prolonging PT/INR and causing easy bruising and bleeding.'),
('9487e72c-e188-5030-aff1-0bd73cfb1932'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the difference between hexokinase (HK) and glucokinase (GK) in glucose phosphorylation?', '["Hexokinase has low Km (high affinity) and is inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate; glucokinase has high Km (low affinity), is not product-inhibited, and acts as the hepatic glucose sensor","Both have identical affinity for glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glucokinase is inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Hexokinase is induced by insulin in the liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucokinase is absent from the liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hexokinase has low Km (high affinity) and is inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate; glucokinase has high Km (low affinity), is not product-inhibited, and acts as the hepatic glucose sensor', 'Hexokinase has a low Km and is feedback-inhibited by glucose-6-phosphate, whereas glucokinase has a high Km, is not product-inhibited, and serves as the hepatic glucose sensor.'),
('c4243bbd-0517-591b-8388-6ec9a534a1bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Zinc is absorbed primarily in the:', '["Duodenum/jejunum. ZIP4 apical (up by Zn deficiency)","Solely via basolateral ZnT1","In the colon only","Through passive diffusion in the stomach","Regulated exclusively by dietary iron"]'::jsonb, 'Duodenum/jejunum. ZIP4 apical (up by Zn deficiency)', 'Zinc is absorbed in the duodenum and jejunum by apical ZIP4, which is upregulated by zinc deficiency, with basolateral efflux via ZnT1 and metallothionein regulation by MTF-1.'),
('56bd3d9e-d76b-525e-8623-ef99b37384ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes the TCA (Krebs) cycle?', '["Occurs in the mitochondrial matrix; acetyl-CoA and oxaloacetate form citrate; yields 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, and 1 GTP per turn; isocitrate dehydrogenase is rate-limiting","Occurs in the cytosol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acetyl-CoA enters the cycle directly from the cytosol without transport mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","NADH is the only reduced cofactor produced without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Produces 2 ATP per turn only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occurs in the mitochondrial matrix; acetyl-CoA and oxaloacetate form citrate; yields 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, and 1 GTP per turn; isocitrate dehydrogenase is rate-limiting', 'The TCA cycle runs in the mitochondrial matrix, condensing acetyl-CoA with oxaloacetate; each turn yields 3 NADH, 1 FADH2, and 1 GTP, with isocitrate dehydrogenase as the rate-limiting enzyme.'),
('e165ee1e-bef1-5a80-8b5c-2e54a804f88f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: Which neurotransmitter is released at sympathetic postganglionic endings to inhibit the gut?', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Sympathetic postganglionic endings release norepinephrine, which inhibits GI motility.'),
('0abd3887-c586-5531-b5a7-6b0800f5e4ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes how the gut microbiota influences MASLD?', '["Dysbiosis promotes steatosis through increased energy harvest (SCFAs), intestinal permeability (endotoxemia activating TLR4), altered choline metabolism (TMAO), inflammation, and fibrosis","Dysbiosis has no effect on the liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The gut microbiota only affects the colon mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased intestinal permeability protects the liver without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","SCFAs are not produced by gut bacteria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dysbiosis promotes steatosis through increased energy harvest (SCFAs), intestinal permeability (endotoxemia activating TLR4), altered choline metabolism (TMAO), inflammation, and fibrosis', 'Gut dysbiosis promotes steatosis by increasing energy harvest (SCFAs), intestinal permeability with TLR4 endotoxemia, altered choline metabolism (TMAO), and hepatic inflammation and fibrosis.'),
('d6ec8208-6d18-5bbf-b281-e0660f3d7a1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'A pregnant woman at 32 weeks presents with pruritus of the palms and soles. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy: pruritus (palms and soles) in the third trimester, elevated bile acids, linked to ATP8B1/ABCB11 mutations, with risk of preterm delivery","A disease of the first trimester only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Associated with meconium aspiration always mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Serum bile acids are normal without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carries no fetal risk leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy: pruritus (palms and soles) in the third trimester, elevated bile acids, linked to ATP8B1/ABCB11 mutations, with risk of preterm delivery', 'Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy presents with third-trimester pruritus of the palms and soles, elevated bile acids, ATP8B1/ABCB11 transporter mutations, and an increased risk of preterm delivery.'),
('0ce75729-0e60-53a0-9e5e-53e4905363fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide) is secreted by:', '["G cells resulting in decreased functional activity","S cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","I cells mediated by specific receptor activation","K cells of duodenum in response to glucose/fat → insulinotropic (incretin)","L cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'K cells of duodenum in response to glucose/fat → insulinotropic (incretin)', 'GIP is released by K cells of the duodenum and proximal jejunum in response to glucose and fat, and it potentiates glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (incretin effect).'),
('8f340dc9-1f6f-5c26-978d-82d95aabedb8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency and the liver?', '["PiZZ genotype: abnormal Z protein accumulates in the ER (PAS-positive inclusions); neonatal cholestasis may resolve, about 10% develop cirrhosis by adulthood, with HCC risk; no specific therapy, liver transplant is curative","Always resolves without sequelae accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","There is specific enzyme replacement therapy mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","No risk of hepatocellular carcinoma without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Accumulation occurs in the cytoplasm of stellate cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PiZZ genotype: abnormal Z protein accumulates in the ER (PAS-positive inclusions); neonatal cholestasis may resolve, about 10% develop cirrhosis by adulthood, with HCC risk; no specific therapy, liver transplant is curative', 'In PiZZ alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency, mutant Z protein accumulates in hepatocyte ER as PAS-positive inclusions; up to 10% develop cirrhosis and HCC, and liver transplantation is the only curative therapy.'),
('9386fcfd-576c-5f57-86ea-d7150751a2eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly differentiates Gilbert, Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes?', '["Gilbert syndrome: mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (UGT1A1 promoter polymorphism) with normal LFTs; Crigler-Najjar: severe unconjugated (type 1 lethal, type 2 phenobarbital-responsive); Dubin-Johnson and Rotor: conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Dubin-Johnson syndrome causes unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gilbert syndrome causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Crigler-Najjar type 2 is always lethal without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Rotor syndrome shows a darkly pigmented liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gilbert syndrome: mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (UGT1A1 promoter polymorphism) with normal LFTs; Crigler-Najjar: severe unconjugated (type 1 lethal, type 2 phenobarbital-responsive); Dubin-Johnson and Rotor: conjugated hyperbilirubinemia', 'Gilbert and Crigler-Najjar cause unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia from reduced UGT1A1, while Dubin-Johnson (MRP2 defect, dark liver) and Rotor (OATP defect, no dark liver) cause conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.'),
('c83167fc-8362-54f4-abe6-6c169819ff4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Na+ absorption in the small intestine:', '["Na+/H+ exchange (NHE3) resulting in decreased functional activity","ENaC (colon) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple mechanisms: Na+/glucose cotransport","Only one mechanism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Electroneutral Na+Cl- leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple mechanisms: Na+/glucose cotransport', 'In the small intestine sodium is absorbed by several mechanisms, including Na+-glucose cotransport (SGLT1), Na+/H+ exchange (NHE3) and electroneutral NaCl absorption.'),
('164a0984-a336-516a-a2c1-4e49fc43fd0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which statement correctly describes focal nodular hyperplasia (FNH)?', '["Benign polyclonal hyperplasia of normal hepatocytes containing a central stellate scar; associated with a vascular malformation, not OCP-related, with essentially no malignant potential","A malignant neoplasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Caused by oral contraceptive use mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Monoclonal proliferation of hepatocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Requires surgical resection in all cases leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Benign polyclonal hyperplasia of normal hepatocytes containing a central stellate scar; associated with a vascular malformation, not OCP-related, with essentially no malignant potential', 'FNH is a benign polyclonal hepatocyte hyperplasia with a central stellate scar and an underlying vascular malformation; it is not OCP-related and has essentially no malignant potential.'),
('c4b1e8c5-c500-54ee-8acf-175a7d0a2ed5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which of the following are indications for liver transplantation?', '["Acute liver failure","Decompensated cirrhosis","HCC within Milan criteria","Selected cholestatic and metabolic liver diseases","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Acute liver failure, decompensated cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma within Milan criteria, and selected cholestatic and metabolic liver diseases are all accepted indications for liver transplantation.'),
('6ff89728-6c42-592a-bea6-afb5770d4c0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Which neurotransmitter is released at parasympathetic postganglionic endings?', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","GABA","Serotonin","Dopamine","Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Parasympathetic postganglionic fibers release acetylcholine, the major excitatory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system.'),
('6479e448-a0c5-5bc3-b34f-18f9c08cee46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) is best defined as:', '["Decreased bacterial counts in the small bowel","Bacterial overgrowth limited to the colon",">10^5 CFU/mL in small bowel. Malabsorption","A purely motor disorder without microbial involvement","Bacterial overgrowth of the stomach only"]'::jsonb, '>10^5 CFU/mL in small bowel. Malabsorption', 'SIBO is the presence of more than 10^5 CFU/mL of bacteria in the small bowel, causing malabsorption with diarrhea, bloating and B12 deficiency.'),
('ca590bca-a993-515b-9cea-d11398a76804'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT):', '["Epithelial cells gain apical-basal polarity","EMT is induced by loss of TGF-beta","EMT involves gain of E-cadherin","EMT decreases cell motility","Epithelial lose polarity"]'::jsonb, 'Epithelial lose polarity', 'In EMT, epithelial cells lose polarity and E-cadherin and gain mesenchymal features (vimentin); TGF-beta induces EMT, which contributes to wound healing, fibrosis and metastasis.'),
('87ad1b4c-024d-5977-bc6b-fe9ce6afaf53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which vitamin is stored in the liver for approximately 3-5 years?', '["Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)","Vitamin D","Vitamin E","Vitamin K","Vitamin A"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 (cobalamin)', 'The liver stores vitamin B12, which has a very long half-life, so clinically evident B12 deficiency can take 3-5 years to develop after intake stops.'),
('f088d7b2-96ea-5a04-9aa9-7e4fd430df50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'The liver has how many anatomical lobes and how many surgical segments?', '["6 lobes resulting in decreased functional activity","4 lobes (right, left, caudate, quadrate); 8 surgical segments (Couinaud)","3 lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","5 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 lobes (right, left, caudate, quadrate); 8 surgical segments (Couinaud)', 'The liver has four anatomical lobes (right, left, caudate, quadrate) and is divided into eight surgical segments by the Couinaud classification.'),
('ea689793-6b6b-51b3-93db-133bb4d89c49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which cells produce bile?', '["Kupffer cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Gallbladder accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bile duct epithelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Hepatocytes (approximately 500-1000 mL/day)","Pancreas leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocytes (approximately 500-1000 mL/day)', 'Hepatocytes produce bile at a rate of about 500-1000 mL per day; the gallbladder concentrates it rather than producing it.'),
('a63e30d0-2c67-5694-9509-e1ac8ab3f68f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'The hepatic artery provides what percentage of liver blood flow?', '["20-25% of liver blood flow (oxygen-rich)","All blood supply to the liver","Venous drainage","Bile production","Glucose regulation"]'::jsonb, '20-25% of liver blood flow (oxygen-rich)', 'The hepatic artery delivers 20-25% of hepatic blood flow and is oxygen-rich, while the portal vein provides the remaining 75-80%.'),
('23c305b1-5aef-5636-a876-493ccf6d6e75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Cirrhosis is characterized by which histological feature?', '["Inflammation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Fibrosis and nodular regeneration of the liver","Cancer only mediated by specific receptor activation","Bile duct obstruction without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fatty infiltration only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrosis and nodular regeneration of the liver', 'Cirrhosis is defined by diffuse hepatic fibrosis with regenerative nodules that distort the normal liver architecture.'),
('2c7d373a-71fe-55a9-a4fc-2c169c6e71b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which of the following is a serious complication of portal hypertension?', '["Decreased blood pressure","Improved liver function","Increased urine output","Esophageal varices","Ascites"]'::jsonb, 'Esophageal varices', 'Esophageal varices are the most dangerous complication of portal hypertension because their rupture causes life-threatening upper gastrointestinal bleeding.'),
('c817372c-40d3-5691-b9e5-fd61db1bf9cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Hepatic encephalopathy is caused primarily by which factor?', '["Decreased albumin","Increased clotting factors","Portal hypertension","Accumulation of ammonia and other neurotoxins","Increased bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of ammonia and other neurotoxins', 'Hepatic encephalopathy results from accumulation of ammonia and other gut-derived neurotoxins that cross the blood-brain barrier and impair brain function.'),
('4c6927a8-1afb-5395-9f7a-4a327964bbfc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is jaundice (icterus)?', '["Red skin resulting in decreased functional activity","Pale skin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blue skin mediated by specific receptor activation","Yellow discoloration of skin and sclera due to elevated bilirubin","Green skin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Yellow discoloration of skin and sclera due to elevated bilirubin', 'Jaundice is the yellow discoloration of the skin and sclerae caused by an elevated serum bilirubin concentration.'),
('360adcf4-9d01-5b14-a754-30d657662130'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'A patient presents with yellow skin and sclera. Labs show elevated unconjugated bilirubin, normal transaminases, and no bilirubinuria. What is the most likely mechanism?', '["Hemolysis (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia)","Bile duct obstruction","Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Liver cell damage"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolysis (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia)', 'The combination of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal liver enzymes and no bilirubinuria indicates increased bilirubin production, most commonly from hemolysis.'),
('c310bda1-ea47-5a05-8ae7-32cce56726ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which substance does the liver most abundantly store?', '["Glycogen","Fat-soluble vitamins","Vitamin B12","Iron","Copper"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen', 'The liver stores glycogen (up to about 100 g), along with iron, copper, vitamin B12, and fat-soluble vitamins, but glycogen is the principal macronutrient store.'),
('7edf152d-d4c0-5f84-91cd-c6748afefedd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Where is the liver located?', '["In the mediastinum","In the pelvis","In the retroperitoneum","In the left upper quadrant","In the right upper quadrant of the abdomen"]'::jsonb, 'In the right upper quadrant of the abdomen', 'The liver lies principally in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen, protected by the lower thoracic cage.'),
('7d9b60d9-a540-5252-8e8e-fcf71a625125'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('f8a25271-67d6-543e-a52c-1ab3ad3ebbc6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('0980852d-a013-56ff-8da1-590576aec2ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: The main short-chain fatty acids produced by colonic fermentation of fiber are:', '["Vitamins resulting in decreased functional activity","Amino acids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acetate propionate butyrate (energy source for colonocytes + systemic effects)","Bile acids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholesterol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acetate propionate butyrate (energy source for colonocytes + systemic effects)', 'Fermentation of dietary fiber by colonic bacteria yields acetate, propionate and butyrate, which serve as the main fuel for colonocytes.'),
('2ef70a9a-5fd8-53e9-9419-1ed2c76cc32e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('a89dcb82-4972-50a4-ace9-ad11161fa098'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'After a carbohydrate-rich meal, the liver stores excess glucose in which form?', '["Glucose directly","Fructose","Starch","Lactate","Glycogen (glycogenesis)"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen (glycogenesis)', 'Postprandial hyperglycemia stimulates insulin, which promotes glycogenesis so the liver stores glucose as glycogen.'),
('53e60219-a3cf-5a63-9e4e-fd515e8d8e94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: The major inhibitory neurotransmitter of the enteric nervous system is:', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'The major inhibitory neurotransmitter influencing the enteric nervous system is norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers.'),
('2b63fa16-a627-5cb7-8eb4-dc365c89cf8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the primary hemodynamic cause of ascites in liver disease?', '["Portal hypertension","Hypoalbuminemia","Increased aldosterone","Decreased aldosterone","Increased albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Portal hypertension', 'Portal hypertension raises sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure, driving fluid into the peritoneal cavity; hypoalbuminemia and hyperaldosteronism contribute secondarily.'),
('8de55154-2ab3-5d63-bf6d-8bdb32e1ee55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: A high-fiber diet promotes which gut microbe?', '["A high-fiber diet reduces microbial diversity","A high-fat diet promotes Bifidobacterium","A high-fiber diet promotes Proteobacteria","A high-fiber diet decreases SCFA production","High fiber: Bifido"]'::jsonb, 'High fiber: Bifido', 'A high-fiber diet promotes beneficial bacteria such as Bifidobacterium and Lactobacillus, increasing the production of short-chain fatty acids.'),
('b60fb83e-6ef2-50e9-9ff5-22e3602c8a06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('42e70b99-3eb8-5d75-b5b0-5b47f840f4d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('96f6ef2b-94ae-54c5-9a75-17489bbbfd43'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which hormone stimulates gallbladder contraction and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi after a fatty meal?', '["Secretin","Gastrin","Cholecystokinin (CCK)","Motilin","Gastric inhibitory peptide (GIP)"]'::jsonb, 'Cholecystokinin (CCK)', 'CCK is released by duodenal I cells in response to fat and protein, causing gallbladder contraction and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi to expel bile.'),
('57b5d1d0-2e88-5780-8c47-713d6056af38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Albumin produced by the liver functions mainly to do what?', '["Carry oxygen resulting in decreased functional activity","Clot blood accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fight infection mediated by specific receptor activation","Maintain plasma oncotic pressure and transport hormones","Transport drugs and hormones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain plasma oncotic pressure and transport hormones', 'Albumin is the main determinant of plasma oncotic pressure and also transports hormones, fatty acids, bilirubin, and drugs in the circulation.'),
('a155ff4d-f060-5109-a3e9-b3ca408dfcda'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which structure is contained within the porta hepatis?', '["Cystic duct","IVC and aorta","Splenic vein and artery","Pancreatic duct","Portal vein"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'The porta hepatis transmits the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct; the IVC is not among them.'),
('5c51f62a-410a-5e80-b7cb-5d3d62c498ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('f255439c-0a16-5099-a639-0b8a794b24d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('3fa6d4a2-6bcd-51d2-9805-8659b3eff0bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Which neurotransmitter is the principal inhibitory transmitter acting on the gut?', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Norepinephrine is the principal inhibitory transmitter released by sympathetic postganglionic fibers onto the gut.'),
('77863e2e-7636-54e2-a549-c7d8bc68923d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('47df13a5-7f80-5d15-9388-38a15c647061'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Bilirubin is a breakdown product of which compound?', '["Proteins resulting in decreased functional activity","Fats accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Heme from hemoglobin (senescent RBCs)","Bile salts without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholesterol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Heme from hemoglobin (senescent RBCs)', 'Senescent red blood cells are phagocytosed, and their hemoglobin heme is catabolized via heme oxygenase to biliverdin and then bilirubin.'),
('6b81af3c-234c-5d0a-909f-d097c97de9e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: The postprandial pattern of continuous contraction replaces the MMC after:', '["Medication resulting in decreased functional activity","Fasting accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)","Sleep without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Exercise leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding (postprandial pattern - continuous contraction)', 'After feeding, the fasting MMC is replaced by a continuous postprandial pattern of contraction.'),
('cf517c33-1e70-51ff-8970-9b45ade5d3cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein binds:', '["Basic drugs","Acidic drugs","Neutral drugs","Lipid-soluble drugs","Hydrophilic drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Basic drugs', 'AAG binds cationic drugs (lidocaine, imipramine, propranolol).'),
('7b7c2eaa-3fa3-5159-9fe5-6b46e198dadb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which of the following does the liver synthesize?', '["Only clotting factors","Only complement","Only albumin","Albumin and clotting factors","Only bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin and clotting factors', 'The liver synthesizes albumin and the majority of plasma clotting factors, along with complement proteins.'),
('89c43286-5840-5e16-ab9a-f8b4046c6605'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Which nerves provide parasympathetic supply to the distal colon and rectum?', '["Vagus","Lumbar splanchnic","Phrenic","Thoracic splanchnic","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-4)', 'The pelvic splanchnic nerves arising from S2-S4 carry parasympathetic innervation to the distal colon and rectum.'),
('e8ef60c7-6285-5a80-8d32-ccae34c4f0c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: Monosaccharide absorption in the small intestine:', '["All by GLUT5 resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only active accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","All by SGLT1 mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only passive without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose and galactose: SGLT1 (Na+ dependent secondary active transport). Fructose: GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion)"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose and galactose: SGLT1 (Na+ dependent secondary active transport). Fructose: GLUT5 (facilitated diffusion)', 'Glucose and galactose are absorbed by the Na+-dependent SGLT1 transporter, while fructose is absorbed by the facilitated-diffusion transporter GLUT5.'),
('148d0575-e963-5ef7-9eb6-c1751f6dc873'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in GIT Pt2: Which statement best describes the migrating motor complex (MMC)?', '["Defecation resulting in decreased functional activity","Satiety accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)","Postprandial pattern without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vomiting leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting (housekeeper)', 'The MMC is a cyclic pattern of contraction during fasting that acts as the housekeeper of the gut.'),
('a44aa270-8b4a-57d1-8b2c-677e6baf4643'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, What is the function of the ileocecal sphincter?', '["Site of digestive enzyme secretion","Site of vitamin B12 absorption","Site of bile storage","Site of water absorption","Sphincter preventing backflow from colon to ileum"]'::jsonb, 'Sphincter preventing backflow from colon to ileum', 'The ileocecal sphincter prevents backflow of colonic contents into the ileum and regulates the passage of chyme into the cecum.'),
('80916382-d9e7-5d21-82e5-5390179288a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: Which nerves provide sympathetic innervation to the abdominal organs?', '["Cranial nerves resulting in decreased functional activity","Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)","Pelvic mediated by specific receptor activation","Vagus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Phrenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2)', 'The abdominal organs receive sympathetic innervation through the thoracolumbar splanchnic nerves (T5-L2).'),
('248c4dc2-6630-5930-9086-0840362dfd2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Pre-hepatic jaundice is caused by which mechanism?', '["Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Bile duct obstruction","Liver cell damage","Hemolysis (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia)"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolysis (unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia)', 'Pre-hepatic jaundice is produced by hemolysis, which increases bilirubin production and causes unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.'),
('54702d9f-01bd-543f-be8c-c784832ca8b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: Somatostatin is released from:', '["Chief cells resulting in decreased functional activity","ECL cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","G cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Parietal cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","D cells of stomach and pancreas (inhibitory paracrine hormone)"]'::jsonb, 'D cells of stomach and pancreas (inhibitory paracrine hormone)', 'Somatostatin is released by D cells in the stomach and pancreatic islets and acts as a universal inhibitory paracrine hormone.'),
('0c11502a-2fb6-5524-adac-268ae357c4c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What does secretin cause?', '["Pepsin secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Gastrin release accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gallbladder contraction mediated by specific receptor activation","CCK release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bicarbonate-rich bile and pancreatic juice secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Bicarbonate-rich bile and pancreatic juice secretion', 'Secretin, released in response to duodenal acid, stimulates ductal cells to secrete bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice and bile.'),
('5b7ed936-06fa-5a46-b2b5-63307c740d27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, The major inhibitory neurotransmitter acting on the enteric nervous system is:', '["Histamine","Gastrin","Norepinephrine (sympathetic)","Acetylcholine","Substance P"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine (sympathetic)', 'Postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine, which acts on enteric neurons and smooth muscle to inhibit GI motility and secretion.'),
('cbd93dff-b28f-5d82-afab-1de1831e5fea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in GIT Pt2: The principal excitatory neurotransmitter released by enteric motor neurons onto GI smooth muscle is:', '["ACh (excitatory)","NE (inhibitory)","Dopamine","VIP (inhibitory)","Nitric oxide"]'::jsonb, 'ACh (excitatory)', 'Acetylcholine is the principal excitatory neurotransmitter of enteric motor neurons, contracting GI smooth muscle via muscarinic receptors.'),
('40a6df3f-d1c5-5f47-9d01-b5a0b81d8e95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Pancreatic amylase digests:', '["Protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Nucleic acids accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Starch (glycogen/amylose → maltose maltotriose dextrins)","Fat without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fiber leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Starch (glycogen/amylose → maltose maltotriose dextrins)', 'Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes starch, glycogen and amylose into maltose, maltotriose and alpha-limit dextrins.'),
('96fcbc82-7bc5-5fb8-b173-0e8c5011b888'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: The migrating motor complex (MMC) is characterized by:', '["Bursts of contractions during fasting (interdigestive)","Continuous accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occurs only during sleep mediated by specific receptor activation","Irregular without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Postprandial leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bursts of contractions during fasting (interdigestive)', 'The MMC is a cyclic, interdigestive (fasting) pattern of contractions that sweeps the gut clean between meals.'),
('6cb73374-d4ed-5e39-a43c-6c046c82fb49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'How is the liver anatomically divided and how many surgical segments does it contain?', '["4 lobes (right, left, caudate, quadrate); 8 surgical segments (Couinaud)","3 lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","6 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 lobe leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '4 lobes (right, left, caudate, quadrate); 8 surgical segments (Couinaud)', 'The liver has four anatomical lobes and is divided into eight surgical segments by the Couinaud classification, centered on the portal veins.'),
('bdf0e3a8-6efc-578c-a5c9-cbfcf120a2cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of GIT Pt2, Secondary bile acids (deoxycholic and lithocholic) are formed by:', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Pancreas accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gallbladder mediated by specific receptor activation","Bacterial dehydroxylation in colon → deoxycholic (from cholic) and lithocholic (from CDCA)","Liver leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial dehydroxylation in colon → deoxycholic (from cholic) and lithocholic (from CDCA)', 'Colonic bacteria dehydroxylate primary bile acids: cholic acid becomes deoxycholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid becomes lithocholic acid.'),
('c8c65da8-01f3-5f70-ad4d-d49653f71472'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Into which parts is the gallbladder divided?', '["Fundus, body, and neck","Neck and cystic duct only","One single chamber","Fundus and body only","Cystic duct only"]'::jsonb, 'Fundus, body, and neck', 'The gallbladder is divided into three parts: the fundus, the body, and the neck, which continues as the cystic duct.'),
('3adcc125-a1d6-526c-8ba9-d2b506a5a4d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'The stomach has how many anatomical regions?', '["5 (cardia, fundus, body, antrum, pylorus)","3 (fundus, body, antrum)","2 (body and pylorus)","1","4 (cardia, fundus, body, pylorus)"]'::jsonb, '5 (cardia, fundus, body, antrum, pylorus)', 'The stomach is conventionally divided into five regions: cardia, fundus, body, antrum, and pylorus.'),
('e6d37dd9-34fe-5c95-bcb4-b124c0c45387'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: Acute hyperglycemia (blood glucose >200 mg/dL) affects GI motility by:', '["Acute BG >200 delays gastric emptying","Hastening gastric emptying","Increasing antral contractions","Increasing MMC frequency","Decreasing pyloric tone"]'::jsonb, 'Acute BG >200 delays gastric emptying', 'Acute hyperglycemia delays gastric emptying by reducing antral contractions, disrupting the MMC and increasing pyloric tone; hypoglycemia increases motility.'),
('b17d5d2d-619f-5205-a824-d4f9f3dafe6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which hormones stimulate hepatic glycogenolysis?', '["CCK resulting in decreased functional activity","Secretin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GLP-1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Glucagon and epinephrine","Insulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon and epinephrine', 'Both glucagon and epinephrine stimulate hepatic glycogenolysis through the cAMP-protein kinase A cascade that activates glycogen phosphorylase.'),
('9d60084a-ccd4-5e3d-b01f-699a4ad21051'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'What is the primary product of anaerobic glycolysis in exercising muscle?', '["Alanine","Lactate","Fatty acids","Acetyl-CoA","Glycerol"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate', 'During anaerobic exercise, pyruvate from glycolysis is reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase, and the lactate is transported to the liver for gluconeogenesis.'),
('7a8791fb-fc8d-5104-8a0a-cc1994bd2dcf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which lipoprotein mediates reverse cholesterol transport (peripheral tissues to liver)?', '["VLDL synthesis resulting in decreased functional activity","Reverse cholesterol transport (peripheral tissues to liver)","TG transport mediated by specific receptor activation","Cholesterol storage without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dietary fat transport leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse cholesterol transport (peripheral tissues to liver)', 'HDL mediates reverse cholesterol transport, collecting cholesterol from peripheral tissues and delivering it to the liver for disposal.'),
('19f72c40-0bf2-5914-a9ee-aa62c18caeb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'In the study of GIT Pt2, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('6a6375b7-9317-5e6a-a681-71093fc913d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding GIT Pt2: In the intestinal crypt, Wnt signaling:', '["Degradation of beta-catenin","APC mutation inactivates Wnt signaling","Wnt from Paneth/stromal to Frizzled/LRP","APC loss inhibits proliferation","Wnt signaling inhibits proliferation"]'::jsonb, 'Wnt from Paneth/stromal to Frizzled/LRP', 'Wnt ligands from Paneth and stromal cells bind Frizzled/LRP receptors, stabilizing beta-catenin and driving TCF/LEF-mediated proliferation; APC mutation causes constitutive Wnt signaling.'),
('be49d236-67f3-53ef-9fbf-bb12f6406ac6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'GIT Liver - GIT Part 2', 'Which liver function assessment evaluates albumin and clotting factor production?', '["Liver synthetic function","Renal function","Pancreatic function","Bone marrow","GI motility"]'::jsonb, 'Liver synthetic function', 'Hepatic synthetic function is measured by serum albumin and PT/INR, which reflect production of proteins and clotting factors by the liver.'),
('56387ce8-b314-5853-bf52-c23797e37ca4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What percentage of total body weight is composed of total body water (TBW) in males versus females?', '["60% (male) and 50-55% (female)","40% in both sexes","70% in both sexes","80% in both sexes","30% in both sexes"]'::jsonb, '60% (male) and 50-55% (female)', 'TBW averages about 60% of body weight in males and 50-55% in females. The sex difference reflects a higher proportion of body fat and lower lean mass in females, since adipose tissue contains little water.'),
('a7fcfe3b-c43a-5ace-a980-f7f721ab5875'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Intracellular fluid (ICF) comprises:', '["20% of body weight","40% of body weight (2/3 of TBW)","5% of body weight","50% of body weight","15% of body weight"]'::jsonb, '40% of body weight (2/3 of TBW)', 'ICF constitutes about 40% of body weight, which is two-thirds of total body water. The remaining one-third of TBW (about 20% of body weight) is extracellular fluid.'),
('fd0cfe66-2261-59d5-87c3-f799f9caeda3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Extracellular fluid (ECF) comprises:', '["20% of body weight (1/3 of TBW)","30% of body weight","10% of body weight","40% of body weight","50% of body weight"]'::jsonb, '20% of body weight (1/3 of TBW)', 'ECF makes up about 20% of body weight, i.e., one-third of total body water. It is divided into the interstitial fluid and the plasma.'),
('836269b7-e16a-5233-84fb-a8d6159ef743'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the normal serum sodium concentration range?', '["Potassium resulting in decreased functional activity","Calcium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Chloride mediated by specific receptor activation","Magnesium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sodium (Na+ 135-145 mEq/L)"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium (Na+ 135-145 mEq/L)', 'Normal serum sodium is 135-145 mEq/L. Hyponatremia (<135 mEq/L) and hypernatremia (>145 mEq/L) reflect disturbances of body water relative to sodium.'),
('9762035d-700d-5558-acf7-ea11bd345213'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The major cation of intracellular fluid is:', '["Protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Magnesium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Potassium (K+ 140 mEq/L)","Calcium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sodium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potassium (K+ 140 mEq/L)', 'Potassium is the dominant intracellular cation, present at roughly 140 mEq/L. Sodium and chloride dominate the extracellular compartment, while the Na+/K+-ATPase maintains the steep potassium gradient across cell membranes.'),
('be101411-be79-5dd7-b0ac-68cbd4c7a89e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt1: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('6be4e16d-6722-5537-a9c8-a8393cc97f31'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Plasma osmolality normal range is:', '["320-350 mOsm/kg","275-295 mOsm/kg","295-320 mOsm/kg","200-250 mOsm/kg","250-275 mOsm/kg"]'::jsonb, '275-295 mOsm/kg', 'Normal plasma osmolality is 275-295 mOsm/kg. Osmolality is chiefly determined by sodium, glucose, and urea, and it is tightly regulated by ADH and thirst.'),
('95bc877a-d250-5a3a-90c0-6dfaf1ec5306'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the difference between osmolality and osmolarity?', '["Osmolality > osmolarity in all fluids","Interchangeable terms","Osmolality < osmolarity in all fluids","Osmolality = mOsm/kg solvent; Osmolarity = mOsm/L solution","Osmolarity is measured; osmolality is calculated"]'::jsonb, 'Osmolality = mOsm/kg solvent; Osmolarity = mOsm/L solution', 'Osmolality is the concentration of solute per kilogram of solvent and is the value measured clinically, whereas osmolarity is solute per liter of solution and is usually calculated. For dilute body fluids the two values are nearly equal.'),
('cf5919c3-5b5d-5b9c-8374-322b9a831a03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the clinical definition of edema?', '["Cell swelling resulting in decreased functional activity","Decreased interstitial fluid volume accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased plasma oncotic pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased plasma volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Palpable swelling from increased interstitial fluid volume"]'::jsonb, 'Palpable swelling from increased interstitial fluid volume', 'Edema is clinically defined as palpable swelling produced by an increase in interstitial fluid volume. It results when filtration of fluid out of capillaries exceeds lymphatic return.'),
('c516c3cb-1a49-513c-b667-c940ff20f956'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which measure is the gold standard for estimating glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?', '["Inulin clearance (gold standard)","BUN","Cystatin C","Creatinine clearance (clinical standard)","Uric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Inulin clearance (gold standard)', 'Inulin is freely filtered and is neither reabsorbed nor secreted by the renal tubules, so its clearance exactly equals GFR. Creatinine clearance is the practical clinical approximation because inulin requires continuous infusion.'),
('c1925961-a4f9-5292-8e17-44cf4adfd75e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Normal GFR in young adults is:', '["60-90 mL/min/1.73 m2","30-60 mL/min/1.73 m2","150-200 mL/min/1.73 m2","120-130 mL/min/1.73 m2","200-250 mL/min/1.73 m2"]'::jsonb, '120-130 mL/min/1.73 m2', 'In young healthy adults GFR averages about 120-130 mL/min/1.73 m2. It declines progressively with age and with kidney disease, which is the basis of CKD staging.'),
('60976cb6-64b9-51eb-b5b6-630aceee5a24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Creatinine clearance (CrCl) is calculated by:', '["Cockcroft-Gault: [(140-age) × weight]/(72 × SCr) (×0.85 female)","Urea clearance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CKD-EPI equation mediated by specific receptor activation","MDRD equation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inulin clearance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cockcroft-Gault: [(140-age) × weight]/(72 × SCr) (×0.85 female)', 'The Cockcroft-Gault formula estimates creatinine clearance from age, weight, and serum creatinine, with a 0.85 correction in females. It was originally derived to guide drug dosing, whereas CKD-EPI and MDRD estimate eGFR.'),
('574765dc-1aaf-503f-a68d-d3db17b9e276'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal blood flow (RBF) is about:', '["600 mL/min","1800 mL/min","500 mL/min","1200 mL/min (20-25% of CO)","700 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '1200 mL/min (20-25% of CO)', 'The kidneys receive about 1200 mL/min of blood, roughly 20-25% of cardiac output. This is disproportionately large relative to kidney weight and is required to support filtration and clearance.'),
('85d014cd-f34a-5317-b304-28eaa52244d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal blood flow autoregulation maintains flow between MAP of:', '["120-180 mmHg","60-120 mmHg","80-180 mmHg","50-150 mmHg","100-200 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-180 mmHg', 'Renal blood flow and GFR remain relatively constant when mean arterial pressure is between 80 and 180 mmHg. Outside this range autoregulation fails and flow becomes pressure-dependent.'),
('6d5da4c1-aace-5f74-9e36-e6222d77703c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The filtration fraction (FF) is:', '["RBF/GFR resulting in decreased functional activity","GFR/RBF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RPF/GFR mediated by specific receptor activation","GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)","FF = GFR - RPF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)', 'Filtration fraction is the ratio of GFR to renal plasma flow, normally about 0.18-0.20. It rises when the efferent arteriole constricts (e.g., with angiotensin II), raising peritubular oncotic pressure.'),
('d84df345-032b-5bcb-becd-5e919e36114f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'A 35-year-old male with CKD has a serum creatinine of 1.8 mg/dL. Which anatomical structure of the glomerular filtration barrier is most likely involved in proteinuria?', '["Fenestrated endothelium + GBM + podocyte slit diaphragm","Endothelium only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Capillary only mediated by specific receptor activation","Podocyte only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basement membrane only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fenestrated endothelium + GBM + podocyte slit diaphragm', 'The glomerular filtration barrier consists of the fenestrated endothelium, the glomerular basement membrane, and the podocyte slit diaphragm acting together. Damage to any component impairs selectivity and allows protein leakage.'),
('02a9fa66-a273-5d0f-b932-a16688d4bb2d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What feature of the glomerular basement membrane restricts the filtration of plasma proteins?', '["Rich in negatively charged heparan sulfate → restricts plasma proteins","Fenestrated accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High permeability to albumin mediated by specific receptor activation","Neutral charge without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thin and loosely packed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rich in negatively charged heparan sulfate → restricts plasma proteins', 'The glomerular basement membrane is rich in negatively charged heparan sulfate proteoglycans. This negative charge repels the negatively charged plasma proteins, a property termed charge selectivity.'),
('7a71bf47-9bb4-5e6d-ad3f-631093ecdd3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the effective pore size of the glomerular filtration barrier?', '["15 nm","8 nm (or 70 kDa)","4 nm","1 nm","20 nm"]'::jsonb, '8 nm (or 70 kDa)', 'The filtration barrier has an effective pore size of about 8 nm, corresponding to a molecular weight cutoff of roughly 70 kDa. Larger or negatively charged molecules are restricted from the filtrate.'),
('dba7b43e-2133-5f45-903a-e03c4ad27ba9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the correct expression for net filtration pressure across the glomerular capillary?', '["Glomerular capillary pressure - (Bowman capsule pressure + oncotic pressure)","Oncotic pressure only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Net hydrostatic pressure only mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillary pressure only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bowman pressure only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular capillary pressure - (Bowman capsule pressure + oncotic pressure)', 'Net filtration pressure equals glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus the opposing Bowman''s capsule hydrostatic pressure and the colloid oncotic pressure. The capillary pressure (45-50 mmHg) is the main driving force for filtration.'),
('71015c20-ce98-5956-af87-790df0a5f497'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the approximate normal hydrostatic pressure in glomerular capillaries?', '["30 mmHg","60 mmHg","45-50 mmHg","80 mmHg","25 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '45-50 mmHg', 'Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure is approximately 45-50 mmHg, considerably higher than in most systemic capillaries. It is the principal force driving glomerular filtration.'),
('9cd0f5d6-1cee-5b05-8604-828e4dd8d3d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Bowman''s capsule hydrostatic pressure (Pbc) is:', '["10-15 mmHg","5 mmHg","40 mmHg","2 mmHg","25 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '10-15 mmHg', 'Bowman''s capsule hydrostatic pressure is about 10-15 mmHg. It opposes glomerular filtration; obstruction of the urinary tract raises Pbc and reduces net filtration pressure and GFR.'),
('4f015dd6-7a0e-5b1c-b95c-d6c41a546d6f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure (πgc) is:', '["10 mmHg resulting in decreased functional activity","40 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","15 mmHg mediated by specific receptor activation","20 mmHg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","25 mmHg at afferent end (rising to 35 at efferent due to filtration)"]'::jsonb, '25 mmHg at afferent end (rising to 35 at efferent due to filtration)', 'Plasma oncotic pressure in the glomerular capillary is about 25 mmHg at the afferent end and rises to 35 mmHg at the efferent end. It increases along the capillary because protein-free filtrate is removed, concentrating plasma proteins.'),
('c6ee20b1-3036-5f22-9731-08ca3a9b3684'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Filtration equilibrium occurs when:', '["Filtration ceases transiently resulting in decreased functional activity","Net filtration pressure reaches zero (πgc equals Pgc - Pbc) near the efferent end","Only in healthy kidneys mediated by specific receptor activation","Only in disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","All plasma is filtered leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Net filtration pressure reaches zero (πgc equals Pgc - Pbc) near the efferent end', 'Filtration equilibrium refers to the state in which rising oncotic pressure brings net filtration pressure to zero before the efferent end of the capillary. In humans this is nearly achieved, so filtration stops along the capillary.'),
('6b2ef8bd-3c77-5ad2-a44d-5cb1569e6e04'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Afferent arteriole resistance determines:', '["Venous drainage","Tubular reabsorption","Bowman pressure","Efferent resistance","RBF and Pgc (↓RA → ↑RBF ↑Pgc → ↑GFR)"]'::jsonb, 'RBF and Pgc (↓RA → ↑RBF ↑Pgc → ↑GFR)', 'Afferent arteriolar resistance is the main regulator of renal blood flow and glomerular capillary pressure. Dilating the afferent arteriole increases RBF, Pgc, and GFR, while constricting it has the opposite effects.'),
('f245c1a7-8a80-5ce2-889c-ea44566049d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Efferent arteriole resistance determines:', '["Pgc only resulting in decreased functional activity","Post-glomerular resistance (↑RE → ↑Pgc but ↓RBF)","RBF only mediated by specific receptor activation","No significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Filtration only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Post-glomerular resistance (↑RE → ↑Pgc but ↓RBF)', 'Efferent arteriolar resistance is the post-glomerular resistance. Constriction (e.g., by angiotensin II) raises glomerular capillary pressure and can preserve GFR even as renal blood flow falls.'),
('fbafac90-fdb5-5929-9fed-845491351d29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Autoregulation of GFR is mediated by:', '["Myogenic response (stretch → contraction) + Tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF)","Hormonal control only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endothelial control only mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural control only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tubular transport only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Myogenic response (stretch → contraction) + Tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF)', 'GFR autoregulation depends on the myogenic response of the afferent arteriole and tubuloglomerular feedback. The myogenic response constricts vessels when stretched by rising pressure, while TGF adjusts afferent tone based on NaCl delivery to the macula densa.'),
('11aa521a-7474-5d68-9d66-db0d2e367c6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism in the kidney?', '["Tubular reabsorption of glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Afferent dilation only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Podocyte feedback mediated by specific receptor activation","Macula densa senses NaCl delivery → signals afferent arteriole to adjust resistance","Efferent constriction only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Macula densa senses NaCl delivery → signals afferent arteriole to adjust resistance', 'In tubuloglomerular feedback, the macula densa monitors NaCl delivery in the distal tubule and signals the afferent arteriole to adjust its resistance. High NaCl delivery constricts the afferent arteriole, lowering GFR and stabilizing the filtered load.'),
('4cf4fae9-c22c-58d1-9bc4-42f9404db471'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Macula densa is located:', '["Proximal tubule resulting in decreased functional activity","Glomerulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collecting duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Bowman capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the thick ascending limb at the junction with the glomerular afferent arteriole"]'::jsonb, 'In the thick ascending limb at the junction with the glomerular afferent arteriole', 'The macula densa is a specialized plaque of cells in the thick ascending limb where it passes between the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own glomerulus. It senses tubular NaCl concentration to regulate GFR and renin release.'),
('9b8a1792-159a-5ed4-aa96-8f03c42900dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus is composed of:', '["Only mesangial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Macula densa + JG cells (renin) + extraglomerular mesangial cells","Only afferent arteriole mediated by specific receptor activation","Only JG cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only macula densa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Macula densa + JG cells (renin) + extraglomerular mesangial cells', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus consists of the macula densa, the juxtaglomerular (granular) cells of the afferent arteriole that store and release renin, and the extraglomerular mesangial cells. Together they mediate tubuloglomerular feedback and renin secretion.'),
('c7a3ab0e-09e6-5b83-906f-b7bb2010ac29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Angiotensin II effects include:', '["Increased thirst and ADH release","All of the above","Vasoconstriction (↑SVR)","Proximal Na+/H+ stimulation","Aldosterone secretion (↑Na+ reabsorption)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor that raises systemic vascular resistance, stimulates aldosterone and ADH secretion, increases thirst, and enhances proximal tubular Na+/H+ exchange. All of these effects act together to defend blood pressure and blood volume.'),
('d11a2021-bae3-5dbc-b3c2-98d0baf4869d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What percentage of filtered Na+ and water is reabsorbed by the proximal tubule?', '["75%","80%","45%","50%","65%"]'::jsonb, '65%', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs approximately 65% of filtered sodium and water. This is largely an obligatory, isosmotic reabsorption driven by basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase.'),
('9e13d20e-12d1-5682-b6dd-fcbdd3b0c750'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Proximal tubule sodium reabsorption is ultimately driven by the basolateral:', '["Na+/K+ ATPase","Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3)","SGLT2","ENaC","NKCC2"]'::jsonb, 'Na+/K+ ATPase', 'The basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase creates the electrochemical gradient that drives all apical sodium entry in the proximal tubule, including NHE3, SGLT2, and amino-acid cotransporters. It is therefore the primary energy source for proximal sodium reabsorption.'),
('68fa9196-626c-5ea6-932f-209204aed33d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Approximately what fraction of filtered sodium is reabsorbed by the loop of Henle?', '["25%","50%","65%","90%","99%"]'::jsonb, '25%', 'The loop of Henle reabsorbs about 25% of filtered sodium, mainly by active NKCC2 transport in the thick ascending limb. This segment also contributes to the medullary osmotic gradient used for urine concentration.'),
('412d7930-b6e3-5013-8ba5-5501042ee764'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What percentage of filtered bicarbonate is reabsorbed by the proximal tubule?', '["65%","70%","99%","50%","80%"]'::jsonb, '80%', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs approximately 80% of filtered bicarbonate. Reabsorption requires apical H+ secretion and brush-border carbonic anhydrase, and it is impaired by carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide.'),
('a4ec6672-fd30-5261-8b6c-630a2db1f8ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Proximal tubule is the site of action for:', '["Spironolactone resulting in decreased functional activity","Hydrochlorothiazide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amiloride mediated by specific receptor activation","Acetazolamide (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor → diuresis + HCO3- loss)","Furosemide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Acetazolamide (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor → diuresis + HCO3- loss)', 'Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, blocking HCO3- reabsorption and producing a weak diuresis with alkaline urine and metabolic acidosis. Other classes act on different nephron segments.'),
('c91466a0-7062-5b3b-bcdb-ecb79b0414e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The thin ascending limb of the loop of Henle is:', '["Impermeable to water","Permeable to water","Impermeable to NaCl","Permeable to NaCl (passive diffusion)","A site of active solute transport"]'::jsonb, 'Impermeable to water', 'The thin ascending limb is impermeable to water but permeable to NaCl. Sodium chloride diffuses passively out of the lumen, diluting the tubular fluid and contributing to the medullary osmotic gradient.'),
('daaf000e-98b5-5782-85db-aa1f5a3f8a1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which diuretic class acts on the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle?', '["Amiloride resulting in decreased functional activity","Spironolactone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thiazides mediated by specific receptor activation","Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide)","Acetazolamide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide)', 'Loop diuretics inhibit the NKCC2 cotransporter in the thick ascending limb, the segment that reabsorbs about 25% of filtered sodium. They are the most potent diuretics and also increase calcium and magnesium excretion.'),
('2ce52da1-8531-5573-9c2a-60a08192f19c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'In the thick ascending limb, calcium and magnesium are reabsorbed paracellularly because of the:', '["Cl- and HCO3- reabsorption","Only water movement","Water and urea reabsorption","Na+ and K+ reabsorption","Ca2+ and Mg2+ (driven by lumen-positive potential)"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+ and Mg2+ (driven by lumen-positive potential)', 'NKCC2 transport creates a lumen-positive transepithelial potential in the thick ascending limb. This positive potential drives paracellular reabsorption of calcium and magnesium, which is why loop diuretics increase urinary Ca2+ and Mg2+.'),
('ff24d150-a33a-5253-acfd-4a87710f3f92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which transporter mediates NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb?', '["Na+ channel resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+/K+ ATPase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Water channel mediated by specific receptor activation","NKCC2 cotransporter (same as TAL)","Ca2+ channel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NKCC2 cotransporter (same as TAL)', 'The thick ascending limb reabsorbs sodium via the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- (NKCC2) cotransporter, the target of loop diuretics. It is also the site where the macula densa senses tubular NaCl.'),
('f214d8fe-6c16-52c2-a04d-1ce89c762f15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The distal convoluted tubule (DCT) reabsorbs Na+ and Cl- via the:', '["SGLT2 resulting in decreased functional activity","ENaC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+/H+ exchanger mediated by specific receptor activation","Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) - blocked by thiazide diuretics","NKCC2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) - blocked by thiazide diuretics', 'The distal convoluted tubule reabsorbs sodium and chloride through the apical NCC cotransporter. Thiazide diuretics block NCC, causing natriuresis and increased calcium reabsorption.'),
('b3113cf4-3a37-5092-9761-50d0f614b816'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Thiazide diuretics act on:', '["Na+ channel in DCT","CA in proximal tubule","NCC in DCT (block → ↑Na+ and Cl- excretion)","ENaC in collecting duct","NKCC2 in TAL"]'::jsonb, 'NCC in DCT (block → ↑Na+ and Cl- excretion)', 'Thiazides inhibit the Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule, increasing sodium and chloride excretion. They enhance distal calcium reabsorption, which is useful in calcium-stone disease.'),
('0cc5fd1d-24a2-5407-bb56-531b684adbfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The connecting tubule and collecting duct:', '["Only K+ secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Only H+ secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Principal cells: Na+ reabsorption (ENaC) and K+ secretion (ROMK). Intercalated cells: H+ or HCO3- secretion","Only water reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Na+ reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Principal cells: Na+ reabsorption (ENaC) and K+ secretion (ROMK). Intercalated cells: H+ or HCO3- secretion', 'Principal cells reabsorb sodium through ENaC and secrete potassium via ROMK, while type A and type B intercalated cells secrete H+ or HCO3-. This segment is the final site for regulating sodium, potassium, and acid-base balance.'),
('13e00f7f-ba33-58d6-bb6d-c09a2869d25a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'ENaC (epithelial Na+ channel) is located on:', '["Proximal tubule resulting in decreased functional activity","DCT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Apical membrane of principal cells (collecting duct/connecting tubule)","Basolateral membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TAL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apical membrane of principal cells (collecting duct/connecting tubule)', 'ENaC sits on the apical membrane of principal cells in the connecting tubule and collecting duct. Aldosterone increases ENaC expression, while amiloride and triamterene block the channel.'),
('547ac742-cc66-5bc4-99f0-253c7876deec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'ENaC is blocked by:', '["HCTZ resulting in decreased functional activity","Furosemide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acetazolamide mediated by specific receptor activation","Spironolactone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amiloride and triamterene (K+-sparing diuretics)"]'::jsonb, 'Amiloride and triamterene (K+-sparing diuretics)', 'Amiloride and triamterene directly block ENaC in the collecting duct, making them potassium-sparing diuretics. Spironolactone is an aldosterone receptor antagonist rather than a direct ENaC blocker.'),
('ebb36b96-6860-588c-b424-7ccdfa7e75e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Aldosterone acts on principal cells by:', '["↑K+ secretion only resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting Na+/K+ ATPase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binding mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) → ↑ENaC and ROMK → ↑Na+ reabsorption","Stimulating NCC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking ENaC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) → ↑ENaC and ROMK → ↑Na+ reabsorption', 'Aldosterone binds the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor in principal cells and increases apical ENaC and ROMK expression. This enhances sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion, raising blood pressure and lowering serum potassium.'),
('f912495d-1ff1-56b7-92fe-686819455cbb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Spironolactone and eplerenone are:', '["Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists → ↓ENaC → Na+ loss","NCC blockers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+ channel blockers mediated by specific receptor activation","ENaC blockers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Potassium-losing diuretics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists → ↓ENaC → Na+ loss', 'Spironolactone and eplerenone competitively block the mineralocorticoid receptor, reducing ENaC expression and promoting natriuresis while sparing potassium. They are therefore potassium-sparing diuretics.'),
('809c8e0b-1a7e-5a99-b234-915ffd1f3d3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Urine concentration depends on:', '["ADH (inserts AQP2) + medullary hypertonicity (generated by countercurrent multiplication)","Only medullary gradient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only GFR mediated by specific receptor activation","Only ADH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only water intake leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADH (inserts AQP2) + medullary hypertonicity (generated by countercurrent multiplication)', 'Concentrated urine requires both a hypertonic medullary interstitium and ADH. ADH inserts aquaporin-2 channels in the collecting duct, allowing water to be drawn out osmotically along the medullary gradient.'),
('c7f5ef48-75b4-55b6-9fd1-bc33694d3f54'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Urea recycling contributes to medullary hypertonicity when:', '["Urea moves from the collecting duct (ADH-dependent) → medullary interstitium → ascending vasa recta/thin limb → adds to gradient","Urea is only filtered accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urea moves with water only mediated by specific receptor activation","K+ gradient builds urea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only NaCl contributes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Urea moves from the collecting duct (ADH-dependent) → medullary interstitium → ascending vasa recta/thin limb → adds to gradient', 'In the presence of ADH the inner medullary collecting duct is urea-permeable, so urea diffuses into the interstitium and is recycled into the thin limb. This urea trapping adds substantially to medullary hypertonicity.'),
('52ea3f21-41c8-5b8c-97aa-83fbca7a94fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The countercurrent multiplier (loop of Henle) and countercurrent exchanger (vasa recta) function so that:', '["Multiplier: generates gradient. Exchanger: maintains gradient","Exchanger: generates the gradient","Multiplier: maintains the gradient","They serve identical functions","They oppose each other"]'::jsonb, 'Multiplier: generates gradient. Exchanger: maintains gradient', 'The loop of Henle acts as a countercurrent multiplier that actively generates the medullary osmotic gradient, while the vasa recta acts as a countercurrent exchanger that preserves the gradient by not washing it out.'),
('aa52b915-ef9f-599f-9553-dc2f7bbe48df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Free water clearance (CH2O) =:', '["V × Posm resulting in decreased functional activity","V - Cosm (where Cosm = Uosm × V / Posm; positive = water excretion)","Cosm - V mediated by specific receptor activation","Uosm × V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Posm/V leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'V - Cosm (where Cosm = Uosm × V / Posm; positive = water excretion)', 'Free water clearance is urine flow rate minus osmolar clearance (V - Cosm). A positive CH2O indicates excretion of dilute urine (free water loss), while a negative value (TcH2O) indicates free water reabsorption in concentrated urine.'),
('bb84c4fd-51f0-5662-9727-afa636ceff85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which formula represents free water reabsorption (TcH2O), which is positive when urine is concentrated?', '["Posm/Uosm resulting in decreased functional activity","Uosm × V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","V - Cosm mediated by specific receptor activation","V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cosm - V (positive in concentrated urine)"]'::jsonb, 'Cosm - V (positive in concentrated urine)', 'Free water reabsorption (TcH2O) equals Cosm - V and is positive when the kidney is concentrating urine under the influence of ADH. It quantifies the water reabsorbed in excess of that needed to keep urine isosmotic.'),
('76e553d5-31a9-5de3-824a-9e710a1e3cae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The diluting segments of the nephron are:', '["TAL and DCT (both impermeable to water)","Loop only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collecting duct only mediated by specific receptor activation","DCT only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proximal tubule only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TAL and DCT (both impermeable to water)', 'The thick ascending limb and distal convoluted tubule are impermeable to water. They reabsorb solute without water, diluting the tubular fluid and producing free water, which is essential for excreting dilute urine.'),
('cd8d78e5-b414-532d-b26c-b07dee017b08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which brainstem center coordinates detrusor contraction with sphincter relaxation during micturition?', '["Cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pontine micturition center (Barrington nucleus)","Basal ganglia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Frontal cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine micturition center (Barrington nucleus)', 'The pontine micturition center coordinates the micturition reflex by simultaneously activating the detrusor and relaxing the external urethral sphincter. The frontal cortex provides voluntary control, and sacral segments provide the spinal reflex arc.'),
('7f3a6333-3b97-5eeb-9a05-5e0894902b49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which reflex arc mediates normal bladder emptying?', '["Stretch receptors in bladder (Aδ fibers) → sacral (S2-4) parasympathetic → detrusor contraction + internal sphincter relaxation","Sensory fibers only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sphincter activity only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor fibers only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Detrusor activity only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch receptors in bladder (Aδ fibers) → sacral (S2-4) parasympathetic → detrusor contraction + internal sphincter relaxation', 'Bladder filling activates stretch receptors whose afferents travel to the sacral micturition center. Sacral parasympathetic outflow then contracts the detrusor and relaxes the internal urethral sphincter to empty the bladder.'),
('33b9990d-9dbe-583b-8693-8dad55969c7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which neural pathway provides voluntary control of micturition?', '["Sphincter alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Detrusor alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal cortex → pontine center → initiates/permits detrusor contraction and external sphincter relaxation","Spinal cord alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal cortex → pontine center → initiates/permits detrusor contraction and external sphincter relaxation', 'Voluntary micturition is initiated by the frontal cortex, which signals the pontine micturition center to permit detrusor contraction and relaxation of the external sphincter. Loss of cortical control can cause neurogenic bladder.'),
('f40069cd-c10f-574a-a0e2-76d3a46a2d04'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Bladder filling is primarily a:', '["Active contraction resulting in decreased functional activity","Passive process (viscoelastic relaxation of detrusor) with minimal pressure rise","High-pressure process mediated by specific receptor activation","Active relaxation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Secretory process leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Passive process (viscoelastic relaxation of detrusor) with minimal pressure rise', 'Bladder filling occurs passively through the viscoelastic relaxation of the detrusor muscle, so intravesical pressure rises very little until the bladder is nearly full. This property, called accommodation, prevents premature voiding.'),
('9d3844dc-1d4e-5c4e-b3e0-775d1b2b7c4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The internal urethral sphincter (bladder neck):', '["Smooth muscle (α1-adrenergic → contraction maintains continence)","Striated muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Voluntary skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Innervated by parasympathetic nerves only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Relaxed by α1-agonists leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle (α1-adrenergic → contraction maintains continence)', 'The internal urethral sphincter is smooth muscle innervated by sympathetic nerves. α1-adrenergic stimulation contracts it to maintain urinary continence, while α-blockers relax it.'),
('58cfc1ec-6264-557d-990e-08da6a1571c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia and in which condition is it commonly seen?', '["Normal coordinated voiding resulting in decreased functional activity","Involuntary bladder contraction with normal sphincter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Overflow incontinence mediated by specific receptor activation","Loss of coordination between detrusor contraction and external sphincter relaxation (neurogenic bladder)","Urinary retention from prostatic obstruction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of coordination between detrusor contraction and external sphincter relaxation (neurogenic bladder)', 'Detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia is the uncoordinated contraction of the detrusor with simultaneous contraction of the external urethral sphincter during voiding. It is characteristic of suprasacral spinal cord injury and other neurogenic bladder disorders.'),
('0a9c76a8-6c53-50b8-8b8e-58934ab659fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Overflow incontinence:', '["Leakage from overdistended bladder (chronic retention / neurogenic)","Sphincter weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Detrusor overactivity mediated by specific receptor activation","Fistula without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nocturnal enuresis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Leakage from overdistended bladder (chronic retention / neurogenic)', 'Overflow incontinence is the leakage of urine from an overdistended, chronically retained bladder, often due to neurogenic bladder, outlet obstruction, or detrusor underactivity. Intravesical pressure eventually exceeds outlet resistance.'),
('d76cc1f5-3a85-5fa5-8b11-a97ce5be7979'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the medical term for involuntary urination during sleep?', '["Nocturnal enuresis","Overflow incontinence","Daytime incontinence","Stress incontinence","Urge incontinence"]'::jsonb, 'Nocturnal enuresis', 'Nocturnal enuresis is the involuntary passage of urine during sleep, most common in children. It may reflect delayed maturation of bladder control and is sometimes associated with urinary tract infection or overactive bladder.'),
('f057b362-c6c0-584e-86fe-0d55757c6ca8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which hormone is synthesized by the kidneys?', '["Erythropoietin","Aldosterone","ADH","Calcitonin","Insulin"]'::jsonb, 'Erythropoietin', 'The kidneys produce erythropoietin from peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the cortex in response to hypoxia. Aldosterone comes from the adrenal cortex, and ADH from the hypothalamus/posterior pituitary.'),
('01756224-7dc4-564c-9ae6-cdeaaf119111'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which cells are the primary targets of erythropoietin (EPO) in the bone marrow?', '["Spleen cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Kidney cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bone marrow erythroid progenitor cells (CFU-E)","Liver cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lung cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bone marrow erythroid progenitor cells (CFU-E)', 'Erythropoietin acts on erythroid progenitors (CFU-E) in the bone marrow, promoting their proliferation and differentiation into red blood cells. Low renal EPO production in CKD causes anemia.'),
('f5881511-cbef-5d2f-b777-cd18abef0c24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renalase is:', '["An enzyme produced by the kidneys that metabolizes circulating catecholamines","A neuromodulator of renal nerves accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An incretin mediated by specific receptor activation","A steroid hormone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fat-soluble vitamin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An enzyme produced by the kidneys that metabolizes circulating catecholamines', 'Renalase is an amine oxidase secreted by the kidneys that degrades circulating catecholamines. Reduced renalase activity in CKD contributes to the hypertension of chronic kidney disease.'),
('ec22d86e-ff4a-549c-95b2-cea8be580ff5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The kidney maintains acid-base balance by:', '["Reabsorbing filtered HCO3- (proximal tubule)","Generating new HCO3-","All of the above","Only excreting acid","Excreting H+ as titratable acid and NH4+ (distal nephron)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The kidney defends acid-base balance by reclaiming filtered bicarbonate, excreting H+ as titratable acid and ammonium, and generating new bicarbonate. All three processes act together to match acid production.'),
('08f4b164-9259-5163-ae5b-436837833df2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Proximal tubule reabsorbs filtered HCO3- via:', '["Passive diffusion resulting in decreased functional activity","H+ secretion (NHE3) + apical CA IV → H2CO3 → H2O + CO2 (CO2 diffuses into cell)","H+ ATPase only mediated by specific receptor activation","Paracellular transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct HCO3- transport leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+ secretion (NHE3) + apical CA IV → H2CO3 → H2O + CO2 (CO2 diffuses into cell)', 'Proximal bicarbonate reabsorption begins with H+ secretion by NHE3; H+ combines with filtered HCO3- to form H2CO3, which brush-border carbonic anhydrase (CA IV) splits into CO2 and water. CO2 diffuses into the cell and is reconverted to HCO3-, which exits via NBCe1.'),
('87315cd8-0d2e-5442-84cb-3ac036eed9cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which transporters are characteristic of type A intercalated cells in the collecting duct?', '["Cl- channel only resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ channel only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","H+-ATPase (apical) and H+/K+ ATPase → secretes H+ into lumen (generates HCO3- intracellularly)","ENaC only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NHE3 only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+-ATPase (apical) and H+/K+ ATPase → secretes H+ into lumen (generates HCO3- intracellularly)', 'Type A intercalated cells secrete H+ via apical H+-ATPase and H+/K+-ATPase, generating new bicarbonate that is returned to the blood. This is the site of distal acid excretion, defective in type 1 RTA.'),
('04332641-158e-5c36-8cfb-6cbd25554e54'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Ammoniagenesis (NH3/NH4+ production) occurs in the:', '["Distal tubule resulting in decreased functional activity","Glomerulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TAL mediated by specific receptor activation","Collecting duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proximal tubule: glutamine → NH4+ + α-ketoglutarate → new HCO3- generated"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal tubule: glutamine → NH4+ + α-ketoglutarate → new HCO3- generated', 'The proximal tubule produces NH4+ from glutamine, generating α-ketoglutarate that is metabolized to bicarbonate. Each ammonium excreted represents one new bicarbonate added to the blood, a key adaptive response in acidosis.'),
('26733bd1-3e56-5890-ba00-7206ba3f28c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'NH4+ is secreted in the proximal tubule via:', '["NHE3 (NH4+ substitutes for H+ on the exchanger)","H+ ATPase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+ channel mediated by specific receptor activation","K+ channel without affecting the primary tissue pathway","UT-A leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NHE3 (NH4+ substitutes for H+ on the exchanger)', 'In the proximal tubule, NH4+ is secreted into the lumen by substituting for H+ on the NHE3 exchanger. This is one component of net acid excretion that rises in chronic acidosis.'),
('4894f3cc-27b7-5a0a-8069-76651d90549a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'NH3 diffuses into the collecting duct lumen and is excreted because it:', '["Is transported as NH4+ directly resulting in decreased functional activity","Freely diffuses as NH3 (gas) and is trapped as NH4+ (protonated) in the acid lumen","Is pumped by H+-ATPase mediated by specific receptor activation","Moves paracellularly without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Requires Na+/K+ ATPase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Freely diffuses as NH3 (gas) and is trapped as NH4+ (protonated) in the acid lumen', 'Ammonia (NH3) is a lipid-soluble gas that freely diffuses into the acidic collecting duct lumen, where it combines with secreted H+ to form NH4+. The charged NH4+ cannot diffuse back, so acid is trapped and excreted.'),
('2000c7eb-a261-5af3-8428-0fe4c8867747'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Net acid excretion (NAE) =:', '["NH4+ + Titratable acid - HCO3- excretion (normal 50-100 mEq/day)","NH4+ only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Titratable acid only mediated by specific receptor activation","HCO3- only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NH4+ + HCO3- leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NH4+ + Titratable acid - HCO3- excretion (normal 50-100 mEq/day)', 'Net acid excretion equals ammonium plus titratable acid minus excreted bicarbonate, normally about 50-100 mEq/day. It represents the total amount of acid removed by the kidneys and new bicarbonate generated.'),
('b1436169-dcd9-543b-ba2b-466d6b4362d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Titratable acid in the urine consists of:', '["NH4+ resulting in decreased functional activity","HCO3- accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","H+ buffered by filtered phosphate (HPO42- → H2PO4-) and creatinine","Urea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Protein that is fully deaminated leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+ buffered by filtered phosphate (HPO42- → H2PO4-) and creatinine', 'Titratable acid is the H+ excreted with filtered buffers, mainly phosphate (HPO42- → H2PO4-) and creatinine. It is measured by the alkali needed to titrate urine back to pH 7.4 and is a component of net acid excretion.'),
('6c7a7ebd-1190-5d4f-a5e3-8ad77d95bd78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal mechanisms that maintain metabolic alkalosis include:', '["Less H+ secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","NH3 production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increased HCO3- reabsorption (proximal) + ↓H+ secretion (distal)","More HCO3- excretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Equal HCO3- reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased HCO3- reabsorption (proximal) + ↓H+ secretion (distal)', 'Metabolic alkalosis is maintained by increased proximal bicarbonate reabsorption combined with reduced distal H+ secretion. This prevents the excess bicarbonate from being excreted, perpetuating the alkalosis.'),
('b2f6e5b5-2997-5bb4-a36a-607d5d5b4eed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal response to metabolic acidosis:', '["↑NH4+ production (days) + ↑TA + ↑H+ secretion → ↑HCO3- regeneration","↓NH4+ production accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","↑HCO3- excretion mediated by specific receptor activation","No change without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓Titratable acid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '↑NH4+ production (days) + ↑TA + ↑H+ secretion → ↑HCO3- regeneration', 'In metabolic acidosis the kidneys increase H+ secretion, titratable acid, and especially NH4+ production (which takes days), regenerating bicarbonate. The increased ammonium excretion is the largest adaptive response.'),
('6766b543-ca89-5b89-b7f8-f87f90799801'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'High dietary K+ intake stimulates:', '["↓Na+ absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Aldosterone (directly) → ↑K+ secretion in collecting duct","No change mediated by specific receptor activation","↑K+ absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓K+ reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone (directly) → ↑K+ secretion in collecting duct', 'High potassium intake directly stimulates aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex. Aldosterone then increases K+ secretion in the collecting duct principal cells, promoting potassium excretion and restoring balance.'),
('c8cd174d-9942-565f-b46e-f1c576f775b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Treatment for hyperkalemia includes:', '["β2-agonists (albuterol)","All of the above","IV calcium gluconate (stabilizes myocardium)","Kayexalate (binds K+ in gut → removal)","Insulin + glucose (shifts K+ into cells)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Hyperkalemia is managed by calcium gluconate to stabilize the myocardium, insulin plus glucose and β2-agonists to shift K+ into cells, and Kayexalate or dialysis to remove potassium from the body.'),
('989be179-3336-5c6b-89df-c07996787eee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal Ca2+ handling is best described as:', '["25% reabsorbed in TAL (paracellular)","Filtered Ca2+ 65% reabsorbed in PT (paracellular)","10% reabsorbed in DCT/CNT (active TRPV5)","Reabsorbed only in PT","Reabsorbed only in TAL"]'::jsonb, 'Filtered Ca2+ 65% reabsorbed in PT (paracellular)', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs about 65% of filtered calcium, mainly by paracellular transport driven by the lumen-positive potential and solvent drag. The TAL reabsorbs 25% and the DCT/CNT 10% via active TRPV5 channels regulated by PTH.'),
('160f1f67-67c8-53b5-8e3d-450b553972b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'PTH increases Ca2+ reabsorption in:', '["CT resulting in decreased functional activity","PT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","DCT/CNT (↑TRPV5 expression and activity)","Glomerulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TAL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'DCT/CNT (↑TRPV5 expression and activity)', 'PTH stimulates calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule and connecting tubule by upregulating apical TRPV5 channels. This is the hormonally regulated site of renal calcium conservation.'),
('cde10637-c4af-5ae3-8d71-31b70f9d512a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Where is the majority of filtered phosphate reabsorbed in the kidney?', '["Collecting duct resulting in decreased functional activity","Distal tubule accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TAL mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomerulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reabsorbed in the proximal tubule (NaPi-IIa cotransporter) - regulated by PTH and FGF23"]'::jsonb, 'Reabsorbed in the proximal tubule (NaPi-IIa cotransporter) - regulated by PTH and FGF23', 'About 85% of filtered phosphate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule via the apical NaPi-IIa cotransporter. PTH and FGF23 downregulate this transporter, causing phosphaturia.'),
('667bb3b3-5684-5c2a-a1da-aac45b2267f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which statement about FGF23 is correct?', '["Phosphaturic hormone (↓NaPi-IIa → ↓Pi reabsorption) + ↓1,25-(OH)2D synthesis","Increases vitamin D synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases phosphate reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Acts like PTH by raising calcium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↑Phosphate reabsorption in the DCT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphaturic hormone (↓NaPi-IIa → ↓Pi reabsorption) + ↓1,25-(OH)2D synthesis', 'FGF23 promotes renal phosphate excretion by downregulating NaPi-IIa and suppresses renal 1α-hydroxylase, lowering 1,25-(OH)2D production. It rises early in CKD as a counter-regulatory phosphate-lowering hormone.'),
('02b62ae6-4184-5d4d-ba8a-61051e3ea9b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which molecule serves as the co-receptor required for FGF23 to exert its phosphaturic effects?', '["Phosphatonins resulting in decreased functional activity","Water channel accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+ channel mediated by specific receptor activation","Ca2+ receptor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Klotho (required for FGF23 signaling)"]'::jsonb, 'Klotho (required for FGF23 signaling)', 'Klotho is a transmembrane co-receptor that allows FGF23 to bind and activate its FGF receptor in the proximal tubule. Without Klotho, FGF23 cannot exert its renal phosphaturic and vitamin-D-regulating actions.'),
('4a71d42d-9450-599c-aad3-4972255f8e3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'How is urate handled by the kidney and what transporter is involved?', '["All excreted resulting in decreased functional activity","50% reabsorbed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mostly secreted after filtration mediated by specific receptor activation","Filtered → 90% reabsorbed in PT (URAT1 transporter)","10% reabsorbed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtered → 90% reabsorbed in PT (URAT1 transporter)', 'Urate is freely filtered and about 90% is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, largely via the URAT1 transporter. Uricosuric drugs such as probenecid inhibit URAT1, increasing urate excretion.'),
('ba8d0df7-df51-55dd-9281-90e2c1ac3a88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which enzyme converts 25-OH-vitamin D to the active hormone 1,25-(OH)2D in the kidney?', '["1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubule","25-hydroxylase (liver) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","24-hydroxylase mediated by specific receptor activation","7α-hydroxylase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","11β-hydroxylase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubule', 'Renal proximal tubular mitochondria contain 1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1), which hydroxylates 25-OH-D to the active 1,25-(OH)2D. The enzyme is stimulated by PTH and low phosphate and suppressed by FGF23.'),
('c1ab4fde-129d-5998-a89f-e6774c278e18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'How is blood volume related to plasma volume and hematocrit?', '["Plasma volume + RBC volume = blood volume; BV = PV / (1 - Hct)","Blood volume is independent of plasma volume","Hematocrit equals blood volume","Body hematocrit equals venous hematocrit","Blood volume cannot be estimated from PV and Hct"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma volume + RBC volume = blood volume; BV = PV / (1 - Hct)', 'Blood volume is the sum of plasma and red cell volumes. Because the body hematocrit is about 0.91 of the venous hematocrit, blood volume can be estimated from plasma volume as BV = PV / (1 - Hct).'),
('d8872407-abaf-5aba-8976-36d00e4ca393'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which statement correctly describes the ionic composition of body fluid compartments?', '["ECF is Na+-rich and ICF is K+-rich","ECF is K+-rich","ICF is Na+-rich","ECF and ICF have identical composition","Cl- is the major ECF cation"]'::jsonb, 'ECF is Na+-rich and ICF is K+-rich', 'Sodium is the major extracellular cation (142 mEq/L) and potassium is the major intracellular cation (140 mEq/L). This gradient, maintained by the Na+/K+-ATPase, is fundamental to cell function and volume regulation.'),
('91431e93-e0c3-5856-9157-b344ac9d2b86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is effective arterial blood volume (EABV) and how is it regulated?', '["Not directly measurable; reflects the arterial blood volume ''perceived'' by baroreceptors; decreased in HF and cirrhosis despite total ECF excess","A measurable compartment equal to total blood volume accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Same as total body water mediated by specific receptor activation","Increased whenever ECF is expanded without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unaffected by cardiac output leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Not directly measurable; reflects the arterial blood volume ''perceived'' by baroreceptors; decreased in HF and cirrhosis despite total ECF excess', 'Effective arterial blood volume is the arterial blood volume sensed by the baroreceptors and is not directly measurable. It is reduced in heart failure and cirrhosis despite total body fluid excess, driving sodium and water retention.'),
('acaa42fc-91b0-5bca-b208-f53177245936'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Loop diuretics:', '["Block NKCC2 in TAL; increase Ca2+ and Mg2+ excretion; may cause hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis and ototoxicity","Block NCC in DCT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Block ENaC in collecting duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Act as osmotic diuretics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Block NKCC2 in TAL; increase Ca2+ and Mg2+ excretion; may cause hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis and ototoxicity', 'Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb. They are the most potent diuretics (up to 25% of filtered sodium), increase calcium and magnesium excretion, and can cause hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis and ototoxicity.'),
('f00892ca-6677-5684-b2c4-3e0c9ad8fd3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Thiazide diuretics:', '["Block NCC in DCT; increase Ca2+ reabsorption; may cause hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperglycemia","Block NKCC2 in TAL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Block ENaC in collecting duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibit carbonic anhydrase in PT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Act as osmotic diuretics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Block NCC in DCT; increase Ca2+ reabsorption; may cause hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperglycemia', 'Thiazides block the NCC cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule, a moderate-potency diuresis. They increase distal calcium reabsorption (useful in calcium stones) and can cause hypokalemia, hyponatremia, and hyperglycemia.'),
('a81ccf1c-2d66-5baf-8de6-31f130c1bd1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Acetazolamide:', '["CA inhibitor in PT (and other sites); blocks HCO3- reabsorption; causes metabolic acidosis and alkaline urine; weak diuretic; used for glaucoma","Inhibits NKCC2 in TAL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocks NCC in DCT mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases HCO3- reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causes metabolic alkalosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CA inhibitor in PT (and other sites); blocks HCO3- reabsorption; causes metabolic acidosis and alkaline urine; weak diuretic; used for glaucoma', 'Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule and other tissues, blocking HCO3- reabsorption and producing a weak diuresis with metabolic acidosis and alkaline urine. It is used for glaucoma, altitude sickness, and epilepsy.'),
('c087fcbb-1842-5754-b926-50269362473f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Mannitol acts as an:', '["Osmotic diuretic (freely filtered, not reabsorbed → osmotic water retention → diuresis; used for cerebral edema)","Loop diuretic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thiazide diuretic mediated by specific receptor activation","K+-sparing diuretic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osmotic diuretic (freely filtered, not reabsorbed → osmotic water retention → diuresis; used for cerebral edema)', 'Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic that is freely filtered but not reabsorbed, so it osmotically retains water in the tubule and increases urine output. It is used to reduce cerebral edema and is contraindicated in anuria.'),
('2197dcf4-c428-51e0-b4bb-e25238e816e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal autoregulation:', '["Maintains RBF and GFR constant across MAP 80-180 mmHg via myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback","Operates only above MAP 200 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maintains RBF and GFR constant at all pressures mediated by specific receptor activation","Is mediated by a single mechanism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depends entirely on neural input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintains RBF and GFR constant across MAP 80-180 mmHg via myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback', 'Autoregulation keeps renal blood flow and GFR constant when mean arterial pressure ranges from 80 to 180 mmHg. The myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback act together on the afferent arteriole.'),
('2ed7d2c4-e206-5a60-a10a-0ad5a8242827'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The myogenic response of the afferent arteriole:', '["Vascular smooth muscle contracts in response to stretch (increased transmural pressure); independent of TGF; important for RBF autoregulation","Depends on tubuloglomerular feedback accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Causes vasodilation when stretched mediated by specific receptor activation","Occurs only in efferent arterioles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is not blocked by Ca2+ channel blockers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular smooth muscle contracts in response to stretch (increased transmural pressure); independent of TGF; important for RBF autoregulation', 'The myogenic response is the intrinsic contraction of vascular smooth muscle when stretched by rising pressure, and relaxation when pressure falls. It is independent of tubuloglomerular feedback, depends on Ca2+ influx, and helps autoregulate renal blood flow.'),
('d2fb86b1-ffad-5fc3-bdf0-fc2b426d3838'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'During normal pregnancy, renal blood flow:', '["Increases by 50-80% due to vasodilation","Decreases by about 50%","Remains unchanged","Increases only in the third trimester","Falls because of progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Increases by 50-80% due to vasodilation', 'Renal blood flow rises by 50-80% in pregnancy because of afferent vasodilation and increased cardiac output. GFR increases 50%, so serum creatinine falls and physiological glycosuria may occur.'),
('29368676-b15e-5edb-b7a0-9ec5a87e72a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal plasma flow (RPF) is estimated by the clearance of:', '["PAH (para-aminohippurate)","Inulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Creatinine mediated by specific receptor activation","Glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urea leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PAH (para-aminohippurate)', 'PAH is both filtered and actively secreted by the proximal tubule, achieving 90% extraction in a single pass, so its clearance approximates renal plasma flow. RBF = RPF / (1 - Hct).'),
('78e1fb45-f6c6-5725-a749-36ba9d3115c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'GFR is determined by:', '["Kf (ultrafiltration coefficient) × (Pgc - Pbc - πgc)","Pgc alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kf alone mediated by specific receptor activation","πgc alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pbc alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kf (ultrafiltration coefficient) × (Pgc - Pbc - πgc)', 'GFR equals the ultrafiltration coefficient (Kf) multiplied by net filtration pressure (capillary pressure minus Bowman''s pressure minus oncotic pressure). All four determinants can be altered by disease.'),
('41879766-b68d-5b4b-b385-d47be2820e8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the characteristic renal biopsy finding in minimal change disease?', '["Normal light microscopy with diffuse foot process effacement on electron microscopy","Segmental glomerulosclerosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subepithelial immune deposits mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial IgA deposits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Crescent formation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal light microscopy with diffuse foot process effacement on electron microscopy', 'Minimal change disease shows normal glomeruli on light microscopy with diffuse podocyte foot process effacement on electron microscopy. It is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and responds to steroids.'),
('2abe35de-3f58-5dc7-9bfd-0073ff491d97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the pathognomonic histological finding of diabetic nephropathy?', '["Nodular glomerulosclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules); earliest change is GBM thickening","Crescentic glomerulonephritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subepithelial immune deposits mediated by specific receptor activation","Mesangial IgA deposits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Linear capillary immune staining leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nodular glomerulosclerosis (Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules); earliest change is GBM thickening', 'Diabetic nephropathy is characterized by nodular glomerulosclerosis with Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules; the earliest change is glomerular basement membrane thickening. It progresses from microalbuminuria to proteinuria and declining GFR.'),
('283f50de-f2f5-52af-81de-d366ee76830e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Contrast-induced acute kidney injury (CI-AKI):', '["Occurs after IV/intra-arterial contrast; risk increased by CKD, diabetes, volume depletion","Occurs only after oral contrast accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is not related to CKD mediated by specific receptor activation","Is prevented by nephrotoxic drugs without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Always requires dialysis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occurs after IV/intra-arterial contrast; risk increased by CKD, diabetes, volume depletion', 'CI-AKI follows intravenous or intra-arterial iodinated contrast and is most likely when pre-existing CKD, diabetes, or volume depletion is present. Prevention relies on volume expansion and minimizing contrast dose.'),
('afaf0b8e-70c3-5bf2-a399-de110230d21e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('b5cbf803-fa0a-5bf1-948b-01893e275ba6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal phosphate handling:', '["85% of filtered PO4 is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule via NaPi-IIa/IIc, downregulated by PTH and FGF23","Phosphate is reabsorbed mainly in the collecting duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PTH increases phosphate reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","FGF23 increases phosphate reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1,25-(OH)2D promotes phosphaturia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '85% of filtered PO4 is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule via NaPi-IIa/IIc, downregulated by PTH and FGF23', 'About 85% of filtered phosphate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule through NaPi-IIa/IIc cotransporters. PTH and FGF23 downregulate these transporters to cause phosphaturia, while 1,25-(OH)2D promotes phosphate absorption.'),
('bbaee134-6e4b-5c86-a50b-4989b083dad1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Tubular transport maximum (Tm):', '["Maximum rate of transport for substances with carrier-mediated transport (glucose TmG 375 mg/min; phosphate TmP); splay reflects nephron heterogeneity","Applies to freely filtered substances only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is identical in all nephrons mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Is infinite for glucose without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cannot be demonstrated for any solute leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of transport for substances with carrier-mediated transport (glucose TmG 375 mg/min; phosphate TmP); splay reflects nephron heterogeneity', 'Transport maximum is the saturation rate of a carrier-mediated transport system, e.g., TmG 375 mg/min for glucose. Splay is the gradual transition to saturation caused by differences in Tm among nephrons.'),
('638a9943-f7fb-51b5-9477-ccdeb1fa4c6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Fanconi syndrome is best described as:', '["Generalized proximal tubular dysfunction causing glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonaturia","Isolated distal tubular defect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary collecting duct disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomerular basement membrane disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated hyperoxaluria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Generalized proximal tubular dysfunction causing glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonaturia', 'Fanconi syndrome is a generalized proximal tubular transport defect producing glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis with bicarbonaturia. Causes include inherited disorders, heavy metals, and myeloma.'),
('115649c0-3237-5aa0-b3eb-adb1ee07ae1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The transport maximum for glucose (TmG) is approximately:', '["375 mg/min","125 mg/min","625 mg/min","90 mg/min","1000 mg/min"]'::jsonb, '375 mg/min', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose up to a transport maximum of about 375 mg/min. When filtered load exceeds this capacity (roughly plasma glucose >180 mg/dL with splay), glycosuria appears.'),
('0875367f-7744-5330-9ba9-676e890b5119'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The descending thin limb of the loop of Henle is:', '["Highly permeable to water and relatively impermeable to solutes","Impermeable to water accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A site of active NaCl transport mediated by specific receptor activation","The main site of aldosterone action without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urea-impermeable only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Highly permeable to water and relatively impermeable to solutes', 'The descending thin limb is highly permeable to water but relatively impermeable to solutes. Water exits osmotically, concentrating the tubular fluid toward 1200 mOsm/kg at the papillary tip.'),
('666c47a2-85d0-546c-944e-89fd0d754fc2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renin release from juxtaglomerular cells is stimulated by:', '["Low renal perfusion pressure (renal baroreceptor)","High renal perfusion pressure","Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa","Atrial natriuretic peptide","High dietary sodium intake"]'::jsonb, 'Low renal perfusion pressure (renal baroreceptor)', 'Renin is released by juxtaglomerular cells when renal perfusion pressure falls, when macula densa NaCl delivery decreases, and during β1-sympathetic stimulation. Renin is an aspartyl protease that converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I.'),
('a5e6d2c8-3bff-5acb-9007-bc722a5481d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'During tubuloglomerular feedback, high NaCl delivery to the macula densa:', '["Triggers afferent arteriolar constriction via ATP/adenosine (A1 receptors), reducing GFR","Dilates the afferent arteriole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Has no effect on GFR mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases GFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulates efferent vasodilation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Triggers afferent arteriolar constriction via ATP/adenosine (A1 receptors), reducing GFR', 'High NaCl delivery activates NKCC2 in the macula densa, raising intracellular Cl- and releasing ATP/adenosine, which constricts the afferent arteriole via A1 receptors. This reduces GFR; the reverse occurs at low delivery, and the response is blunted by nitric oxide.'),
('a3462f9a-fb26-5523-a61c-5e1fe73d9f78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which apical transporter mediates the major fraction of proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption?', '["Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3)","NKCC2","ENaC","NCC","Na+/Cl- symporter"]'::jsonb, 'Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3)', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs most sodium via the apical Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3), which also drives HCO3- reclamation. Other apical mechanisms (SGLT2, amino-acid cotransporters) contribute the rest, all energized by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase.'),
('fd958196-390d-5392-893d-79f68571b0fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule depends on:', '["Apical Na+/H+ exchange (NHE3) plus brush-border carbonic anhydrase (CA IV); HCO3- exits basolaterally via NBCe1","Passive diffusion of HCO3- accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ENaC-mediated transport mediated by specific receptor activation","NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple filtration without reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Apical Na+/H+ exchange (NHE3) plus brush-border carbonic anhydrase (CA IV); HCO3- exits basolaterally via NBCe1', 'Proximal HCO3- reabsorption (4500 mEq/day, 80-90%) requires apical NHE3 and brush-border carbonic anhydrase IV; CO2 enters the cell and is reconverted to HCO3-, which exits via the basolateral Na+/3HCO3- cotransporter (NBCe1).'),
('24e4555f-168c-5367-8a7f-a8766401c68b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Ammonia production in the proximal tubule during acidosis:', '["Increases, generating new bicarbonate; NH3 is synthesized from glutamine and diffuses into the lumen","Decreases accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Occurs only in the collecting duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Does not contribute to H+ excretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uses urea as its substrate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases, generating new bicarbonate; NH3 is synthesized from glutamine and diffuses into the lumen', 'Chronic acidosis stimulates proximal tubular glutaminase, increasing NH3 production from glutamine. NH3 diffuses into the lumen and traps H+ as NH4+, allowing H+ excretion and generating new bicarbonate within hours to days.'),
('86c62c92-4de0-5730-8ffe-e012cd82e172'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'With normal aging, renal tubular function:', '["Declines, including decreased TmG, reduced phosphate reabsorption, and decreased tubular drug clearance","Remains unchanged until age 80 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Shows increased TmG mediated by specific receptor activation","Improves drug clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is not clinically relevant leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Declines, including decreased TmG, reduced phosphate reabsorption, and decreased tubular drug clearance', 'Aging reduces tubular mass and transport, lowering TmG, phosphate reabsorption (TmP/GFR), and organic anion/cation secretion. This decreases clearance of drugs excreted by the tubules and contributes to the age-related decline in renal function.'),
('02bb4fd6-addb-5dbb-9ddf-576e1cc44566'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'In the kidney, PTH:', '["Increases Ca2+ reabsorption (TAL + DCT), decreases Pi reabsorption, and stimulates 1α-hydroxylase to produce 1,25-(OH)2D","Increases phosphate reabsorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreases Ca2+ reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibits 1α-hydroxylase without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Has no renal effects leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases Ca2+ reabsorption (TAL + DCT), decreases Pi reabsorption, and stimulates 1α-hydroxylase to produce 1,25-(OH)2D', 'PTH promotes renal calcium reabsorption in the TAL and DCT, causes phosphaturia by downregulating NaPi-IIa, and activates 1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1). These effects raise plasma calcium and lower phosphate.'),
('2c6c46aa-0d22-5eb7-9fcf-840c917a4e82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the function of semicarbazide-sensitive amine oxidase (SSAO) secreted by the kidney?', '["Metabolizes circulating catecholamines; regulates BP; depletion in CKD contributes to hypertension","Degrades renalase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates vitamin D mediated by specific receptor activation","Promotes phosphate reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reabsorbs glucose in the proximal tubule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolizes circulating catecholamines; regulates BP; depletion in CKD contributes to hypertension', 'SSAO is an amine oxidase secreted by the kidneys that metabolizes circulating catecholamines, helping regulate blood pressure. Reduced renal SSAO in CKD increases sympathetic activity and contributes to hypertension.'),
('59081574-dc0b-5a05-bf92-74ce6d999a65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal 1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) is stimulated by:', '["PTH and low phosphate","FGF23","High calcium","High phosphate","Calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'PTH and low phosphate', '1α-hydroxylase in proximal tubular mitochondria is upregulated by PTH and low phosphate and suppressed by FGF23, high calcium, and high phosphate. It converts 25-OH-D to the active 1,25-(OH)2D.'),
('a838951f-3f5a-5798-93ac-62f69fc54bf3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The kidneys filter calcium at a rate of approximately:', '["10 g/day, with 98% reabsorbed and about 200 mg excreted","1 g/day accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","50 g/day mediated by specific receptor activation","No calcium is filtered without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 g/day leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '10 g/day, with 98% reabsorbed and about 200 mg excreted', 'About 10 g of calcium is filtered daily, of which 98% is reabsorbed (PT 60-65%, TAL 20-25%, DCT 10-15%), leaving roughly 200 mg in the urine. PTH increases and loop diuretics decrease renal calcium reabsorption.'),
('77bdf7eb-bcae-5aa1-a30c-d8b82b0f1d5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The main difference between plasma and interstitial fluid is that:', '["Plasma contains much more protein (7 g/dL), generating oncotic pressure, whereas interstitial fluid has little protein","Interstitial fluid has more protein than plasma accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Their composition is identical mediated by specific receptor activation","Plasma contains more potassium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interstitial fluid contains red cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma contains much more protein (7 g/dL), generating oncotic pressure, whereas interstitial fluid has little protein', 'Plasma is protein-rich (7 g/dL), producing an oncotic pressure of about 25 mmHg that pulls water into the capillaries. Interstitial fluid contains very little protein because the capillary endothelium is only slightly permeable to proteins.'),
('70018247-dc52-57bf-a0c0-230576248b53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'According to Starling forces, fluid is:', '["Filtered at the arterial end and largely reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillary; excess (2-4 L/day) returns via lymphatics","Reabsorbed at the arterial end accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filtered equally along the entire capillary mediated by specific receptor activation","Never filtered without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Returned to circulation only by veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtered at the arterial end and largely reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillary; excess (2-4 L/day) returns via lymphatics', 'Hydrostatic pressure exceeds oncotic pressure at the arterial end, favoring filtration, while the balance reverses at the venous end, favoring reabsorption. The small net excess of about 2-4 L/day is returned by lymphatics; imbalance causes edema.'),
('bf88df66-eb33-57ff-bd8a-1a3ee706d22f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Total body water is measured using the:', '["Dilution principle (V = amount injected ÷ equilibrated concentration), with tritiated water as the tracer","Clamping technique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiographic method mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct weighing of plasma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Echocardiographic method leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilution principle (V = amount injected ÷ equilibrated concentration), with tritiated water as the tracer', 'Compartment volumes are measured by dilution: V = amount of tracer injected divided by its concentration after equilibration. Tritiated water labels TBW, inulin labels ECF, and 125I-albumin labels plasma.'),
('4d9a07e2-8770-5420-aabf-f289abed2d35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'In major burns, the primary fluid shift is:', '["Increased capillary permeability → protein and fluid loss into the interstitium → decreased plasma volume and shock","Expansion of plasma volume accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Movement of fluid into the intracellular space mediated by specific receptor activation","Pure water loss without protein shift without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduced interstitial fluid volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased capillary permeability → protein and fluid loss into the interstitium → decreased plasma volume and shock', 'Burns increase capillary permeability, allowing protein and fluid to leak into the interstitial space. The resulting fall in plasma volume can cause hypovolemic shock, and fluid sequestration in pancreatitis produces similar third-spacing.'),
('eafb323e-0349-52df-bcbd-426dcf2b828f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Plasma osmolality is maintained by the:', '["ADH-thirst feedback loop; osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus with thirst threshold 290-295 mOsm/kg and ADH threshold 280-285 mOsm/kg","Aldosterone-thirst loop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renin release alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Baroreceptors overriding osmoreceptors at <5% volume loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidneys without any hormone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADH-thirst feedback loop; osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus with thirst threshold 290-295 mOsm/kg and ADH threshold 280-285 mOsm/kg', 'Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus drive ADH release (threshold 280-285 mOsm/kg) and thirst (threshold 290-295 mOsm/kg). Even a 1% change in osmolality significantly alters ADH, while baroreceptors override osmoreceptors only after >10% volume loss.'),
('3d9545b2-2d95-5c98-8dac-7dd8cd3be960'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Antidiuretic hormone is:', '["Synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, transported to the posterior pituitary, and released in response to increased plasma osmolality","Synthesized in the anterior pituitary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Released in response to decreased osmolality mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Stored in the thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not affected by blood volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, transported to the posterior pituitary, and released in response to increased plasma osmolality', 'ADH is synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei and transported down the supraopticohypophyseal tract to the posterior pituitary for storage. It is released in response to increased plasma osmolality and decreased blood volume, and also by pain, nausea, and stress.'),
('ff74f21b-4ccb-5328-b00b-f6ff6a2fe489'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The hypertonic (3%) saline infusion test is used:', '["When the water deprivation test is equivocal, to distinguish diabetes insipidus from primary polydipsia by measuring ADH and urine osmolality","To treat all forms of hyponatremia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","As first-line treatment for diabetes insipidus mediated by specific receptor activation","To diagnose SIADH by water loading without affecting the primary tissue pathway","To replace the water deprivation test entirely leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'When the water deprivation test is equivocal, to distinguish diabetes insipidus from primary polydipsia by measuring ADH and urine osmolality', 'Infusing 3% saline while measuring plasma osmolality and ADH clarifies equivocal water deprivation results: central DI lacks an ADH response, nephrogenic DI has an ADH response without urinary concentration, and primary polydipsia responds normally. It carries a risk of volume overload.'),
('cb112df4-75d7-5c43-86ad-60ae3a273816'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'During prolonged fasting, the renal cortex contributes to glucose homeostasis by:', '["Producing glucose from lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% during prolonged fasting and generating bicarbonate in acidosis","Secreting insulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Storing glycogen mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxidizing glucose exclusively without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Producing ketone bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Producing glucose from lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% during prolonged fasting and generating bicarbonate in acidosis', 'Renal gluconeogenesis uses lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% of glucose during prolonged fasting. In acidosis, glutamine metabolism both produces glucose and generates bicarbonate for acid-base balance.'),
('f1c1f3e7-3f07-5663-a9a2-1e7aa72e9cd8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'In the kidney, the kallikrein-kinin system:', '["Produces bradykinin (a vasodilator) in the connecting tubule; bradykinin stimulates nitric oxide and natriuresis and is degraded by kininase (ACE)","Produces a vasoconstrictor peptide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not involve the kidney mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases aldosterone only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is unaffected by ACE inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produces bradykinin (a vasodilator) in the connecting tubule; bradykinin stimulates nitric oxide and natriuresis and is degraded by kininase (ACE)', 'Renal kallikrein in the connecting tubule cleaves kininogen to bradykinin, a vasodilator that stimulates nitric oxide and natriuresis. ACE (kininase) degrades bradykinin, which explains why ACE inhibitors increase bradykinin while reducing angiotensin II.'),
('27d1f613-de07-5f8b-81a1-b937fc7f6309'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'In chronic kidney disease, insulin requirements often fall because the kidneys:', '["Filter and degrade insulin (proximal tubule endocytosis + lysosomal degradation), so reduced renal clearance prolongs insulin action and increases hypoglycemia risk","Increase insulin clearance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secrete insulin mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Degrade glucagon only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Have no role in insulin metabolism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filter and degrade insulin (proximal tubule endocytosis + lysosomal degradation), so reduced renal clearance prolongs insulin action and increases hypoglycemia risk', 'The kidneys clear roughly 30-80% of circulating insulin by filtration and proximal tubular degradation. With CKD, insulin clearance falls and insulin action is prolonged, so basal doses usually must be reduced when GFR is low.'),
('6a2f0656-bca6-5997-a953-25e3ce09ebbf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Afferent arteriolar resistance (the main regulator of RBF and Pgc) is increased by:', '["Sympathetic (α1) activation, angiotensin II, adenosine (A1), and endothelin; TxA2 reduces RBF","Nitric oxide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prostaglandins (PGE2) mediated by specific receptor activation","Atrial natriuretic peptide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopamine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic (α1) activation, angiotensin II, adenosine (A1), and endothelin; TxA2 reduces RBF', 'Afferent arteriolar constriction by sympathetic α1 activity, angiotensin II, adenosine A1 receptors, and endothelin reduces renal blood flow and glomerular pressure. Nitric oxide, PGE2, ANP, and dopamine act as dilators opposing this constriction.'),
('9042cda9-6d80-5cd3-b439-6b92e5912bd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'As a GFR marker, creatinine:', '["Is freely filtered and slightly secreted (10-15%), so creatinine clearance overestimates GFR; levels are affected by muscle mass, age, gender, and diet","Is exclusively secreted accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is reabsorbed in the collecting duct mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Is unaffected by muscle mass without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Underestimates true GFR leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Is freely filtered and slightly secreted (10-15%), so creatinine clearance overestimates GFR; levels are affected by muscle mass, age, gender, and diet', 'Creatinine is produced from muscle creatine, is freely filtered, and is also secreted by 10-15%, so its clearance modestly overestimates GFR. Serum creatinine depends on muscle mass, age, gender, and diet.'),
('b4bd88f9-0bc0-5059-beed-055bf519efbb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The ''splay'' in a glucose titration curve results from:', '["Nephron heterogeneity—different nephrons have different transport maxima, producing a gradual rather than abrupt transition to glycosuria","Equal Tm in all nephrons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absence of transport carriers mediated by specific receptor activation","A single abrupt transport maximum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Artifacts present only in disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nephron heterogeneity—different nephrons have different transport maxima, producing a gradual rather than abrupt transition to glycosuria', 'Splay is the gradual transition between complete reabsorption and glycosuria. It occurs because individual nephrons have different transport maxima, so some reach saturation before others as filtered load rises.'),
('b6ef7201-c572-5a17-9065-88dd458192f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Glomerulotubular balance refers to:', '["The proximal tubule reabsorbing a constant 60-70% of the filtered load despite changes in GFR, partly due to changes in peritubular oncotic pressure","Fractional reabsorption varying directly with GFR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Balance between afferent and efferent tone mediated by specific receptor activation","Reabsorption proportional to aldosterone levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fixed absolute amount of reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal tubule reabsorbing a constant 60-70% of the filtered load despite changes in GFR, partly due to changes in peritubular oncotic pressure', 'Glomerulotubular balance keeps proximal fractional reabsorption near 60-70% as GFR changes. Higher GFR increases filtration fraction and peritubular oncotic pressure, which drives more reabsorption, and increased flow also enhances NHE3 activity.'),
('d7546fa5-6068-5cde-aa8a-6606b07c2d85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The amount of glomerular filtrate formed is determined mainly by:', '["Net filtration pressure and the permeability/surface area (Kf) of the filtration barrier","Tubular reabsorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blood pressure only mediated by specific receptor activation","Renal blood flow only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plasma protein concentration alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Net filtration pressure and the permeability/surface area (Kf) of the filtration barrier', 'GFR depends on net filtration pressure (capillary minus Bowman''s minus oncotic pressure) and on Kf, the product of capillary permeability and surface area. Changes in any of these alter the rate of filtrate formation.'),
('5a700c61-b587-542a-8c44-60a694ffdf45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Within the autoregulatory range, GFR is kept constant chiefly by:', '["The myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback","Passive filtration changes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hormonal control only mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural control only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Random fluctuation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback', 'Between MAP 80 and 180 mmHg, GFR is stabilized by the afferent arteriolar myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback. Outside this range, GFR becomes pressure-dependent.'),
('e51a5b63-3476-58d7-a540-d2a6400e3769'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Which enzyme is secreted by juxtaglomerular cells and initiates the RAAS cascade?', '["Renin","Aldosterone","Angiotensinogen","Erythropoietin","ADH"]'::jsonb, 'Renin', 'Juxtaglomerular cells secrete renin, an aspartyl protease that converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. Angiotensin-converting enzyme then generates angiotensin II, the principal effector of the RAAS.'),
('46007d38-5354-5772-a52f-045862a54de2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What percentage of filtered sodium is reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle?', '["75% of filtered Na+","100% of filtered Na+","10% of filtered Na+","50% of filtered Na+","25% of filtered Na+ and water"]'::jsonb, '25% of filtered Na+ and water', 'The thick ascending limb reabsorbs about 25% of filtered sodium and water through active NKCC2 transport. It is water-impermeable, so the reabsorbed NaCl helps build the medullary osmotic gradient.'),
('aba64737-d738-59a8-af6a-b2ee3cf706a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Thick ascending limb transport is inhibited by:', '["Thiazides resulting in decreased functional activity","Spironolactone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amiloride mediated by specific receptor activation","Acetazolamide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide)"]'::jsonb, 'Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide)', 'Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, blocking sodium, potassium, and chloride reabsorption. This makes them the most potent diuretics and also increases calcium and magnesium excretion.'),
('33480856-e825-556f-addf-47dd95155c0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The DCT reabsorbs Na+ and Cl- via:', '["NHE3, inhibited by acetazolamide","Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC), inhibited by thiazides","NKCC2, inhibited by loop diuretics","Passive diffusion","ENaC, inhibited by amiloride"]'::jsonb, 'Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC), inhibited by thiazides', 'The distal convoluted tubule reabsorbs sodium and chloride through the apical NCC cotransporter, which is the target of thiazide diuretics. This segment reabsorbs about 5-8% of filtered sodium.'),
('75451a98-e656-5653-9cc6-44a1468d0ca6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Principal cells in the collecting duct:', '["Reabsorb K+ only","Reabsorb Na+ and water, secrete K+","Reabsorb Ca2+ only","Secrete H+","Secrete Na+"]'::jsonb, 'Reabsorb Na+ and water, secrete K+', 'Principal cells reabsorb sodium through ENaC and water through aquaporins (ADH-dependent), while secreting potassium via ROMK channels. Aldosterone and ADH regulate these functions.'),
('271fcad1-f455-59d8-b3cb-8b77b07327d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Intercalated cells in the collecting duct:', '["Secrete H+ (type A) or HCO3- (type B)","Reabsorb Na+ accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reabsorb water mediated by specific receptor activation","Secrete K+ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reabsorb Cl- leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secrete H+ (type A) or HCO3- (type B)', 'Type A intercalated cells secrete H+ and generate bicarbonate, while type B intercalated cells secrete HCO3- during alkalosis. These cells mediate the fine regulation of acid-base balance.'),
('b6d7be56-481e-510e-a546-28344472a70d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The normal filtered load of glucose (GFR 125 mL/min, plasma glucose 100 mg/dL) is approximately:', '["200 mg/min","50 mg/min","500 mg/min","125 mg/min","375 mg/min"]'::jsonb, '125 mg/min', 'Filtered load equals GFR multiplied by plasma concentration: 125 mL/min × 1 mg/mL = 125 mg/min. This is well below the transport maximum (TmG 375 mg/min), so normally all glucose is reabsorbed.'),
('f7479e73-ade8-5b14-beba-656a6ea81b84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'PAH clearance is used to measure:', '["GFR","Free water clearance","Tubular reabsorption","Tubular secretion rate","Renal plasma flow (RPF)"]'::jsonb, 'Renal plasma flow (RPF)', 'PAH is filtered and avidly secreted so that 90% is extracted in one pass; its clearance therefore approximates renal plasma flow. RBF is then calculated as RPF/(1 - Hct).'),
('9b009079-a55b-5f29-94f3-c9edb3a15c51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the approximate total body water as a percentage of body weight in a healthy adult male?', '["30%","40%","60%","70%","80%"]'::jsonb, '60%', 'Total body water is approximately 60% of body weight in adult males and about 50-55% in females. The difference reflects sex differences in body fat, which contains little water.'),
('87d94253-5d41-559e-8d92-91e849d0b077'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Intracellular fluid constitutes what fraction of total body water?', '["2/3 (approximately 40% of body weight)","3/4 (45% of body weight)","1/3 (20% of body weight)","1/2 (30% of body weight)","1/4 (15% of body weight)"]'::jsonb, '2/3 (approximately 40% of body weight)', 'Intracellular fluid is about two-thirds of total body water, roughly 40% of body weight. The remaining one-third of TBW is extracellular fluid.'),
('4f8271f5-1004-5a52-a470-ccc2294ed8d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Edema can result from:', '["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, decreased plasma oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, or lymphatic obstruction","Only increased oncotic pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only reduced capillary pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Only decreased permeability without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only lymph overproduction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, decreased plasma oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, or lymphatic obstruction', 'Edema develops when capillary filtration exceeds reabsorption and lymphatic drainage. The four mechanisms are raised capillary hydrostatic pressure, reduced plasma oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, and lymphatic obstruction.'),
('49812343-b676-5406-a6d5-63188e8684ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The efferent arteriole supplies blood to:', '["Peritubular capillaries and vasa recta","Interlobular artery","Arcuate artery","Glomerular capillaries","Afferent arteriole"]'::jsonb, 'Peritubular capillaries and vasa recta', 'The efferent arteriole drains the glomerulus into the peritubular capillary network in the cortex and, for juxtamedullary nephrons, into the vasa recta of the medulla. This arrangement supports tubular reabsorption and countercurrent exchange.'),
('47831157-4469-5faa-b3ad-b726dfa45729'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal blood flow (RBF) is autoregulated between:', '["60-120 mmHg resulting in decreased functional activity","40-80 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","50-150 mmHg mediated by specific receptor activation","180-250 mmHg without affecting the primary tissue pathway","80-180 mmHg mean arterial pressure"]'::jsonb, '80-180 mmHg mean arterial pressure', 'Renal blood flow is kept constant across a mean arterial pressure range of 80-180 mmHg by the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback. Below or above this range, flow becomes pressure-dependent.'),
('1a605b83-ca6e-5d63-9983-51a468a23ee6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Macula densa cells sense changes in:', '["K+ concentration resulting in decreased functional activity","Blood pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NaCl concentration in the distal tubule fluid","Blood volume without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plasma osmolarity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NaCl concentration in the distal tubule fluid', 'Macula densa cells monitor NaCl delivery in the distal tubular fluid. This signal adjusts afferent arteriolar resistance (tubuloglomerular feedback) and influences renin release.'),
('5ae050f7-625e-50d9-b837-3d33990322ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Angiotensin II raises GFR in renal hypoperfusion by:', '["Constricting the efferent arteriole, which increases filtration fraction and maintains GFR, while also stimulating Na+ reabsorption","Dilating the efferent arteriole accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reducing Na+ reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibiting aldosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Constricting the afferent arteriole only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constricting the efferent arteriole, which increases filtration fraction and maintains GFR, while also stimulating Na+ reabsorption', 'By constricting the efferent arteriole, angiotensin II raises glomerular capillary pressure and filtration fraction, helping preserve GFR when perfusion is low. It simultaneously stimulates aldosterone and proximal Na+ reabsorption to defend blood volume.'),
('b61319a9-101c-58d5-92f1-13d432a25f43'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Hypertension with hypokalemia suggests excess aldosterone action on:', '["Collecting duct (principal cells)","DCT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Loop of Henle mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximal tubule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glomerulus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Collecting duct (principal cells)', 'Aldosterone excess acts on collecting duct principal cells, increasing ENaC-mediated Na+ reabsorption (causing hypertension) and ROMK-mediated K+ secretion (causing hypokalemia). Hyperaldosteronism classically presents with hypertension and hypokalemia.'),
('d439c1af-d024-50b5-9963-c177c1b6d368'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Vasopressin (ADH) increases water reabsorption by acting on:', '["Collecting duct (V2 receptors → AQP2 insertion)","V1 receptors in the proximal tubule","NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb","NCC in the distal tubule","SGLT2 in the proximal tubule"]'::jsonb, 'Collecting duct (V2 receptors → AQP2 insertion)', 'ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, triggering insertion of aquaporin-2 channels into the apical membrane. Water is then reabsorbed osmotically along the medullary gradient, concentrating the urine.'),
('329f9409-8a91-569b-a9fe-a903a2880991'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Clearance (Cx) of a substance is calculated as:', '["Ux × V / Px","Px / (Ux × V)","Px × V / Ux","Ux × Px / V","V × Px / Ux"]'::jsonb, 'Ux × V / Px', 'Clearance equals the urine concentration of the substance times the urine flow rate, divided by its plasma concentration. It represents the volume of plasma completely cleared of the substance per unit time.'),
('868a4e40-86ed-5d9c-8162-5b28a145b0dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The clearance of inulin measures:', '["GFR","Renal plasma flow","Tubular secretion","Free water clearance","Renal blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'GFR', 'Inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals the glomerular filtration rate. It is the gold-standard method for measuring GFR.'),
('fb292655-26b6-5404-bd2c-9a1662cb21fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Creatinine clearance slightly overestimates true GFR because:', '["Creatinine is freely filtered and also secreted (10-15%) by the proximal tubule","Creatinine is protein-bound accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Creatinine is reabsorbed mediated by specific receptor activation","Creatinine is metabolized in the kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Creatinine is not filtered leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Creatinine is freely filtered and also secreted (10-15%) by the proximal tubule', 'Creatinine is filtered and additionally secreted by the proximal tubule (10-15%), so the amount appearing in urine exceeds the filtered load. This tubular secretion makes creatinine clearance overestimate true GFR.'),
('a0730ec0-c316-5b35-81c3-46a2c95af24b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The splay in the glucose titration curve is explained by:', '["Nephrons having different transport maxima (nephron heterogeneity)","All nephrons being identical accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tm being the same in all nephrons mediated by specific receptor activation","SGLT having infinite capacity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glucose not being reabsorbed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrons having different transport maxima (nephron heterogeneity)', 'Splay reflects the fact that individual nephrons have different transport maxima, so glycosuria begins gradually as some nephrons saturate before others. It explains why glucose appears in urine at a plasma level below the theoretical TmG.'),
('2924bbe2-f008-53f0-bc09-ee883bf86ba9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'PTH promotes renal phosphate excretion by:', '["Decreasing NaPi-2a cotransporter expression in the proximal tubule","Increasing NaPi-2a expression","Inhibiting FGF23","Increasing phosphate reabsorption in the collecting duct","Stimulating 24-hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Decreasing NaPi-2a cotransporter expression in the proximal tubule', 'PTH binds receptors in the proximal tubule and causes internalization and degradation of apical NaPi-2a cotransporters, reducing phosphate reabsorption and increasing urinary phosphate excretion.'),
('0e8b6ba4-69d2-50d9-903b-46d5d3670eb6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal handling of most drugs involves:', '["Glomerular filtration, tubular reabsorption, tubular secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption","Filtration only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretion only mediated by specific receptor activation","Reabsorption only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Passive diffusion only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular filtration, tubular reabsorption, tubular secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption', 'Drugs are cleared by the kidneys through glomerular filtration, tubular reabsorption, tubular secretion (organic anion/cation transporters), and post-secretory reabsorption. This integrated handling determines net renal excretion.'),
('0bc96a35-44d7-5502-910e-a75a9f379854'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Erythropoietin (EPO) is produced by:', '["Macula densa resulting in decreased functional activity","Peritubular fibroblasts in the renal cortex","Mesangial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Podocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","JG cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peritubular fibroblasts in the renal cortex', 'Erythropoietin is synthesized by peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the renal cortex in response to hypoxia. Reduced production in chronic kidney disease causes anemia.'),
('813298de-73ff-5084-b34e-e02f4993d354'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', '1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is activated in the kidney by:', '["Vitamin D receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","24-hydroxylase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","25-hydroxylase in liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Parathyroid hormone alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubule cells"]'::jsonb, '1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubule cells', 'Renal 1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubular mitochondria converts 25-OH-vitamin D to the active hormone 1,25-(OH)2D. The enzyme is regulated by PTH, phosphate, calcium, and FGF23.'),
('9187925a-7b03-5ac5-82b4-f67c4d858bdf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The bicarbonate buffer system in the kidney involves:', '["Reclamation of filtered HCO3- and generation of new HCO3-","Bicarbonate is not handled by the kidney","Both H+ and HCO3- are excreted","Excretion of HCO3- only","Reabsorption of H+ only"]'::jsonb, 'Reclamation of filtered HCO3- and generation of new HCO3-', 'The kidney conserves bicarbonate by reclaiming the filtered HCO3- and regenerates new HCO3- through H+ excretion as titratable acid and ammonium. These processes replace bicarbonate consumed in buffering fixed acids.'),
('34bca4f9-a3ea-5a4b-a90e-1767e0641f2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Ammoniagenesis in the kidney:', '["Produces NH3 from glutamine in the proximal tubule, aiding H+ excretion and generating new bicarbonate","Produces urea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is inhibited by acidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Occurs mainly in the collecting duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Does not affect acid excretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produces NH3 from glutamine in the proximal tubule, aiding H+ excretion and generating new bicarbonate', 'The proximal tubule metabolizes glutamine to NH3 and α-ketoglutarate; NH3 diffuses into the lumen and traps H+ as NH4+, while α-ketoglutarate yields new bicarbonate. Production rises within hours to days in acidosis.'),
('fd3ee8dd-d6e1-5208-9e8f-09f000ac3043'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Titratable acidity of the urine is largely due to H+ bound to:', '["Filtered phosphate (H2PO4-) and creatinine","Free H+ ions only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bicarbonate mediated by specific receptor activation","Carbonic acid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ammonium only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtered phosphate (H2PO4-) and creatinine', 'H+ secreted into the tubular lumen is buffered by filtered phosphate (HPO42- → H2PO4-) and creatinine. This buffered acid is called titratable acid and is one component of net acid excretion.'),
('8b4fb58f-54c4-5faa-8c02-7a9980f1f740'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The kidney defends acid-base balance by:', '["Reclaiming filtered HCO3-, excreting H+, and generating new HCO3-","Secreting HCO3- only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Reabsorbing H+ only mediated by specific receptor activation","Excreting HCO3- only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Playing no role in acid-base balance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Reclaiming filtered HCO3-, excreting H+, and generating new HCO3-', 'The kidney maintains acid-base balance through three coordinated processes: reclaiming filtered bicarbonate, excreting H+ as titratable acid and ammonium, and generating new bicarbonate. All three are increased in acidosis.'),
('febc9527-38e7-5fbf-81f9-2c521ba719b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal tubular acidosis (RTA) type 1 (distal) is due to:', '["Hyperkalemia","Defective ammonia production","Defective H+ secretion in the collecting duct","Aldosterone deficiency","Defective HCO3- reabsorption in the proximal tubule"]'::jsonb, 'Defective H+ secretion in the collecting duct', 'Distal (type 1) RTA results from impaired H+ secretion by type A intercalated cells in the collecting duct. It causes a non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia, nephrocalcinosis, and an inappropriately alkaline urine.'),
('913aa185-e9ca-50c0-935e-76927d1468cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'RTA type 2 (proximal) is due to:', '["Impaired NH3 production resulting in decreased functional activity","Defective HCO3- reabsorption in the proximal tubule","Defective H+ secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyperkalemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aldosterone deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defective HCO3- reabsorption in the proximal tubule', 'Proximal (type 2) RTA results from defective bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. It produces a non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with bicarbonaturia and can occur in Fanconi syndrome.'),
('253677e7-4302-5c7a-bce3-07e9840ca96a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Obligatory urine volume is:', '["1 L/day resulting in decreased functional activity","400-500 mL/day (minimum volume to excrete solutes at maximum concentration)","3 L/day mediated by specific receptor activation","100 mL/day without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 L/day leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '400-500 mL/day (minimum volume to excrete solutes at maximum concentration)', 'Obligatory urine volume is the 400-500 mL/day minimum needed to excrete the daily solute load at maximum urine concentration. A lower volume cannot be achieved without solute accumulation.'),
('f90aac52-e8ff-581c-80de-83f626b54b4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Central diabetes insipidus results from:', '["Deficient ADH secretion from the posterior pituitary","ADH resistance in the kidney accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excessive water intake mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary polydipsia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aldosterone excess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Deficient ADH secretion from the posterior pituitary', 'Central diabetes insipidus is caused by deficient ADH production or release from the posterior pituitary. It presents with polyuria, polydipsia, and dilute urine, and responds to exogenous desmopressin.'),
('0acc3627-e6a8-5f79-9b0f-276ae13db962'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Central diabetes insipidus is distinguished from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus by:', '["Response to exogenous vasopressin: urine osmolality rises in central DI but not in nephrogenic DI","Serum sodium level accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Urine volume alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Presence of polydipsia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Plasma osmolality alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Response to exogenous vasopressin: urine osmolality rises in central DI but not in nephrogenic DI', 'In central DI the kidney can respond to hormone, so exogenous vasopressin concentrates the urine. In nephrogenic DI the kidney is resistant to ADH, so urine osmolality fails to rise despite a normal or increased ADH level.'),
('a56a29f4-1c74-5ab4-940e-71d85c3afae8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the key feature of prerenal acute kidney injury (AKI)?', '["Show active sediment resulting in decreased functional activity","Nephrons are structurally normal but hypoperfused","Cannot concentrate urine mediated by specific receptor activation","Nephrons are damaged without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Have elevated FENa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrons are structurally normal but hypoperfused', 'In prerenal AKI the nephrons are structurally intact but underperfused due to renal hypoperfusion. It is reversible with restoration of perfusion and is associated with a low fractional excretion of sodium (FENa <1%).'),
('79cd729b-697a-5c07-8bb4-97f98a56cf77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Acute tubular necrosis (ATN) shows:', '["RBC casts resulting in decreased functional activity","WBC casts accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FENa >2%, muddy brown granular casts","Normal FENa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","FENa <1%, hyaline casts leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'FENa >2%, muddy brown granular casts', 'Acute tubular necrosis, the most common intrinsic cause of AKI, shows FENa >2% and muddy brown granular casts in the urine sediment. This contrasts with prerenal AKI, which has FENa <1% and hyaline casts.'),
('8e003c3a-0288-590d-bd8c-821a122e8a53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the pathophysiology of hepatorenal syndrome?', '["Glomerulonephritis resulting in decreased functional activity","Functional renal failure in liver disease with severe renal vasoconstriction","Papillary necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Prerenal azotemia responsive to fluids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ATN in cirrhosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Functional renal failure in liver disease with severe renal vasoconstriction', 'Hepatorenal syndrome is a functional, largely reversible renal failure in advanced liver disease. Splanchnic vasodilation and the resulting neurohormonal activation cause intense renal vasoconstriction with low urine sodium and a bland sediment.'),
('a0228221-6d9e-5edf-a347-4faad79d8960'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Loop diuretics act on:', '["DCT (NCC)","Thick ascending limb (NKCC2)","Collecting duct (ENaC)","Glomerulus","Proximal tubule (NHE3)"]'::jsonb, 'Thick ascending limb (NKCC2)', 'Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. This makes them the most potent class of diuretics and also increases calcium and magnesium excretion.'),
('7fff956e-29fb-5867-bfdb-feddfdbb94ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Amiloride acts on:', '["NKCC2 in TAL resulting in decreased functional activity","NCC in DCT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ENaC in the collecting duct (principal cells)","CA in PT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Na+/K+ ATPase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ENaC in the collecting duct (principal cells)', 'Amiloride directly blocks the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) on the apical membrane of collecting duct principal cells. It is a potassium-sparing diuretic.'),
('4e948a8b-2e77-5ee9-af6a-046d82ff7a48'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Spironolactone acts as:', '["An ENaC blocker resulting in decreased functional activity","An osmotic diuretic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A CA inhibitor mediated by specific receptor activation","A NKCC2 blocker without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An aldosterone receptor (MR) antagonist in the collecting duct"]'::jsonb, 'An aldosterone receptor (MR) antagonist in the collecting duct', 'Spironolactone competitively blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor, reducing ENaC and ROMK activity in collecting duct principal cells. It is a potassium-sparing diuretic used in hyperaldosteronism and heart failure.'),
('698ab342-1fee-5ea0-80f8-f0dec8814a0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Acetazolamide inhibits:', '["NCC resulting in decreased functional activity","Carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule","Na+/K+ ATPase mediated by specific receptor activation","NKCC2 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ENaC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule', 'Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule, blocking HCO3- reabsorption. It causes an alkaline urine with metabolic acidosis and is a weak diuretic.'),
('a692d730-f71f-5409-aa47-8b7e8a6c21e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Osmotic diuretics (e.g., mannitol) act by:', '["Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase resulting in decreased functional activity","Increasing tubular fluid osmolarity, reducing water reabsorption","Antagonizing ADH mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking aldosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking ion transporters leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing tubular fluid osmolarity, reducing water reabsorption', 'Mannitol is filtered but not reabsorbed, increasing the osmolarity of tubular fluid. Water is retained osmotically in the lumen, producing a diuresis without major electrolyte loss.'),
('80fbcde2-313c-5083-be72-6fda9fcc22c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the clinical significance of a urine pH below 5.5 in a patient with metabolic acidosis?', '["Indicates proximal renal tubular acidosis","Suggests impaired renal acid excretion (distal RTA)","Indicates appropriate renal H+ excretion","Suggests alkalosis","Indicates hyperaldosteronism"]'::jsonb, 'Indicates appropriate renal H+ excretion', 'An acid urine (pH <5.5) during metabolic acidosis shows that the distal nephron is actively secreting H+, which is the normal renal response (e.g., in diarrhea). In distal RTA, by contrast, the urine is inappropriately alkaline.'),
('c517b517-c43b-52d9-82c0-bb46d23742d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Calcium oxalate stones are associated with:', '["Alkaline urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperuricosuria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Magnesium deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia","Cystinuria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia', 'Calcium oxalate stones form when urine calcium or oxalate is high or citrate (a stone inhibitor) is low. Hyperuricosuria can also promote calcium oxalate stones by heterogeneous nucleation.'),
('04f3edee-d6e8-506a-925d-160c4ac9f26e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Glucagon is degraded by:', '["The pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","The kidney (filtration and proximal tubule degradation)","Red blood cells mediated by specific receptor activation","The liver only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lungs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The kidney (filtration and proximal tubule degradation)', 'Small peptide hormones such as glucagon are cleared largely by the kidneys through glomerular filtration and proximal tubular catabolism. This is why peptide hormone clearance falls in advanced CKD.'),
('ab4787dd-a32d-534f-bde7-26e210a0cfee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The kidney''s role in glucose homeostasis includes:', '["Gluconeogenesis (during fasting) and glucose filtration/reabsorption","Insulin secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Glycogen storage mediated by specific receptor activation","Only glucose reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only glucose filtration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gluconeogenesis (during fasting) and glucose filtration/reabsorption', 'The kidney contributes to glucose balance by reabsorbing filtered glucose via SGLT2 and by performing gluconeogenesis during fasting. It does not secrete insulin or store glycogen.'),
('dc23853c-c360-5339-b806-23abcda02352'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'How many nephrons does each human kidney contain approximately?', '["1 million per kidney","100,000 per kidney","100 per kidney","500,000 per kidney","10 million per kidney"]'::jsonb, '1 million per kidney', 'Each human kidney contains approximately one million nephrons. Nephron number declines with age and is reduced in conditions such as hypertension and chronic kidney disease.'),
('214e854b-cf5c-5e94-84bf-bf144522fb6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Renal plasma flow (RPF) is normally:', '["125 mL/min","625 mL/min","300 mL/min","1000 mL/min","50 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '625 mL/min', 'Renal plasma flow is about 625 mL/min in a young adult (renal blood flow 1200 mL/min adjusted for hematocrit). PAH clearance approximates RPF.'),
('f694c3a5-bab4-5df7-8ac5-6ad042bc93e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Beta-2 microglobulin is filtered and:', '["Secreted by the distal tubule","Produced by the kidney","Not handled by the kidney","Reabsorbed and catabolized by proximal tubule cells","Excreted unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'Reabsorbed and catabolized by proximal tubule cells', 'Beta-2 microglobulin, a low-molecular-weight protein, is freely filtered and then reabsorbed and catabolized by proximal tubular cells. In proximal tubular damage or with high production (e.g., myeloma), urinary levels rise.'),
('9f50fc94-7294-5a44-a1a8-256f72fd17d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The renal threshold for glucose (the plasma glucose level at which glycosuria begins) is approximately:', '["100 mg/dL resulting in decreased functional activity","180 mg/dL (10 mmol/L) plasma glucose","250 mg/dL mediated by specific receptor activation","50 mg/dL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","400 mg/dL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '180 mg/dL (10 mmol/L) plasma glucose', 'When plasma glucose exceeds about 180 mg/dL, the filtered load surpasses the reabsorptive capacity (TmG 375 mg/min), and glucose spills into the urine. The resulting osmotic diuresis produces polyuria in uncontrolled diabetes.'),
('681edf67-1e01-5fb9-87f9-1e9e9484e4d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the renal handling of water?', '["Reabsorption only in PT resulting in decreased functional activity","Filtration, passive reabsorption in PT, and ADH-dependent reabsorption in CD","Filtration only mediated by specific receptor activation","Secretion only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reabsorption only in the collecting duct leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtration, passive reabsorption in PT, and ADH-dependent reabsorption in CD', 'Water is freely filtered at the glomerulus, reabsorbed obligatorily and isosmotically in the proximal tubule, and reabsorbed facultatively in the collecting duct under ADH control. This arrangement allows both large obligatory reabsorption and precise regulation of excretion.'),
('52b734a6-e931-57e1-9b64-a148dfc7454e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'FGF23 is a hormone that:', '["Increases phosphate excretion and inhibits vitamin D activation","Stimulates PTH secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases phosphate reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Decreases phosphate excretion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increases Ca2+ reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increases phosphate excretion and inhibits vitamin D activation', 'FGF23 promotes renal phosphate excretion by downregulating NaPi-IIa and suppresses renal 1α-hydroxylase, reducing 1,25-(OH)2D production. It is a key phosphaturic hormone that rises early in CKD.'),
('d9e5ea14-2122-52da-97df-200aeb1ec039'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Bartter syndrome is caused by:', '["Defective ENaC (CD) resulting in decreased functional activity","Defective NHE3 (PT) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Defective NKCC2 (TAL) → salt wasting, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis","Defective NCC (DCT) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Defective AQP2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defective NKCC2 (TAL) → salt wasting, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis', 'Bartter syndrome results from mutations in NKCC2 or related transporters of the thick ascending limb. It produces salt wasting with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, hypercalciuria, and normal or low blood pressure.'),
('1024bb2f-3748-59db-be6c-c4860c398391'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Gitelman syndrome is caused by:', '["Defective NKCC2 (TAL) resulting in decreased functional activity","Defective ENaC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Defective NHE3 mediated by specific receptor activation","Defective NCC (DCT) → hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypocalciuria","Defective AQP2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Defective NCC (DCT) → hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypocalciuria', 'Gitelman syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NCC cotransporter of the distal convoluted tubule. It mimics thiazide treatment, producing hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypocalciuria.'),
('acd031d6-501f-5257-98ce-534b7be61fdb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Liddle syndrome is caused by:', '["Gain-of-function mutation in ENaC → hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis","Loss of ENaC function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","MR mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","NCC mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NKCC2 mutation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Gain-of-function mutation in ENaC → hypertension, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis', 'Liddle syndrome is an autosomal dominant gain-of-function of ENaC, causing excessive sodium reabsorption and potassium loss. It presents with hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis but low aldosterone.'),
('efeaf590-1448-58b2-9c09-d833af5dba7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 results from:', '["Loss-of-function mutation in ENaC or MR → salt wasting, hyperkalemia, acidosis","Gain of ENaC function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","NCC mutation mediated by specific receptor activation","NKCC2 mutation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CA deficiency leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss-of-function mutation in ENaC or MR → salt wasting, hyperkalemia, acidosis', 'Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 is caused by loss-of-function mutations of ENaC or the mineralocorticoid receptor. It produces renal salt wasting with hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis despite high aldosterone levels.'),
('06c71daf-726e-5540-9e9a-7f1d1d0a5b3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (NDI) can be caused by:', '["SIADH resulting in decreased functional activity","X-linked V2 receptor mutation or aquaporin-2 mutation (AD)","ADH deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Excessive water intake without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Primary polydipsia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked V2 receptor mutation or aquaporin-2 mutation (AD)', 'Congenital nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is caused by X-linked V2 receptor mutations or autosomal aquaporin-2 mutations, making the collecting duct unresponsive to ADH. Acquired causes include lithium and hypercalcemia.'),
('316b7331-d177-5f80-9a44-ecf0467f6f59'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Treatment of SIADH (euvolemic hyponatremia) includes:', '["Fluid restriction, vaptans (V2 receptor antagonists), and salt tablets","Water loading accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ADH administration mediated by specific receptor activation","Demeclocycline for all patients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lithium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fluid restriction, vaptans (V2 receptor antagonists), and salt tablets', 'SIADH is treated by fluid restriction as first-line therapy, with hypertonic saline for symptomatic cases and vaptans (V2 antagonists) or salt tablets for resistant hyponatremia. Rapid overcorrection must be avoided to prevent osmotic demyelination.'),
('5e3af9b1-ea50-55e2-b71a-72b09b843491'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis) is caused by:', '["Hypocalcemia resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypernatremia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyponatremia itself mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypokalemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Too rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia (>8-12 mEq/L/day)"]'::jsonb, 'Too rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia (>8-12 mEq/L/day)', 'Central pontine myelinolysis follows overly rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia, generally >8-12 mEq/L per day. It damages the pons and other areas and can cause quadriparesis and altered mental status.'),
('cce0aba3-21b0-5202-8961-f5879fade6f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Normal saline (0.9% NaCl) has an osmolarity of:', '["400 mOsm/L","308 mOsm/L","250 mOsm/L","154 mOsm/L","616 mOsm/L"]'::jsonb, '308 mOsm/L', '0.9% saline contains 154 mmol/L of NaCl, giving an osmolarity of about 308 mOsm/L (two osmotically active particles per NaCl). It is therefore approximately isotonic with plasma.'),
('53314bd8-f069-59ac-a311-3fee189aba6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The fenestrations in glomerular capillary endothelium:', '["Are 70-100 nm pores that allow fluid and small solutes to pass while retaining cells and large proteins","Are 5 nm pores accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Are tight junctions mediated by specific receptor activation","Do not permit filtration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Are larger than 1 μm leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Are 70-100 nm pores that allow fluid and small solutes to pass while retaining cells and large proteins', 'Glomerular endothelial fenestrations are 70-100 nm pores that permit water and small solutes to filter while retaining blood cells. The overall barrier combines these fenestrations with the GBM and podocyte slit diaphragm.'),
('70813c17-873a-571a-9676-34903225f1f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The slit diaphragm between podocyte foot processes contains:', '["Nephrin, podocin, and CD2AP as key structural proteins","Only actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Only laminin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only tubulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrin, podocin, and CD2AP as key structural proteins', 'The podocyte slit diaphragm is composed of nephrin, podocin, and CD2AP. Mutations in nephrin cause congenital nephrotic syndrome, and these proteins are reduced in acquired podocyte injury.'),
('ab3ff7ae-df43-5efb-b2bf-f60fa06a1798'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The GBM is composed mainly of:', '["Type IV collagen (α3, α4, α5 chains), laminin, nidogen, and heparan sulfate","Type I collagen accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Elastin mediated by specific receptor activation","Fibronectin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Type III collagen leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV collagen (α3, α4, α5 chains), laminin, nidogen, and heparan sulfate', 'The glomerular basement membrane consists chiefly of type IV collagen (α3-α5 chains), laminin, nidogen, and heparan sulfate proteoglycans. Its negative charge and fine structure provide size and charge selectivity.'),
('f39550ef-422d-52d5-aeeb-eecbaef210f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'How much fluid is filtered by the kidneys per day?', '["50 L/day","1 L/day","20 L/day","180 L/day (125 mL/min)","500 L/day"]'::jsonb, '180 L/day (125 mL/min)', 'The glomeruli filter about 180 L/day, or 125 mL/min, in adults. Almost all of this is reabsorbed, since normal urine output is only 1-2 L/day.'),
('c4aba515-079e-5f5e-8d03-edd9e3962a6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The renal corpuscle consists of:', '["The proximal tubule resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the glomerular capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only Bowman''s capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","The juxtaglomerular apparatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glomerulus (capillary tuft) and Bowman''s capsule (parietal and visceral layers)"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerulus (capillary tuft) and Bowman''s capsule (parietal and visceral layers)', 'The renal corpuscle is formed by the glomerular capillary tuft surrounded by Bowman''s capsule. The visceral layer of Bowman''s capsule is made of podocytes, and filtrate passes through this structure into the tubular system.'),
('c648f022-c7f9-5ccf-a805-a6b2fc3e0453'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The juxtaglomerular cells (granular cells) are:', '["Macula densa cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Endothelial cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Podocytes mediated by specific receptor activation","Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that secrete renin","Mesangial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Modified smooth muscle cells in the afferent arteriole that secrete renin', 'Juxtaglomerular cells are modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole that synthesize, store, and release renin. Renin release is stimulated by low renal perfusion pressure, low distal NaCl, and β1-sympathetic activity.'),
('c606951c-ed3d-5d64-9d97-311722c21c1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The functional unit of the kidney is the:', '["Collecting duct","Glomerulus","Renal corpuscle","Bowman capsule","Nephron"]'::jsonb, 'Nephron', 'The nephron, comprising the renal corpuscle and tubule, is the functional unit of the kidney. Each kidney contains roughly one million nephrons.'),
('2e4348a7-addb-5558-add4-accef9511a3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the normal intracellular potassium concentration?', '["Protein resulting in decreased functional activity","Magnesium accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Potassium (K+ 140 mEq/L)","Calcium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sodium leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Potassium (K+ 140 mEq/L)', 'The intracellular potassium concentration is approximately 140 mEq/L, maintained by the Na+/K+-ATPase. It is much higher than the extracellular concentration of about 4 mEq/L.'),
('3cfe5068-9fba-5395-b6a8-07a205c04355'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'A 70-year-old male presents with altered mental status and K+ 5.8 mEq/L. The most appropriate immediate step to protect the myocardium is:', '["IV calcium gluconate","Insulin and glucose","Kayexalate","Hemodialysis","β2-agonists"]'::jsonb, 'IV calcium gluconate', 'IV calcium gluconate stabilizes the cardiac membrane against the arrhythmogenic effects of hyperkalemia and is the first line of therapy when ECG changes are present. Insulin with glucose, β2-agonists, Kayexalate, and dialysis remove or shift potassium.'),
('e11e8015-2b99-5a6d-b60d-9d4654d0aa1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'What is the normal serum chloride range?', '["Phosphate resulting in decreased functional activity","Bicarbonate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sulfate mediated by specific receptor activation","Protein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Chloride (Cl- 100-108 mEq/L)"]'::jsonb, 'Chloride (Cl- 100-108 mEq/L)', 'Normal serum chloride is 100-108 mEq/L. It is the major extracellular anion and largely parallels sodium.'),
('2453a0df-e118-58db-b86a-490ece03139f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'A 70-year-old male presents with altered mental status and K+ 5.8 mEq/L. Which ECG change is most likely?', '["Tall peaked T waves","Prolonged QT interval","ST segment depression","U waves","Shortened PR interval"]'::jsonb, 'Tall peaked T waves', 'Hyperkalemia classically produces tall peaked T waves, followed by QRS widening, loss of P waves, and eventually a sine-wave pattern. Immediate treatment with calcium gluconate protects the myocardium.'),
('eeb71637-c661-5ae6-ba8a-e7f8b7cd2374'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The most probable diagnosis in a patient with K+ 5.8 mEq/L is:', '["Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hypomagnesemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia', 'A serum potassium of 5.8 mEq/L exceeds the normal range of 3.5-5.0 mEq/L, establishing hyperkalemia. It can cause cardiac arrhythmias and requires prompt management.'),
('4f614bad-6534-5b63-9912-d842688c3247'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'The filtration fraction (GFR/RPF) is:', '["RBF/GFR resulting in decreased functional activity","GFR/RBF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RPF/GFR mediated by specific receptor activation","GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)","FF = GFR - RPF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)', 'The filtration fraction is the ratio GFR/RPF, normally 0.18-0.20. A high FF (e.g., in nephrotic syndrome) raises peritubular oncotic pressure and promotes sodium retention.'),
('ca2f5f6c-3251-5f44-9f5d-ba5a03bc8b99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Net filtration pressure across the glomerular capillary equals:', '["Glomerular capillary pressure - (Bowman capsule pressure + oncotic pressure)","Oncotic pressure only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Net hydrostatic pressure only mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillary pressure only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bowman pressure only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular capillary pressure - (Bowman capsule pressure + oncotic pressure)', 'Net filtration pressure is the glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus the sum of Bowman''s capsule pressure and plasma oncotic pressure. It is the driving force for filtrate formation.'),
('a26b68d1-29ab-521b-bbba-138b2fc803a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 1', 'Bowman''s capsule hydrostatic pressure (Pbc) is approximately:', '["10-15 mmHg","5 mmHg","40 mmHg","2 mmHg","25 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '10-15 mmHg', 'Bowman''s capsule hydrostatic pressure is about 10-15 mmHg. It opposes filtration, and obstruction of the urinary tract raises it, reducing GFR.'),
