('3c9db116-916a-572a-8c60-79022cd6d4c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following are components of the juxtaglomerular apparatus?', '["Macula densa, juxtaglomerular cells, and extraglomerular mesangial cells","Podocytes and mesangial cells only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proximal tubule cells only mediated by specific receptor activation","Distal convoluted tubule cells only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Afferent and efferent arterioles only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Macula densa, juxtaglomerular cells, and extraglomerular mesangial cells', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus is formed by the macula densa of the distal tubule, juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole, and extraglomerular mesangial (lacis) cells; it regulates renin release and GFR.'),
('3d341189-f9c0-5d05-ab1d-0bfe84553767'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Podocytes are:', '["Interstitial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Endothelial cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesangial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus that form slit diaphragms","Parietal epithelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus that form slit diaphragms', 'Podocytes are visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus; their foot processes interdigitate to form slit diaphragms, a key component of the filtration barrier.'),
('2a2506bf-564d-53d9-9b65-919d04c7471e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following are components of the glomerular basement membrane?', '["Type IV collagen, laminin, nidogen, and heparan sulfate proteoglycans","Elastin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Type I collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Type III collagen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fibrillin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Type IV collagen, laminin, nidogen, and heparan sulfate proteoglycans', 'The glomerular basement membrane contains type IV collagen, laminin, nidogen/entactin, and heparan sulfate proteoglycans, which provide its size and charge selectivity.'),
('9aed3908-7747-5c8e-858c-136b82737c87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Which transporters are characteristic of type A intercalated cells in the collecting duct?', '["Cl- channel only resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+ channel only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","H+-ATPase (apical) and H+/K+ ATPase → secretes H+ into lumen (generates HCO3- intracellularly)","ENaC only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","NHE3 only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+-ATPase (apical) and H+/K+ ATPase → secretes H+ into lumen (generates HCO3- intracellularly)', 'Type A intercalated cells secrete H+ via apical H+-ATPase and H+/K+-ATPase, generating new bicarbonate that is returned to the blood. This is the site of distal acid excretion, defective in type 1 RTA.'),
('525840fc-4d06-529b-80ee-682bb84fa59b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Acetazolamide:', '["CA inhibitor in PT (and other sites); blocks HCO3- reabsorption; causes metabolic acidosis and alkaline urine; weak diuretic; used for glaucoma","Inhibits NKCC2 in TAL accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Blocks NCC in DCT mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases HCO3- reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Causes metabolic alkalosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CA inhibitor in PT (and other sites); blocks HCO3- reabsorption; causes metabolic acidosis and alkaline urine; weak diuretic; used for glaucoma', 'Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in the proximal tubule and other tissues, blocking HCO3- reabsorption and producing a weak diuresis with metabolic acidosis and alkaline urine. It is used for glaucoma, altitude sickness, and epilepsy.'),
('f88b3e5a-13fe-5e52-bcab-2097a2308fc4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Membranous nephropathy is associated with:', '["Anti-PLA2R antibodies → subepithelial immune deposits","Anti-GBM antibodies accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","C3 deposits mediated by specific receptor activation","IgA deposits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ANCA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-PLA2R antibodies → subepithelial immune deposits', 'Primary membranous nephropathy is caused by autoantibodies against phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R) producing subepithelial immune deposits.'),
('eae568ea-a6bc-5338-bf3e-7fbf43090ca5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: In the kidney, the kallikrein-kinin system:', '["Produces bradykinin (a vasodilator) in the connecting tubule; bradykinin stimulates nitric oxide and natriuresis and is degraded by kininase (ACE)","Produces a vasoconstrictor peptide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Does not involve the kidney mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases aldosterone only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is unaffected by ACE inhibitors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produces bradykinin (a vasodilator) in the connecting tubule; bradykinin stimulates nitric oxide and natriuresis and is degraded by kininase (ACE)', 'Renal kallikrein in the connecting tubule cleaves kininogen to bradykinin, a vasodilator that stimulates nitric oxide and natriuresis. ACE (kininase) degrades bradykinin, which explains why ACE inhibitors increase bradykinin while reducing angiotensin II.'),
('9b55ec58-c28b-5235-aeb8-eebc2d9aa34e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Total body water is measured using the:', '["Dilution principle (V = amount injected ÷ equilibrated concentration), with tritiated water as the tracer","Clamping technique accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Radiographic method mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct weighing of plasma without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Echocardiographic method leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dilution principle (V = amount injected ÷ equilibrated concentration), with tritiated water as the tracer', 'Compartment volumes are measured by dilution: V = amount of tracer injected divided by its concentration after equilibration. Tritiated water labels TBW, inulin labels ECF, and 125I-albumin labels plasma.'),
('c320cf00-92f4-56bf-a412-9cdaf6742c03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Goodpasture syndrome involves:', '["Anti-GBM antibodies (α3 chain of collagen IV) → linear IgG on GBM","C3 deposits accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Anti-PLA2R mediated by specific receptor activation","IgA deposits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ANCA leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-GBM antibodies (α3 chain of collagen IV) → linear IgG on GBM', 'Goodpasture syndrome is caused by autoantibodies against the α3 chain of type IV collagen in the GBM and alveolar basement membrane, producing linear IgG staining and a pulmonary-renal syndrome.'),
('3138cc43-2d94-5c88-9e05-65d9db7925db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'ANCA-associated vasculitis includes:', '["Only MPA","GPA (C-ANCA/anti-PR3)","MPA (P-ANCA/anti-MPO), and EGPA","GPA, MPA, and EGPA","Anti-GBM disease"]'::jsonb, 'GPA, MPA, and EGPA', 'The ANCA-associated vasculitides are granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA, C-ANCA/anti-PR3), microscopic polyangiitis (MPA, P-ANCA/anti-MPO), and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA).'),
('d8363fc3-52c1-585e-9582-347cb4c3f00f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Diabetic nephropathy is characterized by:', '["Crescents resulting in decreased functional activity","GBM thickening, mesangial expansion, and Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules","Minimal change mediated by specific receptor activation","Membranous deposits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","IgA deposits leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GBM thickening, mesangial expansion, and Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules', 'Diabetic nephropathy shows diffuse GBM thickening, mesangial expansion, and nodular Kimmelstiel-Wilson lesions on histology, with clinical proteinuria and declining GFR.'),
('5b4b2b6c-6f22-5436-bd25-73b0b2c68b03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Hypertensive nephropathy shows:', '["Crescents resulting in decreased functional activity","Minimal change accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Membranous deposits mediated by specific receptor activation","IgA deposits without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vascular sclerosis, glomerular ischemia, and tubular atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular sclerosis, glomerular ischemia, and tubular atrophy', 'Chronic hypertension damages the kidney through arteriolar sclerosis, glomerular ischemia with hyalinosis, and tubular atrophy, leading to progressive nephrosclerosis.'),
('110b337c-7ea2-5b84-854d-315b4ae4e971'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What are the genetic basis and protein products of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)?', '["PKD1 (85%) or PKD2 (15%) → polycystin proteins","PAX2 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","WT1 mediated by specific receptor activation","NPHS1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","COL4A5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'PKD1 (85%) or PKD2 (15%) → polycystin proteins', 'ADPKD results from mutations in PKD1 (polycystin-1, 85%) or PKD2 (polycystin-2, 15%), causing progressive renal cyst formation and renal failure.'),
('60e8042d-f96a-5bc5-b840-270e60d6d626'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('b4c24d75-df26-59d6-9b72-2bf0f75f640d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Treatment for hyperkalemia includes:', '["β2-agonists (albuterol)","All of the above","IV calcium gluconate (stabilizes myocardium)","Kayexalate (binds K+ in gut → removal)","Insulin + glucose (shifts K+ into cells)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Hyperkalemia is managed by calcium gluconate to stabilize the myocardium, insulin plus glucose and β2-agonists to shift K+ into cells, and Kayexalate or dialysis to remove potassium from the body.'),
('73061657-0479-5df6-ad78-304ba8bd467d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What are the major risk factors for contrast-induced nephropathy (CIN)?', '["Male sex resulting in decreased functional activity","Low contrast volume accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Young age mediated by specific receptor activation","Normal GFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CKD, diabetes, volume depletion, high contrast dose, and older age"]'::jsonb, 'CKD, diabetes, volume depletion, high contrast dose, and older age', 'Chronic kidney disease, diabetes, volume depletion, large contrast volumes, and older age are the principal risk factors for contrast-induced nephropathy.'),
('09944263-68a3-5410-998d-df4c93768171'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Hyperkalemia characteristically produces which ECG progression?', '["Normal ECG resulting in decreased functional activity","Prolonged QT → T inversion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peaked T → prolonged PR → wide QRS → sine wave → VF","Flat T → ST elevation without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ST depression → T inversion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peaked T → prolonged PR → wide QRS → sine wave → VF', 'Hyperkalemia produces a characteristic ECG progression: peaked T waves, PR prolongation, QRS widening, a sine-wave pattern, and finally ventricular fibrillation.'),
('4bd8bc1c-282d-5de6-9649-7a1e1dd5ba91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which ECG finding is characteristic of hypokalemia?', '["ST depression","Peaked T waves","Wide QRS","U waves, flat T waves","Prolonged QT"]'::jsonb, 'U waves, flat T waves', 'Hypokalemia typically causes U waves and flat or inverted T waves, with possible ST depression and arrhythmias.'),
('496fc673-c37c-50ac-8323-23d829b22649'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Thick ascending limb transport is inhibited by:', '["Thiazides resulting in decreased functional activity","Spironolactone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Amiloride mediated by specific receptor activation","Acetazolamide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide)"]'::jsonb, 'Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide)', 'Loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, blocking sodium, potassium, and chloride reabsorption. This makes them the most potent diuretics and also increases calcium and magnesium excretion.'),
('7f16f315-c87a-5ae4-adaa-14e478af3c78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Hypomagnesemia causes:', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Metabolic acidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Neuromuscular irritability, tetany, and refractory hypokalemia/hypocalcemia","CNS depression without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypertension leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuromuscular irritability, tetany, and refractory hypokalemia/hypocalcemia', 'Hypomagnesemia causes neuromuscular hyperexcitability with tetany and seizures, and it can produce hypokalemia and hypocalcemia that are refractory to replacement until magnesium is corrected.'),
('50427781-e3df-5632-b1b4-ddc90f2c3b2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is activated in the kidney by:', '["Vitamin D receptor resulting in decreased functional activity","24-hydroxylase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","25-hydroxylase in liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Parathyroid hormone alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubule cells"]'::jsonb, '1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubule cells', 'Renal 1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) in proximal tubular mitochondria converts 25-OH-vitamin D to the active hormone 1,25-(OH)2D. The enzyme is regulated by PTH, phosphate, calcium, and FGF23.'),
('f680c8e3-52a9-51df-ba94-94126dced2c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Loop diuretics cause which electrolyte abnormalities?', '["Metabolic acidosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia, metabolic alkalosis","Hyperkalemia mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyponatremia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hypercalcemia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia, metabolic alkalosis', 'By blocking the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb, loop diuretics cause hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia, and metabolic alkalosis.'),
('5d5b0b7f-0668-53a0-8d8c-3a3396a2bfe1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Proximal tubule reabsorbs filtered HCO3- via:', '["Passive diffusion resulting in decreased functional activity","H+ secretion (NHE3) + apical CA IV → H2CO3 → H2O + CO2 (CO2 diffuses into cell)","H+ ATPase only mediated by specific receptor activation","Paracellular transport without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Direct HCO3- transport leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'H+ secretion (NHE3) + apical CA IV → H2CO3 → H2O + CO2 (CO2 diffuses into cell)', 'Proximal bicarbonate reabsorption begins with H+ secretion by NHE3; H+ combines with filtered HCO3- to form H2CO3, which brush-border carbonic anhydrase (CA IV) splits into CO2 and water. CO2 diffuses into the cell and is reconverted to HCO3-, which exits via NBCe1.'),
('ff29327a-dfc2-52a1-bf59-fc7f9d3d263c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Potassium-sparing diuretics cause:', '["Hypokalemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis', 'Potassium-sparing diuretics (spironolactone, amiloride, triamterene) reduce distal K+ and H+ secretion, causing hyperkalemia and a hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis.'),
('7580747e-7038-51f4-a48c-624d1c522d23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Uremic syndrome symptoms at GFR <15 mL/min include:', '["Only electrolyte abnormalities resulting in decreased functional activity","Asymptomatic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Only anemia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nausea, fatigue, pruritus, neuropathy, pericarditis, and bleeding"]'::jsonb, 'Nausea, fatigue, pruritus, neuropathy, pericarditis, and bleeding', 'With GFR below 15 mL/min, accumulation of uremic toxins produces anorexia, nausea, fatigue, pruritus, peripheral neuropathy, pericarditis, and bleeding tendency.'),
('cc1e268f-b950-585a-b557-c23fbdf0c215'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, Vasopressin (ADH) increases water reabsorption by acting on:', '["Collecting duct (V2 receptors → AQP2 insertion)","V1 receptors in the proximal tubule","NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb","NCC in the distal tubule","SGLT2 in the proximal tubule"]'::jsonb, 'Collecting duct (V2 receptors → AQP2 insertion)', 'ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct principal cells, triggering insertion of aquaporin-2 channels into the apical membrane. Water is then reabsorbed osmotically along the medullary gradient, concentrating the urine.'),
('f1b75671-2642-5825-9be0-12993e3802f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Hemodialysis uses:', '["The peritoneum as the membrane resulting in decreased functional activity","A semipermeable membrane with countercurrent dialysate flow","Continuous hemofiltration only mediated by specific receptor activation","Plasma exchange without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Intermittent hemofiltration only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A semipermeable membrane with countercurrent dialysate flow', 'Hemodialysis removes solutes across a synthetic semipermeable membrane with dialysate flowing countercurrent to blood, maximizing the concentration gradient.'),
('d3114823-3c58-5632-bc24-627f353ead18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the principle of peritoneal dialysis?', '["Plasmapheresis resulting in decreased functional activity","The peritoneum as the dialysis membrane with dialysate instilled into the abdomen","Hemofiltration mediated by specific receptor activation","An artificial membrane without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Continuous venovenous hemofiltration leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The peritoneum as the dialysis membrane with dialysate instilled into the abdomen', 'Peritoneal dialysis uses the patient''s peritoneum as a natural semipermeable membrane; dialysate instilled into the peritoneal cavity exchanges solutes and fluid.'),
('bf515491-b784-5346-a94c-c20bcfbf8027'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Renin release from juxtaglomerular cells is stimulated by:', '["Low renal perfusion pressure (renal baroreceptor)","High renal perfusion pressure","Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa","Atrial natriuretic peptide","High dietary sodium intake"]'::jsonb, 'Low renal perfusion pressure (renal baroreceptor)', 'Renin is released by juxtaglomerular cells when renal perfusion pressure falls, when macula densa NaCl delivery decreases, and during β1-sympathetic stimulation. Renin is an aspartyl protease that converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I.'),
('31fc23fc-ad9d-5e4c-a44a-dfb5687941c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The peritoneal equilibration test (PET) is used to:', '["Assess residual GFR resulting in decreased functional activity","Check ultrafiltration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Measure dialysis adequacy mediated by specific receptor activation","Diagnose peritonitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Assess peritoneal membrane transport characteristics for PD prescription"]'::jsonb, 'Assess peritoneal membrane transport characteristics for PD prescription', 'The PET characterizes the peritoneal membrane''s solute transport and ultrafiltration, guiding the peritoneal dialysis prescription.'),
('d75d371a-0363-5ae4-a3a2-f1cbde447a8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Adequacy of dialysis is measured by:', '["Urine output resulting in decreased functional activity","Creatinine clearance accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kt/Vurea (urea clearance × time / V)","Serum K+ without affecting the primary tissue pathway","BUN leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kt/Vurea (urea clearance × time / V)', 'Dialysis adequacy is quantified by Kt/Vurea, where K is dialyzer urea clearance, t is treatment time, and V is the volume of urea distribution.'),
('e86a3dfc-127a-5e62-b365-2191333f99bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The countercurrent exchanger (vasa recta):', '["Actively transports NaCl resulting in decreased functional activity","Reabsorbs glucose accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Generates the osmotic gradient mediated by specific receptor activation","Passively exchanges solutes and water to maintain the medullary gradient","Dilutes the medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Passively exchanges solutes and water to maintain the medullary gradient', 'The vasa recta act as countercurrent exchangers, passively exchanging solutes and water with the interstitium to preserve the medullary osmotic gradient without washing it out.'),
('09c6d6f7-78d9-5725-a84d-c3892fd051ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Which formula represents free water reabsorption (TcH2O), which is positive when urine is concentrated?', '["Posm/Uosm resulting in decreased functional activity","Uosm × V accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","V - Cosm mediated by specific receptor activation","V without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cosm - V (positive in concentrated urine)"]'::jsonb, 'Cosm - V (positive in concentrated urine)', 'Free water reabsorption (TcH2O) equals Cosm - V and is positive when the kidney is concentrating urine under the influence of ADH. It quantifies the water reabsorbed in excess of that needed to keep urine isosmotic.'),
('5e90e183-78b3-5e91-b48d-2f728bee837a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: The internal urethral sphincter (bladder neck):', '["Smooth muscle (α1-adrenergic → contraction maintains continence)","Striated muscle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Voluntary skeletal muscle mediated by specific receptor activation","Innervated by parasympathetic nerves only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Relaxed by α1-agonists leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle (α1-adrenergic → contraction maintains continence)', 'The internal urethral sphincter is smooth muscle innervated by sympathetic nerves. α1-adrenergic stimulation contracts it to maintain urinary continence, while α-blockers relax it.'),
('004eeec4-9442-5d98-aa82-ba81c9df5538'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Fibromuscular dysplasia of the renal artery is most common in:', '["Older women resulting in decreased functional activity","Elderly men (proximal artery) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Men of any age mediated by specific receptor activation","Children without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Young women (distal main renal artery)"]'::jsonb, 'Young women (distal main renal artery)', 'Fibromuscular dysplasia, a cause of secondary hypertension, classically affects young women and involves the distal main renal artery.'),
('725be413-1897-5458-abae-9a13ee130911'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis affects:', '["Elderly women (distal main renal artery) resulting in decreased functional activity","Young men accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Children mediated by specific receptor activation","Elderly patients with diffuse atherosclerosis (ostium/proximal)","Young women leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Elderly patients with diffuse atherosclerosis (ostium/proximal)', 'Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis affects elderly patients with diffuse atherosclerosis and typically involves the ostium or proximal portion of the renal artery.'),
('e6ab8a0f-1f63-5ab1-b1b7-38a1926b7504'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Flash pulmonary edema in bilateral RAS is due to:', '["Infection resulting in decreased functional activity","Impaired Na+ excretion with preserved LV function → rapid volume overload","LV dysfunction mediated by specific receptor activation","Pulmonary embolism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ARDS leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired Na+ excretion with preserved LV function → rapid volume overload', 'In bilateral renal artery stenosis, reduced renal perfusion impairs Na+ excretion, causing rapid volume overload and flash pulmonary edema despite preserved left ventricular function.'),
('14407a64-2834-5de4-ac48-e2ccb8ef2e38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male presents with cardiac arrhythmia and serum K+ of 3.0 mEq/L. Which underlying condition is most likely?', '["No significant underlying condition resulting in decreased functional activity","Bartter syndrome accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Metabolic acidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary aldosteronism (HTN, aldosterone → Na+ retention, K+ loss, and target organ damage)","Diabetes insipidus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary aldosteronism (HTN, aldosterone → Na+ retention, K+ loss, and target organ damage)', 'Primary aldosteronism (Conn''s syndrome) causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and arrhythmias through aldosterone-mediated Na+ retention and K+ loss, with resulting target organ damage.'),
('4625462e-15d9-57b0-a7af-7fd7aa6d2645'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: In major burns, the primary fluid shift is:', '["Increased capillary permeability → protein and fluid loss into the interstitium → decreased plasma volume and shock","Expansion of plasma volume accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Movement of fluid into the intracellular space mediated by specific receptor activation","Pure water loss without protein shift without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduced interstitial fluid volume leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased capillary permeability → protein and fluid loss into the interstitium → decreased plasma volume and shock', 'Burns increase capillary permeability, allowing protein and fluid to leak into the interstitial space. The resulting fall in plasma volume can cause hypovolemic shock, and fluid sequestration in pancreatitis produces similar third-spacing.'),
('c4a50b0a-8c37-5708-a612-b3bfa2b50cdb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: The connecting tubule and collecting duct:', '["Only K+ secretion resulting in decreased functional activity","Only H+ secretion accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Principal cells: Na+ reabsorption (ENaC) and K+ secretion (ROMK). Intercalated cells: H+ or HCO3- secretion","Only water reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only Na+ reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Principal cells: Na+ reabsorption (ENaC) and K+ secretion (ROMK). Intercalated cells: H+ or HCO3- secretion', 'Principal cells reabsorb sodium through ENaC and secrete potassium via ROMK, while type A and type B intercalated cells secrete H+ or HCO3-. This segment is the final site for regulating sodium, potassium, and acid-base balance.'),
('41ed531f-be96-5ecd-b5c8-16b8a1b9aa65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What are the two main parameters used in KDIGO criteria for staging AKI?', '["Only Cr level resulting in decreased functional activity","BUN alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only urine output mediated by specific receptor activation","KDIGO criteria based on Cr increase and urine output","Electrolytes alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'KDIGO criteria based on Cr increase and urine output', 'The KDIGO classification stages AKI using the rise in serum creatinine and the reduction in urine output.'),
('a5ff9bc2-f737-5f38-9ec0-27bb2a33eb45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Urine osmolality in prerenal AKI is typically:', '["Isosthenuric (300)",">500 mOsm/kg (concentrated)","<350 mOsm/kg (dilute)","100 mOsm/kg","Variable"]'::jsonb, '>500 mOsm/kg (concentrated)', 'In prerenal azotemia, intact tubules respond to hypoperfusion by conserving salt and water, producing concentrated urine with osmolality above 500 mOsm/kg.'),
('c8c20de3-58b4-549f-86ef-b18af1af284d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the most common cause of acute kidney injury in hospitalized patients?', '["AIN, glomerulonephritis, and vascular causes","Only obstruction","Only infection","ATN","Prerenal azotemia"]'::jsonb, 'ATN', 'Acute tubular necrosis (ATN), usually from ischemia or nephrotoxins, is the most common cause of intrinsic AKI in hospitalized patients.'),
('71bb9f93-51f5-521f-a4a5-762933521228'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which feature suggests postrenal (obstructive) AKI?', '["Active urine sediment resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal renal ultrasound accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FENa <1% mediated by specific receptor activation","BUN:Cr <15 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Urine output is variable or anuric with dilated collecting system on ultrasound"]'::jsonb, 'Urine output is variable or anuric with dilated collecting system on ultrasound', 'Postrenal (obstructive) AKI produces fluctuating or anuric urine output with a dilated collecting system on ultrasound because obstruction raises intratubular pressure.'),
('fd0a562a-8147-5e15-8ab6-26be45972421'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Glomerulotubular balance refers to:', '["The proximal tubule reabsorbing a constant 60-70% of the filtered load despite changes in GFR, partly due to changes in peritubular oncotic pressure","Fractional reabsorption varying directly with GFR accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Balance between afferent and efferent tone mediated by specific receptor activation","Reabsorption proportional to aldosterone levels without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A fixed absolute amount of reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The proximal tubule reabsorbing a constant 60-70% of the filtered load despite changes in GFR, partly due to changes in peritubular oncotic pressure', 'Glomerulotubular balance keeps proximal fractional reabsorption near 60-70% as GFR changes. Higher GFR increases filtration fraction and peritubular oncotic pressure, which drives more reabsorption, and increased flow also enhances NHE3 activity.'),
('68318a09-d4dc-55f4-a563-b40ad0605232'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Muddy brown granular casts are characteristic of:', '["Prerenal azotemia","Interstitial nephritis","Urinary tract infection","Glomerulonephritis","Acute tubular necrosis (ATN)"]'::jsonb, 'Acute tubular necrosis (ATN)', 'Muddy brown granular casts are the classic urinary sediment finding in acute tubular necrosis.'),
('606db0a2-66c4-5221-b767-1fb9a0b66015'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the classic urine sediment finding in glomerulonephritis?', '["Hyaline casts","Muddy brown granular casts","WBC casts","Dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts","Eosinophiluria"]'::jsonb, 'Dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts', 'Glomerular bleeding produces dysmorphic red blood cells and red cell casts, the classic sediment of glomerulonephritis.'),
('7595e9b4-4d5a-5a48-a835-18a3a79efb05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Renal autoregulation:', '["Maintains RBF and GFR constant across MAP 80-180 mmHg via myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback","Operates only above MAP 200 mmHg accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Maintains RBF and GFR constant at all pressures mediated by specific receptor activation","Is mediated by a single mechanism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Depends entirely on neural input leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Maintains RBF and GFR constant across MAP 80-180 mmHg via myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback', 'Autoregulation keeps renal blood flow and GFR constant when mean arterial pressure ranges from 80 to 180 mmHg. The myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback act together on the afferent arteriole.'),
('bbccfe2d-017d-5a3b-8a36-362809b8a6cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: The countercurrent multiplier (loop of Henle) and countercurrent exchanger (vasa recta) function so that:', '["Multiplier: generates gradient. Exchanger: maintains gradient","Exchanger: generates the gradient","Multiplier: maintains the gradient","They serve identical functions","They oppose each other"]'::jsonb, 'Multiplier: generates gradient. Exchanger: maintains gradient', 'The loop of Henle acts as a countercurrent multiplier that actively generates the medullary osmotic gradient, while the vasa recta acts as a countercurrent exchanger that preserves the gradient by not washing it out.'),
('e2ee9606-413d-588c-ab61-70a97b1753bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Renal biopsy is indicated for which of the following clinical scenarios?', '["All patients with hypertension resulting in decreased functional activity","Unexplained AKI, nephrotic syndrome, RPGN, and unexplained renal disease","All patients with diabetes mediated by specific receptor activation","All patients with proteinuria without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only patients with renal stones leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unexplained AKI, nephrotic syndrome, RPGN, and unexplained renal disease', 'Renal biopsy is indicated for unexplained acute kidney injury, nephrotic syndrome, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis, and other unexplained renal disease where the diagnosis guides treatment.'),
('b182079b-8d0b-5616-a12e-831c560e04ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Nephrotic syndrome is defined as:', '["Hematuria only resulting in decreased functional activity","Normal albumin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Proteinuria >3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminemia, edema, hyperlipidemia","HTN only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proteinuria >1 g/day leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Proteinuria >3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminemia, edema, hyperlipidemia', 'Nephrotic syndrome comprises proteinuria >3.5 g/day, hypoalbuminemia, edema, and hyperlipidemia.'),
('b248a208-1ec5-58c2-8bfa-cd74b79a9ac9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following best characterizes nephritic syndrome?', '["Proteinuria >3.5 g/day with edema resulting in decreased functional activity","Hematuria, hypertension, oliguria, RBC casts, and mild proteinuria","Isolated hematuria without hypertension mediated by specific receptor activation","Nephrotic-range proteinuria only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sterile pyuria only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hematuria, hypertension, oliguria, RBC casts, and mild proteinuria', 'Nephritic syndrome presents with hematuria, RBC casts, hypertension, oliguria, and mild-to-moderate proteinuria, reflecting glomerular inflammation.'),
('59d6a289-0828-5008-a923-030ea61011eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Nephrotic-range proteinuria is:', '[">1 g/24h resulting in decreased functional activity",">5 g/24h accompanied by associated clinical symptoms",">500 mg/24h mediated by specific receptor activation",">3.5 g/24h (or UPCR >3.5 g/g)",">10 g/24h leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '>3.5 g/24h (or UPCR >3.5 g/g)', 'Nephrotic-range proteinuria is defined as >3.5 g per 24 hours, equivalent to a urine protein-to-creatinine ratio >3.5 g/g.'),
('b08374cd-247d-500c-959e-6730255e5ace'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Selective proteinuria (mostly albumin) is seen in:', '["FSGS","Membranous GN","Minimal change disease","Diabetic nephropathy","IgA nephropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Minimal change disease', 'Minimal change disease, with effacement of podocyte foot processes but a largely intact charge barrier, causes highly selective proteinuria composed mainly of albumin.'),
('1df763f3-919a-5b9e-8b18-9afb5fe8dac4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Hematuria of glomerular origin is characterized by:', '["Eumorphic RBCs resulting in decreased functional activity","WBCs only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Clots mediated by specific receptor activation","Dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts","Crystals only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dysmorphic RBCs and RBC casts', 'Glomerular hematuria produces dysmorphic red blood cells and red cell casts, distinguishing it from lower urinary tract bleeding.'),
('69da3a40-3b86-581f-86f4-903479aeea50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Loop diuretics are used in AKI to:', '["Reverse tubular necrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Replace renal function accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Prevent AKI mediated by specific receptor activation","Improve GFR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Manage volume overload (but do not prevent or treat AKI)"]'::jsonb, 'Manage volume overload (but do not prevent or treat AKI)', 'Loop diuretics are used in AKI to control volume overload and hyperkalemia; they do not prevent AKI, improve GFR, or reduce mortality.'),
('33c02588-774c-599f-a5b6-80dfb1573a14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Dopamine in AKI:', '["Increases renal blood flow and prevents AKI","Is the standard of care","Prevents contrast nephropathy","Reduces mortality","Has no proven benefit for prevention or treatment of AKI"]'::jsonb, 'Has no proven benefit for prevention or treatment of AKI', 'Low-dose (''renal-dose'') dopamine has shown no benefit in preventing or treating AKI and is not recommended.'),
('3c0d3d84-5ed0-519f-bba4-010e6a2f8c96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, ENaC is blocked by:', '["HCTZ resulting in decreased functional activity","Furosemide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Acetazolamide mediated by specific receptor activation","Spironolactone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Amiloride and triamterene (K+-sparing diuretics)"]'::jsonb, 'Amiloride and triamterene (K+-sparing diuretics)', 'Amiloride and triamterene directly block ENaC in the collecting duct, making them potassium-sparing diuretics. Spironolactone is an aldosterone receptor antagonist rather than a direct ENaC blocker.'),
('833953e8-6af6-5cf9-bd66-ee1ae8fcb99e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Nesiritide (BNP) in AKI:', '["Is protective resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevents AKI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Has no role in AKI treatment; may worsen renal function","Is an ACEi without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is a diuretic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Has no role in AKI treatment; may worsen renal function', 'Nesiritide, a recombinant BNP, has no proven benefit in AKI and may worsen renal function, so it is not used for this indication.'),
('22c4199f-12a7-565a-b079-794dc61a0458'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the proposed mechanism of adenosine antagonists in preventing contrast nephropathy?', '["Is first-line treatment resulting in decreased functional activity","Is protective accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is standard of care mediated by specific receptor activation","Prevents contrast nephropathy without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adenosine antagonist — unclear benefit; not routinely recommended"]'::jsonb, 'Adenosine antagonist — unclear benefit; not routinely recommended', 'Adenosine antagonists (e.g., theophylline) were proposed to block adenosine-mediated afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction in contrast nephropathy, but evidence of benefit is unclear and they are not routinely recommended.'),
('4b6a65ae-edbf-5061-9a6a-a974aad94b8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The KDOQI guidelines recommend starting dialysis when:', '["Creatinine >2 resulting in decreased functional activity","GFR <30 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","GFR <50 mediated by specific receptor activation","GFR <10 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GFR <15 with uremic symptoms or refractory complications"]'::jsonb, 'GFR <15 with uremic symptoms or refractory complications', 'KDOQI recommends initiating dialysis when GFR falls below 15 mL/min with uremic symptoms or refractory complications such as hyperkalemia, acidosis, or volume overload.'),
('a31cc99f-a119-5940-ab73-eae62c3335f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('3c11d457-0392-52c5-9e51-c62104fc36cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Renal osteodystrophy encompasses:', '["Only osteoporosis resulting in decreased functional activity","Only adynamic bone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High-turnover (osteitis fibrosa cystica from hyperPTH) and low-turnover (adynamic)","Only osteomalacia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only osteitis fibrosa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'High-turnover (osteitis fibrosa cystica from hyperPTH) and low-turnover (adynamic)', 'Renal osteodystrophy includes high-turnover bone disease (osteitis fibrosa cystica from secondary hyperparathyroidism) and low-turnover forms such as adynamic bone disease.'),
('bfb64fb5-c7b7-58b3-b5ef-1c6a3b40a82e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Vitamin D analogs in CKD:', '["Decrease Ca2+ resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase PTH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Are not used mediated by specific receptor activation","Calcitriol, paricalcitol, and doxercalciferol suppress PTH","Increase phosphate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Calcitriol, paricalcitol, and doxercalciferol suppress PTH', 'Vitamin D analogs (calcitriol, paricalcitol, doxercalciferol) suppress PTH secretion by the parathyroid glands, treating secondary hyperparathyroidism in CKD.'),
('6386e1a5-070a-5f47-96d1-98e1dbb32c83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'FGF23 is elevated in early CKD and:', '["Promotes phosphaturia and inhibits vitamin D activation","Promotes phosphate reabsorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases PTH mediated by specific receptor activation","Increases vitamin D without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decreases Ca2+ leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes phosphaturia and inhibits vitamin D activation', 'FGF23 rises early in CKD; it promotes renal phosphate excretion and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase, reducing active vitamin D production.'),
('959db47b-e268-5683-a84c-0f798f91cc66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What are the three main systemic complications of chronic kidney disease (CKD)?', '["Only volume overload","Hyperkalemia","HTN, uremic toxins, anemia","Ca/P disorders, inflammation","Hematuria only"]'::jsonb, 'HTN, uremic toxins, anemia', 'As nephrons are progressively lost, CKD produces hypertension, accumulation of uremic toxins, and anemia from EPO deficiency as major systemic complications.'),
('3d98f5aa-0753-5730-adcc-540b88e20c34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the most common neurologic complication of end-stage renal disease (ESRD)?', '["Myasthenia gravis resulting in decreased functional activity","A distal, symmetric, sensorimotor polyneuropathy in advanced CKD","Mononeuropathy mediated by specific receptor activation","ALS without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Guillain-Barre syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A distal, symmetric, sensorimotor polyneuropathy in advanced CKD', 'Uremic polyneuropathy, a distal symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy, is the most common neurologic complication of advanced CKD/ESRD.'),
('8bbf586a-1e5a-5b54-818b-3a7844a4b23b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Restless legs syndrome (RLS) in CKD:', '["Uncommon resulting in decreased functional activity","Not associated with iron accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Resolves without treatment mediated by specific receptor activation","Common in dialysis patients; associated with iron deficiency and neuropathy","Not treatable leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Common in dialysis patients; associated with iron deficiency and neuropathy', 'RLS is common in dialysis patients and is associated with iron deficiency, uremic neuropathy, and anemia; it is treatable with iron and other agents.'),
('5e8f003a-7dec-5faf-a78c-787c11d0e7b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: The filtration fraction (FF) is:', '["RBF/GFR resulting in decreased functional activity","GFR/RBF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RPF/GFR mediated by specific receptor activation","GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)","FF = GFR - RPF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)', 'Filtration fraction is the ratio of GFR to renal plasma flow, normally about 0.18-0.20. It rises when the efferent arteriole constricts (e.g., with angiotensin II), raising peritubular oncotic pressure.'),
('9abb9f85-65e7-5382-a5b4-5cec0eb1ae4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: The transport maximum for glucose (TmG) is approximately:', '["375 mg/min","125 mg/min","625 mg/min","90 mg/min","1000 mg/min"]'::jsonb, '375 mg/min', 'The proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose up to a transport maximum of about 375 mg/min. When filtered load exceeds this capacity (roughly plasma glucose >180 mg/dL with splay), glycosuria appears.'),
('4b5c391f-648a-5a7b-a7c7-11a8b3f821bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Immunosuppression in transplant includes:', '["Plasmapheresis resulting in decreased functional activity","Only CNI accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only steroids mediated by specific receptor activation","Only MMF without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Induction (basiliximab/ATG) + maintenance (CNI + MMF/AZA + steroids)"]'::jsonb, 'Induction (basiliximab/ATG) + maintenance (CNI + MMF/AZA + steroids)', 'Transplant immunosuppression uses induction agents (basiliximab, ATG) followed by maintenance therapy with a calcineurin inhibitor plus mycophenolate/azathioprine and steroids.'),
('62600e92-3129-5436-a436-35712ca2d84e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'CNI nephrotoxicity manifests as:', '["Papillary necrosis resulting in decreased functional activity","ATN accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cyst formation mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomerulonephritis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Afferent arteriolar hyalinosis, tubular atrophy, and interstitial fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent arteriolar hyalinosis, tubular atrophy, and interstitial fibrosis', 'Calcineurin inhibitor nephrotoxicity produces afferent arteriolar hyalinosis, tubular atrophy, and striped interstitial fibrosis, with chronic renal dysfunction.'),
('6737dc94-679d-5959-87f5-45a2f85de2f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Which neural pathway provides voluntary control of micturition?', '["Sphincter alone resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Detrusor alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Frontal cortex → pontine center → initiates/permits detrusor contraction and external sphincter relaxation","Spinal cord alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal cortex → pontine center → initiates/permits detrusor contraction and external sphincter relaxation', 'Voluntary micturition is initiated by the frontal cortex, which signals the pontine micturition center to permit detrusor contraction and relaxation of the external sphincter. Loss of cortical control can cause neurogenic bladder.'),
('603e08e9-78b8-57ae-b79a-1c195016c0f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'A kidney transplant recipient develops acute antibody-mediated rejection. Which intervention directly removes circulating pathogenic antibodies?', '["MMF only","Increasing CNI","High-dose steroids only","Plasmapheresis","IVIG, ± rituximab/bortezomib/eculizumab"]'::jsonb, 'Plasmapheresis', 'Plasmapheresis directly removes circulating donor-specific antibodies and is a mainstay of therapy for acute antibody-mediated rejection.'),
('c772ad3b-658b-5e18-802a-0655d41cf0e5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Which statement about FGF23 is correct?', '["Phosphaturic hormone (↓NaPi-IIa → ↓Pi reabsorption) + ↓1,25-(OH)2D synthesis","Increases vitamin D synthesis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Increases phosphate reabsorption mediated by specific receptor activation","Acts like PTH by raising calcium without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↑Phosphate reabsorption in the DCT leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphaturic hormone (↓NaPi-IIa → ↓Pi reabsorption) + ↓1,25-(OH)2D synthesis', 'FGF23 promotes renal phosphate excretion by downregulating NaPi-IIa and suppresses renal 1α-hydroxylase, lowering 1,25-(OH)2D production. It rises early in CKD as a counter-regulatory phosphate-lowering hormone.'),
('8ec265a6-a108-5f73-b6af-7811bf57d645'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, During prolonged fasting, the renal cortex contributes to glucose homeostasis by:', '["Producing glucose from lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% during prolonged fasting and generating bicarbonate in acidosis","Secreting insulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Storing glycogen mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxidizing glucose exclusively without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Producing ketone bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Producing glucose from lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% during prolonged fasting and generating bicarbonate in acidosis', 'Renal gluconeogenesis uses lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% of glucose during prolonged fasting. In acidosis, glutamine metabolism both produces glucose and generates bicarbonate for acid-base balance.'),
('097adbaf-e44f-5e47-b1b0-6d246154f164'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Delayed graft function (DGF) after renal transplantation is defined as:', '["Chronic rejection resulting in decreased functional activity","BK nephropathy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Need for dialysis within the first week after transplant (usually ATN)","Hyperacute rejection without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acute rejection leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Need for dialysis within the first week after transplant (usually ATN)', 'Delayed graft function is the need for dialysis within the first week after transplantation, usually resulting from ischemic acute tubular necrosis.'),
('ebd5bc35-1834-50cd-a2b0-dad4f58fa885'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which statement best describes living donor nephrectomy?', '["Surgical removal of a kidney from a healthy donor for transplantation","Mostly laparoscopic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","High risk for donor mediated by specific receptor activation","Experimental without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Not performed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surgical removal of a kidney from a healthy donor for transplantation', 'Living donor nephrectomy is the surgical removal of a kidney from a healthy donor for transplantation, commonly performed laparoscopically with low donor risk.'),
('f9cd7efa-5361-53c6-b6bd-635e5331cefe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The KDPI (Kidney Donor Profile Index) is used to:', '["Determine recipient compatibility resulting in decreased functional activity","Predict deceased donor kidney graft survival (higher KDPI = worse)","Assess rejection risk mediated by specific receptor activation","Match donors to recipients without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Measure HLA mismatch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Predict deceased donor kidney graft survival (higher KDPI = worse)', 'The KDPI estimates the risk of graft failure for deceased donor kidneys; a higher KDPI predicts worse graft survival.'),
('8e391e39-1458-5592-99cb-7e036d876907'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Renal 1α-hydroxylase (CYP27B1) is stimulated by:', '["PTH and low phosphate","FGF23","High calcium","High phosphate","Calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'PTH and low phosphate', '1α-hydroxylase in proximal tubular mitochondria is upregulated by PTH and low phosphate and suppressed by FGF23, high calcium, and high phosphate. It converts 25-OH-D to the active 1,25-(OH)2D.'),
('0e9306b9-2311-5b34-b107-53863cd0d5d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, What is the function of semicarbazide-sensitive amine oxidase (SSAO) secreted by the kidney?', '["Metabolizes circulating catecholamines; regulates BP; depletion in CKD contributes to hypertension","Degrades renalase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Activates vitamin D mediated by specific receptor activation","Promotes phosphate reabsorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reabsorbs glucose in the proximal tubule leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolizes circulating catecholamines; regulates BP; depletion in CKD contributes to hypertension', 'SSAO is an amine oxidase secreted by the kidneys that metabolizes circulating catecholamines, helping regulate blood pressure. Reduced renal SSAO in CKD increases sympathetic activity and contributes to hypertension.'),
('637e1873-972d-528e-95f5-9dd753c60e3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'A pregnant patient is diagnosed with a urinary tract infection. Which treatment is appropriate?', '["Tetracyclines resulting in decreased functional activity","Fluoroquinolones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only symptomatic relief mediated by specific receptor activation","Treatment with safe antibiotics (penicillins, cephalosporins, nitrofurantoin)","Aminoglycosides leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Treatment with safe antibiotics (penicillins, cephalosporins, nitrofurantoin)', 'UTIs in pregnancy are treated with pregnancy-safe antibiotics such as penicillins, cephalosporins, and nitrofurantoin, avoiding tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones.'),
('be1c015d-225e-5233-87b4-97df2cf47d0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy:', '["Only treat if symptomatic resulting in decreased functional activity","Resolves spontaneously accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Should be screened and treated (prevents pyelonephritis)","Is not harmful without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Does not need treatment leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Should be screened and treated (prevents pyelonephritis)', 'Pregnant patients are screened for asymptomatic bacteriuria and treated because it otherwise progresses to symptomatic pyelonephritis and adverse pregnancy outcomes.'),
('08ceaf62-43ad-5981-bfc6-3fceb3cff876'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which presentation is classic for acute ureteral obstruction (renal colic)?', '["Severe flank pain radiating to groin with nausea and hematuria","Burning on urination only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dull constant pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Fever only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Painless hematuria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Severe flank pain radiating to groin with nausea and hematuria', 'Renal colic from acute ureteral obstruction causes severe flank pain radiating to the groin, with nausea, vomiting, and microscopic or macroscopic hematuria.'),
('2699edd2-996b-5ec4-9b76-4cf862f40355'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the role of alpha-blockers (tamsulosin) in the management of ureteral stones?', '["Dissolves stones resulting in decreased functional activity","Prevents stones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facilitates passage of distal ureteral stones (medical expulsive therapy)","Increases stone size without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reduces pain only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitates passage of distal ureteral stones (medical expulsive therapy)', 'Alpha-blockers such as tamsulosin relax ureteral smooth muscle, facilitating spontaneous passage of distal ureteral stones (medical expulsive therapy).'),
('53a1758c-8534-585e-be17-f785dcfbe2ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'ESWL (extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy) is used for:', '["Cystine stones","Only ureteral stones","All stones","Large staghorn calculi","Renal and upper ureteral stones <2 cm"]'::jsonb, 'Renal and upper ureteral stones <2 cm', 'Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy is best for renal and upper ureteral stones under 2 cm; larger, cystine, and staghorn stones respond poorly.'),
('579f5768-f7ec-507c-a269-7f695a5f05d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: High dietary K+ intake stimulates:', '["↓Na+ absorption resulting in decreased functional activity","Aldosterone (directly) → ↑K+ secretion in collecting duct","No change mediated by specific receptor activation","↑K+ absorption without affecting the primary tissue pathway","↓K+ reabsorption leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone (directly) → ↑K+ secretion in collecting duct', 'High potassium intake directly stimulates aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex. Aldosterone then increases K+ secretion in the collecting duct principal cells, promoting potassium excretion and restoring balance.'),
('06676745-1de6-59de-a966-eb2cfff54746'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, The renal threshold for glucose (the plasma glucose level at which glycosuria begins) is approximately:', '["100 mg/dL resulting in decreased functional activity","180 mg/dL (10 mmol/L) plasma glucose","250 mg/dL mediated by specific receptor activation","50 mg/dL without affecting the primary tissue pathway","400 mg/dL leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '180 mg/dL (10 mmol/L) plasma glucose', 'When plasma glucose exceeds about 180 mg/dL, the filtered load surpasses the reabsorptive capacity (TmG 375 mg/min), and glucose spills into the urine. The resulting osmotic diuresis produces polyuria in uncontrolled diabetes.'),
('5911781a-f522-5706-9c4a-2e89271d691c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following are recognized risk factors for renal cell carcinoma (RCC)?', '["Smoking only resulting in decreased functional activity","Obesity only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypertension only mediated by specific receptor activation","Smoking, obesity, hypertension, acquired cystic kidney disease, and von Hippel-Lindau syndrome","Diabetes mellitus only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking, obesity, hypertension, acquired cystic kidney disease, and von Hippel-Lindau syndrome', 'Smoking, obesity, hypertension, acquired cystic kidney disease in CKD, and von Hippel-Lindau syndrome are recognized risk factors for renal cell carcinoma.'),
('f677144d-be5b-55b3-aacf-befcdccc828b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Angiotensin II effects include:', '["Increased thirst and ADH release","All of the above","Vasoconstriction (↑SVR)","Proximal Na+/H+ stimulation","Aldosterone secretion (↑Na+ reabsorption)"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor that raises systemic vascular resistance, stimulates aldosterone and ADH secretion, increases thirst, and enhances proximal tubular Na+/H+ exchange. All of these effects act together to defend blood pressure and blood volume.'),
('92f3489f-4cbe-55f0-904e-7e6720ab5a3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Wilms tumor (nephroblastoma) presents in:', '["Elderly resulting in decreased functional activity","Adults accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adolescents mediated by specific receptor activation","Neonates without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Children (peak 3-4 years) with an abdominal mass"]'::jsonb, 'Children (peak 3-4 years) with an abdominal mass', 'Wilms tumor is the most common renal malignancy of childhood, peaking at 3-4 years of age, and typically presents as an asymptomatic abdominal mass. Neonates, adults, and the elderly are not the typical affected age groups.'),
('d3f470b6-92bd-518b-95fb-7809bd033c4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding transitional cell (urothelial) carcinoma of the urinary tract, which statement is correct?', '["It is a rare tumor of the kidney","It is the most common cancer of the renal pelvis and ureter","It is always benign","It occurs only in the bladder","It does not occur in the urinary tract"]'::jsonb, 'It is the most common cancer of the renal pelvis and ureter', 'Transitional cell (urothelial) carcinoma is the most common malignancy of the renal pelvis and ureter and also accounts for most bladder cancers. Risk factors include smoking, aromatic amines, and cyclophosphamide.'),
('954051f0-a150-5b1f-8099-5ab914b3eb90'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Bladder cancer risk factors include:', '["Only amines resulting in decreased functional activity","Smoking, aromatic amines, cyclophosphamide, and schistosomiasis (squamous)","Only smoking mediated by specific receptor activation","Only schistosomiasis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only cyclophosphamide leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking, aromatic amines, cyclophosphamide, and schistosomiasis (squamous)', 'Bladder cancer is strongly associated with smoking, occupational aromatic amines, cyclophosphamide exposure, and schistosomiasis, which predisposes to squamous cell carcinoma. No single isolated factor accounts for the full risk profile.'),
('ec6012ad-e3c1-52c5-ab65-1a01b028e63c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Urine concentration depends on:', '["ADH (inserts AQP2) + medullary hypertonicity (generated by countercurrent multiplication)","Only medullary gradient accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only GFR mediated by specific receptor activation","Only ADH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only water intake leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADH (inserts AQP2) + medullary hypertonicity (generated by countercurrent multiplication)', 'Concentrated urine requires both a hypertonic medullary interstitium and ADH. ADH inserts aquaporin-2 channels in the collecting duct, allowing water to be drawn out osmotically along the medullary gradient.'),
('01e85cf2-2d4f-50d9-80fa-51a893e5c5d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Prostate cancer screening with PSA:', '["Has benefit resulting in decreased functional activity","Controversial; reduces mortality but with overdiagnosis risk","Is for all men >40 mediated by specific receptor activation","Is universally recommended without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is accurate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Controversial; reduces mortality but with overdiagnosis risk', 'PSA screening modestly reduces prostate cancer mortality but is controversial because of overdiagnosis and overtreatment of indolent tumors. Guidelines therefore recommend shared decision-making rather than universal screening.'),
('afa5aa74-4fce-5f63-b916-3c3cf86ed84c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Glomerular filtration rate (GFR) is normally about:', '["80 mL/min","200 mL/min","125 mL/min","50 mL/min","60 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '125 mL/min', 'Normal GFR is about 125 mL/min (180 L/day) in an average adult, reflecting the large volume of plasma filtered by all glomeruli. Values well below or above this reflect abnormal filtration.'),
('4e694410-3b8b-53cc-9c7d-f50acf74c0ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following increases sodium and water excretion (natriuresis)?', '["Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)","Aldosterone","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","Angiotensin II","Renal sympathetic activation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)', 'ANP, released from atrial myocytes in response to stretch, inhibits sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct and dilates the afferent arteriole, promoting natriuresis. Aldosterone, ADH, angiotensin II, and sympathetic activity all promote salt and water retention.'),
('2a6b5eb3-ea3d-5be7-8862-c1580d38a3fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes pressure natriuresis?', '["Increased renal perfusion pressure directly increases sodium excretion","Increased sympathetic tone increases sodium excretion","Aldosterone increases sodium excretion","ADH promotes sodium loss","Angiotensin II promotes natriuresis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased renal perfusion pressure directly increases sodium excretion', 'Pressure natriuresis is the direct relationship between renal perfusion pressure and sodium excretion; higher pressure suppresses tubular sodium reabsorption. Sympathetic activity, aldosterone, ADH, and angiotensin II all promote sodium and water retention, the opposite response.'),
('82a28249-fb96-5ea6-ba56-b0c01ef3f9e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Renal blood flow (RBF) is about:', '["600 mL/min","1800 mL/min","500 mL/min","1200 mL/min (20-25% of CO)","700 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '1200 mL/min (20-25% of CO)', 'The kidneys receive about 1200 mL/min of blood, roughly 20-25% of cardiac output. This is disproportionately large relative to kidney weight and is required to support filtration and clearance.'),
('792ffe8c-9495-59d3-a892-72304ba1a108'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: During prolonged fasting, the renal cortex contributes to glucose homeostasis by:', '["Producing glucose from lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% during prolonged fasting and generating bicarbonate in acidosis","Secreting insulin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Storing glycogen mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Oxidizing glucose exclusively without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Producing ketone bodies leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Producing glucose from lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% during prolonged fasting and generating bicarbonate in acidosis', 'Renal gluconeogenesis uses lactate, glutamine, and glycerol, contributing up to 20-40% of glucose during prolonged fasting. In acidosis, glutamine metabolism both produces glucose and generates bicarbonate for acid-base balance.'),
('89949321-1fe6-544d-993b-4f50ad1b1a4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following statements about atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) is correct?', '["It is released by atrial myocytes in response to stretch and promotes natriuresis","It is released by the posterior pituitary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","It stimulates aldosterone secretion mediated by specific receptor activation","It increases renin release without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It promotes sodium and water retention leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It is released by atrial myocytes in response to stretch and promotes natriuresis', 'ANP is secreted by atrial myocytes when atrial pressure rises; it increases GFR and inhibits sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct, promoting natriuresis and opposing the RAAS. It suppresses renin and aldosterone release.'),
('5bfd4aea-3886-5bee-8bb7-45d58da830ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: According to Starling forces, fluid is:', '["Filtered at the arterial end and largely reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillary; excess (2-4 L/day) returns via lymphatics","Reabsorbed at the arterial end accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Filtered equally along the entire capillary mediated by specific receptor activation","Never filtered without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Returned to circulation only by veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Filtered at the arterial end and largely reabsorbed at the venous end of the capillary; excess (2-4 L/day) returns via lymphatics', 'Hydrostatic pressure exceeds oncotic pressure at the arterial end, favoring filtration, while the balance reverses at the venous end, favoring reabsorption. The small net excess of about 2-4 L/day is returned by lymphatics; imbalance causes edema.'),
('f5d1d513-d393-5201-94d0-c1ddfcc7ece9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Aldosterone acts on principal cells by:', '["↑K+ secretion only resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting Na+/K+ ATPase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binding mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) → ↑ENaC and ROMK → ↑Na+ reabsorption","Stimulating NCC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking ENaC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) → ↑ENaC and ROMK → ↑Na+ reabsorption', 'Aldosterone binds the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor in principal cells and increases apical ENaC and ROMK expression. This enhances sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion, raising blood pressure and lowering serum potassium.'),
('f7dc9bfd-ccfd-5ebe-a865-d0389de5a48f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Angiotensin II increases proximal tubular sodium reabsorption mainly by:', '["Stimulating apical NHE3 (Na+/H+ exchanger)","Opening apical ENaC channels","Activating the V2 receptor for water","Inhibiting Na+/K+-ATPase","Blocking the sodium-glucose cotransporter"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating apical NHE3 (Na+/H+ exchanger)', 'Angiotensin II enhances proximal tubular sodium reabsorption by stimulating the apical Na+/H+ exchanger NHE3 and basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase. ENaC is the principal-cell target of aldosterone, while V2 receptors mediate ADH''s water effect.'),
('95d8561f-b8ee-5656-acf7-777591ee4125'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following hormones decreases sodium reabsorption and promotes natriuresis?', '["Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)","Aldosterone","Angiotensin II","Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)","Cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)', 'ANP inhibits sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct and increases GFR, producing natriuresis and diuresis. Aldosterone, angiotensin II, ADH, and cortisol all promote sodium or water retention.'),
('23d97c24-6947-50ca-b71d-7b150eb62e33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Renal sympathetic innervation increases sodium reabsorption through:', '["beta1-receptor stimulation of renin release and direct proximal tubular sodium reabsorption","V2-receptor activation in the collecting duct accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","alpha1-mediated afferent arteriolar vasodilation mediated by specific receptor activation","Inhibition of the macula densa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Stimulation of ANP release leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'beta1-receptor stimulation of renin release and direct proximal tubular sodium reabsorption', 'Renal sympathetic nerves increase renin secretion via beta1 receptors on juxtaglomerular cells and also directly stimulate proximal tubular sodium reabsorption. This supports sodium conservation during hypotension or stress.'),
('0c802503-2a21-5533-aea4-92866d0bf9d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis is characterized by:', '["Impaired H+ secretion in the collecting duct, urine pH >5.5, hypokalemia, and nephrocalcinosis","Proximal bicarbonate wasting with a low urine pH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism mediated by specific receptor activation","A water handling defect with dilute urine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A selective defect in potassium secretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired H+ secretion in the collecting duct, urine pH >5.5, hypokalemia, and nephrocalcinosis', 'In distal (type 1) RTA, the collecting duct cannot secrete H+, so urine pH cannot fall below 5.5 despite systemic acidosis; hypokalemia and nephrocalcinosis result from the alkaline, calcium-rich urine.'),
('22ccb069-cb1b-56b9-8bc8-83ff46d5543c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis, the urine pH during systemic acidosis is typically:', '[">5.5","About 4.5","Neutral (7.0)","Random and flow dependent","Alkaline only after bicarbonate loading"]'::jsonb, '>5.5', 'Because the collecting duct cannot secrete H+, the urine cannot be maximally acidified, so urine pH remains above 5.5 even during systemic acidemia. Proximal RTA can acidify urine normally once plasma bicarbonate falls below its reabsorptive threshold.'),
('51c54e29-8ec0-530d-864b-18721f6577b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Nephrocalcinosis is a characteristic complication of which acid-base disorder?', '["Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis","Proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis","Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis', 'Persistent alkaline urine with hypercalciuria and hypocitraturia in distal RTA promotes calcium phosphate precipitation, causing nephrolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis. Type 4 RTA features hyperkalemia without stone disease.'),
('5808cc9f-3f6c-5a3c-8233-420cef65dee4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis is due to:', '["Impaired bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule","Impaired H+ secretion in the collecting duct","Aldosterone deficiency or resistance","Increased ammonium excretion","Impaired urine concentrating ability"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule', 'In type 2 RTA, the proximal tubule fails to reabsorb filtered bicarbonate, wasting HCO3- in urine; when plasma HCO3- falls below the reduced threshold, the distal nephron can still acidify urine. It is often accompanied by other proximal tubule defects (Fanconi syndrome).'),
('26d6e03b-ae85-5101-a2e9-2a276a5d2cb6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, GFR is determined by:', '["Kf (ultrafiltration coefficient) × (Pgc - Pbc - πgc)","Pgc alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kf alone mediated by specific receptor activation","πgc alone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pbc alone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Kf (ultrafiltration coefficient) × (Pgc - Pbc - πgc)', 'GFR equals the ultrafiltration coefficient (Kf) multiplied by net filtration pressure (capillary pressure minus Bowman''s pressure minus oncotic pressure). All four determinants can be altered by disease.'),
('4d6a816b-7fc7-5fe4-89dc-d5161028a2e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the principal cause of NSAID-induced AKI?', '["Inhibition of COX, reducing vasodilatory prostaglandins and causing afferent arteriolar constriction with decreased GFR","Interstitial nephritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papillary necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct tubular toxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glomerulonephritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of COX, reducing vasodilatory prostaglandins and causing afferent arteriolar constriction with decreased GFR', 'NSAIDs block cyclooxygenase, reducing vasodilatory prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2) that maintain afferent arteriolar dilation; the resulting afferent vasoconstriction lowers GFR, especially in volume-depleted states. Interstitial nephritis and papillary necrosis are distinct NSAID nephrotoxicities.'),
('ef89006f-6cdc-5b02-a9af-ef3856cf0c14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, The distal convoluted tubule (DCT) reabsorbs Na+ and Cl- via the:', '["SGLT2 resulting in decreased functional activity","ENaC accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+/H+ exchanger mediated by specific receptor activation","Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) - blocked by thiazide diuretics","NKCC2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) - blocked by thiazide diuretics', 'The distal convoluted tubule reabsorbs sodium and chloride through the apical NCC cotransporter. Thiazide diuretics block NCC, causing natriuresis and increased calcium reabsorption.'),
('793fcbce-7687-5163-8561-805eb8105cc3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Prostaglandin inhibition by NSAIDs produces acute kidney injury by:', '["Inhibition of COX, reducing vasodilatory prostaglandins and causing afferent arteriolar constriction with decreased GFR","Interstitial nephritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papillary necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct tubular toxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glomerulonephritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of COX, reducing vasodilatory prostaglandins and causing afferent arteriolar constriction with decreased GFR', 'Renal prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2) help keep the afferent arteriole dilated when pressure is low. By inhibiting COX, NSAIDs cause afferent vasoconstriction and a drop in GFR, the most common functional form of NSAID-induced AKI.'),
('5856fc34-4367-5027-9eaa-ebfd1ddd3a06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In a patient with reduced effective arterial volume, NSAIDs can precipitate AKI mainly because they:', '["Inhibition of COX, reducing vasodilatory prostaglandins and causing afferent arteriolar constriction with decreased GFR","Interstitial nephritis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Papillary necrosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Direct tubular toxicity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glomerulonephritis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of COX, reducing vasodilatory prostaglandins and causing afferent arteriolar constriction with decreased GFR', 'In low-flow states the afferent arteriole depends on prostaglandin-mediated dilation to maintain GFR. NSAID-induced COX inhibition removes this support, causing afferent constriction and functional AKI that reverses on drug withdrawal.'),
('963e873f-ed6c-5812-91aa-6dfb8037ca50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('e2ce4d9b-bcc8-5e6a-96c5-116e0c9ad017'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: Which transporter mediates NaCl reabsorption in the thick ascending limb?', '["Na+ channel resulting in decreased functional activity","Na+/K+ ATPase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Water channel mediated by specific receptor activation","NKCC2 cotransporter (same as TAL)","Ca2+ channel leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'NKCC2 cotransporter (same as TAL)', 'The thick ascending limb reabsorbs sodium via the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- (NKCC2) cotransporter, the target of loop diuretics. It is also the site where the macula densa senses tubular NaCl.'),
('a997279d-4a2b-59e4-b6f1-77edeb541428'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Edema can result from:', '["Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, decreased plasma oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, or lymphatic obstruction","Only increased oncotic pressure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only reduced capillary pressure mediated by specific receptor activation","Only decreased permeability without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only lymph overproduction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure, decreased plasma oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, or lymphatic obstruction', 'Edema develops when capillary filtration exceeds reabsorption and lymphatic drainage. The four mechanisms are raised capillary hydrostatic pressure, reduced plasma oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, and lymphatic obstruction.'),
('f0364055-187d-5585-bdad-f992dd42940f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease is most commonly caused by a mutation in:', '["PKD1","PKD2","PKHD1","COL4A5","NPHS2"]'::jsonb, 'PKD1', 'PKD1 (chromosome 16) accounts for about 85% of ADPKD cases; PKD2 (chromosome 4) accounts for most of the rest. PKHD1 causes autosomal recessive PKD, while COL4A5 and NPHS2 cause Alport syndrome and nephrotic syndromes.'),
('47fa5bd0-0788-5d4c-afd2-a084d52feecc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Hypertension in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease typically:', '["Develops early, before significant loss of renal function","Appears only at end-stage renal disease","Is unrelated to cyst growth","Is caused by reduced renin release","Is rare before age 50"]'::jsonb, 'Develops early, before significant loss of renal function', 'Hypertension often appears early in ADPKD, driven by cyst-induced renal ischemia and activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and it accelerates cyst growth and renal decline.'),
('41744077-b040-54e0-83be-8c8981ca86e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, The filtration fraction (GFR/RPF) is:', '["RBF/GFR resulting in decreased functional activity","GFR/RBF accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","RPF/GFR mediated by specific receptor activation","GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)","FF = GFR - RPF leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GFR/RPF (normal 0.18-0.20)', 'The filtration fraction is the ratio GFR/RPF, normally 0.18-0.20. A high FF (e.g., in nephrotic syndrome) raises peritubular oncotic pressure and promotes sodium retention.'),
('8bf99a08-c2ab-5293-95e7-ca0bf8e3e686'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: The renal corpuscle consists of:', '["The proximal tubule resulting in decreased functional activity","Only the glomerular capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only Bowman''s capsule mediated by specific receptor activation","The juxtaglomerular apparatus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Glomerulus (capillary tuft) and Bowman''s capsule (parietal and visceral layers)"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerulus (capillary tuft) and Bowman''s capsule (parietal and visceral layers)', 'The renal corpuscle is formed by the glomerular capillary tuft surrounded by Bowman''s capsule. The visceral layer of Bowman''s capsule is made of podocytes, and filtrate passes through this structure into the tubular system.'),
('2f871cbd-2b7d-5798-9b47-695298fbbbfb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Afferent arteriole resistance determines:', '["Venous drainage","Tubular reabsorption","Bowman pressure","Efferent resistance","RBF and Pgc (↓RA → ↑RBF ↑Pgc → ↑GFR)"]'::jsonb, 'RBF and Pgc (↓RA → ↑RBF ↑Pgc → ↑GFR)', 'Afferent arteriolar resistance is the main regulator of renal blood flow and glomerular capillary pressure. Dilating the afferent arteriole increases RBF, Pgc, and GFR, while constricting it has the opposite effects.'),
('90fa6950-a7eb-5f62-8147-1803c7bb626c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which protein is defective in autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease?', '["Fibrocystin (encoded by PKHD1)","Polycystin-1 (encoded by PKD1)","Polycystin-2 (encoded by PKD2)","Nephrin","Podocin"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrocystin (encoded by PKHD1)', 'PKHD1 encodes fibrocystin, a protein located in primary cilia of collecting duct epithelium; its loss causes ARPKD. Polycystin-1 and -2 mutations cause the dominant form, while nephrin and podocin mutations cause congenital nephrotic syndrome.'),
('1cd47f8c-b4fd-5cef-864b-d6326cc05238'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: The myogenic response of the afferent arteriole:', '["Vascular smooth muscle contracts in response to stretch (increased transmural pressure); independent of TGF; important for RBF autoregulation","Depends on tubuloglomerular feedback accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Causes vasodilation when stretched mediated by specific receptor activation","Occurs only in efferent arterioles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Is not blocked by Ca2+ channel blockers leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vascular smooth muscle contracts in response to stretch (increased transmural pressure); independent of TGF; important for RBF autoregulation', 'The myogenic response is the intrinsic contraction of vascular smooth muscle when stretched by rising pressure, and relaxation when pressure falls. It is independent of tubuloglomerular feedback, depends on Ca2+ influx, and helps autoregulate renal blood flow.'),
('8c6b236f-7a20-50bd-82ac-21d3be7a5704'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'ARPKD typically differs from ADPKD by:', '["Presenting in infancy with massively enlarged kidneys and hepatic fibrosis","Presenting in adult life with hypertension","Following an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern","Producing cysts only in the renal cortex","Sparing the liver completely"]'::jsonb, 'Presenting in infancy with massively enlarged kidneys and hepatic fibrosis', 'ARPKD presents in neonates and infants with bilateral enlarged echogenic kidneys (often with oligohydramnios) and congenital hepatic fibrosis, whereas ADPKD presents later in life. Both conditions cause renal cysts, but the inheritance and hepatic findings differ.'),
('19a08115-4f20-5b8c-9cc1-824c6fb88824'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which feature most strongly suggests autosomal recessive rather than autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?', '["Presentation in infancy with enlarged kidneys and hepatic fibrosis","Hypertension first diagnosed after age 40","Cysts found incidentally in an adult","Family history affecting three generations","Cysts confined to the renal cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Presentation in infancy with enlarged kidneys and hepatic fibrosis', 'ARPKD manifests in infancy with massive renal enlargement and hepatic fibrosis, while ADPKD typically presents in adulthood. An affected parent (rather than only siblings) and adult-onset cysts favor the dominant form.'),
('11165a56-050a-5140-a2e5-8d55547c7669'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The approximate total body calcium content of a normal adult is:', '["About 1000 g, with 99% stored in bone","About 1000 mg, with 90% in blood","About 10 g, mostly in muscle","About 5 g, with 40% in serum","About 500 g, with 50% in bone"]'::jsonb, 'About 1000 g, with 99% stored in bone', 'A normal adult contains roughly 1000 g (1 kg) of calcium, about 99% in bone as hydroxyapatite. Only about 1% is in blood and soft tissue, where free (ionized) calcium regulates neuromuscular excitability.'),
('fb6d8b6a-3f25-57fa-8176-f7e598df7930'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the approximate total body calcium in an adult?', '["About 1000 g (1 kg)","About 1000 mg","About 10 g","About 100 g","About 5 kg"]'::jsonb, 'About 1000 g (1 kg)', 'Total body calcium is approximately 1000 g (1 kg). The vast majority (99%) is in bone, while the small extracellular pool of ionized calcium is tightly regulated by PTH, calcitriol, and calcitonin.'),
('3b82c41a-5229-56ad-9be4-ac9db9091bf1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Plasma osmolality is maintained by the:', '["ADH-thirst feedback loop; osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus with thirst threshold 290-295 mOsm/kg and ADH threshold 280-285 mOsm/kg","Aldosterone-thirst loop accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Renin release alone mediated by specific receptor activation","Baroreceptors overriding osmoreceptors at <5% volume loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Kidneys without any hormone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ADH-thirst feedback loop; osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus with thirst threshold 290-295 mOsm/kg and ADH threshold 280-285 mOsm/kg', 'Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus drive ADH release (threshold 280-285 mOsm/kg) and thirst (threshold 290-295 mOsm/kg). Even a 1% change in osmolality significantly alters ADH, while baroreceptors override osmoreceptors only after >10% volume loss.'),
('9414fd52-4b01-583c-8726-4439c7cd5dbf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, Net filtration pressure across the glomerular capillary equals:', '["Glomerular capillary pressure - (Bowman capsule pressure + oncotic pressure)","Oncotic pressure only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Net hydrostatic pressure only mediated by specific receptor activation","Capillary pressure only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bowman pressure only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular capillary pressure - (Bowman capsule pressure + oncotic pressure)', 'Net filtration pressure is the glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus the sum of Bowman''s capsule pressure and plasma oncotic pressure. It is the driving force for filtrate formation.'),
('23136ff7-97ff-5a7c-a0af-6e695a042717'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'A corrected calcium measurement is most important when:', '["Serum albumin is abnormal","Serum creatinine is elevated","Serum potassium is high","Serum phosphate is low","Urine output is reduced"]'::jsonb, 'Serum albumin is abnormal', 'Because about 40% of plasma calcium is bound to albumin, hypoalbuminemia lowers total calcium without changing ionized calcium. The corrected value is calculated as measured Ca + 0.8 x (4 - albumin).'),
('41bf7dfd-56c0-59b1-a24c-8101abb2a980'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'FGF23 promotes phosphaturia by:', '["Decreasing NaPi-2a and NaPi-2c cotransporters in the proximal tubule","Stimulating renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase","Increasing intestinal phosphate absorption","Acting on the collecting duct","Stimulating Na+/K+-ATPase"]'::jsonb, 'Decreasing NaPi-2a and NaPi-2c cotransporters in the proximal tubule', 'FGF23, secreted by osteocytes, internalizes NaPi-2a and NaPi-2c sodium-phosphate cotransporters in the proximal tubule, reducing phosphate reabsorption and increasing urinary phosphate excretion.'),
('9002c8b1-194e-5bf4-a450-014e3fb28d29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Normal saline (0.9% NaCl) has an osmolarity of:', '["400 mOsm/L","308 mOsm/L","250 mOsm/L","154 mOsm/L","616 mOsm/L"]'::jsonb, '308 mOsm/L', '0.9% saline contains 154 mmol/L of NaCl, giving an osmolarity of about 308 mOsm/L (two osmotically active particles per NaCl). It is therefore approximately isotonic with plasma.'),
('ef7465f4-c2db-5e25-a922-880e89dfa9ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Elevated FGF23 levels are characteristically found in:', '["X-linked hypophosphatemia (XLH)","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Hypercalcemia of malignancy","Vitamin D intoxication","Milk-alkali syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked hypophosphatemia (XLH)', 'In XLH, loss-of-function mutations of PHEX raise FGF23, causing renal phosphate wasting and low 1,25(OH)2D with rickets or osteomalacia. FGF23 excess is not characteristic of hyperparathyroidism, hypercalcemia, or vitamin D toxicity.'),
('c552fe62-e551-5ac9-b697-4915baa3a540'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following is a direct renal effect of FGF23?', '["Increased urinary phosphate excretion (phosphaturia)","Increased sodium reabsorption","Increased potassium excretion","Stimulation of renin release","Inhibition of water reabsorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increased urinary phosphate excretion (phosphaturia)', 'FGF23 directly increases urinary phosphate excretion by removing NaPi-2a/2c cotransporters from the proximal tubular brush border. It simultaneously lowers 1,25(OH)2D synthesis.'),
('a4299c75-97cf-50d1-b7de-2f2ba044ea3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: Amiloride acts on:', '["NKCC2 in TAL resulting in decreased functional activity","NCC in DCT accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","ENaC in the collecting duct (principal cells)","CA in PT without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Na+/K+ ATPase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'ENaC in the collecting duct (principal cells)', 'Amiloride directly blocks the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) on the apical membrane of collecting duct principal cells. It is a potassium-sparing diuretic.'),
('45bd696a-ce5b-52d1-8aa0-0938bba91498'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which urinary buffer is most important for titratable acidity (H+ excretion with non-ammonium buffers)?', '["Phosphate (HPO4^2-/H2PO4^-)","Bicarbonate accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Creatinine mediated by specific receptor activation","Urate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sulfate leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphate (HPO4^2-/H2PO4^-)', 'Phosphate is the dominant urinary buffer contributing to titratable acidity; HPO4^2- accepts secreted H+ to form H2PO4^- and is excreted. Ammonia provides the separate, highly regulated ammonium excretion pathway.'),
('9f397b12-a1ea-5a15-b326-7313b6444210'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: The slit diaphragm between podocyte foot processes contains:', '["Nephrin, podocin, and CD2AP as key structural proteins","Only actin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only collagen mediated by specific receptor activation","Only laminin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only tubulin leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nephrin, podocin, and CD2AP as key structural proteins', 'The podocyte slit diaphragm is composed of nephrin, podocin, and CD2AP. Mutations in nephrin cause congenital nephrotic syndrome, and these proteins are reduced in acquired podocyte injury.'),
('fadc5231-3790-5484-8a50-8fecd8f8d37e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: The clearance of inulin measures:', '["GFR","Renal plasma flow","Tubular secretion","Free water clearance","Renal blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'GFR', 'Inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, so its clearance equals the glomerular filtration rate. It is the gold-standard method for measuring GFR.'),
('6faa35bc-8d3c-50e4-a142-f4bef6f63e8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('c26be178-7c5a-5861-84b9-9bd32476ea5b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The metabolic acidosis of diabetic ketoacidosis is classified as:', '["High anion gap metabolic acidosis","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Hypochloremic metabolic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'High anion gap metabolic acidosis', 'Ketoacids are unmeasured anions that raise the anion gap while consuming bicarbonate. Renal compensation increases ammonium excretion to eliminate the acid load, which helps distinguish this from chronic diarrhea-induced hyperchloremic acidosis.'),
('81a1b40c-bdda-5429-a4bf-be153460df1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The osmolal gap is calculated as:', '["Measured osmolality minus calculated osmolality (2 x Na + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8)","Measured osmolality plus calculated osmolality accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","2 x Na minus (Cl + HCO3) mediated by specific receptor activation","Calculated osmolality minus measured osmolality without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Na + Cl + HCO3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Measured osmolality minus calculated osmolality (2 x Na + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8)', 'Osmolal gap = measured serum osmolality - calculated osmolality, normally <10 mOsm/kg. An elevated gap indicates unmeasured osmotically active solutes such as methanol, ethylene glycol, isopropanol, or ethanol.'),
('1b58197a-4d5c-54c8-b6c0-15f332eb6272'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Aldosterone acts on principal cells by:', '["↑K+ secretion only resulting in decreased functional activity","Inhibiting Na+/K+ ATPase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Binding mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) → ↑ENaC and ROMK → ↑Na+ reabsorption","Stimulating NCC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking ENaC leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding mineralocorticoid receptor (MR) → ↑ENaC and ROMK → ↑Na+ reabsorption', 'Aldosterone binds the intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor in principal cells and increases apical ENaC and ROMK expression. This enhances sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion, raising blood pressure and lowering serum potassium.'),
('82402c5b-96ab-5c4d-8f9d-a25b4693269d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Macula densa is located:', '["Proximal tubule resulting in decreased functional activity","Glomerulus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Collecting duct mediated by specific receptor activation","Bowman capsule without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In the thick ascending limb at the junction with the glomerular afferent arteriole"]'::jsonb, 'In the thick ascending limb at the junction with the glomerular afferent arteriole', 'The macula densa is a specialized plaque of cells in the thick ascending limb where it passes between the afferent and efferent arterioles of its own glomerulus. It senses tubular NaCl concentration to regulate GFR and renin release.'),
('e229f748-833b-5f15-a63b-027ad7cb71c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'A 60-year-old male with chronic diarrhea presents with ABG: pH 7.25, pCO2 30, HCO3 12. Which diagnosis is most consistent with these findings?', '["Normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Respiratory alkalosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Respiratory acidosis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Metabolic alkalosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis', 'Chronic diarrhea causes loss of bicarbonate-rich intestinal fluid, producing a hyperchloremic (normal anion gap) metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation, as shown by the low pCO2. Ketoacidosis and lactic acidosis would widen the anion gap.'),
('0bd090c9-26fd-5770-acaa-9ae4446d9cdc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which toxic alcohol ingestion classically produces both an elevated osmolal gap and a high anion gap metabolic acidosis?', '["Ethylene glycol (antifreeze)","Ethanol accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Isopropanol mediated by specific receptor activation","Acetaminophen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cocaine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ethylene glycol (antifreeze)', 'Ethylene glycol raises the osmolal gap because it is osmotically active, and its metabolite glycolate causes a high anion gap metabolic acidosis with calcium oxalate crystalluria. Methanol behaves similarly via formate; isopropanol raises the osmolal gap without an anion gap acidosis.'),
('32febbfd-bb1e-5cd6-a472-0eeaefc96986'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'High anion gap metabolic acidosis is caused by which of the following?', '["Lactic acidosis","Chronic diarrhea","Proximal renal tubular acidosis","Acetazolamide therapy","Ureterosigmoidostomy"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acidosis', 'Lactic acidosis, ketoacidosis, uremia, and toxic alcohol ingestions generate unmeasured anions that raise the anion gap. Diarrhea, proximal RTA, acetazolamide, and ureterosigmoidostomy cause hyperchloremic (normal anion gap) acidosis.'),
('d7d3d9f0-4dfd-5fd5-a43b-5618a8ae3453'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What accumulates during tissue hypoperfusion and causes metabolic acidosis?', '["Lactic acid","Bicarbonate","Chloride","Potassium","Ammonium"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acid', 'Tissue hypoperfusion forces anaerobic glycolysis, generating lactic acid; the accompanying H+ consumes bicarbonate, producing a high anion gap metabolic acidosis (lactic acidosis).'),
('2f751bc7-8f02-5e6b-90b2-590a250d56a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which condition characteristically causes a high anion gap metabolic acidosis?', '["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Chronic diarrhea","Proximal renal tubular acidosis","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor therapy","Chronic vomiting"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetic ketoacidosis', 'Diabetic ketoacidosis produces unmeasured ketoacid anions, widening the anion gap. Chronic diarrhea, proximal RTA, and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) acidosis, while vomiting produces alkalosis.'),
('cbbaed59-6194-5548-a766-7a87ea42aef4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, The thin ascending limb of the loop of Henle is:', '["Impermeable to water","Permeable to water","Impermeable to NaCl","Permeable to NaCl (passive diffusion)","A site of active solute transport"]'::jsonb, 'Impermeable to water', 'The thin ascending limb is impermeable to water but permeable to NaCl. Sodium chloride diffuses passively out of the lumen, diluting the tubular fluid and contributing to the medullary osmotic gradient.'),
('443066e0-f02b-597f-93bf-1131fa99117c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: What is the effective pore size of the glomerular filtration barrier?', '["15 nm","8 nm (or 70 kDa)","4 nm","1 nm","20 nm"]'::jsonb, '8 nm (or 70 kDa)', 'The filtration barrier has an effective pore size of about 8 nm, corresponding to a molecular weight cutoff of roughly 70 kDa. Larger or negatively charged molecules are restricted from the filtrate.'),
('427ff00e-22ef-5d8f-a1a3-a521f896fc4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which spinal segments provide parasympathetic innervation to the bladder?', '["S2-S4 (pelvic nerve)","T10-L2 (hypogastric nerve)","L4-S1","C8-T1","T1-T4"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4 (pelvic nerve)', 'Parasympathetic fibers of the pelvic nerve arise from S2-S4 and release acetylcholine onto M3 receptors, contracting the detrusor during micturition. The sympathetic outflow to the bladder comes from T10-L2.'),
('07312b22-9c91-5a9d-80ee-4df76ff3987b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Obligatory urine volume is:', '["1 L/day resulting in decreased functional activity","400-500 mL/day (minimum volume to excrete solutes at maximum concentration)","3 L/day mediated by specific receptor activation","100 mL/day without affecting the primary tissue pathway","2 L/day leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '400-500 mL/day (minimum volume to excrete solutes at maximum concentration)', 'Obligatory urine volume is the 400-500 mL/day minimum needed to excrete the daily solute load at maximum urine concentration. A lower volume cannot be achieved without solute accumulation.'),
('0da7c99b-995e-53af-86bf-1f2ae1bededd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Sympathetic innervation of the bladder arises from which spinal segments?', '["T10-L2 (hypogastric nerve)","S2-S4 (pelvic nerve)","C8-T1","L4-S1","Craniosacral outflow via the vagus"]'::jsonb, 'T10-L2 (hypogastric nerve)', 'The hypogastric nerve, arising from T10-L2, provides sympathetic innervation that promotes urine storage by relaxing the detrusor (beta-3 receptors) and contracting the internal sphincter (alpha-1 receptors).'),
('77c78759-2720-5a96-b6b1-df67c617ed55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which receptor mediates detrusor contraction during micturition?', '["Muscarinic M3 receptor activated by acetylcholine from the pelvic nerve","Alpha-1-adrenergic receptor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Beta-3-adrenergic receptor mediated by specific receptor activation","Nicotinic receptor at the motor endplate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dopamine D1 receptor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Muscarinic M3 receptor activated by acetylcholine from the pelvic nerve', 'During micturition, pelvic parasympathetic fibers release acetylcholine, which acts on M3 muscarinic receptors to contract the detrusor. Alpha-1 receptors contract the internal sphincter and beta-3 receptors relax the detrusor, both promoting storage.'),
('1b8be358-faa4-5b72-b3fa-62f087fb5985'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in Renal Pt2: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('e4587fab-ec36-59e9-8542-1d7fc45a5bc8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'In the study of Renal Pt2, Calcium oxalate stones are associated with:', '["Alkaline urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperuricosuria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Magnesium deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia","Cystinuria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia', 'Calcium oxalate stones form when urine calcium or oxalate is high or citrate (a stone inhibitor) is low. Hyperuricosuria can also promote calcium oxalate stones by heterogeneous nucleation.'),
('3d9a5fb4-1633-5673-ac45-2687486267cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Erythropoietin (EPO) is produced by:', '["Macula densa resulting in decreased functional activity","Peritubular fibroblasts in the renal cortex","Mesangial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Podocytes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","JG cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peritubular fibroblasts in the renal cortex', 'Erythropoietin is synthesized by peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the renal cortex in response to hypoxia. Reduced production in chronic kidney disease causes anemia.'),
('a57d1b53-17ce-50d9-bc97-2938c470db1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Renal Pt2: Mannitol acts as an:', '["Osmotic diuretic (freely filtered, not reabsorbed → osmotic water retention → diuresis; used for cerebral edema)","Loop diuretic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thiazide diuretic mediated by specific receptor activation","K+-sparing diuretic without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Osmotic diuretic (freely filtered, not reabsorbed → osmotic water retention → diuresis; used for cerebral edema)', 'Mannitol is an osmotic diuretic that is freely filtered but not reabsorbed, so it osmotically retains water in the tubule and increases urine output. It is used to reduce cerebral edema and is contraindicated in anuria.'),
('7d4e4a70-0cf8-560c-be7e-61028195d565'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, Osmotic diuretics (e.g., mannitol) act by:', '["Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase resulting in decreased functional activity","Increasing tubular fluid osmolarity, reducing water reabsorption","Antagonizing ADH mediated by specific receptor activation","Blocking aldosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blocking ion transporters leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing tubular fluid osmolarity, reducing water reabsorption', 'Mannitol is filtered but not reabsorbed, increasing the osmolarity of tubular fluid. Water is retained osmotically in the lumen, producing a diuresis without major electrolyte loss.'),
('22f8de59-e8bf-57e4-8f1b-852511590768'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following best describes free water clearance?', '["V - Cosm (the rate of solute-free water excretion)","Uosm xV / Posm","The clearance of a substance that is freely filtered and not reabsorbed","The renal plasma flow","The filtration fraction"]'::jsonb, 'V - Cosm (the rate of solute-free water excretion)', 'Free water clearance quantifies the volume of solute-free water excreted or reabsorbed by the kidney, computed as V - Cosm. It is positive in water diuresis and negative in water conservation.'),
('6d325ae2-c62e-5936-90cd-eb9a4a1b4a31'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'What is the role of the macula densa in renal autoregulation?', '["Secretes erythropoietin resulting in decreased functional activity","Senses NaCl concentration and signals afferent arteriole to constrict or dilate","Reabsorbs glucose via SGLT2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Secretes aldosterone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Filters plasma at the glomerulus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Senses NaCl concentration and signals afferent arteriole to constrict or dilate', 'Macula densa cells detect NaCl concentration in the distal tubular fluid and, through tubuloglomerular feedback, adjust afferent arteriolar tone to maintain a stable GFR. Low NaCl delivery promotes afferent dilation and renin release.'),
('8a725718-64fb-5b8d-960d-912879d7008b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Ammoniagenesis in the kidney:', '["Produces NH3 from glutamine in the proximal tubule, aiding H+ excretion and generating new bicarbonate","Produces urea accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Is inhibited by acidosis mediated by specific receptor activation","Occurs mainly in the collecting duct without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Does not affect acid excretion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Produces NH3 from glutamine in the proximal tubule, aiding H+ excretion and generating new bicarbonate', 'The proximal tubule metabolizes glutamine to NH3 and α-ketoglutarate; NH3 diffuses into the lumen and traps H+ as NH4+, while α-ketoglutarate yields new bicarbonate. Production rises within hours to days in acidosis.'),
('b02f365c-68e8-56a9-b02b-12a3743fba2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Angiotensin II preferentially constricts which renal arteriole to maintain glomerular filtration?', '["Afferent arteriole","Efferent arteriole","Interlobular artery","Arcuate artery","Peritubular capillaries"]'::jsonb, 'Efferent arteriole', 'Angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, raising glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure to maintain GFR and filtration fraction when renal perfusion pressure falls. It also constricts the afferent arteriole at high concentrations.'),
('4a445f2a-870e-5369-98f0-ae9c38ca9d91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, Fanconi syndrome is best described as:', '["Generalized proximal tubular dysfunction causing glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonaturia","Isolated distal tubular defect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary collecting duct disorder mediated by specific receptor activation","Glomerular basement membrane disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Isolated hyperoxaluria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Generalized proximal tubular dysfunction causing glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and bicarbonaturia', 'Fanconi syndrome is a generalized proximal tubular transport defect producing glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, and proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis with bicarbonaturia. Causes include inherited disorders, heavy metals, and myeloma.'),
('f50066fa-8176-5576-ada6-f4f02d1a9e01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus plays a critical role in regulating which of the following?', '["Glomerular filtration rate and systemic blood pressure","Tubular reabsorption of glucose","Urine concentration in the collecting duct","Acid-base balance in the proximal tubule","Calcium reabsorption in the loop of Henle"]'::jsonb, 'Glomerular filtration rate and systemic blood pressure', 'The juxtaglomerular apparatus regulates GFR through tubuloglomerular feedback and systemic blood pressure through the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. It does not primarily govern glucose, acid-base, or calcium handling.'),
('f721a6b9-a108-5440-847b-c0af2eb1e4df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which option correctly describes podocytes?', '["Interstitial cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Endothelial cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mesangial cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus that form slit diaphragms","Parietal epithelial cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus that form slit diaphragms', 'Podocytes are visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus whose foot processes form slit diaphragms, a key size- and charge-selective component of the filtration barrier. Damage to podocytes causes proteinuria.'),
('9c7096c7-1025-53b2-a152-809349c5d8a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Renal Pt2: Calcium oxalate stones are associated with:', '["Alkaline urine resulting in decreased functional activity","Hyperuricosuria accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Magnesium deficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia","Cystinuria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria, hypocitraturia', 'Calcium oxalate stones form when urine calcium or oxalate is high or citrate (a stone inhibitor) is low. Hyperuricosuria can also promote calcium oxalate stones by heterogeneous nucleation.'),
('f0758dc1-a007-50f3-99a4-23fa66af0755'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'Which of the following correctly describes podocytes?', '["Visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus","Endothelial cells of the glomerular capillaries","Mesangial cells of the glomerulus","Parietal epithelial cells lining Bowman''s capsule","Interstitial cells of the renal medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral epithelial cells of the glomerulus', 'Podocytes are the visceral epithelial cells covering the glomerular basement membrane and forming slit diaphragms. Parietal epithelial cells line Bowman''s capsule, and mesangial cells provide structural support.'),
('e024711d-64a2-51db-8195-04591a4ac305'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal Body Fluids - Renal Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of Renal Pt2, Weak acids are trapped in:', '["Alkaline urine","Acidic stomach","Blood pH 7.4","Duodenum","Jejunum"]'::jsonb, 'Alkaline urine', 'Weak acids become ionised (trapped) in alkaline environments.'),
('71c832a9-3189-5b16-a9de-dd1d45ce1c78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the normal anatomical extent of the spinal cord in adults?', '["Foramen magnum to L1-L2","C1 to L5","C1 to S5","Medulla to sacrum","Brainstem to coccyx"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen magnum to L1-L2', 'In adults the spinal cord extends from the foramen magnum to the L1-L2 vertebral level; the lumbar and sacral nerve roots continue below this as the cauda equina.'),
('835fc3c5-ebd3-59ed-9b63-fd4681da7527'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The white matter of the spinal cord contains:', '["Cell bodies resulting in decreased functional activity","Only motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ascending (sensory) and descending (motor) tracts","Interneurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ascending (sensory) and descending (motor) tracts', 'The spinal white matter is made up of myelinated axons organized into ascending (sensory) and descending (motor) tracts; cell bodies lie in the central gray matter.'),
('59e24919-4995-5874-8c72-87ff12dcec6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The spinothalamic tract crosses (decussates):', '["Anterior white commissure within 1-2 segments of entry","In the thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","In the pons mediated by specific receptor activation","In the medulla without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the pyramidal decussation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior white commissure within 1-2 segments of entry', 'Second-order neurons carrying pain and temperature cross in the anterior white commissure within one to two segments of their entry into the cord, then ascend in the contralateral spinothalamic tract to the ventral posterior lateral nucleus of the thalamus.'),
('b99afdb9-da3c-5ead-9a79-a1f7cff487af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The spinocerebellar tracts carry:', '["Vibration resulting in decreased functional activity","Unconscious proprioception from muscle spindles and GTO to the cerebellum","Pain mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor commands without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fine touch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unconscious proprioception from muscle spindles and GTO to the cerebellum', 'Dorsal and ventral spinocerebellar tracts transmit unconscious proprioceptive signals from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs to the cerebellum for coordination of movement.'),
('26e35cff-4524-5736-960d-570eb37408d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The lateral corticospinal tract (crossed) controls:', '["Neck resulting in decreased functional activity","Trunk accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Axial muscles mediated by specific receptor activation","Proximal muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Distal limb muscles (fine motor control)"]'::jsonb, 'Distal limb muscles (fine motor control)', 'The lateral corticospinal tract decussates at the pyramids and, through its contacts with lower motor neurons, mediates fine, skilled voluntary movements of distal limb muscles.'),
('220a16d4-098b-5461-ac92-5b74ee5d4c92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The corticobulbar tract:', '["Travels only to the spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Runs from the motor cortex to cranial nerve motor nuclei (bilateral except lower face)","Projects to the basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Innervates only limb muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Projects to the cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Runs from the motor cortex to cranial nerve motor nuclei (bilateral except lower face)', 'The corticobulbar tract connects the motor cortex with the lower motor neuron nuclei of the cranial nerves; most are innervated bilaterally, while the subnucleus for the lower facial muscles receives only contralateral input.'),
('7164cf13-9c38-5d2b-a126-1152b9bff906'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following is characteristic of an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion?', '["Spasticity","Muscle atrophy","Flaccid paralysis","Areflexia","Fasciculations"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity', 'UMN lesions remove descending inhibition, producing spastic paralysis, hyperreflexia, clonus, and an extensor plantar response; atrophy, fasciculations, flaccidity, and areflexia indicate lower motor neuron damage.'),
('06f090eb-0b59-5b5f-8bcc-0d33b6e1fb8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the Babinski sign and what does it indicate?', '["Normal plantar flexion response resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsiflexion of the big toe with fanning of the other toes on stroking the sole (UMN lesion)","Plantar flexion of the toes mediated by specific receptor activation","Withdrawal of the limb from the stimulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence of any plantar response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflexion of the big toe with fanning of the other toes on stroking the sole (UMN lesion)', 'Stroking the lateral sole normally produces plantar flexion of the toes; dorsiflexion of the big toe with fanning of the remaining toes indicates an upper motor neuron (corticospinal) lesion.'),
('63b6c385-b160-57f0-aa4b-5192e420f6bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 70-year-old female develops left-sided weakness after a stroke. Which cortical region is most likely damaged?', '["Precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4)","Postcentral gyrus","Prefrontal cortex","Occipital lobe","Superior temporal gyrus"]'::jsonb, 'Precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4)', 'The primary motor cortex lies in the precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4); its upper motor neurons drive contralateral movement, so damaging it produces contralateral muscle weakness.'),
('c312dff7-4401-5d1b-8558-d59989345fe9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The premotor cortex (area 6) is involved in:', '["Sensory integration resulting in decreased functional activity","Planning and programming of movements (preparation before execution)","Pain processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Execution of movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proprioception leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Planning and programming of movements (preparation before execution)', 'The premotor cortex and supplementary motor area plan and program voluntary movements in advance of execution, organizing sequences and preparing the motor cortex.'),
('c29fa0e7-c4c6-5694-9cf0-2397cf408158'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 35-year-old male presents with progressive muscle weakness, atrophy, and fasciculations. Which finding is most consistent with his lower motor neuron lesion?', '["Hyporeflexia","Hyperreflexia","Positive Babinski sign","Spasticity","Clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Hyporeflexia', 'Lower motor neuron disease produces weakness with atrophy and fasciculations together with reduced or absent reflexes (hyporeflexia); spasticity, hyperreflexia, and Babinski sign are upper motor neuron findings.'),
('5fd86ca8-2647-5811-a951-614fd3b2c8fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 60-year-old male presents with resting tremor, bradykinesia, and rigidity. Which region of the brain characteristically degenerates in this disorder?', '["Substantia nigra (pars compacta/reticulata)","Putamen","Subthalamic nucleus","Globus pallidus (internal/external)","Caudate nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia nigra (pars compacta/reticulata)', 'Parkinson disease results from loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, which disrupts the basal ganglia motor loop and produces resting tremor, bradykinesia, and rigidity.'),
('79e16bdb-f05b-5c17-8212-579499987ae9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The basal ganglia are primarily concerned with:', '["Regulation of movement initiation, scaling, and sequencing","Relay of smell to the cortex","Fine-touch discrimination","Balance via the vestibulospinal tract","Conscious visual perception"]'::jsonb, 'Regulation of movement initiation, scaling, and sequencing', 'The basal ganglia regulate the initiation, scaling, and sequencing of voluntary movement through cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical loops; they do not directly execute movement or process primary sensation.'),
('0ae3974f-e8c3-5769-ba98-99e66ee2088b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which sequence describes the direct motor pathway of the basal ganglia?', '["No significant net effect resulting in decreased functional activity","Functions only for posture accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex to striatum to GPi/SNr (inhibition) to thalamus (disinhibition) to cortex (facilitates movement)","Suppresses movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Functions only for tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex to striatum to GPi/SNr (inhibition) to thalamus (disinhibition) to cortex (facilitates movement)', 'In the direct pathway, cortical excitation of the striatum inhibits GPi/SNr, which disinhibits the thalamus so it can excite the cortex and facilitate movement.'),
('e8386278-2402-5dd1-94eb-e264ce2eb125'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which sequence describes the indirect motor pathway of the basal ganglia?', '["Cortex to striatum to GPe (inhibition) to STN (disinhibition) to GPi/SNr (excitation) to reduced thalamus to reduced movement (suppresses unwanted movement)","Causes an imbalance without a defined pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facilitates movement mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Has no significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acts only on tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex to striatum to GPe (inhibition) to STN (disinhibition) to GPi/SNr (excitation) to reduced thalamus to reduced movement (suppresses unwanted movement)', 'In the indirect pathway, disinhibition of the subthalamic nucleus increases GPi/SNr output, which inhibits the thalamus and thereby suppresses unwanted movement.'),
('1321f42f-8a3d-527f-bc87-ed0d6a7da579'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Dopamine released by the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc) onto the striatum:', '["Only excites the direct pathway resulting in decreased functional activity","Only inhibits the indirect pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Excites the D1 (direct) pathway and inhibits the D2 (indirect) pathway; the net effect is to promote movement","Has no significant effect on movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Suppresses all basal ganglia output leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Excites the D1 (direct) pathway and inhibits the D2 (indirect) pathway; the net effect is to promote movement', 'Striatal dopamine excites D1-receptor neurons of the direct pathway and inhibits D2-receptor neurons of the indirect pathway; the combined effect is net facilitation of intended movement.'),
('46882fae-cdf5-5c45-9955-a155f7ab9356'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Hemiballismus is caused by a lesion of the:', '["Subthalamic nucleus, producing contralateral violent flinging movements","Substantia nigra pars compacta accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Putamen mediated by specific receptor activation","Caudate nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Globus pallidus internus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Subthalamic nucleus, producing contralateral violent flinging movements', 'Hemiballismus follows damage (often hemorrhagic) to the contralateral subthalamic nucleus, causing violent uncontrolled flinging movements of the limbs.'),
('736161bd-375a-57d7-8cf9-91b424c2cb37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following best describes a primary function of the cerebellum?', '["Initiation of movement","Fine-tuning (error correction) of movement","Balance","Motor learning","Coordination of movement"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of movement', 'The cerebellum monitors and coordinates ongoing voluntary movement by comparing intended with actual movement and correcting errors; balance and motor learning are subsidiary roles.'),
('ff8582aa-3901-5b8c-9ecb-77a48d09aa1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The principal source of mossy fibers to the cerebellar hemispheres is the:', '["Vestibular nuclei","Cerebral cortex directly","Inferior olive","Spinal cord directly","Pontine nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine nuclei', 'Mossy fibers arise mainly from the pontine nuclei, the largest relay from the cerebral cortex, and also from the spinal cord and vestibular nuclei; climbing fibers come from the inferior olive.'),
('b9651e31-9beb-56f8-8262-746f36f83224'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Purkinje cells of the cerebellar cortex are best characterized as:', '["Firing spontaneously without a defined role","Modulatory cells of the granule layer","The main output cells of the cerebellar cortex","Excitatory (glutamatergic) projection neurons","Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei', 'Purkinje cells are the sole output of the cerebellar cortex; they are inhibitory (GABAergic) and synapse on the deep cerebellar and vestibular nuclei.'),
('9fe3c715-a229-5450-b038-aa241d1e00b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which is the largest and most lateral deep cerebellar nucleus, projecting to the motor cortex via the thalamus?', '["Only dentate nucleus","Interposed (globose and emboliform)","Dentate (lateral) nucleus","Only fastigial nucleus","Fastigial (medial) nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate (lateral) nucleus', 'The dentate nucleus is the largest and most lateral deep cerebellar nucleus; it projects via the superior cerebellar peduncle and ventrolateral thalamus to the motor cortex.'),
('3bb9eaff-01b3-5fba-a7bd-2df7ba5c498b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The cerebrocerebellum (lateral hemispheres) is involved in:', '["Tremor production only resulting in decreased functional activity","Posture only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Planning and coordination of skilled voluntary movements (cortex to pons to dentate to thalamus to motor cortex)","Gait only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Balance only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Planning and coordination of skilled voluntary movements (cortex to pons to dentate to thalamus to motor cortex)', 'The lateral hemispheres (cerebrocerebellum) receive input from the cerebral cortex via the pontine nuclei and, through dentate-thalamocortical projections, plan and coordinate skilled voluntary movements.'),
('175819e2-6ae8-53a5-9f00-eb688e4d9c26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 70-year-old female with a midline (vermal) cerebellar lesion would most likely complain primarily of:', '["Nystagmus","Limb incoordination","Dysarthria","Imbalance and truncal ataxia","Intention tremor"]'::jsonb, 'Imbalance and truncal ataxia', 'Midline (vermal) lesions produce truncal ataxia and imbalance with relative sparing of the limbs; lateral hemisphere lesions cause ipsilateral limb dysmetria and intention tremor.'),
('b6cf8ad5-874e-51ab-8328-67553d1d1c7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'All of the following are cerebellar signs EXCEPT:', '["Intention tremor","Dysmetria","Hypotonia","Nystagmus","Spasticity"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity', 'Cerebellar lesions cause intention tremor, dysmetria, hypotonia, and nystagmus; spasticity, by contrast, reflects upper motor neuron rather than cerebellar disease.'),
('9ad6c0e7-9328-5ead-ba87-4003bc4ef4f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Intention (kinetic) tremor is characteristically seen with:', '["Cerebellar hemisphere lesions (worsens as target approaches)","Essential tremor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Resting tremor mediated by specific receptor activation","Parkinson disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basal ganglia lesions leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar hemisphere lesions (worsens as target approaches)', 'Intention tremor appears or accentuates as the hand approaches a target and localizes to the ipsilateral cerebellar hemisphere and its dentate circuitry, unlike the resting tremor of parkinsonism.'),
('11dab656-13d8-5694-9ca6-edb15661f02c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the check reflex and what does its loss indicate?', '["Clonus resulting in decreased functional activity","Loss of the check reflex when resistance is suddenly released, indicating cerebellar dysfunction","Delayed relaxation of muscle stretch reflexes mediated by specific receptor activation","A brisk flexor withdrawal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prolonged reaction time only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of the check reflex when resistance is suddenly released, indicating cerebellar dysfunction', 'When a limb pulls against resistance that is abruptly released, antagonists normally stop it; impairment of this check reflex causes rebound and points to ipsilateral cerebellar hemisphere disease.'),
('48c96dd8-6804-53c4-b6da-d78ec2d4af23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which type of gait is characteristic of a cerebellar lesion?', '["Narrow-based gait resulting in decreased functional activity","Wide-based, lurching, drunk-like gait; truncal ataxia with vermis lesions and veering ipsilateral with hemisphere lesions","Shuffling gait mediated by specific receptor activation","Circumducting (hemiplegic) gait without affecting the primary tissue pathway","High-stepping (steppage) gait leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Wide-based, lurching, drunk-like gait; truncal ataxia with vermis lesions and veering ipsilateral with hemisphere lesions', 'Cerebellar disease produces a wide-based, staggering, ataxic gait; vermal lesions cause truncal ataxia while hemisphere lesions produce veering toward the side of the lesion.'),
('ce54bf9f-0e1f-525a-a9d8-67deca17df14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following is a feature of the cerebellar cognitive-affective syndrome (CCAS)?', '["Executive dysfunction","Spasticity","Flaccid paralysis","Resting tremor","Babinski sign"]'::jsonb, 'Executive dysfunction', 'CCAS, associated with posterior lobe (crus) lesions, comprises executive dysfunction, visuospatial impairments, linguistic changes, and flattened affect; spasticity and resting tremor reflect other systems.'),
('37ec9eee-0b92-5928-a719-c47f8ee04b3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The thalamus serves as the:', '["Only motor coordinating center resulting in decreased functional activity","Relay and processing center for most sensory modalities (except olfaction) to the cortex","Only memory center mediated by specific receptor activation","Only autonomic center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only sensory center for smell leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Relay and processing center for most sensory modalities (except olfaction) to the cortex', 'Nearly all sensory pathways (except olfaction) synapse in the thalamus before reaching the cortex; the thalamus also participates in motor and arousal circuits.'),
('e64b6dd1-2ee0-5662-ba3b-2ea6a2b964e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which specific thalamic nucleus relays somatosensation from the body (trunk and limbs) to the primary sensory cortex?', '["VPM (face)","MGN (hearing)","LGN (vision)","VPL (body sensation)","VLP (motor)"]'::jsonb, 'VPL (body sensation)', 'The ventral posterolateral nucleus (VPL) receives the medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract and projects to the somatic sensory cortex for the body; the VPM serves the face.'),
('1fc4f5ea-4fd4-5ac5-b7f2-7a70ecef2f38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH) of the posterior pituitary are:', '["Released from the median eminence into the portal system resulting in decreased functional activity","Synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, transported to the posterior pituitary via the supraopticohypophyseal tract, and released into the blood","Stored in the third ventricle mediated by specific receptor activation","Released from the hypothalamus into the fourth ventricle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Synthesized by the anterior pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei, transported to the posterior pituitary via the supraopticohypophyseal tract, and released into the blood', 'Oxytocin and ADH are produced in the supraoptic and paraventricular hypothalamic nuclei, carried down the supraopticohypophyseal tract, and stored in the posterior pituitary for release into the blood.'),
('83117725-24fe-5774-9993-1c90afe28992'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is produced by the:', '["Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","Arachnoid granulations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral capillary endothelium mediated by specific receptor activation","Pineal gland without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Simple filtration of plasma through the ependyma leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles', 'CSF is actively secreted by the choroid plexuses of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles; it is not a mere plasma filtrate, and the arachnoid granulations absorb rather than produce it.'),
('f5060023-1e1e-52a4-9586-bfefdb227cd6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the normal pathway of cerebrospinal fluid circulation?', '["Reverse direction (from the subarachnoid space into the ventricles) resulting in decreased functional activity","Lateral ventricles to foramina of Monro to third ventricle to aqueduct of Sylvius to fourth ventricle to foramina of Luschka and Magendie to subarachnoid space","Down the central canal into the lumbar subarachnoid space only mediated by specific receptor activation","To the third ventricle only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Production by the arachnoid granulations leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral ventricles to foramina of Monro to third ventricle to aqueduct of Sylvius to fourth ventricle to foramina of Luschka and Magendie to subarachnoid space', 'CSF flows from the lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina (Monro) to the third ventricle, then via the cerebral aqueduct to the fourth ventricle and out through the foramina of Luschka and Magendie into the subarachnoid space.'),
('cb5ac821-dcc7-564c-b7fe-20305de579c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Absorption of cerebrospinal fluid into the venous circulation occurs at the:', '["Brain capillaries resulting in decreased functional activity","Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus (one-way valves)","Spinal veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Choroid plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perineural spaces leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus (one-way valves)', 'CSF is absorbed chiefly through the arachnoid granulations into the superior sagittal sinus, functioning as one-way valves; smaller amounts drain along cranial and spinal nerve sheaths into lymphatics.'),
('c78ebb16-4697-56a0-8230-b8f1e76c85f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which circumventricular organ lacks a blood-brain barrier and acts as the chemoreceptor trigger zone for vomiting?', '["OVLT (osmoreception)","Area postrema (vomiting CTZ)","Subfornical organ","Pineal gland","Neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary)"]'::jsonb, 'Area postrema (vomiting CTZ)', 'The area postrema is a circumventricular organ without a blood-brain barrier that senses blood-borne emetogens and triggers vomiting; the OVLT, subfornical organ, and neurohypophysis are other CVOs with different functions.'),
('939353dd-cfc3-59c4-9f1f-cf32fa5cf605'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The internal organization of the spinal cord is best described as:', '["Central white matter resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Gray matter only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Central gray matter (butterfly) with dorsal sensory and ventral motor horns, surrounded by white matter funiculi containing tracts","White matter only without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Gray matter arranged peripherally leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central gray matter (butterfly) with dorsal sensory and ventral motor horns, surrounded by white matter funiculi containing tracts', 'The cord has centrally placed gray matter shaped like a butterfly (dorsal sensory and ventral motor horns) encircled by white matter funiculi containing ascending and descending tracts.'),
('6d5c3806-f5cc-5100-b642-4421e954216a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The conus medullaris (terminal end of the adult spinal cord) lies at:', '["L3-L4","L1-L2","S1-S2","Sacro-coccygeal level","T12-L1"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'In adults the spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris near the L1-L2 vertebral level; below this the nerve roots form the cauda equina within the vertebral canal.'),
('199b3b30-e289-52d5-9be5-0e8d9e0836ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Cauda equina syndrome is caused by:', '["Compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus medullaris","Compression at the level of the conus medullaris","Injury to the lateral corticospinal tract at the medulla","Occlusion of the anterior spinal artery at T4","Lesion of the cervical dorsal columns"]'::jsonb, 'Compression of the lumbosacral nerve roots below the conus medullaris', 'Cauda equina syndrome follows compression of the lumbar and sacral nerve roots within the canal below the conus medullaris, producing radicular pain, saddle anesthesia, leg weakness, and sphincter dysfunction.'),
('726d3f85-f136-57aa-831f-aa7b7ccca9e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Spinal shock is best defined as:', '["Recovery of reflexes to normal resulting in decreased functional activity","Permanent loss of all spinal reflexes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Temporary loss of spinal reflexes below a cord transection, initially areflexic/flaccid and later hyperreflexic/spastic","Loss of only motor function below the lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of only sensory function below the lesion leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Temporary loss of spinal reflexes below a cord transection, initially areflexic/flaccid and later hyperreflexic/spastic', 'Spinal shock is the transient areflexia and flaccid paralysis below a complete cord transection, lasting days to weeks; it is later replaced by spasticity, hyperreflexia, and clonus.'),
('74033e0a-537d-5c3e-8f50-d0d0b759604b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) and what is its function?', '["Only pain pathways resulting in decreased functional activity","Only sleep-promoting fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only waking-promoting fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","From brainstem reticular formation to thalamus to cortex, maintaining arousal and regulating sleep-wake transitions","Only motor control pathways leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'From brainstem reticular formation to thalamus to cortex, maintaining arousal and regulating sleep-wake transitions', 'The ARAS is a network of brainstem reticular formation neurons projecting via the thalamus to the cortex that sustains arousal and consciousness and regulates sleep-wake transitions.'),
('194dee7e-dd3a-5976-a994-c48980c2ccf9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The locus coeruleus is best described as a:', '["Norepinephrine-containing nucleus in the pons with widespread projections that promote arousal","Cholinergic nucleus of the basal forebrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serotonergic nucleus of the raphe mediated by specific receptor activation","Dopaminergic nucleus of the midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABAergic nucleus of the thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine-containing nucleus in the pons with widespread projections that promote arousal', 'The locus coeruleus is a small nucleus in the dorsal pons rich in noradrenergic neurons whose diffuse projections promote arousal, attention, and the stress response.'),
('29631275-8824-5e87-a984-36011e424a51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The raphe nuclei are best characterized as:', '["Serotonergic neurons (midbrain/pons/medulla) that modulate pain and sleep","Cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain","Noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus","Dopaminergic neurons of the ventral tegmentum","GABAergic inhibitory interneurons"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonergic neurons (midbrain/pons/medulla) that modulate pain and sleep', 'Raphe nuclei contain serotonergic neurons that project to the dorsal horn and forebrain; descending raphe-spinal pathways inhibit pain transmission and the system participates in sleep regulation.'),
('d608e022-594e-5f49-98b5-058f6a3b2b1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The ventral tegmental area (VTA) is characterized by:', '["Dopaminergic neurons (mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways) mediating reward","Cholinergic neurons projecting to the cortex","Substantia nigra pars compacta neurons","Serotonergic neurons of the raphe","Glutamatergic neurons of the locus coeruleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic neurons (mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways) mediating reward', 'VTA dopaminergic neurons project through the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex, mediating reward, motivation, and reinforcement.'),
('137e732d-9959-5408-a96d-17f5ac41a6b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following best describes the neurons of the nucleus basalis of Meynert?', '["Cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain that project diffusely to the cerebral cortex and hippocampus","Noradrenergic neurons of the pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopaminergic neurons of the midbrain mediated by specific receptor activation","Serotonergic neurons of the raphe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABAergic interneurons of the hippocampus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain that project diffusely to the cerebral cortex and hippocampus', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert supplies cholinergic innervation to the cortex and hippocampus; its degeneration contributes to the memory loss of Alzheimer disease.'),
('c8b65e9f-74d8-5342-b6e2-678245820705'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'How many pairs of cranial nerves are there and what are their components?', '["12 pairs (CN I-XII), comprising purely sensory, purely motor, and mixed nerves","10 pairs, all purely motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","8 pairs, all purely sensory mediated by specific receptor activation","12 pairs, all purely motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","12 pairs, all purely sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '12 pairs (CN I-XII), comprising purely sensory, purely motor, and mixed nerves', 'The twelve pairs of cranial nerves include purely sensory (I, II, VIII), purely motor (III, IV, VI, XI, XII), and mixed nerves, each associated with specific brainstem nuclei and functions.'),
('299dc0cb-95bd-50bf-86bb-52d874845a59'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which cranial nerve mediates taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue?', '["Taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue","Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue","Taste from the epiglottis","Taste from the tip of the tongue","Purely motor innervation of tongue muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) carries taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue via its lingual branch; the anterior two-thirds is served by the chorda tympani (CN VII).'),
('0bf043af-64e3-5301-9d54-48fa56c17646'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which cranial nerve provides parasympathetic fibers to the thoracic and abdominal viscera?', '["Preganglionic parasympathetic to the thoracic and abdominal viscera via the vagus (CN X)","Motor supply to the muscles of facial expression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor supply to the lateral rectus mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic supply to the parotid gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic parasympathetic to the thoracic and abdominal viscera via the vagus (CN X)', 'The vagus nerve carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the thoracic and abdominal organs and also supplies motor innervation to pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles and visceral sensation.'),
('f1b3e40d-6646-5f28-9e28-854bd13b39a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which cranial nerve is motor only and innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles?', '["Motor only, supplying the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles","Special sensory nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory only mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mixed nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor only, supplying the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) is purely motor, innervating the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; its paralysis causes shoulder droop and weak head rotation.'),
('295af543-6aa9-5045-a4e8-d91a621e35ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which cranial nerve provides motor innervation to all tongue muscles except palatoglossus?', '["Sensory only resulting in decreased functional activity","Taste fibers accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sympathetic fibers mediated by specific receptor activation","Mixed fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor only, to all intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles except palatoglossus"]'::jsonb, 'Motor only, to all intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles except palatoglossus', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) is purely motor to the intrinsic and extrinsic tongue muscles except palatoglossus, which is supplied by the pharyngeal plexus (CN X).'),
('f9821994-3e19-5784-bbc1-89ccabf5a95f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What happens when light is shone into one eye, and which reflex pathway mediates this?', '["Constriction of both pupils (direct and consensual); afferent via CN II, efferent via parasympathetic (Edinger-Westphal) fibers of CN III","Constriction of the stimulated eye only (no consensual response) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constriction of the opposite eye only mediated by specific receptor activation","No response in either eye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilatation of both pupils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constriction of both pupils (direct and consensual); afferent via CN II, efferent via parasympathetic (Edinger-Westphal) fibers of CN III', 'Light in one eye normally constricts both pupils via the direct and consensual light reflexes: afferent limb CN II, efferent limb the Edinger-Westphal (parasympathetic) fibers of CN III.'),
('0839287d-9c8a-5e3e-b1b7-f3950b1c3455'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the pupillary finding in Horner syndrome?', '["Miosis (a constricted pupil)","Mydriasis (a dilated pupil)","An oval, irregular pupil","A hippus pupil","Light-near dissociation"]'::jsonb, 'Miosis (a constricted pupil)', 'Interruption of the sympathetic pathway in Horner syndrome removes sympathetic tone to the iris dilator, causing miosis (constricted pupil) together with ptosis, anhidrosis, and slight enophthalmos.'),
('eb214ad8-415e-5f9b-bbf4-f86ef650109a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'During accommodation (looking from far to near), what happens to the lens?', '["It becomes thinner (flatter) resulting in decreased functional activity","It becomes thicker (more convex) as the ciliary muscle contracts and the zonules relax","It moves posteriorly within the eye mediated by specific receptor activation","It does not change its shape without affecting the primary tissue pathway","It becomes less transparent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'It becomes thicker (more convex) as the ciliary muscle contracts and the zonules relax', 'On near vision the ciliary muscle contracts, relaxing the zonules so the elastic lens bulges (thickens) for near focus; this accompanies pupillary constriction and convergence as part of the accommodation triad.'),
('b626da66-9210-5340-9903-1e89fca8e290'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is light-near dissociation and in which condition is it characteristically seen?', '["Light-near dissociation (pupil constricts to accommodation but not to light), seen in neurosyphilis (Argyll Robertson pupil)","Mydriasis with preserved accommodation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Miosis with preserved light reflex (Horner) mediated by specific receptor activation","A fixed dilated pupil (CN III palsy) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A tonic pupil in Adie syndrome leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Light-near dissociation (pupil constricts to accommodation but not to light), seen in neurosyphilis (Argyll Robertson pupil)', 'The Argyll Robertson pupil of neurosyphilis constricts to accommodation but not to light (light-near dissociation), because the light reflex pathway near the midbrain is damaged while near pathways remain intact.'),
('3f108bff-2878-5062-8559-b216d97628c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Adie''s (tonic) pupil is best explained by:', '["A brisk light response resulting in decreased functional activity","Denervation hypersensitivity of the ciliary ganglion producing a large pupil that reacts slowly to light and accommodation","Light-near dissociation with miosis mediated by specific receptor activation","An irregular pupil with segmental vermiform movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small pupil with anhidrosis and ptosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Denervation hypersensitivity of the ciliary ganglion producing a large pupil that reacts slowly to light and accommodation', 'Adie syndrome follows postganglionic parasympathetic (ciliary ganglion) damage; denervation hypersensitivity leaves a large pupil that reacts minimally to light but slowly to accommodation, often with vermiform iris movement.'),
('4c22b8c0-ea05-57ce-aad9-3a24f07fc1dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Left homonymous hemianopia results from:', '["Nasal field loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Full field loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bitemporal field loss mediated by specific receptor activation","A lesion of the right optic tract, radiations, or visual cortex causing loss of the left visual field in both eyes","Loss of the right visual field in both eyes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lesion of the right optic tract, radiations, or visual cortex causing loss of the left visual field in both eyes', 'Right-sided lesions of the optic tract, optic radiations, or occipital cortex cause loss of the left visual field of both eyes (left homonymous hemianopia).'),
('bdbaa063-d40b-5f0c-8add-b8a0e243311c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Homonymous superior quadrantanopia is produced by:', '["Total visual field loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Homonymous inferior field loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A temporal or parietal lobe lesion (Meyer''s loop) causing contralateral superior quadrant field loss","Nasal field loss without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bitemporal field loss leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A temporal or parietal lobe lesion (Meyer''s loop) causing contralateral superior quadrant field loss', 'Meyer''s loop fibers sweep through the temporal lobe, so a temporal lobe lesion produces a contralateral superior quadrantanopia; the parietal lobe (Baum''s loop) causes inferior quadrantanopia.'),
('e8f82059-0529-5cd3-8bc5-0dac52ceec36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Where is the primary visual cortex (V1) located and how is it organized?', '["Area 18 of the occipital lobe resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Prefrontal cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Area 19 of the occipital lobe mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior temporal gyrus without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Striate cortex (area 17) in the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe, with retinotopic organization and posterior macular representation"]'::jsonb, 'Striate cortex (area 17) in the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe, with retinotopic organization and posterior macular representation', 'V1 (striate cortex, area 17) lies in the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe, is retinotopically organized, and represents the macula posteriorly; association areas 18 and 19 surround it.'),
('b3ff40c9-95b4-5b35-ae79-473370f74aec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Blindsight refers to:', '["Normal conscious vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Partial conscious vision in the affected hemifield accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Unconscious detection of and responding to visual stimuli in patients with occipital (V1) lesions, mediated via the superior colliculus","Complete bilateral blindness without any visual responses without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Impaired color vision only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Unconscious detection of and responding to visual stimuli in patients with occipital (V1) lesions, mediated via the superior colliculus', 'Blindsight is the unconscious ability of patients with V1 (occipital) lesions to localize or respond to visual stimuli, mediated by intact retinotectal (superior colliculus) pathways despite absent conscious vision.'),
('32b6425a-def3-59e8-8557-c28d721f5c74'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A visual evoked potential (VEP) records the:', '["Retinal photoreceptor response resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Occipital cortical response to a visual stimulus (prolonged P100 latency indicates optic nerve demyelination, e.g., in multiple sclerosis)","Brainstem auditory response mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor nerve conduction without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oculomotor response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital cortical response to a visual stimulus (prolonged P100 latency indicates optic nerve demyelination, e.g., in multiple sclerosis)', 'Visual evoked potentials record the occipital cortical response to a visual stimulus; a prolonged P100 latency indicates optic nerve demyelination, as seen in multiple sclerosis.'),
('0fbfd093-40ae-5528-a321-edbf440e1356'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The substantia gelatinosa (lamina II of the dorsal horn) is important for:', '["Pain modulation by interneurons that release enkephalin","Transmission of vibration and proprioception","Direct synaptic relay of fine touch to the cortex","Motor outflow to skeletal muscle","Generation of the stretch reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Pain modulation by interneurons that release enkephalin', 'Lamina II contains inhibitory interneurons releasing the endogenous opioid enkephalin, which presynaptically inhibits substance P release from nociceptor terminals, forming the substrate of gate-control analgesia.'),
('bfb9e104-1961-5cb1-ba53-7d014662e846'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The propriospinal system consists of:', '["Interneurons connecting different spinal segments that modulate motor output and coordinate locomotion","Primary sensory neurons of the dorsal root ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Long ascending fibers of the medial lemniscus mediated by specific receptor activation","Gamma motor neurons innervating intrafusal fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cranial nerve motor nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Interneurons connecting different spinal segments that modulate motor output and coordinate locomotion', 'Propriospinal neurons are interneurons linking different spinal cord segments; they coordinate muscle groups, generate locomotor rhythm via central pattern generators, and modulate spinal reflexes.'),
('13e0062e-bffb-55a4-9f75-f081bb9e53be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Central cord syndrome characteristically causes:', '["Disproportionate weakness of the upper compared with lower limbs, with cape-like suspended sensory loss","Lower limb weakness greater than upper limb weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ipsilateral weakness with loss of proprioception mediated by specific receptor activation","Bitemporal hemianopia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ataxia with intention tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Disproportionate weakness of the upper compared with lower limbs, with cape-like suspended sensory loss', 'Central cord syndrome (cervical hyperextension or syringomyelia) damages centrally located fibers, so hand and arm function is worse than the legs, with a suspended cape-like sensory loss and relative sacral sparing.'),
('4da0b736-5bbb-549e-a16c-acd2ab8873b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Posterior cord syndrome results from:', '["Dorsal column injury (posterior spinal artery territory) with loss of proprioception and vibration","Loss of pain and temperature with preserved proprioception accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Contralateral spastic paresis mediated by specific receptor activation","Radicular pain along one dermatome without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Loss of pain and temperature over the face leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column injury (posterior spinal artery territory) with loss of proprioception and vibration', 'Posterior cord syndrome follows posterior spinal artery occlusion or tabes dorsalis; dorsal column damage causes ipsilateral loss of proprioception, vibration, and fine touch below the lesion, with sensory ataxia and a positive Romberg.'),
('7393a36b-e337-534f-b2ee-44a0788b0449'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The dorsal (posterior) spinocerebellar tract:', '["Originates in Clarke''s nucleus (T1-L2), ascends ipsilaterally, and enters the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle, carrying unconscious proprioception from muscle spindles","Crosses at the pyramidal decussation and enters via the superior cerebellar peduncle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Carries conscious proprioception to the ventrolateral thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Originates in dorsal root ganglia at all levels and enters via the middle cerebellar peduncle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Originates in the accessory cuneate nucleus for the lower limbs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Originates in Clarke''s nucleus (T1-L2), ascends ipsilaterally, and enters the cerebellum via the inferior cerebellar peduncle, carrying unconscious proprioception from muscle spindles', 'The dorsal spinocerebellar tract arises from Clarke''s nucleus, ascends without crossing, and enters the inferior cerebellar peduncle, transmitting unconscious limb proprioception to the cerebellum; the cuneocerebellar tract serves the upper limb.'),
('e6f60979-abb2-50b5-83ec-ab1d8ec65f60'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following findings is characteristic of an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion?', '["Spasticity with hyperreflexia and an extensor plantar response","Flaccid paralysis with muscle atrophy","Fasciculations and areflexia","Wasting with fasciculations in the affected limb","Areflexic flaccid tone"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity with hyperreflexia and an extensor plantar response', 'UMN lesions (cortex or spinal cord above the anterior horn) produce spastic paralysis, hyperreflexia, clonus, and a Babinski sign without early wasting; LMN lesions cause flaccid paresis, atrophy, and fasciculations.'),
('4e1d3b21-1858-5dae-9de6-c18bcb0d8ded'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which major fiber systems traverse the posterior limb of the internal capsule?', '["Corticospinal fibers and somatosensory (thalamocortical) radiations","Corticobulbar fibers only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optic radiations mediated by specific receptor activation","Olfactory tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial longitudinal fasciculus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal fibers and somatosensory (thalamocortical) radiations', 'The internal capsule is white matter between the caudate/putamen and the thalamus: the genu carries corticobulbar fibers while the posterior limb carries corticospinal and somatosensory radiations, so a capsular lesion causes contralateral hemiparesis and hemisensory loss.'),
('42319424-4459-5c5d-8b44-74e4f04a0619'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The medial medullary (Dejerine) syndrome, due to anterior spinal artery occlusion, is characterized by:', '["Ipsilateral hypoglossal palsy, contralateral hemiparesis, and contralateral loss of proprioception","Ipsilateral ataxia, Horner syndrome, and contralateral loss of pain and temperature","Vertigo, nystagmus, and dysphagia with hiccups","Lateral gaze palsy and facial droop","Third nerve palsy with contralateral tremor"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral hypoglossal palsy, contralateral hemiparesis, and contralateral loss of proprioception', 'Dejerine (medial medullary) syndrome from anterior spinal artery occlusion involves the pyramid (contralateral hemiparesis), medial lemniscus (contralateral proprioception loss), and hypoglossal nerve (ipsilateral tongue deviation).'),
('d476e2b3-4fe0-5a3f-a2d6-7c8ba180bf78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Global aphasia is most characteristically caused by:', '["A large left-hemisphere (MCA) lesion involving both Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas, with severe impairment of all language modalities","A lesion of Wernicke''s area alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lesion of Broca''s area alone mediated by specific receptor activation","An arcuate fasciculus lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lesion of the left supplementary motor area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large left-hemisphere (MCA) lesion involving both Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas, with severe impairment of all language modalities', 'Global aphasia follows large left MCA infarcts destroying both anterior (Broca) and posterior (Wernicke) language zones, causing non-fluent output, poor comprehension, and impaired repetition together with hemiparesis.'),
('169f8855-b053-5bae-9404-a05bd42aecd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The distinguishing feature of transcortical motor aphasia is:', '["Non-fluent speech with intact repetition","Fluent speech with impaired repetition","Non-fluent speech with impaired repetition","Isolated word-finding difficulty","Impaired comprehension with preserved repetition"]'::jsonb, 'Non-fluent speech with intact repetition', 'Transcortical motor aphasia is non-fluent but repetition is spared because the arcuate fasciculus and perisylvian language areas remain intact, typically after ACA territory (SMA) strokes; echolalia may occur.'),
('5a12a02c-dfcd-5bbd-9036-7a4fbd5adc69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Subcortical aphasia is characteristically associated with:', '["Lesions of the basal ganglia (striatocapsular) or the thalamus","Lesions of Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas alone","Occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Degeneration of the substantia nigra","Spinal cord transection"]'::jsonb, 'Lesions of the basal ganglia (striatocapsular) or the thalamus', 'Aphasia can arise from striatocapsular or thalamic lesions that disrupt cortical-subcortical language loops, producing non-fluent, hypophonic output with relatively intact comprehension.'),
('6a277a77-6f23-5867-932f-5b34f638a229'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which basal ganglia loop is primarily concerned with planning and executing movement via the putamen?', '["Motor loop: premotor/motor cortex to putamen to GPi/SNr to VA/VL thalamus to cortex","Oculomotor loop (frontal eye fields via the caudate)","Prefrontal (executive) loop","Limbic loop (ventral striatum/nucleus accumbens)","No loop projects to the motor cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Motor loop: premotor/motor cortex to putamen to GPi/SNr to VA/VL thalamus to cortex', 'The putamen-based motor loop relays cortical motor signals through GPi/SNr to the VA/VL thalamus and back to the motor and premotor cortex to scale and execute movement.'),
('0ff44ab5-8998-500c-9aa1-f3324540a89e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The dermatome over the occiput and back of the head corresponds to which cervical level?', '["C2","C4","C6","C7","C8"]'::jsonb, 'C2', 'C2 supplies the posterior scalp (occiput); C4 the neck and shoulders, C6 the thumb and index finger, C7 the middle finger, and C8 the ring and little fingers.'),
('708e7c7c-6808-5348-a750-3c189d2b693d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The corticospinal (upper motor neuron) tract is best described as:', '["Cell bodies in the motor cortex, axons descending in the corticospinal tract to synapse on lower motor neurons in the anterior horn; its lesion causes spastic paralysis","Cell bodies in the anterior horn with axons to muscle; its lesion causes flaccid paralysis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A purely sensory ascending pathway mediated by specific receptor activation","A pathway from the cerebellum to the cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A tract that innervates only cranial nerve nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cell bodies in the motor cortex, axons descending in the corticospinal tract to synapse on lower motor neurons in the anterior horn; its lesion causes spastic paralysis', 'Upper motor neuron cell bodies lie in the motor cortex and descend in the corticospinal tract to synapse on anterior horn (LMN) cells; UMN lesions produce spastic paralysis and a Babinski sign.'),
('14010f52-4487-5c50-b75e-c179860c8d5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The pontine (medial) reticulospinal tract:', '["Originates in the pontine reticular formation and is excitatory to extensor and antigravity muscles, descending ipsilaterally","Originates in the medullary reticular formation and facilitates extensor tone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crosses at the pyramidal decussation mediated by specific receptor activation","Carries pain fibers to the thalamus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Innervates only cranial nerve nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Originates in the pontine reticular formation and is excitatory to extensor and antigravity muscles, descending ipsilaterally', 'Pontine reticulospinal fibers descend ipsilaterally to excite axial and extensor (antigravity) musculature, while the medullary (lateral) reticulospinal tract inhibits extensor activity.'),
('3ed31349-8152-526c-8053-bfa51fcf377f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract:', '["Originates in the lateral vestibular nucleus (Deiters) and excites extensor and antigravity motor neurons","Descends in the medial longitudinal fasciculus to cervical segments only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Crosses the midline and inhibits extensors mediated by specific receptor activation","Carries conscious proprioception to the cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Terminates on dorsal horn spinothalamic cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Originates in the lateral vestibular nucleus (Deiters) and excites extensor and antigravity motor neurons', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract from Deiters'' nucleus descends ipsilaterally to excite extensor antigravity motor neurons, maintaining upright posture; the medial vestibulospinal tract in the MLF serves head and neck balance.'),
('0662c39e-8fed-52e6-8856-670782d2eeb9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The red nucleus of the midbrain is best described as:', '["A midbrain nucleus that facilitates flexor muscle tone via the rubrospinal tract","A pontine nucleus producing norepinephrine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A medullary nucleus that inhibits extensors mediated by specific receptor activation","The source of the vestibulospinal tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A deep cerebellar nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A midbrain nucleus that facilitates flexor muscle tone via the rubrospinal tract', 'The red nucleus in the midbrain gives rise to the rubrospinal tract, which facilitates flexor tone; it is small in humans, where the corticospinal tract dominates.'),
('382c456b-ccb4-5598-89ea-91cad506d5aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The corticospinal (pyramidal) tract is primarily responsible for:', '["Fine voluntary movement, especially of distal limb muscles","Autonomic outflow to the viscera","Balance and posture via the vestibulospinal tract","Pain and temperature sensation","Rhythmic generation of locomotion"]'::jsonb, 'Fine voluntary movement, especially of distal limb muscles', 'The corticospinal tract provides fine, skilled, voluntary control mainly of distal limb muscles; balance (vestibulospinal), posture (reticulospinal), and locomotion (central pattern generators) are handled by other pathways.'),
('e9791d26-4b81-510b-be63-8437822c4101'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'How many pairs of spinal nerves are there and how are they distributed?', '["31 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal","30 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 0 coccygeal","31 pairs: 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 2 coccygeal","29 pairs in total","33 pairs: 9 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 2 coccygeal"]'::jsonb, '31 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal', 'There are 31 pairs of spinal nerves: 8 cervical (C1-C8), 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal, with cervical and lumbar enlargements for the limbs.'),
('70cac9fe-227b-53bc-a365-82469dc133d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Renshaw cells are best described as:', '["Glycinergic inhibitory interneurons of the ventral horn that mediate recurrent inhibition of alpha motor neurons","Excitatory interneurons releasing glutamate onto motor neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Primary afferent neurons of the dorsal root mediated by specific receptor activation","GABAergic neurons of the dorsal horn mediating pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sympathetic preganglionic neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glycinergic inhibitory interneurons of the ventral horn that mediate recurrent inhibition of alpha motor neurons', 'Renshaw cells receive recurrent collaterals from alpha motor neuron axons and release glycine to inhibit them and neighboring motor neurons, limiting firing rate and stabilizing output.'),
('f57075f3-44fd-5ad3-8c4f-4dc326c80b0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which neurotransmitter is primarily released at the neuromuscular junction?', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","Serotonin","Norepinephrine","Dopamine","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter released from somatic motor nerve endings; it acts on nicotinic receptors of the motor end plate to trigger muscle contraction.'),
('af65838a-8a7f-5682-8b7d-7f2d7e7ee801'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 30-year-old female presents with resting tremor, bradykinesia, and rigidity. Which region most characteristically shows degeneration in this disorder?', '["Thalamus and hypothalamus","Cerebral cortex","Substantia nigra pars compacta","Caudate nucleus","Putamen"]'::jsonb, 'Substantia nigra pars compacta', 'The triad of resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia indicates Parkinson disease, caused by dopamine neuron loss in the substantia nigra pars compacta.'),
('6f2af37f-4cad-5be8-807f-927c5d80a829'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following best describes the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS)?', '["A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal and sleep-wake cycles","A spinal tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A single thalamic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","A cerebellar peduncle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A hormone-secreting gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal and sleep-wake cycles', 'The ARAS is a diffusely interconnected network of brainstem reticular formation neurons that projects to the thalamus and cortex to sustain wakefulness and consciousness.'),
('bbe993fc-b37c-5237-a4e8-bb68014a1e52'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the corticospinal tract and what is its function?', '["An ascending sensory pathway resulting in decreased functional activity","A visual pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord controlling voluntary movement","A cerebellar pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autonomic pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord controlling voluntary movement', 'The corticospinal tract is the principal descending motor pathway, an upper motor neuron tract from the cortex to the spinal cord that drives voluntary skilled movement.'),
('f510f6b6-7452-5486-8696-45a68e7e2372'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway carries:', '["Motor commands","Pain and temperature","Fine touch","Vibration","Proprioception to the ipsilateral cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Fine touch', 'The dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway carries fine touch, vibration, and proprioception; its fibers ascend ipsilaterally in the dorsal columns before crossing at the medial lemniscus decussation.'),
('bf35507e-4ea8-55a2-81b3-ddfac0196cc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 45-year-old female presents with progressive muscle weakness and atrophy. Which findings best fit a lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion?', '["Fasciculations with areflexia","Spasticity","Hyperreflexia","Positive Babinski sign","Clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Fasciculations with areflexia', 'Progressive weakness and atrophy are hallmarks of LMN involvement, accompanied by fasciculations and reduced or absent reflexes; hyperreflexia, spasticity, clonus, and Babinski sign indicate UMN disease.'),
('076ed5ad-b211-5e22-b5e2-0222a2956e1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following best characterizes a lower motor neuron lesion?', '["Flaccid paralysis with muscle atrophy and fasciculations","Spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia","Clonus and extensor plantar response","Loss of pain and temperature on the contralateral side","Ipsilateral cerebellar ataxia"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid paralysis with muscle atrophy and fasciculations', 'LMN lesions interrupt the final common pathway to muscle, producing flaccid paralysis with decreased tone, wasting, fasciculations, and hyporeflexia.'),
('35b45ecd-5c43-52cc-8a40-9367a54048a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which anatomical feature is the structural basis of the blood-brain barrier?', '["Dura mater resulting in decreased functional activity","Arachnoid mater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, supported by astrocyte foot processes","Pia mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Choroid plexus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, supported by astrocyte foot processes', 'The blood-brain barrier is formed by non-fenestrated cerebral capillary endothelium sealed with tight junctions and reinforced by astrocytic end-feet and pericytes.'),
('3efb1120-d86e-5950-91fe-3cb28dc882dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Where is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) produced?', '["Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","Arachnoid granulations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord gray matter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebral capillaries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles', 'CSF is actively secreted by the choroid plexuses of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles; arachnoid granulations absorb it rather than produce it.'),
('77b9fdd5-95b8-53d4-96a2-727b2ed41bc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The three major divisions of the brain are:', '["Cerebrum and brainstem only","Cerebral cortex, basal ganglia, and thalamus","Cerebellum and brainstem only","Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem","Frontal, parietal, and occipital lobes"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem', 'The three major divisions of the brain are the cerebrum, the cerebellum, and the brainstem (midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata).'),
('50582f8d-c896-5e5a-b82b-893bf3000b10'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which type of aphasia presents with non-fluent speech but preserved comprehension?', '["Broca''s aphasia","Wernicke''s aphasia","Global aphasia","Conduction aphasia","Anomic aphasia"]'::jsonb, 'Broca''s aphasia', 'Broca''s (expressive) aphasia, from a left inferior frontal (areas 44/45) lesion, produces effortful non-fluent speech with relatively preserved comprehension.'),
('a4afc145-0163-5711-983e-1cf073227f0e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following is a component of the basal ganglia?', '["Caudate nucleus","Hippocampus","Lateral geniculate nucleus","Cerebellar dentate nucleus","Sensory cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Caudate nucleus', 'The basal ganglia comprise the caudate, putamen, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, and substantia nigra; the hippocampus and geniculate nuclei are not components.'),
('c9f71023-13b9-5a52-bfd1-404bc66ba162'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The cerebellum is responsible for:', '["Higher thought resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision mediated by specific receptor activation","Coordination of voluntary movement","Balance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of voluntary movement', 'The cerebellum coordinates and refines voluntary movement and contributes to balance and tone; conscious sensation and higher intellect are cortical functions.'),
('87ccdfd9-1938-5c0e-bee6-9ab10337560f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) and where is it located?', '["A spinal tract resulting in decreased functional activity","A cranial nerve nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thalamic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","A part of the cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal and consciousness"]'::jsonb, 'A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal and consciousness', 'The ARAS is a network of neurons within the brainstem reticular formation that maintains arousal and consciousness via projections to the thalamus and cortex.'),
('e292bf37-db3e-5670-beab-90182283c747'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion signs include:', '["Loss of sensation only resulting in decreased functional activity","Fasciculations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pain only mediated by specific receptor activation","Flaccid paralysis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spasticity, hyperreflexia, positive Babinski sign, and clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity, hyperreflexia, positive Babinski sign, and clonus', 'UMN lesions remove descending inhibition, producing spasticity, hyperreflexia, clonus, and a positive Babinski sign; fasciculations and flaccid paresis indicate LMN disease.'),
('ffc897d0-7860-5381-9e84-910f660d70ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 40-year-old female presents with progressive flaccid weakness, muscle atrophy, and fasciculations. Which examination finding is most consistent with a lower motor neuron lesion?', '["Rigidity","Spasticity","Hyperreflexia","Babinski sign","Flaccid paralysis"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid paralysis', 'Lower motor neuron lesions cause flaccid paralysis with atrophy, fasciculations, and hyporeflexia; spasticity, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign point to upper motor neuron disease.'),
('faa8eb7d-1c63-58b7-8efa-c5728142675e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the structural basis of the blood-brain barrier (BBB)?', '["Arachnoid mater resulting in decreased functional activity","Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, supported by astrocyte end-feet","Dura mater mediated by specific receptor activation","Pia mater only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neurons and glia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, supported by astrocyte end-feet', 'The BBB is formed by tight junctions between cerebral capillary endothelial cells and is reinforced by astrocyte end-feet and pericytes, restricting passage of most blood-borne substances into the brain.'),
('f4e8541b-8a8a-5b56-8cb2-874ffc9b2365'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the primary site of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) formation?', '["Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","Arachnoid granulations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Meninges mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles', 'CSF is actively secreted by the choroid plexuses of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles; the arachnoid granulations are its main absorption site.'),
('a41deec3-bf93-5de5-a099-61aee87dc6a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which part of the brain is responsible for higher cognitive functions, voluntary motor control, and sensory processing?', '["Cerebrum","Cerebellum","Brainstem","Cerebral cortex","Basal ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebrum', 'The cerebrum (cerebral hemispheres, cortex, and underlying basal ganglia and white matter) mediates higher cognition, voluntary motor control, and sensory processing.'),
('0a4b7a8b-eb8a-5ea4-853b-227b6cc61c63'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The cerebrum is best described as:', '["Two cerebral hemispheres connected by the corpus callosum","Two hemispheres that are not interconnected","A single undivided mass","Four lobes plus the brainstem","The cerebellum and its peduncles"]'::jsonb, 'Two cerebral hemispheres connected by the corpus callosum', 'The cerebrum consists of two hemispheres joined by the corpus callosum, the largest white-matter commissure, which allows interhemispheric transfer of information.'),
('1e7e0299-a79a-5ed7-bf20-1831b8f03706'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following best describes the role of the cerebral cortex?', '["Higher functions including consciousness, thought, language, memory, and voluntary motor control","Only thought accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only motor control mediated by specific receptor activation","Only sensory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Reflexes only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Higher functions including consciousness, thought, language, memory, and voluntary motor control', 'The cerebral cortex integrates higher functions such as consciousness, cognition, language, and memory, in addition to voluntary motor and sensory processing, rather than serving any single modality.'),
('3099e118-15f6-5097-9e0b-9749a5262134'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the primary function of the lateral corticospinal tract?', '["Balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hearing only mediated by specific receptor activation","Emotion only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor function (skilled voluntary movement)"]'::jsonb, 'Motor function (skilled voluntary movement)', 'The lateral corticospinal tract, after crossing at the pyramids, mediates skilled, fine voluntary motor control, especially of the distal limb muscles.'),
('3e70f8e7-cf9c-5a03-87fb-548fbc7b8792'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which type of aphasia presents with fluent but meaningless speech and impaired comprehension?', '["Wernicke''s aphasia","Broca''s aphasia","Global aphasia","Conduction aphasia","Transcortical sensory aphasia"]'::jsonb, 'Wernicke''s aphasia', 'Wernicke''s (receptive) aphasia, from a left posterior superior temporal lesion, produces fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension.'),
('2fa0c0bb-5e47-5506-90eb-0c0d7b702d89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which type of neuron degenerates in Parkinson''s disease?', '["Dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta","Cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain","Serotonergic neurons in the raphe nuclei","GABAergic neurons in the striatum","Glutamatergic neurons in the cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta', 'Parkinson''s disease reflects degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, which reduces striatal dopamine and disrupts the basal ganglia motor loop.'),
('202bad04-d936-5b4f-a42f-60b04024b37a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The thalamus is best described as:', '["A reflex center resulting in decreased functional activity","An emotion center accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A motor coordination center mediated by specific receptor activation","A balance center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sensory relay station for all senses except smell"]'::jsonb, 'A sensory relay station for all senses except smell', 'The thalamus receives nearly all sensory input (except olfaction) and relays it to the cortex; it also participates in motor and arousal circuits.'),
('ab407169-1216-57e1-8b5c-95130d2338c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The hypothalamus is a major center for:', '["Homeostasis including temperature regulation, hunger, thirst, and endocrine control","Hearing only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vision only mediated by specific receptor activation","Memory only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fine motor coordination only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Homeostasis including temperature regulation, hunger, thirst, and endocrine control', 'The hypothalamus integrates autonomic and endocrine homeostasis, controlling body temperature, appetite, thirst, circadian rhythms, and pituitary function.'),
('98dd3fa0-b1f8-569b-844a-a92a70de19c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 40-year-old female presents with ataxia and intention tremor. Which of the following best explains her findings?', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coordination of voluntary movement","Balance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor learning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of voluntary movement', 'Ataxia and intention tremor indicate cerebellar (hemisphere) dysfunction; the cerebellum coordinates voluntary movement and contributes to balance and motor learning.'),
('57f1d6eb-6ca2-5f34-81e3-ef93eafcdc19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following is NOT part of the brainstem?', '["Midbrain","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Thalamus","Reticular formation"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus', 'The brainstem comprises the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata, along with the reticular formation; the thalamus belongs to the diencephalon.'),
('14ad10c0-5644-558d-ad3a-c1dfa582b162'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The reticular formation is best described as:', '["A part of the cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal, consciousness, and sleep-wake cycles","A spinal tract mediated by specific receptor activation","A cranial nerve nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A basal ganglia nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal, consciousness, and sleep-wake cycles', 'The reticular formation is a diffusely interconnected network of brainstem nuclei that maintains arousal and consciousness and regulates sleep-wake cycles.'),
('5556436a-a7c8-5400-82c9-011eae4631fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The dorsal (posterior) horn of the spinal cord receives:', '["Sensory (afferent) input from the body","Autonomic signals","Cerebellar input","Motor (efferent) output","Visual input"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory (afferent) input from the body', 'The dorsal horn receives primary afferent (sensory) fibers carrying touch, pain, and temperature from the body; motor output exits via the ventral horn.'),
('256ec5e2-f476-5037-9488-721bba96dd4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The corticospinal tract is:', '["A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord","A visual pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An autonomic pathway mediated by specific receptor activation","A cerebellar pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An ascending sensory pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord', 'The corticospinal tract is the descending upper motor neuron pathway from the motor cortex to the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord.'),
('01a29b5c-17d0-5504-a366-e6f39aa47340'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 65-year-old female presents with progressive muscle weakness and atrophy. Which findings best indicate lower motor neuron involvement?', '["Fasciculations with areflexia","Loss of sensation only","Pain only","Spasticity and hyperreflexia","Babinski sign and clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Fasciculations with areflexia', 'Progressive weakness and atrophy with fasciculations are lower motor neuron findings, associated with hyporeflexia and flaccidity; spasticity, hyperreflexia, clonus, and Babinski sign indicate UMN disease.'),
('04eadbc4-bb3d-51ba-a886-c724ffe068c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which examination finding is most consistent with a lower motor neuron lesion?', '["Spasticity","Hyperreflexia","Babinski sign","Flaccid paralysis","Clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid paralysis', 'Lower motor neuron lesions cause flaccid paralysis with hyporeflexia, atrophy, and fasciculations; spasticity, clonus, hyperreflexia, and a Babinski sign reflect upper motor neuron disease.'),
('0eeaec0a-3587-534c-aae7-37d410b025b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The blood-brain barrier is formed by:', '["Neurons and glia resulting in decreased functional activity","Arachnoid mater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pia mater only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dura mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells', 'The blood-brain barrier is formed by tight junctions sealing the cerebral capillary endothelial cells, reinforced by astrocyte end-feet and pericytes.'),
('31156981-10cb-5e42-9d08-2f5c820a1f1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'First-line for GAD:', '["SSRIs","Benzodiazepines","Barbiturates","Antipsychotics","Opioids"]'::jsonb, 'SSRIs', 'SSRIs are first-line for GAD (efficacy, safety, no dependence).'),
('dc54c3d2-2d15-5143-836e-26b81392207d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'How is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) absorbed back into the venous circulation?', '["Lymphatics resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus","Choroid plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus', 'CSF returns to venous blood through arachnoid granulations projecting into the superior sagittal sinus; the choroid plexus produces CSF rather than absorbing it.'),
('ecad7a95-e5ff-598b-98aa-a7260b74f5b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The circle of Willis is:', '["A part of the cerebellum resulting in decreased functional activity","A nerve plexus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The main artery of the brain mediated by specific receptor activation","A vascular anastomosis at the base of the brain formed by the internal carotid and vertebrobasilar arteries","A venous sinus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A vascular anastomosis at the base of the brain formed by the internal carotid and vertebrobasilar arteries', 'The circle of Willis is an anastomotic ring at the base of the brain connecting the internal carotid and vertebrobasilar systems, providing collateral blood flow.'),
('38a5e56b-7af8-5e51-8321-50342a1af295'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The central nervous system (CNS) consists of:', '["Brain only","Spinal cord and peripheral nerves","Spinal cord only","Brain and spinal cord","Brain and peripheral nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Brain and spinal cord', 'The CNS comprises the brain and spinal cord; the peripheral nerves and ganglia form the peripheral nervous system.'),
('efcabb20-d631-5859-9d8b-54c60744410d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The basic functional unit of the CNS is the:', '["Axon","Synapse","Neuron (nerve cell)","Glial cell","Receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Neuron (nerve cell)', 'The neuron is the basic functional unit of the CNS, transmitting electrical and chemical signals; glial cells provide structural and metabolic support.'),
('4d8a6cbd-e714-50e8-8374-d41dcfd5e9e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The three major anatomical divisions of the brain are:', '["Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem","Cerebral cortex, basal ganglia, and thalamus","Medulla, pons, and midbrain only","Cerebellum and spinal cord only","Hypothalamus, pituitary, and pineal gland"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem', 'The three major parts of the brain are the cerebrum, the cerebellum, and the brainstem; the midbrain, pons, and medulla are subdivisions of the brainstem.'),
('6098fe80-9a6a-5f66-922f-d0a330d5e469'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Higher cognitive functions, including consciousness, thought, memory, and language, are mediated by:', '["The cerebral cortex, especially the prefrontal and association areas","The cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The spinal cord gray matter mediated by specific receptor activation","The basal ganglia only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The reticular formation only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The cerebral cortex, especially the prefrontal and association areas', 'Consciousness, abstract thought, memory, and language depend on the cerebral cortex, particularly the association cortices of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes.'),
('9611fcb2-960c-5bf7-880e-f7092011bd0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which of the following is a primary function of the frontal lobe?', '["Voluntary motor control and executive function","Auditory processing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Visual processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Vestibular reflexes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Voluntary motor control and executive function', 'The frontal lobe houses the primary motor cortex (area 4) and prefrontal executive and planning regions; auditory and visual processing are temporal and occipital functions.'),
('d49ddd05-ca4e-53ba-823d-24b78a54a352'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The primary auditory cortex and Wernicke''s language area are located in the:', '["Temporal lobe","Frontal lobe","Parietal lobe","Occipital lobe","Cerebellar vermis"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe', 'The primary auditory cortex (Heschl''s gyrus), Wernicke''s comprehension area, and the hippocampus (memory) all lie in the temporal lobe.'),
('f58a5789-4d56-5156-ba5f-63484a8f2a36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The occipital lobe is involved in:', '["Sensation resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual processing (primary visual cortex)","Motor control mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual processing (primary visual cortex)', 'The occipital lobe contains the striate (V1) visual cortex where visual processing occurs; hearing is a temporal function and somatic sensation is parietal.'),
('87735744-fa12-5d95-941d-a5642a07c94c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Broca''s aphasia is characterized by:', '["Non-fluent speech with preserved comprehension","Fluent speech with impaired comprehension","Reading difficulty","Writing difficulty","Complete loss of speech"]'::jsonb, 'Non-fluent speech with preserved comprehension', 'Broca''s aphasia, from a left inferior frontal lesion, produces effortful non-fluent speech with relatively preserved comprehension.'),
('3801f1bb-1e05-55a0-8447-d948fa199454'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Wernicke''s aphasia is characterized by:', '["Non-fluent speech with preserved comprehension","Reading difficulty only","Fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension","Complete mutism","Writing difficulty only"]'::jsonb, 'Fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension', 'Wernicke''s aphasia, from a left posterior superior temporal lesion, produces fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension.'),
('7c1edf85-4c43-5e33-91f1-2b92e3264b8c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Parkinson''s disease involves degeneration of:', '["Cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain","Glutamatergic neurons in the cortex","GABAergic neurons in the striatum","Serotonergic neurons in the raphe nuclei","Dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta', 'Parkinson''s disease results from loss of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, reducing striatal dopamine and disrupting the basal ganglia motor loop.'),
('4bd3e9b7-7d8b-5ec7-9116-664a1833f41b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The thalamus functions as:', '["An emotion center resulting in decreased functional activity","A sensory relay station for all senses except smell","A motor coordination center mediated by specific receptor activation","A balance center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A reflex center leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sensory relay station for all senses except smell', 'Nearly all sensory pathways except olfaction relay in the thalamus before reaching the cerebral cortex, making it the principal sensory gateway.'),
('b49cb629-fd57-55eb-a334-02aa23aeea78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which region acts as the body''s thermostat, integrating warm and cold signals to trigger heat loss or heat conservation?', '["Preoptic and anterior hypothalamic nuclei","Lateral geniculate nucleus","Cerebellar vermis","Spinal dorsal horn","Basal forebrain cholinergic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Preoptic and anterior hypothalamic nuclei', 'The preoptic and anterior hypothalamic nuclei form the thermoregulatory center: warm signals trigger heat loss and cold signals trigger heat conservation and heat production.'),
('e291aa66-f2c3-5dbf-af35-af3765b2f77c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 30-year-old male presents with ataxia and intention tremor. Which function is most likely impaired?', '["Higher thought resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Language mediated by specific receptor activation","Coordination of voluntary movement","Balance leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of voluntary movement', 'Ataxia and intention tremor indicate cerebellar (hemisphere) dysfunction; the cerebellum coordinates voluntary movement and contributes to balance.'),
('f8514283-6281-5814-8579-66077d7b2e9c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Damage to which brainstem region would produce third (oculomotor) and fourth (trochlear) cranial nerve palsies?', '["Midbrain","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Thalamus","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain', 'The oculomotor (III) and trochlear (IV) nuclei lie in the midbrain; cranial nerve nuclei V-VIII are in the pons and IX-XII mostly in the medulla.'),
('e3498017-7c78-5d26-ac2c-35f51184cb05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Lithium toxicity signs:', '["Tremor, ataxia, seizures, renal failure","HTN, tachycardia","Hepatotoxicity","Bone marrow suppression","Pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, 'Tremor, ataxia, seizures, renal failure', 'Lithium: coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion, seizures.'),
('cbd90430-6532-5340-b90c-dbf0d5281173'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The spinal cord extends from:', '["Foramen magnum to L1-L2 (conus medullaris)","Brain to the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T1 to T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","L1 to S5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C1 to C7 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen magnum to L1-L2 (conus medullaris)', 'In adults the spinal cord runs from the foramen magnum to the L1-L2 level, ending at the conus medullaris, below which lies the cauda equina.'),
('1db8f99c-aaf1-5cdd-a29f-3bf0a91ae652'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The spinal cord has:', '["Central gray matter (butterfly-shaped) surrounded by white matter tracts","Central white matter surrounded by gray matter accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Mixed fibers only mediated by specific receptor activation","Only white matter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Only gray matter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Central gray matter (butterfly-shaped) surrounded by white matter tracts', 'The spinal cord has centrally placed gray matter shaped like a butterfly surrounded by white matter funiculi containing ascending and descending tracts.'),
('a398fd9d-3d5b-57b3-bb19-bfc6085ccc95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The ventral (anterior) horn of the spinal cord contains:', '["Lower motor neurons (motor output to skeletal muscles)","Upper motor neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Interneurons mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory neurons without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Autonomic neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Lower motor neurons (motor output to skeletal muscles)', 'The ventral (anterior) horn contains alpha and gamma lower motor neurons whose axons leave via the ventral roots to innervate skeletal muscle.'),
('c9eb7178-b000-54e0-b51f-10544e83338f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The cerebrum is divided into:', '["Two hemispheres only resulting in decreased functional activity","3 lobes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","5 lobes mediated by specific receptor activation","6 lobes without affecting the primary tissue pathway","4 lobes (frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital)"]'::jsonb, '4 lobes (frontal, parietal, temporal, occipital)', 'Each cerebral hemisphere is divided into four lobes: frontal, parietal, temporal, and occipital, with the insula often considered a fifth.'),
('6a436bc2-7ea3-5102-a230-1b7df5ef07fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The precentral gyrus is the:', '["Auditory cortex","Visual cortex","Primary motor cortex","Wernicke area","Primary sensory cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor cortex', 'The precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4) is the primary motor cortex; lesions here produce contralateral motor weakness.'),
('56f4ca2e-2600-5db2-8c41-9523bf85b18e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'A 70-year-old female develops right-sided weakness after a stroke. Which cortical region is most likely damaged?', '["Precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4)","Postcentral gyrus","Prefrontal cortex","Occipital lobe","Superior temporal gyrus"]'::jsonb, 'Precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4)', 'The primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4) controls contralateral voluntary movement, so damage there causes contralateral weakness.'),
('c4b71575-f949-511f-96d3-a1a210e34516'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which brain region contains the primary motor cortex?', '["Precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4)","Postcentral gyrus","Prefrontal cortex","Occipital lobe","Superior temporal gyrus"]'::jsonb, 'Precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4)', 'The primary motor cortex is located in the precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4) of the frontal lobe, anterior to the central sulcus.'),
('3734127a-a39d-5042-87d4-fb2eabbd2f94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The premotor cortex (Brodmann area 6) primarily functions in:', '["Sensory integration resulting in decreased functional activity","Planning and programming of movements (preparation before execution)","Pain processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Execution of movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proprioception leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Planning and programming of movements (preparation before execution)', 'Area 6 plans and programs voluntary movements in advance of execution, organizing sequences and preparing the motor cortex before action.'),
('30fc76d7-eff7-5c34-aae7-71f4e9c8f64e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the primary function of the cerebellum?', '["Initiation of movement","Fine-tuning (error correction) of movement","Balance","Motor learning","Coordination of movement"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of movement', 'The cerebellum''s principal role is the coordination and fine control of voluntary movement; it also supports balance and motor learning.'),
('4e0fb3f8-a361-5103-9691-0b41d05819f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which option correctly describes Purkinje cells?', '["Firing spontaneously without a defined role","Modulatory cells of the granule layer","The main output cells of the cerebellar cortex","Excitatory (glutamatergic) projection neurons","Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei', 'Purkinje cells are the sole output of the cerebellar cortex; they are inhibitory (GABAergic) and synapse on the deep cerebellar and vestibular nuclei.'),
('7af39029-1ade-56cd-9163-e6862d90b170'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Purkinje cells are:', '["Firing spontaneously without a defined role","Modulatory cells of the granule layer","The main output cells of the cerebellar cortex","Excitatory (glutamatergic) projection neurons","Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei', 'Purkinje cells are inhibitory (GABAergic) and constitute the only output of the cerebellar cortex, projecting to the deep cerebellar nuclei.'),
('9b9cef9c-09a9-5003-8640-11ba0c7704db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Purkinje cells of the cerebellar cortex are:', '["Firing spontaneously without a defined role","Modulatory cells of the granule layer","The main output cells of the cerebellar cortex","Excitatory (glutamatergic) projection neurons","Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei', 'Purkinje cells are GABAergic inhibitory neurons and are the sole output of the cerebellar cortex, synapsing on the deep cerebellar nuclei.'),
('b3e74ebf-d763-5aa9-bfaf-1adb5a1509a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'CSF is produced by the:', '["Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","Arachnoid granulations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple filtration of blood plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningeal veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles', 'CSF is actively secreted by the choroid plexuses of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles; it is not a simple plasma filtrate, and the arachnoid granulations absorb it.'),
('81013c77-67a4-5415-b157-4fa08f6ed761'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Which structure produces cerebrospinal fluid in the brain ventricles?', '["Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","Arachnoid granulations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simple filtration of blood plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebral capillaries without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Meningeal veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles', 'The choroid plexuses, modified ependyma of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles, actively secrete cerebrospinal fluid.'),
('5c0bdf9b-0ed1-5db3-9f5e-e83230da0fbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The conus medullaris is at:', '["L3-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)","S1-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacro-coccygeal level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12-L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)', 'In adults the spinal cord terminates at the conus medullaris near the L1-L2 vertebral level, below which the nerve roots form the cauda equina.'),
('837becaf-a4e1-576c-afdc-d437d27ae622'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'At what vertebral level does the spinal cord terminate in adults?', '["L3-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)","S1-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacro-coccygeal level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12-L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)', 'The adult spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris at the L1-L2 vertebral level; lumbar and sacral roots continue below as the cauda equina.'),
('8b445f80-18a1-5b6a-9fa2-288b100f1e2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The conus medullaris in adults is located at the:', '["L3-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)","S1-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacro-coccygeal level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12-L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)', 'The adult spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris at the L1-L2 level; below this, the cauda equina occupies the vertebral canal.'),
('4ad111b4-3ebd-5585-9a92-c2f9a2fa195d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'The cranial nerves consist of:', '["12 pairs (CN I-XII), comprising purely sensory, purely motor, and mixed nerves","10 pairs, all purely motor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","8 pairs, all purely sensory mediated by specific receptor activation","12 pairs, all purely motor without affecting the primary tissue pathway","12 pairs, all purely sensory leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, '12 pairs (CN I-XII), comprising purely sensory, purely motor, and mixed nerves', 'There are twelve pairs of cranial nerves that are purely sensory, purely motor, or mixed, each associated with specific brainstem nuclei and functions.'),
('0f37d122-ae0a-5368-8a3b-ac73d9da9b96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue is carried by the:', '["Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)","Facial nerve (CN VII) via the chorda tympani","Vagus nerve (CN X)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII)"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve, via its lingual branch, carries taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue; CN VII serves the anterior two-thirds and CN X the epiglottis.'),
('350eb96c-1b94-56ac-a7b7-609e67c812af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Valproic acid teratogenicity:', '["Neural tube defects","Cardiac malformations","Cleft palate","Limb defects","Renal agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Neural tube defects', '2-5% risk of spina bifida; avoid in childbearing women.'),
('9d3d51b7-4d96-5e02-9c55-377a72cdb895'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'What is the corticospinal tract primarily responsible for?', '["Fine voluntary movement, especially of distal limb muscles","Autonomic outflow to the viscera","Balance and posture via the vestibulospinal tract","Pain and temperature sensation","Rhythmic generation of locomotion"]'::jsonb, 'Fine voluntary movement, especially of distal limb muscles', 'The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract subserves fine, skilled voluntary movement, particularly of the distal limb muscles.'),
('a867c71d-1727-5640-9ec8-e9377ab2adb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'How many pairs of spinal nerves emerge from the adult spinal cord?', '["31 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal","30 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 0 coccygeal","31 pairs: 7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 2 coccygeal","29 pairs in total","33 pairs: 9 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 2 coccygeal"]'::jsonb, '31 pairs: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal', 'The adult spinal cord gives rise to 31 pairs of spinal nerves: 8 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 sacral, and 1 coccygeal.'),
('94181e50-c1c1-5827-b75b-e4d8b406d942'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 1', 'Carbamazepine autoinduction via:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","P-gp","UGT"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Induces own metabolism via CYP3A4.'),
('fa9ca98a-f9d6-5c1c-919d-8548df098ccd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'A 40-year-old male presents with spastic weakness, hyperreflexia, and a positive Babinski sign on the right side. (CNS Case #1)', '["Upper motor neurons (corticospinal tract)","Dorsal column pathway","Neuromuscular junction","Cerebellar hemisphere","Lower motor neurons (anterior horn cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Upper motor neurons (corticospinal tract)', 'UMNS signs include spasticity, hyperreflexia, and positive Babinski sign.'),
