('d2b4f8dc-bae6-59bd-b104-25f0bad6a44a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, Which sequence describes the indirect motor pathway of the basal ganglia?', '["Cortex to striatum to GPe (inhibition) to STN (disinhibition) to GPi/SNr (excitation) to reduced thalamus to reduced movement (suppresses unwanted movement)","Causes an imbalance without a defined pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Facilitates movement mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Has no significant effect without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Acts only on tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cortex to striatum to GPe (inhibition) to STN (disinhibition) to GPi/SNr (excitation) to reduced thalamus to reduced movement (suppresses unwanted movement)', 'In the indirect pathway, disinhibition of the subthalamic nucleus increases GPi/SNr output, which inhibits the thalamus and thereby suppresses unwanted movement.'),
('d0423bb4-7c4f-5e32-9a00-3cf7844b5c18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'What is the lesion site in Gerstmann syndrome?', '["Finger agnosia","Left-right disorientation","Acalculia","Alexia with constructional apraxia","Left (dominant) angular gyrus lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Left (dominant) angular gyrus lesion', 'Gerstmann syndrome, comprising agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia and left-right disorientation, is caused by a lesion of the left dominant angular gyrus.'),
('1a2556d5-39c1-52b7-b54e-aed51139157e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'What is the visual disconnection syndrome caused by bilateral parietal-occipital lesions?', '["Only ataxia resulting in decreased functional activity","Oculomotor apraxia (cannot voluntarily shift gaze) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Simultanagnosia (cannot see the whole picture) mediated by specific receptor activation","Bilateral parietal-occipital lesions cause optic ataxia (cannot reach under visual guidance)","Only apraxia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral parietal-occipital lesions cause optic ataxia (cannot reach under visual guidance)', 'Balint syndrome from bilateral parietal-occipital lesions comprises optic ataxia (impaired reaching under visual guidance), oculomotor apraxia and simultanagnosia.'),
('ca012033-a08d-5b0c-9658-7538fa6c2c39'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: Damage to which brainstem region would produce third (oculomotor) and fourth (trochlear) cranial nerve palsies?', '["Midbrain","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Thalamus","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain', 'The oculomotor (III) and trochlear (IV) nuclei lie in the midbrain; cranial nerve nuclei V-VIII are in the pons and IX-XII mostly in the medulla.'),
('594047c0-8a12-5c40-9f36-5e956ee39ab8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cerebellar hemispheric lesions produce deficits that are:', '["Ipsilateral ataxia","Contralateral ataxia","Bilateral symmetric ataxia","Ipsilateral paralysis","Contralateral paralysis"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral ataxia', 'Cerebellar hemisphere lesions produce ipsilateral signs such as ataxia, hypotonia, dysmetria and intention tremor, because each cerebellar hemisphere controls the same side of the body.'),
('0f47b728-ea1d-53e6-9463-8d32fc022b20'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'What is the major efferent pathway of the cerebellum to the motor cortex?', '["Dentatothalamic: dentate to superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP), decussation, red nucleus, then thalamus VA/VL to M1","Fastigiobulbar: fastigial to inferior cerebellar peduncle to vestibular nuclei","Interpositorubral: interposed nucleus to SCP to red nucleus to rubrospinal tract","Only vestibular connections","Through the middle cerebellar peduncle (afferent route)"]'::jsonb, 'Dentatothalamic: dentate to superior cerebellar peduncle (SCP), decussation, red nucleus, then thalamus VA/VL to M1', 'The dentatothalamic pathway exits the cerebellum via the superior cerebellar peduncle, decussates, and projects through the VA/VL thalamus to the primary motor cortex.'),
('ec1980fa-ddc9-5fe9-800a-f2d5cc3a7501'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'How do the basal ganglia and cerebellum differ in motor control?', '["Rigidity resulting in decreased functional activity","Tremor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Error correction (motor learning) mediated by specific receptor activation","Both project through the thalamus to the motor cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Basal ganglia initiate and scale movement (putamen to GPi to VA/VL to SMA); the cerebellum coordinates ongoing movement"]'::jsonb, 'Basal ganglia initiate and scale movement (putamen to GPi to VA/VL to SMA); the cerebellum coordinates ongoing movement', 'The basal ganglia initiate and scale voluntary movement via the putamen-GPi-VA/VL-SMA circuit, while the cerebellum coordinates ongoing movement and motor learning; both project through the thalamus to the motor cortex.'),
('ddca17b5-f7fb-532d-9037-c471b9bfe36b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'What is the master circadian pacemaker and how does it receive light input?', '["Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN): light input via melanopsin retinal ganglion cells and the retinohypothalamic tract","Pineal gland: light input via direct optic nerve fibres accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hippocampus: light input via the fornix mediated by specific receptor activation","Amygdala: light input via the olfactory tract without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebellum: light input via the spinocerebellar tract leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN): light input via melanopsin retinal ganglion cells and the retinohypothalamic tract', 'The SCN is the master circadian clock; it receives light input through melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells via the retinohypothalamic tract and drives pineal melatonin release.'),
('026815f4-5815-500e-895a-7b2ed3c4583a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: Which of the following is a feature of the cerebellar cognitive-affective syndrome (CCAS)?', '["Executive dysfunction","Spasticity","Flaccid paralysis","Resting tremor","Babinski sign"]'::jsonb, 'Executive dysfunction', 'CCAS, associated with posterior lobe (crus) lesions, comprises executive dysfunction, visuospatial impairments, linguistic changes, and flattened affect; spasticity and resting tremor reflect other systems.'),
('7aeb2474-7a0e-543f-ad12-e6b8a8b92c2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which disorder results from a lesion of the subthalamic nucleus (STN)?', '["Huntington disease: tremor at rest","Dystonia: excessive dopamine","Hemiballismus: subthalamic nucleus (STN) lesion","Parkinson disease: dopamine excess","Tourette syndrome: serotonin excess"]'::jsonb, 'Hemiballismus: subthalamic nucleus (STN) lesion', 'A subthalamic nucleus lesion removes the indirect pathway, decreasing GPi output and disinhibiting the thalamus, causing violent flinging movements of the contralateral limbs (hemiballismus).'),
('b8edbd1b-469a-5039-8ff0-cf82ab156e7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, Subcortical aphasia is characteristically associated with:', '["Lesions of the basal ganglia (striatocapsular) or the thalamus","Lesions of Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas alone","Occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Degeneration of the substantia nigra","Spinal cord transection"]'::jsonb, 'Lesions of the basal ganglia (striatocapsular) or the thalamus', 'Aphasia can arise from striatocapsular or thalamic lesions that disrupt cortical-subcortical language loops, producing non-fluent, hypophonic output with relatively intact comprehension.'),
('5e9d74f3-0883-558e-abcf-93d7f857af45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: The dermatome over the occiput and back of the head corresponds to which cervical level?', '["C2","C4","C6","C7","C8"]'::jsonb, 'C2', 'C2 supplies the posterior scalp (occiput); C4 the neck and shoulders, C6 the thumb and index finger, C7 the middle finger, and C8 the ring and little fingers.'),
('a058a1dc-9932-50df-bf7e-3ce7c138e483'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which fibre type mediates fast, sharp (first) pain in the spinothalamic pathway?', '["A-delta fibres","C fibres","A-beta fibres","A-alpha fibres","B fibres"]'::jsonb, 'A-delta fibres', 'Fast, sharp (first) pain is carried by thinly myelinated A-delta fibres, while slow, burning (second) pain is carried by unmyelinated C fibres in the spinothalamic pathway.'),
('4e985be6-29a3-59ae-990a-7e31a63ddc27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: Which cranial nerve provides parasympathetic fibers to the thoracic and abdominal viscera?', '["Preganglionic parasympathetic to the thoracic and abdominal viscera via the vagus (CN X)","Motor supply to the muscles of facial expression accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor supply to the lateral rectus mediated by specific receptor activation","Taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic supply to the parotid gland leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic parasympathetic to the thoracic and abdominal viscera via the vagus (CN X)', 'The vagus nerve carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the thoracic and abdominal organs and also supplies motor innervation to pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles and visceral sensation.'),
('acd0abee-8248-5385-9c0e-a536dfe1a81c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: Where is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) produced?', '["Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles","Arachnoid granulations accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebral cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord gray matter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebral capillaries leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus (modified ependyma) in the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles', 'CSF is actively secreted by the choroid plexuses of the lateral, third, and fourth ventricles; arachnoid granulations absorb it rather than produce it.'),
('5af1cc65-ee9d-508c-81d3-f1fcffe817b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'What is the lesion location for transcortical motor aphasia?', '["Arcuate fasciculus resulting in decreased functional activity","Wernicke area accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Left angular gyrus mediated by specific receptor activation","Medial occipital cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prefrontal cortex (anterior watershed between MCA and ACA) or SMA, producing non-fluent speech with intact repetition"]'::jsonb, 'Prefrontal cortex (anterior watershed between MCA and ACA) or SMA, producing non-fluent speech with intact repetition', 'Transcortical motor aphasia results from lesions of the prefrontal cortex (anterior watershed zone) or supplementary motor area, producing non-fluent speech with preserved repetition.'),
('8c817066-94bb-569e-b494-5209bdc42b8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: The three major anatomical divisions of the brain are:', '["Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem","Cerebral cortex, basal ganglia, and thalamus","Medulla, pons, and midbrain only","Cerebellum and spinal cord only","Hypothalamus, pituitary, and pineal gland"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem', 'The three major parts of the brain are the cerebrum, the cerebellum, and the brainstem; the midbrain, pons, and medulla are subdivisions of the brainstem.'),
('682ed16e-7822-5024-9b03-2a835a9a4eb9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, Left homonymous hemianopia results from:', '["Nasal field loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Full field loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bitemporal field loss mediated by specific receptor activation","A lesion of the right optic tract, radiations, or visual cortex causing loss of the left visual field in both eyes","Loss of the right visual field in both eyes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lesion of the right optic tract, radiations, or visual cortex causing loss of the left visual field in both eyes', 'Right-sided lesions of the optic tract, optic radiations, or occipital cortex cause loss of the left visual field of both eyes (left homonymous hemianopia).'),
('1095111a-109c-5595-be75-519ab1015a0e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is characterised by:', '["Lower motor neuron degeneration only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Upper motor neuron degeneration only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pure sensory neuropathy mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Cerebellar degeneration without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Combined UMN and LMN degeneration, with UMN signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski, pseudobulbar affect) and LMN signs (atrophy, fasciculations)"]'::jsonb, 'Combined UMN and LMN degeneration, with UMN signs (spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski, pseudobulbar affect) and LMN signs (atrophy, fasciculations)', 'ALS involves degeneration of both upper and lower motor neurons, producing spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski and pseudobulbar affect from UMN loss and atrophy with fasciculations from LMN loss.'),
('f2b8d45e-a331-5907-90ee-6154693480ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In diabetic autonomic neuropathy, which abnormality typically appears first?', '["Parasympathetic (vagal) involvement first: resting tachycardia","Sympathetic involvement first: orthostatic hypotension","Gastroparesis first","Gustatory sweating first","Only somatic neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (vagal) involvement first: resting tachycardia', 'Diabetic autonomic neuropathy usually first affects parasympathetic (vagal) fibres, causing resting tachycardia and reduced heart rate variability; sympathetic failure with orthostatic hypotension appears later.'),
('657536d2-e240-53b4-a7d5-e3f64b18da18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'How many pairs of cranial nerves are there and what is the correct numbering?', '["I through X resulting in decreased functional activity","I through XII in order from olfactory (I) to hypoglossal (XII)","1 through 10 mediated by specific receptor activation","I through XIV without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 through 8 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'I through XII in order from olfactory (I) to hypoglossal (XII)', 'There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, numbered I-XII in craniocaudal order, beginning with the olfactory nerve (I) and ending with the hypoglossal nerve (XII).'),
('5ce4dc8e-0363-515a-a1ae-b2b60185cd12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cranial nerve IV (trochlear) provides which function?', '["Parasympathetic","Mixed","Motor to the superior oblique muscle","Motor to the lateral rectus muscle","Sensory"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the superior oblique muscle', 'The trochlear nerve (CN IV) is purely motor and innervates the superior oblique muscle, which intorts and depresses the eye.'),
('1bf6bf22-88e8-5438-a701-e6e948097509'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, The corticospinal tract is:', '["A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord","A visual pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","An autonomic pathway mediated by specific receptor activation","A cerebellar pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An ascending sensory pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord', 'The corticospinal tract is the descending upper motor neuron pathway from the motor cortex to the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord.'),
('4ca338b7-f2e7-5399-9d2b-121db374b651'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cranial nerve VIII (vestibulocochlear) is:', '["Mixed resulting in decreased functional activity","Only hearing accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory: hearing (cochlear) and balance (vestibular)","Motor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory: hearing (cochlear) and balance (vestibular)', 'The vestibulocochlear nerve is purely sensory, carrying hearing (cochlear) and balance (vestibular) information.'),
('ac778f4d-1ae5-5657-bd79-35712a7763d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: How is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) absorbed back into the venous circulation?', '["Lymphatics resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus","Choroid plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus', 'CSF returns to venous blood through arachnoid granulations projecting into the superior sagittal sinus; the choroid plexus produces CSF rather than absorbing it.'),
('9f31f480-3064-5c98-b34b-0f3d26210b25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, Central cord syndrome characteristically causes:', '["Disproportionate weakness of the upper compared with lower limbs, with cape-like suspended sensory loss","Lower limb weakness greater than upper limb weakness accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Ipsilateral weakness with loss of proprioception mediated by specific receptor activation","Bitemporal hemianopia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ataxia with intention tremor leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Disproportionate weakness of the upper compared with lower limbs, with cape-like suspended sensory loss', 'Central cord syndrome (cervical hyperextension or syringomyelia) damages centrally located fibers, so hand and arm function is worse than the legs, with a suspended cape-like sensory loss and relative sacral sparing.'),
('a0166a7a-bca0-5f1c-895e-35bf6e0cb5f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'The blood supply of the brain comes from:', '["Facial arteries only resulting in decreased functional activity","Internal carotid arteries (anterior) and vertebral arteries (posterior)","Subclavian veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Jugular veins only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","External carotid arteries only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid arteries (anterior) and vertebral arteries (posterior)', 'The brain is supplied by the internal carotid arteries anteriorly and the vertebral arteries posteriorly, which connect in the circle of Willis.'),
('bc30bf2b-e443-5c70-9b35-8fcc595444cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cranial nerve VII (facial) is:', '["Sensory only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor only mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Mixed: motor to the facial muscles, taste to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and parasympathetic fibres to the salivary and lacrimal glands","Only taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed: motor to the facial muscles, taste to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, and parasympathetic fibres to the salivary and lacrimal glands', 'CN VII is a mixed nerve carrying motor fibres to the facial muscles, taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and parasympathetic fibres to the salivary and lacrimal glands.'),
('edb4013a-ac9a-5e85-a3cd-81a19edc3e82'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which cranial nerve provides motor innervation to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles?', '["Motor to the larynx resulting in decreased functional activity","Mixed accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Motor to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles","Sensory without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Parasympathetic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; weakness causes difficulty turning the head and shrugging the shoulder.'),
('f411de4b-b452-585c-9e3f-5634f4337c9a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'The limbic system is primarily involved in:', '["Language","Motor coordination","Vision","Emotion and memory","Audition"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion and memory', 'The limbic system mediates emotion, motivation and memory, involving structures such as the amygdala, hippocampus and cingulate gyrus.'),
('59c38da6-0e8a-5287-8342-19d5cf36f9da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, The thalamus is best described as:', '["A reflex center resulting in decreased functional activity","An emotion center accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A motor coordination center mediated by specific receptor activation","A balance center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A sensory relay station for all senses except smell"]'::jsonb, 'A sensory relay station for all senses except smell', 'The thalamus receives nearly all sensory input (except olfaction) and relays it to the cortex; it also participates in motor and arousal circuits.'),
('90658014-5d0f-5929-b8df-1b3c86df0949'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: The corticobulbar tract:', '["Travels only to the spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Runs from the motor cortex to cranial nerve motor nuclei (bilateral except lower face)","Projects to the basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Innervates only limb muscles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Projects to the cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Runs from the motor cortex to cranial nerve motor nuclei (bilateral except lower face)', 'The corticobulbar tract connects the motor cortex with the lower motor neuron nuclei of the cranial nerves; most are innervated bilaterally, while the subnucleus for the lower facial muscles receives only contralateral input.'),
('2654bf67-da45-5982-9dfb-251ab907d0f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, Global aphasia is most characteristically caused by:', '["A large left-hemisphere (MCA) lesion involving both Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas, with severe impairment of all language modalities","A lesion of Wernicke''s area alone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A lesion of Broca''s area alone mediated by specific receptor activation","An arcuate fasciculus lesion without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A lesion of the left supplementary motor area leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A large left-hemisphere (MCA) lesion involving both Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas, with severe impairment of all language modalities', 'Global aphasia follows large left MCA infarcts destroying both anterior (Broca) and posterior (Wernicke) language zones, causing non-fluent output, poor comprehension, and impaired repetition together with hemiparesis.'),
('6399f95e-52a6-58a0-aa0d-b55bcf792e15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'The 12 cranial nerves are numbered:', '["I through XII in order from olfactory to hypoglossal","I through XIV accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","1 through 10 mediated by specific receptor activation","I through X without affecting the primary tissue pathway","1 through 8 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'I through XII in order from olfactory to hypoglossal', 'The 12 cranial nerves are numbered I-XII in craniocaudal order, beginning with the olfactory nerve (I) and ending with the hypoglossal nerve (XII).'),
('94e41155-b4e4-5927-b876-a7444922fc8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: What is the primary function of the lateral corticospinal tract?', '["Balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Vision only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hearing only mediated by specific receptor activation","Emotion only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor function (skilled voluntary movement)"]'::jsonb, 'Motor function (skilled voluntary movement)', 'The lateral corticospinal tract, after crossing at the pyramids, mediates skilled, fine voluntary motor control, especially of the distal limb muscles.'),
('88855c0c-f22d-5f8d-af05-97316cff2999'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, How is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) absorbed back into the venous circulation?', '["Lymphatics resulting in decreased functional activity","Cerebral capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus","Choroid plexus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal veins leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid granulations (villi) projecting into the superior sagittal sinus', 'CSF returns to venous blood through arachnoid granulations projecting into the superior sagittal sinus; the choroid plexus produces CSF rather than absorbing it.'),
('fd204f7e-6ef5-5290-9679-a48bf4f34b38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which cranial nerve is mixed (sensory and motor) and innervates the palate, pharynx and larynx?', '["Sensory only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only parasympathetic mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Only to the heart without affecting the primary tissue pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN X (vagus): mixed, with motor and sensory fibres to the palate, pharynx and larynx, and parasympathetic fibres to the thoracic and abdominal viscera"]'::jsonb, 'CN X (vagus): mixed, with motor and sensory fibres to the palate, pharynx and larynx, and parasympathetic fibres to the thoracic and abdominal viscera', 'The vagus (CN X) is a mixed nerve providing motor and sensory fibres to the palate, pharynx and larynx, plus parasympathetic supply to the thoracic and abdominal viscera.'),
('8d58b8bb-29d4-52d8-9058-408a8749d3e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, What is the normal anatomical extent of the spinal cord in adults?', '["Foramen magnum to L1-L2","C1 to L5","C1 to S5","Medulla to sacrum","Brainstem to coccyx"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen magnum to L1-L2', 'In adults the spinal cord extends from the foramen magnum to the L1-L2 vertebral level; the lumbar and sacral nerve roots continue below this as the cauda equina.'),
('622701b6-5dce-55f7-a763-8da575aa16b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: The locus coeruleus is best described as a:', '["Norepinephrine-containing nucleus in the pons with widespread projections that promote arousal","Cholinergic nucleus of the basal forebrain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Serotonergic nucleus of the raphe mediated by specific receptor activation","Dopaminergic nucleus of the midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GABAergic nucleus of the thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine-containing nucleus in the pons with widespread projections that promote arousal', 'The locus coeruleus is a small nucleus in the dorsal pons rich in noradrenergic neurons whose diffuse projections promote arousal, attention, and the stress response.'),
('185e2cce-5e65-5ba8-8cd0-0fc6adea92e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: The spinal cord extends from:', '["Foramen magnum to L1-L2 (conus medullaris)","Brain to the sacrum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T1 to T12 mediated by specific receptor activation","L1 to S5 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","C1 to C7 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen magnum to L1-L2 (conus medullaris)', 'In adults the spinal cord runs from the foramen magnum to the L1-L2 level, ending at the conus medullaris, below which lies the cauda equina.'),
('73cb367b-7476-5160-91ee-b1dc4f2735f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which structure passes through the cavernous sinus?', '["Internal carotid artery and CN III, IV, V1, V2 and VI","CN II (optic) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","CN VIII (vestibulocochlear) mediated by specific receptor activation","CN XII (hypoglossal) without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Facial nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid artery and CN III, IV, V1, V2 and VI', 'The cavernous sinus contains the internal carotid artery and CN III, IV, V1, V2 and VI; cavernous sinus pathology can cause ophthalmoplegia and facial pain.'),
('75c62269-7f7e-5de0-8d5b-093a6bac4fad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'The anterior and posterior circulations of the brain arise from which vessels?', '["Jugular veins only resulting in decreased functional activity","Facial arteries only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Subclavian veins mediated by specific receptor activation","Internal carotid arteries (anterior) and vertebral arteries (posterior)","External carotid arteries only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid arteries (anterior) and vertebral arteries (posterior)', 'The anterior cerebral circulation arises from the internal carotid arteries, while the posterior circulation arises from the vertebral arteries.'),
('b2f5093c-4ee9-55f1-861b-d56c8388235d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'What is the most common cause of ischaemic stroke?', '["Metabolic disorder only","Thrombosis","Embolism","Hypoperfusion of the cerebral arteries","Venous congestion only"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombosis', 'Thrombosis, most often from large-artery atherosclerosis or small-vessel disease, is the most common cause of ischaemic stroke.'),
('d2b3133a-778a-5289-b9b6-559508e8652c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: What is the primary function of the cerebellum?', '["Initiation of movement","Fine-tuning (error correction) of movement","Balance","Motor learning","Coordination of movement"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of movement', 'The cerebellum''s principal role is the coordination and fine control of voluntary movement; it also supports balance and motor learning.'),
('8e677a74-7351-5d02-bd16-c87c874db94d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cranial nerve I (olfactory) is which type of nerve?', '["Motor","Special motor","Mixed","Sensory (smell)","Parasympathetic"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory (smell)', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I) is purely sensory and mediates the sense of smell.'),
('cd2671b8-2fbc-531f-b5e5-faa53fb61516'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, The conus medullaris is at:', '["L3-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)","S1-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacro-coccygeal level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12-L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)', 'In adults the spinal cord terminates at the conus medullaris near the L1-L2 vertebral level, below which the nerve roots form the cauda equina.'),
('dd81d048-58da-5ad9-a821-dc63c73f1043'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: The precentral gyrus is the:', '["Auditory cortex","Visual cortex","Primary motor cortex","Wernicke area","Primary sensory cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor cortex', 'The precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4) is the primary motor cortex; lesions here produce contralateral motor weakness.'),
('ab6ddbf1-1ec7-51c7-aae8-57d3e4af6e06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, A 45-year-old female presents with progressive muscle weakness and atrophy. Which findings best fit a lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion?', '["Fasciculations with areflexia","Spasticity","Hyperreflexia","Positive Babinski sign","Clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Fasciculations with areflexia', 'Progressive weakness and atrophy are hallmarks of LMN involvement, accompanied by fasciculations and reduced or absent reflexes; hyperreflexia, spasticity, clonus, and Babinski sign indicate UMN disease.'),
('cfb1630d-abc0-5f78-b7c4-4ec9377b20c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: Which specific thalamic nucleus relays somatosensation from the body (trunk and limbs) to the primary sensory cortex?', '["VPM (face)","MGN (hearing)","LGN (vision)","VPL (body sensation)","VLP (motor)"]'::jsonb, 'VPL (body sensation)', 'The ventral posterolateral nucleus (VPL) receives the medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract and projects to the somatic sensory cortex for the body; the VPM serves the face.'),
('00146ab4-74e4-516d-8356-5fefa18969fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: The blood-brain barrier is formed by:', '["Neurons and glia resulting in decreased functional activity","Arachnoid mater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pia mater only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dura mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells', 'The blood-brain barrier is formed by tight junctions sealing the cerebral capillary endothelial cells, reinforced by astrocyte end-feet and pericytes.'),
('2bbbe613-c19f-5a80-b224-271e51bf6add'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cranial nerve XII (hypoglossal) is:', '["Motor to the palate resulting in decreased functional activity","Parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory mediated by specific receptor activation","Motor to the tongue muscles (except palatoglossus)","Mixed leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the tongue muscles (except palatoglossus)', 'The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) is purely motor to the intrinsic and most extrinsic tongue muscles, except palatoglossus; a unilateral lesion deviates the tongue toward the lesion.'),
('ad9d8278-b8e8-52b3-86e8-f239e68d8c91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which brain structure is the primary centre for fear processing and emotional memory?', '["Language resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor coordination accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Balance mediated by specific receptor activation","Emotion processing (especially fear and aggression) and emotional memory","Vision leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion processing (especially fear and aggression) and emotional memory', 'The amygdala processes fear and aggression and forms emotional memories, attaching emotional significance to experiences.'),
('bdbfa32f-3208-5be8-acc2-88e576d78f12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Phenytoin toxicity:', '["Cerebellar ataxia, nystagmus, gingival hyperplasia","Weight gain accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hepatotoxicity mediated by specific receptor activation","Pancreatitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Nephrotoxicity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar ataxia, nystagmus, gingival hyperplasia', 'Dose-dependent cerebellar symptoms + chronic gingival hyperplasia.'),
('1fc74362-b192-5761-b7ca-8adcd6423f01'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: The medial medullary (Dejerine) syndrome, due to anterior spinal artery occlusion, is characterized by:', '["Ipsilateral hypoglossal palsy, contralateral hemiparesis, and contralateral loss of proprioception","Ipsilateral ataxia, Horner syndrome, and contralateral loss of pain and temperature","Vertigo, nystagmus, and dysphagia with hiccups","Lateral gaze palsy and facial droop","Third nerve palsy with contralateral tremor"]'::jsonb, 'Ipsilateral hypoglossal palsy, contralateral hemiparesis, and contralateral loss of proprioception', 'Dejerine (medial medullary) syndrome from anterior spinal artery occlusion involves the pyramid (contralateral hemiparesis), medial lemniscus (contralateral proprioception loss), and hypoglossal nerve (ipsilateral tongue deviation).'),
('b989f293-dfa1-5616-af01-2244c517fb2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'The middle cerebral artery (MCA) supplies:', '["The occipital lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","The lateral surface of the hemisphere (face and arm motor and sensory cortex)","The medial surface mediated by specific receptor activation","The brainstem without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The lateral surface of the hemisphere (face and arm motor and sensory cortex)', 'The MCA supplies the lateral surface of the hemisphere, including the face and arm areas of the motor and sensory cortex.'),
('d3ca4390-7b5e-50e1-b0f5-485f73da2ebd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'The anterior cerebral artery (ACA) supplies:', '["The occipital lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","The medial surface of the hemisphere (leg motor and sensory cortex)","The basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","The lateral surface without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The medial surface of the hemisphere (leg motor and sensory cortex)', 'The ACA supplies the medial surface of the hemisphere, including the leg areas of the motor and sensory cortex; occlusion causes contralateral leg weakness.'),
('ce63fe36-e8e6-5880-994a-56ad3f095f17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'The posterior cerebral artery (PCA) supplies:', '["The parietal lobe resulting in decreased functional activity","The cerebellum accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","The occipital lobe (visual cortex) and the inferomedial temporal lobe","The frontal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","The brainstem leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'The occipital lobe (visual cortex) and the inferomedial temporal lobe', 'The PCA supplies the occipital lobe, including the visual cortex, and the inferomedial temporal lobe; occlusion causes contralateral hemianopia.'),
('5fe23193-e5db-5ecd-933e-48f9d493fc73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'A stroke (cerebrovascular accident) is:', '["A sudden neurological deficit due to ischaemia or haemorrhage in the brain","A seizure accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A head injury mediated by specific receptor activation","A brain tumour without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A migraine leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A sudden neurological deficit due to ischaemia or haemorrhage in the brain', 'A stroke is a sudden focal neurological deficit caused by cerebral ischaemia or haemorrhage.'),
('cc335d91-80f4-54d3-9529-9e63b3d32b97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which pathological process results from Virchow''s triad of endothelial injury, stasis and hypercoagulability?', '["Electrolyte imbalance","Haemorrhage only","Metabolic disorder only","Venous congestion only","Thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 'Thrombosis', 'Virchow''s triad (endothelial injury, stasis and hypercoagulability) predisposes to thrombosis, a common mechanism of ischaemic stroke.'),
('7775967b-6ecb-5905-9dac-fe742ee063f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: The principal source of mossy fibers to the cerebellar hemispheres is the:', '["Vestibular nuclei","Cerebral cortex directly","Inferior olive","Spinal cord directly","Pontine nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Pontine nuclei', 'Mossy fibers arise mainly from the pontine nuclei, the largest relay from the cerebral cortex, and also from the spinal cord and vestibular nuclei; climbing fibers come from the inferior olive.'),
('2935dc6a-e12d-50b4-8afb-45644901a5c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Multiple sclerosis (MS) is:', '["An autoimmune demyelinating disease of the CNS (multifocal white matter lesions)","A vascular disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A degenerative disease mediated by specific receptor activation","An infectious disease without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A metabolic disease leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'An autoimmune demyelinating disease of the CNS (multifocal white matter lesions)', 'MS is an autoimmune demyelinating disease producing multifocal CNS white-matter plaques, typically with a relapsing-remitting course.'),
('bb62e0eb-b1f9-5fdb-95cc-ebb4e9bffe93'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: What is the ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) and where is it located?', '["A spinal tract resulting in decreased functional activity","A cranial nerve nucleus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A thalamic nucleus mediated by specific receptor activation","A part of the cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal and consciousness"]'::jsonb, 'A network of neurons in the brainstem involved in arousal and consciousness', 'The ARAS is a network of neurons within the brainstem reticular formation that maintains arousal and consciousness via projections to the thalamus and cortex.'),
('c8463def-3bdd-581d-a7fc-63d8ac0d4132'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, Which option correctly describes Purkinje cells?', '["Firing spontaneously without a defined role","Modulatory cells of the granule layer","The main output cells of the cerebellar cortex","Excitatory (glutamatergic) projection neurons","Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory (GABAergic) neurons that project to the deep cerebellar nuclei', 'Purkinje cells are the sole output of the cerebellar cortex; they are inhibitory (GABAergic) and synapse on the deep cerebellar and vestibular nuclei.'),
('afdd7c37-5df1-544a-a510-eaf1e5e9309c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cranial nerve IV (trochlear) is:', '["Mixed","Sensory","Motor to the lateral rectus muscle","Parasympathetic","Motor to the superior oblique muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the superior oblique muscle', 'The trochlear nerve is purely motor to the superior oblique muscle and is the only cranial nerve to exit the brainstem dorsally.'),
('7b441aeb-f22b-5672-bf1f-ab11f28d5a17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which cranial nerve innervates the lateral rectus muscle?', '["Motor to the medial rectus muscle","Mixed","Sensory","Parasympathetic","Motor to the lateral rectus muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Motor to the lateral rectus muscle', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) innervates the lateral rectus muscle to abduct the eye; its palsy causes convergent strabismus.'),
('8ee9bf85-2a86-5917-b29c-ce8e302235d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Gabapentin used for:', '["Neuropathic pain/partial seizures","GAD accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Insomnia mediated by specific receptor activation","Schizophrenia without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Bipolar leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Neuropathic pain/partial seizures', 'Binds alpha-2-delta calcium channel subunit.'),
('9fcc1c57-8adb-5127-b387-c6a203c254d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: What is the pupillary finding in Horner syndrome?', '["Miosis (a constricted pupil)","Mydriasis (a dilated pupil)","An oval, irregular pupil","A hippus pupil","Light-near dissociation"]'::jsonb, 'Miosis (a constricted pupil)', 'Interruption of the sympathetic pathway in Horner syndrome removes sympathetic tone to the iris dilator, causing miosis (constricted pupil) together with ptosis, anhidrosis, and slight enophthalmos.'),
('991644e2-4efd-578c-8e6c-6055d4d8fa84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Cranial nerve IX (glossopharyngeal) is:', '["Sensory only resulting in decreased functional activity resulting in decreased functional activity","Motor only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only taste mediated by specific receptor activation mediated by specific receptor activation","Mixed: motor to the stylopharyngeus muscle, taste to the posterior one-third of the tongue, and parasympathetic fibres to the parotid gland","Parasympathetic to the parotid only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed: motor to the stylopharyngeus muscle, taste to the posterior one-third of the tongue, and parasympathetic fibres to the parotid gland', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve is mixed: it is motor to the stylopharyngeus muscle, transmits taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue, and carries parasympathetic fibres to the parotid gland.'),
('1ff06cae-d0d6-5b5f-a14c-0a73069f1dfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: Which cranial nerve is motor only and innervates the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles?', '["Motor only, supplying the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles","Special sensory nerve accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory only mediated by specific receptor activation","Parasympathetic nerve without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Mixed nerve leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Motor only, supplying the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles', 'The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) is purely motor, innervating the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles; its paralysis causes shoulder droop and weak head rotation.'),
('b158c855-5c6d-51a3-9940-a97416476fa3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: The occipital lobe is involved in:', '["Sensation resulting in decreased functional activity","Visual processing (primary visual cortex)","Motor control mediated by specific receptor activation","Auditory processing without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Taste leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Visual processing (primary visual cortex)', 'The occipital lobe contains the striate (V1) visual cortex where visual processing occurs; hearing is a temporal function and somatic sensation is parietal.'),
('46914efc-76e5-54a8-952f-2aef4c9c14de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, What happens when light is shone into one eye, and which reflex pathway mediates this?', '["Constriction of both pupils (direct and consensual); afferent via CN II, efferent via parasympathetic (Edinger-Westphal) fibers of CN III","Constriction of the stimulated eye only (no consensual response) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Constriction of the opposite eye only mediated by specific receptor activation","No response in either eye without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dilatation of both pupils leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Constriction of both pupils (direct and consensual); afferent via CN II, efferent via parasympathetic (Edinger-Westphal) fibers of CN III', 'Light in one eye normally constricts both pupils via the direct and consensual light reflexes: afferent limb CN II, efferent limb the Edinger-Westphal (parasympathetic) fibers of CN III.'),
('8dd6da47-c372-56bf-a46a-65c8572810a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Conduction aphasia is characterised by:', '["Non-fluent speech resulting in decreased functional activity","Poor comprehension accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Complete language loss mediated by specific receptor activation","Fluent speech with intact repetition without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Poor repetition with fluent speech and relatively good comprehension (arcuate fasciculus lesion, parietal-temporal)"]'::jsonb, 'Poor repetition with fluent speech and relatively good comprehension (arcuate fasciculus lesion, parietal-temporal)', 'Conduction aphasia, from arcuate fasciculus damage in the parietal-temporal region, causes fluent speech with markedly impaired repetition but relatively good comprehension.'),
('efa1f73f-7a40-5f13-849b-d45d558259f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, Which neurotransmitter is primarily released at the neuromuscular junction?', '["Acetylcholine (ACh)","Serotonin","Norepinephrine","Dopamine","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine (ACh)', 'Acetylcholine is the neurotransmitter released from somatic motor nerve endings; it acts on nicotinic receptors of the motor end plate to trigger muscle contraction.'),
('91597939-19f0-5aad-a00f-f4b8cb0152b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Benzodiazepine OD antidote:', '["Flumazenil","Naloxone","NAC","Physostigmine","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'Flumazenil', 'Competitive antagonist at GABA-A benzodiazepine site.'),
('dd1a9040-cc87-504c-83b6-89c62dbd65eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which structure relays all sensory pathways except olfaction to the cerebral cortex?', '["Cerebral cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cerebellum mediated by specific receptor activation","Spinal cord without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus: relays all sensory pathways except olfaction, plus motor and cognitive loops"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus: relays all sensory pathways except olfaction, plus motor and cognitive loops', 'The thalamus is the relay station for all sensory pathways except olfaction, which projects directly to the piriform cortex, and it also carries motor and cognitive loops.'),
('d356d848-0129-5c86-b91c-97571765907b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'What EEG pattern is characteristic of deep sleep (stage 3-4)?', '["EEG synchronisation (slow waves)","Desynchronised EEG (like awake)","Low-voltage fast activity","Alpha rhythm (8-13 Hz)","Beta rhythm (13-30 Hz)"]'::jsonb, 'EEG synchronisation (slow waves)', 'Deep sleep (stages 3-4, N3) is characterised by large-amplitude synchronised slow waves on the EEG.'),
('40964116-bd92-563d-b725-3b5f1142dcc9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: A 40-year-old female presents with ataxia and intention tremor. Which of the following best explains her findings?', '["Vision resulting in decreased functional activity","Sensation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Coordination of voluntary movement","Balance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Motor learning leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Coordination of voluntary movement', 'Ataxia and intention tremor indicate cerebellar (hemisphere) dysfunction; the cerebellum coordinates voluntary movement and contributes to balance and motor learning.'),
('2a3d0b3d-7e7b-503c-a349-ba57760444c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, Adie''s (tonic) pupil is best explained by:', '["A brisk light response resulting in decreased functional activity","Denervation hypersensitivity of the ciliary ganglion producing a large pupil that reacts slowly to light and accommodation","Light-near dissociation with miosis mediated by specific receptor activation","An irregular pupil with segmental vermiform movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","A small pupil with anhidrosis and ptosis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Denervation hypersensitivity of the ciliary ganglion producing a large pupil that reacts slowly to light and accommodation', 'Adie syndrome follows postganglionic parasympathetic (ciliary ganglion) damage; denervation hypersensitivity leaves a large pupil that reacts minimally to light but slowly to accommodation, often with vermiform iris movement.'),
('1a133ae6-a6ad-5bb8-8ae1-4e6bcc515926'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which option correctly describes cranial nerve I (olfactory)?', '["Motor","Sensory (smell)","Mixed","Parasympathetic","Special motor"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory (smell)', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I) is purely sensory and mediates the sense of smell.'),
('e0a64a72-f0b5-5e04-a5d5-5e351e816751'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'General anaesthetics work by:', '["Enhancing GABA-A","Blocking NMDA only","Glycine activation","Na+ channel blockade","Opioid receptor stimulation"]'::jsonb, 'Enhancing GABA-A', 'Most enhance GABA-A receptor function.'),
('4857b495-274a-5b23-8c9e-5eae5962b05d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'During therapeutic management of CNS Pt2, Left homonymous hemianopia results from:', '["Nasal field loss resulting in decreased functional activity","Full field loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Bitemporal field loss mediated by specific receptor activation","A lesion of the right optic tract, radiations, or visual cortex causing loss of the left visual field in both eyes","Loss of the right visual field in both eyes leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A lesion of the right optic tract, radiations, or visual cortex causing loss of the left visual field in both eyes', 'Right-sided lesions of the optic tract, optic radiations, or occipital cortex cause loss of the left visual field of both eyes (left homonymous hemianopia).'),
('02b1fc85-0299-5428-b4a6-90dc1f958ff9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: The ventral tegmental area (VTA) is characterized by:', '["Dopaminergic neurons (mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways) mediating reward","Cholinergic neurons projecting to the cortex","Substantia nigra pars compacta neurons","Serotonergic neurons of the raphe","Glutamatergic neurons of the locus coeruleus"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic neurons (mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways) mediating reward', 'VTA dopaminergic neurons project through the mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex, mediating reward, motivation, and reinforcement.'),
('678ea804-a0de-572b-8254-5e3399775343'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, Which of the following is a component of the basal ganglia?', '["Caudate nucleus","Hippocampus","Lateral geniculate nucleus","Cerebellar dentate nucleus","Sensory cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Caudate nucleus', 'The basal ganglia comprise the caudate, putamen, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, and substantia nigra; the hippocampus and geniculate nuclei are not components.'),
('d279fb6d-b048-55d0-b004-7db3a456fdd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: The premotor cortex (area 6) is involved in:', '["Sensory integration resulting in decreased functional activity","Planning and programming of movements (preparation before execution)","Pain processing mediated by specific receptor activation","Execution of movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Proprioception leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Planning and programming of movements (preparation before execution)', 'The premotor cortex and supplementary motor area plan and program voluntary movements in advance of execution, organizing sequences and preparing the motor cortex.'),
('df47cc54-03e5-56b3-84c1-bb3178fc0963'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Ketamine mechanism:', '["NMDA antagonism","GABA-A enhancement","Mu agonism","Na+ blockade","Muscarinic activation"]'::jsonb, 'NMDA antagonism', 'Dissociative anaesthesia with maintained airways.'),
('e7a08047-d82f-5e71-9101-403d49e2ec7d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'TCA overdose most concerning ECG:', '["QRS widening","T-wave peaking","ST elevation","U waves","J waves"]'::jsonb, 'QRS widening', 'Na+ channel blockade predicts seizures/arrhythmias.'),
('044e0f34-beec-5ad9-bbf8-65764a5d8e1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: The raphe nuclei are best characterized as:', '["Serotonergic neurons (midbrain/pons/medulla) that modulate pain and sleep","Cholinergic neurons of the basal forebrain","Noradrenergic neurons of the locus coeruleus","Dopaminergic neurons of the ventral tegmentum","GABAergic inhibitory interneurons"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonergic neurons (midbrain/pons/medulla) that modulate pain and sleep', 'Raphe nuclei contain serotonergic neurons that project to the dorsal horn and forebrain; descending raphe-spinal pathways inhibit pain transmission and the system participates in sleep regulation.'),
('0774e7f7-71ab-596c-aab7-d4c6b2f68ed0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, The blood-brain barrier is formed by:', '["Neurons and glia resulting in decreased functional activity","Arachnoid mater accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pia mater only mediated by specific receptor activation","Dura mater without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells', 'The blood-brain barrier is formed by tight junctions sealing the cerebral capillary endothelial cells, reinforced by astrocyte end-feet and pericytes.'),
('0c8e460a-8a30-57d4-8993-dcfc1ac270e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: First-line for GAD:', '["SSRIs","Benzodiazepines","Barbiturates","Antipsychotics","Opioids"]'::jsonb, 'SSRIs', 'SSRIs are first-line for GAD (efficacy, safety, no dependence).'),
('6c9a8b73-4d01-5fcc-8ba3-a7becba6a8c4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in CNS Pt2: Which of the following is NOT part of the brainstem?', '["Midbrain","Pons","Medulla oblongata","Thalamus","Reticular formation"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus', 'The brainstem comprises the midbrain, pons, and medulla oblongata, along with the reticular formation; the thalamus belongs to the diencephalon.'),
('3ebe3912-3c01-587b-a676-1c57d38ba8cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'TCA overdose treatment:', '["Sodium bicarbonate","Flumazenil","Physostigmine","Naloxone","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium bicarbonate', 'Alkalinises serum, reduces cardiac Na+ channel binding.'),
('7bebf802-690c-5677-8806-0e11a0a28762'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: The distinguishing feature of transcortical motor aphasia is:', '["Non-fluent speech with intact repetition","Fluent speech with impaired repetition","Non-fluent speech with impaired repetition","Isolated word-finding difficulty","Impaired comprehension with preserved repetition"]'::jsonb, 'Non-fluent speech with intact repetition', 'Transcortical motor aphasia is non-fluent but repetition is spared because the arcuate fasciculus and perisylvian language areas remain intact, typically after ACA territory (SMA) strokes; echolalia may occur.'),
('b04117ea-ba97-5bdc-88ac-a37a186151b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, What is the Babinski sign and what does it indicate?', '["Normal plantar flexion response resulting in decreased functional activity","Dorsiflexion of the big toe with fanning of the other toes on stroking the sole (UMN lesion)","Plantar flexion of the toes mediated by specific receptor activation","Withdrawal of the limb from the stimulus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Absence of any plantar response leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflexion of the big toe with fanning of the other toes on stroking the sole (UMN lesion)', 'Stroking the lateral sole normally produces plantar flexion of the toes; dorsiflexion of the big toe with fanning of the remaining toes indicates an upper motor neuron (corticospinal) lesion.'),
('7b154a56-026d-5ba1-abd2-a961fb5ed72d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: The conus medullaris in adults is located at the:', '["L3-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)","S1-S2 mediated by specific receptor activation","Sacro-coccygeal level without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T12-L1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2 in adults (terminal end of the spinal cord)', 'The adult spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris at the L1-L2 level; below this, the cauda equina occupies the vertebral canal.'),
('8a349ffd-2b66-5f6f-a8dc-e5b34fd57b8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'SSRIs inhibit reuptake of:', '["Serotonin only","5-HT and NE","Dopamine only","GABA","ACh"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin only', 'Selectively block SERT.'),
('023a0e43-8fa1-5c5f-9959-3719353b59c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Which cranial nerve is purely sensory?', '["Motor only resulting in decreased functional activity","Only parasympathetic accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only to the heart mediated by specific receptor activation","Sensory only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","CN VIII (vestibulocochlear): purely sensory for hearing and balance"]'::jsonb, 'CN VIII (vestibulocochlear): purely sensory for hearing and balance', 'Among the cranial nerves, CN VIII is purely sensory, carrying only auditory and vestibular information with no motor fibres.'),
('270aa77c-c47d-51c7-97a0-b2df1ce90e2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'Regarding clinical cases in CNS Pt2: What is the corticospinal tract and what is its function?', '["An ascending sensory pathway resulting in decreased functional activity","A visual pathway accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord controlling voluntary movement","A cerebellar pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","An autonomic pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'A descending motor pathway (upper motor neuron) from the cortex to the spinal cord controlling voluntary movement', 'The corticospinal tract is the principal descending motor pathway, an upper motor neuron tract from the cortex to the spinal cord that drives voluntary skilled movement.'),
('fb8f3f58-bdc6-5e92-aedb-950288d9b96d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'In the study of CNS Pt2, The cerebrum is best described as:', '["Two cerebral hemispheres connected by the corpus callosum","Two hemispheres that are not interconnected","A single undivided mass","Four lobes plus the brainstem","The cerebellum and its peduncles"]'::jsonb, 'Two cerebral hemispheres connected by the corpus callosum', 'The cerebrum consists of two hemispheres joined by the corpus callosum, the largest white-matter commissure, which allows interhemispheric transfer of information.'),
('582d3e99-fec7-50fb-91cd-609aba99d37f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Central Nervous System - CNS Part 2', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding CNS Pt2: Which of the following findings is characteristic of an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion?', '["Spasticity with hyperreflexia and an extensor plantar response","Flaccid paralysis with muscle atrophy","Fasciculations and areflexia","Wasting with fasciculations in the affected limb","Areflexic flaccid tone"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity with hyperreflexia and an extensor plantar response', 'UMN lesions (cortex or spinal cord above the anterior horn) produce spastic paralysis, hyperreflexia, clonus, and a Babinski sign without early wasting; LMN lesions cause flaccid paresis, atrophy, and fasciculations.'),
('64bd2485-67b8-5402-a985-6f9d676e3d92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The approximate total refractive power of the human eye is:', '["+30 D","+40 D","+10 D","+20 D","+60 D"]'::jsonb, '+60 D', 'The unaccommodated human eye focuses parallel light with a total refractive power of about 60 D. The cornea contributes roughly 43 D and the crystalline lens about 15-17 D.'),
('aad34b72-32fd-5d6b-898d-22f8a3f60eb0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The approximate refractive power of the crystalline lens in the resting (unaccommodated) eye is:', '["20 D","10 D","17 D","30 D","43 D"]'::jsonb, '17 D', 'At rest the crystalline lens provides about 15-17 D of the eye''s total refractive power of roughly 60 D. The cornea supplies the remaining 43 D.'),
('59704f17-ef64-547f-9aea-71d3ca3b97cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What is the term for the normal refractive state of the eye?', '["Astigmatism","Emmetropia","Nearsightedness","Farsightedness","Presbyopia"]'::jsonb, 'Emmetropia', 'Emmetropia is the normal refractive state in which parallel light rays focus exactly on the retina without accommodation. Myopia, hyperopia, astigmatism, and presbyopia are refractive errors.'),
('c6983ee3-c232-5e74-ba20-2bce8fa831db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Myopia is corrected with:', '["Concave (minus) lenses","Convex (plus) lenses","Prisms","Cylindrical lenses","Bifocal lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Concave (minus) lenses', 'The myopic eye focuses parallel rays in front of the retina. Concave (minus, diverging) lenses spread the light so it comes to focus on the retina.'),
('6cd51dcf-84d1-5d25-ae5b-103227d57896'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Hyperopia (farsightedness) is due to:', '["Increased corneal power","Eyeball too short","Eyeball too long","Excessive lens curvature","Increased axial length"]'::jsonb, 'Eyeball too short', 'In hyperopia the eyeball is too short, so parallel light focuses behind the retina and the eye must accommodate even for distant objects.'),
('d17677a4-a9a9-5d60-a3e2-ee40df720c08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Hyperopia is corrected with:', '["Prisms","Convex lenses","Concave lenses","Bifocals","Cylindrical lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Convex lenses', 'Convex (plus, converging) lenses add refractive power so that parallel rays are focused on the retina of the short hyperopic eye.'),
('2eb28ab2-f817-5746-b22a-70a6ea6bfdbf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Astigmatism is caused by:', '["Unequal curvature of cornea or lens","Lens hardening","Elongated eyeball","Shortened eyeball","Ciliary muscle weakness"]'::jsonb, 'Unequal curvature of cornea or lens', 'Astigmatism results from unequal curvature of the cornea or lens, producing different focal powers in different meridians.'),
('f1c733c4-db0a-5344-a617-9a1c58cb66ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which type of lens is used to correct astigmatism?', '["Concave lenses","Spherical lenses","Bifocals","Cylindrical lenses","Convex lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Cylindrical lenses', 'Cylindrical lenses have different powers in different meridians, compensating for the asymmetric corneal curvature that causes astigmatism.'),
('e9cc80c1-f6f6-5d11-ab64-e9fd0be6aa14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Special Senses: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('ad86af26-fdbf-5b15-85e0-5844a78e6a12'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which type of lens is used to correct hyperopia (farsightedness)?', '["Convex (converging) lenses","Bifocal lenses","Prisms","Cylindrical lenses","Concave lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Convex (converging) lenses', 'Convex (converging) lenses add positive power so that parallel rays are focused on the retina of the hyperopic eye.'),
('dbc7d576-7b81-5921-bf9f-22511496a139'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Pupillary dilation (mydriasis) is produced by activation of:', '["Parasympathetic stimulation via CN III","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)","Sympathetic stimulation","Somatic motor fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic stimulation', 'Sympathetic fibers innervate the dilator pupillae, and their activation causes pupillary dilation. Parasympathetic stimulation via CN III constricts the pupil.'),
('d27250ee-2ebd-570c-9d22-43ca265fc575'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'During accommodation for near vision the lens becomes:', '["More convex","Less refractive","Flatter","Unchanged","Thinner"]'::jsonb, 'More convex', 'During accommodation the ciliary muscle contracts, relaxing the zonular fibers so the elastic lens bulges and becomes more convex, increasing its refractive power.'),
('8a193038-a2e6-5a09-a00b-ddd321ef7bcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The near point of vision for a young adult is approximately:', '["50 cm","10 cm","5 cm","100 cm","25 cm"]'::jsonb, '10 cm', 'At about age 20 the near point of vision is roughly 10 cm. It recedes with advancing age as lens elasticity declines.'),
('0413cd76-4263-5a8c-b5f3-21296ccd1c92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The far point of the emmetropic eye is:', '["1 meter","25 cm","50 cm","10 cm","Infinity"]'::jsonb, 'Infinity', 'Parallel rays from an infinitely distant object focus on the retina of the emmetropic eye without accommodation, so its far point is at infinity.'),
('2cc2ff58-c63b-521a-8042-e34ce8acf321'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The amplitude of accommodation decreases with age due to:', '["Lens sclerosis and loss of elasticity","Ciliary muscle weakness","Vitreous degeneration","Corneal flattening","Increased intraocular pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Lens sclerosis and loss of elasticity', 'Age-related lens sclerosis with loss of elasticity progressively reduces the lens''s ability to increase its curvature, decreasing accommodative amplitude.'),
('0520e5bd-99d9-59d9-9258-8342dcdbfc8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The ciliary muscle is innervated by:', '["Oculomotor nerve (CN III) parasympathetic","Sympathetic fibers","Trochlear nerve (CN IV)","Trigeminal nerve (CN V)","Abducens nerve (CN VI)"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III) parasympathetic', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus travel in CN III and synapse in the ciliary ganglion, which innervates the ciliary muscle.'),
('1ff6514a-9890-50f4-8dfb-3a3f97470d72'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The pupillary light reflex involves which cranial nerves?', '["CN II and CN VII","CN III and CN IV","CN II and CN III","CN III and CN V","CN II and CN VI"]'::jsonb, 'CN II and CN III', 'CN II (optic nerve) carries the afferent light signal, and CN III (oculomotor, parasympathetic) mediates the efferent constriction of the pupil.'),
('7b87de72-44fc-5796-9dc7-e894bf8061e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The direct pupillary light reflex refers to:', '["Constriction of the contralateral eye","Dilation of the illuminated eye","Constriction of the illuminated eye only","Constriction of both eyes","Dilation of both eyes"]'::jsonb, 'Constriction of the illuminated eye only', 'The direct pupillary light reflex is the constriction of the pupil of the eye that is illuminated. Constriction of the fellow eye is the consensual reflex.'),
('4956e290-a6ee-59d3-8888-4711c144daa4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The consensual pupillary light reflex refers to:', '["Dilation of both eyes","Constriction of both eyes","Constriction of the non-illuminated eye","Constriction of the illuminated eye","Dilation of the non-illuminated eye"]'::jsonb, 'Constriction of the non-illuminated eye', 'The consensual reflex is constriction of the pupil of the eye not directly illuminated, mediated by bilateral pathways from the pretectal nuclei.'),
('dedffb3a-07bd-5568-8be9-1a8eb4463034'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The afferent limb of the pupillary light reflex is mediated by:', '["Optic tract","Optic nerve (CN II)","Superior colliculus","Lateral geniculate body","Oculomotor nerve (CN III)"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (CN II)', 'Retinal light signals travel through the optic nerve (CN II) to the pretectal nuclei, forming the afferent limb; the efferent limb is carried by CN III.'),
('2a394c5b-97c8-5362-93e1-a86166ebeea7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The efferent limb of the pupillary light reflex is via:', '["Parasympathetic fibers in CN III","Sympathetic fibers","Trigeminal nerve","Trochlear nerve","Optic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic fibers in CN III', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers in CN III synapse in the ciliary ganglion, and postganglionic fibers then constrict the pupillary sphincter.'),
('cf22c191-a854-560f-859b-15ed69d3574f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What is the pathway of visual information from the retina to the lateral geniculate nucleus?', '["Lateral geniculate","Superior colliculus","Retinal ganglion cells via optic tract","Visual cortex","Primary visual cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Retinal ganglion cells via optic tract', 'Axons of retinal ganglion cells form the optic nerve and optic tract and synapse in the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus.'),
('afb32af5-ad1e-5695-8cac-0c12f513f1d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The preganglionic parasympathetic neurons that mediate pupillary constriction are located in which nucleus?', '["Edinger-Westphal nucleus","Occipital cortex","Frontal eye field","Superior colliculus","Pretectal nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Edinger-Westphal nucleus', 'Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers for pupillary constriction arise from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus and travel in CN III to the ciliary ganglion.'),
('5cdc5538-aef6-5856-b417-4cd6a0b679d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The retina is derived from:', '["Mesoderm","Neural ectoderm","Endoderm","Surface ectoderm","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Neural ectoderm', 'The retina develops as an outgrowth of the forebrain vesicle and is therefore derived from neural ectoderm.'),
('509b3473-e525-5447-953b-687f9684667a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The fovea centralis contains predominantly:', '["Amacrine cells","Rods","Ganglion cells","Cones","Bipolar cells"]'::jsonb, 'Cones', 'The fovea consists almost entirely of cones, giving it the highest visual acuity of any retinal region.'),
('4f06c865-88f3-5572-a990-282b6fd334b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Special Senses: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('c06d2dfd-2165-556f-86b7-8bfba202895e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What is the macular pigment composed of and what is its function?', '["High rod density","High cone density","Central depression","Lack of blood vessels","Yellow pigment (xanthophyll)"]'::jsonb, 'Yellow pigment (xanthophyll)', 'The macula lutea contains yellow xanthophyll pigments, mainly lutein and zeaxanthin, which filter blue light and protect the foveal photoreceptors.'),
('7d4bcff1-4635-5da1-8550-541f3ffcc8ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What happens to photoreceptor membrane potential when light strikes the retina?', '["Light opens Na+ channels","Light increases transmitter release","Light depolarizes them","Light increases cGMP levels","Light hyperpolarizes them"]'::jsonb, 'Light hyperpolarizes them', 'Light closes cGMP-gated Na+ channels, hyperpolarizing the photoreceptor and decreasing glutamate release.'),
('f814c0fc-5353-537c-8214-bc02a6798ad2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Absorption of light by rhodopsin in rod photoreceptors leads to:', '["Decrease in cGMP","Decrease in cAMP","Increase in ATP","Increase in cGMP","Increase in cAMP"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease in cGMP', 'Light activates transducin, which stimulates phosphodiesterase to hydrolyze cGMP; falling cGMP closes Na+ channels and hyperpolarizes the cell.'),
('13855218-305f-5949-8670-a49e02b2bda8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', '11-cis retinal is isomerized to all-trans retinal by:', '["cGMP","Enzymatic reaction","Heat","Light energy","Transducin"]'::jsonb, 'Light energy', 'Light absorption isomerizes the 11-cis retinal chromophore of rhodopsin to all-trans retinal, initiating the phototransduction cascade.'),
('1d094f8c-0182-569e-8d30-14eaa18d0a47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Special Senses: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('6b8ed412-bb2c-5473-82a4-18178b668fe4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In the study of Special Senses, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('b2b63f2b-6d1c-524a-b4ca-9f4e23343396'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Approximately how many photoreceptor cells are in the human retina?', '["6 million","1 million","20 million","500 million","120 million"]'::jsonb, '120 million', 'The human retina contains roughly 120 million rods and about 6 million cones, totaling well over 100 million photoreceptors.'),
('0208178e-65d3-591a-8e5c-c60c71c52b7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Rod density is highest at:', '["Ora serrata resulting in decreased functional activity","Macula accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Peripheral retina 20 degrees from fovea","Fovea without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Optic disc leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral retina 20 degrees from fovea', 'Rod density peaks in the peripheral retina about 20 degrees from the fovea and falls to zero in the foveola.'),
('33e1aee9-d80b-5e80-bf37-36b8220e5c67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The pigment in rods is:', '["Iodopsin","Photopsin","Rhodopsin","Melanopsin","Porphyropsin"]'::jsonb, 'Rhodopsin', 'Rods contain rhodopsin, composed of the protein opsin bound to the chromophore 11-cis retinal.'),
('3c0a5899-6e91-5a98-99ad-bcf1db0ce8d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'How many types of cone photoreceptors are present in the human retina?', '["5","4","3","2","1"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Three cone types exist, S (blue), M (green), and L (red), providing trichromatic color vision.'),
('ab8b1465-d0d4-54b0-9618-9360db9c82b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'S cones are most sensitive to which wavelength?', '["500 nm","480 nm","420 nm (blue)","530 nm (green)","560 nm (red)"]'::jsonb, '420 nm (blue)', 'S (short-wavelength) cones peak in sensitivity at about 420 nm in the blue range.'),
('e5fc87fe-5456-5ac1-9444-c8eae45c5f2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'M cones are most sensitive to which wavelength?', '["500 nm","600 nm","560 nm","530 nm (green)","420 nm"]'::jsonb, '530 nm (green)', 'M (medium-wavelength) cones peak in sensitivity at about 530 nm in the green range.'),
('f1fd87ae-ef8b-53cf-8a66-b6a15e4e46ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'L cones are most sensitive to which wavelength?', '["500 nm","620 nm","560 nm (red)","530 nm","420 nm"]'::jsonb, '560 nm (red)', 'L (long-wavelength) cones peak in sensitivity at about 560 nm in the red range.'),
('312428ba-b86f-50f3-9db2-12d93e1d87c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The phototransduction cascade in cones involves:', '["Go protein","Gs protein","Gi protein","Transducin","Gustducin"]'::jsonb, 'Transducin', 'Cone phototransduction uses the G protein transducin, which activates phosphodiesterase to reduce cGMP and close cation channels.'),
('af0b5466-1823-5a5b-b95d-20bf3004688f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In rod photoreceptors, cGMP keeps sodium channels open in the dark. When light activates the phototransduction cascade, an enzyme that is stimulated by activated transducin:', '["Breaks down cGMP to GMP","Synthesizes cGMP","Phosphorylates opsin","Converts ATP to cAMP","Hydrolyzes GTP"]'::jsonb, 'Breaks down cGMP to GMP', 'Light-activated transducin stimulates cGMP phosphodiesterase, which hydrolyzes cGMP to GMP. This closes cGMP-gated sodium channels and hyperpolarizes the photoreceptor.'),
('9ddb58b1-b218-5382-9819-99b197e52a1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What happens to retinal sensitivity as ambient light intensity increases (photopic conditions)?', '["Increased retinal sensitivity","Increased rhodopsin","Rod dominance","Decreased cone activity","Decreased retinal sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased retinal sensitivity', 'In photopic (bright-light) conditions the less-sensitive cone system dominates and rods are saturated. Retinal sensitivity therefore decreases compared with scotopic conditions.'),
('5e5ba526-62e9-578b-98f3-069ac24d928e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Dark adaptation takes approximately how long for full adaptation?', '["5 minutes","2 minutes","60 minutes","10 minutes","20-30 minutes"]'::jsonb, '20-30 minutes', 'Full dark adaptation of the rod system requires about 20-30 minutes, the time needed for rhodopsin regeneration and maximal rod sensitivity.'),
('c3844b16-0826-5311-99b1-52bec2969e85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Vitamin A deficiency causes:', '["Color blindness","Tunnel vision","Blindness","Photophobia","Night blindness (nyctalopia)"]'::jsonb, 'Night blindness (nyctalopia)', 'Retinal, a vitamin A derivative, is the chromophore of rhodopsin. Deficiency impairs rhodopsin regeneration and dark adaptation, producing night blindness.'),
('7981c6f4-1db1-59f3-affa-bf5dbbca5f2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'During therapeutic management of Special Senses, Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('b34a0393-5447-5466-b5f2-a597f0caa0e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which type of cone photoreceptor has peak sensitivity at medium wavelengths (530 nm)?', '["S cones","L cones","M cones (green)","All cones","Rods"]'::jsonb, 'M cones (green)', 'M (medium-wavelength) cones peak near 530 nm, S cones near 420-440 nm, and L cones near 560 nm.'),
('db28a911-88f7-5d68-a476-8bb037ca2c8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Tritanopia refers to deficiency of:', '["S cones (blue)","L cones","M cones","Rods","All cones"]'::jsonb, 'S cones (blue)', 'Tritanopia is loss of S (blue) cone function, a rare autosomal dominant condition causing blue-yellow color discrimination defects.'),
('2b429740-71e2-500f-8331-522916cbc0ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The visual pathway from retina to visual cortex involves:', '["Optic nerve > optic chiasm > optic tract > LGN > optic radiations > V1","Optic nerve > LGN > optic chiasm > V1 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Optic nerve > optic tract > V1 mediated by specific receptor activation","Retina > pretectum > V1 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Retina > superior colliculus > V1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve > optic chiasm > optic tract > LGN > optic radiations > V1', 'Retinal ganglion cell axons travel through the optic nerve, partially cross at the chiasm, continue in the optic tract, synapse at the LGN, and reach V1 via the optic radiations.'),
('907df929-ce4d-59e9-9bd1-a4b9358e9e8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'At which anatomical structure do the nasal fibers of each optic nerve cross?', '["Optic chiasm","Lateral geniculate","Optic nerve","Optic radiations","Optic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Optic chiasm', 'Nasal retinal fibers decussate at the optic chiasm, while temporal fibers remain ipsilateral.'),
('2621beb7-df52-54a3-b9fe-fb0f8ac5d6f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which fibers of the visual pathway remain ipsilateral and do not cross at the optic chiasm?', '["Temporal fibers","Nasal fibers","Macular fibers","All fibers cross","None remain ipsilateral"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal fibers', 'Temporal (lateral) retinal fibers pass through the chiasm without crossing. Nasal fibers, by contrast, decussate to the contralateral side.'),
('ba4f2449-0ff0-58a9-9765-7d504294760a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Regarding clinical cases: Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('7fe79b75-3bbe-5615-a475-2b5ac64c2ca7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Magnocellular LGN layers receive input from:', '["Rods","K-type cells","M-type retinal ganglion cells","P-type retinal ganglion cells","Bipolar cells"]'::jsonb, 'M-type retinal ganglion cells', 'Magnocellular LGN layers (1-2) receive input from M-type retinal ganglion cells; parvocellular layers receive input from P-type cells.'),
('3724b10c-2139-5e87-957d-11f093b46d84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A complete lesion of the right optic nerve most likely produces which visual field defect?', '["Right homonymous hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Left eye blindness","Right eye blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Right eye blindness', 'A complete optic nerve lesion causes monocular blindness in the ipsilateral eye because fibers from that eye never reach the chiasm.'),
('8959be89-1d54-55cc-ae1e-8f45939bd4cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Lesion at the optic chiasm causes:', '["Right homonymous hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Complete blindness","Left homonymous hemianopia","Binasal hemianopia"]'::jsonb, 'Bitemporal hemianopia', 'Chiasmal lesions interrupt the crossing nasal fibers from both eyes, producing bitemporal hemianopia or bitemporal superior quadrantanopia.'),
('2fa7bc7f-8f75-59ec-9eca-1248cc1a6e69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A lesion of the left optic tract most likely produces which visual field defect?', '["Bitemporal hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia","Binasal hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Left eye blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Right homonymous hemianopia', 'The left optic tract carries fibers representing the right visual field from both eyes, so its injury causes a contralateral right homonymous hemianopia.'),
('5523a2ed-884a-5ca4-96ad-3b45c1f16ab9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Lesion of right optic radiation causes:', '["Superior quadrantanopia","Left eye blindness","Bitemporal hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia"]'::jsonb, 'Left homonymous hemianopia', 'The right optic radiation carries fibers subserving the left visual field, so a complete right-sided lesion causes left homonymous hemianopia.'),
('d4bc027b-5dd4-5025-83ef-3394932147e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Meyer loop carries fibers from:', '["Temporal retina","Macula","Nasal retina","Upper retinal quadrant","Lower retinal quadrant"]'::jsonb, 'Lower retinal quadrant', 'Meyer loop carries fibers from the inferior (lower) retinal quadrant, which subserve the superior visual field.'),
('d0ab68e1-8da6-561a-9d97-125ae5dd1051'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Lesion of right Meyer loop causes:', '["Left hemianopia","Left superior quadrantanopia","Right superior quadrantanopia","Left inferior quadrantanopia","Right inferior quadrantanopia"]'::jsonb, 'Left superior quadrantanopia', 'A right Meyer loop lesion interrupts inferior retinal fibers encoding the left superior visual field, producing left superior quadrantanopia.'),
('ada581a1-e773-5273-a702-c4a2d2e7d50a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The retinotopic map in V1 is:', '["Distorted with foveal magnification","Exact copy accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Linear mediated by specific receptor activation","Inverted only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Uniform leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Distorted with foveal magnification', 'Cortical magnification assigns disproportionately large V1 area to the fovea, so the retinotopic map is highly distorted, not linear or uniform.'),
('56f376d5-8699-5f1f-bf4e-5040470f4d96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Simple cells in V1 respond to:', '["Complex patterns resulting in decreased functional activity","Oriented bars in specific position","Motion direction mediated by specific receptor activation","Faces without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Color only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Oriented bars in specific position', 'Simple cells have distinct ON/OFF subregions and respond best to a bar or edge of a specific orientation at a specific retinal position.'),
('2d6e9270-7e79-586a-809e-3cb8457fbd5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Complex cells in V1 respond to:', '["Oriented bars anywhere in receptive field","Color","Specific objects","Both orientation and direction","Direction of motion"]'::jsonb, 'Both orientation and direction', 'Complex cells respond to oriented bars across a larger receptive field and are often direction-selective, encoding both stimulus orientation and direction of motion.'),
('97102291-6a19-5925-ad52-231728c195bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The dorsal stream (where pathway) projects to:', '["Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe","Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe","Limbic system"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal lobe', 'The dorsal stream projects to the parietal lobe and processes object location and motion, whereas the ventral stream projects to the temporal lobe for object identity.'),
('1e7527e9-ed2a-5632-bce8-2faff2d95973'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Bilateral damage to the primary visual cortex (V1) causes complete loss of vision while pupillary light reflexes remain intact. This condition is known as:', '["Prosopagnosia","Agnosia","Cortical blindness","Akinetopsia","Blindsight"]'::jsonb, 'Cortical blindness', 'Cortical blindness results from bilateral occipital (V1) damage; the retinas and pupillary reflexes remain intact, distinguishing it from prechiasmal blindness.'),
('f8f9dd9c-9da2-5f95-878a-1be5e703ef73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Damage to which cortical region characteristically produces prosopagnosia (inability to recognize familiar faces)?', '["LGN resulting in decreased functional activity","Frontal lobe accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fusiform face area (temporal lobe)","Parietal lobe without affecting the primary tissue pathway","V1 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fusiform face area (temporal lobe)', 'The fusiform face area in the inferior temporal lobe is specialized for facial recognition; its damage produces prosopagnosia.'),
('a96228a9-0903-5ceb-a720-9c51290956f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which cortical visual area is primarily specialized for color perception?', '["V1 resulting in decreased functional activity","LGN accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","V2 mediated by specific receptor activation","V4 (color area)","V5 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'V4 (color area)', 'Area V4 processes color information; V5/MT encodes motion, and V1 is the primary visual cortex that performs initial orientation analysis.'),
('060fd025-eefe-57ca-8cea-47f865467562'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The blind spot corresponds to:', '["Fovea","Optic disc","Pars plana","Macula","Ora serrata"]'::jsonb, 'Optic disc', 'The optic disc contains the optic nerve head but no photoreceptors, creating the physiologic blind spot.'),
('afce9079-803c-5b59-ab5b-1b3f323c7d74'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The physiological blind spot is located:', '["15 degrees nasal to fixation","15 degrees temporal to fixation","30 degrees temporal","At fixation point","At fovea"]'::jsonb, '15 degrees temporal to fixation', 'The optic disc lies in the nasal retina, projecting its blind spot about 15 degrees temporal to the point of fixation.'),
('7b6d6243-d837-5473-8d1a-52a1569101ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which visual acuity test uses a standardized chart with decreasing letter sizes?', '["Perimetry","Snellen chart","Tonometry","Retinoscopy","Ishihara plates"]'::jsonb, 'Snellen chart', 'The Snellen chart measures distance visual acuity using rows of letters that progressively decrease in size.'),
('4b729b5c-8b21-5786-b7e8-d3456426f8e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', '20/20 vision means:', '["Sees at 20 ft what normal sees at 40 ft","Sees at 20 ft what normal sees at 20 ft","Better than normal","Perfect vision at all distances","Sees at 40 ft what normal sees at 20 ft"]'::jsonb, 'Sees at 20 ft what normal sees at 20 ft', '20/20 vision means the patient sees at 20 feet what a person with normal acuity sees at 20 feet.'),
('74ce889d-e2a4-5516-a0a8-83b49e126d5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'During therapeutic management of Special Senses, EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('a275ddd1-7054-503e-b095-2808cdd72a62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Regarding clinical cases in Special Senses: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('22dffbf6-e877-5365-bc5e-41dbcbf12506'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Cataract is:', '["Retinal degeneration","Corneal opacity","Lens opacity","Optic atrophy","Vitreous opacity"]'::jsonb, 'Lens opacity', 'A cataract is an opacity of the lens that scatters light and reduces visual acuity; it is most commonly age-related.'),
('85326f44-d793-51bb-bde2-f44491129c36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Pupillary dilation (mydriasis) is mediated by activation of which of the following?', '["Trigeminal","Parasympathetic (CN III)","CN IV","Sympathetic fibers","CN VI"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic fibers', 'Sympathetic fibers innervate the pupillary dilator muscle, causing mydriasis; parasympathetic fibers traveling with CN III mediate pupillary constriction.'),
('642f9bd1-56f2-560d-87b6-d0f6ee0e8884'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which cranial nerve provides parasympathetic innervation to the pupil for constriction?', '["Somatic motor","CN V","Parasympathetic (CN III)","Sympathetic","CN IV"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (CN III)', 'Parasympathetic fibers traveling with CN III innervate the pupillary sphincter via the ciliary ganglion, mediating pupillary constriction and accommodation.'),
('2565cbba-af85-54d3-8526-989d0dcc7a8c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The ciliary muscle contraction during accommodation causes:', '["Lens becoming more convex","Pupil dilation","Decreased refractive power","Corneal flattening","Lens flattening"]'::jsonb, 'Lens becoming more convex', 'Ciliary muscle contraction relaxes zonular tension, allowing the elastic lens to become more convex, which increases refractive power for near vision.'),
('aabe4464-7673-5764-9a7d-f1b22adf6a08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Presbyopia, the age-related loss of accommodative ability, characteristically becomes symptomatic in which age group?', '["Young adults (20s)","Elderly","Infants","Neonates","Middle age"]'::jsonb, 'Middle age', 'Lens elasticity declines progressively with age; presbyopia typically becomes symptomatic around age 40-50, i.e., in middle age.'),
('c933f706-4e33-5c4b-8e33-74fddccda6ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In an uncorrected myopic (nearsighted) eye, parallel rays from a distant object are focused:', '["At the retina resulting in decreased functional activity","At a finite distance in front of eye","Beyond infinity mediated by specific receptor activation","At infinity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Beyond retina leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'At a finite distance in front of eye', 'In myopia the eyeball is too long or the refractive power too strong, so parallel rays converge at a finite point anterior to the retina.'),
('96703440-e941-50a9-ac62-d919e316e91a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Myopia is corrected with which type of lens?', '["Concave (minus) lens","Convex (plus) lens","Cylindrical lens","Prism lens","Bifocal lens"]'::jsonb, 'Concave (minus) lens', 'Myopia is corrected with concave (minus, diverging) lenses, which shift the image focus posteriorly onto the retina.'),
('a07f94b6-a5ee-582a-86c2-087a6fbc8f0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The optic disc diameter is approximately:', '["1 mm","1.5 mm","0.5 mm","5 mm","3 mm"]'::jsonb, '1.5 mm', 'The optic disc (optic nerve head) measures approximately 1.5 mm in diameter.'),
('cd33bb36-615e-5411-a714-03d52e006cfb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which photoreceptor types are present in the human retina?', '["Rods only","Both rods and cones","Cones only","Neither","Only S cones"]'::jsonb, 'Both rods and cones', 'The human retina contains both rod and cone photoreceptors: rods mediate dim-light (scotopic) vision and cones mediate bright-light and color vision.'),
('efe17990-0de3-58be-ac71-c295a3593b09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Vision in dim (scotopic) light depends primarily on which photoreceptor system?', '["Neither","Both rods and cones","Cones only","Rods only","Only M cones"]'::jsonb, 'Rods only', 'Rods contain rhodopsin and provide highly sensitive, achromatic vision in dim light, so night vision is mediated predominantly by the rod system. Cones require bright light and mediate color vision.'),
('19487395-b884-530d-81f8-726d9aa570b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Amacrine cells modulate which of the following synaptic transmissions?', '["Pupillary reflex","Rod-cone coupling","Photoreceptor to bipolar transmission","Horizontal cell function","Bipolar to ganglion cell transmission"]'::jsonb, 'Bipolar to ganglion cell transmission', 'Amacrine cells synapse on bipolar cell terminals and ganglion cell dendrites in the inner plexiform layer, modulating bipolar-to-ganglion cell transmission. They contribute to motion detection and surround responses.'),
('b8dbf483-2abf-5e3c-ad8b-19319c0f27c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The outer plexiform layer of the retina contains synapses between:', '["Rods and cones","Ganglion cells and LGN","Photoreceptors and bipolar cells","Bipolar cells and ganglion cells","Horizontal and amacrine cells"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptors and bipolar cells', 'In the outer plexiform layer, photoreceptor terminals synapse with bipolar cell dendrites and with horizontal cells. This is the first synaptic relay of the visual pathway.'),
('908d061b-bbc7-59a2-99a5-c12c144a765e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The inner plexiform layer of the retina contains synapses between:', '["Ganglion cells and LGN","Bipolar cells and ganglion cells","Rods and cones","Horizontal and amacrine cells","Photoreceptors and bipolar cells"]'::jsonb, 'Bipolar cells and ganglion cells', 'Bipolar cell axons synapse with ganglion cell dendrites within the inner plexiform layer, where amacrine cells also modulate transmission. This is the second synaptic relay of the visual pathway.'),
('f7ee4d0b-343a-5865-b08f-9005da1bce47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'P-type retinal ganglion cells project to:', '["Pretectum","V5/MT","Superior colliculus","Parvocellular LGN layers","Magnocellular LGN layers"]'::jsonb, 'Parvocellular LGN layers', 'P-type (midget) retinal ganglion cells terminate in the parvocellular layers (3-6) of the lateral geniculate nucleus, conveying color and fine spatial detail.'),
('c6f2428f-4780-5845-b422-9d9e0a2976d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'M-type retinal ganglion cells project to:', '["Superior colliculus","Koniocellular layers","Parvocellular layers","V4","Magnocellular LGN layers (1-2)"]'::jsonb, 'Magnocellular LGN layers (1-2)', 'M-type (parasol) retinal ganglion cells project to the magnocellular layers (1-2) of the LGN, transmitting motion and low-spatial-frequency information.'),
('0aefb874-e1cb-5fb6-990e-b177b2820889'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'At what wavelength is photopic (cone-mediated) vision most sensitive?', '["507 nm (blue-green)","580 nm","555 nm (green-yellow)","650 nm","450 nm"]'::jsonb, '555 nm (green-yellow)', 'The photopic luminosity function peaks at about 555 nm in bright-light conditions. Under scotopic conditions the peak shifts to about 507 nm.'),
('c13e1352-4bca-56ae-b0bd-52865b44efdf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The scotopic spectral sensitivity curve peaks at:', '["450 nm","507 nm (blue-green)","580 nm","555 nm","650 nm"]'::jsonb, '507 nm (blue-green)', 'Rod vision is most sensitive near 507 nm, in the blue-green range. This scotopic peak underlies the Purkinje shift seen in dim light.'),
('1b54b15a-41c2-54fe-aa38-6a503b268b2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The Purkinje shift describes the:', '["Light adaptation resulting in decreased functional activity","Color vision loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dark adaptation mediated by specific receptor activation","Day to night vision shift without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Shift from cone to rod spectral sensitivity in dim light"]'::jsonb, 'Shift from cone to rod spectral sensitivity in dim light', 'In dim light, rod-dominated vision shifts maximal spectral sensitivity from the photopic peak (about 555 nm) toward the scotopic peak (about 507 nm, blue-green). Blue objects therefore appear relatively brighter at night.'),
('74d45f8b-802d-518e-ae81-2d061e655955'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The a-wave of the electroretinogram (ERG) reflects:', '["Amacrine cells","Ganglion cell activity","Bipolar cell activity","Inner nuclear layer","Photoreceptor hyperpolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptor hyperpolarization', 'Light hyperpolarizes photoreceptor outer segments, generating the ERG a-wave. The subsequent b-wave reflects bipolar cell depolarization.'),
('345e9696-84a0-5a31-9115-52f87561dc3e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The minimum angle of resolution at the fovea is approximately:', '["5 minutes of arc","1 minute of arc","2 minutes of arc","10 minutes of arc","0.5 minute of arc"]'::jsonb, '1 minute of arc', 'The fovea resolves two points separated by about one minute of arc, matching normal 20/20 Snellen acuity. This property underlies clinical measures of visual acuity.'),
('d9e6adad-5f66-5833-b35b-a73c2b0f4df7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Night blindness due to vitamin A deficiency most directly impairs function of which system?', '["Rod system","Ganglion cells","Both","Horizontal cells","Cone system"]'::jsonb, 'Rod system', 'Rods use rhodopsin, and vitamin A (retinal) deficiency impairs rhodopsin regeneration, selectively compromising rod-mediated scotopic vision. Night blindness is therefore a rod-system disorder.'),
('4b3eaaf6-d6cb-5513-8947-7ed409f720d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Lateral inhibition within the retina is mediated primarily by:', '["ON bipolar cells","Ganglion cells","Horizontal cells","Amacrine cells","OFF bipolar cells"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal cells', 'Horizontal cells provide lateral inhibitory signals in the outer plexiform layer, generating the surround responses that create center-surround receptive fields.'),
('30d37cc4-382b-594f-8fa9-0a647d0ffdc5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'ON-center bipolar cells are depolarized by:', '["Light in surround","Light in center of receptive field","Dark in center of receptive field","Both light and dark","Dark in surround"]'::jsonb, 'Light in center of receptive field', 'ON-center bipolar cells depolarize when light falls on the center of their receptive field and hyperpolarize when light falls on the surround. This antagonistic arrangement enhances contrast detection.'),
('f8c9418a-62db-53db-aac6-8e39c1e6a938'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'OFF-center bipolar cells are depolarized by:', '["Dark in center of receptive field","Light in surround","Dark in surround","Light in center of receptive field","Flickering light"]'::jsonb, 'Dark in center of receptive field', 'OFF-center bipolar cells depolarize when the center of the field is darkened and hyperpolarize when the center is illuminated. They respond in the opposite direction to ON-center cells.'),
('e4c45250-ca23-5879-89b5-acbc5bd1613c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The receptive field of a retinal ganglion cell is characterized by:', '["Center-surround antagonism","Center-only response with no surround modulation","Uniform sensitivity","Linear summation","Independent cone signals"]'::jsonb, 'Center-surround antagonism', 'Retinal ganglion cell receptive fields have an antagonistic center-surround architecture that enhances edge contrast. Surround inhibition arises from lateral interactions mediated by horizontal and amacrine cells.'),
('c4410e80-4867-5654-a114-fd81e73aeb20'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The optic radiation (geniculocalcarine tract) chiefly terminates in the:', '["Pretectum resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior colliculus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","V4 mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary visual cortex (V1)","V2 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Primary visual cortex (V1)', 'Geniculocortical fibers from the LGN end mainly in the primary visual cortex (striate cortex, V1) around the calcarine sulcus. Damage to this projection produces homonymous field defects.'),
('f731f3dc-d85c-592d-a9bd-063dc5b1b1bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Bitemporal hemianopia is most commonly caused by:', '["Occipital stroke","Pituitary tumor compressing chiasm","Retro-orbital tumor","Glaucoma","Optic nerve lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary tumor compressing chiasm', 'Bitemporal hemianopia results from compression of the crossing nasal retinal fibers at the optic chiasm, typically by a pituitary macroadenoma.'),
('30b12043-ab50-512e-92c8-9f0d035640bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing suggests:', '["Optic tract lesion","Chiasmal lesion","Optic nerve lesion","LGN lesion","Occipital pole lesion (PCA stroke)"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital pole lesion (PCA stroke)', 'Macular sparing in a homonymous hemianopia points to an occipital pole lesion, usually a posterior cerebral artery stroke, because the macular cortex may receive collateral supply from the middle cerebral artery. Optic tract and chiasmal lesions do not spare the macula centrally.'),
('d5d7e550-bbe0-5455-81a4-84b4283d1544'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Anisocoria refers to:', '["Reactive pupils","Constricted pupils","Unequal pupil size","Equal pupils","Fixed pupils"]'::jsonb, 'Unequal pupil size', 'Anisocoria is inequality of pupil diameter. Determining whether the anisocoria is greatest in bright or dim light helps distinguish sympathetic (Horner) from parasympathetic (CN III) causes.'),
('bb91a73d-1be5-5702-bc23-7530b16ccfab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Marcus Gunn pupil (relative afferent pupillary defect) indicates:', '["Optic nerve damage (afferent defect)","Horner syndrome","CN III palsy","Argyll Robertson pupil","Sympathetic damage"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve damage (afferent defect)', 'In a relative afferent pupillary defect, light directed at the affected eye produces less pupillary constriction than light on the normal eye, indicating an afferent defect, most commonly optic nerve damage. Sympathetic (Horner) damage does not produce this pattern.'),
('7e604d5b-1fb1-5f76-bf9f-6669fe726f3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The accommodative response requires which of the following?', '["CN II only","CN III only","Both CN II and CN III","Sympathetic fibers","CN VI"]'::jsonb, 'Both CN II and CN III', 'Accommodation needs an afferent limb (CN II) and a parasympathetic efferent limb (CN III) that contracts the ciliary muscle. Neither nerve alone is sufficient.'),
('293559d3-94f9-5e39-b399-5e3f8d8c9f03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The tympanic membrane separates the:', '["Cochlea and vestibule","External ear and middle ear","None of the above","Outer and inner ear","Middle ear and inner ear"]'::jsonb, 'External ear and middle ear', 'The tympanic membrane forms the boundary between the external acoustic meatus and the middle ear cavity. The malleus attaches to its medial surface.'),
('f8d4e59a-2535-5bd8-835d-13077a072956'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The middle ear contains how many ossicles?', '["5","2","6","4","3"]'::jsonb, '3', 'The three auditory ossicles are the malleus, incus, and stapes, which transmit vibration from the tympanic membrane to the oval window.'),
('81099426-4407-5491-8d92-c6793bb52f1f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which ossicle is attached to the tympanic membrane?', '["Stapes","All three","Incus","Malleus","None of these"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus', 'The handle of the malleus is embedded in the tympanic membrane, articulating with the incus at its other end. The stapes footplate sits in the oval window.'),
('af656d2a-0806-59c4-9b6e-0349f97eb9f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The primary function of the middle ear apparatus (tympanic membrane and ossicular chain) is:', '["Impedance matching (gain 22x)","Attenuate sound","Convert sound to neural signal","Produce the endocochlear potential","Filter high frequencies"]'::jsonb, 'Impedance matching (gain 22x)', 'The tympanic membrane and ossicles overcome the air-fluid impedance mismatch at the oval window, providing a mechanical gain of about 22-fold. This efficiently transfers sound energy into the cochlea.'),
('1e15c8a7-a47d-515e-9608-c564e4c5a9c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In the study of Special Senses, Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('4e346c48-9495-53f7-bea8-5270c3ee164a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Special Senses: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('49852d54-f081-5914-8357-5be8f5f44ecf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The acoustic reflex (stapedius contraction) protects the inner ear against:', '["High frequency sounds","Low frequency sounds","All sounds","Loud sounds (attenuation 20 dB)","Silence"]'::jsonb, 'Loud sounds (attenuation 20 dB)', 'Contraction of the stapedius, together with the tensor tympani, increases middle ear impedance and attenuates loud sounds by roughly 20 dB, protecting the cochlea.'),
('083860c2-db70-54a2-9c1f-8924b79a940c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The Eustachian (auditory) tube connects the:', '["Outer ear and middle ear","Middle ear and nasopharynx","Middle ear and inner ear","Cochlea and semicircular canals","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear and nasopharynx', 'The Eustachian tube links the middle ear cavity with the nasopharynx, equalizing middle ear pressure with ambient atmospheric pressure.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('ba4b9f2b-2338-5984-bb6a-f4c06b8785a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The cochlea is part of the:', '["Middle ear","Inner ear (bony labyrinth)","External ear","Auditory nerve","Brainstem"]'::jsonb, 'Inner ear (bony labyrinth)', 'The cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals constitute the inner ear within the bony labyrinth. The cochlea houses the organ of Corti for hearing.'),
('de6d8e43-2ff5-5de3-a811-c67a0841c19e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In the study of Special Senses, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('e295f088-54bc-5e61-8966-a1aeb500d735'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The stereocilia of the hair cells of the organ of Corti are embedded in the:', '["Reissner membrane","Endolymph","Basilar membrane","Perilymph","Tectorial membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Tectorial membrane', 'The tips of the outer hair cell stereocilia are embedded in the gelatinous tectorial membrane. Basilar membrane motion shears the stereocilia against the tectorial membrane, opening transduction channels.'),
('844ee8d8-e352-51f8-9853-1bc9452c4686'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The number of inner hair cells in one cochlea is approximately:', '["3500","12000","500","None of the above","20000"]'::jsonb, '3500', 'About 3,500 inner hair cells are arranged in a single row along the cochlea and provide the bulk of the afferent auditory input. Nearly all auditory nerve fibers synapse on them.'),
('7f02912a-ec6c-541a-825e-af82ec4aa141'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The number of outer hair cells in one cochlea is approximately:', '["12000","3500","500","None of the above","20000"]'::jsonb, '12000', 'About 12,000 outer hair cells are arranged in three to four rows. They amplify and sharpen basilar membrane motion rather than serving as the primary transducers.'),
('6260adba-4647-5b95-8378-0a8b069d9657'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Outer hair cells function primarily as:', '["Cochlear amplification (electromotility)","Protection accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Sensory transduction mediated by specific receptor activation","Support without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural transmission leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear amplification (electromotility)', 'Outer hair cells change length with membrane voltage (electromotility by the motor protein prestin), amplifying basilar membrane vibration and sharpening frequency tuning. Inner hair cells carry out the actual sensory transduction.'),
('f4566077-dfa3-576d-bc85-8778acfe2b35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The stereocilia of hair cells are interconnected by:', '["Desmosomes","Gap junctions","Adherens junctions","Tight junctions","Tip links"]'::jsonb, 'Tip links', 'Tip links mechanically connect stereocilia of different heights and gate the mechanosensitive transduction channels. Deflection toward the tallest stereocilium increases channel open probability.'),
('66476162-bb5c-54cd-9de3-a1fc94bc8a30'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The frequency of a sound is encoded by the:', '["Size of stapes","Number of hair cells","Shape of cochlea","Rate of hair cell firing","Place of maximal basilar membrane vibration"]'::jsonb, 'Place of maximal basilar membrane vibration', 'The basilar membrane is tonotopic: high frequencies produce maximal vibration near the base and low frequencies near the apex. The place of maximal displacement therefore encodes pitch.'),
('2e637f69-e490-5c49-9157-c8e086605d6f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The loudness of a sound is encoded by the:', '["Type of hair cell resulting in decreased functional activity","Rate of firing of auditory nerve fibers","Place of vibration mediated by specific receptor activation","Which ear without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cochlear nucleus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Rate of firing of auditory nerve fibers', 'Intensity is signaled by the firing rate of auditory nerve fibers and by recruitment of additional fibers. Place of vibration encodes frequency rather than loudness.'),
('61434924-4d52-56cf-8b10-fdc002674ee3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'High-frequency sounds produce maximal basilar membrane displacement at the:', '["Semicircular canals","Base (near oval window)","Middle turn","All regions equally","Apex (helicotrema)"]'::jsonb, 'Base (near oval window)', 'The basilar membrane is narrow and stiff at the base near the oval window, so high frequencies peak there. Low frequencies peak at the wider, more compliant apex near the helicotrema.'),
('58764877-ed0e-55b0-997b-42f784cd3541'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The traveling wave theory of hearing was proposed by:', '["Rinne","Helmholtz","Sperry","Weber","von Bekesy"]'::jsonb, 'von Bekesy', 'Georg von Bekesy demonstrated that sound waves travel along the basilar membrane, producing maximal displacement at a location determined by frequency. This traveling wave theory explains how pitch is coded spatially in the cochlea.'),
('46dbab7b-64d8-54dc-9e74-ae779d71a116'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The place theory of hearing was proposed by:', '["Weber","Fourier","Galileo","Helmholtz","von Bekesy"]'::jsonb, 'Helmholtz', 'Helmholtz proposed that each frequency stimulates a specific region (place) of the basilar membrane, forming the basis of pitch coding. This place (resonance) theory underlies understanding of frequency discrimination.'),
('cb176e29-b1ac-5d11-bf10-2d4610e02669'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The lateral lemniscus carries auditory information to:', '["Thalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Midbrain (inferior colliculus)","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Cortex without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medulla leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (inferior colliculus)', 'The lateral lemniscus ascends from the cochlear nuclei and superior olivary complex to the inferior colliculus in the midbrain, the major relay of the ascending auditory pathway before the medial geniculate body.'),
('f970f06a-60bb-5bbc-9e52-8fe4a6a9eff2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The inferior colliculus is part of the:', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Midbrain (auditory midbrain)","Cerebellum without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain (auditory midbrain)', 'The inferior colliculus is the principal auditory nucleus of the midbrain, receiving input from the lateral lemniscus and projecting to the medial geniculate body in the thalamus.'),
('266e0efc-7d6b-5a71-a0c4-fbafa01708a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The medial geniculate body is in the:', '["Medulla","Midbrain","Pons","Thalamus","Temporal lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus', 'The medial geniculate body is the auditory relay nucleus of the thalamus, receiving fibers from the inferior colliculus and projecting to the primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe.'),
('542c08c9-08ed-5c4f-82e4-abcb153dc776'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Tonotopic organization means:', '["Uniform distribution","Random organization","All frequencies at one location","Different frequencies mapped to different locations","Same frequency across areas"]'::jsonb, 'Different frequencies mapped to different locations', 'In tonotopic organization, neurons are arranged in an orderly spatial map according to the sound frequencies to which they respond. This spatial frequency map extends from the cochlea through the auditory pathway to the auditory cortex.'),
('1295388f-ed68-5a37-a955-e94dc508bcc4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Sound localization depends on:', '["Frequency only resulting in decreased functional activity","Harmonics accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Phase only mediated by specific receptor activation","Amplitude only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Interaural time and intensity differences"]'::jsonb, 'Interaural time and intensity differences', 'Sound localization relies primarily on interaural time differences, especially for low-frequency sounds, and on interaural intensity differences (head shadow), especially for high-frequency sounds.'),
('c7288f68-d08c-57e7-b25e-11f364d291fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Interaural time difference is used for:', '["Mid frequencies","All frequencies","Both low and high","Low frequencies","High frequencies"]'::jsonb, 'Low frequencies', 'Interaural time differences are the dominant cue for localizing low-frequency sounds, whose long wavelengths cannot be efficiently shadowed by the head. Phase locking of neurons to low frequencies enables time-based localization.'),
('6cef4bfd-817f-56e0-ae3d-0200e1b130b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Interaural intensity difference is used for:', '["All frequencies","High frequencies (above 3 kHz)","Both","Mid frequencies","Low frequencies"]'::jsonb, 'High frequencies (above 3 kHz)', 'Interaural intensity differences arise because the head casts an acoustic shadow, which is effective only for wavelengths shorter than the head diameter. This makes intensity cues most useful for frequencies above about 3 kHz.'),
('353b214d-8cdd-5798-a991-cad12ef87184'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The head shadow effect is most prominent for:', '["All frequencies","Low frequencies","Very low frequencies","Mid frequencies","High frequencies"]'::jsonb, 'High frequencies', 'The head attenuates sound reaching the far ear, creating an interaural intensity difference, but this shadow is effective only when the wavelength is smaller than the head size. High-frequency sounds therefore show the strongest head shadow effect.'),
('b2499446-11ed-5e8e-9246-9d9446f5aaa7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Regarding clinical cases: Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('be663868-3dd9-5613-a7cb-d7172f8ee857'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In conductive hearing loss the Rinne test shows:', '["AC > BC (normal)","BC > AC (abnormal)","AC = BC","Both decreased","Both increased"]'::jsonb, 'BC > AC (abnormal)', 'In conductive hearing loss the route through the middle ear is impaired, so bone conduction is heard longer than air conduction, giving a negative (abnormal) Rinne result (BC > AC).'),
('cc782320-1ff6-55bc-9ee1-7e6b04ae60aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 40-year-old female presents with gradual hearing loss over the past 6 months and is found to have conductive hearing loss. The Rinne test in such a patient will typically show:', '["AC = BC","Both absent","BC > AC","AC > BC (normal)","BC = AC"]'::jsonb, 'BC > AC', 'Conductive hearing loss impairs sound transmission through the middle ear, so bone conduction outlasts air conduction on the Rinne test (BC > AC), a negative (abnormal) Rinne result.'),
('6135d8f2-c090-5937-8345-c9afcba83092'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A Weber test with lateralization to the worse ear indicates:', '["Mixed hearing loss","Sensorineural hearing loss","Central deafness","Normal hearing","Conductive hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 'Conductive hearing loss', 'In conductive hearing loss, ambient noise masks bone conduction in the normal ear, so sound lateralizes to the affected (worse) ear on the Weber test. In sensorineural loss, it lateralizes to the better ear.'),
('d91e4ef9-740d-5b1c-a02e-ab56eda099fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Audiometry measures:', '["Reflex testing","Tympanic membrane mobility","Hearing threshold at different frequencies","Speech discrimination","Cochlear function"]'::jsonb, 'Hearing threshold at different frequencies', 'Pure-tone audiometry determines the softest sound a patient can hear at various frequencies, yielding an audiogram of hearing thresholds. It distinguishes conductive, sensorineural, and mixed hearing losses.'),
('6b4b80db-5498-5480-b631-46ebaa05cab3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 30-year-old female presents with gradual sensorineural hearing loss. What is the most likely underlying cause?', '["Brainstem lesion","External or middle ear pathology","Cochlear damage","Hyperfunction of the ossicles","Cortical lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear damage', 'Sensorineural hearing loss results from damage to the cochlea (hair cells) or the auditory nerve, not from external or middle ear disease. Cochlear damage, such as noise- or age-related hair cell loss, is the most common cause.'),
('5cd7485c-69d0-5e9e-969a-5b5de8646ada'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Sensorineural hearing loss is due to:', '["Ossicular fixation","Tympanic perforation","Middle ear problem","Cochlear or auditory nerve damage","Outer ear problem"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear or auditory nerve damage', 'Sensorineural hearing loss arises from pathology in the cochlea or the auditory nerve (CN VIII). Disorders affecting the outer or middle ear cause conductive hearing loss instead.'),
('90bb9c51-06bd-570b-bbec-b1a3cc9c299e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Tinnitus is:', '["Ringing or buzzing in ears without external sound","Hearing loss accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Fullness mediated by specific receptor activation","Vertigo without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ear pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ringing or buzzing in ears without external sound', 'Tinnitus is the perception of sound (commonly ringing or buzzing) in the absence of an external acoustic stimulus. It is a symptom associated with many inner ear and systemic disorders.'),
('b8e5eeb4-13f4-55ba-a429-c46610c17a78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Special Senses: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('6f5d88a5-8d09-550a-a07e-a31172e7cd33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'During therapeutic management of Special Senses, Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('7162f107-f07b-5c16-aec4-4d0a3cbc9616'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The utricle and saccule detect:', '["Vibration resulting in decreased functional activity","Linear acceleration and static tilt","Angular acceleration mediated by specific receptor activation","Rotational movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sound leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration and static tilt', 'The utricle and saccule contain maculae with otoconia that respond to linear acceleration, gravity, and static head tilt. Rotational acceleration is detected by the semicircular canals.'),
('ce0da1c0-3d5c-51a6-b53e-62585b9fa273'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'How many semicircular canals are present in an inner ear?', '["2","5","6","4","3"]'::jsonb, '3', 'Each inner ear has three semicircular canals (anterior, posterior, and horizontal), oriented roughly orthogonal to each other, to detect rotation in any plane.'),
('903073b5-8f26-57f8-928d-ffa98fa79441'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The semicircular canal ampulla contains:', '["Otoconia","Hair cells and cupula","Endolymph","Basilar membrane","Tectorial membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Hair cells and cupula', 'Each ampulla houses a crista with hair cells whose stereocilia project into the cupula; endolymph movement bends the cupula and stimulates the hair cells. Otoconia are found in the maculae of the utricle and saccule, not the ampulla.'),
('7ca667db-2dbc-5df6-9a2e-1a42eeb3262f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The macula of the utricle is oriented:', '["Randomly oriented resulting in decreased functional activity","Circular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Horizontal (detects horizontal acceleration)","Vertical without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Oblique leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal (detects horizontal acceleration)', 'The utricular macula lies roughly horizontal and responds to horizontal linear acceleration and head tilt. The saccular macula is vertically oriented and detects vertical acceleration.'),
('0c5caf5b-a51f-566a-8e6d-55d197ea0984'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The macula of the saccule is oriented:', '["Circular resulting in decreased functional activity","Vertical (detects vertical acceleration)","Horizontal mediated by specific receptor activation","Oblique without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Randomly oriented leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical (detects vertical acceleration)', 'The saccular macula lies in an approximately vertical plane and detects vertical linear acceleration and gravity. The utricular macula is oriented horizontally.'),
('b1e8797e-bfaf-5a03-a32e-0b238c924a62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Head rotation causes endolymph movement in:', '["Opposite direction to rotation (due to inertia)","Same direction as rotation accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Perpendicular to rotation mediated by specific receptor activation","No movement without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Random direction leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Opposite direction to rotation (due to inertia)', 'Owing to inertia, the endolymph lags behind and moves opposite to the direction of head rotation, displacing the cupula and stimulating the hair cells.'),
('4efdd186-da19-590c-b805-6641831324ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The vestibular nuclei are located in the:', '["Midbrain resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Cortex mediated by specific receptor activation","Medulla (rostral medulla/pons)","Cerebellum leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla (rostral medulla/pons)', 'The vestibular nuclear complex lies in the rostral medulla and pons at the floor of the fourth ventricle. It receives input from the vestibular nerve and coordinates balance, eye movements, and posture.'),
('58add99c-3c29-535c-bd1d-68bf3522b299'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex helps maintain:', '["Balance resulting in decreased functional activity","Eye fixation on target during head movement","Hearing mediated by specific receptor activation","Posture without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Head position leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Eye fixation on target during head movement', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex generates compensatory eye movements that keep the eyes fixed on a target during head movement. This is achieved by vestibular nuclei connections to the oculomotor nuclei, largely through the MLF.'),
('f546ef5f-35d9-5eda-85f1-491f83d0be76'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) connects:', '["Cerebellum and spinal cord resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basal ganglia mediated by specific receptor activation","Vestibular nuclei and oculomotor nuclei","Cochlear nuclei leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nuclei and oculomotor nuclei', 'The MLF links vestibular nuclei with the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nuclei, coordinating conjugate eye movements in response to head rotation. Damage to it causes internuclear ophthalmoplegia.'),
('c115b3af-52bd-583b-8e8c-98d0bc723d91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The direction of nystagmus is classically described by the:', '["Endolymph flow","Fast component direction","Both components","Cupula deflection direction","Slow component direction"]'::jsonb, 'Fast component direction', 'Nystagmus is named according to the direction of its fast (corrective) component, even though the slow vestibular component carries the reflex response. For example, COWS describes the direction based on the fast phase.'),
('c0712260-8533-511b-a2d7-4ca47751ec63'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The COWS acronym stands for:', '["Caloric test mnemonic","Vestibular reflex","Cold Opposite Warm Same","Nystagmus direction","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'Cold Opposite Warm Same', 'COWS is the caloric test mnemonic meaning Cold Opposite, Warm Same, referring to the direction of the nystagmus fast phase. Cold water in the right ear produces left-beating nystagmus, while warm water produces right-beating nystagmus.'),
('924001c7-3788-5c64-b4f1-d5eeb9f49c30'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) is due to:', '["Autoimmune resulting in decreased functional activity","Vascular accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endolymphatic hydrops mediated by specific receptor activation","Viral labyrinthitis without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cupulolithiasis (otoconia in semicircular canal)"]'::jsonb, 'Cupulolithiasis (otoconia in semicircular canal)', 'BPPV results from dislodged otoconia entering a semicircular canal, causing abnormal endolymph flow and vertigo with head position changes. Endolymphatic hydrops characterizes Meniere disease, not BPPV.'),
('642b85f4-a207-5a75-814a-f7668875e2c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The cell bodies of the primary afferent neurons of the vestibular nerve are located in the:', '["Scarpa ganglion (vestibular ganglion)","Vestibular nucleus","Spiral ganglion","Organ of Corti","Cochlear nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Scarpa ganglion (vestibular ganglion)', 'First-order vestibular neurons have their cell bodies in the vestibular (Scarpa) ganglion in the internal auditory canal. The spiral ganglion contains cochlear (auditory) first-order neurons.'),
('8348ec5d-7998-5550-8780-b8018ff5e4b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The vestibular nerve fibers project mainly to:', '["Thalamus","Medial geniculate","Inferior colliculus","Vestibular nuclei (medulla/pons)","Cochlear nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nuclei (medulla/pons)', 'Most vestibular afferents synapse in the vestibular nuclear complex in the medulla and pons. A smaller proportion project directly to the cerebellum via the juxtarestiform body.'),
('ce8ac2d8-728a-535c-a144-a23d4ae47202'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The cerebellum receives vestibular input via the:', '["Vestibular nuclei resulting in decreased functional activity","Superior cerebellar peduncle accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inferior cerebellar peduncle (juxtarestiform body)","Middle cerebellar peduncle without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Medial lemniscus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior cerebellar peduncle (juxtarestiform body)', 'Vestibular fibers reach the cerebellum mostly through the inferior cerebellar peduncle, including the juxtarestiform body, terminating in the flocculonodular lobe and fastigial nucleus.'),
('7fb7a2e7-a1d8-50b8-80b3-b50f9a9b7435'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Postural reflexes involve the:', '["Vestibular nuclei and vestibulospinal tracts","Basal ganglia accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Red nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cerebral cortex leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nuclei and vestibulospinal tracts', 'The vestibular nuclei give rise to the medial and lateral vestibulospinal tracts, which regulate extensor muscle tone and maintain upright posture. Damage to this system impairs balance and postural reflexes.'),
('5d152613-8625-5453-98dd-6fa5ab14e1d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In the study of Special Senses, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('178a8665-fd35-58f5-87c0-328b4478060a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'In which sensory organs are bipolar neurons the primary receptor cells?', '["Olfactory epithelium, retina, and cochlea","Skin touch receptors only","Muscle spindles only","Joint proprioceptors only","Visceral pain receptors only"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory epithelium, retina, and cochlea', 'Bipolar neurons serve as the primary receptor cells in the olfactory epithelium and as the sensory relay cells of the retina and cochlea. Skin touch, muscle spindle, joint, and visceral receptors rely on pseudounipolar ganglion neurons instead.'),
('8ef38208-8863-5ab6-a714-9fee2c88cdd3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Olfactory receptors are:', '["Ionotropic receptors","Metabotropic (GPCR) receptors","Mechanoreceptors","Thermoreceptors","Voltage-gated receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Metabotropic (GPCR) receptors', 'Olfactory receptors are G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) that activate adenylyl cyclase and raise cAMP, opening cyclic-nucleotide-gated cation channels. This metabotropic cascade produces the generator potential in olfactory neurons.'),
('a0649398-f7a5-5e88-9348-e1d66fb29286'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The olfactory nerve (CN I) passes from the nasal cavity into the skull through the:', '["Internal auditory meatus","Foramen ovale","Jugular foramen","Optic foramen","Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Cribriform plate of ethmoid bone', 'Olfactory nerve filaments traverse the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to reach the olfactory bulb. Fractures in this region can tear these filaments and cause anosmia.'),
('a0b5c2bf-26ee-562f-99a0-f9bb7e8892f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Olfactory receptor neurons are continuously replaced throughout adulthood. Their average lifespan (turnover time) is approximately:', '["Every few hours","Every year","Every 10 days","Every 30-60 days","Never"]'::jsonb, 'Every 30-60 days', 'Olfactory receptor neurons are among the few neurons replaced throughout life, turning over every 30-60 days from basal stem cells in the olfactory epithelium. This regenerative capacity underlies the recovery of smell after most causes of transient anosmia.'),
('d867c27d-6bf7-5e2e-ab30-bf2022030b76'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Olfactory transduction uses:', '["cGMP resulting in decreased functional activity","IP3 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Direct ion channel mediated by specific receptor activation","cAMP second messenger system (Golf -> AC -> cAMP)","Tyrosine kinase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP second messenger system (Golf -> AC -> cAMP)', 'Odorant binding activates the G-protein Golf, which stimulates adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and open cyclic nucleotide-gated cation channels, depolarizing the olfactory receptor neuron.'),
('7b2975e8-4271-5d3a-bcdd-d88fca748e06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The first synapse in the olfactory pathway occurs in:', '["Piriform cortex","Olfactory mucosa","Olfactory bulb","Olfactory tract","Cribriform plate"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory bulb', 'Axons of olfactory receptor neurons pass through the cribriform plate and synapse on mitral and tufted cells within glomeruli of the olfactory bulb, the first relay of the pathway.'),
('fbeb524c-f8a3-50da-9674-c44784b37a3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The olfactory cortex includes all EXCEPT:', '["Primary visual cortex","Piriform cortex","Entorhinal cortex","Olfactory tubercle","Amygdala"]'::jsonb, 'Primary visual cortex', 'The primary olfactory cortex comprises the piriform and entorhinal cortices, olfactory tubercle, and amygdala. The primary visual cortex lies in the occipital lobe and is uninvolved in olfaction.'),
('a30031be-0807-5497-bec4-ce512e3ef1b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Anosmia refers to:', '["Loss of taste","Loss of smell","Loss of balance","Loss of hearing","Loss of vision"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of smell', 'Anosmia is the partial or complete loss of the sense of smell, most often from nasal obstruction, trauma, or olfactory nerve damage. Loss of taste is termed ageusia.'),
('6d653069-b723-506b-a6ed-a3b7c048fe2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The mitral cells in the olfactory bulb receive input from:', '["Granule cells resulting in decreased functional activity","Periglomerular cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Basal cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Olfactory receptor cells (primary afferents)","Tufted cells leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory receptor cells (primary afferents)', 'Mitral cell dendrites receive synaptic input from the primary afferents, the axons of olfactory receptor neurons, within the glomeruli. Granule and periglomerular cells are inhibitory interneurons of the bulb.'),
('3e0abcac-1ad7-5139-8d63-a3166011b2e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The cribriform plate is part of:', '["Maxilla","Temporal bone","Frontal bone","Sphenoid bone","Ethmoid bone"]'::jsonb, 'Ethmoid bone', 'The cribriform plate is a midline portion of the ethmoid bone through which olfactory nerve fibers pass from the nasal cavity to the olfactory bulb.'),
('17be8983-6848-516c-b9bd-39946ce2cc1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Olfactory receptors are G-protein coupled to:', '["Gq resulting in decreased functional activity","Gi accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Go mediated by specific receptor activation","Golf (Gs-like)","Gz leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Golf (Gs-like)', 'Olfactory receptors couple to the Gs-like G-protein Golf, which activates adenylyl cyclase type III to increase cAMP and open cyclic nucleotide-gated channels.'),
('40a92136-11a1-58fe-a6af-907ac380d602'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The olfactory tract projects to:', '["Thalamus then to cortex resulting in decreased functional activity","LGN accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Medial geniculate mediated by specific receptor activation","Superior colliculus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Directly to olfactory cortex (no thalamic relay)"]'::jsonb, 'Directly to olfactory cortex (no thalamic relay)', 'Unlike most sensory systems, olfaction bypasses the thalamus: the olfactory tract projects directly to the piriform cortex and other olfactory cortical areas. The LGN, medial geniculate, and superior colliculus serve other sensory modalities.'),
('490ff29a-28e0-5d9f-ab54-49ad5fa9ff40'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The approximate number of taste buds in the adult human tongue is:', '["2000","500","10","None of the above","10000"]'::jsonb, '10000', 'The adult human tongue contains approximately 10,000 taste buds, with the highest density on the fungiform, circumvallate, and foliate papillae.'),
('19ee7d4a-4363-5790-a1aa-746085a27790'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Each taste bud contains how many taste receptor cells?', '["50-100","10-20","200-500","1","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '50-100', 'Each taste bud is made up of about 50-100 taste receptor cells (types I, II, and III) plus basal progenitor cells and supporting cells.'),
('7e9e774f-453c-5e9b-afc0-834c8ba961d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The primary taste qualities are:', '["Sweet resulting in decreased functional activity","Sour only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Salty mediated by specific receptor activation","Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami","Bitter only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami', 'There are five classic primary taste qualities: sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami, each detected by dedicated receptor mechanisms on taste cells.'),
('d370aa36-fc30-5462-b226-ee48bf199f90'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What is the primary mechanism of salt taste transduction?', '["Ca2+ release resulting in decreased functional activity","cAMP accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Na+ entry through ENaC channels","GPCR without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TRP channels leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ entry through ENaC channels', 'Salty taste results from Na+ entering apical amiloride-sensitive ENaC channels on type I taste cells, depolarizing the cell and generating a receptor potential.'),
('3e3cfa0d-8ad7-59f3-a9ee-50a47a819d1e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Sweet taste transduction in type II taste cells is primarily mediated by:', '["Tyrosine kinase","cGMP","Direct ion channel","GPCR (T1R2 + T1R3) -> cAMP","ENaC"]'::jsonb, 'GPCR (T1R2 + T1R3) -> cAMP', 'Sweet compounds bind the T1R2/T1R3 GPCR, which signals through the G-protein gustducin to raise cAMP and ultimately elevate intracellular Ca2+, triggering ATP release as the neurotransmitter.'),
('19e6a7af-1f8d-5019-98fc-d43f75c6ca73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which signaling cascade is activated by bitter taste receptors in type II taste cells to trigger calcium release?', '["GPCR (T2R family) -> Gq -> IP3 -> Ca2+","TRP channels accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T1R family mediated by specific receptor activation","ENaC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","cAMP leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GPCR (T2R family) -> Gq -> IP3 -> Ca2+', 'Bitter T2R receptors couple to Gq, activating phospholipase C-beta2 to generate IP3, which releases Ca2+ from intracellular stores and opens TRPM5 channels, leading to ATP release.'),
('ea3148f6-d718-53ae-8caf-aeeecd9465d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Umami taste detects:', '["Glutamate (amino acid)","Sweet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Salty mediated by specific receptor activation","Bitter without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sour leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate (amino acid)', 'Umami is evoked by L-glutamate and the nucleotides inosine and guanosine monophosphate, detected by T1R1/T1R3 GPCRs on taste cells.'),
('0f7a80d3-dc37-5d5c-8739-b8ebe76d0c88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) carries taste sensation from the:', '["Posterior 1/3 of tongue","Pharynx","Anterior 2/3 of tongue","Soft palate","Epiglottis"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior 2/3 of tongue', 'The chorda tympani of CN VII carries taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, with cell bodies in the geniculate ganglion projecting to the solitary nucleus. The posterior third of the tongue is served by CN IX.'),
('0d453eca-4da6-5980-869e-dfd4e8bf1b2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) innervates taste buds mainly on the:', '["Larynx","Posterior 1/3 of tongue","Soft palate","Epiglottis","Anterior 2/3 of tongue"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior 1/3 of tongue', 'CN IX supplies gustatory fibers to the posterior third of the tongue and also carries afferents from the carotid sinus and carotid body. CN VII and CN X serve the other regions listed.'),
('882986a0-aad7-530f-a03a-66b7d353ba1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The vagus nerve (CN X) innervates taste buds on the:', '["Posterior tongue","Anterior tongue","Hard palate","Epiglottis and pharynx","Lips"]'::jsonb, 'Epiglottis and pharynx', 'The vagus nerve carries taste fibers from the epiglottis and the pharyngeal and laryngeal mucosa, with cell bodies in the inferior (nodose) ganglion.'),
('e43ff7b4-6ae5-59ba-bac2-619b5b2ade97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The first central synapse for taste is in the:', '["Hypothalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Pons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Nucleus of the solitary tract (NST) in medulla","Midbrain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus of the solitary tract (NST) in medulla', 'All three gustatory nerves (VII, IX, X) synapse for the first time on neurons of the rostral nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla.'),
('b443a2a6-859b-5ae7-9e9b-1e8959e3a91f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The nucleus of the solitary tract (NST) is located in the:', '["Medulla oblongata","Cortex","Thalamus","Midbrain","Pons"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla oblongata', 'The nucleus of the solitary tract lies dorsolaterally within the medulla oblongata and receives gustatory and visceral afferents.'),
('bf21ad8a-6a65-5652-a022-199f1942690b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'From the nucleus of the solitary tract, taste fibers project to the:', '["Cerebellum","Spinal cord","Thalamus (VPM)","Hypothalamus","Basal ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus (VPM)', 'Taste fibers ascend ipsilaterally from the NST to the ventroposteromedial (VPM) nucleus of the thalamus, which relays to the primary gustatory cortex. Smaller collateral projections reach the hypothalamus and amygdala.'),
('e834443b-3704-52dd-97c4-99bc675e9eef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Special Senses: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('ea893d30-704f-5e19-9623-55346a3cd286'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Supertasters have:', '["More taste buds than average","Larger tongue","More filiform papillae","Fewer taste buds","More saliva"]'::jsonb, 'More taste buds than average', 'Supertasters have a higher density of fungiform papillae and taste buds, making them unusually sensitive to bitter compounds such as PROP and PTC. Filiform papillae, the most numerous on the tongue, contain no taste buds.'),
('d481fc6a-0fc3-52b3-af9d-284918b70b2d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which statement best describes the detection threshold for bitter taste compared with the other taste qualities?', '["Same as sweet resulting in decreased functional activity","Higher than sweet accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Much lower than other tastes (most sensitive)","Equal to sour without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Variable leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Much lower than other tastes (most sensitive)', 'Bitter substances are detected at the lowest concentrations of any taste quality, a protective mechanism against plant toxins and poisons. Sweet and salty stimuli require considerably higher concentrations.'),
('0010ccd2-848e-5fbb-8e01-e5e16c79494d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The normal turnover time of taste bud cells is:', '["1 year","10-14 days","Few days","6 months","1 month"]'::jsonb, '10-14 days', 'Taste receptor cells are replaced every 10-14 days by basal cells in the taste bud, which explains why taste usually recovers after superficial burns or trauma to the tongue.'),
('0317fd03-5b14-504a-86e7-4addd82d3126'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Meissner corpuscles detect:', '["Pain resulting in decreased functional activity","Skin stretch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vibration mediated by specific receptor activation","Light touch and texture discrimination","Deep pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Light touch and texture discrimination', 'Meissner corpuscles are rapidly adapting mechanoreceptors in the dermal papillae that signal light touch and fine texture discrimination.'),
('6c711c65-be8b-57a0-a29e-103ff881a586'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which mechanoreceptor detects deep pressure and high-frequency vibration?', '["Pacinian corpuscles","Meissner''s corpuscles","Merkel cells","Ruffini endings","Free nerve endings"]'::jsonb, 'Pacinian corpuscles', 'Pacinian (lamellar) corpuscles are rapidly adapting encapsulated receptors in the deep dermis and subcutaneous tissue that respond to deep pressure and high-frequency vibration.'),
('1f863ad6-572c-551a-a30e-7db443c4c70f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Merkel discs detect:', '["Light touch resulting in decreased functional activity","Temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vibration mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Sustained pressure and texture (slowly adapting)"]'::jsonb, 'Sustained pressure and texture (slowly adapting)', 'Merkel discs are slowly adapting mechanoreceptors that signal sustained pressure and texture, enabling continuous tactile discrimination.'),
('3fb71769-7ce9-56e6-9c62-1c1f746f3df7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Ruffini endings detect:', '["Light touch resulting in decreased functional activity","Temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vibration mediated by specific receptor activation","Pain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Skin stretch and proprioception"]'::jsonb, 'Skin stretch and proprioception', 'Ruffini endings are slowly adapting mechanoreceptors in the dermis and subcutaneous tissue that respond to skin stretch and contribute to proprioceptive and joint position sense.'),
('0d86d6e5-a951-55f6-a9be-aa05a5e6641d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Damage to the anterolateral (spinothalamic) pathway in the spinal cord classically impairs which sensations on the contralateral side?', '["Vibration","Pain and temperature","Light touch","Texture","Pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature', 'The spinothalamic (anterolateral) tract carries pain and temperature from the contralateral body side. Vibration, discriminative touch, and pressure travel in the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway.'),
('dbe0c9d5-a0d3-5955-8e65-dd2361b6aedc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Meissner corpuscles are located in the:', '["Dermal papillae (superficial skin)","Deep dermis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tendon mediated by specific receptor activation","Subcutaneous tissue without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dermal papillae (superficial skin)', 'Meissner corpuscles lie high in the dermal papillae of glabrous skin, particularly fingertip, lip, and eyelid skin, suited for light-touch detection.'),
('b0a36f4c-9a74-5a05-8d4f-715788e376cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Ruffini endings are located in the:', '["Muscle spindles resulting in decreased functional activity","Epidermis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Joints mediated by specific receptor activation","Dermis and subcutaneous tissue","Tendons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dermis and subcutaneous tissue', 'Ruffini endings reside in the dermis and subcutaneous tissue of skin and in joint capsules, where they detect skin stretch and tension.'),
('46850efe-1a49-59ba-bbdc-e29620da0812'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Meissner corpuscles adapt:', '["Slowly","Rapidly","Not at all","Moderately","Very slowly"]'::jsonb, 'Rapidly', 'Meissner corpuscles are rapidly adapting receptors, firing at stimulus onset and offset, which suits detection of moving light touch and texture changes.'),
('cda015ec-6486-5f9e-9762-4ba0c41e5df5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Pacinian corpuscles adapt:', '["Slowly resulting in decreased functional activity","Moderately accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Not at all mediated by specific receptor activation","None of the above without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Very rapidly (fastest adapting)"]'::jsonb, 'Very rapidly (fastest adapting)', 'Pacinian corpuscles are the fastest-adapting mechanoreceptors, responding mainly at stimulus onset and offset, which makes them ideal for vibration detection.'),
('1f240252-f677-5540-bf43-79539dfec562'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Merkel discs adapt:', '["Very rapidly","Not at all","Rapidly adapting","Slowly adapting (SA)","Moderately"]'::jsonb, 'Slowly adapting (SA)', 'Merkel discs are slowly adapting (SA-I) receptors that produce a sustained discharge during constant pressure, supporting continuous tactile texture perception.'),
('0f72d0e5-9062-5673-9219-a910e4fdcf6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Ruffini endings adapt:', '["Moderately","Rapidly adapting","Very rapidly","Not at all","Slowly adapting (SA2)"]'::jsonb, 'Slowly adapting (SA2)', 'Ruffini endings are slowly adapting type II (SA2) mechanoreceptors that give sustained responses to maintained skin stretch.'),
('6ae4cbf9-d052-52cc-99fb-28b10964db73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A beta (Abeta) fibers carry:', '["Pain only resulting in decreased functional activity","Pain and temperature accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Touch, pressure, vibration, and proprioception (discriminative touch)","Autonomic visceral transmission without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unmyelinated slow-burning pain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Touch, pressure, vibration, and proprioception (discriminative touch)', 'Abeta fibers are large, myelinated, rapidly conducting axons from Meissner, Pacinian, Merkel, and Ruffini receptors that carry discriminative touch, pressure, vibration, and proprioception. Pain and temperature are carried by Adelta and C fibers.'),
('bf2e9076-a2be-5096-9507-8115fd71c84d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A delta (Adelta) fibers carry:', '["Pain and temperature (fast pain)","Light touch accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Vibration mediated by specific receptor activation","Proprioception without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Deep pressure leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature (fast pain)', 'Adelta fibers are thinly myelinated, moderately fast fibers that transmit fast, sharp pain and cold sensation. Unmyelinated C fibers mediate slow, burning pain.'),
('4bae30a7-60f9-584e-b094-7d76ae853084'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The spinothalamic tract carries:', '["Pain and temperature (plus crude touch)","Discriminative touch and proprioception","Vibration and two-point discrimination","Motor commands to skeletal muscle","Autonomic visceral reflexes"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature (plus crude touch)', 'The spinothalamic (anterolateral) tract conveys pain, temperature, and crude touch. Discriminative touch, vibration, and proprioception are carried by the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway.'),
('2f4a9a0a-c59b-5d31-a877-c19b5401812d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The dorsal columns are located in the:', '["Spinal cord (posterior/dorsal funiculus)","Cortex accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","Brainstem without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Spinal cord (anterior) leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord (posterior/dorsal funiculus)', 'The dorsal (posterior) columns occupy the posterior funiculus of the spinal cord and carry fine touch, vibration, and conscious proprioception to the brainstem.'),
('d153c786-2d83-5ac2-b6ba-f6876965b692'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 25-year-old male presents with progressive muscle weakness and atrophy. Which pathophysiological process best explains this presentation?', '["Spinal cord","Dorsal root ganglion (DRG)","Brainstem","Thalamus","Ventral horn"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral horn', 'Progressive weakness with atrophy reflects lower motor neuron disease, and the cell bodies of these neurons lie in the ventral (anterior) horn of the spinal cord. The dorsal root ganglion is a sensory structure and does not produce this picture.'),
('69562bf2-7c2b-50a3-972a-cdb041e9841c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 45-year-old female presents with progressive muscle weakness and atrophy. What is the underlying mechanism of this condition?', '["Medulla (nucleus gracilis/cuneatus decussation)","Spinal cord level","Thalamus","Cortex","Midbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord level', 'Weakness with atrophy localizes to lower motor neurons, whose cell bodies lie in the anterior horn at the appropriate spinal cord level. The medullary gracile and cuneate nuclei are sensory relays rather than motor structures.'),
('cfc83432-f76f-568d-b6e7-34f230461e91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The medial lemniscus is located in the:', '["Midbrain","Brainstem (medulla and pons)","Spinal cord","Internal capsule","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Brainstem (medulla and pons)', 'After the internal arcuate fibers decussate, the medial lemniscus ascends through the medulla and pons and terminates in the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus.'),
('e49b1893-684b-529d-a866-8b0fbceab013'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Where are the cell bodies of the first-order sensory neurons of the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway?', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinal cord (at level of entry)","Pons mediated by specific receptor activation","Midbrain without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord (at level of entry)', 'The cell bodies of the first-order neurons of the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway lie in the dorsal root ganglia at the level of entry. The gracile and cuneate nuclei in the medulla house the second-order neurons.'),
('8b7d2871-a486-5469-83e6-dd51bbd326ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The anterior spinothalamic tract carries:', '["Crude touch and pressure","Vibration","Proprioception","Pain and temperature","Fine touch"]'::jsonb, 'Crude touch and pressure', 'The anterior spinothalamic tract conveys crude touch and pressure, whereas the lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature.'),
('833a5b72-1a80-500e-abf1-80d6b9d2cb5c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What is the anatomical term for a group of motor neuron cell bodies in the CNS that innervate a single muscle?', '["Bone segments","Motor neuron pools","Lymphatic drainage areas","Blood vessel territories","Areas of skin innervated by a single spinal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Motor neuron pools', 'A motor neuron pool is the cluster of alpha motor neuron cell bodies in the ventral horn that innervates a single muscle. The other options describe unrelated anatomical territories.'),
('bb90763f-2159-50ad-8ec8-10a5ca18dfdb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The largest area of sensory cortex represents:', '["Trunk resulting in decreased functional activity","Legs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Back mediated by specific receptor activation","Arms without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hands (fingertips) and lips/face"]'::jsonb, 'Hands (fingertips) and lips/face', 'Body parts with the densest innervation, the fingertips and the lips/face, occupy the largest area of the somatosensory homunculus.'),
('8c231afb-6370-5abe-b511-c91e5a2ed4d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 40-year-old male presents with muscle weakness. His serum sodium is 120 mEq/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Back","Hyponatremia","Fingertips and lips","Thigh","Arm"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia', 'A sodium of 120 mEq/L is severe hyponatremia, which impairs nerve and muscle cell function and clinically manifests as muscle weakness and fatigue.'),
('e7abf326-25c9-55c4-b57e-2114ce8a0bb3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Receptive field size is smallest at:', '["On thigh","On forearm","On back","At fingertips","On trunk"]'::jsonb, 'At fingertips', 'Receptive fields are smallest where sensory innervation density is greatest, such as the fingertips, allowing the finest two-point discrimination.'),
('329cc94e-a90a-536f-9a75-8b8c484352bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Tonic receptors:', '["Detect change only resulting in decreased functional activity","Detect vibration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Detect sustained stimuli (slowly adapting)","Only respond to onset without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adapt rapidly leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Detect sustained stimuli (slowly adapting)', 'Tonic (slowly adapting) receptors fire continuously during a maintained stimulus and encode stimulus intensity and duration, unlike rapidly adapting phasic receptors.'),
('c1164ca6-2e63-55b9-b31e-383ca99b221b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Proprioception is carried by:', '["Spinocerebellar tract","Dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway","Rubrospinal tract","Spinothalamic tract","Corticospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway', 'Conscious proprioception (position sense) ascends in the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway. The spinocerebellar tracts relay unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum.'),
('53378863-7916-5419-a705-18c4431c0f57'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 30-year-old female presents with impaired temperature sensation. Which pathway is most likely involved?', '["Corticospinal resulting in decreased functional activity","Spinocerebellar accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system)","Rubrospinal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Dorsal column pathway leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system)', 'Pain and temperature ascend in the spinothalamic (anterolateral) tract. Injury to this pathway produces a contralateral dissociated loss of pain and temperature.'),
('05927060-c9e3-5317-806e-938db9c0ee29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Pain is carried by:', '["Corticospinal resulting in decreased functional activity","Medial lemniscus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system)","Dorsal column pathway without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Fasciculus gracilis leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system)', 'Pain and temperature fibers cross in the spinal cord and ascend in the spinothalamic (anterolateral) tract to the thalamus.'),
('a80dd34f-524e-5271-9beb-fa51b6bc40d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The gate control theory of pain was proposed by:', '["Cannon","Wall and Melzack","Hodgkin","Pavlov","Sherrington"]'::jsonb, 'Wall and Melzack', 'Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall proposed the gate control theory in 1965, in which spinal cord interneurons modulate the transmission of pain signals to the brain.'),
('b08bd801-7506-5dbc-a32a-0ffd133a6ef0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Substance P is:', '["A neurotransmitter for pain (nociception)","An endogenous opioid","A hormone","A neuropeptide (tachykinin)","A cytokine"]'::jsonb, 'A neuropeptide (tachykinin)', 'Substance P is an 11-amino-acid tachykinin neuropeptide released by primary nociceptive afferents in the dorsal horn, where it promotes pain transmission.'),
('618df96a-52db-5aa9-86f6-906e7c0491d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The periaqueductal gray (PAG) mediates:', '["Pain facilitation","Descending pain inhibition (analgesia)","Autonomic function","Motor control","Sensory processing"]'::jsonb, 'Descending pain inhibition (analgesia)', 'The periaqueductal gray is the main supraspinal center of the endogenous analgesic system; its stimulation activates descending pathways that inhibit pain transmission in the dorsal horn.'),
('5d21ae9f-a4ba-5248-b390-f58147a1d2cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Endogenous opioids include:', '["Glutamate only resulting in decreased functional activity","Enkephalins accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endorphins mediated by specific receptor activation","Substance P only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Endorphins, enkephalins, and dynorphins"]'::jsonb, 'Endorphins, enkephalins, and dynorphins', 'The three main families of endogenous opioid peptides are the endorphins, enkephalins, and dynorphins, all of which act on opioid receptors to produce analgesia.'),
('cf191749-f4d3-503f-9bdc-82419c99de14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Referred pain is due to:', '["Cortical reorganization resulting in decreased functional activity","Convergence of visceral and somatic afferents on same spinal neurons","Phantom limb mediated by specific receptor activation","Divergence of pain fibers without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Psychogenic leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Convergence of visceral and somatic afferents on same spinal neurons', 'Referred pain occurs because visceral and somatic pain afferents converge onto the same dorsal horn neurons, so visceral pain is perceived at the corresponding somatic dermatome. Divergence would spread pain rather than refer it.'),
('318e0fc2-2441-542b-866d-8949c6ab0433'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'What is the term for increased sensitivity to pain, where normally painful stimuli become more painful?', '["Pain at rest","Loss of pain","Decreased pain sensitivity","Hyperalgesia","Increased pain sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperalgesia', 'Hyperalgesia is heightened pain sensitivity in which normally painful stimuli produce exaggerated pain, resulting from sensitization of peripheral nociceptors or central pain pathways.'),
('cfdad0f6-78af-58f8-afa8-d690b0213932'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The spinocervicothalamic pathway carries:', '["Vibration","Temperature","Proprioception","Crude touch","Pain"]'::jsonb, 'Crude touch', 'The spinocervicothalamic pathway is a minor ascending tract that conveys crude touch (and some pain information) via the lateral cervical nucleus to the thalamus.'),
('4531f781-6973-5b31-a28c-75b24614127a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 50-year-old male presents with muscle weakness. His serum sodium is 120 mEq/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["CN V (trigeminal)","CN X","CN VII","Hyponatremia","CN IX"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia', 'Hyponatremia (Na+ 120 mEq/L) is an electrolyte disturbance that alters membrane excitability and presents with muscle weakness; cranial nerve pathology is not suggested by these electrolyte values.'),
('932b30d3-f563-5925-9911-d97005d0c166'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which nucleus of the trigeminal (CN V) complex relays discriminative touch from the face?', '["Chief sensory nucleus (touch)","Spinal nucleus (pain and temperature)","Mesencephalic nucleus (proprioception)","Motor nucleus of CN V","Nucleus solitarius"]'::jsonb, 'Chief sensory nucleus (touch)', 'The chief (principal) sensory nucleus of CN V relays discriminative touch; the spinal nucleus relays pain and temperature, the mesencephalic nucleus relays jaw proprioception, and the motor nucleus supplies the muscles of mastication.'),
('760917be-031d-5fad-8fee-9a2eedb9c22b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Proprioception from muscle spindles is carried by:', '["Ib afferents","II afferents","IV afferents","Ia afferents","III afferents"]'::jsonb, 'Ia afferents', 'Group Ia afferents from the annulospiral endings of muscle spindles signal muscle length and velocity and excite motoneurons in the stretch reflex; Ib afferents arise from Golgi tendon organs.'),
('cee1e63c-8117-5985-81f3-c8a61996c3b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Spermatogenesis takes approximately how many days?', '["74 days (about 64-74)","7 days","14 days","30 days","100 days"]'::jsonb, '74 days (about 64-74)', 'Complete spermatogenesis from spermatogonium to mature spermatozoon takes about 74 days, with a typical range of 64-74 days.'),
('afcbba28-a494-5951-9fdb-3f49f69e299e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The muscle spindle receives which combination of motor and sensory innervation?', '["Beta motor neurons","Alpha motor neurons only","Only autonomic","Gamma motor neurons and Ia afferents","Only sensory"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma motor neurons and Ia afferents', 'Intrafusal spindle fibers are activated by gamma motor neurons, and spindle sensory information is carried to the cord by Ia afferents; this arrangement maintains spindle sensitivity during muscle shortening.'),
('b25e36f6-9f15-574f-b967-7d964cddb369'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 50-year-old female presents with difficulty climbing stairs due to proximal muscle weakness. Which neurons are most directly responsible for generating force in the affected muscles?', '["Alpha motor neurons","Beta motor neurons","Gamma motor neurons","Delta motor neurons","Upper motor neurons"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha motor neurons', 'Alpha motor neurons (lower motor neurons) directly innervate extrafusal skeletal muscle fibers, so their loss causes weakness and atrophy. Gamma neurons only tune spindle sensitivity and do not generate force.'),
('a796d936-e78c-52da-a028-c98546776c2f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The patellar (knee jerk) reflex is mediated by which spinal segments?', '["L2-L4","C5-C6","S2-S4","L5-S1","T1-T2"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4', 'The patellar tendon reflex is mediated by the L2, L3, and L4 spinal segments through the femoral nerve.'),
('17817787-fdd7-581e-abee-097a5c5d4e45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The Achilles tendon (ankle) reflex is mediated by which spinal segments?', '["L2-L4 resulting in decreased functional activity","C5-C6 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","T1-T2 mediated by specific receptor activation","S2-S4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","L5-S2 (S1 primarily)"]'::jsonb, 'L5-S2 (S1 primarily)', 'The ankle jerk is mediated chiefly by the S1 spinal segment (L5-S2), conducted through the tibial nerve.'),
('6dbcb563-8c1b-5a59-9b92-4c6361a7d110'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'The Babinski sign (extensor plantar response) indicates:', '["Basal ganglia lesion","Upper motor neuron lesion","Spinal cord transection","Cerebellar lesion","Lower motor neuron lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Upper motor neuron lesion', 'The extensor plantar response (Babinski sign) is a hallmark of upper motor neuron (corticospinal) damage, because descending cortical inhibition of the extensor reflex is lost.'),
('ae48a550-26d0-5199-8614-617ab339aa53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Clonus is associated with:', '["Basal ganglia disease","Upper motor neuron lesion","Cerebellar disease","Lower motor neuron lesion","Sensory loss"]'::jsonb, 'Upper motor neuron lesion', 'Clonus is rhythmic involuntary muscle contraction seen with upper motor neuron lesions, as loss of descending inhibition produces exaggerated stretch reflexes.'),
('82458c11-80fa-5a23-adc5-5ae9191ec640'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Spasticity is due to:', '["Muscle disease","Cerebellar lesion","Basal ganglia lesion","Upper motor neuron lesion","Lower motor neuron lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Upper motor neuron lesion', 'Spasticity (velocity-dependent increase in muscle tone) results from damage to upper motor neurons, which removes descending inhibition of spinal reflexes.'),
('4a357a2b-c82d-550e-adf4-2b98731272fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'A 70-year-old male presents with cardiac arrhythmia. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 120 mEq/L. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Hyponatremia","Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypocalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia', 'A serum sodium of 120 mEq/L defines severe hyponatremia, which can present with cardiac arrhythmias and muscle weakness by altering membrane excitability.'),
('e9d52734-07bd-55b2-a44b-6ceac00b56e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Hyponatremia is defined as a serum sodium concentration below which value?', '["135 mEq/L","140 mEq/L","145 mEq/L","150 mEq/L","155 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '135 mEq/L', 'Hyponatremia is a serum sodium concentration below 135 mEq/L; sodium values below roughly 125 mEq/L may cause arrhythmias and neurological dysfunction.'),
('20fb00bf-079b-5c95-b71f-6214a3e94869'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Correction of which electrolyte deficiency is most appropriate for this patient with cardiac arrhythmia and a serum sodium of 120 mEq/L?', '["Sodium (as sodium chloride)","Potassium (as potassium chloride)","Calcium (as calcium gluconate)","Magnesium (as magnesium sulfate)","Phosphate (as sodium phosphate)"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium (as sodium chloride)', 'In symptomatic severe hyponatremia the deficient electrolyte is sodium; careful sodium replacement restores the serum level while monitoring to avoid osmotic demyelination.'),
('a81ca37f-81cc-519a-b236-c85282408508'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Which condition is the most common cause of hyponatremia in hospitalized patients?', '["SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone)","Diabetes insipidus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Adrenal insufficiency mediated by specific receptor activation","Primary hyperaldosteronism without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperthyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone)', 'SIADH is the most common cause of euvolemic hyponatremia, especially in hospitalized patients, because excess antidiuretic hormone impairs free-water excretion.'),
('d7e037f3-6004-5158-8dba-f13a262d791f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Severe acute hyponatremia is most dangerous because it most often causes:', '["Cerebral edema with herniation","Pulmonary fibrosis","Hepatic failure","Acute renal failure","Bone fractures"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral edema with herniation', 'A rapid fall in serum sodium draws water into brain cells, causing cerebral edema and, if severe, herniation; symptoms include headache, seizures, and coma.'),
('4eb67af4-51ba-5530-84e0-f352709cd90d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses - Special Senses', 'Myopia (nearsightedness) is corrected with:', '["Concave (minus) lenses","Convex (plus) lenses","Prisms","Cylindrical lenses","Bifocal lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Concave (minus) lenses', 'In myopia the image focuses in front of the retina, and concave (minus, diverging) lenses shift the focal point posteriorly onto the retina.'),
('de8aec2d-a7e0-5fdf-9a07-479df967f069'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hormones are categorized into several chemical classes. Which of the following is NOT a recognized chemical class of hormones?', '["Peptide/protein","Steroid","Nucleotide","Eicosanoid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'Hormones are classified by chemical structure into peptide/protein, steroid, amine, and eicosanoid classes. Nucleotides are not a class of hormones; they are the building blocks of nucleic acids and include signaling molecules such as ATP and cAMP.'),
('2c3a3e5d-e1ec-5565-886e-82d49519663b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Where are peptide hormones initially synthesized?', '["On ribosomes of the rough endoplasmic reticulum","In the Golgi apparatus as preprohormones","By free ribosomes","In the nucleus","In mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'On ribosomes of the rough endoplasmic reticulum', 'Peptide hormones are initially synthesized on ribosomes bound to the rough endoplasmic reticulum as preprohormones. They are later processed into prohormones and active hormones in the Golgi apparatus before being packaged into secretory vesicles.'),
('49e3e249-b3f3-541e-9085-6e1b5d5cf674'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which of the following is the precursor for steroid hormone synthesis?', '["Nucleotides","Amino acids","Fatty acids","Cholesterol","Glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol', 'All steroid hormones are synthesized from cholesterol, which is converted to pregnenolone as the first committed step. This occurs in steroidogenic tissues such as the adrenal cortex and the gonads.'),
('a0d74f4a-3643-5653-a271-6943ca5b380e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Amine hormones (catecholamines and thyroid hormones) are derived from which amino acids?', '["Glucose","Tyrosine or tryptophan","Cholesterol","Nucleotides","Fatty acids"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine or tryptophan', 'Amine hormones are synthesized from amino acids: catecholamines and thyroid hormones derive from tyrosine, while serotonin and melatonin derive from tryptophan.'),
('61b80cd7-7fda-5153-b7d7-3caba3a790a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Eicosanoids are derived from which of the following?', '["Glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Tyrosine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachidonic acid (fatty acid)","Tryptophan without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholesterol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachidonic acid (fatty acid)', 'Eicosanoids such as prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes are derived from arachidonic acid, a 20-carbon polyunsaturated fatty acid released from membrane phospholipids.'),
('8b537a1f-d52e-5ec1-9cc0-e9dd9183d775'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Where are peptide hormones stored before their release?', '["In lysosomes resulting in decreased functional activity","Not stored accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Secretory vesicles (ready for release)","In nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","In Golgi only leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Secretory vesicles (ready for release)', 'Peptide hormones are stored in membrane-bound secretory vesicles until a stimulus triggers their release by exocytosis. Unlike steroid hormones, which are synthesized on demand and not stored, peptide hormones can be released rapidly from these preformed vesicles.'),
('f821c36d-37cc-57c7-b9bf-cdd81718c1c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which of the following statements best describes steroid hormone synthesis?', '["Stored in Golgi","Stored in nucleus","Synthesized on demand (not stored)","Stored in vesicles","Stored in cytoplasm"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesized on demand (not stored)', 'Steroid hormones are lipid-soluble and diffuse out of cells immediately after synthesis. They are not stored in vesicles and are instead synthesized on demand from cholesterol.'),
('3a7d73f6-dfb8-5288-94e9-2b9361f8478c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'How are peptide hormones transported in the blood?', '["Bound to carrier proteins","Bound to albumin","Bound to globulins","In RBCs","Freely dissolved (unbound)"]'::jsonb, 'Freely dissolved (unbound)', 'Peptide hormones are water-soluble and travel freely dissolved in plasma without carrier proteins. By contrast, steroid and thyroid hormones circulate bound to plasma transport proteins.'),
('e34b0ab2-961b-5b8c-a029-703d7d2e2af2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 40-year-old female presents with palpitations, weight loss, and heat intolerance, suggestive of hyperthyroidism. How are the responsible hormones transported in the blood?', '["In RBCs","In WBCs","Bound to carrier proteins","Freely dissolved","In platelets"]'::jsonb, 'Bound to carrier proteins', 'The clinical picture indicates hyperthyroidism with excess thyroid hormone. Thyroid hormones circulate largely bound to carrier proteins, chiefly thyroxine-binding globulin, with only a small free fraction.'),
('64c05fd9-cfc8-540b-8aef-2795d8e05887'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the characteristic plasma half-life of peptide hormones?', '["Long (hours to days)","Very long (weeks)","Same as steroids","Variable","Short (minutes)"]'::jsonb, 'Short (minutes)', 'Peptide hormones are water-soluble and rapidly degraded in the circulation, giving them short half-lives of minutes. This allows rapid adjustments in their plasma concentrations.'),
('2f5622de-2d10-5ce1-ab19-889a2a86c0c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the typical plasma half-life of steroid hormones?', '["Very long (weeks)","Years","Short (minutes)","Relatively long (hours)","Seconds"]'::jsonb, 'Relatively long (hours)', 'Steroid hormones are lipid-soluble and circulate bound to carrier proteins, which slows their degradation and clearance. They therefore have relatively long half-lives of hours to days.'),
('0b9734c7-2768-5d5c-8582-fd6d5906edea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'How many transmembrane domains does a typical G-protein coupled receptor (GPCR) have?', '["3","5","4","7","2"]'::jsonb, '7', 'GPCRs are seven-transmembrane receptors, with seven alpha-helical domains crossing the plasma membrane. They couple to heterotrimeric G proteins to relay extracellular signals to intracellular effectors.'),
('57c7975c-d1cb-56ac-a7ab-041f6d43e1d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 60-year-old female presents with altered mental status and hyponatremia (Na+ 120 mEq/L) from syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion. Which second messenger mediates the antidiuretic action of ADH in the collecting duct?', '["Ca2+","cGMP","DAG","cAMP","IP3"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP', 'ADH acts on V2 receptors in the collecting duct via the Gs-adenylyl cyclase pathway, raising cAMP. Elevated cAMP promotes insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels, enhancing water reabsorption and worsening the hyponatremia.'),
('21050502-b7c2-5a3f-a1cf-a09a8fbd729d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Stimulation of the Gi protein decreases which of the following second messengers?', '["Ca2+","IP3","cGMP","DAG","cAMP"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP', 'Gi inhibits adenylyl cyclase, thereby reducing cAMP production. This opposes the stimulatory effect of Gs-coupled receptors on cAMP synthesis.'),
('52d29162-ce89-5777-b303-c9a7b99e54af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The Gq protein activates which of the following?', '["Guanylyl cyclase resulting in decreased functional activity","Phospholipase C (PLC) -> IP3 and DAG","Tyrosine kinase mediated by specific receptor activation","PKA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adenylyl cyclase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Phospholipase C (PLC) -> IP3 and DAG', 'Gq activates phospholipase C, which cleaves membrane phosphatidylinositol bisphosphate into IP3 and DAG. IP3 releases calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum, while DAG activates protein kinase C.'),
('190875d9-881f-5746-8a77-d6b0af754c67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The second messenger cAMP activates which of the following kinases?', '["Protein kinase A (PKA)","Protein kinase C","Protein kinase G","CaM kinase","Tyrosine kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Protein kinase A (PKA)', 'cAMP binds the regulatory subunits of protein kinase A, releasing its catalytic subunits to phosphorylate downstream target proteins. PKA is the principal effector kinase of the cAMP signaling pathway.'),
('1e5d9c27-d6bb-5e7f-a868-067883fb4817'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The second messenger cGMP activates which of the following?', '["PKC","Tyrosine kinase","Protein kinase G (PKG)","PKA","CaM kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Protein kinase G (PKG)', 'cGMP activates protein kinase G, which mediates physiological responses such as nitric-oxide-induced vasodilation and ANP-induced natriuresis.'),
('45a16e43-cce3-5032-8899-1f61ed5a9291'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the primary effect of IP3 on intracellular calcium?', '["Activation of PKA resulting in decreased functional activity","Release of Ca2+ from endoplasmic reticulum","cGMP increase mediated by specific receptor activation","Activation of PKC without affecting the primary tissue pathway","cAMP increase leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Release of Ca2+ from endoplasmic reticulum', 'IP3 binds IP3 receptors on the endoplasmic reticulum and triggers release of stored calcium into the cytosol. The resulting rise in cytosolic calcium drives calcium-dependent cellular responses.'),
('dcd63618-dca5-5f62-a056-f07adbd6d0bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'DAG (diacylglycerol) activates which of the following?', '["PKG","Tyrosine kinase","PKA","CaM kinase","Protein kinase C (PKC)"]'::jsonb, 'Protein kinase C (PKC)', 'DAG remains in the plasma membrane and activates protein kinase C, often together with the calcium released by IP3. PKC then phosphorylates downstream targets to propagate the Gq signaling cascade.'),
('fd0231b9-afa8-5ed6-a627-dbfa106f487b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The calcium-calmodulin complex activates which of the following?', '["CaM kinase (calmodulin-dependent kinase)","Adenylyl cyclase accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","PKC mediated by specific receptor activation","PKA without affecting the primary tissue pathway","PKG leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'CaM kinase (calmodulin-dependent kinase)', 'Calcium binds calmodulin, and the calcium-calmodulin complex activates CaM kinase, which phosphorylates a range of cellular targets. This links rises in cytosolic calcium to enzyme regulation.'),
('206aa447-985e-55e8-9cd8-7607b74e622a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The JAK-STAT signaling pathway is utilized by which of the following hormones?', '["Catecholamines","Insulin","Growth hormone and prolactin","Thyroid hormone","Steroid hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Growth hormone and prolactin', 'Growth hormone and prolactin signal through receptors that associate with JAK tyrosine kinases, activating the JAK-STAT pathway. Insulin uses receptor tyrosine kinases, while steroid and thyroid hormones act through nuclear receptors.'),
('37666568-426b-5c1f-86f8-b342a835d37e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Nuclear hormone receptors characteristically contain which combination of domains?', '["G-protein binding","Tyrosine kinase domain","7 transmembrane domains","Ion channel","DNA-binding domain + ligand-binding domain"]'::jsonb, 'DNA-binding domain + ligand-binding domain', 'Nuclear receptors contain a ligand-binding domain, a DNA-binding domain, and a transactivation domain. After ligand binding they regulate gene transcription directly, consistent with the slow genomic actions of steroid and thyroid hormones.'),
('3b5d96b2-f881-5ad0-9637-38da6635db2f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The hypothalamic-pituitary axis connects the hypothalamus to which structures?', '["Cerebellum to pituitary","Hypothalamus to posterior pituitary only","Cortex to pituitary","Hypothalamus to anterior and posterior pituitary","Thalamus to pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus to anterior and posterior pituitary', 'The hypothalamus regulates both pituitary lobes: releasing and inhibiting hormones travel through the portal system to the anterior lobe, while axons of the hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract reach the posterior lobe.'),
('1303a707-0aaf-501e-8b47-984b90b0c369'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'How do hypothalamic releasing hormones reach the anterior pituitary?', '["Via the hypophyseal portal blood vessels","By direct axonal transport from hypothalamic neurons","Through the systemic arterial circulation","Via the cerebrospinal fluid of the third ventricle","By diffusion across the blood-brain barrier"]'::jsonb, 'Via the hypophyseal portal blood vessels', 'Hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones are secreted into the hypophyseal portal system, a capillary network that delivers them directly to the anterior pituitary. This portal route ensures high, targeted concentrations of these hormones.'),
('65e7e30c-4a58-5a85-86d3-8d33b9c9f67c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The posterior pituitary is derived from which of the following?', '["Mesoderm resulting in decreased functional activity","Oral ectoderm (Rathke pouch) accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Endoderm mediated by specific receptor activation","Neural crest without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Neural ectoderm (down growth from hypothalamus)"]'::jsonb, 'Neural ectoderm (down growth from hypothalamus)', 'The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) develops as a down-growth from the hypothalamus and is therefore of neural ectodermal origin. The anterior pituitary, by contrast, arises from oral ectoderm of Rathke''s pouch.'),
('136cf535-1a02-5805-920f-22b1b8d0b4e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The anterior pituitary is derived from which of the following?', '["Neural crest","Mesoderm","Endoderm","Neural ectoderm","Oral ectoderm (Rathke pouch)"]'::jsonb, 'Oral ectoderm (Rathke pouch)', 'The anterior pituitary develops from an up-growth of oral ectoderm called Rathke''s pouch. The posterior lobe, in contrast, arises from neural ectoderm of the hypothalamus.'),
('359e73a1-6ec2-595b-a9de-8ae4114addb0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The hypothalamus is connected to the posterior pituitary via which structure?', '["Blood vessels resulting in decreased functional activity","Capillaries accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract (axons)","Portal veins without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Lymphatics leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract (axons)', 'The hypothalamus projects axons through the infundibulum to form the hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract, which transports ADH and oxytocin to the posterior pituitary for storage and release.'),
('103062dd-5674-55e4-8a14-73521d7537c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hormone is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary to regulate water balance?', '["GnRH","CRH","TRH","Oxytocin","ADH (vasopressin)"]'::jsonb, 'ADH (vasopressin)', 'ADH (vasopressin) is synthesized in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus and transported to the posterior pituitary for release. It increases water reabsorption in the renal collecting ducts.'),
('4121e56c-86b4-50a8-b080-9bb2b553a43c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hormones does the posterior pituitary store and release?', '["ADH only","CRH only","Oxytocin only","ADH and oxytocin","GH and prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'ADH and oxytocin', 'The posterior pituitary stores and releases ADH and oxytocin, both synthesized in the hypothalamus. It does not synthesize hormones itself; GH and prolactin are products of the anterior pituitary.'),
('2125718e-dc25-560e-b29f-6ebed12b90b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hypothalamic releasing hormones are secreted into which of the following?', '["Posterior pituitary","Hypophyseal portal blood","Third ventricle","Systemic circulation","CSF"]'::jsonb, 'Hypophyseal portal blood', 'Hypothalamic releasing and inhibiting hormones are secreted into the hypophyseal portal blood, which carries them directly to the anterior pituitary. This portal system provides precise regulation of anterior pituitary secretion.'),
('22fa49bf-49ff-509c-b05d-115bd6102fde'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which anterior pituitary hormones are stimulated by TRH from the hypothalamus?', '["ACTH","MSH","GH","TSH and prolactin","FSH and LH"]'::jsonb, 'TSH and prolactin', 'TRH stimulates the release of both TSH and prolactin from the anterior pituitary. Its principal role is thyroid regulation through stimulation of TSH.'),
('f2063974-9291-5908-883f-cc204ac1d921'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'CRH (corticotropin-releasing hormone) stimulates the release of which of the following?', '["GH resulting in decreased functional activity","TSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","FSH mediated by specific receptor activation","LH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","ACTH (and POMC products)"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH (and POMC products)', 'CRH stimulates anterior pituitary corticotrophs to release ACTH and other POMC-derived peptides. ACTH then promotes cortisol secretion from the adrenal cortex.'),
('155670d0-1dbb-5d38-9f8b-1fa9d043e09f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hypothalamic hormone stimulates the release of both FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary?', '["GnRH (Gonadotropin-releasing hormone)","TRH (Thyrotropin-releasing hormone)","CRH (Corticotropin-releasing hormone)","GHRH (Growth hormone-releasing hormone)","Somatostatin"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH (Gonadotropin-releasing hormone)', 'GnRH from the hypothalamus stimulates gonadotrophs of the anterior pituitary to release both FSH and LH, which then regulate gonadal steroidogenesis and gametogenesis.'),
('45e6a38f-3b30-5095-a3b7-1267e46a7a89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 30-year-old male presents with short stature. Which of the following hormonal changes most likely accounts for this finding?', '["ACTH release","GH inhibition","Prolactin release","TSH release","GH release"]'::jsonb, 'GH inhibition', 'Short stature results from inadequate growth hormone action, which can follow reduced GH secretion or increased inhibition of its release. Diminished GH limits growth at the epiphyseal plates.'),
('4830c26d-812a-5d88-8153-00be0883104c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which two anterior pituitary hormones are stimulated by GHRH and TRH, respectively?', '["TSH only","GH and TSH","ACTH","GH only","FSH"]'::jsonb, 'GH and TSH', 'GHRH stimulates growth hormone release from somatotrophs, while TRH stimulates TSH release from thyrotrophs. Together, these two hypothalamic hormones promote GH and TSH secretion.'),
('25ee2808-d053-57f2-a4c2-e2bdbb2517ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 35-year-old female presents with galactorrhea and amenorrhea. Which of the following hormonal changes best explains this clinical picture?', '["Stimulate GH","Inhibit prolactin release","Stimulate prolactin","Stimulate ACTH","Inhibit TSH"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulate prolactin', 'Galactorrhea with amenorrhea indicates hyperprolactinemia. Excess prolactin drives milk secretion and suppresses gonadotropin release, disrupting the ovarian cycle and causing amenorrhea, most often due to a prolactin-secreting adenoma.'),
('b60dbcf4-9493-57a2-8b12-ee6d800dc1ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Prolactin releasing factors include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Dopamine","Serotonin","Estrogen","VIP","TRH"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine', 'Dopamine is the principal prolactin-inhibiting factor (PIF) released by the hypothalamus. TRH, VIP, serotonin, and estrogen all stimulate prolactin release.'),
('3f27b1bb-39dd-5faa-81d6-48aeb94e7e13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which anterior pituitary cell type synthesizes and secretes growth hormone?', '["Thyrotrophs resulting in decreased functional activity","Corticotrophs accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gonadotrophs mediated by specific receptor activation","Somatotrophs (acidophilic cells)","Lactotrophs leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Somatotrophs (acidophilic cells)', 'Somatotrophs are the acidophilic anterior pituitary cells that secrete growth hormone. Thyrotrophs, corticotrophs, gonadotrophs, and lactotrophs secrete TSH, ACTH, FSH/LH, and prolactin, respectively.'),
('81f76c5c-f6c5-5cae-8062-bf992550ba68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'TSH is secreted by which cell type of the anterior pituitary?', '["Lactotrophs","Corticotrophs","Thyrotrophs","Gonadotrophs","Somatotrophs"]'::jsonb, 'Thyrotrophs', 'Thyrotrophs are the anterior pituitary cells that synthesize and secrete TSH in response to TRH from the hypothalamus. TSH then stimulates the thyroid gland to produce and release thyroid hormones.'),
('b5255097-c4f7-5358-a6ea-5a4e679ad7ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'FSH and LH are secreted by which anterior pituitary cell type?', '["Thyrotrophs","Corticotrophs","Lactotrophs","Somatotrophs","Gonadotrophs"]'::jsonb, 'Gonadotrophs', 'Gonadotrophs are the anterior pituitary cells that secrete the gonadotropins FSH and LH. Their release is stimulated by hypothalamic GnRH.'),
('068da5a3-1a2b-5a1d-8526-17d056accefe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 55-year-old male presents with galactorrhea. Which anterior pituitary cell type is most likely responsible for the excess prolactin?', '["Lactotrophs (acidophilic)","Somatotrophs","Thyrotrophs","Corticotrophs","Gonadotrophs"]'::jsonb, 'Lactotrophs (acidophilic)', 'Lactotrophs are the acidophilic anterior pituitary cells that secrete prolactin. Excessive prolactin secretion from these cells, as in a prolactinoma, produces galactorrhea.'),
('0150847a-8083-5d50-8b99-a2e8bdd55aea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the intracellular signaling pathway activated by growth hormone (GH)?', '["Ion channel","GPCR","Nuclear receptor","JAK-STAT pathway (GH receptor)","Tyrosine kinase"]'::jsonb, 'JAK-STAT pathway (GH receptor)', 'GH binds its receptor, activating JAK2, which phosphorylates STAT proteins; the STAT dimers then translocate to the nucleus and regulate gene transcription.'),
('f330365a-0767-5b22-8944-42ced7e3dcb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) is mainly produced in:', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Liver (in response to GH)","Bone without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Liver (in response to GH)', 'GH stimulates the liver to synthesize and secrete IGF-1, which mediates most of the growth-promoting effects of GH.'),
('fcc9da4d-d32e-5a3c-9023-7b2cd157a8c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'IGF-1 exerts negative feedback on:', '["Anterior pituitary (inhibits GH)","Hypothalamus (inhibits GHRH)","Both hypothalamus and pituitary","Thyroid","Posterior pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Both hypothalamus and pituitary', 'IGF-1 inhibits GH secretion at the pituitary and suppresses GHRH release from the hypothalamus, completing the negative feedback loop.'),
('a9092075-1178-5ca1-bdd9-081f1fe85369'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which of the following is an action of insulin?', '["Increased lipolysis","Increased IGF-1 production","Increased glucose uptake (hypoglycemia)","Increased linear bone growth","Decreased protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased glucose uptake (hypoglycemia)', 'Insulin stimulates glucose uptake in muscle and adipose tissue by promoting GLUT4 translocation to the cell membrane, lowering blood glucose.'),
('1d838ab9-419a-5b8b-afa8-f5b9698452ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'GH increases blood glucose by:', '["Stimulating insulin","Decreasing glucose uptake (anti-insulin effect)","Increasing glucose uptake","Stimulating glycogenesis","Inhibiting gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Decreasing glucose uptake (anti-insulin effect)', 'GH opposes the action of insulin by reducing glucose uptake in peripheral tissues and promoting lipolysis, thereby raising blood glucose.'),
('fe11b74e-f350-51db-bd62-75a36c058fcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'GH deficiency in children causes:', '["Dwarfism (proportionate)","Gigantism","Acromegaly","Cushing syndrome","Achondroplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Dwarfism (proportionate)', 'Childhood GH deficiency slows growth of all bones proportionally, producing proportionate dwarfism, unlike achondroplasia, which predominantly affects the limbs.'),
('e642f5bb-2a0a-5986-9c24-d16dcaad0495'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'GH deficiency in adults causes:', '["Acromegaly","Increased bone density","Gigantism","Decreased bone density","Increased muscle mass"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased bone density', 'Adult GH deficiency reduces bone remodeling and muscle mass while increasing visceral fat, predisposing to reduced bone density and osteopenia.'),
('6785c078-6bd8-5390-8b79-6f9cafd72884'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'GH excess in children causes:', '["Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Precocious puberty","Cushing syndrome","Gigantism"]'::jsonb, 'Gigantism', 'Excess GH before epiphyseal closure causes proportionate overgrowth of long bones, producing gigantism.'),
('2c654702-de23-58df-a003-a64de127ab0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'GH excess in adults causes:', '["Gigantism","Cushing syndrome","Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Hyperthyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Acromegaly', 'GH excess after epiphyseal closure cannot increase height but causes acromegaly, characterized by growth of the hands, feet, and facial bones.'),
('02332538-4337-5dc0-a06b-6025183e45fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Acromegaly features include all EXCEPT:', '["Coarse facial features","Proportionate tall stature","Prognathism","Enlarged hands and feet","Organomegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Proportionate tall stature', 'Acromegaly develops after epiphyseal closure, so height does not increase; the other listed features reflect bony and soft-tissue overgrowth.'),
('72ec92e1-2799-5a62-88a1-949eb1e7c0e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Gigantism features include:', '["Prognathism resulting in decreased functional activity","Organomegaly only accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Enlarged hands only mediated by specific receptor activation","Tall stature (proportionate overgrowth before puberty)","Obesity leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tall stature (proportionate overgrowth before puberty)', 'GH excess before puberty stimulates proportionate growth of all bones, resulting in tall stature rather than isolated organ or acral enlargement.'),
('306fa280-4ad0-51ca-bdac-439f9a7fbb96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hyperglycemia causes:', '["Increased IGF-1","Decreased GH (inhibition)","Increased GHRH","Increased prolactin secretion","Increased GH"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased GH (inhibition)', 'Elevated blood glucose inhibits GH secretion and suppresses GHRH, whereas hypoglycemia stimulates GH release.'),
('5253417c-d94c-5bd9-9265-466a7fd074e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'ADH (vasopressin) is primarily synthesized in:', '["Paraventricular nucleus (PVN)","Arcuate nucleus","Preoptic area","Supraoptic nucleus (SON)","Ventromedial nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Supraoptic nucleus (SON)', 'ADH is synthesized mainly in the supraoptic nucleus of the hypothalamus, while oxytocin predominates in the paraventricular nucleus; both are transported to the posterior pituitary.'),
('c9467bc0-9ff8-54ae-ba7b-84a563c9503f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Oxytocin is primarily synthesized in:', '["Supraoptic nucleus (SON)","Mammillary body","Preoptic area","Arcuate nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus (PVN) and SON"]'::jsonb, 'Paraventricular nucleus (PVN) and SON', 'Oxytocin is synthesized chiefly in the paraventricular nucleus (with some in the supraoptic nucleus) of the hypothalamus and released from the posterior pituitary.'),
('e3b59171-c697-57a9-bca2-277c38606837'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 60-year-old male presents with polyuria and polydipsia. What is the underlying mechanism of this condition?', '["Water reabsorption in collecting duct","Sweat production","Milk ejection","Uterine contraction","Vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Water reabsorption in collecting duct', 'Polyuria and polydipsia result from impaired ADH-mediated water reabsorption in the collecting duct, as occurs in diabetes insipidus.'),
('b33e0391-8302-5c8f-88e8-5bf371f30633'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'ADH acts on which receptor in the kidney?', '["V1a receptor","V3 receptor","V2 receptor","V4 receptor","V1b receptor"]'::jsonb, 'V2 receptor', 'In the kidney, ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct cells, activating the cAMP pathway and inserting aquaporin-2 channels to reabsorb water.'),
('4eca059b-639d-5767-9287-6841947337ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'V1 receptors for ADH mediate:', '["Vasodilation (relaxation of vascular smooth muscle)","Water reabsorption","Vasoconstriction (smooth muscle contraction)","Uterine contraction","Milk ejection"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction (smooth muscle contraction)', 'V1 receptors couple to the IP3/calcium pathway in vascular smooth muscle, producing vasoconstriction, whereas V2 receptors mediate water reabsorption.'),
('f6ec8a39-644c-5abd-894a-37fb973ea339'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'ADH secretion is stimulated by:', '["Increased plasma osmolality","Decreased plasma osmolality","Hypoxia","Increased blood pressure","Both increased osmolality and hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Both increased osmolality and hypotension', 'ADH release is increased by rising plasma osmolality (detected by osmoreceptors) and by hypotension or hypovolemia (detected by baroreceptors).'),
('b8d7e301-1869-5fd0-8d93-3458f45aef68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 35-year-old female presents with polyuria and polydipsia. What is the most likely site of pathology?', '["Kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothalamus (OVLT and subfornical organ)","Carotid body mediated by specific receptor activation","Posterior pituitary without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Aortic arch leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus (OVLT and subfornical organ)', 'The osmoreceptors in the circumventricular organs (OVLT and subfornical organ) of the hypothalamus regulate thirst and ADH release; their dysfunction impairs water balance.'),
('c5a57fa2-2d33-5c2f-bbff-693de642da15'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thirst is primarily stimulated by:', '["Angiotensin II","Increased plasma osmolality","Decreased plasma osmolality","Increased plasma volume","Both increased osmolality and angiotensin II"]'::jsonb, 'Both increased osmolality and angiotensin II', 'Thirst is stimulated by increased plasma osmolality acting on hypothalamic osmoreceptors and by angiotensin II, which acts on the subfornical organ.'),
('6aebf21a-0472-5f8c-a1cc-650730e874e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate ADH) causes:', '["Polyuria resulting in decreased functional activity","Alkalosis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hypotension mediated by specific receptor activation","Hyponatremia and concentrated urine","Hypernatremia leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia and concentrated urine', 'In SIADH, excess ADH causes water retention with dilutional hyponatremia while urine remains concentrated (high urine osmolality).'),
('a8c314e1-969f-5d3c-97a4-a579e4f9cfcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('4537232f-64f7-5a5d-9202-fe37ce414c3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('98e5f495-ab05-54bb-be9b-b2abe5528a5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('4aac0180-a737-5541-933b-6243515d79c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hormone is primarily responsible for stimulating milk production (lactogenesis)?', '["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Progesterone","Human chorionic somatomammotropin"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin', 'Prolactin from the anterior pituitary stimulates mammary alveolar cells to synthesize milk; oxytocin later causes milk ejection.'),
('73140f73-9b02-5326-9580-6c719e0aab0b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Oxytocin secretion is stimulated by:', '["Stress","Suckling (neuroendocrine reflex)","Increased osmolality","Hypotension","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Suckling (neuroendocrine reflex)', 'Suckling triggers a neuroendocrine reflex that releases oxytocin from the posterior pituitary to promote milk ejection.'),
('a5198ebf-2141-52f7-bd72-3fb8c60a7659'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Milk ejection reflex involves:', '["Prolactin -> milk synthesis","Estrogen -> ductal growth","Oxytocin -> myoepithelial cell contraction","Progesterone -> alveolar growth","GH -> growth"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin -> myoepithelial cell contraction', 'Suckling releases oxytocin, which contracts mammary myoepithelial cells, expelling milk from the alveoli into the ducts.'),
('44cc6270-dcd8-585c-a170-a051a73aae7e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which of the following is most characteristic of the sympathetic ''fight-or-flight'' state?', '["Warmth (peripheral vasodilation)","Digestion","Sleep","Feeding","Stress and sympathetic activation"]'::jsonb, 'Stress and sympathetic activation', 'Acute stress triggers sympathetic activation with catecholamine release, increasing heart rate and blood pressure while inhibiting digestion; the other options reflect resting or parasympathetic states.'),
('82b1a5ef-9a0f-5e18-a053-99cf4e3eb213'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) are formed by iodination of which residues within thyroglobulin?', '["Methionine resulting in decreased functional activity","Tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin","Cholesterol mediated by specific receptor activation","Phenylalanine without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Tryptophan leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine residues of thyroglobulin', 'Thyroglobulin contains tyrosine residues that are iodinated and coupled to form T3 and T4.'),
('d076d081-60ec-538c-b406-bff0d937f66a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'T3 is more potent than T4 because:', '["Higher affinity for thyroid hormone receptor","Higher concentration","More protein binding","Longer half-life","Higher secretion rate"]'::jsonb, 'Higher affinity for thyroid hormone receptor', 'T3 binds the nuclear thyroid hormone receptor with greater affinity than T4, producing a more potent biological effect despite lower circulating levels.'),
('fd093b37-c5a5-5974-a435-45116cc475f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroid hormone synthesis requires:', '["Iodine and thyroglobulin","Iron and zinc","Cholesterol and LDL","Calcium and phosphate","Tyrosine and tryptophan"]'::jsonb, 'Iodine and thyroglobulin', 'Thyroid hormone synthesis requires iodide, which is trapped and oxidized, and thyroglobulin, which provides the tyrosine residues that are iodinated.'),
('e4bacd10-5c5e-5f1b-947a-67e81412dffb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Iodide is trapped in the thyroid via:', '["Simple diffusion","Iodide channels","Na+/I- symporter (NIS)","Facilitated diffusion","Active transport by ATPase"]'::jsonb, 'Na+/I- symporter (NIS)', 'The Na+/I- symporter (NIS) on the basolateral membrane of follicular cells actively transports iodide into the thyroid against its concentration gradient.'),
('6aa82991-1959-5c59-9c6e-f7b8fdd84a64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroglobulin is synthesized in:', '["Hypothalamus resulting in decreased functional activity","Liver accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pituitary mediated by specific receptor activation","Parafollicular cells without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid follicular cells (released into colloid)"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid follicular cells (released into colloid)', 'Thyroglobulin is produced by thyroid follicular cells and secreted into the colloid, where iodination and coupling produce the thyroid hormones.'),
('2ffcb2eb-769f-5933-87ad-82360070b5d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the thyroid gland, which step releases stored T4 and T3 for secretion into the blood?', '["Coupling of MIT and DIT","Oxidation of iodide","Formation of T3 and T4","Release of T4 from thyroglobulin","Iodination of tyrosine (organification)"]'::jsonb, 'Release of T4 from thyroglobulin', 'Thyroid hormones are stored in the colloid bound to thyroglobulin; endocytosis and proteolysis release T4 (and T3) into the circulation.'),
('de631c3d-b4b7-5f01-ba65-cfeb206f973f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'MIT and DIT stand for:', '["Reverse T3 resulting in decreased functional activity","Triiodothyronine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thyroxine mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroglobulin without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Monoiodotyrosine and diiodotyrosine"]'::jsonb, 'Monoiodotyrosine and diiodotyrosine', 'MIT (monoiodotyrosine) and DIT (diiodotyrosine) are the iodinated tyrosine intermediates that couple to form T3 and T4.'),
('00e35f01-05ca-5c41-8d88-21ef59e12f5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Coupling of two DIT molecules forms:', '["T4 (thyroxine)","rT3","T3","MIT","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 'T4 (thyroxine)', 'Two diiodotyrosine (DIT) residues couple to form tetraiodothyronine (T4), while MIT and DIT combine to form T3.'),
('5fda3539-eea3-5f63-bf2f-bb2306c49222'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Coupling of one MIT and one DIT forms:', '["None of the above","rT3","T4","T3 (triiodothyronine)","DIT"]'::jsonb, 'T3 (triiodothyronine)', 'Condensation of one MIT with one DIT yields triiodothyronine (T3); the combination of two DIT residues yields T4.'),
('cf03cd06-9eb1-5e93-9c97-5869873db88f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the thyroid gland, where are thyroid hormones stored before release?', '["Secretory vesicles resulting in decreased functional activity","Cytoplasm accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Colloid (extracellular) as part of thyroglobulin","Nucleus without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Blood leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Colloid (extracellular) as part of thyroglobulin', 'Thyroid hormones are stored extracellularly in the follicular lumen (colloid) attached to thyroglobulin until proteolysis releases them.'),
('5ccebbe5-3236-56f7-ac68-4e8139f2898d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroid hormone release is stimulated by:', '["Iodine resulting in decreased functional activity","TRH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TSH (cAMP pathway)","T4 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T3 leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'TSH (cAMP pathway)', 'TSH binds its receptor on follicular cells and, via the cAMP pathway, stimulates endocytosis of colloid and release of T4 and T3.'),
('c1f323fb-c4b7-5f22-85f3-8d13a8d429ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroid hormones are transported in blood bound to:', '["All of the above","Albumin","Transthyretin","Thyroid-binding globulin (TBG)","Only TBG"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Circulating T4 and T3 bind chiefly to thyroid-binding globulin (TBG) and also to transthyretin and albumin.'),
('01429b4c-3b0d-5bb7-9049-5a2eb99f6578'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The half-life of T4 is approximately:', '["6-8 hours","3 months","7 days","1 day","30 days"]'::jsonb, '7 days', 'T4 has a half-life of about 7 days due to its strong protein binding, whereas T3 has a shorter half-life of about 1-2 days.'),
('45f9812e-28dd-54fe-9d67-a2a068a1d857'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The half-life of T3 is approximately:', '["6-8 hours","7 days","3 months","30 days","1 day"]'::jsonb, '1 day', 'T3 has a half-life of about 1 day, whereas T4 persists for about 7 days; the shorter half-life of T3 reflects its lower affinity for binding proteins.'),
('df7dc258-5690-51c6-a8f0-2d3d2286cbb5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroid hormone actions include all EXCEPT:', '["Increased thermogenesis","Increased protein catabolism","Increased heart rate and contractility","Increased basal metabolic rate","Decreased oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased oxygen consumption', 'Thyroid hormone increases oxygen consumption in most tissues, so decreased oxygen consumption is not one of its actions; the other listed effects are all produced by thyroid hormone.'),
('c0906f12-37f6-5acf-a824-cd37908ec77b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('f01b1242-d74d-5b17-842b-1ddfa711de94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 35-year-old male presents with palpitations, weight loss, and heat intolerance. What cardiovascular finding is expected in this condition?', '["Bradycardia resulting in decreased functional activity","Tachycardia and increased contractility","Decreased cardiac output mediated by specific receptor activation","Arrhythmias only without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Heart block leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia and increased contractility', 'Hyperthyroidism sensitizes the heart to catecholamines, producing tachycardia and increased myocardial contractility; bradycardia and heart block are features of hypothyroidism.'),
('05f9495d-82f6-578c-9e45-83ff4178eb81'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hyperthyroidism features include all EXCEPT:', '["Exophthalmos","Weight loss","Tachycardia","Bradycardia","Heat intolerance"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia', 'Hyperthyroidism causes tachycardia, not bradycardia; exophthalmos, weight loss, and heat intolerance are characteristic hyperthyroid features.'),
('d3843444-fe9d-5acf-adce-065cff5510a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the pathogenic antibody in Graves'' disease?', '["TSI (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)","TSH-blocking antibodies","Anti-TPO antibodies","Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies","Anti-GAD antibodies"]'::jsonb, 'TSI (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin)', 'In Graves disease, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin (TSI) binds to and activates the TSH receptor, driving unregulated thyroid hormone production.'),
('efe7290b-17d8-51bc-b663-b1c75cb8b1b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the mechanism of Graves'' ophthalmopathy?', '["Fibroblast activation and glycosaminoglycan deposition in orbit","TSH excess accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iodine excess mediated by specific receptor activation","T4 excess without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T3 excess leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fibroblast activation and glycosaminoglycan deposition in orbit', 'Graves ophthalmopathy results from fibroblast activation and glycosaminoglycan deposition within the orbit, causing proptosis, extraocular muscle enlargement, and potentially vision loss.'),
('45846ee0-5d05-538d-9e26-74c02505c63d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyrotoxic crisis (thyroid storm) is typically precipitated by:', '["TSH excess","Exercise","Low dietary iodine intake","Parathyroid surgery in a euthyroid patient","Stress"]'::jsonb, 'Stress', 'Thyroid storm is typically precipitated by infection, surgery, trauma, iodine load, or stress in a patient with uncontrolled hyperthyroidism.'),
('00da92a3-8dfa-5061-90f3-74e7d3999eb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hypothyroidism features include all EXCEPT:', '["Constipation","Tachycardia","Fatigue","Weight gain","Cold intolerance"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia', 'Hypothyroidism causes bradycardia, not tachycardia; constipation, fatigue, weight gain, and cold intolerance are characteristic hypothyroid features.'),
('e1f98651-c210-523e-abda-1c4a180bd180'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Congenital hypothyroidism causes:', '["Cretinism (mental retardation + dwarfism)","Graves disease accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Myxedema mediated by specific receptor activation","Hashimoto without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Goiter leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Cretinism (mental retardation + dwarfism)', 'Untreated congenital hypothyroidism causes cretinism, characterized by intellectual disability and impaired growth (dwarfism).'),
('50fad804-00fc-5ae1-b374-ec565bb94842'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Myxedema is a feature of:', '["Addison disease","Hyperthyroidism","Cushing syndrome","Severe hypothyroidism","Acromegaly"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hypothyroidism', 'Myxedema refers to the mucinous, non-pitting edema seen in severe, long-standing hypothyroidism.'),
('3aa96f4d-4947-5f3f-acba-a7cd8ecf5e8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Myxedema coma is best described as:', '["Adrenal crisis","Life-threatening hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism crisis","Thyroid storm","Pituitary apoplexy"]'::jsonb, 'Life-threatening hypothyroidism', 'Myxedema coma is a life-threatening complication of severe hypothyroidism characterized by hypothermia, bradycardia, hypoventilation, and altered mental status.'),
('45fcfb81-d69f-5801-9483-01dd96659233'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'TSH secretion is inhibited by:', '["TRH resulting in decreased functional activity","ACTH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Gonadotropins mediated by specific receptor activation","Both T3/T4 and somatostatin/dopamine","Growth hormone leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both T3/T4 and somatostatin/dopamine', 'TSH secretion is suppressed by thyroid hormones via negative feedback and by somatostatin and dopamine; TRH, in contrast, stimulates TSH release.'),
('3ed11254-0638-510d-a451-7565805773f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('07a12039-05c5-5f3e-bddd-7f437d1a3e14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Why does iodine deficiency cause goiter?', '["Graves disease resulting in decreased functional activity","Myxedema accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperthyroidism mediated by specific receptor activation","Thyroid cancer without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Goiter (increased TSH due to low T3/T4)"]'::jsonb, 'Goiter (increased TSH due to low T3/T4)', 'Iodine deficiency lowers T3/T4 production, removing negative feedback on the pituitary; the resulting rise in TSH drives thyroid hypertrophy (goiter).'),
('ab1c770a-c12d-5a48-b6ac-b9bce44dfdea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The Wolff-Chaikoff effect is:', '["Decrease in TBG resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase in T4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Block of TSH mediated by specific receptor activation","Stimulation by iodide without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Inhibition of thyroid hormone synthesis by excess iodide"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of thyroid hormone synthesis by excess iodide', 'The Wolff-Chaikoff effect is the inhibition of thyroid hormone synthesis and organification by excess iodide, a protective autoregulatory mechanism.'),
('df1e511f-e6d2-5cd2-93cb-f8b4363e9f35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which agents inhibit iodide uptake by the thyroid gland?', '["TSH resulting in decreased functional activity","T4 accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Iodine mediated by specific receptor activation","T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Perchlorate and thiocyanate"]'::jsonb, 'Perchlorate and thiocyanate', 'Perchlorate and thiocyanate competitively inhibit the sodium-iodide symporter, blocking active iodide uptake by the thyroid gland.'),
('46e1d62a-8ef4-57e0-81f9-4dead6132760'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Radioactive iodine (I-131) is used to treat:', '["Hashimoto resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroid cancer accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Hyperthyroidism (Graves) mediated by specific receptor activation","Both hyperthyroidism and thyroid cancer","Hypothyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Both hyperthyroidism and thyroid cancer', 'I-131 is concentrated by thyroid tissue and emits beta radiation that destroys follicular cells, treating both hyperthyroidism and differentiated thyroid cancer.'),
('d8e4d048-b89a-587d-8411-40e6bac33681'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Calcitonin is produced by:', '["Thyroid peroxidase","Parathyroid chief cells","Parathyroid oxyphil cells","Parafollicular (C) cells of thyroid","Thyroid follicular cells"]'::jsonb, 'Parafollicular (C) cells of thyroid', 'Calcitonin is secreted by the parafollicular C cells of the thyroid gland, which derive from the ultimobranchial body.'),
('e96e5cf8-e6a6-5064-81bc-4951bb5071e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Calcitonin acts to:', '["Increase blood calcium resulting in decreased functional activity","Decrease vitamin D accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decrease blood calcium (inhibits osteoclasts)","Increase phosphate without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Increase PTH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease blood calcium (inhibits osteoclasts)', 'Calcitonin lowers blood calcium primarily by inhibiting osteoclast-mediated bone resorption.'),
('c32825f6-57b4-5b80-b50c-c2f82a591222'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The primary function of calcitonin in humans is:', '["Vitamin D activation","Minor role (physiologic significance small)","Major calcium regulator","Phosphate regulation","Magnesium regulation"]'::jsonb, 'Minor role (physiologic significance small)', 'In humans, calcitonin has little physiologic significance in calcium homeostasis, and its deficiency does not produce clinical disease.'),
('38c03ec8-2a8d-55d0-affd-0f71ec164e92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'TSH acts on thyroid via:', '["Tyrosine kinase","JAK-STAT","Guanylyl cyclase","GPCR -> Gs -> cAMP pathway","Phospholipase C"]'::jsonb, 'GPCR -> Gs -> cAMP pathway', 'The TSH receptor is aG protein-coupled receptor coupled to Gs, which activates adenylyl cyclase and the cAMP pathway in thyroid follicular cells.'),
('f05365a9-869e-57c4-b830-2de48433b1d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The thyroid gland develops from which embryological structure?', '["Endoderm of gut","Foramen cecum (base of tongue)","Neural crest","Mesoderm","Branchial arches"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen cecum (base of tongue)', 'The thyroid gland originates as an endodermal diverticulum at the foramen cecum at the base of the tongue and descends into the neck.'),
('3e8f16f9-c44b-5db0-9cfb-9c6fdf3ae47e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A cyst found at the midline of the neck that moves with swallowing is most likely:', '["Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline neck)","Parathyroid cyst","Branchial cleft cyst","Lymph node","Thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroglossal duct cyst (midline neck)', 'A midline neck mass that moves with swallowing or tongue protrusion is a thyroglossal duct cyst, the most common congenital neck mass.'),
('5ac3b18f-3120-5e49-987f-69a78beb2f84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The parathyroid glands are derived from which embryological tissue?', '["Thyroid follicular cells","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Endoderm","Neural crest"]'::jsonb, 'Endoderm', 'The parathyroid glands derive from the endoderm of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches; neural crest cells mainly contribute connective tissue and thyroid C cells.'),
('e99dc367-11b5-5ecf-a1be-cbd3849b6531'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('e3a5a2d0-4732-5137-993d-3a2d760cb0a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroid hormone is essential for which of the following?', '["Reproduction only","Digestion","Muscle growth only","Bone growth only","Fetal brain development"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal brain development', 'Thyroid hormone is indispensable for fetal and early postnatal brain development; deficiency during this period causes irreversible intellectual disability.'),
('6ccb99ed-7f32-5504-a980-b6a8172f9ba1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'T3 exerts its actions primarily through which type of receptor?', '["GPCRs resulting in decreased functional activity","Cytoplasmic receptors accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Tyrosine kinases mediated by specific receptor activation","Nuclear receptors (bind T3 with high affinity)","Membrane receptors leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear receptors (bind T3 with high affinity)', 'T3 binds nuclear thyroid hormone receptors with high affinity and regulates gene transcription, which underlies most thyroid hormone actions.'),
('2f2a029a-d5cf-57e6-a7a4-1fb026e9f306'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The thyroid hormone receptor regulates gene transcription by which mechanism?', '["Opens ion channels","Forms heterodimer with RXR and binds TRE","Forms homodimer only","Activates adenylyl cyclase","Binds as monomer"]'::jsonb, 'Forms heterodimer with RXR and binds TRE', 'Thyroid hormone receptors form heterodimers with RXR and bind thyroid hormone response elements (TREs) to modulate gene expression.'),
('ab855242-0e07-579d-b099-6473d5a8d0d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Thyroid hormone increases:', '["Beta-adrenergic receptor number","None of the above","All of the above","Na+/K+ ATPase activity","Mitochondrial enzyme activity"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Thyroid hormone increases beta-adrenergic receptor number, Na+/K+-ATPase activity, and mitochondrial enzyme activity, all of which raise metabolic rate.'),
('bf7ec605-f389-57c4-b748-366546d0ea83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 is via:', '["5-deiodinase type 1 and 2","Monoamine oxidase","5-deiodinase type 3","Thyroid peroxidase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase"]'::jsonb, '5-deiodinase type 1 and 2', 'Type 1 and type 2 iodothyronine deiodinases convert T4 to T3 in peripheral tissues; type 3 deiodinase instead inactivates T4 to reverse T3.'),
('f2bb6552-1f75-5962-8f17-222cd637932c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Reverse T3 (rT3) is best described as:', '["Same as T3 resulting in decreased functional activity","The major thyroid hormone accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Inactive metabolite (produced by D3 deiodinase)","More potent than T3 without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Active and potent leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Inactive metabolite (produced by D3 deiodinase)', 'Reverse T3 is an inactive metabolite of T4 formed by type 3 deiodinase, which removes iodine from the inner ring rather than the outer ring.'),
('9c608546-9be6-5139-a71f-d07881f781a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 55-year-old male with a chronic illness presents with fatigue and cold intolerance. Labs show low T3 with normal TSH. This pattern is most consistent with:', '["True hypothyroidism resulting in decreased functional activity","Thyroiditis accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Low T3 with normal TSH in non-thyroidal illness","TSH deficiency without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Hyperthyroidism leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Low T3 with normal TSH in non-thyroidal illness', 'In non-thyroidal illness (euthyroid sick syndrome), T4-to-T3 conversion is impaired, giving low T3 with normal TSH; true hypothyroidism would show a raised TSH.'),
('d0fa5aa7-9b7d-5842-9e3b-c22a3eb322b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'T3 and T4 regulate their own secretion by negative feedback at which sites?', '["TSH resulting in decreased functional activity","Iodine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Calcitonin mediated by specific receptor activation","TRH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pituitary and hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary and hypothalamus', 'T3 and T4 suppress TRH release from the hypothalamus and TSH release from the anterior pituitary, forming the negative feedback loop that regulates thyroid hormone levels.'),
('58bfd081-947d-57ca-b0d0-1a48093f616e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Amiodarone can cause:', '["Thyroiditis","Hyperthyroidism","Hypothyroidism","Both hyper- and hypothyroidism","No significant effect"]'::jsonb, 'Both hyper- and hypothyroidism', 'Amiodarone is iodine-rich and can cause both hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism; hypothyroidism is more common in iodine-replete regions.'),
('3ca7c37c-e4d1-5a76-818d-1dd298d769f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Lithium can cause:', '["Hyperthyroidism","Thyroid storm","No significant effect","Hypothyroidism","Goiter only"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothyroidism', 'Lithium inhibits thyroid hormone release and organification, commonly causing hypothyroidism.'),
('0b83f321-ddb4-5053-af2e-c2db5a9e2013'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Selenium is required for:', '["Thyroid hormone synthesis","Deiodinase enzyme function","TRH synthesis","Iodide trapping","TSH synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Deiodinase enzyme function', 'Selenium is a cofactor for the iodothyronine deiodinases, the enzymes that activate and inactivate thyroid hormones.'),
('5b4fa79f-8f2e-5163-99cb-c2c8ef7d5e7e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The TSH receptor is located on:', '["Parathyroid","Anterior pituitary thyrotrophs","Thyroid follicular cell membrane","Hypothalamic neurons","Thyroid C-cells"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid follicular cell membrane', 'The TSH receptor is located on the basolateral membrane of thyroid follicular cells and is aG protein-coupled receptor coupled to Gs.'),
('ef9e5f6c-27ce-5a76-94c8-b43c97dae36d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'TSH stimulates all thyroid processes EXCEPT:', '["Calcitonin secretion","Thyroglobulin synthesis","Thyroid hormone release","Iodide organification","Iodide uptake"]'::jsonb, 'Calcitonin secretion', 'TSH stimulates iodide uptake, organification, thyroglobulin synthesis, and hormone release by follicular cells, but it does not stimulate C cells to secrete calcitonin.'),
('c98fa403-12ac-56bd-8940-c37067562fdb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What class of hormones does insulin belong to?', '["Tripeptide","Hexapeptide","Polypeptide","Steroid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Polypeptide', 'Insulin is a polypeptide hormone composed of A and B chains joined by disulfide bonds.'),
('e451c539-e5f7-5265-baa1-bfa4a5f974b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'GnRH is a:', '["Steroid","Glycoprotein","Hexapeptide","Decapeptide","Tripeptide"]'::jsonb, 'Decapeptide', 'GnRH is a decapeptide secreted by the hypothalamus that stimulates the release of LH and FSH from the anterior pituitary.'),
('7d278c0d-8b02-571e-ada4-0d6b4a5a7513'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which class of hormones requires cell surface receptors for signal transduction?', '["Steroid hormones","Nucleotides","Eicosanoids","Amines","Peptide hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Peptide hormones', 'Peptide/protein hormones are hydrophilic and cannot cross the plasma membrane, so they act through cell surface receptors such as GPCRs and receptor tyrosine kinases. Steroid hormones, by contrast, diffuse through the membrane to bind intracellular receptors.'),
('f6e30a2b-d958-594d-9ced-f4951e338e90'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Somatostatin exists in:', '["50 aa form","100 aa form","10 aa form","Single 41 aa form","14 aa and 28 aa forms"]'::jsonb, '14 aa and 28 aa forms', 'Somatostatin is cleaved from preprosomatostatin into two active forms: a 14-amino-acid peptide (SS-14) and a 28-amino-acid form (SS-28). Both inhibit growth hormone, TSH, insulin, and glucagon secretion.'),
('32fda15c-f3f3-5c16-82b8-5bec5fd43c0a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Cleavage of POMC (pro-opiomelanocortin) in the anterior pituitary produces primarily:', '["ACTH","MSH","TSH","GH","beta-endorphin"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH', 'In anterior pituitary corticotrophs, prohormone convertase 1 cleaves POMC predominantly into ACTH and beta-lipotropin. MSH predominates in the intermediate lobe, which is essentially absent in adults.'),
('c13fcbee-e402-59d0-9017-181e40b188b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'MSH (melanocyte-stimulating hormone) from POMC causes:', '["Increased metabolism","Increased pigmentation","Increased lipolysis","Decreased pigmentation","Decreased appetite"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pigmentation', 'MSH activates melanocortin-1 receptors on melanocytes, increasing melanin synthesis and skin pigmentation. This also explains the hyperpigmentation seen when ACTH levels are high, as in Addison disease.'),
('3e2e1182-c3f2-5b82-9ad0-9e9642a3fc75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('86fa4efc-0924-550f-9389-68308c8428c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Anterior pituitary hormones are all:', '["Steroids","Amines","Peptides/proteins","Nucleotides","Eicosanoids"]'::jsonb, 'Peptides/proteins', 'Every anterior pituitary hormone—GH, prolactin, ACTH, TSH, FSH, and LH—is a peptide or protein hormone. Because they cannot cross the cell membrane, they signal through cell surface receptors.'),
('e7e275b4-885b-5e44-b9a0-d64f3c9e40e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which of the following is the molecular structure of human growth hormone?', '["Single chain polypeptide (191 aa)","Dipeptide accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Disulfide-linked dimer mediated by specific receptor activation","Glycoprotein without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Steroid leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Single chain polypeptide (191 aa)', 'Human growth hormone is a single-chain, non-glycosylated polypeptide of 191 amino acids secreted by somatotrophs. It is structurally homologous to prolactin and human placental lactogen.'),
('6b03f672-35e5-5bfe-9c03-c649d70c75e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 30-year-old male presents with galactorrhea. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["ACTH resulting in decreased functional activity","FSH accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","TSH mediated by specific receptor activation","LH without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Prolactin excess (hyperprolactinemia)"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin excess (hyperprolactinemia)', 'Galactorrhea in a man is most often due to hyperprolactinemia, typically from a prolactinoma or reduced hypothalamic dopamine. Excess prolactin stimulates milk production and suppresses the gonadal axis.'),
('e64dcffd-5588-5399-a67d-0ba626cbf81e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hypothalamic factor normally inhibits prolactin secretion?', '["VIP resulting in decreased functional activity","Serotonin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Dopamine from hypothalamus","Estrogen without affecting the primary tissue pathway","TRH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine from hypothalamus', 'Dopamine released by tuberoinfundibular neurons of the hypothalamus is the principal prolactin-inhibiting factor (PIF). Loss of this inhibition raises prolactin and can cause galactorrhea.'),
('59c058fc-4321-5548-8ef6-0bc31b409dca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hyperprolactinemia in a female most characteristically produces:', '["Increased fertility","Hypergonadism","Precocious puberty","Early menopause","Galactorrhea and hypogonadism"]'::jsonb, 'Galactorrhea and hypogonadism', 'Hyperprolactinemia stimulates lactogenesis, causing galactorrhea, while suppressing GnRH and gonadotropin secretion, which produces hypogonadism with amenorrhea and infertility.'),
('17a9841f-d0a4-5720-a636-179f387ecd64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hormone primarily stimulates milk synthesis (lactogenesis)?', '["Breast development at puberty","Milk synthesis (lactogenesis)","Sexual behavior","Uterine contraction","Milk ejection"]'::jsonb, 'Milk synthesis (lactogenesis)', 'Prolactin acts on mammary alveolar cells to stimulate milk synthesis and secretion. Milk ejection is instead driven by oxytocin triggered by suckling.'),
('499e83cf-cada-59ec-a652-1b123f6e428b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'LH in females causes:', '["Estrogen production","FSH release","Follicular growth","Prolactin release","Ovulation and luteinization"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation and luteinization', 'The LH surge triggers rupture of the mature follicle and ovulation, and the remnant is luteinized into the corpus luteum, which secretes progesterone.'),
('da982021-bacd-561c-8641-7b9fc545333d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'FSH in males stimulates:', '["Erection","Semen production","Testosterone production","Leydig cell function","Spermatogenesis (via Sertoli cells)"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogenesis (via Sertoli cells)', 'FSH binds receptors on Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis and stimulate secretion of androgen-binding protein and inhibin. It does not directly stimulate Leydig cells.'),
('b63c108d-0db5-56e8-bc7d-f5c0f9dc0455'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which cells in the testes produce testosterone?', '["Spermatogenesis","Leydig cells","Semen production","Sertoli cells","Erection"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cells', 'Leydig (interstitial) cells synthesize and secrete testosterone under the control of LH. Testosterone then supports spermatogenesis within the seminiferous tubules.'),
('98ed249c-e843-5543-90e3-fe1b7aa925db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Androgen-binding protein (ABP) is produced by:', '["Sertoli cells (under FSH)","Spermatogonia","Leydig cells","Epididymis","Prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells (under FSH)', 'Androgen-binding protein is produced by Sertoli cells in response to FSH. It binds testosterone and concentrates it within the seminiferous tubules for spermatogenesis.'),
('147d4909-b72e-5b45-8689-ae2fe261ed3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Inhibin is produced by:', '["Pituitary resulting in decreased functional activity","Leydig cells accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Theca cells mediated by specific receptor activation","Sertoli cells (males) and granulosa cells (females)","Hypothalamus leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells (males) and granulosa cells (females)', 'Inhibin, which selectively suppresses FSH secretion by negative feedback, is made by Sertoli cells in males and granulosa cells in females.'),
('2cb2c666-15e3-5814-bd8f-5fc11e304306'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Activin stimulates:', '["GH secretion","LH secretion","TSH secretion","Prolactin","FSH secretion"]'::jsonb, 'FSH secretion', 'Activin is produced in the pituitary and gonads and acts on gonadotrophs to increase FSH synthesis and secretion. Inhibin and follistatin oppose this action.'),
('816b599f-04e9-596e-8d2a-9f436cc455e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The arcuate nucleus produces:', '["Dopamine","GnRH","GHRH","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The arcuate nucleus contains tuberoinfundibular dopamine neurons (PIF), GHRH neurons, and GnRH neurons. It therefore contributes to control of prolactin, GH, and gonadotropins.'),
('386d6874-2b19-5111-a7b7-41246ab1c574'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hypothalamic hormone inhibits growth hormone (GH) release from the anterior pituitary?', '["GHRH","Somatostatin","TRH","Dopamine","GnRH"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Somatostatin, also called growth hormone-inhibiting hormone, is released by periventricular hypothalamic neurons and suppresses GH secretion. GHRH provides the opposing stimulatory signal.'),
('f4c58fc9-cac4-5cb6-bb62-035ea58d16ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hypothalamic nucleus is primarily involved in regulating hunger and satiety?', '["Arcuate nucleus","Suprachiasmatic nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus","Ventromedial nucleus","Lateral hypothalamic area"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate nucleus', 'The arcuate nucleus integrates appetite signals, with NPY/AgRP neurons promoting hunger and POMC/CART neurons promoting satiety. It is thus central to energy balance.'),
('6f625ebe-31d3-5d0f-b0ab-0fe8da9b50d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hypothalamic area is classically known as the hunger (feeding) center?', '["Lateral hypothalamic area","Ventromedial nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus","Preoptic area"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral hypothalamic area', 'The lateral hypothalamic area is classically the feeding (hunger) center; its stimulation drives eating and its destruction causes anorexia. The ventromedial nucleus is the satiety center.'),
('6c48c2ba-4b1b-5678-ba29-70a0b42b7ea5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The preoptic area contains:', '["Feeding center resulting in decreased functional activity","ADH neurons accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Thermoregulatory center (heat loss)","Satiety center without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GnRH neurons leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Thermoregulatory center (heat loss)', 'The preoptic/anterior hypothalamic area contains the heat-loss center, which promotes sweating and cutaneous vasodilation to dissipate heat. The posterior hypothalamus mediates heat conservation.'),
('7f287047-e51f-52e7-a1d0-6177a2bd74c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which brain structure serves as the master circadian clock?', '["Medulla resulting in decreased functional activity","Thalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pineal gland mediated by specific receptor activation","Hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)","Pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN)', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus receives direct retinal input through the retinohypothalamic tract and coordinates circadian rhythms across the body, making it the master clock.'),
('67272d8f-2a74-5afa-99b5-f64ae508cae8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Melatonin is secreted by:', '["Thyroid resulting in decreased functional activity","Hypothalamus accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Pineal gland (from tryptophan/serotonin)","Adrenal without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Pituitary leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Pineal gland (from tryptophan/serotonin)', 'Pinealocytes synthesize melatonin from tryptophan via serotonin. Secretion rises in darkness and peaks at night to entrain the sleep-wake cycle.'),
('7f41757c-db99-546f-865b-c07f2354f344'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Melatonin secretion is characteristically:', '["High during day","High during night","Low during day","Constant","Low during night"]'::jsonb, 'High during night', 'Melatonin secretion is suppressed by light and reaches its maximum during darkness at night. This nocturnal peak helps set the circadian sleep-wake rhythm.'),
('b59e3d82-5073-5f07-aaea-18fcb4d6815b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The pineal gland is located:', '["On the kidney resulting in decreased functional activity","In the epithalamus (posterior to third ventricle)","In the hypothalamus mediated by specific receptor activation","In the pituitary fossa without affecting the primary tissue pathway","At the base of brain leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'In the epithalamus (posterior to third ventricle)', 'The pineal gland sits in the epithalamus, attached to the posterior wall of the third ventricle. It lies behind (dorsal to) the superior colliculi.'),
('c1dff9a2-b9f2-5d4b-84a1-a3bd9596a5c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Plasma cortisol concentration is normally highest at:', '["Midnight","Noon","Evening (6 PM)","After meals","Early morning (6-8 AM)"]'::jsonb, 'Early morning (6-8 AM)', 'Cortisol shows a circadian rhythm with its peak in the early morning (about 6-8 AM) shortly before awakening, declining to a trough near midnight. This rhythm is lost in Cushing syndrome.'),
('f4c36a6e-9fd7-50e7-a528-21d22d879d91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Sleep stages affect GH:', '["GH peaks during slow-wave sleep (stage 3-4)","GH decreases in sleep accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","No change mediated by specific receptor activation","GH peaks during REM without affecting the primary tissue pathway","GH peaks upon waking leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'GH peaks during slow-wave sleep (stage 3-4)', 'Growth hormone is secreted in pulses, with the largest burst during slow-wave (deep) sleep in stages 3-4. Sleep deprivation markedly reduces GH release.'),
('9395fc4b-d7dc-5961-b261-e2a4fae86fd0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hormonal half-life depends primarily on:', '["Storage","Rate of secretion","Synthesis rate","Binding proteins","Rate of clearance/degradation"]'::jsonb, 'Binding proteins', 'Carrier proteins shield hormones from rapid hepatic and renal clearance, so protein-bound hormones such as cortisol and T4 have long half-lives. Peptide hormones lacking carriers are degraded within minutes.'),
('563a6078-ee04-590c-aa11-3f300f5e605e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The liver degrades most:', '["Steroid hormones (conjugation)","Peptide hormones (proteolysis)","Thyroid hormones (deiodination)","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The liver is the principal site of hormone inactivation: it conjugates steroids, proteolytically degrades peptide hormones, and deiodinates thyroid hormones during their clearance.'),
('e1e37289-8c61-5592-b534-84fcec8be175'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The kidneys normally clear which substances from the plasma?', '["Bound hormones resulting in decreased functional activity","Only thyroid hormones accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Only peptides mediated by specific receptor activation","Only steroids without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Free (unbound) hormones and metabolites"]'::jsonb, 'Free (unbound) hormones and metabolites', 'The glomerulus filters only the free (unbound) fraction of circulating hormones together with their water-soluble metabolites. Protein-bound hormones are too large to be filtered and remain in the circulation.'),
('21f0e710-661c-528b-a971-003afaf74368'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Feedback regulation can be:', '["Positive feedback (e.g. LH surge)","Feed-forward","Negative feedback (most common)","Both negative and positive","Only positive feedback"]'::jsonb, 'Both negative and positive', 'Feedback can be negative, in which a hormone suppresses its own further secretion (the most common pattern), or positive, in which it amplifies its own production—as in the pre-ovulatory LH surge.'),
('a703f4ec-92f9-505c-826f-91d9a2e093ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Positive feedback is exemplified by:', '["Cortisol on CRH resulting in decreased functional activity","Estrogen-induced LH surge before ovulation","Insulin on glucagon mediated by specific receptor activation","TSH on thyroid without affecting the primary tissue pathway","T3 on TSH leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen-induced LH surge before ovulation', 'The pre-ovulatory rise in estrogen triggers a massive LH surge that causes ovulation, a classic positive feedback loop. The other listed pairs are examples of negative feedback.'),
('5e342cec-3bb0-5344-a7a6-789550537d71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'GnRH must be secreted in a pulsatile manner to:', '["Have effect","Stimulate GH","Inhibit FSH/LH","Stimulate TSH","Stimulate FSH/LH release"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulate FSH/LH release', 'Pulsatile GnRH maintains the GnRH receptors on gonadotrophs needed for normal FSH and LH secretion. Continuous GnRH instead down-regulates these receptors and suppresses gonadotropins.'),
('66f78f3b-b7b4-52d5-9b2b-13e129ddbcc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The HPA axis is:', '["Stress response system","CRH -> ACTH -> Cortisol","Hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The HPA axis is the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis: CRH from the hypothalamus stimulates ACTH from the anterior pituitary, which drives cortisol release. It is the central neuroendocrine response to stress.'),
('52ed6f8d-94fa-58aa-a36a-742b1cfd2309'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The HPT axis is:', '["Regulates metabolism","TRH -> TSH -> T3/T4","Hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The HPT axis is the hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis, in which TRH drives TSH and TSH drives thyroidal T3/T4 secretion. Thyroid hormones regulate the metabolic rate and participate in negative feedback.'),
('f84bb994-68fd-550a-9234-de546895682e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Endocrine disorders can result from:', '["Receptor defects","Hormone deficiency","Hormone excess","Signal transduction defects","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Endocrine disease can arise from too much or too little hormone, from receptor abnormalities, or from defects in post-receptor signaling. Any disruption in the hormone-receptor-effector cascade produces dysfunction.'),
('9717a95d-39ca-5565-8c84-efdb15f1cb9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Pseudohypoparathyroidism is characterized by:', '["Calcium deficiency","End-organ resistance to PTH","PTH deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","PTH excess"]'::jsonb, 'End-organ resistance to PTH', 'Pseudohypoparathyroidism results from target-organ resistance to PTH, so serum calcium is low despite elevated PTH levels. The renal tubules and bone fail to respond to the hormone.'),
('2eb7969b-daeb-5b19-bb75-96025c77bcc2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'A 55-year-old male presents with episodic headache, palpitations, sweating, and paroxysmal hypertension. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Pheochromocytoma","Type 1 diabetes","Hashimoto thyroiditis","Graves disease","Addison disease"]'::jsonb, 'Pheochromocytoma', 'Episodic headache, palpitations, sweating, and paroxysmal hypertension are classic features of a pheochromocytoma, a catecholamine-secreting tumor of adrenal medullary chromaffin cells.'),
('a0f94ab2-fb9e-5ac5-8c70-9c010528ec4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Polyendocrine deficiency syndromes involve:', '["Adrenal tumor resulting in decreased functional activity","Pituitary tumor accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Multiple endocrine gland autoimmune failure","Single gland failure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Thyroid cancer leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple endocrine gland autoimmune failure', 'Polyendocrine deficiency syndromes (autoimmune polyglandular syndromes) result from autoimmune destruction affecting several endocrine glands at once, such as in APS-1 and APS-2, causing combined deficiencies.'),
('9aed06cb-b766-52be-a3fe-fc15ea122201'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The enteric endocrine system includes:', '["I cells (CCK)","G cells (gastrin)","L cells (GLP-1)","S cells (secretin)","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Enteroendocrine cells of the gut mucosa secrete these hormones: G cells gastrin, S cells secretin, I cells cholecystokinin (CCK), and L cells GLP-1. Together they coordinate digestion and metabolism.'),
('8a627b24-6b4b-5411-94fd-22c569f02823'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('5a9dbaad-8540-5b81-b0e9-0e97b9c9f546'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the primary action of erythropoietin (EPO)?', '["Increase O2 delivery","Stimulate RBC production in bone marrow","Increase hemoglobin","Decrease blood viscosity","Stimulate platelet production"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulate RBC production in bone marrow', 'Erythropoietin binds receptors on erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow and stimulates red blood cell production, which secondarily raises hemoglobin and oxygen-carrying capacity.'),
('c7e3f3e0-d24b-5000-9f64-63d73eba790f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt1: Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('34806444-a7ab-5d98-9a35-197170a5f61b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The hormone atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) is produced by:', '["Atrial cardiac myocytes","Lung accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Kidney mediated by specific receptor activation","Ventricles without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Adrenal leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Atrial cardiac myocytes', 'ANP is synthesized and released by atrial cardiac myocytes in response to atrial stretch from volume overload.'),
('ee0d0e66-6714-5389-9679-f16a47766e8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the primary physiologic action of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)?', '["Vasoconstrict resulting in decreased functional activity","Increase sodium reabsorption accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Stimulate RAAS mediated by specific receptor activation","Increase blood pressure without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Decrease blood pressure and volume (natriuresis)"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease blood pressure and volume (natriuresis)', 'ANP promotes renal sodium excretion (natriuresis) and vasodilation, thereby lowering blood volume and blood pressure.'),
('81c71628-d788-5990-8b39-5bdb4b63e428'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) is produced by:', '["Adrenal resulting in decreased functional activity","Ventricles (in response to ventricular stretch)","Brain mediated by specific receptor activation","Kidney without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Atria leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ventricles (in response to ventricular stretch)', 'BNP is secreted mainly by the cardiac ventricles in response to ventricular wall stretch from volume or pressure overload; its name is historical.'),
('3797555c-9e5a-57c2-81b8-b94d51b42516'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Leptin acts primarily on which brain region to suppress appetite?', '["Liver","Hypothalamus","Adipose tissue (white fat)","Pancreas","Stomach"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus', 'Leptin crosses the blood-brain barrier and acts on POMC neurons in the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus to suppress appetite and increase energy expenditure. It is produced by adipose tissue, so the hypothalamus is the receptor site.'),
('3c9f9d41-b9d0-55c7-a646-d131aac7cd31'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Leptin is primarily produced by which tissue and acts on which brain region?', '["Adipose","Hypothalamus (arcuate nucleus POMC neurons)","Adipose tissue; hypothalamus","Liver","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Adipose tissue; hypothalamus', 'Leptin is secreted by white adipose tissue and acts on arcuate nucleus POMC neurons in the hypothalamus to reduce food intake and increase energy expenditure.'),
('cb4b067a-bfab-57e8-a11c-cc65b7672926'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which organ produces ghrelin and what is its physiologic role?', '["Pancreas","Liver","Stomach (P/D1 cells)","Adipose tissue","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Stomach (P/D1 cells)', 'Ghrelin is secreted by endocrine cells (P/D1 cells) in the gastric fundus; as an orexigenic hormone it stimulates appetite.'),
('c4ebe873-a49b-54d2-8218-611ad83851dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Ghrelin stimulates:', '["Satiety","Leptin release","Insulin release","Appetite and GH release","Sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Appetite and GH release', 'Ghrelin is a potent appetite-stimulating (orexigenic) hormone and also stimulates growth hormone release from the anterior pituitary.'),
('dd011efa-40ba-5536-96c9-75d284bf9b47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which adipokine from adipose tissue is linked to insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome?', '["Muscle","Brain","Liver","Resistin","Pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Resistin', 'Resistin is an adipokine secreted by adipose tissue and macrophages; elevated levels are associated with insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.'),
('21e10dd7-43bd-525d-a60c-ed6b1f1d0ba0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Adiponectin is produced by:', '["Pancreas resulting in decreased functional activity","Adipose tissue (increases insulin sensitivity)","Liver mediated by specific receptor activation","Gut without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Muscle leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Adipose tissue (increases insulin sensitivity)', 'Adiponectin is secreted by white adipose tissue and acts as an insulin sensitizer with anti-inflammatory effects.'),
('f1a27551-34ad-5c39-8d9b-79e102a66a73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Adiponectin levels are:', '["Decreased in fasting resulting in decreased functional activity","Increased in diabetes accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Decreased in obesity and insulin resistance","Increased in obesity without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Unchanged leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased in obesity and insulin resistance', 'Adiponectin levels fall in obesity and insulin resistance and are inversely related to visceral adiposity; levels rise with weight loss.'),
('01c7b7fa-0043-5ee6-a9d3-5ee02e0b0fe4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the clinical stage of organ dysfunction characterized by loss of feedback control?', '["Normal physiological function","Severe impairment","Moderate impairment","Mild impairment","Advanced dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Advanced dysfunction', 'Advanced (decompensated) dysfunction is marked by loss of feedback control and clinical decompensation, often requiring exogenous hormone support.'),
('24f820e8-34ea-53ef-8c9e-c78257ff266f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: Potency refers to:', '["Dose required for given effect","Maximal response","Duration","Safety","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Dose required for given effect', 'More potent = lower dose needed for same effect.'),
('99e9d210-0fb5-5856-b758-b29f751862ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('7759215c-9986-523d-9eed-a1bbad8ece6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('e28d6865-14e2-5fae-9206-869d6da57626'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The stage of endocrine dysfunction requiring exogenous hormone replacement is:', '["Normal physiological function","Loss of feedback control","Advanced dysfunction","Impaired homeostatic regulation","Severe impairment"]'::jsonb, 'Severe impairment', 'Severe impairment represents end-organ failure in which the gland can no longer secrete adequate hormone, so exogenous replacement is required to sustain homeostasis.'),
('85215ad7-38ac-5dde-b7c6-75bbbb90dad7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('99744cbd-dbe3-5f9d-bb7b-96f8bb67c6b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('2285fc14-11f4-5a2b-b233-8716f9e6f45f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('d8dffd71-6634-5f5a-8b79-aaeef9659f70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('cf2d37ab-838f-573a-957c-01d874ee316a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which represents the earliest stage of endocrine organ dysfunction?', '["End-organ damage","Loss of feedback control","Subclinical abnormality","Maladaptive response","Adaptive response"]'::jsonb, 'Subclinical abnormality', 'The subclinical stage shows detectable laboratory abnormalities (e.g., elevated TSH) without clinical symptoms, representing the earliest detectable stage of dysfunction.'),
('7562ae95-16b8-54e0-8e32-0c3890095fac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Loss of feedback control with decompensation is characteristic of which stage of organ dysfunction?', '["Normal physiological function","Subclinical abnormality","Decompensated function","Impaired homeostatic regulation","Advanced dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Advanced dysfunction', 'Loss of feedback control with clinical decompensation characterizes advanced (decompensated) endocrine dysfunction.'),
('464efdd2-6579-5bc4-b8f5-5be3e26921f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: Grapefruit juice inhibits:', '["Intestinal CYP3A4","Hepatic CYP3A4","CYP2D6","Renal excretion","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal CYP3A4', 'Furanocoumarins in grapefruit inhibit intestinal CYP3A4.'),
('4835f57c-dcdb-5adc-8cf7-a065227ff743'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Impaired homeostatic regulation with subclinical changes represents which stage?', '["End-organ damage","Early dysfunction","Loss of feedback control","Mild impairment","Normal physiological function"]'::jsonb, 'Early dysfunction', 'Early dysfunction is defined by impaired homeostatic regulation with subclinical laboratory changes but no overt symptoms.'),
('45a79813-d104-5382-8580-dfcf06489859'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Zero-order kinetics means:', '["Constant amount eliminated/time","Constant fraction/time","Dose-dependent elimination","First-order at high doses","Saturable absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Constant amount eliminated/time', 'Zero-order: constant amount eliminated regardless of concentration.'),
('8724772c-f8c9-5764-8683-0c03a73a9afc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt1: Phenytoin zero-order kinetics because:', '["CYP enzymes are saturated","Renal secretion saturated","Protein binding saturated","Gut absorption saturated","Biliary excretion saturated"]'::jsonb, 'CYP enzymes are saturated', 'CYP2C9/CYP3A4 become saturated at therapeutic concentrations.'),
('da30c713-a88a-54c6-b1cb-b69bd117fba8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Phase I metabolism reaction:', '["Oxidation","Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidation', 'Phase I: oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis.'),
('08b715cc-118a-58ea-80f5-b025d610913e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Intact negative feedback regulation of hormone levels represents:', '["Early dysfunction","Adaptive response","Functional reserve depletion","Loss of feedback control","Normal physiological function"]'::jsonb, 'Normal physiological function', 'Intact negative feedback keeps hormone secretion within the homeostatic range, representing normal physiological function.'),
('c6f7b697-8c30-5644-a04d-bf4e54068fe3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Highest capacity Phase II reaction:', '["Glucuronidation","Sulfation","Acetylation","Methylation","Glutathione conjugation"]'::jsonb, 'Glucuronidation', 'Due to abundant UDP-glucuronic acid cofactor.'),
('09332584-c607-5ddd-bca0-0c5cf261a285'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Acetylation polymorphism affects:', '["Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide","Warfarin, phenytoin accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Codeine, tramadol mediated by specific receptor activation","Omeprazole without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Diazepam leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide', 'NAT2 polymorphism: slow vs fast acetylators.'),
('8f517942-7e5c-5465-bf0d-be6f30e227a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt1: Major binding protein for acidic drugs:', '["Albumin","Alpha-1 acid glycoprotein","Globulin","Transferrin","Ceruloplasmin"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Albumin binds acidic (anionic) drugs.'),
('ac35eefe-dd5a-5613-8de7-5e1afe3ff523'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the endocrine dysfunction continuum, normal homeostatic control of hormone secretion represents:', '["Hungry bone syndrome","Chronic renal failure","Primary hyperparathyroidism","Hypocalcemia","Normal physiological function"]'::jsonb, 'Normal physiological function', 'Intact negative feedback maintains hormone secretion within the homeostatic range; in the dysfunction continuum this reflects normal physiological function.'),
('67c30bc6-5928-5b4a-931c-9807c762107f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Homeostatic regulation of hormone levels with no clinical abnormalities represents:', '["Subclinical abnormality","Normal physiological function","Adaptive response","Moderate impairment","Advanced dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 'Normal physiological function', 'Homeostatic regulation of hormone levels with no clinical abnormalities represents normal physiological function.'),
('e44e3b6c-e443-504d-96c0-c7bc5a0f9792'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, P-glycoprotein is an efflux transporter that:', '["Pumps drugs out of cells","Enhances absorption","Binds to proteins","Metabolises drugs","Excretes in bile only"]'::jsonb, 'Pumps drugs out of cells', 'P-gp efflux at intestine, kidney, and BBB limits drug absorption.'),
('ffe8fab9-5e41-58e1-b784-c53956731114'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Loading dose formula:', '["Vd x Css / F","CL x Css x tau / F","Vd x CL / Css","Css x tau / CL","Fx Css / Vd"]'::jsonb, 'Vd x Css / F', 'Loading dose depends on Vd and target concentration.'),
('3147864c-f969-58f7-a617-701d0cef02a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Laboratory abnormalities without clinical symptoms represent which stage of endocrine dysfunction?', '["Subclinical abnormality","Mild impairment","Moderate impairment","End-organ damage","Severe impairment"]'::jsonb, 'Subclinical abnormality', 'Subclinical dysfunction is characterized by detectable laboratory abnormalities without accompanying clinical symptoms.'),
('a567a400-2260-5511-b4cb-be18c05c641a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Steady state reached after:', '["4-5 half-lives","1 half-life","2 half-lives","6-7 half-lives","10 half-lives"]'::jsonb, '4-5 half-lives', 'Css reached after 4-5 half-lives.'),
('9a924214-5ac4-5348-b4f4-83e0be0cbcff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: Codeine converted to morphine by:', '["CYP2D6","CYP3A4","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2D6', 'CYP2D6 O-demethylates codeine. Poor metabolizers get no benefit.'),
('ce9bdab0-6b2e-5581-9d87-1e129aac7697'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the earliest stage of organ dysfunction in the endocrine dysfunction continuum?', '["Decompensated function","Early dysfunction","Functional reserve depletion","Moderate impairment","Maladaptive response"]'::jsonb, 'Early dysfunction', 'Early dysfunction is the first stage of the continuum, marked by impaired homeostatic regulation with subclinical changes before overt symptoms.'),
('bc6292b2-d32f-5d2e-95d2-b4c319244c66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Large Vd means drug is:', '["Distributed into tissues","Confined to plasma","Rapidly excreted","Bound to albumin","Hepatically metabolised"]'::jsonb, 'Distributed into tissues', 'Large Vd >1 L/kg indicates extensive tissue distribution.'),
('d67de533-ce7c-50ec-a0b2-c9dfa805b42d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt1: Maintenance dose primarily depends on:', '["Clearance","Volume of distribution","Half-life","Bioavailability","Protein binding"]'::jsonb, 'Clearance', 'Maintenance dose = CL x Css x tau / F.'),
('40c7757b-1431-5a4f-bfaf-ed0caa42f93f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Loading dose primarily depends on:', '["Volume of distribution","Clearance","Half-life","Bioavailability","Renal function"]'::jsonb, 'Volume of distribution', 'Loading dose = Vd x Css / F.'),
('26185862-ff7d-5ebb-a684-a51df295bc47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: Fick''s law: diffusion rate proportional to:', '["Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness","Molecular weight x concentration accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Protein binding x pH mediated by specific receptor activation","Vd x clearance without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Ionisation x pKa leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Surface area x concentration gradient / thickness', 'Rate = SA xD x dC/dx.'),
('e1823cea-ad5f-5dd9-9abc-effe29c024c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Orally absorbed drugs first enter:', '["Portal vein","Systemic circulation","Lymphatics","IVC","Thoracic duct"]'::jsonb, 'Portal vein', 'Portal vein -> liver (first-pass) -> systemic circulation.'),
('2fdaba8c-7671-5f59-943d-4b1df39c0dfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Acetaminophen toxic metabolite:', '["NAPQI","MPP+","MPTP","N-oxide","Sulfate conjugate"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI', 'NAPQI normally detoxified by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione.'),
('847e8ef7-802f-59fb-a887-a854f5ab9dd1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt1: Acetaminophen antidote:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Fomepizole","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine', 'NAC replenishes glutathione stores.'),
('305df505-80ab-5579-b11c-d3e6a7750eb1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Protein binding fraction (fu) ranges:', '["0 to 1","-1 to 0","0 to 100","-100 to 100","1 to 100"]'::jsonb, '0 to 1', 'Only unbound (free) drug is pharmacologically active.'),
('59905a07-bacd-51e4-bf67-e2945f24e4c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: EC50 is concentration producing:', '["50% max effect","100% max effect","No effect","Toxic effects","Lethal in 50%"]'::jsonb, '50% max effect', 'EC50 = concentration at 50% of maximal response.'),
('3afd3cab-d871-55a8-9afc-c5e607cd1813'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the MMSE score range that indicates mild cognitive impairment?', '["24-30","18-23","10-17","0-9","31-36"]'::jsonb, '18-23', 'On the Mini-Mental State Examination, scores of 18-23 indicate mild cognitive impairment, 24-30 are considered normal, 10-17 indicate moderate impairment, and 0-9 indicate severe impairment.'),
('2c58d646-1260-50d7-b549-c48d08d8421c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: Efficacy refers to:', '["Ability to produce maximal response","Dose for effect accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Absorption rate mediated by specific receptor activation","Duration without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Safety leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to produce maximal response', 'Emax = maximum response a drug can produce.'),
('c497b0d1-3287-5891-8ac7-11c4abfe048f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt1: What is the therapeutic index of a drug?', '["TD50 / ED50","ED50 / TD50","LD50 / ED50","ED50 / LD50","TD50 / LD50"]'::jsonb, 'TD50 / ED50', 'TI = TD50/ED50. Wider TI = safer drug.'),
('69339784-5d06-50f8-9e44-3dccc9354fec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Bioavailability is defined as:', '["Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation","Fraction absorbed from GI tract accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Total drug in plasma mediated by specific receptor activation","Rate of elimination without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Volume of distribution leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Fraction of unchanged drug reaching systemic circulation', 'F = fraction of dose reaching systemic circulation unchanged.'),
('e56bdd8d-eb79-559c-b97f-e03cd5620268'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: Primary site of first-pass metabolism:', '["Liver","Kidney","Lung","Intestine","Spleen"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Orally absorbed drugs enter portal circulation and are metabolised by the liver first.'),
('9d7d5a63-a517-5f7f-a071-50f0a1798e00'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'In the study of Endocrinology Pt1, Volume of distribution (Vd) equals:', '["Amount of drug / Plasma concentration","Clearance / Rate","Half-life x Clearance","Dose / F","Absorption rate"]'::jsonb, 'Amount of drug / Plasma concentration', 'Vd = Amount / Concentration. Large Vd = extensive tissue distribution.'),
('7f55f4db-7d45-5cb8-b1a2-d9ac87743f98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'From a pathophysiological standpoint regarding Endocrinology Pt1: CYP enzyme metabolising most drugs:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP2C19","CYP1A2"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'CYP3A4 metabolises 50% of clinically used drugs.'),
('71948799-bf23-52e7-a0f7-e74ce9eb862b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Concerning diagnostic evaluations in Endocrinology Pt1: Rifampicin induces:', '["CYP3A4 and other CYPs","CYP2D6 only","CYP2C19 only","CYP1A2 only","CYP2C9 only"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and other CYPs', 'Rifampicin induces CYP3A4, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, and P-gp.'),
('5aa44307-75d0-5994-8adc-d3ab6fa456dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'During therapeutic management of Endocrinology Pt1, Erythromycin inhibits:', '["CYP3A4","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4', 'Erythromycin/clarithromycin are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.'),
('8c4da46b-d03d-506c-a52a-ad2f7d501bc7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Regarding clinical cases in Endocrinology Pt1: St. John''s Wort induces:', '["CYP3A4 and P-gp","CYP2D6","CYP2C9","CYP1A2","CYP2C19"]'::jsonb, 'CYP3A4 and P-gp', 'St. John''s Wort induces CYP3A4 and P-gp, reducing drug levels.'),
('53490ceb-4f49-58c0-ad4d-12a6d99df56d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Decompensated endocrine failure that requires exogenous hormone support represents which stage of dysfunction?', '["Severe impairment","Early dysfunction","Maladaptive response","Impaired homeostatic regulation","Moderate impairment"]'::jsonb, 'Severe impairment', 'When an endocrine gland can no longer produce adequate hormone even under maximal stimulation and exogenous replacement is required, the failure is classified as severe impairment.'),
('fbf31511-b422-5bbb-b107-289d5e730cc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Intact feedback mechanisms with normal hormone levels represent which state?', '["Impaired homeostatic regulation","Normal physiological function","Functional reserve depletion","Early dysfunction","Compensated function"]'::jsonb, 'Normal physiological function', 'Normal hormone levels with intact negative feedback indicate that the endocrine axis is operating within the normal physiological range without requiring compensation.'),
('39796cd0-a92f-5023-918e-ccb44e3fbfef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'The hypothalamus is connected to the pituitary gland via which of the following?', '["Median eminence","Optic chiasm","Infundibulum","All of the above","Hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal system"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'The hypothalamus connects to the pituitary through the infundibulum (pituitary stalk), which contains the median eminence and the hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal vessels, so all of the listed components are involved.'),
('f0e0a971-38d2-5521-98e6-0ecac396e349'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hormone is released by the posterior pituitary?', '["FSH and LH","ACTH","Oxytocin and ADH","TSH","Growth hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin and ADH', 'The posterior pituitary stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin (ADH), which are synthesized in the hypothalamus. FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH, and growth hormone are secreted by the anterior pituitary.'),
('f8a7d44c-5323-5e2b-9e10-259c40799a0f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hormone directly stimulates cortisol release from the adrenal cortex?', '["TSH","CRH","ADH","ACTH","Prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH', 'ACTH acts directly on the adrenal cortex to stimulate cortisol secretion. CRH acts at the anterior pituitary to trigger ACTH release, making ACTH the direct stimulus for cortisol production.'),
('0cf8ce7d-c03c-52de-9be3-ac6d447a51f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'ACTH is secreted by which structure?', '["Posterior pituitary","Hypothalamus","Adrenal cortex","Adrenal medulla","Anterior pituitary (corticotrophs)"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior pituitary (corticotrophs)', 'ACTH is produced by corticotroph cells of the anterior pituitary and is released in response to CRH from the hypothalamus.'),
('43b1e21a-950a-5436-8b47-d7c9ea740bf8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which hormones are produced by the adrenal cortex?', '["ADH and oxytocin","Renin and angiotensin","ACTH and MSH","Cortisol, aldosterone, androgens","Epinephrine and norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Cortisol, aldosterone, androgens', 'The adrenal cortex synthesizes cortisol (zona fasciculata), aldosterone (zona glomerulosa), and androgens (zona reticularis). Catecholamines are produced by the adrenal medulla.'),
('49d5032f-dea7-5efa-b491-84813708711b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Cushing syndrome is caused by which of the following?', '["Cortisol deficiency","Excess aldosterone","Excess cortisol","Aldosterone deficiency","Excess ADH"]'::jsonb, 'Excess cortisol', 'Cushing syndrome results from chronic glucocorticoid excess, most often from prolonged exogenous steroid use or endogenous overproduction of cortisol by the adrenal glands or pituitary.'),
('5cdccd03-36bb-5d00-af9e-d3e5e916d20d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Addison disease is caused by which of the following?', '["Adrenal medulla insufficiency","Hypothalamic insufficiency","Adrenal cortex insufficiency","Pituitary insufficiency","Thyroid insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal cortex insufficiency', 'Addison disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) is caused by destruction or dysfunction of the adrenal cortex, leading to deficiency of cortisol and aldosterone.'),
('8a1d3d47-c6ea-59a5-8dec-ba33b3353296'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) produces which combination of findings?', '["Normal BP with hypernatremia","Hypertension and hypokalemia","Hypotension and hyperkalemia","Hypertension and hyperkalemia","Hypotension and hypokalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and hypokalemia', 'Primary hyperaldosteronism causes excessive sodium retention and potassium excretion, producing hypertension with hypokalemia.'),
('c22404d9-2207-54c5-9016-1cb9ecc3f8c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Graves disease is associated with which of the following?', '["Thyroid cancer","Thyroiditis","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism and exophthalmos","Thyroid nodule"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthyroidism and exophthalmos', 'Graves disease is an autoimmune disorder caused by thyroid-stimulating antibodies that lead to hyperthyroidism. Infiltrative ophthalmopathy (exophthalmos) is a characteristic extra-thyroidal feature.'),
('0d615629-ef17-505e-8d3e-8815d9d31de8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Hashimoto thyroiditis is characterized as which of the following?', '["Viral thyroiditis","Autoimmune hyperthyroidism","Autoimmune hypothyroidism","Bacterial thyroiditis","Subacute thyroiditis"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune hypothyroidism', 'Hashimoto thyroiditis is an autoimmune disorder in which autoantibodies progressively destroy the thyroid gland, causing hypothyroidism. It is the most common cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient regions.'),
('308e0bbb-7d86-5cd5-bfc7-35d5e4b45e33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Type 1 diabetes mellitus is caused by which of the following?', '["Beta cell exhaustion","Pancreatitis","Insulin resistance","Autoimmune destruction of beta cells","Pancreatectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of beta cells', 'Type 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of the insulin-producing pancreatic beta cells, causing absolute insulin deficiency.'),
('d061aa9d-b0bd-5ba2-aa87-833ccff7e330'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Type 2 diabetes mellitus is characterized by which of the following?', '["Insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency","Absolute insulin deficiency","Pancreatic enzyme deficiency","Autoimmune beta cell destruction","Glucagon excess"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency', 'Type 2 diabetes is characterized by insulin resistance in peripheral tissues combined with relative insulin deficiency as beta-cell function declines.'),
('d20d20ee-028c-53c4-b587-ec8367f95aa3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the normal fasting blood glucose level?', '["<70 mg/dL","<100 mg/dL","<140 mg/dL","<126 mg/dL","<200 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '<100 mg/dL', 'Normal fasting plasma glucose is below 100 mg/dL. Values of 100-125 mg/dL indicate impaired fasting glucose, and a fasting level of 126 mg/dL or higher suggests diabetes mellitus.'),
('f97da395-0dbd-53fe-8371-8f2718b36067'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'HbA1c reflects average blood glucose control over which period?', '["1 month","2 weeks","6 months","1 week","2-3 months"]'::jsonb, '2-3 months', 'HbA1c reflects the average blood glucose concentration over the preceding 2-3 months because it measures the glycation of hemoglobin across the red blood cell lifespan.'),
('8d37a609-6904-577d-a9cb-d43d8caaab6d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'All of the following are chemical classes of hormones except:', '["Peptide/protein","Steroid","Nucleotide","Eicosanoid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'Hormones are classified chemically as peptide/protein, steroid, or amine molecules, and eicosanoids derived from fatty acids act as local signaling molecules. Nucleotides are the building blocks of nucleic acids and are not a class of hormones.'),
('6c015c07-773a-5926-a038-6de32ee49113'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'What is the basic building block of nucleic acids?', '["Peptide/protein","Steroid","Nucleotide","Eicosanoid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'Nucleotides are the monomeric building blocks of nucleic acids (DNA and RNA). Each nucleotide consists of a phosphate group, a pentose sugar, and a nitrogenous base.'),
('a3e2017f-4d17-5789-bc0e-5222ad918656'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Each of the following is a chemical class of hormones except:', '["Peptide/protein","Steroid","Nucleotide","Eicosanoid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'Peptide/protein, steroid, and amine hormones, along with eicosanoid lipid signaling molecules, are recognized chemical classes of hormones. Nucleotides are components of nucleic acids and do not function as hormones.'),
('65e14647-d8f9-58e2-8692-1aad71938740'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Which of the following is not a chemical class of hormones?', '["Peptide/protein","Steroid","Nucleotide","Eicosanoid","Amine"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'Peptide/protein, steroid, amine, and eicosanoid molecules constitute recognized hormone classes, whereas nucleotides are nucleic acid building blocks and are not classified as hormones.'),
('b53d4bb5-8cf2-5e36-a4a0-41259cc85d9c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology - Endocrinology Part 1', 'Eicosanoids are derived from which of the following precursors?', '["Glucose resulting in decreased functional activity","Tyrosine accompanied by associated clinical symptoms","Arachidonic acid (fatty acid)","Tryptophan without affecting the primary tissue pathway","Cholesterol leading to subsequent cellular adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Arachidonic acid (fatty acid)', 'Eicosanoids such as prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes are derived from arachidonic acid, a 20-carbon polyunsaturated fatty acid released from membrane phospholipids.'),
